¥dmeta¢fmodulecDRClgenerated_atx2026-09-01T00:08:39.362589Zjbook_order˜¸BcGen¤dnamegGenesisiosis_namecGenlabbreviationcGenhchapters˜2˜ƒx/In the beginning God created heaven, and earth.€€ƒxzAnd the earth was void and empty, and darkness was upon the face of the deep; and the spirit of God moved over the waters.€€ƒx0And God said: Be light made. And light was made.€€ƒxSAnd God saw the light that it was good; and he divided the light from the darkness.€€ƒx_And he called the light Day, and the darkness Night; and there was evening and morning one day.€€ƒxlAnd God said: Let there be a firmament made amidst the waters: and let it divide the waters from the waters.€€ƒxˆAnd God made a firmament, and divided the waters that were under the firmament, from those that were above the firmament, and it was so.€€ƒxVAnd God called the firmament, Heaven; and the evening and morning were the second day.€€ƒxGod also said; Let the waters that are under the heaven, be gathered together into one place: and let the dry land appear. And it was so done.€€ƒx{And God called the dry land, Earth; and the gathering together of the waters, he called Seas. And God saw that it was good.€€ƒx¼And he said: let the earth bring forth green herb, and such as may seed, and the fruit tree yielding fruit after its kind, which may have seed in itself upon the earth. And it was so done.€€ƒxÇAnd the earth brought forth the green herb, and such as yieldeth seed according to its kind, and the tree that beareth fruit, having seed each one according to its kind. And God saw that it was good.€€ƒx3And the evening and the morning were the third day.€€ƒx§And God said: Let there be lights made in the firmament of heaven, to divide the day and the night, and let them be for signs, and for seasons, and for days and years:€€ƒxZTo shine in the firmament of heaven, and to give light upon the earth, and it was so done.€€ƒxtAnd God made two great lights: a greater light to rule the day; and a lesser light to rule the night: and the stars.€€ƒxCAnd he set them in the firmament of heaven to shine upon the earth.€€ƒxjAnd to rule the day and the night, and to divide the light and the darkness. And God saw that it was good.€€ƒx0And the evening and morning were the fourth day.€€ƒx”God also said: let the waters bring forth the creeping creature having life, and the fowl that may fly over the earth under the firmament of heaven.€€ƒxÌAnd God created the great whales, and every living and moving creature, which the waters brought forth, according to their kinds, and every winged fowl according to its kind. And God saw that it was good.€€ƒxƒAnd he blessed them, saying: Increase and multiply, and fill the waters of the sea: and let the birds be multiplied upon the earth.€€ƒx/And the evening and morning were the fifth day.€€ƒx«And God said: Let the earth bring forth the living creature in its kind, cattle and creeping things, and beasts of the earth, according to their kinds. And it was so done.€€ƒx£And God made the beasts of the earth according to their kinds, and cattle, and every thing that creepeth on the earth after its kind. And God saw that it was good.€€ƒxäAnd he said: Let us make man to our image and likeness: and let him have dominion over the fishes of the sea, and the fowls of the air, and the beasts, and the whole earth, and every creeping creature that moveth upon the earth.€€ƒxjAnd God created man to his own image: to the image of God he created him: male and female he created them.€€ƒxÉAnd God blessed them, saying: Increase and multiply, and fill the earth, and subdue it, and rule over the fishes of the sea, and the fowls of the air, and all living creatures that move upon the earth.€€ƒxœAnd God said: Behold I have given you every herb bearing seed upon the earth, and all trees that have in themselves seed of their own kind, to be your meat:€€ƒx²And to all beasts of the earth, and to every fowl of the air, and to all that move upon the earth, and wherein there is life, that they may have to feed upon. And it was so done.€€ƒxuAnd God saw all the things that he had made, and they were very good. And the evening and morning were the sixth day.€€˜ƒxJSo the heavens and the earth were finished, and all the furniture of them.€€ƒx‚And on the seventh day God ended his work which he had made: and he rested on the seventh day from all his work which he had done.€€ƒx|And he blessed the seventh day, and sanctified it: because in it he had rested from all his work which God created and made.€€ƒxŠThese are the generations of the heaven and the earth, when they were created, in the day that the Lord God made the heaven and the earth:€€ƒxÆAnd every plant of the field before it sprung up in the earth, and every herb of the ground before it grew: for the Lord God had not rained upon the earth; and there was not a man to till the earth.€€ƒxJBut a spring rose out of the earth, watering all the surface of the earth.€€ƒxƒAnd the Lord God formed man of the slime of the earth: and breathed into his face the breath of life, and man became a living soul.€€ƒxqAnd the Lord God had planted a paradise of pleasure from the beginning: wherein he placed man whom he had formed.€€ƒxÅAnd the Lord God brought forth of the ground all manner of trees, fair to behold, and pleasant to eat of: the tree of life also in the midst of paradise: and the tree of knowledge of good and evil.€€ƒxnAnd a river went out of the place of pleasure to water paradise, which from thence is divided into four heads.€€ƒxhThe name of the one is Phison: that is it which compasseth all the land of Hevilath, where gold groweth.€€ƒxTAnd the gold of that land is very good: there is found bdellium, and the onyx stone.€€ƒxcAnd the name of the second river is Gehon: the same is it that compasseth all the land of Ethiopia.€€ƒxvAnd the name of the third river is Tigris: the same passeth along by the Assyrians. And the fourth river is Euphrates.€€ƒxbAnd the Lord God took man, and put him into the paradise of pleasure, to dress it, and to keep it.€€ƒxGAnd he commanded him, saying: Of every tree of paradise thou shalt eat:€€ƒx‰But of the tree of knowledge of good and evil, thou shalt not eat. For in what day soever thou shalt eat of it, thou shalt die the death.€€ƒxdAnd the Lord God said: It is not good for man to be alone: let us make him a help like unto himself.€€ƒxåAnd the Lord God having formed out of the ground all the beasts of the earth, and all the fowls of the air, brought them to Adam to see what he would call them: for whatsoever Adam called any living creature the same is its name.€€ƒx¥And Adam called all the beasts by their names, and all the fowls of the air, and all the cattle of the field: but for Adam there was not found a helper like himself.€€ƒx€Then the Lord God cast a deep sleep upon Adam: and when he was fast asleep, he took one of his ribs, and filled up flesh for it.€€ƒx]And the Lord God built the rib which he took from Adam into a woman: and brought her to Adam.€€ƒx€And Adam said: This now is bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh; she shall be called woman, because she was taken out of man.€€ƒxpWherefore a man shall leave father and mother, and shall cleave to his wife: and they shall be two in one flesh.€€ƒxJAnd they were both naked: to wit, Adam and his wife: and were not ashamed.€€˜ƒxÉNow the serpent was more subtle than any of the beasts of the earth which the Lord God had made. And he said to the woman: Why hath God commanded you, that you should not eat of every tree of paradise?€€ƒx]And the woman answered him, saying: Of the fruit of the trees that are in paradise we do eat:€€ƒx£But of the fruit of the tree which is in the midst of paradise, God hath commanded us that we should not eat; and that we should not touch it, lest perhaps we die.€€ƒxCAnd the serpent said to the woman: No, you shall not die the death.€€ƒxŒFor God doth know that in what day soever you shall eat thereof, your eyes shall be opened: and you shall be as Gods, knowing good and evil.€€ƒx¶And the woman saw that the tree was good to eat, and fair to the eyes, and delightful to behold: and she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave to her husband, who did eat.€€ƒx’And the eyes of them both were opened: and when they perceived themselves to be naked, they sewed together fig leaves, and made themselves aprons.€€ƒxµAnd when they heard the voice of the Lord God walking in paradise at the afternoon air, Adam and his wife hid themselves from the face of the Lord God, amidst the trees of paradise.€€ƒx>And the Lord God called Adam, and said to him: Where art thou?€€ƒxdAnd he said: I heard thy voice in paradise; and I was afraid, because I was naked, and I hid myself.€€ƒxšAnd he said to him: And who hath told thee that thou wast naked, but that thou hast eaten of the tree whereof I commanded thee that thou shouldst not eat?€€ƒxeAnd Adam said: The woman, whom thou gavest me to be my companion, gave me of the tree, and I did eat.€€ƒxvAnd the Lord God said to the woman: Why hast thou done this? And she answered: The serpent deceived me, and I did eat.€€ƒxÕAnd the Lord God said to the serpent: Because thou hast done this thing, thou art cursed among all cattle, and beasts of the earth: upon thy breast shalt thou go, and earth shalt thou eat all the days of thy life.€€ƒxI will put enmities between thee and the woman, and thy seed and her seed: she shall crush thy head, and thou shalt lie in wait for her heel.€€ƒxÊTo the woman also he said: I will multiply thy sorrows, and thy conceptions: in sorrow shalt thou bring forth children, and thou shalt be under thy husband's power, and he shall have dominion over thee.€€ƒyAnd to Adam he said: Because thou hast hearkened to the voice of thy wife, and hast eaten of the tree, whereof I commanded thee, that thou shouldst not eat, cursed is the earth in thy work: with labour and toil shalt thou eat thereof all the days of thy life.€€ƒx\Thorns and thistles shall it bring forth to thee, and thou shalt eat the herbs of the earth.€€ƒxIn the sweat of thy face shalt thou eat bread till thou return to the earth out of which thou wast taken: for dust thou art, and into dust thou shalt return.€€ƒxWAnd Adam called the name of his wife Eve: because she was the mother of all the living.€€ƒxPAnd the Lord God made for Adam and his wife garments of skins, and clothed them.€€ƒx·And he said: Behold Adam is become as one of us, knowing good and evil: now therefore lest perhaps he put forth his hand and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever.€€ƒxeAnd the Lord God sent him out of the paradise of pleasure, to till the earth from which he was taken.€€ƒx˜And he cast out Adam: and placed before the paradise of pleasure Cherubims, and a flaming sword, turning every way, to keep the way of the tree of life.€€˜ƒxjAnd Adam knew Eve his wife; who conceived and brought forth Cain, saying: I have gotten a man through God.€€ƒx]And again she brought forth his brother Abel. And Abel was a shepherd, and Cain a husbandman.€€ƒxfAnd it came to pass after many days, that Cain offered, of the fruits of the earth, gifts to the Lord.€€ƒx{Abel also offered of the firstlings of his flock, and of their fat: and the Lord had respect to Abel, and to his offerings.€€ƒxhBut to Cain and his offerings he had no respect: and Cain was exceeding angry, and his countenance fell.€€ƒxPAnd the Lord said to him: Why art thou angry? and why is thy countenance fallen?€€ƒx´If thou do well, shalt thou not receive? but if ill, shall not sin forthwith be present at the door? but the lust thereof shall be under thee, and thou shalt have dominion over it.€€ƒxAnd Cain said to Abel his brother: Let us go forth abroad. And when they were in the field, Cain rose up against his brother Abel, and slew him.€€ƒxlAnd the Lord said to Cain: Where is thy brother Abel? And he answered: I know not: am I my brother's keeper?€€ƒxfAnd he said to him: What hast thou done? the voice of thy brother's blood crieth to me from the earth.€€ƒxNow therefore cursed shalt thou be upon the earth, which hath opened her mouth and received the blood of thy brother at thy hand.€€ƒxvWhen thou shalt till it, it shall not yield to thee its fruit: a fugitive and a vagabond shalt thou be upon the earth.€€ƒxQAnd Cain said to the Lord: My iniquity is greater than that I may deserve pardon.€€ƒxÎBehold thou dost cast me out this day from the face of the earth, and from thy face I shall be hid, and I shall be a vagabond and a fugitive on the earth: every one therefore that findeth me, shall kill me.€€ƒx¾And the Lord said to him: No, it shall not so be: but whosoever shall kill Cain, shall be punished sevenfold. And the Lord set a mark upon Cain, that whosoever found him should not kill him.€€ƒxkAnd Cain went out from the face of the Lord, and dwelt as a fugitive on the earth at the east side of Eden.€€ƒx”And Cain knew his wife, and she conceived, and brought forth Henoch: and he built a city, and called the name thereof by the name of his son Henoch.€€ƒxgAnd Henoch begot Irad, and Irad begot Maviael, and Maviael begot Mathusael, and Mathusael begot Lamech,€€ƒxQWho took two wives: the name of the one was Ada, and the name of the other Sella.€€ƒx[And Ada brought forth Jabel: who was the father of such as dwell in tents, and of herdsmen.€€ƒxcAnd his brother's name was Jubal: he was the father of them that play upon the harp and the organs.€€ƒxŒSella also brought forth Tubalcain, who was a hammerer and artificer in every work of brass and iron. And the sister of Tubalcain was Noema.€€ƒxºAnd Lamech said to his wives Ada and Sella: Hear my voice, ye wives of Lamech, hearken to my speech: for I have slain a man to the wounding of myself, and a stripling to my own bruising.€€ƒxTSevenfold vengeance shall be taken for Cain: but for Lamech seventy times sevenfold.€€ƒx–Adam also knew his wife again: and she brought forth a son, and called his name Seth, saying: God hath given me another seed for Abel, whom Cain slew.€€ƒxgBut to Seth also was born a son, whom he called Enos: this man began to call upon the name of the Lord.€€˜ ƒxpThis is the book of the generation of Adam. In the day that God created man, he made him to the likeness of God.€€ƒxqHe created them male and female; and blessed them: and called their name Adam, in the day when they were created.€€ƒxsAnd Adam lived a hundred and thirty years, and begot a son to his own image and likeness, and called his name Seth.€€ƒxeAnd the days of Adam, after he begot Seth, were eight hundred years: and he begot sons and daughters.€€ƒxUAnd all the time that Adam lived, came to nine hundred and thirty years, and he died.€€ƒx9Seth also lived a hundred and five years, and begot Enos.€€ƒx`And Seth lived after he begot Enos, eight hundred and seven years, and begot sons and daughters.€€ƒxIAnd all the days of Seth were nine hundred and twelve years, and he died.€€ƒx.And Enos lived ninety years, and begot Cainan.€€ƒxYAfter whose birth he lived eight hundred and fifteen years, and begot sons and daughters.€€ƒxGAnd all the days of Enos were nine hundred and five years, and he died.€€ƒx3And Cainan lived seventy years, and begot Malaleel.€€ƒxfAnd Cainan lived after he begot Malaleel, eight hundred and forty years, and begot sons and daughters.€€ƒxHAnd all the days of Cainan were nine hundred and ten years, and he died.€€ƒx4And Malaleel lived sixty-five years and begot Jared.€€ƒxfAnd Malaleel lived after he begot Jared, eight hundred and thirty years, and begot sons and daughters.€€ƒxSAnd all the days of Malaleel were eight hundred and ninety-five years, and he died.€€ƒx@And Jared lived a hundred and sixty-two years, and begot Henoch.€€ƒxYAnd Jared lived after he begot Henoch, eight hundred years, and begot sons and daughters.€€ƒxMAnd all the days of Jared were nine hundred and sixty-two years, and he died.€€ƒx7And Henoch lived sixty-five years, and begot Mathusala.€€ƒxrAnd Henoch walked with God: and lived after he begot Mathusala, three hundred years, and begot sons and daughters.€€ƒxCAnd all the days of Henoch were three hundred and sixty-five years.€€ƒxCAnd he walked with God, and was seen no more: because God took him.€€ƒxGAnd Mathusala lived a hundred and eighty-seven years, and begot Lamech.€€ƒxmAnd Mathlusala lived after he begot Lamech, seven hundred and eighty-two years, and begot sons and daughters.€€ƒxRAnd all the days of Mathusala were nine hundred and sixty-nine years, and he died.€€ƒxAAnd Lamech lived a hundred and eighty-two years, and begot a son.€€ƒxAnd he called his name Noe, saying: This same shall comfort us from the works and labours of our hands on the earth, which the Lord hath cursed.€€ƒxfAnd Lamech lived after he begot Noe, five hundred and ninety-five years, and begot sons and daughters.€€ƒx¡And all the days of Lamech came to seven hundred and seventy-seven years, and he died. And Noe, when he was five hundred years old, begot Sem, Cham, and Japheth.€€ƒ`€€–ƒxsThe sons of God seeing the daughters of men, that they were fair, took to themselves wives of all which they chose.€€ƒx€And God said: My spirit shall not remain in man for ever, because he is flesh, and his days shall be a hundred and twenty years.€€ƒx¹Now giants were upon the earth in those days. For after the sons of God went in to the daughters of men, and they brought forth children, these are the mighty men of old, men of renown.€€ƒxŠAnd God seeing that the wickedness of men was great on the earth, and that all the thought of their heart was bent upon evil at all times,€€ƒxcIt repented him that he had made man on the earth. And being touched inwardly with sorrow of heart,€€ƒxÇHe said: I will destroy man, whom I have created, from the face of the earth, from man even to beasts, from the creeping thing even to the fowls of the air, for it repenteth me that I have made them.€€ƒx$But Noe found grace before the Lord.€€ƒxhThese are the generations of Noe: Noe was a just and perfect man in his generations, he walked with God.€€ƒx0And he begot three sons, Sem, Cham, and Japheth.€€ƒxEAnd the earth was corrupted before God, and was filled with iniquity.€€ƒxhAnd when God had seen that the earth was corrupted (for all flesh had corrupted its way upon the earth),€€ƒxHe said to Noe: The end of all flesh is come before me, the earth is filled with iniquity through them, and I will destroy them with the earth.€€ƒxwMake thee an ark of timber planks: thou shalt make little rooms in the ark, and thou shalt pitch it within and without.€€ƒx•And thus shalt thou make it. The length of the ark shall be three hundred cubits: the breadth of it fifty cubits, and the height of it thirty cubits.€€ƒxËThou shalt make a window in the ark, and in a cubit shalt thou finish the top of it: and the door of the ark thou shalt set in the side: with lower, middle chambers, and third stories shalt thou make it.€€ƒx¶Behold, I will bring the waters of a great flood upon the earth, to destroy all flesh, wherein is the breath of life under heaven. All things that are in the earth shall be consumed.€€ƒx”And I will establish my covenant with thee, and thou shalt enter into the ark, thou and thy sons, and thy wife, and the wives of thy sons with thee.€€ƒx–And of every living creature of all flesh, thou shalt bring two of a sort into the ark, that they may live with thee: of the male sex, and the female.€€ƒxÀOf fowls according to their kind, and of beasts in their kind, and of every thing that creepeth on the earth according to its kind: two of every sort shall go in with thee, that they may live.€€ƒx„Thou shalt take unto thee of all food that may be eaten, and thou shalt lay it up with thee: and it shall be food for thee and them.€€ƒx/And Noe did all things which God commanded him.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒxBOf all clean beasts take seven and seven, the male and the female.€€ƒxÈBut of the beasts that are unclean two and two, the male and the female. Of the fowls also of the air seven and seven, the male and the female: that seed may be saved upon the face of the whole earth.€€ƒx¯For yet a while, and after seven days, I will rain upon the earth forty days and forty nights: and I will destroy every substance that I have made, from the face of the earth.€€ƒx8And Noe did all things which the Lord had commanded him.€€ƒxTAnd he was six hundred years old, when the waters of the flood overflowed the earth.€€ƒx{And Noe went in and his sons, his wife and the wives of his sons with him into the ark, because of the waters of the flood.€€ƒx]And of beasts clean and unclean, and of fowls, and of every thing that moveth upon the earth,€€ƒxXTwo and two went in to Noe into the ark, male and female, as the Lord had commanded Noe.€€ƒxSAnd after the seven days were passed, the waters of the flood overflowed the earth.€€ƒxÅIn the six hundredth year of the life of Noe, in the second month, in the seventeenth day of the month, all the fountains of the great deep were broken up, and the floodgates of heaven were opened:€€ƒx=And the rain fell upon the earth forty days and forty nights.€€ƒxŠIn the selfsame day Noe, and Sem, and Cham, and Japheth, his sons: his wife, and the three wives of his sons with them, went into the ark.€€ƒxÓThey and every beast according to its kind, and all the cattle in their kind, and every thing that moveth upon the earth, according to its kind, and every fowl according to its kind, all birds, and all that fly,€€ƒxVWent in to Noe into the ark, two and two of all flesh, wherein was the breath of life.€€ƒxAnd they that went in, went in male and female of all flesh, as God had commanded him: and the Lord shut him in on the outside.€€ƒxtAnd the flood was forty days upon the earth: and the waters increased, and lifted up the ark on high from the earth.€€ƒxrFor they overflowed exceedingly: and filled all on the face of the earth: and the ark was carried upon the waters.€€ƒxwAnd the waters prevailed beyond measure upon the earth: and all the high mountains under the whole heaven were covered.€€ƒxHThe water was fifteen cubits higher than the mountains which it covered.€€ƒx¤And all flesh was destroyed that moved upon the earth, both of fowl and of cattle, and of beasts, and of all creeping things that creep upon the earth: and all men.€€ƒxFAnd all things wherein there is the breath of life on the earth, died.€€ƒxèAnd he destroyed all the substance that was upon the earth, from man even to beast, and the creeping things and fowls of the air: and they were destroyed from the earth: and Noe only remained, and they that were with him in the ark.€€ƒxAAnd the waters prevailed upon the earth a hundred and fifty days.€€ƒ`€€–ƒxtThe fountains also of the deep, and the floodgates of heaven, were shut up, and the rain from heaven was restrained.€€ƒxxAnd the waters returned from off the earth going and coming: and they began to be abated after a hundred and fifty days.€€ƒxqAnd the ark rested in the seventh month, the seven and twentieth day of the month, upon the mountains of Armenia.€€ƒx—And the waters were going and decreasing until the tenth month: for in the tenth month, the first day of the month, the tops of the mountains appeared.€€ƒxpAnd after that forty days were passed, Noe opening the window of the ark, which he had made, sent forth a raven:€€ƒxRWhich went forth and did not return, till the waters were dried up upon the earth.€€ƒxdHe sent forth also a dove after him, to see if the waters had now ceased upon the face of the earth.€€ƒx¿But she not finding where her foot might rest, returned to him into the ark: for the waters were upon the whole earth: and he put forth his hand, and caught her, and brought her into the ark.€€ƒxTAnd having waited yet seven other days, he again sent forth the dove out of the ark.€€ƒx«And she came to him in the evening carrying a bough of an olive tree, with green leaves, in her mouth. Noe therefore understood that the waters were ceased upon the earth.€€ƒxeAnd he stayed yet other seven days: and he sent forth the dove, which returned not any more unto him.€€ƒxãTherefore in the six hundredth and first year, the first month, the first day of the month, the waters were lessened upon the earth, and Noe opening the covering of the ark, looked, and saw that the face of the earth was dried.€€ƒxSIn the second month, the seven and twentieth day of the month, the earth was dried.€€ƒxAnd God spoke to Noe, saying:€€ƒxSGo out of the ark, thou and thy wife, thy sons and the wives of thy sons with thee.€€ƒxÒAll living things that are with thee of all flesh, as well in fowls as in beasts, and all creeping things that creep upon the earth, bring out with thee, and go ye upon the earth: increase and multiply upon it.€€ƒxOSo Noe went out, he and his sons: his wife, and the wives of his sons with him.€€ƒxAnd all living things, and cattle, and creeping things that creep upon the earth, according to their kinds went out of the ark.€€ƒx|And Noe built an altar unto the Lord: and taking of all cattle and fowls that were clean, offered holocausts upon the altar.€€ƒxöAnd the Lord smelled a sweet savour, and said: I will no more curse the earth for the sake of man: for the imagination and thought of man's heart are prone to evil from his youth: therefore I will no more destroy every living soul as I have done.€€ƒxrAll the days of the earth, seedtime and harvest, cold and heat, summer and winter, night and day, shall not cease.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒxÃAnd let the fear and dread of you be upon all the beasts of the earth, and upon all the fowls of the air, and all that move upon the earth: all the fishes of the sea are delivered into your hand.€€ƒxxAnd every thing that moveth, and liveth shall be meat for you: even as the green herbs have I delivered them all to you:€€ƒx/Saving that flesh with blood you shall not eat.€€ƒx¬For I will require the blood of your lives at the hand of every beast, and at the hand of man, at the hand of every man, and of his brother, will I require the life of man.€€ƒx`Whosoever shall shed man's blood, his blood shall be shed: for man was made to the image of God.€€ƒxABut increase you and multiply, and go upon the earth and fill it.€€ƒx4Thus also said God to Noe, and to his sons with him:€€ƒxKBehold I will establish my covenant with you, and with your seed after you:€€ƒx°And with every living soul that is with you, as well in all birds, as in cattle and beasts of the earth, that are come forth out of the ark, and in all the beasts of the earth.€€ƒx®I will establish my covenant with you, and all flesh shall be no more destroyed with the waters of a flood, neither shall there be from henceforth a flood to waste the earth.€€ƒx•And God said: This is the sign of the covenant which I give between me and you, and to every living soul that is with you, for perpetual generations.€€ƒxiI will set my bow in the clouds, and it shall be the sign of a covenant between me and between the earth.€€ƒxNAnd when I shall cover the sky with clouds, my bow shall appear in the clouds:€€ƒx›And I will remember my covenant with you, and with every living soul that beareth flesh: and there shall no more be waters of a flood to destroy all flesh.€€ƒxºAnd the bow shall be in the clouds, and I shall see it, and shall remember the everlasting covenant, that was made between God and every living soul of all flesh which is upon the earth.€€ƒxAnd God said to Noe: This shall be the sign of the covenant, which I have established, between me and all flesh upon the earth.€€ƒxmAnd the sons of Noe, who came out of the ark, were Sem, Cham, and Japheth: and Cham is the father of Chanaan.€€ƒx\These three are the sons of Noe: and from these was all mankind spread over the whole earth.€€ƒxFAnd Noe a husbandman began to till the ground, and planted a vineyard.€€ƒxGAnd drinking of the wine was made drunk, and was uncovered in his tent.€€ƒxŠWhich when Cham the father of Chanaan had seen, to wit, that his father's nakedness was uncovered, he told it to his two brethren without.€€ƒx½But Sem and Japheth put a cloak upon their shoulders, and going backward, covered the nakedness of their father: and their faces were turned away, and they saw not their father's nakedness.€€ƒxXAnd Noe awaking from the wine, when he had learned what his younger son had done to him,€€ƒxPHe said: Cursed be Chanaan, a servant of servants shall he be unto his brethren.€€ƒxDAnd he said: Blessed be the Lord God of Sem, be Chanaan his servant.€€ƒxZMay God enlarge Japheth, and may he dwell in the tents of Sem, and Chanaan be his servant.€€ƒx<And Noe lived after the flood three hundred and fifty years.€€ƒxMAnd all his days were in the whole nine hundred and fifty years: and he died.€€ƒ`€€˜ ƒx`The sons of Japheth: Gomer, and Magog, and Madai, and Javan, and Thubal, and Mosoch, and Thiras.€€ƒx8And the sons of Gomer: Ascenez and Riphath and Thogorma.€€ƒx>And the sons of Javan: Elisa and Tharsis, Cetthim and Dodanim.€€ƒxˆBy these were divided the islands of the Gentiles in their lands, every one according to his tongue and their families in their nations.€€ƒx?And the Sons of Cham: Chus, and Mesram, and Phuth, and Chanaan.€€ƒxrAnd the sons of Chus: Saba, and Hevila, and Sabatha, and Regma, and Sabatacha. The sons of Regma: Saba, and Dadan.€€ƒx:Now Chus begot Nemrod: he began to be mighty on the earth.€€ƒxqAnd he was a stout hunter before the Lord. Hence came a proverb: Even as Nemrod the stout hunter before the Lord.€€ƒxhAnd the beginning of his kingdom was Babylon, and Arach, and Achad, and Chalanne in the land of Sennaar.€€ƒx\Out of that land came forth Assur, and built Ninive, and the streets of the city, and Chale.€€ƒx<Resen also between Ninive and Chale: this is the great city.€€ƒx:And Mesraim begot Ludim, and Anamim and Laabim, Nephthuim.€€ƒxSAnd Phetrusim, and Chasluim; of whom came forth the Philistines, and the Capthorim.€€ƒx3And Chanaan begot Sidon his firstborn, the Hethite,€€ƒx7And the Jebusite, and the Amorrhite, and the Gergesite.€€ƒx#The Hevite and Aracite: the Sinite,€€ƒxtAnd the Aradian, the Samarite, and the Hamathite: and afterwards the families of the Chanaanites were spread abroad.€€ƒx—And the limits of Chanaan were from Sidon as one comes to Gerara even to Gaza, until thou enter Sodom and Gomorrha, and Adama, and Seboim even to Lesa.€€ƒxfThese are the children of Cham in their kindreds and tongues, and generations, and lands, and nations.€€ƒxbOf Sem also the father of all the children of Heber, the elder brother of Japheth, sons were born.€€ƒxAThe sons of Sem: Elam and Assur, and Arphaxad, and Lud, and Aram.€€ƒx4The sons of Aram: Us, and Hull, and Gether; and Mes.€€ƒx0But Arphaxad begot Sale, of whom was born Heber.€€ƒxŠAnd to Heber were born two sons: the name of the one was Phaleg, because in his days was the earth divided: and his brother's name Jectan.€€ƒx;Which Jectan begot Elmodad, and Saleph, and Asarmoth, Jare,€€ƒx And Aduram, and Uzal, and Decla,€€ƒxAnd Ebal, and Abimael, Saba,€€ƒxDAnd Ophir, and Hevila, and Jobab. All these were the sons of Jectan.€€ƒxWAnd their dwelling was from Messa as we go on as far as Sephar, a mountain in the east.€€ƒxfThese are the children of Sem according to their kindreds and tongues, and countries in their nations.€€ƒx…These are the families of Noe, according to their people and nations. By these were the nations divided on the earth after the flood.€€ƒ`€€˜ ƒx`And when they removed from the east, they found a plain in the land of Sennaar, and dwelt in it.€€ƒx—And each one said to his neighbour: Come let us make brick, and bake them with fire. And they had brick instead of stones, and slime instead of mortar:€€ƒx§And they said: Come, let us make a city and a tower, the top whereof may reach to heaven; and let us make our name famous before we be scattered abroad into all lands.€€ƒx_And the Lord came down to see the city and the tower, which the children of Adam were building.€€ƒx²And he said: Behold, it is one people, and all have one tongue: and they have begun to do this, neither will they leave off from their designs, till they accomplish them in deed.€€ƒxwCome ye, therefore, let us go down, and there confound their tongue, that they may not understand one another's speech.€€ƒxaAnd so the Lord scattered them from that place into all lands, and they ceased to build the city.€€ƒx½And therefore the name thereof was called Babel, because there the language of the whole earth was confounded: and from thence the Lord scattered them abroad upon the face of all countries.€€ƒxpThese are the generations of Sem: Sem was a hundred years old when he begot Arphaxad, two years after the flood.€€ƒxXAnd Sem lived after he begot Arphaxad, five hundred years, and begot sons and daughters.€€ƒx5And Arphaxad lived thirty-five years, and begot Sale.€€ƒxdAnd Arphaxad lived after he begot Sale, three hundred and three years, and begot sons and daughters.€€ƒx.Sale also lived thirty years, and begot Heber.€€ƒx`And Sale lived after he begot Heber, four hundred and three years: and begot sons and daughters.€€ƒx4And Heber lived thirty-four years, and begot Phaleg.€€ƒxcAnd Heber lived after he begot Phaleg, four hundred and thirty years: and begot sons and daughters.€€ƒx.Phaleg also lived thirty years, and begot Reu.€€ƒx^And Phaleg lived after he begot Reu, two hundred and nine years, and begot sons and daughters.€€ƒx0And Reu lived thirty-two years, and begot Sarug.€€ƒx^And Reu lived after he begot Sarug, two hundred and seven years, and begot sons and daughters.€€ƒx/And Sarug lived thirty years, and begot Nachor.€€ƒxWAnd Sarug lived after he begot Nachor, two hundred years, and begot sons and daughters.€€ƒx8And Nachor lived nine and twenty years, and begot Thare.€€ƒxbAnd Nachor lived after he begot Thare, a hundred and nineteen years, and begot sons and daughters.€€ƒxEAnd Thare lived seventy years, and begot Abram, and Nachor, and Aran.€€ƒx`And these are the generations of Thare: Thare begot Abram, Nachor, and Aran. And Aran begot Lot.€€ƒxYAnd Aran died before Thare his father, in the land of his nativity in Ur of the Chaldees.€€ƒx«And Abram and Nachor married wives: the name of Abram's wife was Sarai: and the name of Nachor's wife, Melcha, the daughter of Aran, father of Melcha and father of Jescha.€€ƒx*And Sarai was barren, and had no children.€€ƒxûAnd Thare took Abram his son, and Lot the son of Aran, his son's son, and Sarai his daughter in law, the wife of Abram his son, and brought them out of Ur of the Chaldees, to go into the land of Chanaan: and they came as far as Haran, and dwelt there.€€ƒxLAnd the days of Thare were two hundred and five years, and he died in Haran.€€ƒ`€€”ƒxoAnd I will make of thee a great nation, and I will bless thee, and magnify thy name, and thou shalt be blessed.€€ƒx~I will bless them that bless thee, and curse them that curse thee, and IN THEE shall all the kindreds of the earth be blessed.€€ƒx‡So Abram went out as the Lord had commanded him, and Lot went with him: Abram was seventy-five years old when he went forth from Haran.€€ƒxæAnd he took Sarai his wife, and Lot his brother's son, and all the substance which they had gathered, and the souls which they had gotten in Haran: and they went out to go into the land of Chanaan. And when they were come into it,€€ƒx…Abram passed through the country unto the place of Sichem, as far as the noble vale: now the Chanaanite was at that time in the land.€€ƒx•And the Lord appeared to Abram, and said to him: To thy seed will I give this land. And he built there an altar to the Lord, who had appeared to him.€€ƒxİAnd passing on from thence to a mountain, that was on the east side of Bethel, he there pitched his tent, having Bethel on the west, and Hai on the east: he built there also an altar to the Lord, and called upon his name.€€ƒx=And Abram went forward, going and proceeding on to the south.€€ƒx‡And there came a famine in the country: and Abram went down into Egypt, to sojourn there: for the famine was very grievous in the land.€€ƒxlAnd when he was near to enter into Egypt, he said to Sarai his wife: I know that thou art a beautiful woman:€€ƒxqAnd that when the Egyptians shall see thee, they will say: She is his wife: and they will kill me, and keep thee.€€ƒxSay, therefore, I pray thee, that thou art my sister: that I may be well used for thee, and that my soul may live for thy sake.€€ƒx\And when Abram was come into Egypt, the Egyptians saw the woman that she was very beautiful.€€ƒxjAnd the princes told Pharao, and praised her before him: and the woman was taken into the house of Pharao.€€ƒxAnd they used Abram well for her sake. And he had sheep and oxen and he asses, and men servants, and maid servants, and she asses, and camels.€€ƒx^But the Lord scourged Pharao and his house with most grievous stripes for Sarai, Abram's wife.€€ƒxƒAnd Pharao called Abram, and said to him: What is this that thou hast done to me? Why didst thou not tell me that she was thy wife?€€ƒxFor what cause didst thou say, she was thy sister, that I might take her to my wife? Now therefore there is thy wife, take her, and go thy way.€€ƒxiAnd Pharao gave his men orders concerning Abram: and they led him away and his wife, and all that he had.€€ƒ`€€’ƒx6And he was very rich in possession of gold and silver.€€ƒxAnd he returned by the way, that he came, from the south to Bethel, to the place where before he had pitched his tent between Bethel and Hai,€€ƒxbIn the place of the altar which he had made before, and there he called upon the name of the Lord.€€ƒxVBut Lot also, who was with Abram, had flocks of sheep, and herds of beasts, and tents.€€ƒx‰Neither was the land able to bear them, that they might dwell together: for their substance was great, and they could not dwell together.€€ƒx˜Whereupon also there arose a strife between the herdsmen of Abram and of Lot. And at that time the Chanaanite and the Pherezite dwelled in that country.€€ƒx™Abram therefore said to Lot: Let there be no quarrel, I beseech thee, between me and thee, and between my herdsmen and thy herdsmen: for we are brethren.€€ƒx³Behold the whole land is before thee: depart from me, I pray thee: if thou wilt go to the left hand, I will take the right: if thou choose the right hand, I will pass to the left.€€ƒxÑAnd Lot lifting up his eyes, saw all the country about the Jordan, which was watered throughout, before the Lord destroyed Sodom and Gomorrha, as the paradise of the Lord, and like Egypt as one comes to Segor.€€ƒx‰And Lot chose to himself the country about the Jordan, and he departed from the east: and they were separated one brother from the other.€€ƒxoAbram dwelt in the land of Chanaan: and Lot abode in the towns, that were about the Jordan, and dwelt in Sodom.€€ƒx^And the men of Sodom were very wicked, and sinners before the face of the Lord beyond measure.€€ƒx¹And the Lord said to Abram, after Lot was separated from him: Lift up thy eyes, and look from the place wherein thou now art, to the north and to the south, to the east and to the west.€€ƒxMAll the land which thou seest, I will give to thee, and to thy seed for ever.€€ƒxAnd I will make thy seed as the dust of the earth: if any man be able to number the dust of the earth, he shall be able to number thy seed also.€€ƒxcArise and walk through the land in the length, and the breadth thereof: for I will give it to thee.€€ƒxSo Abram removing his tent, came, and dwelt by the vale of Mambre, which is in Hebron: and he built there an altar to the Lord.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒxÁMade war against Bara, king of Sodom, and against Bersa, king of Gomorrha, and against Sennaab, king of Adama, and against Semeber, king of Seboim, and against the king of Bala, which is Segor.€€ƒxJAll these came together into the woodland vale, which now is the salt sea.€€ƒxbFor they had served Chodorlahomor twelve years, and in the thirteenth year they revolted from him.€€ƒx¼And in the fourteenth year came Chodorlahomor, and the kings that were with him: and they smote the Raphaim in Astarothcarnaim, and the Zuzim with them, and the Emim in Save of Cariathaim.€€ƒxeAnd the Chorreans in the mountains of Seir, even to the plains of Pharan, which is in the wilderness.€€ƒxªAnd they returned, and came to the fountain of Misphat, the same is Cades: and they smote all the country of the Amalecites, and the Amorrhean that dwelt in Asasonthamar.€€ƒxÛAnd the king of Sodom, and the king of Gomorrha, and the king of Adama, and the king of Seboim, and the king of Bala, which is Segor, went out: and they set themselves against them in battle array, in the woodland vale:€€ƒx¡To wit, against Chodorlahomor king of the Elamites, and Thadal king of nations, and Amraphel king of Sennaar, and Arioch king of Pontus: four kings against five.€€ƒxºNow the woodland vale had many pits of slime. And the king of Sodom, and the king of Gomorrha turned their backs, and were overthrown there: and they that remained, fled to the mountain.€€ƒxnAnd they took all the substance of the Sodomites, and Gomorrhites, and all their victuals, and went their way:€€ƒxPAnd Lot also, the son of Abram's brother, who dwelt in Sodom, and his substance.€€ƒx¾And behold one, that had escaped, told Abram the Hebrew, who dwelt in the vale of Mambre the Amorrhite, the brother of Escol, and the brother of Aner: for these had made a league with Abram.€€ƒx·Which when Abram had heard, to wit, that his brother Lot was taken, he numbered of the servants born in his house, three hundred and eighteen, well appointed: and pursued them to Dan.€€ƒx–And dividing his company, he rushed upon them in the night, and defeated them: and pursued them as far as Hoba, which is on the left hand of Damascus.€€ƒxoAnd he brought back all the substance, and Lot his brother, with his substance, the women also, and the people.€€ƒxµAnd the king of Sodom went out to meet him, after he returned from the slaughter of Chodorlahomor, and of the kings that were with him in the vale of Save, which is the king's vale.€€ƒxoBut Melchisedech, the king of Salem, bringing forth bread and wine, for he was the priest of the most high God,€€ƒx[Blessed him, and said: Blessed be Abram by the most high God, who created heaven and earth.€€ƒxwAnd blessed be the most high God, by whose protection, the enemies are in thy hands. And he gave him the tithes of all.€€ƒxWAnd the king of Sodom said to Abram: Give me the persons, and the rest take to thyself.€€ƒxhAnd he answered him: I lift up my hand to the Lord God the most high, the possessor of heaven and earth,€€ƒx‰That from the very woof thread unto the shoe latchet, I will not take of any things that are thine, lest thou say: I have enriched Abram.€€ƒx”Except such things as the young men have eaten, and the shares of the men that came with me, Aner, Escol, and Mambre: these shall take their shares.€€ƒ`€€•ƒxAnd Abram said: Lord God, what wilt thou give me? I shall go without children: and the son of the steward of my house is this Damascus Eliezer.€€ƒxjAnd Abram added: But to me thou hast not given seed: and lo my servant born in my house, shall be my heir.€€ƒx And immediately the word of the Lord came to him, saying : He shall not be thy heir: but he that shall come out of thy bowels, him shalt thou have for thy heir.€€ƒx‘And he brought him forth abroad, and said to him: Look up to heaven and number the stars if thou canst. And he said to him: So shall thy seed be.€€ƒx;Abram believed God, and it was reputed to him unto justice.€€ƒxŠAnd he said to him: I am the Lord who brought thee out from Ur of the Chaldees, to give thee this land, and that thou mightest possess it.€€ƒxBBut he said: Lord God, whereby may I know that I shall possess it?€€ƒx˜And the Lord answered, and said: Take me a cow of three years old, and a she-goat of three years. and a ram of three years, a turtle also, and a pigeon.€€ƒxŠAnd he took all these, and divided them in the midst, and laid the two pieces of each one against the other: but the birds he divided not.€€ƒxFAnd the fowls came down upon the carcasses, and Abram drove them away.€€ƒxlAnd when the sun was setting, a deep sleep fell upon Abram, and a great and darksome horror seized upon him.€€ƒx·And it was said unto him: Know thou beforehand that thy seed shall be a stranger in a land not their own, and they shall bring them under bondage, and afflict them four hundred years.€€ƒxlBut I will judge the nation which they shall serve, and after this they shall come out with great substance.€€ƒxKAnd thou shalt go to thy fathers in peace, and be buried in a good old age.€€ƒxBut in the fourth generation they shall return hither: for as yet the iniquities of the Amorrhites are not at the full until this present time.€€ƒxŒAnd when the sun was set, there arose a dark mist, and there appeared a smoking furnace, and a lamp of fire passing between those divisions.€€ƒxThat day God made a covenant with Abram, saying: To thy seed will I give this land, from the river to Egypt even to the great river Euphrates.€€ƒx+The Cineans, and Cenezites, the Cedmonites,€€ƒx7And the Hethites, and the Pherezites, the Raphaim also,€€ƒxOAnd the Amorrhites, and the Chanaanites, and the Gergesites, and the Jebusites.€€ƒ`€€ƒx´She said to her husband: Behold, the Lord hath restrained me from bearing: go in unto my handmaid, it may be I may have children of her at least. And when he agreed to her request,€€ƒx†She took Agar the Egyptian her handmaid, ten years after they first dwelt in the land of Chanaan, and gave her to her husband to wife.€€ƒxYAnd he went in to her. But she perceiving that she was with child, despised her mistress.€€ƒx¶And Sarai said to Abram: Thou dost unjustly with me: I gave my handmaid into thy bosom, and she perceiving herself to be with child, despiseth me. The Lord judge between me and thee.€€ƒx˜And Abram made answer, and said to her: Behold thy handmaid is in thy own hand, use her as it pleaseth thee. And when Sarai afflicted her, she ran away.€€ƒxAnd the angel of the Lord having found her, by a fountain of water in the wilderness, which is in the way to Sur in the desert,€€ƒx’He said to her: Agar, handmaid of Sarai, whence comest thou? and whither goest thou? And she answered: I flee from the face of Sarai, my mistress.€€ƒxaAnd the angel of the Lord said to her: Return to thy mistress, and humble thyself under her hand.€€ƒxdAnd again he said: I will multiply thy seed exceedingly, and it shall not be numbered for multitude.€€ƒx£And again: Behold, said he, thou art with child, and thou shalt bring forth a son: and thou shalt call his name Ismael, because the Lord hath heard thy affliction.€€ƒx–He shall be a wild man: his hand will be against all men, and all men's hands against him: and he shall pitch his tents over against all his brethren.€€ƒx¥And she called the name of the Lord that spoke unto her: Thou the God who hast seen me. For she said: Verily, here have I seen the hinder parts of him that seeth me.€€ƒxnTherefore she called that well, the well of him that liveth and seeth me. The same is between Cades and Barad.€€ƒxBAnd Agar brought forth a son to Abram: who called his name Ismael.€€ƒxJAbram was four score and six years old when Agar brought him forth Ismael.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒxVAnd I will make my covenant between me and thee: and I will multiply thee exceedingly.€€ƒxAbram fell flat on his face.€€ƒxdAnd God said to him: I am, and my covenant is with thee, and thou shalt be a father of many nations.€€ƒx…Neither shall thy name be called any more Abram: but thou shalt be called Abraham: because I have made thee a father of many nations.€€ƒxmAnd I will make thee increase exceedingly, and I will make nations of thee, and kings shall come out of thee.€€ƒxµAnd I will establish my covenant between me and thee, and between thy seed after thee in their generations, by a perpetual covenant: to be a God to thee, and to thy seed after thee.€€ƒx”And I will give to thee, and to thy seed, the land of thy sojournment, all the land of Chanaan, for a perpetual possession, and I will be their God.€€ƒxsAgain God said to Abraham: And thou therefore shalt keep my covenant, and thy seed after thee in their generations.€€ƒx‡This is my covenant which you shall observe between me and you, and thy seed after thee: All the male-kind of you shall be circumcised.€€ƒxrAnd you shall circumcise the flesh of your foreskin, that it may be for a sign of the covenant between me and you.€€ƒxÖAn infant of eight days old shall be circumcised among you, every manchild in your generations: he that is born in the house, as well as the bought servant, shall be circumcised, and whosoever is not of your stock:€€ƒx@And my covenant shall be in your flesh for a perpetual covenant.€€ƒx’The male whose flesh of his foreskin shall not be circumcised, that soul shall be destroyed out of his people: because he hath broken my covenant.€€ƒxMGod said also to Abraham: Sarai thy wife thou shalt not call Sarai, but Sara.€€ƒx“And I will bless her, and of her I will give thee a son, whom I will bless, and he shall become nations, and kings of people shall spring from him.€€ƒxºAbraham fell upon his face, and laughed, saying in his heart: Shall a son, thinkest thou, be born to him that is a hundred years old? and shall Sara that is ninety years old bring forth?€€ƒx7And he said to God: O that Ismael may live before thee.€€ƒxÂAnd God said to Abraham: Sara thy wife shall bear thee a son, and thou shalt call his name Isaac, and I will establish my covenant with him for a perpetual covenant, and with his seed after him.€€ƒx±And as for Ismael I have also heard thee. Behold, I will bless him, and increase, and multiply him exceedingly: he shall beget twelve chiefs, and I will make him a great nation.€€ƒxoBut my covenant I will establish with Isaac, whom Sara shall bring forth to thee at this time in the next year.€€ƒxEAnd when he had left off speaking with him, God went up from Abraham.€€ƒxîAnd Abraham took Ismael his son, and all that were born in his house: and all whom he had bought, every male among the men of his house: and he circumcised the flesh of their foreskin forthwith the very same day, as God had commanded him.€€ƒxUAbraham was ninety and nine years old, when he circumcised the flesh of his foreskin.€€ƒxOAnd Ismael his son was full thirteen years old at the time of his circumcision.€€ƒx=The self-same day was Abraham circumcised and Ismael his son.€€ƒxˆAnd all the men of his house, as well they that were born in his house, as the bought servants and strangers, were circumcised with him.€€ƒ`€€˜!ƒxÃAnd when he had lifted up his eyes, there appeared to him three men standing near to him: and as soon as he saw them, he ran to meet them from the door of his tent, and adored down to the ground.€€ƒxWAnd he said: Lord, if I have found favour in thy sight, pass not away from thy servant.€€ƒxSBut I will fetch a little water, and wash ye your feet, and rest ye under the tree.€€ƒx¶And I will set a morsel of bread, and strengthen ye your heart, afterwards you shall pass on: for therefore are you come aside to your servant. And they said: Do as thou hast spoken.€€ƒxAbraham made haste into the tent to Sara, and said to her: Make haste, temper together three measures of flour, and make cakes upon the hearth.€€ƒx‘And he himself ran to the herd, and took from thence a calf, very tender and very good, and gave it to a young man, who made haste and boiled it.€€ƒxyHe took also butter and milk, and the calf which he had boiled, and set before them: but he stood by them under the tree.€€ƒxfAnd when they had eaten, they said to him: Where is Sara thy wife? He answered: Lo she is in the tent.€€ƒx¹And he said to him: I will return and come to thee at this time, life accompanying, and Sara, thy wife, shall have a son. Which when Sara heard, she laughed behind the door of the tent.€€ƒxoNow they were both old, and far advanced in years, and it had ceased to be with Sara after the manner of women.€€ƒxsAnd she laughed secretly, saying: After I am grown old, and my lord is an old man, shall I give myself to pleasure?€€ƒxlAnd the Lord said to Abraham: Why did Sara laugh, saying: Shall I, who am an old woman, bear a child indeed?€€ƒxIs there any thing hard to God? According to appointment I will return to thee at this same time, life accompanying, and Sara shall have a son.€€ƒxgSara denied, saying: I did not laugh: for she was afraid. But the Lord said: Nay; but thou didst laugh.€€ƒxƒAnd when the men rose up from thence, they turned their eyes towards Sodom: and Abraham walked with them, bringing them on the way.€€ƒxAAnd the Lord said: Can I hide from Abraham what I am about to do:€€ƒxkSeeing he shall become a great and mighty nation, and in him all the nations of the earth shall be blessed?€€ƒxâFor I know that he will command his children, and his household after him, to keep the way of the Lord, and do judgment and justice: that for Abraham's sake, the Lord may bring to effect all the things he hath spoken unto him.€€ƒxmAnd the Lord said: The cry of Sodom and Gomorrha is multiplied, and their sin is become exceedingly grievous.€€ƒx€I will go down and see whether they have done according to the cry that is come to me; or whether it be not so, that I may know.€€ƒxnAnd they turned themselves from thence, and went their way to Sodom: but Abraham as yet stood before the Lord.€€ƒxFAnd drawing nigh, he said: Wilt thou destroy the just with the wicked?€€ƒx˜If there be fifty just men in the city, shall they perish withal? and wilt thou not spare that place for the sake of the fifty just, if they be therein?€€ƒx×Far be it from thee to do this thing, and to slay the just with the wicked, and for the just to be in like case as the wicked; this is not beseeming thee: thou who judgest all the earth, wilt not make this judgment.€€ƒxuAnd the Lord said to him: If I find in Sodom fifty just within the city, I will spare the whole place for their sake.€€ƒxoAnd Abraham answered, and said: Seeing I have once begun, I will speak to my Lord, whereas I am dust and ashes.€€ƒx¦What if there be five less than fifty just persons? wilt thou for five and forty destroy the whole city: And he said: I will not destroy it, if I find five and forty.€€ƒxAnd again he said to him: But if forty be found there, what wilt thou do? He said: I will not destroy it for the sake of forty.€€ƒx•Lord, saith he, be not angry, I beseech thee, if I speak: What if thirty shall be found there? He answered: I will not do it, if I find thirty there.€€ƒx“Seeing, saith he, I have once begun, I will speak to my Lord: What if twenty be found there? He said: I will not destroy it for the sake of twenty.€€ƒx¡I beseech thee, saith he, be not angry, Lord, if I speak yet once more: What if ten shall be found there? And he said: I will not destroy it for the sake of ten.€€ƒx`And the Lord departed, after he had left speaking to Abraham: and Abraham returned to his place.€€ƒ`€€˜&ƒxÊAnd said: I beseech you, my lords, turn in to the house of your servant, and lodge there: wash your feet, and in the morning you shall go on your way. And they said: No, but we will abide in the street.€€ƒx–He pressed them very much to turn in unto him: and when they were come into his house, he made them a feast, and baked unleavened bread, and they ate:€€ƒxnBut before they went to bed, the men of the city beset the house, both young and old, all the people together.€€ƒx„And they called Lot, and said to him: Where are the men that came in to thee at night? bring them out hither, that we may know them:€€ƒx<Lot went out to them, and shut the door after him, and said:€€ƒx?Do not so, I beseech you, my brethren, do not commit this evil.€€ƒxÙI have two daughters who, as yet, have not known man; I will bring them out to you, and abuse you them as it shall please you, so that you do no evil to these men, because they are come in under the shadow of my roof.€€ƒyBut they said: Get thee back thither. And again: Thou camest in, said they, as a stranger, was it to be a judge? therefore we will afflict thee more than them. And they pressed very violently upon Lot: and they were even at the point of breaking open the doors.€€ƒxTAnd behold the men put out their hand, and drew in Lot unto them, and shut the door.€€ƒx}And them, that were without, they struck with blindness from the least to the greatest, so that they could not find the door.€€ƒx…And they said to Lot: Hast thou here any of thine? son in law, or sons, or daughters, all that are thine bring them out of this city:€€ƒxrFor we will destroy this place, because their cry is grown loud before the Lord, who hath sent us to destroy them.€€ƒxÖSo Lot went out, and spoke to his sons in law that were to have his daughters, and said: Arise: get you out of this place, because the Lord will destroy this city. And he seemed to them to speak as it were in jest.€€ƒx©And when it was morning, the angels pressed him, saying: Arise, take thy wife, and the two daughters that thou hast: lest thou also perish in the wickedness of the city.€€ƒxxAnd as he lingered, they took his hand, and the hand of his wife, and of his two daughters, because the Lord spared him.€€ƒxçAnd they brought him forth, and set him without the city: and there they spoke to him, saying: Save thy life: look not back, neither stay thou in all the country about: but save thy self in the mountain, lest thou be also consumed.€€ƒx.And Lot said to them: I beseech thee, my Lord,€€ƒxÍBecause thy servant hath found grace before thee, and thou hast magnified thy mercy, which thou hast shewn to me, in saving my life, and I cannot escape to the mountain, lest some evil seize me, and I die.€€ƒx•There is this city here at hand, to which I may flee, it is a little one, and I shall be saved in it: is it not a little one, and my soul shall live?€€ƒxvAnd he said to him: Behold also in this, I have heard thy prayers, not to destroy the city for which thou hast spoken.€€ƒxˆMake haste, and be saved there: because I cannot do any thing till thou go in thither. Therefore the name of that city was called Segor.€€ƒx=The sun was risen upon the earth, and Lot entered into Segor.€€ƒx[And the Lord rained upon Sodom and Gomorrha brimstone and fire from the Lord out of heaven.€€ƒx‡And he destroyed these cities, and all the country about, all the inhabitants of the cities, and all things that spring from the earth.€€ƒxBAnd his wife looking behind her, was turned into a statue of salt.€€ƒxbAnd Abraham got up early in the morning, and in the place where he had stood before with the Lord:€€ƒxHe looked towards Sodom and Gomorrha, and the whole land of that country: and he saw the ashes rise up from the earth as the smoke of a furnace.€€ƒx“Now when God destroyed the cities of that country, remembering Abraham, he delivered Lot out of the destruction of the cities wherein he had dwelt.€€ƒx·And Lot went up out of Segor, and abode in the mountain, and his two daughters with him (for he was afraid to stay in Segor) and he dwelt in a cave, he and his two daughters with him.€€ƒx”And the elder said to the younger: Our father is old, and there is no man left on the earth, to come in unto us after the manner of the whole earth.€€ƒxhCome, let us make him drunk with wine, and let us lie with him, that we may preserve seed of our father.€€ƒx±And they made their father drink wine that night: and the elder went in, and lay with her father: but he perceived not, neither when his daughter lay down, nor when she rose up.€€ƒxÃAnd the next day the elder said to the younger: Behold I lay last night with my father, let us make him drink wine also to night, and thou shalt lie with him, that we may save seed of our father.€€ƒx°They made their father drink wine that night also, and the younger daughter went in, and lay with him: and neither then did he perceive when she lay down, nor when she rose up.€€ƒx<So the two daughters of Lot were with child by their father.€€ƒxgAnd the elder bore a son, and she called his name Moab: he is the father of the Moabites unto this day.€€ƒx‹The younger also bore a son, and she called his name Ammon; that is, the son of my people: he is the father of the Ammonites unto this day.€€ƒ`€€’ƒxcAnd he said of Sara his wife: She is my sister. So Abimelech the king of Gerara sent, and took her.€€ƒxAnd God came to Abimelech in a dream by night, and he said to him: Lo thou shalt die for the woman that thou hast taken: for she hath a husband.€€ƒxhNow Abimelech had not touched her, and he said: Lord, wilt thou slay a nation that is ignorant and just?€€ƒx‘Did not he say to me: She is my sister: and she say, He is my brother? in the simplicity of my heart, and cleanness of my hands have I done this.€€ƒx¥And God said to him: And I know that thou didst it with a sincere heart: and therefore I withheld thee from sinning against me, and I suffered thee not to touch her.€€ƒxÍNow therefore restore the man his wife, for he is a prophet: and he shall pray for thee, and thou shalt live: but if thou wilt not restore her, know that thou shalt surely die, thou and all that are thine.€€ƒxAnd Abimelech forthwith rising up in the night, called all his servants: and spoke all these words in their hearing, and all the men were exceedingly afraid.€€ƒxäAnd Abimelech called also for Abraham, and said to him: What hast thou done to us? what have we offended thee in, that thou hast brought upon me and upon my kingdom a great sin? thou hast done to us what thou oughtest not to do.€€ƒxYAnd again he expostulated with him, and said: What sawest thou, that thou hast done this?€€ƒx“Abraham answered: I thought with myself, saying: Perhaps there is not the fear of God in this place: and they will kill me for the sake of my wife:€€ƒx…Howbeit, otherwise also she is truly my sister, the daughter of my father, and not the daughter of my mother, and I took her to wife.€€ƒx¯And after God brought me out of my father's house, I said to her: Thou shalt do me this kindness: In every place, to which we shall come, thou shalt say that I am thy brother.€€ƒxvAnd Abimelech took sheep and oxen, and servants and handmaids, and gave to Abraham: and restored to him Sara his wife,€€ƒxIAnd said: The land is before you, dwell wheresoever it shall please thee.€€ƒxÜAnd to Sara he said: Behold I have given thy brother a thousand pieces of silver, this shall serve thee for a covering of thy eyes to all that are with thee, and whithersoever thou shalt go: and remember thou wast taken.€€ƒxfAnd when Abraham prayed, God healed Abimelech and his wife, and his handmaids, and they bore children:€€ƒxdFor the Lord had closed up every womb of the house of Abimelech, on account of Sara, Abraham's wife.€€ƒ`€€˜"ƒxWAnd she conceived and bore a son in her old age, at the time that God had foretold her.€€ƒxBAnd Abraham called the name of his son, whom Sara bore him, Isaac.€€ƒx@And he circumcised him the eighth day, as God had commanded him,€€ƒxOWhen he was a hundred years old: for at this age of his father, was Isaac born.€€ƒx^And Sara said: God hath made a laughter for me: whosoever shall hear of it will laugh with me.€€ƒxAnd again she said: Who would believe that Abraham should hear that Sara gave suck to a son, whom she bore to him in his old age?€€ƒx]And the child grew, and was weaned: and Abraham made a great feast on the day of his weaning.€€ƒxgAnd when Sara had seen the son of Agar, the Egyptian, playing with Isaac, her son, she said to Abraham:€€ƒxfCast out this bondwoman and her son; for the son of the bondwoman shall not be heir with my son Isaac.€€ƒx)Abraham took this grievously for his son.€€ƒxºAnd God said to him: Let it not seem grievous to thee for the boy, and for thy bondwoman: in all that Sara hath said to thee, hearken to her voice: for in Isaac shall thy seed be called.€€ƒxUBut I will make the son also of the bondwoman a great nation, because he is thy seed.€€ƒxÌSo Abraham rose up in the morning, and taking bread and a bottle of water, put it upon her shoulder, and delivered the boy, and sent her away. And she departed, and wandered in the wilderness of Bersabee.€€ƒxdAnd when the water in the bottle was spent, she cast the boy under one of the trees that were there.€€ƒxÀAnd she went her way, and sat over against him a great way off, as far as a bow can carry, for she said: I will not see the boy die: and sitting over against, she lifted up her voice and wept.€€ƒxÇAnd God heard the voice of the boy: and an angel of God called to Agar from heaven, saying: What art thou doing, Agar? fear not; for God hath heard the voice of the boy, from the place wherein he is.€€ƒxUArise, take up the boy, and hold him by the hand, for I will make him a great nation.€€ƒxpAnd God opened her eyes: and she saw a well of water, and went and filled the bottle, and gave the boy to drink.€€ƒxbAnd God was with him: and he grew, and dwelt in the wilderness, and became a young man, an archer.€€ƒxfAnd he dwelt in the wilderness of Pharan, and his mother took a wife for him out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒxxAt the same time Abimelech, and Phicol the general of his army, said to Abraham: God is with thee in all that thou dost.€€ƒxÔSwear therefore by God, that thou wilt not hurt me, nor my posterity, nor my stock: but according to the kindness that I have done to thee, thou shalt do to me, and to the land wherein thou hast lived a stranger.€€ƒxAnd Abraham said: I will swear.€€ƒxZAnd he reproved Abimelech for a well of water, which his servants had taken away by force.€€ƒxtAnd Abimelech answered: I knew not who did this thing: and thou didst not tell me, and I heard not of it till today.€€ƒx]Then Abraham took sheep and oxen, and gave them to Abimelech: and both of them made a league.€€ƒx2And Abraham set apart seven ewelambs of the flock.€€ƒxTAnd Abimelech said to him: What mean these seven ewelambs which thou hast set apart?€€ƒxrBut he said: Thou shalt take seven ewelambs at my hand: that they may be a testimony for me, that I dug this well.€€ƒxOTherefore that place was called Bersabee; because there both of them did swear.€€ƒx,And they made a league for the well of oath.€€ƒxÅAnd Abimelech and Phicol, the general of his army, arose and returned to the land of the Palestines. But Abraham planted a grove in Bersabee, and there called upon the name of the Lord God eternal.€€ƒx?And he was a sojourner in the land of the Palestines many days.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒxÅHe said to him: Take thy only begotten son Isaac, whom thou lovest, and go into the land of vision; and there thou shalt offer him for an holocaust upon one of the mountains which I will shew thee.€€ƒxÍSo Abraham rising up in the night, saddled his ass, and took with him two young men, and Isaac his son: and when he had cut wood for the holocaust, he went his way to the place which God had commanded him.€€ƒxEAnd on the third day, lifting up his eyes, he saw the place afar off.€€ƒxAnd he said to his young men: Stay you here with the ass; I and the boy will go with speed as far as yonder, and after we have worshipped, will return to you.€€ƒxŸAnd he took the wood for the holocaust, and laid it upon Isaac his son; and he himself carried in his hands fire and a sword. And as they two went on together,€€ƒx’Isaac said to his father: My father. And he answered: What wilt thou, son? Behold, saith he, fire and wood: where is the victim for the holocaust?€€ƒxgAnd Abraham said: God will provide himself a victim for an holocaust, my son. So they went on together.€€ƒxÅAnd they came to the place which God had shewn him, where he built an altar, and laid the wood in order upon it; and when he had bound Isaac his son, he laid him on the altar upon the pile of wood.€€ƒxDAnd he put forth his hand, and took the sword, to sacrifice his son.€€ƒxqAnd behold, an angel of the Lord from heaven called to him, saying: Abraham, Abraham. And he answered: Here I am.€€ƒx­And he said to him: Lay not thy hand upon the boy, neither do thou any thing to him: now I know that thou fearest God, and hast not spared thy only begotten son for my sake.€€ƒx¨Abraham lifted up his eyes, and saw behind his back a ram, amongst the briers, sticking fast by the horns, which he took and offered for a holocaust instead of his son.€€ƒxAnd he called the name of that place, The Lord seeth. Whereupon, even to this day, it is said: In the mountain the Lord will see.€€ƒxNAnd the angel of the Lord called to Abraham a second time from heaven, saying:€€ƒx†By my own self have I sworn, saith the Lord: because thou hast done this thing, and hast not spared thy only begotten son for my sake:€€ƒx¤I will bless thee, and I will multiply thy seed as the stars of heaven, and as the sand that is by the sea shore; thy seed shall possess the gates of their enemies.€€ƒxaAnd in thy seed shall all the nations of the earth be blessed, because thou hast obeyed my voice.€€ƒxZAbraham returned to his young men, and they went to Bersabee together, and he dwelt there.€€ƒxcAfter these things, it was told Abraham, that Melcha also had borne children to Nachor his brother.€€ƒxOHus, the firstborn, and Buz, his brother, and Camuel the father of the Syrians,€€ƒx.And Cased, and Azau, and Pheldas, and Jedlaph,€€ƒx`And Bathuel, of whom was born Rebecca: these eight did Melcha bear to Nachor, Abraham's brother.€€ƒxLAnd his concubine, named Roma, bore Tabee, and Gaham, and Tahas, and Maacha.€€ƒ`€€”ƒxvAnd she died in the city of Arbee which is Hebron, in the land of Chanaan: and Abraham came to mourn and weep for her.€€ƒxZAnd after he rose up from the funeral obsequies, he spoke to the children of Heth, saying:€€ƒxpI am a stranger and sojourner among you: give me the right of a burying place with you, that I may bury my dead.€€ƒx&The children of Heth answered, saying:€€ƒx²My lord, hear us, thou art a prince of God among us: bury thy dead in our principal sepulchres: and no man shall have power to hinder thee from burying thy dead in his sepulchre.€€ƒxXAbraham rose up, and bowed down to the people of the land, to wit, the children of Heth:€€ƒx}And said to them: If it please your soul that I should bury my dead, hear me, and intercede for me to Ephron the son of Seor.€€ƒx±That he may give me the double cave, which he hath in the end of his field: For as much money as it is worth he shall give it me before you, for a possession of a burying place.€€ƒxœNow Ephron dwelt in the midst of the children of Heth. And Ephron made answer to Abraham in the hearing of all that went in at the gate of the city, saying:€€ƒx¼Let it not be so, my lord, but do thou rather hearken to what I say: The field I deliver to thee, and the cave that is therein; in the presence of the children of my people, bury thy dead.€€ƒx1Abraham bowed down before the people of the land.€€ƒxAnd he spoke to Ephron, in the presence of the people: I beseech thee to hear me: I will give money for the field; take it, and so will I bury my dead in it.€€ƒtAnd Ephron answered:€€ƒx¡My lord, hear me. The ground which thou desirest, is worth four hundred sicles of silver: this is the price between me and thee: but what is this? bury thy dead.€€ƒx°And when Abraham had heard this, he weighed out the money that Ephron had asked, in the hearing of the children of Heth, four hundred sicles of silver, of common current money.€€ƒx¬And the field that before was Ephron's, wherein was the double cave, looking towards Mambre, both it and the cave, and all the trees thereof, in all its limits round about,€€ƒxWas made sure to Abraham for a possession, in the sight of the children of Heth, and of all that went in at the gate of his city.€€ƒx‰And so Abraham buried Sara, his wife, in the double cave of the field, that looked towards Mambre, this is Hebron in the land of Chanaan.€€ƒxzAnd the field was made sure to Abraham, and the cave that was in it, for a possession to bury in, by the children of Heth.€€ƒ`€€˜CƒxjAnd he said to the elder servant of his house, who was ruler over all he had: Put thy hand under my thigh,€€ƒx£That I may make thee swear by the Lord, the God of heaven and earth, that thou take not a wife for my son, of the daughters of the Chanaanites, among whom I dwell:€€ƒx]But that thou go to my own country and kindred, and take a wife from thence for my son Isaac.€€ƒx’The servant answered: If the woman will not come with me into this land, must I bring thy son back again to the place from whence thou camest out?€€ƒxDAnd Abraham said: Beware thou never bring my son back again thither.€€ƒxÿThe Lord God of heaven, who took me out of my father's house, and out of my native country, who spoke to me, and swore to me, saying: To thy seed will I give this land: he will send his angel before thee, and thou shalt take from thence a wife for my son.€€ƒxuBut if the woman will not follow thee, thou shalt not be bound by the oath: only bring not my son back thither again.€€ƒxjThe servant, therefore, put his hand under the thigh of Abraham, his lord, and swore to him upon his word.€€ƒx®And he took ten camels of his master's herd, and departed, carrying something of all his goods with him, and he set forward and went on to Mesopotamia, to the city of Nachor.€€ƒx¤And when he had made the camels lie down without the town, near a well of water, in the evening, at the time when women are wont to come out to draw water, he said:€€ƒxnO Lord, the God of my master, Abraham, meet me today, I beseech thee, and shew kindness to my master, Abraham.€€ƒxxBehold, I stand nigh the spring of water, and the daughters of the inhabitants of this city will come out to draw water:€€ƒy&Now, therefore, the maid to whom I shall say: Let down thy pitcher that I may drink: and she shall answer, Drink, and I will give thy camels drink also: let it be the same whom thou hast provided for thy servant Isaac: and by this, I shall understand that thou hast shewn kindness to my master.€€ƒx¾He had not yet ended these words within himself, and behold Rebecca came out, the daughter of Bathuel, son of Melcha, wife to Nachor the brother of Abraham, having a pitcher on her shoulder:€€ƒxšAn exceeding comely maid, and a most beautiful virgin, and not known to man: and she went down to the spring, and filled her pitcher, and was coming back.€€ƒxZAnd the servant ran to meet her, and said: Give me a little water to drink of thy pitcher.€€ƒxhAnd she answered: Drink, my lord. And quickly she let down the pitcher upon her arm, and gave him drink.€€ƒx\And when he had drunk, she said: I will draw water for thy camels also, till they all drink.€€ƒxƒAnd pouring out the pitcher into the troughs, she ran back to the well to draw water; and having drawn, she gave to all the camels.€€ƒxqBut he musing, beheld her with silence, desirous to know whether the Lord had made his journey prosperous or not.€€ƒxˆAnd after that the camels had drunk, the man took out golden earrings, weighing two sicles; and as many bracelets, of ten sicles weight.€€ƒxhAnd he said to her: Whose daughter art thou? tell me: is there any place in thy father's house to lodge?€€ƒx[And she answered: I am the daughter of Bathuel, the son of Melcha, whom she bore to Nachor.€€ƒxhAnd she said, moreover, to him: We have good store of both straw and hay, and a large place to lodge in.€€ƒx0The man bowed himself down, and adored the Lord,€€ƒx½Saying: Blessed be the Lord God of my master Abraham, who hath not taken away his mercy and truth from my master, and hath brought me the straight way into the house of my master's brother.€€ƒxIThen the maid ran, and told in her mother's house all that she had heard.€€ƒxVAnd Rebecca had a brother, named Laban, who went out in haste to the man, to the well.€€ƒxâAnd when he had seen the earrings and bracelets in his sister's hands, and had heard all that she related, saying, Thus and thus the man spoke to me: he came to the man who stood by the camels, and near to the spring of water,€€ƒx…And said to him: Come in, thou blessed of the Lord; why standest thou without? I have prepared the house, and a place for the camels.€€ƒx¨And he brought him into his lodging; and he unharnessed the camels, and gave straw and hay, and water to wash his feet, and the feet of the men that were come with him.€€ƒxjAnd bread was set before him. But he said: I will not eat, till I tell my message. He answered him: Speak.€€ƒx)And he said: I am the servant of Abraham:€€ƒx²And the Lord hath blessed my master wonderfully, and he is become great: and he hath given him sheep and oxen, silver and gold, men servants and women servants, camels and asses.€€ƒxmAnd Sara, my master's wife, hath borne my master a son in her old age, and he hath given him all that he had.€€ƒxuAnd my master made me swear, saying: Thou shalt not take a wife for my son of the Chanaanites, in whose land I dwell:€€ƒx[But thou shalt go to my father's house, and shalt take a wife of my own kindred for my son:€€ƒxBBut I answered my master: What if the woman will not come with me?€€ƒx´The Lord, said he, in whose sight I walk, will send his angel with thee, and will direct thy way: and thou shalt take a wife for my son of my own kindred, and of my father's house.€€ƒxjBut thou shalt be clear from my curse, when thou shalt come to my kindred, if they will not give thee one.€€ƒx…And I came today to the well of water, and said: O Lord God of my master, Abraham, if thou hast prospered my way, wherein I now walk,€€ƒxŸBehold, I stand by the well of water, and the virgin, that shall come out to draw water, who shall hear me say: Give me a little water to drink of thy pitcher:€€ƒx–And shall say to me: Both drink thou, and I will also draw for thy camels: let the same be the woman, whom the Lord hath prepared for my master's son.€€ƒxàAnd whilst I pondered these things secretly with myself, Rebecca appeared, coming with a pitcher, which she carried on her shoulder: and she went down to the well and drew water. And I said to her: Give me a little to drink.€€ƒx£And she speedily let down the pitcher from her shoulder, and said to me: Both drink thou, and to thy camels I will give drink. I drank, and she watered the camels.€€ƒxİAnd I asked her, and said: Whose daughter art thou? And she answered: I am the daughter of Bathuel, the son of Nachor, whom Melcha bore to him. So I put earrings on her to adorn her face, and I put bracelets on her hands.€€ƒx¯And falling down, I adored the Lord, blessing the Lord God of my master, Abraham, who hath brought me the straight way to take the daughter of my master's brother for his son.€€ƒx®Wherefore, if you do according to mercy and truth with my master, tell me: but if it please you otherwise, tell me that also, that I may go to the right hand, or to the left.€€ƒx€And Laban and Bathuel answered: The word hath proceeded from the Lord: we cannot speak any other thing to thee but his pleasure.€€ƒx~Behold, Rebecca is before thee, take her and go thy way, and let her be the wife of thy master's son, as the Lord hath spoken.€€ƒxSWhich when Abraham's servant heard, falling down to the ground, he adored the Lord.€€ƒxAnd bringing forth vessels of silver and gold, and garments, he gave them to Rebecca, for a present. He offered gifts also to her brothers, and to her mother.€€ƒx¦And a banquet was made, and they ate and drank together, and lodged there. And in the morning, the servant arose, and said: Let me depart, that I may go to my master.€€ƒxtAnd her brother and mother answered: Let the maid stay, at least, ten days with us, and afterwards she shall depart.€€ƒxgStay me not, said he, because the Lord hath prospered my way: send me away, that I may go to my master.€€ƒx6And they said: Let us call the maid, and ask her will.€€ƒxhAnd they called her, and when she was come, they asked: Wilt thou go with this man? She said: I will go.€€ƒxMSo they sent her away, and her nurse, and Abraham's servant, and his company.€€ƒx¨Wishing prosperity to their sister, and saying: Thou art our sister, mayst thou increase to thousands of thousands; and may thy seed possess the gates of their enemies.€€ƒxiSo Rebecca and her maids, being set upon camels, followed the man: who with speed returned to his master.€€ƒxAt the same time, Isaac was walking along the way to the well which is called Of the living and the seeing: for he dwelt in the south country:€€ƒxAnd he was gone forth to meditate in the field, the day being now well spent: and when he had lifted up his eyes, he saw camels coming afar off.€€ƒx8Rebecca also, when she saw Isaac, lighted off the camel,€€ƒx¯And said to the servant: Who is that man who cometh towards us along the field? And he said to her: That man is my master. But she quickly took her cloak, and covered herself.€€ƒx0And the servant told Isaac all that he had done.€€ƒxªWho brought her into the tent of Sara his mother, and took her to wife: and he loved her so much, that it moderated the sorrow which was occasioned by his mother's death.€€ƒ`€€˜"ƒxLWho bore him Zamram, and Jecsan, and Madan, and Madian, and Jesboc, and Sue.€€ƒx_Jecsan also begot Saba, and Dadan. The children of Dadan were Assurim, and Latusim, and Loomim.€€ƒxpBut of Madian was born Epha, and Opher, and Henoch, and Abida, and Eldaa: all these were the children of Cetura.€€ƒx.And Abraham gave all his possessions to Isaac:€€ƒx„And to the children of the concubines he gave gifts, and separated them from Isaac his son, while he yet lived, to the east country.€€ƒxEAnd the days of Abraham's life were a hundred and seventy-five years.€€ƒx}And decaying he died in a good old age, and having lived a long time, and being full of days: and was gathered to his people.€€ƒx˜And Isaac and Ismael his sons buried him in the double cave, which was situated in the field of Ephron the son of Seor the Hethite, over against Mambre,€€ƒxTWhich he had bought of the children of Heth: there was he buried, and Sara his wife.€€ƒxeAnd after his death, God blessed Isaac his son, who dwelt by the well named Of the living and seeing.€€ƒxnThese are the generations of Ismael the son of Abraham, whom Agar the Egyptian, Sara's servant, bore unto him:€€ƒxAnd these are the names of his children according to their calling and generations. The firstborn of Ismael was Nabajoth, then Cedar, and Adbeel, and Mabsam,€€ƒxAnd Masma, and Duma, and Massa,€€ƒx4Hadar, and Thema, and Jethur, and Naphis, and Cedma.€€ƒxsThese are the sons of Ismael: and these are their names by their castles and towns, twelve princes of their tribes.€€ƒxwAnd the years of Ismael's life were a hundred and thirty-seven, and decaying he died, and was gathered unto his people.€€ƒx˜And he dwelt from Hevila as far as Sur, which looketh towards Egypt, to them that go towards the Assyrians. He died in the presence of all his brethren.€€ƒxPThese also are the generations of Isaac the son of Abraham: Abraham begot Isaac:€€ƒxyWho when he was forty years old, took to wife Rebecca the daughter of Bathuel the Syrian of Mesopotamia, sister to Laban.€€ƒxqAnd Isaac besought the Lord for his wife, because she was barren: and he heard him, and made Rebecca to conceive.€€ƒx•But the children struggled in her womb, and she said: If it were to be so with me, what need was there to conceive? And she went to consult the Lord.€€ƒx¶And he answering, said: Two nations are in thy womb, and two peoples shall be divided out of thy womb, and one people shall overcome the other, and the elder shall serve the younger.€€ƒxPAnd when her time was come to be delivered, behold twins were found in her womb.€€ƒxÂHe that came forth first was red, and hairy like a skin: and his name was called Esau. Immediately the other coming forth, held his brother's foot in his hand: and therefore he was called Jacob.€€ƒxDIsaac was threescore years old when the children were born unto him.€€ƒxtAnd when they were grown up, Esau became a skilful hunter, and a husbandman: but Jacob, a plain man, dwelt in tents.€€ƒxIIsaac loved Esau, because he ate of his hunting: and Rebecca loved Jacob.€€ƒxFAnd Jacob boiled pottage: to whom Esau, coming faint out of the field,€€ƒxgSaid: Give me of this red pottage, for I am exceeding faint. For which reason his name was called Edom.€€ƒx4And Jacob said to him: Sell me thy first birthright.€€ƒx?He answered: Lo I die, what will the first birthright avail me?€€ƒxTJacob said: Swear therefore to me. Esau swore to him, and sold his first birthright.€€ƒx’And so taking bread and the pottage of lentils, he ate, and drank, and went on his way; making little account of having sold his first birthright.€€ƒ`€€˜#ƒxlAnd the Lord appeared to him, and said: Go not down into Egypt, but stay in the land that I shall tell thee.€€ƒxµAnd sojourn in it, and I will be with thee, and will bless thee: for to thee and to thy seed I will give all these countries, to fulfil the oath which I swore to Abraham thy father.€€ƒx«And I will multiply thy seed like the stars of heaven: and I will give to thy posterity all these countries: and in thy seed shall all the nations of the earth be blessed.€€ƒxlBecause Abraham obeyed my voice, and kept my precepts and commandments, and observed my ceremonies and laws.€€ƒxSo Isaac abode in Gerara.€€ƒxØAnd when he was asked by the men of that place, concerning his wife, he answered: She is my sister: for he was afraid to confess that she was his wife, thinking lest perhaps they would kill him because of her beauty.€€ƒx¡And when very many days were passed, and he abode there, Abimelech, king of the Palestines, looking out through a window, saw him playing with Rebecca, his wife.€€ƒxAnd calling for him, he said: It is evident she is thy wife: why didst thou feign her to be thy sister? He answered: I feared lest I should die for her sake.€€ƒx¹And Abimelech said: Why hast thou deceived us? Some man of the people might have lain with thy wife, and thou hadst brought upon us a great sin. And he commanded all the people, saying:€€ƒxBHe that shall touch this man's wife, shall surely be put to death.€€ƒxbAnd Isaac sowed in that land, and he found that same year a hundredfold: and the Lord blessed him.€€ƒxcAnd the man was enriched, and he went on prospering and increasing, till he became exceeding great.€€ƒxlAnd he had possessions of sheep and of herds, and a very great family. Wherefore the Palestines envying him,€€ƒxxStopped up at that time all the wells, that the servants of his father, Abraham, had digged, filling them up with earth:€€ƒxiInsomuch that Abimelech himself said to Isaac: Depart from us, for thou art become much mightier than we.€€ƒxBSo he departed, and came to the torrent of Gerara, to dwell there:€€ƒxìAnd he digged again other wells, which the servants of his father, Abraham, had digged, and which, after his death, the Philistines had of old stopped up: and he called them by the same names, by which his father before had called them.€€ƒx7And they digged in the torrent, and found living water:€€ƒx¿But there also the herdsmen of Gerara strove against the herdsmen of Isaac, saying: It is our water. Wherefore he called the name of the well, on occasion of that which had happened, Calumny.€€ƒxjAnd they digged also another; and for that they quarrelled likewise, and he called the name of it, Enmity.€€ƒxÑGoing forward from thence, he digged another well, for which they contended not; therefore he called the name thereof, Latitude, saying: Now hath the Lord given us room, and made us to increase upon the earth.€€ƒx+And he went up from that place to Bersabee,€€ƒxÄWhere the Lord appeared to him that same night, saying: I am the God of Abraham thy father, do not fear, for I am with thee: I will bless thee, and multiply thy seed for my servant Abraham's sake.€€ƒx‚And he built there an altar: and called upon the name of the Lord, and pitched his tent; and commanded his servants to dig a well.€€ƒxsTo which place when Abimelech, and Ochozath his friend, and Phicol chief captain of his soldiers, came from Gerara,€€ƒx]Isaac said to them: Why are ye come to me, a man whom you hate, and have thrust out from you?€€ƒx‰And they answered: We saw that the Lord is with thee, and therefore we said: Let there be an oath between us, and let us make a covenant,€€ƒx¼That thou do us no harm, as we on our part have touched nothing of thine, nor have done any thing to hurt thee; but with peace have sent thee away, increased with the blessing of the Lord.€€ƒx=And he made them a feast, and after they had eaten and drunk:€€ƒxhArising in the morning, they swore one to another: and Isaac sent them away peaceably to their own home.€€ƒx‚And behold, the same day the servants of Isaac came, telling him of a well which they had digged, and saying: We have found water.€€ƒxaWhereupon he called it Abundance: and the name of the city was called Bersabee, even to this day.€€ƒx‘And Esau being forty years old, married wives, Judith, the daughter of Beeri, the Hethite, and Basemath, the daughter of Elon, of the same place.€€ƒx5And they both offended the mind of Isaac and Rebecca.€€ƒ`€€˜.ƒxWAnd his father said to him, Thou seest that I am old, and know not the day of my death.€€ƒxaTake thy arms, thy quiver, and bow, and go abroad; and when thou hast taken something by hunting,€€ƒx~Make me a savoury meat thereof, as thou knowest I like, and bring it that I may eat: and my soul may bless thee, before I die.€€ƒxcAnd when Rebecca had heard this, and he was gone into the field to fulfil his father's commandment,€€ƒx`She said to her son Jacob: I heard thy father talking with Esau, thy brother, and saying to him:€€ƒxqBring me of thy hunting, and make me meats that I may eat, and bless thee in the sight of the Lord, before I die.€€ƒx)Now therefore, my son, follow my counsel:€€ƒx‚And go thy way to the flock, bring me two kids of the best, that I may make of them meat for thy father, such as he gladly eateth.€€ƒxTWhich when thou hast brought in, and he hath eaten, he may bless thee before he die.€€ƒxYAnd he answered her: Thou knowest that Esau, my brother, is a hairy man, and I am smooth:€€ƒx™If my father should feel me, and perceive it, I fear lest he will think I would have mocked him, and I shall bring upon me a curse instead of a blessing.€€ƒx‚And his mother said to him: Upon me be this curse, my son: only hear thou my voice, and go, fetch me the things which I have said.€€ƒxhHe went, and brought, and gave them to his mother. She dressed meats, such as she knew his father liked.€€ƒxNAnd she put on him very good garments of Esau, which she had at home with her:€€ƒx[And the little skins of the kids she put about his hands, and covered the bare of his neck.€€ƒxNAnd she gave him the savoury meat, and delivered him bread that she had baked.€€ƒx`Which when he had carried in, he said: My father? But he answered: I hear. Who art thou, my son?€€ƒxAnd Jacob said: I am Esau, thy firstborn: I have done as thou didst command me: arise, sit and eat of my venison, that thy soul may bless me.€€ƒx—And Isaac said to his son: How couldst thou find it so quickly, my son? He answered: It was the will of God, that what I sought came quickly in my way:€€ƒxlAnd Isaac said: Come hither, that I may feel thee, my son, and may prove whether thou be my son Esau, or no.€€ƒxHe came near to his father, and when he had felt him, Isaac said: The voice indeed is the voice of Jacob; but the hands, are the hands of Esau.€€ƒx[And he knew him not, because his hairy hands made him like to the elder. Then blessing him,€€ƒx1He said: Art thou my son Esau? He answered: I am.€€ƒx·Then he said: Bring me the meats of thy hunting, my son, that my soul may bless thee. And when they were brought, and he had eaten, he offered him wine also, which after he had drunk,€€ƒx9He said to him: Come near me, and give me a kiss, my son.€€ƒxÕHe came near, and kissed him. And immediately as he smelled the fragrant smell of his garments, blessing him, he said: Behold, the smell of my son is as the smell of a plentiful field, which the Lord hath blessed.€€ƒx`God give thee of the dew of heaven, and of the fatness of the earth, abundance of corn and wine.€€ƒxßAnd let peoples serve thee, and tribes worship thee: be thou lord of thy brethren, and let thy mother's children bow down before thee. Cursed be he that curseth thee: and let him that blesseth thee be filled with blessings.€€ƒxSIsaac had scarce ended his words, when, Jacob being now gone out abroad, Esau came,€€ƒxAnd brought in to his father meats, made of what he had taken in hunting, saying: Arise, my father, and eat of thy son's venison; that thy soul may bless me.€€ƒxTAnd Isaac said to him: Why! who art thou? He answered: I am thy firstborn son, Esau.€€ƒyIsaac was struck with fear, and astonished exceedingly; and wondering beyond what can be believed, said: Who is he then that even now brought me venison that he had taken, and I ate of all before thou camest? and I have blessed him, and he shall be blessed.€€ƒxEsau having heard his father's words, roared out with a great cry; and, being in a consternation, said: Bless me also, my father.€€ƒx?And he said: Thy brother came deceitfully and got thy blessing.€€ƒyBut he said again: Rightly is his name called Jacob; for he hath supplanted me lo this second time: My birthright he took away before, and now this second time he hath stolen away my blessing. And again he said to his father: Hast thou not reserved me also a blessing?€€ƒx½Isaac answered: I have appointed him thy lord, and have made all his brethren his servants: I have established him with corn and wine, and after this, what shall I do more for thee, my son?€€ƒxzAnd Esau said to him: Hast thou only one blessing, father? I beseech thee bless me also. And when he wept with a loud cry,€€ƒx]Isaac being moved, said to him: In the fat of the earth, and in the dew of heaven from above,€€ƒx¦Shall thy blessing be. Thou shalt live by the sword, and shalt serve thy brother: and the time shall come, when thou shalt shake off and loose his yoke from thy neck.€€ƒxÇEsau therefore always hated Jacob, for the blessing wherewith his father had blessed him; and he said in his heart: The days will come of the mourning for my father, and I will kill my brother Jacob.€€ƒxThese things were told to Rebecca: and she sent and called Jacob, her son, and said to him: Behold Esau, thy brother, threateneth to kill thee.€€ƒxTNow therefore, my son, hear my voice, arise and flee to Laban, my brother, to Haran:€€ƒxTAnd thou shalt dwell with him a few days, till the wrath of thy brother be assuaged,€€ƒx¹And his indignation cease, and he forget the things thou hast done to him: afterwards I will send, and bring thee from thence hither. Why shall I be deprived of both my sons in one day?€€ƒx™And Rebecca said to Isaac: I am weary of my life, because of the daughters of Heth: if Jacob take a wife of the stock of this land, I choose not to live.€€ƒ`€€–ƒx£But go, and take a journey to Mesopotamia of Syria, to the house of Bathuel, thy mother's father, and take thee a wife thence of the daughters of Laban, thy uncle.€€ƒxsAnd God almighty bless thee, and make thee to increase and multiply thee: that thou mayst be a multitude of people.€€ƒx¡And give the blessings of Abraham to thee, and to thy seed after thee: that thou mayst possess the land of thy sojournment, which he promised to thy grandfather.€€ƒx¡And when Isaac had sent him away, he took his journey and went to Mesopotamia of Syria, to Laban, the son of Bathuel, the Syrian, brother to Rebecca, his mother.€€ƒxæAnd Esau seeing that his father had blessed Jacob, and had sent him into Mesopotamia of Syria, to marry a wife thence; and that after the blessing he had charged him, saying: Thou shalt not take a wife of the daughters of Chanaan:€€ƒx8And that Jacob obeying his parents, was gone into Syria:€€ƒxVExperiencing also, that his father was not well pleased with the daughters of Chanaan:€€ƒx‰He went to Ismael, and took to wife, besides them he had before, Maheleth, the daughter of Ismael, Abraham's son, the sister of Nabajoth.€€ƒx9But Jacob being departed from Bersabee, went on to Haran.€€ƒx¦And when he was come to a certain place, and would rest in it after sunset, he took of the stones that lay there, and putting under his head, slept in the same place.€€ƒx•And he saw in his sleep a ladder standing upon the earth, and the top thereof touching heaven: the angels also of God ascending and descending by it.€€ƒx¸And the Lord leaning upon the ladder saying to him: I am the Lord God of Abraham thy father, and the God of Isaac: The land, wherein thou sleepest, I will give to thee and to thy seed.€€ƒxÒAnd thy seed shall be as the dust of the earth: thou shalt spread abroad to the west, and to the east, and to the north, and to the south: and IN THEE and thy seed, all the tribes of the earth SHALL BE BLESSED.€€ƒx«And I will be thy keeper whithersoever thou goest, and will bring thee back into this land: neither will I leave thee, till I shall have accomplished all that I have said.€€ƒxaAnd when Jacob awaked out of sleep, he said: Indeed the Lord is in this place, and I knew it not.€€ƒxrAnd trembling, he said: How terrible is this place? this is no other but the house of God, and the gate of heaven.€€ƒxAnd Jacob arising in the morning, took the stone which he had laid under his head, and set it up for a title, pouring oil upon the top of it.€€ƒxHAnd he called the name of the city Bethel, which before was called Luza.€€ƒx™And he made a vow, saying: If God shall be with me, and shall keep me in the way, by which I walk, and shall give me bread to eat, and raiment to put on,€€ƒxOAnd I shall return prosperously to my father's house: the Lord shall be my God:€€ƒxAnd this stone, which I have set up for a title, shall be called the house of God: and of all things that thou shalt give to me, I will offer tithes to thee.€€ƒ`€€˜#ƒx¢And he saw a well in the field, and three flocks of sheep lying by it: for the beasts were watered out of it, and the mouth thereof was closed with a great stone.€€ƒx¢And the custom was, when all the sheep were gathered together, to roll away the stone, and after the sheep were watered, to put it on the mouth of the well again.€€ƒxPAnd he said to the shepherds: Brethren, whence are you? They answered: Of Haran.€€ƒxUAnd he asked them, saying: Know you Laban, the son of Nachor? They said: We know him.€€ƒxmHe said: Is he in health? He is in health, say they: and behold, Rachel, his daughter, cometh with his flock.€€ƒx¨And Jacob said: There is yet much day remaining, neither is it time to bring the flocks into the folds again: first give the sheep drink, and so lead them back to feed.€€ƒxThey answered: We cannot, till all the cattle be gathered together, and we remove the stone from the well's mouth, that we may water the flocks.€€ƒx^They were yet speaking, and behold Rachel came with her father's sheep; for she fed the flock.€€ƒx£And when Jacob saw her, and knew her to be his cousin german, and that they were the sheep of Laban, his uncle: he removed the stone wherewith the well was closed.€€ƒxKAnd having watered the flock, he kissed her: and lifting up his voice wept.€€ƒxtAnd he told her that he was her father's brother, and the son of Rebecca: but she went in haste and told her father.€€ƒxÉWho, when he heard that Jacob his sister's son was come, ran forth to meet him: and embracing him, and heartily kissing him, brought him into his house. And when he had heard the causes of his journey,€€ƒxYHe answered: Thou art my bone and my flesh. And after the days of one month were expired,€€ƒxrHe said to him: Because thou art my brother, shalt thou serve me without wages? Tell me what wages thou wilt have.€€ƒx[Now he had two daughters, the name of the elder was Lia; and the younger was called Rachel.€€ƒxQBut Lia was blear-eyed: Rachel was well favoured, and of a beautiful countenance.€€ƒxgAnd Jacob being in love with her, said: I will serve thee seven years for Rachel, thy younger daughter.€€ƒxWLaban answered: It is better that I give her to thee than to another man; stay with me.€€ƒxmSo Jacob served seven years for Rachel: and they seemed but a few days, because of the greatness of his love.€€ƒx`And he said to Laban: Give me my wife; for now the time is fulfilled, that I may go in unto her.€€ƒxUAnd he, having invited a great number of his friends to the feast, made the marriage.€€ƒx5And at night he brought in Lia, his daughter, to him,€€ƒxGiving his daughter a handmaid, named Zelpha. Now when Jacob had gone in to her according to custom, when morning was come he saw it was Lia.€€ƒx„And he said to his father-in-law: What is it that thou didst mean to do? did not I serve thee for Rachel? why hast thou deceived me?€€ƒxZLaban answered: It is not the custom in this place, to give the younger in marriage first.€€ƒxƒMake up the week of days of this match: and I will give thee her also, for the service that thou shalt render me other seven years.€€ƒxKHe yielded to his pleasure: and after the week was past, he married Rachel:€€ƒx.To whom her father gave Bala, for her servant.€€ƒx—And having at length obtained the marriage he wished for, he preferred the love of the latter before the former, and served with him other seven years.€€ƒxZAnd the Lord seeing that he despised Lia, opened her womb, but her sister remained barren.€€ƒx}And she conceived and bore a son, and called his name Ruben, saying: The Lord saw my affliction: now my husband will love me.€€ƒxœAnd again she conceived and bore a son, and said: Because the Lord heard that I was despised, he hath given this also to me: and she called his name Simeon.€€ƒx¸And she conceived the third time, and bore another son, and said: Now also my husband will be joined to me, because I have borne him three sons: and therefore she called his name Levi.€€ƒx‹The fourth time she conceived and bore a son, and said: Now will I praise the Lord: and for this she called him Juda. And she left bearing.€€ƒ`€€˜+ƒxgAnd Jacob being angry with her, answered: Am I as God, who hath deprived thee of the fruit of thy womb?€€ƒx{But she said: I have here my servant Bala: go in unto her, that she may bear upon my knees, and I may have children by her.€€ƒx'And she gave him Bala in marriage: who,€€ƒx@When her husband had gone in unto her, conceived and bore a son.€€ƒx~And Rachel said: The Lord hath judged for me, and hath heard my voice, giving me a son; and therefore she called his name Dan.€€ƒx+And again Bala conceived, and bore another,€€ƒxnFor whom Rachel said: God hath compared me with my sister, and I have prevailed: and she called him Nephthali.€€ƒxWLia perceiving that she had left of bearing, gave Zelpha, her handmaid, to her husband.€€ƒx4And when she had conceived, and brought forth a son,€€ƒx5She said: Happily. And therefore called his name Gad.€€ƒxZelpha also bore another.€€ƒxfAnd Lia said: This is for my happiness: for women will call me blessed. Therefore she called him Aser.€€ƒx¯And Ruben going out in the time of the wheat harvest into the field, found mandrakes: which he brought to his mother Lia. And Rachel said: Give me part of thy son's mandrakes.€€ƒxÎShe answered: Dost thou think it a small matter, that thou hast taken my husband from me, unless thou take also my son's mandrakes? Rachel said: He shall sleep with thee this night, for thy son's mandrakes.€€ƒxÃAnd when Jacob returned at even from the field, Lia went out to meet him, and said: Thou shalt come in unto me, because I have hired thee for my son's mandrakes. And he slept with her that night.€€ƒxCAnd God heard her prayers; and she conceived: and bore a fifth son:€€ƒxqAnd said: God hath given me a reward, because I gave my handmaid to my husband. And she called his name Issachar.€€ƒx0And Lia conceived again, and bore the sixth son,€€ƒx©And said: God hath endowed me with a good dowry; this turn also my husband will be with me, because I have borne him six sons: and therefore she called his name Zabulon.€€ƒx+After whom she bore a daughter, named Dina.€€ƒxAThe Lord also remembering Rachel, heard her, and opened her womb.€€ƒxKAnd she conceived, and bore a son, saying: God hath taken away my reproach.€€ƒxJAnd she called his name Joseph: saying: The Lord give me also another son.€€ƒx{And when Joseph was born, Jacob said to his father-in-law: Send me away, that I may return into my country, and to my land.€€ƒx†Give me my wives, and my children, for whom I have served thee, that I may depart: thou knowest the service that I have rendered thee.€€ƒxyLaban said to him: Let me find favour in thy sight: I have learned, by experience, that God hath blessed me for thy sake.€€ƒx*Appoint thy wages which I shall give thee.€€ƒxiBut he answered: Thou knowest how I have served thee, and how great thy possession hath been in my hands.€€ƒxÅThou hadst but little before I came to thee, and now thou art become rich: and the Lord hath blessed thee at my coming. It is reasonable, therefore, that I should now provide also for my own house.€€ƒxAnd Laban said: What shall I give thee? But he said: I require nothing; but if thou wilt do what I demand, I will feed and keep thy sheep again.€€ƒxÕGo round through all thy flocks, and separate all the sheep of divers colours, and speckled; and all that is brown and spotted, and of divers colours, as well among the sheep as among the goats, shall be my wages.€€ƒxæAnd my justice shall answer for me tomorrow before thee, when the time of the bargain shall come; and all that is not of divers colours, and spotted, and brown, as well among the sheep as among the goats, shall accuse me of theft.€€ƒx0And Laban said: I like well what thou demandest.€€ƒxèAnd he separated the same day the she-goats, and the sheep, and the he-goats, and the rams of divers colours, and spotted; and all the flock of one colour, that is, of white and black fleece, he delivered into the hands of his sons.€€ƒxmAnd he set the space of three days journey betwixt himself and his son-in-law, who fed the rest of his flock.€€ƒyAnd Jacob took green rods of poplar, and of almond, and of plane-trees, and pilled them in part: so when the bark was taken off, in the parts that were pilled, there appeared whiteness: but the parts that were whole, remained green: and by this means the colour was divers.€€ƒx¿And he put them in the troughs, where the water was poured out; that when the flocks should come to drink, they might have the rods before their eyes, and in the sight of them might conceive.€€ƒx‘And it came to pass, that in the very heat of coition, the sheep beheld the rods, and brought forth spotted, and of divers colours, and speckled.€€ƒx×And Jacob separated the flock, and put the rods in the troughs before the eyes of the rams; and all the white and the black were Laban's, and the rest were Jacob's, when the flocks were separated one from the other.€€ƒx¸So when the ewes went first to ram, Jacob put the rods in the troughs of water before the eyes of the rams, and of the ewes, that they might conceive while they were looking upon them.€€ƒxŸBut when the later coming was, and the last conceiving, he did not put them. And those that were lateward, became Laban's; and they of the first time, Jacob's.€€ƒxoAnd the man was enriched exceedingly, and he had many flocks, maid-servants and men-servants, camels and asses.€€ƒ`€€˜7ƒxaAnd perceiving also, that Laban's countenance was not towards him as yesterday and the other day.€€ƒxsEspecially the Lord saying to him: Return into the land of thy fathers and to thy kindred, and I will be with thee.€€ƒxKHe sent, and called Rachel and Lia into the field, where he fed the flocks,€€ƒxAnd said to them: I see your father's countenance is not towards me as yesterday and the other day: but the God of my father hath been with me.€€ƒxIAnd you know that I have served your father to the uttermost of my power.€€ƒx|Yea your father hath also overreached me, and hath changed my wages ten times: and yet God hath not suffered him to hurt me.€€ƒxÛIf at any time, he said: The speckled shall be thy wages: all the sheep brought forth speckled: but when he said on the contrary: Thou shalt take all the white one for thy wages: all the flocks brought forth white ones.€€ƒx?And God hath taken your father's substance, and given it to me.€€ƒx¹For after the time came of the ewes conceiving, I lifted up my eyes, and saw in my sleep, that the males which leaped upon the females were of divers colours, and spotted, and speckled.€€ƒxNAnd the angel of God said to me in my sleep: Jacob. And I answered: Here I am.€€ƒx²And he said: Lift up thy eyes, and see that all the males leaping upon the females, are of divers colours, spotted and speckled. For I have seen all that Laban hath done to thee.€€ƒx¢I am the God of Bethel, where thou didst anoint the stone, and make a vow to me. Now therefore arise, and go out of this land, and return into thy native country.€€ƒxjAnd Rachel and Lia answered: Have we any thing left among the goods and inheritance of our father's house?€€ƒxOHath he not counted us as strangers, and sold us, and eaten up the price of us?€€ƒx†But God hath taken our father's riches, and delivered them to us, and to our children: wherefore, do all that God hath commanded thee.€€ƒxTThen Jacob rose up, and having set his children and wives upon camels, went his way.€€ƒx–And he took all his substance, and flocks, and whatsoever he had gotten in Mesopotamia, and went forward to Isaac, his father, to the land of Chanaan.€€ƒxYAt that time Laban was gone to shear his sheep, and Rachel stole away her father's idols.€€ƒxIAnd Jacob would not confess to his father-in-law that he was flying away.€€ƒx}And when he was gone, together with all that belonged to him, and having passed the river, was going on towards mount Galaad,€€ƒx4It was told Laban on the third day, that Jacob fled.€€ƒxmAnd he took his brethren with him, and pursued after him seven days; and overtook him in the mount of Galaad.€€ƒxcAnd he saw in a dream God, saying to him: Take heed thou speak not any thing harshly against Jacob.€€ƒx“Now Jacob had pitched his tent in the mountain: and when he, with his brethren, had overtaken him, he pitched his tent in the same mount of Galaad.€€ƒx‚And he said to Jacob: Why hast thou done thus, to carry away, without my knowledge, my daughters as captives taken with the sword?€€ƒx Why wouldst thou run away privately, and not acquaint me, that I might have brought thee on the way with joy, and with songs, and with timbrels, and with harps?€€ƒxbThou hast not suffered me to kiss my sons and daughters; thou hast done foolishly; and now indeed,€€ƒx‘It is in my power to return thee evil; but the God of your father said to me yesterday: Take heed thou speak not any thing harshly against Jacob.€€ƒx€Suppose thou didst desire to go to thy friends, and hadst a longing after thy father's house: why hast thou stolen away my gods?€€ƒxtJacob answered: That I departed unknown to thee, it was for fear lest thou wouldst take away thy daughters by force.€€ƒyBut, whereas, thou chargest me with theft: with whomsoever thou shalt find thy gods, let him be slain before our brethren. Search, and if thou find any of thy things with me, take them away. Now when he said this, he knew not that Rachel had stolen the idols.€€ƒxŒSo Laban went into the tent of Jacob, and of Lia, and of both the handmaids, and found them not. And when he was entered into Rachel's tent,€€ƒx†She, in haste, hid the idols under the camel's furniture, and sat upon them: and when he had searched all the tent, and found nothing,€€ƒx¬She said: Let not my lord be angry that I cannot rise up before thee, because it has now happened to me according to the custom of women. So his careful search was in vain.€€ƒx‡And Jacob being angry, said in a chiding manner: For what fault of mine, and for what offence on my part hast thou so hotly pursued me,€€ƒx¶And searched all my household stuff? What hast thou found of all the substance of thy house? lay it here before my brethren, and thy brethren, and let them judge between me and thee.€€ƒxyHave I, therefore, been with thee twenty years? thy ewes and goats were not barren, the rams of thy flocks I did not eat:€€ƒx‹Neither did I shew thee that which the beast had torn; I made good all the damage: whatsoever was lost by theft, thou didst exact it of me:€€ƒxWDay and night was I parched with heat, and with frost, and sleep departed from my eyes.€€ƒxŸAnd in this manner have I served thee in thy house twenty years, fourteen for thy daughters, and six for thy flocks: thou hast changed also my wages ten times.€€ƒxÎUnless the God of my father, Abraham, and the fear of Isaac, had stood by me, peradventure now thou hadst sent me away naked: God beheld my affliction and the labour of my hands, and rebuked thee yesterday.€€ƒx§Laban answered him: The daughters are mine, and the children, and thy flocks, and all things that thou seest are mine: what can I do to my children, and grandchildren?€€ƒx`Come, therefore, let us enter into a league; that it may be for a testimony between me and thee.€€ƒx2And Jacob took a stone, and set it up for a title.€€ƒxyAnd he said to his brethren: Bring hither stones. And they, gathering stones together, made a heap, and they ate upon it.€€ƒx„And Laban called it, The witness heap; and Jacob, The hillock of testimony: each of them according to the propriety of his language.€€ƒx—And Laban said: This heap shall be a witness between me and thee this day, and therefore the name thereof was called Galaad, that is, The witness heap.€€ƒxOThe Lord behold and judge between us, when we shall be gone one from the other.€€ƒxIf thou afflict my daughters, and if thou bring in other wives over them: none is witness of our speech but God, who is present and beholdeth.€€ƒxdAnd he said again to Jacob: Behold this heap, and the stone which I have set up between me and thee,€€ƒx´Shall be a witness: this heap, I say, and the stone, be they for a testimony, if either I shall pass beyond it going towards thee, or thou shalt pass beyond it thinking harm to me.€€ƒx†The God of Abraham, and the God of Nachor, the God of their father, judge between us. And Jacob swore by the fear of his father Isaac:€€ƒx…And after he had offered sacrifices in the mountain, he called his brethren to eat bread. And when they had eaten, they lodged there:€€ƒxmBut Laban arose in the night, and kissed his sons and daughters, and blessed them: and returned to his place.€€ƒ`€€˜ ƒxyAnd when he saw them, he said: These are the camps of God, and he called the name of that place Mahanaim, that is, Camps.€€ƒxdAnd he sent messengers before him to Esau, his brother, to the land of Seir, to the country of Edom:€€ƒx¥And he commanded them, saying: Thus shall ye speak to my lord Esau: Thus saith thy brother Jacob: I have sojourned with Laban, and have been with him until this day:€€ƒxI have oxen, and asses, and sheep, and menservants, and womenservants: and now I send a message to my lord, that I may find favour in thy sight.€€ƒxAnd the messengers returned to Jacob, saying: We came to Esau, thy brother, and behold he cometh with speed to meet thee with four hundred men.€€ƒx¥Then Jacob was greatly afraid; and in his fear divided the people that was with him, and the flocks, and the sheep, and the oxen, and the camels, into two companies,€€ƒxbSaying: If Esau come to one company, and destroy it, the other company that is left, shall escape.€€ƒx°And Jacob said: O God of my father Abraham, and God of my father Isaac: O Lord who saidst to me, Return to thy land, and to the place of thy birth, and I will do well for thee.€€ƒx¹I am not worthy of the least of all thy mercies, and of thy truth which thou hast fulfilled to thy servant. With my staff I passed over this Jordan; and now I return with two companies.€€ƒx‰Deliver me from the hand of my brother Esau, for I am greatly afraid of him; lest perhaps he come, and kill the mother with the children.€€ƒx‡Thou didst say, that thou wouldst do well by me, and multiply my seed like the sand of the sea, which cannot be numbered for multitude.€€ƒxpAnd when he had slept there that night, he set apart, of the things which he had, presents for his brother Esau,€€ƒxJTwo hundred she-goats, twenty he-goats, two hundred ewes, and twenty rams,€€ƒxmThirty milch camels with their colts, forty kine, and twenty bulls, twenty she-asses, and ten of their foals.€€ƒx¢And he sent them by the hands of his servants, every drove by itself, and he said to his servants: Go before me, and let there be a space between drove and drove.€€ƒx™And he commanded the first, saying: If thou meet my brother Esau, and he ask thee: Whose art thou? or whither goest thou? or whose are these before thee?€€ƒxoThou shalt answer: Thy servant Jacob's: he hath sent them as a present to my lord Esau; and he cometh after us.€€ƒx“In like manner he commanded the second, and the third, and all that followed the droves, saying: Speak ye the same words to Esau, when ye find him.€€ƒxÈAnd ye shall add: Thy servant Jacob himself also followeth after us; for he said: I will appease him with the presents that go before, and afterwards I will see him, perhaps he will be gracious to me.€€ƒxKSo the presents went before him, but himself lodged that night in the camp.€€ƒxwAnd rising early, he took his two wives and his two handmaids, with his eleven sons, and passed over the ford of Jaboc.€€ƒx;And when all things were brought over that belonged to him,€€ƒxDHe remained alone; and behold, a man wrestled with him till morning.€€ƒxkAnd when he saw that he could not overcome him, he touched the sinew of his thigh, and forthwith it shrank.€€ƒxqAnd he said to him: Let me go, for it is break of day. He answered: I will not let thee go, except thou bless me.€€ƒx2And he said: What is thy name? He answered: Jacob.€€ƒx”But he said: Thy name shall not be called Jacob, but Israel; for if thou hast been strong against God, how much more shalt thou prevail against men?€€ƒx„Jacob asked him: Tell me by what name art thou called? He answered: Why dost thou ask my name? And he blessed him in the same place.€€ƒxqAnd Jacob called the name of the place Phanuel, saying: I have seen God face to face, and my soul has been saved.€€ƒx\And immediately the sun rose upon him, after he was past Phanuel; but he halted on his foot.€€ƒxšTherefore the children of Israel, unto this day, eat not the sinew, that shrank in Jacob's thigh: because he touched the sinew of his thigh and it shrank.€€ƒ`€€”ƒx„And he put both the handmaids and their children foremost: and Lia and her children in the second place: and Rachel and Joseph last.€€ƒxhAnd he went forward and bowed down with his face to the ground seven times, until his brother came near.€€ƒxqThen Esau ran to meet his brother, and embraced him: and clasping him fast about the neck, and kissing him, wept.€€ƒxÀAnd lifting up his eyes, he saw the women and their children, and said: What mean these? And do they belong to thee? He answered: They are the children which God hath given to me, thy servant.€€ƒxEThen the handmaids and their children came near and bowed themselves.€€ƒxtLia also, with her children, came near and bowed down in like manner; and last of all, Joseph and Rachel bowed down.€€ƒxdAnd Esau said: What are the droves that I met? He answered: That I might find favour before my lord.€€ƒxGBut he said: I have plenty, my brother, keep what is thine for thyself.€€ƒxÕAnd Jacob said: Do not so I beseech thee, but if I have found favour in thy eyes, receive a little present at my hands: for I have seen thy face, as if I should have seen the countenance of God: be gracious to me,€€ƒx¤And take the blessing which I have brought thee, and which God hath given me, who giveth all things. He took it with much ado at his brother's earnest pressing him,€€ƒxJAnd said: Let us go on together, and I will accompany thee in thy journey.€€ƒx¸And Jacob said: My lord, thou knowest that I have with me tender children, and sheep, and kine with young: which if I should cause to be overdriven, in one day all the flocks will die.€€ƒx›May it please my lord to go before his servant: and I will follow softly after him, as I shall see my children to be able, until I come to my lord in Seir.€€ƒxáEsau answered: I beseech thee, that some of the people, at least, who are with me, may stay to accompany thee in the way. And he said: There is no necessity: I want nothing else but only to find favour, my lord, in thy sight.€€ƒx9So Esau returned that day, the way that he came, to Seir.€€ƒx€And Jacob came to Socoth: where having built a house, and pitched tents, he called the name of the place Socoth, that is, Tents.€€ƒxAnd he passed over to Salem, a city of the Sichemites, which is in the land of Chanaan, after he returned from Mesopotamia of Syria: and he dwelt by the town.€€ƒx‰And he bought that part of the field, in which he pitched his tents, of the children of Hemor, the father of Sichem, for a hundred lambs.€€ƒxMAnd raising an altar there, he invoked upon it the most mighty God of Israel.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒx¢And when Sichem the son of Hemor the Hevite, the prince of that land, saw her, he was in love with her: and took her away, and lay with her, ravishing the virgin.€€ƒx`And his soul was fast knit unto her; and whereas she was sad, he comforted her with sweet words.€€ƒxCAnd going to Hemor his father, he said: Get me this damsel to wife.€€ƒx€But when Jacob had heard this, his sons being absent, and employed in feeding the cattle, he held his peace till they came back.€€ƒxCAnd when Hemor the father of Sichem was come out to speak to Jacob,€€ƒxÆBehold his sons came from the field: and hearing what had passed, they were exceeding angry, because he had done a foul thing in Israel, and committed an unlawful act, in ravishing Jacob's daughter.€€ƒxiAnd Hemor spoke to them: The soul of my son Sichem has a longing for your daughter: give her him to wife:€€ƒxcAnd let us contract marriages one with another: give us your daughters, and take you our daughters.€€ƒxLAnd dwell with us: the land is at your command, till, trade, and possess it.€€ƒxƒSichem also said to her father and to her brethren: Let me find favour in your sight, and whatsoever you shall appoint I will give:€€ƒxoRaise the dowry, and ask gifts, and I will gladly give what you shall demand: only give me this damsel to wife.€€ƒxoThe sons of Jacob answered Sichem and his father deceitfully, being enraged at the deflowering of their sister:€€ƒxyWe cannot do what you demand, nor give our sister to one that is uncircumcised; which with us is unlawful and abominable.€€ƒxmBut in this we may be allied with you, if you will be like us, and all the male sex among you be circumcised:€€ƒxqThen will we mutually give and take your daughters, and ours; and we will dwell with you, and will be one people:€€ƒxIBut if you will not be circumcised, we will take our daughter and depart.€€ƒx/Their offer pleased Hemor, and Sichem, his son:€€ƒx§And the young man made no delay, but forthwith fulfilled what was required: for he loved the damsel exceedingly, and he was the greatest man in all his father's house.€€ƒx>And going into the gate of the city, they spoke to the people:€€ƒxÛThese men are peaceable, and are willing to dwell with us: let them trade in the land, and till it, which being large and wide wanteth men to till it: we shall take their daughters for wives, and we will give them ours.€€ƒx…One thing there is for which so great a good is deferred: We must circumcise every male among us, following the manner of the nation.€€ƒxAnd their substance, and cattle, and all that they possess, shall be ours; only in this let us condescend, and by dwelling together, we shall make one people.€€ƒx3And they all agreed, and circumcised all the males.€€ƒxËAnd behold the third day, when the pain of the wound was greatest: two of the sons of Jacob, Simeon and Levi, the brothers of Dina, taking their swords, entered boldly into the city and slew all the men.€€ƒx]And they killed also Hemor and Sichem, and took away their sister Dina out of Sichem's house.€€ƒxxAnd when they were gone out, the other sons of Jacob came upon the slain; and plundered the city in revenge of the rape.€€ƒxvAnd they took their sheep, and their herds, and their asses, wasting all they had in their houses and in their fields.€€ƒx/And their children and wives they took captive.€€ƒy'And when they had boldly perpetrated these things, Jacob said to Simeon and Levi: You have troubled me, and made me hateful to the Chanaanites and Pherezites, the inhabitants of this land. We are few: they will gather themselves together and kill me; and both I, and my house shall be destroyed.€€ƒx:They answered: Should they abuse our sister as a strumpet?€€ƒ`€€˜ƒx“And Jacob having called together all his household, said: Cast away the strange gods that are among you, and be cleansed, and change your garments.€€ƒx–Arise, and let us go up to Bethel, that we may make there an altar to God; who heard me in the day of my affliction, and accompained me in my journey.€€ƒx«So they gave him all the strange gods they had, and the earrings which were in their ears: and he buried them under the turpentine tree, that is behind the city of Sichem.€€ƒxŒAnd when they were departed, the terror of God fell upon all the cities round about, and they durst not pursue after them as they went away.€€ƒxsAnd Jacob came to Luza, which is in the land of Chanaan, surnamed Bethel: he and all the people that were with him.€€ƒxAnd he built there an altar, and called the name of that place, The house of God: for there God appeared to him when he fled from his brother.€€ƒx£At the same time Debora, the nurse of Rebecca, died, and was buried at the foot of Bethel, under an oak, and the name of that place was called, The oak of weeping.€€ƒxaAnd God appeared again to Jacob, after he returned from Mesopotamia of Syria, and he blessed him,€€ƒxhSaying: Thou shalt not be called any more Jacob, but Israel shall be thy name. And he called him Israel.€€ƒxAnd said to him: I am God almighty, increase thou and be multiplied. Nations and peoples of nations shall be from thee, and kings shall come out of thy loins.€€ƒx`And the land which I gave to Abraham and Isaac, I will give to thee, and to thy seed after thee.€€ƒxAnd he departed from him.€€ƒx†But he set up a monument of stone, in the place where God had spoken to him: pouring drink-offerings upon it, and pouring oil thereon:€€ƒx*And calling the name of that place Bethel.€€ƒxAnd going forth from thence, he came in the spring time to the land which leadeth to Ephrata: wherein when Rachel was in travail,€€ƒx‚By reason of her hard labour, she began to be in danger, and the midwife said to her: Fear not, for thou shalt have this son also.€€ƒxĞAnd when her soul was departing for pain, and death was now at hand, she called the name of her son Benoni, that is, the son of my pain: but his father called him Benjamin, that is, the son of the right hand.€€ƒxYSo Rachel died, and was buried in the highway that leadeth to Ephrata, this is Bethlehem.€€ƒxdAnd Jacob erected a pillar over her sepulchre: this is the pillar of Rachel's monument, to this day.€€ƒx=Departing thence, he pitched his tent beyond the Flock tower.€€ƒx And when he dwelt in that country, Ruben went, and slept with Bala the concubine of his father: which he was not ignorant of. Now the sons of Jacob were twelve.€€ƒxaThe sons of Lia: Ruben the first born, and Simeon, and Levi, and Juda, and Issachar, and Zabulon.€€ƒx(The sons of Rachel: Joseph and Benjamin.€€ƒx7The sons of Bala, Rachel's handmaid: Dan and Nephthali.€€ƒx}The sons of Zelpha, Lia's handmaid: Gad and Aser: these are the sons of Jacob, that were born to him in Mesopotamia of Syria.€€ƒxrAnd he came to Isaac his father in Mambre, the city of Arbee, this is Hebron: wherein Abraham and Isaac sojourned.€€ƒx6And the days of Isaac were a hundred and eighty years.€€ƒx…And being spent with age he died, and was gathered to his people, being old and full of days: and his sons Esau and Jacob buried him.€€ƒ`€€˜+ƒx—Esau took wives of the daughters of Chanaan: Ada the daughter of Elon the Hethite, and Oolibama the daughter of Ana, the daughter of Sebeon the Hevite:€€ƒx9And Basemath, the daughter of Ismael, sister of Nabajoth.€€ƒx+And Ada bore Eliphaz: Basemath bore Rahuel.€€ƒxtOolibama bore Jehus, and Ihelon, and Core. These are the sons of Esau, that were born to him in the land of Chanaan.€€ƒxğAnd Esau took his wives, and his sons and daughters, and every soul of his house, and his substance, and cattle, and all that he was able to acquire in the land of Chanaan: and went into another country, and departed from his brother Jacob.€€ƒxFor they were exceeding rich, and could not dwell together: neither was the land in which they sojourned able to bear them, for the multitude of their flocks.€€ƒx)And Esau dwelt in mount Seir: he is Edom.€€ƒxIAnd these are the generations of Esau, the father of Edom, in mount Seir.€€ƒxuAnd these the names of his sons: Eliphaz the son of Ada, the wife of Esau: and Rahuel, the son of Basemath, his wife.€€ƒx@And Eliphaz had sons: Theman, Omar, Sepho, and Gatham and Cenez.€€ƒx€And Thamna was the concubine of Eliphaz, the son of Esau: and she bore him Amalech. These are the sons of Ada, the wife of Esau.€€ƒxoAnd the sons of Rahuel were Nahath and Zara, Samma and Meza. These were the sons of Basemath, the wife of Esau.€€ƒx–And these were the sons of Oolibama, the daughter of Ana, the daughter of Sebeon, the wife of Esau, whom she bore to him, Jehus, and Ihelon, and Core.€€ƒxThese were dukes of the sons of Esau: the sons of Eliphaz, the firstborn of Esau: duke Theman, duke Omar, duke Sepho, duke Cenez,€€ƒxtDuke Core, duke Gatham, duke Amalech: these are the sons of Eliphaz, in the land of Edom, and these the sons of Ada.€€ƒxÈAnd these were the sons of Rahuel, the son of Esau: duke Nahath, duke Zara, duke Samma, duke Meza. And these are the dukes of Rahuel, in the land of Edom: these the sons of Basemath, the wife of Esau.€€ƒxAnd these the sons of Oolibama, the wife of Esau: duke Jehus, duke Ihelon, duke Core. These are the dukes of Oolibama, the daughter of Ana, and wife of Esau.€€ƒxJThese are the sons of Esau, and these the dukes of them: the same is Edom.€€ƒxlThese are the sons of Seir, the Horrite, the inhabitants of the land: Lotan, and Sobal, and Sebeon, and Ana,€€ƒxgAnd Dison, and Eser, and Disan. These are dukes of the Horrites, the sons of Seir, in the land of Edom.€€ƒxGAnd Lotan had sons: Hori and Heman. And the sister of Lotan was Thamna.€€ƒxOAnd these the sons of Sobal: Alvan, and Manahat, and Ebal, and Sepho, and Onam.€€ƒxAnd these the sons of Sebeon: Aia and Ana. This is Ana that found the hot waters in the wilderness, when he fed the asses of Sebeon, his father:€€ƒx0And he had a son Dison, and a daughter Oolibama.€€ƒxNAnd these were the sons of Dison: Hamdan, and Eseban, and Jethram, and Charan.€€ƒx>These also were the sons of Eser: Balaan, and Zavan, and Acan.€€ƒx!And Dison had sons: Hus and Aram.€€ƒxPThese were dukes of the Horrites: duke Lotan, duke Sobal, duke Sebeon, duke Ana,€€ƒxcDuke Dison, duke Eser, duke Disan: these were dukes of the Horrites that ruled in the land of Seir.€€ƒxcAnd the kings that ruled in the land of Edom, before the children of Israel had a king, were these:€€ƒx6Bela the son of Beor, and the name of his city Denaba.€€ƒxJAnd Bela died, and Jobab, the son of Zara, of Bosra, reigned in his stead.€€ƒxTAnd when Jobab was dead, Husam, of the land of the Themanites, reigned in his stead.€€ƒxšAnd after his death, Adad, the son of Badad, reigned in his stead, who defeated the Madianites in the country of Boab; and the name of his city was Avith.€€ƒxFAnd when Adad was dead, there reigned in his stead, Semla, of Masreca.€€ƒxEAnd he being dead, Saul, of the river Rohoboth, reigned in his stead.€€ƒxQAnd when he also was dead, Balanan, the son of Achobor, succeeded to the kingdom.€€ƒx¥This man also being dead, Adar reigned in his place; and the name of his city was Phau: and his wife was called Meetabel, the daughter of Matred, daughter of Mezaab.€€ƒxAnd these are the names of the dukes of Esau in their kindreds, and places, and callings: duke Thamna, duke Alva, duke Jetheth,€€ƒx%Duke Oolibama, duke Ela, duke Phinon,€€ƒx%Duke Cenez, duke Theman, duke Mabsar,€€ƒxDuke Magdiel, duke Hiram: these are the dukes of Edom dwelling in the land of their government; the same is Esau, the father of the Edomites.€€ƒ`€€˜$ƒyAnd these are his generations: Joseph, when he was sixteen years old, was feeding the flock with his brethren, being but a boy: and he was with the sons of Bala and of Zelpha his father's wives: and he accused his brethren to his father of a most wicked crime.€€ƒxxNow Israel loved Joseph above all his sons, because he had him in his old age: and he made him a coat of divers colours.€€ƒxAnd his brethren seeing that he was loved by his father, more than all his sons, hated hem, and could not speak peaceably to him.€€ƒxxNow it fell out also that he told his brethren a dream, that he had dreamed: which occasioned them to hate him the more.€€ƒx3And he said to them: Hear my dream which I dreamed.€€ƒx•I thought we were binding sheaves in the field: and my sheaf arose as it were, and stood, and your sheaves standing about bowed down before my sheaf.€€ƒxµHis brethren answered: Shalt thou be our king? or shall we be subject to thy dominion? Therefore this matter of his dreams and words ministered nourishment to their envy and hatred.€€ƒx—He dreamed also another dream, which he told his brethren, saying: I saw in a dream, as it were the sun, and the moon, and eleven stars worshipping me.€€ƒxÍAnd when he had told this to his father, and brethren, his father rebuked him and said: What meaneth this dream that thou hast dreamed? shall I and thy mother, and thy brethren worship thee upon the earth?€€ƒxTHis brethren therefore envied him: but his father considered the thing with himself.€€ƒxEAnd when his brethren abode in Sechem, feeding their father's flocks,€€ƒxpIsrael said to him: Thy brethren feed the sheep in Sichem: come, I will send thee to them. And when he answered:€€ƒxÁI am ready: he said to him: Go, and see if all things be well with thy brethren, and the cattle: and bring me word again what is doing. So being sent from the vale of Hebron, he came to Sichem:€€ƒxKAnd a man found him there wandering in the field, and asked what he sought.€€ƒxHBut he answered: I seek my brethren, tell me where they feed the flocks.€€ƒx®And the man said to him: They are departed from this place: for I heard them say: Let us go to Dothain. And Joseph went forward after his brethren, and found them in Dothain.€€ƒxSAnd when they saw him afar off, before he came nigh them, they thought to kill him:€€ƒx3And said one to another: Behold the dreamer cometh.€€ƒxCome, let us kill him, and cast him into some old pit: and we will say: Some evil beast hath devoured him: and then it shall appear what his dreams avail him:€€ƒxPAnd Ruben hearing this, endeavoured to deliver him out of their hands, and said:€€ƒxèDo not take away his life, nor shed his blood: but cast him into this pit, that is in the wilderness, and keep your hands harmless: now he said this, being desirous to deliver him out of their hands and to restore him to his father.€€ƒxrAnd as soon as he came to his brethren, they forthwith stript him of his outside coat, that was of divers colours:€€ƒx7And cast him into an old pit where there was not water.€€ƒxšAnd sitting down to eat bread, they saw some Ismaelites on their way coming from Galaad, with their camels, carrying spices, and balm, and myrrh to Egypt.€€ƒxaAnd Juda said to his brethren: What will it profit us to kill our brother, and conceal his blood?€€ƒx™It is better that he be sold to the Ismaelites, and that our hands be not defiled: for he is our brother and our flesh. His brethren agreed to his words.€€ƒx£And when the Madianite merchants passed by, they drew him out of the pit, and sold him to the Ismaelites, for twenty pieces of silver: and they led him into Egypt.€€ƒx2And Ruben returning to the pit, found not the boy:€€ƒxlAnd rending his garments he went to his brethren, and said: The boy doth not appear, and whither shall I go?€€ƒxSAnd they took his coat, and dipped it in the blood of a kid, which they had killed:€€ƒxsSending some to carry it to their father, and to say: This we have found: see whether it be thy son's coat, or not.€€ƒx|And the father acknowledging it, said: It is my son's coat, an evil wild beast hath eaten him, a beast hath devoured Joseph.€€ƒxPAnd tearing his garments, he put on sackcloth, mourning for his son a long time.€€ƒxÊAnd all his children being gathered together to comfort their father in his sorrow, he would not receive comfort, but said: I will go down to my son into hell, mourning. And whilst he continued weeping,€€ƒx^The Madianites sold Joseph in Egypt to Putiphar, an eunuch of Pharao, captain of the soldiers.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒxkAnd he saw there the daughter of a man of Chanaan, called Sue: and taking her to wife, he went in unto her.€€ƒx;And she conceived, and bore a son, and called his name Her.€€ƒx:And conceiving again, she bore a son, and called him Onan.€€ƒx\She bore also a third: whom she called Sela. After whose birth, she ceased to bear any more.€€ƒxDAnd Juda took a wife for Her, his first born, whose name was Thamar.€€ƒx[And Her, the first born of Juda, was wicked in the sight of the Lord: and was slain by him.€€ƒx|Juda, therefore, said to Onan his son: Go in to thy brother's wife and marry her, that thou mayst raise seed to thy brother.€€ƒx¯He knowing that the children should not be his, when he went in to his brother's wife, he spilled his seed upon the ground, lest children should be born in his brother's name.€€ƒxCAnd therefore the Lord slew him, because he did a detestable thing:€€ƒxäWherefore Juda said to Thamar his daughter-in-law: Remain a widow in thy father's house, till Sela my son grow up: for he was afraid lest he also might die, as his brethren did. She went her way, and dwelt in her father's house.€€ƒxèAnd after many days were past: the daughter of Sue the wife of Juda died: and when he had taken comfort after his mourning, he went up to Thamnas, to the shearers of his sheep, he and Hiras the Odollamite, the shepherd of his flock.€€ƒxXAnd it was told Thamar that her father-in-law was come up to Thamnas to shear his sheep.€€ƒxÆAnd she put off the garments of her widowhood, and took a veil: and changing her dress, sat in the cross way, that leadeth to Thamnas: because Sela was grown up, and she had not been married to him.€€ƒxgWhen Juda saw her, he thought she was a harlot: for she had covered her face, lest she should be known.€€ƒx£And going to her, he said: Suffer me to lie with thee: for he knew her not to be his daughter-in-law. And she answered: What wilt thou give me to enjoy my company?€€ƒx¦He said: I will send thee a kid out of the flock. And when she said again: I will suffer what thou wilt, if thou give me a pledge, till thou send what thou promisest.€€ƒx°Juda said: What wilt thou have for a pledge? She answered: Thy ring and bracelet, and the staff which thou holdest in thy hand. The woman therefore at one copulation conceived.€€ƒxvAnd she arose and went her way: and putting off the apparel which she had taken, put on the garments of her widowhood.€€ƒx–And Juda sent a kid by his shepherd, the Odollamite, that he might receive the pledge again, which he had given to the woman: but he, not finding her,€€ƒxŒAsked the men of that place: Where is the woman that sat in the cross way? And when they all made answer: There was no harlot in this place,€€ƒxŒHe returned to Juda, and said to him: I have not found her; moreover, the men of that place said to me, that there never sat a harlot there.€€ƒxŒJuda said: Let her take it to herself, surely she cannot charge us with a lie, I sent the kid which I promised: and thou didst not find her.€€ƒxÇAnd behold, after three months, they told Juda, saying: Thamar, thy daughter-in-law, hath played the harlot, and she appeareth to have a big belly. And Juda said: Bring her out that she may be burnt.€€ƒx´But when she was led to execution, she sent to her father in law, saying: By the man, to whom these things belong, I am with child. See whose ring, and bracelet, and staff this is.€€ƒx„But he acknowledging the gifts, said: She is juster than I: because I did not give her to Sela, my son. However he knew her no more.€€ƒxÀAnd when she was ready to be brought to bed, there appeared twins in her womb: and in the very delivery of the infants, one put forth a hand, whereon the midwife tied a scarlet thread, saying:€€ƒx This shall come forth the first.€€ƒx”But he drawing back his hand, the other came forth: and the woman said: Why is the partition divided for thee? and therefore called his name Phares.€€ƒxdAfterwards his brother came out, on whose hand was the scarlet thread: and she called his name Zara.€€ƒ`€€—ƒxiAnd the Lord was with him, and he was a prosperous man in all things: and he dwelt in his master's house:€€ƒx_Who knew very well that the Lord was with him, and made all that he did to prosper in his hand.€€ƒx¼And Joseph found favour in the sight of his master, and ministered to him: and being set over all by him, he governed the house committed to him, and all things that were delivered to him:€€ƒxƒAnd the Lord blessed the house of the Egyptian for Joseph's sake, and multiplied all his substance, both at home and in the fields.€€ƒx}Neither knew he any other thing, but the bread which he ate. And Joseph was of a beautiful countenance, and comely to behold.€€ƒxQAnd after many days, his mistress cast her eyes on Joseph, and said: Lie with me.€€ƒxBut he in no wise consenting to that wicked act said to her: Behold, my master hath delivered all things to me, and knoweth not what he hath in his own house:€€ƒx²Neither is there any thing which is not in my power, or that he hath not delivered to me, but thee, who art his wife; how then can I do this wicked thing, and sin against my God?€€ƒxtWith such words as these day by day, both the woman was importunate with the young man, and he refused the adultery.€€ƒxyNow it happened on a certain day, that Joseph went into the house, and was doing some business, without any man with him:€€ƒxyAnd she catching the skirt of his garment, said: Lie with me. But he leaving the garment in her hand, fled, and went out.€€ƒxIAnd when the woman saw the garment in her hands, and herself disregarded,€€ƒx She called to her the men of her house, and said to them: See, he hath brought in a Hebrew, to abuse us: he came in to me, to lie with me; and when I cried out,€€ƒxHAnd he heard my voice, he left the garment that I held, and got him out.€€ƒxpFor a proof therefore of her fidelity, she kept the garment, and shewed it to her husband when he returned home:€€ƒxMAnd said: The Hebrew servant, whom thou hast brought, came to me to abuse me.€€ƒxIAnd when he heard me cry, he left the garment which I held, and fled out.€€ƒx`His master hearing these things, and giving too much credit to his wife's words, was very angry,€€ƒx`And cast Joseph into the prison, where the king's prisoners were kept, and he was there shut up.€€ƒxwBut the Lord was with Joseph, and having mercy upon him gave him favour in the sight of the chief keeper of the prison:€€ƒxpWho delivered into his hand all the prisoners that were kept in custody: and whatsoever was done, was under him.€€ƒxŠNeither did he himself know any thing, having committed all things to him: for the Lord was with him, and made all that he did to prosper.€€ƒ`€€—ƒxXAnd Pharao being angry with them, (now the one was chief butler, the other chief baker,)€€ƒx_He sent them to the prison of the commander of the soldiers, in which Joseph also was prisoner.€€ƒx‚But the keeper of the prison delivered them to Joseph, and he served them. Some little time passed, and they were kept in custody.€€ƒxeAnd they both dreamed a dream the same night, according to the interpretation agreeing to themselves:€€ƒxDAnd when Joseph was come into them in the morning, and saw them sad,€€ƒxGHe asked them, saying: Why is your countenance sadder today than usual?€€ƒx±They answered: We have dreamed a dream, and there is nobody to interpret it to us. And Joseph said to them: Doth not interpretation belong to God? Tell me what you have dreamed:€€ƒx>The chief butler first told his dream: I saw before me a vine,€€ƒxyOn which were three branches, which by little and little sent out buds, and after the blossoms brought forth ripe grapes:€€ƒx†And the cup of Pharao was in my hand: and I took the grapes, and pressed them into the cup which I held, and I gave the cup to Pharao.€€ƒxaJoseph answered: This is the interpretation of the dream: The three branches, are yet three days:€€ƒx³After which Pharao will remember thy service, and will restore thee to thy former place: and thou shalt present him the cup according to thy office, as before thou was wont to do.€€ƒxOnly remember me when it shall be well with thee, and do me this kindness: to put Pharao in mind to take me out of this prison:€€ƒxkFor I was stolen away out of the land of the Hebrews, and here without any fault was cast into the dungeon.€€ƒxThe chief baker seeing that he had wisely interpreted the dream, said: I also dreamed a dream, That I had three baskets of meal upon my head:€€ƒx…And that in one basket which was uppermost, I carried all meats that are made by the art of baking, and that the birds ate out of it.€€ƒx`Joseph answered: This is the interpretation of the dream: The three baskets, are yet three days:€€ƒxnAfter which Pharao will take thy head from thee, and hang thee on a cross, and the birds shall tear thy flesh.€€ƒx¥The third day after this was the birthday of Pharao: and he made a great feast for his servants, and at the banquet remembered the chief butler, and the chief baker.€€ƒx=And he restored the one to his place, to present him the cup:€€ƒxRThe other he hanged on a gibbet, that the truth of the interpreter might be shewn.€€ƒxMBut the chief butler, when things prospered with him, forgot his interpreter.€€ƒ`€€˜9ƒxWOut of which came up seven kine, very beautiful and fat: and they fed in marshy places.€€ƒx‡Other seven also came up out of the river, ill-favoured, and lean fleshed: and they fed on the very bank of the river, in green places:€€ƒx_And they devoured them, whose bodies were very beautiful and well conditioned. So Pharao awoke.€€ƒxcHe slept again, and dreamed another dream: Seven ears of corn came up upon one stalk full and fair:€€ƒx1Then seven other ears sprung up thin and blasted,€€ƒxHAnd devoured all the beauty of the former. Pharao awaked after his rest:€€ƒxÛAnd when morning was come, being struck with fear, he sent to all the interpreters of Egypt, and to all the wise men: and they being called for, he told them his dream, and there was not any one that could interpret it.€€ƒxDThen at length the chief butler remembering, said: I confess my sin:€€ƒxƒThe king being angry with his servants, commanded me and the chief baker to be cast into the prison of the captain of the soldiers.€€ƒxHWhere in one night both of us dreamed a dream foreboding things to come.€€ƒxnThere was there a young man a Hebrew, servant to the same captain of the soldiers: to whom we told our dreams,€€ƒx†And we heard what afterwards the event of the thing proved to be so. For I was restored to my office: and he was hanged upon a gibbet.€€ƒxŠForthwith at the king's command Joseph was brought out of the prison, and they shaved him: and changing his apparel brought him in to him.€€ƒx”And he said to him: I have dreamed dreams, and there is no one that can expound them: Now I have heard that thou art very wise at interpreting them:€€ƒxGJoseph answered: Without me, God shall give Pharao a prosperous answer.€€ƒxQSo Pharao told what he had dreamed: Methought I stood upon the bank of the river,€€ƒx„And seven kine came up out of the river, exceeding beautiful and full of flesh: and they grazed on green places in a marshy pasture.€€ƒx‚And behold, there followed these, other seven kine, so very ill-favoured and lean, that I never saw the like in the land of Egypt:€€ƒx*And they devoured and consumed the former,€€ƒx{And yet gave no mark of their being full: but were as lean and ill-favoured as before. I awoke, and then fell asleep again,€€ƒxSAnd dreamed a dream: Seven ears of corn grew up upon one stalk, full and very fair.€€ƒx7Other seven also thin and blasted, sprung of the stalk:€€ƒx{And they devoured the beauty of the former: I told this dream to the conjecturers, and there is no man that can expound it.€€ƒxZJoseph answered: The king's dream is one: God hath shewn to Pharao what he is about to do.€€ƒx}The seven beautiful kine, and the seven full ears, are seven years of plenty: and both contain the same meaning of the dream.€€ƒxAnd the seven lean and thin kine that came up after them, and the seven thin ears that were blasted with the burning wind, are seven years of famine to come:€€ƒx'Which shall be fulfilled in this order.€€ƒxPBehold, there shall come seven years of great plenty in the whole land of Egypt:€€ƒxAfter which shall follow other seven years of so great scarcity, that all the abundance before shall be forgotten: for the famine shall consume all the land,€€ƒxLAnd the greatness of the scarcity shall destroy the greatness of the plenty.€€ƒxµAnd for that thou didst see the second time a dream pertaining to the same thing: it is a token of the certainty, and that the word of God cometh to pass, and is fulfilled speedily.€€ƒxiNow therefore let the king provide a wise and industrious man, and make him ruler over the land of Egypt:€€ƒxŠThat he may appoint overseers over all the countries: and gather into barns the fifth part of the fruits, during the seven fruitful years,€€ƒxuThat shall now presently ensue: and let all the corn be laid up, under Pharao's hands, and be reserved in the cities.€€ƒx“And let it be in readiness, against the famine of seven years to come, which shall oppress Egypt, and the land shall not be consumed with scarcity.€€ƒx1The counsel pleased Pharao, and all his servants.€€ƒxUAnd he said to them: Can we find such another man, that is full of the spirit of God?€€ƒx}He said therefore to Joseph: Seeing God hath shewn thee all that thou hast said, can I find one wiser and one like unto thee?€€ƒx‹Thou shalt be over my house, and at the commandment of thy mouth all the people shall obey: only in the kingly throne will I be above thee.€€ƒx\And again Pharao said to Joseph: Behold, I have appointed thee over the whole land of Egypt.€€ƒxAnd he took his ring from his own hand, and gave it into his hand: and he put upon him a robe of silk, and put a chain of gold about his neck.€€ƒx¼And he made him go up into his second chariot, the crier proclaiming that all should bow their knee before him, and that they should know he was made governor over the whole land of Egypt.€€ƒxzAnd the king said to Joseph: I am Pharao: without thy commandment no man shall move hand or foot in all the land of Egypt.€€ƒxÔAnd he turned his name, and called him in the Egyptian tongue the saviour of the world. And he gave him to wife Aseneth, the daughter of Putiphare, priest of Heliopolis. Then Joseph went out to the land of Egypt.€€ƒxm(Now he was thirty years old when he stood before king Pharao), and he went round all the countries of Egypt.€€ƒx†And the fruitfulness of the seven years came: and the corn being bound up into sheaves, was gathered together into the barns of Egypt.€€ƒx9And all the abundance of grain was laid up in every city.€€ƒxuAnd there was so great abundance of wheat, that it was equal to the sand of the sea, and the plenty exceeded measure.€€ƒxƒAnd before the famine came, Joseph had two sons born: whom Aseneth, the daughter of Putiphare, priest of Heliopolis, bore unto him.€€ƒx{And he called the name of the firstborn Manasses, saying: God hath made me to forget all my labours, and my father's house.€€ƒx\And he named the second Ephraim, saying: God hath made me to grow in the land of my poverty.€€ƒxFNow when the seven years of plenty that had been in Egypt were passed:€€ƒx¡The seven years of scarcity, which Joseph had foretold, began to come: and the famine prevailed in the whole world, but there was bread in all the land of Egypt.€€ƒxœAnd when there also they began to be famished, the people cried to Pharao, for food. And he said to them: Go to Joseph: and do all that he shall say to you.€€ƒx“And the famine increased daily in all the land: and Joseph opened all the barns, and sold to the Egyptians: for the famine had oppressed them also.€€ƒxVAnd all provinces came into Egypt, to buy food, and to seek some relief of their want.€€ƒ`€€˜&ƒx~I have heard that wheat is sold in Egypt: Go ye down, and buy us necessaries, that we may live, and not be consumed with want.€€ƒx>So the ten brethren of Joseph went down, to buy corn in Egypt:€€ƒxrWhilst Benjamin was kept at home by Jacob, who said to his brethren: Lest perhaps he take any harm in the journey.€€ƒxpAnd they entered into the land of Egypt with others that went to buy. For the famine was in the land of Chanaan.€€ƒxŒAnd Joseph was governor in the land of Egypt, and corn was sold by his direction to the people. And when his brethren had bowed down to him,€€ƒx¨And he knew them, he spoke as it were to strangers, somewhat roughly, asking them: Whence came you? They answered: From the land of Chanaan, to buy necessaries of life.€€ƒx:And though he knew his brethren, he was not known by them.€€ƒxAnd remembering the dreams, which formerly he had dreamed, he said to them: You are spies. You are come to view the weaker parts of the land.€€ƒxLBut they said: It is not so, my lord; but thy servants are come to buy food.€€ƒxhWe are all the sons of one man: we are come as peaceable men, neither do thy servants go about any evil.€€ƒx`And he answered them: It is otherwise: you are come to consider the unfenced parts of this land.€€ƒx™But they said: We thy servants are twelve brethren, the sons of one man in the land of Chanaan: the youngest is with our father, the other is not living.€€ƒx0He saith, This is it that I said: You are spies.€€ƒx~I shall now presently try what you are: by the health of Pharao, you shall not depart hence, until your youngest brother come.€€ƒx¨Send one of you to fetch him: and you shall be in prison, till what you have said be proved, whether it be true or false: or else by the health of Pharao you are spies.€€ƒx$So he put them in prison three days.€€ƒxqAnd the third day he brought them out of prison, and said: Do as I have said, and you shall live: for I fear God.€€ƒx•If you be peaceable men, let one of your brethren be bound in prison: and go ye your ways, and carry the corn that you have bought, unto your houses.€€ƒx{And bring your youngest brother to me, that I may find your words to be true, and you may not die. They did as he had said.€€ƒxåAnd they talked one to another: We deserve to suffer these things, because we have sinned against our brother, seeing the anguish of his soul, when he besought us, and we would not hear: therefore is this affliction come upon us.€€ƒxˆAnd Ruben, one of them, said: Did not I say to you: Do not sin against the boy; and you would not hear me? Behold his blood is required.€€ƒxUAnd they knew not that Joseph understood, because he spoke to them by an interpreter.€€ƒxUAnd he turned himself away a little while, and wept: and returning, he spoke to them.€€ƒxäAnd taking Simeon, and binding him in their presence, he commanded his servants to fill their sacks with wheat, and to put every man's money again in their sacks, and to give them besides provisions for the way: and they did so.€€ƒx@But they having loaded their asses with the corn went their way.€€ƒxlAnd one of them opening his sack, to give his beast provender in the inn, saw the money in the sack's mouth,€€ƒx¹And said to his brethren: My money is given me again; behold it is in the sack. And they were astonished, and troubled, and said to one another: What is this that God hath done unto us?€€ƒxxAnd they came to Jacob their father in the land of Chanaan, and they told him all things that had befallen them, saying:€€ƒxQThe lord of the land spoke roughly to us, and took us to be spies of the country.€€ƒx?And we answered him: We are peaceable men, and we mean no plot.€€ƒxuWe are twelve brethren born of one father: one is not living, the youngest is with our father in the land of Chanaan.€€ƒx©And he said to us: Hereby shall I know that you are peaceable men: Leave one of your brethren with me, and take ye necessary provision for your houses, and go your ways,€€ƒx¹And bring your youngest brother to me, that I may know you are not spies: and you may receive this man again, that is kept in prison: and afterwards may have leave to buy what you will.€€ƒx”When they had told this, they poured out their corn, and every man found his money tied in the mouth of his sack: and all being astonished together,€€ƒxµTheir father Jacob said: You have made me to be without children: Joseph is not living, Simeon is kept in bonds, and Benjamin you will take away: all these evils are fallen upon me.€€ƒx…And Ruben answered him: Kill my two sons, if I bring him not again to thee: deliver him into my hand, and I will restore him to thee.€€ƒxÉBut he said: My son shall not go down with you: his brother is dead, and he is left alone: if any mischief befall him in the land to which you go, you will bring down my grey hairs with sorrow to hell.€€ƒ`€€˜"ƒx‰And when they had eaten up all the corn, which they had brought out of Egypt, Jacob said to his sons: Go again, and buy us a little food.€€ƒxœJuda answered: The man declared unto us with the attestation of an oath, saying: You shall not see my face, unless you bring your youngest brother with you.€€ƒxeIf therefore thou wilt send him with us, we will set out together, and will buy necessaries for thee.€€ƒxœBut if thou wilt not, we will not go: for the man, as we have often said, declared unto us, saying: You shall not see my face without your youngest brother.€€ƒxiIsrael said to them: You have done this for my misery, in that you told him you had also another brother.€€ƒxñBut they answered: The man asked us in order concerning our kindred: if our father lived: if we had a brother: and we answered him regularly, according to what he demanded: could we know that he would say: Bring hither your brother with you?€€ƒxAnd Juda said to his father: Send the boy with me, that we may set forward, and may live: lest both we and our children perish.€€ƒx‘I take the boy upon me, require him at my hand: unless I bring him again, and restore him to thee, I will be guilty of sin against thee for ever.€€ƒxCIf delay had not been made, we had been here again the second time.€€ƒxâThen Israel said to them: If it must needs be so, do what you will: take of the best fruits of the land in your vessels, and carry down presents to the man, a little balm, and honey, and storax, myrrh, turpentine, and almonds.€€ƒxqAnd take with you double money, and carry back what you found in your sacks, lest perhaps it was done by mistake.€€ƒx.And take also your brother, and go to the man.€€ƒx°And may my almighty God make him favourable to you: and send back with you your brother, whom he keepeth, and this Benjamin: and as for me I shall be desolate without children.€€ƒxpSo the men took the presents, and double money, and Benjamin: and went down into Egypt, and stood before Joseph.€€ƒxÑAnd when he had seen them, and Benjamin with them, he commanded the steward of his house, saying: Bring in the men into the house, and kill victims, and prepare a feast: because they shall eat with me at noon.€€ƒx?He did as he was commanded, and brought the men into the house.€€ƒxñAnd they being much afraid, said there one to another: Because of the money, which we carried back the first time in our sacks, we are brought in: that he may bring upon us a false accusation, and by violence make slaves of us and our asses.€€ƒx=Wherefore, going up to the steward of the house, at the door,€€ƒxPThey said: Sir, we desire thee to hear us. We came down once before to buy food:€€ƒxªAnd when we had bought, and were come to the inn, we opened our sacks, and found our money in the mouths of the sacks: which we have now brought again in the same weight.€€ƒxdAnd we have brought other money besides, to buy what we want: we cannot tell who put it in our bags.€€ƒxĞBut he answered: Peace be with you, fear not: your God, and the God of your father, hath given you treasure in your sacks. For the money, which you gave me, I have for good. And he brought Simeon out to them.€€ƒx{And having brought them into the house, he fetched water, and they washed their feet, and he gave provender to their asses.€€ƒxsBut they made ready the presents, against Joseph came at noon: for they had heard that they should eat bread there.€€ƒx”Then Joseph came in to his house, and they offered him the presents, holding them in their hands; and they bowed down with their face to the ground.€€ƒxˆBut he courteously saluting them again, asked them, saying: Is the old man your father in health, of whom you told me? Is he yet living?€€ƒx}And they answered: Thy servant our father, is in health; he is yet living. And bowing themselves, they made obeisance to him.€€ƒx¶And Joseph lifting up his eyes, saw Benjamin, his brother by the same mother, and said: Is this your young brother, of whom you told me? And he said: God be gracious to thee, my son.€€ƒx{And he made haste, because his heart was moved upon his brother, and tears gushed out: and going into his chamber, he wept.€€ƒxjAnd when he had washed his face, coming out again, he refrained himself, and said: Set bread on the table.€€ƒxÚAnd when it was set on, for Joseph apart, and for his brethren apart, for the Egyptians also that ate with him apart, (for it is unlawful for the Egyptians to eat with the Hebrews, and they think such a feast profane):€€ƒxƒThey sat before him, the firstborn according to his birthright, and the youngest according to his age. And they wondered very much;€€ƒx Taking the messes which they received of him: and the greater mess came to Benjamin, so that it exceeded by five parts. And they drank, and were merry with him.€€ƒ`€€˜"ƒxxAnd in the mouth of the younger's sack put my silver cup, and the price which he gave for the wheat. And it was so done.€€ƒxAAnd when the morning arose, they were sent away with their asses.€€ƒxüAnd when they were now departed out of the city, and had gone forward a little way: Joseph sending for the steward of his house, said: Arise, and pursue after the men: and when thou hast overtaken them, say to them: Why have you returned evil for good?€€ƒx…The cup which you have stolen, is that in which my lord drinketh, and in which he is wont to divine: you have done a very evil thing.€€ƒx[He did as he had commanded him. And having overtaken them, he spoke to them the same words.€€ƒxfAnd they answered: Why doth our lord speak so, as though thy servants had committed so heinous a fact?€€ƒx·The money, that we found in the top of our sacks, we brought back to thee from the land of Chanaan: how then should it be that we should steal out of thy lord's house, gold or silver?€€ƒx{With whomsoever of thy servants shall be found that which thou seekest, let him die, and we will be the bondmen of my lord.€€ƒxAnd he said to them: Let it be according to your sentence: with whomsoever it shall be found, let him be my servant, and you shall be blameless.€€ƒxVThen they speedily took down their sacks to the ground, and every man opened his sack.€€ƒxuWhich when he had searched, beginning at the eldest, and ending at the youngest, he found the cup in Benjamin's sack.€€ƒxUThen they rent their garments, and loading their asses again, returned into the town.€€ƒx›And Juda at the head of his brethren went in to Joseph (for he was not yet gone out of the place) and they all together fell down before him on the ground.€€ƒxoAnd he said to them: Why would you do so? know you not that there is no one like me in the science of divining.€€ƒxëAnd Juda said to him: What shall we answer my lord? or what shall we say, or be able justly to allege? God hath found out the iniquity of thy servants: behold, we are all bondmen to my lord, both we, and he with whom the cup was found.€€ƒx„Joseph answered: God forbid that I should do so: he that stole the cup, he shall be my bondman: and go you away free to your father.€€ƒx¦Then Juda coming nearer, said boldly: I beseech thee, my lord, let thy servant speak a word in thy ears, and be not angry with thy servant: for after Pharao thou art.€€ƒxTMy lord. Thou didst ask thy servants the first time: Have you a father or a brother.€€ƒx×And we answered thee, my lord: We have a father an old man, and a young boy, that was born in his old age; whose brother by the mother is dead; and he alone is left of his mother, and his father loveth him tenderly.€€ƒxWAnd thou saidst to thy servants: Bring him hither to me, and I will set my eyes on him.€€ƒx[We suggested to my lord: The boy cannot leave his father: for if he leave him, he will die.€€ƒxkAnd thou saidst to thy servants: Except your youngest brother come with you, you shall see my face no more.€€ƒx`Therefore when we were gone up to thy servant our father, we told him all that my lord had said.€€ƒx9And our father said: Go again, and buy us a little wheat.€€ƒx›And we said to him: We cannot go: if our youngest brother go down with us, we will set out together: otherwise, without him we dare not see the man's face.€€ƒx9Whereunto he answered: You know that my wife bore me two.€€ƒxPOne went out, and you said: A beast devoured him; and hitherto he appeareth not.€€ƒxtIf you take this also, and any thing befall him in the way, you will bring down my grey hairs with sorrow unto hell.€€ƒxTherefore, if I shall go to thy servant, our father, and the boy be wanting, (whereas his life dependeth upon the life of him,)€€ƒx}And he shall see that he is not with us, he will die, and thy servants shall bring down his grey hairs with sorrow unto hell.€€ƒxLet me be thy proper servant, who took him into my trust, and promised, saying: If I bring him not again, I will be guilty of sin against my father for ever.€€ƒxzTherefore I, thy servant, will stay instead of the boy in the service of my lord, and let the boy go up with his brethren.€€ƒxrFor I cannot return to my father without the boy, lest I be a witness of the calamity that will oppress my father.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒx`And he lifted up his voice with weeping, which the Egyptians, and all the house of Pharao heard.€€ƒxAnd he said to his brethren: I am Joseph: Is my father yet living? His brethren could not answer him, being struck with exceeding great fear.€€ƒxAnd he said mildly to them: Come nearer to me. And when they were come near him, he said: I am Joseph, your brother, whom you sold into Egypt.€€ƒx™Be not afraid, and let it not seem to you a hard case that you sold me into these countries: for God sent me before you into Egypt for your preservation.€€ƒxFor it is two years since the famine began to be upon the land, and five years more remain, wherein there can be neither ploughing nor reaping.€€ƒx\And God sent me before, that you may be preserved upon the earth, and may have food to live.€€ƒx²Not by your counsel was I sent hither, but by the will of God: who hath made me as it were a father to Pharao, and lord of his whole house, and governor in all the land of Egypt.€€ƒx Make haste, and go ye up to my father, and say to him: Thus saith thy son Joseph: God hath made me lord of the whole land of Egypt; come down to me, linger not.€€ƒx¦And thou shalt dwell in the land of Gessen: and thou shalt be near me, thou and thy sons, and thy sons' sons, thy sheep, and thy herds, and all things that thou hast.€€ƒx“And there I will feed thee, (for there are yet five years of famine remaining) lest both thou perish, and thy house, and all things that thou hast.€€ƒxeBehold, your eyes, and the eyes of my brother Benjamin, see that it is my mouth that speaketh to you.€€ƒxuYou shall tell my father of all my glory, and all things that you have seen in Egypt: make haste and bring him to me.€€ƒxAnd falling upon the neck of his brother Benjamin, he embraced him and wept: and Benjamin in like manner wept also on his neck.€€ƒxvAnd Joseph kissed all his brethren, and wept upon every one of them: after which they were emboldened to speak to him.€€ƒxAnd it was heard, and the fame was spread abroad in the king's court: The brethren of Joseph are come; and Pharao with all his family was glad.€€ƒx}And he spoke to Joseph that he should give orders to his brethren, saying: Load your beasts, and go into the land of Chanaan,€€ƒxAnd bring away from thence your father and kindred, and come to me; and I will give you all the good things of Egypt, that you may eat the marrow of the land.€€ƒx¹Give orders also that they take wagons out of the land of Egypt, for the carriage of their children and their wives; and say: Take up your father, and make haste to come with all speed:€€ƒxVAnd leave nothing of your household stuff; for all the riches of Egypt shall be yours.€€ƒx‡And the sons of Israel did as they were bid. And Joseph gave them wagons according to Pharao's commandment: and provisions for the way.€€ƒx—He ordered also to be brought out for every one of them two robes: but to Benjamin he gave three hundred pieces of silver, with five robes of the best:€€ƒxµSending to his father as much money and raiment; adding besides, ten he-asses, to carry off all the riches of Egypt, and as many she-asses, carrying wheat and bread for the journey.€€ƒx[So he sent away his brethren, and at their departing said to them: Be not angry in the way.€€ƒxXAnd they went up out of Egypt, and came into the land of Chanaan, to their father Jacob.€€ƒx¼And they told him, saying: Joseph, thy son, is living; and he is ruler in all the land of Egypt. Which when Jacob heard, he awaked as it were out of a deep sleep, yet did not believe them.€€ƒx…They, on the other side, told the whole order of the thing. And when he saw the wagons, and all that he had sent, his spirit revived,€€ƒxfAnd he said: It is enough for me if Joseph, my son, be yet living: I will go and see him before I die.€€ƒ`€€˜"ƒxyHe heard him, by a vision in the night, calling him, and saying to him: Jacob, Jacob. And he answered him: Lo, here I am.€€ƒx„God said to him: I am the most mighty God of thy father; fear not, go down into Egypt, for I will make a great nation of thee there.€€ƒx|I will go down with thee thither, and will bring thee back again from thence: Joseph also shall put his hands upon thy eyes.€€ƒxŸAnd Jacob rose up from the well of the oath: and his sons took him up, with their children and wives in the wagons, which Pharao had sent to carry the old man,€€ƒxUAnd all that he had in the land of Chanaan: and he came into Egypt with all his seed;€€ƒxCHis sons, and grandsons, daughters, and all his offspring together.€€ƒxuAnd these are the names of the children of Israel, that entered into Egypt, he and his children. His firstborn Ruben,€€ƒx<The sons of Ruben: Henoch and Phallu, and Hesron and Charmi.€€ƒxmThe sons of Simeon: Jamuel and Jamin and Ahod, and Jachin and Sohar, and Saul, the son of a woman of Chanaan.€€ƒx/The sons of Levi: Gerson and Caath, and Merari.€€ƒxœThe sons of Juda: Her and Onan, and Sela, and Phares and Zara. And Her and Onan died in the land of Chanaan. And sons were born to Phares: Hesron and Hamul.€€ƒx9The sons of Issachar: Thola and Phua, and Job and Semron.€€ƒx2The sons of Zabulon: Sared, and Elon, and Jahelel.€€ƒx‘These are the sons of Lia, whom she bore in Mesopotamia of Syria, with Dina, his daughter. All the souls of her sons and daughters, thirty-three.€€ƒxWThe sons of Gad: Sephion and Haggi, and Suni and Esebon, and Heri and Arodi, and Areli.€€ƒxwThe sons of Aser: Jamne and Jesua, and Jessuri and Beria, and Sara their sister. The sons of Beria: Heber and Melchiel.€€ƒxoThese are the sons of Zelpha, whom Laban gave to Lia, his daughter. And these she bore to Jacob, sixteen souls.€€ƒx6The sons of Rachel, Jacob's wife: Joseph and Benjamin.€€ƒx’And sons were born to Joseph, in the land of Egypt, whom Aseneth, the daughter of Putiphare, priest of Heliopolis, bore him: Manasses and Ephraim.€€ƒxxThe sons of Benjamin: Bela and Bechor, and Asbel and Gera, and Naaman and Echi, and Ross and Mophim, and Ophim and Ared.€€ƒxNThese are the sons of Rachel, whom she bore to Jacob: all the souls, fourteen.€€ƒwThe sons of Dan: Husim.€€ƒx=The sons of Nephthali: Jaziel and Guni, and Jeser and Sallem.€€ƒxwThese are the sons of Bala, whom Laban gave to Rachel, his daughter: and these she bore to Jacob: all the souls, seven.€€ƒxrAll the souls that went with Jacob into Egypt, and that came out of his thigh, besides his sons' wives, sixty-six.€€ƒxšAnd the sons of Joseph, that were born to him in the land of Egypt, two souls. All the souls of the house of Jacob, that entered into Egypt, were seventy.€€ƒxZAnd he sent Juda before him to Joseph, to tell him; and that he should meet him in Gessen.€€ƒx®And when he was come thither, Joseph made ready his chariot, and went up to meet his father in the same place: and seeing him, he fell upon his neck, and embracing him, wept.€€ƒxlAnd the father said to Joseph: Now shall I die with joy, because I have seen thy face, and leave thee alive.€€ƒxÎAnd Joseph said to his brethren, and to all his father's house: I will go up, and will tell Pharao, and will say to him: My brethren, and my father's house, that were in the land of Chanaan, are come to me:€€ƒx‹And the men are shepherds, and their occupation is to feed cattle; their flocks, and herds, and all they have, they have brought with them.€€ƒxCAnd when he shall call you, and shall say: What is your occupation?€€ƒxâYou shall answer: We, thy servants, are shepherds, from our infancy until now, both we and our fathers. And this you shall say, that you may dwell in the land of Gessen, because the Egyptians have all shepherds in abomination.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒxFFive men also, the last of his brethren, he presented before the king:€€ƒxtAnd he asked them: What is your occupation? They answered: We, thy servants, are shepherds, both we and our fathers.€€ƒxçWe are come to sojourn in thy land, because there is no grass for the flocks of thy servants, the famine being very grievous in the land of Chanaan: and we pray thee to give orders that we thy servants may be in the land of Gessen.€€ƒxPThe king therefore said to Joseph: Thy father and thy brethren are come to thee.€€ƒxÊThe land of Egypt is before thee: and make them dwell in the best place, and give them the land of Gessen. And if thou knowest that there are industrious men among them, make them rulers over my cattle.€€ƒxfAfter this Joseph brought in his father to the king, and presented him before him: and he blessed him.€€ƒxGAnd being asked by him: How many are the days of the years of thy life?€€ƒx›He answered: The days of my pilgrimage are a hundred and thirty years, few, and evil, and they are not come up to the days of the pilgrimage of my fathers.€€ƒx#And blessing the king, he went out.€€ƒxŠBut Joseph gave a possession to his father and his brethren in Egypt, in the best place of the land, in Ramesses, as Pharao had commanded.€€ƒxNAnd he nourished them, and all his father's house, allowing food to every one.€€ƒxzFor in the whole world there was want of bread, and a famine had oppressed the land, more especially of Egypt and Chanaan;€€ƒxsOut of which he gathered up all the money for the corn which they bought, and brought it in to the king's treasure.€€ƒxŠAnd when the buyers wanted money, all Egypt came to Joseph, saying: Give us bread: why should we die in thy presence, having now no money?€€ƒxdAnd he answered them: Bring me your cattle, and for them I will give you food, if you have no money.€€ƒx²And when they had brought them, he gave them food in exchange for their horses, and sheep, and oxen, and asses: and he maintained them that year for the exchange of their cattle.€€ƒxàAnd they came the second year, and said to him: We will not hide from our lord, how that our money is spent, and our cattle also are gone: neither art thou ignorant that we have nothing now left but our bodies and our lands.€€ƒxÇWhy therefore shall we die before thy eyes? we will be thine, both we and our lands: buy us to be the king's servants, and give us seed, lest for want of tillers the land be turned into a wilderness.€€ƒx™So Joseph bought all the land of Egypt, every man selling his possessions, because of the greatness of the famine. And he brought it into Pharao's hands:€€ƒxWAnd all its people from one end of the borders of Egypt, even to the other end thereof,€€ƒxÑExcept the land of the priests, which had been given them by the king: to whom also a certain allowance of food was given out of the public stores, and therefore they were not forced to sell their possessions.€€ƒx{Then Joseph said to the people: Behold, as you see, both you and your lands belong to Pharao; take seed and sow the fields,€€ƒx—That you may have corn. The fifth part you shall give to the king; the other four you shall have for seed, and for food for your families and children.€€ƒxxAnd they answered: our life is in thy hand; only let my lord look favourably upon us, and we will gladly serve the king.€€ƒx¼From that time unto this day, in the whole land of Egypt, the fifth part is paid to the kings, and it is become as a law, except the land of the priests, which was free from this covenant.€€ƒxuSo Israel dwelt in Egypt, that is, in the land of Gessen, and possessed it; and grew, and was multiplied exceedingly.€€ƒxiAnd he lived in it seventeen years: and all the days of his life came to a hundred and forty-seven years.€€ƒxêAnd when he saw that the day of his death drew nigh, he called his son Joseph, and said to him: If I have found favour in thy sight, put thy hand under my thigh; and thou shalt shew me this kindness and truth, not to bury me in Egypt.€€ƒx¾But I will sleep with my fathers, and thou shalt take me away out of this land, and bury me in the burying place of my ancestors. And Joseph answered him: I will do what thou hast commanded.€€ƒxdAnd he said: Swear then to me. And as he was swearing, Israel adored God, turning to the bed's head.€€ƒ`€€–ƒxmAnd it was told the old man: Behold thy son Joseph cometh to thee. And being strengthened, he sat on his bed.€€ƒx…And when Joseph was come in to him, he said: God almighty appeared to me at Luza, which is in the land of Chanaan, and he blessed me,€€ƒxÁAnd said: I will cause thee to increase and multiply, and I will make of thee a multitude of people: and I will give this land to thee, and to thy seed after thee for an everlasting possession.€€ƒx©So thy two sons, who were born to thee in the land of Egypt before I came hither to thee, shall be mine: Ephraim and Manasses shall be reputed to me as Ruben and Simeon.€€ƒx…But the rest whom thou shalt have after them, shall be thine, and shall be called by the name of their brethren in their possessions.€€ƒxíFor, when I came out of Mesopotamia, Rachel died from me in the land of Chanaan in the very journey, and it was spring time: and I was going to Ephrata, and I buried her near the way of Ephrata, which by another name is called Bethlehem.€€ƒx4Then seeing his sons, he said to him: Who are these?€€ƒxzHe answered: They are my sons, whom God hath given me in this place. And he said: Bring them to me, that I may bless them.€€ƒx”For Israel's eyes were dim by reason of his great age, and he could not see clearly. And when they were brought to him, he kissed and embraced them,€€ƒx[And said to his son: I am not deprived of seeing thee; moreover God hath shewn me thy seed.€€ƒx`And when Joseph had taken them from his father's lap, he bowed down with his face to the ground.€€ƒxµAnd he set Ephraim on his right hand, that is, towards the left hand of Israel; but Manasses on his left hand, to wit, towards his father's right hand, and brought them near to him.€€ƒx­But he, stretching forth his right hand, put it upon the head of Ephraim, the younger brother; and the left upon the head of Manasses, who was the elder, changing his hands.€€ƒxšAnd Jacob blessed the sons of Joseph, and said: God, in whose sight my fathers Abraham and Isaac walked, God that feedeth me from my youth until this day:€€ƒxÅThe angel that delivereth me from all evils, bless these boys: and let my name be called upon them, and the names of my fathers Abraham and Isaac; and may they grow into a multitude upon the earth.€€ƒxØAnd Joseph seeing that his father had put his right hand upon the head of Ephraim, was much displeased: and taking his father's hand, he tried to lift it from Ephraim's head, and to remove it to the head of Manasses.€€ƒxwAnd he said to his father: It should not be so, my father; for this is the firstborn, put thy right hand upon his head.€€ƒxÄBut he refusing, said: I know, my son, I know: and this also shall become a people, and shall be multiplied; but his younger brother shall be greater than he; and his seed shall grow into nations.€€ƒx¶And he blessed them at that time, saying: In thee shall Israel be blessed, and it shall be said: God do to thee as to Ephraim, and as to Manasses. And he set Ephraim before Manasses.€€ƒx~And he said to Joseph, his son: Behold I die, and God will be with you, and will bring you back into the land of your fathers.€€ƒxnI give thee a portion above thy brethren, which I took out of the hand of the Amorrhite with my sword and bow.€€ƒ`€€˜!ƒxYGather yourselves together, and hear, O ye sons of Jacob, hearken to Israel, your father:€€ƒxrRuben, my firstborn, thou art my strength, and the beginning of my sorrow; excelling in gifts, greater in command.€€ƒxuThou art poured out as water, grow thou not; because thou wentest up to thy father's bed, and didst defile his couch.€€ƒx9Simeon and Levi brethren: vessels of iniquity waging war.€€ƒxŸLet not my soul go into their counsel, nor my glory be in their assembly: because in their fury they slew a man, and in their self-will they undermined a wall.€€ƒx“Cursed be their fury, because it was stubborn: and their wrath, because it was cruel: I will divide them in Jacob, and will scatter them in Israel.€€ƒxƒJuda, thee shall thy brethren praise: thy hand shall be on the necks of thy enemies; the sons of thy father shall bow down to thee.€€ƒxŠJuda is a lion's whelp: to the prey, my son, thou art gone up: resting thou hast couched as a lion, and as a lioness, who shall rouse him?€€ƒx—The sceptre shall not be taken away from Juda, nor a ruler from his thigh, till he come that is to be sent, and he shall be the expectation of nations.€€ƒxTying his foal to the vineyard, and his ass, O my son, to the vine. He shall wash his robe in wine, and his garment in the blood of the grape.€€ƒxFHis eyes are more beautiful than wine, and his teeth whiter than milk.€€ƒxXZabulon shall dwell on the seashore, and in the road of ships, reaching as far as Sidon.€€ƒx?Issachar shall be a strong ass, lying down between the borders.€€ƒx‰He saw rest that it was good: and the land that it was excellent: and he bowed his shoulder to carry, and became a servant under tribute.€€ƒx8Dan shall judge his people like another tribe in Israel.€€ƒxvLet Dan be a snake in the way, a serpent in the path, that biteth the horse's heels, that his rider may fall backward.€€ƒx&I will look for thy salvation, O Lord.€€ƒxSGad, being girded, shall fight before him: and he himself shall be girded backward.€€ƒxCAser, his bread shall be fat, and he shall yield dainties to kings.€€ƒx8Nephthali, a hart let loose, and giving words of beauty.€€ƒxhJoseph is a growing son, a growing son and comely to behold: the daughters run to and fro upon the wall;€€ƒxPBut they that held darts, provoked him, and quarrelled with him, and envied him.€€ƒx±His bow rested upon the strong, and the bands of his arms and his hands were loosed, by the hands of the mighty one of Jacob: thence he came forth a pastor, the stone of Israel.€€ƒxÖThe God of thy father shall be thy helper, and the Almighty shall bless thee with the blessings of heaven above, with the blessings of the deep that lieth beneath, with the blessings of the breasts and of the womb.€€ƒxâThe blessings of thy father are strengthened with the blessings of his fathers: until the desire of the everlasting hills should come: may they be upon the head of Joseph, and upon the crown of the Nazarite among his brethren.€€ƒxgBenjamin a ravenous wolf, in the morning shall eat the prey, and in the evening shall divide the spoil.€€ƒx‰All these are the twelve tribes of Israel: these things their father spoke to them, and he blessed every one with their proper blessings.€€ƒx¡And he charged them, saying: I am now going to be gathered to my people: bury me with my fathers in the double cave, which is in the field of Ephron the Hethite,€€ƒxOver against Mambre, in the land of Chanaan, which Abraham bought together with the field, of Ephron the Hethite, for a possession to bury in.€€ƒxxThere they buried him, and Sara his wife: there was Isaac buried with Rebecca, his wife: there also Lia doth lie buried.€€ƒx˜And when he had ended the commandments, wherewith he instructed his sons, he drew up his feet upon the bed, and died: and he was gathered to his people.€€ƒ`€€ƒxRAnd when Joseph saw this, he fell upon his father's face, weeping and kissing him.€€˜ƒx¥And while they were fulfilling his commands, there passed forty days: for this was the manner with bodies that were embalmed, and Egypt mourned for him seventy days.€€ƒx”And the time of the mourning being expired, Joseph spoke to the family of Pharao: If I have found favour in your sight, speak in the ears of Pharao:€€ƒxÃFor my father made me swear to him, saying: Behold I die; thou shalt bury me in my sepulchre which I have digged for myself in the land of Chanaan. So I will go up and bury my father, and return.€€ƒxRAnd Pharao said to him: Go up and bury thy father according as he made thee swear.€€ƒxsSo he went up, and there went with him all the ancients of Pharao's house, and all the elders of the land of Egypt.€€ƒx„And the house of Joseph with his brethren, except their children, and their flocks and herds, which they left in the land of Gessen.€€ƒxKHe had also in his train chariots and horsemen: and it was a great company.€€ƒx´And they came to the threshing floor of Atad, which is situated beyond the Jordan: where celebrating the exequies with a great and vehement lamentation, they spent full seven days.€€ƒx«And when the inhabitants of Chanaan saw this, they said: This is a great mourning to the Egyptians. And therefore the name of that place was called, The mourning of Egypt.€€ƒx2So the sons of Jacob did as he had commanded them.€€ƒxÑAnd carrying him into the land of Chanaan, they buried him in the double cave, which Abraham had bought together with the field for a possession of a burying place, of Ehpron, the Hethite, over against Mambre.€€ƒxsAnd Joseph returned into Egypt with his brethren, and all that were in his company, after he had buried his father.€€ƒx°Now he being dead, his brethren were afraid, and talked one with another: Lest perhaps he should remember the wrong he suffered, and requite us all the evil that we did to him.€€ƒxOAnd they sent a message to him, saying: Thy father commanded us before he died,€€ƒyThat we should say thus much to thee from him: I beseech thee to forget the wickedness of thy brethren, and the sin and malice they practised against thee: we also pray thee, to forgive the servants of the God of thy father this wickedness. And when Joseph heard this, he wept.€€ƒxfAnd his brethren came to him; and worshipping prostrate on the ground, they said: We are thy servants.€€ƒx>And he answered them: Fear not: can we resist the will of God?€€ƒx„You thought evil against me: but God turned it into good, that he might exalt me, as at present you see, and might save many people.€€ƒx`Fear not: I will feed you and your children. And he comforted them, and spoke gently and mildly.€€ƒxáAnd he dwelt in Egypt with all his father's house; and lived a hundred and ten years. And he saw the children of Ephraim to the third generation. The children also of Machir, the sons of Manasses, were born on Joseph's knees.€€ƒx§After which he told his brethren: God will visit you after my death, and will make you go up out of this land, to the land which he swore to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.€€ƒxeAnd he made them swear to him, saying: God will visit you, carry my bones with you out of this place:€€ƒxeAnd he died, being a hundred and ten years old. And being embalmed, he was laid in a coffin in Egypt.€€ƒrThe Book of Exodus€€ƒ`€€ƒxzThese are the names of the children of Israel, that went into Egypt with Jacob: they went in every man with his household:€€dExod¤dnamefExodusiosis_namedExodlabbreviationdExodhchapters˜(–ƒx!Dan, and Nephthali, Gad and Aser.€€ƒxXAnd all the souls that came out of Jacob's thigh, were seventy: but Joseph was in Egypt.€€ƒxAAfter he was dead, and all his brethren, and all that generation,€€ƒxuThe children of Israel increased, and sprung up into multitudes, and growing exceedingly strong they filled the land.€€ƒxIIn the mean time there arose a new king over Egypt, that knew not Joseph:€€ƒxiAnd he said to his people: Behold the people of the children of Israel are numerous and stronger than we.€€ƒx¡Come let us wisely oppress them, lest they multiply: and if any war shall rise against us, join with our enemies, and having overcome us, depart out of the land.€€ƒx–Therefore he set over them masters of the works, to afflict them with burdens: and they built for Pharao cities of tabernacles, Phithom, and Ramesses.€€ƒxNBut the more they oppressed them, the more they were multiplied and increased.€€ƒxSAnd the Egyptians hated the children of Israel, and afflicted them and mocked them:€€ƒxAnd they made their life bitter with hard works in clay and brick, and with all manner of service, wherewith they were overcharged in the works of the earth.€€ƒxkAnd the king of Egypt spoke to the midwives of the Hebrews: of whom one was called Sephora, the other Phua,€€ƒxªCommanding them: When you shall do the office of midwives to the Hebrew women, and the time of delivery is come: if it be a man child, kill it: if a woman, keep it alive.€€ƒxkBut the midwives feared God, and did not do as the king of Egypt had commanded, but saved the men children.€€ƒxmAnd the king called for them and said: What is it that you meant to do, that you would save the men children?€€ƒx©They answered: The Hebrew women are not as the Egyptian women: for they themselves are skilful in the office of a midwife; and they are delivered before we come to them.€€ƒxbTherefore God dealt well with the midwives: and the people multiplied and grew exceedingly strong.€€ƒx:And because the midwives feared God, he built them houses.€€ƒx§Pharao therefore charged all his people, saying: Whatsoever shall be born of the male sex, ye shall cast into the river: whatsoever of the female, ye shall save alive.€€ƒ`€€ƒxUAfter this there went a man of the house of Levi; and took a wife of his own kindred.€€ƒxWAnd she conceived, and bore a son: and seeing him a goodly child, hid him three months.€€˜ƒxCHis sister standing afar off, and taking notice what would be done.€€ƒxÙAnd behold the daughter of Pharao came down to wash herself in the river: and her maids walked by the river's brink. And when she saw the basket in the sedges she sent one of her maids for it: and when it was brought,€€ƒxShe opened it, and seeing within it an infant crying, having compassion on it, she said: This is one of the babes of the Hebrews.€€ƒxcAnd the child's sister said to her: Shall I go, and call to thee a Hebrew woman, to nurse the babe?€€ƒx6She answered: Go. The maid went and called her mother.€€ƒxÌAnd Pharao's daughter said to her: Take this child, and nurse him for me: I will give thee thy wages. The woman took and nursed the child: and when he was grown up, she delivered him to Pharao's daughter.€€ƒxaAnd she adopted him for a son, and called him Moses, saying: Because I took him out of the water.€€ƒxšIn those days, after Moses was grown up, he went out to his brethren: and saw their affliction, and an Egyptian striking one of the Hebrews, his brethren.€€ƒxwAnd when he had looked about this way and that way, and saw no one there, he slew the Egyptian and hid him in the sand.€€ƒxƒAnd going out the next day, he saw two Hebrews quarrelling: and he said to him that did the wrong: Why strikest thou thy neighbour?€€ƒx¶But he answered: Who hath appointed thee prince and judge over us? wilt thou kill me, as thou didst yesterday kill the Egyptian? Moses feared, and said: How is this come to be known?€€ƒxAnd Pharao heard of this word, and sought to kill Moses: but he fled from his sight, and abode in the land of Madian, and he sat down by a well.€€ƒxAnd the priest of Madian had seven daughters, who came to draw water: and when the troughs were filled, desired to water their father's flocks.€€ƒxjAnd the shepherds came and drove them away: and Moses arose, and defending the maids, watered their sheep.€€ƒxbAnd when they returned to Raguel their father, he said to them: Why are ye come sooner than usual?€€ƒxˆThey answered: A man of Egypt delivered us from the hands of the shepherds: and he drew water also with us, and gave the sheep to drink.€€ƒxVBut he said: Where is he? why have you let the man go? call him that he may eat bread.€€ƒxWAnd Moses swore that he would dwell with him. And he took Sephora his daughter to wife:€€ƒxëAnd she bore him a son, whom he called Gersam, saying: I have been a stranger in a foreign country. And she bore another, whom he called Eliezer, saying: For the God of my father, my helper, hath delivered me out of the hand of Pharao.€€ƒx¡Now after a long time the king of Egypt died: and the children of Israel groaning, cried out because of the works: and their cry went up unto God from the works.€€ƒxfAnd he heard their groaning, and remembered the covenant which he made with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.€€ƒxBAnd the Lord looked upon the children of Israel, and he knew them.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¬Now Moses fed the sheep of Jethro, his father in law, the priest of Madian: and he drove the flock to the inner parts of the desert, and came to the mountain of God, Horeb.€€ƒx„And the Lord appeared to him in a flame of fire out of the midst of a bush: and he saw that the bush was on fire, and was not burnt.€€–ƒx•And when the Lord saw that he went forward to see, he called to him out of the midst of the bush. and said: Moses, Moses. And he answered: Here I am.€€ƒxyAnd he said: Come not nigh hither, put off the shoes from thy feet; for the place, whereon thou standest, is holy ground.€€ƒx–And he said: I am the God of thy father, the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob. Moses hid his face: for he durst not look at God.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to him: I have seen the affliction of my people in Egypt, and I have heard their cry because of the rigour of them that are over the works;€€ƒy1And knowing their sorrow, I am come down to deliver them out of the hands of the Egyptians, and to bring them out of that land into a good and spacious land, into a land that floweth with milk and honey, to the places of the Chanaanite, and Hethite, and Amorrhite, and Pherezite, and Hevite, and Jebusite.€€ƒx‡For the cry of the children of Israel is come unto me: and I have seen their affliction, wherewith they are oppressed by the Egyptians.€€ƒxvBut come, and I will send thee to Pharao, that thou mayst bring forth my people, the children of Israel, out of Egypt.€€ƒxwAnd Moses said to God: Who am I that I should go to Pharao, and should bring forth the children of Israel out of Egypt?€€ƒxÎAnd he said to him: I will be with thee; and this thou shalt have for a sign that I have sent thee: When thou shalt have brought my people out of Egypt, thou shalt offer sacrifice to God upon this mountain.€€ƒx¿Moses said to God: Lo, I shall go to the children of Israel, and say to them: The God of your fathers hath sent me to you. If they shall say to me: What is his name? What shall I say to them?€€ƒxwGod said to Moses: I AM WHO AM. He said: Thus shalt thou say to the children of Israel: HE WHO IS, hath sent me to you.€€ƒy␀And God said again to Moses: Thus shalt thou say to the children of Israel: The Lord God of your fathers the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob hath sent me to you; this is my name for ever, and this is my memorial unto all generations.€€ƒyGo and gather together the ancients of Israel, and thou shalt say to them: The Lord God of your fathers, the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob, hath appeared to me, saying: Visiting I have visited you; and I have seen all that hath befallen you in Egypt.€€ƒxİAnd I have said the word to bring you forth out of the affliction of Egypt, into the land of the Chanaanite, and Hethite, and Amorrhite, and Pherezite, and Hevite, and Jebusite, to a land that floweth with milk and honey.€€ƒy
And they shall hear thy voice; and thou shalt go in, thou and the ancients of Israel, to the king of Egypt, and thou shalt say to him: The Lord God of the Hebrews hath called us; we will go three days' journey into the wilderness, to sacrifice unto the Lord our God.€€ƒxLBut I know that the king of Egypt will not let you go, but by a mighty hand.€€ƒx‘For I will stretch forth my hand, and will strike Egypt with all my wonders which I will do in the midst of them: after these he will let you go.€€ƒxxAnd I will give favour to this people, in the sight of the Egyptians: and when you go forth, you shall not depart empty:€€ƒxÂBut every woman shall ask of her neighbour, and of her that is in her house, vessels of silver and of gold, and raiment: and you shall put them on your sons and daughters, and shall spoil Egypt.€€ƒ`€€ƒx}Moses answered, and said: They will not believe me, nor hear my voice, but they will say: The Lord hath not appeared to thee.€€ƒxOThen he said to him: What is that thou holdest in thy hand? He answered: A rod.€€˜ƒx‡And the Lord said: Put out thy hand, and take it by the tail. He put forth his hand, and took hold of it, and it was turned into a rod.€€ƒx—That they may believe, saith he, that the Lord God of their fathers, the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob, hath appeared to thee.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said again: Put thy hand into thy bosom. And when he had put it into his bosom, he brought it forth leprous as snow.€€ƒxyAnd he said: Put back thy hand into thy bosom. He put it back, and brought it out again, and it was like the other flesh.€€ƒx~If they will not believe thee, saith he, nor hear the voice of the former sign, they will believe the word of the latter sign.€€ƒxÌBut if they will not even believe these two signs, nor hear thy voice: take of the river water, and pour it out upon the dry land, and whatsoever thou drawest out of the river, shall be turned into blood.€€ƒx°Moses said: I beseech thee, Lord, I am not eloquent from yesterday and the day before; and since thou hast spoken to thy servant, I have more impediment and slowness of tongue.€€ƒxsThe Lord said to him: Who made man's mouth? or who made the dumb and the deaf, the seeing and the blind? did not I?€€ƒxVGo therefore, and I will be in thy mouth; and I will teach thee what thou shalt speak.€€ƒx<But he said: I beseech thee, Lord, send whom thou wilt send.€€ƒx¯The Lord being angry at Moses, said: Aaron the Levite is thy brother, I know that he is eloquent: behold he cometh forth to meet thee, and seeing thee, shall be glad at heart.€€ƒx~Speak to him, and put my words in his mouth: and I will be in thy mouth, and in his month, and will shew you what you must do.€€ƒx€He shall speak in thy stead to the people, and shall be thy mouth: but thou shalt be to him in those things that pertain to God.€€ƒxAAnd take this rod in thy hand. wherewith thou shalt do the signs.€€ƒxÈMoses went his way, and returned to Jethro his father in law, and said to him; I will go and return to my brethren into Egypt, that I may see if they be yet alive. And Jethro said to him: Go in peace.€€ƒxmAnd the Lord said to Moses, in Madian: Go, and return into Egypt; for they are all dead that sought thy life.€€ƒx„Moses therefore took his wife, and his sons, and set them upon an ass; and returned into Egypt, carrying the rod of God in his hand.€€ƒxÃAnd the Lord said to him as he was returning into Egypt: See that thou do all the wonders before Pharao, which I have put in thy hand: I shall harden his heart, and he will not let the people go.€€ƒxOAnd thou shalt say to him: Thus saith the Lord: Israel is my son, my firstborn.€€ƒx…I have said to thee: Let my son go, that he may serve me, and thou wouldst not let him go: behold I will kill thy son, thy firstborn.€€ƒxXAnd when he was in his journey, in the inn, the Lord met him, and would have killed him.€€ƒx•Immediately Sephora took a very sharp stone, and circumcised the foreskin of her son, and touched his feet, and said: A bloody spouse art thou to me.€€ƒxbAnd he let him go after she had said: A bloody spouse art thou to me, because of the circumcision.€€ƒxƒAnd the Lord said to Aaron: Go into the desert to meet Moses. And he went forth to meet him in the mountain of God, and kissed him.€€ƒxnAnd Moses told Aaron all the words of the Lord, by which he had sent him, and the signs that he had commanded.€€ƒxVAnd they came together, and they assembled all the ancients of the children of Israel.€€ƒxkAnd Aaron spoke all the words which the Lord had said to Moses: and he wrought the signs before the people.€€ƒx¥And the people believed. And they heard that the Lord had visited the children of Israel, and that he had looked upon their affliction: and falling down they adored.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¢After these things, Moses and Aaron went in, and said to Pharao: Thus saith the Lord God of Israel: Let my people go, that they may sacrifice to me in the desert.€€ƒx…But he answered: Who is the Lord, that I should hear his voice, and let Israel go? I know not the Lord, neither will I let Israel go.€€—ƒx~The king of Egypt said to them: Why do you Moses and Aaron draw off the people from their works? Get you gone to your burdens.€€ƒx”And Pharao said: The people of the land are numerous; you see that the multitude is increased; how much more if you give them rest from their works?€€ƒxkTherefore he commanded the same day the overseers of the works, and the task-masters of the people, saying:€€ƒxfYou shall give straw no more to the people to make brick, as before; but let them go and gather straw.€€ƒxËAnd you shall lay upon them the task of bricks, which they did before; neither shall you diminish any thing thereof, for they are idle, and therefore they cry. saying: Let us go and sacrifice to our God.€€ƒxaLet them be oppressed with works, and let them fulfil them; that they may not regard lying words.€€ƒx~And the overseers of the works, and the taskmasters, went out and said to the people: Thus saith Pharao: I allow you no straw;€€ƒx\Go, and gather it where you can find it; neither shall any thing of your work be diminished.€€ƒxKAnd the people was scattered through all the land of Egypt to gather straw.€€ƒxˆAnd the overseers of the works pressed them, saying: Fulfil your work every day, as before ye were wont to do, when straw was given you.€€ƒx¾And they that were over the works of the children of Israel, were scourged by Pharao's taskmasters, saying: Why have you not made up the task of bricks, both yesterday and to day, as before?€€ƒxxAnd the officers of the children of Israel came, and cried out to Pharao, saying: Why dealest thou so with thy servants?€€ƒx˜Straw is not given us, and bricks are required of us as before; behold we, thy servants, are beaten with whips, and thy people is unjustly dealt withal.€€ƒxVAnd he said: You are idle, and therefore you say: Let us go and sacrifice to the Lord.€€ƒxkGo therefore and work: straw shall not be given you, and you shall deliver the accustomed number of bricks.€€ƒxªAnd the officers of the children of Israel saw that they were in evil case, because it was said to them: There shall not a whit be diminished of the bricks for every day.€€ƒxWAnd they met Moses and Aaron, who stood over against them as they came out from Pharao:€€ƒx¥And they said to them: The Lord see and judge, because you have, made our savour to stink before Pharao and his servants, and you have given him a sword, to kill us.€€ƒxqAnd Moses returned to the Lord, and said: Lord, why hast thou afflicted this people? wherefore hast thou sent me?€€ƒxFor since the time that I went in to Pharao to speak in thy name, he hath afflicted thy people: and thou hast not delivered them.€€ƒ`€€ƒx®And the Lord said to Moses: Now thou shalt see what I will do to Pharao: for by a mighty hand shall he let them go, and with a strong hand shall he cast them out of his land.€€ƒx2And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying: I am the Lord€€˜ƒx|And I made a covenant with them, to give them the land of Chanaan, the land of their pilgrimage wherein they were strangers.€€ƒx„I have heard the groaning of the children of Israel, wherewith the Egyptians have oppressed them: and I have remembered my covenant.€€ƒxÌTherefore say to the children of Israel: I am the Lord who will bring you out from the work-prison of the Egyptians, and will deliver you from bondage: and redeem you with a high arm, and great judgments.€€ƒx«And I will take you to myself for my people, I will be your God: and you shall know that I am the Lord your God, who brought you out from the work-prison of the Egyptians:€€ƒxAnd brought you into the land, concerning which I lifted up my hand to give it to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob: and I will give it you to possess: I am the Lord.€€ƒxAnd Moses told all this to the children of Israel: but they did not hearken to him, for anguish of spirit, and most painful work.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx`Go in, and speak to Pharao king of Egypt, that he let the children of Israel go out of his land.€€ƒxšMoses answered before the Lord: Behold the children of Israel do not hearken to me: and how will Pharao hear me, especially as I am of uncircumcised lips?€€ƒxÎAnd the Lord spoke to Moses and Aaron, and he gave them a charge unto the children of Israel, and unto Pharao the king of Egypt, that they should bring forth the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒx‡These are the heads of their houses by their families. The sons of Ruben the firstborn of Israel: Henoch and Phallu, Hesron and Charmi.€€ƒx¬These are the kindreds of Ruben. The sons of Simeon, Jamuel and Jamin, and Ahod, and Jachin, and Soar, and Saul the son of a Chanaanitess: these are the families of Simeon.€€ƒx And these are the names of the sons of Levi by their kindreds: Gerson, and Caath, and Merari. And the years of the life of Levi were a hundred and thirty-seven.€€ƒx7The sons of Gerson: Lobni and Semei, by their kindreds.€€ƒxzThe sons of Caath: Amram, and Isaar, and Hebron and Oziel. And the years of Caath's life, were a hundred and thirty-three.€€ƒxVThe sons of Merari: Moholi and Musi. These are the kindreds of Levi by their families.€€ƒx And Amram took to wife Jochabed his aunt by the father's side: and she bore him Aaron and Moses. And the years of Amram's life, were a hundred and thirty-seven.€€ƒx5The sons also of Isaar: Core, and Nepheg, and Zechri.€€ƒx:The sons also of Oziel: Mizael, and Elizaphan, and Sethri.€€ƒx…And Aaron took to wife Elizabeth the daughter of Aminadab, sister of Nahason, who bore him Nadab, and Abiu, and Eleazar, and Ithamar.€€ƒx]The sons also of Core: Aser, and Elcana, and Abiasaph. These are the kindreds of the Corites.€€ƒx But Eleazar the son of Aaron took a wife of the daughters of Phutiel: and she bore him Phinees. These are the heads of the Levitical families by their kindreds.€€ƒx…These are Aaron and Moses, whom the Lord commanded to bring forth the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt by their companies.€€ƒxŒThese are they that speak to Pharao, king of Egypt, in order to bring out the children of Israel from Egypt: these are that Moses and Aaron,€€ƒx=In the day when the Lord spoke to Moses in the land of Egypt.€€ƒxpAnd the Lord spoke to Moses, saying: I am the Lord; speak thou to Pharao, king of Egypt, all that I say to thee.€€ƒxWAnd Moses said before the Lord: Lo I am of uncircumcised lips, how will Pharao hear me?€€ƒ`€€ƒxzAnd the Lord said to Moses: Behold, I have appointed thee the god of Pharao; and Aaron, thy brother, shall be thy prophet.€€ƒx…Thou shalt speak to him all that I command thee; and he shall speak to Pharao, that he let the children of Israel go out of his land.€€˜ƒx³And he will not hear you: and I will lay my hand upon Egypt, and will bring forth my army and my people, the children of Israel, out of the land of Egypt, by very great judgments.€€ƒx¥And the Egyptians shall know that I am the Lord, who have stretched forth my hand upon Egypt, and have brought forth the children of Israel out of the midst of them.€€ƒx?And Moses and Aaron did as the Lord had commanded; so did they.€€ƒxRAnd Moses was eighty years old, and Aaron eighty-three, when they spoke to Pharao.€€ƒx%And the Lord said to Moses and Aaron:€€ƒx—When Pharao shall say to you, Shew signs; thou shalt say to Aaron: Take thy rod, and cast it down before Pharao, and it shall be turned into a serpent.€€ƒx£So Moses and Aaron went in unto Pharao, and did as the Lord had commanded. And Aaron took the rod before Pharao and his servants, and it was turned into a serpent.€€ƒxAnd Pharao called the wise men and the magicians; and they also by Egyptian enchantments and certain secrets, did in like manner.€€ƒxqAnd they every one cast down their rods, and they were turned into serpents: but Aaron's rod devoured their rods.€€ƒx[And Pharao's heart was hardened, and he did not hearken to them, as the Lord had commanded.€€ƒxVAnd the Lord said to Moses: Pharao's heart is hardened, he will not let the people go.€€ƒxÃGo to him in the morning, behold he will go out to the waters: and thou shalt stand to meet him on the ' bank of the river: and thou shalt take in thy hand the rod that was turned into a serpent.€€ƒx¦And thou shalt say to him: The Lord God of the Hebrews sent me to thee, saying: Let my people go to sacrifice to me in the desert: and hitherto thou wouldst not hear.€€ƒx¼Thus therefore saith the Lord: In this thou shalt know that I am the Lord: behold I will strike with the rod, that is in my hand, the water of the river, and it shall be turned into blood.€€ƒx And the fishes that are in the river, shall die, and the waters shall be corrupted, and the Egyptians shall be afflicted when they drink the water of the river.€€ƒy-The Lord also said to Moses: Say to Aaron, Take thy rod; and stretch forth thy hand upon the waters of Egypt, and upon their rivers, and streams and pools, and all the ponds of waters, that they may be turned into blood: and let blood be in all the land of Egypt, both in vessels of wood and of stone.€€ƒx©And Moses and Aaron did as the Lord had commanded: and lifting up the rod, he struck the water of the river before Pharao and his servants: and it was turned into blood.€€ƒx¬And the fishes that were in the river died; and the river corrupted, and the Egyptians could not drink the water of the river, and there was blood in all the land of Egypt.€€ƒx¤And the magicians of the Egyptians with their enchantments did in like manner; and Pharao's heart was hardened, neither did he hear them, as the Lord had commanded.€€ƒxgAnd he turned himself away, and went into his house, neither did he set his heart to it this time also.€€ƒxwAnd all the Egyptians dug round about the river for water to drink; for they could not drink of the water of the river.€€ƒxFAnd seven days were fully ended, after that the Lord struck the river.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to Moses: Go in to Pharao, and thou shalt say to him: Thus saith the Lord: Let my people go to sacrifice to me.€€ƒxQBut if thou wilt not let them go, behold I will strike all thy coasts with frogs.€€˜ ƒxPAnd the frogs shall come in to thee, and to thy people, and to all thy servants.€€ƒx£And the Lord said to Moses: Say to Aaron: Stretch forth thy hand upon the streams, and upon the rivers and the pools, and bring forth frogs upon the land of Egypt.€€ƒxrAnd Aaron stretched forth his hand upon the waters of Egypt, and the frogs came up, and covered the land of Egypt.€€ƒxwAnd the magicians also, by their enchantments, did in like manner, and they brought forth frogs upon the land of Egypt.€€ƒx²But Pharao called Moses and Aaron, and said to them: Pray ye to the Lord to take away the frogs from me and from my people; and I will let the people go to sacrifice to the Lord.€€ƒxûAnd Moses said to Pharao: Set me a time when I shall pray for thee, and for thy servants, and for thy people, that the frogs may be driven away from thee and from thy house, and from thy servants, and from thy people; and may remain only in the river.€€ƒxŒAnd he answered: To morrow. But he said: I will do according to thy word; that thou mayest know that there is none like to the Lord our God.€€ƒx‰And the frogs shall depart from thee, and from thy house, and from thy servants, and from thy people; and shall remain only in the river.€€ƒxŠAnd Moses and Aaron went forth from Pharao: and Moses cried to the Lord for the promise, which he had made to Pharao concerning the frogs.€€ƒx†And the Lord did according to the word of Moses: and the frogs died out of the houses, and out of the villages, and out of the fields:€€ƒxOAnd they gathered them together into immense heaps, and the land was corrupted.€€ƒxpAnd Pharao seeing that rest was given, hardened his own heart, and did not hear them, as the Lord had commanded.€€ƒx–And the Lord said to Moses: Say to Aaron: Stretch forth thy rod, and strike the dust of the earth; and may there be sciniphs in all the land of Egypt.€€ƒxêAnd they did so. And Aaron stretched forth his hand, holding the rod; and he struck the dust of the earth, and there came sciniphs on men and on beasts: all the dust of the earth was turned into sciniphs through all the land of Egypt.€€ƒx¥And the magicians with their enchantments practised in like manner, to bring forth sciniphs, and they could not: and there were sciniphs as well on men as on beasts.€€ƒx˜And the magicians said to Pharao: This is the finger of God. And Pharao's heart was hardened, and he hearkened not unto them, as the Lord had commanded.€€ƒx»The Lord also said to Moses: Arise early, and stand before Pharao; for he will go forth to the waters: and thou shalt say to him: Thus saith the Lord: Let my people go to sacrifice to me.€€ƒx÷But if thou wilt not let them go, behold I will send in upon thee, and upon thy servants, and upon thy houses, all kind of flies: and the houses of the Egyptians shall be filled with flies of divers kinds, and the whole land wherein they shall be.€€ƒxAnd I will make the land of Gessen wonderful in that day, so that flies shall not be there: and thou shalt know that I am the Lord in the midst of the earth.€€ƒxYAnd I will put a division between my people and thy people: to morrow shall this sign be.€€ƒxÃAnd the Lord did so. And there came a very grievous swarm of flies into the houses of Pharao and of his servants, and into all the land of Egypt: and the land was corrupted by this kind of flies.€€ƒx_And Pharao called Moses and Aaron, and said to them: Go and sacrifice to your God in this land.€€ƒxÎAnd Moses said: It cannot be so: for we shall sacrifice the abominations of the Egyptians to the Lord our God: now if we kill those things which the Egyptians worship, in their presence, they will stone us.€€ƒxwWe will go three days' journey into the wilderness; and we will sacrifice to the Lord our God, as he hath commanded us.€€ƒxwAnd Pharao said: I will let you go to sacrifice to the Lord your God in the wilderness, but go no farther: pray for me.€€ƒxõAnd Moses said: I will go out from thee, and will pray to the Lord: and the flies shall depart from Pharao, and from his servants, and from his people to morrow: but do not deceive any more, in not letting the people go to sacrifice to the Lord.€€ƒx6So Moses went out from Pharao, and prayed to the Lord.€€ƒx˜And he did according to his word: and he took away the flies from Pharao, and from his servants, and from his people: there was not left so much as one.€€ƒxVAnd Pharao's heart was hardened, so that neither this time would he let the people go.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‹And the Lord said to Moses: Go in to Pharao, and speak to him: Thus saith the Lord God of the Hebrews: Let my people go to sacrifice to me.€€ƒx,But if thou refuse, and withhold them still:€€˜#ƒxÊAnd the Lord will make a wonderful difference between the possessions of Israel and the possessions of the Egyptians, that nothing at all shall die of those things that belong to the children of Israel.€€ƒxYAnd the Lord appointed a time, saying: To morrow will the Lord do this thing in the land.€€ƒx™The Lord therefore did this thing the next day: and all the beasts of the Egyptians died, but of the beasts of the children of Israel there died not one.€€ƒx›And Pharao sent to see; and there was not any thing dead of that which Israel possessed. And Pharao's heart was hardened, and he did not let the people go.€€ƒx—And the Lord said to Moses and Aaron: Take to you handfuls of ashes out of the chimney, and let Moses sprinkle it in the air in the presence of Pharao.€€ƒxAnd be there dust upon all the land of Egypt: for there shall be boils and swelling blains both in men and beasts, in the whole land of Egypt.€€ƒx And they took ashes out of the chimney, and stood before Pharao, and Moses sprinkled it in the air; and there came boils with swelling blains in men and beasts.€€ƒxpNeither could the magicians stand before Moses, for the boils that were upon them, and in all the land of Egypt.€€ƒxfAnd the Lord hardened Pharao's heart, and he hearkened not unto them, as the Lord had spoken to Moses.€€ƒx·And the Lord said to Moses: Arise in the morning, and stand before Pharao, and thou shalt say to him: Thus saith the Lord, the God of the Hebrews: Let my people go to sacrifice to me.€€ƒx©For I will at this time send all my plagues upon thy heart, and upon thy servants, and upon thy people; that thou mayst know that there is none like me in all the earth.€€ƒxyFor now I will stretch out my hand to strike thee, and thy people, with pestilence, and thou shalt perish from the earth.€€ƒxzAnd therefore have I raised thee, that I may shew my power in thee, and my name may be spoken of throughout all the earth.€€ƒxADost thou yet hold back my people; and wilt thou not let them go?€€ƒx®Behold I will cause it to rain to morrow at this same hour, an exceeding great hail; such as hath not been in Egypt from the day that it was founded, until this present time.€€ƒxøSend therefore now presently, and gather together thy cattle, and all that thou hast in the field; for men and beasts, and all things that shall be found abroad, and not gathered together out of the fields which the hail shall fall upon, shall die.€€ƒxoHe that feared the word of the Lord among Pharao's servants, made his servants and his cattle flee into houses:€€ƒx_But he that regarded not the word of the Lord, left his servants, and his cattle in the fields.€€ƒxÆAnd the Lord said to Moses: Stretch forth thy hand towards heaven, that there may be hail in the whole land of Egypt upon men, and upon beasts, and upon every herb of the field in the land of Egypt.€€ƒx¯And Moses stretched forth his rod towards heaven, and the Lord sent thunder and hail, and lightnings running along the ground: and the Lord rained hail upon the land of Egypt.€€ƒx¨And the hail and fire mixt with it drove on together: and it was of so great bigness, as never before was seen in the whole land of Egypt since that nation was founded.€€ƒxÀAnd the hail destroyed through all the land of Egypt all things that were in the fields, both man and beast: and the hail smote every herb of the field, and it broke every tree of the country.€€ƒxQOnly in the land of Gessen, where the children of Israel were, the hail fell not.€€ƒxˆAnd Pharao sent and called Moses and Aaron, saying to them: I have sinned this time also, the Lord is just: I and my people, are wicked.€€ƒxƒPray ye to the Lord that the thunderings of God and the hail may cease: that I may let you go, and that ye may stay here no longer.€€ƒxÌMoses said: As soon as I am gone out of the city, I will stretch forth my hands to the Lord, and the thunders shall cease, and the hail shall be no more: that thou mayst know that the earth is the Lord's:€€ƒxHBut I know that neither thou, nor thy servants do yet fear the Lord God.€€ƒxhThe flax therefore, and the barley were hurt, because the barley was green, and the flax was now bolled;€€ƒxOBut the wheat, and other winter corn were not hurt, because they were lateward.€€ƒx¹And when Moses was gone from Pharao out of the city, he stretched forth his hands to the Lord: and the thunders and the hail ceased, neither did there drop any more rain upon the earth.€€ƒx_And Pharao seeing that the rain, and the hail, and the thunders were ceased, increased his sin:€€ƒx¸And his heart was hardened, and the heart of his servants, and it was made exceeding hard: neither did he let the children of Israel go, as the Lord had commanded by the hand of Moses.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‘And the Lord said to Moses: Go in to Pharao; for I have hardened his heart, and the heart of his servants: that I may work these my signs in him,€€ƒx²And thou mayst tell in the ears of thy sons, and of thy grandsons, how often I have plagued the Egyptians, and wrought my signs amongst them: and you may know that I am the Lord.€€˜ƒxkBut if thou resist, and wilt not let them go, behold I will bring in to-morrow the locusts into thy coasts;€€ƒx²To cover the face of the earth, that nothing thereof may appear, but that which the hail hath left may be eaten: for they shall feed upon all the trees that spring in the fields.€€ƒyAnd they shall fill thy houses, and the houses of thy servants, and of all the Egyptians: such a number as thy fathers have not seen, nor thy grandfathers, from the time they were first upon the earth, until this present day. And he turned himself away, and went forth from Pharao.€€ƒx¤And Pharao's servants said to him: How long shall we endure this scandal? Iet the men go to sacrifice to the Lord their God. Dost thou not see that Egypt is undone?€€ƒx†And they called back Moses, and Aaron, to Pharao; and he said to them: Go, sacrifice to the Lord your God: who are they that shall go?€€ƒx’Moses said: We will go with our young and old, with our sons and daughters, with our sheep and herds: for it is the solemnity of the Lord our God.€€ƒx‰And Pharao answered: So be the Lord with you, as I shall let you and your children go: who can doubt but that you intend some great evil?€€ƒxŸIt shall not be so. but go ye men only, and sacrifice to the Lord: for this yourselves also desired. And immediately they were cast out from Pharao's presence.€€ƒx£And the Lord said to Moses: Stretch forth thy hand upon the land of Egypt unto the locust, that it come upon it, and devour every herb that is left after the hail.€€ƒx´And Moses stretched forth his rod upon the land of Egypt: and the Lord brought a burning wind all that day, and night; and when it was morning, the burning wind raised the locusts.€€ƒx­And they came up over the whole land of Egypt; and rested in all the coasts of the Egyptians, innumerable, the like as had not been before that time, nor shall be hereafter.€€ƒyAnd they covered the whole face of the earth, wasting all things. And the grass of the earth was devoured, and what fruits soever were on the trees, which the hail had left; and there remained not any thing that was green on the trees, or in the herbs of the earth, in all Egypt.€€ƒx}Wherefore Pharao in haste called Moses and Aaron, and said to them: I have sinned against the Lord your God, and against you.€€ƒxnBut now forgive me my sin this time also, and pray to the Lord your God, that he take away from me this death.€€ƒxFAnd Moses going forth from the presence of Pharao, prayed to the Lord:€€ƒx«And he made a very strong wind to blow from the west, and it took the locusts and cast them into the Red Sea: there remained not so much as one in all the coasts of Egypt.€€ƒxSAnd the Lord hardened Pharao's heart, neither did he let the children of Israel go.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to Moses: Stretch out thy hand towards heaven: and may there be darkness upon the land of Egypt so thick that it may be felt.€€ƒx|And Moses stretched forth his hand towards heaven: and there came horrible darkness in all the land of Egypt for three days.€€ƒx‡No man saw his brother, nor moved himself out of the place where he was: but wheresoever the children of Israel dwelt, there was light.€€ƒx•And Pharao called Moses and Aaron, and said to them: Go, sacrifice to the Lord: let your sheep only, and herds remain, let your children go with you.€€ƒxXMoses said: Thou shalt give us also sacrifices and burnt-offerings, to the Lord our God.€€ƒxÓAll the flocks shall go with us; there shall not a hoof remain of them: for they are necessary for the service of the Lord our God: especially as we know not what must be offered, till we come to the very place.€€ƒxCAnd the Lord hardened Pharao's heart, and he would not let them go.€€ƒx•And Pharao said to Moses: Get thee from me, and beware thou see not my face any more: in what day soever thou shalt come in my sight, thou shalt die.€€ƒxTMoses answered: So shall it be as thou hast spoken, I will not see thy face anymore.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‹And the Lord said to Moses: Yet one plague more will I bring upon Pharao and Egypt, and after that he shall let you go, and thrust you out.€€ƒxŒTherefore thou shalt tell all the people, that every man ask of his friend, and every woman of her neighbour, vessels of silver and of gold.€€ŠƒxFAnd he said: Thus saith the Lord: At midnight I will enter into Egypt:€€ƒxÏAnd every firstborn in the land of the Egyptians shall die, from the firstborn of Pharao who sitteth on his throne, even to the firstborn of the handmaid that is at the mill, and all the firstborn of beasts.€€ƒxrAnd there shall be a great cry in all the land of Egypt, such as neither hath been before, nor shall be hereafter.€€ƒxÆBut with all the children of Israel there shall not a dog make the least noise, from man even to beast; that you may know how wonderful a difference the Lord maketh between the Egyptians and Israel.€€ƒx And all these thy servants shall come down to me, and shall worship me, saying: Go forth thou, and all the people that is under thee: after that we will go out.€€ƒx”And he went out from Pharao exceeding angry. But the Lord said to Moses: Pharao will not hear you, that many signs may be done in the land of Egypt.€€ƒx¬And Moses and Aaron did all the wonders that are written, before Pharao. And the Lord hardened Pharao's heart, neither did he let the children of Israel go out of his land.€€ƒ`€€ƒx:And the Lord said to Moses and Aaron in the land of Egypt:€€ƒxdThis month shall be to you the beginning of months; it shall be the first in the months of the year.€€˜3ƒxÁBut if the number be less than may suffice to eat the lamb, he shall take unto him his neighbour that joineth to his house, according to the number of souls which may be enough to eat the lamb.€€ƒxoAnd it shall be a lamb without blemish, a male, of one year; according to which rite also you shall take a kid.€€ƒx’And you shall keep it until the fourteenth day of this month; and the whole multitude of the children of Israel shall sacrifice it in the evening.€€ƒx”And they shall take of the blood thereof, and put it upon both the side posts, and on the upper door posts of the houses, wherein they shall eat it.€€ƒxdAnd they shall eat the flesh that night roasted at the fire, and unleavened bread with wild lettuce.€€ƒx–You shall not eat thereof any thing raw, nor boiled in water, but only roasted at the fire; you shall eat the head with the feet and entrails thereof.€€ƒxrNeither shall there remain any thing of it until morning. If there be any thing left, you shall burn it with fire.€€ƒxÍAnd thus you shall eat it: you shall gird your reins, and you shall have shoes on your feet, holding staves in your hands, and you shall eat in haste; for it is the Phase (that is the Passage) of the Lord.€€ƒxÈAnd I will pass through the land of Egypt that night, and will kill every firstborn in the land of Egypt, both man and beast: and against all the gods of Egypt I will execute judgments; I am the Lord.€€ƒxÜAnd the blood shall be unto you for a sign in the houses where you shall be; and I shall see the blood, and shall pass over you; and the plague shall not be upon you to destroy you, when I shall strike the land of Egypt.€€ƒx‹And this day shall be for a memorial to you; and you shall keep it a feast to the Lord in your generations, with an everlasting observance.€€ƒxÜSeven days shall you eat unleavened bread: in the first day there shall be no leaven in your houses; whosoever shall eat any thing leavened, from the first day until the seventh day, that soul shall perish out of Israel.€€ƒx«The first day shall be holy and solemn, and the seventh day shall be kept with the like solemnity: you shall do no work in them, except those things that belong to eating.€€ƒxÏAnd you shall observe the feast of the unleavened bread: for in this same day I will bring forth your army out of the land of Egypt, and you shall keep this day in your generations by a perpetual observance.€€ƒx¤The first month, the fourteenth day of the month, in the evening, you shall eat unleavened bread, until the one and twentieth day of the same month, in the evening.€€ƒxÃSeven days there shall not be found any leaven in your houses: he that shall eat leavened bread, his soul shall perish out of the assembly of Israel, whether he be a stranger or born in the land.€€ƒx]You shall not eat any thing leavened: in all your habitations you shall eat unleavened bread.€€ƒxˆAnd Moses called all the ancients of the children of Israel, and said to them: Go take a lamb by your families, and sacrifice the Phase.€€ƒxÃAnd dip a bunch of hyssop in the blood that is at the door, and sprinkle the transom of the door therewith, and both the door cheeks: let none of you go out of the door of his house till morning.€€ƒxíFor the Lord will pass through striking the Egyptians: and when he shall see the blood on the transom, and on both the posts, he will pass over the door of the house, and not suffer the destroyer to come into your houses and to hurt you.€€ƒxGThou shalt keep this thing as a law for thee and thy children for ever.€€ƒx~And when you have entered into the land which the Lord will give you, as he hath promised, you shall observe these ceremonies.€€ƒxMAnd when your children shall say to you: What is the meaning of this service?€€ƒxßYou shall say to them: It is the victim of the passage of the Lord, when he passed over the houses of the children of Israel in Egypt, striking the Egyptians, and saving our houses. And the people bowing themselves, adored.€€ƒxVAnd the children of Israel going forth, did as the Lord had commanded Moses and Aaron.€€ƒxêAnd it came to pass at midnight, the Lord slew every firstborn in the land of Egypt, from the firstborn of Pharao, who sat on his throne, unto the firstborn of the captive woman that was in the prison, and all the firstborn of cattle.€€ƒx£And Pharao arose in the night, and all his servants, and all Egypt: and there arose a great cry in Egypt; for there was not a house wherein there lay not one dead.€€ƒx¦And Pharao calling Moses and Aaron, in the night, said: Arise and go forth from among my people, you and the children of Israel: go, sacrifice to the Lord as you say.€€ƒxRYour sheep and herds take along with you, as you demanded, and departing bless me.€€ƒxdAnd the Egyptians pressed the people to go forth out of the land speedily, saying: We shall all die.€€ƒxpThe people therefore took dough before it was leavened; and tying it in their cloaks, put it on their shoulders.€€ƒx‰And the children of Israel did as Moses had commanded: and they asked of the Egyptians vessels of silver and gold, and very much raiment.€€ƒxƒAnd the Lord gave favour to the people in the sight of the Egyptians, so that they lent unto them: and they stripped the Egyptians.€€ƒx}And the children of Israel set forward from Ramesse to Socoth, being about six hundred thousand men on foot, beside children.€€ƒx{And a mixed multitude, without number, went up also with them, sheep and herds, and beasts of divers kinds, exceeding many.€€ƒyAnd they baked the meal, which a little before they had brought out of Egypt in dough: and they made hearth cakes unleavened: for it could not be leavened, the Egyptians pressing them to depart, and not suffering them to make any stay; neither did they think of preparing any meat.€€ƒxcAnd the abode of the children of Israel that they made in Egypt, was four hundred and thirty years.€€ƒx_Which being expired, the same day all the army of the Lord went forth out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒx§This is the observable night of the Lord, when he brought them forth out of the land of Egypt: this night all the children of Israel must observe in their generations.€€ƒxeAnd the Lord said to Moses and Aaron: This is the service of the Phase; no foreigner shall eat of it.€€ƒx@But every bought servant shall be circumcised, and so shall eat.€€ƒx4The stranger and the hireling shall not eat thereof.€€ƒxŒIn one house shall it be eaten, neither shall you carry forth of the flesh thereof out of the house, neither shall you break a bone thereof.€€ƒx9All the assembly of the children of Israel shall keep it.€€ƒy"And if any stranger be willing to dwell among you, and to keep the Phase of the Lord, all his males shall first be circumcised, and then shall he celebrate it according to the manner: and he shall be as he that is born in the land: but if any man be uncircumcised, he shall not eat thereof.€€ƒxeThe same law shall be to him that is born in the land, and to the proselyte that sojourneth with you.€€ƒxMAnd all the children of Israel did as the Lord had commanded Moses and Aaron.€€ƒxkAnd the same day the Lord brought forth the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt by their companies.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxˆSanctify unto me every firstborn that openeth the womb among the children of Israel, as well of men as of beasts: for they are all mine.€€–ƒx/This day you go forth in the month of new corn.€€ƒy0And when the Lord shall have brought thee into the land of the Chanaanite, and the Hethite, and the Amorrhite, and the Hevite, and the Jebusite, which he swore to thy fathers that he would give thee, a land that floweth with milk and honey, thou shalt celebrate this manner of sacred rites in this month.€€ƒxfSeven days shalt thou eat unleavened bread: and on the seventh day shall be the solemnity of the Lord.€€ƒxwUnleavened bread shall you eat seven days: there shall not be seen any thing leavened with thee, nor in all thy coasts.€€ƒxpAnd thou shalt tell thy son in that day, saying: This is what the Lord did to me when I came forth out of Egypt.€€ƒxÊAnd it shall be as a sign in thy hand, and as a memorial before thy eyes; and that the law of the Lord be always in thy mouth, for with a strong hand the Lord hath brought thee out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒxBThou shalt keep this observance at the set time from days to days.€€ƒx‡And when the Lord shall have brought thee into the land of the Chanaanite, as he swore to thee and thy fathers, and shall give it thee:€€ƒx¾Thou shalt set apart all that openeth the womb for the Lord, and all that is first brought forth of thy cattle: whatsoever thou shalt have of the male sex, thou shalt consecrate to the Lord.€€ƒx£The firstborn of an ass thou shalt change for a sheep: and if thou do not redeem it, thou shalt kill it. And every firstborn of men thou shalt redeem with a price.€€ƒx½And when thy son shall ask thee to morrow, saying: What is this? thou shalt answer him: With a strong hand did the Lord bring us forth out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage.€€ƒyFor when Pharao was hardened, and would not let us go, the Lord slew every firstborn in the land of Egypt, from the firstborn of man to the firstborn of beasts: therefore I sacrifice to the Lord all that openeth the womb of the male sex, and all the firstborn of my sons I redeem.€€ƒx¥And it shall be as a sign in thy hand, and as a thing hung between thy eyes, for a remembrance: because the Lord hath brought us forth out of Egypt by a strong hand.€€ƒxêAnd when Pharao had sent out the people, the Lord led them not by the way of the land of the Philistines, which is near; thinking lest perhaps they would repent, if they should see wars arise against them, and would return into Egypt.€€ƒx‹But he led them about by the way of the desert, which is by the Red Sea: and the children of Israel went up armed out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒx›And Moses took Joseph's bones with him: because he had adjured the children of Israel, saying: God shall visit you, carry out my bones from hence with you.€€ƒxYAnd marching from Socoth, they encamped in Etham, in the utmost coasts of the wilderness.€€ƒxªAnd the Lord went before them to shew the way, by day in a pillar of a cloud, and by night in a pillar of fire; that he might be the guide of their journey at both times.€€ƒxfThere never failed the pillar of the cloud by day, nor the pillar of fire by night, before the people.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx¹Speak to the children of Israel: Let them turn and encamp over against Phihahiroth, which is between Magdal and the sea over against Beelsephon: you shall encamp before it upon the sea.€€˜ƒx¯And I shall harden his heart and he will pursue you: and I shall be glorified in Pharao, and in all his army: and the Egyptians shall know that I am the Lord. And they did so.€€ƒxáAnd it was told the king of the Egyptians that the people was fled: and the heart of Pharao and of his servants was changed with regard to the people, and they said: What meant we to do, that we let Israel go from serving us?€€ƒx?So he made ready his chariot, and took all his people with him.€€ƒxuAnd he took six hundred chosen chariots, and all the chariots that were in Egypt: and the captains of the whole army.€€ƒx‹And the Lord hardened the heart of Pharao, king of Egypt, and he pursued the children of Israel; but they were gone forth in a mighty hand.€€ƒxÌAnd when the Egyptians followed the steps of them who were gone before, they found them encamped at the sea side: all Pharao's horse and chariots and the whole army were in Phihahiroth, before Beelsephon.€€ƒx›And when Pharao drew near, the children of Israel lifting up their eyes, saw the Egyptians behind them: and they feared exceedingly, and cried to the Lord.€€ƒxªAnd they said to Moses: Perhaps there were no graves in Egypt, therefore thou hast brought us to die in the wilderness: why wouldst thou do this, to lead us out of Egypt?€€ƒx²Is not this the word that we spoke to thee in Egypt, saying: Depart from us, that we may serve the Egyptians? for it was much better to serve them, than to die in the wilderness.€€ƒxµAnd Moses said to the people: Fear not: stand, and see the great wonders of the Lord, which he will do this day; for the Egyptians, whom you see now, you shall see no more for ever.€€ƒx;The Lord will fight for you, and you shall hold your peace.€€ƒxaAnd the Lord said to Moses: Why criest thou to me? Speak to the children of Israel to go forward.€€ƒx But lift thou up thy rod, and stretch forth thy hand over the sea, and divide it: that the children of Israel may go through the midst of the sea on dry ground.€€ƒxœAnd I will harden the heart of the Egyptians to pursue you: and I will be glorified in Pharao, and in all his host, and in his chariots and in his horsemen.€€ƒxAnd the Egyptians shall know that I am the Lord, when I shall be glorified in Pharao, and in his chariots, and in his horsemen.€€ƒxšAnd the angel of God, who went before the camp of Israel, removing, went behind them: and together with him the pillar of the cloud, leaving the forepart,€€ƒx°Stood behind, between the Egyptians' camp and the camp of Israel: and it was a dark cloud, and enlightening the night, so that they could not come at one another all the night.€€ƒx½And when Moses had stretched forth his hand over the sea, the Lord took it away by a strong and burning wind blowing all the night, and turned it into dry ground: and the water was divided.€€ƒxŒAnd the children of Israel went in through the midst of the sea dried up; for the water was as a wall on their right hand and on their left.€€ƒx€And the Egyptians pursuing went in after them, and all Pharao's horses, his chariots and horsemen, through the midst of the sea.€€ƒx”And now the morning watch was come, and behold the Lord looking upon the Egyptian army through the pillar of fire and of the cloud, slew their host.€€ƒxªAnd overthrew the wheels of the chariots, and they were carried into the deep. And the Egyptians said: Let us flee from Israel; for the Lord fighteth for them against us.€€ƒx•And the Lord said to Moses: Stretch forth thy hand over the sea, that the waters may come again upon the Egyptians, upon their chariots and horsemen.€€ƒxğAnd when Moses had stretched forth his hand towards the sea, it returned at the first break of day to the former place: and as the Egyptians were fleeing away, the waters came upon them, and the Lord shut them up in the middle of the waves.€€ƒx´And the waters returned, and covered the chariots and the horsemen of all the army of Pharao, who had come into the sea after them, neither did there so much as one of them remain.€€ƒx—But the children of Israel marched through the midst of the sea upon dry land, and the waters were to them as a wall on the right hand and on the left:€€ƒxLAnd the Lord delivered Israel in that day out of the hands of the Egyptians.€€ƒx¿And they saw the Egyptians dead upon the sea shore, and the mighty hand that the Lord had used against them: and the people feared the Lord, and they believed the Lord, and Moses his servant.€€ƒ`€€ƒx½Then Moses and the children of Israel sung this canticle to the Lord, and said: Let us sing to the Lord: for he is gloriously magnified, the horse and the rider he hath thrown into the sea.€€ƒxšThe Lord is my strength and my praise, and he is become salvation to me: he is my God, and I will glorify him: the God of my father, and I will exalt him.€€˜ƒxiPharao's chariots and his army he hath cast into the sea: his chosen captains are drowned in the Red Sea.€€ƒxGThe depths have covered them, they are sunk to the bottom like a stone.€€ƒx_Thy right hand, O Lord, is magnified in strength: thy right hand, O Lord, hath slain the enemy.€€ƒx†And in the multitude of thy glory thou hast put down thy adversaries: thou hast sent thy wrath, which hath devoured them like stubble.€€ƒx–And with the blast of thy anger the waters were gathered together: the flowing water stood, the depths were gathered together in the midst of the sea.€€ƒx‘The enemy said: I will pursue and overtake, I will divide the spoils, my soul shall have its fill: I will draw my sword, my hand shall slay them.€€ƒxOThy wind blew and the sea covered them: they sunk as lead in the mighty waters.€€ƒx„Who is like to thee, among the strong, O Lord? who is like to thee, glorious in holiness, terrible and praise-worthy, doing wonders?€€ƒx>Thou stretchedst forth thy hand, and the earth swallowed them.€€ƒxIn thy mercy thou hast been a leader to the people which thou hast redeemed: and in thy strength thou hast carried them to thy holy habitation.€€ƒxUNations rose up, and were angry: sorrows took hold on the inhabitants of Philisthiim.€€ƒxThen were the princes of Edom troubled, trembling seized on the stout men of Moab: all the inhabitants of Chanaan became stiff.€€ƒxÃLet fear and dread fall upon them, in the greatness of thy arm: let them become immoveable as a stone, until thy people, O Lord, pass by: until this thy people pass by, which thou hast possessed.€€ƒxÀThou shalt bring them in, and plant them in the mountain of thy inheritance, in thy most firm habitation, which thou hast made, O Lord; thy sanctuary, O Lord, which thy hands have established.€€ƒx'The Lord shall reign for ever and ever.€€ƒxÍFor Pharao went in on horseback with his chariots and horsemen into the sea: and the Lord brought back upon them the waters of the sea: but the children of Israel walked on dry ground in the midst thereof.€€ƒxSo Mary the prophetess, the sister of Aaron, took a timbrel in her hand: and all the women went forth after her with timbrels and with dances.€€ƒx•And she began the song to them, saying: Let us sing to the Lord, for he is gloriously magnified, the horse and his rider he hath thrown into the sea.€€ƒx¢And Moses brought Israel from the Red Sea, and they went forth into the wilderness of Sur: and they marched three days through the wilderness, and found no water.€€ƒxºAnd they came into Mara, and they could not drink the waters of Mara because they were bitter: whereupon he gave a name also agreeable to the place, calling it Mara, that is, bitterness.€€ƒxCAnd the people murmured against Moses, saying: What shall we drink?€€ƒxÇBut he cried to the Lord, and he shewed him a tree, which when he had cast into the waters, they were turned into sweetness. There he appointed him ordinances, and judgments, and there he proved him,€€ƒxïSaying: If thou wilt hear the voice of the Lord thy God, and do what is right before him, and obey his commandments, and keep all his precepts, none of the evils that I laid upon Egypt, will I bring upon thee: for I am the Lord thy healer.€€ƒxAnd the children of Israel came into Elim, where there were twelve fountains of water, and seventy palm trees: and they encamped by the waters.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŞAnd they set forward from Elim, and all the multitude of the children of Israel came into the desert of Sin, which is between Elim and Sinai: the fifteenth day of the second month, after they came out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒxfAnd all the congregation of the children of Israel murmured against Moses and Aaron in the wilderness.€€˜$ƒxÏAnd the Lord said to Moses: Behold I will rain bread from heaven for you; let the people go forth, and gather what is sufficient for every day: that I may prove them whether they will walk in my law, or not.€€ƒxtBut the sixth day let them provide for to bring in: and let it be double to that they were wont to gather every day.€€ƒxAnd Moses and Aaron said to the children of Israel In the evening you shall know that the Lord hath brought you forth out of the land of Egypt:€€ƒx¢And in the morning you shall see the glory of the Lord: for he hath heard your murmuring against the Lord: but as for us, what are we, that you mutter against us?€€ƒxÿAnd Moses said: In the evening the Lord will give you flesh to eat, and in the morning bread to the full: for he hath heard your murmurings, with which you have murmured against him, for what are we? your murmuring is not against us, but against the Lord.€€ƒxŠMoses also said to Aaron: Say to the whole congregation of the children of Israel: Come before the Lord; for he hath heard your murmuring.€€ƒxAnd when Aaron spoke to all the assembly of the children of Israel, they looked towards the wilderness; and behold the glory of the Lord appeared in a cloud.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxÌI have heard the murmuring of the children of Israel, say to them: In the evening you shall eat flesh, and in the morning you shall have your fill of bread; and you shall know that I am the Lord your God.€€ƒx~So it came to pass in the evening, that quails coming up, covered the camp: and in the morning a dew lay round about the camp.€€ƒx And when it had covered the face of the earth, it appeared in the wilderness small, and as it were beaten with a pestle, like unto the hoar frost on the ground.€€ƒx×And when the children of Israel saw it, they said one to another: Manhu! which signifieth: What is this! for they knew not what it was. And Moses said to them: This is the bread which the Lord hath given you to eat.€€ƒxÒThis is the word that the Lord hath commanded: Let every one gather of it as much as is enough to eat; a gomor for every man, according to the number of your souls that dwell in a tent, so shall you take of it.€€ƒxMAnd the children of Israel did so: and they gathered, one more, another less.€€ƒxÊAnd they measured by the measure of a gomor: neither had he more that had gathered more; nor did he find less that had provided less: but every one had gathered, according to what they were able to eat.€€ƒxBAnd Moses said to them: Let no man leave thereof till the morning.€€ƒxšAnd they hearkened not to him, but some of them left until the morning, and it began to be full of worms, and it putrified, and Moses was angry with them.€€ƒxvNow every one of them gathered in the morning, as much as might suffice to eat: and after the sun grew hot, it melted.€€ƒxŠBut on the sixth day they gathered twice as much, that is, two gomors every man: and all the rulers of the multitude came, and told Moses.€€ƒyAnd he said to them: This is what the Lord hath spoken: To morrow is the rest of the sabbath sanctified to the Lord. Whatsoever work is to be done, do it; and the meats that are to be dressed, dress them; and whatsoever shall remain, lay it up until the morning.€€ƒxcAnd they did so as Moses had commanded, and it did not putrify, neither was there worm found in it.€€ƒxpAnd Moses said: Eat it to day, because it is the sabbath of the Lord: to day it shall not be found in the field.€€ƒxgGather it six days; but on the seventh day is the sabbath of the Lord, therefore it shall not be found.€€ƒxSAnd the seventh day came; and some of the people going forth to gather, found none.€€ƒxYAnd the Lord said to Moses: How long will you refuse to keep my commandments, and my law?€€ƒxÇSee that the Lord hath given you the sabbath, and for this reason on the sixth day he giveth you a double provision: let each man stay at home, and let none go forth out of his place the seventh day.€€ƒx3And the people kept the sabbath on the seventh day.€€ƒxAnd the house of Israel called the name thereof Manna: and it was like coriander seed, white, and the taste thereof like to flour with honey.€€ƒyAnd Moses said: This is the word which the Lord hath commanded: Fill a gomor of it, and let it be kept unto generations to come hereafter; that they may know the bread, wherewith I fed you in the wilderness when you were brought forth out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒx™And Moses said to Aaron: Take a vessel, and put manna into it, as much as a gomor can hold; and lay it up before the Lord, to keep unto your generations,€€ƒxKAs the Lord commanded Moses. And Aaron put it in the tabernacle to be kept.€€ƒxªAnd the children of Israel ate manna forty years, till they came to a habitable land: with this meat were they fed, until they reached the borders of the land of Chanaan.€€ƒx)Now a gomor is the tenth part of an ephi.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÖThen all the multitude of the children of Israel setting forward from the desert of Sin, by their mansions, according to the word of the Lord, encamped in Raphidim, where there was no water for the people to drink.€€ƒx—And they chode with Moses, and said: Give us water, that we may drink. And Moses answered them: Why chide you with me? Wherefore do you tempt the Lord?€€ƒxnAnd Moses cried to the Lord, saying: What shall I do to this people? Yet a little more and they will stone me.€€ƒx«And the Lord said to Moses: Go before the people, and take with thee of the ancients of Israel: and take in thy hand the rod wherewith thou didst strike the river, and go.€€ƒxÁBehold I will stand there before thee, upon the rock Horeb, and thou shalt strike the rock, and water shall come out of it that the people may drink. Moses did so before the ancients of Israel:€€ƒx­And he called the name of that place Temptation, because of the chiding of the children of Israel, and for that they tempted the Lord, saying: Is the Lord amongst us or not?€€ƒx7And Amalec came, and fought against Israel in Raphidim.€€ƒxAnd Moses said to Josue: Choose out men; and go out and fight against Amalec: tomorrow I will stand on the top of the hill, having the rod of God in my hand.€€ƒx}Josue did as Moses had spoken, and he fought against Amalec; but Moses, and Aaron, and Hur, went up upon the top of the hill.€€ƒxgAnd when Moses lifted up his hands, Israel overcame; but if he let them down a little, Amalec overcame.€€ƒxÎAnd Moses's hands were heavy: so they took a stone, and put under him, and he sat on it: and Aaron and Hur stayed up his hands on both sides. And it came to pass, that his hands were not weary until sunset.€€ƒxHAnd Josue put Amalec and his people to flight, by the edge of the sword.€€ƒx And the Lord said to Moses: Write this for a memorial in a book, and deliver it to the ears of Josue; for I will destroy the memory of Amalec from under heaven.€€ƒxWAnd Moses built an altar; and called the name thereof, The Lord, my exaltation, saying:€€ƒx{Because the hand of the throne of the Lord, and the war of the Lord shall be against Amalec, from generation to generation.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÃAnd when Jethro the priest of Madian, the kinsman of Moses, had heard all the things that God had done to Moses, and to Israel his people, and that the Lord had brought forth Israel out of Egypt:€€ƒx:He took Sephora, the wife of Moses, whom he had sent back:€€˜ƒxwAnd the other Eliezer: For the God of my father, said he, is my helper, and hath delivered me from the sword of Pharao.€€ƒxˆAnd Jethro, the kinsman of Moses, came with his sons, and his wife to Moses into the desert, where he was camped by the mountain of God.€€ƒxpAnd he sent word to Moses, saying: I Jethro, thy kinsman, come to thee, and thy wife, and thy two sons with her.€€ƒx™And he went out to meet his kinsman, and worshipped and kissed him: and they saluted one another with words of peace. And when he was come into the tent,€€ƒxÄMoses told his kinsman all that the Lord had done to Pharao, and the Egyptians in favour of Israel: and all the labour which had befallen them in the journey, and that the Lord had delivered them.€€ƒxAnd Jethro rejoiced for all the good things that the Lord had done to Israel, because he had delivered them out of the hands of the Egyptians.€€ƒxYAnd he said: Blessed is the Lord, who hath delivered his people out of the hand of Egypt.€€ƒx[Now I know, that the Lord is great above all gods; because they dealt proudly against them.€€ƒx›So Jethro, the kinsman of Moses, offered holocausts and sacrifices to God: and Aaron and all the ancients of Israel came, to eat bread with him before God.€€ƒx\And the next day Moses sat to judge the people, who stood by Moses from morning until night.€€ƒxÄAnd when his kinsman had seen all things that he did among the people, he said: What is it that thou dost among the people? Why sittest thou alone, and all the people wait from morning till night?€€ƒxJAnd Moses answered him: The people come to me to seek the judgment of God?€€ƒx†And when any controversy falleth out among them, they come to me to judge between them, and to shew the precepts of God, and his laws.€€ƒx-But he said: The thing thou dost is not good.€€ƒx“Thou art spent with foolish labour, both thou, and this people that is with thee; the business is above thy strength, thou alone canst not bear it.€€ƒx“But hear my words and counsels, and God shall be with thee. Be thou to the people in those things that pertain to God, to bring their words to him:€€ƒx‘And to shew the people the ceremonies, and the manner of worshipping; and the way wherein they ought to walk, and the work that they ought to do.€€ƒxÃAnd provide out of all the people able men, such as fear God, in whom there is truth, and that hate avarice, and appoint of them rulers of thousands, and of hundreds, and of fifties, and of tens,€€ƒxëWho may judge the people at all times: and when any great matter soever shall fall out, let them refer it to thee, and let them judge the lesser matters only: that so it may be lighter for thee, the burden being shared out unto others.€€ƒx¡If thou dost this, thou shalt fulfil the commandment of God, and shalt be able to bear his precepts: and all this people shall return to their places with peace.€€ƒxLAnd when Moses heard this, he did all things that he had suggested unto him.€€ƒx›And choosing able men out of all Israel, he appointed them rulers of the people, rulers over thousands, and over hundreds, and over fifties, and over tens.€€ƒxAnd they judged the people at all times: and whatsoever was of greater difficulty they referred to him, and they judged the easier cases only.€€ƒxMAnd he let his kinsman depart: and he returned and went into his own country.€€ƒ`€€ƒx{In the third month of the departure of Israel out of the land of Egypt, on this day they came into the wilderness of Sinai:€€ƒx For departing out of Raphidim, and coming to the desert of Sinai, they camped in the same place, and there Israel pitched their tents over against the mountain.€€˜ƒxYou have seen what I have done to the Egyptians, how I have carried you upon the wings of eagles, and have taken you to myself.€€ƒx‹If therefore you will hear my voice, and keep my covenant, you shall be my peculiar possession above all people: for all the earth is mine.€€ƒx}And you shall be to me a priestly kingdom, and a holy nation. These are the words thou shalt speak to the children of Israel.€€ƒxrMoses came; and calling together the elders of the people, he declared all the words which the Lord had commanded.€€ƒx‹And all the people answered together: All that the Lord hath spoken, we will do. And when Moses had related the people's words to the Lord,€€ƒxÎThe Lord said to him: Lo, now will I come to thee in the darkness of a cloud, that the people may hear me speaking to thee, and may believe thee for ever. And Moses told the words of the people to the Lord.€€ƒxpAnd he said to him: Go to the people, and sanctify them to day, and to morrow, and let them wash their garments.€€ƒx‹And let them be ready against the third day; for on the third day the Lord will come down in the sight of all the people, upon Mount Sinai.€€ƒxåAnd thou shalt appoint certain limits to the people round about, and thou shalt say to them: Take heed ye go not up into the mount, and that ye touch not the borders thereof: every one that toucheth the mount, dying he shall die.€€ƒxŞNo hands shall touch him, but he shall be stoned to death, or he shall be shot through with arrows: whether it be beast, or man, he shall not live. When the trumpet shall begin to sound, then let them go up into the mount.€€ƒxoAnd Moses came down from the mount to the people, and sanctified them. And when they had washed their garments,€€ƒxNHe said to them: Be ready against the third day, and come not near your wives.€€ƒyAnd now the third day was come, and the morning appeared: and behold thunders began to be heard, and lightning to flash, and a very thick cloud to cover the mount, and the noise of the trumpet sounded exceeding loud; and the people that was in the camp, feared.€€ƒxuAnd when Moses had brought them forth to meet God, from the place of the camp, they stood at the bottom of the mount.€€ƒx¤And all Mount Sinai was on a smoke: because the Lord was come down upon it in fire, and the smoke arose from it as out of a furnace: and all the mount was terrible.€€ƒx‰And the sound of the trumpet grew by degrees louder and louder, and was drawn out to a greater length: Moses spoke, and God answered him.€€ƒx‘And the Lord came down upon Mount Sinai, in the very top of the mount, and he called Moses unto the top thereof. And when he was gone up thither,€€ƒx¤He said unto him: Go down, and charge the people; lest they should have a mind to pass the limits to see the Lord, and a very great multitude of them should perish.€€ƒxTThe priests also that come to the Lord, let them be sanctified, lest he strike them.€€ƒxAnd Moses said to the Lord: The people cannot come up to Mount Sinai: for thou didst charge, and command, saying: Set limits about the mount, and sanctify it.€€ƒxÂAnd the Lord said to him: Go, get thee down; and thou shalt come up, thou and Aaron with thee: but let not the priests and the people pass the limits, nor come up to the Lord, lest he kill them.€€ƒx4And Moses went down to the people and told them all.€€ƒ`€€ƒx#And the Lord spoke all these words:€€ƒx^I am the Lord thy God, who brought thee out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage.€€˜ƒxºThou shalt not make to thyself a graven thing, nor the likeness of any thing that is in heaven above, or in the earth beneath, nor of those things that are in the waters under the earth.€€ƒxÅThou shalt not adore them, nor serve them: I am the Lord thy God, mighty, jealous, visiting the iniquity of the fathers upon the children, unto the third and fourth generation of them that hate me:€€ƒxPAnd shewing mercy unto thousands to them that love me, and keep my commandments.€€ƒx˜Thou shalt not take the name of the Lord thy God in vain: for the Lord will not hold him guiltless that shall take the name of the Lord his God in vain.€€ƒx-Remember that thou keep holy the sabbath day.€€ƒx7Six days shalt thou labour, and shalt do all thy works.€€ƒxŞBut on the seventh day is the sabbath of the Lord thy God: thou shalt do no work on it, thou nor thy son, nor thy daughter, nor thy manservant, nor thy maidservant, nor thy beast, nor the stranger that is within thy gates.€€ƒx»For in six days the Lord made heaven and earth, and the sea, and all things that are in them, and rested on the seventh day: therefore the Lord blessed the seventh day, and sanctified it.€€ƒxsHonour thy father and thy mother, that thou mayst be longlived upon the land which the Lord thy God will give thee.€€ƒtThou shalt not kill.€€ƒxThou shalt not commit adultery.€€ƒuThou shalt not steal.€€ƒx8Thou shalt not bear false witness against thy neighbour.€€ƒx¦Thou shalt not covet thy neighbour's house; neither shalt thou desire his wife, nor his servant, nor his handmaid, nor his ox, nor his ass, nor any thing that is his.€€ƒx¥And all the people saw the voices and the flames, and the sound of the trumpet, and the mount smoking; and being terrified and struck with fear, they stood afar off,€€ƒx_Saying to Moses: Speak thou to us, and we will hear: let not the Lord speak to us, lest we die.€€ƒxˆAnd Moses said to the people: Fear not; for God is come to prove you, and that the dread of him might be in you, and you should not sin.€€ƒxPAnd the people stood afar off. But Moses went to the dark cloud wherein God was.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to Moses: Thus shalt thou say to the children of Israel: You have seen that I have spoken to you from heaven.€€ƒxQYou shall not make gods of silver, nor shall you make to yourselves gods of gold.€€ƒxŞYou shall make an altar of earth unto me, and you shall offer upon it your holocausts and peace offerings, your sheep and oxen, in every place where the memory of my name shall be: I will come to thee, and will bless thee.€€ƒxŒAnd if thou make an altar of stone unto me, thou shalt not build it of hewn stones; for if thou lift up a tool upon it, it shall be defiled.€€ƒxNThou shalt not go up by steps unto my altar, lest thy nakedness be discovered.€€ƒ`€€ƒx9These are the judgments which thou shalt set before them.€€ƒxmIf thou buy a Hebrew servant, six years shall he serve thee; in the seventh he shall go out free for nothing.€€˜$ƒx©But if his master gave him a wife, and she hath borne sons and daughters; the woman and her children shall be her master's: but he himself shall go out with his raiment.€€ƒx`And if the servant shall say: I love my master and my wife and children, I will not go out free:€€ƒx«His master shall bring him to the gods, and he shall be set to the door and the posts, and he shall bore his ear through with an awl: and he shall be his servant for ever.€€ƒxcIf any man sell his daughter to be a servant, she shall not go out as bondwomen are wont to go out.€€ƒx¦If she displease the eyes of her master to whom she was delivered, he shall let her go: but he shall have no power to sell her to a foreign nation, if he despise her.€€ƒx^But if he have betrothed her to his son, he shall deal with her after the manner of daughters.€€ƒx…And if he take another wife for him, he shall provide her a marriage, and raiment, neither shall he refuse the price of her chastity.€€ƒxEIf he do not these three things, she shall go out free without money.€€ƒxFHe that striketh a man with a will to kill him, shall be put to death.€€ƒxBut he that did not lie in wait for him, but God delivered him into his hands: I will appoint thee a place to which he must flee.€€ƒxIf a man kill his neighbour on set purpose, and by lying in wait for him: thou shalt take him away from my altar that he may die.€€ƒx=He that striketh his father or mother, shall be put to death.€€ƒx]He that shall steal a man, and sell him, being convicted of the guilt, shall be put to death.€€ƒx:He that curseth his father or mother, shall die the death.€€ƒxuIf men quarrel, and the one strike his neighbour with a stone, or with his fist, and he die not, but keepeth his bed:€€ƒxªIf he rise again and walk abroad upon his staff, he that struck him shall be quit, yet so that he make restitution for his work, and for his expenses upon the physicians.€€ƒxsHe that striketh his bondman, or bondwoman, with a rod, and they die under his hands, shall be guilty of the crime.€€ƒxoBut if the party remain alive a day or two, he shall not be subject to the punishment, because it is his money.€€ƒxÉIf men quarrel, and one strike a woman with child and she miscarry indeed, but live herself: he shall be answerable for so much damage as the woman's husband shall require, and as arbiters shall award.€€ƒx@But if her death ensue thereupon, he shall render life for life,€€ƒx;Eye for eye, tooth for tooth, hand for hand, foot for foot,€€ƒx8Burning for burning, wound for wound, stripe for stripe.€€ƒxIf any man strike the eye of his manservant or maidservant, and leave them but one eye, he shall let them go free for the eye which he put out.€€ƒxgAlso if he strike out a tooth of his manservant or maidservant, he shall in like manner make them free.€€ƒxŠIf an ox gore a man or a woman, and they die, he shall be stoned: and his flesh shall not be eaten, but the owner of the ox shall be quit.€€ƒxîBut if the ox was wont to push with his horn yesterday, and the day before, and they warned his master, and he did not shut him up, and he shall kill a man or a woman: then the ox shall be stoned, and his owner also shall be put to death.€€ƒxYAnd if they set a price upon him, he shall give for his life whatsoever is laid upon him.€€ƒxMIf he have gored a son, or a daughter, he shall fall under the like sentence.€€ƒxxIf he assault a bondman or bondwoman, he shall give thirty sicles of silver to their master, and the ox shall be stoned.€€ƒxUIf a man open a pit, and dig one, and cover it not, and an ox or an ass fall into it,€€ƒx`The owner of the pit shall pay the price of the beasts: and that which is dead shall be his own.€€ƒx¬If one man's ox gore another man's ox, and he die: they shall sell the live ox, and shall divide the price, and the carcass of that which died they shall part between them:€€ƒx¨But if he knew that his ox was wont to push yesterday, and the day before, and his master did not keep him in; he shall pay ox for ox, and shall take the whole carcass.€€ƒ`€€ƒx|If any man steal an ox or a sheep, and kill or sell it: he shall restore five oxen for one ox, and four sheep for one sheep.€€ƒxˆIf a thief be found breaking open a house or undermining it, and be wounded so as to die: he that slew him shall not be guilty of blood.€€˜ƒxfIf that which he stole be found with him, alive, either ox, or ass, or sheep: he shall restore double.€€ƒxäIf any man hurt a field or a vineyard, and put in his beast to feed upon that which is other men's: he shall restore the best of whatsoever he hath in his own field, or in his vineyard, according to the estimation of the damage.€€ƒx–If a fire breaking out light upon thorns, and catch stacks of corn, or corn standing in the fields, he that kindled the fire shall make good the loss.€€ƒx£If a man deliver money, or any vessel unto his friend to keep, and they be stolen away from him that received them: if the thief be found, he shall restore double:€€ƒx™If the thief be not known, the master of the house shall be brought to the gods, and shall swear that he did not lay his hand upon his neighbour's goods,€€ƒxÚTo do any fraud, either in ox, or in ass, or sheep, or raiment, or any thing that may bring damage: the cause of both parties shall come to the gods: and if they give judgment, he shall restore double to his neighbour.€€ƒxIf a man deliver ass, ox, sheep, or any beast, to his neighbour's custody, and it die, or be hurt, or be taken by enemies, and no man saw it:€€ƒx¼There shall be an oath between them, that he did not put forth his hand to his neighbour's goods: and the owner shall accept of the oath, and he shall not be compelled to make restitution.€€ƒxOBut if it were taken away by stealth, he shall make the loss good to the owner.€€ƒxjIf it were eaten by a beast, let him bring to him that which was slain, and he shall not make restitution.€€ƒx’If a man borrow of his neighbour any of these things, and it be hurt or die, the owner not being present, he shall be obliged to make restitution.€€ƒx{But if the owner be present, he shall not make restitution, especially if it were hired, and came for the hire of his work.€€ƒxfIf a man seduce a virgin not yet espoused, and lie with her: he shall endow her, and have her to wife.€€ƒx}If the maid's father will not give her to him, he shall give money according to the dowry, which virgins are wont to receive.€€ƒx&Wizards thou shalt not suffer to live.€€ƒx9Whosoever copulateth with a beast; shall be put to death.€€ƒxJHe that sacrificeth to gods, shall be put to death, save only to the Lord.€€ƒxkThou shalt not molest a stranger, nor afflict him: for yourselves also were strangers in the land of Egypt.€€ƒx(You shall not hurt a widow or an orphan.€€ƒxEIf you hurt them, they will cry out to me, and I will hear their cry:€€ƒxƒAnd my rage shall be enkindled, and I will strike you with the sword, and your wives shall be widows, and your children fatherless.€€ƒx If thou lend money to any of my people that is poor, that dwelleth with thee, thou shalt not be hard upon them as an extortioner, nor oppress them with usuries.€€ƒx^If thou take of thy neighbour a garment in pledge, thou shalt give it him again before sunset.€€ƒx¸For that same is the only thing, wherewith he is covered, the clothing of his body, neither hath he any other to sleep in: if he cry to me, I will hear him, because I am compassionate.€€ƒxXThou shalt not speak ill of the gods, and the prince of thy people thou shalt not curse.€€ƒxlThou shalt not delay to pay thy tithes and thy firstfruits: thou shalt give the firstborn of thy sons to me.€€ƒx‘Thou shalt do the same with the firstborn of thy oxen also and sheep: seven days let it be with its dam: the eighth day thou shalt give it to me.€€ƒx{You shall be holy men to me: the flesh that beasts have tasted of before, you shall not eat, but shall cast it to the dogs.€€ƒ`€€ƒxvThou shalt not receive the voice of a lie: neither shalt thou join thy hand to bear false witness for a wicked person.€€ƒxThou shalt not follow the multitude to do evil: neither shalt thou yield in judgment, to the opinion of the most part, to stray from the truth.€€˜!ƒxFIf thou meet thy enemy's ox or ass going astray, bring it back to him.€€ƒx~If thou see the ass of him that hateth thee lie underneath his burden, thou shalt not pass by, but shalt lift him up with him.€€ƒx3Thou shalt not go aside in the poor man's judgment.€€ƒxkThou shalt fly lying. The innocent and just person thou shalt not put to death: because I abhor the wicked.€€ƒx]Neither shalt thou take bribes, which even blind the wise, and pervert the words of the just.€€ƒxyThou shalt not molest a stranger, for you know the hearts of strangers: for you also were strangers in the land of Egypt.€€ƒxGSix years thou shalt sow thy ground, and shalt gather the corn thereof.€€ƒxáBut the seventh year thou shalt let it alone, and suffer it to rest, that the poor of thy people may eat, and whatsoever shall be left, let the beasts of the field eat it: so shalt thou do with thy vineyard and thy oliveyard.€€ƒxœSix days thou shalt work: the seventh day thou shalt cease, that thy ox and thy ass may rest: and the son of thy handmaid and the stranger may be refreshed.€€ƒxŠKeep all things that I have said to you. And by the name of strange gods you shall not swear, neither shall it be heard out of your mouth.€€ƒx8Three times every year you shall celebrate feasts to me.€€ƒxåThou shalt keep the feast of unleavened bread. Seven days shalt thou eat unleavened bread, as I commanded thee, in the time of the month of new corn, when thou didst come forth out of Egypt: thou shalt not appear empty before me.€€ƒxÅAnd the feast of the harvest of the firstfruits of thy work, whatsoever thou hast sown in the field. The feast also in the end of the year, when thou hast gathered in all thy corn out of the field.€€ƒxAThrice a year shall all thy males appear before the Lord thy God.€€ƒx|Thou shalt not sacrifice the blood of my victim upon leaven, neither shall the fat of my solemnity remain until the morning.€€ƒxThou shalt carry the first-fruits of the corn of thy ground to the house of the Lord thy God. Thou shalt not boil a kid in the milk of his dam.€€ƒxˆBehold I will send my angel, who shall go before thee, and keep thee in thy journey, and bring thee into the place that I have prepared.€€ƒx—Take notice of him, and hear his voice, and do not think him one to be contemned: for he will not forgive when thou hast sinned, and my name is in him.€€ƒx„But if thou wilt hear hi voice, and do all that I speak, I will be an enemy to thy enemies, and will afflict them that afflict thee.€€ƒx½And my angel shall go before thee, and shall bring thee in unto the Amorrhite, and the Hethite, and the Pherexite, and the Chanaanite, and the Hevite, and the Jebuzite, whom I will destroy.€€ƒx€Thou shalt not adore their gods, nor serve them. Thou shalt not do their works, but shalt destroy them, and break their statues.€€ƒx†And you shall serve the Lord your God, that I may bless your bread and your waters, and may take away sickness from the midst of thee.€€ƒx\There shall not be one fruitless nor barren in thy land: I will fill the number of thy days.€€ƒx‘I will send my fear before thee, and will destroy all the people to whom thou shalt come: and will turn the backs of all thy enemies before thee:€€ƒxwSending out hornets before, that shall drive away the Hevite, and the Chanaanite, and the Hethite, before thou come in.€€ƒx…I will not cast them out from thy face in one year; lest the land be brought into a wilderness, and the beasts multiply against thee.€€ƒxoBy little and little I will drive them out from before thee, till thou be increased, and dost possess the land.€€ƒxÏAnd I will set thy bounds from the Red Sea to the sea of the Palestines, and from the desert to the river: I will deliver the inhabitants of the land into your hands, and will drive them out from before you.€€ƒx@Thou shalt not enter into league with them, nor with their gods.€€ƒx”Let them not dwell in thy land, lest perhaps they make thee sin against me, if thou serve their gods; which, undoubtedly, will be a scandal to thee.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAnd he said to Moses: Come up to the Lord, thou, and Aaron, Nadab and Abiu, and seventy of the ancients of Israel, and you shall adore afar off.€€ƒxsAnd Moses alone shall come up to the Lord, but they shall not come nigh; neither shall the people come up with him.€€’ƒx­And Moses wrote all the words of the Lord: and rising in the morning, he built an altar at the foot of the mount, and twelve titles according to the twelve tribes of Israel.€€ƒxƒAnd he sent young men of the children of Israel, and they offered holocausts, and sacrificed pacific victims of calves to the Lord.€€ƒx`Then Moses took half of the blood, and put it into bowls; and the rest he poured upon the altar.€€ƒx£And taking the book of the covenant, he read it in the hearing of the people: and they said: All things that the Lord hath spoken, we will do, we will be obedient.€€ƒx¥And he took the blood and sprinkled it upon the people, and he said: This is the blood of the covenant, which the Lord hath made with you concerning all these words.€€ƒxTThen Moses and Aaron, Nadab and Abiu, and seventy of the ancients of Israel went up:€€ƒxvAnd they saw the God of Israel: and under his feet as it were a work of sapphire stone, and as the heaven, when clear.€€ƒx†Neither did he lay his hand upon those of the children of Israel, that retired afar off, and they saw God, and they did eat and drink.€€ƒxÂAnd the Lord said to Moses: Come up to me into the mount, and be there; and I will give thee tables of stone, and the law, and the commandments which I have written; that thou mayest teach them.€€ƒxPMoses rose up, and his minister Josue: and Moses going up into the mount of God,€€ƒx“Said to the ancients: Wait ye here till we return to you. You have Aaron and Hur with you: if any question shall arise, you shall refer it to them.€€ƒx6And when Moses was gone up, a cloud covered the mount.€€ƒxAnd the glory of the Lord dwelt upon Sinai, covering it with a cloud six days: and the seventh day he called him out of the midst of the cloud.€€ƒxAnd the sight of the glory of the Lord, was like a burning fire upon the top of the mount, in the eyes of the children of Israel.€€ƒxxAnd Moses entering into the midst of the cloud, went up into the mountain: And he was there forty days and forty nights.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx†Speak to the children of Israel, that they bring firstfruits to me: of every man that offereth of his own accord, you shall take them.€€˜(ƒxKViolet and purple, and scarlet twice dyed, and fine linen, and goats' hair,€€ƒx;And rams' skins dyed red, and violet skins, and setim wood:€€ƒxGOil to make lights: spices for ointment, and for sweetsmelling incense:€€ƒxEOnyx stones, and precious stones to adorn the ephod and the rational.€€ƒxJAnd they shall make me a sanctuary, and I will dwell in the midst of them:€€ƒx“According to all the likeness of the tabernacle which I will shew thee, and of all the vessels for the service thereof: and thus you shall make it:€€ƒxœFrame an ark of setim wood, the length whereof shall be of two cubits and a half; the breadth, a cubit and a half; the height, likewise, a cubit and a half.€€ƒx{And thou shalt overlay it with the purest gold, within and without; and over it thou shalt make a golden crown round about:€€ƒxƒAnd four golden rings, which thou shalt put at the four corners of the ark: let two rings be on the one side, and two on the other.€€ƒxJThou shalt make bars also of setim wood, and shalt overlay them with gold.€€ƒxnAnd thou shalt put them in through the rings that are in the sides of the ark, that it may be carried on them:€€ƒx[And they shall be always in the rings, neither shall they at any time be drawn out of them.€€ƒxCAnd thou shalt put in the ark the testimony which I will give thee.€€ƒxThou shalt make also a propitiatory of the purest gold: the length thereof shall be two cubits and a half, and the breadth a cubit and a half.€€ƒxRThou shalt make also two cherubims of beaten gold, on the two sides of the oracle.€€ƒx>Let one cherub be on the one side, and the other on the other.€€ƒxİLet them cover both sides of the propitiatory, spreading their wings, and covering the oracle, and let them look one towards the other, their faces being turned towards the propitiatory wherewith the ark is to be covered.€€ƒx<In which thou shalt put the testimony that I will give thee.€€ƒxßThence will I give orders, and will speak to thee over the propitiatory, and from the midst of the two cherubims, which shall be upon the ark of the testimony, all things which I will command the children of Israel by thee.€€ƒx~Thou shalt make a table also of setim wood, of two cubits in length, and a cubit in breadth, and a cubit and a half in height.€€ƒxeAnd thou shalt overlay it with the purest gold: and thou shalt make to it a golden ledge round about.€€ƒxjAnd to the ledge itself a polished crown, four inches high; and over the same another little golden crown.€€ƒxtThou shalt prepare also four golden rings, and shalt put them in the four corners of the same table, over each foot.€€ƒxoUnder the crown shall the golden rings be, that the bars may be put through them, and the table may be carried.€€ƒxoThe bars also themselves thou shalt make of setim wood, and shalt overlay them with gold, to bear up the table.€€ƒxzThou shalt prepare also dishes, and bowls, censers, and cups, wherein the libations are to be offered, of the purest gold.€€ƒxKAnd thou shalt set upon the table loaves of proposition in my sight always.€€ƒx¨Thou shalt make also a candlestick of beaten work, of the finest gold, the shaft thereof, and the branches, the cups, and the bowls, and the lilies going forth from it.€€ƒx\Six branches shall come out of the sides, three out of one side, and three out of the other.€€ƒxşThree cups as it were nuts to every branch, and a bowl withal, and a lily: and three cups likewise of the fashion of nuts in the other branch, and a bowl withal, and a lily. Such shall be the work of the six branches, that are to come out from the shaft:€€ƒxkAnd in the candlestick itself shall be four cups in the manner of a nut, and at every one bowls and lilies.€€ƒxaBowls under two branches in three places, which together make six, coming forth out of one shaft.€€ƒxXAnd both the bowls and the branches shall be of the same beaten work of the purest gold.€€ƒxfThou shalt make also seven lamps, and shalt set them upon the candlestick, to give light over against.€€ƒx^The snuffers also, and where the snuffings shall be put out, shall be made of the purest gold.€€ƒxjThe whole weight of the candlestick, with all the furniture thereof, shall be a talent of the purest gold.€€ƒxLLook, and make it according to the pattern that was shewn thee in the mount.€€ƒ`€€ƒx²And thou shalt make the tabernacle in this manner: Thou shalt make ten curtains of fine twisted linen, and violet and purple, and scarlet twice dyed, diversified with embroidery.€€ƒxƒThe length of one curtain shall be twenty-eight cubits; the breadth shall be four cubits. All the curtains shall be of one measure.€€˜%ƒxnThou shalt make loops of violet in the sides and tops of the curtains, that they may be joined one to another.€€ƒxEvery curtain shall have fifty loops on both sides, so set on, that one loop may be against another loop, and one may be fitted to the other.€€ƒxƒThou shalt make also fifty rings of gold, wherewith the veils of the curtains are to be joined, that it may be made one tabernacle.€€ƒxXThou shalt make also eleven curtains of goats' hair, to cover the top of the tabernacle.€€ƒx}The length of one hair-curtain shall be thirty cubits; and the breadth, four: the measure of all the curtains shall be equal.€€ƒxŸFive of which thou shalt couple by themselves, and the six others thou shalt couple one to another, so as to double the sixth curtain in the front of the roof.€€ƒx¼Thou shalt make also fifty loops in the edge of one curtain, that it may be joined with the other: and fifty loops in the edge of the other curtain, that it may be coupled with its fellow.€€ƒx{Thou shalt make also fifty buckles of brass, wherewith the loops may be joined, that of all there may be made one covering.€€ƒxÁAnd that which shall remain of the curtains, that are prepared for the roof, to wit, one curtain that is over and above, with the half thereof thou shalt cover the back parts of the tabernacle.€€ƒx®And there shall hang down a cubit on the one side, and another on the other side, which is over and above in the length of the curtains, fencing both sides of the tabernacle.€€ƒxƒThou shalt make also another cover to the roof of rams' skins dyed red: and over that again another cover of violet coloured skins.€€ƒxQThou shalt make also the boards of the tabernacle standing upright of setim wood.€€ƒxSLet every one of them be ten cubits in length, and in breadth one cubit and a half.€€ƒx In the sides of the boards shall be made two mortises, whereby one board may be joined to another board: and after this manner shall all the boards be prepared.€€ƒx5Of which twenty shall be in the south side southward.€€ƒxtFor which thou shalt cast forty sockets of silver, that under every board may be put two sockets at the two corners.€€ƒxbIn the second side also of the tabernacle that looketh to the north, there shall be twenty boards,€€ƒxJHaving forty sockets of silver, two sockets shall be put under each board.€€ƒxBBut on the west side of the tabernacle thou shalt make six boards.€€ƒxXAnd again other two which shall be erected in the corners at the back of the tabernacle.€€ƒx½And they shall be joined together from beneath unto the top, and one joint shall hold them all. The like joining shall be observed for the two boards also that are to be put in the corners.€€ƒxnAnd they shall be in all eight boards, and their silver sockets sixteen, reckoning two sockets for each board.€€ƒxhThou shalt make also five bars of setim wood, to hold together the boards on one side of the tabernacle.€€ƒx@And five others on the other side, and as many at the west side:€€ƒxRAnd they shall be put along by the midst of the boards, from one end to the other.€€ƒxÓThe boards also themselves thou shalt overlay with gold, and shalt cast rings of gold to be set upon them, for places for the bars to hold together the boardwork: which bars thou shalt cover with plates of gold.€€ƒx`And thou shalt rear up the tabernacle according to the pattern that was shewn thee in the mount.€€ƒx”Thou shalt make also a veil of violet, and purple, and scarlet twice dyed, and fine twisted linen, wrought with embroidered work and goodly variety:€€ƒx¤And thou shalt hang it up before four pillars of setim wood, which themselves also shall be overlaid with gold, and shall have heads of gold, but sockets of silver.€€ƒx©And the veil shall be hanged on with rings, and within it thou shalt put the ark of the testimony, and the sanctuary and the holy of the holies shall be divided with it.€€ƒxYAnd thou shalt set the propitiatory upon the ark of the testimony, in the holy of holies.€€ƒxœAnd the table without the veil, and over against the table the candlestick in the south side of the tabernacle: for the table shall stand in the north side.€€ƒxThou shalt make also a hanging in the entrance of the tabernacle of violet, and purple, and scarlet twice dyed, and fine twisted linen with embroidered work.€€ƒxAnd thou shalt overlay with gold five pillars of setim wood, before which the hanging shall be drawn: their heads shall be of gold, and the sockets of brass.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŒThou shalt make also an altar of setim wood, which shall be five cubits long, and as many broad, that is four square, and three cubits high.€€ƒx]And there shall be horns at the four corners of the same: and thou shalt cover it with brass.€€•ƒxdAnd a grate of brass in manner of a net; at the four corners of which, shall be four rings of brass,€€ƒxjWhich thou shalt put under the hearth of the altar: and the grate shall be even to the midst of the altar.€€ƒxhThou shalt make also two bars for the altar, of setim wood, which thou shalt cover with plates of brass:€€ƒxaAnd thou shalt draw them through rings, and they shall be on both sides of the altar to carry it.€€ƒxdThou shalt not make it solid, but empty and hollow in the inside, as it was shewn thee in the mount.€€ƒxªThou shalt make also the court of the tabernacle, in the south side whereof southward there shall be hangings of fine twisted linen of a hundred cubits long for one side.€€ƒxoAnd twenty pillars with as many sockets of brass, the heads of which, with their engraving, shall be of silver.€€ƒxµIn like manner also on the north side there shall be hangings of a hundred cubits long, twenty pillars, and as many sockets of brass, and their heads with their engraving of silver.€€ƒx‰But in the breadth of the court, that looketh to the west, there shall be hangings of fifty cubits, and ten pillars, and as many sockets.€€ƒxZIn that breadth also of the court, which looketh to the east, there shall be fifty cubits.€€ƒxiIn which there shall be for one side, hangings of fifteen cubits, and three pillars, and as many sockets.€€ƒxjAnd in the other side, there shall be hangings of fifteen cubits, with three pillars, and as many sockets.€€ƒxŞAnd in the entrance of the court there shall be made a hanging of twenty cubits of violet and purple, and scarlet twice dyed, and fine twisted linen, with embroidered work: it shall have four pillars, with as many sockets.€€ƒxvAll the pillars of the court round about shall be garnished with plates of silver, silver heads, and sockets of brass.€€ƒx¶In length the court shall take up a hundred cubits, in breadth fifty, the height shall be of five cubits, and it shall be made of fine twisted linen, and shall have sockets of brass.€€ƒx‚All the vessels of the tabernacle for all uses and ceremonies, and the pins both of it and of the court, thou shalt make of brass.€€ƒxˆCommand the children of Israel that they bring thee the purest oil of the olives, and beaten with a pestle: that a lamp may burn always,€€ƒyIn the tabernacle of the testimony, without the veil that hangs before the testimony. And Aaron and his sons shall order it, that it may give light before the Lord until the morning. It shall be a perpetual observance throughout their successions among the children of Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxºTake unto thee also Aaron thy brother with his sons, from among the children of Israel, that they may minister to me in the priest's office: Aaron, Nadab, and Abiu, Eleazar, and Ithamar.€€ƒxTAnd thou shalt make a holy vesture for Aaron, thy brother, for glory and for beauty.€€˜+ƒyAnd these shall be the vestments that they shall make: A rational and an ephod, a tunic and a strait linen garment, a mitre and a girdle. They shall make the holy vestments for thy brother Aaron and his sons, that they may do the office of priesthood unto me.€€ƒxYAnd they shall take gold, and violet, and purple, and scarlet twice dyed, and fine linen.€€ƒxAnd they shall make the ephod of gold, and violet, and purple, and scarlet twice dyed, and fine twisted linen, embroidered with divers colours.€€ƒx^It shall have the two edges joined in the top on both sides, that they may be closed together.€€ƒx•The very workmanship also, and all the variety of the work, shall be of gold, and violet, and purple, and scarlet twice dyed, and fine twisted linen.€€ƒxaAnd thou shalt take two onyx stones, and shalt grave on them the names of the children of Israel:€€ƒx^Six names on one stone, and the other six on the other, according to the order of their birth.€€ƒxŸWith the work of an engraver, and the graving of a jeweller, thou shalt engrave them with the names of the children of Israel, set in gold and compassed about:€€ƒx³And thou shalt put them in both sides of the ephod, a memorial for the children of Israel. And Aaron shall bear their names before the Lord upon both shoulders, for a remembrance.€€ƒx#Thou shalt make also hooks of gold.€€ƒxeAnd two little chains of the purest gold, linked one to another, which thou shalt put into the hooks.€€ƒxÍAnd thou shalt make the rational of judgment with embroidered work of divers colours, according to the workmanship of the ephod, of gold, violet, and purple, and scarlet twice dyed, and fine twisted linen.€€ƒxeIt shall be four square and doubled: it shall be the measure of a span both in length and in breadth.€€ƒxvAnd thou shalt set in it four rows of stones . In the first row shall be a sardius stone, and a topaz, and an emerald:€€ƒx4In the second a carbuncle, a sapphire, and a jasper:€€ƒx3In the third a ligurius, an agate, and an amethyst:€€ƒxZIn the fourth a chrysolite, an onyx, and a beryl. They shall be set in gold by their rows.€€ƒx¢And they shall have the names of the children of Israel: with twelve names shall they be engraved, each stone with the name of one according to the twelve tribes.€€ƒxVAnd thou shalt make on the rational chains, linked one to another, of the purest gold:€€ƒxWAnd two rings of gold, which thou shalt put in the two ends at the top of the rational.€€ƒxQAnd the golden chains thou shalt join to the rings, that are in the ends thereof.€€ƒxŠAnd the ends of the chains themselves, thou shalt join together with two hooks, on both sides of the ephod, which is towards the rational.€€ƒx·Thou shalt make also two rings of gold, which thou shalt put in the top parts of the rational, in the borders that are over against the ephod, and look towards the back parts thereof.€€ƒx¶Moreover also other two rings of gold, which are to be set on each side of the ephod beneath, that looketh towards the nether joining, that the rational may be fitted with the ephod,€€ƒxÓAnd may be fastened by the rings thereof unto the rings of the ephod with a violet fillet, that the joining artificially wrought may continue, and the rational and the ephod may not be loosed one from the other.€€ƒx²And Aaron shall bear the names of the children of Israel in the rational of judgment upon his breast, when he shall enter into the sanctuary, a memorial before the Lord for ever.€€ƒxòAnd thou shalt put in the rational of judgment doctrine and truth, which shall be on Aaron's breast, when he shall go in before the Lord: and he shall bear the judgment of the children of Israel on his breast, in the sight of the Lord always.€€ƒx9And thou shalt make the tunic of the ephod all of violet,€€ƒxµIn the midst whereof above shall be a hole for the head, and a border round about it woven, as is wont to be made in the outmost parts of garments, that it may not easily be broken.€€ƒx®And beneath at the feet of the same tunic, round about, thou shalt make as it were pomegranates, of violet, and purple, and scarlet twice dyed, with little bells set between:€€ƒxhSo that there shall be a golden bell and a pomegranate, and again another golden bell and a pomegranate.€€ƒxÂAnd Aaron shall be vested with it in the office of his ministry, that the sound may be heard, when he goeth in and cometh out of the sanctuary, in the sight of the Lord, and that he may not die.€€ƒxqThou shalt make also a plate of the purest gold: wherein thou shalt grave with engraver's work, Holy to the Lord.€€ƒxKAnd thou shalt tie it with a violet fillet, and it shall be upon the mitre,€€ƒyHanging over the forehead of the high priest. And Aaron shall bear the iniquities of those things, which the children of Israel have offered and sanctified, in all their gifts and offerings. And the plate shall be always on his forehead, that the Lord may be well pleased with them.€€ƒxxAnd thou shalt gird the tunic with fine linen, and thou shalt make a fine linen mitre, and a girdle of embroidered work.€€ƒxmMoreover, for the sons of Aaron thou shalt prepare linen tunics, and girdles and mitres for glory and beauty:€€ƒxĞAnd with all these things thou shalt vest Aaron thy brother, and his sons with him. And thou shalt consecrate the hands of them all, and shalt sanctify them, that they may do the office of priesthood unto me.€€ƒxiThou shalt make also linen breeches, to cover the flesh of their nakedness, from the reins to the thighs:€€ƒyAnd Aaron and his sons shall use them when they shall go into the tabernacle of the testimony, or when they approach to the altar to minister in the sanctuary. lest being guilty of iniquity they die. It shall be a law for ever to Aaron, and to his seed after him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‡And thou shalt also do this, that they may be consecrated to me in priesthood. Take a calf from the herd, and two rams without blemish,€€ƒx™And unleavened bread, and a cake without leaven, tempered with oil, wafers also unleavened, anointed with oil: thou shalt make them all of wheaten flour.€€˜.ƒx•And thou shalt bring Aaron and his sons to the door of the tabernacle of the testimony. And when thou hast washed the father and his sons with water,€€ƒx¡Thou shalt clothe Aaron with his vestments, that is, with the linen garment and the tunic, and the ephod and the rational, which thou shalt gird with the girdle.€€ƒxNAnd thou shalt put the mitre upon his head, and the holy plate upon the mitre,€€ƒx_And thou shalt pour the oil of unction upon his head: and by this rite shall he be consecrated.€€ƒxdThou shalt bring his sons also, and shalt put on them the linen tunics, and gird them with a girdle:€€ƒx­To wit, Aaron and his children, and thou shalt put mitres upon them; and they shall be priests to me by a perpetual ordinance. After thou shalt have consecrated their hands,€€ƒx„Thou shalt present also the calf before the tabernacle of the testimony. And Aaron and his sons shall lay their hands upon his head,€€ƒxeAnd thou shalt kill him in the sight of the Lord, beside the door of the tabernacle of the testimony.€€ƒx©And taking some of the blood of the calf, thou shalt put it upon the horns of the altar with thy finger, and the rest of the blood thou shalt pour at the bottom thereof.€€ƒx»Thou shalt take also all the fat that covereth the entrails, and the caul of the liver, and the two kidneys, and the fat that is upon them, and shalt offer a burn offering upon the altar:€€ƒxvBut the flesh of the calf, and the hide and the dung, thou shalt burn abroad, without the camp, because it is for sin.€€ƒx]Thou shalt take also one ram, upon the head whereof Aaron and his sons shall lay their hands.€€ƒxdAnd when thou hast killed him, thou shalt take of the blood thereof, and pour round about the altar.€€ƒx›And thou shalt cut the ram in pieces, and having washed his entrails and feet, thou shalt put them upon the flesh that is cut in pieces, and upon his head.€€ƒx•And thou shalt offer the whole ram for a burnt offering upon the altar: it is an oblation to the Lord, a most sweet savour of the victim of the Lord.€€ƒx]Thou shalt take also the other ram, upon whose head Aaron and his sons shall lay their hands.€€ƒxûAnd when thou hast sacrificed him, thou shalt take of his blood, and put upon the tip of the right ear of Aaron and of his sons, and upon the thumbs and great toes of their right hand and foot, and thou shalt pour the blood upon the altar round about.€€ƒxÕAnd when thou hast taken of the blood that is upon the altar, and of the oil of unction, thou shalt sprinkle Aaron and his vesture, his sons and their vestments. And after they and their vestments are consecrated,€€ƒxäThou shalt take the fat of the ram, and the rump, and the fat that covereth the lungs, and the caul of the liver, and the two kidneys, and the fat that is upon them, and the right shoulder, because it is the ram of consecration:€€ƒx†And one roll of bread, a cake tempered with oil, a wafer out of the basket of unleavened bread, which is set in the sight of the Lord:€€ƒxrAnd thou shalt put all upon the hands of Aaron and of his sons, and shalt sanctify them elevating before the Lord.€€ƒx§And thou shalt take all from their hands; and shalt burn them upon the altar for a holocaust, a most sweet savour in the sight of the Lord, because it is his oblation.€€ƒx¥Thou shalt take also the breast of the ram, wherewith Aaron was consecrated, and elevating it thou shalt sanctify it before the Lord, and it shall fall to thy share.€€ƒxjAnd thou shalt sanctify both the consecrated breast, and the shoulder that thou didst separate of the ram,€€ƒxúWherewith Aaron was consecrated and his sons, and they shall fall to Aaron's share, and his sons', by a perpetual right from the children of Israel: because they are the choicest and the beginnings of their peace victims which they offer to the Lord.€€ƒx‰And the holy vesture, which Aaron shall use, his sons shall have after him, that they may be anointed, and their hands consecrated in it.€€ƒx²He of his sons that shall be appointed high priest in his stead, and that shall enter into the tabernacle of the testimony to minister in the sanctuary, shall wear it seven days.€€ƒxdAnd thou shalt take the ram of the consecration, and shalt boil the flesh thereof in the holy place:€€ƒxAnd Aaron and his sons shall eat it. The loaves also, that are in the basket, they shall eat in the entry of the tabernacle of the testimony,€€ƒxThat it may be an atoning sacrifice, and the hands of the offerers may be sanctified. A stranger shall not eat of them, because they are holy.€€ƒx¯And if there remain of the consecrated flesh, or of the bread, till the morning, thou shalt burn the remainder with fire: they shall not be eaten, because they are sanctified.€€ƒxtAll that I have commanded thee, thou shalt do unto Aaron and his sons. Seven days shalt thou consecrate their hands:€€ƒx±And thou shalt offer a calf for sin every day for expiation. And thou shalt cleanse the altar when thou hast offered the victim of expiation, and shalt anoint it to sanctify it.€€ƒx‚Seven days shalt thou expiate the altar and sanctify it, and it shall be most holy. Every one, that shall touch it, shall be holy.€€ƒx`This is what thou shalt sacrifice upon the altar: Two lambs of a year old every day continually,€€ƒx4One lamb in the morning, and another in the evening.€€ƒx…With one lamb a tenth part of flour tempered with beaten oil, of the fourth part of a hin, and wine for libation of the same measure.€€ƒx¡And the other lamb thou shalt offer in the evening, according to the rite of the morning oblation, and according to what we have said, for a savour of sweetness:€€ƒx´It is a sacrifice to the Lord, by perpetual oblation unto your generations, at the door of the tabernacle of the testimony before the Lord, where I will appoint to speak unto thee.€€ƒx_And there will I command the children of Israel, and the altar shall be sanctified by my glory.€€ƒx…I will sanctify also the tabernacle of the testimony with the altar, and Aaron with his sons, to do the office of priesthood unto me.€€ƒxOAnd I will dwell in the midst of the children of Israel, and will be their God:€€ƒx–And they shall know that I am the Lord their God, who have brought them out of the land of Egypt, that I might abide among them, I the Lord their God.€€ƒ`€€ƒx=Thou shalt make also an altar to burn incense, of setim wood.€€ƒxIt shall be a cubit in length, and another in breadth, that is, four square, and two in height. Horns shall go out of the same.€€˜&ƒxrAnd two golden rings under the crown on either side, that the bars may be put into them, and the altar be carried.€€ƒxRAnd thou shalt make the bars also of setim wood, and shalt overlay them with gold.€€ƒx¸And thou shalt set the altar over against the veil, that hangeth before the ark of the testimony before the propitiatory wherewith the testimony is covered, where I will speak to thee.€€ƒxtAnd Aaron shall burn sweet smelling incense upon it in the morning. When he shall dress the lamps, he shall burn it:€€ƒx~And when he shall place them in the evening, he shall burn an everlasting incense before the Lord throughout your generations.€€ƒxxYou shall not offer upon it incense of another composition, nor oblation, and victim, neither shall you offer libations.€€ƒxÃAnd Aaron shall pray upon the horns thereof once a year, with the blood of that which was offered for sin; and shall make atonement upon it in your generations. It shall be most holy to the Lord.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxÛWhen thou shalt take the sum of the children of Israel, according to their number, every one of them shall give a price for their souls to the Lord, and there shall be no scourge among them, when they shall be reckoned.€€ƒxµAnd this shall every one give that passeth at the naming, half a sicle according to the standard of the temple. A sicle hath twenty obols. Half a sicle shall be offered to the Lord.€€ƒxUHe that is counted in the number from twenty years and upwards, shall give the price.€€ƒxTThe rich man shall not add to half a sicle, and the poor man shall diminish nothing.€€ƒxçAnd the money received, which was contributed by the children of Israel, thou shalt deliver unto the uses of the tabernacle of the testimony, that it may be a memorial of them before the Lord, and he may be merciful to their souls.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx§Thou shalt make also a brazen laver with its foot to wash in: and thou shalt set it between the tabernacle of the testimony and the altar. And water being put into it:€€ƒx9Aaron and his sons shall wash their hands and feet in it:€€ƒx…When they are going into the tabernacle of the testimony, and when they are to come to the altar, to offer on it incense to the Lord,€€ƒx]Lest perhaps they die. It shall be an everlasting law to him, and to his seed by successions.€€ƒxAnd the Lord spoke to Moses,€€ƒx½Saying: Take spices, of principal and chosen myrrh five hundred sicles, and of cinnamon half so much; that is, two hundred and fifty sicles, of calamus in like manner two hundred and fifty,€€ƒxcAnd of cassia five hundred sicles by the weight of the sanctuary, of oil of olives the measure hin:€€ƒxbAnd thou shalt make the holy oil of unction, an ointment compounded after the art of the perfumer,€€ƒx^And therewith thou shalt anoint the tabernacle of the testimony, and the ark of the testament,€€ƒxeAnd the table with the vessels thereof, the candlestick and furniture thereof, the altars of incense,€€ƒxNAnd of holocaust, and all the furniture that belongeth to the service of them.€€ƒxgAnd thou shalt sanctify all, and they shall be most holy: he that shall touch them shall be sanctified.€€ƒxqThou shalt anoint Aaron and his sons, and shalt sanctify them, that they may do the office of priesthood unto me.€€ƒxtAnd thou shalt say to the children of Israel: This oil of unction shall be holy unto me throughout your generations.€€ƒxThe flesh of man shall not be anointed therewith, and you shall make none other of the same composition, because it is sanctified, and shall be holy unto you.€€ƒxoWhat man soever shall compound such, and shall give thereof to a stranger, he shall be cut off from his people.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to Moses: Take unto thee spices, stacte, and onycha, galbanum of sweet savour, and the clearest frankincense, all shall be of equal weight.€€ƒxˆAnd thou shalt make incense compounded by the work of the perfumer, well tempered together, and pure, and most worthy of sanctification.€€ƒxÆAnd when thou hast beaten all into very small powder, thou shalt set of it before the tabernacle of the testimony, in the place where I will appear to thee. Most holy shall this incense be unto you.€€ƒxXYou shall not make such a composition for your own uses, because it is holy to the Lord.€€ƒxcWhat man soever shall make the like, to enjoy the smell thereof, he shall perish out of his people.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx\Behold, I have called by name Beseleel the son of Uri, the son of Hur, of the tribe of Juda,€€’ƒxMTo devise whatsoever may be artificially made of gold, and silver, and brass,€€ƒx4Of marble, and precious stones, and variety of wood.€€ƒxÎAnd I have given him for his companion Ooliab, the son of Achisamech, of the tribe of Dan. And I have put wisdom in the heart of every skilful man, that they may make all things which I have commanded thee,€€ƒx‹The tabernacle of the covenant, and the ark of the testimony, and the propitiatory, that is over it, and all the vessels of the tabernacle,€€ƒxuAnd the table and the vessels thereof, the most pure candlestick with the vessels thereof, and the altars of incense,€€ƒxAAnd of holocaust, and all their vessels, the laver with its foot,€€ƒx‡The holy vestments in the ministry for Aaron the priest, and for his sons, that they may execute their office, about the sacred things:€€ƒxxThe oil of unction, and the incense of spices in the sanctuary, all things which I have commanded thee, shall they make.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxÎSpeak to the children of Israel, and thou shalt say to them: See that you keep my sabbath; because it is a sign between me and you in your generations that you may know that I am the Lord, who sanctify you.€€ƒxµkeep you my sabbath: for it is holy unto you: he that shall profane it, shall be put to death: he that shall do any work in it, his soul shall perish out of the midst of his people.€€ƒx’Six days shall you do work: in the seventh day is the sabbath, the rest holy to the Lord. Every one that shall do any work on this day, shall die.€€ƒxqLet the children of Israel keep the sabbath, and celebrate it in their generations. It is an everlasting covenant€€ƒx”Between me and the children of Israel, and a perpetual sign. For in six days the Lord made heaven and earth, and in the seventh he ceased from work.€€ƒxˆAnd the Lord, when he had ended these words in Mount Sinai, gave to Moses two stone tables of testimony, written with the finger of God.€€ƒ`€€ƒyAnd the people seeing that Moses delayed to come down from the mount, gathering together against Aaron, said: Arise, make us gods, that may go before us: For as to this Moses, the man that brought us out of the land of Egypt, we know not what has befallen him.€€ƒx€And Aaron said to them: Take the golden earrings from the ears of your wives, and your sons and daughters, and bring them to me.€€˜#ƒxÁAnd when he had received them, he fashioned them by founders' work, and made of them a molten calf. And they said: These are thy gods, O Israel, that have brought thee out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒxAnd when Aaron saw this, he built an altar before it, and made proclamation by a crier's voice, saying To morrow is the solemnity of the Lord.€€ƒxŠAnd rising in the morning, they offered holocausts, and peace victims, and the people sat down to eat and drink, and they rose up to play.€€ƒx‚And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying: Go, get thee down: thy people, which thou hast brought out of the land of Egypt, hath sinned.€€ƒxÿThey have quickly strayed from the way which thou didst shew them: and they have made to themselves a molten calf, and have adored it, and sacrificing victims to it, have said: These are thy gods, O Israel, that have brought thee out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒxHAnd again the Lord said to Moses: I see that this people is stiffnecked:€€ƒx}Let me alone, that my wrath may be kindled against them, and that I may destroy them, and I will make of thee a great nation.€€ƒxÅBut Moses besought the Lord his God, saying: Why, O Lord, is thy indignation enkindled against thy people, whom thou hast brought out of the land of Egypt, with great power, and with a mighty hand?€€ƒxÛLet not the Egyptians say, I beseech thee: He craftily brought them out, that he might kill them in the mountains, and destroy them from the earth: let thy anger cease, and be appeased upon the wickedness of thy people.€€ƒxúRemember Abraham, Isaac, and Israel, thy servants, to whom thou sworest by thy own self, saying: I will multiply your seed as the stars of heaven: and this whole land that I have spoken of, I will give to your seed, and you shall possess it for ever:€€ƒxUAnd the Lord was appeased from doing the evil which he had spoken against his people.€€ƒxoAnd Moses returned from the mount, carrying the two tables of the testimony in his hand, written on both sides,€€ƒxNAnd made by the work of God; the writing also of God was graven in the tables.€€ƒxlAnd Josue hearing the noise of the people shouting, said to Moses: The noise of battle is heard in the camp.€€ƒx‰But he answered: It is not the cry of men encouraging to fight, nor the shout of men compelling to flee: but I hear the voice of singers.€€ƒx§And when he came nigh to the camp, he saw the calf, and the dances: and being very angry, he threw the tables out of his hand, and broke them at the foot of the mount:€€ƒx¦And laying hold of the calf which they had made, he burnt it, and beat it to powder, which he strewed into water, and gave thereof to the children of Israel to drink.€€ƒxoAnd he said to Aaron: What has this people done to thee, that thou shouldst bring upon them a most heinous sin?€€ƒxlAnd he answered him: Let not my lord be offended; for thou knowest this people, that they are prone to evil.€€ƒxœThey said to me: make us gods, that may go before us; for as to this Moses, who brought us forth out of the land of Egypt, we know not what is befallen him.€€ƒxˆAnd I said to them: Which of you hath any gold? and they took and brought it to me; and I cast it into the fire, and this calf came out.€€ƒxAnd when Moses saw that the people were naked, (for Aaron had stripped them by occasion of the shame of the filth, and had set them naked among their enemies)€€ƒx§Then standing in the gate of the camp, he said: If any man be on the Lord's side, let him join with me. And all the sons of Levi gathered themselves together unto him:€€ƒxŞAnd he said to them: Thus saith the Lord God of Israel: Put every man his sword upon his thigh: go, and return from gate to gate through the midst of the camp, and let every man kill his brother, and friend, and neighbour.€€ƒx|And the sons of Levi did according to the words of Moses, and there were slain that day about three and twenty thousand men.€€ƒx“And Moses said: You have consecrated your hands this day to the Lord, every man in his son and in his brother, that a blessing may be given to you.€€ƒx³And when the next day was come, Moses spoke to the people: You have sinned a very great sin: I will go up to the Lord, if by any means I may be able to entreat him for your crime.€€ƒx¬And returning to the Lord, he said: I beseech thee: this people hath sinned a heinous sin, and they have made to themselves gods of gold: either forgive them this trespass,€€ƒxDOr if thou do not, strike me out of the book that thou hast written.€€ƒx\And the Lord answered him: He that hath sinned against me, him will I strike out of my book:€€ƒxšBut go thou, and lead this people whither I have told thee: my angel shall go before thee. And I in the day of revenge will visit this sin also of theirs.€€ƒxaThe Lord therefore struck the people for the guilt, on occasion of the calf which Aaron had made.€€ƒ`€€ƒxôAnd the Lord spoke to Moses, saying: Go, get thee up from this place, thou and thy people which thou hast brought out of the land of Egypt, into the land concerning which I swore to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, saying: To thy seed I will give it:€€ƒx¢And I will send an angel before thee, that I may cast out the Chanaanite, and the Amorrhite, and the Hethite, and the Pherezite, and the Hevite, and the Jebusite,€€—ƒxlAnd the people hearing these very bad tidings, mourned: and no man put on his ornaments according to custom.€€ƒxçAnd the Lord said to Moses: Say to the children of Israel: Thou art a stiffnecked people, once I shall come up in the midst of thee, and shall destroy thee. Now presently lay aside thy ornaments, that I may know what to do to thee.€€ƒxDSo the children of Israel laid aside their ornaments by Mount Horeb.€€ƒxïMoses also taking the tabernacle, pitched it without the camp afar off, and called the name thereof, The tabernacle of the covenant. And all the people, that had any question, went forth to the tabernacle of the covenant, without the camp.€€ƒxºAnd when Moses went forth to the tabernacle, all the people rose up, and every one stood in the door of his pavilion, and they beheld the back of Moses, till he went into the tabernacle.€€ƒxŒAnd when he was gone into the tabernacle of the covenant, the pillar of the cloud came down, and stood at the door, and he spoke with Moses.€€ƒx‡And all saw that the pillar of the cloud stood at the door of the tabernacle. And they stood and worshipped at the doors of their tent.€€ƒxÈAnd the Lord spoke to Moses face to face, as a man is wont to speak to his friend. And when he returned into the camp, his servant Josue, the son of Nun, a young man, departed not from the tabernacle.€€ƒxäAnd Moses said to the Lord: Thou commandest me to lead forth this people; and thou dost not let me know whom thou wilt send with me, especially whereas thou hast said: I know thee by name, and thou hast found favour in my sight.€€ƒxœIf therefore I have found favour in thy sight, shew me thy face, that I may know thee, and may find grace before thy eyes: look upon thy people this nation.€€ƒxKAnd the Lord said: My face shall go before thee, and I will give thee rest.€€ƒxSAnd Moses said: If thou thyself dost not go before, bring us not out of this place.€€ƒx¶For how shall we be able to know, I and thy people, that we have found grace in thy sight, unless thou walk with us, that we may be glorified by all people that dwell upon the earth?€€ƒx’And the Lord said to Moses: This word also, which thou hast spoken, will I do; for thou hast found grace before me, and thee I have known by name.€€ƒxAnd he said: Shew me thy glory.€€ƒx¹He answered: I will shew thee all good, and I will proclaim in the name of the Lord before thee: and I will have mercy on whom I will, and I will be merciful to whom it shall please me.€€ƒxRAnd again he said: Thou canst not see my face: for man shall not see me, and live.€€ƒxWAnd again he said: Behold there is a place with me, and thou shalt stand upon the rock.€€ƒxtAnd when my glory shall pass, I will set thee in a hole of the rock, and protect thee with my righthand till I pass:€€ƒx_And I will take away my hand, and thou shalt see my back parts: but my face thou canst not see.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAnd after this he said: Hew thee two tables of stone like unto the former, and I will write upon them the words, which were in the tables, which thou brokest.€€ƒx‚Be ready in the morning, that thou mayst forthwith go up into Mount Sinai, and thou shalt stand with me upon the top of the mount.€€˜#ƒx±Then he cut out two tables of stone, such as had been before; and rising very early he went up into the Mount Sinai, as the Lord had commanded him, carrying with him the tables.€€ƒxdAnd when the Lord was come down in a cloud, Moses stood with him, calling upon the name of the Lord.€€ƒx‚And when he passed before him, he said: O the Lord, the Lord God, merciful and gracious, patient and of much compassion, and true,€€ƒxıWho keepest mercy unto thousands: who takest away iniquity, and wickedness, and sin, and no man of himself is innocent before thee. Who renderest the iniquity of the fathers to the children, and to the grandchildren unto the third and fourth generation.€€ƒxIAnd Moses making haste, bowed down prostrate unto the earth, and adoring,€€ƒx²Said: If I have found grace in thy sight, O Lord, I beseech thee that thou wilt go with us, (for it is a stiffnecked people) and take away our iniquities and sin, and possess us.€€ƒxòThe Lord answered: I will make a covenant in the sight of all, I will do signs such as were never seen upon the earth, nor in any nations; that this people, in the midst of whom thou art, may see the terrible work of the Lord which I will do.€€ƒxÂObserve all things which this day I command thee: I myself will drive out before thy face the Amorrhite, and the Chanaanite, and the Hethite, and the Pherezite, and the Hevite, and the Jebusite.€€ƒx^Beware thou never join in friendship with the inhabitants of that land, which may be thy ruin:€€ƒxHBut destroy their altars, break their statues and cut down their groves:€€ƒxMAdore not any strange god. The Lord his name is jealous, he is a jealous God.€€ƒx¾Make no covenant with the men of those countries; lest, when they have committed fornication with their gods, and have adored their idols, some one call thee to eat of the things sacrificed.€€ƒx´Neither shalt thou take of their daughters a wife for thy son, lest after they themselves have committed fornication, they make thy sons also to commit fornication with their gods.€€ƒx/Thou shalt not make to thyself any molten gods.€€ƒxÜThou shalt keep the feast of the unleavened bread. Seven days shalt thou eat unleavened bread, as I commanded thee in the time of the month of the new corn: for in the month of the spring time thou camest out from Egypt.€€ƒxvAll of the male kind that openeth the womb, shall be mine. Of all beasts; both of oxen and of sheep, it shall be mine.€€ƒxÌThe firstling of an ass thou shalt redeem with a sheep: but if thou wilt not give a price for it, it shall be slain. The firstborn of thy sons thou shalt redeem: neither shalt thou appear before me empty.€€ƒxQSix days shalt thou work, the seventh day thou shalt cease to plough and to reap.€€ƒx¨Thou shalt keep the feast of weeks with the firstfruits of the corn of thy wheat harvest, and the feast when the time of the year returneth that all things are laid in.€€ƒxgThree times in the year all thy males shall appear in the sight of the almighty Lord the God of Israel.€€ƒxßFor when I shall have taken away the nations from thy face, and shall have enlarged thy borders, no man shall lie in wait against thy land when thou shalt go up, and appear in the sight of the Lord thy God thrice in a year.€€ƒxœThou shalt not offer the blood of my sacrifice upon leaven; neither shall there remain in the morning any thing of the victim of the solemnity of the Phase.€€ƒxŠThe first of the fruits of thy ground thou shalt offer in the house of the Lord thy God. Thou shalt not boil a kid in the milk of his dam.€€ƒxsAnd the Lord said to Moses: Write thee these words, by which I have made a covenant both with thee and with Israel.€€ƒxAnd he was there with the Lord forty days and forty nights: he neither ate bread nor drank water, and he wrote upon the tables the ten words of the covenant.€€ƒx£And when Moses came down from the Mount Sinai, he held the two tables of the testimony, and he knew not that his face was horned from the conversation of the Lord.€€ƒx_And Aaron and the children of Israel seeing the face of Moses horned, were afraid to come near.€€ƒxwAnd being called by him, they returned, both Aaron and the rulers of the congregation. And after that he spoke to them,€€ƒx}And all the children of Israel came to him: and he gave them in commandment all that he had heard of the Lord on Mount Sinai.€€ƒx6And having done speaking, he put a veil upon his face.€€ƒx­But when he went in to the Lord, and spoke with him, he took it away until he came forth, and then he spoke to the children of Israel all things that had been commanded him.€€ƒx€And they saw that the face of Moses when he came out was horned, but he covered his face again, if at any time he spoke to them.€€ƒ`€€ƒx˜And all the multitude of the children of Israel being gathered together, he said to them: These are the things which the Lord hath commanded to be done:€€ƒx¡Six days you shall do work; the seventh day shall be holy unto you, the sabbath and the rest of the Lord: he that shall do any work on it, shall be put to death.€€˜#ƒxoAnd Moses said to all the assembly of the children of Israel: This is the word the Lord hath commanded, saying:€€ƒx–Set aside with you firstfruits to the Lord. Let every one that is willing and hath a ready heart, offer them to the Lord: gold, and silver, and brass,€€ƒxGViolet and purple, and scarlet twice dyed, and fine linen, goats' hair,€€ƒx@And rams' skins dyed red, and violet coloured skins, setim wood,€€ƒxIAnd oil to maintain lights, and to make ointment, and most sweet incense,€€ƒxQOnyx stones, and precious stones, for the adorning of the ephod and the rational.€€ƒxTWhosoever of you is wise, let him come, and make that which the Lord hath commanded:€€ƒx†To wit, the tabernacle, and the roof thereof, and the cover, the rings, and the board-work with the bars, the pillars and the sockets:€€ƒxOThe ark and the staves, the propitiatory, and the veil that is drawn before it:€€ƒxGThe table with the bars and the vessels, and the loaves of proposition:€€ƒxrThe candlestick to bear up the lights, the vessels thereof and the lamps, and the oil for the nourishing of fires:€€ƒxThe altar of incense, and the bars, and the oil of unction, and the incense of spices: the hanging at the door of the tabernacle:€€ƒxjThe altar of holocaust, and its grate of brass, with the bars and vessels thereof: the laver and its foot:€€ƒxcThe curtains of the court, with the pillars and the sockets, the hanging in the doors of the entry.€€ƒxFThe pins of the tabernacle, and of the court, with their little cords:€€ƒx The vestments that are to be used in the ministry of the sanctuary, the vesture of Aaron the high priest, and of his sons, to do the office of priesthood to me.€€ƒxUAnd all the multitude of the children of Israel going out from the presence of Moses,€€ƒx¸Offered firstfruits to the Lord with a most ready and devout mind, to make the work of the tabernacle of the testimony. Whatever was necessary to the service and to the holy vestments,€€ƒx€Both men and women gave bracelets and earrings, rings and tablets: every vessel of gold was set aside to be offered to the Lord.€€ƒx„If any man had violet, and purple, and scarlet twice dyed, fine linen and goats' hair, ramskins dyed red, and violet coloured skins,€€ƒxWMetal of silver and brass, they offered it to the Lord, and setim wood for divers uses.€€ƒxfThe skilful women also gave such things as they had spun, violet, purple, and scarlet, and fine linen,€€ƒx0And goats' hair, giving all of their own accord.€€ƒxYBut the princes offered onyx stones, and precious stones, for the ephod and the rational,€€ƒxsAnd spices and oil for the lights, and for the preparing of ointment, and to make the incense of most sweet savour.€€ƒxÎAll, both men and women, with devout mind offered gifts, that the works might be done which the Lord had commanded by the hand of Moses. All the children of Israel dedicated voluntary offerings to the Lord.€€ƒxAnd Moses said to the children of Israel: Behold, the Lord hath called by name Beseleel, the son of Uri, the son of Hur, of the tribe of Juda,€€ƒxkAnd hath filled him with the spirit of God, with wisdom and understanding, and knowledge, and all learning,€€ƒx3To devise and to work in gold and silver and brass,€€ƒxYAnd in engraving stones, and in carpenters' work. Whatsoever can be devised artificially,€€ƒxTHe hath given in his heart: Ooliab also, the son of Achisamech, of the tribe of Dan:€€ƒxÔBoth of them hath he instructed with wisdom, to do carpenters' work, and tapestry, and embroidery in blue and purple, and scarlet twice dyed, and fine linen, and to weave all things, and to invent all new things.€€ƒ`€€ƒxåBeseleel therefore, and Ooliab, and every wise man, to whom the Lord gave wisdom and understanding, to know how to work artificially, made the things that are necessary for the uses of the sanctuary, and which the Lord commanded.€€ƒx«And when Moses had called them, and every skilful man, to whom the Lord had given wisdom, and such as of their own accord had offered themselves to the making of the work,€€˜&ƒx0Whereupon the workmen being constrained to come,€€ƒx:Said to Moses: The people offereth more than is necessary.€€ƒx·Moses therefore commanded proclamation to be made by the crier's voice: Let neither man nor woman offer any more for the work of the sanctuary. And so they ceased from offering gifts,€€ƒxDBecause the things that were offered did suffice, and were too much.€€ƒxÚAnd all the men that were wise of heart, to accomplish the work of the tabernacle, made ten curtains of twisted fine linen, and violet, and purple, and scarlet twice dyed, with varied work, and the art of embroidering:€€ƒxpThe length of one curtain was twenty-eight cubits, and the breadth four: all the curtains were of the same size.€€ƒxZAnd he joined five curtains, one to another, and the other five he coupled one to another.€€ƒx{He made also loops of violet in the edge of one curtain on both sides, and in the edge of the other curtain in like manner,€€ƒxWThat the loops might meet one against another, and might be joined each with the other.€€ƒx~Whereupon also he cast fifty rings of gold, that might catch the loops of the curtains, and they might be made one tabernacle.€€ƒxQHe made also eleven curtains of goats' hair, to cover the roof of the tabernacle:€€ƒx`One curtain was thirty cubits long, and four cubits broad: all the curtains were of one measure.€€ƒx7Five of which he joined apart, and the other six apart.€€ƒx‡And he made fifty loops in the edge of one curtain, and fifty in the edge of another curtain, that they might be joined one to another.€€ƒx€And fifty buckles of brass wherewith the roof might be knit together, that of all the curtains there might be made one covering.€€ƒxmHe made also a cover for the tabernacle of rams' skins dyed red; and another cover over that of violet skins.€€ƒxAHe made also the boards of the tabernacle of setim wood standing.€€ƒxQThe length of one board was ten cubits; and the breadth was one cubit and a half.€€ƒx—There were two mortises throughout every board, that one might be joined to the other. And in this manner he made for all the boards of the tabernacle.€€ƒx1Of which twenty were at the south side southward,€€ƒx—With forty sockets of silver, two sockets were put under one board on the two sides of the corners, where the mortises of the sides end in the corners.€€ƒx[At that side also of the tabernacle, that looketh towards the north, he made twenty boards,€€ƒx:With forty sockets of silver, two sockets for every board.€€ƒxkBut against the west, to wit, at that side of the tabernacle, which looketh to the sea, he made six boards,€€ƒx7And two others at each corner of the tabernacle behind:€€ƒx}Which were also joined from beneath unto the top, and went together into one joint. Thus he did on both sides at the corners:€€ƒxqSo there were in all eight boards, and they had sixteen sockets of silver, to wit, two sockets under every board.€€ƒx`He made also bars of setim wood, five to hold together the boards of one side of the tabernacle,€€ƒx•And five others to join together the boards of the other side; and besides these, five other bars at the west side of the tabernacle towards the sea.€€ƒx[He made also another bar, that might come by the midst of the boards from corner to corner.€€ƒxÓAnd the boards themselves he overlaid with gold casting for them sockets of silver. And their rings he made of gold, through which the bars might be drawn: and he covered the bars themselves with plates of gold.€€ƒxtHe made also a veil of violet, and purple, scarlet and fine twisted linen, varied and distinguished with embroidery:€€ƒxqAnd four pillars of setim wood, which with their heads he overlaid with gold, casting for them sockets of silver.€€ƒxŠHe made also a hanging in the entry of the tabernacle of violet, purple, scarlet, and fine twisted linen, with the work of an embroiderer.€€ƒxbAnd five pillars with their heads, which he covered with gold, and their sockets he cast of brass.€€ƒ`€€ƒxáAnd Beseleel made also, the ark of setim wood: it was two cubits and a half in length, and a cubit and a half in breadth, and the height was of one cubit and a half: and he overlaid it with the purest gold within and without.€€ƒx.And he made to it a crown of gold round about,€€˜ƒx<And he made bars of setim wood, which he overlaid with gold,€€ƒxMAnd he put them into the rings that were at the sides of the ark to carry it.€€ƒx‹He made also the propitiatory, that is, the oracle, of the purest gold, two cubits and a half in length, and a cubit and a half in breadth.€€ƒxUTwo cherubims also of beaten gold, which he set on the two sides of the propitiatory:€€ƒxˆOne cherub in the top of one side, and the other cherub in the top of the other side: two cherubims at the two ends of the propitiatory,€€ƒxhSpreading their wings, and covering the propitiatory, and looking one towards the other, and towards it.€€ƒx~He made also the table of setim wood, in length two cubits, and in breadth one cubit, and in height it was a cubit and a half.€€ƒxVAnd he overlaid it with the finest gold, and he made to it a golden ledge round about,€€ƒxzAnd to the ledge itself he made a polished crown of gold, of four fingers breadth, and upon the same another golden crown.€€ƒx[And he cast four rings of gold, which he put in the four corners at each foot of the table,€€ƒxWOver against the crown: and he put the bars into them, that the table might be carried.€€ƒxLThe bars also themselves he made of setim wood, and overlaid them with gold.€€ƒxAnd the vessels for the divers uses of the table, dishes, bowls, and cups, and censers of pure gold, wherein the libations are to be offered.€€ƒxHe made also the candlestick of beaten work of the finest gold. from the shaft whereof its branches, its cups, and bowls, and lilies came out:€€ƒxISix on the two sides: three branches on one side, and three on the other.€€ƒxúThree cups in manner of a nut on each branch, and bowls withal and lilies: and three cups of the fashion of a nut in another branch, and bowls withal and lilies. The work of the six branches, that went out from the shaft of the candlestick was equal.€€ƒxlAnd in the shaft itself were four cups after the manner of a nut, and bowls withal at every one, and lilies:€€ƒxhAnd bowls under two branches in three places, which together made six branches going out from one shaft.€€ƒxYSo both the bowls, and the branches were of the same, all beaten work of the purest gold.€€ƒx}He made also the seven lamps with their snuffers, and the vessels where the snuffings were to be put out, of the purest gold.€€ƒxFThe candlestick with all the vessels thereof weighed a talent of gold.€€ƒxœHe made also the alter of incense of setim wood, being a cubit on every side foursquare, and in height two cubits: from the corners of which went out horns.€€ƒxVAnd he overlaid it with the purest gold, with its grate, and the sides, and the horns.€€ƒx¡And he made to it a crown of gold round about, and two golden rings under the crown at each side, that the bars might be put into them, and the altar be carried.€€ƒxZAnd the bars themselves he made also of setim wood, and overlaid them with plates of gold.€€ƒx…He compounded also the oil for the ointment of sanctification, and incense of the purest spices, according to the work of a perfumer.€€ƒ`€€ƒx[He made also the altar of holocaust of setim wood, five cubits square, and three in height:€€ƒxUThe horns whereof went out from the corners, and he overlaid it with plates of brass.€€˜ƒxlAnd he made the grate thereof of brass, in manner of a net, and under it in the midst of the altar a hearth,€€ƒxVCasting four rings at the four ends of the net at the top, to put in bars to carry it:€€ƒxKAnd he made the bars of setim wood, and overlaid them with plates of brass:€€ƒx™And he drew them through the rings that stood out in the sides of the altar. And the altar itself was not solid, but hollow, of boards, and empty within.€€ƒxHe made also the laver of brass, with the foot thereof, of the mirrors of the women that watched at the door of the tabernacle.€€ƒxjHe made also the court, in the south side whereof were hangings of fine twisted linen of a hundred cubits.€€ƒxsTwenty pillars of brass with their sockets, the heads of the pillars, and the whole graving of the work, of silver.€€ƒx’In like manner at the north side the hangings, the pillars, and the sockets and heads of the pillars were of the same measure, and work and metal.€€ƒxÂBut on that side that looketh to the west, there were hangings of fifty cubits, ten pillars of brass with their sockets, and the heads of the pillars, and all the graving of the work, of silver.€€ƒx@Moreover, towards the east he prepared hangings of fifty cubits:€€ƒxPFifteen cubits of which, were on one side with three pillars, and their sockets:€€ƒx¦And on the other side (for between the two he made the entry of the tabernacle) there were hangings equally of fifteen cubits, and three pillars, and as many sockets.€€ƒx<All the hangings of the court were woven with twisted linen.€€ƒx—The sockets of the pillars were of brass, and their heads with all their gravings of silver: and he overlaid the pillars of the court also with silver.€€ƒxÙAnd he made in the entry thereof an embroidered hanging of violet, purple, scarlet, and fine twisted linen, that was twenty cubits long, and five cubits high, according to the measure of all the hangings of the court.€€ƒxfAnd the pillars in the entry were four, with sockets of brass, and their heads and gravings of silver.€€ƒxNThe pins also of the tabernacle and of the court round about he made of brass.€€ƒxÊThese are the instruments of the tabernacle of the testimony, which were counted according to the commandment of Moses, in the ceremonies of the Levites, by the hand of Ithamar, son of Aaron the priest:€€ƒxnWhich Beseleel, the son of Uri, the son of Hur of the tribe of Juda, had made, as the Lord commanded by Moses.€€ƒxËHaving for his companion Ooliab, the son of Achisamech, of the tribe of Dan: who also was an excellent artificer in wood, and worker in tapestry and embroidery in violet, purple, scarlet, and fine linen.€€ƒxÅAll the gold that was spent in the work of the sanctuary, and that was offered in gifts, was nine and twenty talents, and seven hundred and thirty sicles according to the standard of the sanctuary.€€ƒx§And it was offered by them that went to be numbered, from twenty years old and upwards, of six hundred and three thousand five hundred and fifty men able to bear arms.€€ƒx‰There were moreover a hundred talents of silver, whereof were cast the sockets of the sanctuary, and of the entry where the veil hangeth.€€ƒx]A hundred sockets were made of a hundred talents, one talent being reckoned for every socket.€€ƒxxAnd of the thousand seven hundred and seventy-five he made the heads of the pillars, which also he overlaid with silver.€€ƒxcAnd there were offered of brass also seventy-two thousand talents, and four hundred sicles besides,€€ƒx³Of which were cast the sockets in the entry of the tabernacle of the testimony, and the altar of brass with the grate thereof, and also the vessels that belong to the use thereof.€€ƒx‡And the sockets of the court as well round about as in the entry thereof, and the pins of the tabernacle, and of the court round about.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAnd he made, of violet and purple, scarlet and fine linen, the vestments for Aaron to wear when he ministered in the holy places, as the Lord commanded Moses.€€ƒx`So he made an ephod of gold, violet, and purple, and scarlet twice dyed, and fine twisted linen,€€˜+ƒxCAnd two borders coupled one to the other in the top on either side,€€ƒxBAnd a girdle of the same colours, as the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒxHe prepared also two onyx stones, fast set and closed in gold, and graven, by the art of a lapidary, with the names of the children of Israel:€€ƒxuAnd he set them in the sides of the ephod, for a memorial of the children of Israel, as the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒx›He made also a rational with embroidered work, according to the work of the ephod, of gold, violet, purple, and scarlet twice dyed, and fine twisted linen:€€ƒx-Foursquare, double, of the measure of a span.€€ƒxcAnd he set four rows of precious stones in it. In the first row was a sardius, a topaz, an emerald.€€ƒx5In the second, a carbuncle, a sapphire, and a jasper.€€ƒx4In the third, a ligurius, an agate, and an amethyst.€€ƒxZIn the fourth, a chrysolite, an onyx, and a beryl, set and enclosed in gold by their rows.€€ƒxsAnd the twelve stones, were engraved with the names of the twelve tribes of Israel, each one with its several name.€€ƒxXThey made also in the rational little chains, linked one to another, of the purest gold,€€ƒx`And two hooks, and as many rings of gold. And they set the rings on either side of the rational,€€ƒx{On which rings the two golden chains should hang, which they put into the hooks that stood out in the corners of the ephod.€€ƒxjThese both before and behind so answered one another, that the ephod and the rational were bound together,€€ƒx·Being fastened to the girdle, and strongly coupled with rings, which a violet fillet joined, lest they should flag loose, and be moved one from the other, as the Lord commanded Moses.€€ƒx4They made also the tunic of the ephod all of violet,€€ƒxaAnd a hole for the head in the upper part at the middle, and a woven border round about the hole:€€ƒxXAnd beneath at the feet pomegranates of violet, purple, scarlet, and fine twisted linen:€€ƒxtAnd little bells of the purest gold, which they put between the pomegranates at the bottom of the tunic round about:€€ƒx”To wit, a bell of gold, and a pomegranate, wherewith the high priest went adorned, when he discharged his ministry, as the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒxHThey made also fine linen tunics with woven work for Aaron and his sons:€€ƒx2And mitres with their little crowns of fine linen:€€ƒx!And linen breeches of fine linen:€€ƒx€And a girdle of fine twisted linen, violet, purple, and scarlet twice dyed, of embroidery work, as the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒxThey made also the plate of sacred veneration of the purest gold, and they wrote on it with the engraving of a lapidary: The Holy of the Lord:€€ƒxXAnd they fastened it to the mitre with a violet fillet, as the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒxSo all the work of the tabernacle and of the roof of the testimony was finished: and the children of Israel did all things which the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒx‡And they offered the tabernacle, and the roof, and the whole furniture, the rings, the boards, the bars, the pillars and their sockets,€€ƒxGThe cover of rams' skins dyed red, and the other cover of violet skins,€€ƒx.The veil, the ark, the bars, the propitiatory,€€ƒxCThe table, with the vessels thereof, and the loaves of proposition:€€ƒxDThe candlestick, the lamps, and the furniture of them, with the oil:€€ƒx?The altar of gold, and the ointment, and the incense of spices:€€ƒx/And the hanging in the entry of the tabernacle:€€ƒx§The altar of brass, the grate, the bars, and all the vessels thereof: the laver, with the foot thereof: the hangings of the court, and the pillars, with their sockets:€€ƒxÛThe hanging in the entry of the court, and the little cords, and the pins thereof. Nothing was wanting of the vessels, that were commanded to be made for the ministry of the tabernacle, and for the roof of the covenant.€€ƒxXThe vestments also, which the priests, to wit, Aaron and his sons, use in the sanctuary,€€ƒx:The children of Israel offered, as the Lord had commanded.€€ƒx8And when Moses saw all things finished, he blessed them.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx_The first month, the first day of the month, thou shalt set up the tabernacle of the testimony,€€˜&ƒx“And thou shalt bring in the table, and set upon it the things that are commanded according to the rite. The candlestick shall stand with its lamps,€€ƒxAnd the altar of gold, whereon the incense is burnt before the ark of the testimony. Thou shalt put the hanging in the entry of the tabernacle,€€ƒx%And before it the altar of holocaust.€€ƒxRThe laver between the altar and the tabernacle, and thou shalt fill it with water.€€ƒxHAnd thou shalt encompass the court with hangings, and the entry thereof.€€ƒxoAnd thou shalt take the oil of unction and anoint the tabernacle with its vessels, that they may be sanctified:€€ƒx+The altar of holocaust and all its vessels:€€ƒxgThe laver with its foot: thou shalt consecrate all with the oil of unction, that they may be most holy.€€ƒxzAnd thou shalt bring Aaron and his sons to the door of the tabernacle of the testimony, and having washed them with water,€€ƒxThou shalt put on them the holy vestments, that they may minister to me, and that the unction of them may prosper to an everlasting priesthood.€€ƒx.And Moses did all that the Lord had commanded.€€ƒx`So in the first month of the second year, the first day of the month, the tabernacle was set up.€€ƒxcAnd Moses reared it up, and placed the boards and the sockets and the bars, and set up the pillars,€€ƒx\And spread the roof over the tabernacle, putting over it a cover, as the Lord had commanded.€€ƒxUAnd he put the testimony in the ark, thrusting bars underneath, and the oracle above.€€ƒxvAnd when he had brought the ark into the tabernacle, he drew the veil before it to fulfil the commandment of the Lord.€€ƒx]And he set the table in the tabernacle of the testimony, at the north side, without the veil,€€ƒxRSetting there in order the loaves of proposition, as the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒxiHe set the candlestick also in the tabernacle of the testimony, over against the table on the south side,€€ƒxAPlacing the lamps in order, according to the precept of the Lord.€€ƒxUHe set also the altar of gold under the roof of the testimony, over against the veil,€€ƒxIAnd burnt upon it the incense of spices, as the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒxLAnd he put also the hanging in the entry of the tabernacle of the testimony,€€ƒxˆAnd the altar of holocaust in the entry of the testimony, offering the holocaust, and the sacrifices upon it, as the Lord had commanded.€€ƒxbAnd he set the laver between the tabernacle of the testimony and the altar, filling it with water.€€ƒx?And Moses and Aaron, and his sons, washed their hands and feet,€€ƒxkWhen they went into the tabernacle of the covenant, and went to the altar, as the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒxHe set up also the court round about the tabernacle and the altar, drawing the hanging in the entry thereof. After all things were perfected,€€ƒxWThe cloud covered the tabernacle of the testimony, and the glory of the Lord filled it.€€ƒxNeither could Moses go into the tabernacle of the covenant, the cloud covering all things, and the majesty of the Lord shining, for the cloud had covered all.€€ƒxjIf at any time the cloud removed from the tabernacle, the children of Israel went forward by their troops:€€ƒx1If it hung over, they remained in the same place.€€ƒx™For the cloud of the Lord hung over the tabernacle by day, and a fire by night, in the sight of all the children of Israel throughout all their mansions.€€ƒuThe Book of Leviticus€€ƒ`€€ƒxYAnd the Lord called Moses, and spoke to him from the tabernacle of the testimony, saying:€€ƒx³Speak to the children of Israel, and thou shalt say to them: The man among you that shall offer to the Lord a sacrifice of the cattle, that is, offering victims of oxen and sheep:€€ƒx™If his offering be a holocaust, and of the herd, he shall offer a male without blemish, at the door of the testimony, to make the Lord favourable to him.€€cLev¤dnameiLeviticusiosis_namecLevlabbreviationcLevhchapters˜‘ƒxLAnd when they have flayed the victim, they shall cut the joints into pieces:€€ƒxLAnd shall put fire on the altar, having before laid in order a pile of wood.€€ƒx|And they shall lay the parts that are cut out in order thereupon: to wit, the head, and all things that cleave to the liver;€€ƒxThe entrails and feet being washed with water. And the priest shall burn them upon the altar for a holocaust, and a sweet savour to the Lord.€€ƒxnAnd if the offering be of the flocks, a holocaust of sheep or of goats, he shall offer a male without blemish.€€ƒx¬And he shall immolate it at the side of the altar that looketh to the north, before the Lord: but the sons of Aaron shall pour the blood thereof upon the altar round about.€€ƒx’And they shall divide the joints, the head, and all that cleave to the liver: and shall lay them upon the wood, under which the fire is to be put.€€ƒx®But the entrails and the feet they shall wash with water. And the priest shall offer it all and burn it all upon the altar for a holocaust, and most sweet savour to the Lord.€€ƒx\But if the oblation of a holocaust to the Lord be of birds, of turtles, or of young pigeons:€€ƒx¥The priest shall offer it at the altar: and twisting back the neck, and breaking the place of the wound, he shall make the blood run down upon the brim of the altar.€€ƒx•But the crop of the throat, and the feathers he shall cast beside the altar at the east side, in the place where the ashes are wont to be poured out.€€ƒxÖAnd he shall break the pinions thereof, and shall not cut, nor divide it with a knife: and shall burn it upon the altar, putting fire under the wood. It is a holocaust and oblation of most sweet savour to the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx˜When any one shall offer an oblation of sacrifice to the Lord, his offering shall be of fine flour: and he shall pour oil upon it, and put frankincense,€€ƒxÙAnd shall bring it to the sons of Aaron the priests. And one of them shall take a handful of the flour and oil, and all the frankincense; and shall put it a memorial upon the altar for a most sweet savour to the Lord.€€ƒxnAnd the remnant of the sacrifice shall be Aaron's, and his sons', holy of holies of the offerings of the Lord.€€ƒxšBut when thou offerest a sacrifice baked in the oven of flour, to wit, loaves without leaven, tempered with oil, and unleavened wafers, anointed with oil:€€ƒxDThou shalt divide it into little pieces, and shalt pour oil upon it.€€ƒx_And if the sacrifice be from the gridiron, in like manner the flour shall be tempered with oil.€€ƒxXAnd when thou offerest it to the Lord, thou shalt deliver it to the hands of the priest.€€ƒx†And when he hath offered it, he shall take a memorial out of the sacrifice, and burn it upon the altar for a sweet savour to the Lord.€€ƒxeAnd whatsoever is left, shall be Aaron's, and his sons': holy of holies of the offerings of the Lord.€€ƒx‘Every oblation that is offered to the Lord shall be made without leaven: neither shall any leaven or honey be burnt in the sacrifice to the Lord.€€ƒx|You shall offer only the firstfruits of them and gifts: but they shall not be put upon the altar, for a savour of sweetness.€€ƒxÄWhatsoever sacrifice thou offerest, thou shalt season it with salt: neither shalt thou take away the salt of the covenant of thy God from thy sacrifice. In all thy oblations thou shalt offer salt.€€ƒxÍBut if thou offer a gift of the firstfruits of thy corn to the Lord, of the ears yet green, thou shalt dry it at the fire, and break it small like meal; and so shalt thou offer thy firstfruits to the Lord:€€ƒxXPouring oil upon it and putting on frankincense, because it is the oblation of the Lord.€€ƒxWhereof the priest shall burn for a memorial of the gift, part of the corn broken small and of the oil, and all the frankincense.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¢And if his oblation be a sacrifice of peace offerings, and he will offer of the herd, whether male or female: he shall offer them without blemish before the Lord.€€ƒxÏAnd he shall lay his hand upon the head of his victim, which shall be slain in the entry of the tabernacle of the testimony: and the sons of Aaron the priests shall pour the blood round about upon the altar.€€ƒxšAnd they shall offer of the sacrifice of peace offerings, for an oblation to the Lord: the fat that covereth the entrails, and all the fat that is within,€€ƒxuThe two kidneys with the fat wherewith the flanks are covered, and the caul of the liver with the two little kidneys.€€‘ƒxˆBut if his oblation and the sacrifice of peace offering be of the flock, whether he offer male or female, they shall be without blemish.€€ƒx#If he offer a lamb before the Lord:€€ƒxÈHe shall put his hand upon the head of the victim. And it shall be slain in the entry of the tabernacle of the testimony: and the sons of Aaron shall pour the blood thereof round about upon the altar.€€ƒxkAnd they shall offer of the victim of peace offerings, a sacrifice to the Lord: the fat and the whole rump,€€ƒx¿With the kidneys, and the fat that covereth the belly and all the vitals and both the little kidneys, with the fat that is about the flanks, and the caul of the liver with the little kidneys.€€ƒxiAnd the priest shall burn them upon the altar, for the food of the fire, and of the oblation of the Lord.€€ƒx7If his offering be a goat, and he offer it to the Lord:€€ƒxÂHe shall put his hand upon the head thereof: and shall immolate it in the entry of the tabernacle of the testimony. And the sons of Aaron shall pour the blood thereof round about upon the altar.€€ƒx‚And they shall take of it for the food of the Lord's fire, the fat that covereth the belly, and that covereth all the vital parts:€€ƒx€The two little kidneys with the caul that is upon them which is by the flanks, and the fat of the liver with the little kidneys.€€ƒx…And the priest shall burn them upon the altar, for the food of the fire, and of a most sweet savour. All the fat shall be the Lord's.€€ƒxqBy a perpetual law for your generations, and in all your habitations: neither blood nor fat shall you eat at all.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx­Say to the children of Israel: The soul that sinneth through ignorance, and doth any thing concerning any of the commandments of the Lord, which he commanded not to be done:€€ƒx…If the priest that is anointed shall sin, making the people to offend, he shall offer to the Lord for his sin a calf without blemish.€€ƒx•And he shall bring it to the door of the testimony before the Lord: and shall put his hand upon the head thereof, and shall sacrifice it to the Lord.€€˜#ƒxƒAnd having dipped his finger in the blood, he shall sprinkle with it seven times before the Lord, before the veil of the sanctuary.€€ƒyAnd he shall put some of the same blood upon the horns of the altar of the sweet incense most acceptable to the Lord, which is in the tabernacle of the testimony. And he shall pour all the rest of the blood at the foot of the altar of holocaust in the entry of the tabernacle.€€ƒx}And he shall take off the fat of the calf for the sin offering, as well that which covereth the entrails, as all the inwards:€€ƒxThe two little kidneys, and the caul that is upon them, which is by the flanks, and the fat of the liver with the little kidneys:€€ƒxyAs it is taken off from the calf of the sacrifice of peace offerings. And he shall burn them upon the altar of holocaust.€€ƒxVBut the skin and all the flesh with the head and the feet and the bowels and the dung:€€ƒxêAnd the rest of the body, he shall carry forth without the camp into a clean place where the ashes are wont to be poured out: and he shall burn them upon a pile of wood. They shall be burnt in the place where the ashes are poured out.€€ƒx‡And if all the multitude of Israel shall be ignorant, and through ignorance shall do that which is against the commandment of the Lord,€€ƒxƒAnd afterwards shall understand their sin: they shall offer for their sin a calf, and shall bring it to the door of the tabernacle.€€ƒx’And the ancients of the people shall put their hands upon the head thereof before the Lord. And the calf being immolated in the sight of the Lord:€€ƒxZThe priest that is anointed shall carry of the blood into the tabernacle of the testimony.€€ƒxKAnd shall dip his finger in it and sprinkle it seven times before the veil.€€ƒyAnd he shall put of the same blood on the horns of the altar that is before the Lord, in the tabernacle of the testimony. And the rest of the blood he shall pour at the foot of the altar of holocaust, which is at the door of the tabernacle of the testimony.€€ƒxLAnd all the fat thereof he shall take off, and shall burn it upon the altar:€€ƒxDoing so with this calf, as he did also with that before. And the priest praying for them, the Lord will be merciful unto them.€€ƒx“But the calf itself he shall carry forth without the camp, and shall burn it as he did the former calf: because it is for the sin of the multitude.€€ƒxjIf a prince shall sin, and through ignorance do any one of the things that the law of the Lord forbiddeth,€€ƒxoAnd afterwards shall come to know his sin: he shall offer a buck goat without blemish, a sacrifice to the Lord.€€ƒx«And he shall put his hand upon the head thereof: and when he hath immolated it in the place where the holocaust is wont to be slain before the Lord, because it is for sin,€€ƒx©The priest shall dip his finger in the blood of the victim for sin, touching therewith the horns of the altar of holocaust, and pouring out the rest at the foot thereof.€€ƒx¯But the fat he shall burn upon it, as is wont to be done with the victims of peace offerings. And the priest shall pray for him, and for his sin: and it shall be forgiven him.€€ƒx™And if any one of the people of the land shall sin through ignorance, doing any of those things that by the law of the Lord are forbidden, and offending,€€ƒxJAnd shall come to know his sin: he shall offer a she goat without blemish.€€ƒx{And he shall put his hand upon the head of the victim that is for sin: and shall immolate it in the place of the holocaust.€€ƒxAnd the priest shall take of the blood with his finger, and shall touch the horns of the altar of holocaust: and shall pour out the rest at the foot thereof.€€ƒxÔBut taking off all the fat, as is wont to be taken away of the victims of peace offerings, he shall burn it upon the altar, for a sweet savour to the Lord: and he shall pray for him, and it shall be forgiven him.€€ƒxRBut if he offer of the flock a victim for his sin, to wit, an ewe without blemish:€€ƒx…He shall put his hand upon the head thereof, and shall immolate it in the place where the victims of holocausts are wont to be slain.€€ƒx¨And the priest shall take of the blood thereof with his finger, and shall touch the horns of the altar of holocaust: and the rest he shall pour out at the foot thereof.€€ƒyAll the fat also he shall take off, as the fat of the ram that is offered for peace offerings is wont to be taken away: and shall burn it upon the altar, for a burnt sacrifice of the Lord. And he shall pray for him and his sin, and it shall be forgiven him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¯If any one sin, and hear the voice of one swearing, and is a witness either because he himself hath seen, or is privy to it: if he do not utter it, he shall bear his iniquity.€€ƒxÅWhosoever toucheth any unclean thing, either that which hath been killed by a beast, or died of itself, or any other creeping thing: and forgetteth his uncleanness, he is guilty, and hath offended.€€ƒxÌAnd if he touch any thing of the uncleanness of man, according to any uncleanness wherewith he is wont to be defiled: and having forgotten it, come afterwards to know it, he shall be guilty of an offence.€€ƒxÊThe person that sweareth, and uttereth with his lips, that he would do either evil or good, and bindeth the same with an oath, and his word: and having forgotten it afterwards understandeth his offence,€€“ƒxfAnd offer of the flocks an ewe lamb, or a she goat, and the priest shall pray for him and for his sin.€€ƒxBut if he be not able to offer a beast, let him offer two turtles, or two young pigeons to the Lord, one for sin, and the other for a holocaust,€€ƒx½And he shall give them to the priest: who shall offer the first for sin, and twist back the head of it to the little pinions, so that it stick to the neck, and be not altogether broken off.€€ƒx’And of its blood he shall sprinkle the side of the altar: and whatever is left, he shall let it drop at the bottom thereof, because it is for sin.€€ƒx•And the other he shall burn for a holocaust, as is wont to be done. And the priest shall pray for him, and for his sin, and it shall be forgiven him.€€ƒxİAnd if his hand be not able to offer two turtles, or two young pigeons, he shall offer for his sin the tenth part of an ephi of flour. He shall not put oil upon it, nor put any frankincense thereon, because it is for sin.€€ƒxAnd he shall deliver it to the priest, who shall take a handful thereof, and shall burn it upon the altar for a memorial of him that offered it:€€ƒxbPraying for him and making atonement. But the part that is left, he himself shall have for a gift.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒyIf any one shall sin through mistake, transgressing the ceremonies in those things that are sacrificed to the Lord, he shall offer for his offence a ram without blemish out of the flocks, that may be bought for two sicles, according to the weight of the sanctuary.€€ƒxÇAnd he shall make good the damage itself which he hath done, and shall add the fifth part besides, delivering it to the priest, who shall pray for him, offering the ram: and it shall be forgiven him.€€ƒxšIf any one sin through ignorance, and do one of those things which by the law of the Lord are forbidden, and being guilty of sin, understand his iniquity:€€ƒxÔHe shall offer of the flocks a ram without blemish to the priest, according to the measure and estimation of the sin. And the priest shall pray for him, because he did it ignorantly: And it shall be forgiven him,€€ƒx2Because by mistake he trespassed against the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx The Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxÈWhosoever shall sin, and despising the Lord, shall deny to his neighbour the thing delivered to his keeping, which was committed to his trust; or shall by force extort any thing, or commit oppression;€€ƒxŒOr shall find a thing lost, and denying it, shall also swear falsely, or shall do any other of the many things, wherein men are wont to sin:€€ƒx0Being convicted of the offence, he shall restore€€˜ƒx¤Moreover for his sin he shall offer a ram without blemish out of the flock: and shall give it to the priest, according to the estimation and measure of the offence.€€ƒxzAnd he shall pray for him before the Lord: and he shall have forgiveness for every thing in doing of which he bath sinned.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx›Command Aaron and his sons: This is the law of a holocaust. It shall be burnt upon the altar, all night until morning: the fire shall be of the same altar.€€ƒx±The priest shall be vested with the tunick and the linen breeches; and he shall take up the ashes of that which the devouring fire hath burnt: and putting them beside the altar,€€ƒx®Shall put off his former vestments, and being clothed with others, shall carry them forth without the camp, and shall cause them to be consumed to dust in a very clean place.€€ƒxÉAnd the fire on the altar shall always burn, and the priest shall feed it, putting wood on it every day in the morning: and laying on the holocaust, shall burn thereupon the fat of the peace offerings.€€ƒxAThis is the perpetual fire which shall never go out on the altar.€€ƒx~This is the law of the sacrifice and libations, which the children of Aaron shall offer before the Lord, and before the altar.€€ƒxËThe priest shall take a handful of the flour that is tempered with oil, and all the frankincense that is put upon the flour: and he shall burn on the altar for a memorial of most sweet odour to the Lord.€€ƒx›And the part of the flour that is left, Aaron and his sons shall eat, without leaven: and he shall eat it in the holy place of the court of the tabernacle.€€ƒx¶And therefore it shall not be leavened, because part thereof is offered for the burnt sacrifice of the Lord. It shall be most holy, as that which is offered for sin and for trespass.€€ƒxÃThe males only of the race of Aaron shall eat it. It shall be an ordinance everlasting in your generations concerning the sacrifices of the Lord: Every one that toucheth them shall be sanctified.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxúThis is the oblation of Aaron, and of his sons, which they must offer to the Lord, in the day of their anointing. They shall offer the tenth part of an ephi of flour for a perpetual sacrifice, half of it in the morning, and half of it in the evening.€€ƒxAIt shall be tempered with oil, and shall be fried in a fryingpan.€€ƒx˜And the priest that rightfully succeedeth his father, shall offer it hot, for a most sweet odour to the Lord: and it shall he wholly burnt on the altar.€€ƒxaFor every sacrifice of the priest shall be consumed with fire: neither shall any man eat thereof.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses. saying:€€ƒx«Say to Aaron and his sons: This is the law of the victim for sin. In the place where the holocaust is offered, it shall be immolated before the Lord. It is holy of holies.€€ƒxZThe priest that offereth it, shall eat it in a holy place, in the court of the tabernacle.€€ƒx”Whatsoever shall touch the flesh thereof, shall be sanctified. If a garment be sprinkled with the blood thereof, it shall be washed in a holy place.€€ƒxŠAnd the earthen vessel, wherein it was sodden, shall be broken: but if the vessel be of brass, it shall be scoured, and washed with water.€€ƒx]Every male of the priestly race shall eat of the flesh thereof, because it is holy of holies.€€ƒx¾For the victim that is slain for sin, the blood of which is carried into the tabernacle of the testimony to make atonement in the sanctuary, shall not be eaten, but shall be burnt with fire.€€ƒ`€€ƒxFThis also is the law of the sacrifice for a trespass: it is most holy.€€ƒx“Therefore where the holocaust is immolated, the victim also for a trespass shall be slain: the blood thereof shall be poured round about the altar.€€ƒxIThey shall offer thereof the rump and the fat that covereth the entrails:€€ƒxnThe two little kidneys, and the fat which is by the flanks, and the caul of the liver with the little kidneys.€€˜&ƒx_Every male of the priestly race, shall eat this flesh in a holy place, because it is most holy.€€ƒx¨As the sacrifice for sin is offered, so is also that for a trespass: the same shall be the law of both these sacrifices. It shall belong to the priest that offereth it.€€ƒxNThe priest that offereth the victim of holocaust, shall have the skin thereof.€€ƒxŸAnd every sacrifice of flour that is baked in the oven, and whatsoever is dressed on the gridiron, or in the fryingpan, shall be the priest's that offereth it.€€ƒxcWhether they be tempered with oil, or dry, all the sons of Aaron shall have one as much as another.€€ƒxPThis is the law of the sacrifice of peace offerings that is offered to the Lord.€€ƒxÆIf the oblation be for thanksgiving, they shall offer loaves without leaven tempered with oil, and unleavened wafers anointed with oil, and fine flour fried, and cakes tempered and mingled with oil.€€ƒxeMoreover loaves of leavened bread with the sacrifice of thanks, which is offered for peace offerings:€€ƒxOf which one shall be offered to the Lord for firstfruits, and shall be the priest's that shall pour out the blood of the victim.€€ƒxbAnd the flesh of it shall be eaten the same day: neither shall any of it remain until the morning.€€ƒx«If any man by vow, or of his own accord offer a sacrifice, it shall in like manner be eaten the same day. And if any of it remain until the morrow, it is lawful to eat it.€€ƒxKBut whatsoever shall be found on the third day shall be consumed with fire.€€ƒxùIf any man eat of the flesh of the victim of peace offerings on the third day, the oblation shall be of no effect: neither shall it profit the offerer. Yea rather, whatsoever soul shall defile itself with such meat, shall be guilty of transgression.€€ƒx‚The flesh that hath touched any unclean thing, shall not be eaten: but shall be burnt with fire. He that is clean shall eat of it.€€ƒx™If any one that is defiled shall eat of the flesh of the sacrifice of peace offerings, which is offered to the Lord, he shall be cut off from his people.€€ƒx¥And he that hath touched the uncleanness of man, or of beast, or of any thing that can defile, and shall eat of such kind of flesh: shall be cut off from his people.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxaSay to the children of Israel: The fat of a sheep, and of an ox, and of a goat you shall not eat.€€ƒxThe fat of a carcass that hath died of itself, and of a beast that was caught by another beast, you shall have for divers uses.€€ƒxuIf any man eat the fat that should be offered for the burnt sacrifice of the Lord, he shall perish out of his people.€€ƒx\Moreover you shall not eat the blood of any creature whatsoever, whether of birds or beasts.€€ƒx@Every one that eateth blood, shall perish from among the people.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx¬Speak to the children of Israel, saying: He that offereth a victim of peace offerings to the Lord, let him offer therewith a sacrifice also, that is, the libations thereof.€€ƒx¡He shall hold in his hands the fat of the victim, and the breast. And when he hath offered and consecrated both to the Lord, he shall deliver them to the priest,€€ƒxUWho shall burn the fat upon the altar. But the breast shall be Aaron's and his sons'.€€ƒxcThe right shoulder also of the victim, of peace offerings shall fall to the priest for firstfruits.€€ƒxxHe among the sons of Aaron, that offereth the blood, and the fat: he shall have the right shoulder also for his portion.€€ƒyFor the breast that is elevated and the shoulder that is separated I have taken of the children of Israel, from off their victims of peace offerings: and have given them to Aaron the priest, and to his sons, by a law for ever, from all the people of Israel.€€ƒxœThis is the anointing of Aaron and his sons, in the ceremonies of the Lord, in the day when Moses offered them, that they might do the office of priesthood,€€ƒx‚And the things that the Lord commanded to be given them by the children of Israel, by a perpetual observance in their generations.€€ƒx‡This is the law of holocaust, and of the sacrifice for sin, and for trespass, and for consecration, and the victims of peace offerings:€€ƒx¦Which the Lord appointed to Moses in mount Sinai, when he commanded the children of Israel, that they should offer their oblations to the Lord in the desert of Sinai.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx|Take Aaron with his sons, their vestments, and the oil of unction: a calf for sin, two rams, a basket with unleavened bread.€€ƒxRAnd thou shalt gather together all the congregation to the door of the tabernacle.€€ƒxyAnd Moses did as the Lord had commanded. And all the multitude being gathered together before the door of the tabernacle:€€˜$ƒxLAnd immediately, he offered Aaron and his sons. And when he had washed them,€€ƒx™He vested the high priest with the strait linen garment, girding him with the girdle, and putting on him the violet tunick: and over it he put the ephod.€€ƒx^And binding it with the girdle, he fitted it to the rational, on which was Doctrine and Truth.€€ƒx¤He put also the mitre upon his head: and upon the mitre over the forehead, he put the plate of gold, consecrated with sanctification, as the Lord had commanded him.€€ƒxgHe took also the oil of unction, with which he anointed the tabernacle, with all the furniture thereof.€€ƒx­And when he had sanctified and sprinkled the altar seven times, he anointed it, and all the vessels thereof: and the laver with the foot thereof, he sanctified with the oil.€€ƒxHAnd he poured it upon Aaron's head: and he anointed and consecrated him.€€ƒx•And after he had offered his sons, he vested them with linen tunicks, and girded them with girdles: and put mitres on them as the Lord had commanded.€€ƒxhHe offered also the calf for sin: and when Aaron and his sons had put their hands upon the head thereof,€€ƒxÎHe immolated it: and took the blood, and dipping his finger in it, he touched the horns of the altar round about. Which being expiated, and sanctified, he poured the rest of the blood at the bottom thereof.€€ƒx‡But the fat that was upon the entrails, and the caul of the liver, and the two little kidneys, with their fat, he burnt upon the altar.€€ƒxmAnd the calf with the skin, and the flesh and the dung, he burnt without the camp, as the Lord had commanded.€€ƒxcHe offered also a ram for holocaust. And when Aaron and his sons had put their hands upon its head:€€ƒxDHe immolated it, and poured the blood thereof round about the altar.€€ƒxdAnd cutting the ram into pieces, the head thereof, and the joints, and the fat he burnt in the fire.€€ƒx¸Having first washed the entrails, and the feet, and the whole ram together he burnt upon the altar: because it was a holocaust of most sweet odour to the Lord, as he had commanded him.€€ƒx~He offered also the second ram, in the consecration of priests: and Aaron, and his sons put their hands upon the head thereof.€€ƒxÂAnd when Moses had immolated it, he took of the blood thereof, and touched the tip of Aaron's right ear, and the thumb of his right hand, and in like manner also the great toe of his right foot.€€ƒyHe offered also the sons of Aaron: and when with the blood of the ram that was immolated, he had touched the tip of the right ear of every one of them, and the thumbs of their right hands, and the great toes of their right feet, the rest he poured on the altar round about.€€ƒx°But the fat, and the rump, and all the fat that covereth the entrails, and the caul of the liver, and the two kidneys with their fat, and with the right shoulder, he separated.€€ƒx¿And taking out of the basket of unleavened bread, which was before the Lord, a loaf without leaven, and a cake tempered with oil and a wafer, he put them upon the fat, and the right shoulder:€€ƒxTDelivering all to Aaron, and to his sons. Who having lifted them up before the Lord,€€ƒx©He took them again from their hands, and burnt them upon the altar of holocaust: because it was the oblation of consecration, for a sweet odour of sacrifice to the Lord.€€ƒx€And he took of the ram of consecration, the breast for his portion, elevating it before the Lord, as the Lord had commanded him.€€ƒx‘And taking the ointment, and the blood that was upon the altar, he sprinkled Aaron, and his vestments, and his sons, and their vestments with it.€€ƒyAnd when he had sanctified them in their vestments, he commanded them, saying: Boil the flesh before the door of the tabernacle, and there eat it. Eat ye also the loaves of consecration, that are laid in the basket, as the Lord commanded me, saying: Aaron and his sons shall eat them.€€ƒxVAnd whatsoever shall be left of the flesh and the loaves, shall be consumed with fire.€€ƒx¼And you shall not go out of the door of the tabernacle for seven days, until the day wherein the time of your consecration shall be expired. For in seven days the consecration is finished:€€ƒx[As at this present it hath been done, that the rite of the sacrifice might be accomplished.€€ƒxƒDay and night shall you remain in the tabernacle observing the watches of the Lord, lest you die. For so it hath been commanded me.€€ƒxPAnd Aaron and his sons did all things which the Lord spoke by the hand of Moses.€€ƒ`€€ƒxqAnd when the eighth day was come, Moses called Aaron and his sons, and the ancients of Israel, and said to Aaron:€€ƒxqTake of the herd a calf for sin, and a ram for a holocaust, both without blemish, and offer them before the Lord.€€ƒx™And to the children of Israel thou shalt say: Take ye a he goat for sin, and a calf, and a lamb, both of a year old, and without blemish for a holocaust.€€ƒxÁAlso a bullock and a ram for peace offerings. And immolate them before the Lord, offering for the sacrifice of every one of them flour tempered with oil: for to day the Lord will appear to you.€€˜ƒxeMoses said: This is the word, which the Lord hath commanded. Do it, and his glory will appear to you.€€ƒxäAnd he said to Aaron: Approach to the altar, and offer sacrifice for thy sin. Offer the holocaust, and pray for thyself and for the people: and when thou hast slain the people's victim, pray for them, as the Lord hath commanded.€€ƒxNAnd forthwith Aaron, approaching to the altar, immolated the calf for his sin.€€ƒx˜And his sons brought him the blood of it: and he dipped his finger therein, and touched the horns of the altar, and poured the rest at the foot thereof.€€ƒxŒAnd the fat, and the little kidneys, and the caul of the liver, which are for sin, he burnt upon the altar, as the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒxDBut the flesh and skins thereof he burnt with fire without the camp.€€ƒx€He immolated also the victim of holocaust: and his sons brought him the blood thereof, which he poured round about on the altar.€€ƒx„And the victim being cut into pieces, they brought to him the head and all the members: all which he burnt with fire upon the altar.€€ƒx9Having first washed the entrails and the feet with water.€€ƒxVThen offering for the sin of the people, he slew the he goat: and expiating the altar,€€ƒxHe offered the holocaust.€€ƒx’Adding in the sacrifice the libations, which are offered withal, and burning them upon the altar, besides the ceremonies of the morning holocaust.€€ƒxHe immolated also the bullock and the ram, and peace offerings of the people: and his sons brought him the blood, which he poured upon the altar round about.€€ƒx{The fat also of the bullock, and the rump of the ram, and the two little kidneys with their fat, and the caul of the liver,€€ƒxFThey put upon the breasts. And after the fat was burnt upon the altar,€€ƒxoAaron separated their breasts, and the right shoulders, elevating them before the Lord, as Moses had commanded.€€ƒx¤And stretching forth his hands to the people, he blessed them. And so the victims for sin, and the holocausts, and the peace offerings being finished, he came down.€€ƒx©And Moses and Aaron went into the tabernacle of the testimony, and afterwards came forth and blessed the people. And the glory of the Lord appeared to all the multitude.€€ƒxºAnd, behold, a fire, coming forth from the Lord, devoured the holocaust, and the fat that was upon the altar: which when the multitude saw, they praised the Lord, falling on their faces.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¦And Nadab and Abiu, the sons of Aaron, taking their censers, put fire therein, and incense on it, offering before the Lord strange fire: which was not commanded them.€€ƒxPAnd fire coming out from the Lord destroyed them: and they died before the Lord.€€ƒxÓAnd Moses said to Aaron: This is what the Lord hath spoken. I will be sanctified in them that approach to me: and I will be glorified in the sight of all the people. And when Aaron heard this, he held his peace.€€ƒxºAnd Moses called Misael and Elisaphan, the sons of Oziel, the uncle of Aaron, and said to them: Go and take away your brethren from before the sanctuary, and carry them without the camp.€€”ƒyAnd Moses said to Aaron, and to Eleazar and Ithamar, his sons: Uncover not your heads, and rend not your garments, lest perhaps you die, and indignation come upon all the congregation. Let your brethren, and all the house of Israel, bewail the burning which the Lord has kindled.€€ƒx¹But you shall not go out of the door of the tabernacle: otherwise you shall perish, for the oil of the holy unction is on you. And they did all things according to the precept of Moses.€€ƒxThe Lord also said to Aaron:€€ƒxÑYou shall not drink wine nor any thing that may make drunk, thou nor thy sons, when you enter into the tabernacle of the testimony, lest you die. Because it is an everlasting precept; through your generations:€€ƒx^And that you may have knowledge to discern between holy and unholy, between unclean and clean:€€ƒxoAnd may teach the children of Israel all my ordinances which the Lord hath spoken to them by the hand of Moses.€€ƒxÚAnd Moses spoke to Aaron, and to Eleazar and Ithamar, his sons that were left: Take the sacrifice that is remaining of the oblation of the Lord, and eat it without leaven beside the altar, because it is holy of holies.€€ƒx…And you shall eat it in a holy place: which is given to thee and thy sons of the oblations of the Lord, as it hath been commanded me.€€ƒyThe breast also that is offered, and the shoulder that is separated, you shall eat in a most clean place, thou and thy sons, and thy daughters with thee. For they are set aside for thee and thy children, of the victims of peace offerings of the children of Israel.€€ƒxÇBecause they have elevated before the Lord the shoulder and the breast, and the fat that is burnt on the altar: and they belong to thee and to thy sons by a perpetual law, as the Lord hath commanded.€€ƒxÌWhile these things were a doing, when Moses sought for the buck goat, that had been offered for sin, he found it burnt. And being angry with Eleazar and Ithamar, the sons of Aaron that were left, he said:€€ƒxÀWhy did you not eat in the holy place the sacrifice for sin, which is most holy, and given to you, that you may bear the iniquity of the people, and may pray for them in the sight of the Lord.€€ƒxEspecially, whereas none of the blood thereof hath been carried within the holy places: and you ought to have eaten it in the sanctuary, as was commanded me?€€ƒxİAaron answered: This day hath been offered the victim for sin, and the holocaust before the Lord: and to me what thou seest has happened. How could I eat it, or please the Lord in the ceremonies, having a sorrowful heart?€€ƒx,Which when Moses had heard he was satisfied.€€ƒ`€€ƒx.And the Lord spoke to Moses and Aaron, saying:€€ƒxpSay to the children of Israel: These are the animals which you are to eat of all the living things of the earth.€€ƒxVWhatsoever hath the hoof divided, and cheweth the cud among the beasts, you shall eat.€€ƒx¥But whatsoever cheweth indeed the cud, and hath a hoof, but divideth it not, as the camel, and others: that you shall not eat, but shall reckon it among the unclean.€€˜/ƒxGThe hare also: for that too cheweth the cud, but divideth not the hoof.€€ƒxGAnd the swine, which, though it divideth the hoof, cheweth not the cud.€€ƒxkThe flesh of these you shall not eat, nor shall you touch their carcasses, because they are unclean to you.€€ƒx±These are the things that breed in the waters, and which it is lawful to eat. All that hath fins, and scales, as well in the sea, as in the rivers, and the pools, you shall eat.€€ƒxzBut whatsoever hath not fins and scales, of those things that move and live in the waters, shall be an abomination to you,€€ƒxSAnd detestable. Their flesh you shall not eat: and their carcasses you shall avoid.€€ƒxCAll that have not fins and scales, in the waters, shall be unclean.€€ƒxOf birds these are they which you must not eat, and which are to be avoided by you: The eagle, and the griffon, and the osprey.€€ƒx7And the kite, and the vulture, according to their kind.€€ƒx?And all that is of the raven kind, according to their likeness.€€ƒxLThe ostrich, and the owl, and the larus, and the hawk according to its kind.€€ƒx1The screech owl, and the cormorant, and the ibis.€€ƒx2And the swan, and the bittern, and the porphyrion.€€ƒxPThe heron, and the charadroin according to its kind, the houp also, and the bat.€€ƒxPOf things that fly, whatsoever goeth upon four feet, shall be abominable to you.€€ƒxlBut whatsoever walketh upon four feet, but hath the legs behind longer, wherewith it hoppeth upon the earth,€€ƒx€That you shall eat: as the bruchus in its kind, the attacus, and ophimachus, and the locust, every, one according to their kind.€€ƒxTBut of flying things whatsoever hath four feet only, shall be an abomination to you.€€ƒxjAnd whosoever shall touch the carcasses of them, shall be defiled: and shall be unclean until the evening:€€ƒxAnd if it be necessary that he carry any of these things when they are dead: he shall wash his clothes, and shall be unclean until the sun set.€€ƒxƒEvery beast that hath a hoof, but divideth it not, nor cheweth the cud shall be unclean: and he that toucheth it, shall be defiled.€€ƒx˜That which walketh upon hands of all animals which go on all four, shall be unclean: he that shall touch their carcasses shall be defiled until evening.€€ƒxAnd he that shall carry such carcasses, shall wash his clothes, and shall be unclean until evening: because all these things are unclean to you.€€ƒx¤These also shall be reckoned among unclean things, of all that move upon the earth. The weasel, and the mouse, and the crocodile, every one according to their kind:€€ƒxLThe shrew, and the chameleon, and the stellio, and the lizard, and the mole.€€ƒx[All these are unclean. He that toucheth their carcasses shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒy%And upon what thing soever any of their carcasses shall fall, it shall be defiled, whether it be a vessel of wood, or a garment, or skins or haircloths: or any thing in which work is done. They shall be dipped in water, and shall be unclean until the evening, and so afterwards shall be clean.€€ƒxkBut an earthen vessel, into which any of these shall fall, shall be defiled: and therefore is to be broken.€€ƒx¡Any meat which you eat, if water from such a vessel be poured upon it, shall be unclean; and every liquor that is drunk out of any such vessel, shall be unclean.€€ƒx©And upon whatsoever thing any of these dead beasts shall fall, it shall be unclean. Whether it be oven, or pots with feet, they shall be destroyed, and shall be unclean.€€ƒx„But fountains and cisterns, and all gatherings together of waters shall be clean. He that toucheth their carcasses shall be defiled.€€ƒx2If it fall upon seed corn, it shall not defile it.€€ƒxvBut if any man pour water upon the seed, and afterwards it be touched by the carcasses, it shall be forthwith defiled.€€ƒxIf any beast die, of which it is lawful for you to eat, he that toucheth the carcass thereof, shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒxqAnd he that eateth or carrieth any thing thereof, shall wash his clothes, and shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒxYAll that creepeth upon the earth shall be abominable: neither shall it be taken for meat.€€ƒxˆWhatsoever goeth upon the breast on four feet, or hath many feet, or traileth on the earth, you shall not eat, because it is abominable.€€ƒxGDo not defile your souls, nor touch aught thereof, lest you be unclean,€€ƒxFor I am the Lord your God. Be holy because I am holy. Defile not your souls by any creeping thing, that moveth upon the earth.€€ƒxVFor I am the Lord, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, that I might be your God.€€ƒxYou shall be holy, because I am holy. This is the law of beasts and fowls, and of every living creature that moveth in the waters, and creepeth on the earth:€€ƒxpThat you may know the differences of the clean, and unclean, and know what you ought to eat, and what to refuse.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxÉSpeak to the children of Israel, and thou shalt say to them: If a woman having received seed shall bear a man child, she shall be unclean seven days, according to the days of separation of her flowers.€€ƒx6And on the eighth day the infant shall be circumcised:€€ƒxÉBut she shall remain three and thirty days in the blood of her purification. She shall touch no holy thing: neither shall she enter into the sanctuary, until the days of her purification, be fulfilled.€€ˆƒyAnd when the days of her purification are expired, for a son, or for a daughter, she shall bring to the door of the tabernacle of the testimony, a lamb of a year old for a holocaust, and a young pigeon or a turtle for sin: and shall deliver them to the priest.€€ƒx¹Who shall offer them before the Lord, and shall pray for her: and so she shall be cleansed from the issue of her blood. This is the law for her that beareth a man child or a maid child.€€ƒxçAnd if her hand find not sufficiency, and she is not able to offer a lamb, she shall take two turtles, or two young pigeons, one for a holocaust, and another for sin: and the priest shall pray for her, and so she shall be cleansed.€€ƒ`€€ƒx.And the Lord spoke to Moses and Aaron, saying:€€ƒxÌThe man in whose skin or flesh shall arise a different colour or a blister, or as it were something shining, that is the stroke of the leprosy, shall be brought to Aaron the priest, or any or of his sons.€€ƒxåAnd if he see the leprosy in his skin, and the hair turned white and the place where the leprosy appears lower than the skin and the rest of the flesh: it is the stroke of the leprosy, and upon his judgment he shall be separated.€€ƒx£But if there be a shining whiteness in the skin, and not lower than the other flesh, and the hair be of the former colour, the priest shall shut him up seven days.€€˜;ƒxÛAnd on the seventh day, he shall look on him. If the leprosy be somewhat obscure, and not spread in the skin, he shall declare him clean, because it is but a scab: and the man shall wash his clothes, and shall be clean.€€ƒxvBut, if the leprosy grow again, after he was seen by the priest and restored to cleanness, he shall be brought to him:€€ƒx&And shall be condemned of uncleanness.€€ƒxLIf the stroke of the leprosy be in a man, he shall be brought to the priest:€€ƒx¤And he shall view him. And when there shall be a white colour in the skin, and it shall have changed the look of the hair, and the living flesh itself shall appear:€€ƒx®It shall be judged an inveterate leprosy, and grown into the skin. The priest therefore shall declare him unclean: and shall not shut him up, because he is evidently unclean.€€ƒxšBut if the leprosy spring out running about in the skin, and cover all the skin from the head to the feet, whatsoever falleth under the sight of the eyes:€€ƒx¦The priest shall view him, and shall judge that the leprosy which he has is very clean: because it is all turned into whiteness, and therefore the man shall be clean.€€ƒx,But when the live flesh shall appear in him:€€ƒx›Then by the judgment of the priest he shall be defiled, and shall be reckoned among the unclean. For live flesh, if it be spotted with leprosy, is unclean.€€ƒx@And if again it be turned into whiteness, and cover all the man:€€ƒx;The priest shall view him, and shall judge him to be clean.€€ƒxTWhen also there has been an ulcer in the flesh and the skin, and it has been healed:€€ƒxuAnd in the place of the ulcer, there appeareth a white scar, or somewhat red, the man shall be brought to the priest.€€ƒx¹And when he shall see the place of the leprosy lower than the other flesh, and the hair turned white: he shall declare him unclean, for the plague of leprosy is broken out in the ulcer.€€ƒx™But if the hair be of the former colour, and the scar somewhat obscure, and be not lower than the flesh that is near it: he shall shut him up seven days.€€ƒx9And if it spread, he shall judge him to have the leprosy:€€ƒxXBut if it stay in its place, it is but the scar of an ulcer: and the man shall be clean.€€ƒx`The flesh also and skin that hath been burnt, and after it is healed hath a white or a red scar:€€ƒxÁThe priest shall view it, and if he see it turned white, and the place thereof is lower than the other skin: he shall declare him unclean, because the evil of leprosy is broken out in the scar.€€ƒx±But if the colour of the hair be not changed, nor the blemish lower than the other flesh, and the appearance of the leprosy be somewhat obscure: he shall shut him up seven days,€€ƒxtAnd on the seventh day he shall view him. If the leprosy be grown farther in the skin, he shall declare him unclean.€€ƒx©But if the whiteness stay in its place, and be not very clear, it is the sore of a burning: and therefore he shall be cleansed, because it is only the scar of a burning.€€ƒx_If the leprosy break out in the head or the beard of a man or woman, the priest shall see them,€€ƒx°And if the place be lower than the other flesh, and the hair yellow, and thinner than usual: he shall declare them unclean, because it is the leprosy of the head and the beard;€€ƒx†But if he perceive the place of the spot is equal with the flesh that is near it, and the hair black: he shall shut him up seven days,€€ƒx And on the seventh day he shall look upon it. If the spot be not grown, and the hair keep its colour, and the place of the blemish be even with the other flesh:€€ƒx`The man shall be shaven all but the place of the spot: and he shall be shut up other seven days.€€ƒxªIf on the seventh day the evil seem to have stayed in its place, and not lower than the other flesh, he shall cleanse him: and his clothes being washed he shall be clean.€€ƒx=But if after his cleansing the spot spread again in the skin:€€ƒxYHe shall seek no more whether the hair be turned yellow, because he is evidently unclean.€€ƒxƒBut if the spot be stayed, and the hair be black, let him know that the man is healed: and let him confidently pronounce him clean.€€ƒx6If a whiteness appear in the skin of a man or a woman,€€ƒx­The priest shall view them. If he find that a darkish whiteness shineth in the skin, let him know that it is not the leprosy, but a white blemish, and that the man is clean.€€ƒxCThe man whose hair falleth off from his head, he is bald and clean:€€ƒxDAnd if the hair fall from his forehead, he is bald before and clean.€€ƒxYBut if in the bald head or in the bald forehead there be risen a white or reddish colour:€€ƒxjAnd the priest perceive this, he shall condemn him undoubtedly of leprosy which is risen in the bald part.€€ƒx`Now whosoever shall be defiled with the leprosy, and is separated by the judgment of the priest:€€ƒx‰Shall have his clothes hanging loose, his head bare, his mouth covered with a cloth: and he shall cry out that he is defiled and unclean.€€ƒxRAll the time that he is a leper and unclean he shall dwell alone without the camp.€€ƒx6A woollen or linen garment that shall have the leprosy€€ƒxDIn the warp, and the woof: or skin, or whatsoever is made of a skin:€€ƒxpIf it be infected with a white or red spot, it shall be accounted the leprosy, and shall be shewn to the priest.€€ƒx:And he shall look upon it and shall shut it up seven days.€€ƒx¶And on the seventh day when he looketh on it again, if he find that it is grown, it is a fixed leprosy. He shall judge the garment unclean, and every thing wherein it shall be found.€€ƒx*And therefore it shall be burnt with fire.€€ƒx#But if he see that it is not grown,€€ƒxuHe shall give orders, and they shall wash that part wherein the leprosy is: and he shall shut it up other seven days.€€ƒxåAnd when he shall see that the former colour is not returned, nor yet the leprosy spread, he shall judge it unclean: and shall burn it with fire, for the leprosy has taken hold of the outside of the garment, or through the whole.€€ƒxŒBut if the place of the leprosy be somewhat dark, after the garment is washed, he shall tear it off, and divide it from that which is sound.€€ƒx‰And if after this there appear in those places that before were without spot, a flying and wandering leprosy: it must be burnt with fire.€€ƒxhIf it cease, he shall wash with water the parts that are pure, the second time: and they shall be clean.€€ƒx«This is the law touching the leprosy of any woollen or linen garment, either in the warp or woof, or any thing of skins: how it ought to be cleaned, or pronounced unclean.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxZThis is the rite of a leper, when he is to be cleansed. He shall be brought to the priest:€€ƒxKWho going out of the camp, when he shall find that the leprosy is cleansed,€€ƒx—Shall command him that is to be purified, to offer for himself two living sparrows, which it is lawful to eat, and cedar wood, and scarlet, and hyssop.€€˜9ƒxBut the other that is alive, he shall dip, with the cedar wood, and the scarlet and the hyssop, in the blood of the sparrow that is immolated:€€ƒx¬Wherewith he shall sprinkle him that is to be cleansed seven times, that he may be rightly purified. And he shall let go the living sparrow, that it may fly into the field.€€ƒxÙAnd when the man hath washed his clothes, he shall shave all the hair of his body, and shall be washed with water: and being purified he shall enter into the camp, yet so that he tarry without his own tent seven days.€€ƒx¬And on the seventh day he shall shave the hair of his head, and his beard and his eyebrows, and the hair of all his body. And having washed again his clothes, and his body,€€ƒx¾On the eighth day, he shall take two lambs without blemish, and an ewe of a year old without blemish, and three tenths of flour tempered with oil for a sacrifice, and a sextary of oil apart.€€ƒx•And when the priest that purifieth the man, hath presented him, and all these things before the Lord, at the door of the tabernacle of the testimony:€€ƒx{He shall take a lamb, and offer it for a trespass offering with the sextary of oil. And having offered all before the Lord,€€ƒxõHe shall immolate the lamb, where the victim for sin is wont to be immolated, and the holocaust, that is, in the holy place. For as that which is for sin, so also the victim for a trespass offering pertaineth to the priest: it is holy of holies.€€ƒxßAnd the priest taking of the blood of the victim that was immolated for trespass, shall put it upon the tip of the right ear of him that is cleansed, and upon the thumb of his right hand and the great toe of his right foot.€€ƒx?And he shall pour of the sextary of oil into his own left hand,€€ƒxRAnd shall dip his right finger in it, and sprinkle it before the Lord seven times.€€ƒxçAnd the rest of the oil in his left hand, he shall pour upon the tip of the right ear of him that is cleansed, and upon the thumb of his right hand and the great toe of his right foot, and upon the blood that was shed for trespass:€€ƒrAnd upon his head.€€ƒxwAnd he shall pray for him before the Lord, and shall offer the sacrifice for sin. Then shall he immolate the holocaust.€€ƒxZAnd put it on the altar with the libations thereof: and the man shall be rightly cleansed.€€ƒxéBut if he be poor, and his hand cannot find the things aforesaid: he shall take a lamb for an offering for trespass, that the priest may pray for him, and a tenth part of flour tempered with oil for a sacrifice, and a sextary of oil:€€ƒxaAnd two turtles or two young pigeons, of which one may be for sin, and the other for a holocaust.€€ƒxŒAnd he shall offer them on the eighth day of his purification to the priest, at the door of the tabernacle of the testimony before the Lord.€€ƒxdAnd the priest receiving the lamb for trespass, and the sextary of oil, shall elevate them together.€€ƒxÁAnd the lamb being immolated, he shall put of the blood thereof upon the tip of the right ear of him that is cleansed, and upon the thumb of his right hand, and the great toe of his right foot.€€ƒx9But he shall pour part of the oil into his own left hand,€€ƒxaAnd dipping the finger of his right hand in it, he shall sprinkle it seven times before the Lord.€€ƒx¿And he shall touch the tip of the right ear of him that is cleansed, and the thumb of his right hand and the great toe of his right foot, in the place of the blood that was shed for trespass.€€ƒx‘And the other part of the oil that is in his left hand, he shall pour upon the head of the purified person, that he may appease the Lord for him.€€ƒx-And he shall offer a turtle, or young pigeon:€€ƒxFOne for trespass, and the other for a holocaust, with their libations.€€ƒxfThis is the sacrifice of a leper, that is not able to have all things that appertain to his cleansing.€€ƒx.And the Lord spoke to Moses and Aaron, saying:€€ƒxƒWhen you shall come into the land of Chanaan, which I will give you for a possession, if there be the plague or leprosy in a house:€€ƒx~He whose house it is, shall go and tell the priest, saying: It seemeth to me, that there is the plague of leprosy in my house,€€ƒxøAnd he shall command, that they carry forth all things out of the house, before he go into it, and see whether it have the leprosy, let all things become unclean that are in the house. And afterwards he shall go in to view the leprosy of the house.€€ƒx|And if he see in the walls thereof as it were little dints, disfigured with paleness or redness, and lower than all he rest:€€ƒxNHe shall go out of the door of the house, and forthwith shut it up seven days,€€ƒx_And returning on the seventh day, he shall look upon it. If he find that the leprosy is spread,€€ƒxxHe shall command, that the stones wherein the leprosy is, be taken out, and cast without the city into an unclean place:€€ƒx‹And that the house be scraped on the inside round about, and the dust of the scrapings be scattered without the city into an unclean place:€€ƒxvAnd that other stones be laid in the place of them that were taken away, and the house be plastered with other mortar.€€ƒxeBut if after the stones be taken out, and the dust scraped off, and it be plastered with other earth.€€ƒxŠThe priest going in perceive that the leprosy is returned, and the walls full of spots, it is a lasting leprosy, and the house is unclean.€€ƒx‹And they shall destroy it forthwith, and shall cast the stones and timber thereof, and all the dust without the town into an unclean place.€€ƒxPHe that entereth into the house when it is shut, shall be unclean until evening,€€ƒxIAnd he that sleepeth in it, and eateth any thing, shall wash his clothes.€€ƒx’But if the priest going in perceive that the leprosy is not spread in the house, after it was plastered again, he shall purify it, it being cured.€€ƒxeAnd for the purification thereof he shall take two sparrows, and cedar wood, and scarlet, and hyssop.€€ƒxJAnd having immolated one sparrow in an earthen vessel, over living waters,€€ƒxİHe shall take the cedar wood, and the hyssop, and the scarlet, and the living sparrow, and shall dip all in the blood of the sparrow that is immolated, and in the living water: and he shall sprinkle the house seven times.€€ƒx«And shall purify it as well with the blood of the sparrow, as with the living water, and with the living sparrow, and with the cedar wood, and the hyssop, and the scarlet.€€ƒx…And when he hath let go the sparrow to fly freely away into the field, he shall pray for the house: and it shall be rightly cleansed.€€ƒx4This is the law of every kind of leprosy and stroke.€€ƒx&Of the leprosy of garments and houses,€€ƒxeOf a scar and of blisters breaking out of a shining spot, and when the colours are diversely changed:€€ƒx6That it may be known when a thing is clean or unclean.€€ƒ`€€ƒx.And the Lord spoke to Moses and Aaron, saying:€€ƒxgSpeak to the children of Israel, and say to them: The man that hath an issue of seed, shall be unclean.€€ƒx„And then shall he be judged subject to this evil, when a filthy humour, at every moment, cleaveth to his flesh, and gathereth there.€€ƒxVEvery bed on which he sleepeth, shall be unclean, and every place on which he sitteth.€€˜!ƒx‰If a man sit where that man hath sitten, he also shall wash his clothes: and being washed with water, shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒx{He that toucheth his flesh, shall wash his clothes: and being himself washed with water shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒx•If such a man cast his spittle upon him that is clean, he shall wash his clothes: and being washed with water, he shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒx4The saddle on which he hath sitten shall be unclean.€€ƒxäAnd whatsoever has been under him that hath the issue of seed, shall be unclean until the evening. He that carrieth any of these things, shall wash his clothes: and being washed with water, he shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒx¦Every person whom such a one shall touch, not having washed his hands before, shall wash his clothes: and being washed with water, shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒxjIf he touch a vessel of earth, it shall be broken: but if a vessel of wood, it shall be washed with water.€€ƒx¯If he who suffereth this disease be healed, he shall number seven days after his cleansing: and having washed his clothes, and all his body in living water, he shall be clean.€€ƒx¼And on the eighth day he shall take two turtles, or two young pigeons, and he shall come before the Lord, to the door of the tabernacle of the testimony, and shall give them to the priest.€€ƒx˜Who shall offer one for sin, and the other for a holocaust: and he shall pray for him before the Lord, that he may be cleansed of the issue of his seed.€€ƒx‚The man from whom the seed of copulation goeth out, shall wash all his body with water: and he shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒxiThe garment or skin that he weareth, he shall wash with water: and it shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒxgThe woman, with whom he copulateth, shall be washed with water: and shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒxbThe woman, who at the return of the month, hath her issue of blood, shall be separated seven days.€€ƒx@Every one that toucheth her, shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒxmAnd every thing that she sleepeth on, or that she sitteth on in the days of her separation, shall be defiled.€€ƒxyHe that toucheth her bed shall wash his clothes: and being himself washed with water, shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒx—Whosoever shall touch any vessel on which she sitteth, shall wash his clothes: and himself being washed with water, shall be defiled until the evening.€€ƒx‘If a man copulateth with her in the time of her flowers, he shall be unclean seven days: and every bed on which he shall sleep, shall be defiled.€€ƒxòThe woman that hath still issue of blood many days out of her ordinary time, or that ceaseth not to flow after the monthly courses, as long as she is subject to this disease, shall be unclean, in the same manner as if she were in her flowers.€€ƒxYEvery bed on which she sleepeth, and every vessel on which she sitteth, shall be defiled.€€ƒxxWhosoever toucheth them shall wash his clothes: and himself being washed with water, shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒxSIf the blood stop and cease to run, she shall count seven days of her purification:€€ƒx“And on the eighth day she shall offer for herself to the priest, two turtles, or two young pigeons, at the door of the tabernacle of the testimony:€€ƒxAnd he shall offer one for sin, and the other for a holocaust, and he shall pray for her before the Lord, and for the issue of her uncleanness.€€ƒx²You shall teach therefore the children of Israel to take heed of uncleanness, that they may not die in their filth, when they shall have defiled my tabernacle that is among them.€€ƒxVThis is the law of him that hath the issue of seed, and that is defiled by copulation.€€ƒxˆAnd of the woman that is separated in her monthly times, or that hath a continual issue of blood, and of the man that sleepeth with her.€€ƒ`€€ƒx|And the Lord spoke to Moses, after the death of the two sons of Aaron when they were slain upon their offering strange fire:€€ƒxôAnd he commanded him, saying: Speak to Aaron thy brother, that he enter not at all into the sanctuary, which is within the veil before the propitiatory, with which the ark is covered, lest he die, (for I will appear in a cloud over the oracle),€€ƒxZUnless he first do these things. He shall offer a calf for sin, and a ram for a holocaust.€€ƒxüHe shall be vested with a linen tunick: he shall cover his nakedness with linen breeches: he shall be girded with a linen girdle, and he shall put a linen mitre upon his head. For these are holy vestments: all which he shall put on, after he is washed.€€˜"ƒxQAnd when he hath offered the cattle and prayed for himself and for his own house:€€ƒxiHe shall make the two buck goats to stand before the Lord in the door of the tabernacle of the testimony.€€ƒxfAnd casting lots upon them both, one to be offered to the Lord, and the other to be the emissary goat:€€ƒxFThat whose lot fell to be offered to the Lord, he shall offer for sin.€€ƒx˜But that whose lot was to be the emissary goat, he shall present before the Lord, that he may pour prayers upon him, and let him go into the wilderness.€€ƒx…After these things are duly celebrated, he shall offer the calf: and praying for himself and for his own house, he shall immolate it.€€ƒxÅAnd taking the censer, which he hath filled with the burning coals of the altar, and taking up with his hands the compounded perfume for incense, he shall go in within the veil into the holy place:€€ƒx‘That when the perfumes are put upon the fire, the cloud and vapour thereof may cover the oracle, which is over the testimony, and he may not die.€€ƒx{He shall take also of the blood of the calf, and sprinkle with his finger seven times towards the propitiatory to the east.€€ƒxÜAnd when he hath killed the buck goat for the sin of the people, he shall carry in the blood thereof within the veil, as he was commanded to do with the blood of the calf, that he may sprinkle it over against the oracle:€€ƒyAnd may expiate the sanctuary from the uncleanness of the children of Israel, and from their transgressions, and all their sins. According to this rite shall he do to the tabernacle of the testimony, which is fixed among them in the midst of the filth of their habitation.€€ƒx®Let no man be in the tabernacle when the high priest goeth into the sanctuary, to pray for himself and his house, and for the whole congregation of Israel, until he come out.€€ƒxÃAnd when he is come out to the altar that is before the Lord, let him pray for himself: and taking the blood of the calf, and of the buck goat, let him pour it upon the horns thereof round about.€€ƒx|And sprinkling with his finger seven times, let him expiate, and sanctify it from the uncleanness of the children of Israel.€€ƒxkAfter he hath cleaned the sanctuary, and the tabernacle, and the altar, then let him offer the living goat.€€ƒxïAnd putting both hands upon his head, let him confess all the iniquities of the children of Israel, and all their offences and sins. And praying that they may light on its head, he shall turn him out by a man ready for it, into the desert.€€ƒxrAnd when the goat hath carried all their iniquities into an uninhabited land, and shall be let go into the desert:€€ƒx®Aaron shall return into the tabernacle of the testimony, and putting off the vestments, which he had on him before when he entered into the sanctuary, and leaving them there,€€ƒxÛHe shall wash his flesh in the holy place, and shall put on his own garments. And after that he is come out and hath offered his own holocaust, and that of the people, he shall pray both for himself, and for the people.€€ƒxAAnd the fat that is offered for sins, he shall burn on the altar.€€ƒx}But he that hath let go the emissary goat, shall wash his clothes, and his body with water, and so shall enter into the camp.€€ƒxúBut the calf and the buck goat, that were sacrificed for sin, and whose blood was carried into the sanctuary, to accomplish the atonement, they shall carry forth without the camp, and shall burn with fire: their skins and their flesh, and their dung.€€ƒxjAnd whosoever burneth them shall wash his clothes, and flesh with water: and so shall enter into the camp.€€ƒxåAnd this shall be to you an everlasting ordinance. The seventh month, the tenth day of the month, you shall afflict your souls, and shall do no work, whether it be one of your own country, or a stranger that sojourneth among you.€€ƒxzUpon this day shall be the expiation for you, and the cleansing from all your sins. You shall be cleansed before the Lord.€€ƒxVFor it is a sabbath of rest: and you shall afflict your souls by a perpetual religion.€€ƒxÓAnd the priest that is anointed, and whose hands are consecrated to do the office of the priesthood in his father's stead, shall make atonement. And he shall be vested with the linen robe and the holy vestments.€€ƒxyAnd he shall expiate the sanctuary and the tabernacle of the testimony and the altar: the priest also and all the people.€€ƒx¨And this shall be an ordinance for ever, that you pray for the children of Israel, and for all their sins once a year. He did therefore as the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxˆSpeak to Aaron and his sons, and to all the children of Israel, saying to them: This is the word, which the Lord hath commanded, saying:€€ƒxsAny man whosoever of the house of Israel, if he kill an ox, or a sheep, or a goat in the camp, or without the camp,€€ƒx«And offer it not at the door of the tabernacle an oblation to the Lord, shall be guilty of blood. As if he had shed blood, so shall he perish from the midst of his people.€€ƒx¥And the priest shall pour the blood upon the altar of the Lord, at the door of the tabernacle of the testimony: and shall burn the fat for a sweet odour to the Lord.€€ƒx¦And they shall no more sacrifice their victims to devils, with whom they have committed fornication. It shall be an ordinance for ever to them and to their posterity.€€ƒxAnd thou shalt say to them: The man of the house of Israel, and of the strangers who sojourn among you, that offereth a holocaust or a victim,€€ƒx‹And bringeth it not to the door of the tabernacle of the testimony, that it may be offered to the Lord, shall perish from among his people.€€ƒx¶If any man whosoever of the house of Israel, and of the strangers that sojourn among them, eat blood, I will set my face against his soul, and will cut him off from among his people.€€ƒxÀBecause the life of the flesh is in the blood: and I have given it to you, that you may make atonement with it upon the altar for your souls, and the blood may be for an expiation of the soul.€€ƒx~Therefore I have said to the children of Israel: No soul of you, nor of the strangers that sojourn among you, shall eat blood.€€ƒxàAny man whosoever of the children of Israel, and of the strangers that sojourn among you, if by hunting or fowling, he take a wild beast or a bird, which is lawful to eat, let him pour out its blood, and cover it with earth.€€ƒxáFor the life of all flesh is in the blood. Therefore I said to the children of Israel: you shall not eat the blood of any flesh at all, because the life of the flesh is in the blood, and whosoever eateth it, shall be cut off.€€ƒyThe soul that eateth that which died of itself, or has been caught by a beast, whether he be one of your own country or a stranger, shall wash his clothes and himself with water, and shall be defiled until the evening: and in this manner he shall be made clean.€€ƒxLBut if he do not wash his clothes, and his body, he shall bear his iniquity.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxTSpeak to the children of Israel, and thou shalt say to them: I am the Lord your God.€€ƒxàYou shall not do according to the custom of the land of Egypt, in which you dwelt: neither shall you act according to the manner of the country of Chanaan, into which I will bring you. Nor shall you walk in their ordinances.€€ƒxiYou shall do my judgments, and shall observe my precepts, and shall walk in them. I am the Lord your God.€€˜ƒxaNo man shall approach to her that is near of kin to him, to uncover her nakedness. I am the Lord.€€ƒxŒThou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy father, or the nakedness of thy mother: she is thy mother, thou shalt not uncover her nakedness.€€ƒxaThou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy father's wife: for it is the nakedness of thy father.€€ƒxjThou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy sister by father or by mother: whether born at home or abroad.€€ƒxxThou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy son's daughter, or thy daughter's daughter: because it is thy own nakedness.€€ƒxyThou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy father's wife's daughter, whom she bore to thy father: and who is thy sister.€€ƒxdThou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy father's sister: because she is the flesh of thy father.€€ƒx_Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy mother's sister: because she is thy mother's flesh.€€ƒx‰Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy father's brother: neither shalt thou approach to his wife, who is joined to thee by affinity.€€ƒx‚Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy daughter in law: because she is thy son's wife, neither shalt thou discover her shame.€€ƒxgThou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy brother's wife: because it is the nakedness of thy brother.€€ƒx×Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy wife and her daughter. Thou shalt not take her son's daughter or her daughter's daughter, to discover her shame: because they are her flesh, and such copulation is incest.€€ƒx…Thou shalt not take thy wife's sister for a harlot, to rival her: neither shalt thou discover her nakedness, while she is yet living.€€ƒx`Thou shalt not approach to a woman having her flowers: neither shalt thou uncover her nakedness.€€ƒxSThou shalt not lie with thy neighbour's wife: nor be defiled with mingling of seed.€€ƒxxThou shalt not give any of thy seed to be consecrated to the idol Moloch, nor defile the name of thy God. I am the Lord.€€ƒxPThou shalt not lie with mankind as with womankind: because it is an abomination.€€ƒxªThou shalt not copulate with any beast: neither shalt thou be defiled with it. A woman shall not lie down to a beast, nor copulate with it: because it is a heinous crime.€€ƒx~Defile not yourselves with any of these things with which all the nations have been defiled, which I will cast out before you,€€ƒxrAnd with which the land is defiled: the abominations of which I will visit, that it may vomit out its inhabitants.€€ƒx™Keep ye my ordinances and my judgments: and do not any of these abominations. Neither any of your own nation, nor any stranger that sojourneth among you.€€ƒxqFor all these detestable things the inhabitants of the land have done, that were before you, and have defiled it.€€ƒx…Beware then, lest in like manner, it vomit you also out, if you do the like things: as it vomited out the nation that was before you.€€ƒxbEvery soul that shall commit any of these abominations, shall perish from the midst of his people.€€ƒxŒKeep my commandments. Do not the things which they have done, that have been before you: and be not defiled therein. I am the Lord your God.€€ƒ`€€ƒx The Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx…Speak to all the congregation of the children of Israel. And thou shalt say to them: Be ye holy, because I the Lord your God am holy.€€ƒxXLet every one fear his father, and his mother. Keep my sabbaths. I am the Lord your God.€€ƒxQTurn ye not to idols: nor make to yourselves molten gods. I am the Lord your God.€€˜%ƒxYou shall eat it on the same day it was offered, and the next day. And whatsoever shall be left until the third day, you shall burn with fire.€€ƒxQIf after two days any man eat thereof, he shall be profane and guilty of impiety:€€ƒx‚And shall bear his iniquity, because he hath defiled the holy thing of the Lord. And that soul shall perish from among his people.€€ƒx¤When thou reapest the corn of thy land, thou shalt not cut down all that is on the face of the earth to the very ground: nor shalt thou gather the ears that remain.€€ƒx¤Neither shalt thou gather the bunches and grapes that fall down in thy vineyard: but shalt leave them to the poor and the strangers to take. I am the Lord your God.€€ƒxTYou shall not steal. You shall not lie: neither shall any man deceive his neighbour.€€ƒxXThou shalt not swear falsely by my name, nor profane the name of thy God. I am the Lord.€€ƒx Thou shalt not calumniate thy neighbour, nor oppress him by violence. The wages of him that hath been hired by thee shall not abide with thee until the morning.€€ƒxThou shalt not speak evil of the deaf, nor put a stumbling block before the blind: but thou shalt fear the Lord thy God, because I am the Lord.€€ƒx·Thou shalt not do that which is unjust, nor judge unjustly. Respect not the person of the poor: nor honour the countenance of the mighty. But judge thy neighbour according to justice.€€ƒx‡Thou shalt not be a detractor nor a whisperer among the people. Thou shalt not stand against the blood of thy neighbour. I am the Lord.€€ƒxfThou shalt not hate thy brother in thy heart: But reprove him openly, lest thou incur sin through him.€€ƒxuSeek not revenge, nor be mindful of the injury of thy citizens. Thou shalt love thy friend as thyself. I am the Lord.€€ƒxÅKeep ye my laws. Thou shalt not make thy cattle to gender with beasts of any other kind. Thou shalt not sow thy field with different seeds. Thou shalt not wear a garment that is woven of two sorts.€€ƒxßIf a man carnally lie with a woman that is a bondservant and marriageable, and yet not redeemed with a price, nor made free: they both shall be scourged: and they shall not be put to death, because she was not a free woman.€€ƒxfAnd for his trespass he shall offer a ram to the Lord, at the door of the tabernacle of the testimony.€€ƒx‚And the priest shall pray for him: and for his sin before the Lord: and he shall have mercy on him, and the sin shall be forgiven.€€ƒxÏWhen you shall be come into the land, and shall have planted in it fruit trees, you shall take away the firstfruits of them. The fruit that comes forth shall be unclean to you: neither shall you eat of them.€€ƒxWBut in the fourth year, all their fruit shall be sanctified, to the praise of the Lord.€€ƒxoAnd in the fifth year you shall eat the fruits thereof, gathering the increase thereof. I am the Lord your God.€€ƒxFYou shall not eat with blood. You shall not divine nor observe dreams.€€ƒx<Nor shall you cut your hair roundwise: nor shave your beard.€€ƒx†You shall not make any cuttings in your flesh, for the dead: neither shall you make in yourselves any figures or marks. I am the Lord.€€ƒx^Make not thy daughter a common strumpet, lest the land be defiled, and filled with wickedness.€€ƒx?Keep ye my sabbaths, and reverence my sanctuary. I am the Lord.€€ƒxpGo not aside after wizards: neither ask any thing of soothsayers, to be defiled by them. I am the Lord your God.€€ƒxoRise up before the hoary head, and honour the person of the aged man: and fear the Lord thy God. I am the Lord.€€ƒxJIf a stranger dwell in your land, and abide among you, do not upbraid hin:€€ƒxŸBut let him be among you as one of the same country. And you shall love him as yourselves: for you were strangers in the land of Egypt. I am the Lord your God.€€ƒxGDo not any unjust thing in judgment, in rule, in weight, or in measure.€€ƒx™Let the balance be just and the weights equal, the bushel just, and the sextary equal. I am the Lord your God, that brought you out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒxGKeep all my precepts, and all my judgments: and do them. I am the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxÛThus shalt thou say to the children of Israel: If any man of the children Israel, or of the strangers that dwell in Israel, give of his seed to the idol Moloch, dying let him die. The people of the land shall stone him.€€ƒx¿And I will set my face against him: and I will cut him off from the midst of his people, because he hath given of his seed to Moloch, and hath defiled my sanctuary, and profaned my holy name.€€ƒx©And if the people of the land neglecting, and as it were little regarding my commandment, let alone the man that hath given of his seed to Moloch, and will not kill him:€€˜ƒx»The soul that shall go aside after magicians, and soothsayers, and shall commit fornication with them: I will set my face against that soul, and destroy it out of the midst of its people.€€ƒxDSanctify yourselves, and be ye holy: because I am the Lord your God.€€ƒx?Keep my precepts, and do them. I am the Lord that sanctify you.€€ƒx{He that curseth his father, or mother, dying let him die. He hath cursed his father, and mother: let his blood be upon him.€€ƒx–If any man commit adultery with the wife of another, and defile his neighbour's wife: let them be put to death, both the adulterer and the adulteress.€€ƒx„If a man lie with his stepmother, and discover the nakedness of his father, let them both be put to death: their blood be upon them.€€ƒxxIf any man lie with his daughter in law: let both die, because they have done a heinous crime. Their blood be upon them.€€ƒx‚If any one lie with a man as with a woman, both have committed an abomination: let them be put to death. Their blood be upon them.€€ƒx¼If any man after marrying the daughter, marry her mother, he hath done a heinous crime. He shall be burnt alive with them: neither shall so great an abomination remain in the midst of you.€€ƒxaHe that shall copulate with any beast or cattle, dying let him die: the beast also ye shall kill.€€ƒxkThe woman that shall lie under any beast, shall be killed together with the same. Their blood be upon them.€€ƒy;If any man take his sister, the daughter of his father, or the daughter of his mother, and see her nakedness, and she behold her brother's shame: they have committed a crime. They shall be slain, in the sight of their people, because they have discovered one another's nakedness. And they shall bear their iniquity.€€ƒx¨If any man lie with a woman in her flowers, and uncover her nakedness, and she open the fountain of her blood: both shall be destroyed out of the midst of their people.€€ƒx¼Thou shalt not uncover the nakedness of thy aunt by thy mother, and of thy aunt by thy father. He that doth this, hath uncovered the shame of his own flesh: both shall bear their iniquity.€€ƒx»If any man lie with the wife of his uncle by the father, or of his uncle by the mother, and uncover the shame of his near akin, both shall bear their sin. They shall die without children.€€ƒx‡He that marrieth his brother's wife, doth an unlawful thing: he hath uncovered his brother's nakedness. They shall be without children.€€ƒx{Keep my laws and my judgments, and do them: lest the land into which you are to enter to dwell therein, vomit you also out.€€ƒxWalk not after the laws of the nations, which I will cast out before you. For they have done all these things: and therefore I abhorred them.€€ƒx´But to you I say: Possess their land which I will give you for an inheritance, a land flowing with milk and honey. I am the Lord your God, who have separated you from other people.€€ƒxâTherefore do you also separate the clean beast from the unclean, and the clean fowl from the unclean. Defile not your souls with beasts, or birds, or any things that move on the earth, and which I have shewn you to be unclean:€€ƒx{You shall be holy unto me, because I the Lord am holy: and I have separated you from other people, that you should be mine.€€ƒx‡A man, or woman, in whom there is a pythonical or divining spirit, dying let them die. They shall stone them. Their blood be upon them.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¥The Lord said also to Moses: Speak to the priests the sons of Aaron, and thou shalt say for them: Let not a priest incur an uncleanness at the death of his citizens.€€ƒx But only for his kin, such as are near in blood: that is to say, for his father and for his mother, and for his son, and for his daughter, for his brother also:€€ƒx1And for a maiden sister, who hath had no husband.€€ƒxZBut not even for the prince of his people shall he do any thing that may make him unclean.€€˜ƒx­They shall be holy to their God, and shall not profane his name. For they offer the burnt offering of the Lord, and the bread of their God: and therefore they shall be holy.€€ƒx–They shall not take to wife a harlot or a vile prostitute, nor one that has been put away from her husband: because they are consecrated to their God,€€ƒxtAnd offer the loaves of proposition. Let them therefore be holy because I also am holy: the Lord, who sanctify them.€€ƒxtIf the daughter of a priest be taken in whoredom and dishonour the name of her father, she shall be burnt with fire.€€ƒy3The high priest, that is to say, the priest who is the greatest among his brethren, upon whose head the oil of unction hath been poured; and whose hands have been consecrated for the priesthood; and who hath been vested with the holy vestments. He shall not uncover his head: he shall not rend his garments.€€ƒxjNor shall he go in at all to any dead person: not even for his father, or his mother, shall he be defiled.€€ƒx Neither shall he go out of the holy places, lest he defile the sanctuary of the Lord: because the oil of the holy unction of his God is upon him. I am the Lord.€€ƒx%He shall take a virgin unto his wife.€€ƒxmBut a widow or one that is divorced, or defied, or a harlot, he shall not take: but a maid of his own people.€€ƒxwHe shall not mingle the stock of his kindred with the common people of this nation: for I am the Lord who sanctify him.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxsSay to Aaron: Whosoever of thy seed throughout their families, hath a blemish, he shall not offer bread to his God.€€ƒx€Neither shall he approach to minister to him: If he be blind; if he be lame; if he have a little, or a great, or a crooked nose;€€ƒx&If his foot, or if his hand be broken;€€ƒxIf he be crookbacked; or blear eyed; or have a pearl in his eye, or a continual scab, or a dry scurf in his body, or a rupture.€€ƒx†Whosoever of the seed of Aaron the priest hath a blemish: he shall not approach to offer sacrifices to the Lord, nor bread to his God.€€ƒxJHe shall eat nevertheless of the loaves that are offered in the sanctuary.€€ƒx¥Yet so that he enter not within the veil, nor approach to the altar: because he hath a blemish, and he must not defile my sanctuary. I am the Lord who sanctify them.€€ƒxoMoses, therefore spoke to Aaron, and to his sons and to all Israel, all the things that had been commanded him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx#And the Lord spoke to Moses saying:€€ƒxÉSpeak to Aaron and to his sons, that they beware of those things that are consecrated of the children of Israel: and defile not the name of the things sanctified to me, which they offer. I am the Lord.€€ƒxøSay to them and to their posterity: Every man of your race, that approacheth to those things that are consecrated, and which the children of Israel have offered to the Lord, in whom there is uncleanness, shall perish before the Lord. I am the Lord.€€ƒyThe man of the seed of Aaron, that is a leper, or that suffereth a running of the seed, shall not eat of those things that are sanctified to me, until he be healed. He that toucheth any thing unclean by occasion of the dead: and he whose seed goeth from him as in generation:€€˜!ƒx…Shall be unclean until the evening, and shall not eat those things that are sanctified. But when he hath washed his flesh with water,€€ƒxhAnd the sun is down, then being purified, he shall eat of the sanctified things, because it is his meat.€€ƒx}That which dieth of itself, and that which was taken by a beast, they shall not eat, nor be defiled therewith. I am the Lord.€€ƒx—Let them keep my precepts, that they may not fall into sin, and die in the sanctuary, when they shall have defiled it. I am the Lord who sanctify them.€€ƒxvNo stranger shall eat of the sanctified things: a sojourner of the priests, or a hired servant, shall not eat of them.€€ƒxkBut he whom the priest hath bought, and he that is his servant, born in his house, these shall eat of them.€€ƒxŠIf the daughter of a priest be married to any of the people, she shall not eat of those things that are sanctified nor of the firstfruits.€€ƒxÏBut if she be a widow, or divorced, and having no children return to her father's house, she shall eat of her father's meats, as she was wont to do when she was a maid. No stranger hath leave to eat of them.€€ƒxŸHe that eateth of the sanctified things through ignorance, shall add the fifth part with that which he ate, and shall give it to the priest into the sanctuary.€€ƒxiAnd they shall not profane the sanctified things of the children of Israel, which they offer to the Lord:€€ƒx‰Lest perhaps they bear the iniquity of their trespass, when they shall have eaten the sanctified things. I am the Lord who sanctify them.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒy=Speak to Aaron, and to his sons, and to all the children of Israel, and thou shalt say to them: The man of the house of Israel, and of the strangers who dwell with you, that offereth his oblation, either paying his vows, or offering of his own accord, whatsoever it be which he presenteth for a holocaust of the Lord,€€ƒxiTo be offered by you: it shall be a male without blemish of the beeves, or of the sheep, or of the goats.€€ƒxLIf it have a blemish you shall not offer it: neither shall it be acceptable.€€ƒxğThe man that offereth a victim of peace offerings to the Lord, either paying his vows, or offering of his own accord, whether of beeves or of sheep, shall offer it without blemish, that it may be acceptable. There shall be no blemish in it.€€ƒx©If it be blind, or broken, or have a scar or blisters, or a scab, or a dry scurf: you shall not offer them to the Lord, nor burn any thing of them upon the Lord's altar.€€ƒx|An ox or a sheep, that hath the ear and the tail cut off, thou mayst offer voluntarily: but a vow may not be paid with them.€€ƒx you shall not offer to the Lord any beast that hath the testicles bruised, or crushed, or cut and taken away: neither shall you do any such things in your land.€€ƒx´you shall not offer bread to your God, from the hand of a stranger, nor any other thing that he would give: because they are all corrupted, and defiled. You shall not receive them.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxµWhen a bullock, or a sheep, or a goat, is brought forth, they shall be seven days under the udder of their dam: but the eighth day, and thenceforth, they may be offered to the Lord.€€ƒxaWhether it be a cow, or a sheep, they shall not be sacrificed the same day with their young ones.€€ƒxQIf you immolate a victim for thanksgiving to the Lord, that he may be favourable,€€ƒxqYou shall eat it the same day. There shall not any of it remain until the morning of the next day. I am the Lord.€€ƒx1Keep my commandments, and do them. I am the Lord.€€ƒxzProfane not my holy name, that I may be sanctified in the midst of the children of Israel. I am the Lord who sanctify you:€€ƒxVAnd who brought you out of the land of Egypt, that I might be your God. I am the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxySpeak to the children of Israel, and thou shalt say to them: These are the feasts of the Lord, which you shall call holy.€€ƒxÁSix days shall ye do work: the seventh day, because it is the rest of the sabbath, shall be called holy. You shall do no work on that day: it is the sabbath of the Lord in all your habitations.€€ƒxTThese also are the holy days of the Lord, which you must celebrate in their seasons.€€˜,ƒxˆAnd the fifteenth day of the same month is the solemnity of the unleavened bread of the Lord. Seven days shall you eat unleavened bread.€€ƒx\The first day shall be most solemn unto you, and holy: you shall do no servile work therein.€€ƒx But you shall offer sacrifice in fire to the Lord seven days. And the seventh day shall be more solemn, and more holy: and you shall do no servile work therein.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxçSpeak to the children of Israel, and thou shalt say to them: When you shall have entered into the land which I will give you, and shall reap your corn, you shall bring sheaves of ears, the firstfruits of your harvest to the priest.€€ƒx†Who shall lift up the sheaf before the Lord, the next day after the sabbath, that it may be acceptable for you, and shall sanctify it.€€ƒxˆAnd on the same day that the sheaf is consecrated, a lamb without blemish of the first year shall be killed for a holocaust of the Lord.€€ƒx¾And the libations shall be offered with it: two tenths of flour tempered with oil, for a burnt offering of the Lord, and a most sweet odour. Libations also of wine, the fourth part of a hin.€€ƒxÒYou shall not eat either bread, or parched corn, or frumenty or the harvest, until the day that you shall offer thereof to your God. It is a precept for ever throughout your generations, and all your dwellings.€€ƒx|You shall count therefore from the morrow after the sabbath, wherein you offered the sheaf of firstfruits, seven full weeks.€€ƒx‡Even unto the morrow after the seventh week be expired, that is to say, fifty days: and so you shall offer a new sacrifice to the Lord.€€ƒxOut of all your dwellings, two loaves of the firstfruits, of two tenths of flour leavened, which you shall bake for the firstfruits of the Lord.€€ƒy␀And you shall offer with the loaves seven lambs without blemish of the first year, and one calf from the herd, and they shall be for a holocaust with their two rams: and they shall be for a holocaust with their libations for a most sweet odour to the Lord.€€ƒxlYou shall offer also a buck goat for sin, and two lambs of the first year for sacrifices of peace offerings.€€ƒxwAnd when the priest hath lifted them up with the loaves of the firstfruits before the Lord, they shall fall to his use.€€ƒx©And you shall call this day most solemn, and most holy. You shall do no servile work therein. It shall be an everlasting ordinance in all your dwellings and generations.€€ƒxÕAnd when you reap the corn of your land, you shall not cut it to the very ground: neither shall you gather the ears that remain. But you shall leave them for the poor and for the strangers. I am the Lord your God.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx¯Say to the children of Israel: The seventh month, on the first day of the month, you shall keep a sabbath, a memorial, with the sound of trumpets, and it shall be called holy.€€ƒxRYou shall do no servile work therein, and you shall offer a holocaust to the Lord.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxÏUpon the tenth day of this seventh month shall be the day of atonement. It shall be most solemn, and shall be called holy: and you shall await your souls on that day, and shall offer a holocaust to the Lord.€€ƒx‹You shall do no servile work in the time of this day: because it is a day of propitiation, that the Lord your God may be merciful unto you.€€ƒxQEvery soul that is not afflicted on this day, shall perish from among his people.€€ƒxUAnd every soul that shall do any work, the same will I destroy from among his people.€€ƒxYou shall do no work therefore on that day: it shall be an everlasting ordinance unto you in all your generations, and dwellings.€€ƒx It is a sabbath of rest, and you shall afflict your souls beginning on the ninth day of the month. From evening until evening you shall celebrate your sabbaths.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx‘Say to the children of Israel: From the fifteenth day of this same seventh month, shall be kept the feast of tabernacles, seven days to the Lord.€€ƒx•The first day shall be called most solemn and most holy: you shall do no servile work therein. And seven days you shall offer holocausts to the Lord.€€ƒx¹The eighth day also shall be most solemn and most holy: and you shall offer holocausts to the Lord. For it is the day of assembly and congregation. You shall do no servile work therein.€€ƒx¼These are the feasts of the Lord which you shall call most solemn and most holy, and shall offer on them oblations to the Lord: holocausts and libations according to the rite of every day.€€ƒxŠBesides the sabbaths of the Lord, and your gifts, and those things that you offer by vow, or which you shall give to the Lord voluntarily.€€ƒxíSo from the fifteenth day of the seventh month, when you shall have gathered in all the fruits of your land, you shall celebrate the feast of the Lord seven days. On the first day and the eighth shall be a sabbath: that is a day of rest.€€ƒxËAnd you shall take to you on the first day the fruits of the fairest tree, and branches of palm trees, and boughs of thick trees, and willows of the brook: And you shall rejoice before the Lord your God.€€ƒx¯And you shall keep the solemnity thereof seven days in the year. It shall be an everlasting ordinance in your generations. In the seventh month shall you celebrate this feast.€€ƒxnAnd you shall dwell in bowers seven days. Every one that is of the race of Israel, shall dwell in tabernacles:€€ƒxŸThat your posterity may know, that I made the children of Israel to dwell in tabernacles, when I brought them out of the land of Egypt. I am the Lord your God.€€ƒxLAnd Moses spoke concerning the feasts of the Lord to the children of Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx‚Command the children of Israel, that they bring unto thee the finest and clearest oil of olives, to furnish the lamps continually,€€ƒx¾Without the veil of the testimony in the tabernacle of the covenant. And Aaron shall set them from evening until morning before the Lord, by a perpetual service and rite in your generations.€€ƒxMThey shall be set upon the most pure candlestick before the Lord continually.€€—ƒxdAnd thou shalt set them six and six, one against another, upon the most clean table before the Lord.€€ƒxyAnd thou shalt put upon them the clearest frankincense, that the bread may be for a memorial of the oblation of the Lord.€€ƒxyEvery sabbath they shall be changed before the Lord: being received of the children of Israel by an everlasting covenant.€€ƒxAnd they shall be Aaron's and his sons', that they may eat them in the holy place: because it is most holy of the sacrifices of the Lord by a perpetual right.€€ƒx¦And behold there went out the son of a woman of Israel, whom she had of an Egyptian, among the children of Israel: and fell at words in the camp with a man of Israel.€€ƒx¢And when he had blasphemed the name, and had cursed it, he was brought to Moses. (Now his mother was called Salumith, the daughter of Dabri, of the tribe of Dan.)€€ƒxOAnd they put him into prison, till they might know what the Lord would command.€€ƒxAnd the Lord spoke to Moses,€€ƒx’Saying: Bring forth the blasphemer without the camp: and let them that heard him, put their hands upon his head: and let all the people stone him.€€ƒxaAnd thou shalt speak to the children of Israel: The man that curseth his God, shall bear his sin:€€ƒxÆAnd he that blasphemeth the name of the Lord, dying let him die. All the multitude shall stone him, whether he be a native or a stranger. He that blasphemeth the name of the Lord, dying let him die.€€ƒx6He that striketh and killeth a man: dying let him die.€€ƒxWHe that killeth a beast, shall make it good that is to say, shall give beast for beast.€€ƒx_He that giveth a blemish to any of his neighbours: as he hath done, so shall it be done to him:€€ƒx‚Breach for breach, eye for ere, tooth for tooth, shall he restore. What blemish he gave, the like shall he be compelled to suffer.€€ƒxYHe that striketh a beast, shall render another. He that striketh a man shall be punished.€€ƒxzLet there be equal judgment among you, whether he be a stranger, or a native that offends: because I am the Lord your God.€€ƒxÁAnd Moses spoke to the children of Israel. And they brought forth him that had blasphemed, without the camp: and they stoned him. And the children of Israel did as the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒ`€€ƒx3And the Lord spoke to Moses in mount Sinai, saying:€€ƒxªSpeak to the children of Israel, and thou shalt say to them: When you shall have entered into the land which I will give you, observe the rest of the sabbath of the Lord.€€ƒxtSix years thou shalt sow thy field and six years thou shalt prune thy vineyard, and shalt gather the fruits thereof.€€ƒxBut in the seventh year there shall be a sabbath to the land, of the resting of the Lord. Thou shalt not sow thy field, nor prune thy vineyard.€€˜7ƒx•But they shall be unto you for meat, to thee and to thy manservant, to thy maidservant and thy hireling, and to the strangers that sojourn with thee.€€ƒxCAll things that grow shall be meat to thy beasts and to thy cattle.€€ƒx}Thou shalt also number to thee seven weeks of years: that is to say, seven times seven, which together make forty-nine years.€€ƒxAnd thou shalt sound the trumpet in the seventh month, the tenth day of the month, in the time of the expiation in all your land.€€ƒxæAnd thou shalt sanctify the fiftieth year, and shalt proclaim remission to all the inhabitants of thy land: for it is the year of jubilee. Every man shall return to his possession, and every one shall go back to his former family:€€ƒxºBecause it is the jubilee and the fiftieth year. You shall not sow, nor reap the things that grow in the field of their own accord, neither shall you gather the firstfruits of the vines,€€ƒx\Because of the sanctification of the jubilee. But as they grow you shall presently eat them.€€ƒxAIn the year of the jubilee all shall return to their possessions.€€ƒxªWhen thou shalt sell any thing to thy neighbour, or shalt buy of him: grieve not thy brother. But thou shalt buy of him according to the number of years from the jubilee.€€ƒxEAnd he shall sell to thee according to the computation of the fruits.€€ƒxÅThe more years remain after the jubilee, the more shall the price increase: and the less time is counted, so much the less shall the purchase cost. For he shall sell to thee the time of the fruits.€€ƒx_Do not afflict your countrymen: but let every one fear his God. Because I am the Lord your God.€€ƒxhDo my precepts, and keep my judgments, and fulfil them: that you may dwell in the land without any fear.€€ƒxcAnd the ground may yield you its fruits, of which you may eat your fill, fearing no man's invasion.€€ƒxYBut if you say: What shall we eat the seventh year, if we sow not, nor gather our fruits?€€ƒxYI will give you my blessing the sixth year: and it shall yield the fruits of three years.€€ƒxˆAnd the eighth year you shall sow, and shall eat of the old fruits, until the ninth year: till new grow up, you shall eat the old store.€€ƒxkThe land also shall not be sold for ever: because it is mine, and you are strangers and sojourners with me.€€ƒx^For which cause all the country of your possession shall be under the condition of redemption.€€ƒxsIf thy brother being impoverished sell his little possession, and his kinsman will: he may redeem what he had sold.€€ƒxJBut if he have no kinsman, and he himself can find the price to redeem it:€€ƒx£The value of the fruits shall be counted from that time when he sold it. And the overplus he shall restore to the buyer, and so shall receive his possession again.€€ƒxÖBut if his hands find not the means to repay the price, the buyer shall have what he bought, until the year of the jubilee. For in that year all that is sold shall return to the owner, and to the ancient possessor.€€ƒxsHe that selleth a house within the walls of a city, shall have the liberty to redeem it, until one year be expired.€€ƒx¡If he redeem it not, and the whole year be fully out, the buyer shall possess it, and his posterity for ever, and it cannot be redeemed, not even in the jubilee.€€ƒxºBut if the house be in a village, that hath no walls, it shall be sold according to the same law as the fields. If it be not redeemed before, in the jubilee it shall return to the owner.€€ƒxCThe houses of Levites, which are in cities, may always be redeemed.€€ƒx´If they be not redeemed, in the jubilee they shall all return to the owners: because the houses of the cities of the Levites are for their possessions among the children of Israel.€€ƒxHBut let not their suburbs be sold, because it is a perpetual possession.€€ƒxzIf thy brother be impoverished, and weak of hand, and thou receive him as a stranger and sojourner, and he live with thee:€€ƒxcTake not usury of him nor more than thou gavest. Fear thy God, that thy brother may live with thee.€€ƒxVThou shalt not give him thy money upon usury: nor exact of him any increase of fruits.€€ƒxƒI am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, that I might give you the land of Chanaan, and might be your God.€€ƒxyIf thy brother constrained by poverty, sell himself to thee: thou shalt not oppress him with the service of bondservants.€€ƒxfBut he shall be as a hireling, and a sojourner: he shall work with thee until the year of the jubilee.€€ƒxwAnd afterwards he shall go out with his children: and shall return to his kindred and to the possession of his fathers.€€ƒxgFor they are my servants, and I brought them out of the land of Egypt: let them not be sold as bondmen.€€ƒx+Afflict him not by might: but fear thy God.€€ƒxQLet your bondmen, and your bondwomen, be of the nations that are round about you:€€ƒxwAnd of the strangers that sojourn among you, or that were born of them in your land. These you shall have for servants:€€ƒxŸAnd by right of inheritance shall leave them to your posterity, and shall possess them for ever. But oppress not your brethren the children of Israel by might.€€ƒxIf the hand of a stranger or a sojourner grow strong among you, and thy brother being impoverished sell himself to him, or to any of his race:€€ƒxQAfter the sale he may be redeemed. He that will of his brethren shall redeem him:€€ƒxˆEither his uncle, or his uncle's son, or his kinsman, by blood, or by affinity. But if he himself be able also, he shall redeem himself:€€ƒxÊCounting only the years from the time of his selling unto the year of the jubilee: and counting the money that he was sold for, according to the number of the years and the reckoning of a hired servant.€€ƒxfIf there be many years that remain until the jubilee, according to them shall he also repay the price.€€ƒxIf few, he shall make the reckoning with him according to the number of the years: and shall repay to the buyer of what remaineth of the years.€€ƒxdHis wages being allowed for which he served before: he shall not afflict him violently in thy sight.€€ƒxjAnd if by these means he cannot be redeemed, in the year of the jubilee he shall go out with his children.€€ƒxZFor the children of Israel are my servants, whom I brought forth out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÈI am the Lord your God. You shall not make to yourselves any idol or graven thing: neither shall you erect pillars, nor set up a remarkable stone in your land, to adore it. For I am the Lord your God.€€ƒx<Keep my sabbaths, and reverence my sanctuary. I am the Lord.€€ƒxgIf you walk in my precepts, and keep my commandments, and do them, I will give you rain in due seasons.€€ƒxXAnd the ground shall bring forth its increase: and the trees shall be filled with fruit.€€˜.ƒx°I will give peace in your coasts: you shall sleep, and there shall be none to make you afraid. I will take away evil beasts: and the sword shall not pass through your quarters.€€ƒx>You shall pursue your enemies: and they shall fall before you.€€ƒx€Five of yours shall pursue a hundred others: and a hundred of you ten thousand. Your enemies shall fall before you by the sword.€€ƒxnI will look on you, and make you increase: you shall be multiplied, and I will establish my covenant with you.€€ƒx[You shall eat the oldest of the old store: and, new coming on, you shall cast away the old.€€ƒxQI will set my tabernacle in the midst of you: and my soul shall not cast you off.€€ƒxHI will walk among you, and will be your God: and you shall be my people.€€ƒx·I am the Lord your God: who have brought you out of the land of the Egyptians, that you should not serve them: and who have broken the chains of your necks, that you might go upright.€€ƒx8But if you will not hear me, nor do all my commandments:€€ƒx†If you despise my laws, and contemn my judgments so as not to do those things which are appointed by me, and to make void my covenant:€€ƒxÛI also will do these things to you. I will quickly visit you with poverty, and burning heat, which shall waste your eyes, and consume your lives. You shall sow your seed in vain, which shall be devoured by your enemies.€€ƒx¦I will set my face against you, and you shall fall down before your enemies: and shall be made subject to them that hate you. You shall flee when no man pursueth you.€€ƒxaBut if you will not yet for all this obey me: I will chastise you seven times more for your sins.€€ƒxyAnd I will break the pride of your stubbornness: and I will make to you the heaven above as iron, and the earth as brass.€€ƒxsYour labour shall be spent in vain: the ground shall not bring forth her increase: nor the trees yield their fruit.€€ƒxuIf you walk contrary to me, and will not hearken to me, I will bring seven times more plagues upon you for your sins.€€ƒx˜And I will send in upon you the beasts of the field, to destroy you and your cattle, and make you few in number: and that your highways may be desolate.€€ƒx@And if even so you will not amend, but will walk contrary to me:€€ƒxPI also will walk contrary to you, and will strike you seven times for your sins.€€ƒxÙAnd I will bring in upon you the sword that shall avenge my covenant. And when you shall flee into the cities, I will send the pestilence in the midst of you. And you shall be delivered into the hands of your enemies,€€ƒx®After I shall have broken the staff of your bread: so that ten women shall bake your bread in one oven, and give it out by weight: and you shall eat, and shall not be filled,€€ƒxHBut if you will not for all this hearken to me, but will walk against me€€ƒxhI will also go against you with opposite fury: and I will chastise you with seven plagues for your sins,€€ƒxCSo that you shall eat the flesh of your sons and of your daughters.€€ƒxI will destroy your high places, and break your idols. You shall fall among the ruins of your idols, and my soul shall abhor you.€€ƒx‘Insomuch that I will bring your cities to be a wilderness: and I will make your sanctuaries desolate: and will receive no more your sweet odours.€€ƒxuAnd I will destroy your land: and your enemies shall be astonished at it, when they shall be the inhabitants thereof.€€ƒxAnd I will scatter you among the Gentiles: and I will draw out the sword after you. And your land shall be desert, and your cities destroyed.€€ƒxXThen shall the land enjoy her sabbaths all the days of her desolation. When you shall be€€ƒxIn the enemy's land, she shall keep a sabbath, and rest in the sabbaths of her desolation: because she did not rest in your sabbaths, when you dwelt therein.€€ƒxõAnd as to them that shall remain of you I will send fear in their hearts in the countries of their enemies. The sound of a flying leaf shall terrify them: and they shall flee as it were from the sword. They shall fall, when no man pursueth them.€€ƒxvAnd they shall every one fall upon their brethren as fleeing from wars: none of you shall dare to resist your enemies.€€ƒxKYou shall perish among the Gentiles: and an enemy's land shall consume you.€€ƒx²And if of them also some remain, they shall pine away in their iniquities, in the land of their enemies: and they shall be afflicted for the sins of their fathers, and their own.€€ƒx“Until they confess their iniquities, and the iniquities of their ancestors, whereby they have transgressed against me, and walked contrary unto me.€€ƒx Therefore I also will walk against them, and bring them into their enemies' land until their uncircumcised mind be ashamed. Then shall they pray for their sins.€€ƒxoAnd I will remember my covenant, that I made with Jacob, and Isaac, and Abraham. I will remember also the land:€€ƒx¶Which when she shall be left by them, shall enjoy her sabbaths, being desolate for them. But they shall pray for their sins, because they rejected my judgments, and despised my laws.€€ƒxòAnd yet for all that when they were in the land of their enemies, I did not cast them off altogether. Neither did I so despise them that they should be quite consumed: and I should make void my covenant with them. For I am the Lord their God.€€ƒy&And I will remember my former covenant, when I brought them out of the land of Egypt, in the sight of the Gentiles, to be their God. I am the Lord. These are the judgments, and precepts, and laws, which the Lord gave between him and the children of Israel, in mount Sinai, by the hand of Moses.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx¬Speak to the children of Israel, and thou shalt say to them: The man that shall have made a vow, and promised his soul to God, shall give the price according to estimation.€€ƒxƒIf it be a man from twenty years old unto sixty years old, he shall give fifty sicles of silver, after the weight of the sanctuary:€€ƒsIf a woman, thirty.€€ƒxYBut from the fifth year until the twentieth, a man shall give twenty sicles: a woman ten.€€˜"ƒxPA man that is sixty years old or upward, shall give fifteen sicles: a woman ten.€€ƒxªIf he be poor, and not able to pay the estimation, he shall stand before the priest: and as much as he shall value him at, and see him able to pay, so much shall he give.€€ƒxTBut a beast that may be sacrificed to the Lord, if any one shall vow, shall be holy,€€ƒxÛAnd cannot be changed: that is to say, neither a better for a worse, nor a worse for a better. And if he shall change it: both that which was changed, and that for which it was changed, shall be consecrated to the Lord.€€ƒxsAn unclean beast, which cannot be sacrificed to the Lord, if any man shall vow, shall be brought before the priest:€€ƒx;Who judging whether it be good or bad, shall set the price.€€ƒxZWhich, if he that offereth it will give, he shall add above the estimation the fifth part.€€ƒx¸If a man shall vow his house, and sanctify it to the Lord, the priest shall consider it, whether it be good or bad: and it shall be sold according to the price, which he shall appoint.€€ƒx~But if he that vowed, will redeem it, he shall give the fifth part of the estimation over and above: and shall have the house.€€ƒxèAnd if he vow the field of his possession, and consecrate it to the Lord, the price shall be rated according to the measure of the seed. If the ground be sown with thirty bushels of barley, let it be sold for fifty sicles of silver.€€ƒx…If he vow his field immediately from the year of jubilee that is beginning: as much as it may be worth, at so much it shall be rated.€€ƒx˜But if some time after, the priest shall reckon the money according to the number of years that remain until the jubilee, and the price shall be abated.€€ƒx‚And if he that had vowed, will redeem his field, he shall add the fifth part of the money of the estimation, and shall possess it.€€ƒxlAnd if he will not redeem it, but it be sold to any other man, he that vowed it, may not redeem it any more.€€ƒxFor when the day of jubilee cometh, it shall be sanctified to the Lord, and as a possession consecrated, pertaineth to the right of the priest.€€ƒx`If a field that was bought, and not of a man's ancestors' possession, be sanctified to the Lord:€€ƒx‰The priest shall reckon the price according to the number of years, unto the jubilee. And he that had vowed, shall give that to the Lord.€€ƒxrBut in the jubilee, it shall return to the former owner, who had sold it, and had it in the lot of his possession.€€ƒx`All estimation shall be made according to the sicle of the sanctuary. A sicle hath twenty obols.€€ƒx{The firstborn, which belong to the Lord, no man may sanctify and vow: whether it be bullock, or sheep, they are the Lord's.€€ƒxïAnd if it be an unclean beast, he that offereth it shall redeem it, according to thy estimation, and shall add the fifth part of the price. If he will not redeem it, it shall be sold to another for how much soever it was estimated by thee.€€ƒxÀAny thing that is devoted to the Lord, whether it be man, or beast, or field, shall not be sold: neither may it be redeemed. Whatsoever is once consecrated shall be holy of holies to the Lord.€€ƒxXAnd any consecration that is offered by man, shall not be redeemed, but dying shall die.€€ƒxnAll tithes of the land, whether of corn, or of the fruits of trees, are the Lord's, and are sanctified to him.€€ƒxKAnd if any man will redeem his tithes, he shall add the fifth part of them.€€ƒxOf all the tithes of oxen, and sheep, and goats, that pass under the shepherd's rod, every tenth that cometh shall be sanctified to the Lord.€€ƒxçIt shall not be chosen neither good nor bad, neither shall it be changed for another. If any man change it: both that which was changed, and that for which it was changed, shall be sanctified to the Lord, and shall not be redeemed.€€ƒx`These are the precepts which the Lord commanded Moses for the children of Israel in mount Sinai.€€ƒsThe Book of Numbers€€ƒ`€€ƒx­And the Lord spoke to Moses in the desert of Sinai in the tabernacle of the covenant, the first day of the second month, the second year of their going out of Egypt, saying:€€ƒx™Take the sum of all the congregation of the children of Israel by their families, and houses, and the names of every one, as many as are of the male sex,€€ƒxƒFrom twenty years old and upwards, of all the men of Israel fit for war, and you shall number them by their troops, thou and Aaron.€€ƒx[And there shall be with you the princes of the tribes, and of the houses in their kindreds,€€cNum¤dnamegNumbersiosis_namecNumlabbreviationcNumhchapters˜$˜6ƒx&Of Juda, Nahasson the son of Aminadab.€€ƒx'Of Issachar, Nathanael the son of Suar.€€ƒx#Of Zabulon, Eliab the son of Helon.€€ƒxmAnd of the sons of Joseph: of Ephraim, Elisama the son of Ammiud: of Manasses, Gamaliel the son of Phadassur.€€ƒx&Of Benjamin, Abidan the son of Gedeon.€€ƒx&Of Dan, Ahiezer the son of Ammisaddai.€€ƒx#Of Aser, Phegiel the son of Ochran.€€ƒx!Of Gad, Eliasaph the son of Duel.€€ƒx#Of Nephtali, Ahira the son of Enan.€€ƒxuThese are the most noble princes of the multitude by their tribes and kindreds, and the chiefs of the army of Israel:€€ƒxFWhom Moses and Aaron took with all the multitude of the common people:€€ƒx¹And assembled them on the first day of the second month, reckoning them up by the kindreds, and houses, and families, and heads, and names of every one from twenty years old and upward,€€ƒxOAs the Lord had commanded Moses. And they were numbered in the desert of Sinai.€€ƒxÌOf Ruben the eldest son of Israel, by their generations and families and houses and names of every head, all that were of the male sex, from twenty years old and upward, that were able to go forth to war,€€ƒx%Were forty-six thousand five hundred.€€ƒxğOf the sons of Simeon by their generations and families, and houses of their kindreds, were reckoned up by the names and heads of every one, all that were of the male sex, from twenty years old and upward, that were able to go forth to war,€€ƒx"Fifty-nine thousand three hundred.€€ƒxÆOf the sons of Gad, by their generations and families and houses of their kindreds were reckoned up by the names of every one from twenty years old and upward, all that were able to go forth to war,€€ƒx*Forty-five thousand six hundred and fifty.€€ƒx·Of the sons of Juda, by their generations and families and houses of their kindreds, by the names of every one from twenty years old and upward, all that were able to go forth to war,€€ƒx3Were reckoned up seventy-four thousand six hundred.€€ƒx´Of the sons of Issachar, by their generations and families and houses of their kindreds, by the names of every one from twenty years old and upward, all that could go forth to war,€€ƒx2Were reckoned up fifty-four thousand four hundred.€€ƒxÉOf the sons of Zabulon, by the generations and families and houses of their kindreds, were reckoned up by the names of every one from twenty years old and upward, all that were able to go forth to war,€€ƒx"Fifty-seven thousand four hundred.€€ƒxéOf the sons of Joseph, namely, of the sons of Ephraim, by the generations and families and houses of their kindreds, were reckoned up by the names of every one, from twenty years old and upward, all that were able to go forth to war,€€ƒxForty thousand five hundred.€€ƒxÌMoreover of the sons of Manasses, by the generations and families and houses of their kindreds, were reckoned up by the names of every one from twenty years old and upward, all that could go forth to war,€€ƒx Thirty-two thousand two hundred.€€ƒxÌOf the sons of Benjamin, by their generations and families and houses of their kindreds, were reckoned up by the names of every one from twenty years old and upward, all that were able to go forth to war,€€ƒx"Thirty-five thousand four hundred.€€ƒxÇOf the sons of Dan, by their generations and families and houses of their kindreds, were reckoned up by the names of every one from twenty years old and upward, all that were able to go forth to war,€€ƒx!Sixty-two thousand seven hundred.€€ƒxÈOf the sons of Aser, by their generations and families and houses of their kindreds, were reckoned up by the names of every one from twenty years old and upward, all that were able to go forth to war,€€ƒx$Forty-one thousand and five hundred.€€ƒxÃOf the sons of Nephtali, by their generations and families and houses of their kindreds, were reckoned up by the names of every one from twenty years old and upward, were able to go forth to war,€€ƒx"Fifty-three thousand four hundred.€€ƒxThese are they who were numbered by Moses and Aaron, and the twelve princes of Israel, every one by the houses of their kindreds.€€ƒx‹And the whole number of the children of Israel by their houses and families, from twenty years old and upward, that were able to go to war,€€ƒx?Were six hundred and three thousand five hundred and fifty men.€€ƒxLBut the Levites in the tribes of their families were not numbered with them.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxfNumber not the tribe of Levi, neither shalt thou put down the sum of them with the children of Israel:€€ƒyBut appoint them over the tabernacle of the testimony, and all the vessels thereof, and whatsoever pertaineth to the ceremonies. They shall carry the tabernacle and all the furniture thereof: and they shall minister, and shall encamp round about the tabernacle.€€ƒx¦When you are to go forward, the Levites shall take down the tabernacle: when you are to camp, they shall set it up. What stranger soever cometh to it, shall be slain.€€ƒxQAnd the children of Israel shall camp every man by his troops and bands and army.€€ƒxÓBut the Levites shall pitch their tents round about the tabernacle, lest there come indignation upon the multitude of the children of Israel, and they shall keep watch, and guard the tabernacle of the testimony.€€ƒxZAnd the children of Israel did according to all things which the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒ`€€ƒx.And the Lord spoke to Moses and Aaron, saying:€€ƒxœAll the children of Israel shall camp by their troops, ensigns, and standards, and the houses of their kindreds, round about the tabernacle of the covenant.€€ƒxƒOn the east Juda shall pitch his tents by the bands of his army: and the prince of his sons; shall be Nahasson the son of Aminadab.€€ƒx[And the whole sum of the fighting men of his stock, were seventy-four thousand six hundred.€€ƒxbNext unto him they of the tribe of Issachar encamped, whose prince was Nathanael, the son of Suar.€€˜"ƒx>In the tribe of Zabulon the prince was Eliab the son of Helon.€€ƒxVAnd all the army of fighting men of his stock, were fifty-seven thousand four hundred.€€ƒxŒAll that were numbered in the camp of Juda, were a hundred and eighty-six thousand four hundred: and they by their troops shall march first.€€ƒxbIn the camp of the sons of Ruben, on the south side, the prince shall be Elisur the son of Sedeur:€€ƒxaAnd the whole army of his fighting men, that were numbered, were forty-six thousand five hundred.€€ƒx_Beside him camped they of the tribe of Simeon: whose prince was Salamiel the son of Surisaddai.€€ƒxcAnd the whole army of his fighting men, that were numbered, were fifty-nine thousand three hundred.€€ƒx<In the tribe of Gad the prince was Eliasaph the son of Duel.€€ƒxjAnd the whole army of his righting men that were numbered, were forty-five thousand six hundred and fifty.€€ƒx¤All that were reckoned up in the camp of Ruben, were a hundred and fifty-one thousand four hundred and fifty, by their troops: they shall march in the second place.€€ƒxÓAnd the tabernacle of the testimony shall be carried by the officers of the Levites and their troops. As it shall be set up, so shall it be taken down. Every one shall march according to their places, and ranks.€€ƒxfOn the west side shall be the camp of the sons of Ephraim, whose prince was Elisama the son of Ammiud.€€ƒxYThe whole army of his fighting men, that were numbered, were forty thousand five hundred.€€ƒx`And with them the tribe of the sons of Manasses, whose prince was Gamaliel the son of Phadassur.€€ƒxaAnd the whole army of his fighting men, that were numbered, were thirty-two thousand two hundred.€€ƒxMIn the tribe of the sons of Benjamin the prince was Abidan the son of Gedeon.€€ƒxbAnd the whole army of fighting men, that were reckoned up, were thirty-five thousand four hundred.€€ƒx’All that were numbered in the camp of Ephraim, were a hundred and eight-thousand one hundred by their troops: they shall march in the third place.€€ƒxYOn the north side camped the sons of Dan: whose prince was Ahiezar the son of Ammisaddai.€€ƒx^The whole army of his fighting men, that were numbered, were sixty-two thousand seven hundred.€€ƒxeBeside him they of the tribe of Aser pitched their tents: whose prince was Phegiel the son of Ochran.€€ƒx]The whole army of his fighting men, that were numbered, were forty-one thousand five hundred.€€ƒxJOf the tribe of the sons of Nephtali the prince was Ahira the son of Enan.€€ƒxKThe whole army of his fighting men, were fifty-three thousand four hundred.€€ƒxzAll that were numbered in the camp of Dan, were a hundred and fifty-seven thousand six hundred: and they shall march last.€€ƒx¶This is the number of the children of Israel, of their army divided according to the houses of their kindreds and their troops, six hundred and three thousand five hundred and fifty.€€ƒxdAnd the Levites were not numbered among the children of Israel: for so the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒx©And the children of Israel did according to all things that the Lord had commanded. They camped by their troops, and marched by the families and houses of their fathers.€€ƒ`€€ƒxdThese are the generations of Aaron and Moses in the day that the Lord spoke to Moses in mount Sinai.€€ƒxcAnd these the names of the sons of Aaron: his firstborn Nadab, then Abiu, and Eleazar, and Ithamar.€€ƒx”These the names of the sons of Aaron the priests that were anointed, and whose hands were filled and consecrated, to do the functions of priesthood.€€ƒxÏNow Nadab and Abiu died, without children, when they offered strange fire before the Lord, in the desert of Sinai: and Eleazar and Ithamar performed the priestly office in the presence of Aaron their father.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€˜3ƒxkAnd observe whatsoever appertaineth to the service of the multitude before the tabernacle of the testimony,€€ƒxQAnd let them keep the vessels of the tabernacle, serving in the ministry thereof.€€ƒx+And thou shalt give the Levites for a gift,€€ƒxÛTo Aaron and to his sons, to whom they are delivered by the children of Israel. But thou shalt appoint Aaron and his sons over the service of priesthood. The stranger that approacheth to minister, shall be put to death.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxœI have taken the Levites from the children of Israel, for every firstborn that openeth the womb among the children of Israel, and the Levites shall be mine.€€ƒxÂFor every firstborn is mine: since I struck the firstborn in the land of Egypt: I have sanctified to myself whatsoever is firstborn in Israel both of man and beast, they are mine: I am the Lord.€€ƒx;And the Lord spoke to Moses in the desert of Sinai, saying:€€ƒxpNumber the sons of Levi by the houses of their fathers and their families, every male from one month and upward.€€ƒx.Moses numbered them as the Lord had commanded.€€ƒxJAnd there were found sons of Levi by their names, Gerson and Caath Merari.€€ƒx$The sons of Gerson: Lebni and Semei.€€ƒx7The sons of Caath: Amram, and Jesaar, Hebron and Oziel:€€ƒx$The sons of Merari, Moholi and Musi.€€ƒx<Of Gerson were two families, the Lebnites, and the Semeites:€€ƒxfOf which were numbered, people of the male sex from one month and upward, seven thousand five hundred.€€ƒx4These shall pitch behind the tabernacle on the west,€€ƒx,Under their prince Eliasaph the son of Lael.€€ƒx<And their charge shall be in the tabernacle of the covenant:€€ƒyUThe tabernacle itself and the cover thereof, the hanging that is drawn before the doors of the tabernacle of the covenant, and the curtains of the court: the hanging also that is hanged in the entry of the court of the tabernacle, and whatsoever belongeth to the rite of the altar, the cords of the tabernacle, and all the furniture thereof.€€ƒx«Of the kindred of Caath come the families of the Amramites and Jesaarites and Hebronites and Ozielites. These are the families of the Caathites reckoned up by their names:€€ƒxvAll of the male sex from one month and upward, eight thousand six hundred: they shall have the guard of the sanctuary,€€ƒx!And shall camp on the south side.€€ƒx5And their prince shall be Elisaphan the son of Oziel:€€ƒx¸And they shall keep the ark, and the table and the candlestick, the altars, and the vessels of the sanctuary, wherewith they minister, and the veil, and all the furniture of this kind.€€ƒx‘And the prince of the princes of the Levites, Eleazar, the son of Aaron the priest, shall be over them that watch for the guard of the sanctuary.€€ƒxYAnd of Merari are the families of the Moholites, and Musites, reckoned up by their names:€€ƒxIAll of the male kind from one month and upward, six thousand two hundred.€€ƒxLTheir prince Suriel the son of Abihaiel: their shall camp on the north side.€€ƒx Under their custody shall be the boards of the tabernacle, and the bars, and the pillars and their sockets, and all things that pertain to this kind of service:€€ƒx[And the pillars of the court round about with their sockets, and the pins with their cords.€€ƒxüBefore the tabernacle of the covenant, that is to say on the east side shall Moses and Aaron camp, with their sons, having the custody of the sanctuary, in the midst of the children of Israel. What stranger soever cometh unto it, shall be put to death.€€ƒx±All the Levites, that I Moses and Aaron numbered according to the precept of the Lord, by their f families, of the male kind from one month and upward, were twenty-two thousand.€€ƒx›And the Lord said to Moses: Number the firstborn of the male sex of the children of Israel, from one month and upward, and thou shalt take the sum of them.€€ƒxµAnd thou shalt take the Levites to me for all the firstborn of the children of Israel, I am the Lord: and their cattle for all the firstborn of the cattle of the children of Israel:€€ƒxVMoses reckoned up, as the Lord had commanded, the firstborn of the children of Israel:€€ƒxpAnd the males by their names, from one month and upward, were twenty-two thousand two hundred and seventy-three.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx›Take the Levites for the firstborn of the children of Israel, and the cattle of the Levites for their cattle, and the Levites shall be mine. I am the Lord.€€ƒxŠBut for the price of the two hundred and seventy-three, of the firstborn of the children of Israel, that exceed the number of the Levites,€€ƒxpThou shalt take five sicles for every bead, according to the weight of the sanctuary. A sicle hath twenty obols.€€ƒxVAnd thou shalt give the money to Aaron and his sons, the price of them that are above.€€ƒxdMoses therefore took the money of them that were above, and whom they had redeemed from the Levites,€€ƒxˆFor the firstborn of the children of Israel, one thousand three hundred and sixty-five sicles, according to the weight of the sanctuary,€€ƒxXAnd gave it to Aaron and his sons according to the word that the Lord had commanded him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx/And the Lord spoke to Moses, and Aaron, saying:€€ƒx^Take the sum of the sons of Caath from the midst of the Levites, by their houses and families.€€ƒxƒFrom thirty years old and upward, to fifty years old, of all that go in to stand and to minister in the tabernacle of the covenant.€€ƒx)This is the service of the sons of Caath:€€ƒxçWhen the camp is; to set forward, Aaron and his sons shall go into the tabernacle of the covenant, and the holy of holies, and shall take down the veil that hangeth before the door, and shall wrap up the ark of the testimony in it,€€˜1ƒxÌThey shall wrap up also the table of proposition in a cloth of violet, and shall put with it the censers and little mortars, the cups and bowls to pour out the libations: the loaves shall be always on it:€€ƒxŠAnd they shall spread over it a cloth of scarlet, which again they shall cover with a covering of violet skins, and shall put in the bars.€€ƒxÏThey shall take also a cloth of violet wherewith they shall cover the candlestick with the lamps and tongs thereof and the snuffers and all the oil vessels, which are necessary for the dressing of the lamps:€€ƒxHAnd over all they shall put a cover of violet skins and put in the bars.€€ƒx‰And they shall wrap up the golden altar also in a cloth of violet, and shall spread over it a cover of violet skins, and put in the bars.€€ƒx©All the vessels wherewith they minister in the sanctuary, they shall wrap up in a cloth of violet, and shall spread over it a cover of violet skins, and put in the bars.€€ƒxYThey shall cleanse the altar also from the ashes, and shall wrap it up in a purple cloth,€€ƒyAnd shall put it with all the vessels that they use in the ministry thereof, that is to say, firepans, fleshhooks and forks, pothooks and shovels. They shall cover all the vessels of the altar together with a covering of violet skins, and shall put in the bars.€€ƒyIAnd when Aaron and his sons have wrapped up the sanctuary and the vessels thereof at the removing of the camp, then shall the sons of Caath enter in to carry the things wrapped up: and they shall not touch the vessels of the sanctuary, lest they die. These are the burdens of the sons of Caath: in the tabernacle of the covenant:€€ƒy:And over them shall be Eleazar the son of Aaron the priest, to whose charge pertaineth the oil to dress the lamps, and the sweet incense, and the sacrifice, that is always offered, and the oil of unction, and whatsoever pertaineth to the service of the tabernacle, and of all the vessels that are in the sanctuary.€€ƒx.And the Lord spoke to Moses and Aaron, saying:€€ƒx>Destroy not the people of Caath from the midst of the Levites:€€ƒxÛBut do this to them, that they may live, and not die, by touching the holies of holies. Aaron and his sons shall go in, and they shall appoint every man his work, and shall divide the burdens that every man is to carry.€€ƒx}Let not others by any curiosity see the things that are in the sanctuary before they be wrapped up, otherwise they shall die.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxRTake the sum of the sons of Gerson also by their houses and families and kindreds.€€ƒx‚From thirty years old and upward, unto fifty years old. Number them all that go in and minister in the tabernacle of the covenant.€€ƒx3This is the office of the family of the Gersonites:€€ƒxÈTo carry the curtains of the tabernacle and the roof of the covenant, the other covering, and the violet covering over all, and the hanging that hangeth in the entry of the tabernacle of the covenant,€€ƒx The curtains of the court, and the veil in the entry that is before tabernacle. All things that pertain to the altar, the cords and the vessels of the ministry,€€ƒx…The sons of Gerson shall carry, by the commandment of Aaron and his sons: and each man shall know to what burden he must be assigned.€€ƒxŸThis is the service of the family of the Gersonites in the tabernacle of the covenant, and they shall be under the hand of Ithamar the son of Aaron the priest.€€ƒxYThou shalt reckon up the sons of Merari also by the families and houses of their fathers,€€ƒxœFrom thirty years old and upward, unto fifty years old, all that go in to the office of their ministry, and to the service of the covenant of the testimony.€€ƒx{These are their burdens: They shall carry the boards of the tabernacle and the bars thereof, the pillars and their sockets,€€ƒx§The pillars also of the court round about, with their sockets and pins and cords. They shall receive by account all the vessels and furniture, and so shall carry them.€€ƒx±This is the office of the family of the Merarites, and their ministry in the tabernacle of the covenant: and they shall be under the hand of Ithamar the son of Aaron the priest.€€ƒx…So Moses and Aaron and the princes of the synagogue reckoned up the sons of Caath, by their kindreds and the houses of their fathers,€€ƒxyFrom thirty years old and upward, unto fifty years old, all that go in to the ministry of the tabernacle of the covenant:€€ƒx9And they were found two thousand seven hundred and fifty.€€ƒx°This is the number of the people of Caath that go in to the tabernacle of the covenant: these did Moses and Aaron number according to the word of the Lord by the hand of Moses.€€ƒxRThe sons of Gerson also were numbered by the kindreds and houses of their fathers,€€ƒxuFrom thirty years old and upward, unto fifty years old, all that go in to minister in the tabernacle of the covenant:€€ƒx8And they were found two thousand six hundred and thirty.€€ƒxfThis is the people of the Gersonites, whom Moses and Aaron numbered according to the word of the Lord.€€ƒxRThe sons of Merari also were numbered by the kindreds and houses of their fathers,€€ƒx}From thirty years old and upward, unto fifty years old, all that go in to fulfil the rites of the tabernacle of the covenant:€€ƒx/And they were found three thousand two hundred.€€ƒx‰This is the number of the sons of Merari, whom Moses and Aaron reckoned up according to the commandment of the Lord by the hand of Moses.€€ƒx—All that were reckoned up of the Levites, and whom Moses and Aaron and the princes of Israel took by name, by the kindreds and houses of their fathers,€€ƒxFrom thirty years old and upward, until fifty years old, that go into the ministry of the tabernacle, and to carry the burdens,€€ƒx3Were in all eight thousand five hundred and eighty.€€ƒx‰Moses reckoned them up according to the word of the Lord, every one according to their office and burdens, as the Lord had commanded him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx‹Command the children of Israel, that they cast out of the camp every leper, and whosoever hath an issue of seed, or is defiled by the dead:€€ƒxjWhether it be man or woman, cast ye them out of the camp, lest they defile it when I shall dwell with you,€€ƒxnAnd the children of Israel did so, and they cast them forth without the camp, as the Lord had spoken to Moses.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€˜ƒxˆThey shall confess their sin, and restore the principal itself, and the fifth part over and above, to him against whom they have sinned.€€ƒx®But if there be no one to receive it, they shall give it to the Lord, and it shall be the priest's, besides the ram that is offered for expiation, to be an atoning sacrifice.€€ƒxSAll the firstfruits also, which the children of Israel offer, belong to the priest:€€ƒxzAnd whatsoever is offered into the sanctuary by every one, and is delivered into the hands of the priest, it shall be his.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxƒSpeak to the children of Israel, and thou shalt say to them: The man whose wife shall have gone astray, and contemning her husband,€€ƒx°Shall have slept with another man, and her husband cannot discover it, but the adultery is secret, and cannot be proved by witnesses, because she was not found in the adultery:€€ƒxzIf the spirit of jealousy stir up the husband against his wife, who either is defiled, or is charged with false suspicion,€€ƒxşHe shall bring her to the priest, and shall offer an oblation for her, the tenth part of a measure of barley meal: he shall not pour oil thereon, nor put frankincense upon it: because it is a sacrifice of jealousy, and an oblation searching out adultery.€€ƒx@The priest therefore shall offer it, and set it before the Lord.€€ƒx~And he shall take holy water in an earthen vessel, and he shall cast a little earth of the pavement of the tabernacle into it.€€ƒyAnd when the woman shall stand before the Lord, he shall uncover her head, and shall put on her hands the sacrifice of remembrance, and the oblation of jealousy: and he himself shall hold the most bitter waters, whereon he hath heaped curses with execration.€€ƒxÙAnd he shall adjure her, and shall say: If another man hath not slept with thee, and if thou be not defiled by forsaking thy husband's bed, these most bitter waters, on which I have heaped curses, shall not hurt thee.€€ƒx^But if thou hast gone aside from thy husband, and art defiled, and hast lain with another man:€€ƒx±These curses shall light upon thee: The Lord make thee a curse, and an example for all among his people: may he make thy thigh to rot, and may thy belly swell and burst asunder.€€ƒx}Let the cursed waters enter into thy belly, and may thy womb swell and thy thigh rot. And the woman shall answer, Amen, amen.€€ƒxAnd the priest shall write these curses in a book, and shall wash them out with the most bitter waters, upon which he hath heaped the curses,€€ƒxEAnd he shall give them her to drink. And when she hath drunk them up,€€ƒx–The priest shall take from her hand the sacrifice of jealousy, and shall elevate it before the Lord, and shall put it upon the altar: yet so as first,€€ƒx’To take a handful of the sacrifice of that which is offered, and burn it upon the altar: and so give the most bitter waters to the woman to drink.€€ƒxüAnd when she hath drunk them, if she be defiled, and having despised her husband be guilty of adultery, the malediction shall go through her, and her belly swelling, her thigh shall rot: and the woman shall be a curse, and an example to all the people.€€ƒxJBut if she be not defiled, she shall not be hurt, and shall bear children.€€ƒxYThis is the law of jealousy. If a woman hath gone aside from her husband, and be defiled,€€ƒx—And the husband stirred up by the spirit of jealousy bring her before the Lord, and the priest do to her according to all things that are here written:€€ƒx@The husband shall be blameless, and she shall bear her iniquity.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx¡Speak to the children of Israel, and thou shalt say to them: When a man, or woman, shall make a vow to be sanctified, and will consecrate themselves to the Lord:€€ƒxëThey shall abstain from wine, and from every thing that may make a man drunk. They shall not drink vinegar of wine, or of any other drink, nor any thing that is pressed out of the grape: nor shall they eat grapes either fresh or dried.€€ƒx’All the days that they are consecrated to the Lord by vow: they shall eat nothing that cometh of the vineyard, from the raisin even to the kernel.€€ƒx¸All the time of his separation no razor shall pass over his head, until the day be fulfilled of his consecration to the Lord. He shall be holy, and shall let the hair of his head grow.€€˜ƒxºNeither shall he make himself unclean, even for his father, or for his mother, or for his brother, or for his sister, when they die, because the consecration of his God is upon his head.€€ƒx<All the days of his separation he shall be holy to the Lord.€€ƒx¹But if any man die suddenly before him: the head of his consecration shall be defiled: and he shall shave it forthwith on the same day of his purification, and again on the seventh day.€€ƒxƒAnd on the eighth day he shall bring two turtles, or two young pigeons to the priest in the entry of the covenant of the testimony.€€ƒx¬And the priest shall offer one for sin, and the other for a holocaust, and shall pray for him, for that he hath sinned by the dead: and he shall sanctify his head that day:€€ƒx´And shall consecrate to the Lord the days of his separation, offering a lamb of one year for sin: yet so that the former days be made void, because his sanctification was profaned.€€ƒx¡This is the law of consecration. When the days which he had determined by vow shall be expired, he shall bring him to the door of the tabernacle of the covenant,€€ƒxãAnd shall offer his oblation to the Lord: one he lamb of a year old without blemish for a holocaust, and one ewe lamb of a year old without blemish for a sin offering, and one ram without blemish for a victim of peace offering,€€ƒx}A basket also of unleavened bread, tempered with oil, and wafers without leaven anointed with oil, and the libations of each:€€ƒxkAnd the priest shall present them before the Lord, and shall offer both the sin offering and the holocaust.€€ƒx°But the ram he shall immolate for a sacrifice of peace offering to the Lord, offering at the same time the basket of unleavened bread, and the libations that are due by custom.€€ƒxäThen shall the hair of the consecration of the Nazarite, be shaved off before the door of the tabernacle of the covenant: and he shall take his hair, and lay it upon the fire, which is under the sacrifice of the peace offerings.€€ƒxÇAnd shall take the boiled shoulder of the ram, and one unleavened cake out of the basket, and one unleavened wafer, and he shall deliver them into the hands of the Nazarite, after his head is shaven.€€ƒxöAnd receiving them again from him, he shall elevate them in the sight of the Lord: and they being sanctified shall belong to the priest, as the breast, which was commanded to be separated, and the shoulder. After this the Nazarite may drink wine.€€ƒyThis is the law of the Nazarite, when he hath vowed his oblation to the Lord in the time of his consecration, besides those things which his hand shall find, according to that which he had vowed in his mind, so shall he do for the fulfilling of his sanctification.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxbSay to Aaron and his sons: Thus shall you bless the children of Israel, and you shall say to them:€€ƒx#The Lord bless thee, and keep thee.€€ƒx7The Lord shew his face to thee, and have mercy on thee.€€ƒx;The Lord turn his countenance to thee, and give thee peace.€€ƒxQAnd they shall invoke my name upon the children of Israel, and I will bless them.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¾And it came to pass in the day that Moses had finished the tabernacle, and set it up, and had anointed and sanctified it with all its vessels, the altar likewise and all the vessels thereof,€€ƒxThe princes of Israel and the heads of the families, in every tribe, who were the rulers of them who had been numbered, offered€€ƒx¡Their gifts before the Lord, six wagons covered, and twelve oxen. Two princes offered one wagon, and each one an ox, and they offered them before the tabernacle.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to Moses:€€ƒx—Receive them from them to serve in the ministry of the tabernacle, and thou shalt deliver them to the Levites according to the order of their ministry.€€˜YƒxUTwo wagons and four oxen he gave to the sons of Gerson, according to their necessity.€€ƒx£The other four wagons, and eight oxen he gave to the sons of Merari, according to their offices and service, under the hand of Ithamar the son of Aaron the priest.€€ƒx‰But to the sons of Caath he gave no wagons or oxen: because they serve in the sanctuary and carry their burdens upon their own shoulders.€€ƒxyAnd the princes offered for the dedication of the altar on the day when it was anointed, their oblation before the altar.€€ƒx|And the Lord said to Moses: Let each of the princes one day after another offer their gifts for the dedication of the altar.€€ƒxUThe first day Nahasson the son of Aminadab of the tribe of Juda offered his offering:€€ƒxÊAnd his offering was a silver dish weighing one hundred and thirty sicles, a silver bowl of seventy sicles according to the weight of the sanctuary, both full of flour tempered with oil for a sacrifice:€€ƒx6A little mortar of ten sicles of gold full of incense:€€ƒxEAn ox of the herd, and a ram, and lamb of a year old for a holocaust:€€ƒxAnd a buck goat for sin:€€ƒxAnd for the sacrifice of peace offerings, two oxen, five rams, five he goats, five lambs of a year old. This was the offering of Nahasson the son of Aminadab.€€ƒx]The second day Nathanael the son of Suar, prince of the tribe of Issachar, made his offering,€€ƒx¶A silver dish weighing one hundred and thirty sicles, a silver bowl of seventy sicles, according to the weight of the sanctuary, both full of flour tempered with oil for a sacrifice:€€ƒx<A little mortar of gold weighing ten sicles full of incense:€€ƒxGAn ox of the herd, and a ram, and a lamb of a year old for a holocaust:€€ƒxAnd a buck goat for sin:€€ƒxAnd for the sacrifice of peace offerings, two oxen, five rams, five buck goats, five lambs of a year old. This was the offering of Nathanael the son of Suar.€€ƒxHThe third day the prince of the sons of Zabulon, Eliab the son of Helon,€€ƒx³Offered a silver dish weighing one hundred and thirty sicles, a silver bowl of seventy sicles by the weight of the sanctuary, both full of flour tempered with oil for a sacrifice:€€ƒx<A little mortar of gold weighing ten sicles full of incense:€€ƒxGAn ox of the herd, and a ram, and a lamb of a year old for a holocaust:€€ƒxAnd a buck goat for sin:€€ƒx™And for the sacrifice of peace offerings, two oxen, five rams, five buck goats, five lambs of a year old. This is the oblation of Eliab the son of Helon.€€ƒxIThe fourth day the prince of the sons of Ruben, Elisur the son of Sedeur,€€ƒx½Offered a silver dish weighing one hundred and thirty sicles, a silver bowl of seventy sicles according to the weight of the sanctuary, both full of flour tempered with oil for a sacrifice:€€ƒx<A little mortar of gold weighing ten sicles full of incense:€€ƒxHAn ox of the herd, and a ram, and a lamb of a year old, for a holocaust:€€ƒxAnd a buck goat for sin:€€ƒx•And for victims of peace offerings two oxen, five rams, five buck goats, five lambs of a year old. This was the offering of Elisur the son of Sedeur.€€ƒxOThe fifth day the prince of the sons of Simeon, Salamiel the son of Surisaddai,€€ƒx¶Offered a silver dish weighing one hundred and thirty sicles, a silver bowl of seventy sicles after the weight of the sanctuary, both full of flour tempered with oil for a sacrifice:€€ƒx<A little mortar of gold weighing ten sicles full of incense:€€ƒxGAn ox of the herd, and a ram, and a lamb of a year old for a holocaust:€€ƒxAnd a buck goat for sin:€€ƒxŸAnd for sacrifices of peace offerings, two oxen, five rams, five buck goats, five lambs of a year old. This was the offering of Salamiel the son of Surisaddai.€€ƒxFThe sixth day the prince of the sons of Gad, Eliasaph the son of Duel,€€ƒx±Offered a silver dish weighing a hundred and thirty sicles, a silver bowl of seventy sicles by the weight of the sanctuary, both full of flour tempered with oil for a sacrifice:€€ƒx<A little mortar of gold weighing ten sicles full of incense:€€ƒxGAn ox of the herd, and a ram, and a lamb of a year old for a holocaust:€€ƒxAnd a buck goat for sin:€€ƒx™And for sacrifices of peace offerings, two oxen, five rams, five buck goats, five lambs of a year old. This was the offering of Eliasaph the son of Duel.€€ƒxMThe seventh day the prince of the sons of Ephraim, Elisama the son of Ammiud,€€ƒx»Offered a silver dish weighing a hundred and thirty sicles, a silver bowl of seventy sicles according to the weight of the sanctuary, both full of flour tempered with oil for a sacrifice:€€ƒx<A little mortar of gold weighing ten sicles full of incense:€€ƒxGAn ox of the herd, and a ram, and a lamb of a year old for a holocaust:€€ƒxAnd a buck goat for sin:€€ƒxšAnd for sacrifices of peace offerings, two oxen, five rams, five buck goats, five lambs of a year old. This was the offering of Elisama the son of Ammiud.€€ƒxQThe eighth day the prince of the sons of Manasses, Gamaliel the son of Phadassur,€€ƒx½Offered a silver dish, weighing a hundred and thirty sicles, a silver bowl of seventy sicles, according to the weight of the sanctuary, both full of flour tempered with oil for a sacrifice:€€ƒx<A little mortar of gold weighing ten sicles full of incense:€€ƒxGAn ox of the herd, and a ram, and a lamb of a year old for a holocaust:€€ƒxAnd a buck goat for sin:€€ƒxAnd for sacrifices of peace offerings, two oxen, five rams, five buck goats, five lambs of a year old. This was the offering of Gamaliel the son of Phadassur.€€ƒxKThe ninth day the prince of the sons of Benjamin, Abidan the son of Gedeon,€€ƒx±Offered a silver dish weighing a hundred and thirty sicles, a silver bowl of seventy sicles by the weight of the sanctuary, both full of flour tempered with oil for a sacrifice:€€ƒx<A little mortar of gold weighing ten sicles full of incense:€€ƒxGAn ox of the herd, and a ram, and a lamb of a year old for a holocaust:€€ƒxAnd a buck goat for sin:€€ƒx™And for sacrifices of peace offerings, two oxen, five rams, five buck goats, five lambs of a year old. This was the offering of Abidan the son of Gedeon.€€ƒxLThe tenth day the princes of the sons of Dan, Ahiezer the son of Ammisaddai,€€ƒx¼Offered a silver dish weighing a hundred and thirty sicles, a silver bowl of seventy sicles, according to the weight of the sanctuary, both full of flour tempered with oil for a sacrifice:€€ƒx<A little mortar of gold weighing ten sicles full of incense:€€ƒxGAn ox of the herd, and a ram, and a lamb of a year old for a holocaust:€€ƒxAnd a buck goat for sin:€€ƒxAnd for sacrifices of peace offerings, two oxen, five rams, five buck goats, five lambs of a year old. This was the offering of Ahiezer the son of Ammisaddai.€€ƒxKThe eleventh day the prince of the sons of Aser, Phegiel the son of Ochran,€€ƒx¼Offered a silver dish weighing a hundred and thirty sicles, a silver bowl of seventy sicles, according to the weight of the sanctuary, both full of flour tempered with oil for a sacrifice:€€ƒx<A little mortar of gold weighing ten sicles full of incense:€€ƒxGAn ox of the herd, and a ram, and a lamb of a year old for a holocaust:€€ƒxAnd a buck goat for sin:€€ƒxšAnd for sacrifices of peace offerings, two oxen, five rams, five buck goats, five lambs of a year old. This was the offering of Phegiel the son of Ochran.€€ƒxJThe twelfth day the prince of the sons of Nephtali, Ahira the son of Enan,€€ƒx¼Offered a silver dish weighing a hundred and thirty sicles, a silver bowl of seventy sicles, according to the weight of the sanctuary, both full of flour tempered with oil for a sacrifice:€€ƒx<A little mortar of gold weighing ten sicles full of incense:€€ƒxGAn ox of the herd, and a ram, and a lamb of a year old for a holocaust:€€ƒxAnd a buck goat for sin:€€ƒx–And for sacrifices of peace offerings, two oxen, five rams, five buck goats, five lambs of a year old. This was the offering of Ahira the son of Enan.€€ƒxÊThese were the offerings made by the princes of Israel in the dedication of the altar, in the day wherein it was consecrated. Twelve dishes of silver: twelve silver bowls: twelve little mortars of gold:€€ƒxÎEach dish weighing a hundred and thirty sicles of silver, and each bowl seventy sicles: that is, putting all the vessels of silver together, two thousand four hundred sicles, by the weight of the sanctuary.€€ƒxŸTwelve little mortars of gold full of incense, weighing ten sicles apiece, by the weight of the sanctuary: that is, in all a hundred and twenty sicles of gold.€€ƒx…Twelve oxen out of the herd for a holocaust, twelve rams, twelve lambs of a year old, and their libations: twelve buck goats for sin.€€ƒxÁAnd for sacrifices of peace offerings, oxen twenty-four, rams sixty, buck goats sixty, lambs of a year old sixty. These things were offered in the dedication of the altar, when it was anointed.€€ƒxèAnd when Moses entered into the tabernacle of the covenant, to consult the oracle, he heard the voice of one speaking to him from the propitiatory, that is over the ark between the two cherubims, and from this place he spoke to him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒyISpeak to Aaron, and thou shalt say to him: When thou shalt place the seven lamps, let the candlestick be set up on the south side. Give orders therefore that the lamps look over against the north, towards the table of the loaves of proposition, over against that part shall they give light, towards which the candlestick looketh.€€ƒx]And Aaron did so, and he put the lamps upon the candlestick, as the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒxëNow this was the work of the candlestick, it was of beaten gold, both the shaft in the middle, and all that came out of both sides of the branches: according to the pattern which the Lord had shewn to Moses, so he made the candlestick.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€˜ƒxÀAccording to this rite: Let them be sprinkled with the water of purification, and let them shave all the hairs of their flesh. And when they shall have washed their garments, and are cleansed,€€ƒxœThey shall take an ox of the herd, and for the offering thereof fine flour tempered with oil: and thou shalt take another ox of the herd for a sin offering:€€ƒx…And thou shalt bring the Levites before the tabernacle of the covenant, calling together all the multitude of the children of Israel:€€ƒxaAnd when the Levites are before the Lord, the children of Israel shall put their hands upon them:€€ƒx‡And Aaron shall offer the Levites, as a gift in the sight of the Lord from the children of Israel, that they may serve in his ministry.€€ƒxªThe Levites also shall put their hands upon the heads of the oxen, of which thou shalt sacrifice one for sin, and the other for a holocaust to the Lord, to pray for them.€€ƒxuAnd thou shalt set the Levites in the sight of Aaron and of his, and shalt consecrate them being offered to the Lord,€€ƒxMAnd shalt separate them from the midst of the children of Israel, to be mine.€€ƒxÙAnd afterwards they shall enter into the tabernacle of the covenant, to serve me. And thus shalt thou purify and consecrate them for an oblation of the Lord: for as a gift they were given me by the children of Israel.€€ƒxJI have taken them instead of the firstborn that open every womb in Israel,€€ƒx¶For all the firstborn of the children of Israel, both of men and of beasts, are mine. From the day that I slew every firstborn in the land of Egypt, have I sanctified them to myself:€€ƒxMAnd I have taken the Levites for all the firstborn of the children of Israel:€€ƒyAnd have delivered them for a gift to Aaron and his sons out of the midst of the people, to serve me for Israel in the tabernacle of the covenant, and to pray for them, lest there should be a plague among the people, if they should presume to approach unto my sanctuary.€€ƒx~And Moses and Aaron and all the multitude of the children of Israel did with the Levites all that the Lord had commanded Moses€€ƒxzAnd they were purified, and washed their garments. And Aaron lifted them up in the sight of the Lord, and prayed for them,€€ƒx»That being purified they might go into the tabernacle of the covenant to do their services before Aaron and his sons. As the Lord had commanded Moses touching the Levites, so was it done.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx‡This is the law of the Levites: From twenty-five years old and upwards, they shall go in to minister in the tabernacle of the covenant.€€ƒx`And when they shall have accomplished the fiftieth year of their age, they shall cease to serve:€€ƒxÛAnd they shall be the ministers of their brethren in the tabernacle of the covenant, to keep the things that are committed to their care, but not to do the works. Thus shalt thou order the Levites touching their charge.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŠThe Lord spoke to Moses in the desert of Sinai, the second year after they were come out of the land of Egypt, in the first month, saying:€€ƒx:Let the children of Israel make the phase in its due time,€€ƒxlThe fourteenth day of this month in the evening, according to all the ceremonies and justifications thereof.€€ƒxKAnd Moses commanded the children of Israel that they should make the phase.€€ƒx¶And they made it in its proper time: the fourteenth day of the month at evening, in mount Sinai. The children of Israel did according to all things that the Lord had commanded Moses.€€—ƒx®Said to them: We are unclean by occasion of the soul of a man. Why are we kept back that we may not offer in its season the offering to the Lord among the children of Israel?€€ƒx]And Moses answered them: Stay that I may consult the Lord what he will ordain concerning you.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx³Say to the children of Israel: The man that shall be unclean by occasion of one that is dead, or shall be in a journey afar off in your nation, let him make the phase to the Lord.€€ƒxIn the second month, on the fourteenth day of the month in the evening, they shall eat it with unleavened bread and wild lettuce:€€ƒxƒThey shall not leave any thing thereof until morning, nor break a bone thereof, they shall observe all the ceremonies of the phase.€€ƒxÓBut if any man is clean, and was not on a journey, and did not make the phase, that soul shall be cut off from among his people, because he offered not sacrifice to the Lord in due season: he shall bear his sin.€€ƒxöThe sojourner also and the stranger if they be among you, shall make the phase to the Lord according to the ceremonies and justifications thereof. The same ordinances shall be with you both for the stranger, and for him that was born in the land.€€ƒx¯Now on the day that the tabernacle was reared up, a cloud covered it. But from the evening there was over the tabernacle, as it were, the appearance of fire until the morning.€€ƒx^So it was always: by day the cloud covered it, and by night as it were the appearance of fire.€€ƒxªAnd when the cloud that covered the tabernacle was taken up, then the children of Israel marched forward: and in the place where the cloud stood still, there they camped.€€ƒx¸At the commandment of the Lord they marched, and at his commandment they pitched the tabernacle. All the days that the cloud abode over the tabernacle, they remained in the same place:€€ƒx}And if it was so that it continued over it a long time, the children of Israel kept the watches of the Lord, and marched not,€€ƒx¥For as many days soever as the cloud stayed over the tabernacle. At the commandment of the Lord they pitched their tents, and at his commandment they took them down.€€ƒxÃIf the cloud tarried from evening until morning, and immediately at break of day left the tabernacle, they marched forward: and if it departed after a day and a night, they took down their tents.€€ƒxÓBut if it remained over the tabernacle for two days or a month or a longer time, the children of Israel remained in the same place, and marched not: but immediately as soon as it departed, they removed the camp.€€ƒx£By the word of the Lord they pitched their tents, and by his word they marched: and kept the watches of the Lord according to his commandment by the hand of Moses.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxzMake thee two trumpets of beaten silver, wherewith thou mayest call together the multitude when the camp is to be removed.€€ƒxAnd when thou shalt sound the trumpets, all the multitude shall gather unto thee to the door of the tabernacle of the covenant.€€ƒx`If thou sound but once, the princes and the heads of the multitude of Israel shall come to thee.€€ƒx{But if the sound of the trumpets be longer, and with interruptions, they that are on the east side, shall first go forward.€€˜$ƒxBut when the people is to be gathered together, the sound of the trumpets shall be plain, and they shall not make a broken sound.€€ƒxwAnd the sons of Aaron the priest shall sound the trumpets: and this shall be an ordinance for ever in your generations.€€ƒxúIf you go forth to war out of your land against the enemies that fight against you, you shall sound aloud with the trumpets, and there shall be a remembrance of you before the Lord your God, that you may be delivered out of the hands of your enemies.€€ƒyIf at any time you shall have a banquet, and on your festival days, and on the first days of your months, you shall sound the trumpets over the holocausts, and the sacrifices of peace offerings, that they may be to you for a remembrance of your God. I am the Lord your God.€€ƒxThe second year, in the second month, the twentieth day of the month, the cloud was taken up from the tabernacle of the covenant.€€ƒx~And the children of Israel marched by their troops from the desert of Sinai, and the cloud rested in the wilderness of Pharan.€€ƒxYAnd the first went forward according to the commandment of the Lord by the hand of Moses.€€ƒxPThe sons of Juda by their troops: whose prince was Nahasson the son of Aminadab.€€ƒxOIn the tribe of the sons of Issachar, the prince was Nathanael the son of Suar.€€ƒx?In the tribe of Zabulon, the prince was Eliab the son of Helon.€€ƒx]And the tabernacle was taken down, and the sons of Gerson and Merari set forward, bearing it.€€ƒxjAnd the sons of Ruben also marched, by their troops and ranks, whose prince was Helisur the son of Sedeur.€€ƒxJAnd in the tribe of Simeon, the prince was Salamiel the son of Surisaddai.€€ƒxAAnd in the tribe of Gad, the prince was Eliasaph the son of Duel.€€ƒx‰Then the Caathites also marched carrying the sanctuary. So long was the tabernacle carried, till they came to the place of setting it up.€€ƒxrThe sons of Ephraim also moved their camp by their troops, in whose army the prince was Elisama the son of Ammiud.€€ƒxWAnd in the tribe of the sons of Manasses, the prince was Gamaliel the son of Phadassur.€€ƒxFAnd in the tribe of Benjamin, the prince was Abidan the son of Gedeon.€€ƒx}The last of all the camp marched the sons of Dan by their troops, in whose army the prince was Ahiezer the son of Ammisaddai.€€ƒxOAnd in the tribe of the sons of Aser, the prince was Phegiel the son of Ochran.€€ƒxOAnd in the tribe of the sons of Nephtali, the prince was Ahira the son of Enan.€€ƒxnThis was the order of the camps, and marches of the children of Israel by their troops, when they set forward.€€ƒxÛAnd Moses said to Hobab the son of Raguel the Madianite, his kinsman: We are going towards the place which the Lord will give us: come with us, that we may do thee good: for the Lord hath promised good things to Israel.€€ƒxbBut he answered him: I will not go with thee, but I will return to my country, wherein I was born.€€ƒx~And he said: Do not leave us: for thou knowest in what places we should encamp in the wilderness, and thou shalt be our guide.€€ƒxpAnd if thou comest with us, we will give thee what is the best of the riches which the Lord shall deliver to us.€€ƒx¨So they marched from the mount of the Lord three days' journey, and the ark of the covenant of the Lord went before them, for three days providing a place for the camp.€€ƒxBThe cloud also of the Lord was over them by day when they marched.€€ƒx”And when the ark was lifted up, Moses said: Arise, O Lord, and let thy enemies be scattered, and let them that hate thee, flee from before thy face.€€ƒxZAnd when it was set down, he said: Return, O Lord, to the multitude of the host of Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒyIn the mean time there arose a murmuring of the people against the Lord, as it were repining at their fatigue. And when the Lord heard it he was angry. And the fire of the Lord being kindled against them, devoured them that were at the uttermost part of the camp.€€ƒx\And when the people cried to Moses, Moses prayed to the Lord, and the fire was swallowed up.€€ƒxoAnd he called the name of that place, The burning: for that the fire of the Lord had been kindled against them.€€ƒx¾For a mixt multitude of people, that came up with them, burned with desire, sitting and weeping, the children of Israel also being joined with them, and said: Who shall give us flesh to eat?€€ƒx•We remember the fish that we ate in Egypt free cost: the cucumbers come into our mind, and the melons, and the leeks, and the onions, and the garlic.€€˜#ƒxANow the manna was like coriander seed, of the colour of bdellium.€€ƒx±And the people went about, and gathering it, ground it in a mill, or beat it in a mortar, and boiled it in a pot, and made cakes thereof of the taste of bread tempered with oil.€€ƒxNAnd when the dew fell in the night upon the camp, the manna also fell with it.€€ƒx»Now Moses heard the people weeping by their families, every one at the door of his tent. And the wrath of the Lord was exceedingly enkindled: to Moses also the thing seemed insupportable.€€ƒx§And he said to the Lord: Why hast thou afflicted thy servant? Wherefore do I not find favour before thee? And why hast thou laid the weight of all this people upon me?€€ƒxæHave I conceived all this multitude, or begotten them, that thou shouldst say to me: Carry them in thy bosom as the nurse is wont to carry the little infant, and bear them into the land, for which thou hast sworn to their fathers?€€ƒxxWhence should I have flesh to give to so great a multitude? They weep against me, saying: Give us flesh that we may eat.€€ƒxLI am not able alone to bear all this people, because it is too heavy for me.€€ƒxBut if it seem unto thee otherwise, I beseech thee to kill me, and let me find grace in thy eyes, that I be not afflicted with so great evils.€€ƒyAnd the Lord said to Moses: Gather unto me seventy men of the ancients of Israel, whom thou knowest to be ancients and masters of the people: and thou shalt bring them to the door of the tabernacle of the covenant, and shalt make them stand there with thee,€€ƒx¾That I may come down and speak with thee: and I will take of thy spirit, and will give to them, that they may bear with thee the burden of the people, and thou mayest not be burthened alone.€€ƒxÜAnd thou shalt say to the people: Be ye sanctified: to morrow you shall eat flesh: for I have heard you say: Who will give us flesh to eat? It was well with us in Egypt. That the Lord may give you flesh, and you may eat:€€ƒx?Not for one day, nor two, nor five, nor ten, no nor for twenty.€€ƒxÙBut even for a month of days, till it come out at your nostrils, and become loathsome to you, because you have cast off the Lord, who is in the midst of you, and have wept before him, saying: Why came we out of Egypt?€€ƒx„And Moses said: There are six hundred thousand footmen of this people, and sayest thou: I will give them flesh to eat a whole month?€€ƒx™Shall then a multitude of sheep and oxen be killed, that it may suffice for their food? or shall the fishes of the sea be gathered together to fill them?€€ƒx}And the Lord answered him: Is the hand of the Lord unable? Thou shalt presently see whether my word shall come to pass or no.€€ƒx¢Moses therefore came, and told the people the words of the Lord, and assembled seventy men of the ancients of Israel, and made them to stand about the tabernacle.€€ƒx×And the Lord came down in a cloud, and spoke to him, taking away of the spirit that was in Moses, and giving to the seventy men. And when the spirit had rested on them they prophesied, nor did they cease afterwards.€€ƒxÊNow there remained in the camp two of the men, of whom one was called Eldad, and the other Medad, upon whom the spirit rested; for they also had been enrolled, but were not gone forth to the tabernacle.€€ƒxzAnd when they prophesied in the camp, there ran a young man, and told Moses, saying: Eldad and Medad prophesy in the camp.€€ƒxoForthwith Josue the son of Nun, the minister of Moses, and chosen out of many, said: My lord Moses forbid them.€€ƒx€But he said: Why hast thou emulation for me? O that all the people might prophesy, and that the Lord would give them his spirit!€€ƒx?And Moses returned, with the ancients of Israel, into the camp.€€ƒxóAnd a wind going out from the Lord, taking quails up beyond the sea brought them, and cast them into the camp for the space of one day's journey, on every side of the camp round about, and they flew in the air two cubits high above the ground.€€ƒx®The people therefore rising up all that day, and night, and the next day, gathered together of quails, he that did least, ten cores: and they dried them round about the camp.€€ƒxÀAs yet the flesh was between their teeth, neither had that kind of meat failed: when behold the wrath of the Lord being provoked against the people, struck them with an exceeding great plague.€€ƒx±And that place was called, The graves of lust: for there they buried the people that had lusted. And departing from the graves of lust, they came unto Haseroth, and abode there.€€ƒ`€€ƒxJAnd Mary and Aaron spoke against Moses, because of his wife the Ethiopian,€€ƒx~And they said: Hath the Lord spoken by Moses only? Hath he not also spoken to us in like manner? And when the Lord heard this,€€ƒxH(For Moses was a man exceeding meek above all men that dwelt upon earth)€€ƒx‹Immediately he spoke to him, and to Aaron and Mary: Come out you three only to the tabernacle of the covenant. And when they were come out,€€ƒx‰The Lord came down in a pillar of the cloud, and stood in the entry of the tabernacle calling to Aaron and Mary. And when they were come,€€ƒx‘He said to them: Hear my words: if there be among you a prophet of the Lord, I will appear to him in a vision, or I will speak to him in a dream.€€ƒx£For I speak to him mouth to mouth: and plainly, and not by riddles and figures doth he see the Lord. Why then were you not afraid to speak ill of my servant Moses?€€ƒx'And being angry with them he went away:€€ƒx´The cloud also that was over the tabernacle departed: and behold Mary appeared white as snow with a leprosy. And when Aaron had looked on her, and saw her all covered with leprosy,€€ƒxgHe said to Moses: I beseech thee, my lord, lay not upon us this sin, which we have foolishly committed:€€ƒx•Let her not be as one dead, and as an abortive that is cast forth from the mother's womb. Lo, now one half of her flesh is consumed with the leprosy.€€ƒxCAnd Moses cried to the Lord, saying O God, I beseech thee heal her.€€ƒxÛAnd the Lord answered him: If her father had spitten upon her face, ought she not to have been ashamed for seven days at least? Let her be separated seven days without the camp, and afterwards she shall be called again.€€ƒxxMary therefore was put out of the camp seven days: and the people moved not from that place until Mary was called again.€€ƒ`€€ƒxVAnd the people marched from Haseroth, and pitched their tents in the desert of Pharan.€€ƒx*And there the Lord spoke to Moses, saying.€€ƒxuSend men to view the land of Chanaan, which I will give to the children of Israel, one of every tribe, of the rulers.€€ƒxoMoses did what the Lord had commanded, sending from the desert of Pharan, principal men, whose names are these:€€ƒx0Of the tribe of Ruben, Sammua the son of Zechur.€€ƒx/Of the tribe of Simeon, Saphat the son of Huri.€€ƒx/Of the tribe of Juda, Caleb the son of Jephone.€€˜!ƒx-Of the tribe of Ephraim, Osee the son of Nun.€€ƒx2Of the tribe of Benjamin, Phalti the son of Raphu.€€ƒx1Of the tribe of Zabulon, Geddiel the son of Sodi.€€ƒxJOf the tribe of Joseph, of the sceptre of Manasses, Gaddi the son of Susi.€€ƒx/Of the tribe of Dan, Ammiel the son of Gemalli.€€ƒx/Of the tribe of Aser, Sthur the son of Michael.€€ƒx2Of the tribe of Nephtali, Nahabi the son of Vapsi.€€ƒx+Of the tribe of Gad, Guel the son of Machi.€€ƒxkThese are the names of the men, whom Moses sent to view the land: and he called Osee the son of Nun, Josue.€€ƒx‰And Moses sent them to view the land of Chanaan, and said to them: Go you up by the south side. And when you shall come to the mountains,€€ƒxŠView the land, of what sort it is, and the people that are the inhabitants thereof, whether they be strong or weak: few in number or many:€€ƒx[The land itself, whether it be good or bad: what manner of cities, walled or without walls:€€ƒx±The ground, fat or barren, woody or without trees. Be of good courage, and bring us of the fruits of the land. Now it was the time when the firstripe grapes are fit to be eaten.€€ƒxlAnd when they were gone up, they viewed the land from the desert of Sin, unto Rohob as you enter into Emath.€€ƒx³And they went up at the south side, and came to Hebron, where were Achiman and Sisai and Tholmai the sons of Enac. For Hebron was built seven years before Tanis the city of Egypt.€€ƒxÓAnd forward as far as the torrent of the cluster of grapes, they cut off a branch with its cluster of grapes, which two men carried upon a lever. They took also of the pomegranates and of the figs of that place:€€ƒxWhich was called Nehelescol, that is to say, the torrent of the cluster of grapes, because from thence the children of Israel had carried a cluster of grapes.€€ƒxdAnd they that went to spy out the land returned after forty days, having gone round all the country,€€ƒxÑAnd came to Moses and Aaron and to all the assembly of the children of Israel to the desert of Pharan, which is in Cades. And speaking to them and to all the multitude, they shewed them the fruits of the land:€€ƒxšAnd they related and said: We came into the land to which thou sentest us, which in very deed floweth with milk and honey as may be known by these fruits:€€ƒxhBut it hath very strong inhabitants, and the cities are great and walled. We saw there the race of Enac.€€ƒx¨Amalec dwelleth in the south, the Hethite and the Jebusite and the Amorrhite in the mountains: but the Chanaanite abideth by the sea and near the streams of the Jordan.€€ƒx¢In the mean time Caleb, to still the murmuring of the people that rose against Moses, said: Let us go up and possess the land, for we shall be able to conquer it.€€ƒx}But the others, that had been with him, said: No, we are not able to go up to this people, because they are stronger than we.€€ƒxÉAnd they spoke ill of the land, which they had viewed, before the children of Israel, saying: The land which we have viewed, devoureth its inhabitants: the people, that we beheld are of a tall stature.€€ƒxtThere we saw certain monsters of the sons of Enac, of the giant kind: in comparison of whom, we seemed like locusts.€€ƒ`€€ƒx5Therefore the whole multitude crying wept that night.€€ƒxHAnd all the children of Israel murmured against Moses and Aaron, saying:€€ƒxüWould God that we had died in Egypt: and would God we may die in this vast wilderness, and that the Lord may not bring us into this land, lest we fall by the sword, and our wives and children be led away captives. Is it not better to return into Egypt?€€ƒxUAnd they said one to another: Let us appoint a captain, and let us return into Egypt.€€ƒxxAnd when Moses and Aaron heard this, they fell down flat upon the ground before the multitude of the children of Israel.€€ƒxuBut Josue the son of Nun, and Caleb the son of Jephone, who themselves also had viewed the land, rent their garments,€€˜-ƒxdIf the Lord be favourable, he will bring us into it, and give us a land flowing with milk and honey.€€ƒx²Be not rebellious against the Lord: and fear ye not the people of this land, for we are able to eat them up as bread. All aid is gone from them: the Lord is with us, fear ye not.€€ƒx£And when all the multitude cried out, and would have stoned them, the glory of the Lord appeared over the tabernacle of the covenant to all the children of Israel.€€ƒx–And the Lord said to Moses: How long will this people detract me? how long will they not believe me for all the signs that I have wrought before them?€€ƒx“I will strike them therefore with pestilence, and will consume them: but thee I will make a ruler over a great nation, and a mightier than this is.€€ƒxkAnd Moses said to the Lord: That the Egyptians, from the midst of whom thou hast brought forth this people,€€ƒxòAnd the inhabitants of this land, (who have heard that thou, O Lord, art among this people, and art seen face to face, and thy cloud protecteth them, and thou goest before them in a pillar of a cloud by day, and in a pillar of fire by night,)€€ƒxSMay hear that thou hast killed so great a multitude as it were one man and may say:€€ƒxqHe could not bring the people into the land for which he had sworn, therefore did he kill them in the wilderness.€€ƒxKLet then the strength of the Lord be magnified, as thou hast sworn, saying:€€ƒxÅThe Lord is patient and full of mercy, by taking away iniquity and wickedness, and leaving no man clear, who visitest the sins of the fathers upon the children unto the third and fourth generation.€€ƒx¬Forgive, I beseech thee, the sins of this people, according to the greatness of thy mercy, as thou hast been merciful to them from their going out of Egypt unto this place.€€ƒx9And the Lord said: I have forgiven according to thy word.€€ƒxJAs I live: and the whole earth shall be filled with the glory of the Lord.€€ƒx¯But yet all the men that have seen my majesty, and the signs that I have done in Egypt, and in the wilderness, and have tempted me now ten times, and have not obeyed my voice,€€ƒxzShall not see the land for which I swore to their fathers, neither shall any one of them that hath detracted me behold it.€€ƒx™My servant Caleb, who being full of another spirit hath followed me, I will bring into this land which he hath gone round: and his seed shall possess it.€€ƒxFor the Amalecite and the Chanaanite dwell in the valleys. To morrow remove the camp, and return into the wilderness by the way of the Red Sea.€€ƒx.And the Lord spoke to Moses and Aaron, saying:€€ƒxmHow long doth this wicked multitude murmur against me? I have heard the murmurings of the children of Israel.€€ƒxrSay therefore to them: As I live, saith the Lord: According as you have spoken in my hearing, so will I do to you.€€ƒx†In the wilderness shall your carcasses lie. All you that were numbered from twenty years old and upward, and have murmured against me,€€ƒx“Shall not enter into the land, over which I lifted up my hand to make you dwell therein, except Caleb the son of Jephone, and Josue the son of Nun.€€ƒx”But your children, of whom you said, that they should be a prey to the enemies, will I bring in: that they may see the land which you have despised.€€ƒx+Your carcasses shall lie in the wilderness.€€ƒx–Your children shall wander in the desert forty years, and shall bear your fornication, until the carcasses of their fathers be consumed in the desert,€€ƒx¸According to the number of the forty days, wherein you viewed the land: a year shall be counted for a day. And forty years you shall receive your iniquities, and shall know my revenge:€€ƒx˜For as I have spoken, so will I do to all this wicked multitude, that hath risen up together against me: in this wilderness shall it faint away and die.€€ƒxµTherefore all the men, whom Moses had sent to view the land, and who at their return had made the whole multitude to murmur against him, speaking ill of the land that it was naught,€€ƒx.Died and were struck in the sight of the Lord.€€ƒx>But Josue the son of Nun, and Caleb had gone to view the land.€€ƒxbAnd Moses spoke all these words to all the children of Israel, and the people mourned exceedingly.€€ƒx¹And behold rising up very early in the morning, they went up to the top of the mountain, and said: We are ready to go up to the place, of which the Lord hath spoken: for we have sinned.€€ƒxoAnd Moses said to them: Why transgress you the word of the Lord, which shall not succeed prosperously with you?€€ƒxKGo not up, for the Lord is not with you: lest you fall before your enemies.€€ƒx¡The Amalecite and the Chanaanite are before you, and by their sword you shall fall, because you would not consent to the Lord, neither will the Lord be with you.€€ƒx‰But they being blinded went up to the top of the mountain. But the ark of the testament of the Lord and Moses departed not from the camp.€€ƒx†And the Amalecite came down, and the Chanaanite that dwelt in the mountain: and smiting and slaying them pursued them as far as Horma.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx‹Speak to the children of Israel and thou shalt say to them: When you shall be come unto the land of your habitation, which I will give you,€€ƒxÈAnd shall make an offering to the Lord, for a holocaust, or a victim, paying your vows, or voluntarily offering gifts, or in your solemnities burning a sweet savour unto the Lord, of oxen or of sheep:€€ƒx‘Whosoever immolateth the victim, shall offer a sacrifice of fine flour, the tenth part of an ephi, tempered with the fourth part of a hin of oil:€€ƒxxAnd he shall give the same measure of wine to pour out in libations for the holocaust or for the victim. For every lamb,€€ƒxAnd for every ram there shall be a sacrifice of flour of two tenths, which shall be tempered with the third part of a hin of oil:€€˜)ƒxmBut when thou offerest a holocaust or sacrifice of oxen, to fulfil thy vow or for victims of peace offerings,€€ƒxSThou shalt give for every ox three tenths of flour tempered with half a hin of oil,€€ƒx]And wine for libations of the same measure, for an offering of most sweet savour to the Lord.€€ƒrThus shalt thou do€€ƒx&For every ox and ram and lamb and kid.€€ƒx6Both they that are born in the land, and the strangers€€ƒx+Shall offer sacrifices after the same rite.€€ƒx`There shall be all one law and judgment both for you and for them who are strangers in the land.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx<Speak to the children of Israel, and thou shalt say to them:€€ƒx6When you are come into the land which I will give you,€€ƒxWAnd shall eat of the bread of that country, you shall separate firstfruits to the Lord,€€ƒxFOf the things you eat. As you separate firstfruits of your barnfloors:€€ƒx=So also shall you give firstfruits of your dough to the Lord.€€ƒx[And if through ignorance you omit any of these things, which the Lord hath spoken to Moses,€€ƒxVAnd by him hath commanded you from the day that he began to command and thenceforward,€€ƒxáAnd the multitude have forgotten to do it: they shall offer a calf out of the herd, a holocaust for a most sweet savour to the Lord, and the sacrifice and libations thereof, as the ceremonies require, and a buck goat for sin:€€ƒxõAnd the priest shall pray for all the multitude of the children of Israel: and it shall be forgiven them, because they sinned ignorantly, offering notwithstanding a burnt offering to the Lord for themselves and for their sin and their Ignorance:€€ƒxªAnd it shall be forgiven all the people of the children of Israel: and the strangers that sojourn among them: because it is the fault of all the people through ignorance.€€ƒxZBut if one soul shall sin ignorantly, he shall offer a she goat of a year old for his sin.€€ƒxAnd the priest shall pray for him, because he sinned ignorantly before the Lord: and he shall obtain his pardon, and it shall be forgiven him.€€ƒxZThe same law shall be for all that sin by ignorance, whether they be natives or strangers.€€ƒx½But the soul that committeth any thing through pride, whether he be born in the land or a stranger (because he hath been rebellious against the Lord) shall be cut off from among his people:€€ƒx„For he hath contemned the word of the Lord, and made void his precept: therefore shall he be destroyed, and shall bear his iniquity.€€ƒxAnd it came to pass, when the children of Israel were in the wilderness, and had found a man gathering sticks on the sabbath day,€€ƒxAThat they brought him to Moses and Aaron and the whole multitude.€€ƒxGAnd they put him into prison, not knowing what they should do with him.€€ƒx_And the Lord said to Moses: Let that man die, let all the multitude stone him without the camp.€€ƒxZAnd when they had brought him out, they stoned him, and he died as the Lord had commanded.€€ƒxThe Lord also said to Moses:€€ƒxšSpeak to the children of Israel, and thou shalt tell them to make to themselves fringes in the corners of their garments, putting in them ribands of blue:€€ƒxŸThat when they shall see them, they may remember all the commandments of the Lord, and not follow their own thoughts and eyes going astray after divers things,€€ƒx[But rather being mindful of the precepts of the Lord, may do them and be holy to their God.€€ƒx[I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, that I might be your God.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¢And behold Core the son of Isaar, the son of Caath, the son of Levi, and Dathan and Abiron the sons of Eliab, and Hon the son of Pheleth of the children of Ruben,€€ƒx¯Rose up against Moses, and with them two hundred and fifty others of the children of Israel, leading men of the synagogue, and who in the time of assembly were called by name.€€ƒxİAnd when they had stood up against Moses and Aaron, they said: Let it be enough for you, that all the multitude consisteth of holy ones, and the Lord is among them: Why lift you up yourselves above the people of the Lord?€€ƒx0When Moses heard this, he fell flat on his face:€€ƒxËAnd speaking to Core and all the multitude, he said: In the morning the Lord will make known who belong to him, and the holy he will join to himself: and whom he shall choose, they shall approach to him.€€ƒxVDo this therefore: Take every man of you your censers, thou Core, and all thy company.€€˜2ƒx0And he said again to Core: Hear ye sons of Levi.€€ƒyIs it a small thing unto you, that the God of Israel hath spared you from all the people, and joined you to himself, that you should serve him in the service of the tabernacle, and should stand before the congregation of the people, and should minister to him?€€ƒx“Did he therefore make thee and all thy brethren the sons of Levi to approach unto him, that you should challenge to yourselves the priesthood also,€€ƒxfAnd that all thy company should stand against the Lord? for what is Aaron that you murmur against him?€€ƒxaThen Moses sent to call Dathan and Abiron the sons of Eliab. But they answered: We will not come.€€ƒx«Is it a small matter to thee, that thou hast brought us out of a land that flowed with milk and honey, to kill us in the desert, except thou rule also like a lord over us?€€ƒx¾Thou hast brought us indeed into a land that floweth with rivers of milk and honey, and hast given us possessions of fields and vineyards; wilt thou also pull out our eyes? We will not come.€€ƒx¾Moses therefore being very angry, said to the Lord: Respect not their sacrifices: thou knowest that I have not taken of them so much as a young ass at any time, nor have injured any of them.€€ƒxiAnd he said to Core: Do thou and thy congregation stand apart before the Lord to morrow, and Aaron apart.€€ƒxTake every one of you censers, and put incense upon them, offering to the Lord two hundred and fifty censers: let Aaron also hold his censer.€€ƒx2When they had done this, Moses and Aaron standing,€€ƒxzAnd had drawn up all the multitude against them to the door of the tabernacle, the glory of the Lord appeared to them all.€€ƒx/And the Lord speaking to Moses and Aaron, said:€€ƒxTSeparate yourselves from among this congregation, that I may presently destroy them.€€ƒxThey fell flat on their face, and said: O most mighty, the God of the spirits of all flesh, for one man's sin shall thy wrath rage against all?€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to Moses:€€ƒx]Command the whole people to separate themselves from the tents of Core and Dathan and Abiron.€€ƒxYAnd Moses arose, and went to Dathan and Abiron: and the ancients of Israel following him,€€ƒx…He said to the multitude: Depart from the tents of these wicked men, and touch nothing of theirs, lest you be involved in their sins.€€ƒx¯And when they were departed from their tents round about, Dathan and Abiron coming out stood in the entry of their pavilions with their wives and children, and all the people.€€ƒxAnd Moses said: By this you shall know that the Lord hath sent me to do all things that you see, and that I have not forged them of my own head:€€ƒx—If these men die the common death of men, and if they be visited with a plague, wherewith others also are wont to be visited, the Lord did not send me.€€ƒxËBut if the Lord do a new thing, and the earth opening her mouth swallow them down, and all things that belong to them, and they go down alive into hell, you shall know that they have blasphemed the Lord.€€ƒx\And immediately as he had made an end of speaking, the earth broke asunder under their feet:€€ƒxNAnd opening her mouth, devoured them with their tents and all their substance.€€ƒxjAnd they went down alive into hell, the ground closing upon them, and they perished from among the people.€€ƒxBut all Israel, that was standing round about, fled at the cry of them that were perishing: saying: Lest perhaps the earth swallow us up also.€€ƒxfAnd a fire coming out from the Lord, destroyed the two hundred and fifty men that offered the incense.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxªCommand Eleazar the son of Aaron the priest to take up the censers that lie in the burning, and to scatter the fire of one side and the other: because they are sanctified€€ƒxòIn the deaths of the sinners: and let him beat them into plates, and fasten them to the altar, because incense hath been offered in them to the Lord, and they are sanctified, that the children of Israel may see them for a sign and a memorial.€€ƒx¦Then Eleazar the priest took the brazen censers, wherein they had offered, whom the burning fire had devoured, and beat them into plates, fastening them to the altar:€€ƒy4That the children of Israel might have for the time to come wherewith they should be admonished, that no stranger or any one that is not of the seed of Aaron should come near to offer incense to the Lord, lest he should suffer as Core suffered, and all his congregation, according as the Lord spoke to Moses.€€ƒxThe following day all the multitude of the children of Israel murmured against Moses and Aaron, saying: You have killed the people of the Lord.€€ƒx:And when there arose a sedition, and the tumult increased,€€ƒx’Moses and Aaron fled to the tabernacle of the covenant. And when they were gone into it, the cloud covered it, and the glory of the Lord appeared.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to Moses:€€ƒxtGet you out from the midst of this multitude, this moment will I destroy them. And as they were lying on the ground,€€ƒxÔMoses said to Aaron: Take the censer, and putting fire in it from the altar, put incense upon it, and go quickly to the people to pray for them: for already wrath is gone out from the Lord, and the plague rageth.€€ƒx†When Aaron had done this, and had run to the midst of the multitude which the burning fire was now destroying, he offered the incense:€€ƒx^And standing between the dead and the living, he prayed for the people, and the plague ceased.€€ƒx‹And the number of them that were slain was fourteen thousand and seven hundred men, besides them that had perished in the sedition of Core.€€ƒxiAnd Aaron returned to Moses to the door of the tabernacle of the covenant after the destruction was over.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx´Speak to the children of Israel, and take of every one of them a rod by their kindreds, of all the princes of the tribes, twelve rods, and write the name of every man upon his rod.€€ƒxcAnd the name of Aaron shall be for the tribe of Levi, and one rod shall contain all their families:€€ƒxnAnd thou shalt lay them up in the tabernacle of the covenant before the testimony, where I will speak to thee.€€ƒx¨Whomsoever of these I shall choose, his rod shall blossom: and I will make to cease from me the murmurings of the children of Israel, wherewith they murmur against you.€€ƒx–And Moses spoke to the children of Israel: and all the princes gave him rods one for every tribe: and there were twelve rods besides the rod of Aaron.€€ƒxÎHe returned on the following day, and found that the rod of Aaron for the house of Levi, was budded: and that the buds swelling it hid bloomed blossoms, which spreading the leaves, were formed into almonds.€€ƒxMoses therefore brought out all the rods from before the Lord to all the children of Israel: and they saw, and every one received their rods.€€ƒxäAnd the Lord said to Moses: Carry back the rod of Aaron into the tabernacle of the testimony, that it may be kept there for a token of the rebellious children of Israel, and that their complaints may cease from me lest they die.€€ƒx(And Moses did as the Lord had commanded.€€ƒxPAnd the children of Israel said to Moses: Behold we are consumed, we all perish.€€ƒxkWhosoever approacheth to the tabernacle of the Lord, he dieth. Are we all to a man to be utterly destroyed?€€ƒ`€€ƒxÂAnd the Lord said to Aaron: Thou, and thy sons, and thy father's house with thee shall bear the iniquity of the sanctuary: and thou and thy sons with thee shall bear the sins of your priesthood.€€ƒx×And take with thee thy brethren also of the tribe of Levi, and the sceptre of thy father, and let them be ready in hand, and minister to thee: but thou and thy sons shall minister in the tabernacle of the testimony.€€ƒx×And the Levites shall watch to do thy commands, and about all the works of the tabernacle: only they shall not come nigh the vessels of the sanctuary nor the altar, lest both they die, and you also perish with them.€€ƒx”But let them be with thee, and watch in the charge of the tabernacle, and in all the ceremonies thereof. A stranger shall not join himself with you.€€ƒx}Watch ye in the charge of the sanctuary, and in the ministry of the altar: lest indignation rise upon the children of Israel.€€ƒx«I have given you your brethren the Levites from among the children of Israel, and have delivered them for a gift to the Lord, to serve in the ministries of the tabernacle.€€˜ ƒxéAnd the Lord said to Aaron: Behold I have given thee the charge of my firstfruits. All things that are sanctified by the children of Israel, I have delivered to thee and to thy sons for the priestly office, by everlasting ordinances.€€ƒxöThese therefore shalt thou take of the things that are sanctified, and are offered to the Lord. Every offering, and sacrifice, and whatsoever is rendered to me for sin and for trespass, and becometh holy of holies, shall be for thee and thy sons.€€ƒxpThou shalt eat it in the sanctuary: the males only shall eat thereof, because it is a consecrated thing to thee.€€ƒxÆBut the firstfruits, which the children of Israel shall vow and offer, I have given to thee, and to thy sons, and to thy daughters, by a perpetual law. He that is clean in thy house, shall eat them.€€ƒx„All the best of the oil, and of the wine, and of the corn, whatsoever firstfruits they offer to the Lord, I have given them to thee.€€ƒx¨All the firstripe of the fruits, that the ground bringeth forth, and which are brought to the Lord, shall be for thy use: he that is clean in thy house, shall eat them.€€ƒxJEvery thing that the children of Israel shall give by vow, shall be thine.€€ƒxõWhatsoever is firstborn of all flesh, which they offer to the Lord, whether it be of men, or of beasts, shall belong to thee: only for the firstborn of man thou shalt take a price, and every beast that is unclean thou shalt cause to be redeemed,€€ƒxˆAnd the redemption of it shall be after one month, for five sicles of silver, by the weight of the sanctuary. A sicle hath twenty obols.€€ƒxùBut the firstling of a cow, and of a sheep and of a goat thou shalt not cause to be redeemed, because they are sanctified to the Lord. Their blood only thou shalt pour upon the altar, and their fat thou shalt burn for a most sweet odour to the Lord.€€ƒxfBut the flesh shall fall to thy use, as the consecrated breast, and the right shoulder shall be thine.€€ƒxîAll the firstfruits of the sanctuary which the children of Israel offer to the Lord, I have given to thee and to thy sons and daughters, by a perpetual ordinance. It is a covenant of salt for ever before the Lord, to thee and to thy sons.€€ƒxºAnd the Lord said to Aaron: You shall possess nothing in their land, neither shall you have a portion among them: I am thy portion and inheritance in the midst of the children of Israel.€€ƒx›And I have given to the sons of Levi all the tithes of Israel for a possession, for the ministry wherewith they serve me in the tabernacle of the covenant:€€ƒx_That the children of Israel may not approach any more to the tabernacle, nor commit deadly sin,€€ƒx¼But only the sons of Levi may serve me in the tabernacle, and bear the sins of the people. It shall be an everlasting ordinance in your generations. They shall not possess any other thing,€€ƒxbBut be content with the oblation or tithes, which I have separated for their uses and necessities.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxØCommand the Levites, and declare unto them: When you shall receive of the children of Israel the tithes, which I have given you, offer the firstfruits of them to the Lord, that is to say, the tenth part of the tenth:€€ƒxnThat it may be reckoned to you as an oblation of firstfruits, as well of the barnfloors as of the winepresses:€€ƒxxAnd of all the things of which you receive tithes, offer the firstfruits to the Lord, and give them to Aaron the priest.€€ƒx‡All the things that you shall offer of the tithes, and shall separate for the gifts of the Lord, shall be the best and choicest things.€€ƒxÀAnd thou shalt say to them: If you offer all the goodly and the better things of the tithes, it shall be reckoned to you as if you had given the firstfruits of the barnfloor and the winepress:€€ƒxªAnd you shall eat them in all your places, both you and your families: because it is your reward for the ministry, wherewith you serve in the tabernacle of the testimony.€€ƒxŸAnd you shall not sin in this point, by reserving the choicest and fat things to yourselves, lest you profane the oblations of the children of Israel, and die.€€ƒ`€€ƒx.And the Lord spoke to Moses and Aaron, saying:€€ƒx×This is the observance of the victim, which the Lord hath ordained. Command the children of Israel, that they bring unto thee a red cow of full age, in which there is no blemish, and which hath not carried the yoke:€€ƒxˆAnd you shall deliver her to Eleazar the priest, who shall bring her forth without the camp, and shall immolate her in the sight of all:€€ƒxkAnd dipping his finger in her blood, shall sprinkle it over against the door of the tabernacle seven times,€€ƒxvAnd shall burn her in the sight of all delivering up to the fire her skin, and her flesh, and her blood, and her dung.€€ƒx†The priest shall also take cedar wood, and hyssop, and scarlet twice dyed, and cast it into the flame, with which the cow is consumed.€€–ƒxlHe also that hath burned her, shall wash his garments, and his body, and shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒyAnd a man that is clean shall gather up the ashes of the cow, and shall pour them forth without the camp in a most clean place, that they may be reserved for the multitude of the children of Israel, and for a water of aspersion: because the cow was burnt for sin.€€ƒxîAnd when he that carried the ashes of the cow, hath washed his garments, he shall be unclean until the evening. The children of Israel, and the strangers that dwell among them, shall observe this for a holy thing by a perpetual ordinance.€€ƒxJHe that toucheth the corpse of a man, and is therefore unclean seven days,€€ƒx³Shall be sprinkled with this water on the third day, and on the seventh, and so shall be cleansed. If he were not sprinkled on the third day, he cannot be cleansed on the seventh.€€ƒyEvery one that toucheth the corpse of a man, and is not sprinkled with this mixture, shall profane the tabernacle of the Lord, and shall perish out of Israel: because he was not sprinkled with the water of expiation, he shall be unclean, and his uncleanness shall remain upon him.€€ƒx‰This is the law of a man that dieth in a tent: All that go into his tent and all the vessels that are there, shall be unclean seven days.€€ƒxEThe vessel that hath no cover, nor binding over it, shall be unclean.€€ƒx•If any man in the field touch the corpse of a man that was slain, or that died of himself, or his bone, or his grave, he shall be unclean seven days.€€ƒx~And they shall take of the ashes of the burning and of the sin offering, and shall pour living waters upon them into a vessel.€€ƒx®And a man that is clean shall dip hyssop in them, and shall sprinkle therewith all the tent, and all the furniture, and the men that are defiled with touching any such thing:€€ƒxÔAnd in this manner he that is clean shall purify the unclean on the third and on the seventh day. And being expiated the seventh day, he shall wash both himself and his garments, and be unclean until the evening.€€ƒxËIf any man be not expiated after this rite, his soul shall perish out of the midst of the church: because he hath profaned the sanctuary of the Lord, and was not sprinkled with the water of purification.€€ƒx¿This precept shall be an ordinance for ever. He also that sprinkled the water, shall wash his garments. Every one that shall touch the waters of expiation, shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒxœWhatsoever a person toucheth who is unclean, he shall make it unclean: and the person that toucheth any of these things, shall be unclean until the evening.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¸And the children of Israel, and all the multitude came into the desert of Sin, in the first month: and the people abode in Cades. And Mary died there, and was buried in the same place.€€ƒxDAnd the people wanting water, came together against Moses and Aaron:€€ƒx_And making a sedition, they said: Would God we had perished among our brethren before the Lord.€€ƒxlWhy have you brought out the church of the Lord into the wilderness, that both we and our cattle should die?€€ƒxÉWhy have you made us come up out of Egypt, and have brought us into this wretched place which cannot be sowed, nor bringeth forth figs, nor vines, nor pomegranates, neither is there any water to drink?€€ƒyVAnd Moses and Aaron leaving the multitude, went into the tabernacle of the covenant, and fell flat upon the ground, and cried to the Lord, and said. O Lord God, hear the cry of this people, and open to them thy treasure, a fountain of living water, that being satisfied, they may cease to murmur. And the glory of the Lord appeared over them.€€˜ƒxñTake the rod, and assemble the people together, thou and Aaron thy brother, and speak to the rock before them, and it shall yield waters. And when thou hast brought forth water out of the rock, all the multitude and their cattle shall drink.€€ƒxQMoses therefore took the rod, which was before the Lord, as he had commanded him,€€ƒx And having gathered together the multitude before the rock, he said to them: Hear, ye rebellious and incredulous: Can we bring you forth water out of this rock?€€ƒx¤And when Moses bad lifted up his hand, and struck the rock twice with the rod, there came forth water in great abundance, so that the people and their cattle drank,€€ƒx½And the Lord said to Moses and Aaron: Because you have not believed me, to sanctify me before the children of Israel, you shall not bring these people into the land, which I will give them.€€ƒxƒThis is the Water of contradiction, where the children of Israel strove with words against the Lord, and he was sanctified in them.€€ƒx¡In the mean time Moses sent messengers from Cades to the king of Edom, to say: Thus saith thy brother Israel: Thou knowest all the labour that hath come upon us:€€ƒx€In what manner our fathers went down into Egypt, and there we dwelt a long time, and the Egyptians afflicted us and our fathers.€€ƒx²And how we cried to the Lord, and he heard us, and sent an angel, who hath brought us out of Egypt. Lo, we are now in the city of Cades, which is in the uttermost of thy borders,€€ƒy.And we beseech thee that we may have leave to pass through thy country. We will not go through the fields, nor through the vineyards, we will not drink the waters of thy wells, but we will go by the common highway, neither turning aside to the right hand, nor to the left, till we are past thy borders.€€ƒxcAnd Edom answered them: Thou shalt not pass by me: if thou dost I will come out armed against thee.€€ƒxÒAnd the children of Israel said: We will go by the beaten way: and if we and our cattle drink of thy waters, we will give thee what is just: there shall be no difficulty in the price, only let us pass speedily.€€ƒxBut he answered: Thou shalt not pass. And immediately he came forth to meet them with an infinite multitude, and a strong hand,€€ƒx„Neither would he condescend to their desire to grant them passage through his borders. Wherefore Israel turned another way from him.€€ƒxsAnd when they had removed the camp from Cades, they came to mount Hor, which is in the borders of the land of Edom:€€ƒxWhere the Lord spoke to Moses:€€ƒx»Let Aaron, saith he, go to his people: for he shall not go into the land which I have given the children of Israel, because he was incredulous to my words, at the waters of contradiction.€€ƒxBTake Aaron and his son with him, and bring them up into mount Hor:€€ƒx—And when thou hast stripped the father of his vesture, thou shalt vest therewith Eleazar his son: Aaron shall be gathered to his people, and die there.€€ƒx^Moses did as the Lord had commanded: and they went up into mount Hor before all the multitude.€€ƒxUAnd when he had stripped Aaron of his vestments, he vested Eleazar his son with them.€€ƒxKAnd Aaron being dead in the top of the mountain, he came down with Eleazar.€€ƒxlAnd all the multitude seeing that Aaron was dead, mourned for him thirty days throughout all their families.€€ƒ`€€ƒxËAnd when king Arad the Chanaanite, who dwelt towards the south, had heard this, to wit, that Israel was come by the way of the spies, he fought against them, and overcoming them carried off their spoils.€€ƒxˆBut Israel binding himself by vow to the Lord, said: If thou wilt deliver thus people into my hand, I will utterly destroy their cities.€€ƒxÄAnd the Lord heard the prayers of Israel, and delivered up the Chanaanite, and they cut them off and destroyed their cities: and they called the name of that place Horma, that is to say, Anathema.€€ƒx£And they marched from mount Hor, by the way that leadeth to the Red Sea, to compass the land of Edom. And the people began to be weary of their journey and labour:€€ƒxÅAnd speaking against God and Moses, they said: Why didst thou bring us out of Egypt, to die in the wilderness? There is no bread, nor have we any waters: our soul now loatheth this very light food.€€˜#ƒx¾Upon which they came to Moses, and said; We have sinned, because we have spoken against the Lord and thee: pray that he may take away these serpents from us. And Moses prayed for the people.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to him: Make a brazen serpent, and set it up for a sign: whosoever being struck shall look on it, shall live.€€ƒx€Moses therefore made a brazen serpent, and set it up for a sign: which when they that were bitten looked upon, they were healed.€€ƒx<And the children of Israel setting forwards camped in Oboth.€€ƒxoAnd departing thence they pitched their tents in Jeabarim, in the wilderness, that faceth Moab toward the east.€€ƒx9And removing from thence, they came to the torrent Zared:€€ƒxÄWhich they left and encamped over against Arnon, which is in the desert and standeth out on the borders of the Amorrhite. For Arnon is the border of Moab, dividing the Moabites and the Amorrhites.€€ƒxzWherefore it is said in the book of the wars of the Lord: As he did in the Red Sea, so will he do in the streams of Arnon.€€ƒxrThe rocks of the torrents were bowed down that they might rest in Ar, and lie down in the borders of the Moabites.€€ƒx‰When they went from that place, the well appeared whereof the Lord said to Moses: Gather the people together, and I will give them water.€€ƒxFThen Israel sung this song: Let the well spring up. They sung thereto:€€ƒx°The well, which the princes dug, and the chiefs of the people prepared by the direction of the lawgiver, and with their staves. And they marched from the wilderness to Mathana.€€ƒx6From Mathana unto Nahaliel: from Nahaliel unto Bamoth.€€ƒxgFrom Bamoth, is a valley in the country of Moab, to the top of Phasga, which looked towards the desert.€€ƒxCAnd Israel sent messengers to Sehon king of the Amorrhites, saying:€€ƒxØI beseech thee that I may have leave to pass through thy land: we will not go aside into the fields or the vineyards, we will not drink waters of the wells, we will go the king's highway, till we be past thy borders.€€ƒx­And he would not grant that Israel should pass by his borders: but rather gathering an army, went forth to meet them in the desert, and came to Jasa and fought against them.€€ƒxáAnd he was slain by them with the edge of the sword, and they possessed his land from the Arnon unto the Jeboc, and to the confines of the children of Ammon: for the borders of the Ammonites, were kept with a strong garrison.€€ƒxySo Israel took all his cities, and dwelt in the cities of the Amorrhite, to wit, in Hesebon, and in the villages thereof.€€ƒx©Hesebon was the city of Sehon the king of the Amorrhites, who fought against the king of Moab: and took all the land, that had been of his dominion, as far as the Arnon.€€ƒxbTherefore it is said in the proverb: Come into Hesebon, let the city of Sehon be built and set up:€€ƒx™A fire is gone out of Hesebon, a flame from the city of Sehon, and hath consumed Ar of the Moabites, and the inhabitants of the high places of the Arnon.€€ƒxWoe to thee Moab: thou art undone, O people of Chamos. He hath given his sons to flight, and his daughters into captivity to Sehon the king of the Amorrhites.€€ƒxZTheir yoke is perished from Hesebon unto Dibon, they came weary to Nophe, and unto Medaba.€€ƒx-So Israel dwelt in the land of the Amorrhite.€€ƒxmAnd Moses sent some to take a view of Jazer: and they took the villages of it, and conquered the inhabitants.€€ƒxAnd they turned themselves, and went up by the way of Basan, and Og the king of Basan came against them with all his people, to fight in Edrai.€€ƒxÜAnd the Lord said to Moses: Fear him not, for I have delivered him and all his people, and his country into thy hand: and thou shalt do to him as thou didst to Sehon the king of the Amorrhites, the inhabitant of Hesebon.€€ƒxqSo they slew him also with his sons, and all his people, not letting any one escape, and they possessed his land.€€ƒ`€€ƒxrAnd they went forward and encamped in the plains of Moab, over against where Jericho is situate beyond the Jordan.€€ƒxNAnd Balac the son of Sephor, seeing all that Israel had done to the Amorrhite,€€ƒxZAnd that the Moabites were in great fear of him, and were not able to sustain his assault,€€ƒx·He said to the elders of Madian: So will this people destroy all that dwell in our borders, as the ox is wont to eat the grass to the very roots. Now he was at that time king in Moab.€€ƒyHe sent therefore messengers to Balaam the son of Beor, a soothsayer, who dwelt by the river of the land of the children of Ammon, to call him, and to say: Behold a people is come out of Egypt, that hath covered the face of the earth, sitting over against me.€€˜)ƒx²And the ancients of Moab, and the elders of Madian, went with the price of divination in their hands. And where they were come to Balaam, and had told him all the words of Balac:€€ƒx–He answered: Tarry here this night and I will answer whatsoever the Lord shall say to me. And while they stayed with Balaam, God came and said to him:€€ƒx'What mean these men that are with thee?€€ƒxJHe answered: Balac the son of Sephor king of the Moabites hath sent to me,€€ƒx¥Saying: Behold a people that is come out of Egypt, hath covered the face of the land: come and curse them, if by any means I may fight with them and drive them away.€€ƒxlAnd God said to Balaam: Thou shalt not go with them, nor shalt thou curse the people: because it is blessed.€€ƒx{And he rose in the morning and said to the princes: Go into your country, because the Lord hath forbid me to come with you.€€ƒxDThe princes returning, said to Balac: Balaam would not come with us.€€ƒx>Then he sent many more and more noble than he had sent before:€€ƒxfWho, when they were come to Balaam, said: Thus saith Balac the son of Sephor, Delay not to come to me:€€ƒxcFor I am ready to honour thee, and will give thee whatsoever thou wilt: come and curse this people.€€ƒx”Balaam answered: If Balac would give me his house full of silver and gold, I cannot alter the word of the Lord my God, to speak either more or less.€€ƒx`I pray you to stay here this night also, that I may know what the Lord will answer me once more.€€ƒx¦God therefore came to Balaam in the night, and said to him: If these men be come to call thee, arise and go with them: yet so, that thou do what I shall command thee.€€ƒxABalaam arose in the morning, and saddling his ass went with them.€€ƒxAnd God was angry. And an angel of the Lord stood in the way against Balaam, who sat on the ass, and had two servants with him.€€ƒxÁThe ass seeing the angel standing in the way, with a drawn sword, turned herself out of the way, and went into the field. And when Balaam beat her, and had a mind to bring her again to the way,€€ƒx[The angel stood in a narrow place between two walls, wherewith the vineyards were enclosed.€€ƒxsAnd the ass seeing him, thrust herself close to the wall, and bruised the foot of the rider. But he beat her again:€€ƒx—And nevertheless the angel going on to a narrow place, where there was no way to turn aside either to the right hand or to the left, stood to meet him.€€ƒx‹And when the ass saw the angel standing, she fell under the feet of the rider: who being angry beat her sides more vehemently with a staff.€€ƒx€And the Lord opened the mouth of the ass, and she said: What have I done to thee? Why strikest thou me, lo, now this third time?€€ƒxuBalaam answered: Because thou hast deserved it, and hast served me ill: I would I had a sword that I might kill thee.€€ƒx­The ass said: Am not I thy beast, on which thou hast been always accustomed to ride until this present day? tell me if I ever did the like thing to thee. But he said: Never.€€ƒxœForthwith the Lord opened the eyes of Balaam, and he saw the angel standing in the way with a drawn sword, and he worshipped him falling flat on the ground.€€ƒx”And the angel said to him: Why beatest thou thy ass these three times? I am come to withstand thee, because thy way is perverse, and contrary to me:€€ƒx…And unless the ass had turned out of the way, giving place to me who stood against thee, I had slain thee, and she should have lived.€€ƒx€Balaam said: I have sinned, not knowing that thou didst stand against me: and now if it displease thee that I go, I will return.€€ƒxˆThe angel said: Go with these men, and see thou speak no other thing than what I shall command thee. He went therefore with the princes.€€ƒxAnd when Balac heard it he came forth to meet him in a town of the Moabites, that is situate in the uttermost borders of Arnon.€€ƒx”And he said to Balaam: I sent messengers to call thee, why didst thou not come immediately to me? was it because I am not able to reward thy coming?€€ƒxuHe answered him: Lo, here I am: shall I have power to speak any other thing but that which God shall put in my mouth?€€ƒxaSo they went on together, and came into a city, that was in the uttermost borders of his kingdom.€€ƒxlAnd when Balac had killed oxen and sheep, he sent presents to Balaam, and to the princes that were with him.€€ƒxuAnd when morning was come, he brought him to the high places of Baal, and he beheld the uttermost part of the people.€€ƒ`€€ƒxnAnd Balaam said to Balac: Build me here seven altars, and prepare as many calves, and the same number of rams.€€ƒxkAnd when he had done according to the word of Balaam, they laid together a calf and a ram upon every altar.€€ƒxªAnd Balaam said to Balac: Stand a while by thy burnt offering, until I go, to see if perhaps the Lord will meet me, and whatsoever he shall command, I will speak to thee.€€ƒx˜And when he was gone with speed, God met him. And Balaam speaking to him, said: I have erected seven altars, and have laid on everyone a calf and a ram.€€ƒx]And the Lord put the word in his mouth, and said: Return to Balac, and thus shalt thou speak.€€˜ƒx·And taking up his parable, he said: Balac king of the Moabites hath brought me from Aram, from the mountains of the east: Come, said he, and curse Jacob: make haste and detest Israel.€€ƒxpHow shall I curse him, whom God hath not cursed? By what means should I detest him, whom the Lord detesteth not?€€ƒxI shall see him from the tops of the rocks, and shall consider him from the hills. This people shall dwell alone, and shall not be reckoned among the nations.€€ƒx”Who can count the dust of Jacob, and know the number of the stock of Israel? Let my soul die the death of the just, and my last end be like to them.€€ƒx€And Balac said to Balaam: What is this that thou dost? I sent for thee to curse my enemies: and thou contrariwise blessest them.€€ƒxIHe answered him: Can I speak any thing else but what the Lord commandeth?€€ƒx“Balac therefore said: Come with me to another place from whence thou mayest see part of Israel, and canst not see them all: curse them from thence.€€ƒxAnd when he had brought him to a high place, upon the top of mount Phasga, Balaam built seven altars, and laying on every one a calf and a ram,€€ƒxJHe said to Balac: Stand here by thy burnt offering while I go to meet him.€€ƒx{And when the Lord had met him, and had put the word in his mouth, he said: Return to Balac, and thus shalt thou say to him.€€ƒx“Returning he found him standing by his burnt sacrifice, and the princes of the Moabites with him. And Balac said to him: What hath the Lord spoken?€€ƒx[But he taking up his parable, said: Stand, O Balac, and give ear: hear, thou son of Sephor:€€ƒx¦God is not a man, that he should lie, nor is the son of man, that he should be changed. Hath he said then, and will he not do? hath he spoken, and will he not fulfil?€€ƒx=I was brought to bless, the blessing I am not able to hinder.€€ƒxThere is no idol in Jacob, neither is there an image god to be seen in Israel. The Lord his God is with him, and the sound of the victory of the king in him.€€ƒxLGod hath brought him out of Egypt, whose strength is like to the rhinoceros.€€ƒx‰There is no soothsaying in Jacob, nor divination in Israel. In their times it shall be told to Jacob and to Israel what God hath wrought.€€ƒx¢Behold the people shall rise up as a lioness, and shall lift itself up as a lion: it shall not lie down till it devour the prey, and drink the blood of the slain.€€ƒx7And Balac said to Balaam: Neither curse, nor bless him.€€ƒxYAnd he said: Did I not tell thee, that whatsoever God should command me, that I would do?€€ƒxŠAnd Balac said to him: Come and I will bring thee to another place; if peradventure it please God that thou mayest curse them from thence.€€ƒx_And when he had brought him upon the top of mount Phogor, which looketh towards the wilderness,€€ƒxhBalaam said to him: Build me here seven altars, and prepare as many calves, and the same number of rams.€€ƒxKBalac did as Balaam had said: and he laid on every altar, a calf and a ram.€€ƒ`€€ƒx©And when Balaam saw that it pleased the Lord that he should bless Israel, he went not as he had gone before, to seek divination: but setting his face towards the desert,€€ƒxvAnd lifting up his eyes, he saw Israel abiding in their tents by their tribes: and the spirit of God rushing upon him,€€ƒxnHe took up his parable and said: Balaam the son of Beor hath said: The man hath said, whose eye is stopped up:€€ƒx†The bearer of the words of God hath said, he that hath beheld the vision of the Almighty, he that falleth, and so his eyes are opened:€€ƒxCHow beautiful are thy tabernacles O Jacob, and thy tents, O Israel!€€˜ƒx’Water shall flow out of his bucket, and his seed shall be in many waters. For Agag his king shall be removed, and his kingdom shall be taken away.€€ƒx´God hath brought him out of Egypt, whose strength is like to the rhinoceros. They shall devour the nations that are his enemies, and break their bones, and pierce them with arrows.€€ƒx»Lying down he hath slept as a lion, and as a lioness, whom none shall dare to rouse. He that blesseth thee, shall also himself be blessed: he that curseth thee shall be reckoned accursed.€€ƒx¥And Balac being angry against Balaam, clapped his hands together and said: I called thee to curse my enemies, and thou on the contrary hast blessed them three times.€€ƒx…Return to thy place. I had determined indeed greatly to honour thee, but the Lord hath deprived thee of the honour designed for thee.€€ƒxVBalaam made answer to Balac: Did I not say to thy messengers, whom thou sentest to me:€€ƒxÖIf Balac would give me his house full of silver and gold, I cannot go beyond the word of the Lord my God, to utter any thing of my own head either good or evil: but whatsoever the Lord shall say, that I will speak?€€ƒxqBut yet going to my people, I will give thee counsel, what this people shall do to thy people in the latter days.€€ƒx}Therefore taking up his parable, again he said: Balaam the son of Beor hath said: The man whose eye is stopped up, hath said:€€ƒx›The hearer of the words of God hath said, who knoweth the doctrine of the Highest, and seeth the visions of the Almighty, who falling hath his eyes opened:€€ƒx×I shall see him, but not now: I shall behold him, but not near. A STAR SHALL RISE out of Jacob and a sceptre shall spring up from Israel: and shall strike the chiefs of Moab, and shall waste all the children of Seth€€ƒxoAnd he shall possess Idumea: the inheritance of Seir shall come to their enemies, but Israel shall do manfully.€€ƒxVOut of Jacob shall he come that shall rule, and shall destroy the remains of the city.€€ƒx€And when he saw Amalec, he took up his parable, and said: Amalec the beginning of nations, whose latter ends shall be destroyed.€€ƒx…He saw also the Cinite: and took up his parable, and said: Thy habitation indeed is strong: but though thou build thy nest in a rock,€€ƒxsAnd thou be chosen of the stock of Cin, how long shalt thou be able to continue? For Assur shall take thee captive.€€ƒx^And taking up his parable, again he said: Alas, who shall live when God shall do these things?€€ƒx™They shall come in galleys from Italy, they shall overcome the Assyrians, and shall waste the Hebrews, and at the last they themselves also shall perish.€€ƒxUAnd Balaam rose, and returned to his place: Balac also returned the way that he came.€€ƒ`€€ƒxiAnd Israel at that time abode in Settim, and the people committed fornication with the daughters of Moab,€€ƒxQWho called them to their sacrifices. And they ate of them, and adored their gods.€€ƒxHAnd Israel was initiated to Beelphegor: upon which the Lord being angry,€€ƒxŒSaid to Moses: Take all the princes of the people, and hang them up on gibbets against the sun: that my fury may be turned away from Israel.€€ƒxrAnd Moses said to the judges of Israel: Let every man kill his neighbours, that have been initiated to Beelphegor.€€’ƒxˆAnd when Phinees the son of Eleazar the son of Aaron the priest saw it, he rose up from the midst of the multitude, and taking a dagger,€€ƒxÅWent in after the Israelite into the brothel house, and thrust both of them through together, to wit, the man and the woman in the genital parts. And the scourge ceased from the children of Israel.€€ƒx2And there were slain four and twenty thousand men.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to Moses:€€ƒxáPhinees the son of Eleazar the son of Aaron the priest, hath turned away my wrath from the children of Israel: because he was moved with my zeal against them, that I myself might not destroy the children of Israel in my zeal.€€ƒxATherefore say to him: behold I give him the peace of my covenant,€€ƒx¾And the covenant of the priesthood for ever shall be both to him and his seed, because he hath been zealous for his God, and hath made atonement for the wickedness of the children of Israel.€€ƒxAnd the name of the Israelite, that was slain with the woman of Madian, was Zambri the son of Salu, a prince of the kindred and tribe of Simeon.€€ƒxAnd the Madianite woman, that was slain with him, was called Cozbi the daughter of Sur, a most noble prince among the Madianites.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒx=Let the Madianites find you their enemies, and slay you them:€€ƒxéBecause they also have acted like enemies against you, and have guilefully deceived you by the idol Phogor, and Cozbi their sister, a daughter of a prince of Madian, who was slain in the day of the plague for the sacrilege of Phogor.€€ƒ`€€ƒxkAfter the blood of the guilty was shed, the Lord said to Moses and to Eleazar the son of Aaron, the priest:€€ƒx”Number the whole sum of the children of Israel from twenty years old and upward, by their houses and kindreds, all that are able to go forth to war.€€ƒxMoses therefore and Eleazar the priest, being in the plains of Moab upon the Jordan over against Jericho, spoke to them that were€€ƒx\From twenty years old and upward, as the Lord had commanded: and this is the number of them:€€ƒx’Ruben the firstborn of Israel. His sons were Henoch, of whom is the family of the Henochites: and Phallu, of whom is the family of the Phalluites:€€˜AƒxyThese are the families of the stock of Ruben: whose number was found to be forty-three thousand seven hundred and thirty.€€ƒxThe son of Phallu was Eliab.€€ƒxÃHis sons, were Namuel and Dathan and Abiron. These are Dathan and Abiron the princes of the people, that rose against Moses and Aaron in the sedition of Core, when they rebelled against the Lord:€€ƒxœAnd the earth opening her mouth swallowed up Core, many others dying, when the fire burned two hundred and fifty men. And there was a great miracle wrought,€€ƒx1That when Core perished, his sons did not perish.€€ƒx³The sons of Simeon by their kindreds: Namuel, of him is the family of the Namuelites: Jamin, of him is the family of the Jaminites: Jachim, of him is the family of the Jachimites:€€ƒxVZare, of him is the family of the Zarites: Saul, of him is the family of the Saulites.€€ƒxmThese are the families of the stock of Simeon, of which the whole number was twenty-two thousand two hundred.€€ƒx¨The sons of Gad by their kindreds: Sephon, of him is the family of the Sephonites: Aggi, of him is the family of the Aggites: Suni, of him is the family of the Sunites:€€ƒxTOzni, of him is the family of the Oznites: Her, of him is the family of the Herites:€€ƒxYArod, of him is the family of the Arodites: Ariel, of him is the family of the Arielites.€€ƒxYThese are the families of Gad, of which the whole number was forty thousand five hundred.€€ƒxEThe sons of Juda, Her and Onan, who both died in the land of Chanaan.€€ƒx¶And the sons of Juda by their kindreds were: Sela, of whom is the family of the Selaites: Phares, of whom is the family of the Pharesites: Zare, of whom is the family of the Zarites.€€ƒx…Moreover the sons of Phares were: Hesron, of whom is the family of the Hesronites: and Hamul, of whom is the family of the Hamulites.€€ƒx`These are the families of Juda, of which the whole number was seventy-six thousand five hundred.€€ƒxƒThe sons of Issachar, by their kindreds: Thola of whom is the family of the Tholaites: Phua, of whom is the family of the Phuaites:€€ƒx_Jasub, of whom is the family of the Jasubites: Semran, of whom is the family of the Semranites.€€ƒxWThese are the kindreds of Issachar, whose number was sixty-four thousand three hundred.€€ƒx±The sons of Zabulon by their kindreds: Sared, of whom is the family of the Saredites: Elon, of whom is the family of the Elonites: Jalel, of whom is the family of the Jalelites.€€ƒxPThese are the kindreds of Zabulon, whose number was sixty thousand five hundred.€€ƒx:The sons of Joseph by their kindred, Manasses and Ephraim.€€ƒxƒOf Manasses was born Machir, of whom is the family of the Machirites. Machir begot Galaad, of whom is the family of the Galaadites.€€ƒxrGalaad had sons: Jezer, of whom is the family of the Jezerites: and Helec, of whom is the family of the Helecites:€€ƒxiAnd Asriel, of whom is the family of the Asrielites: and Sechem, of whom is the family of the Sechemites:€€ƒxiAnd Semida, of whom is the family of the Semidaites: and Hepher, of whom is the family of the Hepherites.€€ƒx•And Hepher was the father of Salphaad, who had no sons, but only daughters, whose names are these: Maala, and Noa, and Hegla, and Melcha, and Thersa.€€ƒx\These are the families of Manasses, and the number of them fifty-two thousand seven hundred.€€ƒxÊAnd the sons of Ephraim by their kindreds were these: Suthala, of whom is the family of the Suthalaites: Becher, of whom is the family of the Becherites: Thehen, of whom is the family of the Thehenites.€€ƒxINow the son of Suthala was Heran, of whom is the family of the Heranites.€€ƒxaThese are the kindreds of the sons of Ephraim: whose number was thirty-two thousand five hundred.€€ƒxäThese are the sons of Joseph by their families. The sons of Benjamin in their kindreds: Bela, of whom is the family of the Belaites: Asbel, of whom is the family of the Asbelites: Ahiram, of whom is the family of the Ahiramites:€€ƒxaSupham, of whom is the family of the Suphamites: Hupham, of whom is the family of the Huphamites.€€ƒxwThe sons of Bela: Hered, and Noeman. Of Hered, is the family of the Heredites: of Noeman, the family of the Noemanites.€€ƒxcThese are the sons of Benjamin by their kindreds, whose number was forty-five thousand six hundred.€€ƒx‚The sons of Dan by their kindreds: Suham, of whom is the family of the Suhamites: These are the kindreds of Dan by their families.€€ƒxFAll were Suhamites, whose number was sixty-four thousand four hundred.€€ƒx¯The sons of Aser by their kindreds: Jemna, of whom is the family of the Jemnaites: Jessui, of whom is the family of the Jessuites: Brie, of whom is the family of the Brieites.€€ƒxyThe sons of Brie: Heber, of whom is the family of the Heberites: and Melchiel, of whom is the family of the Melchielites.€€ƒx/And the name of the daughter of Aser, was Sara.€€ƒx_These are the kindreds of the sons of Aser, and their number fifty-three thousand four hundred.€€ƒx„The sons of Nephtali by their kindreds: Jesiel, of whom is the family of the Jesielites: Guni, of whom is the family of the Gunites:€€ƒx_Jeser, of whom is the family of the Jeserites: Sellem, of whom is the family of the Sellemites.€€ƒxtThese are the kindreds of the sons of Nephtali by their families: whose number was forty-five thousand four hundred.€€ƒxxThis is the sum of the children of Israel, that were reckoned up, six hundred and one thousand seven hundred and thirty.€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxZTo these shall the land be divided for their possessions according to the number of names.€€ƒx£To the greater number thou shalt give a greater portion, and to the fewer a less: to every one, as they have now been reckoned up, shall a possession be delivered:€€ƒxAYet so that by lot the land be divided to the tribe and families.€€ƒxLWhatsoever shall fall by lot, that shall be taken by the more, or the fewer.€€ƒxÎThis also is the number of the sons of Levi by their families: Gerson, of whom is the family of the Gersonites: Caath, of whom is the family of the Caathites: Merari, of whom is the family of the Merarites.€€ƒxvThese are the families of Levi: The family of Lobni, the family of Hebroni, the family of Core. Now Caath begot Amram:€€ƒx˜Who had to wife Jochabed the daughter of Levi, who was born to him in Egypt. She bore to her husband Amram sons, Aaron and Moses, and Mary their sister.€€ƒx;Of Aaron were born Nadab and Abiu, and Eleazar and Ithamar:€€ƒxTOf whom Nadab and Abiu died, when they had offered the strange fire before the Lord.€€ƒxÍAnd all that were numbered, were twenty-three thousand males from one month old and upward: for they were not reckoned up among the children of Israel, neither was a possession given to them with the rest.€€ƒxThis is the number of the children of Israel, that were enrolled by Moses and Eleazar the priest, in the plains of Moab upon the Jordan, over against Jericho.€€ƒxiAmong whom there was not one of them that were numbered before by Moses and Aaron in the desert of Sinai.€€ƒx”For the Lord had foretold that they should die in the wilderness. And none remained of them, but Caleb the son of Jephone, and Josue the son of Nun.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÔThen came the daughters of Salphaad, the son of Hepher, the son of Galaad, the son of Machir, the son of Manasses, who was the son of Joseph: and their names are Maala, and Noa, and Hegla, and Melcha, and Thersa.€€ƒxAnd they stood before Moses and Eleazar the priest, and all the princes of the people at the door of the tabernacle of the covenant, and said:€€ƒyOur father died in the desert, and was not in the sedition, that was raised against the Lord under Core, but he died in his own sin: and he had no male children. Why is his name taken away out of his family, because he had no son? Give us a possession among the kinsmen of our father.€€ƒx;And Moses referred their cause to the judgment of the Lord.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to him:€€—ƒx<And to the children of Israel thou shalt speak these things:€€ƒxKWhen a man dieth without a son, his inheritance shall pass to his daughter.€€ƒx7If he have no daughter, his brethren shall succeed him.€€ƒxTAnd if he have no brethren, you shall give the inheritance to his father's brethren.€€ƒxÏBut if he have no uncles by the father, the inheritance shall be given to them that are the next akin. And this shall be to the children of Israel sacred by a perpetual law, as the Lord hath commanded Moses.€€ƒxˆThe Lord also said to Moses: Go up into this mountain Abarim, and view from thence the land which I will give to the children of Israel.€€ƒxaAnd when thou shalt have seen it, thou also shalt go to thy people, as thy brother Aaron is gone:€€ƒxÑBecause you offended me in the desert of Sin in the contradiction of the multitude, neither would you sanctify me before them at the waters. These are the waters of contradiction in Cades of the desert of Sin.€€ƒwAnd Moses answered him:€€ƒx`May the Lord the God of the spirits of all flesh provide a man, that may be over this multitude:€€ƒx‡And may go out and in before them, and may lead them out, or bring them in: lest the people of the Lord be as sheep without a shepherd.€€ƒxlAnd the Lord said to him: take Josue the son of Nun, a man in whom is the Spirit, and put thy hand upon him.€€ƒxCAnd he shall stand before Eleazar the priest and all the multitude:€€ƒxAnd thou shalt give him precepts in the sight of all, and part of thy glory, that all the congregation of the children of Israel may hear him.€€ƒx¼If any thing be to be done, Eleazar the priest shall consult the Lord for him. He and all the children of Israel with him, and the rest of the multitude shall go out and go in at his word.€€ƒxŒMoses did as the Lord had commanded. And, when he had taken Josue, he set him before Eleazar the priest, and all the assembly of the people,€€ƒxUAnd laying his hands on his head, he repeated all things that the Lord had commanded.€€ƒ`€€ƒxThe Lord also said to Moses:€€ƒxCommand the children of Israel, and thou shalt say to them: Offer ye my oblation and my bread, and burnt sacrifice of most sweet odour, in their due seasons.€€ƒx~These are the sacrifices which you shall offer: Two lambs of a year old without blemish every day for the perpetual holocaust:€€ƒxBOne you shall offer in the mornings, and the other in the evening:€€ƒxAnd the tenth part of an ephi of flour, which shall be tempered with the, purest oil, of the measure of the fourth part of a hin.€€˜ƒxpAnd for a libation you shall offer of wine the fourth part of a hin for every lamb in the sanctuary of the Lord.€€ƒxÀAnd you shall offer the other lamb in like manner in the evening according to all the rites of the morning sacrifice, and of the libations thereof, an oblation of most sweet odour to the Lord.€€ƒxšAnd on the sabbath day you shall offer two lambs of a year old without blemish, and two tenths of flour tempered with oil in sacrifice, and the libations,€€ƒxIWhich regularly are poured out every sabbath for the perpetual holocaust.€€ƒx›And on the first day of the month you shall offer a holocaust to the Lord, two calves of the herd, one ram, and seven lambs of a year old, without blemish,€€ƒxAnd three tenths of flour tempered with oil in sacrifice for every calf: and two tenths of flour tempered with oil for every ram:€€ƒx›And the tenth of a tenth of flour tempered with oil in sacrifice for every lamb. It is a holocaust of most sweet odour and an offering by fire to the Lord.€€ƒxÿAnd these shall be the libations of wine that are to be poured out for every victim: Half a hin for every calf, a third for a ram, and a fourth for a lamb. This shall be the holocaust for every month, as they succeed one another in the course of the year.€€ƒx{A buck goat also shall be offered to the Lord for a sin offering over and above the perpetual holocaust with its libations.€€ƒx[And in the first month, on the four tenth day of the month, shall be the phase of the Lord,€€ƒxVAnd on the fifteenth day the solemn feast: seven days shall they eat unleavened bread.€€ƒxaAnd the first day of them shall be venerable and holy: you shall not do any servile work therein.€€ƒx‹And you shall offer a burnt sacrifice a holocaust to the Lord, two calves of the herd, one ram, seven lambs of a year old, without blemish:€€ƒx…And for the sacrifice of every one three tenths of flour which shall be tempered with oil to every calf, and two tenths to every ram,€€ƒxPAnd the tenth of a tenth, to every lamb, that is to say, to all the seven lambs:€€ƒx5And one buck goat for sin, to make atonement for you,€€ƒx;Besides the morning holocaust which you shall always offer.€€ƒx²So shall you do every day of the seven days for the food of the fire, and for a most sweet odour to the Lord, which shall rise from the holocaust, and from the libations of each.€€ƒxbThe seventh day also shall be most solemn and holy unto you, you shall do no servile work therein.€€ƒx®The day also of firstfruits, when after the weeks are accomplished, you shall offer new fruits to the Lord, shall be venerable and holy: you shall do no servile work therein.€€ƒx”And you shall offer a holocaust for a most sweet odour to the Lord, two calves of the herd, one ram, and seven lambs of a year old, without blemish:€€ƒxfAnd in the sacrifices of them three tenths of flour tempered with oil to every calf, two to every ram,€€ƒxSThe tenth of a tenth to every lamb, which in all are seven lambs: a buck goat also,€€ƒxXWhich is slain for expiation: besides the perpetual holocaust and the libations thereof.€€ƒx>You shall offer them all without blemish with their libations.€€ƒ`€€ƒxªThe first day also of the seventh month shall be venerable and holy unto you; you shall do no servile work therein, because it is the day of the sounding and of trumpets.€€ƒx‘And you shall offer a holocaust for a most sweet odour to the Lord, one calf of the herd, one ram and seven lambs of a year old, without blemish.€€ƒxeAnd for their sacrifices, three tenths of flour tempered with oil to every calf, two tenths to a ram,€€ƒx2One tenth to a lamb, which in all are seven lambs:€€ƒxJAnd a buck goat for sin, which is offered for the expiation of the people,€€˜(ƒx–The tenth day also of this seventh month shall be holy and venerable unto you, and you shall afflict your souls; you shall do no servile work therein.€€ƒx’And you shall offer a holocaust to the Lord for a most sweet odour, one calf of the herd, one ram, and seven lambs of a year old, without blemish:€€ƒxeAnd for their sacrifices, three tenths of flour tempered with oil to every calf, two tenths to a ram,€€ƒxAThe tenth of a tenth to every lamb, which are in all seven lambs:€€ƒx£And a buck goat for sin, besides the things that are wont to be offered for sin, for expiation, and for the perpetual holocaust with their sacrifice and libations.€€ƒx°And on the fifteenth day of the seventh month, which shall be unto you holy and venerable, you shall do no servile work, but shall celebrate a solemnity to the Lord seven days.€€ƒxAnd you shall offer a holocaust for a most sweet odour to the Lord, thirteen calves of the herd, two rams, and fourteen lambs of a year old, without blemish:€€ƒx˜And for their libations three tenths of flour tempered with oil to every calf, being in all thirteen calves: and two tenths to each ram, being two rams,€€ƒxDAnd the tenth of a tenth to every lamb, being in all fourteen lambs:€€ƒxeAnd a buck goat for sin, besides the perpetual holocaust, and the sacrifice and the libation thereof.€€ƒxxOn the second day you shall offer twelve calves of the herd, two rams and fourteen lambs of a year old, without blemish:€€ƒxAnd the sacrifices and the libations for every one, for the calves and for the rams and for the lambs you shall duly celebrate:€€ƒxoAnd a buck goat for a sin offering besides the perpetual holocaust, and the sacrifice and the libation thereof.€€ƒxiThe third day you shall offer eleven calves, two rams, and fourteen lambs of a year old, without blemish:€€ƒxŠAnd the sacrifices and the libations of every one for the calves and for the rams and for the lambs you shall offer according to the rite:€€ƒxfAnd a buck goat for sin, besides the perpetual holocaust, and the sacrifice, and the libation thereof.€€ƒxgThe fourth day you shall offer ten calves, two rams, and fourteen lambs of a year old, without blemish:€€ƒxˆAnd the sacrifices and the libations of every one for the calves and for the rams and for the lambs you shall celebrate in right manner:€€ƒxeAnd a buck goat for sin, besides the perpetual holocaust, and the sacrifice and the libation thereof.€€ƒxgThe fifth day you shall offer nine calves, two rams, and fourteen lambs of a year old, without blemish:€€ƒxAnd the sacrifices and the libations of every one for the calves and for the rams and for the lambs you shall celebrate according to the rite:€€ƒxeAnd a buck goat for sin, besides the perpetual holocaust, and the sacrifice and the libation thereof.€€ƒxhThe sixth day you shall offer eight calves, two rams, and fourteen lambs of a year old, without blemish:€€ƒxAnd the sacrifices and the libations of every one for the calves and for the rams and for the lambs you shall celebrate according to the rite:€€ƒxeAnd a buck goat for sin, besides the perpetual holocaust, and the sacrifice and the libation thereof.€€ƒxmThe seventh day you shall offer seven calves and two rams, and fourteen lambs of a year old, without blemish:€€ƒxAnd the sacrifices and the libations of every one for the calves and for the rams and for the lambs you shall celebrate according to the rite:€€ƒxeAnd a buck goat for sin, besides the perpetual holocaust, and the sacrifice and the libation thereof.€€ƒxFOn the eighth day, which is most solemn, you shall do no servile work:€€ƒx†But you shall offer a holocaust for a most sweet odour to the Lord, one calf, one ram, and seven lambs of a year old, without blemish:€€ƒxAnd the sacrifices and the libations of every one for the calves and for the rams and for the lambs you shall celebrate according to the rite:€€ƒxeAnd a buck goat for sin, besides the perpetual holocaust, and the sacrifice and the libation thereof.€€ƒx·These things shall you offer to the Lord in your solemnities: besides your vows and voluntary oblations for holocaust, for sacrifice, for libation, and for victims of peace offerings.€€ƒ`€€ƒxJAnd Moses told the children of Israel all that the Lord had commanded him:€€ƒxrAnd he said to the princes of the tribes of the children of Israel: This is the word that the Lord hath commanded:€€ƒx…If any man make a vow to the Lord, or bind himself by an oath: he shall not make his word void but shall fulfil all that he promised.€€ƒyIf a woman vow any thing, and bind herself by an oath, being in her father's house, and but yet a girl in age: if her father knew the vow that she hath promised, and the oath wherewith she hath bound her soul, and held his peace, she shall be bound by the vow:€€ƒx<Whatsoever she promised and swore, she shall fulfil in deed.€€ƒxÄBut if her father, immediately as soon as he heard it, gainsaid it, both her vows and her oaths shall be void, neither shall she be bound to what she promised, because her father hath gainsaid it.€€ƒx‚The day that her husband shall hear it, and not gainsay it, she shall be bound to the vow, and shall give whatsoever she promised.€€ƒx™But if as soon as he heareth he gainsay it, and make her promises and the words wherewith she had bound her soul of no effect: the Lord will forgive her.€€ƒxFThe widow, and she that is divorced, shall fulfil whatsoever they vow.€€ƒxOIf the wife in the house of her husband, hath bound herself by vow and by oath,€€ƒx}If her husband hear, and hold his peace, and doth not disallow the promise, she shall accomplish whatsoever she had promised.€€ƒxBut if forthwith he gainsay it, she shall not be bound by the promise: because her husband gainsaid it, and the Lord will be merciful to her.€€ƒx¸If she vow and bind herself by oath, to afflict her soul by fasting, or abstinence from other things, it shall depend on the will of her husband, whether she shall do it, or not do it.€€ƒxĞBut if the husband hearing it hold his peace, and defer the declaring his mind till another day: whatsoever she had vowed and promised, she shall fulfil: because immediately as he heard it, he held his peace.€€ƒxGBut if he gainsay it after that he knew it, he shall bear her iniquity.€€ƒxÃThese are the laws which the Lord appointed to Moses between the husband and the wife, between the father and the daughter that is as yet but a girl in age, or that abideth in her father's house.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxdRevenge first the children of Israel on the Madianites, and so thou shalt be gathered to thy people.€€ƒxjAnd Moses forthwith said: Arm of you men to fight, who may take the revenge of the Lord on the Madianites.€€ƒxPLet a thousand men be chosen out of every tribe of Israel to be sent to the war.€€ƒxgAnd they gave a thousand of every tribe, that is to say, twelve thousand men well appointed for battle.€€˜6ƒx]And when they had fought against the Madianites and had overcome them, they slew all the men.€€ƒxAnd their kings Evi, and Recem, and Sur, and Hur, and Rebe, five princes of the nation: Balaam also the son of Beor they killed with the sword.€€ƒxŒAnd they took their women, and their children captives, and all their cattle, and all their goods: and all their possessions they plundered:€€ƒxCAnd all their cities, and their villages, and castles, they burned.€€ƒxWAnd they carried away the booty, and all that they had taken both of men and of beasts.€€ƒxæAnd they brought them to Moses, and Eleazar the priest, and to all the multitude of the children of Israel. But the rest of the things for use they carried to the camp on the plains of Moab, beside the Jordan over against Jericho.€€ƒxoAnd Moses and Eleazar the priest and all the princes of the synagogue went forth to meet them without the camp.€€ƒx{And Moses being angry with the chief officers of the army, the tribunes, and the centurions that were come from the battle,€€ƒx#Said: Why have you saved the women?€€ƒx¹Are not these they, that deceived the children of Israel by the counsel of Balaam, and made you transgress against the Lord by the sin of Phogor, for which also the people was punished?€€ƒx|Therefore kill all that are of the male sex, even of the children: and put to death the women, that have carnally known men.€€ƒxFBut the girls, and all the women that are virgins save for yourselves:€€ƒx”And stay without the camp seven days. He that hath killed a man, or touched one that is killed, shall be purified the third day and the seventh day.€€ƒx‰And of all the spoil, every garment, or vessel, or any thing made for use, of the skins, or hair of goats, or of wood, shall be purified.€€ƒxEleazar also the priest spoke to the men of the army, that had fought, in this manner: This is the ordinance of the law, which the Lord hath commanded Moses:€€ƒx9Gold, and silver, and brass, and iron, and lead, and tin,€€ƒx™And all that may pass through the fire, shall be purified by fire, but whatsoever cannot abide the fire, shall be sanctified with the water of expiation:€€ƒxoAnd you shall wash your garments the seventh day, and being purified, you shall afterwards enter into the camp.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to Moses:€€ƒxTake the sum of the things that were taken both of man and beast, thou and Eleazar the priest and the princes of the multitude:€€ƒxAnd thou shalt divide the spoil equally, between them that fought and went out to the war, and between the rest of the multitude.€€ƒx§And thou shalt separate a portion to the Lord from them that fought and were in the battle, one soul of five hundred as well of persons as of oxen and asses and sheep.€€ƒx[And thou shalt give it to Eleazar the priest, because they are the firstfruits of the Lord.€€ƒxôOut of the moiety also of the children of Israel thou shalt take the fiftieth head of persons, and of oxen, and asses, and sheep, and of all beasts, and thou shalt give them to the Levites that watch in the charge of the tabernacle of the Lord.€€ƒx4And Moses and Eleazar did as the Lord had commanded.€€ƒxTAnd the spoil which the army had taken, was six hundred seventy-five thousand sheep,€€ƒxSeventy-two thousand oxen,€€ƒxSixty-one thousand asses:€€ƒxJAnd thirty-two thousand persons of the female sex, that had not known men.€€ƒx{And one half was given to them that had been in the battle, to wit, three hundred thirty-seven thousand five hundred sheep:€€ƒxXOut of which, for the portion of the Lord, were reckoned six hundred seventy five sheep.€€ƒx:And out of the thirty-six thousand oxen, seventy-two oxen:€€ƒx?Out of the thirty thousand five hundred asses, sixty-one asses:€€ƒx]Out of the sixteen thousand persons, there fell to the portion of the Lord, thirty-two souls.€€ƒxoAnd Moses delivered the number of the firstfruits of the Lord to Eleazar the priest, as had been commanded him,€€ƒxgOut of the half of the children of Israel, which he had separated for them that had been in the battle.€€ƒxBut out of the half that fell to the rest of the multitude, that is to say, out of the three hundred thirty-seven thousand five hundred sheep,€€ƒx(And out of the thirty-six thousand oxen,€€ƒx2And out of the thirty thousand five hundred asses,€€ƒx(And out of the sixteen thousand persons,€€ƒxMoses took the fiftieth head, and gave it to the Levites that watched in the tabernacle of the Lord, as the Lord had commanded.€€ƒxcAnd when the commanders of the army, and the tribunes and centurions were come to Moses, they said:€€ƒx€We thy servants have reckoned up the number of the fighting men, whom we had under our hand, and not so much as one was wanting.€€ƒx¼Therefore we offer as gifts to the Lord what gold every one of us could find in the booty, in garters and tablets, rings and bracelets, and chains, that thou mayst pray to the Lord for us.€€ƒxGAnd Moses and Eleazar the priest received all the gold in divers kinds,€€ƒxeIn weight sixteen thousand seven hundred and fifty sicles, from the tribunes and from the centurions.€€ƒx<For that which every one had taken in the booty was his own.€€ƒxˆAnd that which was received they brought into the tabernacle of the testimony, for a memorial of the children of Israel before the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¬And the sons of Ruben and Gad had many flocks of cattle, and their substance in beasts was infinite. And when they saw the lands of Jazer and Galaad fit for feeding cattle,€€ƒxVThey came to Moses and Eleazar the priest, and the princes of the multitude, and said:€€ƒx]Ataroth, and Dibon, and Jazer, and Nemra, Hesebon, and Eleale, and Saban, and Nebo, and Beon,€€ƒx¬The land, which the Lord hath conquered in the sight of the children of Israel, is a very fertile soil for the feeding of beasts: and we thy servants have very much cattle:€€ƒx‘And we pray thee, if we have found favour in thy sight, that thou give it to us thy servants in possession, and make us not pass over the Jordan.€€˜*ƒx…Why do ye overturn the minds of the children of Israel, that they may not dare to pass into the place which the Lord hath given them?€€ƒxOWas it not thus your fathers did, when I sent from Cadesbarne to view the land?€€ƒx×And when they were come as far as the valley of the cluster, having viewed all the country, they overturned the hearts of the children of Israel, that they should not enter into the coasts, which the Lord gave them.€€ƒx"And he swore in his anger, saying:€€ƒx¼If these men, that came up out of Egypt, from twenty years old and upward, shall see the land, which I promised with an oath to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob: because they would not follow me,€€ƒxeExcept Caleb the son of Jephone the Cenezite, and Josue the son of Nun: these have fulfilled my will.€€ƒx¢And the Lord being angry against Israel, led them about through the desert forty years, until the whole generation, that had done evil in his sight, was consumed.€€ƒx˜And behold, said he, you are risen up instead of your fathers, the increase and offspring of sinful men, to augment the fury of the Lord against Israel.€€ƒxFor if you will not follow him, he will leave the people in the wilderness, and you shall be the cause of the destruction of all.€€ƒxsBut they coming near, said: We will make sheepfolds, and stalls for our cattle, and strong cities for our children:€€ƒxæAnd we ourselves will go armed and ready for battle before the children of Israel, until we bring them in unto their places. Our little ones, and all we have, shall be in walled cities, for fear of the ambushes of the inhabitants.€€ƒxZWe will not return into our houses until the children of Israel possess their inheritance:€€ƒxrNeither will we seek any thing beyond the Jordan, because we have already our possession on the east side thereof,€€ƒxaAnd Moses said to them: If you do what you promise, go on well appointed for war before the Lord:€€ƒxVAnd let every fighting man pass over the Jordan, until the Lord overthrow his enemies:€€ƒxªAnd all the land be brought under him, then shall you be blameless before the Lord and before Israel, and you shall obtain the countries that you desire, before the Lord.€€ƒxxBut if you do not what you say, no man can doubt but you sin against God: and know ye, that your sin shall overtake you.€€ƒx€Build therefore cities for your children, and folds and stalls for your sheep and beasts, and accomplish what you have promised.€€ƒxiAnd the children of Gad and Ruben said to Moses: We are thy servants, we will do what my lord commandeth.€€ƒxXWe will leave our children, and our wives and sheep and cattle, in the cities of Galaad:€€ƒx\And we thy servants all well appointed will march on to the war, as thou, my lord, speakest.€€ƒx–Moses therefore commanded Eleazar the priest, and Josue the son of Nun, and the princes of the families of all the tribes of Israel, and said to them:€€ƒx¸If the children of Gad, and the children of Ruben pass with you over the Jordan, all armed for war before the Lord, and the land be made subject to you: give them Galaad in possession.€€ƒxqBut if they will not pass armed with you into the land of Chanaan, let them receive places to dwell in among you.€€ƒxtAnd the children of Gad, and the children of Ruben answered: As the Lord hath spoken to his servants, so will we do:€€ƒx‰We will go armed before the Lord into the land of Chanaan, and we confess that we have already received our possession beyond the Jordan.€€ƒxóMoses therefore gave to the children of Gad and of Ruben, and to the half tribe of Manasses the son of Joseph, the kingdom of Sehon king of the Amorrhites, and the kingdom of Og king of Basan, and their land and the cities thereof round about.€€ƒx8And the sons of Gad built Dibon, and Ataroth, and Aroer,€€ƒx.And Etroth, and Sophan, and Jazer, and Jegbaa,€€ƒxGAnd Bethnemra, and Betharan, fenced cities, and folds for their cattle.€€ƒxDBut the children of Ruben built Hesebon, and Eleale, and Cariathaim,€€ƒxoAnd Nabo, and Baalmeon (their names being changed) and Sabama: giving names to the cities which they had built.€€ƒx‹Moreover the children of Machir, the son of Manasses, went into Galaad, and wasted it, cutting off the Amorrhites, the inhabitants thereof.€€ƒxTAnd Moses gave the land of Galaad to Machir the son of Manasses, and he dwelt in it.€€ƒx‡And Jair the son of Manasses went, and took the villages thereof, and he called them Havoth Jair, that is to say, the villages of Jair.€€ƒxbNobe also went, and took Canath with the villages thereof: and he called it by his own name, Nobe.€€ƒ`€€ƒx}These are the mansions of the children of Israel, who went out of Egypt by their troops under the conduct of Moses and Aaron,€€ƒxuWhich Moses wrote down according to the places of their encamping, which they changed by the commandment of the Lord.€€ƒx»Now the children of Israel departed from Ramesses the first month, on the fifteenth day of the first month, the day after the phase, with a mighty hand, in the sight of all the Egyptians,€€ƒxkWho were burying their firstborn, whom the Lord had slain (upon their gods also he had executed vengeance,)€€ƒxAnd they camped in Soccoth.€€˜8ƒx|Departing from thence they came over against Phihahiroth, which looketh towards Beelsephon, and they camped before Magdalum.€€ƒx­And departing from Phihahiroth, they passed through the midst of the sea into the wilderness: and having marched three days through the desert of Etham, they camped in Mara.€€ƒx‰And departing from Mara, they came into Elim, where there were twelve fountains of waters, and seventy palm trees: and there they camped.€€ƒxhBut departing from thence also, they pitched their tents by the Red Sea. And departing from the Red Sea,€€ƒx!They camped in the desert of Sin.€€ƒx1And they removed from thence, and came to Daphca.€€ƒx/And departing from Daphca, they camped in Alus.€€ƒxfAnd departing from Alus, they pitched their tents in Raphidim, where the people wanted water to drink.€€ƒx@And departing from Raphidim, they camped in the desert of Sinai.€€ƒxMBut departing also from the desert of Sinai, they came to the graves of lust.€€ƒx?And departing from the graves of lust, they camped in Haseroth.€€ƒx&And from Haseroth they came to Rethma.€€ƒx7And departing from Rethma, they camped in Remmomphares.€€ƒx0And they departed from thence and came to Lebna.€€ƒx)Removing from Lebna they camped in Ressa.€€ƒx0And departing from Ressa, they came to Ceelatha.€€ƒx?And they removed from thence and camped in the mountain Sepher.€€ƒx7Departing from the mountain Sepher, they came to Arada,€€ƒx-From thence they went and camped in Maceloth.€€ƒx2And departing from Maceloth, they came to Thahath.€€ƒx+Removing from Thahath they camped in Thare.€€ƒxAAnd they departed from thence, and pitched their tents in Methca.€€ƒx1And removing from Methca, they camped in Hesmona.€€ƒx2And departing from Hesmona, they came to Moseroth.€€ƒx6And removing from Moseroth, they camped in Benejaacan.€€ƒx9And departing from Benejaacan, they came to mount Gadgad.€€ƒx.From thence they went and camped in Jetebatha.€€ƒx(And from Jetebatha they came to Hebrona.€€ƒx6And departing from Hebrona, they camped in Asiongaber.€€ƒxIThey removed from thence and came into the desert of Sin, which is Cades.€€ƒxaAnd departing from Cades, they camped in mount Hor, in the uttermost borders of the land of Edom.€€ƒxÛAnd Aaron the priest went up into mount Hor at the commandment of the Lord: and there he died in the fortieth year of the coming forth of the children of Israel out of Egypt, the fifth month, the first day of the month,€€ƒx1When he was a hundred and twenty-three years old.€€ƒx~And king Arad the Chanaanite, who dwelt towards the south, heard that the children of Israel were come to the land of Chanaan.€€ƒx8And they departed from mount Hor, and camped in Salmona.€€ƒx,From whence they removed and came to Phunon.€€ƒx0And departing from Phunon, they camped in Oboth.€€ƒxOAnd from Oboth they came to Ijeabarim, which is in the borders of the Moabites.€€ƒxBAnd departing from Ijeabarim they pitched their tents in Dibongab.€€ƒx5From thence they went and camped in Helmondeblathaim.€€ƒx\And departing from Helmondeblathaim, they came to the mountains of Abarim over against Nabo.€€ƒxsAnd departing from the mountains of Abarim, they passed to the plains of Moab, by the Jordan, over against Jericho.€€ƒxVAnd there they camped from Bethsimoth even to Ablesatim in the plains of the Moabites,€€ƒxWhere the Lord said to Moses:€€ƒxCommand the children of Israel, and say to them: When you shall have passed over the Jordan, entering into the land of Chanaan,€€ƒx†Destroy all the inhabitants of that land: Beat down their pillars, and break in pieces their statues, and waste all their high places,€€ƒxQCleansing the land, and dwelling in it. For I have given it you for a possession.€€ƒxõAnd you shall divide it among you by lot. To the more you shall give a larger part, and to the fewer a lesser. To every one as the lot shall fall, so shall the inheritance be given. The possession shall be divided by the tribes and the families.€€ƒxÍBut if you will not kill the inhabitants of the land: they that remain, shall be unto you as nails in your eyes, and spears in your sides, and they shall be your adversaries in the land of your habitation.€€ƒx=And whatsoever I had thought to do to them, I will do to you.€€ƒ`€€ƒx$And the Lord spoke to Moses, saying:€€ƒxÃCommand the children of Israel, and thou shalt say to them: When you are entered into the land of Chanaan, and it shall be fallen into your possession by lot, it shall be bounded by these limits:€€ƒx‹The south side shall begin from the wilderness of Sin, which is by Edom: and shall have the most salt sea for its furthest limits eastward:€€ƒxòWhich limits shall go round on the south side by the ascent of the Scorpion and so into Senna, and reach toward the south as far as Cadesbarne, from whence the frontiers shall go out to the town called Adar, and shall reach as far as Asemona.€€ƒxwAnd the limits shall fetch a compass from Asemona to the torrent of Egypt, and shall end in the shore of the great sea.€€˜ƒxiBut toward the north side the borders shall begin from the great sea, reaching to the most high mountain,€€ƒxEFrom which they shall come to Emath, as far as the borders of Sedada:€€ƒxrAnd the limits shall go as far as Zephrona, and the village of Enan. These shall be the borders on the north side.€€ƒxhFrom thence they shall mark out the grounds towards the east side from the village of Enan unto Sephama.€€ƒx•And from Sephama the bounds shall go down to Rebla over against the fountain of Daphnis: from thence they shall come eastward to the sea of Cenereth,€€ƒx”And shall reach as far as the Jordan, and at the last shall be closed in by the most salt sea. This shall be your land with its borders round about.€€ƒxÃAnd Moses commanded the children of Israel, saying: This shall be the land which you shall possess by lot, and which the Lord hath commanded to be given to the nine tribes, and to the half tribe.€€ƒx¬For the tribe of the children of Ruben by their families, and the tribe of the children of Gad according to the number of their kindreds, and half of the tribe of Manasses,€€ƒxtThat is, two tribes and a half, have received their portion beyond the Jordan over against Jericho at the east side.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to Moses:€€ƒxrThese are the names of the men, that shall divide the land unto you: Eleazar the priest, and Josue the son of Nun,€€ƒxAnd one prince of every tribe,€€ƒxFWhose names are these: Of the tribe of Juda, Caleb the son of Jephone.€€ƒx1Of the tribe of Simeon, Samuel the son of Ammiud.€€ƒx5Of the tribe of Benjamin, Elidad the son of Chaselon.€€ƒx<Of the tribe of the children of Dan, Bocci the son of Jogli.€€ƒxMOf the children of Joseph of the tribe of Manasses, Hanniel the son of Ephod.€€ƒx3Of the tribe of Ephraim, Camuel the son of Sephtan.€€ƒx7Of the tribe of Zabulon, Elisaphan the son of Pharnach.€€ƒx?Of the tribe of Issachar, Phaltiel the prince, the son of Ozan.€€ƒx.Of the tribe of Aser, Ahiud the son of Salomi.€€ƒx4Of the tribe of Nephtali: Phedael the son of Ammiud.€€ƒxdThese are they Whom the Lord hath commanded to divide the land of Chanaan to the children of Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxhAnd the Lord spoke these things also to Moses in the plains of Moab by the Jordan, over against Jericho:€€ƒxVCommand the children of Israel that they give to the Levites out of their possessions,€€ƒx‡Cities to dwell in, and their suburbs round about: that they may abide in the towns, and the suburbs may be for them cattle and beasts:€€ƒx_Which suburbs shall reach from the walls of the cities outward, a thousand paces on every side:€€ƒy2Toward the east shall be two thousand cubits: and toward the south in like manner shall be two thousand cubits: toward the sea also, which looketh to the west, shall be the same extent: and the north side shall be bounded with the like limits. And the cities shall be in the midst, and the suburbs without.€€˜"ƒx/That is, in all forty-eight with their suburbs.€€ƒyAnd of these cities which shall be given out of the possessions of the children of Israel, from them that have more, more shall be taken: and from them that have less, fewer. Each shall give towns to the Levites according to the extent of their inheritance.€€ƒwThe Lord said to Moses:€€ƒxSpeak to the children of Israel, and thou shalt say to them: When you shall have passed over the Jordan into the land of Chanaan,€€ƒxcDetermine what cities shall be for the refuge of fugitives, who have shed blood against their will.€€ƒx¦And when the fugitive shall be in them, the kinsman of him that is slain may not have power to kill him, until he stand before the multitude, and his cause be judged.€€ƒxDAnd of those cities, that are separated for the refuge of fugitives,€€ƒxCThree shall be beyond the Jordan, and three in the land of Chanaan,€€ƒxƒAs well for the children of Israel as for strangers and sojourners, that he may flee to them, who hath shed blood against his will.€€ƒxpIf any man strike with iron, and he die that was struck: he shall be guilty of murder, and he himself shall die.€€ƒxXIf he throw a stone, and he that is struck die: he shall be punished in the same manner.€€ƒx]If he that is struck with wood die: he shall be revenged by the blood of him that struck him.€€ƒxnThe kinsman of him that was slain, shall kill the murderer: as soon as he apprehendeth him, he shall kill him.€€ƒxPIf through hatred any one push a man, or fling any thing at him with ill design:€€ƒx­Or being his enemy, strike him with his hand, and he die: the striker shall be guilty of murder: the kinsman of him that was slain as soon as he findeth him, shall kill him.€€ƒx,But if by chance medley, and without hatred,€€ƒx&And enmity, he do any of these things,€€ƒxwAnd this be proved in the hearing of the people, and the cause be debated between him that struck, and the next of kin:€€ƒxêThe innocent shall be delivered from the hand of the revenger, and shall be brought back by sentence into the city, to which he had fled, and he shall abide there until the death of the high priest, that is anointed with the holy oil.€€ƒx^If the murderer be found without the limits of the cities that are appointed for the banished,€€ƒxZAnd be struck by him that is the avenger of blood: he shall not be guilty that killed him.€€ƒxŸFor the fugitive ought to have stayed in the city until the death of the high priest: and after he is dead, then shall the manslayer return to his own country.€€ƒxLThese things shall be perpetual, and for an ordinance in all your dwellings.€€ƒxbThe murderer shall be punished by witnesses: none shall be condemned upon the evidence of one man.€€ƒxTYou shall not take money of him that is guilty of blood, but he shall die forthwith.€€ƒxlThe banished and fugitives before the death of the high priest may by no means return into their own cities.€€ƒx´Defile not the land of your habitation, which is stained with the blood of the innocent: neither can it otherwise be expiated, but by his blood that hath shed the blood of another.€€ƒxAnd thus shall your possession be cleansed, myself abiding with you. For I am the Lord that dwell among the children of Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxºAnd the princes of the families of Galaad, the son of Machir, the son of Manasses, of the stock of the children of Joseph, came and spoke to Moses before the princes of Israel, and said:€€ƒxÔThe Lord hath commanded thee, my lord, that thou shouldst divide the land by lot to the children of Israel, and that thou shouldst give to the daughters of Salphaad our brother the possession due to their father:€€ƒx¤Now if men of another tribe take them to wives, their possession will follow them, and being transferred to another tribe, will be a diminishing of our inheritance.€€ƒxÔAnd so it shall come to pass, that when the jubilee, the is, the fiftieth year of remission, is come, the distribution made by the lots shall be confounded, and the possession of the one shall pass to the others.€€ƒx„Moses answered the children of Israel, and said by the command of the Lord: The tribe of the children of Joseph hath spoken rightly.€€ƒx‹Lest the possession of the children of Israel be mingled from tribe to tribe. For all men shall marry wives of their own tribe and kindred:€€ƒxeAnd all women shall take husbands of the same tribe: that the inheritance may remain in the families.€€ƒxBAnd that the tribes be not mingled one with another, but remain so€€ƒxWAs they were separated by the Lord. And the daughters of Salphaad did as was commanded:€€ƒxmAnd Maala, and Thersa, and Hegla, and Melcha, and Noa were married to the sons of their uncle by their father€€ƒxšOf the family of Manasses, who was the son of Joseph: and the possession that had been allotted to them, remained in the tribe and family of their father.€€ƒx­These are the commandments and judgment, which the Lord commanded by the hand of Moses to the children of Israel, in the plains of Moab upon the Jordan over against Jericho.€€ƒwThe Book of Deuteronomy€€ƒ`€€ƒxÌThese are the words, which Moses spoke to all Israel beyond the Jordan, in the plain wilderness, over against the Red Sea, between Pharan and Thophel and Laban and Haseroth, where there is very much gold.€€ƒxGEleven days' journey from Horeb by the way of mount Seir to Cadesbarne.€€ƒxŸIn the fortieth year, the eleventh month, the first day of the month, Moses spoke to the children of Israel all that the Lord had commanded him to say to them:€€ƒx…After that he had slain Sehon king of the Amorrhites, who dwelt in Hesebon: and Og king of Basan who abode in Astaroth, and in Edrai,€€dDeut¤dnamekDeuteronomyiosis_namedDeutlabbreviationdDeuthchapters˜"˜.ƒyTurn you, and come to the mountain of the Amorrhites, and to the other places that are next to it, the plains and the hills and the vales towards the south, and by the sea shore, the land of the Chanaanites, and of Libanus, as far as the great river Euphrates.€€ƒxÊBehold, said he, I have delivered it to you: go in and possess it, concerning which the Lord swore to your fathers Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, that he would give it to them, and to their seed after them.€€ƒxAnd I said to you at that time:€€ƒx‡I alone am not able to bear you: for the Lord your God hath multiplied you, and you are this day as the stars of heaven, for multitude.€€ƒxb(The Lord God of your fathers add to this number many thousands, and bless you as he hath spoken.)€€ƒxVI alone am not able to bear your business, and the charge of you and your differences.€€ƒx–Let me have from among you wise and understanding men, and such whose conversation is approved among your tribes, that I may appoint them your rulers.€€ƒxAThen you answered me: The thing is good which thou meanest to do.€€ƒxµAnd I took out of your tribes men wise and honourable, and appointed them rulers, tribunes, and centurions, and officers over fifties, and over tens, who might teach you all things.€€ƒxxAnd I commanded them, saying: Hear them, and judge that which is just: whether he be one of your country, or a stranger.€€ƒxîThere shall be no difference of persons, you shall hear the little as well as the great: neither shall you respect any man's person, because it is the judgment of God. And if any thing seem hard to you, refer it to me, and I will hear it.€€ƒx3And I commanded you all things that you were to do.€€ƒxÖAnd departing from Horeb, we passed through the terrible and vast wilderness, which you saw, by the way of the mountain of the Amorrhite, as the Lord our God had commanded us. And when we were come into Cadesbarne,€€ƒxeI said to you: You are come to the mountain of the Amorrhite, which the Lord our God will give to us.€€ƒxœSee the land which the Lord thy God giveth thee: go up and possess it, as the Lord our God hath spoken to thy fathers: fear not, nor be any way discouraged.€€ƒx“And you came all to me, and said: Let us send men who may view the land, and bring us word what way we shall go up, and to what cities we shall go.€€ƒxPAnd because the saying pleased me, I sent of you twelve men, one of every tribe:€€ƒx†Who, when they had set forward and had gone up to the mountains, came as far as the valley of the cluster: and having viewed the land,€€ƒxTaking of the fruits thereof, to shew its fertility, they brought them to us, and said: The land is good, which the Lord our God will give us.€€ƒxOAnd you would not go up, but being incredulous to the word of the Lord our God,€€ƒx½You murmured in your tents, and said: The Lord hateth us, and therefore he hath brought us out of the land of Egypt, that he might deliver us into the hand of the Amorrhite, and destroy us.€€ƒxÖWhither shall we go up? the messengers have terrified our hearts, saying: The multitude is very great, and taller than we: the cities are great, and walled up to the sky, we have seen the sons of the Enacims there.€€ƒx:And I said to you: Fear not, neither be ye afraid of them:€€ƒxeThe Lord God, who is your leader, himself will fight for you, as he did in Egypt in the sight of all.€€ƒxµAnd in the wilderness (as thou hast seen) the Lord thy God hath carried thee, as a man is wont to carry his little son, all the way that you have come, until you came to this place.€€ƒx;And yet for all this you did not believe the Lord your God,€€ƒx±Who went before you in the way, and marked out the place, wherein you should pitch your tents, in the night shewing you the way by fire, and in the day by the pillar of a cloud.€€ƒxVAnd when the Lord had heard the voice of your words, he was angry and swore, and said:€€ƒxtNot one of the men of this wicked generation shall see the good land, which I promised with an oath to your fathers:€€ƒx¨Except Caleb the son of Jephone: for he shall see it, and to him I will give the land that he hath trodden upon, and to his children, because he hath followed the Lord.€€ƒx£Neither is his indignation against the people to be wondered at, since the Lord was angry with me also on your account, and said: Neither shalt thou go in thither.€€ƒx‰But Josue the son of Nun, thy minister, he shall go in for thee: exhort and encourage him, and he shall divide the land by lot to Israel.€€ƒxÚYour children, of whom you said that they should be led away captives, and your sons who know not this day the difference of good and evil, they shall go in: and to them I will give the land, and they shall possess it.€€ƒxDBut return you and go into the wilderness by the way of the Red Sea.€€ƒx£And you answered me: We have sinned against the Lord: we will go up and fight, as the Lord our God hath commanded. And when you went ready armed unto the mountain,€€ƒxuThe Lord said to me: Say to them: Go not up, and fight not, for I am not with you: lest you fall before your enemies.€€ƒx‚I spoke, and you hearkened not: but resisting the commandment of the Lord, and swelling with pride, you went up into the mountain.€€ƒx—And the Amorrhite that dwelt in the mountains coming out, and meeting you, chased you, as bees do: and made slaughter of you from Seir as far as Horma.€€ƒxgAnd when you returned and wept before the Lord, he heard you not, neither would he yield to your voice.€€ƒx'So you abode in Cadesbarne a long time.€€ƒ`€€ƒx™And departing from thence we came into the wilderness that leadeth to the Red Sea, as the Lord had spoken to me: and we compassed mount Seir a long time.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to me:€€ƒxBYou have compassed this mountain long enough: go toward the north:€€ƒxœAnd command thou the people, saying: You shall pass by the borders of your brethren the children of Esau, who dwell in Seir, and they will be afraid of you.€€ƒxÇTake ye then good heed that you stir not against them. For I will not give you of their land so much as the step of one foot can tread upon, because I have given mount Seir to Esau, for a possession.€€˜%ƒxÛThe Lord thy God hath blessed thee in every work of thy hands: the Lord thy God dwelling with thee, knoweth thy journey, how thou hast passed through this great wilderness, for forty years, and thou hast wanted nothing.€€ƒx¿And when we had passed by our brethren the children of Esau, that dwelt in Seir, by the way of the plain from Elath and from Asiongaber, we came to the way that leadeth to the desert of Moab.€€ƒxÅAnd the Lord said to me: Fight not against the Moabites, neither go to battle against them: for I will not give thee any of their land, because I have given Ar to the children of Lot in possession.€€ƒxyThe Emims first were the inhabitants thereof, a people great, and strong, and so tall, that like the race of the Enacims,€€ƒxfThey were esteemed as giants, and were like the sons of the Enacims. But the Moabites call them Emims.€€ƒx¶The Horrhites also formerly dwelt in Seir: who being driven out and destroyed, the children of Esau dwelt there, as Israel did in the land of his possession, which the Lord gave him.€€ƒx8Then rising up to pass the torrent Zared, we came to it.€€ƒxÜAnd the time that we journeyed from Cadesbarne till we passed over the torrent Zared, was thirty-eight years: until all the generation of the men that were fit for war was consumed out of the camp, as the Lord had sworn:€€ƒxRFor his hand was against them, that they should perish from the midst of the camp.€€ƒx)And after all the fighting men were dead,€€ƒxThe Lord spoke to me, saying:€€ƒx@Thou shalt pass this day the borders of Moab, the city named Ar:€€ƒyAnd when thou comest nigh the frontiers of the children of Ammon, take heed thou fight not against them, nor once move to battle: for I will not give thee of the land of the children of Ammon, because I have given it to the children of Lot for a possession.€€ƒxgIt was accounted a land of giants: and giants formerly dwelt in it, whom the Ammonites call Zomzommims,€€ƒx“A people great and many, and of tall stature, like the Enacims whom the Lord destroyed before their face: and he made them to dwell in their stead,€€ƒx¢As he had done in favour of the children of Esau, that dwell in Seir, destroying the Horrhites, and delivering their land to them, which they possess to this day.€€ƒx£The Hevites also, that dwelt in Haserim as far as Gaza, were expelled by the Cappadocians: who came out of Cappadocia, and destroyed them and dwelt in their stead.€€ƒx­Arise ye, and pass the torrent Arnon: Behold I have delivered into thy hand Sehon king of Hesebon the Amorrhite, and begin thou to possess his land and make war against him.€€ƒxÊThis day will I begin to send the dread and fear of thee upon the nations that dwell under the whole heaven: that when they hear thy name they may fear and tremble, and be in pain like women in travail.€€ƒxoSo I sent messengers from the wilderness of Cademoth to Sehon the king of Hesebon with peaceable words, saying:€€ƒxWe will pass through thy land, we will go along by the highway: we will not turn aside neither to the right hand nor to the left.€€ƒx†Sell us meat for money, that we may eat: give us water for money and so we will drink. We only ask that thou wilt let us pass through,€€ƒx±As the children of Esau have done, that dwell in Seir, and the Moabites, that abide in Ar: until we come to the Jordan, and pass to the land which the Lord our God will give us.€€ƒx¹And Sehon the king of Hesebon would not let us pass: because the Lord thy God had hardened his spirit, and fixed his heart, that he might be delivered into thy hands, as now thou seest.€€ƒxjAnd the Lord said to me: Behold I have begun to deliver unto thee Sehon and his land, begin to possess it.€€ƒxCAnd Sehon came out to meet us with all his people to fight at Jasa.€€ƒx[And the Lord our God delivered him to us: and we slew him with his sons and all his people.€€ƒx~And we took all his cities at that time, killing the inhabitants of them, men and women and children. We left nothing of them:€€ƒxnExcept the cattle which came to the share of them that took them: and the spoils of the cities, which we took:€€ƒxÏFrom Aroer, which is upon the bank of the torrent Arnon, a town that is situate in a valley, as far as Galaad. There was not a village or city, that escaped our hands: the Lord our God delivered all unto us:€€ƒxÈExcept the land of the children of Ammon, to which we approached not: and all that border upon the torrent Jeboc, and the cities in the mountains, and all the places which the Lord our God forbade us.€€ƒ`€€ƒx|Then we turned and went by the way of Basan: and Og the king of Basan came out to meet us with his people to fight in Edrai.€€ƒxÒAnd the Lord said to me: Fear him not: because he is delivered into thy hand, with all his people and his land: and thou shalt do to him as thou hast done to Sehon king of the Amorrhites, that dwelt in Hesebon.€€ƒx|So the Lord our God delivered into our hands, Og also, the king of Basan, and all his people: and we utterly destroyed them,€€ƒxŒWasting all his cities at one time, there was not a town that escaped us: sixty cities, all the country of Argob the kingdom of Og in Basan.€€ƒxvAll the cities were fenced with very high walls, and with gates and bars, besides innumerable towns that had no walls.€€˜ƒxABut the cattle and the spoils of the cities we took for our prey.€€ƒx And we took at that time the land out of the hand of the two kings of the Amorrhites, that were beyond the Jordan: from the torrent Arnon unto the mount Hermon,€€ƒx:Which the Sidonians call Sarion, and the Amorrhites Sanir:€€ƒx”All the cities that are situate in the plain, and all the land of Galaad and Basan as far as Selcha and Edrai, cities of the kingdom of Og in Basan.€€ƒxÜFor only Og king of Basan remained of the race of the giants. His bed of iron is shewn, which is in Rabbath of the children of Ammon, being nine cubits long, and four broad after the measure of the cubit of a man's hand.€€ƒx®And we possessed the land at that time from Aroer, which is upon the bank of the torrent Arnon, unto the half of mount Galaad: and I gave the cities thereof to Ruben and Gad.€€ƒx®And I delivered the other part of Galaad, and all Basan the kingdom of Og to the half tribe of Manasses, all the country of Argob: and all Basan is called the Land of giants.€€ƒxÒJair the son of Manasses possessed all the country of Argob unto the borders of Gessuri, and Machati. And he called Basan by his own name, Havoth Jair, that is to say, the towns of Jair, until this present day.€€ƒxTo Machir also I gave Galaad.€€ƒxÊAnd to the tribes of Ruben and Gad I gave of the land of Galaad as far as the torrent Arnon, half the torrent, and the confines even unto the torrent Jeboc, which is the border of the children of Ammon:€€ƒxªAnd the plain of the wilderness, and the Jordan, and the borders of Cenereth unto the sea of the desert, which is the most salt sea, to the foot of mount Phasga eastward.€€ƒxÁAnd I commanded you at that time, saying: The Lord your God giveth you this land for an inheritance, go ye well appointed before your brethren the children of Israel, all the strong men of you.€€ƒxLeaving your wives and children and cattle. For I know you have much cattle, and they must remain in the cities, which I have delivered to you.€€ƒxÕUntil the Lord give rest to your brethren, as he hath given to you: and they also possess the land, which he will give them beyond the Jordan: then shall every man return to his possession, which I have given you.€€ƒx°I commanded Josue also at that time, saying: Thy eyes have seen what the Lord your God hath done to these two kings: so will he do to all the kingdoms to which thou shalt pass.€€ƒx8Fear them not: for the Lord your God will fight for you.€€ƒx-And I besought the Lord at that time, saying:€€ƒxÍLord God, thou hast begun to shew unto thy servant thy greatness, and most mighty hand, for there is no other God either in heaven or earth, that is able to do thy works, or to be compared to thy strength.€€ƒxvI will pass over therefore, and will see this excellent land beyond the Jordan, and this goodly mountain, and Libanus.€€ƒx‚And the Lord was angry with me on your account and heard me not, but said to me: It is enough: speak no more to me of this matter.€€ƒx¯Go up to the top of Phasga, and cast thy eyes round about to the west, and to the north, and to the south, and to the east, and behold it, for thou shalt not pass this Jordan.€€ƒxCommand Josue, and encourage and strengthen him: for he shall go before this people, and shall divide unto them the land which thou shalt see.€€ƒx=And we abode in the valley over against the temple of Phogor.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÉAnd now, O Israel, hear the commandments and judgments which I teach thee: that doing them, thou mayst live, and entering in mayst possess the land which the Lord the God of your fathers will give you.€€ƒx—You shall not add to the word that I speak to you, neither shall you take away from it: keep the commandments of the Lord your God which I command you.€€ƒx}Your eyes have seen all that the Lord hath done against Beelphegor, how he hath destroyed all his worshippers from among you.€€ƒxOBut you that adhere to the Lord your God, are all alive until this present day.€€ƒx–You know that I have taught you statutes and justices, as the Lord my God hath commanded me: so shall you do them in the land which you shall possess:€€˜1ƒxtNeither is there any other nation so great, that hath gods so nigh them, as our God is present to all our petitions.€€ƒx–For what other nation is there so renowned that hath ceremonies, and just judgments, and all the law, which I will set forth this day before our eyes?€€ƒxÔKeep thyself therefore, and thy soul carefully. Forget not the words that thy eyes have seen, and let them not go out of thy heart all the days of thy life. Thou shalt teach them to thy sons and to thy grandsons,€€ƒyFrom the day in which thou didst stand before the Lord thy God in Horeb, when the Lord spoke to me, saying: Call together the people unto me, that they may hear my words, and may learn to fear me all the time that they live on the earth, and may teach their children.€€ƒx~And you came to the foot of the mount, which burned even unto heaven: and there was darkness, and a cloud and obscurity in it.€€ƒxxAnd the Lord spoke to you from the midst of the fire. You heard the voice of his words, but you saw not any form at all.€€ƒxuAnd he shewed you his covenant, which he commanded you to do, and the ten words that he wrote in two tables of stone.€€ƒxAnd he commanded me at that time that I should teach you the ceremonies and judgments which you shall do in the land, that you shall possess.€€ƒxKeep therefore your souls carefully. You saw not any similitude in the day that the Lord God spoke to you in Horeb from the midst of the fire:€€ƒx_Lest perhaps being deceived you might make you a graven similitude, or image of male or female,€€ƒxZThe similitude of any beasts, that are upon the earth, or of birds, that fly under heaven,€€ƒxfOr of creeping things, that move on the earth, or of fishes, that abide in the waters under the earth:€€ƒxøLest perhaps lifting up thy eyes to heaven, thou see the sun and the moon, and all the stars of heaven, and being deceived by error thou adore and serve them, which the Lord thy God created for the service of all the nations, that are under heaven.€€ƒxBut the Lord hath taken you and brought you out of the iron furnaces of Egypt, to make you his people of inheritance, as it is this present day.€€ƒxAnd the Lord was angry with me for your words, and he swore that I should not pass over the Jordan, nor enter into the excellent land, which he will give you.€€ƒxiBehold I die in this land, I shall not pass over the Jordan: you shall pass, and possess the goodly land.€€ƒx¹Beware lest thou ever forget the covenant of the Lord thy God, which he hath made with thee: and make to thyself a graven likeness of those things which the Lord hath forbid to be made:€€ƒx<Because the Lord thy God is a consuming fire, a jealous God.€€ƒx·If you shall beget sons and grandsons, and abide in the land, and being deceived, make to yourselves any similitude, committing evil before the Lord your God, to provoke him to wrath:€€ƒxÜI call this day heaven and earth to witness, that you shall quickly perish out of the land, which, when you have passed over the Jordan, you shall possess. You shall not dwell therein long, but the Lord will destroy you,€€ƒxrAnd scatter you among all nations, and you shall remain a few among the nations, to which the Lord shall lead you.€€ƒx‚And there you shall serve gods, that were framed with men's hands: wood and stone, that neither see, nor hear, nor eat, nor smell.€€ƒx–And when thou shalt seek there the Lord thy God, thou shalt find him: yet so, if thou seek him with all thy heart, and all the affliction of thy soul.€€ƒxƒAfter all the things aforesaid shall find thee, in the latter time thou shalt return to the Lord thy God, and shalt hear his voice.€€ƒx›Because the Lord thy God is a merciful God: he will not leave thee, nor altogether destroy thee, nor forget the covenant, by which he swore to thy fathers.€€ƒxãAsk of the days of old, that have been before thy time from the day that God created man upon the earth, from one end of heaven to the other end thereof, if ever there was done the like thing, or it hath been known at any time,€€ƒxlThat a people should hear the voice of God speaking out of the midst of fire, as thou hast heard, and lived:€€ƒyIf God ever did so as to go, and take to himself a nation out of the midst of nations by temptations, signs, and wonders, by fight, and a strong hand, and stretched out arm, and horrible visions according to all the things that the Lord your God did for you in Egypt, before thy eyes.€€ƒxSThat thou mightest know that the Lord he is God, and there is no other besides him.€€ƒx¹From heaven he made thee to hear his voice, that he might teach thee. And upon earth he shewed thee his exceeding great fire, and thou didst hear his words out of the midst of the fire,€€ƒxˆBecause he loved thy fathers, and chose their seed after them. And he brought thee out of Egypt, going before thee with his great power,€€ƒx¬To destroy at thy coming very great nations, and stronger than thou art, and to bring thee in, and give thee their land for a possession, as thou seest at this present day.€€ƒx‰Know therefore this day, and think in thy heart that the Lord he is God in heaven above, and in the earth beneath, and there is no other.€€ƒxÍKeep his precepts and commandments, which I command thee: that it may be well with thee, and thy children after thee, and thou mayst remain a long time upon the land, which the Lord thy God will give thee.€€ƒxEThen Moses set aside three cities beyond the Jordan at the east side,€€ƒx«That any one might flee to them who should kill his neighbour unwillingly, and was not his enemy a day or two before, and that he might escape to some one of these cities:€€ƒx¹Bosor in the wilderness, which is situate in the plains of the tribe of Ruben: and Ramoth in Galaad, which is in the tribe of Gad: and Golan in Basan, which is in the tribe of Manasses.€€ƒx>This is the law, that Moses set before the children of Israel,€€ƒx‚And these are the testimonies and ceremonies and judgments, which he spoke to the children of Israel, when they came out of Egypt,€€ƒxÇBeyond the Jordan in the valley over against the temple of Phogor, in the land of Sehon king of the Amorrhites, that dwelt in Hesebon, whom Moses slew. And the children of Israel coming out of Egypt,€€ƒx“Possessed his land, and the land of Og king of Basan, of the two kings of the Amorrhites, who were beyond the Jordan towards the rising of the sun:€€ƒxnFrom Aroer, which is situate upon the bank of the torrent Arnon, unto mount Sion, which is also called Hermon,€€ƒxtAll the plain beyond the Jordan at the east side, unto the sea of the wilderness, and unto the foot of mount Phasga.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¦And Moses called all Israel, and said to them: Hear, O Israel, the ceremonies and judgments, which I speak in your ears this day: learn them, and fulfil them in work.€€ƒx2The Lord our God made a covenant with us in Horeb.€€ƒxWHe made not the covenant with our fathers, but with us, who are now present and living.€€ƒxBHe spoke to us face to face in the mount out of the midst of fire.€€ƒx£I was the mediator and stood between the Lord and you at that time, to shew you his words, for you feared the fire, and went not up into the mountain, and he said:€€˜!ƒx-Thou shalt not have strange gods in my sight.€€ƒx¸Thou shalt not make to thy self a graven thing, nor the likeness of any things, that are in heaven above, or that are in the earth beneath, or that abide in the waters under the earth.€€ƒxØThou shalt not adore them, and thou shalt not serve them. For I am the Lord thy God, a jealous God, visiting the iniquity of the fathers upon their children unto the third and fourth generation, to them that hate me,€€ƒxVAnd shewing mercy unto many thousands, to them that love me, and keep my commandments.€€ƒx€Thou shalt not take the name of the Lord thy God in vain: for he shall not be unpunished that taketh his name upon a vain thing.€€ƒxXObserve the day of the sabbath, to sanctify it, as the Lord thy God hath commanded thee.€€ƒx7Six days shalt thou labour, and shalt do all thy works.€€ƒyZThe seventh is the day of the sabbath, that is, the rest of the Lord thy God. Thou shalt not do any work therein, thou nor thy son nor thy daughter, nor thy manservant nor thy maidservant, nor thy ox, nor thy ass, nor any of thy beasts, nor the stranger that is within thy gates: that thy manservant and thy maidservant may rest, even as thyself.€€ƒxÙRemember that thou also didst serve in Egypt, and the Lord thy God brought thee out from thence with a strong hand, and a stretched out arm. Therefore hath he commanded thee that thou shouldst observe the sabbath day.€€ƒx¹Honour thy father and mother, as the Lord thy God hath commanded thee, that thou mayst live a long time, and it may be well with thee in the land, which the Lord thy God will give thee.€€ƒtThou shalt not kill.€€ƒx#Neither shalt thou commit adultery.€€ƒxAnd thou shalt not steal.€€ƒx<Neither shalt thou bear false witness against thy neighbour.€€ƒx¥Thou shalt not covet thy neighbour's wife: nor his house, nor his field, nor his manservant, nor his maidservant, nor his ox, nor his ass, nor any thing that is his.€€ƒxóThese words the Lord spoke to all the multitude of you in the mountain, out of the midst of the fire and the cloud, and the darkness, with a loud voice, adding nothing more: and he wrote them in two tables of stone, which he delivered unto me.€€ƒx§But you, after you heard the voice out of the midst of the darkness, and saw the mountain burn, came to me, all the princes of the tribes and the elders, and you said:€€ƒx¿Behold the Lord our God hath shewn us his majesty and his greatness, we have heard his voice out of the midst of the fire, and have proved this day that God speaking with man, man hath lived.€€ƒx”Why shall we die therefore, and why shall this exceeding great fire comsume us: for if we hear the voice of the Lord our God any more, we shall die.€€ƒx•What is all flesh, that it should hear the voice of the living God, who speaketh out of the midst of the fire, as we have heard, and be able to live?€€ƒx‘Approach thou rather: and hear all things that the Lord our God shall say to thee, and thou shalt speak to us, and we will hear and will do them.€€ƒx And when the Lord had heard this, he said to me: I have heard the voice of the words of this people, which they spoke to thee: they have spoken all things well.€€ƒx¢Who shall give them to have such a mind, to fear me, and to keep all my commandments at all times, that it may be well with them and with their children for ever?€€ƒx+Go and say to them: Return into your tents.€€ƒxÑBut stand thou here with me, and I will speak to thee all my commandments, and ceremonies and judgments: which thou shalt teach them, that they may do them in the land, which I will give them for a possession.€€ƒxŠKeep therefore and do the things which the Lord God hath commanded you: you shall not go aside neither to the right hand, nor to the left.€€ƒx®But you shall walk in the way that the Lord your God hath commanded, that you may live, and it may be well with you, and your days may be long in the land of your possession.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÁThese are the precepts, and ceremonies, and judgments, which the Lord your God commanded that I should teach you, and that you should do them in the land into which you pass over to possess it:€€ƒxÃThat thou mayst fear the Lord thy God, and keep all his commandments and precepts, which I command thee, and thy sons, and thy grandsons, all the days of thy life, that thy days may be prolonged.€€ƒxïHear, O Israel, and observe to do the things which the Lord hath commanded thee, that it may be well with thee, and thou mayst be greatly multiplied, as the Lord the God of thy fathers hath promised thee a land flowing with milk and honey.€€ƒx-Hear, O Israel, the Lord our God is one Lord.€€ƒxlThou shalt love the Lord thy God with thy whole heart, and with thy whole soul, and with thy whole strength.€€˜ƒx’And thou shalt tell them to thy children, and thou shalt meditate upon them sitting in thy house, and walking on thy journey, sleeping and rising.€€ƒxbAnd thou shalt bind them as a sign on thy hand, and they shall be and shall move between thy eyes.€€ƒxFAnd thou shalt write them in the entry, and on the doors of thy house.€€ƒxÌAnd when the Lord thy God shall have brought thee into the land, for which he swore to thy fathers Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob: and shall have given thee great and goodly cities, which thou didst not build,€€ƒxŒHouses full of riches, which thou didst not set up, cisterns which thou didst not dig, vineyards and oliveyards, which thou didst not plant,€€ƒx&And thou shalt have eaten and be full:€€ƒxÕTake heed diligently lest thou forget the Lord, who brought thee out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage. Thou shalt fear the Lord thy God, and shalt serve him only, and thou shalt swear by his name.€€ƒxUYou shall not go after the strange gods of all the nations, that are round about you:€€ƒx¶Because the Lord thy God is a jealous God in the midst of thee: lest at any time the wrath of the Lord thy God be kindled against thee, and take thee away from the face of the earth.€€ƒxXThou shalt not tempt the Lord thy God, as thou temptedst him in the place of temptation.€€ƒxgKeep the precepts of the Lord thy God, and the testimonies and ceremonies which he hath commanded thee.€€ƒxÀAnd do that which is pleasing and good in the sight of the Lord, that it may be well with thee: and going in thou mayst possess the goodly land, concerning which the Lord swore to thy fathers,€€ƒxEThat he would destroy all thy enemies before thee, as he hath spoken.€€ƒx—And when thy son shall ask thee to morrow, saying: What mean these testimonies, and ceremonies and judgments, which the Lord our God hath commanded us?€€ƒxsThou shalt say to him: We were bondmen of Pharao in Egypt, and the Lord brought us out of Egypt with a strong hand.€€ƒxrAnd he wrought signs and wonders great and very grievous in Egypt against Pharao, and all his house, in our sight,€€ƒx|And he brought us out from thence, that he might bring us in and give us the land, concerning which he swore to our fathers.€€ƒx®And the Lord commanded that we should do all these ordinances, and should fear the Lord our God, that it might be well with us all the days of our life, as it is at this day.€€ƒxsAnd he will be merciful to us, if we keep and do all his precepts before the Lord our God, as he hath commanded us.€€ƒ`€€ƒyWWhen the Lord thy God shall have brought thee into the land, which thou art going in to possess, and shall have destroyed many nations before thee, the Hethite, and the Gergezite, and the Amorrhite, and the Chanaanite, and the Pherezite, and the Hevite, and the Jebusite, seven nations much more numerous than thou art, and stronger than thou:€€ƒx•And the Lord thy God shall have delivered them to thee, thou shalt utterly destroy them. Thou shalt make no league with them, nor shew mercy to them:€€ƒx|Neither shalt thou make marriages with them. Thou shalt not give thy daughter to his son, nor take his daughter for thy son:€€ƒx¢For she will turn away thy son from following me, that he may rather serve strange gods, and the wrath of the Lord will be kindled, and will quickly destroy thee.€€ƒx‘But thus rather shall you deal with them: Destroy their altars, and break their statues, and cut down their groves, and burn their graven things.€€˜ƒx†Not because you surpass all nations in number, is the Lord joined unto you, and hath chosen you, for you are the fewest of any people:€€ƒxâBut because the Lord hath loved you, and hath kept his oath, which he swore to your fathers: and hath brought you out with a strong hand, and redeemed you from the house of bondage, out of the hand of Pharao the king of Egypt.€€ƒxÆAnd thou shalt know that the Lord thy God, he is a strong and faithful God, keeping his covenant and mercy to them that love him, and to them that keep his commandments, unto a thousand generations:€€ƒxˆAnd repaying forthwith them that hate him, so as to destroy them, without further delay immediately rendering to them what they deserve.€€ƒx^Keep therefore the precepts and ceremonies and judgments, which I command thee this day to do.€€ƒx£If after thou hast heard these judgments, thou keep and do them, the Lord thy God will also keep his covenant to thee, and the mercy which he swore to thy fathers:€€ƒyAnd he will love thee and multiply thee, and will bless the fruit of thy womb, and the fruit of thy land, thy corn, and thy vintage, thy oil, and thy herds, and the flocks of thy sheep upon the land, for which he swore to thy fathers that he would give it thee.€€ƒxrBlessed shalt thou be among all people. No one shall be barren among you of either sex, neither of men nor cattle.€€ƒxThe Lord will take away from thee all sickness: and the grievous infirmities of Egypt, which thou knowest, he will not bring upon thee, but upon thy enemies.€€ƒx©Thou shalt consume all the people, which the Lord thy God will deliver to thee. Thy eye shall not spare them, neither shalt thou serve their gods, lest they be thy ruin.€€ƒx]If thou say in thy heart: These nations are more than I, how shall I be able to destroy them?€€ƒxTFear not, but remember what the Lord thy God did to Pharao and to all the Egyptians,€€ƒxİThe exceeding great plagues, which thy eyes saw, and the signs and wonders, and the strong hand, and the stretched out arm, with which the Lord thy God brought thee out: so will he do to all the people, whom thou fearest.€€ƒxMoreover the Lord thy God will send also hornets among them, until he destroy and consume all that have escaped thee, and could hide themselves.€€ƒxfThou shalt not fear them, because the Lord thy God is in the midst of thee, a God mighty and terrible:€€ƒxÁHe will consume these nations in thy sight by little and little and by degrees. Thou wilt not be able to destroy them altogether: lest perhaps the beasts of the earth should increase upon thee.€€ƒxjBut the Lord thy God shall deliver them in thy sight: and shall slay them until they be utterly destroyed.€€ƒx¤And he shall deliver their kings into thy hands, and thou shalt destroy their names from under Heaven: no man shall be able to resist thee, until thou destroy them.€€ƒxæTheir graven things thou shalt burn with fire: thou shalt not covet the silver and gold of which they are made, neither shalt thou take to thee any thing thereof, lest thou offend, because it is an abomination to the Lord thy God.€€ƒxÓNeither shalt thou bring any thing of the idol into thy house, lest thou become an anathema, like it. Thou shalt detest it as dung, and shalt utterly abhor it as uncleanness and filth, because it is an anathema.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÂAll the commandments, that I command thee this day, take great care to observe: that you may live, and be multiplied, and going in may possess the land, for which the Lord swore to your fathers.€€ƒyAnd thou shalt remember all the way through which the Lord thy God hath brought thee for forty years through the desert, to afflict thee and to prove thee, and that the things that were known in thy heart might be made known, whether thou wouldst keep his commandments or no.€€ƒxÏHe afflicted thee with want, and gave thee manna for thy food, which neither thou nor thy fathers knew: to shew that not in bread alone doth man live, but in every word that proceedeth from the mouth of God.€€ƒx|Thy raiment, with which thou wast covered, hath not decayed for age, and thy foot is not worn, lo this is the fortieth year,€€ƒxsThat thou mayst consider in thy heart, that as a man traineth up his son, so the Lord thy God hath trained thee up.€€”ƒxFor the Lord thy God will bring thee into a good land, of brooks and of waters, and of fountains: in the plains of which and the hills deep rivers break out:€€ƒx}A land of wheat, and barley, and vineyards, wherein fig trees and pomegranates, and oliveyards grow: a land of oil and honey.€€ƒx›Where without any want thou shalt eat thy bread, and enjoy abundance of all things: where the stones are iron, and out of its hills are dug mines of brass:€€ƒx{That when thou hast eaten, and art full, thou mayst bless the Lord thy God for the excellent land which he hath given thee.€€ƒxTake heed, and beware lest at any time thou forget the Lord thy God, and neglect his commandments and judgments and ceremonies, which I command thee this day:€€ƒxWLest after thou hast eaten and art filled, hast built goodly houses, and dwelt in them,€€ƒxfAnd shalt have herds of oxen and flocks of sheep, and plenty of gold and of silver, and of all things,€€ƒx‡Thy heart be lifted up, and thou remember not the Lord thy God, who brought thee out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage:€€ƒxÙAnd was thy leader in the great and terrible wilderness, wherein there was the serpent burning with his breath, and the scorpion and the dipsas, and no waters at all: who brought forth streams out of the hardest rock,€€ƒx“And fed thee in the wilderness with manna which thy fathers knew not. And after he had afflicted and proved thee, at the last he had mercy on thee,€€ƒxyLest thou shouldst say in thy heart: My own might, and the strength of my own hand have achieved all these things for me.€€ƒxªBut remember the Lord thy God, that he hath given thee strength, that he might fulfil his covenant, concerning which he swore to thy fathers, as this present day sheweth.€€ƒx’But if thou forget the Lord thy God, and follow strange gods, and serve and adore them: behold now I foretell thee that thou shalt utterly perish.€€ƒxŒAs the nations, which the Lord destroyed at thy entrance, so shall you also perish, if you be disobedient to the voice of the Lord your God.€€ƒ`€€ƒx™Hear, O Israel: Thou shalt go over the Jordan this day; to possess nations very great, and stronger than thyself, cities great, and walled up to the sky,€€ƒxzA people great and tall, the sons of the Enacims, whom thou hast seen, and heard of, against whom no man is able to stand.€€ƒxãThou shalt know therefore this day that the Lord thy God himself will pass over before thee, a devouring and consuming fire, to destroy and extirpate and bring them to nothing before thy face quickly, as he hath spoken to thee.€€ƒxÎSay not in thy heart, when the Lord thy God shall have destroyed them in thy sight: For my justice hath the Lord brought me in to possess this land, whereas these nations are destroyed for their wickedness.€€ƒy)For it is not for thy justices, and the uprightness of thy heart that thou shalt go in to possess their lands: but because they have done wickedly, they are destroyed at thy coming in: and that the Lord might accomplish his word, which he promised by oath to thy fathers Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.€€˜ƒx¿Remember, and forget not how thou provokedst the Lord thy God to wrath in the wilderness. From the day that thou camest out of Egypt unto this place, thou hast always strove against the Lord.€€ƒx[For in Horeb, also thou didst provoke him, and he was angry, and would have destroyed thee,€€ƒxÔWhen I went up into the mount to receive the tables of stone, the tables of the covenant which the Lord made with you: and I continued in the mount forty days and nights, neither eating bread, nor drinking water.€€ƒxÌAnd the Lord gave me two tables of stone written with the finger of God, and containing all the words that he spoke to you in the mount from the midst of the fire, when the people were assembled together.€€ƒxzAnd when forty days were passed, and as many nights, the Lord gave me the two tables of stone, the tables of the covenant,€€ƒxĞAnd said to me: Arise, and go down from hence quickly: for thy people, which thou hast brought out of Egypt, have quickly forsaken the way that thou hast shewn them, and have made to themselves a molten idol.€€ƒxEAnd again the Lord said to me: I see that this people is stiffnecked:€€ƒx“Let me alone that I may destroy them, and abolish their name from under heaven, and set thee over a nation, that is greater and stronger than this.€€ƒxeAnd when I came down from the burning mount, and held the two tables of the covenant with both hands,€€ƒxšAnd saw that you had sinned against the Lord your God, and had made to yourselves a molten calf, and had quickly forsaken his way, which he had shewn you:€€ƒx@I cast the tables out of my hands, and broke them in your sight.€€ƒxÆAnd I fell down before the Lord as before, forty days and nights neither eating bread, nor drinking water, for all your sins, which you had committed against the Lord, and had provoked him to wrath:€€ƒxFor I feared his indignation and anger, wherewith being moved against you, he would have destroyed you. And the Lord heard me this time also.€€ƒxqAnd he was exceeding angry against Aaron also, and would have destroyed him, and I prayed in like manner for him.€€ƒxÖAnd your sin that you had committed, that is, the calf, I took, and burned it with fire, and breaking it into pieces, until it was as small as dust, I threw it into the torrent, which cometh down from the mountain.€€ƒxeAt the burning also, and at the place of temptation, and at the graves of lust you provoked the Lord:€€ƒxØAnd when he sent you from Cadesbarne, saying: Go up, and possess the land that I have given you, and you slighted the commandment of the Lord your God, and did not believe him, neither would you hearken to his voice:€€ƒxABut were always rebellious from the day that I began to know you.€€ƒxAnd I lay prostrate before the Lord forty days and nights, in which I humbly besought him, that he would not destroy you as he had threatened:€€ƒx°And praying, I said: O Lord God, destroy not thy people, and thy inheritance, which thou hast redeemed in thy greatness, whom thou hast brought out of Egypt with a strong hand.€€ƒx~Remember thy servants Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob: look not on the stubbornness of this people, nor on their wickedness and sin:€€ƒxñLest perhaps the inhabitants of the land, out of which thou hast brought us, say: The Lord could not bring them into the land that he promised them, and he hated them: therefore he brought them out, that he might kill them in the wilderness,€€ƒxwWho are thy people and thy inheritance, whom thou hast brought out by thy great strength, and in thy stretched out arm.€€ƒ`€€ƒx•At that time the Lord said to me: Hew thee two tables of stone like the former, and come up to me into the mount: and thou shalt make an ark of wood,€€ƒxzAnd I will write on the tables the words that were in them, which thou brokest before, and thou shalt put them in the ark.€€ƒxŒAnd I made an ark of setim wood. And when I had hewn two tables of stone like the former, I went up into the mount, having them in my hands.€€ƒxËAnd he wrote in the tables, according as he had written before, the ten words, which the Lord spoke to you in the mount from the midst of the fire, when the people were assembled: and he gave them to me.€€ƒxœAnd returning from the mount, I came down, and put the tables into the ark, that I had made, and they are there till this present, as the Lord commanded me.€€–ƒx{From thence they came to Gadgad, from which place they departed, and camped in Jetebatha, in a land of waters and torrents.€€ƒx´At that time he separated the tribe of Levi, to carry the ark of the covenant of the Lord, and to stand before him in the ministry, and to bless in his name until this present day.€€ƒx‹Wherefore Levi hath no part nor possession with his brethren: because the Lord himself is his possession, as the Lord thy God promised him.€€ƒx}And I stood in the mount, as before, forty days and nights: and the Lord heard me this time also, and would not destroy thee.€€ƒx”And he said to me: Go, and walk before the people, that they may enter, and possess the land, which I swore to their fathers that I would give them.€€ƒxÌAnd now, Israel, what doth the Lord thy God require of thee, but that thou fear the Lord thy God, and walk in his ways, and love him, and serve the Lord thy God, with all thy heart, and with all thy soul:€€ƒxxAnd keep the commandments of the Lord, and his ceremonies, which I command thee this day, that it may be well with thee?€€ƒxiBehold heaven is the Lord's thy God, and the heaven of heaven, the earth and all things that are therein.€€ƒx¬And yet the Lord hath been closely joined to thy fathers, and loved them and chose their seed after them, that is to say, you, out of all nations, as this day it is proved.€€ƒxOCircumcise therefore the foreskin of your heart, and stiffen your neck no more.€€ƒx—Because the Lord your God he is the God of gods, and the Lord of lords, a great God and mighty and terrible, who accepteth no person nor taketh bribes.€€ƒxgHe doth judgment to the fatherless and the widow, loveth the stranger, and giveth him food and raiment.€€ƒxZAnd do you therefore love strangers, because you also were strangers in the land of Egypt.€€ƒxlThou shalt fear the Lord thy God, and serve him only: to him thou shalt adhere, and shalt swear by his name.€€ƒxqHe is thy praise, and thy God, that hath done for thee these great and terrible things, which thy eyes have seen.€€ƒxIn seventy souls thy fathers went down into Egypt: and behold now the Lord thy God hath multiplied thee as the stars of heaven.€€ƒ`€€ƒxuTherefore love the Lord thy God and observe his precepts and ceremonies, his judgments and commandments at all times.€€ƒx¢Know this day the things that your children know not, who saw not the chastisements of the Lord your God, his great doings and strong hand, and stretched out arm,€€ƒx[The signs and works which he did in the midst of Egypt to king Pharao, and to all his land,€€ƒxÂAnd to all the host of the Egyptians, and to their horses and chariots: how the waters of the Red Sea covered them, when they pursued you, and how the Lord destroyed them until this present day:€€ƒxKAnd what he hath done to you in the wilderness, til you came to this place:€€˜ ƒxGYour eyes have seen all the great works of the Lord, that he hath done,€€ƒx…That you may keep all his commandments, which I command you this day, and may go in, and possess the land, to which you are entering,€€ƒxAnd may live in it a long time: which the Lord promised by oath to your fathers, and to their seed, a land which floweth with milk and honey.€€ƒxÃFor the land, which thou goest to possess, is not like the land of Egypt, from whence thou camest out, where, when the seed is sown, waters are brought in to water it after the manner of gardens.€€ƒxABut it is a land of hills and plains, expecting rain from heaven.€€ƒxvAnd the Lord thy God doth always visit it, and his eyes are on it from the beginning of the year unto the end thereof.€€ƒx›If then you obey my commandments, which I command you this day, that you love the Lord your God, and serve him with all your heart, and with all your soul:€€ƒx|He will give to your land the early rain and the latter rain, that you may gather in your corn, and your wine, and your oil,€€ƒxWAnd your hay out of the fields to feed your cattle, and that you may eat and be filled.€€ƒxqBeware lest perhaps your heart be deceived, and you depart from the Lord, and serve strange gods, and adore them:€€ƒx²And the Lord being angry shut up heaven, that the rain come not down, nor the earth yield her fruit, and you perish quickly from the excellent land, which the Lord will give you.€€ƒxvLay up these words in your hearts and minds, and hang them for a sign on your hands, and place them between your eyes.€€ƒx™Teach your children that they meditate on them, when thou sittest in thy house, and when thou walkest on the way, and when thou liest down and risest up.€€ƒx@Thou shalt write them upon the posts and the doors of thy house:€€ƒx´That thy days may be multiplied, and the days of thy children in the land which the Lord swore to thy fathers, that he would give them as long as the heaven hangeth over the earth.€€ƒxŠFor if you keep the commandments which I command you, and do them, to love the Lord your God, and walk in all his ways, cleaving unto him,€€ƒx~The Lord will destroy all these nations before your face, and you shall possess them, which are greater and stronger than you.€€ƒx«Every place, that your foot shall tread upon, shall be yours. From the desert, and from Libanus, from the great river Euphrates unto the western sea shall be your borders.€€ƒxšNone shall stand against you: the Lord your God shall lay the dread and fear of you upon all the land that you shall tread upon, as he hath spoken to you.€€ƒxABehold I set forth in your sight this day a blessing and a curse:€€ƒx\A blessing, if you obey the commandments of the Lord your God, which I command you this day:€€ƒxA curse, if you obey not the commandments of the Lord your God, but revolt from the way which now I shew you, and walk after strange gods which you know not.€€ƒx©And when the Lord thy God shall have brought thee into the land, whither thou goest to dwell, thou shalt put the blessing upon mount Garizim, the curse upon mount Hebal:€€ƒxàWhich are beyond the Jordan, behind the way that goeth to the setting of the sun, in the land of the Chanaanite who dwelleth in the plain country over against Galgala, which is near the valley that reacheth and entereth far.€€ƒx„For you shall pass over the Jordan, to possess the land, which the Lord your God will give you, that you may have it and possess it.€€ƒxbSee therefore that you fulfil the ceremonies and judgments, which I shall set this day before you.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¹These are the precepts and judgments, that you must do in the land, which the Lord the God of thy fathers will give thee, to possess it all the days that thou shalt walk upon the earth.€€ƒx–Destroy all the places in which the nations, that you shall possess, worshipped their gods upon high mountains, and hills, and under every shady tree:€€ƒxœOverthrow their altars, and break down their statues, burn their groves with fire, and break their idols in pieces: destroy their names out of those places.€€ƒx)You shall not do so to the Lord your God:€€ƒxˆBut you shall come to the place, which the Lord your God shall choose out of all your tribes, to put his name there, and to dwell in it:€€˜ ƒxÂAnd you shall eat there in the sight of the Lord your God: and you shall rejoice in all things, whereunto you shall put your hand, you and your houses wherein the Lord your God hath blessed you.€€ƒxdYou shall not do there the things we do here this day, every man that which seemeth good to himself.€€ƒxsFor until this present time you are not come to rest, and to the possession, which the Lord your God will give you.€€ƒx·You shall pass over the Jordan, and shall dwell in the land which the Lord your God will give you, that you may have rest from all enemies round about: and may dwell without any fear,€€ƒy!In the place, which the Lord your God shall choose, that his name may be therein. Thither shall you bring all the things that I command you, holocausts, and victims, and tithes, and the firstfruits of your hands: and whatsoever is the choicest in the gifts which you shall vow to the Lord.€€ƒxÙThere shall you feast before the Lord your God, you and your sons and your daughters, your menservants and maidservants, and the Levite that dwelleth in your cities. For he hath no other part and possession among you.€€ƒxIBeware lest thou offer thy holocausts in every place that thou shalt see:€€ƒx„But in the place which the Lord shall choose in one of thy tribes shalt thou offer sacrifices, and shalt do all that I command thee.€€ƒycBut if thou desirest to eat, and the eating of flesh delight thee, kill, and eat according to the blessing of the Lord thy God, which he hath given thee, in thy cities: whether it be unclean, that is to say, having blemish or defect: or clean, that is to say, sound and without blemish, such as may be offered, as the roe, and the hart, shalt thou eat it:€€ƒxVOnly the blood thou shalt not eat, but thou shalt pour it out upon the earth as water.€€ƒxåThou mayst not eat in thy towns the tithes of thy corn, and thy wine, and thy oil, the firstborn of thy herds and thy cattle, nor any thing that thou vowest, and that thou wilt offer voluntarily, and the firstfruits of thy hands:€€ƒyGBut thou shalt eat them before the Lord thy God in the place which the Lord thy God shall choose, thou and thy son and thy daughter, and thy manservant, and maidservant, and the Levite that dwelleth in thy cities: and thou shalt rejoice and be refreshed before the Lord thy God in all things, whereunto thou shalt put thy hand.€€ƒxPTake heed thou forsake not the Levite all the time that thou livest in the land.€€ƒx…When the Lord thy God shall have enlarged thy borders, as he hath spoken to thee, and thou wilt eat the flesh that thy soul desireth:€€ƒxÛAnd if the place which the Lord thy God shall choose, that his name should be there, be far off, thou shalt kill of thy herds and of thy flocks, as I have commanded thee, and shalt eat in thy towns, as it pleaseth thee.€€ƒxrEven as the roe and the hart is eaten, so shalt thou eat them: both the clean and unclean shall eat of them alike.€€ƒx‰Only beware of this, that thou eat not the blood, for the blood is for the soul: and therefore thou must not eat the soul with the flesh:€€ƒx/But thou shalt pour it upon the earth as water,€€ƒx~That it may be well with thee and thy children after thee, when thou shalt do that which is pleasing in the sight of the Lord.€€ƒxŠBut the things which thou hast sanctified and vowed to the Lord, thou shalt take, and shalt come to the place which the Lord shall choose:€€ƒx·And shalt offer thy oblations, the flesh and the blood upon the altar of the Lord thy God: the blood of thy victims thou shalt pour on the altar: and the flesh thou thyself shalt eat.€€ƒxÇObserve and hear all the things that I command thee, that it may be well with thee and thy children after thee for ever, when thou shalt do what is good and pleasing in the sight of the Lord thy God.€€ƒx¥When the Lord thy God shall have destroyed before thy face the nations, which thou shalt go in to possess, and when thou shalt possess them, and dwell in their land:€€ƒxÁBeware lest thou imitate them, after they are destroyed at thy coming in, and lest thou seek after their ceremonies, saying: As these nations have worshipped their gods, so will I also worship.€€ƒxÄThou shalt not do in like manner to the Lord thy God. For they have done to their gods all the abominations which the Lord abhorreth, offering their sons and daughters, and burning them with fire.€€ƒxXWhat I command thee, that only do thou to the Lord: neither add any thing, nor diminish.€€ƒ`€€ƒx|If there rise in the midst of thee a prophet or one that saith he hath dreamed a dream, and he foretell a sign and a wonder,€€ƒx‹And that come to pass which he spoke, and he say to thee: Let us go and follow strange gods, which thou knowest not, and let us serve them:€€ƒx¸Thou shalt not hear the words of that prophet or dreamer: for the Lord your God trieth you, that it may appear whether you love him with all your heart, and with all your soul, or not.€€ƒxˆFollow the Lord your God, and fear him, and keep his commandments, and hear his voice: him you shall serve, and to him you shall cleave.€€ƒyGAnd that prophet or forger of dreams shall be slain: because he spoke to draw you away from the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, and redeemed you from the house of bondage: to make thee go out of the way, which the Lord thy God commanded thee: and thou shalt take away the evil out of the midst of thee.€€’ƒxbOf all the nations round about, that are near or afar off, from one end of the earth to the other,€€ƒxXConsent not to him, hear him not, neither let thy eye spare him to pity and conceal him,€€ƒxvBut thou shalt presently put him to death. Let thy hand be first upon him, and afterwards the hands of all the people.€€ƒxªWith stones shall he be stoned to death: because he would have withdrawn thee from the Lord thy God, who brought thee out of the land of Egypt, from the house of bondage:€€ƒxIThat all Israel hearing may fear, and may do no more any thing like this.€€ƒx`If in one of thy cities, which the Lord thy God shall give thee to dwell in, thou hear some say:€€ƒx¬Children of Belial are gone out of the midst of thee, and have withdrawn the inhabitants of their city, and have said: Let us go, and serve strange gods which you know not:€€ƒxºInquire carefully and diligently, the truth of the thing by looking well into it, and if thou find that which is said to be certain, and that this abomination hath been really committed,€€ƒx—Thou shalt forthwith kill the inhabitants of that city with the edge of the sword, and shalt destroy it and all things that are in it, even the cattle.€€ƒxûAnd all the household goods that are there, thou shalt gather together in the midst of the streets thereof, and shall burn them with the city itself, so as to comsume all for the Lord thy God, and that it be a heap for ever: it shall be built no more.€€ƒx¸And there shall nothing of that anathema stick to thy hand: that the Lord may turn from the wrath of his fury, and may have mercy on thee, and multiply thee as he swore to thy fathers,€€ƒx²When thou shalt hear the voice of the Lord thy God, keeping all his precepts, which I command thee this day, that thou mayst do what is pleasing in the sight of the Lord thy God.€€ƒ`€€ƒxfBe ye children of the Lord your God: you shall not cut yourselves, nor make any baldness for the dead;€€ƒx‡Because thou art a holy people to the Lord thy God: and he chose thee to be his peculiar people of all nations that are upon the earth.€€ƒx$Eat not the things that are unclean.€€ƒxMThese are the beasts that you shall eat, the ox, and the sheep, and the goat,€€ƒx]The hart and the roe, the buffle, the chamois, the pygarg, the wild goat, the camelopardalus.€€˜ƒxÌBut of them that chew the cud, but divide not the hoof, you shall not eat, such as the camel, the hare, and the cherogril: because they chew the cud, but divide not the hoof, they shall be unclean to you.€€ƒx The swine also, because it divideth the hoof, but cheweth not the cud, shall be unclean, their flesh you shall not eat, and their carcasses you shall not touch.€€ƒxbThese shall you eat of all that abide in the waters: All that have fins and scales, you shall eat.€€ƒxQSuch as are without fins and scales, you shall not eat, because they are unclean.€€ƒx'All birds that are clean you shall eat.€€ƒxFThe unclean eat not: to wit, the eagle, and the grype, and the osprey,€€ƒxDThe ringtail, and the vulture, and the kite according to their kind:€€ƒxAnd all of the raven's kind:€€ƒxPAnd the ostrich, and the owl, and the larus, and the hawk according to its kind:€€ƒx'The heron, and the swan, and the stork,€€ƒx6And the cormorant, the porphirion, and the night crow,€€ƒxTThe bittern, and the charadrion, every one in their kind: the houp also and the bat.€€ƒx[Every thing that creepeth, and hath little wings, shall be unclean, and shall not be eaten.€€ƒx!All that is clean, you shall eat.€€ƒxèBut whatsoever is dead of itself, eat not thereof. Give it to the stranger, that is within thy gates, to eat, or sell it to him: because thou art the holy people of the Lord thy God. Thou shalt not boil a kid in the milk of his dam.€€ƒx[Every year thou shalt set aside the tithes of all thy fruits that the earth bringeth forth,€€ƒyAnd thou shalt eat before the Lord thy God in the place which he shall choose, that his name may be called upon therein, the tithe of thy corn, and thy wine, and thy oil, and the firstborn of thy herds and thy sheep: that thou mayst learn to fear the Lord thy God at all times.€€ƒxBut when the way and the place which the Lord thy God shall choose, are far off, and he hath blessed thee, and thou canst not carry all these things thither,€€ƒxŠThou shalt sell them all, and turn them into money, and shalt carry it in thy hand, and shalt go to the place which the Lord shall choose:€€ƒxîAnd thou shalt buy with the same money whatsoever pleaseth thee, either of the herds or of sheep, wine also and strong drink, and all that thy soul desireth: and thou shalt eat before the Lord thy God, and shalt feast, thou and thy house:€€ƒxvAnd the Levite that is within thy gates, beware thou forsake him not, because he hath no other part in thy possession.€€ƒx„The third year thou shalt separate another tithe of all things that grow to thee at that time, and shalt lay it up within thy gates.€€ƒyAnd the Levite that hath no other part nor possession with thee, and the stranger and the fatherless and the widow, that are within thy gates, shall come and shall eat and be filled: that the Lord thy God may bless thee in all the works of thy hands that thou shalt do.€€ƒ`€€ƒx0In the seventh year thou shalt make a remission,€€ƒx¸Which shall be celebrated in this order. He to whom any thing is owing from his friend or neighbour or brother, cannot demand it again, because it is the year of remission of the Lord.€€ƒxOf the foreigner or stranger thou mayst exact it: of thy countryman and neighbour thou shalt not have power to demand it again.€€ƒxˆAnd there shall be no poor nor beggar among you: that the Lord thy God may bless thee in the land which he will give thee in possession.€€ƒx©Yet so if thou hear the voice of the Lord thy God, and keep all things that he hath ordained, and which I command thee this day, he will bless thee, as he hath promised.€€—ƒxºIf one of thy brethren that dwelleth within thy gates of thy city in the land which the Lord thy God will give thee, come to poverty: thou shalt not harden thy heart, nor close thy hand,€€ƒxcBut shalt open it to the poor man, thou shalt lend him, that which thou perceivest he hath need of.€€ƒy!Beware lest perhaps a wicked thought steal in upon thee, and thou say in thy heart: The seventh year of remission draweth nigh; and thou turn away thy eyes from thy poor brother, denying to lend him that which he asketh: lest he cry against thee to the Lord, and it become a sin unto thee.€€ƒxÉBut thou shalt give to him: neither shalt thou do any thing craftily in relieving his necessities: that the Lord thy God may bless thee at all times, and in all things to which thou shalt put thy hand.€€ƒxŸThere will not be wanting poor in the land of thy habitation: therefore I command thee to open thy hand to thy needy and poor brother, that liveth in the land.€€ƒxWhen thy brother a Hebrew man, or Hebrew woman is sold to thee, and hath served thee six years, in the seventh year thou shalt let him go free:€€ƒxIAnd when thou sendest him out free, thou shalt not let him go away empty:€€ƒx‹But shall give him for his way out of thy flocks, and out of thy barnfloor, and thy winepress, wherewith the Lord thy God shall bless thee.€€ƒxŒRemember that thou also wast a bondservant in the land of Egypt, and the Lord thy God made thee free, and therefore I now command thee this.€€ƒxoBut if he say: I will not depart: because he loveth thee, and thy house, and findeth that he is well with thee:€€ƒx£Thou shalt take an awl, and bore through his ear in the door of thy house, and he shall serve thee for ever: thou shalt do in like manner to thy womanservant also.€€ƒxÎTurn not away thy eyes from them when thou makest them free: because he hath served thee six years according to the wages of a hireling: that the Lord thy God may bless thee in all the works that thou dost.€€ƒxêOf the firstlings, that come of thy herds and thy sheep, thou shalt sanctify to the Lord thy God whatsoever is of the male sex. Thou shalt not work with the firstling of a bullock, and thou shalt not shear the firstlings of thy sheep.€€ƒx}In the sight of the Lord thy God shalt thou eat them every year, in the place that the Lord shall choose, thou and thy house.€€ƒx„But if it have a blemish, or be lame, or blind, or in any part disfigured or feeble, it shall not be sacrificed to the Lord thy God.€€ƒxBut thou shalt eat it within the gates of thy city: the clean and the unclean shall eat them alike, as the roe and as the hart.€€ƒxXOnly thou shalt take heed not to eat their blood, but pour it out on the earth as water.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÄObserve the month of new corn, which is the first of the spring, that thou mayst celebrate the phase to the Lord thy God: because in this month the Lord thy God brought thee out of Egypt by night.€€ƒxŸAnd thou shalt sacrifice the phase to the Lord thy God, of sheep, and of oxen, in the place which the Lord thy God shall choose, that his name may dwell there.€€ƒxîThou shalt not eat with it leavened bread: seven days shalt thou eat without leaven, the bread of affliction, because thou camest out of Egypt in fear: that thou mayst remember the day of thy coming out of Egypt, all the days of thy life.€€ƒx¨No leaven shall be seen in all thy coasts for seven days, neither shall any of the flesh of that which was sacrificed the first day in the evening remain until morning.€€ƒxbThou mayst not immolate the phase in any one of thy cities, which the Lord thy God will give thee:€€–ƒx‘And thou shalt dress, and eat it in the place which the Lord thy God shall choose, and in the morning rising up thou shalt go into thy dwellings.€€ƒxˆSix days shalt thou eat unleavened bread: and on the seventh day, because it is the assembly of the Lord thy God, thou shalt do no work.€€ƒxeThou shalt number unto thee seven weeks from that day, wherein thou didst put the sickle to the corn.€€ƒx«And thou shalt celebrate the festival of weeks to the Lord thy God, a voluntary oblation of thy hand, which thou shalt offer according to the blessing of the Lord thy God.€€ƒy=And thou shalt feast before the Lord thy God, thou, and thy son, and thy daughter, and thy manservant, and thy maidservant, and the Levite that is within thy gates, and the stranger and the fatherless, and the widow, who abide with you: in the place which the Lord thy God shall choose, that his name may dwell there:€€ƒxtAnd thou shalt remember that thou wast a servant in Egypt: and thou shalt keep and do the things that are commanded.€€ƒxThou shalt celebrate the solemnity also of tabernacles seven days, when thou hast gathered in thy fruit of the barnfloor and of the winepress.€€ƒxÓAnd thou shalt make merry in thy festival time, thou, thy son, and thy daughter, thy manservant, and thy maidservant, the Levite also and the stranger, and the fatherless and the widow that are within thy gates.€€ƒxÖSeven days shalt thou celebrate feasts to the Lord thy God in the place which the Lord shall choose: and the Lord thy God will bless thee in all thy fruits, and in every work of thy hands, and thou shalt be in joy.€€ƒy␀Three times in a year shall all thy males appear before the Lord thy God in the place which he shall choose: in the feast of unleavened bread, in the feast of weeks, and in the feast of tabernacles. No one shall appear with his hands empty before the Lord:€€ƒx|But every one shall offer according to what he hath, according to the blessing of the Lord his God, which he shall give him.€€ƒx©Thou shalt appoint judges and magistrates in all thy gates, which the Lord thy God shall give thee, in all thy tribes: that they may judge the people with just judgment,€€ƒxAnd not go aside to either part. Thou shalt not accept person nor gifts: for gifts blind the eyes of the wise, and change the words of the just.€€ƒx…Thou shalt follow justly after that which is just: that thou mayst live and possess the land, which the Lord thy God shall give thee.€€ƒxKThou shalt plant no grove, nor any tree near the altar of the Lord thy God:€€ƒx]Neither shalt thou make nor set up to thyself a statue: which things the Lord thy God hateth.€€ƒ`€€ƒx—Thou shalt not sacrifice to the Lord thy God a sheep, or an ox, wherein there is blemish, or any fault: for that is an abomination to the Lord thy God.€€ƒx½When there shall be found among you within any of thy gates, which the Lord thy God shall give thee, man or woman that do evil in the sight of the Lord thy God, and transgress his covenant,€€ƒxSo as to go and serve strange gods, and adore them, the sun and the moon, and all the host of heaven, which I have not commanded:€€ƒxAnd this is told thee, and hearing it thou hast inquired diligently, and found it to be true, and that the abomination is committed in Israel:€€ƒx‹Thou shalt bring forth the man or the woman, who have committed that most wicked thing, to the gates of thy city, and they shall be stoned.€€”ƒx°The hands of the witnesses shall be first upon him to kill him, and afterwards the hands of the rest of the people: that thou mayst take away the evil out of the midst of thee.€€ƒyIf thou perceive that there be among you a hard and doubtful matter in judgment between blood and blood, cause and cause, leprosy and leprosy: and thou see that the words of the judges within thy gates do vary: arise, and go up to the place, which the Lord thy God shall choose.€€ƒx·And thou shalt come to the priests of the Levitical race, and to the judge, that shall be at that time: and thou shalt ask of them, and they shall shew thee the truth of the judgment.€€ƒx„And thou shalt do whatsoever they shall say, that preside in the place, which the Lord shall choose, and what they shall teach thee,€€ƒx~According to his law; and thou shalt follow their sentence: neither shalt thou decline to the right hand nor to the left hand.€€ƒxŞBut he that will be proud, and refuse to obey the commandment of the priest, who ministereth at that time to the Lord thy God, and the decree of the judge, that man shall die, and thou shalt take away the evil from Israel:€€ƒxRAnd all the people hearing it shall fear, that no one afterwards swell with pride.€€ƒx¯When thou art come into the land, which the Lord thy God will give thee, and possessest it, and shalt say: I will set a king over me, as all nations have that are round about:€€ƒx£Thou shalt set him whom the Lord thy God shall choose out of the number of thy brethren. Thou mayst not make a man of another nation king, that is not thy brother.€€ƒxåAnd when he is made king, he shall not multiply horses to himself, nor lead back the people into Egypt, being lifted up with the number of his horsemen, especially since the Lord hath commanded you to return no more the same way.€€ƒx\He shall not have many wives, that may allure his mind, nor immense sums of silver and gold.€€ƒx±But after he is raised to the throne of his kingdom, he shall copy out to himself the Deuteronomy of this law in a volume, taking the copy of the priests of the Levitical tribe,€€ƒx¸And he shall have it with him, and shall read it all the days of his life, that he may learn to fear the Lord his God, and keep his words and ceremonies, that are commanded in the law;€€ƒx¢And that his heart be not lifted up with pride over his brethren, nor decline to the right or to the left, that he and his sons may reign a long time over Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¾The priests and Levites, and all that are of the same tribe, shall have no part nor inheritance with the rest of Israel, because they shall eat the sacrifices of the Lord, and his oblations,€€ƒxŒAnd they shall receive nothing else of the possession of their brethren: for the Lord himself is their inheritance, as he hath said to them.€€ƒx¶This shall be the priest's due from the people, and from them that offer victims: whether they sacrifice an ox, or a sheep, they shall give to the priest the shoulder and the breast:€€ƒxkThe firstfruits also of corn, of wine, and of oil, and a part of the wool from the shearing of their sheep.€€ƒx„For the Lord thy God hath chosen him of all thy tribes, to stand and to minister to the name of the Lord, him and his sons for ever.€€–ƒx…He shall minister in the name of the Lord his God, as all his brethren the Levites do, that shall stand at that time before the Lord.€€ƒxHe shall receive the same portion of food that the rest do: besides that which is due to him in his own city, by succession from his fathers.€€ƒx“When thou art come into the land which the Lord thy God shall give thee, beware lest thou have a mind to imitate the abominations of those nations.€€ƒxÚNeither let there be found among you any one that shall expiate his son or daughter, making them to pass through the fire: or that consulteth soothsayers, or observeth dreams and omens, neither let there be any wizard,€€ƒxwNor charmer, nor any one that consulteth pythonic spirits, or fortune tellers, or that seeketh the truth from the dead.€€ƒxgFor the Lord abhorreth all these things, and for these abominations he will destroy them at thy coming.€€ƒx@Thou shalt be perfect, and without spot before the Lord thy God.€€ƒx‰These nations, whose land thou shalt possess, hearken to soothsayers and diviners: but thou art otherwise instructed by the Lord thy God.€€ƒxuThe Lord thy God will raise up to thee a PROPHET of thy nation and of thy brethren like unto me: him thou shalt hear:€€ƒxŞAs thou desiredst of the Lord thy God in Horeb, when the assembly was gathered together, and saidst: Let me not hear any more the voice of the Lord my God, neither let me see any more this exceeding great fire, lest I die.€€ƒx:And the Lord said to me: They have spoken all things well.€€ƒx®I will raise them up a prophet out of the midst of their brethren like to thee: and I will put my words in his mouth, and he shall speak to them all that I shall command him.€€ƒx]And he that will not hear his words, which he shall speak in my name, I will be the revenger.€€ƒx¡But the prophet, who being corrupted with pride, shall speak in my name things that I did not command him to say, or in the name of strange gods, shall be slain.€€ƒx^And if in silent thought thou answer: How shall I know the word that the Lord hath not spoken?€€ƒxüThou shalt have this sign: Whatsoever that same prophet foretelleth in the name of the Lord, and it cometh not to pass: that thing the Lord hath not spoken, but the prophet hath forged it by the pride of his mind: and therefore thou shalt not fear him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¢When the Lord thy God hath destroyed the nations, whose land he will deliver to thee, and thou shalt possess it, and shalt dwell in the cities and houses thereof:€€ƒxoThou shalt separate to thee three cities in the midst of the land, which the Lord will give thee in possession,€€ƒxÂPaving diligently the way: and thou shalt divide the whole province of thy land equally into three parts: that he who is forced to flee for manslaughter, may have near at hand whither to escape.€€ƒxÆThis shall be the law of the slayer that fleeth, whose life is to be saved: He that killeth his neighbor ignorantly, and who is proved to have had no hatred against him yesterday and the day before:€€ƒxñBut to have gone with him to the wood to hew wood, and in cutting down the tree the axe slipped out of his hand, and the iron slipping from the handle struck his friend, and killed him: he shall flee to one of the cities aforesaid, and live:€€•ƒx]Therefore I command thee, that thou separate three cities at equal distance one from another.€€ƒxAnd when the Lord thy God shall have enlarged thy borders, as he swore to the fathers, and shall give thee all the land that he promised them,€€ƒy(Yet so, if thou keep his commandments, and do the things which I command thee this day, that thou love the Lord thy God, and walk in his ways at all times) thou shalt add to thee other three cities, and shalt double the number of the three cities aforesaid:€€ƒxŒThat innocent blood may not be shed in the midst of the land which the Lord thy God will give thee to possess, lest thou be guilty of blood.€€ƒxBut if any man hating his neighbour, lie in wait for his life, and rise and strike him, and he die, and he flee to one of the cities aforesaid,€€ƒx¯The ancients of his city shall send, and take him out of the place of refuge, and shall deliver him into the hand of the kinsman of him whose blood was shed, and he shall die.€€ƒx{Thou shalt not pity him, and thou shalt take away the guilt of innocent blood out of Israel, that it may be well with thee.€€ƒxÁThou shalt not take nor remove thy neighbour's landmark, which thy predecessors have set in thy possession, which the Lord thy God will give thee in the land that thou shalt receive to possess.€€ƒx–One witness shall not rise up against any man, whatsoever the sin or wickedness be: but in the mouth of two or three witnesses every word shall stand.€€ƒxFIf a lying witness stand against a man, accusing him of transgression,€€ƒx’Both of them, between whom the controversy is, shall stand before the Lord in the sight of the priests and the judges that shall be in those days.€€ƒxuAnd when after most diligent inquisition, they shall find that the false witness hath told a lie against his brother:€€ƒxvThey shall render to him as he meant to do to his brother, and thou shalt take away the evil out of the midst of thee:€€ƒxAThat others hearing may fear, and may not dare to do such things.€€ƒxuThou shalt not pity him, but shalt require life for life, eye for eye, tooth for tooth, hand for hand, foot for foot.€€ƒ`€€ƒxíIf thou go out to war against thy enemies, and see horsemen and chariots, and the numbers of the enemy's army greater than thine, thou shalt not fear them: because the Lord thy God is with thee, who brought thee out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒxyAnd when the battle is now at hand, the priest shall stand before the army, and shall speak to the people in this manner:€€ƒx‘Hear, O Israel, you join battle this day against your enemies, let not your heart be dismayed, be not afraid, do not give back, fear ye them not:€€ƒxzBecause the Lord your God is in the midst of you, and will fight for you against your enemies, to deliver you from danger.€€ƒxõAnd the captains shall proclaim through every band in the hearing of the army: What man is there, that hath built a new house, and hath not dedicated it? let him go and return to his house, lest he die in the battle, and another man dedicate it.€€”ƒx—What man is there, that hath espoused a wife, and not taken her? let him go, and return to his house, lest he die in the war, and another man take her.€€ƒy	After these things are declared they shall add the rest, and shall speak to the people: What man is there that is fearful, and faint hearted? let him go, and return to his house, lest he make the hearts of his brethren to fear, as he himself is possessed with fear.€€ƒx‰And when the captains of the army shall hold their peace, and have made an end of speaking, every man shall prepare their bands to fight.€€ƒxRIf at any time thou come to fight against a city, thou shalt first offer it peace.€€ƒx…If they receive it, and open the gates to thee, all the people that are therein, shall be saved, and shall serve thee paying tribute.€€ƒxYBut if they will not make peace, and shall begin war against thee, thou shalt besiege it.€€ƒxŒAnd when the Lord thy God shall deliver it into thy hands, thou shalt slay all that are therein of the male sex, with the edge of the sword,€€ƒxÒExcepting women and children, cattle and other things, that are in the city. And thou shalt divide all the prey to the army, and thou shalt eat the spoils of thy enemies, which the Lord thy God shall give thee.€€ƒxŠSo shalt thou do to all cities that are at a great distance from thee, and are not of these cities which thou shalt receive in possession.€€ƒxTBut of those cities that shall be given thee, thou shalt suffer none at all to live:€€ƒxÅBut shalt kill them with the edge of the sword, to wit, the Hethite, and the Amorrhite, and the Chanaanite, the Pherezite, and the Hevite, and the Jebusite, as the Lord thy God hath commanded thee:€€ƒx€Lest they teach you to do all the abominations which they have done to their gods: and you should sin against the Lord your God.€€ƒy5When thou hast besieged a city a long time, and hath compassed it with bulwarks, to take it, thou shalt not cut down the trees that may be eaten of, neither shalt thou spoil the country round about with axes: for it is a tree, and not a man, neither can it increase the number of them that fight against thee.€€ƒxªBut if there be any trees that are not fruitful, but wild, and fit for other uses, cut them down, and make engines, until thou take the city, which fighteth against thee.€€ƒ`€€ƒx™When there shall be found in the land, which the Lord thy God will give thee, the corpse of a man slain, and it is not known who is guilty of the murder,€€ƒxƒThy ancients and judges shall go out, and shall measure from the place where the body lieth the distance of every city round about:€€ƒx®And the ancients of that city which they shall perceive to be nearer than the rest, shall take a heifer of the herd, that hath not drawn in the yoke, nor ploughed the ground,€€ƒx’And they shall bring her into a rough and stony valley, that never was ploughed, nor sown: and there they shall strike off the head of the heifer:€€ƒxèAnd the priests the sons of Levi shall come, whom the Lord thy God hath chosen to minister to him, and to bless in his name, and that by their word every matter should be decided, and whatsoever is clean or unclean should be judged.€€—ƒxJAnd shall say: Our hands did not shed this blood, nor did our eyes see it.€€ƒxÂBe merciful to thy people Israel, whom thou hast redeemed, O Lord, and lay not innocent blood to their charge, in the midst of thy people Israel. And the guilt of blood shall be taken from them:€€ƒx}And thou shalt be free from the innocent's blood, that was shed, when thou shalt have done what the Lord hath commanded thee.€€ƒxIf thou go out to fight against thy enemies, and the Lord thy God deliver them into thy hand, and thou lead them away captives,€€ƒxeAnd seest in the number of the captives a beautiful woman, and lovest her, and wilt have her to wife,€€ƒxVThou shalt bring her into thy house: and she shall shave her hair, and pare her nails,€€ƒxâAnd shall put off the raiment, wherein she was taken: and shall remain in thy house, and mourn for her father and mother one month: and after that thou shalt go in unto her, and shalt sleep with her, and she shall be thy wife.€€ƒx But if afterwards she please thee not, thou shalt let her go free, but thou mayst not sell her for money nor oppress her by might because thou hast humbled her.€€ƒxˆIf a man have two wives, one beloved, and the other hated, and they have had children by him, and the son of the hated be the firstborn,€€ƒx˜And he meaneth to divide his substance among his sons: he may not make the son of the beloved the firstborn, and prefer him before the son of the hated.€€ƒxÅBut he shall acknowledge the son of the hated for the firstborn, and shall give him a double portion of all he hath: for this is the first of his children, and to him are due the first birthrights.€€ƒxIf a man have a stubborn and unruly son, who will not hear the commandments of his father or mother, and being corrected, slighteth obedience:€€ƒx[They shall take him and bring him to the ancients of the city, and to the gate of judgment,€€ƒx¨And shall say to them: This our son is rebellious and stubborn, he slighteth hearing our admonitions, he giveth himself to revelling, and to debauchery and banquetings:€€ƒx›The people of the city shall stone him: and he shall die, that you may take away the evil out of the midst of you, and all Israel hearing it may be afraid.€€ƒx~When a man hath committed a crime for which he is to be punished with death, and being condemned to die is hanged on a gibbet:€€ƒxÖHis body shall not remain upon the tree, but shall be buried the same day: for he is accursed of God that hangeth on a tree: and thou shalt not defile thy land, which the Lord thy God shall give thee in possession.€€ƒ`€€ƒx}Thou shalt not pass by if thou seest thy brother's ox, or his sheep go astray: but thou shalt bring them back to thy brother.€€ƒx¤And if thy brother be not nigh, or thou know him not: thou shalt bring them to thy house, and they shall be with thee until thy brother seek them, and receive them.€€ƒxµThou shalt do in like manner with his ass, and with his raiment, and with every thing that is thy brother's, which is lost: if thou find it, neglect it not as pertaining to another.€€ƒx~If thou see thy brother's ass or his ox to be fallen down in the way, thou shalt not slight it, but shalt lift it up with him.€€ƒx‘A woman shall not be clothed with man's apparel, neither shall a man use woman's apparel: for he that doth these things is abominable before God.€€˜ƒxBut shalt let her go, keeping the young which thou hast caught: that it may be well with thee, and thou mayst live a long time.€€ƒx³When thou buildest a new house, thou shalt make a battlement to the roof round about: lest blood be shed in thy house, and thou be guilty, if any one slip, and fall down headlong.€€ƒx’Thou shalt not sow thy vineyard with divers seeds: lest both the seed which thou hast sown, and the fruit of the vineyard, be sanctified together.€€ƒx5Thou shalt not plough with an ox and an ass together.€€ƒxJThou shalt not wear a garment that is woven of woollen and linen together.€€ƒxeThou shalt make strings in the hem at the four corners of thy cloak, wherewith thou shalt be covered.€€ƒx/If a man marry a wife, and afterwards hate her,€€ƒxœAnd seek occasions to put her away, laying to her charge a very ill name, and say: I took this woman to wife, and going in to her, I found her not a virgin:€€ƒxHer father and mother shall take her, and shall bring with them the tokens of her virginity to the ancients of the city that are in the gate:€€ƒx^And the father shall say: I gave my daughter unto this man to wife: and because he hateth her,€€ƒxÖHe layeth to her charge a very ill name, so as to say: I found not thy daughter a virgin: and behold these are the tokens of my daughter's virginity. And they shall spread the cloth before the ancients of the city:€€ƒx@And the ancients of that city shall take that man, and beat him,€€ƒxòCondemning him besides in a hundred sicles of silver, which he shall give to the damsel's father, because he hath defamed by a very ill name a virgin of Israel: and he shall have her to wife, and may not put her away all the days of his life.€€ƒxRBut if what he charged her with be true, and virginity be not found in the damsel:€€ƒyThey shall cast her out of the doors of her father's house, and the men of the city shall stone her to death, and she shall die: because she hath done a wicked thing in Israel, to play the whore in her father's house: and thou shalt take away the evil out of the midst of thee.€€ƒxIf a man lie with another man's wife, they shall both die, that is to say, the adulterer and the adulteress: and thou shalt take away the evil out of Israel.€€ƒxfIf a man have espoused a damsel that is a virgin, and some one find her in the city, and lie with her,€€ƒxıThou shalt bring them both out to the gate of that city, and they shall be stoned: the damsel, because she cried not out, being in the city: the man, because he hath humbled his neighbour's wife. And thou shalt take away the evil from the midst of thee.€€ƒxuBut if a man find a damsel that is betrothed, in the field, and taking hold of her, lie with her, he alone shall die:€€ƒx¥The damsel shall suffer nothing, neither is she guilty of death: for as a robber riseth against his brother, and taketh away his life, so also did the damsel suffer:€€ƒxHShe was alone in the field: she cried, and there was no man to help her.€€ƒx|If a man find a damsel that is a virgin, who is not espoused, and taking her, lie with her, and the matter come to judgment:€€ƒx¼He that lay with her shall give to the father of the maid fifty sicles of silver, and shall have her to wife, because he hath humbled her: he may not put her away all the days of his life.€€ƒx=No man shall take his father's wife, nor remove his covering.€€ƒ`€€ƒxpAn eunuch, whose testicles are broken or cut away, or yard cut off, shall not enter into the church of the Lord.€€ƒx|A mamzer, that is to say, one born of a prostitute, shall not enter into the church of the Lord, until the tenth generation.€€ƒxsThe Ammonite and the Moabite, even after the tenth generation shall not enter into the church of the Lord for ever:€€ƒxÃBecause they would not meet you with bread and water in the way, when you came out of Egypt: and because they hired against thee Balaam, the son of Beor, from Mesopotamia in Syria, to curse thee.€€ƒxoAnd the Lord thy God would not hear Balaam, and he turned his cursing into thy blessing, because he loved thee.€€˜ƒxxThou shalt not abhor the Edomite, because he is thy brother: nor the Egyptian, because thou wast a stranger in his land.€€ƒx\They that are born of them, in the third generation shall enter into the church of the Lord.€€ƒx^When thou goest out to war against thy enemies, thou shalt keep thyself from every evil thing.€€ƒxfIf there be among you any man, that is defiled in a dream by night, he shall go forth out of the camp,€€ƒxtAnd shall not return, before he be washed with water in the evening: and after sunset he shall return into the camp.€€ƒx_Thou shalt have a place without the camp, to which thou mayst go for the necessities of nature,€€ƒx‹Carrying a paddle at thy girdle. And when thou sittest down, thou shalt dig round about, and with the earth that is dug up thou shalt cover€€ƒxçThat which thou art eased of: (for the Lord thy God walketh in the midst of thy camp, to deliver thee, and to give up thy enemies to thee:) and let thy camp be holy, and let no uncleanness appear therein, lest he go away from thee.€€ƒxFThou shalt not deliver to his master the servant that is fled to thee.€€ƒxvHe shall dwell with thee in the place that shall please him, and shall rest in one of thy cities: give him no trouble.€€ƒx`There shall be no whore among the daughters of Israel, nor whoremonger among the sons of Israel.€€ƒxÈThou shalt not offer the hire of a strumpet, nor the price of a dog, in the house of the Lord thy God, whatsoever it be that thou hast vowed: because both these are an abomination to the Lord thy God.€€ƒxQThou shalt not lend to thy brother money to usury, nor corn, nor any other thing:€€ƒx¿But to the stranger. To thy brother thou shalt lend that which he wanteth, without usury: that the Lord thy God may bless thee in all thy works in the land, which thou shalt go in to possess.€€ƒx²When thou hast made a vow to the Lord thy God, thou shalt not delay to pay it: because the Lord thy God will require it. And if thou delay, it shall be imputed to thee for a sin.€€ƒx4If thou wilt not promise, that shalt be without sin.€€ƒx²But that which is once gone out of thy lips, thou shalt observe, and shalt do as thou hast promised to the Lord thy God, and hast spoken with thy own will and with thy own mouth.€€ƒxwGoing into thy neighbour's vineyard, thou mayst eat as many grapes as thou pleasest: but must carry none out with thee:€€ƒxxIf thou go into thy friend's corn, thou mayst break the ears, and rub them in thy hand: but not reap them with a sickle.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¾If a man take a wife, and have her, and she find not favour in his eyes, for some uncleanness: he shall write a bill of divorce, and shall give it in her hand, and send her out of his house.€€ƒx7And when she is departed, and marrieth another husband,€€ƒxlAnd he also hateth her, and hath given her a bill of divorce, and hath sent her out of his house or is dead:€€ƒxÇThe former husband cannot take her again to wife: because she is defiled, and is become abominable before the Lord: lest thou cause thy land to sin, which the Lord thy God shall give thee to possess.€€ƒxÏWhen a man hath lately taken a wife, he shall not go out to war, neither shall any public business be enjoined him, but he shall be free at home without fault, that for one year he may rejoice with his wife.€€–ƒx½If any man be found soliciting his brother of the children of Israel, and selling him shall take a price, he shall be put to death, and thou shalt take away the evil from the midst of thee.€€ƒxÙObserve diligently that thou incur not the stroke of the leprosy, but thou shalt do whatsoever the priests of the Levitical race shall teach thee, according to what I have commanded them, and fulfil thou it carefully.€€ƒxSRemember what the Lord your God did to Mary, in the way when you came out of Egypt.€€ƒx}When thou shalt demand of thy neighbour any thing that he oweth thee, thou shalt not go into his house to take away a pledge:€€ƒxJBut thou shalt stand without, and he shall bring out to thee what he hath.€€ƒxCBut if he be poor, the pledge shall not lodge with thee that night,€€ƒx¶But thou shalt restore it to him presently before the going down of the sun: that he may sleep in his own raiment and bless thee, and thou mayst have justice before the Lord thy God.€€ƒx¡Thou shalt not refuse the hire of the needy, and the poor, whether he be thy brother, or a stranger that dwelleth with thee in the land, and is within thy gates:€€ƒxßBut thou shalt pay him the price of his labour the same day, before the going down of the sun, because he is poor, and with it maintaineth his life: lest he cry against thee to the Lord, and it be reputed to thee for a sin.€€ƒx‚The fathers shall not be put to death for the children, nor the children for the fathers, but every one shall die for his own sin,€€ƒx‰Thou shalt not pervert the judgment of the stranger nor of the fatherless, neither shalt thou take away the widow's raiment for a pledge.€€ƒx…Remember that thou wast a slave in Egypt, and the Lord thy God delivered thee from thence. Therefore I command thee to do this thing.€€ƒyWhen thou hast reaped the corn in thy field, and hast forgot and left a sheaf, thou shalt not return to take it away: but thou shalt suffer the stranger, and the fatherless and the widow to take it away: that the Lord thy God may bless thee in all the works of thy hands.€€ƒx¾If thou have gathered the fruit of thy olive trees, thou shalt not return to gather whatsoever remaineth on the trees: but shalt leave it for the stranger, for the fatherless, and the widow.€€ƒxœIf thou make the vintage of thy vineyard, thou shalt not gather the clusters that remain, but they shall be for the stranger, the fatherless, and the widow.€€ƒx_Remember that thou also wast a bondman in Egypt, and therefore I command thee to do this thing.€€ƒ`€€ƒx×If there be a controversy between men, and they call upon the judges: they shall give the prize of justice to him whom they perceive to be just: and him whom they find to be wicked, they shall condemn of wickedness.€€ƒxÌAnd if they see that the offender be worthy of stripes: they shall lay him down, and shall cause him to be beaten before them. According to the measure of the sin shall the measure also of the stripes be:€€ƒxjYet so, that they exceed not the number of forty: lest thy brother depart shamefully torn before thy eyes.€€ƒxEThou shalt not muzzle the ox that treadeth out thy corn on the floor.€€ƒx½When brethren dwell together, and one of them dieth without children, the wife of the deceased shall not marry to another: but his brother shall take her, and raise up seed for his brother:€€“ƒxÿBut if he will not take his brother's wife, who by law belongeth to him, the woman shall go to the gate of the city, and call upon the ancients, and say: My husband's brother refuseth to raise up his brother's name in Israel: and will not take me to wife.€€ƒxpAnd they shall cause him to be sent for forthwith, and shall ask him. If he answer: I will not take her to wife:€€ƒxÅThe woman shall come to him before the ancients, and shall take off his shoe from his foot, and spit in his face, and say: So shall it be done to the man that will not build up his brother's house:€€ƒx@And his name shall be called in Israel, the house of the unshod.€€ƒxÕIf two men have words together, and one begin to fight against the other, and the other's wife willing to deliver her husband out of the hand of the stronger, shall put forth her hand, and take him by the secrets,€€ƒxUThou shalt cut off her hand, neither shalt thou be moved with any pity in her regard.€€ƒxDThou shalt not have divers weights in thy bag, a greater and a less:€€ƒx@Neither shall there be in thy house a greater bushel and a less.€€ƒx©Thou shalt have a just and a true weight, and thy bushel shall be equal and true: that thou mayest live a long time upon the land which the Lord thy God shall give thee.€€ƒxWFor the Lord thy God abhorreth him that doth these things, and he hateth all injustice.€€ƒxJRemember what Amalec did to thee in the way when thou camest out of Egypt:€€ƒx’How he met thee: and slew the hindmost of the army, who sat down, being weary, when thou wast spent with hunger and labour, and he feared not God.€€ƒxÙTherefore when the Lord thy God shall give thee rest, and shall have subdued all the nations round about in the land which he hath promised thee: thou shalt blot out his name from under heaven. See thou forget it not.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAnd when thou art come into the land which the Lord thy God will give thee to possess, and hast conquered it, and dwellest in it:€€ƒx«Thou shalt take the first of all thy fruits, and put them in a basket, and shalt go to the place which the Lord thy God shall choose, that his name may be invocated there:€€ƒxÓAnd thou shalt go to the priest that shall be in those days, and say to him: I profess this day before the Lord thy God, that I am come into the land, for which he swore to our fathers, that he would give it us.€€ƒx`And the priest taking the basket at thy hand, shall set it before the altar of the Lord thy God:€€ƒxåAnd thou shalt speak thus in the sight of the Lord thy God: The Syrian pursued my father, who went down into Egypt, and sojourned there in a very small number, and grew into a nation great and strong and of an infinite multitude.€€“ƒxyAnd we cried to the Lord God of our fathers: who heard us, and looked down upon our affliction, and labour, and distress:€€ƒxsAnd brought us out of Egypt with a strong hand, and a stretched out arm, with great terror, with signs and wonders:€€ƒxRAnd brought us into this place, and gave us this land flowing with milk and honey.€€ƒx©And therefore now I offer the firstfruits of the land which the Lord hath given me. And thou shalt leave them in the sight of the Lord thy God, adoring the Lord thy God.€€ƒx›And thou shalt feast in all the good things which the Lord thy God hath given thee, and thy house, thou and the Levite, and the stranger that is with thee.€€ƒxäWhen thou hast made an end of tithing all thy fruits, in the third year of tithes thou shalt give it to the Levite, and to the stranger, and to the fatherless, and to the widow, that they may eat within thy gates, and be filled:€€ƒy4And thou shalt speak thus in the sight of the Lord thy God: I have taken that which was sanctified out of my house, and I have given it to the Levite, and to the stranger, and to the fatherless, and to the widow, as thou hast commanded me: I have not transgressed thy commandments nor forgotten thy precepts.€€ƒxÙI have not eaten of them in my mourning, nor separated them for any uncleanness, nor spent any thing of them in funerals. I have obeyed the voice of the Lord my God, and have done all things as thou hast commanded me.€€ƒxÇLook from thy sanctuary, and thy high habitation of heaven, and bless thy people Israel, and the land which thou hast given us, as thou didst swear to our fathers, a land flowing with milk and honey.€€ƒxœThis day the Lord thy God hath commanded thee to do these commandments and judgments: and to keep and fulfil them with all thy heart, and with all thy soul.€€ƒx•Thou hast chosen the Lord this day to be thy God, and to walk in his ways and keep his ceremonies, and precepts, and judgments, and obey his command.€€ƒxAnd the Lord hath chosen thee this day, to be his peculiar people, as he hath spoken to thee, and to keep all his commandments:€€ƒx°And to make thee higher than all nations which he hath created, to his own praise, and name, and glory: that thou mayst be a holy people of the Lord thy God, as he hath spoken.€€ƒ`€€ƒxwAnd Moses with the ancients of Israel commanded the people, saying: Keep every commandment that I command you this day.€€ƒx¦And when you are passed over the Jordan into the land which the Lord thy God will give thee, thou shalt set up great stones, and shalt plaster them over with plaster,€€ƒxéThat thou mayst write on them all the words of this law, when thou art passed over the Jordan: that thou mayst enter into the land which the Lord thy God will give thee, a land flowing with milk and honey, as he swore to thy fathers.€€ƒx˜Therefore when you are passed over the Jordan, set up the stones which I command you this day, in mount Hebal, and thou shalt plaster them with plaster:€€ƒx_And thou shalt build there an altar to the Lord thy God, of stones which iron hath not touched,€€˜ƒxSAnd shalt immolate peace victims, and eat there, and feast before the Lord thy God.€€ƒxSAnd thou shalt write upon the stones all the words of this law plainly and clearly.€€ƒx”And Moses and the priests of the race of Levi said to all Israel: Attend, and hear, O Israel: This day thou art made the people of the Lord thy God:€€ƒxUThou shalt hear his voice, and do the commandments and justices which I command thee.€€ƒx3And Moses commanded the people in that day, saying:€€ƒxThese shall stand upon mount Garizim to bless the people, when you are passed the Jordan: Simeon, Levi, Juda, Issachar, Joseph, and Benjamin.€€ƒxpAnd over against them shall stand on mount Hebal to curse: Ruben, Gad, and Aser, and Zabulon, Dan, and Nephtali.€€ƒxTAnd the Levites shall pronounce, and say to all the men of Israel with a loud voice:€€ƒxÍCursed be the man that maketh a graven and molten thing, the abomination of the Lord, the work of the hands of artificers, and shall put it in a secret place: and all the people shall answer and say: Amen.€€ƒxZCursed be he that honoureth not his father and mother: and all the people shall say: Amen.€€ƒxYCursed be he that removeth his neighbour's landmarks: and all the people shall say: Amen.€€ƒx`Cursed be he that maketh the blind to wander out of his way: and all the people shall say: Amen.€€ƒxCursed be he that perverteth the judgment of the stranger, of the fatherless and the widow: and all the people shall say: Amen.€€ƒxkCursed be he that lieth with his father's wife, and uncovereth his bed: and all the people shall say: Amen.€€ƒxKCursed be he that lieth with any beast: and all the people shall say: Amen.€€ƒxzCursed be he that lieth with his sister, the daughter of his father, or of his mother: and all the people shall say: Amen.€€ƒxSCursed be he that lieth with his mother-in-law: and all the people shall say: Amen.€€ƒxUCursed be he that secretly killeth his neighbour: and all the people shall say: Amen.€€ƒx_Cursed be he that taketh gifts, to slay an innocent person: and all the people shall say: Amen.€€ƒx|Cursed be he that abideth not in the words of this law, and fulfilleth them not in work: and all the people shall say: Amen.€€ƒ`€€ƒxªNow if thou wilt hear the voice of all his commandments, which I command thee this day, the Lord thy God will make thee higher than all the nations that are on the earth.€€ƒxaAnd all these blessings shall come upon thee and overtake thee: yet so if thou hear his precepts.€€ƒx<Blessed shalt thou be in the city, and blessed in the field.€€ƒx–Blessed shall be the fruit of thy womb, and the fruit of thy ground, and the fruit of thy cattle, the droves of thy herds, and the folds of thy sheep.€€ƒx2Blessed shall be thy barns and blessed thy stores.€€˜Dƒx°The Lord shall cause thy enemies, that rise up against thee, to fall down before thy face: one way shall they come out against thee, and seven ways shall they flee before thee.€€ƒx—The Lord will send forth a blessing upon thy storehouses, and upon all the works of thy hands: and will bless thee in the land that thou shalt receive.€€ƒx™The Lord will raise thee up to be a holy people to himself, as he swore to thee: if thou keep the commandments of the Lord thy God, and walk in his ways.€€ƒxuAnd all the people of the earth shall see that the name of the Lord is invocated upon thee, and they shall fear thee.€€ƒxÀThe Lord will make thee abound with all goods, with the fruit of thy womb, and the fruit of thy cattle, with the fruit of thy land, which the Lord swore to thy fathers that he would give thee.€€ƒxÏThe Lord will open his excellent treasure, the heaven, that it may give rain in due season: and he will bless all the works of thy hands. And thou shalt lend to many nations, and shalt not borrow of any one.€€ƒxÛAnd the Lord shall make thee the head and not the tail: and thou shalt be always above, and not beneath: yet so if thou wilt hear the commandments of the Lord thy God which I command thee this day, and keep and do them,€€ƒxrAnd turn not away from them neither to the right hand, nor to the left, nor follow strange gods, nor worship them.€€ƒxÈBut if thou wilt not hear the voice of the Lord thy God, to keep and to do all his commandments and ceremonies, which I command thee this day, all these curses shall come upon thee, and overtake thee.€€ƒx6Cursed shalt thou be in the city, cursed in the field.€€ƒx0Cursed shall be thy barn, and cursed thy stores.€€ƒxwCursed shall be the fruit of thy womb, and the fruit of thy ground, the herds of thy oxen, and the flocks of thy sheep.€€ƒx5Cursed shalt thou be coming in, and cursed going out.€€ƒxĞThe Lord shall send upon thee famine and hunger, and a rebuke upon all the works which thou shalt do: until he consume and destroy thee quickly, for thy most wicked inventions, by which thou hast forsaken me.€€ƒxtMay the Lord set the pestilence upon thee, until he consume thee out of the land, which thou shalt go in to possess.€€ƒx´May the Lord afflict thee with miserable want, with the fever and with cold, with burning and with heat, and with corrupted air and with blasting, and pursue thee till thou perish.€€ƒxUBe the heaven, that is over thee, of brass: and the ground thou treadest on, of iron.€€ƒxuThe Lord give thee dust for rain upon thy land, and let ashes come down from heaven upon thee, till thou be consumed.€€ƒx«The Lord make thee to fall down before thy enemies, one way mayst thou go out against them, and flee seven ways, and be scattered throughout all the kingdoms of the earth.€€ƒxxAnd be thy carcass meat for all the fowls of the air, and the beasts of the earth, and be there none to drive them away.€€ƒx©The Lord strike thee with the ulcer of Egypt, and the part of thy body, by which the dung is cast out, with the scab and with the itch: so that thou canst not be healed.€€ƒxAThe Lord strike thee with madness and blindness and fury of mind.€€ƒxßAnd mayst thou grope at midday as the blind is wont to grope in the dark, and not make straight thy ways. And mayst thou at all times suffer wrong, and be oppressed with violence, and mayst thou have no one to deliver thee.€€ƒx¥Mayst thou take a wife, and another sleep with her. Mayst thou build a house, and not dwell therein. Mayest thou plant a vineyard and not gather the vintage thereof.€€ƒxÇMay thy ox be slain before thee, and thou not eat thereof. May thy ass be taken away in thy sight, and not restored to thee. May thy sheep be given to thy enemies, and may there be none to help thee.€€ƒx«May thy sons and thy daughters be given to another people, thy eyes looking on, and languishing at the sight of them all the day, and may there be no strength in thy hand.€€ƒx›May a people which thou knowest not, eat the fruits of thy land, and all thy labours: and mayst thou always suffer oppression, and be crushed at all times.€€ƒxIAnd be astonished at the terror of those things which thy eyes shall see:€€ƒx•May the Lord strike thee with a very sore ulcer in the knees and in the legs, and be thou incurable from the sole of the foot to the top of the head.€€ƒx¾The Lord shall bring thee, and thy king, whom thou shalt have appointed over thee, into a nation which thou and thy fathers know not: and there thou shalt serve strange gods, wood and stone.€€ƒxiAnd thou shalt be lost, as a proverb and a byword to all people, among whom the Lord shall bring thee in.€€ƒxdThou shalt cast much seed into the ground, and gather little: because the locusts shall consume all.€€ƒx‹Thou shalt plant a vineyard, and dig it, and shalt not drink the wine, nor gather any thing thereof: because it shall be wasted with worms.€€ƒxThou shalt have olive trees in all thy borders, and shalt not be anointed with the oil: for the olives shall fall off and perish.€€ƒxhThou shalt beget sons and daughters, and shalt not enjoy them: because they shall be led into captivity.€€ƒxCThe blast shall consume all the trees and the fruits of thy ground.€€ƒxƒThe stranger that liveth with thee in the land, shall rise up over thee, and shall be higher: and thou shalt go down, and be lower.€€ƒxkHe shall lend to thee, and thou shalt not lend to him. He shall be as the head, and thou shalt be the tail.€€ƒxäAnd all these curses shall come upon thee, and shall pursue and overtake thee, till thou perish: because thou heardst not the voice of the Lord thy God, and didst not keep his commandments and ceremonies which he commanded thee.€€ƒxIAnd they shall be as signs and wonders on thee, and on thy seed for ever.€€ƒxnBecause thou didst not serve the Lord thy God with joy and gladness of heart, for the abundance of all things:€€ƒxÂThou shalt serve thy enemy, whom the Lord will send upon thee, in hunger, and thirst, and nakedness, and in want of all things: and he shall put an iron yoke upon thy neck, till he consume thee.€€ƒx¦The Lord will bring upon thee a nation from afar, and from the uttermost ends of the earth, like an eagle that flyeth swiftly, whose tongue thou canst not understand,€€ƒx^A most insolent nation, that will shew no regard to the ancients, nor have pity on the infant,€€ƒxÍAnd will devour the fruit of thy cattle, and the fruits of thy land: until thou be destroyed, and will leave thee no wheat, nor wine, nor oil, nor herds of oxen, nor flocks of sheep: until he destroy thee.€€ƒxØAnd consume thee in all thy cities, and thy strong and high wall be brought down, wherein thou trustedst in all thy land. Thou shalt be besieged within thy gates in all thy land which the Lord thy God will give thee:€€ƒxÇAnd thou shalt eat the fruit of thy womb, and the flesh of thy sons and of thy daughters, which the Lord thy God shall give thee, in the distress and extremity wherewith thy enemy shall oppress thee.€€ƒxuThe man that is nice among you, and very delicate, shall envy his own brother, and his wife, that lieth in his bosom,€€ƒxÇSo that he will not give them of the flesh of his children, which he shall eat: because he hath nothing else in the siege and the want, wherewith thy enemies shall distress thee within all thy gates.€€ƒx×The tender and delicate woman, that could not go upon the ground, nor set down her foot for over much niceness and tenderness, will envy her husband who lieth in her bosom, the flesh of her son, and of her daughter,€€ƒy	And the filth of the afterbirths, that come forth from between her thighs, and the children that are born the same hour. For they shall eat them secretly for the want of all things, in the siege and distress, wherewith thy enemy shall oppress thee within thy gates.€€ƒx¡If thou wilt not keep, and fulfil all the words of this law, that are written in this volume, and fear his glorious and terrible name: that is, The Lord thy God:€€ƒx€The Lord shall increase thy plagues, and the plagues of thy seed, plagues great and lasting, infirmities grievous and perpetual.€€ƒx{And he shall bring back on thee all the afflictions of Egypt, which thou wast afraid of, and they shall stick fast to thee.€€ƒxŠMoreover the Lord will bring upon thee all the diseases, and plagues, that are not written in the volume of this law till he consume thee:€€ƒx‘And you shall remain few in number, who before were as the stars of heaven for multitude, because thou heardst not the voice of the Lord thy God.€€ƒxÜAnd as the Lord rejoiced upon you before doing good to you, and multiplying you: so he shall rejoice destroying and bringing you to nought, so that you shall be taken away from the land which thou shalt go in to possess.€€ƒxÑThe Lord shall scatter thee among all people, from the farthest parts of the earth to the ends thereof: and there thou shalt serve strange gods, which both thou art ignorant of and thy fathers, wood and stone.€€ƒxÒNeither shalt thou be quiet, even in those nations, nor shall there be any rest for the sole of thy foot. For the Lord will give thee a fearful heart, and languishing eyes, and a soul consumed with pensiveness:€€ƒxwAnd thy life shall be as it were hanging before thee. Thou shalt fear night and day, neither shalt thou trust thy life.€€ƒxâIn the morning thou shalt say: Who will grant me evening? and at evening: Who will grant me morning? for the fearfulness of thy heart, wherewith thou shalt be terrified, and for those things which thou shalt see with thy eyes.€€ƒxàThe Lord shall bring thee again with ships into Egypt, by the way whereof he said to thee that thou shouldst see it no more. There shalt thou be set to sale to thy enemies for bondmen and bondwomen, and no man shall buy you.€€ƒ`€€ƒx²These are the words of the covenant which the Lord commanded Moses to make with the children of Israel in the land of Moab: beside that covenant which he made with them in Horeb.€€ƒx¸And Moses called all Israel, and said to them: You have seen all the things that the Lord did before you in the land of Egypt to Pharao, and to all his servants, and to his whole land.€€ƒxPThe great temptations, which thy eyes have seen, those mighty signs and wonders,€€ƒxvAnd the Lord hath not given you a heart to understand, and eyes to see, and ears that may hear, unto this present day.€€ƒx‰He hath brought you forty years through the desert: your garments are not worn out, neither are the shoes of your feet consumed with age.€€˜ƒx|And you came to this place: and Sehon king of Hesebon, and Og king of Basan, came out against us to fight. And we slew them.€€ƒxhAnd took their land, and delivered it for a possession to Ruben and Gad, and the half tribe of Manasses.€€ƒxdKeep therefore the words of this covenant, and fulfil them: that you may understand all that you do.€€ƒxYou all stand this day before the Lord your God, your princes, and tribes, and ancients, and doctors, all the people of Israel,€€ƒx‰Your children and your wives, and the stranger that abideth with thee in the camp, besides the hewers of wood, and them that bring water:€€ƒx{That thou mayst pass in the covenant of the Lord thy God, and in the oath which this day the Lord thy God maketh with thee.€€ƒx™That he may raise thee up a people to himself, and he may be thy God as he hath spoken to thee, and as he swore to thy fathers Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.€€ƒxGNeither with you only do I make this covenant, and confirm these oaths,€€ƒx2But with all that are present and that are absent.€€ƒx~For you know how we dwelt in the land of Egypt, and how we have passed through the midst of nations, and passing through them,€€ƒx€You have seen their abominations and filth, that is to say, their idols, wood and stone, silver and gold, which they worshipped.€€ƒxüLest perhaps there should be among you a man or a woman, a family or a tribe, whose heart is turned away this day from the Lord our God, to go and serve the gods of those nations: and there should be among you a root bringing forth gall and bitterness.€€ƒxÉAnd when he shall hear the words of this oath, he should bless himself in his heart saying: I shall have peace, and will walk on in the naughtiness of my heart: and the drunken may consume the thirsty,€€ƒyAnd the Lord should not forgive him: but his wrath and jealousy against that man should be exceedingly enkindled at that time, and all the curses that are written in this volume should light upon him: and the Lord should blot out his name from under heaven,€€ƒx‰And utterly destroy him out of all the tribes of Israel, according to the curses that are contained in the book of this law and covenant:€€ƒx×And the following generation shall say, and the children that shall be born hereafter, and the strangers that shall come from afar, seeing the plagues of that land and the evils wherewith the Lord hath afflicted it,€€ƒxûBurning it with brimstone, and the heat of salt, so that it cannot be sown any more, nor any green thing grow therein, after the example of the destruction of Sodom and Gomorrha, Adama and Seboim, which the Lord destroyed in his wrath and indignation:€€ƒx}And all the nations shall say: Why hath the Lord done thus to this land? what meaneth this exceeding great heat of his wrath?€€ƒx–And they shall answer: Because they forsook the covenant of the Lord, which he made with their fathers, when he brought them out of the land of Egypt:€€ƒxpAnd they have served strange gods, and adored them, whom they knew not, and for whom they had not been assigned:€€ƒxTherefore the wrath of the Lord was kindled against this land, to bring upon it all the curses that are written in this volume:€€ƒx And he hath cast them out of their land, in anger and in wrath, and in very great indignation, and hath thrown them into a strange land, as it is seen this day.€€ƒxŠSecret things to the Lord our God: things that are manifest, to us and to our children for ever, that we may do all the words of this law.€€ƒ`€€ƒxöNow when all these things shall be come upon thee, the blessing or the curse, which I have set forth before thee, and thou shalt be touched with repentance of thy heart among all the nations, into which the Lord thy God shall have scattered thee,€€ƒx‘And shalt return to him, and obey his commandments, as I command thee this day, thou and thy children, with all thy heart, and with all thy soul:€€ƒx¥The Lord thy God will bring back again thy captivity, and will have mercy on thee, and gather thee again out of all the nations, into which he scattered thee before.€€ƒxbIf thou be driven as far as the poles of heaven, the Lord thy God will fetch thee back from hence,€€ƒx½And will take thee to himself, and bring thee into the land which thy fathers possessed, and thou shalt possess it: and blessing thee, he will make thee more numerous than were thy fathers.€€”ƒx_And he will turn all these curses upon thy enemies, and upon them that hate and persecute thee.€€ƒxBut thou shalt return, and hear the voice of the Lord thy God, and shalt do all the commandments which I command thee this day:€€ƒy&And the Lord thy God will make thee abound in all the works of thy hands, in the fruit of thy womb, and in the fruit of thy cattle, in the fruitfulness of thy land, and in the plenty of all things. For the Lord will return to rejoice over thee in all good things, as he rejoiced in thy fathers:€€ƒxÁYet so if thou hear the voice of the Lord thy God, and keep his precepts and ceremonies, which are written in this law: and return to the Lord thy God with all thy heart, and with all thy soul.€€ƒxXThis commandment, that I command thee this day is not above thee, nor far off from thee:€€ƒxˆNor is it in heaven, that thou shouldst say: Which of us can go up to heaven to bring it unto us, and we may hear and fulfil it in work?€€ƒx©Nor is it beyond the sea: that thou mayst excuse thyself, and say: Which of us can cross the sea, and bring it unto us: that we may hear, and do that which is commanded?€€ƒxZBut the word is very nigh unto thee, in thy mouth and in thy heart, that thou mayst do it.€€ƒxbConsider that I have set before thee this day life and good, and on the other hand death and evil:€€ƒx³That thou mayst love the Lord thy God, and walk in his ways, and keep his commandments and ceremonies and judgments, and bless thee in the land, which thou shalt go in to possess.€€ƒxƒBut if thy heart be turned away, so that thou wilt not hear, and being deceived with error thou adore strange gods, and serve them:€€ƒx¨I foretell thee this day that thou shalt perish, and shalt remain but a short time in the land, to which thou shalt pass over the Jordan, and shalt go in to possess it.€€ƒxªI call heaven and earth to witness this day, that I have set before you life and death, blessing and cursing. Choose therefore life, that both thou and thy seed may live:€€ƒyAnd that thou mayst love the Lord thy God, and obey his voice, and adhere to him (for he is thy life, and the length of thy days,) that thou mayst dwell in the land, for which the Lord swore to thy fathers Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob that he would give it them.€€ƒ`€€ƒx8And Moses went, and spoke all these words to all Israel,€€ƒx¹And he said to them: I am this day a hundred and twenty years old, I can no longer go out and come in, especially as the Lord also hath said to me: Thou shalt not pass over this Jordan.€€ƒxÁThe Lord thy God then will pass over before thee: he will destroy all these nations in thy sight, and thou shalt possess them: and this Josue shall go over before thee, as the Lord hath spoken.€€ƒxAnd the Lord shall do to them as he did to Sehon and Og the kings of the Amorrhites, and to their land, and shall destroy them.€€ƒx|Therefore when the Lord shall have delivered these also to you, you shall do in like manner to them as I have commanded you.€€˜ƒxİAnd Moses called Josue, and said to him before all Israel: Take courage, and be valiant: for thou shalt bring this people into the land which the Lord swore he would give to their fathers, and thou shalt divide it by lot.€€ƒx‡And the Lord who is your leader, he himself will be with thee: he will not leave thee, nor forsake thee: fear not, neither be dismayed.€€ƒxŸAnd Moses wrote this law, and delivered it to the priests the sons of Levi, who carried the ark of the covenant of the Lord, and to all the ancients of Israel.€€ƒxhAnd he commanded them, saying: After seven years, in the year of remission, in the feast of tabernacles,€€ƒx¾When all Israel come together, to appear in the sight of the Lord thy God in the place which the Lord shall choose, thou shalt read the words of this law before all Israel, in their hearing.€€ƒxÜAnd the people being all assembled together, both men and women, children and strangers, that are within thy gates: that hearing they may learn, and fear the Lord your God, and keep, and fulfil all the words of this law:€€ƒx³That their children also, who now are ignorant, may hear, and fear the Lord their God, all the days that they live in the land whither you are going over the Jordan to possess it.€€ƒxãAnd the Lord said to Moses: Behold the days of thy death are nigh: call Josue, and stand ye in the tabernacle of the testimony, that I may give him a charge. So Moses and Josue went and stood in the tabernacle of the testimony:€€ƒxaAnd the Lord appeared there in the pillar of a cloud, which stood in the entry of the tabernacle.€€ƒyAnd the Lord said to Moses: Behold thou shalt sleep with thy fathers, and this people rising up will go a fornicating after strange gods in the land, to which it goeth in to dwell: there will they forsake me, and will make void the covenant, which I have made with them,€€ƒy'And my wrath shall be kindled against them in that day: and I will forsake them, and will hide my face from them, and they shall be devoured: all evils and afflictions shall find them, so that they shall say in that day: In truth it is because God is not with me, that these evils have found me.€€ƒx€But I will hide, and cover my face in that day, for all the evils which they have done, because they have followed strange gods.€€ƒxÉNow therefore write you this canticle, and teach the children of Israel: that they may know it by heart, and sing it by mouth, and this song may be unto me for a testimony among the children of Israel.€€ƒyFor I will bring them into the land, for which I swore to their fathers, that floweth with milk and honey. And when they have eaten, and are full and fat, they will turn away after strange gods, and will serve them: and will despise me, and make void my covenant.€€ƒy8And after many evils and afflictions shall have come upon them, this canticle shall answer them for a testimony, which no oblivion shall take away out of the mouth of their seed. For I know their thoughts, and what they are about to do this day, before that I bring them into the land which I have promised them.€€ƒxLMoses therefore wrote the canticle, and taught it to the children of Israel.€€ƒx¾And the Lord commanded Josue the son of Nun, and said: Take courage, and be valiant: for thou shalt bring the children of Israel into the land which I have promised, and I will be with thee.€€ƒxSTherefore after Moses had wrote the words of this law in a volume, and finished it:€€ƒxRHe commanded the Levites, who carried the ark of the covenant of the Lord, saying:€€ƒxŠTake this book, and put it in the side of the ark of the covenant of the Lord your God: that it may be there for a testimony against thee.€€ƒx¶For I know thy obstinacy, and thy most stiff neck. While I am yet living, and going in with you, you have always been rebellious against the Lord: how much more when I shall be dead?€€ƒx¨Gather unto me all the ancients of your tribes, and your doctors, and I will speak these words in their hearing, and will call heaven and earth to witness against them.€€ƒy	For I know that, after my death, you will do wickedly, and will quickly turn aside form the way that I have commanded you: and evils shall come upon you in the latter times, when you shall do evil in the sight of the Lord, to provoke him by the works of your hands.€€ƒxƒMoses therefore spoke, in the hearing of the whole assembly of Israel, the words of this canticle, and finished it even to the end.€€ƒ`€€ƒxXHear, O ye heavens, the things I speak, let the earth give ear to the words of my mouth.€€ƒx|Let my doctrine gather as the rain, let my speech distil as the dew, as a shower upon the herb, and as drops upon the grass.€€ƒxLBecause I will invoke the name of the Lord: give ye magnificence to our God.€€ƒx}The works of God are perfect, and all his ways are judgments: God is faithful and without any iniquity, he is just and right.€€ƒxuThey have sinned against him, and are none of his children in their filth: they are a wicked and perverse generation.€€˜4ƒx‡Remember the days of old, think upon every generation: ask thy father, and he will declare to thee: thy elders and they will tell thee.€€ƒx When the Most High divided the nations: when he separated the sons of Adam, he appointed the bounds of people according to the number of the children of Israel.€€ƒxGBut the Lord's portion is his people: Jacob the lot of his inheritance.€€ƒx—He found him in a desert land, in a place of horror, and of vast wilderness: he led him about, and taught him: and he kept him as the apple of his eye.€€ƒx‰As the eagle enticing her young to fly, and hovering over them, he spread his wings, and hath taken him and carried him on his shoulders.€€ƒxEThe Lord alone was his leader: and there was no strange god with him.€€ƒx’He set him upon high land: that he might eat the fruits of the fields, that he might suck honey out of the rock, and oil out of the hardest stone,€€ƒxºButter of the herd, and milk of the sheep with the fat of lambs, and of the rams of the breed of Basan: and goats with the marrow of wheat, and might drink the purest blood of the grape.€€ƒxƒThe beloved grew fat, and kicked: he grew fat, and thick and gross, he forsook God who made him, and departed from God his saviour.€€ƒxXThey provoked him by strange gods, and stirred him up to anger, with their abominations.€€ƒxThey sacrificed to devils and not to God: to gods whom they knew not: that were newly come up, whom their fathers worshipped not.€€ƒxZThou hast forsaken the God that begot thee, and hast forgotten the Lord that created thee.€€ƒxVThe Lord saw, and was moved to wrath: because his own sons and daughters provoked him.€€ƒx•And he said: I will hide my face from them, and will consider what their last end shall be: for it is a perverse generation, and unfaithful children.€€ƒx¹They have provoked me with that which was no god, and have angered me with their vanities: and I will provoke them with that which is no people, and will vex them with a foolish nation.€€ƒx¥A fire is kindled in my wrath, and shall burn even to the lowest hell: and shall devour the earth with her increase, and shall burn the foundations of the mountains.€€ƒxAI will heap evils upon them, and will spend my arrows among them.€€ƒxËThey shall be consumed with famine, and birds shall devour them with a most bitter bite: I will send the teeth of beasts upon them, with the fury of creatures that trail upon the ground, and of serpents.€€ƒx‡Without, the sword shall lay them waste, and terror within, both the young man and the virgin, the sucking child with the man in years.€€ƒxOI said: Where are they? I will make the memory of them to cease from among men.€€ƒx®But for the wrath of the enemies I have deferred it: lest perhaps their enemies might be proud, and should say: Our mighty hand, and not the Lord, hath done all these things.€€ƒx6They are a nation without counsel, and without wisdom.€€ƒxUO that they would be wise and would understand, and would provide for their last end.€€ƒxHow should one pursue after a thousand, and two chase ten thousand? Was it not, because their God had sold them, and the Lord had shut them up?€€ƒxDFor our God is not as their gods: our enemies themselves are judges.€€ƒxŠTheir vines are of the vineyard of Sodom, and of the suburbs of Gomorrha: their grapes are grapes of gall, and their clusters most bitter.€€ƒxMTheir wine is the gall of dragons, and the venom of asps, which is incurable.€€ƒxFAre not these things stored up with me, and sealed up in my treasures?€€ƒx“Revenge is mine, and I will repay them in due time, that their foot may slide: the day of destruction is at hand, and the time makes haste to come.€€ƒxÄThe Lord will judge his people, and will have mercy on his servants: he shall see that their hand is weakened, and that they who were shut up have also failed, and they that remained are consumed.€€ƒx=And he shall say: Where are their gods, in whom they trusted?€€ƒxOf whose victims they ate the fat, and drank the wine of their drink offerings: let them arise and help you, and protect you in your distress.€€ƒxµSee ye that I alone am, and there is no other God besides me: I will kill and I will make to live: I will strike, and I will heal, and there is none that can deliver out of my hand.€€ƒxBI will lift up my hand to heaven, and I will say: I live for ever.€€ƒx‘If I shall whet my sword as the lightning, and my hand take hold on judgment: I will render vengeance to my enemies, and repay them that hate me.€€ƒx™I will make my arrows drunk with blood, and my sword shall devour flesh, of the blood of the slain and of the captivity, of the bare head of the enemies.€€ƒx¬Praise his people, ye nations, for he will revenge the blood of his servants: and will render vengeance to their enemies, and he will be merciful to the land of his people.€€ƒxkSo Moses came and spoke all the words of this canticle in the ears of the people, and Josue the son of Nun.€€ƒx5And he ended all these words, speaking to all Israel.€€ƒxÂAnd he said to them: Set your hearts on all the words, which I testify to you this day: which you shall command your children to observe and to do, and to fulfil all that is written in this law:€€ƒxÁFor they are not commanded you in vain, but that every one should live in them, and that doing them you may continue a long time in the land whither you are going over the Jordan to possess it.€€ƒx1And the Lord spoke to Moses the same day, saying:€€ƒxùGo up into this mountain Abarim, (that is to say, of passages,) unto mount Nebo, which is in the land of Moab over against Jericho: and see the land of Chanaan, which I will deliver to the children of Israel to possess, and die thou in the mountain.€€ƒx‹When thou art gone up into it thou shalt be gathered to thy people, as Aaron thy brother died in mount Hor, and was gathered to his people:€€ƒxÂBecause you trespassed against me in the midst of the children of Israel, at the waters of contradiction, in Cades of the desert of Sin: and you did not sanctify me among the children of Israel.€€ƒxsThou shalt see the land before thee, which I will give to the children of Israel, but thou shalt not enter into it.€€ƒ`€€ƒxhThis is the blessing, wherewith the man of God, Moses, blessed the children of Israel, before his death.€€ƒx«And he said: The Lord came from Sinai, and from Seir he rose up to us: he hath appeared from mount Pharan, and with him thousands of saints. In his right hand a fiery law.€€ƒx|He hath loved the people, all the saints are in his hand: and they that approach to his feet, shall receive of his doctrine.€€ƒxDMoses commanded us a law, the inheritance of the multitude of Jacob.€€ƒxkHe shall be king with the most right, the princes of the people, being assembled with the tribes of Israel.€€˜ƒx¯This is the blessing of Juda. Hear, O Lord, the voice of Juda, and bring him in unto his people: his hands shall fight for him, and he shall be his helper against his enemies.€€ƒxTo Levi also he said: Thy perfection, and thy doctrine be to thy holy man, whom thou hast proved in the temptation, and judged at the waters of contradiction:€€ƒxÈWho hath said to his father, and to his mother: I do not know you; and to his brethren: I know you not: and their own children they have not known. These have kept thy word, and observed thy covenant,€€ƒxpThy judgments, O Jacob, and thy law, O Israel: they shall put incense in thy wrath and holocaust upon thy altar.€€ƒx†Bless, O Lord, his strength, and receive the works of his hands. Strike the backs of his enemies, and let not them that hate him rise.€€ƒxµAnd to Benjamin he said: The best beloved of the Lord shall dwell confidently in him: as in a bride chamber shall he abide all the day long, and between his shoulders shall be rest.€€ƒxTo Joseph also he said: Of the blessing of the Lord be his land, of the fruits of heaven, and of the dew, and of the deep that lieth beneath.€€ƒx7Of the fruits brought forth by the sun and by the moon.€€ƒxMOf the tops of the ancient mountains, of the fruits of the everlasting hills:€€ƒx¿And of the fruits of the earth, and of the fulness thereof. The blessing of him that appeared in the bush, come upon the head of Joseph, and upon the crown of the Nazarite among his brethren.€€ƒxàHis beauty as of the firstling of a bullock, his horns as the horns of a rhinoceros: with them shall he push the nations even to the ends of the earth. These are the multitudes of Ephraim and these the thousands of Manasses.€€ƒx^And to Zabulon he said: Rejoice, O Zabulon, in thy going out; and Issachar in thy tabernacles.€€ƒx¶They shall call the people to the mountain: there shall they sacrifice the victims of justice. Who shall suck as milk the abundance of the sea, and the hidden treasures of the sands.€€ƒx‚And to Gad he said: Blessed be Gad in his breadth: he hath rested as a lion, and hath seized upon the arm and the top of the head.€€ƒx±And he saw his pre-eminence, that in his portion the teacher was laid up: who was with the princes of the people, and did the justices of the Lord, and his judgment with Israel.€€ƒxOTo Dan also he said: Dan is a young lion, he shall flow plentifully from Basan.€€ƒxAnd To Nephtali he said: Nephtali shall enjoy abundance, and shall be full of the blessings of the Lord: he shall possess the sea and the south.€€ƒx€To Aser also he said: Let Aser be blessed with children, let him be acceptable to his brethren, and let him dip his foot in oil.€€ƒxYHis shoe shall be iron and brass. As the days of thy youth, so also shall thy old age be.€€ƒxœThere is no other god like the God of the rightest: he that is mounted upon the heaven is thy helper. By his magnificence the clouds run hither and thither.€€ƒx—His dwelling is above, and underneath are the everlasting arms: he shall cast out the enemy from before thee, and shall say: Be thou brought to nought.€€ƒx~Israel shall dwell in safety, and alone. The eye of Jacob in a land of corn and wine, and the heavens shall be misty with dew.€€ƒxÌBlessed art thou, Israel: who is like to thee, O people, that art saved by the Lord? the shield of thy help, and the sword of thy glory: thy enemies shall deny thee, and thou shalt tread upon their necks.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¤Then Moses went up from the plains of Moab upon mount Nebo, to the top of Phasga over against Jericho: and the Lord shewed him all the land of Galaad as far as Dan.€€ƒxjAnd all Nephtali, and the land of Ephraim and Manasses, and all the land of Juda unto the furthermost sea,€€ƒxcAnd the south part, and the breadth of the plain of Jericho the city of palm trees as far as Segor.€€ƒx¿And the Lord said to him: This is the land, for which I swore to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, saying: I will give it to thy seed. Thou hast seen it with thy eyes, and shalt not pass over to it.€€ƒxbAnd Moses the servant of the Lord died there, in the land of Moab, by the commandment of the Lord:€€ŒƒxiMoses was a hundred and twenty years old when he died: his eye was not dim, neither were his teeth moved.€€ƒx”And the children of Israel mourned for him in the plains of Moab thirty days: and the days of their mourning in which they mourned Moses were ended.€€ƒxµAnd Josue the son of Nun was filled with the spirit of wisdom, because Moses had laid his hands upon him. And the children of Israel obeyed him, and did as the Lord commanded Moses.€€ƒx]And there arose no more a prophet in Israel like unto Moses, whom the Lord knew face to face,€€ƒx‰In all the signs and wonders, which he sent by him, to do in the land of Egypt to Pharao, and to all his servants, and to his whole land,€€ƒxOAnd all the mighty hand, and great miracles, which Moses did before all Israel.€€ƒrThe Book of Joshua€€ƒ`€€ƒxNow it came to pass after the death of Moses, the servant of the Lord, that the Lord spoke to Joshua, the son of Nun, the minister of Moses, and said to him:€€ƒx•Moses my servant is dead: arise, and pass over this Jordan, thou and thy people with thee, into the land which I will give to the children of Israel.€€ƒxgI will deliver to you every place that the sole of your foot shall tread upon, as I have said to Moses.€€ƒxªFrom the desert, and from Libanus unto the great river Euphrates, all the land of the Hethites, unto the great sea toward the going down of the sun, shall be your border.€€dJosh¤dnamefJoshuaiosis_namedJoshlabbreviationdJoshhchapters˜’ƒxîTake courage therefore, and be very valiant: that thou mayst observe and do all the law, which Moses my servant hath commanded thee: turn not from it to the right hand or to the left, that thou mayst understand all things which thou dost.€€ƒxÕLet not the book of this law depart from thy mouth: but thou shalt meditate on it day and night, that thou mayst observe and do all things that are written in it: then shalt thou direct thy way, and understand it.€€ƒx£Behold I command thee, take courage, and be strong. Fear not, and be not dismayed: because the Lord thy God is with thee in all things whatsoever thou shalt go to.€€ƒx|And Joshua commanded the princes of the people, saying: Pass through the midst of the camp, and command the people, and say:€€ƒxšPrepare your victuals: for after the third day you shall pass over the Jordan, and shall go in to possess the land, which the Lord your God will give you.€€ƒxNAnd he said to the Rubenites, and the Gadites, and the half tribe of Manasses:€€ƒx‡Remember the word, which Moses the servant of the Lord commanded you, saying: The Lord your God hath given you rest, and all this land.€€ƒxÔYour wives, and children; and cattle, shall remain in the land which Moses gave you on this side of the Jordan: but pass you over armed before your brethren all of you that are strong of hand, and fight for them,€€ƒy:Until the Lord give rest to your brethren, as he hath given you, and they also possess the land which the Lord your God will give them: and so you shall return into the land of your possession, and you shall dwell in it, which Moses the servant of the Lord gave you beyond the Jordan, toward the rising of the sun.€€ƒx‰And they made answer to Joshua, and said: All that thou hast commanded us, we will do: and whither soever thou shalt send us, we will go.€€ƒxvAs we obeyed Moses in all things, so will we obey thee also: only be the Lord thy God with thee, as he was with Moses.€€ƒxHe that shall gainsay thy mouth, and not obey all thy words, that thou shalt command him, let him die: only take thou courage, and do manfully.€€ƒ`€€ƒxíAnd Joshua, the son of Nun, sent from Setim two men, to spy secretly: and said to them: Go, and view the land, and the city of Jericho. They went, and entered into the house of a woman that was a harlot, named Rahab, and lodged with her.€€ƒxAnd it was told the king of Jericho, and was said: Behold there are men come in hither, by night, of the children of Israel, to spy the land.€€ƒx¬And the king of Jericho sent to Rahab, saying: Bring forth the men that came to thee, and are entered into thy house: for they are spies, and are come to view all the land.€€ƒxmAnd the woman taking the men, hid them, and said: I confess they came to me, but I knew not whence they were:€€ƒx§And at the time of shutting the gate in the dark, they also went out together. I know not whither they are gone: pursue after them quickly, and you will overtake them.€€˜ƒx Now they that were sent, pursued after them, by the way that leadeth to the fords of the Jordan: and as soon as they were gone out, the gate was presently shut.€€ƒx[The men that were hid were not yet asleep, when behold the woman went up to them, and said:€€ƒx•I know that the Lord hath given this land to you: for the dread of you is fallen upon us, and all the inhabitants of the land have lost all strength.€€ƒxáWe have heard that the Lord dried up the water of the Red Sea, at your going in, when you came out of Egypt: and what things you did to the two kings of the Amorrhites, that were beyond the Jordan, Sehon and Og whom you slew.€€ƒxÙAnd at the hearing these things, we were affrighted, and our heart fainted away, neither did there remain any spirit in us, at your coming in: for the Lord your God he is God in heaven above, and in the earth beneath.€€ƒx›Now, therefore, swear ye to me by the Lord, that as I have shewed mercy to you, so you also will shew mercy to my father's house: and give me a true token.€€ƒxƒThat you will save my father and mother, my brethren and sisters, and all things that are theirs, and deliver our souls from death.€€ƒx¦They answered her: Be our lives for you unto death, only if thou betray us not. And when the Lord shall have delivered us the land, we will shew thee mercy and truth.€€ƒx[Then she let them down with a cord out of a window: for her house joined close to the wall.€€ƒx³And she said to them: Get ye up to the mountains, lest perhaps they meet you as they return: and there lie ye hid three days, till they come back, and so you shall go on your way.€€ƒxXAnd they said to her: We shall be blameless of this oath, which thou hast made us swear,€€ƒxØIf, when we come into the land, this scarlet cord be a sign, and thou tie it in the window, by which thou hast let us down: and gather together thy father and mother, and brethren, and all thy kindred into thy house.€€ƒxÙWhosoever shall go out of the door of thy house, his blood shall be upon his own head, and we shall be quit. But the blood of all that shall be with thee in the house, shall light upon our head, if any man touch them.€€ƒxuBut if thou wilt betray us, and utter this word abroad, we shall be quit of this oath, which thou hast made us swear.€€ƒx|And she answered: As you have spoken, so be it done: and sending them on their way, she hung the scarlet cord in the window.€€ƒx±But they went and came to the mountains, and stayed there three days, till they that pursued them were returned. For having sought them through all the way, they found them not.€€ƒxÄAnd when they were gone back into the city, the spies returned, and came down from the mountain: and passing over the Jordan, they came to Joshua, the son of Nun, and told him all that befel them,€€ƒxyAnd said: the Lord hath delivered all this land into our hands, and all the inhabitants thereof are overthrown with fear.€€ƒ`€€ƒxµAnd Joshua rose before daylight, and removed the camp: and they departed from Setim, and came to the Jordan: he, and all the children of Israel, and they abode there for three days.€€ƒx<After which, the heralds went through the midst of the camp,€€ƒx½And began to proclaim: When you shall see the ark of the covenant of the Lord your God, and the priests of the race of Levi carrying it, rise you up also, and follow them as they go before:€€ƒxØAnd let there be between you and the ark the space of two thousand cubits: that you may see it afar off, and know which way you must go: for you have not gone this way before: and take care you come not near the ark.€€ƒxaAnd Joshua said to the people: Be ye sanctified: for tomorrow the Lord will do wonders among you.€€‘ƒx”And the Lord said to Joshua: This day will I begin to exalt thee before Israel: that they may know that as I was with Moses, so I am with thee also.€€ƒx¤And do thou command the priests, that carry the ark of the covenant, and say to them: When you shall have entered into part of the water of the Jordan, stand in it.€€ƒx_And Joshua said to the children of Israel: Come hither, and hear the word of the Lord your God.€€ƒyAnd again he said: By this you shall know, that the Lord, the living God, is in the midst of you, and that he shall destroy, before your sight, the Chanaanite and the Hethite, the Hevite and the Pherezite, the Gergesite also, and the Jebusite, and the Amorrhite.€€ƒxaBehold, the ark of the covenant of the Lord of all the earth shall go before you into the Jordan.€€ƒxBPrepare ye twelve men of the tribes of Israel, one of every tribe.€€ƒyAnd when the priests, that carry the ark of the Lord the God of the whole earth, shall set the soles of their feet in the waters of the Jordan, the waters that are beneath shall run down and go off: and those that come from above, shall stand together upon a heap.€€ƒxŠSo the people went out of their tents, to pass over the Jordan: and the priests that carried the ark of the covenant, went on before them.€€ƒx¨And as soon as they came into the Jordan, and their feet were dipped in part of the water, (now the Jordan, it being harvest time, had filled the banks of its channel,)€€ƒy,The waters that came down from above stood in one place, and swelling up like a mountain, were seen afar off, from the city that is called Adom, to the place of Sarthan: but those that were beneath, ran down into the sea of the wilderness, (which now is called the Dead Sea) until they wholly failed.€€ƒxòAnd the people marched over against Jericho: and the priests that carried the ark of the covenant of the Lord, stood girded upon the dry ground in the midst of the Jordan, and all the people passed over, through the channel that was dried up.€€ƒ`€€ƒx8And when they were passed over, the Lord said to Joshua:€€ƒx&Choose twelve men, one of every tribe:€€ƒxÑAnd command them to take out of the midst of the Jordan, where the feet of the priests stood, twelve very hard stones, which you shall set in the place of the camp, where you shall pitch your tents this night.€€ƒxgAnd Joshua called twelve men, whom he had chosen out of the children of Israel, one out of every tribe,€€ƒxÅAnd he said to them: Go before the ark of the Lord your God to the midst of the Jordan, and carry from thence every man a stone on your shoulders, according to the number of the children of Israel,€€˜ƒxÒYou shall answer them: The waters of the Jordan ran off before the ark of the covenant of the Lord when it passed over the same: therefore were these stones set for a monument of the children of Israel forever.€€ƒyThe children of Israel therefore did as Joshua commanded them, carrying out of the channel of the Jordan twelve stones, as the Lord had commanded him according to the number of the children of Israel unto the place wherein they camped, and there they set them.€€ƒx¶And Joshua put other twelve stones in the midst of the channel of the Jordan, where the priests stood that carried the ark of the covenant: and they are there until this present day.€€ƒxíNow the priests that carried the ark, stood in the midst of the Jordan, till all things were accomplished, which the Lord had commanded Joshua to speak to the people, and Moses had said to him. And the people made haste, and passed over.€€ƒxpAnd when they had all passed over, the ark also of the Lord passed over, and the priests went before the people.€€ƒx‹The children of Ruben also, and Gad, and half the tribe of Manasses, went armed before the children of Israel, as Moses had commanded them.€€ƒxxAnd forty thousand fighting men by their troops and bands, marched through the plains and fields of the city of Jericho.€€ƒx†In that day the Lord magnified Joshua in the sight of all Israel, that they should fear him, as they had feared Moses, while he lived.€€ƒsAnd he said to him:€€ƒxVCommand the priests, that carry the ark of the covenant, to come up out of the Jordan.€€ƒx<And he commanded them, saying: Come ye up out of the Jordan.€€ƒx½And when they that carried the ark of the covenant of the Lord, were come up, and began to tread on the dry ground, the waters returned into their channel, and ran as they were wont before.€€ƒx”And the people came up out of the Jordan, the tenth day of the first month, and camped in Galgal, over against the east side of the city of Jericho.€€ƒxgAnd the twelve stones, which they had taken out of the channel of the Jordan, Joshua pitched in Galgal,€€ƒx‡And said to the children of Israel: When your children shall ask their fathers tomorrow, and shall say to them: What mean these stones?€€ƒxVYou shall teach them, and say: Israel passed over this Jordan through the dry channel,€€ƒxTThe Lord your God drying up the waters thereof in your sight, until you passed over:€€ƒxOAs he had done before in the Red Sea, which he dried up till we passed through:€€ƒxThat all the people of the earth may learn the most mighty hand of the Lord, that you also may fear the Lord your God for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒyNow when all the kings of the Amorrhites, who dwelt beyond the Jordan, westward, and all the kings of Chanaan, who possessed the places near the great sea, had heard that the Lord had dried up the waters of the Jordan before the children of Israel, till they passed over, their heart failed them, and there remained no spirit in them, fearing the coming in of the children of Israel.€€ƒxwAt that time the Lord said to Joshua: Make thee knives of stone, and circumcise the second time the children of Israel.€€ƒxkHe did what the Lord had commanded, and he circumcised the children of Israel in the hill of the foreskins.€€ƒxÉNow this is the cause of the second circumcision: All the people that came out of Egypt that were males, all the men fit for war, died in the desert, during the time of the long going about in the way:€€ƒxúDuring the forty years of the journey in the wide wilderness, were uncircumcised: till all they were consumed that had not heard the voice of the Lord, and to whom he had sworn before, that he would not shew them the land flowing with milk and honey.€€ƒx¾The children of these succeeded in the place of their fathers, and were circumcised by Joshua: for they were uncircumcised even as they were born, and no one had circumcised them in the way.€€ƒxiNow after they were all circumcised, they remained in the same place of the camp, until they were healed.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to Joshua: This day have I taken away from you the reproach of Egypt. And the name of that place was called Galgal, until this present day.€€ƒxAnd the children of Israel abode in Galgal, and they kept the phase, on the fourteenth day of the month at evening, in the plains of Jericho:€€ƒxeAnd they ate on the next day unleavened bread of the corn of the land, and frumenty of the same year.€€ƒx¼And the manna ceased after they ate of the corn of the land, neither did the children of Israel use that food any more, but they ate of the corn of the present year of the land of Chanaan.€€ƒxÚAnd when Joshua was in the field of the city of Jericho, he lifted up his eyes, and saw a man standing over against him, holding a drawn sword, and he went to him, and said: Art thou one of ours, or of our adversaries?€€ƒxPAnd he answered: No: but I am prince of the host of the Lord, and now I am come.€€ƒx`Joshua fell on his face to the ground. And worshipping, said: What saith my lord to his servant?€€ƒxLoose, saith he, thy shoes from off thy feet: for the place whereon thou standest is holy. And Joshua did as was commanded him.€€ƒ`€€ƒxqNow Jericho was close shut up and fenced, for fear of the children of Israel, and no man durst go out or come in.€€ƒxwAnd the Lord said to Joshua: Behold I have given into thy hands Jericho, and the king thereof, and all the valiant men.€€ƒxTGo round about the city all ye fighting men once a day: so shall ye do for six days.€€˜ƒy$And when the voice of the trumpet shall give a longer and broken tune, and shall sound in your ears, all the people shall shout together with a very great shout, and the walls of the city shall fall to the ground, and they shall enter in every one at the place against which they shall stand.€€ƒxÊThen Joshua, the son of Nun, called the priests, and said to them: Take the ark of the covenant: and let seven other priests take the seven trumpets of the jubilee, and march before the ark of the Lord.€€ƒx`And he said to the people: Go, and compass the city, armed, marching before the ark of the Lord.€€ƒx~And when Joshua had ended his words, and the seven priests blew the seven trumpets before the ark of the covenant of the Lord,€€ƒxˆAnd all the armed men went before, the rest of the common people followed the ark, and the sound of the trumpets was heard on all sides.€€ƒxÂBut Joshua had commanded the people, saying: You shall not shout, nor shall your voice be heard, nor any word go out of your mouth: until the day come wherein I shall say to you: Cry, and shout.€€ƒx`So the ark of the Lord went about the city once a day, and returning into the camp, abode there.€€ƒxCAnd Joshua rising before day, the priests took the ark of the Lord,€€ƒyAnd seven of them seven trumpets, which are used in the jubilee: and they went before the ark of the Lord, walking and sounding the trumpets: and the armed men went before them, and the rest of the common people followed the ark, and they blew the trumpets.€€ƒxiAnd they went round about the city the second day once, and returned into the camp. So they did six days.€€ƒx_But the seventh day, rising up early, they went about the city, as it was ordered, seven times.€€ƒx™And when in the seventh going about the priests sounded with the trumpets, Joshua said to all Israel: Shout: for the Lord hath delivered the city to you:€€ƒxÁAnd let this city be an anathema, and all things that are in it, to the Lord. Let only Rahab, the harlot, live, with all that are with her in the house: for she hid the messengers whom we sent.€€ƒx¤But beware ye lest you touch ought of those things that are forbidden, and you be guilty of transgression, and all the camp of Israel be under sin, and be troubled.€€ƒxˆBut whatsoever gold or silver there shall be, or vessels of brass and iron, let it be consecrated to the Lord, laid up in his treasures.€€ƒxöSo all the people making a shout, and the trumpets sounding, when the voice and the sound thundered in the ears of the multitude, the walls forthwith fell down: and every man went up by the place that was over against him: and they took the city,€€ƒxAnd killed all that were in it, man and woman, young and old. The oxen also, and the sheep, and the asses, they slew with the edge of the sword.€€ƒx¥But Joshua said to the two men that had been sent for spies: Go into the harlot's house, and bring her out, and all things that are hers, as you assured her by oath.€€ƒx¡And the young men went in, and brought out Rahab, and her parents, her brethren also, and all her goods, and her kindred, and made them to stay without the camp.€€ƒx®But they burned the city, and all things that were therein; except the gold and silver, and vessels of brass and iron, which they consecrated unto the treasury of the Lord. _€€ƒxÿBut Joshua saved Rahab the harlot, and her father's house, and all she had, and they dwelt in the midst of Israel until this present day: because she hid the messengers whom he had sent to spy out Jericho. At that time, Joshua made an imprecation, saying:€€ƒx»Cursed be the man before the Lord, that shall raise up and build the city of Jericho. In his firstborn may he lay the foundation thereof, and in the last of his children set up its gates.€€ƒxMAnd the Lord was with Joshua, and his name was noised throughout all the land€€ƒ`€€ƒyBut the children of Israel transgressed the commandment, and took to their own use of that which was accursed. For Achan, the son of Charmi, the son of Zabdi, the son of Zare, of the tribe of Juda, took something of the anathema: and the Lord was angry against the children of Israel.€€ƒxÒAnd when Joshua sent men from Jericho against Hai, which is beside Bethaven, on the east side of the town of Bethel, he said to them: Go up, and view the country: and they fulfilled his command, and viewed Hai.€€ƒxÌAnd returning, they said to him: Let not all the people go up, but let two or three thousand men go, and destroy the city: why should all the people be troubled in vain, against enemies that are very few?€€˜ƒyAnd were defeated by the men of the city of Hai, and there fell of them six and thirty men: and the enemies pursued them from the gate as far as Sabarim, and they slew them as they fled by the descent: and the heart of the people was struck with fear, and melted like water.€€ƒxµBut Joshua rent his garments, and fell flat on the ground, before the ark of the Lord, until the evening, both he and all the ancients of Israel: and they put dust upon their heads.€€ƒxÕAnd Joshua said: Alas, O Lord God, why wouldst thou bring this people over the river Jordan, to deliver us into the hand of the Amorrhite, and to destroy us? would God we had stayed beyond the Jordan, as we began.€€ƒxRMy Lord God, what shall I say, seeing Israel turning their backs to their enemies?€€ƒxÂThe Chanaanites, and all the inhabitants of the land, will hear of it, and being gathered together will surround us, and cut off our name from the earth: and what wilt thou do to thy great name?€€ƒxFAnd the Lord said to Joshua: Arise, why liest thou flat on the ground?€€ƒx“Israel hath sinned, and transgressed my covenant: and they have taken of the anathema, and have stolen and lied, and have hid it among their goods.€€ƒxÆNeither can Israel stand before his enemies, but he shall flee from them: because he is defiled with the anathema. I will be no more with you, till you destroy him that is guilty of this wickedness.€€ƒyArise, sanctify the people, and say to them: Be ye sanctified against tomorrow: for thus saith the Lord God of Israel: The curse is in the midst of thee, O Israel: thou canst not stand before thy enemies, till he be destroyed out of thee, that is defiled with this wickedness.€€ƒx½And you shall come in the morning, every one by your tribes: and what tribe soever the lot shall find, it shall come by its kindreds, and the kindred by its houses and tho house by the men.€€ƒxÌAnd whosoever he be that shall be found guilty of this fact, he shall be burnt with fire, with all his substance, because he hath transgressed the covenant of the Lord, and hath done wickedness in Israel.€€ƒxuJoshua, therefore, when he rose in the morning, made Israel to come by their tribes, and the tribe of Juda was found.€€ƒx…Which being brought by in families, it was found to be the family of Zare. Bringing that also by the houses, he found it to be Zabdi:€€ƒx~And bringing his house man by man, he found Achan, the son of Charmi, the son of Zabdi, the son of Zare, of the tribe of Juda.€€ƒx‚And Joshua said to Achan: My son, give glory to the Lord God of Israel, and confess, and tell me what thou hast done, hide it not.€€ƒx„And Achan answered Joshua, and said to him: Indeed I have sinned against the Lord, the God of Israel, and thus and thus have I done.€€ƒyFor I saw among the spoils a scarlet garment, exceeding good, and two hundred sicles of silver, and a golden rule of fifty sicles: and I coveted them, and I took them away, and hid them in the ground in the midst of my tent, and the silver I covered with the earth that I dug up.€€ƒxtJoshua therefore sent ministers: who running to his tent, found all hid in the same place, together with the silver.€€ƒxŠAnd taking them away out of the tent, they brought them to Joshua, and to all the children of Israel, and threw them down before the Lord.€€ƒyThen Joshua, and all Israel with him, took Achan, the son of Zare, and the silver, and the garment, and the golden rule, his sons also, and his daughters, his oxen, and asses, and sheep, the tent also, and all the goods: and brought them to the valley of Achor:€€ƒx£Where Joshua said: Because thou hast troubled us, the Lord trouble thee this day. And all Israel stoned him: and all things that were his, were consumed with fire.€€ƒxâAnd they gathered together upon him a great heap of stones, which remaineth until this present day And the wrath of the Lord was turned away from them. And the name of that place was called the Valley of Achor, until this day.€€ƒ`€€ƒx÷And the Lord said to Joshua: Fear not, nor be thou dismayed: take with thee all the multitude of fighting men, arise, and go up to the town of Hai: Behold I have delivered into thy hand the king thereof, and the people, and the city, and the land.€€ƒxèAnd thou shalt do to the city of Hai, and to the king thereof, as thou hast done to Jericho, and to the king thereof: but the spoils, and all the cattle, you shall take for a prey to yourselves: lay an ambush for the city behind it.€€ƒx“And Joshua arose, and all the army of the fighting men with him, to go up against Hai: and he sent thirty thousand chosen valiant men in the night,€€˜#ƒxÈBut I, and the rest of the multitude which is with me, will approach on the contrary side against the city. And when they shall come out against us, we will flee, and turn our backs, as we did before:€€ƒxaTill they pursuing us be drawn farther from the city: for they will think that we flee as before.€€ƒx¢And whilst we are fleeing, and they pursuing, you shall rise out of the ambush, and shall destroy the city: and the Lord your God will deliver it into your hands.€€ƒxeAnd when you shall have taken it, set it on fire, and you shall do all things so as I have commanded.€€ƒxÂAnd he sent them away, and they went on to the place of the ambush, and abode between Bethel and Hai, on the west side of the city of Hai. But Joshua staid that night in the midst of the people,€€ƒxAnd rising early in the morning, he mustered his soldiers, and went up with the ancients in the front of the army, environed with the aid of the fighting men.€€ƒx¢And when they were come, and were gone up over against the city, they stood on the north side of the city, between which and them there was a valley in the midst.€€ƒx}And he had chosen five thousand men, and set them to lie in ambush between Bethel and Hai, on the west side of the same city:€€ƒxÎBut all the rest of the army went in battle array on the north side, so that the last of that multitude reached to the west side of the city. So Joshua went that night, and stood in the midst of the valley.€€ƒxÑAnd when the king of Hai saw this, he made haste in the morning, and went out with all the army of the city, and set it in battle array, toward the desert, not knowing that there lay an ambush behind his back.€€ƒxnBut Joshua, and all Israel gave back, making as if they were afraid, and fleeing by the way of the wilderness.€€ƒxmBut they shouting together, and encouraging one another, pursued them. And when they were come from the city,€€ƒx‡And not one remained in the city of Hai and of Bethel, that did not pursue after Israel, leaving the towns open as they had rushed out,€€ƒxxThe Lord said to Joshua: Lift up the shield that is in thy hand, towards the city of Hai, for I will deliver it to thee.€€ƒx™And when he had lifted up his shield towards the city, the ambush, that lay hid, rose up immediately: and going to the city, took it, and set it on fire.€€ƒy3And the men of the city, that pursued after Joshua, looking back, and seeing the smoke of the city rise up to heaven, had no more power to flee this way or that way: especially as they that had counterfeited flight, and were going toward the wilderness, turned back most valiantly against them that pursued.€€ƒx…So Joshua, and all Israel, seeing that the city was taken, and that the smoke of the city rose up, returned, and slew the men of Hai.€€ƒxûAnd they also that had taken and set the city on fire, issuing out of the city to meet their own men, began to cut off the enemies who were surrounded by them. So that the enemies being cut off on both sides, not one of so great a multitude was saved.€€ƒxJAnd they took the king of the city of Hai alive and brought him to Joshua.€€ƒx·So all being slain that had pursued after Israel, in his flight to the wilderness, and falling by the sword in the same place, the children of Israel returned and laid waste the city.€€ƒxvAnd the number of them that fell that day, both of men and women, was twelve thousand persons, all of the city of Hai.€€ƒx†But Joshua drew not back his hand, which he had stretched out on high, holding the shield, till all the inhabitants of Hai were slain.€€ƒxuAnd the children of Israel divided among them, the cattle and the prey of the city, as the Lord had commanded Joshua.€€ƒx2And he burnt the city, and made it a heap forever:€€ƒy And he hung the king thereof on a gibbet, until the evening and the going down of the sun. Then Joshua commanded, and they took down his carcass from the gibbet: and threw it in the very entrance of the city, heaping upon it a great heap of stones, which remaineth until this present day.€€ƒxJThen Joshua built an altar to the Lord, the God of Israel, in Mount Hebal,€€ƒyAs Moses, the servant of the Lord, had commanded the children of Israel, and it is written in the book of the law of Moses: an altar of unhewn stones, which iron had not touched: and he offered upon it holocausts to the Lord, and immolated victims of peace offerings.€€ƒxrAnd he wrote upon stones, the Deuteronomy of the law of Moses, which he had ordered before the children of Israel.€€ƒynAnd all the people, and the ancients, and the princes, and judges, stood on both sides of the ark, before the priests that carried the ark of the covenant of the Lord, both the stranger and he that was born among them, half of them by Mount Garizim, and half by Mount Hebal, as Moses the servant of the Lord, had commanded. And first he blessed the people of Israel.€€ƒx{After this, he read all the words of the blessing and the cursing, and all things that were written in the book of the law.€€ƒx¶He left out nothing of those things which Moses had commanded, but he repeated all before all the people of Israel, with the women and children, and strangers, that dwelt among them.€€ƒ`€€ƒy7Now when these things were heard of, all the kings beyond the Jordan, that dwelt in the mountains, and in the plains, in the places near the sea, and on the coasts of the great sea, they also that dwell by Libanus, the Hethite, and the Amorrhite, the Chanaanite, the Pherezite, and the Hevite, and the Jebusite,€€ƒxcGathered themselves together, to fight against Joshua and Israel with one mind, and one resolution.€€ƒxSBut they that dwelt in Gabaon, hearing all that Joshua had done to Jericho and Hai:€€˜ƒxÄAnd very old shoes, which for a show of age were clouted with patches, and old garments upon them: the loaves also, which they carried for provisions by the way, were hard, and broken into pieces:€€ƒxàAnd they went to Joshua, who then abode in the camp at Galgal, and said to him, and to all Israel with him: We are come from a far country, desiring to make peace with you. And the children of Israel answered them, and said:€€ƒx\Perhaps you dwell in the land which falls to our lot; if so, we can make no league with you.€€ƒxdBut they said to Joshua: We are thy servants. Joshua said to them: Who are you? and whence came you?€€ƒx«They answered: From a very far country thy servants are come in the name of the Lord thy God. For we have heard the fame of his power, all the things that he did in Egypt.€€ƒx‰And to the two kings of the Amorrhites, that were beyond the Jordan, Sehon, king of Hesebon, and Og, king of Basan, that was in Astaroth:€€ƒx·And our ancients, and all the inhabitants of our country, said to us: Take with you victuals for a long way, and go meet them, and say: We are your servants, make ye a league with us.€€ƒx—Behold, these loaves we took hot, when we set out from our houses to come to you, now they are become dry, and broken in pieces by being exceeding old.€€ƒxÕThese bottles of wine when we filled them were new, now they are rent and burst. These garments we have on, and the shoes we have on our feet, by reason of the very long journey, are worn out, and almost consumed.€€ƒxOThey took therefore of their victuals, and consulted not the mouth of the Lord.€€ƒx•And Joshua made peace with them, and entering into a league, promised that they should not be slain: the princes also of the multitude swore to them.€€ƒxiNow three days after the league was made, they heard that they dwelt nigh, and they should be among them.€€ƒx¤And the children of Israel removed the camp, and came into their cities on the third day, the names of which are, Gabaon, and Caphira, and Beroth, and Cariathiarim.€€ƒx«And they slew them not, because the princes of the multitude had sworn in the name of the Lord, the God of Israel. Then all the common people murmured against the princes.€€ƒx~And they answered them: We have sworn to them in the name of the Lord, the God of Israel, and therefore we may not touch them.€€ƒx…But this we will do to them: Let their lives be saved, lest the wrath of the Lord be stirred up against us, if we should be forsworn:€€ƒx€But so let them live, as to serve the whole multitude in hewing wood, and bringing in water. As they were speaking these things,€€ƒx’Josue called the Gabaonites and said to them: Why would you impose upon us, saying: We dwell far off from you, whereas you are in the midst of us?€€ƒx„Therefore you shall be under a curse, and your race shall always be hewers of wood, and carriers of water, into the house of my God.€€ƒy$They answered: It was told us, thy servants, that the Lord thy God had promised his servant Moses, to give you all the land, and to destroy all the inhabitants thereof. Therefore we feared exceedingly and provided for our lives, compelled by the dread we had of you, and we took this counsel.€€ƒxPAnd now we are in thy hand: deal with us as it seemeth good and right unto thee.€€ƒxxSo Joshua did as he had said, and delivered them from the hand of the children of Israel, that they should not be slain.€€ƒxÖAnd he gave orders in that day, that they should be in the service of all the people, and of the altar of the Lord, hewing wood, and carrying water, until this present time, in the place which the Lord hath chosen.€€ƒ`€€ƒyWhen Adonisedec, king of Jerusalem, had heard these things, to wit, that Joshua had taken Hai, and had destroyed it, (for as he had done to Jericho and the king thereof, so did he to Hai and its king) and that the Gabaonites were gone over to Israel, and were their confederates,€€ƒx¢He was exceedingly afraid. For Gabaon was a great city, and one of the royal cities, and greater than the town of Hai, and all its fighting men were most valiant.€€ƒx«Therefore Adonisedec, king of Jerusalem, sent to Oham, king of Hebron, and to Pharam, king of Jerimoth, and to Japhia, king of Lachis, and to Dabir, king of Eglon, saying:€€˜+ƒxòSo the five kings of the Amorrhites being assembled together, went up: the king of Jerusalem, the king of Hebron, the king of Jerimoth, the king of Lachis, the king of Eglon, they and their armies, and camped about Gabaon, laying siege to it.€€ƒyNBut the inhabitants of the city of Gabaon, which was besieged, sent to Joshua, who then abode in the camp at Galgal, and said to him: Withdraw not thy hands from helping thy servants: come up quickly, and save us, and bring us succour: for all the kings of the Amorrhites, who dwell in the mountains, are gathered together against us.€€ƒx\And Joshua went up from Galgal, and all the army of the warriors with him, most valiant men.€€ƒx‡But the Lord said to Joshua: Fear them not: for I have delivered them into thy hands: none of them shall be able to stand against thee.€€ƒxFSo Joshua going up from Galgal all the night, came upon them suddenly.€€ƒxĞAnd the Lord troubled them, at the sight of Israel: and he slew them with a great slaughter, in Gabaon, and pursued them by the way of the ascent to Bethoron, and cut them off all the way to Azeca and Maceda.€€ƒyAnd when they were fleeing from the children of Israel, and were in the descent of Bethoron, the Lord cast down upon them great stones from heaven, as far as Azeca: and many more were killed with the hailstones, than were slain by the swords of the children of Israel,€€ƒxÙThen Joshua spoke to the Lord, in the day that he delivered the Amorrhite in the sight of the children of Israel, and he said before them: Move not, O sun, toward Gabaon, nor thou, O moon, toward the valley of Ajalon.€€ƒxçAnd the sun and the moon stood still, till the people revenged themselves of their enemies. Is not this written in the book of the just? So the sun stood still in the midst of heaven, and hasted not to go down the space of one day.€€ƒxmThere was not before, nor after, so long a day, the Lord obeying the voice of a man, and fighting for Israel.€€ƒx>And Joshua returned, with all Israel, into the camp of Galgal.€€ƒxUFor the five kings were fled, and had hid themselves in a cave of the city of Maceda.€€ƒx[And it was told Joshua, that the five kings were found hid in a cave of the city of Maceda.€€ƒx‡And he commanded them that were with him, saying: Roll great stones to the mouth of the cave, and set careful men to keep them shut up:€€ƒxÑAnd stay you not, but pursue after the enemies, and kill all the hindermost of them as they flee, and do not suffer them whom the Lord God hath delivered into your hands, to shelter themselves in their cities.€€ƒx–So the enemies being slain with a great slaughter, and almost utterly consumed, they that were able to escape from Israel, entered into fenced cities.€€ƒxºAnd all the army returned to Joshua, in Maceda, where the camp then was, in good health, and without the loss of any one: and no man durst move his tongue against the children of Israel.€€ƒxvAnd Joshua gave orders, saying: Open the mouth of the cave, and bring forth to me the five kings that lie hid therein.€€ƒxÑAnd the ministers did as they were commanded: and they brought out to him the five kings out of the cave: the king of Jerusalem, the king of Hebron, the king of Jerimoth, the king of Lachis, the king of Eglon.€€ƒyAnd when they were brought out to him, he called all the men of Israel, and said to the chiefs of the army that were with him: Go, and set your feet on the necks of these kings. And when they had gone, and put their feet upon the necks of them lying under them,€€ƒxšHe said again to them: Fear not, neither be ye dismayed, take courage, and be strong: for so will the Lord do to all your enemies, against whom you fight.€€ƒxeAnd Joshua struck, and slew them, and hanged them upon five gibbets; and they hung until the evening.€€ƒx÷And when the sun was down, he commanded the soldiers to take them down from the gibbets. And after they were taken down, they cast them into the cave, where they had lain hid, and put great stones at the mouth thereof, which remain until this day.€€ƒxğThe same day Joshua took Maceda, and destroyed it with the edge of the sword, and killed the king and all the inhabitants thereof: he left not in it the least remains. And he did to the king of Maceda, as he had done to the king of Jericho.€€ƒxJAnd he passed from Maceda with all Israel to Lebna, and fought against it:€€ƒyAnd the Lord delivered it with the king thereof into the hands of Israel: and they destroyed the city with the edge of the sword, and all the inhabitants thereof. They left not in it any remains. And they did to the king of Lebna, as they had done to the king of Jericho.€€ƒx_From Lebna he passed unto Lachis, with all Israel: and investing it with his army, besieged it.€€ƒxªAnd the Lord delivered Lachis into the hands of Israel, and he took it the following day, and put it to the sword, and every soul that was in it, as he had done to Lebna.€€ƒx€At that time Horam, king of Gazer, came up to succour Lachis: and Joshua slew him with all his people so as to leave none alive.€€ƒx6And he passed from Lachis to Eglon, and surrounded it,€€ƒxzAnd took it the same day: and put to the sword all the souls that were in it, according to all that he had done to Lachis.€€ƒxLHe went up also with all Israel from Eglon to Hebron, and fought against it:€€ƒyTook it, and destroyed it with the edge of the sword: the king also thereof, and all the towns of that country, and all the souls that dwelt in it: he left not therein any remains: as he had done to Eglon, so did he also to Hebron, putting to the sword all that he found in it.€€ƒxReturning from thence to Dabir,€€ƒy␀He took it, and destroyed it: the king also thereof, and all the towns round about, he destroyed with the edge of the sword: he left not in it any remains: as he had done to Hebron and Lebna, and to their kings, so did he to Dabir, and to the king thereof.€€ƒxçSo Joshua conquered all the country of the hills, and of the south, and of the plain, and of Asedoth, with their kings: he left not any remains therein, but slew all that breathed, as the Lord, the God of Israel, had commanded him.€€ƒxCFrom Cadesbarne even to Gaza. All the land of Gosen even to Gabaon,€€ƒxtAnd all their kings, and their lands he took and wasted at one onset: for the Lord the God of Israel fought for him.€€ƒxCAnd he returned with all Israel to the place of the camp in Galgal.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‹And when Jabin king of Asor had heard these things, he sent to Jobab king of Madon, and to the king of Semeron, and to the king of Achsaph:€€ƒx±And to the kings of the north, that dwelt in the mountains and in the plains over against the south side of Ceneroth, and in the levels and the countries of Dor by the sea side:€€ƒxØTo the Chanaanites also on the east and on the west, and the Amorrhite, and the Hethite, and the Pherezite, and the Jebusite in the mountains: to the Hevite also who dwelt at the foot of Hermon in the land of Maspha.€€—ƒxWAnd all these kings assembled together at the waters of Merom, to fight against Israel.€€ƒxÙAnd the Lord said to Joshua: Fear them not: for to morrow at this same hour I will deliver all these to be slain in the sight of Israel: thou shalt hamstring their horses, and thou shalt burn their chariots with fire.€€ƒxpAnd Joshua came, and all the army with him, against them to the waters of Merom on a sudden, and fell upon them.€€ƒyAnd the Lord delivered them into the hands of Israel. And they defeated them, and chased them as far as the great Sidon and the waters of Maserophot, and the field of Masphe, which is on the east thereof. He slew them all, so as to leave no remains of them:€€ƒx`And he did as the Lord had commanded him, he hamstringed their horses and burned their chariots.€€ƒx†And presently turning back he took Asor: and slew the king thereof with the sword. Now Asor of old was the head of all these kingdoms.€€ƒxAnd he cut off all the souls that abode there: he left not in it any remains, but utterly destroyed all, and burned the city itself with fire.€€ƒxŠAnd he took and put to the sword and destroyed all the cities round about, and their kings, as Moses the servant of God had commanded him.€€ƒxƒExcept the cities that were on hills and high places, the rest Israel burned: only Asor that was very strong he consumed with fire.€€ƒxvAnd the children of Israel divided among themselves all the spoil of these cities and the cattle, killing all the men.€€ƒx»As the Lord had commanded Moses his servant, so did Moses command Joshua, and he accomplished all: he left not one thing undone of all the commandments which the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒx®So Joshua took all the country of the hills, and of the south, and the land of Gosen, and the plains and the west country, and the mountain of Israel, and the plains thereof:€€ƒx–And part of the mountain that goeth up to Seir as far as Baalgad, by the plain of Libanus under mount Hermon: all their kings he took, smote and slew.€€ƒx0Joshua made war a long time against these kings.€€ƒx‡There was not a city that delivered itself to the children of Israel, except the Hevite, who dwelt in Gabaon: for he took all by fight.€€ƒxØFor it was the sentence of the Lord, that their hearts should be hardened, and they should fight against Israel, and fall, and should not deserve any clemency, and should be destroyed as the Lord had commanded Moses.€€ƒx±At that time Joshua came and cut off the Enancims from the mountains, from Hebron, and Dabir, and Anab, and from all the mountain of Juda and Israel, and destroyed their cities.€€ƒx£He left not any of the stock of the Enacims, in the land of the children of Israel: except the cities of Gaza, and Geth, and Azotus, in which alone they were left.€€ƒxºSo Joshua took all the land, as the Lord spoke to Moses, and delivered it in possession to the children of Israel, according to their divisions and tribes. And the land rested from wars.€€ƒ`€€ƒxçThese are the kings, whom the children of Israel slew and possessed their land beyond the Jordan towards the rising of the sun, from the torrent Arnon unto mount Hermon, and all the east country that looketh towards the wilderness.€€ƒy	Sehon king of the Amorrhites, who dwelt in Hesebon, and had dominion from Aroer, which is seated upon the bank of the torrent Arnon, and of the middle part in the valley, and of half Galaad, as far as the torrent Jaboc, which is the border of the children of Ammon.€€ƒxïAnd from the wilderness, to the sea of Ceneroth towards the east, and to the sea of the wilderness, which is the most salt sea, on the east side by the way that leadeth to Bethsimoth: and on the south side that lieth under Asedoth, Phasga.€€˜ƒxUOf Gessuri and Machati, and of half Galaad: the borders of Sehon the king of Hesebon.€€ƒx°Moses the servant of the Lord, and the children of Israel slew them, and Moses delivered their land in possession to the Rubenites, and Gadites, and the half tribe of Manasses.€€ƒy$These are the kings of the land, whom Joshua and the children of Israel slew beyond the Jordan on the west side from Baalgad in the field of Libanus, unto the mount, part of which goeth up into Seir: and Joshua delivered it in possession to the tribes of Israel, to every one their divisions,€€ƒxÜAs well in the mountains as in the plains and the champaign countries. In Asedoth, and in the wilderness, and in the south was the Hethite and the Amorrhite, the Chanaanite and the Pherezite, the Hevite and the Jebusite.€€ƒxNThe king of Jericho one: the king of Hai, which is on the side of Bethel, one:€€ƒx2The king of Jerusalem one, the king of Hebron one,€€ƒx2The king of Jerimoth one, thee king of Lachis one,€€ƒx-The king of Eglon one, the king of Gazer one,€€ƒx-The king of Dabir one, the king of Gader one,€€ƒx-The king of Herma one, the king of Hered one,€€ƒx/The king of Lebna one, the king of Odullam one,€€ƒx/The king of Maceda one, the king of Bethel one,€€ƒx.The king of Taphua one, the king of Opher one,€€ƒx-The king of Aphec one, the king of Saron one,€€ƒx,The king of Madon one, the king of Asor one,€€ƒx1The king of Semeron one, the king of Achsaph one,€€ƒx0The king of Thenac one, the king of Mageddo one,€€ƒx;Thee king of Cades one, the king of Jachanan of Carmel one,€€ƒxWThe king of Dor, and of the province of Dor one, the king of the nations of Galgal one,€€ƒx5The king of Thersa one: all the kings thirty and one.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¶Joshua was old, and far advanced in years, and the Lord said to him: Thou art grown old, and advanced in age, and there is a very large country left, which is not yet divided by lot:€€ƒx0To wit, all Galilee, Philistia, and all Gessuri.€€ƒxëFrom the troubled river, that watereth Egypt, unto the border of Accaron northward: the land of Chanaan, which is divided among the lords of the Philistines, the Gazites, the Azotians, the Ascalonites, the Gethites, and the Accronites.€€˜!ƒxzAnd his confines. The country also of Libanus towards the east from Baalgad under mount Hermon to the entering into Emath.€€ƒyOf all that dwell in the mountains from Libanus, to the waters of Maserephoth, and all the Sidonians. I am he that will cut them off from before the face of the children of Israel. So let their land come in as a part of the inheritance of Israel, as I have commanded thee.€€ƒx\And now divide the land in possession to the nine tribes, and to the half tribe of Manasses,€€ƒx‘With whom Ruben and Gad have possessed the land, which Moses the servant of the Lord delivered to them beyond the river Jordan, on the east side.€€ƒx†From Aroer, which is upon the bank of the torrent Arnon, and in the midst of the valley and all the plains of Medaba, as far as Dibon:€€ƒxwAnd all the cities of Sehon, king of the Amorrhites, who reigned in Hesebon, unto the borders of the children of Ammon.€€ƒxjAnd Galaad, and the borders of Gessuri and Machati, and all mount Hermon, and all Basan as far as Salecha,€€ƒx‘All the kingdom of Og in Basan, who reigned in Astaroth and Edrai, he was of the remains of the Raphaims: and Moses overthrew and destroyed them.€€ƒx„And the children of Israel would not destroy Gessuri and Machati and they have dwelt in the midst of Israel, until this present day.€€ƒx“But to the tribe of Levi he gave no possession: but the sacrifices and victims of thee Lord God of Israel, are his inheritance, as he spoke to him.€€ƒxQAnd Moses gave a possession to the children of Ruben according to their kindreds.€€ƒx¦And their border was from Aroer, which is on the bank of the torrent Arnon, and in the midst of the valley of the same torrent: all the plain, that leadeth to Medaba,€€ƒxsAnd Hesebon, and all their villages, which are in the plains. Dibon also, and Bamothbaal, and the town of Baalmaon,€€ƒx&And Jassa, and Cidimoth, and Mephaath,€€ƒxIAnd Cariathaim, and Sabama, and Sarathasar in the mountain of the valley.€€ƒx0Bethphogor and Asedoth, Phasga and Bethiesimoth,€€ƒxïAnd all the cities of the plain, and all the kingdoms of Sehon king of the Amorrhites, that reigned in Hesebon, whom Moses slew with the princes of Madian: Hevi, and Recem, and Sur and Hur, and Rebe, dukes of Sehon inhabitants of the land.€€ƒxvBalaam also the son of Beor the soothsayer, the children of Israel slew with the sword among the rest that were slain.€€ƒx‘And the river Jordan was the border of the children of Ruben. This is the possession of the Rubenites, by their kindreds, of cities and villages.€€ƒxuAnd Moses gave to the tribe of Gad and to his children by their kindreds a possession, of which this is the division.€€ƒx‹The border of Jaser, and all the cities of Galaad, and half the land of the children of Ammon: as far as Aroer which is over against Rabba:€€ƒx\And from Hesebon unto Ramoth, Masphe and Betonim: and from Manaim unto the borders of Dabir.€€ƒxöAnd in the valley Betharan and Bethnemra, and Socoth, and Saphon the other part of the kingdom of Sehon king of Hesebon: the limit of this also is the Jordan, as far as the uttermost part of the sea of Cenereth beyond the Jordan on the east side,€€ƒx\This is the possession of the children of Gad by their families, their cities, and villages.€€ƒxcHe gave also to the half tribe of Manasses and his children possession according to their kindreds,€€ƒx£The beginning whereof is this: from Manaim all Basan, and all the kingdoms of Og king of Basan, and all the villages of Jair, which are in Basan, threescore towns.€€ƒxÂAnd half Galaad, and Astaroth, and Edrai, cities of the kingdom of Og in Basan: to the children of Machir, the son of Manasses, to one half of the children of Machir according to their kindreds.€€ƒxnThis possession Moses divided in the plains of Moab, beyond the Jordan, over against Jericho on the east side,€€ƒx„But to the tribe of Levi he gave no possession: because the Lord the God of Israel himself is their possession, as he spoke to them.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÀThis is what the children of Israel possessed in the land of Chanaan, which Eleazar the priest, and Joshua the son of Nun, and the princes of the families by the tribes of Israel gave to them.€€ƒxiDividing all by lot, as the Lord had commanded the hand of Moses, to the nine tribes, and the half tribe.€€ƒxŠFor to two tribes and a half Moses had given possession beyond the Jordan: besides the Levites, who received no land among their brethren:€€ƒxYAs the Lord had commanded Moses so did the children of Israel, and they divided the land.€€ƒxÊThen the children of Juda came to Joshua in Galgal, and Caleb the son of Jephone the Cenezite spoke to him: Thou knowest what the Lord spoke to Moses the man of God concerning me and thee in Cadesbarne.€€ƒx–I was forty years old when Moses the servant of the Lord sent me from Cadesbarne, to view the land, and I brought him word again as to me seemed true,€€ƒx|But my brethren, that had gone up with me, discouraged the heart of the people: and I nevertheless followed the Lord my God.€€ƒx®And Moses swore in that day, saying: The land which thy foot hath trodden upon shall be thy possession, and thy children for ever, because thou hast followed the Lord my God.€€ƒxæThe Lord therefore hath granted me life, as he promised until this present day, It is forty and five years since the Lord spoke this word to Moses, when Israel journeyed through the wilderness: this day I am eighty-five years old,€€ƒxšAs strong as I was at that time when I was sent to view the land: the strength of that time continueth in me until this day, as well to fight as to march.€€ƒxßGive me therefore this mountain, which the Lord promised, in thy hearing also, wherein are the Enacims, and cities great and strong: if so be the Lord will be with me, and I shall be able to destroy them, as he promised me.€€ƒx:And Joshua blessed him, and gave him Hebron in possession.€€ƒx”And from that time Hebron belonged to Caleb the son of Jephone the Cenezite, until this present day: because he followed the Lord the God of Israel.€€ƒx„The name of Hebron before was called Cariath-Arbe: Adam the greatest among the Enacims was laid there and the land rested from wars.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¨Now the lot of the children of Juda by their kindreds was this: From the frontier of Edom, to the desert of Sin southward, and to the uttermost part of the south coast.€€ƒxiIts beginning was from the top of the most salt sea, and from the bay thereof, that looketh to the south.€€ƒx®And it goeth out towards the ascent of the Scorpion, and passeth on to Sina: and ascendeth into Cadesbarne, and reacheth into Esron, going up to Addar, and compassing Carcaa.€€˜?ƒx¦But on the east side the beginning shall be the most salt sea even to the end of the Jordan: and towards the north from the bay of the sea unto the same river Jordan.€€ƒx‚And the border goeth up into Beth-Hagla, and passeth by the north into Beth-Araba: going up to the stone of Boen the son of Ruben.€€ƒyCAnd reaching as far as the borders of Debara from the valley of Achor, and so northward looking towards Galgal, which is opposite to the ascent of Adommin, on the south side of the torrent, and the border passeth the waters that are called the fountain of the sun: and the goings out thereof shall be at the fountain Rogel.€€ƒyAnd it goeth up by the valley of the son of Ennom on the side of the Jebusite towards the south, the same is Jerusalem: and thence ascending to the top of the mountain, which is over against Geennom to the west in the end of the valley of Raphaim, northward.€€ƒxŞAnd it passeth on from the top of the mountain to the fountain of the water of Nephtoa: and reacheth to the towns of mount Ephron: and it bendeth towards Baala, which is Cariathiarim, that is to say, the city of the woods.€€ƒx°And it compasseth from Baala westward unto mount Seir: and passeth by the side of mount Jarim to the north into Cheslon: and goeth down into Bethsames, and passeth into Thamna.€€ƒx¯And reacheth northward to a part of Accaron at the side: and bendeth to Sechrona, and passeth mount Baala: and cometh into Jebneel, and is bounded westward with the great sea.€€ƒxLThese are the borders round about of the children of Juda in their kindreds.€€ƒx¨But to Caleb the son of Jephone he gave a portion in the midst of the children of Juda, as the Lord had commanded him: Cariath-Arbe the father of Enac, which is Hebron.€€ƒxhAnd Caleb destroyed out of it the three sons of Enac, Sesai and Ahiman, and Tholmai of the race of Enac.€€ƒxŠAnd going up from thence he came to the inhabitants of Dabir, which before was called Cariath-Sepher, that is to say, the city of letters.€€ƒxiAnd Caleb said: He that shall smite Cariath-Sepher, and take it, I will give him Axa my daughter to wife.€€ƒxoAnd Othoniel the son of Cenez, the younger brother of Caleb, took it: and he gave him Axa his daughter to wife.€€ƒxªAnd as they were going together, she was moved by her husband to ask a field of her father, and she sighed as she sat on her ass. And Caleb said to her: What aileth thee?€€ƒx±But she answered: Give me a blessing: thou hast given me a southern and dry land, give me also a land that Is watered. And Caleb gave her the upper and the nether watery ground.€€ƒxNThis is the possession of the tribe of the children of Juda by their kindreds.€€ƒx…And the cities from the uttermost parts of the children of Juda by the borders of Edom to the south, were Cabseel and Eder and Jagur,€€ƒxAnd Cina and Dimona and Adada,€€ƒxAnd Cades and Asor and Jethnam,€€ƒxZiph and Telem and Baloth,€€ƒx,New Asor and Carioth, Hesron, which is Asor.€€ƒvAmam, Sama and Molada,€€ƒx*And Asergadda and Hassemon and Bethphelet,€€ƒx)And Hasersual and Bersabee and Baziothia,€€ƒxAnd Baala and Jim and Esem,€€ƒx!And Eltholad and Cesil and Harma,€€ƒx&And Siceleg and Medemena and Sensenna,€€ƒxULebaoth and Selim and Aen and Remmon: all the cities twenty-nine, and their villages.€€ƒx.But in the plains: Estaol and Sarea and Asena,€€ƒx,And Zanoe and Engannim and Taphua and Enaim,€€ƒx*And Jerimoth and Adullam, Socho and Azeca,€€ƒxXAnd Saraim and Adithaim and Gedera and Gederothaim: fourteen cities, and their villages.€€ƒx Sanan and Hadassa and Magdalgad,€€ƒxDelean and Masepha and Jecthel,€€ƒxLachis and Bascath and Eglon,€€ƒx Chebbon and Leheman and Cethlis,€€ƒxTAnd Gideroth and Bethdagon and Naama and Maceda: sixteen cities, and their villages.€€ƒxLabana and Ether and Asan,€€ƒxJephtha and Esna and Nesib,€€ƒxAAnd Ceila and Achzib and Maresa: nine cities, and their villages.€€ƒx,Accaron with the towns and villages thereof.€€ƒxZFrom Accaron even to the sea: all places that lie towards Azotus and the villages thereof.€€ƒx‘Azotus with its towns and villages. Gaza with its towns and villages, even to the torrent of Egypt, and the great sea that is the border thereof.€€ƒx0And in the mountain Samir and Jether and Socoth,€€ƒx+And Danna and Cariath-senna, this is Dabir:€€ƒxAnab and Istemo and Anim,€€ƒx:Gosen and Olon and Gilo: eleven cities and their villages.€€ƒxArab and Ruma and Esaan,€€ƒx%And Janum and Beththaphua and Apheca,€€ƒxSAthmatha and Cariath-Arbe, this is Hebron and Sior: nine cities and their villages.€€ƒx"Maon and Carmel and Ziph and Jota,€€ƒxJezrael and Jucadam and Zanoe,€€ƒx8Accain, Gabaa and Thamna: ten cities and their villages.€€ƒxHalhul, and Bessur, and Gedor,€€ƒxBMareth, and Bethanoth, and Eltecon: six cities and their villages.€€ƒxcCariathbaal, the same is Cariathiarim the city of woods, and Arebba: two cities and their villages.€€ƒx-In the desert Betharaba, Meddin and Sachacha,€€ƒxMAnd Nebsan, and the city of salt, and Engaddi: six cities and their villages.€€ƒx¦But the children of Juda could not destroy the Jebusite that dwelt in Jerusalem: and the Jebusite dwelt with the children of Juda in Jerusalem until this present day.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¶And the lot of the sons of Joseph fell from the Jordan over against Jericho and the waters thereof, on the east: the wilderness which goeth up from Jericho to the mountain of Bethel:€€ƒxOAnd goeth out from Bethel to Luza: and passeth the border of Archi, to Ataroth,€€ƒx And goeth down westward, by the border of Jephleti, unto the borders of Beth-horon the nether, and to Gazer: and the countries of it are ended by the great sea:€€ŠƒxAnd the border of the children of Ephraim was according to their kindreds: and their possession towards the east was Ataroth-addar unto Beth-horon the upper.€€ƒx³And the confines go out unto the sea: but Machmethath looketh to the north, and it goeth round the borders eastward into Thanath-selo: and passeth along on the east side to Janoe.€€ƒxnAnd it goeth down from Janoe into Ataroth and Naaratha: and it cometh to Jericho, and goeth out to the Jordan.€€ƒxÊFrom Taphua it passeth on towards the sea into the valley of reeds, and the goings out thereof are at the most salt sea. This is the possession of the tribe of the children of Ephraim by their families.€€ƒx‹And there were cities with their villages separated for the children of Ephraim in the midst of the possession of the children of Manasses.€€ƒx™And the children of Ephraim slew not the Chanaanite, who dwelt in Gazer: and the Chanaanite dwelt in the midst of Ephraim until this day, paying tribute.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÆAnd this lot fell to the tribe of Manasses for he is the firstborn of Joseph to Machir the firstborn of Manasses the father of Galaad, who was a warlike man, and had for possession Galaad and Basan.€€ƒyLAnd to the rest of the children of Manasses according to their families: to the children of Abiezer, and to the children of Helec, and to the children of Esriel, and to the children of Sechem, and to the children of Hepher, and to the children of Semida: these are the male children of Manasses the son of Joseph, by their kindreds.€€ƒx½But Salphaad the son of Hepher the son of Galaad the son of Machir the son of Manasses had no sons, but only daughters: whose names are these, Maala and Noa and Hegla and Melcha and Thersa.€€’ƒx_And there fell ten portions to Manasses, beside the land of Galaad and Basan beyond the Jordan.€€ƒxFor the daughters of Manasses possessed inheritance in the midst of his sons. And the land of Galaad fell to the lot of the rest of the children of Manasses.€€ƒx¤And the border of Manasses was from Aser, Machmethath which looketh towards Sichem: and it goeth out on the right hand by the inhabitants of the fountain of Taphua.€€ƒx€For the lot of Manasses took in the land of Taphua, which is on the borders of Manasses, and belongs to the children of Ephraim.€€ƒyAnd the border goeth down to the valley of the reeds, to the south of the torrent of the cities of Ephraim, which are in the midst of the cities of Manasses: the border of Manasses is on the north side of the torrent, and the outgoings of it are at the sea:€€ƒxâSo that the possession of Ephraim is on the south, and on the north that of Manasses, and the sea is the border of both, and they are joined together in the tribe of Aser on the north, and in the tribe of Issachar on the east.€€ƒy‰And the inheritance of Manasses in Issachar and in Aser, was Bethsan and its villages, and Jeblaam with its villages, and the inhabitants of Dor, with the towns thereof: the inhabitants also of Endor with the villages thereof: and in like manner the inhabitants of Thenac with the villages thereof: and the inhabitants of Mageddo with their villages, and the third part of the city of Nopheth.€€ƒxmNeither could the children of Manasses overthrow these cities, but the Chanaanite began to dwell in his land.€€ƒx“But after that the children of Israel were grown strong, they subdued the Chanaanites, and made them their tributaries, and they did not kill them.€€ƒx¸And the children of Joseph spoke to Joshua, and said: Why hast thou given me but one lot and one portion to possess, whereas I am of so great a multitude, and the Lord hath blessed me?€€ƒxÙAnd Joshua said to them: If thou be a great people, go up into the woodland, and cut down room for thyself in the land of the Pherezite and the Raphaims: because the possession of mount Ephraim is too narrow for thee.€€ƒxæAnd the children of Joseph answered him: We cannot go up to the mountains, for the Chanaanites that dwell in the low lands, wherein are situate Bethsan with its towns, and Jezrael in the midst of the valley, have chariots of iron.€€ƒx’And Joshua said to the house of Joseph, to Ephraim and Manasses: Thou art a great people, and of great strength, thou shalt not have one lot only:€€ƒxåBut thou shalt pass to the mountain, and shalt cut down the wood, and make thyself room to dwell in: and mayst proceed farther, when thou hast destroyed the Chanaanites, who as thou sayest have iron chariots, and are very strong.€€ƒ`€€ƒx—And all the children of Israel assembled together in Silo, and there they set up the tabernacle of the testimony, and the land was subdued before them.€€ƒxkBut there remained seven tribes of the children of Israel, which as yet had not received their possessions.€€ƒx–And Joshua said to them: How long are you indolent and slack, and go not in to possess the land which the Lord the God of your fathers hath given you?€€˜ƒx‚Divide to yourselves the land into seven parts: let Juda be in his bounds on the south side, and the house of Joseph on the north.€€ƒx™The land in the midst between these mark ye out into seven parts; and you shall come hither to me, that I may cast lots for you before the Lord your God.€€ƒyFor the Levites have no part among you, but the priesthood of the Lord is their inheritance. And Gad and Ruben, and the half tribe of Manasses have already received their possessions beyond the Jordan eastward: which Moses the servant of the Lord gave them.€€ƒxÄAnd when the men were risen up, to go to mark out the land, Joshua commanded them saying: Go round the land and mark it out, and return to me: that I may cast lots for you before the Lord in Silo.€€ƒx‰So they went and surveying it divided it into seven parts, writing them down in a book. And they returned to Joshua, to the camp in Silo.€€ƒxjAnd he cast lots before the Lord in Silo, and divided the land to the children of Israel into seven parts.€€ƒx–And first came up the lot of the children of Benjamin by their families, to possess the land between the children of Juda, and the children of Joseph.€€ƒxÀAnd their border northward was from the Jordan: going along by the side of Jericho on the north side, and thence going up westward to the mountains, and reaching to the wilderness of Bethaven,€€ƒxAnd passing along southward by Luza, the same is Bethel, and it goeth down into Ataroth-addar to the mountain, that is on the south of the nether Beth-horon.€€ƒyAnd it bendeth thence going round towards the sea, south of the mountain that looketh towards Beth-horon to the southwest: and the outgoings thereof are into Cariathbaal, which is called also Cariathiarim, a city of the children of Juda This is their coast towards the sea, westward.€€ƒxŠBut on the south side the border goeth out from part of Cariathiarim towards the sea, and cometh to the fountain of the waters of Nephtoa.€€ƒyGAnd it goeth down to that part of the mountain that looketh on the valley of the children of Ennom: and is over against the north quarter in the furthermost part of the valley of Raphaim, and it goeth down into Geennom (that is the valley of Ennom) by the side of the Jebusite to the south: and cometh to the fountain of Rogel,€€ƒx_Passing thence to the north, and going out to Ensemes, that is to say, the fountain of the sun:€€ƒxöAnd It passeth along to the hills that are over against the ascent of Adommim: and it goeth down to Abenboen, that is, the stone of Boen the son of Ruben: and it passeth on the north side to the champaign countries; and goeth down Into the plain,€€ƒx‹And it passeth by Bethhagla northward: and the outgoings thereof are towards the north of the most salt sea at the south end of the Jordan.€€ƒxWhich is the border of it on the east side. This is the possession of the children of Benjamin by their borders round about, and their families.€€ƒx<And their cities were, Jericho and Bethhagla and Vale-Casis,€€ƒx"Betharaba and Samaraim and Bethel,€€ƒxAnd Avim and Aphara and Ophera,€€ƒxFThe town Emona and Ophni and Gabee: twelve cities, and their villages.€€ƒxGabam and Rama and Beroth,€€ƒx#And Mesphe, and Caphara, and Amosa,€€ƒx!And Recem, Jarephel, and Tharela,€€ƒx®And Sela, Eleph and Jebus, which is Jerusalem, Gabaath and Cariath: fourteen cities, and their villages. This is the possession of the children of Benjamin by their families.€€ƒ`€€ƒxeAnd the second lot came forth for the children of Simeon by their kindreds: and their inheritance was€€ƒxUIn the midst of the possession of the children of Juda: Bersabee and Sabee and Molada€€ƒxAnd Hasersual, Bala and Asem,€€˜3ƒx-And Siceleg and Bethmarchaboth and Hasersusa,€€ƒxAAnd Bethlebaoth and Sarohen: thirteen cities, and their villages.€€ƒx?And Remmon and Athor and Asan: four cities, and their villages.€€ƒx©And all the villages round about these cities to Baalath Beer Ramath to the south quarter. This is the inheritance of the children of Simeon according to their kindreds,€€ƒx©In the possession and lot of the children of Juda: because it was too great, and therefore the children of Simeon had their possession in the midst of their inheritance.€€ƒxwAnd the third lot fell to the children of Zabulon by their kindreds: and the border of their possession was unto Sarid.€€ƒxyAnd It went up from the sea and from Merala, and came to Debbaseth: as far as the torrent, which is over against Jeconam.€€ƒxAnd it returneth from Sarid eastward to the borders of Ceseleththabor: and it goeth out to Dabereth and ascendeth towards Japhie.€€ƒxvAnd it passeth along from thence to the east side of Gethhepher and Thacasin: and goeth out to Remmon, Amthar and Noa.€€ƒxeAnd it turneth about to the north of Hanathon: and the outgoings thereof are the valley of Jephtahel,€€ƒx]And Cateth and Naalol and Semeron and Jedala and Bethlehem: twelve cities and their villages.€€ƒxqThis is the inheritance of the tribe of the children of Zabulon by their kindreds, the cities and their villages.€€ƒx6The fourth lot came out to Issachar by their kindreds.€€ƒx7And his inheritance was Jezrael and Casaloth and Sunem,€€ƒx%And Hapharaim and Seon and Anaharath,€€ƒxAnd Rabboth and Cesion, Abes,€€ƒx3And Rameth and Engannim and Enhadda and Bethpheses.€€ƒx™And the border thereof cometh to Thabor and Sehesima and Bethsames: and the outgoings thereof shall be at the Jordan: sixteen cities, and their villages.€€ƒx`This is the possession of the sons of Issachar by their kindreds, the cities and their villages.€€ƒxNAnd the fifth lot fell to the tribe of the children of Aser by their kindreds:€€ƒx;And their border was Halcath and Chali and Beten and Axaph,€€ƒx_And Elmelech and Amaad and Messal: and it reacheth to Carmel by the sea and Sihor and Labanath,€€ƒxÁAnd it returneth towards the east to Bethdagon: and passeth along to Zabulon and to the valley of Jephthael towards the north to Bethemec and Nehiel. And it goeth out to the left side of Cabul,€€ƒxFAnd to Abaran and Rohob and Hamon and Cana, as far as the great Sidon.€€ƒxAnd it returneth to Horma to the strong city of Tyre, and to Hosa: and the outgoings thereof shall be at the sea from the portion of Achziba:€€ƒxDAnd Amma and Aphec and Rohob: twenty-two cities, and their villages.€€ƒxdThis is the possession of the children of Aser by their kindreds, and the cities and their villages.€€ƒxAThe sixth lot came out to the sons of Nephtali by their families:€€ƒxlAnd the border began from Heleph and Elon to Saananim, and Adami, which is Neceb, and Jebnael even to Lecum:€€ƒxĞAnd the border returneth westward to Azanotthabor, and goeth out from thence to Hucuca, and passeth along to Zabulon southward, and to Aser westward, and to Juda upon the Jordan towards the rising of the sun.€€ƒxLAnd the strong cities are Assedim, Ser, and Emath, and Reccath and Cenereth,€€ƒxAnd Edema and Arama, Asor,€€ƒxAnd Cedes and Edri, Enhasor,€€ƒx`And Jeron and Magdalel, Horem, and Bethanath and Bethsames: nineteen cities, and their villages.€€ƒxqThis is the possession of the tribe of the children of Nephtali by their kindreds, the cities and their villages.€€ƒxNThe seventh lot came out to the tribe of the children of Dan by their families€€ƒxeAnd the border of their possession was Saraa and Esthaol, and Hirsemes, that is, the city of the sun,€€ƒxSelebin and Aialon and Jethela,€€ƒxElon and Themna and Acron,€€ƒxElthece, Gebbethon and Balaath,€€ƒx,And Juda and Bane and Barach and Gethremmon:€€ƒxDAnd Mejarcon and Arecon, with the border that looketh towards Joppe,€€ƒxãAnd is terminated there. And the children of Dan went up and fought against Lesem, and took it: and they put it to the sword, and possessed it, and dwelt in it, calling the name of it Lesem Dan, by the name of Dan their father.€€ƒxiThis is the possession of the tribe of the sons of Dan, by their kindreds, the cities and their villages.€€ƒx¬And when he had made an end of dividing the land by lot to each one by their tribes, the children of Israel gave a possession to Joshua the son of Nun in the midst of them,€€ƒx“According to the commandment of the Lord, the city which he asked for, Thamnath Saraa, in mount Ephraim: and he built up the city, and dwelt in it.€€ƒyThese are the possessions which Eleazar the priest, and Joshua the son of Nun, and the princes of the families, and of the tribes of the children of Israel, distributed by lot in Silo, before the Lord at the door of the tabernacle of the testimony, and they divided the land.€€ƒ`€€ƒxRAnd the Lord spoke to Joshua, saying: Speak to children of Israel and say to them:€€ƒxGAppoint cities of refuge, of which I spoke to you by the hand of Moses:€€ƒxƒThat whosoever shall kill a person unawares may flee to them, and may escape the wrath of the kinsman, who is the avenger of blood.€€‰ƒxÅAnd when the avenger of blood shall pursue him, they shall not deliver him into his hands, because he slew his neighbour unawares, and is not proved to have been his enemy two or three days before,€€ƒxùAnd he shall dwell in that city, till he stand before judgment to give an account of his fact, and till the death of the high priest, who shall be at that time: then shall the manslayer return, and go into his own city and house from whence he fled.€€ƒx‘And they appointed Cedes in Galilee of mount Nephtali, and Sichem in mount Ephraim, and Cariath-Arbe, the same is Hebron in the mountain of Juda.€€ƒxÜAnd beyond the Jordan to the east of Jericho, they appointed Bosor, which is upon the plain of the wilderness of the tribe of Ruben, and Ramoth in Galaad of the tribe of Gad, and Gaulon in Basan of the tribe of Manasses.€€ƒy=These cities were appointed for all the children of Israel, and for the strangers, that dwelt among them, that whosoever had killed a person unawares might flee to them, and not die by the hand of the kinsman, coveting to revenge the blood that was shed, until he should stand before the people to lay open his cause.€€ƒ`€€ƒx±Then the princes of the families of Levi came to Eleazar the priest, and to Joshua the son of Nun, and to the princes of the kindreds of all the tribes of the children of Israel€€ƒx»And they spoke to them in Silo in the land of Chanaan, and said: The Lord commanded by the hand of Moses, that cities should be given us to dwell in, and their suburbs to feed our cattle.€€ƒx|And the children of Israel gave out of their possessions according to the commandment of the Lord, cities and their suburbs.€€˜-ƒx§And to the rest of the children of Caath, that is, to thee Levites, who remained, out of the tribes of Ephraim, and of Dan, and the half tribe of Manasses, ten cities.€€ƒxµAnd the lot came out to children of Gerson, that they should take of the tribes of Issachar and of Aser and of Nephtali, and of the half tribe of Manasses in Basan, thirteen cities.€€ƒxmAnd to the sons of Merari by their kindreds, of the tribes of Ruben and of Gad and of Zabulon, twelve cities.€€ƒx”And the children of Israel gave to the Levites the cities and their suburbs, as the Lord commanded by the hand of Moses, giving to every one by lot.€€ƒx^Of the tribes of the children of Juda and of Simeon Joshua gave cities: whose names are these,€€ƒxhTo the sons of Aaron, of the families of Caath of the race of Levi (for the first lot came out for them)€€ƒxzThe city of Arbe the father of Enac, which is called Hebron, in the mountain of Juda, and the suburbs thereof round about.€€ƒxdBut the fields and the villages thereof he had given to Caleb the son of Jephone for his possession.€€ƒxŒHe gave therefore to the children of Aaron the priest, Hebron a city of refuge, and the suburbs thereof, and Lebna with the suburbs thereof,€€ƒvAnd Jether and Estemo,€€ƒuAnd Holon, and Dabir,€€ƒxkAnd Ain, and Jeta, and Bethsames, with their suburbs: nine cities out of the two tribes, as hath been said.€€ƒxDAnd out of the tribe of the children of Benjamin, Gabaon, and Gabae,€€ƒx9And Anathoth and Almon, with, their suburbs: four cities.€€ƒx_All the cities together of the children of Aaron the priest, were thirteen, with their suburbs,€€ƒxgAnd to the rest of the families of the children of Caath of the race of Levi was given this possession.€€ƒxrOf the tribe of Ephraim, Sichem one of the cities of refuge, with the suburbs thereof in mount Ephraim, and Gazer,€€ƒx=And Cibsaim, and Beth-horon, with their suburbs, four cities.€€ƒx-And of he tribe of Dan, Eltheco and Gabathon,€€ƒx;And Aialon and Gethremmon, with their suburbs, four cities.€€ƒxYAnd of the half tribe of Manasses, Thanac and Gethremmon, with their suburbs, two cities.€€ƒxoAll the cities were ten, with their suburbs, which were given to the children of Caath, of the inferior degree.€€ƒx®To the children of Gerson also of the race of Levi out of the half tribe of Manasses, Gaulon in Basan, one of the cities of refuge, and Bosra, with their suburbs, two cities.€€ƒx3And of the tribe of Issachar, Cesion, and Dabereth,€€ƒx<And Jaramoth, and Engannim, with their suburbs, four cities.€€ƒx*And of the tribe of Aser, Masal and Abdon,€€ƒx8And Helcath, and Rohob, with their suburbs, four cities.€€ƒxOf the tribe also of Nephtali, Cedes in Galilee, one of the cities of refuge: and Hammoth Dor, and Carthan, with their suburbs, three cities.€€ƒxLAll the cities of the families of Gerson, were thirteen, with their suburbs.€€ƒx‡And to the children of Merari, Levites of the inferior degree, by their families were given of the tribe of Zabulon, Jecnam and Cartha,€€ƒx5And Damna and Naalol, four cities with their suburbs.€€ƒx½Of the tribe of Ruben beyond the Jordan over against Jericho, Bosor in the wilderness, one of the cities of refuge, Misor and Jaser and Jethson and Mephaath, four cities with their suburbs.€€ƒx…Of the tribe of Gad, Ramoth in Galaad, one of the cities of refuge, and Manaim and Hesebon and Jaser, four cities with their suburbs,€€ƒxUAll the cities of the children of Merari by their families and kindreds, were twelve.€€ƒxbSo all the cities of the Levites within the possession of the children of Israel were forty-eight,€€ƒx5With their suburbs, each distributed by the families.€€ƒx€And the Lord God gave to Israel all the land that he had sworn to give to their fathers: and they possessed it, and dwelt in it.€€ƒxAnd he gave them peace from all nations round about: and none of their enemies durst stand against them, but were brought under their dominion.€€ƒxiNot so much as one word, which he had promised to perform unto them, was made void, but all came to pass.€€ƒ`€€ƒx^At the same time Joshua called the Rubenites, and the Gadites, and the half tribe of Manasses,€€ƒx|And said to them: You have done all that Moses the servant of the Lord commanded you: you have also obeyed me in all things,€€ƒxyNeither have you left your brethren this long time, until this present day, keeping the commandment of the Lord your God.€€ƒxİTherefore as the Lord your God hath given your brethren rest and peace, as he promised: return, and go to your dwellings, and to the land of your possession, which Moses the servant of the Lord gave you beyond the Jordan:€€ƒy.Yet so that you observe attentively, and in work fulfil the commandment and the law which Moses the servant of the Lord commanded you: that you love the Lord your God, and walk in all his ways, and keep all his commandments, and cleave to him, and serve him with all your heart, and with all your soul.€€˜"ƒyNow to half the tribe of Manasses, Moses had given a possession in Basan: and therefore to the half that remained, Joshua gave a lot among the rest of their brethren beyond the Jordan to the west. And when he sent them away to their dwellings and had blessed them,€€ƒxÂHe said to them: With much substance and riches, you return to your settlements, with silver and gold, brass and iron, and variety of raiment: divide the prey of your enemies with your brethren.€€ƒy'So the children of Ruben, and the children of Gad, and the half tribe of Manasses returned, and parted from the children of Israel in Silo, which is in Chanaan, to go into Galaad the land of their possession, which they had obtained according to the commandment of the Lord by the hand of Moses.€€ƒx|And when they were come to banks of the Jordan, in the land of Chanaan, they built an altar immensely great near the Jordan.€€ƒyAnd when the children of Israel had heard of it, and certain messengers brought them an account that the children of Ruben, and of Gad, and the half tribe of Manasses had built an altar in the land of Chanaan, upon the banks of the Jordan, over against the children of Israel:€€ƒx<They all assembled in Silo, to go up and fight against them.€€ƒxfAnd in the mean time they sent to them into the land of Galaad, Phinees the son of Eleazar the priest,€€ƒx-And ten princes with him, one of every tribe.€€ƒxyWho came to the children of Ruben, and of Gad, and the half tribe of Manasses, into the land of Galaad, and said to them:€€ƒx¿Thus saith all the people of the Lord: What meaneth this transgression? Why have you forsaken the Lord the God of Israel, building a sacrilegious altar, and revolting from the worship of him?€€ƒx•Is it a small thing to you that you sinned with Beelphegor, and the stain of that crime remaineth in us to this day? and many of the people perished.€€ƒx\And you have forsaken the Lord to day, and to morrow his wrath will rage against all Israel.€€ƒxüBut if you think the land of your possession to be unclean, pass over to the land wherein is the tabernacle of the Lord, and dwell among us: only depart not from the Lord, and from our society, by building an altar beside the altar of the Lord our God.€€ƒxÈDid not Achan the son of Zare transgress the commandment of the Lord, and his wrath lay upon all the people of Israel? And he was but one man, and would to God he alone had perished in his wickedness.€€ƒxyAnd the children of Ruben, and of Gad, and of the half tribe of Manasses answered the princes of the embassage of Israel:€€ƒxØThe Lord the most mighty God, the Lord the most mighty God, he knoweth, and Israel also shall understand: If with the design of transgression we have set up this altar, let him not save us, but punish us immediately:€€ƒxAnd if we did it with that mind, that we might lay upon it holocausts, and sacrifice, and victims of peace offerings, let him require and judge:€€ƒx§And not rather with this thought and design, that we should say: To morrow your children will say to our children: What have you to do with the Lord the God of Israel?€€ƒyThe Lord hath put the river Jordan for a border between us and you, O ye children of Ruben, and ye children of Gad: and therefore you have no part in the Lord. And by this occasion your children shall turn away our children from the fear of the Lord. We therefore thought it best,€€ƒxMAnd said: Let us build us an altar, not for holocausts, nor to offer victims,€€ƒy"But for a testimony between us and you, and our posterity and yours, that we may serve the Lord, and that we may have a right to offer both holocausts, and victims and sacrifices of peace offerings: and that your children to morrow may not say to our children: You have no part in the Lord.€€ƒxµAnd if they will say so, they shall answer them: Behold the altar of the Lord, which our fathers made, not for holocausts, nor for sacrifice, but for a testimony between us and you.€€ƒyGod keep us from any such wickedness that we should revolt from the Lord, and leave off following his steps, by building an altar to offer holocausts, and sacrifices, and victims, beside the altar of the Lord our God, which is erected before his tabernacle.€€ƒxçAnd when Phinees the priest, and the princes of the embassage, who were with him, had heard this, they were satisfied: and they admitted most willingly the words of the children of Ruben, and Gad, and of the half tribe of Manasses,€€ƒxÑAnd Phinees the priest the son of Eleazar said to them: Now we know that the Lord is with us, because you are not guilty of this revolt, and you have delivered the children of Israel from the hand of the Lord.€€ƒx±And he returned with the princes from the children of Ruben and Gad, out of the land of Galaad, into the land of Chanaan, to the children of Israel, and brought them word again.€€ƒxÂAnd the saying pleased all that heard it. And the children of Israel praised God, and they no longer said that they would go up against them, and fight, and destroy the land of their possession.€€ƒx~And the children of Ruben, and the children of Gad called the altar which they had built, Our testimony, that the Lord is God,€€ƒ`€€ƒx¯And when a long time was passed, after that the Lord had given peace to Israel, all the nations round about being subdued. and Joshua being now old, and far advanced in years:€€ƒx¦Joshua called for all Israel, and for the elders, and for the princes, and for the judges, and for the masters, and said to them: I am old, and far advanced in years,€€ƒxtAnd you see all that the Lord your God hath done to all the nations round about, how he himself hath fought for you:€€ƒx†And now since he hath divided to you by lot all the land, from the east of the Jordan unto the great sea, ant many nations yet remain:€€ƒx†The Lord your God will destroy them, and take them away from before your face, and you shall possess the land as he hath promised you.€€ƒxšLest after that you are come in among the Gentiles, who will remain among you, you should swear by the name of their gods, and serve them, and adore them:€€ƒxFBut cleave ye unto the Lord your God, as you have done until this day.€€ƒx…And then the Lord God will take away before your eyes nations that are great and very strong, and no man shall be able to resist you.€€ƒx€One of you shall chase a thousand men of the enemies: because the Lord your God himself will fight for you, as he hath promised.€€ƒxKThis only take care of with all diligence, that you love the Lord your God.€€ƒx}But if you will embrace the errors of these nations that dwell among you, and make marriages with them, and join friendships:€€ƒy!Know ye for a certainty that the Lord your God will not destroy them before your face, but they shall be a pit and a snare in your way, and a stumbling-block at your side, and stakes in your eyes, till he take you away and destroy you from off this excellent land, which he hath given you.€€ƒx¶Behold this day I am going into the way of all the earth, and you shall know with all your mind that of all the words which the Lord promised to perform for you, not one hath failed,€€ƒx÷Therefore as he hath fulfilled in deed, what he promised, and all things prosperous have come: so will he bring upon you all the evils he hath threatened, till he take you away and destroy you from off this excellent land, which he hath given you,€€ƒy<When you shall have transgressed the covenant of the Lord your God, which he hath made with you, and shall have served strange gods, and adored them: then shall the indignation of the Lord rise up quickly and speedily against you, and you shall be taken away from this excellent land, which he hath delivered to you.€€ƒ`€€ƒx·And Joshua gathered together all the tribes of Israel in Sichem, and called for the ancients, and the princes and the judges, and the masters: and they stood in the sight of the Lord:€€ƒxÈAnd he spoke thus to the people: Thus saith the Lord the God of Israel: Your fathers dwelt of old on the other side of the river, Thare the father of Abraham, and Nachor: and they served strange gods.€€ƒx„And I took your father Abraham from the borders of Mesopotamia: and brought him into the land of Chanaan: and I multiplied his seed,€€ƒxAnd gave him Isaac: and to him again I gave Jacob and Esau. And I gave to Esau mount Seir for his possession: but Jacob and his children went down into Egypt.€€ƒxKAnd I sent Moses and Aaron, and I struck Egypt with many signs and wonders.€€˜!ƒxåAnd the children of Israel cried to the Lord: and he put darkness between you and the Egyptians, and brought the sea upon them, and covered them. Your eyes saw all that I did in Egypt, and you dwelt in the wilderness a long time.€€ƒx¿And I brought you into the land of the Amorrhite, who dwelt beyond the Jordan. And when they fought against you, I delivered them into your hands, and you possessed their land, and slew them.€€ƒx‚And Balac son of Sephor king of Moab arose and fought against Israel. And he sent and called for Balaam son of Beor, to curse you:€€ƒxhAnd I would not hear him, but on the contrary I blessed you by him, and I delivered you out of his hand.€€ƒyAnd you passed over the Jordan, and you came to Jericho. And the men of that city fought against you, the Amorrhite, and the Pherezite, and the Chanaanite, and the Hethite, and the Gergesite, and the Hevite, and the Jebusite: and I delivered them into your hands.€€ƒxƒAnd I sent before you and I drove them out from their places, the two kings of the Amorrhites, not with thy sword nor with thy bow,€€ƒx’And I gave you a land, in which you had not laboured, and cities to dwell in which you built not, vineyards and oliveyards, which you planted not.€€ƒx²Now therefore fear the Lord, and serve him with a perfect and most sincere heart: and put away the gods which your fathers served in Mesopotamia and in Egypt, and serve the Lord.€€ƒy0But if it seem evil to you to serve the Lord, you have your choice: choose this day that which pleaseth you, whom you would rather serve, whether the gods which your fathers served in Mesopotamia, or the gods of the Amorrhites, in whose land you dwell: but as for me and my house we will serve thee Lord,€€ƒx_And the people answered, and said, God forbid we should leave the Lord, and serve strange gods.€€ƒxôThe Lord our God he brought us and our fathers out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage: and did very great signs in our sight, and preserved us in all the way by which we journeyed, and among all the people through whom we passed.€€ƒx›And he hath cast out all the nations, the Amorrhite the inhabitant of the land into which we are come. Therefore we will serve the Lord, for he is our God.€€ƒx£And Joshua said to the people: You will not be able to serve the Lord: for he is a holy God, and mighty and jealous, and will not forgive your wickedness and sins.€€ƒxŒIf you leave the Lord, and serve strange gods, he will turn, and will afflict you, and will destroy you after all the good he hath done you.€€ƒxaAnd the people said to Joshua: No, it shall not be so as thou sayest, but we will serve the Lord.€€ƒx‘And Joshua said to the people, You are witnesses, that you yourselves have chosen you the Lord to serve him. And they answered: We are witnesses.€€ƒxtNow therefore, said he, put away strange gods from among you, and incline your hearts to the Lord the God of Israel.€€ƒxkAnd the people said to Joshua: We will serve the Lord our God, and we will be obedient to his commandments.€€ƒxmJoshua therefore on that day made a covenant, and set before the people commandments and judgments in Sichem.€€ƒxAnd he wrote all these things in the volume of the law of the Lord: and he took a great stone, and set it under the oak that was in the sanctuary of the Lord.€€ƒxáAnd he said to all the people: Behold this stone shall be a testimony unto you, that it hath heard all the words of the Lord, which he hath spoken to you: lest perhaps hereafter you will deny it, and lie to the Lord your God.€€ƒx>And he sent the people away every one to their own possession,€€ƒxmAnd after these things Joshua the son of Nun the servant of the Lord died, being a hundred and ten years old:€€ƒxˆAnd they buried him in the border of his possession in Thamnathsare, which is situate in mount Ephraim, on the north side of mount Gaas.€€ƒxµAnd Israel served the Lord all the days of Joshua, and of the ancients that lived a long time after Joshua, and that had known all the works of the Lord which he had done in Israel.€€ƒyAnd the bones of Joseph which the children of Israel had taken out of Egypt, they buried in Sichem, in that part of the field which Jacob had bought of the sons of Hemor the father of Sichem, for a hundred young ewes, and it was in the possession of the sons of Joseph.€€ƒx‹Eleazar also the son of Aaron died: and they buried him in Gabaath that belongeth to Phinees his son, which was given him in mount Ephraim.€€ƒrThe Book of Judges€€ƒ`€€ƒx£After the death of Joshua, the children of Israel consulted the Lord, saying: Who shall go up before us against the Chanaanite, and shall be the leader of the war?€€ƒxUAnd the Lord said: Juda shall go up: behold I have delivered the land into his hands.€€ƒx¯And Juda said to Simeon, his brother: Come up with me into my lot, and fight against the Chanaanite, that I also may go along with thee into thy lot. And Simeon went with him.€€ƒxAnd Juda went up, and the Lord delivered the Chanaanite, and the Pherezite into their hands: and they slew of them in Bezec ten thousand men.€€dJudg¤dnamefJudgesiosis_namedJudglabbreviationdJudghchapters•˜$ƒxŞAnd Adonibezec said: Seventy kings, having their fingers and toes cut off, gathered up the leavings of the meat under my table: as I have done, so hath God requited me. And they brought him to Jerusalem, and he died there.€€ƒxoAnd the children of Juda besieging Jerusalem, took it, and put it to the sword, and set the whole city on fire.€€ƒxAnd afterwards they went down and fought against the Chanaanite, who dwelt in the mountains, and in the south, and in the plains.€€ƒxAnd Juda going forward against the Chanaanite, that dwelt in Hebron, (the name whereof was in former times Cariath-Arbe) slew Sesai, and Ahiman, and Tholmai:€€ƒx‹And departing from thence, he went to the inhabitants of Dabir, the ancient name of which was Cariath-Sepher, that is, the city of letters.€€ƒxpAnd Caleb said: He that shall take Cariath-Sepher, and lay it waste, to him will I give my daughter Axa to wife.€€ƒxtAnd Othoniel, the son of Cenez, the younger brother of Caleb, having taken it, he gave him Axa his daughter to wife.€€ƒx¤And as she was going on her way, her husband admonished her to ask a field of her father. And as she sighed sitting on her ass, Caleb said to her: What aileth thee?€€ƒxBut she answered: Give me a blessing, for thou hast given me a dry land: give me also a watery land So Caleb gave her the upper and the nether watery ground.€€ƒxÍAnd the children of the Cinite, the kinsman of Moses, went up from the city of palms, with the children of Juda, into the wilderness of his lot, which is at the south side of Arad, and they dwelt with him.€€ƒx·And Juda went with Simeon, his brother, and they together defeated the Chanaanites that dwelt in Sephaath, and slew them. And the name of the city was called Horma, that is, Anathema.€€ƒxUAnd Juda took Gaza, with its confines, and Ascalon, and Accaron, with their confines.€€ƒx¬And the Lord was with Juda, and he possessed the hill country: but was not able to destroy the inhabitants of the valley, because they had many chariots armed with scythes.€€ƒxaAnd they gave Hebron to Caleb, as Moses had said, who destroyed out of it the three sons of Enac.€€ƒx«But the sons of Benjamin did not destroy the Jebusites that inhabited Jerusalem: and the Jebusite hath dwelt with the sons of Benjamin in Jerusalem until this present day.€€ƒxLThe house of Joseph also went up against Bethel, and the Lord was with them.€€ƒxDFor when they were besieging the city, which before was called Luza,€€ƒx}They saw a man coming out of the city, and they said to him: Shew us the entrance into the city, and we will shew thee mercy.€€ƒx|And when he had shewed them, they smote the city with the edge of the sword: but that man, and all his kindred, they let go:€€ƒx‚Who being sent away, went into the land of Hetthim, and built there a city, and called it Luza: which is so called until this day.€€ƒx¿Manasses also did not destroy Bethsan, and Thanac, with their villages; nor the inhabitants of Dor, and Jeblaam, and Mageddo, with their villages. And the Chanaanite began to dwell with them.€€ƒxXBut after Israel was grown strong, he made them tributaries, and would not destroy them.€€ƒxQEphraim also did not slay the Chanaanite that dwelt in Gazer, bnt dwelt with him.€€ƒx}Zabulon destroyed not the inhabitants of Cetron, and Naalol: but the Chanaanite dwelt among them, and became their tributary.€€ƒx„Aser also destroyed not the inhabitants of Accho, and of Sidon, of Ahalab, and of Achazib, and of Helba, and of Aphec, and of Rohob:€€ƒxbAnd he dwelt in the midst of the Chanaanites, the inhabitants of that land, and did not slay them.€€ƒxÓNephthali also destroyed not the inhabitants of Bethsames, and of Bethanath: and he dwelt in the midst of the Chanaanites, the inhabitants of the land, and the Bethsamites and Bethanites were tributaries to him.€€ƒxtAnd the Amorrhite straitened the children of Dan in the mountain, and gave them not a place to go down to the plain:€€ƒx©And he dwelt in the mountain Hares, that is, of potsherds, in Aialon and Salebim. And the hand of the house of Joseph was heavy upon him, and he became tributary to him.€€ƒxeAnd the border of the Amorrhite was from the ascent of the scorpion, the rock, and the higher places.€€ƒ`€€ƒxúAnd an angel of the Lord went up from Galgal to the place of weepers, and said: I made you go out of Egypt, and have brought you into the land for which I swore to your fathers: and I promised that I would not make void my covenant with you for ever:€€ƒx®On condition that you should not make a league with the inhabitants of this land, but should throw down their altars: and you would not hear my voice: why have you done this?€€ƒxuWherefore I would not destroy them from before your face; that you may have enemies, and their gods may be your ruin.€€ƒxuAnd when the angel of the Lord spoke these words to all the children of Israel: they lifted up their voice, and wept.€€ƒxxAnd the name of that place was called, The place of weepers, or of tears: and there they offered sacrifices to the Lord.€€—ƒx¬And they served the Lord all his days, and the days of the ancients, that lived a long time after him, and who knew all the works of the Lord, which he had done for Israel.€€ƒx\And Josue, the son of Nun, the servant of the Lord, died, being a hundred and ten years old;€€ƒxxAnd they buried him in the borders of his possession in Thamnathsare, in Mount Ephraim, on the north side of Mount Gaas.€€ƒxAnd all that generation was gathered to their fathers: and there arose others that knew not the Lord and the works which he had done for Israel.€€ƒxTAnd the children of Israel did evil in the sight of the Lord, and they served Baalim€€ƒxóAnd they left the Lord, the God of their fathers, who had brought them out of the land of Egypt: and they followed strange gods, and the gods of the people that dwelt round about them, and they adored them: and they provoked the Lord to anger,€€ƒx,Forsaking him, and serving Baal and Astaroth€€ƒxËAnd the Lord being angry against Israel, delivered them into the hands of plunderers: who took them and sold them to their enemies, that dwelt round about: neither could they stand against their enemies:€€ƒx–But whithersoever they meant to go, the hand of the Lord was upon them, as he had said, and as he had sworn to them: and they were greatly distressed.€€ƒxAnd the Lord raised up judges, to deliver them from the hands of those that oppressed them: but they would not hearken to them,€€ƒxÄCommitting fornication with strange gods, and adoring them. They quickly forsook the way, in which their fathers had walked: and hearing the commandments of the Lord, they did all things contrary.€€ƒx±And when the Lord raised them up judges, in their days, he was moved to mercy, and heard the groanings of the afflicted, and delivered them from the slaughter of the oppressors.€€ƒxüBut after the judge was dead, they returned, and did much worse things than their fathers had done, following strange gods, serving them, and adoring them. They left not their own inventions, and the stubborn way, by which they were accustomed to walk.€€ƒxÀAnd the wrath of the Lord was kindled against Israel, and he said: Behold this nation hath made void my covenant, which I had made with their fathers, and hath despised to hearken to my voice:€€ƒxBI also will not destroy the nations which Josue left when he died:€€ƒxThat through them I may try Israel, whether they will keep the way of the Lord, and walk in it, as their fathers kept it, or not.€€ƒxƒThe Lord therefore left all these nations, and would not quickly destroy them, neither did he deliver them into the hands of Josue.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‰These are the nations which the Lord left, that by them he might instruct Israel, and all that had not known the wars of the Chanaanites:€€ƒxdThat afterwards their children might learn to fight with their enemies, and to be trained up to war:€€ƒx°The five princes of the Philistines, and all the Chanaanites, and the Sidonians, and the Hevites that dwelt in Mount Libanus, from Mount Baal Hermon to the entering into Emath.€€ƒx­And he left them, that he might try Israel by them, whether they would hear the commandments of the Lord, which he had commanded their fathers, by the hand of Moses, or not.€€ƒx˜So the children of Israel dwelt in the midst of the Chanaanite, and the Hethite, and the Amorrhite, and the Pherezite, and the Hevite, and the Jebusite:€€˜ƒxfAnd they did evil in the sight of the Lord, and they forgot their God, and served Baalim and Astaroth.€€ƒxAnd the Lord being angry with Israel, delivered them into the hands of Chusan Rasathaim, king of Mesopotamia, and they served him eight years.€€ƒxAnd they cried to the Lord, who raised them up a saviour, and delivered them; to wit, Othoniel, the son of Cenez, the younger brother of Caleb:€€ƒx¤And the spirit of the Lord was in him, and he judged Israel. And he went out to fight, and the Lord delivered Chusan Rasathaim, king of Syria, and he overthrew him:€€ƒxFAnd the land rested forty years, and Othoniel, the son of Cenez, died.€€ƒxšAnd the children of Israel did evil again in the sight of the Lord: who strengthened against them Eglon, king of Moab: because they did evil in his sight.€€ƒxAnd he joined to him the children of Ammon, and Amalec: and he went and overthrew Israel, and possessed the city of palm trees.€€ƒxFAnd the children of Israel served Eglon, king of Moab, eighteen years.€€ƒxêAnd afterwards they cried to the Lord, who raised them up a saviour, called Aod, the son of Cera, the son of Jemini, who used the left hand as well as the right. And the children of Israel sent presents to Eglon, king of Moab, by him.€€ƒx§And he made himself a two-edged sword, with a haft in the midst of the length of the palm of the hand, and was girded therewith, under his garment, on the right thigh.€€ƒxNAnd he presented the gifts to Eglon, king of Moab Now Eglon was exceeding fat.€€ƒxaAnd when he had presented the gifts unto him he followed his companions that came along with him.€€ƒxµThen returning from Galgal, where the idols were, he said to the king: I have a secret message to thee, O king. And he commanded silence: and all being gone out that were about him,€€ƒx˜Aod went in to him: now he was sitting in a summer parlour alone, and he said: I have a word from God to thee. And he forthwith rose up from his throne.€€ƒxhAnd Aod put forth his left hand, and took the dagger from his right thigh, and thrust it into his belly,€€ƒyWith such force that the haft went in after the blade into the wound, and was closed up with the abundance of fat. So that he did not draw out the dagger, but left it in the body as he had struck it in: and forthwith, by the secret parts of nature, the excrements of the belly came out.€€ƒxFAnd Aod carefully shutting the doors of the parlour, and locking them,€€ƒx¢Went out by a postern door. And the king's servants going in, saw the doors of the parlour shut, and they said: Perhaps he is easing nature in his summer parlour.€€ƒx¦And waiting a long time, till they were ashamed, and seeing that no man opened the door, they took a key: and opening, they found their lord lying dead on the ground.€€ƒx‰But Aod, while they were in confusion, escaped, and passed by the place of the idols from whence he had returned. And he came to Seirath:€€ƒx„And forthwith he sounded the trumpet in Mount Ephraim: and the children of Israel went down with him, he himself going in the front.€€ƒxòAnd he said to them: Follow me: for the Lord hath delivered our enemies, the Moabites, into our hands. And they went down after him, and seized upon the fords of the Jordan, which are in the way to Moab: and they suffered no man to pass over:€€ƒxvBut they slew of the Moabites at that time, about ten thousand, all strong and valiant men: none of them could escape.€€ƒxYAnd Moab was humbled that day under the hand of Israel: and the land rested eighty years.€€ƒx„After him was Samgar, the son of Anath, who slew of the Philistines six hundred men with a ploughshare: and he also defended Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxZAnd the children of Israel again did evil in the sight of the Lord after the death of Aod:€€ƒx¶And the Lord delivered them up into the hands of Jabin, king of Chanaan, who reigned in Asor: and he had a general of his army named Sisara, and he dwelt in Haroseth of the Gentiles.€€ƒx“And the children of Israel cried to the Lord: for he had nine hundred chariots set with scythes and for twenty years had grievously oppressed them.€€ƒx^And there was at that time Debbora, a prophetess, the wife of Lapidoth, who judged the people.€€ƒx£And she sat under a palm tree, which was called by her name, between Rama and Bethel, in Mount Ephraim: and the children of Israel came up to her for all judgment.€€˜ƒx°And I will bring unto thee in the place of the torrent Cison, Sisara, the general of Jabin's army, and his chariots, and all his multitude, and will deliver them into thy hand.€€ƒxjAnd Barac said to her: If thou wilt come with me, I will go: if thou wilt not come with me, I will not go.€€ƒxßShe said to him: I will go, indeed, with thee, but at this time the victory shall not be attributed to thee, because Sisara shall be delivered into the hand of a woman. Debbora therefore arose, and went with Barac to Cedes.€€ƒxxAnd he called unto him Zabulon and Nephthali, and went up with ten thousand fighting men, having Debbora in his company.€€ƒxàNow Haber, the Cinite, had some time before departed from the rest of the Cinites, his brethren, the sons of Hobab, the kinsman of Moses: and had pitched his tents unto the valley, which is called Sennim, and was near Cedes.€€ƒxTAnd it was told Sisara, that Barac, the son of Abinoem, was gone up to Mount Thabor:€€ƒxAnd he gathered together his nine hundred chariots armed with scythes, and all his army, from Haroseth of the Gentiles, to the torrent Cison.€€ƒxÕAnd Debbora said to Barac: Arise, for this is the day wherein the Lord hath delivered Sisara into thy hands: behold, he is thy leader. And Barac went down from Mount Thabor, and ten thousand fighting men with him.€€ƒxÕAnd the Lord struck a terror into Sisara, and all his chariots, and all his multitude, with the edge of the sword, at the sight of Barac; insomuch, that Sisara leaping down from off his chariot, fled away on foot,€€ƒx–And Barac pursued after the fleeing chariots, and the army, unto Haroseth of the Gentiles; and all the multitude of the enemies was utterly destroyed.€€ƒx¦But Sisara fleeing, came to the tent of Jahel, the wife of Haber, the Cinite, for there was peace between Jabin, the king of Asor, and the house of Haber, the Cinite.€€ƒxAnd Jahel went forth to meet Sisara, and said to him: Come in to me, my lord; come in, fear not. He went into her tent, and being covered by her with a cloak,€€ƒx‘Said to her: Give me, I beseech thee, a little water, for I am very thirsty. She opened a bottle of milk, and gave him to drink, and covered him.€€ƒx¥And Sisara said to her: Stand before the door of the tent, and when any shall come and inquire of thee, saying: Is there any man here? thou shalt say: There is none.€€ƒy5So Jahel, Haber's wife, took a nail of the tent, and taking also a hammer: and going in softly, and with silence, she put the nail upon the temples of his head, and striking it with the hammer, drove it through his brain fast into the ground: and so passing from deep sleep to death, he fainted away and died.€€ƒxôAnd behold, Barac came pursuing after Sisara: and Jahel went out to meet him, and said to him: Come, and I will shew thee the man whom thou seekest. And when he came into her tent, he saw Sisara lying dead, and the nail fastened in his temples.€€ƒxRSo God that day humbled Jabin, the king of Chanaan, before the children of Israel:€€ƒxrWho grew daily stronger, and with a mighty hand overpowered Jabin, king of Chanaan, till they quite destroyed him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx>In that day Debbora and Barac, son of Abinoem, sung, and said:€€ƒxRO you of Israel, that have willingly offered your lives to danger, bless the Lord.€€ƒxƒHear, O ye kings, give ear, O ye princes: It is I, it is I, that will sing to the Lord, I will sing to the Lord, the God of Israel.€€ƒx‰O Lord, when thou wentest out of Seir, and passedst by the regions of Edom, the earth trembled, and the heavens and clouds dropped water.€€ƒxjThe mountains melted before the face of the Lord, and Sinai before the face of the Lord the God of Israel.€€˜ƒx\The valiant men ceased, and rested in Israel: until Debbora arose, a mother arose in Israel.€€ƒx‹The Lord chose new wars, and he himself overthrew the gates of the enemies: a shield and spear was not seen among forty thousand of Israel.€€ƒxvMy heart loveth the princes of Israel: O you, that of your own good will offered yourselves to danger, bless the Lord.€€ƒxXSpeak, you that ride upon fair asses, and you that sit in judgment, and walk in the way.€€ƒyWhere the chariots were dashed together, and the army of the enemies was choked, there let the justices of the Lord be rehearsed, and his clemency towards the brave men of Israel: then the people of the Lord went down to the gates, and obtained the sovereignty.€€ƒx{Arise, arise, O Debbora, arise, arise, and utter a canticle. Arise, Barac, and take hold of thy captives, O son of Abinoem.€€ƒxRThe remnants of the people are saved, the Lord hath fought among the valiant ones.€€ƒxÁOut of Ephraim he destroyed them into Amalec, and after him out of Benjamin into thy people, O Amalec: Out of Machir there came down princes, and out of Zabulon they that led the army to fight.€€ƒxãThe captains of Issachar were with Debbora, and followed the steps of Barac, who exposed himself to danger, as one going headlong, and into a pit. Ruben being divided against himself, there was found a strife of courageous men.€€ƒx©Why dwellest thou between two borders, that thou mayst hear the bleatings of the flocks? Ruben being divided against himself, there was found a strife of courageous men.€€ƒxxGalaad rested beyond the Jordan, and Dan applied himself to ships: Aser dwelt on the sea shore, and abode in the havens.€€ƒxOBut Zabulon and Nephthali offered their lives to death in the region of Merome.€€ƒxwThe kings came and fought, the kings of Chanaan fought in Thanac, by the waters of Mageddo and yet they took no spoils.€€ƒxtThere was war made against them from heaven: the stars, remaining in their order and courses, fought against Sisara.€€ƒx†The torrent of Cison dragged their carcasses, the torrent of Cadumim, the torrent of Cison: tread thou, my soul, upon the strong ones.€€ƒxjThe hoofs of the horses were broken whilst the stoutest of the enemies fled amain, and fell headlong down.€€ƒx£Curse ye the land of Meroz, said the angel of the Lord: curse the inhabitants thereof, because they came not to the help of the Lord, to help his most valiant men.€€ƒx[Blessed among women be Jahel, the wife of Haber the Cinite, and blessed be she in her tent.€€ƒx\He asked her water, and she gave him milk, and offered him butter in a dish fit for princes.€€ƒx»She put her left hand to the nail, and her right hand to the workman's hammer, and she struck Sisara, seeking in his head a place for the wound, and strongly piercing through his temples.€€ƒxuBetween her feet he fell: he fainted, and he died: he rolled before her feet, and there he lay lifeless and wretched.€€ƒx¥His mother looked out at a window, and howled: and she spoke from the dining room: Why is his chariot so long in coming back? Why are the feet of his horses so slow?€€ƒxYOne that was wiser than the rest of his wives, returned this answer to her mother in law:€€ƒxÛPerhaps he is now dividing the spoils, and the fairest of the women is chosen out for him: garments of divers colours are given to Sisara for his prey, and furniture of different kinds is heaped together to adorn necks.€€ƒxkSo let all thy enemies perish, O Lord: but let them that love thee shine, as the sun shineth in his rising.€€ƒx$And the land rested for forty years.€€ƒ`€€ƒx~And the children of Israel again did evil in the sight of the Lord: and he delivered them into the hand of Madian seven years,€€ƒxAnd they were grievously oppressed by them. And they made themselves dens and caves in the mountains, and strong holds to resist.€€ƒxZAnd when Israel had sown, Madian and Amalec, and the rest of the eastern nations, came up:€€ƒxÎAnd pitching their tents among them, wasted all things as they were in the blade, even to the entrance of Gaza: and they left nothing at all in Israel for sustenance of life, nor sheep, nor oxen, nor asses.€€˜(ƒx:And Israel was humbled exceedingly in the sight of Madian.€€ƒx?And he cried to the Lord, desiring help against the Madianites.€€ƒx§And he sent unto them a prophet, and he spoke: Thus saith the Lord, the God of Israel: I made you to come up out of Egypt, and brought you out of the house of bondage,€€ƒxŸAnd delivered you out of the hands of the Egyptians, and of all the enemies that afflicted you: and I cast them out at your coming in, and gave you their land.€€ƒx‚And I said: I am the Lord your God, fear not the gods of the Amorrhites, in whose land you dwell. And you would not hear my voice.€€ƒxáAnd an angel of the Lord came, and sat under an oak that was in Ephra, and belonged to Joas, the father of the family of Ezri. And when Gedeon, his son, was threshing and cleansing wheat by the winepress, to flee from Madian,€€ƒx^The angel of the Lord appeared to him, and said: The Lord is with thee, O most valiant of men.€€ƒyAnd Gedeon said to him: I beseech thee, my lord, if the Lord be with us, why have these evils fallen upon us? Where are his miracles, which our fathers have told us of, saying: The Lord brought us out of Egypt but now the Lord hath forsaken us, and delivered us into the hand of Madian.€€ƒx–And the Lord looked upon him, and said: Go, in this thy strength, and thou shalt deliver Israel out of the hand of Madian: know that I have sent thee.€€ƒx§He answered, and said: I beseech thee, my lord wherewith shall I deliver Israel? Behold, my family is the meanest in Manasses, and I am the least in my father's house.€€ƒxXAnd the Lord said to him: I will be with thee: and thou shalt cut off Madian as one man.€€ƒxcAnd he said: If I have found grace before thee, give me a sign that it is thou that speakest to me:€€ƒx‚And depart not hence, till I return to thee, and bring a sacrifice, and offer it to thee. And he answered: I will wait thy coming.€€ƒxÔSo Gedeon went in, and boiled a kid, and made unleavened loaves of a measure of flour: and putting the flesh in a basket, and the broth of the flesh into a pot, he carried all under the oak, and presented to him.€€ƒx¦And the angel of the Lord said to him: Take the flesh and the unleavened loaves, and lay them upon that rock, and pour out the broth thereon. And when he had done so,€€ƒyThe angel of the Lord put forth the tip of the rod, which he held in his hand, and touched the flesh and the unleavened loaves: and there arose a fire from the rock, and consumed the flesh and the unleavened loaves: and the angel of the Lord vanished out of his sight.€€ƒxAnd Gedeon seeing that it was the angel of the Lord, said: Alas, my Lord God: for I have seen the angel of the Lord face to face.€€ƒxKAnd the Lord said to him: Peace be with thee: fear not, thou shalt not die.€€ƒx¢And Gedeon built there an altar to the Lord, and called it the Lord's peace, until this present day. And when he was yet in Ephra, which is of the family of Ezri,€€ƒxÕThat night the Lord said to him: Take a bullock of thy father's, and another bullock of seven years, and thou shalt destroy the altar of Baal, which is thy father's: and cut down the grove that is about the altar:€€ƒyAnd thou shalt build un altar to the Lord thy God, in the top of this rock, whereupon thou didst lay the sacrifice before: and thou shalt take the second bullock, and shalt offer a holocaust upon a pile of the wood, which thou shalt cut down out of the grove.€€ƒxºThen Gedeon, taking ten men of his servants, did as the Lord had commanded him. But fearing his father's house, and the men of that city, he would not do it by day, but did all by night.€€ƒx¸And when the men of that town were risen in the morning, they saw the altar of Baal destroyed, and the grove cut down, and the second bullock laid upon the altar, which then was built.€€ƒx˜And they said one to another: Who hath done this? And when they inquired for the author of the fact, it was said: Gedeon, the son of Joas, did all this.€€ƒx‹And they said to Joas: Bring out thy son hither, that he may die: because he hath destroyed the altar of Baal, and hath cut down his grove.€€ƒxÚHe answered them: Are you the avengers of Baal, that you fight for him? he that is his adversary, let him die before to morrow light appear: if he be a god, let him revenge himself on him that hath cast down his altar.€€ƒxFrom that day Gedeon was called Jerobaal, because Joas had said: Let Baal revenge himself on him that hath cast down his altar.€€ƒx‰Now all Madian, and Amalec, and the eastern people, were gathered together, and passing over the Jordan, camped in the valley of Jezrael.€€ƒxBut the spirit of the Lord came upon Gedeon, and he sounded the trumpet, and called together the house of Abiezer, to follow him.€€ƒxœAnd he sent messengers into all Manasses, and they also followed him : and other messengers into Aser and Zabulon, and Nephthali, and they came to meet him.€€ƒxOAnd Gedeon said to God: If thou wilt save Israel by my hand, as thou hast said,€€ƒxÃI will put this fleece of wool on the floor: if there be dew in the fleece only, and it be dry on all the ground beside, I shall know that by my hand, as thou hast said, thou wilt deliver Israel.€€ƒx[And it was so. And rising before day, wringing the fleece, he filled a vessel with the dew.€€ƒx½And he said again to God: Let not thy wrath be kindled against me, if I try once more, seeking a sign in the fleece. I pray that the fleece only may be dry, and all the ground wet with dew.€€ƒxsAnd God did that night as he had requested: and it was dry on the fleece only, and there was dew on all the ground.€€ƒ`€€ƒxĞThen Jerobaal, who is the same as Gedeon, rising up early, and all the people with him, came to the fountain that is called Harad. Now the camp of Madian was in the valley, on the north side of the high hill.€€ƒxÊAnd the Lord said to Gedeon: The people that are with thee are many, and Madian shall not be delivered into their hands: lest Israel should glory against me, and say: I was delivered by my own strength.€€ƒxÙSpeak to the people, and proclaim in the hearing of all: Whosoever is fearful and timorous, let him return. So two and twenty thousand men went away from Mount Galaad and returned home, and only ten thousand remained.€€ƒxáAnd the Lord said to Gedeon: The people are still too many, bring them to the waters, and there I will try them: and of whom I shall say to thee, This shall go with thee, let him go: whom I shall forbid to go, let him return.€€˜ƒx£And the number of them that had lapped water; casting it with the hand to their mouth, was three hundred men: and all the rest of the multitude had drunk kneeling.€€ƒx¶And the Lord said to Gedeon: By the three hundred men, that lapped water, I will save you, and deliver Madian into thy hand: but let all the rest of the people return to their place.€€ƒxìSo taking victuals and trumpets according to their number, he ordered all the rest of the multitude to depart to their tents: and he with the three hundred gave himself to the battle. Now the camp of Madia was beneath him in the valley.€€ƒxsThe same night the Lord said to him: Arise, and go down into the camp: because I have delivered them into thy hand.€€ƒxMBut if thou be afraid to go alone, let Phara, thy servant, go down with thee.€€ƒxñAnd when thou shalt hear what they are saying, then shall thy hands be strengthened, and thou shalt go down more secure to the enemies' camp. And he went down with Phara his servant, into part of the camp, where was the watch of men in arms.€€ƒx·But Madian and Amalec, and all the eastern people, lay scattered in the valley, as a multitude of locusts: their camels also were innumerable, as the sand that lieth on the sea shore.€€ƒy1And when Gedeon was come, one told his neighbour a dream: and in this manner related what he had seen: I dreamt a dream, and it seemed to me as if a hearth cake of barley bread rolled and came down into the camp of Madian: and when it was come to a tent, it struck it, and beat it down flat to the ground.€€ƒx´He to whom he spoke, answered: This is nothing else but the sword of Gedeon, the son of Joas, a man of Israel. For the Lord hath delivered Madian, and all their camp into his hand.€€ƒxÃAnd when Gedeon had heard the dream, and the interpretation thereof, he adored: and returned to the camp of Israel, and said: Arise, for the Lord hath delivered the camp of Madian into our hands.€€ƒxAnd he divided the three hundred men into three parts, and gave them trumpets in their hands, and empty pitchers, and lamps within the pitchers.€€ƒx~And he said to them: What you shall see me do, do you the same: I will go into one part of the camp, and do you as I shall do.€€ƒxWhen the trumpet shall sound in my hand, do you also blow the trumpets on every side of the camp, and shout together to the Lord and to Gedeon.€€ƒxïAnd Gedeon, and the three hundred men that were with him, went into part of the camp, at the beginning of the midnight watch, and the watchmen being alarmed, they began to sound their trumpets, and to clap the pitchers one against another.€€ƒyAnd when they sounded their trumpets in three places round about the camp, and had broken their pitchers, they held their lamps in their left hands, and with their right hands the trumpets which they blew, and they cried out: The sword of the Lord and of Gedeon:€€ƒxˆStanding every man in his place round about the enemies' camp. So all the camp was troubled, and crying out and howling, they fled away:€€ƒx“And the three hundred men nevertheless persisted sounding the trumpets. And the Lord sent the sword into all the camp, and they killed one another,€€ƒx­Fleeing as far as Bethsetta, and the border of Abelmahula, in Tebbath. But the men of Israel, shouting from Nephthali, and Aser, and from all Manasses, pursued after Madian.€€ƒxìAnd Gedeon sent messengers into all Mount Ephraim, saying: Come down to meet Madian, and take the waters before them to Bethbera and the Jordan. And all Ephraim shouted, and took the waters before them and the Jordan as far as Bethbera.€€ƒxŞAnd having taken two men of Madian, Oreb and Zeb: Oreb they slew in the rock of Oreb, and Zeb in the winepress of Zeb. And they pursued Madian, carrying the heads of Oreb and Zeb to Gedeon, beyond the waters of the Jordan.€€ƒ`€€ƒxËAnd the men of Ephraim said to him: What is this that thou meanest to do, that thou wouldst not call us, when thou wentest to fight against Madian? And they chid him sharply, and almost offered violence.€€ƒx™And he answered them: What could I have done like to that which you have done? Is not one bunch of grapes of Ephraim better than the vintages of Abiezer?€€ƒxÚThe Lord hath delivered into your hands the princes of Madian, Oreb and Zeb: what could I have done like to what you have done? And when he had said this, their spirit was appeased, with which they swelled against him.€€ƒx«And when Gedeon was come to the Jordan, he passed over it with the three hundred men that were with him: who were so weary that they could not pursue after them that fled.€€˜#ƒx®The princes of Soccoth answered: Peradventure the palms of the hands of Zebee and Salmana are in thy hand, and therefore thou demandest that we should give bread to thy army.€€ƒx¥And he said to them: When the Lord therefore shall have delivered Zebee and Salmana into my hands, I will thresh your flesh with the thorns and briers of the desert.€€ƒx¤And going up from thence, he came to Phanuel: and he spoke the like things to the men of that place. And they also answered him, as the men of Soccoth had answered.€€ƒxfHe said, therefore, to them also: When I shall return a conqueror in peace, I will destroy this tower.€€ƒxĞBut Zebee and Salmana were resting with all their army. For fifteen thousand men were left of all the troops of the eastern people, and one hundred and twenty thousand warriors that drew the sword were slain.€€ƒx¥And Gedeon went up by the way of them that dwelt in tents, on the east of Nobe and Jegbaa, and smote the camp of the enemies, who were secure, and suspected no hurt.€€ƒxdAnd Zebee and Salmana fled, and Gedeon pursued and took them, all their host being put in confusion.€€ƒx4And returning from the battle before the sun rising,€€ƒx”He took a boy of the men of Soccoth: and he asked him the names of the princes and ancients of Soccoth, and he described unto him seventy-seven men.€€ƒyAnd he came to Soccoth, and said to them: Behold Zebee, and Salmana, concerning whom you upbraided me, saying: Peradventure the hands of Zebee and Salmana are in thy hands, and therefore thou demandest that we should give bread to the men that are weary and faint.€€ƒxŒSo he took the ancients of the city, and thorns and briers of the desert, and tore them with the same, and cut in pieces the men of Soccoth.€€ƒxEAnd he demolished the tower of Phanuel, and slew the men of the city.€€ƒx¢And he said to Zebee and Salmana: What manner of men were they, whom you slew in Thabor? They answered: They were like thee, and one of them as the son of a king.€€ƒx€He answered them: They were my brethren, the sons of my mother. As the Lord liveth, if you had saved them, I would not kill you.€€ƒxAnd he said to Jether, his eldest son: Arise, and slay them. But he drew not his sword: for he was afraid, being but yet a boy.€€ƒyAnd Zebee and Salmana said: Do thou rise and run upon us: because the strength of a man is according to his age: Gedeon rose up, and slew Zebee and Salmana: and he took the ornaments and bosses, with which the necks of the camels of kings are wont to be adorned.€€ƒx”And all the men of Israel said to Gedeon: Rule thou over us, and thy son, and thy son's son: because thou hast delivered us from the hand of Madian.€€ƒxtAnd he said to them: I will not rule over you, neither shall my son rule over you, but the Lord shall rule over you.€€ƒxAnd he said to them: I desire one request of you: Give me the earlets of your spoils. For the Ismaelites were accustomed to wear golden earlets.€€ƒxƒThey answered: We will give them most willingly. And spreading a mantle on the ground, they cast upon it the earlets of the spoils.€€ƒy␀And the weight of the earlets that he requested, was a thousand seven hundred sicles of gold, besides the ornaments, and jewels, and purple raiment, which the kings of Madian were wont to use, and besides the golden chains that were about the camels necks.€€ƒx£And Gedeon made an ephod thereof, and put it in his city Ephra. And all Israel committed fornication with it, and it became a ruin to Gedeon, and to all his house.€€ƒx¢But Madian was humbled before the children of Israel, neither could they any more lift up their heads: but the land rested for forty years, while Gedeon presided.€€ƒx>So Jerobaal, the son of Joas, went and dwelt in his own house:€€ƒxJAnd he had seventy sons, who came out of his thigh, for he had many wives.€€ƒxSAnd his concubine, that he had in Sichem, bore him a son, whose name was Abimelech.€€ƒxƒAnd Gedeon, the son of Joas died in a good old age, and was buried in the sepulchre of his father, in Ephra, of the family of Ezri.€€ƒx§But after Gedeon was dead, the children of Israel turned again, and committed fornication with Baalim. And they made a covenant with Baal, that he should be their god:€€ƒxqAnd they remembered not the Lord their God, who delivered them out of the hands of all their enemies round about:€€ƒxtNeither did they shew mercy to the house of Jerobaal Gedeon, according to all the good things he had done to Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒx—And Abimelech, the son of Jerobaal, went to Sichem, to his mother's brethren, and spoke to them, and to all the kindred of his mother's father, saying:€€ƒxÛSpeak to all the men of Sichem: whether is better for you that seventy men, all the sons of Jerobaal, should rule over you, or that one man should rule over you? And withal, consider that I am your bone, and your flesh.€€ƒxœAnd his mother's brethren spoke of him to all the men of Sichem, all these words, and they inclined their hearts after Abimelech, saying: He is our brother:€€ƒx¤And they gave him seventy weight of silver out of the temple of Baalberith: wherewith he hired to himself men that were needy, and vagabonds, and they followed him.€€˜9ƒx¤And all the men of Sichem were gathered together, and all the families of the city of Mello: and they went and made Abimelech king, by the oak that stood in Sichem.€€ƒx©This being told to Joatham, he went, and stood on the top of Mount Garizim: and lifting up his voice, he cried, and said: Hear me, ye men of Sichem, so may God hear you.€€ƒx_The trees went to anoint a king over them: and they said to the olive tree: Reign thou over us.€€ƒxuAnd it answered: Can I leave my fatness, which both gods and men make use of, to come to be promoted among the trees?€€ƒx@And the trees said to the fig tree: Come thou and reign over us.€€ƒxuAnd it answered them: Can I leave my sweetness, and my delicious fruits, and go to be promoted among the other trees?€€ƒx<And the trees said to the vine: Come thou and reign over us.€€ƒxnAnd it answered them: Can I forsake my wine, that cheereth God and men, and be promoted among the other trees?€€ƒxCAnd all the trees said to the bramble: Come thou and reign over us.€€ƒx¼And it answered them: If, indeed, you mean to make me king, come ye, and rest under my shadow: but if you mean it not, let fire come out from the bramble, and devour the cedars of Libanus.€€ƒxáNow, therefore, if you have done well, and without sin, in appointing Abimelech king over you, and have dealt well with Jerobaal, and with his house, and have made a suitable return for the benefits of him who fought for you,€€ƒxHAnd exposed his life to dangers, to deliver you from the hand of Madian,€€ƒxİAnd you are now risen up against my father's house, and have killed his sons, seventy men, upon one stone, and have made Abimelech, the son of his handmaid, king over the inhabitants of Sichem, because he is your brother:€€ƒxIf therefore you have dealt well, and without fault, with Jerobaal and his house, rejoice ye, this day, in Abimelech, and may he rejoice in you.€€ƒxÉBut if unjustly: let fire come out from him, and consume the inhabitants of Sichem, and the town of Mello: and let fire come out from the men of Sichem and from the town of Mello, and devour Abimelech.€€ƒxkAnd when he had said thus, he fled, and went into Bera: and dwelt there for fear of Abimelech, his brother.€€ƒx-So Abimelech reigned over Israel three years.€€ƒxnAnd the Lord sent a very evil spirit between Abimelech and the inhabitants of Sichem; who began to detest him,€€ƒxÆAnd to lay the crime of the murder of the seventy sons of Jerobaal, and the shedding of their blood, upon Abimelech, their brother, and upon the rest of the princes of the Sichemites, who aided him.€€ƒx¿And they set an ambush against him on the top of the mountains: and while they waited for his coming, they committed robberies, taking spoils of all that passed by: and it was told Abimelech.€€ƒxˆAnd Gaal, the son of Obed, came with his brethren, and went over to Sichem. And the inhabitants of Sichem, taking courage at his coming,€€ƒxÅWent out into the fields, wasting the vineyards, and treading down the grapes: and singing and dancing, they went into the temple of their god, and in their banquets and cups they cursed Abimelech.€€ƒxñAnd Gaal, the son of Obed, cried: Who is Abimelech, and what is Sichem, that we should serve him? Is he not the son of Jerobaal, and hath made Zebul, his servant, ruler over the men of Emor, the father of Sichem? Why then shall we serve him?€€ƒx¿Would to God that some man would put this people under my hand, that I might remove Abimelech out of the way. And it was said to Abimelech: Gather together the multitude of an army, and come.€€ƒx]For Zebul, the ruler of the city, hearing the words of Gaal, the son of Obed, was very angry,€€ƒx¨And sent messengers privately to Abimelech, saying: Behold, Gaal, the son of Obed, is come into Sichem with his brethren, and endeavoureth to set the city against thee.€€ƒx\Arise, therefore, in the night, with the people that is with thee, and lie hid in the field:€€ƒxšAnd betimes in the morning, at sun rising, set upon the city, and when he shall come out against thee, with his people, do to him what thou shalt be able.€€ƒxfAbimelech, therefore, arose with all his army, by night, and laid ambushes near Sichem in four places.€€ƒxªAnd Gaal, the son of Obed, went out, and stood in the entrance of the gate of the city. And Abimelech rose up, and all his army with him, from the places of the ambushes.€€ƒxÛAnd when Gaal saw the people, he said to Zebul: Behold, a multitude cometh down from the mountains. And he answered him: Thou seest the shadows of the mountains as if they were the heads of men, and this is thy mistake.€€ƒx‹Again Gaal said: Behold, there cometh people down from the midst of the land, and one troop cometh by the way that looketh towards the oak.€€ƒxÁAnd Zebul said to him: Where is now thy mouth, wherewith thou saidst: Who is Abimelech, that we should serve him? Is not this the people which thou didst despise? Go out, and fight against him.€€ƒxUSo Gaal went out, in the sight of the people of Sichem, and fought against Abimelech,€€ƒx}Who chased and put him to flight, and drove him to the city: and many were slain of his people, even to the gate of the city:€€ƒx„And Abimelech sat down in Ruma: but Zebul drove Gaal, and his companions, out of the city, and would not suffer them to abide in it.€€ƒxVSo the day following the people went out into the field. And it was told to Abimelech,€€ƒx©And he took his army, and divided it into three companies, and laid ambushes in the fields. And seeing that the people came out of the city, he arose, and set upon them,€€ƒxWith his own company, assaulting and besieging the city: whilst the two other companies chased the enemies that were scattered about the field.€€ƒxAnd Abimelech assaulted the city all that day: and took it, and killed the inhabitants thereof, and demolished it, so that he sowed salt in it.€€ƒxâAnd when they who dwelt in the tower of Sichem, had heard this, they went into the temple of their god Berith, where they had made a covenant with him, and from thence the place had taken its name, and it was exceeding strong.€€ƒxRAbimelech also hearing that the men of the tower of Sichem were gathered together,€€ƒxàWent up into mount Selmon, he and all his people with him: and taking an axe, he cut down the bough of a tree, and laying it on his shoulder, and carrying it, he said to his companions: What you see me do, do ye out of hand.€€ƒy0So they cut down boughs from the trees, every man as fast as he could, and followed their leader. And surrounding the fort, they set it on fire: and so it came to pass, that with the smoke and with the fire a thousand persons were killed, men and women together, of the inhabitants of the town of Sichem.€€ƒxrThen Abimelech, departing from thence, came to the town of Thebes, which he surrounded and besieged with his army.€€ƒxşAnd there was in the midst of the city a high tower, to which both the men and the women were fled together, and all the princes of the city, and having shut and strongly barred the gate, they stood upon the battlements of the tower to defend themselves.€€ƒxrAnd Abimelech, coming near the tower, fought stoutly: and, approaching to the gate, endeavoured to set fire to it:€€ƒx„And behold, a certain woman casting a piece of a millstone from above, dashed it against the head of Abimelech, and broke his skull.€€ƒx¶And he called hastily to his armourbearer, and said to him: Draw thy sword, and kill me: lest it should be said that I was slain by a woman. He did as he was commanded, and slew him.€€ƒxWAnd when he was dead all the men of Israel that were with him, returned to their homes.€€ƒxaAnd God repaid the evil that Abimelech had done against his father, killing his seventy brethren.€€ƒxxThe Sichemites also were rewarded for what they had done, and the curse of Joatham, the son of Jerobaal, came upon them.€€ƒ`€€ƒx“After Abimelech, there arose a ruler in Israel, Thola, son of Phua, the uncle of Abimelech, a man of Issachar, who dwelt in Samir of mount Ephraim:€€ƒxRAnd he judged Israel three and twenty years, and he died, and was buried in Samir.€€ƒxQTo him succeeded Jair, the Galaadite, who judged Israel for two and twenty years,€€ƒxÍHaving thirty sons, that rode on thirty ass colts, and were princes of thirty cities, which from his name were called Havoth Jair, that is, the towns of Jair, until this present day, in the land of Galaad.€€’ƒyBut the children of Israel, adding new sins to their old ones, did evil in the sight of the Lord, and served idols, Baalim and Astaroth, and the gods of Syria, and of Sidon, and of Moab, and of the children of Ammon, and of the Philistines: and they left the Lord, and did not serve him.€€ƒxsAnd the Lord being angry with them, delivered them into the hands of the Philistines, and of the children of Ammon.€€ƒx›And they were afflicted, and grievously oppressed for eighteen years, all they that dwelt beyond the Jordan in the land of the Amorrhite, who is in Galaad:€€ƒxŒInsomuch that the children of Ammon, passing over the Jordan, wasted Juda, and Benjamin, and Ephraim: and Israel was distressed exceedingly.€€ƒx…And they cried to the Lord, and said, We have sinned against thee, because we have forsaken the Lord our God, and have served Baalim.€€ƒxuAnd the Lord said to them: Did not the Egyptians, and the Amorrhites, and the children of Ammon, and the Philistines,€€ƒxuThe Sidonians also, and Amalec, and Chanaan, oppress you, and you cried to me, and I delivered you out of their hand?€€ƒxeAnd yet you have forsaken me, and have worshipped strange gods: therefore I will deliver you no more:€€ƒx_Go, and call upon the gods which you have chosen: let them deliver you in the time of distress.€€ƒxAnd the children of Israel said to the Lord: We have sinned, do thou unto us whatsoever pleaseth thee: only deliver us this time.€€ƒx¡And saying these things, they cast away out of their coasts all the idols of strange gods, and served the Lord their God: and he was touched with their miseries.€€ƒx¤And the children of Ammon shouting together, pitched their tents in Galaad: against whom the children of Israel assembled themselves together, and camped in Maspha.€€ƒx¨And the princes of Galaad said one to another: Whosoever of us shall first begin to fight against the children of Ammon, he shall be the leader of the people of Galaad.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‘There was at that time Jephte, the Galaadite, a most valiant man, and a warrior, the son of a woman that was a harlot, and his father was Galaad.€€ƒxÀNow Galaad had a wife of whom he had sons: who, after they were grown up, thrust out Jephte, saying: Thou canst not inherit in the house of our father, because thou art born of another mother.€€ƒx™Then he fled and avoided them, and dwelt in the land of Tob: and there were gathered to him needy men and robbers, and they followed him as their prince.€€ƒx<In those days the children of Ammon made war against Israel.€€˜(ƒx\And they said to him: Come thou, and be our prince, and fight against the children of Ammon.€€ƒx”And he answered them: Are not you the men that hated me, and cast me out of my father's house, and now you are come to me, constrained by necessity?€€ƒxÆAnd the princes of Galaad said to Jephte: For this cause we are now come to thee, that thou mayst go with us, and fight against the children of Ammon, and be head over all the inhabitants of Galaad.€€ƒxºJephte also said to them: If you be come to me sincerely, that I should fight for you against the children of Ammon, and the Lord shall deliver them into my hand, shall I be your prince?€€ƒx~They answered him: The Lord, who heareth these things, he himself is mediator and witness that we will do as we have promised.€€ƒx•Jephte therefore went with the princes of Galaad, and all the people made him their prince. And Jephte spoke all his words before the Lord in Maspha.€€ƒxŸAnd he sent messengers to the king of the children of Ammon, to say in his name: What hast thou to do with me, that thou art come against me, to waste my land?€€ƒxÅAnd he answered them: Because Israel took away my land, when he came up out of Egypt, from the confines of the Arnon unto the Jaboc and the Jordan: now, therefore, restore the same peaceably to me.€€ƒxSAnd Jephte again sent word by them, and commanded them to say to the king of Ammon:€€ƒxdThus saith Jephte: Israel did not take away the land of Moab, nor the land of the children of Ammon:€€ƒxeBut when they came up out of Egypt, he walked through the desert to the Red Sea, and came into Cades.€€ƒxğAnd he sent messengers to the king of Edom, saying: Suffer me to pass through thy land. But he would not condescend to his request. He sent also to the king of Moab, who, likewise, refused to give him passage. He abode, therefore, in Cades,€€ƒxÒAnd went round the land of Edom at the side, and the land of Moab: and came over against the east coast of the land of Moab, and camped on the other side of the Arnon: and he would not enter the bounds of Moab.€€ƒx˜So Israel sent messengers to Sehon, king of the Amorrhites, who dwelt in Hesebon, and they said to him: Suffer me to pass through thy land to the river.€€ƒx¸But he, also despising the words of Israel, suffered him not to pass through his borders: but gathering an infinite multitude, went out against him to Jasa, and made strong opposition.€€ƒx¦And the Lord delivered him, with all his army, into the hands of Israel, and he slew him, and possessed all the land of the Amorrhite, the inhabitant of that country,€€ƒx^And all the coasts thereof from the Arnon to the Jaboc, and from the wilderness to the Jordan.€€ƒx‡So the Lord, the God of Israel, destroyed the Amorrhite, his people of Israel fighting against him, and wilt thou now possess his land?€€ƒx™Are not those things which thy god Chamos possesseth, due to thee by right? But what the Lord our God hath obtained by conquest, shall be our possession:€€ƒx’Unless, perhaps, thou art better than Balac, the son of Sephor, king of Moab: or canst shew that he strove against Israel, and fought against him,€€ƒxßWhereas he hath dwelt in Hesebon, and the villages thereof, and in Aroer, and its villages, and in all the cities near the Jordan, for three hundred years. Why have you for so long a time attempted nothing about this claim?€€ƒx·Therefore I do not trespass against thee, but thou wrongest me by declaring an unjust war against me. The Lord be judge, and decide this day, between Israel and the children of Ammon.€€ƒxtAnd the king of the children of Ammon would not hearken to the words of Jephte, which he sent him by the messengers.€€ƒx¥Therefore the spirit of the Lord came upon Jephte, and going round Galaad, and Manasses, and Maspha of Galaad, and passing over from thence to the children of Ammon,€€ƒx\He made a vow to the Lord, saying: If thou wilt deliver the children of Ammon into my hands,€€ƒx³Whosoever shall first come forth out of the doors of my house, and shall meet me, when I return in peace from the children of Ammon, the same will I offer a holocaust to the Lord.€€ƒxrAnd Jephte passed over to the children of Ammon to fight against them: and the Lord delivered them into his hands.€€ƒxÕAnd he smote them from Aroer till you come to Mennith, twenty cities, and as far as Abel, which is set with vineyards, with a very great slaughter: and the children of Ammon were humbled by the children of Israel.€€ƒxŠAnd when Jephte returned into Maspha, to his house, his only daughter met him with timbrels and with dances: for he had no other children.€€ƒxÂAnd when he saw her, he rent his garments, and said: Alas! my daughter, thou hast deceived me, and thou thyself art deceived: for I have opened my mouth to the Lord, and I can do no other thing.€€ƒx¾And she answered him: My father, if thou hast opened thy mouth to the Lord, do unto me whatsoever thou hast promised, since the victory hath been granted to thee, and revenge of thy enemies.€€ƒx¬And she said to her father: Grant me only this, which I desire: Let me go, that I may go about the mountains for two months, and may bewail my virginity with my companions.€€ƒx¡And he answered her: Go. And he sent her away for two months. And when she was gone with her comrades and companions, she mourned her virginity in the mountains.€€ƒx·And the two months being expired, she returned to her father, and he did to her as he had vowed, and she knew no man. From thence came a fashion in Israel, and a custom has been kept:€€ƒxƒThat, from year to year, the daughters of Israel assemble together, and lament the daughter of Jephte the Galaadite, for four days.€€ƒ`€€ƒx÷But behold there arose a sedition in Ephraim. And passing towards the north, they said to Jephte: When thou wentest to fight against the children of Ammon, why wouldst thou not call us, that we might go with thee? Therefore we will burn thy house.€€ƒxAnd he answered them: I and my people were at great strife with the children of Ammon: and I called you to assist me, and you would not do it.€€ƒxÏAnd when I saw this, I put my life in my own hands, and passed over against the children of Ammon and the Lord delivered them into my hands. What have I deserved, that you should rise up to fight against me?€€ƒxÖThen calling to him all the men of Galaad, he fought against Ephraim: and the men of Galaad defeated Ephraim, because he had said: Galaad is a fugitive of Ephraim, and dwelleth in the midst of Ephraim and Manasses.€€ƒy3They asked him: Say then, Scibboleth, which is interpreted, An ear of corn. But he answered, Sibboleth, not being able to express an ear of corn by the same letter. Then presently they took him and killed him in the very passage of the Jordan. And there fell at that time of Ephraim, two and forty thousand.€€ƒxfAnd Jephte, the Galaadite, judged Israel six years: and he died, and was buried in his city of Galaad.€€ƒx,After him Abesan of Bethlehem judged Israel:€€ƒxÆHe had thirty sons, and as many daughters, whom he sent abroad, and gave to husbands, and took wives for his sons, of the same number, bringing them into his house. And he judged Israel seven years:€€ƒx)And he died, and was buried in Bethlehem.€€ƒxGTo him succeeded Ahialon, a Zabulonite: and he judged Israel ten years:€€ƒx'And he died, and was buried in Zabulon.€€ƒxBAfter him, Abdon, the son of Illel, a Pharathonite, judged Israel:€€ƒxvAnd he had forty sons, and of them thirty grandsons, mounted upon seventy ass colts, and he judged Israel eight years:€€ƒxZAnd he died, and was buried in Pharathon, in the land of Ephraim, in the mount of Amalech.€€ƒ`€€ƒxˆAnd the children of Israel did evil again in the sight of the Lord: and he delivered them into the hands of the Philistines forty years.€€ƒxlNow there was a certain man of Saraa, and of the race of Dan, whose name was Manue, and his wife was barren.€€ƒxAnd an angel of the Lord appeared to her, and said: Thou art barren and without children: but thou shalt conceive and bear a son.€€ƒxXNow therefore beware, and drink no wine nor strong drink, and eat not any unclean thing.€€˜ƒxÚAnd when she was come to her husband, she said to him: A man of God came to me, having the countenance of an angel, very awful. And when I asked him whence he came, and by what name he was called, he would not tell me:€€ƒxöBut he answered thus: Behold thou shalt conceive and bear a son: beware thou drink no wine, nor strong drink, nor eat any unclean thing: for the child shall be a Nazarite of God from his infancy, from his mother's womb until the day of his death.€€ƒxÆThen Manue prayed to the Lord, and said: I beseech thee, O Lord, that the man of God, whom thou didst send, may come again, and teach us what we ought to do concerning the child, that shall be born.€€ƒxÂAnd the Lord heard the prayer of Manue, and the angel of the Lord appeared again to his wife, as she was sitting in the field. But Manue her husband was not with her. And when she saw the angel,€€ƒxtShe made haste, and ran to her husband: and told him, saying: Behold the man hath appeared to me, whom I saw before.€€ƒx‚He rose up, and followed his wife: and coming to the man, said to him: Art thou he that spoke to the woman? And he answered: I am.€€ƒx…And Manue said to him: When thy word shall come to pass, what wilt thou that the child should do? or from what shall he keep himself?€€ƒxsAnd the angel of the Lord said to Manue: From all the things I have spoken of to thy wife, let her refrain herself:€€ƒx¸And let her eat nothing that cometh of the vine, neither let her drink wine or strong drink, nor eat any unclean thing: and whatsoever I have commanded her, let her fulfil and observe.€€ƒxrAnd Manue said to the angel of the Lord: I beseech thee to consent to my request, and let us dress a kid for thee.€€ƒx´And the angel answered him: If thou press me I will not eat of thy bread: but if thou wilt offer a holocaust, offer it to the Lord. And Manue knew not it was the angel of the Lord.€€ƒx_And he said to him: What is thy name, that, if thy word shall come to pass, we may honour thee?€€ƒxAAnd he answered him: Why askest thou my name, which is wonderful?€€ƒx¡Then Manue took a kid of the flocks, and the libations, and put them upon a rock, offering to the Lord, who doth wonderful things: and he and his wife looked on.€€ƒx®And when the flame from the altar went up towards heaven, the angel of the Lord ascended also in the same. And when Manue and his wife saw this, they fell flat on the ground;€€ƒxtAnd the angel of the Lord appeared to them no more. And forthwith Manue understood that it was an angel of the Lord,€€ƒxJAnd he said to his wife: We shall certainly die, because we have seen God.€€ƒxàAnd his wife answered him: If the Lord had a mind to kill us, he would not have received a holocaust and libations at our hands; neither would he have shewed us all these things, nor have told us the things that are to come.€€ƒx]And she bore a son, and called his name Samson. And the child grew, and the Lord blessed him.€€ƒx^And the Spirit of the Lord began to be with him in the camp of Dan, between Saraa and Esthaol.€€ƒ`€€ƒxaThen Samson went down to Thamnatha, and seeing there a woman of the daughters of the Philistines,€€ƒxŸHe came up, and told his father and his mother, saying: I saw a woman in Thamnatha of the daughters of the Philistines: I beseech you, take her for me to wife.€€ƒyAnd his father and mother said to him: Is there no woman among the daughters of thy brethren, or among all my people, that thou wilt take a wife of the Philistines, who are uncircumcised? And Samson said to his father: Take this woman for me; for she hath pleased my eyes.€€ƒx°Now his parents knew not that the thing was done by the Lord, and that he sought an occasion against the Philistines: for at that time the Philistines had dominion over Israel.€€”ƒxÀAnd the Spirit of the Lord came upon Samson, and he tore the lion as he would have torn a kid in pieces, having nothing at all in his hand: and he would not tell this to his father and mother.€€ƒxCAnd he went down, and spoke to the woman that had pleased his eyes.€€ƒxªAnd after some days, returning to take her, he went aside to see the carcass of the lion, and behold there was a swarm of bees in the mouth of the lion, and a honey-comb.€€ƒxÒAnd when he had taken it in his hands, he went on eating: and coming to his father and mother, he gave them of it, and they ate: but he would not tell them that he had taken the honey from the body of the lion.€€ƒxkSo his father went down to the woman, and made a feast for his son Samson: for so the young men used to do.€€ƒx_And when the citizens of that place saw him, they brought him thirty companions to be with him.€€ƒx«And Samson said to them: I will propose to you a riddle, which if you declare unto me within the seven days of the feast, I will give you thirty shirts, and as many coats:€€ƒx§But if you shall not be able to declare it, you shall give me thirty shirts and the same number of coats. They answered him: Put forth the riddle, that we may hear it.€€ƒx˜And he said to them: Out of the eater came forth meat, and out of the strong came forth sweetness. And they could not for three days expound the riddle.€€ƒy
And when the seventh day came, they said to the wife of Samson: Sooth thy husband, and persuade him to tell thee what the riddle meaneth. But if thou wilt not do it, we will burn thee, and thy father's house. Have you called us to the wedding on purpose to strip us?€€ƒySo she wept before Samson and complained, saying: Thou hatest me, and dost not love me: therefore thou wilt not expound to me the riddle, which thou hast proposed to the sons of my people. But he answered: I would not tell it to my father and mother: and how can I tell it to thee?€€ƒx°So she wept before him the seven days of the feast: and, at length, on the seventh day, as she was troublesome to him, he expounded it. And she immediately told her countrymen.€€ƒxßAnd they, on the seventh day before the sun went down, said to him: What is sweeter than honey? and what is stronger than a lion? And he said to them: If you had not ploughed with my heifer, you had not found out my riddle.€€ƒxéAnd the Spirit of the Lord came upon him, and he went down to Ascalon, and slew there thirty men whose garments he took away, and gave to them that had declared the riddle. And being exceeding angry, he went up to his father's house:€€ƒxKBut his wife took one of his friends and bridal companions for her husband.€€ƒ`€€ƒxóAnd a while after, when the days of the wheat harvest were at hand, Samson came, meaning to visit his wife, and he brought her a kid of the flock. And when he would have gone into her chamber, as usual, her father would not suffer him, saying:€€ƒx£I thought thou hadst hated her, and therefore I gave her to thy friend: but she hath a sister, who is younger and fairer than she, take her to wife instead of her.€€ƒxzAnd Samson answered him: From this day I shall be blameless in what I do against the Philistines: for I will do you evils.€€ƒxrAnd he went and caught three hundred foxes, and coupled them tail to tail, and fastened torches between the tails:€€”ƒy	Then the Philistines said: Who hath done this thing? And it was answered: Samson, the son in law of the Thamnathite, because he took away his wife, and gave her to another, hath done these things. And the Philistines went up and burnt both the woman and her father.€€ƒxnBut Samson said to them: Although you have done this, yet will I be revenged of you, and then I will be quiet.€€ƒx¢And he made a great slaughter of them, so that in astonishment they laid the calf of the leg upon the thigh. And going down he dwelt in a cavern of the rock Etam.€€ƒx¥Then the Philistines going up into the land of Juda, camped in the place which afterwards was called Lechi, that is, the Jawbone, where their army was spread abroad.€€ƒxªAnd the men of the tribe of Juda said to them: Why are you come up against us? They answered: We are come to bind Samson, and to pay him for what he hath done against us.€€ƒxîWherefore three thousand men of Juda went down to the cave of the rock Etam, and said to Samson: Knowest thou not that the Philistines rule over us? Why wouldst thou do thus? And he said to them: As they did to me, so have I done to them.€€ƒx¶And they said to him: We are come to bind thee, and to deliver thee into the hands of the Philistines. And Samson said to them: Swear to me, and promise me that you will not kill me.€€ƒxThey said: We will not kill thee: but we will deliver thee up bound. And they bound him with two new cords, and brought him from the rock Etam.€€ƒyNow when he was come to the place of the Jawbone, and the Philistines shouting went to meet him, the Spirit of the Lord came strongly upon him: and as flax is wont to be consumed at the approach of fire, so the bands with which he was bound were broken and loosed.€€ƒxuAnd finding a jawbone, even the jawbone of an ass, which lay there, catching it up, he slew therewith a thousand men.€€ƒxAnd he said: With the jawbone of an ass, with the jaw of the colt of asses, I have destroyed them, and have slain a thousand men.€€ƒx´And when he had ended these words, singing, he threw the jawbone out of his hand, and called the name of that place Ramathlechi, which is interpreted the lifting up of the jawbone.€€ƒxŞAnd being very thirsty, he cried to the Lord, and said: Thou hast given this very great deliverance and victory into the hand of thy servant: and behold I die for thirst, and shall fall into the hands of the uncircumcised.€€ƒyThen the Lord opened a great tooth in the jaw of the ass and waters issued out of it. And when he had drunk them, he refreshed his spirit, and recovered his strength. Therefore the name of that place was called The Spring of him that invoked from the jawbone, until this present day.€€ƒxCAnd he judged Israel, in the days of the Philistines, twenty years.€€ƒ`€€ƒxNHe went also into Gaza, and saw there a woman, a harlot, and went in unto her.€€ƒyAnd when the Philistines had heard this, and it was noised about among them, that Samson was come into the city, they surrounded him, setting guards at the gate of the city, and watching there all the night in silence, that in the morning they might kill him as he went out.€€ƒxáBut Samson slept till midnight, and then rising, he took both the doors of the gate, with the posts thereof and the bolt, and laying them on his shoulders, carried them up to the top of the hill, which looketh towards Hebron.€€ƒxYAfter this he loved a woman, who dwelt in the valley of Sorec, and she was called Dalila.€€˜ƒx¤And Dalila said to Samson: Tell me, I beseech thee, wherein thy greatest strength lieth, and what it is, wherewith if thou wert bound, thou couldst not break loose.€€ƒx‹And Samson answered her: If I shall be bound with seven cords, made of sinews not yet dry, but still moist, I shall be weak like other men.€€ƒxoAnd the princes of the Philistines brought unto her seven cords, such as he spoke of, with which she bound him;€€ƒy7Men lying privately in wait with her, and in the chamber, expecting the event of the thing, and she cried out to him: The Philistines are upon thee, Samson. And he broke the bands, as a man would break a thread of tow twined with spittle, when it smelleth the fire: so it was not known wherein his strength lay.€€ƒxŒAnd Dalila said to him: Behold thou hast mocked me, and hast told me a false thing: but now at least tell me wherewith thou mayest be bound.€€ƒxuAnd he answered her: If I shall be bound with new ropes, that were never in work, I shall be weak and like other men.€€ƒx¼Dalila bound him again with these, and cried out: The Philistines are upon thee, Samson, there being an ambush prepared for him in the chamber. But he broke the bands like threads of webs.€€ƒyAnd Dalila said to him again: How long dost thou deceive me, and tell me lies? Shew me wherewith thou mayest be bound. And Samson answered her: If thou plattest the seven locks of my head with a lace, and tying them round about a nail, fastenest it in the ground, I shall be weak.€€ƒx¦And when Dalila had done this, she said to him: The Philistines are upon thee, Samson. And awaking out of his sleep, he drew out the nail with the hairs and the lace.€€ƒx¾And Dalila said to him: How dost thou say thou lovest me, when thy mind is not with me? Thou hast told me lies these three times, and wouldst not tell me wherein thy greatest strength lieth.€€ƒxŸAnd when she pressed him much, and continually hung upon him for many days, giving him no time to rest, his soul fainted away, and was wearied even unto death.€€ƒyThen opening the truth of the thing, he said to her: The razor hath never come upon my head, for I am a Nazarite, that is to say, consecrated to God from my mother's womb: If my head be shaven, my strength shall depart from me, and I shall become weak, and shall be like other men.€€ƒxñThen seeing that he had discovered to her all his mind, she sent to the princes of the Philistines, saying: Come up this once more, for now he hath opened his heart to me. And they went up, taking with them the money which they had promised.€€ƒxßBut she made him sleep upon her knees, and lay his head in her bosom. And she called a barber and shaved his seven locks, and began to drive him away, and thrust him from her: for immediately his strength departed from him.€€ƒxÃAnd she said: The Philistines are upon thee, Samson. And awaking from sleep, he said in his mind: I will go out as I did before, and shake myself, not knowing that the Lord was departed from him.€€ƒx›Then the Philistines seized upon him, and forthwith pulled out his eyes, and led him bound in chains to Gaza, and shutting him up in prison made him grind.€€ƒx%And now his hair began to grow again,€€ƒx·And the princes of the Philistines assembled together, to offer great sacrifices to Dagon their god, and to make merry, saying: Our god hath delivered our enemy Samson into our hands.€€ƒx±And the people also seeing this, praised their god, and said the same: Our god hath delivered our adversary into our hands, him that destroyed our country, and killed very many.€€ƒx÷And rejoicing in their feasts, when they had now taken their good cheer, they commanded that Samson should be called, and should play before them. And being brought out of prison, he played before them; and they made him stand between two pillars.€€ƒx™And he said to the lad that guided his steps: Suffer me to touch the pillars which support the whole house, and let me lean upon them, and rest a little.€€ƒxŞNow the house was full of men and women, and all the princes of the Philistines were there. Moreover about three thousand persons of both sexes, from the roof and the higher part of the house, were beholding Samson's play.€€ƒxÑBut he called upon the Lord, saying: O Lord God remember me, and restore to me now my former strength, O my God, that I may revenge myself on my enemies, and for the loss of my two eyes I may take one revenge.€€ƒx„And laying hold on both the pillars on which the house rested, and holding the one with his right hand, and the other with his left,€€ƒxöHe said: Let me die with the Philistines. And when he had strongly shook the pillars, the house fell upon all the princes, and the rest of the multitude, that was there: and he killed many more at his death, than he had killed before in his life.€€ƒx´And his brethren and all his kindred, going down took his body, and buried it between Saraa and Esthaol, in the buryingplace of his father Manue: and he judged Israel twenty years.€€ƒ`€€ƒxEThere was at that time a man of mount Ephraim, whose name was Michas.€€ƒxòWho said to his mother: The eleven hundred pieces of silver, which thou hadst put aside for thyself, and concerning which thou didst swear in my hearing, behold I have, and they are with me. And she said to him. Blessed be my son by the Lord.€€ƒxÑSo he restored them to his mother, who said to him: I have consecrated and vowed this silver to the Lord, that my son may receive it at my hand, and make a graven and a molten god; so now I deliver it to thee.€€ƒx¼And he restored them to his mother: and she took two hundred pieces of silver and gave them to the silversmith, to make of them a graven and a molten God, which was in the house of Michas.€€ƒx`In those days there was no king in Israel, but every one did that which seemed right to himself.€€ƒxqThere was also another young man of Bethlehem Juda, of the kindred thereof: and he was a Levite, and dwelt there.€€ƒxíNow he went out from the city of Bethlehem, and desired to sojourn wheresoever he should find it convenient for him. And when he was come to mount Ephraim, as he was on his journey, and had turned aside a little into the house of Michas,€€ƒxªHe was asked by him whence he came. And he answered: I am a Levite of Bethlehem Juda, and I am going to dwell where I can, and where I shall find a place to my advantage.€€ƒxªAnd Michas said: Stay with me, and be unto me a father and a priest, and I will give thee every year ten pieces of silver, and a double suit of apparel, and thy victuals.€€ƒxLHe was content, and abode with the man, and was unto him as one of his sons.€€ƒxRAnd Michas filled his hand, and had the young man with him for his priest, saying:€€ƒxQNow I know God will do me good, since I have a priest of the race of the Levites.€€ƒ`€€ƒx±In those days there was no king in Israel, and the tribe of Dan sought them an inheritance to dwell in: for unto that day they had not received their lot among the other tribes.€€ƒy6So the children of Dan sent five most valiant men, of their stock and family, from Saraa and Esthaol, to spy out the land, and to view it diligently: and they said to them: Go, and view the land. They went on their way, and when they came to mount Ephraim, they went into the house of Michas, and rested there:€€ƒx¦And knowing the voice of the young man the Levite, and lodging with him, they said to him: Who brought thee hither? what dost thou here? why wouldst thou come hither?€€ƒxcHe answered them: Michas hath done such and such things for me, and hath hired me to be his priest.€€˜ƒxYHe answered them: Go in peace: the Lord looketh on your way, and the journey that you go.€€ƒySo the five men going on came to Lais: and they saw how the people dwelt therein without any fear, according to the custom of the Sidonians, secure and easy, having no man at all to oppose them, being very rich, and living separated, at a distance from Sidon and from all men.€€ƒxsAnd they returned to their brethren in Saraa and Esthaol, who asked them what they had done: to whom they answered:€€ƒx²Arise, and let us go up to them: for we have seen the land which is exceeding rich and fruitful: neglect not, lose no time: let us go and possess it, there will be no difficulty.€€ƒx¯We shall come to a people that is secure, into a spacious country, and the Lord will deliver the place to us, in which there is no want of any thing that groweth on the earth.€€ƒxyThere went therefore of the kindred of Dan, to wit, from Saraa and Esthaol, six hundred men, furnished with arms for war.€€ƒxƒAnd going up they lodged in Cariathiarim of Juda: which place from that time is called the camp of Dan, and is behind Cariathiarim.€€ƒx[From thence they passed into mount Ephraim. And when they were come to the house of Michas,€€ƒxãThe five men, that before had been sent to view the land of Lais, said to the rest of their brethren: You know that in these houses there is an ephod and theraphim, and a graven and a molten god: see what you are pleased to do.€€ƒx¨And when they had turned a little aside, they went into the house of the young man the Levite, who was in the house of Michas: and they saluted him with words of peace.€€ƒxIAnd the six hundred men stood before the door, appointed with their arms.€€ƒxíBut they that were gone into the house of the young man, went about to take away the graven god, and the ephod, and the theraphim, and the molten god, and the priest stood before the door, the six hundred valiant men waiting not far off.€€ƒx”So they that were gone in took away the graven thing, the ephod, and the idols, and the molten god, And the priest said to them: What are you doing?€€ƒxòAnd they said to him: Hold thy peace, and put thy finger on thy mouth, and come with us, that we may have thee for a father, and a priest. Whether is better for thee, to be a priest in the house of one man, or in a tribe and family in Israel?€€ƒx|When he heard this, he agreed to their words, and took the ephod, and the idols, and the graven god, and departed with them.€€ƒxqAnd when they were going forward, and had put before them the children and the cattle, and all that was valuable,€€ƒxAnd were now at a distance from the house of Michas, the men that dwelt in the houses of Michas gathering together followed them,€€ƒxmAnd began to shout out after them. They looked back, and said to Michas: What aileth thee? Why dost thou cry?€€ƒx‰And he answered: You have taken away my gods which I have made me, and the priest, and all that I have, and do you say: What aileth thee?€€ƒx…And the children of Dan said to him: See thou say no more to us, lest men enraged come upon thee, and thou perish with all thy house.€€ƒxyAnd so they went on the journey they had begun. But Michas seeing that they were stronger than he, returned to his house.€€ƒxÒAnd the six hundred men took the priest, and the things we spoke of before, and came to Lais, to a people that was quiet and secure, and smote them with the edge of the sword: and the city they burnt with fire,€€ƒxÖThere being no man at all who brought them any succour, because they dwelt far from Sidon, and had no society or business with any man. And the city was in the land of Rohob: and they rebuilt it, and dwelt therein,€€ƒxzCalling the name of the city Dan, after the name of their father, who was the son of Israel, which before was called Lais.€€ƒx´And they set up to themselves the graven idol, and Jonathan the son of Gersam, the son of Moses, he and his sons were priests in the tribe of Dan, until the day of their captivity.€€ƒx„And the idol of Michas remained with them all the time that the house of God was in Silo. In those days there was no king in Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxfThere was a certain Levite, who dwelt on the side of mount Ephraim, who took a wife of Bethlehem Juda:€€ƒxbAnd she left him, and returned to her father's house in Bethlehem, and abode with him four months.€€ƒy6And her husband followed her, willing to be reconciled with her, and to speak kindly to her, and to bring her back with him, having with him a servant and two asses: and she received him, and brought him into her father's house. And when his father in law had heard this, and had seen him, he met him with joy,€€ƒx‡And embraced the man. And the son in law tarried in the house of his father in law three days, eating with him and drinking familiarly.€€˜ƒx¬And they sat down together, and ate and drank. And the father of the young woman said to his son in law: I beseech thee to stay here to day, and let us make merry together.€€ƒx‚But he rising up, began to be for departing. And nevertheless his father in law earnestly pressed him, and made him stay with him.€€ƒyBut when morning was come, the Levite prepared to go on his journey. And his father in law said to him again: I beseech thee to take a little meat, and strengthening thyself, till the day be farther advanced, afterwards thou mayest depart. And they ate together.€€ƒy'And the young man arose to set forward with his wife and servant. And his father in law spoke to him again: Consider that the day is declining, and draweth toward evening: tarry with me to day also, and spend the day in mirth, and to morrow thou shalt depart, that thou mayest go into thy house.€€ƒxÈHis son in law would not consent to his words: but forthwith went forward, and came over against Jebus, which by another name is called Jerusalem, leading with him two asses loaden, and his concubine.€€ƒx´And now they were come near Jebus, and the day was far spent: and the servant said to his master: Come, I beseech thee, let us turn into the city of the Jebusites, and lodge there.€€ƒxHis master answered him: I will not go into the town of another nation, who are not of the children of Israel, but I will pass over to Gabaa:€€ƒxTAnd when I shall come thither, we will lodge there, or at least in the city of Rama.€€ƒxSo they passed by Jebus, and went on their journey, and the sun went down upon them when they were by Gabaa, which is in the tribe of Benjamin:€€ƒxAnd they turned into it to lodge there. And when they were come in, they sat in the street of the city, for no man would receive them to lodge.€€ƒxÙAnd behold they saw an old man, returning out of the field and from his work in the evening, and he also was of mount Ephraim, and dwelt as a stranger in Gabaa; but the men of that country were the children of Jemini.€€ƒx¡And the old man lifting up his eyes, saw the man sitting with his bundles in the street of the city, and said to him: Whence comest thou? and whither goest thou?€€ƒxäHe answered him: We came out from Bethlehem Juda, and we are going to our home, which is on the side of mount Ephraim, from whence we went to Bethlehem: and now we go to the house of God, and none will receive us under his roof:€€ƒx°We have straw and hay for provender of the asses, and bread and wine for the use of myself and of thy handmaid, and of the servant that is with me: we want nothing but lodging.€€ƒxŒAnd the old man answered him: Peace be with thee: I will furnish all things that are necessary: only I beseech thee, stay not in the street.€€ƒx‹And he brought him into his house, and gave provender to his asses: and after they had washed their feet, he entertained them with a feast.€€ƒyfWhile they were making merry, and refreshing their bodies with meat and drink, after the labour of the journey, the men of that city, sons of Belial (that is, without yoke), came and beset the old man's house, and began to knock at the door, calling to the master of the house, and saying: Bring forth the man that came into thy house, that we may abuse him:€€ƒx§And the old man went out to them, and said: Do not so, my brethren, do not so wickedly: because this man is come into my lodging, and cease I pray you from this folly.€€ƒxÌI have a maiden daughter, and this man hath a concubine, I will bring them out to you, and you may humble them, and satisfy your lust: only, I beseech you, commit not this crime against nature on the man.€€ƒx×They would not be satisfied with his words; which the man seeing, brought out his concubine to them, and abandoned her to their wickedness: and when they had abused her all the night, they let her go in the morning.€€ƒxtBut the woman, at the dawning of the day, came to the door of the house, where her lord lodged, and there fell down.€€ƒx·And in the morning the man arose, and opened the door, that he might end the journey he had begun: and behold his concubine lay before the door with her hands spread on the threshold.€€ƒxÇHe thinking she was taking her rest, said to her: Arise, and let us be going. But as she made no answer, perceiving she was dead, he took her up, and laid her upon his ass, and returned to his house.€€ƒx§And when he was come home, he took a sword, and divided the dead body of his wife with her bones into twelve parts, and sent the pieces into all the borders of Israel.€€ƒxáAnd when every one had seen this, they all cried out: There was never such a thing done in Israel, from the day that our fathers came up out of Egypt, until this day: give sentence, and decree in common what ought to be done.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‘Then all the children of Israel went out, and gathered together as one man, from Dan to Bersabee, with the land of Galaad, to the Lord in Maspha:€€ƒxAnd all the chiefs of the people, and all the tribes of Israel, met together in the assembly of the people of God, four hundred thousand footmen fit for war.€€ƒxÔ(Nor were the children of Benjamin ignorant that the children of Israel were come up to Maspha.) And the Levite, the husband of the woman that was killed being asked, how so great a wickedness had been committed,€€ƒxKAnswered: I came into Gabaa, of Benjamin, with my wife, and there I lodged:€€˜0ƒx¾And I took her and cut her in pieces, and sent the parts into all the borders of your possession: because there never was so heinous a crime, and so great an abomination committed in Israel.€€ƒxGYou are all here, O children of Israel, determine what you ought to do.€€ƒx•And all the people standing, answered as by the voice of one man: We will not return to our tents, neither shall any one of us go into his own house:€€ƒx,But this we will do in common against Gabaa:€€ƒyWe will take ten men of a hundred out of all the tribes of Israel, and a hundred out of a thousand, and a thousand out of ten thousand, to bring victuals for the army, that we may fight against Gabaa of Benjamin, and render to it for its wickedness, what it deserveth.€€ƒxcAnd all Israel were gathered together against the city, as one man, with one mind, and one counsel:€€ƒx}And they sent messengers to all the tribe of Benjamin, to say to them: Why hath so great an abomination been found among you?€€ƒxİDeliver up the men of Gabaa, that have committed this heinous crime, that they may die, and the evil may be taken away out of Israel. But they would not hearken to the proposition of their brethren the children of Israel:€€ƒxBut out of all the cities which were of their lot, they gathered themselves together into Gabaa, to aid them, and to fight against the whole people of Israel.€€ƒxtAnd there were found of Benjamin five and twenty thousand men that drew the sword, besides the inhabitants of Gabaa,€€ƒxÎWho were seven hundred most valiant men, fighting with the left hand as well as with the right: and slinging stones so sure that they could hit even a hair, and not miss by the stone's going on either side.€€ƒx‰Of the men of Israel also, beside the children of Benjamin, were found four hundred thousand that drew swords and were prepared to fight.€€ƒxîAnd they arose and came to the house of God, that is, to Silo: and they consulted God, and said: Who shall be in our army the first to go to the battle against the children of Benjamin? And the Lord answered them: Let Juda be your leader.€€ƒxLAnd forthwith the children of Israel rising in the morning, camped by Gabaa:€€ƒxOAnd going out from thence to fight against Benjamin, began to assault the city.€€ƒxuAnd the children of Benjamin coming out of Gabaa slew of the children of Israel that day two and twenty thousand men.€€ƒxƒAgain Israel, trusting in their strength and their number, set their army in array in the same place, where they had fought before:€€ƒxñYet so that they first went up and wept before the Lord until night: and consulted him and said: Shall I go out any more to fight against the children of Benjamin my brethren or not? And he answered them: Go up against them, and join battle.€€ƒx`And when the children of Israel went out the next day to fight against the children of Benjamin,€€ƒx¬The children of Benjamin sallied forth out of the gates of Gabaa: and meeting them, made so great a slaughter of them, as to kill eighteen thousand men that drew the sword.€€ƒxÊWherefore all the children of Israel came to the house of God, and sat and wept before the Lord: and they fasted that day till the evening, and offered to him holocausts, and victims of peace offerings,€€ƒxgAnd inquired of him concerning their state. At that time the ark of the covenant of the Lord was there,€€ƒy&And Phinees, the son of Eleazar, the son of Aaron, was over the house. So they consulted the Lord, and said: Shall we go out any more to fight against the children of Benjamin, our brethren, or shall we cease? And the Lord said to them: Go up, for to morrow I will deliver them into your hands.€€ƒxFAnd the children of Israel set ambushes round about the city of Gabaa:€€ƒxcAnd they drew up their army against Benjamin the third time, as they had done the first and second.€€ƒy=And the children of Benjamin boldly issued out of the city, and seeing their enemies flee, pursued them a long way, so as to wound and kill some of them, as they had done the first and second day, whilst they fled by two highways, whereof one goeth up to Bethel and the other to Gabaa, and they slew about thirty men:€€ƒxÊFor they thought to cut them off as they did before. But they artfully feigning a flight, designed to draw them away from the city, and by their seeming to flee, to bring them to the highways aforesaid.€€ƒxíThen all the children of Israel rising up out of the places where they were, set their army in battle array, in the place which is called Baalthamar. The ambushes also, which were about the city, began by little and little to come forth,€€ƒyAnd to march from the west side of the city. And other ten thousand men chosen out of all Israel, attacked the inhabitants of the city. And the battle grew hot against the children of Benjamin: and they understood not that present death threatened them on every side.€€ƒx±And the Lord defeated them before the children of Israel, and they slew of them in that day five and twenty thousand, and one hundred, all fighting men, and that drew the sword.€€ƒxîBut the children of Benjamin, when they saw themselves to be too weak, began to flee. Which the children of Israel seeing, gave them place to flee, that they might come to the ambushes that were prepared, which they had set near the city.€€ƒx¸And they that were in ambush arose on a sudden out of their coverts, and whilst Benjamin turned their backs to the slayers, went into the city, and smote it with the edge of the sword.€€ƒxÜNow the children of Israel had given a sign to them, whom they had laid in ambushes, that after they had taken the city, they should make a fire: that by the smoke rising on high, they might shew that the city was taken.€€ƒx§And when the children of Israel saw this in the battle, (for the children of Benjamin thought they fled, and pursued them vigorously, killing thirty men of their army)€€ƒx¡And perceived, as it were, a pillar of smoke rise up from the city; and Benjamin looking back, saw that the city was taken, and that the flames ascended on high:€€ƒx–They that before had made as if they fled, turning their faces, stood bravely against them. Which the children of Benjamin seeing, turned their backs,€€ƒxAnd began to go towards the way of the desert, the enemy pursuing them thither also. And they that fired the city came also out to meet them.€€ƒx±And so it was, that they were slain on both sides by the enemies, and there was no rest of their men dying. They fell and were beaten down on the east side of the city of Gabaa.€€ƒxbAnd they that were slain in the same place, were eighteen thousand men, all most valiant soldiers.€€ƒyCAnd when they that remained of Benjamin saw this, they fled into the wilderness, and made towards the rock that is called Remmon. In that flight also, as they were straggling, and going different ways; they slew of them five thousand men. And as they went farther, they still pursued them, and slew also other two thousand.€€ƒx‘And so it came to pass, that all that were slain of Benjamin, in divers places, were five and twenty thousand fighting men, most valiant for war.€€ƒxªAnd there remained of all the number of Benjamin only six hundred men that were able to escape, and flee to the wilderness: and they abode in the rock Remmon four months.€€ƒx¹But the children of Israel returning, put all the remains of the city to the sword, both men and beasts, and all the cities and villages of Benjamin were consumed with devouring flames.€€ƒ`€€ƒxˆNow the children of Israel had also sworn in Maspha, saying: None of us shall give of his daughters to the children of Benjamin to wife.€€ƒx•And they all came to the house of God in Silo, and sitting before him till the evening, lifted up their voices, and began to lament and weep, saying:€€ƒx…O Lord God of Israel, why is so great an evil come to pass in thy people, that this day one tribe should be taken away from among us?€€ƒxvAnd rising early the next day, they built an altar: and offered there holocausts, and victims of peace, and they said:€€˜ƒx…And the children of Israel being moved with repentance for their brother Benjamin, began to say: One tribe is taken away from Israel.€€ƒxbWhence shall they take wives? For we have all in general sworn, not to give our daughters to them.€€ƒx½Therefore they said: Who is there of all the tribes of Israel, that came not up to the Lord to Maspha? And, behold, the inhabitants of Jabes Galaad were found not to have been in that army.€€ƒxK(At that time also when they were in Silo, no one of them was found there,)€€ƒx¬So they sent ten thousand of the most valiant men, and commanded them, saying: Go and put the inhabitants of Jabes Galaad to the sword, with their wives and their children.€€ƒx‚And this is what you shall observe: Every male, and all women that have known men, you shall kill, but the virgins you shall save.€€ƒx¤And there were found of Jabes Galaad four hundred virgins, that had not known the bed of a man, and they brought them to the camp in Silo, into the land of Chanaan.€€ƒx€And they sent messengers to the children of Benjamin, that were in the rock Remmon, and commanded them to receive them in peace.€€ƒx™And the children of Benjamin came at that time, and wives were given them of Jabes Galaad: but they found no others, whom they might give in like manner.€€ƒxZAnd all Israel was very sorry, and repented for the destroying of one tribe out of Israel.€€ƒx|And the ancients said: What shall we do with the rest, that have not received wives? for all the women in Benjamin are dead.€€ƒxjAnd we must use all care, and provide with great diligence, that one tribe be not destroyed out of Israel.€€ƒx«For as to our own daughters we cannot give them, being bound with an oath and a curse, whereby we said: Cursed be he that shall give Benjamin any of his daughters to wife.€€ƒxøSo they took counsel, and said: Behold, there is a yearly solemnity of the Lord in Silo, which is situate on the north of the city of Bethel, and on the east side of the way, that goeth from Bethel to Sichem, and on the south of the town of Lebona.€€ƒxWAnd they commanded the children of Benjamin and said: Go, and lie hid in the vineyards,€€ƒxËAnd when you shall see the daughters of Silo come out, as the custom is, to dance, come ye on a sudden out of the vineyards, and catch you every man his wife among them, and go into the land of Benjamin.€€ƒy3And when their fathers and their brethren shall come, and shall begin to complain against you, and to chide, we will say to them: Have pity on them: for they took them not away as by the right of war or conquest, but when they asked to have them, you gave them not, and the fault was committed on your part.€€ƒxıAnd the children of Benjamin did as they had been commanded: and, according to their number, they carried off for themselves every man his wife of them that were dancing: and they went into their possession, and built up their cities, and dwelt in them.€€ƒx¸The children of Israel also returned by their tribes, and families, to their dwellings. In those days there was no king in Israel: but every one did that which seemed right to himself.€€ƒpThe Book of Ruth€€ƒ`€€ƒx»In the days of the judges, when the judges ruled, there came a famine in the land. And a certain man of Bethlehem Juda, went to sojourn in the land of Moab with his wife and his two sons.€€ƒx½He was named Elimelech, and his wife Noemi: and his two sons, the one Mahalon, and the other Chelion, Ephrathites of Bethlehem Juda. And entering into the country of Moab, they abode there.€€ƒxHAnd Elimelech the husband of Noemi died: and she remained with her sons.€€ƒx|And they took wives of the women of Moab, of which one was called Orpha, and the other Ruth. And they dwelt their ten years,€€dRuth¤dnamedRuthiosis_namedRuthlabbreviationdRuthhchapters„–ƒx—Wherefore she went forth out of the place of her sojournment, with both her daughters in law: and being now in the way to return into the land of Juda,€€ƒx}She said to them: Go ye home to your mothers, the Lord deal mercifully with you, as you have dealt with the dead and with me.€€ƒx˜May he grant you to find rest in the houses of the husbands whom you shall take. And she kissed them. And they lifted up their voice, and began to weep,€€ƒx2And to say: We will go on with thee to thy people.€€ƒxˆBut she answered them: Return, my daughters: why come ye with me? have I any more sons in my womb, that you may hope for husbands of me?€€ƒxœReturn again, my daughters, and go your ways: for I am now spent with age, and not fit for wedlock. Although I might conceive this night, and bear children,€€ƒxóIf you would wait till they were grown up, and come to man's estate, you would be old women before you marry. Do not so, my daughters, I beseech you: for I am grieved the more for your distress, and the hand of the Lord is gone out against me.€€ƒxAnd they lifted up their voice, and began to weep again: Orpha kissed her mother in law, and returned: Ruth stuck close to her mother in law.€€ƒxiAnd Noemi said to her: Behold thy kinswoman is returned to her people, and to her gods, go thou with her.€€ƒxİShe answered: Be not against me, to desire that I should leave thee and depart: for whithersoever thou shalt go, I will go: and where thou shalt dwell, I also will dwell. Thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God.€€ƒx°The land that shall receive thee dying, in the same will I die: and there will I be buried. The Lord do so and so to me, and add more also, if aught but death part me and thee.€€ƒx“Then Noemi seeing that Ruth was steadfastly determined to go with her, would not be against it, nor persuade her any more to return to her friends:€€ƒx¥So they went together, and came to Bethlehem. And when they were come into the city, the report was quickly spread among all: and the women said: This is that Noemi.€€ƒx˜But she said to them: Call me not Noemi (that is, beautiful,) but call me Mara (that is, bitter), for the Almighty hath quite filled me with bitterness.€€ƒx”I went out full and the Lord hath brought me back empty. Why then do you call me Noemi, whom the Lord hath humbled, and the Almighty hath afflicted?€€ƒx£So Noemi came with Ruth, the Moabitess, her daughter in law, from the land of her sojournment: and returned into Bethlehem, in the beginning of the barley harvest.€€ƒ`€€ƒx\Now her husband Elimelech had a kinsman, a powerful man, and very rich, whose name was Booz.€€ƒyAnd Ruth, the Moabitess, said to her mother in law: If thou wilt, I will go into the field, and glean the ears of corn that escape the hands of the reapers, wheresoever I shall find grace with a householder, that will be favourable to me. And she answered her: Go, my daughter.€€ƒx She went, therefore, and gleaned the ears of corn after the reapers. And it happened that the owner of that field was Booz, who was of the kindred of Elimelech.€€ƒx€And behold, he came out of Bethlehem, and said to the reapers: The Lord be with you. And they answered him: The Lord bless thee.€€ƒxRAnd Booz said to the young man that was set over the reapers: Whose maid is this ?€€—ƒx½And she desired leave to glean the ears of corn that remain, following the steps of the reapers: and she hath been in the field from morning till now, and hath not gone home for one moment.€€ƒx‹And Booz said to Ruth: Hear me, daughter, do not go to glean in any other field, and do not depart from this place: but keep with my maids,€€ƒx°And follow where they reap. For I have charged my young men, not to molest thee: and if thou art thirsty, go to the vessels, and drink of the waters whereof the servants drink.€€ƒxÚShe fell on her face, and worshipping upon the ground, said to him: Whence cometh this to me, that I should find grace before thy eyes, and that thou shouldst vouchsafe to take notice of me, a woman of another country?€€ƒxùAnd he answered her: All hath been told me, that thou hast done to thy mother in law after the death of thy husband: and how thou hast left thy parents, and the land wherein thou wast born, and art come to a people which thou knewest not heretofore.€€ƒx§The Lord render unto thee for thy work, and mayst thou receive a full reward of the Lord the God of Israel, to whom thou art come, and under whose wings thou art fled.€€ƒx And she said: I have found grace in thy eyes, my lord, who hast comforted me, and hast spoken to the heart of thy handmaid, who am not like to one of thy maids.€€ƒxæAnd Booz said to her: At mealtime come thou hither, and eat of the bread, and dip thy morsel in the vinegar. So she sat at the side of the reapers, and she heaped to herself frumenty, and ate and was filled, and took the leavings.€€ƒx™And she arose from thence, to glean the ears of corn as before. And Booz commanded his servants, saying: If she would even reap with you, hinder her not:€€ƒx™And let fall some of your handfuls of purpose, and leave them, that she may gather them without shame, and let no man rebuke her when she gathereth them.€€ƒxºShe gleaned therefore in the field till evening: and beating out with a rod, and threshing what she had gleaned, she found about the measure of an ephi of barley, that is, three bushels:€€ƒxµWhich she took up, and returned into the city, and shewed it to her mother in law: moreover, she brought out, and gave her of the remains of her meat, wherewith she had been filled.€€ƒxêAnd her mother in law said to her: Where hast thou gleaned today, and where hast thou wrought? blessed be he that hath had pity on thee. And she told her with whom she had wrought: and she told the man's name, that he was called Booz.€€ƒx¶And Noemi answered her: Blessed be he of the Lord: because the same kindness which he shewed to the living, he hath kept also to the dead. And again she said: The man is our kinsman.€€ƒxoAnd Ruth said: He also charged me, that I should keep close to his reapers, till all the corn should be reaped.€€ƒxAnd her mother in law said to her: It is better for thee, my daughter, to go out to reap with his maids, lest in another man's field some one may resist thee.€€ƒx†So she kept close to the maids of Booz: and continued to glean with them, till all the barley and the wheat were laid up in the barns.€€ƒ`€€ƒx—After she was returned to her mother in law, Noemi said to her: My daughter, I will seek rest for thee, and will provide that it may be well with thee.€€ƒxThis Booz, with whose maids thou wast joined in the field, is our near kinsman, and behold this night he winnoweth barley in the threshingfloor.€€ƒx®Wash thyself therefore and anoint thee, and put on thy best garments, and go down to the barnfloor: but let not the man see thee, till he shall have done eating and drinking.€€ƒxåAnd when he shall go to sleep, mark the place wherein he sleepeth: and thou shalt go in, and lift up the clothes wherewith he is covered towards his feet, and shalt lay thyself down there: and he will tell thee what thou must do.€€ƒx7She answered: Whatsoever thou shalt command, I will do.€€’ƒxœAnd when Booz had eaten, and drunk, and was merry, he went to sleep by the heap of sheaves, and she came softly, and uncovering his feet, laid herself down.€€ƒxlAnd behold, when it was now midnight the man was afraid, and troubled: and he saw a woman lying at his feet,€€ƒxAnd he said to her: Who art thou ? And she answered: I am Ruth, thy handmaid: spread thy coverlet over thy servant, for thou art a near kinsman.€€ƒx§And he said: Blessed art thou of the Lord, my daughter, and thy latter kindness has surpassed the former: because thou hast not followed young men either poor or rich.€€ƒxªFear not therefore, but whatsoever thou shalt say to me I will do to thee. For all the people that dwell within the gates of my city, know that thou art a virtuous woman.€€ƒxONeither do I deny myself to be near of kin, but there is another nearer than I.€€ƒxÈRest thou this night: and when morning is come, if he will take thee by the right of kindred, all is well: but if he will not, I will undoubtedly take thee, so the Lord liveth: sleep till the morning.€€ƒx¨So she slept at his feet till the night was going off. And she arose before men could know one another, and Booz said: Beware lest any man know that thou camest hither.€€ƒxèAnd again he said: Spread thy mantle, wherewith thou art covered, and hold it with both hands. And when she spread it and held it, he measured six measures of barley, and laid it upon her. And she carried it, and went into the city,€€ƒxAnd came to her mother in law; who said to her: What hast thou done, daughter? And she told her all that the man had done to her.€€ƒx‚And she said: Behold he hath given me six measures of barley: for he said: I will not have thee return empty to thy mother in law.€€ƒx”And Noemi said: Wait, my daughter, till we see what end the thing will have. For the man will not rest until he have accomplished what he hath said.€€ƒ`€€ƒxğThen Booz went up to the gate, and sat there. And when he had seen the kinsman going by, of whom he had spoken before, he said to him, calling him by his name: Turn aside for a little while, and sit down here. He turned aside, and sat down.€€ƒxUAnd Booz, taking ten men of the ancients of the city, said to them: Sit ye down here.€€ƒxThey sat down, and he spoke to the kinsman: Noemi, who is returned from the country of Moab will sell a parcel of land that belonged to our brother Elimelech.€€ƒyI would have thee to understand this, and would tell thee before all that sit here, and before the ancients of my people. If thou wilt take possession of it by the right of kindred: buy it, and possess it: but if it please thee not, tell me so, that I may know what I have to do. For there is no near kinsman besides thee, who art first, and me, who am second. But he answered: I will buy the field.€€ƒxËAnd Booz said to him: When thou shalt buy the field at the woman's hand, thou must take also Ruth, the Moabitess, who was the wife of the deceased: to raise up the name of thy kinsman in his inheritance.€€–ƒyNow this in former times was the manner in Israel between kinsmen, that if at any time one yielded his right to another: that the grant might be sure, the man put off his shoe and gave it to his neighbour; this was a testimony of cession of right in Israel.€€ƒx\So Booz said to his kinsman: Put off thy shoe. And immediately he took it off from his foot.€€ƒx°And he said to the ancients, and to all the people: You are witnesses this day, that I have bought all that was Elimelech's, and Chelion's, and Mahalon's, of the hand of Noemi:€€ƒxòAnd have taken to wife Ruth, the Moabitess, the wife of Mahalon, to raise up the name of the deceased in his inheritance lest his name be cut off, from among his family and his brethren and his people. You, I say, are witnesses of this thing.€€ƒyThen all the people that were in the gate, and the ancients, answered: We are witnesses: The Lord make this woman who cometh into thy house, like Rachel, and Lia, who built up the house of Israel: that she may be an example of virtue in Ephrata, and may have a famous name in Bethlehem:€€ƒxAnd that the house may be, as the house of Phares, whom Thamar bore unto Juda, of the seed which the Lord shall give thee of this young woman.€€ƒxvBooz therefore took Ruth, and married her: and went in unto her, and the Lord gave her to conceive, and to bear a son.€€ƒx”And the women said to Noemi: Blessed be the Lord, who hath not suffered thy family to want a successor: that his name should be preserved in Israel.€€ƒx»And thou shouldst have one to comfort thy soul, and cherish thy old age. For he is born of thy daughter in law: who loveth thee: and is much better to thee, than if thou hadst seven sons.€€ƒx^And Noemi taking the child, laid it in her bosom, and she carried it, and was a nurse unto it.€€ƒx¦And the women, her neighbours, congratulating with her, and saying, There is a son born to Noemi, called his name Obed: he is the father of Isai, the father of David.€€ƒx8These are the generations of Phares: Phares begot Esron,€€ƒx&Esron begot Aram, Aram begot Aminadab,€€ƒx/Aminadab begot Nahasson, Nahasson begot Salmon,€€ƒx#Salmon begot Booz, Booz begot Obed,€€ƒx"Obed begot Isai, Isai begot David.€€ƒxThe First Book of Samuel€€ƒ`€€ƒx¥There was a man of Ramathaimsophim, of Mount Ephraim, and his name was Elcana, the son of Jeroham, the son of Eliu, the son of Thohu, the son of Suph, an Ephraimite:€€ƒx„And he had two wives, the name of one was Anna, and the name of the other Phenenna. Phenenna had children: but Anna had no children.€€ƒxÈAnd this man went up out of his city upon the appointed days, to adore and to offer sacrifice to the Lord of hosts in Silo. And the two sons of Heli, Ophni and Phinees, were there priests of the Lord.€€ƒx|Now the day came, and Elcana offered sacrifice, and gave to Phenenna, his wife, and to all her sons and daughters, portions:€€d1Sam¤dnamehI Samueliosis_named1Samlabbreviationd1Samhchapters˜˜ƒxœAnd thus she did every year, when the time returned, that they went up to the temple of the Lord: and thus she provoked her: but Anna wept, and did not eat.€€ƒx©Then Elcana, her husband, said to her: Anna, why weepest thou? and why dost thou not eat? and why dost thou afflict thy heart? Am not I better to thee than ten children?€€ƒxŠSo Anna arose after she had eaten and drunk in Silo: And Heli, the priest, sitting upon a stool before the door of the temple of the Lord;€€ƒxQAs Anna had her heart full of grief, she prayed to the Lord, shedding many tears,€€ƒyAnd she made a vow, saying: O Lord of hosts, if thou wilt look down, and wilt be mindful of me, and not forget thy handmaid, and wilt give to thy servant a manchild: I will give him to the Lord all the days of his life, and no razor shall come upon his head.€€ƒx]And it came to pass, as she multiplied prayers before the Lord, that Heli observed her mouth.€€ƒxNow Anna spoke in her heart, and only her lips moved, but her voice was not heard at all. Heli therefore thought her to be drunk,€€ƒxjAnd said to her: How long wilt thou be drunk? digest a little the wine, of which thou hast taken too much.€€ƒx¬Anna answering, said: Not so, my lord: for I am an exceeding unhappy woman, and have drunk neither wine nor any strong drink, but I have poured out my soul before the Lord.€€ƒx‚Count not thy handmaid for one of the daughters of Belial: for out of the abundance of my sorrow and grief have I spoken till now.€€ƒxpThen Heli said to her: Go in peace: and the God of Israel grant thee thy petition, which thou hast asked of him.€€ƒx“And she said: Would to God thy handmaid may find grace in thy eyes. So the woman went on her way, and ate, and her countenance was no more changed.€€ƒx²And they rose in the morning, and worshipped before the Lord: and they returned, and came into their house at Ramatha. And Elcana knew Anna his wife: And the Lord remembered her.€€ƒx“And it came to pass when the time was come about, Anna conceived and bore a son, and called his name Samuel: because she had asked him of the Lord.€€ƒxlAnd Elcana, her husband, went up, and all his house, to offer to the Lord the solemn sacrifice, and his vow.€€ƒx´But Anna went not up: for she said to her husband: I will not go till the child be weaned, and till I may carry him, that he may appear before the Lord, and may abide always there.€€ƒxÖAnd Elcana, her husband, said to her: Do what seemeth good to thee, and stay till thou wean him: and I pray that the Lord may fulfil his word. So the woman staid at home, and gave her son suck, till she weaned him.€€ƒx×And after she had weaned him, she carried him with her, with three calves, and three bushels of flour, and a bottle of wine, and she brought him to the house of the Lord in Silo. Now the child was as yet very young:€€ƒx9And they immolated a calf, and offered the child to Heli.€€ƒx…And Anna said: I beseech thee, my lord, as thy soul liveth, my lord: I am that woman, who stood before thee here praying to the Lord.€€ƒxZFor this child did I pray, and the Lord hath granted me my petition, which I asked of him.€€ƒxTherefore I also have lent him to the Lord all the days of his life, he shall be lent to the Lord. And they adored the Lord there. And Anna prayed, and said:€€ƒ`€€ƒx’My heart hath rejoiced in the Lord, and my horn is exalted in my God: my mouth is enlarged over my enemies: because I have joyed in thy salvation.€€ƒxlThere is none holy as the Lord is: for there is no other beside thee, and there is none strong like our God.€€ƒx¢Do not multiply to speak lofty things, boasting: let old matters depart from your mouth: for the Lord is a God of all knowledge, and to him are thoughts prepared.€€ƒxGThe bow of the mighty is overcome, and the weak are girt with strength.€€ƒx«They that were full before, have hired out themselves for bread: and the hungry are filled, so that the barren hath borne many: and she that had many children is weakened.€€˜$ƒxBThe Lord maketh poor and maketh rich, he humbleth and he exalteth:€€ƒxİHe raiseth up the needy from the dust, and lifteth up the poor from the dunghill: that he may sit with princes, and hold the throne of glory. For the poles of the earth are the Lord's, and upon them he hath set the world.€€ƒx‚He will keep the feet of his saints, and the wicked shall be silent in darkness; because no man shall prevail by his own strength.€€ƒxÔThe adversaries of the Lord shall fear him: and upon them shall he thunder in the heavens: The Lord shall judge the ends of the earth, and he shall give empire to his king, and shall exalt the horn of his Christ.€€ƒxAnd Elcana went to Ramatha, to his house: but the child ministered in the sight of the Lord before the face of Heli the priest.€€ƒxCNow the sons of Heli were children of Belial, not knowing the Lord,€€ƒxÀNor the office of the priests to the people: but whosoever had offered a sacrifice, the servant of the priest came, while the flesh was in boiling, with a fleshhook of three teeth in his hand,€€ƒxÈAnd thrust it into the kettle, or into the cauldron, or into the pot, or into the pan: and all that the fleshhook brought up, the priest took to himself. Thus did they to all Israel that came to Silo.€€ƒx½Also before they burnt the fat, the servant of the priest came, and said to the man that sacrificed: Give me flesh to boil for the priest: for I will not take of thee sodden flesh, but raw.€€ƒxıAnd he that sacrificed said to him: Let the fat first be burnt to day, according to the custom, and then take to thee as much as thy soul desireth. But he answered, and said to him: Not so: but thou shalt give it me now, or else I will take it by force.€€ƒxWherefore the sin of the young men was exceeding great before the Lord: because they withdrew men from the sacrifice of the Lord.€€ƒx[But Samuel ministered before the face of the Lord: being a child girded with a linen ephod.€€ƒx˜And his mother made him a little coat, which she brought to him on the appointed days, when she went up with her husband, to offer the solemn sacrifice.€€ƒxªAnd Heli blessed Elcana and his wife: and he said to him: The Lord give thee seed of this woman, for the loan thou hast lent to the Lord. And they went to their own home.€€ƒxˆAnd the Lord visited Anna, and she conceived, and bore three sons, and two daughters: and the child Samuel became great before the Lord.€€ƒx“Now Heli was very old, and he heard all that his sons did to all Israel: and how they lay with the women that waited at the door of the tabernacle:€€ƒxlAnd he said to them: Why do ye these kinds of things, which I hear, very wicked things, from all the people?€€ƒxmDo not so, my sons: for it is no good report that I hear, that you make the people of the Lord to transgress.€€ƒxàIf one man shall sin against another, God may be appeased in his behalf: but if a man shall sin against the Lord, who shall pray for him? And they hearkened not to the voice of their father, because the Lord would slay them.€€ƒxNBut the child Samuel advanced, and grew on, and pleased both the Lord and men.€€ƒxªAnd there came a man of God to Heli, and said to him: Thus saith the Lord: Did I not plainly appear to thy father's house, when they were in Egypt in the house of Pharao?€€ƒxáAnd I chose him out of all the tribes of Israel to be my priest, to go up to my altar, and burn incense to me, and to wear the ephod before me: and I gave to thy father's house of all the sacrifices of the children of Israel.€€ƒxÏWhy have you kicked away my victims, and my gifts which I commanded to be offered in the temple: and thou hast rather honoured thy sons than me, to eat the firstfruits of every sacrifice of my people Israel?€€ƒy Wherefore thus saith the Lord the God of Israel: I said indeed that thy house, and the house of thy father, should minister in my sight, for ever. But now saith the Lord: Far be this from me: but whosoever shall glorify me, him will I glorify: but they that despise me, shall be despised.€€ƒx…Behold the days come: and I will cut off thy arm, and the arm of thy father's house, that there shall not be an old man in thy house.€€ƒx…And thou shalt see thy rival in the temple, in all the prosperity of Israel, and there shall not be an old man in thy house for ever.€€ƒxÂHowever, I will not altogether take away a man of thee from my altar: but that thy eyes may faint, and thy soul be spent: and a great part of thy house shall die, when they come to man's estate.€€ƒxAnd this shall be a sign to thee, that shall come upon thy two sons, Ophni and Phinees: in one day they shall both of them die.€€ƒx¯And I will raise me up a faithful priest, who shall do according to my heart, and my soul and I will build him a faithful house, and he shall walk all days before my anointed.€€ƒyAnd it shall come to pass, that whosoever shall remain in thy house shall come that he may be prayed for, and shall offer a piece of silver, and a roll of bread, and shall say: Put me, I beseech thee, to somewhat of the priestly office, that I may eat a morsel of bread.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‹Now the child Samuel ministered to the Lord before Heli, and the word of the Lord was precious in those days, there was no manifest vision.€€ƒxkAnd it came to pass one day when Heli lay in his place, and his eyes were grown dim, that he could not see:€€ƒxbBefore the lamp of God went out, Samuel slept in the temple of the Lord, where the ark of God was.€€ƒx7And the Lord called Samuel. And he answered: Here am I.€€ƒxƒAnd he ran to Heli, and said: Here am I: for thou didst call me. He said: I did not call: go back and sleep. And he went and slept.€€•ƒx\Now Samuel did not yet know the Lord, neither had the word of the Lord been revealed to him.€€ƒxRAnd the Lord called Samuel again the third time. And he arose up and went to Heli,€€ƒy	And said: Here am I: for thou didst call me. Then Heli understood that the Lord called the child, and he said to Samuel: Go, and sleep: and if he shall call thee any more, thou shalt say: Speak, Lord, for thy servant heareth. So Samuel went, and slept in his place.€€ƒx•And the Lord came, and stood, and he called, as he had called the other times, Samuel, Samuel. And Samuel said: Speak, Lord, for thy servant heareth.€€ƒxtAnd the Lord said to Samuel: Behold I do a thing in Israel: and whosoever shall hear it, both his ears shall tingle.€€ƒxIn that day I will raise up against Heli all the things I have spoken concerning his house: I will begin, and I will make an end.€€ƒx˜For I have foretold unto him, that I will judge his house for ever, for iniquity, because he knew that his sons did wickedly, and did not chastise them.€€ƒx†Therefore have I sworn to the house of Heli, that the iniquity of his house shall not be expiated with victims nor offerings for ever.€€ƒx{And Samuel slept till morning, and opened the doors of the house of the Lord. And Samuel feared to tell the vision to Heli.€€ƒxNThen Heli called Samuel, and said: Samuel, my son. And he answered: Here am I.€€ƒx×And he asked him: What is the word that the Lord hath spoken to thee? I beseech thee hide it not from me. May God do so and so to thee, and add so and so, if thou hide from me one word of all that were said to thee.€€ƒxˆSo Samuel told him all the words, and did not hide them from him. And he answered: It is the Lord: let him do what is good in his sight.€€ƒxXAnd Samuel grew, and the Lord was with him, and not one of his words fell to the ground.€€ƒxZAnd all Israel, from Dan to Bersabee, knew that Samuel was a faithful prophet of the Lord.€€ƒx«And the Lord again appeared in Silo, for the Lord revealed himself to Samuel in Silo, according to the word of the Lord. And the word of Samuel came to pass to all Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÖAnd it came to pass in those days, that the Philistines gathered themselves together to fight: and Israel went out to war against the Philistines, and camped by the Stone of help. And the Philistines came to Aphec,€€ƒxÕAnd put their army in array against Israel. And when they had joined battle, Israel turned their backs to the Philistines: and there were slain in that fight, here and there in the fields, about four thousand men.€€ƒyAnd the people returned to the camp: and the ancients of Israel said: Why hath the Lord defeated us to day before the Philistines? Let us fetch unto us the ark of the covenant of the Lord from Silo, and let it come in the midst of us, that it may save us from the hand of our enemies.€€ƒxÙSo the people sent to Silo, and they brought from thence the ark of the covenant of the Lord of hosts, sitting upon the cherubims: and the two sons of Heli, Ophni and Phinees, were with the ark of the covenant of God.€€ƒx…And when the ark of the covenant of the Lord was come into the camp, all Israel shouted with a great shout, and the earth rang again.€€–ƒx[And the Philistines were afraid, saying: God is come into the camp. And sighing, they said:€€ƒxÔWoe to us: for there was no such great joy yesterday, and the day before: Woe to us. Who shall deliver us from the hand of these high Gods? these are the Gods that struck Egypt with all the plagues in the desert.€€ƒxTake courage, and behave like men, ye Philistines: lest you come to be servants to the Hebrews, as they have served you: take courage and fight.€€ƒx»So the Philistines fought, and Israel was overthrown, and every man fled to his own dwelling: and there was an exceeding great slaughter; for there fell of Israel thirty thousand footmen.€€ƒxVAnd the ark of God was taken: and the two sons of Heli, Ophni and Phinees, were slain.€€ƒx†And there ran a man of Benjamin out of the army, and came to Silo the same day, with his clothes rent, and his head strewed with dust.€€ƒxÊAnd when he was come, Heli sat upon a stool over against the way, watching. For his heart was fearful for the ark of God. And when the man was come into the city, he told it: and all the city cried out.€€ƒx„And Heli heard the noise of the cry, and he said: What meaneth the noise of this uproar? But he made haste, and came, and told Heli.€€ƒxUNow Heli was ninety and eight years old, and his eyes were dim, and he could not see.€€ƒxAnd he said to Heli: I am he that came from the battle, and have fled out of the field this day. And he said to him: What is there done, my son?€€ƒxÜAnd he that brought the news answered, and said: Israel is fled before the Philistines, and there has been a great slaughter of the people: moreover thy two sons, Ophni and Phinees, are dead: and the ark of God is taken.€€ƒxÄAnd when he had named the ark of God, he fell from his stool backwards by the door, and broke his neck and died. For he was an old man, and far advanced in years: And he judged Israel forty years.€€ƒy
And his daughter in law, the wife of Phinees, was big with child, and near her time: and hearing the news that the ark of God was taken, and her father in law, and her husband, were dead, she bowed herself and fell in labour: for her pains came upon her on a sudden.€€ƒx£And when she was upon the point of death, they that stood about her said to her: Fear not, for thou hast borne a son. She answered them not, nor gave heed to them.€€ƒxšAnd she called the child Ichabod, saying: The glory is gone from Israel, because the ark of God was taken, and for her father in law, and for her husband:€€ƒxRAnd she said: The glory is departed from Israel, because the ark of God was taken.€€ƒ`€€ƒx[And the Philistines took the ark of God, and carried it from the Stone of help into Azotus.€€ƒxfAnd the Philistines took the ark of God, and brought it into the temple of Dagon, and set it by Dagon.€€ƒx­And when the Azotians arose early the next day, behold Dagon lay upon his face on the ground before the ark of the Lord: and they took Dagon, and set him again in his place.€€ƒxİAnd the next day again, when they rose in the morning, they found Dagon lying upon his face on the earth before the ark of the Lord: and the head of Dagon, and both the palms of his hands, were cut off upon the threshold:€€ƒx¹And only the stump of Dagon remained in its place. For this cause neither the priests of Dagon, nor any that go into the temple, tread on the threshold of Dagon in Azotus unto this day.€€Œƒx¤And the men of Azotus seeing this kind of plague, said: The ark of the God of Israel shall not stay with us: for his hand is heavy upon us, and upon Dagon, our god.€€ƒyAnd sending, they gathered together all the lords of the Philistines to them, and said: What shall we do with the ark of the God of Israel? And the Gethites answered: Let the ark of the God of Israel be carried about. And they carried the ark of the God of Israel about.€€ƒy"And while they were carrying it about, the hand of the Lord came upon every city with an exceeding great slaughter: and he smote the men of every city, both small and great, and they had emerods in their secret parts. And the Gethites consulted together, and made themselves seats of skins.€€ƒxÓTherefore they sent the ark of God into Accaron. And when the ark of God was come into Accaron, the Accaronites cried out, saying: They have brought the ark of the God of Israel to us, to kill us and our people.€€ƒxÉThey sent therefore, and gathered together all the lords of the Philistines: and they said: Send away the ark of the God of Israel, and let it return into its own place, and not kill us and our people.€€ƒxÄFor there was the fear of death in every city, and the hand of God was exceeding heavy. The men also that did not die, were afflicted with the emerods: and the cry of every city went up to heaven.€€ƒ`€€ƒxCNow the ark of God was in the land of the Philistines seven months.€€ƒx¯And the Philistines called for the priests and the diviners, saying: What shall we do with the ark of the Lord? tell us how we are to send it back to its place. And they said:€€ƒxÆIf you send back the ark of the God of Israel, send it not away empty, but render unto him what you owe for sin, and then you shall be healed: and you shall know why his hand departeth not from you.€€ƒxQThey answered: What is it we ought to render unto him for sin? and they answered:€€ƒy¨According to the number of the provinces of the Philistines you shall make five golden emerods, and five golden mice: for the same plague hath been upon you all, and upon your lords. And you shall make the likeness of your emerods, and the likeness of the mice, that have destroyed the land, and you shall give glory to the God of Israel: to see if he will take off his hand from you, and from your gods, and from your land.€€•ƒxNow, therefore, take and make a new cart: and two kine that have calved, on which there hath come no yoke, tie to the cart, and shut up their calves at home.€€ƒxĞAnd you shall take the ark of the Lord, and lay it on the cart, and the vessels of gold, which you have paid him for sin, you shall put into a little box at the side thereof: and send it away, that it may go.€€ƒxİAnd you shall look: and if it go up by the way of his own coasts, towards Bethsames, then he hath done us this great evil: but if not, we shall know that it is not his hand hath touched us, but it hath happened by chance.€€ƒxThey did therefore in this manner: and taking two kine, that had sucking calves, they yoked them to the cart, and shut up their calves at home.€€ƒxAnd they laid the ark of God upon the cart, and the little box that had in it the golden mice, and the likeness of the emerods.€€ƒyAnd the kine took the straight way, that leadeth to Bethsames, and they went along the way, lowing as they went: and turned not aside neither to the right hand nor to the left: and the lords of the Philistines followed them as far as the borders of Bethsames.€€ƒxzNow the Bethsamites were reaping wheat in the valley: and lifting up their eyes, they saw the ark, and rejoiced to see it.€€ƒxÆAnd the cart came into the field of Josue, a Bethsamite, and stood there. And there was a great stone, and they cut in pieces the wood of the cart, and laid the kine upon it a holocaust to the Lord.€€ƒxıAnd the Levites took down the ark of God, and the little box that was at the side of it, wherein were the vessels of gold, and they put them upon the great stone. The men also of Bethsames offered holocausts, and sacrificed victims that day to the Lord.€€ƒxWAnd the five princes of the Philistines saw, and they returned to Accaron the same day.€€ƒx£And these are the golden emerods, which the Philistines returned for sin to the Lord: For Azotus one, for Gaza one, for Ascalon one, for Geth one, for Accaron one:€€ƒy+And the golden mice, according to the number of the cities of the Philistines, of the five provinces, from the fenced city to the village that was without wall, and to the great Abel (the stone) whereon they set down the ark of the Lord, which was till that day in the field of Josue the Bethsamite.€€ƒxøBut he slew of the men of Bethsames, because they had seen the ark of the Lord, and he slew of the people seventy men, and fifty thousand of the common people. And the people lamented, because the Lord had smitten the people with a great slaughter.€€ƒx|And the men of Bethsames said: Who shall be able to stand before the Lord this holy God? and to whom shall he go up from us?€€ƒx And they sent messengers to the inhabitants of Cariathiarim, saying: The Philistines have brought back the ark of the Lord, come ye down and fetch it up to you.€€ƒ`€€ƒx½And the men of Cariathiarim came, and fetched up the ark of the Lord, and carried it into the house of Abinadab, in Gabaa: and they sanctified Eleazar, his son, to keep the ark of the Lord.€€ƒxÂAnd it came to pass, that from the day the ark of the Lord abode in Cariathiarim, days were multiplied (for it was now the twentieth year) and all the house of Israel rested, following the Lord.€€ƒyAnd Samuel spoke to all the house of Israel, saying: If you turn to the Lord with all your heart, put away the strange gods from among you, Baalim and Astaroth: and prepare your hearts unto the Lord, and serve him only, and he will deliver you out of the hand of the Philistines.€€ƒxSThen the children of Israel put away Baalim and Astaroth, and served the Lord only.€€ƒxTAnd Samuel said: Gather all Israel to Masphath, that I may pray to the Lord for you.€€‘ƒxãAnd the Philistines heard that the children of Israel were gathered together to Masphath, and the lords of the Philistines went up against Israel. And when the children of Israel heard this, they were afraid of the Philistines.€€ƒx}And they said to Samuel: Cease not to cry to the Lord our God for us, that he may save us out of the hand of the Philistines.€€ƒx’And Samuel took a sucking lamb, and offered it whole for a holocaust to the Lord: and Samuel cried to the Lord for Israel, and the Lord heard him.€€ƒyAnd it came to pass, when Samuel was offering the holocaust, the Philistines began the battle against Israel: but the Lord thundered with a great thunder on that day upon the Philistines, and terrified them, and they were overthrown before the face of Israel.€€ƒx…And the men of Israel going out of Masphath, pursued after the Philistines, and made slaughter of them till they came under Bethchar.€€ƒx˜And Samuel took a stone, and laid it between Masphath and Sen: and he called the place The stone of help. And he said: Thus far the Lord hath helped us.€€ƒxªAnd the Philistines were humbled, and they did not come any more into the borders of Israel. And the hand of the Lord was against the Philistines, all the days of Samuel.€€ƒxîAnd the cities which the Philistines had taken from Israel, were restored to Israel, from Accaron to Geth, and their borders: and he delivered Israel from the hand of the Philistines, and there was peace between Israel and the Amorrhites.€€ƒx2And Samuel judged Israel all the days of his life:€€ƒxrAnd he went every year about to Bethel and to Galgal and to Masphath, and he judged Israel in the foresaid places.€€ƒxzAnd he returned to Ramatha: for there was his house, and there he judged Israel: he built also there an altar to the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx^And it came to pass, when Samuel was old, that he appointed his sons to be judges over Israel.€€ƒxdNow the name of his firstborn son was Joel: and the name of the second was Abia, judges in Bersabee.€€ƒxpAnd his sons walked not in his ways: but they turned aside after lucre, and took bribes, and perverted judgment.€€ƒxJThen all the ancients of Israel being assembled came to Samuel to Ramatha.€€ƒxAnd they said to him: Behold thou art old, and thy sons walk not in thy ways: make us a king, to judge us, as all nations have.€€–ƒx©And the Lord said to Samuel: Hearken to the voice of the people in all that they say to thee. For they have not rejected thee, but me, that I should not reign over them.€€ƒx¸According to all their works, they have done from the day that I brought them out of Egypt until this day: as they have forsaken me, and served strange gods, so do they also unto thee.€€ƒx…Now, therefore, hearken to their voice: but yet testify to them, and foretell them the right of the king, that shall reign over them.€€ƒxXThen Samuel told all the words of the Lord to the people that had desired a king of him,€€ƒxÓAnd said: This will be the right of the king that shall reign over you: He will take your sons, and put them in his chariots, and will make them his horsemen, and his running footmen, to run before his chariots,€€ƒx–And he will appoint of them to be his tribunes, and his centurions, and to plough his fields, and to reap his corn, and to make him arms and chariots.€€ƒxXYour daughters also he will take to make him ointments, and to be his cooks, and bakers.€€ƒxjAnd he will take your fields, and your vineyards, and your best oliveyards, and give them to his servants.€€ƒxyMoreover he will take the tenth of your corn, and of the revenues of your vineyards, to give to his eunuchs and servants.€€ƒx}Your servants also, and handmaids, and your goodliest young men, and your asses, he will take away, and put them to his work.€€ƒx>Your flocks also he will tithe, and you shall be his servants.€€ƒx¸And you shall cry out in that day from the face of the king, whom you have chosen to yourselves: and the Lord will not hear you in that day, because you desired unto yourselves a king.€€ƒxiBut the people would not hear the voice of Samuel, and they said, Nay: but there shall be a king over us,€€ƒxvAnd we also will be like all nations: and our king shall judge us, and go out before us, and fight our battles for us.€€ƒxYAnd Samuel heard all the words of the people, and rehearsed them in the ears of the Lord.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to Samuel: Hearken to their voice, and make them a king. And Samuel said to the men of Israel: Let every man go to his city.€€ƒ`€€ƒx°Now there was a man of Benjamin, whose name was Cis, the son of Abiel, the son of Seror, the son of Bechorath, the son of Aphia, the son of a man of Jemini, valiant and strong.€€ƒxÈAnd he had a son whose name was Saul, a choice and goodly man, and there was not among the children of Israel a goodlier person than he: from his shoulders and upward he appeared above all the people.€€ƒxÄAnd the asses of Cis, Saul's father, were lost: and Cis said to his son Saul: Take one of the servants with thee, and arise, go, and seek the asses. And when they had passed through Mount Ephraim,€€ƒx°And through the land of Salisa, and had not found them, they passed also through the land of Salim, and they were not there: and through the land of Jemini, and found them not.€€ƒx¯And when they were come to the land of Suph, Saul said to the servant that was with him: Come, let us return, lest perhaps my father forget the asses, and be concerned for us.€€˜ƒxÃAnd Saul said to his servant: Behold we will go: but what shall we carry to the man of God? the bread is spent in our bags: and we have no present to make to the man of God, nor any thing at all.€€ƒx°The servant answered Saul again, and said: Behold there is found in my hand the fourth part of a sicle of silver, let us give it to the man of God, that he may tell us our way.€€ƒx¬Now in time past in Israel, when a man went to consult God, he spoke thus: Come, let us go to the seer. For he that is now called a prophet, in time past was called a seer.€€ƒx{And Saul said to his servant: Thy word is very good, come let us go. And they went into the city, where the man of God was.€€ƒxAnd when they went up the ascent to the city, they found maids coming out to draw water, and they said to them: Is the seer here?€€ƒx²They answered and said to them: He is: behold he is before you, make haste now: for he came to day into the city, for there is a sacrifice of the people to day in the high place.€€ƒyAs soon as you come into the city, you shall immediately find him, before he go up to the high place to eat: for the people will not eat till he come; because he blesseth the victim, and afterwards they eat that are invited. Now, therefore, go up, for to day you shall find him.€€ƒx And they went up into the city. And when they were walking in the midst of the city, behold Samuel was coming out over against them, to go up to the high place.€€ƒxPNow the Lord had revealed to the ear of Samuel the day before Saul came, saying:€€ƒyTo morrow about this same hour I will send thee a man of the land of Benjamin, and thou shalt anoint him to be ruler over my people Israel: and he shall save my people out of the hand of the Philistines: for I have looked down upon my people, because their cry is come to me.€€ƒx}And when Samuel saw Saul, the Lord said to him: Behold the man, of whom I spoke to thee, this man shall reign over my people.€€ƒxqAnd Saul came to Samuel in the midst of the gate, and said: Tell me, I pray thee, where is the house of the seer?€€ƒxÃAnd Samuel answered Saul, saying: I am the seer; go up before me to the high place, that you may eat with me to day, and I will let thee go in the morning: and tell thee all that is in thy heart.€€ƒxĞAnd as for the asses, which were lost three days ago, be not solicitous, because they are found. And for whom shall be all the best things of Israel? Shall they not be for thee and for all thy father's house?€€ƒxÄAnd Saul answering, said: Am not I a son of Jemini of the least tribe of Israel, and my kindred the last among all the families of the tribe of Benjamin? Why then hast thou spoken this word to me?€€ƒx¦Then Samuel taking Saul, and his servant, brought them into the parlour, and gave them a place at the head of them that were invited. For there were about thirty men.€€ƒxmAnd Samuel said to the cook: Bring the portion which I gave thee, and commanded thee to set it apart by thee.€€ƒxäAnd the cook took up the shoulder, and set it before Saul. And Samuel said: Behold what is left, set it before thee, and eat; because it was kept of purpose for thee, when I invited the people. And Saul ate with Samuel that day.€€ƒx¬And they went down from the high place into the town, and he spoke with Saul upon the top of the house: and he prepared a bed for Saul on the top of the house and he slept.€€ƒxàAnd when they were risen in the morning, and it began now to be light, Samuel called Saul on the top of the house, saying: Arise, that I may let thee go. And Saul arose: and they went out both of them: to wit, he and Samuel.€€ƒxÄAnd as they were going down in the end of the city, Samuel said to Saul: Speak to the servant to go before us, and pass on: but stand thou still a while, that I may tell thee the word of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒyQAnd Samuel took a little vial of oil, and poured it upon his head, and kissed him, and said: Behold, the Lord hath anointed thee to be prince over his inheritance, and thou shalt deliver his people out of the hands of their enemies, that are round about them. And this shall be a sign unto thee, that God hath anointed thee to be prince.€€ƒy<When thou shalt depart from me this day, thou shalt find two men by the sepulchre of Rachel in the borders of Benjamin to the south, and they shall say to thee: The asses are found which thou wentest to seek: and thy father, thinking no more of the asses, is concerned for you, and saith: What shall I do for my son?€€ƒxıAnd when thou shalt depart from thence, and go farther on, and shalt come to the oak of Thabor, there shall meet thee three men going up to God to Bethel, one carrying three kids, and another three loaves of bread, and another carrying a bottle of wine.€€ƒxaAnd they will salute thee, and will give thee two loaves, and thou shalt take them at their hand.€€ƒy3After that thou shalt come to the hill of God, where the garrison of the Philistines is: and when thou shalt be come there into the city, thou shalt meet a company of prophets coming down from the high place, with a psaltery, and a timbrel, and a pipe, and a harp before them, and they shall be prophesying.€€˜ƒxnWhen therefore these signs shall happen to thee, do whatsoever thy hand shall find, for the Lord is with thee.€€ƒxéAnd thou shalt go down before me to Galgal, (for I will come down to thee), that thou mayst offer an oblation, and sacrifice victims of peace: seven days shalt thou wait, till I come to thee, and I will shew thee what thou art to do.€€ƒx~So when he had turned his back to go from Samuel, God gave unto him another heart, and all these things came to pass that day.€€ƒxŸAnd they came to the foresaid hill, and behold a company of prophets met him: and the Spirit of the Lord came upon him, and he prophesied in the midst of them.€€ƒxÖAnd all that had known him yesterday and the day before, seeing that he was with the prophets, and prophesied, said to each other: What is this that hath happened to the son of Cis? is Saul also among the prophets?€€ƒxzAnd one answered another, saying: And who is their father? therefore it became a proverb: Is Saul also among the prophets?€€ƒxFAnd when he had made an end of prophesying, he came to the high place.€€ƒxAnd Saul's uncle said to him, and to his servant: Whither went you? They answered: To seek the asses: and not finding them, we went to Samuel.€€ƒx<And his uncle said to him: Tell me what Samuel said to thee.€€ƒx–And Saul said to his uncle: He told us that the asses were found. But of the matter of the kingdom of which Samuel had spoken to him, he told him not.€€ƒx<And Samuel called together the people to the Lord in Maspha:€€ƒx×And he said to the children of Israel: Thus saith the Lord the God of Israel: I brought up Israel out of Egypt, and delivered you from the hand of the Egyptians, and from the hand of all the kings who afflicted you.€€ƒxçBut you this day have rejected your God, who only hath saved you out of all your evils and your tribulations: and you have said: Nay: but set a king over us. Now therefore stand before the Lord by your tribes, and by your families.€€ƒx^And Samuel brought to him all the tribes of Israel, and the lot fell on the tribe of Benjamin.€€ƒxÀAnd he brought the tribe of Benjamin and the kindreds thereof, and the lot fell upon the kindred of Metri, and it came to Saul, the son of Cis. They sought him therefore, and he was not found.€€ƒxyAnd after this they consulted the Lord whether he would come thither. And the Lord answered: Behold he is hidden at home.€€ƒx•And they ran and fetched him thence: and he stood in the midst of the people, and he was higher than any of the people from the shoulders and upward.€€ƒx¸And Samuel said to all the people: Surely you see him whom the Lord hath chosen, that there is none like him among all the people. And all the people cried and said: God save the king.€€ƒx«And Samuel told the people the law of the kingdom, and wrote it in a book, and laid it up before the Lord: and Samuel sent away all the people, every one to his own house.€€ƒxwSaul also departed to his own house in Gabaa: and there went with him a part of the army, whose hearts God had touched.€€ƒx¤But the children of Belial said: Shall this fellow be able to save us? And they despised him, and brought him no presents; but he dissembled as though he heard not.€€ƒ`€€ƒxĞAnd it came to pass about a month after this, that Naas, the Ammonite, came up, and began to fight against Jabes Galaad. And all the men of Jabes said to Naas: Make a covenant with us, and we will serve thee.€€ƒxªAnd Naas, the Ammonite, answered them: On this condition will I make a covenant with you, that I may pluck out all your right eyes, and make you a reproach in all Israel.€€ƒx³And the ancients of Jabes said to him: Allow us seven days, that we may send messengers to all the coasts of Israel: and if there be no one to defend us, we will come out to thee.€€ƒxThe messengers therefore came to Gabaa of Saul: and they spoke these words in the hearing of the people: and all the people lifted up their voices, and wept.€€ƒx›And behold Saul came, following oxen out of the field, and he said: What aileth the people that they weep? And they told him the words of the men of Jabes.€€ƒyAnd taking both the oxen, he cut them in pieces, and sent them into all the coasts of Israel, by messengers, saying: Whosoever shall not come forth, and follow Saul and Samuel, so shall it be done to his oxen. And the fear of the Lord fell upon the people, and they went out as one man.€€ƒx‡And he numbered them in Bezec: and there were of the children of Israel three hundred thousand: and of the men of Juda thirty thousand.€€ƒxäAnd they said to the messengers that came: Thus shall you say to the men of Jabes Galaad: To morrow, when the sun shall be hot, you shall have relief. The messengers therefore came, and told the men of Jabes, and they were glad.€€ƒx`And they said: In the morning we will come out to you: and you shall do what you please with us.€€ƒyAnd it came to pass, when the morrow was come, that Saul put the people in three companies: and he came into the midst of the camp in the morning watch, and he slew the Ammonites until the day grew hot, and the rest were scattered, so that two of them were not left together.€€ƒxsAnd the people said to Samuel: Who is he that said: Shall Saul reign over us? Bring the men, and we will kill them.€€ƒxkAnd Saul said: No man shall be killed this day: because the Lord this day hath wrought salvation in Israel:€€ƒxaAnd Samuel said to the people: Come, and let us go to Galgal, and let us renew the kingdom there.€€ƒx×And all the people went to Galgal, and there they made Saul king, before the Lord in Galgal, and they sacrificed there victims of peace before the Lord. And there Saul and all the men of Israel rejoiced exceedingly.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAnd Samuel said to all Israel: Behold I have hearkened to your voice in all that you said to me, and have made a king over you.€€ƒx¨And now the king goeth before you: but I am old and greyheaded: and my sons are with you: having then conversed with you from my youth until this day, behold here I am.€€ƒxÿSpeak of me before the Lord, and before his anointed, whether I have taken any man's ox, or ass: if I have wronged any man, if I have oppressed any man, if I have taken a bribe at any man's hand: and I will despise it this day, and will restore it to you.€€ƒx]And they said: Thou hast not wronged us, nor oppressed us, nor taken ought at any man's hand.€€ƒx§And he said to them: The Lord is witness against you, and his anointed is witness this day, that you have not found any thing in my hand. And they said: He is witness.€€˜ƒx­Now, therefore, stand up, that I may plead in judgment against you before the Lord, concerning all the kindness of the Lord, which he hath shewn to you, and to your fathers:€€ƒx«How Jacob went into Egypt, and your fathers cried to the Lord: and the Lord sent Moses and Aaron, and brought your fathers out of Egypt, and made them dwell in this place.€€ƒxİAnd they forgot the Lord their God, and he delivered them into the hands of Sisara, captain of the army of Hasor, and into the hands of the Philistines, and into the hand of the king of Moab, and they fought against them.€€ƒxÑBut afterwards they cried to the Lord, and said: We have sinned, because we have forsaken the Lord, and have served Baalim and Astaroth: but now deliver us from the hand of our enemies, and we will serve thee.€€ƒx“And the Lord sent Jerobaal, and Badan, and Jephte, and Samuel, and delivered you from the hand of your enemies round about, and you dwelt securely.€€ƒx¨But seeing that Naas, king of the children of Ammon, was come against you, you said to me: Nay, but a king shall reign over us: whereas the Lord your God was your king.€€ƒxkNow, therefore, your king is here, whom you have chosen and desired: Behold the Lord hath given you a king.€€ƒxÆIf you will fear the Lord, and serve him, and hearken to his voice, and not provoke the mouth of the Lord: then shall both you, and the king who reigneth over you, be followers of the Lord your God.€€ƒx–But if you will not hearken to the voice of the Lord, but will rebel against his words, the hand of the Lord shall be upon you, and upon your fathers.€€ƒxNNow then stand, and see this great thing which the Lord will do in your sight.€€ƒxÜIs it not wheat harvest to day? I will call upon the Lord, and he shall send thunder and rain: and you shall know, and see that you yourselves have done a great evil in the sight of the Lord, in desiring a king over you.€€ƒxLAnd Samuel cried unto the Lord, and the Lord sent thunder and rain that day.€€ƒxÙAnd all the people greatly feared the Lord and Samuel. And all the people said to Samuel: Pray for thy servants to the Lord thy God, that we may not die, for we have added to all our sins this evil, to ask for a king.€€ƒx™And Samuel said to the people: Fear not, you have done all this evil: but yet depart not from following the Lord, but serve the Lord with all your heart.€€ƒxkAnd turn not aside after vain things, which shall never profit you, nor deliver you, because they are vain.€€ƒxwAnd the Lord will not forsake his people for his great name's sake: because the Lord hath sworn to make you his people.€€ƒxAnd far from me be this sin against the Lord, that I should cease to pray for you: and I will teach you the good and right way.€€ƒx‹Therefore fear the Lord, and serve him in truth, and with your whole heart, for you have seen the great works which he hath done among you.€€ƒxPBut if you will still do wickedly: both you and your king shall perish together.€€ƒ`€€ƒxZSaul was a child of one year when he began to reign, and he reigned two years over Israel.€€ƒxêAnd Saul chose him three thousand men of Israel: and two thousand were with Saul in Machmas, and in mount Bethel: and a thousand with Jonathan in Gabaa of Benjamin: and the rest of the people he sent back every man to their dwellings.€€ƒxºAnd Jonathan smote the garrison of the Philistines which was in Gabaa. And when the Philistines had heard of it, Saul sounded the trumpet over all the land, saying: Let the Hebrews hear.€€ƒx¿And all Israel heard this report: Saul hath smitten the garrison of the Philistines: and Israel took courage against the Philistines. And the people were called together after Saul to Galgal.€€ƒxüThe Philistines also were assembled to fight against Israel, thirty thousand chariots, and six thousand horsemen, and a multitude of people besides, like the sand on the seashore for number. And going up they camped in Machmas, at the east of Bethaven.€€—ƒx¦And some of the Hebrews passed over the Jordan into the land of Gad and Galaad. And when Saul was yet in Galgal, all the people that followed him were greatly afraid.€€ƒx„And he waited seven days, according to the appointment of Samuel, and Samuel came not to Galgal, and the people slipt away from him.€€ƒx^Then Saul said: Bring me the holocaust, and the peace offerings. And he offered the holocaust.€€ƒxzAnd when he had made an end of offering the holocaust, behold Samuel came: and Saul went forth to meet him and salute him.€€ƒx×And Samuel said to him: What hast thou done? Saul answered: Because I saw that the people slipt from me, and thou wast not come according to the days appointed, and the Philistines were gathered together in Machmas,€€ƒx™I said: Now will the Philistines come down upon me to Galgal, and I have not appeased the face of the Lord. Forced by necessity, I offered the holocaust.€€ƒxêAnd Samuel said to Saul: Thou hast done foolishly, and hast not kept the commandments of the Lord thy God, which he commanded thee. And if thou hadst not done thus, the Lord would now have established thy kingdom over Israel for ever:€€ƒxÚBut thy kingdom shall not continue. The Lord hath sought him a man according to his own heart: and him hath the Lord commanded to be prince over his people, because thou hast not observed that which the Lord commanded.€€ƒyAnd Samuel arose and went up from Galgal to Gabaa of Benjamin. And the rest of the people went up after Saul, to meet the people who fought against them, going from Galgal to Gabaa, in the hill of Benjamin. And Saul numbered the people, that were found with him, about six hundred men.€€ƒxAnd Saul, and Jonathan his son, and the people that were present with them, were in Gabaa of Benjamin: But the Philistines encamped in Machmas.€€ƒxŒAnd there went out of the camp of the Philistines three companies to plunder. One company went towards the way of Ephra to the land of Sual;€€ƒx†And another went by the way of Bethoron, and the third turned to the way of the border, above the valley of Seboim towards the desert.€€ƒx Now there was no smith to be found in all the land of Israel, for the Philistines had taken this precaution, lest the Hebrews should make them swords or spears.€€ƒx{So all Israel went down to the Philistines, to sharpen every man his ploughshare, and his spade, and his axe, and his rake.€€ƒx~So that their shares, and their spades, and their forks, and their axes, were blunt, even to the goad, which was to be mended.€€ƒx±And when the day of battle was come, there was neither sword nor spear found in the hand of any of the people that were with Saul and Jonathan, except Saul and Jonathan his son.€€ƒxPAnd the army of the Philistines went out in order to advance further in Machmas.€€ƒ`€€ƒxôNow it came to pass one day that Jonathan, the son of Saul, said to the young man that bore his armour: Come, and let us go over to the garrison of the Philistines, which is on the other side of yonder place. But he told not this to his father.€€ƒx“And Saul abode in the uttermost part of Gabaa, under the pomegranate tree, which was in Magron: and the people with him were about six hundred men.€€ƒxºAnd Achias, the son of Achitob, brother of Ichabod the son of Phinees, the son of Heli, the priest of the Lord in Silo, wore the ephod. And the people knew not whither Jonathan was gone.€€ƒyNow there were between the ascents, by which Jonathan sought to go over to the garrison of the Philistines, rocks standing up on both sides, and steep cliffs like teeth on the one side, and on the other, the name of the one was Boses, and the name of the other was Sene:€€ƒxjOne rock stood out toward the north, over against Machmas, and the other to the south, over against Gabaa.€€˜4ƒxŒAnd his armourbearer said to him: Do all that pleaseth thy mind: go whither thou wilt, and I will be with thee wheresoever thou hast a mind.€€ƒxZAnd Jonathan said: Behold we will go over to these men. And when we shall be seen by them,€€ƒxqIf they shall speak thus to us: Stay till we come to you: let us stand still in our place, and not go up to them.€€ƒx†But if they shall say: Come up to us: let us go up, because the Lord hath delivered them into our hands, this shall be a sign unto us.€€ƒx©So both of them discovered themselves to the garrison of the Philistines: and the Philistines said: Behold the Hebrews come forth out of the holes wherein they were hid.€€ƒxùAnd the men of the garrison spoke to Jonathan, and to his armourbearer, and said: Come up to us, and we will shew you a thing. And Jonathan said to his armourbearer: Let us go up, follow me: for the Lord hath delivered them into the hands of Israel.€€ƒx¤And Jonathan went up creeping on his hands and feet, and his armourbearer after him. And some fell before Jonathan, others his armourbearer slew as he followed him.€€ƒx¨And the first slaughter which Jonathan and his armourbearer made, was of about twenty men, within half an acre of land, which a yoke of oxen is wont to plough in a day.€€ƒxÂAnd there was a miracle in the camp, in the fields: and all the people of their garrison, who had gone out to plunder, were amazed, and the earth trembled: and it happened as a miracle from God.€€ƒxAnd the watchmen of Saul, who were in Gabaa of Benjamin looked, and behold a multitude overthrown, and fleeing this way and that.€€ƒx¬And Saul said to the people that were with him: Look, and see who is gone from us. And when they had sought, it was found that Jonathan and his armourbearer were not there.€€ƒxxAnd Saul said to Achias: Bring the ark of the Lord. (For the ark of God was there that day with the children of Israel.)€€ƒxÆAnd while Saul spoke to the priest, there arose a great uproar in the camp of the Philistines: and it increased by degrees, and was heard more clearly. And Saul said to the priest: Draw in thy hand.€€ƒxÎThen Saul, and all the people that were with him, shouted together, and they came to the place of the fight: and behold every man's sword was turned upon his neighbour, and there was a very great slaughter.€€ƒxÀMoreover, the Hebrews that had been with the Philistines yesterday and the day before, and went up with them into the camp, returned to be with the Israelites, who were with Saul and Jonathan.€€ƒxÊAnd all the Israelites that had hid themselves in mount Ephraim, hearing that the Philistines fled, joined themselves with their countrymen in the fight. And there were with Saul about ten thousand men.€€ƒxMAnd the Lord saved Israel that day. And the fight went on as far as Bethaven.€€ƒxÖAnd the men of Israel were joined together that day: and Saul adjured the people, saying: Cursed be the man that shall eat food till evening, till I be revenged of my enemies. So none of the people tasted any food.€€ƒxWAnd all the common people came into a forest, in which there was honey upon the ground.€€ƒx‰And when the people came into the forest, behold the honey dropped, but no man put his hand to his mouth. For the people feared the oath.€€ƒxŞBut Jonathan had not heard when his father adjured the people: and he put forth the end of the rod, which he had in his hand, and dipt it in a honeycomb: and he carried his hand to his mouth, and his eyes were enlightened.€€ƒx®And one of the people answering, said: Thy father hath bound the people with an oath, saying: Cursed be the man that shall eat any food this day. (And the people were faint.)€€ƒx”And Jonathan said: My father hath troubled the land: you have seen yourselves that my eyes are enlightened, because I tasted a little of this honey:€€ƒx˜How much more if the people had eaten of the prey of their enemies, which they found? had there not been made a greater slaughter among the Philistines?€€ƒxhSo they smote that day the Philistines, from Machmas to Aialon. And the people were wearied exceedingly.€€ƒxˆAnd falling upon the spoils, they took sheep, and oxen, and calves, and slew them on the ground: and the people ate them with the blood.€€ƒxAnd they told Saul that the people had sinned against the Lord, eating with the blood. And he said: You have transgressed: roll here to me now a great stone.€€ƒy.And Saul said: Disperse yourselves among the people, and tell them to bring me every man his ox and his ram and slay them upon this stone, and eat, and you shall not sin against the Lord, in eating with the blood. So all the people brought every man his ox with him till the night: and slew them there.€€ƒx[And Saul built an altar to the Lord: and he then first began to build an altar to the Lord.€€ƒxøAnd Saul said: Let us fall upon the Philistines by night, and destroy them till the morning light, and let us not leave a man of them. And the people said: Do all that seemeth good in thy eyes. And the priest said: Let us draw near hither unto God.€€ƒx•And Saul consulted the Lord: Shall I pursue after the Philistines? wilt thou deliver them into the hands of Israel? And he answered him not that day.€€ƒxsAnd Saul said: Bring hither all the corners of the people: and know, and see by whom this sin hath happened to day.€€ƒx”As the Lord liveth, who is the Saviour of Israel, if it was done by Jonathan, my son, he shall surely die. In this none of the people gainsayed him.€€ƒx¦And he said to all Israel: Be you on one side and I, with Jonathan, my son, will be on the other side. And the people answered Saul: Do what seemeth good in thy eyes.€€ƒyCAnd Saul said to the Lord: O Lord God of Israel, give a sign, by which we may know, what the meaning is, that thou answerest not thy servant to day: If this iniquity be in me, or in my son Jonathan, give a proof: or if this iniquity be in thy people, give holiness. And Jonathan and Saul were taken, and the people escaped.€€ƒxSAnd Saul said: (Cast lots between me, and Jonathan, my son. And Jonathan was taken.€€ƒx½And Saul said to Jonathan: Tell me what thou hast done. And Jonathan told him, and said: I did but taste a little honey with the end of the rod, which was in my hand, and behold I must die.€€ƒxdAnd Saul said: May God do so and so to me, and add still more: for dying thou shalt die, O Jonathan.€€ƒy&And the people said to Saul: Shall Jonathan then die, who hath wrought this great salvation in Israel? this must not be: As the Lord liveth, there shall not one hair of his head fall to the ground, for he hath wrought with God this day. So the people delivered Jonathan, that he should not die.€€ƒxkAnd Saul went back, and did not pursue after the Philistines: and the Philistines went to their own places.€€ƒxùAnd Saul having his kingdom established over Israel, fought against all his enemies round about, against Moab, and against the children of Ammon, and Edom, and the kings of Soba, and the Philistines: and whithersoever he turned himself, he overcame.€€ƒxqAnd gathering together an army, he defeated Amalec, and delivered Israel from the hand of them that spoiled them.€€ƒx¬And the sons of Saul, were Jonathan, and Jessui, and Melchisua: and the names of his two daughters, the name of the firstborn was Merob, and the name of the younger Michol.€€ƒx¡And the name of Saul's wife was Achinoam, the daughter of Achimaas; and the name of the captain of his army was Abner, the son of Ner, the cousin german of Saul.€€ƒxOFor Cis was the father of Saul, and Ner, the father of Abner, was son of Abiel.€€ƒxAnd there was a great war against the Philistines all the days of Saul. For whomsoever Saul saw to be a valiant man, and fit for war, he took him to himself.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŒAnd Samuel said to Saul: The Lord sent me to anoint thee king over his people Israel: now therefore hearken thou unto the voice of the Lord:€€ƒx”Thus saith the Lord of hosts: I have reckoned up all that Amalec hath done to Israel: how he opposed them in the way when they came up out of Egypt.€€ƒxÆNow therefore go, and smite Amalec, and utterly destroy all that he hath: spare him not, nor covet anything that is his: but slay both man and woman, child and suckling, ox and sheep, camel and ass.€€ƒx|So Saul commanded the people, and numbered them as lambs: two hundred thousand footmen, and ten thousand of the men of Juda.€€ƒxNAnd when Saul was come to the city of Amalec, he laid ambushes in the torrent.€€˜#ƒxYAnd Saul smote Amalec from Hevila, until thou comest to Sur, which is over against Egypt.€€ƒxjAnd he took Agag, the king of Amalec, alive: but all the common people he slew with the edge of the sword.€€ƒxüAnd Saul and the people spared Agag, and the best of the flocks of sheep, and of the herds, and the garments and the rams, and all that was beautiful, and would not destroy them: but every thing that was vile, and good for nothing, that they destroyed.€€ƒx(And the word of the Lord came to Samuel,€€ƒx©It repenteth me that I have made Saul king: for he hath forsaken me, and hath not executed my commandments. And Samuel was grieved, and he cried unto the Lord all night.€€ƒy\And when Samuel rose early, to go to Saul in the morning, it was told Samuel that Saul was come to Carmel, and had erected for himself a triumphant arch, and returning had passed on, and gone down to Galgal. And Samuel came to Saul, and Saul was offering a holocaust to the Lord, out of the choicest of the spoils, which he had brought from Amalec.€€ƒxwAnd when Samuel was come to Saul, Saul said to him: Blessed be thou of the Lord, I have fulfilled the word of the Lord.€€ƒx…And Samuel said: What meaneth then this bleating of the flocks, which soundeth in my ears, and the lowing of the herds, which I hear?€€ƒx¿And Saul said: They have brought them from Amalec: for the people spared the best of the sheep and of the herds, that they might be sacrificed to the Lord thy God, but the rest we have slain.€€ƒx}And Samuel said to Saul: Suffer me, and I will tell thee what the Lord hath said to me this night. And he said to him: Speak.€€ƒx¥And Samuel said: When thou wast a little one in thy own eyes, wast thou not made the head of the tribes of Israel? And the Lord anointed thee to be king over Israel.€€ƒxšAnd the Lord sent thee on the way, and said: Go, and kill the sinners of Amalec, and thou shalt fight against them until thou hast utterly destroyed them.€€ƒx‚Why then didst thou not hearken to the voice of the Lord: but hast turned to the prey, and hast done evil in the eyes of the Lord?€€ƒxÂAnd Saul said to Samuel: Yea, I have hearkened to the voice of the Lord, and have walked in the way by which the Lord sent me, and have brought Agag, the king of Amalec, and Amalec I have slain.€€ƒxšBut the people took of the spoils, sheep and oxen, as the firstfruits of those things that were slain, to offer sacrifice to the Lord their God in Galgal.€€ƒx×And Samuel said: Doth the Lord desire holocausts and victims, and not rather that the voice of the Lord should be obeyed? For obedience is better than sacrifices: and to hearken rather than to offer the fat or rams.€€ƒxÚBecause it is like the sin of witchcraft, to rebel: and like the crime of idolatry, to refuse to obey. Forasmuch, therefore, as thou hast rejected the word of the Lord, the Lord hath also rejected thee from being king.€€ƒxœAnd Saul said to Samuel: I have sinned, because I have transgressed the commandment of the Lord, and thy words, fearing the people, and obeying their voice.€€ƒxTBut now bear, I beseech thee, my sin, and return with me, that I may adore the Lord.€€ƒx£And Samuel said to Saul: I will not return with thee, because thou hath rejected the word of the Lord, and the Lord hath rejected thee from being king over Israel.€€ƒx_And Samuel turned about to go away: but he laid hold upon the skirt of his mantle, and it rent.€€ƒxAnd Samuel said to him: The Lord hath rent the kingdom of Israel from thee this day, and hath given it to thy neighbour who is better than thee.€€ƒx{But the triumpher in Israel will not spare, and will not be moved to repentance: for he is not a man that he should repent.€€ƒxšThen he said: I have sinned: yet honour me now before the ancients of my people, and before Israel, and return with me, that I may adore the Lord thy God.€€ƒx<So Samuel turned again after Saul: and Saul adored the Lord.€€ƒx®And Samuel said: Bring hither to me Agag, the king of Amalec. And Agag was presented to him very fat, and trembling. And Agag said: Doth bitter death separate in this manner?€€ƒx And Samuel said: As thy sword hath made women childless, so shall thy mother be childless among women. And Samuel hewed him in pieces before the Lord in Galgal.€€ƒxGAnd Samuel departed to Ramatha: but Saul went up to his house in Gabaa.€€ƒxAnd Samuel saw Saul no more till the day of his death: nevertheless, Samuel mourned for Saul, because the Lord repented that he had made him king over Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxïAnd the Lord said to Samuel: How long wilt thou mourn for Saul, whom I have rejected from reigning over Israel? fill thy horn with oil, and come, that I may send thee to Isai, the Bethlehemite: for I have provided me a king among his sons.€€ƒxÈAnd Samuel said: How shall I go? for Saul will hear of it, and he will kill me. And the Lord said: Thou shalt take with thee a calf of the herd, and thou shalt say: I am come to sacrifice to the Lord.€€ƒx‰And thou shalt call Isai to the sacrifice, and I will shew thee what thou art to do, and thou shalt anoint him whom I shall shew to thee.€€ƒx©Then Samuel did as the Lord had said to him. And he came to Bethlehem, and the ancients of the city wondered, and meeting him, they said: Is thy coming hither peaceable?€€ƒxÃAnd he said: It is peaceable: I am come to offer sacrifice to the Lord, be ye sanctified, and come with me to the sacrifice. And he sanctified Isai and his sons, and called them to the sacrifice.€€—ƒxïAnd the Lord said to Samuel: Look not on his countenance, nor on the height of his stature: because I have rejected him, nor do I judge according to the look of man: for man seeth those things that appear, but the Lord beholdeth the heart.€€ƒxhAnd Isai called Abinadab, and brought him before Samuel. And he said: Neither hath the Lord chosen this,€€ƒxNAnd Isai brought Samma, and he said of him: Neither hath the Lord chosen this.€€ƒxxIsai therefore brought his seven sons before Samuel: and Samuel said to Isai: The Lord hath not chosen any one of these.€€ƒxĞAnd Samuel said to Isai: Are here all thy sons? He answered: There remaineth yet a young one, who keepeth the sheep. And Samuel said to Isai: Send, and fetch him: for we will not sit down till he come hither.€€ƒxœHe sent therefore and brought him. Now he was ruddy and beautiful to behold, and of a comely face. And the Lord said: Arise, and anoint him, for this is he.€€ƒx»Then Samuel took the horn of oil, and anointed him in the midst of his brethren: and the Spirit of the Lord came upon David from that day forward: and Samuel rose up, and went to Ramatha.€€ƒx]But the Spirit of the Lord departed from Saul, and an evil spirit from the Lord troubled him.€€ƒxXAnd the servants of Saul said to him: Behold now an evil spirit from God troubleth thee.€€ƒxèLet our lord give orders, and thy servants who are before thee, will seek out a man skilful in playing on the harp, that when the evil spirit from the Lord is upon thee, he may play with his hand, and thou mayst bear it more easily.€€ƒx`And Saul said to his servants: Provide me then some man that can play well, and bring him to me.€€ƒxéAnd one of the servants answering, said: Behold I have seen a son of Isai, the Bethlehemite, a skilful player, and one of great strength, and a man fit for war, and prudent in his words, and a comely person: and the Lord is with him.€€ƒxZThen Saul sent messengers to Isai, saying: Send me David, thy son, who is in the pastures.€€ƒxŠAnd Isai took an ass laden with bread, and a bottle of wine, and a kid of the flock, and sent them by the hand of David, his son, to Saul.€€ƒxjAnd David came to Saul, and stood before him: and he loved him exceedingly, and made him his armourbearer.€€ƒx_And Saul sent to Isai, saying: Let David stand before me: for he hath found favour in my sight.€€ƒx¸So whensoever the evil spirit from the Lord was upon Saul, David took his harp, and played with his hand, and Saul was refreshed, and was better, for the evil spirit departed from him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx˜Now the Philistines gathering together their troops to battle, assembled at Socho of Juda: and camped between Socho and Azeca, in the borders of Dommim.€€ƒxAnd Saul and the children of Israel being gathered together, came to the valley of Terebinth, and they set the army in array to fight against the Philistines.€€ƒxAnd the Philistines stood on a mountain on the one side, and Israel stood on a mountain on the other side: and there was a valley between them.€€ƒxƒAnd there went out a man baseborn from the camp of the Philistines, named Goliath, of Geth, whose height was six cubits and a span:€€ƒx¥And he had a helmet of brass upon his head, and he was clothed with a coat of mail with scales, and the weight of his coat of mail was five thousand sicles of brass:€€˜:ƒx˜And the staff of his spear was like a weaver's beam, and the head of his spear weighed six hundred sicles of iron: and his armourbearer went before him.€€ƒxèAnd standing, he cried out to the bands of Israel, and said to them: Why are you come out prepared to fight? am not I a Philistine, and you the servants of Saul? Choose out a man of you, and let him come down and fight hand to hand.€€ƒxŸIf he be able to fight with me, and kill me, we will be servants to you: but if I prevail against him, and kill him, you shall be servants, and shall serve us.€€ƒx{And the Philistine said: I have defied the bands of Israel this day: give me a man, and let him fight with me hand to hand.€€ƒxiAnd Saul and all the Israelites hearing these words of the Philistine, were dismayed, and greatly afraid.€€ƒx»Now David was the son of that Ephrathite, of Bethlehem Juda, before mentioned, whose name was Isai, who had eight sons, and was an old man in the days of Saul, and of great age among men.€€ƒx¹And his three eldest sons followed Saul to the battle: and the names of his three sons that went to the battle, were Eliab, the firstborn, and the second, Abinadab, and the third Samma:€€ƒxEBut David was the youngest. So the three eldest having followed Saul,€€ƒxLDavid went, and returned from Saul, to feed his father's flock at Bethlehem.€€ƒxRNow the Philistine came out morning and evening, and presented himself forty days.€€ƒx†And Isai said to David, his son: Take for thy brethren an ephi of frumenty, and these ten loaves, and run to the camp to thy brethren,€€ƒx‚And carry these ten little cheeses to the tribune: and go see thy brethren, if they are well: and learn with whom they are placed.€€ƒxvBut Saul, and they, and all the children of Israel, were in the valley of Terebinth, fighting against the Philistines.€€ƒx÷David, therefore, arose in the morning, and gave the charge of the flock to the keeper: and went away loaded, as Isai had commanded him. And he came to the place of Magala, and to the army, which was going out to fight, and shouted for the battle.€€ƒxaFor Israel had put themselves in array, and the Philistines who stood against them were prepared.€€ƒxµAnd David leaving the vessels which he had brought, under the care of the keeper of the baggage, ran to the place of the battle, and asked if all things went well with his brethren.€€ƒxÚAnd as he talked with them, that baseborn man, whose name was Goliath, the Philistine, of Geth, shewed himself coming up from the camp of the Philistines: and he spoke according to the same words, and David heard them,€€ƒx[And all the Israelites, when they saw the man, fled from his face, fearing him exceedingly.€€ƒyAnd some one of Israel said: Have you seen this man that is come up, for he is come up to defy Israel. And the man that shall slay him, the king will enrich with great riches, and will give him his daughter, and will make his father's house free from tribute in Israel.€€ƒxÿAnd David spoke to the men that stood by him, saying: What shall be given to the man that shall kill this Philistine, and shall take away the reproach from Israel? for who is this uncircumcised Philistine, that he should defy the armies of the living God?€€ƒxoAnd the people answered him the same words, saying: These things shall be given to the man that shall slay him.€€ƒy Now when Eliab his eldest brother heard this, when he was speaking with others, he was angry with David, and said: Why camest thou hither? and why didst thou leave those few sheep in the desert? I know thy pride, and the wickedness of thy heart: that thou art come down to see the battle.€€ƒx>And David said: What have I done? is there not cause to speak?€€ƒxpAnd he turned a little aside from him to another: and said the same word. And the people answered him as before.€€ƒxKAnd the words which David spoke were heard, and were rehearsed before Saul.€€ƒxšAnd when he was brought to Saul, he said to him. Let not any man's heart be dismayed in him: I thy servant will go, and will fight against the Philistine.€€ƒxAnd Saul said to David: Thou art not able to withstand this Philistine, nor to fight against him: for thou art but a boy, but he is a warrior from his youth.€€ƒxŒAnd David said to Saul: Thy servant kept his father's sheep, and there came a lion, or a bear, and took a ram out of the midst of the flock:€€ƒx¯And I pursued after them, and struck them, and delivered it out of their mouth: and they rose up against me, and I caught them by the throat, and I strangled, and killed them.€€ƒyFor I thy servant have killed both a lion and a bear: and this uncircumcised Philistine shall be also as one of them. I will go now, and take away the reproach of the people: for who is this uncircumcised Philistine, who hath dared to curse the army of the living God?€€ƒxÓAnd David said: The Lord who delivered me out of the paw of the lion, and out of the paw of the bear, he will deliver me out of the hand of this Philistine. And Saul said to David: Go, and the Lord be with thee.€€ƒxuAnd Saul clothed David with his garments, and put a helmet of brass upon his head, and armed him with a coat of mail.€€ƒxÕAnd David having girded his sword upon his armour, began to try if he could walk in armour: for he was not accustomed to it. And David said to Saul: I cannot go thus, for I am not used to it. And he laid them off,€€ƒxòAnd he took his staff, which he had always in his hands: and chose him five smooth stones out of the brook, and put them into the shepherd's scrip, which he had with him, and he took a sling in his hand, and went forth against the Philistine.€€ƒx^And the Philistine came on, and drew nigh against David, and his armourbearer went before him.€€ƒx~And when the Philistine looked, and beheld David, he despised him. For he was a young man, ruddy, and of a comely countenance.€€ƒxAnd the Philistine said to David: Am I a dog, that thou comest to me with a staff? And the Philistine cursed David by his gods.€€ƒxtAnd he said to David: Come to me, and I will give thy flesh to the birds of the air, and to the beasts of the earth.€€ƒxÓAnd David said to the Philistine: Thou comest to me with a sword, and with a spear, and with a shield: but I come to thee in the name of the Lord of hosts, the God of the armies of Israel, which thou hast defied€€ƒy(This day, and the Lord will deliver thee into my hand, and I will slay thee, and take away thy head from thee: and I will give the carcasses of the army of the Philistines this day to the birds of the air, and to the beasts of the earth: that all the earth may know that there is a God in Israel.€€ƒxAnd all this assembly shall know that the Lord saveth not with sword and spear: for it is his battle, and he will deliver you into our hands.€€ƒxŠAnd when the Philistine arose, and was coming, and drew nigh to meet David, David made haste, and ran to the fight to meet the Philistine.€€ƒx·And he put his hand into his scrip, and took a stone, and cast it with the sling, and fetching it about, struck the Philistine in the forehead, and he fell on his face upon the earth.€€ƒx‘And David prevailed over the Philistine, with a sling and a stone, and he struck, and slew the Philistine. And as David had no sword in his hand,€€ƒxÁHe ran, and stood over the Philistine, and took his sword, and drew it out of the sheath, and slew him, and cut off his head. And the Philistines seeing that their champion was dead, fled away.€€ƒxıAnd the men of Israel and Juda rising up shouted, and pursued after the Philistines till they came to the valley and to the gates of Accaron, and there fell many wounded of the Philistines in the way of Saraim, and as far as Geth, and as far as Accaron.€€ƒxcAnd the children of Israel returning, after they had pursued the Philistines, fell upon their camp.€€ƒxhAnd David taking the head of the Philistine, brought it to Jerusalem: but his armour he put in his tent.€€ƒxÜNow at the time that Saul saw David going out against the Philistines, he said to Abner, the captain of the army: Of what family is this young man descended, Abner? And Abner said: As thy soul liveth, O king, I know not.€€ƒx=And the king said: Inquire thou, whose son this young man is.€€ƒx™And when David was returned, after the Philistine was slain, Abner took him, and brought him in before Saul, with the head of the Philistine in his hand.€€ƒx|And Saul said to him: Young man, of what family art thou? And David said: I am the son of thy servant Isai the Bethlehemite.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAnd it came to pass, when he had made an end of speaking to Saul, the son of Jonathan was knit with the soul of David, and Jonathan loved him as his own soul.€€ƒxOAnd Saul took him that day, and would not let him return to his father's house.€€ƒxIAnd David and Jonathan made a covenant, for he loved him as his own soul.€€ƒx®And Jonathan stripped himself of the coat with which he was clothed, and gave it to David, and the rest of his garments, even to his sword, and to his bow, and to his girdle.€€ƒxâAnd David went out to whatsoever business Saul sent him, and he behaved himself prudently: and Saul set him over the soldiers, and he was acceptable in the eyes of all the people, and especially in the eyes of Saul's servants.€€˜ƒxgAnd the women sung as they played, and they said: Saul slew his thousands, and David his ten thousands.€€ƒxËAnd Saul was exceeding angry, and this word was displeasing in his eyes, and he said: They have given David ten thousands, and to me they have given but a thousand, what can he have more but the kingdom?€€ƒxIAnd Saul did not look on David with a good eye from that day and forward.€€ƒx½And the day after, the evil spirit from God came upon Saul, and he prophesied in the midst of his house. And David played with his hand as at other times. And Saul held a spear in his hand,€€ƒxbAnd threw it, thinking to nail David to the wall: and David stept aside out of his presence twice.€€ƒxYAnd Saul feared David, because the Lord was with him, and was departed from Saul himself.€€ƒxTherefore Saul removed him from him, and made him a captain over a thousand men, and he went out and came in before the people.€€ƒxDAnd David behaved wisely in all his ways, and the Lord was with him.€€ƒxGAnd Saul saw that he was exceeding prudent, and began to beware of him.€€ƒxMBut all Israel and Juda loved David, for he came in and went out before them.€€ƒxüAnd Saul said to David: Behold my elder daughter Merob, her will I give thee to wife: only be a valiant man, and fight the battles of the Lord. Now Saul said within himself: Let not my hand be upon him, but let the hands of the Philistines be upon him.€€ƒxAnd David said to Saul: Who am I, or what is my life, or my father's family in Israel, that I should be son in law of the king?€€ƒx™And it came to pass at the time when Merob, the daughter of Saul, should have been given to David, that she was given to Hadriel, the Molathite, to wife.€€ƒx^But Michol, the other daughter of Saul, loved David. And it was told Saul, and it pleased him.€€ƒxÑAnd Saul said: I will give her to him, that she may be a stumblingblock to him, and that the hand of the Philistines may be upon him. And Saul said to David: In two things thou shalt be my son in law this day.€€ƒx­And Saul commanded his servants to speak to David privately, saying: Behold, thou pleasest the king, and all his servants love thee. Now, therefore be the king's son in law.€€ƒx¾And the servants of Saul spoke all these words in the ear of David. And David said: Doth it seem to you a small matter to be the king's son in law? But I am a poor man, and of small ability.€€ƒxQAnd the servants of Saul told him, saying: Such words as these hath David spoken.€€ƒxßAnd Saul said: Speak thus to David: The king desireth not any dowry, but only a hundred foreskins of the Philistines, to be avenged of the king's enemies. Now Saul thought to deliver David into the hands of the Philistines.€€ƒxŠAnd when his servants had told David the words that Saul had said, the word was pleasing in the eyes of David to be the king's son in law.€€ƒyAnd after a few days David rose up, and went with the men that were under him, and he slew of the Philistines two hundred men, and brought their foreskins and numbered them out to the king, that he might be his son in law. Saul therefore gave him Michol, his daughter, to wife.€€ƒxgAnd Saul saw, and understood that the Lord was with David. And Michol, the daughter of Saul, loved him.€€ƒxMAnd Saul began to fear David more: and Saul became David's enemy continually.€€ƒx½And the princes of the Philistines went forth: and from the beginning of their going forth, David behaved himself more wisely than all the servants of Saul, and his name became very famous.€€ƒ`€€ƒx’And Saul spoke to Jonathan, his son, and to all his servants, that they should kill David. But Jonathan, the son of Saul, loved David exceedingly.€€ƒx»And Jonathan told David, saying: Saul, my father, seeketh to kill thee: wherefore look to thyself, I beseech thee, in the morning and thou shalt abide in a secret place, and shalt be hid.€€ƒxAnd I will go out and stand beside my father in the field where thou art: and I will speak of thee to my father, and whatsoever I shall see, I will tell thee.€€ƒxÍAnd Jonathan spoke good things of David to Saul, his father: and said to him: Sin not, O king, against thy servant, David, because he hath not sinned against thee, and his works are very good towards thee.€€ƒxìAnd he put his life in his hand, and slew the Philistine, and the Lord wrought great salvation for all Israel. Thou sawest it and didst rejoice. Why therefore wilt thou sin against innocent blood, by killing David, who is without fault?€€˜ƒx¤Then Jonathan called David, and told him all these words: and Jonathan brought in David to Saul, and he was before him, as he had been yesterday and the day before.€€ƒx—And the war began again, and David went out, and fought against the Philistines, and defeated them with a great slaughter, and they fled from his face.€€ƒxˆAnd the evil spirit from the Lord came upon Saul; and he sat in his house, and held a spear in his hand: and David played with his hand.€€ƒxĞAnd Saul endeavoured to nail David to the wall with his spear. And David slipt away out of the presence of Saul: and the spear missed him, and was fastened in the wall, and David fled, and escaped that night.€€ƒxİSaul therefore sent his guards to David's house to watch him, that he might be killed in the morning. And when Michol, David's wife, had told him this, saying: Unless thou save thyself this night, to morrow thou wilt die:€€ƒxJShe let him down through a window. And he went and fled away, and escaped.€€ƒx†And Michol took an image, and laid it on the bed, and put a goat's skin, with the hair at the head of it, and covered it with clothes.€€ƒxLAnd Saul sent officers to seize David; and it was answered that he was sick.€€ƒx[And again Saul sent to see David, saying: Bring him to me in the bed, that he may be slain.€€ƒxdAnd when the messengers were come in, they found an image upon the bed, and a goat skin at his head.€€ƒx¯And Saul said to Michol: Why hast thou deceived me so, and let my enemy go and flee away? And Michol answered Saul: Because he said to me: Let me go, or else I will kill thee.€€ƒx’But David fled and escaped, and came to Samuel in Ramatha, and told him all that Saul had done to him: and he and Samuel went and dwelt in Najoth.€€ƒxLAnd it was told Saul by some, saying: Behold David is in Najoth, in Ramatha.€€ƒxÊSo Saul sent officers to take David: and when they saw a company of prophets prophesying, and Samuel presiding over them, the Spirit of the Lord came also upon them, and they likewise began to prophesy.€€ƒx¹And when this was told Saul, he sent other messengers: but they also prophesied. And again Saul sent messengers the third time: and they prophesied also. And Saul being exceeding angry,€€ƒxÎWent also himself to Ramatha, and came as far as the great cistern, which is in Socho, and he asked, and said: In what place are Samuel and David? And it was told him: Behold they are in Najoth, in Ramatha.€€ƒx”And he went to Najoth, in Ramatha, and the Spirit of the Lord came upon him also, and he went on, and prophesied till he came to Najoth, in Ramatha.€€ƒxËAnd he stripped himself also of his garments, and prophesied with the rest before Samuel, and lay down naked all that day and night. This gave occasion to a proverb: What! is Saul too among the prophets?€€ƒ`€€ƒx¶But David fled from Najoth, which is in Ramatha, and came and said to Jonathan: What have I done? what is my iniquity, and what is my sin against thy father, that he seeketh my life?€€ƒxÅAnd he said to him: (God forbid, thou shalt not die: for my father will do nothing, great or little, without first telling me: hath then my father hid this word only from me? no, this shall not be.€€ƒyAnd he swore again to David. And David said: Thy father certainly knoweth that I have found grace in thy sight, and he will say: Let not Jonathan know this, lest he be grieved. But truly as the Lord liveth, and thy soul liveth, there is but one step (as I may say) between me and death.€€ƒxTAnd Jonathan said to David: Whatsoever thy soul shall say to me, I will do for thee.€€ƒxÑAnd David said to Jonathan: Behold to morrow is the new moon, and I, according to custom, am wont to sit beside the king to eat: let me go then that I may be hid in the field till the evening of the third day.€€˜*ƒxzIf he shall say: It is well: thy servant shall have peace: but if he be angry, know that his malice is come to its height.€€ƒxÌDeal mercifully then with thy servant: for thou hast brought me, thy servant, into a covenant of the Lord with thee. But if there be any iniquity in me, do thou kill me, and bring me not in to thy father.€€ƒx£And Jonathan said: Far be this from thee: for if I should certainly know that evil is determined by my father against thee, I could do no otherwise than tell thee.€€ƒxmAnd David answered Jonathan: Who shall bring me word, if thy father should answer thee harshly concerning me?€€ƒx|And Jonathan said to David: Come, and let us go out into the field. And when they were both of them gone out into the field,€€ƒxÖJonathan said to David: O Lord God of Israel, if I shall discover my father's mind, to morrow, or the day after, and there be any thing good for David, and I send not immediately to thee, and make it known to thee,€€ƒyMay the Lord do so and so to Jonathan, and add still more. But if my father shall continue in malice against thee, I will discover it to thy ear, and will send thee away, that thou mayst go in peace, and the Lord be with thee, as he hath been with my father.€€ƒxIAnd if I live, thou shalt shew me the kindness of the Lord: but if I die,€€ƒy␀Thou shalt not take away thy kindness from my house for ever, when the Lord shall have rooted out the enemies of David, every one of them from the earth, may he take away Jonathan from his house, and may the Lord require it at the hands of David's enemies.€€ƒxuJonathan therefore made a covenant with the house of David: and the Lord required it at the hands of David's enemies.€€ƒxZAnd Jonathan swore again to David, because he loved him: for he loved him as his own soul.€€ƒxMAnd Jonathan said to him: To morrow is the new moon, and thou wilt be missed:€€ƒxáFor thy seat will be empty till after to morrow. So thou shalt go down quickly, and come to the place where thou must he hid, on the day when it is lawful to work, and thou shalt remain beside the stone, which is called Ezel.€€ƒx_And I will shoot three arrows near it, and will shoot as if I were exercising myself at a mark.€€ƒxAAnd I will send a boy, saying to him: Go and fetch me the arrows.€€ƒy'If I shall say to the boy: Behold the arrows are on this side of thee, take them up: come thou to me, because there is peace to thee, and there is no evil, as the Lord liveth. But if I shall speak thus to the boy: Behold the arrows are beyond thee: go in peace, for the Lord hath sent thee away.€€ƒx^And concerning the word which I and thou have spoken, the Lord be between thee and me forever.€€ƒxYSo David was hid in the field, and the new moon came, and the king sat down to eat bread.€€ƒx«And when the king sat down upon his chair, (according to custom) which was beside the wall, Jonathan arose, and Abner sat by Saul's side, and David's place appeared empty.€€ƒxrAnd Saul said nothing that day, for he thought it might have happened to him, that he was not clean, nor purified.€€ƒxÂAnd when the second day after the new moon was come, David's place appeared empty again. And Saul said to Jonathan, his son: Why cometh not the son of Isai to meat neither yesterday, nor to day?€€ƒxNAnd Jonathan answered Saul: He asked leave of me earnestly to go to Bethlehem.€€ƒxíAnd he said: Let me go, for there is a solemn sacrifice in the city, one of my brethren hath sent for me: and now if I have found favour in thy eyes, I will go quickly, and see my brethren. For this cause he came not to the king's table.€€ƒxÚThen Saul being angry against Jonathan, said to him: Thou son of a woman that is the ravisher of a man, do I not know that thou lovest the son of Isai to thy own confusion, and to the confusion of thy shameless mother?€€ƒx°For as long as the son of Isai liveth upon earth, thou shalt not be established, nor thy kingdom. Therefore now presently send, and fetch him to me: for he is the son of death.€€ƒxSAnd Jonathan answering Saul, his father, said: Why shall he die? What hath he done?€€ƒxuAnd Saul caught up a spear to strike him. And Jonathan understood that it was determined by his father to kill David.€€ƒx¶So Jonathan rose from the table in great anger, and did not eat bread on the second day after the new moon. For he was grieved for David, because his father had put him to confusion.€€ƒx{And when the morning came, Jonathan went into the field according to the appointment with David, and a little boy with him.€€ƒx~And he said to his boy: Go, and fetch me the arrows which I shoot. And when the boy ran, he shot another arrow beyond the boy.€€ƒx¤The boy therefore came to the place of the arrow which Jonathan had shot: and Jonathan cried after the boy, and said: Behold the arrow is there further beyond thee.€€ƒxšAnd Jonathan cried again after the boy, saying: Make haste speedily, stand not. And Jonathan's boy gathered up the arrows, and brought them to his master:€€ƒxSAnd he knew not at all what was doing: for only Jonathan and David knew the matter.€€ƒx_Jonathan therefore gave his arms to the boy, and said to him: Go, and carry them into the city.€€ƒxÆAnd when the boy was gone, David rose out of his place, which was toward the south, and falling on his face to the ground, adored thrice: and kissing one another, they wept together; but David more.€€ƒxÅAnd Jonathan said to David: Go in peace: and let all stand that we have sworn both of us in the name of the Lord, saying: The Lord be between me and thee, and between my seed and thy seed for ever.€€ƒx?And David arose, and departed: and Jonathan went into the city.€€ƒ`€€ƒx And David came to Nobe, to Achimelech, the priest and Achimelech was astonished at David's coming. And he said to him: Why art thou alone, and no man with thee?€€ƒxıAnd David said to Achimelech, the priest: The king hath commanded me a business, and said: Let no man know the thing for which thou art sent by me, and what manner of commands I have given thee: and I have appointed my servants to such and such a place.€€ƒxuNow therefore if thou have any thing at hand, though it were but five loaves, give me, or whatsoever thou canst find.€€ƒxAnd the priest answered David, saying: I have no common bread at hand, but only holy bread, if the young men be clean, especially from women?€€ƒxÌThe priest therefore gave him hallowed bread: for there was no bread there, but only the loaves of proposition, which had been taken away from before the face of the Lord, that hot loaves might be set up.€€ƒx¤Now a certain man of the servants of Saul was there that day, within the tabernacle of the Lord: and his name was Doeg, an Edomite, the chiefest of Saul's herdsmen.€€ƒx­And David said to Achimelech: Hast thou here at hand a spear, or a sword? for I brought not my own sword, nor my own weapons with me, for the king's business required haste.€€ƒyAnd the priest said: Lo, here is the sword of Goliath, the Philistine, whom thou slewest in the valley of Terebinth, wrapped up in a cloth behind the ephod: if thou wilt take this, take it, for here there is no other but this. And David said: There is none like that, give it me.€€ƒx]And David arose and fled that day from the face of Saul: and came to Achis, the king of Geth:€€ƒxÓAnd the servants of Achis, when they saw David, said to him: Is not this David, the king of the land? Did they not sing to him in their dances, saying: Saul hath slain his thousands, and David his ten thousands?€€ƒxnBut David laid up these words in his heart, and was exceedingly afraid at the face of Achis, the king of Geth.€€ƒx§And he changed his countenance before them, and slipt down between their hands: and he stumbled against the doors of the gate, and his spittle ran down upon his beard.€€ƒxXAnd Achis said to his servants: You saw the man was mad: why have you brought him to me?€€ƒx‡Have we need of mad men, that you have brought in this fellow, to play the madman in my presence? shall this fellow come into my house?€€ƒ`€€ƒx¥David therefore went from thence, and fled to the cave of Odollam. And when his brethren, and all his father's house, had heard of it, they went down to him thither.€€ƒx¿And all that were in distress, and oppressed with debt, and under affliction of mind, gathered themselves unto him: and he became their prince, and there were with him about four hundred men.€€ƒxµAnd David departed from thence into Maspha of Moab: and he said to the king of Moab: Let my father and my mother tarry with you, I beseech thee, till I know what God will do for me.€€ƒxuAnd he left them under the eyes of the king of Moab, and they abode with him all the days that David was in the hold.€€—ƒxÖAnd SauI heard that David was seen, and the men that were with him. Now whilst Saul abode in Gabaa, and was in the wood, which is by Rama, having his spear in his hand, and all his servants were standing about him,€€ƒxºHe said to his servants that stood about him: Hear me now, ye sons of Jemini: will the son of Isai give every one of you fields, and vineyards, and make you all tribunes, and centurions:€€ƒy?That all of you have conspired against me, and there is no one to inform me, especially when even my son hath entered into league with the son of Isai? There is not one of you that pitieth my case, nor that giveth me any information: because my son hath raised up my servant against me, plotting against me to this day.€€ƒx´And Doeg, the Edomite, who stood by, and was the chief among the servants of Saul, answering, said: I saw the son of Isai, in Nobe, with Achimelech, the son of Achitob, the priest.€€ƒxlAnd he consulted the Lord for him, and gave him victuals, and gave him the sword of Goliath, the Philistine.€€ƒx¬Then the king sent to call for Achimelech, the priest, the son of Achitob, and all his father's house, the priests that were in Nobe, and they came all of them to the king.€€ƒxXAnd Saul said to Achimelech: Hear, thou son of Achitob. He answered: Here I am, my lord.€€ƒxèAnd Saul said to him: Why have you conspired against me, thou, and the son of Isai, and thou hast given him bread and a sword, and hast consulted the Lord for him, that he should rise up against me, continuing a traitor to this day.€€ƒxÀAnd Achimelech answering the king, said: And who amongst all thy servants is so faithful as David, who is the king's son in law, and goeth forth at thy bidding, and is honourable in thy house?€€ƒxéDid I begin to day to consult the Lord for him? far be this from me: let not the king suspect such a thing against his servant, or any one in all my father's house: for thy servant knew nothing of this matter, either little or great.€€ƒxUAnd the king said: Dying thou shalt die, Achimelech, thou and all thy father's house.€€ƒyAnd the king said to the messengers that stood about him: Turn, and kill the priests of the Lord, for their hand is with David, because they knew that he was fled, and they told it not to me. And the king's servants would not put forth their hands against the priests of the Lord.€€ƒxºAnd the king said to Doeg: Turn thou, and fall upon the priests. And Doeg, the Edomite, turned, and fell upon the priests, and slew in that day eighty-five men that wore the linen ephod.€€ƒx«And Nobe, the city of the priests, he smote with the edge of the sword, both men and women, children and sucklings, and ox, and ass, and sheep, with the edge of the sword.€€ƒxkBut one of the sons of Achimelech, the son of Achitob, whose name was Abiathar, escaped, and fled to David,€€ƒx9And told him that Saul had slain the priests of the Lord.€€ƒxÃAnd David said to Abiathar: I knew that day when Doeg, the Edomite, was there, that without doubt he would tell Saul: I have been the occasion of the death of all the souls of thy father's house.€€ƒxrAbide thou with me, fear not: for he that seeketh my life, seeketh thy life also, and with me thou shalt be saved.€€ƒ`€€ƒx`And they told David, saying: Behold the Philistines fight against Ceila, and they rob the barns.€€ƒx¯Therefore David consulted the Lord, saying: Shall I go and smite these Philistines? And the Lord said to David: Go, and thou shalt smite the Philistines, and shalt save Ceila.€€ƒx™And the men that were with David, said to him: Behold we are in fear here in Judea, how much more if we go to Ceila against the bands of the Philistines?€€ƒx”Therefore David consulted the Lord again. And he answered and said to him: Arise, and go to Ceila: for I will deliver the Philistines into thy hand.€€˜ƒxxNow at that time, when Abiathar, the son of Achimelech, fled to David, to Ceila, he came down, having an ephod with him.€€ƒx°And it was told Saul that David was come to Ceila: and Saul said: The Lord hath delivered him into my hands, and he is shut up, being come into a city that hath gates and bars.€€ƒxfAnd Saul commanded all the people to go down to fight against Ceila, and to besiege David and his men.€€ƒx€Now when David understood that Saul secretly prepared evil against him, he said to Abiathar, the priest: Bring hither the ephod.€€ƒxAnd David said: O Lord God of Israel, thy servant hath heard a report, that Saul designeth to come to Ceila, to destroy the city for my sake:€€ƒx²Will the men of Ceila deliver me into his hands? and will Saul come down, as thy servant hath heard? O Lord God of Israel, tell thy servant. And the Lord said: He will come down.€€ƒxAnd David said: Will the men of Ceila deliver me and my men into the hands of Saul? And the Lord said: They will deliver thee up.€€ƒxòThen David and his men, who were about six hundred, arose, and departing from Ceila, wandered up and down, uncertain where they should stay: and it was told Saul that David was fled from Ceila, and had escaped: wherefore he forbore to go out.€€ƒx¿But David abode in the desert in strong holds, and he remained in a mountain of the desert of Ziph, in a woody hill. And Saul sought him always: but the Lord delivered him not into his hands.€€ƒxfAnd David saw that Saul was come out to seek his life. And David was in the desert of Ziph, in a wood.€€ƒx~And Jonathan, the son of Saul, arose, and went to David, into the wood, and strengthened his hands in God: and he said to him:€€ƒxFear not: for the hand of my father, Saul, shall not find thee, and thou shalt reign over Israel, and I shall be next to thee; yea and my father knoweth this.€€ƒxnAnd they two made a covenant before the Lord: and David abode in the wood: but Jonathan returned to his house.€€ƒx³And the Ziphites went up to Saul, in Gabaa, saying: Lo, doth not David lie hid with us in the strong holds of the wood, in mount Hachila, which is on the right hand of the desert.€€ƒx‚Now therefore come down, as thy soul hath desired to come down: and it shall be our business to deliver him into the king's hands.€€ƒxFAnd Saul said: Blessed be ye of the Lord, for you have pitied my case.€€ƒxËGo, therefore, I pray you, and use all diligence, and curiously inquire, and consider the place where his foot is, and who hath seen him there: for he thinketh of me, that I lie craftily in wait for him.€€ƒx÷Consider, and see all his lurking holes, wherein he is hid, and return to me with the certainty of the thing, that I may go with you. And if he should even go down into the earth to hide himself, I will search him out in all the thousands of Juda.€€ƒxŠAnd they arose, and went to Ziph before Saul: and David and his men were in the desert of Maon, in the plain at the right hand of Jesimon.€€ƒxÜThen Saul and his men went to seek him: and it was told David, and forthwith he went down to the rock, and abode in the wilderness of Maon: and when Saul had heard of it, he pursued after David in the wilderness of Maon.€€ƒxúAnd Saul went on this side of the mountain: and David and his men were on the other side of the mountain: and David despaired of being able to escape from the face of Saul: and Saul and his men encompassed David and his men round about, to take them.€€ƒxvAnd a messenger came to Saul, saying: Make haste to come, for the Philistines have poured in themselves upon the land.€€ƒx”Wherefore Saul returned, leaving the pursuit of David, and went to meet the Philistines. For this cause they called that place the rock of division.€€ƒ`€€ƒxEThen David went up from thence, and dwelt in strong holds of Engaddi.€€ƒx|And when Saul was returned from following the Philistines, they told him, saying: Behold, David is in the desert of Engaddi.€€ƒxºSaul, therefore, took three thousand chosen men out of all Israel, and went out to seek after David and his men, even upon the most craggy rocks, which are accessible only to wild goats.€€ƒx­And he came to the sheepcotes which were in his way. And there was a cave, into which Saul went, to ease nature: now David and his men lay hid in the inner part of the cave.€€ƒxöAnd the servants of David said to him: Behold the day, of which the Lord said to thee: I will deliver thy enemy unto thee, that thou mayst do to him as it shall seem good in thy eyes. Then David arose, and secretly cut off the hem of Saul's robe.€€–ƒx³And he said to his men: The Lord be merciful unto me, that I may do no such thing to my master, the Lord's anointed, as to lay my hand upon him, because he is the Lord's anointed.€€ƒx‹And David stopped his men with his words, and suffered them not to rise against Saul: but Saul, rising up out of the cave, went on his way.€€ƒx½And David also rose up after him: and going out of the cave, cried after Saul, saying: My lord the king. And Saul looked behind him: and David bowing himself down to the ground, worshipped,€€ƒxWAnd said to Saul: Why dost thou hear the words of men that say: David seeketh thy hurt?€€ƒxúBehold this day thy eyes have seen, that the Lord hath delivered thee into my hand, in the cave, and I had a thought to kill thee, but my eye hath spared thee. For I said: I will not put out my hand against my lord, because he is the Lord's anointed.€€ƒy2Moreover, see and know, O my father, the hem of thy robe in my hand, that when I cut off the hem of thy robe, I would not put out my hand against thee. Reflect, and see, that there is no evil in my hand, nor iniquity, neither have I sinned against thee: but thou liest in wait for my life, to take it away.€€ƒxgThe Lord judge between me and thee and the Lord revenge me of thee: but my hand shall not be upon thee.€€ƒx®As also it is said in the old proverb: From the wicked shall wickedness come forth: therefore my hand shall not be upon thee. After whom dost thou come out, O king of Israel?€€ƒx<After whom dost thou pursue? After a dead dog, after a flea.€€ƒxnBe the Lord judge, and judge between me and thee, and see, and judge my cause, and deliver me out of thy hand.€€ƒx“And when David had made an end of speaking these words to Saul, Saul said: Is this thy voice, my son David? And Saul lifted up his voice, and wept:€€ƒxsAnd he said to David: Thou art more just than I: for thou hast done good to me, and I have rewarded thee with evil.€€ƒxŠAnd thou hast shewed this day what good things thou hast done to me: how the Lord delivered me into thy hand, and thou hast not killed me.€€ƒx•For who when he hath found his enemy, will let him go well away? But the Lord reward thee for this good turn, for what thou hast done to me this day.€€ƒx]And now as I know that thou shalt surely be king, and have the kingdom of Israel in thy hand:€€ƒxxSwear to me by the Lord, that thou wilt not destroy my seed after me, nor take away my name from the house of my father.€€ƒx\And David swore to Saul. So Saul went home: and David and his men went up into safer places.€€ƒ`€€ƒxµAnd Samuel died, and all Israel was gathered together, and they mourned for him, and buried him in his house in Ramatha. And David rose, and went down into the wilderness of Pharan.€€ƒxçNow there was a certain man in the wilderness of Maon, and his possessions were in Carmel, and the man was very great: and he had three thousand sheep, and a thousand goats: and it happened that he was shearing his sheep in Carmel.€€ƒxÓNow the name of the man was Nabal: and the name of his wife was Abigail. And she was a prudent and very comely woman: but her husband was churlish, and very bad and ill natured: and he was of the house of Caleb.€€ƒxJAnd when David heard in the wilderness, that Nabal was shearing his sheep,€€˜,ƒxqAnd you shall say: Peace be to my brethren, and to thee, and peace to thy house, and peace to all that thou hast.€€ƒxÓI have heard that thy shepherds that were with us in the desert were shearing: we never molested them, neither was there ought missing to them of the flock at any time, all the while they were with us in Carmel.€€ƒxÌAsk thy servants, and they will tell thee. Now therefore let thy servants find favour in thy eyes: for we are come in a good day, whatsoever thy hand shall find give to thy servants, and to thy son David.€€ƒxoAnd when David's servants came, they spoke to Nabal all these words in David's name, and then held their peace.€€ƒx™But Nabal answering the servants of David, said: Who is David? and what is the son of Isai? servants are multiplied now days who flee from their masters.€€ƒx›Shall I then take my bread, and my water, and the flesh of my cattle, which I have killed for my shearers, and give to men whom I know not whence they are?€€ƒxiSo the servants of David went back their way, and returning came and told him all the words that he said.€€ƒxêThen David said to his young men: Let every man gird on his sword. And they girded on every man his sword. And David also girded on his sword: and there followed David about four hundred men, and two hundred remained with the baggage.€€ƒx¢But one of the servants told, Abigail, the wife of Nabal, saying: Behold, David sent messengers out of the wilderness, to salute our master: and he rejected them.€€ƒx’These men were very good to us, and gave us no trouble: Neither did we ever lose any thing all the time that we conversed with them in the desert.€€ƒxcThey were a wall unto us, both by night and day, all the while we were with them keeping the sheep.€€ƒx²Wherefore consider, and think what thou hast to do: for evil is determined against thy husband, and against thy house, and he is a son of Belial, so that no man can speak to him.€€ƒxîThen Abigail made haste and took two hundred loaves, and two vessels of wine, and five sheep ready dressed, and five measures of parched corn, and a hundred clusters of raisins, and two hundred cakes of dry figs, and laid them upon asses:€€ƒxqAnd she said to her servants: Go before me: behold, I will follow after you: but she told not her husband, Nabal.€€ƒx•And when she had gotten upon an ass, and was coming down to the foot of the mountain, David and his men came down over against her, and she met them.€€ƒx¹And David said: Truly in vain have I kept all that belonged to this fellow in the wilderness, and nothing was lost of all that pertained unto him: and he hath returned me evil for good.€€ƒx’May God do so and so, and add more to the foes of David, if I leave of all that belong to him till the morning, any that pisseth against the wall.€€ƒxƒAnd when Abigail saw David, she made haste and lighted off the ass, and fell before David, on her face, and adored upon the ground.€€ƒx¢And she fell at his feet, and said: Upon me let this iniquity be, my lord: let thy handmaid speak, I beseech thee, in thy ears, and hear the words of thy servant.€€ƒxÑLet not my lord the king, I pray thee, regard this naughty man, Nabal: for according to his name, he is a fool, and folly is with him: but I, thy handmaid, did not see thy servants, my lord, whom thou sentest.€€ƒxÚNow therefore, my lord, the Lord liveth, and thy soul liveth, who hath withholden thee from coming to blood, and hath saved thy hand to thee: and now let thy enemies be as Nabal, and all they that seek evil to my lord.€€ƒxŠWherefore receive this blessing, which thy handmaid hath brought to thee, my lord: and give it to the young men that follow thee, my lord.€€ƒxÜForgive the iniquity of thy handmaid: for the Lord will surely make for my lord a faithful house, because thou, my lord, fightest the battles of the Lord: let not evil therefore be found in thee all the days of thy life.€€ƒyFor if a man at any time shall rise, and persecute thee, and seek thy life, the soul of my lord shall be kept, as in the bundle of the living, with the Lord thy God: but the souls of thy enemies shall be whirled, as with the violence and whirling of a sling.€€ƒx’And when the Lord shall have done to thee, my lord, all the good that he hath spoken concerning thee, and shall have made thee prince over Israel,€€ƒxåThis shall not be an occasion of grief to thee, and a scruple of heart to my lord, that thou hast shed innocent blood, or hast revenged thyself: and when the Lord shall have done well by my lord, thou shalt remember thy handmaid.€€ƒxAnd David said to Abigail: Blessed be the Lord the God of Israel, who sent thee this day to meet me, and blessed be thy speech:€€ƒxeAnd blessed be thou, who hast kept me to day from coming to blood, and revenging me with my own hand.€€ƒxçOtherwise, as the Lord liveth, the God of Israel, who hath withholden me from doing thee any evil, if thou hadst not quickly come to meet me, there had not been left to Nabal by the morning light, any that pisseth against the wall.€€ƒxŸAnd David received at her hand all that she had brought him, and said to her: Go in peace into thy house, behold I have heard thy voice, and honoured thy face.€€ƒxÅAnd Abigail came to Nabal: and behold he had a feast in his house, like the feast of a king: and Nabal's heart was merry, for he was very drunk: and she told him nothing less or more until morning.€€ƒx“But early in the morning, when Nabal had digested his wine, his wife told him these words, and his heart died within him, and he became as a stone.€€ƒxBAnd after ten days had passed, the Lord struck Nabal, and he died.€€ƒyJAnd when David had heard that Nabal was dead, he said: Blessed be the Lord, who hath judged the cause of my reproach, at the hand of Nabal, and hath kept his servant from evil, and the Lord hath returned the wickedness of Nabal upon his head. Then David sent and treated with Abigail, that he might take her to himself for a wife.€€ƒxŠAnd David's servants came to Abigail, to Carmel, and spoke to her, saying: David hath sent us to thee, to take thee to himself for a wife.€€ƒxŸAnd she arose, and bowed herself down with her face to the earth, and said: Behold, let thy servant be a handmaid, to wash the feet of the servants of my lord.€€ƒx©And Abigail arose, and made haste, and got upon an ass, and five damsels went with her, her waiting maids, and she followed the messengers of David, and became his wife.€€ƒxTMoreover David took also Achinoam of Jezrahel: and they were both of them his wives.€€ƒx`But Saul gave Michol, his daughter, David's wife, to Phalti, the son of Lais, who was of Gallim.€€ƒ`€€ƒx„And the men of Ziph came to Saul in Gabaa, saying: Behold David is hid in the hill of Hachila, which is over against the wilderness.€€ƒx•And Saul arose, and went down to the wilderness of Ziph having with him three thousand chosen men of Israel, to seek David in the wilderness of Ziph.€€ƒxµAnd Saul encamped in Gabaa Hachila, which was over against the wilderness in the way: and David abode in the wilderness. And seeing that Saul was come after him into the wilderness,€€ƒxCHe sent spies, and learned that he was most certainly come thither.€€˜ƒx¹David spoke to Achimelech, the Hethite, and Abisai, the son of Sarvia, the brother of Joab, saying: Who will go down with me to Saul into the camp? And Abisai said: I will go with thee.€€ƒxÁSo David and Abisai came to the people by night, and found Saul lying and sleeping in the tent, and his spear fixed in the ground at his head: and Abner and the people sleeping round about him.€€ƒxÄAnd Abisai said to David: God hath shut up thy enemy this day into thy hands: now then I will run him through with my spear, even to the earth at once, and there shall be no need of a second time.€€ƒx}And David said to Abisai: Kill him not: for who shall put forth his hand against the Lord's anointed, and shall be guiltless?€€ƒxAnd David said: As the Lord liveth, unless the Lord shall strike him, or his day shall come to die, or he shall go down to battle, and perish:€€ƒx·The Lord be merciful unto me, and keep me that I never put forth my hand against the Lord's anointed. But now take the spear which is at his head, and the cup of water, and let us go.€€ƒxÔSo David took the spear, and the cup of water which was at Saul's head, and they went away: and no man saw it, or knew it, or awaked, but they were all asleep, for a deep sleep from the Lord was fallen upon them.€€ƒx}And when David was gone over to the other side, and stood on the top of the hill afar off, and a good space was between them,€€ƒx¬David cried to the people, and to Abner, the son of Ner, saying: Wilt thou not answer, Abner? And Abner answering, said: Who art thou, that criest, and disturbest the king?€€ƒx¿And David said to Abner: Art not thou a man? and who is like unto thee in Israel? why then hast thou not kept thy lord the king? for there came one of the people in to kill the king thy lord.€€ƒxßThis thing is not good, that thou hast done: as the Lord liveth, you are the sons of death, who have not kept your master, the Lord's anointed. And now where is the king's spear, and the cup of water, which was at his head?€€ƒxyAnd Saul knew David's voice, and said: Is this thy voice, my son David? And David said: It is my voice, my lord the king.€€ƒxnAnd he said: Wherefore doth my lord persecute his servant? What have I done? or what evil is there in my hand?€€ƒyGNow therefore hear, I pray thee, my lord the king, the words of thy servant: If the Lord stir thee up against me, let him accept of sacrifice: but if the sons of men, they are cursed in the sight of the Lord, who have cast me out this day, that I should not dwell in the inheritance of the Lord, saying: Go, serve strange gods.€€ƒx And now let not my blood be shed upon the earth before the Lord: for the king of Israel is come out to seek a flea, as the partridge is hunted in the mountains.€€ƒxæAnd Saul said: I have sinned; return, my son David, for I will no more do thee harm, because my life hath been precious in thy eyes this day: for it appeareth that I have done foolishly, and have been ignorant in very many things.€€ƒxjAnd David answering, said: Behold the king's spear: let one of the king's servants come over and fetch it.€€ƒxÉAnd the Lord will reward every one according to his justice, and his faithfulness: for the Lord hath delivered thee this day into my hand, and I would not put forth my hand against the Lord's anointed.€€ƒx›And as thy life hath been much set by this day in my eyes, so let my life be much set by in the eyes of the Lord, and let him deliver me from all distress.€€ƒxµThen Saul said to David: Blessed art thou, my son David: and truly doing thou shalt do, and prevailing thou shalt prevail. And David went on his way, and Saul returned to his place.€€ƒ`€€ƒyAnd David said in his heart: I shall one day or other fall into the hands of Saul: is it not better for me to flee, and to be saved in the land of the Philistines, that Saul may despair of me, and cease to seek me in all the coasts of Israel? I will flee then out of his hands.€€ƒx}And David arose, and went away, both he and the six hundred men that were with him, to Achis, the son of Maoch, king of Geth.€€ƒx¶And David dwelt with Achis at Geth, he and his men; every man with his household, and David with his two wives, Achinoam, the Jezrahelitess, and Abigail, the wife of Nabal of Carmel.€€ƒxRAnd it was told Saul that David was fled to Geth, and he sought no more after him.€€ŒƒxlThen Achis gave him Siceleg that day: for which reason Siceleg belongeth to the kings of Juda unto this day.€€ƒxQAnd the time that David dwelt in the country of the Philistines, was four months.€€ƒx¸And David and his men went up, and pillaged Gessuri, and Gerzi, and the Amalecites: for these were of old the inhabitants of the countries, as men go to Sur, even to the land of Egypt.€€ƒx»And David wasted all the land, and left neither man nor woman alive: and took away the sheep, and the oxen, and the asses, and the camels, and the apparel, and returned and came to Achis.€€ƒx§And Achis said to him: Whom hast thou gone against to day? David answered: Against the south of Juda, and against the south of Jerameel, and against the south of Ceni.€€ƒxáAnd David saved neither man nor woman, neither brought he any of them to Geth, saying: Lest they should speak against us. So did David, and such was his proceeding all the days that he dwelt in the country of the Philistines.€€ƒxyAnd Achis believed David, saying: He hath done much harm to his people Israel: Therefore he shall be my servant for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒxîAnd it came to pass in those days, that the Philistines gathered together their armies, to be prepared for war against Israel: And Achis said to David: Know thou now assuredly, that thou shalt go out with me to the war, thou, and thy men.€€ƒx’And David said to Achis: Now thou shalt know what thy servant will do. And Achis said to David: And I will appoint thee to guard my life for ever.€€ƒx¢Now Samuel was dead, and all Israel mourned for him, and buried him in Ramatha, his city. And Saul had put away all the magicians and soothsayers out of the land.€€ƒx‹And the Philistines were gathered together, and came and encamped in Sunam: and Saul also gathered together all Israel, and came to Gelboe.€€˜ƒxgAnd he consulted the Lord, and he answered him not, neither by dreams, nor by priests, nor by prophets.€€ƒxÊAnd Saul said to his servants: Seek me a woman that hath a divining spirit, and I will go to her, and enquire by her. And his servants said to him: There is a woman that hath a divining spirit at Endor.€€ƒxáThen he disguised himself: and put on other clothes, and he went, and two men with him, and they came to the woman by night, and he said to her: Divine to me by thy divining spirit, and bring me up him whom I shall tell thee.€€ƒxÛAnd the woman said to him: Behold thou knowest all that Saul hath done, and how he hath rooted out the magicians and soothsayers from the land: why then dost thou lay a snare for my life, to cause me to be put to death?€€ƒxsAnd Saul swore unto her by the Lord, saying: As the Lord liveth, there shall no evil happen to thee for this thing.€€ƒxZAnd the woman said to him: Whom shall I bring up to thee? And he said, Bring me up Samuel.€€ƒxAnd when the woman saw Samuel, she cried out with a loud voice, and said to Saul: Why hast thou deceived me? for thou art Saul.€€ƒx{And the king said to her: Fear not: what hast thou seen? and the woman said to Saul: I saw gods ascending out of the earth.€€ƒxÒAnd he said to her: What form is he of? And she said: An old man cometh up, and he is covered with a mantle. And Saul understood that it was Samuel, and he bowed himself with his face to the ground, and adored.€€ƒyNAnd Samuel said to Saul: Why hast thou disturbed my rest, that I should be brought up? And Saul said: I am in great distress: for the Philistines fight against me, and God is departed from me, and would not hear me, neither by the hand of prophets, nor by dreams: therefore I have called thee, that thou mayst shew me what I shall do.€€ƒxkAnd Samuel said: Why askest thou me, seeing the Lord has departed from thee, and is gone over to thy rival?€€ƒx†For the Lord will do to thee as he spoke by me, and he will rend thy kingdom out of thy hand, and will give it to thy neighbour David:€€ƒxºBecause thou didst not obey the voice of the Lord, neither didst thou execute the wrath of his indignation upon Amalec. Therefore hath the Lord done to thee what thou sufferest this day.€€ƒxÙAnd the Lord also will deliver Israel with thee into the hands of the Philistines: and to morrow thou and thy sons shall be with me: and the Lord will also deliver the army of Israel into the hands of the Philistines.€€ƒxªAnd forthwith Saul fell all along on the ground; for he was frightened with the words of Samuel, and there was no strength in him, for he had eaten no bread all that day.€€ƒxÓAnd the woman came to Saul, (for he was very much troubled) and said to him: Behold thy handmaid hath obeyed thy voice, and I have put my life in my hand: and I hearkened unto the words which thou spokest to me.€€ƒxÅNow therefore, I pray thee, hearken thou also to the voice of thy handmaid, and let me set before thee a morsel of bread, that thou mayst eat and recover strength, and be able to go on thy journey.€€ƒx­But he refused, and said: I will not eat. But his servants and the woman forced him, and at length hearkening to their voice, he arose from the ground, and sat upon the bed.€€ƒxNow the woman had a fatted calf in the house, and she made haste and killed it: and taking meal, kneaded it, and baked some unleavened bread,€€ƒxqAnd set it before Saul, and before his servants. And when they had eaten they rose up, and walked all that night.€€ƒ`€€ƒx†Now all the troops of the Philistines were gathered together to Aphec: and Israel also encamped by the fountain, which is in Jezrahel.€€ƒx„And the lords of the Philistines marched with their hundreds and their thousands: but David and his men were in the rear with Achis.€€ƒyFAnd the princes of the Philistines said to Achis: What mean these Hebrews? And Achis said to the princes of the Philistines: Do you not know David who was the servant of Saul, the king of Israel, and hath been with me many days, or years, and I have found no fault in him, since the day that he fled over to me until this day?€€ƒyABut the prices of the Philistines were angry with him, and they said to him: Let this man return, and abide in his place, which thou hast appointed him, and let him not go down with us to battle, lest he be an adversary to us, when we shall begin to fight: for how can he otherwise appease his master, but with our heads?€€‹ƒy Then Achis called David, and said to him: As the Lord liveth, thou art upright and good in my sight: and so is thy going out, and thy coming in with me in the army: and I have not found any evil in thee, since the day that thou camest to me unto this day: but thou pleasest not the lords.€€ƒx]Return therefore, and go in peace, and offend not the eyes of the princes of the Philistines.€€ƒxÙAnd David said to Achis: But what have I done, or what hast thou found in me thy servant, from the day that I have been in thy sight until this day, that I may not go and fight against the enemies of my lord the king?€€ƒx¶And Achis answering, said to David: I know that thou art good in my sight, as an angel of God: But the princes of the Philistines have said: He shall not go up with us to the battle.€€ƒx§Therefore arise in the morning, thou, and the servants of thy lord, who came with thee: and when you are up before day, and it shall begin to be light, go on your way.€€ƒxªSo David and his men arose in the night, that they might set forward in the morning, and returned to the land of the Philistines: and the Philistines went up to Jezrahel.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¶Now when David and his men were come to Siceleg on the third day, the Amalecites had made an invasion on the south side upon Siceleg, and had smitten Siceleg, and burnt it with fire,€€ƒx£And had taken the women captives that were in it, both little and great: and they had not killed any person, but had carried them with them, and went on their way.€€ƒx™So when David and his men came to the city, and found it burnt with fire, and that their wives, and their sons, and their daughters, were taken captives,€€ƒxfDavid and the people that were with him, lifted up their voices, and wept till they had no more tears.€€˜ƒx¿And David was greatly afflicted: for the people had a mind to stone him, for the soul of every man was bitterly grieved for his sons and daughters: but David took courage in the Lord his God.€€ƒxAnd he said to Abiathar, the priest, the son of Achimelech: Bring me hither the ephod. And Abiathar brought the ephod to David.€€ƒxÓAnd David consulted the Lord, saying: Shall I pursue after these robbers, and shall I overtake them, or not? And the Lord said to him: Pursue after them: for thou shalt surely overtake them and recover the prey.€€ƒx†So David went, he and the six hundred men that were with him, and they came to the torrent Besor: and some, being weary, stayed there.€€ƒxzBut David pursued, he and four hundred men: for two hundred stayed, who, being weary, could not go over the torrent Besor.€€ƒxvAnd they found an Egyptian in the field, and brought him to David: and they gave him bread to eat, and water to drink,€€ƒxÏAs also a piece of a cake of figs, and two bunches of raisins. And when he had eaten them, his spirit returned, and he was refreshed: for he had not eaten bread, nor drunk water, three days and three nights.€€ƒxéAnd David said to him: To whom dost thou belong; or whence dost thou come? and whither art thou going? He said: I am a young man of Egypt, the servant of an Amalecite: and my master left me, because I began to be sick three days ago.€€ƒxFor we made an invasion on the south side of Cerethi, and upon Juda, and upon the south of Caleb, and we burnt Siceleg with fire.€€ƒxãAnd David said to him: Canst thou bring me to this company? and he said: Swear to me by God, that thou wilt not kill me, nor deliver me into the hands of my master, and I will bring thee to this company. And David swore to him.€€ƒyAnd when he had brought him, behold they were lying spread abroad upon all the ground, eating and drinking, and as it were keeping a festival day, for all the prey and the spoils which they had taken out of the land of the Philistines, and out of the land of Juda.€€ƒx­And David slew them from the evening unto the evening of the next day, and there escaped not a man of them, but four hundred young men, who had gotten upon camels, and fled.€€ƒxSSo David recovered all that the Amalecites had taken, and he rescued his two wives.€€ƒxAnd there was nothing missing small or great, neither of their sons or their daughters, nor of the spoils, and whatsoever they had taken, David recovered all.€€ƒxpAnd he took all the flocks and the herds, and made them go before him: and they said: This is the prey of David.€€ƒyAnd David came to the two hundred men, who, being weary, had stayed, and were not able to follow David, and he had ordered them to abide at the torrent Besor: and they came out to meet David, and the people that were with him. And David coming to the people, saluted them peaceably.€€ƒyThen all the wicked and unjust men, that had gone with David, answering, said: Because they came not with us, we will not give them any thing of the prey which we have recovered: but let every man take his wife, and his children, and be contented with them, and go his way.€€ƒx³But David said: You shall not do so, my brethren, with these things, which the Lord hath given us, who hath kept us, and hath delivered the robbers that invaded us into our hands:€€ƒx²And no man shall hearken to you in this matter. But equal shall be the portion of him that went down to battle, and of him that abode at the baggage, and they shall divide alike.€€ƒxuAnd this hath been done from that day forward, and since was made a statute and an ordinance, and as a law in Israel.€€ƒx¥Then David came to Siceleg, and sent presents of the prey to the ancients of Juda, his neighbours, saying: Receive a blessing of the prey of the enemies of the Lord.€€ƒxcTo them that were in Bethel, and that were in Ramoth to the south, and to them that were in Jether.€€ƒxUAnd to them that were in Aroer, and that were in Sephamoth, and that were in Esthamo,€€ƒxfAnd that were in Rachal, and that were in the cities of Jerameel, and that were in the cities of Ceni,€€ƒxPAnd that were in Arama, and that were in the lake Asan, and that were in Athach,€€ƒxjAnd that were in Hebron, and to the rest that were in those places, in which David had abode with his men.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‡And the Philistines fought against Israel, and the men of Israel fled from before the Philistines, and fell down slain in mount Gelboe.€€ƒx}And the Philistines fell upon Saul, and upon his sons, and they slew Jonathan, and Abinadab, and Melchisua, the sons of Saul.€€ƒx„And the whole weight of the battle was turned upon Saul: and the archers overtook him, and he was grievously wounded by the archers.€€ƒxõThen Saul said to his armourbearer: Draw thy sword, and kill me: lest these uncircumcised come, and slay me, and mock at me. And his armourbearer would not: for he was struck with exceeding great fear. Then Saul took his sword, and fell upon it.€€ƒx_So Saul died, and his three sons, and his armourbearer, and all his men that same day together.€€ƒxàAnd the men of Israel, that were beyond the valley, and beyond the Jordan, seeing that the Israelites were fled, and that Saul was dead, and his sons, forsook their cities, and fled: and the Philistines came and dwelt there.€€ƒxxAnd on the morrow the Philistines came to strip the slain, and they found Saul and his three sons lying in mount Gelboe.€€ƒx¸And they cut off Saul's head, and stripped him of his armour, and sent into the land of the Philistines round about, to publish it in the temples of their idols and among their people.€€ƒxaAnd they put his armour in the temple of Astaroth, but his body they hung on the wall of Bethsan.€€ƒx]Now when the inhabitants of Jabes Galaad had heard all that the Philistines had done to Saul,€€ƒx¿All the most valiant men arose, and walked all the night, and took the body of Saul, and the bodies of his sons, from the wall of Bethsan: and they came to Jabes Galaad, and burnt them there.€€ƒxWAnd they took their bones, and buried them in the wood of Jabes: and fasted seven days.€€ƒxThe Second Book of Samuel€€ƒ`€€ƒx‚Now it came to pass, after Saul was dead, that David returned from the slaughter of the Amalecites, and abode two days in Siceleg.€€ƒx»And on the third day, there appeared a man who came out of Saul's camp, with his garments rent, and dust strewed on his head: and when he came to David, he fell upon his face, and adored.€€ƒxhAnd David said to him: From whence comest thou? And he said to him: I am fled out of the camp of Israel.€€d2Sam¤dnameiII Samueliosis_named2Samlabbreviationd2Samhchapters˜˜ƒxœAnd the young man that told him, said: I came by chance upon mount Gelboe, and Saul leaned upon his spear: and the chariots and horsemen drew nigh unto him,€€ƒxOAnd looking behind him, and seeing me, he called me. And I answered, Here am I.€€ƒxFAnd he said to me: Who art thou? And I said to him: I am an Amalecite.€€ƒxnAnd he said to me: Stand over me, and kill me: for anguish is come upon me, and as yet my whole life is in me.€€ƒxÖSo standing over him, I killed him: for I knew that he could not live after the fall: and I took the diadem that was on his head, and the bracelet that was on his arm, and have brought them hither to thee, my lord.€€ƒx`Then David took hold of his garments and rent them, and likewise all the men that were with him.€€ƒx¼And they mourned, and wept, and fasted until evening for Saul, and for Jonathan his son, and for the people of the Lord, and for the house of Israel, because they were fallen by the sword.€€ƒxrAnd David said to the young man that told him: Whence art thou? He answered: I am the son of a stranger of Amalec.€€ƒx[David said to him: Why didst thou not fear to put out thy hand to kill the Lord's anointed?€€ƒxjAnd David calling one of his servants, said: Go near and fall upon him. And he struck him so that he died.€€ƒxŒAnd David said to him: Thy blood be upon thy own head: for thy own mouth hath spoken against thee, saying: I have slain the Lord's anointed.€€ƒxMAnd David made this kind of lamentation over Saul, and over Jonathan his son.€€ƒxÒ(Also he commanded that they should teach the children of Juda the use of the bow, as it is written in the book of the just.) And he said: Consider, O Israel, for them that are dead, wounded on thy high places.€€ƒxSThe illustrious of Israel are slain upon thy mountains: how are the valiant fallen?€€ƒxTell it not in Geth, publish it not in the streets of Ascalon: lest the daughters of the Philistines rejoice, lest the daughters of the uncircumcised triumph.€€ƒxÚYe mountains of Gelboe, let neither dew, nor rain come upon you, neither be they fields of firstfruits: for there was cast away the shield of the valiant, the shield of Saul as though he had not been anointed with oil.€€ƒxFrom the blood of the slain, from the fat of the valiant, the arrow of Jonathan never turned back, and the sword of Saul did not return empty.€€ƒxSaul and Jonathan, lovely, and comely in their life, even in death they were not divided: they were swifter than eagles, stronger than lions.€€ƒx}Ye daughters of Israel, weep over Saul, who clothed you with scarlet in delights, who gave ornaments of gold for your attire.€€ƒxHHow are the valiant fallen in battle? Jonathan slain in the high places?€€ƒxI grieve for thee, my brother Jonathan: exceeding beautiful, and amiable to me above the love of women. As the mother loveth her only son, so did I love thee.€€ƒx<How are the valiant fallen, and the weapons of war perished?€€ƒ`€€ƒxĞAnd after these things David consulted the Lord, saying: Shall I go up into one of the cities of Juda? And the Lord said to him: Go up. And David said: Whither shall I go up? And he answered him: Into Hebron.€€ƒxhSo David went up, and his two wives Achinoam the Jezrahelitess, and Abigail the wife of Nabal of Carmel:€€ƒxzAnd the men also that were with him, David brought up every man with his household: and they abode in the towns of Hebron.€€ƒxšAnd the men of Juda came, and anointed David there, to be king over the house of Juda. And it was told David that the men of Jabes Galaad had buried Saul.€€˜ ƒxŠAnd now the Lord surely will render you mercy and truth, and I also will requite you for this good turn, because you have done this thing.€€ƒx˜Let your hands be strengthened, and be ye men of valour: for although your master Saul be dead, yet the house of Juda hath anointed me to be their king.€€ƒxtBut Abner the son of Ner, general of Saul's army, took Isboseth the son of Saul, and led him about through the camp,€€ƒx}And made him king over Galaad, and over Gessuri, and over Jezrahel, and over Ephraim, and over Benjamin, and over all Israel.€€ƒx•Isboseth the son of Saul was forty years old when he began to reign over Israel, and he reigned two years; and only the house of Juda followed David.€€ƒxwAnd the number of the days that David abode, reigning in Hebron over the house of Juda, was seven years and six months.€€ƒxiAnd Abner the son of Ner, and the servants of Isboseth the son of Saul, went out from the camp to Gabaon.€€ƒxóAnd Joab the son of Sarvia, and the servants of David went out, and met them by the pool of Gabaon. And when they were come together, they sat down over against one another: the one on the one side of the pool, and the other on the other side.€€ƒxeAnd Abner said to Joab: Let the young men rise, and play before us. And Joab answered: Let them rise.€€ƒxŠThen there arose and went over twelve in number of Benjamin, of the part of Isboseth the son of Saul, and twelve of the servants of David.€€ƒxÇAnd every one catching his fellow by the head, thrust his sword into the side of his adversary, and they fell down together: and the name of the place was called: The field of the valiant, in Gabaon.€€ƒx{And there was a very fierce battle that day: and Abner was put to flight, with the men of Israel, by the servants of David.€€ƒxœAnd there were the three sons of Sarvia there, Joab, and Abisai, and Asael: now Asael was a most swift runner, like one of the roes that abide in the woods.€€ƒxeAnd Asael pursued after Abner, and turned not to the right hand nor to the left from following Abner.€€ƒxMAnd Abner looked behind him, and said: Art thou Asael? And he answered: I am.€€ƒx­And Abner said to him: Go to the right hand or to the left, and lay hold on one of the young men and take thee his spoils. But Asael would not leave off following him close.€€ƒxªAnd again Abner said to Asael: Go off, and do not follow me, lest I be obliged to stab thee to the ground, and I shall not be able to hold up my face to Joab thy brother.€€ƒyBut he refused to hearken to him, and would not turn aside: wherefore Abner struck him with his spear with a back stroke in the groin, and thrust him through, and he died upon the spot: and all that came to the place where Asael fell down and died stood still.€€ƒx¾Now while Joab and Abisai pursued after Abner, the sun went down: and they came as far as the hill of the aqueduct, that lieth over against the valley by the way of the wilderness in Gabaon.€€ƒx‚And the children of Benjamin gathered themselves together to Abner: and being joined in one body, they stood on the top of a hill.€€ƒxêAnd Abner cried out to Joab, and said: Shall thy sword rage unto utter destruction? knowest thou not that it is dangerous to drive people to despair? how long dost thou defer to bid the people cease from pursuing after their brethren?€€ƒx•And Joab said: As the Lord liveth, if thou hadst spoke sooner, even in the morning the people should have retired from pursuing after their brethren.€€ƒx}Then Joab sounded the trumpet, and all the army stood still, and did not pursue after Israel any farther, nor fight any more.€€ƒx•And Abner and his men walked all that night through the plains: and they passed the Jordan, and having gone through all Beth-horon, came to the camp.€€ƒxAnd Joab returning, after he had left Abner, assembled all the people: and there were wanting of David's servants nineteen men, beside Asael.€€ƒx}But the servants of David had killed of Benjamin, and of the men that were with Abner, three hundred and sixty, who all died.€€ƒx¹And they took Asael, and buried him in the sepulchre of his father in Bethlehem and Joab, and the men that were with him, marched all the night, and they came to Hebron at break of day.€€ƒ`€€ƒx«Now there was a long war between the house of Saul and the house of David: David prospering and growing always stronger and stronger, but the house of Saul decaying daily.€€ƒxaAnd sons were born to David in Hebron: and his firstborn was Ammon of Achinoam the Jezrahelitess:€€ƒxAnd his second Cheleab of Abigail the wife of Nabal of Carmel: and the third Absalom the son of Maacha the daughter of Tholmai king of Gessur:€€ƒxVAnd the fourth Adonias, the son of Haggith: and the fifth Saphathia the son of Abital:€€˜'ƒxwNow while there was war between the house of Saul and the house of David, Abner the son of Ner ruled the house of Saul.€€ƒxWAnd Saul had a concubine named Respha, the daughter of Aia. And Isboseth said to Abner:€€ƒy}Why didst thou go in to my father's concubine? And he was exceedingly angry for the words of Isboseth, and said: Am I a dog's head against Juda this day, who have shewn mercy to the house of Saul thy father, and to his brethren and friends, and have not delivered thee into the hands of David, and hast thou sought this day against me to charge me with a matter concerning a woman?€€ƒxZSo do God to Abner, and more also, unless as the Lord hath sworn to David, so I do to him,€€ƒx‰That the kingdom be translated from the house of Saul, and the throne of David be set up over Israel, and over Juda from Dan to Bersabee.€€ƒx:And he could not answer him a word, because he feared him.€€ƒxÆAbner therefore sent messengers to David for himself, saying: Whose is the land? and that they should say: Make a league with me, and my hand shall be with thee: and I will bring all Israel to thee.€€ƒxÎAnd he said: Very well: I will make a league with thee: but one thing I require of thee, saying: Thou shalt not see my face before thou bring Michol the daughter of Saul: and so thou shalt come, and see me.€€ƒx˜And David sent messengers to Isboseth the son of Saul, saying: Restore my wife Michol, whom I espoused to me for a hundred foreskins of the Philistines.€€ƒxKAnd Isboseth sent, and took her from her husband Phaltiel, the son of Lais.€€ƒxoAnd her husband followed her, weeping as far as Bahurim: and Abner said to him: Go and return. And he returned.€€ƒxˆAbner also spoke to the ancients of Israel, saying: Both yesterday and the day before you sought for David that he might reign over you.€€ƒx¸Now then do it: because the Lord hath spoken to David, saying: By the hand of my servant David I will save my people Israel from the hands of the Philistines, and of all their enemies.€€ƒx~And Abner spoke also to Benjamin. And he went to speak to David in Hebron all that seemed good to Israel, and to all Benjamin.€€ƒxqAnd he came to David in Hebron with twenty men: and David made a feast for Abner, and his men that came with him.€€ƒyAnd Abner said to David: I will rise, that I may gather all Israel unto thee my lord the king, and may enter into a league with thee, and that thou mayst reign over all as thy soul desireth. Now when David had brought Abner on his way, and he was gone in peace,€€ƒxËImmediately, David's servants and Joab came, after having slain the robbers, with an exceeding great booty. And Abner was not with David in Hebron, for he had now sent him away, and he was gone in peace.€€ƒx³And Joab and all the army that was with him, came afterwards: and it was told Joab, that Abner the son of Ner came to the king, and he hath sent him away, and he is gone in peace.€€ƒx’And Joab went in to the king, and said: What hast thou done? Behold Abner came to thee: Why didst thou send him away, and he is gone and departed?€€ƒx­Knowest thou not Abner the son of Ner, that to this end he came to thee, that he might deceive thee, and to know thy going out, and thy coming in, and to know all thou dost?€€ƒxˆThen Joab going out from David, sent messengers after Abner, and brought him back from the cistern of Sira, David knowing nothing of it.€€ƒxÖAnd when Abner was returned to Hebron, Joab took him aside to the middle of the gate, to speak to him treacherously: and he stabbed him there in the groin, and he died, in revenge of the blood of Asael his brother.€€ƒx And when David heard of it, after the thing was now done, he said: I, and my kingdom are innocent before the Lord for ever of the blood of Abner the son of Ner:€€ƒxûAnd may it come upon the head of Joab, and upon all his father's house: and let there not fail from the house of Joab one that hath an issue of seed, or that is a leper, or that holdeth the distaff, or that falleth by the sword, or that wanteth bread.€€ƒxmSo Joab and Abisai his brother slew Abner, because he had killed their brother Asael at Gabaon in the battle.€€ƒxËAnd David said to Joab, and to all the people that were with him: Rend your garments, and gird yourselves with sackcloths, and mourn before the funeral of Abner. And king David himself followed the bier.€€ƒx‡And when they had buried Abner in Hebron, king David lifted up his voice, and wept at the grave of Abner: and all the people also wept.€€ƒxfAnd the king mourning and lamenting over Abner, said: Not as cowards are wont to die, hath Abner died.€€ƒx®Thy hands were not bound, nor thy feet laden with fetters: but as men fall before the children of iniquity, so didst thou fall. And all the people repeating it wept over him.€€ƒx¸And when all the people came to take meat with David, while it was yet broad day, David swore, saying: So do God to me, and more also, if I taste bread or any thing else before sunset.€€ƒxvAnd all the people heard, and they were pleased, and all that the king did seemed good in the sight of all the people.€€ƒx}And all the people, and all Israel understood that day that it was not the king's doing, that Abner the son of Ner was slain.€€ƒxnThe king also said to his servants: Do you not know that a prince and a great man is slain this day in Israel?€€ƒx£But I as yet am tender, though anointed king. And these men the sons of Sarvia are too hard for me: the Lord reward him that doth evil according to his wickedness.€€ƒ`€€ƒx|And Isboseth the son of Saul heard that Abner was slain in Hebron: and his hands were weakened, and all Israel was troubled.€€ƒxİNow the son of Saul had two men captains of his bands, the name of the one was Baana, and the name of the other Rechab, the sons of Remmon a Berothite of the children of Benjamin: for Beroth also was reckoned in Benjamin.€€ƒxPAnd the Berothites fled into Gethaim, and were sojourners there until that time.€€ƒyAnd Jonathan the son of Saul had a son that was lame of his feet: for he was five years old when the tidings came of Saul and Jonathan from Jezrahel. And his nurse took him up and fled: and as she made haste to flee, he fell and became lame: and his name was Miphiboseth.€€Œƒx‡And they entered into the house secretly taking ears of corn, and Rechab and Baana his brother stabbed him in the groin, and fled away.€€ƒxÈFor when they came into the house, he was sleeping upon his bed in a parlour, and they struck him and killed him and taking away his head they went off by the way of the wilderness, walking all night.€€ƒxîAnd they brought the head of Isboseth to David to Hebron: and they said to the king: Behold the head of Isboseth the son of Saul thy enemy who sought thy life: and the Lord hath revenged my lord the king this day of Saul, and of his seed.€€ƒx©But David answered Rechab, and Baana his brother, the sons of Remmon the Berothite, and said to them: As the Lord liveth, who hath delivered my soul out of all distress,€€ƒx¦The man that told me, and said: Saul is dead, who thought he brought good tidings, I apprehended, and slew him in Siceleg, who should have been rewarded for his news.€€ƒxªHow much more now when wicked men have slain an innocent man in his own house, upon his bed, shall I not require his blood at your hand, and take you away from the earth?€€ƒxÕAnd David commanded his servants and they slew them: and cutting off their hands and feet, hanged them up over the pool in Hebron: but the head of Isboseth they took and buried in the sepulchre of Abner in Hebron.€€ƒ`€€ƒxdThen all the tribes of Israel came to David in Hebron, saying: Behold we are thy bone and thy flesh.€€ƒxŞMoreover yesterday also and the day before, when Saul was king over us, thou wast he that did lead out and bring in Israel: and the Lord said to thee: Thou shalt feed my people Israel, and thou shalt be prince over Israel.€€ƒx©The ancients also of Israel came to the king of Hebron, and king David made a league with them in Hebron before the Lord: and they anointed David to be king over Israel.€€ƒxNDavid was thirty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned forty years.€€˜ƒxüAnd the king and all the men that were with him went to Jerusalem to the Jebusites the inhabitants of the land: and they said to David: Thou shalt not come in hither unless thou take away the blind and the lame that say: David shall not come in hither.€€ƒxABut David took the castle of Sion, the same is the city of David.€€ƒy$For David had offered that day a reward to whosoever should strike the Jebusites and get up to the gutters of the tops of the houses, and take away the blind and the lame that hated the soul of David: therefore it is said in the proverb: The blind and the lame shall not come into the temple.€€ƒxnAnd David dwelt in the castle, and called it, The city of David: and built round about from Mello and inwards.€€ƒxQAnd he went on prospering and growing up, and the Lord God of hosts was with him.€€ƒxAnd Hiram the king of Tyre sent messengers to David, and cedar trees, and carpenters, and masons for walls: and they built a house for David.€€ƒx|And David knew that the Lord had confirmed him king over Israel, and that he had exalted his kingdom over his people Israel.€€ƒx‘And David took more concubines and wives of Jerusalem, after he was come from Hebron: and there were born to David other sons also and daughters:€€ƒxoAnd these are the names of them, that were born to him in Jerusalem, Samua, and Sobab, and Nathan, and Solomon,€€ƒx$And Jebahar, and Elisua, and Nepheg,€€ƒx4And Japhia, and Elisama, and Elioda, and Eliphaleth.€€ƒxªAnd the Philistines heard that they had anointed David to be king over Israel: and they all came to seek David: and when David heard of it, he went down to a strong hold.€€ƒxFAnd the Philistines coming spread themselves in the valley of Raphaim.€€ƒxÌAnd David consulted the Lord, Saying: Shall I go up to the Philistines? and wilt thou deliver them into my hand? And the Lord said to David: Go up, for I will surely deliver the Philistines into thy hand.€€ƒxÃAnd David came to Baal Pharisim: and defeated them there, and he said, The Lord hath divided my enemies before me, as waters are divided. Therefore the name of the place was called Baal Pharisim.€€ƒxCAnd they left there their idols: which David and his men took away.€€ƒxSAnd the Philistines came up again and spread themselves into the valley of Raphaim.€€ƒxîAnd David consulted the Lord: Shall I go up against the Philistines, and wilt thou deliver them into my hands? He answered: Go not up against them but fetch a compass behind them, and thou shalt come upon them over against the pear trees.€€ƒxÀAnd when thou shalt hear the sound of one going in the tops of the pear trees, then shalt thou join battle: for then will the Lord go out before thy face to strike the army of the Philistines.€€ƒxnAnd David did as the Lord had commanded him, and he smote the Philistines from Gabaa until thou come to Gezer.€€ƒ`€€ƒxPAnd David again gathered together all the chosen men of Israel, thirty thousand.€€ƒxÉAnd David arose and went, with all the people that were with him of the men of Juda to fetch the ark of God, upon which the name of the Lord of Hosts is invoked, who sitteth over it upon the cherubims.€€ƒx¥And they laid the ark of God upon a new cart: and took it out of the house of Abinadab, who was in Gabaa, and Oza and Ahio, the sons of Abinadab, drove the new cart.€€ƒx‚And when they had taken it out of the house of Abinadab, who was in Gabaa, Ahio having care of the ark of God went before the ark.€€—ƒx™And when they came to the floor of Nachon, Oza put forth his hand to the ark of God, and took hold of it: because the oxen kicked and made it lean aside.€€ƒx‡And the indignation of the Lord was enkindled against Oza, and he struck him for his rashness: and he died there before the ark of God.€€ƒxAnd David was grieved because the Lord had struck Oza, and the name of that place was called: The striking of Oza, to this day.€€ƒx\And David was afraid of the Lord that day, saying: How shall the ark of the Lord come to me?€€ƒxAnd he would not have the ark of the Lord brought in to himself into the city of David: but he caused it to be carried into the house of Obededom the Gethite.€€ƒx†And the ark of the Lord abode in the house of Obededom the Gethite three months: and the Lord blessed Obededom, and all his household.€€ƒyAnd it was told king David, that the Lord had blessed Obededom, and all that he had, because of the ark of God. So David went, and brought away the ark of God out of the house of Obededom into the city of David with joy. And there were with David seven choirs, and calves for victims.€€ƒxbAnd when they that carried the ark of the Lord had gone six paces, he sacrificed and ox and a ram:€€ƒx]And David danced with all his might before the Lord: and David was girded with a linen ephod.€€ƒx‚And David and all the louse of Israel brought the ark of the covenant of the Lord with joyful shouting, and with sound of trumpet.€€ƒxÏAnd when the ark of the Lord was come into the city of David, Michol the daughter of Saul, looking out through a window, saw king David leaping and dancing before the Lord: and she despised him in her heart.€€ƒxÀAnd they brought the ark of the Lord, and set it in its place in the midst of the tabernacle, which David had pitched for it: and David offered holocausts, and peace offerings before the Lord.€€ƒxAnd when he had made an end of offering holocausts and peace offerings, he blessed the people in the name of the Lord of hosts.€€ƒxÙAnd he distributed to all the multitude of Israel, both men and women, to every one, a cake of bread, and a piece of roasted beef, and fine flour fried with oil: and all the people departed every one to his own house.€€ƒy	And David returned to bless his own house: and Michol the daughter of Saul coming out to meet David, said: How glorious was the king of Israel to day, uncovering himself before the handmaids of his servants, and was naked, as if one of the buffoons should be naked.€€ƒx«And David said to Michol: Before the Lord, who chose me rather than thy father, and than all his house, and commanded me to be ruler over the people of the Lord in Israel,€€ƒxªI will both play and make myself meaner than I have done: and I will be little in my own eyes: and with the handmaids of whom thou speakest, I shall appear more glorious.€€ƒxKTherefore Michol the daughter of Saul had no child to the day of her death.€€ƒ`€€ƒxwAnd it came to pass when the king sat in his house, and the Lord had given him rest on every side from all his enemies,€€ƒxyHe said to Nathan the prophet: Dost thou see that I dwell in a house of cedar, and the ark of God is lodged within skins?€€ƒx]And Nathan said to the king: Go, do all that is in they heart: because the Lord is with thee.€€ƒxQBut it came to pass that night, that the word of the Lord came to Nathan, saying:€€˜ƒx±Whereas I have not dwelt in a house from the day that I brought the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt even to this day: but have walked in a tabernacle, and in a tent.€€ƒxæIn all the places that I have gone through with all the children of Israel, did ever I speak a word to any one of the tribes of Israel, whom I commanded to feed my people Israel, saying: Why have you not built me a house of cedar?€€ƒx¬And now thus shalt thou speak to my servant David: Thus saith the Lord of hosts: I took thee out of the pastures from following the sheep to be ruler over my people Israel:€€ƒxÊAnd I have been with thee wheresoever thou hast walked, and have slain all thy enemies from before thy face: and I have made thee a great man, like unto the name of the great ones that are on the earth.€€ƒxÖAnd I will appoint a place for my people Israel, and I will plant them, and they shall dwell therein, and shall be disturbed no more: neither shall the children of iniquity afflict them any more as they did before,€€ƒx³From the day that I appointed judges over my people Israel: and I will give thee rest from all thy enemies. And the Lord foretelleth to thee, that the Lord will make thee a house.€€ƒxºAnd when thy days shall be fulfilled, and thou shalt sleep with thy fathers, I will raise up thy seed after thee, which shall proceed out of the bowels, and I will establish his kingdom.€€ƒx\He shall build a house to my name, and I will establish the throne of his kingdom fore ever.€€ƒx«I will be to him a father, and he shall be to me a son: and if he commit any iniquity, I will correct him with the rod of men, and with the stripes of the children of men.€€ƒxgBut my mercy I will not take away from him, as I took it from Saul, whom I removed from before my face.€€ƒxqAnd thy house shall be faithful, and thy kingdom for ever before thy face, and thy throne shall be firm for ever.€€ƒx[According to all these words and according to all this vision so did Nathan speak to David.€€ƒx…And David went in, and sat before the Lord, and said: Who am I, O Lord God, and what is my house, that thou hast brought me thus far?€€ƒx´Bur yet this hath seemed little in thy sight, O Lord God, unless thou didst also speak of the house of thy servant for a long time to come: for this is the law of Adam, O Lord God:€€ƒxPAnd what can David say more unto thee? for thou knowest thy servant, O Lord God:€€ƒxŒFor thy word's sake, and according to thy own heart thou has done all these great things, so that thou wouldst make it known to thy servant.€€ƒx¨Therefore thou art magnified, O Lord God, because there is none like to thee, neither is there any God besides thee, in all the things that we have heard with our ears.€€ƒy/And what nation is there upon earth, as thy people Israel, whom God went to redeem for a people to himself, and to make him a name, and to do for them great and terrible things, upon the earth, before the face of thy people, whom thou redeemedst to thyself out of Egypt, from the nations and their gods.€€ƒx}For thou hast confirmed to thyself thy people Israel to be an everlasting people: and thou, O Lord God, art become their God.€€ƒx“And now, O Lord God, raise up for ever the word that thou hast spoken, concerning thy servant and concerning his house: and do as thou hast spoken,€€ƒx«That thy name may be magnified for ever, and it may be said: The Lord of hosts is God over Israel. And the house of thy servant David shall be established before the Lord.€€ƒxÄBecause thou, O Lord of hosts, God of Israel, hast revealed to the ear of thy servant, saying: I will build thee a house: therefore hath thy servant found in his heart to pray this prayer to thee.€€ƒxvAnd now, O Lord God, thou art God, and thy words shall be true: for thou hast spoken to thy servant these good things.€€ƒxÍAnd now begin, and bless the house of thy servant, that it may endure for ever before thee: because thou, O Lord God, hast spoken it, and with thy blessing let the house of thy servant be blessed for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒx£And it came to pass after this that David defeated the Philistines, and brought them down, and David took the bridle of tribute out of the hand of the Philistines,€€ƒxĞAnd he defeated Moab, and measured them with a line, casting them down to the earth: and he measured with two lines, one to put to death, and one to save alive: and Moab was made to serve David under tribute.€€ƒxyDavid defeated also Adarezer the son of Rohob king of Soba, when he went to extend his dominion over the river Euphrates.€€ƒx³And David took from him a thousand and seven hundred horsemen, and twenty thousand footmen, and houghed all the chariot horses: and only reserved of them for one hundred chariots.€€’ƒxŸAnd David put garrisons in Syria of Damascus: and Syria served David under tribute, and the Lord preserved David in all his enterprises, whithersoever he went.€€ƒxcAnd David took the arms of gold, which the servants of Adarezer wore and brought them to Jerusalem.€€ƒxnAnd out of Bete, and out of Beroth, cities of Adarezer, king David took and exceeding great quantity of brass.€€ƒxTAnd Thou the king of Emath heard that David had defeated all the forces of Adarezer.€€ƒy%And Thou sent Joram his son to king David, to salute him, and to congratulate with him, and to return him thanks: because he had fought against Adarezer, and had defeated him. For Thou was an enemy to Adarezer, and in his hand were vessels of gold, and vessels of silver, and vessels of brass:€€ƒxŒAnd king David dedicated them to the Lord, together with the silver and gold that he had dedicated of all the nations, which he had subdued:€€ƒx•Of Syria, and of Moab, and of the children Ammon, and of the Philistines, and of Amalec, and of the spoils of Adarezer the son of Rohob king of Soba.€€ƒx}David also made himself a name, when he returned after taking Syria in the valley of the saltpits, killing eighteen thousand:€€ƒxœAnd he put guards in Edom, and placed there a garrison: and all Edom was made to serve David: and the Lord preserved David in all enterprises he went about.€€ƒxXAnd David reigned over all Israel: and David did judgment and justice to all his people.€€ƒxWAnd Joab the son Sarvia was over the army: and Josaphat the son of Ahilud was recorder:€€ƒxoAnd Sadoc the son of Achitob, and Achimelech the son of Abiathar, were the priests: and Saraias was the scribe:€€ƒxhAnd Banaias the son of Joiada was over the Cerethi and Phelethi: and the sons of David were the princes.€€ƒ`€€ƒx|And David said: Is there any one, think you, left of the house of Saul, that I may shew kindness to him for Jonathan's sake?€€ƒx©Now there was of the house of Saul, a servant named Siba: and when the king had called him to him, he said to him: Art thou Siba? And he answered: I am Siba thy servant.€€ƒx¾And the king said: Is there any one left of the house of Saul, that I may shew the mercy of God unto Him? And Siba said to the king: There is a son of Jonathan left, who is lame of his feet.€€ƒxrWhere is he? said he. And Siba said to the king: Behold he is in the house of Machir the son of Ammiel in Lodabar.€€ƒx±And when Miphiboseth the son of Jonathan the son of Saul was come to David, he fell on his face and worshipped. And David said: Miphiboseth? And he answered: Behold thy servant.€€ƒxÀAnd David said to him: Fear not, for I will surely shew thee mercy for Jonathan thy father's sake, and I will restore the lands of Saul the father, and thou shalt eat bread at my table always.€€ƒxkHe bowed down to him, and said: Who am I thy servant, that thou shouldst look upon such a dead dog as I am?€€ƒxThen the King called Siba the servant of Saul, and said to him: All that belonged to Saul, and all his house, I have given to thy master's son.€€ƒyThou therefore and the sons and thy servants shall till the land for him: and thou shalt bring in food for thy master's son, that he may be maintained: and Miphiboseth the son of thy master shall always eat bread at my table. And Siba had fifteen sons and twenty servants.€€ƒx©And Siba said to the king: As thou my lord the hast commanded thy servant, so will thy servant do: and Miphiboseth shall eat at my table, as one of the sons of the King.€€ƒxsAnd Miphiboseth had a young son whose name was Micha: and all that kindred of the house of Siba served Miphiboseth.€€ƒxlBut Miphiboseth dwelt in Jerusalem: because he ate always of the king's table: and he was lame of both feet.€€ƒ`€€ƒxtAnd it came to pass after this, that the king of the children of Ammon died, and Hanon his son reigned in his stead.€€ƒxûAnd David said: I will shew kindness to Hanon the son of Daas, as his father shewed kindness to me. So David sent his servants to comfort him for the death of his father. But when the servants of David were come into the land of the children of Ammon,€€ƒxûThe princes of the children of Ammon said to Hanon their lord: Thinkest thou that for the honour of thy father, David hath sent comforters to thee, and hath not David rather sent his servants to thee to search, and spy into the city, and overthrow it?€€ƒx¦Wherefore Hanon took the servants of David, and shaved off the one half of their beards, and cut away half of their garments even to the buttocks, and sent them away.€€“ƒxêAnd the children of Ammon seeing that they had done an injury to David, sent and hired the Syrians of Rohob, and the Syrians of Soba, twenty thousand footmen, and of the king of Maacha a thousand men, and of Istob twelve thousand men.€€ƒxGAnd when David heard this, he sent Joab and the whole army of warriors.€€ƒxÂAnd the children of Ammon came out, and set their men in array at the entering in of the gate: but the Syrians of Soba, and of Rohob, and of Istob, and of Maacha were by themselves in the field.€€ƒx¤Then Joab seeing that the battle was prepared against him, both before and behind, chose of all the choice men of Israel, and put them in array against the Syrians:€€ƒxsAnd the rest of the people he delivered to Abisai his brother, who set them in array against the children of Ammon.€€ƒxšAnd Joab said: If the Syrians are too strong for me, then thou shalt help me, but if the children of Ammon are too strong for thee, then I will help thee.€€ƒxBe of good courage, and let us fight for our people, and for the city of our God: and the Lord will do what is good in his sight.€€ƒxuAnd Joab and the people that were with him, began to fight against the Syrians: and they immediately fled before him.€€ƒxºAnd the children of Ammon seeing that the Syrians were fled, they fled also before Abisai, and entered into the city: and Joab returned from the children of Ammon, and came to Jerusalem.€€ƒxYThen the Syrians seeing that they had fallen before Israel, gathered themselves together.€€ƒx§And Adarezer sent and fetched the Syrians, that were beyond the river, and brought over their army: and Sobach, the captain of the host of Adarezer, was their general.€€ƒx¼And when this was told David, he gathered all Israel together, and passed over the Jordan, and came to Helam: and the Syrians set themselves in array against David, and fought against him.€€ƒx¿And the Syrians fled before Israel, and David slew of the Syrians the men of seven hundred chariots, and forty thousand horsemen: and smote Sobach the captain of the army, who presently died.€€ƒyAnd all the kings that were auxiliaries of Adarezer, seeing themselves overcome by Israel, were afraid and fled away, eight and fifty thousand men before Israel. And they made peace with Israel: and served them, and all the Syrians were afraid to help the children of Ammon any more.€€ƒ`€€ƒxóAnd it came to pass at the return of the year, at the time when kings go forth to war, that David sent Joab and his servants with him, and all Israel, and they spoiled the children of Ammon, and besieged Rabba: but David remained in Jerusalem.€€ƒxçIn the mean time it happened that David arose from his bed after noon, and walked upon the roof of the king's house: And he saw from the roof of his house a woman washing herself, over against him: and the woman was very beautiful.€€ƒx”And the king sent, and inquired who the woman was. And it was told him, that she was Bethsabee the daughter of Eliam, the wife of Urias the Hethite.€€ƒxŒAnd David sent messengers, and took her, and she came in to him, and he slept with her: and presently she was purified from her uncleanness:€€˜ƒxXAnd David sent to Joab, saying: Send me Urias the Hethite. And Joab sent Urias to David.€€ƒxfAnd Urias came to David. And David asked how Joab did, and the people, and how the war was carried on.€€ƒx£And David said to Urias: Go into thy house, and wash thy feet. And Urias went out from the king's house, and there went out after him a mess of meat from the king.€€ƒx}But Urias slept before the gate of the king's house, with the other servants of his lord, and went not down to his own house.€€ƒx­And it was told David by some that said: Urias went not to his house. And David said to Urias: Didst thou not come from thy journey? why didst thou not go down to thy house?€€ƒy0And Urias said to David: The ark of God and Israel and Juda dwell in tents, and my lord Joab and the servants of my lord abide upon the face of the earth: and shall I go into my house, to eat and to drink, and to sleep with my wife? By thy welfare and by the welfare of thy soul I will not do this thing.€€ƒxƒThen David said to Urias: Tarry here to day, and to morrow I will send thee away. Urias tarried in Jerusalem that day and the next.€€ƒxÃAnd David called him to eat and to drink before him, and he made him drunk: and he went out in the evening, and slept on his couch with the servants of his lord, and went not down into his house.€€ƒx^And when the morning was come, David wrote a letter to Joab: and sent it by the hand of Urias,€€ƒxWriting in the letter: Set ye Urias in the front of the battle, where the fight is strongest: and leave ye him, that he may be wounded and die.€€ƒxgWherefore as Joab was besieging the city, he put Urias in the place where he knew the bravest men were.€€ƒx›And the men coming out of the city, fought against Joab, and there fell some of the people of the servants of David, and Urias the Hethite was killed also.€€ƒx@Then Joab sent, and told David all things concerning the battle.€€ƒxbAnd he charged the messenger, saying: When thou hast told all the words of the battle to the king,€€ƒx¢If thou see him to be angry, and he shall say: Why did you approach so near to the wall to fight? knew you not that many darts are thrown from above off the wall?€€ƒxßWho killed Abimelech the son of Jerobaal? did not a woman cast a piece of a millstone upon him from the wall and slew him in Thebes? Why did you go near the wall? Thou shalt say: Thy servant Urias the Hethite is also slain.€€ƒxSSo the messenger departed, and came and told David all that Joab had commanded him.€€ƒx¯And the messenger said to David: The men prevailed against us, and they came out to us into the field: and we vigorously charged and pursued them even to the gate of the city.€€ƒx¥And the archers shot their arrows at thy servants from off the wall above: and some of the king's servants are slain, and thy servant Urias the Hethite is also dead.€€ƒyAnd David said to the messenger: Thus shalt thou say to Joab: Let not this thing discourage thee: for various is the event of war: and sometimes one, sometimes another is consumed by the sword: encourage thy warriors against the city, and exhort them that thou mayest overthrow it.€€ƒxUAnd the wife of Urias heard that Urias her husband was dead, and she mourned for him.€€ƒxºAnd the mourning being over, David sent and brought her into his house, and she became his wife, and she bore him a son: and this thing which David had done, was displeasing to the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‘And the Lord sent Nathan to David: and when he was come to him, he said to him: There were two men in one city, the one rich, and the other poor.€€ƒx/The rich man had exceeding many sheep and oxen.€€ƒyBut the poor man had nothing at all but one little ewe lamb, which he had bought and nourished up, and which had grown up in his house together with his children, eating of his bread, and drinking of his cup, and sleeping in his bosom: and it was unto him as a daughter.€€ƒxèAnd when a certain stranger was come to the rich man, he spared to take of his own sheep and oxen, to make a feast for that stranger, who was come to him, but took the poor man's ewe, and dressed it for the man that was come to him.€€˜ƒxNHe shall restore the ewe fourfold, because he did this thing, and had no pity.€€ƒx And Nathan said to David: Thou art the man. Thus saith the Lord the God of Israel: I anointed thee king over Israel, and I delivered thee from the hand of Saul,€€ƒx¼And gave thee thy master's house and thy master's wives into thy bosom, and gave thee the house of Israel and Juda: and if these things be little, I shall add far greater things unto thee.€€ƒxåWhy therefore hast thou despised the word of the Lord, to do evil in my sight? Thou hast killed Urias the Hethite with the sword, and hast taken his wife to be thy wife, and hast slain him with the sword of the children of Ammon.€€ƒx’Therefore the sword shall never depart from thy house, because thou hast despised me, and hast taken the wife of Urias the Hethite to be thy wife.€€ƒxØThus saith the Lord: Behold, I will raise up evil against thee out of thy own house, and I will take thy wives before thy eyes and give them to thy neighbour, and he shall lie with thy wives in the sight of this sun.€€ƒxmFor thou didst it secretly: but I will do this thing in the sight of all Israel, and in the sight of the sun.€€ƒxAnd David said to Nathan: I have sinned against the Lord. And Nathan said to David: The Lord also hath taken away thy sin: thou shalt not die.€€ƒx™Nevertheless, because thou hast given occasion to the enemies of the Lord to blaspheme, for this thing, the child that is born to thee, shall surely die.€€ƒx‹And Nathan returned to his house. The Lord also struck the child which the wife of Urias had borne to David, and his life was despaired of.€€ƒxnAnd David besought the Lord for the child: and David kept a fast, and going in by himself lay upon the ground.€€ƒxzAnd the ancients of his house came, to make him rise from the ground: but he would not, neither did he eat meat with them.€€ƒy;And it came to pass on the seventh day that the child died: and the servants of David feared to tell him, that the child was dead. For they said: Behold when the child was yet alive, we spoke to him, and he would not hearken to our voice: how much more will he afflict himself if we tell him that the child is dead?€€ƒx But when David saw his servants whispering, he understood that the child was dead: and he said to his servants: Is the child dead? They answered him He is dead.€€ƒxâThen David arose from the ground, and washed and anointed himself: and when he had changed his apparel, he went into the house of the Lord: and worshipped, and then he came into his own house, and he called for bread, and ate.€€ƒxÁAnd his servants said to him: What thing is this that thou hast done? thou didst fast and weep for the child, while it was alive, but when the child was dead, thou didst rise up, and eat bread.€€ƒxŸAnd he said: While the child was yet alive, I fasted and wept for him: for I said: Who knoweth whether the Lord may not give him to me, and the child may live?€€ƒxBut now that he is dead, why should I fast? Shall I be able to bring him back any more? I shall go to him rather: but he shall not return to me.€€ƒxAnd David comforted Bethsabee his wife, and went in unto her, and slept with her: and she bore a son, and he called his name Solomon, and the Lord loved him.€€ƒxtAnd he sent by the hand of Nathan the prophet, and called his name, Amiable to the Lord, because the Lord loved him.€€ƒxaAnd Joab fought against Rabbath of the children of Ammon, and laid close siege to the royal city.€€ƒxvAnd Joab sent messengers to David, saying: I have fought against Rabbath, and the city of waters is about to be taken.€€ƒxªNow therefore gather thou the rest of the people together, and besiege the city and take it: lest when the city shall be wasted by me, the victory be ascribed to my name.€€ƒxjThen David gathered all the people together, and went out against Rabbath: and after fighting, he took it.€€ƒxßAnd he took the crown of their king from his head, the weight of which was a talent of gold, set with most precious stones, and it was put upon David's head, and the spoils of the city which were very great he carried away.€€ƒyAnd bringing forth the people thereof he sawed them, and drove over them chariots armed with iron: and divided them with knives, and made them pass through brickkilns: so did he to all the cities of the children of Ammon: and David returned, with all the army to Jerusalem.€€ƒ`€€ƒx™And it came to pass after this that Ammon the son of David loved the sister of Absalom the son of David, who was very beautiful, and her name was Thamar.€€ƒxŸAnd he was exceedingly fond of her, so that he fell sick for the love of her: for as she was a virgin, he thought it hard to do any thing dishonestly with her.€€ƒx^Now Ammon had a friend, named Jonadab the son of Semmaa the brother of David, a very wise man:€€ƒxĞAnd he said to him: Why dost thou grow so lean from day to day, O son of the king? why dost thou not tell me the reason of it? And Ammon said to him: I am in love with Thamar the sister of my brother Absalom.€€˜'ƒx×So Ammon lay down, and made as if he were sick: and when the king came to visit him, Ammon said to the king: I pray thee let my sister Thamar come, and make in my sight two little messes, that I may eat at her hand.€€ƒxdThen David sent home to Thamar, saying: Come to the house of thy brother Ammon, and make him a mess.€€ƒx¢And Thamar came to the house of Ammon her brother: but he was laid down: and she took meal and tempered it: and dissolving it in his sight she made little messes.€€ƒx³And taking what she had boiled, she poured it out, and set it before him, but he would not eat: and Ammon said: Put out all persons from me. And when they had put all persons out,€€ƒxÁAmmon said to Thamar: Bring the mess into the chamber, that I may eat at thy hand. And Thamar took the little messes which she had made, and brought them in to her brother Ammon in the chamber.€€ƒxdAnd when she had presented him the meat, he took hold of her, and said: Come lie with me, my sister.€€ƒx{She answered him: Do not so, my brother, do not force me: for no such thing must be done in Israel. Do not thou this folly.€€ƒx™For I shall not be able to bear my shame, and thou shalt be as one of the fools in Israel: but rather speak to the king, and he will not deny me to thee.€€ƒx]But he would not hearken to her prayers, but being stronger overpowered her and lay with her.€€ƒxÍThen Ammon hated her with an exceeding great hatred: so that the hatred wherewith he hated her was greater than the love with which he had loved her before. And Ammon said to her: Arise, and get thee gone.€€ƒxShe answered him: The evil which now thou dost against me, in driving me away, is greater than that which thou didst before. And he would not hearken to her:€€ƒxuBut calling the servants that ministered to him, he said: Thrust this woman out from me: and shut the door after her.€€ƒx«And she was clothed with a long robe: for the king's daughters that were virgins, used such kind of garments. Then his servant thrust her out: and shut the door after her.€€ƒxkAnd she put ashes on her head, and rent her long robe and laid her hands upon her head, and went on crying.€€ƒxíAnd Absalom her brother said to her: Hath thy brother Ammon lain with thee? but now, sister, hold thy peace, he is thy brother: and afflict not thy heart for this thing. So Thamar remained pining away in the house of Absalom her brother.€€ƒx«And when king David heard of these things he was exceedingly grieved: and he would not afflict the spirit of his son Ammon, for he loved him, because he was his firstborn.€€ƒxxBut Absalom spoke not to Ammon neither good nor evil: for Absalom hated Ammon because he had ravished his sister Thamar.€€ƒx—And it came to pass after two years, that the sheep of Absalom were shorn in Baalhasor, which is near Ephraim: and Absalom invited all the king's sons:€€ƒxŒAnd he came to the king, and said to him: Behold thy servant's sheep are shorn. Let the king, I pray, with his servants come to his servant.€€ƒx§And the king said to Absalom: Nay, my son, do not ask that we should all come, and be chargeable to thee. And when he pressed him, and he would not go, he blessed him.€€ƒx°And Absalom said: If thou wilt not come, at least let my brother Ammon, I beseech thee, come with us. And the king said to him: It is not necessary that he should go with thee.€€ƒx‹But Absalom pressed him, so that he let Ammon and all the king's sons go with him. And Absalom made a feast as it were the feast of a king.€€ƒxáAnd Absalom had commanded his servants, saying: Take notice when Ammon shall be drunk with wine, and when I shall say to you: Strike him, and kill him, fear not: for it is I that command you: take courage, and be valiant men.€€ƒx“And the servants of Absalom did to Ammon as Absalom had commanded them. And all the king's sons arose and got up every man upon his mule, and fled.€€ƒx‹And while they were yet in the way, a rumour came to David, saying: Absalom hath slain all the king's sons, and there is not one them left.€€ƒxˆThen the king rose up, and rent his garments: and fell upon the ground, and all his servants, that stood about him, rent their garments.€€ƒxóBut Jonadab the son of Semmaa David's brother answering, said: Let not my lord the king think that all the king's sons are slain: Ammon only is dead, for he was appointed by the mouth of Absalom from the day that he ravished his sister Thamar.€€ƒx…Now therefore let not my lord the king take this thing into his heart, saying: All the king's sons are slain: for Ammon only is dead.€€ƒx§But Absalom fled away: and the young man that kept the watch, lifted up his eyes and looked, and behold there came much people by a by-way on the side of the mountain.€€ƒx]And Jonadab said to the king: Behold the king's sons are come: as thy servant said, so it is.€€ƒx®And when he made an end of speaking, the king's sons also appeared: and coming in they lifted up their voice, and wept: and the king also and all his servants wept very much.€€ƒxuBut Absalom fled, and went to Tholomai the son of Ammiud the king of Gessur. And David mourned for his son every day.€€ƒx²And Absalom after he was fled, and come into Gessur, was there three years. And king David ceased to pursue after Absalom, because he was comforted concerning the death of Ammon.€€ƒ`€€ƒ`€€ƒxVAnd Joab the son of Sarvia, understanding that the king's heart was turned to Absalom,€€ƒxğSent to Thecua, and fetched from thence a wise woman: and said to her: Feign thyself to be a mourner, and put on mourning apparel, and be not anointed with oil, that thou mayest be as a woman that had a long time been mourning for one dead.€€ƒxmAnd thou shalt go in to the king, and shalt speak to him in this manner. And Joab put the words in her mouth.€€ƒx…And when the woman of Thecua was come in to the king, she fell before him upon the ground, and worshipped, and said: Save me, O king.€€˜!ƒxAnd thy handmaid had two sons: and they quarrelled with each other in the field, and there was none to part them: and the one struck the other, and slew him.€€ƒy<And behold the whole kindred rising against thy handmaid, saith: Deliver him that hath slain his brother, that we may kill him for the life of his brother, whom he slew, and that we may destroy the heir: and they seek to quench my spark which is left, and will leave my husband no name, nor remainder upon the earth.€€ƒxXAnd the king said to the woman: Go to thy house, and I will give charge concerning thee.€€ƒxšAnd the woman of Thecua said to the king: Upon me, my lord be the iniquity, and upon the house of my father: but may the king and his throne be guiltless.€€ƒxrAnd the king said: If any one shall say ought against thee, bring him to me, and he shall not touch thee any more.€€ƒxèAnd she said: Let the king remember the Lord his God, that the next of kin be not multiplied to take revenge, and that they may not kill my son. And he said: As the Lord liveth, there shall not one hair of thy son fall to the earth.€€ƒxYThe woman said: Let thy hand maid speak one word to my lord the king. And he said: Speak.€€ƒx©And the woman said: Why hast thou thought such a thing against the people of God, and why hath the king spoken this word, to sin, and not bring home again his own exile?€€ƒxÃWe all die, and like waters that return no more, we fall down into the earth: neither will God have a soul to perish, but recalleth, meaning that he that is cast off should not altogether perish.€€ƒx¿Now therefore I am come, to speak this word to my lord the king before the people. And thy handmaid said: I will speak to the king, it maybe the king will perform the request of his handmaid.€€ƒx™And the king hath hearkened to me to deliver his handmaid out of the hand of all that would destroy me and my son together out of the inheritance of God.€€ƒxìThen let thy handmaid say, that the word of the Lord the king be made as a sacrifice. For even as an angel of God, so is my lord the king, that he is neither moved with blessing nor cursing: wherefore the Lord thy God is also with thee.€€ƒxŠAnd the king answering, said to the woman: Hide not from me the thing that I ask thee. And the woman said to him: Speak, my lord the king.€€ƒyWAnd the king said: Is not the hand of Joab with thee in all this? The woman answered, and said: By the health of thy soul, my lord, O king, it is neither on the left hand, nor on the right, in all these things which my lord the king hath spoken: for thy servant Joab, he commanded me, and he put all these words into the mouth of thy handmaid.€€ƒxÉThat I should come about with this form of speech, thy servant Joab commanded this: but thou, my lord, O king, art wise, according to the wisdom of an angel of God, to understand all things upon earth.€€ƒxzAnd the king said to Joab: Behold I am appeased and have granted thy request: Go therefore and fetch back the boy Absalom.€€ƒxğAnd Joab falling down to the ground upon his face, adored, and blessed the king: and Joab said: This day thy servant hath understood, that I have found grace in thy sight, my lord, O king: for thou hast fulfilled the request of thy servant.€€ƒxEThen Joab arose and went to Gessur, and brought Absalom to Jerusalem.€€ƒxBut the king said: Let him return into his house, and let him not see my face. So Absalom returned into his house, and saw not the king's face.€€ƒx©But in all Israel there was not a man so comely, and so exceedingly beautiful as Absalom: from the sole of the foot to the crown of his head there was no blemish in him.€€ƒxºAnd when he polled his hair (now he was polled once a year, because his hair was burdensome to him) he weighed the hair of his head at two hundred sicles, according to the common weight.€€ƒxoAnd there were born to Absalom three sons: and one daughter, whose name was Thamar, and she was very beautiful.€€ƒxFAnd Absalom dwelt two years in Jerusalem, and saw not the king's face.€€ƒx•He sent therefore to Joab, to send him to the king: but he would not come to him. And when he had sent the second time, and he would not come to him,€€ƒy!He said to his servants: You know the field of Joab near my field, that hath a crop of barley: go now and set it on fire. So the servants of Absalom set the corn on fire. And Joab's servants coming with their garments rent, said: The servants of Absalom have set part of the field on fire.€€ƒxgThen Joab arose, and came to Absalom to his house, and said: Why have thy servants set my corn on fire?€€ƒy7And Absalom answered Joab: I sent to thee beseeching thee to come to me, that I might send thee to the king, to say to him: Wherefore am I come from Gessur? it had been better for me to be there: I beseech thee therefore that I may see the face of the king: and if he be mindful of my iniquity, let him kill me.€€ƒx²So Joab going in to the king, told him all: and Absalom was called for, and, he went in to the king: and prostrated himself on the ground before him: and the king kissed Absalom.€€ƒ`€€ƒxdNow after these things Absalom made himself chariots, and horsemen, and fifty men to run before him.€€ƒx÷And Absalom rising up early stood by the entrance of the gate, and when any man had business to come to the king's judgment, Absalom called him to him, and said: Of what city art thou? He answered, and said: Thy servant is of such tribe of Israel.€€ƒx‡And Absalom answered him: Thy words seem to me good and just. But there is no man appointed by the king to hear thee. And Absalom said:€€ƒxzO that they would make me judge over the land, that all that have business might come to me, that I might do them justice.€€˜%ƒx~And this he did to all Israel that came for judgment, to be heard by the king, and he enticed the hearts of the men of Israel.€€ƒxwAnd after forty years, Absalom said to king David: Let me go, and pay my vows which I have vowed to the Lord in Hebron.€€ƒx˜For thy servant made a vow, when he was in Gessur of Syria, saying: If the Lord shall bring me again into Jerusalem, I will offer sacrifice to the Lord.€€ƒxJAnd king David said to him: Go in peace. And he arose, and went to Hebron.€€ƒx•And Absalom sent spies into all the tribes of Israel, saying: As soon as you shall hear the sound of the trumpet, say ye: Absalom reigneth in Hebron.€€ƒx‘Now there went with Absalom two hundred men out of Jerusalem that were called, going with simplicity of heart, and knowing nothing of the design.€€ƒxÓAbsalom also sent for Achitophel the Gilonite, David's counsellor, from his city Gilo. And while he was offering sacrifices, there was a strong conspiracy, and the people running together increased with Absalom.€€ƒxaAnd there came a messenger to David, saying: All Israel with their whole heart followeth Absalom.€€ƒy	And David said to his servants, that were with him in Jerusalem: Arise and let us flee: for we shall not escape else from the face of Absalom: make haste to go out, lest he come and overtake us, and bring ruin upon us, and smite the city with the edge of the sword.€€ƒxxAnd the king's servants said to him: Whatsoever our lord the king shall command, we thy servants will willingly execute.€€ƒxuAnd the king went forth, and all his household on foot: and the king left ten women his concubines to keep the house:€€ƒxOAnd the king going forth and all Israel on foot, stood afar off from the house:€€ƒxÉAnd all his servants walked by him, and the bands of the Cerethi, and the Phelethi, and all the Gethites, valiant warriors, six hundred men who had followed him from Geth on foot, went before the king.€€ƒxœAnd the king said to Ethai the Gethite: Why comest thou with us: return and dwell with the king, for thou art a stranger, and art come out of thy own place.€€ƒxûYesterday thou camest, and to day shalt thou be forced to go forth with us? but I shall go whither I am going: return thou, and take back thy brethren with thee, and the Lord will shew thee mercy, and truth, because thou hast shewn grace and fidelity.€€ƒxÅAnd Ethai answered the king, saying: As the Lord liveth, and as my lord the king liveth: in what place soever thou shalt be, my lord, O king, either in death, or in life, there will thy servant be.€€ƒx‹And David said to Ethai: Come, and pass over. And Ethai the Gethite passed, and all the men that were with him, and the rest of the people.€€ƒx½And they all wept with a loud voice, and all the people passed over: the king also himself went over the brook Cedron, and all the people marched towards the way that looketh to the desert.€€ƒxáAnd Sadoc the priest also came, and all the Levites with him carrying the ark of the covenant of God, and they set down the ark of God: and Abiathar went up, till all the people that was come out of the city had done passing.€€ƒx¸And the king said to Sadoc: Carry back the ark of God into the city: if I shall find grace in the sight of the Lord, he will bring me again, and he will shew me it, and his tabernacle.€€ƒxfBut if he shall say to me: Thou pleasest me not: I am ready, let him do that which is good before him.€€ƒx¥And the king said to Sadoc the priest: O seer, return into the city in peace: and let Achimaas thy son, and Jonathan the son of Abiathar, your two sons, be with you.€€ƒxcBehold I will lie hid in the plains of the wilderness, till there come word from you to certify me.€€ƒxYSo Sadoc and Abiathar carried back the ark of God into Jerusalem: and they tarried there.€€ƒxÅBut David went up by the ascent of mount Olivet, going up and weeping, walking barefoot, and with his head covered, and all the people that were with them, went up with their heads covered weeping.€€ƒxœAnd it was told David that Achitophel also was in the conspiracy with Absalom, and David said: Infatuate, O Lord, I beseech thee, the counsel of Achitophel.€€ƒx½And when David was come to the top of the mountain, where he was about to adore the Lord, behold Chusai the Arachite, came to meet him with his garment rent and his head covered with earth.€€ƒxIAnd David said to him: If thou come with me, thou wilt be a burden to me:€€ƒxÀBut if thou return into the city, and wilt say to Absalom: I am thy servant, O king: as I have been thy father's servant, so I will be thy servant: thou shalt defeat the counsel of Achitophel.€€ƒxˆAnd thou hast with thee Sadoc, and soever thou shalt hear out of the king's house, thou shalt tell it to Sadoc and Abiathar the priests.€€ƒx¦And there are with them their two sons Achimaas; the son of Sadoc, and Jonathan the son of Abiathar: and you shall send by them to me every thing that you shall hear.€€ƒxTThen Chusai the friend of David went into the city, and Absalom came into Jerusalem.€€ƒ`€€ƒxöAnd when David was a little past the top of the hill, behold Siba the servant of Miphiboseth came to meet him with two asses, laden with two hundred loaves of bread, and a hundred bunches of raisins, a hundred cakes of figs, and a vessel of wine.€€ƒxåAnd the king said to Siba: What mean these things? And Siba answered: The asses are for the king's household to sit on: and the loaves and the figs for thy servants to eat, and the wine to drink if any man be faint in the desert.€€ƒx±And the king said: Where is thy master's son? And Siba answered the king: He remained in Jerusalem, saying: To day, will the house of Israel restore me the kingdom of my father.€€ƒx–And the king said to Siba: I give thee all that belonged to Miphiboseth. And Siba said: I beseech thee let me find grace before thee, my lord, O king.€€—ƒx¤And he threw stones at David, and at all the servants of king David: and all the people, and all the warriors walked on the right, and on the left side of the king.€€ƒxkAnd thus said Semei when he cursed the king: Come out, come out, thou man of blood, and thou man of Belial.€€ƒyThe Lord hath repaid thee for all the blood of the house of Saul: because thou hast usurped the kingdom in his stead, and the Lord hath given the kingdom into the hand of Absalom thy son: and behold thy evils press upon thee, because thou art a man of blood.€€ƒx€And Abisai the son of Sarvia said to the king: Why should this dead dog curse my lord the king? I will go, and cut off his head.€€ƒxÁAnd the king said: What have I to do with you, ye sons of Sarvia? Let him alone and let him curse: for the Lord hath bid him curse David: and who is he that shall dare say, why hath he done so?€€ƒxÔAnd the king said to Abisai, and to all his servants: Behold my son, who came forth from my bowels, seeketh my life: how much more now a son of Jemini? let him alone that he may curse as the Lord hath bidden him.€€ƒxjPerhaps the Lord may look upon my affliction, and the Lord may render me good for the cursing of this day.€€ƒxAnd David and his men with him went by the way. And Semei by the hill's side went over against him, cursing, and casting stones at him, and scattering earth.€€ƒxTAnd the king and all the people with him came weary, and refreshed themselves there.€€ƒxPBut Absalom and all his people came into Jerusalem, and Achitophel was with him.€€ƒx€And when Chusai the Arachite, David's friend, was come to Absalom, he said to him: God save thee, O king, God save thee, O king.€€ƒxbAnd Absalom said to him, Is this thy kindness to thy friend? Why wentest thou not with thy friend?€€ƒxAnd Chusai answered Absalom: Nay: for I will be his, whom the Lord hath chosen, and all this people, and all Israel, and with him will I abide.€€ƒxsBesides this, whom shall I serve? is it not the king's son? as I have served thy father, so will I serve thee also.€€ƒx:And Absalom said to Achitophel: Consult what we are to do.€€ƒxÛAnd Achitophel said to Absalom: Go in to the concubines of thy father, whom he hath left to keep the house: that when all Israel shall hear that thou hast disgraced thy father, their hands may be strengthened with thee.€€ƒxwSo they spread a tent for Absalom on the top of the house, and he went in to his father's concubines before all Israel.€€ƒxÀNow the counsel of Achitophel, which he gave in those days, was as if a man should consult God: so was all the counsel of Achitophel, both when he was with David, and when he was with Absalom.€€ƒ`€€ƒxyAnd Achitophel said to Absalom: I will choose me twelve thousand men, and I will arise and pursue after David this night.€€ƒxµAnd coming upon him (for he is now weary, and weak handed) I will defeat him: and when all the people is put to flight that is with him, I will kill the king who will be left alone.€€ƒx†And I will bring back all the people, as if they were but one man: for thou seekest but one man: and all the people shall be in peace.€€ƒx?And his saying pleased Absalom, and all the ancients of Israel.€€˜ƒx—And when Chusai was come to Absalom, Absalom said to him: Achitophel hath spoken after this manner: shall we do it or not? what counsel dost thou give?€€ƒxYAnd Chusai said to Absalom: The counsel that Achitophel hath given this time is not good.€€ƒyAnd again Chusai said: Thou knowest thy father, and the men that are with him, that they are very valiant, and bitter in their mind, as a bear raging in the wood when her whelps are taken away: and thy father is a warrior, and will not lodge with the people.€€ƒxÔPerhaps he now lieth hid in pits, or in some other place where he liest: and when any one shall fall at the first, every one that heareth it shall say: There is a slaughter among the people that followed Absalom.€€ƒxÁAnd the most valiant man whose heart is as the heart of a lion, shall melt for fear: for all the people of Israel know thy father to be a valiant man, and that all who are with him are valiant.€€ƒx½But this seemeth to me to be good counsel: Let all Israel be gathered to thee, from Dan to Bersabee, as the sand of the sea which cannot be numbered: and thou shalt be in the midst of them.€€ƒxÇAnd we shall come upon him in what place soever he shall be found: and we shall cover him, as the dew falleth upon the ground, and we shall not leave of the men that are with him, not so much as one.€€ƒxÀAnd if he shall enter into any city, all Israel shall cast ropes round about that city, and we will draw it into the river, so that there shall not be found so much as one small stone thereof.€€ƒxõAnd Absalom, and all the men of Israel said: The counsel of Chusai the Arachite is better than the counsel of Achitophel: and by the will of the Lord the profitable counsel of Achitophel was defeated, that the Lord might bring evil upon Absalom.€€ƒx¢And Chusai said to Sadoc and Abiathar the priests: Thus and thus did Achitophel counsel Absalom, and the ancients of Israel: and thus and thus did I counsel them.€€ƒxÊNow therefore send quickly, and tell David, saying: Tarry not this night in the plains of the wilderness, but without delay pass over: lest the king be swallowed up, and all the people that is with him.€€ƒxÌAnd Jonathan and Achimaas stayed by the fountain Rogel: and there went a maid and told them: and they went forward, to carry the message to king David, for they might not be seen, nor enter into the city.€€ƒx­But a certain boy saw them, and told Absalom: but they making haste went into the house of a certain man in Bahurim, who had a well in his court, and they went down into it.€€ƒxƒAnd a woman took, and spread a covering over the mouth of the well, as it were to dry sodden barley and so the thing was not known.€€ƒyAnd when Absalom's servants were come into the house, they said to the woman: Where is Achimaas and Jonathan? and the woman answered them: They passed on in haste, after they had tasted a little water. But they that sought them, when they found them not, returned into Jerusalem.€€ƒxÃAnd when they were gone, they came up out of the well, and going on told king David, and said: Arise, and pass quickly over the river: for this manner of counsel has Achitophel given against you.€€ƒx«So David arose, and all the people that were with him, and they passed over the Jordan, until it grew light, and not one of them was left that was not gone ever the river.€€ƒxŞBut Achitophel seeing that his counsel was not followed, saddled his ass, and arose and went home to his house and to his city, and putting his house in order, hanged himself, and was buried in the sepulchre of his father.€€ƒxfBut David came to the camp, and Absalom passed over the Jordan, he and all the men of Israel with him.€€ƒxÚNow Absalom appointed Amasa in Joab's stead over the army: and Amasa was the son of a man who was called Jethra, of Jezrael, who went in to Abigail the daughter of Naas, the sister of Sarvia who was the mother of Joab.€€ƒx5And Israel camped with Absalom in the land of Galaad.€€ƒx¯And when David was come to the camp, Sobi the son of Naas of Rabbath of the children of Ammon, and Machir the son of Ammihel of Lodabar and Berzellai the Galaadite of Rogelim,€€ƒxBrought him beds, and tapestry, and earthen vessels, and wheat, and barley, and meal, and parched corn, and beans, and lentils, and fried pulse,€€ƒxËAnd honey, and butter, and sheep, and fat calves, and they gave to David and the people that were with him, to eat: for they suspected that the people were faint with hunger and thirst in the wilderness.€€ƒ`€€ƒxaAnd David, having reviewed his people, appointed over them captains of thousands and of hundreds,€€ƒyAnd sent forth a third part of the people under the hand of Joab, and a third part under the hand of Abisai the son of Sarvia Joab's brother, and a third part under the hand of Ethai, who was of Geth: and the king said to the people: I also will go forth with you.€€ƒyAnd the people answered: Thou shalt not go forth: for if we flee away, they will not much mind us: or if half of us should fall, they will not greatly care: for thou alone art accounted for ten thousand: it is better therefore that thou shouldst be in the city to succour us.€€ƒx±And the king said to them: What seemeth good to you, that will I do. And the king stood by the gate: and all the people went forth by their troops, by hundreds and by thousands.€€˜!ƒxiSo the people went out into the field against Israel, and the battle was fought in the forest of Ephraim.€€ƒx}And the people of Israel were defeated there by David's army, and a great slaughter was made that day of twenty thousand men.€€ƒx¬And the battle there was scattered over the face of all the country, and there were many more of the people whom the forest consumed, than whom the sword devoured that day.€€ƒxïAnd it happened that Absalom met the servants of David, riding on a mule: and as the mule went under a thick and large oak, his head stuck in the oak: and while he hung between the heaven and the earth, the mule on which he rode passed on.€€ƒxJAnd one saw this and told Joab, saying: I saw Absalom hanging upon an oak.€€ƒx¤And Joab said to the man that told him: If thou sawest him, why didst thou not stab him to the ground, and I would have given thee ten sicles of silver, and a belt?€€ƒxëAnd he said to Joab: If thou wouldst have paid down in my hands a thousand pieces of silver, I would not lay my hands upon the king's son for in our hearing the king charged thee, and Abisai, and Ethai, saying: Save me the boy Absalom.€€ƒx‰Yea and if I should have acted boldly against my own life, this could not have been hid from the king, and wouldst thou have stood by me?€€ƒxÎAnd Joab said: Not as thou wilt, but I will set upon him in thy sight. So he took three lances in his hand, and thrust them into the heart of Absalom: and whilst he yet panted for life, sticking on the oak,€€ƒxHTen young men, armourbearers of Joab, ran up, and striking him slew him.€€ƒxˆAnd Joab sounded the trumpet, and kept back the people from pursuing after Israel in their flight, being willing to spare the multitude.€€ƒx©And they took Absalom, and cast him into a great pit in the forest, and they laid an exceeding great heap of stones upon him: but all Israel fled to their own dwellings.€€ƒyNow Absalom had reared up for himself, in his lifetime, a pillar, which is in the king's valley: for he said: I have no son, and this shall be the monument of my name. And he called the pillar by his own name, and it is called the hand of Absalom, to this day.€€ƒxˆAnd Achimaas the son of Sadoc said: I will run and tell the king, that the Lord hath done judgment for him from the hand of his enemies.€€ƒx¯And Joab said to him: Thou shalt not be the messenger this day, but shalt bear tidings another day: this day I will not have thee bear tidings, because the king's son is dead.€€ƒxgAnd Joab said to Chusai: Go, and tell the king what thou hast seen. Chusai bowed down to Joab, and ran.€€ƒx·Then Achimaas the son of Sadoc said to Joab again: Why might not I also run after Chusai? And Joab said to him: Why wilt thou run, my son? thou wilt not be the bearer of good tidings.€€ƒxmHe answered: But what if I run? And he said to him: Run. Then Achimaas running by a nearer way passed Chusai.€€ƒx’And David sat between the two gates: and the watchman that was on the top of the gate upon the wall, lifting up his eyes, saw a man running alone.€€ƒx˜And crying out he told the king: and the king said: If he be alone, there are good tidings in his mouth. And as he was coming apace, and drawing nearer,€€ƒxœThe watchman saw another man running, and crying aloud from above, he said: I see another man running alone. And the king said: He also is a good messenger.€€ƒx¯And the watchman said: The running of the foremost seemeth to me like the running of Achimaas the son of Sadoc. And the king said: He is a good man: and cometh with good news.€€ƒxøAnd Achimaas crying out, said to the king: God save thee, O king. And falling down before the king with his face to the ground, he said: Blessed be the Lord thy God, who hath shut up the men that have lifted up their hands against the lord my king.€€ƒx¢And the king said: Is the young man Absalom safe? And Achimaas said: I saw a great tumult, O king, when thy servant Joab sent me thy servant: I know nothing else.€€ƒx/And the king said to him: Pass, and stand here.€€ƒxÙAnd when he had passed, and stood still, Chusai appeared and coming up he said: I bring good tidings, my lord, the king, for the Lord hath judged for thee this day from the hand of all that have risen up against thee.€€ƒxÂAnd the king said to Chusai: Is the young man Absalom safe? And Chusai answering him, said: Let the enemies of my lord, the king, and all that rise against him unto evil, be as the young man is.€€ƒxéThe king therefore being much moved, went up to the high chamber over the gate, and wept. And as he went he spoke in this manner: My son Absalom, Absalom my son: would to God that I might die for thee, Absalom my son, my son Absalom.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAAnd it was told Joab, that the king wept and mourned for his son:€€ƒxˆAnd the victory that day was turned into mourning unto all the people: for the people heard say that day: The king grieveth for his son.€€ƒx‰And the people shunned the going into the city that day as a people would do that hath turned their backs, and fled away from the battle.€€ƒxiAnd the king covered his head, and cried with a loud voice: O my son Absalom, O Absalom my son, O my son.€€˜+ƒyThou lovest them that hate thee, and thou hatest them that love thee: and thou hast shewn this day that thou carest not for thy nobles, nor for thy servants: and I now plainly perceive that if Absalom had lived, and all we had been slain, then it would have pleased thee.€€ƒy/Now therefore arise, and go out, and speak to the satisfaction of thy servants: for I swear to thee by the Lord, that if thou wilt not go forth, there will not tarry with thee so much as one this night: and that will be worse to thee, than all the evils that have befallen thee from thy youth until now.€€ƒxºThen the king arose and sat in the gate: and it was told to all the people that the king sat in the gate: and all the people came before the king, but Israel fled to their own dwellings.€€ƒxàAnd all the people were at strife in all the tribes of Israel, saying: The king delivered us out of the hand of our enemies, and he saved us out of the hand of the Philistines: and now he is fled out of the land for Absalom.€€ƒxsBut Absalom, whom we anointed over us, is dead in the battle: how long are you silent, and bring not back the king?€€ƒxàAnd king David sent to Sadoc, and Abiathar the priests, saying: Speak to the ancients of Juda, saying: Why are you the last to bring the king back to his house? (For the talk of all Israel was come to the king in his house.)€€ƒx`You are my brethren, you are my bone, and my flesh, why are you the last to bring back the king?€€ƒxªAnd say ye to Amasa: Art not thou my bone, and my flesh? So do God to me and add more, if thou be not the chief captain of the army before me always in the place of Joab.€€ƒxAnd he inclined the heart of all the men of Juda, as it were of one man: and they sent to the king, saying: Return thou, and all thy servants.€€ƒx‹And the king returned and came as far as the Jordan, and all Juda came as far as Galgal to meet the king, and to bring him over the Jordan.€€ƒxyAnd Semei the son of Gera the son of Jemini of Bahurim, made haste and went down with the men of Juda to meet king David,€€ƒxŸWith a thousand men of Benjamin, and Siba the servant of the house of Saul: and his fifteen sons, and twenty servants were with him: and going over the Jordan,€€ƒxÕThey passed the fords before the king, that they might help over the king's household, and do according to his commandment. And Semei the son of Gera falling down before the king, when he was come over the Jordan,€€ƒxÅSaid to him: Impute not to me, my lord, the iniquity, nor remember the injuries of thy servant on the day that thou, my lord, the king, wentest out of Jerusalem, nor lay it up in thy heart, O king.€€ƒx—For I thy servant acknowledge my sin: and therefore I am come this day the first of all the house of Joseph, and am come down to meet my lord the king.€€ƒx…But Abisai the son of Sarvia answering, said: Shall Semei for these words not be put to death, because he cursed the Lord's anointed?€€ƒxÌAnd David said: What have I to do with you, ye sons of Sarvia? why are you a satan this day to me? shall there any man be killed this day in Israel? do not I know that this day I am made king over Israel?€€ƒxFAnd the king said to Semei: Thou shalt not die. And he swore unto him.€€ƒxÙAnd Miphiboseth the son of Saul came down to meet the king, and he had neither washed his feet, nor trimmed his beard: nor washed his garments from the day that the king went out, until the day of his return in peace.€€ƒxfAnd when he met the king at Jerusalem, the king said to him: Why camest thou not with me, Miphiboseth?€€ƒx¹And he answering, said: My lord, O king, my servant despised me: for I thy servant spoke to him to saddle me an ass, that I might get on and go with the king: for I thy servant am lame.€€ƒxMoreover he hath also accused me thy servant to thee, my lord the king: but thou my lord the king art as an angel of God, do what pleaseth thee.€€ƒxêFor all of my father's house were no better than worthy of death before my lord the king; and thou hast set me thy servant among the guests of thy table: what just complaint therefore have I? or what right to cry any more to the king?€€ƒx|Then the king said to him: Why speakest thou any more? what I have said is determined: thou and Siba divide the possessions.€€ƒx}And Miphiboseth answered the king: Yea, let him take all, forasmuch as my lord the king is returned peaceably into his house.€€ƒxŠBerzellai also the Galaadite coming down from Rogelim, brought the king over the Jordan, being ready also to wait on him beyond the river.€€ƒx¹Now Berzellai the Galaadite was of a great age, that is to say, fourscore years old, and he provided the king with sustenance when he abode in the camp: for he was a man exceeding rich.€€ƒx_And the king said to Berzellai: Come with me that thou mayest rest secure with me in Jerusalem.€€ƒx~And Berzellai said to the king: How many are the days of the years of my life, that I should go up with the king to Jerusalem?€€ƒxùI am this day fourscore years old, are my senses quick to discern sweet and bitter? or can meat or drink delight thy servant? or can I hear any more the voice of singing men and singing women? why should thy servant be a burden to my lord, the king?€€ƒx\I thy servant will go on a little way from the Jordan with thee: I need not this recompense.€€ƒxûBut I beseech thee let thy servant return, and die in my own city, and be buried by the sepulchre of my father, and of my mother. But there is thy servant Chamaam, let him go with thee, my lord, the king, and do to him whatsoever seemeth good to thee.€€ƒx¡Then the king said to him: Let Chamaam go over with me, and I will do for him whatsoever shall please thee, and all that thou shalt ask of me, thou shalt obtain.€€ƒxAnd when all the people and the king had passed over the Jordan, the king kissed Berzellai, and blessed him: and he returned to his own place.€€ƒxœSo the king went on to Galgal, and Chamaam with him. Now all the people of Juda had brought the king over, and only half of the people of Israel were there.€€ƒxâTherefore all the men of Israel running together to the king, said to him: Why have our brethren the men of Juda stolen thee away, and have brought the king and his household over the Jordan, and all the men of David with him?€€ƒxÁAnd all the men of Juda answered the men of Israel: Because the king is nearer to me: why art thou angry for this matter? have we eaten any thing of the king's, or have any gifts been given us?€€ƒy7And the men of Israel answered the men of Juda, and said: I have ten parts in the king more than thou, and David belongeth to me more than to thee: why hast thou done me a wrong, and why was it not told me first, that I might bring back my king? And the men of Juda answered more harshly than the men of Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxïAnd there happened to be there a man of Belial, whose name was Seba, the son of Bochri, a man of Jemini: and he sounded the trumpet, and said: We have no part in David, nor inheritance in the son of Isai: return to thy dwellings, O Israel.€€ƒxAnd all Israel departed from David, and followed Seba the son of Bochri: but the men of Juda stuck to their king from the Jordan unto Jerusalem.€€ƒyAnd when the king was come into his house at Jerusalem, he took the ten women his concubines, whom he had left to keep the house, and put them inward, allowing them provisions: and he went not in unto them, but they were shut up unto the day of their death living in widowhood.€€ƒxoAnd the king said to Amasa: Assemble to me all the men of Juda against the third day, and be thou here present.€€˜ƒxÊAnd David said to Abisai: Now will Seba the son of Bochri do us more harm than did Absalom: take thou therefore the servants of thy lord, and pursue after him, lest he find fenced cities, and escape us.€€ƒx˜So Joab's men went out with him, and the Cerethi and the Phelethi: and all the valiant men went out of Jerusalem to pursue after Seba the son of Bochri.€€ƒy And when they were at the great stone which is in Gabaon, Amasa coming met them. And Joab had on a close coat of equal length with his habit, and over it was girded with a sword hanging down to his flank, in a scabbard, made in such manner as to come out with the least motion and strike.€€ƒxqAnd Joab said to Amasa: God save thee, my brother. And he took Amasa by the chin with his right hand to kiss him.€€ƒxöBut Amasa did not take notice of the sword, which Joab had, and he struck him in the side, and shed out his bowels to the ground, and gave him not a second wound, and he died. And Joab, and Abisai his brother pursued after Seba the son of Bochri.€€ƒxœIn the mean time some men of Joab's company stopping at the dead body of Amasa, said: Behold he that would have been in Joab's stead the companion of David.€€ƒyAnd Amasa imbrued with blood, lay in the midst of the way. A certain man saw this that all the people stood still to look upon him, so he removed Amasa out of the highway into the field, and covered him with a garment, that they who passed might, not stop on his account.€€ƒxuAnd when he was removed out of the way, all the people went on following Joab to pursue after Seba the son of Bochri.€€ƒx…Now he had passed through all the tribes of Israel unto Abela and Bethmaacha: and all the chosen men were gathered together unto him.€€ƒxÈAnd they came, and besieged him in Abela, and in Bethmaacha, and they cast up works round the city, and the city was besieged: and all the people that were with Joab, laboured to throw down the walls.€€ƒxtAnd a wise woman cried out from the city: Hear, hear, and say to Joab: Come near hither, and I will speak with thee.€€ƒx«And when he was come near to her, she said to him: Art thou Joab? And he answered: I am. And she spoke thus to him: Hear the words of thy handmaid. He answered: I do hear.€€ƒx€And she again said: A saying was used in the old proverb: They that inquire, let them inquire in Abela: and so they made an end.€€ƒxªAm not I she that answer truth in Israel, and thou seekest to destroy the city, and to overthrow a mother in Israel? Why wilt thou throw down the inheritance of the Lord?€€ƒx`And Joab answering said: God forbid, God forbid that I should, I do not throw down, nor destroy.€€ƒyThe matter is not so, but a man of mount Ephraim, Seba the son of Bochri by name, hath lifted up his hand against king David: deliver him only, and we will depart from the city. And the woman said to Joab: Behold his head shall be thrown to thee from the wall.€€ƒySo she went to all the people, and spoke to them wisely: and they cut off the head of Seba the son of Bochri, and cast it out to Joab. And he sounded the trumpet, and they departed from the city, every one to their home: and Joab returned to Jerusalem to the king.€€ƒxoSo Joab was over all the army of Israel: and Banaias the son of Joiada was over the Cerethites and Phelethites,€€ƒxJBut Aduram over the tributes: and Josaphat the son of Ahilud was recorder.€€ƒx5And Siva was scribe: and Sadoc and Abiathar, priests.€€ƒx,And Ira the Jairite was the priest of David.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÎAnd there was a famine in the days of David for three years successively: and David consulted the oracle of the Lord. And the Lord said: It is for Saul, and his bloody house, because he slow the Gabaonites.€€ƒyThen the king, calling for the Gabaonites, said to them: (Now the Gabaonites were not of the children of Israel, but the remains of the Amorrhites: and the children of Israel had sworn to them, and Saul sought to slay them out of zeal, as it were for the children of Israel and Juda:)€€ƒx˜David therefore said to the Gabaonites: What shall I do for you? and what shall be the atonement or you, that you may bless the inheritance of the Lord?€€ƒxïAnd the Gabaonites said to him: We have no contest about silver and gold, but against Saul and against his house: neither do we desire that any man be slain of Israel. And the king said to them: What will you then that I should do for you?€€–ƒx¬Let seven men of his children be delivered unto us, that we may crucify them to the Lord in Gabaa of Saul, once the chosen of the Lord. And the king said: I will give them.€€ƒxŸAnd the king spared Miphiboseth the son of Jonathan the son of Saul, because of the oath of the Lord, that had been between David and Jonathan the son of Saul.€€ƒxâSo the king took the two sons of Respha the daughter of Aia, whom she bore to Saul, Armoni, and Miphiboseth: and the five sons of Michol the daughter of Saul, whom she bore to Hadriel the son of Berzellai, that was of Molathi:€€ƒxÆAnd gave them into the hands of the Gabaonites: and they crucified them on a hill before the Lord: and these seven died together in the first days of the harvest, when the barley began to be reaped.€€ƒxğAnd Respha the daughter of Aia took haircloth, and spread it under her upon the rock from the beginning of the harvest, till water dropped upon them out of heaven: and suffered neither the birds to tear them by day, nor the beasts by night.€€ƒxXAnd it was told David, what Respha the daughter of Aia, the concubine of Saul, had done.€€ƒxåAnd David went, and took the bones of Saul, and the bones of Jonathan his son from the men of Jabes Galaad, who had stolen them from the street of Bethsan, where the Philistines had hanged them when they had slain Saul in Gelboe.€€ƒxŒAnd he brought from thence the bones of Saul, and the bones of Jonathan his son, and they gathered up the bones of them that were crucified,€€ƒx÷And they buried them with the bones of Saul, and of Jonathan his son in the land of Benjamin, in the side, in the sepulchre of Cis his father: and they did all that the king had commanded, and God shewed mercy again to the land after these things.€€ƒxŸAnd the Philistines made war again against Israel, and David went down, and his servants with him, and fought against the Philistines. And David growing faint,€€ƒx˜Jesbibenob, who was of the race of Arapha, the iron of whose spear weighed three hundred ounces, being girded with a new sword, attempted to kill David.€€ƒxÌAnd Abisai the son of Sarvia rescued him, and striking the Philistine killed him. Then David's men swore unto him saying: Thou shalt go no more out with us to battle, lest thou put out the lamp of Israel.€€ƒx”There was also a second battle in Gob against the Philistines: then Sobochai of Husathi slew Saph of the race of Arapha of the family of the giants.€€ƒxÏAnd there was a third battle in Gob against the Philistines, in which Adeodatus the son of the Forrest an embroiderer of Bethlehem slew Goliath the Gethite, the shaft of whose spear was like a weaver's beam.€€ƒx¼A fourth battle was in Geth: where there was a man of great stature, that had six fingers on each hand, and six toes on each foot, four and twenty in all, and he was of the race of Arapha.€€ƒxVAnd he reproached Israel: and Jonathan the son of Samae the brother of David slew him.€€ƒx`These four were born of Arapha in Geth, and they fell by the hand of David, and of his servants.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŸAnd David spoke to the Lord the words of this canticle, in the day that the Lord delivered him out of the hand of all his enemies, and out of the hand of Saul,€€ƒxBAnd he said: The Lord is my rock, and my strength, and my saviour.€€ƒx§God is my strong one, in him will I trust: my shield, and the horn of my salvation: he lifteth me up, and is my refuge: my saviour, thou wilt deliver me from iniquity.€€ƒxZI will call on the Lord who is worthy to be praised: and I shall be saved from my enemies.€€˜3ƒxAThe cords of hell compassed me: the snares of death prevented me.€€ƒx“In my distress I will call upon the Lord, and I will cry to my God: and he will hear my voice out of his temple, and my cry shall come to his ears.€€ƒxvThe earth shook and trembled, the foundations of the mountains were moved, and shaken, because he was angry with them.€€ƒxcA smoke went up from his nostrils, and a devouring fire out of his mouth: coals were kindled by it.€€ƒxEHe bowed the heavens, and came down: and darkness was under his feet.€€ƒxNAnd he rode upon the cherubims, and flew: and slid upon the wings of the wind.€€ƒx^He made darkness a covering round about him: dropping waters out of the clouds of the heavens.€€ƒx=By the brightness before him, the coals of fire were kindled.€€ƒxQThe Lord shall thunder from heaven: and the most high shall give forth his voice.€€ƒx@He shot arrows and scattered them: lightning, and consumed them.€€ƒxAnd the overflowings of the sea appeared, and the foundations of the world were laid open at the rebuke of the Lord, at the blast of the spirit of his wrath.€€ƒxBHe sent from on high, and took me, and drew me out of many waters.€€ƒxhHe delivered me from my most mighty enemy, and from them that hated me: for they were too strong for me.€€ƒxIHe prevented me in the day of my affliction, and the Lord became my stay.€€ƒxSAnd he brought me forth into a large place, he delivered me, because I pleased him.€€ƒxqThe Lord will reward me according to my justice: and according to the cleanness of my hands he will render to me.€€ƒxUBecause I have kept the ways of the Lord, and have not wickedly departed from my God.€€ƒxSFor all his judgments are in my sight: and his precepts I have not removed from me.€€ƒxHAnd I shall be perfect with him: and shall keep myself from my iniquity.€€ƒx}And the Lord will recompense me according to my justice: and according to the cleanness of my hands in the sight of his eyes.€€ƒxBWith the holy one thou wilt be holy: and with the valiant perfect.€€ƒxPWith the elect thou wilt be elect: and with the perverse thou wilt be perverted.€€ƒxTAnd the poor people thou wilt save: and with thy eyes thou shalt humble the haughty.€€ƒxJFor thou art my lamp O Lord: and thou, O Lord, wilt enlighten my darkness.€€ƒxCFor in thee I will run girded: in my God I will leap over the wall.€€ƒxmGod, his way is immaculate, the word of the Lord is tried by fire: he is the shield of all that trust in him.€€ƒx7Who is God but the Lord: and who is strong but our God?€€ƒx>God who hath girded me with strength, and made my way perfect.€€ƒxJMaking my feet like the feet of harts, and setting me upon my high places.€€ƒxDHe teacheth my hands to war: and maketh my arms like a bow of brass.€€ƒxSThou hast given me the shield of my salvation: and thy mildness hath multiplied me.€€ƒxCThou shalt enlarge my steps under me: and my ankles shall not fail.€€ƒx^I will pursue after my enemies, and crush them: and will not return again till I consume them.€€ƒxiI will consume them and break them in pieces, so that they shall not rise: they shall fall under my feet.€€ƒxbThou hast girded me with strength to battle: thou hast made them that resisted me to bow under me.€€ƒxaMy enemies thou hast made to turn their back to me: them that hated me, and I shall destroy them.€€ƒxYThey shall cry, and there shall be none to save: to the Lord, and he shall not hear them.€€ƒx|I shall beat them as small as the dust of the earth: I shall crush them and spread them abroad like the mire of the streets.€€ƒx–Thou wilt save me from the contradictions of my people: thou wilt keep me to be the head of the Gentiles: the people which I know not, shall serve me,€€ƒxUThe sons of the stranger will resist me, at the hearing of the ear they will obey me.€€ƒxKThe strangers are melted away, and shall be straitened in their distresses.€€ƒx\The Lord liveth, and my God is blessed: and the strong God of my salvation shall be exalted:€€ƒx=God who giveth me revenge, and bringest down people under me,€€ƒx}Who bringest me forth from my enemies, and liftest me up from them that resist me: from the wicked man thou shalt deliver me.€€ƒx\Therefore will I give thanks to thee, O Lord, among the Gentiles, and will sing to thy name.€€ƒxfGiving great salvation to his king, and shewing mercy to David his anointed, and to his seed for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒx°Now these are David's last words. David the son of Isai said: The man to whom it was appointed concerning the Christ of the God of Jacob, the excellent psalmist of Israel said:€€ƒxCThe spirit of the Lord hath spoken by me and his word by my tongue.€€ƒxrThe God of Israel said to me, the strong one of Israel spoke, the ruler of men, the just ruler in the fear of God.€€ƒxAs the light of the morning, when the sun riseth, shineth in the morning without clouds, and as the grass springeth out of the earth by rain.€€˜'ƒxaBut transgressors shall all of them be plucked up as thorns: which are not taken away with hands.€€ƒx‹And if a man will touch them, he must be armed with iron and with the staff of a lance: but they shall be set on fire and burnt to nothing.€€ƒxÔThese are the names of the valiant men of David: Jesbaham sitting in the chair was the wisest chief among the three, he was like the most tender little worm of the wood, who killed eight hundred men at one onset.€€ƒx·After him was Eleazar the son of Dodo the Ahohite, one of the three valiant men that were with David when they defied the Philistines, and they were there gathered together to battle.€€ƒy	And when the men of Israel were gone away, he stood and smote the Philistines till his hand was weary, and grew stiff with the sword: and the Lord wrought a great victory that day: and the people that were fled away, returned to take spoils of them that were slain.€€ƒxÎAnd after him was Semma the son of Age of Arari. And the Philistines were gathered together in a troop: for there was a field full of lentils. And when the people were fled from the face of the Philistines,€€ƒxuHe stood in the midst of the field, and defended it, and defeated the Philistines: and the Lord gave a great victory.€€ƒxÑMoreover also before this the three who were princes among the thirty, went down and came to David in the harvest time into the cave of Odollam: and the camp of the Philistines was in the valley of the giants.€€ƒx\And David was then in a hold: and there was a garrison of the Philistines then in Bethlehem.€€ƒx„And David longed, and said: O that some man would get me a drink of the water out of the cistern, that is in Bethlehem, by the gate.€€ƒx×And the three valiant men broke through the camp of the Philistines, and drew water out of the cistern of Bethlehem, that was by the gate, and brought it to David: but he would not drink, but offered it to the Lord,€€ƒxĞSaying: The Lord be merciful to me, that I may not do this: shall I drink the blood of these men that went, and the peril of their lives? therefore he would not drink. These things did these three mighty men.€€ƒx®Abisai also the brother of Joab, the son of Sarvia, was chief among three: and he lifted up his spear against three hundred whom he slew, and he was renowned among the three,€€ƒxVAnd the noblest of three, and was their chief, but to the three first he attained not.€€ƒxºAnd Banaias the son of Joiada a most valiant man, of great deeds, of Cabseel: he slew the two lions of Moab, and he went down, and slew a lion in the midst of a pit, in the time of snow.€€ƒxÌHe also slew an Egyptian, a man worthy to be a sight, having a spear in his hand: but he went down to him with a rod, and forced the spear out of the hand of the Egyptian, and slew him with his own spear.€€ƒx+These things did Banaias the son of Joiada.€€ƒx°And he was renowned among the three valiant men, who were the most honourable among the thirty: but he attained not to the first three: and David made him of his privy council.€€ƒxWAsael the brother of Joab was one of the thirty, Elehanan the son of Dodo of Bethlehem.€€ƒx!Semma of Harodi, Elica of Harodi,€€ƒx1Heles of Phalti, Hira the son of Acces of Thecua,€€ƒx(Abiezer of Anathoth, Mobonnai of Husati,€€ƒx-Selmon the Ahohite, Maharai the Netophathite,€€ƒxkHeled the son of Baana, also a Netophathite, Ithai the son of Ribai of Gabaath of the children of Benjamin,€€ƒx4Banaia the Pharathonite, Heddai of the torrent Gaas,€€ƒx+Abialbon the Arbathite, Azmaveth of Beromi,€€ƒx1Eliaba of Salaboni. The sons of Jassen, Jonathan,€€ƒx3Semma of Orori, Aliam the son of Sarar the Arorite,€€ƒxYEliphelet the son of Aasbai the son of Machati, Eliam the son of Achitophel the Gelonite,€€ƒx!Hesrai of Carmel, Pharai of Arbi,€€ƒx/Igaal the son of Nathan of Soba, Bonni of Gadi,€€ƒxOSelec of Ammoni, Naharai the Berothite, armourbearer of Joab the son of Sarvia,€€ƒx(Ira the Jethrite, Gareb also a Jethrite;€€ƒx+Urias the Hethite, thirty and seven in all.€€ƒ`€€ƒx€And the anger of the Lord was again kindled against Israel, and stirred up David among them, saying: Go, number Israel and Juda.€€ƒx©And the king said to Joab the general of his army: Go through all the tribes of Israel from Dan to Bersabee, and number ye the people that I may know the number of them.€€ƒxíAnd Joab said to the king: The Lord thy God increase thy people, and make them as many more as they are now, and again multiply them a hundredfold in the sight of my lord the king: but what meaneth my lord the king by this kind of thing?€€ƒxÊBut the king's words prevailed over the words of Joab, and of the captains of the army: and Joab, and the captains of the soldiers went out from the presence of the king, to number the people of Israel.€€˜ƒxˆAnd by Jazer they passed into Galaad, and to the lower land of Hodsi, and they came into the woodlands of Dan. And going about by Sidon,€€ƒx‰They passed near the walls of Tyre, and all the land of the Hevite, and the Chanaanite, and they came to the south of Juda into Bersabee:€€ƒxbAnd having gone through the whole land, after nine months and twenty days, they came to Jerusalem.€€ƒxÈAnd Joab gave up the sum of the number of the people to the king, and there were found of Israel eight hundred thousand valiant men that drew the sword: and of Juda five hundred thousand fighting men.€€ƒxòBut David's heart struck him, after the people were numbered: and David said to the Lord: I have sinned very much in what I have done: but I pray thee, O Lord, to take away the iniquity of thy servant, because I have done exceeding foolishly.€€ƒxoAnd David arose in the morning, and the word of the Lord came to Gad the prophet and the seer of David, saying:€€ƒxGo, and say to David: Thus saith the Lord: I give thee thy choice of three things, choose one of them which thou wilt, that I may do it to thee.€€ƒyRAnd when Gad was come to David, he told him, saying: Either seven years of famine shall come to thee in thy land: or thou shalt flee three months before thy adversaries, and they shall pursue thee: or for three days there shall be a pestilence in thy land. Now therefore deliberate, and see what answer I shall return to him that sent me.€€ƒx¤And David said to Gad: I am in a great strait: but it is better that I should fall into the hands of the Lord (for his mercies are many) than into the hands of men.€€ƒxAnd the Lord sent a pestilence upon Israel, from the morning unto the time appointed, and there died of the people from Dan to Bersabee seventy thousand men.€€ƒyAnd when the angel of the Lord had stretched out his hand over Jerusalem to destroy it, the Lord had pity on the affliction, and said to the angel that slew the people: It is enough: now hold thy hand. And the angel of the Lord was by the thrashingfloor of Areuna the Jebusite.€€ƒyAnd David said to the Lord, when he saw the angel striking the people: It is I; I am he that have sinned, I have done wickedly: these that are the sheep, what have they done? let thy hand, I beseech thee, be turned against me, and against my father's house.€€ƒx}And Gad came to David that day, and said: Go up, and build an altar to the Lord in the thrashingfloor of Areuna the Jebusite.€€ƒxPAnd David went up according to the word of Gad which the Lord had commanded him.€€ƒxHAnd Areuna looked, and saw the king and his servants coming towards him:€€ƒyAnd going out he worshipped the king, bowing with his face to the earth, and said: Wherefore is my lord the king come to his servant? And David said to him: To buy the thrashingfloor of thee, and build an altar to the Lord, that the plague, which rageth among the people, may cease.€€ƒx±And Areuna said to David: Let my lord the king take, and offer, as it seemeth good to him: thou hast here oxen for a holocaust, and the wain, and the yokes of the oxen for wood.€€ƒxrAll these things Areuna as a king gave to the king: and Areuna said to the king: The Lord thy God receive thy vow.€€ƒxÓAnd the king answered him, and said: Nay, but I will buy it of thee, at a price, and I will not offer to the Lord my God holocausts free cost. So David bought the floor, and the oxen, for fifty sicles of silver:€€ƒx¨And David built there an altar to the Lord, and offered holocausts and peace offerings: and the Lord became merciful to the land, and the plague was stayed from Israel.€€ƒwThe First Book of Kings€€ƒ`€€ƒxdNow king David was old, and advanced in years: and when he was covered with clothes he was not warm.€€ƒx¾His servants therefore, said to him: Let us seek for our Lord the king, a young virgin, and let her stand before the king, and cherish him, and sleep in his bosom and warm our lord the king.€€ƒx…So they sought a beautiful young woman, in all the coasts of Israel and they found Abisag, a Sunamitess, and brought her to the king.€€d1Kgs¤dnamegI Kingsiosis_named1Kgslabbreviationd1Kgshchapters–˜5ƒxNeither did his father rebuke him at any time, saying: Why hast thou done this? And he also was very beautiful, the next in birth after Absalom.€€ƒxkAnd he conferred with Joab, the son of Sarvia, and with Abiathar, the priest, who furthered Adonias's side.€€ƒx›But Sadoc, the priest, and Banaias, the son of Joiada, and Nathan, the prophet, and Semei, and Rei, and the strength of David's army, was not with Adonias.€€ƒxÓAnd Adonias having slain rams and calves, and all fat cattle, by the stone of Zoheleth, which was near the fountain Rogel, invited all his brethren, the king's sons, and all the men of Juda, the king's servants:€€ƒxhBut Nathan, the prophet, and Banaias, and all the valiant men, and Solomon, his brother, he invited not.€€ƒx—And Nathan said to Bethsabee, the mother of Solomon: Hast thou not heard that Adonias, the son of Haggith, reigneth, and our lord David knoweth it not?€€ƒxTNow then, come, take my counsel, and save thy life, and the life of thy son Solomon.€€ƒxÚGo, and get thee in to king David, and say to him: Didst not thou, my lord, O king, swear to me, thy handmaid, saying: Solomon, thy son, shall reign after me, and he shall sit on my throne? why then doth Adonias reign?€€ƒxkAnd while thou art yet speaking there with the king, I will come in after thee, and will fill up thy words.€€ƒx|So Bethsabee went in to the king into the chamber. Now the king was very old, and Abisag, the Sunamitess, ministered to him.€€ƒx]Bethsabee bowed herself, and worshipped the king. And the king said to her: What is thy will?€€ƒx¦She answered, and said: My lord, thou didst swear to thy handmaid, by the Lord thy God, saying: Solomon, thy son, shall reign after me, and he shall sit on my throne.€€ƒxTAnd behold, now Adonias reigneth, and thou, my lord the king, knowest nothing of it.€€ƒx¿He hath killed oxen, and all fat cattle, and many rams, and invited all the king's sons, and Abiathar, the priest, and Joab, the general of the army: but Solomon, thy servant, he invited not.€€ƒx˜And now, my lord, O king, the eyes of all Israel are upon thee, that thou shouldst tell them, who shall sit on thy throne, my lord the king, after thee.€€ƒxŒOtherwise it shall come to pass, when my lord the king sleepeth with his fathers, that I, and my son, Solomon, shall be accounted offenders.€€ƒxAAs she was yet speaking with the king, Nathan, the prophet, came.€€ƒx•And they told the king, saying: Nathan, the prophet, is here. And when he was come in before the king, and had worshipped, bowing down to the ground,€€ƒxiNathan said: My lord, O king, hast thou said: Let Adonias reign after me, and let him sit upon my throne?€€ƒxúBecause he is gone down to day, and hath killed oxen, and fatlings, and many rams, and invited all the king's sons, and the captains of the army, and Abiathar the priest: and they are eating and drinking before him, and saying: God save king Adonias:€€ƒxzBut me, thy servant, and Sadoc, the priest, and Banaias, the son of Joiada, and Solomon, thy servant, he hath not invited.€€ƒxIs this word come out from my lord the king, and hast thou not told me, thy servant, who should sit on the throne of my lord the king after him?€€ƒxtAnd king David answered, and said: Call to me Bethsabee. And when she was come in to the king, and stood before him,€€ƒx]The king swore, and said: As the Lord liveth, who hath delivered my soul out of all distress,€€ƒx«Even as I swore to thee, by the Lord, the God of Israel, saying: Solomon thy son, shall reign after me, and he shall sit upon my throne in my stead, so will I do this day.€€ƒxoAnd Bethsabee, bowing with her face to the earth, worshipped the king, saying: May my lord David live for ever.€€ƒx•King David also said: Call me Sadoc, the priest, and Nathan, the prophet, and Banaias, the son of Joiada. And when they were come in before the king,€€ƒxvHe said to them: Take with you the servants of your lord, and set my son Solomon upon my mule: and bring him to Gihon:€€ƒxAnd let Sadoc, the priest, and Nathan, the prophet, anoint him there king over Israel: and you shall sound the trumpet, and shall say: God save king Solomon.€€ƒx°And you shall come up after him, and he shall come, and shall sit upon my throne, and he shall reign in my stead: and I will appoint him to be ruler over Israel, and over Juda.€€ƒxnAnd Banaias, the son of Joiada, answered the king, saying: Amen: so say the Lord, the God of my lord the king.€€ƒx…As the Lord hath been with my lord the king, so be he with Solomon, and make his throne higher than the throne of my lord king David.€€ƒxÄSo Sadoc, the priest, and Nathan, the prophet, went down, and Banaias, the son of Joiada, and the Cerethi, and Phelethi: and they set Solomon upon the mule of king David, and brought him to Gihon.€€ƒx¤And Sadoc, the priest, took a horn of oil out of the tabernacle, and anointed Solomon: and they sounded the trumpet, and all the people said: God save king Solomon.€€ƒx™And all the multitude went up after him, and the people played with pipes, and rejoiced with a great joy, and the earth rang with the noise of their cry.€€ƒx¾And Adonias, and all that were invited by him, heard it, and now the feast was at an end. Joab also, hearing the sound of the trumpet, said: What meaneth this noise of the city in an uproar?€€ƒxWhile he yet spoke, Jonathan, the son of Abiathar, the priest, came: and Adonias said to him: Come in, because thou art a valiant man, and bringest good news.€€ƒx]And Jonathan answered Adonias: Not so: for our lord, king David, hath appointed Solomon king;€€ƒx±And hath sent with him Sadoc, the priest, and Nathan, the prophet, and Banaias, the son of Joiada, and the Cerethi, and the Phelethi, and they have set him upon the king's mule:€€ƒxÁAnd Sadoc, the priest, and Nathan, the prophet, have anointed him king, in Gihon: and they are gone up from thence rejoicing, so that the city rang again: this is the noise that you have heard.€€ƒx9Moreover, Solomon sitteth upon the throne of the kingdom.€€ƒxĞAnd the king's servants going in, have blessed our lord king David, saying: May God make the name of Solomon greater than thy name, and make his throne greater than thy throne. And the king adored in his bed:€€ƒxxAnd he said: Blessed be the Lord, the God of Israel, who hath given this day one to sit on my throne, my eyes seeing it.€€ƒx[Then all the guests of Adonias were afraid, and they all arose, and every man went his way.€€ƒxTAnd Adonias fearing Solomon, arose and went, and took hold of the horn of the altar.€€ƒxÎAnd they told Solomon, saying: Behold Adonias fearing king Solomon, hath taken hold of the horn of the altar, saying: Let king Solomon swear to me this day, that he will not kill his servant with the sword.€€ƒx•And Solomon said: If he be a good man, there shall not so much as one hair of his head fall to the ground: but if evil be found in him, he shall die.€€ƒxThen king Solomon sent, and brought him out from the altar: and going in, he worshipped king Solomon: and Solomon said to him: Go to thy house.€€ƒ`€€ƒx[And the days of David drew nigh that he should die, and he charged his son Solomon, saying:€€ƒxJI am going the way of all flesh: take thou courage and shew thyself a man.€€ƒyAnd keep the charge of the Lord thy God, to walk in his ways, and observe his ceremonies, and his precepts, and judgments, and testimonies, as it is written in the law of Moses: that thou mayst understand all thou dost, and whithersoever thou shalt turn thyself:€€ƒyThat the Lord may confirm his words, which he hath spoken of me, saying: If thy children shall take heed to their ways, and shall walk before me in truth, with all their heart, and with all their soul, there shall not be taken away from thee a man on the throne of Israel.€€˜.ƒx\Do, therefore, according to thy wisdom, and let not his hoary head go down to hell in peace.€€ƒxœBut shew kindness to the sons of Berzellai, the Galaadite, and let them eat at thy table: for they met me when I fled from the face of Absalom, thy brother.€€ƒyThou hast also with thee Semei, the son of Gera, the son of Jemini, of Bahurim, who cursed me with a grievous curse, when I went to the camp: but because he came down to meet me when I passed over the Jordan, and I swore to him by the Lord, saying: I will not kill thee with the sword:€€ƒx›Do not thou hold him guiltless. But thou art a wise man, and knowest what to do with him, and thou shalt bring down his grey hairs with blood to the grave.€€ƒxESo David slept with his fathers, and was buried in the city of David.€€ƒxyAnd the days that David reigned in Israel, were forty years: in Hebron he reigned seven years, in Jerusalem thirty-three.€€ƒxbAnd Solomon sat upon the throne of his father David, and his kingdom was strengthened exceedingly.€€ƒx•And Adonias, the son of Haggith, came to Bethsabee the mother of Solomon. And she said to him: Is thy coming peaceable? He answered: It is peaceable.€€ƒxTAnd he added: I have a word to speak with thee. She said to him: Speak. And he said:€€ƒx»Thou knowest that the kingdom was mine, and all Israel had preferred me to be their king: but the kingdom is transferred, and is become my brother's: for it was appointed him by the Lord.€€ƒx^Now therefore, I ask one petition of thee; turn not away my face. And she said to him: Say on.€€ƒx}And he said I pray thee speak to king Solomon (for he cannot deny thee any thing) to give me Abisag, the Sunamitess, to wife.€€ƒx<And Bethsabee said: Well, I will speak for thee to the king.€€ƒxÜThen Bethsabee came to king Solomon, to speak to him for Adonias: and the king arose to meet her, and bowed to her, and sat down upon his throne: and a throne was set for the king's mother, and she sat on his right hand.€€ƒx¡And she said to him: I desire one small petition of thee; do not put me to confusion. And the king said to her: My mother ask, for I must not turn away thy face.€€ƒxTAnd she said: Let Abisag, the Sunamitess, be given to Adonias, thy brother, to wife.€€ƒxßAnd king Solomon answered, and said to his mother: Why dost thou ask Abisag, the Sunamitess, for Adonias? ask for him also the kingdom; for he is my elder brother, and hath Abiathar, the priest, and Joab, the son of Sarvia.€€ƒx‘Then king Solomon swore by the Lord, saying: So and so may God do to me, and add more, if Adonias hath not spoken this word against his own life.€€ƒx¾And now, as the Lord liveth, who hath established me, and placed me upon the throne of David, my father, and who hath made me a house, as he promised, Adonias shall be put to death this day.€€ƒx[And king Solomon sent by the hand of Banaias, the son of Joiada, who slew him, and he died.€€ƒy+And the king said also to Abiathar, the priest: Go to Anathoth, to thy lands, for indeed thou art worthy of death: but I will not at this time put thee to death, because thou didst carry the ark of the Lord God before David, my father, and hast endured trouble in all the troubles my father endured.€€ƒx¢So Solomon cast out Abiathar from being the priest of the Lord, that the word of the Lord might be fulfilled, which he spoke concerning the house of Heli in Silo.€€ƒxºAnd the news came to Joab, because Joab had turned after Adonias, and had not turned after Solomon: and Joab fled into the tabernacle of the Lord, and took hold on the horn of the altar.€€ƒxªAnd it was told king Solomon, that Joab was fled into the tabernacle of the Lord, and was by the altar: and Solomon sent Banaias, the son of Joiada, saying. Go, kill him.€€ƒxóAnd Banaias came to the tabernacle of the Lord, and said to him: Thus saith the king: Come forth. And he said: I will not come forth, but here I will die. Banaias brought word back to the king, saying: Thus saith Joab, and thus he answered me.€€ƒxºAnd the king said to him: Do as he hath said; and kill him, and bury him, and thou shalt remove the innocent blood which hath been shed by Joab, from me, and from the house of my father:€€ƒy!And the Lord shall return his blood upon his own head; because he murdered two men, just and better than himself: and slew them with the sword, my father, David, not knowing it; Abner, the son of Ner, general of the army of Israel, and Amasa, the son of Jether general of the army of Juda;€€ƒx¹And their blood shall return upon the head of Joab, and upon the head of his seed for ever. But to David and his seed, and his house, and to his throne, be peace for ever from the Lord.€€ƒxtSo Banaias, the son of Joiada, went up, and setting upon him slew him, and he was buried in his house in the desert.€€ƒx„And the king appointed Banaias, the son of Joiada in his room over the army; and Sadoc, the priest, he put in the place of Abiathar.€€ƒx’The king also sent, and called for Semei, and said to him: Build thee a house in Jerusalem, and dwell there: and go not out from thence any where.€€ƒx›For on what day soever thou shalt go out, and shalt pass over the brook Cedron, know that thou shalt be put to death: thy blood shall be upon thy own head.€€ƒxAnd Semei said to the king: The saying is good: as my lord the king hath said, so will thy servant do. And Semei dwelt in Jerusalem many days.€€ƒx´And it came to pass after three years, that the servants of Semei ran away to Achis, the son of Maacha, the king of Geth: and it was told Semei that his servants were gone to Geth.€€ƒxxAnd Semei arose, and saddled his ass, and went to Achis, to Geth, to seek his servants, and he brought them out of Geth.€€ƒxWAnd it was told Solomon, that Semei had gone from Jerusalem to Geth, and was come back.€€ƒyAnd sending he called for him, and said to him: Did I not protest to thee by the Lord, and tell thee before: On what day soever thou shalt go out and walk abroad any where, know that thou shalt die? And thou answeredst me: The word that I have heard is good.€€ƒx\Why then hast thou not kept the oath of the Lord, and the commandment that I laid upon thee?€€ƒx¶And the king said to Semei: Thou knowest all the evil, of which thy heart is conscious, which thou didst to David, my father: the Lord hath returned thy wickedness upon thy own head.€€ƒxiAnd king Solomon shall be blessed, and the throne of David shall be established before the Lord for ever.€€ƒx^So the king commanded Banaias, the son of Joiada: and he went out and struck him; and he died.€€ƒ`€€ƒy$And the kingdom was established in the hand of Solomon, and he made affinity with Pharao, the king of Egypt: for he took his daughter, and brought her into the city of David: until he had made an end of building his own house, and the house of the Lord, and the wall of Jerusalem round about.€€ƒxwBut yet the people sacrificed in the high places: for there was no temple built to the name of the Lord until that day.€€ƒxƒAnd Solomon loved the Lord, walking in the precepts of David, his father; only he sacrificed in the high places, and burnt incense.€€ƒx¤He went therefore to Gabaon, to sacrifice there: for that was the great high place: a thousand victims for holocausts, did Solomon offer upon that altar, in Gabaon.€€˜ƒyAnd Solomon said: Thou hast shewed great mercy to thy servant David, my father, even as he walked before thee in truth, and justice, and an upright heart with thee: and thou hast kept thy great mercy for him, and hast given him a son to sit on his throne, as it is this day.€€ƒxAnd now, O Lord God, thou hast made thy servant king instead of David, my father: and I am but a child, and know not how to go out and come in;€€ƒxŒAnd thy servant is in the midst of the people which thou hast chosen, an immense people, which cannot be numbered nor counted for multitude.€€ƒx»Give therefore to thy servant an understanding heart, to judge thy people, and discern between good and evil. For who shall be able to judge this people, thy people, which is so numerous?€€ƒxKAnd the word was pleasing to the Lord, that Solomon had asked such a thing.€€ƒxËAnd the Lord said to Solomon: Because thou hast asked this thing, and hast not asked for thyself long life nor riches, nor the lives of thy enemies, but hast asked for thyself wisdom to discern judgment;€€ƒxÁBehold I have done for thee according to thy words, and have given thee a wise and understanding heart, in so much that there hath been no one like thee before thee, nor shall arise after thee.€€ƒxªYea, and the things also which thou didst not ask, I have given thee; to wit, riches and glory: so that no one hath been like thee among the kings in all days heretofore.€€ƒx{And if thou wilt walk in my ways, and keep my precepts and my commandments, as thy father walked, I will lengthen thy days.€€ƒxşAnd Solomon awaked, and perceived that it was a dream: and when he was come to Jerusalem, he stood before the ark of the covenant of the Lord, and offered holocausts, and sacrificed victims of peace offerings, and made a great feast for all his servants.€€ƒxOThen there came two women that were harlots, to the king, and stood before him.€€ƒx‹And one of them said: I beseech thee, my lord, I and this woman dwelt in one house, and I was delivered of a child with her in the chamber.€€ƒxAnd the third day after I was delivered, she also was delivered; and we were together, and no other person with us in the house; only we two.€€ƒxLAnd this woman's child died in the night: for in her sleep she overlaid him.€€ƒx«And rising in the dead time of the night, she took my child from my side, while I, thy handmaid, was asleep, and laid it in her bosom: and laid her dead child in my bosom.€€ƒx²And when I arose in the morning, to give my child suck, behold it was dead: but considering him more diligently, when it was clear day, I found that it was not mine which I bore.€€ƒxçAnd the other woman answered: It is not so as thou sayest, but thy child is dead, and mine is alive. On the contrary, she said; Thou liest: for my child liveth, and thy child is dead. And in this manner they strove before the king.€€ƒx’Then said the king: The one saith, My child is alive, and thy child is dead. And the other answereth: Nay; but thy child is dead, and mine liveth.€€ƒx]The king therefore said: Bring me a sword. And when they had brought a sword before the king,€€ƒxYDivide, said he, the living child in two, and give half to the one and half to the other.€€ƒxîBut the woman, whose child was alive, said to the king; (for her bowels were moved upon her child) I beseech thee, my lord, give her the child alive, and do not kill it. But the other said: Let it be neither mine nor thine; but divide it.€€ƒxzThe king answered, and said: Give the living child to this woman, and let it not be killed; for she is the mother thereof.€€ƒxAnd all Israel heard the judgment which the king had judged, and they feared the king, seeing that the wisdom of God was in him to do judgment.€€ƒ`€€ƒx)And king Solomon reigned over all Israel:€€ƒxOAnd these were the princes which he had: Azarias, the son of Sadoc, the priest:€€ƒxVElihoreph, and Ahia, the sons of Sisa, scribes: Josaphat, the son of Ahilud, recorder:€€ƒxLBanaias, the son of Joiada, over the army: and Sadoc, and Abiathar, priests.€€˜"ƒxSAnd Ahisar, governor of the house: and Adoniram, the son of Abda, over the tribute.€€ƒx°And Solomon had twelve governors over all Israel, who provided victuals for the king and for his house hold: for every one provided necessaries, each man his month in the year.€€ƒx4And these are their names: Benhur, in mount Ephraim.€€ƒxTBendecar, in Macces, and in Salebim, and in Bethsames, and in Elon, and in Bethanan.€€ƒx?Benhesed, in Aruboth: his was Socho, and all the land of Epher.€€ƒx`Benabinadab, to whom belonged all Nephath-Dor: he had Tapheth, the daughter of Solomon, to wife.€€ƒx¦Bana, the son of Ahilud, who governed Thanac, and Mageddo, and all Bethsan, which is by Sarthana, beneath Jezrael, from Bethsan unto Abelmehula, over against Jecmaan.€€ƒxÇBengaber, in Ramoth Galaad: he had the town of Jair, the son of Manasses, in Galaad: he was chief in all the country of Argob, which is in Basan, threescore great cities with walls, and brazen bolts.€€ƒx/Ahinadab, the son of Addo, was chief in Manaim.€€ƒxOAchimaas, in Nephthali: he also had Basemath, the daughter of Solomon, to wife.€€ƒx/Baana, the son of Husi, in Aser, and in Baloth.€€ƒx)Josaphat, the son of Pharue, in Issachar.€€ƒx#Semei, the son of Ela, in Benjamin.€€ƒxGaber, the son of Uri, in the land of Galaad, in the land of Sehon, the king of the Amorrhites, and of Og, the king of Basan, over all that were in that land.€€ƒxjJuda and Israel were innumerable, as the sand of the sea in multitude; eating and drinking, and rejoicing.€€ƒx¿And Solomon had under him all the kingdoms, from the river to the land of the Philistines, even to the border of Egypt: and they brought him presents, and served him all the days of his life.€€ƒxoAnd the provision of Solomon, for each day, was thirty measures of fine flour, and threescore measures of meal;€€ƒx€Ten fat oxen, and twenty out of the pastures, and a hundred rams; besides venison of harts, roes, and buffles, and fatted fowls.€€ƒxŸFor he had all the country which was beyond the river, from Thaphsa to Gazan, and all the kings of those countries: and he had peace on every side round about.€€ƒxAnd Juda, and Israel, dwelt without any fear, every one under his vine, and under his fig tree, from Dan to Bersabee, all the days of Solomon.€€ƒx\And Solomon had forty thousand stalls of chariot horses, and twelve thousand for the saddle.€€ƒx’And the foresaid governors of the king fed them; and they furnished the necessaries also for king Solomon's table, with great care, in their time.€€ƒxƒThey brought barley also, and straw for the horses and beasts, to the place where the king was, according as it was appointed them.€€ƒxAnd God gave to Solomon wisdom, and understanding exceeding much, and largeness of heart, as the sand that is on the sea shore.€€ƒxZAnd the wisdom of Solomon surpassed the wisdom of all the Orientals, and of the Egyptians;€€ƒx¤And he was wiser than all men: wiser than Ethan, the Ezrahite, and Heman, and Chalcol, and Dorda, the sons of Mahol, and he was renowned in all nations round about.€€ƒxSSolomon also spoke three thousand parables: and his poems were a thousand and five.€€ƒx½And he treated about trees, from the cedar that is in Libanus, unto the hyssop that cometh out of the wall: and he discoursed of beasts, and of fowls, and of creeping things, and of fishes.€€ƒx{And they came from all nations to hear the wisdom of Solomon, and from all the kings of the earth, who heard of his wisdom.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¨And Hiram, king of Tyre, sent his servants to Solomon: for he heard that they had anointed him king in the room of his father: for Hiram had always been David's friend.€€ƒxSolomon sent to Hiram, saying:€€ƒxÓThou knowest the will of David, my father, and that he could not build a house to the name of the Lord his God, because of the wars that were round about him, until the Lord put them under the soles of his feet.€€ƒxfBut now the Lord my God hath given me rest round about; and there is no adversary nor evil occurrence.€€’ƒy.Give orders, therefore, that thy servants cut me down cedar trees, out of Libanus, and let my servants be with thy servants: and I will give thee the hire of thy servants whatsoever thou wilt ask: for thou knowest how there is not among my people a man that has skill to hew wood like to the Sidonians.€€ƒx¶Now when Hiram had heard the words of Solomon, he rejoiced exceedingly, and said: Blessed be the Lord God this day, who hath given to David a very wise son over this numerous people.€€ƒxAnd Hiram sent to Solomon, saying: I have heard all thou hast desired of me; and I will do all thy desire concerning cedar trees, and fir trees.€€ƒy)My servants shall bring them down from Libanus to the sea: and I will put them together in floats, on the sea, and convey them to the place, which thou shalt signify to me, and will land them there, and thou shalt receive them: and thou shalt allow me necessaries to furnish food for my household.€€ƒxNSo Hiram gave Solomon cedar trees, and fir trees, according to all his desire.€€ƒx§And Solomon allowed Hiram twenty thousand measures of wheat, for provision for his house, and twenty measures of the purest oil: thus gave Solomon to Hiram every year.€€ƒxŒAnd the Lord gave wisdom to Solomon, as he promised him: and there was peace between Hiram and Solomon, and they two made a league together.€€ƒxZAnd king Solomon chose workmen out of all Israel, and the levy was of thirty thousand men.€€ƒx‡And he sent them to Libanus, ten thousand every month, by turns, so that two months they were at home: and Adoniram was over this levy.€€ƒxeAnd Solomon had seventy thousand to carry burdens, and eighty thousand to hew stones in the mountain:€€ƒx“Besides the overseers who were over every work, in number three thousand and three hundred, that ruled over the people, and them that did the work.€€ƒx„And the king commanded that they should bring great stones, costly stones, for the foundation of the temple, and should square them:€€ƒxAnd the masons of Solomon, and the masons of Hiram, hewed them: and the Giblians prepared timber and stones to build the house.€€ƒ`€€ƒyAnd it came to pass in the four hundred and eightieth year after the children of Israel came out of the land of Egypt, in the fourth year of the reign of Solomon over Israel, in the month Zio, (the same is the second month) he began to build a house to the Lord.€€ƒxAnd the house, which king Solomon built to the Lord, was threescore cubits in length, and twenty cubits in breadth, and thirty cubits in height.€€ƒx¼And there was a porch before the temple, of twenty cubits in length, according to the measure of the breadth of the temple: and it was ten cubits in breadth, before the face of the temple.€€ƒx*And he made in the temple oblique windows.€€˜&ƒyThe floor that was underneath was five cubits in breadth, and the middle floor was six cubits in breadth, and the third floor was seven cubits in breadth. And he put beams in the house round about on the outside, that they might not be fastened in the walls of the temple.€€ƒx½And the house, when it was in building, was built of stones, hewed and made ready: so that there was neither hammer nor axe, nor any tool of iron heard in the house when it was in building.€€ƒx›The door, for the middle side, was on the right hand of the house: and by winding stairs they went up to the middle room, and from the middle to the third.€€ƒxUSo he built the house, and finished it: and he covered the house with roofs of cedar.€€ƒxnAnd he built a floor over all the house, five cubits in height, and he covered the house with timber of cedar.€€ƒx)And the word of the Lord came to Solomon,€€ƒxØAs for this house, which thou art building, if thou wilt walk in my statutes, and execute my judgments, and keep all my commandments, walking in them, I will fulfil my word to thee, which I spoke to David thy father.€€ƒxaAnd I will dwell in the midst of the children of Israel, and I will not forsake my people Israel.€€ƒx,So Solomon built the house, and finished it.€€ƒxûAnd he built the walls of the house on the inside, with boards of cedar, from the floor of the house to the top of the walls, and to the roofs, he covered it with boards of cedar on the inside: and he covered the floor of the house with planks of fir.€€ƒx°And he built up twenty cubits with boards of cedar at the hinder part of the temple, from the floor to the top: and made the inner house of the oracle to be the holy of holies.€€ƒxMAnd the temple itself, before the doors of the oracle, was forty cubits long.€€ƒxàAnd all the house was covered within with cedar, having the turnings, and the joints thereof artfully wrought, and carvings projecting out: all was covered with boards of cedar: and no stone could be seen in the wall at all.€€ƒxvAnd he made the oracle in the midst of the house, in the inner part, to set there the ark of the covenant of the Lord.€€ƒxÈNow the oracle was twenty cubits in length, and twenty cubits in breadth, and twenty cubits in height. And he covered it, and overlaid it with most pure gold. And the altar also he covered with cedar.€€ƒxoAnd the house before the oracle he overlaid with most pure gold, and fastened on the plates with nails of gold.€€ƒx|And there was nothing in the temple that was not covered with gold: the whole altar of the oracle he covered also with gold.€€ƒxOAnd he made in the oracle two cherubims of olive tree, of ten cubits in height.€€ƒx¸One wing of the cherub was five cubits, and the other wing of the cherub was five cubits: that is, in all ten cubits, from the extremity of one wing to the extremity of the other wing.€€ƒxhThe second cherub also was ten cubits: and the measure, and the work was the same in both the cherubims:€€ƒxTThat is to say, one cherub was ten cubits high, and in like manner the other cherub.€€ƒyAnd he set the cherubims in the midst of the inner temple: and the cherubims stretched forth their wings, and the wing of the one touched one wall, and the wing of the other cherub touched the other wall: and the other wings in the midst of the temple touched one another.€€ƒx(And he overlaid the cherubims with gold.€€ƒxÚAnd all the walls of the temple round about he carved with divers figures and carvings: and he made in them cherubims and palm trees, and divers representations, as it were standing out, and coming forth from the wall.€€ƒxIAnd the floor of the house he also overlaid with gold within and without.€€ƒxaAnd in the entrance of the oracle, he made little doors of olive tree, and posts of five corners,€€ƒxÿAnd two doors of olive tree: and he carved upon them figures of cherubims, and figures of palm trees, and carvings very much projecting; and he overlaid them with gold: and he covered both the cherubims and the palm trees, and the other things, with gold.€€ƒxIAnd he made in the entrance of the temple posts of olive tree foursquare:€€ƒxiAnd two doors of fir tree, one of each side: and each door was double, and so opened with folding leaves.€€ƒxAnd he carved cherubims, and palm trees, and carved work standing very much out: and he overlaid all with golden plates in square work by rule.€€ƒx_And he built the inner court with three rows of polished stones, and one row of beams of cedar.€€ƒxGIn the fourth year was the house of the Lord founded, in the month Zio:€€ƒxÅAnd in the eleventh year, in the month Bul. (which is the eighth month) the house was finished in all the works thereof, and in all the appurtenances thereof: and he was seven years in building it.€€ƒ`€€ƒxPAnd Solomon built his own house in thirteen years, and brought it to perfection.€€ƒxêHe built also the house of the forest of Libanus; the length of it was a hundred cubits, and the breadth fifty cubits, and the height thirty cubits: and four galleries between pillars of cedar: for he had cut cedar trees into pillars.€€ƒx…And he covered the whole vault with boards of cedar, and it was held up with five and forty pillars. And one row had fifteen pillars,€€ƒxSet one against another,€€˜3ƒx°And he made a porch of pillars of fifty cubits in length, and thirty cubits in breadth: and another porch before the greater porch, and pillars, and chapiters upon the pillars.€€ƒxHe made also the porch of the throne wherein is the seat of judgment; and covered it with cedar wood from the floor to the top.€€ƒxÎAnd in the midst of the porch, was a small house, where he sat in judgment of the like work. He made also a house for the daughter of Pharao (whom Solomon had taken to wife) of the same work, as this porch;€€ƒx®All of costly stones, which were sawed by a certain rule and measure, both within and without: from the foundation to the top of the walls, and without, unto the great court.€€ƒxVAnd the foundations were of costly stones, great stones of ten cubits or eight cubits.€€ƒx^And above there were costly stones of equal measure hewed, and in like manner planks of cedar.€€ƒxÇAnd the great court was made round with three rows of hewed stones, and one row of planks of cedar, which also was observed in the inner court of the house of the Lord, and in the porch of the house.€€ƒx3And king Solomon sent, and brought Hiram from Tyre,€€ƒxñThe son of a widow woman, of the tribe of Nephthali, whose father was a Tyrian, an artificer in brass, and full of wisdom, and understanding, and skill to work all work in brass. And when he was come to king Solomon, he wrought all his work.€€ƒxAnd he cast two pillars in brass, each pillar was eighteen cubits high: and a line of twelve cubits compassed both the pillars.€€ƒxµHe made also two chapiters of molten brass, to be set upon the tops of the pillars: the height of one chapiter was five cubits, and the height of the other chapiter was five cubits:€€ƒxÇAnd a kind of network, and chain work wreathed together with wonderful art. Both the chapiters of the pillars were cast: seven rows of nets were on one chapiter, and seven nets on the other chapiter.€€ƒx²And he made the pillars, and two rows round about each network to cover the chapiters, that were upon the top, with pomegranates: and in like manner did he to the other chapiter.€€ƒxhAnd the chapiters that were upon the top of the pillars, were of lily work, in the porch of four cubits.€€ƒxÚAnd again there were other chapiters on the top of the pillars above, according to the measure of the pillar over against the network: and of pomegranates there were two hundred, in rows round about the other chapiter.€€ƒxßAnd he set up the two pillars in the porch of the temple: and when he had set up the pillar on the right hand, he called the name thereof Jachin: in like manner he set up the second pillar, and called the name thereof Booz.€€ƒx\And upon the tops of the pillars he made lily work: so the work of the pillars was finished.€€ƒx¥He made also a molten sea, of ten cubits, from brim to brim, round all about; the height of it was five cubits, and a line of thirty cubits compassed it round about.€€ƒx‹And a graven work, under the brim of it, compassed it for ten cubits going about the sea: there were two rows cast of chamfered sculptures.€€ƒxéAnd it stood upon twelve oxen, of which three looked towards the north, and three towards the west, and three towards the south, and three towards the east: and the sea was above upon them, and their hinder parts were all hid within.€€ƒx˜And the laver was a hand breadth thick: and the brim thereof was like the brim of a cup, or the leaf of a crisped lily: it contained two thousand bates.€€ƒxxAnd he made ten bases of brass, every base was four cubits in length, and four cubits in breadth, and three cubits high.€€ƒx`And the work itself of the bases, was intergraven: and there were gravings between the joinings.€€ƒx¼And between the little crowns and the ledges, were lions, and oxen, and cherubims; and in the joinings likewise above: and under the lions and oxen, as it were bands of brass hanging down.€€ƒx•And every base had four wheels, and axletrees of brass: and at the four sides were undersetters, under the laver molten, looking one against another.€€ƒyThe mouth also of the laver within, was in the top of the chapiter: and that which appeared without, was of one cubit all round, and together it was one cubit and a half: and in the corners of the pillars were divers engravings: and the spaces between the pillars were square, not round.€€ƒx™And the four wheels, which were at the four corners of the base, were joined one to another under the base: the height of a wheel was a cubit and a half.€€ƒx†And they were such wheels as are used to be made in a chariot: and their axletrees, and spokes, and strakes, and naves, were all cast.€€ƒxuAnd the four undersetters, that were at every corner of each base, were of the base itself, cast and joined together.€€ƒx¬And on the top of the base, there was a round compass of half a cubit, so wrought that the laver might be set thereon, having its gravings, and divers sculptures of itself.€€ƒxÎHe engraved also in those plates, which were of brass, and in the corners, cherubims, and lions, and palm trees, in likeness of a man standing, so that they seemed not to be engraven, but added round about.€€ƒxWAfter this manner, he made ten bases, of one casting and measure, and the like graving.€€ƒx‘He made also ten lavers of brass: one laver contained four bates, and was of four cubits: and upon every base, in all ten, he put as many lavers.€€ƒxªAnd he set the ten bases, five on the right side of the temple, and five on the left: and the sea he put on the right side of the temple, over against the east southward.€€ƒxwAnd Hiram made cauldrons, and shovels, and basins, and finished all the work of king Solomon in the temple of the Lord.€€ƒx«The two pillars and the two cords of the chapiters, upon the chapiters of the pillars: and the two networks, to cover the two cords, that were upon the top of the pillars.€€ƒx¬And four hundred pomegranates for the two networks: two rows of pomegranates for each network, to cover the cords of the chapiters, which were upon the tops of the pillars.€€ƒx3And the ten bases, and the ten lavers on the bases.€€ƒx+And one sea, and twelve oxen under the sea.€€ƒx”And the cauldrons, and the shovels, and the basins. All the vessels that Hiram made for king Solomon, for the house of the Lord, were of fine brass.€€ƒxaIn the plains of the Jordan, did the king cast them in a clay ground, between Socoth and Sartham.€€ƒxiAnd Solomon placed all the vessels: but for its exceeding great multitude the brass could not be weighed.€€ƒx™And Solomon made all the vessels for the house of the Lord: the altar of gold, and the table of gold, upon which the loaves of proposition should be set:€€ƒxÄAnd the golden candlesticks, five on the right hand, and five on the left, over against the oracle, of pure gold: and the flowers like lilies, and the lamps over them of gold: and golden snuffers,€€ƒxÔAnd pots, and fleshhooks, and bowls, and mortars, and censers, of most pure gold: and the hinges for the doors of the inner house of the holy of holies, and for the doors of the house of the temple, were of gold.€€ƒxïAnd Solomon finished all the work that he made in the house of the Lord, and brought in the things that David, his father, had dedicated, the silver and the gold, and the vessels, and laid them up in the treasures of the house of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒyThen all the ancients of Israel, with the princes of the tribes, and the heads of the families of the children of Israel, were assembled to king Solomon, in Jerusalem: that they might carry the ark of the covenant of the Lord, out of the city of David, that is, out of Sion.€€ƒxAnd all Israel assembled themselves to king Solomon, on the festival day, in the month of Ethanim, the same is the seventh month.€€ƒxEAnd all the ancients of Israel came, and the priests took up the ark,€€ƒxµAnd carried the ark of the Lord, and the tabernacle of the covenant, and all the vessels of the sanctuary, that were in the tabernacle: and the priests and the Levites carried them.€€˜Bƒx¨And the priests brought in the ark of the covenant of the Lord into its place, into the oracle of the temple, into the holy of holies, under the wings of the cherubims.€€ƒxxFor the cherubims spread forth their wings over the place of the ark, and covered the ark, and the staves thereof above.€€ƒx°And whereas the staves stood out, the ends of them were seen without, in the sanctuary before the oracle, but were not seen farther out, and there they have been unto this day.€€ƒxÆNow in the ark there was nothing else but the two tables of stone, which Moses put there at Horeb, when the Lord made a covenant with the children of Israel, when they came out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒxpAnd it came to pass, when the priests were come out of the sanctuary, that a cloud filled the house of the Lord,€€ƒx}And the priests could not stand to minister because of the cloud: for the glory of the Lord had filled the house of the Lord.€€ƒx@Then Solomon said: The Lord said that he would dwell in a cloud.€€ƒxUBuilding, I have built a house for thy dwelling, to be thy most firm throne for ever.€€ƒxkAnd the king turned his face, and blessed all the assembly of Israel: for all the assembly of Israel stood.€€ƒx›And Solomon said: Blessed be the Lord the God of Israel, who spoke with his mouth to David, my father, and with his own hands hath accomplished it, saying:€€ƒxÒSince the day that I brought my people Israel, out of Egypt, I chose no city out of all the tribes of Israel, for a house to be built, that my name might be there: but I chose David to be over my people Israel.€€ƒxZAnd David, my father, would have built a house to the name of the Lord, the God of Israel:€€ƒx¦And the Lord said to David, my father: Whereas, thou hast thought in thy heart to build a house to my name, thou hast done well in having this same thing in thy mind.€€ƒx†Nevertheless, thou shalt not build me a house, but thy son, that shall come forth out of thy loins, he shall build a house to my name.€€ƒxÙThe Lord hath performed his word which he spoke. And I stand in the room of David, my father, and sit upon the throne of Israel, as the Lord promised: and have built a house to the name of the Lord, the God of Israel.€€ƒx—And I have set there a place for the ark, wherein is the covenant of the Lord, which he made with our fathers, when they came out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒx‚And Solomon stood before the altar of the Lord, in the sight of the assembly of Israel, and spread forth his hands towards heaven,€€ƒxÇAnd said: Lord God of Israel, there is no God like thee, in heaven above, or on the earth beneath: who keepest covenant and mercy with thy servants, that have walked before thee with all their heart:€€ƒx«Who hast kept with thy servant David, my father, what thou hast promised him: with thy mouth thou didst speak, and with thy hands thou hast performed, as this day proveth.€€ƒy7Now, therefore, O Lord God of Israel, keep with thy servant David, my father, what thou hast spoken to him, saying: There shall not be taken away of thee a man in my sight, to sit on the throne of Israel: yet so that thy children take heed to their way, that they walk before me as thou hast walked in my sight.€€ƒxrAnd now, Lord God of Israel, let thy words be established, which thou hast spoken to thy servant David, my father.€€ƒx®Is it then to be thought that God should indeed dwell upon earth? for if heaven, and the heavens of heavens, cannot contain thee, how much less this house which I have built?€€ƒx¥But have regard to the prayer of thy servant, and to his supplications, O Lord, my God: hear the hymn and the prayer, which thy servant prayeth before thee this day:€€ƒxÑThat thy eyes may be open upon this house, night and day: upon the house of which thou hast said: My name shall be there: that thou mayst hearken to the prayer which thy servant prayeth, in this place to thee:€€ƒxáThat thou mayst hearken to the supplication of thy servant, and of thy people Israel, whatsoever they shall pray for in this place, and hear them in the place of thy dwelling in heaven; and when thou hearest, shew them mercy.€€ƒx›If any man trespass against his neighbour, and have an oath upon him, wherewith he is bound, and come, because of the oath, before thy altar, to thy house,€€ƒx¼Then hear thou in heaven: and do and judge thy servants, condemning the wicked, and bringing his way upon his own head, and justifying the just, and rewarding him according to his justice.€€ƒxÊIf thy people Israel shall fly before their enemies (because they will sin against thee) and doing penance, and confessing to thy name, shall come and pray, and make supplications to thee in this house:€€ƒx‡Then hear thou in heaven, and forgive the sin of thy people Israel, and bring them back to the land which thou gavest to their fathers.€€ƒx×If heaven shall be shut up, and there shall be no rain, because of their sins, and they, praying in this place, shall do penance to thy name, and shall be converted from their sins, by occasion of their afflictions:€€ƒxãThen hear thou them in heaven, and forgive the sins of thy servants, and of thy people Israel: and shew them the good way wherein they should walk, and give rain upon thy land, which thou hast given to thy people in possession.€€ƒx½If a famine arise in the land, or a pestilence, or corrupt air, or blasting, or locust, or mildew; if their enemy afflict them, besieging the gates, whatsoever plague, whatsoever infirmity,€€ƒx¯Whatsoever curse or imprecation shall happen to any man of thy people Israel: when a man shall know the wound of his own heart, and shall spread forth his hands in this house;€€ƒxÖThen hear thou in heaven, in the place of thy dwelling, and forgive, and do so as to give to every one according to his ways, as thou shalt see his heart (for thou only knowest the heart of all the children of men)€€ƒxtThat they may fear thee all the days that they live upon the face of the land, which thou hast given to our fathers.€€ƒxÂMoreover also the stranger, who is not of thy people Israel, when he shall come out of a far country for thy name's sake, (for they shall hear every where of thy great name, and thy mighty hand,€€ƒxOAnd thy stretched out arm) so when he shall come, and shall pray in this place,€€ƒy,Then hear thou in heaven, in the firmament of thy dwelling place, and do all those things, for which that stranger shall call upon thee: that all the people of the earth may learn to fear thy name, as do thy people Israel, and may prove that thy name is called upon on this house, which I have built.€€ƒxßIf thy people go out to war against their enemies, by what way soever thou shalt send them, they shall pray to thee towards the way of the city, which thou hast chosen, and towards the house, which I have built to thy name:€€ƒx^And then hear thou in heaven their prayers, and their supplications, and do judgment for them.€€ƒxÊBut if they sin against thee, (for there is no man who sinneth not) and thou being angry, deliver them up to their enemies, so that they be led away captives into the land of their enemies, far or near;€€ƒxÒThen if they do penance in their heart, in the place of captivity, and being converted, make supplication to thee in their captivity, saying: We have sinned, we have done unjustly, we have committed wickedness:€€ƒy.And return to thee with all their heart, and all their soul, in the land of their enemies, to which they have been led captives: and pray to thee towards the way of their land, which thou gavest to their fathers, and of the city which thou hast chosen, and of the temple which I have built to thy name:€€ƒx{Then hear thou in heaven, in the firmament of thy throne, their prayers, and their supplications, and do judgment for them:€€ƒxãAnd forgive thy people, that have sinned against thee, and all their iniquities, by which they have transgressed against thee: and give them mercy before them that have made them captives, that they may have compassion on them.€€ƒx…For they are thy people, and thy inheritance, whom thou hast brought out of the land of Egypt, from the midst of the furnace of iron.€€ƒx—That thy eyes may be open to the supplication of thy servant, and of thy people Israel, to hear them in all things for which they shall call upon thee.€€ƒxËFor thou hast separated them to thyself for an inheritance, from amongst all the people of the earth, as thou hast spoken by Moses, thy servant, when thou broughtest our fathers out of Egypt, O Lord God.€€ƒxîAnd it came to pass, when Solomon had made an end of praying all this prayer and supplication to the Lord, that he rose from before the altar of the Lord: for he had fixed both knees on the ground, and had spread his hands towards heaven.€€ƒxOAnd he stood, and blessed all the assembly of Israel with a loud voice, saying:€€ƒxÉBlessed be the Lord, who hath given rest to his people Israel, according to all that he promised: there hath not failed so much as one word of all the good things that he promised by his servant Moses.€€ƒx[The Lord our God be with us, as he was with our fathers, and not leave us, nor cast us off:€€ƒx±But may he incline our hearts to himself, that we may walk in all his ways, and keep his commandments, and his ceremonies, and all his judgments, which he commanded our fathers.€€ƒx½And let these my words, wherewith I have prayed before the Lord, be nigh unto the Lord our God day and night, that he may do judgment for his servant, and for his people Israel, day by day:€€ƒxfThat all the people of the earth may know, that the Lord he is God, and there is no other besides him.€€ƒx‚Let our hearts also be perfect with the Lord our God, that we may walk in his statutes, and keep his commandments, as at this day.€€ƒxGAnd the king, and all Israel with him, offered victims before the Lord.€€ƒxâAnd Solomon slew victims of peace offerings, which he sacrificed to the Lord, two and twenty thousand oxen, and a hundred and twenty thousand sheep so the king, and all the children of Israel, dedicated the temple of the Lord.€€ƒyDIn that day the king sanctified the middle of the court, that was before the house of the Lord for there he offered the holocaust, and sacrifice, and the fat of the peace offerings: because the brazen altar that was before the Lord, was too little to receive the holocaust, and sacrifice, and the fat of the peace offerings.€€ƒxÛAnd Solomon made at the same time a solemn feast, and all Israel with him, a great multitude, from the entrance of Emath to the river of Egypt, before the Lord our God, seven days and seven days, that is, fourteen days.€€ƒxèAnd on the eighth day, he sent away the people: and they blessed the king, and went to their dwellings, rejoicing, and glad in heart, for all the good things that the Lord had done for David, his servant, and for Israel, his people.€€ƒ`€€ƒx™And it came to pass when Solomon had finished the building of the house of the Lord, and the king's house, and all that he desired and was pleased to do,€€ƒxSThat the Lord appeared to him the second time, as he had appeared to him in Gabaon.€€ƒxíAnd the Lord said to him: I have heard thy prayer and thy supplication, which thou hast made before me: I have sanctified this house, which thou hast built, to put my name there for ever; and my eyes, and my heart, shall be there always.€€ƒx½And if thou wilt walk before me, as thy father walked, in simplicity of heart, and in uprightness: and wilt do all that I have commanded thee, and wilt keep my ordinances, and my judgments,€€˜ƒxÔBut if you and your children, revolting, shall turn away from following me, and will not keep my commandments, and my ceremonies, which I have set before you, but will go and worship strange gods, and adore them:€€ƒxØI will take away Israel from the face of the land which I have given them; and the temple which I have sanctified to my name, I will cast out of my sight; and Israel shall be a proverb, and a byword among all people.€€ƒx·And this house shall be made an example of: every one that shall pass by it, shall be astonished, and shall hiss, and say: Why hath the Lord done thus to this land, and to this house?€€ƒxíAnd they shall answer: Because they forsook the Lord their God, who brought their fathers out of the land of Egypt, and followed strange gods, and adored them, and worshipped them: therefore hath the Lord brought upon them all this evil.€€ƒx„And when twenty years were ended, after Solomon had built the two houses; that is, the house of the Lord, and the house of the king,€€ƒx¶(Hiram, the king of Tyre, furnishing Solomon with cedar trees, and fir trees, and gold, according to all he had need of) then Solomon gave Hiram twenty cities in the land of Galilee.€€ƒxcAnd Hiram came out of Tyre, to see the towns which Solomon had given him, and they pleased him not;€€ƒxzAnd he said: Are these the cities which thou hast given me, brother? And he called them the land of Chabul, unto this day.€€ƒxDAnd Hiram sent to king Solomon a hundred and twenty talents of gold.€€ƒx·This is the sum of the expenses, which king Solomon offered to build the house of the Lord, and his own house, and Mello, and the wall of Jerusalem, and Heser, and Mageddo, and Gazer.€€ƒx³Pharao, the king of Egypt, came up and took Gazer, and burnt it with fire: and slew the Chanaanite that dwelt in the city, and gave it for a dowry to his daughter, Solomon's wife.€€ƒx1So Solomon built Gazer, and Bethhoron the nether,€€ƒx8And Baalath, and Palmira, in the land of the wilderness.€€ƒxøAnd all the towns that belonged to himself, and were not walled, he fortified; the cities also of the chariots, and the cities of the horsemen, and whatsoever he had a mind to build in Jerusalem, and in Libanus, and in all the land of his dominion.€€ƒx’All the people that were left of the Amorrhites, and Hethites, and Pherezites, and Hevites, and Jebusites, that are not of the children of Israel:€€ƒx–Their children, that were left in the land; to wit, such as the children of Israel had not been able to destroy, Solomon made tributary unto this day.€€ƒxµBut of the children of Israel, Solomon made not any to be bondmen, but they were warriors, and his servants, and his princes, and captains, and overseers of the chariots and horses.€€ƒx And there were five hundred and fifty chief officers set over all the works of Solomon, and they had people under them, and had charge over the appointed works.€€ƒxƒAnd the daughter of Pharao came up out of the city of David to her house, which Solomon had built for her: then did he build Mello.€€ƒxÉSolomon also offered three times every year holocausts, and victims of peace offerings, upon the altar which he had built to the Lord, and he burnt incense before the Lord: and the temple was finished.€€ƒxrAnd king Solomon made a fleet in Asiongaber, which is by Ailath, on the shore of the Red Sea, in the land of Edom.€€ƒxnAnd Hiram sent his servants in the fleet, sailors that had knowledge of the sea, with the servants of Solomon.€€ƒxmAnd they came to Ophir; and they brought from thence to king Solomon four hundred and twenty talents of gold.€€ƒ`€€ƒxwAnd the queen of Saba having heard of the fame of Solomon in the name of the Lord, came to try him with hard questions.€€ƒxÛAnd entering into Jerusalem with a great train, and riches, and camels that carried spices, and an immense quantity of gold, and precious stones, she came to king Solomon, and spoke to him all that she had in her heart.€€ƒx“And Solomon informed her of all the things she proposed to him: there was not any word the king was ignorant of, and which he could not answer her.€€ƒx[And when the queen of Saba saw all the wisdom of Solomon, and the house which he had built,€€˜ƒxNAnd she said to the king: The report is true, which I heard in my own country,€€ƒxóConcerning thy words, and concerning thy wisdom. And I did not believe them that told me, till I came myself, and saw with my own eyes, and have found that the half hath not been told me: thy wisdom and thy works exceed the fame which I heard.€€ƒxeBlessed are thy men, and blessed are thy servants, who stand before thee always, and hear thy wisdom.€€ƒxÌBlessed be the Lord thy God, whom thou hast pleased, and who hath set thee upon the throne of Israel, because the Lord hath loved Israel for ever, and hath appointed thee king, to do judgment and justice.€€ƒxÜAnd she gave the king a hundred and twenty talents of gold, and of spices a very great store, and precious stones: there was brought no more such abundance of spices as these which the queen of Saba gave to king Solomon.€€ƒx}(The navy also of Hiram, which brought gold from Ophir, brought from Ophir great plenty of thyine trees, and precious stones.€€ƒxĞAnd the king made of the thyine trees the rails of the house of the Lord, and of the king's house: and citterns and harps for singers: there were no such thyine trees as these brought nor seen unto this day.)€€ƒxÍAnd king Solomon gave the queen of Saba all that she desired, and asked of him: besides what he offered her of himself of his royal bounty. And she returned, and went to her own country, with her servants.€€ƒxqAnd the weight of the gold that was brought to Solomon every year, was six hundred and sixty-six talents of gold:€€ƒx³Besides that which the men brought him that were over the tributes, and the merchants, and they that sold by retail, and all the kings of Arabia, and the governors of the country.€€ƒx|And Solomon made two hundred shields of the purest gold: he allowed six hundred sicles of gold for the plates of one shield.€€ƒx•And three hundred targets of fine gold: three hundred pounds of gold covered one target: and the king put them in the house of the forest of Libanus.€€ƒxUKing Solomon also made a great throne of ivory: and overlaid it with the finest gold.€€ƒxIt had six steps: and the top of the throne was round behind: and there were two hands on either side holding the seat: and two lions stood, one at each hand,€€ƒxAnd twelve little lions stood upon the six steps, on the one side and on the other: there was no such work made in any kingdom.€€ƒxçMoreover, all the vessels out of which king Solomon drank, were of gold: and all the furniture of the house of the forest of Libanus was of most pure gold: there was no silver, nor was any account made of it in the days of Solomon:€€ƒx°For the king's navy, once in three years, went with the navy of Hiram by sea to Tharsis, and brought from thence gold, and silver, and elephants' teeth, and apes, and peacocks.€€ƒxJAnd king Solomon exceeded all the kings of the earth in riches and wisdom.€€ƒxfAnd all the earth desired to see Solomon's face, to hear his wisdom, which God had given in his heart.€€ƒx‡And every one brought him presents, vessels of silver and of gold, garments, and armour, and spices, and horses, and mules, every year.€€ƒxÆAnd Solomon gathered together chariots and horsemen, and he had a thousand four hundred chariots, and twelve thousand horsemen: and he bestowed them in fenced cities, and with the king in Jerusalem.€€ƒxAnd he made silver to be as plentiful in Jerusalem as stones: and cedars to be as common as sycamores which grow in the plains.€€ƒxŒAnd horses were brought for Solomon out of Egypt, and Coa: for the king's merchants bought them out of Coa, and brought them at a set price.€€ƒxÊAnd a chariot of four horses came out of Egypt, for six hundred sicles of silver, and a horse for a hundred and fifty. And after this manner did all the kings of the Hethites, and of Syria, sell horses.€€ƒ`€€ƒx›And king Solomon loved many strange women, besides the daughter of Pharao, and women of Moab, and of Ammon, and of Edom, and of Sidon, and of the Hethites:€€ƒyOf the nations concerning which the Lord said to the children of Israel: You shall not go in unto them, neither shall any of them come into yours: for they will most certainly turn away your hearts to follow their gods. And to these was Solomon joined with a most ardent love.€€ƒxlAnd he had seven hundred wives as queens, and three hundred concubines: and the women turned away his heart.€€ƒx¯And when he was now old, his heart was turned away by women to follow strange gods: and his heart was not perfect with the Lord his God, as was the heart of David, his father.€€˜+ƒxuAnd Solomon did that which was not pleasing before the Lord, and did not fully follow the Lord, as David, his father.€€ƒx˜Then Solomon built a temple for Chamos, the idol of Moab, on the hill that is over against Jerusalem, and for Moloch, the idol of the children of Ammon.€€ƒxxAnd he did in this manner for all his wives that were strangers, who burnt incense, and offered sacrifice to their gods.€€ƒx†And the Lord was angry with Solomon, because his mind was turned away from the Lord, the God of Israel, who had appeared to him twice;€€ƒxAnd had commanded him concerning this thing, that he should not follow strange gods: but he kept not the things which the Lord commanded him.€€ƒxÒThe Lord therefore said to Solomon: Because thou hast done this, and hast not kept my covenant, and my precepts, which I have commanded thee, I will divide and rend thy kingdom, and will give it to thy servant.€€ƒxwNevertheless, in thy days I will not do it, for David thy father's sake: but I will rend it out of the hand of thy son.€€ƒx˜Neither will I take away the whole kingdom; but I will give one tribe to thy son, for the sake of David, my servant, and Jerusalem, which I have chosen.€€ƒx_And the Lord raised up an adversary to Solomon, Adad, the Edomite, of the king's seed, in Edom.€€ƒx‹For when David was in Edom, and Joab, the general of the army, was gone up to bury them that were slain, and had killed every male in Edom,€€ƒx[(For Joab remained there six months with all Israel, till he had slain every male in Edom,)€€ƒx}Then Adad fled, he and certain Edomites of his father's servants, with him, to go into Egypt: and Adad was then a little boy.€€ƒxØAnd they arose out of Madian, and came into Pharan, and they took men with them from Pharan, and went into Egypt, to Pharao, the king of Egypt: who gave him a house, and appointed him victuals, and assigned him land.€€ƒx|And Adad found great favour before Pharao, insomuch that he gave him to wife the own sister of his wife, Taphnes, the queen.€€ƒx›And the sister of Taphnes bore him his son, Genubath; and Taphnes brought him up in the house of Pharao: and Genubath dwelt with Pharao among his children.€€ƒx´And when Adad heard in Egypt that David slept with his fathers, and that Joab, the general of the army, was dead, he said to Pharao: Let me depart, that I may go to my own country.€€ƒx¤And Pharao said to him: Why, what is wanting to thee with me, that thou seekest to go to thy own country? But he answered: Nothing; yet I beseech thee to let me go.€€ƒx€God also raised up against him an adversary, Razon, the son of Eliada, who had fled from his master, Adarezer, the king of Soba.€€ƒx²And he gathered men against him, and he became a captain of robbers, when David slew them of Soba: and they went to Damascus, and dwelt there, and they made him king in Damascus.€€ƒxAnd he was an adversary to Israel all the days of Solomon: and this is the evil of Adad, and his hatred against Israel; and he reigned in Syria.€€ƒx¢Jeroboam also, the son of Nabat, an Ephrathite, of Sareda, a servant of Solomon, whose mother was named Sarua, a widow woman, lifted up his hand against the king.€€ƒx‡And this is the cause of his rebellion against him; for Solomon built Mello, and filled up the breach of the city of David, his father.€€ƒx¥And Jeroboam was a valiant and mighty man: and Solomon seeing him a young man ingenious and industrious, made him chief over the tributes of all the house of Joseph.€€ƒx¿So it came to pass at that time, that Jeroboam went out of Jerusalem, and the prophet Ahias, the Silonite, clad with a new garment, found him in the way: and they two were alone in the field.€€ƒxVAnd Ahias taking his new garment, wherewith he was clad, divided it into twelve parts:€€ƒx¸And he said to Jeroboam: Take to thee ten pieces: for thus saith the Lord, the God of Israel: Behold, I will rend the kingdom out of the hand of Solomon, and will give thee ten tribes.€€ƒx‘But one tribe shall remain to him for the sake of my servant, David, and Jerusalem, the city which I have chosen out of all the tribes of Israel:€€ƒyBecause he hath forsaken me, and hath adored Astarthe, the goddess of the Sidonians, and Chamos, the god of Moab, and Moloch, the god of the children of Ammon: and hath not walked in my ways, to do justice before me, and to keep my precepts, and judgments, as did David, his father.€€ƒxÄYet I will not take away all the kingdom out of his hand, but I will make him prince all the days of his life, for David my servant's sake, whom I chose, who kept my commandments, and my precepts.€€ƒxVBut I will take away the kingdom out of his son's hand, and will give thee ten tribes:€€ƒx³And to his son I will give one tribe, that there may remain a lamp for my servant, David, before me always in Jerusalem, the city which I have chosen, that my name might be there.€€ƒxoAnd I will take thee, and thou shalt reign over all that thy soul desireth, and thou shalt be king over Israel.€€ƒy8If then thou wilt hearken to all that I shall command thee, and wilt walk in my ways, and do what is right before me, keeping my commandments and my precepts, as David, my servant, did: I will be with thee, and will build thee up a faithful house, as I built a house for David, and I will deliver Israel to thee:€€ƒxDAnd I will for this afflict the seed of David, but yet not for ever.€€ƒx˜Solomon, therefore, sought to kill Jeroboam: but he arose, and fled into Egypt, to Sesac, the king of Egypt, and was in Egypt till the death of Solomon.€€ƒx–And the rest of the words of Solomon, and all that he did and his wisdom: behold they are all written in the book of the words of the days of Solomon.€€ƒxRAnd the days that Solomon reigned in Jerusalem, over all Israel, were forty years.€€ƒxAnd Solomon slept with his fathers, and was buried in the city of David, his father; and Roboam, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒxVAnd Roboam went to Sichem: for thither were all Israel come together to make him king.€€ƒxŒBut Jeroboam, the son of Nabat, who was yet in Egypt, a fugitive from the face of king Solomon, hearing of his death, returned out of Egypt.€€ƒxsAnd they sent and called him: and Jeroboam came, and all the multitude of Israel, and they spoke to Roboam, saying:€€ƒxÃThy father laid a grievous yoke upon us: now, therefore, do thou take off a little of the grievous service of thy father, and of his most heavy yoke, which he put upon us, and we will serve thee.€€˜!ƒx®King Roboam took counsel with the old men, that stood before Solomon, his father, while he yet lived, and he said: What counsel do you give me, that I may answer this people?€€ƒx´They said to him: If thou wilt yield to this people to day, and condescend to them, and grant their petition, and wilt speak gentle words to them, they will be thy servants always.€€ƒx›But he left the counsel of the old men, which they had given him, and consulted with the young men that had been brought up with him, and stood before him.€€ƒx›And he said to them: What counsel do you give me, that I may answer this people, who have said to me: Make the yoke, which thy father put upon us, lighter?€€ƒyAnd the young men that had been brought up with him, said: Thus shalt thou speak to this people, who have spoken to thee, saying: Thy father made our yoke heavy, do thou ease us. Thou shalt say to them: My little finger is thicker than the back of my father.€€ƒxŒAnd now my father put a heavy yoke upon you, but I will add to your yoke: my father beat you with whips, but I will beat you with scorpions.€€ƒxSo Jeroboam, and all the people, came to Roboam the third day, as the king had appointed, saying: Come to me again the third day.€€ƒxgAnd the king answered the people roughly, leaving the counsel of the old men, which they had given him,€€ƒxÇAnd he spoke to them according to the counsel of the young men, saying: My father made your yoke heavy, but I will add to your yoke: My father beat you with whips, but I will beat you with scorpions.€€ƒxÁAnd the king condescended not to the people: for the Lord was turned away from him, to make good his word, which he had spoken in the hand of Ahias, the Silonite, to Jeroboam, the son of Nabat.€€ƒyThen the people, seeing that the king would not hearken to them, answered him, saying: What portion have we in David? or what inheritance in the son of Isai? Go home to thy dwellings, O Israel: now, David, look to thy own house. So Israel departed to their dwellings.€€ƒxaBut as for all the children of Israel that dwelt in the cities of Juda, Roboam reigned over them.€€ƒxºThen king Roboam sent Aduram, who was over the tribute: and all Israel stoned him, and he died. Wherefore king Roboam made haste to get him up into his chariot, and he fled to Jerusalem:€€ƒx;And Israel revolted from the house of David, unto this day.€€ƒxóAnd it came to pass when all Israel heard that Jeroboam was come again, that they gathered an assembly, and sent and called him, and made him king over all Israel, and there was none that followed the house of David but the tribe of Juda only.€€ƒxıAnd Roboam came to Jerusalem, and gathered together all the house of Juda, and the tribe of Benjamin, a hundred fourscore thousand chosen men for war, to fight against the house of Israel, and to bring the kingdom again under Roboam, the son of Solomon.€€ƒxABut the word of the Lord came to Semeias, the man of God, saying:€€ƒx†Speak to Roboam, the son of Solomon, the king of Juda, and to all the house of Juda, and Benjamin, and the rest of the people, saying:€€ƒy	Thus saith the Lord: You shall not go up, nor fight against your brethren, the children of Israel: let every man return to his house, for this thing is from me. They hearkened to the word of the Lord, and returned from their journey, as the Lord had commanded them.€€ƒxiAnd Jeroboam built Sichem in mount Ephraim, and dwelt there, and going out from thence, he built Phanuel.€€ƒxSAnd Jeroboam said in his heart: Now shall the kingdom return to the house of David,€€ƒxÈIf this people go up to offer sacrifices in the house of the Lord at Jerusalem: and the heart of this people will turn to their lord Roboam, the king of Juda, and they will kill me, and return to him.€€ƒx«And finding out a device, he made two golden calves, and said to them: Go ye up no more to Jerusalem: Behold thy gods, O Israel, who brought thee out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒx3And he set the one in Bethel, and the other in Dan:€€ƒx^And this thing became an occasion of sin: for the people went to adore the calf as far as Dan.€€ƒxrAnd he made temples in the high places, and priests of the lowest of the people, who were not of the sons of Levi.€€ƒy=And he appointed a feast in the eighth month, on the fifteenth day of the month, after the manner of the feast that was celebrated in Juda. And going up to the altar, he did in like manner in Bethel, to sacrifice to the calves, which he had made: and he placed in Bethel priests of the high places, which he had made.€€ƒxèAnd he went up to the altar, which he had built in Bethel, on the fifteenth day of the eighth month, which he had devised of his own heart: and he ordained a feast to the children of Israel, and went up on the altar to burn incense.€€ƒ`€€ƒx“And behold there came a man of God out of Juda, by the word of the Lord, to Bethel, when Jeroboam was standing upon the altar, and burning incense.€€ƒy7And he cried out against the altar in the word of the Lord, and said: O altar, altar, thus saith the Lord: Behold a child shall be born to the house of David, Josias by name, and he shall immolate upon thee the priests of the high places, who now burn incense upon thee, and he shall burn men's bones upon thee.€€ƒx°And he gave a sign the same day, saying: This shall be the sign, that the Lord hath spoken: Behold the altar shall be rent, and the ashes that are upon it, shall be poured out.€€ƒyAnd when the king had heard the word of the man of God, which he had cried out against the altar in Bethel, he stretched forth his hand from the altar, saying: Lay hold on him. And his hand which he stretched forth against him, withered: and he was not able to draw it back again to him.€€˜"ƒxúAnd the king said to the man of God: Entreat the face of the Lord thy God, and pray for me, that my hand may be restored to me. And the man of God besought the face of the Lord, and the king's hand was restored to him, and it became as it was before.€€ƒx^And the king said to the man of God: Come home with me to dine, and I will make thee presents.€€ƒx”And the man of God answered the king: If thou wouldst give me half thy house, I will not go with thee, nor eat bread, nor drink water in this place:€€ƒx˜For so it was enjoined me by the word of the Lord commanding me: Thou shalt not eat bread, nor drink water, nor return by the same way that thou camest.€€ƒxTSo he departed by another way, and returned not by the way that he came into Bethel.€€ƒxÖNow a certain old prophet dwelt in Bethel, and his sons came to him, and told him all the works that the man of God had done that day in Bethel: and they told their father the words which he had spoken to the king.€€ƒx€And their father said to them: What way went he? His sons shewed him the way by which the man of God went, who came out of Juda.€€ƒxTAnd he said to his sons: Saddle me the ass. And when they had saddled it, he got up,€€ƒx¢And went after the man of God, and found him sitting under a turpentine tree: and he said to him: Art thou the man of God who camest from Juda? He answered: I am.€€ƒx3And he said to him: Come home with me to eat bread.€€ƒxiBut he said: I must not return, nor go with thee, neither will I eat bread, or drink water in this place:€€ƒx¢Because the Lord spoke to me, in the word of the Lord, saying: Thou shalt not eat bread, and thou shalt not drink water there, nor return by the way thou wentest.€€ƒxĞHe said to him: I also am a prophet like unto thee: and an angel spoke to me, in the word of the Lord, saying: Bring him back with thee into thy house, that he may eat bread, and drink water. He deceived him,€€ƒxMAnd brought him back with him: so he ate bread, and drank water in his house.€€ƒxYAnd as they sat at table, the word of the Lord came to the prophet that brought him back:€€ƒxÏAnd he cried out to the man of God who came out of Juda, saying: Thus saith the Lord: Because thou hast not been obedient to the Lord, and hast not kept the commandment which the Lord thy God commanded thee,€€ƒxÓAnd hast returned, and eaten bread, and drunk water in the place wherein he commanded thee that thou shouldst not eat bread, nor drink water, thy dead body shall not be brought into the sepulchre of thy fathers.€€ƒx^And when he had eaten and drunk, he saddled his ass for the prophet, whom he had brought back.€€ƒx£And when he was gone, a lion found him in the way, and killed him, and his body was cast in the way: and the ass stood by him, and the lion stood by the dead body.€€ƒx¨And behold, men passing by, saw the dead body cast in the way, and the lion standing by the body. And they came and told it in the city, wherein that old prophet dwelt.€€ƒy'And when that prophet, who had brought him back out of the way, heard of it, he said: It is the man of God, that was disobedient to the mouth of the Lord, and the Lord hath delivered him to the lion, and he hath torn him, and killed him, according to the word of the Lord, which he spoke to him.€€ƒxHAnd he said to his sons: Saddle me an ass. And when they had saddled it,€€ƒx¥And he was gone, he found the dead body cast in the way, and the ass and the lion standing by the carcass: the lion had not eaten of the dead body, nor hurt the ass.€€ƒx›And the prophet took up the body of the man of God, and laid it upon the ass, and going back brought it into the city of the old prophet, to mourn for him.€€ƒxjAnd he laid his dead body in his own sepulchre: and they mourned over him, saying: Alas! alas, my brother.€€ƒx¢And when they had mourned over him, he said to his sons: When I am dead, bury me in the sepulchre wherein the man of God is buried: lay my bones beside his bones.€€ƒxÑFor assuredly the word shall come to pass which he hath foretold in the word of the Lord, against the altar that is in Bethel: and against all the temples of the high places, that are in the cities of Samaria.€€ƒxæAfter these words, Jeroboam came not back from his wicked way: but on the contrary, he made of the meanest of the people priests of the high places: whosoever would, he filled his hand, and he was made a priest of the high places.€€ƒxlAnd for this cause did the house of Jeroboam sin, and was cut off, and destroyed from the face of the earth.€€ƒ`€€ƒx2At that time Abia, the son of Jeroboam, fell sick.€€ƒxÍAnd Jeroboam said to his wife: Arise, and change thy dress, that thou be not known to be the wife of Jeroboam, and go to Silo, where Ahias, the prophet is, who told me that I should reign over this people.€€ƒx‡Take also with thee ten loaves, and cracknels, and a pot of honey, and go to him: for he will tell thee what will become of this child.€€ƒx¢Jeroboam's wife did as he told her: and rising up, went to Silo, and came to the house of Ahias; but he could not see, for his eyes were dim by reason of his age.€€˜ƒx¸Ahias heard the sound of her feet, coming in at the door, and said: Come in, thou wife of Jeroboam: why dost thou feign thyself to be another? But I am sent to thee with heavy tidings.€€ƒxŸGo, and tell Jeroboam: Thus saith the Lord, the God of Israel: For as much as I exalted thee from among the people, and made thee prince over my people Israel;€€ƒxâAnd rent the kingdom away from the house of David, and gave it to thee, and thou hast not been as my servant, David, who kept my commandments, and followed me with all his heart, doing that which was well pleasing in my sight:€€ƒxŸBut hast done evil above all that were before thee, and hast made thee strange gods, and molten gods, to provoke me to anger, and hast cast me behind thy back:€€ƒyTherefore, behold I will bring evils upon the house of Jeroboam, and will cut off from Jeroboam him that pisseth against the wall, and him that is shut up, and the last in Israel: and I will sweep away the remnant of the house of Jeroboam, as dung is swept away till all be clean.€€ƒx¦Them that shall die of Jeroboam in the city, the dogs shall eat: and them that shall die in the field, the birds of the air shall devour: for the Lord hath spoken it.€€ƒxsArise thou, therefore, and go to thy house: and when thy feet shall be entering into the city, the child shall die,€€ƒxÚAnd all Israel shall mourn for him, and shall bury him: for he only of Jeroboam shall be laid in a sepulchre, because in his regard there is found a good word from the Lord, the God of Israel, in the house of Jeroboam.€€ƒx~And the Lord hath appointed himself a king over Israel, who shall cut off the house of Jeroboam in this day, and in this time:€€ƒyAnd the Lord God shall strike Israel as a reed is shaken in the water: and he shall root up Israel out of this good land, which he gave to their fathers, and shall scatter them beyond the river: because they have made to themselves groves, to provoke the Lord.€€ƒxdAnd the Lord shall give up Israel for the sins of Jeroboam, who hath sinned, and made Israel to sin.€€ƒx‹And the wife of Jeroboam arose, and departed, and came to Thersa: and when she was coming in to the threshold of the house, the child died,€€ƒx•And they buried him. And all Israel mourned for him, according to the word of the Lord, which he spoke by the hand of his servant Ahias, the prophet.€€ƒxAnd the rest of the acts of Jeroboam, how he fought, and how he reigned, behold they are written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel.€€ƒx‡And the days that Jeroboam reigned, were two and twenty years: and he slept with his fathers: and Nadab, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒyAnd Roboam, the son of Solomon, reigned in Juda: Roboam was one and forty years old when he began to reign: and he reigned seventeen years in Jerusalem, the city which the Lord chose out of all the tribes of Israel to put his name there. And his mother's name was Naama, an Ammonitess.€€ƒx‡And Juda did evil in the sight of the Lord, and provoked him above all that their fathers had done, in their sins which they committed.€€ƒxkFor they also built them altars, and statues, and groves, upon every high hill, and under every green tree:€€ƒx°There were also the effeminate in the land, and they did according to all the abominations of the people, whom the Lord had destroyed before the face of the children of Israel.€€ƒx^And in the fifth year of the reign of Roboam, Sesac, king of Egypt, came up against Jerusalem.€€ƒx›And he took away the treasures of the house of the Lord, and the king's treasures, and carried all off: as also the shields of gold which Solomon had made:€€ƒx¹And Roboam made shields of brass instead of them, and delivered them into the hand of the captains of the shieldbearers, and of them that kept watch before the gate of the king's house.€€ƒx¶And when the king went into the house of the Lord, they whose office it was to go before him, carried them: and afterwards they brought them back to the armoury of the shieldbearers.€€ƒx‹Now the rest of the acts of Roboam, and all that he did, behold they are written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Juda.€€ƒx5And there was war between Roboam and Jeroboam always.€€ƒx¬And Roboam slept with his fathers, and was buried with them, in the city of David: and his mother's name was Naama, an Ammonitess: and Abiam, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒx_Now in the eighteenth year of the reign of Jeroboam, the son of Nabat, Abiam reigned over Juda.€€ƒxbHe reigned three years in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Maacha, the daughter of Abessalom.€€ƒx¦And he walked in all the sins of his father, which he had done before him: and his heart was not perfect with the Lord his God, as was the heart of David, his father.€€ƒx|But for David's sake the Lord his God gave him a lamp in Jerusalem, to set up his son after him, and to establish Jerusalem:€€˜"ƒxGBut there was war between Roboam and Jeroboam all the time of his life.€€ƒx¶And the rest of the words of Abiam, and all that he did, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Juda? And there was war between Abiam and Jeroboam.€€ƒxsAnd Abiam slept with his fathers, and they buried him in the city of David: and Asa, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒxPSo in the twentieth year of Jeroboam, king of Israel, reigned Asa, king of Juda,€€ƒxiAnd he reigned one and forty years in Jerusalem. His mother's name was Maacha, the daughter of Abessalom.€€ƒxTAnd Asa did that which was right in the sight of the Lord, as did David, his father:€€ƒxtAnd he took away the effeminate out of the land, and removed all the filth of the idols, which his fathers had made.€€ƒxùMoreover, he also removed his mother, Maacha, from being the princess in the sacrifices of Priapus, and in the grove which she had consecrated to him: and he destroyed her den, and broke in pieces the filthy idol, and burnt it by the torrent Cedron:€€ƒxpBut the high places he did not take away. Nevertheless, the heart of Asa was perfect with the Lord all his days:€€ƒxˆAnd he brought in the things which his father had dedicated, and he had vowed, into the house of the Lord, silver and gold, and vessels.€€ƒxIAnd there was war between Asa, and Baasa, king of Israel, all their days.€€ƒx†And Baasa, king of Israel, went up against Juda, and built Rama, that no man might go out or come in of the side of Asa, king of Juda.€€ƒy%Then Asa took all the silver and gold that remained in the treasures of the house of the Lord, and in the treasures of the king's house, and delivered it into the hands of his servants: and sent them to Benadad, son of Tabremon, the son of Hezion, king of Syria, who dwelt in Damascus, saying:€€ƒxìThere is a league between me and thee, and between my father and thy father: therefore I have sent thee presents of silver and gold: and I desire thee to come, and break thy league with Baasa, king of Israel, that he may depart from me.€€ƒxÇBenadad, hearkening to king Asa, sent the captains of his army against the cities of Israel, and they smote Ahion, and Dan, and Abeldomum Maacha, and all Cenneroth; that is all the land of Nephthali.€€ƒxSAnd when Baasa had heard this, he left off building Rama, and returned into Thersa.€€ƒxäBut king Asa sent word into all Juda, saying: Let no man be excused: and they took away the stones from Rama, and the timber thereof, wherewith Baasa had been building, and with them king Asa built Gabaa of Benjamin, and Maspha.€€ƒxùBut the rest of all the acts of Asa, and all his strength, and all that he did, and the cities that he built, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Juda? But in the time of his old age he was diseased in his feet.€€ƒx†And he slept with his fathers, and was buried with them in the city of David, his father. And Josaphat, his son, reigned in his place.€€ƒxBut Nadab, the son of Jeroboam, reigned over Israel the second year of Asa, king of Juda: and he reigned over Israel two years.€€ƒxAnd he did evil in the sight of the Lord, and walked in the ways of his father, and in his sins, wherewith he made Israel to sin.€€ƒx¹And Baasa, the son of Ahias, of the house of Issachar, conspired against him, and slew him in Gebbethon, which is a city of the Philistines: for Nadab and all Israel besieged Gebbethon.€€ƒxSSo Baasa slew him in the third year of Asa, king of Juda, and reigned in his place.€€ƒxæAnd when he was king, he cut off all the house of Jeroboam: he left not so much as one soul of his seed, till he had utterly destroyed him, according to the word of the Lord, which he had spoken in the hand of Ahias, the Silonite:€€ƒx¤Because of the sin of Jeroboam, which he had sinned, and wherewith he had made Israel to sin, and for the offence wherewith he provoked the Lord, the God of Israel.€€ƒx‰But the rest of the acts of Nadab, and all that he did, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel?€€ƒxLAnd there was war between Asa and Baasa, the king of Israel, all their days.€€ƒx{In the third year of Asa, king of Juda, Baasa, the son of Ahias, reigned over all Israel, in Thersa, four and twenty years.€€ƒxvAnd he did evil before the Lord, and walked in the ways of Jeroboam, and in his sins, wherewith he made Israel to sin.€€ƒ`€€ƒxQThen the word of the Lord came to Jehu, the son of Hanani, against Baasa, saying:€€ƒxÚFor as much as I have exalted thee out of the dust and made thee prince over my people Israel, and thou hast walked in the way of Jeroboam, and hast made my people Israel to sin, to provoke me to anger with their sins:€€ƒx”Behold I will cut down the posterity of Baasa, and the posterity of his house, and I will make thy house as the house of Jeroboam, the son of Nabat.€€ƒx†Him that dieth of Baasa, in the city, the dogs shall eat: and him that dieth of his in the country, the fowls of the air shall devour.€€˜"ƒxbSo Baasa slept with his fathers, and was buried in Thersa: and Ela, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒyaAnd when the word of the Lord came in the hand of Jehu, the son of Hanani, the prophet, against Baasa, and against his house, and against all the evil that he had done before the Lord, to provoke him to anger by the works of his hands, to become as the house of Jeroboam: for this cause he slew him; that is to say, Jehu, the son of Hanani, the prophet.€€ƒxuIn the six and twentieth year of Asa, king of Juda, Ela, the son of Baasa, reigned over Israel, in Thersa, two years.€€ƒx«And his servant Zambri, who was captain of half the horsemen, rebelled against him: now Ela was drinking in Thersa, and drunk in the house of Arsa, the governor of Thersa.€€ƒx‚And Zambri rushing in, struck him, and slew him, in the seven and twentieth year of Asa, king of Juda and he reigned in his stead.€€ƒx¤And when he was king, and sat upon his throne, he slew all the house of Baasa, and he left not one thereof to piss against a wall and all his kinsfolks and friends.€€ƒxAnd Zambri destroyed all the house of Baasa, according to the word of the Lord, that he had spoken to Baasa, in the hand of Jehu, the prophet,€€ƒx˜For all the sins of Baasa, and the sins of Ela, his son, who sinned, and made Israel to sin, provoking the Lord, the God of Israel, with their vanities.€€ƒx‡But the rest of the acts of Ela, and all that he did, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel?€€ƒx›In the seven and twentieth year of Asa, king of Juda, Zambri reigned seven days in Thersa: now the army was besieging Gebbethon, a city of the Philistines.€€ƒx”And when they heard that Zambri had rebelled, and slain the king, all Israel made Amri their king, who was general over Israel in the camp that day.€€ƒxTAnd Amri went up, and all Israel with him, from Gebbethon, and they besieged Thersa.€€ƒx‚And Zambri, seeing that the city was about to be taken, went into the palace, and burnt himself with the king's house: and he died€€ƒx’In his sins, which he had sinned, doing evil before the Lord, and walking in the way of Jeroboam, and in his sin, wherewith he made Israel to sin.€€ƒx˜But the rest of the acts of Zambri, and of his conspiracy and tyranny, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel?€€ƒxŸThen were the people of Israel divided into two parts: one half of the people followed Thebni, the son of Gineth, to make him king: and one half followed Amri.€€ƒx‰But the people that were with Amri, prevailed over the people that followed Thebni, the son of Gineth: and Thebni died, and Amri reigned.€€ƒxzIn the one and thirtieth year of Asa, king of Juda, Amri reigned over Israel twelve years: in Thersa he reigned six years.€€ƒx»And he bought the hill of Samaria of Semer, for two talents of silver: and he built upon it, and he called the city which he built Samaria, after the name of Semer, the owner of the hill.€€ƒx^And Amri did evil in the sight of the Lord, and acted wickedly above all that were before him.€€ƒx³And he walked in all the way of Jeroboam, the son of Nabat, and in his sins, wherewith he made Israel to sin: to provoke the Lord, the God of Israel, to anger with their vanities.€€ƒxNow the rest of the acts of Amri, and the battles he fought, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel?€€ƒxeAnd Amri slept with his fathers, and was buried in Samaria, and Achab, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒx¶Now Achab, the son of Amri, reigned over Israel in the eight and thirtieth year of Asa, king of Juda. And Achab, the son of Amri, reigned over Israel in Samaria two and twenty years.€€ƒx]And Achab, the son of Amri, did evil in the sight of the Lord above all that were before him.€€ƒxÈNor was it enough for him to walk in the sins of Jeroboam, the son of Nabat: but he also took to wife Jezabel, daughter of Ethbaal, king of the Sidonians. And he went, and served Baal, and adored him.€€ƒxVAnd he set up an altar for Baal, in the temple of Baal, which he had built in Samaria;€€ƒx…And he planted a grove: and Achab did more to provoke the Lord, the God of Israel, than all the kings of Israel that were before him.€€ƒxóIn his days Hiel, of Bethel, built Jericho: in Abiram, his firstborn, he laid its foundations: and in his youngest son, Segub, he set up the gates thereof: according to the word of the Lord, which he spoke in the hand of Josue, the son of Nun.€€ƒ`€€ƒxØAnd Elias the Thesbite, of the inhabitants of Galaad, said to Achab: As the Lord liveth, the God of Israel, in whose sight I stand, there shall not be dew nor rain these years, but according to the words of my mouth.€€ƒx-And the word of the Lord came to him, saying:€€ƒxuGet thee hence, and go towards the east, and hide thyself by the torrent of Carith, which is over against the Jordan;€€ƒx^And there thou shalt drink of the torrent: and I have commanded the ravens to feed thee there.€€˜ƒx{And the ravens brought him bread and flesh in the morning, and bread and flesh in the evening; and he drank of the torrent.€€ƒxSBut after some time the torrent was dried up: for it had not rained upon the earth.€€ƒx.Then the word of the Lord came to him, saying:€€ƒxsArise, and go to Sarephta of the Sidonians, and dwell there: for I have commanded a widow woman there to feed thee.€€ƒxĞHe arose, and went to Sarephta. And when he was come to the gate of the city, he saw the widow woman gathering sticks, and he called her, and said to her: Give me a little water in a vessel, that I may drink.€€ƒxŸAnd Elias said to her: Give me thy son. And he took him out of her bosom, and carried him into the upper chamber where he abode, and laid him upon his own bed.€€ƒxñAnd she answered: As the Lord thy God liveth, I have no bread, but only a handful of meal in a pot, and a little oil in a cruise: behold I am gathering two sticks, that I may go in and dress it, for me and my son, that we may eat it and die.€€ƒxºAnd Elias said to her: Fear not; but go, and do as thou hast said but first make for me of the same meal a little hearth cake, and bring it to me, and after make for thyself and thy son.€€ƒx»For thus saith the Lord, the God of Israel: The pot of meal shall not waste, nor the cruise of oil be diminished, until the day wherein the Lord will give rain upon the face of the earth.€€ƒxgShe went, and did according to the word of Elias: and he ate, and she, and her house: and from that day€€ƒxŒThe pot of meal wasted not, and the cruise of oil was not diminished according to the word of the Lord, which he spoke in the hand of Elias.€€ƒx­And it came to pass after this, that the son of the woman, the mistress of the house, fell sick, and the sickness was very grievous, so that there was no breath left in him.€€ƒx¦And she said to Elias: What have I to do with thee, thou man of God? art thou come to me, that my iniquities should be remembered, and that thou shouldst kill my son?€€ƒ`€€ƒx–And he cried to the Lord, and said: O Lord, my God, hast thou afflicted also the widow, with whom I am after a sort maintained, so as to kill her son?€€ƒxµAnd he stretched, and measured himself upon the child three times, and cried to the Lord, and said: O Lord, my God, let the soul of this child, I beseech thee, return into his body.€€ƒxcAnd the Lord heard the voice of Elias: and the soul of the child returned into him, and he revived.€€ƒx¢And Elias took the child, and brought him down from the upper chamber to the house below, and delivered him to his mother, and said to her: Behold thy son liveth.€€ƒxzAnd the woman said to Elias: Now by this I know that thou art a man of God, and the word of the Lord in thy mouth is true.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŸAfter many days, the word of the Lord came to Elias, in the third year, saying: Go, and shew thyself to Achab, that I may give rain upon the face of the earth.€€ƒxTAnd Elias went to shew himself to Achab, and there was a grievous famine in Samaria.€€ƒxXAnd Achab called Abdias the governor of his house: now Abdias feared the Lord very much.€€ƒxšFor when Jezabel killed the prophets of the Lord, he took a hundred prophets, and hid them by fifty and fifty in caves, and fed them with bread and water.€€˜.ƒxˆAnd they divided the countries between them, that they might go round about them: Achab went one way, and Abdias another way by himself.€€ƒxuAnd as Abdias was in the way, Elias met him: and he knew him, and fell on his face, and said: Art thou my lord Elias?€€ƒx>And he answered: I am. Go, and tell thy master: Elias is here.€€ƒx{And he said: What have I sinned, that thou wouldst deliver me, thy servant, into the hand of Achab, that he should kill me?€€ƒx×As the Lord thy God liveth, there is no nation or kingdom, whither my lord hath not sent to seek thee: and when all answered: He is not here: he took an oath of every kingdom and nation, because thou wast not found.€€ƒxAAnd now thou sayest to me: Go and tell thy master: Elias is here.€€ƒxßAnd when I am gone from thee, the Spirit of the Lord will carry thee into a place that I know not: and I shall go in and tell Achab; and he, not finding thee, will kill me: but thy servant feareth the Lord from his infancy.€€ƒxÒHath it not been told thee, my lord, what I did when Jezabel killed the prophets of the Lord; how I hid a hundred men of the prophets of the Lord, by fifty and fifty in caves, and fed them with bread and water?€€ƒxPAnd now thou sayest: Go and tell thy master: Elias is here: that he may kill me.€€ƒxmAnd Elias said: As the Lord of hosts liveth, before whose face I stand, this day I will shew myself unto him.€€ƒxPAbdias therefore went to meet Achab, and told him: and Achab came to meet Elias.€€ƒxEAnd when he had seen him, he said: Art thou he that troublest Israel?€€ƒx“And he said: I have not troubled Israel, but thou and thy father's house, who have forsaken the commandments of the Lord, and have followed Baalim.€€ƒxÂNevertheless send now, and gather unto me all Israel, unto Mount Carmel, and the prophets of Baal four hundred and fifty, and the prophets of the groves four hundred, who eat at Jezabel's table.€€ƒx_Achab sent to all the children of Israel, and gathered together the prophets unto mount Carmel.€€ƒx¹And Elias coming to all the people, said: How long do you halt between two sides? If the Lord be God, follow him: but if Baal, then follow him. And the people did not answer him a word.€€ƒxAnd Elias said again to the people: I only remain a prophet of the Lord: but the prophets of Baal are four hundred and fifty men.€€ƒxâLet two bullocks be given us, and let them choose one bullock for themselves, and cut it in pieces, and lay it upon wood, but put no fire under: and I will dress the other bullock, and lay it on wood, and put no fire under it.€€ƒx»Call ye on the names of your gods, and I will call on the name of my Lord: and the God that shall answer by fire, let him be God. And all the people answering, said: A very good proposal.€€ƒx¤Then Elias said to the prophets of Baal: Choose you one bullock and dress it first, because you are many: and call on the names of your gods; but put no fire under.€€ƒxûAnd they took the bullock, which he gave them, and dressed it: and they called on the name of Baal from morning even until noon, saying: O Baal, hear us. But there was no voice, nor any that answered: and they leaped over the altar that they had made.€€ƒxËAnd when it was now noon, Elias jested at them, saying: Cry with a louder voice: for he is a god; and perhaps he is talking, or is in an inn, or on a journey; or perhaps he is asleep, and must be awaked.€€ƒx†So they cried with a loud voice, and cut themselves after their manner with knives and lancets, till they were all covered with blood.€€ƒxºAnd after midday was past, and while they were prophesying, the time was come of offering sacrifice, and there was no voice heard, nor did any one answer, nor regard them as they prayed.€€ƒxŒElias said to all the people: Come ye unto me. And the people coming near unto him, he repaired the altar of the Lord, that was broken down:€€ƒxšAnd he took twelve stones, according to the number of the tribes of the sons of Jacob to whom the word of the Lord came, saying: Israel shall be thy name.€€ƒx”And he built with the stones an altar to the name of the Lord: and he made a trench for water, of the breadth of two furrows, round about the altar.€€ƒxXAnd he laid the wood in order, and cut the bullock in pieces, and laid it upon the wood.€€ƒyAnd he said: Fill four buckets with water, and pour it upon the burnt offering, and upon the wood. And again he said: Do the same the second time. And when they had done it the second time, he said: Do the same also the third time. And they did so the third time.€€ƒxNAnd the water run round about the altar, and the trench was filled with water.€€ƒyAnd when it was now time to offer the holocaust, Elias, the prophet, came near and said: O Lord God of Abraham, and Isaac, and Israel, shew this day that thou art the God of Israel, and I thy servant, and that according to thy commandment I have done all these things.€€ƒx}Dear me, O Lord, hear me: that this people may learn that thou art the Lord God, and that thou hast turned their heart again.€€ƒx—Then the fire of the Lord fell, and consumed the holocaust, and the wood, and the stones, and the dust, and licked up the water that was in the trench.€€ƒxtAnd when all the people saw this, they fell on their faces, and they said: The Lord, he is God; the Lord, he is God.€€ƒxµAnd Elias said to them: Take the prophets of Baal, and let not one of them escape. And when they had taken them, Elias brought them down to the torrent Cison, and killed them there.€€ƒxYAnd Elias said to Achab: Go up, eat and drink: for there is a sound of abundance of rain.€€ƒxAchab went up to eat and drink: and Elias went up to the top of Carmel, and casting himself down upon the earth, put his face between his knees,€€ƒx¢And he said to his servant: Go up, and look towards the sea. And he went up, and looked, and said: There is nothing. And again he said to him: Return seven times.€€ƒxºAnd at the seventh time: Behold a little cloud arose out of the sea like a man's foot. And he said: Go up, and say to Achab: Prepare thy chariot, and go down, lest the rain prevent thee.€€ƒx°And while he turned himself this way and that way, behold the heavens grew dark, with clouds and wind, and there fell a great rain. And Achab getting up, went away to Jezrahel:€€ƒxtAnd the hand of the Lord was upon Elias, and he girded up his loins, and ran before Achab, till he came to Jezrahel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxeAnd Achab told Jezabel all that Elias had done, and how he had slain all the prophets with the sword.€€ƒx¸And Jezabel sent a messenger to Elias, saying: Such and such things may the gods do to me, and add still more, if by this hour to morrow I make not thy life as the life of one of them.€€ƒx‡Then EIias was afraid, and rising up, he went whithersoever he had a mind: and he came to Bersabee of Juda, and left his servant there,€€ƒxõAnd he went forward, one day's journey into the desert. And when he was there, and sat under a juniper tree, he requested for his soul that he might die, and said: It is enough for me, Lord; take away my soul: for I am no better than my fathers.€€•ƒxHe looked, and behold there was at his head a hearth cake, and a vessel of water: and he ate and drank, and he fell asleep again.€€ƒxˆAnd the angel of the Lord came again the second time, and touched him, and said to him: Arise, eat: for thou hast yet a great way to go.€€ƒxƒAnd he arose, and ate and drank, and walked in the strength of that food forty days and forty nights, unto the mount of God, Horeb.€€ƒxAnd when he was come thither, he abode in a cave. and behold the word of the Lord came unto him, and he said to him: What dost thou here, Elias?€€ƒyAnd he answered: With zeal have I been zealous for the Lord God of hosts: for the children of Israel have forsaken thy covenant: they have thrown down thy altars, they have slain thy prophets with the sword, and I alone am left, and they seek my life to take it away.€€ƒy:And he said to him: Go forth, and stand upon the mount before the Lord: and behold the Lord passeth, and a great and strong wind before the Lord, overthrowing the mountains, and breaking the rocks in pieces: but the Lord is not in the wind. And after the wind, an earthquake: but the Lord is not in the earthquake.€€ƒxsAnd after the earthquake, a fire: but the Lord is not in the fire. And after the fire, a whistling of a gentle air.€€ƒxÇAnd when Elias heard it, he covered his face with his mantle, and coming forth, stood in the entering in of the cave, and behold a voice unto him, saying: What dost thou here, Elias? And he answered:€€ƒxüWith zeal have I been zealous for the Lord God of hosts: because the children of Israel have forsaken thy covenant: they have destroyed thy altars, they have slain thy prophets with the sword; and I alone am left, and they seek my life to take it away.€€ƒx¥And the Lord said to him: Go, and return on thy way, through the desert, to Damascus: and when thou art come thither, thou shalt anoint Hazael to be king over Syria;€€ƒx And thou shalt anoint Jehu, the son of Namsi, to be king over Israel: and Eliseus, the son of Saphat, of Abelmeula, thou shalt anoint to be prophet in thy room.€€ƒx¬And it shall come to pass, that whosoever shall escape the sword of Hazael, shall be slain by Jehu: and whosoever shall escape the sword of Jehu, shall be slain by Eliseus.€€ƒxŸAnd I will leave me seven thousand men in Israel, whose knees have not been bowed before Baal, and every mouth that hath not worshipped him, kissing the hands.€€ƒxèAnd Elias departing from thence, found Eliseus, the son of Saphat, ploughing with twelve yoke of oxen: and he was one of them that were ploughing with, twelve yoke of oxen: and when Elias came up to him, he cast his mantle upon him.€€ƒxèAnd he forthwith left the oxen, and run after Elias, and said: Let me, I pray thee, kiss my father and my mother, and then I will follow thee. And he said to him: Go, and return back: for that which was my part, I have done to thee.€€ƒxåAnd returning back from him, he took a yoke of oxen, and killed them, and boiled the flesh with the plough of the oxen, and gave to the people, and they ate: and rising up, he went away, and followed Elias, and ministered to him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx½And Benadad, king of Syria, gathered together all his host, and there were two and thirty kings with him, and horses, and chariots: and going up, he fought against Samaria, and besieged it.€€ƒx?And sending messengers to Achab, king of Israel, into the city,€€ƒxpHe said: Thus saith Benadad: Thy silver and thy gold is mine: and thy wives and thy goodliest children are mine.€€ƒxiAnd the king of Israel answered: According to thy word, my lord, O king, I am thine, and all that I have.€€˜+ƒxÔTo morrow, therefore, at this same hour, I will send my servants to thee, and they shall search thy house, and the houses of thy servants: and all that pleaseth them, they shall put in their hands, and take away.€€ƒxÖAnd the king of Israel called all the ancients of the land, and said: Mark, and see that he layeth snares for us. For he sent to me for my wives, and for my children, and for my silver and gold: and I said not nay.€€ƒx]And all the ancients, and all the people said to him: Hearken not to him, nor consent to him.€€ƒx©Wherefore he answered the messengers of Benadad: Tell my lord, the king: All that thou didst send for to me, thy servant at first, I will do: but this thing I cannot do.€€ƒxãAnd the messengers returning brought him word. And he sent again, and said: Such and such things may the gods do to me, and more may they add, if the dust of Samaria shall suffice for handfuls for all the people that follow me.€€ƒxcAnd the king of Israel answering, said: Tell him: Let not the girded boast himself as the ungirded.€€ƒx¤And it came to pass, when Benadad heard this word, that he and the kings were drinking in pavilions, and he said to his servants: Beset the city. And they beset it.€€ƒxæAnd behold a prophet coming to Achab, king of Israel, said to him: Thus saith the Lord: Hast thou seen all this exceeding great multitude? behold I will deliver them into thy hand this day: that thou mayst know that I am the Lord.€€ƒx¬And Achab said: By whom? And he said to him: Thus saith the Lord: By the servants of the princes of the provinces. And he said: Who shall begin to fight? And he said: Thou.€€ƒxÆSo he mustered the servants of the princes of the provinces, and he found the number of two hundred and thirty-two: and he mustered after them the people, all the children of Israel, seven thousand:€€ƒx”And they went out at noon. But Benadad was drinking himself drunk in his pavilion, and the two and thirty kings with him, who were come to help him.€€ƒxAnd the servants of the princes of the provinces went out first. And Benadad sent. And they told him, saying: There are men come out of Samaria.€€ƒxjAnd he said: Whether they come for peace, take them alive: or whether they come to fight, take them alive.€€ƒx\So the servants of the princes of the provinces went out, and the rest of the army followed:€€ƒx¬And every one slew the man that came against him: and the Syrians fled, and Israel pursued after them. And Benadad, king of Syria, fled away on horseback with his horsemen.€€ƒxpBut the king of Israel going out overthrew the horses and chariots, and slew the Syrians with a great slaughter.€€ƒx·(And a prophet coming to the king of Israel, said to him: Go, and strengthen thyself, and know, and see what thou dost: for the next year the king of Syria will come up against thee.)€€ƒxÓBut the servants of the king of Syria said to him: Their gods are gods of the hills, therefore they have overcome us: but it is better that we should fight against them in the plains, and we shall overcome them.€€ƒxdDo thou, therefore, this thing: Remove all the kings from thy army, and put captains in their stead:€€ƒy3And make up the number of soldiers that have been slain of thine, and horses, according to the former horses, and chariots, according to the chariots which thou hadst before: and we will fight against them in the plains, and thou shalt see that we shall overcome them. He believed their counsel, and did so.€€ƒxrWherefore, at the return of the year, Benadad mustered the Syrians, and went up to Aphec, to fight against Israel.€€ƒx¼And the children of Israel were mustered, and taking victuals, went out on the other side, and encamped over against them, like two little flocks of goats: but the Syrians filled the land.€€ƒy(And a man of God coming, said to the king of Israel: Thus saith the Lord: Because the Syrians have said: The Lord is God of the hills, but is not God of the valleys: I will deliver all this great multitude into thy hand, and you shall know that I am the Lord.)€€ƒxĞAnd both sides set their armies in array one against the other seven days, and on the seventh day the battle was fought: and the children of Israel slew, of the Syrians, a hundred thousand footmen in one day.€€ƒxÍAnd they that remained fled to Aphec, into the city: and the wall fell upon seven and twenty thousand men, that were left. And Benadad fleeing, went into the city, into a chamber that was within a chamber.€€ƒxèAnd his servants said to him: Behold, we have heard that the kings of the house of Israel are merciful; so let us put sackcloths on our loins, and ropes on our heads, and go out to the king of Israel: perhaps he will save our lives.€€ƒxìSo they girded sackcloths on their loins, and put ropes on their heads, and came to the king of Israel, and said to him: Thy servant, Benadad, saith: I beseech thee let me have my life. And he said: If he be yet alive, he is my brother.€€ƒxáThe men took this for good luck: and in haste caught the word out of his mouth, and said: Thy brother Benadad. And he said to them: Go, and bring him to me. Then Benadad came out to him, and he lifted him up into his chariot.€€ƒxıAnd he said to him: The cities which my father took from thy father, I will restore: and do thou make thee streets in Damascus, as my father made in Samaria and having made a league, I will depart from thee. So he made a league with him, and let him go.€€ƒxƒThen a certain man of the sons of the prophets, said to his companion, in the word of the Lord: Strike me. But he would not strike.€€ƒxÔThen he said to him: Because thou wouldst not hearken to the word of the Lord, behold thou shalt depart from me, and a lion shall slay thee. And when he was gone a little from him, a lion found him, and slew him.€€ƒxYThen he found another man, and said to him: Strike me. And he struck him and wounded him.€€ƒxtSo the prophet went, and met the king in the way, and disguised himself by sprinkling dust on his face and his eyes.€€ƒyAnd as the king passed by, he cried to the king, and said: Thy servant went out to fight hand to hand: and when a certain man was run away, one brought him to me, and said: Keep this man: and if he shall slip away, thy life shall be for his life, or thou shalt pay a talent of silver.€€ƒx®And whilst I, in the hurry, turned this way and that, on a sudden he was not to be seen. And the king of Israel said to him: This is thy judgment, which thyself hast decreed.€€ƒxtBut he forthwith wiped off the dust from his face, and the king of Israel knew him, that he was one of the prophets.€€ƒx§And he said to him: Thus saith the Lord. Because thou hast let go out of thy hand a man worthy of death, thy life shall be for his life, and thy people for his people.€€ƒx^And the king of Israel returned to his house, slighting to hear, and raging came into Samaria.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŒAnd after these things, Naboth the Jezrahelite, who was in Jezrahel, had at that time a vineyard, near the palace of Achab, king of Samaria.€€ƒyAnd Achab spoke to Naboth, saying: Give me thy vineyard, that I may make me a garden of herbs, because it is nigh, and adjoining to my house; and I will give thee for it a better vineyard: or if thou think it more convenient for thee, I will give thee the worth of it in money.€€ƒxhNaboth answered him: The Lord be merciful to me, and not let me give thee the inheritance of my fathers.€€ƒyAnd Achab came into his house angry and fretting, because of the word that Naboth, the Jezrahelite, had spoken to him, saying: I will not give thee the inheritance of my fathers. And casting himself upon his bed, he turned away his face to the wall, and would eat no bread.€€˜ƒxæAnd he answered her: I spoke to Naboth, the Jezrahelite, and said to him: Give me thy vineyard, and take money for it: or if it please thee, I will give thee a better vineyard for it. And he said: I will not give thee my vineyard.€€ƒxØThen Jezabel, his wife, said to him. Thou art of great authority indeed, and governest well the kingdom of Israel. Arise, and eat bread, and be of good cheer; I will give thee the vineyard of Naboth, the Jezrahelite.€€ƒx¨So she wrote letters in Achab's name, and sealed them with his ring, and sent them to the ancients, and the chief men that were in his city, and that dwelt with Naboth.€€ƒxjAnd this was the tenor of the letters: Proclaim a fast, and make Naboth sit among the chief of the people;€€ƒx¶And suborn two men, sons of Belial, against him. and let them bear false witness; that he hath blasphemed God and the king: and then carry him out, and stone him, and so let him die.€€ƒx¶And the men of his city, the ancients and nobles, that dwelt with him in the city, did as Jezabel had commanded them, and as it was written in the letters which she had sent to them;€€ƒxJThey proclaimed a fast, and made Naboth sit among the chief of the people.€€ƒyAnd bringing two men, sons of the devil, they made them sit against him: and they, like men of the devil, bore witness against him before the people: saying: Naboth hath blasphemed God and the king. Wherefore they brought him forth without the city, and stoned him to death.€€ƒx@And they sent to Jezabel, saying: Naboth is stoned, and is dead.€€ƒyAnd it came to pass, when Jezabel heard that Naboth was stoned, and dead, that she said to Achab: Arise, and take possession of the vineyard of Naboth, the Jezrahelite, who would not agree with thee, and give it thee for money: for Naboth is not alive, but dead.€€ƒx˜And when Achab heard this, to wit, that Naboth was dead, he arose, and went down into the vineyard of Naboth, the Jezrahelite, to take possession of it.€€ƒx=And the word of the Lord came to Elias, the Thesbite, saying:€€ƒx‘Arise, and go down to meet Achab, king of Israel, who is in Samaria: behold he is going down to the vineyard of Naboth, to take possession of it:€€ƒyAnd thou shalt speak to him, saying: Thus saith the Lord: Thou hast slain: moreover also thou hast taken possession. And after these words thou shalt add: Thus saith the Lord: In this place, wherein the dogs have licked the blood of Naboth, they shall lick thy blood also.€€ƒxAnd Achab said to Elias: Hast thou found me thy enemy? He said: I have found thee because thou art sold, to do evil in the sight of the Lord.€€ƒx³Behold I will bring evil upon thee, and I will cut down thy posterity, and I will kill of Achab him that pisseth against the wall, and him that is shut up, and the last in Israel.€€ƒxÂAnd I will make thy house like the house of Jeroboam the son of Nabat, and like the house of Baasa the son of Ahias: for what thou hast done to provoke me to anger, and for making Israel to sin.€€ƒxaAnd of Jezabel also, the Lord spoke, saying: The dogs shall eat Jezabel in the field of Jezrahel.€€ƒxqIf Achab die in the city, the dogs shall eat him: but if he die in the field, the birds of the air shall eat him.€€ƒx~Now, there was not such another as Achab, who was sold to do evil in the sight of the Lord: for his wife, Jezabel, set him on,€€ƒxŸAnd he became abominable, insomuch that he followed the idols which the Amorrhites had made, whom the Lord destroyed before the face of the children of Israel.€€ƒx¥And when Achab had heard these words, he rent his garments, and put haircloth upon his flesh, and fasted, and slept in sackcloth, and walked with his head cast down.€€ƒx=And the word of the Lord came to Elias, the Thesbite, saying:€€ƒxÇHast thou not seen Achab humbled before me? therefore, because he hath humbled himself, for my sake, I will not bring the evil in his days, but in his son's days will I bring the evil upon his house.€€ƒ`€€ƒxBAnd there passed three years without war between Syria and Israel.€€ƒxOAnd in the third year, Josaphat, king of Juda, came down to the king of Israel.€€ƒx–(And the king of Israel said to his servants: Know ye not that Ramoth Galaad is ours, and we neglect to take it out of the hand of the king of Syria?)€€ƒxKAnd he said to Josaphat: Wilt thou come with me to battle to Ramoth Galaad?€€˜5ƒxäThen the king of Israel assembled the prophets, about four hundred men, and he said to them: Shall I go to Ramoth Galaad to fight, or shall I forbear? They answered: Go up, and the Lord will deliver it into the hand of the king.€€ƒxZAnd Josaphat said: Is there not here some prophet of the Lord, that we may inquire by him?€€ƒxæAnd the king of Israel said to Josaphat. There is one man left, by whom we may inquire of the Lord; Micheas, the son of Jemla: but I hate him, for he doth not prophecy good to me, but evil. And Josaphat said: Speak not so, O king.€€ƒxrThen the king of Israel called an eunuch, and said to him: Make haste, and bring hither Micheas, the son of Jemla.€€ƒxÆAnd the king of Israel, and Josaphat, king of Juda, sat each on his throne, clothed with royal robes, in a court, by the entrance of the gate of Samaria, and all the prophets prophesied before them.€€ƒx•And Sedecias, the son of Chanaana, made himself horns of iron, and said: Thus saith the Lord: With these shalt thou push Syria, till thou destroy it.€€ƒxAnd all the prophets prophesied in like manner, saying: Go up to Ramoth Galaad, and prosper, for the Lord will deliver it into the king's hands.€€ƒxİAnd the messenger that went to call Micheas, spoke to him, saying: Behold the words of the prophets with one mouth declare good things to the king: let thy word, therefore, be like to theirs, and speak that which is good.€€ƒxdBut Micheas said to him: As the Lord liveth, whatsoever the Lord shall say to me, that will I speak.€€ƒxÑSo he came to the king, and the king said to him: Micheas, shall we go to Ramoth Galaad to battle, or shall we forbear? He answered him: Go up, and prosper, and the Lord shall deliver it into the king's hands.€€ƒxƒBut the king said to him: I adjure thee again and again, that thou tell me nothing but that which is true, in the name of the Lord.€€ƒx¶And he said: I saw all Israel scattered upon the hills, like sheep that have no shepherd; and the Lord said: These have no master: let every man of them return to his house in peace.€€ƒxt(Then the king of Israel said to Josaphat: Did I not tell thee, that he prophesieth no good to me, but always evil?)€€ƒx¶And he added and said: Hear thou, therefore, the word of the Lord: I saw the Lord sitting on his throne, and all the army of heaven standing by him on the right hand and on the left:€€ƒx¤And the Lord said: Who shall deceive Achab, king of Israel, that he may go up, and fall at Ramoth Galaad? And one spoke words of this manner, and another otherwise.€€ƒx€And there came forth a spirit, and stood before the Lord, and said: I will deceive him. And the Lord said to him: By what means?€€ƒx©And he said: I will go forth, and be a lying spirit, in the mouth of all his prophets. And the Lord said: Thou shalt deceive him, and shalt prevail: go forth, and do so.€€ƒx•Now, therefore, behold the Lord hath given a lying spirit in the mouth of all thy prophets that are here, and the Lord hath spoken evil against thee.€€ƒx‘And Sedecias, the son of Chanaana, came, and struck Micheas on the cheek, and said: Hath then the spirit of the Lord left me, and spoken to thee?€€ƒxoAnd Micheas said: Thou shalt see in the day when thou shalt go into a chamber within a chamber to hide thyself.€€ƒxƒAnd the king of Israel said: Take Micheas and let him abide with Amon, the governor of the city, and with Joas, the son of Amalech;€€ƒxAnd tell them: Thus saith the king: Put this man in prison, and feed him with bread of affliction, and water of distress till I return in peace.€€ƒxlAnd Micheas said: If thou return in peace, the Lord hath not spoken by me. And he said: Hear, all ye people.€€ƒxLSo the king of Israel, and Josaphat, king of Juda, went up to Ramoth-Galaad.€€ƒx²And the king of Israel said to Josaphat: Take thy armour, and go into the battle, and put on thy own garments. But the king of Israel changed his dress, and went into the battle.€€ƒx®And the king of Syria had commanded the two and thirty captains of the chariots, saying: You shall not fight against any, small or great, but against the king of Israel only.€€ƒx°So when the captains of the chariots saw Josaphat, they suspected that he was the king of Israel, and making a violent assault, they fought against him: and Josaphat cried out.€€ƒxmAnd the captains of the chariots perceived that he was not the king of Israel, and they turned away from him.€€ƒx÷And a certain man bent his bow, shooting at a venture, and chanced to strike the king of Israel, between the lungs and the stomach. But he said to the driver of his chariot: Turn thy hand, and carry me out of the army, for I am grievously wounded.€€ƒxÂAnd the battle was fought that day, and the king of Israel stood in his chariot against the Syrians, and he died in the evening: and the blood ran out of the wound into the midst of the chariot.€€ƒx‰And the herald proclaimed through all the army, before the sun set, saying: Let every man return to his own city, and to his own country.€€ƒxUAnd the king died, and was carried into Samaria: and they buried the king in Samaria.€€ƒx¥And they washed his chariot in the pool of Samaria and the dogs licked up his blood, and they washed the reins according to the word of the Lord which he had spoken.€€ƒxĞBut the rest of the acts of Achab, and all that he did, and the house of ivory that he made, and all the cities that he built, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel?€€ƒxMSo Achab slept with his fathers; and Ochozias, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒxdBut Josaphat, the son of Asa, began to reign over Juda, in the fourth year of Acbab, king of Israel.€€ƒx¤He was five and thirty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned five and twenty years in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Azuba, the daughter of Salai.€€ƒx‡And he walked in all the way of Asa, his father, and he declined not from it: and he did that which was right in the sight of the Lord.€€ƒx}Nevertheless, he took not away the high places for as yet the people offered sacrifice, and burnt incense in the high places.€€ƒx/And Josaphat had peace with the king of Israel.€€ƒx¢But the rest of the acts of Josaphat, and his works which he did, and his battles, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Juda?€€ƒxmAnd the remnant also of the effeminate, who remained in the days of Asa, his father, he took out of the land.€€ƒx-And there was then no king appointed in Edom.€€ƒx†But king Josaphat made navies on the sea, to sail into Ophir for gold: but they could not go, for the ships were broken in Asiongaber.€€ƒx}Then Ochozias, the son of Achab, said to Josaphat: Let my servants go with thy servants in the ships. And Josaphat would not.€€ƒx‰And Josaphat slept with his fathers, and was buried with them in the city of David, his father: and Joram, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒx And Ochozias, the son of Achab, began to reign over Israel, in Samaria, in the seventeenth year of Josaphat, king of Juda, and he reigned over Israel two years.€€ƒx£And he did evil in the sight of the Lord, and walked in the way of his father and his mother, and in the way of Jeroboam, the son of Nabat, who made Israel to sin.€€ƒx}He served also Baal, and worshipped him, and provoked the Lord, the God of Israel, according to all that his father had done.€€ƒxThe Second Book of Kings€€ƒ`€€ƒx;And Moab rebelled against Israel, after the death of Achab.€€ƒxâAnd Ochozias fell through the lattices of his upper chamber, which he had in Samaria, and was sick: and he sent messengers, saying to them: Go, consult Beelzebub, the god of Accaron, whether I shall recover of this my illness.€€d2Kgs¤dnamehII Kingsiosis_named2Kgslabbreviationd2Kgshchapters˜’ƒxWAnd the messengers turned back to Ochozias. And he said to them: Why are you come back?€€ƒySBut they answered him: A man met us, and said to us: Go, and return to the king, that sent you, and you shall say to him: Thus saith the Lord: Is it because there was no God in Israel, that thou sendest to Beelzebub, the god of Accaron? Therefore thou shalt not come down from the bed, on which thou art gone up, but thou shalt surely die.€€ƒxRAnd he said to them: What manner of man was he who met you, and spoke these words?€€ƒxmBut they said: A hairy man, with a girdle of leather about his loins. And he said: It is Elias, the Thesbite.€€ƒxÙAnd he sent to him a captain of fifty, and the fifty men that were under him. And he went up to him, and as he was sitting on the top of a hill, he said to him: Man of God, the king hath commanded that thou come down.€€ƒxáAnd Elias answering, said to the captain of fifty: If I be a man of God, let fire come down from heaven and consume thee, and thy fifty. And there came down fire from heaven and consumed him, and the fifty that were with him.€€ƒxAnd he again sent to him another captain of fifty men, and his fifty with him. And he said to him: Man of God: Thus saith the king: Make haste and come down.€€ƒx­Elias answering, said: If I be a man of God, let fire come down from heaven, and consume thee, and thy fifty. And fire came down from heaven, and consumed him and his fifty.€€ƒxõAgain he sent a third captain of fifty men, and the fifty that were with him. And when he was come, he fell upon his knees before Elias, and besought him, and said: Man of God, despise not my life, and the lives of thy servants that are with me.€€ƒx¢Behold fire came down from heaven, and consumed the two first captains of fifty men, and the fifties that were with them: but now I beseech thee to spare my life.€€ƒx…And the angel of the Lord spoke to Elias, saying: Go down with him, fear not. He arose therefore, and went down with him to the king,€€ƒy+And said to him: Thus saith the Lord: Because thou hast sent messengers to consult Beelzebub, the god of Accaron, as though there were not a God in Israel, of whom thou mightest inquire the word; therefore, from the bed on which thou art gone up, thou shalt not come down, but thou shalt surely die.€€ƒxÆSo he died, according to the word of the Lord, which Elias spoke; and Joram, his brother, reigned in his stead, in the second year of Joram, the son of Josaphat, king of Juda, because he had no son.€€ƒx…But the rest of the acts of Ochozias, which he did, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel?€€ƒ`€€ƒxƒAnd it came to pass, when the Lord would take up Elias, into heaven, by a whirlwind, that Elias and Eliseus were going from Galgal.€€ƒxŞAnd Elias said to Eliseus: Stay thou here, because the Lord hath sent me as far as Bethel. And Eliseus said to him: As the Lord liveth, and as thy soul liveth, I will not leave thee. And when they were come down to Bethel,€€ƒxÕThe sons of the prophets, that were at Bethel, came forth to Eliseus, and said to him: Dost thou know that, this day, the Lord will take away thy master from thee? And he answered: I also know it: hold your peace.€€˜ƒxÌThe sons of the prophets, that were at Jericho, came to Eliseus, and said to him: Dost thou know that, this day, the Lord will take away thy master from thee? And he said: I also know it: hold your peace.€€ƒxÄAnd Elias said to him: Stay here, because the Lord hath sent me as far as the Jordan. And he said: as the Lord liveth, and as thy soul liveth, I will not leave thee. And they two went on together.€€ƒxAnd fifty men, of the sons of the prophets, followed them, and stood in sight, at a distance: but they two stood by the Jordan.€€ƒxœAnd Elias took his mantle, and folded it together, and struck the waters, and they were divided hither and thither, and they both passed over on dry ground.€€ƒxÈAnd when they were gone over, Elias said to Eliseus: Ask what thou wilt have me to do for thee, before I be taken away from thee. And Eliseus said: I beseech thee, that in me may be thy double spirit.€€ƒx¼And he answered: Thou hast asked a hard thing; nevertheless, if thou see me when I am taken from thee, thou shalt have what thou hast asked: but if thou see me not, thou shalt not have it.€€ƒx£And as they went on, walking and talking together, behold, a fiery chariot and fiery horses parted them both asunder: and Elias went up by a whirlwind into heaven.€€ƒx¿And Eliseus saw him, and cried: My father, my father, the chariot of Israel, and the driver thereof. And he saw him no more: and he took hold of his own garments, and rent them in two pieces.€€ƒxkAnd he took up the mantle of Elias, that fell from him: and going back, he stood on the bank of the Jordan;€€ƒxğAnd he struck the waters with the mantle of Elias, that had fallen from him, and they were not divided. And he said: Where is now the God of Elias? And he struck the waters, and they were divided hither and thither, and Eliseus passed over.€€ƒxÇAnd the sons of the prophets, at Jericho, who were over against him, seeing it, said: The spirit of Elias hath rested upon Eliseus. And coming to meet him, they worshipped him, falling to the ground.€€ƒx÷And they said to him: Behold, there are with thy servants, fifty strong men, that can go, and seek thy master, lest, perhaps, the spirit of the Lord, hath taken him up and cast him upon some mountain, or into some valley. And he said: Do not send.€€ƒx€But they pressed him, till he consented, and said: Send. And they sent fifty men: and they sought three days, but found him not.€€ƒxkAnd they came back to him: for he abode at Jericho, and he said to them: Did I not say to you? Do not send.€€ƒx§And the men of the city, said to Eliseus . Behold the situation of this city is very good, as thou, my lord, seest: but the waters are very bad, and the ground barren.€€ƒxWAnd he said: Bring me a new vessel, and put salt into it. And when they had brought it,€€ƒx¶He went out to the spring of the waters, and cast the salt into it, and said: Thus saith the Lord: I have healed these waters, and there shall be no more in them death or barrenness.€€ƒx[And the waters were healed unto this day, according to the word of Eliseus, which he spoke.€€ƒx¯And he went up from thence to Bethel: and as he was going up by the way, little boys came out of the city and mocked him, saying: Go up, thou bald head, go up, thou bald head.€€ƒxŸAnd looking back, he saw them, and cursed them in the name of the Lord: and there came forth two bears out of the forest, and tore of them, two and forty boys.€€ƒxPAnd from thence he went to mount Carmel, and from thence he returned to Samaria.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‹And Joram the son of Achab, reigned over Israel, in Samaria, in the eighteenth year of Josaphat, king of Juda. And he reigned twelve years.€€ƒx‰And he did evil before the Lord, but not like his father and his mother: for he took away the statues of Baal, which his father had made.€€ƒxvNevertheless, he stuck to the sins of Jeroboam, the son of Nabat, who made Israel to sin, nor did he depart from them.€€˜ƒxWAnd when Achab was dead, he broke the league which he had made with the king of Israel.€€ƒxGAnd king Joram went out that day from Samaria, and mustered all Israel.€€ƒxéAnd he sent to Josaphat; king of Juda, saying: The king of Moab is revolted from me: come with me against him to battle. And he answered: I will come up: he that is mine, is thine: my people are thy people: and my horses, thy horses.€€ƒxNAnd he said: Which way shall we go up? But he answered: By the desert of Edom.€€ƒxÇSo the king of Israel, and the king of Juda, and the king of Edom, went, and they fetched a compass of seven days journey, and there was no water for the army, and for the beasts, that followed them.€€ƒx„And the king of Israel said: Alas, alas, alas, the Lord hath gathered us three kings together, to deliver us into the hands of Moab.€€ƒxäAnd Josaphat said: Is there not here a prophet of the Lord, that we may beseech the Lord by him? And one of the servants of the king of Israel answered: Here is Eliseus, the son of Saphat, who poured water on the hands of Elias.€€ƒxAnd Josaphat said: The word of the Lord is with him. And the king of Israel, and Josaphat, king of Juda, and the king of Edom, went down to him.€€ƒxùAnd Eliseus said to the king of Israel: What have I to do with thee? go to the prophets of thy father, and thy mother. And the king of Israel said to him: Why hath the Lord gathered together these three kings, to deliver them into the hands of Moab?€€ƒxÀAnd Eliseus said to him: As the Lord of hosts liveth, in whose sight I stand, if I did not reverence the face of Josaphat, king of Juda, I would not have hearkened to thee, nor looked on thee.€€ƒxrBut now bring me hither a minstrel. And when the minstrel played, the hand of the Lord came upon him, and he said:€€ƒxFThus saith the Lord: Make the channel of this torrent full of ditches.€€ƒx©For thus saith the Lord: You shall not see wind, nor rain: and yet this channel shall be filled with waters, and you shall drink, you and your families, and your beasts.€€ƒxjAnd this is a small thing in the sight of the Lord: moreover, he will deliver, also, Moab into your hands.€€ƒxÈAnd you shall destroy every fenced city, and every choice city, and shall cut down every fruitful tree, and shall stop up all the springs of waters, and every goodly field you shall cover with stones.€€ƒxŸAnd it came to pass, in the morning, when the sacrifices used to be offered, that behold, water came by the way of Edom, and the country was filled with water.€€ƒx§And all the Moabites hearing that the kings were come up to fight against them, gathered together all that were girded with a belt upon them, and stood in the borders.€€ƒx™And they rose early in the morning, and the sun being now up, and shining upon the waters, the Moabites saw the waters over against them red, like blood,€€ƒx“And they said: It is the blood of the sword: the kings have fought among themselves, and they have killed one another: go now, Moab, to the spoils.€€ƒx‘And they went into the camp of Israel: but Israel rising up, defeated Moab, who fled before them. And they being conquerors, went and smote Moab.€€ƒy'And they destroyed the cities: And they filled every goodly field, every man casting his stone: and they stopt up all the springs of waters: and cut down all the trees that bore fruit, so that brick walls only remained: and the city was beset by the slingers, and a great part thereof destroyed.€€ƒxºAnd when the king of Moab saw this, to wit, that the enemies had prevailed, he took with him seven hundred men that drew the sword, to break in upon the king of Edom: but they could not.€€ƒxíThen he took his eldest son, that should have reigned in his stead, and offered him for a burnt offering upon the wall: and there was great indignation in Israel, and presently they departed from him, and returned into their own country.€€ƒ`€€ƒxïNow a certain woman of the wives of the prophets, cried to Eliseus, saying: Thy servant, my husband, is dead, and thou knowest that thy servant was one that feared God, and behold the creditor is come to take away my two sons to serve him.€€ƒx¾And Eliseus said to her: What wilt thou have me do for thee? Tell me, what hast thou in thy house? And she answered: I, thy handmaid, have nothing in my house but a little oil, to anoint me.€€ƒxNAnd he said to her: Go, borrow of all thy neighbours empty vessels, not a few.€€˜,ƒxrSo the woman went, and shut the door upon her, and upon her sons: they brought her the vessels, and she poured in.€€ƒxAnd when the vessels were full, she said to her son: Bring me yet a vessel. And he answered: I have no more. And the oil stood.€€ƒxƒAnd she came, and told the man of God. And he said: Go, sell the oil, and pay thy creditor: and thou and thy sons live of the rest.€€ƒx»And there was a day when Eliseus passed by Sunam: now there was a great woman there, who detained him to eat bread: and as he passed often that way, he turned into her house to eat bread.€€ƒx`And she said to her husband: I perceive that this is a holy man of God, who often passeth by us.€€ƒx¬Let us, therefore, make him a little chamber, and put a little bed in it for him, and a table, and a stool, and a candlestick, that when he cometh to us he may abide there.€€ƒxZNow, there was a certain day, when he came, and turned into the chamber, and rested there.€€ƒxnAnd he said to Giezi, his servant: Call this Sunamitess. And when he had called her, and she stood before him,€€ƒyHe said to his servant: Say to her: Behold, thou hast diligently served us in all things; what wilt thou have me to do for thee? Hast thou any business, and wilt thou, that I speak to the king, or to the general of the army? And she answered: I dwell in the midst of my own people.€€ƒx{And he said: What will she then that I do for her? And Giezi said: Do not ask, for she hath no son, and her husband is old.€€ƒxMThen he bid him call her. And when she was called, and stood before the door,€€ƒxËHe said to her: At this time, and this same hour, if life be in company, thou shalt have a son in thy womb. But she answered: Do not, I beseech thee, my lord, thou man of God, do not lie to thy handmaid.€€ƒxiAnd the woman conceived, and brought forth a son in the time, and at the same hour that Eliseus had said.€€ƒxXAnd the child grew. And on a certain day, when he went out to his father to the reapers,€€ƒxyHe said to his father: My head acheth, my head acheth. But he said to his servant. Take him, and carry him to his mother.€€ƒxqAnd when he had taken him, and brought him to his mother, she sat him on her knees, until noon, and then he died.€€ƒx_And she went up, and laid him upon the bed of the man of God, and shut the door: and going out,€€ƒx’She called her husband, and said: Send with me, I beseech thee, one of thy servants, and an ass, that I may run to the man of God, and come again.€€ƒxmAnd he said to her: Why dost thou go to him? to day is neither new moon nor sabbath. She answered: I will go.€€ƒx~And she saddled an ass, and commanded her servant: Drive, and make haste, make no stay in going: And do that which I bid thee.€€ƒx¨So she went forward, and came to the man of God, to mount Carmel: and when the man of God saw her coming towards, he said to Giezi, his servant: Behold that Sunamitess.€€ƒx‚Go, therefore, to meet her, and say to her: Is all well with thee, and with thy husband, and with thy son? And she answered: Well.€€ƒxèAnd when she came to the man of God, to the mount, she caught hold on his feet: and Giezi came to remove her. And the man of God said: Let her alone for her soul is in anguish, and the Lord hath hid it from me, and hath not told me.€€ƒxZAnd she said to him: Did I ask a son of my lord? did I not say to thee: Do not deceive me?€€ƒxÏThen he said to Giezi: Gird up thy loins, and take my staff in thy hand, and go. If any man meet thee, salute him not: and if any man salute thee, answer him not: and lay my staff upon the face of the child.€€ƒx‹But the mother of the child said: As the Lord liveth, and as thy soul liveth, I will not leave thee. He arose, therefore, and followed her.€€ƒx»But Giezi was gone before them, and laid the staff upon the face of the child, and there was no voice nor sense: and he returned to meet him, and told him, saying: The child is not risen.€€ƒxREliseus, therefore, went into the house, and behold the child lay dead on his bed:€€ƒxTAnd going in, he shut the door upon him, and upon the child, and prayed to the Lord.€€ƒxÃAnd he went up, and lay upon the child: and put his mouth upon his mouth, and his eyes upon his eyes, and his hands upon his hands: and he bowed himself upon him, and the child's flesh grew warm.€€ƒx’Then he returned and walked in the house, once to and fro: and he went up, and lay upon him: and the child gaped seven times, and opened his eyes.€€ƒxAnd he called Giezi, and said to him: Call this Sunamitess. And she being called, went in to him: and he said: Take up thy son.€€ƒxaShe came and fell at his feet, and worshipped upon the ground: and took up her son, and went out.€€ƒxÛAnd Eliseus returned to Galgal, and there was a famine in the land, and the sons of the prophets dwelt before him: And he said to one of his servants: Set on the great pot, and boil pottage for the sons of the prophets.€€ƒx÷And one went out into the field to gather wild herbs: and he found something like a wild vine, and gathered of it wild gourds of the field, and filled his mantle, and coming back, he shred them into the pot of pottage; for he knew not what it was.€€ƒx·And they poured it out for their companions to eat: and when they had tasted of the pottage, they cried out, saying: Death is in the pot, O man of God. And they could not eat thereof.€€ƒx¶But he said: Bring some meal. And when they had brought it, he cast it into the pot, and said: Pour out for the people, that they may eat. And there was now no bitterness in the pot.€€ƒxÅAnd a certain man came from Baalsalisa, bringing to the man of God, bread of the firstfruits, twenty loaves of barley, and new corn in his scrip. And he said: Give to the people, that they may eat.€€ƒxÒAnd his servant answered him: How much is this, that I should set it before a hundred men? He said again: Give to the people, that they may eat: for thus saith the Lord: They shall eat, and there shall be left.€€ƒx^So he set it before them: and they ate, and there was left, according to the word of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÃNaaman, general of the army, of the king of Syria, was a great man with his master, and honourable: for by him the Lord gave deliverance to Syria: and he was a valiant man, and rich, but a leper.€€ƒxNow there had gone out robbers from Syria, and had led away captive out of the land of Israel, a little maid, and she waited upon Naaman's wife.€€ƒxAnd she said to her mistress: I wish my master had been with the prophet that is in Samaria: he would certainly have healed him of the leprosy which he hath.€€˜ƒxÍAnd the king of Syria said to him: Go; and I will send a letter to the king of Israel. And he departed, and took with him ten talents of silver, and six thousand pieces of gold, and ten changes of raiment;€€ƒx½And brought the letter to the king of Israel, in these words: When thou shalt receive this letter, know that I have sent to thee Naaman, my servant, that thou mayst heal him of his leprosy.€€ƒxëAnd when the king of Israel had read the letter, he rent his garments, and said: Am I God, to be able to kill and give life, that this man hath sent to me to heal a man of his leprosy? mark, and see how he seeketh occasions against me.€€ƒxéAnd when Eliseus, the man of God, had heard this, to wit, that the king of Israel had rent his garments, he sent to him, saying: Why hast thou rent thy garments? let him come to me, and let him know that there is a prophet in Israel.€€ƒx[So Naaman came with his horses and chariots, and stood at the door of the house of Eliseus:€€ƒx‘And Eliseus sent a messenger to him, saying: Go, and wash seven times in the Jordan, and thy flesh shall recover health, and thou shalt be clean.€€ƒxÒNaaman was angry, and went away, saying: I thought he would have come out to me, and standing, would have invoked the name of the Lord his God, and touched with his hand the place of the leprosy, and healed me.€€ƒxÀAre not the Abana, and the Pharphar, rivers of Damascus, better than all the waters of Israel, that I may wash in them, and be made clean? So as he turned, and was going away with indignation,€€ƒxÔHis servants came to him, and said to him: Father, if the prophet had bid thee do some great thing, surely thou shouldst have done it: how much rather what he now hath said to thee: Wash, and thou shalt be clean?€€ƒx¶Then he went down, and washed in the Jordan seven times, according to the word of the man of God; and his flesh was restored, like the flesh of a little child: and he was made clean.€€ƒxæAnd returning to the man of God, with all his train, he came, and stood before him, and said: In truth, I know there is no other God, in all the earth, but only in Israel: I beseech thee, therefore, take a blessing of thy servant.€€ƒxyBut he answered: As the Lord liveth, before whom I stand, I will receive none. And when he pressed him, he still refused.€€ƒxÛAnd Naaman said: As thou wilt: but I beseech thee, grant to me, thy servant, to take from hence two mules' burden of earth: for thy servant will not henceforth offer holocaust, or victim, to other gods, but to the Lord.€€ƒy/But there is only this, for which thou shalt entreat the Lord for thy servant; when my master goeth into the temple of Remmon, to worship there, and he leaneth on my hand: if I bow down in the temple of Remmon, when he boweth down in the same place, that the Lord pardon me, thy servant, for this thing.€€ƒxZAnd he said to him: Go in peace. So he departed from him, in the spring time of the earth.€€ƒxÍBut Giezi, the servant of the man of God, said: My master hath spared Naaman this Syrian, in not receiving of him that which he brought: as the Lord liveth, I will run after him, and take something of him.€€ƒxŠAnd Giezi followed after Naaman: and when he saw him running after him, he leapt down from his chariot to meet him, and said: Is all well?€€ƒxÒAnd he said: Well: my master hath sent me to thee, saying: Just now there are come to me from mount Ephraim, two young men of the sons of the prophets: give them a talent of silver, and two changes of garments.€€ƒxàAnd Naaman said: It is better that thou take two talents. And he forced him, and bound two talents of silver in two bags, and two changes of garments, and laid them upon two of his servants, and they carried them before him.€€ƒx™And when he was come, and now it was the evening, he took them from their hands, and laid them up in the house, and sent the men away, and they departed.€€ƒxƒBut he went in, and stood before his master. And Eliseus said: Whence comest thou, Giezi? He answered: Thy servant went no whither.€€ƒxóBut he said: Was not my heart present, when the man turned back, from his chariot, to meet thee? So now thou hast received money, and received garments, to buy oliveyards and vineyards, and sheep, and oxen, and men-servants, and maid-servants.€€ƒx‚But the leprosy of Naaman, shall also stick to thee, and to thy seed for ever. And he went out from him a leper, as white as snow.€€ƒ`€€ƒxnAnd the sons of the prophets said to Eliseus: Behold, the place where we dwell with thee is too strait for us.€€ƒx–Let us go as far as the Jordan, and take out of the wood every man a piece of timber, that we may build us there a place to dwell in. And he said: Go.€€ƒxUAnd one of them said: But come thou also with thy servants. He answered: I will come.€€˜!ƒx°And it happened, as one was felling some timber, that the head of the ax fell into the water: and he cried out, and said: Alas, alas, alas, my lord, for this same was borrowed.€€ƒx”And the man of God said: Where did it fall? and he shewed him the place: Then he cut off a piece of wood, and cast it in thither: and the iron swam.€€ƒx>And he said: Take it up. And he put out his hand, and took it.€€ƒxˆAnd the king of Syria warred against Israel, and took counsel with his servants, saying: In such and such a place, let us lay an ambush.€€ƒx†And the man of God sent to the king of Israel, saying: Beware that thou pass not to such a place: for the Syrians are there in ambush.€€ƒx”And the king of Israel, sent to the place which the man of God had told him, and prevented him, and looked well to himself there not once nor twice.€€ƒx´And the heart of the king of Syria, was troubled for this thing. And calling together his servants, he said: Why do you not tell me who it is that betrays me to the king of Israel?€€ƒx¶And one of his servants said: No one, my lord, O king: but Eliseus, the prophet, that is in Israel, telleth the king of Israel all the words, that thou speakest in thy privy chamber.€€ƒx~And he said to them: Go, and see where he is: that I may send and take him. And they told him: saying: Behold he is in Dothan.€€ƒxyTherefore, he sent thither horses, and chariots, and the strength of an army: and they came by night, and beset the city.€€ƒx¾And the servant of the man of God, rising early went out, and saw an army round about the city, and horses and chariots: and he told him, saying: Alas, alas, alas, my lord, what shall we do?€€ƒxEBut he answered: Fear not: for there are more with us than with them.€€ƒxÓAnd Eliseus prayed, and said: Lord, open his eyes, that he may see. And the Lord opened the eyes of the servant, and he saw: and behold, the mountain was full of horses, and chariots of fire round about Eliseus.€€ƒxÈAnd the enemies came down to him: but Eliseus prayed to the Lord, saying: Strike, I beseech thee, this people with blindness: and the Lord struck them with blindness, according to the word of Eliseus.€€ƒx›And Eliseus said to them: This is not the way, neither is this the city: follow me, and I will shew you the man whom you seek. So he led them into Samaria.€€ƒx¿And when they were come into Samaria, Eliseus said: Lord, open the eyes of these men, that they may see. And the Lord opened their eyes, and they saw themselves to be in the midst of Samaria.€€ƒxWAnd the king of Israel said to Eliseus, when he saw them: My father, shall I kill them?€€ƒxÔAnd he said: Thou shalt not kill them: for thou didst not take them with thy sword, or thy bow, that thou mayst kill them: but set bread and water before them, that they may eat and drink, and go to their master.€€ƒxÂAnd a great provision of meats was set before them, and they ate and drank; and he let them go: and they went away to their master: and the robbers of Syria came no more into the land of Israel.€€ƒx‡And it came to pass, after these things, that Benadad, king of Syria, gathered together all his army, and went up and besieged Samaria.€€ƒxØAnd there was a great famine in Samaria: and so long did the siege continue, till the head of an ass was sold for fourscore pieces of silver, and the fourth part of a cabe of pigeons' dung, for five pieces of silver.€€ƒxvAnd as the king of Israel was passing by the wall, a certain woman cried out to him, saying: Save me, my lord, O king.€€ƒx®And he said: If the Lord doth not save thee, how can I save thee? out of the barnfloor, or out of the winepress? And the king said to her: What aileth thee? And she answered:€€ƒx`This woman said to me: Give thy son, that we may eat him today, and we will eat my son tomorrow.€€ƒxSo we boiled my son, and ate him. And I said to her on the next day: Give thy son, that we may eat him. And she hath hid her son.€€ƒx™When the king heard this, he rent his garments, and passed by upon the wall. And all the people saw the haircloth which he wore within next to his flesh.€€ƒx‹And the king said: May God do so and so to me, and may he add more, if the head of Eliseus, the son of Saphat, shall stand on him this day.€€ƒydBut Eliseus sat in his house, and the ancients sat with him. So he sent a man before: and before that messenger came, he said to the ancients: Do you know that this son of a murderer hath sent to cut off my head? Look then when the messenger shall come, shut the door, and suffer him not to come in: for behold the sound of his master's feet is behind him.€€ƒx³While he was yet speaking to them, the messenger appeared, who was coming to him. And he said: Behold, so great an evil is from the Lord: what shall I look for more from the Lord?€€ƒ`€€ƒxÒAnd Eliseus said: Hear ye the word of the Lord: Thus saith the Lord: Tomorrow, about this time, a bushel of fine flour shall be sold for a stater, and two bushels of barley for a stater, in the gate of Samaria.€€ƒx÷Then one of the lords, upon whose hand the king leaned, answering the man of God, said: If the Lord should make flood-gates in heaven, can that possibly be which thou sayest? And he said: Thou shalt see it with thy eyes, but shalt not eat thereof.€€ƒx€Now there were four lepers, at the entering in of the gate: and they said one to another: What mean we to stay here till we die?€€”ƒx–So they arose in the evening, to go to the Syrian camp. And when they were come to the first part of the camp of the Syrians, they found no man there.€€ƒyFor the Lord had made them hear, in the camp of Syria, the noise of chariots, and of horses, and of a very great army: and they said one to another: Behold, the king of Israel hath hired against us the kings of the Hethites, and of the Egyptians; and they are come upon us.€€ƒx–Wherefore they arose, and fled away in the dark, and left their tents, and their horses and asses in the camp, and fled, desiring to save their lives.€€ƒySo when these lepers were come to the beginning of the camp, they went into one tent, and ate and drank: and they took from thence silver, and gold, and raiment, and went, and hid it: and they came again, and went into another tent, and carried from thence in like manner, and hid it.€€ƒxàThen they said one to another: We do not well: for this is a day of good tidings. If we hold our peace, and do not tell it till the morning, we shall be charged with a crime: come, let us go, and tell it in the king's court.€€ƒx¯So they came to the gate of the city, and told them, saying: We went to the camp of the Syrians, and we found no man there, but horses, and asses tied, and the tents standing.€€ƒxJThen the guards of the gate went, and told it within in the king's palace.€€ƒy5And he arose in the night, and said to his servants: I tell you what the Syrians have done to us: They know that we suffer great famine, and therefore they are gone out of the camp, and lie hid in the fields, saying: When they come out of the city, we shall take them alive, and then we may get into the city.€€ƒxÎAnd one of his servants answered: Let us take the five horses that are remaining in the city (because there are no more in the whole multitude of Israel, for the rest are consumed), and let us send and see.€€ƒxgThey brought therefore two horses, and the king sent into the camp of the Syrians, saying: Go, and see.€€ƒxÌAnd they went after them, as far as the Jordan: and behold, all the way was full of garments, and vessels, which the Syrians had cast away, in their fright, and the messengers returned, and told the king.€€ƒx¸And the people going out, pillaged the camp of the Syrians: and a bushel of fine flour was sold for a stater, and two bushels of barley for a stater, according to the word of the Lord.€€ƒxÒAnd the king appointed that lord on whose hand he leaned, to stand at the gate: and the people trod upon him in the entrance of the gate; and he died, as the man of God had said, when the king came down to him.€€ƒxñAnd it came to pass, according to the word of the man of God, which he spoke to the king, when he said: Two bushels of barley shall be for a stater, and a bushel of fine flour for a stater, at this very time tomorrow, in the gate of Samaria.€€ƒxáWhen that lord answered the man of God, and said: Although the Lord should make flood-gates in heaven, could this come to pass which thou sayest? And he said to him: Thou shalt see it with thy eyes, and shalt not eat thereof.€€ƒxeAnd so it fell out to him, as it was foretold, and the people trod upon him in the gate, and he died.€€ƒ`€€ƒxêAnd Eliseus spoke to the woman, whose son he had restored to life, saying: Arise, and go thou, and thy household, and sojourn wheresoever thou canst find: for the Lord hath called a famine, and it shall come upon the land seven years.€€ƒx•And she arose, and did according to the word of the man of God: and going with her household, she sojourned in the land of the Philistines many days.€€ƒx¤And when the seven years were ended, the woman returned out of the land of the Philistines, and she went forth to speak to the king for her house and for her lands.€€˜ƒyAnd when he was telling the king how he had raised one dead to life, the woman appeared, whose son he had restored to life, crying to the king for her house, and her lands. And Giezi said: My lord, O king, this is the woman, and this is her son, whom Eliseus raised to life.€€ƒxÓAnd the king asked the woman: and she told him. And the king appointed her an eunuch, saying: Restore her all that is hers, and all the revenues of the lands, from the day that she left the land to this present.€€ƒx}Eliseus also came to Damascus, and Benadad, king of Syria was sick; and they told him, saying: The man of God is come hither.€€ƒx›And the king said to Hazael: Take with thee presents, and go to meet the man of God, and consult the Lord by him, saying: Can I recover of this my illness?€€ƒyAnd Hazael went to meet him, taking with him presents, and all the good things of Damascus, the burdens of forty camels. And when he stood before him, he said: Thy son, Benadad, the king of Syria, hath sent me to thee, saying: Can I recover of this my illness?€€ƒxoAnd Eliseus said to him: Go tell him: Thou shalt recover: but the Lord hath shewed me that he shall surely die.€€ƒxTAnd he stood with him, and was troubled so far as to blush: and the man of God wept.€€ƒy%And Hazael said to him: Why doth my lord weep? And he said: Because I know the evil that thou wilt do to the children of Israel. Their strong cities thou wilt burn with fire, and their young men thou wilt kill with the sword, and thou wilt dash their children, and rip up their pregnant women.€€ƒx¢And Hazael said: But what am I, thy servant, a dog, that I should do this great thing? And Eliseus said: The Lord hath shewed me that thou shalt be king of Syria.€€ƒx™And when he was departed from Eliseus he came to his master, who said to him: What said Eliseus to thee? And he answered: He told me: Thou shalt recover.€€ƒxŠAnd on the next day, he took a blanket, and poured water on it, and spread it upon his face: and he died, and Hazael reigned in his stead.€€ƒx†In the fifth year of Joram, son of Achab, king of Israel, and of Josaphat, king of Juda, reigned Joram, son of Josaphat, king of Juda.€€ƒx`He was two and thirty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned eight years in Jerusalem.€€ƒx´And he walked in the ways of the kings of Israel, as the house of Achab had walked: for the daughter of Achab was his wife: and he did that which was evil in the sight of the Lord.€€ƒx‹But the Lord would not destroy Juda, for David his servant's sake, as he had promised him, to give him a light, and to his children always.€€ƒxLIn his days Edom revolted from being under Juda, and made themselves a king.€€ƒxÍAnd Joram came to Seira, and all the chariots with him: and he arose in the night, and defeated the Edomites that had surrounded him, and the captains of the chariots, but the people fled into their tents.€€ƒxaSo Edom revolted from being under Juda, unto this day. Then Lobna also revolted at the same time.€€ƒx‡But the rest of the acts of Joram, and all that he did, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Juda?€€ƒx}And Joram slept with his fathers, and was buried with them in the city of David, and Ochozias, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒxmIn the twelfth year of Joram, the son of Achab, king of Israel, reigned Ochozias, son of Joram, king of Juda.€€ƒx«Ochozias was two and twenty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned one year in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Athalia the daughter of Amri king of Israel.€€ƒxAnd he walked in the ways of the house of Achab: and he did evil before the Lord, as did the house of Achab: for he was the son in law of the house of Achab.€€ƒxHe went also with Joram, son of Achab, to fight against Hazael, king of Syria, in Ramoth Galaad, and the Syrians wounded Joram:€€ƒy	And he went back to be healed, in Jezrahel: because the Syrians had wounded him in Ramoth, when he fought against Hazael, king of Syria And Ochozias, the son of Joram, king of Juda, went down to visit Joram, the son of Achab, in Jezrahel, because he was sick there.€€ƒ`€€ƒx­And Eliseus the prophet, called one of the sons of the prophets, and said to him: Gird up thy loins, and take this little bottle of oil in thy hand, and go to Ramoth Galaad.€€ƒxÃAnd when thou art come thither, thou shalt see Jehu the son of Josaphat the son of Namsi: and going in, thou shalt make him rise up from amongst his brethren, and carry him into an inner chamber.€€ƒxÑThen taking the little bottle of oil, thou shalt pour it on his head, and shalt say: Thus saith the Lord: I have anointed thee king over Israel. And thou shalt open the door and flee, and shalt not stay there.€€˜%ƒx¹And went in thither: and behold, the captains of the army were sitting, and he said: I have a word to thee, O prince. And Jehu said: Unto whom of us all? And he said: To thee, O prince.€€ƒx¹And he arose, and went into the chamber: and he poured the oil upon his head, and said: Thus saith the Lord God of Israel: I have anointed thee king over Israel, the people of the Lord.€€ƒx¸And thou shalt cut off the house of Achab, thy master, and I will revenge the blood of my servants, the prophets, and the blood of all the servants of the Lord, at the hand of Jezabel.€€ƒx And I will destroy all the house of Achab, and I will cut off from Achab, him that pisseth against the wall, and him that is shut up, and the meanest in Israel.€€ƒx€And I will make the house of Achab, like the house of Jeroboam, the son of Nabat, and like the house of Baasa, the son of Ahias.€€ƒxAnd the dogs shall eat Jezabel, in the field of Jezrahel, and there shall be no one to bury her. And he opened the door and fled.€€ƒx³Then Jehu went forth to the servants of his Lord: and they said to him: Are all things well? why came this madman to thee? And he said to them: You know the man, and what he said.€€ƒx»But they answered: It is false; but rather do thou tell us. And he said to them: Thus and thus did he speak to me: and he said: Thus saith the Lord: I have anointed thee king over Israel.€€ƒxªThen they made haste, and taking every man his garment, laid it under his feet, after the manner of a judgment seat, and they sounded the trumpet, and said: Jehu is king.€€ƒx§So Jehu, the son of Josaphat, the son of Namsi, conspired against Joram. Now Joram had besieged Ramoth Galaad, he, and all Israel, fighting with Hazael, king of Syria:€€ƒxôAnd was returned to be healed in Jezrahel of his wounds; for the Syrians had wounded him, when he fought with Hazael, king of Syria. And Jehu said: If it please you, let no man go forth or flee out of the city, lest he go, and tell in Jezrahel.€€ƒxxAnd he got up, and went into Jezrahel for Joram was sick there, and Ochozias king of Juda, was come down to visit Joram.€€ƒxÕThe watchman therefore, that stood upon the tower of Jezrahel, saw the troop of Jehu coming, and said: I see a troop. And Joram said: Take a chariot, and send to meet them, and let him that goeth say: Is all well?€€ƒxşSo there went one in a chariot to meet him, and said: Thus saith the king: Are all things peaceable? And Jehu said: What hast thou to do with peace? go behind and follow me. And the watchman told, saying: The messenger came to them, but he returneth not.€€ƒx°And he sent a second chariot of horses: and he came to them, and said: Thus saith the king: Is there peace? And Jehu said: What hast thou to do with peace? pass, and follow me.€€ƒxŸAnd the watchman told, saying: He came even to them, but returneth not: and the driving is like the driving of Jehu, the son of Namsi; for he drives furiously.€€ƒxóAnd Joram said: Make ready the chariot. And they made ready his chariot: and Joram, king of Israel, and Ochozias, king of Juda, went out, each in his chariot, and they went out to meet Jehu, and met him in the field of Naboth, the Jezrahelite.€€ƒxµAnd when Joram saw Jehu, he said: Is there peace, Jehu? And he answered: What peace? so long as the fornications of Jezabel, thy mother, and her many sorceries, are in their vigour.€€ƒxWAnd Joram turned his hand, and fleeing, said to Ochozias: There is treachery, Ochozias.€€ƒxœBut Jehu bent his bow with his hand, and shot Joram between the shoulders: and the arrow went out through his heart, and immediately he fell in his chariot.€€ƒxóAnd Jehu said to Badacer, his captain: Take him, and cast him into the field of Naboth, the Jezrahelite: for I remember, when I and thou, sitting in a chariot, followed Achab, this man's father, that the Lord laid this burden upon him, saying:€€ƒxëIf I do not requite thee in this field, saith the Lord, for the blood of Naboth, and for the blood of his children, which I saw yesterday, saith the Lord. So now take him, and cast him into the field, according to the word of the Lord.€€ƒxúBut Ochozias, king of Juda, seeing this, fled by the way of the garden house: and Jehu pursued him, and said: Strike him also in his chariot. And they struck him in the going up to Gaver, which is by Jeblaam: and he fled into Mageddo, and died there.€€ƒx–And his servants laid him upon his chariot, and carried him to Jerusalem: and they buried him in his sepulchre with his fathers, in the city of David.€€ƒxLIn the eleventh year of Joram, the son of Achab, Ochozias reigned over Juda;€€ƒx™And Jehu came into Jezrahel. But Jezabel, hearing of his coming in, painted her face with stibic stone, and adorned her head, and looked out of a window.€€ƒxdAt Jehu coming in at the gate, and said: Can there be peace for Zambri, that hath killed his master?€€ƒxmAnd Jehu lifted up his face to the window, and said: Who is this? And two or three eunuchs bowed down to him.€€ƒxœAnd he said to them: Throw her down headlong; And they threw her down, and the wall was sprinkled with her blood, and the hoofs of the horses trod upon her.€€ƒx‹And when he was come in to eat, and to drink, he said: Go, and see after that cursed woman, and bury her; because she is a king's daughter.€€ƒxqAnd when they went to bury her, they found nothing but the skull, and the feet, and the extremities of her hands.€€ƒxÆAnd coming back they told him. And Jehu said: It is the word of the Lord, which he spoke by his servant Elias, the Thesbite, saying: In the field of Jezrahel the dogs shall eat the flesh of Jezabel.€€ƒxAnd the flesh of Jezabel shall be as dung upon the face of the earth in the field of Jezrahel; so that they who pass by shall say: Is this that same Jezabel?€€ƒ`€€ƒx»And Achab had seventy sons in Samaria: so Jehu wrote letters, and sent to Samaria, to the chief men of the city, and to the ancients, and to them that brought up Achab's children, saying:€€ƒxAs soon as you receive these letters, ye that have your master's sons, and chariots, and horses, and fenced cities, and armour,€€ƒx–Choose the best, and him that shall please you most of your master's sons, and set him on his father's throne, and fight for the house of your master.€€˜$ƒyTherefore they that were over the king's house, and the rulers of the city, and the ancients, and the bringers up of the children, sent to Jehu, saying: We are thy servants: whatsoever thou shalt command us we will do; we will not make us any king: do thou all that pleaseth thee.€€ƒyAnd he wrote letters the second time to them, saying: If you be mine, and will obey me, take the heads of the sons of your master, and come to me to Jezrahel by tomorrow at this time. Now the king's sons, being seventy men, were brought up with the chief men of the city.€€ƒx™And when the letters came to them, they took the king's sons, and slew seventy persons, and put their heads in baskets, and sent them to him to Jezrahel.€€ƒx³And a messenger came, and told him, saying: They have brought the heads of the king's sons. And he said: Lay ye them in two heaps by the entering in of the gate until the morning.€€ƒx¡And when it was light, he went out, and standing, said to all the people: You are just: if I conspired against my master, and slew him; who hath slain all these?€€ƒxÙSee therefore now that there hath not fallen to the ground any of the words of the Lord, which the Lord spoke concerning the house of Achab, and the Lord hath done that which he spoke in the hand of his servant Elias.€€ƒx£So Jehu slew all that were left of the house of Achab in Jezrahel, and all his chief men, and his friends, and his priests, till there were no remains left of him.€€ƒx[And he arose, and went to Samaria: and when he was come to the shepherds' cabin in the way,€€ƒxÖHe met with the brethren of Ochozias, king of Juda, and he said to them: Who are you? And they answered: We are the brethren of Ochozias, and are come down to salute the sons of the king, and the sons of the queen.€€ƒxAnd he said: Take them alive. And they took them alive, and killed them at the pit by the cabin, two and forty men, and he left not any of them.€€ƒy5And when he was departed thence, he found Jonadab, the son of Rechab, coming to meet him, and he blessed him. And he said to him: Is thy heart right as my heart is with thy heart? And Jonadab said: It is. If it be, said he, give me thy hand. He gave him his hand. And he lifted him up to him into the chariot,€€ƒx`And said to him: Come with me, and see my zeal for the Lord. So he made him ride in his chariot,€€ƒx—And brought him into Samaria. And he slew all that were left of Achab, in Samaria, to a man, according to the word of the Lord which he spoke by Elias.€€ƒxyAnd Jehu gathered together all the people, and said to them: Achab worshipped Baal a little, but I will worship him more.€€ƒyNow therefore call to me all the prophets of Baal, and all his servants, and all his priests: let none be wanting, for I have a great sacrifice to offer to Baal: whosoever shall be wanting, shall not live. Now Jehu did this craftily, that he might destroy the worshippers of Baal.€€ƒx9And he said: Proclaim a festival for Baal. And he called,€€ƒxÜAnd he sent into all the borders of Israel; and all the servants of Baal came: there was not one left that did not come. And they went into the temple of Baal: and the house of Baal was filled, from one end to the other.€€ƒx‰And he said to them that were over the wardrobe: Bring forth garments for all the servants of Baal. And they brought them forth garments.€€ƒxãAnd Jehu, and Jonadab, the son of Rechab, went to the temple of Baal, and said to the worshippers of Baal: Search, and see that there be not any with you of the servants of the Lord, but that there be the servants of Baal only.€€ƒxïAnd they went in to offer sacrifices and burnt offerings: but Jehu had prepared him fourscore men without, and said to them: If any of the men escape, whom I have brought into your hands, he that letteth him go, shall answer life for life.€€ƒyAnd it came to pass, when the burnt offering was ended, that Jehu commanded his soldiers and captains, saying: Go in, and kill them: let none escape. And the soldiers and captains slew them with the edge of the sword, and cast them out: and they went into the city of the temple of Baal,€€ƒx:And brought the statue out of Baal's temple, and burnt it,€€ƒxlAnd broke it in pieces. They destroyed also the temple of Baal, and made a jakes in its place unto this day.€€ƒx%So Jehu destroyed Baal out of Israel:€€ƒxŸBut yet he departed not from the sins of Jeroboam, the son of Nabat, who made Israel to sin, nor did he forsake the golden calves that were in Bethel, and Dan.€€ƒyAnd the Lord said to Jehu: because thou hast diligently executed that which was right and pleasing in my eyes, and hast done to the house of Achab according to all that was in my heart: thy children shall sit upon the throne of Israel to the fourth generation.€€ƒx§But Jehu took no heed to walk in the law of the Lord, the God of Israel, with all his heart: for he departed not from the sins of Jeroboam, who had made Israel to sin.€€ƒxhIn those days the Lord began to be weary of Israel: and Hazael ravaged them in all the coasts of Israel,€€ƒx—From the Jordan eastward, all the land of Galaad, and Gad, and Ruben, and Manasses, from Aroer, which is upon the torrent Arnon, and Galaad, and Basan.€€ƒxšBut the rest of the acts of Jehu, and all that he did, and his strength, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel?€€ƒxlAnd Jehu slept with his fathers, and they buried him in Samaria: and Joachaz, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒxSAnd the time that Jehu reigned over Israel, in Samaria, was eight and twenty years.€€ƒ`€€ƒxeNow Athalia, the mother of Ochozias, seeing that her son was dead, arose and slew all the royal seed.€€ƒxúBut Josaba the daughter of king Joram, sister of Ochozias, took Joas, the son of Ochozias, and stole him from among the king's sons that were slain, out of the bedchamber with his nurse: and hid him from the face of Athalia; so that he was not slain.€€ƒx_And he was with her six years, hid in the house of the Lord. And Athalia reigned over the land.€€•ƒxBAnd he commanded them, saying: This is the thing that you must do.€€ƒyLet a third part of you go in on the sabbath, and keep the watch of the king's house. And let a third part be at the gate of Sur; and let a third part be at the gate behind the dwelling of the shieldbearers; and you shall keep the watch of the house of Messa.€€ƒxrBut let two parts of you all that go forth on the sabbath, keep the watch of the house of the Lord about the king.€€ƒxÆAnd you shall compass him round about, having weapons in your hands: and if any man shall enter the precinct of the temple, let him be slain: and you shall be with the king, coming in and going out.€€ƒxÛAnd the centurions did according to all things that Joiada the priest, had commanded them: and taking every one their men, that went in on the sabbath, with them that went out in the sabbath, came to Joiada, the priest.€€ƒx]And he gave them the spears, and the arms of king David, which were in the house of the Lord.€€ƒx¥And they stood, having every one their weapons in their hands, from the right side of the temple, unto the left side of the altar, and of the temple, about the king.€€ƒx¶And he brought forth the king's son, and put the diadem upon him, and the testimony: and they made him king, and anointed him: and clapping their hands, they said: God save the king.€€ƒxjAnd Athalia heard the noise of the people running: and going in to the people into the temple of the Lord,€€ƒxğShe saw the king standing upon a tribunal, as the manner was, and the singers, and the trumpets near him, and all the people of the land rejoicing, and sounding the trumpets: and she rent her garments, and cried: A conspiracy, a conspiracy.€€ƒyBut Joiada commanded the centurions that were over the army, and said to them: Have her forth without the precinct of the temple, and whosoever shall follow her, let him be slain with the sword. For the priest had said: Let her not be slain in the temple of the Lord.€€ƒx|And they laid hands on her: and thrust her out by the way by which the horses go in, by the palace, and she was slain there.€€ƒx›And Joiada made a covenant between the Lord, and the king, and the people, that they should be the people of the Lord; and between the king and the people.€€ƒxöAnd all the people of the land went into the temple of Baal, and broke down his altars, and his images they broke in pieces thoroughly: they slew also Mathan the priest of Baal before the altar. And the priest set guards in the house of the Lord.€€ƒyAnd he took the centurions, and the bands of the Cerethi, and the Phelethi, and all the people of the land, and they brought the king from the house of the Lord: and they came by the way of the gate of the shieldbearers into the palace, and he sat on the throne of the kings.€€ƒxzAnd all the people of the land rejoiced, and the city was quiet: but Athalia was slain with the sword in the king's house.€€ƒx4Now Joas was seven years old when he began to reign.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‰In the seventh year of Jehu, Joas began to reign: and he reigned forty years in Jerusalem. The name of his mother was Sebia, of Bersabee.€€ƒxcAnd Joas did that which was right before the Lord all the days that Joiada, the priest, taught him.€€ƒxoBut yet he took not away the high places: for the people still sacrificed and burnt incense in the high places.€€•ƒxƒLet the priests take it according to their order and repair the house, wheresoever they shall see any thing that wanteth repairing.€€ƒxfNow till the three and twentieth year of king Joas the priests did not make the repairs of the temple.€€ƒxÜAnd king Joas called Joiada, the high priest, and the priests, saying to them: Why do you not repair the temple? Take you, therefore, money no more according to your order, but restore it for the repairing of the temple.€€ƒxjAnd the priests were forbidden to take any more money of the people, and to make the repairs of the house.€€ƒy
And Joiada, the high priest, took a chest, and bored a hole in the top, and set it by the altar at the right hand of them that came into the house of the Lord; and the priests that kept the doors, put therein all the money that was brought to the temple of the Lord.€€ƒxÁAnd when they saw that there was very much money in the chest, the king's scribe, and the high priest, came up, and poured it out, and counted the money that was found in the house of the Lord.€€ƒxÖAnd they gave it out by number and measure into the hands of them that were over the builders of the house of the Lord: and they laid it out to the carpenters, and the masons, that wrought in the house of the Lord,€€ƒxåAnd made the repairs: and to them that cut stones, and to buy timber, and stones to be hewed, that the repairs of the house of the Lord might be completely finished, and wheresoever there was need of expenses to uphold the house.€€ƒxÑBut there were not made of the same money for the temple of the Lord, bowls, or fleshhooks, or censers, or trumpets, or any vessel of gold and silver, of the money that was brought into the temple of the Lord:€€ƒxZFor it was given to them that did the work, that the temple of the Lord might be repaired.€€ƒx|And they reckoned not with the men that received the money to distribute it to the workmen, but they bestowed it faithfully.€€ƒxBut the money for trespass, and the money for sins, they brought not into the temple of the Lord, because it was for the priests.€€ƒxrThen Hazael, king of Syria, went up, and fought against Geth, and took it, and set his face to go up to Jerusalem.€€ƒywWherefore Joas, king of Juda, took all the sanctified things, which Josaphat, and Joram, and Ochozias, his fathers, the kings of Juda, had dedicated to holy uses, and which he himself had offered: and all the silver that could be found in the treasures of the temple of the Lord, and in the king's palace: and sent it to Hazael, king of Syria, and he went off from Jerusalem.€€ƒx†And the rest of the acts of Joas, and all that he did, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Juda?€€ƒxvAnd his servants arose, and conspired among themselves, and slew Joas, in the house of Mello, in the descent of Sella.€€ƒxÊFor Josachar the son of Semaath, and Jozabad the son of Somer his servant, struck him, and he died: and they buried him with his fathers in the city of David; and Amasias, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒx’In the three and twentieth year of Joas son of Ochozias, king of Juda, Joachaz, the son of Jehu, reigned over Israel, in Samaria, seventeen years.€€ƒxŒAnd he did evil before the Lord, and followed the sins of Jeroboam, the son of Nabat, who made Israel to sin; and he departed not from them.€€ƒx²And the wrath of the Lord was kindled against Israel, and he delivered them into the hand of Hazael, the king of Syria, and into the hand of Benadad, the son of Hazael, all days.€€˜ƒx¶And the Lord gave Israel a saviour, and they were delivered out of the hand of the king of Syria: and the children of Israel dwelt in their pavilions as yesterday and the day before.€€ƒxŸBut yet they departed not from the sins of the house of Jeroboam, who made Israel to sin, but walked in them: and there still remained a grove also in Samaria.€€ƒxÑAnd Joachaz had no more left of the people than fifty horsemen, and ten chariots, and ten thousand footmen: for the king of Syria had slain them, and had brought them low as dust by threshing in the barnfloor.€€ƒx›But the rest of the acts of Joachaz, and all that he did, and his valour, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel?€€ƒxlAnd Joachaz slept with his fathers, and they buried him in Samaria: and Joas, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒx~In the seven and thirtieth year of Joas, king of Juda, Joas the son of Joachaz reigned over Israel, in Samaria, sixteen years.€€ƒx§And he did that which is evil in the sight of the Lord: he departed not from all the sins of Jeroboam, the son of Nabat, who made Israel to sin; but he walked in them.€€ƒxÊBut the rest of the acts of Joas, and all that he did, and his valour wherewith he fought against Amasias, king of Juda, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel?€€ƒx|And Joas slept with his fathers; and Jeroboam sat upon his throne. But Joas was buried in Samaria, with the kings of Israel.€€ƒxÆNow Eliseus was sick of the illness whereof he died: and Joas, king of Israel, went down to him, and wept before him, and said: O my father, my father, the chariot of Israel, and the guider thereof.€€ƒx^And Eliseus said to him: Bring a bow and arrows. And when he had brought him a bow and arrows,€€ƒx„He said to the king of Israel: Put thy hand upon the bow. And when he had put his hand, Eliseus put his hands over the king's hands,€€ƒyAnd said: Open the window to the east. And when he had opened it, Eliseus said: Shoot an arrow. And he shot. And Eliseus said: The arrow of the Lord's deliverance, and the arrow of the deliverance from Syria: and thou shalt strike the Syrians in Aphec, till thou consume them.€€ƒx›And he said: Take the arrows. And when he had taken them, he said to him: Strike with an arrow upon the ground. And he struck three times, and stood still.€€ƒx¿And the man of God was angry with him, and said: If thou hadst smitten five or six or seven times, thou hadst smitten Syria even to utter destruction: but now three times shalt thou smite it.€€ƒxaAnd Eliseus died, and they buried him. And the rovers from Moab came into the land the same year.€€ƒx¾And some that were burying a man, saw the rovers, and cast the body into the sepulchre of Eliseus. And when it had touched the bones of Eliseus, the man came to life and stood upon his feet.€€ƒxDNow Hazael, king of Syria, afflicted Israel all the days of Joachaz.€€ƒx×And the Lord had mercy on them, and returned to them, because of his covenant, which he had made with Abraham, and Isaac, and Jacob: and he would not destroy them, nor utterly cast them away, unto this present time.€€ƒxLAnd Hazael, king of Syria, died; and Benadad, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒxãNow Joas the son of Joachaz, took the cities out of the hand of Benadad, the son of Hazael, which he had taken out of the hand of Joachaz, his father, by war; three times did Joas beat him, and he restored the cities to Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxeIn the second year of Joas son of Joachaz, king of Israel, reigned Amasias son of Joas, king of Juda.€€ƒxœHe was five and twenty years old when he began to reign; and nine and twenty years he reigned in Jerusalem; the name of his mother was Joadan, of Jerusalem.€€ƒxAnd he did that which was right before the Lord, but yet not like David his father. He did according to all things that Joas his father, did:€€˜ƒxlAnd when he had possession of the kingdom, he put his servants to death that had slain the king, his father.€€ƒy?But the children of the murderers he did not put to death, according to that which is written in the book of the law of Moses, wherein the Lord commanded, saying: The fathers shall not be put to death for the children, neither shall the children be put to death for the fathers: but every man shall die for his own sin.€€ƒxHe slew of Edom in the valley of the Saltpits, ten thousand men, and took the rock by war, and called the name thereof Jectehel, unto this day.€€ƒxxThen Amasias sent messengers to Joas, son of Joachaz, son of Jehu, king of Israel, saying: Come, let us see one another.€€ƒy	And Joas, king of Israel, sent again to Amasias, king of Juda, saying: A thistle of Libanus sent to a cedar tree, which is in Libanus, saying: Give thy daughter to my son to wife. And the beasts of the forest, that are in Libanus, passed, and trod down the thistle.€€ƒx¿Thou hast beaten and prevailed over Edom, and thy heart hath lifted thee up; be content with this glory, and sit at home; why provokest thou evil, that thou shouldst fall, and Juda with thee?€€ƒx•But Amasias did not rest satisfied. So Joas, king of Israel, went up; and he and Amasias, king of Juda, saw one another in Bethsames, a town in Juda.€€ƒxXAnd Juda was put to the worse before Israel, and they fled every man to their dwellings.€€ƒxûBut Joas, king of Israel, took Amasias, king of Juda, the son of Joas, the son of Ochozias, in Bethsames, and brought him into Jerusalem; and he broke down the wall of Jerusalem, from the gate of Ephraim to the gate of the corner, four hundred cubits.€€ƒx¦And he took all the gold and silver, and all the vessels that were found in the house of the Lord, and in the king's treasures, and hostages, and returned to Samaria.€€ƒxÄBut the rest of the acts of Joas, which he did, and his valour, wherewith he fought against Amasias, king of Juda, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel?€€ƒx‚And Joas slept with his fathers, and was buried in Samaria, with the kings of Israel: and Jeroboam, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒxyAnd Amasias, the son of Joas, king of Juda, lived after the death of Joas, son of Joachaz, king of Israel, fifteen years.€€ƒxtAnd the rest of the acts of Amasias, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Juda?€€ƒx„Now they made a conspiracy against him in Jerusalem: and he fled to Lachis. And they sent after him to Lachis, and killed him there.€€ƒxmAnd they brought him away upon horses, and he was buried in Jerusalem with his fathers, in the city of David.€€ƒxuAnd all the people of Juda took Azarias, who was sixteen years old, and made him king instead of his father, Amasias.€€ƒxTHe built Elath, and restored it to Juda, after that the king slept with his fathers.€€ƒxIn the fifteenth year of Amasias, son of Joas, king of Juda, reigned Jeroboam, the son of Joas, king of Israel, in Samaria, one and forty years:€€ƒx‡And he did that which is evil before the Lord. He departed not from all the sins of Jeroboam, the son of Nabat, who made Israel to sin.€€ƒxıHe restored the borders of Israel from the entrance of Emath, unto the sea of the wilderness, according to the word of the Lord, the God of Israel, which he spoke by his servant, Jonas, the son of Amathi, the prophet, who was of Geth, which is in Opher.€€ƒxÒFor the Lord saw the affliction of Israel, that it was exceedingly bitter, and that they were consumed even to them that were shut up in prison, and the lowest persons, and that there was no one to help Israel.€€ƒx‘And the Lord did not say that he would blot out the name of Israel from under heaven; but he saved them by the hand of Jeroboam, the son of Joas.€€ƒxìBut the rest of the acts of Jeroboam, and all that he did, and his valour, wherewith he fought, and how he restored Damascus and Emath to Juda, in Israel, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel?€€ƒxgAnd Jeroboam slept with his fathers, the kings of Israel; and Zacharias, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒxkIn the seven and twentieth year of Jeroboam, king of Israel, reigned Azarias, son of Amasias, king of Juda.€€ƒx”He was sixteen years old when he began to reign, and he reigned two and fifty years in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Jechelia, of Jerusalem.€€ƒxhAnd he did that which was pleasing before the Lord, according to all that his father, Amasias, had done.€€˜&ƒxÈAnd the Lord struck the king, so that he was a leper unto the day of his death, and he dwelt in a free house apart: but Joatham, the king's son, governed the palace, and judged the people of the land.€€ƒx‰And the rest of the acts of Azarias, and all that he did, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Juda?€€ƒxŒAnd Azarias slept with his fathers: and they buried him with his ancestors in the city of David, and Joatham, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒx‚In the eight and thirtieth year of Azarias, king of Juda, reigned Zacharias, son of Jeroboam, over Israel, in Samaria, six months:€€ƒx›And he did that which is evil before the Lord, as his fathers had done: he departed not from the sins of Jeroboam the son of Nabat, who made Israel to sin.€€ƒxwAnd Sellum, the son of Jabes, conspired against him: and struck him publicly, and killed him, and reigned in his place.€€ƒxxNow the rest of the acts of Zacharias, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel?€€ƒx£This was the word of the Lord, which he spoke to Jehu, saying: Thy children, to the fourth generation, shall sit upon the throne of Israel. And so it came to pass.€€ƒxƒSellum, the son of Jabes, began to reign in the nine and thirtieth year of Azarias, king of Juda: and reigned one month in Samaria.€€ƒx¥And Manahem, the son of Gadi, went up from Thersa, and he came into Samaria, and struck Sellum, the son of Jabes, in Samaria, and slew him, and reigned in his stead.€€ƒx—And the rest of the acts of Sellum, and his conspiracy which he made, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel?€€ƒxËThen Manahem destroyed Thapsa and all that were in it, and the borders thereof from Thersa, because they would not open to him: and he slew all the women thereof that were with child, and ripped them up.€€ƒxzIn the nine and thirtieth year of Azarias, king of Juda, reigned Manahem, son of Gadi, over Israel, ten years, in Samaria.€€ƒx’And he did that which was evil before the Lord: he departed not from the sins of Jeroboam, the son of Nabat, who made Israel to sin, all his days.€€ƒx—And Phul, king of the Assyrians, came into the land, and Manahem gave Phul a thousand talents of silver to aid him and to establish him in the kingdom.€€ƒxÑAnd Manahem laid a tax upon Israel, on all that were mighty and rich, to give the king of the Assyrians, each man fifty sicles of silver: so the king of the Assyrians turned back, and did not stay in the land.€€ƒx‹And the rest of the acts of Manahem, and all that he did, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel?€€ƒxOAnd Manahem slept with his fathers: and Phaceia, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒxwIn the fiftieth year of Azarias, king of Juda, reigned Phaceia, the son of Manahem, over Israel, in Samaria, two years.€€ƒx„And he did that which was evil before the Lord: he departed not from the sins of Jeroboam, the son of Nabat, who made Israel to sin.€€ƒxúAnd Phacee the son of Romelia, his captain, conspired against him, and smote him in Samaria, in the tower of the king's house, near Argob, and near Arie, and with him fifty men of the sons of the Galaadites, and he slew him, and reigned in his stead.€€ƒx‹And the rest of the acts of Phaceia, and all that he did, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel?€€ƒxIn the two and fiftieth year of Azarias, king of Juda, reigned Phacee, the son of Romelia, over Israel, in Samaria, twenty years.€€ƒx„And he did that which was evil before the Lord: he departed not from the sins of Jeroboam, the son of Nabat, who made Israel to sin.€€ƒxôIn the days of Phacee, king of Israel, came Theglathphalasar, king of Assyria, and took Aion, and Abel Domum Maacha, and Janoe, and Cedes, and Asor, and Galaad, and Galilee, and all the land of Nephthali: and carried them captives into Assyria.€€ƒxÁNow Osee, son of Ela, conspired, and formed a plot against Phacee, the son of Romelia, and struck him, and slew him: and reigned in his stead in the twentieth year of Joatham, the son of Ozias.€€ƒxŠBut the rest of the acts of Phacee, and all that he did, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Israel?€€ƒxmIn the second year of Phacee, the son of Romelia king of Israel, reigned Joatham, son of Ozias, king of Juda.€€ƒxHe was five and twenty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned sixteen years in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Jerusa, the daughter of Sadoc.€€ƒxlAnd he did that which was right before the Lord: according to all that his father Ozias had done, so did he.€€ƒxœBut the high places he took not away: the people still sacrificed, and burnt incense in the high places: he built the highest gate of the house of the Lord.€€ƒx‰But the rest of the acts of Joatham, and all that he did, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Juda?€€ƒxcIn those days the Lord began to send into Juda, Rasin king of Syria, and Phacee the son of Romelia.€€ƒxˆAnd Joatham slept with his fathers, and was buried with them in the city of David, his father; and Achaz, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒxfIn the seventeenth year of Phacee, the son of Romelia reigned Achaz, the son of Joatham, king of Juda.€€ƒx¹Achaz was twenty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned sixteen years in Jerusalem: he did not that which was pleasing in the sight of the Lord, his God, as David, his father.€€ƒx×But he walked in the way of the kings of Israel: moreover, he consecrated also his son, making him pass through the fire, according to the idols of the nations which the Lord destroyed before the children of Israel.€€”ƒx¡Then Rasin, king of Syria, and Phacee, son of Romelia, king of Israel, came up to Jerusalem to fight: and they besieged Achaz, but were not able to overcome him.€€ƒx¡At that time Rasin, king of Syria, restored Aila to Syria, and drove the men of Juda out of Aila: and the Edomites came into Aila, and dwelt there unto this day.€€ƒxøAnd Achaz sent messengers to Theglathphalasar, king of the Assyrians, saying: I am thy servant, and thy son: come up, and save me out of the hand of the king of Syria, and out of the hand of the king of Israel, who are risen up together against me.€€ƒx·And when he had gathered together the silver and gold that could be found in the house of the Lord, and in the king's treasures, he sent it for a present to the king of the Assyrians.€€ƒx±And he agreed to his desire: for the king of the Assyrians went up against Damascus, and laid it waste: and he carried away the inhabitants thereof to Cyrene; but Rasin he slew.€€ƒxèAnd king Achaz went to Damascus to meet Theglathphalasar, king of the Assyrians, and when he had seen the altar of Damascus, king Achaz sent to Urias, the priest, a pattern of it, and its likeness, according to all the work thereof.€€ƒx¡And Urias, the priest, built an altar according to all that king Achaz had commanded from Damascus so did Urias, the priest, until king Achaz came from Damascus.€€ƒxˆAnd when the king was come from Damascus, he saw the altar and worshipped it: and went up and offered holocausts, and his own sacrifice;€€ƒxlAnd he offered libations, and poured the blood of the peace offerings, which he had offered, upon the altar.€€ƒxáBut the altar of brass that was before the Lord, he removed from the face of the temple, and from the place of the altar, and from the place of the temple of the Lord: and he set it at the side of the altar towards the north.€€ƒy£And king Achaz commanded Urias, the priest, saying: Upon the great altar offer the morning holocaust, and the evening sacrifice, and the king's holocaust, and his sacrifice, and the holocaust of the whole people of the land, and their sacrifices, and their libations: and all the blood of the holocaust, and all the blood of the victim, thou shalt pour out upon it: but the altar of brass shall be ready at my pleasure.€€ƒxMSo Urias, the priest, did according to all that king Achaz had commanded him.€€ƒx°And king Achaz took away the graven bases, and the laver that was upon them: and he took down the sea from the brazen oxen that held it up, and put it upon a pavement of stone.€€ƒx²The Musach also for the sabbath, which he had built in the temple, and the king's entry from without, he turned into the temple of the Lord, because of the king of the Assyrians.€€ƒx†Now the rest of the acts of Achaz which he did, are they not written in the book of the words of the of the days of the kings of Juda?€€ƒx}And Achaz slept with his fathers, and was buried with them in the city of David, and Ezechias, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒxkIn the twelfth year of Achaz king of Juda, Osee the son of Ela reigned in Samaria, over Israel, nine years.€€ƒxYAnd he did evil before the Lord: but not as the kings of Israel that had been before him.€€ƒxiAgainst him came up Salmanasar, king of the Assyrians; and Osee became his servant, and paid him tribute.€€˜)ƒxVAnd he went through all the land: and going up to Samaria, he besieged it three years.€€ƒxÄAnd in the ninth year of Osee, the king of the Assyrians took Samaria, and carried Israel away to Assyria: and he placed them in Hala, and Habor, by the river of Gozan, in the cities of the Medes.€€ƒxÌFor so it was that the children of Israel had sinned against the Lord, their God, who brought them out of the land of Egypt, from under the hand of Pharao, king of Egypt; and they worshipped strange gods.€€ƒxºAnd they walked according to the way of the nations which the Lord had destroyed in the sight of the children of Israel, and of the kings of Israel: because they had done in like manner.€€ƒx¼And the children of Israel offended the Lord, their God, with things that were not right: and built them high places in all their cities, from the tower of the watchmen to the fenced city.€€ƒxUAnd they made them statues and groves on every high hill, and under every shady tree:€€ƒx¥And they burnt incense there upon altars, after the manner of the nations which the Lord had removed from their face: and they did wicked things, provoking the Lord.€€ƒxrAnd they worshipped abominations, concerning which the Lord had commanded them that they should not do this thing.€€ƒy)And the Lord testified to them in Israel, and in Juda, by the hand of all the prophets and seers, saying: Return from your wicked ways, and keep my precepts, and ceremonies, according to all the law which I commanded your fathers: and as I have sent to you in the hand of my servants the prophets.€€ƒx{And they hearkened not, but hardened their necks like to the neck of their fathers, who would not obey the Lord, their God.€€ƒyBAnd they rejected his ordinances, and the covenant that he made with their fathers, and the testimonies which he testified against them: and they followed vanities, and acted vainly: and they followed the nations that were round about them, concerning which the Lord had commanded them that they should not do as they did.€€ƒx¨And they forsook all the precepts of the Lord, their God: and made to themselves two molten calves, and groves, and adored all the host of heaven: and they served Baal,€€ƒxÆAnd consecrated their sons, and their daughters, through fire: and they gave themselves to divinations, and soothsayings: and they delivered themselves up to do evil before the Lord, to provoke him.€€ƒxtAnd the Lord was very angry with Israel, and removed them from his sight, and there remained only the tribe of Juda.€€ƒxŠBut neither did Juda itself keep the commandments of the Lord, their God: but they walked in the errors of Israel, which they had wrought.€€ƒx•And the Lord cast off all the seed of Israel, and afflicted them, and delivered them into the hand of spoilers, till he cast them away from his face:€€ƒx¾Even from that time, when Israel was rent from the house of David, and made Jeroboam, son of Nabat, their king: for Jeroboam separated Israel from the Lord, and made them commit a great sin.€€ƒxrAnd the children of Israel walked in all the sins of Jeroboam, which he had done: and they departed not from them,€€ƒx´Till the Lord removed Israel from his face, as he had spoken in the hand of all his servants, the prophets: and Israel was carried away out of their land to Assyria, unto this day.€€ƒyAnd the king of the Assyrians brought people from Babylon, and from Cutha, and from Avah, and from Emath, and from Sepharvaim: and placed them in the cities of Samaria instead of the children of Israel: and they possessed Samaria, and dwelt in the cities thereof.€€ƒxtAnd when they began to dwell there, they feared not the Lord: and the Lord sent lions among them, which killed them.€€ƒy9And it was told the king of the Assyrians, and it was said: The nations which thou hast removed, and made to dwell in the cities of Samaria, know not the ordinances of the God of the land: and the Lord hath sent lions among them: and behold they kill them, because they know not the manner of the God of the land.€€ƒxÚAnd the king of the Assyrians commanded, saying: Carry thither one of the priests whom you brought from thence captive, and let him go, and dwell with them: and let him teach them the ordinances of the God of the land.€€ƒx’So one of the priests, who had been carried away captive from Samaria, came and dwelt in Bethel, and taught them how they should worship the Lord.€€ƒx¥And every nation made gods of their own and put them in the temples of the high places, which the Samaritans had made, every nation in their cities where they dwelt.€€ƒxiFor the men of Babylon made Sochothbenoth: and the Cuthites made Nergel: and the men of Emath made Asima.€€ƒxŸAnd the Hevites made Nebahaz, and Tharthac. And they that were of Sepharvaim burnt their children in fire, to Adramelech and Anamelech, the gods of Sepharvaim.€€ƒx¸And nevertheless they worshipped the Lord. And they made to themselves, of the lowest of the people, priests of the high places, and they placed them in the temples of the high places.€€ƒx•And when they worshipped the Lord, they served also their own gods, according to the custom of the nations out of which they were brought to Samaria:€€ƒxÜUnto this day they follow the old manner: they fear not the Lord, neither do they keep his ceremonies, and judgments, and law, and the commandment, which the Lord commanded the children of Jacob, whom he surnamed Israel:€€ƒx›With whom he made a covenant, and charged them, saying: You shall not fear strange gods, nor shall you adore them, nor worship them, nor sacrifice to them.€€ƒx¹But the Lord, your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, with great power, and a stretched out arm, him shall you fear, and him shall you adore, and to him shall you sacrifice.€€ƒx¢And the ceremonies, and judgments, and law, and the commandment, which he wrote for you, you shall observe to do them always: and you shall not fear strange gods.€€ƒxdAnd the covenant that he made with you, you shall not forget: neither shall ye worship strange Gods,€€ƒxZBut fear the Lord, your God, and he shall deliver you out of the hand of all your enemies.€€ƒxHBut they did not hearken to them, but did according to their old custom.€€ƒx£So these nations feared the Lord, but nevertheless served also their idols: their children also, and grandchildren, as their fathers did, so do they unto this day.€€ƒ`€€ƒxlIn the third year of Osee, the son of Ela, king of Israel, reigned Ezechias, the son of Achaz, king of Juda.€€ƒx¦He was five and twenty years old when he began to reign: and he reigned nine and twenty years in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Abi, the daughter of Zacharias.€€ƒxaAnd he did that which was good before the Lord, according to all that David, his father, had done€€˜%ƒx—He trusted in the Lord, the God of Israel: so that after him there was none like him among all the kings of Juda, nor any of them that were before him:€€ƒxuAnd he stuck to the Lord, and departed not from his steps, but kept his commandments, which the Lord commanded Moses.€€ƒx²Wherefore the Lord also was with him, and in all things, to which he went forth, he behaved himself wisely. And he rebelled against the king of the Assyrians, and served him not.€€ƒxrHe smote the Philistines as far as Gaza, and all their borders, from the tower of the watchmen to the fenced city.€€ƒx°In the fourth year of king Ezechias, which was the seventh year of Osee, the son of Ela, king of Israel, Salmanasar, king of the Assyrians, came up to Samaria, and besieged it,€€ƒx‰And took it. For after three years, in the sixth year of Ezechias, that is, in the ninth year of Osee, king of Israel, Samaria was taken:€€ƒxšAnd the king of the Assyrians carried away Israel into Assyria, and placed them in Hala, and in Habor, by the rivers of Gozan, in the cities of the Medes.€€ƒx¯Because they hearkened not to the voice of the Lord, their God, but transgressed his covenant: all that Moses, the servant of the Lord, commanded, they would not hear, nor do.€€ƒx†In the fourteenth year of king Ezechias, Sennacherib, king of the Assyrians, came up against the fenced cities of Juda, and took them.€€ƒy5Then Ezechias, king of Juda, sent messengers to the king of the Assyrians, to Lachis, saying: I have offended, depart from me: and all that thou shalt put upon me, I will bear. And the king of the Assyrians put a tax upon Ezechias, king of Juda, of three hundred talents of silver, and thirty talents of gold.€€ƒxfAnd Ezechias gave all the silver that was found in the house of the Lord, and in the king's treasures.€€ƒx¢At that time Ezechias broke the doors of the temple of the Lord, and the plates of gold which he had fastened on them, and gave them to the king of the Assyrians.€€ƒyAnd the king of the Assyrians sent Tharthan, and Rabsaris, and Rabsaces, from Lachis, to king Ezechias, with a strong army, to Jerusalem: and they went up and came to Jerusalem, and they stood by the conduit of the upper pool, which is in the way of the fuller's field.€€ƒx¯And they called for the king: and there went out to them Eliacim, the son of Helcias, who was over the house, and Sobna, the scribe, and Joahe, the son of Asaph, the recorder.€€ƒx“And Rabsaces said to them: Speak to Ezechias: Thus saith the great king, the king of the Assyrians: What is this confidence, wherein thou trustest?€€ƒxsPerhaps thou hast taken counsel, to prepare thyself for battle. On whom dost thou trust, that thou darest to rebel?€€ƒx¶Dost thou trust in Egypt a staff of a broken reed, upon which if a man lean, it will break and go into his hand, and pierce it? so is Pharao, king of Egypt, to all that trust in him.€€ƒxÒBut if you say to me: We trust in the Lord, our God: is it not he, whose high places and altars Ezechias hath taken away: and hath commanded Juda and Jerusalem: You shall worship before this altar in Jerusalem?€€ƒxNow therefore come over to my master, the king of the Assyrians, and I will give you two thousand horses, and see whether you be able to have riders for them.€€ƒx„And how can you stand against one lord of the least of my master's servants? Dost thou trust in Egypt for chariots and for horsemen?€€ƒxŠIs it without the will of the Lord that I am come up to this place to destroy it? The Lord said to me: Go up to this land, and destroy it.€€ƒxüThen Eliacim, the son of Helcias, and Sobna, and Joahe, said to Rabsaces: We pray thee, speak to us, thy servants, in Syriac: for we understand that tongue: and speak not to us in the Jews' language, in the hearing of the people that are upon the wall.€€ƒxàAnd Rabsaces answered them, saying: Hath my master sent me to thy master, and to thee, to speak these words, and not rather to the men that sit upon the wall, that they may eat their own dung, and drink their urine with you?€€ƒx‘Then Rabsaces stood, and cried out with a loud voice in the Jews' language, and said: Hear the word of the great king, the king of the Assyrians.€€ƒxjThus saith the king: Let not Ezechias deceive you: for he shall not be able to deliver you out of my hand.€€ƒx¡Neither let him make you trust in the Lord, saying: The Lord will surely deliver us, and this city shall not be given into the hand of the king of the Assyrians.€€ƒyDo not hearken to Ezechias. For thus saith the king of the Assyrians: Do with me that which is for your advantage, and come out to me: and every man of you shall eat of his own vineyard, and of his own fig tree: and you shall drink water of your own cisterns,€€ƒyTill I come, and take you away, to a land, like to your own land, a fruitful land, and plentiful in wine, a land of bread and vineyards, a land of olives, and oil, and honey, and you shall live, and not die. Hearken not to Ezechias, who deceiveth you, saying: The Lord will deliver us.€€ƒx^Have any of the gods of the nations delivered their land from the hand of the king of Assyria?€€ƒx‰Where is the god of Emath, and of Arphad? where is the god of Sepharvaim, of Ana, and of Ava? have they delivered Samaria out of my hand?€€ƒx”Who are they among all the gods of the nations that have delivered their country out of my hand, that the Lord may deliver Jerusalem out of my hand?€€ƒxBut the people held their peace, and answered him not a word: for they had received commandment from the king that they should not answer him.€€ƒxÊAnd Eliacim, the son of Helcias, who was over the house, and Sobna, the scribe, and Joahe, the son of Asaph, the recorder, came to Ezechias, with their garments rent, and told him the words of Rabsaces.€€ƒ`€€ƒxˆAnd when king Ezechias heard these words, he rent his garments, and covered himself with sackcloth, and went into the house of the Lord.€€ƒx¦And he sent Eliacim, who was over the house, and Sobna, the scribe, and the ancients of the priests, covered with sackcloths, to Isaias, the prophet, the son of Amos.€€ƒx½And they said to him: Thus saith Ezechias: This day is a day of tribulation, and of rebuke, and of blasphemy: the children are come to the birth, and the woman in travail hath not strength.€€˜%ƒx0So the servants of king Ezechias came to Isaias.€€ƒxÌAnd Isaias said to them: Thus shall you say to your master: Thus saith the Lord: Be not afraid for the words which thou hast heard, with which the servants of the king of the Assyrians have blasphemed me.€€ƒx£Behold I will send a spirit upon him, and he shall hear a message, and shall return into his own country, and I will make him fall by the sword in his own country.€€ƒx~And Rabsaces returned, and found the king of the Assyrians besieging Lobna: for he had heard that he was departed from Lachis.€€ƒxAnd when he heard of Tharaca, king of Ethiopia: Behold, he is come out to fight with thee: and was going against him, he sent messengers to Ezechias, saying:€€ƒxÀThus shall you say to Ezechias, king of Juda: Let not thy God deceive thee, in whom thou trustest: and do not say: Jerusalem shall not be delivered into the hands of the king of the Assyrians.€€ƒx•Behold, thou hast heard what the kings of the Assyrians have done to all countries, how they have laid them waste: and canst thou alone be delivered?€€ƒx«Have the gods of the nations delivered any of them, whom my fathers have destroyed, to wit, Gozan, and Haran, and Reseph, and the children of Eden, that were in Thelassar?€€ƒxsWhere is the king of Emath, and the king of Arphad, and the king of the city of Sepharvaim, and of Ana, and of Ava?€€ƒxAnd when Ezechias had received the letter of the hand of the messengers, and had read it, he went up to the house of the Lord, and spread it before the Lord,€€ƒx­And he prayed in his sight, saying: O Lord God of Israel, who sittest upon the cherubims, thou alone art the God of all the kings of the earth: thou madest heaven and earth:€€ƒx’Incline thy ear, and hear: open, O Lord, thy eyes and see: and hear all the words of Sennacherib, who hath sent to upbraid unto us the living God.€€ƒxaOf a truth, O Lord, the kings of the Assyrians have destroyed nations, and the lands of them all.€€ƒxAnd they have cast their gods into the fire: for they were not gods, but the work of men's hands, of wood and stone, and they destroyed them.€€ƒx‡Now therefore, O Lord our God, save us from his hand, that all the kingdoms of the earth may know that thou art the Lord, the only God.€€ƒxºAnd Isaias, the son of Amos, sent to Ezechias, saying: Thus saith the Lord, the God of Israel: I have heard the prayer thou hast made to me concerning Sennacherib, king of the Assyrians.€€ƒxÃThis is the word that the Lord hath spoken of him: The virgin, the daughter of Sion, hath despised thee, and laughed thee to scorn: the daughter of Jerusalem hath wagged her head behind thy back.€€ƒx£Whom hast thou reproached, and whom hast thou blasphemed? against whom hast thou exalted thy voice, and lifted up thy eyes on high? against the holy one of Israel.€€ƒy@By the hand of thy servants thou hast reproached the Lord, and hast said: With the multitude of my chariots I have gone up to the height of the mountains, to the top of Libanus, and have cut down its tall cedars, and its choice fir trees. And I have entered into the furthest parts thereof, and the forest of its Carmel.€€ƒxuI have cut down, and I have drunk strange waters, and have dried up with the soles of my feet all the shut up waters.€€ƒxËHast thou not heard what I have done from the beginning? from the days of old I have formed it, and now I have brought it to effect: that fenced cities of fighting men should be turned to heaps of ruins:€€ƒxÏAnd the inhabitants of them were weak of hand, they trembled and were confounded, they became like the grass of the field, and the green herb on the tops of houses, which withered before it came to maturity.€€ƒxgThy dwelling, and thy going out, and thy coming in, and thy way I knew before, and thy rage against me.€€ƒxÅThou hast been mad against me, and thy pride hath come up to my ears: therefore I will put a ring in thy nose, and a bit between thy lips, and I will turn thee back by the way by which thou camest.€€ƒxßAnd to thee, O Ezechias, this shall be a sign: Eat this year what thou shalt find: and in the second year, such things as spring of themselves: but in the third year sow and reap: plant vineyards, and eat the fruit of them.€€ƒxcAnd whatsoever shall be left of the house of Juda, shall take root downward, and bear fruit upward.€€ƒxŒFor out of Jerusalem shall go forth a remnant, and that which shall be saved out of mount Sion: the zeal of the Lord of hosts shall do this.€€ƒx½Wherefore thus saith the Lord concerning the king of the Assyrians: He shall not come into this city, nor shoot an arrow into it, nor come before it with shield, nor cast a trench about it.€€ƒx^By the way that he came he shall return: and into this city he shall not come, saith the Lord.€€ƒxaAnd I will protect this city, and will save it for my own sake, and for David, my servant's sake.€€ƒxÔAnd it came to pass that night, that an angel of the Lord came, and slew in the camp of the Assyrians a hundred and eighty-five thousand. And when he arose early in the morning, he saw all the bodies of the dead.€€ƒxbAnd Sennacherib, king of the Assyrians, departing, went away, and he returned and abode in Ninive.€€ƒxÔAnd as he was worshipping in the temple of Nesroch, his god, Adramelech and Sarasar, his sons, slew him with the sword, and they fled into the land of the Armenians, and Asarhaddon, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÈIn those days Ezechias was sick unto death: and Isaias, the son of Amos, the prophet, came and said to him: Thus saith the Lord God: Give charge concerning thy house, for thou shalt die, and not llve.€€ƒxCAnd he turned his face to the wall, and prayed to the Lord, saying:€€ƒx¹I beseech thee, O Lord, remember how I have walked before thee in truth, and with a perfect heart, and have done that which is pleasing before thee. And Ezechias wept with much weeping.€€•ƒxøGo back, and tell Ezechias, the captain of my people: Thus saith the Lord, the God of David, thy father: I have heard thy prayer, and I have seen thy tears: and behold I have healed thee: on the third day thou shalt go up to the temple of the Lord.€€ƒxÍAnd I will add to thy days fifteen years: and I will deliver thee and this city out of the hand of the king of the Assyrians, and I will protect this city for my own sake, and for David, my servant's sake.€€ƒxqAnd Isaias said: Bring me a lump of figs. And when they had brought it, and laid it upon his boil, he was healed.€€ƒx’And Ezechias had said to Isaias: What shall be the sign that the Lord will heal me, and that I will go up to the temple of the Lord the third day?€€ƒxÆAnd Isaias said to him: This shall be the sign from the Lord, that the Lord will do the word which he hath spoken: Wilt thou that the shadow go forward ten lines, or that it go back so many degrees?€€ƒxšAnd Ezechias said: It is an easy matter for the shadow to go forward ten lines: and I do not desire that this be done, but let it return back ten degrees.€€ƒx¤And Isaias, the prophet, called upon the Lord, and he brought the shadow ten degrees backwards by the lines, by which it had already gone down on the dial of Achaz.€€ƒx At that time Berodach Baladan, the son of Baladan, king of the Babylonians, sent letters and presents to Ezechias: for he had heard that Ezechias had been sick.€€ƒyCAnd Ezechias rejoiced at their coming, and he shewed them the house of his aromatical spices, and the gold, and the silver, and divers precious odours, and ointments, and the house of his vessels, and all that he had in his treasures. There was nothing in his house, nor in all his dominions, that Ezechias shewed them not.€€ƒxÅAnd Isaias, the prophet, came to king Ezechias, and said to him: What said these men? or from whence came they to thee? And Ezechias said to him: From a far country, they came to me out of Babylon.€€ƒxªAnd he said: What did they see in thy house? Ezechias said: They saw all the things that are in my house: There is nothing among my treasures that I have not shewed them.€€ƒx7And Isaias said to Ezechias: Hear the word of the Lord.€€ƒxºBehold the days shall come, that all that is in thy house, and that thy fathers have laid up in store unto this day, shall be carried into Babylon: nothing shall be left, saith the Lord.€€ƒxAnd of thy sons also that shall issue from thee, whom thou shalt beget, they shall take away, and they shall be eunuchs in the palace of the king of Babylon.€€ƒxrEzechias said to Isaias: The word of the Lord, which thou hast spoken, is good: let peace and truth be in my days.€€ƒxÑAnd the rest of the acts of Ezechias, and all his might, and how he made a pool, and a conduit, and brought waters into the city, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Juda?€€ƒxPAnd Ezechias slept with his fathers, and Manasses, his son reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŒManasses was twelve years old when he began to reign, and he reigned five and fifty years in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Haphsiba.€€ƒx™And he did evil in the sight of the Lord, according to the idols of the nations, which the Lord destroyed from before the face of the children of Israel.€€ƒxåAnd he turned, and built up the high places, which Ezechias, his father, had destroyed: and he set up altars to Baal, and made groves, as Achab, the king of Israel, had done: and he adored all the host of heaven, and served them.€€˜ƒx\And he built altars for all the host of heaven, in the two courts of the temple of the Lord.€€ƒx¶And he made his son pass through fire: and he used divinations, and observed omens, and appointed pythons, and multiplied soothsayers, to do evil before the Lord, and to provoke him.€€ƒyHe set also an idol of the grove, which he had made, in the temple of the Lord: concerning which the Lord said to David, and to Solomon his son: In this temple, and in Jerusalem, which I have chosen out of all the tribes of Israel, I will put my name for ever.€€ƒxâAnd I will no more make the feet of Israel to be moved out of the land, which I gave to their fathers: only if they will observe to do all that I have commanded them, according to the law which my servant Moses commanded them.€€ƒxBut they hearkened not: but were seduced by Manasses, to do evil more than the nations which the Lord destroyed before the children of Israel.€€ƒxEAnd the Lord spoke in the hand of his servants, the prophets, saying:€€ƒx¯Because Manasses, king of Juda, hath done these most wicked abominations, beyond all that the Amorrhites did before him, and hath made Juda also to sin with his filthy doings:€€ƒx§Therefore thus saith the Lord, the God of Israel: Behold, I will bring on evils upon Jerusalem and Juda: that whosoever shall hear of them, both his ears shall tingle.€€ƒxóAnd I will stretch over Jerusalem the line of Samaria, and the weight of the house of Achab: and I will efface Jerusalem, as writings tables are wont to be effaced, and I will erase and turn it, and draw the pencil often over the face thereof.€€ƒx§And I will leave the remnants of my inheritance, and will deliver them into the hands of their enemies: and they shall become a prey, and a spoil to all their enemies.€€ƒxBecause they have done evil before me, and have continued to provoke me, from the day that their fathers came out of Egypt, even unto this day.€€ƒx­Moreover, Manasses shed also very much innocent blood, till he filled Jerusalem up to the mouth: besides his sins, wherewith he made Juda to sin, to do evil before the Lord.€€ƒx¨Now the rest of the acts of Manasses, and all that he did, and his sin, which he sinned, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Juda?€€ƒx’And Manasses slept with his fathers, and was buried in the garden of his own house, in the garden of Oza: and Amon, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒxªTwo and twenty years old was Amon when he began to reign, and he reigned two years in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Messalemeth, the daughter of Harus, of Jeteba.€€ƒxLAnd he did evil in the sight of the Lord, as Manasses, his father, had done.€€ƒxŒAnd he walked in all the way in which his father had walked: and he served the abominations which his father had served, and he adored them.€€ƒxTAnd forsook the Lord, the God of his fathers, and walked not in the way of the Lord.€€ƒxIAnd his servants plotted against him, and slew the king in his own house.€€ƒxBut the people of the land slew all them that had conspired against king Amon: and made Josias, his son, their king in his stead.€€ƒxBut the rest of the acts of Amon, which he did, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Juda?€€ƒxfAnd they buried him in his sepulchre, in the garden of Oza: and his son, Josias, reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒx§Josias was eight years old when he began to reign: he reigned one and thirty years in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Idida, the daughter of Hadaia, of Besecath.€€ƒx¡And he did that which was right in the sight of the Lord, and walked in all the ways of David, his father: he turned not aside to the right hand, or to the left.€€ƒxœAnd in the eighteenth year of king Josias, the king sent Saphan, the son of Assia, the son of Messulam, the scribe of the temple of the Lord, saying to him:€€”ƒx¬And let it be given to the workmen by the overseers of the house of the Lord: and let them distribute it to those that work in the temple of the Lord, to repair the temple:€€ƒx¡That is, to carpenters and masons, and to such as mend breaches: and that timber may be bought, and stones out of the quarries, to repair the temple of the Lord.€€ƒx†But let there be no reckoning made with them of the money which they receive, but let them have it in their power, and in their trust.€€ƒx©And Helcias, the high priest, said to Saphan, the scribe: I have found the book of the law in the house of the Lord: and Helcias gave the book to Saphan, and he read it.€€ƒy?And Saphan, the scribe, came to the king, and brought him word again concerning that which he had commanded, and said: Thy servants have gathered together the money that was found in the house of the Lord: and they have given it to be distributed to the workmen, by the overseers of the works of the temple of the Lord.€€ƒxAnd Saphan, the scribe, told the king, saying: Helcias, the priest, hath delivered to me a book. And when Saphan had read it before the king,€€ƒxNAnd the king had heard the words of the law of the Lord, he rent his garments.€€ƒx¢And he commanded Helcias, the priest, and Ahicam, the son of Saphan, and Achobor, the son of Micha, and Saphan, the scribe, and Asaia, the king's servant, saying:€€ƒyGo and consult the Lord for me, and for the people, and for all Juda, concerning the words of this book which is found: for the great wrath of the Lord is kindled against us, because our fathers have not hearkened to the words of this book, to do all that is written for us.€€ƒxõSo Helcias, the priest, and Ahicam, and Achobor, and Sapham, and Asaia, went to Holda, the prophetess, the wife of Sellum, the son of Thecua, the son of Araas, keeper of the wardrobe, who dwelt in Jerusalem, in the Second: and they spoke to her.€€ƒx_And she said to them: Thus saith the Lord, the God of Israel: Tell the man that sent you to me:€€ƒxThus saith the Lord: Behold, I will bring evils upon this place, and upon the inhabitants thereof, all the words of the law which the king of Juda hath read:€€ƒxÊBecause they have forsaken me, and have sacrificed to strange gods, provoking me by all the works of their hands: therefore my indignation shall be kindled against this place, and shall not be quenched.€€ƒx¬But to the king of Juda, who sent you to consult the Lord, thus shall you say: Thus saith the Lord, the God of Israel: for as much as thou hast heard the words of the book,€€ƒy+And thy heart hath been moved to fear, and thou hast humbled thyself before the Lord, hearing the words against this place, and the inhabitants thereof, to wit, that they should become a wonder and a curse: and thou hast rent thy garments, and wept before me; I also have heard thee; saith the Lord.€€ƒx®Therefore I will gather thee to thy fathers, and thou shalt be gathered to thy sepulchre in peace; that thy eyes may not see all the evils which I will bring upon this place.€€ƒ`€€ƒx†And they brought the king word again what she had said. And he sent: and all the ancients of Juda and Jerusalem were assembled to him.€€ƒy=And the king went up to the temple of the Lord, and all the men of Juda, and all the inhabitants of Jerusalem with him, the priests, and the prophets, and all the people, both little and great: and in the hearing of them all he read all the words of the book of the covenant, which was found in the house of the Lord.€€ƒyHAnd the king stood upon the step: and he made a covenant with the Lord, to walk after the Lord, and to keep his commandments, and his testimonies, and his ceremonies, with all their heart, and with all their soul, and to perform the words of this covenant, which were written in that book: and the people agreed to the covenant.€€˜%ƒyAnd he destroyed the soothsayers, whom the kings of Juda had appointed to sacrifice in the high places in the cities of Juda, and round about Jerusalem: them also that burnt incense to Baal, and to the sun, and to the moon, and to the twelve signs, and to all the host of heaven.€€ƒxØAnd he caused the grove to be carried out from the house of the Lord, without Jerusalem, to the valley of Cedron, and he burnt it there, and reduced it to dust, and cast the dust upon the graves of the common people.€€ƒx›He destroyed also the pavilions of the effeminate, which were in the house of the Lord, for which the women wove as it were little dwellings for the grove.€€ƒyGAnd he gathered together all the priests out of the cities of Juda: and he defiled the high places, where the priests offered sacrifice, from Gabaa to Bersabee: and he broke down the altars of the gates that were in the entering in of the gate of Josue, governor of the city, which was on the left hand of the gate of the city.€€ƒx–However, the priests of the high places came not up to the altar of the Lord, in Jerusalem: but only eat of the unleavened bread among their brethren.€€ƒxšAnd he defiled Topheth, which is in the valley of the son of Ennom: that no man should consecrate there his son, or his daughter, through fire, to Moloch.€€ƒxéAnd he took away the horses which the kings of Juda had given to the sun, at the entering in of the temple of the Lord, near the chamber of Nathanmelech the eunuch, who was in Pharurim: and he burnt the chariots of the sun with fire.€€ƒyAnd the altars that were upon the top of the upper chamber of Achaz, which the kings of Juda had made, and the altars which Manasses had made in the two courts of the temple of the Lord, the king broke down: and he ran from thence, and cast the ashes of them into the torrent Cedron.€€ƒyThe high places also that were at Jerusalem, on the right side of the Mount of Offence, which Solomon, king of Israel, had built to Astaroth, the idol of the Sidonians, and to Chamos, the scandal of Moab, and to Melchom, the abomination of the children of Ammon, the king defiled.€€ƒxsAnd he broke in pieces the statues, and cut down the groves: and he filled their places with the bones of dead men.€€ƒxíMoreover, the altar also that was at Bethel, and the high place, which Jeroboam, the son of Nabat, who made Israel to sin, had made: both the altar, and the high place, he broke down and burnt, and reduced to powder, and burnt the grove.€€ƒyAnd as Josias turned himself, he saw there the sepulchres that were in the mount: and he sent and took the bones out of the sepulchres, and burnt them upon the altar, and defiled it according to the word of the Lord, which the man of God spoke, who had foretold these things.€€ƒxÖAnd he said: What is that monument which I see? And the men of that city answered: It is the sepulchre of the man of God, who came from Juda, and foretold these things which thou hast done upon the altar of Bethel.€€ƒxAnd he said: Let him alone, let no man move his bones. So his bones were left untouched with the bones of the prophet, that came out of Samaria.€€ƒxãMoreover all the temples of the high places which were in the cities of Samaria, which the kings of Israel had made to provoke the Lord, Josias took away: and he did to them according to all the acts that he had done in Bethel.€€ƒxAnd he slew all the priests of the high places, that were there, upon the altars; and he burnt men's bones upon them: and returned to Jerusalem.€€ƒx†And he commanded all the people, saying: Keep the Phase to the Lord your God, according as it is written in the book of this covenant.€€ƒx˜Now there was no such a Phase kept from the days of the judges, who judged Israel, nor in all the days of the kings of Israel, and of the kings of Juda,€€ƒxaAs was this Phase, that was kept to the Lord in Jerusalem, in the eighteenth year of king Josias.€€ƒyEMoreover the diviners by spirits, and soothsayers, and the figures of idols, and the uncleannesses, and the abominations, that had been in the land of Juda and Jerusalem, Josias took away: that he might perform the words of the law, that were written in the book, which Helcias the priest had found in the temple of the Lord.€€ƒxãThere was no king before him like unto him, that returned to the Lord with all his heart, and with all his soul, and with all his strength, according to all the law of Moses: neither after him did there arise any like unto him.€€ƒx¹But yet the Lord turned not away from the wrath of his great indignation, wherewith his anger was kindled against Juda: because of the provocations, wherewith Manasses had provoked him.€€ƒxÉAnd the Lord said: I will remove Juda also from before my face, as I have removed Israel: and I will cast off this city Jerusalem, which I chose, and the house, of which I said: My name shall be there.€€ƒxˆNow the rest of the acts of Josias, and all that he did, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Juda?€€ƒx·In his days Pharao Nechao, king of Egypt, went up against the king of Assyria to the river Euphrates: and king Josias went to meet him: and was slain at Mageddo, when he had seen him.€€ƒxóAnd his servants carried him dead from Mageddo: and they brought him to Jerusalem, and buried him in his own sepulchre. And the people of the land took Joachaz, the son of Josias: and they anointed him, and made him king in his father's stead.€€ƒx¯Joachaz was three and twenty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned three months in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Amital, the daughter of Jeremias, of Lobna.€€ƒxLAnd he did evil before the Lord, according to all that his fathers had done.€€ƒxÂAnd Pharao Nechao bound him at Rebla, which is in the land of Emath, that he should not reign in Jerusalem: and he set a fine upon the land, of a hundred talents of silver, and a talent of gold.€€ƒxÀAnd Pharao Nechao made Eliacim, the son of Josias, king in the room of Josias his father: and turned his name to Joakim. And he took Joachaz away and carried him into Egypt, and he died there.€€ƒyAnd Joakim gave the silver and the gold to Pharao, after he had taxed the land for every man, to contribute according to the commandment of Pharao: and he exacted both the silver and the gold of the people of the land, of every man according to his ability: to give to Pharao Nechao.€€ƒx«Joakim was five and twenty years old when he began to reign: and he reigned eleven years in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Zebida, the daughter of Phadaia, of Ruma.€€ƒxKAnd he did evil before the Lord according to all that his fathers had done.€€ƒ`€€ƒxƒIn his days Nabuchodonosor, king of Babylon came up, and Joakim became his servant three years: then again he rebelled against him.€€ƒyAnd the Lord sent against him the rovers of the Chaldees, and the rovers of Syria, and the rovers of Moab, and the rovers of the children of Ammon: and he sent them against Juda, to destroy it, according to the word of the Lord, which he had spoken by his servants, the prophets.€€ƒx}And this came by the word of the Lord against Juda, to remove them from before him for all the sins of Manasses which he did;€€”ƒx«But the rest of the acts of Joakim, and all that he did, are they not written in the book of the words of the days of the kings of Juda? And Joakim slept with his fathers:€€ƒx+And Joachin, his son, reigned in his stead.€€ƒxÆAnd the king of Egypt came not again any more out of his own country: for the king of Babylon had taken all that had belonged to the king of Egypt, from the river of Egypt, unto the river Euphrates.€€ƒx¬Joachin was eighteen years old when he began to reign, and he reigned three months in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Nohesta, the daughter of Elnathan, of Jerusalem.€€ƒxKAnd he did evil before the Lord, according to all that his father had done.€€ƒx†At that time the servants of Nabuchodonosor, king of Babylon, came up against Jerusalem, and the city was surrounded with their forts.€€ƒxXAnd Nabuchodonosor, king of Babylon, came to the city, with his servants, to assault it.€€ƒxÈAnd Joachin, king of Juda, went out to the king of Babylon, he, and his mother, and his servants, and his nobles, and his eunuchs: and the king of Babylon received him in the eighth year of his reign.€€ƒyAnd he brought out from thence all the treasures of the house of the Lord, and the treasures of the king's house: and he cut in pieces all the vessels of gold which Solomon, king of Israel, had made in the temple of the Lord, according to the word of the Lord.€€ƒxëAnd he carried away all Jerusalem, and all the princes, and all the valiant men of the army, to the number of ten thousand, into captivity: and every artificer and smith: and none were left, but the poor sort of the people of the land.€€ƒx»And he carried away Joachin into Babylon, and the king's mother, and the king's wives, and his eunuchs: and the judges of the land he carried into captivity, from Jerusalem, into Babylon.€€ƒx»And all the strong men, seven thousand, and the artificers, and the smiths, a thousand, all that were valiant men, and fit for war: and the king of Babylon led them captives into Babylon.€€ƒxSAnd he appointed Matthanias, his uncle, in his stead: and called his name Sedecias.€€ƒx®Sedecias was one and twenty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned eleven years in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Amital, the daughter of Jeremias, of Lobna.€€ƒxGAnd he did evil before the Lord, according to all that Joakim had done.€€ƒxFor the Lord was angry against Jerusalem and against Juda, till he cast them out from his face: and Sedecias revolted from the king of Babylon.€€ƒ`€€ƒxğAnd it came to pass in the ninth year of his reign, in the tenth month, the tenth day of the month, that Nabuchodonosor, king of Babylon, came, he and all his army, against Jerusalem: and they surrounded it: and raised works round about it.€€ƒxNAnd the city was shut up and besieged till the eleventh year of king Sedecias,€€ƒxrThe ninth day of the month: and a famine prevailed in the city, and there was no bread for the people of the land.€€˜ƒx¥And the army of the Chaldees pursued after the king, and overtook him in the plains of Jericho: and all the warriors that were with him were scattered, and left him:€€ƒxjSo they took the king, and brought him to the king of Babylon, to Reblatha, and he gave judgment upon him.€€ƒxAnd he slew the sons of Sedecias before his face, and he put out his eyes, and bound him with chains, and brought him to Babylon.€€ƒxÃIn the fifth month, the seventh day of the month, the same is the nineteenth year of the king of Babylon, came Nabuzardan, commander of the army, a servant of the king of Babylon, into Jerusalem.€€ƒx€And he burnt the house of the Lord, and the king's house, and the houses of Jerusalem, and every great house he burnt with fire.€€ƒx|And all the army of the Chaldees, which was with the commander of the troops, broke down the walls of Jerusalem round about.€€ƒxÎAnd Nabuzardan, the commander of the army, carried away the rest of the people, that remained in the city, and the fugitives, that had gone over to the king of Babylon, and the remnant of the common people.€€ƒxJBut of the poor of the land he left some dressers of vines and husbandmen.€€ƒxÒAnd the pillars of brass that were in the temple of the Lord, and the bases, and the sea of brass, which was in the house of the Lord, the Chaldees broke in pieces, and carried all the brass of them to Babylon.€€ƒxThey took away also the pots of brass, and the mazers, and the forks, and the cups, and the mortars, and all the vessels of brass, with which they ministered.€€ƒxMoreover also the censers, and the bowls, such as were of gold in gold: and such as were of silver in silver, the general of the army took away.€€ƒx‘That is, two pillars, one sea, and the bases which Solomon had made in the temple of the Lord: the brass of all these vessels was without weight.€€ƒxúOne pillar was eighteen cubits high: and the chapiter of brass, which was upon it, was three cubits high: and the network, and the pomegranates that were upon the chapiter of the pillar, were all of brass: and the second pillar had the like adorning.€€ƒxtAnd the general of the army took Seraias, the chief priest, and Sophonias, the second priest, and three doorkeepers:€€ƒy9And out of the city one eunuch, who was captain over the men of war: and five men of them who had stood before the king, whom he found in the city, and Sopher, the captain of the army, who exercised the young soldiers of the people of the land: and threescore men of the common people, who were found in the city:€€ƒxkThese Nabuzardan, the general of the army, took away, and carried them to the king of Babylon, to Reblatha.€€ƒx€And the king of Babylon smote them, and slew them at Reblatha, in the land of Emath: so Juda was carried away out of their land.€€ƒx±But over the people that remained in the land of Juda, which Nabuchodonosor, king of Babylon, had left, he gave the government to Godolias, the son of Ahicam, the son of Saphan.€€ƒygAnd when all the captains of the soldiers had heard this, they and the men that were with them, to wit, that the king of Babylon had made Godolias governor they came to Godolias to Maspha, Ismael, the son of Nathanias, and Johanan, the son of Caree, and Saraia, the son of Thanehumeth, the Netophathite, and Jezonias, the son of Maachathi, they and their men.€€ƒx©And Godolias swore to them and to their men, saying: Be not afraid to serve the Chaldees: stay in the land, and serve the king of Babylon, and it shall be well with you.€€ƒxôBut it came to pass in the seventh month, that Ismael, the son of Nathanias, the son of Elisama, of the seed royal came, and ten men with him, and smote Godolias; so that he died: and also the Jews and the Chaldees that were with him in Maspha.€€ƒx|And all the people, both little and great, and the captains of the soldiers, rising up, went to Egypt, fearing the Chaldees.€€ƒyAnd it came to pass in the seven and thirtieth year of the captivity of Joachin, king of Juda, in the twelfth month, the seven and twentieth day of the month: Evilmerodach, king of Babylon, in the year that he began to reign, lifted up the head of Joachin, king of Juda, out of prison.€€ƒxnAnd he spoke kindly to him: and he set his throne above the throne of the kings that were with him in Babylon.€€ƒxqAnd he changed his garments which he had in prison, and he ate bread always before him, all the days of his life.€€ƒxwAnd he appointed him a continual allowance, which was also given him by the king, day by day, all the days of his life.€€ƒxThe First Book of Chronicles€€ƒ`€€ƒqAdam, Seth, Enos,€€ƒxCainan, Malaleel, Jared,€€d1Chr¤dnamelI Chroniclesiosis_named1Chrlabbreviationd1Chrhchapters˜˜6ƒxTThe sons of Japheth: Gomer, and Magog, and Madai, and Javan, Thubal, Mosoch, Thiras.€€ƒx:And the sons of Gomer: Ascenez, and Riphath, and Thogorma.€€ƒx=And the sons of Javan: Elisa and Tharsis, Cethim and Dodanim.€€ƒx:The sons of Cham: Chus, and Mesrai, and Phut, and Chanaan.€€ƒxrAnd the sons of Chus: Saba, and Hevila, Sabatha, and Regma, and Sabathaca. And the sons of Regma: Saba, and Dadan.€€ƒx8Now Chus begot Nemrod: he began to be mighty upon earth.€€ƒx>But Mesraim begot Ludim, and Anamim, and Laabim, and Nephtuim,€€ƒxKPhetrusim also, and Casluim: from whom came the Philistines, and Caphtorim.€€ƒx7And Chanaan begot Sidon his firstborn, and the Hethite,€€ƒx7And the Jebusite, and the Amorrhite, and the Gergesite,€€ƒx0And the Hevite, and the Aracite, and the Sinite,€€ƒx5And the Aradian, and the Samarite, and the Hamathite.€€ƒxjThe sons of Sem: Elam and Asur, and Arphaxad, and Lud, and Aram, and Hus, and Hul, and Gether, and Mosoch.€€ƒx.And Arphaxad begot Sale, and Sale begot Heber.€€ƒx“And to Heber were born two sons, the name of the one was Phaleg, because in his days the earth was divided; and the name of his brother was Jectan.€€ƒx=And Jectan begot Elmodad, and Saleph, and Asarmoth, and Jare,€€ƒx And Adoram, and Usal, and Decla,€€ƒx!And Hebal, and Abimael, and Saba,€€ƒxCAnd Ophir, and Hevila, and Jobab. All these are the sons of Jectan.€€ƒtSem, Arphaxad, Sale,€€ƒuHeber, Phaleg, Ragau,€€ƒuSerug, Nachor, Thare,€€ƒwAbram, this is Abraham.€€ƒx+And the sons of Abraham, Isaac and Ismahel.€€ƒxnAnd these are the generations of them. The firstborn of Ismahel, Nabajoth, then Cedar, and Adbeel, and Mabsam,€€ƒx-And Masma, and Duma, Massa, Hadad, and Thema,€€ƒx4Jetur, Naphis, Cedma: these are the sons of Ismahel.€€ƒxÎAnd the sons of Cetura, Abraham's concubine, whom she bore: Zamran, Jecsan, Madan, Madian, Jesboc, and Sue. And the sons of Jecsan, Saba, and Dadan. And the sons of Dadan: Assurim, and Latussim, and Laomin.€€ƒxlAnd the sons of Madian: Epha, and Epher, and Henoch, and Abida, and Eldaa. All these are the sons of Cetura.€€ƒx;And Abraham begot Isaac: and his sons were Esau and Israel.€€ƒx;The sons of Esau: Eliphaz, Rahuel, Jehus, Ihelom, and Core.€€ƒxOThe sons of Eliphaz: Theman, Omar, Sephi, Gathan, Cenez, and by Thamna, Amalec.€€ƒx.The sons of Rahuel: Nahath, Zara, Samma, Meza.€€ƒx@The sons of Seir: Lotan, Sobal, Sebeon, Ana, Dison, Eser, Disan.€€ƒxCThe sons of Lotan: Hori, Homam. And the sister of Lotan was Thamna.€€ƒx{The sons of Sobal: Alian, and Manahath, and Ebal, Sephi, and Onam. The sons of Sebeon: Aia, and Ana. The son of Ana: Dison.€€ƒx?The sons of Dison: Hamram, and Eseban, and Jethran, and Charan.€€ƒxPThe sons of Eser: Balaan, and Zavan, and Jacan. The sons of Disan: Hus and Aran.€€ƒx©Now these are the kings that reigned in the land of Edom, before there was a king over the children of Israel: Bale the son of Beor: and the name of his city was Denaba.€€ƒxHAnd Bale died, and Jobab the son of Zare of Bosra, reigned in his stead.€€ƒxWAnd when Jobab also was dead, Husam of the land of the Themanites reigned in his stead.€€ƒx˜And Husam also died, and Adad the son of Badad reigned in his stead, and he defeated the Madianites in the land of Moab: the name of his city was Avith.€€ƒxCAnd when Adad also was dead, Semla of Masreca reigned in his stead.€€ƒxUSemla also died, and Saul of Rohoboth, which is near the river, reigned in his stead.€€ƒxHAnd when Saul was dead, Balanan the son of Achobor reigned in his stead.€€ƒx He also died, and Adad reigned in his stead: and the name of his city was Phau, and his wife was called Meetabel the daughter of Matred, the daughter of Mezaab.€€ƒxtAnd after the death of Adad, there began to be dukes in Edom instead of kings: duke Thamna, duke Alva, duke Jetheth,€€ƒx%Duke Oolibama, duke Ela, duke Phinon,€€ƒx%Duke Cenez, duke Theman, duke Mabsar,€€ƒx6Duke Magdiel, duke Hiram. These are the dukes of Edom.€€ƒ`€€ƒxSAnd these are the sons of Israel: Ruben, Simeon, Levi, Juda, Issachar, and Zabulon,€€ƒx/Dan, Joseph, Benjamin, Nephtali, Gad, and Aser.€€ƒxÀThe sons of Juda: Her, Onan and Sela. These three were born to him of the Chanaanitess the daughter of Sue. And Her the firstborn of Juda, was wicked in the sight of the Lord, and he slew him.€€˜7ƒx.And the sons of Phares, were Hesron and Hamul.€€ƒx[And the sons also of Zare: Zamri, and Ethan, and Eman, and Chalchal, and Dara, five in all.€€ƒx\And the sons of Charmi: Achar, who troubled Israel, and sinned by the theft of the anathema.€€ƒxThe sons of Ethan: Azarias,€€ƒxLAnd the sons of Hesron that were born to him: Jerameel, and Ram, and Calubi.€€ƒxTAnd Ram begot Aminadab, and Aminadab begot Nahasson, prince of the children of Juda.€€ƒx-And Nahasson begot Salma, the father of Booz.€€ƒx)And Booz begot Obed, and Obed begot Isai.€€ƒxJAnd Isai begot Eliab his firstborn, the second Abinadab, the third Simmaa,€€ƒx(The fourth, Nathanael, the fifth Raddai,€€ƒx"The sixth Asom, the seventh David.€€ƒx_And their sisters were Sarvia, and Abigail. The sons of Sarvia: Abisai, Joab, and Asael, three.€€ƒx?And Abigail bore Amasa, whose father was Jether the Ismahelite.€€ƒx{And Caleb the son of Hesron took a wife named Azuba, of whom he had Jerioth: and her sons were Jaser, and Sobab, and Ardon.€€ƒxFAnd when Azuba was dead, Caleb took to wife Ephrata: who bore him Hur.€€ƒx*And Hur begot Uri: and Uri begot Bezeleel.€€ƒxœAnd afterwards Hesron went in to the daughter of Machir the father of Galaad, and took her to wife when he was threescore years old: and she bore him Segub.€€ƒxOAnd Segub begot Jair, and he had three and twenty cities in the land of Galaad.€€ƒx˜And he took Gessur, and Aram the towns of Jair, and Canath, and the villages thereof, threescore cities. All these, the sons of Machir father of Galaad.€€ƒxyAnd when Hesron was dead, Caleb went in to Ephrata. Hesron also had to wife Abia who bore him Ashur the father of Thecua.€€ƒxrAnd the sons of Jerameel the firstborn of Hesron, were Ram his firstborn, and Buna, and Aram, and Asom, and Achia.€€ƒxKAnd Jerameel married another wife, named Atara, who was the mother of Onam.€€ƒxKAnd the sons of Ram the firstborn of Jerameel, were Moos, Jamin, and Achar.€€ƒxLAnd Onam had sons Semei, and Jada. And the sons of Semei: Nadab, and Abisur.€€ƒxKAnd the name of Abisur's wife was Abihail, who bore him Ahobban, and Molid.€€ƒxNAnd the sons of Nadab were Saled and Apphaim. And Saled died without children.€€ƒxNBut the son of Apphaim was Jesi: and Jesi begot Sesan. And Sesan begot Oholai.€€ƒxfAnd the sons of Jada the brother of Semei: Jether and Jonathan. And Jether also died without children.€€ƒxFBut Jonathan begot Phaleth, and Ziza. These were the sons of Jerameel.€€ƒxLAnd Sesan had no sons, but daughters and a servant an Egyptian, named Jeraa.€€ƒx=And he gave him his daughter to wife: and she bore him Ethei.€€ƒx/And Ethei begot Nathan, and Nathan begot Zabad.€€ƒx.And Zabad begot Ophlal, and Ophlal begot Obed.€€ƒx$Obed begot Jehu, Jehu begot Azarias.€€ƒx-Azarias begot Helles, and Helles begot Elasa.€€ƒx*Elasa begot Sisamoi, Sisamoi begot Sellum,€€ƒx.Sellum begot Icamia, and Icamia begot Elisama.€€ƒx‹Now the sons of Caleb the brother of Jerameel were Mesa his firstborn, who was the father of Siph: and the sons of Maresa father of Hebron.€€ƒx@And the sons of Hebron, Core, and Thaphua, and Recem, and Samma.€€ƒxEAnd Samma begot Raham, the father of Jercaam, and Recem begot Sammai.€€ƒx8The son of Sammai, Maon: and Maon the father of Bethsur.€€ƒxWAnd Epha the concubine of Caleb bore Haran, and Mosa, and Gezez. And Haran begot Gezez.€€ƒxYAnd the sons of Jahaddai, Rogom, and Joathan, and Gesan, and Phalet, and Epha, and Saaph.€€ƒx:And Maacha the concubine of Caleb bore Saber, and Tharana.€€ƒxAnd Saaph the father of Madmena begot Sue the father of Machbena, and the father of Gabaa. And the daughter of Caleb was Achsa.€€ƒxhThese were the sons of Caleb, the son of Hur the firstborn of Ephrata, Sobal the father of Cariathiarim.€€ƒx>Salma the father of Bethlehem, Hariph the father of Bethgader.€€ƒxVAnd Sobal the father of Cariathiarim had sons: he that saw half of the places of rest.€€ƒxAnd of the kindred of Cariathiarim, the Jethrites, and Aphuthites, and Semathites, and Maserites. Of them came the Saraites, and Esthaolites.€€ƒxvThe sons of Salma, Bethlehem, and Netophathi, the crowns of the house of Joab, and half of the place of rest of Sarai.€€ƒx·And the families of the scribes that dwell in Jabes, singing and making melody, and abiding in tents. These are the Cinites, who came of Calor (Chamath) father of the house of Rechab.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¢Now these were the sons of David that were born to him in Hebron: the firstborn Amnon of Achinoam the Jezrahelitess, the second Daniel of Abigail the Carmelitess.€€ƒxpThe third Absalom the son of Maacha the daughter of Tolmai king of Gessur, the fourth Adonias the son of Aggith,€€ƒxDThe fifth Saphatias of Abital, the sixth Jethrahem of Egla his wife.€€˜ƒxƒAnd these sons were born to him in Jerusalem: Simmaa, and Sobab, and Nathan, and Solomon, four of Bethsabee the daughter of Ammiel.€€ƒxJebaar also and Elisama,€€ƒx1And Eliphaleth, and Noge, and Nepheg, and Japhia,€€ƒx.And Elisama, and Eliada, and Elipheleth, nine:€€ƒx]All these the sons of David, beside the sons of the concubines: and they had a sister Thamar.€€ƒxQAnd Solomon's son was Roboam: whose son Abia begot Asa. And his son was Josaphat,€€ƒxEThe father of Joram: and Joram begot Ochozias, of whom was born Joas:€€ƒxAAnd his son Amasias begot Azarias. And Joathan the son of Azarias€€ƒx?Begot Achaz, the father of Ezechias, of whom was born Manasses.€€ƒx-And Manasses begot Amon the father of Josias.€€ƒxmAnd the sons of Josias were, the firstborn Johanan, the second Joakim, the third Sedecias, the fourth Sellum.€€ƒx+Of Joakim was born Jechonias, and Sedecias.€€ƒx+The sons of Jechonias were Asir, Salathiel,€€ƒx<Melchiram, Phadaia, Senneser and Jecemia, Sama, and Nadabia.€€ƒxhOf Phadaia were born Zorobabel and Semei. Zorobabel begot Mosollam, Hananias, and Salomith their sister:€€ƒxEHasaba also, and Ohol, and Barachias, and Hasadias, Josabhesed, five.€€ƒxšAnd the son of Hananias was Phaltias the father of Jeseias, whose son was Raphaia. And his son was Arnan, of whom was born Obdia, whose son was Sechenias.€€ƒxvThe son of Sechenias was Semeia, whose sons were Hattus, and Jegaal, and Baria, and Naaria, and Saphat, six in number.€€ƒx?The sons of Naaria, Elioenai, and Ezechias, and Ezricam, three.€€ƒxlThe sons of Elioenai, Oduia, and Eliasub, and Pheleia, and Accub, and Johanan, and Dalaia, and Anani, seven.€€ƒ`€€ƒx@The sons of Juda: Phares, Hesron, and Charmi and Hur, and Sobal.€€ƒxnAnd Raia the son of Sobal begot Jahath, of whom were born Ahumai, and Laad. These are the families of Sarathi.€€ƒxsAnd this is the posterity of Etam: Jezrahel, and Jesema, And Jedebos: and the name of their sister was Asalelphuni.€€˜+ƒx>And Assur the father of Thecua had two wives, Halaa and Naara:€€ƒx]And Naara bore him Ozam, and Hepher, and Themani, and Ahasthari: these are the sons of Naara.€€ƒx1And the sons of Halaa, Sereth, Isaar, and Ethnan.€€ƒxLAnd Cos begot Anob, and Soboba, and the kindred of Aharehel the son of Arum.€€ƒx…And Jabes was more honourable than any of his brethren, and his mother called his name Jabes, saying: Because I bore him with sorrow.€€ƒxãAnd Jabes called upon the God of Israel, saying: If blessing thou wilt bless me, and wilt enlarge my borders, and thy hand be with me, and thou save me from being oppressed by evil. And God granted him the things he prayed for.€€ƒxGAnd Caleb the brother of Sua begot Mahir, who was the father of Esthon.€€ƒxkAnd Esthon begot Bethrapha, and Phesse, and Tehinna father of the city of Naas: these are the men of Recha.€€ƒxaAnd the sons of Cenez were Othoniel, and Saraia. And the sons of Othoniel, Hathath, and Maonathi.€€ƒxnMaonathi begot Ophra, and Saraia begot Joab the father of the Valley of artificers: for artificers were there.€€ƒxcAnd the sons of Caleb the son of Jephone, were Hir, and Ela, and Naham. And the sons of Ela: Cenez.€€ƒx>The sons also of Jaleleel: Ziph, and Zipha, Thiria and Asrael.€€ƒx€And the sons of Esra, Jether, and Mered, and Epher, and Jalon, and he begot Mariam, and Sammai, and Jesba the father of Esthamo.€€ƒxÇAnd his wife Judaia, bore Jared the father of Gedor, and Heber the father of Socho, and Icuthiel the father of Zanoe. And these are the sons of Bethia the daughter of Pharao, whom Mered took to wife.€€ƒxpAnd the sons of his wife Odaia the sister of Naham the father of Celia, Garmi, and Esthamo, who was of Machathi.€€ƒxrThe sons also of Simon, Amnon, and Rinna the son of Hanan, and Thilon. And the sons of Jesi Zoheth, and Benzoheth.€€ƒx®The sons of Sela the son of Juda: Her the father of Lecha, and Laada the father of Maresa, and the families of the house of them that wrought fine linen in the House of oath.€€ƒx¥And he that made the sun to stand, and the men of Lying, and Secure, and Burning, who were princes in Moab, and who returned into Lahem. Now these are things of old.€€ƒxtThese are the potters, and they dwelt in Plantations, and Hedges, with the king for his works, and they abode there.€€ƒx8The sons of Simeon: Namuel and Jamin, Jarib, Zara, Saul:€€ƒx.Sellum his son, Mapsam his son, Masma his son.€€ƒxAThe sons of Masma: Hamuel his son, Zachur his son, Semei his son.€€ƒx The sons of Semei were sixteen, and six daughters: but his brethren had not many sons, and the whole kindred could not reach to the sum of the children of Juda.€€ƒx7And they dwelt in Bersabee, and Molada, and Hasarsuhal,€€ƒx(And in Bala, and in Asom, and in Tholad,€€ƒx-And in Bathuel, and in Horma, and in Siceleg,€€ƒx{And in Bethmarchaboth, and in Hasarsusim, and in Bethberai, and in Saarim. These were their cities unto the reign of David.€€ƒxPTheir towns also were Etam, and Aen, Remmon, and Thochen, and Asan, five cities.€€ƒxƒAnd all their villages round about these cities as far as Baal. This was their habitation, and the distribution of their dwellings.€€ƒx:And Mosabab and Jemlech, and Josaphat, the son of Amasias,€€ƒxJAnd Joel, and Jehu the son of Josabia the son of Saraia, the son of Asiel,€€ƒxTAnd Elioenai, and Jacoba, and Isuhaia, and Asaia, and Adiel, and Ismiel, and Banaia,€€ƒxaZiza also the son of Sephei the son of Allon the son of Idaia the son of Semri the son of Samaia.€€ƒxlThese were named princes in their kindreds, and in the houses of their families were multiplied exceedingly.€€ƒxtAnd they went forth to enter into Gador as far as to the east side of the valley, to seek pastures for their flocks.€€ƒxAnd they found fat pastures, and very good, and a country spacious, and quiet, and fruitful, in which some of the race of Cham had dwelt before.€€ƒyAnd these whose names are written above, came in the days of Ezechias king of Juda: and they beat down their tents, and slew the inhabitants that were found there, and utterly destroyed them unto this day: and they dwelt in their place, because they found there fat pastures.€€ƒx¢Some also of the children of Simeon, five hundred men, went into mount Seir, having for their captains Phaltias and Naaria and Raphaia and Oziel the sons of Jesi:€€ƒx|And they slew the remnant of the Amalecites, who had been able to escape, and they dwelt there in their stead unto this day.€€ƒ`€€ƒxëNow the sons of Ruben the firstborn of Israel, (for he was his firstborn: but forasmuch as he defiled his father's bed, his first birthright was given to the sons of Joseph the son of Israel, and he was not accounted for the firstborn.€€ƒx‡But of the race of Juda, who was the strongest among his brethren, came the princes: but the first birthright was accounted to Joseph.)€€ƒxYThe sons then of Ruben the firstborn of Israel were Enoch, and Phallu, Esron, and Charmi.€€˜ƒx*Micha his son, Reia his son, Baal his son,€€ƒxzBeera his son, whom Thelgathphalnasar king of the Assyrians carried away captive, and he was prince in the tribe of Ruben.€€ƒxxAnd his brethren, and all his kindred, when they were numbered by their families, had for princes Jehiel, and Zacharias.€€ƒxiAnd Bala the son of Azaz, the son of Samma, the son of Joel, dwelt in Aroer as far as Nebo, and Beelmeon.€€ƒx¤And eastward he had his habitation as far as the entrance of the desert, and the river Euphrates. For they possessed a great number of cattle in the land of Galaad.€€ƒx©And in the days of Saul they fought against the Agarites, and slew them, and dwelt in their tents in their stead, in all the country, that looketh to the east of Galaad.€€ƒxWAnd the children of Gad dwelt over against them in the land of Basan, as far as Selcha:€€ƒxGJohel the chief, and Saphan the second: and Janai, and Saphat in Basan.€€ƒx“And their brethren according to the houses of their kindreds, were Michael and Mosollam, and Sebe, and Jorai, and Jacan, and Zie, and Heber, seven.€€ƒxThese were the sons of Abihail, the son of Huri, the son of Jara, the son of Galaad, the son of Michael, the son of Jesisi, the son of Jeddo, the son of Buz.€€ƒx]And their brethren the sons of Abdiel, the son of Guni, chief of the house in their families,€€ƒxsAnd they dwelt in Galaad, and in Basan and in the towns thereof, and in all the suburbs of Saron, unto the borders.€€ƒxhAll these were numbered in the days of Joathan king of Juda, and in the days of Jeroboam king of Israel.€€ƒxçThe Sons of Ruben, and of Gad, and of the half tribe of Manasses, fighting men, bearing shields, and swords, and bending the bow, and trained up to battles, four and forty thousand seven hundred and threescore that went out to war.€€ƒxJThey fought against the Agarites: but the Itureans, and Naphis, and Nodab,€€ƒxÆGave them help. And the Agarites were delivered into their hands, and all that were with them, because they called upon God in the battle: and he heard them, because they had put their faith in him.€€ƒx­And they took all that they possessed, of camels fifty thousand, and of sheep two hundred and fifty thousand, and of asses two thousand, and of men a hundred thousand souls.€€ƒxnAnd many fell down slain: for it was the battle of the Lord. And they dwelt in their stead till the captivity.€€ƒx¨And the children of the half tribe of Manasses possessed the land, from the borders of Basan unto Baal, Hermon, and Sanir, and mount Hermon, for their number was great.€€ƒxÇAnd these were the heads of the house of their kindred, Epher, and Jesi, and Eliel, and Esriel, and Jeremia, and Odoia, and Jediel, most valiant and powerful men, and famous chiefs in their families.€€ƒx„But they forsook the God of their fathers, and went astray after the gods of the people of the land, whom God destroyed before them.€€ƒy!And the God of Israel stirred up the spirit of Phul king of the Assyrians. and the spirit of Thelgathphalnasar king of Assur: and he carried away Ruben, and Gad, and the half tribe of Manasses, and brought them to Lahela, and to Habor, and to Ara, and to the river of Gozan, unto this day.€€ƒ`€€ƒx0The sons of Levi were Gerson, Caath, and Merari.€€ƒx3The Sons of Caath: Amram, Isaar, Hebron, and Oziel.€€ƒxfThe children of Amram: Aaron, Moses, and Mary. The Sons of Aaron: Nadab and Abiu, Eleazar and Ithamar.€€˜Qƒx,And Abisue begot Bocci, and Bocci begot Ozi.€€ƒx.Ozi begot Zaraias, and Zaraias begot Maraioth.€€ƒx6And Maraioth begot Amarias, and Amarias begot Achitob.€€ƒx.Achitob begot Sadoc, and Sadoc begot Achimaas.€€ƒx.Achimaas begot Azarias, Azarias begot Johanan,€€ƒxrJohanan begot Azarias. This is he that executed the priestly office in the house which Solomon built in Jerusalem.€€ƒx5And Azarias begot Amarias, and Amarias begot Achitob.€€ƒx0And Achitob begot Sadoc, and Sadoc begot Sellum,€€ƒx0Sellum begot Helcias, and Helcias begot Azarias,€€ƒx1Azarias begot Saraias, and Saraias begot Josedec.€€ƒxeNow Josedec went out, when the Lord carried away Juda, and Jerusalem, by the hands of Nabuchodonosor.€€ƒx3So the sons of Levi were Gerson, Caath, and Merari.€€ƒx?And these are the names of the sons of Gerson: Lobni and Semei.€€ƒx;The sons of Caath: Amram, and Isaar, and Hebron, and Oziel.€€ƒxdThe sons of Merari: Moholi and Musi. And these are the kindreds of Levi according to their families.€€ƒx8Of Gerson: Lobni his son, Jahath his son, Zamma his son,€€ƒx:Joah his son, Addo his son, Zara his son, Jethrai his son.€€ƒx@The sons of Caath, Aminadab his son, Core his son, Asir his son,€€ƒx/Elcana his son, Abiasaph his son, Asir his son,€€ƒx<Thahath his son, Uriel his son, Ozias his son, Saul his son.€€ƒx)The sons of Elcana: Amasai, and Achimoth.€€ƒx?And Elcana. The sons of Elcana: Sophai his son, Nahath his son,€€ƒx/Eliab his son, Jeroham his son, Elcana his son.€€ƒx4The sons of Samuel: the firstborn Vasseni, and Abia.€€ƒxJAnd the sons of Merari, Moholi: Lobni his son, Semei his son, Oza his son,€€ƒx.Sammaa his son, Haggia his son, Asaia his son.€€ƒxlThese are they, whom David set over the singing men of the house of the Lord, after that the ark was placed.€€ƒxºAnd they ministered before the tabernacle of the testimony, with singing, until Solomon built the house of the Lord in Jerusalem, and they stood according to their order in the ministry.€€ƒxyAnd these are they that stood with their sons, of the sons of Caath, Hemam a singer, the son of Joel, the son of Sammuel,€€ƒxJThe son of Elcana, the son of Jeroham, the son of Eliel, the son of Thohu,€€ƒxIThe son of Suph, the son of Elcana, the son of Mahath, the son of Amasai,€€ƒxNThe son of Elcana, the son of Johel, the son of Azarias, the son of Sophonias,€€ƒxJThe son of Thahath, the son of Asir, the son of Abiasaph, the son of Core,€€ƒxGThe son of Isaar, the son of Caath, the son of Levi, the son of Israel.€€ƒxaAnd his brother Asaph, who stood on his right hand, Asaph the son of Barachias, the son of Samaa.€€ƒx;The son of Michael, the son of Basaia, the, son of Melchia.€€ƒx6The son of Athanai, the son of Zara, the son of Adaia.€€ƒx5The son of Ethan, the son of Zamma, the son of Semei.€€ƒx4The son of Jeth, the son of Gerson, the son of Levi.€€ƒxsAnd the sons of Merari their brethren, on the left hand, Ethan the son of Cusi, the son of Abdi, the son of Meloch,€€ƒx:The son of Hasabia, the son of Amasai, the son of Helcias,€€ƒx5The son of Amasai, the son of Boni, the son of Somer,€€ƒxGThe son of Moholi, the son of Musi, the son of Merari, the son of Levi.€€ƒxtTheir brethren also the Levites, who were appointed for all the ministry of the tabernacle of the house of the Lord.€€ƒxæBut Aaron and his sons offered burnt offerings upon the altar of holocausts, and upon the altar of incense, for every work of the holy of holies: and to pray for Israel according to all that Moses the servant of God had commanded.€€ƒxRAnd these are the sons of Aaron: Eleazar his son, Phinees his son, Abisue his son,€€ƒx,Bocci his son, Ozi his son, Zarahia his son,€€ƒx3Meraioth his son, Amarias his son, Achitob his son,€€ƒx Sadoc his son, Achimaas his son.€€ƒxœAnd these are their dwelling places by the towns and confines, to wit, of the sons of Aaron, of the families of the Caathites: for they fell to them by lot.€€ƒxSAnd they gave them Hebron in the land of Juda, and the suburbs thereof round about:€€ƒxEBut the fields of the city, and the villages to Caleb son of Jephone.€€ƒxdAnd to the sons of Aaron they gave the cities for refuge Hebron, and Lobna, and the suburbs thereof,€€ƒxTAnd Jether and Esthemo, with their suburbs, and Helon, and Dabir with their suburbs:€€ƒx-Asan also, and Bethsames, with their suburbs.€€ƒx«And out of the tribe of Benjamin: Gabee and its suburbs, Almath with its suburbs, Anathoth also with its suburbs: all their cities throughout their families were thirteen.€€ƒx}And to the sons of Caath that remained of their kindred they gave out of the half tribe of Manasses ten cities in possession.€€ƒxÂAnd to the sons of Gerson by their families out of the tribe of Issachar, and out of the tribe of Aser, and out of the tribe of Nephtali, and out of the tribe Manasses in Basan, thirteen cities.€€ƒx¤And to the sons of Merari by their families out of the tribe of Ruben, and out of the tribe of Gad, and out of the tribe of Zabulon, they gave by lot twelve cities.€€ƒxMAnd the children of Israel gave to the Levites the cities, and their suburbs.€€ƒxÉAnd they gave them by lot, out of the tribe of the sons of Juda, and out of the tribe of the sons of Simeon, and out of the tribe of the sons of Benjamin, these cities which they called by their names.€€ƒxxAnd to them that were of the kindred of the sons of Caath, and the cities in their borders were of the tribe of Ephraim.€€ƒxhAnd they gave the cities of refuge Sichem with its suburbs in mount Ephraim, and Gazer with its suburbs,€€ƒx=Jecmaan also with its suburbs, and Beth-horon in like manner,€€ƒx;Helon also with its suburbs, and Gethremmon in like manner,€€ƒx—And out of the half tribe of Manasses, Aner and its suburbs, Baalam and its suburbs, to wit, to them that were left of the family of the sons of Caath.€€ƒxŒAnd to the sons of Gersom, out the kindred of the half tribe of Manasses, Gaulon, in Basan, and its suburbs, and Astharoth with its suburbs.€€ƒxSOut of the tribe of Issachar, Cedes and its suburbs, and Dabereth with its suburbs;€€ƒx7Ramoth also and its suburbs, and Anem with its suburbs.€€ƒxOAnd out of the tribe of Aser: Masal with its suburbs, and Abdon in like manner;€€ƒx7Hucac also and its suburbs, and Rohol with its suburbs.€€ƒx|And out of the tribe of Nephtali, Cedes in Galilee and its suburbs, Hamon with its suburbs, and Cariathaim, and its suburbs.€€ƒx{And to the sons of Merari that remained: out of the tribe of Zabulon, Remmono and its suburbs, and Thabor with its suburbs.€€ƒx°Beyond the Jordan also over against Jericho, on the east side of the Jordan and out of the tribe of Ruben, Bosor in the wilderness with its suburbs, and Jassa with its suburbs;€€ƒx=Cademoth also and its suburbs, and Mephaath with its suburbs;€€ƒxeMoreover also out of the tribe of Gad, Ramoth in Galaad and its suburbs, and Manaim with its suburbs;€€ƒx:Hesebon also with its suburbs, and Jazer with its suburbs.€€ƒ`€€ƒxGNow the sons of Issachar were Thola, and Phua, Jasub and Simeron, four.€€ƒxîThe sons of Thola: Ozi and Raphaia, and Jeriel, and Jemai, and Jebsem, and Samuel, chiefs of the houses of their kindreds. Of the posterity of Thola were numbered in the days of David, two and twenty thousand six hundred most valiant men.€€ƒxiThe sons of Ozi: Izrahia, of whom were born Michael, and Obadia, and Joel, and Jesia, five all great men.€€˜(ƒx~Their brethren also throughout all the house of Issachar, were numbered fourscore and seven thousand most valiant men for war.€€ƒx?The sons of Benjamin were Bela, and Bechor, and Jadihel, three.€€ƒx·The sons of Bela: Esbon, and Ozi, and Ozial, and Jerimoth and Urai, five chiefs of their families, and most valiant warriors, and their number was twenty-two thousand and thirty-four.€€ƒx§And the sons of Bechor were Zamira, and Joas, and Eliezer, and Elioenai, and Amai, and Jerimoth, and Abia, and Anathoth, and Almath: all these were the sons of Bechor.€€ƒx{And they were numbered by the families, heads of their kindreds, most valiant men for war, twenty thousand and two hundred.€€ƒx…And the son of Jadihel: Balan. And the sons of Balan: Jehus and Benjamin, and Aod, and Chanana, and Zethan and Tharsis, and Ahisahar.€€ƒx…All these were sons of Jadihel, heads of their kindreds, most valiant men, seventeen thousand and two hundred fifty to go out to war.€€ƒxCSepham also and Hapham the sons of Hir: and Hasim the sons of Aher.€€ƒxTAnd the sons of Nephtali were Jasiel, and Guni, and Jezer, and Sellum, sons of Bala.€€ƒx_And the son of Manasses, Ezriel: and his concubine the Syrian bore Machir the father of Galaad.€€ƒxšAnd Machir took wives for his sons Happhim, and Saphan: and he had a sister named Maacha: the name of the second was Salphaad, and Salphaad had daughters.€€ƒx’And Maacha the wife of Machir bore a son, and she called his name Phares: and the name of his brother was Sares: and his sons were Ulam and Recen.€€ƒxaAnd the son of Ulam, Baden. These are the sons of Galaad, the son of Machir, the son of Manasses.€€ƒxCAnd his sister named Queen bore Goodlyman, and Abiezer, and Mohola.€€ƒxAAnd the sons of Semida were Ahiu, and Sechem, and Leci and Aniam.€€ƒxxAnd the sons of Ephraim were Suthala, Bared his son, Thahath his son, Elada his son, Thahath his son, and his son Zabad,€€ƒx”And his son Suthala, and his son Ezer, and Elad: and the men of Geth born in the land slew them, because they came down to invade their possessions.€€ƒxQAnd Ephraim their father mourned many days, and his brethren came to comfort him.€€ƒx‘And he went in to his wife: and she conceived and bore a son, and he called his name Beria, because he was born when it went evil with his house:€€ƒxVAnd his daughter was Sara, who built Bethoron, the nether and the upper, and Ozensara.€€ƒxEAnd Rapha was his son, and Reseph, and Thale, of whom was born Thaan,€€ƒx<Who begot Laadan: and his son was Ammiud, who begot Elisama,€€ƒx0Of whom was born Nun, who had Josue for his son.€€ƒxÁAnd their possessions and habitations were Bethel with her daughters, and eastward Noran, and westward Gazer and her daughters, Sichem also with her daughters, as far as Asa with her daughters.€€ƒxÌAnd by the borders of the sons of Manasses Bethsan and her daughters, Thanach and her daughters, Mageddo and her daughters: Dor and her daughters: in these dwelt the children of Joseph, the son of Israel.€€ƒxYThe children of Aser were Jemna, and Jesua, and Jessui, and Baria, and Sara their sister.€€ƒxIAnd the sons of Baria: Haber, and Melchiel: he is the father of Barsaith.€€ƒxFAnd Heber begot Jephlat, and Somer, and Hotham, and Suaa their sister.€€ƒxTThe sons of Jephlat: Phosech, and Chamaal, and Asoth: these are the sons of Jephlat.€€ƒx9And the sons of Somer: Ahi, and Roaga and Haba, and Aram.€€ƒxJAnd the sons of Helem his brother: Supha, and Jemna, and Selles, and Amal.€€ƒxAThe sons of Supha: Sue, Hernapher, and Sual, and Beri, and Jamra.€€ƒx<Bosor and Hod, and Samma, and Salusa, and Jethran, and Bera.€€ƒx2The sons of Jether: Jephone, and Phaspha, and Ara.€€ƒx2And the sons of Olla: Aree, and Haniel, and Resia.€€ƒxÂAll these were sons of Aser, heads of their families, choice and most valiant captains of captains: and the number of them that were of the age that was fit for war, was six and twenty thousand.€€ƒ`€€ƒxINow Benjamin begot Bale his firstborn, Asbel the second, Ahara the third,€€ƒx&Nohaa the fourth, and Rapha the fifth.€€ƒx5And the sons of Bale were Addar, and Gera, and Abiud,€€˜(ƒx#And Gera, and Sephuphan, and Huram.€€ƒxbThese are the sons of Abed, heads of families that dwelt in Gabaa, who were removed into Manahath.€€ƒxJAnd Naaman, and Achia, and Gera he removed them, and begot Oza, and Ahiud.€€ƒxSAnd Saharim begot in the land of Moab, after he sent away Husim and Bara his wives.€€ƒxGAnd he begot of Hodes his wife Jobab, and Sebia, and Mosa, and Molchom,€€ƒxMAnd Jehus and Sechia, and Marma. These were his sons heads of their families.€€ƒx&And Mehusim begot Abitob, and Elphaal.€€ƒxeAnd the sons of Elphaal were Heber, and Misaam, and Samad: who built Ono, and Lod, and its daughters.€€ƒxpAnd Baria, and Sama were heads of their kindreds that dwelt in Aialon: these drove away the inhabitants of Geth.€€ƒx"And Ahio, and Sesac, and Jerimoth,€€ƒx!And Zabadia, and Arod, and Heder,€€ƒx5And Michael, and Jespha, and Joha, the sons of Baria.€€ƒx-And Zabadia, and Mosollam, Hezeci, and Heber,€€ƒx5And Jesamari, and Jezlia, and Jobab, sons of Elphaal,€€ƒx!And Jacim, and Zechri, and Zabdi,€€ƒx&And Elioenai, and Selethai, and Elial,€€ƒx7And Adaia, and Baraia, and Samareth, the sons of Semei.€€ƒx"And Jespham, and Heber, and Eliel,€€ƒx!And Abdon, and Zechri, and Hanan,€€ƒx&And Hanania, and Elam, and Anathothia.€€ƒx,And Jephdaia, and Phanuel the sons of Sesac.€€ƒx%And Samsari, and Sohoria and Otholia,€€ƒx6And Jersia, and Elia, and Zechri, the sons of Jeroham.€€ƒxQThese were the chief fathers, and heads of their families who dwelt in Jerusalem.€€ƒxCAnd at Gabaon dwelt Abigabaon, and the name of his wife was Maacha:€€ƒxCAnd his firstborn son Abdon, and Sur, and Cis, and Baal, and Nadab,€€ƒx/And Gedor, and Ahio, and Zacher, and Macelloth:€€ƒxgAnd Macelloth begot Samaa: and they dwelt over against their brethren in Jerusalem with their brethren.€€ƒxfAnd Ner begot Cis and Cis begot Saul. And Saul begot Jonathan and Melchisua, and Abinadab, and Esbaal.€€ƒxAAnd the son of Jonathan was Meribbaal: and Meribbaal begot Micha.€€ƒxEAnd the sons of Micha were Phithon, and Melech, and Tharaa, and Ahaz.€€ƒx[And Ahaz begot Joada: and Joada begot Alamath, and Azmoth, and Zamri: and Zamri begot Mosa,€€ƒxRAnd Mosa begot Banaa, whose son was Rapha, of whom was born Elasa, who begot Asel.€€ƒxzAnd Asel had six sons whose names were Ezricam, Bochru, Ismahel, Saris, Obdia, and Hanan. All these were the sons of Asel.€€ƒxjAnd the sons of Esec, his brother, were Ulam the firstborn, and Jehus the second, and Eliphalet the third.€€ƒx²And the sons of Ulam were most valiant men, and archers of great strength: and they had many sons and grandsons, even to a hundred and fifty. All these were children of Benjamin.€€ƒ`€€ƒx©And all Israel was numbered: and the sum of them was written in the book of the kings of Israel, and Juda: and they were carried away to Babylon for their transgression.€€ƒxNow the first that dwelt in their possessions, and in their cities, were the Israelites, and the priests, and the Levites, and the Nathineans.€€ƒxAnd in Jerusalem dwelt of the children of Juda, and of the children of Benjamin, and of the children of Ephraim, and of Manasses.€€˜,ƒx1And of Siloni: Asaia the firstborn, and his sons.€€ƒxKAnd of the sons of Zara: Jehuel and their brethren, six hundred and ninety.€€ƒxZAnd of the sons of Benjamin: Salo the son of Mosollam, the son of Oduia, the son of Asana:€€ƒx”And Jobania the son of Jeroham: and Ela the son of Ozi, the son of Mochori and Mosallam the son of Saphatias, the son of Rahuel, the son of Jebania:€€ƒx‰And their brethren by their families, nine hundred and fifty-six. All these were heads of their families, by the houses of their fathers.€€ƒx0And of the priests: Jedaia, Joiarib, and Jachin:€€ƒxAnd Azarias the son of Helcias, the son of Mosollam, the son of Sadoc, the son of Maraioth, the son of Achitob, high priest of the house of God.€€ƒxµAnd Adaias the son of Jeroham, the son of Phassur, the son of Melchias, and Maasai the son of Adiel, the son of Jezra, the son of Mosollam, the son of Mosollamith, the son of Emmer.€€ƒxAnd their brethren heads in their families a thousand seven hundred and threescore, very strong and able men for the work of the ministry in the house of God.€€ƒxjAnd of the Levites: Semeia the son of Hassub the son of Ezricam, the son of Hasebia of the sons of Merari.€€ƒxiAnd Bacbacar the carpenter, and Galal, and Mathania the son of Micha, the son of Zechri the son of Asaph:€€ƒx™And Obdia the son of Semeia, the son of Galal, the son of Idithum: and Barachia the son of Asa, the son of Elcana, who dwelt in the suburbs of Netophati.€€ƒxhAnd the porters were Sellum, and Accub, and Telmon, and Ahiman: and their brother Sellum was the prince,€€ƒxUUntil that time, in the king's gate eastward, the sons of Levi waited by their turns.€€ƒyBut Sellum the son of Core, the son of Abiasaph, the son of Core, with his brethren and his father's house, the Corites were over the works of the service, keepers of the gates of the tabernacle: and their families in turns were keepers of the entrance of the camp of the Lord.€€ƒxAAnd Phinees the son of Eleazar, was their prince before the Lord,€€ƒx_And Zacharias the son of Mosollamia, was porter of the gate of the tabernacle of the testimony:€€ƒx¼All these that were chosen to be porters at the gates, were two hundred and twelve: the they were registered in their proper towns: whom David and Samuel the seer appointed in their trust.€€ƒxjAs well them as their sons, to keep the gates of the house of the Lord, and the tabernacle by their turns.€€ƒxcIn four quarters were the porters: that is to say, toward the east, and west, and north, and south.€€ƒxXAnd their brethren dwelt in village, and came upon their sabbath days from time to time.€€ƒxTo these four Levites were committed the whole number of the porters, and they were over the chambers, and treasures, of the house of the Lord.€€ƒx„And they abode in their watches round about the temple of the Lord: that when it was time, they might open the gates in the morning.€€ƒx‡And some of their stock had the charge of the vessels for the ministry: for the vessels were both brought in and carried out by number.€€ƒxSome of them also had the instruments of the sanctuary committed unto them, and the charge of the fine flour, and wine, and oil, and frankincense, and spices.€€ƒx=And the sons of the priests made the ointments of the spices.€€ƒxuAnd Mathathias a Levite, the firstborn of Sellum the Corite, was overseer of such things as were fried the fryingpan.€€ƒx{And some of the sons of Caath their brethren, were over the loaves of proposition, to prepare always new for every sabbath.€€ƒx³These are the chief of the singing men of the families of the Levites, who dwelt in the chambers, by the temple, that they might serve continually day and night in their ministry.€€ƒxHThe heads of the Levites, princes in their families, abode in Jerusalem.€€ƒxUAnd in Gabaon dwelt Jehiel the father of Gabaon, and the name of his wife was Maacha:€€ƒxHHis firstborn son Abdon, and Sur, and Cis, and Baal, and Ner, and Nadab,€€ƒx3Gedor also, and Ahio, and Zacharias, and Macelloth.€€ƒxfAnd Macelloth begot Samaan: these dwelt over against their brethren in Jerusalem, with their brethren.€€ƒxgNow Ner begot Cis: and Cis begot Saul: and Saul begot Jonathan and Melchisua, and Abinadab, and Esbaal.€€ƒxBAnd the son of Jonathan, was Meribbaal: and Meribbaal begot Micha.€€ƒxFAnd the sons of Micha, were Phithon, and Melech, and Tharaa, and Ahaz.€€ƒxYAnd Ahaz begot Jara, and Jara begot Alamath, and Azmoth, and Zamri. And Zamri begot Mosa.€€ƒxKAnd Mosa begot Banaa: whose son Raphaia begot Elasa: of whom was born Asel.€€ƒxpAnd Asel had six sons whose names are, Ezricam Bochru, Ismahel, Saria, Obdia, Hanan: these are the sons of Asel.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‰Now the Philistines fought against Israel, and the men of Israel fled from before the Philistines, and fell down wounded in mount Gelboe.€€ƒxˆAnd the Philistines drew near pursuing after Saul, and his sons, and they killed Jonathan, and Abinadab, and Melchisua the sons of Saul.€€ƒx_And the battle grew hard against Saul and the archers reached him, and wounded him with arrows.€€ƒxcAnd when his armourbearer saw it, to wit, that Saul was dead, he also fell upon his sword and died.€€ƒxBSo Saul died, and his three sons, and all his house fell together.€€ƒxÔAnd when the men of Israel, that dwelt in the plains, saw this, they fled: and Saul and his sons being dead, they forsook their cities, and were scattered up and down: and the Philistines came, and dwelt in them.€€ƒxAnd the next day the Philistines taking away the spoils of them that were slain, found Saul and his sons lying on mount Gelboe.€€ƒx¼And when they had stripped him, and out off his head, and taken away his armour, they sent it into their land, to be carried about, and shewn in the temples of the idols and to the people.€€ƒxoAnd his armour they dedicated in the temple of their god, and his head they fastened up in the temple of Dagon.€€ƒxcAnd when the men of Jabes Galaad had heard this, to wit, all that the Philistines had done to Saul,€€ƒx¾All the valiant men of them arose, and took the bodies of Saul and of his sons, and brought them to Jabes, and buried their bones under the oak that was in Jabes, and they fasted seven days.€€ƒx£So Saul died for his iniquities, because he transgressed the commandment of the Lord, which he had commanded, and kept it not: and moreover consulted also a witch,€€ƒxiAnd trusted not in the Lord: therefore he slew him, and transferred his kingdom to David the son of Isai.€€ƒ`€€ƒx_Then all Israel gathered themselves to David in Hebron, saying: We are thy bone, and thy flesh.€€ƒxÖYesterday also, and the day before when Saul was king, thou wast he that leddest out and broughtest in Israel: for the Lord thy God said to thee: Thou shalt feed my people Israel, and thou shalt be ruler over them.€€ƒxİSo all the ancients of Israel came to the king to Hebron, and David made a covenant with them before the Lord: and they anointed him king over Israel according to the word of the Lord which he spoke in the hand of Samuel.€€˜/ƒx‡And the inhabitants of Jebus said to David: Thou shalt not come in here. But David took the castle of Sion, which is the city of David.€€ƒx¡And he said: Whosoever shall first strike the Jebusites, shall be the head and chief captain. And Joab the son of Sarvia went up first, and was made the general.€€ƒxMAnd David dwelt in the castle, and therefore it was called the city of David,€€ƒx\And he built the city round about from Mello all round, and Joab built the rest of the city.€€ƒxMAnd David went on growing and increasing, and the Lord of hosts was with him.€€ƒxThese are the chief of the valiant man of David, who helped him to be made king over all Israel, according to the word of the Lord, which he spoke to Israel.€€ƒx°And this is the number of the heroes of David: Jesbaam the son of Hachamoni the chief among the thirty: he lifted up his spear against three hundred wounded by him at one time.€€ƒxYAnd after him was Eleazar his uncle's son the Ahohite, who was one of the three mighties.€€ƒx¾He was with David in Phesdomim, when the Philistines were gathered to that place to battle: and the field of that country was full of barley, and the people fled from before the Philistines.€€ƒx“But these men stood in the midst of the field, and defended it: and they slew the Philistines, and the Lord gave a great deliverance to his people.€€ƒxšAnd three of the thirty captains went down to the rock, wherein David was, to the cave of Odollam, when the Philistines encamped in the valley of Raphaim.€€ƒxJAnd David was in a hold, and the garrison of the Philistines in Bethlehem.€€ƒxrAnd David longed, and said: O that some man would give me water of the cistern of Bethlehem, which is in the gate.€€ƒxñAnd these three broke through the midst of the camp of the Philistines, and drew water out of the cistern of Bethlehem, which was in the gate, and brought it to David to drink: and he would not drink of it, but rather offered it to the Lord,€€ƒx÷Saying: God forbid that I should do this in the sight of my God, and should drink the blood of these men: for with the danger of their lives they have brought me the water. And therefore he would not drink. These things did the three most valiant.€€ƒxšAnd Abisai the brother of Joab, he was chief of three, and he lifted up his spear against three hundred whom he slew, and he was renowned among the three,€€ƒxfAnd illustrious among the second three, and their captain: but yet he attained not to the first three.€€ƒxÀBanaias the son of Joiada a most valiant man, of Cabseel, who had done many acts: he slew the two ariels of Moab: and he went down, and killed a lion in the midst of a pit in the time of snow.€€ƒxáAnd he slew an Egyptian, whose stature was of five cubits, and who had a spear like a weaver's beam: and he went down to him with a staff, and plucked away the spear, that he held in his hand, and slew him with his own spear.€€ƒxZThese things did Banaias the son of Joiada, who was renowned among the three valiant ones,€€ƒxhAnd the first among the thirty, but yet to the three he attained not: and David made him of his council.€€ƒxwMoreover the most valiant men of the army, were Asahel brother of Joab, and Elchanan the son of his uncle of Bethlehem,€€ƒx'Sammoth an Arorite, Helles a Phalonite,€€ƒx8Ira the son of Acces a Thecuite, Abiezer an Anathothite,€€ƒx'Sobbochai a Husathite, Ilai an Ahohite,€€ƒx>Maharai a Netophathite, Heled the son of Baana a Netophathite,€€ƒxPEthai the son of Ribai of Gabaath of the sons of Benjamin, Banai a Pharathonite,€€ƒxWHurai of the torrent Gaas, Abiel an Arbathite, Azmoth a Bauramite, Eliaba a Salabonite,€€ƒxBThe sons of Assem a Gezonite, Jonathan the son of Sage an Ararite,€€ƒx#Ahiam the son of Sachar an Ararite,€€ƒvEliphal the son of Ur,€€ƒx(Hepher a Mecherathite, Ahia a Phelonite,€€ƒx+Hesro a Carmelite, Naarai the son of Azbai,€€ƒx6Joel the brother of Nathan, Mibahar the son of Agarai.€€ƒxSSelec an Ammonite, Naharai a Berothite, the armourbearer of Joab the son of Sarvia.€€ƒx!Ira a Jethrite, Gareb a Jethrite,€€ƒx(Urias a Hethite, Zabad the son of Oholi,€€ƒxRAdina the son of Siza a Rubenite the prince of the Rubenites, and thirty with him:€€ƒx2Hanan the son of Maacha, and Josaphat a Mathanite,€€ƒxEOzia an Astarothite, Samma, and Jehiel the sons of Hotham an Arorite,€€ƒx:Jedihel the son of Zamri, and Joha his brother a Thosaite,€€ƒx{Eliel a Mahumite, and Jeribai, and Josaia the sons of Elnaim, and Jethma a Moabite, Eliel, and Obed, and Jasiel of Masobia.€€ƒ`€€ƒxNow these are they that came to David to Siceleg, while he yet fled from Saul the son of Cis, and they were most valiant and excellent warriors,€€ƒxBending the bow, and using either hand in hurling stones with slings, and shooting arrows: of the brethren of Saul of Benjamin.€€ƒxThe chief was Ahiezer, and Joas, the sons of Samoa of Gabaath, and Jaziel, and Phallet the sons of Azmoth, and Beracha, and Jehu an Anathothite.€€ƒxˆAnd Samaias of Gabaon, the stoutest amongst the thirty and over the thirty; Jeremias, and Jeheziel and Johanan, and Jozabad of Gaderoth;€€˜(ƒxCElcana, and Jesia, and Azareel, and Joezer, and Jesbaam of Carehim:€€ƒx4And Joela, and Zabadia the sons of Jeroham of Gedor.€€ƒxòFrom Gaddi also there went over to David, when he lay hid in the wilderness most valiant men, and excellent warriors, holding shield and spear: whose faces were like the faces of a lion, and they were swift like the roebucks on the mountains.€€ƒx3Ezer the chief, Obdias the second, Eliab the third,€€ƒx'Masmana the fourth, Jeremias the fifth,€€ƒx"Ethi the sixth, Eliel the seventh,€€ƒx&Johanan the eighth, Elzebad the ninth,€€ƒx*Jerenias the tenth, Machbani the eleventh,€€ƒxThese were of the sons of Gad, captains of the army: the least of them was captain over a hundred soldiers, and the greatest over a thousand.€€ƒxÄThese are they who passed over the Jordan in the first month, when it is used to flow over its banks: and they put to flight all that dwelt in the valleys both toward the east and toward the west.€€ƒxZAnd there came also of the men of Benjamin, and of Juda to the hold, in which David abode.€€ƒxòAnd David went out to meet them, and said: If you are come peaceably to me to help me, let my heart be joined to you: but if you plot against me for my enemies whereas I have no iniquity in my hands, let the God of our fathers see, and judge.€€ƒyBut the spirit came upon Amasai the chief among thirty, and he said: We are thine, O David, and for thee, O son of Isai: peace, peace be to thee, and peace to thy helpers. For thy God helpeth thee. So David received them, and made them captains of the band.€€ƒyAnd there were some of Manasses that went over to David, when he came with the Philistines against Saul to fight: but he did not fight with them: because the lords of the Philistines taking counsel sent him back, saying: With the danger of our heads he will return to his master Saul.€€ƒx¾So when he went back to Siceleg, there fled to him of Manasses, Ednas and Jozabad, and Jedihel, and Michael, and Ednas, and Jozabad, and Eliu, and Salathi, captains of thousands in Manasses.€€ƒxpThese helped David against the rovers: for they were all most valiant men, and were made commanders in the army.€€ƒxoMoreover day by day there came some to David to help him till they became a great number, like the army of God.€€ƒx¥And this is the number of the chiefs of the army who came to David, when he was in Hebron, to transfer to him the kingdom of Saul, according to the word of the Lord.€€ƒx\The sons of Juda bearing shield and spear, six thousand eight hundred well appointed to war.€€ƒxFOf the sons of Simeon valiant men for war, seven thousand one hundred.€€ƒx/Of the sons of Levi, four thousand six hundred.€€ƒxRAnd Joiada prince of the race of Aaron, and with him three thousand seven hundred.€€ƒxgSadoc also a young man of excellent disposition, and the house of his father, twenty-two principal men.€€ƒxAnd of the sons of Benjamin the brethren of Saul, three thousand: for hitherto a great part of them followed the house of Saul.€€ƒxiAnd of the sons of Ephraim twenty thousand eight hundred, men of great valour renowned in their kindreds.€€ƒxhAnd of the half tribe of Manasses, eighteen thousand, every one by their names, came to make David king.€€ƒx·Also of the sons of Issachar men of understanding, that knew all times to order what Israel should do, two hundred principal men: and all the rest of the tribe followed their counsel.€€ƒxŸAnd of Zabulon such as went forth to battle, and stood in array well appointed with armour for war, there came fifty thousand to his aid, with no double heart.€€ƒxnAnd of Nephtali, a thousand leaders: and with them seven and thirty thousand, furnished with shield and spear.€€ƒxBOf Dan also twenty-eight thousand six hundred prepared for battle.€€ƒxKAnd of Aser forty thousand going forth to fight, and challenging in battle.€€ƒxªAnd on the other side of the Jordan of the sons of Ruben, and of Gad, and of the half of the tribe of Manasses a hundred and twenty thousand, furnished with arms for war.€€ƒx½All these men of war well appointed to fight, came with a perfect heart to Hebron, to make David king over all Israel: and all the rest also of Israel, were of one heart to make David king.€€ƒxhAnd they were there with David three days eating and drinking: for their brethren had prepared for them.€€ƒxÿMoreover they that were near them even as far as Issachar, and Zabulon, and Nephtali, brought loaves on asses, and on camels, and on mules, and on oxen, to eat: meal, figs, raisins, wine, oil, and oxen, and sheep in abundance, for there was joy in Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒx]David consulted with the captains of thousands, and of hundreds, and with all the commanders.€€ƒy+And he said to all the assembly of Israel: If it please you; and if the words which I speak come from the Lord our God, let us send to the rest of our brethren into all the countries of Israel, and to the priests, and the Levites, that dwell in the suburbs of the cities, to gather themselves to us,€€ƒxZAnd let us bring again the ark of our God to us: for we sought it not in the days of Saul.€€ƒxYAnd all the multitude answered that it should be so: for the word pleased all the people.€€ƒx½And David went up with all the men of Israel to the hill of Cariathiarim which is in Juda, to bring thence the ark of the Lord God sitting upon the cherubims, where his name is called upon.€€ƒxuAnd they carried the ark of God upon a new cart out of the house of Abinadab. And Oza and his brother drove the cart.€€ƒx™And David and all Israel played before God with all their might with hymns, and with harps, and with psalteries, and timbrels, and cymbals, and trumpets,€€ƒx•And when they came to the floor of Chidon, Oza put forth his hand, to hold up the ark: for the ox being wanton had made it lean a little on one side.€€ƒxsAnd the Lord was angry with Oza, and struck him, because he had touched the ark; and he died there before the Lord.€€ƒxpAnd David was troubled because the Lord had divided Oza: and he called that place the Breach of Oza to this day.€€ƒxPAnd he feared God at that time, saying: How can I bring in the ark of God to me?€€ƒxAnd therefore he brought it not home to himself, that is, into the city of David, but carried it aside into the house of Obededom the Gethite.€€ƒxwAnd the ark of God remained in the house of Obededom three months: and the Lord blessed his house, and all that he had.€€ƒ`€€ƒxsAnd Hiram king of Tyre sent messengers to David, and cedar trees, and masons, and carpenters, to build him a house.€€ƒx~And David perceived that the Lord had confirmed him king over Israel, and that his kingdom was exalted over his people Israel.€€ƒxJAnd David took other wives in Jerusalem: and he begot sons, and daughters.€€ƒxjNow these are the names of them that were born to him in Jerusalem: Samua, and Sobad, Nathan, and Solomon,€€‘ƒx!And Noga, and Napheg, and Japhia,€€ƒx%Elisama, and Baaliada, and Eliphalet.€€ƒx”And the Philistines hearing that David was anointed king over all Israel, went all up to seek him: and David heard of it, and went out against them.€€ƒxFAnd the Philistines came and spread themselves in the vale of Raphaim.€€ƒx½And David consulted the Lord, saying: Shall I go up against the Philistines, and wilt thou deliver them into my hand? And the Lord said to him: Go up, and I will deliver them into thy hand.€€ƒxÍAnd when they were come to Baalpharasim, David defeated them there, and he said: God hath divided my enemies by my hand, as waters are divided: and therefore the name of that place was called Baalpharasim.€€ƒxNAnd they left there their gods, and David commanded that they should be burnt.€€ƒx`Another time also the Philistines made an irruption, and spread themselves abroad in the valley.€€ƒxAnd David consulted God again, and God said to him: Go not up after them, turn away from them, and come upon them over against the pear trees.€€ƒx·And when thou shalt hear the sound of one going in the tops of the pear trees, then shalt thou go out to battle. For God is gone out before thee to strike the army of the Philistines.€€ƒxuAnd David did as God had commanded him, and defeated the army of the Philistines, slaying them from Gabaon to Gazera.€€ƒx]And the name of David became famous in all countries, and the Lord made all nations fear aim.€€ƒ`€€ƒx|He made also houses for himself in the city of David: and built a place for the ark of God, and pitched a tabernacle for it.€€ƒx•Then David said: No one ought to carry the ark of God, but the Levites, whom the Lord hath chosen to carry it, and to minister unto himself for ever.€€ƒx†And he gathered all Israel together into Jerusalem, that the ark of God might be brought into its place, which he had prepared for it.€€ƒx,And the sons of Aaron also, and the Levites.€€˜ƒxPOf the sons of Merari, Asaia the chief, and his brethren two hundred and twenty.€€ƒxMOf the sons of Gersom, Joel the chief, and his brethren a hundred and thirty.€€ƒxJOf the sons of Elisaphan, Semeias the chief: and his brethren two hundred.€€ƒx@Of the sons of Hebron, Eliel the chief: and his brethren eighty.€€ƒxPOf the sons of Oziel, Aminadab the chief: and his brethren a hundred and twelve.€€ƒxsAnd David called Sadoc, and Abiathar the priests, and the Levites, Uriel, Asaia, Joel, Semeia, Eliel, and Aminadab:€€ƒxÀAnd he said to them: You that are the heads of the Levitical families, be sanctified with your brethren, and bring the ark of the Lord the God of Israel to the place, which is prepared for it:€€ƒx’Lest as the Lord at first struck us, because you were not present, the same should now also come to pass, by our doing some thing against the law.€€ƒx_So the priests and the Levites were sanctified, to carry the ark of the Lord the God of Israel.€€ƒxŠAnd the sons of Levi took the ark of God as Moses had commanded, according to the word of the Lord, upon their shoulders, with the staves.€€ƒxÓAnd David spoke to the chiefs of the Levites, to appoint some of their brethren to be singers with musical instruments, to wit, on psalteries, and harps, and cymbals, that the joyful noise might resound on high.€€ƒx¦And they appointed Levites, Hemam the son of Joel, and of his brethren Asaph the son of Barachias: and of the sons of Merari, their brethren: Ethan the son of Casaia.€€ƒxîAnd with them their brethren: in the second rank, Zacharias, and Ben, and Jaziel, and Semiramoth, and Jahiel, and Ani, and Eliab, and Banaias, and Maasias, and Mathathias, and Eliphalu, and Macenias, and Obededom, and Jehiel, the porters.€€ƒxHNow the singers, Heman, Asaph, and Ethan, sounded with cymbals of brass.€€ƒxƒAnd Zacharias, and Oziel, and Semiramoth, and Jehiel, and Ani, and Eliab, and Maasias, and Banaias, sung mysteries upon psalteries.€€ƒxAnd Mathathias, and Eliphalu, and Macenias and Obededom, and Jehiel and Ozaziu, sung a song of victory for the octave upon harps.€€ƒxoAnd Chonenias chief of the Levites, presided over the prophecy, to give out the tunes: for he was very skilful.€€ƒx7And Barachias, and Elcana, were doorkeepers of the ark.€€ƒxÊAnd Sebenias, and Josaphat, and Nathanael, and Amasai, and Zacharias, and Banaias, and Eliezer the priests, sounded with trumpets, before the ark of God: and Obededom and Jehias were porters of the ark.€€ƒx¢So David and all the ancients of Israel, and the captains over thousands, went to bring the ark of the covenant of the Lord out of the house of Obededom with joy.€€ƒxŠAnd when God had helped the Levites who carried the ark of the covenant of the Lord, they offered in sacrifice seven oxen, and seven rams.€€ƒx×And David was clothed with a robe of fine linen, and all the Levites that carried the ark, and the singing men, and Chonenias the ruler of the prophecy among the singers: and David also had on him an ephod of linen.€€ƒx¶And all Israel brought the ark of the covenant of the Lord with joyful shouting, and sounding with the sound of the cornet, and with trumpets, and cymbals, and psalteries, and harps.€€ƒxÇAnd when the ark of the covenant of the Lord was come to the city of David, Michol the daughter of Saul looking out at a window, saw king David dancing and playing, and she despised him in her heart.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¡So they brought the ark of God, and set it in the midst of the tent, which David had pitched for it: and they offered holocausts, and peace offerings before God.€€ƒxzAnd when David had made an end of offering holocausts, and peace offerings, he blessed the people in the name of the Lord.€€ƒx€And he divided to all and every one, both men and women, a loaf of bread, and a piece of roasted beef, and flour fried with oil.€€ƒxAnd he appointed Levites to minister before the ark of the Lord, and to remember his works, and to glorify, and praise the Lord God of Israel.€€˜+ƒxqBut Banaias, and Jaziel the priests, to sound the trumpet continually before the ark of the covenant of the Lord.€€ƒxTIn that day David made Asaph the chief to give praise to the Lord with his brethren.€€ƒxTPraise ye the Lord, and call upon his name: make known his doings among the nations.€€ƒxISing to him, yea, sing praises to him: and relate all his wondrous works.€€ƒxKPraise ye his holy name: let the heart of them rejoice, that seek the Lord.€€ƒx;Seek ye the Lord, and his power: seek ye his face evermore.€€ƒx\Remember his wonderful works, which he hath done: his signs, and the judgments of his mouth.€€ƒxBO ye seed of Israel his servants, ye children of Jacob his chosen.€€ƒx;He is the Lord our God: his judgments are in all the earth.€€ƒxWRemember for ever his covenant: the word, which he commanded to a thousand generations.€€ƒx?The covenant which he made with Abraham: and his oath to Isaac.€€ƒx\And he appointed the same to Jacob for a precept: and to Israel for an everlasting covenant:€€ƒxMSaying: To thee will I give the land of Chanaan: the lot of your inheritance.€€ƒxAWhen they were but a small number: very few and sojourners in it.€€ƒxLAnd they passed from nation to nation: and from a kingdom to another people.€€ƒxGHe suffered no man to do them wrong: and reproved kings for their sake.€€ƒx5Touch not my anointed: and do no evil to my prophets.€€ƒxMSing ye to the Lord, all the earth: shew forth from day to day his salvation.€€ƒxCDeclare his glory among the Gentiles: his wonders among all people.€€ƒx[For the Lord is great and exceedingly to be praised: and he is to be feared above all gods.€€ƒxIFor all the gods of the nations are idols: but the Lord made the heavens.€€ƒxFPraise and magnificence are before him: strength and joy in his place.€€ƒxZBring ye to the Lord, O ye families of the nations: bring ye to the Lord glory and empire.€€ƒxzGive to the Lord glory to his name, bring up sacrifice, and come ye in his sight: and adore the Lord in holy becomingness.€€ƒxULet all the earth be moved at his presence: for he hath founded the world immoveable.€€ƒxjLet the heavens rejoice, and the earth be glad: and let them say among the nations: The Lord hath reigned.€€ƒxcLet the sea roar, and the fulness thereof: let the fields rejoice, and all things that are in them.€€ƒxdThen shall the trees of the wood give praise before the Lord: because he is come to judge the earth.€€ƒxKGive ye glory to the Lord, for he is good: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒx°And say ye: Save us, O God our savior: and gather us together, and deliver us from the nations, that we may give glory to thy holy name, and may rejoice in singing thy praises.€€ƒxtBlessed be the Lord the God of Israel from eternity to eternity: and let all the people say Amen, and a hymn to God.€€ƒx¨So he left there before the ark of the covenant of the Lord, Asaph and his brethren to minister in the presence of the ark continually day by day, and in their courses.€€ƒxrAnd Obededom, with his brethren sixty-eight: and Obededom the son of Idithun, and Hosa he appointed to be porters.€€ƒxyAnd Sadoc the priest, and his brethren priests, before the tabernacle of the Lord in the high place, which was in Gabaon.€€ƒxÀThat they should offer holocausts to the Lord upon the altar of holocausts continually, morning and evening, according to all that is written in the law of the Lord, which he commanded Israel.€€ƒx—And after him Heman, and Idithun, and the rest that were chosen, every one by his name to give praise to the Lord: because his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒx«And Heman and Idithun sounded the trumpet, and played on the cymbals, and all kinds of musical instruments to sing praises to God: and the sons of Idithun he made porters.€€ƒxSAnd all the people returned to their houses: and David to bless also his own house.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¤Now when David was dwelling in his house, he said to Nathan the prophet: Behold I dwell in a house of cedar: and the ark of the covenant of the Lord is under skins.€€ƒxLAnd Nathan said to David: Do all that is in thy heart: for God is with thee.€€ƒx6Now that night the word of God came to Nathan, saying:€€ƒxdGo, and speak to David my servant: Thus saith the Lord: Thou shalt not build me a house to dwell in.€€˜ƒx§Abiding with all Israel. Did I ever speak to any one, of all the judges of Israel whom I charged to feed my people, saying: Why have you not built me a house of cedar?€€ƒx¾Now therefore thus shalt thou say to my servant David: Thus saith the Lord of hosts: I took thee from the pastures, from following the flock, that thou shouldst be ruler of my people Israel.€€ƒxÀAnd I have been with thee whithersoever thou hast gone: and have slain all thy enemies before thee, and have made thee a name like that of one of the great ones that are renowned in the earth.€€ƒx¾And I have given a place my people Israel: they shall be planted, and shall dwell therein, and shall be moved no more, neither shall the children of iniquity waste them, as at the beginning,€€ƒx–Since the days that I gave judges to my people Israel, and have humbled all thy enemies. And I declare to thee, that the Lord will build thee a house.€€ƒx And when thou shalt have ended thy days to go to thy fathers, I will raise up thy seed after thee, which shall be of thy sons: and I will establish his kingdom.€€ƒxDHe shall build me a house, and I will establish his throne for ever.€€ƒxI will be to him a father, and he shall be to me a son: and I will not take my mercy away from him, as I took it from him that was before thee.€€ƒxjBut I will settle him in my house, and in my kingdom for ever: and his throne shall be most firm for ever.€€ƒx]According to all these words, and according to all this vision, so did Nathan speak to David.€€ƒxAnd king David came and sat before the Lord, and said: Who am I, O Lord God, and what is my house, that thou shouldst give such things to me?€€ƒxÃBut even this hath seemed little in thy sight, and therefore thou hast also spoken concerning the house of thy servant for the time to come: and hast made me remarkable above all men, O Lord God.€€ƒxTWhat can David add more, seeing thou hast thus glorified thy servant, and known him?€€ƒx•O Lord, for thy servant's sake, according to thy own heart, thou hast shewn all this magnificence, and wouldst have all the great things to be known.€€ƒxqO Lord there is none like thee: and here is no other God beside thee, of all whom we have heard of with our ears.€€ƒxâFor what other nation is there upon earth like thy people Israel, whom God went to deliver, and make a people for himself, and by his greatness and terrors cast out nations before their face whom he had delivered out of Egypt?€€ƒxkAnd thou hast made thy people Israel to be thy own people for ever, and thou, O Lord, art become their God.€€ƒx—Now therefore, O Lord, let the word which thou hast spoken to thy servant, and concerning his house, be established for ever, and do as thou hast said.€€ƒx£And let thy name remain and be magnified for ever: and let it be said: The Lord of hosts is God of Israel, and the house of David his servant remaineth before him.€€ƒx¨For thou, O Lord my God, hast revealed to the ear of thy servant, that thou wilt build him a house: and therefore thy servant hath found confidence to pray before thee.€€ƒxXAnd now O Lord, thou art God: and thou hast promised to thy servant such great benefits.€€ƒxœAnd thou hast begun to bless the house of thy servant, that it may be always before thee: for seeing thou blessest it, O Lord, it shall be blessed for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¡And it came to pass after this, that David defeated the Philistines, and humbled them, and took away Geth, and her daughters out of the hands of the Philistines,€€ƒxYAnd he defeated Moab, and the Moabites were made David's servants, and brought him gifts.€€ƒx‘At that time David defeated also Adarezer king of Soba of the land of Hemath, when he went to extend his dominions as far as the river Euphrates.€€ƒxÅAnd David took from him a thousand chariots, and seven thousand horsemen, and twenty thousand footmen, and he houghed all the chariot horses, only a hundred chariots, which he reserved for himself.€€‘ƒxAnd he put a garrison in Damascus, that Syria also should serve him, and bring gifts. And the Lord assisted him in all things to which he went.€€ƒxgAnd David took the golden quivers which the servants of Adarezer had, and he brought them to Jerusalem.€€ƒx¢Likewise out of Thebath and Chun, cities of Adarezer, he brought very much brass, of which Solomon made the brazen sea, and the pillars, and the vessels of brass.€€ƒxaNow when Thou king of Hemath heard that David had defeated all the army of Adarezer king of Soba,€€ƒx¥He sent Adoram his son to king David to desire peace of him, and to congratulate him that he had defeated and overthrown Adarezer: for Thou was an enemy to Adarezer.€€ƒxÿAnd all the vessels of gold, and silver and brass king David consecrated to the Lord, with the silver and gold which he had taken from all the nations, as well from Edom, and from Moab, and from the sons of Ammon, as from the Philistines, and from Amalec.€€ƒxaAnd Abisai the son of Sarvia slew of the Edomites in the vale of the saltpits, eighteen thousand:€€ƒxyAnd he put a garrison in Edom, that Edom should serve David: and the Lord preserved David in all things to which he went.€€ƒxYSo David reigned over all Israel, and executed judgment and justice among all his people.€€ƒxVAnd Joab the son of Sarvia was over the army, and Josaphat the son of Ahilud recorder.€€ƒxeAnd Sadoc the son of Achitob, and Achimelech the son of Abiathar, were the priests: and Susa, scribe.€€ƒxƒAnd Banaias the son of Joiada was over the bands of the Cerethi, and the Phelethi: and the sons of David were chief about the king.€€ƒ`€€ƒxgNow it came to pass that Naas the king of the children of Ammon died, and his son reigned in his stead.€€ƒxùAnd David said: I will shew kindness to Hanon the son of Naas: for his father did a favour tome. And David sent messengers to comfort him upon the death of his father. But when they were come into the land of the children of Ammon, to comfort Hanon,€€ƒxşThe princes of the children of Ammon said to Hanon: Thou thinkest perhaps that David to do honour to thy father hath sent comforters to thee: and thou dost not take notice, that his servants are come to thee to consider, and search, and spy out thy land.€€ƒx”Wherefore Hanon shaved the heads and beards of the servants of David, and cut away their garments from the buttocks to the feet, and sent them away.€€“ƒxíAnd when the children of Ammon saw that they had done an injury to David, Hanon and the rest of the people sent a thousand talents of silver, to hire them chariots and horsemen out of Mesopotamia and out of Syria Maacha, and out of Soba.€€ƒxçAnd they hired two and thirty thousand chariots, and the king of Maacha, with his people. And they came and camped over against Medaba. And the children of Ammon gathered themselves together out of their cities, and came to battle.€€ƒxJAnd when David heard of it, he sent Joab, and all the army of valiant men:€€ƒx¡And the children of Ammon came out and put their army in array before the gate of the city: and the kings, that were come to their aid, stood apart in the field.€€ƒxWherefore Joab understanding that the battle was set against him before and behind, chose out the bravest men of all Israel, and marched against the Syrians,€€ƒxyAnd the rest of the people he delivered into the hand of Abisai his brother, and they went against the children of Ammon.€€ƒx‘And he said: If the Syrians be too strong for me, then thou shalt help me: but if the children of Ammon be too strong for thee, I will help thee.€€ƒxœBe of good courage and let us behave ourselves manfully for our people, and for the cities of our God: and the Lord will do that which is good in his sight.€€ƒxmSo Joab and the people that were with him, went against the Syrians to the battle: and he put them to flight.€€ƒx¥And the children of Ammon seeing that the Syrians were fled, they likewise fled from Abisai his brother, and went into the city: and Joab also returned to Jerusalem.€€ƒxÇBut the Syrians seeing that they had fallen before Israel, sent messengers, and brought to them the Syrians that were beyond the river: and Sophach, general of the army of Adarezer, was their leader.€€ƒx¨And it was told David, and he gathered together all Israel, and passed the Jordan, and came upon them, and put his army in array against them, and they fought with him.€€ƒxšBut the Syrian fled before Israel: and David slew of the Syrians seven thousand chariots, and forty thousand footmen, and Sophach the general of the army.€€ƒx¦And when the servants of Adarezer saw themselves overcome by Israel, they went over to David, and served him: and Syria would not help the children of Ammon any more.€€ƒ`€€ƒy*And it came to pass after the course of a year, at the time that kings go out to battle, Joab gathered together an army and the strength of the troops, and wasted the land of the children of Ammon: and went and besieged Rabba. But David stayed at Jerusalem, when Joab smote Rabba, and destroyed it.€€ƒxÔAnd David took the crown of Melchom from his head, and found in it a talent weight of gold, and most precious stones, and he made himself a diadem of it: he took also the spoils of the city which were very great.€€ƒyAnd the people that were therein he brought out: and made harrows, and sleds, and chariots of iron to go over them, so that they were cut and bruised to pieces: in this manner David dealt with all the cities of the children of Ammon: and he returned with all his people to Jerusalem.€€ƒx”After this there arose a war at Gazer against the Philistines: in which Sabachai the Husathite slew Saphai of the race of Raphaim, and humbled them.€€ˆƒxÂThere was another battle also in Geth, in which there was a man of great stature, whose fingers and toes were four and twenty, six on each hand and foot: who also was born of the stock of Rapha.€€ƒx¥He reviled Israel: but Jonathan the son of Samaa the brother of David slew him. These were the sons of Rapha in Geth, who fell by the hand of David and his servants.€€ƒ`€€ƒxCAnd Satan rose up against Israel: and moved David to number Israel.€€ƒxAnd David said to Joab, and to the rulers of the people: Go, and number Israel from Bersabee even to Dan, and bring me the number of them that I may know it.€€ƒxĞAnd Joab answered: The Lord make his people a hundred times more than they are: but, my lord the king, are they not all thy servants: why doth my lord seek this thing, which may be imputed as a sin to Israel?€€ƒxpBut the king's word rather prevailed: and Joab departed, and went through all Israel: and returned to Jerusalem.€€ƒxÕAnd he gave David the number of them, whom he had surveyed: and all the number of Israel was found to be eleven hundred thousand men that drew the sword: and of Juda four hundred and seventy thousand fighting men.€€˜ƒxPAnd God was displeased with this thing that was commanded: and he struck Israel.€€ƒxAnd David said to God: I have sinned exceedingly in doing this: I beseech thee take away the iniquity of thy servant, for I have done foolishly.€€ƒx4And the Lord spoke to Gad the seer of David, saying:€€ƒx˜Go, and speak to David, and tell him: Thus saith the Lord: I give thee the choice of three things: choose one which thou wilt, and I will do it to thee.€€ƒx\And when Gad was come to David, he said to him: Thus saith the Lord: choose which thou wilt:€€ƒy0Either three years famine: or three months to flee from thy enemies, and not to be able to escape their sword: or three days to have the sword of the Lord, and pestilence in the land, and the angel of the Lord destroying in all the coasts of Israel: now therefore see what I shall answer him who sent me.€€ƒx®And David said to Gad: I am on every side in a great strait: but it is better for me to fall into the hands of the Lord, for his mercies are many, than into the hands of men.€€ƒxYSo the Lord sent a pestilence upon Israel. And there fell of Israel seventy thousand men.€€ƒyAnd he sent an angel to Jerusalem, to strike it: and as he was striking it, the Lord beheld, and took pity for the greatness of the evil: and said to the angel that destroyed: It is enough, now stop thy hand. And the angel of the Lord stood by the thrashingfloor of Ornan the Jebusite.€€ƒxæAnd David lifting up his eyes, saw the angel of the Lord standing between heaven and earth, with a drawn sword in his hand, turned against Jerusalem: and both he and the ancients clothed in haircloth, fell down flat on the ground.€€ƒy6And David said to God: Am not I he that commanded the people to be numbered? It is I that have sinned: it is I that have done the evil: but as for this flock, what hath it deserved? O Lord my God, let thy hand be turned, I beseech thee, upon me, and upon my father's house: and let not thy people be destroyed.€€ƒxAnd the angel of the Lord commanded Gad to tell David, to go up, and build an altar to the Lord God in the thrashingfloor of Ornan the Jebusite.€€ƒx_And David went up, according to the word of Gad, which he spoke to him in the name of the Lord.€€ƒx‡Now when Ornan looked up, and saw the angel, he and his four sons hid themselves: for at that time he was thrashing wheat in the floor.€€ƒx“And as David was coming to Ornan, Ornan saw him, and went out of the thrashingfloor to meet him, and bowed down to him with his face to the ground.€€ƒxÒAnd David said to him: Give me this place of thy thrashingfloor, that I may build therein an altar to the Lord: but thou shalt take of me as much money as it is worth, that the plague may cease from the people.€€ƒxŞAnd Ornan said to David: Take it, and let my lord the king do all that pleaseth him: and moreover the oxen also I give for a holocaust, and the drays for wood, and the wheat for the sacrifice: I will give it all willingly.€€ƒx³And king David said to him: It shall not be so, but I will give thee money as much as it is worth: for I must not take it from thee, and so offer to the Lord holocausts free cost.€€ƒxPSo David gave to Ornan for the place, six hundred sicles of gold of just weight.€€ƒxÃAnd he built there an altar to the Lord: and he offered holocausts, and peace offerings, and he called upon the Lord, and he heard him by sending fire from heaven upon the altar of the holocaust.€€ƒxPAnd the Lord commanded the angel: and he put up his sword again into the sheath.€€ƒxzAnd David seeing that the Lord had heard him in the thrashingfloor of Ornan the Jebusite, forthwith offered victims there.€€ƒxŠBut the tabernacle of the Lord, which Moses made in the desert, and the altar of holocausts, was at that time in the high place of Gabaon.€€ƒx”And David could not go to the altar there to pray to God: for he was seized with an exceeding great fear, seeing the sword of the angel of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx]Then David said: This is the house of God, And this is the altar for the holocaust of Israel.€€ƒx±And he commanded to gather together all the proselytes of the land of Israel, and out of them he appointed stonecutters to hew stones and polish them, to build the house of God.€€ƒxƒAnd David prepared in abundance iron for the nails of the gates, and for the closures and joinings: and of brass an immense weight.€€ƒx[And the cedar trees were without number, which the Sidonians, and Tyrians brought to David.€€ƒy	And David said: Solomon my son is very young and tender, and the house which I would have to be built to the Lord, must be such as to be renowned in all countries: therefore I will prepare him necessaries. And therefore before his death he prepared all the charges.€€“ƒxiAnd David said to Solomon: My son, it was my desire to have built a house to the name of the Lord my God.€€ƒx³But the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Thou hast shed much blood, and fought many battles, so thou cannot not build house to my name, after shedding so much blood before me:€€ƒxâThe son, that shall be born to thee, shall be a most quiet man: for I will make him rest from all his enemies round about: and therefore he shall be called Peaceable: and I will give peace and quietness to Israel all his days.€€ƒx£He shall build a house to my name, and he shall be a son to me, and I will be a father to him: and I will establish the throne of his kingdom over Israel for ever.€€ƒxNow then, my son, the Lord be with thee, and do thou prosper, and build the house to the Lord thy God, as he hath spoken of thee.€€ƒxƒThe Lord also give thee wisdom and understanding, that thou mayest be able to rule Israel, and to keep the law of the Lord thy God.€€ƒx¿For then thou shalt be able to prosper, if thou keep the commandments, and judgments, which the Lord commanded Moses to teach Israel: take courage and act manfully, fear not, nor be dismayed.€€ƒy&Behold I in my poverty have prepared the charges of the house of the Lord, of gold a hundred thousand talents, and of silver a million of talents: but of brass, and of iron there is no weight, for the abundance surpasseth all account: timber also and stones I have prepared for all the charges.€€ƒx„Thou hast also workmen in abundance, hewers of stones, and masons, and carpenters, and of all trades the most skilful in their work,€€ƒxˆIn gold, and in silver, and in brass, and in iron, whereof there is no number. Arise then, and be doing, and the Lord will be with thee.€€ƒxFDavid also charged all the princes of Israel, to help Solomon his son,€€ƒxÎSaying: You see, that the Lord your God is with you, and hath given you rest round about, and hath delivered all your enemies into your hands, and the land is subdued before the Lord, and before his people.€€ƒyGive therefore your hearts and your souls, to seek the Lord your God and arise, and build a sanctuary to the Lord God, that the ark of the covenant of the Lord, and the vessels consecrated to the Lord, may be brought into the house, which is built to the name of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxHDavid being old and full of days, made Solomon his son king over Israel.€€ƒxPAnd he gathered together all the princes of Israel, and the priests and Levites.€€ƒx€And the Levites were numbered from the age of thirty years, and upwards: and there were found of them thirty-eight thousand men.€€ƒx–Of these twenty-four thousand were chosen, and distributed unto the ministry of the house of the Lord: and six thousand were the overseers and judges.€€ƒx‚Moreover four thousand were porters: and as many singers singing to the Lord with the instruments, which he had made to sing with.€€˜ ƒx)The sons of Gerson were Leedan and Semei.€€ƒxBThe sons of Leedan: the chief Jahiel, and Zethan, and Joel, three.€€ƒxiThe sons of Semei: Salomith, and Hosiel, and Aran, three: these were the heads of the families of Leedan.€€ƒxeAnd the sons of Semei were Leheth, and Ziza, and Jaus, and Baria: these were the sons of Semei, four.€€ƒx•And Leheth was the first, Ziza the second: but Jaus and Baria had not many children, and therefore they were counted in one family, and in one house.€€ƒxAThe sons of Caath were Amram, and Isaar, Hebron, and Oziel, four.€€ƒxÛThe sons of Amram, Aaron, and Moses. And Aaron was separated to minister in the holy of holies, he and his sons for ever, and to burn incense before the Lord, according to his ceremonies, and to bless his name for ever.€€ƒxKThe sons also of Moses, the man of God, were numbered in the tribe of Levi.€€ƒx*The sons of Moses were Gersom and Eliezer:€€ƒx%The sons of Gersom: Subuel the first.€€ƒxƒAnd the sons of Eliezer were: Rohobia the first: and Eliezer had no more sons. But the sons of Rohobia were multiplied exceedingly.€€ƒx&The sons of Isaar: Salomith the first.€€ƒxaThe sons of Hebron: Jeriau the first, Amarias the second, Jahaziel the third, Jecmaam the fourth.€€ƒx5The sons of Oziel: Micha the first, Jesia the second.€€ƒxJThe sons of Merari: Moholi, and Musi. The sons of Moholi: Eleazar and Cis.€€ƒx^And Eleazar died, and had no sons but daughters: and the sons of Cis their brethren took them.€€ƒx8The sons of Musi: Moholi, and Eder, and Jerimoth, three.€€ƒxÏThese are the sons of Levi in their kindreds and families, princes by their courses, and the number of every head that did the works of the ministry of the house of the Lord from twenty years old and upward.€€ƒxqFor David said: The Lord the God of Israel hath given rest to his people, and a habitation in Jerusalem for ever.€€ƒx|And it shall not be the office of the Levites to carry any more the tabernacle, and all the vessels for the service thereof.€€ƒxqSo according to the last precepts of David, the sons of Levi are to be numbered from twenty years old and upward.€€ƒxıAnd they are to be under the hand of the sons of Aaron for the service of the house of the Lord, in the porches, and in the chambers, and in the place of purification, and in the sanctuary, and in all the works of the ministry of the temple of the Lord.€€ƒxÊAnd the priests have the charge of the loaves of proposition, and of the sacrifice of fine flour, and of the unleavened cakes, and of the fryingpan, and of the roasting, and of every weight and measure.€€ƒxAnd the Levites are to stand in the morning to give thanks, and to sing praises to the Lord: and in like manner in the evening,€€ƒxàAs well in the oblation of the holocausts of the Lord, as in the sabbaths and in the new moons, and the rest of the solemnities, according to the number and ceremonies prescribed for every thing, continually before the Lord.€€ƒxÎAnd let them keep the observances of the tabernacle of the covenant, and the ceremonies of the sanctuary, and the charge of the sons of Aaron their brethren, that they may minister in the house of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxpNow these were the divisions of the sons of Aaron: The sons of Aaron: Nadab, and Abiu, and Eleazar, and Ithamar.€€ƒx{But Nadab and Abiu died before their father, and had no children: so Eleazar, and Ithamar did the office of the priesthood.€€ƒx‘And David distributed them, that is, Sadoc of the sons of Eleazar, and Ahimelech of the sons of Ithamar, according to their courses and ministry.€€ƒyAnd there were found many more of the sons of Eleazar among the principal men, than of the sons of Ithamar. And he divided them so, that there were of the sons of Eleazar, sixteen chief men by their families: and of the sons of Ithamar eight by their families and houses.€€ƒx±And he divided both the families one with the other by lot: for there were princes of the sanctuary, and princes of God, both of the sons of Eleazar, and of the sons of Ithamar.€€˜ƒx=Now the first lot came forth to Joiarib, the second to Jedei,€€ƒx)The third to Harim, the fourth to Seorim,€€ƒx*The fifth to Melchia, the sixth to Maiman,€€ƒx)The seventh to Accos, the eighth to Abia,€€ƒx*The ninth to Jesua, the tenth to Sechenia,€€ƒx.The eleventh to Eliasib, the twelfth to Jacim,€€ƒx3The thirteenth to Hoppha, the fourteenth to Isbaab,€€ƒx/The fifteenth to Belga, the sixteenth to Emmer,€€ƒx3The seventeenth to Hezir, the eighteenth to Aphses,€€ƒx6The nineteenth to Pheteia, the twentieth to Hezechiel,€€ƒx@The one and twentieth to Jachin, the two and twentieth to Gamul,€€ƒxFThe three and twentieth to Dalaiau, the four and twentieth to Maaziau.€€ƒxËThese are their courses according to their ministries, to come into the house of the Lord, and according to their manner under the hand of Aaron their father: as the Lord the God of Israel had commanded.€€ƒxqNow of the rest of the sons of Levi, there was of the sons of Amram, Subael: and of the sons of Subael, Jehedeia.€€ƒx-Also of the sons of Rohobia the chief Jesias.€€ƒx>And the son of Isaar Salemoth, and the son of Salemoth Jahath:€€ƒxYAnd his son Jeriau the first, Amarias the second, Jahaziel the third, Jecmaan the fourth.€€ƒx1The son of Oziel, Micha: the son of Micha, Samir.€€ƒx=The brother of Micha, Jesia: and the son of Jesia, Zacharias.€€ƒx=The sons of Merari: Moholi and Musi: the son of Oziau: Benno.€€ƒx?The son also of Merari Oziau, and Soam, and Zacchur, and Hebri.€€ƒx0And the son of Moholi: Eleazar, who had no sons.€€ƒxAnd the son of Cis, Jeramael.€€ƒxsThe sons of Musi: Moholi, Eder, and Jerimoth. These are the sons of Levi according to the houses of their families.€€ƒxëAnd they also cast lots over against their brethren the sons of Aaron before David the king, and Sadoc, and Ahimelech, and the princes of the priestly and Levitical families, both the elder and the younger. The lot divided all equally.€€ƒ`€€ƒxøMoreover David and the chief officers of the army separated for the ministry the sons of Asaph, and of Heman, and of Idithun: to prophesy with harps, and with psalteries, and with cymbals according to their number serving in their appointed office.€€ƒx‡Of the sons of Asaph: Zacchur, and Joseph, and Nathania, and Asarela, sons of Asaph: under the hand of Asaph prophesying near the king.€€ƒxÅAnd of Idithun: the sons of Idithun, Godolias, Sori, Jeseias, and Hasabias, and Mathathias, under the hand of their father Idithun, who prophesied with a harp to give thanks and to praise the Lord.€€ƒx¶Of Heman also: the sons of Heman, Bocciau, Mathaniau, Oziel, Subuel, and Jerimoth, Hananias, Hanani, Eliatha, Geddelthi, and Romemthiezer, and Jesbacassa, Mellothi, Othir, Mahazioth:€€ƒx˜All these were the sons of Heman the seer of the king in the words of God, to lift up the horn: and God gave to Heman fourteen sons and three daughters.€€˜ƒx‚And the number of them with their brethren, that taught the song of the Lord, all the teachers, were two hundred and eighty-eight.€€ƒxpAnd they cast lots by their courses, the elder equally with the younger, the learned and the unlearned together.€€ƒxAnd the first lot came forth to Joseph, who was of Asaph. The second to Godolias, to him and his sons, and his brethren twelve.€€ƒx9The third to Zachur, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒx9The fourth to Isari, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒx;The fifth to Nathania, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒx:The sixth to Bocciau, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒx<The seventh to Isreela, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒx:The eighth to Jesaia, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒx=The ninth to Mathanaias, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒx:The tenth to Semeias, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒx=The eleventh to Azareel, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒx<The twelfth to Hasabia, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒx>The thirteenth to Subael, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒxBThe fourteenth to Mathathias, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒx?The fifteenth to Jerimoth, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒx?The sixteenth to Hananias, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒxCThe seventeenth to Jesbacassa, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒx>The eighteenth to Hanani, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒx@The nineteenth to Mellothi, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒx>The twentieth to Eliatha, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒxDThe one and twentieth to Othir, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒxHThe two and twentieth to Geddelthi, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒxJThe three and twentieth to Mahazioth, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒxLThe four and twentieth to Romemthiezer, to his sons and his brethren twelve.€€ƒ`€€ƒxbAnd the divisions of the porters: of the Corites Meselemia, the son of Core, of the sons of Asaph.€€ƒxmThe sons of Meselemia: Zacharias the firstborn, Jadihel the second, Zabadias the third, Jathanael the fourth,€€ƒx8Elam the fifth, Johanan the sixth, Elioenai the seventh.€€ƒx}And the sons of Obededom, Semeias the firstborn, Jozabad the second, Joaha the third, Sachar the fourth, Nathanael the fifth,€€ƒx[Ammiel the sixth, Issachar the seventh, Phollathi the eighth: for the Lord had blessed him.€€˜ ƒxThe sons then of Semeias were Othni, and Raphael, and Obed, Elizabad, and his brethren most valiant men: and Eliu, and Samachias.€€ƒx}All these of the sons of Obededom: they, and their sons, and their brethren most able men for service, sixty-two of Obededom.€€ƒxHAnd the sons of Meselemia, and their brethren strong men, were eighteen.€€ƒx„And of Hosa, that is, of the sons of Merari: Semri the chief, (for he had not a firstborn, and therefore his father made him chief.)€€ƒxzHelcias the second, Tabelias the third, Zacharias the fourth: all these the sons, and the brethren of Hosa, were thirteen.€€ƒx–Among these were the divisions of the porters, so that the chiefs of the wards, as well as their brethren, always ministered in the house of the Lord.€€ƒx`And they cast lots equally, both little and great, by their families for every one of the gates.€€ƒx|And the lot of the east fell to Selemias. But to his son Zacharias, a very wise and learned man, the north gate fell by lot.€€ƒxpAnd to Obededom and his sons that towards the south: in which part of the house was the council of the ancients.€€ƒxlTo Sephim, and Hosa towards the west, by the gate which leadeth to the way of the ascent: ward against ward.€€ƒx›Now towards the east were six Levites: and towards the north four a day: and towards the south likewise four a day: and where the council was, two and two.€€ƒxXIn the cells also of the porters toward the west four in the way: and two at every cell.€€ƒxJThese are the divisions of the porters of the sons of Core, and of Merari.€€ƒxLNow Achias was over the treasures of the house of God, and the holy vessels.€€ƒxnThe sons of Ledan, the sons of Gersonni: of Ledan were heads of the families, of Ledan, and Gersonni, Jehieli.€€ƒx_The sons of Jehieli: Zathan and Joel, his brethren over the treasures of the house of the Lord,€€ƒxAWith the Amramites, and Isaarites, and Hebronites, and Ozielites.€€ƒxMAnd Subael the son of Gersom, the son of Moses, was chief over the treasures.€€ƒxHis brethren also, Eliezer, whose son Rohobia, and his son Isaias, and his son Joram, and his son Zechri, and his son Selemith.€€ƒx×Which Selemith and his brethren were over the treasures of the holy things, which king David, and the heads of families, and the captains over thousands and over hundreds, and the captains of the host had dedicated,€€ƒxƒOut of the wars, and the spoils won in battles, which they had consecrated to the building and furniture of the temple of the Lord.€€ƒxéAnd all these things that Samuel the seer and Saul the son of Cis, and Abner the son of Ner, and Joab the son of Sarvia had sanctified: and whosoever had sanctified those things, they were under the hand of Selemith and his brethren.€€ƒxuBut Chonenias and his sons were over the Isaarites, for the business abroad over Israel to teach them and judge them.€€ƒxÎAnd of the Hebronites Hasabias, and his brethren most able men, a thousand seven hundred had the charge over Israel beyond the Jordan westward, in all the works of the Lord, and for the service of the king.€€ƒxÉAnd the chief of the Hebronites was Jeria according to their families and kindreds. In the fortieth year of the reign of David they were numbered, and there were found most valiant men in Jazer Galaad,€€ƒxŞAnd his brethren of stronger age, two thousand seven hundred chiefs of families. And king David made them rulers over the Rubenites and the Gadites, and the half tribe of Manasses, for all the service of God, and the king.€€ƒ`€€ƒyNow the children of Israel according to their number, the heads of families, captains of thousands and of hundreds, and officers, that served the king according to their companies, who came in and went out every month in the year, under every chief were four and twenty thousand.€€ƒxzOver the first company the first month Jesboam, the son of Zabdiel was chief, and under him were four and twenty thousand.€€ƒxTOf the sons of Phares, the chief of all the captains in the host in the first month.€€ƒx¥The company of the second month was under Dudia, an Ahohite, and after him was another named Macelloth, who commanded a part of the army of four and twenty thousand.€€ƒx–And the captain of the third company for the third month, was Banaias the son of Joiada the priest: and in his division were four and twenty thousand.€€˜"ƒx“The fourth, for the fourth month, was Asahel the brother of Joab, and Zabadias his son after him: and in his company were four and twenty thousand.€€ƒxmThe fifth captain for the fifth month, was Samaoth a Jezerite: and his company were four and twenty thousand.€€ƒxwThe sixth, for the sixth month, was Hira the son of Acces a Thecuite: and in his company were four and twenty thousand.€€ƒx…The seventh, for the seventh month, was Helles a Phallonite of the sons of Ephraim: and in his company were four and twenty thousand.€€ƒxƒThe eighth, for the eighth month, was Sobochai a Husathite of the race of Zarahi: and in his company were four and twenty thousand.€€ƒxƒThe ninth, for the ninth month, was Abiezer an Anathothite of the sons of Jemini, and in his company were four and twenty thousand.€€ƒx‰The tenth, for the tenth month, was Marai, who was a Netophathite of the race of Zarai: and in his company were four and twenty thousand.€€ƒx‹The eleventh, for the eleventh month, was Banaias, a Pharathonite of the sons of Ephraim: and in his company were four and twenty thousand.€€ƒxŠThe twelfth, for the twelfth month, was Holdai a Netophathite, of the race of Gothoniel: and in his company were four and twenty thousand.€€ƒxŸNow the chiefs over the tribes of Israel were these: over the Rubenites, Eliezer the son of Zechri was ruler: over the Simeonites, Saphatias the son of Maacha:€€ƒxHOver the Levites, Hasabias the son of Camuel: over the Aaronites, Sadoc:€€ƒxLOver Juda, Eliu the brother of David over Issachar, Amri the son of Michael:€€ƒxbOver the Zabulonites, Jesmaias the son of Adias: over the Nephtalites, Jerimoth the son of Ozriel:€€ƒxkOver the sons of Ephraim, Osee the son of Ozaziu: over the half tribe of Manasses, Joel the son of Phadaia:€€ƒxvAnd over the half tribe of Manasses in Galaad, Jaddo the son of Zacharias: and over Benjamin, Jasiel the son of Abner.€€ƒx[And over Dan, Ezrihel the son of Jeroham: these were the princes of the children of Israel.€€ƒx’But David would not number them from twenty years old and under: because the lord had said that he would multiply Israel like the stars of heaven.€€ƒxÕJoab the son of Sarvia began to number, but he finished not: because upon this there fell wrath upon Israel: and therefore the number of them that were numbered, was not registered in the chronicles of king David.€€ƒx³And over the king's treasures was Azmoth the son of Adiel: and over those stores which were in the cities, and in the villages, and, in the castles, was Jonathan the son of Ozias.€€ƒx\And over the tillage, and the husbandmen, who tilled the ground, was Ezri the son of Chelub:€€ƒxqAnd over the dressers of the vine yards, was Semeias a Romathite: and over the wine cellars, Zabdias an Aphonite.€€ƒx}And over the oliveyards and the fig groves, which were in the plains, was Balanam a Gederite: and over the oil cellars, Joas.€€ƒxvAnd over the herds that fed in Saron, was Setrai a Saronite: and over the oxen in the valleys, Saphat the son of Adli:€€ƒxRAnd over the camels, Ubil an Ishmahelite and over the asses, Jadias a Meronathite:€€ƒx^And over the sheep Jaziz an Agarene. All these were the rulers of the substance of king David.€€ƒxAnd Jonathan David's uncle, a counsellor, a wise and learned man: he and Jahiel the son of Hachamoni were with the king's sons.€€ƒxUAnd Achitophel was the king's counsellor, and Chusai the Arachite, the king's friend.€€ƒxnAnd after Achitophel was Joiada the son of Banaias, and Abiathar. And the general of the king's army was Joab.€€ƒ`€€ƒywAnd David assembled all the chief men of Israel, the princes of the tribes, and the captains of the companies, who waited on the king: and the captains over thousands, and over hundreds, and them who had the charge over the substance and possessions of the king, and his sons with the officers of the court, and the men of power, and all the bravest of the army at Jerusalem.€€ƒxæAnd the king rising up, and standing said: Hear me, my brethren and my people: I had a thought to have built a house, in which the ark of the Lord, and the footstool of our God might rest: and prepared all things for the building.€€ƒxpAnd God said to me: Thou shalt not build a house to my name: because thou art a man of war, and hast shed blood.€€ƒyBut the Lord God of Israel chose me of all the house of my father, to be king over Israel for ever: for of Juda he chose the princes: and of the house of Juda, my father's house: and among the sons of my father, it pleased him to choose me king over all Israel.€€ƒx–And among my sons (for the Lord hath given me many sons) he hath chosen Solomon my son, to sit upon the throne of the kingdom of the Lord over Israel.€€•ƒxtAnd I will establish his kingdom for ever, it he continue to keep my commandments, and my judgments, as at this day.€€ƒxÜNow then before all the assembly of Israel, in the hearing of our God, keep ye, and seek all the commandments of the Lord our God: that you may possess the good land, and may leave it to your children after you for ever.€€ƒyAnd thou my son Solomon, know the God of thy father, and serve him with a perfect heart, and a willing mind: for the Lord searcheth all hearts, and understandeth all the thoughts of minds. If thou seek him, thou shalt find him: but if thou forsake him, he will cast thee off for ever.€€ƒxlNow therefore seeing the Lord hath chosen thee to build the house of the sanctuary, take courage, and do it.€€ƒx¾And David gave to Solomon his son a description of the porch, and of the temple, and of the treasures, and of the upper floor, and of the inner chambers, and of the house for the mercy seat,€€ƒxµAs also of all the courts, which he had in his thought, and of the chambers round about, for the treasures of the house of the Lord, and for the treasures of the consecrated things,€€ƒx¥And of the divisions of the priests and of the Levites, for all the works of the house of the Lord, and for all the vessels of the service of the temple of the Lord.€€ƒxzGold by weight for every vessel for the ministry. And silver by weight according to the diversity of the vessels and uses.€€ƒyHe gave also gold for the golden candlesticks, and their lamps, according to the dimensions of every candlestick, and the lamps thereof. In like manner also he gave silver by weight for the silver candlesticks, and for their lamps according to the diversity of the dimensions of them.€€ƒx‘He gave also gold for the tables of proposition, according to the diversity of the tables: in like manner also silver for other tables of silver.€€ƒxæFor fleshhooks also, and bowls, and censors of fine gold, and for little lions of gold, according to the measure he gave by weight, for every lion. In like manner also for lions of silver he set aside a different weight of silver.€€ƒx¸And for the altar of incense, he gave the purest gold: and to make the likeness of the chariot of the cherubims spreading their wings, and covering the ark of the covenant of the Lord.€€ƒx{All these things, said he, came to me written by the hand of the Lord that I might understand all the works of the pattern.€€ƒyAnd David said to Solomon his son: Act like a man, and take courage, and do: fear not, and be not dismayed: for the Lord my God will be with thee, and will not leave thee, nor forsake thee, till thou hast finished all the work for the service of the house of the Lord.€€ƒxÌBehold the courses of the priests and the Levites, for every ministry of the house of the Lord, stand by thee, and are ready, and both the princes, and the people know how to execute all thy commandments.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¹And king David said to all the assembly: Solomon my son, whom alone God hath chosen, is as yet young and tender: and the work is great, for a house is prepared not for man, but for God.€€ƒydAnd I with all my ability have prepared the expenses for the house of my God. Gold for vessels of gold, and silver for vessels of silver, brass for things of brass, iron for things of iron, wood for things of wood: and onyx stones, and stones like alabaster, and of divers colours, and all manner of precious stones, and marble of Paros in great abundance.€€ƒxËNow over and above the things which I have offered into the house of my God I give of my own proper goods, gold and silver for the temple of my God, beside what things I have prepared for the holy house.€€ƒx†Three thousand talents of gold of the gold of Ophir: and seven thousand talents of refined silver, to overlay the walls of the temple.€€ƒyAnd gold for wheresoever there is need of gold: and silver for wheresoever there is need of silver, for the works to be made by the hands of the artificers: now if any man is willing to offer, let him fill his hand to day, and offer what he pleaseth to the Lord.€€˜ƒxŞAnd they gave for the works of the house of the Lord, of gold, five thousand talents, and ten thousand solids: of silver ten thousand talents: and of brass eighteen thousand talents: and of iron a hundred thousand talents.€€ƒxwAnd all they that had stones, gave them to the treasures of the house of the Lord, by the hand of Jahiel the Gersonite.€€ƒxµAnd the people rejoiced, when they promised their offerings willingly: because they offered them to the Lord with all their heart: and David the king rejoiced also with a great joy.€€ƒxAnd he blessed the Lord before all the multitude, and he said: Blessed art thou, O Lord the God of Israel, our father from eternity to eternity.€€ƒxÊThine, O Lord, is magnificence, and power, and glory, and victory: and to thee is praise: for all that is in heaven, and in earth, is thine: thine is the kingdom, O Lord, and thou art above all princes.€€ƒx—Thine are riches, and thine is glory, thou hast dominion over all, in thy hand is power and might: in thy hand greatness, and the empire of all things.€€ƒxNNow therefore our God we give thanks to thee, and we praise thy glorious name.€€ƒx¤Who am I, and what is my people, that we should be able to promise thee all these things? all things are thine: and we have given thee what we received of thy hand.€€ƒx‡For we are sojourners before thee, and strangers, as were all our fathers. I Our days upon earth are as a shadow, and there is no stay.€€ƒx‰O Lord our God, all this store that we have prepared to build thee a house for thy holy name, is from thy hand, and all things are thine.€€ƒxõI know my God that thou provest hearts, and lovest simplicity, wherefore I also in the simplicity of my heart, have joyfully offered all these things: and I have seen with great joy thy people, which are here present, offer thee their offerings.€€ƒx O Lord God of Abraham, and of Isaac, and of Israel our fathers, keep for ever this will of their heart, and let this mind remain always for the worship of thee.€€ƒxÂAnd give to Solomon my son a perfect heart, that he may keep thy commandments, thy testimonies, and thy ceremonies, and do all things: and build the house, for which I have provided the charges.€€ƒxÁAnd David commanded all the assembly: Bless ye the Lord our God. And all the assembly blessed the Lord the God of their fathers: and they bowed themselves and worshipped God, and then the king.€€ƒxàAnd they sacrificed victims to the Lord: and they offered holocausts the next day, a thousand bullocks, a thousand rams, a thousand lambs, with their libations, and with every thing prescribed most abundantly for all Israel.€€ƒxÉAnd they ate, and drank before the Lord that day with great joy. And they anointed the second time Solomon the son of David. And they anointed him to the Lord to be prince, and Sadoc to be high priest.€€ƒx}And Solomon sat on the throne of the Lord as king instead of David his father, and he pleased all: and all Israel obeyed him.€€ƒx|And all the princes, and men of power, and all the sons of king David gave their hand, and were subject to Solomon the king.€€ƒx|And the Lord magnified Solomon over all Israel: and gave him the glory of a reign, such as no king of Israel had before him.€€ƒx1So David the son of Isai reigned over all Israel.€€ƒx†And the days that he reigned over Israel, were forty years: in Hebron he reigned seven years, and in Jerusalem three and thirty years.€€ƒxiAnd he died in a good age, full of days, and riches, and glory. And Solomon his son reigned in his stead.€€ƒxNow the acts of king David first and last are written in the book of Samuel the seer, and in the book of Nathan the prophet, and in the book of Gad the seer:€€ƒxˆAnd of all his reign, and his valour, and of the times that passed under him, either in Israel, or in all the kingdoms of the countries.€€ƒxThe Second Book of Chronicles€€ƒ`€€ƒx„And Solomon the son of David was strengthened in his kingdom, and the Lord his God was with him, and magnified him to a high degree.€€ƒx¨And Solomon gave orders to all Israel, to the captains of thousands, and of hundreds, and to the rulers, and to the judges of all Israel, and the heads of the families:€€ƒx­And he went with all the multitude to the high place of Gabaon, where was the tabernacle of the covenant of the Lord, which Moses the servant of God made, in the wilderness.€€ƒx§For David had brought the ark of God from Cariathiarim to the place, which he had prepared for it, and where he had pitched a tabernacle for it, that is, in Jerusalem.€€d2Chr¤dnamemII Chroniclesiosis_named2Chrlabbreviationd2Chrhchapters˜$‘ƒx^And behold that night God appeared to him, saying: Ask what thou wilt that I should give thee.€€ƒxoAnd Solomon said to God: Thou hast shewn great kindness to my father David: and hast made me king in his stead.€€ƒxÆNow therefore, O Lord God, let thy word be fulfilled, which thou hast promised to David my father: for thou hast made me king over thy great people, which is as innumerable as the dust of the earth.€€ƒxŒGive me wisdom and knowledge that I may come in and go out before thy people: for who can worthily judge this thy people, which is so great?€€ƒyAnd God said to Solomon: Because this choice hath pleased thy heart, and thou hast not asked riches, and wealth, and glory, nor the lives of them that hate thee, nor many days of life: but hast asked wisdom and knowledge, to be able to judge my people, over which I have made thee king,€€ƒx¨Wisdom and knowledge are granted to thee: and I will give thee riches, and wealth, and glory, so that none of the kings before thee, nor after thee, shall be like thee.€€ƒx|Then Solomon came from the high place of Gabaon to Jerusalem before the tabernacle of the covenant, and reigned over Israel.€€ƒxÎAnd he gathered to himself chariots and horsemen, and he had a thousand four hundred chariots, and twelve thousand horsemen: and he placed them in the cities of the chariots, and with the king in Jerusalem.€€ƒxŠAnd the king made silver and gold to be in Jerusalem as stones, and cedar trees as sycamores, which grow in the plains in great multitude.€€ƒxtAnd there were horses brought him from Egypt, and from Coa by the king's merchants, who went, and bought at a price,€€ƒx¾A chariot of four horses for six hundred pieces of silver, and a horse for a hundred and fifty: in like manner market was made in all the kingdoms of the Hethites, and of the kings of Syria.€€ƒ`€€ƒxZAnd Solomon determined to build a house to the name of the Lord, and a palace for himself.€€ƒxAnd he numbered out seventy thousand men to bear burdens, and eighty thousand to hew stones in the mountains, and three thousand six hundred to oversee them.€€ƒx“He sent also to Hiram king of Tyre, saying: As thou didst with David my father, and didst send him cedars, to build him a house, in which he dwelt:€€ƒypSo do with me that I may build a house to the name of the Lord my God, to dedicate it to burn incense before him, and to perfume with aromatical spices, and for the continual setting forth of bread, and for the holocausts, morning and evening, and on the sabbaths, and on the new moons, and the solemnities of the Lord our God for ever, which are commanded for Israel.€€ƒxUFor the house which I desire to build, is great: for our God is great above all gods.€€’ƒySend me therefore a skilful man, that knoweth how to work in gold, and in silver, in brass, and in iron, in purple, in scarlet and in blue, and that hath skill in engraving, with the artificers, which I have with me in Judea and Jerusalem, whom David my father provided.€€ƒx³Send me also cedars, and fir trees, and pine trees from Libanus: for I know that thy servants are skilful in cutting timber in Libanus, and my servants shall be with thy servants,€€ƒxqTo provide me timber in abundance. For the house which I desire to build, is to be exceeding great, and glorious.€€ƒxßAnd I will give thy servants the workmen that are to cut down the trees, for their food twenty thousand cores of wheat, and as many cores of barley, and twenty thousand measures of wine, and twenty thousand measures of oil.€€ƒxŒAnd Hiram king of Tyre sent a letter to Solomon, saying: Because the Lord hath loved his people, therefore he hath made thee king over them.€€ƒxìAnd he added, saying: Blessed be the Lord the God of Israel, who made heaven and earth, who hath given to king David a wise and knowing son, endued with understanding and prudence, to build a house to the Lord, and a palace for himself.€€ƒxHI therefore have sent thee my father Hiram, a wise and most skilful man,€€ƒy™The son of a woman of the daughters of Dan, whose father was a Tyrian, who knoweth how to work in gold, and in silver, in brass, and in iron, and in marble, and in timber, in purple also, and violet, and silk and scarlet: and who knoweth to grave all sort of graving, and to devise ingeniously all that there may be need of in the work with thy artificers, and with the artificers of my lord David thy father.€€ƒxxThe wheat therefore, and the barley and the oil, and the wine, which thou, my lord, hast promised, send to thy servants.€€ƒx±And we will cut down as many trees out of Libanus, as thou shalt want, and will convey them in floats by sea to Joppe: and it will be thy part to bring them thence to Jerusalem.€€ƒx»And Solomon numbered all the proselytes in the land of Israel, after the numbering which David his father had made, and they were found a hundred and fifty-three thousand and six hundred.€€ƒxÊAnd he set seventy thousand of them to carry burdens on their shoulders, and eighty thousand to hew stones in the mountains: and three thousand and six hundred to be overseers of the work of the people.€€ƒ`€€ƒxËAnd Solomon began to build the house of the Lord in Jerusalem, in mount Moria, which had been shewn to David his father, in the place which David had prepared in the thrashingfloor of Ornan the Jebusite.€€ƒxKAnd he began to build in the second month, in the fourth year of his reign.€€ƒx–Now these are the foundations, which Solomon laid, to build the house of God, the length by the first measure sixty cubits, the breadth twenty cubits.€€ƒx×And the porch in the front, which was extended in length according to the measure of the breadth of the house, twenty cubits: and the height was a hundred and twenty cubits: and he overlaid it within with pure gold.€€ƒx¾And the greater house he ceiled with deal boards, and overlaid them with plates of fine gold throughout: and he graved in them palm trees, and like little chains interlaced with one another.€€‘ƒx¸And the gold of the plates with which he overlaid the house, and the beams thereof, and the posts, and the walls, and the doors was of the finest: and he graved cherubims on the walls.€€ƒxùHe made also the house of the holy of holies: the length of it according to the breadth of the temple, twenty cubits, and the breadth of it in like manner twenty cubits: and he overlaid it with plates of gold, amounting to about six hundred talents.€€ƒxyHe made also nails of gold, and the weight of every nail was fifty sicles: the upper chambers also he overlaid with gold.€€ƒxlHe made also in the house of the holy of holies two cherubims of image work: and he overlaid them with gold.€€ƒxÚThe wings of the cherubims were extended twenty cubits, so that one wing was five cubits long, and reached to the wall of the house: and the other was also five cubits long, and reached to the wing of the other cherub.€€ƒx®In like manner the wing of the other cherub, was five cubits long, and reached to the wall: and his other wing was five cubits long, and touched the wing of the other cherub.€€ƒx±So the wings of the two cherubims were spread forth, and were extended twenty cubits: and they stood upright on their feet, and their faces were turned toward the house without.€€ƒxVHe made also a veil of violet, purple, scarlet, and silk: and wrought in it cherubims.€€ƒx†He made also before the doors of the temple two pillars, which were five and thirty cubits high: and their chapiters were five cubits.€€ƒx¥He made also as it were little chains in the oracle, and he put them on the heads of the pillars: and a hundred pomegranates, which he put between the little chains.€€ƒxºThese pillars he put at the entrance of the temple, one on the right hand, and the other on the left: that which was on the right hand, he called Jachin: and that on the left hand, Booz.€€ƒ`€€ƒx`He made also an altar of brass twenty cubits long, and twenty cubits broad, and ten cubits high.€€ƒx“Also a molten sea of ten cubits from brim to brim, round in compass: it was five cubits high, and a line of thirty cubits compassed it round about.€€ƒxšAnd under it there was the likeness of oxen, and certain engravings on the outside of ten cubits compassed the belly of the sea, as it were with two rows.€€ƒy?And the oxen were cast: and the sea itself was set upon the twelve oxen, three of which looked toward the north, and other three toward the west: and other three toward the south, and the other three that remained toward the east, and the sea stood upon them: and the hinder parts of the oxen were inward under the sea.€€ƒx”Now the thickness of it was a handbreadth, and the brim of it was like the brim of a cup, or of a crisped lily: and it held three thousand measures.€€–ƒx°And he made ten golden candlesticks, according to the form which they were commanded to be made by: and he set them in the temple, five on the right hand, and five on the left.€€ƒx„Moreover also ten tables: and he set them in the temple, five on the right side, and five on the left. Also a hundred bowls of gold.€€ƒxlHe made also the court of the priests, and a great hall, and doors in the hall, which he covered with brass.€€ƒxLAnd he set the sea on the right side over against the east toward the south.€€ƒxfAnd Hiram made caldrons, and fleshhooks, and bowls: and finished all the king's work the house of God:€€ƒx~That is to say, the two pillars, and the pommels, and the chapiters, and the network, to cover the chapiters over the pommels.€€ƒx¯And four hundred pomegranates, and two wreaths of network, so that two rows of pomegranates were joined to each wreath, to cover the pommels, and the chapiters of the pillars.€€ƒx<He made also bases, and lavers, which he set upon the bases:€€ƒx'One sea, and twelve oxen under the sea;€€ƒxAnd the caldrons, and fleshhooks, and bowls. All the vessels did Hiram his father make for Solomon in the house of the Lord of the finest brass.€€ƒxeIn the country near the Jordan did the king cast them, in a clay ground between Sochot and Saredatha.€€ƒx\And the multitude of vessels was innumerable, so that the weight of the brass was not known.€€ƒx‡And Solomon made all the vessels for the house of God, and the golden altar, and the tables, upon which were the loaves of proposition,€€ƒxrThe candlesticks also of most pure gold with their lamps to give light before the oracle, according to the manner.€€ƒxSAnd certain flowers, and lamps, and golden tongs: all were made of the finest gold.€€ƒy1The vessels also for the perfumes, and the censers, and the bowls, and the mortars, of pure gold. And he graved the doors of the inner temple, that is, for the holy of holies: and the doors of the temple without were of gold. And thus all the work was finished which Solomon made in the house of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx§Then Solomon brought in all those things that David his father had vowed, the silver, and the gold, and all the vessels he put among the treasures of the house of God.€€ƒxúAnd after this he gathered together the ancients of Israel and all the princes of the tribes, and the heads of the families, of the children of Israel to Jerusalem, to bring the ark of the covenant of the Lord out of the city of David, which is Sion.€€ƒxRAnd all the men of Israel came to the king in the solemn day of the seventh month.€€ƒxKAnd when all the ancients of Israel were come, the Levites took up the ark,€€ƒxªAnd brought it in, together with all the furniture of the tabernacle. And the priests with the Levites carried the vessels of the sanctuary, which were in the tabernacle.€€ƒx®And the priests brought in the ark of the covenant of the Lord into its place, that is, to the oracle of the temple, into the holy of holies under the wings of the cherubims:€€ƒx}So that the cherubims spread their wings over the place, in which the ark was set, and covered the ark itself and its staves.€€ƒxßNow the ends of the staves wherewith the ark was carried, because they were some thing longer, were seen before the oracle: but if a man were a little outward, he could not see them. So the ark has been there unto this day.€€ƒx«And there was nothing else in the ark but the two tables which Moses put there at Horeb when the Lord gave the law to the children of Israel, at their coming out of Egypt.€€ƒxÒNow when the priests were come out of the sanctuary, for all the priests that could be found there, were sanctified: and as yet at that time the courses and orders of the ministries were not divided among them,€€ƒylBoth the Levites and the singing men, that is, both they that were under Asaph, and they that were under Heman, and they that were under Idithun, with their sons, and their brethren, clothed with fine linen, sounded with cymbals, and psalteries, and harps, standing on the east side of the altar, and with them a hundred and twenty priests, sounding with trumpets.€€ƒyrSo when they all sounded together, both with trumpets, and voice, and cymbals, and organs, and with divers kind of musical instruments, and lifted up their voice on high: the sound was heard afar off, so that when they began to praise the Lord, and to say: Give glory to the Lord for he is good, for his mercy endureth for ever: the house of God was filled with a cloud.€€ƒxwNor could the priests stand and minister by reason of the cloud. For the glory of the Lord had filled the house of God.€€ƒ`€€ƒxDThen Solomon said: The Lord promised that he would dwell in a cloud.€€ƒxIBut I have built a house to his name, that he might dwell there for ever.€€ƒxxAnd the king turned his face, and blessed all the multitude of Israel for all the multitude stood attentive and he said:€€ƒxtBlessed be the Lord the God of Israel, who hath accomplished in deed that which he spoke to David my father, saying:€€ƒxİFrom the day that I brought my people out of the land of Egypt, I chose no city among all the tribes of Israel, for a house to be built in it to my name: neither chose I any other man, to be the ruler of my people Israel.€€˜*ƒxbAnd whereas David my father had a mind to build a house to the name of the Lord the God of Israel,€€ƒxThe Lord said to him: Forasmuch as it was thy will to build a house to my name, thou hast done well indeed in having such a will:€€ƒxtBut thou shalt not build the house, but thy son, who shall come out of thy loins, he shall build a house to my name.€€ƒxçThe Lord therefore hath accomplished his word which he spoke: and I am risen up in the place of David my father, and sit upon the throne of Israel, as the Lord promised: and have built a house to the name of the Lord God of Israel.€€ƒxmAnd I have put in it the ark, wherein is the covenant of the Lord, which he made with the children of Israel.€€ƒxuAnd he stood before the altar of the Lord, in presence of all the multitude of Israel, and stretched forth his hands.€€ƒyFor Solomon had made a brazen scaffold, and had set it in the midst of the temple, which was five cubits long, and five cubits broad, and three cubits high: and he stood upon it: then kneeling down in the presence of all the multitude of Israel, and lifting up his hands towards heaven,€€ƒx¯He said: O Lord God of Israel, there is no God like thee in heaven nor in earth: who keepest covenant and mercy with thy servants, that walk before thee with all their hearts:€€ƒx¹Who hast performed to thy servant David my father all that thou hast promised him: and hast accomplished in fact, what thou hast spoken with thy mouth, as also the present time proveth.€€ƒy%Now then, O Lord God of Israel, fulfil to thy servant David my father, whatsoever thou hast promised him, saying: There shall not fail thee a man in my sight, to sit upon the throne of Israel: yet so that thy children take heed to their ways, and walk in my law, as thou hast walked before me.€€ƒxeAnd now, Lord God of Israel, let thy word be established which thou hast spoken to thy servant David.€€ƒx¨Is it credible then that God should dwell with men on the earth? If heaven and the heavens of heavens do not contain thee, how much less this house, which I have built?€€ƒx¾But to this end only it is made, that thou mayest regard the prayer of thy servant and his supplication, O Lord my God: and mayest hear the prayers which thy servant poureth out before thee.€€ƒxŒThat thou mayest open thy eyes upon this house day and night, upon the place wherein thou hast promised that thy name should be called upon,€€ƒxøAnd that thou wouldst hear the prayer which thy servant prayeth in it: hearken then to the prayers of thy servant, and of thy people Israel. Whosoever shall pray in its place, hear thou from thy dwelling place, that is, from heaven, and shew mercy.€€ƒx‚If any man sin against his neighbour, and come to swear against him, and bind himself with a curse before the altar in this house:€€ƒxÆThen hear thou from heaven, and do justice to thy servants, so to requite the wicked by making his wickedness fall upon his own head, and to revenge the just, rewarding him according to his justice.€€ƒx²If thy people Israel be overcome by their enemies, (for they will sin against thee,) and being converted shall do penance, and call upon thy name, and pray to thee in this place,€€ƒx”Then hear thou from heaven, and forgive the sin of thy people Israel and bring them back into the land which thou gavest to them, and their fathers.€€ƒxÙIf the heavens be shut up, and there fall no rain by reason of the sin of the people, and they shall pray to thee in this place, and confess to thy name, and be converted from their sins, where thou dost afflict them,€€ƒxßThen hear thou from heaven, O Lord, and forgive the sins of thy servants and of thy people Israel and teach them the good way in which they may walk: and give rain to thy land which thou hast given to thy people to possess.€€ƒxÖIf a famine arise in the land, or a pestilence or blasting, or mildew, or locusts, or caterpillars: or if their enemies waste the country, and besiege the cities, whatsoever scourge or infirmity shall be upon them:€€ƒxƒThen if any of thy people Israel, knowing his own scourge and infirmity shall pray, and shall spread forth his hands in this house,€€ƒxÖHear thou from heaven, from thy high dwelling place, and forgive, and render to every one according to his ways, which thou knowest him to have in his heart: for thou only knowest the hearts of the children of men:€€ƒxŠThat they may fear thee, and walk in thy ways all the days that they live upon the face of the land, which thou hast given to our fathers.€€ƒx¸If the stranger also, who is not of thy people Israel, come from a far country, for the sake of thy great name, and thy strong hand, and thy stretched out arm, and adore in this place:€€ƒyHear thou from heaven thy firm dwelling place, and do all that which that stranger shall call upon thee for: that all the people of the earth may know thy name, and may fear thee, as thy people Israel, and may know, that thy name is invoked upon this house, which I have built.€€ƒxËIf thy people go out to war against their enemies, by the way that thou shalt send them, and adore thee towards the way of this city, which thou hast chosen, and the house which I have built to thy name:€€ƒxTThen hear thou from heaven their prayers, and their supplications, and revenge them.€€ƒxÒAnd if they sin against thee (for there is no man that sinneth not) and thou be angry with them, and deliver them up to their enemies, and they lead them away captive to a land either afar off, or near at hand,€€ƒxÚAnd if they be converted in their heart in the land to which they were led captive, and do penance, and pray to thee in the land of their captivity saying: We have sinned, we have done wickedly, we have dealt unjustly:€€ƒy'And return to thee with all their heart, and with all their soul, in the land of their captivity, to which they were led away, and adore thee towards the way of their own land which thou gavest their fathers, and of the city, which thou hast chosen, and the house which I have built to thy name:€€ƒx•Then hear thou from heaven, that is, from thy firm dwelling place, their prayers, and do judgment, and forgive thy people, although they have sinned:€€ƒx„For thou art my God: let thy eyes, I beseech thee, be open, and let thy ears be attentive to the prayer, that is made in this place.€€ƒx°Now therefore arise, O Lord God, into thy resting place, thou and the ark of thy strength: let thy priests, O Lord God, put on salvation, and thy saints rejoice in good things.€€ƒx^O Lord God, turn not away the face of thy anointed: remember the mercies of David thy servant.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¦And when Solomon had made an end of his prayer, fire came down from heaven, and consumed the holocausts and the victims: and the majesty of the Lord filled the house.€€ƒxNeither could the priests enter into the temple of the Lord, because the majesty of the Lord had filled the temple of the Lord.€€ƒyMoreover all the children of Israel saw the fire coming down, and the glory of the Lord upon the house: and falling down with their faces to the ground, upon the stone pavement, they adored and praised the Lord: because he is good, because his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxCAnd the king and all the people sacrificed victims before the Lord.€€ƒx§And king Solomon offered a sacrifice of twenty-two thousand oxen, and one hundred and twenty thousand rams: and the king and all the people dedicated the house of God.€€–ƒySolomon also sanctified the middle of the court before the temple of the Lord: for he offered there the holocausts, and the fat of the peace offerings: because the brazen altar, which he had made, could not hold the holocausts and the sacrifices and the fat:€€ƒxŸAnd Solomon kept the solemnity at that time seven days, and all Israel with him, a very great congregation, from the entrance of Emath to the torrent of Egypt.€€ƒx™And he made on the eighth day a solemn assembly, because he had kept the dedication of the altar seven days, and had celebrated the solemnity seven days.€€ƒxÎSo on the three and twentieth day of the seventh month he sent away the people to their dwellings, joyful and glad for the good that the Lord had done to David, and to Solomon, and to all Israel his people.€€ƒx´And Solomon finished the house of the Lord, and the king's house, and all that he had designed in his heart to do, in the house of the Lord, and in his own house, and he prospered.€€ƒxŠAnd the Lord appeared to him by night, and said: I have heard thy prayer, and I have chosen this place to myself for a house of sacrifice.€€ƒx•If I shut up heaven, and there fall no rain, or if I give orders, and command the locust to devour the land, or if I send pestilence among my people:€€ƒxğAnd my people, upon whom my name is called, being converted, shall make supplication to me, and seek out my face, and do penance for their most wicked ways: then will I hear from heaven, and will forgive their sins and will heal their land.€€ƒxeMy eyes also shall be open, and my ears attentive to the prayer of him that shall pray in this place.€€ƒxFor I have chosen, and have sanctified this place, that my name may be there for ever, and my eyes and my heart may remain there perpetually.€€ƒxŸAnd as for thee, if thou walk before me, as David thy father walked, and do according to all that I have commanded thee, and keep my justices and my judgments:€€ƒx™I will raise up the throne of thy kingdom, as I promised to David thy father, saying: There shall not fail thee a man of thy stock to be ruler in Israel.€€ƒx”But if you turn away, and forsake my justices, and my commandments which I have set before you, and shall go and serve strange gods, and adore them,€€ƒxÜI will pluck you up by the root out of my land which I have given you: and this house which I have sanctified to my name, I will cast away from before my face, and will make it a byword, and an example among all nations.€€ƒxAnd this house shall be for a proverb to all that pass by, and they shall be astonished and say: Why hath the Lord done thus to this land, and to this house?€€ƒxìAnd they shall answer: Because they forsook the Lord the God of their fathers, who brought them out of the land of Egypt, and laid hold on strange gods, and adored them, and worshipped them: therefore all these evils are come upon them.€€ƒ`€€ƒx_And at the end of twenty years after Solomon had built the house of the Lord and his own house:€€ƒxgHe built the cities which Hiram had given to Solomon, and caused the children of Israel to dwell there.€€ƒx/He went also into Emath Suba, and possessed it.€€ƒxNAnd he built Palmira in the desert, and he built other strong cities in Emath.€€ƒxjAnd he built Beth-horon the upper, and Beth-horon the nether, walled cities with gates and bars and locks.€€’ƒx All the people that were left of the Hethites, and the Amorrhites, and the Pherezites, and the Hevites, and the Jebusites, that were not of the stock of Israel:€€ƒx†Of their children, and of the posterity, whom the children of Israel had not slain, Solomon made to be the tributaries, unto this day.€€ƒxBut of the children of Israel he set none to serve in the king's works: for they were men of war, and chief captains, and rulers of his chariots and horsemen.€€ƒxdAnd all the chief captains of king Solomon's army were two hundred and fifty, who taught the people.€€ƒx÷And he removed the daughter of Pharao from the city of David, to the house which he had built for her. For the king said: My wife shall not dwell in the house of David king of Israel, for it is sanctified: because the ark of the Lord came into it.€€ƒxkThen Solomon offered holocausts to the Lord upon the altar of the Lord which he had built before the porch,€€ƒyThat every day an offering might be made on it according to the ordinance of Moses, in the sabbaths, and on the new moons, and on the festival days three times a year, that is to say, in the feast of unleavened bread, and in the feast of weeks, and in the feast of tabernacles.€€ƒy>And he appointed according to the order of David his father the offices of the priests in their ministries: and the Levites in their order to give praise, and minister before the priests according to the duty of every day: and the porters in their divisions by gate and gate: for so David the man of God had commanded.€€ƒx•And the priests and Levites departed not from the king's commandments, as to any thing that he had commanded, and as to the keeping of the treasures.€€ƒx{Solomon had all charges prepared, from the day that he founded the house of the Lord, until the day wherein he finished it.€€ƒxjThen Solomon went to Asiongaber, and to Ailath, on the coast of the Red Sea, which is in the land of Edom.€€ƒxÙAnd Hiram sent him ships by the hands of his servants, and skilful mariners, and they went with Solomon's servants to Ophir, and they took thence four hundred and fifty talents of gold, and brought it to king Solomon.€€ƒ`€€ƒy And when the queen of Saba heard of the fame of Solomon, she came to try him with hard questions at Jerusalem, with great riches, and camels, which carried spices, and abundance of gold, and precious stones. And when she was come to Solomon, she proposed to him all that was in her heart.€€ƒxtAnd Solomon explained to her all that she proposed: and there was not any thing that he did not make clear unto her.€€ƒxdAnd when she had seen these things, to wit, the wisdom of Solomon, and the house which he had built,€€ƒyAnd the meats of his table, and the dwelling places of his servants, and the attendance of his officers, and their apparel, his cupbearers also, and their garments, and the victims which he offered in the house of the Lord: there was no more spirit in her, she was so astonished.€€ƒxaAnd she said to the king: The word is true which I heard in my country of thy virtues and wisdom.€€˜ƒxaHappy are thy men, and happy are thy servants, who stand always before thee, and hear thy wisdom.€€ƒxêBlessed be the Lord thy God, who hath been pleased to set thee on his throne, king of the Lord thy God. Because God loveth Israel, and will preserve them forever: therefore hath he made thee king over them, to do judgment and justice.€€ƒxÈAnd she gave to the king a hundred and twenty talents of gold, and spices in great abundance, and most precious stones: there were no such spices as these which the queen of Saba gave to king Solomon.€€ƒx‚And the servants also of Hiram, with the servants of Solomon, brought gold from Ophir, and thyine trees, and most precious stones:€€ƒxÃAnd the king made of the thyine trees stairs in the house of the Lord, and in the king's house, and harps and psalteries for the singing men: never were there seen such trees in the land of Juda.€€ƒxÂAnd king Solomon gave to the queen of Saba all that she desired, and that she asked, and many more things than she brought to him: so she returned, and went to her own country with her servants.€€ƒxrAnd the weight of the gold, that was brought to Solomon every year, was six hundred and sixty-six talents of gold:€€ƒxÁBeside the sum which the deputies of divers nations, and the merchants were accustomed to bring, and all the kings of Arabia, and the lords of the lands, who brought gold and silver to Solomon.€€ƒxuAnd king Solomon made two hundred golden spears, of the sum of six hundred pieces of gold, which went to every spear:€€ƒx´And three hundred golden shields of three hundred pieces of gold, which went to the covering of every shield: and the king put them in the armoury, which was compassed with a wood.€€ƒxKThe king also made a great throne of ivory, and overlaid it with pure gold.€€ƒxƒAnd six steps to go up to the throne, and a footstool of gold, and two arms one on either side, and two lions standing by the arms:€€ƒxuMoreover twelve other little lions standing upon the steps on both sides: there was not such a throne in any kingdom.€€ƒxµAnd all the vessels of the king's table were of gold, and the vessels of the house of the forest of Libanus were of the purest gold. For no account was made of silver in those days.€€ƒx¡For the king's ships went to Tharsis with the servants of Hiram, once in three years: and they brought thence gold and silver, and ivory, and apes, and peacocks.€€ƒxPAnd Solomon was magnified above all the kings of the earth for riches and glory.€€ƒx„And all the kings of the earth desired to see the face of Solomon, that they might hear the wisdom which God had given in his heart.€€ƒx…And every year they brought him presents, vessels of silver and of gold, and garments, and armour, and spices, and horses, and mules.€€ƒx·And Solomon had forty thousand horses in the stables, and twelve thousand chariots, and horsemen, and he placed them in the cities of the chariots and where the king was in Jerusalem.€€ƒxƒAnd he exercised authority over all the kings from the river Euphrates to the land of the Philistines, and to the borders of Egypt.€€ƒxxAnd he made silver as plentiful in Jerusalem as stones: and cedars as common as the sycamores, which grow in the plains.€€ƒxFAnd horses were brought to him out of Egypt, and out of all countries.€€ƒxÑNow the rest of the acts of Solomon first and last are written in the words of Nathan the prophet, and in the books of Ahias the Silonite, and in the vision of Addo the seer, against Jeroboam the son of Nabat.€€ƒx=And Solomon reigned in Jerusalem over all Israel forty years.€€ƒxqAnd he slept with his fathers: and they buried him in the city of David: and Roboam his son reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒxSAnd Roboam went to Sichem: for thither all Israel were assembled, to make him king.€€ƒx~And when Jeroboam the son of Nabat, who was in Egypt, (for he was fled thither from Solomon,) heard it, forthwith he returned.€€ƒxUAnd they sent for him, and he came with all Israel, and they spoke to Roboam, saying:€€ƒxÅThy father oppressed with a most grievous yoke, do thou govern us with a lighter hand than thy father, who laid upon us a heavy servitude, and ease some thing of the burden, that we may serve thee.€€ƒxVAnd he said to them: Come to me again after three days. And when the people were gone,€€“ƒxvAnd they said to him: If thou please this people, and soothe them with kind words, they will be thy servants for ever.€€ƒxŒBut he forsook the counsel of the ancients, and began to treat with the young men, that had been brought up with him, and were in his train.€€ƒx”And he said to them: What seemeth good to you? or what shall I answer this people, who have said to me: Ease the yoke which thy father laid upon us?€€ƒyBut they answered as young men, and brought up with him in pleasures, and said: Thus shalt thou speak to the people, that said to thee: Thy father made our yoke heavy, do thou ease it: thus shalt thou answer them: My little finger is thicker than the loins of my father.€€ƒxMy father laid upon you a heavy yoke, and I will add more weight to it: my father beat you with scourges, but I will beat you with scorpions.€€ƒxSSo Jeroboam, and all the people came to Roboam the third day, as he commanded them.€€ƒxCAnd the king answered roughly, leaving the counsel of the ancients.€€ƒx½And he spoke according to the advice of the young men: My father laid upon you a heavy yoke, which I will make heavier: my father beat you with scourges, but I will beat you with scorpions.€€ƒxÂAnd he condescended not to the people's requests: for it was the will of God, that his word might be fulfilled which he had spoken by the hand of Ahias the Silonite to Jeroboam the son of Nabat.€€ƒxûAnd all the people upon the king's speaking roughly, said thus unto him: We have no part in David, nor inheritance in the son of Isai. Return to thy dwellings, O Israel, and do thou, O David feed thy own house. And Israel went away to their dwellings.€€ƒxPBut Roboam reigned over the children of Israel that dwelt in the cities of Juda.€€ƒx»And king Roboam sent Aduram, who was over the tributes, and the children of Israel stoned him, and he died: and king Roboam made haste to get up into his chariot, and fled into Jerusalem.€€ƒx:And Israel revolted from the house of David unto this day.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÑAnd Roboam came to Jerusalem, and called together all the house of Juda and of Benjamin, a hundred and fourscore thousand chosen men and warriors, to fight against Israel, and to bring back his kingdom to him.€€ƒx@And the word of the Lord came to Semeias the man of God, saying:€€ƒx]Speak to Roboam the son of Solomon the king of Juda, and to all Israel, in Juda and Benjamin:€€ƒxôThus saith the Lord: You shall not go up, nor fight against your brethren: let every man return to his own house, for by my will this thing has been done. And when they heard the word of the Lord, they returned, and did not go against Jeroboam,€€ƒx?And Roboam dwelt in Jerusalem, and built walled cities in Juda.€€—ƒx$And Bethsur, and Socho, and Odollam,€€ƒxAnd Geth, and Maresa, and Ziph,€€ƒx#And Aduram, and Lachis, and Azecha,€€ƒxWSaraa also, and Aialon, and Hebron, which are in Juda and Benjamin, well fenced cities.€€ƒx~And when he had enclosed them with walls, he put in them governors and storehouses of provisions, that is, of oil and of wine.€€ƒx”Moreover in every city he made an armoury of shields and spears, and he fortified them with great diligence, and he reigned over Juda, and Benjamin,€€ƒxYAnd the priests and Levites, that were in all Israel, came to him out of all their seats,€€ƒx·Leaving their suburbs, and their possessions, and passing over to Juda, and Jerusalem, because Jeroboam and his sons had cast them off, from executing the priestly office to the Lord.€€ƒxmAnd he made to himself priests for the high places, and for the devils, and for the calves which he had made.€€ƒxÁMoreover out of all the tribes of Israel, whosoever gave their heart to seek the Lord the God of Israel, came into Jerusalem to sacrifice their victims before the Lord the God of their fathers.€€ƒx¬And they strengthened the kingdom of Juda, and established Roboam the son of Solomon for three years: for they walked in the ways of David and of Solomon, only three years.€€ƒxAnd Roboam took to wife Mahalath, the daughter of Jerimoth the son of David: and Abihail the daughter of Eliab the son of Isai.€€ƒx5And they bore him sons Jehus, and Somorias, and Zoom.€€ƒxnAnd after her he married Maacha the daughter of Absalom, who bore him Abia, and Ethai, and Ziza, and Salomith.€€ƒxÏAnd Roboam loved Maacha the daughter of Absalom above all his wives and concubines: for he had married eighteen wives, and threescore concubines: and he begot eight and twenty sons, and threescore daughters.€€ƒxBut he put at the head of them Abia the son of Maacha to be the chief ruler over all his brethren: for he meant to make him king,€€ƒxÉBecause he was wiser and mightier than all his sons, and in all the countries of Juda, and of Benjamin, and in all the walled cities: and he gave them provisions in abundance, and he sought many wives.€€ƒ`€€ƒxwAnd when the kingdom of Roboam was strengthened and fortified, he forsook the law of the Lord, and all Israel with him.€€ƒx†And in the fifth year of the reign of Roboam, Sesac king of Egypt came up against Jerusalem (because they had sinned against the Lord)€€ƒx´With twelve hundred chariots and threescore thousand horsemen: and the people were without number that came with him out of Egypt, to wit, Libyans, and Troglodites, and Ethiopians.€€ƒx@And he took the strongest cities in Juda, and came to Jerusalem.€€ƒxçAnd Semeias the prophet came to Roboam, and to the princes of Juda, that were gathered together in Jerusalem, fleeing from Sesac, and he said to them: Thus saith the Lord: You have left me, and I have left you in the hand of Sesac.€€ƒxóAnd when the Lord saw that they were humbled, the word of the Lord came to Semeias, saying: Because they are humbled, I will not destroy them, and I will give them a little help, and my wrath shall not fall upon Jerusalem by the hand of Sesac.€€ƒx~But yet they shall serve him, that they may know the difference between my service, and the service of a kingdom of the earth.€€ƒx¦So Sesac king of Egypt departed from Jerusalem, taking away the treasures of the king's house, and he took all with him, and the golden shields that Solomon had made,€€ƒxŒInstead of which the king made brazen ones, and delivered them to the captains of the shieldbearers, who guarded the entrance of the palace.€€ƒx‰And when the king entered into the house of the Lord, the shieldbearers came and took them, and brought them back again to their armoury.€€ƒx¢But yet because they were humbled, the wrath of the Lord turned away from them, and they were not utterly destroyed: for even in Juda there were found good works.€€ƒy5King Roboam therefore was strengthened in Jerusalem, and reigned: he was one and forty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned seventeen years in Jerusalem, the city which the Lord chose out of all the tribes of Israel, to establish his name there: and the name of his mother was Naama an Ammonitess.€€ƒx@But he did evil, and did not prepare his heart to seek the Lord.€€ƒxÃNow the acts of Roboam first and last are written in the books of Semeias the prophet, and of Addo the seer, and diligently recorded: and there was war between Roboam and Jeroboam all their days.€€ƒxnAnd Roboam slept with his fathers, and was buried in the city of David. And Abia his son reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒx@In the eighteenth year of king Jeroboam, Abia reigned over Juda.€€ƒx”Three years he reigned in Jerusalem, and his mother's name was Michaia, the daughter of Uriel of Gabaa: and there was war between Abia and Jeroboam.€€ƒxÚAnd when Abia had begun battle, and had with him four hundred thousand most valiant and chosen men, Jeroboam put his army in array against him, eight hundred thousand men, who were also chosen and most valiant for war.€€ƒxgAnd Abia stood upon mount Semeron, which was in Ephraim, and said: Hear me, O Jeroboam, and all Israel:€€ƒx‰Do you not know that the Lord God of Israel gave to David the kingdom over Israel for ever, to him and to his sons by a covenant of salt?€€–ƒxÇAnd there were gathered to him vain men, and children of Belial: and they prevailed against Roboam the son of Solomon: for Roboam was unexperienced, and of a fearful heart, and could not resist them.€€ƒxÕAnd now you say that you are able to withstand the kingdom of the Lord, which he possesseth by the sons of David, and you have a great multitude of people, and golden calves, which Jeroboam hath made you for gods.€€ƒyAnd you have cast out the priests of the Lord, the sons of Aaron, and the Levites: and you have made you priests, like all the nations of the earth: whosoever cometh and consecrateth his hand with a bullock of the herd, and with seven rams, is made a priest of those who are no gods.€€ƒx‘But the Lord is our God, whom we forsake not, and the priests who minister to the Lord are the sons of Aaron, and the Levites are in their order.€€ƒyaAnd they offer holocausts to the Lord, every day, morning and evening, and incense made according to the ordinance of the law, and the loaves are set forth on a most clean table, and there is with us the golden candlestick, and the lamps thereof, to be lighted always in the evening: for we keep the precepts of the Lord our God, whom you have forsaken.€€ƒxĞTherefore God is the leader in our army, and his priests who sound with trumpets, and resound against you: O children of Israel, fight not against the Lord the God of your fathers, for it is not good for you.€€ƒxµWhile he spoke these things, Jeroboam caused an ambushment to come about behind him. And while he stood facing the enemies, he encompassed Juda, who perceived it not, with his army.€€ƒx¥And when Juda looked back, they saw the battle coming upon them both before and behind, and they cried to the Lord: and the priests began to sound with the trumpets.€€ƒx‡And all the men of Juda shouted: and behold when they shouted, God terrified Jeroboam, and all Israel that stood against Abia and Juda.€€ƒxYAnd the children of Israel fled before Juda, and the Lord delivered them into their hand.€€ƒx}And Abia and his people slew them with a great slaughter, and there fell wounded of Israel five hundred thousand valiant men.€€ƒx²And the children of Israel were brought down, at that time, and the children of Juda were exceedingly strengthened, because they had trusted in the Lord the God of their fathers.€€ƒx’And Abia pursued after Jeroboam, and took cities from him, Bethel and her daughters, and Jesana with her daughters, Ephron also and her daughters.€€ƒxhAnd Jeroboam was not able to resist any more, in the days of Abia: and the Lord struck him, and he died.€€ƒxwBut Abia, being strengthened in his kingdom, took fourteen wives: and begot two and twenty sons, and sixteen daughters.€€ƒxtAnd the rest of the acts of Abia, and of his ways and works, are written diligently in the book of Addo the prophet.€€ƒ`€€ƒx™And Abia slept with his fathers, and they buried him in the city of David: an Asa his son reigned in his stead: in his days the land was quiet ten years.€€ƒxŠAnd Asa did that which was good and pleasing in the sight of his God, and he destroyed the altars of foreign worship, and the high places.€€ƒx/And broke the statues, and cut down the groves.€€ƒxmAnd he commanded Juda to seek the Lord the God of their fathers, and to do the law, and all the commandments.€€ƒx]And he took away out of all the cities of Juda the altars, and temples, and reigned in peace.€€ƒy5And he said to Juda: Let us build these cities, and compass them with walls, and fortify them with towers, and gates, and bars, while all is quiet from wars, because we have sought the Lord the God of our fathers, and he hath given us peace round about. So they built, and there was no hinderance in building.€€ƒxÎAnd Asa had in his army of men that bore shields and spears of Juda three hundred thousand, and of Benjamin that bore shields and drew bows, two hundred and eighty thousand, all these were most valiant men.€€ƒx–And Zara the Ethiopian came out against them with his army of ten hundred thousand men, and with three hundred chariots: and he came as far as Maresa.€€ƒxpAnd Asa went out to meet him, and set his army in array for battle in the vale of Sephata, which is near Maresa:€€ƒy!And he called upon the Lord God, and said: Lord, there is no difference with thee, whether thou help with few, or with many: help us, O Lord our God: for with confidence in thee, and in thy name we are come against this multitude. O Lord thou art our God, let not man prevail against thee.€€ƒxSAnd the Lord terrified the Ethiopians before Asa and Juda: and the Ethiopians fled.€€ƒxêAnd Asa and the people that were with him pursued them to Gerara: and the Ethiopians fell even to utter destruction, for the Lord slew them, and his army fought against them, and they were destroyed. And they took abundance of spoils,€€ƒx’And they took all the cities round about Gerara: for a great fear was come upon all men: and they pillaged the cities, and carried off much booty.€€ƒxsAnd they destroyed the sheepcotes, and took an infinite number of cattle, and of camels: and returned to Jerusalem.€€ƒ`€€ƒx8And the spirit of God came upon Azarias the son of Oded,€€ƒxİAnd he went out to meet Asa, and said to him: Hear ye me, Asa, and all Juda and Benjamin: The Lord is with you, because you have been with him. If you seek him, you shall find: but if you forsake him, he will forsake you.€€ƒxnAnd many days shall pass in Israel, without the true God, and without a priest a teacher, and without the law.€€ƒxtAnd when in their distress they shall return to the Lord the God of Israel, and shall seek him, they shall find him.€€ƒx‹At that time there shall be no peace to him that goeth out and cometh in, but terrors on every side among all the inhabitants of the earth.€€“ƒxmDo you therefore take courage, and let not your hands be weakened: for there shall be a reward for your work.€€ƒyAAnd when Asa had heard the words, and the prophecy of Azarias the son of Oded the prophet, he took courage, and took away the idols out of all the land of Juda, and out of Benjamin, and out of the cities of mount Ephraim, which he had taken, and he dedicated the altar of the Lord, which was before the porch of the Lord.€€ƒxÊAnd he gathered together all Juda and Benjamin, and the strangers with them of Ephraim, and Manasses, and Simeon: for many were come over to him out of Israel, seeing that the Lord his God was with him.€€ƒxcAnd when they were come to Jerusalem in the third month, in the fifteenth year of the reign of Asa,€€ƒx‹They sacrificed to the Lord in that day of the spoils, and of the prey, that they had brought, seven hundred oxen, and seven thousand rams.€€ƒx—And he went in to confirm as usual the covenant, that they should seek the Lord the God of their fathers with all their heart, and with all their soul.€€ƒxqAnd if any one, said he, seek not the Lord the God of Israel, let him die, whether little or great, man or woman.€€ƒxsAnd they swore to the Lord with a loud voice with joyful shouting, and with sound of trumpet, and sound of cornets,€€ƒx®All that were in Juda with a curse: for with all their heart they swore, and with all their will they sought him, and they found him, and the Lord gave them rest round about.€€ƒxÚMoreover Maacha the mother of king Asa he deposed from the royal authority, because she had made in a grove an idol of Priapus: and he entirely destroyed it, and breaking it into pieces, burnt it at the torrent Cedron.€€ƒx\But high places were left in Israel: nevertheless the heart of Asa was perfect all his days.€€ƒx˜And the things which his father had vowed, and he himself had vowed, he brought into the house of the Lord, gold and silver, and vessels of divers uses.€€ƒxLAnd there was no war unto the five and thirtieth year of the kingdom of Asa.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¿And in the six and thirtieth year of his kingdom, Baasa the king of Israel came up against Juda, and built a wall about Rama, that no one might safely go out or come in of the kingdom of Asa.€€ƒx²Then Asa brought out silver and gold out of the treasures of the house of the Lord, and of the king's treasures, and sent to Benadad king of Syria, who dwelt in Damascus, saying:€€ƒxÚThere is a league between me and thee, as there was between my father and thy father, wherefore I have sent thee silver and gold, that thou mayst break thy league with Baasa king of Israel, and make him depart from me.€€ƒx°And when Benadad heard this, he sent the captains of his armies against the cities of Israel: and they took Ahion, and Dan, and Abelmaim, and all the walled cities of Nephtali.€€ƒxWAnd when Baasa heard of it, he left off the building of Rama, and interrupted his work.€€ƒxäAt that time Hanani the prophet came to Asa king of Juda, and said to him: Because thou hast had confidence in the king of Syria, and not in the Lord thy God, therefore hath the army of the king of Syria escaped out of thy hand.€€ƒxÁWere not the Ethiopians, and the Libyans much more numerous in chariots, and horsemen, and an exceeding great multitude: yet because thou trustedst in the Lord, he delivered them into thy hand?€€ƒxÕFor the eyes of the Lord behold all the earth, and give strength to those who with a perfect heart trust in him. Wherefore thou hast done foolishly, and for this cause from this time wars shall arise against thee.€€ƒx®And Asa was angry with the seer, and commanded him to be put in prison: for he was greatly enraged because of this thing: and he put to death many of the people at that time.€€ƒx`But the works of Asa the first and last are written in the book of the kings of Juda and Israel.€€ƒxÂAnd Asa fell sick in the nine and thirtieth year of his reign, of a most violent pain in his feet, and yet in his illness he did not seek the Lord, but rather trusted in the skill of physicians.€€ƒxUAnd he slept with his fathers: and he died in the one and fortieth year of his reign.€€ƒyAnd they buried him in his own sepulchre, which he had made for himself in the city of David: and they laid him on his bed full of spices and odoriferous ointments, which were made by the art of the perfumers, and they burnt them over him with very great pomp.€€ƒ`€€ƒxJAnd Josaphat his son reigned in his stead, and grew strong against Israel.€€ƒx®And he placed numbers of soldiers in all the fortified cities of Juda. And he put garrisons in the land of Juda, and in the cities of Ephraim, which Asa his father had taken.€€ƒxsAnd the Lord was with Josaphat, because he walked in the first ways of David his father: and trusted not in Baalim,€€ƒxfBut in the God of his father, and walked in his commandments, and not according to the sins of Israel.€€ƒxŒAnd the Lord established the kingdom in his hand, and all Juda brought presents to Josaphat: and he acquired immense riches, and much glory.€€“ƒx™And in the third year of his reign, he sent of his princes Benhail, and Abdias, and Zacharias, and Nathanael, and Micheas, to teach in the cites of Juda:€€ƒxÅAnd with them the Levites, Semeias, and Nathanias, and Zabadias, and Asael, and Semiramoth, and Jonathan, and Adonias, and Tobias, and Thobadonias Levites, and with them Elisama, and Joram priests.€€ƒxœAnd they taught the people in Juda, having with them the book of the law of the Lord: and they went about all the cities of Juda, and instructed the people.€€ƒxŠAnd the fear of the Lord came upon all the kingdoms of the lands that were round about Juda, and they durst not make war against Josaphat.€€ƒx§The Philistines also brought presents to Josaphat, and tribute in silver, and the Arabians brought him cattle, seven thousand seven hundred rams, and as many he goats.€€ƒxjAnd Josaphat grew, and became exceeding great: and he built in Juda houses like towers, and walled cities.€€ƒxdAnd he prepared many works in the cities of Juda: and he had warriors, and valiant men in Jerusalem.€€ƒx¨Of whom this is the number of the houses and families of every one: in Juda captains of the army, Ednas the chief, and with him three hundred thousand most valiant men.€€ƒxLAfter him Johanan the captain, and with him two hundred and eighty thousand.€€ƒxyAnd after him was Amasias the son of Zechri, consecrated to the Lord, and with him were two hundred thousand valiant men.€€ƒxdAfter him was Eliada valiant in battle, and with him two hundred thousand armed with bow and shield.€€ƒxUAfter him also was Jozabad, and with him a hundred and eighty thousand ready for war.€€ƒxiAll these were at the hand of the king, beside others, whom he had put in the walled cities, in all Juda.€€ƒ`€€ƒxMNow Josaphat was rich and very glorious, and was joined by affinity to Achab.€€ƒxÊAnd he went down to him after some years to Samaria: and Achab at his coming killed sheep and oxen in abundance for him and the people that came with him: and he persuaded him to go up to Ramoth Galaad.€€ƒx¿And Achab king of Israel said to Josaphat king of Juda: Come with me to Ramoth Galaad. And he answered him: Thou art as I am, and my people as thy people, and we will be with thee in the war.€€ƒxbAnd Josaphat said to the king of Israel: Inquire, I beseech thee, at present the word of the Lord.€€ƒxÛSo the king of Israel gathered together of the prophets four hundred men, and he said to them: Shall we go to Ramoth Galaad to fight, or shall we forbear? But they said: Go up, and God will deliver into the king's hand.€€˜"ƒxøAnd the king of Israel said to Josaphat: There is one man, of whom we may ask the will of the Lord: but I hate him, for he never prophesieth good to me, but always evil: and it is Micheas the son of Jemla. And Josaphat said: Speak not thus, O king.€€ƒxiAnd the king of Israel called one of the eunuchs, and said to him: Call quickly Micheas the son of Jemla.€€ƒxÉNow the king of Israel, and Josaphat king of Juda, both sat on their thrones, clothed in royal robes, and they sat in the open court by the gate of Samaria, and all the prophets prophesied before them.€€ƒxAnd Sedecias the son of Chanaana made him horns of iron, and said: Thus saith the Lord: With these shalt thou push Syria, till thou destroy it.€€ƒxAnd all the prophets prophesied in like manner, and said: Go up to Ramoth Galaad, and thou shalt prosper, and the Lord will deliver them into the king's hand.€€ƒxçAnd the messenger that went to call Micheas, said to him: Behold the words of all the prophets with one mouth declare good to the king: I beseech thee therefore let not thy word disagree with them, and speak thou also good success.€€ƒxcAnd Micheas answered him: As the Lord liveth, whatsoever my God shall say to me, that will I speak.€€ƒxáSo he came to the king: and the king said to him: Micheas, shall we go to Ramoth Galaad to fight, or forbear? And he answered him: Go up, for all shall succeed prosperously, and the enemies shall be delivered into your hands.€€ƒxmAnd the king said: I adjure thee again and again to say nothing but the truth to me, in the name of the Lord.€€ƒx³Then he said: I saw all Israel scattered in the mountains, like sheep without a shepherd: and the Lord said: These have no masters: let every man return to his own house in peace.€€ƒxtAnd the king of Israel said to Josaphat: Did I not tell thee that this man would not prophesy me any good, but evil?€€ƒx©Then he said: Hear ye therefore the word of the Lord: I saw the Lord sitting on his throne, and all the army of heaven standing by him on the right hand and on the left,€€ƒx¡And the Lord said: Who shall deceive Achab king of Israel, that he may go up and fall in Ramoth Galaad? And when one spoke in this manner, and another otherwise:€€ƒx’There came forth a spirit, and stood before the Lord, and said: I will deceive him. And the Lord said to him: By what means wilt thou deceive him?€€ƒx¤And he answered: I will go out, and be a lying spirit in the mouth of all his prophets. And the Lord said: Thou shalt deceive, and shalt prevail: go out, and do so.€€ƒx†Now therefore behold the Lord hath put a spirit of lying in the mouth of all thy prophets, and the Lord hath spoken evil against thee.€€ƒx‘And Sedecias the son of Chanaana came, and struck Micheas on the cheek and said: Which way went the spirit of the Lord from me, to speak to thee?€€ƒxuAnd Micheas said: Thou thyself shalt see in that day, when thou shalt go in from chamber to chamber, to hide thyself.€€ƒx‡And the king of Israel commanded, saying: Take Micheas, and carry him to Amon the governor of the city, and to Joas the son of Amelech,€€ƒxAnd say: Thus saith the king: Put this fellow in prison, and give him bread and water in a small quantity till I return in peace.€€ƒxlAnd Micheas said: If thou return in peace, the Lord hath not spoken by me. And he said: Hear, all ye people.€€ƒxISo the king of Israel and Josaphat king of Juda went up to Ramoth Galaad.€€ƒxÇAnd the king of Israel said to Josaphat: I will change my dress, and so I will go to the battle, but put thou on thy own garments. And the king of Israel having changed his dress, went to the battle.€€ƒxNow the king of Syria had commanded the captains of his cavalry, saying: Fight ye not with small, or great, but with the king of Israel only.€€ƒxËSo when the captains of the cavalry saw Josaphat, they said: This is the king of Israel. And they surrounded him to attack him: but he cried to the Lord, and he helped him, and turned them away from him.€€ƒx\For when the captains of the cavalry saw, that he was not the king of Israel, they left him.€€ƒxåAnd it happened that one of the people shot an arrow at a venture, and struck the king of Israel between the neck and the shoulders, and he said to his chariot man: Turn thy hand, and carry me out of the battle, for I am wounded.€€ƒxŒAnd the fight was ended that day: but the king of Israel stood in his chariot against the Syrians until the evening, and died at the sunset.€€ƒ`€€ƒxFAnd Josaphat king of Juda returned to his house in peace to Jerusalem.€€ƒxÖAnd Jehu the son of Hanani the seer met him, and said to him: Thou helpest the ungodly, and thou art joined in friendship with them that hate the Lord, and therefore thou didst deserve indeed the wrath of the Lord:€€ƒx§But good works are found in thee, because thou hast taken away the groves out of the land of Juda, and hast prepared thy heart to seek the Lord the God of thy fathers.€€ƒx And Josaphat dwelt at Jerusalem: and he went out again to the people from Bersabee to mount Ephraim, and brought them back to the Lord the God of their fathers.€€ƒxOAnd he set judges of the land in all the fenced cities of Juda, in every place.€€‹ƒx¤Let the fear of the Lord be with you, and do all things with diligence: for there is no iniquity with the Lord our God, nor respect of persons, nor desire of gifts.€€ƒx¬In Jerusalem also Josaphat appointed Levites, and priests and chiefs of the families of Israel, to judge the judgment and the cause of the Lord for the inhabitants thereof.€€ƒxlAnd he charged them, saying, Thus shall you do in the fear of the Lord faithfully, and with a perfect heart.€€ƒybEvery cause that shall come to you of your brethren, that dwell in their cities, between kindred and kindred, wheresoever there is question concerning the law, the commandment, the ceremonies, the justifications: shew it them, that they may not sin against the Lord, and that wrath may not come upon you and your brethren: and so doing you shall not sin.€€ƒyXAnd Amarias the priest your high priest shall be chief in the things which regard God: and Zabadias the son of Ismahel, who is ruler in the house of Juda, shall be over those matters which belong to the king's office: and you have before you the Levites for masters, take courage and do diligently, and the Lord will be with you in good things.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAfter this the children of Moab, and the children of Ammon, and with them of the Ammonites, were gathered together to fight against Josaphat.€€ƒx¿And there came messengers, and told Josaphat, saying: There cometh a great multitude against thee from beyond the sea, and out of Syria, and behold they are in Asasonthamar, which is Engaddi.€€ƒxuAnd Josaphat being seized with fear betook himself wholly to pray to the Lord, and he proclaimed a fast for all Juda.€€ƒxxAnd Juda gathered themselves together to pray to the Lord: and all came out of their cities to make supplication to him.€€ƒxvAnd Josaphat stood in the midst of the assembly of Juda, and Jerusalem, in the house of the Lord before the new court,€€˜%ƒxDidst not thou our God kill all the inhabitants of this land before thy people Israel, and gavest it to the seed of Abraham thy friend for ever?€€ƒxFAnd they dwelt in it, and built in it a sanctuary to thy name, saying:€€ƒxçIf evils fall upon us, the sword of judgment, or pestilence, or famine, we will stand in thy presence before this house, in which thy name is called upon: and we will cry to thee in our afflictions, and thou wilt hear, and save us.€€ƒxÒNow therefore behold the children of Ammon, and of Moab, and mount Seir, through whose lands thou didst not allow Israel to pass, when they came out of Egypt, but they turned aside from them, and slew them not,€€ƒx`Do the contrary, and endeavour to cast us out of the possession which thou hast delivered to us.€€ƒx×O our God, wilt thou not then judge them? as for us we have not strength enough, to be able to resist this multitude, which cometh violently upon us. But as we know not what to do, we can only turn our eyes to thee.€€ƒx_And all Juda stood before the Lord with their little ones, and their wives, and their children.€€ƒxÎAnd Jahaziel the son of Zacharias, the son of Banaias, the son of Jehiel, the son of Mathanias, a Levite of the sons of Asaph, was there, upon whom the spirit of the Lord came in the midst of the multitude,€€ƒxÒAnd he said: Attend ye, all Juda, and you that dwell in Jerusalem, and thou king Josaphat: Thus saith the Lord to you: Fear ye not, and be not dismayed at this multitude: for the battle is not yours, but God's.€€ƒx¼To morrow you shall go down against them: for they will come up by the ascent named Sis, and you shall find them at the head of the torrent, which is over against the wilderness of Jeruel.€€ƒx÷It shall not be you that shall fight, but only stand with confidence, and you shall see the help of the Lord over you, O Juda, and Jerusalem: fear ye not, nor be you dismayed: to morrow you shall go out against them, and the Lord will be with you.€€ƒxvThen Josaphat, and Juda, and all the inhabitants of Jerusalem fell flat on the ground before the Lord, and adored him.€€ƒx|And the Levites of the sons of Caath, and of the sons of Core praised the Lord the God of Israel with a loud voice, on high.€€ƒyFAnd they rose early in the morning, and went out through the desert of Thecua: and as they were marching, Josaphat standing in the midst of them, said: Hear me, ye men of Juda, and all the inhabitants of Jerusalem: believe in the Lord your God, and you shall be secure: believe his prophets, and all things shall succeed well.€€ƒxŞAnd he gave counsel to the people, and appointed the singing men of the Lord, to praise him by their companies, and to go before the army, and with one voice to say: Give glory to the Lord, for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxßAnd when they began to sing praises, the Lord turned their ambushments upon themselves, that is to say, of the children of Ammon, and of Moab, and of mount Seir, who were come out to fight against Juda, and they were slain.€€ƒxØFor the children of Ammon, and of Moab, rose up against the inhabitants of mount Seir, to kill and destroy them: and when they had made an end of them, they turned also against one another, and destroyed one another.€€ƒxÃAnd when Juda came to the watch tower, that looketh toward the desert, they saw afar off all the country, for a great space, full of dead bodies, and that no one was left that could escape death.€€ƒyFThen Josaphat came, and all the people with him to take away the spoils of the dead, and they found among the dead bodies, stuff of various kinds, and garments, and most precious vessels: and they took them for themselves, insomuch that they could not carry all, nor in three days take away the spoils, the booty was so great.€€ƒx±And on the fourth day they were assembled in the valley of Blessing: for there they blessed the Lord, and therefore they called that place the valley of Blessing until this day.€€ƒx·And every man of Juda, and the inhabitants of Jerusalem returned, and Josaphat at their head, into Jerusalem with great joy, because the Lord had made them rejoice over their enemies.€€ƒxaAnd they came into Jerusalem with psalteries, and harps, and trumpets into the house of the Lord.€€ƒxˆAnd the fear of the Lord fell upon all the kingdoms of the lands when they heard that the Lord had fought against the enemies of Israel.€€ƒxJAnd the kingdom of Josaphat was quiet, and God gave him peace round about.€€ƒxÍAnd Josaphat reigned over Juda, and he was five and thirty years old, when he began to reign: and he reigned five and twenty years in Jerusalem: and the name of his mother was Azuba the daughter of Selahi.€€ƒxyAnd he walked in the way of his father Asa and departed not from it, doing the things that were pleasing before the Lord.€€ƒx}But yet he took not away the high places, and the people had not yet turned their heart to the Lord the God of their fathers.€€ƒx¢But the rest of the acts of Josaphat, first and last, are written in the words of Jehu the son of Hanani, which he digested into the books of the kings of Israel.€€ƒxtAfter these things Josaphat king of Juda made friendship with Ochozias king of Israel, whose works were very wicked.€€ƒxeAnd he was partner with him in making ships, to go to Tharsis: and they made the ships in Asiongaber.€€ƒxÔAnd Eliezer the son of Dodau of Maresa prophesied to Josaphat, saying: Because thou hast made a league with Ochozias, the Lord hath destroyed thy works, and the ships are broken, and they could not go to Tharsis.€€ƒ`€€ƒx{And Josaphat slept with his fathers, and was buried with them in the city of David: and Joram his son reigned in his stead.€€ƒx§And he had brethren the sons of Josaphat, Azarias, and Jahiel, and Zacharias, and Azaria, and Michael, and Saphatias, all these were the sons of Josaphat king of Juda.€€ƒx¥And their father gave them great gifts of silver, and of gold, and pensions, with strong cities in Juda: but the kingdom he gave to Joram, because he was the eldest.€€ƒx¡So Joram rose up over the kingdom of his father: and when he had established himself, he slew all his brethren with the sword, and some of the princes of Israel.€€ƒxcJoram was two and thirty years old when he began to reign: and he reigned eight years in Jerusalem.€€”ƒx³But the Lord would not destroy the house of David: because of the covenant which he had made with him: and because he had promised to give a lamp to him, and to his sons for ever.€€ƒxTIn those days Edom revolted, from being subject to Juda, and made themselves a king.€€ƒx±And Joram went over with his princes, and all his cavalry with him, and rose in the night, and defeated the Edomites who had surrounded him, and all the captains of his cavalry.€€ƒx½However Edom revolted, from being under the dominion of Juda unto this day: at that time Lobna also revolted, from being under his hand. For he had forsaken the Lord the God of his fathers.€€ƒx‘Moreover he built also high places in the cities of Juda, and he made the inhabitants of Jerusalem to commit fornication, and Juda to transgress.€€ƒxëAnd there was a letter brought him from Eliseus the prophet, in which it was written: Thus saith the Lord the God of David thy father: Because thou hast not walked in the ways of Josaphat thy father nor in the ways of Asa king of Juda,€€ƒyBut hast walked in the ways of the kings of Israel, and hast made Juda and the inhabitants of Jerusalem to commit fornication, imitating the fornication of the house of Achab, moreover also thou hast killed thy brethren, the house of thy father, better men than thyself,€€ƒx‚Behold the Lord will strike thee with a great plague, with all thy people, and thy children, and thy wives, and all thy substance.€€ƒx~And thou shalt be sick of a very grievous disease of thy bowels, till thy vital parts come out by little and little every day.€€ƒxwAnd the Lord stirred up against Joram the spirit of the Philistines, and of the Arabians, who border on the Ethiopians.€€ƒxåAnd they came up into the land of Juda, and wasted it, and they carried away all the substance that was found in the king's house, his sons also, and his wives: so that there was no son left him but Joachaz, who was the youngest.€€ƒxQAnd besides all this the Lord struck him with an incurable disease in his bowels.€€ƒyUAnd as day came after day, and time rolled on, two whole years passed: then after being wasted with a long consumption, so as to void his very bowels, his disease ended with his life. And he died of a most wretched illness, and the people did not make a funeral for him according to the manner of burning, as they had done for his ancestors.€€ƒxÎHe was two and thirty years old when he began his reign, and he reigned eight years in Jerusalem. And he walked not rightly, and they buried him in the city of David: but not in the sepulchres of the kings.€€ƒ`€€ƒxòAnd the inhabitants of Jerusalem made Ochozias his youngest son king in his place: for the rovers of the Arabians, who had broke in upon the camp, had killed all that were his elder brothers. So Ochozias the son of Joram king of Juda reigned.€€ƒx›Ochozias was forty-two years old when he began to reign, and he reigned one year in Jerusalem, and the name of his mother was Athalia the daughter of Amri.€€ƒx^He also walked in the ways of the house of Achab: for his mother pushed him on to do wickedly.€€ƒx”So he did evil in the sight of the Lord, as the house of Achab did: for they were his counsellors after the death of his father, to his destruction.€€ƒx³And he walked after their counsels. And he went with Joram the son of Achab king of Israel, to fight against Hazael king of Syria, at Ramoth Galaad: and the Syrians wounded Joram.€€ŒƒxÊFor it was the will of God against Ochozias that he should come to Joram: and when he was come should go out also against Jehu the son of Namsi, whom the Lord had anointed to destroy the house of Achab.€€ƒxšSo when Jehu was rooting out the house of Achab, he found the princes of Juda, and the sons of the brethren of Ochozias, who served him, and he slew them.€€ƒy(And he sought for Ochozias himself, and took him lying hid in Samaria: and when he was brought to him, he killed him, and they buried him: because he was the son of Josaphat, who had sought the Lord with all his heart. And there was no more hope that any one should reign of the race of Ochozias.€€ƒxuFor Athalia his mother, seeing that her son was dead, rose up, and killed all the royal family of the house of Joram.€€ƒy@But Josabeth the king's daughter took Joas the son of Ochozias, and stole him from among the king's sons that were slain. And she hid him with his nurse in a bedchamber: now Josabeth that hid him, was daughter of king Joram, wife of Joiada the high priest, and sister of Ochozias, and therefore Athalia did not kill him.€€ƒxcAnd he was with them hid in the house of God six years, during which Athalia reigned over the land.€€ƒ`€€ƒyAnd in the seventh year Joiada being encouraged, took the captains of hundreds, to wit, Azarias the son of Jeroham, and Ismahel the son of Johanan, and Azarias the son of Obed, and Maasias the son of Adaias, and Elisaphat the son of Zechri: and made a covenant with them.€€ƒx And they went about Juda, and gathered together the Levites out of all the cities of Juda, and the chiefs of the families of Israel, and they came to Jerusalem.€€ƒx°And all the multitude made a covenant with the king in the house of God: and Joiada said to them: Behold the king's son shall reign, as the Lord hath said of the sons of David.€€ƒx(And this is the thing that you shall do:€€ƒy&A third part of you that come to the sabbath, of the priests, and of the Levites, and of the porters shall be at the gates: and a third part at the king's house: and a third at the gate that is called the Foundation: but let all the rest of the people be in the courts of the house of the Lord.€€•ƒx¼And let the Levites be round about the king, every man with his arms; and if any other come into the temple, let him be slain; and let them be with the king, both coming in, and going out.€€ƒyySo the Levites, and all Juda did according to all that Joiada the high priest had commanded: and they took every one his men that were under him, and that came in by the course of the sabbath, with those who had fulfilled the sabbath, and were to go out. For Joiada the high priest permitted not the companies to depart, which were accustomed to succeed one another every week.€€ƒx“And Joiada the priest gave to the captains the spears, and the shields, and targets of king David, which he had dedicated in the house of the Lord.€€ƒx¯And he set all the people with swords in their hands from the right side of the temple, to the left side of the temple, before the altar, and the temple, round about the king.€€ƒyAnd they brought out the king's son, and put the crown upon him, and the testimony, and gave him the law to hold in his hand, and they made him king: and Joiada the high priest and his sons anointed him: and they prayed for him, and said: God save the king.€€ƒx…Now when Athalia heard the noise of the people running and praising the king, she came in to the people, into the temple of the Lord.€€ƒy9And when she saw the king standing upon the step in the entrance, and the princes, and the companies about him, and all the people of the land rejoicing, and sounding with trumpets, and playing on instruments of divers kinds, and the voice of those that praised, she rent her garments, and said: Treason, treason.€€ƒyAnd Joiada the high priest going out to the captains, and the chiefs of the army, said to them: Take her forth without the precinct of the temple, and when she is without let her be killed with the sword. For the priest commanded that she should not be killed in the house of the Lord.€€ƒxxAnd they laid hold on her by the neck: and when she was come within the horse gate of the palace, they killed her there.€€ƒxxAnd Joiada made a covenant between himself and all the people, and the king, that they should be the people of the lord.€€ƒxªAnd all the people went into the house of Baal, and destroyed it: and they broke down his altars and his idols: and they slew Mathan the priest of Baal before the altars.€€ƒy"And Joiada appointed overseers in the house of the Lord, under the hands of the priests, and the Levites, whom David had distributed in the house of the Lord: to offer holocausts to the Lord, as it is written in the law of Moses, with joy and singing, according to the disposition of David.€€ƒxxHe appointed also porters in the gates of the house of the Lord, that none who was unclean in any thing should enter in.€€ƒyAnd he took the captains of hundreds, and the most valiant men, and the chiefs of the people, and all the people of the land, and they brought down the king from the house of the Lord, and brought him through the upper gate into the king's house, and set him on the royal throne.€€ƒxfAnd all the people of the land rejoiced, and the city was quiet: but Athalia was slain with the sword.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‡Joas was seven years old when he began to reign: and he reigned forty years in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Sebia of Bersabee.€€ƒxPAnd he did that which is good before the Lord all the days of Joiada the priest.€€ƒxEAnd Joiada took for him two wives, by whom he had sons and daughters.€€ƒx;After this Joas had a mind to repair the house of the Lord.€€ƒxêAnd he assembled the priests, and the Levites, and said to them: Go out to the cities of Juda, and gather of all Israel money to repair the temple of your God, from year to year: and do this with speed: but the Levites were negligent.€€˜ƒx¶For that wicked woman Athalia, and her children have destroyed the house of God, and adorned the temple of Baal with all the things that had been dedicated in the temple of the Lord.€€ƒxnAnd the king commanded, and they made a chest: and set it by the gate of the house of the Lord on the outside.€€ƒx­And they made a proclamation in Juda and Jerusalem, that every man should bring to the Lord the money which Moses the servant of God appointed for all Israel, in the desert.€€ƒxAnd all the princes, and all the people rejoiced: and going in they contributed and cast so much into the chest of the Lord, that it was filled.€€ƒyuAnd when it was time to bring the chest before the king by the hands of the Levites, (for they saw there was much money,) the king's scribe, and he whom the high priest had appointed went in: and they poured out the money that was in the chest: and they carried back the chest to its place: and thus they did from day to day, and there was gathered an immense sum of money.€€ƒyAnd the king and Joiada gave it to those who were over the works of the house of the Lord: but they hired with it stonecutters, and artificers of every kind of work to repair the house of the Lord: and such as wrought in iron and brass, to uphold what began to be falling.€€ƒx«And the workmen were diligent, and the breach of the walls was closed up by their hands, and they set up the house of the Lord in its former state, and made it stand firm.€€ƒyDAnd when they had finished all the works, they brought the rest of the money before the king and Joiada: and with it were made vessels for the temple for the ministry, and for holocausts and bowls, and other vessels of gold and silver: and holocausts were offered in the house of the Lord continually all the days of Joiada.€€ƒx^But Joiada grew old and was full of days, and died when he was a hundred and thirty years old.€€ƒxoAnd they buried him in the city of David among the kings, because he had done good to Israel, and to his house.€€ƒxAnd after the death of Joiada, the princes of Juda went in, and worshipped the king: and he was soothed by their services and hearkened to them.€€ƒx“And they forsook the temple of the Lord the God of their fathers, and served groves and idols, and wrath came upon Juda and Jerusalem for this sin.€€ƒxzAnd he sent prophets to them to bring them back to the Lord, and they would not give ear when they testified against them.€€ƒy!The spirit of God then came upon Zacharias the son of Joiada the priest, and he stood in the sight of the people, and said to them: Thus saith the Lord God: Why transgress you the commandment of the Lord which will not be for your good, and have forsaken the Lord, to make him forsake you?€€ƒx‚And they gathered themselves together against him, and stoned him at the king's commandment in the court of the house of the Lord.€€ƒx And king Joas did not remember the kindness that Joiada his father had done to him, but killed his son. And when he died, he said: The Lord see, and require it.€€ƒxËAnd when a year was come about, the army of Syria came up against him: and they came to Juda and Jerusalem, and killed all the princes of the people, and they sent all the spoils to the king of Damascus.€€ƒxàAnd whereas there came a very small number of the Syrians, the Lord delivered into their hands an infinite multitude, because they had forsaken the Lord the God of their fathers: and on Joas they executed shameful judgments.€€ƒyAnd departing they left him in great diseases: and his servants rose up against him, for revenge of the blood of the son of Joiada the priest, and they slew him in his bed, and he died: and they buried him in the city of David, but not in the sepulchres of the kings.€€ƒxNow the men that conspired against him were Zabad the son of Semmaath an Ammonitess, and Jozabad the son of Semarith a Moabitess.€€ƒxĞAnd concerning his sons, and the sum of money which was gathered under him, and the repairing the house of God, they are written more diligently in the book of kings: and Amasias his son reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒx Amasias was five and twenty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned nine and twenty years in Jerusalem, the name of his mother was Joadan of Jerusalem.€€ƒxTAnd he did what was good in the sight of the Lord: but yet not with a perfect heart.€€ƒxuAnd when he saw himself strengthened in his kingdom, he put to death the servants that had slain the king his father.€€ƒxñBut he slew not their children, as it is written in the book of the law of Moses, where the Lord commanded, saying: The fathers shall not be slain for the children, nor the children for their fathers, but every man shall die for his own sin.€€ƒy3Amasias therefore gathered Juda together, and appointed them by families, and captains of thousands and of hundreds in all Juda, and Benjamin: and he numbered them from twenty years old and upwards, and found three hundred thousand young men that could go out to battle, and could hold the spear and shield.€€˜ƒxBut a man of God came to him, and said: O king, let not the army of Israel go out with thee, for the Lord is not with Israel, and all the children of Ephraim:€€ƒx±And if thou think that battles consist in the strength of the army, God will make thee to be overcome by the enemies: for it belongeth to God both to help, and to put to flight.€€ƒxäAnd Amasias said to the man of God: What will then become of the hundred talents which I have given to the soldiers of Israel? and the man of God answered him: The Lord is rich enough to be able to give thee much more than this.€€ƒxœThen Amasias separated the army, that came to him out of Ephraim, to go home again: but they being much enraged against Juda, returned to their own country.€€ƒxAnd Amasias taking courage led forth his people, and went to the vale of saltpits, and slew of the children of Seir ten thousand.€€ƒx«And other ten thousand men the sons of Juda took, and brought to the steep of a certain rock, and cast them down headlong from the top, and they all were broken to pieces.€€ƒxÕBut that army which Amasias had sent back, that they should not go with him to battle, spread themselves among the cities of Juda, from Samaria to Beth-horon, and having killed three thousand took away much spoil.€€ƒx®But Amasias after he had slain the Edomites, set up the gods of the children of Seir, which he had brought thence, to be his gods, and adored them, and burnt incense to them.€€ƒx©Wherefore the Lord being angry against Amasias, sent a prophet to him, to say to him: Why hast thou adored gods that have not delivered their own people out of thy hand?€€ƒyAnd when he spoke these things, he answered him: Art thou the king's counsellor? be quiet, lest I kill thee. And the prophet departing, said: I know that God is minded to kill thee, because thou hast done this evil, and moreover hast not hearkened to my counsel.€€ƒx™Then Amasias king of Juda taking very bad counsel, sent to Joas the son of Joachaz the son of Jehu, king of Israel, saying: Come, let us see one another.€€ƒxïBut he sent back the messengers, saying: The thistle that is in Libanus, sent to the cedar in Libanus, saying: Give thy daughter to my son to wife: and behold the beasts that were in the wood of Libanus passed by and trod down the thistle.€€ƒxÀThou hast said: I have overthrown Edom, and therefore thy heart is lifted up with pride: stay at home, why dost thou provoke evil against thee, that both thou shouldst fall and Juda with thee.€€ƒx”Amasias would not hearken to him, because it was the Lord's will that he should be delivered into the hands of enemies, because of the gods of Edom.€€ƒx‹So Joas king of Israel went up, and they presented themselves to be seen by one another: and Amasias king of Juda was in Bethsames of Juda:€€ƒx>And Juda fell before Israel, and they fled to their dwellings.€€ƒxîAnd Joas king of Israel took Amasias king of Juda, the son of Joas, the son of Joachaz, in Bethsames, and brought him to Jerusalem: and broke down the walls thereof from the gate of Ephraim, to the gate of the corner, four hundred cubits.€€ƒxŞAnd he took all the gold, and silver, and all the vessels, that he found in the house of God, and with Obededom, and in the treasures of the king's house, moreover also the sons of the hostages, he brought back to Samaria.€€ƒxyAnd Amasias the son of Joas king of Juda lived, after the death of Joas the son of Joachaz king of Israel, fifteen years.€€ƒxqNow the rest of the acts of Amasias, the first and last, are written in the book of the kings of Juda and Israel.€€ƒx“And after he revolted from the Lord, they made a conspiracy against him in Jerusalem. And he fled into Lachis, and they sent, and killed him there.€€ƒx\And they brought him back upon horses, and buried him with his fathers in the city of David.€€ƒ`€€ƒx~And all the people of Juda took his son Ozias, who was sixteen years old, and made him king in the room of Amasias his father.€€ƒxeHe built Ailath, and restored it to the dominion of Juda, after that the king slept with his fathers.€€ƒx–Ozias was sixteen years old when he began to reign, and he reigned two and fifty years in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Jechelia of Jerusalem.€€ƒxkAnd he did that which was right in the eyes of the Lord, according to all that Amasias his father had done.€€ƒxAnd he sought the Lord in the days of Zacharias that understood and saw God: and as long as he sought the Lord, he directed him in all things.€€—ƒxwAnd God helped him against the Philistines, and against the Arabians, that dwelt in Gurbaal, and against the Ammonites.€€ƒxAnd the Ammonites gave gifts to Ozias: and his name was spread abroad even to the entrance of Egypt for his frequent victories.€€ƒx¡And Ozias built towers in Jerusalem over the gate of the corner, and over the gate of the valley, and the rest, in the same side of the wall, and fortified them.€€ƒxüAnd he built towers in the wilderness, and dug many cisterns, for he had much cattle both in the plains, and in the waste of the desert: he had also vineyards and dressers of vines in the mountains, and in Carmel: for he was a man that loved husbandry.€€ƒx¼And the army of his fighting men, that went out to war, was under the hand of Jehiel the scribe, and Maasias the doctor, and under the hand of Henanias, who was one of the king's captains.€€ƒx`And the whole number of the chiefs by the families of valiant men were two thousand six hundred.€€ƒxAnd the whole army under them three hundred and seven thousand five hundred: who were fit for war, and fought for the king against the enemy.€€ƒx“And Ozias prepared for them, that is, for the whole army, shields, and spears, and helmets, and coats of mail, and bows, and slings to cast stones.€€ƒxìAnd he made in Jerusalem engines of diverse kinds, which he placed in the towers, and in the corners of the walls, to shoot arrows, and great stones: and his name went forth far abroad, for the Lord helped him, and had strengthened him.€€ƒxËBut when he was made strong, his heart was lifted up to his destruction, and he neglected the Lord his God: and going into the temple of the Lord, he had a mind to burn incense upon the altar of incense.€€ƒxtAnd immediately Azarias the priest going in after him, and with him fourscore priests of the Lord, most valiant men,€€ƒy#Withstood the king and said: It doth not belong to thee, Ozias, to burn incense to the Lord, but to the priests, that is, to the sons of Aaron, who are consecrated for this ministry: go out of the sanctuary, do not despise: for this thing shall not be accounted to thy glory by the Lord God.€€ƒxÙAnd Ozias was angry, and holding in his hand the censer to burn incense, threatened the priests. And presently there rose a leprosy in his forehead before the priests, in the house of the Lord at the altar of incense.€€ƒyAnd Azarias the high priest, and all the rest of the priests looked upon him, and saw the leprosy in his forehead, and they made haste to thrust him out. Yea himself also being frightened, hasted to go out, because he had quickly felt the stroke of the Lord.€€ƒxıAnd Ozias the king was a leper unto the day of his death, and he dwelt in a house apart being full of the leprosy, for which he had been cast out of the house of the Lord. And Joatham his son governed the king's house, and judged the people of the land.€€ƒxeBut the rest of the acts of Ozias first and last were written by Isaias the son of Amos, the prophet.€€ƒxAnd Ozias slept with his fathers, and they buried him in the field of the royal sepulchres, because he was a leper: and Joatham his son reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¡Joatham was five and twenty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned sixteen years in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Jerusa the daughter of Sadoc.€€ƒxºAnd he did that which was right before the Lord, according to all that Ozias his father had done, only that he entered not into the temple of the Lord, and the people still transgressed.€€ƒxXHe built the high gate of the house of the Lord, and on the wall of Ophel he built much.€€ƒxYMoreover he built cities in the mountains of Juda, and castles and towers in the forests.€€ƒy!He fought against the king of the children of Ammon, and overcame them, and the children of Ammon gave him at that time a hundred talents of silver, and ten thousand measures of wheat, and as many measures of barley: so much did the children of Ammon give him in the second and third year.€€‰ƒx~Now the rest of the acts of Joatham, and all his wars, and his works, are written in the book of the kings of Israel and Juda.€€ƒxcHe was five and twenty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned sixteen years in Jerusalem.€€ƒxuAnd Joatham slept with his fathers, and they buried him in the city of David: and Achaz his son reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒx´Achaz was twenty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned sixteen years in Jerusalem: he did not that which was right in the sight of the Lord as David his father had done,€€ƒxXBut walked in the ways of the kings of Israel; moreover also he cast statues for Baalim.€€ƒxÁIt was he that burnt incense in the valley of Benennom, and consecrated his sons in the fire according to the manner of the nations, which the Lord slew at the coming of the children of Israel.€€ƒxgHe sacrificed also, and burnt incense in the high places, and on the hills, and under every green tree.€€ƒyAnd the Lord his God delivered him into the hands of the king of Syria, who defeated him, and took a great booty out of his kingdom, and carried it to Damascus: he was also delivered into the hands of the king of Israel, who overthrew him with a great slaughter.€€˜ƒx›At the same time Zechri a powerful man of Ephraim, slew Maasias the king's son, and Ezricam the governor of his house, and Elcana who was next to the king.€€ƒxœAnd the children of Israel carried away of their brethren two hundred thousand women, boys, and girls, and an immense booty: and they brought it to Samaria.€€ƒyJAt that time there was a prophet of the Lord there, whose name was Oded: and he went out to meet the army that came to Samaria, and said to them: Behold the Lord the God of your fathers being angry with Juda, hath delivered them into your hands, and you have butchered them cruelly, so that your cruelty hath reached up to heaven.€€ƒx¼Moreover you have a mind to keep under the children of Juda and Jerusalem for your bondmen and bondwomen, which ought not to be done: for you have sinned in this against the Lord your God.€€ƒx’But hear ye my counsel, and release the captives that you have brought of your brethren, because a great indignation of the Lord hangeth over you.€€ƒxÖThen some of the chief men of the sons of Ephraim, Azarias the son of Johanan, Barachias the son of Mosollamoth, Ezechias the son of Sellum, and Amasa the son of Adali, stood up against them that came from the war.€€ƒxğAnd they said to them: You shall not bring in the captives hither, lest we sin against the Lord. Why will you add to our sins, and heap up upon our former offences? for the sin is great, and the fierce anger of the Lord hangeth over Israel.€€ƒxgSo the soldiers left the spoils, and all that they had taken, before the princes and all the multitude.€€ƒy½And the men, whom we mentioned above, rose up and took the captives, and with the spoils clothed all them that were naked: and when they had clothed and shod them, and refreshed them with meat and drink, and anointed them because of their labour, and had taken care of them, they set such of them as could not walk, and were feeble, upon beasts, and brought them to Jericho the city of palm trees to their brethren, and they returned to Samaria.€€ƒxFAt that time king Achaz sent to the king of the Assyrians asking help.€€ƒxDAnd the Edomites came and slew many of Juda, and took a great booty.€€ƒxãThe Philistines also spread themselves among the cities of the plains, and to the south of Juda: and they took Bethsames, and Aialon, and Gaderoth, and Socho, and Thamnan, and Gamzo, with their villages, and they dwelt in them.€€ƒx|For the Lord had humbled Juda because of Achaz the king of Juda, for he had stripped it of help, and had contemned the Lord.€€ƒx…And he brought against him Thelgathphalnasar king of the Assyrians, who also afflicted him, and plundered him without any resistance.€€ƒx¦And Achaz stripped the house of the Lord, and the house of the kings, and of the princes, and gave gifts to the king of the Assyrians, and yet it availed him nothing.€€ƒxpMoreover also in the time of his distress he increased contempt against the Lord: king Achaz himself by himself,€€ƒxõSacrificed victims to the gods of Damascus that struck him, and he said: The gods of the kings of Syria help them, and I will appease them with victims, and they will help me; whereas on the contrary they were the ruin of him, and of all Israel.€€ƒx³Then Achaz having taken away all the vessels of the house of God, and broken them, shut up the doors of the temple of God, and made himself altars in all the corners of Jerusalem.€€ƒx}And in all the cities of Juda he built altars to burn frankincense, and he provoked the Lord the God of his fathers to wrath.€€ƒxsBut the rest of his acts, and all his works first and last are written in the book of the kings of Juda and Israel.€€ƒxÀAnd Achaz slept with his fathers, and they buried him in the city of Jerusalem: for they received him not into the sepulchres of the kings of Israel. And Ezechias his son reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒx²Now Ezechias began to reign, when he was five and twenty years old, and he reigned nine and twenty years in Jerusalem: the name of his mother was Abia, the daughter of Zacharias.€€ƒxmAnd he did that which was pleasing in the sight of the Lord, according to all that David his father had done.€€ƒxiIn the first year and month of his reign he opened the doors of the house of the Lord, and repaired them.€€ƒxRAnd he brought the priests and the Levites, and assembled them in the east street.€€ƒx And he said to them: Hear me, ye Levites, and be sanctified, purify the house of the Lord the God of your fathers, and take away all filth out of the sanctuary.€€˜$ƒx¤They have shut up the doors that were in the porch, and put out the lamps, and have not burnt incense, nor offered holocausts in the sanctuary of the God of Israel.€€ƒx»Therefore the wrath of the Lord hath been stirred up against Juda and Jerusalem, and he hath delivered them to trouble, and to destruction, and to be hissed at, as you see with your eyes.€€ƒx~Behold, our fathers are fallen by the sword, our sons, and our daughters, and wives are led away captives for this wickedness.€€ƒxNow therefore I have a mind that we make a covenant with the Lord the God of Israel, and he will turn away the wrath of his indignation from us.€€ƒxMy sons, be not negligent: the Lord hath chosen you to stand before him, and to minister to him, and to worship him, and to burn incense to him.€€ƒyThen the Levites arose, Mahath the son of Amasai, and Joel the son of Azarias, of the sons of Caath: and of the sons of Merari, Cis the son of Abdi, and Azarias the son of Jalaleel. And of the sons of Gerson, Joah the son of Zemma, and Eden the son of Joah.€€ƒxeAnd of the sons of Elisaphan, Samri, and Jahiel. Also of the sons of Asaph, Zacharias, and Mathanias.€€ƒx\And of the sons of Heman, Jahiel, and Semei: and of the sons of Idithun, Semeias, and Oziel.€€ƒx´And they gathered together their brethren, and sanctified themselves, and went in according to the commandment of the king, and the precept of the Lord, to purify the house of God.€€ƒxôAnd the priests went into the temple of the Lord to sanctify it, and brought out all the uncleanness that they found within to the entrance of the house of the Lord, and the Levites took it away, and carried it out abroad to the torrent Cedron.€€ƒyAnd they began to cleanse on the first day of the first month, and on the eighth day of the same month they came into the porch of the temple of the Lord, and they purified the temple in eight days, and on the sixteenth day of the same month they finished what they had begun.€€ƒxÉAnd they went in to king Ezechias, and said to him: We have sanctified all the house of the Lord, and the altar of holocaust, and the vessels thereof, and the table of proposition with all its vessels,€€ƒx¨And all the furniture of the temple, which king Achaz in his reign had defiled, after his transgression; and behold they are all set forth before the altar of the Lord.€€ƒxmAnd king Ezechias rising early, assembled all the rulers of the city, and went up into the house of the Lord:€€ƒxíAnd they offered together seven bullocks, and seven rams, and seven lambs, and seven he goats for sin, for the kingdom, for the sanctuary, for Juda: and he spoke to the priests the sons of Aaron, to offer them upon the altar of the Lord.€€ƒxíTherefore they killed the bullocks, and the priests took the blood, and poured it upon the altar; they killed also the rams, and their blood they poured also upon the altar, and they killed the lambs, and poured the blood upon the altar.€€ƒxsAnd they brought the he goats for sin before the king, and the whole multitude, and they laid their hand upon them:€€ƒxÌAnd the priests immolated them, and sprinkled their blood before the altar for an expiation of all Israel: for the king had commanded that the holocaust and the sin offering should be made for all Israel.€€ƒxúAnd he set the Levites in the house of the Lord with cymbals, and psalteries, and harps according to the regulation of David the king, and of Gad the seer, and of Nathan the prophet: for it was the commandment of the Lord by the hand of his prophets.€€ƒxTAnd the Levites stood, with the instruments of David, and the priests with trumpets.€€ƒxöAnd Ezechias commanded that they should offer holocausts upon the altar: and when the holocausts were offered, they began to sing praises to the Lord, and to sound with trumpets, and divers instruments which David the king of Israel had prepared.€€ƒxxAnd all the multitude adored, and the singers, and the trumpeters, were in their office till the holocaust was finished.€€ƒx\And when the oblation was ended, the king, and all that were with him bowed down and adored.€€ƒx³And Ezechias and the princes commanded the Levites to praise the Lord with the words of David, and Asaph the seer: and they praised him with great joy, and bowing the knee adored.€€ƒxÚAnd Ezechias added, and said: You have filled your hands to the Lord, come and offer victims, and praises in the house of the Lord. And all the multitude offered victims, and praises, and holocausts with a devout mind.€€ƒxzAnd the number of the holocausts which the multitude offered, was seventy bullocks, a hundred rams, and two hundred lambs.€€ƒxLAnd they consecrated to the Lord six hundred oxen, and three thousand sheep.€€ƒxòBut the priests were few, and were not enough to flay the holocausts: wherefore the Levites their brethren helped them, till the work was ended, and priests were sanctified, for the Levites are sanctified with an easier rite than the priests.€€ƒx—So there were many holocausts, and the fat of peace offerings, and the libations of holocausts: and the service of the house of the Lord was completed.€€ƒx—And Ezechias, and all the people rejoiced because the ministry of the Lord was accomplished. For the resolution of doing this thing was taken suddenly.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÆAnd Ezechias sent to all Israel and Juda: and he wrote letters to Ephraim and Manasses, that they should come to the house of the Lord in Jerusalem, and keep the phase to the Lord the God of Israel,€€ƒx}For the king, taking counsel, and the princes, and all the assembly of Jerusalem, decreed to keep the phase the second month.€€ƒx—For they could not keep it in its time; because there were not priests enough sanctified, and the people was not as yet gathered together to Jerusalem.€€ƒx3And the thing pleased the king, and all the people.€€ƒxÛAnd they decreed to send messengers to all Israel from Bersabee even to Dan, that they should come, and keep the phase to the Lord the God of Israel in Jerusalem: for many had not kept it as it is prescribed by the law.€€˜ƒx’Be not like your fathers, and brethren, who departed from the Lord the God of their fathers, and he hath given them up to destruction, as you see.€€ƒxîHarden not your necks, as your fathers did: yield yourselves to the Lord, and come to his sanctuary, which he hath sanctified forever: serve the Lord the God of your fathers, and the wrath of his indignation shall be turned away from you.€€ƒyFor if you turn again to the Lord, your brethren, and children shall find mercy before their masters, that have led them away captive, and they shall return into this land: for the Lord your God is merciful, and will not turn away his face from you, if you return to him.€€ƒx™So the posts went speedily from city to city, through the land of Ephraim, and of Manasses, even to Zabulon, whilst they laughed at them and mocked them.€€ƒxkNevertheless some men of Aser, and of Manasses, and of Zabulon, yielding to the counsel, came to Jerusalem.€€ƒx‘But the hand of God was in Juda, to give them one heart to do the word of the Lord, according to the commandment of the king, and of the princes.€€ƒxsAnd much people were assembled to Jerusalem to celebrate the solemnity of the unleavened bread in the second month:€€ƒx£And they arose and destroyed the altars that were in Jerusalem, and took away all things in which incense was burnt to idols and cast them into the torrent Cedron.€€ƒx¯And they immolated the phase on the fourteenth day of the second month. And the priests and the Levites being at length sanctified offered holocausts in the house of the Lord.€€ƒx¹And they stood in their order according to the disposition and law of Moses the man of God: but the priests received the blood which was to be poured out, from the hands of the Levites,€€ƒx•Because a great number was not sanctified: and therefore the Levites immolated the phase for them that came not in time to be sanctified to the Lord.€€ƒxëFor a great part of the people from Ephraim, and Manasses, and Issachar, and Zabulon, that had not been sanctified, ate the phase otherwise than it is written: and Ezechias prayed for them, saying: The Lord who is good will shew mercy,€€ƒxTo all them, who with their whole heart, seek the Lord the God of their fathers: and will not impute it to them that they are not sanctified.€€ƒx7And the Lord heard him, and was merciful to the people.€€ƒxåAnd the children of Israel, that were found at Jerusalem, kept the feast of unleavened bread seven days with great joy, praising the Lord every day. the Levites also, and the priests, with instruments that agreed to their office.€€ƒxğAnd Ezechias spoke to the heart of all the Levites, that had good understanding concerning the Lord: and they ate during the seven days of the solemnity, immolating victims of peace offerings, and praising the Lord the God of their fathers.€€ƒx[And it pleased the whole multitude to keep other seven days: which they did with great joy.€€ƒxçFor Ezechias the king of Juda had given to the multitude a thousand bullocks, and seven thousand sheep: and the princes had given the people a thousand bullocks, and ten thousand sheep: and a great number of priests was sanctified.€€ƒx½And all the multitude of Juda with the priests and Levites, and all the assembly, that came out of Israel; and the proselytes of the land of Israel, and that dwelt in Juda were full of joy.€€ƒxŠAnd there was a great solemnity in Jerusalem, such as had not been in that city since the time of Solomon the son of David king of Israel.€€ƒx–And the priests and the Levites rose up and blessed the people: and their voice was heard: and their prayer came to the holy dwelling place of heaven.€€ƒ`€€ƒy‘And when these things had been duly celebrated, all Israel that were found in the cities of Juda, went out, and they broke the idols, and cut down the groves, demolished the high places, and destroyed the altars, not only out of all Juda and Benjamin, but out of Ephraim also and Manasses, till they had utterly destroyed them: then all the children of Israel returned to their possessions and cities.€€ƒyAnd Ezechias appointed companies of the priests, and the Levites, by their courses, every man in his own office, to wit, both of the priests, and of the Levites, for holocausts, and for peace offerings, to minister, and to praise, and to sing in the gates of the camp of the Lord.€€ƒxÙAnd the king's part was, that of his proper substance the holocaust should be offered always morning and evening, and on the sabbaths, and the new moons and the other solemnities, as it is written in the law of Moses.€€ƒx›He commanded also the people that dwelt in Jerusalem, to give to the priests, and the Levites their portion, that they might attend to the law of the Lord.€€ƒxŞWhich when it was noised abroad in the ears of the people, the children of Israel offered in abundance the firstfruits of corn, wine, and oil, and honey: and brought the tithe of all things which the ground bringeth forth.€€•ƒxpIn the third month they began to lay the foundations of the heaps, and in the seventh month, they finished them.€€ƒxrAnd when Ezechias and his princes came in, they saw the heaps, and they blessed the Lord and the people of Israel.€€ƒxEAnd Ezechias asked the priests and the Levites, why the heaps lay so.€€ƒy-Azarias the chief priest of the race of Sadoc answered him, saying: Since the firstfruits began to be offered in the house of the Lord, we have eaten, and have been filled, and abundance is left, because the Lord hath blessed his people: and of that which is left is this great store which thou seest.€€ƒxcThen Ezechias commanded to prepare storehouses in the house of the Lord. And when they had done so,€€ƒx±They brought in faithfully both the firstfruits, and the tithes, and all they had vowed. And the overseer of them was Chonenias the Levite, and Semei his brother was the second,€€ƒy@And after him Jehiel, and Azarias, and Nahath, and Asael, and Jerimoth, and Jozabad, and Eliel, and Jesmachias, and Mahath, and Banaias, overseers under the hand of Chonenias, and Semei his brother, by the commandment of Ezechias the king, and Azarias the high priest of the house of God, to whom all things appertained.€€ƒxÌBut Core the son of Jemna the Levite, the porter of the east gate, was overseer of the things which were freely offered to the Lord, and of the firstfruits and the things dedicated for the holy of holies.€€ƒxÇAnd under his charge were Eden, and Benjamin, Jesue, and Semeias, and Amarias, and Sechenias, in the cities of the priests, to distribute faithfully portions to their brethren, both little and great:€€ƒxÌBesides the males from three years old and upward, to all that went into the temple of the Lord, and whatsoever there was need of in the ministry, and their offices according to their courses, day by day.€€ƒxxTo the priests by their families, and to the Levites from the twentieth year and upward, by their classes and companies.€€ƒxAnd to all the multitude, both to their wives, and to their children of both sexes, victuals were given faithfully out of the things that had been sanctified.€€ƒx·Also of the sons of Aaron who were in the fields and in the suburbs of each city, there were men appointed, to distribute portions to all the males, among the priests and the Levites.€€ƒxŠSo Ezechias did all things which we have said in all Juda, and wrought that which was good, and right, and truth, before the Lord his God,€€ƒxIn all the service of the house of the Lord according to the law and the ceremonies, desiring to seek his God with all his heart, and he did it and prospered.€€ƒ`€€ƒx˜After these things, and this truth, Sennacherib king of the Assyrians came and entered into Juda, and besieged the fenced cities, desiring to take them.€€ƒxsAnd when Ezechias saw that Sennacherib was come, and that the whole force of the war was turning against Jerusalem,€€ƒxŸHe took counsel with the princes, and the most valiant men, to stop up the heads of the springs, that were without the city: and as they were all of this mind,€€ƒxÚHe gathered together a very great multitude, and they stopped up all the springs, and the brook, that ran through the midst of the land, saying: Lest the kings of the Assyrians should come, and find abundance of water.€€ƒxÕHe built up also with great diligence all the wall that had been broken down, and built towers upon it, and another wall without: and he repaired Mello in the city of David, and made all sorts of arms and shields:€€˜!ƒx·Behave like men, and take courage: be not afraid nor dismayed for the king of the Assyrians, nor for all the multitude that is with him: for there are many more with us than with him.€€ƒx¬For with him is an arm of flesh: with us the Lord our God, who is our helper, and fighteth for us. And the people were encouraged with these words of Ezechias king of Juda.€€ƒxÍAfter this, Sennacherib king of the Assyrians sent his servants to Jerusalem, (for he with all his army was besieging Lachis,) to Ezechias king of Juda, and to all the people that were in the city, saying:€€ƒxmThus saith Sennacherib king of the Assyrians: In whom do you trust, that you sit still besieged in Jerusalem?€€ƒx©Doth not Ezechias deceive you, to give you up to die by hunger and thirst, affirming that the Lord your God shall deliver you from the hand of the king of the Assyrians?€€ƒxÄIs it not this same Ezechias, that hath destroyed his high places, and his altars, and commanded Juda and Jerusalem, saying: You shall worship before one altar, and upon it you shall burn incense?€€ƒx¦Know you not what I and my fathers have done to all the people of the lands? have the gods of any nations and lands been able to deliver their country out of my hand?€€ƒx¼Who is there among all the gods of the nations, which my fathers have destroyed, that could deliver his people out of my hand, that your God should be able to deliver you out of this hand?€€ƒy,Therefore let not Ezechias deceive you, nor delude you with a vain persuasion, and do not believe him. For if no god of all the nations and kingdoms, could deliver his people out of my hand, and out of the hand of my fathers, consequently neither shall your God be able to deliver you out of my hand.€€ƒxdAnd many other things did his servants speak against the Lord God, and against Ezechias his servant.€€ƒxùHe wrote also letters full of blasphemy against the Lord the God of Israel, and he spoke against him: As the gods of other nations could not deliver their people out of my hand, so neither can the God of Ezechias deliver his people out of this hand.€€ƒxŸMoreover he cried out with a loud voice, in the Jews' tongue, to the people that sat on the walls of Jerusalem, that he might frighten them, and take the city.€€ƒxyAnd he spoke against the God of Jerusalem, as against the gods of the people of the earth, the works of the hands of men.€€ƒxvAnd Ezechias the king, and Isaias the prophet the son of Amos, prayed against this blasphemy, and cried out to heaven.€€ƒy)And the Lord sent an angel, who cut off all the stout men and the warriors, and the captains of the army of the king of the Assyrians: and he returned with disgrace into his own country. And when he was come into the house of his god, his sons that came out of his bowels, slew him with the sword.€€ƒx¶And the Lord saved Ezechias and the inhabitants of Jerusalem, out of the hand of Sennacherib king of the Assyrians, and out of the hand of all, and gave them treasures on every side.€€ƒx¨Many also brought victims, and sacrifices to the Lord to Jerusalem, and presents to Ezechias king of Juda: and he was magnified thenceforth in the sight of all nations.€€ƒxpIn those days Ezechias was sick even to death, and he prayed to the Lord: and he heard him, and gave him a sign.€€ƒx®But he did not render again according to the benefits which he had received, for his heart was lifted up: and wrath was enkindled against him, and against Juda and Jerusalem.€€ƒxÂAnd he humbled himself afterwards, because his heart had been lifted up, both he and the inhabitants of Jerusalem: and therefore the wrath of the Lord came not upon them in the days of Ezechias.€€ƒxÅAnd Ezechias was rich, and very glorious, and he gathered himself great treasures of silver and of gold, and of precious stones, of spices, and of arms, of all kinds, and of vessels of great price.€€ƒx_Storehouses also of corn, of wine, and of oil, and stalls for all beasts, and folds for cattle.€€ƒx‚And he built himself cities: for he had flocks of sheep, and herds without number, for the Lord had given him very much substance.€€ƒxÊThis same Ezechias was, he that stopped the upper source of the waters of Gihon, and turned them away underneath toward the west of the city of David: in all his works he did prosperously what he would.€€ƒxåBut yet in the embassy of the princes of Babylon, that were sent to him, to inquire of the wonder that had happened upon the earth, God left him that he might be tempted, and all things might be made known that were in his heart.€€ƒxqNow the rest of the acts of Ezechias, and of his mercies are written in the book of the kings of Juda and Israel.€€ƒxÙAnd Ezechias slept with his fathers, and they buried him above the sepulchres of the sons of David: and all Juda, and all the inhabitants of Jerusalem celebrated his funeral: and Manasses his son reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒxcManasses was twelve years old when he began to reign, and he reigned fifty-five years in Jerusalem.€€ƒx‰And he did evil before the Lord, according to all the abominations of the nations, which the Lord cast out before the children of Israel:€€ƒxÂAnd he turned, and built again the high places which Ezechias his father had destroyed: and he built altars to Baalim, and made groves, and he adored all the host of heaven, and worshipped them.€€ƒxqHe built also altars in the house of the Lord, whereof the Lord had said: In Jerusalem shall my name be for ever.€€ƒxXAnd he built them for all the host of heaven in the two courts of the house of the Lord.€€˜ƒxéHe set also a graven, and a molten statue in the house of God, of which God had said to David, and to Solomon his son: In this house, and in Jerusalem, which I have chosen out of all the tribes of Israel, will I put my name for ever.€€ƒxöAnd I will not make the foot of Israel to be removed out of the land which I have delivered to their fathers: yet so if they will take heed to do what I have commanded them, and all the law, and the ceremonies, and judgments by the hand of Moses.€€ƒx¦So Manasses seduced Juda, and the inhabitants of Jerusalem, to do evil beyond all the nations, which the Lord had destroyed before the face of the children of Israel.€€ƒx=And the Lord spoke to his people, and they would not hearken.€€ƒx¦Therefore he brought upon them the captains of he army of the king of the Assyrians: and they took Manasses, and carried him bound with chains and fetters to Babylon.€€ƒx{And after that he was in distress he prayed to the Lord his God: and did penance exceedingly before the God of his fathers.€€ƒx¨And he entreated him, and besought him earnestly: and he heard his prayer, and brought him again to Jerusalem into his kingdom, and Manasses knew that the Lord was God.€€ƒyAfter this he built a wall without the city of David, on the west side of Gihon in the valley, from the entering in of the gate round about to Ophel, and raised it up to a great height: and he appointed captains of the army in all the fenced cities of Juda:€€ƒxÎAnd he took away the strange gods, and the idol out of the house of the Lord: the altars also which he had made in the mount of the house of the Lord, and in Jerusalem, and he cast them all out of the city.€€ƒx¢And he repaired the altar of the Lord, and sacrificed upon it victims, and peace offerings, and praise: and he commanded Juda to serve the Lord the God of Israel.€€ƒxRNevertheless the people still sacrificed in the high places to the Lord their God.€€ƒxËBut the rest of the acts of Manasses, and his prayer to his God, and the words of the seers that spoke to him in the name of the Lord the God of Israel, are contained in the words of the kings of Israel.€€ƒxÆHis prayer also, and his being heard and all his sins, and contempt, and places wherein he built high places, and set up groves, and statues before he did penance, are written in the words of Hozai.€€ƒxmAnd Manasses slept with his fathers, and they buried him in his house: and his son Amon reigned in his stead.€€ƒx`Amon was two and twenty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned two years in Jerusalem.€€ƒxAnd he did evil in the sight of the Lord, as Manasses his father had done: he sacrificed to all the idols which Manasses his father had made, and served them.€€ƒxyAnd he did not humble himself before the lord, as Manasses his father had humbled himself, but committed far greater sin.€€ƒxFAnd his servants conspired against him, and slew him in his own house.€€ƒxvBut the rest of the multitude of the people slew them that had killed Amon, and made Josias his son king in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒxdJosias was eight years old when he began to reign, and he reigned one and thirty years in Jerusalem.€€ƒx¢And he did that which was right in the sight of the Lord, and walked in the ways of David his father: he declined not, neither to the right hand, nor to the left.€€ƒyAnd in the eighth year of his reign, when he was yet a boy, he began to seek the God of his father David: and in the twelfth year after he began to reign, he cleansed Juda and Jerusalem from the high places, and the groves, and the idols, and the graven things.€€ƒyAnd they broke down before him the altars of Baalim, and demolished the idols that had been set upon them: and he cut down the groves and the graven things, and broke them in pieces: and strewed the fragments upon the graves of them that had sacrificed to them.€€ƒxeAnd he burnt the bones of the priests on the altars of the idols, and he cleansed Juda and Jerusalem.€€˜!ƒx½And when he had destroyed the altars, and the groves, and had broken the idols in pieces, and had demolished all profane temples throughout all the land of Israel, he returned to Jerusalem.€€ƒxüNow in the eighteenth year of his reign, when he had cleansed the land, and the temple of the Lord, he sent Saphan the son of Elselias, and Maasias the governor of the city, Joha the son of Joachaz the recorder, to repair the house of the Lord his God.€€ƒy0And they came to Helcias the high priest: and received of him the money which had been brought into the house of the Lord, and which the Levites and porters had gathered together from Manasses, and Ephraim, and all the remnant of Israel, and from all Juda, and Benjamin, and the inhabitants of Jerusalem,€€ƒx’Which they delivered into the hands of them that were over the workmen in the house of the Lord, to repair the temple, and mend all that was weak.€€ƒxÈBut they gave it to the artificers, and to the masons, to buy stones out of the quarries, and timber for the couplings of the building, and to rafter the houses, which the kings of Juda had destroyed.€€ƒxÛAnd they did all faithfully. Now the overseers of the workmen were Jahath and Abdias of the sons of Merari, Zacharias and Mosollam of the sons of Caath, who hastened the work: all Levites skilful to play on instruments.€€ƒxxBut over them that carried burdens for divers uses, were scribes, and masters of the number of the Levites, and porters.€€ƒx¦Now when they carried out the money that had been brought into the temple of the Lord, Helcias the priest found the book of the law of the Lord, by the hand of Moses.€€ƒxxAnd he said to Saphan the scribe: I have found the book of the law in the house of the Lord: and he delivered it to him.€€ƒx}But he carried the book to the king, and told him, saying: Lo, all that thou hast committed to thy servants, is accomplished.€€ƒx©They have gathered together the silver that was found in the house of the Lord: and it is given to the overseers of the artificers, and of the workmen, for divers works.€€ƒxNMoreover Helcias the priest gave me this book. And he read it before the king.€€ƒxAAnd when he had heard the words of the law, he rent his garments:€€ƒxAnd he commanded Helcias, and Ahicam the son of Saphan, and Abdon the son of Micha, and Saphan the scribe, and Asaa the king's servant, saying:€€ƒyGo, and pray to the Lord for me, and for the remnant of Israel, and Juda, concerning all the words of this book, which is found: for the great wrath of the Lord hath fallen upon us, because our fathers have not kept the words of the Lord, to do all things that are written in this book.€€ƒyAnd Helcias and they that were sent with him by the king, went to Olda the prophetess, the wife of Sellum the son of Thecuath, the son of Hasra keeper of the wardrobe: who dwelt in Jerusalem in the Second part: and they spoke to her the words above mentioned.€€ƒx_And she answered them: Thus saith the Lord the God of Israel: Tell the man that sent you to me:€€ƒx»Thus saith the Lord: Behold I will bring evils upon this place, and upon the inhabitants thereof, and all the curses that are written in this book which they read before the king of Juda.€€ƒxÇBecause they have forsaken me, and have sacrificed to strange gods, to provoke me to wrath with all the works of their hands, therefore my wrath shall fail upon this place, and shall not be quenched.€€ƒx¯But as to the king of Juda that sent you to beseech the Lord, thus shall you say to him: Thus saith the Lord the God of Israel: Because thou hast heard the words of this book,€€ƒyAnd thy heart was softened, and thou hast humbled thyself in the sight of God for the things that are spoken against this place, and the inhabitants of Jerusalem, and reverencing my face, hast rent thy garments, and wept before me: I also have heard thee, saith the Lord.€€ƒxıFor now I will gather thee to thy fathers, and thou shalt be brought to thy tomb in peace: and thy eyes shall not see all the evil that I will bring upon this place, and the inhabitants thereof. They therefore reported to the king all that she had said.€€ƒx>And he called together all the ancients of Juda and Jerusalem.€€ƒy	And went up to the house of the Lord, and all the men of Juda, and the inhabitants of Jerusalem, the priests and the Levites, and all the people from the least to the greatest. And the king read in their hearing, in the house of the Lord, all the words of the book.€€ƒyAnd standing up in his tribunal, he made a covenant before the Lord to walk after him, and keep his commandments, and testimonies, and justifications with all his heart, and with all his soul, and to do the things that were written in that book which he had read.€€ƒx±And he adjured all that were found in Jerusalem and Benjamin to do the same: and the inhabitants of Jerusalem did according to the covenant of the Lord the God of their fathers.€€ƒxîAnd Josias took away all the abominations out of all the countries of the children of Israel and made all that were left in Israel, to serve the Lord their God. As long as he lived they departed not from the Lord the God of their fathers.€€ƒ`€€ƒxqAnd Josias kept a phase to the Lord in Jerusalem, and it was sacrificed on the fourteenth day of the first month.€€ƒx`And he set the priests in their offices, and exhorted them to minister in the house of the Lord.€€ƒy"And he spoke to the Levites, by whose instruction all Israel was sanctified to the Lord, saying: Put the ark in the sanctuary of the temple, which Solomon the son of David king of Israel built: for you shall carry it no more: but minister now to the Lord your God, and to his people Israel.€€ƒx“And prepare yourselves by your houses, and families according to your courses, as David king of Israel commanded, and Solomon his son hath written.€€ƒxDAnd serve ye in the sanctuary by the families and companies of Levi.€€˜ƒxéAnd Josias gave to all the people that were found there in the solemnity of the phase, of lambs and of kids of the flocks, and of other small cattle thirty thousand, and of oxen three thousand, all these were of the king's substance.€€ƒyAnd his princes willingly offered what they had vowed, both to the people and to the priests and the Levites. Moreover Helcias, and Zacharias, and Jahiel rulers of the house of the Lord, gave to the priests to keep the phase two thousand six hundred small cattle, and three hundred oxen.€€ƒxßAnd Chonenias, and Semeias and Nathanael, his brethren, and Hasabias, and Jehiel, and Jozabad princes of the Levites, gave to the rest of the Levites to celebrate the phase five thousand small cattle, and five hundred oxen.€€ƒxAnd the ministry was prepared, and the priests stood in their office: the Levites also in their companies, according to the king's commandment.€€ƒxxAnd the phase was immolated: and the priests sprinkled the blood with their hand, and the Levites flayed the holocausts:€€ƒx¿And they separated them, to give them by the houses and families of every one, and to be offered to the Lord, as it is written in the book of Moses, and with the oxen they did in like manner.€€ƒxàAnd they roasted the phase with fire, according to that which is written in the law: but the victims of peace offerings they boiled in caldrons, and kettles, and pots, and they distributed them speedily among all the people.€€ƒxíAnd afterwards they made ready for themselves, and for the priests: for the priests were busied in offering of holocausts and the fat until night, wherefore the Levites prepared for themselves, and for the priests the sons of Aaron last.€€ƒy:And the singers the sons of Asaph stood in their order, according to the commandment of David, and Asaph, and Heman, and Idithun, the prophets of the king: and the porters kept guard at every gate, so as not to depart one moment from their service, and therefore their brethren the Levites prepared meats for them.€€ƒx¹So all the service of the Lord was duly accomplished that day, both in keeping the phase and offering holocausts upon the altar of the Lord, according to the commandment of king Josias.€€ƒxvAnd the children of Israel that were found there, kept the phase at that time, and the feast of unleavened seven days.€€ƒyThere was no phase like to this in Israel, from the days of Samuel the prophet: neither did any of all the kings of Israel keep such a phase as Josias kept, with the priests, and the Levites, and all Juda, and Israel that were found, and the inhabitants of Jerusalem.€€ƒxHIn the eighteenth year of the reign of Josias was this phase celebrated.€€ƒxAfter that Josias had repaired the temple, Nechao king of Egypt came up to fight in Charcamis by the Euphrates: and Josias went out to meet him.€€ƒyBut he sent messengers to him, saying: What have I to do with thee, O king of Juda? I come not against thee this day, but I fight against another house, to which God hath commanded me to go in haste: forbear to do against God, who is with me, lest he kill thee.€€ƒx¦Josias would not return, but prepared to fight against him, and hearkened not to the words of Nechao from the mouth of God, but went to fight in the field of Mageddo.€€ƒx~And there he was wounded by the archers, and he said to his servants: Carry me out of the battle, for I am grievously wounded.€€ƒxğAnd they removed him from the chariot into another, that followed him after the manner of kings, and they carried him away to Jerusalem, and he died, and was buried in the monument of his fathers, and all Juda and Jerusalem mourned for him,€€ƒxÄParticularly Jeremias: whose lamentations for Josias all the singing men and singing women repeat unto this day, and it became like a law in Israel: Behold it is found written in the Lamentations.€€ƒxnNow the rest of the acts of Josias and of his mercies, according to what was commanded by the law of the Lord:€€ƒxVAnd his works first and last, are written in the book of the kings of Juda and Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxqThen the people of the land took Joachaz the son of Josias, and made him king instead of his father in Jerusalem.€€ƒxhJoachaz was three and twenty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned three months in Jerusalem.€€ƒx†And the king of Egypt came to Jerusalem, and deposed him, and condemned the land in a hundred talents of silver, and a talent of gold.€€ƒx«And he made Eliakim his brother king in his stead, over Juda and Jerusalem: and he turned his name to Joakim: but he took Joachaz with him and carried him away into Egypt.€€ƒxJoakim was five and twenty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned eleven years in Jerusalem: and he did evil before the Lord his God.€€—ƒxPAnd he carried also thither the vessels of the Lord, and put them in his temple.€€ƒx×But the rest of the acts of Joakim, and his abominations, which he wrought, and the things that were found in him, are contained in the book of the kings of Juda and Israel. And Joachin his son reigned in his stead.€€ƒx”Joachin was eight years old when he began to reign, and he reigned three months and ten days in Jerusalem, and he did evil in the sight of the Lord.€€ƒxäAnd at the return of the year, king Nabuchodonosor sent, and brought him to Babylon, carrying away at the same time the most precious vessels of the house of the Lord: and he made Sedecias his uncle king over Juda and Jerusalem.€€ƒxgSedecias was one and twenty years old when he began to reign: and he reigned eleven years in Jerusalem.€€ƒx“And he did evil in the eyes of the Lord his God, and did not reverence the face of Jeremias the prophet speaking to him from the mouth of the Lord.€€ƒxŸHe also revolted from king Nabuchodonosor, who had made him swear by God: and he hardened his neck and his heart, from returning to the Lord the God of Israel.€€ƒx×Moreover all the chief of the priests, and the people wickedly transgressed according to all the abominations of the Gentiles: and they defiled the house of the Lord, which he had sanctified to himself in Jerusalem.€€ƒx±And the Lord the God of their fathers sent to them, by the hand of his messengers, rising early, and daily admonishing them: because he spared his people and his dwelling place.€€ƒx§But they mocked the messengers of God, and despised his words, and misused the prophets, until the wrath of the Lord arose against his people, and there was no remedy.€€ƒxÿFor he brought upon them the king of the Chaldeans, and he slew their young men with the sword in the house of his sanctuary, he had no compassion on young man, or maiden, old man or even him that stooped for age, but he delivered them all into his hands.€€ƒxšAnd all the vessels of the house of Lord, great and small, and the treasures of the temple and of the king, and of the princes he carried away to Babylon.€€ƒx–And the enemies set fire to the house of God, and broke down the wall of Jerusalem, burnt all the towers, and what soever was precious they destroyed.€€ƒx€Whosoever escaped the sword, was led into Babylon, and there served the king and his sons, till the reign of the king of Persia,€€ƒxÈThat the word of the Lord by the mouth of Jeremias might be fulfilled, and the land might keep her sabbaths: for all the days of the desolation she kept a sabbath, till the seventy years were expired.€€ƒyBut in the first year of Cyrus king of the Persians, to fulfil the word of the Lord, which he had spoken by the mouth of Jeremias, the Lord stirred up the heart of Cyrus, king of the Persians: who commanded it to be proclaimed through all his kingdom, and by writing also, saying:€€ƒyThus saith Cyrus king of the Persians: All the kingdoms of the earth hath the Lord the God of heaven given to me, and he hath charged me to build him a house in Jerusalem, which is in Judea: who is there among you of all his people? The Lord his God be with him, and let him go up.€€ƒpThe Book of Ezra€€ƒ`€€ƒy	In the first year of Cyrus king of the Persians, that the word of the Lord by the mouth of Jeremias might be fulfilled, the Lord stirred up the spirit of Cyrus king of the Persians: and he made a proclamation throughout all his kingdom, and in writing also, saying:€€ƒx¾Thus saith Cyrus king of the Persians: The Lord the God of heaven hath given to me all the kingdoms of the earth, and he hath charged me to build him a house in Jerusalem, which is in Judea.€€ƒxÄWho is there among you of all his people? His God be with him. Let him go up to Jerusalem, which is in Judea, and build the house of the Lord the God of Israel: he is the God that is in Jerusalem.€€ƒxÜAnd let all the rest in all places wheresoever they dwell, help him every man from his place, with silver and gold, and goods, and cattle, besides that which they offer freely to the temple of God, which is in Jerusalem.€€dEzra¤dnamedEzraiosis_namedEzralabbreviationdEzrahchaptersŠ‹ƒx™And king Cyrus brought forth vessels of the temple of the Lord, which Nabuchodonosor had taken from Jerusalem, and had put them in the temple of his god.€€ƒxNow Cyrus king of Persia brought them forth by the hand of Mithridates the son of Gazabar, and numbered them to Sassabasar the prince of Juda.€€ƒx~And this is the number of them: thirty bowls of gold, a thousand bowls of silver, nine and twenty knives, thirty cups of gold,€€ƒxMSilver cups of a second sort, four hundred and ten: other vessels a thousand.€€ƒxŸAll the vessels of gold and silver, five thousand four hundred: all these Sassabasar brought with them that came up from the captivity of Babylon to Jerusalem.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÌNow these are the children of the province, that went out of the captivity, which Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon had carried away to Babylon, and who returned to Jerusalem and Juda, every man to his city.€€ƒx›Who came with Zorobabel, Josue, Nehemia, Saraia, Rahelaia, Mardochai, Belsan, Mesphar, Beguai, Rehum, Baana. The number of the men of the people of Israel:€€ƒx<The children of Pharos two thousand one hundred seventy-two.€€ƒx4The children of Sephatia, three hundred seventy-two.€€ƒx1The children of Area, seven hundred seventy-five.€€˜Fƒx8The children of Elam, a thousand two hundred fifty-four.€€ƒx0The children of Zethua, nine hundred forty-five.€€ƒx,The children of Zachai, seven hundred sixty.€€ƒx,The children of Bani, six hundred forty-two.€€ƒx0The children of Bebai, six hundred twenty-three.€€ƒx9The children of Azgad, a thousand two hundred twenty-two.€€ƒx0The children of Adonicam, six hundred sixty-six.€€ƒx/The children of Beguai, two thousand fifty-six.€€ƒx.The children of Adin, four hundred fifty-four.€€ƒx:The children of Ather, who were of Ezechias, ninety-eight.€€ƒx6The children of Besai, three hundred and twenty-three.€€ƒx+The children of Jora, a hundred and twelve.€€ƒx0The children of Hasum, two hundred twenty-three.€€ƒx$The children of Gebbar, ninety-five.€€ƒx2The children of Bethlehem, a hundred twenty-three.€€ƒxThe men of Netupha, fifty-six.€€ƒx,The men of Anathoth, a hundred twenty-eight.€€ƒx$The children of Azmaveth, forty-two.€€ƒxMThe children of Cariathiarim, Cephira, and Beroth, seven hundred forty-three.€€ƒx7The children of Rama and Gabaa, six hundred twenty-one.€€ƒx)The men of Machmas, a hundred twenty-two.€€ƒx4The men of Bethel and Hai, two hundred twenty-three.€€ƒx The children of Nebo, fifty-two.€€ƒx,The children of Megbis, a hundred fifty-six.€€ƒxBThe children of the other Elam, a thousand two hundred fifty-five.€€ƒx0The children of Harim, three hundred and twenty.€€ƒx>The children of Lod, Hadid and Ono, seven hundred twenty-five.€€ƒx2The children of Jericho, three hundred forty-five.€€ƒx9The children of Senaa, three thousand six hundred thirty.€€ƒxVThe priests: the children of Jadaia of the house of Josue, nine hundred seventy-three.€€ƒx,The children of Emmer, a thousand fifty-two.€€ƒx<The children of Pheshur, a thousand two hundred forty-seven.€€ƒx0The children of Harim, a thousand and seventeen.€€ƒxYThe Levites: the children of Josue and of Cedmihel, the children of Odovia, seventy-four.€€ƒx?The singing men: the children of Asaph, a hundred twenty-eight.€€ƒxÆThe children of the porters: the children of Sellum, the children of Ater, the children of Telmon, the children of Accub, the children of Hatita, the children of Sobai: in all a hundred thirty-nine.€€ƒxXThe Nathinites: the children of Siha, the children of Hasupha, the children of Tabbaoth,€€ƒxCThe children of Ceros, the children of Sia, the children of Phadon,€€ƒxFThe children of Lebana, the children of Hegaba, the children of Accub,€€ƒxEThe children of Hagab, the children of Semlai, the children of Hanan,€€ƒxEThe children of Gaddel, the children of Gaher, the children of Raaia,€€ƒxEThe children of Rasin, the children of Necoda, the children of Gazam,€€ƒxCThe children of Asa, the children of Phasea, the children of Besee,€€ƒxGThe children of Asena, the children of Munim, the children of Nephusim,€€ƒxHThe children of Bacbuc, the children of Hacupha, the children of Harhur,€€ƒxGThe children of Besluth, the children of Mahida, the children of Harsa,€€ƒxFThe children of Bercos, the children of Sisara, the children of Thema,€€ƒx/The children of Nasia, the children of Hatipha,€€ƒxrThe children of the servants of Solomon, the children of Sotai, the children of Sopheret, the children of Pharuda,€€ƒxEThe children of Jala, the children of Dercon, the children of Geddel,€€ƒxxThe children of Saphatia, the children of Hatil, the children of Phochereth, which were of Asebaim, the children of Ami,€€ƒxZAll the Nathinites, and the children of the servants of Solomon, three hundred ninety-two.€€ƒxµAnd these are they that came up from Thelmela, Thelharsa, Cherub, and Adon, and Emer. And they could not shew the house of their fathers and their seed, whether they were of Israel.€€ƒx]The children of Dalaia, the children of Tobia, the children of Necoda, six hundred fifty-two.€€ƒxÈAnd of the children of the priests: the children of Hobia, the children of Accos, the children of Berzellai, who took a wife of the daughters of Berzellai, the Galaadite, and was called by their name:€€ƒxhThese sought the writing of their genealogy, and found it not, and they were cast out of the priesthood.€€ƒx{And Athersatha said to them, that they should not eat of the holy of holies, till there arose a priest learned and perfect.€€ƒxOAll the multitudes as one man, were forty-two thousand three hundred and sixty:€€ƒxªBesides their menservants, and womenservants, of whom there were seven thousand three hundred and thirty-seven: and among them singing men, and singing women two hundred.€€ƒxJTheir horses seven hundred thirty-six, their mules two hundred forty-five,€€ƒxYTheir camels four hundred thirty-five, their asses six thousand seven hundred and twenty.€€ƒx¨And some of the chief of the fathers, when they came to the temple of the Lord, which is in Jerusalem, offered freely to the house of the Lord to build it in its place.€€ƒx²According to their ability, they gave towards the expenses of the work, sixty-one thousand solids of gold, five thousand pounds of silver, and a hundred garments for the priests.€€ƒx§So the priests and the Levites, and some of the people, and the singing men, and the porters, and the Nathinites dwelt in their cities, and all Israel in their cities.€€ƒ`€€ƒx™And now the seventh month was come, and the children of Israel were in their cities: and the people gathered themselves together as one man to Jerusalem.€€ƒyAnd Josue the son of Josedec rose up, and his brethren the priests, and Zorobabel the son of Salathiel, and his brethren, and they built the altar of the God of Israel that they might offer holocausts upon it, as it is written in the law of Moses the man of God.€€ƒx¯And they set the altar of God upon its bases, while the people of the lands round about put them in fear, and they offered upon it a holocaust to the Lord morning and evening.€€ƒx£And they kept the feast of tabernacles, as it is written, and offered the holocaust every day orderly according to the commandment, the duty of the day in its day.€€ƒx»And afterwards the continual holocaust, both on the new moons, and on all the solemnities of the Lord, that were consecrated, and on all in which a freewill offering was made to the Lord.€€ƒxğAnd they gave money to hewers of stones and to masons: and meat and drink, and oil to the Sidonians and Tyrians, to bring cedar trees from Libanus to the sea of Joppe, according to the orders which Cyrus king of the Persians had given them.€€ƒyyAnd in the second year of their coming to the temple of God in Jerusalem, the second month, Zorobabel the son of Salathiel, and Josue the son of Josedec, and the rest of their brethren the priests, and the Levites, and all that were come from the captivity to Jerusalem began, and they appointed Levites from twenty years old and upward, to hasten forward the work of the Lord.€€ƒxéThen Josue and his sons and his brethren, Cedmihel, and his sons, and the children of Juda, as one man, stood to hasten them that did the work in the temple of God: the sons of Henadad, and their sons, and their brethren the Levites.€€ƒxÛAnd when the masons laid the foundations of the temple of the Lord, the priests stood in their ornaments with trumpets: and the Levites the sons of Asaph with cymbals, to praise God by the hands of David king of Israel.€€ƒxøAnd they sung together hymns, and praise to the Lord: because he is good, for his mercy endureth for ever towards Israel. And all the people shouted with a great shout, praising the Lord, because the foundations of the temple of the Lord were laid.€€ƒy␀But many of the priests and the Levites, and the chief of the fathers and the ancients that had seen the former temple; when they had the foundation of this temple before their eyes, wept with a loud voice: and many shouting for joy, lifted up their voice.€€ƒxÊSo that one could not distinguish the voice of the shout of joy, from the noise of the weeping of the people: for one with another the people shouted with a loud shout, and the voice was heard afar off.€€ƒ`€€ƒxƒNow the enemies of Juda and Benjamin heard that the children of the captivity were building a temple to the Lord the God of Israel.€€ƒxéAnd they came to Zorobabel, and the chief of the fathers, and said to them: Let us build with you, for we seek your God as ye do: behold we have sacrificed to him, since the days of Asor Haddan king of Assyria, who brought us hither.€€ƒxşBut Zorobabel, and Josue, and the rest of the chief of the fathers of Israel said to them: You have nothing to do with us to build a house to our God, but we ourselves alone will build to the Lord our God, as Cyrus king of the Persians hath commanded us.€€ƒxdThen the people of the land hindered the hands of the people of Juda, and troubled them in building.€€ƒxAnd they hired counsellors against them, to frustrate their design all the days of Cyrus king of Persia, even until the reign of Darius king of the Persians.€€˜ƒxèAnd in the days of Artaxerxes, Beselam, Mithridates, and Thabeel, and the rest that were in the council wrote to Artaxerxes king of the Persians: and the letter of accusation was written in Syrian, and was read in the Syrian tongue.€€ƒxfReum Beelteem, and Samsai the scribe wrote a letter from Jerusalem to king Artaxerxes, in this manner:€€ƒxáReum Beelteem, and Samsai the scribe and the rest of their counsellors, the Dinites, and the Apharsathacites, the Therphalites, the Apharsites, the Erchuites, the Babylonians, the Susanechites, the Dievites, and the Elamites,€€ƒxÀAnd the rest of the nations, whom the great and glorious Asenaphar brought over: and made to dwell in the cities of Samaria and in the rest of the countries of this side of the river in peace.€€ƒxš(This is the copy of the letter, which they sent to him:) To Artaxerxes the king, thy servants, the men that are on this side of the river, send greeting.€€ƒxÊBe it known to the king, that the Jews, who came up from thee to us, are come to Jerusalem a rebellious and wicked city, which they are building, setting up the ramparts thereof and repairing the walls.€€ƒxÀAnd now be it known to the king, that if this city be built up, and the walls thereof repaired, they will not pay tribute nor toll, nor yearly revenues, and this loss will fall upon the kings.€€ƒx¢But we remembering the salt that we have eaten in the palace, and because we count it a crime to see the king wronged, have therefore sent and certified the king,€€ƒy)That search may be made in the books of the histories of thy fathers, and thou shalt find written in the records: and shalt know that this city is a rebellious city, and hurtful to the kings and provinces, and that wars were raised therein of old time: for which cause also the city was destroyed.€€ƒx‰We certify the king, that if this city be built, and the walls thereof repaired, thou shalt have no possession on this side of the river.€€ƒx¼The king sent word to Reum Beelteem and Samsai the scribe, and to the rest that were in their council, inhabitants of Samaria, and to the rest beyond the river, sending greeting and peace.€€ƒxLThe accusation, which you have sent to us, hath been plainly read before me,€€ƒx¥And I commanded: and search hath been made, and it is found, that this city of old time hath rebelled against kings, and seditions and wars have been raised therein.€€ƒx§For there have been powerful kings in Jerusalem, who have had dominion over all the country that is beyond the river: and have received tribute, and toll and revenues.€€ƒxsNow therefore hear the sentence: Hinder those men, that this city be not built, till further orders be given by me.€€ƒxrSee that you be not negligent in executing this, lest by little and little the evil grow to the hurt of the kings.€€ƒxÓNow the copy of the edict of king Artaxerxes was read before Reum Beelteem, and Samsai the scribe, and their counsellors: and they went up in haste to Jerusalem to the Jews, and hindered them with arm and power.€€ƒx‘Then the work of the house of the Lord in Jerusalem was interrupted, and ceased till the second year of the reign of Darius king of the Persians.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‘Now Aggeus the prophet, and Zacharias the son of Addo, prophesied to the Jews that were in Judea and Jerusalem, in the name of the God of Israel.€€ƒx²Then rose up Zorobabel the son of Salathiel, and Josue the son of Josedec, and began to build the temple of God in Jerusalem, and with them were the prophets of God helping them.€€ƒxàAnd at the same time came to them Thathanai, who was governor beyond the river, and Stharbuzanai, and their counsellors: and said thus to them: Who hath given you counsel to build this house, and to repair the walls thereof?€€ƒx]In answer to which we gave them the names of the men who were the promoters of that building.€€ƒxŞBut the eye of their God was upon the ancients of the Jews, and they could not hinder them. And it was agreed that the matter should be referred to Darius, and then they should give satisfaction concerning that accusation.€€‘ƒxOThe letter which they sent him, was written thus: To Darius the king all peace.€€ƒxôBe it known to the king, that we went to the province of Judea, to the house of the great God, which they are building with unpolished stones, and timber is laid in the walls: and this work is carried on diligently and advanceth in their hands.€€ƒx€And we asked those ancients, and said to them thus: Who hath given you authority to build this house, and to repair these walls?€€ƒxŠWe asked also of them their names, that we might give thee notice: and we have written the names of the men that are the chief among them.€€ƒxÕAnd they answered us in these words, saying: We are the servants of the God of heaven and earth, and we are building a temple that was built these many years ago, and which a great king of Israel built and set up.€€ƒxİBut after that our fathers had provoked the God of heaven to wrath, he delivered them into the hands of Nabuchodonosor the king of Babylon the Chaldean: and he destroyed this house, and carried away the people to Babylon.€€ƒxzBut in the first year of Cyrus the king of Babylon, king Cyrus set forth a decree, that this house of God should be built.€€ƒy2And the vessels also of gold and silver of the temple of God, which Nabuchodonosor had taken out of the temple, that was in Jerusalem, and had brought them to the temple of Babylon, king Cyrus brought out of the temple of Babylon, and they were delivered to one Sassabasar, whom also he appointed governor,€€ƒxAnd said to him: Take these vessels, and go, and put them in the temple that is in Jerusalem, and let the house of God be built in its place.€€ƒx¤Then came this same Sassabasar, and laid the foundations of the temple of God in Jerusalem, and from that time until now it is in building, and is not yet finished.€€ƒy
Now therefore if it seem good to the king, let him search in the king's library, which is in Babylon, whether it hath been decreed by Cyrus the king, that the house of God in Jerusalem should be built, and let the king send his pleasure to us concerning this matter.€€ƒ`€€ƒxiThen king Darius gave orders, and they searched in the library of the books that were laid up in Babylon,€€ƒxuAnd there was found in Ecbatana, which is a castle in the province of Media, a book in which this record was written.€€ƒy"In the first year of Cyrus the king: Cyrus the king decreed, that the house of God should be built, which is in Jerusalem, in the place where they may offer sacrifices, and that they lay the foundations that may support the height of threescore cubits, and the breadth of threescore cubits,€€ƒxsThree rows of unpolished stones, and so rows of new timber: and the charges shall be given out of the king's house.€€ƒyAnd also let the golden and silver vessels of the temple of God, which Nabuchodonosor took out of the temple of Jerusalem, and brought to Babylon, be restored, and carried back to the temple of Jerusalem to their place, which also were placed in the temple of God.€€–ƒx‹And let that temple of God be built by the governor of the Jews, and by their ancients, that they may build that house of God in its place.€€ƒyI also have commanded what must be done by those ancients of the Jews, that the house of God may be built, to wit, that of the king's chest, that is, of the tribute that is paid out of the country beyond the river, the charges be diligently given to those men, lest the work be hindered.€€ƒyAnd if it shall be necessary, let calves also, and lambs, and kids, for holocausts to the God of heaven, wheat, salt, wine, and oil, according to the custom of the priests that are in Jerusalem, be given them day by day, that there be no complaint in any thing.€€ƒxjAnd let them offer oblations to the God of heaven, and pray for the life of the king, and of his children.€€ƒx²And I have made a decree: That if any whosoever, shall alter this commandment, a beam be taken from his house, and set up, and he be nailed upon it, and his house be confiscated.€€ƒy
And may the God, that hath caused his name to dwell there, destroy all kingdoms, and the people that shall put out their hand to resist, and to destroy the house of God, that is in Jerusalem. I Darius have made the decree, which I will have diligently complied with.€€ƒxšSo then Thathanai, governor of the country beyond the river, and Stharbuzanai, and his counsellors diligently executed what Darius the king had commanded.€€ƒyAnd the ancients of the Jews built, and prospered according to the prophecy of Aggeus the prophet, and of Zacharias the son of Addo: and they built and finished, by the commandment of the God of Israel, and by the commandment of Cyrus, and Darius, and Artaxerxes kings of the Persians.€€ƒxAnd they were finishing this house of God, until the third day of the month of Adar, which was in the sixth year of the reign of king Darius.€€ƒx˜And the children of Israel, the priests and the Levites, and the rest of the children of the captivity kept the dedication of the house of God with joy.€€ƒx×And they offered at the dedication of the house of God, a hundred calves, two hundred rams, four hundred lambs, and for a sin offering for all Israel twelve he goats, according to the number of the tribes of Israel.€€ƒx˜And they set the priests in their divisions, and the Levites in their courses over the works of God in Jerusalem, as it is written in the book of Moses.€€ƒxeAnd the children of Israel of the captivity kept the phase, on the fourteenth day of the first month.€€ƒx¹For all the priests and the Levites were purified as one man: all were clean to kill the phase for all the children of the captivity, and for their brethren the priests, and themselves.€€ƒxÉAnd the children of Israel that were returned from captivity, and all that had separated themselves from the filthiness of the nations of the earth to them, to seek the Lord the God of Israel, did eat.€€ƒxôAnd they kept the feast of unleavened bread seven days with joy, for the Lord had made them joyful, and had turned the heart of the king of Assyria to them, that he should help their hands in the work of the house of the Lord the God of Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŠNow after these things in the reign of Artaxerxes king of the Persians, Esdras the son of Saraias, the son of Azarias, the son of Helcias,€€ƒx8The son of Sellum, the son of Sadoc, the son of Achitob,€€ƒx<The son of Amarias, the son of Azarias, the son of Maraioth,€€ƒx6The son of Zarahias, the son of Ozi, the son of Bocci,€€ƒxkThe son of Abisue, the son of Phinees, the son of Eleazar, the son of Aaron, the priest from the beginning.€€˜ƒxôAnd there went up some of the children of Israel, and of the children of the priests, and of the children of the Levites, and of the singing men, and of the porters, and of the Nathinites to Jerusalem in the seventh year of Artaxerxes the king.€€ƒxOAnd they came to Jerusalem in the fifth month, in the seventh year of the king.€€ƒx¶For upon the first day of the first month he began to go up from Babylon, and on the first day of the fifth month he came to Jerusalem according to the good hand of his God upon him.€€ƒx~For Esdras had prepared his heart to seek the law of the Lord, and to do and to teach in Israel the commandments and judgment.€€ƒxÀAnd this is the copy of the letter of the edict, which king Artaxerxes gave to Esdras the priest, the scribe instructed in the words and commandments of the Lord, and his ceremonies in Israel.€€ƒxqArtaxerxes king of kings to Esdras the priest, the most learned scribe of the law of the God of heaven, greeting.€€ƒx©It is decreed by me, that all they of the people of Israel, and of the priests and of the Levites in my realm, that are minded to go into Jerusalem, should go with thee.€€ƒx–For thou art sent from before the king, and his seven counsellors, to visit Judea and Jerusalem according to the law of thy God, which is in thy hand.€€ƒxAnd to carry the silver and gold, which the king and his counsellors have freely offered to the God of Israel, whose tabernacle is in Jerusalem.€€ƒxàAnd all the silver and gold that thou shalt find in all the province of Babylon, and that the people is willing to offer, and that the priests shall offer of their own accord to the house of their God, which is in Jerusalem,€€ƒx¿Take freely, and buy diligently with this money, calves, rams, lambs, with the sacrifices and libations of them, and offer them upon the altar of the temple of your God, that is in Jerusalem.€€ƒxAnd if it seem good to thee, and to thy brethren to do any thing with the rest of the silver and gold, do it according to the will of your God.€€ƒxThe vessels also, that are given thee for the sacrifice of the house of thy God, deliver thou in the sight of God in Jerusalem.€€ƒxÃAnd whatsoever more there shall be need of for the house of thy God, how much soever thou shalt have occasion to spend, it shall be given out of the treasury, and the king's exchequer, and by me.€€ƒxñI Artaxerxes the king have ordered and decreed to all the keepers of the public chest, that are beyond the river, that whatsoever Esdras the priest, the scribe of the law of the God of heaven, shall require of you, you give it without delay,€€ƒx¡Unto a hundred talents of silver, and unto a hundred cores of wheat, and unto a hundred bates of wine, and unto a hundred bates of oil, and salt without measure.€€ƒxÆAll that belongeth to the rites of the God of heaven, let it be given diligently in the house of the God of heaven: lest his wrath should be enkindled against the realm of the king, and of his sons.€€ƒxôWe give you also to understand concerning all the priests, and the Levites, and the singers, and the porters, and the Nathinites, and ministers of the house of this God, that you have no authority to impose toll or tribute, or custom upon them.€€ƒx÷And thou Esdras according to the wisdom of thy God, which is in thy hand, appoint judges and magistrates, that may judge all the people, that is beyond the river, that is, for them who know the law of thy God, yea and the ignorant teach ye freely.€€ƒxÕAnd whosoever will not do the law of thy God, and the law of the king diligently, judgment shall be executed upon him, either unto death, or unto banishment, or to the confiscation of goods, or at least to prison.€€ƒx‹Blessed be the Lord the God of our fathers, who hath put this in the king's heart, to glorify the house of the Lord, which is in Jerusalem,€€ƒxúAnd hath inclined his mercy toward me before the king and his counsellors, and all the mighty princes of the king: and I being strengthened by the hand of the Lord my God, which was upon me, gathered together out of Israel chief men to go up with me.€€ƒ`€€ƒx…Now these are the chief of families, and the genealogy of them, who came up with me from Babylon in the reign of Artaxerxes the king.€€ƒx]Of the sons of Phinees, Gersom. Of the sons of Ithamar, Daniel. Of the sons of David, Hattus.€€ƒxkOf the sons of Sechenias, the son of Pharos, Zacharias, and with him were numbered a hundred and fifty men.€€ƒxVOf the sons of Phahath Moab, Eleoenai the son of Zareha, and with him two hundred men.€€ƒxNOf the sons of Sechenias, the son of Ezechiel, and with him three hundred men.€€˜$ƒxJOf the sons of Alam, Isaias the son of Athalias, and with him seventy men.€€ƒxMOf the sons of Saphatia: Zebodia the son of Michael, and with him eighty men.€€ƒxYOf the sons of Joab, Obedia the son of Jahiel, and with him two hundred and eighteen men.€€ƒxROf the sons of Selomith, the son of Josphia, and with him a hundred and sixty men.€€ƒxTOf the sons of Bebai, Zacharias the son of Bebai: and with him eight and twenty men.€€ƒxTOf the sons of Azgad, Joanan the son of Eccetan, and with him a hundred and ten men.€€ƒxƒOf the sons of Adonicam, who were the last: and these are their names: Eliphelet, and Jehiel, and Samaias, and with them sixty men.€€ƒxBOf the sons of Begui, Uthai and Zachur, and with them seventy men.€€ƒxÇAnd I gathered them together to the river, which runneth down to Ahava, and we stayed there three days: and I sought among the people and among the priests for the sons of Levi, and found none there.€€ƒx²So I sent Eliezer, and Ariel, and Semeias, and Elnathan, and Jarib, and another Elnathan, and Nathan, and Zacharias, and Mosollam, chief men: and Joiarib, and Elnathan, wise men.€€ƒxùAnd I sent them to Eddo, who is chief in the place of Chasphia, and I put in their mouth the words that they should speak to Eddo, and his brethren the Nathinites in the place of Chasphia, that they should bring us ministers of the house of our God.€€ƒxºAnd by the good hand of our God upon us, they brought us a most learned man of the sons of Moholi the son of Levi the son of Israel, and Sarabias and his sons, and his brethren eighteen,€€ƒx_And Hasabias, and with him Isaias of the sons of Merari, and his brethren, and his sons twenty.€€ƒx And of the Nathinites, whom David, and the princes gave for the service of the Levites, Nathinites two hundred and twenty: all these were called by their names.€€ƒxÃAnd I proclaimed there a fast by the river Ahava, that we might afflict ourselves before the Lord our God, and might ask of him a right way for us and for our children, and for all our substance.€€ƒy
For I was ashamed to ask the king for aid and for horsemen, to defend us from the enemy in the way: because we had said to the king: The hand of our God is upon all them that seek him in goodness: and his power and strength, and wrath upon all them that forsake him.€€ƒxSAnd we fasted, and besought our God for this: and it fell out prosperously unto us.€€ƒxpAnd I separated twelve of the chief of the priests, Sarabias, and Hasabias, and with them ten of their brethren,€€ƒxÄAnd I weighed unto them the silver and gold, and the vessels consecrated for the house of our God, which the king and his counsellors, and his princes, and all Israel, that were found had offered.€€ƒx…And I weighed to their hands six hundred and fifty talents of silver, and a hundred vessels of silver, and a hundred talents of gold,€€ƒxlAnd twenty cups of gold, of a thousand solids, and two vessels of the best shining brass, beautiful as gold.€€ƒx¤And I said to them: You are the holy ones of the Lord, and the vessels are holy, and the silver and gold, that is freely offered to the Lord the God of our fathers.€€ƒxÎWatch ye and keep them, till you deliver them by weight before the chief of the priests, and of the Levites, and the heads of the families of Israel in Jerusalem, into the treasure of the house of the Lord.€€ƒxAnd the priests and the Levites received the weight of the silver and gold, and the vessels, to carry them to Jerusalem to the house of our God.€€ƒxÛThen we set forward from the river Ahava on the twelfth day of the first month to go to Jerusalem: and the hand of our God was upon us, and delivered us from the hand of the enemy, and of such as lay in wait by the way.€€ƒx9And we came to Jerusalem, and we stayed there three days.€€ƒyAnd on the fourth day the silver and the gold, and the vessels were weighed in the house of our God by the hand of Meremoth the son of Urias the priest, and with him was Eleazar the son of Phinees, and with them Jozabad the son of Josue, and Noadaia the son of Benoi, Levites.€€ƒx^According to the number and weight of everything: and all the weight was written at that time.€€ƒyMoreover the children of them that had been carried away that were come out of the captivity, offered holocausts to the God of Israel, twelve calves for all the people of Israel, ninety-six rams, seventy-seven lambs, and twelve he goats for sin: all for a holocaust to the Lord.€€ƒx¥And they gave the king's edicts to the lords that were from the king's court, and the governors beyond the river, and they furthered the people and the house of God.€€ƒ`€€ƒyuAnd after these things were accomplished, the princes came to me, saying: The people of Israel, and the priests and Levites have not separated themselves from the people of the lands, and from their abominations, namely, of the Chanaanites, and the Hethites, and the Pherezites, and the Jebusites, and the Ammonites, and the Moabites, and the Egyptians, and the Amorrhites.€€ƒxŞFor they have taken of their daughters for themselves and for their sons, and they have mingled the holy seed with the people of the lands. And the hand of the princes and magistrates hath been first in this transgression.€€ƒx‰And when I had heard this word, I rent my mantle and my coat, and plucked off the hairs of my head and my beard, and I sat down mourning.€€ƒx¼And there were assembled to me all that feared the God of Israel, because of the transgression of those that were come from the captivity, and I sat sorrowful, until the evening sacrifice.€€ƒx¦And at the evening sacrifice I rose up from my affliction, and having rent my mantle and my garment, I fell upon my knees, and spread out my hands to the Lord my God,€€ƒy2From the days of our fathers: and we ourselves also have sinned grievously unto this day, and for our iniquities we and our kings, and our priests have been delivered into the hands of the kings of the lands, and to the sword, and to captivity, and to spoil, and to confusion of face, as it is at this day.€€ƒxîAnd now as a little, and for a moment has our prayer been made before the Lord our God, to leave us a remnant, and give us a pin in his holy place, and that our God would enlighten our eyes, and would give us a little life in our bondage.€€ƒyFor we are bondmen, and in our bondage our God hath not forsaken us, but hath extended mercy upon us before the king of the Persians, to give us life, and to set up the house of our God, and to rebuild the desolations thereof, and to give us a fence in Juda and Jerusalem.€€ƒxXAnd now, O our God, what shall we say after this? for we have forsaken thy commandments,€€ƒyWhich thou hast commanded by the hand of thy servants the prophets, saying: The land which you go to possess, is an unclean land, according to the uncleanness of the people, and of other lands, with their abominations, who have filled it from mouth to mouth with their filth.€€ƒyNow therefore give not your daughters to their sons, and take not their daughters for your sons, and seek not their peace, nor their prosperity for ever: that you may be strengthened, and may eat the good things of the land, and may have your children your heirs for ever.€€ƒx»And after all that is come upon us, for our most wicked deeds, and our great sin, seeing that thou our God hast saved us from our iniquity, and hast given us a deliverance as at this day,€€ƒxËThat we should not turn away, nor break thy commandments, nor join in marriage with the people of these abominations. Art thou angry with us unto utter destruction, not to leave us a remnant to be saved?€€ƒx±O Lord God of Israel, thou art just: for we remain yet to be saved as at this day. Behold we are before thee in our sin, for there can be no standing before thee in this matter.€€ƒ`€€ƒxìNow when Esdras was thus praying, and beseeching, and weeping, and lying before the temple of God, there was gathered to him of Israel an exceeding great assembly of men and women and children, and the people wept with much lamentation.€€ƒxãAnd Sechenias the son of Jehiel of the sons of Elam answered, and said to Esdras: We have sinned against our God, and have taken strange wives of the people of the land: and now if there be repentance in Israel concerning this,€€ƒxéLet us make a covenant with the Lord our God, to put away all the wives, and such as are born of them, according to the will of the Lord, and of them that fear the commandment of the Lord our God: let it be done according to the law.€€ƒxXArise, it is thy part to give orders, and we will be with thee: take courage, and do it.€€ƒx›So Esdras arose, and made the chiefs of the priests and of the Levites, and all Israel, to swear that they would do according to this word, and they swore.€€˜,ƒxˆAnd proclamation was made in Juda and Jerusalem to all the children of the captivity, that they should assemble together into Jerusalem.€€ƒxêAnd that whosoever would not come within three days, according to the counsel of the princes and the ancients, all his substance should be taken away, and he should be cast out of the company of them that were returned from captivity.€€ƒxıThen all the men of Juda, and Benjamin gathered themselves together to Jerusalem within three days, in the ninth month, the twentieth day of the month: and all the people sat in the street of the house of God, trembling because of the sin, and the rain.€€ƒxAnd Esdras the priest stood up, and said to them: You have transgressed, and taken strange wives, to add to the sins of Israel.€€ƒx£And now make confession to the Lord the God of your fathers, and do his pleasure, and separate yourselves from the people of the land, and from your strange wives.€€ƒxhAnd all the multitude answered and said with a loud voice: According to thy word unto us, so be it done.€€ƒx²But as the people are many, and it is time of rain, and we are not able to stand without, and it is not a work of one day or two, (for we have exceedingly sinned in this matter,)€€ƒyLet rulers be appointed in all the multitude: and in all our cities, let them that have taken strange wives come at the times appointed, and with them the ancients and the judges of every city, until the wrath of our God be turned away from us for this sin.€€ƒxˆThen Jonathan the son of Azahel, and Jaasia the son of Thecua were appointed over this, and Mesollam and Sebethai, Levites, helped them:€€ƒxçAnd the children of the captivity did so. And Esdras the priest, and the men heads of the families in the houses of their fathers, and all by their names, went and sat down in the first day of the tenth month to examine the matter.€€ƒxgAnd they made an end with all the men that had taken strange wives by the first day of the first month.€€ƒx¸And there were found among the sons of the priests that had taken strange wives: Of the sons of Josue the son of Josedec, and his brethren, Maasia, and Eliezer, and Jarib, and Godolia.€€ƒxeAnd they gave their hands to put away their wives, and to offer for their offence a ram of the flock.€€ƒx.And of the sons of Emmer, Hanani, and Zebedia.€€ƒxNAnd of the sons of Harim, Maasia, and Elia, and Semeia, and Jehiel, and Ozias.€€ƒxTAnd of the sons of Pheshur, Elioenai, Maasia, Ismael, Nathanael, Jozabed, and Elasa.€€ƒxoAnd of the sons of the Levites, Jozabed, and Semei, and Celaia, the same is Calita, Phataia, Juda, and Eliezer.€€ƒxPAnd of the singing men, Elisiab: and of the porters, Sellum, and Telem, and Uri.€€ƒxvAnd of Israel, of the sons of Pharos, Remeia, and Jezia, and Melchia, and Miamin, and Eliezer, and Melchia, and Banea.€€ƒx[And of the sons of Elam, Mathania, Zacharias, and Jehiel, and Abdi, and Jerimoth, and Elia.€€ƒxXAnd of the sons of Zethua, Elioenai, Eliasib, Mathania, Jerimuth, and Zabad, and Aziaza.€€ƒx<And of the sons of Babai, Johanan, Hanania, Zabbai, Athalai:€€ƒxWAnd of the sons of Bani, Mosollam, and Melluch, and Adaia, Jasub, and Saal, and Ramoth.€€ƒxsAnd of the sons of Phahath, Moab, Edna, and Chalal, Banaias, and Maasias, Mathanias, Beseleel, Bennui, and Manasse.€€ƒxDAnd of the sons of Herem, Eliezer, Josue, Melchias, Semeias, Simeon,€€ƒxBenjamin, Maloch, Samarias.€€ƒxWAnd of the sons of Hasom, Mathanai, Mathatha, Zabad, Eliphelet, Jermai, Manasse, Semei.€€ƒx,Of the sons of Bani, Maaddi, Amram, and Uel,€€ƒxBaneas, and Badaias, Cheliau,€€ƒxVania, Marimuth, and Eliasib,€€ƒxMathanias, Mathania, and Jasi,€€ƒxAnd Bani, and Bennui, Semei,€€ƒx$And Salmias, and Nathan, and Adaias,€€ƒxAnd Mechnedebai, Sisai, Sarai,€€ƒxEzrel, and Selemiau, Semeria,€€ƒwSellum, Amaria, Joseph.€€ƒxTOf the sons of Nebo, Jehiel, Mathathias, Zabad, Zabina, Jeddu, and Joel, and Banaia.€€ƒx[All these had taken strange wives, and there were among them women that had borne children.€€ƒtThe Book of Nehemiah€€ƒ`€€ƒxThe words of Nehemiah the son of Helchias. And it came to pass in the month of Casleu, in the twentieth year, as I was in the castle of Susa,€€ƒx«That Hanani one of my brethren came, he and some men of Juda; and I asked them concerning the Jews, that remained and were left of the captivity, and concerning Jerusalem.€€ƒxŞAnd they said to me: They that have remained, and are left of the captivity there in the province, are in great affliction, and reproach: and the wall of Jerusalem is broken down, and the gates thereof are burnt with fire.€€ƒx‘And when I had heard these words, I sat down, and wept, and mourned for many days: and I fasted, and prayed before the face of the God of heaven.€€cNeh¤dnamehNehemiahiosis_namecNehlabbreviationcNehhchapters‹ƒxWe have been seduced by vanity, and have not kept thy commandments, and ceremonies and judgments, which thou hast commanded thy servant Moses.€€ƒx‹Remember the word that thou commandedst to Moses thy servant, saying: If you shall transgress, I will scatter you abroad among the nations:€€ƒxñBut if you return to me, and keep my commandments, and do them, though you should be led away to the uttermost parts of the world, I will gather you from thence, and bring you back to the place which I have chosen for my name to dwell there.€€ƒxrAnd these are thy servants, and thy people: whom thou hast redeemed by thy great strength, and by thy mighty hand.€€ƒxòI beseech thee, O Lord, let thy ear be attentive to the prayer of thy servant, and to the prayer of thy servants who desire to fear thy name: and direct thy servant this day, and give him mercy before this man. For I was the king's cupbearer.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÖAnd it came to pass in the month of Nisan, in the twentieth year of Artaxerxes the king: that wine was before him, and I took up the wine, and gave it to the king: and I was as one languishing away before his face.€€ƒx×And the king said to me: Why is thy countenance sad, seeing thou dost not appear to be sick? this is not without cause, but some evil, I know not what, is in thy heart. And I was seized with an exceeding great fear:€€ƒxÏAnd I said to the king: O king, live for ever: why should not my countenance be sorrowful, seeing the city of the place of the sepulchres of my fathers is desolate, and the gates thereof are burnt with fire?€€ƒx]Then the king said to me: For what dost thou make request? And I prayed to the God of heaven,€€ƒxÌAnd I said to the king: If it seem good to the king, and if thy servant hath found favour in thy sight, that thou wouldst send me into Judea to the city of the sepulchre of my father, and I will build it.€€”ƒx°And I said to the king: If it seem good to the king, let him give me letters to the governors of the country beyond the river, that they convey me over, till I come into Judea:€€ƒyAnd a letter to Asaph the keeper of the king's forest, to give me timber that I may cover the gates of the tower of the house, and the walls of the city, and the house that I shall enter into. And the king gave me according to the good hand of my God with me.€€ƒx And I came to the governors of the country beyond the river, and gave them the king's letters. And the king had sent with me captains of soldiers, and horsemen.€€ƒx·And Sanaballat the Horonite, and Tobias the servant, the Ammonite, heard it, and it grieved them exceedingly, that a man was come, who sought the prosperity of the children of Israel.€€ƒx2And I came to Jerusalem, and was there three days.€€ƒx½And I arose in the night, I and some few men with me, and I told not any man what God had put in my heart to do in Jerusalem, and there was no beast with me, but the beast that I rode upon.€€ƒx×And I went out by night by the gate of the valley, and before the dragon fountain, and to the dung gate, and I viewed the wall of Jerusalem which was broken down, and the gates thereof which were consumed with fire.€€ƒxƒAnd I passed to the gate of the fountain, and to the king's aqueduct, and there was no place for the beast on which I rode to pass.€€ƒx~And I went up in the night by the torrent, and viewed the wall, and going back I came to the gate of the valley, and returned.€€ƒxÊBut the magistrates knew not whither I went, or what I did: neither had I as yet told any thing to the Jews, or to the priests, or to the nobles, or to the magistrates, or to the rest that did the work.€€ƒxßThen I said to them: You know the affliction wherein we are, because Jerusalem is desolate, and the gates thereof are consumed with fire: come, and let us build up the walls of Jerusalem, and let us be no longer a reproach.€€ƒx½And I shewed them how the hand of my God was good with me, and the king's words, which he had spoken to me, and I said: Let us rise up, and build. And their hands were strengthened in good.€€ƒxâBut Sanaballat the Horonite, and Tobias the servant, the Ammonite, and Gossem the Arabian heard of it, and they scoffed at us, and despised us, and said: What is this thing that you do? are you going to rebel against the king?€€ƒx»And I answered them, and said to them: The God of heaven he helpeth us, and we are his servants: let us rise up and build: but you have no part, nor justice, nor remembrance in Jerusalem.€€ƒ`€€ƒxéThen Eliasib the high priest arose, and his brethren the priests, and they built the flock gate: they sanctified it, and set up the doors thereof, even unto the tower of a hundred cubits they sanctified it unto the tower of Hananeel.€€ƒxXAnd next to him the men of Jericho built: and next to them built Zachur the son of Amri.€€ƒx¹But the fish gate the sons of Asnaa built: they covered it, and set up the doors thereof, and the locks, and the bars. And next to them built Marimuth the son of Urias the son of Accus.€€ƒxyAnd next to him built Mosollam the son of Barachias, the son of Merezebel, and next to them built Sadoc the son of Baana.€€ƒxlAnd next to them the Thecuites built: but their great men did not put their necks to the work of their Lord.€€˜ ƒx¡And next to them built Meltias the Gabaonite, and Jadon the Meronathite, the men of Gabaon and Maspha, for the governor that was in the country beyond the river.€€ƒx­And next to him built Eziel the son of Araia the goldsmith: and next to him built Ananias the son of the perfumer: and they left Jerusalem unto the wall of the broad street.€€ƒxNAnd next to him built Raphaia the son of Hur, lord of the street of Jerusalem.€€ƒxyAnd next to him Jedaia the son of Haromaph over against his own house: and next to him built Hattus the son of Hasebonia.€€ƒxsMelchias the son of Herem, and Hasub the son of Phahath Moab, built half the street, and the tower of the furnaces.€€ƒxkAnd next to him built Sellum the son of Alohes, lord of half the street of Jerusalem, he and his daughters.€€ƒxĞAnd the gate of the valley Hanun built, and the inhabitants of Zanoe: they built it, and set up the doors thereof, and the locks, and the bars, and a thousand cubits in the wall unto the gate of the dunghill.€€ƒx©And the gate of the dunghill Melchias the son of Rechab built, lord of the street of Bethacharam: he built it, and set up the doors thereof, and the locks, and the bars.€€ƒy)And the gate of the fountain, Sellum, the son of Cholhoza, built, lord of the street of Maspha: he built it, and covered it, and set up the doors thereof, and the locks, and the bars, and the walls of the pool of Siloe unto the king's guard, and unto the steps that go down from the city of David.€€ƒxĞAfter him built Nehemiah the son of Azboc, lord of half the street of Bethsur, as far as over against the sepulchre of David, and to the pool, that was built with great labour, and to the house of the mighty.€€ƒx‚After him built the Levites, Rehum the son of Benni. After him built Hasebias, lord of half the street of Ceila in his own street.€€ƒxKAfter him built their brethren Bavai the son of Enadad, lord of half Ceila.€€ƒx}And next to him Aser the son of Josue, lord of Maspha, built another measure, over against the going up of the strong corner.€€ƒx‹After him in the mount Baruch the son of Zachai built another measure, from the corner to the door of the house of Eliasib the high priest.€€ƒx˜After him Merimuth the son of Urias the son of Haccus, built another measure, from the door of the house of Eliasib, to the end of the house of Eliasib.€€ƒxEAnd after him built the priests, the men of the plains of the Jordan.€€ƒx›After him built Benjamin and Hasub, over against their own house: and after him built Azarias the son of Maasias the son of Ananias over against his house.€€ƒx{After him built Bennui the son of Hanadad another measure, from the house of Azarias unto the bending, and unto the corner.€€ƒxµPhalel, the son of Ozi, over against the bending and the tower, which lieth out from the king's high house, that is, in the court of the prison: after him Phadaia the son of Pharos.€€ƒxwAnd the Nathinites dwelt in Ophel, as far as over against the water gate toward the east, and the tower that stood out.€€ƒxAfter him the Thecuites built another measure over against, from the great tower that standeth out unto the wall of the temple.€€ƒxSAnd upward from the horse gate the priests built, every man over against his house.€€ƒx‰After them built Sadoc the son of Emmer over against his house. And after him built Semaia the son of Sechenias, keeper of the east gate.€€ƒy]After him built Hanania the son of Selemia, and Hanun the sixth son of Seleph, another measure: after him built Mosollam the son of Barachias over against his treasury. After him Melcias the goldsmith's son built unto the house of the Nathinites, and of the sellers of small wares, over against the judgment gate, and unto the chamber of the corner.€€ƒx_And within the chamber of the corner of the flock gate, the goldsmiths and the merchants built.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAnd it came to pass, that when Sanaballat heard that we were building the wall he was angry: and being moved exceedingly he scoffed at the Jews.€€ƒyAnd said before his brethren, and the multitude of the Samaritans: What are the silly Jews doing? Will the Gentiles let them alone? will they sacrifice and make an end in a day? are they able to raise stones out of the heaps of the rubbish, which are burnt?€€ƒxqTobias also the Ammonite who was by him said: Let them build: if a fox go up, he will leap over their stone wall.€€ƒx…Hear thou our God, for we are despised: turn their reproach upon their own head, and give them to be despised in a land of captivity.€€ƒx{Cover not their iniquity, and let not their sin be blotted out from before thy face, because they have mocked thy builders.€€ƒxxSo we built the wall, and joined it all together unto the half thereof: and the heart of the people was excited to work.€€—ƒxmAnd they all assembled themselves together, to come, and to fight against Jerusalem, and to prepare ambushes.€€ƒxTAnd we prayed to our God, and set watchmen upon the wall day and night against them.€€ƒxŠAnd Juda said: The strength of the bearer of burdens is decayed, and the rubbish is very much, and we shall not be able to build the wall.€€ƒx‡And our enemies said: Let them not know, nor understand, till we come in the midst of them, and kill them, and cause the work to cease.€€ƒx‰And it came to pass, that when the Jews that dwelt by them came and told us ten times, out of all the places from whence they came to us,€€ƒxlI set the people in the place behind the wall round about in order, with their swords, and spears, and bows.€€ƒyAnd I looked and rose up: and I said to the chief men and the magistrates, and to the rest of the common people: be not afraid of them. Remember the Lord who is great and terrible, and fight for your brethren, your sons, and your daughters, and your wives, and your houses.€€ƒx¬And it came to pass, when our enemies heard that the thing had been told us, that God defeated their counsel. And we returned all of us to the walls, every man to his work.€€ƒxèAnd it came to pass from that day forward, that half of their young men did the work, and half were ready for to fight, with spears, and shields, and bows, and coats of mail, and the rulers were behind them in all the house of Juda.€€ƒx“Of them that built on the wall and that carried burdens, and that laded: with one of his hands he did the work, and with the other he held a sword.€€ƒxxFor every one of the builders was girded with a sword about his reins. And they built, and sounded with a trumpet by me.€€ƒxªAnd I said to the nobles, and to the magistrates, and to the rest of the common people: The work is great and wide, and we are separated on the wall one far from another:€€ƒxqIn what place soever you shall hear the sound of the trumpet, run all thither unto us: our God will fight for us.€€ƒxuAnd let us do the work: and let one half of us hold our spears from the rising of the morning, till the stars appear.€€ƒx£At that time also I said to the people: Let every one with his servant stay in the midst of Jerusalem, and let us take our turns in the night, and by day, to work.€€ƒx¡Now I and my brethren, and my servants, and the watchmen that followed me, did not put off our clothes: only every man stripped himself when he was to be washed.€€ƒ`€€ƒx\Now there was a great cry of the people, and of their wives against their brethren the Jews.€€ƒxŒAnd there were some that said: Our sons and our daughters are very many: let us take up corn for the price of them, and let us eat and live.€€ƒxˆAnd there were some that said: Let us mortgage our lands, and our vineyards, and our houses, and let us take corn because of the famine.€€ƒxiAnd others said: Let us borrow money for the king's tribute, and let us give up our fields and vineyards:€€ƒyAnd now our flesh is as the flesh of our brethren: and our children as their children. Behold we bring into bondage our sons and our daughters, and some of our daughters are bondwomen already, neither have we wherewith to redeem them, and our fields and our vineyards other men possess.€€ƒxLAnd I was exceedingly angry when I heard their cry according to these words.€€“ƒxøAnd I said to them: We, as you know, have redeemed according to our ability our brethren the Jews, that were sold to the Gentiles: and will you then sell your brethren, for us to redeem them? And they held their peace, and found not what to answer.€€ƒx And I said to them: The thing you do is not good: why walk you not in the fear of our God, that we be not exposed to the reproaches of the Gentiles our enemies?€€ƒx¢Both I and my brethren, and my servants, have lent money and corn to many: let us all agree not to call for it again; let us forgive the debt that is owing to us.€€ƒxòRestore ye to them this day their fields, and their vineyards, and their oliveyards, and their houses: and the hundredth part of the money, and of the corn, the wine, and the oil, which you were wont to exact of them, give it rather for them.€€ƒxºAnd they said: We will restore, and we will require nothing of them: and we will do as thou sayest. And I called the priests and took an oath of them, to do according to what I had said.€€ƒy Moreover I shook my lap, and said: So may God shake every man that shall not accomplish this word, out of his house, and out of his labours, thus may he be shaken out, and become empty. And all the multitude said: Amen. And they praised God. And the people did according to what was said.€€ƒyAnd from the day, in which the king commanded me to be governor in the land of Juda, from the twentieth year even to the two and thirtieth year of Artaxerxes the king, for twelve years, I and my brethren did not eat the yearly allowance that was due to the governors.€€ƒxîBut the former governors that had been before me, were chargeable to the people, and took of them in bread, and wine, and in money every day forty sicles: and their officers also oppressed the people. But I did not so for the fear of God.€€ƒxwMoreover I built in the work of the wall, and I bought no land, and all my servants were gathered together to the work.€€ƒx­The Jews also and the magistrates to the number of one hundred and fifty men, were at my table, besides them that came to us from among the nations that were round about us.€€ƒyAnd there was prepared for me day be day one ox, and six choice rams, besides fowls, and once in ten days I gave store of divers wines, and many other things: yet I did not require my yearly allowance as governor: for the people were very much impoverished.€€ƒxRRemember me, O my God, for good according to all that I have done for this people.€€ƒ`€€ƒxïAnd it came to pass, when Sanaballat, and Tobias, and Gossem the Arabian, and the rest of our enemies, heard that I had built the wall, and that there was no breach left in it, (though at that time I had not set up the doors in the gates,)€€ƒx›Sanaballat and Gossem sent to me, saying: Come, and let us make a league together in the villages, in the plain of Ono. But they thought to do me mischief.€€ƒxAnd I sent messengers to them, saying: I am doing a great work, and I cannot come down, lest it be neglected whilst I come, and go down to you.€€ƒxbAnd they sent to me according to this word, four times: and I answered them after the same manner.€€ƒxŠAnd Sanaballat sent his servant to me the fifth time according to the former word, and he had a letter in his hand written in this manner:€€ƒxÇIt is reported amongst the Gentiles, and Gossem hath said it, that thou and the Jews think to rebel, and therefore thou buildest the wall, and hast a mind to set thyself king over them: for which end€€“ƒx|And I sent to them, saying: There is no such thing done as thou sayest: but thou feignest these things out of thy own heart.€€ƒx¥For all these men thought to frighten us, thinking that our hands would cease from the work, and that we would leave off. Wherefore I strengthened my hands the more:€€ƒy"And I went into the house of Samaia the son of Delaia, the son of Metabeel privately. And he said: Let us consult together in the house of God in the midst of the temple: and let us shut the doors of the temple, for they will come to kill thee, and in the night they will come to slay thee.€€ƒx‹And I said: Should such a man as I flee? and who is there that being as I am, would go into the temple, to save his life? I will not go in.€€ƒx‘And I understood that God had not sent him, but that he had spoken to me as if he had been prophesying, and Tobias, and Sanaballat had hired him.€€ƒx~For he had taken money, that I being afraid should do this thing, and sin, and they might have some evil to upbraid me withal.€€ƒx¬Remember me, O Lord, for Tobias and Sanaballat, according to their works of this kind: and Noadias the prophet, and the rest of the prophets that would have put me in fear.€€ƒxaBut the wall was finished the five and twentieth day of the month of Elul, in two and fifty days.€€ƒxËAnd it came to pass when all our enemies heard of it, that all nations which were round about us, were afraid, and were cast down within themselves, for they perceived that this work was the work of God.€€ƒx…Moreover in those days many letters were sent by the principal men of the Jews to Tobias, and from Tobias there came letters to them.€€ƒx»For there were many in Judea sworn to him, because he was the son in law of Sechenias the son of Area, and Johanan his son had taken to wife the daughter of Mosollam the son of Barachias.€€ƒxqAnd they praised him also before me, and they related my words to him: And Tobias sent letters to put me in fear.€€ƒ`€€ƒxpNow after the wall was built, and I had set up the doors, and numbered the porters and singing men, and Levites:€€ƒx–I commanded Hanani my brother, and Hananias ruler of the house of Jerusalem, (for he seemed as a sincere man, and one that feared God above the rest,)€€ƒyAnd I said to them: Let not the gates of Jerusalem be opened till the sun be hot. And while they were yet standing by the gates were shut, and barred: and I set watchmen of the inhabitants of Jerusalem, every one by their courses, and every man over against his house.€€ƒxmAnd the city was very wide and great, and the people few in the midst thereof, and the houses were not built.€€ƒxÌBut God had put in my heart, and I assembled the princes and magistrates, and common people, to number them: and I found a book of the number of them who came up at first and therein it was found written:€€ƒxŞThese are the children of the province, who came up from the captivity of them that had been carried away, whom Nabuchodonosor the king of Babylon had carried away, and who returned into Judea, every one into his own city.€€˜Iƒx=The children of Pharos, two thousand one hundred seventy-two.€€ƒx4The children of Sephatia, three hundred seventy-two.€€ƒx,The children of Area, six hundred fifty-two.€€ƒxdThe children of Phahath Moab of the children of Josue and Joab, two thousand eight hundred eighteen.€€ƒx:The children of Elam, one thousand two hundred fifty-four.€€ƒx1The children of Zethua, eight hundred forty-five.€€ƒx,The children of Zachai, seven hundred sixty.€€ƒx0The children of Bannui, six hundred forty-eight.€€ƒx0The children of Bebai, six hundred twenty-eight.€€ƒx=The children of Azgad, two thousand three hundred twenty-two.€€ƒx2The children of Adonicam, six hundred sixty-seven.€€ƒx1The children of Beguai, two thousand sixty-seven.€€ƒx-The children of Adin, six hundred fifty-five.€€ƒx:The children of Ater, children of Hezechias, ninety-eight.€€ƒx2The children of Hasem, three hundred twenty-eight.€€ƒx1The children of Besai, three hundred twenty-four.€€ƒx-The children of Hareph, a hundred and twelve.€€ƒx$The children of Gabaon, ninety-five.€€ƒx?The children of Bethlehem, and Netupha, a hundred eighty-eight.€€ƒx,The men of Anathoth, a hundred twenty-eight.€€ƒx!The men of Bethazmoth, forty-two.€€ƒxHThe men of Cariathiarim, Cephira, and Beroth, seven hundred forty-three.€€ƒx1The men of Rama and Geba, six hundred twenty-one.€€ƒx)The men of Machmas, a hundred twenty-two.€€ƒx2The men of Bethel and Hai, a hundred twenty-three.€€ƒx%The men of the other Nebo, fifty-two.€€ƒx?The men of the other Elam, one thousand two hundred fifty-four.€€ƒx0The children of Harem, three hundred and twenty.€€ƒx2The children of Jericho, three hundred forty-five.€€ƒx@The children of Lod, of Hadid and Ono, seven hundred twenty-one.€€ƒx:The children of Senaa, three thousand nine hundred thirty.€€ƒxYThe priests: the children of Idaia in the house of Josue, nine hundred and seventy-three.€€ƒx.The children of Emmer, one thousand fifty-two.€€ƒx>The children of Phashur, one thousand two hundred forty-seven.€€ƒx>The children of Arem, one thousand and seventeen. The Levites:€€ƒx,The children of Josue and Cedmihel, the sons€€ƒx(Of Oduia, seventy-four. The singing men:€€ƒx-The children of Asaph, a hundred forty-eight.€€ƒx°The porters: the children of Sellum, the children of Ater, the children of Telmon, the children of Accub, the children of Hatita, the children of Sobai: a hundred thirty-eight.€€ƒxXThe Nathinites: the children of Soha, the children of Hasupha, the children of Tebbaoth,€€ƒxŒThe children of Ceros, the children os Siaa, the children of Phadon, the children of Lebana, the children of Hagaba, the children of Selmai,€€ƒxEThe children of Hanan, the children of Geddel, the children of Gaher,€€ƒxEThe children of Raaia, the children of Rasin, the children of Necoda,€€ƒxCThe children of Gezem, the children of Asa, the children of Phasea,€€ƒxHThe children of Besai, the children of Munim, the children of Nephussim,€€ƒxHThe children of Bacbuc, the children of Hacupha, the children of Harhur,€€ƒxGThe children of Besloth, the children of Mahida, the children of Harsa,€€ƒxFThe children of Bercos, the children of Sisara, the children of Thema,€€ƒx/The children of Nasia, the children of Hatipha,€€ƒxtThe children of the servants of Solomon, the children of Sothai, the children of Sophereth, the children of Pharida,€€ƒxGThe children of Jahala, the children of Darcon, the children of Jeddel,€€ƒxuThe children of Saphatia, the children of Hatil, the children of Phochereth, who was born of Sabaim, the son of Amon.€€ƒxZAll the Nathinites, and the children of the servants of Solomon, three hundred ninety-two.€€ƒx®And these are they that came up from Telmela, Thelharsa, Cherub, Addon, and Emmer: and could not shew the house of their fathers, nor their seed, whether they were of Israel.€€ƒx]The children of Dalaia, the children of Tobia, the children of Necoda, six hundred forty-two.€€ƒxºAnd of the priests, the children of Habia, the children of Accos, the children of Berzellai, who took a wife of the daughters of Berzellai the Galaadite, and he was called by their name.€€ƒxeThese sought their writing in the record, and found it not: and they were cast out of the priesthood.€€ƒxAnd Athersatha said to them, that they should not eat of the holies of holies, until there stood up a priest learned and skilful.€€ƒxMAll the multitude as it were one man, forty-two thousand three hundred sixty,€€ƒx¦Beside their menservants and womenservants, who were seven thousand three hundred thirty-seven: and among them singing men, and singing women, two hundred forty-five.€€ƒxKTheir horses, seven hundred thirty-six: their mules two hundred forty-five.€€ƒx[Their camels, four hundred thirty-five, their asses, six thousand seven hundred and twenty.€€ƒx´And some of the heads of the families gave unto the work. Athersatha gave into the treasure a thousand drams of gold, fifty bowls, and five hundred and thirty garments for priests.€€ƒx‘And some of the heads of families gave to the treasure of the work, twenty thousand drams of gold, and two thousand two hundred pounds of silver.€€ƒx—And that which the rest of the people gave, was twenty thousand drams of gold, and two thousand pounds of silver, and sixty-seven garments for priests.€€ƒx¤And the priests, and the Levites, and the porters, and the singing men, and the rest of the common people, and the Nathinites, and all Israel dwelt in their cities.€€ƒ`€€ƒy#And the seventh month came: and the children of Israel were in their cities. And all the people were gathered together as one man to the street which is before the water gate, and they spoke to Esdras the scribe, to bring the book of the law of Moses, which the Lord had commanded to Israel.€€ƒx™Then Esdras the priest brought the law before the multitude of men and women, and all those that could understand, in the first day of the seventh month.€€ƒxâAnd he read it plainly in the street that was before the water gate, from the morning until midday, before the men, and the women, and all those that could understand: and the ears of all the people were attentive to the book.€€ƒyAnd Esdras the scribe stood upon a step of wood, which he had made to speak upon, and there stood by him Mathathias, and Semeia, and Ania, and Uria, and Helcia, and Maasia, on his right hand: and on the left, Phadaia, Misael, and Melchia, and Hasum, and Hasbadana, Zacharia and Mosollam.€€ƒxƒAnd Esdras opened the book before all the people: for he was above all the people: and when he had opened it, all the people stood.€€ƒx¯And Esdras blessed the Lord the great God: and all the people answered, Amen, amen: lifting up their hands: and they bowed down, and adored God with their faces to the ground.€€’ƒxzAnd they read in the book of the law of God distinctly and plainly to be understood: and they understood when it was read.€€ƒxùAnd Nehemiah (he is Athersatha) and Esdras the priest and scribe, and the Levites who interpreted to all the people, said: This is a holy day to the Lord our God: do not mourn, nor weep: for all the people wept, when they heard the words of the law.€€ƒxßAnd he said to them: Go, eat fat meats, and drink sweet wine, and send portions to them that have not prepared for themselves: because it is the holy day of the Lord, and be not sad: for the joy of the Lord is our strength.€€ƒxkAnd the Levites stilled all the people, saying: Hold your peace, for the day is holy, and be not sorrowful.€€ƒx’So all the people went to eat and drink, and to send portions, and to make great mirth: because they understood the words that he had taught them.€€ƒxÄAnd on the second day the chiefs of the families of all the people, the priests, and the Levites were gathered together to Esdras the scribe, that he should interpret to them the words of the law.€€ƒx±And they found written in the law, that the Lord had commanded by the hand of Moses, that the children of Israel should dwell in tabernacles, on the feast, in the seventh month:€€ƒy#And that they should proclaim and publish the word in all their cities, and in Jerusalem, saying: Go forth to the mount, and fetch branches of olive, and branches of beautiful wood, branches of myrtle, and branches of palm, and branches of thick trees, to make tabernacles, as it is written.€€ƒxúAnd the people went forth, and brought. And they made themselves tabernacles every man on the top of his house, and in their courts, and in the courts of the house of God, and in the street of the water gate, and in the street of the gate of Ephraim.€€ƒxõAnd all the assembly of them that were returned from the captivity, made tabernacles, and dwelt in tabernacles: for since the days of Josue the son of Nun the children of Israel had not done so, until that day: and there was exceeding great joy.€€ƒxÀAnd he read in the book of the law of God day by day, from the first day till the last, and they kept the solemnity seven days, and in the eighth day a solemn assembly according to the manner.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‰And in the four and twentieth day of the month the children of Israel came together with fasting and with sackcloth, and earth upon them.€€ƒxŸAnd the seed of the children of Israel separated themselves from every stranger: and they stood, and confessed their sins, and the iniquities of their fathers.€€ƒx«And they rose up to stand: and they read in the book of the law of the Lord their God, four times in the day, and four times they confessed, and adored the Lord their God.€€ƒx´And there stood up upon the step of the Levites, Josue, and Bani, and Cedmihel, Sabania, Bonni, Sarebias, Bani, and Chanani: and they cried with a loud voice to the Lord their God.€€ƒxãAnd the Levites Josue and Cedmihel, Bonni, Hasebnia, Serebia, Oduia, Sebnia, and Phathahia, said: Arise, bless the Lord your God from eternity to eternity: and blessed be the high name of thy glory with all blessing and praise.€€ƒyThou thyself, O Lord alone, thou hast made heaven, and the heaven of heavens, and all the host thereof: the earth and all things that are in it: the seas and all that are therein: and thou givest life to all these things, and the host of heaven adoreth thee.€€˜&ƒy9And thou didst find his heart faithful before thee: and thou madest a covenant with him, to give him the land of the Chanaanite, of the Hethite, and of the Amorrhite, and of the Pherezite, and of the Jebusite, and of the Gergezite, to give it to his seed: and thou hast fulfilled thy words, because thou art just.€€ƒxeAnd thou sawest the affliction of our fathers in Egypt: and thou didst hear their cry by the Red Sea.€€ƒxÙAnd thou shewedst signs and wonders upon Pharao, and upon all his servants, and upon the people of his land: for thou knewest that they dealt proudly against them: and thou madest thyself a name, as it is at this day.€€ƒx·And thou didst divide the sea before them, and they passed through the midst of the sea on dry land: but their persecutors thou threwest into the depth, as a stone into mighty waters.€€ƒx‹And in a pillar of a cloud thou wast their leader by day, and in a pillar of fire by night, that they might see the way by which they went.€€ƒx§Thou camest down also to mount Sinai, and didst speak with them from heaven, and thou gavest them right judgments, and the law of truth, ceremonies, and good precepts.€€ƒx“Thou madest known to them thy holy sabbath, and didst prescribe to them commandments, and ceremonies, and the law by the hand of Moses thy servant.€€ƒxüAnd thou gavest them bread from heaven in their hunger, and broughtest forth water for them out of the rock in their thirst, and thou saidst to them that they should go in, and possess the land, upon which thou hadst lifted up thy hand to give it them.€€ƒxgBut they and our fathers dealt proudly, and hardened their necks and hearkened not to thy commandments.€€ƒy5And they would not hear, and they remembered not thy wonders which thou hadst done for them. And they hardened their necks, and gave the head to return to their bondage, as it were by contention. But thou, a forgiving God, gracious, and merciful, longsuffering, and full of compassion, didst not forsake them.€€ƒxœYea when they had made also to themselves a molten calf, and had said: This is thy God, that brought thee out of Egypt: and had committed great blasphemies:€€ƒxßYet thou, in thy many mercies, didst not leave them in the desert: the pillar of the cloud departed not from them by day to lead them in the way, and the pillar of fire by night to shew them the way by which they should go.€€ƒx˜And thou gavest them thy good Spirit to teach them, and thy manna thou didst not withhold from their mouth, and thou gavest them water for their thirst.€€ƒxForty years didst thou feed them in the desert, and nothing was wanting to them: their garments did not grow old, and their feet were not worn.€€ƒx¸And thou gavest them kingdoms, and nations, and didst divide lots for them: and they possessed the land of Sehon, and the land of the king of Hesebon, and the land of Og king of Basan.€€ƒx¸And thou didst multiply their children as the stars of heaven, and broughtest them to the land concerning which thou hadst said to their fathers, that they should go in and possess it.€€ƒxşAnd the children came and possessed the land, and thou didst humble before them the inhabitants of the land, the Chanaanites, and gavest them into their hands, with their kings, and the people of the land, that they might do with them as it pleased them.€€ƒyAnd they took strong cities and a fat land, and possessed houses full of all goods: cisterns made by others, vineyards, and oliveyards, and fruit trees in abundance: and they ate, and were filled, and became fat, and abounded with delight in thy great goodness.€€ƒxØBut they provoked thee to wrath, and departed from thee, and threw thy law behind their backs: and they killed thy prophets, who admonished them earnestly to return to thee: and they were guilty of great blasphemies.€€ƒy$And thou gavest them into the hands of their enemies, and they afflicted them. And in the time of their tribulation they cried to thee, and thou heardest from heaven, and according to the multitude of thy tender mercies thou gavest them saviours, to save them from the hands of their enemies.€€ƒy
But after they had rest, they returned to do evil in thy sight: and thou leftest them in the hand of their enemies, and they had dominion over them. Then they returned, and cried to thee: and thou heardest from heaven, and deliveredst them many times in thy mercies.€€ƒy
And thou didst admonish them to return to thy law. But they dealt proudly, and hearkened not to thy commandments, but sinned against thy judgments, which if a man do, he shall live in them: and they withdrew the shoulder, and hardened their neck, and would not hear.€€ƒxÔAnd thou didst forbear with them for many years, and didst testify against them by thy spirit by the hand of thy prophets: and they heard not, and thou didst deliver them into the hand of the people of the lands.€€ƒxYet in thy very many mercies thou didst not utterly consume them, nor forsake them: because thou art a merciful and gracious God.€€ƒy3Now therefore our God, great, strong, and terrible, who keepest covenant and mercy, turn not away from thy face all the labour which hath come upon us, upon our kings, and our princes, and our priests, and our prophets, and our fathers, and all the people from the days of the king of Assur, until this day.€€ƒxpAnd thou art just in all things that have come upon us: because thou hast done truth, but we have done wickedly.€€ƒx«Our kings, our princes, our priests, and our fathers have not kept thy law, and have not minded thy commandments, and thy testimonies which thou hast testified among them.€€ƒxİAnd they have not served thee in their kingdoms, and in thy manifold goodness, which thou gavest them, and in the large and fat land, which thou deliveredst before them, nor did they return from their most wicked devices.€€ƒx²Behold we ourselves this day are bondmen: and the land, which thou gavest our fathers, to eat the bread thereof, and the good things thereof, and we ourselves are servants in it.€€ƒxÍAnd the fruits thereof grow up for the kings, whom thou hast set over us for our sins, and they have dominion over our bodies, and over our beasts, according to their will, and we are in great tribulation.€€ƒxzAnd because of all this we ourselves make a covenant, and write it, and our princes, our Levites, and our priests sign it.€€ƒ`€€ƒxPAnd the subscribers were Nehemiah, Athersatha the son of Hachelai, and Sedecias,€€ƒxSaraias, Azarias, Jeremias,€€ƒxPheshur, Amarias, Melchias,€€ƒxHattus, Sebenia, Melluch,€€ƒxHarem, Merimuth, Obdias,€€ƒxDaniel, Genthon, Baruch,€€˜'ƒx+Maazia, Belgia, Semeia: these were priests.€€ƒxSAnd the Levites, Josue the son of Azanias, Bennui of the sons of Henadad, Cedmihel,€€ƒx;And their brethren, Sebenia, Oduia, Celita, Phalaia, Hanan,€€ƒvMicha, Rohob, Hasebia,€€ƒxZachur, Serebia, Sabania,€€ƒtOdaia, Bani, Baninu.€€ƒxAThe heads of the people, Pharos, Phahath Moab, Elam, Zethu, Bani,€€ƒtBonni, Azgad, Bebai,€€ƒuAdonia, Begoai, Adin,€€ƒtAter, Hezecia, Azur,€€ƒtOdaia, Hasum, Besai,€€ƒxHareph, Anathoth, Nebai,€€ƒxMegphias, Mosollam, Hazir,€€ƒxMesizabel, Sadoc, Jeddua,€€ƒvPheltia, Hanan, Anaia,€€ƒuOsee, Hanania, Hasub,€€ƒvAlohes, Phalea, Sobec,€€ƒwRehum, Hasebna, Maasia,€€ƒtEchaia, Hanan, Anan,€€ƒvMelluch, Haran, Baana:€€ƒx×And the rest of the people, priests, Levites, porters, and singing men, Nathinites, and all that had separated themselves from the people of the lands to the law of God, their wives, their sons, and their daughters.€€ƒy=All that could understand, promising for their brethren, with their chief men, and they came to promise, and swear that they would walk in the law of God, which he gave in the hand of Moses the servant of God, that they would do and keep all the commandments of the Lord our God, and his judgments and his ceremonies.€€ƒxjAnd that we would not give our daughters to the people of the land, nor take their daughters for our sons.€€ƒx÷And if the people of the land bring in things to sell, or any things for use, to sell them on the sabbath day, that we would not buy them on the sabbath, or on the holy day. And that we would leave the seventh year, and the exaction of every hand.€€ƒxxAnd we made ordinances for ourselves, to give the third part of a sicle every year for the work of the house of our God,€€ƒyFor the loaves of proposition, and for the continual sacrifice, and for a continual holocaust on the sabbaths, on the new moons, on the set feasts, and for the holy things, and for the sin offering: that atonement might be made for Israel, and for every use of the house of our God.€€ƒy And we cast lots among the priests, and the Levites, and the people for the offering of wood, that it might be brought into the house of our God by the houses of our fathers at set times, from year to year: to burn upon the altar of the Lord our God, as it is written in the law of Moses:€€ƒx‘And that we would bring the firstfruits of our land, and the firstfruits of all fruit of every tree, from year to year, in the house of our Lord.€€ƒxÜAnd the firstborn of our sons, and of our cattle, as it is written in the law, and the firstlings of our oxen, and of our sheep, to be offered in the house of our God, to the priests who minister in the house of our God.€€ƒy0And that we would bring the firstfruits of our meats, and of our libations, and the fruit of every tree, of the vintage also and of oil to the priests, to the storehouse of our God, and the tithes of our ground to the Levites. The Levites also shall receive the tithes of our works out of all the cities.€€ƒxĞAnd the priest the son of Aaron shall be with the Levites in the tithes of the Levites, and the Levites shall offer the tithe of their tithes in the house of our God, to the storeroom into the treasure house.€€ƒyFor the children of Israel and the children of Levi shall carry to the treasury the firstfruits of corn, of wine, and of oil: and the sanctified vessels shall be there, and the priests, and the singing men, and the porters, and ministers, and we will not forsake the house of our God.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¸And the princes of the people dwelt at Jerusalem: but the rest of the people cast lots, to take one part in ten to dwell in Jerusalem the holy city, and nine parts in the other cities.€€ƒx[And the people blessed all the men that willingly offered themselves to dwell in Jerusalem.€€ƒxüThese therefore are the chief men of the province, who dwelt in Jerusalem, and in the cities of Juda. And every one dwelt in his possession, in their cities: Israel, the priests, the Levites, the Nathinites, and the children of the servants of Solomon.€€ƒxÿAnd in Jerusalem there dwelt some of the children of Juda, and some of the children of Benjamin: of the children of Juda, Athaias the son of Aziam, the son of Zacharias, the son of Amarias, the son of Saphatias, the son of Malaleel: of the sons of Phares,€€ƒx•Maasia the son of Baruch, the son of Cholhoza, the son of Hazia, the son of Adaia, the son of Joiarib, the son of Zacharias, the son of the Silonite:€€ƒx`All these the sons of Phares, who dwelt in Jerusalem, were four hundred sixty-eight valiant men.€€˜$ƒx8And after him Gebbai, Sellai, nine hundred twenty-eight.€€ƒx\And Joel the son of Zechri their ruler, and Judas the son of Senua was second over the city.€€ƒx3And of the priests Idaia the son of Joarib, Jachin,€€ƒx‰Saraia the son of Helcias, the son of Mosollam, the son of Sadoc, the son of Meraioth, the son of Achitob the prince of the house of God,€€ƒxĞAnd their brethren that do the works of the temple: eight hundred twenty-two. And Adaia the son of Jeroham, the son of Phelelia, the son of Amsi, the son of Zacharias, the son of Pheshur, the son of Melchias,€€ƒxAnd his brethren the chiefs of the fathers: two hundred forty-two. And Amassai the son of Azreel, the son of Ahazi, the son of Mosollamoth, the son of Emmer,€€ƒxkAnd their brethren who were very mighty, a hundred twenty-eight: and their ruler Zabdiel son of the mighty.€€ƒxeAnd of the Levites Semeia the son of Hasub, the son of Azaricam, the son of Hasabia, the son of Boni,€€ƒxtAnd Sabathai and Jozabed, who were over all the outward business of the house of God, of the princes of the Levites,€€ƒxòAnd Mathania the son of Micha, the son of Zebedei, the son of Asaph, was the principal man to praise, and to give glory in prayer, and Becbecia, the second, one of his brethren, and Abda the son of Samua, the son of Galal, the son of Idithun.€€ƒx>All the Levites in the holy city were two hundred eighty-four.€€ƒx^And the porters, Accub, Telmon, and their brethren, who kept the doors: a hundred seventy-two.€€ƒxpAnd the rest of Israel, the priests and the Levites were in all the cities of Juda, every man in his possession.€€ƒxQAnd the Nathinites, that dwelt in Ophel, and Siaha, and Gaspha of the Nathinites.€€ƒxÒAnd the overseer of the Levites in Jerusalem, was Azzi the son of Bani, the son of Hasabia, the son of Mathania, the son of Micha. Of the sons of Asaph, were the singing men in the ministry of the house of God.€€ƒx^For the king's commandment was concerning them, and an order among the singing men day by day.€€ƒxAnd Phathahia the son of Mesezebel of the children of Zara the son of Juda was at the hand of the king, in all matters concerning the people,€€ƒx×And in the houses through all their countries. Of the children of Juda some dwelt at Cariath-Arbe, and in the villages thereof: and at Dibon, and in the villages thereof: and at Cabseel, and in the villages thereof.€€ƒx0And at Jesue, and at Molada, and at Bethphaleth,€€ƒx?And at Hasersuel, and at Bersabee, and in the villages thereof,€€ƒx<And at Siceleg, and at Mochona, and in the villages thereof,€€ƒx-And at Remmon, and at Saraa, and at Jerimuth,€€ƒx¤Zanoa, Odollam, and in their villages, at Lachis and its dependencies, and at Azeca and the villages thereof. And they dwelt from Bersabee unto the valley of Ennom.€€ƒxlAnd the children of Benjamin, from Geba, at Mechmas, and at Hai, and at Bethel, and in the villages thereof,€€ƒxAt Anathoth, Nob, Anania,€€ƒtAsor, Rama, Gethaim,€€ƒx!Hadid, Seboim, and Neballat, Lod,€€ƒx And Ono the valley of craftsmen.€€ƒx6And of the Levites were portions of Juda and Benjamin.€€ƒ`€€ƒxNow these are the priests and the Levites, that went up with Zorobabel the son of Salathiel, and Josue: Saraia, Jeremias, Esdras,€€ƒxAmaria, Melluch, Hattus,€€ƒxSebenias, Rheum, Merimuth,€€ƒtAddo, Genthon, Abia,€€ƒuMiamin, Madia, Belga,€€ƒx1Semeia, and Joiarib, Idaia, Sellum Amoc, Helcias,€€˜/ƒxpAnd the Levites, Jesua, Bennui, Cedmihel, Sarebia, Juda, Mathanias, they and their brethren were over the hymns:€€ƒxDAnd Becbecia, and Hanni, and their brethren every one in his office.€€ƒxKAnd Josue begot Joacim, and Joacim begot Eliasib, and Eliasib begot Joiada,€€ƒx4And Joiada begot Jonathan and Jonathan begot Jeddoa.€€ƒxnAnd in the days of Joacim the priests and heads of the families were: Of Saraia, Maraia: of Jeremias, Hanania:€€ƒx,Of Esdras, Mosollam: and of Amaria, Johanan:€€ƒx)Of Milicho, Jonathan: of Sebenia, Joseph:€€ƒx#Of Haram, Edna: of Maraioth, Helci:€€ƒx)Of Adaia, Zacharia: of Genthon, Mosollam:€€ƒx.Of Abia, Zechri: of Miamin and Moadia, Phelti:€€ƒx%Of Belga, Sammua of Semaia, Jonathan:€€ƒx&Of Joiarib, Mathanai: of Jodaia, Azzi:€€ƒx!Of Sellai, Celai: of Amoc, Heber:€€ƒx)Of Helcias, Hasebia: of Idaia, Nathanael.€€ƒx¦The Levites the chiefs of the families in the days of Eliasib, and Joiada, and Johanan, and Jeddoa, were recorded, and the priests in the reign of Darius the Persian.€€ƒx‚The sons of Levi, heads of the families were written in the book of Chronicles, even unto the days of Jonathan the son of Eliasib.€€ƒxèNow the chief of the Levites were Hasebia, Serebia, and Josue the son of Cedmihel: and their brethren by their courses, to praise and to give thanks according to the commandment of David the man of God, and to wait equally in order.€€ƒx}Mathania, and Becbecia, Obedia, and Mosollam, Telmon, Accub, were keepers of the gates and of the entrances before the gates.€€ƒx•These were in the days of Joacim the son of Josue, the son of Josedec, and in the days of Nehemiah the governor, and of Esdras the priest and scribe.€€ƒx÷And at the dedication of the wall of Jerusalem they sought the Levites out of all their places, to bring them to Jerusalem, and to keep the dedication, and to rejoice with thanksgiving, and with singing, and with cymbals, and psalteries and harps.€€ƒx‡And the sons of the singing men were gathered together out of the plain country about Jerusalem, and out of the villages of Nethuphati,€€ƒx“And from the house of Galgal, and from the countries of Geba and Azmaveth: for the singing men had built themselves villages round about Jerusalem.€€ƒxiAnd the priests and the Levites were purified, and they purified the people, and the gates, and the wall.€€ƒx¨And I made the princes of Juda go up upon the wall, and I appointed two great choirs to give praise. And they went on the right hand upon the wall toward the dung gate.€€ƒx<And after them went Osaias, and half of the princes of Juda,€€ƒxQAnd Azarias, Esdras, and Mosollam, Judas, and Benjamin, and Semeia, and Jeremias.€€ƒx­And of the sons of the priests with trumpets, Zacharias the son of Jonathan, the son of Semeia, the son of Mathania, the son of Michaia, the son of Zechur, the son of Asaph,€€ƒxÍAnd his brethren Semeia, and Azareel, Malalai, Galalai, Maai, Nathanael, and Judas, and Hanani, with the musical instruments of David the man of God: and Esdras the scribe before them at the fountain gate.€€ƒx™And they went up over against them by the stairs of the city of David, at the going up of the wall of the house of David, and to the water gate eastward:€€ƒxÀAnd the second choir of them that gave thanks went on the opposite side, and I after them, and the half of the people upon the wall, and upon the tower of the furnaces, even to the broad wall,€€ƒxÅAnd above the gate of Ephraim, and above the old gate, and above the fish gate and the tower of Hananeel, and the tower of Emath, and even to the flock gate: and they stood still in the watch gate.€€ƒx{And the two choirs of them that gave praise stood still at the house of God, and I and the half of the magistrates with me.€€ƒx]And the priests, Eliachim, Maasia, Miamin, Michea, Elioenai, Zacharia, Hanania with trumpets,€€ƒx—And Maasia, and Semeia, and Eleazar, and Azzi, and Johanan, and Melchia, and Elam, and Ezer. And the singers sung loud, and Jezraia was their overseer:€€ƒxÌAnd they sacrificed on that day great sacrifices, and they rejoiced: for God had made them joyful with great joy: their wives also and their children rejoiced, and the joy of Jerusalem was heard afar off.€€ƒy7They appointed also in that day men over the storehouses of the treasure, for the libations, and for the firstfruits, and for the tithes, that the rulers of the city might bring them in by them in honour of thanksgiving, for the priests and Levites: for Juda was joyful in the priests and Levites that assisted.€€ƒx«And they kept the watch of their God, and the observance of expiation, and the singing men, and the porters, according to the commandment of David, and of Solomon his son.€€ƒx‹For in the days of David and Asaph from the beginning there were chief singers appointed, to praise with canticles, and give thanks to God.€€ƒxÖAnd all Israel, in the days of Zorobabel, and in the days of Nehemiah gave portions to the singing men, and to the porters, day by day, and they sanctified the Levites, and the Levites sanctified the sons of Aaron.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÁAnd on that day they read in the book of Moses in the hearing of the people: and therein was found written, that the Ammonites and the Moabites should not come in to the church of God for ever:€€ƒx Because they met not the children of Israel with bread and water: and they hired against them Balaam, to curse them, and our God turned the curse into blessing.€€ƒxaAnd it came to pass, when they had heard the law, that they separated every stranger from Israel.€€ƒxAnd over this thing was Eliasib the priest, who was set over the treasury of the house of our God, and was near akin to Tobias.€€ƒyAnd he made him a great storeroom, where before him they laid up gifts, and frankincense, and vessels, and the tithes of the corn, of the wine, and of the oil, the portions of the Levites, and of the singing men, and of the porters, and the firstfruits of the priests.€€ƒx®But in all this time I was not in Jerusalem, because in the two and thirtieth year of Artaxerxes king of Babylon, I went to the king, and after certain days I asked the king:€€ƒxAnd I came to Jerusalem, and I understood the evil that Eliasib had done for Tobias, to make him a storehouse in the courts of the house of God.€€˜ƒxmAnd I commanded and they cleansed again the vessels of the house of God, the sacrifice, and the frankincense.€€ƒxºAnd I perceived that the portions of the Levites had not been given them: and that the Levites, and the singing men, and they that ministered were fled away every man to his own country:€€ƒxªAnd I pleaded the matter against the magistrates, and said: Why have we forsaken the house of God? And I gathered them together, and I made them to stand in their places.€€ƒx[And all Juda brought the tithe of the corn, and the wine, and the oil into the storehouses.€€ƒyAnd we set over the storehouses Selemias the priest, and Sadoc the scribe, and of the Levites Phadaia, and next to them Hanan the son of Zachur, the son of Mathania: for they were approved as faithful, and to them were committed the portions of their brethren.€€ƒxŒRemember me, O my God, for this thing, and wipe not out my kindnesses, which I have done relating to the house of my God and his ceremonies.€€ƒy0In those days I saw in Juda some treading the presses on the sabbath, and carrying sheaves, and lading asses with wine, and grapes, and figs, and all manner of burthens, and bringing them into Jerusalem on the sabbath day. And I charged them that they should sell on a day on which it was lawful to sell.€€ƒx’Some Tyrians also dwelt there, who brought fish, and all manner of wares: and they sold them on the sabbaths to the children of Juda in Jerusalem.€€ƒx}And I rebuked the chief men of Juda, and said to them: What is this evil thing that you are doing, profaning the sabbath day:€€ƒx¢Did not our fathers do these things, and our God brought all this evil upon us, and upon this city? And you bring more wrath upon Israel by violating the sabbath.€€ƒy"And it came to pass, that when the gates of Jerusalem were at rest on the sabbath day, I spoke: and they shut the gates, and I commanded that they should not open them till after the sabbath: and I set some of my servants at the gates, that none should bring in burthens on the sabbath day.€€ƒxaSo the merchants, and they that sold all kinds of wares, stayed without Jerusalem, once or twice.€€ƒx®And I charged them, and I said to them: Why stay you before the wall? if you do so another time, I will lay hands on you. And from that time they came no more on the sabbath.€€ƒxáI spoke also to the Levites that they should be purified, and should come to keep the gates, and to sanctify the sabbath day: for this also remember me, O my God, and spare me according to the multitude of thy tender mercies.€€ƒx]In those days also I saw Jews that married wives, women of Azotus, and of Ammon, and of Moab.€€ƒx And their children spoke half in the speech of Azotus, and could not speak the Jews' language, but they spoke according to the language of this and that people.€€ƒxùAnd I chid them, and laid my curse upon them. And I beat some of them, and shaved off their hair, and made them swear by God that they would not give their daughters to their sons, nor take their daughters for their sons, nor for themselves, saying:€€ƒx÷Did not Solomon king of Israel sin in this kind of thing: and surely among many nations, there was not a king like him, and he was beloved of his God, and God made him king over all Israel: and yet women of other countries brought even him to sin.€€ƒxsAnd shall we also be disobedient and do all this great evil to transgress against our God, and marry strange women:€€ƒx…And one of the sons of Joiada the son of Eliasib the high priest, was son in law to Sanaballat the Horonite, and I drove him from me.€€ƒx]Remember them, O Lord my God, that defile the priesthood, and the law of priests and Levites.€€ƒx~So I separated from them all strangers, and I appointed the courses of the priests and the Levites, every man in his ministry:€€ƒxqAnd for the offering of wood at times appointed, and for the firstfruits: remember me, O my God, unto good. Amen.€€ƒqThe Book of Tobit€€ƒ`€€ƒx·Tobias of the tribe and city of Nephtali, (which is in the upper parts of Galilee above Naasson, beyond the way that leadeth to the west, having on the right hand the city of Sephet,)€€ƒx~When he was made captive in the days of Salmanasar king of the Assyrians, even in his captivity, forsook not the way of truth,€€ƒxbBut every day gave all he could get to his brethren his fellow captives, that were of his kindred.€€ƒxdAnd when he was younger than any of the tribe of Nephtali, yet did he no childish thing in his work.€€ƒxuMoreover when all went to the golden calves which Jeroboam king of Israel had made, he alone fled the company of all,€€ƒx’And went to Jerusalem to the temple of the Lord, and there adored the Lord God of Israel, offering faithfully all his firstfruits, and his tithes,€€dEsth¤dnamefEstheriosis_namedEsthlabbreviationdEsthhchaptersŠ–ƒxvBut when he was a man, he took to wife Anna of his own tribe, and had a son by her, whom he called after his own name,€€ƒxLAnd from his infancy he taught him to fear God, and to abstain from all sin.€€ƒxhAnd when by the captivity he with his wife and his son and all his tribe was come to the city of Ninive,€€ƒxd(When all ate of the meats of the Gentiles) he kept his soul and never was defiled with their meats.€€ƒxsAnd because he was mindful of the Lord with all his heart, God gave him favour in the sight of Salmanasar the king.€€ƒx^And he gave him leave to go whithersoever he would, with liberty to do whatever he had a mind.€€ƒxUHe therefore went to all that were in captivity, and gave them wholesome admonitions.€€ƒx…And when he was come to Rages a city of the Medes, and had ten talents of silver of that with which he had been honoured by the king:€€ƒx¦And when amongst a great multitude of his kindred, he saw Gabelus in want, who was one of his tribe, taking a note of his hand he gave him the aforesaid sum of money.€€ƒx“But after a long time, Salmanasar the king being dead, when Sennacherib his son, who reigned in his place, had a hatred for the children of Israel:€€ƒxzTobias daily went among all his kindred and comforted them, and distributed to every one as he was able, out of his goods:€€ƒxmHe fed the hungry, and gave clothes to the naked, and was careful to bury the dead, and they that were slain.€€ƒxÖAnd when king Sennacherib was come back, fleeing from Judea by reason of the slaughter that God had made about him for his blasphemy, and being angry slew many of the children of Israel, Tobias buried their bodies.€€ƒx]But when it was told the king, he commanded him to be slain, and took away all his substance.€€ƒx`But Tobias fleeing naked away with his son and with his wife, lay concealed, for many loved him.€€ƒx?But after forty-five days, the king was killed by his own sons.€€ƒxLAnd Tobias returned to his house, and all his substance was restored to him.€€ƒ`€€ƒxhBut after this, when there was a festival of the Lord, and a good dinner was prepared in Tobias's house,€€ƒxTHe said to his son: Go, and bring some of our tribe that fear God, to feast with us.€€ƒxÓAnd when he had gone, returning he told him, that one of the children of Israel lay slain in the street. And he forthwith leaped up from his place at the table, and left his dinner, and came fasting to the body.€€ƒxnAnd taking it up carried it privately to his house, that after the sun was down, he might bury him cautiously.€€—ƒx€Remembering the word which the Lord spoke by Amos the prophet: Your festival days shall be turned into lamentation and mourning.€€ƒx1So when the sun was down, he went and buried him.€€ƒxÑNow all his neighbours blamed him, saying: once already commandment was given for thee to be slain because of this matter, and thou didst scarce escape the sentence of death, and dost thou again bury the dead?€€ƒx’But Tobias fearing God more than the king, carried off the bodies of them that were slain, and hid them in his house, and at midnight buried them.€€ƒx{Now it happened one day that being wearied with burying, he came to his house, and cast himself down by the wall and slept,€€ƒxcAnd as he was sleeping, hot dung out of a swallow's nest fell upon his eyes, and he was made blind.€€ƒxNow this trial the Lord therefore permitted to happen to him, that an example might be given to posterity of his patience, as also of holy Job.€€ƒxœFor whereas he had always feared God from his infancy, and kept his commandments, he repined not against God because the evil of blindness had befallen him,€€ƒx[But continued immoveable in the fear of God, giving thanks to God all the days of his life.€€ƒxaFor as the kings insulted over holy Job: so his relations and kinsmen mocked at his life, saying:€€ƒxEWhere is thy hope, for which thou gavest alms, and buriedst the dead?€€ƒx.But Tobias rebuked them, saying: Speak not so:€€ƒx~For we are the children of saints, and look for that life which God will give to those that never change their faith from him.€€ƒx‚Now Anna his wife went daily to weaving work, and she brought home what she could get for their living by the labour of her hands.€€ƒxLWhereby it came to pass, that she received a young kid, and brought it home:€€ƒxÉAnd when her husband heard it bleating, he said: Take heed, lest perhaps it be stolen: restore ye it to its owners, for it is not lawful for us either to eat or to touch any thing that cometh by theft.€€ƒxqAt these words his wife being angry answered: It is evident the hope is come to nothing, and thy alms now appear.€€ƒx<And with these and other such like words, she upbraided him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx1Then Tobias sighed, and began to pray with tears,€€ƒxoSaying, Thou art just, O Lord, and all thy judgments are just, and all thy ways mercy, and truth, and judgment:€€ƒxuAnd now, O Lord, think of me, and take not revenge of my sins, neither remember my offences, nor those of my parents.€€ƒxÄFor we have not obeyed thy commandments, therefore are we delivered to spoil and to captivity, and death, and are made a fable, and a reproach to all nations, amongst which thou hast scattered us.€€ƒxAnd now, O Lord, do with me according to thy will, and command my spirit to be received in peace: for it is better for me to die, than to live.€€ƒx˜Now it happened on the same day, that Sara daughter of Raguel, in Rages a city of the Medes, received a reproach from one of her father's servant maids,€€ƒxzBecause she had been given to seven husbands and a devil named Asmodeus had killed them, at their first going in unto her.€€ƒxŸSo when she reproved the maid for her fault, she answered her, saying: May we never see son, or daughter of thee upon the earth, thou murderer of thy husbands.€€ƒx¿Wilt thou kill me also, as thou hast already killed seven husbands? At these words, she went into an upper chamber of her house: and for three days and three nights did neither eat nor drink:€€ƒx_But continuing in prayer with tears besought God, that he would deliver her from this reproach.€€ƒxaAnd it came to pass on the third day when she was making an end of her prayer, blessing the Lord,€€ƒx³She said: Blessed is thy name, O God of our fathers, who when thou hast been angry, wilt shew mercy, and in the time of tribulation forgivest the sins of them that call upon thee.€€ƒx:To thee, O Lord, I turn my face, to thee I direct my eyes.€€ƒxfI beg, O Lord, that thou loose me from the bond of this reproach, or else take me away from the earth.€€ƒx`Thou knowest, O Lord, that I never coveted a husband, and have kept my soul clean from all lust.€€ƒxuNever have I joined myself with them that play: neither have I made myself partaker with them that walk in lightness.€€ƒxCBut a husband I consented to take, with thy fear, not with my lust.€€ƒx|And either I was unworthy of them, or they perhaps were not worthy of me: because perhaps thou hast kept me for another man,€€ƒx&For thy counsel is not in man's power.€€‘ƒxFor thou art not delighted in our being lost, because after a storm thou makest a calm, and after tears and weeping thou pourest in joyfulness.€€ƒx/Be thy name, O God of Israel, blessed for ever,€€ƒx`At that time the prayers of them both were heard in the sight of the glory of the most high God:€€ƒx†And the holy angel of the Lord, Raphael was sent to heal them both, whose prayers at one time were rehearsed in the sight of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxkTherefore when Tobias thought that his prayer was heard that he might die, he called to him Tobias his son,€€ƒx`And said to him: Hear, my son, the words of my mouth, and lay them as a foundation in thy heart.€€ƒxpWhen God shall take my soul, thou shalt bury my body: and thou shalt honour thy mother all the days of her life:€€ƒxUFor thou must be mindful what and how great perils she suffered for thee in her womb.€€ƒxHAnd when she also shall have ended the time of her life, bury her by me.€€ƒxAnd all the days of thy life have God in thy mind: and take heed thou never consent to sin, nor transgress the commandments of the Lord our God.€€ƒx¦Give alms out of thy substance, and turn not away thy face from any poor person: for so it shall come to pass that the face of the Lord shall not be turned from thee.€€ƒx%According to thy ability be merciful.€€ƒxgIf thou have much give abundantly: if thou have little, take care even so to bestow willingly a little.€€ƒxKFor thus thou storest up to thyself a good reward for the day of necessity.€€ƒx`For alms deliver from all sin, and from death, and will not suffer the soul to go into darkness.€€ƒxTAlms shall be a great confidence before the most high God, to all them that give it.€€ƒxgNever suffer pride to reign in thy mind, or in thy words: for from it all perdition took its beginning.€€ƒx‡If any man hath done any work for thee, immediately pay him his hire, and let not the wages of thy hired servant stay with thee at all.€€ƒxTSee thou never do to another what thou wouldst hate to have done to thee by another.€€ƒxSEat thy bread with the hungry and the needy, and with thy garments cover the naked,€€ƒxpLay out thy bread, and thy wine upon the burial of a just man, and do not eat and drink thereof with the wicked.€€ƒx"Seek counsel always of a wise man.€€ƒxiBless God at all times: and desire of him to direct thy ways, and that all thy counsels may abide in him.€€ƒxªI tell thee also, my son, that I lent ten talents of silver, while thou wast yet a child, to Gabelus, in Rages a city of the Medes, and I have a note of his hand with me:€€ƒx†Now therefore inquire how thou mayst go to him, and receive of him the foresaid sum of money, and restore to him the note of his hand.€€ƒx”Fear not, my son: we lead indeed a poor life, but we shall have many good things if we fear God, and depart from all sin, and do that which is good.€€ƒ`€€ƒxfThen Tobias answered his father, and said: I will do all things, father, which thou hast commanded me.€€ƒx¥But how I shall get this money, I cannot tell; he knoweth not me, and I know not him: what token shall I give him? nor did I ever know the way which leadeth thither.€€ƒx„Then his father answered him, and said: I have a note of his hand with me, which when thou shalt shew him, he will presently pay it.€€ƒxdThen Tobias going forth, found a beautiful young man, standing girded, and as it were ready to walk.€€ƒxlAnd not knowing that he was an angel of God, he saluted him, and said: From whence art thou, good young man?€€ƒx‚But he answered: Of the children of Israel. And Tobias said to him: Knowest thou the way that leadeth to the country of the Medes?€€ƒxÒAnd he answered: I know it: and I have often walked through all the ways thereof, and I have abode with Gabelus our brother, who dwelleth at Rages a city of the Medes, which is situate in the mount of Ecbatana.€€ƒx`And Tobias said to him: Stay for me, I beseech thee, till I tell these same things to my father.€€ƒxŒThen Tobias going in told all these things to his father. Upon which his father being in admiration, desired that he would come in unto him.€€ƒx<So going in he saluted him, and said: Joy be to thee always.€€ƒxhAnd Tobias said: What manner of joy shall be to me, who sit in darkness and see not the light of heaven?€€ƒxPAnd the young man said to him: Be of good courage, thy cure from God is at hand.€€ƒx‘And Tobias said to him: Canst thou conduct my son to Gabelus at Rages, a city of the Medes? and when thou shalt return, I will pay thee thy hire.€€ƒxRAnd the angel said to him: I will conduct him thither, and bring him back to thee.€€ƒxUAnd Tobias said to him: I pray thee, tell me, of what family, or what tribe art thou?€€ƒx~And Raphael the angel answered: Dost thou seek the family of him thou hirest, or the hired servant himself to go with thy son?€€ƒxNBut lest I should make thee uneasy, I am Azarias the son of the great Ananias.€€ŠƒxVAnd the angel said to him: I will lead thy son safe, and bring him to thee again safe.€€ƒxvAnd Tobias answering, said: May you have a good journey, and God be with you in your way, and his angel accompany you.€€ƒx–Then all things being ready, that were to be carried in their journey, Tobias bade his father and his mother farewell, and they set out both together.€€ƒx‡And when they were departed, his mother began to weep, and to say: Thou hast taken the staff of our old age, and sent him away from us.€€ƒx>I wish the money for which thou hast sent him, had never been.€€ƒx_For our poverty was sufficient for us, that we might account it as riches, that we saw our son.€€ƒx{And Tobias said to her: Weep not, our son will arrive thither safe, and will return safe to us, and thy eyes shall see him.€€ƒxœFor I believe that the good angel of God doth accompany him, and doth order all things well that are done about him, so that he shall return to us with joy.€€ƒx=At these words his mother ceased weeping, and held her peace.€€ƒ`€€‘ƒxTAnd he went out to wash his feet, and behold a monstrous fish came up to devour him.€€ƒx\And Tobias being afraid of him, cried out with a loud voice, saying: Sir, he cometh upon me.€€ƒx¤And the angel said to him: Take him by the gill, and draw him to thee. And when he had done so, he drew him out upon the land, and he began to pant before his feet.€€ƒx©Then the angel said to him: Take out the entrails of this fish, and lay up his heart, and his gall, and his liver for thee: for these are necessary for useful medicines.€€ƒx¾And when he had done so, he roasted the flesh thereof, and they took it with them in the way: the rest they salted as much as might serve them, till they came to Rages the city of the Medes.€€ƒx¨Then Tobias asked the angel, and said to him: I beseech thee, brother Azarias, tell me what remedies are these things good for, which thou hast bid me keep of the fish?€€ƒxÏAnd the angel, answering, said to him: If thou put a little piece of its heart upon coals, the smoke thereof driveth away all kind of devils, either from man or from woman, so that they come no more to them.€€ƒxfAnd the gall is good for anointing the eyes, in which there is a white speck, and they shall be cured.€€ƒx6And Tobias said to him: Where wilt thou that we lodge?€€ƒx¶And the angel answering, said: Here is one whose name is Raguel, a near kinsman of thy tribe, and he hath a daughter named Sara, but he hath no son nor any other daughter beside her.€€ƒxAAll his substance is due to thee, and thou must take her to wife.€€ƒxCAsk her therefore of her father, and he will give her thee to wife.€€ƒx–Then Tobias answered, and said: I hear that she hath been given to seven husbands, and they all died: moreover I have heard, that a devil killed them.€€ƒx¤Now I am afraid, lest the same thing should happen to me also: and whereas I am the only child of my parents, I should bring down their old age with sorrow to hell.€€ƒxpThen the angel Raphael said to him: Hear me, and I will shew thee who they are, over whom the devil can prevail.€€ƒxâFor they who in such manner receive matrimony, as to shut out God from themselves, and from their mind, and to give themselves to their lust, as the horse and mule, which have not understanding, over them the devil hath power.€€ƒx¥But thou when thou shalt take her, go into the chamber, and for three days keep thyself continent from her, and give thyself to nothing else but to prayers with her.€€˜ ƒxTBut the second night thou shalt be admitted into the society of the holy Patriarchs.€€ƒxXAnd the third night thou shalt obtain a blessing that sound children may be born of you.€€ƒxÎAnd when the third night is past, thou shalt take the virgin with the fear of the Lord, moved rather for love of children than for lust, that in the seed of Abraham thou mayst obtain a blessing in children.€€ƒ`€€ƒx>And they went in to Raguel, and Raguel received them with joy.€€ƒx_And Raguel looking upon Tobias, said to Anna his wife: How like is this young man to my cousin?€€ƒxRAnd when he had spoken these words, he said: Whence are ye young men our brethren?€€ƒxKBut they said: We are of the tribe of Nephtali, of the captivity of Ninive.€€ƒxSAnd Raguel said to them: Do you know Tobias my brother? And they said: We know him.€€ƒxAnd when he was speaking many good things of him, the angel said to Raguel: Tobias concerning whom thou inquirest is this young man's father.€€ƒx­And Raguel went to him, and kissed him with tears and weeping upon his neck, said: A blessing be upon thee, my son, because thou art the son of a good and most virtuous man.€€ƒx0And Anna his wife, and Sara their daughter wept.€€ƒxAnd after they had spoken, Raguel commanded a sheep to be killed, and a feast to be prepared. And when he desired them to sit down to dinner,€€ƒx†Tobias said: I will not eat nor drink here this day, unless thou first grant me my petition, and promise to give me Sara thy daughter.€€ƒxøNow when Raguel heard this he was afraid, knowing what had happened to those seven husbands, that went in unto her: and he began to fear lest it might happen to him also in like manner: and as he was in suspense, and gave no answer to his petition,€€ƒx¢The angel said to him: Be not afraid to give her to this man, for to him who feareth God is thy daughter due to be his wife: therefore another could not have her.€€ƒxVThen Raguel said: I doubt not but God hath regarded my prayers and tears in his sight.€€ƒx½And I believe he hath therefore made you come to me, that this maid might be married to one of her own kindred, according to the law of Moses: and now doubt not but I will give her to thee.€€ƒxæAnd taking the right hand of his daughter, he gave it into the right hand of Tobias, saying: The God of Abraham, and the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob be with you, and may he join you together, and fulfil his blessing in you.€€ƒx5And taking paper they made a writing of the marriage.€€ƒx-And afterwards they made merry, blessing God.€€ƒxPAnd Raguel called to him Anna his wife, and bade her to prepare another chamber.€€ƒx;And she brought Sara her daughter in thither, and she wept.€€ƒxyAnd she said to her: Be of good cheer, my daughter: the Lord of heaven give thee joy for the trouble thou hast undergone.€€ƒ`€€ƒx@And after they had supped, they brought in the young man to her.€€ƒxoAnd Tobias remembering the angel's word, took out of his bag part of the liver, and laid it upon burning coals.€€ƒxRThen the angel Raphael took the devil, and bound him in the desert of upper Egypt.€€ƒxôThen Tobias exhorted the virgin, and said to her: Sara, arise, and let us pray to God to day, and to morrow, and the next day: because for these three nights we are joined to God: and when the third night is over, we will be in our own wedlock.€€ƒxfFor we are the children of saints, and we must not be joined together like heathens that know not God.€€ƒxWSo they both arose, and prayed earnestly both together that health might be given them.€€ƒx¬And Tobias said: Lord God of our fathers, may the heavens and the earth, and the sea, and the fountains, and the rivers, and all thy creatures that are in them, bless thee.€€ƒƒx«And now, Lord, thou knowest, that not for fleshly lust do I take my sister to wife, but only for the love of posterity, in which thy name may be blessed for ever and ever.€€ƒxhSara also said: Have mercy on us, O Lord, have mercy on us, and let us grow old both together in health.€€ƒxˆAnd it came to pass about the cockcrowing, Raguel ordered his servants to be called for, and they went with him together to dig a grave.€€cJob¤dnamecJobiosis_namecJoblabbreviationcJobhchapters˜*–ƒx[Send one of thy maids, and let her see if he be dead, that I may bury him before it be day.€€ƒxvSo she sent one of her maidservants, who went into the chamber, and found them safe and sound, sleeping both together.€€ƒxWAnd returning she brought the good news: and Raguel and Anna his wife blessed the Lord,€€ƒx\And said: We bless thee, O Lord God of Israel, because it hath not happened as we suspected.€€ƒx\For thou hast shewn thy mercy to us, and hast shut out from us the enemy that persecuted us.€€ƒxçAnd thou hast taken pity upon two only children. Make them, O Lord, bless thee more fully: and to offer up to thee a sacrifice of thy praise, and of their health, that all nations may know, that thou alone art God in all the earth.€€ƒxcAnd immediately Raguel commanded his servants, to fill up the pit they had made, before it was day.€€ƒxAnd he spoke to his wife to make ready a feast, and prepare all kind of provisions that are necessary for such as go a journey.€€ƒx…He caused also two fat kine, and four wethers to be killed, and a banquet to be prepared for all his neighbours, and all his friends.€€ƒx7And Raguel adjured Tobias, to abide with him two weeks.€€ƒx¥And of all things which Raguel possessed, he gave one half to Tobias, and made a writing, that the half that remained should after their decease come also to Tobias.€€ƒ`€€ƒxThen Tobias called the angel to him, whom he took to be a man, and said to him: Brother Azarias, I pray thee hearken to my words:€€ƒxYIf I should give myself to be thy servant I should not make a worthy return for thy care.€€ƒxàHowever, I beseech thee, to take with thee beasts and servants, and to go to Gabelus to Rages the city of the Medes: and to restore to him his note of hand, and receive of him the money, and desire him to come to my wedding.€€ƒxkFor thou knowest that my father numbereth the days: and if I stay one day more, his soul will be afflicted.€€ƒxRAnd indeed thou seest how Raguel hath adjured me, whose adjuring I cannot despise.€€ƒx¸Then Raphael took four of Raguel's servants, and two camels, and went to Rages the city of the Medes: and finding Gabelus, gave him his note of hand, and received of him all the money.€€ƒxwAnd he told him concerning Tobias the son of Tobias, all that had been done: and made him come with him to the wedding.€€ƒxŸAnd when he was come into Raguel's house he found Tobias sitting at the table: and he leaped up, and they kissed each other: and Gabelus wept, and blessed God,€€ƒx‡And said: The God of Israel bless thee, because thou art the son of a very good and just man, and that feareth God, and doth almsdeeds:€€ƒx<And may a blessing come upon thy wife and upon your parents.€€ƒx{And when all had said, Amen, they went to the feast: but the marriage feast they celebrated also with the fear of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx©But as Tobias made longer stay upon occasion of the marriage, Tobias his father was solicitous, saying: Why thinkest thou doth my son tarry, or why is he detained there?€€ƒxBIs Gabelus dead, thinkest thou, and no man will pay him the money?€€ƒxªAnd he began to be exceeding sad, both he and Anna his wife with him: and they began both to weep together, because their son did not return to them on the day appointed.€€ƒxçBut his mother wept and was quite disconsolate, and said: Woe, woe is me, my son; why did we send thee to go to a strange country, the light of our eyes, the staff of our old age, the comfort of our life, the hope of our posterity?€€ƒxSWe having all things together in thee alone, ought not to have let thee go from us.€€ƒx|And Tobias said to her: Hold thy peace, and be not troubled, our son is safe: that man with whom we sent him is very trusty.€€ƒxĞBut she could by no means be comforted, but daily running out looked round about, and went into all the ways by which there seemed any hope he might return, that she might if possible see him coming afar off.€€ƒxxBut Raguel said to his son in law: Stay here, and I will send a messenger to Tobias thy father, that thou art in health.€€ƒx‚And Tobias said to him: I know that my father and mother now count the days, and their spirit is grievously afflicted within them.€€ƒyAnd when Raguel had pressed Tobias with many words, and he by no means would hearken to him, he delivered Sara unto him, and half of all his substance in menservants, and womenservants, in cattle, in camels, and in kine, and in much money, and sent him away safe and joyful from him,€€ƒxÅSaying: The holy angel of the Lord be with you in your journey, and bring you through safe, and that you may find all things well about your parents, and my eyes may see your children before I die.€€˜ƒx¤Admonishing her to honour her father and mother in law, to love her husband, to take care of the family, to govern the house, and to behave herself irreprehensibly.€€ƒ`€€ƒxcAnd as they were returning they came to Charan, which is in the midway to Ninive, the eleventh day.€€ƒxQAnd the angel said: Brother Tobias, thou knowest how thou didst leave thy father.€€ƒx†If it please thee therefore, let us go before, and let the family follow softly after us, together with thy wife, and with the beasts.€€ƒx­And as this their going pleased him, Raphael said to Tobias: Take with thee of the gall of the fish, for it will be necessary. So Tobias took some of that gall and departed.€€ƒx\But Anna sat beside the way daily, on the top of a hill, from whence she might see afar off.€€ƒx¹And while she watched his coming from that place, she saw him afar off, and presently perceived it was her son coming: and returning she told her husband, saying: Behold thy son cometh.€€ƒx¢And Raphael said to Tobias: As soon as thou shalt come into thy house, forthwith adore the Lord thy God: and giving thanks to him, go to thy father, and kiss him.€€ƒxèAnd immediately anoint his eyes with this gall of the fish, which thou carriest with thee. For be assured that his eyes shall be presently opened, and thy father shall see the light of heaven, and shall rejoice in the sight of thee.€€ƒxœThen the dog, which had been with them in the way, ran before, and coming as if he had brought the news, shewed his joy by his fawning and wagging his tail.€€ƒx„And his father that was blind, rising up, began to run stumbling with his feet: and giving a servant his hand, went to meet his son.€€ƒxSAnd receiving him kissed him, as did also his wife, and they began to weep for joy.€€ƒxKAnd when they had adored God, and given him thanks, they sat down together.€€ƒxGThen Tobias taking of the gall of the fish, anointed his father's eyes.€€ƒxjAnd he stayed about half an hour: and a white skin began to come out of his eyes, like the skin of an egg.€€ƒxOAnd Tobias took hold of it, and drew it from his eyes, and recovered his sight.€€ƒxCAnd they glorified God, both he and his wife and all that knew him.€€ƒx‹And Tobias said: I bless thee, O Lord God of Israel, because thou hast chastised me, and thou hast saved me and behold I see Tobias my son.€€ƒxÈAnd after seven days Sara his son's wife and all the family arrived safe, and the cattle, and the camels, and an abundance of money of his wife's: and that money also which he had received of Gabelus,€€ƒxhAnd he told his parents all the benefits of God, which he had done to him by the man that conducted him.€€ƒx–And Achior and Nabath the kinsmen of Tobias came, rejoicing for Tobias, and congratulating with him for all the good things that God had done for him.€€ƒx@And for seven days they feasted and rejoiced all with great joy.€€ƒ`€€ƒxmThen Tobias called to him his son and said to him: What can we give to this holy man, that is come with thee?€€ƒxrTobias answering, said to his father: Father, what wages shall we give him? or what can be worthy of his benefits?€€•ƒxBut I beseech thee, my father, to desire him, that he would vouchsafe to accept of one half of all things that have been brought.€€ƒxSo the father and the son calling him, took him aside: and began to desire him that he would vouchsafe to accept of half of all things that they had brought,€€ƒx“Then he said to them secretly, Bless ye the God of heaven, give glory to him in the sight of all that live, because he hath shewn his mercy to you.€€ƒxTFor it is good to hide the secret of a king: to reveal and confess the works of God.€€ƒxKPrayer is good with fasting and alms more than to lay up treasures of gold.€€ƒx|For alms delivereth from death, and the same is that which purgeth away sins, and maketh to find mercy and life everlasting.€€ƒxEBut they that commit sin and iniquity, are enemies to their own soul.€€ƒxLI discover then the truth unto you, and I will not hide the secret from you.€€ƒx¶When thou didst pray with tears, and didst bury the dead, and didst leave thy dinner, and hide the dead by day in thy house, and bury them by night, I offered thy prayer to the Lord.€€ƒx\And because thou wast acceptable to God, it was necessary that temptation should prove thee.€€ƒx^And now the Lord hath sent me to heal thee, and to deliver Sara thy son's wife from the devil.€€ƒxHFor I am the angel Raphael, one of the seven, who stand before the Lord.€€ƒx}And when they had heard these things, they were troubled, and being seized with fear they fell upon the ground on their face.€€ƒx6And the angel said to them: Peace be to you, fear not.€€ƒx_For when I was with you, I was there by the will of God: bless ye him, and sing praises to him.€€ƒxpI seemed indeed to eat and to drink with you but I use an invisible meat and drink, which cannot be seen by men.€€ƒxnIt is time therefore that I return to him that sent me: but bless ye God, and publish all his wonderful works.€€ƒxaAnd when he had said these things, he was taken from their sight, and they could see him no more.€€ƒxyThen they lying prostrate for three hours upon their face, blessed God, and rising up, they told all his wonderful works.€€ƒ`€€ƒx†And Tobias the elder opening his mouth, blessed the Lord, and said: Thou art great O Lord, for ever, and thy kingdom is unto all ages.€€˜ƒx[Give glory to the Lord, ye children of Israel, and praise him in the sight of the Gentiles:€€ƒx»Because he hath therefore scattered you among the Gentiles, who know not him, that you may declare his wonderful works, and make them know that there is no other almighty God besides him.€€ƒxOHe hath chastised us for our iniquities: and he will save us for his own mercy.€€ƒx‰See then what he hath done with us, and with fear and trembling give ye glory to him: and extol the eternal King of worlds in your works.€€ƒxsAs for me, I will praise him in the land of my captivity: because he hath shewn his majesty toward a sinful nation,€€ƒxlBe converted therefore, ye sinners, and do justice before God, believing that he will shew his mercy to you.€€ƒx&And I and my soul will rejoice in him.€€ƒxJBless ye the Lord, all his elect, keep days of joy, and give glory to him.€€ƒxPJerusalem, city of God, the Lord hath chastised thee for the works of thy hands.€€ƒxÇGive glory to the Lord for thy good things, and bless the God eternal that he may rebuild his tabernacle in thee, and may call back all the captives to thee, and thou mayst rejoice for ever and ever.€€ƒxYThou shalt shine with a glorious light: and all the ends of the earth shall worship thee,€€ƒxNations from afar shall come to thee: and shall bring gifts, and shall adore the Lord in thee, and shall esteem thy land as holy.€€ƒx0For they shall call upon the great name in thee,€€ƒx˜They shall be cursed that shall despise thee: and they shall be condemned that shall blaspheme thee: and blessed shall they be that shall build thee up,€€ƒxvBut thou shalt rejoice in thy children, because they shall all be blessed, and shall be gathered together to the Lord.€€ƒxCBlessed are all they that love thee, and that rejoice in thy peace,€€ƒxoMy soul, bless thou the Lord, because the Lord our God hath delivered Jerusalem his city from all her troubles.€€ƒxQHappy shall I be if there shall remain of my seed, to see the glory of Jerusalem.€€ƒx|The gates of Jerusalem shall be built of sapphire, and of emerald, and all the walls thereof round about of precious stones.€€ƒxfAll its streets shall be paved with white and clean stones: and Alleluia shall be sung in its streets,€€ƒx[Blessed be the Lord, who hath exalted it, and may he reign over it for ever and ever, Amen.€€ƒ`€€ƒx˜And the words of Tobias were ended. And after Tobias was restored to his sight, he lived two and forty years, and saw the children of his grandchildren.€€ƒxRAnd after he had lived a hundred and two years, he was buried honorably in Ninive.€€ƒxlFor he was six and fifty years old when he lost the sight of his eyes, and sixty when he recovered it again.€€ƒxeAnd the rest of his life was in joy, and with great increase of the fear of God he departed in peace.€€ƒx‚And at the hour of his death he called unto him his son Tobias and his children, seven young men, his grandsons, and said to them:€€˜ƒx³And all the land thereof that is desert shall be filled with people, and the house of God which is burnt in it, shall again be rebuilt: and all that fear God shall return thither.€€ƒx_And the Gentiles shall leave their idols, and shall come into Jerusalem, and shall dwell in it.€€ƒxOAnd all the kings of the earth shall rejoice in it, adoring the King of Israel.€€ƒxsHearken therefore, my children, to your father: serve the Lord in truth, and seek to do the things that please him:€€ƒxAnd command your children that they do justice and almsdeeds, and that they be mindful of God, and bless him at all times in truth, and with all their power.€€ƒx¤And now, children, hear me, and do not stay here: but as soon as you shall bury your mother by me in one sepulchre, without delay direct your steps to depart hence:€€ƒx9For I see that its iniquity will bring it to destruction.€€ƒx¹And it came to pass that after the death of his mother, Tobias departed out of Ninive with his wife, and children, and children's children, and returned to his father and mother in law.€€ƒxÕAnd he found them in health in a good old age: and he took care of them, and he closed their eyes: and all the inheritance of Raguel's house came to him: and he saw his children's children to the fifth generation.€€ƒx[And after he had lived ninety-nine years in the fear of the Lord, with joy they buried him.€€ƒx¶And all his kindred, and all his generation continued in good life, and in holy conversation, so that they were acceptable both to God, and to men, and to all that dwelt in the land.€€ƒrThe Book of Judith€€ƒ`€€ƒx‡Now Arphaxad king of the Medes had brought many nations under his dominions, and he built a very strong city, which he called Ecbatana,€€ƒxæOf stones squared and hewed: he made the walls thereof seventy cubits broad, and thirty cubits high, and the towers thereof he made a hundred cubits high. But on the square of them, each side was extended the space of twenty feet.€€ƒxDAnd he made the gates thereof according to the height of the towers:€€ƒxYAnd he gloried as a mighty one in the force of his army and in the glory of his chariots.€€ƒx›Now in the twelfth year of his reign, Nabuchodonosor king of the Assyrians, who reigned in Ninive the great city, fought against Arphaxad and overcame him,€€ƒxIn the great plain which is called Ragua, about the Euphrates, and the Tigris, and the Jadason, in the plain of Erioch the king of the Elicians.€€ƒxThen was the kingdom of Nabuchodonosor exalted, and his heart was elevated: and he sent to all that dwelt in Cilicia and Damascus, and Libanus,€€ƒxuAnd to the nations that are in Carmelus, and Cedar, and to the inhabitants of Galilee in the great plain of Asdrelon,€€ƒx“And to all that were in Samaria, and beyond the river Jordan even to Jerusalem, and all the land of Jesse till you come to the borders of Ethiopia.€€ƒxCTo all these Nabuchodonosor king of the Assyrians, sent messengers:€€ƒx_But they all with one mind refused, and sent them back empty, and rejected them without honour.€€ƒx‘Then king Nabuchodonosor being angry against all that land, swore by his throne and kingdom that he would revenge himself of all those countries.€€ƒ`€€ƒxĞIn the thirteenth year of the reign of Nabuchodonosor, the two and twentieth day of the first month, the word was given out in the house of Nabuchodonosor king of the Assyrians, that he would revenge himself.€€ƒxƒAnd he called all the ancients, and all the governors, and his officers of war, and communicated to them the secret of his counsel:€€ƒxKAnd he said that his thoughts were to bring all the earth under his empire.€€ƒxmAnd when this saying pleased them all, Nabuchodonosor, the king, called Holofernes the general of his armies,€€•ƒx^Thy eye shall not spare any kingdom, and all the strong cities thou shalt bring under my yoke.€€ƒxïThen Holofernes called the captains, and officers of the power of the Assyrians: and he mustered men for the expedition, and the king commanded him, a hundred and twenty thousand fighting men on foot, and twelve thousand archers, horsemen.€€ƒxÑAnd he made all his warlike preparations to go before with a multitude of innumerable camels, with all provisions sufficient for the armies in abundance, and herds of oxen, and flocks of sheep, without number.€€ƒxAHe appointed corn to be prepared out of all Syria in his passage.€€ƒxGBut gold and silver he took out of the king's house in great abundance.€€ƒx…And he went forth he and all the army, with the chariots, and horsemen, and archers, who covered the face of the earth, like locusts.€€ƒxËAnd when he had passed through the borders of the Assyrians, he came to the great mountains of Ange, which are on the left of Cilicia: and he went up to all their castles, and took all the strong places.€€ƒxÒAnd he took by assault the renowned city of Melothus, and pillaged all the children of Tharsis, and the children of Ismahel, who were over against the face of the desert, and on the south of the land of Cellon.€€ƒx¨And he passed over the Euphrates and came into Mesopotamia: and he forced all the stately cities that were there, from the torrent of Mambre, till one comes to the sea:€€ƒxdAnd he took the borders thereof, from Cilicia to the coasts of Japheth, which are towards the south.€€ƒx”And he carried away all the children of Madian, and stripped them of all their riches, and all that resisted him he slew with the edge of the sword.€€ƒx¶And after these things he went down into the plains of Damascus in the days of the harvest, and he set all the corn on fire, and he caused all the trees and vineyards to be cut down.€€ƒx?And the fear of them fell upon all the inhabitants of the land.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¶Then the kings and the princes of all the cities and provinces, of Syria, Mesopotamia, and Syria Sobal, and Libya, and Cilicia sent their ambassadors, who coming to Holofernes, said:€€ƒxÄLet thy indignation towards us cease, for it is better for us to live and serve Nabuchodonosor the great king, and be subject to thee, than to die and to perish, or suffer the miseries of slavery.€€ƒxÅAll our cities and our possessions, all mountains and hills, and fields, and herds of oxen, and flocks of sheep, and goats, and horses, and camels, and all our goods, and families are in thy sight:€€ƒx&Let all we have be subject to thy law,€€ƒx*Both we and our children are thy servants.€€ƒxICome to us a peaceable lord, and use our service as it shall please thee,€€ƒxThen he came down from the mountains with horsemen, in great power, and made himself master of every city, and all the inhabitants of the land.€€–ƒx­And so great a fear lay upon all those provinces, that the inhabitants of all the cities, both princes and nobles, as well as the people, went out to meet him at his coming.€€ƒxQAnd received him with garlands, and lights, and dances, and timbrels, and flutes.€€ƒxcAnd though they did these things, they could not for all that mitigate the fierceness of his heart:€€ƒx>For he both destroyed their cities, and cut down their groves.€€ƒxÂFor Nabuchodonosor the king had commanded him to destroy all the gods of the earth, that he only might be called God by those nations which could be brought under him by the power of Holofernes.€€ƒx„And when he had passed through all Syria Sobal, and all Apamea, and all Mesopotamia, he came to the Idumeans into the land of Gabaa,€€ƒxAnd he took possession of their cities, and stayed there for thirty days, in which days he commanded all the troops of his army to be united.€€ƒ`€€ƒxqThen the children of Israel, who dwelt in the land of Juda, hearing these things, were exceedingly afraid of him.€€ƒx¤Dread and horror seized upon their minds, lest he should do the same to Jerusalem and to the temple of the Lord, that he had done to other cities and their temples.€€ƒxmAnd they sent into all Samaria round about, as far as Jericho, and seized upon all the tops of the mountains:€€ƒx[And they compassed their towns with walls and gathered together corn for provision for war.€€ƒx¤And Eliachim the priest wrote to all that were over against Esdrelon, which faceth the great plain near Dothain, and to all by whom there might be a passage of way,€€ƒxµthat they should take possession of the ascents of the mountains, by which there might be any way to Jerusalem, and should keep watch where the way was narrow between the mountains.€€ƒxVAnd the children of Israel did as the priests of the Lord Eliachim had appointed them.€€ƒxAnd all the people cried to the Lord with great earnestness, and they humbled their souls in fastings, and prayers, both they and their wives.€€ƒx­And the priests put on haircloths, and they caused the little children to lie prostrate before the temple of the Lord, and the altar of the Lord they covered with haircloth.€€ƒy␀And they cried to the Lord the God of Israel with one accord, that their children might not be made a prey, and their wives carried off, and their cities destroyed, and their holy things profaned, and that they might not be made a reproach to the Gentiles.€€ƒxRThen Eliachim the high priest of the Lord went about all Israel and spoke to them,€€ƒx‰Saying: Know ye that the Lord will hear your prayers, if you continue with perseverance in fastings and prayers in the sight of the Lord.€€ƒxîRemember Moses the servant of the Lord overcame Amalec that trusted in his own strength, and in his power, and in his army, and in his shields, and in his chariots, and in his horsemen, not by fighting with the sword, but by holy prayers:€€ƒxTSo all the enemies of Israel be, if you persevere in this work which you have begun.€€˜#ƒx”So that even they who offered the holocausts to the Lord, offered the sacrifices to the Lord girded with haircloths, and with ashes upon their head.€€ƒxWAnd they all begged of God with all their heart, that he would visit his people Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxªAnd it was told Holofernes the general of the army of the Assyrians, that the children of Israel prepared themselves to resist, and had shut up the ways of the mountains.€€ƒxAnd he was transported with exceeding great fury and indignation, and he called all the princes of Moab and the leaders of Ammon.€€ƒxæAnd he said to them: Tell me what is this people that besetteth the mountains: or what are their cities, and of what sort, and how great: also what is their power, or what is their multitude: or who is the king over their warfare:€€ƒx‰And why they above all that dwell in the east, have despised us, and have not come out to meet us, that they might receive us with peace?€€ƒxøThen Achior captain of all the children of Ammon answering, said; If thou vouchsafe, my lord, to hear, I will tell the truth in thy sight concerning this people, that dwelleth in the mountains, and there shall not a false word come out of my mouth.€€ƒx1This people is of the offspring of the Chaldeans.€€ƒx€They dwelt first in Mesopotamia, because they would not follow the gods of their fathers, who were in the land of the Chaldeans.€€ƒxaWherefore forsaking the ceremonies of their fathers, which consisted in the worship of many gods,€€ƒyThey worshipped one God of heaven, who also commanded them to depart from thence, and to dwell in Charan. And when there was a famine over all the land, they went down into Egypt, and there for four hundred years were so multiplied, that the army of them could not be numbered.€€ƒxÓAnd when the king of Egypt oppressed them, and made slaves of them to labour in clay and brick, in the building of his cities, they cried to their Lord, and he struck the whole land of Egypt with divers plagues.€€ƒx¦And when the Egyptians had cast them out from them, and the plague had ceased from them, and they had a mind to take them again, and bring them back to their service,€€ƒxÄThe God of heaven opened the sea to them in their flight, so that the waters were made to stand firm as a wall on either side, and they walked through the bottom of the sea and passed it dry foot.€€ƒx¾And when an innumerable army of the Egyptians pursued after them in that place, they were so overwhelmed with the waters, that there was not one left, to tell what had happened to posterity.€€ƒx‚After they came out of the Red Sea, they abode in the deserts of mount Sina, in which never man could dwell, or son of man rested.€€ƒxmThere bitter fountains were made sweet for them to drink, and for forty years they received food from heaven.€€ƒxuWheresoever they went in without bow and arrow, and without shield and sword, their God fought for them and overcame.€€ƒxtAnd there was no one that triumphed over this people, but when they departed from the worship of the Lord their God.€€ƒx|But as often as beside their own God, they worshipped any other, they were given to spoil and to the sword, and to reproach.€€ƒx‚And as often as they were penitent for having revolted from the worship of their God, the God of heaven gave them power to resist.€€ƒxñSo they overthrew the king of the Chanaanites, and of the Jebusites, and of the Pherezites, and of the Hethites, and of the Hevites, and of the Amorrhites, and all the mighty ones in Hesebon, and they possessed their lands, and their cities:€€ƒxoAnd as long as they sinned not in the sight of their God, it was well with them: for their God hateth iniquity.€€ƒxÕAnd even some years ago when they had revolted from the way which God had given them to walk therein, they were destroyed in battles by many nations and very many of them were led away captive into a strange land.€€ƒxÙBut of late returning to the Lord their God, from the different places wherein they were scattered, they are come together and are gone up into all these mountains, and possess Jerusalem again, where their holies are.€€ƒxİNow therefore, my lord, search if there be any iniquity of theirs in the sight of their God: let us go up to them, because their God will surely deliver them to thee, and they shall be brought under the yoke of thy power:€€ƒx­But if there be no offence of this people in the sight of their God, we cannot resist them because their God will defend them: and we shall be a reproach to the whole earth.€€ƒx¤And it came to pass, when Achior had ceased to speak these words, all the great men of Holofernes were angry, and they had a mind to kill him, saying to each other:€€ƒx£Who is this, that saith the children of Israel can resist king Nabuchodonosor, and his armies, men unarmed, and without force, and without skill in the art of war?€€ƒxµThat Achior therefore may know that he deceiveth us, let us go up into the mountains: and when the bravest of them shall be taken, then shall he with them be stabbed with the sword,€€ƒxfThat every nation may know that Nabuchodonosor is god of the earth, and besides him there is no other.€€ƒ`€€ƒxpAnd it came to pass when they had left off speaking, that Holofernes being in a violent passion, said to Achior:€€ƒx˜Because thou hast prophesied unto us, saying: That the nation of Israel is defended by their God, to shew thee that there is no God, but Nabuchodonosor:€€–ƒyAnd thou shalt find that Nabuchodonosor is lord of the whole earth: and then the sword of my soldiers shall pass through thy sides, and thou shalt be stabbed and fall among the wounded of Israel, and thou shalt breathe no more till thou be destroyed with them.€€ƒx·But if thou think thy prophecy true, let not thy countenance sink, and let the paleness that is in thy face, depart from thee, if thou imaginest these my words cannot be accomplished.€€ƒyAnd that thou mayst know that thou shalt experience these things together with them, behold from this hour thou shalt be associated to their people, that when they shall receive the punishment they deserve from my sword, thou mayst fall under the same vengeance.€€ƒxThen Holofernes commanded his servants to take Achior, and to lead him to Bethulia, and to deliver him into the hands of the children of Israel.€€ƒxAnd the servants of Holofernes taking him, went through the plains: but when they came near the mountains, the slingers came out against them.€€ƒx¢Then turning out of the way by the side of the mountain, they tied Achior to a tree hand and foot, and so left him bound with ropes, and returned to their master.€€ƒxáAnd the children of Israel coming down from Bethulia, came to him, and loosing him they brought him to Bethulia, and setting him in the midst of the people, asked him what was the matter that the Assyrians had left him bound.€€ƒxvIn those days the rulers there, were Ozias the son of Micha of the tribe of Simeon, and Charmi, called also Gothoniel.€€ƒxÍAnd Achior related in the midst of the ancients, and in the presence of all the people, all that he had said being asked by Holofernes: and how the people of Holofernes would have killed him for this word,€€ƒy'And how Holofernes himself being angry had commanded him to be delivered for this cause to the Israelites: that when he should overcome the children of Israel, then he might command Achior also himself to be put to death by diverse torments, for having said: The God of heaven is their defender.€€ƒxÊAnd when Achior had declared all these things, all the people fell upon their faces, adoring the Lord, and all of them together mourning and weeping poured out their prayers with one accord to the Lord,€€ƒySaying: O Lord God of heaven and earth, behold their pride, and look on our low condition, and have regard to the face of thy saints, and shew that thou forsakest not them that trust on thee, and that thou humblest them that presume of themselves, and glory in their own strength.€€ƒx„So when their weeping was ended, and the people's prayer, in which they continued all the day, was concluded, they comforted Achior,€€ƒxSaying: The God of our fathers, whose power thou hast set forth, will make this return to thee, that thou rather shalt see their destruction.€€ƒx½And when the Lord our God shall give this liberty to his servants, let God be with thee also in the midst of us: that as it shall please thee, so thou with all thine mayst converse with us.€€ƒxgThen Ozias, after the assembly was broken up, received him into his house, and made him a great supper.€€ƒxdAnd all the ancients were invited, and they refreshed themselves together after their fast was over.€€ƒxAnd afterwards all the people were called together, and they prayed all the night long within the church, desiring help of the God of Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxRBut Holofernes on the next day gave orders to his army, to go up against Bethulia.€€ƒxóNow there were in his troops a hundred and twenty thousand footmen, and two and twenty thousand horsemen, besides the preparations of those men who had been taken, and who had been brought away out of the provinces and cities of all the youth.€€ƒxëAll these prepared themselves together to fight against the children of Israel, and they came by the hillside to the top, which looketh toward Dothain, from the place which is called Belma, unto Chelmon, which is over against Esdrelon.€€”ƒx«And taking their arms of war, they posted themselves at the places, which by a narrow pathway lead directly between the mountains, and they guarded them all day and night.€€ƒxÈNow Holofernes, in going round about, found that the fountain which supplied them with water, ran through an aqueduct without the city on the south side: and he commanded their aqueduct to be cut off.€€ƒx«Nevertheless there were springs not far from the walls, out of which they were seen secretly to draw water, to refresh themselves a little rather than to drink their fill.€€ƒxÙBut the children of Ammon and Moab came to Holofernes, saying: The children of Israel trust not in their spears, nor in their arrows, but the mountains are their defence, and the steep hills and precipices guard them.€€ƒy:Wherefore that thou mayst overcome them without joining battle, set guards at the springs that they may not draw water out of them, and thou shalt destroy them without sword, or at least being wearied out they will yield up their city, which they suppose, because it is situate in the mountains, to be impregnable.€€ƒxrAnd these words pleased Holofernes, and his officers, and he placed all round about a hundred men at every spring.€€ƒyAnd when they had kept this watch for full twenty days, the cisterns, and the reserve of waters failed among all the inhabitants of Bethulia, so that there was not within the city, enough to satisfy them, no not for one day, for water was daily given out to the people by measure.€€ƒxyThen all the men and women, young men, and children, gathering themselves together to Ozias, all together with one voice,€€ƒx¾Said: God be judge between us and thee, for thou hast done evil against us, in that thou wouldst not speak peaceably with the Assyrians, and for this cause God hath sold us into their hands.€€ƒxrAnd therefore there is no one to help us, while we are cast down before their eyes in thirst, and sad destruction.€€ƒxAnd now assemble ye all that are in the city, that we may of our own accord yield ourselves all up to the people of Holofernes.€€ƒxÃFor it is better, that being captives we should live and bless the Lord, than that we should die, and be a reproach to all flesh, after we have seen our wives and our infants die before our eyes.€€ƒy/We call to witness this day heaven and earth, and the God of our fathers, who taketh vengeance upon us according to our sins, conjuring you to deliver now the city into the hand of the army of Holofernes, that our end may be short by the edge of the sword, which is made longer by the drought of thirst.€€ƒx¡And when they had said these things, there was great weeping and lamentation of all in the assembly, and for many hours with one voice they cried to God, saying:€€ƒxSWe have sinned with our fathers, we have done unjustly, we have committed iniquity:€€ƒx«Have thou mercy on us, because thou art good, or punish our iniquities by chastising us thyself, and deliver not them that trust in thee to a people that knoweth not thee,€€ƒx=That they may not say among the Gentiles: Where is their God?€€ƒx^And when being wearied with these cries, and tired with these weepings, they held their peace,€€ƒx}Ozias rising up all in tears, said: Be of good courage, my brethren, and let us wait these five days for mercy from the Lord.€€ƒxWFor perhaps he will put a stop to his indignation, and will give glory to his own name.€€˜ƒ`€€ƒykNow it came to pass, when Judith a widow had heard these words, who was the daughter of Merari, the son of Idox, the son of Joseph, the son of Ozias, the son of Elai, the son of Jamnor, the son of Gedeon, the son of Raphaim, the son of Achitob, the son of Melchias, the son of Enan, the son of Nathanias, the son of Salathiel, the son of Simeon, the son of Ruben:€€ƒxIAnd her husband was Manasses, who died in the time of the barley harvest:€€ƒx¬For he was standing over them that bound sheaves in the field; and the heat came upon his head, and he died in Bethulia his own city, and was buried there with his fathers.€€ƒxAAnd Judith his relict was a widow now three years and six months.€€ƒxqAnd she made herself a private chamber in the upper part of her house, in which she abode shut up with her maids.€€ƒx–And she wore haircloth upon her loins, and fasted all the days of her life, except the sabbaths, and new moons, and the feasts of the house of Israel.€€ƒxAnd she was exceedingly beautiful, and her husband left her great riches, and very many servants, and large possessions of herds of oxen, and flocks of sheep.€€ƒx‡And she was greatly renowned among all, because she feared the Lord very much, neither was there any one that spoke an ill word of her.€€ƒx—When therefore she had heard that Ozias had promised that he would deliver up the city after the fifth day, she sent to the ancients Chabri and Charmi.€€ƒx¯And they came to her, and she said to them: What is this word, by which Ozias hath consented to give up the city to the Assyrians, if within five days there come no aid to us?€€ƒx$And who are you that tempt the Lord?€€ƒxiThis is not a word that may draw down mercy, but rather that may stir up wrath, and enkindle indignation.€€ƒxlYou have set a time for the mercy of the Lord, and you have appointed him a day, according to your pleasure.€€ƒxxBut forasmuch as the Lord is patient, let us be penitent for this same thing, and with many tears let us beg his pardon:€€ƒxQFor God will not threaten like man, nor be inflamed to anger like the son of man.€€ƒxeAnd therefore let us humble our souls before him, and continuing in an humble spirit, in his service:€€ƒx²Let us ask the Lord with tears, that according to his will so he would shew his mercy to us: that as our heart is troubled by their pride, so also we may glorify in our humility.€€ƒxeFor we have not followed the sins of our fathers, who forsook their God, and worshipped strange gods.€€ƒx†For which crime they were given up to their enemies, to the sword, and to pillage, and to confusion: but we know no other God but him.€€ƒxÔLet us humbly wait for his consolation, and the Lord our God will require our blood of the afflictions of our enemies, and he will humble all the nations that shall rise up against us, and bring them to disgrace.€€ƒyAnd now, brethren, as you are the ancients among the people of God, and their very soul resteth upon you: comfort their hearts by your speech, that they may be mindful how our fathers were tempted that they might be proved, whether they worshipped their God truly.€€ƒxyThey must remember how our father Abraham was tempted, and being proved by many tribulations, was made the friend of God.€€ƒxrSo Isaac, so Jacob, so Moses, and all that have pleased God, passed through many tribulations, remaining faithful.€€ƒx–But they that did not receive the trials with the fear of the Lord, but uttered their impatience and the reproach of their murmuring against the Lord,€€˜ƒxRAs for us therefore let us not revenge ourselves for these things which we suffer.€€ƒxßBut esteeming these very punishments to be less than our sins deserve, let us believe that these scourges of the Lord, with which like servants we are chastised, have happened for our amendment, and not for our destruction.€€ƒxˆAnd Ozias and the ancients said to her: All things which thou hast spoken are true, and there is nothing to be reprehended in thy words.€€ƒxJNow therefore pray for us, for thou art a holy woman, and one fearing God.€€ƒxQAnd Judith said to them: As you know that what I have been able to say is of God:€€ƒxbSo that which I intend to do prove ye if it be of God, and pray that God may strengthen my design.€€ƒx®You shall stand at the gate this night, and I will go out with my maidservant: and pray ye, that as you have said, in five days the Lord may look down upon his people Israel.€€ƒx‘But I desire that you search not into what I am doing, and till I bring you word let nothing else be done but to pray for me to the Lord our God.€€ƒxŒAnd Ozias the prince of Juda said to her: Go in peace, and the Lord be with thee to take revenge of our enemies. So returning they departed.€€ƒ`€€ƒx³And when they were gone, Judith went into her oratory: and putting on haircloth, laid ashes on her head: and falling down prostrate before the Lord, she cried to the Lord, saying:€€ƒx«Lord God of my father Simeon, who gavest him a sword to execute vengeance against strangers, who had defiled by their uncleanness, and uncovered the virgin unto confusion:€€ƒxÕAnd who gavest their wives to be made a prey, and their daughters into captivity: and all their spoils to be divided to the servants, who were zealous with thy zeal: assist, I beseech thee, O Lord God, me a widow.€€ƒx{For thou hast done the things of old, and hast devised one thing after another: and what thou hast designed hath been done.€€ƒxTFor all thy ways are prepared, and in thy providence thou hast placed thy judgments.€€ƒxäLook upon the camp of the Assyrians now, as thou wast pleased to look upon the camp of the Egyptians, when they pursued armed after thy servants, trusting in their chariots, and in their horsemen, and in a multitude of warriors.€€ƒx=But thou lookedst over their camp, and darkness wearied them.€€ƒx:The deep held their feet, and the waters overwhelmed them.€€ƒx´So may it be with these also, O Lord, who trust in their multitude, and in their chariots, and in their pikes, and in their shields, and in their arrows, and glory in their spears,€€ƒxeAnd know not that thou art our God, who destroyest wars from the beginning, and the Lord is thy name.€€ƒyLift up thy arm as from the beginning, and crush their power with thy power: let their power fall in their wrath, who promise themselves to violate thy sanctuary, and defile the dwelling place of thy name, and to beat down with their sword the horn of thy altar.€€ƒxHBring to pass, O Lord, that his pride may be cut off with his own sword.€€ƒxxLet him be caught in the net of his own eyes in my regard, and do thou strike him by the graces of the words of my lips.€€ƒx]Give me constancy in my mind, that I may despise him: and fortitude that I may overthrow him.€€ƒx]For this will be a glorious monument for thy name, when he shall fall by the hand of a woman.€€ƒxßFor thy power, O Lord, is not in a multitude, nor is thy pleasure in the strength of horses, nor from the beginning have the proud been acceptable to thee: but the prayer of the humble and the meek hath always pleased thee.€€ƒxœO God of the heavens, creator of the waters, and Lord of the whole creation, hear me a poor wretch, making supplication to thee, and presuming of thy mercy.€€ƒx—Remember, O Lord, thy covenant, and put thou words in my mouth, and strengthen the resolution in my heart, that thy house may continue in thy holiness:€€–ƒ`€€ƒx„And it came to pass, when she had ceased to cry to the Lord, that she rose from the place wherein she lay prostrate before the Lord.€€ƒx~And she called her maid, and going down into her house she took off her haircloth, and put away the garments of her widowhood,€€ƒyCAnd she washed her body, and anointed herself with the best ointment, and plaited the hair of her head, and put a bonnet upon her head, and clothed herself with the garments of her gladness, and put sandals on her feet, and took her bracelets, and lilies, and earlets, and rings, and adorned herself with all her ornaments.€€ƒxäAnd the Lord also gave her more beauty: because all this dressing up did not proceed from sensuality, but from virtue: and therefore the Lord increased this her beauty, so that she appeared to all men's eyes incomparably lovely.€€ƒxŒAnd she gave to her maid a bottle of wine to carry, and a vessel of oil, and parched corn, and dry figs, and bread and cheese, and went out.€€ƒxcAnd when they came to the gate of the city, they found Ozias, and the ancients of the city waiting.€€ƒxOAnd when they saw her they were astonished, and admired her beauty exceedingly.€€ƒyBut they asked her no question, only they let her pass, saying: The God of our fathers give thee grace, and may he strengthen all the counsel of thy heart with his power, that Jerusalem may glory in thee, and thy name may be in the number of the holy and just.€€ƒxFAnd they that were there said, all with one voice: So be it, so be it.€€ƒxKBut Judith praying to the Lord, passed through the gates, she and her maid.€€ƒx´And it came to pass, when she went down the hill, about break of day, that the watchmen of the Assyrians met her, and stopped her, saying: Whence comest thou or whither goest thou?€€ƒxôAnd she answered: I am a daughter of the Hebrews, and I am fled from them, because I knew they would be made a prey to you, because they despised you, and would not of their own accord yield themselves, that they might find mercy in your sight.€€ƒxÙFor this reason I thought with myself, saying: I will go to the presence of the prince Holofernes, that I may tell him their secrets, and shew him by what way he may take them, without the loss of one man of his army.€€ƒx„And when the men had heard her words, they beheld her face, and their eyes were amazed, for they wondered exceedingly at her beauty.€€ƒxcAnd they said to her: Thou hast saved thy life by taking this resolution, to come down to our lord.€€ƒxÊAnd be assured of this, that when thou shalt stand before him, he will treat thee well, and thou wilt be most acceptable to his heart. And they brought her to the tent of Holofernes, telling him of her.€€ƒxUAnd when she was come into his presence, forthwith Holofernes was caught by his eyes.€€ƒxºAnd his officers said to him: Who can despise the people of the Hebrews, who have such beautiful women, that we should not think it worth our while for their sakes to fight against them?€€ƒx{And Judith seeing Holofernes sitting under a canopy, which was woven of purple and gold, with emeralds and precious stones:€€ƒx¥After she had looked on his face, bowed down to him, prostrating herself to the ground. And the servants of Holofernes lifted her up, by the command of their master.€€ƒ`€€˜#ƒxZAnd if thy people had not despised me, I would never have lifted up my spear against them.€€ƒx`But now tell me, for what cause hast thou left them, and why it hath pleased thee to come to us?€€ƒx™And Judith said to him: Receive the words of thy handmaid, for if thou wilt follow the words of thy handmaid, the Lord will do with thee a perfect thing.€€ƒxÏFor as Nabuchodonosor the king of the earth liveth, and his power liveth which is in thee for chastising of all straying souls: not only men serve him through thee, but also the beasts of the field obey him.€€ƒxÒFor the industry of thy mind is spoken of among all nations, and it is told through the whole world, that thou only art excellent, and mighty in all his kingdom, and thy discipline is cried up in all provinces.€€ƒxeIt is known also what Achior said, nor are we ignorant of what thou hast commanded to be done to him.€€ƒx›For it is certain that our God is so offended with sins, that he hath sent word by his prophets to the people, that he will deliver them up for their sins.€€ƒx]And because the children of Israel know they have offended their God, thy dread is upon them.€€ƒxsMoreover also a famine hath come upon them, and for drought of water they are already to be counted among the dead.€€ƒxQAnd they have a design even to kill their cattle, and to drink the blood of them.€€ƒyDAnd the consecrated things of the Lord their God which God forbade them to touch, in corn, wine, and oil, these have they purposed to make use of, and they design to consume the things which they ought not to touch with their hands: therefore because they do these things, it is certain they will be given up to destruction.€€ƒxmAnd I thy handmaid knowing this, am fled from them, and the Lord hath sent me to tell thee these very things.€€ƒxrFor I thy handmaid worship God even now that I am with thee, and thy handmaid will go out, and I will pray to God,€€ƒyAnd he will tell me when he will repay them for their sins, and I will come and tell thee, so that I may bring thee through the midst of Jerusalem, and thou shalt have all the people of Israel, as sheep that have no shepherd, and there shall not so much as one dog bark against thee:€€ƒx:Because these things are told me by the providence of God.€€ƒxPAnd because God is angry with them, I am sent to tell these very things to thee.€€ƒxtAnd all these words pleased Holofernes, and his servants, and they admired her wisdom, and they said one to another:€€ƒxUThere is not such another woman upon earth in look, in beauty, and in sense of words.€€ƒx|And Holofernes said to her: God hath done well who sent thee before the people, that thou mightest give them into our hands:€€ƒxÈAnd because thy promise is good, if thy God shall do this for me, he shall also be my God, and thou shalt be great in the house of Nabuchodonosor, and thy name shall be renowned through all the earth.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŸThen he ordered that she should go in where his treasures were laid up, and bade her tarry there, and he appointed what should be given her from his own table.€€ƒx²And Judith answered him and said: Now I cannot eat of these things which thou commandest to be given me, lest sin come upon me: but I will eat of the things which I have brought.€€ƒxsAnd Holofernes said to her: If these things which thou hast brought with thee fail thee, what shall we do for thee?€€ƒxÑAnd Judith said: As thy soul liveth, my lord, thy handmaid shall not spend all these things till God do by my hand that which I have purposed. And his servants brought her into the tent which he had commanded.€€ƒxˆAnd when she was going in, she desired that she might have liberty to go out at night and before day to prayer, and to beseech the Lord.€€ƒxqAnd he commanded his chamberlains, that she might go out and in, to adore her God as she pleased, for three days.€€ƒxfAnd she went out in the nights into the valley of Bethulia, and washed herself in a fountain of water.€€ƒx|And as she came up, she prayed to the Lord the God of Israel, that he would direct her way to the deliverance of his people.€€ƒxXAnd going in, she remained pure in the tent, until she took her own meat in the evening.€€ƒxÇAnd it came to pass on the fourth day, that Holofernes made a supper for his servants, and said to Vagao his eunuch: Go, and persuade that Hebrew woman, to consent of her own accord to dwell with me.€€ƒxsFor it is looked upon as shameful among the Assyrians, if a woman mock a man, by doing so as to pass free from him.€€ƒx¼Then Vagao went in to Judith, and said: Let not my good maid be afraid to go in to my lord, that she may be honoured before his face, that she may eat with him and drink wine and be merry.€€ƒxAAnd Judith answered him: Who am I, that I should gainsay my lord?€€ƒxAll that shall be good and best before his eyes, I will do. And whatsoever shall please him, that shall be best to me all the days of my life.€€–ƒxSAnd the heart of Holofernes was smitten, for he was burning with the desire of her.€€ƒxgAnd Holofernes said to her: Drink now, and sit down and be merry; for thou hast found favour before me.€€ƒx_And Judith said: I will drink my lord, because my life is magnified this day above all my days.€€ƒxMAnd she took and ate and drank before him what her maid had prepared for her.€€ƒxxAnd Holofernes was made merry on her occasion, and drank exceeding much wine, so much as he had never drunk in his life.€€ƒ`€€ƒxzAnd when it was grown late, his servants made haste to their lodgings, and Vagao shut the chamber doors, and went his way.€€ƒx(And they were all overcharged with wine.€€ƒx$And Judith was alone in the chamber.€€ƒxDBut Holofernes lay on his bed, fast asleep, being exceedingly drunk.€€ƒxOAnd Judith spoke to her maid to stand without before the chamber, and to watch:€€ƒxZAnd Judith stood before the bed praying with tears, and the motion of her lips in silence,€€ƒySaying: Strengthen me, O Lord God of Israel, and in this hour look on the works of my hands, that as thou hast promised, thou mayst raise up Jerusalem thy city: and that I may bring to pass that which I have purposed, having a belief that it might be done by thee.€€ƒx{And when she had said this, she went to the pillar that was at his bed's head, and loosed his sword that hung tied upon it.€€ƒxwAnd when she had drawn it out, she took him by the hair of his head, and said: Strengthen me, O Lord God, at this hour.€€ƒx†And she struck twice upon his neck, and cut off his head, and took off his canopy from the pillars, and rolled away his headless body.€€ƒxvAnd after a while she went out, and delivered the head of Holofernes to her maid, and bade her put it into her wallet.€€ƒx¤And they two went out according to their custom, as it were to prayer, and they passed the camp, and having compassed the valley, they came to the gate of the city.€€ƒx…And Judith from afar off cried to the watchmen upon the walls: Open the gates for God is with us, who hath shewn his power in Israel.€€ƒxaAnd it came to pass, when the men had heard her voice, that they called the ancients of the city.€€ƒxfAnd all ran to meet her from the least to the greatest: for they now had no hopes that she would come.€€ƒx¡And lighting up lights they all gathered round about her: and she went up to a higher place, and commanded silence to be made. And when all had held their peace,€€ƒxŸAnd by me his handmaid he hath fulfilled his mercy, which he promised to the house of Israel: and he hath killed the enemy of his people by my hand this night.€€ƒyThen she brought forth the head of Holofernes out of the wallet, and shewed it them, saying: Behold the head of Holofernes the general of the army of the Assyrians, and behold his canopy, wherein he lay in his drunkenness, where the Lord our God slew him by the hand of a woman.€€ƒy=But as the same Lord liveth, his angel hath been my keeper both going hence, and abiding there, and returning from thence hither: and the Lord hath not suffered me his handmaid to be defiled, but hath brought me back to you without pollution of sin, rejoicing for his victory, for my escape, and for your deliverance.€€ƒxVGive all of you glory to him, because he is good, because his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxAnd they all adored the Lord, and said to her: The Lord hath blessed thee by his power, because by thee he hath brought our enemies to nought.€€ƒx—And Ozias the prince of the people of Israel, said to her: Blessed art thou, O daughter, by the Lord the most high God, above all women upon the earth.€€ƒxBlessed be the Lord who made heaven and earth, who hath directed thee to the cutting off the head of the prince of our enemies.€€ƒy9Because he hath so magnified thy name this day, that thy praise shall not depart out of the mouth of men who shall be mindful of the power of the Lord for ever, for that thou hast not spared thy life, by reason of the distress and tribulation of thy people, but hast prevented our ruin in the presence of our God.€€ƒx,And all the people said: So be it, so be it.€€ƒxÜAnd Achior being called for came, and Judith said to him: The God of Israel, to whom thou gavest testimony, that he revengeth himself of his enemies, he hath cut off the head of all the unbelievers this night by my hand.€€ƒy
And that thou mayst find that it is so, behold the head of Holofernes, who in the contempt of his pride despised the God of Israel: and threatened them with death, saying: When the people of Israel shall be taken, I will command thy sides to be pierced with a sword.€€ƒxˆThen Achior seeing the head of Holofernes, being seized with a great fear he fell on his face upon the earth, and his soul swooned away.€€ƒx^But after he had recovered his spirits he fell down at her feet, and reverenced her, and said:€€ƒx¢Blessed art thou by thy God in every tabernacle of Jacob, for in every nation which shall hear thy name, the God of Israel shall be magnified on occasion of thee.€€ƒ`€€ƒx]And Judith said to all the people: Hear me, my brethren, hang ye up this head upon our walls.€€•ƒxFThen the watchmen must needs run to awake their prince for the battle.€€ƒx™And when the captains of them shall run to the tent of Holofernes, and shall find him without his head wallowing in his blood, fear shall fall upon them.€€ƒxvAnd when you shall know that they are fleeing, go after them securely, for the Lord will destroy them under your feet.€€ƒy	Then Achior seeing the power that the God of Israel had wrought, leaving the religion of the Gentiles, he believed God, and circumcised the flesh of his foreskin, and was joined to the people of Israel, with all the succession of his kindred until this present day.€€ƒx¤And immediately at break of day, they hung up the head of Holofernes upon the walls, and every man took his arms, and they went out with a great noise and shouting.€€ƒx<And the watchmen seeing this, ran to the tent of Holofernes.€€ƒxÕAnd they that were in the tent came, and made a noise, before the door of the chamber to awake him, endeavouring by art to break his rest, that Holofernes might awake, not by their calling him, but by their noise.€€ƒxXFor no man durst knock, or open and go into the chamber of the general of the Assyrians.€€ƒx‰But when his captains and tribunes were come, and all the chiefs of the army of the king of the Assyrians, they said to the chamberlains:€€ƒxfGo in, and awake him, for the mice, coming out of their holes, have presumed to challenge us to fight.€€ƒx‘Then Vagao going into his chamber, stood before the curtain, and made a clapping with his hands: for he thought that he was sleeping with Judith.€€ƒyBut when with hearkening, he perceived no motion of one lying, he came near to the curtain, and lifting it up, and seeing the body of Holofernes, lying upon the ground, without the head, weltering in his blood, he cried out with a loud voice, with weeping, and rent his garments.€€ƒxSAnd he went into the tent of Judith, and not finding her, he ran out to the people,€€ƒxAnd said: One Hebrew woman hath made confusion in the house of king Nabuchodonosor: for behold Holofernes lieth upon the ground, and his head is not upon him.€€ƒx»Now when the chiefs of the army of the Assyrians had heard this, they all rent their garments, and an intolerable fear and dread fell upon them, and their minds were troubled exceedingly.€€ƒx:And there was a very great cry in the midst of their camp.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¶And when all the army heard that Holofernes was beheaded, courage and counsel fled from them, and being seized with trembling and fear they thought only to save themselves by flight.€€ƒxùSo that no one spoke to his neighbour, but hanging down the head, leaving all things behind, they made haste to escape from the Hebrews, who, as they heard, were coming armed upon them, and fled by the ways of the fields, and the paths of the hills.€€ƒx†So the children of Israel seeing them fleeing, followed after them. And they went down sounding with trumpets and shouting after them.€€ƒx°And because the Assyrians were not united together, they went without order in their flight; but the children of Israel pursuing in one body, defeated all that they could find.€€˜ƒx·And every country, and every city, sent their chosen young men armed after them, and they pursued them with the edge of the sword until they came to the extremities of their confines.€€ƒx»And the rest that were in Bethulia went into the camp of the Assyrians, and took away the spoils which the Assyrians in their flight had left behind them, and they were laden exceedingly,€€ƒyBut they that returned conquerors to Bethulia, brought with them all things that were theirs, so that there was no numbering of their cattle, and beasts, and all their moveables, insomuch that from the least to the greatest all were made rich by their spoils.€€ƒx`And Joachim the high priest came from Jerusalem to Bethulia with all his ancients to see Judith.€€ƒx®And when she was come out to him, they all blessed her with one voice, saying: Thou art the glory of Jerusalem, thou art the joy of Israel, thou art the honour of our people:€€ƒxıFor thou hast done manfully, and thy heart has been strengthened, because thou hast loved chastity, and after thy husband hast not known any other: therefore also the hand of the Lord hath strengthened thee, and therefore thou shalt be blessed for ever.€€ƒx,And all the people said: So be it, so be it.€€ƒxiAnd thirty days were scarce sufficient for the people of Israel to gather up the spoils of the Assyrians.€€ƒxãBut all those things that were proved to be the peculiar goods of Holofernes, they gave to Judith in gold, and silver, and garments and precious stones, and all household stuff, and they all were delivered to her by the people.€€ƒxjAnd all the people rejoiced, with the women, and virgins, and young men, playing on instruments and harps.€€ƒ`€€ƒx3Then Judith sung this canticle to the Lord, saying:€€ƒx~Begin ye to the Lord with timbrels, sing ye to the Lord with cymbals, tune unto him a new psalm, extol and call upon his name.€€ƒx6The Lord putteth an end to wars, the Lord is his name.€€ƒx`He hath set his camp in the midst of his people, to deliver us from the hand of all our enemies.€€ƒx¤The Assyrian came out of the mountains from the north in the multitude of his strength: his multitude stopped up the torrents, and their horses covered the valleys.€€ƒxŠHe bragged that he would set my borders on fire, and kill my young men with the sword, to make my infants a prey, and my virgins captives.€€ƒxlBut the almighty Lord hath struck him, and hath delivered him into the hands of a woman, and hath slain him.€€ƒxÏFor their mighty one did not fall by young men, neither did the sons of Titan strike him, nor tall giants oppose themselves to him, but Judith the daughter of Merari weakened him with the beauty of her face.€€ƒxyFor she put off her the garments of widowhood, and put on her the garments of joy, to give joy to the children of Israel.€€ƒxmShe anointed her face with ointment, and bound up her locks with a crown, she took a new robe to deceive him.€€ƒxgHer sandals ravished his eyes, her beauty made his soul her captive, with a sword she cut off his head.€€ƒxDThe Persians quaked at her constancy, and the Medes at her boldness.€€ƒxXThen the camp of the Assyrians howled, when my lowly ones appeared, parched with thirst.€€ƒx¤The sons of the damsels have pierced them through, and they have killed them like children fleeing away: they perished in battle before the face of the Lord my God.€€ƒxBLet us sing a hymn to the Lord, let us sing a new hymn to our God.€€ƒxXO Adonai, Lord, great art thou, and glorious in thy power, and no one can overcome thee.€€ƒx·Let all thy creatures serve thee: because thou hast spoken, and they were made: thou didst send forth thy spirit, and they were created, and there is no one that can resist thy voice.€€ƒxoThe mountains shall be moved from the foundations with the waters: the rocks shall melt as wax before thy face.€€˜ƒx’Woe be to the nation that riseth up against my people: for the Lord almighty will take revenge on them, in the day of judgment he will visit them.€€ƒx]For he will give fire, and worms into their flesh, that they may burn, and may feel for ever.€€ƒxÒAnd it came to pass after these things, that all the people, after the victory, came to Jerusalem to adore the Lord: and as soon as they were purified, they all offered holocausts, and vows, and their promises.€€ƒx And Judith offered for an anathema of oblivion all the arms of Holofernes, which the people gave her, and the canopy that she had taken away out of his chamber.€€ƒx‚And the people were joyful in the sight of the sanctuary, and for three months the joy of this victory was celebrated with Judith.€€ƒx‘And after those days every man returned to his house, and Judith was made great in Bethulia, and she was most renowned in all the land of Israel.€€ƒxAnd chastity was joined to her virtue, so that she knew no man all the days of her life, after the death of Manasses her husband.€€ƒx5And on festival days she came forth with great glory.€€ƒx•And she abode in her husband's house a hundred and five years, and made her handmaid free, and she died, and was buried with her husband in Bethulia.€€ƒx*And all the people mourned for seven days.€€ƒxaAnd all the time of her life there was none that troubled Israel, nor many years after her death.€€ƒxªBut the day of the festivity of this victory is received by the Hebrews in the number of holy days, and is religiously observed by the Jews from that time until this day.€€ƒrThe Book of Esther€€ƒ`€€ƒxfIn the days of Assuerus, who reigned from India to Ethiopia over a hundred and twenty seven provinces:€€ƒxXWhen he sat on the throne of his kingdom, the city Susan was the capital of his kingdom.€€ƒxÖNow in the third year of his reign he made a great feast for all the princes, and for his servants, for the most mighty of the Persians, and the nobles of the Medes, and the governors of the provinces in his sight,€€ƒx£That he might shew the riches of the glory of his kingdom, and the greatness, and boasting of his power, for a long time, to wit, for a hundred and fourscore days.€€ƒyAnd when the days of the feast were expired, he invited all the people that were found in Susan, from the greatest to the least: and commanded a feast to be made seven days in the court of the garden, and of the wood, which was planted by the care and the hand of the king.€€ƒylAnd there were hung up on every side sky coloured, and green, and violet hangings, fastened with cords of silk, and of purple, which were put into rings of ivory, and were held up with marble pillars. The beds also were of gold and silver, placed in order upon a floor paved with porphyry and white marble: which was embellished with painting of wonderful variety.€€ƒxÏAnd they that were invited, drank in golden cups, and the meats were brought in divers vessels one after another. Wine also in abundance and of the best was presented, as was worthy of a king's magnificence.€€ƒx¼Neither was there any one to compel them to drink that were not willing, but as the king had appointed, who set over every table one of his nobles, that every man might take what he would.€€ƒxfAlso Vasthi the queen made a feast for the women in the palace, where king Assuerus was used to dwell.€€ƒxùNow on the seventh day, when the king was merry, and after very much drinking was well warmed with wine, he commanded Mauman, and Bazatha, and Harbona, and Bagatha, and Abgatha, and Zethar, and Charcas, the seven eunuchs that served in his presence,€€ƒx¢To bring in queen Vasthi before the king, with the crown set upon her head, to shew her beauty to all the people and the princes: for she was exceeding beautiful.€€ƒx²But she refused, and would not come at the king's commandment, which he had signified to her by the eunuchs. Whereupon the king, being angry, and inflamed with a very great fury,€€ƒx¶Asked the wise men, who according to the custom of the kings, were always near his person, and all he did was by their counsel, who knew the laws, and judgments of their forefathers:€€ƒxï(Now the chief and nearest him were, Charsena, and Sethar, and Admatha, and Tharsis, and Mares, and Marsana, and Mamuchan, seven princes of the Persians and of the Medes, who saw the face of the king, and were used to sit first after him:)€€ƒx•What sentence ought to pass upon Vasthi the queen, who had refused to obey the commandment of king Assuerus, which he had sent to her by the eunuchs?€€˜"ƒxÃFor this deed of the queen will go abroad to all women, so that they will despise their husbands, and will say: King Assuerus commanded that queen Vasthi should come in to him, and she would not.€€ƒx¨And by this example all the wives of the princes of the Persians and the Medes will slight the commandments of their husbands: wherefore the king's indignation is just.€€ƒyIf it please thee, let an edict go out from thy presence, and let it be written according to the law of the Persians and of the Medes, which must not be altered, that Vasthi come in no more to the king, but another, that is better than her, be made queen in her place.€€ƒx³And let this be published through all the provinces of thy empire, (which is very wide,) and let all wives, as well of the greater as of the lesser, give honour to their husbands.€€ƒxeHis counsel pleased the king, and the princes: and the king did according to the counsel of Mamuchan.€€ƒxôAnd he sent letters to all the provinces of his kingdom, as every nation could hear and read, in divers languages and characters, that the husbands should be rulers and masters in their houses: and that this should be published to every people.€€ƒ`€€ƒx€After this, when the wrath of king Assuerus was appeased, he remembered Vasthi, and what she had done and what she had suffered:€€ƒxmAnd the king's servants and his officers said: Let young women be sought for the king, virgins and beautiful,€€ƒybAnd let some persons be sent through all the provinces to look for beautiful maidens and virgins: and let them bring them to the city of Susan, and put them into the house of the women under the hand of Egeus the eunuch, who is the overseer and keeper of the king's women: and let them receive women's ornaments, and other things necessary for their use.€€ƒx³And whosoever among them all shall please the king's eyes, let her be queen instead of Vasthi. The word pleased the king: and he commanded it should be done as they had suggested.€€ƒx‡There was a man in the city of Susan, a Jew, named Mardochai, the son of Jair, the son of Semei, the son of Cis, of the race of Jemini,€€ƒx}Who had been carried away from Jerusalem at the time that Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon carried away Jechonias king of Juda,€€ƒxòAnd he had brought up his brother's daughter Edissa, who by another name was called Esther: now she had lost both her parents: and was exceeding fair and beautiful. And her father and mother being dead, Mardochai adopted her for his daughter.€€ƒyAnd when the king's ordinance was noised abroad, and according to his commandment many beautiful virgins were brought to Susan, and were delivered to Egeus the eunuch: Esther also among the rest of the maidens was delivered to him to be kept in the number of the women.€€ƒyAnd she pleased him, and found favour in his sight. And he commanded the eunuch to hasten the women's ornaments, and to deliver to her her part, and seven of the most beautiful maidens of the king's house, and to adorn and deck out both her and her waiting maids.€€ƒxsAnd she would not tell him her people nor her country. For Mardochai had charged her to say nothing at all of that:€€ƒx­And he walked every day before the court of the house, in which the chosen virgins were kept, having a care for Esther's welfare, and desiring to know what would befall her.€€ƒyNow when every virgin's turn came to go in to the king, after all had been done for setting them off to advantage, it was the twelfth month: so that for six months they were anointed with oil of myrrh, and for other six months they used certain perfumes and sweet spices.€€ƒxÌAnd when they were going in to the king, whatsoever they asked to adorn themselves they received: and being decked out, as it pleased them, they passed from the chamber of the women to the king's chamber.€€ƒy>And she that went in at evening, came out in the morning, and from thence she was conducted to the second house, that was under the hand of Susagaz the eunuch, who had the charge over the king's concubines: neither could she return any more to the king, unless the king desired it, and had ordered her by name to come.€€ƒy­And as the time came orderly about, the day was at hand, when Esther, the daughter of Abihail the brother of Mardochai, whom he had adopted for his daughter, was to go in to the king. But she sought not women's ornaments, but whatsoever Egeus the eunuch the keeper of the virgins had a mind, he gave her to adorn her. For she was exceeding fair, and her incredible beauty made her appear agreeable and amiable in the eyes of all.€€ƒx}So she was brought to the chamber of king Assuerus the tenth month, which is called Tebeth, in the seventh year of his reign.€€ƒx½And the king loved her more than all the women, and she had favour and kindness before him above all the women, and he set the royal crown on her head, and made her queen instead of Vasthi.€€ƒxäAnd he commanded a magnificent feast to be prepared for all the princes, and for his servants, for the marriage and wedding of Esther. And he gave rest to all the provinces, and bestowed gifts according to princely magnificence.€€ƒxmAnd when the virgins were sought the second time, and gathered together, Mardochai stayed at the king's gate,€€ƒxòNeither had Esther as yet declared her country and people, according to his commandment. For whatsoever he commanded, Esther observed: and she did all things in the same manner as she was wont at that time when he brought her up a little one.€€ƒxşAt that time, therefore, when Mardochai abode at the king's gate, Bagathan and Thares, two of the king's eunuchs, who were porters, and presided in the first entry of the palace, were angry: and they designed to rise up against the king, and to kill him.€€ƒx™And Mardochai had notice of it, and immediately he told it to queen Esther: and she to the king in Mardochai's name, who had reported the thing unto her.€€ƒxœIt was inquired into, and found out: and they were both hanged on a gibbet. And it was put in the histories, and recorded in the chronicles before the king.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¢After these things, king Assuerus advanced Aman, the son of Amadathi, who was of the race of Agag: and he set his throne above all the princes that were with him.€€ƒxÇAnd all the king's servants, that were at the doors of the palace, bent their knees, and worshipped Aman: for so the emperor had commanded them, only Mardochai did not bend his knee, nor worship him.€€ƒxˆAnd the king's servants that were chief at the doors of the palace, said to him: Why dost thou alone not observe the king's commandment?€€˜ƒxNow when Aman had heard this, and had proved by experience that Mardochai did not bend his knee to him, nor worship him, he was exceeding angry.€€ƒxØAnd he counted it nothing to lay his hands upon Mardochai alone: for he had heard that he was of the nation of the Jews, and he chose rather to destroy all the nation of the Jews that were in the kingdom of Assuerus.€€ƒy&In the first month (which is called Nisan) in the twelfth year of the reign of Assuerus, the lot was cast into an urn, which in Hebrew is called Phur, before Aman, on what day and what month the nation of the Jews should be destroyed: and there came out the twelfth month, which is called Adar.€€ƒyCAnd Aman said to king Assuerus: There is a people scattered through all the provinces of thy kingdom, and separated one from another, that use new laws and ceremonies, and moreover despise the king's ordinances: and thou knowest very well that it is not expedient for thy kingdom that they should grow insolent by impunity.€€ƒxlIf it please thee, decree that they may be destroyed, and I will pay ten thousand talents to thy treasurers.€€ƒxAnd the king took the ring that he used, from his own hand, and gave it to Aman, the son of Amadathi of the race of Agag, the enemy of the Jews.€€ƒxŠAnd he said to him: As to the money which thou promisest, keep it for thyself: and as to the people, do with them as seemeth good to thee.€€ƒyxAnd the king's scribes were called in the first month Nisan, on the thirteenth day of the same mouth: and they wrote, as Aman had commanded, to all the king's lieutenants, and to the judges of the provinces, and of divers nations, as every nation could read, and hear according to their different languages, in the name of king Assuerus: and the letters, sealed with his ring,€€ƒxıWere sent by the king's messengers to all provinces, to kill and destroy all the Jews, both young and old, little children, and women, in one day, that is, on the thirteenth of the twelfth month, which is called Adar, and to make a spoil of their goods.€€ƒxqAnd the contents of the letters were to this effect, that all provinces might know and be ready against that day.€€ƒxÍThe couriers that were sent made haste to fulfil the king's commandment. And immediately the edict was hung up in Susan, the king and Aman feasting together, and all the Jews that were in the city weeping.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÙNow when Mardochai had heard these things, he rent his garments, and put on sackcloth, strewing ashes on his head and he cried with a loud voice in the street in the midst of the city, shewing the anguish of his mind.€€ƒx„And he came lamenting in this manner even to the gate of the palace: for no one clothed with sackcloth might enter the king's court.€€ƒxÍAnd in all provinces, towns, and places, to which the king's cruel edict was come, there was great mourning among the Jews, with fasting, wailing, and weeping, many using sackcloth and ashes for their bed.€€ƒxÏThen Esther's maids and her eunuchs went in, and told her. And when she heard it she was in a consternation and she sent a garment, to clothe him, and to take away the sackcloth: but he would not receive it.€€ƒx¡And she called for Athach the eunuch, whom the king had appointed to attend upon her, and she commanded him to go to Mardochai, and learn of him why he did this.€€ƒxkAnd Athach going out went to Mardochai, who was standing in the street of the city, before the palace gate:€€ƒx‡And Mardochai told him all that had happened, how Aman had promised to pay money into the king's treasures, to have the Jews destroyed.€€ƒx²He gave him also a copy of the edict which was hanging up in Susan, that he should shew it to the queen, and admonish her to go in to the king, and to entreat him for her people.€€ƒxAAnd Athach went back and told Esther all that Mardochai had said.€€ƒx0She answered him, and bade him say to Mardochai:€€ƒy«All the king's servants, and all the provinces that are under his dominion, know, that whosoever, whether man or woman, cometh into the king's inner court, who is not called for, is immediately to be put to death without any delay: except the king shall hold out the golden sceptre to him, in token of clemency, that so he may live. How then can I go in to the king, who for these thirty days now have not been called unto him?€€ƒx"And when Mardochai had heard this,€€ƒx‘He sent word to Esther again, saying: Think not that thou mayst save thy life only, because thou art in the king's house, more than all the Jews:€€ƒxÿFor if thou wilt now hold thy peace, the Jews shall be delivered by some other occasion: and thou, and thy father's house shall perish. And who knoweth whether thou art not therefore come to the kingdom, that thou mightest be ready in such a time as this?€€ƒx2And again Esther sent to Mardochai in these words:€€ƒy0Go, and gather together all the Jews whom thou shalt find in Susan, and pray ye for me. Neither eat nor drink for three days and three nights: and I with my handmaids will fast in like manner, and then I will go in to the king, against the law, not being called, and expose myself to death and to danger.€€ƒx=So Mardochai went, and did all that Esther had commanded him.€€ƒ`€€‘ƒx¿And when he saw Esther the queen standing, she pleased his eyes, and he held out toward her the golden sceptre, which he held in his hand and she drew near, and kissed the top of his sceptre.€€ƒxšAnd the king said to her: What wilt thou, queen Esther? what is thy request? if thou shouldst even ask one half of the kingdom, it shall be given to thee.€€ƒxˆBut she answered: If it please the king, I beseech thee to come to me this day, and Aman with thee to the banquet which I have prepared.€€ƒx¢And the king said forthwith: Call ye Aman quickly, that he may obey Esther's will. So the king and Aman came to the banquet which the queen had prepared for them.€€ƒxÑAnd the king said to her, after he had drunk wine plentifully: What dost thou desire should be given thee? and for what thing askest thou? although thou shouldst ask the half of my kingdom, thou shalt have it.€€ƒx5And Esther answered: My petition and request is this:€€ƒxìIf I have found favour in the king's sight, and if it please the king to give me what I ask, and to fulfil my petition: let the king and Aman come to the banquet which I have prepared them, and to morrow I will open my mind to the king.€€ƒxóSo Aman went out that day joyful and merry. And when he saw Mardochai sitting before the gate of the palace, and that he not only did not rise up to honour him, but did not so much as move from the place where he sat, he was exceedingly angry:€€ƒxsBut dissembling his anger, and returning into his house, he called together to him his friends, and Zares his wife:€€ƒx®And he declared to them the greatness of his riches, and the multitude of his children, and with how great glory the king had advanced him above all his princes and servants.€€ƒxAnd after this he said: Queen Esther also hath invited no other to the banquet with the king, but me: and with her I am also to dine to morrow with the king:€€ƒxAnd whereas I have all these things, I think I have nothing, so long as I see Mardochai the Jew sitting before the king's gate.€€ƒyGThen Zares his wife, and the rest of his friends answered him: Order a great beam to be prepared, fifty cubits high, and in the morning speak to the king, that Mardochai may be hanged upon it, and so thou shalt go full of joy with the king to the banquet. The counsel pleased him, and he commanded a high gibbet to be prepared.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¦That night the king passed without sleep, and he commanded the histories and chronicles of former times to be brought him. And when they were reading them before him,€€ƒxœThey came to that place where it was written, how Mardochai had discovered the treason of Bagathan and Thares the eunuchs, who sought to kill king Assuerus.€€ƒx³And when the king heard this, he said: What honour and reward hath Mardochai received for this fidelity? His servants and ministers said to him: He hath received no reward at all.€€˜ƒxVThe servants answered: Aman standeth in the court, and the king said: Let him come in.€€ƒxÍAnd when he was come in, he said to him: What ought to be done to the man whom the king is desirous to honour? But Aman thinking in his heart, and supposing that the king would honour no other but himself,€€ƒx3Answered: The man whom the king desireth to honour,€€ƒxOught to be clothed with the king's apparel, and to be set upon the horse that the king rideth upon, and to have the royal crown upon his head,€€ƒxÉAnd let the first of the king's princes and nobles hold his horse, and going through the street of the city, proclaim before him and say: Thus shall he be honoured, whom the king hath a mind to honour.€€ƒxäAnd the king said to him: Make haste and take the robe and the horse, and do as thou hast spoken to Mardochai the Jew, who sitteth before the gates of the palace. Beware thou pass over any of those things which thou hast spoken.€€ƒx×So Aman took the robe and the horse, and arraying Mardochai in the street of the city, and setting him on the horse, went before him, and proclaimed: This honour is he worthy of, whom the king hath a mind to honour.€€ƒxxBut Mardochai returned to the palace gate: and Aman made haste to go to his house, mourning and having his head covered:€€ƒyAnd he told Zares his wife, and his friends, all that had befallen him. And the wise men whom he had in counsel, and his wife answered him: If Mardochai be of the seed of the Jews, before whom thou hast begun to fall, thou canst not resist him, but thou shalt fall in his sight.€€ƒx€As they were yet speaking, the king's eunuchs came, and compelled him to go quickly to the banquet which the queen had prepared.€€ƒ`€€ƒx6So the king and Aman went in, to drink with the queen.€€ƒxâAnd the king said to her again the second day, after he was warm with wine: What is thy petition, Esther, that it may be granted thee? and what wilt thou have done: although thou ask the half of my kingdom, thou shalt have it.€€ƒxšThen she answered: If I have found favour in thy sight, O king, and if it please thee, give me my life for which I ask, and my people for which I request.€€ƒyFor we are given up, I and my people, to be destroyed, to be slain, and to perish. And would God we were sold for bondmen and bondwomen: the evil might be borne with, and I would have mourned in silence: but now we have an enemy, whose cruelty redoundeth upon the king.€€ƒxdAnd king Assuerus answered and said: Who is this, and of what power, that he should do these things?€€ƒx¾And Esther said: It is this Aman that is our adversary and most wicked enemy. Aman hearing this was forthwith astonished, not being able to bear the countenance of the king and of the queen.€€ƒxâBut the king being angry rose up, and went from the place of the banquet into the garden set with trees. Aman also rose up to entreat Esther the queen for his life, for he understood that evil was prepared for him by the king.€€ƒyHAnd when the king came back out of the garden set with trees, and entered into the place of the banquet, he found Aman was fallen upon the bed on which Esther lay, and he said: He will force the queen also in my presence, in my own house. The word was not yet gone out of the king's mouth, and immediately they covered his face.€€ƒxôAnd Harbona, one of the eunuchs that stood waiting on the king, said: Behold the gibbet which he hath prepared for Mardochai, who spoke for the king, standeth in Aman's house, being fifty cubits high. And the king said to him: Hang him upon it.€€ƒxcSo Aman was hanged on the gibbet, which he had prepared for Mardochai: and the king's wrath ceased.€€ƒ`€€ƒx±On that day king Assuerus gave the house of Aman, the Jews' enemy, to queen Esther, and Mardochai came in before the king. For Esther had confessed to him that he was her uncle.€€ƒx‘And the king took the ring which he had commanded to be taken again from Aman, and gave it to Mardochai. And Esther set Mardochai over her house.€€ƒy
And not content with these things, she fell down at the king's feet and wept, and speaking to him besought him, that he would give orders that the malice of Aman the Agagite, and his most wicked devices which he had invented against the Jews, should be of no effect.€€†ƒy8And said: If it please the king, and if I have found favour in his sight, and my request be not disagreeable to him, I beseech thee, that the former letters of Aman the traitor and enemy of the Jews, by which he commanded that they should be destroyed in all the king's provinces, may be reversed by new letters.€€ƒx>For how can I endure the murdering and slaughter of my people?€€ƒxÃAnd king Assuerus answered Esther the queen, and Mardochai the Jew: I have given Aman's house to Esther, and I have commanded him to be hanged on a gibbet, because he durst lay hands on the Jews.€€ƒxëWrite ye therefore to the Jews, as it pleaseth you in the king's name, and seal the letters with my ring. For this was the custom, that no man durst gainsay the letters which were sent in the king's name, and were sealed with his ring.€€ƒyThen the king's scribes and secretaries were called for (now it was the time of the third month which is called Siban) the three and twentieth day of the month, and letters were written, as Mardochai had a mind, to the Jews, and to the governors, and to the deputies, and to the judges, who were rulers over the hundred and twenty-seven provinces, from India even to Ethiopia: to province and province, to people and people, according to their languages and characters, and to the Jews, according as they could read and hear.€€ƒx¿And these letters which were sent in the king's name, were sealed with his ring, and sent by posts: who were to run through all the provinces, to prevent the former letters with new messages.€€ƒxqAnd one day of revenge was appointed through all the provinces, to wit, the thirteenth of the twelfth month Adar.€€ƒxÇAnd this was the content of the letter, that it should be notified in all lands and peoples that were subject to the empire of king Assuerus, that the Jews were ready to be revenged of their enemies.€€ƒx]So the swift posts went out carrying the messages, and the king's edict was hung up in Susan.€€ƒxşAnd Mardochai going forth out of the palace, and from the king's presence, shone in royal apparel, to wit, of violet and sky colour, wearing a golden crown on his head, and clothed with a cloak of silk and purple. And all the city rejoiced, and was glad.€€ƒxFBut to the Jews, a new light seemed to rise, joy, honour, and dancing.€€ƒyDAnd in all peoples, cities, and provinces, whithersoever the king's commandments came, there was wonderful rejoicing, feasts and banquets, and keeping holy day: Insomuch that many of other nations and religion, joined themselves to their worship and ceremonies. For a great dread of the name of the Jews had fallen upon all.€€ƒ`€€ƒy+So on the thirteenth day of the twelfth month, which as we have said above is called Adar, when all the Jews were designed to be massacred, and their enemies were greedy after their blood, the case being altered, the Jews began to have the upper hand, and to revenge themselves of their adversaries.€€ƒxáAnd they gathered themselves together in every city, and town, and place, to lay their hands on their enemies, and their persecutors. And no one durst withstand them, for the fear of their power had gone through every people.€€ƒx°And the judges of the provinces, and the governors, and lieutenants, and every one in dignity, that presided over every place and work, extolled the Jews for fear of Mardochai:€€ƒx For they knew him to be prince of the palace, and to have great power: and the fame of his name increased daily, and was spread abroad through all men's mouths.€€ƒxSo the Jews made a great slaughter of their enemies, and killed them, repaying according to what they had prepared to do to them:€€ƒx‘Insomuch that even in Susan they killed five hundred men, besides the ten sons of Aman the Agagite, the enemy of the Jews: whose names are these:€€ƒx'Pharsandatha, and Delphon, and Esphatha€€—ƒx3And Phermesta, and Arisai, and Aridai, and Jezatha.€€ƒxMAnd when they had slain them, they would not touch the spoils of their goods.€€ƒxSAnd presently the number of them that were killed in Susan was brought to the king.€€ƒxûAnd he said to the queen: The Jews have killed five hundred men in the city of Susan, besides the ten sons of Aman: how many dost thou think they have slain in all the provinces? What askest thou more, and what wilt thou have me to command to be done?€€ƒx´And she answered: If it please the king, let it be granted to the Jews, to do to morrow in Susan as they have done to day, and that the ten sons of Aman may be hanged upon gibbets.€€ƒx…And the king commanded that it should be so done. And forthwith the edict was hung up in Susan, and the ten sons of Aman were hanged.€€ƒx¡And on the fourteenth day of the month Adar the Jews gathered themselves together, and they killed in Susan three hundred men: but they took not their substance.€€ƒyMoreover through all the provinces which were subject to the king's dominion the Jews stood for their lives, and slew their enemies and persecutors: insomuch that the number of them that were killed amounted to seventy-five thousand, and no man took any of their goods.€€ƒyNow the thirteenth day of the month Adar was the first day with them all of the slaughter, and on the fourteenth day they left off. Which they ordained to be kept holy day, so that all times hereafter they should celebrate it with feasting, joy, and banquets.€€ƒxşBut they that were killing in the city of Susan, were employed in the slaughter on the thirteenth and fourteenth day of the same month: and on the fifteenth day they rested. And therefore they appointed that day to be a holy day of feasting and gladness.€€ƒxáBut those Jews that dwelt in towns not walled and in villages, appointed the fourteenth day of the month Adar for banquets and gladness, so as to rejoice on that day, and send one another portions of their banquets and meats.€€ƒx©And Mardochai wrote all these things, and sent them comprised in letters to the Jews that abode in all the king's provinces, both those that lay near and those afar off,€€ƒx©That they should receive the fourteenth and fifteenth day of the month Adar for holy days, and always at the return of the year should celebrate them with solemn honour:€€ƒyBecause on those days the Jews revenged themselves of their enemies, and their mourning and sorrow were turned into mirth and joy, and that these should be days of feasting and gladness, in which they should send one to another portions of meats, and should give gifts to the poor.€€ƒxŒAnd the Jews undertook to observe with solemnity all they had begun to do at that time, which Mardochai by letters had commanded to be done.€€ƒxºFor Aman, the son of Amadathi of the race of Agag, the enemy and adversary of the Jews, had devised evil against them, to kill them and destroy them; and had cast Phur, that is, the lot.€€ƒy␀And afterwards Esther went in to the king, beseeching him that his endeavors might be made void by the king's letters: and the evil that he had intended against the Jews, might return upon his own head. And so both he and his sons were hanged upon gibbets.€€ƒxİAnd since that time these days are called Phurim, that is, of lots: because Phur, that is, the lot, was cast into the urn. And all things that were done, are contained in the volume of this epistle, that is, of this book:€€ƒyaAnd the things that they suffered, and that were afterwards changed, the Jews took upon themselves and their seed, and upon all that had a mind to be joined to their religion, so that it should be lawful for none to pass these days without solemnity: which the writing testifieth, and certain times require, as the years continually succeed one another.€€ƒy,These are the days which shall never be forgot: and which all provinces in the whole world shall celebrate throughout all generations: neither is there any city wherein the days of Phurim, that is, of lots, must not be observed by the Jews, and by their posterity, which is bound to these ceremonies.€€ƒxºAnd Esther the queen, the daughter of Abihail, and Mardochai the Jew, wrote also a second epistle, that with all diligence this day should be established a festival for the time to come.€€ƒx“And they sent to all the Jews that were in the hundred and twenty-seven provinces of king Assuerus, that they should have peace, and receive truth,€€ƒxæAnd observe the days of lots, and celebrate them with joy in their proper time: as Mardochai and Esther had appointed, and they undertook them to be observed by themselves and by their seed, fasts, and cries, and the days of lots,€€˜ƒ`€€ƒxNAnd king Assuerus made all the land, and all the islands of the sea tributary.€€ƒxšAnd his strength and his empire, and the dignity and greatness wherewith he exalted Mardochai, are written in the books of the Medes, and of the Persians:€€ƒxòAnd how Mardochai of the race of the Jews, was next after king Assuerus: and great among the Jews, and acceptable to the people of his brethren, seeking the good of his people, and speaking those things which were for the welfare of his seed.€€ƒx0Then Mardochai said: God hath done these things.€€ƒxbI remember a dream that I saw, which signified these same things: and nothing thereof hath failed.€€ƒx¬The little fountain which grew into a river, and was turned into a light, and into the sun, and abounded into many waters, is Esther, whom the king married, and made queen.€€ƒx#But the two dragons are I and Aman.€€ƒxZThe nations that were assembled are they that endeavoured to destroy the name of the Jews.€€ƒx®And my nation is Israel, who cried to the Lord, and the Lord saved his people: and he delivered us from all evils, and hath wrought great signs and wonders among the nations:€€ƒxkAnd he commanded that there should be two lots, one of the people of God, and the other of all the nations.€€ƒxYAnd both lots came to the day appointed already from that time before God to all nations:€€ƒxEAnd the Lord remembered his people, and had mercy on his inheritance.€€ƒxõAnd these days shall be observed in the month of Adar on the fourteenth, and fifteenth day of the same month, with all diligence, and joy of the people gathered into one assembly, throughout all the generations hereafter of the people of Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxùIn the fourth year of the reign of Ptolemy and Cleopatra, Dositheus, who said he was a priest, and of the Levitical race, and Ptolemy his son brought this epistle of Phurim, which they said Lysimachus the son of Ptolemy had interpreted in Jerusalem.€€ƒx´In the second year of the reign of Artaxerxes the great, in the first day of the month Nisan, Mardochai the son of Jair, the son of Semei, the son of Cis, of the tribe of Benjamin:€€ƒxgA Jew who dwelt in the city of Susan, a great man and among the first of the king's court, had a dream.€€ƒxŠNow he was of the number of the captives, whom Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon had carried away from Jerusalem with Jechonias king of Juda:€€ƒxAnd this was his dream: Behold there were voices, and tumults, and thunders, and earthquakes, and a disturbance upon the earth.€€ƒxKAnd behold two great dragons came forth ready to fight one against another.€€ƒxUAnd at their cry all nations were stirred up to fight against the nation of the just.€€ƒxfAnd that was a day of darkness and danger, of tribulation and distress, and great fear upon the earth.€€ƒx\And the nation of the just was troubled fearing their own evils, and was prepared for death.€€ƒx~And they cried to God: and as they were crying, a little fountain grew into a very great river, and abounded into many waters.€€ƒx[The light and the sun rose up, and the humble were exalted, and they devoured the glorious.€€ƒx°And when Mardochai had seen this, and arose out of his bed, he was thinking what God would do: and he kept it fixed in his mind, desirous to know what the dream should signify.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒx½And when he understood their designs, and had diligently searched into their projects, he learned that they went about to lay violent hands on king Artaxerxes, and he told the king thereof.€€ƒxfThen the king had them both examined, and after they had confessed, commanded them to be put to death.€€ƒxmBut the king made a record of what was done: and Mardochai also committed the memory of the thing to writing.€€ƒxkAnd the king commanded him, to abide in the court of the palace, and gave him presents for the information.€€ƒxµBut Aman the son of Amadathi the Bugite was in great honour with the king, and sought to hurt Mardochai and his people, because of the two eunuchs of the king who were put to death.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÕAnd this was the copy of the letter: Artaxerxes the great king who reigneth from India to Ethiopia, to the princes and governors of the hundred and twenty-seven provinces, that are subject to his empire, greeting.€€ƒyWhereas I reigned over many nations, and had brought all the world under my dominion, I was not willing to abuse the greatness of my power, but to govern my subjects with clemency and that they might live quietly without any terror, and might enjoy peace, which is desired by all men,€€ƒxŸBut when I asked my counsellors how this might be accomplished, one that excelled the rest in wisdom and fidelity, and was second after the king, Aman by name,€€ƒxæTold me that there was a people scattered through the whole world, which used new laws, and acted against the customs of all nations, despised the commandments of kings, and violated by their opposition the concord of all nations.€€ƒxÏWherefore having learned this, and seeing one nation in opposition to all mankind using perverse laws, and going against our commandments, and disturbing the peace and concord of the provinces subject to us,€€ƒyMWe have commanded that all whom Aman shall mark out, who is chief over all the provinces, and second after the king, and whom we honour as a father, shall be utterly destroyed by their enemies, with their wives and children, and that none shall have pity on them, on the fourteenth day of the twelfth month Adar of this present year:€€ƒxrThat these wicked men going down to hell in one day, may restore to our empire the peace which they had disturbed.€€ƒx;But Mardochai besought the Lord, remembering all his works,€€ƒx•And said: O Lord, Lord, almighty king, for all things are in thy power, and there is none that can resist thy will, if thou determine to save Israel.€€ƒxQThou hast made heaven and earth and all things that are under the cope of heaven.€€ƒxDThou art Lord of all, and there is none that can resist thy majesty.€€ƒxŒThou knowest all things, and thou knowest that it was not out of pride and or any desire of glory, that I refused to worship the proud Aman,€€ƒxg(For I would willingly and readily for the salvation of Israel have kissed even the steps of his feet,)€€ƒxqBut I feared lest I should transfer the honour of my God to a man, and lest I should adore any one except my God.€€ƒxAnd now, O Lord, O king, O God of Abraham, have mercy on thy people, because our enemies resolve to destroy us, and extinguish thy inheritance.€€ƒxKDespise not thy portion, which thou hast redeemed for thyself out of Egypt.€€ƒxÂHear my supplication, and be merciful to thy lot and inheritance, and turn our mourning into joy, that we may live and praise thy name, O Lord, and shut not the mouths of them that sing to thee.€€ƒxzAnd all Israel with like mind and supplication cried to the Lord, because they saw certain death hanging over their heads.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒy:And when she had laid away her royal apparel, she put on garments suitable for weeping and mourning: instead of divers precious ointments, she covered her head with ashes and dung, and she humbled her body with fasts: and all the places in which before she was accustomed to rejoice, she filled with her torn hair.€€ƒx™And she prayed to the Lord the God of Israel, saying: O my Lord, who alone art our king, help me a desolate woman, and who have no other helper but thee.€€ƒxMy danger is in my hands.€€ƒxäI have heard of my father that thou, O Lord, didst take Israel from among all nations, and our fathers from all their predecessors, to possess them as an everlasting inheritance, and thou hast done to them as thou hast promised.€€ƒx`We have sinned in thy sight, and therefore thou hast delivered us into the hands of our enemies:€€ƒx9For we have worshipped their gods. Thou art just, O Lord.€€ƒx‹And now they are not content to oppress us with most hard bondage, but attributing the strength of their hands to the power of their idols,€€ƒx They design to change thy promises, and destroy thy inheritance, and shut the mouths of them that praise thee, and extinguish the glory of thy temple and altar,€€ƒxpThat they may open the mouths of Gentiles, and praise the strength of idols, and magnify for ever a carnal king.€€ƒx¬Give not, O Lord, thy sceptre to them that are not, lest they laugh at our ruin: but turn their counsel upon themselves, and destroy him that hath begun to rage against us.€€ƒx†Remember, O Lord, and shew thyself to us in the time of our tribulation, and give me boldness, O Lord, king of gods, and of all power:€€ƒx¸Give me a well ordered speech in my mouth in the presence of the lion, and turn his heart to the hatred of our enemy, that both he himself may perish, and the rest that consent to him.€€ƒxzBut deliver us by thy hand, and help me, who have no other helper, but thee, O Lord, who hast the knowledge of all things.€€ƒxtAnd thou knowest that I hate the glory of the wicked, and abhor the bed of the uncircumcised, and of every stranger.€€ƒxÔThou knowest my necessity, that I abominate the sign of my pride and glory, which is upon my head in the days of my public appearance, and detest it as a menstruous rag, and wear it not in the days of my silence,€€ƒxAnd that I have not eaten at Aman's table, nor hath the king's banquet pleased me, and that I have not drunk the wine of the drink offerings:€€ƒx|And that thy handmaid hath never rejoiced, since I was brought hither unto this day but in thee, O Lord, the God of Abraham.€€ƒxšO God, who art mighty above all, hear the voice of them, that have no other hope, and deliver us from the hand of the wicked, and deliver me from my fear.€€ƒ`€€ƒxyAnd he commanded her (no doubt but he was Mardochai) to go to the king, and petition for her people, and for her country.€€ƒxŸRemember, (said he,) the days of thy low estate, how thou wast brought up by my hand, because Aman the second after the king hath spoken against us unto death.€€ƒxXAnd do thou call upon the Lord, and speak to the king for us, and deliver us from death.€€ƒxZAnd on the third day she laid away the garments she wore, and put on her glorious apparel.€€ƒxwAnd glittering in royal robes, after she had called upon God the ruler and Saviour of all, she took two maids with her,€€ƒxzAnd upon one of them she leaned, as if for delicateness and overmuch tenderness she were not able to bear up her own body.€€ƒxQAnd the other maid followed her lady, bearing up her train flowing on the ground.€€ƒxBut she with a rosy colour in her face, and with gracious and bright eyes hid a mind full of anguish, and exceeding great fear.€€ƒxİSo going in she passed through all doors in order, and stood before the king, where he sat upon his royal throne, clothed with his royal robes, and glittering with gold, and precious stones, and he was terrible to behold.€€ƒxÄAnd when he had lifted up his countenance, and with burning eyes had shewn the wrath of his heart, the queen sunk down, and her colour turned pale, and she rested her weary head upon her handmaid.€€ƒxÁAnd God changed the king's spirit into mildness, and all in haste and in fear he leaped from his throne, and holding her up in his arms, till she came to herself, caressed her with these words:€€ƒx7What is the matter, Esther? I am thy brother, fear not.€€˜(ƒx&Come near then, and touch the sceptre.€€ƒxŠAnd as she held her peace, he took the golden sceptre, and laid it upon her neck, and kissed her, and said: Why dost thou not speak to me?€€ƒxiShe answered: I saw thee, my lord, as an angel of God, and my heart was troubled for fear of thy majesty.€€ƒxFFor thou, my lord, art very admirable, and thy face is full of graces.€€ƒxKAnd while she was speaking, she fell down again, and was almost in a swoon.€€ƒx>But the king was troubled, and all his servants comforted her.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¢The great king Artaxerxes, from India to Ethiopia, to the governors and princes of a hundred and twenty-seven provinces, which obey our command, sendeth greeting.€€ƒxfMany have abused unto pride the goodness of princes, and the honour that hath been bestowed upon them:€€ƒxAnd not only endeavour to oppress the king's subjects, but not bearing the glory that is given them, take in hand, to practise also against them that gave it.€€ƒxÄNeither are they content not to return thanks for benefits received, and to violate in themselves the laws of humanity, but they think they can also escape the justice of God who seeth all things.€€ƒxÎAnd they break out into so great madness, as to endeavour to undermine by lies such as observe diligently the offices committed to them, and do all things in such manner as to be worthy of all men's praise,€€ƒxxWhile with crafty fraud they deceive the ears of princes that are well meaning, and judge of others by their own nature.€€ƒxªNow this is proved both from ancient histories, and by the things which are done daily, how the good designs of kings are depraved by the evil suggestions of certain men.€€ƒx9Wherefore we must provide for the peace of all provinces.€€ƒxÙNeither must you think, if we command different things, that it cometh of the levity of our mind, but that we give sentence according to the quality and necessity of times, as the profit of the commonwealth requireth.€€ƒxğNow that you may more plainly understand what we say, Aman the son of Amadathi, a Macedonian both in mind and country, and having nothing of the Persian blood, but with his cruelty staining our goodness, was received being a stranger by us:€€ƒx…And found our humanity so great towards him, that he was called our father, and was worshipped by all as the next man after the king:€€ƒxaBut he was so far puffed up with arrogancy, as to go about to deprive us of our kingdom and life.€€ƒxĞFor with certain new and unheard of devices he hath sought the destruction of Mardochai, by whose fidelity and good services our life was saved, and of Esther the partner of our kingdom with all their nation:€€ƒx¤Thinking that after they were slain, he might work treason against us left alone without friends, and might transfer the kingdom of the Persians to the Macedonians.€€ƒx‘But we have found that the Jews, who were by that most wicked man appointed to be slain, are in no fault at all, but contrariwise, use just laws,€€ƒx¯And are the children of the highest and the greatest, and the ever living God, by whose benefit the kingdom was given both to our fathers and to us, and is kept unto this day.€€ƒxZWherefore know ye that those letters which he sent in our name, are void and of no effect.€€ƒx§For which crime both he himself that devised it, and all his kindred hang on gibbets, before the gates of this city Susan: not we, but God repaying him as he deserved.€€ƒxtBut this edict, which we now send, shall be published in all cities, that the Jews may freely follow their own laws.€€ƒx›And you shall aid them that they may kill those who had prepared themselves to kill them, on the thirteenth day of the twelfth month, which is called Adar.€€ƒxSFor the almighty God hath turned this day of sadness and mourning into joy to them.€€ƒxWherefore you shall also count this day among other festival days, and celebrate it with all joy, that it may be known also in times to come,€€ƒx¬That all they who faithfully obey the Persians, receive a worthy reward for their fidelity: but they that are traitors to their kingdom, are destroyed for their wickedness.€€ƒxîAnd let every province and city, that will not be partaker of this solemnity, perish by the sword and by fire, and be destroyed in such manner as to be made unpassable, both to men and beasts, for an example of contempt, and disobedience.€€ƒoThe Book of Job€€ƒ`€€ƒx€There was a man in the land of Hus, whose name was Job, and that man was simple and upright, and fearing God, and avoiding evil.€€ƒx:And there were born to him seven sons and three daughters.€€ƒxÛAnd his possession was seven thousand sheep, and three thousand camels, and five hundred yoke of oxen, and five hundred she asses, and a family exceedingly great: and this man was great among all the people of the east.€€ƒxAnd his sons went, and made a feast by houses, every one in his day. And sending, they called their three sisters, to eat and drink with them.€€ƒyAnd when the days of their feasting were gone about, Job sent to them, and sanctified them: and rising up early, offered holocausts for every one of them. For he said: Lest perhaps my sons have sinned, and have blessed God in their hearts. So did Job all days.€€ƒxlNow on a certain day, when the sons of God came to stand before the Lord, Satan also was present among them.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to him: Whence comest thou? And he answered and said: I have gone round about the earth, and walked through it.€€–ƒx5And Satan answering, said: Doth Job fear God in vain?€€ƒx¥Hast thou not made a fence for him, and his house, and all his substance round about, blessed the works of his hands, and his possession hath increased on the earth?€€ƒxjBut stretch forth thy hand a little, and touch all that he hath, and see if he bless thee not to thy face.€€ƒx¦Then the Lord said to Satan: Behold, all that he hath is in thy hand: only put not forth thy hand upon his person. And Satan went forth from the presence of the Lord.€€ƒxxNow upon a certain day, when his sons and daughters were eating and drinking wine, in the house of their eldest brother,€€ƒxdThere came a messenger to Job, and said: The oxen were ploughing, and the asses feeding beside them,€€ƒxzAnd the Sabeans rushed in, and took all away, and slew the servants with the sword; and I alone have escaped to tell thee.€€ƒx¼And while he was yet speaking, another came, and said: The fire of God fell from heaven, and striking the sheep and the servants, hath consumed them; and I alone have escaped to tell thee.€€ƒxñAnd while he also was yet speaking, there came another, and said: The Chaldeans made three troops, and have fallen upon the camels, and taken them; moreover, they have slain the servants with the sword: and I alone have escaped to tell thee.€€ƒx•He was yet speaking, and behold another came in, and said: Thy sons and daughters were eating and drinking wine in the house of their eldest brother,€€ƒx¾A violent wind came on a sudden from the side of the desert, and shook the four corners of the house, and it fell upon thy children, and they are dead: and I alone have escaped to tell thee.€€ƒxoThen Job rose up, and rent his garments, and having shaven his head, fell down upon the ground, and worshipped,€€ƒxËAnd said: Naked came I out of my mother's womb, and naked shall I return thither: the Lord gave, and the Lord hath taken away: as it hath pleased the Lord, so is it done: blessed be the name of the Lord.€€ƒx[In all these things Job sinned not by his lips, nor spoke he any foolish thing against God.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAnd it came to pass, when on a certain day the sons of God came, and stood before the Lord, and Satan came amongst them, and stood in his sight,€€ƒx…That the Lord said to Satan: Whence comest thou? And he answered, and said: I have gone round about the earth, and walked through it.€€ƒyAnd the Lord said to Satan: Hast thou considered my servant, Job, that there is none like him in the earth, a man simple and upright, and fearing God, and avoiding evil, and still keeping his innocence? But thou hast moved me against him, that I should afflict him without cause.€€ƒx`And Satan answered, and said: Skin for skin; and all that a man hath, he will give for his life:€€ƒxvBut put forth thy hand, and touch his bone and his flesh, and then thou shalt see that he will bless thee to thy face.€€ƒxMAnd the Lord said to Satan: Behold, he is in thy hand, but yet save his life.€€ƒx”So Satan went forth from the presence of the Lord, and struck Job with a very grievous ulcer, from the sole of the foot even to the top of his head:€€˜!ƒxXAnd his wife said to him: Dost thou still continue in thy simplicity? bless God and die.€€ƒxÊAnd he said to her: Thou hast spoken like one of the foolish women: If we have received good things at the hand of God, why should we not receive evil? In all these things Job did not sin with his lips.€€ƒyNow when Job's three friends heard all the evil that had befallen him, they came every one from his own place, Eliphaz, the Themanite, and Baldad, the Suhite, and Sophar, the Naamathite. For they had made an appointment to come together and visit him, and comfort him.€€ƒx®And when they had lifted up their eyes afar off, they knew him not, and crying out, they wept, and rending their garments, they sprinkled dust upon their heads toward heaven.€€ƒxŠAnd they sat with him on the ground seven day and seven nights and no man spoke to him a word: for they saw that his grief was very great.€€ƒ`€€ƒx5After this, Job opened his mouth, and cursed his day,€€ƒlAnd he said:€€ƒxdLet the day perish wherein I was born, and the night in which it was said: A man child is conceived.€€ƒxlLet that day be turned into darkness, let not God regard it from above, and let not the light shine upon it.€€ƒxrLet darkness, and the shadow of death, cover it, let a mist overspread it, and let it be wrapped up in bitterness.€€ƒxzLet a darksome whirlwind seize upon that night, let it not be counted in the days of the year, nor numbered in the months.€€ƒx5Let that night be solitary, and not worthy of praise.€€ƒxKLet them curse it who curse the day, who are ready to raise up a leviathan:€€ƒxLet the stars be darkened with the mist thereof: let it expect light, and not see it, nor the rising of the dawning of the day:€€ƒx\Because it shut not up the doors of the womb that bore me, nor took away evils from my eyes.€€ƒxQWhy did I not die in the womb? why did I not perish when I came out of the belly?€€ƒx8Why received upon the knees? why suckled at the breasts?€€ƒxNFor now I should have been asleep and still, and should have rest in my sleep:€€ƒxDWith kings and consuls of the earth, who build themselves solitudes:€€ƒxFOr with princes, that possess gold, and fill their houses with silver:€€ƒxjOr as a hidden untimely birth, I should not be; or as they that, being conceived, have not seen the light.€€ƒxRThere the wicked cease from tumult, and there the wearied in strength are at rest.€€ƒx]And they sometime bound together without disquiet, have not heard the voice of the oppressor.€€ƒxGThe small and great are there, and the servant is free from his master.€€ƒx]Why is light given to him that is in misery, and life to them that are in bitterness of soul?€€ƒxHThat look for death, and it cometh not, as they that dig for a treasure:€€ƒx<And they rejoice exceedingly when they have found the grave?€€ƒxHTo a man whose way is hidden, and God hath surrounded him with darkness?€€ƒxABefore I eat I sigh: and as overflowing waters, so is my roaring:€€ƒxaFor the fear which I feared, hath come upon me: and that which I was afraid of, hath befallen me.€€ƒxgHave I not dissembled? have I not kept silence? have I not been quiet? and indignation is come upon me.€€ƒ`€€˜%ƒxnIf we begin to speak to thee, perhaps thou wilt take it ill; but who can withhold the words he hath conceived?€€ƒxIBehold thou hast taught many, and thou hast strengthened the weary hands:€€ƒxcThy words have confirmed them that were staggering, and thou hast strengthened the trembling knees:€€ƒxfBut now the scourge is come upon thee, and thou faintest: It hath touched thee, and thou art troubled.€€ƒxOWhere is thy fear, thy fortitude, thy patience, and the perfection of thy ways?€€ƒxYRemember, I pray thee, who ever perished being innocent? or when were the just destroyed?€€ƒxUOn the contrary, I have seen those who work iniquity, and sow sorrows, and reap them,€€ƒxGPerishing by the blast of God, and consumed by the spirit of his wrath.€€ƒxhThe roaring of the lion, and the voice of the lioness, and the teeth of the whelps of lions, are broken:€€ƒxSThe tiger hath perished for want of prey, and the young lions are scattered abroad.€€ƒxtNow there was a word spoken to me in private, and my ears by stealth, as it were, received the veins of its whisper.€€ƒxHIn the horror of a vision by night, when deep sleep is wont to hold men,€€ƒxEFear seized upon me, and trembling, and all my bones were affrighted:€€ƒxBAnd when a spirit passed before me, the hair of my flesh stood up.€€ƒx{There stood one whose countenance I knew not, an image before my eyes, and I heard the voice, as it were, of a gentle wind.€€ƒxXShall man be justified in comparison of God, or shall a man be more pure than his maker?€€ƒxUBehold, they that serve him are not steadfast, and in his angels he found wickedness:€€ƒxtHow much more shall they that dwell in houses of clay, who have an earthly foundation, be consumed as with the moth?€€ƒxoFrom morning till evening they shall be cut down: and because no one understandeth, they shall perish for ever.€€ƒx^And they that shall be left, shall be taken away from them: they shall die, and not in wisdom.€€ƒ`€€ƒxPCall now, if there be any that will answer thee, and turn to some of the saints.€€ƒxBAnger indeed killeth the foolish, and envy slayeth the little one.€€ƒxKI have seen a fool with a strong root, and I cursed his beauty immediately.€€ƒxsHis children shall be far from safety, and shall be destroyed in the gate, and there shall be none to deliver them.€€ƒx|Whose harvest the hungry shall eat, and the armed man shall take him by violence, and the thirsty shall drink up his riches.€€ƒxYNothing upon earth is done without a cause, and sorrow doth not spring out of the ground.€€ƒx+Man is born to labour, and the bird to fly.€€ƒx@Wherefore I will pray to the Lord, and address my speech to God:€€ƒxLWho doth great things, and unsearchable and wonderful things without number:€€ƒxPWho giveth rain upon the face of the earth, and watereth all things with waters:€€ƒxOWho setteth up the humble on high, and comforteth with health those that mourn.€€ƒxoWho bringeth to nought the designs of the malignant, so that their hands cannot accomplish what they had begun:€€ƒxWWho catcheth the wise in their craftiness, and disappointeth the counsel of the wicked:€€ƒxOThey shall meet with darkness in the day, and grope at noonday as in the night.€€ƒxeBut he shall save the needy from the sword of their mouth, and the poor from the hand of the violent.€€ƒxKAnd to the needy there shall be hope, but iniquity shall draw in her mouth.€€ƒxCFor he woundeth, and cureth: he striketh, and his hands shall heal.€€ƒxUIn six troubles he shall deliver thee, and in the seventh, evil shall not touch thee.€€ƒxVIn famine he shall deliver thee from death; and in battle, from the hand of the sword.€€ƒxeThou shalt be hidden from the scourge of the tongue: and thou shalt not fear calamity when it cometh.€€ƒxdIn destruction and famine thou shalt laugh: and thou shalt not be afraid of the beasts of the earth.€€ƒxuBut thou shalt have a covenant with the stones of the lands, and the beasts of the earth shall be at peace with thee.€€ƒxaAnd thou shalt know that thy tabernacle is in peace, and visiting thy beauty, thou shalt not sin.€€ƒxfThou shalt know also that thy seed shall be multiplied, and thy offspring like the grass of the earth.€€ƒxZThou shalt enter into the grave in abundance, as a heap of wheat is brought in its season.€€ƒxnBehold, this is even so, as we have searched out: which thou having heard, consider it thoroughly in thy mind.€€ƒ`€€ƒxBut Job answered, and said:€€ƒxiO that my sins, whereby I have deserved wrath, and the calamity that I suffer, were weighed in a balance.€€ƒxZAs the sand of the sea, this would appear heavier: therefore, my words are full of sorrow:€€ƒxyFor the arrows of the Lord are in me, the rage whereof drinketh up my spirit, and the terrors of the Lord war against me.€€ƒxdWill the wild ass bray when he hath grass? or will the ox low when he standeth before a full manger?€€˜!ƒxTThe things which before my soul would not touch, now, through anguish, are my meats.€€ƒxRWho will grant that my request may come: and that God may give me what I look for?€€ƒx[And that he that hath begun may destroy me, that he may let loose his hand, and cut me off?€€ƒxzAnd that this may be my comfort, that afflicting me with sorrow, he spare not, nor I contradict the words of the Holy one.€€ƒx]For what is my strength, that I can hold out? or what is my end, that I should keep patience?€€ƒxDMy strength is not the strength of stones, nor is my flesh of brass.€€ƒx\Behold there is no help for me in myself, and my familiar friends also are departed from me.€€ƒxJHe that taketh away mercy from his friend, forsaketh the fear of the Lord.€€ƒxRMy brethren have passed by me, as the torrent that passeth swiftly in the valleys.€€ƒx>They that fear the hoary frost, the snow shall fall upon them.€€ƒx~At the time when they shall be scattered they shall perish: and after it groweth hot, they shall be melted out of their place.€€ƒxRThe paths of their steps are entangled: they shall walk in vain, and shall perish.€€ƒxGConsider the paths of Thema, the ways of Saba, and wait a little while.€€ƒxgThey are confounded, because I have hoped: they are come also even unto me, and are covered with shame.€€ƒx@Now you are come: and now, seeing my affliction, you are afraid.€€ƒx6Did I say: Bring to me, and give me of your substance?€€ƒxVOr deliver me from the hand of the enemy, and rescue me out of the hand of the mighty?€€ƒxZTeach me, and I will hold my peace: and if I have been ignorant of any thing, instruct me.€€ƒx\Why have you detracted the words of truth, whereas there is none of you that can reprove me?€€ƒxFYou dress up speeches only to rebuke, and you utter words to the wind.€€ƒxLYou rush in upon the fatherless, and you endeavour to overthrow your friend.€€ƒxDHowever, finish what you have begun: give ear and see whether I lie.€€ƒxUAnswer, I beseech you, without contention: and speaking that which is just, judge ye.€€ƒxTAnd you shall not find iniquity in my tongue, neither shall folly sound in my mouth.€€ƒ`€€ƒxVThe life of man upon earth is a warfare, and his days are like the days of a hireling.€€ƒxTAs a servant longeth for the shade, as the hireling looketh for the end of his work;€€ƒxNSo I also have had empty months, and have numbered to myself wearisome nights.€€ƒx•If I lie down to sleep, I shall say: When shall I rise? and again, I shall look for the evening, and shall be filled with sorrows even till darkness.€€ƒxbMy flesh is clothed with rottenness and the filth of dust; my skin is withered and drawn together.€€ƒxfMy days have passed more swiftly than the web is cut by the weaver, and are consumed without any hope.€€ƒxRRemember that my life is but wind, and my eye shall not return to see good things.€€ƒxSNor shall the sight of man behold me: thy eyes are upon me, and I shall be no more.€€˜ƒxVNor shall he return any more into his house, neither shall his place know him any more€€ƒx~Wherefore, I will not spare my mouth, I will speak in the affliction of my spirit: I will talk with the bitterness of my soul.€€ƒx?Am I a sea, or a whale, that thou hast enclosed me in a prison?€€ƒx]If I say: My bed shall comfort me, and I shall be relieved, speaking with myself on my couch:€€ƒx?Thou wilt frighten me with dreams, and terrify me with visions.€€ƒx<So that my soul rather chooseth hanging, and my bones death.€€ƒxUI have done with hope, I shall now live no longer: spare me, for my days are nothing.€€ƒxVWhat is a man, that thou shouldst magnify him or why dost thou set thy heart upon him?€€ƒxFThou visitest him early in the morning, and thou provest him suddenly.€€ƒxJHow long wilt thou not spare me, nor suffer me to swallow down my spittle?€€ƒx…I have sinned: what shall I do to thee, O keeper of men? why hast thou set me opposite to thee. and am I become burdensome to myself?€€ƒx§Why dost thou not remove my sin, and why dost thou not take away my iniquity? Behold now I shall sleep in the dust: and if thou seek me in the morning, I shall not be.€€ƒ`€€ƒx,Then Baldad, the Suhite, answered, and said:€€ƒxgHow long wilt thou speak these things, and how long shall the words of thy mouth be like a strong wind?€€ƒxMDoth God pervert judgment, or doth the Almighty overthrow that which is just?€€ƒxcAlthough thy children have sinned against him, and he hath left them in the hand of their iniquity:€€ƒxCYet if thou wilt arise early to God, and wilt beseech the Almighty:€€ƒx|If thou wilt walk clean and upright, he will presently awake unto thee, and will make the dwelling of thy justice peaceable:€€ƒxcIn so much, that if thy former things were small thy latter things would be multiplied exceedingly.€€ƒx[For inquire of the former generation, and search diligently into the memory of the fathers:€€ƒxY(For we are but of yesterday, and are ignorant that our days upon earth are but a shadow)€€ƒxYAnd they shall teach thee: they shall speak to thee, and utter words out of their hearts.€€ƒxICan the rush be green without moisture? or sedge bush grow without water?€€˜)ƒxXEven so are the ways of all that forget God, and the hope of the hypocrite shall perish:€€ƒxMHis folly shall not please him, and his trust shall be like the spider's web.€€ƒxaHe shall lean upon his house, and it shall not stand: he shall prop it up, and it shall not rise:€€ƒxdHe seemeth to have moisture before the sun cometh; and at his rising, his blossom shall shoot forth.€€ƒxTHis roots shall be thick upon a heap of stones; and among the stones he shall abide.€€ƒxZIf one swallow him up out of his place, he shall deny him, and shall say: I know thee not.€€ƒxNFor this is the joy of his way, that others may spring again out of the earth.€€ƒxKGod will not cast away the simple, nor reach out his hand to the evil doer:€€ƒxEUntil thy mouth be filled with laughter, and thy lips with rejoicing.€€ƒxeThey that hate thee, shall be clothed with confusion: and the dwelling of the wicked shall not stand.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAnd Job answered, and said:€€ƒxLIndeed I know it is so, and that man cannot be justified, compared with God.€€ƒxEIf he will contend with him, he cannot answer him one for a thousand.€€ƒxWHe is wise in heart, and mighty in strength: who hath resisted him, and hath had peace?€€ƒxQWho hath removed mountains, and they whom he overthrew in his wrath, knew it not.€€ƒxHWho shaketh the earth out of her place, and the pillars thereof tremble.€€ƒx_Who commandeth the sun, and it riseth not: and shutteth up the stars, as it were, under a seal:€€ƒxKWho alone spreadeth out the heavens, and walketh upon the waves of the sea.€€ƒxMWho maketh Arcturus, and Orion, and Hyades, and the inner parts of the south.€€ƒxWWho doth things great and incomprehensible, and wonderful, of which there is no number.€€ƒxLIf he come to me, I shall not see him: if he depart, I shall not understand.€€ƒxRIf he examine on a sudden, who shall answer him? or who can say: Why dost thou so?€€ƒxUGod, whose wrath no man can resist, and under whom they stoop that bear up the world.€€ƒxBWhat am I then, that I should answer him, and have words with him?€€ƒxhI, who although I should have any just thing, would not answer, but would make supplication to my judge.€€ƒxVAnd if he should hear me when I call, I should not believe that he had heard my voice.€€ƒxPFor he shall crush me in a whirlwind, and multiply my wounds even without cause.€€ƒxEHe alloweth not my spirit to rest, and he filleth me with bitterness.€€ƒxcIf strength be demanded, he is most strong: if equity of judgment, no man dare bear witness for me.€€ƒxtIf I would justify myself, my own mouth shall condemn me: if I would shew myself innocent, he shall prove me wicked.€€ƒxeAlthough I should be simple, even this my soul shall be ignorant of, and I shall be weary of my life.€€ƒxUOne thing there is that I have spoken, both the innocent and the wicked he consumeth.€€ƒxPIf he scourge, let him kill at once, and not laugh at the pains of the innocent.€€ƒx€The earth is given into the hand of the wicked, he covereth the face of the judges thereof: and if it be not he, who is it then?€€ƒxRMy days have been swifter than a post: they have fled away and have not seen good.€€ƒxMThey have passed by as ships carrying fruits, as an eagle flying to the prey.€€ƒxNIf I say: I will not speak so: I change my face, and am tormented with sorrow.€€ƒxFI feared all my works, knowing that thou didst not spare the offender.€€ƒx8But if so also I am wicked, why have I laboured in vain?€€ƒxUIf I be washed, as it were, with snow waters, and my hands shall shine ever so clean:€€˜ƒxhFor I shall not answer a man that is like myself: nor one that may be heard with me equally in judgment.€€ƒxQThere is none that may be able to reprove both, and to put his hand between both.€€ƒxCLet him take his rod away from me, and let not his fear terrify me.€€ƒxLI will speak, and will not fear him: for I cannot answer while I am in fear.€€ƒ`€€ƒxoMy soul is weary of my life, I will let go my speech against myself, I will speak in the bitterness of my soul.€€ƒxEI will say to God: Do not condemn me: tell me why thou judgest me so?€€ƒxŠDoth it seem good to thee that thou shouldst calumniate me, and oppress me, the work of thy own hands, and help the counsel of the wicked?€€ƒx9Hast thou eyes of flesh: or, shalt thou see as man seeth?€€ƒxGAre thy days as the days of man, and are thy years as the times of men:€€ƒxFThat thou shouldst inquire after my iniquity, and search after my sin?€€ƒxmAnd shouldst know that I have done no wicked thing, whereas there is no man that can deliver out of thy hand?€€ƒxrThy hands have made me, and fashioned me wholly round about, and dost thou thus cast me down headlong on a sudden?€€ƒx^Remember, I beseech thee, that thou hast made me as the clay, and thou wilt bring me into dust€€ƒx<Hast thou not milked me as milk, and curdled me like cheese?€€ƒxZThou hast clothed me with skin and flesh: thou hast put me together with bones and sinews:€€ƒxQThou hast granted me life and mercy, and thy visitation hath preserved my spirit.€€ƒx]Although thou conceal these things in thy heart, yet I know that thou rememberest all things.€€ƒxpIf I have sinned, and thou hast spared me for an hour: why dost thou not suffer me to be clean from my iniquity?€€ƒxsAnd if I be wicked, woe unto me: and if just, I shall not lift up my head, being filled with affliction and misery.€€ƒx\And for pride thou wilt take me as a lioness, and returning, thou tormentest me wonderfully.€€ƒxdThou renewest thy witnesses against me, and multipliest thy wrath upon me, and pains war against me.€€ƒxeWhy didst thou bring me forth out of the womb? O that I had been consumed, that eye might not see me!€€ƒxLI should have been as if I had not been, carried from the womb to the grave.€€ƒxnShall not the fewness of my days be ended shortly? Suffer me, therefore, that I may lament my sorrow a little:€€ƒxZBefore I go and return no more, to a land that is dark and covered with the mist of death:€€ƒxhA land of misery and darkness, where the shadow of death, and no order, but everlasting horror dwelleth.€€ƒ`€€ƒx.Then Sophar the Naamathite answered, and said:€€ƒxUShall not he that speaketh much, hear also? or shall a man full of talk be justified?€€˜ƒxAFor thou hast said: My word is pure, and I am clean in thy sight.€€ƒxKAnd I wish that God would speak with thee, and would open his lips to thee,€€ƒxªThat he might shew thee the secrets of wisdom, and that his law is manifold, and thou mightest understand that he exacteth much less of thee, than thy iniquity deserveth.€€ƒx]Peradventure thou wilt comprehend the steps of God, and wilt find out the Almighty perfectly?€€ƒx`He is higher than heaven, and what wilt thou do? he is deeper than hell, and how wilt thou know?€€ƒxFThe measure of him is longer than the earth, and broader than the sea.€€ƒxXIf he shall overturn all things, or shall press them together, who shall contradict him?€€ƒxVFor he knoweth the vanity of men, and when he seeth iniquity, doth he not consider it?€€ƒxZA vain man is lifted up into pride, and thinketh himself born free like a wild ass's colt.€€ƒxCBut thou hast hardened thy heart, and hast spread thy hands to him.€€ƒxqIf thou wilt put away from thee the iniquity that is in thy hand, and let not injustice remain in thy tabernacle:€€ƒx_Then mayst thou lift up thy face without spot, and thou shalt be steadfast, and shalt not fear.€€ƒxSThou shalt also forget misery, and remember it only as waters that are passed away.€€ƒx•And brightness like that of the noonday, shall arise to thee at evening: and when thou shalt think thyself consumed, thou shalt rise as the day star.€€ƒxeAnd thou shalt have confidence, hope being set before thee, and being buried thou shalt sleep secure.€€ƒx^Thou shalt rest, and there shall be none to make thee afraid: and many shall entreat thy face.€€ƒxzBut the eyes of the wicked shall decay, and the way to escape shall fail them, and their hope the abomination of the soul.€€ƒ`€€ƒxThen Job answered, and said:€€ƒx6Are you then men alone, and shall wisdom die with you?€€ƒxXI also have a heart as well as you: for who is ignorant of these things, which you know?€€ƒxˆHe that is mocked by his friends as I, shall call upon God and he will hear him: for the simplicity of the just man is laughed to scorn.€€ƒxOThe lamp despised in the thoughts of the rich, is ready for the time appointed.€€ƒxvThe tabernacles of robbers abound, and they provoke God boldly; whereas it is he that hath given all into their hands:€€˜"ƒxSSpeak to the earth, and it shall answer thee: and the fishes of the sea shall tell.€€ƒxEWho is ignorant that the hand of the Lord hath made all these things?€€ƒxTIn whose hand is the soul of every living thing, and the spirit of all flesh of man.€€ƒxMDoth not the ear discern words, and the palate of him that eateth, the taste?€€ƒx9In the ancient is wisdom, and in length of days prudence.€€ƒxCWith him is wisdom and strength, he hath counsel and understanding.€€ƒxeIf he pull down, there is no man that can build up: if he shut up a man, there is none that can open.€€ƒxpIf he withhold the waters, all things shall be dried up: and if he send them out, they shall overturn the earth.€€ƒxYWith him is strength and wisdom: he knoweth both the deceivers, and him that is deceived.€€ƒxFHe bringeth counsellors to a foolish end, and judges to insensibility.€€ƒxBHe looseth the belt of kings, and girdeth their loins with a cord.€€ƒx?He leadeth away priests without glory, and overthroweth nobles.€€ƒxVHe changeth the speech of the true speakers, and taketh away the doctrine of the aged.€€ƒxIHe poureth contempt upon princes, and relieveth them that were oppressed.€€ƒxYHe discovereth deep things out of darkness, and bringeth up to light the shadow of death.€€ƒxaHe multiplieth nations, and destroyeth them, and restoreth them again after they were overthrown.€€ƒxHe changeth the heart of the princes of the people of the earth, and deceiveth them that they walk in vain where there is no way.€€ƒxnThey shall grope as in the dark, and not in the light, and he shall make them stagger like men that are drunk.€€ƒ`€€ƒxeBehold my eye hath seen all these things, and my ear hath heard them, and I have understood them all.€€ƒxFAccording to your knowledge I also know: neither am I inferior to you.€€ƒxFBut yet I will speak to the Almighty, and I desire to reason with God.€€ƒxVHaving first shewn that you are forgers of lies, and maintainers of perverse opinions.€€ƒxOAnd I wish you would hold your peace, that you might be thought to be wise men.€€ƒxDHear ye therefore my reproof, and attend to the judgment of my lips.€€ƒxIHath God any need of your lie, that you should speak deceitfully for him?€€ƒxADo you accept this person, and do you endeavour to judge for God?€€ƒx{Or shall it please him, from whom nothing can be concealed? or shall he be deceived as a man, with your deceitful dealings?€€ƒx>He shall reprove you, because in secret you accept his person.€€ƒxZAs soon as he shall move himself, he shall trouble you: and his dread shall fall upon you.€€ƒxUYour remembrance shall be compared to ashes, and your necks shall be brought to clay.€€ƒxXHold your peace a little while, that I may speak whatsoever my mind shall suggest to me.€€ƒxDWhy do I tear my flesh with my teeth, and carry my soul in my hands?€€ƒx]Although he should kill me, I will trust in him: but yet I will reprove my ways in his sight.€€‘ƒx<Hear ye my speech, and receive with your ears hidden truths.€€ƒx8If I shall be judged, I know that I shall be found just.€€ƒxWWho is he that will plead against me? let him come: why am I consumed holding my peace?€€ƒxHTwo things only do not to me, and then from thy face I shall not be hid:€€ƒx@Withdraw thy hand far from me, and let not thy dread terrify me.€€ƒxMCall me, and I will answer thee: or else I will speak, and do thou answer me.€€ƒxIHow many are my iniquities and sins? make me know my crimes and offenses.€€ƒx4Why hidest thou thy face, and thinkest me thy enemy?€€ƒxjAgainst a leaf, that is carried away with the wind, thou shewest thy power, and thou pursuest a dry straw.€€ƒxXFor thou writest bitter things against me, and wilt consume me for the sins of my youth.€€ƒxnThou hast put my feet in the stocks, and hast observed all my paths, and hast considered the steps of my feet:€€ƒxHWho am to be consumed as rottenness, and as a garment that is motheaten.€€ƒ`€€ƒxKMan born of a woman, living for a short time, is filled with many miseries.€€ƒxqWho cometh forth like a flower, and is destroyed, and fleeth as a shadow, and never continueth in the same state.€€ƒxhAnd dost thou think it meet to open thy eyes upon such an one, and to bring him into judgment with thee?€€ƒxVWho can make him clean that is conceived of unclean seed? is it not thou who only art?€€bPs¤dnamefPsalmsiosis_namebPslabbreviationbPshchapters˜–†ƒxUA tree hath hope: if it be cut, it growth green again, and the boughs thereof sprout.€€ƒxDIf its roots be old in the earth, and its stock be dead in the dust:€€ƒx]At the scent of water, it shall spring, and bring forth leaves, as when it was first planted.€€ƒxQBut man when he shall be dead, and stripped and consumed, I pray you where is he?€€ƒxWAs if the waters should depart out of the sea, and an emptied river should be dried up;€€ƒxƒSo man when he is fallen asleep shall not rise again; till the heavens be broken, he shall not awake, nor rise up out of his sleep.€€Œƒx|Shall man that is dead, thinkest thou, live again? all the days in which I am now in warfare, I expect until my change come.€€ƒxiThou shalt call me, and I will answer thee: to the work of thy hands thou shalt reach out thy right hand.€€ƒx6Thou indeed hast numbered my steps, but spare my sins.€€ƒxPThou hast sealed up my offences as it were in a bag, but hast cured my iniquity.€€ƒxLA mountain falling cometh to nought, and a rock is removed out of its place.€€ƒxŠWaters wear away the stones, and with inundation the ground by little and little is washed away: so in like manner thou shalt destroy man.€€ƒxƒThou hast strengthened him for a little while, that he may pass away for ever: thou shalt change his face, and shalt send him away.€€ƒxJWhether his children come to honour or dishonour, he shall not understand.€€ƒx[But yet his flesh, while he shall live, shall have pain, and his soul shall mourn over him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx.And Eliphaz the Themanite, answered, and said:€€ƒxbWill a wise man answer as if he were speaking in the wind, and fill his stomach with burning heat?€€ˆƒx]As much as is in thee, thou hast made void fear, and hast taken away prayers from before God.€€ƒxUFor thy iniquity hath taught thy mouth, and thou imitatest the tongue of blasphemers.€€ƒxPThy own mouth shall condemn thee, and not I: and thy own lips shall answer thee.€€ƒxIArt thou the first man that was born, or wast thou made before the hills?€€ƒxHHast thou heard God's counsel, and shall his wisdom be inferior to thee?€€ƒxVWhat knowest thou that we are ignorant of? what dost thou understand that we know not?€€ƒxIThere are with us also aged and ancient men, much elder than thy fathers.€€ƒxTIs it a great matter that God should comfort thee? but thy wicked words hinder this.€€ˆƒxLWhy doth thy spirit swell against God, to utter such words out of thy mouth?€€ƒxfWhat is man that he should be without spot, and he that is born of a woman that he should appear just?€€ƒxXBehold among his saints none is unchangeable, and the heavens are not pure in his sight.€€ƒxTHow much more is man abominable, and unprofitable, who drinketh iniquity like water?€€ƒxAI will shew thee, hear me: and I will tell thee what I have seen.€€ƒx,Wise men confess and hide not their fathers.€€ƒxJTo whom alone the earth was given, and no stranger hath passed among them.€€ƒx^The wicked man is proud all his days, and the number of the years of his tyranny is uncertain.€€ŒƒxlHe believeth not that he may return from darkness to light, looking round about for the sword on every side.€€ƒx_When he moveth himself to seek bread, he knoweth that the day of darkness is ready at his hand.€€ƒxjTribulation shall terrify him, and distress shall surround him, as a king that is prepared for the battle.€€ƒxcFor he hath stretched out his hand against God, and hath strengthened himself against the Almighty.€€ƒxNHe hath run against him with his neck raised up, and is armed with a fat neck.€€ƒxEFatness hath covered his face, and the fat hangeth down on his sides.€€ƒxSHe hath dwelt in desolate cities, and in desert houses that are reduced into heaps.€€ƒxlHe shall not be enriched, neither shall his substance continue, neither shall he push his root in the earth.€€ƒx„He shall not depart out of darkness: the flame shall dry up his branches, and he shall be taken away by the breath of his own mouth.€€ƒx]He shall not believe, being vainly deceived by error, that he may be redeemed with any price.€€ƒxIBefore his days be full he shall perish: and his hands shall wither away.€€ƒxtHe shall be blasted as a vine when its grapes are in the first flower, and as an olive tree that casteth its flower.€€ŠƒxZHe hath conceived sorrow, and hath brought forth iniquity, and his womb prepareth deceits.€€ƒ`€€ƒxThen Job answered, and said:€€ƒxLI have often heard such things as these: you are all troublesome comforters.€€ƒxEShall windy words have no end? or is it any trouble to thee to speak?€€ƒxFI also could speak like you: and would God your soul were for my soul.€€ƒxDI would comfort you also with words, and would wag my head over you.€€ƒxMI would strengthen you with my mouth, and would move my lips, as sparing you.€€ƒxkBut what shall I do? If I speak, my pain will not rest: and if I hold my peace, it will not depart from me.€€ƒxMBut now my sorrow hath oppressed me, and all my limbs are brought to nothing.€€‘ƒx•He hath gathered together his fury against me, and threatening me he hath gnashed with his teeth upon me: my enemy hath beheld me with terrible eyes.€€ƒxzThey have opened their mouths upon me, and reproaching me they have struck me on the cheek, they are filled with my pains.€€ƒx\God hath shut me up with the unjust man, and hath delivered me into the hands of the wicked.€€ƒx—I that was formerly so wealthy, am all on a sudden broken to pieces: he hath taken me by my neck, he hath broken me, and hath set me up to be his mark.€€ƒx‹He hath compassed me round about with his lances, he hath wounded my loins, he hath not spared, and hath poured out my bowels on the earth,€€ƒxNHe hath torn me with wound upon wound, he hath rushed in upon me like a giant.€€ƒxJI have sowed sackcloth upon my skin, and have covered my flesh with ashes.€€ƒx8My face is swollen with weeping, and my eyelids are dim.€€ƒxaThese things have I suffered without the iniquity of my hand, when I offered pure prayers to God.€€ƒxQO earth, cover not thou my blood, neither let my cry find a hiding place in thee.€€ƒxQFor behold my witness is in heaven, and he that knoweth my conscience is on high.€€ƒx>My friends are full of words: my eye poureth out tears to God.€€ƒx]And O that a man might so be judged with God, as the son of man is judged with his companion!€€ƒxYFor behold short years pass away, and I am walking in a path by which I shall not return.€€ƒ`€€ƒxZMy spirit shall be wasted, my days shall be shortened and only the grave remaineth for me.€€ƒx4I have not sinned, and my eye abideth in bitterness.€€‰ƒxVThou hast set their heart far from understanding, therefore they shall not be exalted.€€ƒxOHe promiseth a prey to his companions, and the eyes of his children shall fail.€€ƒxSHe hath made me as it were a byword of the people, and I am an example before them.€€ƒxRMy eye is dim through indignation, and my limbs are brought as it were to nothing.€€ƒx`The just shall be astonished at this, and the innocent shall be raised up against the hypocrite.€€ƒxdAnd the just man shall hold on his way, and he that hath clean hands shall be stronger and stronger.€€ƒxVWherefore be you all converted, and come, and I shall not find among you any wise man.€€ƒxJMy days have passed away, my thoughts are dissipated, tormenting my heart.€€ƒxKThey have turned night into day, and after darkness I hope for light again.€€”ƒxQI have said to rottenness: Thou art my father; to worms, my mother and my sister.€€ƒxBWhere is now then my expectation, and who considereth my patience?€€ƒxhAll that I have shall go down into the deepest pit: thinkest thou that there at least I shall have rest?€€ƒ`€€ƒx*Then Baldad the Suhite answered, and said:€€ƒxIHow long will you throw out words? understand first, and so let us speak.€€ƒx:Why are we reputed as beasts, and counted vile before you?€€ƒxThou that destroyest thy soul in thy fury, shall the earth be forsaken for thee, and shall rocks be removed out of their place?€€ƒxWShall not the light of the wicked be extinguished, and the flame of his fire not shine?€€ƒx[The light shall be dark in his tabernacle, and the lamp that is over him, shall be put out.€€ƒx_The step of his strength shall be straitened, and his own counsel shall cast him down headlong.€€ƒxBFor he hath thrust his feet into a net, and walketh in its meshes.€€ƒxQThe sole of his foot shall be held in a snare, and thirst shall burn against him.€€ƒxAA gin is hidden for him in the earth, and his trap upon the path.€€ƒxCFears shall terrify him on every side, and shall entangle his feet.€€ƒxGLet his strength be wasted with famine, and let hunger invade his ribs.€€ƒxOLet it devour the beauty of his skin, let the firstborn death consume his arms.€€ƒxcLet his confidence be rooted out of his tabernacle, and let destruction tread upon him like a king.€€ƒxgLet the companions of him that is not, dwell in his tabernacle, let brimstone be sprinkled in his tent.€€ƒxCLet his roots be dried up beneath, and his harvest destroyed above.€€’ƒxUHe shall drive him out of light into darkness, and shall remove him out of the world.€€ƒx`His seed shall not subsist, nor his offspring among his people, nor any remnants in his country.€€ƒxjThey that come after him shall be astonished at his day, and horror shall fall upon them that went before.€€ƒx]These then are the tabernacles of the wicked, and this the place of him that knoweth not God.€€ƒ`€€ƒxThen Job answered, and said:€€ƒxCHow long do you afflict my soul, and break me in pieces with words?€€ƒxKBehold, these ten times you confound me, and are not ashamed to oppress me.€€ƒx;For if I have been ignorant, my ignorance shall be with me.€€ƒxHBut you set yourselves up against me, and reprove me with my reproaches.€€ƒxsAt least now understand, that God hath not afflicted me with an equal judgment, and compassed me with his scourges.€€ƒxkBehold I shall cry suffering violence, and no one will hear: I shall cry aloud, and there is none to judge.€€ƒx]He hath hedged in my path round about, and I cannot pass, and in my way he hath set darkness.€€ƒxGHe hath stripped me of my glory, and hath taken the crown from my head.€€ƒxuHe hath destroyed me on every side, and I am lost, and he hath taken away my hope, as from a tree that is plucked up.€€ƒxEHis wrath is kindled against me, and he hath counted me as his enemy.€€ƒxqHis troops have come together, and have made themselves a way by me, and have besieged my tabernacle round about.€€ƒx^He hath put my brethren far from me, and my acquaintance like strangers have departed from me.€€‡ƒx|They that dwell in my house, and my maidservants have counted me as a stranger, and I have been like an alien in their eyes.€€ƒxQI called my servant, and he gave me no answer, I entreated him with my own mouth.€€ƒxIMy wife hath abhorred my breath, and I entreated the children of my womb.€€ƒxMEven fools despised me, and when I was gone from them, they spoke against me.€€ƒxhThey that were sometime my counsellors, have abhorred me: and he whom I loved most is turned against me.€€ƒxhThe flesh being consumed, my bone hath cleaved to my skin, and nothing but lips are left about my teeth.€€ƒxhHave pity on me, have pity on me, at least you my friends, because the hand of the Lord hath touched me.€€ˆƒxiWho will grant me that my words may be written? who will grant me that they may be marked down in a book?€€ƒx]With an iron pen and in a plate of lead, or else be graven with an instrument in flint stone?€€ƒxVFor I know that my Redeemer liveth, and in the last day I shall rise out of the earth.€€ƒxNAnd I shall be clothed again with my skin, and in my flesh I shall see my God.€€ƒxhWhom I myself shall see, and my eyes shall behold, and not another: this my hope is laid up in my bosom.€€ƒx\Why then do you say now: Let us persecute him, and let us find occasion of word against him?€€ƒxxFlee then from the face of the sword, for the sword is the revenger of iniquities: and know ye that there is a judgment.€€ƒ`€€†ƒxfTherefore various thoughts succeed one another in me, and my mind is hurried away to different things.€€ƒxoThe doctrine with which thou reprovest me, I will hear, and the spirit of my understanding shall answer for me.€€ƒxDThis I know from the beginning, since man was placed upon the earth,€€ƒxVThat the praise of the wicked is short, and the joy of the hypocrite but for a moment.€€ƒxDIf his pride mount up even to heaven, and his head touch the clouds:€€ƒxeIn the end he shall be destroyed like a dunghill, and they that had seen him, shall say: Where is he?€€‡ƒx_The eyes that had seen him, shall see him no more, neither shall his place any more behold him.€€ƒxXHis children shall be oppressed with want, and his hands shall render to him his sorrow.€€ƒxaHis bones shall be filled with the vices of his youth, and they shall sleep with him in the dust.€€ƒxLFor when evil shall be sweet in his mouth, he will hide it under his tongue.€€ƒxCHe will spare it, and not leave it, and will hide it in his throat.€€ƒxHHis bread in his belly shall be turned into the gall of asps within him,€€ƒx`The riches which he hath swallowed, he shall vomit up, and God shall draw them out of his belly.€€…ƒxLLet him not see the streams of the river, the brooks of honey and of butter.€€ƒx‹He shall be punished for all that he did, and yet shall not be consumed: according to the multitude of his devices so also shall he suffer.€€ƒxgBecause he broke in and stripped the poor: he hath violently taken away a house which he did not build.€€ƒxoAnd yet his belly was not filled: and when he hath the things he coveted, he shall not be able to possess them.€€ƒxVThere was nothing left of his meat, and therefore nothing shall continue of his goods:€€‹ƒxwMay his belly be filled, that God may send forth the wrath of his indignation upon him, and rain down his war upon him.€€ƒxGHe shall flee from weapons of iron, and shall fall upon a bow of brass.€€ƒx‹The sword is drawn out, and cometh forth from its scabbard, and glittereth in his bitterness: the terrible ones shall go and come upon him.€€ƒx‰All darkness is hid in his secret places: a fire that is not kindled shall devour him, he shall be afflicted when left in his tabernacle.€€ƒxOThe heavens shall reveal his iniquity, and the earth shall rise up against him.€€ƒx_The offspring of his house shall be exposed, he shall be pulled down in the day of God's wrath.€€ƒx^This is the portion of a wicked man from God, and the inheritance of his doings from the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxThen Job answered, and said:€€ƒx.Hear, I beseech you, my words, and do penance.€€ƒxISuffer me, and I will speak, and after, if you please, laugh at my words.€€ƒxCHearken to me and be astonished, and lay your finger on your mouth.€€ƒxOAs for me, when I remember, I am afraid, and trembling taketh hold on my flesh.€€ƒxMWhy then do the wicked live, are they advanced, and strengthened with riches?€€ƒxeTheir seed continueth before them, a multitude of kinsmen, and of children's children in their sight.€€ƒxKTheir houses are secure and peaceable, and the rod of God is not upon them.€€ƒxdTheir cattle have conceived, and failed not: their cow has calved, and is not deprived of her fruit.€€ƒxITheir little ones go out like a flock, and their children dance and play.€€ƒxKThey take the timbrel, and the harp, and rejoice at the sound of the organ.€€ƒxFThey spend their days in wealth, and in a moment they go down to hell.€€ƒxNWho have said to God: Depart from us, we desire not the knowledge of thy ways.€€ƒx\Who is the Almighty, that we should serve him? and what doth it profit us if we pray to him?€€ƒxbYet because their good things are not in their hand, may the counsel of the wicked be far from me.€€ƒxHow often shall the lamp of the wicked be put out, and a deluge come upon them, and he shall distribute the sorrows of his wrath?€€ƒx`They shall be as chaff before the face of the wind, and as ashes which the whirlwind scattereth.€€ƒxhGod shall lay up the sorrow of the father for his children: and when he shall repay, then shall he know.€€˜2ƒxtFor what is it to him what befalleth his house after him: and if the number of his months be diminished by one half?€€ƒxCShall any one teach God knowledge, who judgeth those that are high?€€ƒx/One man dieth strong, and hale, rich and happy.€€ƒxDHis bowels are full of fat, and his bones are moistened with marrow.€€ƒx;But another dieth in bitterness of soul without any riches:€€ƒxJAnd yet they shall sleep together in the dust, and worms shall cover them.€€ƒxBSurely I know your thoughts, and your unjust judgments against me.€€ƒx_For you say: Where is the house of the prince? and where are the dwelling places of the wicked?€€ƒxaAsk any one of them that go by the way, and you shall perceive that he knoweth these same things.€€ƒxjBecause the wicked man is reserved to the day of destruction, and he shall be brought to the day of wrath.€€ƒxQWho shall reprove his way to his face? and who shall repay him what he hath done?€€ƒxKHe shall be brought to the graves, and shall watch in the heap of the dead.€€ƒx~He hath been acceptable to the gravel of Cocytus, and he shall draw every man after him, and there are innumerable before him.€€ƒxYHow then do ye comfort me in vain, whereas your answer is shewn to be repugnant to truth?€€ƒ`€€ƒx.Then Eliphaz the Themanite answered, and said:€€ƒxGCan man be compared with God, even though he were of perfect knowledge?€€ƒx\What doth it profit God if thou be just? or what dost thou give him if thy way be unspotted?€€ƒxAShall he reprove thee for fear, and come with thee into judgment:€€ƒx@And not for thy manifold wickedness and thy infinite iniquities?€€ƒxmFor thou hast taken away the pledge of thy brethren without cause, and stripped them naked of their clothing.€€ƒxRThou hast not given water to the weary, thou hast withdrawn bread from the hungry.€€ƒxbIn the strength of thy arm thou didst possess the land, and being the most mighty thou holdest it.€€ƒx\Thou hast sent widows away empty, and the arms of the fatherless thou hast broken in pieces.€€ƒxJTherefore art thou surrounded with snares, and sudden fear troubleth thee.€€ƒxˆAnd didst thou think that thou shouldst not see darkness, and that thou shouldst not be covered with the violence of overflowing waters?€€ƒxbDost not thou think that God is higher than heaven, and is elevated above the height of the stars?€€ƒxMAnd thou sayst: What doth God know? and he judgeth as it were through a mist.€€ƒxiThe clouds are his covert, and he doth not consider our things, and he walketh about the poles of heaven.€€ƒxIDost thou desire to keep the path of ages, which wicked men have spurned?€€ƒxTWho were taken away before their time, and a flood hath overthrown their foundation.€€ƒxXWho said to God: Depart from us: and looked upon the Almighty as if he could do nothing:€€ƒxZWhereas he had filled their houses with good things: whose way of thinking be far from me.€€ƒxRThe just shall see, and shall rejoice, and the innocent shall laugh them to scorn.€€ƒxRIs not their exaltation cut down, and hath not fire devoured the remnants of them?€€ƒxYSubmit thyself then to him, and be at peace: and thereby thou shalt have the best fruits.€€ƒx@Receive the law of his mouth, and lay up his words in thy heart.€€ƒxqIf thou wilt return to the Almighty, thou shalt be built up, and shalt put away iniquity far from thy tabernacle.€€ƒx>He shall give for earth flint, and for flint torrents of gold.€€ƒx\And the Almighty shall be against thy enemies, and silver shall be heaped together for thee.€€ƒxVThen shalt thou abound in delights in the Almighty, and shalt lift up thy face to God.€€ƒxGThou shalt pray to him, and he will hear thee, and thou shalt pay vows.€€ƒxXThou shalt decree a thing, and it shall come to thee, and light shall shine in thy ways.€€ƒxiFor he that hath been humbled, shall be in glory: and he that shall bow down his eyes, he shall be saved.€€ƒxQThe innocent shall be saved, and he shall be saved by the cleanness of his hands.€€ƒ`€€ƒxThen Job answered, and said:€€ƒxbNow also my words are in bitterness, and the hand of my scourge is more grievous than my mourning.€€ƒxNWho will grant me that I might know and find him, and come even to his throne?€€ƒxII would set judgment before him, and would fill my mouth with complaints.€€ƒxqI would not that he should contend with me with much strength, nor overwhelm me with the weight of his greatness.€€ƒxGLet him propose equity against me, and let my judgment come to victory.€€ƒxVBut if I go to the east, he appeareth not; if to the west, I shall not understand him.€€ƒx|If to the left hand, what shall I do? I shall not take hold on him: if I turn myself to the right hand, I shall not see him.€€ƒxNBut he knoweth my way, and has tried me as gold that passeth through the fire:€€ƒxTMy foot hath followed his steps, I have kept his way, and have not declined from it.€€ƒxiI have not departed from the commandments of his lips, and the words of his mouth I have hid in my bosom.€€ƒxoFor he is alone, and no man can turn away his thought: and whatsoever his soul hath desired, that hath he done.€€ƒxbAnd when he shall have fulfilled his will in me, many other like things are also at hand with him.€€ƒxbAnd therefore I am troubled at his presence, and when I consider him, I am made pensive with fear.€€ƒx>God hath softened my heart, and the Almighty hath troubled me.€€ƒxjFor I have not perished because of the darkness that hangs over me, neither hath the mist covered my face.€€ƒ`€€ƒxOTimes are not hid from the Almighty: but they that know him, know not his days.€€‰ƒxaThey have driven away the ass of the fatherless, and have taken away the widow's ox for a pledge.€€ƒx\They have overturned the way of the poor, and have oppressed together the meek of the earth.€€ƒxvOthers like wild asses in the desert go forth to their work: by watching for a prey they get bread for their children.€€ƒxwThey reap the field that is not their own, and gather the vintage of his vineyard whom by violence they have oppressed.€€ƒxUThey send men away naked, taking away their clothes who have no covering in the cold:€€ƒxZWho are wet, with the showers of the mountains, and having no covering embrace the stones.€€ƒxOThey have violently robbed the fatherless, and stripped the poor common people.€€ƒxlFrom the naked and them that go without clothing, and from the hungry they have taken away the ears of corn.€€ƒxtThey have taken their rest at noon among the stores of them, who after having trodden the winepresses suffer thirst.€€ƒxnThey have been rebellious to the light, they have not known his ways, neither have they returned by his paths.€€ƒx}The murderer riseth at the very break of day, he killeth the needy, and the poor man: but in the night he will be as a thief.€€ƒxeThe eye of the adulterer observeth darkness, saying: No eye shall see me: and he will cover his face.€€ƒxzHe diggeth through houses in the dark, as in the day they had appointed for themselves, and they have not known the light.€€ƒxtIf the morning suddenly appear, it is to them the shadow of death: and they walk in darkness as if it were in light.€€ƒxyHe is light upon the face of the water: cursed be his portion on the earth, let him not walk by the way of the vineyards.€€ƒxNLet him pass from the snow waters to excessive heat, and his sin even to hell.€€ƒxLet mercy forget him: may worms be his sweetness: let him be remembered no more, but be broken in pieces as an unfruitful tree.€€ƒxSFor he hath fed the barren that beareth not, and to the widow he hath done no good.€€ƒxeHe hath pulled down the strong by his might: and when he standeth up, he shall not trust to his life.€€ƒxcGod hath given him place for penance, and he abuseth it unto pride: but his eyes are upon his ways.€€ƒx¶They are lifted up for a little while and shall not stand, and shall be brought down as all things, and shall be taken away, and as the tops of the ears of corn they shall be broken.€€ƒxWAnd if it be not so, who can convince me that I have lied, and set my words before God?€€˜ƒx*Then Baldad the Suhite answered, and said:€€ƒxCPower and terror are with him, who maketh peace in his high places.€€ƒxPIs there any numbering of his soldiers? and upon whom shall not his light arise?€€ƒxSCan man be justified compared with God, or he that is born of a woman appear clean?€€ƒxMBehold even the moon doth not shine, and the stars are not pure in his sight.€€ƒxDHow much more is man than rottenness and the son of man than a worm?€€ƒ`€€ƒxThen Job answered, and said:€€ƒxlWhose helper art thou? is it of him that is weak? and dost thou hold up the arm of him that has no strength?€€ƒxqTo whom hast thou given counsel? perhaps to him that hath no wisdom, and thou hast shewn thy very great prudence.€€ƒx?Whom hast thou desired to teach? was it not him that made life?€€ƒxHBehold the giants groan under the waters, and they that dwell with them.€€ƒxCHell is naked before him, and there is no covering for destruction.€€ƒxTHe stretched out the north over the empty space, and hangeth the earth upon nothing.€€ƒxZHe bindeth up the waters in his clouds, so that they break not out and fall down together.€€ƒxGHe withholdeth the face of his throne, and spreadeth his cloud over it.€€ƒxLHe hath set bounds about the waters, till light and darkness come to an end.€€ƒx5The pillars of heaven tremble, and dread at his beck.€€ƒx^By his power the seas are suddenly gathered together, and his wisdom has struck the proud one.€€ƒx^His spirit hath adorned the heavens, and his obstetric hand brought forth the winding serpent.€€ƒx£Lo, these things are said in part of his ways: and seeing we have heard scarce a little drop of his word, who shall be able to behold the thunder of his greatness?€€ƒ`€€ƒx0Job also added, taking up his parable, and said:€€ƒxiAs God liveth, who hath taken away my judgment, and the Almighty, who hath brought my soul to bitterness,€€ƒxHAs long as breath remaineth in me, and the spirit of God in my nostrils,€€ƒxIMy lips shall not speak iniquity, neither shall my tongue contrive lying.€€ƒx^God forbid that I should judge you to be just: till I die I will not depart from my innocence.€€ƒxtMy justification, which I have begun to hold, I will not forsake: for my heart doth not reprehend me in all my life.€€ƒxCLet my enemy be as the ungodly, and my adversary as the wicked one.€€ƒxpFor what is the hope of the hypocrite if through covetousness he take by violence, and God deliver not his soul?€€ƒx9Will God hear his cry, when distress shall come upon him?€€†ƒxWI will teach you by the hand of God, what the Almighty hath, and I will not conceal it.€€ƒxGBehold you all know it, and why do you speak vain things without cause?€€ƒx{This is the portion of a wicked man with God, and the inheritance of the violent, which they shall receive of the Almighty.€€ƒxiIf his sons be multiplied, they shall be for the sword, and his grandsons shall not be filled with bread.€€ƒxWThey that shall remain of him, shall be buried in death, and his widows shall not weep.€€ƒxGIf he shall heap together silver as earth, and prepare raiment as clay,€€ŠƒxHHe hath built his house as a moth, and as a keeper he hath made a booth.€€ƒxkThe rich man when he shall sleep shall take away nothing with him: he shall open his eyes and find nothing.€€ƒxTPoverty like water shall take hold on him, a tempest shall oppress him in the night:€€ƒxiA burning wind shall take him up, and carry him away, and as a whirlwind shall snatch him from his place.€€ƒxYAnd he shall cast upon him, and shall not spare: out of his hand he would willingly flee.€€ƒxNHe shall clasp his hands upon him, and shall hiss at him, beholding his place.€€ƒ`€€ƒxPSilver hath beginnings of its veins, and gold hath a place wherein it is melted.€€ƒxPIron is taken out of the earth, and stone melted with heat is turned into brass.€€ƒx†He hath set a time for darkness, and the end of all things he considereth, the stone also that is in the dark and the shadow of death.€€–ƒxOThe land, out of which bread grew in its place, hath been overturned with fire.€€ƒxJThe stones of it are the place of sapphires, and the clods of it are gold.€€ƒxPThe bird hath not known the path, neither hath the eye of the vulture beheld it.€€ƒxYThe children of the merchants have not trodden it, neither hath the lioness passed by it.€€ƒx[He hath stretched forth his hand to the flint, he hath overturned mountains from the roots.€€ƒxPIn the rocks he hath cut out rivers, and his eye hath seen every precious thing.€€ƒx]The depths also of rivers he hath searched, and hidden things he hath brought forth to light.€€ƒxIBut where is wisdom to be found, and where is the place of understanding?€€ƒxaMan knoweth not the price thereof, neither is it found in the land of them that live in delights.€€ƒxGThe depth saith: It is not in me: and the sea saith: It is not with me.€€ƒxZThe finest gold shall not purchase it, neither shall silver be weighed in exchange for it.€€ƒxsIt shall not be compared with the dyed colours of India, or with the most precious stone sardonyx, or the sapphire.€€ƒxUGold or crystal cannot equal it, neither shall any vessels of gold be changed for it.€€ƒxmHigh and eminent things shall not be mentioned in comparison of it: but wisdom is drawn out of secret places.€€ƒxdThe topaz of Ethiopia shall not be equal to it, neither shall it be compared to the cleanest dyeing.€€ƒxCWhence then cometh wisdom? and where is the place of understanding?€€ƒxLIt is hid from the eyes of all living, and the fowls of the air know it not.€€ƒxNDestruction and death have said: With our ears we have heard the fame thereof.€€ƒxBGod understandeth the way of it, and he knoweth the place thereof.€€ƒxXFor he beholdeth the ends of the world: and looketh on all things that are under heaven.€€ƒxCWho made a weight for the winds, and weighed the waters by measure.€€ƒxCWhen he gave a law for the rain, and a way for the sounding storms.€€ŒƒxkAnd he said to man: Behold the fear of the Lord, that is wisdom: and to depart from evil, is understanding.€€ƒ`€€ƒx0Job also added, taking up his parable, and said:€€ƒxlWho will grant me, that I might be according to the months past, according to the days in which God kept me?€€ƒxIWhen his lamp shined over my head, and I walked by his light in darkness?€€ƒxIAs I was in the days of my youth, when God was secretly in my tabernacle?€€ƒx>When the Almighty was with me: and my servants round about me?€€ƒxLWhen I washed my feet with butter, and the rock poured me out rivers of oil?€€ƒxTWhen I went out to the gate of the city, and in the street they prepared me a chair?€€ƒxLThe young men saw me, and hid themselves: and the old men rose up and stood.€€ƒx@The princes ceased to speak, and laid the finger on their mouth.€€ƒxFThe rulers held their peace, and their tongue cleaved to their throat.€€ƒx\Because I had delivered the poor man that cried out; and the fatherless, that had no helper.€€ƒxbThe blessing of him that was ready to perish came upon me, and I comforted the heart of the widow.€€ƒx\I was clad with justice: and I clothed myself with my judgment, as with a robe and a diadem.€€ƒx2I was an eye to the blind, and a foot to the lame.€€ƒx]I was the father of the poor: and the cause which I knew not, I searched out most diligently.€€ƒxNI broke the jaws of the wicked man, and out of his teeth I took away the prey.€€ƒxNAnd I said: I shall die in my nest, and as a palm tree shall multiply my days.€€ƒxJMy root is opened beside the waters, and dew shall continue in my harvest.€€ƒxJMy glory shall always be renewed, and my bow in my hand shall be repaired.€€ƒx_They that heard me, waited for my sentence, and being attentive held their peace at my counsel.€€ƒxDTo my words they durst add nothing, and my speech dropped upon them.€€ƒxSThey waited for me as for rain, and they opened their mouth as for a latter shower.€€ƒxgIf at any time I laughed on them, they believed not, and the light of my countenance fell not on earth.€€ƒx“If I had a mind to go to them, I sat first, and when I sat as a king, with his army standing about him, yet I was a comforter of them that mourned.€€‰ƒxcBut now the younger in time scorn me, whose fathers I would not have set with the dogs of my flock:€€ƒx`The strength of whose hands was to me as nothing, and they were thought unworthy of life itself.€€ƒx_Barren with want and hunger, who gnawed in the wilderness, disfigured with calamity and misery.€€ƒxPAnd they ate grass, and barks of trees, and the root of junipers was their food.€€ƒxrWho snatched up these things out of the valleys, and when they had found any of them, they ran to them with a cry.€€ƒxWThey dwelt in the desert places of torrents, and in caves of earth, or upon the gravel.€€ƒxeThey pleased themselves among these kind of things, and counted it delightful to be under the briers.€€ƒxNThe children of foolish and base men, and not appearing at all upon the earth.€€ƒx<Now I am turned into their song, and am become their byword.€€‹ƒxZFor he hath opened his quiver, and hath afflicted me, and hath put a bridle into my mouth.€€ƒx”At the right hand of my rising, my calamities forthwith arose: they have overthrown my feet, and have overwhelmed me with their paths as with waves.€€ƒxtThey have destroyed my ways, they have lain in wait against me, and they have prevailed, and there was none to help.€€ƒxyThey have rushed in upon me, as when a wall is broken, and a gate opened, and have rolled themselves down to my miseries.€€ƒxsI am brought to nothing: as a wind thou hast taken away my desire: and my prosperity hath passed away like a cloud.€€ƒxLAnd now my soul fadeth within myself, and the days of affliction possess me.€€ƒxWIn the night my bone is pierced with sorrows: and they that feed upon me, do not sleep.€€ƒxpWith the multitude of them my garment is consumed, and they have girded me about, as with the collar of my coat.€€ƒx:I am compared to dirt, and am likened to embers and ashes.€€ƒxPI cry to thee, and thou hearest me not: I stand up, and thou dost not regard me.€€ƒx\Thou art changed to be cruel toward me, and in the hardness of thy hand thou art against me.€€Œƒx`I know that thou wilt deliver me to death, where a house is appointed for every one that liveth.€€ƒxlBut yet thou stretchest not forth thy hand to their consumption: and if they shall fall down thou wilt save.€€ƒxUI wept heretofore for him that was afflicted, and my soul had compassion on the poor.€€ƒx_I expected good things, and evils are come upon me: I waited for light, and darkness broke out.€€ƒxVMy inner parts have boiled without any rest, the days of affliction have prevented me.€€ƒxGI went mourning without indignation; I rose up, and cried in the crowd.€€ƒx9I was the brother of dragons, and companion of ostriches.€€ƒxEMy skin is become black upon me, and my bones are dried up with heat.€€ƒxNMy harp is turned to mourning, and my organ into the voice of those that weep.€€ƒ`€€ƒxPI made a covenant with my eyes, that I would not so much as think upon a virgin.€€ƒx_For what part should God from above have in me, and what inheritance the Almighty from on high?€€˜ƒx6Doth not he consider my ways, and number all my steps?€€ƒxBIf I have walked in vanity, and my foot hath made haste to deceit:€€ƒxCLet him weigh me in a just balance, and let God know my simplicity.€€ƒxuIf my step hath turned out of the way, and if my heart hath followed my eyes, and if a spot hath cleaved to my hands:€€ƒxIThen let me sow and let another reap: and let my offspring be rooted out.€€ƒxYIf my heart hath been deceived upon a woman, and if I have laid wait at my friend's door:€€ƒxELet my wife be the harlot of another, and let other men lie with her.€€ƒx:For this is a heinous crime, and a most grievous iniquity.€€ƒxWIt is a fire that devoureth even to destruction, and rooteth up all things that spring.€€ƒxuIf I have despised to abide judgment with my manservant, or my maidservant, when they had any controversy against me:€€ƒxeFor what shall I do when God shall rise to judge? and when he shall examine, what shall I answer him?€€ƒxdDid not he that made me in the womb make him also: and did not one and the same form me in the womb?€€ƒxYIf I have denied to the poor what they desired, and have made the eyes of the widow wait:€€ƒxKIf I have eaten my morsel alone, and the fatherless hath not eaten thereof:€€ƒx[(For from my infancy mercy grew up with me: and it came out with me from my mother's womb:)€€ƒxfIf I have despised him that was perishing for want of clothing, and the poor man that had no covering:€€ƒxXIf his sides have not blessed me, and if he were not warmed with the fleece of my sheep:€€ƒx`If I have lifted up my hand against the fatherless, even when I saw myself superior in the gate:€€ƒxMLet my shoulder fall from its joint, and let my arm with its bones be broken.€€ƒx\For I have always feared God as waves swelling over me, and his weight I was unable to bear.€€ƒxNIf I have thought gold my strength, and have said to fine gold: My confidence:€€ƒxMIf I have rejoiced over my great riches, and because my hand had gotten much.€€ƒxDIf I beheld the sun when it shined and the moon going in brightness:€€ƒxNAnd my heart in secret hath rejoiced, and I have kissed my hand with my mouth:€€‹ƒxdIf I have been glad at the downfall of him that hated me, and have rejoiced that evil had found him.€€ƒxJFor I have not been given my mouth to sin, by wishing a curse to his soul.€€ƒx_If the men of my tabernacle have not said: Who will give us of his flesh that we may be filled?€€ƒxEThe stranger did not stay without, my door was open to the traveller.€€ƒxJIf as a man I have hid my sin, and have concealed my iniquity in my bosom.€€ƒx If I have been afraid at a very great multitude, and the contempt of kinsmen hath terrified me: and have not rather held my peace, and not gone out of the door.€€ƒxxWho would grant me a hearing, that the Almighty may hear my desire: and that he himself that judgeth would write a book,€€ƒxCThat I may carry it on my shoulder, and put it about me as a crown?€€ƒxHAt every step of mine I would pronounce it, and offer it as to a prince.€€ƒxAIf my land cry against me, and with it the furrows thereof mourn:€€ƒxdIf I have eaten the fruits thereof without money, and have afflicted the son of the tillers thereof:€€–ƒ`€€ƒxKSo these three men ceased to answer Job, because he seemed just to himself.€€ƒx¬And Eliu the son of Barachel the Buzite of the kindred of Ram, was angry and was moved to indignation: now he was angry against Job, because he said he was just before God.€€ƒxnAnd he was angry with his friends, because they had not found a reasonable answer, but only had condemned Job.€€ƒxVSo Eliu waited while Job was speaking because they were his elders that were speaking.€€ƒxQBut when he saw that the three were not able to answer, he was exceedingly angry.€€ƒx±Then Eliu the son of Barachel the Buzite answered, and said: I am younger in days, and you are more ancient, therefore hanging down my head, I was afraid to shew you my opinion.€€ƒx[For I hoped that greater age would speak, and that a multitude of years would teach wisdom.€€ƒxbBut, as I see, there is a spirit in men, and the inspiration of the Almighty giveth understanding.€€ƒxUThey that are aged are not the wise men, neither do the ancients understand judgment.€€ƒxFTherefore I will speak: Hearken to me, I also will shew you my wisdom.€€ƒxjFor I have waited for your words, I have given ear to your wisdom, as long as you were disputing in words.€€ƒxŒAnd as long as I thought you said some thing, I considered: but, as I see, there is none of you that can convince Job, and answer his words.€€ƒxKLest you should say: We have found wisdom, God hath cast him down, not man.€€ƒxPHe hath spoken nothing to me, and I will not answer him according to your words.€€ƒxCThey were afraid, and answered no more, and they left off speaking.€€ƒx\Therefore because I have waited, and they have not spoken: they stood, and answered no more:€€ƒx7I also will answer my part, and will shew my knowledge.€€ƒxPFor I am full of matter to speak of, and the spirit of my bowels straiteneth me.€€ƒxSBehold, my belly is as new wine which wanteth vent, which bursteth the new vessels.€€ƒxLI will speak and take breath a little: I will open my lips, and will answer.€€ƒxGI will not accept the person of man, and I will not level God with man.€€–ƒ`€€ƒx@Hear therefore, O Job, my speeches, and hearken to all my words.€€ƒxFBehold now I have opened my mouth, let my tongue speak within my jaws.€€ƒxLMy words are from my upright heart, and my lips shall speak a pure sentence.€€ƒxGThe spirit of God made me, and the breath of the Almighty gave me life.€€ƒx7If thou canst, answer me, and stand up against my face.€€ƒxPBehold God hath made me as well as thee, and of the same clay I also was formed.€€ƒxWBut yet let not my wonder terrify thee, and let not my eloquence be burdensome to thee.€€ƒxJNow thou hast said in my hearing, and I have heard the voice of thy words:€€ƒxLI am clean, and without sin: I am unspotted, and there is no iniquity in me.€€ƒxXBecause he hath found complaints against me, therefore he hath counted me for his enemy.€€ƒxAHe hath put my feet in the stocks, he hath observed all my paths.€€ƒxhNow this is the thing in which thou art not justified: I will answer thee, that God is greater than man.€€ƒxMDost thou strive against him, because he hath not answered thee to all words?€€ƒxHGod speaketh once, and repeateth not the selfsame thing the second time.€€ƒxgBy a dream in a vision by night, when deep sleep falleth upon men, and they are sleeping in their beds:€€ƒxYThen he openeth the ears of men, and teaching instructeth them in what they are to learn.€€ƒxWThat he may withdraw a man from the things he is doing, and may deliver him from pride.€€ƒxJRescuing his soul from corruption: and his life from passing to the sword.€€ƒxMHe rebuketh also by sorrow in the bed, and he maketh all his bones to wither.€€ƒx_Bread becometh abominable to him in his life, and to his soul the meat which before he desired.€€ƒxUHis flesh shall be consumed away, and his bones that were covered shall be made bare.€€˜ƒx_If there shall be an angel speaking for him, one among thousands, to declare man's uprightness,€€ƒxHe shall have mercy on him, and shall say: Deliver him, that he may not go down to corruption: I have found wherein I may be merciful to him.€€ƒxPHis flesh is consumed with punishments, let him return to the days of his youth.€€ƒx€He shall pray to God, and he will be gracious to him: and he shall see his face with joy, and he will render to man his justice.€€ƒxHe shall look upon men, and shall say: I have sinned, and indeed I have offended, and I have not received what I have deserved.€€ƒx[He hath delivered his soul from going into destruction, that it may live and see the light.€€ƒxBBehold, all these things God worketh three times within every one.€€ƒxbThat he may withdraw their souls from corruption, and enlighten them with the light of the living.€€ƒxCAttend, Job, and hearken to me, and hold thy peace, whilst I speak.€€ƒxZBut if thou hast any thing to say, answer me, speak: for I would have thee to appear just.€€ƒxLAnd if thou have not, hear me: hold thy peace, and I will teach thee wisdom.€€ƒ`€€ƒx+And Eliu continued his discourse, and said:€€ƒx;Hear ye, wise men, my words, and ye learned, hearken to me:€€ƒxFFor the ear trieth words, and the mouth discerneth meats by the taste.€€ƒxNLet us choose to us judgment, and let us see among ourselves what is the best.€€ƒxBFor Job hath said: I am just, and God hath overthrown my judgment.€€ƒxFFor in judging me there is a lie: my arrow is violent without any sin.€€ƒx@What man is there like Job, who drinketh up scorning like water?€€ƒxOWho goeth in company with them that work iniquity, and walketh with wicked men?€€ƒxEFor he hath said: Man shall not please God, although he run with him.€€ƒxhTherefore, ye men of understanding, hear me: far from God be wickedness, and iniquity from the Almighty.€€ƒxaFor he will render to a man his work, and according to the ways of every one he will reward them.€€ƒx`For in very deed God will not condemn without cause, neither will the Almighty pervert judgment.€€ƒx^What other hath he appointed over the earth? or whom hath he set over the world which he made?€€ƒxNIf he turn his heart to him, he shall draw his spirit and breath unto himself.€€ƒxAAll flesh shall perish together, and man shall return into ashes.€€ƒxYIf then thou hast understanding, hear what is said, and hearken to the voice of my words.€€ŒƒxHWho saith to the king: Thou art an apostate: who calleth rulers ungodly:€€ƒx’Who accepteth not the persons of princes: nor hath regarded the tyrant, when he contended against the poor man: for all are the work of his hands.€€ƒxƒThey shall suddenly die, and the people shall be troubled at midnight, and they shall pass, and take away the violent without hand.€€ƒxJFor his eyes are upon the ways of men, and he considereth all their steps.€€ƒx_There is no darkness, and there is no shadow of death, where they may be hid who work iniquity.€€ƒxHFor it is no longer in the power of man to enter into judgment with God.€€ƒx]He shall break in pieces many and innumerable, and shall make others to stand in their stead.€€ƒxdFor he knoweth their works: and therefore he shall bring night on them, and they shall be destroyed.€€ƒx4He hath struck them, as being wicked, in open sight.€€ƒxXWho as it were on purpose have revolted from him, and would not understand all his ways:€€ƒx\So that they caused the cry of the needy to come to him, and he heard the voice of the poor.€€ƒx£For when he granteth peace, who is there that can condemn? When he hideth his countenance, who is there that can behold him, whether it regard nations, or all men?€€˜(ƒxESeeing then I have spoken of God, I will not hinder thee in thy turn.€€ƒxNIf I have erred, teach thou me: if I have spoken iniquity, I will add no more.€€ƒxDoth God require it of thee, because it hath displeased thee? for thou begannest to speak, and not I: but if thou know any thing better, speak.€€ƒxGLet men of understanding speak to me, and let a wise man hearken to me.€€ƒxBBut Job hath spoken foolishly, and his words sound not discipline.€€ƒxPMy father, let Job be tried even to the end: cease not from the man of iniquity.€€ƒx˜Because he addeth blasphemy upon his sins, let him be tied fast in the mean time amongst us: and then let him provoke God to judgment with his speeches.€€ƒ`€€ƒx Moreover Eliu spoke these words:€€ƒxUDoth thy thought seem right to thee, that thou shouldst say: I am more just than God?€€ƒx`For thou saidst: That which is right doth not please thee: or what will it profit thee if I sin?€€ƒx=Therefore I will answer thy words, and thy friends with thee.€€ƒxKLook up to heaven and see, and behold the sky, that it is higher than thee.€€ƒxkIf thou sin, what shalt thou hurt him? and if thy iniquities be multiplied, what shalt thou do against him?€€ƒxVAnd if thou do justly, what shalt thou give him, or what shall he receive of thy hand?€€ƒxYThy wickedness may hurt a man that is like thee: and thy justice may help the son of man.€€ƒxsBy reason of the multitude of oppressors they shall cry out: and shall wail for the violence of the arm of tyrants.€€ƒxSAnd he hath not said: Where is God, who made me, who hath given songs in the night?€€ƒxeWho teacheth us more than the beasts of the earth, and instructeth us more than the fowls of the air.€€ƒxMThere shall they cry, and he will not hear, because of the pride of evil men.€€ƒx]God therefore will not hear in vain, and the Almighty will look into the causes of every one.€€ƒxSYea, when thou shalt say: He considereth not: be judged before him, and expect him.€€ƒxVFor he doth not now bring on his fury, neither doth he revenge wickedness exceedingly.€€ƒxQTherefore Job openeth his mouth in vain, and multiplieth words without knowledge.€€ƒ`€€ƒxEliu also proceeded, and said:€€ƒx[Suffer me a little, and I will shew thee: for I have yet somewhat to speak in God's behalf.€€ƒxNI will repeat my knowledge from the beginning, and I will prove my Maker just.€€ƒxUFor indeed my words are without a lie, and perfect knowledge shall be proved to thee.€€ƒxEGod doth not cast away the mighty, whereas he himself also is mighty.€€ƒxABut he saveth not the wicked, and he giveth judgment to the poor.€€ƒxpHe will not take away his eyes from the just, and he placeth kings on the throne for ever, and they are exalted.€€ƒxGAnd if they shall be in chains, and be bound with the cords of poverty:€€ƒxWHe shall shew them their works, and their wicked deeds, because they have been violent.€€ƒxcHe also shall open their ear, to correct them: and shall speak, that they may return from iniquity.€€ƒxcIf they shall hear and observe, they shall accomplish their days in good, and their years in glory.€€ƒxSBut if they hear not, they shall pass by the sword, and shall be consumed in folly.€€ƒx^Dissemblers and crafty men prove the wrath of God, neither shall they cry when they are bound.€€ƒxETheir soul shall die in a storm, and their life among the effeminate.€€ƒxTHe shall deliver the poor out of his distress, and shall open his ear in affliction.€€–ƒxXThy cause hath been judged as that of the wicked, cause and judgment thou shalt recover.€€ƒxiTherefore let not anger overcome thee to oppress any man: neither let multitude of gifts turn thee aside.€€ƒxKLay down thy greatness without tribulation, and all the mighty of strength.€€ƒx7Prolong not the night that people may come up for them.€€ƒxXBeware thou turn not aside to iniquity: for this thou hast begun to follow after misery.€€ƒxNBehold, God is high in his strength, and none is like him among the lawgivers.€€ƒxOWho can search out his ways? or who can say to him: Thou hast wrought iniquity?€€ƒxHRemember that thou knowest not his work, concerning which men have sung.€€ƒx.All men see him, every one beholdeth afar off.€€ƒxVBehold, God is great, exceeding our knowledge: the number of his years is inestimable.€€ƒxEHe lifteth up the drops of rain, and poureth out showers like floods:€€ƒx0Which flow from the clouds that cover all above.€€ƒx)If he will spread out clouds as his tent,€€ƒxOAnd lighten with his light from above, he shall cover also the ends of the sea.€€ƒx@For by these he judgeth people, and giveth food to many mortals.€€ƒxBIn his hands he hideth the light, and commandeth it to come again.€€ƒx^He sheweth his friend concerning it, that it is his possession, and that he may come up to it.€€ƒ`€€ƒx:At this my heart trembleth, and is moved out of its place.€€ƒxXHear ye attentively the terror of his voice, and the sound that cometh out of his mouth.€€ƒxPHe beholdeth under all the heavens, and his light is upon the ends of the earth.€€ƒx‡After it a noise shall roar, he shall thunder with the voice of his majesty, and shall not be found out, when his voice shall be heard.€€ƒxfHe commandeth the snow to go down upon the earth, and the winter rain, and the shower of his strength.€€ƒxEHe sealeth up the hand of all men, that every one may know his works.€€ƒxDThen the beast shall go into his covert, and shall abide in his den.€€ƒxGOut of the inner parts shall a tempest come, and cold out of the north.€€ƒxTWhen God bloweth there cometh frost, and again the waters are poured out abundantly.€€ƒx8Corn desireth clouds, and the clouds spread their light:€€ƒxWhich go round about, whithersoever the will of him that governeth them shall lead them, to whatsoever he shall command them upon the face of the whole earth:€€ƒxqWhether in one tribe, or in his own land, or in what place soever of his mercy he shall command them to be found.€€ƒxLHearken to these things, Job: Stand, and consider the wondrous works of God.€€ƒxMDost thou know when God commanded the rains, to shew his light of his clouds?€€ƒxGKnowest thou the great paths of the clouds, and the perfect knowledges?€€ƒxCAre not thy garments hot, when the south wind blows upon the earth?€€ƒxdThou perhaps hast made the heavens with him, which are most strong, as if they were of molten brass.€€‘ƒx[Who shall tell him the things I speak? even if a man shall speak, he shall be swallowed up.€€ƒx€But now they see not the light: the air on a sudden shall be thickened into clouds, and the wind shall pass and drive them away.€€ƒx:Cold cometh out of the north, and to God praise with fear.€€ƒxkWe cannot find him worthily: he is great in strength, and in judgment, and in justice, and he is ineffable.€€ƒxgTherefore men shall fear him, and all that seem to themselves to be wise, shall not dare to behold him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx8Then the Lord answered Job out of a whirlwind, and said:€€ƒx:Who is this that wrappeth up sentences in unskilful words?€€ƒxBGird up thy loins like a man: I will ask thee, and answer thou me.€€ƒx]Where wast thou when I laid the foundations of the earth? tell me if thou hast understanding.€€ƒx[Who hath laid the measures thereof, if thou knowest or who hath stretched the line upon it?€€ƒxGUpon what are its bases grounded? or who laid the corner stone thereof,€€ƒxYWhen the morning stars praised me together, and all the sons of God made a joyful melody?€€ƒxOWho shut up the sea with doors, when it broke forth as issuing out of the womb:€€ƒxXWhen I made a cloud the garment thereof, and wrapped it in a mist as in swaddling bands?€€ƒx6I set my bounds around it, and made it bars and doors:€€ƒxlAnd I said: Hitherto thou shalt come, and shalt go no further, and here thou shalt break thy swelling waves.€€ƒxjAnd didst thou hold the extremities of the earth shaking them, and hast thou shaken the ungodly out of it?€€ƒxAThe seal shall be restored as clay, and shall stand as a garment.€€ƒxRFrom the wicked their light shall be taken away, and the high arm shall be broken.€€ƒxYHast thou entered into the depths of the sea, and walked in the lowest parts of the deep?€€ƒxSHave the gates of death been opened to thee, and hast thou seen the darksome doors?€€ƒxSHast thou considered the breadth of the earth? tell me, if thou knowest all things.€€ƒxJWhere is the way where light dwelleth, and where is the place of darkness?€€ƒxcThat thou mayst bring every thing to its own bounds, and understand the paths of the house thereof.€€ƒx\Didst thou know then that thou shouldst be born? and didst thou know the number of thy days?€€ƒxbHast thou entered into the storehouses of the snow, or hast thou beheld the treasures of the hail:€€ƒxSWhich I have prepared for the time of the enemy, against the day of battle and war?€€ƒxABy what way is the light spread, and heat divided upon the earth?€€ƒxAWho gave a course to violent showers, or a way for noisy thunder:€€‹ƒxUThat it should fill the desert and desolate land, and should bring forth green grass?€€ƒx9Who is the father of rain? or who begot the drops of dew?€€ƒxOOut of whose womb came the ice? and the frost from heaven who hath gendered it?€€ƒxOThe waters are hardened like a stone, and the surface of the deep is congealed.€€ƒxuShalt thou be able to join together the shining stars the Pleiades, or canst thou stop the turning about of Arcturus?€€ƒxrCanst thou bring forth the day star in its time, and make the evening star to rise upon the children of the earth?€€ƒx\Dost thou know the order of heaven, and canst thou set down the reason thereof on the earth?€€ƒxWCanst thou lift up thy voice to the clouds, that an abundance of waters may cover thee?€€ƒx`Canst thou send lightnings, and will they go, and will they return and say to thee: Here we are?€€ƒxLWho hath put wisdom in the heart of man? or who gave the cock understanding?€€ƒxYWho can declare the order of the heavens, or who can make the harmony of heaven to sleep?€€…ƒxPWilt thou take the prey for the lioness, and satisfy the appetite of her whelps,€€ƒx5When they couch in the dens and lie in wait in holes?€€ƒxmWho provideth food for the raven, when her young ones cry to God, wandering about, because they have no meat?€€ƒ`€€ƒxvKnowest thou the time when the wild goats bring forth among the rocks, or hast thou observed the hinds when they fawn?€€˜ƒxVThey bow themselves to bring forth young, and they cast them, and send forth roarings.€€ƒxMTheir young are weaned and go to feed: they go forth, and return not to them.€€ƒxCWho hath sent out the wild ass free, and who hath loosed his bonds?€€ƒxUTo whom I have given a house in the wilderness, and his dwellings in the barren land.€€ƒxLHe scorneth the multitude of the city, he heareth not the cry of the driver.€€ƒxWHe looketh round about the mountains of his pasture, and seeketh for every green thing.€€ƒxKShall the rhinoceros be willing to serve thee, or will he stay at thy crib?€€ƒxnCanst thou bind the rhinoceros with thy thong to plough, or will he break the clods of the valleys after thee?€€ƒxNWilt thou have confidence in his great strength, and leave thy labours to him?€€ƒxXWilt thou trust him that he will render thee the seed, and gather it into thy barnfloor?€€ƒxHThe wing of the ostrich is like the wings of the heron, and of the hawk.€€ƒxPWhen she leaveth her eggs on the earth, thou perhaps wilt warm them in the dust.€€ƒxaShe forgetteth that the foot may tread upon them, or that the beasts of the field may break them.€€ƒxzShe is hardened against her young ones, as though they were not hers, she hath laboured in vain, no fear constraining her.€€ƒxMFor God hath deprived her of wisdom, neither hath he given her understanding.€€ƒx[When time shall be, she setteth up her wings on high: she scorneth the horse and his rider.€€ƒxFWilt thou give strength to the horse or clothe his neck with neighing?€€ƒxLWilt thou lift him up like the locusts? the glory of his nostrils is terror.€€ƒx_He breaketh up the earth with his hoof, he pranceth boldly, he goeth forward to meet armed men.€€ƒx8He despiseth fear, he turneth not his back to the sword.€€ƒxFAbove him shall the quiver rattle, the spear and shield shall glitter.€€ƒxqChasing and raging he swalloweth the ground, neither doth he make account when the noise of the trumpet soundeth.€€ƒxWhen he heareth the trumpet he saith: Ha, ha: he smelleth the battle afar off, the encouraging of the captains, and the shouting of the army.€€ƒxLDoth the hawk wax feathered by thy wisdom, spreading her wings to the south?€€ƒxIWill the eagle mount up at thy command, and make her nest in high places?€€ƒxjShe abideth among the rocks, and dwelleth among cragged flints, and stony hills, where there is no access.€€‘ƒxcHer young ones shall suck up blood: and wheresoever the carcass shall be, she is immediately there.€€ƒx&And the Lord went on, and said to Job:€€ƒxkShall he that contendeth with God be so easily silenced? surely he that reproveth God, ought to answer him.€€ƒx%Then Job answered the Lord, and said:€€ƒxUWhat can I answer, who hath spoken inconsiderately? I will lay my hand upon my mouth.€€ƒx_One thing I have spoken, which I wish I had not said: and another, to which I will add no more.€€ƒ`€€ƒx6And the Lord answering Job out of the whirlwind, said:€€ƒxCGird up thy loins like a man: I will ask thee, and do thou tell me.€€ƒxNWilt thou make void my judgment: and condemn me, that thou mayst be justified?€€ƒxLAnd hast thou an arm like God, and canst thou thunder with a voice like him?€€ƒxdClothe thyself with beauty, and set thyself up on high, and be glorious, and put on goodly garments.€€ƒxTScatter the proud in thy indignation, and behold every arrogant man, and humble him.€€ƒxSLook on all that are proud, and confound them, and crush the wicked in their place,€€ƒxDHide them in the dust together, and plunge their faces into the pit.€€ƒx=Then I will confess that thy right hand is able to save thee.€€ƒxBBehold behemoth whom I made with thee, he eateth grass like an ox.€€‹ƒxVHe setteth up his tail like a cedar, the sinews of his testicles are wrapped together.€€ƒxCHis bones are like pipes of brass, his gristle like plates of iron.€€ƒxNHe is the beginning of the ways of God, who made him, he will apply his sword.€€ƒxUTo him the mountains bring forth grass: there all the beasts of the field shall play.€€ƒxMHe sleepeth under the shadow, in the covert of the reed, and in moist places.€€ƒxNThe shades cover his shadow, the willows of the brook shall compass him about.€€ƒxiBehold, he will drink up a river, and not wonder: and he trusteth that the Jordan may run into his mouth.€€ƒxXIn his eyes as with a hook he shall take him, and bore through his nostrils with stakes.€€ƒxXCanst thou draw out the leviathan with a hook, or canst thou tie his tongue with a cord?€€ƒxICanst thou put a ring in his nose, or bore through his jaw with a buckle?€€ƒxEWill he make many supplications to thee, or speak soft words to thee?€€‰ƒxIShalt thou play with him as with a bird, or tie him up for thy handmaids?€€ƒx<Shall friends cut him in pieces, shall merchants divide him?€€ƒxJWilt thou fill nets with his skin, and the cabins of fishes with his head?€€ƒx>Lay thy hand upon him: remember the battle, and speak no more.€€ƒxNBehold his hope shall fail him, and in the sight of all he shall be cast down.€€ƒ`€€ƒxRI will not stir him up, like one that is cruel, for who can resist my countenance?€€ƒx\Who hath given me before that I should repay him? All things that are under heaven are mine.€€ƒxLI will not spare him, nor his mighty words, and framed to make supplication.€€ƒxGWho can open the doors of his face? his teeth are terrible round about.€€ƒxUHis body is like molten shields, shut close up with scales pressing upon one another.€€ƒxKOne is joined to another, and not so much as any air can come between them:€€ƒxUThey stick one to another and they hold one another fast, and shall not be separated.€€ƒxWHis sneezing is like the shining of fire, and his eyes like the eyelids of the morning.€€ƒx>Out of his mouth go forth lamps, like torches of lighted fire.€€ƒxGOut of his nostrils goeth smoke, like that of a pot heated and boiling.€€ƒxEHis breath kindleth coals, and a flame cometh forth out of his mouth.€€ƒxAIn his neck strength shall dwell, and want goeth before his face.€€ƒx…The members of his flesh cleave one to another: he shall send lightnings against him, and they shall not be carried to another place.€€ƒxFHis heart shall be as hard as a stone, and as firm as a smith's anvil.€€ƒx`When he shall raise him up, the angels shall fear, and being affrighted shall purify themselves.€€ƒx\When a sword shall lay at him, it shall not be able to hold, nor a spear, nor a breastplate.€€ƒx<For he shall esteem iron as straw, and brass as rotten wood.€€”ƒx[As stubble will he esteem the hammer, and he will laugh him to scorn who shaketh the spear.€€ƒxUThe beams of the sun shall be under him, and he shall strew gold under him like mire.€€ƒxXHe shall make the deep sea to boil like a pot, and shall make it as when ointments boil.€€ƒxFA path shall shine after him, he shall esteem the deep as growing old.€€ƒxWThere is no power upon earth that can be compared with him who was made to fear no one.€€ƒxIHe beholdeth every high thing, he is king over all the children of pride.€€ƒ`€€ƒx%Then Job answered the Lord, and said:€€ƒxFI know that thou canst do all things, and no thought is hid from thee.€€ƒx‰Who is this that hideth counsel without knowledge? Therefore I have spoken unwisely, and things that above measure exceeded my knowledge.€€ƒx=Hear, and I will speak: I will ask thee, and do thou tell me.€€ƒxJWith the hearing of the ear, I have heard thee, but now my eye seeth thee.€€ƒx?Therefore I reprehend myself, and do penance in dust and ashes.€€ƒxéAnd after the Lord had spoken these words to Job, he said to Eliphaz the Themanite: My wrath is kindled against thee, and against thy two friends, because you have not spoken the thing that is right before me, as my servant Job hath.€€ƒyTake unto you therefore seven oxen and seven rams, and go to my servant Job, and offer for yourselves a holocaust, and my servant Job shall pray for you: his face I will accept, that folly be not imputed to you: for you have not spoken right things before me, as my servant Job hath.€€ƒxŸSo Eliphaz the Themanite, and Baldad the Suhite, and Sophar the Naamathite went, and did as the Lord had spoken to them, and the Lord accepted the face of Job.€€ƒx…The Lord also was turned at the penance of Job, when he prayed for his friends. And the Lord gave Job twice as much as he had before.€€ƒyAnd all his brethren came to him, and all his sisters, and all that knew him before, and they ate bread with him in his house: and bemoaned him, and comforted him upon all the evil that God had brought upon him. And every man gave him one ewe, and one earring of gold.€€ƒx·And the Lord blessed the latter end of Job more than his beginning. And he had fourteen thousand sheep, and six thousand camels, and a thousand yoke of oxen, and a thousand she asses.€€ƒx+And he had seven sons, and three daughters.€€—ƒx’And there were not found in all the earth women so beautiful as the daughters of Job: and their father gave them inheritance among their brethren.€€ƒx¸And Job lived after these things, a hundred and forty years, and he saw his children, and his children's children, unto the fourth generation, and he died an old man, and full of days.€€ƒrThe Book of Psalms€€ƒ`€€ƒxŠBlessed is the man who hath not walked in the counsel of the ungodly, nor stood in the way of sinners, nor sat in the chair of pestilence:€€ƒxWBut his will is in the law of the Lord, and on his law he shall meditate day and night.€€ƒxÆAnd he shall be like a tree which is planted near the running waters, which shall bring forth its fruit, in due season. And his leaf shall not fall off: and all whatsoever he shall do shall prosper.€€ƒx`Not so the wicked, not so: but like the dust, which the wind driveth from the face of the earth.€€ƒx^Therefore the wicked shall not rise again in judgment: nor sinners in the council of the just.€€ƒxQFor the Lord knoweth the way of the just: and the way of the wicked shall perish.€€ƒ`€€ƒx@Why have the Gentiles raged, and the people devised vain things?€€ƒxhThe kings of the earth stood up, and the princes met together, against the Lord, and against his Christ.€€ƒxJLet us break their bonds asunder: and let us cast away their yoke from us.€€ƒxOHe that dwelleth in heaven shall laugh at them: and the Lord shall deride them.€€ƒxGThen shall he speak to them in his anger, and trouble them in his rage.€€ƒxWBut I am appointed king by him over Sion, his holy mountain, preaching his commandment.€€ƒxIThe Lord hath said to me: Thou art my son, this day have I begotten thee.€€ƒxwAsk of me, and I will give thee the Gentiles for thy inheritance, and the utmost parts of the earth for thy possession.€€ƒx_Thou shalt rule them with a rod of iron, and shalt break them in pieces like a potter's vessel.€€ƒxOAnd now, O ye kings, understand: receive instruction, you that judge the earth.€€ƒxAServe ye the Lord with fear: and rejoice unto him with trembling.€€ƒxYEmbrace discipline, lest at any time the Lord be angry, and you perish from the just way.€€“ƒ`€€ƒxAThe psalm of David when he fled from the face of his son Absalom.€€ƒxWWhy, O Lord, are they multiplied that afflict me? many are they who rise up against me.€€ƒx>Many say to my soul: There is no salvation for him in his God.€€ƒxKBut thou, O Lord, art my protector, my glory, and the lifter up of my head.€€ƒxPI have cried to the Lord with my voice: and he hath heard me from his holy hill.€€ƒx]I have slept and have taken my rest: and I have risen up, because the Lord hath protected me.€€ƒxYI will not fear thousands of the people surrounding me: arise, O Lord; save me, O my God.€€ƒxjFor thou hast struck all them who are my adversaries without cause: thou hast broken the teeth of sinners.€€ƒx>Salvation is of the Lord: and thy blessing is upon thy people.€€ƒ`€€ƒx+Unto the end, in verses. A psalm for David.€€ƒxŒWhen I called upon him, the God of my justice heard me: when I was in distress, thou hast enlarged me. Have mercy on me: and hear my prayer.€€ƒxcO ye sons of men, how long will you be dull of heart? why do you love vanity, and seek after lying?€€ƒxmKnow ye also that the Lord hath made his holy one wonderful: the Lord will hear me when I shall cry unto him.€€ƒx^Be ye angry, and sin not: the things you say in your hearts, be sorry for them upon your beds.€€ƒx_Offer up the sacrifice of justice, and trust in the Lord: many say, Who sheweth us good things?€€ƒx^The light of thy countenance, O Lord, is signed upon us: thou hast given gladness in my heart.€€ƒxEBy the fruit of their corn, their wine, and oil, they are multiplied:€€‰ƒx5For thou, O Lord, singularly hast settled me in hope.€€ƒ`€€ƒxHUnto the end, for her that obtaineth the inheritance. A psalm for David.€€ƒx1Give ear, O Lord, to my words, understand my cry.€€ƒx8Hearken to the voice of my prayer, O my King and my God.€€ƒxIFor to thee will I pray: O Lord, in the morning thou shalt hear my voice.€€ƒxjIn the morning I will stand before thee, and I will see: because thou art not a God that willest iniquity.€€ƒxUNeither shall the wicked dwell near thee: nor shall the unjust abide before thy eyes.€€ƒx†Thou hatest all the workers of iniquity: thou wilt destroy all that speak a lie. The bloody and the deceitful man the Lord will abhor.€€†ƒxVConduct me, O Lord, in thy justice: because of my enemies, direct my way in thy sight.€€ƒx:For there is no truth in their mouth: their heart is vain.€€ƒxçTheir throat is an open sepulchre: they dealt deceitfully with their tongues: judge them, O God. Let them fall from their devices: according to the multitude of their wickednesses cast them out: for they have provoked thee, O Lord.€€ƒx›But let all them be glad that hope in thee: they shall rejoice for ever, and thou shalt dwell in them. And all they that love thy name shall glory in thee.€€ƒx^For thou wilt bless the just. O Lord, thou hast crowned us, as with a shield of thy good will.€€ƒ`€€‡ƒxGO Lord, rebuke me not in thy indignation, nor chastise me in thy wrath.€€ƒxTHave mercy on me, O Lord, for I am weak: heal me, O Lord, for my bones are troubled.€€ƒx@And my soul is troubled exceedingly: but thou, O Lord, how long?€€ƒxHTurn to me, O Lord, and deliver my soul: O save me for thy mercy's sake.€€ƒx]For there is no one in death, that is mindful of thee: and who shall confess to thee in hell?€€ƒxeI have laboured in my groanings, every night I will wash my bed: I will water my couch with my tears.€€ƒxPMy eye is troubled through indignation: I have grown old amongst all my enemies.€€—ƒxFThe Lord hath heard my supplication: the Lord hath received my prayer.€€ƒxpLet all my enemies be ashamed, and be very much troubled: let them be turned back, and be ashamed very speedily.€€ƒ`€€ƒxYThe psalm of David, which he sung to the Lord, for the words of Chusi, the son of Jemini.€€ƒxeO Lord, my God, in thee have I put my trust; save me from all them that persecute me, and deliver me.€€ƒxdLest at any time he seize upon my soul like a lion, while there is no one to redeem me, nor to save.€€ƒxLO Lord, my God, if I have done this thing, if there be iniquity in my hands:€€ƒx`If I have rendered to them that repaid me evils, let me deservedly fall empty before my enemies.€€ƒxuLet the enemy pursue my soul, and take it, and tread down my life, on the earth, and bring down my glory to the dust.€€ƒx•Rise up, O Lord, in thy anger: and be thou exalted in the borders of my enemies. And arise, O Lord, my God, in the precept which thou hast commanded:€€ƒxZAnd a congregation of people shall surround thee. And for their sakes return thou on high.€€ƒxlThe Lord judgeth the people. Judge me, O Lord, according to my justice, and according to my innocence in me.€€ƒxƒThe wickedness of sinners shall be brought to nought; and thou shalt direct the just: the searcher of hearts and reins is God. Just€€ƒx:Is my help from the Lord; who saveth the upright of heart.€€ƒx?God is a just judge, strong and patient: is he angry every day?€€ƒxbExcept you will be converted, he will brandish his sword; he hath bent his bow, and made it ready.€€ƒxeAnd in it he hath prepared to instruments of death, he hath made ready his arrows for them that burn.€€ƒxcBehold he hath been in labour with injustice: he hath conceived sorrow, and brought forth iniquity.€€ƒxHHe hath opened a pit and dug it: and he is fallen into the hole he made.€€ƒx\His sorrow shall be turned on his own head: and his iniquity shall come down upon his crown.€€ƒxlI will give glory to the Lord according to his justice: and will sing to the name of the Lord the most high.€€ƒ`€€ƒx1Unto the end, for the presses: a psalm for David.€€ƒx“Out of the mouth of infants and of sucklings thou hast perfected praise, because of thy enemies, that thou mayst destroy the enemy and the avenger.€€ƒxhFor I will behold thy heavens, the works of thy fingers: the moon and the stars which thou hast founded.€€ƒxUWhat is man, that thou art mindful of him? or the son of man, that thou visitest him?€€ƒx^Thou hast made him a little less than the angels, thou hast crowned him with glory and honour:€€ƒx-And hast set him over the works of thy hands.€€ƒxkThou hast subjected all things under his feet, all sheep and oxen: moreover, the beasts also of the fields.€€ƒxXThe birds of the air, and the fishes of the sea, that pass through the paths of the sea.€€ƒx?O Lord, our Lord, how admirable is thy name in the whole earth!€€ƒ`€€ƒxBUnto the end, for the hidden things of the Son. A psalm for David.€€ƒxWI will give praise to thee, O Lord, with my whole heart: I will relate all thy wonders.€€ƒxOI will be glad, and rejoice in thee: I will sing to thy name, O thou most high.€€ƒxWWhen my enemy shall be turned back: they shall be weakened, and perish before thy face.€€‹ƒxuThou hast rebuked the Gentiles, and the wicked one hath perished; thou hast blotted out their name for ever and ever.€€ƒx€The swords of the enemy have failed unto the end: and their cities thou hast destroyed. Their memory hath perished with a noise:€€ƒxIBut the Lord remaineth for ever. He hath prepared his throne in judgment:€€ƒxMAnd he shall judge the world in equity, he shall judge the people in justice.€€ƒxRAnd the Lord is become a refuge for the poor: a helper in due time in tribulation.€€ƒxeAnd let them trust in thee who know thy name: for thou hast not forsaken them that seek thee, O Lord.€€ƒxOSing ye to the Lord, who dwelleth in Sion: declare his ways among the Gentiles:€€ƒx^For requiring their blood, he hath remembered them: he hath not forgotten the cry of the poor.€€ƒxLHave mercy on me, O Lord: see my humiliation which I suffer from my enemies.€€ƒxyThou that liftest me up from the gates of death, that I may declare all thy praises in the gates of the daughter of Sion.€€ƒx¢I will rejoice in thy salvation: the Gentiles have stuck fast in the destruction which they prepared. Their foot hath been taken in the very snare which they hid.€€‹ƒxFThe wicked shall be turned into hell, all the nations that forget God.€€ƒxgFor the poor man shall not be forgotten to the end: the patience of the poor shall not perish for ever.€€ƒxTArise, O Lord, let not man be strengthened: let the Gentiles be judged in thy sight.€€ƒx[Appoint, O Lord, a lawgiver over them: that the Gentiles may know themselves to be but men.€€ƒxfWhy, O Lord, hast thou retired afar off? why dost thou slight us in our wants, in the time of trouble?€€ƒxkWhilst the wicked man is proud, the poor is set on fire: they are caught in the counsels which they devise.€€ƒxTFor the sinner is praised in the desires of his soul: and the unjust man is blessed.€€ƒx_The sinner hath provoked the Lord according to the multitude of his wrath he will not seek him:€€ƒx‹God is not before his eyes: his ways are filthy at all times. Thy judgments are removed from his sight: he shall rule over all his enemies.€€ƒxmFor he hath said in his heart: I shall not be moved from generation to generation, and shall be without evil.€€ƒxfHis mouth is full of cursing, and of bitterness, and of deceit: under his tongue are labor and sorrow.€€‘ƒx¶His eyes are upon the poor man: He lieth in wait in secret like a lion in his den. He lieth in ambush that he may catch the poor man: to catch the poor, whilst he draweth him to him.€€ƒxcIn his net he will bring him down, he will crouch and fall, when he shall have power over the poor.€€ƒxfFor he hath said in his heart: God hath forgotten, he hath turned away his face not to see to the end.€€ƒx@Arise, O Lord God, let thy hand be exalted: forget not the poor.€€ƒx^Wherefore hath the wicked provoked God? for he hath said in his heart: He will not require it.€€ƒx¦Thou seest it, for thou considerest labour and sorrow: that thou mayst deliver them into thy hands. To thee is the poor man left: thou wilt be a helper to the orphan.€€ƒxgBreak thou the arm of the sinner and of the malignant: his sin shall be sought, and shall not be found.€€ƒxaThe Lord shall reign to eternity, yea, for ever and ever: ye Gentiles shall perish from his land.€€ƒx^The Lord hath heard the desire of the poor: thy ear hath heard the preparation of their heart.€€ƒxkTo judge for the fatherless and for the humble, that man may no more presume to magnify himself upon earth.€€ƒ`€€ƒxUnto the end. A psalm to David.€€ƒxuIn the Lord I put my trust: how then do you say to my soul: Get thee away from hence to the mountain, like a sparrow.€€ƒx‚For, lo, the wicked have bent their bow: they have prepared their arrows in the quiver, to shoot in the dark the upright of heart.€€ƒxXFor they have destroyed the things which thou hast made: but what has the just man done?€€ƒxƒThe Lord is in his holy temple, the Lord's throne is in heaven. His eyes look on the poor man: his eyelids examine the sons of men.€€ƒxZThe Lord trieth the just and the wicked: but he that loveth iniquity, hateth his own soul.€€ŒƒxXFor the Lord is just, and hath loved justice: his countenance hath beheld righteousness.€€ƒ`€€ƒx0Unto the end: for the octave, a psalm for David.€€ƒx^Save me, O Lord, for there is now no saint: truths are decayed from among the children of men.€€ƒxxThey have spoken vain things, every one to his neighbour: with deceitful lips, and with a double heart have they spoken.€€ƒxSMay the Lord destroy all deceitful lips, and the tongue that speaketh proud things.€€ƒxUWho have said: We will magnify our tongue: our lips are our own: who is Lord over us?€€ƒx¤By reason of the misery of the needy, and the groans of the poor, now will I arise, saith the Lord. I will set him in safety: I will deal confidently in his regard.€€ƒxnThe words of the Lord are pure words: as silver tried by the fire, purged from the earth, refined seven times.€€ƒxJThou, O Lord, wilt preserve us: and keep us from this generation for ever.€€ƒxaThe wicked walk round about: according to thy highness, thou hast multiplied the children of men.€€ƒ`€€ˆƒxJHow long shall I take counsels in my soul, sorrow in my heart all the day?€€ƒx+How long shall my enemy be exalted over me?€€ƒxVConsider, and hear me, O Lord, my God. Enlighten my eyes, that I never sleep in death:€€ƒxpLest at any time my enemy say: I have prevailed against him. They that trouble me, will rejoice when I am moved:€€ƒx¶But I have trusted in thy mercy. My heart shall rejoice in thy salvation: I will sing to the Lord, who giveth me good things: yea, I will sing to the name of the Lord, the most high.€€ƒ`€€ƒx·Unto the end, a psalm for David. The fool hath said in his heart: There is no God. They are corrupt, and are become abominable in their ways: there is none that doth good, no not one.€€ƒxtThe Lord hath looked down from heaven upon the children of men, to see if there be any that understand and seek God.€€ŒƒxSShall not all they know that work iniquity, who devour my people as they eat bread?€€ƒx_They have not called upon the Lord: there have they trembled for fear, where there was no fear.€€ƒxrFor the Lord is in the just generation: you have confounded the counsel of the poor man; but the Lord is his hope.€€ƒx¤Who shall give out of Sion the salvation of Israel? when the Lord shall have turned away the captivity of his people, Jacob shall rejoice, and Israel shall be glad.€€ƒ`€€ƒx_A psalm for David. Lord, who shall dwell in thy tabernacle? or who shall rest in thy holy hill?€€ƒx5He that walketh without blemish, and worketh justice:€€ƒx¡He that speaketh truth in his heart, who hath not used deceit in his tongue: Nor hath done evil to his neighbour: nor taken up a reproach against his neighbours.€€ƒx’In his sight the malignant is brought to nothing: but he glorifieth them that fear the Lord. He that sweareth to his neighbour, and deceiveth not;€€ƒx‹He that hath not put out his money to usury, nor taken bribes against the innocent: He that doth these things, shall not be moved for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒxbThe inscription of a title to David himself. Preserve me, O Lord, for I have put my trust in thee.€€‹ƒxRTo the saints, who are in his land, he hath made wonderful all my desires in them.€€ƒx­Their infirmities were multiplied: afterwards they made haste. I will not gather together their meetings for bloodofferings: nor will I be mindful of their names by my lips.€€ƒxkThe Lord is the portion of my inheritance and of my cup: it is thou that wilt restore my inheritance to me.€€ƒxRThe lines are fallen unto me in goodly places: for my inheritance is goodly to me.€€ƒxrI will bless the Lord, who hath given me understanding: moreover, my reins also have corrected me even till night.€€ƒxSI set the Lord always in my sight: for he is at my right hand, that I be not moved.€€ƒxkTherefore my heart hath been glad, and my tongue hath rejoiced: moreover, my flesh also shall rest in hope.€€ƒx_Because thou wilt not leave my soul in hell; nor wilt thou give thy holy one to see corruption.€€ƒxThou hast made known to me the ways of life, thou shalt fill me with joy with thy countenance: at thy right hand are delights even to the end.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŒThe prayer of David. Hear, O Lord, my justice: attend to my supplication. Give ear unto my prayer, which proceedeth not from deceitful lips.€€ŠƒxwThou hast proved my heart, and visited it by night, thou hast tried me by fire: and iniquity hath not been found in me.€€ƒxkThat my mouth may not speak the works of men: for the sake of the words of thy lips, I have kept hard ways.€€ƒxDPerfect thou my goings in thy paths: that my footsteps be not moved.€€ƒxcI have cried to thee, for thou, O God, hast heard me: O incline thy ear unto me, and hear my words.€€ƒxJShew forth thy wonderful mercies; thou who savest them that trust in thee.€€ƒxpFrom them that resist thy right hand keep me, as the apple of thy eye. Protect me under the shadow of thy wings.€€ƒxVFrom the face of the wicked who have afflicted me. My enemies have surrounded my soul:€€ƒx=They have shut up their fat: their mouth hath spoken proudly.€€ƒxlThey have cast me forth, and now they have surrounded me: they have set their eyes bowing down to the earth.€€ƒxcThey have taken me, as a lion prepared for the prey; and as a young lion dwelling in secret places.€€ƒxèFrom the enemies of thy hand. O Lord, divide them from the few of the earth in their life: their belly is filled from thy hidden stores. They are full of children: and they have left to their little ones the rest of their substance.€€ƒxkBut as for me, I will appear before thy sight in justice: I shall be satisfied when thy glory shall appear.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÔUnto the end, for David, the servant of the Lord, who spoke to the Lord the words of this canticle, in the day that the Lord delivered him from the hand of all his enemies, and from the hand of Saul: and he said:€€ƒx&I will love thee, O Lord, my strength:€€ƒx§The Lord is my firmament, my refuge, and my deliverer. My God is my helper, and in him will I put my trust. My protector, and the horn of my salvation, and my support.€€ƒxJPraising, I will call upon the Lord: and I shall be saved from my enemies.€€ƒxMThe sorrows of death surrounded me: and the torrents of iniquity troubled me.€€ƒxIThe sorrows of hell encompassed me: and the snares of death prevented me.€€ƒx•In my affliction I called upon the Lord, and I cried to my God: And he heard my voice from his holy temple: and my cry before him came into his ears.€€ƒx|The earth shook and trembled: the foundations of the mountains were troubled and were moved, because he was angry with them.€€ƒx^There went up a smoke in his wrath: and a fire flamed from his face: coals were kindled by it.€€ƒxEHe bowed the heavens, and came down, and darkness was under his feet.€€”ƒxcAnd he made darkness his cover, his pavilion round about him: dark waters in the clouds of the air.€€ƒxPAt the brightness that was before him the clouds passed, hail and coals of fire.€€ƒx[And the Lord thundered from heaven, and the Highest gave his voice: hail and coals of fire.€€ƒxaAnd he sent forth his arrows, and he scattered them: he multiplied lightnings, and troubled them.€€ƒx˜Then the fountains of waters appeared, and the foundations of the world were discovered: At thy rebuke, O Lord, at the blast of the spirit of thy wrath.€€ƒxFHe sent from on high, and took me: and received me out of many waters.€€ƒxhHe delivered me from my strongest enemies, and from them that hated me: for they were too strong for me.€€ƒxPThey prevented me in the day of my affliction: and the Lord became my protector.€€ƒx]And he brought me forth into a large place: he saved me, because he was well pleased with me.€€ƒxnAnd the Lord will reward me according to my justice; and will repay me according to the cleanness of my hands:€€ƒxTBecause I have kept the ways of the Lord; and have not done wickedly against my God.€€ƒxTFor all his judgments are in my sight: and his justices I have not put away from me.€€ƒxIAnd I shall be spotless with him: and shall keep myself from my iniquity.€€ƒxpAnd the Lord will reward me according to my justice: and according to the cleanness of my hands before his eyes.€€ƒxQWith the holy thou wilt be holy; and with the innocent man thou wilt be innocent:€€ƒxTAnd with the elect thou wilt be elect: and with the perverse thou wilt be perverted.€€ƒxPFor thou wilt save the humble people; but wilt bring down the eyes of the proud.€€ƒxCFor thou lightest my lamp, O Lord: O my God, enlighten my darkness.€€ƒx\For by thee I shall be delivered from temptation; and through my God I shall go over a wall.€€ƒxxAs for my God, his way is undefiled: the words of the Lord are fire-tried: he is the protector of all that trust in him.€€‡ƒx?God, who hath girt me with strength; and made my way blameless.€€ƒxRWho hath made my feet like the feet of harts: and who setteth me upon high places.€€ƒxKWho teacheth my hands to war: and thou hast made my arms like a brazen bow.€€ƒx»And thou hast given me the protection of thy salvation: and thy right hand hath held me up: And thy discipline hath corrected me unto the end: and thy discipline, the same shall teach me.€€ƒxCThou hast enlarged my steps under me; and my feet are not weakened.€€ƒxdI will pursue after my enemies, and overtake them: and I will not turn again till they are consumed.€€ƒxVI will break them, and they shall not be able to stand: they shall fall under my feet.€€˜#ƒx]And thou hast made my enemies turn their back upon me, and hast destroyed them that hated me.€€ƒxPThey cried, but there was none to save them, to the Lord: but he heard them not.€€ƒxwAnd I shall beat them as small as the dust before the wind; I shall bring them to nought, like the dirt in the streets.€€ƒxcThou wilt deliver me from the contradictions of the people; thou wilt make me head of the Gentiles.€€ƒxYA people which I knew not, hath served me: at the hearing of the ear they have obeyed me.€€ƒxtThe children that are strangers have lied to me, strange children have faded away, and have halted from their paths.€€ƒxSThe Lord liveth, and blessed be my God, and let the God of my salvation be exalted.€€ƒx_O God, who avengest me, and subduest the people under me, my deliverer from my enraged enemies.€€ƒxfAnd thou wilt lift me up above them that rise up against me: from the unjust man thou wilt deliver me.€€ƒxdTherefore will I give glory to thee, O Lord, among the nations, and I will sing a psalm to thy name.€€ƒxiGiving great deliverance to his king, and shewing mercy to David, his anointed: and to his seed for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒx Unto the end. A Psalm for David.€€ƒx[The heavens shew forth the glory of God, and the firmament declareth the work of his hands.€€ƒxADay to day uttereth speech, and night to night sheweth knowledge.€€ƒxFThere are no speeches nor languages, where their voices are not heard.€€ƒx[Their sound hath gone forth into all the earth: and their words unto the ends of the world.€€ƒx†He hath set his tabernacle in the sun: and he as a bridegroom coming out of his bridechamber, Hath rejoiced as a giant to run the way:€€ƒxŠHis going out is from the end of heaven, And his circuit even to the end thereof: and there is no one that can hide himself from his heat.€€ƒxxThe law of the Lord is unspotted, converting souls: the testimony of the Lord is faithful, giving wisdom to little ones.€€ƒxvThe justices of the Lord are right, rejoicing hearts: the commandment of the Lord is lightsome, enlightening the eyes.€€ƒxvThe fear of the Lord is holy, enduring for ever and ever: the judgments of the Lord are true, justified in themselves.€€ƒx`More to be desired than gold and many precious stones: and sweeter than honey and the honeycomb.€€ƒxJFor thy servant keepeth them, and in keeping them there is a great reward.€€ƒx@Who can understand sins? from my secret ones cleanse me, O Lord:€€ƒx And from those of others spare thy servant. If they shall have no dominion over me, then shall I be without spot: and I shall be cleansed from the greatest sin.€€ƒxAnd the words of my mouth shall be such as may please: and the meditation of my heart always in thy sight. O Lord, my helper and my Redeemer.€€ƒ`€€ƒx Unto the end. A psalm for David.€€ƒx`May the Lord hear thee in the day of tribulation: may the name of the God of Jacob protect thee.€€ƒxFMay he send thee help from the sanctuary: and defend thee out of Sion.€€ƒxUMay he be mindful of all thy sacrifices: and may thy whole burntoffering be made fat.€€ƒxJMay he give thee according to thy own heart; and confirm all thy counsels.€€ƒxQWe will rejoice in thy salvation; and in the name of our God we shall be exalted.€€ƒx¯The Lord fulfil all thy petitions: now have I known that the Lord hath saved his anointed. He will hear him from his holy heaven: the salvation of his right hand is in powers.€€˜$ƒxGThey are bound, and have fallen: but we are risen, and are set upright.€€ƒxKO Lord, save the king: and hear us in the day that we shall call upon thee.€€ƒ`€€ƒx Unto the end. A psalm for David.€€ƒx_In thy strength, O Lord, the king shall joy; and in thy salvation he shall rejoice exceedingly.€€ƒx^Thou hast given him his heart's desire: and hast not withholden from him the will of his lips.€€ƒxnFor thou hast prevented him with blessings of sweetness: thou hast set on his head a crown of precious stones.€€ƒxPHe asked life of thee: and thou hast given him length of days for ever and ever.€€ƒxTHis glory is great in thy salvation: glory and great beauty shalt thou lay upon him.€€ƒxxFor thou shalt give him to be a blessing for ever and ever: thou shalt make him joyful in gladness with thy countenance.€€ƒx^For the king hopeth in the Lord: and through the mercy of the most High he shall not be moved.€€ƒx^Let thy hand be found by all thy enemies: let thy right hand find out all them that hate thee.€€ƒxˆThou shalt make them as an oven of fire, in the time of thy anger: the Lord shall trouble them in his wrath, and fire shall devour them.€€ƒx]Their fruit shalt thou destroy from the earth: and their seed from among the children of men.€€ƒxqFor they have intended evils against thee: they have devised counsels which they have not been able to establish.€€ƒxXFor thou shalt make them turn their back: in thy remnants thou shalt prepare their face.€€ƒxPBe thou exalted, O Lord, in thy own strength: we will sing and praise thy power.€€ƒ`€€ƒx<Unto the end, for the morning protection, a psalm for David.€€ƒxfO God my God, look upon me: why hast thou forsaken me? Far from my salvation are the words of my sins.€€ƒxoO my God, I shall cry by day, and thou wilt not hear: and by night, and it shall not be reputed as folly in me.€€ƒx:But thou dwellest in the holy place, the praise of Israel.€€ƒxNIn thee have our fathers hoped: they have hoped, and thou hast delivered them.€€ƒxWThey cried to thee, and they were saved: they trusted in thee, and were not confounded.€€ƒxPBut I am a worm, and no man: the reproach of men, and the outcast of the people.€€ƒxcAll they that saw me have laughed me to scorn: they have spoken with the lips, and wagged the head.€€ƒxYHe hoped in the Lord, let him deliver him: let him save him, seeing he delighteth in him.€€ƒxZFor thou art he that hast drawn me out of the womb: my hope from the breasts of my mother.€€ƒxJI was cast upon thee from the womb. From my mother's womb thou art my God,€€ƒxODepart not from me. For tribulation is very near: for there is none to help me.€€ƒx;Many calves have surrounded me: fat bulls have besieged me.€€ƒxIThey have opened their mouths against me, as a lion ravening and roaring.€€ƒxzI am poured out like water; and all my bones are scattered. My heart is become like wax melting in the midst of my bowels.€€ƒx…My strength is dried up like a potsherd, and my tongue hath cleaved to my jaws: and thou hast brought me down into the dust of death.€€ƒxrFor many dogs have encompassed me: the council of the malignant hath besieged me. They have dug my hands and feet.€€ƒxIThey have numbered all my bones. And they have looked and stared upon me.€€…ƒxUBut thou, O Lord, remove not thy help to a distance from me; look towards my defence.€€ƒxMDeliver, O God, my soul from the sword: my only one from the hand of the dog.€€ƒxMSave me from the lion's mouth; and my lowness from the horns of the unicorns.€€ƒxVI will declare thy name to my brethren: in the midst of the church will I praise thee.€€ƒxIYe that fear the Lord, praise him: all ye the seed of Jacob, glorify him.€€˜ƒx`With thee is my praise in a great church: I will pay my vows in the sight of them that fear him.€€ƒx€The poor shall eat and shall be filled: and they shall praise the Lord that seek him: their hearts shall live for ever and ever.€€ƒxŒAll the ends of the earth shall remember, and shall be converted to the Lord: And all the kindreds of the Gentiles shall adore in his sight.€€ƒxKFor the kingdom is the Lord's; and he shall have dominion over the nations.€€ƒxsAll the fat ones of the earth have eaten and have adored: all they that go down to the earth shall fall before him.€€ƒx;And to him my soul shall live: and my seed shall serve him.€€ƒx There shall be declared to the Lord a generation to come: and the heavens shall shew forth his justice to a people that shall be born, which the Lord hath made.€€ƒ`€€ƒx@A psalm for David. The Lord ruleth me: and I shall want nothing.€€ƒxYHe hath set me in a place of pasture. He hath brought me up, on the water of refreshment:€€ƒx[He hath converted my soul. He hath led me on the paths of justice, for his own name's sake.€€ƒx˜For though I should walk in the midst of the shadow of death, I will fear no evils, for thou art with me. Thy rod and thy staff, they have comforted me.€€ƒxThou hast prepared a table before me against them that afflict me. Thou hast anointed my head with oil; and my chalice which inebreateth me, how goodly is it!€€ƒxxAnd thy mercy will follow me all the days of my life. And that I may dwell in the house of the Lord unto length of days.€€ƒ`€€ƒxOn the first day of the week, a psalm for David. The earth is the Lord's and the fulness thereof: the world, and all they that dwell therein.€€ƒxKFor he hath founded it upon the seas; and hath prepared it upon the rivers.€€ƒxUWho shall ascend into the mountain of the Lord: or who shall stand in his holy place?€€ƒxwThe innocent in hands, and clean of heart, who hath not taken his soul in vain, nor sworn deceitfully to his neighbour.€€ƒxJHe shall receive a blessing from the Lord, and mercy from God his Saviour.€€ƒx]This is the generation of them that seek him, of them that seek the face of the God of Jacob.€€ƒxmLift up your gates, O ye princes, and be ye lifted up, O eternal gates: and the King of Glory shall enter in.€€ƒxXWho is this King of Glory? the Lord who is strong and mighty: the Lord mighty in battle.€€ƒxmLift up your gates, O ye princes, and be ye lifted up, O eternal gates: and the King of Glory shall enter in.€€”ƒ`€€ƒxKUnto the end, a psalm for David. To thee, O Lord, have I lifted up my soul.€€ƒx9In thee, O my God, I put my trust; let me not be ashamed.€€ƒx[Neither let my enemies laugh at me: for none of them that wait on thee shall be confounded.€€ƒxvLet all them be confounded that act unjust things without cause. Shew, O Lord, thy ways to me, and teach me thy paths.€€ƒxnDirect me in thy truth, and teach me; for thou art God my Saviour; and on thee have I waited all the day long.€€ƒxeRemember, O Lord, thy bowels of compassion; and thy mercies that are from the beginning of the world.€€ƒx€The sins of my youth and my ignorances do not remember. According to thy mercy remember thou me: for thy goodness' sake, O Lord.€€ƒxTThe Lord is sweet and righteous: therefore he will give a law to sinners in the way.€€ƒxDHe will guide the mild in judgment: he will teach the meek his ways.€€ƒxgAll the ways of the Lord are mercy and truth, to them that seek after his covenant and his testimonies.€€ƒxFFor thy name's sake, O Lord, thou wilt pardon my sin: for it is great.€€ƒx\Who is the man that feareth the Lord? He hath appointed him a law in the way he hath chosen.€€ƒxIHis soul shall dwell in good things: and his seed shall inherit the land.€€ƒx_The Lord is a firmament to them that fear him: and his covenant shall be made manifest to them.€€ƒxOMy eyes are ever towards the Lord: for he shall pluck my feet out of the snare.€€ƒxALook thou upon me, and have mercy on me; for I am alone and poor.€€ƒxHThe troubles of my heart are multiplied: deliver me from my necessities.€€ƒx;See my abjection and my labour; and forgive me all my sins.€€ƒxUConsider my enemies for they are multiplied, and have hated me with an unjust hatred.€€˜ƒxOThe innocent and the upright have adhered to me: because I have waited on thee.€€ƒx1Deliver Israel, O God, from all his tribulations.€€ƒ`€€ƒx•Unto the end, a psalm for David. Judge me, O Lord, for I have walked in my innocence: and I have put my trust in the Lord, and shall not be weakened.€€ƒx9Prove me, O Lord, and try me; burn my reins and my heart.€€ƒxFFor thy mercy is before my eyes; and I am well pleased with thy truth.€€ƒx`I have not sat with the council of vanity: neither will I go in with the doers of unjust things.€€ƒxOI have hated the assembly of the malignant; and with the wicked I will not sit.€€ƒxLI will wash my hands among the innocent; and will compass thy altar, O Lord:€€ƒxLThat I may hear the voice of thy praise: and tell of all thy wondrous works.€€ƒxVI have loved, O Lord, the beauty of thy house; and the place where thy glory dwelleth.€€ƒxKTake not away my soul, O God, with the wicked: nor my life with bloody men:€€ƒxEIn whose hands are iniquities: their right hand is filled with gifts.€€ƒxNBut as for me, I have walked in my innocence: redeem me, and have mercy on me.€€ƒxPMy foot hath stood in the direct way: in the churches I will bless thee, O Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¥The psalm of David before he was anointed. The Lord is my light and my salvation, whom shall I fear? The Lord is the protector of my life: of whom shall I be afraid?€€ƒx„Whilst the wicked draw near against me, to eat my flesh. My enemies that trouble me, have themselves been weakened, and have fallen.€€ƒxIf armies in camp should stand together against me, my heart shall not fear. If a battle should rise up against me, in this will I be confident.€€ƒxÀOne thing I have asked of the Lord, this will I seek after; that I may dwell in the house of the Lord all the days of my life. That I may see the delight of the Lord, and may visit his temple.€€ƒxyFor he hath hidden me in his tabernacle; in the day of evils, he hath protected me in the secret place of his tabernacle.€€ƒxÔHe hath exalted me upon a rock: and now he hath lifted up my head above my enemies. I have gone round, and have offered up in his tabernacle a sacrifice of jubilation: I will sing, and recite a psalm to the Lord.€€ƒxVHear, O Lord, my voice, with which I have cried to thee: have mercy on me and hear me.€€ƒxZMy heart hath said to thee: My face hath sought thee: thy face, O Lord, will I still seek.€€ƒx—Turn not away thy face from me; decline not in thy wrath from thy servant. Be thou my helper, forsake me not; do not thou despise me, O God my Saviour.€€ƒxHFor my father and my mother have left me: but the Lord hath taken me up.€€ƒxXSet me, O Lord, a law in thy way, and guide me in the right path, because of my enemies.€€ƒx‰Deliver me not over to the will of them that trouble me; for unjust witnesses have risen up against me; and iniquity hath lied to itself.€€—ƒxYExpect the Lord, do manfully, and let thy heart take courage, and wait thou for the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¨A psalm for David himself. Unto thee will I cry, O Lord: O my God, be not thou silent to me: lest if thou be silent to me, I become like them that go down into the pit.€€ƒxlHear, O Lord, the voice of my supplication, when I pray to thee; when I lift up my hands to thy holy temple.€€ƒx Draw me not away together with the wicked; and with the workers of iniquity destroy me not: Who speak peace with their neighbour, but evils are in their hearts.€€ƒx®Give them according to their works, and according to the wickedness of their inventions. According to the works of their hands give thou to them: render to them their reward.€€ƒxBecause they have not understood the works of the Lord, and the operations of his hands: thou shalt destroy them, and shalt not build them up.€€ƒxDBlessed be the Lord, for he hath heard the voice of my supplication.€€ƒx®The Lord is my helper and my protector: in him hath my heart confided, and I have been helped. And my flesh hath flourished again, and with my will I will give praise to him.€€ƒx[The Lord is the strength of his people, and the protector of the salvation of his anointed.€€ƒx[Save, O Lord, thy people, and bless thy inheritance: and rule them and exalt them for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒxˆA psalm for David, at the finishing of the tabernacle. Bring to the Lord, O ye children of God: bring to the Lord the offspring of rams.€€ƒxmBring to the Lord glory and honour: bring to the Lord glory to his name: adore ye the Lord in his holy court.€€ƒxjThe voice of the Lord is upon the waters; the God of majesty hath thundered, The Lord is upon many waters.€€ƒxIThe voice of the Lord is in power; the voice of the Lord in magnificence.€€ƒx[The voice of the Lord breaketh the cedars: yea, the Lord shall break the cedars of Libanus.€€ƒxZAnd shall reduce them to pieces, as a calf of Libanus, and as the beloved son of unicorns.€€ƒx1The voice of the Lord divideth the flame of fire:€€ƒxWThe voice of the Lord shaketh the desert: and the Lord shall shake the desert of Cades.€€ƒx}The voice of the Lord prepareth the stags: and he will discover the thick woods: and in his temple all shall speak his glory.€€ƒxIThe Lord maketh the flood to dwell: and the Lord shall sit king for ever.€€ƒxUThe Lord will give strength to his people: the Lord will bless his people with peace.€€Šƒx:A psalm of a canticle, at the dedication of David's house.€€ƒxdI will extol thee, O Lord, for thou hast upheld me: and hast not made my enemies to rejoice over me.€€ƒx=O Lord my God, I have cried to thee, and thou hast healed me.€€ƒxkThou hast brought forth, O Lord, my soul from hell: thou hast saved me from them that go down into the pit.€€ƒxQSing to the Lord, O ye his saints: and give praise to the memory of his holiness.€€ƒxFor wrath is in his indignation; and life in his good will. In the evening weeping shall have place, and in the morning gladness.€€ƒx3And in my abundance I said: I shall never be moved.€€ƒxuO Lord, in thy favour, thou gavest strength to my beauty. Thou turnedst away thy face from me, and I became troubled.€€ƒxDTo thee, O Lord, will I cry: and I will make supplication to my God.€€ƒxsWhat profit is there in my blood, whilst I go down to corruption? Shall dust confess to thee, or declare thy truth?€€ŒƒxnThou hast turned for me my mourning into joy: thou hast cut my sackcloth, and hast compassed me with gladness:€€ƒxtTo the end that my glory may sing to thee, and I may not regret: O Lord my God, I will give praise to thee for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒx/Unto the end, a psalm for David, in an ecstasy.€€ƒxUIn thee, O Lord, have I hoped, let me never be confounded: deliver me in thy justice.€€ƒxxBow down thy ear to me: make haste to deliver me. Be thou unto me a God, a protector, and a house of refuge, to save me.€€ƒxbFor thou art my strength and my refuge; and for thy name's sake thou wilt lead me, and nourish me.€€ƒx_Thou wilt bring me out of this snare, which they have hidden for me: for thou art my protector.€€ƒxTInto thy hands I commend my spirit: thou hast redeemed me, O Lord, the God of truth.€€ƒxWThou hast hated them that regard vanities, to no purpose. But I have hoped in the Lord:€€ƒxwI will be glad and rejoice in thy mercy. For thou hast regarded my humility, thou hast saved my soul out of distresses.€€ƒxbAnd thou hast not shut me up in the hands of the enemy: thou hast set my feet in a spacious place.€€”ƒx|For my life is wasted with grief: and my years in sighs. My strength is weakened through poverty and my bones are disturbed.€€ƒx“I am become a reproach among all my enemies, and very much to my neighbours; and a fear to my acquaintances. They that saw me without fled from me.€€ƒxUI am forgotten as one dead from the heart. I am become as a vessel that is destroyed.€€ƒx‰For I have heard the blame of many that dwell round about. While they assembled together against me, they consulted to take away my life.€€ƒxABut I have put my trust in thee, O Lord: I said: Thou art my God.€€ƒxeMy lots are in thy hands. Deliver me out of the hands of my enemies; and from them that persecute me.€€ƒx>Make thy face to shine upon thy servant; save me in thy mercy.€€ƒxvLet me not be confounded, O Lord, for I have called upon thee. Let the wicked be ashamed, and be brought down to hell.€€ƒx]Let deceitful lips be made dumb. Which speak iniquity against the just, with pride and abuse.€€ƒx»O how great is the multitude of thy sweetness, O Lord, which thou hast hidden for them that fear thee! Which thou hast wrought for them that hope in thee, in the sight of the sons of men.€€ƒx™Thou shalt hide them in the secret of thy face, from the disturbance of men. Thou shalt protect them in thy tabernacle from the contradiction of tongues.€€ƒxUBlessed be the Lord, for he hath shewn his wonderful mercy to me in a fortified city.€€ƒx‘But I said in the excess of my mind: I am cast away from before thy eyes. Therefore thou hast heard the voice of my prayer, when I cried to thee.€€ƒxuO love the Lord, all ye his saints: for the Lord will require truth, and will repay them abundantly that act proudly.€€ƒxQDo ye manfully, and let your heart be strengthened, all ye that hope in the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxlTo David himself, understanding. Blessed are they whose iniquities are forgiven, and whose sins are covered.€€ƒx`Blessed is the man to whom the Lord hath not imputed sin, and in whose spirit there is no guile.€€ƒxLBecause I was silent my bones grew old; whilst I cried out all the day long.€€ƒxfFor day and night thy hand was heavy upon me: I am turned in my anguish, whilst the thorn is fastened.€€˜HƒxFor this shall every one that is holy pray to thee in a seasonable time. And yet in a flood of many waters, they shall not come nigh unto him.€€ƒxmThou art my refuge from the trouble which hath encompassed me: my joy, deliver me from them that surround me.€€ƒx{I will give thee understanding, and I will instruct thee in this way, in which thou shalt go: I will fix my eyes upon thee.€€ƒxŒDo not become like the horse and the mule, who have no understanding. With bit and bridle bind fast their jaws, who come not near unto thee.€€ƒx[Many are the scourges of the sinner, but mercy shall encompass him that hopeth in the Lord.€€ƒxLBe glad in the Lord, and rejoice, ye just, and glory, all ye right of heart.€€ƒ`€€ƒxOA psalm for David. Rejoice in the Lord, O ye just: praise becometh the upright.€€ƒxbGive praise to the Lord on the harp; sing to him with the psaltery, the instrument of ten strings.€€ƒxASing to him a new canticle, sing well unto him with a loud noise.€€ƒxPFor the word of the Lord is right, and all his works are done with faithfulness.€€ƒxIHe loveth mercy and judgment; the earth is full of the mercy of the Lord.€€ƒxkBy the word of the Lord the heavens were established; and all the power of them by the spirit of his mouth:€€ƒx^Gathering together the waters of the sea, as in a vessel; laying up the depths in storehouses.€€ƒx[Let all the earth fear the Lord, and let all the inhabitants of the world be in awe of him.€€ƒxDFor he spoke and they were made: he commanded and they were created.€€ƒx†The Lord bringeth to nought the counsels of nations; and he rejecteth the devices of people, and casteth away the counsels of princes.€€ƒx\But the counsel of the Lord standeth for ever: the thoughts of his heart to all generations.€€ƒx`Blessed is the nation whose God is the Lord: the people whom he hath chosen for his inheritance.€€ƒxEThe Lord hath looked from heaven: he hath beheld all the sons of men.€€ƒx\From his habitation which he hath prepared, he hath looked upon all that dwell on the earth.€€ƒxTHe who hath made the hearts of every one of them: who understandeth all their works.€€ƒx^The king is not saved by a great army: nor shall the giant be saved by his own great strength.€€ƒxYVain is the horse for safety: neither shall he be saved by the abundance of his strength.€€ƒxZBehold the eyes of the Lord are on them that fear him: and on them that hope in his mercy.€€ƒx;To deliver their souls from death; and feed them in famine.€€ƒxBOur soul waiteth for the Lord: for he is our helper and protector.€€ƒxIFor in him our heart shall rejoice: and in his holy name we have trusted.€€ƒx<Let thy mercy, O Lord, be upon us, as we have hoped in thee.€€ƒ`€€ƒxeFor David, when he changed his countenance before Achimelech, who dismissed him, and he went his way.€€ƒxKI will bless the Lord at all times, his praise shall be always in my mouth.€€ƒxDIn the Lord shall my soul be praised: let the meek hear and rejoice.€€ƒx?O magnify the Lord with me; and let us extol his name together.€€ƒxMI sought the Lord, and he heard me; and he delivered me from all my troubles.€€ƒxJCome ye to him and be enlightened: and your faces shall not be confounded.€€ƒxSThis poor man cried, and the Lord heard him: and saved him out of all his troubles.€€ƒxZThe angel of the Lord shall encamp round about them that fear him: and shall deliver them.€€ƒxOO taste, and see that the Lord is sweet: blessed is the man that hopeth in him.€€ƒxMFear the Lord, all ye his saints: for there is no want to them that fear him.€€ƒxnThe rich have wanted, and have suffered hunger: but they that seek the Lord shall not be deprived of any good.€€ƒxECome, children, hearken to me: I will teach you the fear of the Lord.€€ƒx?Who is the man that desireth life: who liveth to see good days?€€ƒx<Keep thy tongue from evil, and thy lips from speaking guile.€€ƒx@Turn away from evil and do good: seek after peace and pursue it.€€ƒxHThe eyes of the Lord are upon the just: and his ears unto their prayers.€€ƒxwBut the countenance of the Lord is against them that do evil things: to cut off the remembrance of them from the earth.€€ƒxVThe just cried, and the Lord heard them: and delivered them out of all their troubles.€€ƒx_The Lord is nigh unto them that are of a contrite heart: and he will save the humble of spirit.€€ƒxUMany are the afflictions of the just; but out of them all will the Lord deliver them.€€ƒxBThe Lord keepeth all their bones, not one of them shall be broken.€€ƒxRThe death of the wicked is very evil: and they that hate the just shall be guilty.€€ƒx`The Lord will redeem the souls of his servants: and none of them that trust in him shall offend.€€ƒ`€€ƒx`For David himself. Judge thou, O Lord, them that wrong me: overthrow them that fight against me.€€ƒx5Take hold of arms and shield: and rise up to help me.€€ƒxlBring out the sword, and shut up the way against them that persecute me: say to my soul: I am thy salvation.€€ƒx‚Let them be confounded and ashamed that seek after my soul. Let them be turned back and be confounded that devise evil against me.€€ƒxULet them become as dust before the wind: and let the angel of the Lord straiten them.€€ƒxRLet their way become dark and slippery; and let the angel of the Lord pursue them.€€ƒxpFor without cause they have hidden their net for me unto destruction: without cause they have upbraided my soul.€€ƒx‰Let the snare which he knoweth not come upon him: and let the net which he hath hidden catch him: and into that very snare let them fall.€€ƒxOBut my soul shall rejoice in the Lord; and shall be delighted in his salvation.€€ƒx¬All my bones shall say: Lord, who is like to thee? Who deliverest the poor from the hand of them that are stronger than he; the needy and the poor from them that strip him.€€ƒx;Unjust witnesses rising up have asked me things I knew not.€€ƒx=They repaid me evil for good: to the depriving me of my soul.€€ƒx›But as for me, when they were troublesome to me, I was clothed with haircloth. I humbled my soul with fasting; and my prayer shall be turned into my bosom.€€ƒxfAs a neighbour and as an own brother, so did I please: as one mourning and sorrowful so was I humbled.€€ƒxiBut they rejoiced against me, and came together: scourges were gathered together upon me, and I knew not.€€ƒx}They were separated, and repented not: they tempted me, they scoffed at me with scorn: they gnashed upon me with their teeth.€€ƒxeLord, when wilt thou look upon me? rescue thou my soul from their malice: my only one from the lions.€€ƒxTI will give thanks to thee in a great church; I will praise thee in a strong people.€€ƒxaFor they spoke indeed peaceably to me; and speaking in the anger of the earth they devised guile.€€ƒxdAnd they opened their mouth wide against me; they said: Well done, well done, our eyes have seen it.€€ƒxGThou hast seen, O Lord, be not thou silent: O Lord, depart not from me.€€ƒxIArise, and be attentive to my judgment: to my cause, my God, and my Lord.€€ƒxSJudge me, O Lord my God according to thy justice, and let them not rejoice over me.€€ƒxvLet them not say in their hearts: It is well, it is well, to our mind: neither let them say: We have swallowed him up.€€ƒx’Let them blush: and be ashamed together, who rejoice at my evils. Let them be clothed with confusion and shame, who speak great things against me.€€ƒxLet them rejoice and be glad, who are well pleased with my justice, and let them say always: The Lord be magnified, who delights in the peace of his servant.€€ƒxFAnd my tongue shall meditate thy justice, thy praise all the day long.€€ƒ`€€ƒx4Unto the end, for the servant of God, David himself.€€ƒx`The unjust hath said within himself, that he would sin: there is no fear of God before his eyes.€€ƒxVFor in his sight he hath done deceitfully, that his iniquity may be found unto hatred.€€“ƒxsHe hath devised iniquity on his bed, he hath set himself on every way that is not good: but evil he hath not hated.€€ƒxJO Lord, thy mercy is in heaven, and thy truth reacheth even to the clouds.€€ƒxrThy justice is as the mountains of God, thy judgments are a great deep. Men and beasts thou wilt preserve, O Lord:€€ƒxxO how hast thou multiplied thy mercy, O God! But the children of men shall put their trust under the cover of thy wings.€€ƒxuThey shall be inebriated with the plenty of thy house; and thou shalt make them drink of the torrent of thy pleasure.€€ƒxKFor with thee is the fountain of life; and in thy light we shall see light.€€ƒxYExtend thy mercy to them that know thee, and thy justice to them that are right in heart.€€ƒxQLet not the foot of pride come to me, and let not the hand of the sinner move me.€€ƒxQThere the workers of iniquity are fallen, they are cast out, and could not stand.€€ƒ`€€ƒx>Be not emulous of evildoers; nor envy them that work iniquity.€€ƒxWFor they shall shortly wither away as grass, and as the green herbs shall quickly fall.€€ƒx]Trust in the Lord, and do good, and dwell in the land, and thou shalt be fed with its riches.€€ƒxEDelight in the Lord, and he will give thee the requests of thy heart.€€ƒx@Commit thy way to the Lord, and trust in him, and he will do it.€€ƒxRAnd he will bring forth thy justice as the light, and thy judgment as the noonday.€€ƒxsBe subject to the Lord and pray to him. Envy not the man who prospereth in his way; the man who doth unjust things.€€ƒx?Cease from anger, and leave rage; have no emulation to do evil.€€ƒx^For evildoers shall be cut off: but they that wait upon the Lord, they shall inherit the land.€€ƒxMBut the meek shall inherit the land, and shall delight in abundance of peace.€€ƒxMThe sinner shall watch the just man: and shall gnash upon him with his teeth.€€ƒxJBut the Lord shall laugh at him: for he foreseeth that his day shall come.€€ƒx}The wicked have drawn out the sword: they have bent their bow. To cast down the poor and needy, to kill the upright of heart.€€ƒxILet their sword enter into their own hearts, and let their bow be broken.€€ƒxDBetter is a little to the just, than the great riches of the wicked.€€ƒxZFor the arms of the wicked shall be broken in pieces; but the Lord strengtheneth the just.€€ƒxTThe Lord knoweth the days of the undefiled; and their inheritance shall be for ever.€€ƒx^They shall not be confounded in the evil time; and in the days of famine they shall be filled:€€ƒxBecause the wicked shall perish. And the enemies of the Lord, presently after they shall be honoured and exalted, shall come to nothing and vanish like smoke.€€ƒxVThe sinner shall borrow, and not pay again; but the just sheweth mercy and shall give.€€ƒxQFor such as bless him shall inherit the land: but such as curse him shall perish.€€ƒxSWith the Lord shall the steps of a man be directed, and he shall like well his way.€€ƒxTWhen he shall fall he shall not be bruised, for the Lord putteth his hand under him.€€ƒxdI have been young and now am old; and I have not seen the just forsaken, nor his seed seeking bread.€€ƒxRHe sheweth mercy, and lendeth all the day long; and his seed shall be in blessing.€€ˆƒx§For the Lord loveth judgment, and will not forsake his saints: they shall be preserved for ever. The unjust shall be punished, and the seed of the wicked shall perish.€€ƒxJBut the just shall inherit the land, and shall dwell therein for evermore.€€ƒxQThe mouth of the just shall meditate wisdom: and his tongue shall speak judgment.€€ƒxJThe law of his God is in his heart, and his steps shall not be supplanted.€€ƒxBThe wicked watcheth the just man, and seeketh to put him to death,€€ƒxVBut the Lord will not leave him in his hands; nor condemn him when he shall be judged.€€ƒx{Expect the Lord and keep his way: and he will exalt thee to inherit the land: when the sinners shall perish thou shalt see.€€ƒxPI have seen the wicked highly exalted, and lifted up like the cedars of Libanus.€€’ƒxQKeep innocence, and behold justice: for there are remnants for the peaceable man.€€ƒxTBut the unjust shall be destroyed together: the remnants of the wicked shall perish.€€ƒxaBut the salvation of the just is from the Lord, and he is their protector in the time of trouble.€€ƒx„And the Lord will help them and deliver them: and he will rescue them from the wicked, and save them because they have hoped in him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx4A psalm for David, for a remembrance of the sabbath.€€ƒxHRebuke me not, O Lord, in thy indignation; nor chastise me in thy wrath.€€ƒxIFor thy arrows are fastened in me: and thy hand hath been strong upon me.€€ƒxiThere is no health in my flesh, because of thy wrath: there is no peace for my bones, because of my sins.€€ƒxXFor my iniquities are gone over my head: and as a heavy burden are become heavy upon me.€€ƒx@My sores are putrefied and corrupted, because of my foolishness.€€ƒx^I am become miserable, and am bowed down even to the end: I walked sorrowful all the day long.€€ƒxKFor my loins are filled with illusions; and there is no health in my flesh.€€ƒxOI am afflicted and humbled exceedingly: I roared with the groaning of my heart.€€ƒxLLord, all my desire is before thee, and my groaning is not hidden from thee.€€ƒx_My heart is troubled, my strength hath left me, and the light of my eyes itself is not with me.€€ƒxnMy friends and my neighbours have drawn near, and stood against me. And they that were near me stood afar off:€€ƒx…And they that sought my soul used violence. And they that sought evils to me spoke vain things, and studied deceits all the day long.€€ŒƒxOAnd I became as a man that heareth not: and that hath no reproofs in his mouth.€€ƒxDFor in thee, O Lord, have I hoped: thou wilt hear me, O Lord my God.€€ƒxzFor I said: Lest at any time my enemies rejoice over me: and whilst my feet are moved, they speak great things against me.€€ƒxDFor I am ready for scourges: and my sorrow is continually before me.€€ƒx<For I will declare my iniquity: and I will think for my sin.€€ƒx^But my enemies live, and are stronger than I: and they that hate me wrongfully are multiplied.€€ƒxOThey that render evil for good, have detracted me, because I followed goodness.€€ƒx;For sake me not, O Lord my God: do not thou depart from me.€€ƒx5Attend unto my help, O Lord, the God of my salvation.€€ƒ`€€ƒx7Unto the end, for Idithun himself, a canticle of David.€€ƒx…I said: I will take heed to my ways: that I sin not with my tongue. I have set a guard to my mouth, when the sinner stood against me.€€ƒxIMy heart grew hot within me: and in my meditation a fire shall flame out.€€ƒx~I spoke with my tongue: O Lord, make me know my end. And what is the number of my days: that I may know what is wanting to me.€€ƒx‰Behold thou hast made my days measurable. and my substance is as nothing before thee. And indeed all things are vanity: every man living.€€ƒx‹Surely man passeth as an image: yea, and he is disquieted in vain. He storeth up: and he knoweth not for whom he shall gather these things.€€ƒxKAnd now what is my hope? is it not the Lord? and my substance is with thee.€€ƒxQDeliver thou me from all my iniquities: thou hast made me a reproach to the fool.€€ƒxAI was dumb, and I opened not my mouth, because thou hast done it.€€ƒxTRemove thy scourges from me. The strength of thy hand hath made me faint in rebukes:€€ƒx„Thou hast corrected man for iniquity. And thou hast made his soul to waste away like a spider: surely in vain is any man disquieted.€€ƒx—Hear my prayer, O Lord, and my supplication: give ear to my tears. Be no silent: for I am a stranger with thee, and a sojourner as all my fathers were.€€ƒxIO forgive me, that I may be refreshed, before I go hence, and be no more.€€ƒ`€€ƒx(Unto the end, a psalm for David himself.€€‘ƒxAnd he heard my prayers, and brought me out of the pit of misery and the mire of dregs. And he set my feet upon a rock, and directed my steps.€€ƒx|And he put a new canticle into my mouth, a song to our God. Many shall see, and shall fear: and they shall hope in the Lord.€€ƒxvBlessed is the man whose trust is in the name of the Lord; and who hath not had regard to vanities, and lying follies.€€ƒx®Thou hast multiplied thy wonderful works, O Lord my God: and in thy thoughts there is no one like to thee. I have declared and I have spoken they are multiplied above number.€€ƒxˆSacrifice and oblation thou didst not desire; but thou hast pierced ears for me. Burnt offering and sin offering thou didst not require:€€ƒxGThen said I, Behold I come. In the head of the book it is written of me€€ƒx]That I should do thy will: O my God, I have desired it, and thy law in the midst of my heart.€€ƒxhI have declared thy justice in a great church, lo, I will not restrain my lips: O Lord, thou knowest it.€€ƒx›I have not hid thy justice within my heart: I have declared thy truth and thy salvation. I have not concealed thy mercy and thy truth from a great council.€€ƒxeWithhold not thou, O Lord, thy tender mercies from me: thy mercy and thy truth have always upheld me.€€ƒx·For evils without number have surrounded me; my iniquities have overtaken me, and I was not able to see. They are multiplied above the hairs of my head: and my heart hath forsaken me.€€ƒxABe pleased, O Lord, to deliver me. Look down, O Lord, to help me.€€ƒx™Let them be confounded and ashamed together, that seek after my soul to take it away. Let them be turned backward and be ashamed that desire evils to me.€€ƒxPLet them immediately bear their confusion, that say to me: 'Tis well, 'tis well.€€ƒxyLet all that seek thee rejoice and be glad in thee: and let such as love thy salvation say always: The Lord be magnified.€€ƒxtBut I am a beggar and poor: the Lord is careful for me. Thou art my helper and my protector: O my God, be not slack.€€ƒ`€€‡ƒxnBlessed is he that understandeth concerning the needy and the poor: the Lord will deliver him in the evil day.€€ƒx€The Lord preserve him and give him life, and make him blessed upon the earth: and deliver him not up to the will of his enemies.€€ƒxWThe Lord help him on his bed of sorrow: thou hast turned all his couch in his sickness.€€ƒxUI said: O Lord, be thou merciful to me: heal my soul, for I have sinned against thee.€€ƒxOMy enemies have spoken evils against me: when shall he die and his name perish?€€ƒxAnd if he came in to see me, he spoke vain things: his heart gathered together iniquity to itself. He went out and spoke to the same purpose.€€ƒxGAll my enemies whispered together against me: they devised evils to me.€€’ƒx^For even the man of my peace, in whom I trusted, who ate my bread, hath greatly supplanted me.€€ƒxSBut thou, O Lord, have mercy on me, and raise me up again: and I will requite them.€€ƒxbBy this I know, that thou hast had a good will for me: because my enemy shall not rejoice over me.€€ƒxaBut thou hast upheld me by reason of my innocence: and hast established me in thy sight for ever.€€ƒxTBlessed be the Lord the God of Israel from eternity to eternity. So be it. So be it.€€ƒ`€€ƒx1Unto the end, understanding for the sons of Core.€€ƒxWAs the hart panteth after the fountains of water; so my soul panteth after thee, O God.€€ƒxgMy soul hath thirsted after the strong living God; when shall I come and appear before the face of God?€€ƒx[My tears have been my bread day and night, whilst it is said to me daily: Where is thy God?€€ƒxÏThese things I remembered, and poured out my soul in me: for I shall go over into the place of the wonderful tabernacle, even to the house of God: With the voice of joy and praise; the noise of one feasting.€€ƒxWhy art thou sad, O my soul? and why dost thou trouble me? Hope in God, for I will still give praise to him: the salvation of my countenance,€€ƒx‹And my God. My soul is troubled within my self: therefore will I remember thee from the land of Jordan and Hermoniim, from the little hill.€€ƒxkDeep calleth on deep, at the noise of thy flood-gates. All thy heights and thy billows have passed over me.€€ƒx~In the daytime the Lord hath commanded his mercy; and a canticle to him in the night. With me is prayer to the God of my life.€€ƒxyI will say to God: Thou art my support. Why hast thou forgotten me? and why go I mourning, whilst my enemy afflicteth me?€€ƒx}Whilst my bones are broken, my enemies who trouble me have reproached me; Whilst they say to me day by day: Where is thy God?€€ƒx¥Why art thou cast down, O my soul? and why dost thou disquiet me? Hope thou in God, for I will still give praise to him: the salvation of my countenance, and my God.€€˜4ƒxŒA psalm for David. Judge me, O God, and distinguish my cause from the nation that is not holy: deliver me from the unjust and deceitful man.€€ƒxrFor thou art God my strength: why hast thou cast me off? and why do I go sorrowful whilst the enemy afflicteth me?€€ƒxxSent forth thy light and thy truth: they have conducted me, and brought me unto thy holy hill, and into thy tabernacles.€€ƒxHAnd I will go in to the altar of God: to God who giveth joy to my youth.€€ƒxtTo thee, O God my God, I will give praise upon the harp: why art thou sad, O my soul? and why dost thou disquiet me?€€ƒx^Hope in God, for I will still give praise to him: the salvation of my countenance, and my God.€€ƒ`€€ƒx:Unto the end, for the sons of Core, to give understanding.€€ƒx‡We have heard, O God, with our ears: our fathers have declared to us, The work thou hast wrought in their days, and in the days of old.€€ƒxjThy hand destroyed the Gentiles, and thou plantedst them: thou didst afflict the people and cast them out.€€ƒxËFor they got not the possession of the land by their own sword: neither did their own arm save them. But thy right hand and thy arm, and the light of thy countenance: because thou wast pleased with them.€€ƒxHThou art thyself my king and my God, who commandest the saving of Jacob.€€ƒx|Through thee we will push down our enemies with the horn: and through thy name we will despise them that rise up against us.€€ƒx?For I will not trust in my bow: neither shall my sword save me.€€ƒxZBut thou hast saved us from them that afflict us: and hast put them to shame that hate us.€€ƒxUIn God shall we glory all the day long: and in thy name we will give praise for ever.€€ƒxfBut now thou hast cast us off, and put us to shame: and thou , O God, wilt not go out with our armies.€€ƒx`Thou hast made us turn our back to our enemies: and they that hated us plundered for themselves.€€ƒxWThou hast given us up like sheep to be eaten: thou hast scattered us among the nations.€€ƒx[Thou hast sold thy people for no price: and there was no reckoning in the exchange of them.€€ƒxeThou hast made us a reproach to our neighbours, a scoff and derision to them that are round about us.€€ƒxVThou hast made us a byword among the Gentiles: a shaking of the head among the people.€€ƒxUAll the day long my shame is before me: and the confusion of my face hath covered me,€€ƒx`At the voice of him that reproacheth and detracteth me: at the face of the enemy and persecutor.€€ƒxrAll these things have come upon us, yet we have not forgotten thee: and we have not done wickedly in thy covenant.€€ƒxZAnd our heart hath not turned back: neither hast thou turned aside our steps from thy way.€€ƒx]For thou hast humbled us in the place of affliction: and the shadow of death hath covered us.€€ƒxaIf we have forgotten the name of our God, and if we have spread forth our hands to a strange god:€€ƒx¯Shall not God search out these things: for he knoweth the secrets of the heart. Because for thy sake we are killed all the day long: we are counted as sheep for the slaughter.€€ƒxHArise, why sleepest thou, O Lord? arise, and cast us not off to the end.€€ƒxHWhy turnest thou thy face away? and forgettest our want and our trouble?€€ƒxJFor our soul is humbled down to the dust: our belly cleaveth to the earth.€€ƒx9Arise, O Lord, help us and redeem us for thy name's sake.€€ƒ`€€ƒxrUnto the end, for them that shall be changed, for the sons of Core, for understanding. A canticle for the Beloved.€€ƒxzMy heart hath uttered a good word: I speak my works to the king: My tongue is the pen of a scrivener that writeth swiftly.€€ƒxwThou art beautiful above the sons of men: grace is poured abroad in thy lips; therefore hath God blessed thee for ever.€€ƒx2Gird thy sword upon thy thigh, O thou most mighty.€€ƒxªWith thy comeliness and thy beauty set out, proceed prosperously, and reign. Because of truth and meekness and justice: and thy right hand shall conduct thee wonderfully.€€ƒxZThy arrows are sharp: under thee shall people fall, into the hearts of the king's enemies.€€ƒx_Thy throne, O God, is forever and ever: the sceptre of thy kingdom is a sceptre of uprightness.€€ƒxƒThou hast loved justice, and hated iniquity: therefore God, thy God, hath anointed thee with the oil of gladness above thy fellows.€€ƒxUMyrrh and stacte and cassia perfume thy garments, from the ivory houses: out of which€€ƒxˆThe daughters of kings have delighted thee in thy glory. The queen stood on thy right hand, in gilded clothing; surrounded with variety.€€ƒx`Hearken, O daughter, and see, and incline thy ear: and forget thy people and thy father's house.€€ƒxcAnd the king shall greatly desire thy beauty; for he is the Lord thy God, and him they shall adore.€€ƒxhAnd the daughters of Tyre with gifts, yea, all the rich among the people, shall entreat thy countenance.€€ƒxAAll the glory of the king's daughter is within in golden borders,€€ƒx|Clothed round about with varieties. After her shall virgins be brought to the king: her neighbours shall be brought to thee.€€ƒxeThey shall be brought with gladness and rejoicing: they shall be brought into the temple of the king.€€ƒx_Instead of thy fathers, sons are born to thee: thou shalt make them princes over all the earth.€€ƒx}They shall remember thy name throughout all generations. Therefore shall people praise thee for ever; yea, for ever and ever.€€‘ƒx3Unto the end, for the sons of Core, for the hidden.€€ƒxZOur God is our refuge and strength: a helper in troubles, which have found us exceedingly.€€ƒx{Therefore we will not fear, when the earth shall be troubled; and the mountains shall be removed into the heart of the sea.€€ƒxUTheir waters roared and were troubled: the mountains were troubled with his strength.€€ƒxhThe stream of the river maketh the city of God joyful: the most High hath sanctified his own tabernacle.€€ƒxZGod is in the midst thereof, it shall not be moved: God will help it in the morning early.€€ƒx^Nations were troubled, and kingdoms were bowed down: he uttered his voice, the earth trembled.€€ƒxAThe Lord of armies is with us: the God of Jacob is our protector.€€ƒxOCome and behold ye the works of the Lord: what wonders he hath done upon earth,€€ƒxMaking wars to cease even to the end of the earth. He shall destroy the bow, and break the weapons: and the shield he shall burn in the fire.€€ƒxhBe still and see that I am God; I will be exalted among the nations, and I will be exalted in the earth.€€ƒxAThe Lord of armies is with us: the God of Jacob is our protector.€€ƒ`€€ƒx#Unto the end, for the sons of Core.€€ƒxHO clap your hands, all ye nations: shout unto God with the voice of joy,€€ƒx@For the Lord is high, terrible: a great king over all the earth.€€ƒxDHe hath subdued the people under us; and the nations under our feet.€€ƒxEGod is ascended with jubilee, and the Lord with the sound of trumpet.€€ƒxDSing praises to our God, sing ye: sing praises to our king, sing ye.€€ƒx5For God is the king of all the earth: sing ye wisely.€€ƒxAGod shall reign over the nations: God sitteth on his holy throne.€€ƒxƒThe princes of the people are gathered together, with the God of Abraham: for the strong gods of the earth are exceedingly exalted.€€ƒ`€€ƒxKA psalm of a canticle, for the sons of Core, on the second day of the week.€€ƒx^Great is the Lord, and exceedingly to be praised in the city of our God, in his holy mountain.€€ƒxmWith the joy of the whole earth is mount Sion founded, on the sides of the north, the city of the great king.€€ƒx<In her houses shall God be known, when he shall protect her.€€ƒxOFor behold the kings of the earth assembled themselves: they gathered together.€€ƒxDSo they saw, and they wondered, they were troubled, they were moved:€€ƒxFTrembling took hold of them. There were pains as of a woman in labour.€€ƒxEWith a vehement wind thou shalt break in pieces the ships of Tharsis.€€ƒxzAs we have heard, so have we seen, in the city of the Lord of hosts, in the city of our God: God hath founded it for ever.€€ƒx>We have received thy mercy, O God, in the midst of thy temple.€€ƒx\Let mount Sion rejoice, and the daughters of Juda be glad; because of thy judgments, O Lord.€€ƒx8Surround Sion, and encompass her: tell ye in her towers.€€ƒxhSet your hearts on her strength; and distribute her houses, that ye may relate it in another generation.€€ƒx]For this is God, our God unto eternity, and for ever and ever: he shall rule us for evermore.€€ƒ`€€ƒx+Unto the end, a psalm for the sons of Core.€€ƒxMHear these things, all ye nations: give ear, all ye inhabitants of the world.€€ƒxMAll you that are earthborn, and you sons of men: both rich and poor together.€€ƒxJMy mouth shall speak wisdom: and the meditation of my heart understanding.€€ƒxOI will incline my ear to a parable; I will open my proposition on the psaltery.€€ƒxMWhy shall I fear in the evil day? the iniquity of my heel shall encompass me.€€ƒxRThey that trust in their own strength, and glory in the multitude of their riches,€€ƒxQNo brother can redeem, nor shall man redeem: he shall not give to God his ransom,€€ƒxGNor the price of the redemption of his soul: and shall labour for ever,€€ƒx"And shall still live unto the end.€€…ƒx‹And their sepulchres shall be their houses for ever. Their dwelling places to all generations: they have called their lands by their names.€€ƒxqAnd man when he was in honour did not understand; he is compared to senseless beasts, and is become like to them.€€ƒxaThis way of theirs is a stumblingblock to them: and afterwards they shall delight in their mouth.€€ƒx­They are laid in hell like sheep: death shall feed upon them. And the just shall have dominion over them in the morning; and their help shall decay in hell from their glory.€€ƒxLBut God will redeem my soul from the hand of hell, when he shall receive me.€€—ƒxWFor when he shall die he shall take nothing away; nor shall his glory descend with him.€€ƒxeFor in his lifetime his soul will be blessed: and he will praise thee when thou shalt do well to him.€€ƒxOHe shall go in to the generations of his fathers: and he shall never see light.€€ƒxoMan when he was in honour did not understand: he hath been compared to senseless beasts, and made like to them.€€ƒ`€€ƒxA psalm for Asaph. The God of gods, the Lord hath spoken: and he hath called the earth. From the rising of the sun, to the going down thereof:€€ƒx)Out of Sion the loveliness of his beauty.€€ƒx—God shall come manifestly: our God shall come, and shall not keep silence. A fire shall burn before him: and a mighty tempest shall be round about him.€€ƒxDHe shall call heaven from above, and the earth, to judge his people.€€ƒxMGather ye together his saints to him: who set his covenant before sacrifices.€€ƒx<And the heavens shall declare his justice: for God is judge.€€ƒx]Hear, O my people, and I will speak: O Israel, and I will testify to thee: I am God, thy God.€€ƒx[I will not reprove thee for thy sacrifices: and thy burnt offerings are always in my sight.€€ƒxHI will not take calves out of thy house: nor he goats out of thy flocks.€€ƒxPFor all the beasts of the woods are mine: the cattle on the hills, and the oxen.€€ƒxHI know all the fowls of the air: and with me is the beauty of the field.€€ƒx]If I should be hungry, I would not tell thee: for the world is mine, and the fulness thereof.€€ƒxGShall I eat the flesh of bullocks? or shall I drink the blood of goats?€€ƒxHOffer to God the sacrifice of praise: and pay thy vows to the most High.€€ƒxWAnd call upon me in the day of trouble: I will deliver thee, and thou shalt glorify me.€€ƒxfBut to the sinner God hath said: Why dost thou declare my justices, and take my covenant in thy mouth?€€ƒxFSeeing thou hast hated discipline: and hast cast my words behind thee.€€ƒxaIf thou didst see a thief thou didst run with him: and with adulterers thou hast been a partaker.€€‹ƒx_Sitting thou didst speak against thy brother, and didst lay a scandal against thy mother's son:€€ƒx˜These things hast thou done, and I was silent. Thou thoughtest unjustly that I should be like to thee: but I will reprove thee, and set before thy face.€€ƒxhUnderstand these things, you that forget God; lest he snatch you away, and there be none to deliver you.€€ƒxmThe sacrifice of praise shall glorify me: and there is the way by which I will shew him the salvation of God.€€ƒ`€€ƒxUnto the end, a psalm of David,€€ƒxHWhen Nathan the prophet came to him, after he had sinned with Bethsabee.€€ƒxHave mercy on me, O God, according to thy great mercy. And according to the multitude of thy tender mercies blot out my iniquity.€€ƒx>Wash me yet more from my iniquity, and cleanse me from my sin.€€ƒx7For I know my iniquity, and my sin is always before me.€€ƒxTo thee only have I sinned, and have done evil before thee: that thou mayst be justified in thy words, and mayst overcome when thou art judged.€€ƒxnFor behold thou hast loved truth: the uncertain and hidden things of thy wisdom thou hast made manifest to me.€€ƒxvThou shalt sprinkle me with hyssop, and I shall be cleansed: thou shalt wash me, and I shall be made whiter than snow.€€ƒxcTo my hearing thou shalt give joy and gladness: and the bones that have been humbled shall rejoice.€€ƒx@Turn away thy face from my sins, and blot out all my iniquities.€€ƒxMCreate a clean heart in me, O God: and renew a right spirit within my bowels.€€ƒxECast me not away from thy face; and take not thy holy spirit from me.€€ƒxRRestore unto me the joy of thy salvation, and strengthen me with a perfect spirit.€€ƒxLI will teach the unjust thy ways: and the wicked shall be converted to thee.€€ƒx^Deliver me from blood, O God, thou God of my salvation: and my tongue shall extol thy justice.€€ƒxFO Lord, thou wilt open my lips: and my mouth shall declare thy praise.€€ƒxsFor if thou hadst desired sacrifice, I would indeed have given it: with burnt offerings thou wilt not be delighted.€€ƒxfA sacrifice to God is an afflicted spirit: a contrite and humbled heart, O God, thou wilt not despise.€€ƒxaDeal favourably, O Lord, in thy good will with Sion; that the walls of Jerusalem may be built up.€€Œƒ`€€ƒx&Unto the end, understanding for David,€€ƒxPWhen Doeg the Edomite came and told Saul: David went to the house of Achimelech.€€ƒx@Why dost thou glory in malice, thou that art mighty in iniquity?€€ƒx_All the day long thy tongue hath devised injustice: as a sharp razor, thou hast wrought deceit.€€ƒx[Thou hast loved malice more than goodness: and iniquity rather than to speak righteousness.€€ƒx:Thou hast loved all the words of ruin, O deceitful tongue.€€ƒx–Therefore will God destroy thee for ever: he will pluck thee out, and remove thee from thy dwelling place: and thy root out of the land of the living.€€ƒx=The just shall see and fear, and shall laugh at him, and say:€€ƒxuBehold the man that made not God his helper: But trusted in the abundance of his riches: and prevailed in his vanity.€€ƒxtBut I, as a fruitful olive tree in the house of God, have hoped in the mercy of God for ever, yea for ever and ever.€€ƒxI will praise thee for ever, because thou hast done it: and I will wait on thy name, for it is good in the sight of thy saints.€€‰ƒx`Unto the end, for Maeleth, understandings to David. The fool said in his heart: There is no God.€€ƒxVThey are corrupted, and become abominable in iniquities: there is none that doth good.€€ƒxrGod looked down from heaven on the children of men: to see if there were any that did understand, or did seek God.€€ƒxeAll have gone aside, they are become unprofitable together, there is none that doth good, no not one.€€ƒxSShall not all the workers of iniquity know, who eat up my people as they eat bread?€€ƒxÏThey have not called upon God: there have they trembled for fear, where there was no fear. For God hath scattered the bones of them that please men: they have been confounded, because God hath despised them.€€ƒx˜Who will give out of Sion the salvation of Israel? when God shall bring back the captivity of his people, Jacob shall rejoice, and Israel shall be glad.€€ƒ`€€ƒx1Unto the end, in verses, understanding for David.€€‰ƒx:Save me, O God, by thy name, and judge me in thy strength.€€ƒx9O God, hear my prayer: give ear to the words of my mouth.€€ƒx~For strangers have risen up against me; and the mighty have sought after my soul: and they have not set God before their eyes.€€ƒxFFor behold God is my helper: and the Lord is the protector of my soul.€€ƒxCTurn back the evils upon my enemies; and cut them off in thy truth.€€ƒx^I will freely sacrifice to thee, and will give praise, O God, to thy name: because it is good:€€ƒx[For thou hast delivered me out of all trouble: and my eye hath looked down upon my enemies.€€ƒ`€€ƒx1Unto the end, in verses, understanding for David.€€…ƒxMBe attentive to me and hear me. I am grieved in my exercise; and am troubled,€€ƒx‘At the voice of the enemy, and at the tribulation of the sinner. For they have cast iniquities upon me: and in wrath they were troublesome to me.€€ƒxHMy heart is troubled within me: and the fear of death is fallen upon me.€€ƒxBFear and trembling are come upon me: and darkness hath covered me.€€ƒxNAnd I said: Who will give me wings like a dove, and I will fly and be at rest?€€ˆƒxNI waited for him that hath saved me from pusillanimity of spirit, and a storm.€€ƒxdCast down, O Lord, and divide their tongues; for I have seen iniquity and contradiction in the city.€€ƒx]Day and night shall iniquity surround it upon its walls: and in the midst thereof are labour,€€ƒxGAnd injustice. And usury and deceit have not departed from its streets.€€ƒx¬For if my enemy had reviled me, I would verily have borne with it. And if he that hated me had spoken great things against me, I would perhaps have hidden my self from him.€€ƒx6But thou a man of one mind, my guide, and my familiar,€€ƒxWWho didst take sweetmeats together with me: in the house of God we walked with consent.€€ƒxLet death come upon them, and let them go down alive into hell. For there is wickedness in their dwellings: in the midst of them.€€˜ƒxVEvening and morning, and at noon I will speak and declare: and he shall hear my voice.€€ƒxbHe shall redeem my soul in peace from them that draw near to me: for among many they were with me.€€ƒxrGod shall hear, and the Eternal shall humble them. For there is no change with them, and they have not feared God:€€ƒxJHe hath stretched forth his hand to repay. They have defiled his covenant,€€ƒx‰They are divided by the wrath of his countenance, and his heart hath drawn near. His words are smoother than oil, and the same are darts.€€ƒxgCast thy care upon the Lord, and he shall sustain thee: he shall not suffer the just to waver for ever.€€ƒx¢But thou, O God, shalt bring them down into the pit of destruction. Bloody and deceitful men shall not live out half their days; but I will trust in thee, O Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¨Unto the end, for a people that is removed at a distance from the sanctuary: for David, for an inscription of a title (or pillar) when the Philistines held him in Geth.€€ƒxwHave mercy on me, O God, for man hath trodden me under foot; all the day long he hath afflicted me fighting against me.€€ƒx[My enemies have trodden on me all the day long; for they are many that make war against me.€€ƒxBFrom the height of the day I shall fear: but I will trust in thee.€€ƒxhIn God I will praise my words, in God I have put my trust: I will not fear what flesh can do against me.€€ƒxVAll the day long they detested my words: all their thoughts were against me unto evil.€€ƒx^They will dwell and hide themselves: they will watch my heel. As they have waited for my soul,€€ƒx\For nothing shalt thou save them: in thy anger thou shalt break the people in pieces. O God,€€ƒx]I have declared to thee my life: thou hast set me tears in thy sight, As also in thy promise.€€ƒxoThen shall my enemies be turned back. In what day soever I shall call upon thee, behold I know thou art my God.€€ƒx€In God will I praise the word, in the Lord will I praise his speech. In God have I hoped, I will not fear what man can do to me.€€ƒxBIn me, O God, are vows to thee, which I will pay, praises to thee:€€ƒxˆBecause thou hast delivered my soul from death, my feet from falling: that I may please in the sight of God, in the light of the living.€€ƒ`€€ƒxjUnto the end, destroy not, for David, for an inscription of a title, when he fled from Saul into the cave.€€ƒxHave mercy on me, O God, have mercy on me: for my soul trusteth in thee. And in the shadow of thy wings will I hope, until iniquity pass away.€€ƒxAI will cry to God the most high; to God who hath done good to me.€€ƒxHe hath sent from heaven and delivered me: he hath made them a reproach that trod upon me. God hath sent his mercy and his truth,€€ƒx§And he hath delivered my soul from the midst of the young lions. I slept troubled. The sons of men, whose teeth are weapons and arrows, and their tongue a sharp sword.€€ƒxMBe thou exalted, O God, above the heavens, and thy glory above all the earth.€€–ƒxOMy heart is ready, O God, my heart is ready: I will sing, and rehearse a psalm.€€ƒx?Arise, O my glory, arise psaltery and harp: I will arise early.€€ƒxdI will give praise to thee, O Lord, among the people: I will sing a psalm to thee among the nations.€€ƒxNFor thy mercy is magnified even to the heavens: and thy truth unto the clouds.€€ƒxMBe thou exalted, O God, above the heavens: and thy glory above all the earth.€€ƒ`€€ƒxDUnto the end, destroy not, for David, for an inscription of a title.€€ƒxEIf in very deed ye speak justice: judge right things, ye sons of men.€€ƒxMFor in your heart you work iniquity: your hands forge injustice in the earth.€€ƒxkThe wicked are alienated from the womb; they have gone astray from the womb: they have spoken false things.€€ƒxbTheir madness is according to the likeness of a serpent: like the deaf asp that stoppeth her ears:€€ƒxVWhich will not hear the voice of the charmers; nor of the wizard that charmeth wisely.€€ƒxeGod shall break in pieces their teeth in their mouth: the Lord shall break the grinders of the lions.€€ƒx`They shall come to nothing, like water running down; he hath bent his bow till they be weakened.€€ƒxiLike wax that melteth they shall be taken away: fire hath fallen on them, and they shall not see the sun.€€ƒxWBefore your thorns could know the brier; he swalloweth them up, as alive, in his wrath.€€ƒxiThe just shall rejoice when he shall see the revenge: he shall wash his hands in the blood of the sinner.€€ƒxnAnd man shall say: If indeed there be fruit to the just: there is indeed a God that judgeth them on the earth.€€ƒ`€€ƒxuUnto the end, destroy not, for David for an inscription of a title, when Saul sent and watched his house to kill him.€€ƒxVDeliver me from my enemies, O my God; and defend me from them that rise up against me.€€ƒxEDeliver me from them that work iniquity, and save me from bloody men.€€˜#ƒxbNeither is it my iniquity, nor my sin, O Lord: without iniquity have I run, and directed my steps.€€ƒx«Rise up thou to meet me, and behold: even thou, O Lord, the God of hosts, the God of Israel. Attend to visit all the nations: have no mercy on all them that work iniquity.€€ƒxcThey shall return at evening, and shall suffer hunger like dogs: and shall go round about the city.€€ƒxiBehold they shall speak with their mouth, and a sword is in their lips: for who, say they, hath heard us?€€ƒxSBut thou, O Lord, shalt laugh at them: thou shalt bring all the nations to nothing.€€ƒx;I will keep my strength to thee: for thou art my protector:€€ƒx#My God, his mercy shall prevent me.€€ƒxGod shall let me see over my enemies: slay them not, lest at any time my people forget. Scatter them by thy power; and bring them down, O Lord, my protector:€€ƒx–For the sin of their mouth, and the word of their lips: and let them be taken in their pride. And for their cursing and lying they shall be talked of,€€ƒx¤When they are consumed: when they are consumed by thy wrath, and they shall be no more. And they shall know that God will rule Jacob, and all the ends of the earth.€€ƒxbThey shall return at evening and shall suffer hunger like dogs: and shall go round about the city.€€ƒxNThey shall be scattered abroad to eat, and shall murmur if they be not filled.€€ƒxBut I will sing thy strength: and will extol thy mercy in the morning. For thou art become my support, and my refuge, in the day of my trouble.€€ƒxRUnto thee, O my helper, will I sing, for thou art God my defence: my God my mercy.€€ƒ`€€ƒxmUnto the end, for them that shall be changed, for the inscription of a title, to David himself, for doctrine,€€ƒx‰When he set fire to Mesopotamia of Syria and Sobal: and Joab returned and slew of Edom, in the vale of the saltpits, twelve thousand men.€€ƒxdO God, thou hast cast us off, and hast destroyed us; thou hast been angry, and hast had mercy on us.€€ƒxgThou hast moved the earth, and hast troubled it: heal thou the breaches thereof, for it has been moved.€€ƒxSThou hast shewn thy people hard things; thou hast made us drink the wine of sorrow.€€ƒx|Thou hast given a warning to them that fear thee: that they may flee from before the bow: That thy beloved may be delivered.€€ƒx)Save me with thy right hand, and hear me.€€ƒxwGod hath spoken in his holy place: I will rejoice, and I will divide Sichem; and will mete out the vale of tabernacles.€€ƒx^Galaad is mine, and Manasses is mine: and Ephraim is the strength of my head. Juda is my king:€€ƒxhMoab is the pot of my hope. Into Edom will I stretch out my shoe: to me the foreigners are made subject.€€ƒxCWho will bring me into the strong city? who will lead me into Edom?€€ƒx]Wilt not thou, O God, who hast cast us off? and wilt not thou, O God, go out with our armies?€€ƒx<Give us help from trouble: for vain is the salvation of man.€€ƒxUThrough God we shall do mightily: and he shall bring to nothing them that afflict us.€€ƒ`€€ƒx"Unto the end, in hymns, for David.€€ƒx8Hear, O God, my supplication: be attentive to my prayer.€€ƒx†To thee have I cried from the ends of the earth: when my heart was in anguish, thou hast exalted me on a rock. Thou hast conducted me;€€ƒxNFor thou hast been my hope; a tower of strength against the face of the enemy.€€ƒx]In thy tabernacle I shall dwell for ever: I shall be protected under the covert of thy wings.€€˜-ƒxXThou wilt add days to the days of the king: his years even to generation and generation.€€ƒxNHe abideth for ever in the sight of God: his mercy and truth who shall search?€€ƒx]So will I sing a psalm to thy name for ever and ever: that I may pay my vows from day to day.€€ƒ`€€ƒx,Unto the end, for Idithun, a psalm of David.€€ƒxBShall not my soul be subject to God? for from him is my salvation.€€ƒxNFor he is my God and my saviour: he is my protector, I shall be moved no more.€€ƒxvHow long do you rush in upon a man? you all kill, as if you were thrusting down a leaning wall, and a tottering fence.€€ƒxyBut they have thought to cast away my price; I ran in thirst: they blessed with their mouth, but cursed with their heart.€€ƒxDBut be thou, O my soul, subject to God: for from him is my patience.€€ƒxGFor he is my God and my saviour: he is my helper, I shall not be moved.€€ƒxUIn God is my salvation and my glory: he is the God of my help, and my hope is in God.€€ƒxiTrust in him, all ye congregation of people: pour out your hearts before him. God is our helper for ever.€€ƒxrBut vain are the sons of men, the sons of men are liars in the balances: that by vanity they may together deceive.€€ƒx_Trust not in iniquity, and covet not robberies: if riches abound, set not your heart upon them.€€ƒxQGod hath spoken once, these two things have I heard, that power belongeth to God,€€ƒxTAnd mercy to thee, O Lord; for thou wilt render to every man according to his works.€€ƒ`€€ƒx4A psalm of David while he was in the desert of Edom.€€ƒxvO God, my God, to thee do I watch at break of day. For thee my soul hath thirsted; for thee my flesh, O how many ways!€€ƒx‡In a desert land, and where there is no way, and no water: so in the sanctuary have I come before thee, to see thy power and thy glory.€€ƒx=For thy mercy is better than lives: thee my lips will praise.€€ƒxQThus will I bless thee all my life long: and in thy name I will lift up my hands.€€ƒxbLet my soul be filled as with marrow and fatness: and my mouth shall praise thee with joyful lips.€€ƒxNIf I have remembered thee upon my bed, I will meditate on thee in the morning:€€ƒxRBecause thou hast been my helper. And I will rejoice under the cover of thy wings:€€ƒxBMy soul hath stuck close to thee: thy right hand hath received me.€€ƒxVBut they have sought my soul in vain, they shall go into the lower parts of the earth:€€ƒxYThey shall be delivered into the hands of the sword, they shall be the portions of foxes.€€ƒxBut the king shall rejoice in God, all they shall be praised that swear by him: because the mouth is stopped of them that speak wicked things.€€ƒ`€€ƒx Unto the end, a psalm for David.€€ƒxdHear O God, my prayer, when I make supplication to thee: deliver my soul from the fear of the enemy.€€ƒxiThou hast protected me from the assembly of the malignant; from the multitude of the workers of iniquity.€€ƒxZFor they have whetted their tongues like a sword; they have bent their bow a bitter thing,€€ƒx!To shoot in secret the undefiled.€€ƒxThey will shoot at him on a sudden, and will not fear: they are resolute in wickedness. They have talked of hiding snares; they have said: Who shall see them?€€ƒxfThey have searched after iniquities: they have failed in their search. Man shall come to a deep heart:€€ƒxBAnd God shall be exalted. The arrows of children are their wounds:€€ƒxNAnd their tongues against them are made weak. All that saw them were troubled;€€ƒxXAnd every man was afraid. And they declared the works of God, and understood his doings.€€ƒxiThe just shall rejoice in the Lord, and shall hope in him: and all the upright in heart shall be praised.€€ƒ`€€ƒx~To the end, a psalm of David. The canticle of Jeremias and Ezechiel to the people of the captivity, when they began to go out.€€ƒxSA hymn, O God, becometh thee in Sion: and a vow shall be paid to thee in Jerusalem.€€˜0ƒxXThe words of the wicked have prevailed over us: and thou wilt pardon our transgressions.€€ƒxBlessed is he whom thou hast chosen and taken to thee: he shall dwell in thy courts. We shall be filled with the good things of thy house; holy is thy temple,€€ƒxyWonderful in justice. Hear us, O God our saviour, who art the hope of all the ends of the earth, and in the sea afar off.€€ƒxJThou who preparest the mountains by thy strength, being girded with power:€€ƒx[Who troublest the depth of the sea, the noise of its waves. The Gentiles shall be troubled,€€ƒx˜And they that dwell in the uttermost borders shall be afraid at thy signs: thou shalt make the outgoings of the morning and of the evening to be joyful.€€ƒx¿Thou hast visited the earth, and hast plentifully watered it; thou hast many ways enriched it. The river of God is filled with water, thou hast prepared their food: for so is its preparation.€€ƒxlFill up plentifully the streams thereof, multiply its fruits; it shall spring up and rejoice in its showers.€€ƒxcThou shalt bless the crown of the year of thy goodness: and thy fields shall be filled with plenty.€€ƒxdThe beautiful places of the wilderness shall grow fat: and the hills shall be girded about with joy,€€ƒxvThe rams of the flock are clothed, and the vales shall abound with corn: they shall shout, yea they shall sing a hymn.€€ƒ`€€ƒx^Unto the end, a canticle of a psalm of the resurrection. Shout with joy to God, all the earth,€€ƒx6Sing ye a psalm to his name; give glory to his praise.€€ƒxqSay unto God, How terrible are thy works, O Lord! in the multitude of thy strength thy enemies shall lie to thee.€€ƒxPLet all the earth adore thee, and sing to thee: let it sing a psalm to thy name.€€ƒxTCome and see the works of God; who is terrible in his counsels over the sons of men.€€ƒxgWho turneth the sea into dry land, in the river they shall pass on foot: there shall we rejoice in him.€€ƒxvWho by his power ruleth for ever: his eyes behold the nations; let not them that provoke him be exalted in themselves.€€ƒxKO bless our God, ye Gentiles: and make the voice of his praise to be heard.€€ƒxHWho hath set my soul to live: and hath not suffered my feet to be moved:€€ƒxPFor thou, O God, hast proved us: thou hast tried us by fire, as silver is tried.€€ƒxHThou hast brought us into a net, thou hast laid afflictions on our back:€€ƒxyThou hast set men over our heads. We have passed through fire and water, and thou hast brought us out into a refreshment.€€ƒxGI will go into thy house with burnt offerings: I will pay thee my vows,€€ƒxLWhich my lips have uttered, And my mouth hath spoken, when I was in trouble.€€ƒxzI will offer up to thee holocausts full of marrow, with burnt offerings of rams: I will offer to thee bullocks with goats.€€ƒxdCome and hear, all ye that fear God, and I will tell you what great things he hath done for my soul.€€ƒx@I cried to him with my mouth: and I extolled him with my tongue.€€ƒxDIf I have looked at iniquity in my heart, the Lord will not hear me.€€ƒxOTherefore hath God heard me, and hath attended to the voice of my supplication.€€ƒxJBlessed be God, who hath not turned away my prayer, nor his mercy from me.€€ƒ`€€ƒx8Unto the end, in hymns, a psalm of a canticle for David.€€ƒx€May God have mercy on us, and bless us: may he cause the light of his countenance to shine upon us, and may he have mercy on us.€€ƒxBThat we may know thy way upon earth: thy salvation in all nations.€€ƒxFLet people confess to thee, O God: let all people give praise to thee.€€ƒxtLet the nations be glad and rejoice: for thou judgest the people with justice, and directest the nations upon earth.€€ƒxOLet the people, O God, confess to thee: let all the people give praise to thee:€€ƒx<The earth hath yielded her fruit. May God, our God bless us,€€ƒx9May God bless us: and all the ends of the earth fear him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx6Unto the end, a psalm of a canticle for David himself.€€ƒxfLet God arise, and let his enemies be scattered: and let them that hate him flee from before his face.€€ƒx}As smoke vanisheth, so let them vanish away: as wax melteth before the fire, so let the wicked perish at the presence of God.€€ƒxOAnd let the just feast, and rejoice before God: and be delighted with gladness.€€ƒx¸Sing ye to God, sing a psalm to his name, make a way for him who ascendeth upon the west: the Lord is his name. Rejoice ye before him: but the wicked shall be troubled at his presence,€€ƒxMWho is the father of orphans, and the judge of widows. God in his holy place:€€˜+ƒxdO God, when thou didst go forth in the sight of thy people, when thou didst pass through the desert:€€ƒxvThe earth was moved, and the heavens dropped at the presence of the God of Sina, at the presence of the God of Israel.€€ƒxpThou shalt set aside for thy inheritance a free rain, O God: and it was weakened, but thou hast made it perfect.€€ƒxXIn it shall thy animals dwell; in thy sweetness, O God, thou hast provided for the poor.€€ƒxOThe Lord shall give the word to them that preach good tidings with great power.€€ƒxfThe king of powers is of the beloved, of the beloved; and the beauty of the house shall divide spoils.€€ƒxšIf you sleep among the midst of lots, you shall be as the wings of a dove covered with silver, and the hinder parts of her back with the paleness of gold.€€ƒx^When he that is in heaven appointeth kings over her, they shall be whited with snow in Selmon.€€ƒxJThe mountain of God is a fat mountain. A curdled mountain, a fat mountain.€€ƒxWhy suspect, ye curdled mountains? A mountain in which God is well pleased to dwell: for there the Lord shall dwell unto the end.€€ƒxƒThe chariot of God is attended by ten thousands; thousands of them that rejoice: the Lord is among them in Sina, in the holy place.€€ƒx£Thou hast ascended on high, thou hast led captivity captive; thou hast received gifts in men. Yea for those also that do not believe, the dwelling of the Lord God.€€ƒx`Blessed be the Lord day by day: the God of our salvation will make our journey prosperous to us.€€ƒxXOur God is the God of salvation: and of the Lord, of the Lord are the issues from death.€€ƒxaBut God shall break the heads of his enemies: the hairy crown of them that walk on in their sins.€€ƒxWThe Lord said: I will turn them from Basan, I will turn them into the depth of the sea:€€ƒxeThat thy foot may be dipped in the blood of thy enemies; the tongue of thy dogs be red with the same.€€ƒx[They have seen thy goings, O God, the goings of my God: of my king who is in his sanctuary.€€ƒx[Princes went before joined with singers, in the midst of young damsels playing on timbrels.€€ƒxDIn the churches bless ye God the Lord, from the fountains of Israel.€€ƒx‡There is Benjamin a youth, in ecstasy of mind. The princes of Juda are their leaders: the princes of Zabulon, the princes of Nephthali.€€ƒxICommand thy strength, O God confirm, O God, what thou hast wrought in us.€€ƒxAFrom thy temple in Jerusalem, kings shall offer presents to thee.€€ƒx¾Rebuke the wild beasts of the reeds, the congregation of bulls with the kine of the people; who seek to exclude them who are tried with silver. Scatter thou the nations that delight in wars:€€ƒxVAmbassadors shall come out of Egypt: Ethiopia shall soon stretch out her hands to God.€€ƒxKSing to God, ye kingdoms of the earth: sing ye to the Lord: Sing ye to God,€€ƒxkWho mounteth above the heaven of heavens, to the east. Behold he will give to his voice the voice of power:€€ƒxRGive ye glory to God for Israel, his magnificence, and his power is in the clouds.€€ƒxwGod is wonderful in his saints: the God of Israel is he who will give power and strength to his people. Blessed be God.€€ƒ`€€ƒx8Unto the end, for them that shall be changed; for David.€€ƒx=Save me, O God: for the waters are come in even unto my soul.€€ƒx‹I stick fast in the mire of the deep and there is no sure standing. I am come into the depth of the sea, and a tempest hath overwhelmed me.€€ƒxeI have laboured with crying; my jaws are become hoarse, my eyes have failed, whilst I hope in my God.€€ƒx´They are multiplied above the hairs of my head, who hate me without cause. My enemies are grown strong who have wrongfully persecuted me: then did I pay that which I took not away.€€ƒxMO God, thou knowest my foolishness; and my offences are not hidden from thee:€€ƒx—Let not them be ashamed for me, who look for thee, O Lord, the Lord of hosts. Let them not be confounded on my account, who seek thee, O God of Israel.€€ƒxGBecause for thy sake I have borne reproach; shame hath covered my face.€€ƒxMI am become a stranger to my brethren, and an alien to the sons of my mother.€€ƒxoFor the zeal of thy house hath eaten me up: and the reproaches of them that reproached thee are fallen upon me.€€ƒxCAnd I covered my soul in fasting: and it was made a reproach to me.€€ƒx?And I made haircloth my garment: and I became a byword to them.€€ƒxXThey that sat in the gate spoke against me: and they that drank wine made me their song.€€ƒxrDraw me out of the mire, that I may not stick fast: deliver me from them that hate me, and out of the deep waters.€€ƒxtLet not the tempest of water drown me, nor the deep water swallow me up: and let not the pit shut her mouth upon me.€€ƒxfHear me, O Lord, for thy mercy is kind; look upon me according to the multitude of thy tender mercies.€€ƒxSAnd turn not away thy face from thy servant: for I am in trouble, hear me speedily.€€ƒxAAttend to my soul, and deliver it: save me because of my enemies.€€ƒx9Thou knowest my reproach, and my confusion, and my shame.€€ƒxØIn thy sight are all they that afflict me; my heart hath expected reproach and misery. And I looked for one that would grieve together with me, but there was none: and for one that would comfort me, and I found none.€€ƒxRAnd they gave me gall for my food, and in my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink.€€ƒxVLet their table become as a snare before them, and a recompense, and a stumblingblock.€€ƒxSLet their eyes be darkened that they see not; and their back bend thou down always.€€ƒxQPour out thy indignation upon them: and let thy wrathful anger take hold of them.€€ƒx[Let their habitation be made desolate: and let there be none to dwell in their tabernacles.€€ƒxgBecause they have persecuted him whom thou hast smitten; and they have added to the grief of my wounds.€€˜ƒx]Let them be blotted out of the book of the living; and with the just let them not be written.€€ƒxBBut I am poor and sorrowful: thy salvation, O God, hath set me up.€€ƒxRI will praise the name of God with a canticle: and I will magnify him with praise.€€ƒxVAnd it shall please God better than a young calf, that bringeth forth horns and hoofs.€€ƒxDLet the poor see and rejoice: seek ye God, and your soul shall live.€€ƒxFFor the Lord hath heard the poor: and hath not despised his prisoners.€€ƒxYLet the heavens and the earth praise him; the sea, and every thing that creepeth therein.€€ƒx|For God will save Sion, and the cities of Juda shall be built up. And they shall dwell there, and acquire it by inheritance.€€ƒx_And the seed of his servants shall possess it; and they that love his name shall dwell therein.€€ƒ`€€ƒxQUnto the end, a psalm for David, to bring to remembrance that the Lord saved him.€€ƒx<O God, come to my assistance; O Lord, make haste to help me.€€ƒx5Let them be confounded and ashamed that seek my soul:€€ƒx¤Let them be turned backward, and blush for shame that desire evils to me: Let them be presently turned away blushing for shame that say to me: 'Tis well, 'tis well.€€ƒxyLet all that seek thee rejoice and be glad in thee; and let such as love thy salvation say always: The Lord be magnified.€€ƒxdBut I am needy and poor; O God, help me. Thou art my helper and my deliverer: O Lord, make no delay.€€ƒ`€€ƒx„A psalm for David. Of the sons of Jonadab, and the former captives. In thee, O Lord, I have hoped, let me never be put to confusion:€€ƒxODeliver me in thy justice, and rescue me. Incline thy ear unto me, and save me.€€ƒxƒBe thou unto me a God, a protector, and a place of strength: that thou mayst make me safe. For thou art my firmament and my refuge.€€ƒxzDeliver me, O my God, out of the hand of the sinner, and out of the hand of the transgressor of the law and of the unjust.€€ƒxAFor thou art my patience, O Lord: my hope, O Lord, from my youth.€€ƒx{By thee have I been confirmed from the womb: from my mother's womb thou art my protector. Of thee I shall continually sing:€€ƒx@I am become unto many as a wonder, but thou art a strong helper.€€ƒx^Let my mouth be filled with praise, that I may sing thy glory; thy greatness all the day long.€€ƒx\Cast me not off in the time of old age: when my strength shall fail, do not thou forsake me.€€ƒx]For my enemies have spoken against me; and they that watched my soul have consulted together,€€ƒxUSaying: God hath forsaken him: pursue and take him, for there is none to deliver him.€€ƒx@O God, be not thou far from me: O my God, make haste to my help.€€ƒx€Let them be confounded and come to nothing that detract my soul; let them be covered with confusion and blame that seek my hurt.€€ƒx7But I will always hope; and will add to all thy praise.€€‡ƒxYI will enter into the powers of the Lord: O Lord, I will be mindful of thy justice alone.€€ƒx[Thou hast taught me, O God, from my youth: and till now I will declare thy wonderful works.€€ƒx„And unto old age and grey hairs: O God, forsake me not, Until I shew forth thy arm to all the generation that is to come: Thy power,€€ƒxdAnd thy justice, O God, even to the highest great things thou hast done: O God, who is like to thee?€€ƒx How great troubles hast thou shewn me, many and grievous: and turning thou hast brought me to life, and hast brought me back again from the depths of the earth:€€ƒxPThou hast multiplied thy magnificence; and turning to me thou hast comforted me.€€ƒxFor I will also confess to thee thy truth with the instruments of psaltery: O God, I will sing to thee with the harp, thou holy one of Israel.€€Šƒx‚Yea and my tongue shall meditate on thy justice all the day; when they shall be confounded and put to shame that seek evils to me.€€ƒ`€€ƒsA psalm on Solomon.€€ƒx†Give to the king thy judgment, O God, and to the king's son thy justice: To judge thy people with justice, and thy poor with judgment.€€ƒxFLet the mountains receive peace for the people: and the hills justice.€€ƒxuHe shall judge the poor of the people, and he shall save the children of the poor: and he shall humble the oppressor.€€ƒxSAnd he shall continue with the sun and before the moon, throughout all generations.€€ƒx[He shall come down like rain upon the fleece; and as showers falling gently upon the earth.€€ƒxYIn his days shall justice spring up, and abundance of peace, till the moon be taken away.€€ƒxQAnd he shall rule from sea to sea, and from the river unto the ends of the earth.€€ŠƒxsThe kings of Tharsis and the islands shall offer presents: the kings of the Arabians and of Saba shall bring gifts:€€ƒxHAnd all kings of the earth shall adore him: all nations shall serve him.€€ƒxPFor he shall deliver the poor from the mighty: and the needy that had no helper.€€ƒxKHe shall spare the poor and needy: and he shall save the souls of the poor.€€ƒxhHe shall redeem their souls from usuries and iniquity: and their names shall be honourable in his sight.€€ƒx†And he shall live, and to him shall be given of the gold of Arabia, for him they shall always adore: they shall bless him all the day.€€ƒx¸And there shall be a firmament on the earth on the tops of mountains, above Libanus shall the fruit thereof be exalted: and they of the city shall flourish like the grass of the earth.€€ƒx¡Let his name be blessed for evermore: his name continueth before the sun. And in him shall all the tribes of the earth be blessed: all nations shall magnify him.€€ƒxHBlessed be the Lord, the God of Israel, who alone doth wonderful things.€€ƒxzAnd blessed be the name of his majesty for ever: and the whole earth shall be filled with his majesty. So be it. So be it.€€‰ƒ`€€ƒxPA psalm for Asaph. How good is God to Israel, to them that are of a right heart!€€ƒx>But my feet were almost moved; my steps had well nigh slipped.€€ƒxQBecause I had a zeal on occasion of the wicked, seeing the prosperity of sinners.€€ƒxNFor there is no regard to their death, nor is there strength in their stripes.€€ƒxQThey are not in the labour of men: neither shall they be scourged like other men.€€ƒx_Therefore pride hath held them fast: they are covered with their iniquity and their wickedness.€€ƒxjTheir iniquity hath come forth, as it were from fatness: they have passed into the affection of the heart.€€ƒxKThey have thought and spoken wickedness: they have spoken iniquity on high.€€ˆƒxJTherefore will my people return here and full days shall be found in them.€€ƒxJAnd they said: How doth God know? and is there knowledge in the most High?€€ƒxTBehold these are sinners; and yet, abounding in the world they have obtained riches.€€ƒx[And I said: Then have I in vain justified my heart, and washed my hands among the innocent.€€ƒxTAnd I have been scourged all the day; and my chastisement hath been in the mornings.€€ƒxUIf I said: I will speak thus; behold I should condemn the generation of thy children.€€ƒxCI studied that I might know this thing, it is a labour in my sight:€€ƒxPUntil I go into the sanctuary of God, and understand concerning their last ends.€€’ƒxtHow are they brought to desolation? they have suddenly ceased to be: they have perished by reason of their iniquity.€€ƒx`As the dream of them that awake, O Lord; so in thy city thou shalt bring their image to nothing.€€ƒx@For my heart hath been inflamed, and my reins have been changed:€€ƒx,And I am brought to nothing, and I knew not.€€ƒx>I am become as a beast before thee: and I am always with thee.€€ƒxuThou hast held me by my right hand; and by thy will thou hast conducted me, and with thy glory thou hast received me.€€ƒxHFor what have I in heaven? and besides thee what do I desire upon earth?€€ƒxxFor thee my flesh and my heart hath fainted away: thou art the God of my heart, and the God that is my portion for ever.€€ƒxkFor behold they that go far from thee shall perish: thou hast destroyed all them that are disloyal to thee.€€ƒx”But it is good for me to adhere to my God, to put my hope in the Lord God: That I may declare all thy praises, in the gates of the daughter of Sion.€€ƒ`€€ƒx„Understanding for Asaph. O God, why hast thou cast us off unto the end: why is thy wrath enkindled against the sheep of thy pasture?€€ƒx¦Remember thy congregation, which thou hast possessed from the beginning. The sceptre of thy inheritance which thou hast redeemed: mount Sion in which thou hast dwelt.€€ƒxrLift up thy hands against their pride unto the end; see what things the enemy hath done wickedly in the sanctuary.€€ƒxxAnd they that hate thee have made their boasts, in the midst of thy solemnity. They have set up their ensigns for signs,€€ƒx`And they knew not both in the going out and on the highest top. As with axes in a wood of trees,€€ƒx]They have cut down at once the gates thereof, with axe and hatchet they have brought it down.€€ƒxcThey have set fire to thy sanctuary: they have defiled the dwelling place of thy name on the earth.€€“ƒxQOur signs we have not seen, there is now no prophet: and he will know us no more.€€ƒxYHow long, O God, shall the enemy reproach: is the adversary to provoke thy name for ever?€€ƒx\Why dost thou turn away thy hand: and thy right hand out of the midst of thy bosom for ever?€€ƒxUBut God is our king before ages: he hath wrought salvation in the midst of the earth.€€ƒxfThou by thy strength didst make the sea firm: thou didst crush the heads of the dragons in the waters.€€ƒxjThou hast broken the heads of the dragon: thou hast given him to be meat for the people of the Ethiopians.€€ƒxXThou hast broken up the fountains and the torrents: thou hast dried up the Ethan rivers.€€ƒxWThine is the day, and thine is the night: thou hast made the morning light and the sun.€€ƒx[Thou hast made all the borders of the earth: the summer and the spring were formed by thee.€€ƒx_Remember this, the enemy hath reproached the Lord: and a foolish people hath provoked thy name.€€ƒxiDeliver not up to beasts the souls that confess to thee: and forget not to the end the souls of thy poor.€€ƒxtHave regard to thy covenant: for they that are the obscure of the earth have been filled with dwellings of iniquity.€€ƒx[Let not the humble be turned away with confusion: the poor and needy shall praise thy name.€€ƒxwArise, O God, judge thy own cause: remember thy reproaches with which the foolish man hath reproached thee all the day.€€ƒx]Forget not the voices of thy enemies: the pride of them that hate thee ascendeth continually.€€ƒ`€€ƒx;Unto the end, corrupt not, a psalm of a canticle for Asaph.€€ƒxnWe will praise thee, O God: we will praise, and we will call upon thy name. We will relate thy wondrous works:€€ƒx0When I shall take a time, I will judge justices.€€‚ƒxTI said to the wicked: Do not act wickedly: and to the sinners: Lift not up the horn.€€ƒx>Lift not up your horn on high: speak not iniquity against God.€€˜ƒxEFor God is the judge. One he putteth down, and another he lifteth up:€€ƒxÈFor in the hand of the Lord there is a cup of strong wine full of mixture. And he hath poured it out from this to that: but the dregs thereof are not emptied: all the sinners of the earth shall drink.€€ƒx=But I will declare for ever: I will sing to the God of Jacob.€€ƒxVAnd I will break all the horns of sinners: but the horns of the just shall be exalted.€€ƒ`€€ƒxIUnto the end, in praises, a psalm for Asaph: a canticle to the Assyrians.€€ƒx3In Judea God is known: his name is great in Israel.€€ƒx1And his place is in peace: and his abode in Sion:€€ƒxOThere hath he broken the powers of bows, the shield, the sword, and the battle.€€ƒx9Thou enlightenest wonderfully from the everlasting hills.€€ƒxAll the foolish of heart were troubled. They have slept their sleep; and all the men of riches have found nothing in their hands.€€ƒxQAt thy rebuke, O God of Jacob, they have all slumbered that mounted on horseback.€€ƒxGThou art terrible, and who shall resist thee? from that time thy wrath.€€ƒxTThou hast caused judgment to be heard from heaven: the earth trembled and was still,€€ƒx>When God arose in judgment, to save all the meek of the earth.€€ƒxoFor the thought of man shall give praise to thee: and the remainders of the thought shall keep holiday to thee.€€ƒxoVow ye, and pay to the Lord your God: all you that are round about him bring presents. To him that is terrible,€€ƒx_Even to him who taketh away the spirit of princes: to the terrible with the kings of the earth.€€ƒ`€€ƒx,Unto the end, for Idithun, a psalm of Asaph.€€ƒxOI cried to the Lord with my voice; to God with my voice, and he gave ear to me.€€ƒxIn the days of my trouble I sought God, with my hands lifted up to him in the night, and I was not deceived. My soul refused to be comforted:€€ƒxSI remembered God, and was delighted, and was exercised, and my spirit swooned away.€€ƒx?My eyes prevented the watches: I was troubled, and I spoke not.€€ƒxGI thought upon the days of old: and I had in my mind the eternal years.€€ƒxZAnd I meditated in the night with my own heart: and I was exercised and I swept my spirit.€€ƒxKWill God then cast off for ever? or will he never be more favourable again?€€ƒxEOr will he cut off his mercy for ever, from generation to generation?€€ƒxNOr will God forget to shew mercy? or will he in his anger shut up his mercies?€€˜°ƒx\I remembered the works of the Lord: for I will be mindful of thy wonders from the beginning.€€ƒxMAnd I will meditate on all thy works: and will be employed in thy inventions.€€ƒxHThy way, O God, is in the holy place: who is the great God like our God?€€ƒxUThou art the God that dost wonders. Thou hast made thy power known among the nations:€€ƒxOWith thy arm thou hast redeemed thy people the children of Jacob and of Joseph.€€ƒxdThe waters saw thee, O God, the waters saw thee: and they were afraid, and the depths were troubled.€€ƒxTGreat was the noise of the waters: the clouds sent out a sound. For thy arrows pass:€€ƒxhThe voice of thy thunder in a wheel. Thy lightnings enlightened the world: the earth shook and trembled.€€ƒxZThy way is in the sea, and thy paths in many waters: and thy footsteps shall not be known.€€ƒxJThou hast conducted thy people like sheep, by the hand of Moses and Aaron.€€ƒ`€€ƒxdUnderstanding for Asaph. Attend, O my people, to my law: incline your ears to the words of my mouth.€€ƒxOI will open my mouth in parables: I will utter propositions from the beginning.€€ƒxGHow great things have we heard and known, and our fathers have told us.€€ƒxœThey have not been hidden from their children, in another generation. Declaring the praises of the Lord, and his powers, and his wonders which he hath done.€€ƒx And he set up a testimony in Jacob: and made a law in Israel. How great things he commanded our fathers, that they should make the same known to their children:€€ƒxThat another generation might know them. The children that should be born and should rise up, and declare them to their children.€€ƒxgThat they may put their hope in God and may not forget the works of God: and may seek his commandments.€€ƒx¬That they may not become like their fathers, a perverse and exasperating generation. A generation that set not their heart aright: and whose spirit was not faithful to God.€€ƒx`The sons of Ephraim who bend and shoot with the bow: they have turned back in the day of battle.€€ƒxFThey kept not the covenant of God: and in his law they would not walk.€€ƒxEAnd they forgot his benefits, and his wonders that he had shewn them.€€ƒxfWonderful things did he do in the sight of their fathers, in the land of Egypt, in the field of Tanis.€€ƒx\He divided the sea and brought them through: and he made the waters to stand as in a vessel.€€ƒxRAnd he conducted them with a cloud by day: and all the night with a light of fire.€€ƒxWHe struck the rock in the wilderness: and gave them to drink, as out of the great deep.€€ƒxLHe brought forth water out of the rock: and made streams run down as rivers.€€ƒxiAnd they added yet more sin against him: they provoked the most High to wrath in the place without water.€€ƒxGAnd they tempted God in their hearts, by asking meat for their desires.€€ƒxPAnd they spoke ill of God: they said: Can God furnish a table in the wilderness?€€ƒxBecause he struck the rock, and the waters gushed out, and the streams overflowed. Can he also give bread, or provide a table for his people?€€ƒxpTherefore the Lord heard, and was angry: and a fire was kindled against Jacob, and wrath came up against Israel.€€ƒxCBecause they believed not in God: and trusted not in his salvation.€€ƒxOAnd he had commanded the clouds from above, and had opened the doors of heaven.€€ƒxSAnd had rained down manna upon them to eat, and had given them the bread of heaven.€€ƒxBMan ate the bread of angels: he sent them provisions in abundance.€€ƒxVHe removed the south wind from heaven: and by his power brought in the southwest wind.€€ƒxWAnd he rained upon them flesh as dust: and feathered fowls like as the sand of the sea.€€ƒxFAnd they fell in the midst of their camp, round about their pavilions.€€ƒxLSo they did eat, and were filled exceedingly, and he gave them their desire:€€ƒxXthey were not defrauded of that which they craved. As yet their meat was in their mouth:€€ƒxvAnd the wrath of God came upon them. And he slew the fat ones amongst them, and brought down the chosen men of Israel.€€ƒxSIn all these things they sinned still: and they behaved not for his wondrous works.€€ƒxAAnd their days were consumed in vanity, and their years in haste.€€ƒxaWhen he slew them, then they sought him: and they returned, and came to him early in the morning.€€ƒxTAnd they remembered that God was their helper: and the most high God their redeemer.€€ƒxNAnd they loved him with their mouth: and with their tongue they lied unto him:€€ƒxWBut their heart was not right with him: nor were they counted faithful in his covenant.€€ƒx™But he is merciful, and will forgive their sins: and will not destroy them. And many a time did he turn away his anger: and did not kindle all his wrath.€€ƒxKAnd he remembered that they are flesh: a wind that goeth and returneth not.€€ƒx_How often did they provoke him in the desert: and move him to wrath in the place without water?€€ƒxIAnd they turned back and tempted God: and grieved the holy one of Israel.€€ƒxhThey remembered not his hand, in the day that he redeemed them from the hand of him that afflicted them:€€ƒxIHow he wrought his signs in Egypt, and his wonders in the field of Tanis.€€ƒxSAnd he turned their rivers into blood, and their showers that they might not drink.€€ƒx`He sent amongst them divers sorts of flies, which devoured them: and frogs which destroyed them.€€ƒxJAnd he gave up their fruits to the blast, and their labours to the locust.€€ƒxTAnd he destroyed their vineyards with hail, and their mulberry trees with hoarfrost.€€ƒxEAnd he gave up their cattle to the hail, and their stock to the fire.€€ƒxtAnd he sent upon them the wrath of his indignation: indignation and wrath and trouble, which he sent by evil angels.€€ƒxrHe made a way for a path to his anger: he spared not their souls from death, and their cattle he shut up in death.€€ƒxuAnd he killed all the firstborn in the land of Egypt: the firstfruits of all their labour in the tabernacles of Cham.€€ƒxYAnd he took away his own people as sheep: and guided them in the wilderness like a flock.€€ƒx[And he brought them out in hope and they feared not: and the sea overwhelmed their enemies.€€ƒxÓAnd he brought them into the mountain of his sanctuary: the mountain which his right hand had purchased. And he cast out the Gentiles before them: and by lot divided to them their land by a line of distribution.€€ƒx?And he made the tribes of Israel to dwell in their tabernacles.€€ƒxTYet they tempted, and provoked the most high God: and they kept not his testimonies.€€ƒxqAnd they turned away, and kept not the covenant: even like their fathers they were turned aside as a crooked bow.€€ƒx^They provoked him to anger on their hills: and moved him to jealousy with their graven things.€€ƒxVGod heard, and despised them, and he reduced Israel exceedingly as it were to nothing.€€ƒxPAnd he put away the tabernacle of Silo, his tabernacle where he dwelt among men.€€ƒx]And he delivered their strength into captivity: and their beauty into the hands of the enemy.€€ƒxKAnd he shut up his people under the sword: and he despised his inheritance.€€ƒxCFire consumed their young men: and their maidens were not lamented.€€ƒx@Their priests fell by the sword: and their widows did not mourn.€€ƒxfAnd the Lord was awaked as one out of sleep, and like a mighty man that hath been surfeited with wine.€€ƒxUAnd he smote his enemies on the hinder parts: he put them to an everlasting reproach.€€ƒxMAnd he rejected the tabernacle of Joseph: and chose not the tribe of Ephraim:€€ƒx:But he chose the tribe of Juda, mount Sion which he loved.€€ƒxQAnd he built his sanctuary as of unicorns, in the land which he founded for ever.€€ƒxAnd he chose his servant David, and took him from the flocks of sheep: he brought him from following the ewes great with young,€€ƒx5To feed Jacob his servant and Israel his inheritance.€€ƒxbAnd he fed them in the innocence of his heart: and conducted them by the skilfulness of his hands.€€ƒ`€€ƒx›A psalm for Asaph. O God, the heathens are come into thy inheritance, they have defiled thy holy temple: they have made Jerusalem as a place to keep fruit.€€ƒx‰They have given the dead bodies of thy servants to be meat for the fowls of the air: the flesh of thy saints for the beasts of the earth.€€ƒxaThey have poured out their blood as water, round about Jerusalem and there was none to bury them.€€ƒxaWe are become a reproach to our neighbours: a scorn and derision to them that are round about us.€€ƒxUHow long, O Lord, wilt thou be angry for ever: shall thy zeal be kindled like a fire?€€ƒxwPour out thy wrath upon the nations that have not known thee: and upon the kingdoms that have not called upon thy name.€€ƒx@Because they have devoured Jacob; and have laid waste his place.€€ƒxjRemember not our former iniquities: let thy mercies speedily prevent us, for we are become exceeding poor.€€ƒx|Help us, O God, our saviour: and for the glory of thy name, O Lord, deliver us: and forgive us our sins for thy name's sake:€€ƒx»Lest they should say among the Gentiles: Where is their God? And let him be made known among the nations before our eyes, By the revenging the blood of thy servants, which hath been shed:€€ƒx¡Let the sighing of the prisoners come in before thee. According to the greatness of thy arm, take possession of the children of them that have been put to death.€€ƒxpAnd render to our neighbours sevenfold in their bosom: the reproach wherewith they have reproached thee, O Lord.€€ƒx’But we thy people, and the sheep of thy pasture, will give thanks to thee for ever. We will shew forth thy praise, unto generation and generation.€€ƒ`€€ƒxMUnto the end, for them that shall be changed, a testimony for Asaph, a psalm.€€ƒx}Give ear, O thou that rulest Israel: thou that leadest Joseph like a sheep. Thou that sittest upon the cherubims, shine forth€€ƒxOBefore Ephraim, Benjamin, and Manasses. Stir up thy might, and come to save us.€€ƒx?Convert us, O God: and shew us thy face, and we shall be saved.€€ƒxSO Lord God of hosts, how long wilt thou be angry against the prayer of thy servant?€€ƒx_How long wilt thou feed us with the bread of tears: and give us for our drink tears in measure?€€ƒx^Thou hast made us to be a contradiction to our neighbours: and our enemies have scoffed at us.€€ƒxEO God of hosts, convert us: and shew thy face, and we shall be saved.€€ƒxZThou hast brought a vineyard out of Egypt: thou hast cast out the Gentiles and planted it.€€ƒxjThou wast the guide of its journey in its sight: thou plantedst the roots thereof, and it filled the land.€€ƒxOThe shadow of it covered the hills: and the branches thereof the cedars of God.€€ƒxLIt stretched forth its branches unto the sea, and its boughs unto the river.€€ƒx^Why hast thou broken down the hedge thereof, so that all they who pass by the way do pluck it?€€ƒxXThe boar out of the wood hath laid it waste: and a singular wild beast hath devoured it.€€ƒxTTurn again, O God of hosts, look down from heaven, and see, and visit this vineyard:€€ƒxuAnd perfect the same which thy right hand hath planted: and upon the son of man whom thou hast confirmed for thyself.€€ƒxNThings set on fire and dug down shall perish at the rebuke of thy countenance.€€ƒxmLet thy hand be upon the man of thy right hand: and upon the son of man whom thou hast confirmed for thyself.€€ƒxSAnd we depart not from thee, thou shalt quicken us: and we will call upon thy name.€€ƒxIO Lord God of hosts, convert us and shew thy face, and we shall be saved.€€ƒ`€€ƒx=Unto the end, for the winepresses, a psalm for Asaph himself.€€ƒx:Rejoice to God our helper: sing aloud to the God of Jacob.€€ƒxPTake a psalm, and bring hither the timbrel: the pleasant psaltery with the harp.€€ƒxHBlow up the trumpet on the new moon, on the noted day of your solemnity.€€ƒxFFor it is a commandment in Israel, and a judgment to the God of Jacob.€€ƒxuHe ordained it for a testimony in Joseph, when he came out of the land of Egypt: he heard a tongue which he knew not.€€ƒxFHe removed his back from the burdens: his hands had served in baskets.€€ƒx•Thou calledst upon me in affliction, and I delivered thee: I heard thee in the secret place of tempest: I proved thee at the waters of contradiction.€€ƒxTHear, O my people, and I will testify to thee: O Israel, if thou wilt hearken to me,€€ƒxJthere shall be no new god in thee: neither shalt thou adore a strange god.€€ƒxnFor I am the Lord thy God, who brought thee out of the land of Egypt: open thy mouth wide, and I will fill it.€€ƒxABut my people heard not my voice: and Israel hearkened not to me.€€ƒxbSo I let them go according to the desires of their heart: they shall walk in their own inventions.€€ƒx;If my people had heard me: if Israel had walked in my ways:€€ƒxVI should soon have humbled their enemies, and laid my hand on them that troubled them.€€ƒxKThe enemies of the Lord have lied to him: and their time shall be for ever.€€ƒxRAnd he fed them with the fat of wheat, and filled them with honey out of the rock.€€ƒ`€€ƒxnA psalm for Asaph. God hath stood in the congregation of gods: and being in the midst of them he judgeth gods.€€ƒxGHow long will you judge unjustly: and accept the persons of the wicked?€€ƒxJJudge for the needy and fatherless: do justice to the humble and the poor.€€ƒxERescue the poor; and deliver the needy out of the hand of the sinner.€€ƒxnThey have not known nor understood: they walk on in darkness: all the foundations of the earth shall be moved.€€ƒxCI have said: You are gods and all of you the sons of the most High.€€ƒxCBut you like men shall die: and shall fall like one of the princes.€€ƒxQArise, O God, judge thou the earth: for thou shalt inherit among all the nations.€€ƒ`€€ƒx A canticle of a psalm for Asaph.€€ƒxSO God, who shall be like to thee? hold not thy peace, neither be thou still, O God.€€ƒxWFor lo, thy enemies have made a noise: and they that hate thee have lifted up the head.€€ƒx^They have taken a malicious counsel against thy people, and have consulted against thy saints.€€ƒx}They have said: Come and let us destroy them, so that they be not a nation: and let the name of Israel be remembered no more.€€ƒxZFor they have contrived with one consent: they have made a covenant together against thee,€€ƒxLThe tabernacle of the Edomites, and the Ishmahelites: Moab, and the Agarens,€€ƒxKGebal, and Ammon and Amalec: the Philistines, with the inhabitants of Tyre.€€ƒx\Yea, and the Assyrian also is joined with them: they are come to the aid of the sons of Lot.€€ƒxUDo to them as thou didst to Madian and to Sisara: as to Jabin at the brook of Cisson.€€ƒx8Who perished at Endor: and became as dung for the earth.€€ƒxQMake their princes like Oreb, and Zeb, and Zebee, and Salmana. All their princes,€€ƒxFWho have said: Let us possess the sanctuary of God for an inheritance.€€ƒxAO my God, make them like a wheel; and as stubble before the wind.€€ƒxAAs fire which burneth the wood: and as a flame burning mountains:€€ƒxPSo shalt thou pursue them with thy tempest: and shalt trouble them in thy wrath.€€ƒxBFill their faces with shame; and they shall seek thy name, O Lord.€€ƒxZLet them be ashamed and troubled for ever and ever: and let them be confounded and perish.€€ƒx]And let them know that the Lord is thy name: thou alone art the most High over all the earth.€€ƒ`€€ƒx@Unto the end, for the winepresses, a psalm for the sons of Core.€€ƒx0How lovely are thy tabernacles, O Lord of hosts!€€ƒxomy soul longeth and fainteth for the courts of the Lord. My heart and my flesh have rejoiced in the living God.€€ƒx For the sparrow hath found herself a house, and the turtle a nest for herself where she may lay her young ones: Thy altars, O Lord of hosts, my king and my God.€€ƒx[Blessed are they that dwell in thy house, O Lord: they shall praise thee for ever and ever.€€ƒx]Blessed is the man whose help is from thee: in his heart he hath disposed to ascend by steps,€€ƒx5In the vale of tears, in the place which he hath set.€€ƒxsFor the lawgiver shall give a blessing, they shall go from virtue to virtue: the God of gods shall be seen in Sion.€€ƒx>O Lord God of hosts, hear my prayer: give ear, O God of Jacob.€€ƒx@Behold, O God our protector: and look on the face of thy Christ.€€‡ƒxCFor God loveth mercy and truth: the Lord will give grace and glory.€€ƒxzHe will not deprive of good things them that walk in innocence: O Lord of hosts, blessed is the man that trusteth in thee.€€ƒ`€€ƒx,Unto the end, for the sons of Core, a psalm.€€ƒxOLord, thou hast blessed thy land: thou hast turned away the captivity of Jacob.€€ƒxPThou hast forgiven the iniquity of thy people: thou hast covered all their sins.€€ƒx[Thou hast mitigated all thy anger: thou hast turned away from the wrath of thy indignation.€€ˆƒxaWilt thou be angry with us for ever: or wilt thou extend thy wrath from generation to generation?€€ƒxRThou wilt turn, O God, and bring us to life: and thy people shall rejoice in thee.€€ƒx7Shew us, O Lord, thy mercy; and grant us thy salvation.€€ƒxœI will hear what the Lord God will speak in me: for he will speak peace unto his people: And unto his saints: and unto them that are converted to the heart.€€ƒxVSurely his salvation is near to them that fear him : that glory may dwell in our land.€€ƒxCMercy and truth have met each other: justice and peace have kissed.€€ƒxKTruth is sprung out of the earth: and justice hath looked down from heaven.€€ƒxEFor the Lord will give goodness: and our earth shall yield her fruit.€€‰ƒ`€€ƒxZA prayer for David himself. Incline thy ear, O Lord, and hear me: for I am needy and poor.€€ƒxSPreserve my soul, for I am holy: save thy servant, O my God, that trusteth in thee.€€ƒx?Have mercy on me, O Lord, for I have cried to thee all the day.€€ƒxSGive joy to the soul of thy servant, for to thee, O Lord, I have lifted up my soul.€€ƒxXFor thou, O Lord, art sweet and mild: and plenteous in mercy to all that call upon thee.€€ƒxGGive ear, O Lord, to my prayer: and attend to the voice of my petition.€€ƒxMI have called upon thee in the day of my trouble: because thou hast heard me.€€ƒx^There is none among the gods like unto thee, O Lord: and there is none according to thy works.€€„ƒxAFor thou art great and dost wonderful things: thou art God alone.€€ƒxmConduct me, O Lord, in thy way, and I will walk in thy truth: let my heart rejoice that it may fear thy name.€€ƒx]I will praise thee, O Lord my God, with my whole heart, and I will glorify thy name for ever:€€ƒxYFor thy mercy is great towards me: and thou hast delivered my soul out of the lower hell.€€ˆƒx^And thou, O Lord, art a God of compassion, and merciful, patient, and of much mercy, and true.€€ƒxhO look upon me, and have mercy on me: give thy command to thy servant, and save the son of thy handmaid.€€ƒx‡Shew me a token for good: that they who hate me may see, and be confounded, because thou, O Lord, hast helped me and hast comforted me.€€ƒ`€€ƒx\For the sons of Core, a psalm of a canticle. The foundations thereof are the holy mountains:€€ƒxEThe Lord loveth the gates of Sion above all the tabernacles of Jacob.€€ƒx0Glorious things are said of thee, O city of God.€€ƒxŠI will be mindful of Rahab and of Babylon knowing me. Behold the foreigners, and Tyre, and the people of the Ethiopians, these were there.€€…ƒx]The Lord shall tell in his writings of peoples and of princes, of them that have been in her.€€ƒx4The dwelling in thee is as it were of all rejoicing.€€ƒ`€€ƒxuA canticle of a psalm for the sons of Core: unto the end, for Maheleth, to answer understanding of Eman the Ezrahite.€€ƒxWO Lord, the God of my salvation: I have cried in the day, and in the night before thee.€€†ƒxFFor my soul is filled with evils: and my life hath drawn nigh to hell.€€ƒxSI am counted among them that go down to the pit: I am become as a man without help,€€ƒx‚Free among the dead. Like the slain sleeping in the sepulchres, whom thou rememberest no more: and they are cut off from thy hand.€€ƒxSThey have laid me in the lower pit: in the dark places, and in the shadow of death.€€ƒxLThy wrath is strong over me: and all thy waves thou hast brought in upon me.€€ƒx†Thou hast put away my acquaintance far from me: they have set me an abomination to themselves. I was delivered up, and came not forth:€€…ƒx_Wilt thou shew wonders to the dead? or shall physicians raise to life, and give praise to thee?€€ƒxOShall any one in the sepulchre declare thy mercy: and thy truth in destruction?€€ƒxUShall thy wonders be known in the dark; and thy justice in the land of forgetfulness?€€ƒxSBut I, O Lord, have cried to thee: and in the morning my prayer shall prevent thee.€€ƒxMLord, why castest thou off my prayer: why turnest thou away thy face from me?€€†ƒx>Thy wrath hath come upon me: and thy terrors have troubled me.€€ƒx\They have come round about me like water all the day: they have compassed me about together.€€ƒxWFriend and neighbour thou hast put far from me: and my acquaintance, because of misery.€€ƒ`€€ƒx)Of understanding, for Ethan the Ezrahite.€€ƒxuThe mercies of the Lord I will sing for ever. I will shew forth thy truth with my mouth to generation and generation.€€ˆƒxGI have made a covenant with my elect: I have sworn to David my servant:€€ƒx_Thy seed will I settle for ever. And I will build up thy throne unto generation and generation.€€ƒxYThe heavens shall confess thy wonders, O Lord: and thy truth in the church of the saints.€€ƒxeFor who in the clouds can be compared to the Lord: or who among the sons of God shall be like to God?€€ƒxjGod, who is glorified in the assembly of the saints: great and terrible above all them that are about him.€€ƒxeO Lord God of hosts, who is like to thee? thou art mighty, O Lord, and thy truth is round about thee.€€ƒxPThou rulest the power of the sea: and appeasest the motion of the waves thereof.€€ƒxtThou hast humbled the proud one, as one that is slain: with the arm of thy strength thou hast scattered thy enemies.€€ˆƒxUThe north and the sea thou hast created. Thabor and Hermon shall rejoice in thy name:€€ƒxPThy arm is with might. Let thy hand be strengthened, and thy right hand exalted:€€ƒxaJustice and judgment are the preparation of thy throne. Mercy and truth shall go before thy face:€€ƒxhBlessed is the people that knoweth jubilation. They shall walk, O Lord, in the light of thy countenance:€€ƒxYAnd in thy name they shall rejoice all the day, and in thy justice they shall be exalted.€€ƒx]For thou art the glory of their strength: and in thy good pleasure shall our horn be exalted.€€ƒxJFor our protection is of the Lord, and of our king the holy one of Israel.€€ƒxThen thou spokest in a vision to thy saints, and saidst: I have laid help upon one that is mighty, and have exalted one chosen out of my people.€€ƒƒx<For my hand shall help him: and my arm shall strengthen him.€€ƒx[The enemy shall have no advantage over him: nor the son of iniquity have power to hurt him.€€ƒx]And I will cut down his enemies before his face; and them that hate him I will put to flight.€€’ƒxEAnd I will set his hand in the sea; and his right hand in the rivers.€€ƒxTHe shall cry out to me: Thou art my father: my God, and the support of my salvation.€€ƒxDAnd I will make him my firstborn, high above the kings of the earth.€€ƒxGI will keep my mercy for him for ever: and my covenant faithful to him.€€ƒxVAnd I will make his seed to endure for evermore: and his throne as the days of heaven.€€ƒxAAnd if his children forsake my law, and walk not in my judgments:€€ƒx:If they profane my justices: and keep not my commandments:€€ƒxEI will visit their iniquities with a rod and their sins with stripes.€€ƒxOBut my mercy I will not take away from him: nor will I suffer my truth to fail.€€ƒxbNeither will I profane my covenant: and the words that proceed from my mouth I will not make void.€€ƒx<Once have I sworn by my holiness: I will not lie unto David:€€ƒxHis seed shall endure for ever.€€ƒxhAnd his throne as the sun before me: and as the moon perfect for ever, and a faithful witness in heaven.€€ƒxKBut thou hast rejected and despised: thou hast been angry with my anointed.€€ƒx`Thou hast overthrown the covenant of thy servant: thou hast profaned his sanctuary on the earth.€€ƒxGThou hast broken down all his hedges: thou hast made his strength fear.€€ƒxTAll that pass by the way have robbed him: he is become a reproach to his neighbours.€€ƒxdThou hast set up the right hand of them that oppress him: thou hast made all his enemies to rejoice.€€ƒƒx[Thou hast made his purification to cease: and thou hast cast his throne down to the ground.€€ƒxOThou hast shortened the days of his time: thou hast covered him with confusion.€€ƒxQHow long, O Lord, turnest thou away unto the end? shall thy anger burn like fire?€€ƒƒxeWho is the man that shall live, and not see death: that shall deliver his soul from the hand of hell?€€ƒx^Lord, where are thy ancient mercies, according to what thou didst swear to David in thy truth?€€ƒxdBe mindful, O Lord, of the reproach of thy servants (which I have held in my bosom) of many nations:€€•ƒx5Blessed be the Lord for evermore. So be it. So be it.€€ƒ`€€ƒx`A prayer of Moses the man of God. Lord, thou hast been our refuge from generation to generation.€€ƒxrBefore the mountains were made, or the earth and the world was formed; from eternity and to eternity thou art God.€€ƒxXTurn not man away to be brought low: and thou hast said: Be converted, O ye sons of men.€€ƒx_For a thousand years in thy sight are as yesterday, which is past. And as a watch in the night,€€ƒx6Things that are counted nothing, shall their years be.€€ƒxIn the morning man shall grow up like grass; in the morning he shall flourish and pass away: in the evening he shall fall, grow dry, and wither.€€ƒxKFor in thy wrath we have fainted away: and are troubled in thy indignation.€€ƒxWThou hast set our iniquities before thy eyes: our life in the light of thy countenance.€€ƒxmFor all our days are spent; and in thy wrath we have fainted away. Our years shall be considered as a spider:€€ƒxÑThe days of our years in them are threescore and ten years. But if in the strong they be fourscore years: and what is more of them is labour and sorrow. For mildness is come upon us: and we shall be corrected.€€ƒx4Who knoweth the power of thy anger, and for thy fear€€ƒxXCan number thy wrath? So make thy right hand known: and men learned in heart, in wisdom.€€ƒxEReturn, O Lord, how long? and be entreated in favour of thy servants.€€ƒxbWe are filled in the morning with thy mercy: and we have rejoiced, and are delighted all our days.€€ƒxgWe have rejoiced for the days in which thou hast humbled us: for the years in which we have seen evils.€€ƒxGLook upon thy servants and upon their works: and direct their children.€€ƒx‘And let the brightness of the Lord our God be upon us: and direct thou the works of our hands over us; yea, the work of our hands do thou direct.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‡The praise of a canticle for David. He that dwelleth in the aid of the most High, shall abide under the protection of the God of Jacob.€€˜ƒxPFor he hath delivered me from the snare of the hunters: and from the sharp word.€€ƒxQHe will overshadow thee with his shoulders: and under his wings thou shalt trust.€€ƒx`His truth shall compass thee with a shield: thou shalt not be afraid of the terror of the night.€€ƒxzOf the arrow that flieth in the day, of the business that walketh about in the dark: of invasion, or of the noonday devil.€€ƒxgA thousand shall fall at thy side, and ten thousand at thy right hand: but it shall not come nigh thee.€€ƒxNBut thou shalt consider with thy eyes: and shalt see the reward of the wicked.€€ƒxKBecause thou, O Lord, art my hope: thou hast made the most High thy refuge.€€ƒxOThere shall no evil come to thee: nor shall the scourge come near thy dwelling.€€ƒxLFor he hath given his angels charge over thee; to keep thee in all thy ways.€€ƒxPIn their hands they shall bear thee up: lest thou dash thy foot against a stone.€€ƒxiThou shalt walk upon the asp and the basilisk: and thou shalt trample under foot the lion and the dragon.€€ƒx\Because he hoped in me I will deliver him: I will protect him because he hath known my name.€€ƒxrHe shall cry to me, and I will hear him: I am with him in tribulation, I will deliver him, and I will glorify him.€€ƒxFI will fill him with length of days; and I will shew him my salvation.€€ƒ`€€ƒx)A psalm of a canticle on the sabbath day.€€ƒxLIt is good to give praise to the Lord: and to sing to thy name, O most High.€€ƒxCTo shew forth thy mercy in the morning, and thy truth in the night:€€ƒxTUpon an instrument of ten strings, upon the psaltery: with a canticle upon the harp.€€ƒxgFor thou hast given me, O Lord, a delight in thy doings: and in the works of thy hands I shall rejoice.€€ƒxAO Lord, how great are thy works! thy thoughts are exceeding deep.€€ƒxLThe senseless man shall not know: nor will the fool understand these things.€€ƒxWhen the wicked shall spring up as grass: and all the workers of iniquity shall appear: That they may perish for ever and ever:€€ƒx-But thou, O Lord, art most high for evermore.€€ƒxxFor behold thy enemies, O Lord, for behold thy enemies shall perish: and all the workers of iniquity shall be scattered.€€ƒxYBut my horn shall be exalted like that of the unicorn: and my old age in plentiful mercy.€€‰ƒxWThe just shall flourish like the palm tree: he shall grow up like the cedar of Libanus.€€ƒxdThey that are planted in the house of the Lord shall flourish in the courts of the house of our God.€€ƒxKThey shall still increase in a fruitful old age: and shall be well treated,€€ƒxXThat they may shew, That the Lord our God is righteous, and there is no iniquity in him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx©The Lord hath reigned, he is clothed with beauty: the Lord is clothed with strength, and hath girded himself. For he hath established the world which shall not be moved.€€ƒx=My throne is prepared from of old: thou art from everlasting.€€ƒxpThe floods have lifted up, O Lord: the floods have lifted up their voice. The floods have lifted up their waves,€€ƒxbWith the noise of many waters. Wonderful are the surges of the sea: wonderful is the Lord on high.€€ˆƒ`€€ƒxTThe Lord is the God to whom revenge belongeth: the God of revenge hath acted freely.€€ƒxKLift up thyself, thou that judgest the earth: render a reward to the proud.€€ƒx=How long shall sinners, O Lord: how long shall sinners glory?€€ƒxIShall they utter, and speak iniquity: shall all speak who work injustice?€€ƒxSThy people, O Lord, they have brought low: and they have afflicted thy inheritance.€€ƒxRThey have slain the widow and the stranger: and they have murdered the fatherless.€€ƒxVAnd they have said: The Lord shall not see: neither shall the God of Jacob understand.€€˜ƒx\He that planted the ear, shall he not hear? or he that formed the eye, doth he not consider?€€ƒxPHe that chastiseth nations, shall he not rebuke: he that teacheth man knowledge?€€ƒx9The Lord knoweth the thoughts of men, that they are vain.€€ƒxXBlessed is the man whom thou shalt instruct, O Lord: and shalt teach him out of thy law.€€ƒxSThat thou mayst give him rest from the evil days: till a pit be dug for the wicked.€€ƒxWFor the Lord will not cast off his people: neither will he forsake his own inheritance.€€ƒx^Until justice be turned into judgment: and they that are near it are all the upright in heart.€€ƒxkWho shall rise up for me against the evildoers? or who shall stand with me against the workers of iniquity?€€ƒxEUnless the Lord had been my helper, my soul had almost dwelt in hell.€€ƒx<If I said: My foot is moved: thy mercy, O Lord, assisted me.€€ƒx]According to the multitude of my sorrows in my heart, thy comforts have given joy to my soul.€€ƒxKDoth the seat of iniquity stick to thee, who framest labour in commandment?€€ƒxKThey will hunt after the soul of the just, and will condemn innocent blood.€€ƒx:But the Lord is my refuge: and my God the help of my hope.€€ƒxvAnd he will render them their iniquity : and in their malice he will destroy them: the Lord our God will destroy them.€€ƒ`€€ƒxNCome let us praise the Lord with joy: let us joyfully sing to God our saviour.€€ƒx^Let us come before his presence with thanksgiving; and make a joyful noise to him with psalms.€€ƒx=For the Lord is a great God, and a great King above all gods.€€ƒxXFor in his hand are all the ends of the earth: and the heights of the mountains are his.€€ƒxFFor the sea is his, and he made it: and his hands formed the dry land.€€ƒxGCome let us adore and fall down: and weep before the Lord that made us.€€ƒx[For he is the Lord our God: and we are the people of his pasture and the sheep of his hand.€€ƒx;To day if you shall hear his voice, harden not your hearts:€€ƒx\Forty years long was I offended with that generation, and I said: These always err in heart.€€ƒxdAnd these men have not known my ways: so I swore in my wrath that they shall not enter into my rest.€€ƒ`€€ƒxA canticle for David himself, when the house was built after the captivity. Sing ye to the Lord a new canticle: sing to the Lord, all the earth.€€ƒxQSing ye to the Lord and bless his name: shew forth his salvation from day to day.€€ƒxCDeclare his glory among the Gentiles: his wonders among all people.€€ƒxXFor the Lord is great, and exceedingly to be praised: he is to be feared above all gods.€€ƒxKFor all the gods of the Gentiles are devils: but the Lord made the heavens.€€ƒxHPraise and beauty are before him: holiness and majesty in his sanctuary.€€ƒx[Bring ye to the Lord, O ye kindreds of the Gentiles, bring ye to the Lord glory and honour:€€ƒxUBring to the Lord glory unto his name. Bring up sacrifices, and come into his courts:€€ƒxPAdore ye the Lord in his holy court. Let all the earth be moved at his presence.€€ƒx“Say ye among the Gentiles, the Lord hath reigned. For he hath corrected the world, which shall not be moved: he will judge the people with justice.€€ŠƒxiThe fields and all things that are in them shall be joyful. Then shall all the trees of the woods rejoice€€ƒx›before the face of the Lord, because he cometh: because he cometh to judge the earth. He shall judge the world with justice, and the people with his truth.€€ƒ`€€ƒx„For the same David, when his land was restored again to him. The Lord hath reigned, let the earth rejoice: let many islands be glad.€€ƒxbClouds and darkness are round about him: justice and judgment are the establishment of his throne.€€ƒxCA fire shall go before him, and shall burn his enemies round about.€€ƒxIHis lightnings have shone forth to the world: the earth saw and trembled.€€ƒxiThe mountains melted like wax, at the presence of the Lord: at the presence of the Lord of all the earth.€€ƒx?The heavens declared his justice: and all people saw his glory.€€ƒxrLet them be all confounded that adore graven things, and that glory in their idols. Adore him, all you his angels:€€‡ƒx`For thou art the most high Lord over all the earth: thou art exalted exceedingly above all gods.€€ƒxƒYou that love the Lord, hate evil: the Lord preserveth the souls of his saints, he will deliver them out of the hand of the sinner.€€ƒx:Light is risen to the just, and joy to the right of heart.€€ƒxRRejoice, ye just, in the Lord: and give praise to the remembrance of his holiness.€€ƒ`€€ƒx©A psalm for David himself. Sing ye to the Lord a new canticle: because he hath done wonderful things. His right hand hath wrought for him salvation, and his arm is holy.€€ƒxbThe Lord hath made known his salvation: he hath revealed his justice in the sight of the Gentiles.€€ŒƒxCSing joyfully to God, all the earth; make melody, rejoice and sing.€€ƒxPSing praise to the Lord on the harp, on the harp, and with the voice of a psalm:€€ƒxVWith long trumpets, and sound of cornet. Make a joyful noise before the Lord our king:€€ƒxULet the sea be moved and the fullness thereof: the world and they that dwell therein.€€ƒxGThe rivers shall clap their hands, the mountains shall rejoice together€€ƒx…At the presence of the Lord: because he cometh to judge the earth. He shall judge the world with justice, and the people with equity.€€ƒ`€€ƒx„A psalm for David himself. The Lord hath reigned, let the people be angry: he that sitteth on the cherubims: let the earth be moved.€€ƒx5The Lord is great in Sion, and high above all people.€€ƒxDLet them give praise to thy great name: for it is terrible and holy:€€ƒxsAnd the king's honour loveth judgment. Thou hast prepared directions: thou hast done judgment and justice in Jacob.€€ƒxCExalt ye the Lord our God, and adore his footstool, for it is holy.€€ƒxoHe spoke to them in the pillar of the cloud. They kept his testimonies, and the commandment which he gave them.€€ƒxuThou didst hear them, O Lord our God: thou wast a merciful God to them, and taking vengeance on all their inventions.€€ƒxXExalt ye the Lord our God, and adore at his holy mountain: for the Lord our God is holy.€€ƒ`€€ƒrA psalm of praise.€€ƒx{Sing joyfully to God, all the earth: serve ye the Lord with gladness. Come in before his presence with exceeding great joy.€€ƒxrKnow ye that the Lord he is God: he made us, and not we ourselves. We are his people and the sheep of his pasture.€€ƒxhGo ye into his gates with praise, into his courts with hymns: and give glory to him. Praise ye his name:€€ƒx_For the Lord is sweet, his mercy endureth for ever, and his truth to generation and generation.€€ƒ`€€ƒxWA psalm for David himself. Mercy and judgment I will sing to thee, O Lord: I will sing,€€ƒxˆAnd I will understand in the unspotted way, when thou shalt come to me. I walked in the innocence of my heart, in the midst of my house.€€ƒxRI will not set before my eyes any unjust thing: I hated the workers of iniquities.€€ƒxhThe perverse heart did not cleave to me: and the malignant, that turned aside from me, I would not know.€€ƒxThe man that in private detracted his neighbour, him did I persecute. With him that had a proud eye, and an unsatiable heart, I would not eat.€€•ƒx~He that worketh pride shall not dwell in the midst of my house: he that speaketh unjust things did not prosper before my eyes.€€ƒx…In the morning I put to death all the wicked of the land: that I might cut off all the workers of iniquity from the city of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxaThe prayer of the poor man, when he was anxious, and poured out his supplication before the Lord.€€ƒx5Hear, O Lord, my prayer: and let my cry come to thee.€€ƒx”Turn not away thy face from me: in the day when I am in trouble, incline thy ear to me. In what day soever I shall call upon thee, hear me speedily.€€ƒxWFor my days are vanished like smoke, and my bones are grown dry like fuel for the fire.€€ƒxRI am smitten as grass, and my heart is withered: because I forgot to eat my bread.€€ƒxCThrough the voice of my groaning, my bone hath cleaved to my flesh.€€ƒxVI am become like to a pelican of the wilderness: I am like a night raven in the house.€€ƒxEI have watched, and am become as a sparrow all alone on the housetop.€€ƒxYAll the day long my enemies reproached me: and they that praised me did swear against me.€€ƒxBFor I did eat ashes like bread, and mingled my drink with weeping.€€ƒxWBecause of thy anger and indignation: for having lifted me up thou hast thrown me down.€€ƒxBMy days have declined like a shadow, and I am withered like grass.€€ƒxIBut thou, O Lord, endurest for ever: and thy memorial to all generations.€€ƒxbThou shalt arise and have mercy on Sion: for it is time to have mercy on it, for the time is come.€€ƒx`For the stones thereof have pleased thy servants: and they shall have pity on the earth thereof.€€ƒxWAll the Gentiles shall fear thy name, O Lord, and all the kings of the earth thy glory.€€ƒxCFor the Lord hath built up Sion: and he shall be seen in his glory.€€ƒxXHe hath had regard to the prayer of the humble: and he hath not despised their petition.€€ŠƒxfBecause he hath looked forth from his high sanctuary: from heaven the Lord hath looked upon the earth.€€ƒxkThat he might hear the groans of them that are in fetters: that he might release the children of the slain:€€ƒxPThat they may declare the name of the Lord in Sion: and his praise in Jerusalem;€€ƒx@When the people assemble together, and kings, to serve the Lord.€€ƒxSHe answered him in the way of his strength: Declare unto me the fewness of my days.€€ƒxWCall me not away in the midst of my days: thy years are unto generation and generation.€€ƒx_In the beginning, O Lord, thou foundedst the earth: and the heavens are the works of thy hands.€€ƒx˜They shall perish but thou remainest: and all of them shall grow old like a garment: And as a vesture thou shalt change them, and they shall be changed.€€ƒx?But thou art always the selfsame, and thy years shall not fail.€€ƒxVThe children of thy servants shall continue and their seed shall be directed for ever.€€”ƒx`For David himself. Bless the Lord, O my soul: and let all that is within me bless his holy name.€€ƒxFBless the Lord, O my soul, and never forget all he hath done for thee.€€ƒx?Who forgiveth all thy iniquities: who healeth all thy diseases.€€ƒxUWho redeemeth thy life from destruction: who crowneth thee with mercy and compassion.€€ƒxXWho satisfieth thy desire with good things: thy youth shall be renewed like the eagle's.€€ƒx>The Lord doth mercies, and judgment for all that suffer wrong.€€ƒxJHe hath made his ways known to Moses: his wills to the children of Israel.€€ƒxMThe Lord is compassionate and merciful: longsuffering and plenteous in mercy.€€ƒx;He will not always be angry: nor will he threaten for ever.€€ƒx]He hath not dealt with us according to our sins: nor rewarded us according to our iniquities.€€ƒxuFor according to the height of the heaven above the earth: he hath strengthened his mercy towards them that fear him.€€ƒxSAs far as the east is from the west, so far hath he removed our iniquities from us.€€ƒx_As a father hath compassion on his children, so hath the Lord compassion on them that fear him:€€ƒx:For he knoweth our frame. He remembereth that we are dust:€€ƒxIMan's days are as grass, as the flower of the field so shall he flourish.€€ƒx[For the spirit shall pass in him, and he shall not be: and he shall know his place no more.€€ƒxBut the mercy of the Lord is from eternity and unto eternity upon them that fear him: And his justice unto children's children,€€ƒxMTo such as keep his covenant, And are mindful of his commandments to do them.€€ƒxQThe Lord hath prepared his throne in heaven: and his kingdom shall rule over all.€€ƒx€Bless the Lord, all ye his angels: you that are mighty in strength, and execute his word, hearkening to the voice of his orders.€€ƒx^Bless the Lord, all his works: in every place of his dominion, O my soul, bless thou the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx|For David himself. Bless the Lord, O my soul: O Lord my God, thou art exceedingly great. Thou hast put on praise and beauty:€€ƒx\And art clothed with light as with a garment. Who stretchest out the heaven like a pavilion:€€ƒx}Who coverest the higher rooms thereof with water. Who makest the clouds thy chariot: who walkest upon the wings of the winds.€€ƒx@Who makest thy angels spirits: and thy ministers a burning fire.€€ƒxWWho hast founded the earth upon its own bases: it shall not be moved for ever and ever.€€ƒxTThe deep like a garment is its clothing: above the mountains shall the waters stand.€€ƒxKAt thy rebuke they shall flee: at the voice of thy thunder they shall fear.€€ƒx]The mountains ascend, and the plains descend into the place which thou hast founded for them.€€ƒxcThou hast set a bound which they shall not pass over; neither shall they return to cover the earth.€€ƒx^Thou sendest forth springs in the vales: between the midst of the hills the waters shall pass.€€ƒxUAll the beasts of the field shall drink: the wild asses shall expect in their thirst.€€ƒxkOver them the birds of the air shall dwell: from the midst of the rocks they shall give forth their voices.€€‰ƒxoBringing forth grass for cattle, and herb for the service of men. That thou mayst bring bread out of the earth:€€ƒxAnd that wine may cheer the heart of man. That he may make the face cheerful with oil: and that bread may strengthen man's heart.€€ƒxXThe trees of the field shall be filled, and the cedars of Libanus which he hath planted:€€ƒxYThere the sparrows shall make their nests. The highest of them is the house of the heron.€€ƒxDThe high hills are a refuge for the harts, the rock for the irchins.€€ƒxBHe hath made the moon for seasons: the sun knoweth his going down.€€ƒx`Thou hast appointed darkness, and it is night: in it shall all the beasts of the woods go about:€€ƒxJThe young lions roaring after their prey, and seeking their meat from God.€€ƒxWThe sun ariseth, and they are gathered together: and they shall lie down in their dens.€€†ƒxkHow great are thy works, O Lord ? thou hast made all things in wisdom: the earth is filled with thy riches.€€ƒx{So is this great sea, which stretcheth wide its arms: there are creeping things without number: Creatures little and great.€€ƒxQThere the ships shall go. This sea dragon which thou hast formed to play therein.€€ƒx6All expect of thee that thou give them food in season.€€ƒxnWhat thou givest to them they shall gather up: when thou openest thy hand, they shall all be filled with good.€€ƒx’But if thou turnest away thy face, they shall be troubled: thou shalt take away their breath, and they shall fail, and shall return to their dust.€€dProv¤dnamehProverbsiosis_namedProvlabbreviationdProvhchapters˜˜!ƒx]He looketh upon the earth, and maketh it tremble: he troubleth the mountains, and they smoke.€€ƒx^I will sing to the Lord as long as I live: I will sing praise to my God while I have my being.€€ƒxHLet my speech be acceptable to him: but I will take delight in the Lord.€€ƒxrLet sinners be consumed out of the earth, and the unjust, so that they be no more: O my soul, bless thou the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxUGive glory to the Lord, and call upon his name: declare his deeds among the Gentiles.€€ƒxDSing to him, yea sing praises to him: relate all his wondrous works.€€ƒxLGlory ye in his holy name: let the heart of them rejoice that seek the Lord.€€ƒx>Seek ye the Lord, and be strengthened: seek his face evermore.€€ƒx^Remember his marvellous works which he hath done; his wonders, and the judgments of his mouth.€€ƒx>O ye seed of Abraham his servant; ye sons of Jacob his chosen.€€ƒx;He is the Lord our God: his judgments are in all the earth.€€ƒx`He hath remembered his covenant for ever: the word which he commanded to a thousand generations.€€ƒx0Which he made to Abraham; and his oath to Isaac:€€ƒxYAnd he appointed the same to Jacob for a law, and to Israel for an everlasting testament:€€ƒxMSaying: To thee will I give the land of Chanaan, the lot of your inheritance.€€ƒxHWhen they were but a small number: yea very few, and sojourners therein:€€ƒxNAnd they passed from nation to nation, and from one kingdom to another people.€€ƒxGHe suffered no man to hurt them: and he reproved kings for their sakes.€€ƒx8Touch ye not my anointed: and do no evil to my prophets.€€ƒxVAnd he called a famine upon the land: and he broke in pieces all the support of bread.€€ƒx<He sent a man before them: Joseph, who was sold for a slave.€€ƒx<They humbled his feet in fetters: the iron pierced his soul,€€ƒx7Until his word came. The word of the Lord inflamed him.€€ƒxWThe king sent, and he released him: the ruler of the people, and he set him at liberty.€€ƒxAHe made him master of his house, and ruler of all his possession.€€ƒxMThat he might instruct his princes as himself, and teach his ancients wisdom.€€ƒxJAnd Israel went into Egypt: and Jacob was a sojourner in the land of Cham.€€ƒxRAnd he increased his people exceedingly: and strengthened them over their enemies.€€ƒxTHe turned their heart to hate his people: and to deal deceitfully with his servants.€€ƒx<He sent Moses his servant: Aaron the man whom he had chosen.€€ƒxKHe gave them power to shew them signs, and his wonders in the land of Cham.€€ƒxAHe sent darkness, and made it obscure: and grieved not his words.€€–ƒxETheir land brought forth frogs, in the inner chambers of their kings.€€ƒxPHe spoke, and there came divers sorts of flies and sciniphs in all their coasts.€€ƒx7He gave them hail for rain, a burning fire in the land.€€ƒxgAnd he destroyed their vineyards and their fig trees: and he broke in pieces the trees of their coasts.€€ƒxMHe spoke, and the locust came, and the bruchus, of which there was no number.€€ƒxZAnd they devoured all the grass in their land, and consumed all the fruit of their ground.€€ƒxQAnd he slew all the firstborn in their land: the firstfruits of all their labour.€€ƒxgAnd he brought them out with silver and gold: and there was not among their tribes one that was feeble.€€ƒxFEgypt was glad when they departed: for the fear of them lay upon them.€€ƒxQHe spread a cloud for their protection, and fire to give them light in the night.€€ƒxLThey asked, and the quail came: and he filled them with the bread of heaven.€€ƒxGHe opened the rock, and waters flowed: rivers ran down in the dry land.€€ƒxPBecause he remembered his holy word, which he had spoken to his servant Abraham.€€ƒxGAnd he brought forth his people with joy, and his chosen with gladness.€€ƒxYAnd he gave them the lands of the Gentiles: and they possessed the labours of the people:€€ƒxCThat they might observe his justifications, and seek after his law.€€ƒ`€€ƒxHGive glory to the Lord, for he is good: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxNWho shall declare the powers of the Lord? who shall set forth all his praises?€€ƒxABlessed are they that keep judgment, and do justice at all times.€€ƒxNRemember us, O Lord, in the favour of thy people: visit us with thy salvation.€€ƒx†That we may see the good of thy chosen, that we may rejoice in the joy of thy nation: that thou mayst be praised with thy inheritance.€€˜#ƒx¤Our fathers understood not thy wonders in Egypt: they remembered not the multitude of thy mercies: And they provoked to wrath going up to the sea, even the Red Sea.€€ƒxNAnd he saved them for his own name's sake: that he might make his power known.€€ƒxgAnd he rebuked the Red Sea and it was dried up: and he led them through the depths, as in a wilderness.€€ƒxiAnd he saved them from the hand of them that hated them: and he redeemed them from the hand of the enemy.€€ƒxOAnd the water covered them that afflicted them: there was not one of them left.€€ƒx7And they believed his words: and they sang his praises.€€ƒxRThey had quickly done, they forgot his works: and they waited not for his counsel.€€ƒx]And they coveted their desire in the desert: and they tempted God in the place without water.€€ƒxBAnd he gave them their request: and sent fulness into their souls.€€ƒxDAnd they provoked Moses in the camp, Aaron the holy one of the Lord.€€ƒxQThe earth opened and swallowed up Dathan: and covered the congregation of Abiron.€€ƒxJAnd a fire was kindled in their congregation: the flame burned the wicked.€€ƒxAThey made also a calf in Horeb: and they adored the graven thing.€€ƒxKAnd they changed their glory into the likeness of a calf that eateth grass.€€ƒxDThey forgot God, who saved them, who had done great things in Egypt,€€ƒxCWondrous works in the land of Cham: terrible things in the Red Sea.€€ƒx•And he said that he would destroy them: had not Moses his chosen stood before him in the breach: To turn away his wrath, lest he should destroy them.€€ƒxFAnd they set at nought the desirable land. They believed not his word,€€ƒxNAnd they murmured in their tents: they hearkened not to the voice of the Lord.€€ƒxEAnd he lifted up his hand over them: to overthrow them in the desert;€€ƒxTAnd to cast down their seed among the nations, and to scatter them in the countries.€€ƒxKThey also were initiated to Beelphegor: and ate the sacrifices of the dead.€€ƒxWAnd they provoked him with their inventions: and destruction was multiplied among them.€€ƒxBThen Phinees stood up, and pacified him: and the slaughter ceased.€€ƒxRAnd it was reputed to him unto justice, to generation and generation for evermore.€€ƒx_They provoked him also at the waters of contradiction: and Moses was afflicted for their sakes:€€ƒxHBecause they exasperated his spirit. And he distinguished with his lips.€€ƒxCThey did not destroy the nations of which the Lord spoke unto them.€€ƒxBAnd they were mingled among the heathens, and learned their works:€€ƒx?And served their idols, and it became a stumblingblock to them.€€ƒx>And they sacrificed their sons, and their daughters to devils.€€ƒx¡And they shed innocent blood: the blood of their sons and of their daughters which they sacrificed to the idols of Chanaan. And the land was polluted with blood,€€ƒxQAnd was defiled with their works: and they went aside after their own inventions.€€ƒxTAnd the Lord was exceedingly angry with his people: and he abhorred his inheritance.€€ƒxeAnd he delivered them into the hands of the nations: and they that hated them had dominion over them.€€˜ƒxxMany times did he deliver them. But they provoked him with their counsel: and they were brought low by their iniquities.€€ƒxDAnd he saw when they were in tribulation: and he heard their prayer.€€ƒx[And he was mindful of his covenant: and repented according to the multitude of his mercies.€€ƒxUAnd he gave them unto mercies, in the sight of all those that had made them captives.€€ƒx†Save us, O Lord, our God: and gather us from among the nations: That we may give thanks to thy holy name, and may glory in thy praise.€€ƒxwBlessed be the Lord the God of Israel, from everlasting to everlasting: and let all the people say: So be it, so be it.€€ƒ`€€ƒxHGive glory to the Lord, for he is good: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒx‰Let them say so that have been redeemed by the Lord, whom he hath redeemed from the hand of the enemy: and gathered out of the countries.€€ƒxQFrom the rising and from the setting of the sun, from the north and from the sea.€€ƒxoThey wandered in a wilderness, in a place without water: they found not the way of a city for their habitation.€€ƒx9They were hungry and thirsty: their soul fainted in them.€€ƒx_And they cried to the Lord in their tribulation: and he delivered them out of their distresses.€€ƒxOAnd he led them into the right way, that they might go to a city of habitation.€€ƒx^Let the mercies of the Lord give glory to him: and his wonderful works to the children of men.€€ƒxWFor he hath satisfied the empty soul, and hath filled the hungry soul with good things.€€ƒxNSuch as sat in darkness and in the shadow of death: bound in want and in iron.€€ƒxYBecause they had exasperated the words of God: and provoked the counsel of the most High:€€ƒx^And their heart was humbled with labours: they were weakened, and there was none to help them.€€ƒx_Then they cried to the Lord in their affliction: and he delivered them out of their distresses.€€ƒx^And he brought them out of darkness, and the shadow of death; and broke their bonds in sunder.€€ƒx^Let the mercies of the Lord give glory to him, and his wonderful works to the children of men.€€ƒx;Because he hath broken gates of brass, and burst iron bars.€€ƒx^He took them out of the way of their iniquity: for they were brought low for their injustices.€€ƒxVTheir soul abhorred all manner of meat: and they drew nigh even to the gates of death.€€ƒx^And they cried to the Lord in their affliction: and he delivered them out of their distresses.€€ƒxNHe sent his word, and healed them: and delivered them from their destructions.€€—ƒxOAnd let them sacrifice the sacrifice of praise: and declare his works with joy.€€ƒxJThey that go down to the sea in ships, doing business in the great waters:€€ƒxCThese have seen the works of the Lord, and his wonders in the deep.€€ƒxXHe said the word, and there arose a storm of wind: and the waves thereof were lifted up.€€ƒx_They mount up to the heavens, and they go down to the depths: their soul pined away with evils.€€ƒxYThey were troubled, and reeled like a drunken man; and all their wisdom was swallowed up.€€ƒx\And they cried to the Lord in their affliction: and he brought them out of their distresses.€€ƒx@And he turned the storm into a breeze: and its waves were still.€€ƒxbAnd they rejoiced because they were still: and he brought them to the haven which they wished for.€€ƒx^Let the mercies of the Lord give glory to him, and his wonderful works to the children of men.€€ƒx`And let them exalt him in the church of the people: and praise him in the chair of the ancients.€€ƒxSHe hath turned rivers into a wilderness: and the sources of waters into dry ground:€€ƒxOA fruitful land into barrenness, for the wickedness of them that dwell therein.€€ƒxTHe hath turned a wilderness into pools of waters, and a dry land into water springs.€€ƒxLAnd hath placed there the hungry; and they made a city for their habitation.€€ƒxNAnd they sowed fields, and planted vineyards: and they yielded fruit of birth.€€ƒxhAnd he blessed them, and they were multiplied exceedingly: and their cattle he suffered not to decrease.€€ƒxbThen they were brought to be few: and they were afflicted through the trouble of evils and sorrow.€€ƒxzContempt was poured forth upon their princes: and he caused them to wander where there was no passing, and out of the way.€€ƒxSAnd he helped the poor out of poverty: and made him families like a flock of sheep.€€ƒxMThe just shall see, and shall rejoice, and all iniquity shall stop her mouth.€€ƒxUWho is wise, and will keep these things; and will understand the mercies of the Lord?€€ƒ`€€˜#ƒx^My heart is ready, O God, my heart is ready: I will sing, and will give praise, with my glory.€€ƒxMArise, my glory; arise, psaltery and harp: I will arise in the morning early.€€ƒxZI will praise thee, O Lord, among the people: and I will sing unto thee among the nations.€€ƒxMFor thy mercy is great above the heavens: and thy truth even unto the clouds.€€ƒxLBe thou exalted, O God, above the heavens, and thy glory over all the earth:€€ƒxHThat thy beloved may be delivered. Save with thy right hand and hear me.€€ƒxvGod hath spoken in his holiness. I will rejoice, and I will divide Sichem and I will mete out the vale of tabernacles.€€ƒx\Galaad is mine: and Manasses is mine and Ephraim the protection of my head. Juda is my king:€€ƒx`Moab the pot of my hope. Over Edom I will stretch out my shoe: the aliens are become my friends.€€ƒxCWho will bring me into the strong city? who will lead me into Edom?€€ƒx`Wilt not thou, O God, who hast cast us off ? and wilt not thou, O God, go forth with our armies?€€ƒx:O grant us help from trouble: for vain is the help of man.€€ƒxKThrough God we shall do mightily: and he will bring our enemies to nothing.€€ƒ`€€ƒx Unto the end, a psalm for David.€€ƒx|O God, be not thou silent in my praise: for the mouth of the wicked and the mouth of the deceitful man is opened against me.€€ƒx”They have spoken against me with deceitful tongues; and they have compassed me about with words of hatred; and have fought against me without cause.€€ƒxVInstead of making me a return of love, they detracted me: but I gave myself to prayer.€€ƒx9And they repaid me evil for good: and hatred for my love.€€ƒxHSet thou the sinner over him: and may the devil stand at his right hand.€€ƒxPWhen he is judged, may he go out condemned; and may his prayer be turned to sin.€€ƒx8May his days be few: and his bishopric let another take.€€ƒx5May his children be fatherless, and his wife a widow.€€ƒxbLet his children be carried about vagabonds, and beg; and let them be cast out of their dwellings.€€ƒxOMay the usurer search all his substance: and let strangers plunder his labours.€€ƒxIMay there be none to help him: nor none to pity his fatherless offspring.€€ƒxLMay his posterity be cut off; in one generation may his name be blotted out.€€ƒxyMay the iniquity of his fathers be remembered in the sight of the Lord: and let not the sin of his mother be blotted out.€€ƒxZMay they be before the Lord continually, and let the memory of them perish from the earth:€€ƒx(because he remembered not to shew mercy,€€ƒxYBut persecuted the poor man and the beggar; and the broken in heart, to put him to death.€€ƒxŞAnd he loved cursing, and it shall come unto him: and he would not have blessing, and it shall be far from him. And he put on cursing, like a garment: and it went in like water into his entrails, and like oil in his bones.€€ƒxlMay it be unto him like a garment which covereth him; and like a girdle with which he is girded continually.€€ƒx]This is the work of them who detract me before the Lord; and who speak evils against my soul.€€ƒxaBut thou, O Lord, do with me for thy name's sake: because thy mercy is sweet. Do thou deliver me,€€˜ƒxRI am taken away like the shadow when it declineth: and I am shaken off as locusts.€€ƒxGMy knees are weakened through fasting: and my flesh is changed for oil.€€ƒxLAnd I am become a reproach to them: they saw me and they shaked their heads.€€ƒx8Help me, O Lord my God; save me; according to thy mercy.€€ƒxMAnd let them know that this is thy hand: and that thou, O Lord, hast done it.€€ƒxsThey will curse and thou wilt bless: let them that rise up against me be confounded: but thy servant shall rejoice.€€ƒxtLet them that detract me be clothed with shame: and let them be covered with their confusion as with a double cloak.€€ƒx_I will give great thanks to the Lord with my mouth: and in the midst of many I will praise him.€€ƒxVBecause he hath stood at the right hand of the poor, to save my soul from persecutors.€€ƒ`€€ƒxoA psalm for David. The Lord said to my Lord: Sit thou at my right hand: Until I make thy enemies thy footstool.€€ƒxeThe Lord will send forth the sceptre of thy power out of Sion: rule thou in the midst of thy enemies.€€ƒxŠWith thee is the principality in the day of thy strength: in the brightness of the saints: from the womb before the day star I begot thee.€€ƒxoThe Lord hath sworn, and he will not repent: Thou art a priest for ever according to the order of Melchisedech.€€ƒxEThe Lord at thy right hand hath broken kings in the day of his wrath.€€ƒx`He shall judge among nations, he shall fill ruins: he shall crush the heads in the land of many.€€ƒxNHe shall drink of the torrent in the way: therefore shall he lift up the head.€€ƒ`€€ƒxeI will praise thee, O Lord, with my whole heart; in the council of the just, and in the congregation.€€ƒxGGreat are the works of the Lord: sought out according to all his wills.€€ƒxRHis work is praise and magnificence: and his justice continueth for ever and ever.€€ƒxVHe hath made a remembrance of his wonderful works, being a merciful and gracious Lord:€€ƒxVHe hath given food to them that fear him. He will be mindful for ever of his covenant:€€ƒx8He will shew forth to his people the power of his works.€€ƒxeThat he may give them the inheritance of the Gentiles: the works of his hands are truth and judgment.€€ƒxYAll his commandments are faithful: confirmed for ever and ever, made in truth and equity.€€ƒxnHe hath sent redemption to his people: he hath commanded his covenant for ever. Holy and terrible is his name:€€˜$ƒ`€€ƒx[Blessed is the man that feareth the Lord: he shall delight exceedingly in his commandments.€€ƒxVHis seed shall be mighty upon earth: the generation of the righteous shall be blessed.€€ƒxTGlory and wealth shall be in his house: and his justice remaineth for ever and ever.€€ƒx]To the righteous a light is risen up in darkness: he is merciful, and compassionate and just.€€ƒx]Acceptable is the man that sheweth mercy and lendeth: he shall order his words with judgment:€€ƒx'Because he shall not be moved for ever.€€ƒxyThe just shall be in everlasting remembrance: he shall not fear the evil hearing. His heart is ready to hope in the Lord:€€ƒxPHis heart is strengthened, he shall not be moved until he look over his enemies.€€ƒx|He hath distributed, he hath given to the poor: his justice remaineth for ever and ever: his horn shall be exalted in glory.€€ƒx}The wicked shall see, and shall be angry, he shall gnash with his teeth and pine away: the desire of the wicked shall perish.€€ƒ`€€ƒx=Praise the Lord, ye children: praise ye the name of the Lord.€€ƒxBBlessed be the name of the Lord, from henceforth now and for ever.€€ƒxeFrom the rising of the sun unto the going down of the same, the name of the Lord is worthy of praise.€€ƒxDThe Lord is high above all nations; and his glory above the heavens.€€ƒx1Who is as the Lord our God, who dwelleth on high:€€ƒx:and looketh down on the low things in heaven and in earth?€€ƒxQRaising up the needy from the earth, and lifting up the poor out of the dunghill:€€ƒxCThat he may place him with princes, with the princes of his people.€€ƒxMWho maketh a barren woman to dwell in a house, the joyful mother of children.€€ƒ`€€ƒxJWhen Israel went out of Egypt, the house of Jacob from a barbarous people:€€ƒx2Judea was made his sanctuary, Israel his dominion.€€ƒx-The sea saw and fled: Jordan was turned back.€€ƒxKThe mountains skipped like rams, and the hills like the lambs of the flock.€€ƒxbWhat ailed thee, O thou sea, that thou didst flee: and thou, O Jordan, that thou wast turned back?€€ƒxOYe mountains, that ye skipped like rams, and ye hills, like lambs of the flock?€€ƒxUAt the presence of the Lord the earth was moved, at the presence of the God of Jacob:€€ƒxUWho turned the rock into pools of water, and the stony hill into fountains of waters.€€ƒx9Not to us, O Lord, not to us; but to thy name give glory.€€ƒxZFor thy mercy, and for thy truth's sake: lest the Gentiles should say: Where is their God?€€ƒxFBut our God is in heaven: he hath done all things whatsoever he would.€€ƒxMThe idols of the Gentiles are silver and gold, the works of the hands of men.€€ƒx;They have mouths and speak not: they have eyes and see not.€€ƒx;They have ears and hear not: they have noses and smell not.€€’ƒxMLet them that make them become like unto them: and all such as trust in them.€€ƒxSThe house of Israel hath hoped in the Lord: he is their helper and their protector.€€ƒxRThe house of Aaron hath hoped in the Lord: he is their helper and their protector.€€ƒxWThey that fear the Lord have hoped in the Lord: he is their helper and their protector.€€ƒxThe Lord hath been mindful of us, and hath blessed us. He hath blessed the house of Israel: he hath blessed the house of Aaron.€€ƒx>He hath blessed all that fear the Lord, both little and great.€€ƒxFMay the Lord add blessings upon you: upon you, and upon your children.€€ƒx6Blessed be you of the Lord, who made heaven and earth.€€ƒxVThe heaven of heaven is the Lord's: but the earth he has given to the children of men.€€ƒxMThe dead shall not praise thee, O Lord: nor any of them that go down to hell.€€ƒxABut we that live bless the Lord: from this time now and for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒx@I have loved, because the Lord will hear the voice of my prayer.€€ƒxNBecause he hath inclined his ear unto me: and in my days I will call upon him.€€ƒxlThe sorrows of death have compassed me: and the perils of hell have found me. I met with trouble and sorrow:€€ƒx@And I called upon the name of the Lord. O Lord, deliver my soul.€€ƒx9The Lord is merciful and just, and our God sheweth mercy.€€ƒxJThe Lord is the keeper of little ones: I was humbled, and he delivered me.€€˜ ƒxSFor he hath delivered my soul from death: my eyes from tears, my feet from falling.€€ƒx1I will please the Lord in the land of the living.€€ƒ`€€ƒxNI have believed, therefore have I spoken; but I have been humbled exceedingly.€€ƒx)I said in my excess: Every man is a liar.€€ƒxPWhat shall I render to the Lord, for all the things that he hath rendered to me?€€ƒxPI will take the chalice of salvation; and I will call upon the name of the Lord.€€ƒx5I will pay my vows to the Lord before all his people:€€ƒx=Precious in the sight of the Lord is the death of his saints.€€ƒxgO Lord, for I am thy servant: I am thy servant, and the son of thy handmaid. Thou hast broken my bonds:€€ƒx\I will sacrifice to thee the sacrifice of praise, and I will call upon the name of the Lord.€€ƒx>I will pay my vows to the Lord in the sight of all his people:€€ƒxJIn the courts of the house of the Lord, in the midst of thee, O Jerusalem.€€ƒ`€€ƒx=O Praise the Lord, all ye nations: praise him, all ye people.€€ƒxQFor his mercy is confirmed upon us: and the truth of the Lord remaineth for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒxIGive praise to the Lord, for he is good: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxFLet Israel now say, that he is good: that his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxALet the house of Aaron now say, that his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxFLet them that fear the Lord now say, that his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxMIn my trouble I called upon the Lord: and the Lord heard me, and enlarged me.€€ƒx?The Lord is my helper: I will not fear what man can do unto me.€€ƒx7The Lord is my helper: and I will look over my enemies.€€ƒxIIt is good to confide in the Lord, rather than to have confidence in man.€€ƒxAIt is good to trust in the Lord, rather than to trust in princes.€€ƒxZAll nations compassed me about; and, in the name of the Lord I have been revenged on them.€€ƒxaSurrounding me they compassed me about: and in the name of the Lord I have been revenged on them.€€ƒxyThey surrounded me like bees, and they burned like fire among thorns: and in the name of the Lord I was revenged on them.€€ƒxKBeing pushed I was overturned that I might fall: but the Lord supported me.€€ƒxEThe Lord is my strength and my praise: and he is become my salvation.€€ƒxJThe voice of rejoicing and of salvation is in the tabernacles of the just.€€˜ƒxCI shall not die, but live: and shall declare the works of the Lord.€€ƒxRThe Lord chastising hath chastised me: but he hath not delivered me over to death.€€ƒxVOpen ye to me the gates of justice: I will go in to them, and give praise to the Lord.€€ƒx;This is the gate of the Lord, the just shall enter into it.€€ƒxRI will give glory to thee because thou hast heard me: and art become my salvation.€€ƒxQThe stone which the builders rejected; the same is become the head of the corner.€€ƒx:This is the Lord's doing, and it is wonderful in our eyes.€€ƒxMThis is the day which the Lord hath made: let us be glad and rejoice therein.€€ƒx+O Lord, save me: O Lord, give good success.€€ƒxdBlessed be he that cometh in the name of the Lord. We have blessed you out of the house of the Lord.€€ƒxsThe Lord is God, and he hath shone upon us. Appoint a solemn day, with shady boughs, even to the horn of the altar.€€ƒxThou art my God, and I will praise thee: thou art my God, and I will exalt thee. I will praise thee, because thou hast heard me, and art become my salvation.€€ƒxFO praise ye the Lord, for he is good: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒxFBlessed are the undefiled in the way, who walk in the law of the Lord.€€ƒxSBlessed are they that search his testimonies: that seek him with their whole heart.€€ƒx9For they that work iniquity, have not walked in his ways.€€ƒx@Thou hast commanded thy commandments to be kept most diligently.€€ƒx;O! that my ways may be directed to keep thy justifications.€€ƒxLThen shall I not be confounded, when I shall look into all thy commandments.€€ƒxeI will praise thee with uprightness of heart, when I shall have learned the judgments of thy justice.€€ƒxBI will keep thy justifications: O! do not thou utterly forsake me.€€ƒxABy what doth a young man correct his way? by observing thy words.€€ƒxUWith my whole heart have I sought after thee: let me not stray from thy commandments.€€ƒxEThy words have I hidden in my heart, that I may not sin against thee.€€ƒx6Blessed art thou, O Lord: teach me thy justifications.€€ƒx>With my lips I have pronounced all the judgments of thy mouth.€€ƒxFI have been delighted in the way of thy testimonies, as in all riches.€€ƒxBI will meditate on thy commandments: and I will consider thy ways.€€ƒx@I will think of thy justifications: I will not forget thy words.€€ƒxHGive bountifully to thy servant, enliven me: and I shall keep thy words.€€˜ƒxAI am a sojourner on the earth: hide not thy commandments from me.€€ƒxBMy soul hath coveted to long for thy justifications, at all times.€€ƒxOThou hast rebuked the proud: they are cursed who decline from thy commandments.€€ƒxRRemove from me reproach and contempt: because I have sought after thy testimonies.€€ƒxZFor princes sat, and spoke against me: but thy servant was employed in thy justifications.€€ƒxIFor thy testimonies are my meditation: and thy justifications my counsel.€€ƒxLMy soul hath cleaved to the pavement: quicken thou me according to thy word.€€ƒxMI have declared my ways, and thou hast heard me: teach me thy justifications.€€ƒxdMake me to understand the way of thy justifications: and I shall be exercised in thy wondrous works.€€ƒxJMy soul hath slumbered through heaviness: strengthen thou me in thy words.€€ƒxHRemove from me the way of iniquity: and out of thy law have mercy on me.€€ƒxCI have chosen the way of truth: thy judgments I have not forgotten.€€ƒx=I have stuck to thy testimonies, O Lord: put me not to shame.€€ƒxII have run the way of thy commandments, when thou didst enlarge my heart.€€ƒx_Set before me for a law the way of thy justifications, O Lord: and I will always seek after it.€€ƒxYGive me understanding, and I will search thy law; and I will keep it with my whole heart.€€ƒxHLead me into the path of thy commandments; for this same I have desired.€€ƒx>Incline my heart into thy testimonies and not to covetousness.€€ƒxITurn away my eyes that they may not behold vanity: quicken me in thy way.€€ƒx/Establish thy word to thy servant, in thy fear.€€ƒxRTurn away my reproach, which I have apprehended: for thy judgments are delightful.€€ƒxCBehold I have longed after thy precepts: quicken me in thy justice.€€ƒxMLet thy mercy also come upon me, O Lord: thy salvation according to thy word.€€ƒxWSo shall I answer them that reproach me in any thing; that I have trusted in thy words.€€ƒxhAnd take not thou the word of truth utterly out of my mouth: for in thy words, I have hoped exceedingly.€€ƒx2So shall I always keep thy law, for ever and ever.€€ƒxDAnd I walked at large: because I have sought after thy commandments.€€ƒxCAnd I spoke of thy testimonies before kings: and I was not ashamed.€€˜ƒxgAnd I lifted up my hands to thy commandments, which I loved: and I was exercised in thy justifications.€€ƒxMBe thou mindful of thy word to thy servant, in which thou hast given me hope.€€ƒxMThis hath comforted me in my humiliation: because thy word hath enlivened me.€€ƒxGThe proud did iniquitously altogether: but I declined not from thy law.€€ƒx@I remembered, O Lord, thy judgments of old: and I was comforted.€€ƒxMA fainting hath taken hold of me, because of the wicked that forsake thy law.€€ƒxNThy justifications were the subject of my song, in the place of my pilgrimage.€€ƒxGIn the night I have remembered thy name, O Lord: and have kept thy law.€€ƒx?This happened to me: because I sought after thy justifications.€€ƒx6O Lord, my portion, I have said, I would keep thy law.€€ƒxOI entreated thy face with all my heart: have mercy on me according to thy word.€€ƒxCI have thought on my ways: and turned my feet unto thy testimonies.€€ƒxBI am ready, and am not troubled: that I may keep thy commandments.€€ƒxNThe cords of the wicked have encompassed me: but I have not forgotten thy law.€€ƒxRI rose at midnight to give praise to thee; for the judgments of thy justification.€€ƒxMI am a partaker with all them that fear thee, and that keep thy commandments.€€ƒxEThe earth, O Lord, is full of thy mercy: teach me thy justifications.€€ƒxDThou hast done well with thy servant, O Lord, according to thy word.€€ƒxUTeach me goodness and discipline and knowledge; for I have believed thy commandments.€€ƒx@Before I was humbled I offended; therefore have I kept thy word.€€ƒx?Thou art good; and in thy goodness teach me thy justifications.€€ƒxmThe iniquity of the proud hath been multiplied over me: but I will seek thy commandments with my whole heart.€€ƒxBTheir heart is curdled like milk: but I have meditated on thy law.€€ƒxQIt is good for me that thou hast humbled me, that I may learn thy justifications.€€ƒxGThe law of thy mouth is good to me, above thousands of gold and silver.€€˜#ƒx`They that fear thee shall see me, and shall be glad : because I have greatly hoped in thy words.€€ƒxUI know, O Lord, that thy judgments are equity: and in thy truth thou hast humbled me.€€ƒxKO! let thy mercy be for my comfort, according to thy word unto thy servant.€€ƒxTLet thy tender mercies come unto me, and I shall live: for thy law is my meditation.€€ƒxqLet the proud be ashamed, because they have done unjustly towards me: but I will be employed in thy commandments.€€ƒxGLet them that fear thee turn to me: and they that know thy testimonies.€€ƒxNLet my heart be undefiled in thy justifications, that I may not be confounded.€€ƒxQMy soul hath fainted after thy salvation: and in thy word I have very much hoped.€€ƒxDMy eyes have failed for thy word, saying: When wilt thou comfort me?€€ƒxTFor I am become like a bottle in the frost: I have not forgotten thy justifications.€€ƒx`How many are the days of thy servant: when wilt thou execute judgment on them that persecute me?€€ƒx3The wicked have told me fables: but not as thy law.€€ƒxNAll thy statutes are truth: they have persecuted me unjustly, do thou help me.€€ƒxWThey had almost made an end of me upon earth: but I have not forsaken thy commandments.€€ƒxVQuicken thou me according to thy mercy: and I shall keep the testimonies of thy mouth.€€ƒx3For ever, O Lord, thy word standeth firm in heaven.€€ƒxOThy truth unto all generations: thou hast founded the earth, and it continueth.€€ƒx=By thy ordinance the day goeth on: for all things serve thee.€€ƒxSUnless thy law had been my meditation, I had then perhaps perished in my abjection.€€ƒxLThy justifications I will never forget: for by them thou hast given me life.€€ƒx?I am thine, save thou me: for I have sought thy justifications.€€ƒxSThe wicked have waited for me to destroy me: but I have understood thy testimonies.€€ƒxII have seen an end of all perfection: thy commandment is exceeding broad.€€ƒxDO how have I loved thy law, O Lord! it is my meditation all the day.€€ƒxYThrough thy commandment, thou hast made me wiser than my enemies: for it is ever with me.€€ƒxWI have understood more than all my teachers: because thy testimonies are my meditation.€€ƒxPI have had understanding above ancients: because I have sought thy commandments.€€ƒxII have restrained my feet from every evil way: that I may keep thy words.€€ƒxGI have not declined from thy judgments, because thou hast set me a law.€€ƒxBHow sweet are thy words to my palate! more than honey to my mouth.€€ƒx[By thy commandments I have had understanding: therefore have I hated every way of iniquity.€€ƒx7Thy word is a lamp to my feet, and a light to my paths.€€ƒxDI have sworn and am determined to keep the judgments of thy justice.€€ƒxPI have been humbled, O Lord, exceedingly: quicken thou me according to thy word.€€ƒxSThe free offerings of my mouth make acceptable, O Lord: and teach me thy judgments.€€˜!ƒxISinners have laid a snare for me: but I have not erred from thy precepts.€€ƒxcI have purchased thy testimonies for an inheritance for ever: because they are the joy of my heart.€€ƒxKI have inclined my heart to do thy justifications for ever, for the reward.€€ƒx0I have hated the unjust: and have loved thy law.€€ƒxJThou art my helper and my protector: and in thy word I have greatly hoped.€€ƒxKDepart from me, ye malignant: and I will search the commandments of my God.€€ƒxbUphold me according to thy word, and I shall live: and let me not be confounded in my expectation.€€ƒxPHelp me, and I shall be saved: and I will meditate always on thy justifications.€€ƒxZThou hast despised all them that fall off from thy judgments; for their thought is unjust.€€ƒxdI have accounted all the sinners of the earth prevaricators: therefore have I loved thy testimonies.€€ƒxEPierce thou my flesh with thy fear: for I am afraid of thy judgments.€€ƒxII have done judgment and justice: give me not up to them that slander me.€€ƒx>Uphold thy servant unto good: let not the proud calumniate me.€€ƒxJMy eyes have fainted after thy salvation: and for the word of thy justice.€€ƒxNDeal with thy servant according to thy mercy: and teach me thy justifications.€€ƒxHI am thy servant: give me understanding that I may know thy testimonies.€€ƒx8It is time, O Lord, to do: they have dissipated thy law.€€ƒxATherefore have I loved thy commandments above gold and the topaz.€€ƒxOTherefore was I directed to all thy commandments: I have hated all wicked ways.€€ƒxBThy testimonies are wonderful: therefore my soul hath sought them.€€ƒxSThe declaration of thy words giveth light: and giveth understanding to little ones.€€ƒxEI opened my mouth, and panted: because I longed for thy commandments.€€ƒx]Look thou upon me, and have mercy on me according to the judgment of them that love thy name.€€ƒxQDirect my steps according to thy word: and let no iniquity have dominion over me.€€ƒxFRedeem me from the calumnies of men: that I may keep thy commandments.€€ƒxIMake thy face to shine upon thy servant: and teach me thy justifications.€€ƒxMMy eyes have sent forth springs of water: because they have not kept thy law.€€ƒx1Thou art just, O Lord: and thy judgment is right.€€ƒxGThou hast commanded justice thy testimonies: and thy truth exceedingly.€€ƒxDMy zeal hath made me pine away: because my enemies forgot thy words.€€ƒx?Thy word is exceedingly refined: and thy servant hath loved it.€€ƒxBI am very young and despised; but I forget not thy justifications.€€ƒx:Thy justice is justice for ever: and thy law is the truth.€€˜!ƒxNThy testimonies are justice for ever: give me understanding, and I shall live.€€ƒxMI cried with my whole heart, hear me, O Lord: I will seek thy justifications.€€ƒx=I cried unto thee, save me: that I may keep thy commandments.€€ƒxVI prevented the dawning of the day, and cried: because in thy words I very much hoped.€€ƒxOMy eyes to thee have prevented the morning: that I might meditate on thy words.€€ƒx]Hear thou my voice, O Lord, according to thy mercy: and quicken me according to thy judgment.€€ƒx[They that persecute me have drawn nigh to iniquity; but they are gone far off from thy law.€€ƒx2Thou art near, O Lord: and all thy ways are truth.€€ƒxaI have known from the beginning concerning thy testimonies: that thou hast founded them for ever.€€ƒxCSee my humiliation and deliver me for I have not forgotten thy law.€€ƒxEJudge my judgment and redeem me: quicken thou me for thy word's sake.€€ƒxOSalvation is far from sinners; because they have not sought thy justifications.€€ƒxDMany, O Lord, are thy mercies: quicken me according to thy judgment.€€ƒx]Many are they that persecute me and afflict me; but I have not declined from thy testimonies.€€ƒxKI beheld the transgressors, and pined away; because they kept not thy word.€€ƒxKBehold I have loved thy commandments, O Lord; quicken me thou in thy mercy.€€ƒxSThe beginning of thy words is truth: all the judgments of thy justice are for ever.€€ƒxUPrinces have persecuted me without cause: and my heart hath been in awe of thy words.€€ƒx@I will rejoice at thy words, as one that hath found great spoil.€€ƒx=I have hated and abhorred iniquity; but I have loved thy law.€€ƒxPSeven times a day I have given praise to thee, for the judgments of thy justice.€€ƒxPMuch peace have they that love thy law, and to them there is no stumbling block.€€ƒxAI looked for thy salvation, O Lord: and I loved thy commandments.€€ƒxBMy soul hath kept thy testimonies and hath loved them exceedingly.€€ƒxWI have kept thy commandments and thy testimonies: because all my ways are in thy sight.€€ƒxaLet my supplication, O Lord, come near in thy sight: give me understanding according to thy word.€€ƒxJLet my request come in before thee; deliver thou me according to thy word.€€ƒxHMy lips shall utter a hymn, when thou shalt teach me thy justifications.€€ƒxMMy tongue shall pronounce thy word: because all thy commandments are justice.€€ƒxCLet thy hand be with me to save me; for I have chosen thy precepts.€€ƒxFI have longed for thy salvation, O Lord; and thy law is my meditation.€€ƒxJMy soul shall live and shall praise thee: and thy judgments shall help me.€€ƒxnI have gone astray like a sheep that is lost: seek thy servant, because I have not forgotten thy commandments.€€˜ƒx3In my trouble I cried to the Lord: and he heard me.€€ƒxAO Lord, deliver my soul from wicked lips, and a deceitful tongue.€€ƒxSWhat shall be given to thee, or what shall be added to thee, to a deceitful tongue?€€ƒx:The sharp arrows of the mighty, with coals that lay waste.€€ƒxWWoe is me, that my sojourning is prolonged! I have dwelt with the inhabitants of Cedar:€€ƒx#My soul hath been long a sojourner.€€ƒxfWith them that hated peace I was peaceable: when I spoke to them they fought against me without cause.€€ƒ`€€ƒxMI have lifted up my eyes to the mountains, from whence help shall come to me.€€ƒx4My help is from the Lord, who made heaven and earth.€€ƒxRMay he not suffer thy foot to be moved: neither let him slumber that keepeth thee.€€ƒx?Behold he shall neither slumber nor sleep, that keepeth Israel.€€ƒxGThe Lord is thy keeper, the Lord is thy protection upon thy right hand.€€ƒx:The sun shall not burn thee by day: nor the moon by night.€€ƒx@The Lord keepeth thee from all evil: may the Lord keep thy soul.€€ƒxTMay the Lord keep thy coming in and thy going out; from henceforth now and for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒxjA gradual canticle. I rejoiced at the things that were said to me: We shall go into the house of the Lord.€€ƒx2Our feet were standing in thy courts, O Jerusalem.€€ƒx?Jerusalem, which is built as a city, which is compact together.€€ƒxrFor thither did the tribes go up, the tribes of the Lord: the testimony of Israel, to praise the name of the Lord.€€ƒxHBecause their seats have sat in judgment, seats upon the house of David.€€ƒxbPray ye for the things that are for the peace of Jerusalem: and abundance for them that love thee.€€ƒx:Let peace be in thy strength: and abundance in thy towers.€€ƒxIFor the sake of my brethren, and of my neighbours, I spoke peace of thee.€€ƒxMBecause of the house of the Lord our God, I have sought good things for thee.€€ƒ`€€ƒx9To thee have I lifted up my eyes, who dwellest in heaven.€€˜ƒxTHave mercy on us, O Lord, have mercy on us: for we are greatly filled with contempt.€€ƒxYFor our soul is greatly filled: we are a reproach to the rich, and contempt to the proud.€€ƒ`€€ƒxUA gradual canticle. If it had not been that the Lord was with us, let Israel now say:€€ƒxJIf it had not been that the Lord was with us, When men rose up against us,€€ƒxQPerhaps they had swallowed us up alive. When their fury was enkindled against us,€€ƒx'Perhaps the waters had swallowed us up.€€ƒxbOur soul hath passed through a torrent: perhaps our soul had passed through a water insupportable.€€ƒxGBlessed be the Lord, who hath not given us to be a prey to their teeth.€€ƒxuOur soul hath been delivered as a sparrow out of the snare of the fowlers. The snare is broken, and we are delivered.€€ƒx?Our help is in the name of the Lord, who made heaven and earth.€€ƒ`€€ƒxtA gradual canticle. They that trust in the Lord shall be as mount Sion: he shall not be moved for ever that dwelleth€€ƒxsIn Jerusalem. Mountains are round about it: so the Lord is round about his people from henceforth now and for ever.€€ƒx…For the Lord will not leave the rod of sinners upon the lot of the just: that the just may not stretch forth their hands to iniquity.€€ƒxEDo good, O Lord, to those that are good, and to the upright of heart.€€ƒxkBut such as turn aside into bonds, the Lord shall lead out with the workers of iniquity: peace upon Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxcA gradual canticle. When the Lord brought back the captivity of Sion, we became like men comforted.€€ƒx“Then was our mouth filled with gladness; and our tongue with joy. Then shall they say among the Gentiles: The Lord hath done great things for them.€€ƒx=The Lord hath done great things for us: we are become joyful.€€ƒx;Turn again our captivity, O Lord, as a stream in the south.€€ƒx)They that sow in tears shall reap in joy.€€ƒx.Going they went and wept, casting their seeds.€€˜ƒ`€€ƒx¦A gradual canticle of Solomon. Unless the Lord build the house, they labour in vain that build it. Unless the Lord keep the city, he watcheth in vain that keepeth it.€€ƒx’It is vain for you to rise before light, rise ye after you have sitten, you that eat the bread of sorrow. When he shall give sleep to his beloved,€€ƒxSBehold the inheritance of the Lord are children: the reward, the fruit of the womb.€€ƒxSAs arrows in the hand of the mighty, so the children of them that have been shaken.€€ƒx„Blessed is the man that hath filled the desire with them; he shall not be confounded when he shall speak to his enemies in the gate.€€ƒ`€€ƒxSA gradual canticle. Blessed are all they that fear the Lord: that walk in his ways.€€ƒx^For thou shalt eat the labours of thy hands: blessed art thou, and it shall be well with thee.€€ƒxlThy wife as a fruitful vine, on the sides of thy house. Thy children as olive plants, round about thy table.€€ƒx<Behold, thus shall the man be blessed that feareth the Lord.€€ƒxnMay the Lord bless thee out of Sion: and mayst thou see the good things of Jerusalem all the days of thy life.€€ƒx>And mayst thou see thy children's children, peace upon Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒxXA gradual canticle. Often have they fought against me from my youth, let Israel now say.€€ƒxTOften have they fought against me from my youth: but they could not prevail over me.€€ƒxJThe wicked have wrought upon my back: they have lengthened their iniquity.€€ƒx3The Lord who is just will cut the necks of sinners:€€ƒx:Let them all be confounded and turned back that hate Sion.€€ƒxVLet them be as grass upon the tops of houses: which withereth before it be plucked up:€€ƒxQWho with the mower filleth not his hand: nor he that gathereth sheaves his bosom.€€ƒxyAnd they that passed by have not said: The blessing of the Lord be upon you: we have blessed you in the name of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxCA gradual canticle. Out of the depths I have cried to thee, O Lord:€€ƒxOLord, hear my voice. Let thy ears be attentive to the voice of my supplication.€€ƒx@If thou, O Lord, wilt mark iniquities: Lord, who shall stand it.€€ƒx‡For with thee there is merciful forgiveness: and by reason of thy law, I have waited for thee, O Lord. My soul hath relied on his word:€€ƒxmy soul hath hoped in the Lord.€€ƒxEFrom the morning watch even until night, let Israel hope in the Lord.€€˜ƒx3And he shall redeem Israel from all his iniquities.€€ƒ`€€ƒxœA gradual canticle of David. Lord, my heart is not exalted: nor are my eyes lofty. Neither have I walked in great matters, nor in wonderful things above me.€€ƒxwIf I was not humbly minded, but exalted my soul: As a child that is weaned is towards his mother, so reward in my soul.€€ƒx>Let Israel hope in the Lord, from henceforth now and for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAA gradual canticle. O Lord, remember David, and all his meekness.€€ƒx=How he swore to the Lord, he vowed a vow to the God of Jacob:€€ƒx^If I shall enter into the tabernacle of my house: if I shall go up into the bed wherein I lie:€€ƒx;If I shall give sleep to my eyes, or slumber to my eyelids,€€ƒx`Or rest to my temples: until I find out a place for the Lord, a tabernacle for the God of Jacob.€€ƒxRBehold we have heard of it in Ephrata: we have found it in the fields of the wood.€€ƒxPWe will go into his tabernacle: we will adore in the place where his feet stood.€€ƒxTArise, O Lord, into thy resting place: thou and the ark, which thou hast sanctified.€€ƒxDLet thy priests be clothed with justice: and let thy saints rejoice.€€ƒxEFor thy servant David's sake, turn not away the face of thy anointed.€€ƒxvThe Lord hath sworn truth to David, and he will not make it void: of the fruit of thy womb I will set upon thy throne.€€ƒx•If thy children will keep my covenant, and these my testimonies which I shall teach them: Their children also for evermore shall sit upon thy throne.€€ƒxBFor the Lord hath chosen Sion: he hath chosen it for his dwelling.€€ƒxKThis is my rest for ever and ever: here will I dwell, for I have chosen it.€€ƒxDBlessing I will bless her widow: I will satisfy her poor with bread.€€ƒx`I will clothe her priests with salvation, and her saints shall rejoice with exceeding great joy.€€ƒxQThere will I bring forth a horn to David: I have prepared a lamp for my anointed.€€ƒxXHis enemies I will clothe with confusion: but upon him shall my sanctification flourish.€€ƒ`€€ƒxlA gradual canticle of David. Behold how good and how pleasant it is for brethren to dwell together in unity:€€ƒx…Like the precious ointment on the head, that ran down upon the beard, the beard of Aaron, Which ran down to the skirt of his garment:€€ƒxzAs the dew of Hermon, which descendeth upon mount Sion. For there the Lord hath commanded blessing, and life for evermore.€€ƒ`€€ƒx™A gradual canticle. Behold now bless ye the Lord, all ye servants of the Lord: Who stand in the house of the Lord, in the courts of the house of our God.€€˜ƒxCMay the Lord out of Sion bless thee, he that made heaven and earth.€€ƒ`€€ƒxNAlleluia. Praise ye the name of the Lord: O you his servants, praise the Lord:€€ƒxOYou that stand in the house of the Lord, in the courts of the house of our God.€€ƒxOPraise ye the Lord, for the Lord is good: sing ye to his name, for it is sweet.€€ƒxKFor the Lord hath chosen Jacob unto himself: Israel for his own possession.€€ƒxGFor I have known that the Lord is great, and our God is above all gods.€€ƒx`Whatsoever the Lord pleased he hath done, in heaven, in earth, in the sea, and in all the deeps.€€ƒxHe bringeth up clouds from the end of the earth: he hath made lightnings for the rain. He bringeth forth winds out of his stores:€€ƒx8He slew the firstborn of Egypt from man even unto beast.€€ƒxfHe sent forth signs and wonders in the midst of thee, O Egypt: upon Pharao, and upon all his servants.€€ƒx-He smote many nations, and slew mighty kings:€€ƒxTSehon king of the Amorrhites, and Og king of Basan, and all the kingdoms of Chanaan.€€ƒxPAnd gave their land for an inheritance, for an inheritance to his people Israel.€€ƒxJThy name, O Lord, is for ever: thy memorial, O Lord, unto all generations.€€ƒxTFor the Lord will judge his people, and will be entreated in favour of his servants.€€ƒxHThe idols of the Gentiles are silver and gold, the works of men's hands.€€ƒxHThey have a mouth, but they speak not: they have eyes, but they see not.€€ƒxOThey have ears, but they hear not: neither is there any breath in their mouths.€€ƒxMLet them that make them be like to them: and every one that trusteth in them.€€ƒxDBless the Lord, O house of Israel: bless the Lord, O house of Aaron.€€ƒxHBless the Lord, O house of Levi: you that fear the Lord, bless the Lord.€€ƒx;Blessed be the Lord out of Sion, who dwelleth in Jerusalem.€€ƒ`€€ƒxKAlleluia. Praise the Lord, for he is good: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒx;Praise ye the God of gods: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒx=Praise ye the Lord of lords: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒx>Who alone doth great wonders: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxGWho made the heavens in understanding: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxLWho established the earth above the waters: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒx;Who made the great lights: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€˜ƒxJThe moon and the stars to rule the night: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxFWho smote Egypt with their firstborn: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxHWho brought out Israel from among them: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxQWith a mighty hand and with a stretched out arm: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxDWho divided the Red Sea into parts: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxRAnd brought out Israel through the midst thereof: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxRAnd overthrew Pharao and his host in the Red Sea: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxGWho led his people through the desert: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒx7Who smote great kings: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒx7And slew strong kings: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒx>Sehon king of the Amorrhites: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒx6And Og king of Basan: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxKAnd he gave their land for an inheritance: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxJFor an inheritance to his servant Israel: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxLFor he was mindful of us in our affliction: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxEAnd he redeemed us from our enemies: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒx>Who giveth food to all flesh: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxAGive glory to the God of heaven: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒxAGive glory to the Lord of lords: for his mercy endureth for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒxKUpon the rivers of Babylon, there we sat and wept: when we remembered Sion:€€ƒx?On the willows in the midst thereof we hung up our instruments.€€ƒxœFor there they that led us into captivity required of us the words of songs. And they that carried us away, said: Sing ye to us a hymn of the songs of Sion.€€ƒx9How shall we sing the song of the Lord in a strange land?€€ƒx>If I forget thee, O Jerusalem, let my right hand be forgotten.€€ƒxlLet my tongue cleave to my jaws, if I do not remember thee: If I make not Jerusalem the beginning of my joy.€€ƒx{Remember, O Lord, the children of Edom, in the day of Jerusalem: Who say: Rase it, rase it, even to the foundation thereof.€€ƒxoO daughter of Babylon, miserable: blessed shall he be who shall repay thee thy payment which thou hast paid us.€€ƒxHBlessed be he that shall take and dash thy little ones against the rock.€€˜#ƒx¥For David himself. I will praise thee, O Lord, with my whole heart: for thou hast heard the words of my mouth. I will sing praise to thee in the sight of the angels:€€ƒxI will worship towards thy holy temple, and I will give glory to thy name. For thy mercy, and for thy truth: for thou hast magnified thy holy name above all.€€ƒx\In what day soever I shall call upon thee, hear me: thou shalt multiply strength in my soul.€€ƒxbMay all the kings of the earth give glory to thee: for they have heard all the words of thy mouth.€€ƒxNAnd let them sing in the ways of the Lord: for great is the glory of the Lord.€€ƒxOFor the Lord is high, and looketh on the low: and the high he knoweth afar off.€€ƒx¬If I shall walk in the midst of tribulation, thou wilt quicken me: and thou hast stretched forth thy hand against the wrath of my enemies: and thy right hand hath saved me.€€ƒxgThe Lord will repay for me: thy mercy, O Lord, endureth for ever: O despise not the works of thy hands.€€ƒ`€€ƒxHUnto the end, a psalm of David. Lord, thou hast proved me, and known me:€€ƒx2Thou hast known my sitting down, and my rising up.€€ƒxVThou hast understood my thoughts afar off: my path and my line thou hast searched out.€€ƒxHAnd thou hast foreseen all my ways: for there is no speech in my tongue.€€ƒx{Behold, O Lord, thou hast known all things, the last and those of old: thou hast formed me, and hast laid thy hand upon me.€€ƒxNThy knowledge is become wonderful to me: it is high, and I cannot reach to it.€€ƒxJWhither shall I go from thy spirit? or whither shall I flee from thy face?€€ƒxRIf I ascend into heaven, thou art there: if I descend into hell, thou art present.€€ƒxUIf I take my wings early in the morning, and dwell in the uttermost parts of the sea:€€ƒxIEven there also shall thy hand lead me: and thy right hand shall hold me.€€ƒxYAnd I said: Perhaps darkness shall cover me: and night shall be my light in my pleasures.€€ƒxŒBut darkness shall not be dark to thee, and night shall be light all the day: the darkness thereof, and the light thereof are alike to thee.€€ƒxOFor thou hast possessed my reins: thou hast protected me from my mother's womb.€€ƒxnI will praise thee, for thou art fearfully magnified: wonderful are thy works, and my soul knoweth right well.€€ƒxrMy bone is not hidden from thee, which thou hast made in secret: and my substance in the lower parts of the earth.€€ƒxtThy eyes did see my imperfect being, and in thy book all shall be written: days shall be formed, and no one in them.€€ƒxnBut to me thy friends, O God, are made exceedingly honourable: their principality is exceedingly strengthened.€€ƒxbI will number them, and they shall be multiplied above the sand, I rose up and am still with thee.€€ƒxEIf thou wilt kill the wicked, O God: ye men of blood, depart from me:€€ƒxBBecause you say in thought: They shall receive thy cities in vain.€€ƒxVHave I not hated them, O Lord, that hated thee: and pined away because of thy enemies?€€ƒxKI have hated them with a perfect hatred: and they are become enemies to me.€€ƒxBProve me, O God, and know my heart: examine me, and know my paths.€€ƒxNAnd see if there be in me the way of iniquity: and lead me in the eternal way.€€ƒ`€€ƒxUnto the end, a psalm of David.€€˜"ƒxTWho have devised iniquities in their hearts: all the day long they designed battles.€€ƒxXThey have sharpened their tongues like a serpent: the venom of asps is under their lips.€€ƒxuKeep me, O Lord, from the hand of the wicked: and from unjust men deliver me. Who have proposed to supplant my steps:€€ƒx‰The proud have hidden a net for me. And they have stretched out cords for a snare: they have laid for me a stumblingblock by the wayside.€€ƒxPI said to the Lord: Thou art my God: hear, O Lord, the voice of my supplication.€€ƒx`O Lord, Lord, the strength of my salvation: thou hast overshadowed my head in the day of battle.€€ƒx…Give me not up, O Lord, from my desire to the wicked: they have plotted against me; do not thou forsake me, lest they should triumph.€€ƒxTThe head of them compassing me about: the labour of their lips shall overwhelm them.€€ƒxxBurning coals shall fall upon them; thou wilt cast them down into the fire: in miseries they shall not be able to stand.€€ƒxmA man full of tongue shall not be established in the earth: evil shall catch the unjust man unto destruction.€€ƒxMI know that the Lord will do justice to the needy, and will revenge the poor.€€ƒxiBut as for the just, they shall give glory to thy name: and the upright shall dwell with thy countenance.€€ƒ`€€ƒxOI have cried to thee, O Lord, hear me: hearken to my voice, when I cry to thee.€€ƒxdLet my prayer be directed as incense in thy sight; the lifting up of my hands, as evening sacrifice.€€ƒxESet a watch, O Lord, before my mouth: and a door round about my lips.€€ƒxIncline not my heart to evil words; to make excuses in sins. With men that work iniquity: and I will not communicate with the choicest of them.€€ƒxÁThe just man shall correct me in mercy, and shall reprove me: but let not the oil of the sinner fatten my head. For my prayer shall still be against the things with which they are well pleased:€€ƒxmTheir judges falling upon the rock have been swallowed up. They shall hear my words, for they have prevailed:€€ƒxmAs when the thickness of the earth is broken up upon the ground: Our bones are scattered by the side of hell.€€ƒx[But to thee, O Lord, Lord, are my eyes: in thee have I put my trust, take not away my soul.€€ƒxmKeep me from the snare, which they have laid for me, and from the stumblingblocks of them that work iniquity.€€ƒx:The wicked shall fall in his net: I am alone until I pass.€€ƒ`€€ƒx=Of understanding for David, A prayer when he was in the cave.€€ƒxQI cried to the Lord with my voice: with my voice I made supplication to the Lord.€€ƒxGIn his sight I pour out my prayer, and before him I declare my trouble:€€ƒxtWhen my spirit failed me, then thou knewest my paths. In this way wherein I walked, they have hidden a snare for me.€€ƒx—I looked on my right hand, and beheld, and there was no one that would know me. Flight hath failed me: and there is no one that hath regard to my soul.€€ƒxXI cried to thee, O Lord: I said: Thou art my hope, my portion in the land of the living.€€ƒxsAttend to my supplication: for I am brought very low. Deliver me from my persecutors; for they are stronger than I.€€ƒxdBring my soul out of prison, that I may praise thy name: the just wait for me, until thou reward me.€€ƒ`€€ƒxA psalm of David, when his son Absalom pursued him. Hear, O Lord, my prayer: give ear to my supplication in thy truth: hear me in thy justice.€€˜ƒx›For the enemy hath persecuted my soul: he hath brought down my life to the earth. He hath made me to dwell in darkness as those that have been dead of old:€€ƒxFAnd my spirit is in anguish within me: my heart within me is troubled.€€ƒxdI remembered the days of old, I meditated on all thy works: I meditated upon the works of thy hands.€€ƒxPI stretched forth my hands to thee: my soul is as earth without water unto thee.€€ƒxŠHear me speedily, O Lord: my spirit hath fainted away. Turn not away thy face from me, lest I be like unto them that go down into the pit.€€ƒx›Cause me to hear thy mercy in the morning; for in thee have I hoped. Make the way known to me, wherein I should walk: for I have lifted up my soul to thee.€€ƒx8Deliver me from my enemies, O Lord, to thee have I fled:€€ƒx`Teach me to do thy will, for thou art my God. Thy good spirit shall lead me into the right land:€€ƒxifor thy name's sake, O Lord, thou wilt quicken me in thy justice. Thou wilt bring my soul out of trouble:€€ƒxyAnd in thy mercy thou wilt destroy my enemies. And thou wilt cut off all them that afflict my soul: for I am thy servant.€€ƒ`€€ƒxRBlessed be the Lord my God, who teacheth my hands to fight, and my fingers to war.€€ƒx~My mercy, and my refuge: my support, and my deliverer: My protector, and I have hoped in him: who subdueth my people under me.€€ƒxgLord, what is man, that thou art made known to him? or the son of man, that thou makest account of him?€€ƒx8Man is like to vanity: his days pass away like a shadow.€€ƒxRLord, bow down thy heavens and descend: touch the mountains, and they shall smoke.€€ƒxeSend forth lightning, and thou shalt scatter them: shoot out thy arrows, and thou shalt trouble them.€€ƒxqPut forth thy hand from on high, take me out, and deliver me from many waters: from the hand of strange children:€€ƒxSWhose mouth hath spoken vanity: and their right hand is the right hand of iniquity.€€ƒxyTo thee, O God, I will sing a new canticle: on the psaltery and an instrument of ten strings I will sing praises to thee.€€ƒx\Who givest salvation to kings: who hast redeemed thy servant David from the malicious sword:€€ƒx’Deliver me, And rescue me out of the hand of strange children; whose mouth hath spoken vanity: and their right hand is the right hand of iniquity:€€ƒx~Whose sons are as new plants in their youth: Their daughters decked out, adorned round about after the similitude of a temple:€€ƒxvTheir storehouses full, flowing out of this into that. Their sheep fruitful in young, abounding in their goings forth:€€ƒxYTheir oxen fat. There is no breach of wall, nor passage, nor crying out in their streets.€€ƒxjThey have called the people happy, that hath these things: but happy is that people whose God is the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxxPraise, for David himself. I will extol thee, O God my king: and I will bless thy name for ever; yea, for ever and ever.€€˜ƒxSGreat is the Lord, and greatly to be praised: and of his greatness there is no end.€€ƒxSGeneration and generation shall praise thy works: and they shall declare thy power.€€ƒxeThey shall speak of the magnificence of the glory of thy holiness: and shall tell thy wondrous works.€€ƒxXAnd they shall speak of the might of thy terrible acts: and shall declare thy greatness.€€ƒxbThey shall publish the memory of the abundance of thy sweetness: and shall rejoice in thy justice.€€ƒxBThe Lord is gracious and merciful: patient and plenteous in mercy.€€ƒxHThe Lord is sweet to all: and his tender mercies are over all his works.€€ƒxFLet all thy works, O Lord, praise thee: and let thy saints bless thee.€€ƒxJThey shall speak of the glory of thy kingdom: and shall tell of thy power:€€ƒx]To make thy might known to the sons of men: and the glory of the magnificence of thy kingdom.€€ƒxThy kingdom is a kingdom of all ages: and thy dominion endureth throughout all generations. The Lord is faithful in all his words: and holy in all his works.€€ƒxIThe Lord lifteth up all that fall: and setteth up all that are cast down.€€ƒxNThe eyes of all hope in thee, O Lord: and thou givest them meat in due season.€€ƒxGThou openest thy hand, and fillest with blessing every living creature.€€ƒx<The Lord is just in all his ways: and holy in all his works.€€ƒxVThe Lord is nigh unto all them that call upon him: to all that call upon him in truth.€€ƒxXHe will do the will of them that fear him: and he will hear their prayer, and save them.€€ƒxLThe Lord keepeth all them that love him; but all the wicked he will destroy.€€ƒxsMy mouth shall speak the praise of the Lord: and let all flesh bless his holy name forever; yea, for ever and ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒx"Alleluia, of Aggeus and Zacharias.€€ƒxŠPraise the Lord, O my soul, in my life I will praise the Lord: I will sing to my God as long as I shall be. Put not your trust in princes:€€ƒx6In the children of men, in whom there is no salvation.€€ƒxkHis spirit shall go forth, and he shall return into his earth: in that day all their thoughts shall perish.€€ƒxZBlessed is he who hath the God of Jacob for his helper, whose hope is in the Lord his God:€€ƒxDWho made heaven and earth, the sea, and all things that are in them.€€ƒx–Who keepeth truth for ever: who executeth judgment for them that suffer wrong: who giveth food to the hungry. The Lord looseth them that are fettered:€€ƒxgThe Lord enlighteneth the blind. The Lord lifteth up them that are cast down: the Lord loveth the just.€€˜ƒxOThe Lord shall reign for ever: thy God, O Sion, unto generation and generation.€€ƒ`€€ƒx\Alleluia. Praise ye the Lord, because psalm is good: to our God be joyful and comely praise.€€ƒxPThe Lord buildeth up Jerusalem: he will gather together the dispersed of Israel.€€ƒx>Who healeth the broken of heart, and bindeth up their bruises.€€ƒxIWho telleth the number of the stars: and calleth them all by their names.€€ƒxPGreat is our Lord, and great is his power: and of his wisdom there is no number.€€ƒxNThe Lord lifteth up the meek, and bringeth the wicked down even to the ground.€€ƒx?Sing ye to the Lord with praise: sing to our God upon the harp.€€ƒx“Who covereth the heaven with clouds, and prepareth rain for the earth. Who maketh grass to grow on the mountains, and herbs for the service of men.€€ƒxLWho giveth to beasts their food: and to the young ravens that call upon him.€€ƒxZHe shall not delight in the strength of the horse: nor take pleasure in the legs of a man.€€ƒxSThe Lord taketh pleasure in them that fear him: and in them that hope in his mercy.€€ƒ`€€ƒx5Praise the Lord, O Jerusalem: praise thy God, O Sion.€€ƒx^Because he hath strengthened the bolts of thy gates, he hath blessed thy children within thee.€€ƒxLWho hath placed peace in thy borders: and filleth thee with the fat of corn.€€ƒxDWho sendeth forth his speech to the earth: his word runneth swiftly.€€ƒx7Who giveth snow like wool: scattereth mists like ashes.€€ƒxQHe sendeth his crystal like morsels: who shall stand before the face of his cold?€€ƒx_He shall send out his word, and shall melt them: his wind shall blow, and the waters shall run.€€ƒxJWho declareth his word to Jacob: his justices and his judgments to Israel.€€ƒxoHe hath not done in like manner to every nation: and his judgments he hath not made manifest to them. Alleluia.€€ƒ`€€ƒxFPraise ye the Lord from the heavens: praise ye him in the high places.€€ƒx<Praise ye him, all his angels, praise ye him, all his hosts.€€ƒxBPraise ye him, O sun and moon: praise him, all ye stars and light.€€˜ƒxcPraise the name of the Lord. For he spoke, and they were made: he commanded, and they were created.€€ƒxkHe hath established them for ever, and for ages of ages: he hath made a decree, and it shall not pass away.€€ƒx=Praise the Lord from the earth, ye dragons, and all ye deeps:€€ƒx;Fire, hail, snow, ice, stormy winds, which fulfil his word:€€ƒx7Mountains and all hills, fruitful trees and all cedars:€€ƒx4Beasts and all cattle: serpents and feathered fowls:€€ƒxGKings of the earth and all people: princes and all judges of the earth:€€ƒxQYoung men and maidens: let the old with the younger, praise the name of the Lord:€€ƒxFor his name alone is exalted.€€ƒx³The praise of him is above heaven and earth: and he hath exalted the horn of his people. A hymn to all his saints to the children of Israel, a people approaching to him. Alleluia.€€ƒ`€€ƒxRSing ye to the Lord a new canticle: let his praise be in the church of the saints.€€ƒx^Let Israel rejoice in him that made him: and let the children of Sion be joyful in their king.€€ƒxZLet them praise his name in choir: let them sing to him with the timbrel and the psaltery.€€ƒxXFor the Lord is well pleased with his people: and he will exalt the meek unto salvation.€€ƒxFThe saints shall rejoice in glory: they shall be joyful in their beds.€€ƒxUThe high praises of God shall be in their mouth: and two-edged swords in their hands:€€ƒxFTo execute vengeance upon the nations, chastisements among the people:€€ƒxITo bind their kings with fetters, and their nobles with manacles of iron.€€ƒx]To execute upon them the judgment that is written: this glory is to all his saints. Alleluia.€€ƒ`€€ƒxSPraise ye the Lord in his holy places: praise ye him in the firmament of his power.€€ƒx]Praise ye him for his mighty acts: praise ye him according to the multitude of his greatness.€€ƒxHPraise him with the sound of trumpet: praise him with psaltery and harp.€€ƒxFPraise him with timbrel and choir: praise him with strings and organs.€€ƒxnPraise him on high sounding cymbals: praise him on cymbals of joy: let every spirit praise the Lord. Alleluia.€€ƒ`€€ƒtThe Book of Proverbs€€˜ƒx:The parables of Solomon, the son of David, king of Israel,€€ƒx To know wisdom, and instruction:€€ƒxsTo understand the words of prudence: and to receive the instruction of doctrine, justice, and judgment, and equity:€€ƒxNTo give subtilty to little ones, to the young man knowledge and understanding.€€ƒx_A wise man shall hear, and shall be wiser: and he that understandeth shall possess governments.€€ƒxjHe shall understand a parable and the interpretation, the words of the wise, and their mysterious sayings.€€ƒxVThe fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom. Fools despise wisdom and instruction.€€ƒxRMy son, hear the instruction of thy father, and forsake not the law of thy mother:€€ƒxEThat grace may be added to thy head, and a chain of gold to thy neck.€€ƒx:My son, if sinners shall entice thee, consent not to them.€€ƒxqIf they shall say: Come with us, let us lie in wait for blood, let us hide snares for the innocent without cause:€€ƒxULet us swallow him up alive like hell, and whole as one that goeth down into the pit.€€ƒxKWe shall find all precious substance, we shall fill our houses with spoils.€€ƒx3Cast in thy lot with us, let us all have one purse.€€ƒxDMy son, walk not thou with them, restrain thy foot from their paths.€€ƒx9For their feet run to evil, and make haste to shed blood.€€ƒxDBut a net is spread in vain before the eyes of them that have wings.€€ƒxbAnd they themselves lie in wait for their own blood, and practise deceits against their own souls.€€ƒxFSo the ways of every covetous man destroy the souls of the possessors.€€ƒx?Wisdom preacheth abroad, she uttereth her voice in the streets:€€ƒxrAt the head of multitudes she crieth out, in the entrance of the gates of the city she uttereth her words, saying:€€ƒxO children, how long will you love childishness, and fools covet those things which are hurtful to themselves, and the unwise hate knowledge?€€ƒxXTurn ye at my reproof: behold I will utter my spirit to you, and will shew you my words.€€ƒx]Because I called, and you refused: I stretched out my hand, and there was none that regarded.€€ƒxFYou have despised all my counsel, and have neglected my reprehensions.€€ƒxbI also will laugh in your destruction, and will mock when that shall come to you which you feared.€€ƒx‹When sudden calamity shall fall on you, and destruction, as a tempest, shall be at hand: when tribulation and distress shall come upon you:€€˜!ƒxKBecause they have hated instruction, and received not the fear of the Lord,€€ƒx9Nor consented to my counsel, but despised all my reproof.€€ƒx`Therefore they shall eat the fruit of their own way, and shall be filled with their own devices.€€ƒx`The turning away of little ones shall kill them, and the prosperity of fools shall destroy them.€€ƒxgBut he that shall hear me, shall rest without terror, and shall enjoy abundance, without fear of evils.€€ƒ`€€ƒxOMy son, if thou wilt receive my words, and wilt hide my commandments with thee,€€ƒxGThat thy ear may hearken to wisdom: incline thy heart to know prudence.€€ƒxEFor if thou shalt call for wisdom, and incline thy heart to prudence:€€ƒxIIf thou shalt seek her as money, and shalt dig for her as for a treasure:€€ƒxUThen shalt thou understand the fear of the Lord, and shalt find the knowledge of God:€€ƒxSBecause the Lord giveth wisdom: and out of his mouth cometh prudence and knowledge.€€ƒxVHe wilt keep the salvation of the righteous, and protect them that walk in simplicity,€€ƒx>Keeping the paths of justice, and guarding the ways of saints.€€ƒxRThen shalt thou understand justice, and judgment, and equity, and every good path.€€ƒxDIf wisdom shall enter into thy heart, and knowledge please thy soul:€€ƒx:Counsel shall keep thee, and prudence shall preserve thee,€€ƒx_That thou mayst be delivered from the evil way, and from the man that speaketh perverse things:€€ƒx/Who leave the right way, and walk by dark ways:€€ƒxMWho are glad when they have done evil, and rejoice in the most wicked things:€€ƒx2Whose ways are perverse, and their steps infamous.€€ƒxdThat thou mayst be delivered from the strange woman, and from the stranger, who softeneth her words;€€ƒx%And forsaketh the guide of her youth,€€ƒxfAnd hath forgotten the covenant of her God: for her house inclineth unto death, and her paths to hell.€€ƒx`None that go in unto her, shall return again, neither shall they take hold of the paths of life.€€ƒxIThat thou mayst walk in a good way: and mayst keep the paths of the just.€€ƒxYFor they that are upright, shall dwell in the earth; and the simple shall continue in it.€€ƒxiBut the wicked shall be destroyed from the earth: and they that do unjustly, shall be taken away from it.€€ƒ`€€ƒxBMy son, forget not my law, and let thy heart keep my commandments.€€ƒxHFor they shall add to thee length of days, and years of life, and peace.€€ƒxgLet not mercy aud truth leave thee, put them about thy neck, and write them in the tables of thy heart.€€ƒxEAnd thou shalt find grace, and good understanding before God and men.€€˜ƒx;In all thy ways think on him, and he will direct thy steps.€€ƒx?Be not wise in thy own conceit: fear God, and depart from evil:€€ƒxAFor it shall be health to thy navel, and moistening to thy bones.€€ƒxPHonour the Lord with thy substance, and give him of the first of all thy fruits;€€ƒxWAnd thy barns shall be filled with abundance, and thy presses shall run over with wine.€€ƒx_My son, reject not the correction of the Lord: and do not faint when thou art chastised by him:€€ƒxXFor whom the Lord loveth, he chastiseth: and as a father in the son he pleaseth himself.€€ƒx@Blessed is the man that findeth wisdom, and is rich in prudence:€€ƒxnThe purchasing thereof is better than the merchandise of silver, and her fruit than the chief and purest gold:€€ƒxiShe is more precious than all riches: and all the things that are desired, are not to be compared to her.€€ƒxKLength of days is in her right hand, and in her left hand riches and glory.€€ƒx=Her ways are beautiful ways, and all her paths are peaceable.€€ƒx\She is a tree of life to them that lay hold on her: and he that shall retain her is blessed.€€ƒxTThe Lord by wisdom hath founded the earth, hath established the heavens by prudence.€€ƒxMBy his wisdom the depths have broken out, and the clouds grow thick with dew.€€ƒxLMy son, let not these things depart from thy eyes: keep the law and counsel:€€ƒx<And there shall be life to thy soul, and grace to thy mouth.€€ƒxLThen shalt thou walk confidently in thy way, and thy foot shall not stumble:€€ƒxRIf thou sleep, thou shalt not fear: thou shalt rest, and thy sleep shall be sweet.€€ƒxOBe not afraid of sudden fear, nor of the power of the wicked falling upon thee.€€ƒxPFor the Lord will be at thy side, and will keep thy foot that thou be not taken.€€ƒxYDo not withhold him from doing good, who is able: if thou art able, do good thyself also.€€ƒxnSay not to thy friend: Go, and come again: and to morrow I will give to thee: when thou canst give at present.€€ƒxFPractise not evil against thy friend, when he hath confidence in thee.€€ƒxGStrive not against a man without cause, when he hath done thee no evil.€€ƒx4Envy not the unjust man, and do not follow his ways.€€ƒxYFor every mocker is an abomination to the Lord, and his communication is with the simple.€€ƒxcWant is from the Lord in the house of the wicked: but the habitations of the just shall be blessed.€€ƒx@He shall scorn the scorners, and to the meek he will give grace.€€ƒxAThe wise shall possess glory: the promotion of fools is disgrace.€€ƒ`€€dEccl¤dnamelEcclesiastesiosis_namedEccllabbreviationdEcclhchaptersŒ’ƒxUFor I also was my father's son, tender, and as an only son in the sight of my mother:€€ƒxfAnd he taught me, and said: Let thy heart receive my words, keep my commandments, and thou shalt live.€€ƒxQGet wisdom, get prudence: forget not, neither decline from the words of my mouth.€€ƒxPForsake her not, and she shall keep thee: love her, and she shall preserve thee.€€ƒxSThe beginning of wisdom, get wisdom, and with all thy possession purchase prudence.€€ƒxhTake hold on her, and she shall exalt thee: thou shalt be glorified by her, when thou shalt embrace her.€€ƒxSShe shall give to thy head increase of graces, and protect thee with a noble crown.€€ƒxSHear, O my son, and receive my words, that years of life may be multiplied to thee.€€ƒxLI will shew thee the way of wisdom, I will lead thee by the paths of equity:€€ƒxƒWhich when thou shalt have entered, thy steps shall not be straitened, and when thou runnest, thou shalt not meet a stumblingblock.€€ƒxHTake hold on instruction, leave it not: keep it, because it is thy life.€€ƒxYBe not delighted in the paths of the wicked, neither let the way of evil men please thee.€€ƒx7Flee from it, pass not by it: go aside, and forsake it.€€ƒxqFor they sleep not, except they have done evil: and their sleep is taken away unless they have made some to fall.€€ƒxAThey eat the bread of wickedness, and drink the wine of iniquity.€€ƒxaBut the path of the just, as a shining light, goeth forwards, and increaseth even to perfect day.€€ƒxAThe way of the wicked is darksome: they know not where they fall.€€ƒx?My son, hearken to my words, and incline thy ear to my sayings.€€˜ƒxCFor they are life to those that find them, and health to all flesh.€€ƒxGWith all watchfulness keep thy heart, because life issueth out from it.€€ƒxKRemove from thee a froward mouth, and let detracting lips be far from thee.€€ƒxGLet thy eyes look straight on, and let thy eyelids go before thy steps.€€ƒxKMake straight the path for thy feet, and all thy ways shall be established.€€ƒy
Decline not to the right hand, nor to the left: turn away thy foot from evil. For the Lord knoweth the ways that are on the right hand: but those are perverse which are on the left hand. But he will make thy courses straight, he will bring forward thy ways in peace.€€ƒ`€€ƒx@My son, attend to my wisdom, and incline thy ear to my prudence,€€ƒxeThat thou mayst keep thoughts, and thy lips may preserve instruction. Mind not the deceit of a woman.€€ƒx\For the lips of a harlot are like a honeycomb dropping, and her throat is smoother than oil.€€ƒxBBut her end is bitter as wormwood, and sharp as a two-edged sword.€€ƒx@Her feet go down into death, and her steps go in as far as hell.€€ƒxNThey walk not by the path of life, her steps are wandering, and unaccountable.€€ƒxKNow, therefore, my son, hear me, and depart not from the words of my mouth.€€ƒxFRemove thy way far from her, and come not nigh the doors of her house.€€ƒx=Give not thy honour to strangers, and thy years to the cruel.€€ƒxVLest strangers be filled with thy strength, and thy labours be in another man's house,€€ƒxWAnd thou mourn at the last, when thou shalt have spent thy flesh and thy body, and say;€€ƒxDWhy have I hated instruction, and my heart consented not to reproof,€€ƒx]And have not heard the voice of them that taught me, and have not inclined my ear to masters?€€ƒxSI have almost been in all evil, in the midst of the church and of the congregation.€€ƒxDDrink water out of thy own cistern, and the streams of thy own well:€€ƒxKLet thy fountains be conveyed abroad, and in the streets divide thy waters.€€ƒxIKeep them to thyself alone, neither let strangers be partakers with thee.€€ƒx@Let thy vein be blessed, and rejoice with the wife of thy youth:€€ƒxLet her be thy dearest hind, and most agreeable fawn: let her breasts inebriate thee at all times: be thou delighted continually with her love.€€–ƒxBThe Lord beholdeth the ways of man, and considereth all his steps.€€ƒxYHis own iniquities catch the wicked, and he is fast bound with the ropes of his own sins.€€ƒxoHe shall die, because he hath not received instruction, and in the multitude of his folly he shall be deceived.€€ƒ`€€ƒxXMy son, if thou be surety for thy friend, thou hast engaged fast thy hand to a stranger,€€ƒxMThou art ensnared with the words of thy mouth, and caught with thy own words.€€ƒxšDo, therefore, my son, what I say, and deliver thyself: because thou art fallen into the hand of thy neighbour. Run about, make haste, stir up thy friend:€€ƒx<Give not sleep to thy eyes, neither let thy eyelids slumber.€€ƒxRDeliver thyself as a doe from the hand, and as a bird from the hand of the fowler.€€ƒxCGo to the ant, O sluggard, and consider her ways, and learn wisdom:€€ƒx;Which, although she hath no guide, nor master, nor captain,€€ƒxTProvideth her meat for herself in the summer, and gathereth her food in the harvest.€€ƒxLHow long wilt thou sleep, O sluggard? when wilt thou arise out of thy sleep?€€ƒxaThou wilt sleep a little, thou wilt slumber a little, thou wilt fold thy hands a little to sleep:€€ƒx¬And want shall come upon thee, as a traveller, and poverty as a man armed. But if thou be diligent, thy harvest shall come as a fountain, and want shall flee far from thee.€€ƒxNA man that is an apostate, an unprofitable man, walketh with a perverse mouth,€€ƒxKHe winketh with the eyes, presseth with the foot, speaketh with the finger.€€ƒxIWith a wicked heart he deviseth evil, and at all times he soweth discord.€€ƒx|To such a one his destruction shall presently come, and he shall suddenly be destroyed, and shall no longer have any remedy.€€ƒxPSix things there are, which the Lord hateth, and the seventh his soul detesteth:€€ƒx=Haughty eyes, a lying tongue, hands that shed innocent blood,€€ƒxMA heart that deviseth wicked plots, feet that are swift to run into mischief,€€ƒxSMy son, keep the commandments of thy father, and forsake not the law of thy mother.€€ƒx@Bind them in thy heart continually, and put them about thy neck.€€ƒxxWhen thou walkest, let them go with thee: when thou sleepest, let them keep thee, and when thou awakest, talk with them.€€ƒxhBecause the commandment is a lamp, and the law a light, and reproofs of instruction are the way of life:€€ƒx\That they may keep thee from the evil woman, and from the flattering tongue of the stranger.€€ƒxALet not thy heart covet her beauty, be not caught with her winks:€€ƒx`For the price of a harlot is scarce one loaf: but the woman catcheth the precious soul of a man.€€ƒx<Can a man hide fire in his bosom, and his garments not burn?€€ƒx9Or can he walk upon hot coals, and his feet not be burnt?€€ƒxXSo he that goeth in to his neighbour's wife, shall not be clean when he shall touch her.€€ƒxZThe fault is not so great when a man hath stolen: for he stealeth to fill his hungry soul:€€ƒxaAnd if he be taken, he shall restore sevenfold, and shall give up all the substance of his house.€€ƒxSBut he that is an adulterer, for the folly of his heart shall destroy his own soul:€€ƒxWHe gathereth to himself shame and dishonour, and his reproach shall not be blotted out:€€ƒxRBecause the jealousy and rage of the husband will not spare in the day of revenge,€€ƒx_Nor will he yield to any man's prayers, nor will he accept for satisfaction ever so many gifts.€€”ƒx=My son, keep my words, and lay up my precepts with thee. Son,€€ƒxNKeep my commandments, and thou shalt live: and my law as the apple of thy eye:€€ƒx@Bind it upon thy fingers, write it upon the tables of thy heart.€€ƒx@Say to wisdom: Thou art my sister: and call prudence thy friend,€€ƒxhThat she may keep thee from the woman that is not thine, and from the stranger who sweeteneth her words.€€ƒx?For I looked out of the window of my house through the lattice,€€ƒx4And I see little ones, I behold a foolish young man,€€ƒxRWho passeth through the street by the corner, and goeth nigh the way of her house,€€ƒxKIn the dark when it grows late, in the darkness and obscurity of the night.€€ƒxfAnd behold a woman meeteth him in harlot's attire, prepared to deceive souls: talkative and wandering,€€ƒx9Not bearing to be quiet, not able to abide still at home,€€ƒxCNow abroad, now in the streets, now lying in wait near the corners.€€ƒx[And catching the young man, she kisseth him, and with an impudent face, flattereth, saying:€€ƒx=I vowed victims for prosperity, this day I have paid my vows.€€ƒxRTherefore I am come out to meet thee, desirous to see thee, and I have found thee.€€ƒx\I have woven my bed with cords, I have covered it with painted tapestry, brought from Egypt.€€ƒx7I have perfumed my bed with myrrh, aloes, and cinnamon.€€ƒxhCome, let us be inebriated with the breasts, and let us enjoy the desired embraces, till the day appear.€€ƒx>For my husband is not at home, he is gone a very long journey.€€ƒxNHe took with him a bag of money: he will return home the day of the full moon.€€Œƒx’Immediately he followeth her as an ox led to be a victim, and as a lamb playing the wanton, and not knowing that he is drawn like a fool to bonds,€€ƒxyTill the arrow pierce his liver: as if a bird should make haste to the snare, and knoweth not that his life is in danger.€€ƒxENow, therefore, my son, hear me, and attend to the words of my mouth.€€ƒxTLet not thy mind be drawn away in her ways: neither be thou deceived with her paths.€€ƒxNFor she hath cast down many wounded, and the strongest have been slain by her.€€ƒxKHer house is the way to hell, reaching even to the inner chambers of death.€€ƒ`€€ƒx<Doth not wisdom cry aloud, and prudence put forth her voice?€€ƒxPStanding in the top of the highest places by the way, in the midst of the paths,€€ƒxEBeside the gates of the city, in the very doors she speaketh, saying:€€ƒx<O ye men, to you I call, and my voice is to the sons of men.€€ƒx>O little ones understand subtlety, and ye unwise, take notice.€€˜ƒxAMy mouth shall meditate truth, and my lips shall hate wickedness.€€ƒxEAll my words are just, there is nothing wicked, nor perverse in them.€€ƒxMThey are right to them that understand, and just to them that find knowledge.€€ƒxIReceive my instruction, and not money: choose knowledge rather than gold.€€ƒxoFor wisdom is better than all the most precious things: and whatsoever may be desired cannot be compared to it.€€ƒx@I, wisdom, dwell in counsel, and am present in learned thoughts.€€ƒxvThe fear of the Lord hateth evil; I hate arrogance, and pride, and every wicked way, and a mouth with a double tongue.€€ƒx?Counsel and equity is mine, prudence is mine, strength is mine.€€ƒx4By me kings reign, and lawgivers decree just things.€€ƒx2By me princes rule, and the mighty decree justice.€€ƒxYI love them that love me: and they that in the morning early watch for me, shall find me.€€ƒx:With me are riches and glory, glorious riches and justice.€€ƒx\For my fruit is better than gold and the precious stone, and my blossoms than choice silver.€€ƒxDI walk in the way of justice, in the midst of the paths of judgment,€€ƒxBThat I may enrich them that love me, and may fill their treasures.€€ƒx`The Lord possessed me in the beginning of his ways, before he made any thing from the beginning.€€ƒxBI was set up from eternity, and of old, before the earth was made.€€ƒxoThe depths were not as yet, and I was already conceived, neither had the fountains of waters as yet sprung out.€€ƒxlThe mountains, with their huge bulk, had not as yet been established: before the hills, I was brought forth:€€ƒxJHe had not yet made the earth, nor the rivers, nor the poles of the world.€€ƒxjWhen he prepared the heavens, I was present: when with a certain law, and compass, he enclosed the depths:€€ƒxFWhen he established the sky above, and poised the fountains of waters:€€ƒxWhen he compassed the sea with its bounds, and set a law to the waters that they should not pass their limits: when he balanced the foundations of the earth;€€ƒx`I was with him forming all things: and was delighted every day, playing before him at all times;€€ƒxJPlaying in the world: and my delights were to be with the children of men.€€ƒxINow, therefore, ye children, hear me: blessed are they that keep my ways.€€ƒx1Hear instruction, and be wise, and refuse it not.€€ƒxnBlessed is the man that heareth me, and that watcheth daily at my gates, and waiteth at the posts of my doors.€€ƒxOHe that shall find me, shall find life, and shall have salvation from the Lord.€€‘ƒ`€€ƒxGWisdom hath built herself a house, she hath hewn her out seven pillars.€€ƒxFShe hath slain her victims, mingled her wine, and set forth her table.€€ƒxMShe hath sent her maids to invite to the tower, and to the walls of the city:€€ƒxJWhosoever is a little one, let him come to me. And to the unwise she said:€€ƒxDCome, eat my bread, and drink the wine which I have mingled for you.€€ƒxAForsake childishness, and live, and walk by the ways of prudence.€€ƒxqHe that teacheth a scorner, doth an injury to himself; and he that rebuketh a wicked man, getteth himself a blot.€€ƒxRRebuke not a scorner, lest he hate thee. Rebuke a wise man, and he will love thee.€€ƒxzGive an occasion to a wise man, and wisdom shall be added to him. Teach a just man, and he shall make haste to receive it.€€ƒx[The fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom: and the knowledge of the holy is prudence.€€ƒxQFor by me shall thy days be multiplied, and years of life shall be added to thee.€€ƒx_If thou be wise, thou shalt be so to thyself: and if a scorner, thou alone shalt bear the evil.€€ƒxSA foolish woman and clamorous, and full of allurements, and knowing nothing at all,€€ƒxGSat at the door of her house, upon a seat, in a high place of the city,€€ƒx;To call them that pass by the way, and go on their journey:€€ƒxFHe that is a little one, let him turn to me. And to the fool she said:€€’ƒxYAnd he did not know that giants are there, and that her guests are in the depths of hell.€€ƒ`€€ƒxQA wise son maketh the father glad: but a foolish son is the sorrow of his mother.€€ƒxSTreasures of wickedness shall profit nothing: but justice shall deliver from death.€€ƒxyThe Lord will not afflict the soul of the just with famine, and he will disappoint the deceitful practices of the wicked.€€ƒx´The slothful hand hath wrought poverty: but the hand of the industrious getteth riches. He that trusteth to lies feedeth the winds: and the same runneth after birds, that fly away.€€ƒxmHe that gathereth in the harvest, is a wise son: but he that snorteth in the summer, is the son of confusion.€€ƒxeThe blessing of the Lord is upon the head of the just: but iniquity covereth the mouth of the wicked.€€ƒxMThe memory of the just is with praises: and the name of the wicked shall rot.€€ƒxAThe wise of heart receiveth precepts: a fool is beaten with lips.€€ƒxcHe that walketh sincerely, walketh confidently: but he that perverteth his ways, shall be manifest.€€ƒxZHe that winketh with the eye, shall cause sorrow: and the foolish in lips shall be beaten.€€ƒxWThe mouth of the just is a vein of life: and the mouth of the wicked covereth iniquity.€€ƒx:Hatred stirreth up strifes: and charity covereth all sins.€€ƒxYIn the lips of the wise is wisdom found: and a rod on the back of him that wanteth sense.€€ƒxJWise men lay up knowledge: but the mouth of the fool is next to confusion.€€ƒx_The substance of a rich man is the city of his strength: the fear of the poor is their poverty.€€ƒxHThe work of the just is unto life: but the fruit of the wicked unto sin.€€”ƒx>Lying lips hide hatred: he that uttereth reproach, is foolish.€€ƒxbIn the multitude of words there shall not want sin: but he that refraineth his lips, is most wise.€€ƒxYThe tongue of the just is as choice silver: but the heart of the wicked is nothing worth.€€ƒxdThe lips of the just teach many: but they that are ignorant, shall die in the want of understanding.€€ƒxUThe blessing of the Lord maketh men rich: neither shall affliction be joined to them.€€ƒxNA fool worketh mischief as it were for sport: but wisdom is prudence to a man.€€ƒx\That which the wicked feareth, shall come upon him: to the just their desire shall be given.€€ƒxhAs a tempest that passeth, so the wicked shall be no more: but the just is as an everlasting foundation.€€ƒxYAs vinegar to the teeth, and smoke to the eyes, so is the sluggard to them that sent him.€€ƒxXThe fear of the Lord shall prolong days: and the years of the wicked shall be shortened.€€ƒxLThe expectation of the just is joy: but the hope of the wicked shall perish.€€ƒxTThe strength of the upright is the way of the Lord: and fear to them that work evil.€€ƒxKThe just shall never be moved: but the wicked shall not dwell on the earth.€€ƒxXThe mouth of the just shall bring forth wisdom: the tongue of the perverse shall perish.€€ƒxgThe lips of the just consider what is acceptable: and the mouth of the wicked uttereth perverse things.€€ƒ`€€ƒxUA deceitful balance is an abomination before the Lord: and a just weight is his will.€€ƒxZWhere pride is, there also shall be reproach: but where humility is, there also is wisdom.€€ƒxdThe simplicity of the just shall guide them: and the deceitfulness of the wicked shall destroy them.€€ƒxTRiches shall not profit in the day of revenge: but justice shall deliver from death.€€ŠƒxdThe justice of the righteous shall deliver them: and the unjust shall be caught in their own snares.€€ƒxqWhen the wicked man is dead, there shall be no hope any more: and the expectation of the solicitous shall perish.€€ƒxPThe just is delivered out of distress: and the wicked shall be given up for him.€€ƒxaThe dissembler with his mouth deceiveth his friend: but the just shall be delivered by knowledge.€€ƒxlWhen it goeth well with the just, the city shall rejoice: and when the wicked perish, there shall be praise.€€ƒxmBy the blessing of the just the city shall be exalted: and by the mouth of the wicked it shall be overthrown.€€ƒxUHe that despiseth his friend, is mean of heart: but the wise man will hold his peace.€€ƒx}He that walketh deceitfully, revealeth secrets: but he that is faithful, concealeth the thing committed to him by his friend.€€ƒxcWhere there is no governor, the people shall fall: but there is safety where there is much counsel.€€ƒxpHe shall be afflicted with evil, that is surety for a stranger: but he that is aware of snares, shall be secure.€€ƒx`A merciful man doth good to his own soul: but he that is cruel casteth off even his own kindred.€€ƒx_The wicked maketh an unsteady work: but to him that soweth justice, there is a faithful reward.€€ƒx@Clemency prepareth life: and the pursuing of evil things, death.€€ƒxXA perverse heart is abominable to the Lord: and his will is in them that walk sincerely.€€ƒxYHand in hand the evil man shall not be innocent: but the seed of the just shall be saved.€€ƒx;A golden ring in a swine's snout, a woman fair and foolish.€€ƒxQThe desire of the just is all good, the expectation of the wicked is indignation.€€ƒxqSome distribute their own goods, and grow richer: others take away what is not their own, and are always in want.€€ƒxeThe soul that blesseth, shall be made fat: and he that inebriateth, shall be inebriated also himself.€€ƒxiHe that hideth up corn, shall be cursed among the people: but a blessing upon the head of them that sell.€€ƒxsWell doth he rise early who seeketh good things; but he that seeketh after evil things, shall be oppressed by them.€€ƒxXHe that trusteth in his riches shall fall: but the just shall spring up as a green leaf.€€ƒx\He that troubleth his own house, shall inherit the winds: and the fool shall serve the wise.€€ƒxOThe fruit of the just man is a tree of life: and he that gaineth souls is wise.€€dSong¤dnameoSong of Solomoniosis_namedSonglabbreviationdSonghchaptersˆ‘ƒxTHe that loveth correction, loveth knowledge: but he that hateth reproof, is foolish.€€ƒxhHe that is good, shall draw grace from the Lord: but he that trusteth in his own devices, doth wickedly.€€ƒxYMan shall not be strengthened by wickedness: and the root of the just shall not be moved.€€ƒxxA diligent woman is a crown to her husband: and she that doth things worthy of confusion, is as rottenness in his bones.€€ƒxUThe thoughts of the just are judgments: and the counsels of the wicked are deceitful.€€ƒxXThe words of the wicked lie in wait for blood: the mouth of the just shall deliver them.€€ƒxSTurn the wicked, and they shall not be: but the house of the just shall stand firm.€€ƒxdA man shall be known by his learning: but he that is vain and foolish, shall be exposed to contempt.€€ƒx^Better is the poor man that provideth for himself, than he that is glorious and wanteth bread.€€ƒxSThe just regardeth the lives of his beasts: but the bowels of the wicked are cruel.€€ƒxfHe that tilleth his land shall be satisfied with bread: but he that pursueth idleness is very foolish.€€ƒx¾He that is delighted in passing his time over wine, leaveth a reproach in his strong holds. The desire of the wicked is the fortification of evil men: but the root of the just shall prosper.€€ƒxfFor the sins of the lips ruin draweth nigh to the evil man: but the just shall escape out of distress.€€ƒx…By the fruit of his own mouth shall a man be filled with good things, and according to the works of his hands it shall be repaid him.€€ƒxYThe way of a fool is right in his own eyes: but he that is wise hearkeneth unto counsels.€€ƒxOA fool immediately sheweth his anger: but he that dissembleth injuries is wise.€€ƒxiHe that speaketh that which he knoweth, sheweth forth justice: but he that lieth, is a deceitful witness.€€‘ƒxeThe lip of truth shall be steadfast for ever: but he that is a hasty witness, frameth a lying tongue.€€ƒxjDeceit is in the heart of them that think evil things: but joy followeth them that take counsels of peace.€€ƒxkWhatsoever shall befall the just man, shall not make him sad: but the wicked shall be filled with mischief.€€ƒxULying lips are an abomination to the Lord: but they that deal faithfully, please him.€€ƒxMA cautious man concealeth knowledge: and the heart of fools publisheth folly.€€ƒx[The hand of the valiant shall bear rule: but that which is slothful shall be under tribute.€€ƒx\Grief in the heart of a man shall bring him low, but with a good word he shall be made glad.€€ƒxjHe that neglecteth a loss for the sake of a friend, is just: but the way of the wicked shall deceive them.€€ƒx^The deceitful man shall not find gain: but the substance of a just man shall be precious gold.€€ƒxAIn the path of justice is life: but the bye-way leadeth to death.€€ƒ`€€ƒxiA wise son heareth the doctrine of his father: but he that is a scorner, heareth not when he is reproved.€€ƒxnOf the fruit of his own month shall a man be filled with good things: but the soul of transgressors is wicked.€€ƒxjHe that keepeth his mouth keepeth his soul: but he that hath no guard on his speech shall meet with evils.€€ƒxYThe sluggard willeth, and willeth not: but the soul of them that work, shall be made fat.€€ƒxVThe just shall hate a lying word: but the wicked confoundeth, and shall be confounded.€€ƒxPJustice keepeth the way of the innocent: but wickedness overthroweth the sinner.€€‹ƒxYThe ransom of a man's life are his riches: but he that is poor, beareth not reprehension.€€ƒxNThe light of the just giveth joy: but the lamp of the wicked shall be put out.€€ƒxlAmong the proud there are always contentions: but they that do all things with counsel, are ruled by wisdom.€€ƒxzSubstance got in haste shall be diminished: but that which by little and little is gathered with the hand, shall increase.€€ƒxTHope that is deferred afflicteth the soul: desire when it cometh, is a tree of life.€€ƒxÏWhosoever speaketh ill of any thing, bindeth himself for the time to come: but he that feareth the commandment, shall dwell in peace. Deceitful souls go astray in sins: the just are merciful, and shew mercy.€€ƒxVThe law of the wise is a fountain of life, that he may decline from the ruin of death.€€ƒxHGood instruction shall give grace: in the way of scorners is a deep pit.€€ƒx[The prudent man doth all things with counsel: but he that is a fool, layeth open his folly.€€ƒxZThe messenger of the wicked shall fall into mischief: but a faithful ambassador is health.€€ƒxgPoverty and shame to him that refuseth instruction: but he that yieldeth to reproof shall be glorified.€€ƒxZHe that walketh with the wise, shall be wise: a friend of fools shall become like to them.€€ƒx<Evil pursueth sinners: and to the just good shall be repaid.€€ƒxfThe good man leaveth heirs, sons, and grandsons: and the substance of the sinner is kept for the just.€€ƒxWMuch food is in the tillage of fathers: but for others it is gathered without judgment.€€ƒxXHe that spareth the rod, hateth his son: but he that loveth him, correcteth him betimes.€€ƒxXThe just eateth and filleth his soul: but the belly of the wicked is never to be filled.€€ƒ`€€ƒxhA wise woman buildeth her house: but the foolish will pull down with her hands that also which is built.€€ƒxdHe that walketh in the right way, and feareth God, is despised by him that goeth by an infamous way.€€ƒxSIn the mouth of a fool is the rod of pride: but the lips of the wise preserve them.€€ƒxsWhere there are no oxen, the crib is empty: but where there is much corn, there the strength of the ox is manifest.€€ƒxHA faithful witness will not lie: but a deceitful witness uttereth a lie.€€ƒxOA scorner seeketh wisdom, and findeth it not: the learning of the wise is easy.€€ƒxBGo against a foolish man, and he knoweth not the lips of prudence.€€ƒxZThe wisdom of a discreet man is to understand his way: and the imprudence of fools erreth.€€ƒx?A fool will laugh at sin, but among the just grace shall abide.€€ƒx[The house of the wicked shall be destroyed: but the tabernacles of the just shall flourish.€€ƒxOThere is a way which seemeth just to a man: but the ends thereof lead to death.€€ƒxSLaughter shall be mingled with sorrow, and mourning taketh hold of the ends of joy.€€ƒxNA fool shall be filled with his own ways, and the good man shall be above him.€€ƒxËThe innocent believeth every word: the discreet man considereth his steps. No good shall come to the deceitful son: but the wise servant shall prosper in his dealings, and his way shall be made straight.€€ƒxUA wise man feareth, and declineth from evil: the fool leapeth over, and is confident.€€ƒxBThe impatient man shall work folly: and the crafty man is hateful.€€ƒxKThe childish shall possess folly, and the prudent shall look for knowledge.€€ƒxVThe evil shall fall down before the good: and the wicked before the gates of the just.€€ƒx^The poor man shall be hateful even to his own neighbour: but the friends of the rich are many.€€ƒxHe that despiseth his neighbour, sinneth: but he that sheweth mercy to the poor, shall be blessed. He that believeth in the Lord, loveth mercy.€€ƒxAThey err that work evil: but mercy and truth prepare good things.€€ƒx`In much work there shall be abundance: but where there are many words, there is oftentimes want.€€ƒxGThe crown of the wise, is their riches: the folly of fools, imprudence.€€ƒxIA faithful witness delivereth souls: and the double dealer uttereth lies.€€ƒx\In the fear of the Lord is confidence of strength, and there shall be hope for his children.€€ƒxyIn the multitude of people is the dignity of the king: and in the small number of the people the dishonour of the prince.€€ƒx_He that is patient, is governed with much wisdom: but he that is impatient, exalteth his folly.€€ƒxUSoundness of heart is the life of the flesh: but envy is the rottenness of the bones.€€ƒxdHe that oppresseth the poor, upbraideth his maker: but he that hath pity on the poor, honoureth him.€€ƒxZThe wicked man shall be driven out in his wickedness: but the just hath hope in his death.€€ƒxSIn the heart of the prudent resteth wisdom, and it shall instruct all the ignorant.€€ƒx<Justice exalteth a nation: but sin maketh nations miserable.€€ƒx[A wise servant is acceptable to the king: he that is good for nothing shall feel his anger.€€ƒ`€€ƒx@A mild answer breaketh wrath: but a harsh word stirreth up fury.€€ƒxUThe tongue of the wise adorneth knowledge: but the mouth of fools bubbleth out folly.€€ƒxAThe eyes of the Lord in every place behold the good and the evil.€€ƒx[A peaceable tongue is a tree of life: but that which is immoderate, shall crush the spirit.€€ƒxXThe house of the just is very much strength: and in the fruits of the wicked is trouble.€€ƒxRThe lips of the wise shall disperse knowledge: the heart of fools shall be unlike.€€ƒxZThe victims of the wicked are abominable to the Lord: the vows of the just are acceptable.€€ƒxaThe way of the wicked is an abomination to the Lord: he that followeth justice is beloved by him.€€ƒx`Instruction is grievous to him that forsaketh the way of life: he that hateth reproof shall die.€€ƒxZHell and destruction are before the Lord: how much more the hearts of the children of men?€€ƒxLA corrupt man loveth not one that reproveth him: nor will he go to the wise.€€ƒxYA glad heart maketh a cheerful countenance: but by grief of mind the spirit is cast down.€€ƒxYThe heart of the wise seeketh instruction: and the mouth of fools feedeth on foolishness.€€ƒxKAll the days of the poor are evil: a secure mind is like a continual feast.€€ƒxSBetter is a little with the fear of the Lord, than great treasures without content.€€ƒxQIt is better to be invited to herbs with love, than to a fatted calf with hatred.€€ƒx]A passionate man stirreth up strifes: he that is patient appeaseth those that are stirred up.€€ƒxLA wise son maketh a father joyful: but the foolish man despiseth his mother.€€ƒxEFolly is joy to the fool: and the wise man maketh straight his steps.€€ƒxuDesigns are brought to nothing where there is no counsel: but where there are many counsellors, they are established.€€ƒxMA man rejoiceth in the sentence of his mouth: and a word in due time is best.€€ƒxQThe path of life is above for the wise, that he may decline from the lowest hell.€€ƒx[The Lord will destroy the house of the proud: and will strengthen the borders of the widow.€€ƒxfEvil thoughts are an abomination to the Lord: and pure words most beautiful shall be confirmed by him.€€ƒx¼He that is greedy of gain troubleth his own house: but he that hateth bribes shall live. By mercy and faith sins are purged away: and by the fear of the Lord every one declineth from evil.€€ƒxXThe mind of the just studieth obedience: the mouth of the wicked overfloweth with evils.€€ƒxJThe Lord is far from the wicked: and he will hear the prayers of the just.€€ƒxKThe light of the eyes rejoiceth the soul: a good name maketh the bones fat.€€ƒxPThe ear that heareth the reproofs of life, shall abide in the midst of the wise.€€ƒxqHe that rejecteth instruction, despiseth his own soul: but he that yieldeth to reproof, possesseth understanding.€€ƒxNThe fear of the Lord is the lesson of wisdom: and humility goeth before glory.€€cIsa¤dnamefIsaiahiosis_namecIsalabbreviationcIsahchapters˜B˜ƒxOAll the ways of a man are open to his eyes: the Lord is the weigher of spirits.€€ƒxCLay open thy works to the Lord: and thy thoughts shall be directed.€€ƒxLThe Lord hath made all things for himself: the wicked also for the evil day.€€ƒxÖEvery proud man is an abomination to the Lord: though hand should be joined to hand, he is not innocent. The beginning of a good way is to do justice: and this is more acceptable with God, than to offer sacrifices.€€ƒxZBy mercy and truth iniquity is redeemed; and by the fear of the Lord men depart from evil.€€ƒxVWhen the ways of man shall please the Lord, he will convert even his enemies to peace.€€ƒxCBetter is a little with justice, than great revenues with iniquity.€€ƒxGThe heart of man disposeth his way: but the Lord must direct his steps.€€ƒxKDivination is in the lips of the king, his mouth shall not err in judgment.€€ƒxVWeight and balance are judgments of the Lord: and his work all the weights of the bag.€€ƒx\They that act wickedly are abominable to the king: for the throne is established by justice.€€ƒxQJust lips are the delight of kings: he that speaketh right things shall be loved.€€ƒxOThe wrath of a king is as messengers of death: and the wise man will pacify it.€€ƒx`In the cheerfulness of the king's countenance is life: and his clemency is like the latter rain.€€ƒxgGet wisdom, because it is better than gold: and purchase prudence, for it is more precious than silver.€€ƒxTThe path of the just departeth from evils: he that keepeth his soul keepeth his way.€€ƒxJPride goeth before destruction: and the spirit is lifted up before a fall.€€ƒxOIt is better to be humbled with the meek, than to divide spoils with the proud.€€ƒxXThe learned in word shall find good things: and he that trusteth in the Lord is blessed.€€ƒxiThe wise in heart shall be called prudent: and he that is sweet in words, shall attain to greater things.€€ƒxcKnowledge is a fountain of life to him that possesseth it: the instruction of fools is foolishness.€€ƒxPThe heart of the wise shall instruct his mouth: and shall add grace to his lips.€€ƒxRWell ordered words are as a honeycomb: sweet to the soul, and health to the bones.€€ƒxOThere is a way that seemeth to a man right: and the ends thereof lead to death.€€ƒx`The soul of him that laboureth, laboureth for himself, because his mouth hath obliged him to it.€€ƒx?The wicked man diggeth evil, and in his lips is a burning fire.€€ƒxNA perverse man stirreth up quarrels: and one full of words separateth princes.€€ƒxOAn unjust man allureth his friend: and leadeth him into a way that is not good.€€ƒxWHe that with fixed eyes deviseth wicked things, biting his lips, bringeth evil to pass.€€ƒxGOld age is a crown of dignity, when it is found in the ways of justice.€€ƒxfThe patient man is better than the valiant: and he that ruleth his spirit, than he that taketh cities.€€–ƒ`€€ƒxJBetter is a dry morsel with joy, than a house full of victims with strife.€€ƒxaA wise servant shall rule over foolish sons, and shall divide the inheritance among the brethren.€€ƒxSAs silver is tried by fire, and gold in the furnace: so the Lord trieth the hearts.€€ƒxRThe evil man obeyeth an unjust tongue: and the deceitful hearkeneth to lying lips.€€ƒxxHe that despiseth the poor, reproacheth his maker: and he that rejoiceth at another man's ruin, shall not be unpunished.€€ƒxZChildren's children are the crown of old men: and the glory of children are their fathers.€€ƒx=Eloquent words do not become a fool, nor lying lips a prince.€€ƒx|The expectation of him that expecteth is a most acceptable jewel: whithersoever he turneth himself, he understandeth wisely.€€ƒxhHe that concealeth a transgression, seeketh friendships: he that repeateth it again, separateth friends.€€ƒxLA reproof availeth more with a wise man, than a hundred stripes with a fool.€€ƒxQAn evil man always seeketh quarrels: but a cruel angel shall be sent against him.€€ƒxXIt is better to meet a bear robbed of her whelps, than a fool trusting in his own folly.€€ƒxFHe that rendereth evil for good, evil shall not depart from his house.€€ƒxtThe beginning of quarrels is as when one letteth out water: and before he suffereth reproach, he forsaketh judgment.€€ƒx_He that justifieth the wicked, and he that condemneth the just, both are abominable before God.€€ƒx¯What doth it avail a fool to have riches, seeing he cannot buy wisdom? He that maketh his house high, seeketh a downfall: and he that refuseth to learn, shall fall into evils.€€ƒxMHe that is a friend loveth at all times: and a brother is proved in distress.€€ƒx@A foolish man will clap hands, when he is surety for his friend.€€ƒxXHe that studieth discords, loveth quarrels: and he that exalteth his door, seeketh ruin.€€ƒxmHe that is of a perverse heart, shall not find good: and he that perverteth his tongue, shall fall into evil.€€ƒxTA fool is born to his own disgrace: and even his father shall not rejoice in a fool.€€˜ƒxXThe wicked man taketh gifts out of the bosom, that he may pervert the paths of judgment.€€ƒxWWisdom shineth in the face of the wise: the eyes of fools are in the ends of the earth.€€ƒxUA foolish son is the anger of the father: and the sorrow of the mother that bore him.€€ƒxXIt is no good thing to do hurt to the just: nor to strike the prince, who judgeth right.€€ƒxoHe that setteth bounds to his words, is knowing and wise: and the man of understanding is of a precious spirit.€€ƒxpEven a fool, if he will hold his peace, shall be counted wise: and if he close his lips, a man of understanding.€€ƒ`€€ƒxeHe that hath a mind to depart from a friend, seeketh occasions: he shall ever be subject to reproach.€€ƒx`A fool receiveth not the words of prudence: unless thou say those things which are in his heart.€€ƒxjThe wicked man, when he is come into the depths of sins, contemneth: but ignominy and reproach follow him.€€ƒxeWords from the mouth of a man are as deep water: and the fountain of wisdom is an overflowing stream.€€ƒxYIt is not good to accept the person of the wicked, to decline from the truth of judgment.€€ƒxMThe lips of a fool intermeddle with strife: and his mouth provoketh quarrels.€€ƒxNThe mouth of a fool is his destruction: and his lips are the ruin of his soul.€€ƒxÄThe words of the double tongued are as if they were harmless: and they reach even to the inner parts of the bowels. Fear casteth down the slothful: and the souls of the effeminate shall be hungry.€€ƒxYHe that is loose and slack in his work, is the brother of him that wasteth his own works.€€ƒxUThe name of the Lord is a strong tower: the just runneth to it, and shall be exalted.€€ƒxeThe substance of the rich man is the city of his strength, and as a strong wall compassing him about.€€ƒx]Before destruction, the heart of a man is exalted: and before he be glorified, it is humbled.€€ƒx[He that answereth before he heareth, sheweth himself to be a fool, and worthy of confusion.€€ƒx_The spirit of a man upholdeth his infirmity: but a spirit that is easily angered, who can bear?€€ƒxRA wise heart shall acquire knowledge: and the ear of the wise seeketh instruction.€€ƒxCA man's gift enlargeth his way, and maketh him room before princes.€€ƒxNThe just is first accuser of himself: his friend cometh, and shall search him.€€ƒxIThe lot suppresseth contentions, and determineth even between the mighty.€€ƒxjA brother that is helped by his brother, is like a strong city: and judgments are like the bars of cities.€€†ƒx_Death and life are in the power of the tongue: they that love it, shall eat the fruits thereof.€€ƒxÛHe that hath found a good wife, hath found a good thing, and shall receive a pleasure from the Lord. He that driveth away a good wife, driveth away a good thing: but he that keepeth an adulteress, is foolish and wicked.€€ƒxHThe poor will speak with supplications, and the rich will speak roughly.€€ƒx@A man amiable in society, shall be more friendly than a brother.€€ƒ`€€ƒxpBetter is the poor man, that walketh in his simplicity, than a rich man that is perverse in his lips and unwise.€€˜ƒxRThe folly of a man supplanteth his steps: and he fretteth in his mind against God.€€ƒxORiches make many friends: but from the poor man, even they whom he had, depart.€€ƒxUA false witness shall not be unpunished: and he that speaketh lies, shall not escape.€€ƒxWMany honour the person of him that is mighty, and are friends of him that giveth gifts.€€ƒx”The brethren of the poor man hate him: moreover also his friends have departed far from him. He that followeth after words only, shall have nothing.€€ƒxiBut he that possesseth a mind, loveth his own soul, and he that keepeth prudence, shall find good things.€€ƒxQA false witness shall not be unpunished: and he that speaketh lies, shall perish.€€ƒxRDelicacies are not seemly for a fool: nor for a servant to have rule over princes.€€ƒxQThe learning of a man is known by patience: and his glory is to pass over wrongs.€€ƒxiAs the roaring of a lion, so also is the anger of a king: and his cheerfulness as the dew upon the grass.€€ƒxkA foolish son is the grief of his father: and a wrangling wife is like a roof continually dropping through.€€ƒxTHouse and riches are given by parents: but a prudent wife is properly from the Lord.€€ƒxMSlothfulness casteth into a deep sleep, and an idle soul shall suffer hunger.€€ƒxeHe that keepeth the commandment, keepeth his own soul: but he that neglecteth his own way, shall die.€€ƒxKHe that hath mercy on the poor, lendeth to the Lord: and he will repay him.€€ƒxJChastise thy son, despair not: but to the killing of him set not thy soul.€€ƒxcHe that is impatient, shall suffer damage: and when he shall take away, he shall add another thing.€€ƒxQHear counsel, and receive instruction, that thou mayst be wise in thy latter end.€€ƒxYThere are many thoughts in the heart of a man: but the will of the Lord shall stand firm.€€ƒxCA needy man is merciful: and better is the poor than the lying man.€€ƒxeThe fear of the Lord is unto life: and he shall abide in the fulness without being visited with evil.€€ƒx]The slothful hideth his hand under his armpit, and will not so much as bring it to his mouth.€€ƒxuThe wicked man being scourged, the fool shall be wiser: but if thou rebuke a wise man, he will understand discipline.€€ƒxTHe that afflicteth his father, and chaseth away his mother, is infamous and unhappy.€€ƒxXCease not, O my son, to hear instruction, and be not ignorant of the words of knowledge.€€ƒxTAn unjust witness scorneth judgment: and the mouth of the wicked devoureth iniquity.€€ƒxRJudgments are prepared for scorners: and striking hammers for the bodies of fools.€€ƒ`€€ƒxhWine is a luxurious thing, and drunkenness riotous: whosoever is delighted therewith, shall not be wise.€€ƒxnAs the roaring of a lion, so also is the dread of a king: he that provoketh him, sinneth against his own soul.€€ƒxwBecause of the cold the sluggard would not plough: he shall beg therefore in the summer, and it shall not be given him.€€ƒxRCounsel in the heart of a man is like deep water: but a wise man will draw it out.€€ƒx@Many men are called merciful: but who shall find a faithful man?€€ƒxQThe just that walketh in his simplicity, shall leave behind him blessed children.€€ƒxYThe king, that sitteth on the throne of judgment, scattereth away all evil with his look.€€ƒx3Who can say: My heart is clean, I am pure from sin?€€ƒxEDiverse weights and diverse measures, both are abominable before God.€€ƒxFBy his inclinations a child is known, if his works be clean and right.€€ƒxBThe hearing ear, and the seeing eye, the Lord hath made them both.€€ƒxSLove not sleep, lest poverty oppress thee: open thy eyes, and be filled with bread.€€ƒx\It is naught, it is naught, saith every buyer: and when he is gone away, then he will boast.€€ƒxYThere is gold and a multitude of jewels: but the lips of knowledge are a precious vessel.€€ƒxeTake away the garment of him that is surety for a stranger, and take a pledge from him for strangers.€€˜ƒxPDesigns are strengthened by counsels: and wars are to be managed by governments.€€ƒx_Meddle not with him that revealeth secrets, and walketh deceitfully, and openeth wide his lips.€€ƒx[He that curseth his father, and mother, his lamp shall be put out in the midst of darkness.€€ƒxXThe inheritance gotten hastily in the beginning, in the end shall be without a blessing.€€ƒxISay not: I will return evil: wait for the Lord, and he will deliver thee.€€ƒxTDiverse weights are an abomination before the Lord: a deceitful balance is not good.€€ƒx\The steps of men are guided by the Lord: but who is the man that can understand his own way?€€ƒxCIt is ruin to a man to devour holy ones, and after vows to retract.€€ƒxDA wise king scattereth the wicked, and bringeth over them the wheel.€€ƒxaThe spirit of a man is the lamp of the Lord, which searcheth all the hidden things of the bowels.€€ƒxNMercy and truth preserve the king, and his throne is strengthened by clemency.€€ƒxUThe joy of young men is their strength: and the dignity of old men, their grey hairs.€€ƒxaThe blueness of a wound shall wipe away evils: and stripes in the more inward parts of the belly.€€ƒ`€€ƒxyAs the divisions of waters, so the heart of the king is in the hand of the Lord: whithersoever he will, he shall turn it.€€ƒxNEvery way of a man seemeth right to himself: but the Lord weigheth the hearts.€€ƒx>To do mercy and judgment, pleaseth the Lord more than victims.€€ƒxUHaughtiness of the eyes is the enlarging of the heart: the lamp of the wicked is sin.€€ƒxcThe thoughts of the industrious always bring forth abundance: but every sluggard is always in want.€€ƒxoHe that gathereth treasures by a lying tongue, is vain and foolish, and shall stumble upon the snares of death.€€ƒxXThe robberies of the wicked shall be their downfall, because they would not do judgment.€€ƒxUThe perverse way of a man is strange: but as for him that is pure, his work is right.€€ƒxcIt is better to sit in a corner of the housetop, than with a brawling woman, and in a common house.€€ƒxMThe soul of the wicked desireth evil, he will not have pity on his neighbour.€€ƒxuWhen a pestilent man is punished, the little one will be wiser: and if he follow the wise, he will receive knowledge.€€–ƒxeHe that stoppeth his ear against the cry of the poor, shall also cry himself, and shall not be heard.€€ƒxNA secret present quencheth anger: and a gift in the bosom, the greatest wrath.€€ƒxKIt is joy to the just to do judgment: and dread to them that work iniquity.€€ƒx]A man that shall wander out of the way of doctrine, shall abide in the company of the giants.€€ƒxdHe that loveth good cheer, shall be in want: he that loveth wine, and fat things, shall not be rich.€€ƒxJThe wicked is delivered up for the just: and the unjust for the righteous.€€ƒxTIt is better to dwell in a wilderness, than with a quarrelsome and passionate woman.€€ƒxkThere is a treasure to be desired, and oil in the dwelling of the just: and the foolish man shall spend it.€€ƒxIHe that followeth justice and mercy, shall find life, justice, and glory.€€ƒxkThe wise man hath scaled the city of the strong, and hath cast down the strength of the confidence thereof.€€ƒxIHe that keepeth his mouth and his tongue, keepeth his soul from distress.€€ƒxJThe proud and the arrogant is called ignorant, who in anger worketh pride.€€ƒxEDesires kill the slothful: for his hands have refused to work at all.€€ƒxXHe longeth and desireth all the day: but he that is just, will give, and will not cease.€€ƒxTThe sacrifices of the wicked are abominable, because they are offered of wickedness.€€ƒxEA lying witness shall perish: an obedient man shall speak of victory.€€ƒx[The wicked man impudently hardeneth his face: but he that is righteous, correcteth his way.€€ƒxOThere is no wisdom, there is no prudence, there is no counsel against the Lord.€€ƒxHThe horse is prepared for the day of battle: but the Lord giveth safety.€€ƒ`€€ƒxRA good name is better than great riches: and good favour is above silver and gold.€€ƒxKThe rich and poor have met one another: the Lord is the maker of them both.€€•ƒxIThe fruit of humility is the fear of the Lord, riches and glory and life.€€ƒxjArms and swords are in the way of the perverse: but he that keepeth his own soul, departeth far from them.€€ƒxcIt is a proverb: A young man according to his way, even when he is old, he will not depart from it.€€ƒxOThe rich ruleth over the poor: and the borrower is servant to him that lendeth.€€ƒx^He that soweth iniquity, shall reap evils, and with the rod of his anger he shall be consumed.€€ƒxÈHe that is inclined to mercy, shall be blessed: for of his bread he hath given to the poor. He that maketh presents, shall purchase victory and honour: but he carrieth away the souls of the receivers.€€ƒxdCast out the scoffer, and contention shall go out with him, and quarrels and reproaches shall cease.€€ƒx`He that loveth cleanness of heart, for the grace of his lips shall have the king for his friend.€€ƒxTThe eyes of the Lord preserve knowledge: and the words of the unjust are overthrown.€€ƒx^The slothful man saith: There is a lion without, I shall be slain in the midst of the streets.€€ƒx_The mouth of a strange woman is a deep pit: he whom the Lord is angry with, shall fall into it.€€ƒxYFolly is bound up in the heart of a child, and the rod of correction shall drive it away.€€ƒxxHe that oppresseth the poor, to increase his own riches, shall himself give to one that is richer, and shall be in need.€€ƒxTIncline thy ear, and hear the words of the wise: and apply thy heart to my doctrine:€€ƒx`Which shall be beautiful for thee, if thou keep it in thy bowels, and it shall flow in thy lips:€€ƒxSThat thy trust may be in the Lord, wherefore I have also shewn it to thee this day.€€ƒxSBehold I have described it to thee three manner of ways, in thoughts and knowledge:€€ƒxlThat I might shew thee the certainty, and the words of truth, to answer out of these to them that sent thee.€€ƒxYDo no violence to the poor, because he is poor: and do not oppress the needy in the gate:€€ƒxZBecause the Lord will judge his cause: and will afflict them that have afflicted his soul.€€ƒxDBe not a friend to an angry man, and do not walk with a furious man:€€˜"ƒx\Be not with them that fasten down their hands, and that offer themselves sureties for debts:€€ƒxmFor if thou have not wherewith to restore, what cause is there that he should take the covering from thy bed?€€ƒx>Pass not beyond the ancient bounds which thy fathers have set.€€ƒxtHast thou seen a man swift in his work? he shall stand before kings, and shall not be before those that are obscure.€€ƒ`€€ƒxZWhen thou shalt sit to eat with a prince, consider diligently what is set before thy face:€€ƒxTAnd put a knife to thy throat, if it be so that thou have thy soul in thy own power.€€ƒx>Be not desirous of his meats, in which is the bread of deceit.€€ƒx6Labour not to be rich: but set bounds to thy prudence.€€ƒx˜Lift not up thy eyes to riches which thou canst not have: because they shall make themselves wings like those of an eagle, and shall fly towards heaven.€€ƒx6Eat not with an envious man, and desire not his meats:€€ƒx’Because, like a soothsayer, and diviner, he thinketh that which he knoweth not. Eat and drink, will he say to thee: and his mind is not with thee.€€ƒx[The meats which thou hadst eaten, thou shalt vomit up: and shalt loose thy beautiful words.€€ƒxXSpeak not in the ears of fools: because they will despise the instruction of thy speech.€€ƒxTTouch not the bounds of little ones: and enter not into the field of the fatherless:€€ƒxMFor their near kinsman is strong: and he will judge their cause against thee.€€ƒxMLet thy heart apply itself to instruction and thy ears to words of knowledge.€€ƒx\Withhold not correction from a child: for if thou strike him with the rod, he shall not die.€€ƒxAThou shalt beat him with the rod, and deliver his soul from hell.€€ƒx>My son, if thy mind be wise, my heart shall rejoice with thee:€€ƒxDAnd my reins shall rejoice, when thy lips shall speak what is right.€€ƒxULet not thy heart envy sinners: but be thou in the fear of the Lord all the day long:€€ƒx\Because thou shalt have hope in the latter end, and thy expectation shall not be taken away.€€ƒx>Hear thou, my son, and be wise: and guide thy mind in the way.€€ƒx]Be not in the feasts of great drinkers, nor in their revellings, who contribute flesh to eat:€€ƒx„Because they that give themselves to drinking, and that club together, shall be consumed: and drowsiness shall be clothed with rags.€€ƒxSHearken to thy father, that begot thee: and despise not thy mother when she is old.€€ƒxFBuy truth, and do not sell wisdom, and instruction, and understanding.€€ƒxbThe father of the just rejoiceth greatly: he that hath begotten a wise son, shall have joy in him.€€ƒxLLet thy father and thy mother be joyful, and let her rejoice that bore thee.€€ƒx9My son, give me thy heart: and let thy eyes keep my ways.€€ƒxBFor a harlot is a deep ditch: and a strange woman is a narrow pit.€€ƒx[She lieth in wait in the way as a robber, and him whom she shall see unwary, she will kill.€€ƒxˆWho hath woe? whose father hath woe? who hath contentions? who falls into pits? who hath wounds without cause? who hath redness of eyes?€€ƒxoLook not upon the wine when it is yellow, when the colour thereof shineth in the glass: it goeth in pleasantly,€€ƒxYBut in the end, it will bite like a snake, and will spread abroad poison like a basilisk.€€ƒxOThy eyes shall behold strange women, and thy heart shall utter perverse things.€€ƒxmAnd thou shalt be as one sleeping in the midst of the sea, and as a pilot fast asleep when the stern is lost.€€ƒxAnd thou shalt say: They have beaten me, but I was not sensible of pain: they drew me, and I felt not: when shall I awake and find wine again?€€ƒ`€€ƒx=Seek not to be like evil men, neither desire to be with them:€€ƒxDBecause their mind studieth robberies, and their lips speak deceits.€€ƒxMBy wisdom the house shall be built, and by prudence it shall be strengthened.€€ƒxZBy instruction the storerooms shall be filled with all precious and most beautiful wealth.€€ƒx;A wise man is strong: and a knowing man, stout and valiant.€€ƒx`Because war is managed by due ordering: and there shall be safety where there are many counsels.€€ƒxGWisdom is too high for a fool; in the gate he shall not open his mouth.€€ƒx5He that deviseth to do evils, shall be called a fool.€€ƒxJThe thought of a fool is sin: and the detractor is the abomination of men.€€ƒxXIf thou lose hope, being weary in the day of distress, thy strength shall be diminished.€€†ƒx»If thou say: I have not strength enough: he that seeth into the heart, he understandeth, and nothing deceiveth the keeper of thy soul, and he shall render to a man according to his works.€€ƒxREat honey, my son, because it is good, and the honeycomb most sweet to thy throat.€€ƒxŠSo also is the doctrine of wisdom to thy soul: which when thou hast found, thou shalt have hope in the end, and thy hope shall not perish.€€ƒxXLie not in wait, nor seek after wickedness in the house of the just, nor spoil his rest.€€ƒxfFor a just man shall fall seven times, and shall rise again: but the wicked shall fall down into evil.€€ƒxRWhen thy enemy shall fall, be not glad, and in his ruin let not thy heart rejoice:€€–ƒx=Contend not with the wicked, nor seek to be like the ungodly.€€ƒxYFor evil men have no hope of things to come, and the lamp of the wicked shall be put out.€€ƒxLMy son, fear the Lord, and the king: and have nothing to do with detractors.€€ƒxLFor their destruction shall rise suddenly: and who knoweth the ruin of both?€€ƒxUThese things also to the wise: It is not good to have respect to persons in judgment.€€ƒxoThey that say to the wicked man: Thou art just: shall be cursed by the people, and the tribes shall abhor them.€€ƒxKThey that rebuke him shall be praised: and a blessing shall come upon them.€€ƒx2He shall kiss the lips, who answereth right words.€€ƒxdPrepare thy work without, and diligently till thy ground: that afterward thou mayst build thy house.€€ƒxZBe not witness without cause against thy neighbour: and deceive not any man with thy lips.€€ƒxbSay not: I will do to him as he hath done to me: I will render to every one according to his work.€€ƒxRI passed by the field of the slothful man, and by the vineyard of the foolish man:€€ƒxwAnd behold it was all filled with nettles, and thorns had covered the face thereof, and the stone wall was broken down.€€ƒx[Which when I had seen, I laid it up in my heart, and by the example I received instruction.€€ƒxhThou wilt sleep a little, said I, thou wilt slumber a little, thou wilt fold thy hands a little to rest.€€ƒxHAnd poverty shall come to thee as a runner, and beggary as an armed man.€€ƒ`€€ƒxXThese are also parables of Solomon, which the men of Ezechias, king of Juda, copied out.€€ƒx\It is the glory of God to conceal the word, and the glory of kings to search out the speech.€€ƒxOThe heaven above and the earth beneath, and the heart of kings is unsearchable.€€ƒxNTake away the rust from silver, and there shall come forth a most pure vessel:€€ƒxaTake away wickedness from the face of the king, and his throne shall be established with justice.€€˜ ƒxvFor it is better that it should be said to thee: Come up hither; than that thou shouldst be humbled before the prince.€€ƒx¡The things which thy eyes have seen, utter not hastily in a quarrel: lest afterward thou mayst not be able to make amends, when thou hast dishonoured thy friend.€€ƒxKTreat thy cause with thy friend, and discover not the secret to a stranger:€€ƒxªLest he insult over thee, when he hath heard it, and cease not to upbraid thee. Grace and friendship deliver a man: keep these for thyself, lest thou fall under reproach.€€ƒxFTo speak a word in due time, is like apples of gold on beds of silver.€€ƒxaAs an earring of gold and a bright pearl, so is he that reproveth the wise, and the obedient ear.€€ƒxxAs the cold of snow in the time of harvest, so is a faithful messenger to him that sent him, for he refresheth his soul.€€ƒxkAs clouds, and wind, when no rain followeth, so is the man that boasteth, and doth not fulfil his promises.€€ƒxOBy patience a prince shall be appeased, and a soft tongue shall break hardness.€€ƒxfThou hast found honey, eat what is sufficient for thee, lest being glutted therewith thou vomit it up.€€ƒxUWithdraw thy foot from the house of thy neighbour, lest having his fill he hate thee.€€ƒxeA man that beareth false witness against his neighbour, is like a dart and a sword and a sharp arrow.€€ƒx]To trust in an unfaithful man in the time of trouble, is like a rotten tooth, and weary foot,€€ƒxÜAnd one that looseth his garment in cold weather. As vinegar upon nitre, so is he that singeth songs to a very evil heart. As a moth doth by a garment, and a worm by the wood: so the sadness of a man consumeth the heart.€€ƒxOIf thy enemy be hungry, give him to eat: if he thirst, give him water to drink:€€ƒxKFor thou shalt heap hot coals upon his head, and the Lord will reward thee.€€ƒxPThe north wind driveth away rain, as doth a sad countenance a backbiting tongue.€€ƒxcIt is better to sit in a corner of the housetop: than with a brawling woman, and in a common house.€€ƒxHAs cold water to a thirsty soul, so are good tidings from a far country.€€ƒxjA just man falling down before the wicked, is as a fountain troubled with the foot and a corrupted spring.€€ƒxqAs it is not good for a man to eat much honey, so he that is a searcher of majesty shall be overwhelmed by glory.€€ƒxvAs a city that lieth open and is not compassed with walls, so is a man that cannot refrain his own spirit in speaking.€€ƒ`€€ƒxJAs snow in summer, and rain in harvest, so glory is not seemly for a fool.€€ƒx|As a bird flying to other places, and a sparrow going here or there: so a curse uttered without cause shall come upon a man.€€ƒxNA whip for a horse, and a snaffle for an ass, and a rod for the back of fools.€€ƒxEAnswer not a fool according to his folly, lest thou be made like him.€€ƒxIAnswer a fool according to his folly, lest he imagine himself to be wise.€€ƒxTHe that sendeth words by a foolish messenger, is lame of feet and drinketh iniquity.€€ƒxUAs a lame man hath fair legs in vain: so a parable is unseemly in the mouth of fools.€€ƒx[As he that casteth a stone into the heap of Mercury: so is he that giveth honour to a fool.€€ƒx[As if a thorn should grow in the hand of a drunkard: so is a parable in the mouth of fools.€€‰ƒxNAs a dog that returneth to his vomit, so is the fool that repeateth his folly.€€ƒx]Hast thou seen a man wise in his own conceit? there shall be more hope of a fool than of him.€€ƒxOThe slothful man saith: There is a lion in the way, and a lioness in the roads.€€ƒxGAs the door turneth upon its hinges, so doth the slothful upon his bed.€€ƒx[The slothful hideth his hand under his armpit, and it grieveth him to turn it to his mouth.€€ƒxNThe sluggard is wiser in his own conceit, than seven men that speak sentences.€€ƒxpAs he that taketh a dog by the ears, so is he that passeth by in anger, and meddleth with another man's quarrel.€€ƒx<As he is guilty that shooteth arrows, and lances unto death.€€ƒxaSo is the man that hurteth his friend deceitfully: and when he is taken, saith: I did it in jest.€€ƒxTAs coals are to burning coals, and wood to fire, so an angry man stirreth up strife.€€ƒxdThe words of a talebearer are as it were simple, but they reach to the innermost parts of the belly.€€ƒxaSwelling lips joined with a corrupt heart, are like an earthern vessel adorned with silver dross.€€ƒxHAn enemy is known by his lips, when in his heart he entertaineth deceit.€€ƒxWWhen he shall speak low, trust him not: because there are seven mischiefs in his heart.€€ƒxZHe that covereth hatred deceitfully, his malice shall be laid open in the public assembly.€€ƒx_He that diggeth a pit, shall fall into it: and he that rolleth a stone, it shall return to him.€€ƒxGA deceitful tongue loveth not truth: and a slippery mouth worketh ruin.€€ƒ`€€ƒxSBoast not for to morrow, for thou knowest not what the day to come may bring forth.€€ƒxQLet another praise thee, and not thy own mouth: a stranger, and not thy own lips.€€ƒxVA stone is heavy, and sand weighty: but the anger of a fool is heavier than them both.€€ƒxeAnger hath no mercy: nor fury, when it breaketh forth: and who can bear the violence of one provoked?€€ƒx'Open rebuke is better than hidden love.€€ƒxoA soul that is full shall tread upon the honeycomb: and a soul that is hungry shall take even bitter for sweet.€€ƒxKAs a bird that wandereth from her nest, so is a man that leaveth his place.€€ƒxaOintment and perfumes rejoice the heart: and the good counsels of a friend are sweet to the soul.€€ƒx³Thy own friend, and thy father's friend, forsake not: and go not into thy brother's house in the day of thy affliction. Better is a neighbour that is near than a brother afar off.€€ƒxgStudy wisdom, my son, and make my heart joyful, that thou mayst give an answer to him that reproacheth.€€ƒxXThe prudent man seeing evil hideth himself: little ones passing on have suffered losses.€€ƒxeTake away his garment that hath been surety for a stranger: and take from him a pledge for strangers.€€ƒxiHe that blesseth his neighbour with a loud voice, rising in the night, shall be like to him that curseth.€€ƒxHRoofs dropping through in a cold day, and a contentious woman are alike.€€ƒxcHe that retaineth her, is as he that would hold the wind, and shall call the oil of his right hand.€€ƒxHIron sharpeneth iron, so a man sharpeneth the countenance of his friend.€€ƒxwHe that keepeth the fig tree, shall eat the fruit thereof: and he that is the keeper of his master, shall be glorified.€€ƒxkAs the faces of them that look therein, shine in the water, so the hearts of men are laid open to the wise.€€ƒxNHell and destruction are never filled: so the eyes of men are never satisfied.€€‡ƒx„Though thou shouldst bray a fool in the mortar, as when a pestle striketh upon sodden barley, his folly would not be taken from him.€€ƒxOBe diligent to know the countenance of thy cattle, and consider thy own flocks:€€ƒx^For thou shalt not always have power: but a crown shall be given to generation and generation.€€ƒxfThe meadows are open, and the green herbs have appeared, and the hay is gathered out of the mountains.€€ƒx@Lambs are for thy clothing: and kids for the price of the field.€€ƒx~Let the milk of the goats be enough for thy food, and for the necessities of thy house, and for maintenance for thy handmaids.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒx¯For the sins of the land many are the princes thereof: and for the wisdom of a man, and the knowledge of those things that are said, the life of the prince shall be prolonged.€€ƒxWA poor man that oppresseth the poor, is like a violent shower, which bringeth a famine.€€ƒx^They that forsake the law, praise the wicked man: they that keep it, are incensed against him.€€ƒx]Evil men think not on judgment: but they that seek after the Lord, take notice of all things.€€ƒxPBetter is the poor man walking in his simplicity, than the rich in crooked ways.€€ƒxYHe that keepeth the law, is a wise son: but he that feedeth gluttons, shameth his father.€€ƒxmHe that heapeth together riches by usury and loan, gathereth them for him that will be bountiful to the poor.€€ƒxWHe that turneth away his ears from hearing the law, his prayer shall be an abomination.€€ƒxwHe that deceiveth the just in a wicked way, shall fall in his own destruction: and the upright shall possess his goods.€€ƒx\The rich man seemeth to himself wise: but the poor man that is prudent shall search him out.€€ƒxSIn the joy of the just there is great glory: when the wicked reign, men are ruined.€€ƒxlHe that hideth his sins, shall not prosper: but he that shall confess, and forsake them, shall obtain mercy.€€ƒx`Blessed is the man that is always fearful: but he that is hardened in mind shall fall into evil.€€ƒxQAs a roaring lion, and a hungry bear, so is a wicked prince over the poor people.€€ƒxqA prince void of prudence shall oppress many by calumny: but he that hateth covetousness, shall prolong his days.€€ƒxdA man that doth violence to the blood of a person, if he flee even to the pit, no man will stay him.€€ƒx_He that walketh uprightly, shall be saved: he that is perverse in his ways, shall fall at once.€€ƒxuHe that tilleth his ground, shall be filled with bread: but he that followeth idleness, shall be filled with poverty.€€ƒxaA faithful man shall be much praised: but he that maketh haste to be rich, shall not be innocent.€€ƒxwHe that hath respect to a person in judgment, doth not well: such a man even for a morsel of bread forsaketh the truth.€€ƒxeA man that maketh haste to be rich, and envieth others, is ignorant that poverty shall come upon him.€€ƒxuHe that rebuketh a man, shall afterward find favour with him, more than he that by a flattering tongue deceiveth him.€€ƒxxHe that stealeth any thing from his father, or from his mother: and saith, This is no sin, is the partner of a murderer.€€ƒxqHe that boasteth and puffeth up himself, stirreth up quarrels: but he that trusteth in the Lord, shall be healed.€€ƒx\He that trusteth in his own heart, is a fool: but he that walketh wisely, he shall be saved.€€†ƒxcWhen the wicked rise up, men shall hide themselves: when they perish, the just shall be multiplied.€€ƒ`€€ƒx~The man that with a stiff neck despiseth him that reproveth him, shall suddenly be destroyed: and health shall not follow him.€€ƒxjWhen just men increase, the people shall rejoice: when the wicked shall bear rule, the people shall mourn.€€ƒxsA man that loveth wisdom, rejoiceth his father: but he that maintaineth harlots, shall squander away his substance.€€ƒxAA just king setteth up the land: a covetous man shall destroy it.€€‘ƒx]A snare shall entangle the wicked man when he sinneth: and the just shall praise and rejoice.€€ƒxQThe just taketh notice of the cause of the poor: the wicked is void of knowledge.€€ƒx?Corrupt men bring a city to ruin: but wise men turn away wrath.€€ƒxXIf a wise man contend with a fool, whether he be angry, or laugh, he shall find no rest.€€ƒx>Bloodthirsty men hate the upright: but just men seek his soul.€€ƒxSA fool uttereth all his mind: a wise man deferreth, and keepeth it till afterwards.€€ƒxGA prince that gladly heareth lying words, hath all his servants wicked.€€ƒx]The poor man and the creditor have met one another: the Lord is the enlightener of them both.€€ƒxRThe king that judgeth the poor in truth, his throne shall be established for ever.€€ƒxjThe rod and reproof give wisdom: but the child that is left to his own will, bringeth his mother to shame.€€ƒxbWhen the wicked are multiplied, crimes shall be multiplied: but the just shall see their downfall.€€ƒxOInstruct thy son and he shall refresh thee, and shall give delight to thy soul.€€ƒxhWhen prophecy shall fail, the people shall be scattered abroad: but he that keepeth the law, is blessed.€€ƒxgA slave will not be corrected by words: because he understandeth what thou sayest, and will not answer.€€ƒxZHast thou seen a man hasty to speak? folly is rather to be looked for, than his amendment.€€ƒxaHe that nourisheth his servant delicately from his childhood, afterwards shall find him stubborn.€€ƒxkA passionate man provoketh quarrels: and he that is easily stirred up to wrath, shall be more prone to sin.€€˜ƒxsHe that is partaker with a thief, hateth his own soul: he heareth one putting him to his oath, and discovereth not.€€ƒx[He that feareth man shall quickly fall: he that trusteth in the Lord, shall be set on high.€€ƒx[Many seek the face of the prince: but the judgment of every one cometh forth from the Lord.€€ƒx‘The just abhor a wicked man: and the wicked loathe them that are in the right way. The son that keepeth the word, shall be free from destruction.€€ƒ`€€ƒx–The words of Gatherer the son of Vomiter. The vision which the man spoke, with whom God is, and who being strengthened by God, abiding with him, said:€€ƒxCI am the most foolish of men, and the wisdom of men is not with me.€€ƒxDI have not learned wisdom, and have not known the science of saints.€€ƒyWho hath ascended up into heaven, and descended? who hath held the wind in his hands? who hath bound up the waters together as in a garment? who hath raised up all the borders of the earth? what is his name, and what is the name of his son, if thou knowest?€€ƒxJEvery word of God is fire tried: he is a buckler to them that hope in him.€€ƒxGAdd not any thing to his words, lest thou be reproved and found a liar:€€ƒxBTwo things I have asked of thee, deny them not to me before I die.€€ƒxvRemove far from me vanity, and lying words. Give me neither beggary, nor riches: give me only the necessaries of life:€€ƒx¡Lest perhaps being filled, I should be tempted to deny, and say: Who is the Lord? or being compelled by poverty, I should steal, and forswear the name of my God.€€ƒxFAccuse not a servant to his master, lest he curse thee, and thou fall.€€ƒxQThere is a generation that curseth their father, and doth not bless their mother.€€ƒx[A generation that are pure in their own eyes, and yet are not washed from their filthiness.€€ƒxHA generation, whose eyes are lofty, and their eyelids lifted up on high.€€ƒxA generation that for teeth hath swords, and grindeth with their jaw teeth, to devour the needy from off the earth, and the poor from among men.€€ƒx”The horseleech hath two daughters that say: Bring, bring. There are three things that never are satisfied, and the fourth never saith: It is enough.€€ƒxxHell and the mouth of the womb, and the earth which is not satisfied with water: and the fire never saith: It is enough.€€ƒx¦The eye that mocketh at his father, and that despiseth the labour of his mother in bearing him, let the ravens of the brooks pick it out, and the young eagles eat it.€€ƒxEThree things are hard to me, and the fourth I am utterly ignorant of.€€ƒxKThere are three things, which go well, and the fourth that walketh happily:€€ƒxmSuch also is the way of an adulterous woman, who eateth and wipeth her mouth, and saith: I have done no evil.€€’ƒxCBy a slave when he reigneth: by a fool when be is filled with meat:€€ƒx\By an odious woman when she is married: and by a bondwoman when she is heir to her mistress.€€ƒxQThere are four very little things of the earth, and they are wiser than the wise.€€ƒxHThe ants, a feeble people, which provide themselves food in the harvest:€€ƒx<The rabbit, a weak people, which maketh its bed in the rock:€€ƒx<The locust hath no king, yet they all go out by their bands:€€ƒxFThe stellio supporteth itself on hands, and dwelleth in kings' houses.€€ƒ`€€ƒxJA lion, the strongest of beasts, who hath no fear of any thing he meeteth:€€ƒxKA cock girded about the loins: and a ram: and a king, whom none can resist.€€ƒxˆThere is that hath appeared a fool after he was lifted up on high: for if he had understood, he would have laid his hand upon his mouth.€€ƒxÀAnd he that strongly squeezeth the paps to bring out milk, straineth out butter: and he that violently bloweth his nose, bringeth out blood: and he that provoketh wrath, bringeth forth strife.€€ƒ`€€ƒxIThe words of king Lamuel. The vision wherewith his mother instructed him.€€ƒxSWhat, O my beloved, what, O the beloved of my womb, what, O the beloved of my vows?€€ƒxAGive not thy substance to women, and thy riches to destroy kings.€€ƒxkGive not to kings, O Lamuel, give not wine to kings: because there is no secret where drunkenness reigneth:€€ƒx\And lest they drink and forget judgments, and pervert the cause of the children of the poor.€€—ƒxILet them drink, and forget their want, and remember their sorrow no more.€€ƒxNOpen thy mouth for the dumb, and for the causes of all the children that pass.€€ƒxPOpen thy mouth, decree that which is just, and do justice to the needy and poor.€€ƒxWWho shall find a valiant woman? far, and from the uttermost coasts is the price of her.€€ƒxNThe heart of her husband trusteth in her, and he shall have no need of spoils.€€ƒx@She will render him good, and not evil all the days of her life.€€ƒxLShe hath sought wool and flax, and hath wrought by the counsel of her hands.€€ƒxBShe is like the merchant's ship, she bringeth her bread from afar.€€ƒx`And she hath risen in the night, and given a prey to her household, and victuals to her maidens.€€ƒxdShe hath considered a field, and bought it: with the fruit of her hands she hath planted a vineyard.€€ƒxGShe hath girded her loins with strength, and hath strengthened her arm.€€ƒx_She hath tasted, and seen that her traffic is good: her lamp shall not be put out in the night.€€ƒx[She hath put out her hand to strong things, and her fingers have taken hold of the spindle.€€ƒxOShe hath opened her hand to the needy, and stretched out her hands to the poor.€€ƒxmShe shall not fear for her house in the cold of snow: for all her domestics are clothed with double garments.€€ƒxXShe hath made for herself clothing of tapestry: fine linen, and purple, is her covering.€€ƒxWHer husband is honourable in the gates, when he sitteth among the senators of the land.€€ƒxKShe made fine linen, and sold it, and delivered a girdle to the Chanaanite.€€ƒxLStrength and beauty are her clothing, and she shall laugh in the latter day.€€ƒxNShe hath opened her mouth to wisdom, and the law of clemency is on her tongue.€€ƒxRShe hath looked well on the paths of her house, and hath not eaten her bread idle.€€ƒxNHer children rose up, and called her blessed: her husband, and he praised her.€€ƒxKMany daughters have gathered together riches: thou hast surpassed them all.€€ŒƒxNGive her of the fruit of her hands: and let her works praise her in the gates.€€ƒxThe Words of Ecclesiastes€€ƒ`€€ƒx?The words of Ecclesiastes, the son of David, king of Jerusalem.€€ƒxMVanity of vanities, said Ecclesiastes: vanity of vanities, and all is vanity.€€ƒxEWhat hath a man more of all his labour, that he taketh under the sun?€€ƒx\One generation passeth away, and another generation cometh: but the earth standeth for ever.€€ƒxSThe sun riseth, and goeth down, and returneth to his place: and there rising again,€€ƒx˜Maketh his round by the south, and turneth again to the north: the spirit goeth forward surveying all places round about, and returneth to his circuits.€€ƒx‡All the rivers run into the sea, yet the sea doth not overflow: unto the place from whence the rivers come, they return, to flow again.€€ƒx€All things are hard: man cannot explain them by word. The eye is not filled with seeing, neither is the ear filled with hearing.€€ƒxuWhat is it that hath been? the same thing that shall be. What is it that hath been done? the same that shall be done.€€•ƒx«There is no remembrance of former things: nor indeed of those things which hereafter are to come, shall there be any remembrance with them that shall be in the latter end.€€ƒx1I Ecclesiastes was king over Israel in Jerusalem,€€ƒxÂAnd I proposed in my mind to seek and search out wisely concerning all things that are done under the sun. This painful occupation hath God given to the children of men, to be exercised therein.€€ƒxeI have seen all things that are done under the sun, and behold all is vanity, and vexation of spirit.€€ƒxKThe perverse are hard to be corrected, and the number of fools is infinite.€€ƒxÈI have spoken in my heart, saying: Behold I am become great, and have gone beyond all in wisdom, that were before me in Jerusalem: and my mind hath contemplated many things wisely, and I have learned.€€ƒx¢And I have given my heart to know prudence, and learning, and errors, and folly: and I have perceived that in these also there was labour, and vexation of spirit,€€ƒxcBecause in much wisdom there is much indignation: and he that addeth knowledge, addeth also labour.€€ƒ`€€ƒxtI said in my heart: I will go, and abound with delights, and enjoy good things. And I saw that this also was vanity.€€ƒxLLaughter I counted error: and to mirth I said: Why art thou vainly deceived?€€ƒxõI thought in my heart, to withdraw my flesh from wine, that I might turn my mind to wisdom, and might avoid folly, till I might see what was profitable for the children of men: and what they ought to do under the sun, all the days of their life.€€ƒx@I made me great works, I built me houses, and planted vineyards,€€ƒxCI made gardens, and orchards, and set them with trees of all kinds,€€ƒxMAnd I made me ponds of water, to water therewith the wood of the young trees,€€ƒx™I got me menservants, and maidservants, and had a great family: and herds of oxen, and great flocks of sheep, above all that were before me in Jerusalem:€€ƒxĞI heaped together for myself silver and gold, and the wealth of kings, and provinces: I made me singing men, and singing women, and the delights of the sons of men, cups and vessels to serve to pour out wine:€€ƒx`And I surpassed in riches all that were before me in Jerusalem: my wisdom also remained with me.€€ƒxãAnd whatsoever my eyes desired, I refused them not: and I withheld not my heart from enjoying every pleasure, and delighting itself in the things which I had prepared: and esteemed this my portion, to make use of my own labour.€€ƒxÖAnd when I turned myself to all the works which my hands had wrought, and to the labours wherein I had laboured in vain, I saw in all things vanity, and vexation of mind, and that nothing was lasting under the sun.€€ƒxuI passed further to behold wisdom, and errors and folly, (What is man, said I that he can follow the King his maker?)€€ƒxuThe eyes of a wise man are in his head: the fool walketh in darkness: and I learned that they were to die both alike.€€ƒxÜAnd I said in my heart: If the death of the fool and mine shall be one, what doth it avail me, that I have applied myself more to the study of wisdom? And speaking with my own mind, I perceived that this also was vanity.€€ƒxÇFor there shall be no remembrance of the wise no more than of the fool forever, and the times to come shall cover all things together with oblivion: the learned dieth in like manner as the unlearned.€€ƒxAnd therefore I was weary of my life, when I saw that all things under the sun are evil, and all vanity and vexation of spirit.€€ƒxwAgain I hated all my application wherewith I had earnestly laboured under the sun, being like to have an heir after me,€€ƒx²Whom I know not whether he will be a wise man or a fool, and he shall have rule over all my labours with which I have laboured and been solicitous: and is there anything so vain?€€ƒxLWherefore I left off and my heart renounced labouring anymore under the sun.€€ƒxœFor when a man laboureth in wisdom, and knowledge, and carefulness, he leaveth what he hath gotten to an idle man: so this also is vanity, and a great evil.€€ƒx|For what profit shall a man have of all his labour, and vexation of spirit, with which he hath been tormented under the sun?€€ƒxrAll his days are full of sorrows and miseries, even in the night he doth not rest in mind: and is not this vanity?€€ƒxuIs it not better to eat and drink, and to shew his soul good things of his labours? and this is from the hand of God.€€ƒx1Who shall so feast and abound with delights as I?€€ƒy#God hath given to a man that is good in his sight, wisdom, and knowledge, and joy: but to the sinner he hath given vexation, and superfluous care, to heap up and to gather together, and to give it to him that hath pleased God: but this also is vanity, and a fruitless solicitude of the mind.€€˜ƒxNAll things have their season, and in their times all things pass under heaven.€€ƒxcA time to be born and a time to die. A time to plant, and a time to pluck up that which is planted.€€ƒxKA time to kill, and a time to heal. A time to destroy, and a time to build.€€ƒxJA time to weep, and a time to laugh. A time to mourn, and a time to dance.€€ƒxfA time to scatter stones, and a time to gather. A time to embrace, and a time to be far from embraces.€€ƒxKA time to get, and a time to lose. A time to keep, and a time to cast away.€€ƒxOA time to rend, and a time to sew. A time to keep silence, and a time to speak.€€ƒxIA time of love, and a time of hatred. A time of war, and a time of peace.€€ƒx!What hath man more of his labour?€€ƒxTI have seen the trouble, which God hath given the sons of men to be exercised in it.€€ƒx¸He hath made all things good in their time, and hath delivered the world to their consideration, so that man cannot find out the work which God hath made from the beginning to the end.€€ƒx]And I have known that there was no better thing than to rejoice, and to do well in this life.€€ƒx^For every man that eateth and drinketh, and seeth good of his labour, this is the gift of God.€€ƒx­I have learned that all the works which God hath made, continue for ever: we cannot add any thing, nor take away from those things which God hath made that he may be feared.€€ƒx‚That which hath been made, the same continueth: the things that shall be, have already been: and God restoreth that which is past.€€ƒx^I saw under the sun in the place of judgment wickedness, and in the place of justice iniquity.€€ƒxpAnd I said in my heart: God shall judge both the just and the wicked, and then shall be the time of every thing.€€ƒxjI said in my heart concerning the sons of men, that God would prove them, and shew them to be like beasts.€€ƒxÜTherefore the death of man, and of beasts is one, and the condition of them both is equal: as man dieth, so they also die: all things breathe alike, and man hath nothing more than beast: all things are subject to vanity.€€ƒx]And all things go to one place: of earth they were made, and into earth they return together.€€ƒxrWho knoweth if the spirit of the children of Adam ascend upward, and if the spirit of the beasts descend downward?€€ƒx°And I have found that nothing is better than for a man to rejoice in his work, and that this is his portion. For who shall bring him to know the things that shall be after him?€€ƒ`€€ƒxäI turned myself to other things, and I saw the oppressions that are done under the sun, and the tears of the innocent, and they had no comforter; and they were not able to resist their violence, being destitute of help from any.€€ƒx.And I praised the dead rather than the living:€€ƒxsAnd I judged him happier than them both, that is not yet born, nor hath seen the evils that are done under the sun.€€ƒx°Again I considered all the labours of men, and I remarked that their industries are exposed to the envy of their neighbour: so in this also there is vanity, and fruitless care.€€ƒxFThe fool foldeth his hands together, and eateth his own flesh, saying:€€ƒxVBetter is a handful with rest, than both hands full with labour, and vexation of mind.€€˜ƒyThere is but one, and he hath not a second, no child, no brother, and yet he ceaseth not to labour, neither are his eyes satisfied with riches, neither doth he reflect, saying: For whom do I labour, and defraud my soul of good things? in this also is vanity, and a grievous vexation.€€ƒxkIt is better therefore that two should be together, than one: for they have the advantage of their society:€€ƒx{If one fall he shall be supported by the other: woe to him that is alone, for when he falleth, he hath none to lift him up.€€ƒxTAnd if two lie together, they shall warm one another: how shall one alone be warmed?€€ƒxaAnd if a man prevail against one, two shall withstand him: a threefold cord is not easily broken.€€ƒxwBetter is a child that is poor and wise, than a king that is old and foolish, who knoweth not to foresee for hereafter.€€ƒx{Because out of prison and chains sometimes a man cometh forth to a kingdom: and another born king is consumed with poverty.€€ƒxhI saw all men living, that walk under the sun with the second young man, who shall rise up in his place.€€ƒx²The number of the people, of all that were before him is infinite: and they that shall come afterwards, shall not rejoice in him: but this also is vanity, and vexation of spirit.€€ƒx¥Keep thy foot, when thou goest into the house of God, and draw nigh to hear. For much better is obedience, than the victims of fools, who know not what evil they do.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¡Speak not any thing rashly, and let not thy heart be hasty to utter a word before God. For God is in heaven, and thou upon earth: therefore let thy words be few.€€ƒxADreams follow many cares: and in many words shall be found folly.€€ƒx˜If thou hast vowed any thing to God, defer not to pay it: for an unfaithful and foolish promise displeaseth him: but whatsoever thou hast vowed, pay it.€€ƒxVAnd it is much better not to vow, than after a vow not to perform the things promised.€€ƒx«Give not thy mouth to cause thy flesh to sin: and say not before the angel: There is no providence: lest God be angry at thy words, and destroy all the works of thy hands.€€ƒxeWhere there are many dreams, there are many vanities, and words without number: but do thou fear God.€€ƒxŞIf thou shalt see the oppressions of the poor, and violent judgments, and justice perverted in the province, wonder not at this matter: for he that is high hath another higher, and there are others still higher than these:€€ƒxJMoreover there is the king that reigneth over all the land subject to him.€€ƒx‚A covetous man shall not be satisfied with money: and he that loveth riches shall reap no fruit from them: so this also is vanity.€€ƒxWhere there are great riches, there are also many to eat them. And what doth it profit the owner, but that he seeth the riches with his eyes?€€ƒx{Sleep is sweet to a labouring man, whether he eat little or much: but the fulness of the rich will not suffer him to sleep.€€ƒxkThere is also another grievous evil, which I have seen under the sun: riches kept to the hurt of the owner.€€ƒxhFor they are lost with very great affliction: he hath begotten a son, who shall be in extremity of want.€€˜!ƒxxA most deplorable evil: as he came, so shall he return. What then doth it profit him that he hath laboured for the wind?€€ƒx]All the days of his life he eateth in darkness, and in many cares, and in misery, and sorrow.€€ƒxáThis therefore hath seemed good to me, that a man should eat and drink, and enjoy the fruit of his labour, wherewith he hath laboured under the sun, all the days of his life, which God hath given him: and this is his portion.€€ƒxµAnd every man to whom God hath given riches, and substance, and hath given him power to eat thereof, and to enjoy his portion, and to rejoice of his labour: this is the gift of God.€€ƒxeFor he shall not much remember the days of his life, because God entertaineth his heart with delight.€€ƒ`€€ƒxYThere is also another evil, which I have seen under the sun, and that frequent among men:€€ƒxèA man to whom God hath given riches, and substance, and honour, and his soul wanteth nothing of all that he desireth: yet God doth not give him power to eat thereof, but a stranger shall eat it up. This is vanity and a great misery.€€ƒxçIf a man beget a hundred children, and live many years, and attain to a great age, and his soul make no use of the goods of his substance, and he be without burial: of this man I pronounce, that the untimely born is better than he.€€ƒxSFor he came in vain, and goeth to darkness, and his name shall be wholly forgotten.€€ƒxBHe hath not seen the sun, nor known the distance of good and evil:€€ƒxkAlthough he lived two thousand years, and hath not enjoyed good things: do not all make haste to one place?€€ƒxIAll the labour of man is for his mouth, but his soul shall not be filled.€€ƒxiWhat hath the wise man more than the fool? and what the poor man, but to go thither, where there is life?€€ƒxBetter it is to see what thou mayst desire, than to desire that which thou canst not know. But this also is vanity, and presumption of spirit.€€ƒx—He that shall be, his name is already called: and it is known, that he is a man, and cannot contend in judgment with him that is stronger than himself.€€ƒx8There are many words that have much vanity in disputing.€€ƒ`€€ƒxûWhat needeth a man to seek things that are above him, whereas he knoweth not what is profitable for him in his life, in all the days of his pilgrimage, and the time that passeth like a shadow? Or who can tell him what shall be after him under the sun?€€ƒx`A good name is better than precious ointments: and the day of death than the day of one's birth.€€ƒx¦It is better to go to the house of mourning, than to the house of feasting: for in that we are put in mind of the end of all, and the living thinketh what is to come.€€ƒxoAnger is better than laughter: because by the sadness of the countenance the mind of the offender is corrected.€€ƒx^The heart of the wise is where there is mourning, and the heart of fools where there is mirth.€€ƒxWIt is better to be rebuked by a wise man, than to be deceived by the flattery of fools.€€ƒxjFor as the crackling of thorns burning under a pot, so is the laughter of a fool: now this also is vanity.€€ƒxKOppression troubleth the wise, and shall destroy the strength of his heart.€€ƒxbBetter is the end of a speech than the beginning. Better is the patient man than the presumptuous.€€ƒx?Be not quickly angry: for anger resteth in the bosom of a fool.€€ƒxSay not: What thinkest thou is the cause that former times were better than they are now? for this manner of question is foolish.€€ƒx\Wisdom with riches is more profitable, and bringeth more advantage to them that see the sun.€€ƒxŠFor as wisdom is a defence, so money is a defence: but learning and wisdom excel in this, that they give life to him that possesseth them.€€ƒxIConsider the works of God, that no man can correct whom he hath despised.€€ƒx­In the good day enjoy good things, and beware beforehand of the evil day: for God hath made both the one and the other, that man may not find against him any just complaint.€€‰ƒxRBe not over just: and be not more wise than is necessary, lest thou become stupid.€€ƒxJBe not overmuch wicked: and be not foolish, lest thou die before thy time.€€ƒx„It is good that thou shouldst hold up the just, yea and from him withdraw not thy hand: for he that feareth God, neglecteth nothing.€€ƒxDWisdom hath strengthened the wise more than ten princes of the city.€€ƒxEFor there is no just man upon earth, that doth good, and sinneth not.€€ƒxjBut do not apply thy heart to all words that are spoken: lest perhaps thou hear thy servant reviling thee.€€ƒxKFor thy conscience knoweth that thou also hast often spoken evil of others.€€ƒx`I have tried all things in wisdom. I have said: I will be wise: and it departed farther from me,€€ƒxBMuch more than it was: it is a great depth, who shall find it out?€€”ƒxÙAnd I have found a woman more bitter than death, who is the hunter's snare, and her heart is a net, and her hands are bands. He that pleaseth God shall escape from her: but he that is a sinner, shall be caught by her.€€ƒxmLo this have I found, said Ecclesiastes, weighing one thing after another, that I might find out the account,€€ƒxƒWhich yet my soul seeketh, and I have not found it. One man among a thousand I have found, a woman among them all I have not found.€€ƒx´Only this I have found, that God made man right, and he hath entangled himself with an infinity of questions. Who is as the wise man? and who hath known the resolution of the word?€€ƒ`€€ƒxYThe wisdom of a man shineth in his countenance, and the most mighty will change his face.€€ƒxII observe the mouth of the king, and the commandments of the oath of God.€€ƒxpBe not hasty to depart from his face, and do not continue in an evil work: for he will do all that pleaseth him:€€ƒxPAnd his word is full of power: neither can any man say to him: Why dost thou so?€€ƒxlHe that keepeth the commandment, shall find no evil. The heart of a wiser man understandeth time and answer.€€ƒxQThere is a time and opportunity for every business, and great affliction for man:€€ƒxZBecause he is ignorant of things past, and things to come he cannot know by any messenger.€€ƒxµIt is not in man's power to stop the spirit, neither hath he power in the day of death, neither is he suffered to rest when war is at hand, neither shall wickedness save the wicked.€€ƒxAll these things I have considered, and applied my heart to all the works that are done under the sun. Sometimes one man ruleth over another to his own hurt.€€ƒxŸI saw the wicked buried: who also when they were yet living were in the holy place, and were praised in the city as men of just works: but this also is vanity.€€ƒxtFor because sentence is not speedily pronounced against the evil, the children of men commit evils without any fear.€€ƒx£But though a sinner do evil a hundred times, and by patience be borne withal, I know from thence that it shall be well with them that fear God, who dread his face.€€ƒx‘But let it not be well with the wicked, neither let his days be prolonged, but as a shadow let them pass away that fear not the face of the Lord.€€ƒyThere is also another vanity, which is done upon the earth. There are just men to whom evils happen, as though they had done the works of the wicked: and there are wicked men, who are as secure as though they had the deeds of the just: but this also I judge most vain.€€ƒxñTherefore I commended mirth, because there was no good for a man under the sun, but to eat, and drink, and be merry, and that he should take nothing else with him of his labour in the days of his life, which God hath given him under the sun.€€˜ƒyAnd I understood that man can find no reason of all those works of God that are done under the sun: and the more he shall labour to seek, so much the less shall he find: yea, though the wise man shall say, that he knoweth it, he shall not be able to find it.€€ƒ`€€ƒxáAll these things have I considered in my heart, that I might carefully understand them: there are just men and wise men, and their works are in the hand of God: and yet man knoweth not whether he be worthy of love, or hatred:€€ƒyRBut all things are kept uncertain for the time to come, because all things equally happen to the just and to the wicked, to the good and to the evil, to the clean and to the unclean, to him that offereth victims, and to him that despiseth sacrifices. As the good is, so also is the sinner: as the perjured, so he also that sweareth truth.€€ƒy
This is a very great evil among all things that are done under the sun, that the same things happen to all men: whereby also the hearts of the children of men are filled with evil, and with contempt while they live, and afterwards they shall be brought down to hell.€€ƒxeThere is no man that liveth always, or that hopeth for this: a living dog is better than a dead lion.€€ƒx’For the living know that they shall die, but the dead know nothing more, neither have they a reward any more: for the memory of them is forgotten.€€ƒx™Their love also, and their hatred, and their envy are all perished, neither have they any part in this world, and in the work that is done under the sun.€€ƒxdGo then, and eat thy bread with joy, and drink thy wine with gladness: because thy works please God.€€ƒxMAt all times let thy garments be white, and let not oil depart from thy head.€€ƒxîLive joyfully with the wife whom thou lovest, all the days of thy unsteady life, which are given to thee under the sun, all the time of thy vanity: for this is thy portion in life, and in thy labour wherewith thou labourest under the sun.€€ƒx™Whatsoever thy hand is able to do, do it earnestly: for neither work, nor reason, nor wisdom, nor knowledge shall be in hell, whither thou art hastening.€€ƒxàI turned me to another thing, and I saw that under the sun, the race is not to the swift, nor the battle to the strong, nor bread to the wise, nor riches to the learned, nor favour to the skilful: but time and chance in all.€€ƒxµMan knoweth not his own end: but as fishes are taken with the hook, and as birds are caught with the snare, so men are taken in the evil time, when it shall suddenly come upon them.€€ƒxQThis wisdom also I have seen under the sun, and it seemed to me to be very great:€€ƒx”A little city, and few men in it: there came against it a great king, and invested it, and built bulwarks round about it, and the siege was perfect.€€ƒx†Now there was found in it a man poor and wise, and he delivered the city by his wisdom, and no man afterward remembered that poor man.€€ƒxyAnd I said that wisdom is better than strength: how then is the wisdom of the poor man slighted, and his words not heard?€€ƒxVThe words of the wise are heard in silence, more than the cry of a prince among fools.€€ƒxdBetter is wisdom, than weapons of war: and he that shall offend in one, shall lose many good things.€€ƒ`€€ƒxuDying flies spoil the sweetness of the ointment. Wisdom and glory is more precious than a small and shortlived folly.€€ƒxZThe heart of a wise man is in his right hand, and the heart of a fool is in his left hand.€€ƒxdYea, and the fool when he walketh in the way, whereas he himself is a fool, esteemeth all men fools.€€‘ƒxoThere is an evil that I have seen under the sun, as it were by an error proceeding from the face of the prince:€€ƒx9A fool set in high dignity, and the rich sitting beneath.€€ƒxPI have seen servants upon horses: and princes walking on the ground as servants.€€ƒxbHe that diggeth a pit, shall fall into it: and he that breaketh a hedge, a serpent shall bite him.€€ƒxdHe that removeth stones, shall be hurt by them: and he that cutteth trees, shall be wounded by them.€€ƒxIf the iron be blunt, and be not as before, but be made blunt, with much labour it shall be sharpened: and after industry shall follow wisdom.€€ƒxLIf a serpent bite in silence, he is nothing better that backbiteth secretly.€€ƒxeThe words of the mouth of a wise man are grace: but the lips of a fool shall throw him down headlong.€€ƒxTThe beginning of his words is folly, and the end of his talk is a mischievous error.€€ƒxuA fool multiplieth words. A man cannot tell what hath been before him: and what shall be after him, who can tell him?€€ƒxKThe labour of fools shall afflict them that know not how to go to the city.€€ƒxWWoe to thee, O land, when thy king is a child, and when the princes eat in the morning.€€ƒxwBlessed is the land, whose king is noble, and whose princes eat in due season for refreshment, and not for riotousness.€€ƒxrBy slothfulness a building shall be brought down, and through the weakness of hands, the house shall drop through.€€ƒx\For laughter they make bread, and wine that the living may feast: and all things obey money.€€ƒxÕDetract not the king, no not in thy thought; and speak not evil of the rich man in thy private chamber: because even the birds of the air will carry thy voice, and he that hath wings will tell what thou hast said.€€ƒ`€€ŠƒxcGive a portion to seven, and also to eight: for thou knowest not what evil shall be upon the earth.€€ƒx¥If the clouds be full, they will pour out rain upon the earth. If the tree fall to the south, or to the north, in what place soever it shall fall, there shall it be.€€ƒx`He that observeth the wind, shall not sow: and he that considereth the clouds, shall never reap.€€ƒx¾As thou knowest not what is the way of the spirit, nor how the bones are joined together in the womb of her that is with child: so thou knowest not the works of God, who is the maker of all.€€ƒx´In the morning sow thy seed, and in the evening let not thy hand cease: for thou knowest not which may rather spring up, this or that: and if both together, it shall be the better.€€ƒxEThe light is sweet, and it is delightful for the eyes to see the sun.€€ƒx·If a man live many years, and have rejoiced in them all, he must remember the darksome time, and the many days: which when they shall come, the things past shall be accused of vanity.€€ƒxòRejoice therefore, O young man, in thy youth, and let thy heart be in that which is good in the days of thy youth, and walk in the ways of thy heart, and in the sight of thy eyes: and know that for all these God will bring thee into judgment.€€ƒx_Remove anger from thy heart, and put away evil from thy flesh. For youth and pleasure are vain.€€ƒ`€€–ƒxmBefore the sun, and the light, and the moon, and the stars be darkened, and the clouds return after the rain:€€ƒx¸When the keepers of the house shall tremble, and the strong men shall stagger, and the grinders shall be idle in a small number, and they that look through the holes shall be darkened:€€ƒx´And they shall shut the doors in the street, when the grinder's voice shall be low, and they shall rise up at the voice of the bird, and all the daughters of music shall grow deaf.€€ƒyAnd they shall fear high things, and they shall be afraid in the way, the almond tree shall flourish, the locust shall be made fat, and the caper tree shall be destroyed: because man shall go into the house of his eternity, and the mourners shall go round about in the street.€€ƒxšBefore the silver cord be broken, and the golden fillet shrink back, and the pitcher be crushed at the fountain, and the wheel be broken upon the cistern,€€ƒxbAnd the dust return into its earth, from whence it was, and the spirit return to God, who gave it.€€ƒxAVanity of vanities, said Ecclesiastes, and all things are vanity.€€ƒx”And whereas Ecclesiastes was very wise, he taught the people, and declared the things that he had done: and seeking out, he set forth many parables.€€ƒxJHe sought profitable words, and wrote words most right, and full of truth.€€ƒxThe words of the wise are as goads, and as nails deeply fastened in, which by the counsel of masters are given from one shepherd.€€ƒxyMore than these, my son, require not. Of making many books there is no end: and much study is an affliction of the flesh.€€ƒxsLet us all hear together the conclusion of the discourse. Fear God, and keep his commandments: for this is all man:€€ƒxgAnd all things that are done, God will bring into judgment for every error, whether it be good or evil.€€ƒsThe Song of Solomon€€ƒ`€€ƒxQLet him kiss me with the kiss of his mouth: for thy breasts are better than wine,€€ƒxmSmelling sweet of the best ointments. Thy name is as oil poured out: therefore young maidens have loved thee.€€ƒxÑDraw me: we will run after thee to the odour of thy ointments. The king hath brought me into his storerooms: we will be glad and rejoice in thee, remembering thy breasts more than wine: the rightous love thee.€€ƒxiI am black but beautiful, O ye daughters of Jerusalem, as the tents of Cedar, as the curtains of Solomon.€€ƒxÅDo not consider me that I am brown, because the sun hath altered my colour: the sons of my mother have fought against me, they have made me the keeper in the vineyards: my vineyard I have not kept.€€ƒx“Shew me, O thou whom my soul loveth, where thou feedest, where thou liest in the midday, lest I begin to wander after the flocks of thy companions.€€ƒx™If thou know not thyself, O fairest among women, go forth, and follow after the steps of the flocks, and feed thy kids beside the tents of the shepherds.€€˜&ƒxAThy cheeks are beautiful as the turtledove's, thy neck as jewels.€€ƒx5We will make thee chains of gold, inlaid with silver.€€ƒxLWhile the king was at his repose, my spikenard sent forth the odour thereof.€€ƒxIA bundle of myrrh is my beloved to me, he shall abide between my breasts.€€ƒxCA cluster of cypress my love is to me, in the vineyards of Engaddi.€€ƒxVBehold thou are fair, O my love, behold thou are fair, thy eyes are as those of doves.€€ƒxEBehold thou art fair, my beloved, and comely. Our bed is flourishing.€€ƒxCThe beams of our houses are of cedar, our rafters of cypress trees.€€ƒ`€€ƒx:I am the flower of the field, and the lily of the valleys.€€ƒx<As the lily among thorns, so is my love among the daughters.€€ƒx£As the apple tree among the trees of the woods, so is my beloved among the sons. I sat down under his shadow, whom I desired: and his fruit was sweet to my palate.€€ƒxEHe brought me into the cellar of wine, he set in order charity in me.€€ƒxTStay me up with flowers, compass me about with apples: because I languish with love.€€ƒxDHis left hand is under my head, and his right hand shall embrace me.€€ƒx™I adjure you, O ye daughters of Jerusalem, by the roes, and the harts of the field, that you stir not up, nor make the beloved to awake, till she please.€€ƒx^The voice of my beloved, behold he cometh leaping upon the mountains, skipping over the hills.€€ƒx‰My beloved is like a roe, or a young hart. Behold he standeth behind our wall, looking through the windows, looking through the lattices.€€ƒxbBehold my beloved speaketh to me: Arise, make haste, my love, my dove, my beautiful one, and come.€€ƒx2For winter is now past, the rain is over and gone.€€ƒxqThe flowers have appeared in our land, the time of pruning is come: the voice of the turtle is heard in our land:€€ƒx„The fig tree hath put forth her green figs: the vines in flower yield their sweet smell. Arise, my love, my beautiful one, and come:€€ƒx£My dove in the clefts of the rock, in the hollow places of the wall, shew me thy face, let thy voice sound in my ears: for thy voice is sweet, and thy face comely.€€ƒxSCatch us the little foxes that destroy the vines: for our vineyard hath flourished.€€ƒx<My beloved to me, and I to him who feedeth among the lilies,€€ƒxƒTill the day break, and the shadows retire. Return: be like, my beloved, to a roe, or to a young hart upon the mountains of Bether.€€ƒ`€€ƒxUIn my bed by night I sought him whom my soul loveth: I sought him, and found him not.€€ƒx’I will rise, and will go about the city: in the streets and the broad ways I will seek him whom my soul loveth: I sought him, and I found him not.€€ƒxQThe watchmen who keep the city, found me: Have you seen him, whom my soul loveth?€€ƒx¾When I had a little passed by them, I found him whom my soul loveth: I held him: and I will not let him go, till I bring him into my mother's house, and into the chamber of her that bore me.€€ƒxI adjure you, O daughters of Jerusalem, by the roes and the harts of the fields, that you stir not up, nor awake my beloved, till she please.€€ƒx–Who is she that goeth up by the desert, as a pillar of smoke of aromatical spices, of myrrh, and frankincense, and of all the powders of the perfumer?€€ƒx\Behold threescore valiant ones of the most valiant of Israel, surrounded the bed of Solomon?€€ƒxlAll holding swords, and most expert in war: every man's sword upon his thigh, because of fears in the night.€€ƒx;King Solomon hath made him a litter of the wood of Libanus:€€ƒx’The pillars thereof he made of silver, the seat of gold, the going up of purple: the midst he covered with charity for the daughters of Jerusalem.€€ƒx¤Go forth, ye daughters of Sion, and see king Solomon in the diadem, wherewith his mother crowned him in the day of his espousal, in the day of the joy of his heart.€€–ƒx¯How beautiful art thou, my love, how beautiful art thou! thy eyes are doves' eyes, besides what is hid within. Thy hair is as flocks of goats, which come up from mount Galaad.€€ƒx‚Thy teeth as flocks of sheep, that are shorn, which come up from the washing, all with twins, and there is none barren among them.€€ƒx†Thy lips are as a scarlet lace: and thy speech sweet. Thy cheeks are as a piece of a pomegranate, besides that which lieth hid within.€€ƒx‚Thy neck, is as the tower of David, which is built with bulwarks: a thousand bucklers hang upon it, all the armour of valiant men.€€ƒxPThy two breasts like two young roes that are twins, which feed among the lilies.€€ƒxpTill the day break, and the shadows retire, I will go to the mountain of myrrh, and to the hill of frankincense.€€ƒx>Thou art all fair, O my love, and there is not a spot in thee.€€ƒxÅCome from Libanus, my spouse, come from Libanus, come: thou shalt be crowned from the top of Amana, from the top of Sanir and Hermon, from the dens of the lions, from the mountains of the leopards.€€ƒxThou hast wounded my heart, my sister, my spouse, thou hast wounded my heart with one of thy eyes, and with one hair of thy neck.€€ƒx How beautiful are thy breasts, my sister, my spouse! thy breasts are more beautiful than wine, and the sweet smell of thy ointments above all aromatical spices.€€ƒx“Thy lips, my spouse, are as a dropping honeycomb, honey and milk are under thy tongue; and the smell of thy garments, as the smell of frankincense.€€ƒxTMy sister, my spouse, is a garden enclosed, a garden enclosed, a fountain sealed up.€€ƒxaThy plants are a paradise of pomegranates with the fruits of the orchard. Cypress with spikenard.€€ƒx{Spikenard and saffron, sweet cane and cinnamon, with all the trees of Libanus, myrrh and aloes with all the chief perfumes.€€ƒx`The fountain of gardens: the well of living waters, which run with a strong stream from Libanus.€€ƒxpArise, O north wind, and come, O south wind, blow through my garden, and let the aromatical spices thereof flow.€€ƒ`€€ƒy?Let my beloved come into his garden, and eat the fruit of his apple trees. I am come into my garden, O my sister, my spouse, I have gathered my myrrh, with my aromatical spices: I have eaten the honeycomb with my honey, I have drunk my wine with my milk: eat, O friends, and drink, and be inebriated, my dearly beloved.€€ƒx½I sleep, and my heart watcheth: the voice of my beloved knocking: Open to me, my sister, my love, my dove, my undefiled: for my head is full of dew, and my locks of the drops of the nights.€€ƒxaI have put off my garment, how shall I put it on? I have washed my feet, how shall I defile them?€€ƒxTMy beloved put his hand through the key hole, and my bowels were moved at his touch.€€ƒxnI arose up to open to my beloved: my hands dropped with myrrh, and my fingers were full of the choicest myrrh.€€ˆƒx€The keepers that go about the city found me: they struck me: and wounded me: the keepers of the walls took away my veil from me.€€ƒxlI adjure you, O daughters of Jerusalem, if you find my beloved, that you tell him that I languish with love.€€ƒx¤What manner of one is thy beloved of the beloved, O thou most beautiful among women? what manner of one is thy beloved of the beloved, that thou hast so adjured us?€€ƒx7My beloved is white and ruddy, chosen out of thousands.€€ƒxVHis head is as the finest gold: his locks as branches of palm trees, black as a raven.€€ƒxjHis eyes as doves upon brooks of waters, which are washed with milk, and sit beside the plentiful streams.€€ƒxoHis cheeks are as beds of aromatical spices set by the perfumers. His lips are as lilies dropping choice myrrh.€€ƒxbHis hands are turned and as of gold, full of hyacinths. His belly as of ivory, set with sapphires.€€˜ƒxrHis throat most sweet, and he is all lovely: such is my beloved, and he is my friend, O ye daughters of Jerusalem.€€ƒx„Whither is thy beloved gone, O thou most beautiful among women? whither is thy beloved turned aside, and we will seek him with thee?€€ƒ`€€ƒxwMy beloved is gone down into his garden, to the bed of aromatical spices, to feed in the gardens, and to gather lilies.€€ƒxDI to my beloved, and my beloved to me, who feedeth among the lilies.€€ƒx^Thou art beautiful, O my love, sweet and comely as Jerusalem terrible as an army set in array.€€ƒxvTurn away thy eyes from me, for they have made me flee away. Thy hair is as a flock of goats, that appear from Galaad.€€ƒxsThy teeth as a flock of sheep, which come up from the washing, all with twins, and there is none barren among them.€€ƒxOThy cheeks are as the bark of a pomegranate, beside what is hidden within thee.€€ƒxXThere are threescore queens, and fourscore concubines, and young maidens without number.€€ƒxÕOne is my dove, my perfect one is but one, she is the only one of her mother, the chosen of her that bore her. The daughters saw her, and declared her most blessed: the queens and concubines, and they praised her.€€ƒxzWho is she that cometh forth as the morning rising, fair as the moon, bright as the sun, terrible as an army set in array?€€ƒxI went down into the garden of nuts, to see the fruits of the valleys, and to look if the vineyard had flourished, and the pomegranates budded.€€ƒx=I knew not: my soul troubled me for the chariots of Aminadab.€€ƒxEReturn, return, O Sulamitess: return, return that we may behold thee.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÖWhat shalt thou see in the Sulamitess but the companies of camps? How beautiful are thy steps in shoes, O prince's daughter! The joints of thy thighs are like jewels, that are made by the hand of a skilful workman.€€ƒxlThy navel is like a round bowl never wanting cups. Thy belly is like a heap of wheat, set about with lilies.€€ƒx7Thy two breasts are like two young roes that are twins.€€ƒxÀThy neck as a tower of ivory. Thy eyes like the fishpools in Hesebon, which are in the gate of the daughter of the multitude. Thy nose is as the tower of Libanus, that looketh toward Damascus.€€ƒxcThy head is like Carmel: and the hairs of thy head as the purple of the king bound in the channels.€€ƒx@How beautiful art thou, and how comely, my dearest, in delights!€€ƒxJThy stature is like to a palm tree, and thy breasts to clusters of grapes.€€ƒx¯I said: I will go up into the palm tree, and will take hold of the fruit thereof: and thy breasts shall be as the clusters of the vine: and the odour of thy mouth like apples.€€ƒxjThy throat like the best wine, worthy for my beloved to drink, and for his lips and his teeth to ruminate.€€ƒx/I to my beloved, and his turning is towards me.€€ƒxOCome, my beloved, let us go forth into the field, let us abide in the villages.€€ƒxºLet us get up early to the vineyards, let us see if the vineyard flourish, if the flowers be ready to bring forth fruits, if the pomegranates flourish: there will I give thee my breasts.€€ƒxoThe mandrakes give a smell. In our gates are all fruits: the new and the old, my beloved, I have kept for thee.€€ƒ`€€ƒx—Who shall give thee to me for my brother, sucking the breasts of my mother, that I may find thee without, and kiss thee, and now no man may despise me?€€˜ƒxAHis left hand under my head, and his right hand shall embrace me.€€ƒx`I adjure you, O daughters of Jerusalem, that you stir not up, nor awake my love till she please.€€ƒxËWho is this that cometh up from the desert, flowing with delights, leaning upon her beloved? Under the apple tree I raised thee up: there thy mother was corrupted, there she was defloured that bore thee.€€ƒx–Put me as a seal upon thy heart, as a seal upon thy arm, for love is strong as death, jealousy as hard as hell, the lamps thereof are fire and flames.€€ƒx¡Many waters cannot quench charity, neither can the floods drown it: if a man should give all the substance of his house for love, he shall despise it as nothing.€€ƒxqOur sister is little, and hath no breasts. What shall we do to our sister in the day when she is to be spoken to?€€ƒxzIf she be a wall: let us build upon it bulwarks of silver: if she be a door, let us join it together with boards of cedar.€€ƒxbI am a wall: and my breasts are as a tower since I am become in his presence as one finding peace.€€ƒxThe peaceable had a vineyard, in that which hath people: he let out the same to keepers, every man bringeth for the fruit thereof a thousand pieces of silver.€€ƒxwMy vineyard is before me. A thousand are for thee, the peaceable, and two hundred for them that keep the fruit thereof.€€ƒxOThou that dwellest in the gardens, the friends hearken: make me hear thy voice.€€ƒxoFlee away, O my beloved, and be like to the roe, and to the young hart upon the mountains of aromatical spices.€€ƒuThe Wisdom of Solomon€€ƒ`€€ƒxwLove justice, you that are the judges of the earth. Think of the Lord in goodness, and seek him in simplicity of heart:€€ƒxbFor he is found by them that tempt him not: and he sheweth himself to them that have faith in him.€€ƒx_For perverse thoughts separate from God: and his power, when it is tried, reproveth the unwise:€€ƒxUFor wisdom will not enter into a malicious soul, nor dwell in a body subject to sins.€€ƒx·For the Holy Spirit of discipline will flee from the deceitful, and will withdraw himself from thoughts that are without understanding, and he shall not abide when iniquity cometh in.€€ƒxÀFor the spirit of wisdom is benevolent, and will not acquit the evil speaker from his lips: for God is witness of his reins, and he is a true searcher of his heart, and a hearer of his tongue.€€ƒxzFor the Spirit of the Lord hath filled the whole world: and that which containeth all things, hath knowledge of the voice.€€ƒxkTherefore he that speaketh unjust things, cannot be hid, neither shall the chastising judgment pass him by.€€ƒx”For inquisition shall be made into the thoughts of the ungodly, and the hearing of his words shall come to God, to the chastising of his iniquities.€€ƒxYFor the ear of jealousy heareth all things, and the tumult of murmuring shall not be hid.€€ƒxÊKeep yourselves, therefore, from murmuring, which profiteth nothing, and refrain your tongue from detraction, for an obscure speech shall not go for nought: and the mouth that belieth, killeth the soul.€€ƒxdSeek not death in the error of your life, neither procure ye destruction by the works of your hands.€€ƒxRFor God made not death, neither hath he pleasure in the destruction of the living.€€ƒx±For he created all things that they might be: and he made the nations of the earth for health: and there is no poison of destruction in them, nor kingdom of hell upon the earth.€€ƒx&For justice is perpetual and immortal.€€˜ƒ`€€ƒxËFor they have said, reasoning with themselves, but not right: The time of our life is short and tedious, and in the end of a man there is no remedy, and no man hath been known to have returned from hell:€€ƒxœFor we are born of nothing, and after this we shall be as if we had not been: for the breath in our nostrils is smoke: and speech a spark to move our heart,€€ƒyWhich being put out, our body shall be ashes, and our spirit shall be poured abroad as soft air, and our life shall pass away as the trace of a cloud, and shall be dispersed as a mist, which is driven away by the beams of the sun, and overpowered with the heat thereof:€€ƒx\And our name in time shall be forgotten, and no man shall have any remembrance of our works.€€ƒxFor our time is as the passing of a shadow, and there is no going back of our end: for it is fast sealed, and no man returneth:€€ƒxvCome, therefore, and let us enjoy the good things that are present, and let us speedily use the creatures as in youth.€€ƒxeLet us fill ourselves with costly wine, and ointments: and let not the flower of the time pass by us.€€ƒxZLet us crown ourselves with roses, before they be withered: let no meadow escape our riot.€€ƒx€Let none of us go without his part in luxury: let us every where leave tokens of joy: for this is our portion, and this our lot.€€ƒxiLet us oppress the poor just man, and not spare the widow, nor honour the ancient grey hairs of the aged.€€ƒxbBut let our strength be the law of justice: for that which is feeble is found to be nothing worth.€€ƒxÖLet us, therefore, lie in wait for the just, because he is not for our turn, and he is contrary to our doings, and upbraideth us with transgressions of the law, and divulgeth against us the sins of our way of life.€€ƒxRHe boasteth that he hath the knowledge of God, and calleth himself the son of God.€€ƒx(He is become a censurer of our thoughts.€€ƒxnHe is grievous unto us, even to behold: for his life is not like other men's, and his ways are very different.€€ƒx³We are esteemed by him as triflers, and he abstaineth from our ways as from filthiness, and he preferreth the latter end of the just, and glorieth that he hath God for his father.€€ƒxyLet us see then if his words be true, and let us prove what shall happen to him, and we shall know what his end shall be.€€ƒxiFor if he be the true son of God, he will defend him, and will deliver him from the hands of his enemies.€€ƒxaLet us examine him by outrages and tortures, that we may know his meekness, and try his patience.€€ƒxbLet us condemn him to a most shameful death: for there shall be respect had unto him by his words.€€ƒxPThese things they thought, and were deceived: for their own malice blinded them.€€ƒxpAnd they knew not the secrets of God, nor hoped for the wages of justice, nor esteemed the honour of holy souls.€€ƒxTFor God created man incorruptible, and to the image of his own likeness he made him.€€ƒx8But by the envy of the devil, death came into the world:€€˜ƒ`€€ƒx`But the souls of the just are in the hand of God, and the torment of death shall not touch them.€€ƒxXIn the sight of the unwise they seemed to die: and their departure was taken for misery:€€ƒxKAnd their going away from us, for utter destruction: but they are in peace.€€ƒxYAnd though in the sight of men they suffered torments, their hope is full of immortality.€€ƒx|Afflicted in few things, in many they shall be well rewarded: because God hath tried them, and found them worthy of himself.€€ƒx“As gold in the furnace, he hath proved them, and as a victim of a holocaust, he hath received them, and in time there shall be respect had to them.€€ƒxKThe just shall shine, and shall run to and fro like sparks among the reeds.€€ƒxTThey shall judge nations, and rule over people, and their Lord shall reign for ever.€€ƒxThey that trust in him shall understand the truth: and they that are faithful in love, shall rest in him: for grace and peace are to his elect.€€ƒx~But the wicked shall be punished according to their own devices: who have neglected the just, and have revolted from the Lord.€€ƒxFor he that rejecteth wisdom, and discipline, is unhappy: and their hope is vain, and their labours without fruit, and their works unprofitable.€€ƒx3Their wives are foolish, and their children wicked.€€ƒxœTheir offspring is cursed, for happy is the barren: and the undefiled, that hath not known bed in sin, she shall have fruit in the visitation of holy souls.€€ƒxÊAnd the eunuch, that hath not wrought iniquity with his hands, nor thought wicked things against God for the precious gift of faith shall be given to him, and a most acceptable lot in the temple of God.€€ƒxPFor the fruit of good labours is glorious, and the root of wisdom never faileth.€€ƒxrBut the children of adulterers shall not come to perfection, and the seed of the unlawful bed shall be rooted out.€€ƒxfAnd if they live long, they shall be nothing regarded, and their last old age shall be without honour.€€ƒx\And if they die quickly, they shall have no hope, nor speech of comfort in the day of trial.€€ƒx+For dreadful are the ends of a wicked race.€€ƒ`€€ƒx†How beautiful is the chaste generation with glory: for the memory thereof is immortal: because it is known both with God and with men.€€ƒx¦When it is present, they imitate it: and they desire it, when it hath withdrawn itself, and it triumpheth crowned for ever, winning the reward of undefiled conflicts.€€ƒx}But the multiplied brood of the wicked shall not thrive, and bastard slips shall not take deep root, nor any fast foundation.€€ƒx And if they flourish in branches for a time, yet standing not fast, they shall be shaken with the wind, and through the force of winds they shall be rooted out.€€ƒx‚For the branches not being perfect, shall be broken, and their fruits shall be unprofitable, and sour to eat, and fit for nothing.€€ƒxrFor the children that are born of unlawful beds, are witnesses of wickedness against their parents in their trial.€€ƒxBBut the just man, if he be prevented with death, shall be in rest.€€˜ƒxAnd a spotless life is old age.€€ƒxMHe pleased God, and was beloved, and living among sinners, he was translated.€€ƒx^He was taken away, lest wickedness should alter his understanding, or deceit beguile his soul.€€ƒxuFor the bewitching of vanity obscureth good things, and the wandering of concupiscence overturneth the innocent mind.€€ƒx>Being made perfect in a short space, he fulfilled a long time.€€ƒx±For his soul pleased God: therefore he hastened to bring him out of the midst of iniquities: but the people see this, and understand not, nor lay up such things in their hearts:€€ƒx`That the grace of God, and his mercy is with his saints, and that he hath respect to his chosen.€€ƒxtBut the just that is dead, condemneth the wicked that are living, and youth soon ended, the long life of the unjust.€€ƒxFor they shall see the end of the wise man, and it shall not understand what God hath designed for him, and why the Lord hath set him in safety.€€ƒxRThey shall see him, and shall despise him: but the Lord shall laugh them to scorn.€€ƒyAnd they shall fall after this without honour, and be a reproach among the dead for ever: for he shall burst them puffed up and speechless, and shall shake them from the foundations, and they shall be utterly laid waste: they shall be in sorrow, and their memory shall perish.€€ƒxvThey shall come with fear at the thought of their sins, and their iniquities shall stand against them to convict them.€€ƒ`€€ƒxtThen shall the just stand with great constancy against those that have afflicted them, and taken away their labours.€€ƒx{These seeing it, shall be troubled with terrible fear, and shall be amazed at the suddenness of their unexpected salvation,€€ƒx™Saying within themselves, repenting, and groaning for anguish of spirit: These are they, whom we had sometime in derision, and for a parable of reproach.€€ƒxCWe fools esteemed their life madness, and their end without honour.€€ƒx[Behold, how they are numbered among the children of God, and their lot is among the saints.€€ƒx•Therefore we have erred from the way of truth, and the light of justice hath not shined unto us, and the sun of understanding hath not risen upon us.€€ƒxŠWe wearied ourselves in the way of iniquity and destruction, and have walked through hard ways, but the way of the Lord we have not known.€€ƒxVWhat hath pride profited us? or what advantage hath the boasting of riches brought us?€€ƒxPAll those things are passed away like a shadow, and like a post that runneth on,€€ƒxAnd as a ship, that passeth through the waves: whereof when it is gone by, the trace cannot be found. nor the path of its keel in the waters:€€ƒyOr as when a bird flieth through the air, of the passage of which no mark can be found, but only the sound of the wings beating the light air, and parting it by the force of her flight: she moved her wings, and hath flown through, and there is no mark found afterwards of her way:€€ƒxOr as when an arrow is shot at a mark, the divided air quickly cometh together again, so that the passage thereof is not known:€€ƒx€So we also being born, forthwith ceased to be: and have been able to shew no mark of virtue: but are consumed in our wickedness.€€ƒx.Such things as these the sinners said in hell:€€ƒxîFor the hope of the wicked is as dust, which is blown away with the wind, and as a thin froth which is dispersed by the storm: and a smoke that is scattered abroad by the wind: and as the remembrance of a guest of one day that passeth by.€€˜ƒx¶Therefore shall they receive a kingdom of glory, and a crown of beauty at the hand of the Lord: for with his right hand he will cover them, and with his holy arm he will defend them.€€ƒx[And his zeal will take armour, and he will arm the creature for the revenge of his enemies.€€ƒxYHe will put on justice as a breastplate, and will take true judgment instead of a helmet:€€ƒx-He will take equity for an invincible shield:€€ƒxnAnd he will sharpen his severe wrath for a spear, and the whole world shall fight with him against the unwise.€€ƒx‡Then shafts of lightning shall go directly from the clouds, as from a bow well bent, they shall be shot out, and shall fly to the mark.€€ƒxªAnd thick hail shall be cast upon them from the stone casting wrath: the water of the sea shall rage against them, and the rivers shall run together in a terrible manner.€€ƒxÄA mighty wind shall stand up against them, and as a whirlwind shall divide them: and their iniquity shall bring all the earth to a desert, and wickedness shall overthrow the thrones of the mighty.€€ƒ`€€ƒxKWisdom is better than strength: and a wise man is better than a strong man.€€ƒx]Hear, therefore, ye kings, and understand, learn ye that are judges of the ends of the earth.€€ƒxXGive ear, you that rule the people, and that please yourselves in multitudes of nations:€€ƒx}For power is given you by the Lord, and strength by the most High, who will examine your works: and search out your thoughts:€€ƒxŠBecause being ministers of his kingdom, you have not judged rightly, nor kept the law of justice, nor walked according to the will of God.€€ƒxiHorribly and speedily will he appear to you: for a most severe judgment shall be for them that bear rule.€€ƒxXFor to him that is little, mercy is granted: but the mighty shall be mightily tormented.€€ƒx¥For God will not except any man's person, neither will he stand in awe of any man's greatness: for he made the little and the great, and he hath equally care of all.€€ƒx6But a greater punishment is ready for the more mighty.€€ƒx`To you, therefore, O kings, are these my words, that you may learn wisdom, and not fall from it.€€ƒxƒFor they that have kept just things justly, shall be justified: and they that have learned these things, shall find what to answer.€€ƒxMCovet ye, therefore, my words, and love them, and you shall have instruction.€€ƒxxWisdom is glorious, and never fadeth away, and is easily seen by them that love her, and is found by them that seek her.€€ƒxPShe preventeth them that covet her, so that she first sheweth herself unto them.€€ƒx_He that awaketh early to seek her, shall not labour: for he shall find her sitting at his door.€€ƒxoTo think, therefore, upon her, is perfect understanding: and he that watcheth for her, shall quickly be secure.€€ƒx?For the beginning of her is the most true desire of discipline.€€ƒxŒAnd the care of discipline is love: and love is the keeping of her laws: and the keeping of her laws is the firm foundation of incorruption:€€ƒx&And incorruption bringeth near to God.€€ƒxCTherefore the desire of wisdom bringeth to the everlasting kingdom.€€ƒxuIf then your delight be in thrones, and sceptres, O ye kings of the people, love wisdom, that you may reign for ever.€€ƒx=Love the light of wisdom, all ye that bear rule over peoples.€€ƒxñNow what wisdom is, and what was her origin, I will declare: and I will not hide from you the mysteries of God, but will seek her out from the beginning of her birth, and bring the knowledge of her to light, and will not pass over the truth:€€ƒxVNeither will I go with consuming envy: for such a man shall not be partaker of wisdom.€€ƒxpNow the multitude of the wise is the welfare of the whole world: and a wise king is the upholding of the people.€€ƒxOReceive, therefore, instruction by my words, and it shall be profitable to you.€€ƒ`€€ƒxI myself am a mortal man, like all others, and of the race of him, that was first made of the earth, and in the womb of my mother I was fashioned to be flesh.€€ƒxmIn the time of ten months I was compacted in blood, of the seed of man, and the pleasure of sleep concurring.€€ƒx8I was nursed in swaddling clothes, and with great cares.€€ƒx7For none of the kings had any other beginning of birth.€€ƒx@For all men have one entrance into life, and the like going out.€€ƒxqWherefore I wished, and understanding was given me: and I called upon God, and the spirit of wisdom came upon me:€€ƒxbAnd I preferred her before kingdoms and thrones, and esteemed riches nothing in comparison of her.€€ƒx¤Neither did I compare unto her any precious stone: for all gold, in comparison of her, is as a little sand; and silver, in respect to her, shall be counted as clay.€€ƒxmI loved her above health and beauty, and chose to have her instead of light: for her light cannot be put out.€€ƒx[Now all good things came to me together with her, and innumerable riches through her hands,€€ƒxpAnd I rejoiced in all these: for this wisdom went before me, and I knew not that she was the mother of them all.€€ƒx\Which I have learned without guile, and communicate without envy, and her riches I hide not.€€ƒx„For she is an infinite treasure to men: which they that use, become the friends of God, being commended for the gifts of discipline.€€ƒx±And God hath given to me to speak as I would, and to conceive thoughts worthy of those things that are given me: because he is the guide of wisdom, and the director of the wise:€€ƒxaFor in his hand are both we, and our words, and all wisdom, and the knowledge and skill of works.€€ƒxŒFor he hath given me the true knowledge of the things that are: to know the disposition of the whole world, and the virtues of the elements,€€ƒxpThe beginning, and ending, and midst of the times, the alterations of their courses, and the changes of seasons,€€–ƒx™The natures of living creatures, and rage of wild beasts, the force of winds, and reasonings of men, the diversities of plants, and the virtues of roots,€€ƒx{And all such things as are hid, and not foreseen, I have learned: for wisdom, which is the worker of all things, taught me.€€ƒx¹For in her is the spirit of understanding; holy, one, manifold, subtile, eloquent, active, undefiled, sure, sweet, loving that which is good, quick, which nothing hindereth, beneficent,€€ƒx‹Gentle, kind, steadfast, assured, secure, having all power, overseeing all things, and containing all spirits: intelligible, pure, subtile:€€ƒxcFor wisdom is more active than all active things; and reacheth everywhere, by reason of her purity.€€ƒx˜For she is a vapour of the power of God, and a certain pure emmanation of the glory of the Almighty God: and therefore no defiled thing cometh into her.€€ƒxuFor she is the brightness of eternal light, and the unspotted mirror of God's majesty, and the image of his goodness.€€ƒxÈAnd being but one, she can do all things: and remaining in herself the same, she reneweth all things, and through nations conveyeth herself into holy souls, she maketh the friends of God and prophets.€€ƒx6For God loveth none but him that dwelleth with wisdom.€€ƒx„For she is more beautiful than the sun, and above all the order of the stars: being compared with the light, she is found before it.€€ƒx=For after this cometh night, but no evil can overcome wisdom.€€ƒ`€€ƒxSShe reacheth, therefore, from end to end mightily, and ordereth all things sweetly.€€ƒx„Her have I loved, and have sought her out from my youth, and have desired to take for my spouse, and I became a lover of her beauty.€€ƒxiShe glorifieth her nobility by being conversant with God: yea, and the Lord of all things hath loved her.€€ƒxQFor it is she that teacheth the knowledge of God and is the chooser of his works.€€ƒxVAnd if riches be desired in life, what is richer than wisdom, which maketh all things?€€ƒxTAnd if sense do work: who is a more artful worker than she of those things that are?€€ƒxÈAnd if a man love justice: her labours have great virtues: for she teacheth temperance, and prudence, and justice, and fortitude, which are such things as men can have nothing more profitable in life.€€ƒxøAnd if a man desire much knowledge: she knoweth things past, and judgeth of things to come: she knoweth the subtilties of speeches, and the solutions of arguments: she knoweth signs and wonders before they be done, and the events of times and ages.€€ƒx¢I purposed, therefore, to take her to me to live with me: knowing that she will communicate to me of her good things, and will be a comfort in my cares and grief.€€ƒxeFor her sake I shall have glory among the multitude, and honour with the ancients, though I be young:€€˜ƒx•They shall wait for me when I hold my peace, and they shall look upon me when I speak; and if I talk much, they shall lay their hands on their mouth.€€ƒxƒMoreover, by the means of her I shall have immortality: and shall leave behind me an everlasting memory to them that come after me.€€ƒxDI shall set the people in order: and nations shall be subject to me.€€ƒxmTerrible kings hearing, shall be afraid of me: among the multitude I shall be found good, and valiant in war.€€ƒx˜When I go into my house, I shall repose myself with her: for her conversation hath no bitterness, nor her company any tediousness, but joy and gladness.€€ƒxnThinking these things with myself, and pondering them in my heart, that to be allied to wisdom is immortality,€€ƒxşAnd that there is great delight in her friendship, and inexhaustible riches in the works of her hands, and in the exercise of conference with her, wisdom, and glory in the communication of her words: I went about seeking, that I might take her to myself.€€ƒx6And I was a witty child, and had received a good soul.€€ƒx8And whereas I was more good, I came to a body undefiled.€€ƒxÎAnd as I knew that I could not otherwise be continent, except God gave it, and this also was a point of wisdom, to know whose gift it was, I went to the Lord, and besought him, and said with my whole heart:€€ƒ`€€ƒxMGod of my fathers, and Lord of mercy, who hast made all things with thy word,€€ƒxkAnd by thy wisdom hast appointed man, that he should have dominion over the creature that was made by thee,€€ƒxjThat he should order the world according to equity and justice, and execute justice with an upright heart:€€ƒxXGive me wisdom, that sitteth by thy throne, and cast me not off from among thy children:€€ƒxFor I am thy servant, and the son of thy handmaid, a weak man, and of short time, and falling short of the understanding of judgment and laws.€€ƒxqFor if one be perfect among the children of men, yet if thy wisdom be not with him, he shall be nothing regarded.€€ƒxTThou hast chosen me to be king of thy people, and a judge of thy sons and daughters:€€ƒx½And hast commanded me to build a temple on thy holy mount, and an altar in the city of thy dwelling place, a resemblance of thy holy tabernacle, which thou hast prepared from the beginning:€€ƒx»And thy wisdom with thee, which knoweth thy works, which then also was present when thou madest the world, and knew what was agreeable to thy eyes, and what was right in thy commandments.€€ƒx£Send her out of thy holy heaven, and from the throne of thy majesty, that she may be with me, and may labour with me, that I may know what is acceptable with thee:€€ƒxxFor she knoweth and understandeth all things, and shall lead me soberly in my works, and shall preserve me by her power.€€ƒxvSo shall my works be acceptable, and I shall govern thy people justly, and shall be worthy of the throne of my father.€€ƒxcFor who among men is he that can know the counsel of God? or who can think what the will of God is?€€ƒxGFor the thoughts of mortal men are fearful, and our counsels uncertain.€€ƒxFor the corruptible body is a load upon the soul, and the earthly habitation presseth down the mind that museth upon many things.€€‹ƒx]And who shall know thy thought, except thou give wisdom, and send thy holy Spirit from above:€€ƒxlAnd so the ways of them that are upon earth may be corrected, and men may learn the things that please thee?€€ƒxXFor by wisdom they were healed, whosoever have pleased thee, O Lord, from the beginning.€€ƒ`€€ƒxdShe preserved him, that was first formed by God, the father of the world, when he was created alone,€€ƒxLAnd she brought him out of his sin, and gave him power to govern all things.€€ƒxoBut when the unjust went away from her in his anger, he perished by the fury wherewith he murdered his brother.€€ƒxFor whose cause, when water destroyed the earth, wisdom healed it again, directing the course of the just by contemptible wood.€€ƒx¾Moreover, when the nations had conspired together to consent to wickedness, she knew the just, and preserved him without blame to God, and kept him strong against the compassion for his son.€€ƒxrShe delivered the just man, who fled from the wicked that were perishing, when the fire came down upon Pentapolis:€€ƒxÄWhose land, for a testimony of their wickedness, is desolate, and smoketh to this day, and the trees bear fruits that ripen not, and a standing pillar of salt is a monument of an incredulous soul.€€—ƒx@But wisdom hath delivered from sorrow them that attend upon her.€€ƒxêShe conducted the just, when he fled from his brother's wrath, through the right ways, and shewed him the kingdom of God, and gave him the knowledge of the holy things, made him honourable in his labours, and accomplished his labours.€€ƒxVIn the deceit of them that overreached him, she stood by him, and made him honourable.€€ƒxªShe kept him safe from his enemies, and she defended him from seducers, and gave him a strong conflict, that he might overcome, and know that wisdom is mightier than all.€€ƒxoShe forsook not the just when he was sold, but delivered him from sinners: she went down with him into the pit.€€ƒxÍAnd in bands she left him not, till she brought him the sceptre of the kingdom, and power against those that oppressed him: and shewed them to be liars that had accused him, and gave him everlasting glory.€€ƒxXShe delivered the just people, and blameless seed, from the nations that oppressed them.€€ƒxfShe entered into the soul of the servant of God and stood against dreadful kings in wonders and signs.€€ƒx­And she rendered to the just the wages of their labours, and conducted them in a wonderful way: and she was to them for a covert by day, and for the light of stars by night:€€ƒxVAnd she brought them through the Red Sea, and carried them over through a great water.€€ƒxŒBut their enemies she drowned in the sea, and from the depth of hell she brought them out. Therefore the just took the spoils of the wicked.€€ƒx]And they sung to thy holy name, O Lord, and they praised with one accord thy victorious hand.€€ƒxRFor wisdom opened the mouth of the dumb, and made the tongues of infants eloquent.€€ƒ`€€ƒx;She prospered their works in the hands of the holy prophet.€€ƒxfThey went through wildernesses that were not inhabited, and in desert places they pitched their tents.€€ƒxOThey stood against their enemies, and revenged themselves of their adversaries.€€ƒx•They were thirsty, and they called upon thee, and water was given them out of the high rock, and a refreshment of their thirst out of the hard stone.€€ƒxŒFor by what things their enemies were punished, when their drink failed them, while the children of Israel abounded therewith, and rejoiced:€€ƒx5By the same things they in their need were benefited.€€ƒxZFor instead of a fountain of an ever running river, thou gavest human blood to the unjust.€€ƒxˆAnd whilst they were diminished for a manifest reproof of their murdering the infants, thou gavest to thine abundant water unlooked for:€€ƒxbShewing by the thirst that was then, how thou didst exalt thine, and didst kill their adversaries.€€ƒxsFor thou didst admonish and try them as a father: but the others, as a severe king, thou didst examine and condemn.€€ƒx9For whether absent or present, they were tormented alike.€€ƒxZFor a double affliction came upon them, and a groaning for the remembrance of things past.€€ƒx‘For when they heard that by their punishments the others were benefited, they remembered the Lord, wondering at the end of what was come to pass.€€ƒxÑFor whom they scorned before, when he was thrown out at the time of his being wickedly exposed to perish, him they admired in the end, when they saw the event: their thirsting being unlike to that of the just.€€ƒx½But for the foolish devices of their iniquity, because some being deceived worshipped dumb serpents and worthless beasts, thou didst send upon them a multitude of dumb beasts for vengeance:€€ƒxYThat they might know that by what things a man sinneth, by the same also he is tormented.€€ƒx‹For thy almighty hand, which made the world of matter without form, was not unable to send upon them a multitude of bears, or fierce lions,€€ƒx¥Or unknown beasts of a new kind, full of rage; either breathing out a fiery vapour, or sending forth a stinking smoke, or shooting horrible sparks out of their eyes:€€ƒxnWhereof not only the hurt might be able to destroy them, but also the very sight might kill them through fear.€€ƒxĞYea, and without these, they might have been slain with one blast, persecuted by their own deeds, and scattered by the breath of thy power: but thou hast ordered all things in measure, and number, and weight.€€ƒx\For great power always belonged to thee alone: and who shall resist the strength of thy arm?€€ƒx‰For the whole world before thee is as the least grain of the balance, and as a drop of the morning dew, that falleth down upon tho earth.€€ƒx{But thou hast mercy upon all, because thou canst do all things, and overlookest the sins of men for the sake of repentance.€€ƒx‘For thou lovest all things that are, and hatest none of the things which thou hast made: for thou didst not appoint, or make any thing hating it.€€ŒƒxGBut thou sparest all: because they are thine, O Lord, who lovest souls.€€ƒ`€€ƒx:O how good and sweet is thy Spirit, O Lord, in all things!€€ƒxÚAnd therefore thou chastisest them that err, by little and little: and admonishest them, and speakest to them, concerning the things wherein they offend: that leaving their wickedness, they may believe in thee, O Lord.€€ƒxFFor those ancient inhabitants of thy holy land, whom thou didst abhor,€€ƒxQBecause they did works hateful to thee by their sorceries, and wicked sacrifices,€€ƒx‹And those merciless murderers of their own children, and eaters of men's bowels, and devourers of blood from the midst of thy consecration,€€ƒxzAnd those parents sacrificing with their own hands helpless souls, it was thy will to destroy by the hands of our parents,€€ƒxfThat the land which of all is most dear to thee, might receive a worthy colony of the children of God.€€ƒxxYet even those thou sparedst as men, and didst send wasps forerunners of thy host, to destroy them by little and little.€€ƒxˆNot that thou wast unable to bring the wicked under the just by war, or by cruel beasts, or with one rough word to destroy them at once:€€ƒxÍBut executing thy judgments by degrees, thou gavest them place of repentance, not being ignorant that they were a wicked generation, and their malice natural, and that their thought could never be changed.€€‘ƒxÚFor who shall say to thee: What hast thou done? or who shall withstand thy judgment? or who shall come before thee to be a revenger of wicked men? or who shall accuse thee, if the nations perish, which thou hast made ?€€ƒx|For there is no other God but thou, who hast care of all, that thou shouldst shew that thou dost not give judgment unjustly.€€ƒxYNeither shall king, nor tyrant, in thy sight inquire about them whom thou hast destroyed.€€ƒxFor so much then, as thou art just, thou orderest all things justly: thinking it not agreeable to the power, to condemn him who deserveth not to be punished.€€ƒxqFor thy power is the beginning of justice: and because thou art Lord of all, thou makest thyself gracious to all.€€ƒxFor thou shewest thy power, when men will not believe thee to be absolute in power, and thou convincest the boldness of them that know thee not.€€ƒxŠBut thou being master of power, judgest with tranquillity, and with great favour disposest of us: for thy power is at hand when thou wilt.€€ƒxÁBut thou hast taught thy people by such works, that they must be just and humane, and hast made thy children to be of a good hope: because in judging, thou givest place for repentance for sins.€€ƒx»For if thou didst punish the enemies of thy servants, and that deserved to die, with so great deliberation, giving them time and place whereby they might be changed from their wickedness:€€ƒx‚With what circumspection hast thou judged thy own children, to whose parents thou hast sworn, and made covenants of good promises?€€ƒx¿Therefore whereas thou chastisest us, thou scourgest our enemies very many ways, to the end that when we judge we may think on thy goodness: and when we are judged, we may hope for thy mercy.€€ƒx‘Wherefore thou hast also greatly tormented them, who, in their life, have lived foolishly and unjustly, by the same things which they worshipped.€€ƒx¾For they went astray for a long time in the ways of error, holding those things for gods which are the most worthless among beasts, living after the manner of children without understanding.€€ƒxSTherefore thou hast sent a judgment upon them, as senseless children, to mock them.€€ƒxfBut they that were not amended by mockeries and reprehensions, experienced the worthy judgment of God.€€ƒyFor seeing, with indignation, that they suffered by those very things which they took for gods, when they were destroyed by the same, they acknowledged him the true God, whom in time past they denied that they knew: for which cause the end also of their condemnation came upon them.€€ƒ`€€ƒxªBut have imagined either the fire, or the wind, or the swift air, or the circle of the stars, or the great water, or the sun and moon, to be the gods that rule the world.€€ƒx½With whose beauty, if they, being delighted, took them to be gods: let them know how much the Lord of them is more beautiful than they: for the first author of beauty made all those things.€€ƒx~Or if they admired their power, and their effects, let them understand by them, that he that made them, is mightier than they:€€ƒxtFor by the greatness of the beauty, and of the creature, the creator of them may be seen, so as to be known thereby.€€ƒxlBut yet as to these they are less to be blamed. For they perhaps err, seeking God, and desirous to find him.€€ƒxrFor being conversant among his works, they search: and they are persuaded that the things are good which are seen.€€ƒx+But then again they are not to be pardoned.€€ƒxFor if they were able to know so much as to make a judgment of the world: how did they not more easily find out the Lord thereof?€€ƒxèBut unhappy are they, and their hope is among the dead, who have called gods the works of the hand of men, gold and silver, the inventions of art, and the resemblances of beasts, or an unprofitable stone the work of an ancient hand.€€ƒxÖOr if an artist, a carpenter, hath cut down a tree proper for his use in the wood, and skilfully taken off all the bark thereof, and with his art, diligently formeth a vessel profitable for the common uses of life,€€ƒx2And useth the chips of his work to dress his meat:€€ƒx÷And taking what was left thereof, which is good for nothing, being a crooked piece of wood, and full of knots, carveth it diligently when he hath nothing else to do, and by the skill of his art fashioneth it, and maketh it like the image of a man:€€ƒx|Or the resemblance of some beast, laying it over with vermilion, and painting it red, and covering every spot that is in it:€€Œƒx{Providing for it, lest it should fall, knowing that it is unable to help itself: for it is an image, and hath need of help.€€ƒxŸAnd then maketh prayer to it, enquiring concerning his substance, and his children, or his marriage. And he is not ashamed to speak to that which hath no life:€€ƒx”And for health he maketh supplication to the weak, and for life prayeth to that which is dead, and for help calleth upon that which is unprofitable:€€ƒx«And for a good journey he petitioneth him that cannot walk: and for getting, and for working, and for the event of all things he asketh him that is unable to do any thing.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¥Again, another designing to sail, and beginning to make his voyage through the raging waves, calleth upon a piece of wood more frail than the wood that carrieth him.€€ƒxKFor this the desire of gain devised, and the workman built it by his skill.€€ƒx{But thy providence, O Father, governeth it: for thou hast made a way even in the sea, and a most sure path among the waves,€€ƒx`Shewing that thou art able to save out of all things, yea, though a man went to sea without art.€€ƒxœBut that the works of thy wisdom might not be idle: therefore men also trust their lives even to a little wood, and passing over the sea by ship, are saved.€€ƒx­And from the beginning also, when the proud giants perished, the hope of the world fleeing to a vessel, which was governed by thy hand, left to the world seed of generation.€€ƒx0For blessed is the wood, by which justice cometh€€•ƒx;But to God the wicked and his wickedness are hateful alike.€€ƒxNFor that which is made, together with him that made it, shall suffer torments.€€ƒxÒTherefore there shall be no respect had even to the idols of the Gentiles: because the creatures of God are turned to an abomination, and a temptation to the souls of men, and a snare to the feet of the unwise.€€ƒxoFor the beginning of fornication is the devising of idols: and the invention of them is the corruption of life.€€ƒxIFor neither were they from the beginning, neither shall they be for ever.€€ƒxoFor by the vanity of men they came into the world: and therefore they shall be found to come shortly to an end.€€ƒxòFor a father being afflicted with bitter grief, made to himself the image of his son, who was quickly taken away: and him who then had died as a man, he began now to worship as a god, and appointed him rites and sacrifices among his servants.€€ƒxŒThen, in process of time, wicked custom prevailing, this error was kept as a law, and statues were worshipped by the commandment of tyrants.€€ƒyAnd those whom men could not honour in presence, because they dwelt far off, they brought their resemblance from afar, and made an express image of the king, whom they had a mind to honour: that by this their diligence, they might honour as present, him that was absent.€€ƒxqAnd to the worshipping of these, the singular diligence also of the artificer helped to set forward the ignorant.€€ƒx{For he being willing to please him that employed him, laboured with all his art to make the resemblance in the best manner.€€ƒx‡And the multitude of men, carried away by the beauty of the work, took him now for a god, that little before was but honoured as a man.€€ƒx›And this was the occasion of deceiving human life: for men serving either their affection, or their kings, gave the incommunicable name to stones and wood.€€ƒx¡And it was not enough for them to err about the knowledge of God, but whereas they lived in a great war of ignorance, they call so many and so great evils peace.€€ƒxhFor either they sacrifice their own children, or use hidden sacrifices, or keep watches full of madness,€€ƒx~So that now they neither keep life, nor marriage undefiled, but one killeth another through envy, or grieveth him by adultery:€€ƒxšAnd all things are mingled together, blood, murder, theft, and dissimulation, corruption and unfaithfulness, tumults and perjury, disquieting of the good,€€ƒx„Forgetfulness of God, defiling of souls, changing of nature, disorder in marriage, and the irregularity of adultery and uncleanness.€€ƒxXFor the worship of abominable idols is the cause, and the beginning and end of all evil.€€ƒxyFor either they are mad when they are merry: or they prophesy lies, or they live unjustly, or easily forswear themselves.€€ƒxjFor whilst they trust in idols, which are without life, though they swear amiss, they look not to be hurt.€€ƒxˆFor it is not the power of them, by whom they swear, but the just vengeance of sinners always punisheth the transgression of the unjust.€€ƒ`€€ƒxTBut thou, our God, art gracious and true, patient, and ordering all things in mercy.€€ƒxmFor if we sin, we are thine, knowing thy greatness: and if we sin not, we know that we are counted with thee.€€ƒxhFor to know thee is perfect justice: and to know thy justice, and thy power, is the root of immortality.€€ƒxFor the invention of mischievous men hath not deceived us, nor the shadow of a picture, a fruitless labour, a graven figure with divers colours,€€ƒxhThe sight whereof enticeth the fool to lust after it, and he loveth the lifeless figure of a dead image.€€ƒx–The lovers of evil things deserve to have no better things to trust in, both they that make them, and they that love them, and they that worship them.€€ƒy
The potter also tempering soft earth, with labour fashioneth every vessel for our service, and of the same clay he maketh both vessels that are for clean uses, and likewise such as serve to the contrary: but what is the use of these vessels, the potter is the judge.€€ƒxêAnd of the same clay by a vain labour he maketh a god: he who a little before was made of earth himself, and a little after returneth to the same out of which he was taken, when his life, which was lent him, shall be called for again.€€ƒxŞBut his care is, not that he shall labour, nor that his life is short, but he striveth with the goldsmiths and silversmiths: and he endeavoureth to do like the workers in brass, and counteth it a glory to make vain things.€€ƒxQFor his heart is ashes, and his hope vain earth and his life more base than clay:€€ƒx…Forasmuch as he knew not his maker, and him that inspired into him the soul that worketh, and that breathed into him a living spirit.€€ƒx‹Yea, and they have counted our life a pastime and the business of life to be gain, and that we must be getting every way, even out of evil.€€•ƒxsBut all the enemies of thy people that hold them in subjection, are foolish, and unhappy, and proud beyond measure:€€ƒxãFor they have esteemed all the idols of the heathens for gods, which neither have the use of eyes to see, nor noses to draw breath, nor ears to hear, nor fingers of hands to handle, and as for their feet, they are slow to walk.€€ƒxsFor man made them: and he that borroweth his own breath, fashioned them. For no man can make a god like to himself.€€ƒx½For being mortal himself, he formeth a dead thing with his wicked hands. For he is better than they whom he worshippeth, because he indeed hath lived, though he were mortal, but they never.€€ƒxsMoreover, they worship also the vilest creatures: but things without sense, compared to these, are worse than they.€€ƒx}Yea, neither by sight can any man see good of these beasts. But they have fled from the praise of God, and from his blessing.€€ƒ`€€ƒx|For these things, and by the like things to these, they were worthily punished, and were destroyed by a multitude of beasts.€€ƒx¥Instead of which punishment, dealing well with thy people, thou gavest them their desire of delicious food, of a new taste, preparing for them quails for their meat:€€ƒxñTo the end, that they indeed desiring food, by means of those things that were shewn and sent among them, might loath even that which was necessary to satisfy their desire. But these, after suffering want for a short time, tasted a new meat.€€ƒx¥For it was requisite that inevitable destruction should come upon them that exercised tyranny: but to these it should only be shewn how their enemies were destroyed.€€ƒxkFor when the fierce rage of beasts came upon these, they were destroyed by the bitings of crooked serpents.€€ƒx·But thy wrath endured not for ever, but they were troubled for a short time for their correction, having a sign of salvation, to put them in remembrance of the commandment of thy law.€€ƒx_For he that turned to it, was not healed by that which he saw, but by thee, the Saviour of all.€€ƒxZAnd in this thou didst shew to our enemies, that thou art he who deliverest from all evil.€€ƒxFor the bitings of locusts, and of flies, killed them, and there was found no remedy for their life: because they were worthy to be destroyed by such things.€€ƒxfBut not even the teeth of venomous serpents overcame thy children: for thy mercy came and healed them.€€ƒxŸFor they were examined for the remembrance of thy words, and were quickly healed, lest falling into deep forgetfulness, they might not be able to use thy help.€€ƒxrFor it was neither herb, nor mollifying plaster, that healed them, but thy word, O Lord, which healeth all things.€€ƒx{For it is thou, O Lord, that hast power of life and death, and leadest down to the gates of death, and bringest back again:€€ƒx’A man indeed killeth through malice, and when the spirit is gone forth, it shall not return, neither shall he call back the soul that is received:€€–ƒx For the wicked that denied to know thee, were scourged by the strength of thy arm, being persecuted by strange waters, and hail, and rain, and consumed by fire.€€ƒx€And which was wonderful, in water, which extinguisheth all things, the fire had more force: for the world fighteth for the just.€€ƒxÆFor at one time the fire was mitigated, that the beasts which were sent against the wicked might not be burnt, but that they might see, and perceive that they were persecuted by the judgment of God.€€ƒxwAnd at another time the fire, above its own power, burnt in the midst of water, to destroy the fruits of a wicked land.€€ƒxÎInstead of which things, thou didst feed thy people with the food of angels, and gavest them bread from heaven, prepared without labour; having in it all that is delicious, and the sweetness of every taste.€€ƒx}For thy sustenance shewed thy sweetness to thy children, and serving every man's will, it was turned to what every man liked.€€ƒx³But snow and ice endured the force of fire, and melted not: that they might know that the fire, burning in the hail, and flashing in the rain, destroyed the fruits of the enemies.€€ƒxXBut this same again, that the just might be nourished, did even forget its own strength.€€ƒx¨For the creature serving thee, the Creator, is made fierce against the unjust for their punishment: and abateth its strength for the benefit of them that trust in thee.€€ƒx¢Therefore even then it was transformed into all things, and was obedient to thy grace, that nourisheth all, according to the will of them that desired it of thee:€€ƒx¥That thy children, O Lord, whom thou lovedst, might know that it is not the growing of fruits that nourisheth men, but thy word preserveth them that believe in thee.€€ƒxiFor that which could not be destroyed by fire, being warmed with a little sunbeam, presently melted away:€€ƒxzThat it might be known to all, that we ought to prevent the sun to bless thee, and adore thee at the dawning of the light.€€ƒxlFor the hope of the unthankful shall melt away as the winter's ice, and shall run off as unprofitable water.€€ƒ`€€ƒxrFor thy judgments, O Lord, are great, and thy words cannot be expressed: therefore undisciplined souls have erred.€€ƒxâFor while the wicked thought to be able to have dominion over the holy nation, they themselves being fettered with the bonds of darkness, and a long night, shut up in their houses, lay there exiled from the eternal providence.€€ƒx¸And while they thought to lie hid in their obscure sins, they were scattered under a dark veil of forgetfullness, being horribly afraid, and troubled with exceeding great astonishment.€€ƒx–For neither did the den that held them, keep them from fear: for noises coming down troubled them, and sad visions appearing to them, affrighted them.€€ƒxwAnd no power of fire could give them light, neither could the bright flames of the stars enlighten that horrible night.€€ƒx­But there appeared to them a sudden fire, very dreadful: and being struck with the fear of that face, which was not seen, they thought the things which they saw to be worse:€€ƒxkAnd the delusions of their magic art were put down, and their boasting of wisdom was reproachfully rebuked.€€‹ƒxÏFor though no terrible thing disturbed them: yet being scared with the passing by of beasts, and hissing of serpents, they died for fear and denying that they saw the air, which could by no means be avoided.€€ƒxˆFor whereas wickedness is fearful, it beareth witness of its condemnation: for a troubled conscience always forecasteth grievous things.€€ƒxHFor fear is nothing else but a yielding up of the succours from thought.€€ƒx‚And while there is less expectation from within, the greater doth it count the ignorance of that cause which bringeth the torment.€€ƒx‘But they that during that night, in which nothing could be done, and which came upon them from the lowest and deepest hell, slept the same sleep,€€ƒxšWere sometimes molested with the fear of monsters, sometimes fainted away, their soul failing them: for a sudden and unlooked for fear was come upon them.€€ƒxVMoreover, if any of them had fallen down, he was kept shut up in prison without irons.€€ƒxœFor if any one were a husbandman, or a shepherd, or a labourer in the field, and was suddenly overtaken, he endured a necessity from which he could not fly.€€ƒxÕFor they were all bound together with one chain of darkness. Whether it were a whistling wind, or the melodious voice of birds, among the spreading branches of trees, or a fall of water running down with violence,€€ƒxîOr the mighty noise of stones tumbling down, or the running that could not be seen of beasts playing together, or the roaring voice of wild beasts, or a rebounding echo from the highest mountains: these things made them to swoon for fear.€€ƒxaFor the whole world was enlightened, with a clear light, and none were hindered in their labours.€€Œƒ`€€ƒx±But thy saints had a very great light, and they heard their voice indeed, but did not see their shape. And because they also did not suffer the same things, they glorified thee:€€ƒxŠAnd they that before had been wronged, gave thanks, because they were not hurt now: and asked this gift, that there might be a difference.€€ƒx™Therefore they received a burning pillar of fire for a guide of the way which they knew not, and thou gavest them a harmless sun of a good entertainment.€€ƒx±The others indeed were worthy to be deprived of light, and imprisoned in darkness, who kept thy children shut up, by whom the pure light of the law was to be given to the world.€€ƒxÒAnd whereas they thought to kill the babes of the just: one child being cast forth, and saved to reprove them, thou tookest away a multitude of their children, and destroyedst them altogether in a mighty water.€€ƒx‡For that night was known before by our fathers, that assuredly knowing what oaths they had trusted to, they might be of better courage.€€ƒxPSo thy people received the salvation of the just, and destruction of the unjust.€€ƒxUFor as thou didst punish the adversaries so thou didst also encourage and glorify us.€€ƒxÑFor the just children of good men were offering sacrifice secretly, and they unanimously ordered a law of justice: that the just should receive both good and evil alike, singing now the praises of the fathers.€€ƒx‘But on the other side there sounded an ill according cry of the enemies, and a lamentable mourning was heard for the children that were bewailed.€€ƒxqAnd the servant suffered the same punishment as the master, and a common man suffered in like manner as the king.€€“ƒx¯For whereas they would not believe any thing before by reason of the enchantments, then first upon the destruction of the firstborn, they acknowledged the people to be of God.€€ƒxYFor while all things were in quiet silence, and the night was in the midst of her course,€€ƒxThy Almighty word leaped down from heaven from thy royal throne, as a fierce conqueror into the midst of the land of destruction,€€ƒx˜With a sharp sword carrying thy unfeigned commandment, and he stood and filled all things with death, and standing on the earth, reached even to heaven.€€ƒxZThen suddenly visions of evil dreams troubled them, and fears unlooked for came upon them.€€ƒxMAnd one thrown here, another there, half dead, shewed the cause of his death.€€ƒx€For the visions that troubled them foreshewed these things, lest they should perish, and not know why they suffered these evils.€€ƒx¦But the just also were afterwards touched by an assault of death, and there was a disturbance of the multitude in the wilderness: but thy wrath did not long continue;€€ƒxâFor a blameless man made haste to pry for the people, bringing forth the shield of his ministry, prayer, and by incense making supplication, withstood the wrath, and put an end to the calamity, shewing that he was thy servant.€€ƒx¹And he overcame the disturbance, not by strength of body nor with force of arms, but with a word he subdued him that punished them, alleging the oath and covenant made with the fathers.€€ƒxFor when they were now fallen down dead by heaps one upon another, he stood between and stayed the assault, and cut off the way to the living.€€ƒx¾For in the priestly robe which he wore, was the whole world: and in the four rows of the stones, the glory of the fathers was graven, and thy majesty was written upon the diadem of his head.€€ƒxfAnd to these the destroyer gave place, and was afraid of them: for the proof only of wrath was enough.€€ƒ`€€ƒx{But as to the wicked, even to the end there came upon them wrath without mercy. For he knew before also what they would do:€€ƒxzFor when they had given them leave to depart and had sent them away with great care, they repented and pursued after them.€€ƒx°For whilst they were yet mourning, and lamenting at the graves of the dead, they took up another foolish device: and pursued them as fugitives whom they had pressed to be gone:€€ƒxÏFor a necessity, of which they were worthy, brought them to this end: and they lost the remembrance of those things which had happened, that their punishment might fill up what was wanting to their torments:€€ƒxTAnd that thy people might wonderfully pass through, but they might find a new death.€€Œƒx¬For a cloud overshadowed their camps and where water was before, dry land appeared, and in the Red Sea a way without hindrance, and out of the great deep a springing field:€€ƒxgThrough which all the nation passed which was protected with thy hand, seeing thy miracles and wonders.€€ƒxuFor they fed on their food like horses, and they skipped like lambs, praising thee, O Lord, who hadst delivered them.€€ƒxÖFor they were yet mindful of those things which had been done in the time of their sojourning, how the ground brought forth flies instead of cattle, and how the river cast up a multitude of frogs instead of fishes.€€ƒxrAnd at length they saw a new generation of birds, when being led by their appetite, they asked for delicate meats.€€ƒxİFor to satisfy their desire, the quail came up to them from the sea: and punishments came upon the sinners, not without foregoing signs by the force of thunders: for they suffered justly according to their own wickedness.€€ƒx¾For they exercised a more detestable inhospitality than any: others indeed received not strangers unknown to them, but these brought their guests into bondage that had deserved well of them.€€ƒxwAnd not only so, but in another respect also they were worse: for the others against their will received the strangers.€€ƒxgBut these grievously afflicted them whom they had received with joy, and who lived under the same laws.€€ƒx¶But they were struck with blindness: as those others were at the doors of the just man, when they were covered with sudden darkness, and every one sought the passage of his own door.€€ƒx¶For while the elements are changed in themselves, as in an instrument the sound of the quality is changed, yet all keep their sound: which may clearly be perceived by the very sight.€€ƒxƒFor the things of the land were turned into things of the water: and the things that before swam in the water passed upon the land.€€˜ƒyBOn the other side, the flames wasted not the flesh of corruptible animals walking therein, neither did they melt that good food, which was apt to melt as ice. For in all things thou didst magnify thy people, O Lord, and didst honour them, and didst not despise them, but didst assist them at all times, and in every place.€€ƒyThe Wisdom of Ben Sirach  The knowledge of many and great things hath been shewn us by the law, and the prophets, and others that have followed them: for which things Israel is to be commended for doctrine and wisdom, because not only they that speak must needs be skilful, but strangers also, both speaking and writing, may by their means become most learned. My grandfather Jesus, after he had much given himself to a diligent reading of the law, and the prophets, and other books, that were delivered to us from our fathers, had a mind also to write something himself, pertaining to doctrine and wisdom; that such as are desirous to learn, and are made knowing in these things, may be more and more attentive in mind, and be strengthened to live according to the law. I entreat you therefore to come with benevolence, and to read with attention, and to pardon us for those things wherein we may seem, while we follow the image of wisdom, to come short in the composition of words; for the Hebrew words have not the same force in them when translated into another tongue. And not only these, but the law also itself, and the prophets, and the rest of the books, have no small difference, when they are spoken in their own language. For in the eight and thirtieth year coming into Egypt, when Ptolemy Evergetes was king, and continuing there a long time, I found there books left, of no small nor contemptible learning. Therefore I thought it good, and necessary for me to bestow some diligence and labour to interpret this book; and with much watching and study in some space of time, I brought the book to an end, and set it forth for the service of them that are willing to apply their mind, and to learn how they ought to conduct themselves, who purpose to lead their life according to the law of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxWAll wisdom is from the Lord God, and hath been always with him, and is before all time.€€ƒxºWho hath numbered the sand of the sea, and the drops of rain, and the days of the world? Who hath measured the height of heaven, and the breadth of the earth, and the depth of the abyss?€€ƒxEWho hath searched out the wisdom of God that goeth before all things?€€ƒx_Wisdom hath been created before all things, and the understanding of prudence from everlasting.€€ƒx]The word of God on high is the fountain of wisdom, and her ways are everlasting commandments.€€ƒxTTo whom hath the root of wisdom been revealed, and who hath known her wise counsels?€€ƒx}To whom hath the discipline of wisdom been revealed and made manifest? and who hath understood the multiplicity of her steps?€€ƒxThere is one most high Creator Almighty, and a powerful king, and greatly to be feared, who sitteth upon his throne, and is the God of dominion.€€ƒxRHe created her in the Holy Ghost, and saw her, and numbered her, and measured her.€€ƒx}And he poured her out upon all his works, and upon all flesh according to his gift, and hath given her to them that love him.€€ƒxLThe fear of the Lord is honour, and glory, and gladness, and a crown of joy.€€ƒxcThe fear of the Lord shall delight the heart, and shall give joy, and gladness, and length of days.€€ƒxtWith him that feareth the Lord, it shall go well in the latter end, and in the day of his death he shall be blessed.€€ƒx%The love of God is honourable wisdom.€€ƒxgAnd they to whom she shall shew herself love her by the sight, and by the knowledge of her great works.€€ƒx¦The fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom, and was created with the faithful in the womb, it walketh with chosen women, and is known with the just and faithful.€€ƒx7The fear of the Lord is the religiousness of knowledge.€€ƒxOReligiousness shall keep and justify the heart, it shall give joy and gladness.€€ƒx`It shall go well with him that feareth the Lord, and in the days of his end he shall be blessed.€€ƒxMTo fear God is the fulness of wisdom, and fulness is from the fruits thereof.€€ƒxWShe shall fill all her house with her increase, and the storehouses with her treasures.€€ƒxWThe fear of the Lord is a crown of wisdom, filling up peace and the fruit of salvation:€€˜ƒxoWisdom shall distribute knowledge, and understanding of prudence: and exalteth the glory of them that hold her.€€ƒxPThe root of wisdom is to fear the Lord: and the branches thereof are long-lived.€€ƒxuIn the treasures of wisdom is understanding, and religiousness of knowledge: but to sinners wisdom is an abomination.€€ƒx%The fear of the Lord driveth out sin:€€ƒx`For he that is without fear, cannot be justified: for the wrath of his high spirits is his ruin.€€ƒxQA patient man shall bear for a time, and afterwards joy shall be restored to him.€€ƒxcA good understanding will hide his words for a time, and the lips of many shall declare his wisdom.€€ƒx>In the treasures of wisdom is the signification of discipline:€€ƒx5But the worship of God is an abomination to a sinner.€€ƒxHSon, if thou desire wisdom, keep justice, and God will give her to thee.€€ƒxVFor the fear of the Lord is wisdom and discipline: and that which is agreeable to him,€€ƒx:Is faith, and meekness: and he will fill up his treasures.€€ƒxTBe not incredulous to the fear of the Lord: and come not to him with a double heart.€€ƒxYBe not a hypocrite in the sight of men, and let not thy lips be a stumblingblock to thee.€€ƒxCWatch over them, lest thou fall, and bring dishonour upon thy soul,€€ƒxRAnd God discover thy secrets, and cast thee down in the midst of the congregation.€€ƒxTBecause thou camest to the Lord wickedly, and thy heart is full of guile and deceit.€€ƒ`€€ƒxoSon, when thou comest to the service of God, stand in justice and in fear, and prepare thy soul for temptation.€€ƒx€Humble thy heart, and endure: incline thy ear, and receive the words of understanding: and make not haste in the time of clouds.€€ƒxmWait on God with patience: join thyself to God, and endure, that thy life may be increased in the latter end.€€ƒxiTake all that shall be brought upon thee: and in thy sorrow endure, and in thy humiliation keep patience.€€ƒx\For gold and silver are tried in the fire, but acceptable men in the furnace of humiliation.€€ƒxqBelieve God, and he will recover thee: and direct thy way, and trust in him. Keep his fear, and grow old therein.€€cJer¤dnamehJeremiahiosis_namecJerlabbreviationcJerhchapters˜4“ƒxPYe that fear the Lord hope in him, and mercy shall come to you for your delight.€€ƒxFYe that fear the Lord, love him, and your hearts shall be enlightened.€€ƒxtMy children behold the generations of men: and know ye that no one hath hoped in the Lord, and hath been confounded.€€ƒxtFor who hath continued in his commandment, and hath been forsaken? or who hath called upon him, and he despised him?€€ƒxŒFor God is compassionate and merciful, and will forgive sins in the day of tribulation: and he is a protector to all that seek him in truth.€€ƒxWoe to them that are of a double heart and to wicked lips, and to the hands that do evil, and to the sinner that goeth on the earth two ways.€€ƒxiWoe to them that are fainthearted, who believe not God: and therefore they shall not be protected by him.€€ƒxrWoe to them that have lost patience, and that have forsaken the right ways, and have gone aside into crooked ways.€€ƒx<And what will they do, when the Lord shall begin to examine?€€ƒxhThey that fear the Lord, will not be incredulous to his word: and they that love him, will keep his way.€€ƒxˆThey that fear the Lord, will seek after the things that are well pleasing to him: and they that love him, shall be filled with his law.€€ƒx_They that fear the Lord, will prepare their hearts, and in his sight will sanctify their souls,€€ƒxaThey that fear the Lord, keep his commandments, and will have patience even until his visitation,€€ƒxfSaying: If we do not penance, we shall fall into the hands of the Lord, and not into the hands of men.€€ƒx>For according to his greatness, so also is his mercy with him.€€ƒ`€€ƒxXThe sons of wisdom are the church of the just: and their generation, obedience and love.€€ƒxLChildren, hear the judgment of your father, and so do that you may be saved.€€ƒx†For God hath made the father honourable to the children: and seeking the judgment of the mothers, hath confirmed it upon the children.€€˜%ƒxEAnd he that honoureth his mother is as one that layeth up a treasure.€€ƒxpHe that honoureth his father shall have joy in his own children, and in the day of his prayer he shall be heard.€€ƒxwHe that honoureth his father shall enjoy a long life: and he that obeyeth the father, shall be a comfort to his mother.€€ƒxtHe that feareth the Lord, honoureth his parents, and will serve them as his masters that brought him into the world.€€ƒx6Honour thy father, in work and word, and all patience,€€ƒx[That a blessing may come upon thee from him, and his blessing may remain in the latter end.€€ƒxpThe father's blessing establisheth the houses of the children: but the mother's curse rooteth up the foundation.€€ƒxLGlory not in the dishonour of thy father: for his shame is no glory to thee.€€ƒxpFor the glory of a man is from the honour of his father, and a father without honour is the disgrace of the son.€€ƒxGSon, support the old age of thy father, and grieve him not in his life;€€ƒx¡And if his understanding fail, have patience with him, and despise him not when thou art in thy strength: for the relieving of the father shall not be forgotten.€€ƒx;For good shall be repaid to thee for the sin of thy mother.€€ƒxŸAnd in justice thou shalt be built up, and in the day of affliction thou shalt be remembered: and thy sins shall melt away as the ice in the fair warm weather.€€ƒxgOf what an evil fame is he that forsaketh his father: and he is cursed of God that angereth his mother.€€ƒxSMy son, do thy works in meekness, and thou shalt be beloved above the glory of men.€€ƒxbThe greater thou art, the more humble thyself in all things, and thou shalt find grace before God:€€ƒxFFor great is the power of God alone, and he is honoured by the humble.€€ƒxÉSeek not the things that are too high for thee, and search not into things above thy ability: but the things that God hath commanded thee, think on them always, and in many of his works be not curious.€€ƒxPFor it is not necessary for thee to see with thy eyes those things that are hid.€€ƒxcIn unnecessary matters be not over curious, and in many of his works thou shalt not be inquisitive.€€ƒxAFor many things are shewn to thee above the understanding of men.€€ƒxVAnd the suspicion of them hath deceived many, and hath detained their minds in vanity.€€ƒxWA hard heart shall fear evil at the last: and he that loveth danger shall perish in it.€€ƒxkA heart that goeth two ways shall not have success, and the perverse of heart shall be scandalized therein.€€ƒxOA wicked heart shall be laden with sorrows, and the sinner will add sin to sin.€€ƒx†The congregation of the proud shall not be healed: for the plant of wickedness shall take root in them, and it shall not be perceived.€€ƒx_The heart of the wise is understood in wisdom, and a good ear will hear wisdom with all desire.€€ƒxsA wise heart, and which hath understanding, will abstain from sins, and in the works of justice shall have success.€€ƒx8Water quencheth a flaming fire, and alms resisteth sins:€€ƒx„And God provideth for him that sheweth favour: he remembereth him afterwards, and in the time of his fall he shall find a sure stay.€€ƒ`€€ƒxLSon, defraud not the poor of alms, and turn not away thy eyes from the poor.€€ƒxBDespise not the hungry soul: and provoke not the poor in his want.€€ƒxUAfflict not the heart of the needy, and defer not to gibe to him that is in distress.€€ƒxTReject not the petition of the afflicted: and turn not away thy face from the needy.€€ƒx}Turn not away thy eyes from the poor for fear of anger: and leave not to them that ask of thee to curse thee behind thy back.€€ƒxzFor the prayer of him that curseth thee in the bitterness of his soul, shall be heard, for he that made him will hear him.€€˜ƒxoBow down thy ear cheerfully to the poor, and pay what thou owest, and answer him peaceable words with mildness.€€ƒxcDeliver him that suffereth wrong out of the hand of the proud: and be not fainthearted in thy soul.€€ƒxWIn judging be merciful to the fatherless as a father, and as a husband to their mother.€€ƒxjAnd thou shalt be as the obedient son of the most High, and he will have mercy on thee more than a mother.€€ƒx€Wisdom inspireth life into her children, and protecteth them that seek after her, and will go before them in the way of justice.€€ƒx^And he that loveth her, loveth life: and they that watch for her, shall embrace her sweetness.€€ƒxfThey that hold her fast, shall inherit life: and whithersoever she entereth, God will give a blessing.€€ƒxZThey that serve her, shall be servants to the holy one: and God loveth them that love her.€€ƒxbHe that hearkeneth to her, shall judge nations: and he that looketh upon her, shall remain secure.€€ƒxSIf he trust to her, he shall inherit her, and his generation shall be in assurance.€€ƒxJFor she walketh with him in temptation, and at the first she chooseth him.€€ƒx£She will bring upon him fear and dread and trial: and she will scourge him with the affliction of her discipline, till she try him by her laws, and trust his soul.€€ƒxOThen she will strengthen him, and make a straight way to him, and give him joy,€€ƒxqAnd will disclose her secrets to him, and will heap upon him treasures of knowledge and understanding of justice.€€ƒxWBut if he go astray, she will forsake him, and deliver him into the hands of his enemy.€€ƒx)Son, observe the time, and fly from evil.€€ƒx-For thy soul be not ashamed to say the truth.€€ƒx[For there is a shame that bringeth sin, and there is a shame that bringeth glory and grace.€€ƒxDAccept no person against thy own person, nor against thy soul a lie.€€ƒx(Reverence not thy neighbour in his fall:€€ƒxUAnd refrain not to speak in the time of salvation. Hide not thy wisdom in her beauty.€€ƒx™For by the tongue wisdom is discerned: and understanding, and knowledge, and learning by the word of the wise, and steadfastness in the works of justice.€€ƒxNIn nowise speak against the truth, but be ashamed of the lie of thy ignorance.€€ƒxPBe not ashamed to confess thy sins, but submit not thyself to every man for sin.€€ƒx]Resist not against the face of the mighty, and do not strive against the stream of the river.€€˜ƒx>Be not hasty in thy tongue: and slack and remiss in thy works.€€ƒxiBe not as a lion in thy house, terrifying them of thy household, and oppressing them that are under thee.€€ƒxOLet not thy hand be stretched out to receive, and shut when thou shouldst give.€€ƒ`€€ƒx–Set not thy heart upon unjust possessions, and say not: I have enough to live on: for it shall be of no service in the time of vengeance and darkness.€€ƒx4Follow not in thy strength the desires of thy heart:€€ƒxjAnd say not: How mighty am I? and who shall bring me under for my deeds? for God will surely take revenge.€€ƒx`Say not: I have sinned, and what harm hath befallen me? for the most High is a patient rewarder.€€ƒxABe not without fear about sin forgiven, and add not sin upon sin:€€ƒx\And say not: The mercy of the Lord is great, he will have mercy on the multitude of my sins.€€ƒxNFor mercy and wrath quickly come from him, and his wrath looketh upon sinners.€€ƒxHDelay not to be converted to the Lord, and defer it not from day to day.€€ƒxXFor his wrath shall come on a sudden, and in the time of vengeance he will destroy thee.€€ƒxlBe not anxious for goods unjustly gotten: for they shall not profit thee in the day of calamity and revenge.€€ƒxhWinnow not with every wind, and go not into every way: for so is every sinner proved by a double tongue.€€ƒxBe steadfast in the way of the Lord, and in the truth of thy judgment, and in knowledge, and let the word of peace and justice keep with thee.€€ƒx[Be meek to hear the word, that thou mayst understand: and return a true answer with wisdom.€€ƒxIf thou have understanding, answer thy neighbour: but if not, let thy hand be upon thy mouth, lest thou be surprised in an unskilful word, and be confounded.€€ƒxTHonour and glory is in the word of the wise, but the tongue of the fool is his ruin.€€ƒxJBe not called a whisperer, and be not taken in thy tongue, and confounded.€€ƒxšFor confusion and repentance is upon a thief, and an evil mark of disgrace upon the double tongued, but to the whisperer hatred, and enmity, and reproach.€€ƒx&Justify alike the small and the great.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¥Instead of a friend become not an enemy to thy neighbour: for an evil man shall inherit reproach and shame, so shall every sinner that is envious and double tongued.€€ƒxaExtol not thyself in the thoughts of thy soul like a bull: lest thy strength be quashed by folly,€€ƒxbAnd it eat up thy leaves, and destroy thy fruit, and thou be left as a dry tree in the wilderness.€€ƒx‹For a wicked soul shall destroy him that hath it, and maketh him to be a joy to his enemies, and shall lead him into the lot of the wicked.€€ƒxgA sweet word multiplieth friends, and appeaseth enemies, and a gracious tongue in a good man aboundeth.€€ƒxCBe in peace with many, but let one of a thousand be thy counsellor.€€ƒx[If thou wouldst get a friend, try him before thou takest him, and do not credit him easily.€€ƒx\For there is a friend for his own occasion, and he will not abide in the day of thy trouble.€€˜ƒx]And there is a friend a companion at the table, and he will not abide in the day of distress.€€ƒxzA friend if he continue steadfast, shall be to thee as thyself, and shall act with confidence among them of thy household.€€ƒxpIf he humble himself before thee, and hide himself from thy face, thou shalt have unanimous friendship for good.€€ƒx@Separate thyself from thy enemies, and take heed of thy friends.€€ƒxYA faithful friend is a strong defence: and he that hath found him, hath found a treasure.€€ƒxƒNothing can be compared to a faithful friend, and no weight of gold and silver is able to countervail the goodness of his fidelity.€€ƒxgA faithful friend is the medicine of life and immortality: and they that fear the Lord, shall find him.€€ƒxgHe that feareth God, shall likewise have good friendship: because according to him shall his friend be.€€ƒxaMy son, from thy youth up receive instruction, and even to thy grey hairs thou shalt find wisdom.€€ƒxLCome to her as one that plougheth, and soweth, and wait for her good fruits:€€ƒxYFor in working about her thou shalt labour a little, and shalt quickly eat of her fruits.€€ƒxZHow very unpleasant is wisdom to the unlearned, and the unwise will not continue with her.€€ƒxdShe shall be to them as a mighty stone of trial, and they will cast her from them before it be long.€€ƒx¤For the wisdom of doctrine is according to her name, and she is not manifest unto many, but with them to whom she is known, she continueth even to the sight of God.€€ƒxEGive ear, my son, and take wise counsel, and cast not away my advice.€€ƒx<Put thy feet into her fetters, and thy neck into her chains:€€ƒxGBow down thy shoulder, and bear her, and be not grieved with her bands.€€ƒxDCome to her with all thy mind, and keep her ways with all thy power.€€ƒxcSearch for her, and she shall be made known to thee, and when thou hast gotten her, let her not go:€€ƒxVFor in the latter end thou shalt find rest in her, and she shall be turned to thy joy.€€ƒxjThen shall her fetters be a strong defence for thee, and a firm foundation, and her chain a robe of glory:€€ƒxHFor in her is the beauty of life, and her bands are a healthful binding.€€ƒx]Thou shalt put her on as a robe of glory, and thou shalt set her upon thee as a crown of joy.€€ƒxiMy son, if thou wilt attend to me, thou shalt learn: and if thou wilt apply thy mind, thou shalt be wise.€€ƒxkIf thou wilt incline thy ear, thou shalt receive instruction: and if thou love to hear, thou shalt be wise.€€ƒx¾Stand in the multitude of ancients that are wise, and join thyself from thy heart to their wisdom, that thou mayst hear every discourse of God, and the sayings of praise may not escape thee.€€ƒxuAnd if thou see a man of understanding, go to him early in the morning, and let thy foot wear the steps of his doors.€€ƒx«Let thy thoughts be upon the precepts of God, and meditate continually on his commandments: and he will give thee a heart, and the desire of wisdom shall be given to thee.€€ƒ`€€ƒx1Do no evils, and no evils shall lay hold of thee.€€ƒx9Depart from the unjust, and evils shall depart from thee.€€˜ƒxGSeek not of the Lord a preeminence, nor of the king the seat of honour.€€ƒxhJustify not thyself before God, for he knoweth the heart: and desire not to appear wise before the king.€€ƒx­Seek not to be made a judge, unless thou have strength enough to extirpate iniquities: lest thou fear the person of the powerful, and lay a stumblingblock for thy integrity.€€ƒxTOffend not against the multitude of a city, neither cast thyself in upon the people,€€ƒxBNor bind sin to sin: for even in one thou shalt not be unpunished.€€ƒx Be not fainthearted in thy mind:€€ƒx&Neglect not to pray, and to give alms.€€ƒx€Say not: God will have respect to the multitude of my gifts, and when I offer to the most high God, he will accept my offerings.€€ƒxtLaugh no man to scorn in the bitterness of his soul: for there is one that humbleth and exalteth, God who seeth all.€€ƒxMDevise not a lie against thy brother: neither do the like against thy friend.€€ƒxMBe not willing to make any manner of lie: for the custom thereof is not good.€€ƒxWBe not full of words in a multitude of ancients, and repeat not the word in thy prayer.€€ƒxBHate not laborious works, nor husbandry ordained by the most High.€€ƒx9Number not thyself among the multitude of the disorderly.€€ƒx+Remember wrath, for it will not tarry long.€€ƒx]Humble thy spirit very much: for the vengeance on the flesh of the ungodly is fire and worms.€€ƒxhDo not transgress against thy friend deferring money, nor despise thy dear brother for the sake of gold.€€ƒx€Depart not from a wise and good wife, whom thou hast gotten in the fear of the Lord: for the grace of her modesty is above gold.€€ƒxZHurt not the servant that worketh faithfully, nor the hired man that giveth thee his life.€€ƒxdLet a wise servant be dear to thee as thy own soul, defraud him not of liberty, nor leave him needy.€€ƒxZHast thou cattle? have an eye to them: and if they be for thy profit, keep them with thee.€€ƒxPHast thou children? instruct them, and bow down their neck from their childhood.€€ƒx^Hast thou daughters? have a care of their body, and shew not thy countenance gay towards them.€€ƒxTMarry thy daughter well, and thou shalt do a great work, and give her to a wise man.€€ƒxIf thou hast a wife according to thy soul, cast her not off: and to her that is hateful, trust not thyself. With thy whole heart,€€ƒx>Honour thy father, and forget not the groanings of thy mother:€€ƒxnRemember that thou hadst not been born but through them: and make a return to them as they have done for thee.€€ƒx;With all thy soul fear the Lord, and reverence his priests.€€ƒxMWith all thy strength love him that made thee: and forsake not his ministers.€€ƒx^Honour God with all thy soul and give honour to the priests, and purify thyself with thy arms.€€˜"ƒxyOffer to the Lord the gift of thy shoulders, and the sacrifice of sanctification, and the firstfruits of the holy things:€€ƒx[And stretch out thy hand to the poor, that thy expiation and thy blessing may be perfected.€€ƒxWA gift hath grace in the sight of all the living, and restrain not grace from the dead.€€ƒxKBe not wanting in comforting them that weep, and walk with them that mourn.€€ƒxSBe not slow to visit the sick: for by these things thou shalt be confirmed in love.€€ƒxAIn all thy works remember thy last end, and thou shalt never sin.€€ƒ`€€ƒx>Strive not with a powerful man, lest thou fall into his hands.€€ƒxBContend not with a rich man, lest he bring an action against thee.€€ƒxiFor gold and silver hath destroyed many, and hath reached even to the heart of kings, and perverted them.€€ƒxNStrive not with a man that is full of tongue, and heap not wood upon his fire.€€ƒxFCommunicate not with an ignorant man, lest he speak ill of thy family.€€ƒxuDespise not a man that turneth away from sin, nor reproach him therewith: remember that we are all worthy of reproof.€€ƒx?Despise not a man in his old age; for we also shall become old.€€ƒx|Rejoice not at the death of thy enemy; knowing that we all die, and are not willing that others should rejoice at our death.€€ƒxfDespise not the discourse of them that are ancient and wise, but acquaint thyself with their proverbs.€€ƒxlFor of them thou shalt learn wisdom, and instruction of understanding, and to serve great men without blame.€€ƒxZLet not the discourse of the ancients escape thee, for they have learned of their fathers:€€ƒxRFor of them thou shalt learn understanding, and to give an answer in time of need.€€ƒxnKindle not the coals of sinners by rebuking them, lest thou be burnt with the flame of the fire of their sins.€€ƒxdStand not against the face of an injurious person, lest he sit as a spy to entrap thee in thy words.€€ƒxWLend not to a man that is mightier than thyself: and if thou lendest, count it as lost.€€ƒxVBe not surety above thy power: and if thou be surety, think as if thou wert to pay it.€€ƒxJJudge not against a judge: for he judgeth according to that which is just.€€ƒxGo not on the way with a bold man, lest he burden thee with his evils: for he goeth according to his own will, and thou shalt perish together with his folly.€€ƒx§Quarrel not with a passionate man, and go not into the desert with a bold man: for blood is as nothing in his sight, and where there is no help he will overthrow thee.€€ƒxKAdvise not with fools, for they cannot love but such things as please them.€€ƒxYBefore a stranger do no matter of counsel: for thou knowest not what he will bring forth.€€ƒxgOpen not thy heart to every man: lest he repay thee with an evil turn, and speak reproachfully to thee.€€ƒ`€€ƒxeBe not jealous over the wife of thy bosom, lest she shew in thy regard the malice of a wicked lesson.€€ƒxdGive not the power of thy soul to a woman, lest she enter upon thy strength, and thou be confounded.€€ƒxPLook not upon a woman that hath a mind for many: lest thou fall into her snares.€€ƒxvUse not much the company of her that is a dancer, and hearken not to her, lest thou perish by the force of her charms.€€–ƒxYGive not thy soul to harlots in any point: lest thou destroy thyself and thy inheritance.€€ƒxaLook not round about thee in the ways of the city, nor wander up and down in the streets thereof.€€ƒxUTurn away thy face from a woman dressed up, and gaze not about upon another's beauty.€€ƒxXFor many have perished by the beauty of a woman, and hereby lust is enkindled as a fire.€€ƒxGEvery woman that is a harlot, shall be trodden upon as dung in the way.€€ƒxoMany by admiring the beauty of another man's wife, have become reprobate, for her conversation burneth as fire.€€ƒxISit not at all with another man's wife, nor repose upon the bed with her:€€ƒxrAnd strive not with her over wine, lest thy heart decline towards her and by thy blood thou fall into destruction.€€ƒx?Forsake not an old friend, for the new will not be like to him.€€ƒxVA new friend is as new wine: it shall grow old, and thou shalt drink it with pleasure.€€ƒxWEnvy not the glory and riches of a sinner: for thou knowest not what his ruin shall be.€€ƒxhBe not pleased with the wrong done by the unjust, knowing that even to hell the wicked shall not please.€€ƒx`Keep thee far from the man that hath power to kill, so thou shalt not suspect the fear of death.€€ƒxEAnd if thou come to him, commit no fault, lest he take away thy life.€€ƒxˆKnow it to be a communication with death: for thou art going in the midst of snares, and walking upon the arms of them that are grieved.€€ƒxTAccording to thy power beware of thy neighbour, and treat with the wise and prudent.€€ƒxDLet just men be thy guests, and let thy glory be in the fear of God.€€ƒxdAnd let the thought of God be in thy mind, and all thy discourse on the commandments of the Highest.€€ƒxWorks shall be praised for the hand of the artificers, and the prince of the people for the wisdom of his speech, but the word of the ancients for the sense.€€ƒx_A man full of tongue is terrible in his city, and he that is rash in his word shall be hateful.€€ƒ`€€ƒxYA wise judge shall judge his people, and the government of a prudent man shall be steady.€€˜ƒxqAn unwise king shall be the ruin of his people: and cities shall be inhabited through the prudence of the rulers.€€ƒxjThe power of the earth is in the hand of God, and in his time he will raise up a profitable ruler over it.€€ƒxgThe prosperity of man is in the hand of God, and upon the person of the scribe he shall lay his honour.€€ƒx[Remember not any injury done thee by thy neighbour, and do thou nothing by deeds of injury.€€ƒxNPride is hateful before God and men: and all iniquity of nations is execrable.€€ƒxxA kingdom is translated from one people to another, because of injustices, and wrongs, and injuries, and divers deceits.€€ƒxPBut nothing is more wicked than the covetous man. Why is earth, and ashes proud?€€ƒxœThere is not a more wicked thing than to love money: for such a one setteth even his own soul to sale: because while he liveth he hath cast away his bowels.€€ƒxLAll power is of short life. A long sickness is troublesome to the physician.€€ƒxaThe physician cutteth off a short sickness: so also a king is to day, and to morrow he shall die.€€ƒxKFor when a man shall die, he shall inherit serpents, and beasts, and worms.€€ƒx;The beginning of the pride of man, is to fall off from God:€€ƒxµBecause his heart is departed from him that made him: for pride is the beginning of all sin: he that holdeth it, shall be filled with maledictions, and it shall ruin him in the end.€€ƒx`Therefore hath the Lord disgraced the assemblies of the wicked, and hath utterly destroyed them.€€ƒxZGod hath overturned the thrones of proud princes, and hath set up the meek in their stead.€€ƒxaGod hath made the roots of proud nations to wither, and hath planted the humble of these nations.€€ƒxcThe Lord hath overthrown the lands of the Gentiles, and hath destroyed them even to the foundation.€€ƒx|He hath made some of them to wither away, and hath destroyed them, and hath made the memory of them to cease from the earth.€€ƒxjGod hath abolished the memory of the proud, and hath preserved the memory of them that are humble in mind.€€ƒx<Pride was not made for men: nor wrath for the race of women.€€ƒxŒThat seed of men shall be honoured, which feareth God: but that seed shall be dishonoured, which transgresseth the commandments of the Lord.€€ƒxeIn the midst of brethren their chief is honourable: so shall they that fear the Lord, be in his eyes.€€ƒxQThe fear of God is the glory of the rich, and of the honourable, and of the poor.€€ƒxRDespise not a just man that is poor, and do not magnify a sinful man that is rich.€€ƒxnThe great man, and the judge, and the mighty is in honour: and there is none greater than he that feareth God.€€ƒx¹They that are free shall serve a servant that is wise: and a man that is prudent and well instructed will not murmur when he is reproved; and he that is ignorant, shall not be honoured.€€˜ƒxjBetter is he that laboureth, and aboundeth in all things, than he that boasteth himself and wanteth bread.€€ƒxNMy son, keep thy soul in meekness, and give it honour according to its desert.€€ƒxoWho will justify him that sinneth against his own soul? and who will honour him that dishonoureth his own soul?€€ƒxiThe poor man is glorified by his discipline and fear, and there is a man that is honoured for his wealth.€€ƒxwBut he that is glorified in poverty, how much more in wealth? and he that is glorified in wealth, let him fear poverty.€€ƒ`€€ƒx`The wisdom of the humble shall exalt his head, and shall make him sit in the midst of great men.€€ƒxDPraise not a man for his beauty, neither despise a man for his look.€€ƒxOThe bee is small among flying things but her fruit hath the chiefest sweetness.€€ƒx³Glory not in apparel at any time, and be not exalted in the day of thy honour: for the works of the Highest only are wonderful, and his works are glorious, and secret, and hidden.€€ƒx\Many tyrants have sat on the throne, and he whom no man would think on, hath worn the crown.€€ƒxmMany mighty men have been greatly brought down, and the glorious have been delivered into the hand of others.€€ƒxOBefore thou inquire, blame no man: and when thou hast inquired, reprove justly.€€ƒx^Before thou hear, answer not a word: and interrupt not others in the midst of their discourse.€€ƒxYStrive not in a matter which doth not concern thee, and sit not in judgment with sinners.€€ƒx»My son, meddle not with many matters: and if thou be rich, thou shalt not be free from sin: for if thou pursue after thou shalt not overtake; and if thou run before thou shalt not escape.€€ƒxlThere is an ungodly man that laboureth, and maketh haste, and is in sorrow, and is so much the more in want.€€ƒx`Again, there is an inactive man that wanteth help, is very weak in ability, and full of poverty:€€ƒx¯Yet the eye of God hath looked upon him for good, and hath lifted him up from his low estate, and hath exalted his head: and many have wondered at him, and have glorified God.€€ƒxGGood things and evil, life and death, poverty and riches, are from God.€€ƒxpWisdom and discipline, and the knowledge of the law are with God. Love and the ways of good things are with him.€€ƒxbError and darkness are created with sinners: and they that glory in evil things, grow old in evil.€€ƒxWThe gift of God abideth with the just, and his advancement shall have success for ever.€€ƒxYThere is one that is enriched by living sparingly, and this is the portion of his reward.€€ƒxMIn that he saith: I have found me rest, and now I will eat of my goods alone:€€—ƒxfBe steadfast in thy covenant, and be conversant therein, and grow old in the work of thy commandments.€€ƒxKAbide not in the works of sinners. But trust in God, and stay in thy place,€€ƒxHFor it is easy in the eyes of God on a sudden to make the poor man rich.€€ƒxdThe blessing of God maketh haste to reward the just, and in a swift hour his blessing beareth fruit.€€ƒx9Say not: What need I, and what good shall I have by this?€€ƒxLSay not: I am sufficient for myself: and what shall I be made worse by this?€€ƒxmIn the day of good things be not unmindful of evils: and in the day of evils be not unmindful of good things:€€ƒxXFor it is easy before God in the day of death to reward every one according to his ways.€€ƒxsThe affliction of an hour maketh one forget great delights, and in the end of a man is the disclosing of his works.€€ƒxDPraise not any man before death, for a man is known by his children.€€ƒxMBring not every man into thy house: for many are the snares of the deceitful.€€ƒxÜFor as corrupted bowels send forth stinking breath, and as the partridge is brought into the cage, and as the roe into the snare: so also is the heart of the proud, and as a spy that looketh on the fall of his neighbour.€€ƒxUFor he lieth in wait and turneth good into evil, and on the elect he will lay a blot.€€ƒxpOf one spark cometh a great fire, and of one deceitful man much blood: and a sinful man lieth in wait for blood.€€ƒxkTake heed to thyself of a mischievous man, for he worketh evils: lest he bring upon thee reproach for ever.€€ƒxhReceive a stranger in, and he shall overthrow thee with a whirlwind, and shall turn thee out of thy own.€€ƒ`€€ƒx^If thou do good, know to whom thou dost it, and there shall be much thanks for thy good deeds.€€ƒxdDo good to the just, and thou shalt find great recompense: and if not of him, assuredly of the Lord.€€ƒx–For there is no good for him that is always occupied in evil, and that giveth no alms: for the Highest hateth sinners, and hath mercy on the penitent.€€ƒx“Give to the merciful and uphold not the sinner: God will repay vengeance to the ungodly and to sinners, and keep them against the day of vengeance.€€ƒx+Give to the good, and receive not a sinner.€€ƒxDo good to the humble, and give not to the ungodly: hold back thy bread, and give it not to him, lest thereby he overmaster thee.€€‘ƒxYA friend shall not be known in prosperity, and an enemy shall not be hidden in adversity.€€ƒx\In the prosperity of a man, his enemies are grieved: and a friend is known in his adversity.€€ƒx@Never trust thy enemy for as a brass pot his wickedness rusteth:€€ƒxPThough he humble himself and go crouching, yet take good heed and beware of him.€€ƒx½Set him not by thee, neither let him sit on thy right hand, lest he turn into thy place, and seek to take thy seat and at the last thou acknowledge my words, and be pricked with my sayings.€€ƒx©Who will pity an enchanter struck by a serpent, or any that come near wild beasts? so is it with him that keepeth company with a wicked man, and is involved in his sins.€€ƒxYFor an hour he will abide with thee: but if thou begin to decline, he will not endure it.€€ƒxeAn enemy speaketh sweetly with his lips, but in his heart he lieth in wait, to throw thee into a pit.€€ƒxbAn enemy weepeth with his eyes: but if he find an opportunity he will not be satisfied with blood:€€ƒx=And if evils come upon thee, thou shalt find him there first.€€ƒx_An enemy hath tears in his eyes, and while he pretendeth to help thee, will undermine thy feet.€€ƒxYHe will shake his head, and clap his hands, and whisper much, and change his countenance.€€ƒ`€€ƒxqHe that toucheth pitch, shall be defiled with it: and he that hath fellowship with the proud, shall put on pride.€€ƒx˜He shall take a burden upon him that hath fellowship with one more honourable than himself. And have no fellowship with one that is richer than thyself.€€ƒxwWhat agreement shall the earthen pot have with the kettle? for if they knock one against the other, it shall be broken.€€ƒxdThe rich man hath done wrong, and yet he will fume: but the poor is wronged and must hold his peace.€€˜ƒxkIf thou have any thing, he will live with thee, and will make thee bare, and he will not be sorry for thee.€€ƒx”If he have need of thee he will deceive thee, and smiling upon thee will put thee in hope; he will speak thee fair, and will say: What wantest thou?€€ƒxÈAnd he will shame thee by his meats, till he have drawn thee dry twice or thrice, and at last he will laugh at thee: and afterward when he seeth thee, he will forsake thee, and shake his head at thee.€€ƒx.Humble thyself to God, and wait for his hands.€€ƒx<Beware that thou be not deceived into folly, and be humbled.€€ƒxKBe not lowly in thy wisdom, lest being humbled thou be deceived into folly.€€ƒxbIf thou be invited by one that is mightier, withdraw thyself: for so he will invite thee the more.€€ƒxdBe not troublesome to him, lest thou be put back: and keep not far from him, lest thou be forgotten.€€ƒx£Affect not to speak with him as an equal, and believe not his many words: for by much talk he will sift thee, and smiling will examine thee concerning thy secrets.€€ƒxjHis cruel mind will lay up thy words: and he will not spare to do thee hurt, and to cast thee into prison.€€ƒxiTake heed to thyself, and attend diligently to what thou hearest: for thou walkest in danger of thy ruin.€€ƒxNWhen thou hearest those things, see as it were in sleep, and thou shalt awake.€€ƒx;Love God all thy life, and call upon him for thy salvation.€€ƒxNEvery beast loveth its like: so also every man him that is nearest to himself.€€ƒxcAll flesh shall consort with the like to itself, and every man shall associate himself to his like.€€ƒxYIf the wolf shall at any time have fellowship with the lamb, so the sinner with the just.€€ƒxUWhat fellowship hath a holy man with a dog, or what part hath the rich with the poor?€€ƒxYThe wild ass is the lion's prey in the desert: so also the poor are devoured by the rich.€€ƒxXAnd as humility is an abomination to the proud: so also the rich man abhorreth the poor.€€ƒxˆWhen a rich man is shaken, he is kept up by his friends: but when a poor man is fallen down, he is thrust away even by his acquaintance.€€ƒxsWhen a rich man hath been deceived, he hath many helpers: he hath spoken proud things, and they have justified him.€€ƒxbThe poor man was deceived, and he is rebuked also: he hath spoken wisely, and could have no place.€€ƒx]The rich man spoke, and all held their peace, and what he said they extol even to the clouds.€€ƒxZThe poor man spoke, and they say: Who is this? and if he stumble, they will overthrow him.€€ƒxrRiches are good to him that hath no sin in his conscience: and poverty is very wicked in the mouth of the ungodly.€€ƒxJThe heart of a man changeth his countenance, either for good, or for evil.€€ƒxZThe token of a good heart, and a good countenance thou shalt hardly find, and with labour.€€–ƒxpBlessed is the man that hath not slipped by a word out of his mouth, and is not pricked with the remorse of sin.€€ƒxVHappy is he that hath had no sadness of his mind, and who is not fallen from his hope.€€ƒxdRiches are not comely for a covetous man and a niggard, and what should an envious man do with gold?€€ƒxHe that gathereth together by wronging his own soul, gathereth for others, and another will squander away his goods in rioting.€€ƒxaHe that is evil to himself, to whom will he be good? and he shall not take pleasure in his goods.€€ƒx[There is none worse than he that envieth himself, and this is the reward of his wickedness:€€ƒxiAnd if he do good, he doth it ignorantly, and unwillingly: and at the last he discovereth his wickedness.€€ƒx[The eye of the envious is wicked: and he turneth away his face, and despiseth his own soul.€€ƒxŠThe eye of the covetous man is insatiable in his portion of iniquity: he will not be satisfied till he consume his own soul, drying it up.€€ƒx}An evil eye is towards evil things: and he shall not have his fill of bread, but shall be needy and pensive at his own table.€€ƒxVMy son, if thou have any thing, do good to thyself, and offer to God worthy offerings.€€ƒxˆRemember that death is not slow, and that the covenant of hell hath been shewn to thee: for the covenant of this world shall surely die.€€ƒxnDo good to thy friend before thou die, and according to thy ability, stretching out thy hand give to the poor.€€ƒxWDefraud not thyself of the good day, and let not the part of a good gift overpass thee.€€ƒxHShalt thou not leave to others to divide by lot thy sorrows and labours?€€ƒx$Give and take, and justify thy soul.€€ƒxDBefore thy death work justice: for in hell there is no finding food.€€ƒxRAll flesh shall fade as grass, and as the leaf that springeth out on a green tree.€€ƒxqSome grow, and some fall off: so is the generation of flesh and blood, one cometh to an end, and another is born.€€ƒx^Every work that is corruptible shall fail in the end: and the worker thereof shall go with it.€€ƒx^And every excellent work shall be justified: and the worker thereof shall be honoured therein.€€ƒx“Blessed is the man that shall continue in wisdom, and that shall meditate in his justice, and in his mind shall think of the all seeing eye of God.€€•ƒx=He who looketh in at her windows, and hearkeneth at her door.€€ƒx›He that lodgeth near her house, and fastening a pin in her walls shall set up his tent high unto her, where good things shall rest in his lodging for ever.€€ƒxPHe shall set his children under her shelter, and shall lodge under her branches:€€ƒxTHe shall be protected under her covering from the heat, and shall rest in her glory.€€ƒ`€€ƒxYHe that feareth God, will do good: and he that possesseth justice, shall lay hold on her,€€ƒxbAnd she will meet him as an honourable mother, and will receive him as a wife married of a virgin.€€ƒx²With the bread of life and understanding, she shall feed him, and give him the water of wholesome wisdom to drink: and she shall be made strong in him, and he shall not be moved.€€ƒxjAnd she shall hold him fast, and he shall not be confounded: and she shall exalt him among his neighbours.€€ƒx£And in the midst of the church she shall open his mouth, and shall fill him with the spirit of wisdom and understanding, and shall clothe him with a robe of glory.€€ƒxkShe shall heap upon him a treasure of joy and gladness, and shall cause him to inherit an everlasting name.€€ƒx‡But foolish men shall not obtain her, and wise men shall meet her, foolish men shall not see her: for she is far from pride and deceit.€€ƒxLying men shall be mindful of her: but men that speak truth shall be found with her, and shall advance, even till they come to the sight of God.€€ƒx.Praise is not seemly in the mouth of a sinner:€€ƒx¢For wisdom came forth from God: for praise shall be with the wisdom of God, and shall abound in a faithful mouth, and the sovereign Lord will give praise unto it.€€ƒx_Say not: It is through God, that she is not with me: for do not thou the things that he hateth.€€ƒxESay not: He hath caused me to err: for he hath no need of wicked men.€€ƒxSThe Lord hateth all abomination of error, and they that fear him shall not love it.€€ƒxMGod made man from the beginning, and left him in the hand of his own counsel.€€ƒx'He added his commandments and precepts.€€ƒxfIf thou wilt keep the commandments and perform acceptable fidelity for ever, they shall preserve thee.€€•ƒx[Before man is life and death, good and evil, that which he shall choose shall be given him:€€ƒxZFor the wisdom of God is great, and he is strong in power, seeing all men without ceasing.€€ƒxWThe eyes of the Lord are towards them that fear him, and he knoweth al the work of man.€€ƒxQHe hath commanded no man to do wickedly, and he hath given no man license to sin;€€ƒxGFor he desireth not a multitude of faithless and unprofitable children.€€ƒ`€€ƒxzRejoice not in ungodly children, if they be multiplied: neither be delighted in them, if the fear of God be not with them.€€ƒx7Trust not to their life, and respect not their labours.€€ƒxEFor better is one that feareth God, than a thousand ungodly children.€€ƒxIAnd it is better to die without children, than to leave ungodly children.€€ƒxaBy one that is wise a country shall be inhabited, the tribe of the ungodly shall become desolate.€€ƒxTMany such things hath my eyes seen, and greater things than these my ear hath heard.€€ƒxkIn the congregation of sinners a fire shall be kindled, and in an unbelieving nation wrath shall flame out.€€ƒxkThe ancient giants did not obtain pardon for their sins, who were destroyed trusting to their own strength:€€ƒx_And he spared not the place where Lot sojourned, but abhorred them for the pride of their word.€€ƒx\He had not pity on them, destroying the whole nation that extolled themselves in their sins.€€ƒx¹So did he with the six hundred thousand footmen, who were gathered together in the hardness of their heart: and if one had been stiffnecked, it is a wonder if he had escaped unpunished:€€ƒxWFor mercy and wrath are with him. He is mighty to forgive, and to pour out indignation:€€ƒxRAccording as his mercy is, so his correction judgeth a man according to his works.€€ƒxlThe sinner shall not escape in his rapines, and the patience of him that sheweth mercy shall not be put off.€€ƒxAll mercy shall make a place for every man according to the merit of his works, and according to the wisdom of his sojournment.€€˜ƒxZIn such a multitude I shall not be known: for what is my soul in such an immense creation?€€ƒx‰Behold the heaven, and the heavens of heavens, the deep, and all the earth, and the things that are in them, shall be moved in his sight,€€ƒxˆThe mountains also, and the hills, and the foundations of the earth: when God shall look upon them, they shall be shaken with trembling.€€ƒxUAnd in all these things the heart is senseless: and every heart is understood by him.€€ƒxPAnd his ways who shall understand, and the storm, which no eye of man shall see?€€ƒx´For many of his works are hidden, but the works of his justice who shall declare? or who shall endure? for the testament is far from some, and the examination of all is in the end.€€ƒxmHe that wanteth understanding thinketh vain things, and the foolish, and erring man, thinketh foolish things.€€ƒxfHearken to me, my son, and learn the discipline of understanding, and attend to my words in thy heart.€€ƒyAnd I will shew forth good doctrine in equity, and will seek to declare wisdom: and attend to my words in thy heart, whilst with equity of spirit I tell thee the virtues that God hath put upon his works from the beginning, and I shew forth in truth his knowledge.€€ƒxThe works of God are done in judgment from the beginning, and from the making of them he distinguished their parts, and their beginnings in their generations.€€ƒxxHe beautified their works for ever, they have neither hungered, nor laboured, and they have not ceased from their works.€€ƒx9Nor shall any of them straiten his neighbour at any time.€€ƒx$Be not thou incredulous to his word.€€ƒxCAfter this God looked upon the earth, and filled it with his goods.€€ƒxgThe soul of every living thing hath shewn forth before the face thereof, and into it they return again.€€ƒ`€€ƒx?God created man of the earth, and made him after his own image.€€ƒxTAnd he turned him into it again, and clothed him with strength according to himself.€€ƒxhHe gave him the number of his days and time, and gave him power over all things that are upon the earth.€€ƒxQHe put the fear of him upon all flesh, and he had dominion over beasts and fowls.€€ƒx´He created of him a helpmate like to himself, he gave them counsel, and a tongue, and eyes, and ears, and a heart to devise: and he filled them with the knowledge of understanding.€€ƒxsHe created in them the science of the spirit, he fired their heart with wisdom, and shewed them both good and evil.€€ƒxIHe set his eye upon their hearts to shew them the greatness of his works:€€ƒx‘That they might praise the name which he hath sanctified: and glory in his wondrous act that they might declare the glorious things of his works.€€ƒxKMoreover he gave them instructions, and the law of life for an inheritance.€€ƒxXHe made an everlasting covenant with them, and he shewed them his justice and judgments.€€ƒxAnd their eye saw the majesty of his glory, and their ears heard his glorious voice, and he said to them: Beware of all iniquity.€€—ƒxDTheir ways are always before him, they are not hidden from his eyes.€€ƒx!Over every nation he set a ruler.€€ƒx0And Israel was made the manifest portion of God.€€ƒxeAnd all their works are as the sun in the sight of God: and his eyes are continually upon their ways.€€ƒxaTheir covenants were not hid by their iniquity, and all their iniquities are in the sight of God.€€ƒxiThe alms of a man is as a signet with him, and shall preserve the grace of a man as the apple of the eye:€€ƒxœAnd afterward he shall rise up, and shall render them their reward, to every one upon their own head, and shall turn them down into the bowels of the earth.€€ƒx But to the penitent he hath given the way of justice, and he hath strengthened them that were fainting in patience, and hath appointed to them the lot of truth.€€ƒx'Turn to the Lord, and forsake thy sins:€€ƒx=Make thy prayer before the face of the Lord, and offend less.€€ƒxSReturn to the Lord, and turn away from thy injustice, and greatly hate abomination.€€ƒxzAnd know the justices and judgments of God, and stand firm in the lot set before thee, and in prayer to the most high God.€€ƒxKGo to the side of the holy age, with them that live and give praise to God.€€ƒxjTarry not in the error of the ungodly, give glory before death. Praise perisheth from the dead as nothing.€€ƒx•Give thanks whilst thou art living, whilst thou art alive and in health thou shalt give thanks, and shalt praise God, and shalt glory in his mercies.€€ƒxRHow great is the mercy of the Lord, and his forgiveness to them that turn to him !€€ƒxxFor all things cannot be in men, because the son of man is not immortal, and they are delighted with the vanity of evil.€€ƒxšWhat is brighter than the sun; yet it shall be eclipsed. Or what is more wicked than that which flesh and blood hath invented? and this shall be reproved.€€ƒxPHe beholdeth the power of the height of heaven: and all men are earth and ashes.€€ƒ`€€ƒxHe that liveth for ever created all things together. God only shall be justified, and he remaineth an invincible king for ever.€€ƒx!Who is able to declare his works?€€ƒx+For who shall search out his glorious acts?€€ƒxcNothing may be taken away, nor added, neither is it possible to find out the glorious works of God.€€ƒxZWhen a man hath done, then shall he begin: and when he leaveth off, he shall be at a loss.€€ƒxNWhat is man, and what is his grace? and what is his good, or what is his evil?€€ƒxµThe number of the days of men at the most are a hundred years, as a drop of water of the sea are they esteemed: and as a pebble of the sand, so are a few years compared to eternity.€€ƒxHTherefore God is patient in them, and poureth forth his mercy upon them.€€ƒxhHe hath seen the presumption of their heart that it is wicked, and hath known their end that it is evil.€€ƒx^Therefore hath he filled up his mercy in their favour, and hath shewn them the way of justice.€€ƒxVThe compassion of man is toward his neighbour: but the mercy of God is upon all flesh.€€ƒxJHe hath mercy, and teacheth, and correcteth, as a shepherd doth his flock.€€ƒxdHe hath mercy on him that receiveth the discipline of mercy, and that maketh haste in his judgments.€€ƒxlMy son, in thy good deeds, make no complaint, and when thou givest any thing, add not grief by an evil word.€€ƒxRShall not the dew assuage the heat? so also the good word is better than the gift.€€ƒxHLo, is not a word better than a gift? but both are with a justified man.€€ƒxNA fool will upbraid bitterly: and a gift of one ill taught consumeth the eyes.€€ƒxBBefore judgment prepare thee justice, and learn before thou speak.€€’ƒxWHumble thyself before thou art sick, and in the time of sickness shew thy conversation.€€ƒxˆLet nothing hinder thee from praying always, and be not afraid to be justified even to death: for the reward of God continueth for ever.€€ƒxFBefore prayer prepare thy soul: and be not as a man that tempteth God.€€ƒxlRemember the wrath that shall be at the last day, and the time of repaying when he shall turn away his face.€€ƒx_Remember poverty in the time of abundance, and the necessities of poverty in the day of riches.€€ƒxiFrom the morning until the evening the time shall be changed, and all these are swift in the eyes of God.€€ƒxRA wise man will fear in every thing, and in the days of sins will beware of sloth.€€ƒxXEvery man of understanding knoweth wisdom, and will give praise to him that findeth her.€€ƒx¥They that were of good understanding in words, have also done wisely themselves: and have understood truth and justice, and have poured forth proverbs and judgments.€€ƒx8Go not after thy lusts, but turn away from thy own will.€€ƒxNIf thou give to thy soul her desires, she will make thee a joy to thy enemies.€€ƒxcTake no pleasure in riotous assemblies, be they ever so small: for their concertation is continual.€€ƒx‹Make not thyself poor by borrowing to contribute to feasts when thou hast nothing in thy purse: for thou shalt be an enemy to thy own life.€€ƒ`€€ƒxuA workman that is a drunkard shall not be rich: and he that contemneth small things, shall fall by little and little.€€ƒxDWine and women make wise men fall off, and shall rebuke the prudent:€€ƒxÄAnd he that joineth himself to harlots, will be wicked. Rottenness and worms shall inherit him, and he shall be lifted up for a greater example, and his soul shall be taken away out of the number.€€ƒx‡He that is hasty to give credit, is light of heart, and shall be lessened: and he that sinneth against his own soul, shall be despised.€€ƒxqHe that sinneth against his own soul, shall repent: and he that is delighted with wickedness, shall be condemned.€€ƒxNRehearse not again a wicked and harsh word, and thou shalt not fare the worse.€€ƒxUTell not thy mind to friend or foe: and if there be a sin with thee, disclose it not.€€ƒx‰For he will hearken to thee, and will watch thee, and as it were defending thy sin he will hate thee, and so will he be with thee always.€€ƒxkHast thou heard a word against thy neighbour? let it die within thee, trusting that it will not burst thee.€€ƒxcAt the hearing of a word the fool is in travail, as a woman groaning in the bringing forth a child.€€ƒxPAs an arrow that sticketh in a man's thigh: so is a word in the heart of a fool.€€ƒxuReprove a friend, lest he may not have understood, and say: I did it not: or if he did it, that he may do it no more.€€ƒxoReprove thy neighbour, for it may be he hath not said it: and if he hath said it, that he may not say it again.€€ƒx:Admonish thy friend: for there is often a fault committed.€€ƒx`And believe not every word. There is one, that slippeth with the tongue, but not from his heart.€€ƒxiFor who is there that hath not offended with his tongue? Admonish thy neighbour before thou threaten him.€€ƒxœAnd give place to the fear of the most High: for the fear of God is all wisdom, and therein is to fear God, and the disposition of the law is in all wisdom.€€ƒxXBut the learning of wickedness is not wisdom: and the device of sinners is not prudence.€€˜ƒx¬Better is a man that hath less wisdom, and wanteth understanding, with the fear of God, than he that aboundeth in understanding, and transgresseth the law of the most High.€€ƒx7There is an exquisite subtilty, and the same is unjust.€€ƒxAnd there is one that uttereth an exact word telling the truth. There is one that humbleth himself wickedly, and his interior is full of deceit:€€ƒx·And there is one that submitteth himself exceedingly with a great lowliness: and there is one that casteth down his countenance, and maketh as if he did not see that which is unknown:€€ƒxmAnd if he be hindered from sinning for want of power, if he shall find opportunity to do evil, he will do it.€€ƒx_A man is known by his look, and a wise man, when thou meetest him, is known by his countenance.€€ƒx`The attire of the body, and the laughter of the teeth, and the gait of the man, shew what he is.€€ƒx¦There is a lying rebuke in the anger of an injurious man: and there is a judgment that is not allowed to be good: and there is one that holdeth his peace, he is wise.€€ƒ`€€ƒxdHow much better is it to reprove, than to be angry, and not to hinder him that confesseth in prayer.€€ƒx3The lust of an eunuch shall deflour a young maiden:€€ƒx2So is he that by violence executeth of the unwise.€€ƒx`How good is it, when thou art reproved, to shew repentance! for so thou shalt escape wilful sin.€€ƒxvThere is one that holdeth his peace, that is found wise: and there is another that is hateful, that is bold in speech.€€ƒxThere is one that holdeth his peace, because he knoweth not what to say: and there is another that holdeth his peace, knowing the proper time.€€ƒxgA wise man will hold his peace till he see opportunity: but a babbler, and a fool, will regard no time.€€ƒxrHe that useth many words shall hurt his own soul: and he that taketh authority to himself unjustly shall be hated.€€ƒxiThere is success in evil things to a man without discipline, and there is a finding that turneth to loss.€€ƒx_There is a gift that is not profitable: and there is a gift, the recompense of which is double.€€ƒxgThere is an abasement because of glory: and there is one that shall lift up his head from a low estate.€€ƒxNThere is that buyeth much for a small price, and restoreth the same sevenfold.€€ƒx\A man wise in words shall make himself beloved: but the graces of fools shall be poured out.€€ƒxGThe gift of the fool shall do thee no good: for his eyes are sevenfold.€€ƒxdHe will give a few things, and upbraid much: and the opening of his mouth is the kindling of a fire.€€ƒxVTo day a man lendeth, and to morrow he asketh it again: such a man as this is hateful.€€ƒxMA fool shall have no friend, and there shall be no thanks for his good deeds.€€ƒxdFor they that eat his bread, are of a false tongue. How often, and how many will laugh him to scorn!€€ƒxFor he doth not distribute with right understanding that which was to be had: in like manner also that which was not to be had.€€ƒxuThe slipping of a false tongue is as one that falleth on the pavement: so the fall of the wicked shall come speedily.€€ƒx[A man without grace is as a vain fable, it shall be continually in the mouth of the unwise.€€˜(ƒxYThere is that is hindered from sinning through want, and in his rest he shall be pricked.€€ƒx©There is that will destroy his own soul through shamefacedness, and by occasion of an unwise person he will destroy it: and by respect of person he will destroy himself.€€ƒx\There is that for bashfulness promiseth to his friend, and maketh him his enemy for nothing.€€ƒxeA lie is a foul blot in a man, and yet it will be continually in the mouth of men without discipline.€€ƒx^A thief is better than a man that is always lying: but both of them shall inherit destruction.€€ƒx^The manners of lying men are without honour: and their confusion is with them without ceasing.€€ƒx_A wise man shall advance himself with his words, and a prudent man shall please the great ones.€€ƒxœHe that tilleth his land shall make a high heap of corn: and he that worketh justice shall be exalted: and he that pleaseth great men shall escape iniquity.€€ƒxjPresents and gifts blind the eyes of judges, and make them dumb in the mouth, so that they cannot correct.€€ƒxWO Wisdom that is hid, and treasure that is not seen: what profit is there in them both?€€ƒxHBetter is he that hideth his folly, than the man that hideth his wisdom.€€ƒ`€€ƒxjMy son, hast thou sinned? do so no more: but for thy former sins also pray that they may be forgiven thee.€€ƒxhFlee from sins as from the face of a serpent: for if thou comest near them, they will take hold of thee.€€ƒxDThe teeth thereof are the teeth of a lion, killing the souls of men.€€ƒxQAll iniquity is like a two-edged sword, there is no remedy for the wound thereof.€€ƒxŸInjuries and wrongs will waste riches: and the house that is very rich shall be brought to nothing by pride: so the substance of the proud shall be rooted out.€€ƒxnThe prayer out of the mouth of the poor shall reach the ears of God, and judgment shall come for him speedily.€€ƒxsHe that hateth to be reproved walketh in the trace of a sinner: and he that feareth God will turn to his own heart.€€ƒx\He that is mighty by a bold tongue is known afar off, but a wise man knoweth to slip by him.€€ƒxqHe that buildeth his house at other men's charges, is as he that gathereth himself stones to build in the winter.€€ƒx`The congregation of sinners is like tow heaped together, and the end of them is a flame of fire.€€ƒx`The way of sinners is made plain with stones, and in their end is hell, and darkness, and pains.€€ƒx<He that keepeth justice shall get the understanding thereof.€€ƒx>The perfection of the fear of God is wisdom and understanding.€€ƒx0He that is not wise in good, will not be taught.€€ƒxfBut there is a wisdom that aboundeth in evil: and there is no understanding where there is bitterness.€€ƒxjThe knowledge of a wise man shall abound like a flood, and his counsel continueth like a fountain of life.€€ƒxPThe heart of a fool is like a broken vessel, and no wisdom at all shall it hold.€€ƒx¹A man of sense will praise every wise word he shall hear, and will apply it to himself: the luxurious man hath heard it, and it shall displease him, and he will cast it behind his back.€€ƒxeThe talking of a fool is like a burden in the way: but in the lips of the wise, grace shall be found.€€ƒxkThe mouth of the prudent is sought after in the church, and they will think upon his words in their hearts.€€ƒxpAs a house that is destroyed, so is wisdom to a fool: and the knowledge of the unwise is as words without sense.€€ƒxRDoctrine to a fool is as fetters on the feet, and like manacles on the right hand.€€ƒxYA fool lifteth up his voice in laughter: but a wise man will scarce laugh low to himself.€€ƒxZLearning to the prudent is as an ornament of gold, and like a bracelet upon his right arm.€€ƒxyThe foot of a fool is soon in his neighbour's house: but a man of experience will be abashed at the person of the mighty.€€ƒxbA fool will peep through the window into the house: but he that is well taught will stand without.€€ƒxbIt is the folly of a man to hearken at the door: and a wise man will be grieved with the disgrace.€€ƒxoThe lips of the unwise will be telling foolish things: but the words of the wise shall be weighed in a balance.€€Šƒx=While the ungodly curseth the devil, he curseth his own soul.€€ƒx¢The talebearer shall defile his own soul, and shall be hated by all: and he that shall abide with him shall be hateful: the silent and wise man shall be honoured.€€ƒ`€€ƒxRThe sluggard is pelted with a dirty stone, and all men will speak of his disgrace.€€ƒxcThe sluggard is pelted with the dung of oxen: and every one that toucheth him will shake his hands.€€ƒx]A son ill taught is the confusion of the father: and a foolish daughter shall be to his loss.€€ƒxwA wise daughter shall bring an inheritance to her husband: but she that confoundeth, becometh a disgrace to her father.€€ƒxƒShe that is bold shameth both her father and husband, and will not be inferior to the ungodly: and shall be disgraced by them both.€€ƒxnA tale out of time is like music in mourning: but the stripes and instruction of wisdom are never out of time.€€ƒxEHe that teacheth a fool, is like one that glueth a potsherd together.€€˜&ƒxzHe speaketh with one that is asleep, who uttereth wisdom to a fool: and in the end of the discourse he saith: Who is this?€€ƒxcWeep for the dead, for his light hath failed: and weep for the fool, for his understanding faileth.€€ƒx2Weep but a little for the dead, for he is at rest.€€ƒx9For the wicked life of a wicked fool is worse than death.€€ƒxfThe mourning for the dead is seven days: but for a fool and an ungodly man all the days of their life.€€ƒxATalk not much with a fool and go not with him that hath no sense.€€ƒxdKeep thyself from him, that thou mayst not have trouble, and thou shalt not be defiled with his sin.€€ƒxZTurn away from him, and thou shalt find rest, and shalt not be wearied out with his folly.€€ƒx@What is heavier than lead? and what other name hath he but fool?€€ƒxoSand and salt, and a mass of iron is easier to bear, than a man without sense, that is both foolish and wicked.€€ƒx—A frame of wood bound together in the foundation of a building, shall not be loosed: so neither shall the heart that is established by advised counsel.€€ƒxLThe thought of him that is wise at all times, shall not be depraved by fear.€€ƒxlAs pales set in high places, and plasterings made without cost, will not stand against the face of the wind:€€ƒxcSo also a fearful heart in the imagination of a fool shall not resist against the violence of fear.€€ƒxAs a fearful heart in the thought of a fool at all times will not fear, so neither shall he that continueth always in the commandments of God.€€ƒxhHe that pricketh the eye, bringeth out tears: and he that pricketh the heart, bringeth forth resentment.€€ƒxpHe that flingeth a stone at birds, shall drive them away: so he that upbraideth his friend, breaketh friendship.€€ƒxeAlthough thou hast drawn a sword at a friend, despair not: for there may be a returning. To a friend,€€ƒxÜIf thou hast opened a sad mouth, fear not, for there may be a reconciliation: except upbraiding, and reproach, and pride, and disclosing of secrets, or a treacherous wound: for in all these cases a friend will flee away.€€ƒx[Keep fidelity with a friend in his poverty, that in his prosperity also thou mayst rejoice.€€ƒxnIn the time of his trouble continue faithful to him, that thou mayst also be heir with him in his inheritance.€€ƒx“As the vapour of a chimney, and the smoke of the fire goeth up before the fire: so also injurious words, and reproaches, and threats, before blood.€€ƒxˆI will not be ashamed to salute a friend, neither will I hide myself from his face: and if any evil happen to me by him, I will bear it.€€ƒx5But every one that shall hear it, will beware of him.€€ƒxWho will set a guard before my mouth, and a sure seal upon my lips, that I fall not by them, and that my tongue destroy me not?€€ƒ`€€ƒxmO Lord, father, and sovereign ruler of my life, leave me not to their counsel: nor suffer me to fall by them.€€ƒx£Who will set scourges over my thoughts, and the discipline of wisdom over my heart, that they spare me not in their ignorances, and that their sins may not appear:€€ƒxLest my ignorances increase, and my offences be multiplied, and my sins abound, and I fall before my adversaries, and my enemy rejoice over me?€€ƒxBO Lord, father, and God of my life, leave me not to their devices.€€ƒxGGive me not haughtiness of my eyes, and turn away from me all coveting.€€ƒx“Take from me the greediness of the belly, and let not the lusts of the flesh take hold of me, and give me not over to a shameless and foolish mind.€€ƒx—Hear, O ye children, the discipline of the mouth, and he that will keep it shall not perish by his lips, nor be brought to fall into most wicked works.€€ƒx]A sinner is caught in his own vanity, and the proud and the evil speakers shall fall thereby.€€ƒxLLet not thy mouth be accustomed to swearing: for in it there are many falls.€€ƒx‡And let not the naming of God be usual in thy mouth, and meddle not with the names of saints, for thou shalt not escape free from them.€€ƒx’For as a slave daily put to the question, is never without a blue mark: so every one that sweareth, and nameth, shall not be wholly pure from sin.€€ƒxgA man that sweareth much, shall be filled with iniquity, and a scourge shall not depart from his house.€€˜ƒxfAnd if he swear in vain, he shall not be justified: for his house shall be filled with his punishment.€€ƒx`There is also another speech opposite to death, let it not be found in the inheritance of Jacob.€€ƒx^For from the merciful all these things shall be taken away, and they shall not wallow in sins.€€ƒxULet not thy mouth be accustomed to indiscreet speech: for therein is the word of sin.€€ƒxORemember thy father and thy mother, for thou sittest in the midst of great men:€€ƒx°Lest God forget thee in their sight, and thou, by thy daily custom be infatuated and suffer reproach: and wish that thou hadst not been born, and curse the day of thy nativity.€€ƒxbThe man that is accustomed to opprobrious words, will never be corrected all the days of his life.€€ƒxMTwo sorts of men multiply sins, and the third bringeth wrath and destruction.€€ƒxSA hot soul is a burning fire, it will never be quenched, till it devour some thing.€€ƒxcAnd a man that is wicked in the mouth of his flesh, will not leave off till he hath kindled a fire.€€ƒx_To a man that is a fornicator all bread is sweet, he will not be weary of sinning unto the end.€€ƒx\Every man that passeth beyond his own bed, despising his own soul, and saying: Who seeth me?€€ƒxƒDarkness compasseth me about, and the walls cover me, and no man seeth me: whom do I fear? the most High will not remember my sins.€€ƒxAnd he understandeth not that his eye seeth all things, for such a man's fear driveth him from the fear of God, and the eyes of men fearing him:€€ƒxÒAnd he knoweth not that the eyes of the Lord are far brighter than the sun, beholding round about all the ways of men, and the bottom of the deep, and looking into the hearts of men, into the most hidden parts.€€ƒxFor all things were known to the Lord God, before they were created: so also after they were perfected he beholdeth all things.€€ƒx‡This man shall be punished in the streets of the city, and he shall be chased as a colt: and where he suspected not, he shall be taken.€€ƒxYAnd he shall be in disgrace with all men, because he understood not the fear of the Lord.€€ƒxQSo every woman also that leaveth her husband, and bringeth in an heir by another:€€ƒxËFor first she hath been unfaithful to the law of the most High: and secondly, she hath offended against her husband: thirdly, she hath fornicated in adultery, and hath gotten her children of another man.€€ƒx]This woman shall be brought into the assembly, and inquisition shall be made of her children.€€ƒxNHer children shall not take root, and her branches shall bring forth no fruit.€€ƒxQShe shall leave her memory to be cursed, and her infamy shall not be blotted out.€€ƒxªAnd they that remain shall know, that there is nothing better than the fear of God: and that there is nothing sweeter than to have regard to the commandments of the Lord.€€–ƒ`€€ƒxkWisdom shall praise her own self, and shall be honoured in God, and shall glory in the midst of her people,€€ƒxoAnd shall open her mouth in the churches of the most High, and shall glorify herself in the sight of his power,€€ƒxcAnd in the midst of her own people she shall be exalted, and shall be admired in the holy assembly.€€ƒxlAnd in the multitude of the elect she shall have praise, and among the blessed she shall be blessed, saying:€€ƒxMI came out of the mouth of the most High, the firstborn before all creatures:€€ƒxnI made that in the heavens there should rise light that never faileth, and as a cloud I covered all the earth:€€ƒxGI dwelt in the highest places, and my throne is in a pillar of a cloud.€€ƒx‡I alone have compassed the circuit of heaven, and have penetrated into the bottom of the deep, and have walked in the waves of the sea,€€ƒx5And have stood in all the earth: and in every people,€€ƒx.And in every nation I have had the chief rule:€€ƒx¢And by my power I have trodden under my feet the hearts of all the high and low: and in all these I sought rest, and I shall abide in the inheritance of the Lord.€€ƒxgThen the creator of all things commanded, and said to me: and he that made me, rested in my tabernacle,€€ƒxjAnd he said to me: Let thy dwelling be in Jacob, and thy inheritance in Israel, and take root in my elect.€€ƒx©From the beginning, and before the world, was I created, and unto the world to come I shall not cease to be, and in the holy dwelling place I have ministered before him.€€ƒxhAnd so was I established in Sion, and in the holy city likewise I rested, and my power was in Jerusalem.€€ƒx†And I took root in an honourable people, and in the portion of my God his inheritance, and my abode is in the full assembly of saints.€€ƒxKI was exalted like a cedar in Libanus, and as a cypress tree on mount Sion.€€ƒxHI was exalted like a palm tree in Cades, and as a rose plant in Jericho:€€ƒxcAs a fair olive tree in the plains, and as a plane tree by the water in the streets, was I exalted.€€ƒxeI gave a sweet smell like cinnamon, and aromatical balm: I yielded a sweet odour like the best myrrh:€€ƒxAnd I perfumed my dwelling as storax, and galbanum, and onyx, and aloes, and as the frankincense not cut, and my odour is as the purest balm.€€‘ƒxeAs the vine I have brought forth a pleasant odour: and my flowers are the fruit of honour and riches.€€ƒxNI am the mother of fair love, and of fear, and of knowledge, and of holy hope.€€ƒxXIn me is all grace of the way and of the truth, in me is all hope of life and of virtue.€€ƒxECome over to me, all ye that desire me, and be filled with my fruits.€€ƒxUFor my spirit is sweet above honey, and my inheritance above honey and the honeycomb.€€ƒx*My memory is unto everlasting generations.€€ƒxMThey that eat me, shall yet hunger: and they that drink me, shall yet thirst.€€ƒx[He that hearkeneth to me, shall not be confounded: and they that work by me, shall not sin.€€ƒx1They that explain me shall have life everlasting.€€ƒxeAll these things are the book of life, and the covenant of the most High, and the knowledge of truth.€€ƒxxMoses commanded a law in the precepts of justices, and an inheritance to the house of Jacob, and the promises to Israel.€€ƒxuHe appointed to David his servant to raise up of him a most mighty king, and sitting on the throne of glory for ever.€€ƒxUWho filleth up wisdom as the Phison, and as the Tigris in the days of the new fruits.€€ƒxmWho maketh understanding to abound as the Euphrates, who multiplieth it as the Jordan in the time of harvest.€€ƒxVWho sendeth knowledge as the light, and riseth up as Gehon in the time of the vintage.€€ƒxOWho first hath perfect knowledge of her, and a weaker shall not search her out.€€ƒx]For her thoughts are more vast than the sea, and her counsels more deep than the great ocean.€€˜ ƒx{I, like a brook out of a river of a mighty water; I, like a channel of a river, and like an aqueduct, came out of paradise.€€ƒx^I said: I will water my garden of plants, and I will water abundantly the fruits of my meadow.€€ƒxJAnd behold my brook became a great river, and my river came near to a sea:€€ƒx_For I make doctrine to shine forth to all as the morning light, and I will declare it afar off.€€ƒxƒI will penetrate to all the lower parts of the earth, and will behold all that sleep, and will enlighten all that hope in the Lord.€€ƒxšI will yet pour out doctrine as prophecy, and will leave it to them that seek wisdom, and will not cease to instruct their offspring even to the holy age.€€ƒxQSee ye that I have not laboured myself only, but for all that seek out the truth.€€ƒ`€€ƒxNWith three things my spirit is pleased, which are approved before God and men:€€ƒx_The concord of brethren, and the love of neighbours, and man and wife that agree well together.€€ƒxCThree sorts my soul hateth, and I am greatly grieved at their life:€€ƒx[A poor man that is proud: a rich man that is a liar: an old man that is a fool, and doting.€€ƒx]The things that thou hast not gathered in thy youth, how shalt thou find them in thy old age?€€ƒxKO how comely is judgment for a grey head, and for ancients to know counsel!€€ƒxTO how comely is wisdom for the aged, and understanding and counsel to men of honour!€€ƒxLMuch experience is the crown of old men, and the fear of God is their glory.€€ƒxxNine things that are not to be imagined by the heart have I magnified, and the tenth I will utter to men with my tongue.€€ƒxZA man that hath joy of his children: and he that liveth and seeth the fall of his enemies.€€ƒxBlessed is he that dwelleth with a wise woman, and that hath not slipped with his tongue, and that hath not served such as are unworthy of him.€€ƒx\Blessed is he that findeth a true friend, and that declareth justice to an ear that heareth.€€ƒxeHow great is he that findeth wisdom and knowledge! but there is none above him that feareth the Lord.€€ƒx1The fear of God hath set itself above all things:€€ƒxqBlessed is the man, to whom it is given to have the fear of God: he that holdeth it, to whom shall he be likened?€€ƒxfThe fear of God is the beginning of his love: and the beginning of faith is to be fast joined unto it.€€ƒxTThe sadness of the heart is every plague: and the wickedness of a woman is all evil.€€ƒx>And a man will choose any plague, but the plague of the heart:€€ƒx2And any wickedness, but the wickedness of a woman:€€ƒx?And any affliction, but the affliction from them that hate him:€€ƒx,And any revenge, but the revenge of enemies.€€ƒx2There is no head worse than the head of a serpent:€€ƒx‘And there is no anger above the anger of a woman. It will be more agreeable to abide with a lion and a dragon, than to dwell with a wicked woman.€€ƒx˜The wickedness of a woman changeth her face: and she darkeneth her countenance as a bear: and sheweth it like sackcloth. In the midst of her neighbours,€€˜ƒxSAll malice is short to the malice of a woman, let the lot of sinners fall upon her.€€ƒxfAs the climbing of a sandy way is to the feet of the aged, so is a wife full of tongue to a quiet man.€€ƒxBLook not upon a woman's beauty, and desire not a woman for beauty.€€ƒx7A woman's anger, and impudence, and confusion is great.€€ƒx=A woman, if she have superiority, is contrary to her husband.€€ƒxXA wicked woman abateth the courage, and maketh a heavy countenance, and a wounded heart.€€ƒxQFeeble hands, and disjointed knees, a woman that doth not make her husband happy.€€ƒx@From the woman came the beginning of sin, and by her we all die.€€ƒxZGive no issue to thy water, no, not a little: nor to a wicked woman liberty to gad abroad.€€ƒxPIf she walk not at thy hand, she will confound thee in the sight of thy enemies.€€ƒx7Cut her off from thy flesh, lest she always abuse thee.€€ƒ`€€ƒxKHappy is the husband of a good wife: for the number of his years is double.€€ƒxXA virtuous woman rejoiceth her husband, and shall fulfil the years of his life in peace.€€ƒxtA good wife is a good portion, she shall be given in the portion of them that fear God, to a man for his good deeds.€€ƒxSRich or poor, if his heart is good, his countenance shall be cheerful at all times.€€ƒxSOf three things my heart hath been afraid, and at the fourth my face hath trembled:€€ƒxCThe accusation of a city, and the gathering together of the people:€€ƒx6And a false calumny, all are more grievous than death.€€ƒx7A jealous woman is the grief and mourning of the heart.€€ƒxMWith a jealous woman is a scourge of the tongue which communicateth with all.€€ƒx‰As a yoke of oxen that is moved to and fro, so also is a wicked woman: he that hath hold of her, is as he that taketh hold of a scorpion.€€ƒxNA drunken woman is a great wrath: and her reproach and shame shall not be hid.€€ƒx\The fornication of a woman shall be known by the haughtiness of her eyes and by her eyelids.€€˜(ƒxJTake heed of the impudence of her eyes, and wonder not if she slight thee.€€ƒxÂShe will open her mouth as a thirsty traveller to the fountain, and will drink of every water near her, and will sit down by every hedge, and open her quiver against every arrow, until she fail.€€ƒxQThe grace of a diligent woman shall delight her husband, and shall fat his bones.€€ƒx"Her discipline is the gift of God.€€ƒx^Such is a wise and silent woman, and there is nothing so much worth as a well instructed soul.€€ƒx0A holy and shamefaced woman is grace upon grace.€€ƒx+And no price is worthy of a continent soul.€€ƒx€As the sun when it riseth to the world in the high places of God, so is the beauty of a good wife for the ornament of her house.€€ƒxZAs the lamp shining upon the holy candlestick, so is the beauty of the face in a ripe age,€€ƒx^As golden pillars upon bases of silver, so are the firm feet upon the soles of a steady woman.€€ƒxfAs everlasting foundations upon a solid rock, so the commandments of God in the heart of a holy woman.€€ƒxHAt two things my heart is grieved, and the third bringeth anger upon me.€€ƒxCA man of war fainting through poverty, and a man of sense despised:€€ƒxZAnd he that passeth over from justice to sin, God hath prepared such an one for the sword.€€ƒx©Two sorts of callings have appeared to me hard and dangerous: a merchant is hardly free from negligence: and a huckster shall not be justified from the sins of the lips.€€ƒ`€€ƒx[Through poverty many have sinned: and he that seeketh to be enriched, turneth away his eye.€€ƒxAs a stake sticketh fast in the midst of the joining of stones, so also in the midst of selling and buying, sin shall stick fast.€€ƒx'Sin shall be destroyed with the sinner.€€ƒxcUnless thou hold thyself diligently in the fear of the Lord, thy house shall quickly be overthrown.€€ƒxhAs when one sifteth with a sieve, the dust will remain: so will the perplexity of a man in his thoughts.€€ƒxNThe furnace trieth the potter's vessels, and the trial of affliction just men.€€ƒxfAs the dressing of a tree sheweth the fruit thereof, so a word out of the thought of the heart of man.€€ƒxBPraise not a man before he speaketh, for this is the trial of men.€€ƒxêIf thou followest justice, thou shalt obtain her: and shalt put her on as a long robe of honour, and thou shalt dwell with her: and she shall protect thee for ever, and in the day of acknowledgment thou shalt find a strong foundation.€€ƒxMBirds resort unto their like: so truth will return to them that practise her.€€ƒxQThe lion always lieth in wait for prey: so do sins for them that work iniquities.€€ƒxNA holy man continueth in wisdom as the sun: but a fool is changed as the moon.€€ƒxcIn the midst of the unwise keep in the word till its time: but be continually among men that think.€€ƒxSThe discourse of sinners is hateful, and their laughter is at the pleasures of sin.€€ƒx~The speech that sweareth much shall make the hair of the head stand upright: and its irreverence shall make one stop his ears.€€ƒx^In the quarrels of the road is the shedding of blood: and their cursing is a grievous hearing.€€ƒxgHe that discloseth the secret of a friend loseth his credit, and shall never find a friend to his mind.€€ƒx7Love thy neighbour, and be joined to him with fidelity.€€ƒx;But if thou discover his secrets, follow no more after him.€€ƒxbFor as a man that destroyeth his friend, so is he that destroyeth the friendship of his neighbour.€€ƒxwAnd as one that letteth a bird go out of his hand, so hast thou let thy neighbour go, and thou shalt not get him again.€€ƒx}Follow after him no more, for he is gone afar off, he is fled, as a roe escaped out of the snare because his soul is wounded.€€ƒxGThou canst no more bind him up. And of a curse there is reconciliation:€€ƒxLBut to disclose the secrets of a friend, leaveth no hope to an unhappy soul.€€˜,ƒx§In the sight of thy eyes he will sweeten his mouth, and will admire thy words: but at the last he will writhe his mouth, and on thy words he will lay a stumblingblock.€€ƒxFI have hated many things but not like him, and the Lord will hate him.€€ƒxoIf one cast a stone on high, it will fall upon his own head: and the deceitful stroke will wound the deceitful.€€ƒx¬He that diggeth a pit, shall fall into it: and he that setteth a stone for his neighbour, shall stumble upon it: and he that layeth a snare for another, shall perish in it.€€ƒxoA mischievous counsel shall be rolled back upon the author, and he shall not know from whence it cometh to him.€€ƒxYMockery and reproach are of the proud, and vengeance as a lion shall lie in wait for him.€€ƒxyThey shall perish in a snare that are delighted with the fall of the just: and sorrow shall consume them before they die.€€ƒxXAnger and fury are both of them abominable, and the sinful man shall be subject to them.€€ƒ`€€ƒxxHe that seeketh to revenge himself, shall find vengeance from the Lord, and he will surely keep his sins in remembrance.€€ƒxjForgive thy neighbour if he hath hurt thee: and then shall thy sins be forgiven to thee when thou prayest.€€ƒx;Man to man reserveth anger, and doth he seek remedy of God?€€ƒxMHe hath no mercy on a man like himself, and doth he entreat for his own sins?€€ƒxqHe that is but flesh, nourisheth anger, and doth he ask forgiveness of God? who shall obtain pardon for his sins?€€ƒx/Remember thy last things, and let enmity cease:€€ƒx7For corruption and death hang over in his commandments.€€ƒx>Remember the fear of God, and be not angry with thy neighbour.€€ƒxTRemember the covenant of the most High, and overlook the ignorance of thy neighbour.€€ƒx6Refrain from strife, and thou shalt diminish thy sins.€€ƒxŒFor a passionate man kindleth strife, and a sinful man will trouble his friends, and bring in debate in the midst of them that are at peace.€€ƒx¢For as the wood of the forest is, so the fire burneth, and as a man's strength is, so shall his anger be, and according to his riches he shall increase his anger.€€ƒxwA hasty contention kindleth a fire and a hasty quarrel sheddeth blood and a tongue that beareth witness bringeth death.€€ƒx|If thou blow the spark, it shall burn as a fire: and if thou spit upon it, it shall be quenched: both come out of the mouth.€€ƒx^The whisperer and the double tongue is accursed: for he hath troubled many that were at peace.€€ƒx\The tongue of a third person hath disquieted many, and scattered them from nation to nation.€€ƒx]It hath destroyed the strong cities of the rich, and hath overthrown the houses of great men.€€ƒxFIt hath cut in pieces the forces of people, and undone strong nations.€€ƒx]The tongue of a third person hath cast out valiant women, and deprived them of their labours.€€ƒxfHe that hearkeneth to it, shall never have rest, neither shall he have a friend in whom he may repose.€€ƒx[The stroke of a whip maketh a blue mark: but the stroke of the tongue will break the bones.€€ƒx`Many have fallen by the edge of the sword, but not so many as have perished by their own tongue.€€ƒx±Blessed is he that is defended from a wicked tongue, that hath not passed into the wrath thereof, and that hath not drawn the yoke thereof, and hath not been bound in its bands.€€ƒxAFor its yoke is a yoke of iron: and its bands are bands of brass.€€ƒxEThe death thereof is a most evil death: and hell is preferable to it.€€ƒxŠIts continuance shall not be for a long time, but it shall possess the ways of the unjust: and the just shall not be burnt with its flame.€€ƒx®They that forsake God shall fall into it, and it shall burn in them, and shall not be quenched, and it shall be sent upon them as a lion, and as a leopard it shall tear them.€€ƒx^Hedge in thy ears with thorns, hear not a wicked tongue, and make doors and bars to thy mouth.€€ƒxaMelt down thy gold and silver, and make a balance for thy words, and a just bridle for thy mouth:€€ƒx’And take heed lest thou slip with thy tongue, and fall in the sight of thy enemies who lie in wait for thee, and thy fall be incurable unto death.€€ƒ`€€ƒxkHe that sheweth mercy, lendeth to his neighbour: and he that is stronger in hand, keepeth the commandments.€€ƒx\Lend to thy neighbour in the time of his need, and pay thou thy neighbour again in due time.€€ƒxiKeep thy word, and deal faithfully with him: and thou shalt always find that which is necessary for thee.€€ƒxeMany have looked upon a thing lent as a thing found, and have given trouble to them that helped them.€€˜ƒx{But when they should repay, they will ask time, and will return tedious and murmuring words, and will complain of the time:€€ƒxrAnd if he be able to pay, he will stand off, he will scarce pay one half, and will count it as if he had found it:€€ƒx^But if not, he will defraud him of his money, and he shall get him for an enemy without cause.€€ƒxlAnd he will pay him with reproaches and curses, and instead of honour and good turn will repay him injuries.€€ƒxeMany have refused to lend, not out of wickedness, but they were afraid to be defrauded without cause.€€ƒxNBut yet towards the poor be thou more hearty, and delay not to shew him mercy.€€ƒxdHelp the poor because of the commandment: and send him not away empty handed because of his poverty.€€ƒxXLose thy money for thy brother and thy friend: and hide it not under a stone to be lost.€€ƒxgPlace thy treasure in the commandments of the most High, and it shall bring thee more profit than gold.€€ƒxZShut up alms in the heart of the poor, and it shall obtain help for thee against all evil.€€ƒx@Better than the shield of the mighty, and better than the spear:€€ƒx*It shall fight for thee against thy enemy.€€ƒx_A good man is surety for his neighbour: and he that hath lost shame, will leave him to himself.€€ƒxKForget not the kindness of thy surety: for he hath given his life for thee.€€ƒx2The sinner and the unclean fleeth from his surety.€€ƒx€A sinner attributeth to himself the goods of his surety: and he that is of an unthankful mind will leave him that delivered him.€€ƒxYA man is surety for his neighbour: and when he hath lost all shame, he shall forsake him.€€ƒx[Evil suretyship hath undone many of good estate, and hath tossed them as a wave of the sea.€€ƒxmIt hath made powerful men to go from place to place round about, and they have wandered in strange countries.€€ƒx›A sinner that transgresseth the commandment of the Lord, shall fall into an evil suretyship: and he that undertaketh many things, shall fall into judgment.€€ƒxZRecover thy neighbour according to thy power, and take heed to thyself that thou fall not.€€ƒx\The chief thing for man's life is water and bread, and clothing, and a house to cover shame.€€ƒxiBetter is the poor man's fare under a roof of boards, than sumptuous cheer abroad in another man's house.€€ƒx_Be contented with little instead of much, and thou shalt not hear the reproach of going abroad.€€ƒxIt is a miserable life to go as a guest from house to house: for where a man is a stranger, he shall not deal confidently, nor open his mouth.€€ƒxgHe shall entertain and feed, and give drink to the unthankful, and moreover he shall hear bitter words.€€–ƒxpGive place to the honourable presence of my friends: for I want my house, my brother being to be lodged with me.€€ƒxtThese things are grievous to a man of understanding: the upbraiding of houseroom, and the reproaching of the lender.€€ƒ`€€ƒ`€€ƒxŠHe that loveth his son, frequently chastiseth him, that he may rejoice in his latter end, and not grope after the doors of his neighbours.€€ƒxrHe that instructeth his son shall be praised in him, and shall glory in him in the midst of them of his household.€€ƒxjHe that teacheth his son, maketh his enemy jealous, and in the midst of his friends he shall glory in him.€€ƒxkHis father is dead, and he is as if he were not dead: for he hath left one behind him that is like himself.€€ƒxWhile he lived he saw and rejoiced in him: and when he died he was not sorrowful, neither was he confounded before his enemies.€€ƒxvFor he left behind him a defender of his house against his enemies, and one that will requite kindness to his friends.€€ƒxeFor the souls of his sons he shall bind up his wounds, and at every cry his bowels shall be troubled.€€ƒxYA horse not broken becometh stubborn, and a child left to himself will become headstrong.€€ƒxeGive thy son his way, and he shall make thee afraid: play with him, and he shall make thee sorrowful.€€ƒxTLaugh not with him, lest thou have sorrow, and at the last thy teeth be set on edge.€€ƒx?Give him not liberty in his youth, and wink not at his devices.€€ƒxBow down his neck while he is young, and beat his sides while he is a child, lest he grow stubborn, and regard thee not, and so be a sorrow of heart to thee.€€ƒxVInstruct thy son, and labour about him, lest his lewd behaviour be an offence to thee.€€ƒxtBetter is a poor man who is sound, and strong of constitution, than a rich man who is weak and afflicted with evils.€€ƒxwHealth of the soul in holiness of justice, is better than all gold and silver: and a sound body, than immense revenues.€€ƒxsThere is no riches above the riches of the health of the body: and there is no pleasure above the joy of the heart.€€ƒxRBetter is death than a bitter life, and everlasting rest, than continual sickness.€€ƒx]Good things that are hidden in a mouth that is shut, are as messes of meat set about a grave.€€“ƒxLSo is he that is persecuted by the Lord, bearing the reward of his iniquity:€€ƒxSHe seeth with his eyes, and groaneth, as an eunuch embracing a virgin, and sighing.€€ƒxLGive not up thy soul to sadness, and afflict not thyself in thy own counsel.€€ƒx„The joyfulness of the heart, is the life of a man, and a never failing treasure of holiness: and the joy of a man is length of life.€€ƒxˆHave pity on thy own soul, pleasing God, and contain thyself: gather up thy heart in his holiness: and drive away sadness far from thee.€€ƒx;For sadness hath killed many, and there is no profit in it.€€ƒxXEnvy and anger shorten a man's days, and pensiveness will bring old age before the time.€€ƒx[A cheerful and good heart is always feasting: for his banquets are prepared with diligence.€€ƒ`€€ƒxTWatching for riches consumeth the flesh, and the thought thereof driveth away sleep.€€ƒxfThe thinking beforehand turneth away the understanding, and a grievous sickness maketh the soul sober.€€ƒxoThe rich man hath laboured in gathering riches together, and when he resteth he shall be filled with his goods.€€ƒxSThe poor man hath laboured in his low way of life, and in the end he is still poor.€€ƒxmHe that loveth gold, shall not be justified: and he that followeth after corruption, shall be filled with it.€€ƒxUMany have been brought to fall for gold, and the beauty thereof hath been their ruin.€€ƒxƒGold is a stumblingblock to them that sacrifice to it: woe to them that eagerly follow after it, and every fool shall perish by it.€€ƒx†Blessed is the rich man that is found without blemish: and that hath not gone after gold, nor put his trust in money nor in treasures.€€ƒxQWho is he, and we will praise him? for he hath done wonderful things in his life.€€ƒx½Who hath been tried thereby, and made perfect, he shall have glory everlasting. He that could have transgressed, and hath not transgressed: and could do evil things, and hath not done them:€€˜ ƒxJArt thou set at a great table? be not the first to open thy mouth upon it.€€ƒx1Say not: There are many things which are upon it.€€ƒx#Remember that a wicked eye is evil.€€ƒxeWhat is created more wicked than an eye? therefore shall it weep over all the face when it shall see.€€ƒxXStretch not out thy hand first, lest being disgraced with envy thou be put to confusion.€€ƒxBe not hasty in a feast.€€ƒx5Judge of the disposition of thy neighbour by thyself.€€ƒxaUse as a frugal man the things that are set before thee: lest if thou eatest much, thou be hated.€€ƒxELeave off first, for manners' sake: and exceed not, lest thou offend.€€ƒxkAnd if thou sittest among many, reach not thy hand out first of all, and be not the first to ask for drink.€€ƒx…How sufficient is a little wine for a man well taught, and in sleeping thou shalt not be uneasy with it, and thou shalt feel no pain.€€ƒx>Watching, and choler, and gripes, are with an intemperate man:€€ƒxuSound and wholesome sleep with a moderate man: he shall sleep till morning, and his soul shall be delighted with him.€€ƒxAnd if thou hast been forced to eat much, arise, go out, and vomit: and it shall refresh thee, and thou shalt not bring sickness upon thy body.€€ƒxMHear me, my son, and despise me not: and in the end thou shalt find my words.€€ƒx?In all thy works be quick, and no infirmity shall come to thee.€€ƒxjThe lips of many shall bless him that is liberal of his bread, and the testimony of his truth is faithful.€€ƒxqAgainst him that is niggardly of his bread, the city will murmur, and the testimony of his niggardliness is true.€€ƒxEChallenge not them that love wine: for wine hath destroyed very many.€€ƒxTFire trieth hard iron: so wine drunk to excess shall rebuke the hearts of the proud.€€ƒx`Wine taken with sobriety is equal life to men: if thou drink it moderately, thou shalt be sober.€€ƒx.What is his life, who is diminished with wine?€€ƒxWhat taketh away life? death.€€ƒxSWine was created from the beginning to make men joyful, and not to make them drunk.€€ƒxBWine drunken with moderation is the joy of the soul and the heart.€€ƒx*Sober drinking is health to soul and body.€€ƒxEWine drunken with excess raiseth quarrels, and wrath, and many ruins.€€ƒx3Wine drunken with excess is bitterness of the soul.€€ƒxaThe heat of drunkenness is the stumblingblock of the fool, lessening strength and causing wounds.€€ƒxPRebuke not thy neighbour in a banquet of wine: and despise him not in his mirth.€€ƒxISpeak not to him words of reproach: and press him not in demanding again.€€ƒ`€€•ƒxkHave care of them, and so sit down, and when thou hast acquitted thyself of all thy charge, take thy place:€€ƒxvThat thou mayst rejoice for them, and receive a crown as an ornament of grace, and get the honour of the contribution.€€ƒx1Speak, thou that art elder: for it becometh thee,€€ƒxETo speak the first word with careful knowledge, and hinder not music.€€ƒxbWhere there is no hearing, pour not out words, and be not lifted up out of season with thy wisdom.€€ƒxFA concert of music in a banquet of wine is as a carbuncle set in gold.€€ƒxgAs a signet of an emerald in a work of gold: so is the melody of music with pleasant and moderate wine.€€ƒxEHear in silence, and for thy reverence good grace shall come to thee.€€ƒx+Young man, scarcely speak in thy own cause.€€ƒx0If thou be asked twice, let thy answer be short.€€ƒxSIn many things be as if thou wert ignorant, and hear in silence and withal seeking.€€ƒxbIn the company of great men take not upon thee: and when the ancients are present, speak not much.€€ƒx|Before a storm goeth lightning: and before shamefacedness goeth favour: and for thy reverence good grace shall come to thee.€€ƒx†And at the time of rising be not slack: but be first to run home to thy house, and there withdraw thyself, and there take thy pastime.€€ƒx=And do what thou hast a mind, but not in sin or proud speech.€€ƒxmAnd for all these things bless the Lord, that made thee, and that replenisheth thee with all his good things.€€ƒxpHe that feareth the Lord, will receive his discipline: and they that will seek him early, shall find a blessing.€€ƒx|He that seeketh the law, shall be filled with it: and he that dealeth deceitfully, shall meet with a stumblingblock therein.€€ƒxWThey that fear the Lord, shall find just judgment, and shall kindle justice as a light.€€ƒxNA sinful man will flee reproof, and will find an excuse according to his will.€€ƒx]A man of counsel will not neglect understanding, a strange and proud man will not dread fear:€€˜ƒxWMy son, do thou nothing without counsel, and thou shalt not repent when thou hast done.€€ƒx˜Go not in the way of ruin, and thou shalt not stumble against the stones: trust not thyself to a rugged way, lest thou set a stumblingblock to thy soul.€€ƒxGAnd beware of thy own children, and take heed of them of thy household.€€ƒx]In every work of thine regard thy soul in faith: for this is the keeping of the commandments.€€ƒxpHe that believeth God, taketh heed to the commandments: and he that trusteth in him, shall fare never the worse.€€ƒ`€€ƒxsNo evils shall happen to him that feareth the Lord, but in temptation God will keep him and deliver him from evils.€€ƒxoA wise man hateth not the commandments and justices, and he shall not be dashed in pieces as a ship in a storm.€€ƒxUA man of understanding is faithful to the law of God, and the law is faithful to him.€€ƒx—He that cleareth up a question, shall prepare what to say, and so having prayed he shall be heard, and shall keep discipline, and then he shall answer.€€ƒxZThe heart of a fool is as a wheel of a cart: and his thoughts are like a rolling axletree.€€ƒxgA friend that is a mocker, is like a stallion horse: he neigheth under every one that sitteth upon him.€€ƒxkWhy doth one day excel another, and one light another, and one year another year, when all come of the sun?€€ƒxfBy the knowledge of the Lord they were distinguished, the sun being made, and keeping his commandment.€€ƒxcAnd he ordered the seasons, and holidays of them, and in them they celebrated festivals at an hour.€€ƒx·Some of them God made high and great days, and some of them he put in the number of ordinary days. And all men are from the ground, and out of the earth, from whence Adam was created.€€ƒxJWith much knowledge the Lord hath divided them and diversified their ways.€€ƒxºSome of them hath he blessed, and exalted: and some of them hath he sanctified, and set near himself: and some of them hath he cursed and brought low, and turned them from their station.€€ƒx=As the potter's clay is in his hand, to fashion and order it:€€ƒxŒAll his ways are according to his ordering: so man is in the hand of him that made him, and he will render to him according to his judgment.€€ƒx²Good is set against evil, and life against death: so also is the sinner against a just man. And so look upon all the works of the most High. Two and two, and one against another.€€ƒxMAnd I awaked last of all, and as one that gathereth after the grapegatherers.€€ƒxhIn the blessing of God I also have hoped: and as one that gathereth grapes, have I filled the winepress.€€ƒxOSee that I have not laboured for myself only, but for all that seek discipline.€€ƒx^Hear me, ye great men, and all ye people, and hearken with your ears, ye rulers of the church.€€ƒx£Give not to son or wife, brother or friend, power over thee while thou livest; and give not thy estate to another, lest thou repent, and thou entreat for the same.€€ƒxHAs long as thou livest, and hast breath in thee, let no man change thee.€€ƒxlFor it is better that thy children should ask of thee, than that thou look toward the hands of thy children.€€’ƒxLet no stain sully thy glory. In the time when thou shalt end the days of thy life, and in the time of thy decease, distribute thy inheritance.€€ƒx]Fodder, and a wand, and a burden are for an ass: bread, and correction, and work for a slave.€€ƒx`He worketh under correction, and seeketh to rest: let his hands be idle, and he seeketh liberty.€€ƒxLThe yoke and the thong bend a stiff neck, and continual labours bow a slave.€€ƒxUTorture and fetters are for a malicious slave: send him to work, that he be not idle:€€ƒx#For idleness hath taught much evil.€€ƒx³Set him to work: for so it is fit for him. And if he be not obedient, bring him down with fetters, but be not excessive towards any one, and do no grievous thing without judgment.€€ƒx“If thou have a faithful servant, let him be to thee as thy own soul: treat him as a brother: because in the blood of thy soul thou hast gotten him.€€ƒx,If thou hurt him unjustly, he will run away:€€ƒxXAnd if he rise up and depart, thou knowest not whom to ask, and in what way to seek him.€€ƒ`€€ƒxbThe hopes of a man that is void of understanding are vain and deceitful: and dreams lift up fools.€€ƒxrThe man that giveth heed to lying visions, is like to him that catcheth at a shadow, and followeth after the wind.€€ƒxvThe vision of dreams is the resemblance of one thing to another: as when a man's likeness is before the face of a man.€€ƒxXWhat can be made clean by the unclean? and what truth can come from that which is false?€€ƒxNDeceitful divinations and lying omens and the dreams of evildoers, are vanity:€€ƒx‡And the heart fancieth as that of a woman in travail: except it be a vision sent forth from the most High, set not thy heart upon them.€€ƒxQFor dreams have deceived many, and they have failed that put their trust in them.€€ƒx¯What doth he know, that hath not been tried? A man that hath much experience, shall think of many things: and he that hath learned many things, shall shew forth understanding.€€ƒxsHe that hath no experience, knoweth little: and he that hath been experienced in many things, multiplieth prudence.€€ƒxyHe that hath not been tried, what manner of things doth he know? he that hath been surprised, shall abound with subtlety.€€ƒxBI have seen many things by travelling, and many customs of things.€€ƒxiSometimes I have been in danger of death for these things, and I have been delivered by the grace of God.€€ƒxWThe spirit of those that fear God, is sought after, and by his regard shall be blessed.€€ƒx[For their hope is on him that saveth them, and the eyes of God are upon them that love him.€€ƒx_He that feareth the Lord shall tremble at nothing, and shall not be afraid: for he is his hope.€€ƒx1The soul of him that feareth the Lord is blessed.€€ƒx.To whom doth he look, and who is his strength?€€ƒxThe eyes of the Lord are upon them that fear him, he is their powerful protector, and strong stay, a defence from the heat, and a cover from the sun at noon,€€ƒx•A preservation from stumbling, and a help from falling: he raiseth up the soul, and enlighteneth the eyes, and giveth health, and life, and blessing.€€ƒx‚The offering of him that sacrificeth of a thing wrongfully gotten, is stained, and the mockeries of the unjust are not acceptable.€€ƒxMThe Lord is only for them that wait upon him in the way of truth and justice.€€ƒx³The most High approveth not the gifts of the wicked: neither hath he respect to the oblations of the unjust, nor will he be pacified for sins by the multitude of their sacrifices.€€ƒxvHe that offereth sacrifice of the goods of the poor, is as one that sacrificeth the son in the presence of his father.€€’ƒxVHe that taketh away the bread gotten by sweat, is like him that killeth his neighbour.€€ƒxUHe that sheddeth blood, and he that defraudeth the laborer of his hire, are brothers.€€ƒxUWhen one buildeth up, and another pulleth down: what profit have they but the labour?€€ƒxAWhen one prayeth, and another curseth: whose voice will God hear?€€ƒxgHe that washeth himself after touching the dead, if he toucheth him again, what doth his washing avail?€€ƒxSo a man that fasteth for his sins, and doth the same again, what doth his humbling himself profit him? who will hear his prayer?€€ƒ`€€ƒx/He that keepeth the law, multiplieth offerings.€€ƒx^It is a wholesome sacrifice to take heed to the commandments, and to depart from all iniquity.€€ƒxtAnd to depart from injustice, is to offer a propitiatory sacrifice for injustices, and a begging of pardon for sins.€€ƒx]He shall return thanks, that offereth fine flour: and he that doth mercy, offereth sacrifice.€€ƒxoTo depart from iniquity is that which pleaseth the Lord, and to depart from injustice, is an entreaty for sins.€€ƒx5Thou shalt not appear empty in the sight of the Lord.€€ƒxFFor all these things are to be done because of the commandment of God.€€ƒxjThe oblation of the just maketh the altar fat, and is an odour of sweetness in the sight of the most High.€€ƒx[The sacrifice of the just is acceptable, and the Lord will not forget the memorial thereof.€€ƒxSGive glory to God with a good heart: and diminish not the firstfruits of thy hands.€€ƒxLIn every gift shew a cheerful countenance, and sanctify thy tithes with joy.€€–ƒxGFor the Lord maketh recompense, and will give thee seven times as much.€€ƒx8Do not offer wicked gifts, for such he will not receive.€€ƒxjAnd look not upon an unjust sacrifice, for the Lord is judge, and there is not with him respect of person.€€ƒxkThe Lord will not accept any person against a poor man, and he will hear the prayer of him that is wronged.€€ƒxeHe will not despise the prayers of the fatherless: nor the widow, when she poureth out her complaint.€€ƒx_Do not the widow's tears run down the cheek, and her cry against him that causeth them to fall?€€ƒxhFor from the cheek they go up even to heaven, and the Lord that heareth will not be delighted with them.€€ƒxbHe that adoreth God with joy, shall be accepted, and his prayer shall approach even to the clouds.€€ƒx£The prayer of him that humbleth himself, shall pierce the clouds: and till it come nigh he will not be comforted: and he will not depart till the most High behold.€€ƒx£And the Lord will not be slack, but will judge for the just, and will do judgment: and the Almighty will not have patience with them, that he may crush their back:€€ƒx‡And he will repay vengeance to the Gentiles, till he have taken away the multitude of the proud, and broken the sceptres of the unjust,€€ƒx|Till he have rendered to men according to their deeds: and according to the works of Adam, and according to his presumption,€€ƒxZTill he have judged the cause of his people, and he shall delight the just with his mercy.€€ƒxcThe mercy of God is beautiful in the time of affliction, as a cloud of rain in the time of drought.€€ƒ`€€ƒxVHave mercy upon us, O God of all, and behold us, and shew us the light of thy mercies:€€ƒx£And send thy fear upon the nations, that have not sought after thee: that they may know that there is no God beside thee, and that they may shew forth thy wonders.€€ƒxGLift up thy hand over the strange nations, that they may see thy power.€€ƒxmFor as thou hast been sanctified in us in their sight, so thou shalt be magnified among them in our presence,€€ƒx^That they may know thee, as we also have known thee, that there is no God beside thee, O Lord.€€ƒx'Renew thy signs, and work new miracles.€€ƒx$Glorify thy hand, and thy right arm.€€ƒx-Take away the adversary, and crush the enemy.€€ƒxQHasten the time, and remember the end, that they may declare thy wonderful works.€€ƒxgLet him that escapeth be consumed by the rage of the fire: and let them perish that oppress thy people.€€ƒxSCrush the head of the princes of the enemies that say: There is no other beside us.€€ƒx¯Gather together all the tribes of Jacob: that they may know that there no God besides thee, and may declare thy great works: and thou shalt inherit them as from the beginning.€€ƒxwHave mercy on thy people, upon whom thy name is invoked: and upon Israel, whom thou hast raised up to be thy firstborn.€€ƒxSHave mercy on Jerusalem, the city which thou hast sanctified, the city of thy rest.€€ƒxDFill Sion with thy unspeakable words, and thy people with thy glory.€€ƒxŠGive testimony to them that are thy creatures from the beginning, and raise up the prophecies which the former prophets spoke in thy name.€€ƒxxReward them that patiently wait for thee, that thy prophets may be found faithful: and hear the prayers of thy servants,€€ƒx²According to the blessing of Aaron over thy people, and direct us into the way of justice, and let all know that dwell upon the earth, that thou art God the beholder of all ages.€€ƒx?The belly will devour all meat, yet one is better than another.€€ƒx=The palate tasteth venison and the wise heart false speeches.€€˜ƒxHA woman will receive every man: yet one daughter is better than another.€€ƒx_The beauty of a woman cheereth the countenance of her husband, and a man desireth nothing more.€€ƒxlIf she have a tongue that can cure, and likewise mitigate and shew mercy: her husband is not like other men.€€ƒxlHe that possesseth a good wife, beginneth a possession: she is a help like to himself, and a pillar of rest.€€ƒxrWhere there is no hedge, the possession shall be spoiled: and where there is no wife, he mourneth that is in want.€€ƒx•Who will trust him that hath no rest, and that lodgeth wheresoever the night taketh him, as a robber well appointed, that skippeth from city to city.€€ƒ`€€ƒx…Every friend will say: I also am his friend: but there is a friend, that is only a friend in name. Is not this a grief even to death?€€ƒx9But a companion and a friend shall be turned to an enemy.€€ƒx_O wicked presumption, whence camest thou to cover the earth with thy malice, and deceitfulness?€€ƒxsThere is a companion who rejoiceth with his friend in his joys, but in the time of trouble, he will be against him.€€ƒxxThere is a companion who condoleth with his friend for his belly's sake, and he will take up a shield against the enemy.€€ƒxMForget not thy friend in thy mind, and be not unmindful of him in thy riches.€€ƒxaConsult not with him that layeth a snare for thee, and hide thy counsel from them that envy thee.€€ƒxWEvery counsellor giveth out counsel, but there is one that is a counsellor for himself.€€ƒx^Beware of a counsellor. And know before what need he hath: for he will devise to his own mind:€€ƒx8Lest he thrust a stake into the ground, and say to thee:€€ƒxPThy way is good; and then stand on the other side to see what shall befall thee.€€ƒy'Treat not with a man without religion concerning holiness, nor with an unjust man concerning justice, nor with a woman touching her of whom she is jealous, nor with a coward concerning war, nor with a merchant about traffic, nor with a buyer of selling, nor with an envious man of giving thanks,€€ƒxkNor with the ungodly of piety, nor with the dishonest of honesty, nor with the field laborer of every work,€€ƒxNor with him that worketh by the year of the finishing of the year, nor with an idle servant of much business: give no heed to these in any matter of counsel.€€ƒxZBut be continually with a holy man, whomsoever thou shalt know to observe the fear of God,€€ƒxnWhose soul is according to thy own soul: and who, when thou shalt stumble in the dark, will be sorry for thee.€€ƒxpAnd establish within thyself a heart of good counsel: for there is no other thing of more worth to thee than it.€€ƒxuThe soul of a holy man discovereth sometimes true things, more than seven watchmen that sit in a high place to watch.€€ƒxVBut above all these things pray to the most High, that he may direct thy way in truth.€€ƒxZIn all thy works let the true word go before thee, and steady counsel before every action.€€ƒxıA wicked word shall change the heart: out of which four manner of things arise, good and evil, life and death: and the tongue is continually the ruler of them. There is a man that is subtle and a teacher of many, and yet is unprofitable to his own soul.€€ƒx=A skilful man hath taught many, and is sweet to his own soul.€€ƒxQHe that speaketh sophistically, is hateful: he shall be destitute of every thing.€€…ƒxdThere is a wise man that is wise to his own soul: and the fruit of his understanding is commendable.€€ƒxXA wise man instructeth his own people, and the fruits of his understanding are faithful.€€ƒxNA wise man shall be filled with blessings, and they that see shall praise him.€€ƒxWThe life of a man is in the number of his days: but the days of Israel are innumerable.€€ƒxSA wise man shall inherit honour among his people, and his name shall live for ever.€€˜ƒxQFor all things are not expedient for all, and every kind pleaseth not every soul.€€ƒxFBe not greedy in any feasting, and pour not out thyself upon any meat:€€ƒxMFor in many meats there will be sickness, and greediness will turn to choler.€€ƒxOBy surfeiting many have perished, but he that is temperate, shall prolong life.€€ƒ`€€ƒxWHonour the physician for the need thou hast of him: for the most High hath created him.€€ƒxDFor all healing is from God, and he shall receive gifts of the king.€€ƒxeThe skill of the physician shall lift up his head, and in the sight of great men he shall be praised.€€ƒxZThe most High hath created medicines out of the earth, and a wise man will not abhor them.€€ƒx*Was not bitter water made sweet with wood?€€ƒx’The virtue of these things is come to the knowledge of men, and the most High hath given knowledge to men, that he may be honoured in his wonders.€€ƒx¼By these he shall cure and shall allay their pains, and of these the apothecary shall make sweet confections, and shall make up ointments of health, and of his works there shall be no end.€€ƒx7For the peace of God is over all the face of the earth.€€ƒxZMy son, in thy sickness neglect not thyself, but pray to the Lord, and he shall heal thee.€€ƒxVTurn away from sin and order thy hands aright, and cleanse thy heart from all offence.€€ƒxqGive a sweet savour, and a memorial of fine flour, and make a fat offering, and then give place to the physician.€€ƒxXFor the Lord created him: and let him not depart from thee, for his works are necessary.€€ƒx9For there is a time when thou must fall into their hands:€€ƒxrAnd they shall beseech the Lord, that he would prosper what they give for ease and remedy, for their conversation.€€ƒxVHe that sinneth in the sight of his Maker, shall fall into the hands of the physician.€€ƒx¦My son, shed tears over the dead, and begin to lament as if thou hadst suffered some great harm, and according to judgment cover his body, and neglect not his burial.€€ƒxeAnd for fear of being ill spoken of weep bitterly for a day, and then comfort thyself in thy sadness.€€ƒx[And make mourning for him according to his merit for a day, or two, for fear of detraction.€€ƒxpFor of sadness cometh death, and it overwhelmeth the strength, and the sorrow of the heart boweth down the neck.€€ƒx_In withdrawing aside sorrow remaineth: and the substance of the poor is according to his heart.€€ƒxVGive not up thy heart to sadness, but drive it from thee: and remember the latter end.€€ƒx`Forget it not: for there is no returning, and thou shalt do him no good, and shalt hurt thyself.€€ƒxXRemember my judgment: for thine also shall be so: yesterday for me, and to day for thee.€€‡ƒxjThe wisdom of a scribe cometh by his time of leisure: and he that is less in action, shall receive wisdom.€€ƒxÕWith what wisdom shall he be furnished that holdeth the plough, and that glorieth in the goad, that driveth the oxen therewith, and is occupied in their labours, and his whole talk is about the offspring of bulls?€€ƒxSHe shall give his mind to turn up furrows, and his care is to give the kine fodder.€€ƒxóSo every craftsman and workmaster that laboureth night and day, he who maketh graven seals, and by his continual diligence varieth the figure: he shall give his mind to the resemblance of the picture, and by his watching shall finish the work.€€ƒxSo doth the smith sitting by the anvil and considering the iron work. The vapour of the fire wasteth his flesh, and he fighteth with the heat of the furnace.€€ƒxgThe noise of the hammer is always in his ears, and his eye is upon the pattern of the vessel he maketh.€€ƒxVHe setteth his mind to finish his work, and his watching to polish them to perfection.€€˜/ƒxRHe fashioneth the clay with his arm, and boweth down his strength before his feet:€€ƒxYHe shall give his mind to finish the glazing, and his watching to make clean the furnace.€€ƒxEAll these trust to their hands, and every one is wise in his own art.€€ƒx"Without these a city is not built.€€ƒx]And they shall not dwell, nor walk about therein, and they shall not go up into the assembly.€€ƒxÍUpon the judges' seat they shall not sit, and the ordinance of judgment they shall not understand, neither shall they declare discipline and judgment, and they shall not be found where parables are spoken:€€ƒx§But they shall strengthen the state of the world, and their prayer shall be in the work of their craft, applying their soul, and searching in the law of the most High.€€ƒ`€€ƒx`The wise man will seek out the wisdom of all the ancients, and will be occupied in the prophets.€€ƒx`He will keep the sayings of renowned men, and will enter withal into the subtilties of parables.€€ƒxfHe will search out the hidden meanings of proverbs, and will be conversant in the secrets of parables.€€ƒx?He shall serve among great men, and appear before the governor.€€ƒxOHe shall pass into strange countries: for he shall try good and evil among men.€€ƒxqHe will give his heart to resort early to the Lord that made him, and he will pray in the sight of the most High.€€ƒxJHe will open his mouth in prayer, and will make supplication for his sins.€€ƒxYFor if it shall please the great Lord, he will fill him with the spirit of understanding:€€ƒxiAnd he will pour forth the words of his wisdom as showers, and in his prayer he will confess to the Lord.€€ƒxYAnd he shall direct his counsel, and his knowledge, and in his secrets shall he meditate.€€ƒxkHe shall shew forth the discipline he hath learned, and shall glory in the law of the covenant of the Lord.€€ƒx>Many shall praise his wisdom, and it shall never be forgotten.€€ƒxhThe memory of him shall not depart away, and his name shall be in request from generation to generation.€€ƒxMNations shall declare his wisdom, and the church shall shew forth his praise.€€ƒxeIf he continue, he shall leave a name above a thousand: and if he rest, it shall be to his advantage.€€ƒxQI will yet meditate that I may declare: for I am filled as with a holy transport.€€ƒxmBy a voice he saith: Hear me, ye divine offspring, and bud forth as the rose planted by the brooks of waters.€€ƒx&Give ye a sweet odour as frankincense.€€ƒxSend forth flowers, as the lily, and yield a smell, and bring forth leaves in grace, and praise with canticles, and bless the Lord in his works.€€ƒx®Magnify his name, and give glory to him with the voice of your lips, and with the canticles of your mouths, and with harps, and in praising him, you shall say in this manner:€€ƒx-All the works of the Lord are exceeding good.€€ƒx`At his word the waters stood as a heap: and at the words of his mouth the receptacles of waters:€€ƒxUFor at his commandment favour is shewn, and there is no diminishing of his salvation.€€ƒxNThe works of all flesh are before him, and there is nothing hid from his eyes.€€ƒxNHe seeth from eternity to eternity, and there is nothing wonderful before him.€€ƒx`There is no saying: What is this, or what is that? for all things shall be sought in their time.€€ƒx*His blessing hath overflowed like a river.€€ƒxnAnd as a flood hath watered the earth; so shall his wrath inherit the nations, that have not sought after him.€€ƒx®Even as he turned the waters into a dry land, and the earth was made dry: and his ways were made plain for their journey: so to sinners they are stumblingblocks in his wrath.€€ƒxbGood things were created for the good from the beginning, so for the wicked, good and evil things.€€ƒx®The principal things necessary for the life of men, are water, fire, and iron, salt, milk, and bread of flour, and honey, and the cluster of the grape, and oil, and clothing.€€ƒxqAll these things shall be for good to the holy, so to the sinners and the ungodly they shall be turned into evil.€€ƒxbThere are spirits that are created for vengeance, and in their fury they lay on grievous torments.€€ƒxsIn the time of destruction they shall pour out their force: and they shall appease the wrath of him that made them.€€ƒxDFire, hail, famine, and death, all these were created for vengeance.€€ƒxsThe teeth of beasts, and scorpions, and serpents, and the sword taking vengeance upon the ungodly unto destruction.€€ƒx–In his commandments they shall feast, and they shall be ready upon earth when need is, and when their time is come they shall not transgress his word.€€ƒxxTherefore from the beginning I was resolved, and I have meditated, and thought on these things and left them in writing,€€ƒxOAll the works of the Lord are good, and he will furnish every work in due time.€€˜'ƒx[Now therefore with the whole heart and mouth praise ye him, and bless the name of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÁGreat labour is created for all men, and a heavy yoke is upon the children of Adam from the day of their coming out of their mother's womb, until the day of their burial into the mother of all.€€ƒxfTheir thoughts, and fears of the heart, their imagination of things to come, and the day of their end:€€ƒxXFrom him that sitteth on a glorious throne, unto him that is humbled in earth and ashes:€€ƒx¹From him that weareth purple, and beareth the crown, even to him that is covered with rough linen: wrath, envy, trouble, unquietness, and the fear of death, continual anger, and strife,€€ƒxTAnd in the time of rest upon his bed, the sleep of the night changeth his knowledge.€€ƒx\A little and as nothing is his rest, and afterward in sleep, as in the day of keeping watch.€€ƒx He is troubled in the vision of his heart, as if he had escaped in the day of battle. In the time of his safety he rose up, and wondereth that there is no fear.€€ƒx]Such things happen to all flesh, from man even to beast, and upon sinners are sevenfold more.€€ƒxeMoreover, death, and bloodshed, strife, and sword, oppressions, famine, and affliction, and scourges:€€ƒxPAll these things are created for the wicked, and for their sakes came the flood.€€ƒxjAll things that are of the earth, shall return to the earth again, and all waters shall return to the sea.€€ƒxSAll bribery, and injustice shall be blotted out, and fidelity shall stand for ever.€€ƒxwThe riches of the unjust shall be dried up like a river, and shall pass away with a noise like a great thunder in rain.€€ƒxZWhile he openeth his hands he shall rejoice: but transgressors shall pine away in the end.€€ƒx{The offspring of the ungodly shall not bring forth many branches, and make a noise as unclean roots upon the top of a rock.€€ƒxeThe weed growing over every water, and at the bank of the river, shall be pulled up before all grass.€€ƒxDGrace is like a paradise in blessings, and mercy remaineth for ever.€€ƒxnThe life of a laborer that is content with what he hath, shall be sweet, and in it thou shalt find a treasure.€€ƒxsChildren, and the building of a city shall establish a name, but a blameless wife shall be counted above them both.€€ƒxLWine and music rejoice the heart, but the love of wisdom is above them both.€€ƒxYThe flute and the psaltery make a sweet melody, but a pleasant tongue is above them both.€€ƒxJThy eye desireth favour and beauty, but more than these green sown fields.€€ƒxbA friend and companion meeting together in season, but above them both is a wife with her husband.€€ƒxSBrethren are a help in the time of trouble, but mercy shall deliver more than they.€€ƒxNGold and silver make the feet stand sure: but wise counsel is above them both.€€ƒxORiches and strength lift up the heart: but above these is the fear of the Lord.€€ƒxNThere is no want in the fear of the Lord, and it needeth not to seek for help.€€ƒx^The fear of the Lord is like a paradise of blessing, and they have covered it above all glory.€€ƒxNMy son, in thy lifetime be not indigent: for it is better to die than to want.€€ƒx…The life of him that looketh toward another man's table is not to be counted a life: for he feedeth his soul with another man's meat.€€ƒx<But a man, well instructed and taught, will look to himself.€€ƒx[Begging will be sweet in the mouth of the unwise, but in his belly there shall burn a fire.€€ƒ`€€ƒx[O death, how bitter is the remembrance of thee to a man that hath peace in his possessions!€€ƒxiTo a man that is at rest, and whose ways are prosperous in all things, and that is yet able to take meat!€€ƒx^O death thy sentence is welcome to the man that is in need, and to him whose strength faileth:€€ƒxlWho is in a decrepit age, and that is in care about all things, and to the distrustful that loseth patience!€€˜.ƒxtAnd what shall come upon thee by the good pleasure of the most High? whether ten, or a hundred, or a thousand years.€€ƒx0For among the dead there is no accusing of life.€€ƒxoThe children of sinners become children of abominations, and they that converse near the houses of the ungodly.€€ƒxpThe inheritance of the children of sinners shall perish, and with their posterity shall be a perpetual reproach.€€ƒx[The children will complain of an ungodly father, because for his sake they are in reproach.€€ƒxIWoe to you, ungodly men, who have forsaken the law of the most high Lord.€€ƒxkAnd if you be born, you shall be born in malediction: and if you die, in malediction shall be your portion.€€ƒxtAll things that are of the earth, shall return into the earth: so the ungodly shall from malediction to destruction.€€ƒxZThe mourning of men is about their body, but the name of the ungodly shall be blotted out.€€ƒxoTake care of a good name: for this shall continue with thee, more than a thousand treasures precious and great.€€ƒxMA good life hath its number of days: but a good name shall continue for ever.€€ƒx‚My children, keep discipline in peace: for wisdom that is hid, and a treasure that is not seen, what profit is there in them both?€€ƒxMBetter is the man that hideth his folly, than the man that hideth his wisdom.€€ƒxBWherefore have a shame of these things I am now going to speak of.€€ƒx_For it is not good to keep all shamefacedness: and all things do not please all men in opinion.€€ƒxfBe ashamed of fornication before father and mother: and of a lie before a governor and a man in power:€€ƒx[Of an offence before a prince, and a judge: of iniquity before a congregation and a people:€€ƒx[Of injustice before a companion and friend: and in regard to the place where thou dwellest,€€ƒx}Of theft, and of the truth of God, and the covenant: of leaning with thy elbow over meat, and of deceit in giving and taking:€€ƒxqOf silence before them that salute thee: of looking upon a harlot: and of turning away thy face from thy kinsman.€€ƒxZTurn not away thy face from thy neighbour, and of taking away a portion and not restoring.€€ƒxfGaze not upon another man's wife, and be not inquisitive after his handmaid, and approach not her bed.€€ƒxYBe ashamed of upbraiding speeches before friends: and after thou hast given, upbraid not.€€ƒ`€€ƒxñRepeat not the word which thou hast heard, and disclose not the thing that is secret; so shalt thou be truly without confusion, and shalt find favour before all men: be not ashamed of any of these things, and accept no person to sin thereby:€€ƒxYOf the law of the most High, and of his covenant, and of judgment to justify the ungodly:€€ƒxZOf the affair of companions and travellers, and of the gift of the inheritance of friends:€€ƒx?Of exactness of balance and weights, of getting much or little:€€ƒxƒOf the corruption of buying, and of merchants, and of much correction of children, and to make the side of a wicked slave to bleed.€€ƒx(Sure keeping is good over a wicked wife.€€ƒxWhere there are many hands, shut up, and deliver all things in number, and weight: and put all in writing that thou givest out or receivest in.€€ƒx¿Be not ashamed to inform the unwise and foolish, and the aged, that are judged by young men: and thou shalt be well instructed in all things, and well approved in the sight of all men living.€€ƒxÕThe father waketh for the daughter when no man knoweth, and the care for her taketh away his sleep, when she is young, lest she pass away the flower of her age, and when she is married, lest she should be hateful:€€ƒx¶In her virginity, lest she should be corrupted, and be found with child in her father's house: and having a husband, lest she should misbehave herself, or at the least become barren.€€ƒxàKeep a sure watch over a shameless daughter: lest at anytime she make thee become a laughingstock to thy enemies, and a byword in the city, and a reproach among the people, and she make thee ashamed before all the multitude.€€ƒx9Behold not everybody's beauty: and tarry not among women.€€ƒxHFor from garments cometh a moth, and from a woman the iniquity of a man.€€ƒxmFor better is the iniquity of a man, than a woman doing a good turn, and a woman bringing shame and reproach.€€ƒx}I will now remember the works of the Lord, and I will declare the things I have seen. By the words of the Lord are his works.€€ƒx`The sun giving light hath looked upon all things, and full of the glory of the Lord is his work.€€ƒx’Hath not the Lord made the saints to declare all his wonderful works, which the Lord Almighty hath firmly settled to be established for his glory?€€ƒxYHe hath searched out the deep, and the heart of men: and considered their crafty devices.€€ƒx¾For the Lord knoweth all knowledge, and hath beheld the signs of the world, he declareth the things that are past, and the things that are to come, and revealeth the traces of hidden things.€€ƒx>No thought escapeth him, and no word can hide itself from him.€€ƒxzHe hath beautified the glorious works of his wisdom: and he is from eternity to eternity, and to him nothing may be added,€€ƒx@Nor can he be diminished, and he hath no need of any counsellor.€€˜@ƒxRAll these things live, and remain for ever, and for every use all things obey him.€€ƒxOAll things are double, one against another, and he hath made nothing defective.€€ƒxcHe hath established the good things of every one. And who shall be filled with beholding his glory?€€ƒ`€€ƒxQThe firmament on high is his beauty, the beauty of heaven with its glorious shew.€€ƒxjThe sun when he appeareth shewing forth at his rising, an admirable instrument, the work of the most High.€€ƒxpAt noon he burneth the earth, and who can abide his burning heat? As one keeping a furnace in the works of heat:€€ƒx‚The sun three times as much, burneth the mountains, breathing out fiery vapours, and shining with his beams, he blindeth the eyes.€€ƒxNGreat is the Lord that made him, and at his words he hath hastened his course.€€ƒxYAnd the moon in all in her season, is for a declaration of times and a sign of the world.€€ƒxYFrom the moon is the sign of the festival day, a light that decreaseth in her perfection.€€ƒxMThe month is called after her name, increasing wonderfully in her perfection.€€ƒxYBeing an instrument of the armies on high, shining gloriously in the firmament of heaven.€€ƒxXThe glory of the stars is the beauty of heaven; the Lord enlighteneth the world on high.€€ƒx[By the words of the holy one they stand in judgment, and shall never fall in their watches.€€ƒxZLook upon the rainbow, and bless him that made it: it is very beautiful in its brightness.€€ƒxlIt encompasseth the heaven about with the circle of its glory, the hands of the most High have displayed it.€€ƒxnBy his commandment he maketh the snow to fall apace, and sendeth forth swiftly the lightnings of his judgment.€€ƒxIThrough this are the treasures opened, and the clouds fly out like birds.€€ƒxIBy his greatness he hath fixed the clouds, and the hailstones are broken.€€ƒxVAt his sight shall the mountains be shaken, and at his will the south wind shall blow.€€ƒx_The noise of his thunder shall strike the earth, so doth the northern storm, and the whirlwind:€€ƒxxAnd as the birds lighting upon the earth, he scattereth snow, and the falling thereof, is as the coming down of locusts.€€ƒxkThe eye admireth at the beauty of the whiteness thereof, and the heart is astonished at the shower thereof.€€ƒxlHe shall pour frost as salt upon the earth: and when it freezeth, it shall become like the tops of thistles.€€ƒxªThe cold north wind bloweth, and the water is congealed into crystal; upon every gathering together of waters it shall rest, and shall clothe the waters as a breastplate.€€ƒxgAnd it shall devour the mountains, and burn the wilderness, and consume all that is green as with fire.€€ƒx€A present remedy of all is the speedy coming of a cloud, and a dew that meeteth it, by the heat that cometh, shall overpower it.€€ƒxuAt his word the wind is still, and with his thought he appeaseth the deep, and the Lord hath planted islands therein.€€ƒxiLet them that sail on the sea, tell the dangers thereof: and when we hear with our ears, we shall admire.€€ƒxzThere are great and wonderful works: a variety of beasts, and of all living things, and the monstrous creatures of whales.€€ƒx^Through him is established the end of their journey, and by his word all things are regulated.€€ƒxTWe shall say much, and yet shall want words: but the sum of our words is, He is all.€€ƒx\What shall we be able to do to glorify him? for the Almighty himself is above all his works.€€ƒxFThe Lord is terrible, and exceeding great, and his power is admirable.€€ƒxhGlorify the Lord as much as ever you can, for he will yet far exceed, and his magnificence is wonderful.€€ƒxLBlessing the Lord, exalt him as much as you can; for he is above all praise.€€ƒxbWhen you exalt him put forth all your strength, and be not weary: for you can never go far enough.€€ƒxZWho shall see him, and declare him? and who shall magnify him as he is from the beginning?€€ƒxjThere are many things hidden from us that are greater than these: for we have seen but a few of his works.€€ƒxIBut the Lord hath made all things, and to the godly he hath given wisdom.€€ƒ`€€ƒxDLet us now praise men of renown and our fathers in their generation.€€ƒxNThe Lord hath wrought great glory through his magnificence from the beginning.€€ƒx”Such as have borne rule in their dominions, men of great power, and endued with their wisdom, shewing forth in the prophets the dignity of prophets,€€ƒxlAnd ruling over the present people, and by the strength of wisdom instructing the people in most holy words.€€ƒx[Such as by their skill sought out musical tunes, and published canticles of the scriptures.€€ƒxLRich men in virtue, studying beautifulness: living at peace in their houses.€€ƒxQAll these have gained glory in their generations, and were praised in their days.€€ƒx^They that were born of them have left a name behind them, that their praises might be related:€€ƒx«And there are some, of whom there is no memorial: who are perished, as if they had never been: and are become as if they had never been born, and their children with them.€€ƒx?But these were men of mercy, whose godly deeds have not failed:€€ƒx%Good things continue with their seed,€€ƒxSTheir posterity are a holy inheritance, and their seed hath stood in the covenants.€€ƒxeAnd their children for their sakes remain for ever: their seed and their glory shall not be forsaken.€€ƒxWTheir bodies are buried in peace, and their name liveth unto generation and generation.€€ƒxLLet the people shew forth their wisdom, and the church declare their praise.€€ƒxaHenoch pleased God, and was translated into paradise, that he may give repentance to the nations.€€ƒxSNoe was found perfect, just, and in the time of wrath he was made a reconciliation.€€ƒxETherefore was there a remnant left to the earth, when the flood came.€€ƒxiThe covenants of the world were made with him, that all flesh should no more be destroyed with the flood.€€ƒxªAbraham was the great father of a multitude of nations, and there was not found the like to him in glory, who kept the law of the most High, and was in covenant with him.€€ƒxRIn his flesh he established the covenant, and in temptation he was found faithful.€€ƒxjTherefore by an oath he gave him glory in his posterity, that he should increase as the dust of the earth,€€˜"ƒxHAnd he did in like manner with Isaac for the sake of Abraham his father.€€ƒxaThe Lord gave him the blessing of all nations, and confirmed his covenant upon the head of Jacob.€€ƒxpHe acknowledged him in his blessings, and gave him an inheritance, and divided him his portion in twelve tribes.€€ƒxQAnd he preserved for him men of mercy, that found grace in the eyes of all flesh.€€ƒ`€€ƒxBMoses was beloved of God, and men: whose memory is in benediction.€€ƒx‚He made him like the saints in glory, and magnified him in the fear of his enemies, and with his words he made prodigies to cease.€€ƒxwHe glorified him in the sight of kings, and gave him commandments in the sight of his people, and shewed him his glory.€€ƒxMHe sanctified him in his faith, and meekness, and chose him out of all flesh.€€ƒx>For he heard him, and his voice, and brought him into a cloud.€€ƒx’And he gave him commandments before his face, and a law of life and instruction, that he might teach Jacob his covenant, and Israel his judgments.€€ƒxGHe exalted Aaron his brother, and like to himself of the tribe of Levi:€€ƒxsHe made an everlasting covenant with him, and gave him the priesthood of the nation, and made him blessed in glory,€€ƒx{And he girded him about with a glorious girdle, and clothed him with a robe of glory, and crowned him with majestic attire.€€ƒx‡He put upon him a garment to the feet, and breeches, and an ephod, and he compassed him with many little bells of gold all round about,€€ƒx‰That as he went there might be a sound, and a noise made that might be heard in the temple, for a memorial to the children of his people.€€ƒxrHe gave him a holy robe of gold, and blue, and purple, a woven work of a wise man, endued with judgment and truth:€€ƒx¹Of twisted scarlet the work of an artist, with precious stones cut and set in gold, and graven by the work of a lapidary for a memorial, according to the number of the tribes of Israel.€€ƒx”And a crown of gold upon his mitre wherein was engraved Holiness, an ornament of honour: a work of power, and delightful to the eyes for its beauty.€€ƒxABefore him there were none so beautiful, even from the beginning.€€ƒxdNo stranger was ever clothed with them, but only his children alone, and his grandchildren for ever.€€ƒx1His sacrifices were consumed with fire every day.€€ƒx6Moses filled his hands and anointed him with holy oil.€€ƒx½This was made to him for an everlasting testament, and to his seed as the days of heaven, to execute the office of the priesthood, and to have praise, and to glorify his people in his name.€€ƒxHe chose him out of all men living, to offer sacrifice to God, incense, and a good savour, for a memorial to make reconciliation for his people:€€ƒxAnd he gave him power in his commandments, in the covenants of his judgments, that he should teach Jacob his testimonies, and give light to Israel in his law.€€ƒx²And strangers stood up against him, and through envy the men that were with Dathan and Abiron, compassed him about in the wilderness, and the congregation of Core in their wrath.€€ƒx\The Lord God saw and it pleased him not, and they were consumed in his wrathful indignation.€€ƒxEHe wrought wonders upon them, and consumed them with a flame of fire.€€ƒx|And he added glory to Aaron, and gave him an inheritance, and divided unto him the firstfruits of the increase of the earth.€€ƒx’He prepared them bread in the first place unto fulness: for the sacrifices also of the Lord they shall eat, which he gave to him, and to his seed.€€ƒxBut he shall not inherit among the people in the land, and he hath no portion among the people: for he himself is his portion and inheritance.€€ƒx[Phinees the son of Eleazar is the third in glory, by imitating him in the fear of the Lord:€€ƒxyAnd he stood up in the shameful fall of the people: in the goodness and readiness of his soul he appeased God for Israel.€€cLam¤dnamelLamentationsiosis_namecLamlabbreviationcLamhchapters…–ƒ`€€ƒx}Valiant in war was Jesus the son of Nave, who was successor of Moses among the prophets, who was great according to his name,€€ƒxVery great for the saving the elect of God, to overthrow the enemies that rose up against them, that he might get the inheritance for Israel.€€ƒxeHow great glory did he gain when he lifted up his hands, and stretched out swords against the cities?€€ƒxJWho before him hath so resisted? for the Lord himself brought the enemies.€€ƒx>Was not the sun stopped in his anger, and one day made as two?€€ƒx¡He called upon the most high Sovereign when the enemies assaulted him on every side, and the great and holy God heard him by hailstones of exceeding great force.€€ƒxmHe made a violent assault against the nation of his enemies, and in the descent he destroyed the adversaries.€€ƒxpThat the nations might know his power, that it is not easy to fight against God. And he followed the mighty one:€€ƒxºAnd in the days of Moses he did a work of mercy, he and Caleb the son of Jephone, in standing against the enemy, and withholding the people from sins, and appeasing the wicked murmuring.€€ƒxÏAnd they two being appointed, were delivered out of the danger from among the number of six hundred thousand men on foot, to bring them into their inheritance, into the land that floweth with milk and honey.€€ƒx¹And the Lord gave strength also to Caleb, and his strength continued even to his old age, so that he went up to the high places of the land, and his seed obtained it for an inheritance:€€ƒxPThat all the children of Israel might see, that it is good to obey the holy God.€€ƒxiThen all the judges, every one by name, whose heart was not corrupted: who turned not away from the Lord,€€ƒxQThat their memory might be blessed, and their bones spring up out of their place,€€ƒxZAnd their name continue for ever, the glory of the holy men remaining unto their children.€€ƒx„Samuel the prophet of the Lord, the beloved of the Lord his God, established a new government, and anointed princes over his people.€€ƒx|By the law of the Lord he judged the congregation, and the God of Jacob beheld, and by his fidelity he was proved a prophet.€€ƒxNAnd he was known to be faithful in his words, because he saw the God of light:€€ƒx“And called upon the name of the Lord Almighty, in fighting against the enemies who beset him on every side, when he offered a lamb without blemish.€€ƒxVAnd the Lord thundered from heaven, and with a great noise made his voice to be heard.€€ƒxPAnd he crushed the princes of the Tyrians, and all the lords of the Philistines:€€–ƒx»And after this he slept, and he made known to the king, and shewed him the end of his life, and he lifted up his voice from the earth in prophecy to blot out the wickedness of the nation.€€ƒ`€€ƒx3Then Nathan the prophet arose in the days of David.€€ƒx`And as the fat taken away from the flesh, so was David chosen from among the children of Israel.€€ƒxvHe played with lions as with lambs: and with bears he did in like manner as with the lambs of the flock, in his youth.€€ƒxBDid not he kill the giant, and take away reproach from his people?€€ƒxYIn lifting up his hand, with the stone in the sling he beat down the boasting of Goliath:€€ƒx˜For he called upon the Lord the Almighty, and he gave strength in his right hand, to take away the mighty warrior, and to set up the horn of his nation.€€ƒxySo in ten thousand did he glorify him, and praised him in the blessings of the Lord, in offering to him a crown of glory:€€ƒx‡For he destroyed the enemies on every side, and extirpated the Philistines the adversaries unto this day: he broke their horn for ever.€€ƒx[In all his works he gave thanks to the holy one, and to the most High, with words of glory.€€ƒxqWith his whole heart he praised the Lord, and loved God that made him: and he gave him power against his enemies:€€ƒxNAnd he set singers before the altar, and by their voices he made sweet melody.€€ƒxÇAnd to the festivals he added beauty, and set in order the solemn times even to the end of his life, that they should praise the holy name of the Lord, and magnify the holiness of God in the morning.€€ƒx‡The Lord took away his sins, and exalted his horn for ever: and he gave him a covenant of the kingdom, and a throne of glory in Israel.€€ƒxZAfter him arose up a wise son, and for his sake he cast down all the power of the enemies.€€ƒxºSolomon reigned in days of peace, and God brought all his enemies under him, that he might build a house in his name, and prepare a sanctuary for ever: O how wise wast thou in thy youth!€€ƒxLAnd thou wast filled as a river with wisdom, and thy soul covered the earth.€€ƒx}And thou didst multiply riddles in parables: thy name went abroad to the islands far off, and thou wast beloved in thy peace.€€ƒxbThe countries wondered at thee for thy canticles, and proverbs, and parables, and interpretations,€€ƒxAAnd at the name of the Lord God, whose surname is, God of Israel.€€ƒxDThou didst gather gold as copper, and didst multiply silver as lead,€€˜BƒxxThou hast stained thy glory, and defiled thy seed so as to bring wrath upon thy children, and to have thy folly kindled,€€ƒxcThat thou shouldst make the kingdom to be divided, and out of Ephraim a rebellious kingdom to rule.€€ƒxáBut God will not leave off his mercy, and he will not destroy, nor abolish his own works, neither will he cut up by the roots the offspring of his elect: and he will not utterly take away the seed of him that loveth the Lord.€€ƒxEWherefore he gave a remnant to Jacob, and to David of the same stock.€€ƒx(And Solomon had an end with his fathers.€€ƒx<And he left behind him of his seed, the folly of the nation,€€ƒxSEven Roboam that had little wisdom, who turned away the people through his counsel:€€ƒx‡And Jeroboam the son of Nabat, who caused Israel to sin, and shewed Ephraim the way of sin, and their sins were multiplied exceedingly.€€ƒx+They removed them far away from their land.€€ƒxdAnd they sought out all iniquities, till vengeance came upon them, and put an end to all their sins.€€ƒ`€€ƒxKAnd Elias the prophet stood up, as a fire, and his word burnt like a torch.€€ƒx He brought a famine upon them, and they that provoked him in their envy, were reduced to a small number, for they could not endure the commandments of the Lord.€€ƒx[By the word of the Lord he shut up the heaven, and he brought down fire from heaven thrice.€€ƒxOThus was Elias magnified in his wondrous works. And who can glory like to thee?€€ƒxZWho raisedst up a dead man from below, from the lot of death, by the word of the Lord God.€€ƒxtWho broughtest down kings to destruction, and brokest easily their power in pieces, and the glorious from their bed.€€ƒxGWho heardest judgment in Sina, and in Horeb the judgments of vengeance.€€ƒxKWho anointedst kings to penance, and madest prophets successors after thee.€€ƒxGWho wast taken up in a whirlwind of fire, in a chariot of fiery horses.€€ƒx£Who art registered in the judgments of times to appease the wrath of the Lord, to reconcile the heart of the father to the son, and to restore the tribes of Jacob.€€ƒxFBlessed are they that saw thee, and were honoured with thy friendship.€€ƒxIFor we live only in our life, but after death our name shall not be such.€€ƒx¡Elias was indeed covered with the whirlwind, and his spirit was filled up in Eliseus: in his days he feared not the prince, and no man was more powerful than he.€€ƒx@No word could overcome him, and after death his body prophesied.€€ƒxCIn his life he did great wonders, and in death he wrought miracles.€€ƒxFor all this the people repented not, neither did they depart from their sins till they were cast out of their land, and were scattered through all the earth.€€ƒxJAnd there was left but a small people, and a prince in the house of David.€€ƒxISome of these did that which pleased God: but others committed many sins.€€ƒx„Ezechias fortified his city, and brought in water into the midst thereof, and he digged a rock with iron, and made a well for water.€€ƒx¨In his days Sennacherib came up, and sent Rabsaces, and lifted up his hand against them, and he stretched out his hand against Sion, and became proud through his power.€€ƒxPThen their hearts and hands trembled, and they were in pain as women in travail.€€ƒx™And they called upon the Lord who is merciful, and spreading their hands, they lifted them up to heaven: and the holy Lord God quickly heard their voice.€€ƒxHe was not mindful of their sins, neither did he deliver them up to their enemies, but he purified them by the hand of Isaias, the holy prophet.€€ƒxQHe overthrew the army of the Assyrians, and the angel of the Lord destroyed them.€€ƒx³For Ezechias did that which pleased God, and walked valiantly in the way of David his father, which Isaias, the great prophet, and faithful in the sight of God, had commanded him.€€ƒxDIn his days the sun wen backward, and he lengthened the king's life.€€ƒxkWith a great spirit he saw the things that are to come to pass at last, and comforted the mourners in Sion.€€ƒxPHe showed what should come to pass for ever, and secret things before they came.€€ƒ`€€ƒx\The memory of Josias is like the composition of a sweet smell made by the art of a perfumer:€€ƒxZHis remembrance shall be sweet as honey in every mouth, and as music at a banquet of wine.€€ƒxjHe was directed by God unto the repentance of the nation, and he took away the abominations of wickedness.€€ƒxaAnd he directed his heart towards the Lord, and in the days of sinners he strengthened godliness.€€ƒx9Except David, and Ezechias and Josias, all committed sin.€€ƒxUFor the kings of Juda forsook the law of the most High, and despised the fear of God.€€ƒxJSo they gave their kingdom to others, and their glory to a strange nation,€€ƒxvThey burnt the chosen city of holiness, and made the streets thereof desolate according to the prediction of Jeremias.€€ƒx˜For they treated him evil, who was consecrated a prophet from his mother's womb, to overthrow, and pluck up, and destroy, and to build again, and renew.€€ƒx`It was Ezechiel that saw the glorious vision, which was shewn him upon the chariot of cherubims.€€ƒxnFor he made mention of the enemies under the figure of rain, and of doing good to them that shewed right ways.€€ƒxŒAnd may the bones of the twelve prophets spring up out of their place: for they strengthened Jacob, and redeemed themselves by strong faith.€€ƒxIHow shall we magnify Zorobabel? for he was as a signet on the right hand;€€ƒxIn like manner Jesus the son of Josedec who in their days built the house, and set up a holy temple to the Lord, prepared for everlasting glory.€€ƒxšAnd let Nehemias be a long time remembered, who raised up for us our walls that were cast down, and set up the gates and the bars, who rebuilt our houses.€€ƒxPNo man was born upon earth like Henoch: for he also was taken up from the earth.€€ƒx‡Nor as Joseph, who was a man born prince of his brethren, the support of his family, the ruler of his brethren, the stay of the people:€€ƒx<And his bones were visited, and after death they prophesied.€€ƒxRSeth and Sem obtained glory among men: and above every soul Adam in the beginning,€€ƒ`€€ƒxtSimon the high priest, the son of Onias, who in his life propped up the house, and in his days fortified the temple.€€ƒxgBy him also the height of the temple was founded, the double building and the high walls of the temple.€€ƒxYIn his days the wells of water flowed out, and they were filled as the sea above measure.€€ƒx>He took care of his nation, and delivered it from destruction.€€ƒxHe prevailed to enlarge the city, and obtained glory in his conversation with the people: and enlarged the entrance of the house and the court.€€ƒx^He shone in his days as the morning star in the midst of a cloud, and as the moon at the full.€€–ƒxÛAnd as the rainbow giving light in bright clouds, and as the flower of roses in the days of the spring, and as the lilies that are on the brink of the water, and as the sweet smelling frankincense in the time of summer.€€ƒx7As a bright fire, and frankincense burning in the fire.€€ƒx=As a massy vessel of gold, adorned with every precious stone.€€ƒxšAs an olive tree budding forth, and a cypress tree rearing itself on high, when he put on the robe of glory, and was clothed with the perfection of power.€€ƒxGWhen he went up to the holy altar, he honoured the vesture of holiness.€€ƒx´And when he took the portions out of the hands of the priests, he himself stood by the altar. And about him was the ring of his brethren: and as the cedar planted in mount Libanus,€€ƒxdAnd as branches of palm trees, they stood round about him, and all the sons of Aaron in their glory.€€ƒx®And the oblation of the Lord was in their hands, before all the congregation of Israel: and finishing his service, on the altar, to honour the offering of the most high King,€€ƒxVHe stretched forth his hand to make a libation, and offered of the blood of the grape.€€ƒxNHe poured out at the foot of the altar a divine odour to the most high Prince.€€ƒxƒThen the sons of Aaron shouted, they sounded with beaten trumpets, and made a great noise to be heard for a remembrance before God.€€ƒx¡Then all the people together made haste, and fell down to the earth upon their faces, to adore the Lord their God, and to pray to the Almighty God the most High.€€ƒxgAnd the singers lifted up their voices, and in the great house the sound of sweet melody was increased.€€ƒxŠAnd the people in prayer besought the Lord the most High, until the worship of the Lord was perfected, and they had finished their office.€€ƒx›Then coming down, he lifted up his hands over all the congregation of the children of Israel, to give glory to God with his lips, and to glory in his name:€€ƒx=And he repeated his prayer, willing to shew the power of God.€€ƒx±And now pray ye to the God of all, who hath done great things in all the earth, who hath increased our days from our mother's womb, and hath done with us according to his mercy.€€ƒx\May he grant us joyfulness of heart, and that there be peace in our days in Israel for ever:€€ƒxTThat Israel may believe that the mercy of God is with us, to deliver us in his days.€€ƒxXThere are two nations which my soul abhorreth: and the third is no nation: which I hate:€€ƒx^They that sit on mount Seir, and the Philistines, and the foolish people that dwell in Sichem.€€ƒx‹Jesus the son of Sirach, of Jerusalem, hath written in this book the doctrine of wisdom and instruction, who renewed wisdom from his heart.€€–ƒxcFor if he do them, he shall be strong to do all things: because the light of God guideth his steps.€€ƒ`€€ƒxyA prayer of Jesus the son of Sirach. I will give glory to thee, O Lord, O King, and I will praise thee, O God my Saviour.€€ƒxOI will give glory to thy name: for thou hast been a helper and protector to me.€€ƒx½And hast preserved my body from destruction, from the snare of an unjust tongue, and from the lips of them that forge lies, and in the sight of them that stood by, thou hast been my helper.€€ƒx}And thou hast delivered me, according to the multitude of the mercy of thy name, from them that did roar, prepared to devour.€€ƒxjOut of the hands of them that sought my life, and from the gates of afflictions, which compassed me about:€€ƒxcFrom the oppression of the flame which surrounded me, and in the midst of the fire I was not burnt.€€ƒx‰From the depth of the belly of hell, and from an unclean tongue, and from lying words, from an unjust king, and from a slanderous tongue:€€ƒx,My soul shall praise the Lord even to death.€€ƒx-And my life was drawing near to hell beneath.€€ƒx~They compassed me on every side, and there was no one that would help me. I looked for the succour of men, and there was none.€€ƒxYI remembered thy mercy, O Lord, and thy works, which are from the beginning of the world.€€ƒxeHow thou deliverest them that wait for thee, O Lord, and savest them out of the hands of the nations.€€ƒx\Thou hast exalted my dwelling place upon the earth and I have prayed for death to pass away.€€ƒxI called upon the Lord, the father of my Lord, that he would not leave me in the day of my trouble, and in the time of the proud without help.€€ƒxbI will praise thy name continually, and will praise it with thanksgiving, and my prayer was heard.€€ƒxRAnd thou hast saved me from destruction, and hast delivered me from the evil time.€€ƒxNTherefore I will give thanks, and praise thee, and bless the name of the Lord.€€ƒxWWhen I was yet young, before I wandered about, I sought for wisdom openly in my prayer.€€ƒxyI prayed for her before the temple, and unto the very end I will seek after her, and she flourished as a grape soon ripe.€€ƒx`My heart delighted in her, my foot walked in the right way, from my youth up I sought after her.€€dEzek¤dnamegEzekieliosis_namedEzeklabbreviationdEzekhchapters˜0˜ƒx0To him that giveth me wisdom, will I give glory.€€ƒx]For I have determined to follow her: I have had a zeal for good, and shall not be confounded.€€ƒxEMy soul hath wrestled for her, and in doing it I have been confirmed.€€ƒxGI stretched forth my hands on high, and I bewailed my ignorance of her.€€ƒx8I directed my soul to her, and in knowledge I found her.€€ƒxTI possessed my heart with her from the beginning: therefore I shall not be forsaken.€€ƒxVMy entrails were troubled in seeking her: therefore shall I possess a good possession.€€ƒxMThe Lord hath given me a tongue for my reward: and with it I will praise him.€€ƒx[Draw near to me, ye unlearned, and gather yourselves together into the hours of discipline.€€ƒxVWhy are ye slow and what do you say of these things? your souls are exceeding thirsty.€€ƒxOI have opened my mouth, and have spoken: buy her for yourselves without silver,€€ƒxlAnd submit your neck to the yoke, and let your soul receive discipline: for she is near at hand to be found.€€ƒxWBehold with your eyes how I have laboured a little, and have found much rest to myself.€€ƒxTReceive ye discipline as a great sum of money, and possess abundance of gold by her.€€ƒxQLet your soul rejoice in his mercy and you shall not be confounded in his praise.€€ƒxMWork your work before the time, and he will give you your reward in his time.€€ƒvThe Prophecy of Isaiah€€ƒ`€€ƒx“The vision of Isaiah the Son of Amos, which he saw concerning Juda and Jerusalem in the days of Ozias, Joathan, Achaz, and Ezechias, kings of Juda.€€ƒxHear, O ye heavens, and give ear, O earth, for the Lord hath spoken. I have brought up children, and exalted them: but they have despised me.€€ƒxyThe ox knoweth his owner, and the ass his master's crib: but Israel hath not known me, and my people hath not understood.€€ƒxÃWoe to the sinful nation, a people laden with iniquity, a wicked seed, ungracious children: they have forsaken the Lord, they have blasphemed the Holy One of Israel, they are gone away backwards.€€ƒxzFor what shall I strike you any more, you that increase transgression? the whole head is sick, and the whole heart is sad.€€ƒx´From the sole of the foot unto the top of the head, there is no soundness therein: wounds and bruises and swelling sores: they are not bound up, nor dressed, nor fomented with oil.€€ƒx›Your land is desolate, your cities are burnt with fire: your country strangers devour before your face, and it shall be desolate as when wasted by enemies.€€ƒxŒAnd the daughter of Sion shall be left as a covert in a vineyard, and as a lodge in a garden of cucumbers, and as a city that is laid waste.€€ƒxjExcept the Lord of hosts had left us seed, we had been as Sodom, and we should have been like to Gomorrha.€€ƒxeHear the word of the Lord, ye rulers of Sodom, give ear to the law of our God, ye people of Gomorrha.€€ŠƒxnWhen you came to appear before me, who required these things at your hands, that you should walk in my courts?€€ƒx£Offer sacrifice no more in vain: incense is an abomination to me. The new moons, and the sabbaths and other festivals I will not abide, your assemblies are wicked.€€ƒxsMy soul hateth your new moons, and your solemnities: they are become troublesome to me, I am weary of bearing them.€€ƒxšAnd when you stretch forth your hands, I will turn away my eyes from you: and when you multiply prayer, I will not hear: for your hands are full of blood.€€ƒxcWash yourselves, be clean, take away the evil of your devices from my eyes, cease to do perversely,€€ƒxcLearn to do well: seek judgment, relieve the oppressed, judge for the fatherless, defend the widow.€€ƒxªAnd then come, and accuse me, saith the Lord: if your sins be as scarlet, they shall be made as white as snow: and if they be red as crimson, they shall be white as wool.€€ƒxUIf you be willing, and will hearken to me, you shall eat the good things of the land.€€ƒx{But if you will not, and will provoke me to wrath: the sword shall devour you because the mouth of the Lord hath spoken it.€€ƒxmHow is the faithful city, that was full of judgment, become a harlot? justice dwelt in it, but now murderers.€€˜ƒx¯Thy princes are faithless, companions of thieves: they all love bribes, they run after rewards. They judge not for the fatherless: and the widow's cause cometh not in to them.€€ƒx™Therefore saith the Lord the God of hosts, the mighty one of Israel: Ah! I will comfort myself over my adversaries: and I will be revenged of my enemies.€€ƒxiAnd I will turn my hand to thee, and I will clean purge away thy dross, and I will take away all thy tin.€€ƒx˜And I will restore thy judges as they were before, and thy counsellors as of old. After this thou shalt be called the city of the just, a faithful city.€€ƒxMSion shall be redeemed in judgment, and they shall bring her back in justice.€€ƒxsAnd he shall destroy the wicked, and the sinners together: and they that have forsaken the Lord, shall be consumed.€€ƒx‰For they shall be confounded for the idols, to which they have sacrificed: and you shall be ashamed of the gardens which you have chosen.€€ƒxWWhen you shall be as an oak with the leaves falling off, and as a garden without water.€€ƒxAnd your strength shall be as the ashes of tow, and your work as a spark: and both shall burn together, and there shall be none to quench it.€€ƒ`€€ƒxHThe word that Isaiah the son of Amos saw, concerning Juda and Jerusalem.€€ƒx²And in the last days the mountain of the house of the Lord shall be prepared on the top of mountains, and it shall be exalted above the hills, and all nations shall flow unto it.€€ƒy
And many people shall go, and say: Come and let us go up to the mountain of the Lord, and to the house of the God of Jacob, and he will teach us his ways, and we will walk in his paths: for the law shall come forth from Sion, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem.€€ƒxëAnd he shall judge the Gentiles, and rebuke many people: and they shall turn their swords into ploughshares, and their spears into sickles: nation shall not lift up sword against nation, neither shall they be exercised any more to war.€€ƒxDO house of Jacob, come ye, and let us walk in the light of the Lord.€€ƒx³For thou hast cast off thy people, the house of Jacob: because they are filled as in times past, and have had soothsayers as the Philistines, and have adhered to strange children.€€ƒxRTheir land is filled with silver and gold: and there is no end of their treasures.€€ƒx¼And their land is filled with horses: and their chariots are innumerable. Their land also is full of idols: they have adored the work of their own hands, which their own fingers have made.€€ƒxWAnd man hath bowed himself down, and man hath been debased: therefore forgive them not.€€ƒx|Enter thou into the rock, and hide thee in the pit from the face of the fear of the Lord, and from the glory of his majesty.€€ƒx†The lofty eyes of man are humbled, and the haughtiness of men shall be made to stoop: and the Lord alone shall be exalted in that day.€€ƒx˜Because the day of the Lord of hosts shall be upon every one that is proud and highminded, and upon every one that is arrogant, and he shall be humbled.€€ƒxRAnd upon all the tall and lofty cedars of Libanus, and upon all the oaks of Basan.€€ƒx@And upon all the high mountains and upon all the elevated hills.€€ƒx1And upon every high tower, and every fenced wall.€€ƒxGAnd upon all the ships of Tharsis, and upon all that is fair to behold.€€ƒx‹And the loftiness of men shall be bowed down, and the haughtiness of men shall be humbled, and the Lord alone shall be exalted in that day.€€‘ƒx¿And they shall go into the holes of rocks, and into the caves of the earth from the face of the fear of the Lord, and from the glory of his majesty, when he shall rise up to strike the earth.€€ƒx…In that day a man shall cast away his idols of silver, and his idols of gold, which he had made for himself to adore, moles and bats.€€ƒx»And he shall go into the clefts of rocks, and into the holes of stones from the face of the fear of the Lord, and from the glory of his majesty, when he shall rise up to strike the earth.€€ƒxYCease ye therefore from the man, whose breath is in his nostrils, for he is reputed high.€€ƒ`€€ƒx®For behold the sovereign Lord of hosts shall take away from Jerusalem, and from Juda the valiant and the strong, the whole strength of bread, and the whole strength of water.€€ƒxdThe strong man, and the man of war, the judge, and the prophet and the cunning man, and the ancient.€€ƒx…The captain over fifty, and the honourable in countenance, and the counsellor, and the architect, and the skilful in eloquent speech.€€ƒxVAnd I will give children to be their princes, and the effeminate shall rule over them.€€ƒx¨And the people shall rush one upon another, and every man against his neighbour: the child shall make a tumult against the ancient, and the base against the honourable.€€ƒxFor a man shall take hold of his brother, one of the house of his father, saying: Thou hast a garment, be thou our ruler, and let this ruin be under thy hand.€€ƒx†In that day he shall answer, saying: I am no healer, and in my house there is no bread, nor clothing: make me not ruler of the people.€€ƒxFor Jerusalem is ruined, and Juda is fallen: because their tongue, and their devices are against the Lord, to provoke the eyes of his majesty.€€ƒx³The shew of their countenance hath answered them: and they have proclaimed abroad their sin as Sodom, and they have not hid it: woe to their souls, for evils are rendered to them.€€ƒxNSay to the just man that it is well, for he shall eat the fruit of his doings.€€ƒxLWoe to the wicked unto evil: for the reward of his hands shall be given him.€€ƒx¹As for my people, their oppressors have stripped them, and women have ruled over them. O my people, they that call thee blessed, the same deceive thee, and destroy the way of thy steps.€€‘ƒx£The Lord will enter into judgment with the ancients of his people, and its princes: for you have devoured the vineyard, and the spoil of the poor is in your house.€€ƒx_Why do you consume my people, and grind the faces of the poor? saith the Lord the God of hosts.€€ƒxÒAnd the Lord said: Because the daughters of Sion are haughty, and have walked with stretched out necks, and wanton glances of their eyes, and made a noise as they walked with their feet and moved in a set pace:€€ƒxnThe Lord will make bald the crown of the head of the daughters of Sion, and the Lord will discover their hair.€€ƒxMIn that day the Lord will take away the ornaments of shoes, and little moons,€€ƒx5And chains and necklaces, and bracelets, and bonnets,€€ƒxSAnd bodkins, and ornaments of the legs, and tablets, and sweet balls, and earrings,€€ƒx.And rings, and jewels hanging on the forehead,€€ƒxLAnd changes of apparel, and short cloaks, and fine linen, and crisping pins,€€ƒx=And lookingglasses, and lawns, and headbands, and fine veils.€€ƒx¡And instead of a sweet smell there shall be stench, and instead of a girdle, a cord, and instead of curled hair, baldness, and instead of a stomacher, haircloth.€€ƒxMThy fairest men also shall fall by the sword, and thy valiant ones in battle.€€ƒxOAnd her gates shall lament and mourn, and she shall sit desolate on the ground.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¯And in that day seven women shall take hold of one man, saying: We will eat our own bread, and wear our own apparel: only let us be called by thy name, take away our reproach.€€ƒx¨In that day the bud of the Lord shall be in magnificence and glory, and the fruit of the earth shall be high, and a great joy to them that shall have escaped of Israel.€€ƒx¯And it shall come to pass, that every one that shall be left in Sion, and that shall remain in Jerusalem, shall be called holy, every one that is written in life in Jerusalem.€€ƒxÒAnd the Lord will create upon every place of mount Sion, and where he is called upon, a cloud by day, and a smoke and the brightness of a flaming fire in the night: for over all the glory shall be a protection.€€ƒxŠAnd there shall be a tabernacle for a shade in the daytime from the heat, and for a security and covert from the whirlwind, and from rain.€€ƒ`€€ƒx…I will sing to my beloved the canticle of my cousin concerning his vineyard. My beloved had a vineyard on a hill in a fruitful place.€€ƒxøAnd he fenced it in, and picked the stones out of it, and planted it with the choicest vines, and built a tower in the midst thereof, and set up a winepress therein: and he looked that it should bring forth grapes, and it brought forth wild grapes.€€ƒx]And now, O ye inhabitants of Jerusalem, and ye men of Juda, judge between me and my vineyard.€€ƒx°What is there that I ought to do more to my vineyard, that I have not done to it? was it that I looked that it should bring forth grapes, and it hath brought forth wild grapes?€€ƒx´And now I will shew you what I will do to my vineyard. I will take away the hedge thereof, and it shall be wasted: I will break down the wall thereof, and it shall be trodden down.€€ƒx¬And I will make it desolate: it shall not be pruned, and it shall not be digged: but briers and thorns shall come up: and I will command the clouds to rain no rain upon it.€€ƒxÆFor the vineyard of the Lord of hosts is the house of Israel: and the man of Juda, his pleasant plant: and I looked that he should do judgment, and behold iniquity: and do justice, and behold a cry.€€ƒxŠWoe to you that join house to house and lay field to field, even to the end of the place: shall you alone dwell in the midst of the earth?€€ƒx…These things are in my ears, saith the Lord of hosts: Unless many great and fair houses shall become desolate, without an inhabitant.€€ƒxoFor ten acres of vineyard shall yield one little measure, and thirty bushels of seed shall yield three bushels.€€ƒxzWoe to you that rise up early in the morning to follow drunkenness, and to drink in the evening, to be inflamed with wine.€€˜ƒx£Therefore is my people led away captive, because they had not knowledge, and their nobles have perished with famine, and their multitude were dried up with thirst.€€ƒx°Therefore hath hell enlarged her soul, and opened her mouth without any bounds, and their strong ones, and their people, and their high and glorious ones shall go down into it.€€ƒxhAnd man shall be brought down, and man shall be humbled, and the eyes of the lofty shall be brought low.€€ƒxdAnd the Lord of hosts shall be exalted in judgment, and the holy God shall be sanctified in justice.€€ƒxpAnd the lambs shall feed according to their order, and strangers shall eat the deserts turned into fruitfulness.€€ƒxRWoe to you that draw iniquity with cords of vanity, and sin as the rope of a cart.€€ƒx™That say: Let him make haste, and let his work come quickly, that we may see it: and let the counsel of the Holy One of Israel come, that we may know it.€€ƒx”Woe to you that call evil good, and good evil: that put darkness for light, and light for darkness: that put bitter for sweet, and sweet for bitter.€€ƒxLWoe to you that are wise in your own eyes, and prudent in your own conceits.€€ƒxGWoe to you that are mighty to drink wine, and stout men at drunkenness.€€ƒxRThat justify the wicked for gifts, and take away the justice of the just from him.€€ƒyTherefore as the tongue of the fire devoureth the stubble, and the heat of the flame consumeth it: so shall their root be as ashes, and their bud shall go up as dust: for they have cast away the law of the Lord of hosts, and have blasphemed the word of the Holy One of Israel.€€ƒy0Therefore is the wrath of the Lord kindled against his people, and he hath stretched out his hand upon them, and struck them: and the mountains were troubles, and their carcasses became as dung in the midst of the streets. For after this his anger is not turned away, but his hand is stretched out still.€€ƒx—And he will lift up a sign to the nations afar off, and will whistle to them from the ends of the earth: and behold they shall come with speed swiftly.€€ƒxµThere is none that shall faint, nor labour among them: they shall not slumber nor sleep, neither shall the girdle of their loins be loosed, nor the latchet of their shoes be broken.€€ƒx˜Their arrows are sharp, and all their bows are bent. The hoofs of their horses shall be like the flint, and their wheels like the violence of a tempest.€€ƒxÀTheir roaring like that of a lion, they shall roar like young lions: yea they shall roar, and take hold of the prey, and they shall keep fast hold of it, and there shall be none to deliver it.€€ƒxÄAnd they shall make a noise against them that day, like the roaring of the sea; we shall look towards the land, and behold darkness of tribulation, and the light is darkened with the mist thereof.€€ƒ`€€ƒxzIn the year that king Ozias died, I saw the Lord sitting upon a throne high and elevated: and his train filled the temple.€€ƒx¬Upon it stood the seraphims: the one had six wings, and the other had six wings: with two they covered his face, and with two they covered his feet, and with two they flew.€€ƒxuAnd they cried one to another, and said: Holy, holy, holy, the Lord God of hosts, all the earth is full of his glory,€€ƒxlAnd the lintels of the doors were moved at the voice of him that cried, and the house was filled with smoke.€€ƒxÎAnd I said: Woe is me, because I have held my peace; because I am a man of unclean lips, and I dwell in the midst of a people that hath unclean lips, and I have seen with my eyes the King the Lord of hosts.€€ƒxvAnd one of the seraphims flew to me, and in his hand was a live coal, which he had taken with the tongs off the altar.€€ƒxŒAnd he touched my mouth, and said: Behold this hath touched thy lips, and thy iniquities shall be taken away, and thy sin shall be cleansed.€€ƒxzAnd I heard the voice of the Lord, saying: Whom shall I send? and who shall go for us? And I said: Lo, here am I, send me.€€’ƒxËBlind the heart of this people, and make their ears heavy, and shut their eyes: lest they see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their heart, and be converted and I heal them.€€ƒxšAnd I said: How long, O Lord? And he said: Until the cities be wasted without inhabitant, and the houses without man, and the land shall be left desolate.€€ƒxlAnd the Lord shall remove men far away, and she shall be multiplied that was left in the midst of the earth.€€ƒxÏAnd there shall be still a tithing therein, and she shall turn, and shall be made a show as a turpentine tree, and as an oak that spreadeth its branches: that which shall stand therein, shall be a holy seed.€€ƒ`€€ƒxòAnd it came to pass in the days of Achaz the son of Joathan, the son of Ozias, king of Juda, that Rasin king of Syria and Phacee the son of Romelia king of Israel, came up to Jerusalem, to fight against it: but they could not prevail over it.€€ƒx²And they told the house of David, saying: Syria hath rested upon Ephraim, and his heart was moved, and the heart of his people, as the trees of the woods are moved with the wind.€€ƒxœAnd the Lord said to Isaiah: Go forth to meet Achaz, thou and Jasub thy son that is left, to the conduit of the upper pool in the way of the fuller's field.€€ƒxÓAnd thou shalt say to him: See thou be quiet: fear not, and let not thy heart be afraid of the two tails of these firebrands, smoking with the wrath of the fury of Rasin king of Syria, and of the son of Romelia.€€ƒxgBecause Syria hath taken counsel against thee, unto the evil of Ephraim and the son of Romelia, saying:€€ƒxtLet us go up to Juda, and rouse it up, and draw it away to us, and make the son of Tabeel king in the midst thereof.€€ƒxCThus saith the Lord God: It shall not stand, and this shall not be.€€ƒxBut the head of Syria is Damascus, and the head of Damascus is Rasin: and within threescore and five years, Ephraim shall cease to be a people:€€ƒxƒAnd the head of Ephraim is Samaria, and the head of Samaria is the son of Romelia. If you will not believe, you shall not continue.€€ƒx*And the Lord spoke again to Achaz, saying:€€ƒx]Ask thee a sign of the Lord thy God, either unto the depth of hell, or unto the height above.€€ƒx>And Achaz said: I will not ask, and I will not tempt the Lord.€€ƒxŠAnd he said: Hear ye therefore, O house of David: Is it a small thing for you to be grievous to men, that you are grievous to my God also?€€‹ƒx[He shall eat butter and honey, that he may know to refuse the evil, and to choose the good.€€ƒx’For before the child know to refuse the evil and to choose the good, the land which thou abhorrest shall be forsaken of the face of her two kings.€€ƒxÄThe Lord shall bring upon thee, and upon thy people, and upon the house of thy father, days that have not come since the time of the separation of Ephraim from Juda with the king of the Assyrians.€€ƒx³And it shall come to pass in that day, that the Lord shall hiss for the fly, that is in the uttermost parts of the rivers of Egypt, and for the bee that is in the land of Assyria.€€ƒx­And they shall come, and shall all of them rest in the torrents of the valleys, and in the holes of the rocks, and upon all places set with shrubs, and in all hollow places.€€ƒxµIn that day the Lord shall shave with a razor that is hired by them that are beyond the river, by the king of the Assyrians, the head and the hairs of the feet, and the whole beard.€€ƒx[And it shall come to pass in that day, that a man shall nourish a young cow, and two sheep.€€ƒxˆAnd for the abundance of milk he shall eat butter: for butter and honey shall every one eat that shall be left in the midst of the land.€€ƒxšAnd it shall come to pass in that day, that every place where there were a thousand vines, at a thousand pieces of silver, shall become thorns and briers.€€ƒxcWith arrows and with bows they shall go in thither: for briers and thorns shall be in all the land.€€ƒx¹And as for the hills that shall be raked with a rake, the fear of thorns and briers shall not come thither, but they shall be for the ox to feed on, and the lesser cattle to tread upon.€€–ƒxŠAnd the Lord said to me: Take thee a great book, and write in it with a man's pen. Take away the spoils with speed, quickly take the prey.€€ƒx\And I took unto me faithful witnesses, Urias the priest, and Zacharias the son of Barachias.€€ƒx©And I went to the prophetess, and she conceived, and bore a son. And the Lord said to me: Call his name, Hasten to take away the spoils: Make hast to take away the prey.€€ƒx¦For before the child know to call his father and his mother, the strength of Damascus, and the spoils of Samaria shall be taken away before the king of the Assyrians.€€ƒx'And the Lord spoke to me again, saying:€€ƒx‡Forasmuch as this people hath cast away the waters of Siloe, that go with silence, and hath rather taken Rasin, and the son of Romelia:€€ƒxÓTherefore behold the Lord will bring upon them the waters of the river strong and many, the king of the Assyrians, and all his glory: and he shall come up over all his channels, and shall overflow all his banks.€€ƒx«And shall pass through Juda, overflowing, and going over shall reach even to the neck. And the stretching out of his wings shall fill the breadth of thy, land, O Emmanuel.€€ƒx¨Gather yourselves together, O ye people, and be overcome, and give ear, all ye lands afar off: strengthen yourselves, and be overcome, gird yourselves, and be overcome.€€ƒxpTake counsel together, and it shall be defeated: speak a word, and it shall not be done: because God is with us.€€ƒxFor thus saith the Lord to me: As he hath taught me, with a strong arm, that I should not walk in the way of this people, saying:€€ƒxxSay ye not: A conspiracy: for all that this people speaketh, is a conspiracy: neither fear ye their fear, nor be afraid.€€ƒxXSanctify the Lord of hosts himself: and let him be your fear, and let him be your dread.€€ƒxµAnd he shall be a sanctification to you. But for a stone of stumbling, and for a rock of offence to the two houses of Israel, for a snare and a ruin to the inhabitants of Jerusalem.€€ƒxlAnd very many of them shall stumble and fall, and shall be broken in pieces, and shall be snared, and taken.€€ƒx7Bind up the testimony, seal the law among my disciples.€€ƒxeAnd I will wait for the Lord, who hath hid his face from the house of Jacob, and I will look for him.€€ƒxBehold I and my children, whom the Lord hath given me for a sign, and for a wonder in Israel from the Lord of hosts, who dwelleth in mount Sion.€€ƒx¨And when they shall say to you: Seek of pythons, and of diviners, who mutter in their enchantments: should not the people seek of their God, for the living of the dead?€€ƒx}To the law rather, and to the testimony. And if they speak not according to this word, they shall not have the morning light.€€ƒx¤And they shall pass by it, they shall fall, and be hungry: and when they shall be hungry, they will be angry, and curse their king, and their God, and look upwards.€€ƒx¢And they shall look to the earth, and behold trouble and darkness, weakness and distress, and a mist following them, and they cannot fly away from their distress.€€˜ƒx¼At the first time the land of Zabulon, and the land of Nephtali was lightly touched: and at the last the way of the sea beyond the Jordan of the Galilee of the Gentiles was heavily loaded.€€ƒx…The people that walked in darkness, have seen a great light: to them that dwelt in the region of the shadow of death, light is risen.€€ƒxÍThou hast multiplied the nation, and hast not increased the joy. They shall rejoice before thee, as they that rejoice in the harvest, as conquerors rejoice after taking a prey, when they divide the spoils.€€ƒxŒFor the yoke of their burden, and the rod of their shoulder, and the sceptre of their oppressor thou hast overcome, as in the day of Madian.€€ƒxzFor every violent taking of spoils, with tumult, and garment mingled with blood, shall be burnt, and be fuel for the fire.€€ƒx×For a CHILD IS BORN to us, and a son is given to us, and the government is upon his shoulder: and his name shall be called Wonderful, Counsellor, God the Mighty, the Father of the world to come, the Prince of Peace.€€ƒyHis empire shall be multiplied, and there shall be no end of peace: he shall sit upon the throne of David, and upon his kingdom; to establish it and strengthen it with judgment and with justice, from henceforth and for ever: the zeal of the Lord of hosts will perform this.€€ƒxAThe Lord sent a word into Jacob, and it hath lighted upon Israel.€€ƒx~And all the people of Ephraim shall know, and the inhabitants of Samaria that say in the pride and haughtiness of their heart:€€ƒx‡The bricks are fallen down, but we will build with square stones: they have cut down the sycamores, but we will change them for cedars.€€ƒxcAnd the Lord shall set up the enemies of Rasin over him, and shall bring on his enemies in a crowd:€€ƒxÂThe Syrians from the east, and, the Philistines from the west: and they shall devour Israel with open mouth. For all this his indignation is not turned away, but his hand is stretched out still.€€ƒxiAnd the people are not returned to him who hath struck them, and have not sought after the Lord of hosts.€€ƒx}And the Lord shall destroy out of Israel the head and the tail, him that bendeth down, and him that holdeth back, in one day.€€ƒx\The aged and honourable, he is the head: and the prophet that teacheth lies, he is the tail.€€ƒx‚And they that call this people blessed, shall cause them to err: and they that are called blessed, shall be thrown down, headlong.€€ƒyTherefore the Lord shall have no joy in their young men: neither shall he have mercy on their fatherless, and widows: for every one is a hypocrite and wicked, and every mouth hath spoken folly. For all this his indignation is not turned away, but his hand is stretched out still.€€ƒx³For wickedness is kindled as a fire, it shall devour the brier and the thorn: and shall kindle in the thicket of the forest, and it shall be wrapped up in smoke ascending on high.€€ƒx…By the wrath of the Lord of hosts the land is troubled, and the people shall be as fuel for the fire: no man shall spare his brother.€€ƒxñAnd he shall turn to the right hand, and shall be hungry: and shall eat on the left hand, and shall not be filled: every one shall eat the flesh of his own arm: Manasses Ephraim, and Ephraim Manasses, and they together shall be against Juda.€€ƒx_After all these things his indignation is not turned away, but his hand is stretched out still.€€ƒ`€€ƒxHWoe to them that make wicked laws: and when they write, write injustice:€€ƒx¢To oppress the poor in judgment, and do violence to the cause of the humble of my people: that widows might be their prey, and that they might rob the fatherless.€€ƒx™What will you do in the day of visitation, and of the calamity which cometh from afar? to whom will ye flee for help? and where will ye leave your glory?€€˜ƒxcWoe to the Assyrian, he is the rod and the staff of my anger, and my indignation is in their hands.€€ƒxÎI will send him to a deceitful nation, and I will give him a charge against the people of my wrath, to take away the spoils, and to lay hold on the prey, and to tread them down like the mire of the streets.€€ƒx‡But he shall not take it so, and his heart shall not think so: but his heart shall be set to destroy, and to cut off nations not a few.€€ƒqFor he shall say:€€ƒxqAre not my princes as so many kings? is not Calano as Charcamis: and Emath as Arphad? is not Samaria as Damascus?€€ƒxaAs my hand hath found the kingdoms of the idol, so also their idols of Jerusalem, and of Samaria.€€ƒxWShall I not, as I have done to Samaria and her idols, so do to Jerusalem and her idols?€€ƒxáAnd it shall come to pass, that when the Lord shall have performed all his works in mount Sion, and in Jerusalem, I will visit the fruit of the proud heart of the king of Assyria, and the glory of the haughtiness of his eyes.€€ƒxûFor he hath said: By the strength of my own hand I have done it, and by my own wisdom I have understood: and I have removed the bounds of the people, and have taken the spoils of the princes, and as a mighty man hath pulled down them that sat on high.€€ƒxİAnd my hand hath found the strength of the people as a nest; and as eggs are gathered, that are left, so have I gathered all the earth: and there was none that moved the wing, or opened the mouth, or made the least noise.€€ƒxêShall the axe boast itself against him that cutteth with it? or shall the saw exalt itself against him by whom it is drawn? as if a rod should lift itself up against him that lifteth it up, and a staff exalt itself, which is but wood.€€ƒxªTherefore the sovereign Lord, the Lord of hosts, shall send leanness among his fat ones: and under his glory shall be kindled a burning, as it were the burning of a fire.€€ƒx¦And the light of Israel shall be as a fire, and the Holy One thereof as a flame: and his thorns and his briers shall be set on fire, and shall be devoured in one day.€€ƒxAnd the glory of his forest, and of his beautiful hill, shall be consumed from the soul even to the flesh, and he shall run away through fear.€€ƒx‡And they that remain of the trees of his forest shall be so few, that they shall easily be numbered, and a child shall write them down.€€ƒxèAnd it shall come to pass in that day, that the remnant of Israel, and they that shall escape of the house of Jacob, shall lean no more upon him that striketh them: but they shall lean upon the Lord the Holy One of Israel, in truth.€€ƒxPThe remnant shall be converted, the remnant, I say, of Jacob, to the mighty God.€€ƒx™For if thy people, O Israel, shall be as the sand of the sea, a remnant of them shall be converted, the consumption abridged shall overflow with justice.€€ƒxcFor the Lord God of hosts shall make a consumption, and an abridgment in the midst of all the land.€€ƒxÓTherefore, thus saith the Lord the God of hosts: O my people that dwellest in Sion, be not afraid of the Assyrian: he shall strike thee with his rod, and he shall lift up his staff over thee in the way of Egypt.€€ƒxvFor yet a little and a very little while, and my indignation shall cease, and my wrath shall be upon their wickedness.€€ƒx¼And the Lord of hosts shall raise up a scourge against him, according to the slaughter of Madian in the rock of Oreb, and his rod over the sea, and he shall lift it up in the way of Egypt.€€ƒx¸And it shall come to pass in that day, that his burden shall be taken away from off thy shoulder, and his yoke from off thy neck, and the yoke shall putrefy at the presence of the oil.€€ƒx^He shall come into Aiath, he shall pass into Magron: at Machmas he shall lay up his carriages.€€ƒx_They have passed in haste, Gaba is our lodging: Rama was astonished, Gabaath of Saul fled away.€€ƒxHLift up thy voice, O daughter of Gallim, attend, O Laisa, poor Anathoth.€€ƒx;Medemena is removed: ye inhabitants of Gabim, take courage.€€ƒx…It is yet day enough, to remain in Nobe: he shall shake his hand against the mountain of the daughter of Sion, the hill of Jerusalem.€€—ƒxfAnd the thickets of the forest shall be cut down with iron, and Libanus with its high ones shall fall.€€ƒ`€€ƒxfAnd there shall come forth a rod out of the root of Jesse, and a flower shall rise up out of his root.€€ƒx¯And the spirit of the Lord shall rest upon him: the spirit of wisdom, and of understanding, the spirit of counsel, and of fortitude, the spirit of knowledge, and of godliness.€€ƒx¨And he shall be filled with the spirit of the fear of the Lord, He shall not judge according to the sight of the eyes, nor reprove according to the hearing of the ears.€€ƒxÑBut he shall judge the poor with justice, and shall reprove with equity the meek of the earth: and he shall strike the earth with the rod of his mouth, and with the breath of his lips he shall slay the wicked.€€ƒxPAnd justice shall be the girdle of his loins: and faith the girdle of his reins.€€ƒx¯The wolf shall dwell with the lamb: and the leopard shall lie down with the kid: the calf and the lion, and the sheep shall abide together, and a little child shall lead them.€€ƒxqThe calf and the bear shall feed: their young ones shall rest together: and the lion shall eat straw like the ox.€€ƒxƒAnd the sucking child shall play on other hole of the asp: and the weaned child shall thrust his hand into the den of the basilisk.€€ƒxœThey shall not hurt, nor shall they kill in all my holy mountain, for the earth is filled with the knowledge of the Lord, as the covering waters of the sea.€€ƒxIn that day the root of Jesse, who standeth for an ensign of the people, him the Gentiles shall beseech, and his sepulchre shall be glorious.€€ƒy,And it shall come to pass in that day, that the Lord shall set his hand the second time to possess the remnant of his people, which shall be left from the Assyrians, and from Egypt, and from Phetros, and from Ethiopia, and from Elam, and from Sennaar, and from Emath, and from the islands of the sea.€€ƒx±And he shall set up a standard unto the nations, and shall assemble the fugitives of Israel, and shall gather together the dispersed of Juda from the four quarters of the earth.€€ƒx™And the envy of Ephraim shall be taken away, and the enemies of Juda shall perish: Ephraim shall not envy Juda, and Juda shall not fight against Ephraim.€€ƒxÜBut they shall fly upon the shoulders of the Philistines by the sea, they together shall spoil the children of the east: Edom, till Moab shall be under the rule of their hand, and the children of Ammon shall be obedient.€€ƒxâAnd the Lord shall lay waste the tongue of the sea of Egypt, and shall lift up his hand over the river in the strength of his spirit: and he shall strike it in the seven streams, so that men may pass through it in their shoes.€€ƒx¯And there shall be a highway for the remnant of my people, which shall be left from the Assyrians: as there was for Israel in the day that he came up out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒ`€€ƒx–And thou shalt say in that day: I will give thanks to thee, O Lord, for thou wast angry with me: thy wrath is turned away, and thou hast comforted me.€€ƒx•Behold, God is my saviour, I will deal confidently, and will not fear: because the Lord is my strength, and my praise, and he is become my salvation.€€ƒx?Thou shall draw waters with joy out of the saviour's fountains:€€ƒx‘And you shall say in that day: Praise ye the Lord, and call upon his name: make his works known among the people: remember that his name is high.€€—ƒxuRejoice, and praise, O thou habitation of Sion: for great is he that is in the midst of thee, the Holy One of Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒx7The burden of Babylon which Isaiah the son of Amos saw.€€ƒxtUpon the dark mountain lift ye up a banner, exalt the voice, lift up the hand, and let the rulers go into the gates.€€ƒxoI have commanded my sanctified ones, and have called my strong ones in my wrath, them that rejoice in my glory.€€ƒx¾The noise of a multitude in the mountains, as it were of many people, the noise of the sound of kings, of nations gathered together: the Lord of hosts hath given charge to the troops of war.€€ƒxˆTo them that come from a country afar off, from the end of heaven: the Lord and the instruments of his wrath, to destroy the whole land.€€ƒxWHowl ye, for the day of the Lord is near: it shall come as a destruction from the Lord.€€ƒxFTherefore shall all hands be faint, and every heart of man shall melt,€€ƒxÅAnd shall be broken. Gripings and pains, shall take hold of them, they shall be in pain as a woman in labour. Every one shall be amazed at his neighbour, their countenances shall be as faces burnt.€€ƒx­Behold, the day of the Lord shall come, a cruel day, and full of indignation, and of wrath, and fury, to lay the land desolate, and to destroy the sinners thereof out of it.€€ƒx¢For the stars of heaven, and their brightness shall not display their light: the sun shall be darkened in his rising, and the moon shall not shine with her light.€€ƒx´And I will visit the evils of the world, and against the wicked for their iniquity: and I will make the pride of infidels to cease, and will bring down the arrogancy of the mighty.€€ƒxJA man shall be more precious than gold, yea a man than the finest of gold.€€ƒx¡For this I will trouble the heaven: and the earth shall be moved out of her place, for the indignation of the Lord of hosts, and for the day of his fierce wrath.€€ƒx»And they shall be as a doe fleeing away, and as a sheep: and there shall be none to gather them together: every man shall turn to his own people, and every one shall flee to his own land.€€ƒxsEvery one that shall be found, shall be slain: and every one that shall come to their aid, shall fall by the sword.€€ƒxTheir inhabitants shall be dashed in pieces before their eyes: their houses shall be pillaged, and their wives shall be ravished.€€ƒxYBehold I will stir up the Medes against them, who shall not seek silver, nor desire gold:€€ƒx”But with their arrows they shall kill the children, and shall have no pity upon the sucklings of the womb, and their eye shall not spare their sons.€€ƒx…And that Babylon, glorious among kingdoms, the famous pride of the Chaldeans, shall be even as the Lord destroyed Sodom and Gomorrha.€€ƒx´It shall no more be inhabited for ever, and it shall not be founded unto generation and generation: neither shall the Arabian pitch his tents there, nor shall shepherds rest there.€€ƒx˜But wild beasts shall rest there, and their houses shall be filled with serpents, and ostriches shall dwell there, and the hairy ones shall dance there:€€ˆƒ`€€ƒyHer time is near at hand, and her days shall not be prolonged. For the Lord will have mercy on Jacob, and will yet choose out of Israel, and will make them rest upon their own ground: and the stranger shall be joined with them, and shall adhere to the house of Jacob.€€ƒxùAnd the people shall take them, and bring them into their place: and the house of Israel shall possess them in the land of the Lord for servants and handmaids: and they shall make them captives that had taken them, and shall subdue their oppressors.€€ƒx¯And it shall come to pass in that day, that when God shall give thee rest from thy labour, and from thy vexation, and from the hard bondage, wherewith thou didst serve before,€€ƒxŠThou shalt take up this parable against the king of Babylon, and shalt say: How is the oppressor come to nothing, the tribute hath ceased?€€ƒxDThe Lord hath broken the staff of the wicked, the rod of the rulers,€€ƒxThat struck the people in wrath with an incurable wound, that brought nations under in fury, that persecuted in a cruel manner.€€ƒxAThe whole earth is quiet and still, it is glad and hath rejoiced.€€˜?ƒx´Hell below was in an uproar to meet thee at thy coming, it stirred up the giants for thee. All the princes of the earth are risen up from their thrones, all the princes of nations.€€ƒxeAll shall answer, and say to thee: Thou also art wounded as well as we, thou art become like unto us.€€ƒx…Thy pride is brought down to hell, thy carcass is fallen down: under thee shall the moth be strewed, and worms shall be thy covering.€€ƒxŠHow art thou fallen from heaven, O Lucifer, who didst rise in the morning? how art thou fallen to the earth, that didst wound the nations?€€ƒx®And thou saidst in thy heart: I will ascend into heaven, I will exalt my throne above the stars of God, I will sit in the mountain of the covenant, in the sides of the north.€€ƒxKI will ascend above the height of the clouds, I will be like the most High.€€ƒxFBut yet thou shalt be brought down to hell, into the depth of the pit.€€ƒx€They that shall see thee, shall turn toward thee, and behold thee. Is this the man that troubled the earth, that shook kingdoms,€€ƒxpThat made the world a wilderness, and destroyed the cities thereof, that opened not the prison to his prisoners?€€ƒxYAll the kings of the nations have all of them slept in glory, every one in his own house.€€ƒx¿But thou art cast out of thy grave, as an unprofitable branch defiled, and wrapped up among them that were slain by the sword, and art gone down to the bottom of the pit, as a rotten carcass.€€ƒx¨Thou shalt not keep company with them, even in burial: for thou hast destroyed thy land, thou hast slain thy people: the seed of the wicked shall not be named for ever.€€ƒxŸPrepare his children for slaughter for the iniquity of their fathers: they shall not rise up, nor inherit the land, nor fill the face of the world with cities.€€ƒx¢And I will rise up against them, saith the Lord of hosts: and I will destroy the name of Babylon, and the remains, and the bud, and the offspring, saith the Lord.€€ƒxAnd I will make it a possession for the ericius and pools of waters, and I will sweep it and wear it out with a besom, saith the Lord of hosts.€€ƒxgThe Lord of hosts hath sworn, saying: Surely as I have thought, so shall it be: and as I have purposed,€€ƒxÌSo shall it fall out: That I will destroy the Assyrian in my land, and upon my mountains tread him under foot: and his yoke shall be taken away from them, and his burden shall be taken off their shoulder.€€ƒxzThis is the counsel, that I have purposed upon all the earth, and this is the hand that is stretched out upon all nations.€€ƒxwFor the Lord of hosts hath decreed, and who can disannul it? and his hand is stretched out: and who shall turn it away?€€ƒx2In the year that king Achaz died, was this burden:€€ƒxÁRejoice not thou, whole Philistia, that the rod of him that struck thee is broken in pieces: for out of the root of the serpent shall come forth a basilisk, and his seed shall swallow the bird.€€ƒxAnd the firstborn of the poor shall be fed, and the poor shall rest with confidence: and I will make thy root perish with famine, and I will kill thy remnant.€€ƒxHowl, O gate; cry, O city: all Philistia is thrown down: for a smoke shall come from the north, and there is none that shall escape his troop.€€ƒx‹And what shall be answered to the messengers of the nations? That the Lord hath founded Sion, and the poor of his people shall hope in him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx’The burden of Moab. Because in the night Ar of Moab is laid waste, it is silent: because the wall of Moab is destroyed in the night, it is silent.€€ƒx°The house is gone up, and Dibon to the high places to mourn over Nabo, and over Medaba, Moab hath howled: on all their heads shall be baldness, and every beard shall be shaven.€€ƒxˆIn their streets they are girded with sackcloth: on the tops of their houses, and in their streets all shall howl and come down weeping.€€ƒx”Hesebon shall cry, and Eleale, their voice is heard even to Jasa. For this shall the well appointed men of Moab howl, his soul shall howl to itself.€€ƒx×My heart shall cry to Moab, the bars thereof shall flee unto Segor a heifer of three years old: for by the ascent of Luith they shall go up weeping: and in the way of Oronaim they shall lift up a cry of destruction.€€ƒxFor the waters of Nemrim shall be desolate, for the grass is withered away, the spring is faded, all the greenness is perished.€€ƒxwAccording to the greatness of their work, is their visitation also: they shall lead them to the torrent of the willows.€€ƒxFor the cry is gone round about the border of Moab: the howling thereof unto Gallim, and unto the well of Elim the cry thereof.€€ƒxFor the waters of Dibon are filled with blood: for I will bring more upon Dibon: the lion upon them that shall flee of Moab, and upon the remnant of the land.€€ƒ`€€ƒxuSend forth, O Lord, the lamb, the ruler of the earth, from Petra of the desert, to the mount of the daughter of Sion.€€ƒxœAnd it shall come to pass, that as a bird fleeing away, and as young ones flying out of the nest, so shall the daughters of Moab be in the passage of Arnon.€€ƒx‡Take counsel, gather a council: make thy shadow as the night in the midday: hide them that flee, and betray not them that wander about.€€ƒxÇMy fugitives shall dwell with thee: O Moab, be thou a covert to them from the face of the destroyer: for the dust is at an end, the wretch is consumed: he hath failed, that trod the earth under foot.€€ƒx®And a throne shall be prepared in mercy, and one shall sit upon it in truth in the tabernacle of David, judging and seeking judgment and quickly rendering that which is just.€€ƒx†We have heard of the pride of Moab, he is exceeding proud: his pride and his arrogancy, and his indignation is more than his strength.€€ƒxzTherefore shall Moab howl to Moab, every one shall howl: to them that rejoice upon the brick walls, tell ye their stripes.€€ƒxşFor the suburbs of Hesebon are desolate, and the lords of the nations have destroyed the vineyard of Sabama: the branches thereof have reached even to Jazer: they have wandered in the wilderness, the branches thereof are left, they are gone over the sea.€€ƒxÖTherefore I will lament with the weeping of Jazer the vineyard of Sabama: I will water thee with my tears, O Hesebon, and Eleale: for the voice of the treaders hath rushed in upon thy vintage, and upon thy harvest.€€ƒxéAnd gladness and joy shall be taken away from Carmel, and there shall be no rejoicing nor shouting in the vineyards. He shall not tread out wine in the press that was wont to tread it out: the voice of the treaders I have taken away.€€ƒx]Wherefore my bowels shall sound like a harp for Moab, and my inward parts for the brick wall.€€ƒxšAnd it shall come to pass, when it is seen that Moab is wearied on his high places, that he shall go in to his sanctuaries to pray, and shall not prevail.€€ƒx=This is the word, that the Lord spoke to Moab from that time:€€ƒxÍAnd now the Lord hath spoken, saying: In three years, as the years of a hireling, the glory of Moab shall be taken away for all the multitude of the people, and it shall be left small and feeble, not many.€€ƒ`€€ƒxkThe burden of Damascus. Behold Damascus shall cease to be a city, and shall be as a ruinous heap of stones.€€ƒxuThe cities of Aroer shall be left for flocks, and they shall rest there, and there shall be none to make them afraid.€€ƒx£And aid shall cease from Ephraim, and the kingdom from Damascus: and the remnant of Syria shall be as the glory of the children of Israel: saith the Lord of hosts.€€ƒx€And it shall come to pass in that day, that the glory of Jacob shall be made thin, and the fatness of his flesh shall grow lean.€€ƒxµAnd it shall be as when one gathereth in the harvest that which remaineth, and his arm shall gather the ears of corn: and it shall be as he that seeketh ears in the vale of Raphaim.€€ƒxòAnd the fruit thereof that shall be left upon it, shall be as one cluster of grapes, and as the shaking of the olive tree, two or three berries in the top of a bough, or four or five upon the top of the tree, saith the Lord the God of Israel.€€ƒxgIn that day man shall bow down himself to his Maker, and his eyes shall look to the Holy One of Israel.€€ƒx›And he shall not look to the altars which his hands made; and he shall not have respect to the things that his fingers wrought, such as groves and temples.€€ƒx£In that day his strong cities shall be forsaken, as the ploughs, and the corn that were left before the face of the children of Israel, and thou shalt be desolate.€€ƒx›Because thou hast forgotten God thy saviour, and hast not remembered thy strong helper: therefore shalt thou plant good plants, and shalt sow strange seed.€€ƒx°In the day of thy planting shall be the wild grape, and in the morning thy seed shall flourish: the harvest is taken away in the day of inheritance, and shall grieve thee much.€€ƒx„Woe to the multitude of many people, like the multitude of the roaring sea: and the tumult of crowds, like the noise of many waters.€€ƒxäNations shall make a noise like the noise of waters overflowing, but he shall rebuke him, and he shall flee far off: and he shall be carried away as the dust of the mountains before the wind, and as a whirlwind before a tempest.€€˜ƒ`€€ƒxKWoe to the land, the winged cymbal, which is beyond the rivers of Ethiopia,€€ƒyThat sendeth ambassadors by the sea, and in vessels of bulrushes upon the waters. Go, ye swift angels, to a nation rent and torn in pieces: to a terrible people, after which there is no other: to a nation expecting and trodden underfoot, whose land the rivers have spoiled.€€ƒx§All ye inhabitants of the world, who dwell on the earth, when the sign shall be lifted up on the mountains, you shall see, and you shall hear the sound of the trumpet.€€ƒx–For thus saith the Lord to me: I will take my rest, and consider in my place, as the noon light is clear, and as a cloud of dew in the day of harvest.€€ƒxÈFor before the harvest it was all flourishing, and it shall bud without perfect ripeness, and the sprigs thereof shall be cut off with pruning hooks: and what is left shall be cut away and shaken out.€€ƒxÄAnd they shall be left together to the birds of the mountains, and the beasts of the earth: and the fowls shall be upon them all the summer, and all the beasts of the earth shall winter upon them.€€ƒy?At that time shall a present be brought to the Lord of hosts, from a people rent and torn in pieces: from a terrible people, after which there hath been no other: from a nation expecting, expecting and trodden under foot, whose land the rivers have spoiled, to the place of the name of the Lord of hosts, to mount Sion.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÎThe burden of Egypt. Behold the Lord will ascend upon a swift cloud, and will enter into Egypt, and the idols of Egypt shall be moved at his presence, and the heart of Egypt shall melt in the midst thereof.€€ƒx±And I will set the Egyptians to fight against the Egyptians: and they shall fight brother against brother, and friend against friend, city against city, kingdom against kingdom.€€ƒx¾And the spirit of Egypt shall be broken in the bowels thereof, and I will cast down their counsel: and they shall consult their idols, and their diviners, and their wizards, and soothsayers.€€ƒxAnd I will deliver Egypt into the hand of cruel masters, and a strong king shall rule over them, saith the Lord the God of hosts.€€ƒxRAnd the water of the sea shall be dried up, and the river shall be wasted and dry.€€ƒx…And the rivers shall fail: the streams of the banks shall be diminished, and be dried up. The reed and the bulrush shall wither away.€€ƒxŸThe channel of the river shall be laid bare from its fountain, and every thing sown by the water shall be dried up, it shall wither away, and shall be no more.€€ƒx’The fishers also shall mourn, and all that cast a hook into the river shall lament, and they that spread nets upon the waters shall languish away.€€ƒxNThey shall be confounded that wrought in flax, combing and weaving fine linen.€€ƒxXAnd its watery places shall be dry, all they shall mourn that made pools to take fishes.€€ƒx±The princes of Tanis are become fools, the wise counsellors of Pharao have given foolish counsel: how will you say to Pharao: I am the son of the wise, the son of ancient kings?€€ƒxiWhere are now thy wise men? let them tell thee, and shew what the Lord of hosts hath purposed upon Egypt.€€ƒxˆThe princes of Tanis are become fools, the princes of Memphis are gone astray, they have deceived Egypt, the stay of the people thereof.€€ƒx¡The Lord hath mingled in the midst thereof the spirit of giddiness: and they have caused Egypt to err in all its works, as a drunken man staggereth and vomiteth.€€ƒxhAnd there shall be no work for Egypt, to make head or tail, him that bendeth down, or that holdeth back.€€˜ ƒx³And the land of Juda shall be a terror to Egypt: everyone that shall remember it shall tremble because of the counsel of the Lord of hosts, which he hath determined concerning it.€€ƒxªIn that day there shall be five cities in the land of Egypt, speaking the language of Chanaan, and swearing by the Lord of hosts: one shall be called the city of the sun.€€ƒx…In that day there shall be an altar of the Lord in the midst of the land of Egypt, and a monument of the Lord at the borders thereof:€€ƒxĞIt shall be for a sign, and for a testimony to the Lord of hosts in the land of Egypt. For they shall cry to the Lord because of the oppressor, and he shall send them a Saviour and a defender to deliver them.€€ƒxÅAnd the Lord shall be known by Egypt, and the Egyptians shall know the Lord in that day, and shall worship him with sacrifices and offerings: and they shall make vows to the Lord, and perform them.€€ƒx›And the Lord shall strike Egypt with a scourge, and shall heal it, and they shall return to the Lord, and he shall be pacified towards them, and heal them.€€ƒxµIn that day there shall be a way from Egypt to the Assyrians, and the Assyrian shall enter into Egypt, and the Egyptian to the Assyrians, and the Egyptians shall serve the Assyrian.€€ƒxlIn that day shall Israel be the third to the Egyptian and the Assyrian: a blessing in the midst of the land,€€ƒx”Which the Lord of hosts hath blessed, saying: Blessed be my people of Egypt, and the work of my hands to the Assyrian: but Israel is my inheritance.€€ƒ`€€ƒx–In the year that Tharthan entered into Azotus, when Sargon the king of the Assyrians had sent him, and he had fought against Azotus, and had taken it:€€ƒxÑAt that same time the Lord spoke by the hand of Isaiah the son of Amos, saying Go, and loose the sackcloth from off thy loins, and take off thy shoes from thy feet. And he did so, and went naked, and barefoot.€€ƒx–And the Lord said: As my servant Isaiah hath walked, naked and barefoot, it shall be a sign and a wonder of three years upon Egypt, and upon Ethiopia,€€ƒx»So shall the king of the Assyrians lead away the prisoners of Egypt, and the captivity of Ethiopia, young and old, naked and barefoot, with their buttocks uncovered to the shame of Egypt.€€ƒxWAnd they shall be afraid, and ashamed of Ethiopia their hope, and of Egypt their glory.€€ƒxÅAnd the inhabitants of this isle shall say in that day: Lo this was our hope, to whom we fled for help, to deliver us from the face of the king of the Assyrians: and how shall we be able to escape?€€ƒ`€€ƒxwThe burden of the desert of the sea. As whirlwinds come from the south, it cometh from the desert from a terrible land.€€ƒx¼A grievous vision is told me: he that is unfaithful dealeth unfaithfully: and he that is a spoiler, spoileth. Go up, O Elam, besiege, O Mede: I have made all the mourning thereof to cease.€€ƒx²Therefore are my loins filled with pain, anguish hath taken hold of me, as the anguish of a woman in labour: I fell down at the hearing of it, I was troubled at the seeing of it.€€ƒxQMy heart failed, darkness amazed me: Babylon my beloved is become a wonder to me.€€ƒxkPrepare the table, behold in the watchtower them that eat and drink: arise, ye princes, take up the shield.€€ƒxeFor thus hath the Lord said to me: Go, and set a watchman: and whatsoever he shall see, let him tell.€€ƒx„And he saw a chariot with two horsemen, a rider upon an ass, and a rider upon a camel: and he beheld them diligently with much heed.€€ƒx†And a lion cried out: I am upon the watchtower of the Lord, standing continually by day: and I am upon my ward, standing whole nights.€€ƒx¾Behold this man cometh, the rider upon the chariot with two horsemen, and he answered, and said: Babylon is fallen, she is fallen, and all the graven gods thereof are broken unto the ground.€€ƒxˆO my thrashing, and the children of my floor, that which I have heard of the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel, I have declared unto you.€€ƒxgThe burden of Duma calleth to me out of Seir: Watchman, what of the night? watchman, what of the night?€€ƒxWThe watchman said: The morning cometh, also the night: if you seek, seek: return, come.€€ƒxXThe burden in Arabia. In the forest at evening you shall sleep, in the paths of Dedanim.€€ƒxmMeeting the thirsty bring him water, you that inhabit the land of the south, meet with bread him that fleeth.€€ƒx„For they are fled from before the swords, from the sword that hung over them, from the bent bow, from the face of a grievous battle.€€ƒxŒAnd the residue of the number of strong archers of the children of Cedar shall be diminished: for the Lord the God of Israel hath spoken it.€€ƒ`€€ƒxmThe burden of the valley of vision. What aileth thee also, that thou too art wholly gone up to the housetops?€€ƒxjFull of clamour, a populous city, a joyous city: thy slain are not slain by the sword, nor dead in battle.€€ƒxvAll the princes are fled together, and are bound hard: all that were found, are bound together, they are fled far off.€€ƒxˆTherefore have I said: Depart from me, I will weep bitterly: labour not to comfort me, for the devastation of the daughter of my people.€€ƒx®For it is a day of slaughter and of treading down, and of weeping to the Lord the God of hosts in the valley of vision, searching the wall, and magnificent upon the mountain.€€ƒxcAnd Elam took the quiver, the chariot of the horseman, and the shield was taken down from the wall.€€ƒxfAnd thy choice valleys shall be full of chariots, and the horsemen shall place themselves in the gate.€€ƒxtAnd the covering of Juda shall be discovered, and thou shalt see in that day the armoury of the house of the forest.€€ƒx…And you shall see the breaches of the city of David, that they are many: and you have gathered together the waters of the lower pool,€€ƒxVAnd have numbered the houses of Jerusalem, and broken down houses to fortify the wall.€€ƒx¹And you made a ditch between the two walls for the water of the old pool: and you have not looked up to the maker thereof, nor regarded him even at a distance, that wrought it long ago.€€ƒxAnd the Lord, the God of hosts, in that day shall call to weeping, and to mourning, to baldness, and to girding with sackcloth:€€˜1ƒx•And the voice of the Lord of hosts was revealed in my ears: Surely this iniquity shall not be forgiven you till you die, saith the Lord God of hosts.€€ƒx•Thus saith the Lord God of hosts: Go, get thee in to him that dwelleth in the tabernacle, to Sobna who is over the temple: and thou shalt say to him:€€ƒxÁWhat dost thou here, or as if thou wert somebody here? for thou hast hewed thee out a sepulchre here, thou hast hewed out a monument carefully in a high place, a dwelling for thyself in a rock.€€ƒxuBehold the Lord will cause thee to be carried away, as a cock is carried away, and he will lift thee up as a garment.€€ƒx×He will crown thee with a crown of tribulation, he will toss thee like a ball into a large and spacious country: there shalt thou die, and there shall the chariot of thy glory be, the shame of the house of thy Lord.€€ƒxNAnd I will drive thee out from thy station, and depose thee from thy ministry.€€ƒx^And it shall come to pass in that day, that I will call my servant Eliacim the son of Helcias,€€ƒxËAnd I will clothe him with thy robe, and will strengthen him with thy girdle, and will give thy power into his hand: and he shall be as a father to the inhabitants of Jerusalem, and to the house of Juda.€€ƒxAnd I will lay the key of the house of David upon his shoulder: and he shall open, and none shall shut: and he shall shut, and none shall open.€€ƒxqAnd I will fasten him as a peg in a sure place, and he shall be for a throne of glory to the house of his father.€€ƒx«And they shall hang upon him all the glory of his father's house, divers kinds of vessels, every little vessel, from the vessels of cups even to every instrument of music.€€ƒx×In that day, saith the Lord of hosts, shall the peg be removed, that was fastened in the sure place: and it shall be broken and shall fall: and that which hung thereon, shall perish, because the Lord hath spoken it.€€ƒ`€€ƒxThe burden of Tyre. Howl, ye ships of the sea, for the house is destroyed, from whence they were wont to come: from the land of Cethim it is revealed to them.€€ƒxgBe silent, you that dwell in the island: the merchants of Sidon passing over the sea, have filled thee.€€ƒxxThe seed of the Nile in many waters, the harvest of the river is her revenue: and she is become the mart of the nations.€€ƒxÅBe thou ashamed, O Sidon: for the sea speaketh, even the strength of the sea, saying: I have not been in labour, nor have I brought forth, nor have I nourished up young men, nor brought up virgins.€€ƒxQWhen it shall be heard in Egypt, they will be sorry when they shall hear of Tyre:€€ƒx7Pass over the seas, howl, ye inhabitants of the island.€€ƒxpIs not this your city, which gloried from of old in her antiquity? her feet shall carry her afar off to sojourn.€€ƒx‹Who hath taken this counsel against Tyre, that was formerly crowned, whose merchants were princes, and her traders the nobles of the earth?€€ƒx‚The Lord of hosts hath designed it, to pull down the pride of all glory, and bring to disgrace all the glorious ones of the earth.€€ƒxLPass thy land as a river, O daughter of the sea, thou hast a girdle no more.€€ƒxHe stretched out his hand over the sea, he troubled kingdoms: the Lord hath given a charge against Chanaan, to destroy the strong ones thereof.€€ƒx˜And he said: Thou shalt glory no more, O virgin daughter of Sidon, who art oppressed: arise and sail over to Cethim, there also thou shalt have no rest.€€ƒxÚBehold the land of the Chaldeans, there was not such a people, the Assyrians founded it: they have led away the strong ones thereof into captivity, they have destroyed the houses thereof, they have, brought it to ruin.€€ƒx=Howl, O ye ships of the sea, for your strength is laid waste.€€ƒxÉAnd it shall come to pass in that day that thou, O Tyre, shalt be forgotten, seventy years, according to the days of one king: but after seventy years, there shall be unto Tyre as the song of a harlot.€€ƒx|Take a harp, go about the city, harlot that hast been forgotten: sing well, sing many a song, that thou mayst be remembered.€€ƒxáAnd it shall come to pass after seventy years, that the Lord will visit Tyre, and will bring her back again to her traffic: and she shall commit fornication again with all the kingdoms of the world upon the face of the earth.€€ƒxùAnd her merchandise and her hire shall be sanctified to the Lord: they shall not be kept in store, nor laid up: for her merchandise shall be for them that shall dwell before the Lord, that they may eat unto fulness, and be clothed for a continuance.€€ƒ`€€ƒxBehold the Lord shall lay waste the earth, and shall strip it, and shall afflict the face thereof, and scatter abroad the inhabitants thereof.€€ƒy And it shall be as with the people, so with the priest: and as with the servant so with his master: as with the handmaid, so with her mistress: as with the buyer, so with the seller: as with the lender, so with the borrower: as with him that calleth for his money, so with him that oweth.€€ƒxsWith desolation shall the earth be laid waste, and it shall be utterly spoiled: for the Lord hath spoken this word.€€ƒx|The earth mourned, and faded away, and is weakened: the world faded away, the height of the people of the earth is weakened.€€ƒxªAnd the earth is infected by the inhabitants thereof: because they have transgressed the laws, they have changed the ordinance, they have broken the everlasting covenant.€€ƒxŸTherefore shall a curse devour the earth, and the inhabitants thereof shall sin: and therefore they that dwell therein shall be mad, and few men shall be left.€€ƒxSThe vintage hath mourned, the vine hath languished away, all the merry have sighed.€€ƒxmThe mirth of timbrels hath ceased, the noise of them that rejoice is ended, the melody of the harp is silent.€€ƒxWThey shall not drink wine with a song: the drink shall be bitter to them that drink it.€€ƒxLThe city of vanity is broken down, every house is shut up, no man cometh in.€€ƒxjThere shall be a crying for wine in the streets: all mirth is forsaken: the joy of the earth is gone away.€€ƒxEDesolation is left in the city, and calamity shall oppress the gates.€€ƒxºFor it shall be thus in the midst of the earth, in the midst of the people, as if a few olives, that remain, should be shaken out of the olive tree: or grapes, when the vintage is ended.€€ƒx†These shall lift up their voice, and shall give praise: when the Lord shall be glorified, they shall make a joyful noise from the sea.€€ƒxkTherefore glorify ye the Lord in instruction: the name of the Lord God of Israel in the islands of the sea.€€ƒxöFrom the ends of the earth we have heard praises, the glory of the just one. And I said: My secret to myself, my secret to myself, woe is me: the prevaricators have prevaricated, and with the prevarication of transgressors they have prevaricated.€€ƒxOFear, and the pit, and the snare are upon thee, O thou inhabitant of the earth.€€˜ ƒx{With breaking shall the earth be broken, with crushing shall the earth be crushed, with trembling shall the earth be moved.€€ƒx¿With shaking shall the earth be shaken as a drunken man, and shall be removed as the tent of one night: and the iniquity thereof shall be heavy upon it, and it shall fall, and not rise again.€€ƒxAnd it shall come to pass, that in that day the Lord shall visit upon the host of heaven on high, and upon the kings of the earth, on the earth.€€ƒx©And they shall be gathered together as in the gathering of one bundle into the pit, and they shall be shut up there in prison: and after many days they shall be visited.€€ƒx°And the moon shall blush, and the sun shall be ashamed, when the Lord of hosts shall reign in mount Sion, and in Jerusalem, and shall be glorified in the sight of his ancients.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‘O Lord, thou art my God, I will exalt O thee, and give glory to thy name: for thou hast done wonderful things, thy designs of old faithful, amen.€€ƒxFor thou hast reduced the city to a heap, the strong city to ruin, the house of strangers, to be no city, and to be no more built up for ever.€€ƒxXTherefore shall a strong people praise thee, the city of mighty nations shall fear thee.€€ƒxÔBecause thou hast been a strength to the poor, a strength to the needy in his distress: a refuge from the whirlwind, a shadow from the heat. For the blast of the mighty is like a whirlwind beating against a wall.€€ƒx¢Thou shalt bring down the tumult of strangers, as heat in thirst: and as with heat under a burning cloud, thou shalt make the branch of the mighty to wither away.€€ƒx¨And the Lord of hosts shall make unto all people in this mountain, a feast of fat things, a feast of wine, of fat things full of marrow, of wine purified from the lees.€€ƒx‡And he shall destroy in this mountain the face of the bond with which all people were tied, and the web that he began over all nations.€€ƒxÌHe shall cast death down headlong for ever: and the Lord God shall wipe away tears from every face, and the reproach of his people he shall take away from off the whole earth: for the Lord hath spoken it.€€ƒxÆAnd they shall say in that day: Lo, this is our God, we have waited for him, and he will save us: this is the Lord, we have patiently waited for him, we shall rejoice and be joyful in his salvation.€€ƒx‹For the hand of the Lord shall rest in this mountain: and Moab shall be trodden down under him, as straw is broken in pieces with the wain.€€ƒx¨And he shall stretch forth his hands under him, as he that swimmeth stretcheth forth his hands to swim: and he shall bring down his glory with the dashing of his hands.€€ƒx|And the bulwarks of thy high walls shall fall, and be brought low, and shall be pulled down to the ground, even to the dust.€€ƒ`€€ƒxIn that day shall this canticle be sung in the land of Juda. Sion the city of our strength a saviour, a wall and a bulwark shall be set therein.€€ƒxMOpen ye the gates, and let the just nation, that keepeth the truth, enter in.€€ƒxYThe old error is passed away: thou wilt keep peace: peace, because we have hoped in thee.€€ƒxIYou have hoped in the Lord for evermore, in the Lord God mighty for ever.€€ƒx£For he shall bring down them that dwell on high, the high city he shall lay low. He shall bring it down even to the ground, he shall pull it down even to the dust.€€ƒxKThe foot shall tread it down, the feet of the poor, the steps of the needy.€€ƒxGThe way of the just is right, the path of the just is right to walk in.€€ƒx…And in the way of thy judgments, O Lord, we have patiently waited for thee: thy name, and thy remembrance are the desire of the soul.€€ƒxÕMy soul hath desired thee in the night: yea, and with my spirit within me in the morning early I will watch to thee. When thou shalt do thy judgments on the earth, the inhabitants of the world shall learn justice.€€ƒx Let us have pity on the wicked, but he will not learn justice: in the land of the saints he hath done wicked things, and he shall not see the glory of the Lord.€€ƒx„Lord, let thy hand be exalted, and let them not see: let the envious people see, and be confounded: and let fire devour thy enemies.€€ƒxJLord, thou wilt give us peace: for thou hast wrought all our works for us.€€ƒxjO Lord our God, other lords besides thee have had dominion over us, only in thee let us remember thy name.€€˜ƒx¢Thou hast been favourable to the nation, O Lord, thou hast been favourable to the nation: art thou glorified? thou hast removed all the ends of the earth far off.€€ƒxmLord, they have sought after thee in distress, in the tribulation of murmuring thy instruction was with them.€€ƒx™As a woman with child, when she draweth near the time of her delivery, is in pain, and crieth out in her pangs: so are we become in thy presence, O Lord.€€ƒx²We have conceived, and been as it were in labour, and have brought forth wind: we have not wrought salvation on the earth, therefore the inhabitants of the earth have not fallen.€€ƒxÆThy dead men shall live, my slain shall rise again: awake, and give praise, ye that dwell in the dust: for thy dew is the dew of the light: and the land of the giants thou shalt pull down into ruin.€€ƒx†Go, my people, enter into thy chambers, shut thy doors upon thee, hide thyself a little for a moment, until the indignation pass away.€€ƒx¼For behold the Lord will come out of his place, to visit the iniquity of the inhabitant of the earth against him: and the earth shall disclose her blood, and shall cover her slain no more.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¶In that day the Lord with his hard, and great, and strong sword shall visit leviathan the bar serpent, and leviathan the crooked serpent, and shall slay the whale that is in the sea.€€ƒx@In that day there shall be singing to the vineyard of pure wine.€€ƒxmI am the Lord that keep it, I will suddenly give it drink: lest any hurt come to it, I keep it night and day.€€ƒx‹There is no indignation in me: who shall make me a thorn and a brier in battle: shall I march against it, shall, I set it on fire together?€€ƒxfOr rather shall it take hold of my strength, shall it make peace with me, shall it make peace with me?€€ƒxvWhen they shall rush in unto Jacob, Israel shall blossom and bud, and they shall fill the face of the world with seed.€€ƒx|Hath he struck him according to the stroke of him that struck him? or is he slain, as he killed them that were slain by him?€€ƒxˆIn measure against measure, when it shall be cast off, thou shalt judge it. He hath meditated with his severe spirit in the day of heat.€€ƒyTherefore upon this shall the iniquity of the house of Jacob be forgiven: and this is all the fruit, that the sin thereof should be taken away, when he shall have made all the stones of the altar, as burnt stones broken in pieces, the groves and temples shall not stand.€€ƒxÇFor the strong city shall be desolate, the beautiful city shall be forsaken, and shall be left as a wilderness: there the calf shall feed, and there shall he lie down, and shall consume its branches.€€ƒxÊIts harvest shall be destroyed with drought, women shall come and teach it: for it is not a wise people, therefore he that made it, shall not have mercy on it: and he that formed it, shall not spare it.€€ƒxÄAnd it shall come to pass, that in that day the Lord will strike from the channel of the river even to the torrent of Egypt, and you shall be gathered together one by one, O ye children of Israel.€€ƒyAnd it shall come to pass, that in that day a noise shall be made with a great trumpet, and they that were lost, shall come from the land of the Assyrians, and they that were outcasts in the land of Egypt, and they shall adore the Lord in the holy mount in Jerusalem.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¡Woe to the crown of pride, to the drunkards of Ephraim, and to the fading flower the glory his joy, who were on the head of the fat valley, staggering with wine.€€ƒx¢Behold the Lord is mighty and strong, as a storm of hail: a destroying whirlwind, as the violence of many waters overflowing, and sent forth upon a spacious land.€€ƒxKThe crown of pride of the drunkards of Ephraim shall be trodden under feet.€€ƒxèAnd the fading tower the glory of his joy, who is on the head of the fat valley, shall be as a hasty fruit before the ripeness of autumn: which when he that seeth it shall behold, as soon he taketh it in his hand, he will eat it up.€€ƒxkIn that day the Lord of hosts shall be a crown of glory, and a garland of joy to the residue of his people:€€ƒxyAnd a spirit of judgment to him that sitteth in judgment, and strength to them that return out of the battle to the gate.€€ƒy&But these also have been ignorant through wine, and through drunkenness have erred: the priest and the prophet have been ignorant through drunkenness, they are swallowed up with wine, they have gone astray in drunkenness, they have not known him that seeth, they have been ignorant of judgment.€€ƒxQFor all the tables were full of vomit and filth, so that there was no more place.€€ƒxšWhom shall he teach knowledge? and whom shall he make to understand the hearing? them that are weaned from the milk, that are drawn away from the breasts.€€˜1ƒxRFor with the speech of lips, and with another tongue he will speak to this people.€€ƒxhTo whom he said: This is my rest, refresh the weary, and this is my refreshing: and they would not hear.€€ƒxÛAnd the word of the Lord shall be to them: Command, command again; command, command again; expect, expect again; a little there, a little there: that they may go, and fall backward, and be broken, and snared, and taken.€€ƒxcWherefore hear the word of the Lord, ye scornful men, who rule over my people that is in Jerusalem.€€ƒx÷For you have said: We have entered into a league with death, and we have made a covenant with hell. When the overflowing scourge shall pass through, it shall not come upon us: for we have placed our hope in lies, and by falsehood we are protected.€€ƒxËTherefore thus saith the Lord God: Behold I will lay a stone in the foundations of Sion, a tried stone, a corner stone, a precious stone, founded in the foundation. He that believeth, let him not hasten.€€ƒx“And I will set judgment in weight, and justice in measure: and hail shall overturn the hope of falsehood: and waters shall overflow its protection.€€ƒx¥And your league with death shall be abolished, and your covenant with hell shall not stand: when the overflowing scourge shall pass, you shall be trodden down by it.€€ƒxÆWhensoever it shall pass through, it shall take you away: because in the morning early it shall pass through, in the day and in the night, and vexation alone shall make you understand what you hear.€€ƒx]For the bed is straitened, so that one must fall out, and a short covering cannot cover both.€€ƒxØFor the Lord shall stand up as in the mountain of divisions: he shall be angry as in the valley which is in Gabaon: that he may do his work, his strange work: that he may perform his work, his work is strange to him.€€ƒx˜And now do not mock, lest your bonds be tied strait. For I have heard of the Lord the God of hosts a consumption and a cutting short upon all the earth.€€ƒx9Give ear, and hear my voice, hearken, and hear my speech.€€ƒxSShall the ploughman plough all the day to sow, shall he open and harrow his ground?€€ƒx¤Will he not, when he hath made plain the surface thereof, sow gith, and scatter cummin, and put wheat in order, and barley, and millet, and vetches in their bounds?€€ƒx=For he will instruct him in judgment: his God will teach him.€€ƒxŸFor gith shall not be thrashed with saws, neither shall the cart wheel turn about upon cummin: but gith shall be beaten out with a rod, and cumin with a staff.€€ƒx–But breadcorn shall be broken small: but the thrasher shall not thrash it for ever, neither shall the cart wheel hurt it, nor break it with its teeth.€€ƒxgThis also is come forth from the Lord God of hosts, to make his counsel wonderful, and magnify justice.€€ƒ`€€ƒxgWoe to Ariel, to Ariel the city which David took: year is added to year. the solemnities are at an end.€€ƒxmAnd I will make a trench about Ariel, and it shall be in sorrow and mourning, and it shall be to me as Ariel.€€ƒx|And I will make a circle round about thee, and I will cast up a rampart against thee, and raise up bulwarks to besiege thee.€€ƒxÜThou shalt be brought down, thou shall speak out of the earth, and thy speech shall be heard out of the ground: and thy voice shall be from the earth like that of the python, and out of the earth thy speech shall mutter.€€ƒx•And the multitude of them that fan thee, shall be like small dust: and as ashes passing away, the multitude of them that have prevailed against thee.€€ƒxÎAnd it shall be at an instant suddenly. A visitation shall come from the Lord of hosts in thunder, and with earthquake, and with a great noise of whirlwind and tempest; and with the flame of devouring fire.€€ƒx­And the multitude of all nations that have fought against Ariel, shall be as the dream of a vision by night, and all that have fought, and besieged and prevailed against it.€€ƒy$And as he that is hungry dreameth, and eateth, but when he is awake, his soul is empty: and as he that is thirsty dreameth, and drinketh and after he is awake, is yet faint with thirst, and his soul is empty: so shall be the multitude of all the Gentiles, that have fought against mount Sion.€€ƒxnBe astonished, and wonder, waver, and stagger: be drunk, and not with wine: stagger, and not with drunkenness.€€ƒx“For the Lord hath mingled for you the spirit of a deep sleep, he will shut up your eyes, he will cover your prophets and princes, that see visions.€€ƒxÏAnd the vision of all shall be unto you as the words of a book that is sealed which when they shall deliver to one that is learned, they shall say: Read this: and he shall answer: I cannot, for it is sealed.€€ƒx†And the book shall be given to one that knoweth no letters, and it shall be said to him: Read: and he shall answer: I know no letters.€€ƒxÍAnd the Lord said: Forasmuch as this people draw near me with their mouth, and with their lips glorify me, but their heart is far from me, and they have feared me with the commandment and doctrines of men:€€ƒxÎTherefore behold I will proceed to cause an admiration in this people, by a great and wonderful miracle: for wisdom shall perish from their wise men, and the understanding of their prudent men shall be hid.€€ƒx—Woe to you that are deep of heart, to hide your counsel from the Lord: and their works are in the dark, and they say: Who seeth us, and who knoweth us?€€ƒxåThis thought of yours is perverse: as if the clay should think against the potter, and the work should say to the maker thereof: Thou madest me not: or the thing framed should say to him that fashioned it: Thou understandest not.€€ƒxwIs it not yet a very little while, and Libanus shall be turned into charmel, and charmel shall be esteemed as a forest?€€ƒx}And in that day the deaf shall hear the words of the book, and out of darkness and obscurity the eyes of the blind shall see.€€ƒxlAnd the meek shall increase their joy in the Lord, and the poor men shall rejoice in the Holy One of Israel.€€ƒxqFor he that did prevail hath failed, the scorner is consumed, and they are all cut off that watched for iniquity:€€ƒxqThat made men sin by word, and supplanted him that reproved them in the gate, and declined in vain from the just.€€ƒxŸTherefore thus saith the Lord to the house of Jacob, he that redeemed Abraham: Jacob shall not now be confounded, neither shall his countenance now be ashamed:€€ƒxµBut when he shall see his children, the work of my hands in the midst of him sanctifying my name, and they shall sanctify the Holy One of Jacob, and shall glorify the God of Israel:€€ƒxeAnd they that erred in spirit, shall know understanding, and they that murmured, shall learn the law.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¨Woe to you, apostate children, saith the Lord, that you would take counsel, and not of me: and would begin a web, and not by my spirit, that you might add sin upon sin:€€ƒxWho walk to go down into Egypt, and have not asked at my mouth, hoping for help in the strength of Pharao, and trusting in the shadow of Egypt.€€ƒxoAnd the strength of Pharao shall be to your confusion, and the confidence of the shadow of Egypt to your shame.€€ƒxEFor thy princes were in Tanis, and thy messengers came even to Hanes.€€˜ƒy0The burden of the beasts of the south. In a land of trouble and distress, from whence come the lioness, and the lion, the viper and the flying basilisk, they carry their riches upon the shoulders of beasts, and their treasures upon the bunches of camels to a people that shall not be able to profit them.€€ƒxuFor Egypt shall help in vain, and to no purpose: therefore have I cried concerning this: It is pride only, sit still.€€ƒxNow therefore go in and write for them upon box, and note it diligently in a book, and it shall be in the latter days for a testimony for ever.€€ƒxaFor it is a people that provoketh to wrath, and lying children that will not hear the law of God.€€ƒx˜Who say to the seers: See not: and to them that behold: Behold not for us those things that are right: speak unto us pleasant things, see errors for us.€€ƒxgTake away from me the way, turn away the path from me, let the Holy One of Israel cease from before us.€€ƒx•Therefore thus saith the Holy One of Israel: Because you have rejected this word, and have trusted in oppression and tumult, and have leaned upon it:€€ƒxµTherefore shall this iniquity be to you as a breach that falleth, and is found wanting in a high wall, for the destruction thereof shall come on a sudden, when it is not looked for.€€ƒxÿAnd it shall be broken small, as the potter's vessel is broken all to pieces with mighty breaking, and there shall not a sherd be found of the pieces thereof, wherein a little fire may be carried from the hearth, or a little water be drawn out of the pit.€€ƒx¥For thus saith the Lord God the Holy One of Israel: If you return and be quiet, you shall be saved: in silence and in hope shall your strength be. And you would not:€€ƒx¨But have said: No, but we will flee to horses: therefore shall you flee. And we will mount upon swift ones: therefore shall they be swifter that shall pursue after you.€€ƒx¬A thousand men shall flee for fear of one: and for fear of five shall you flee, till you be left as the mast of ship on the top of a mountain, and as an ensign upon a hill.€€ƒx¹Therefore the Lord waiteth that he may have mercy on you: and therefore shall he be exalted sparing you: because the Lord is the God of judgment: blessed are all they that wait for him.€€ƒx·For the people of Sion shall dwell in Jerusalem: weeping thou shalt not weep, he will surely have pity on thee: at the voice of thy cry, as soon as he shall hear, he will answer thee.€€ƒxœAnd the Lord will give you spare bread, and short water: and will not cause thy teacher to flee away from thee any more, and thy eyes shall see thy teacher.€€ƒx¦And thy ears shall hear the word of one admonishing thee behind thy back: This is the way, walk ye in it: and go not aside neither to the right hand, nor to the left.€€ƒx×And thou shalt defile the plates of thy graven things of silver, and the garment of thy molten things of gold, and shalt cast them away as the uncleanness of a menstruous woman. Thou shalt say to it: Get thee hence.€€ƒxĞAnd rain shall be given to thy seed, wheresoever thou shalt sow in the land: and the bread of the corn of the land shall be most plentiful, and fat. The lamb in that day shall feed at large in thy possession:€€ƒxrAnd thy oxen, and the ass colts that till the ground, shall eat mingled provender as it was winnowed in the floor.€€ƒx¢And there shall be upon every high mountain, and upon every elevated hill rivers of running waters in the day of the slaughter of many, when the tower shall fall.€€ƒxîAnd the light of the moon shall be as the light of the sun, and the light of the sun shall be sevenfold, as the light of seven days: in the day when the Lord shall bind up the wound of his people, and shall heal the stroke of their wound.€€ƒx Behold the name of the Lord cometh from afar, his wrath burneth, and is heavy to bear: his lips are filled with indignation, and his tongue as a devouring fire.€€ƒx£His breath as a torrent overflowing even to the midst of the neck, to destroy the nations unto nothing, and the bridle of error that was in the jaws of the people.€€ƒx¸You shall have a song as in the night of the sanctified solemnity, and joy of heart, as where one goeth with a pipe, to come into the mountain of the Lord, to the Mighty One of Israel.€€ƒxØAnd the Lord shall make the glory of his voice to be heard, and shall shew the terror of his arm, in the threatening of wrath, and the flame of devouring fire: he shall crush to pieces with whirlwind, and hailstones.€€ƒxOFor at the voice of the Lord the Assyrian shall fear being struck with the rod.€€ƒx¨And the passage of the rod shall be strongly grounded, which the Lord shall make to rest upon him with timbrels and harps, and in great battles he shall overthrow them.€€‘ƒ`€€ƒyWoe to them that go down to Egypt for help, trusting in horses, and putting their confidence in chariots, because they are many: and in horsemen, because they are very strong: and have not trusted in the Holy One of Israel, and have not sought after the Lord.€€ƒx³But he that is the wise one hath brought evil, and hath not removed his words: and he will rise up against the house of the wicked, and against the aid of them that work iniquity.€€ƒxÑEgypt is man, and not God: and their horses, flesh, and not spirit: and the Lord shall put down his hand, and the helper shall fall, and he that is helped shall fall, and they shall all be confounded together.€€ƒy2For thus saith the Lord to me: Like as the lion roareth, and the lions whelp upon his prey, and when a multitude of shepherds shall come against him, he will not fear at their voice, nor be afraid of their multitude: so shall the Lord of hosts come down to fight upon mount Sion, and upon the hill thereof.€€ƒxqAs birds flying, so will the Lord of hosts protect Jerusalem, protecting and delivering, passing over and saving.€€ƒx8Return as you had deeply revolted, O children of Israel.€€ƒx|For in that day a man shall cast away his idols of silver, and his idols of gold, which your hands have made for you to sin.€€ƒx»And the Assyrian shall fall by the sword not of a man, and the sword not of a man shall devour him, and he shall flee not at the face of the sword, and his young men shall be tributaries.€€ƒx¡And his strength shall pass away with dread, and his princes fleeing shall be afraid: the Lord hath said it, whose fire is in Sion, and his furnace in Jerusalem.€€ƒ`€€ƒxIBehold a king shall reign in justice, and princes shall rule in judgment.€€ƒx²And a man shall be as when one is hid from the wind, and hideth himself from a storm, as rivers of waters in drought, and the shadow of a rock that standeth out in a desert land.€€ƒxdThe eyes of them that see shall not be dim, and the ears of them that hear shall hearken diligently.€€ƒxnAnd the heart of fools shall understand knowledge, and the tongue of stammerers shall speak readily and plain.€€ƒxUThe fool shall no more be called prince: neither shall the deceitful be called great:€€ƒxÓFor the fool will speak foolish things, and his heart will work iniquity, to practise hypocrisy, and speak to the Lord deceitfully, and to make empty the soul of the hungry, and take away drink from the thirsty.€€•ƒxfBut the prince will devise such things as are worthy of a prince, and he shall stand above the rulers.€€ƒxYRise up, ye rich women, and hear my voice: ye confident daughters, give ear to my speech.€€ƒx„For after days and a year, you that are confident shall be troubled: for the vintage is at an end, the gathering shall come no more.€€ƒxlBe astonished, ye rich women, be troubled, ye confident ones: strip you, and be confounded, gird your loins.€€ƒxNMourn for your breasts, for the delightful country, for the fruitful vineyard.€€ƒx€Upon the land of my people shall thorns and briers come up: how much more upon all the houses of joy, of the city that rejoiced?€€ƒx¢For the house is forsaken, the multitude of the city is left, darkness and obscurity are come upon its dens for ever. A joy of wild asses, the pastures of flocks.€€ƒxUntil the spirit be poured upon us from on high: and the desert shall be as a charmel, and charmel shall be counted for a forest.€€ƒxLAn judgment shall dwell in the wilderness, and justice shall sit in charmel.€€ƒxdAnd the work of justice shall be peace, and the service of justice quietness, and security for ever.€€ƒxjAnd my people shall sit in the beauty of peace, and in the tabernacles of confidence, and in wealthy rest.€€ƒxTBut hail shall be in the descent of the forest, and the city shall be made very low.€€ƒxXBlessed are ye that sow upon all waters, sending thither the foot of the ox and the ass.€€ƒ`€€ƒyWoe to thee that spoilest, shalt not thou thyself also be spoiled? and thou that despisest, shalt not thyself also be despised? when thou shalt have made an end of spoiling, thou shalt be spoiled: when being wearied thou shalt cease to despise, thou shalt be despised.€€ƒx€O Lord, have mercy on us: for we have waited for thee: be thou our arm in the morning, and our salvation in the time of trouble.€€ƒxcAt the voice of the angel the people fled, and at the lifting up thyself the nations are scattered.€€ƒxmAnd your spoils shall be gathered together as the locusts are gathered, as when the ditches are full of them.€€ƒx`The Lord is magnified, for he hath dwelt on high: he hath filled Sion with judgment and justice.€€ƒxwAnd there shall be faith in thy times: riches of salvation, wisdom and knowledge: the fear of the Lord is his treasure.€€ƒxPBehold they that see shall cry without, the angels of peace shall weep bitterly.€€˜$ƒxThe land hath mourned, and languished: Libanus is confounded, and become foul, and Saron is become as a desert: and Basan and Carmel are shaken.€€ƒxUNow will I rise up, saith the Lord: now will I be exalted, now will I lift up myself.€€ƒx]You shall conceive heat, you shall bring forth stubble: your breath as fire shall devour you.€€ƒxcAnd the people shall be as ashes after a fire, as a bundle of thorns they shall be burnt with fire.€€ƒxUHear, you that are far off, what I have done, and you that are near know my strength.€€ƒxªThe sinners in Sion are afraid, trembling hath seized upon the hypocrites. Which of you can dwell with devouring fire? which of you shall dwell with everlasting burnings?€€ƒxÚHe that walketh in justices, and speaketh truth, that casteth away avarice by oppression, and shaketh his hands from all bribes, that stoppeth his ears lest he hear blood, and shutteth his eyes that he may see no evil.€€ƒxsHe shall dwell on high, the fortifications of rocks shall be his highness: bread is given him, his waters are sure.€€ƒxKHis eyes shall see the king in his beauty, they shall see the land far off.€€ƒx‰Thy heart shall meditate fear: where is the learned? where is he that pondered the words of the law? where is the teacher of little ones?€€ƒx¢The shameless people thou shalt not see, the people of profound speech: so that thou canst not understand the eloquence of his tongue, in whom there is no wisdom.€€ƒxéLook upon Sion the city of our solemnity: thy eyes shall see Jerusalem, a rich habitation, a tabernacle that cannot be removed: neither shall the nails thereof be taken away for ever, neither shall any of the cords thereof be broken.€€ƒx³Because only there our Lord is magnificent: a place of rivers, very broad and spacious streams: no ship with oars shall pass by it, neither shall the great galley pass through it.€€ƒx[For the Lord is our judge, the Lord is our lawgiver, the Lord is our king: he will save us.€€ƒxŞThy tacklings are loosed, and they shall be of no strength: thy mast shall be in such condition, that thou shalt not be able to spread the flag. Then shall the spoils of much prey be divided: the lame shall take the spoil.€€ƒxNeither shall he that is near, say: I am feeble. The people that dwell therein, shall have their iniquity taken away from them.€€ƒ`€€ƒxšCome near, ye Gentiles, and hear, and hearken, ye people: let the earth hear, and all that is therein, the world, and every thing that cometh forth of it.€€ƒxFor the indignation of the Lord is upon all nations, and his fury upon all their armies: he hath killed them, and delivered them to slaughter.€€ƒxTheir slain shall be cast forth, and out of their carcasses shall rise a stink: the mountains shall be melted with their blood.€€ƒxÁAnd all the host of the heavens shall pine away, and the heavens shall be folded together as a book: and all their host shall fall down as the leaf falleth from the vine, and from the fig tree.€€ƒxFor my sword is inebriated in heaven: behold it shall come down upon Idumea, and upon the people of my slaughter unto judgment.€€ƒxáThe sword of the Lord is filled with blood, it is made thick with the blood of lambs and buck goats, with the blood of rams full of marrow: for there is a victim of the Lord in Bosra and a great slaughter in the land of Edom.€€ƒxšAnd the unicorns shall go down with them, and the bulls with the mighty: their land shall be soaked with blood, and their ground with the fat of fat ones.€€ƒx`For it is the day of the vengeance of the Lord, the year of recompenses of the judgment of Sion.€€ƒx‹And the streams thereof shall be turned into pitch, and the ground thereof into brimstone: and the land thereof shall become burning pitch.€€ƒx¯Night and day it shall not be quenched, the smoke thereof shall go up for ever: from generation to generation it shall lie waste, none shall pass through it for ever and ever.€€ƒxºThe bittern and ericius shall possess it: and the ibis and the raven shall dwell in it: and a line shall be stretched out upon it, to bring it to nothing, and a plummet, unto desolation.€€ƒxzThe nobles thereof shall not be there: they shall call rather upon the king, and all the princes thereof shall be nothing.€€ƒx§And thorns and nettles shall grow up in its houses, and the thistle in the fortresses thereof: and it shall be the habitation of dragons, and the pasture of ostriches.€€ƒxAnd demons and monsters shall meet, and the hairy ones shall cry out one to another, there hath the lamia lain down, and found rest for herself.€€ƒx¿There hath the ericius had its hole, and brought up its young ones, and hath dug round about, and cherished them in the shadow thereof: thither are the kites gathered together one to another.€€ƒxÛSearch ye diligently in the book of the Lord, and read: not one of them was wanting, one hath not sought for the other: for that which proceedeth out of my mouth, he hath commanded, and his spirit it hath gathered them.€€ƒx¨And he hath cast the lot for them, and his hand hath divided it to them by line: they shall possess it for ever, from generation to generation they shall dwell therein.€€ƒ`€€ƒx|The land that was desolate and impassable shall be glad, and the wilderness shall rejoice, and shall flourish like the lily.€€˜ƒx;Strengthen ye the feeble hands, and confirm the weak knees.€€ƒx“Say to the fainthearted: Take courage, and fear not: behold your God will bring the revenge of recompense: God himself will come and will save you.€€ƒxXThen shall the eyes of the blind be opened, and the ears of the deaf shall be unstopped.€€ƒx™Then shall the lame man leap as a hart, and the tongue of the dumb shall be free: for waters are broken out in the desert, and streams in the wilderness.€€ƒx·And that which was dry land, shall become a pool, and the thirsty land springs of water. In the dens where dragons dwelt before, shall rise up the verdure of the reed and the bulrush.€€ƒx½And a path and a way shall be there, and it shall be called the holy way: the unclean shall not pass over it, and this shall be unto you a straight way, so that fools shall not err therein.€€ƒx‹No lion shall be there, nor shall any mischievous beast go up by it, nor be found there: but they shall walk there that shall be delivered.€€ƒxÌAnd the redeemed of the Lord shall return, and shall come into Sion with praise, and everlasting joy shall be upon their heads: they shall obtain joy and gladness, and sorrow and mourning shall flee away.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¡And it came to pass in the fourteenth year of king Ezechias, that Sennacherib king of the Assyrians came up against all the fenced cities of Juda, and took them.€€ƒx¹And the king of the Assyrians sent Rabsaces from Lachis to Jerusalem, to king Ezechias with a great army, and he stood by the conduit of the upper pool in the way of the fuller's field.€€ƒxŒAnd there went out to him Eliacim the son of Helcias, who was over the house, and Sobna the scribe, and Joahe the son of Asaph the recorder.€€ƒxAnd Rabsaces said to them: Tell Ezechias: Thus saith the great king, the king of the Assyrians: What is this confidence wherein thou trustest?€€ƒxtOr with what counsel or strength dost thou prepare for war? on whom dost thou trust, that thou art revolted from me?€€ƒx¶Lo thou trustest upon this broken staff of a reed, upon Egypt: upon which if a man lean, it will go into his hand, and pierce it: so is Pharao king of Egypt to all that trust in him.€€ƒxÇBut if thou wilt answer me: We trust in the Lord our God: is it not he whose high places and altars Ezechias hath taken away, and hath said to Juda and Jerusalem: You shall worship before this altar?€€ƒx©And now deliver thyself up to my lord the king of the Assyrians, and I will give thee two thousand horses, and thou wilt not be able on thy part to find riders for them.€€ƒx¢And how wilt thou stand against the face of the judge of one place, of the least of my master's servants? But if thou trust in Egypt, in chariots and in horsemen:€€ƒx„And am I now come up without the Lord against this land to destroy it? The Lord said to me: Go up against this land, and destroy it.€€ƒxÑAnd Eliacim, and Sobna, and Joahe said to Rabsaces: Speak to thy servants in the Syrian tongue: for we understand it: speak not to us in the Jews' language in the hearing of the people, that are upon the wall.€€ƒxØAnd Rabsaces said to them: Hath my master sent me to thy master and to thee, to speak all these words; and not rather to the men that sit on the wall; that they may eat their own dung, and drink their urine with you?€€ƒx’Then Rabsaces stood, and cried out with a loud voice in the Jews' language, and said: Hear the words of the great king, the king of the Assyrians.€€ƒx[Thus saith the king: Let not Ezechias deceive you, for he shall not be able to deliver you.€€ƒx§And let not Ezechias make you trust in the Lord, saying: The Lord will surely deliver us, and this city shall not be given into the hands of the king of the Assyrians.€€ƒx÷Do not hearken to Ezechias: for thus said the king of the Assyrians: Do with me that which is for your advantage, and come out to me, and eat ye every one of his vine, and every one of his fig tree, and drink ye every one the water of his cistern,€€ƒxuTill I come and take you away to a land, like to your own, a land of corn and of wine, a land of bread and vineyards.€€•ƒxtWhere is the god of Emath and of Arphad? where is the god of Sepharvaim? have they delivered Samaria out of my hand?€€ƒx“Who is there among all the gods of these lands, that hath delivered his country out of my hand, that the Lord may deliver Jerusalem out of my hand?€€ƒxkAnd they held their peace, and answered him not a word. For the king had commanded, saying: Answer him not.€€ƒxÉAnd Eliacim the son of Helcias, that was over the house, and Sobna the scribe, and Joahe the son of Asaph the recorder, went in to Ezechias with their garments rent, and told him the words of Rabsaces.€€ƒ`€€ƒx˜And it came to pass, when king Ezechias had heard it, that he rent his garments and covered himself with sackcloth, and went into the house of the Lord.€€ƒx And he sent Eliacim who was over the house, and Sobna the scribe, and the ancients of the priests covered with sackcloth, to Isaiah the son of Amos the prophet.€€ƒx¿And they said to him: Thus saith Ezechias: This day is a day of tribulation, and of rebuke, and of blasphemy: for the children are come to the birth, and there is not strength to bring forth.€€ƒyIt may be the Lord thy God will hear the words of Rabsaces, whom the king of the Assyrians his master hath sent to blaspheme the living God, and to reproach with words which the Lord thy God hath heard: wherefore lift up by prayer for the remnant that is left.€€ƒx,And the servants of Ezechias came to Isaiah.€€ƒxÊAnd Isaiah said to them: Thus shall you say to your master: Thus saith the Lord: Be not afraid of the words that thou hast heard, with which the servants of the king of the Assyrians have blasphemed me.€€ƒx¦Behold, I will send a spirit upon him, and he shall hear a message, and shall return to his own country, and I will cause him to fall by the sword in his own country.€€ƒx~And Rabsaces returned, and found the king of the Assyrians besieging Lobna. For he had heard that he was departed from Lachis.€€ƒxšAnd he heard say about Tharaca the king of Ethiopia: He is come forth to fight against thee. And when he heard it, he sent messengers to Ezechias, saying:€€ƒxÁThus shall you speak to Ezechias the king of Juda, saying: Let not thy God deceive thee, in whom thou trustest, saying: Jerusalem shall not be given into the hands of the king of the Assyrians.€€ƒxBehold thou hast heard all that the kings of the Assyrians have done to all countries which they have destroyed, and canst thou be delivered?€€ƒx›Have the gods of the nations delivered them whom my fathers have destroyed, Gozam, and Haram, and Reseph, and the children of Eden, that were in Thalassar?€€ƒxoWhere is the king of Emath, and the king of Arphad, and the king of the city of Sepharvaim, of Ana, and of Ava?€€ƒx˜And Ezechias took the letter from the hand of the messengers, and read it, and went up to the house of the Lord, and Ezechias spread it before the Lord.€€ƒx(And Ezechias prayed to the Lord, saying:€€ƒx–Lord of hosts, God of Israel who sitteth upon the cherubims, thou alone art the God of all the kingdoms of the earth, thou hast made heaven and earth.€€˜ƒx^For of a truth, O Lord, the kings of the Assyrians have laid waste lands, and their countries.€€ƒx”And they have cast their gods into the fire, for they were not gods, but the works of men's hands, of wood and stone: and they broke them in pieces.€€ƒxzAnd now, O Lord our God, save us out of his hand: and let all the kingdoms of the earth know, that thou only art the Lord.€€ƒx±And Isaiah the son of Amos sent to Ezechias, saying: Thus saith the Lord the God of Israel: For the prayer thou hast made to me concerning Sennacherib the king of the Assyrians:€€ƒx½This is the word which the Lord hath spoken of him: The virgin the daughter of Sion hath despised thee, and laughed thee to scorn: the daughter of Jerusalem hath wagged the head after thee.€€ƒx§Whom hast thou reproached, and whom hast thou blasphemed, and against whom hast thou exalted thy voice, and lifted up thy eyes on high? Against the Holy One of Israel.€€ƒy6By the hand of thy servants thou hast reproached the Lord: and hast said: With the multitude of my chariots I have gone up to the height of the mountains, to the top of Libanus: and I will cut down its tall cedars, and its choice fir trees, and will enter to the top of its height, to the forest of its Carmel.€€ƒxlI have digged, and drunk water, and have dried up with the sole of my foot, all the rivers shut up in banks.€€ƒxİHast thou not heard what I have done to him of old? from the days of old I have formed it: and now I have brought it to effect: and it hath come to pass that hills fighting together, and fenced cities should be destroyed.€€ƒxÜThe inhabitants of them were weak of hand, they trembled, and were confounded: they became like the grass of the field, and the herb of the pasture, and like the grass of the housetops, which withered before it was ripe.€€ƒxSI know thy dwelling, and thy going out, and thy coming in, and thy rage against me.€€ƒx¼When thou wast mad against me, thy pride came up to my ears: therefore I will put a ring in thy nose, and a bit between thy lips, and I will turn thee back by the way by which thou camest.€€ƒxÌBut to thee this shall be a sign: Eat this year the things that spring of themselves, and in the second year eat fruits: but in the third year sow and reap, and plant vineyards, and eat the fruit of them.€€ƒx}And that which shall be saved of the house of Juda, and which is left, shall take root downward, and shall bear fruit upward:€€ƒxzFor out of Jerusalem shall go forth a remnant, and salvation from mount Sion: the zeal of the Lord of hosts shall do this.€€ƒx½Wherefore thus saith the Lord concerning the king of the Assyrians: He shall not come into this city, nor shoot an arrow into it, nor come before it with shield, nor cast a trench about it.€€ƒx_By the way that he came, he shall return, and into this city he shall not come, saith the Lord.€€ƒxeAnd I will protect this city, and will save it for my own sake, and for the sake of David my servant.€€ƒx²And the angel of the Lord went out and slew in the camp of the Assyrians a hundred and eighty-five thousand. And they arose in the morning, and behold they were all dead corpses.€€ƒxcAnd Sennacherib the king of the Assyrians went out and departed, and returned, and dwelt in Ninive.€€ƒxŞAnd it came to pass, as he was worshipping in the temple of Nesroch his god, that Adramelech and Sarasar his sons slew him with the sword: and they fled into the land of Ararat, and Asarhaddon his son reigned in his stead.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÇIn those days Ezechias was sick even to death, and Isaiah the son of Amos the prophet cane unto him, and said to him: Thus saith the Lord: Take order with thy house, for thou shalt die, and not live.€€ƒxEAnd Ezechias turned his face toward the wall, and prayed to the Lord,€€ƒxÁAnd said: I beseech thee, O Lord, remember how I have walked before thee in truth, and with a perfect heart, and have done that which is good in thy sight. And Ezechias wept with great weeping.€€ƒx0And the word of the Lord came to Isaiah, saying:€€’ƒxjAnd I will deliver thee and this city out of the hand of the king of the Assyrians, and I will protect it.€€ƒxeAnd this shall be a sign to thee from the Lord, that the Lord will do this word which he hath spoken:€€ƒxÓBehold I will bring again the shadow of the lines, by which it is now gone down in the sun dial of Achaz with the sun, ten lines backward. And the sun returned ten lines by the degrees by which it was gone down.€€ƒx_The writing of Ezechias king of Juda, when he had been sick, and was recovered of his sickness.€€ƒxfI said: In the midst of my days I shall go to the gates of hell: I sought for the residue of my years.€€ƒxwI said: I shall not see the Lord God in the land of the living. I shall behold man no more, nor the inhabitant of rest.€€ƒxÛMy generation is at an end, and it is rolled away from me, as a shepherd's tent. My life is cut off, as by a weaver: whilst I was yet but beginning, he cut me off: from morning even to night thou wilt make an end of me.€€ƒxwI hoped till morning, as a lion so hath he broken all my bones: from morning even to night thou wilt make an end of me.€€ƒxI will cry like a young swallow, I will meditate like a dove: my eyes are weakened looking upward: Lord, I suffer violence, answer thou for me.€€ƒx”What shall I say, or what shall he answer for me, whereas he himself hath done it? I will recount to thee all my years in the bitterness of my soul.€€ƒx€O Lord, if man's life be such, and the life of my spirit be in such things as these, thou shalt correct me, and make me to live.€€ƒx”Behold in peace is my bitterness most bitter: but thou hast delivered my soul that it should not perish, thou hast cast all my sins behind thy back.€€ƒx‚For hell shall not confess to thee, neither shall death praise thee: nor shall they that go down into the pit, look for thy truth.€€ƒx~The living, the living, he shall give praise to thee, as I do this day: the father shall make the truth known to the children.€€ƒx_O Lord, save me, and we will sing our psalms all the days of our life in the house of the Lord.€€ƒx‚Now Isaiah had ordered that they should take a lump of figs, and lay it as a plaster upon the wound, and that he should be healed.€€ƒxZAnd Ezechias had said: What shall be the sign that I shall go up to the house of the Lord?€€ƒ`€€˜ ƒypAnd Ezechias rejoiced at their coming, and he shewed them the storehouses of his aromatical spices, and of the silver, and of the gold, and of the sweet odours, and of the precious ointment, and all the storehouses of his furniture, and all things that were found in his treasures. There was nothing in his house, nor in all his dominion that Ezechias shewed them not.€€ƒx¼Then Isaiah the prophet came to king Ezechias, and said to him: What said these men, and from whence came they to thee? And Ezechias said: From a far country they came to me, from Babylon.€€ƒx°And he said: What saw they in thy house? And Ezechias said: All things that are in my house have they seen, there was not any thing which I have not shewn them in my treasures.€€ƒx@And Isaiah said to Ezechias: Hear the word of the Lord of hosts.€€ƒxËBehold the days shall come that all that is in thy house, and that thy fathers have laid up in store until this day, shall be carried away into Babylon: there shall not any thing be left, saith the Lord.€€ƒxAnd of thy children, that shall issue from thee, whom thou shalt beget, they shall take away, and they shall be eunuchs in the palace of the king of Babylon.€€ƒx†And Ezechias said to Isaiah: The word of the Lord, which he hath spoken, is good. And he said: Only let peace and truth be in my days.€€ƒ`€€ƒx6Be comforted, be comforted, my people, saith your God.€€ƒx±Speak ye to the heart of Jerusalem, and call to her: for her evil is come to an end, her iniquity is forgiven: she hath received of the hand of the Lord double for all her sins.€€ƒx|The voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare ye the way of the Lord, make straight in the wilderness the paths of our God.€€ƒxEvery valley shall be exalted, and every mountain and hill shall be made low, and the crooked shall become straight, and the rough ways plain.€€ƒxvAnd the glory of the Lord shall be revealed, and all flesh together shall see, that the mouth of the Lord hath spoken.€€ƒx†The voice of one, saying: Cry. And I said: What shall I cry? All flesh is grass, and all the glory thereof as the flower of the field.€€ƒxThe grass is withered, and the flower is fallen, because the spirit of the Lord hath blown upon it. Indeed the people is grass:€€ƒx\The grass is withered, and the flower is fallen: but the word of our Lord endureth for ever.€€ƒxÛGet thee up upon a high mountain, thou that bringest good tidings to Sion: lift up thy voice with strength, thou that bringest good tidings to Jerusalem: lift it up, fear not. Say to the cities of Juda: Behold your God:€€ƒxBehold the Lord God shall come with strength, and his arm shall rule: Behold his reward is with him and his work is before him.€€ƒx³He shall feed his flock like a shepherd: he shall gather together the lambs with his arm, and shall take them up in his bosom, and he himself shall carry them that are with young.€€ƒxÙWho hath measured the waters in the hollow of his hand, and weighed the heavens with his palm? who hath poised with three fingers the bulk of the earth, and weighed the mountains in scales, and the hills in a balance?€€ƒx`Who hath forwarded the spirit of the Lord? or who hath been his counsellor, and hath taught him?€€ƒx With whom hath he consulted, and who hath instructed him, and taught him the path of justice, and taught him knowledge, and shewed him the way of understanding?€€ƒx‹Behold the Gentiles are as a drop of a bucket, and are counted as the smallest grain of a balance: behold the islands are as a little dust.€€ƒx`And Libanus shall not be enough to burn, nor the beasts thereof sufficient for a burnt offering.€€ƒxiAll nations are before him as if they had no being at all, and are counted to him as nothing, and vanity.€€ƒxGTo whom then have you likened God? or what image will you make for him?€€ƒx{Hath the workman cast a graven statue? or hath the goldsmith formed it with gold, or the silversmith with plates of silver?€€ƒxHe hath chosen strong wood, and that will not rot: the skilful workman seeketh how he may set up an idol that may not be moved.€€ƒxŒDo you not know? hath it not been heard? hath it not been told you from the beginning? have you not understood the foundations of the earth?€€ƒx»It is he that sitteth upon the globe of the earth, and the inhabitants thereof are as locusts: he that stretcheth out the heavens as nothing, and spreadeth them out as a tent to dwell in.€€ƒxgHe that bringeth the searchers of secrets to nothing, that hath made the judges of the earth as vanity.€€ƒx¸And surely their stock was neither planted, nor sown, nor rooted in the earth: suddenly he hath blown upon them, and they are withered, and a whirlwind shall take them away as stubble.€€˜!ƒxâLift up your eyes on high, and see who hath created these things: who bringeth out their host by number, and calleth them all by their names: by the greatness of his might, and strength, and power, not one of them was missing.€€ƒxzWhy sayest thou, O Jacob, and speakest, O Israel: My way is hid from the Lord, and my judgment is passed over from my God?€€ƒxÄKnowest thou not, or hast thou not heard? the Lord is the everlasting God, who hath created the ends of the earth: he shall not faint, nor labour, neither is there any searching out of his wisdom.€€ƒx`It is he that giveth strength to the weary, and increaseth force and might to them that are not.€€ƒxCYou shall faint, and labour, and young men shall fall by infirmity.€€ƒx›But they that hope in the Lord shall renew their strength, they shall take wings as eagles, they shall run and not be weary, they shall walk and not faint.€€ƒ`€€ƒx•Let the islands keep silence before me, and the nations take new strength: let them come near, and then speak, let us come near to judgment together.€€ƒxëWho hath raised up the just one from the east, hath called him to follow him? he shall give the nations in his sight, and he shall rule over kings: he shall give them as the dust to his sword, as stubble driven by the wind, to his bow.€€ƒxRHe shall pursue them, he shall pass in peace, no path shall appear after his feet.€€ƒx|Who hath wrought and done these things, calling the generations from the beginning? I the Lord, I am the first and the last.€€ƒx`The islands saw it, and feared, the ends of the earth were astonished, they drew near, and came.€€ƒxUEvery one shall help his neighbour, and shall say to his brother: Be of good courage.€€ƒx´The coppersmith striking with the hammer encouraged him that forged at that time, saying: It is ready for soldering: and he strengthened it with nails, that it should not be moved.€€ƒxYBut thou Israel, art my servant, Jacob whom I have chosen, the seed of Abraham my friend:€€ƒxÁIn whom I have taken thee from the ends of the earth, and from the remote parts thereof have called thee, and said to thee: Thou art my servant, I have chosen thee, and have not cast thee away.€€ƒx£Fear not, for I am with thee: turn not aside, for I am thy God: I have strengthened thee, and have helped thee, and the right hand of my just one hath upheld thee.€€ƒxBehold all that fight against thee shall be confounded and ashamed, they shall be as nothing, and the men shall perish that strive against thee.€€ƒx“Thou shalt seek them, and shalt not find the men that resist thee: they shall be as nothing: and as a thing consumed the men that war against thee.€€ƒxdFor I am the Lord thy God, who take thee by the hand, and say to thee: Fear not, I have helped thee.€€ƒx‡Fear not, thou worm of Jacob, you that are dead of Israel: I have helped thee, saith the Lord: and thy Redeemer the Holy One of Israel.€€ƒxI have made thee as a new thrashing wain, with teeth like a saw: thou shalt thrash the mountains, and break them in pieces: and shalt make the hills as chaff.€€ƒx²Thou shalt fan them, and the wind shall carry them away, and the whirlwind shall scatter them: and thou shalt rejoice in the Lord, in the Holy One of Israel thou shalt be joyful.€€ƒx©The needy and the poor seek for waters, and there are none: their tongue hath been dry with thirst. I the Lord will hear them, I the God of Israel will not forsake them.€€ƒx¬I will open rivers in the high hills, and fountains in the midst of the plains: I will turn the desert into pools of waters, and the impassable land into streams of waters.€€ƒx§I will plant in the wilderness the cedar, and the thorn, and the myrtle, and the olive tree: I will set in the desert the fir tree, the elm, and the box tree together:€€ƒx—That they may see and know, and consider, and understand together that the hand of the Lord hath done this, and the Holy One of Israel hath created it.€€ƒxnBring your cause near, saith the Lord: bring hither, if you have any thing to allege, saith the King of Jacob.€€ƒxÕLet them come, and tell us all things that are to come: tell us the former things what they were: and we will set our heart upon them and shall know the latter end of them, and tell us the things that are to come.€€ƒx˜Shew the things that are to come hereafter, and we shall know that ye are gods. Do ye also good or evil, if you can: and let us speak, and see together.€€ƒxqBehold, you are of nothing, and your work of that which hath no being: he that hath chosen you is an abomination.€€ƒx¸I have raised up one from the north, and he shall come from the rising of the sun: he shall call upon my name, and he shall make princes to be as dirt, and as the potter treading clay.€€ƒx¼Who hath declared from the beginning, that we may know: and from time of old, that we may say: Thou art just. There is none that sheweth, nor that foretelleth, nor that heareth your words.€€˜ƒxfAnd I saw, and there was no one even among them to consult, or who, when I asked, could answer a word.€€ƒx\Behold they are all in the wrong, and their works are vain: their idols are wind and vanity.€€ƒ`€€ƒxšBehold my servant, I will uphold him: my elect, my soul delighteth in him: I have given my spirit upon him, he shall bring forth judgment to the Gentiles.€€ƒxVHe shall not cry, nor have respect to person, neither shall his voice be heard abroad.€€ƒxtThe bruised reed he shall not break, and smoking flax he shall not quench, he shall bring forth judgment unto truth.€€ƒxpHe shall not be sad, nor troublesome, till he set judgment in the earth, and the islands shall wait for his law.€€ƒxÜThus saith the Lord God that created the heavens, and stretched them out: that established the earth, and the things that spring out of it: that giveth breath to the people upon it, and spirit to them that tread thereon.€€ƒx¨I the Lord have called thee in justice, and taken thee by the hand, and preserved thee. And I have given thee for a covenant of the people, for a light of the Gentiles:€€ƒx‘That thou mightest open the eyes of the blind, and bring forth the prisoner out of prison, and them that sit in darkness out of the prison house.€€ƒxaI the Lord, this is my name: I will not give my glory to another, nor my praise to graven things.€€ƒxƒThe things that were first, behold they are come: and new things do I declare: before they spring forth, I will make you hear them.€€ƒx¨Sing ye to the Lord a new song, his praise is from the ends of the earth: you that go down to the sea, and all that are therein: ye islands, and ye inhabitants of them.€€ƒx¢Let the desert and the cities thereof be exalted: Cedar shall dwell in houses: ye inhabitants of Petra, give praise, they shall cry from the top of the mountains.€€ƒxOThey shall give glory to the Lord, and shall declare his praise in the islands.€€ƒxThe Lord shall go forth as a mighty man, as a man of war shall he stir up zeal: he shall shout and cry: he shall prevail against his enemies.€€ƒx•I have always held my peace, I have kept silence, I have been patient, I will speak now as a woman in labour: I will destroy, and swallow up at once.€€ƒx›I will lay waste the mountains and hills, and will make all their grass to wither: and I will turn rivers into islands, and will dry up the standing pools.€€ƒyAnd I will lead the blind into the way which they know not: and in the paths which they were ignorant of I will make them walk: I will make darkness light before them, and crooked things straight: these things have I done to them, and have not forsaken them.€€ƒx€They are turned back: let them be greatly confounded, that trust in a graven thing, that say to a molten thing: You are our god.€€ƒx6Hear, ye deaf, and, ye blind, behold that you may see.€€ƒx¡Who is blind, but my servant? or deaf, but he to whom I have sent my messengers? Who is blind, but he that is sold? or who is blind, but the servant of the Lord?€€ƒxfThou that seest many things, wilt thou not observe them? thou that hast ears open, wilt thou not hear?€€ƒxOAnd the Lord was willing to sanctify him, and to magnify the law, and exalt it.€€ƒxèBut this is a people that is robbed and wasted: they are all the snare of young men, and they are hid in the houses of prisons: they are made a prey, and there is none to deliver them: a spoil, and there is none that saith: Restore.€€ƒxbWho is there among you that will give ear to this, that will attend and hearken for times to come?€€ƒx½Who hath given Jacob for a spoil, and Israel to robbers? hath not the Lord himself, against whom we have sinned? And they would not walk in his ways, and they have not hearkened to his law.€€ƒx®And he hath poured out upon him the indignation of his fury, and a strong battle, and hath burnt him round about, and he knew not: and set him on fire, and he understood not.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¢And now thus saith the Lord that created thee, O Jacob, and formed thee, O Israel: Fear not, for I have redeemed thee, and called thee by thy name: thou art mine.€€ƒxÄWhen thou shalt pass through the waters, I will be with thee, and the rivers shall not cover thee: when thou shalt walk in the fire, thou shalt not be burnt, and the flames shall not burn in thee:€€ƒx‡Since thou becamest honourable in my eyes, thou art glorious: I have loved thee, and I will give men for thee, and people for thy life.€€ƒxaFear not, for I am with thee: I will bring thy seed from the east, and gather thee from the west.€€ƒxˆI will say to the north: Give up: and to the south: Keep not back: bring my sons from afar, and my daughters from the ends of the earth.€€ƒxjAnd every one that calleth upon my name, I have created him for my glory. I have formed him, and made him.€€ƒxSBring forth the people that are blind, and have eyes: that are deaf, and have ears.€€ƒxëAll the nations are assembled together, and the tribes are gathered: who among you can declare this, and shall make us hear the former things? let them bring forth their witnesses, let them be justified, and hear, and say: It is truth.€€ƒxÑYou are my witnesses, saith the Lord, and my servant whom I have chosen: that you may know, and believe me, and understand that I myself am. Before me there was no God formed, and after me there shall be none.€€ƒx8I am, I am the Lord: and there is no saviour besides me.€€ƒx’I have declared, and have saved. I have made it heard, and there was no strange one among you. You are my witnesses, saith the Lord, and I am God.€€ƒxAnd from the beginning I am the same, and there is none that can deliver out of my hind: I will work, and who shall turn it away?€€ƒx¬Thus saith the Lord your redeemer, the Holy One of Israel: For your sake I sent to Babylon, and have brought down all their bars, and the Chaldeans glorying in their ships.€€ƒx>I am the Lord your Holy One, the Creator of Israel, your King.€€ƒxPThus saith the Lord, who made a way in the sea, and a path in the mighty waters.€€ƒx¯Who brought forth the chariot and the horse, the army and the strong: they lay down to sleep together, and they shall not rise again: they are broken as flax, and are extinct.€€ƒx:Remember not former things, and look not on things of old.€€˜&ƒx¶The beast of the field shall glorify me, the dragons and the ostriches: because I have given waters in the wilderness, rivers in the desert, to give drink to my people, to my chosen.€€ƒxFThis people have I formed for myself, they shall shew forth my praise.€€ƒxYBut thou hast not called upon me, O Jacob, neither hast thou laboured about me, O Israel.€€ƒx®Thou hast not offered me the ram of thy holocaust, nor hast thou glorified me with thy victims: I have not caused thee to serve with oblations, nor wearied thee with incense.€€ƒx¾Thou hast bought me no sweet cane with money, neither hast thou filled me with the fat of thy victims. But thou hast made me to serve with thy sins, thou hast wearied me with thy iniquities.€€ƒx]I am, I am he that blot out thy iniquities for my own sake, and I will not remember thy sins.€€ƒxaPut me in remembrance, and let us plead together: tell if thou hast any thing to justify thyself.€€ƒxGThy first father sinned, and thy teachers have transgressed against me.€€ƒx^And I have profaned the holy princes, I have given Jacob to slaughter, and Israel to reproach.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAAnd now hear, O Jacob, my servant, and Israel whom I have chosen.€€ƒx’Thus saith the Lord that made and formed thee, thy helper from the womb: Fear not, O my servant Jacob, and thou most righteous whom I have chosen.€€ƒx›For I will pour out waters upon the thirsty ground, and streams upon the dry land: I will pour out my spirit upon thy seed, and my blessing upon thy stock.€€ƒxOAnd they shall spring up among the herbs, as willows beside the running waters.€€ƒx·One shall say: I am the Lord's, and another shall call himself by the name of Jacob, and another shall subscribe with his hand, To the Lord, and surname himself by the name of Israel.€€ƒxThus saith the Lord the king of Israel, and his redeemer the Lord of hosts: I am the first, and I am the last, and besides me there is no God.€€ƒxÅWho is like to me? let him call and declare: and let him set before me the order, since I appointed the ancient people: and the things to come, and that shall be hereafter, let them shew unto them.€€ƒx°Fear ye not, neither be ye troubled from that time I have made thee to hear, and have declared: you are my witnesses. Is there a God besides me, a maker, whom I have not known?€€ƒx»The makers of idols are all of them nothing, and their best beloved things shall not profit them. They are their witnesses, that they do not see, nor understand, that they may be ashamed.€€ƒxNWho hath formed a god, and made a graven thing that is profitable for nothing?€€ƒx­Behold, all the partakers thereof shall be confounded: for the makers are men: they shall all assemble together, they shall stand and fear, and shall be confounded together.€€ƒxÌThe smith hath wrought with his file, with coals, and with hammers he hath formed it, and hath wrought with the strength of his arm: he shall hunger and faint, he shall drink no water, and shall be weary.€€ƒxêThe carpenter hath stretched out his rule, he hath formed it with a plane: he hath made it with corners, and hath fashioned it round with the compass: and he hath made the image of a man as it were a beautiful man dwelling in a house.€€ƒxœHe hath cut down cedars, taken the holm, and the oak that stood among the trees of the forest: he hath planted the pine tree, which the rain hath nourished.€€ƒxÉAnd it hath served men for fuel: he took thereof, and warmed himself: and he kindled it, and baked bread: but of the rest he made a god, and adored it: he made a graven thing, and bowed down before it.€€ƒxªPart of it he burnt with fire, and with part of it he dressed his meat: he boiled pottage, and was filled, and was warmed, and said: Aha, I am warm, I have seen the fire.€€ƒx®But the residue thereof he made a god, and a graven thing for himself: he boweth down before it, and adoreth it, and prayeth unto it, saying: Deliver me, for thou art my God.€€ƒx‰They have not known, nor understood: for their eyes are covered that they may not see, and that they may not understand with their heart.€€ƒy"They do not consider in their mind, nor know, nor have the thought to say: I have burnt part of it in the fire, and I have baked bread upon the coals thereof: I have broiled flesh and have eaten, and of the residue thereof shall I make an idol? shall I fall down before the stock of a tree?€€ƒx…Part thereof is ashes: his foolish heart adoreth it, and he will not save his soul, nor say: Perhaps there is a lie in my right hand.€€ƒx†Remember these things, O Jacob, and Israel, for thou art my servant. I have formed thee, thou art my servant, O Israel, forget me not.€€ƒxmI have blotted out thy iniquities as a cloud, and thy sins as a mist: return to me, for I have redeemed thee.€€ƒxëGive praise, O ye heavens, for the Lord hath shewn mercy: shout with joy, ye ends of the earth: ye mountains, resound with praise, thou, O forest, and every tree therein: for the Lord hath redeemed Jacob, and Israel shall be glorified.€€ƒx¿Thus saith the Lord thy redeemer, and thy maker, from the womb: I am the Lord, that make all things, that alone stretch out the heavens, that established the earth, and there is none with me.€€ƒxˆThat make void the tokens of diviners, and make the soothsayers mad. That turn the wise backward, and that make their knowledge foolish.€€ƒxÔThat raise up the word of my servant and perform the counsel of my messengers, who say to Jerusalem: Thou shalt be inhabited: and to the cities of Juda: You shall be built, and I will raise up the wastes thereof.€€ƒxDWho say to the deep: Be thou desolate, and I will dry up thy rivers.€€ƒx¬Who say to Cyrus: Thou art my shepherd, and thou shalt perform all my pleasure. Who say to Jerusalem: Thou shalt be built: and to the temple: Thy foundations shall be laid.€€˜ƒxÖThus saith the Lord to my anointed Cyrus, whose right hand I have taken hold of, to subdue nations before his face, and to turn the backs of kings, and to open the doors before him, and the gates shall not be shut.€€ƒxI will go before thee, and will humble the great ones of the earth: I will break in pieces the gates of brass, and will burst the bars of iron.€€ƒx¨And I will give thee hidden treasures, and the concealed riches of secret places: that thou mayest know that I am the Lord who call thee by thy name, the God of Israel.€€ƒx—For the sake of my servant Jacob, and Israel my elect, I have even called thee by thy name: I have made a likeness of thee, and thou hast not known me.€€ƒxmI am the Lord, and there is none else: there is no God besides me: I girded thee, and thou hast not known me:€€ƒxœThat they may know who are from the rising of the sun, and they who are from the west, that there is none besides me. I am the Lord, and there is none else:€€ƒxjI form the light, and create darkness, I make peace, and create evil: I the Lord that do all these things.€€ƒx»Drop down dew, ye heavens, from above, and let the clouds rain the just: let the earth be opened, and bud forth a saviour: and let justice spring up together: I the Lord have created him.€€ƒx¥Woe to him that gainsayeth his maker, a sherd of the earthen pots: shall the clay say to him that fashioneth it: What art thou making, and thy work is without hands?€€ƒxeWoe to him that saith to his father: Why begettest thou? and to the woman: Why dost thou bring forth?€€ƒx¢Thus saith the Lord the Holy One of Israel, his maker: Ask me of things to come, concerning my children, and concerning the work of my hands give ye charge to me.€€ƒxvI made the earth: and I created man upon it: my hand stretched forth the heavens, and I have commanded all their host.€€ƒx³I have raised him up to justice, and I will direct all his ways: he shall build my city, and let go my captives, not for ransom, nor for presents, saith the Lord the God of hosts.€€ƒyMThus saith the Lord: The labour of Egypt, and the merchandise of Ethiopia, and of Sabaim, men of stature shall come over to thee, and shall be thine: they shall walk after thee, they shall go bound with manacles: and they shall worship thee, and shall make supplication to thee: only in thee is God, and there is no God besides thee.€€ƒx<Verily thou art a hidden God, the God of Israel the saviour.€€ƒx\They are all confounded and ashamed: the forgers of errors are gone together into confusion.€€ƒxƒIsrael is saved in the Lord with an eternal salvation: you shall not be confounded, and you shall not be ashamed for ever and ever.€€ƒxÛFor thus saith the Lord that created the heavens, God himself that formed the earth, and made it, the very maker thereof: he did not create it in vain: he formed it to be inhabited. I am the Lord, and there is no other.€€ƒx®I have not spoken in secret, in a dark place of the earth: I have not said to the seed of Jacob: Seek me in vain. I am the Lord that speak justice, that declare right things.€€ƒx¿Assemble yourselves, and come, and draw near together, ye that are saved of the Gentiles: they have no knowledge that set up the wood of their graven work, and pray to a god that cannot save.€€ƒxèTell ye, and come, and consult together: who hath declared this from the beginning, who hath foretold this from that time? Have not I the Lord, and there is no God else besides me? A just God and a saviour, there is none besides me.€€ƒxjBe converted to me, and you shall be saved, all ye ends of the earth: for I am God, and there is no other.€€ƒx[I have sworn by myself, the word of justice shall go out of my mouth, and shall not return:€€ƒxBFor every knee shall be bowed to me, and every tongue shall swear.€€ƒx„Therefore shall he say: In the Lord are my justices and empire: they shall come to him, and all that resist him shall be confounded.€€ƒxBIn the Lord shall all the seed of Israel be justified and praised.€€ƒ`€€ƒxBel is broken, Nebo is destroyed: their idols are put upon beasts and cattle, your burdens of heavy weight even unto weariness.€€—ƒx€Hearken unto me, O house of Jacob, all the remnant of the house of Israel who are carried by my bowels, are borne up by my womb.€€ƒxŠEven to your old age I am the same, and to your grey hairs I will carry you: I have made you, and I will bear: I will carry and will save.€€ƒxRTo whom have you likened me, and made me equal, and compared me, and made me like?€€ƒxYou that contribute gold out of the bag, and weigh out silver in the scales: and hire a goldsmith to make a god: and they fall down and worship.€€ƒxæThey bear him on their shoulders and carry him, and set him in his place, and he shall stand, and shall not stir out of his place. Yea, when they shall cry also unto him, he shall not hear: he shall not save them from tribulation.€€ƒxFRemember this, and be ashamed: return, ye transgressors, to the heart.€€ƒxcRemember the former age, for I am God, and there is no God beside, neither is there the like to me:€€ƒx¸Who shew from the beginning the things that shall be at last, and from ancient times the things that as yet are not done, saying: My counsel shall stand, and all my will shall be done:€€ƒxÒWho call a bird from the east, and from a far country the man of my own will, and I have spoken, and will bring it to pass: I have created, and I will do it. Hear me, O ye hardhearted, who are far from justice.€€ƒx’I have brought my justice near, it shall not be afar off: and my salvation shall not tarry. I will give salvation in Sion, and my glory in Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒ`€€ƒx¸Come down, sit in the dust, O virgin daughter of Babylon, sit on the ground: there is no throne for the daughter of the Chaldeans, for thou shalt no more be called delicate and tender.€€ƒxqTake a millstone and grind meal: uncover thy shame, strip thy shoulder, make bare thy legs, pass over the rivers.€€ƒxrThy nakedness shall be discovered, and thy shame shall be seen: I will take vengeance, and no man shall resist me.€€ƒxDOur redeemer, the Lord of hosts is his name, the Holy One of Israel.€€ƒx€Sit thou silent, and get thee into darkness, O daughter of the Chaldeans: for thou shalt no more be called the lady of kingdoms.€€ƒxºI was angry with my people, I have polluted my inheritance, and have given them into thy hand: thou hast shewn no mercy to them: upon the ancient thou hast laid thy yoke exceeding heavy.€€ƒxŠAnd thou hast said: I shall be a lady for ever: thou hast not laid these things to thy heart, neither hast thou remembered thy latter end.€€ƒxÌAnd now hear these things, thou that art delicate, and dwellest confidently, that sayest in thy heart: I am, and there is none else besides me: I shall not sit as a widow, and I shall not know barrenness.€€ƒxÌThese two things shall come upon thee suddenly in one day, barrenness and widowhood. All things are come upon thee, because of the multitude of thy sorceries, and for the great hardness of thy enchanters.€€ƒxÖAnd thou hast trusted in thy wickedness, and hast said: There is none that seeth me. Thy wisdom, and, thy knowledge, this hath deceived thee. And thou hast said in thy heart: I am, and besides me there is no other.€€ƒxÓEvil shall come upon thee, and thou shalt not know the rising thereof: and calamity shall fall violently upon thee, which thou canst not keep off: misery shall come upon thee suddenly, which thou shalt not know.€€˜ƒxØThou hast failed in the multitude of thy counsels: let now the astrologers stand and save thee, they that gazed at the stars, and counted the months, that from them they might tell the things that shall come to thee.€€ƒxÇBehold they are as stubble, fire hath burnt them, they shall not deliver themselves from the power of the flames: there are no coals wherewith they may be warmed, nor fire, that they may sit thereat.€€ƒx©Such are all the things become to thee, in which thou hast laboured: thy merchants from thy youth, every one hath erred in his own way, there is none that can save thee.€€ƒ`€€ƒxğHear ye these things, O house of Jacob, you that are called by the name of Israel, and are come forth out of the waters of Juda, you who swear by the name of the Lord, and make mention of the God of Israel, but not in truth, nor in justice.€€ƒxpFor they are called of the holy city, and are established upon the God of Israel: the Lord of hosts is his name.€€ƒxœThe former things of old, I have declared, and they went forth out of my mouth, and I have made them to be heard: I did them suddenly and they came to pass.€€ƒx_For I knew that thou art stubborn, and thy neck is as an iron sinew, and thy forehead as brass.€€ƒx«I foretold thee of old, before they came to pass I told thee, lest thou shouldst say: My idols have done these things, and my graven and molten things have commanded them.€€ƒx¢See now all the things which thou hast heard: but have you declared them? I have shewn thee new things from that time, and things are kept which thou knowest not:€€ƒx‚They are created now, and not of old: and before the day, when thou heardest them not, lest thou shouldst say: Behold I knew them.€€ƒx¯Thou hast neither heard, nor known, neither was thy ear opened of old. For I know that transgressing thou wilt transgress, and I have called thee a transgressor from the womb.€€ƒxsFor my name's sake I will remove my wrath far off: and for my praise I will bridle thee, lest thou shouldst perish.€€ƒx\Behold I have refined thee, but not as silver, I have chosen thee in the furnace of poverty.€€ƒxuFor my own sake, for my own sake will I do it, that I may not be blasphemed: and I will not give my glory to another.€€ƒx`Hearken to me, O Jacob, and thou Israel whom I call: I am he, I am the first, and I am the last.€€ƒxƒMy hand also hath founded the earth, and my right hand hath measured the heavens: I shall call them, and they shall stand together.€€ƒx¿Assemble yourselves together, all you, and hear: who among them hath declared these things? the Lord hath loved him, he will do his pleasure in Babylon, and his arm shall be on the Chaldeans.€€ƒxYI, even I have spoken and called him: I have brought him, and his way is made prosperous.€€ƒx¶Come ye near unto me, and hear this: I have not spoken in secret from the beginning: from the time before it was done, I was there, and now the Lord God hath sent me, and his spirit.€€ƒx¡Thus saith the Lord thy redeemer, the Holy One of Israel: I am the Lord thy God that teach thee profitable things, that govern thee in the way that thou walkest.€€ƒxwO that thou hadst hearkened to my commandments: thy peace had been as a river, and thy justice as the waves of the sea,€€ƒx«And thy seed had been as the sand, and the offspring of thy bowels like the gravel thereof: his name should not have perished, nor have been destroyed from before my face.€€ƒxÎCome forth out of Babylon, flee ye from the Chaldeans, declare it with the voice of joy: make this to be heard, and speak it out even to the ends of the earth. Say: The Lord hath redeemed his servant Jacob.€€ƒx™They thirsted not in the desert, when he led them out: he brought forth water out of the rock for them, and he clove the rock, and the waters gushed out.€€ƒx0There is no peace to the wicked, saith the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxœGive ear, ye islands, and hearken, ye people from afar. The Lord hath called me from the womb, from the bowels of my mother he hath been mindful of my name.€€ƒx¢And he hath made my mouth like a sharp sword: in the shadow of his hand he hath protected me, and hath made me as a chosen arrow: in his quiver he hath hidden me.€€˜1ƒx™And I said: I have laboured in vain, I have spent my strength without cause and in vain: therefore my judgment is with the Lord, and my work with my God.€€ƒxãAnd now saith the Lord, that formed me from the womb to be his servant, that I may bring back Jacob unto him, and Israel will not be gathered together: and I am glorified in the eyes of the Lord, and my God is made my strength.€€ƒy	And he said: It is a small thing that thou shouldst be my servant to raise up the tribes of Jacob, and to convert the dregs of Israel. Behold, I have given thee to be the light of the Gentiles, that thou mayst be my salvation even to the farthest part of the earth.€€ƒy*Thus saith the Lord the redeemer of Israel, his Holy One, to the soul that is despised, to the nation that is abhorred, to the servant of rulers: Kings shall see, and princes shall rise up, and adore for the Lord's sake, because he is faithful, and for the Holy One of Israel, who hath chosen thee.€€ƒyThus saith the Lord: In an acceptable time I have heard thee, and in the day of salvation I have helped thee: and I have preserved thee, and given thee to be a covenant of the people, that thou mightest raise up the earth, and possess the inheritances that were destroyed:€€ƒx¶That thou mightest say to them that are bound: Come forth: and to them that are in darkness: Shew yourselves. They shall feed in the ways, and their pastures shall be in every plain.€€ƒxÅThey shall not hunger, nor thirst, neither shall the heat nor the sun strike them: for he that is merciful to them, shall be their shepherd, and at the fountains of waters he shall give them drink.€€ƒxFAnd I will make all my mountains a way, and my paths shall be exalted.€€ƒxvBehold these shall come from afar, and behold these from the north and from the sea, and these from the south country.€€ƒx­Give praise, O ye heavens, and rejoice, O earth, ye mountains, give praise with jubilation: because the Lord hath comforted his people, and will have mercy on his poor ones.€€ƒxIAnd Sion said: The Lord hath forsaken me, and the Lord hath forgotten me.€€ƒxƒCan a woman forget her infant, so as not to have pity on the son of her womb? and if she should forget, yet will not I forget thee.€€ƒxLBehold, I have graven thee in my hands: thy walls are always before my eyes.€€ƒxWThy builders are come: they that destroy thee and make thee waste shall go out of thee.€€ƒxŞLift up thy eyes round about, and see all these are gathered together, they are come to thee: I live, saith the Lord, thou shalt be clothed with all these as with an ornament, and as a bride thou shalt put them about thee.€€ƒx¹For thy deserts, and thy desolate places, and the land of thy destruction shall now be too narrow by reason of the inhabitants, and they that swallowed thee up shall be chased far away.€€ƒxuThe children of thy barrenness shall still say in thy ears: The place is too strait for me, make me room to dwell in.€€ƒxÊAnd thou shalt say in thy heart: Who hath begotten these? I was barren and brought not forth, led away, and captive: and who hath brought up these? I was destitute and alone: and these, where were they?€€ƒxÍThus saith the Lord God: Behold I will lift up my hand to the Gentiles, and will set up my standard to the people. And they shall bring thy sons in their arms, and carry thy daughters upon their shoulders.€€ƒyAnd kings shall be thy nursing fathers, and queens thy nurses: they shall worship thee with their face toward the earth, and they shall lick up the dust of thy feet. And thou shalt know that I am the Lord, for they shall not be confounded that wait for him.€€ƒxaShall the prey be taken from the strong? or can that which was taken by the mighty, be delivered?€€ƒxäFor thus saith the Lord: Yea verily, even the captivity shall be taken away from the strong: and that which was taken by the mighty, shall be delivered. But I will judge those that have judged thee, and thy children I will save.€€ƒxİAnd I will feed thy enemies with their own flesh: and they shall be made drunk with their own blood, as with new wine: and all flesh shall know, that I am the Lord that save thee, and thy Redeemer the Mighty One of Jacob.€€ƒ`€€ƒxğThus saith the Lord: What is this bill of the divorce of your mother, with which I have put her away? or who is my creditor, to whom I sold you: behold you are sold for your iniquities, and for your wicked deeds have I put your mother away.€€ƒyXBecause I came, and there was not a man: I called, and there was none that would hear. Is my hand shortened and become little, that I cannot redeem? or is there no strength in me to deliver? Behold at my rebuke I will make the sea a desert, I will turn the rivers into dry land: the fishes shall rot for want of water, and shall die for thirst.€€ƒxPI will clothe the heavens with darkness, and will make sackcloth their covering.€€ƒxÄThe Lord hath given me a learned tongue, that I should know how to uphold by word him that is weary: he wakeneth in the morning, in the morning he wakeneth my ear, that I may hear him as a master.€€ƒxKThe Lord God hath opened my ear, and I do not resist: I have not gone back.€€ƒxšI have given my body to the strikers, and my cheeks to them that plucked them: I have not turned away my face from them that rebuked me, and spit upon me.€€ƒx–The Lord God is my helper, therefore am I not confounded: therefore have I set my face as a most hard rock, and I know that I shall not be confounded.€€ƒx}He is near that justifieth me, who will contend with me? let us stand together, who is my adversary? let him come near to me.€€ƒxŒBehold the Lord God is my helper: who is he that shall condemn me? Lo, they shall all be destroyed as a garment, the moth shall eat them up.€€ƒxÂWho is there among you that feareth the Lord, that heareth the voice of his servant, that hath walked in darkness, and hath no light? let him hope in the name of the Lord, and lean upon his God.€€ƒxÁBehold all you that kindle a fire, encompassed with flames, walk in the light of your fire, and in the flames which you have kindled: this is done to you by my hand, you shall sleep in sorrows.€€ƒ`€€ƒx®Give ear to me, you that follow that which is just, and you that seek the Lord: look unto the rock whence you are hewn, and to the hole of the pit from which you are dug out.€€ƒxvLook unto Abraham your father, and to Sara that bore you: for I called him alone, and blessed him, and multiplied him.€€ƒxÿThe Lord therefore will comfort Sion, and will comfort all the ruins thereof: and he will make her desert as a place of pleasure, and her wilderness as the garden of the Lord. Joy and gladness shall be found therein, thanksgiving, and the voice of praise.€€ƒx™Hearken unto me, O my people, and give ear to me, O my tribes: for a law shall go forth from me, and my judgment shall rest to be a light of the nations.€€ƒxMy just one is near at hand, my saviour is gone forth, and my arms shall judge the people: the islands shall look for me, and shall patiently wait for my arm.€€ƒyLift up your eyes to heaven, and look down to the earth beneath: for the heavens shall vanish like smoke, and the earth shall be worn away like a garment, and the inhabitants thereof shall perish in like manner: but my salvation shall be for ever, and my justice shall not fail.€€ƒxœHearken to me, you that know what is just, my people who have my law in your heart: fear ye not the reproach of men, and be not afraid of their blasphemies.€€ƒx§For the worm shall eat them up as a garment: and the moth shall consume them as wool: but my salvation shall be for ever, and my justice from generation to generation.€€ƒx«Arise, arise, put on strength, O thou arm of the Lord, arise as in the days of old, in the ancient generations. Hast not thou struck the proud one, and wounded the dragon?€€ƒxˆHast not thou dried up the sea, the water of the mighty deep, who madest the depth of the sea a way, that the delivered might pass over?€€ƒxÛAnd now they that are redeemed by the Lord, shall return, and shall come into Sion singing praises, and joy everlasting shall be upon their heads, they shall obtain joy and gladness, sorrow and mourning shall flee away.€€ƒxI myself will comfort you: who art thou, that thou shouldst be afraid of a mortal man, and of the son of man, who shall wither away like grass?€€ƒyAnd thou hast forgotten the Lord thy maker, who stretched out the heavens, and founded the earth: and thou hast been afraid continually all the day at the presence of his fury who afflicted thee, and had prepared himself to destroy thee: where is now the fury of the oppressor?€€˜ƒxjBut I am the Lord thy God, who trouble the sea, and the waves thereof swell: the Lord of hosts is my name.€€ƒx¼I have put my words in thy mouth, and have protected thee in the shadow of my hand, that thou mightest plant the heavens, and found the earth: and mightest say to Sion: Thou art my people.€€ƒxÇArise, arise, stand up, O Jerusalem, which hast drunk at the hand of the Lord the cup of his wrath; thou hast drunk even to the bottom of the cup of dead sleep, and thou hast drunk even to the dregs.€€ƒx´There is none that can uphold her among all the children that she hath brought forth: and there is none that taketh her by the hand among all the children that she hath brought up.€€ƒx¡There are two things that have happened to thee: who shall be sorry for thee? desolation, and destruction, and the famine, and the sword, who shall comfort thee?€€ƒx¨Thy children are cast forth, they have slept at the head of all the ways, and the wild ox that is snared: full of the indignation of the Lord, of the rebuke of thy God.€€ƒxUTherefore hear this, thou poor little one, and thou that art drunk but not with wine.€€ƒxÙThus saith thy Sovereign the Lord, and thy God, who will fight for his people: Behold I have taken out of thy hand the cup of dead sleep, the dregs of the cup of my indignation, thou shalt not drink it again any more.€€ƒxÉAnd I will put it in the hand of them that have oppressed thee, and have said to thy soul: Bow down, that we may go over: and thou hast laid thy body as the ground, and as a way to them that went over.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÂArise, arise, put on thy strength, O Sion, put on the garments of thy glory, O Jerusalem, the city of the Holy One: for henceforth the uncircumcised, and unclean shall no more pass through thee.€€ƒxwShake thyself from the dust, arise, sit up, O Jerusalem: loose the bonds from off thy neck, O captive daughter of Sion.€€ƒxXFor thus saith the Lord: You were sold gratis, and you shall be redeemed, without money.€€ƒxFor thus saith the Lord God: My people went down into Egypt at the beginning to sojourn there: and the Assyrian hath oppressed them without any cause at all.€€ƒxÃAnd now what have I here, saith the Lord: for my people is taken away gratis. They that rule over them treat them unjustly, saith the Lord, and my name is continually blasphemed all the day long.€€ƒx^Therefore my people shall know my name in that day: for I myself that spoke, behold I am here.€€ƒxÍHow beautiful upon the mountains are the feet of him that bringeth good tidings, and that preacheth peace: of him that sheweth forth good, that preacheth salvation, that saith to Sion: Thy God shall reign!€€ƒx—The voice of thy watchmen: they have lifted up their voice, they shall praise together: for they shall see eye to eye when the Lord shall convert Sion.€€ƒxRejoice, and give praise together, O ye deserts of Jerusalem: for the Lord hath comforted his people: he hath redeemed Jerusalem.€€ƒx‡The Lord hath prepared his holy arm in the sight of all the Gentiles: and all the ends of the earth shall see the salvation of our God.€€ƒxDepart, depart, go ye out from thence, touch no unclean thing: go out of the midst of her, be ye clean, you that carry the vessels of the Lord.€€ƒxFor you shall not go out in a tumult, neither shall you make haste by flight: for the Lord will go before you, and the God of Israel will gather you together.€€ƒxcBehold my servant shall understand, he shall be exalted, and extolled, and shall be exceeding high.€€ƒxvAs many have been astonished at thee, so shall his visage be inglorious among men, and his form among the sons of men.€€ƒxHe shall sprinkle many nations, kings shall shut their mouth at him: for they to whom it was not told of him, have seen: and they that heard not, have beheld.€€ƒ`€€”ƒxÛAnd he shall grow up as a tender plant before him, and as a root out of a thirsty ground: there is no beauty in him, nor comeliness: and we have seen him, and there was no sightliness, that we should be desirous of him:€€ƒx¦Despised, and the most abject of men, a man of sorrows, and acquainted with infirmity: and his look was as it were hidden and despised, whereupon we esteemed him not.€€ƒx‘Surely he hath borne our infirmities and carried our sorrows: and we have thought him as it were a leper, and as one struck by God and afflicted.€€ƒx‘But he was wounded for our iniquities, he was bruised for our sins: the chastisement of our peace was upon him, and by his bruises we are healed.€€ƒx‡All we like sheep have gone astray, every one hath turned aside into his own way: and the Lord hath laid on him the iniquity of us all.€€ƒxÆHe was offered because it was his own will, and he opened not his mouth: he shall be led as a sheep to the slaughter, and shall be dumb as a lamb before his shearer, and he shall not open his mouth.€€ƒx½He was taken away from distress, and from judgment: who shall declare his generation? because he is cut off out of the land of the living: for the wickedness of my people have I struck him.€€ƒx’And he shall give the ungodly for his burial, and the rich for his death: because he hath done no iniquity, neither was there deceit in his mouth.€€ƒx´And the Lord was pleased to bruise him in infirmity: if he shall lay down his life for sin, he shall see a longlived seed, and the will of the Lord shall be prosperous in his hand.€€ƒx™Because his soul hath laboured, he shall see and be filled: by his knowledge shall this my just servant justify many, and he shall bear their iniquities.€€ƒxöTherefore will I distribute to him very many, and he shall divide the spoils of the strong, because he hath delivered his soul unto death, and was reputed with the wicked: and he hath borne the sins of many, and hath prayed for the transgressors.€€ƒ`€€ƒxâGive praise, O thou barren, that bearest not: sing forth praise, and make a joyful noise, thou that didst not travail with child: for many are the children of the desolate, more than of her that hath a husband, saith the Lord.€€ƒx†Enlarge the place of thy tent, and stretch out the skins of thy tabernacles, spare not: lengthen thy cords, and strengthen thy stakes.€€ƒxŠFor thou shalt pass on to the right hand, and to the left: and thy seed shall inherit the Gentiles, and shall inhabit the desolate cities.€€ƒxÆFear not, for thou shalt not be confounded, nor blush: for thou shalt not be put to shame, because thou shalt forget the shame of thy youth, and shalt remember no more the reproach of thy widowhood.€€ƒxFor he that made thee shall rule over thee, the Lord of hosts is his name: and thy Redeemer, the Holy One of Israel, shall be called the God of all the earth.€€ƒx~For the Lord hath called thee as a woman forsaken and mourning in spirit, and as a wife cast off from her youth, said thy God.€€ƒxSFor a small moment have I forsaken thee, but with great mercies will I gather thee.€€ƒx›In a moment of indignation have I hid my face a little while from thee, but with everlasting kindness have I had mercy on thee, said the Lord thy Redeemer.€€˜ƒx¿For the mountains shall be moved, and the hills shall tremble; but my mercy shall not depart from thee, and the covenant of my peace shall not be moved: said the Lord that hath mercy on thee.€€ƒxO poor little one, tossed with tempest, without all comfort, behold I will lay thy stones in order, and will lay thy foundations with sapphires,€€ƒxpAnd I will make thy bulwarks of jasper: and thy gates of graven stones, and all thy borders of desirable stones.€€ƒx[All thy children shall be taught of the Lord: and great shall be the peace of thy children.€€ƒxŒAnd thou shalt be founded in justice: depart far from oppression, for thou shalt not fear; and from terror, for it shall not come near thee.€€ƒxvBehold, an inhabitant shall come, who was not with me, he that was a stranger to thee before, shall be joined to thee.€€ƒxBehold, I have created the smith that bloweth the coals in the fire, and bringeth forth an instrument for his work, and I have created the killer to destroy.€€ƒxÚNo weapon that is formed against thee shall prosper: and every tongue that resisteth thee in judgment, thou shalt condemn. This is the inheritance of the servants of the Lord, and their justice with me, saith the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAll you that thirst, come to the waters: and you that have no money make haste, buy, and eat: come ye, buy wine and milk without money, and without any price.€€ƒxËWhy do you spend money for that which is not bread, and your labour for that which doth not satisfy you? Hearken diligently to me, and eat that which is good, and your soul shall be delighted in fatness.€€ƒxIncline your ear and come to me: hear and your soul shall live, and I will make an everlasting covenant with you, the faithful mercies of David.€€ƒx_Behold I have given him for a witness to the people, for a leader and a master to the Gentiles.€€ƒxÇBehold thou shalt call a nation, which thou knewest not: and the nations that knew not thee shall run to thee, because of the Lord thy God, and for the Holy One of Israel, for he hath glorified thee.€€ƒxISeek ye the Lord, while he may be found: call upon him, while he is near.€€ƒx¯Let the wicked forsake his way, and the unjust man his thoughts, and let him return to the Lord, and he will have mercy on him, and to our God: for he is bountiful to forgive.€€ƒxMFor my thoughts are not your thoughts: nor your ways my ways, saith the Lord.€€ƒx|For as the heavens are exalted above the earth, so are my ways exalted above your ways, and my thoughts above your thoughts.€€ƒx¼And as the rain and the snow come down from heaven, and return no more thither, but soak the earth, and water it, and make it to spring, and give seed to the sower, and bread to the eater:€€ƒx²So shall my word be, which shall go forth from my mouth: it shall not return to me void, but it shall do whatsoever I please, and shall prosper in the things for which I sent it.€€ƒx®For you shall go out with joy, and be led forth with peace: the mountains and the hills shall sing praise before you, and all the trees of the country shall clap their hands.€€ƒx¾Instead of the shrub, shall come up the fir tree, and instead of the nettle, shall come up the myrtle tree: and the Lord shall be named for an everlasting sign, that shall not be taken away.€€ƒ`€€ƒxwThus saith the Lord: Keep ye judgment, and do justice: for my salvation is near to come, and my justice to be revealed.€€ƒxªBlessed is the man that doth this, and the son of man that shall lay hold on this: that keepeth the sabbath from profaning it, that keepeth his hands from doing any evil.€€ƒx¸And let not the son of the stranger, that adhereth to the Lord, speak, saying: The Lord will divide and separate me from his people. And let not the eunuch say: Behold I am a dry tree.€€ƒx–For thus saith the Lord to the eunuchs, They that shall keep my sabbaths, and shall choose the things that please me, and shall hold fast my covenant:€€˜ƒxÌAnd the children of the stranger that adhere to the Lord, to worship him, and to love his name, to be his servants: every one that keepeth the sabbath from profaning it, and that holdeth fast my covenant:€€ƒxÜI will bring them into my holy mount, and will make them joyful in my house of prayer: their holocausts, and their victims shall please me upon my altar: for my house shall be called the house of prayer, for all nations.€€ƒxjThe Lord God, who gathereth the scattered of Israel, saith: I will still gather unto him his congregation.€€ƒxGAll ye beasts of the field come to devour, all ye beasts of the forest.€€ƒx~His watchmen are all blind, they are all ignorant: dumb dogs not able to bark, seeing vain things, sleeping and loving dreams.€€ƒxÇAnd most impudent dogs, they never had enough: the shepherds themselves knew no understanding: all have turned aside into their own way, every one after his own gain, from the first even to the last.€€ƒxtCome, let us take wine, and be filled with drunkenness: and it shall be as to day, so also to morrow, and much more.€€ƒ`€€ƒxºThe just perisheth, and no man layeth it to heart, and men of mercy are taken away, because there is none that understandeth; for the just man is taken away from before the face of evil.€€ƒxLLet peace come, let him rest in his bed that hath walked in his uprightness.€€ƒx^But draw near hither, you sons of the sorceress, the seed of the adulterer, and of the harlot.€€ƒx‰Upon whom have you jested? upon whom have you opened your mouth wide, and put out your tongue? are not you wicked children, a false seed,€€ƒxrWho seek your comfort in idols under every green tree, sacrificing children in the torrents, under the high rocks?€€ƒx«In the parts of the torrent is thy portion, this is thy lot: and thou hast poured out libations to them, thou hast offered sacrifice. Shall I not be angry at these things?€€ƒxaUpon a high and lofty mountain thou hast laid thy bed, and hast gone up thither to offer victims.€€ƒxùAnd behind the door, and behind the post thou hast set up thy remembrance: for thou hast discovered thyself near me, and hast received an adulterer: thou hast enlarged thy bed, and made a covenant with them: thou hast loved their bed with open hand.€€ƒx¡And thou hast adorned thyself for the king with ointment, and hast multiplied thy perfumes. Thou hast sent thy messengers far off, and wast debased even to hell.€€ƒx–Thou hast been wearied in the multitude of thy ways: yet thou saidst not: I will rest: thou has found life of thy hand, therefore thou hast not asked.€€ƒxÌFor whom hast thou been solicitous and afraid, that thou hast lied, and hast not been mindful of me, nor thought on me in thy heart? for I am silent, and as one that seeth not, and thou hast forgotten me.€€ƒx@I will declare thy justice, and thy works shall not profit thee.€€ƒxØWhen thou shalt cry, let thy companies deliver thee, but the wind shall carry them all off, a breeze shall take them away, but he that putteth his trust in me, shall inherit the land, and shall possess my holy mount.€€ƒxAnd I will say: Make a way: give free passage, turn out of the path, take away the stumblingblocks out of the way of my people.€€ƒxôFor thus saith the High and the Eminent that inhabiteth eternity: and his name is Holy, who dwelleth in the high and holy place, and with a contrite and humble spirit, to revive the spirit of the humble, and to revive the heart of the contrite.€€ƒx’For I will not contend for ever, neither will I be angry unto the end: because the spirit shall go forth from my face, and breathings I will make.€€ƒx˜For the iniquity of his covetousness I was angry, and I struck him: I hid my face from thee, and was angry: and he went away wandering in his own heart.€€ƒxuI saw his ways, and I healed him, and brought him back, and restored comforts to him, and to them that mourn for him.€€ƒxI created the fruit of the lips, peace, peace to him that is far off, and to him that is near, said the Lord, and I healed him.€€ƒxgBut the wicked are like the raging sea, which cannot rest, and the waves thereof cast up dirt and mire.€€ƒx4There is no peace to the wicked, saith the Lord God.€€ƒ`€€ƒx|Cry, cease not, lift up thy voice like a trumpet, and shew my people their wicked doings, and the house of Jacob their sins.€€˜ƒxÄWhy have we fasted, and thou hast not regarded: have we humbled our souls, and thou hast not taken notice? Behold in the day of your fast your own will is found, and you exact of all your debtors.€€ƒxBehold you fast for debates and strife, and strike with the fist wickedly. Do not fast as you have done until this day, to make your cry to be heard on high.€€ƒxáIs this such a fast as I have chosen: for a man to afflict his soul for a day? is this it, to wind his head about like a circle, and to spread sackcloth and ashes? wilt thou call this a fast, and a day acceptable to the Lord?€€ƒx¯Is not this rather the fast that I have chosen? loose the bands of wickedness, undo the bundles that oppress, let them that are broken go free, and break asunder every burden.€€ƒxDeal thy bread to the hungry, and bring the needy and the harbourless into thy house: when thou shalt see one naked, cover him, and despise not thy own flesh.€€ƒx¯Then shall thy light break forth as the morning, and thy health shall speedily arise, and thy justice shall go before thy face, and the glory of the Lord shall gather thee up.€€ƒxâThen shalt thou call, and the Lord shall hear: thou shalt cry, and he shall say, Here I am. If thou wilt take away the chain out of the midst of thee, and cease to stretch out the finger, and to speak that which profiteth not.€€ƒxªWhen thou shalt pour out thy soul to the hungry, and shalt satisfy the afflicted soul, then shall thy light rise up in darkness, and thy darkness shall be as the noonday.€€ƒxÏAnd the Lord will give thee rest continually, and will fill thy soul with brightness, and deliver thy bones, and thou shalt be like a watered garden, and like a fountain of water whose waters shall not fail.€€ƒxÚAnd the places that have been desolate for ages shall be built in thee: thou shalt raise up the foundation of generation and generation: and thou shalt be called the repairer of the fences, turning the paths into rest.€€ƒxûIf thou turn away thy foot from the sabbath, from doing thy own will in my holy day, and call the sabbath delightful, and the holy of the Lord glorious, and glorify him, while thou dost not thy own ways, and thy own will is not found, to speak a word:€€ƒxÌThen shalt thou be delighted in the Lord, and I will lift thee up above the high places of the earth, and will feed thee with the inheritance of Jacob thy father. For the mouth of the Lord hath spoken it.€€ƒ`€€ƒxoBehold the hand of the Lord is not shortened that it cannot save, neither is his ear heavy that it cannot hear.€€ƒx|But your iniquities have divided between you and your God, and your sins have hid his face from you that he should not hear.€€ƒx…For your hands are defiled with blood, and your fingers with iniquity: your lips have spoken lies, and your tongue uttereth iniquity.€€ƒxÃThere is none that calleth upon justice, neither is there any one that judgeth truly: but they trust in a mere nothing, and speak vanities: they have conceived labour, and brought forth iniquity.€€ƒx³They have broken the eggs of asps, and have woven the webs of spiders: he that shall eat of their eggs, shall die: and that which is brought out, shall be hatched into a basilisk.€€ƒx«Their webs shall not be for clothing, neither shall they cover themselves with their works: their works are unprofitable works, and the work of iniquity is in their hands.€€ƒx“Their feet run to evil, and make haste to shed innocent blood: their thoughts are unprofitable thoughts: wasting and destruction are in their ways.€€ƒx¨They have not known the way of peace, and there is no judgment in their steps: their paths are become crooked to them, every one that treadeth in them knoweth no peace.€€ƒx›Therefore is judgment far from us, and justice shall not overtake us. We looked for light, and behold darkness: brightness, and we have walked in the dark.€€ƒx¤We have groped for the wall, and like the blind we have groped as if we had no eyes: we have stumbled at noonday as in darkness, we are in dark places, as dead men.€€ƒxWe shall roar all of us like bears, and shall lament as mournful doves. We have looked for judgment, and there is none: for salvation, and it is far from us.€€ƒx˜For our iniquities are multiplied before thee, and our sins have testified against us: for our wicked doings are with us, and have known our iniquities:€€˜ƒx‘And judgment is turned away backward, and justice hath stood far off: because truth hath fallen down in the street, and equity could not come in.€€ƒx§And truth hath been forgotten: and he that departed from evil, lay open to be a prey: and the Lord saw, and it appeared evil in his eyes, because there is no judgment.€€ƒx²And he saw that there is not a man: and he stood astonished, because there is none to oppose himself: and his own arm brought salvation to him, and his own justice supported him.€€ƒx™He put on justice as a breastplate, and a helmet of salvation upon his head: he put on the garments of vengeance, and was clad with zeal as with a cloak.€€ƒx‚As unto revenge, as it were to repay wrath to his adversaries, and a reward to his enemies: he will repay the like to the islands.€€ƒx¸And they from the west, shall fear the name of the Lord: and they from the rising of the sun, his glory when he shall come as a violent stream, which the spirit of the Lord driveth on:€€ƒxhAnd there shall come a redeemer to Sion, and to them that return from iniquity in Jacob, saith the Lord.€€ƒyThis is my covenant with them, saith the Lord: My spirit that is in thee, and my words that I have put in thy mouth, shall not depart out of thy mouth, nor out of the mouth of thy seed, nor out of the mouth of thy seed's seed, saith the Lord, from henceforth and for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒxhArise, be enlightened, O Jerusalem: for thy light is come, and the glory of the Lord is risen upon thee.€€ƒxŒFor behold darkness shall cover the earth, and a mist the people: but the Lord shall arise upon thee, and his glory shall be seen upon thee.€€ƒxTAnd the Gentiles shall walk in thy light, and kings in the brightness of thy rising.€€ƒxªLift up thy eyes round about, and see: all these are gathered together, they are come to thee: thy sons shall come from afar, and thy daughters shall rise up at thy side.€€ƒx·Then shalt thou see, and abound, and thy heart shall wonder and be enlarged, when the multitude of the sea shall be converted to thee, the strength of the Gentiles shall come to thee.€€ƒx²The multitude of camels shall cover thee, the dromedaries of Madian and Epha: all they from Saba shall come, bringing gold and frankincense: and shewing forth praise to the Lord.€€ƒxÆAll the flocks of Cedar shall be gathered together unto thee, the rams of Nabaioth shall minister to thee: they shall be offered upon my acceptable altar, and I will glorify the house of my majesty.€€ƒxAWho are these, that fly as clouds, and as doves to their windows?€€ƒxõFor, the islands wait for me, and the ships of the sea in the beginning: that I may bring thy sons from afar: their silver, and their gold with them, to the name of the Lord thy God, and to the Holy One of Israel, because he hath glorified thee.€€ƒx¸And the children of strangers shall build up thy walls, and their kings shall minister to thee: for in my wrath have I struck thee, and in my reconciliation have I had mercy upon thee.€€ƒx¨And thy gates shall be open continually: they shall not be shut day nor night, that the strength of the Gentiles may be brought to thee, and their kings may be brought.€€ƒxxFor the nation and the kingdom that will not serve thee, shall perish: and the Gentiles shall be wasted with desolation.€€ƒx´The glory of Libanus shall come to thee, the fir tree, and the box tree, and the pine tree together, to beautify the place of my sanctuary: and I will glorify the place of my feet.€€ƒxÚAnd the children of them that afflict thee, shall come bowing down to thee, and all that slandered thee shall worship the steps of thy feet, and shall call thee the city of the Lord, the Sion of the Holy One of Israel.€€ƒx¦Because thou wast forsaken, and hated, and there was none that passed through thee, I will make thee to be an everlasting glory, a joy unto generation and generation:€€—ƒx¨For brass I will bring gold, and for iron I will bring silver: and for wood brass, and for stones iron: and I will make thy visitation peace, and thy overseers justice.€€ƒx‘Iniquity shall no more be heard in thy land, wasting nor destruction in thy borders, and salvation shall possess thy walls, and praise thy gates.€€ƒxÃThou shalt no more have the sun for thy light by day, neither shall the brightness of the moon enlighten thee: but the Lord shall be unto thee for an everlasting light, and thy God for thy glory.€€ƒx¦Thy sun shall go down no more, and thy moon shall not decrease: for the Lord shall be unto thee for an everlasting light, and the days of thy mourning shall be ended.€€ƒx…And thy people shall be all just, they shall inherit the land for ever, the branch of my planting, the work of my hand to glorify me.€€ƒx}The least shall become a thousand, and a little one a most strong nation: I the Lord will suddenly do this thing in its time.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŞThe spirit of the Lord is upon me, because the Lord hath anointed me: he hath sent me to preach to the meek, to heal the contrite of heart, and to preach a release to the captives, and deliverance to them that are shut up.€€ƒxlTo proclaim the acceptable year of the Lord, and the day of vengeance of our God: to comfort all that mourn:€€ƒxõTo appoint to the mourners of Sion, and to give them a crown for ashes, the oil of joy for mourning, a garment of praise for the spirit of grief: and they shall be called in it the mighty ones of justice, the planting of the Lord to glorify him.€€ƒx¼And they shall build the places that have been waste from of old, and shall raise up ancient ruins, and shall repair the desolate cities, that were destroyed for generation and generation.€€ƒx‰And strangers shall stand and shall feed your flocks: and the sons of strangers shall be your husbandman, and the dressers of your vines.€€ƒx»But you shall be called the priests of the Lord: to you it shall be said: Ye ministers of our God: you shall eat the riches of the Gentiles, and you shall pride yourselves in their glory.€€ƒx™For your double confusion and shame, they shall praise their part: therefore shall they receive double in their land, everlasting joy shall be unto them.€€ƒx›For I am the Lord that love judgment, and hate robbery in a holocaust: and I will make their work in truth, and I will make a perpetual covenant with them.€€ƒx¾And they shall know their seed among the Gentiles, and their offspring in the midst of peoples: all that shall see them, shall know them, that these are the seed which the Lord hath blessed.€€ƒxÿI will greatly rejoice in the Lord, and my soul shall be joyful in my God: for he hath clothed me with the garments of salvation: and with the robe of justice he hath covered me, as a bridegroom decked with a crown, and as a bride adorned with her jewels.€€ƒx²For as the earth bringeth forth her bud, and as the garden causeth her seed to shoot forth: so shall the Lord God make justice to spring forth, and praise before all the nations.€€ƒ`€€ƒxªFor Sion's sake I will not hold my peace, and for the sake of Jerusalem, I will not rest till her just one come forth as brightness, and her saviour be lighted as a lamp.€€ƒx˜And the Gentiles shall see thy just one, and all kings thy glorious one: and thou shalt be called by a new name, which the mouth of the Lord shall name.€€ƒxfAnd thou shalt be a crown of glory in the hand of the Lord, and a royal diadem in the hand of thy God.€€ƒxîThou shalt no more be called Forsaken: and thy land shall no more be called Desolate: but thou shalt be called My pleasure in her, and thy land inhabited. Because the Lord hath been well pleased with thee: and thy land shall be inhabited.€€˜#ƒx°Upon thy walls, O Jerusalem, I have appointed watchmen all the day, and all the night, they shall never hold their peace. You that are mindful of the Lord, hold not your peace,€€ƒx\And give him no silence till he establish, and till he make Jerusalem a praise in the earth.€€ƒxÜThe Lord hath sworn by his right hand, and by the arm of his strength: Surely I will no more give thy corn to be meat for thy enemies: and the sons of the strangers shall not drink thy wine, for which thou hast laboured.€€ƒx„For they that gather it, shall eat it, and shall praise the Lord: and they that bring it together, shall drink it in my holy courts.€€ƒx“Go through, go through the gates, prepare the way for the people, make the road plain, pick out the stones, and lift up the standard to the people.€€ƒx°Behold the Lord hath made it to be heard in the ends of the earth, tell the daughter of Sion: Behold thy Saviour cometh: behold his reward is with him, and his work before him.€€ƒx…And they shall call them, The holy people, the redeemed of the Lord. But thou shalt be called: A city sought after, and not forsaken.€€ƒ`€€ƒx½Who is this that cometh from Edom, with dyed garments from Bosra, this beautiful one in his robe, walking in the greatness of his strength. I, that speak justice, and am a defender to save.€€ƒxVWhy then is thy apparel red, and thy garments like theirs that tread in the winepress?€€ƒxøI have trodden the winepress alone, and of the Gentiles there is not a man with me: I have trampled on them in my indignation, and have trodden them down in my wrath, and their blood is sprinkled upon my garments, and I have stained all my apparel.€€ƒxKFor the day of vengeance is in my heart, the year of my redemption is come.€€ƒx¡I looked about, and there was none to help: I sought, and there was none to give aid: and my own arm hath saved for me, and my indignation itself hath helped me.€€ƒxAnd I have trodden down the people in my wrath, and have made them drunk in my indignation, and have brought down their strength to the earth.€€ƒy"I will remember the tender mercies of the Lord, the praise of the Lord for all the things that the Lord hath bestowed upon us, and for the multitude of his good things to the house of Israel, which he hath given them according to his kindness, and according to the multitude of his mercies.€€ƒx`And he said: Surely they are my people, children that will not deny: so he became their saviour.€€ƒxÂIn all their affliction he was not troubled, and the angel of his presence saved them: in his love, and in his mercy he redeemed them, and he carried them and lifted them up all the days of old.€€ƒx†But they provoked to wrath, and afflicted the spirit of his Holy One: and he was turned to be their enemy, and he fought against them.€€ƒxÖAnd he remembered the days of old of Moses, and of his people: Where is he that brought them up out of the sea, with the shepherds of his flock? where is he that put in the midst of them the spirit of his Holy One?€€ƒx‘He that brought out Moses by the right hand, by the arm of his majesty: that divided the waters before them, to make himself an everlasting name.€€ƒxWHe that led them out through the deep, as a horse in the wilderness that stumbleth not.€€ƒxAs a beast that goeth down in the field, the spirit of the Lord was their leader: so didst thou lead thy people to make thyself a glorious name.€€ƒxÔLook down from heaven, and behold from thy holy habitation and the place of thy glory: where is thy zeal, and thy strength, the multitude of thy bowels, and of thy mercies? they have held back themselves from me.€€ƒx¦For thou art our father, and Abraham hath not known us, and Israel hath been ignorant of us: thou, O Lord, art our father, our redeemer, from everlasting is thy name.€€ƒx¸Why hast thou made us to err, O Lord, from thy ways: why hast thou hardened our heart, that we should not fear thee? return for the sake of thy servants, the tribes of thy inheritance.€€ƒx\They have possessed thy holy people as nothing: our enemies have trodden down thy sanctuary.€€ƒxmWe are become as in the beginning, when thou didst not rule over us, and when we were not called by thy name.€€ƒ`€€ƒxkO that thou wouldst rend the heavens, and wouldst come down: the mountains would melt away at thy presence.€€ƒx­They would melt as at the burning of fire, the waters would burn with fire, that thy name might be made known to thy enemies: that the nations might tremble at thy presence.€€ƒxWhen thou shalt do wonderful things, we shall not bear them: thou didst come down, and at thy presence the mountains melted away.€€ƒx¹From the beginning of the world they have not heard, nor perceived with the ears: the eye hath not seen, O God, besides thee, what things thou hast prepared for them that wait for thee.€€ƒx¸Thou hast met him that rejoiceth, and doth justice: in thy ways they shall remember thee: behold thou art angry, and we have sinned: in them we have been always, and we shall be saved.€€ƒxµAnd we are all become as one unclean, and all our justices as the rag of a menstruous woman: and we have all fallen as a leaf, and our iniquities, like the wind, have taken us away.€€ƒx£There is none that calleth upon thy name: that riseth up, and taketh hold of thee: thou hast hid thy face from us, and hast crushed us in the hand of our iniquity.€€cDan¤dnamefDanieliosis_namecDanlabbreviationcDanhchaptersŒ•ƒx[The city of thy sanctuary is become a desert, Sion is made a desert, Jerusalem is desolate.€€ƒx‘The house of our holiness, and of our glory, where our fathers praised thee, is burnt with fire, and all our lovely things are turned into ruins.€€ƒxjWilt thou refrain thyself, O Lord, upon these things, wilt thou hold thy peace, and afflict us vehemently?€€ƒ`€€ƒx¢They have sought me that before asked not for me, they have found me that sought me not. I said: Behold me, behold me, to a nation that did not call upon my name.€€ƒxI have spread forth my hands all the day to an unbelieving people, who walk in a way that is not good after their own thoughts.€€ƒxrA people that continually provoke me to anger before my face, that immolate in gardens, and sacrifice upon bricks.€€ƒxzThat dwell in sepulchres, and sleep in the temple of idols: that eat swine's flesh, and profane broth is in their vessels.€€ƒxƒThat say: Depart from me, come not near me, because thou art unclean: these shall be smoke in my anger, a fire burning all the day.€€ƒxcBehold it is written before me: I will not be silent, but I will render and repay into their bosom.€€ƒxÕYour iniquities, and the iniquities of your fathers together, saith the Lord, who have sacrificed upon the mountains, and have reproached me upon the hills; and I will measure back their first work in their bosom.€€ƒx¿Thus saith the Lord: As if a grain be found in a cluster, and it be said: Destroy it not, because it is a blessing: so will I do for the sake of my servants, that I may not destroy the whole.€€ƒxšAnd I will bring forth a seed out of Jacob, and out of Juda a possessor of my mountains: and my elect shall inherit it, and my servants shall dwell there.€€ƒx˜And the plains shall be turned to folds of flocks, and the valley of Achor into a place for the herds to lie down in, for my people that have sought me.€€ƒxƒAnd you, that have forsaken the Lord, that have forgotten my holy mount, that set a table for fortune, and offer libations upon it,€€ƒxÛI will number you in the sword, and you shall all fall by slaughter: because I called and you did not answer: I spoke, and you did not hear: and you did evil in my eyes, and you have chosen the things that displease me.€€ƒx“Therefore thus saith the Lord God: Behold my servants shall eat, and you shall be hungry: behold my servants shall drink, and you shall be thirsty.€€ƒxÂBehold my servants shall rejoice, and you shall be confounded: behold my servants shall praise for joyfulness of heart, and you shall cry for sorrow of heart, and shall howl for grief of spirit.€€ƒx…And you shall leave your name for an execration to my elect: and the Lord God shall slay thee, and call his servants by another name.€€ƒxÛIn which he that is blessed upon the earth, shall be blessed in God, amen: and he that sweareth in the earth, shall swear by God, amen: because the former distresses are forgotten, and because they are hid from my eyes.€€ƒxŒFor behold I create new heavens, and a new earth: and the former things shall not be in remembrance, and they shall not come upon the heart.€€˜1ƒx‡And I will rejoice in Jerusalem, and joy in my people, and the voice of weeping shall no more be heard in her, nor the voice of crying.€€ƒxÈThere shall no more be an infant of days there, nor an old man that shall not fill up his days: for the child shall die a hundred years old, and the sinner being a hundred years old shall be accursed.€€ƒxjAnd they shall build houses, and inhabit them; and they shall plant vineyards, and eat the fruits of them.€€ƒxÊThey shall not build, and another inhabit; they shall not plant, and another eat: for as the days of a tree, so shall be the days of my people, and the works of their hands shall be of long continuance.€€ƒxMy elect shall not labour in vain, nor bring forth in trouble; for they are the seed of the blessed of the Lord, and their posterity with them.€€ƒxeAnd it shall come to pass, that before they call, I will hear; as they are yet speaking, I will hear.€€ƒx»The wolf and the lamb shall feed together; the lion and the ox shall eat straw; and dust shall be the serpent's food: they shall not hurt nor kill in all my holy mountain, saith the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx–Thus saith the Lord: Heaven is my throne, and the earth my footstool: what is this house that you will build to me? and what is this place of my rest?€€ƒxÎMy hand made all these things, and all these things were made, saith the Lord. But to whom shall I have respect, but to him that is poor and little, and of a contrite spirit, and that trembleth at my words?€€ƒyXHe that sacrificeth an ox, is as if he slew a man: he that killeth a sheep in sacrifice, as if he should brain a dog: he that offereth an oblation, as if he should offer swine's blood; he that remembereth incense, as if he should bless an idol. All these things have they chosen in their ways, and their soul is delighted in their abominations.€€ƒyWherefore I also will choose their mockeries, and will bring upon them the things they feared: because I called, and there was none that would answer; I have spoken, and they heard not; and they have done evil in my eyes, and have chosen the things that displease me.€€ƒxÜHear the word of the Lord, you that tremble at his word: Your brethren that hate you, and cast you out for my name's sake, have said: Let the Lord be glorified, and we shall see in your joy: but they shall be confounded.€€ƒx}A voice of the people from the city, a voice from the temple, the voice of the Lord that rendereth recompense to his enemies.€€ƒxqBefore she was in labour, she brought forth; before her time came to be delivered, she brought forth a man child.€€ƒxàWho hath ever heard such a thing? and who hath seen the like to this? shall the earth bring forth in one day? or shall a nation be brought forth at once, because Sion hath been in labour, and hath brought forth her children?€€ƒx¥Shall not I that make others to bring forth children, myself bring forth, saith the Lord? shall I, that give generation to others, be barren, saith the Lord thy God?€€ƒxzRejoice with Jerusalem, and be glad with her, all you that love her: rejoice for joy with her, all you that mourn for her.€€ƒx–That you may suck, and be filled with the breasts of her consolations: that you may milk out, and flow with delights, from the abundance of her glory.€€ƒxğFor thus saith the Lord: Behold I will bring upon her as it were a river of peace, and as an overflowing torrent the glory of the Gentiles, which you shall suck; you shall be carried at the breasts, and upon the knees they shall caress you.€€ƒxaAs one whom the mother caresseth, so will I comfort you, and you shall be comforted in Jerusalem.€€ƒx¸You shall see and your heart shall rejoice, and your bones shall flourish like an herb, and the hand of the Lord shall be known to his servants, and he shall be angry with his enemies.€€ƒx—For behold the Lord will come with fire, and his chariots are like a whirlwind, to render his wrath in indignation, and his rebuke with flames of fire.€€ƒxkFor the Lord shall judge by fire, and by his sword unto all flesh, and the slain of the Lord shall be many.€€ƒxĞThey that were sanctified, thought themselves clean in the gardens behind the gate within, they that did eat swine's flesh, and the abomination, and the mouse: they shall be consumed together, saith the Lord.€€ƒxœBut I know their works, and their thoughts: I come that I may gather them together with all nations and tongues: and they shall come and shall see my glory.€€ƒy<And I will set a sign among them, and I will send of them that shall be saved, to the Gentiles into the sea, into Africa, and Lydia them that draw the bow: into Italy, and Greece, to the islands afar off, to them that have not heard of me, and have not seen my glory. And they shall declare my glory to the Gentiles:€€ƒy0And they shall bring all your brethren out of all nations for a gift to the Lord, upon horses, and in chariots, and in litters, and on mules, and in coaches, to my holy mountain Jerusalem, saith the Lord, as if the children of Israel should bring an offering in a clean vessel into the house of the Lord.€€ƒxCAnd I will take of them to be priests, and Levites, saith the Lord.€€ƒx‰For as the new heavens, and the new earth, which I will make to stand before me, saith the Lord: so shall your seed stand, and your name.€€ƒx‚And there shall be month after month, and sabbath after sabbath: and all flesh shall come to adore before my face, saith the Lord.€€ƒxÌAnd they shall go out, and see the carcasses of the men that have transgressed against me: their worm shall not die, and their fire shall not be quenched: and they shall be a loathsome sight to all flesh.€€ƒxThe Prophecy of Jeremiah€€ƒ`€€ƒxhThe words of Jeremiah the son of Helcias, of the priests that were in Anathoth, in the land of Benjamin.€€ƒxThe word of the Lord which came to him in the days of Josias the son of Amon king of Juda, in the thirteenth year of his reign.€€ƒxßAnd which came to him in the days of Joakim the son of Josias king of Juda, unto the end of the eleventh year of Sedecias the son of Josias king of Juda, even unto the carrying away of Jerusalem captive, in the fifth month.€€ƒx,And the word of the Lord came to me, saying:€€ƒx©Before I formed thee in the bowels of thy mother, I knew thee: and before thou camest forth out of the womb, I sanctified thee, and made thee a prophet unto the nations.€€ƒxKAnd I said: Ah, ah, ah, Lord God: behold, I cannot speak, for I am a child.€€ƒx—And the Lord said to me: Say not: I am a child: for thou shalt go to all that I shall send thee: and whatsoever I shall command thee, thou shalt speak.€€ƒxTBe not afraid at their presence: for I am with thee to deliver thee, saith the Lord.€€ƒxzAnd the Lord put forth his hand, and touched my mouth: and the Lord said to me: Behold I have given my words in thy mouth:€€ƒx™Lo, I have set thee this day over the nations, and over kingdoms, to root up, and to pull down, and to waste, and to destroy, and to build, and to plant.€€ƒxiAnd the word of the Lord came to me, saying: What seest thou, Jeremiah? And I said: I see a rod watching.€€ƒxZAnd the Lord said to me: Thou hast seen well: for I will watch over my word to perform it.€€ƒx And the word of the Lord came to me a second time saying: What seest thou? And I said: I see a boiling caldron, and the face thereof from the face of the north.€€ƒxgAnd the Lord said to me: From the north shall an evil break forth upon all the inhabitants of the land.€€ƒyFor behold I will call together all the families of the kingdoms of the north, saith the Lord: and they shall come, and shall set every one his throne in the entrance of the gates of Jerusalem, and upon all the walls thereof round about, and upon all the cities of Juda.€€˜ƒx¬Thou therefore gird up thy loins, and arise, and speak to them all that I command thee. Be not afraid at their presence: for I will make thee not to fear their countenance.€€ƒxÕFor behold I have made thee this day a fortified city, and a pillar of iron, and a wall of brass, over all the land, to the kings of Juda, to the princes thereof, and to the priests, and to the people of the land.€€ƒxnAnd they shall fight against them, and shall not prevail: for I am with thee, saith the Lord, to deliver thee.€€ƒ`€€ƒx,And the word of the Lord came to me, saying:€€ƒxÏGo, and cry in the ears of Jerusalem, saying: Thus saith the Lord: I have remembered thee, pitying thy youth, and the love of thy espousals, when thou followedst me in the desert, in a land that is not sown.€€ƒx‰Israel is holy to the Lord, the firstfruits of his increase: all they that devour him offend: evils shall come upon them, saith the Lord.€€ƒx[Hear ye the word of the Lord, O house of Jacob, and all ye families of the house of Israel:€€ƒx”Thus saith the Lord: What iniquity have your fathers found in me, that they are gone far from me, and have walked after vanity, and are become vain?€€ƒyAnd they have not said: Where is the Lord, that made us come up out of the land of Egypt? that led us through the desert, through a land uninhabited and unpassable, through a land of drought, and the image of death, through a land wherein no man walked, nor any man dwelt?€€ƒxµAnd I brought you into the land of Carmel, to eat the fruit thereof, and the best things thereof: and when ye entered in, you defiled my land and made my inheritance an abomination.€€ƒxµThe priests did not say: Where is the Lord? and they that held the law knew me not, and the pastors transgressed against me: and the prophets prophesied in Baal, and followed idols.€€ƒxgTherefore will I yet contend in judgment with you, saith the Lord, and I will plead with your children.€€ƒxPass over to the isles of Cethim, and see: and send into Cedar, and consider diligently: and see if there hath been done any thing like this.€€ƒxwIf a nation hath changed their gods, and indeed they are not gods: but my people have changed their glory into an idol.€€ƒx]Be astonished, O ye heavens, at this, and ye gates thereof, be very desolate, saith the Lord.€€ƒx¨For my people have done two evils. They have forsaken me, the fountain of living water, and have digged to themselves cisterns, broken cisterns, that can hold no water.€€ƒxGIs Israel a bondman, or a homeborn slave? why then is he become a prey?€€ƒx›The lions have roared upon him, and have made a noise, they have made his land a wilderness: his cities are burnt down, and there is none to dwell in them.€€ƒx`The children also of Memphis, and of Taphnes have defloured thee, even to the crown of the head.€€ƒxwHath not this been done to thee, because thou hast forsaken the Lord thy God at that time, when he led thee by the way?€€ƒx§And now what hast thou to do in the way of Egypt, to drink the troubled water? And what hast thou to do with the way of the Assyrians, to drink the water of the river?€€ƒxğThy own wickedness shall reprove thee, and thy apostasy shall rebuke thee. Know thou, and see that it is an evil and a bitter thing for thee, to have left the Lord thy God, and that my fear is not with thee, saith the Lord the God of hosts.€€ƒx´Of old time thou hast broken my yoke, thou hast burst my bands, and thou saidst: I will not serve. For on every high hill, and under every green tree thou didst prostitute thyself.€€ƒxYet, I planted thee a chosen vineyard, all true seed: how then art thou turned unto me into that which is good for nothing, O strange vineyard?€€ƒxThough thou wash thyself with nitre, and multiply to thyself the herb borith, thou art stained in thy iniquity before me, saith the Lord God.€€ƒx£How canst thou say: I am not polluted, I have not walked after Baalim? see thy ways in the valley, know what thou hast done: as a swift runner pursuing his course.€€ƒxÊA wild ass accustomed to the wilderness in the desire of his heart, snuffed up the wind of his love: none shall turn her away: all that seek her shall not fail: in her monthly filth they shall find her.€€ƒx«Keep thy foot from being bare, and thy throat from thirst. But thou saidst: I have lost all hope, I will not do it: for I have loved strangers, and I will walk after them.€€ƒxAs the thief is confounded when he is taken, so is the house of Israel confounded, they and their kings, their princes and their priests, and their prophets.€€˜%ƒx­Where are the gods, whom thou hast made thee? let them arise and deliver thee in the time of thy affliction: for according to the number of thy cities were thy gods, O Juda.€€ƒxSWhy will you contend with me in judgment? you have all forsaken me, saith the Lord.€€ƒx˜In vain have I struck your children, they have not received correction: your sword hath devoured your prophets, your generation is like a ravaging lion.€€ƒx«See ye the word of the Lord: Am I become a wilderness to Israel, or a lateward springing land? why then have my people said: We are revolted, we will come to thee no more?€€ƒxqWill a virgin forget her ornament, or a bride her stomacher? but my people hath forgotten me days without number.€€ƒxsWhy dost thou endeavour to shew thy way good to seek my love, thou who hast also taught thy malices to be thy ways,€€ƒxšAnd in thy skirts is found the blood of the souls of the poor and innocent? not in ditches have I found them, but in all places, which I mentioned before.€€ƒxÂAnd thou hast said: I am without sin and am innocent: and therefore let thy anger be turned away from me. Behold, I will contend with thee in judgment, because thou hast said: I have not sinned.€€ƒxˆHow exceeding base art thou become, going the same ways over again! and thou shalt be ashamed of Egypt, as thou wast ashamed of Assyria.€€ƒxšFor from thence thou shalt go, and thy hand shall be upon thy head: for the Lord hath destroyed thy trust, and thou shalt have nothing prosperous therein.€€ƒ`€€ƒy"It is commonly said: If a man put away his wife, and she go from him, and marry another man, shall he return to her any more? shall not that woman be polluted, and defiled? but thou hast prostituted thyself to many lovers: nevertheless return to me, saith the Lord, and I will receive thee.€€ƒxæLift up thy eyes on high: and see where thou hast not prostituted thyself: thou didst sit in the ways, waiting for them as a robber in the wilderness: and thou hast polluted the land with thy fornications, and with thy wickedness.€€ƒx~Therefore the showers were withholden, and there was no lateward rain: thou hadst a harlot's forehead, thou wouldst not blush.€€ƒx`Therefore at the least from this time call to me: Thou art my father, the guide of my virginity:€€ƒx‰Wilt thou be angry for ever, or wilt thou continue unto the end? Behold, thou hast spoken, and hast done evil things, and hast been able.€€ƒxÕAnd the Lord said to me in the days of king Josias: Hast thou seen what rebellious Israel hath done? she hath gone of herself upon every high mountain, and under every green tree, and hath played the harlot there.€€ƒxzAnd when she had done all these things, I said: Return to me, and she did not return. And her treacherous sister Juda saw,€€ƒxËThat because the rebellious Israel had played the harlot, I had put her away, and given her a bill of divorce: yet her treacherous sister Juda was not afraid, but went and played the harlot also herself.€€ƒxoAnd by the facility of her fornication she defiled the land, and played the harlot with stones and with stocks.€€ƒxAnd after all this, her treacherous sister Juda hath not returned to me with her whole heart, but with falsehood, saith the Lord.€€ƒxnAnd the Lord said to me: The rebellious Israel hath justified her soul, in comparison of the treacherous Juda.€€ƒxÜGo, and proclaim these words towards the north, and thou shalt say: Return, O rebellious Israel, saith the Lord, and I will not turn away my face from you: for I am holy, saith the Lord, and I will not be angry for ever.€€ƒxÊBut yet acknowledge thy iniquity, that thou hast transgressed against the Lord thy God: and thou hast scattered thy ways to strangers under every green tree, and hast not heard my voice, saith the Lord.€€ƒx¡Return, O ye revolting children, saith the Lord: for I am your I husband: and I will take you, one of a city, and two of a kindred, and will bring you into Sion.€€ƒxkAnd I will give you pastors according to my own heart, and they shall feed you with knowledge and doctrine.€€ƒyAnd when you shall be multiplied, and increase in the land in those days, saith the Lord, they shall say no more: The ark of the covenant of the Lord: neither shall it come upon the heart, neither shall they remember it, neither shall it be visited, neither shall that be done any more.€€ƒxãAt that time Jerusalem shall be called the throne of the Lord: and all the nations shall be gathered together to it, in the name of the Lord to Jerusalem, and they shall not walk after the perversity of their most wicked heart.€€ƒx¤In those days the house of Juda shall go to the house of Israel, and they shall come together out of the land of the north to the land which I gave to your fathers.€€ƒxÓBut I said: How shall I put thee among the children, and give thee a lovely land, the goodly inheritance of the armies of the Gentiles? And I said: Thou shalt call me father and shalt not cease to walk after me.€€ƒxaBut as a woman that despiseth her lover, so hath the house of Israel despised me, saith the Lord.€€ƒx¢A voice was heard in the highways, weeping and howling of the children of Israel: because they have made their way wicked, they have forgotten the Lord their God.€€ƒxxReturn, you rebellious children, and I will heal your rebellions. Behold we come to thee: for thou art the Lord our God.€€ƒx|In very deed the hills were liars, and the multitude of the mountains: truly in the Lord our God is the salvation of Israel.€€ƒxConfusion hath devoured the labour of our fathers from our youth, their flocks and their herds, their sons and their daughters.€€ƒxßWe shall sleep in our confusion, and our shame shall cover us, because we have sinned against the Lord our God, we and our fathers from our youth even to this day, and we have not hearkened to the voice of the Lord our God.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒxŒAnd thou shalt swear: As the Lord liveth, in truth, and in judgment, and in justice: and the Gentiles shall bless him, and shall praise him.€€ƒxtFor thus saith the Lord to the men of Juda and Jerusalem: Break up anew your fallow ground, and sow not upon thorns:€€ƒxıBe circumcised to the Lord, and take away the foreskins of your hearts, ye men of Juda, and ye inhabitants of Jerusalem: lest my indignation come forth like fire, and burn, and there be none that can quench it because of the wickedness of your thoughts.€€ƒx­Declare ye in Juda, and make it heard in Jerusalem: speak, and sound with the trumpet in the land: cry aloud, and say: Assemble yourselves, and let us go into strong cities.€€ƒxuSet up the standard in Sion. Strengthen yourselves, stay not: for I bring evil from the north, and great destruction.€€ƒxÑThe lion is come up out of his den, and the robber of nations hath roused himself: he is come forth out of his place, to make thy land desolate: thy cities shall be laid waste, remaining without an inhabitant.€€ƒxvFor this gird yourselves with haircloth, lament and howl: for the fierce anger of the Lord is not turned away from us.€€ƒxÄAnd it shall come to pass in that day, saith the Lord: That the heart of the king shall perish, and the heart of the princes: and the priests shall be astonished, and the prophets shall be amazed.€€ƒxªAnd I said: Alas, alas, alas, O Lord God, hast thou then deceived this people and Jerusalem, saying: You shall have peace: and behold the sword reacheth even to the soul?€€ƒx¹At that time it shall be said to this people, and to Jerusalem: A burning wind is in the ways that are in the desert of the way of the daughter of my people, not to fan, nor to cleanse.€€ƒx\A full wind from these places shall come to me: and now I will speak my judgments with them.€€ƒxŠBehold he shall come up as a cloud, and his chariots as a tempest: his horses are swifter than eagles: woe unto us, for we are laid waste.€€ƒxuWash thy heart from wickedness, O Jerusalem, that thou mayst be saved: how long shall hurtful thoughts abide in thee?€€ƒxXFor a voice of one declaring from Dan, and giving notice of the idol from mount Ephraim.€€ƒx—Say ye to the nations: Behold it is heard in Jerusalem, that guards are coming from a far country, and give out their voice against the cities of Juda.€€ƒxjThey are set round about her, as keepers of fields: because she hath provoked me to wrath, saith the Lord.€€ƒxThy ways, and thy devices have brought these things upon thee: this is thy wickedness, because it is bitter, because it hath touched thy heart.€€ƒx®My bowels, my bowels are in part, the senses of my heart are troubled within me, I will not hold my peace, for my soul hath heard the sound of the trumpet, the cry of battle.€€ƒxDestruction upon destruction is called for, and all the earth is laid waste: my tents are destroyed on a sudden, and my pavilions in a moment.€€ƒxVHow long shall I see men fleeing away, how long shall I hear the sound of the trumpet?€€ƒx’For my foolish people have not known me: they are foolish and senseless children: they are wise to do evil, but to do good they have no knowledge.€€ƒxeI beheld the earth, and lo it was void, and nothing: and the heavens, and there was no light in them.€€ƒxWI looked upon the mountains, and behold they trembled: and all the hills were troubled.€€ƒxJI beheld, and lo there was no man: and all the birds of the air were gone.€€ƒx¡I looked, and behold Carmel was a wilderness: and all its cities were destroyed at the presence of the Lord, and at the presence of the wrath of his indignation.€€ƒx\For thus saith the Lord: All the land shall be desolate, but yet I will not utterly destroy.€€ƒx¢The Earth shall mourn, and the heavens shall lament from above: because I have spoken, I have purposed, and I have not repented, neither am I turned away from it.€€ƒxÆAt the voice of the horsemen, and the archers, all the city is fled away: they have entered into thickets and climbed up the rocks: all the cities are forsaken, and there dwelleth not a man in them.€€ƒyBut when thou art spoiled what wilt thou do? though thou clothest thyself with scarlet, though thou deckest thee with ornaments of gold, and paintest thy eyes with stibic stone, thou shalt dress thyself out in vain: thy lovers have despised thee, they will seek thy life.€€ƒxêFor I have heard the voice as of a woman in travail, anguishes as of a woman in labour of a child. The voice of the daughter of Sion, dying away, spreading her hands: Woe is me, for my soul hath fainted because of them that are slain.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒxHAnd though they say: The Lord liveth; this also they will swear falsely.€€ƒxéO Lord, thy eyes are upon truth: thou hast struck them, and they have not grieved: thou hast bruised them, and they have refused to receive correction: they have made their faces harder than the rock, and they have refused to return.€€ƒxmBut I said: Perhaps these are poor and foolish, that know not the way of the Lord, the judgment of their God.€€ƒxÒI will go therefore to the great men, and will speak to them: for they have known the way of the Lord, the judgment of their God: and behold these have altogether broken the yoke more, and have burst the bonds.€€ƒyWherefore a lion out of the wood hath slain them, a wolf in the evening hath spoiled them, a leopard watcheth for their cities: every one that shall go out thence shall be taken, because their transgressions are multiplied, their rebellions are strengthened.€€ƒx¹How can I be merciful to thee? thy children have forsaken me, and swear by them that are not gods: I fed them to the full, and they committed adultery, and rioted in the harlot's house.€€ƒx^They are become as amorous horses and stallions: every one neighed after his neighbour's wife.€€ƒxhShall I not visit for these things, saith the Lord? and shall not my soul take revenge on such a nation?€€ƒxŠScale the walls thereof, and throw them down, but do not utterly destroy: take away the branches thereof, because they are not the Lord's.€€ƒxdFor the house of Israel, and the house of Juda have greatly transgressed against me, saith the Lord.€€ƒx~They have denied the Lord, and said, It is not he: and the evil shall not come upon us: we shall not see the sword and famine.€€ƒxuThe prophets have spoken in the wind, and there was no word of God in them: these things therefore shall befall them.€€ƒx­Thus saith the Lord the God of hosts: because you have spoken this word, behold I will make my words in thy mouth as fire, and this people as wood, and it shall devour them.€€ƒxÂBehold I will bring upon you a nation from afar, O house of Israel, saith the Lord: a strong nation, an ancient nation, a nation whose language thou shalt not know, nor understand what they say.€€ƒx;Their quiver is as an open sepulchre, they are all valiant.€€ƒyAnd they shall eat up thy corn, and thy bread: they shall devour thy sons, and thy daughters: they shall eat up thy flocks, and thy herds: they shall eat thy vineyards, and thy figs: and with the sword they shall destroy thy strong cities, wherein thou trustest.€€ƒxVNevertheless in those days, saith the Lord, I will not bring you to utter destruction.€€ƒxåAnd if you shall say: Why hath the Lord our God done all these things to us? thou shalt say to them: As you have forsaken me, and served a strange god in your own land, so shall you serve strangers in a land that is not your own.€€ƒxFDeclare ye this to the house of Jacob, and publish it in Juda, saying:€€ƒxfHear, O foolish people, and without understanding: who have eyes, and see not: and ears, and hear not.€€ƒy%Will not you then fear me, saith the Lord: and will you not repent at my presence? I have set the sand a bound for the sea, an everlasting ordinance, which it shall not pass over: and the waves thereof shall toss themselves, and shall not prevail: they shall swell, and shall not pass over it.€€ƒxeBut the heart of this people is become hard of belief and provoking, they are revolted and gone away.€€ƒx¸And they have not said in their heart: Let us fear the Lord our God, who giveth us the early and the latter rain in due season: who preserveth for us the fulness of the yearly harvest.€€ƒxbYour iniquities have turned these things away, and your sins have withholden good things from you.€€ƒxmFor among my people are found wicked men, that lie in wait as fowlers, setting snares and traps to catch men.€€ƒxlAs a net is full of birds, so their houses are full of deceit: therefore are they become great and enriched.€€ƒxŞThey are grown gross and fat: and have most wickedly transgressed my words. They have not judged the cause of the widow, they have not managed the cause of the fatherless, and they have not judged the judgment of the poor.€€ƒxgShall I not visit for these things, saith the Lord? or shall not my soul take revenge on such a nation?€€˜ƒx”The prophets prophesied falsehood, and the priests clapped their hands: and my people loved such things: what then shall be done in the end thereof?€€ƒ`€€ƒxÌStrengthen yourselves, ye sons of Benjamin, in the midst of Jerusalem, and sound the trumpet in Thecua, and set up the standard over Bethacarem: for evil is seen out of the north, and a great destruction.€€ƒxFI have likened the daughter of Sion to a beautiful and delicate woman.€€ƒxœThe shepherds shall come to her with their flocks: they have pitched their tents against her round about: every one shall feed them that are under his hand.€€ƒx•Prepare ye war against her: arise, and let us go up at midday: woe unto us, for the day is declined, for the shadows of the evening are grown longer.€€ƒx=Arise, and let us go up in the night, and destroy her houses.€€ƒxFor thus saith the Lord of hosts: Hew down her trees, cast up a trench about Jerusalem: this is the city to be visited, all oppression is in the midst of her.€€ƒx¤As a cistern maketh its water cold, so hath she made her wickedness cold: violence and spoil shall be heard in her, infirmity and stripes are continually before me.€€ƒxnBe thou instructed, O Jerusalem, lest my soul depart from thee, lest I make thee desolate, a land uninhabited.€€ƒx¡Thus saith the Lord of hosts: They shall gather the remains of Israel, as in a vine, even to one cluster: turn back thy hand, as a grapegatherer into the basket.€€ƒxÛTo whom shall I speak? and to whom shall I testify, that he may hear? behold, their ears are uncircumcised, and they cannot hear: behold the word of the Lord is become unto them a reproach: and they will not receive it.€€ƒxæTherefore am I full of the fury of the Lord, I am weary with holding in: pour it out upon the child abroad, and upon the council of the young men together: for man and woman shall be taken, the ancient and he that is full of days.€€ƒx®And their houses shall be turned over to others, with their lands and their wives together: for I will stretch forth my hand upon the inhabitants of the land, saith the Lord.€€ƒx’For from the least of them even to the greatest, all are given to covetousness: and from the prophet even to the priest, all are guilty of deceit.€€ƒxtAnd they healed the breach of the daughter of my people disgracefully, saying: Peace, peace: and there was no peace.€€ƒyThey were confounded, because they committed abomination: yea, rather they were not confounded with confusion, and they knew not how to blush: wherefore they shall fall among them that fall: in the time of their visitation they shall fall down, saith the Lord.€€ƒxÈThus saith the Lord: Stand ye on the ways, and see, and ask for the old paths, which is the good way, and walk ye in it: and you shall find refreshment for your souls. And they said: We will not walk.€€ƒxvAnd I appointed watchmen over you, saying: Hearken ye to the sound of the trumpet. And they said: We will not hearken.€€ƒxZTherefore hear, ye nations, and know, O congregation, what great things I will do to them.€€ƒx¢Hear, O earth: Behold I will bring evils upon this people, the fruits of their own thoughts: because they have not heard my words, and they have cast away my law.€€ƒx³To what purpose do you bring me frankincense from Saba, and the sweet smelling cane from a far country? your holocausts are not acceptable, nor are your sacrifices pleasing to me.€€ƒx£Therefore thus saith the Lord: Behold I will bring destruction upon this people, by which fathers and sons together shall fall, neighbour and kinsman shall perish.€€ƒx„Thus saith the Lord: Behold a people cometh from the land of the north, and a great nation shall rise up from the ends of the earth.€€ƒxÒThey shall lay hold on arrow and shield: they are cruel, and will have no mercy. Their voice shall roar like the sea: and they shall mount upon horses, prepared as men for war, against thee, O daughter of Sion.€€ƒxkWe have heard the fame thereof, our hands grow feeble: anguish hath taken hold of us, as a woman in labour.€€ƒxkGo not out into the fields, nor walk in the highway: for the sword of the enemy, and fear is on every side.€€ƒxÀGird thee with sackcloth, O daughter of my people, and sprinkle thee with ashes: make thee mourning as for an only son, a bitter lamentation, because the destroyer shall suddenly come upon us.€€˜ƒxlAll these princes go out of the way, they walk deceitfully, they are brass and iron: they are all corrupted.€€ƒx„The bellows have failed, the lead is consumed in the fire, the founder hath melted in vain: for their wicked deeds are not consumed.€€ƒx<Call them reprobate silver, for the Lord hath rejected them.€€ƒ`€€ƒx5The word that came to Jeremiah from the Lord, saying:€€ƒxµStand in the gate of the house of the Lord, and proclaim there this word, and say: Hear ye the word of the Lord, all ye men of Juda, that enter in at these gates, to adore the Lord.€€ƒx}Thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Make your ways and your doings good: and I will dwell with you in this place.€€ƒxoTrust not in lying words, saying: The temple of the Lord, the temple of the Lord, it is the temple of the Lord.€€ƒxtFor if you will order well your ways, and your doings: if you will execute judgment between a man and his neighbour,€€ƒxœIf you oppress not the stranger, the fatherless, and the widow, and shed not innocent blood in this place, and walk not after strange gods to your own hurt,€€ƒxsI will dwell with you in this place: in the land, which I gave to your fathers from the beginning and for evermore.€€ƒxEBehold you put your trust in lying words, which shall not profit you:€€ƒx€To steal, to murder, to commit adultery, to swear falsely, to offer to Baalim, and to go after strange gods, which you know not.€€ƒx¤And you have come, and stood before me in this house, in which my name is called upon, and have said: We are delivered, because we have done all these abominations.€€ƒxIs this house then, in which my name hath been called upon, in your eyes become a den of robbers? I, I am he: I have seen it, saith the Lord.€€ƒxƒGo ye to my place in Silo, where my name dwelt from the beginning: and see what I did to it for the wickedness of my people Israel:€€ƒxÁAnd now, because you have done all these works, saith the Lord: and I have spoken to you rising up early, and speaking, and you have not heard: and I have called you, and you have not answered:€€ƒxI will do to this house, in which my name is called upon, and in which you trust, and to the place which I have given you and your fathers, as I did to Silo.€€ƒxoAnd I will cast you away from before my face, as I have cast away all your brethren, the whole seed of Ephraim.€€ƒx‘Therefore do not thou pray for this people, nor take to thee praise and supplication for them: and do not withstand me: for I will not hear thee.€€ƒxSSeest thou not what they do in the cities of Juda, and in the streets of Jerusalem?€€ƒxÃThe children gather wood, and the fathers kindle the fire, and the women knead the dough, to make cakes to the queen of heaven, and to offer libations to strange gods, and to provoke me to anger.€€ƒxmDo they provoke me to anger, saith the Lord? is it not themselves, to the confusion of their own countenance?€€ƒxğTherefore thus saith the Lord God: Behold my wrath and my indignation is enkindled against this place, upon men and upon beasts, and upon the trees of the field, and upon the fruits of the land, and it shall burn, and shall not be quenched.€€ƒxrThus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Add your burnt offerings to your sacrifices, and eat ye the flesh.€€ƒx¬For I spoke not to your fathers, and I commanded them not, in the day that I brought them out of the land of Egypt, concerning the matter of burnt offerings and sacrifices.€€ƒxÅBut this thing I commanded them, saying: Hearken to my voice, and I will be your God, and you shall be my people: and walk ye in all the way that I have commanded you, that it may be well with you.€€˜ƒx³From the day that their fathers came out of the land of Egypt, even to this day. And I have sent to you all my servants the prophets, from day to day, rising up early and sending.€€ƒx€And they have not hearkened to me: nor inclined their ear: but have hardened their neck, and have done worse than their fathers.€€ƒx‰And thou shalt speak to them all these words, but they will not hearken to thee: and thou shalt call them, but they will not answer thee.€€ƒx¸And thou shalt say to them: This is a nation which hath not hearkened to the voice of the Lord their God, nor received instruction: faith is lost, and is taken away out of their mouth.€€ƒxŒCut off thy hair, and cast it away: and take up a lamentation on high: for the Lord hath rejected, and forsaken the generation of his wrath,€€ƒx¥Because the children of Juda have done evil in my eyes, saith the Lord. They have set their abominations in the house in which my name is called upon, to pollute it;€€ƒxÃAnd they have built the high places of Topheth, which is in the valley of the son of Ennom, to burn their sons, and their daughters in the fire: which I commanded not, nor thought on in my heart.€€ƒxÙTherefore behold the days shall come, saith the Lord, and it shall no more be called Topheth, nor the valley of the son of Ennom: but the valley of slaughter: and they shall bury in Topheth, because there is no place.€€ƒx•And the carcasses of this people shall be meat for the fowls of the air, and for the beasts of the earth, and there shall be none to drive them away.€€ƒxŞAnd I will cause to cease out of the cities of Juda, and out of the streets of Jerusalem, the voice of joy, and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom and the voice of the bride: for the land shall be desolate.€€ƒ`€€ƒxøAt that time, saith the Lord, they shall cast out the bones of the kings of Juda, and the bones of the princes thereof, and the bones of the priests, and the bones of the prophets, and the bones of the inhabitants of Jerusalem, out of their graves.€€ƒy7And they shall spread them abroad to the sun, and the moon, and all the host of heaven, whom they have loved, and whom they have served, and after whom have walked, and whom they have sought, and adored: they shall not be gathered, and they shall not be buried: they shall be as dung upon the face of the earth.€€ƒx±And death shall be chosen rather than life by all that shall remain of this wicked kindred in all places, which are left, to which I have cast them out, saith the Lord of hosts.€€ƒxŒAnd thou shalt say to them: Thus saith the Lord: Shall not he that falleth, rise again? and he that is turned away, shall he not turn again?€€ƒx…Why then is this people in Jerusalem turned away with a stubborn revolting? they have laid hold on lying, and have refused to return.€€ƒxÊI attended, and hearkened; no man speaketh what is good, there is none that doth penance for his sin, saying: What have I done? They are all turned to their own course, as a horse rushing to the battle.€€ƒx²The kite in the air hath known her time: the turtle, and the swallow, and the stork have observed the time of their coming: but my people have not known the judgment of the Lord.€€ƒx}How do you say: We are wise, and the law of the Lord is with us? Indeed the lying pens of the scribes hath wrought falsehood.€€ƒxˆThe wise men are confounded, they are dismayed, and taken: for they have cast away the word of the Lord, and there is no wisdom in them.€€ƒxÙTherefore will I give their women to strangers, their fields to others for an inheritance: because from the least even to the greatest all follow covetousness: from the prophet even to the priest all deal deceitfully.€€ƒxuAnd they healed the breach of the daughter of my people disgracefully, saying: Peace, peace: when there was no peace.€€ƒyThey are confounded, because they have committed abomination: yea rather they are not confounded with confusion, and they have not known how to blush: therefore shall they fall among them that fall; in the time of their visitation they shall fall, saith the Lord.€€ƒxÈGathering I will gather them together, saith the Lord, there is no grape on the vines, and there are no figs on the fig tree, the leaf is fallen: and I have given them the things that are passed away.€€ƒxèWhy do we sit still? assemble yourselves, and let us enter into the fenced city, and let us be silent there: for the Lord our God hath put us to silence, and hath given us water of gall to drink: for we have sinned against the Lord.€€ƒxMWe looked for peace and no good came: for a time of healing, and behold fear.€€ƒxÎThe snorting of his horses was heard from Dan, all the land was moved at the sound of the neighing of his warriors: and they came and devoured the land, and all that was in it: the city and its inhabitants.€€•ƒx7My sorrow is above sorrow, my heart mourneth within me.€€ƒxÅBehold the voice of the daughter of my people from a far country: Is not the Lord in Sion, or is not her king in her? why then have they provoked me to wrath with their idols, and strange vanities?€€ƒx?The harvest is past, the summer is ended, and we are not saved.€€ƒxwFor the affliction of the daughter of my people I am afflicted, and made sorrowful, astonishment hath taken hold on me.€€ƒxzIs there no balm in Galaad? or is there no physician there? Why then is not the wound of the daughter of my people closed?€€ƒ`€€ƒxWho will give water to my head, and a fountain of tears to my eyes? and I will weep day and night for the slain of the daughter of my people.€€ƒxµWho will give me in the wilderness a lodging place of wayfaring men, and I will leave my people, and depart from them? because they are all adulterers, an assembly of transgressors.€€ƒxĞAnd they have bent their tongue, as a bow, for lies, and not for truth: they have strengthened themselves upon the earth, for they have proceeded from evil to evil, and me they have not known, saith the Lord.€€ƒx§Let every man take heed of his neighbour, and let him not trust in any brother of his: for every brother will utterly supplant, and every friend will walk deceitfully.€€ƒxœAnd a man shall mock his brother, and they will not speak the truth: for they have taught their tongue to speak lies: they have laboured to commit iniquity.€€ƒxfThy habitation is in the midst of deceit: through deceit they have refused to know me, saith the Lord.€€ƒx„Therefore thus saith the Lord of hosts: Behold I will melt, and try them: for what else shall I do before the daughter of my people?€€ƒx’Their tongue is a piercing arrow, it hath spoken deceit: with his mouth one speaketh peace with his friend, and secretly he lieth in wait for him.€€ƒxkShall I not visit them for these things, saith the Lord? or shall not my soul be revenged on such a nation?€€ƒy9For the mountains I will take up weeping and lamentation, and for the beautiful places of the desert, mourning: because they are burnt up, for that there is not a man that passeth through them: and they have not heard the voice of the owner: from the fowl of the air to the beasts they are gone away and departed.€€ƒx‹And I will make Jerusalem to be heaps of sand, and dens of dragons: and I will make the cities of Juda desolate, for want of an inhabitant.€€ƒxÚWho is the wise man, that may understand this, and to whom the word of the mouth of the Lord may come that he may declare this, why the land hath perished, and is burnt up like a wilderness, which none passeth through?€€ƒx€And the Lord said: Because they have forsaken my law, which I gave them, and have not heard my voice, and have not walked in it.€€ƒxpBut they have gone after the perverseness of their own heart, and after Baalim, which their fathers taught them.€€ƒxTherefore thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Behold I will feed this people with wormwood, and give them water of gall to drink.€€˜-ƒx¯Thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Consider ye, and call for the mourning women, and let them come: and send to them that are wise women, and let them make haste:€€ƒxpLet them hasten and take up a lamentation for us: let our eyes shed tears, and our eyelids run down with waters.€€ƒxšFor a voice of wailing is heard out of Sion: How are we wasted and greatly confounded? because we have left the land, because our dwellings are cast down.€€ƒx¨Hear therefore, ye women, the word of the Lord: and let your ears receive the word of his mouth: and teach your daughters wailing: and every one her neighbour mourning.€€ƒxFor death is come up through our windows, it is entered into our houses to destroy the children from without, the young men from the streets.€€ƒx¯Speak: Thus saith the Lord: Even the carcass of man shall fall as dung upon the face of the country, and as grass behind the back of the mower, and there is none to gather it.€€ƒxThus saith the Lord: Let not the wise man glory in his wisdom, and let not the strong man glory in his strength, and let not the rich man glory in his riches:€€ƒxÉBut let him that glorieth glory in this, that he understandeth and knoweth me, for I am the Lord that exercise mercy, and judgment, and justice in the earth: for these things please me, saith the Lord.€€ƒxjBehold, the days come, saith the Lord, and I will visit upon every one that hath the foreskin circumcised.€€ƒyUpon Egypt, and upon Juda, and upon Edom, and upon the children of Ammon, and upon Moab, and upon all that have their hair polled round, that dwell in the desert: for all the nations are uncircumcised in the flesh, but all the house of Israel are uncircumcised in the heart.€€ƒ`€€ƒxNHear ye the word which the Lord hath spoken concerning you, O house of Israel.€€ƒxˆThus saith the Lord: Learn not according to the ways of the Gentiles: and be not afraid of the signs of heaven, which the heathens fear:€€ƒx|For the laws of the people are vain: for the works of the hand of the workman hath cut a tree out of the forest with an axe.€€ƒxuHe hath decked it with silver and gold: he hath put it together with nails and hammers, that it may not fall asunder.€€ƒxÃThey are framed after the likeness of a palm tree, and shall not speak: they must be carried to be removed, because they cannot go. Therefore fear them not, for they can neither do evil nor good.€€ƒxSThere is none like to thee, O Lord: thou art great, and great is thy name in might.€€ƒx¢Who shall not fear thee, O king of nations? for thine is the glory: among all the wise men of the nations, and in all their kingdoms there is none like unto thee.€€ƒxdThey shall be all proved together to be senseless and foolish: the doctrine of their vanity is wood.€€ƒxØSilver spread into plates is brought from Tharsis, and gold from Ophaz: the work of the artificer, and of the hand of the coppersmith: violet and purple is their clothing: all these things are the work of artificers.€€ƒx¯But the Lord is the true God: he is the living God, and the everlasting king: at his wrath the earth shall tremble, and the nations shall not be able to abide his threatening.€€ƒx¡Thus then shall you say to them: The gods that have not made heaven and earth, let them perish from the earth, and from among those places that are under heaven.€€ƒxHe that maketh the earth by his power, that prepareth the world by his wisdom, and stretcheth out the heavens by his knowledge.€€ƒxÂAt his voice he giveth a multitude of waters in the heaven, and lifteth up the clouds from the ends of the earth: he maketh lightnings for rain, and bringeth forth the wind out of his treasures.€€ƒx˜Every man is become a fool for knowledge, every artist is confounded in his graven idol: for what he hath cast is false, and there is no spirit in them.€€ƒx_They are vain things, and a ridiculous work: in the time of their visitation they shall perish.€€ƒx”The portion of Jacob is not like these: for it is he who formed all things: and Israel is the rod of his inheritance: the Lord of hosts is his name.€€ƒxCGather up thy shame out of the land, thou that dwellest in a siege.€€ƒx•For thus saith he Lord: Behold I will cast away far off the inhabitants of the land at this time: and I will afflict them, so that they may be found.€€ƒxsWoe is me for my destruction, my wound is very grievous. But I said: Truly this is my own evil, and I will bear it.€€ƒxµMy tabernacle is laid waste, all my cords are broken: my children are gone out from me, and they are not: there is none to stretch forth my tent any more, and to set up my curtains.€€ƒxŒBecause the pastors have done foolishly, and have not sought the Lord: therefore have they not understood, and all their flock is scattered.€€ƒx”Behold the sound of a noise cometh, a great commotion out of the land of the north: to make the cities of Juda a desert, and a dwelling for dragons.€€ƒxjI know, O Lord, that the way of a man is not his: neither is it in a man to walk, and to direct his steps.€€ƒx^Correct me, O Lord, but yet with judgment: and not in thy fury, lest thou bring me to nothing.€€ƒxâPour out thy indignation upon the nations that have not known thee, and upon the provinces that have not called upon thy name: because they have eaten up Jacob, and devoured him, and consumed him, and have destroyed his glory.€€ƒ`€€ƒx5The word that came from the Lord to Jeremiah, saying:€€ƒxfHear ye the words of this covenant, and speak to the men of Juda, and to the inhabitants of Jerusalem,€€ƒxŠAnd thou shalt say to them: Thus saith the Lord the God of Israel: Cursed is the man that shall not hearken to the words of this covenant,€€ƒxâWhich I commanded your fathers in the day that I brought them out of the land of Egypt, from the iron furnace, saying: Hear ye my voice, and do all things that I command you: and you shall be my people, and I will be your God:€€ƒx¨That I may accomplish the oath which I swore to your fathers, to give them a land flowing with milk and honey, as it is this day. And I answered and said: Amen, O Lord.€€ƒx§And the Lord said to me: Proclaim aloud all these words in the cities of Juda, and in the streets of Jerusalem, saying: Hear ye the words of the covenant, and do them:€€ƒx°For protesting I conjured your fathers in the day that I brought them out of the land of Egypt even to this day: rising early I conjured them, and said: Hearken ye to my voice:€€ƒxÛAnd they obeyed not, nor inclined their ear: but walked every one in the perverseness of his own wicked heart: and I brought upon them all the words of this covenant, which I commanded them to do, but they did them not.€€ƒyThey are returned to the former iniquities of their fathers, who refused to hear my words: so these likewise have gone after strange gods, to serve them: the house of Israel, and the house of Juda have made void my covenant, which I made with their fathers.€€ƒx¨Wherefore thus saith the Lord: Behold I will bring in evils upon them, which they shall not be able to escape: and they shall cry to me, and I will not hearken to them.€€ƒx²And the cities of Juda, and the inhabitants of Jerusalem shall go, and cry to the gods to whom they offer sacrifice, and they shall not save them in the time of their affliction.€€ƒxÃFor according to the number of thy cities were thy gods, O Juda: and according to the number of the streets of Jerusalem thou hast set up altars of confusion, altars to offer sacrifice to Baalim.€€ƒx´Therefore do not thou pray for this people, and do not take up praise and prayer for them: for I will not hear them in the time of their cry to me, in the time of their affliction.€€ƒxWhat is the meaning that my beloved hath wrought much wickedness in my house? shall the holy flesh take away from thee thy crimes, in which thou hast boasted?€€ƒx¬The Lord called thy name, a plentiful olive tree, fair, fruitful, and beautiful: at the noise of a word, a great fire was kindled in it, and the branches thereof are burnt.€€ƒxÜAnd the Lord of hosts that planted thee, hath pronounced evil against thee: for the evils of the house of Israel, and of the house of Juda, which they have done to themselves, to provoke me, offering sacrifice to Baalim.€€ƒxVBut thou, O Lord, hast shewn me, and I have known: then thou shewedst me their doings.€€ƒxîAnd I was as a meek lamb, that is carried to be a victim: and I knew not that they had devised counsels against me, saying: Let us put wood on his bread, and cut him off from the land of the living, and let his name be remembered no more.€€ƒx›But thou, O Lord of Sabaoth, who judgest justly, and triest the reins and the hearts, let me see thy revenge on them: for to thee have I revealed my cause.€€ƒx§Therefore thus saith the Lord to the men of Anathoth, who seek thy life, and say: Thou shalt not prophesy in the name of the Lord, and thou shalt not die in our hands.€€ƒx¢Therefore thus saith the Lord of hosts: Behold I will visit upon them: their young men shall die by the sword, their sons and their daughters shall die by famine.€€cHos¤dnameeHoseaiosis_namecHoslabbreviationcHoshchapters‹ƒxÆThou indeed, O Lord, art just, if I plead with thee, but yet I will speak what is just to thee: Why doth the way of the wicked prosper: why is it well with all them that transgress, and do wickedly?€€ƒxThou hast planted them, and they have taken root: they prosper and bring forth fruit: thou art near in their mouth, and far from their reins.€€ƒx„And thou, O Lord, hast known me, thou hast seen me, and proved my heart with thee: gather them together as for the day of slaughter.€€ƒxÎHow long shall the land mourn, and the herb of every field wither for the wickedness of them that dwell therein? The beasts and the birds are consumed: because they have said: He shall not see our last end.€€ƒxºIf thou hast been wearied with running with footmen, how canst thou contend with horses? and if thou hast been secure in a land of peace, what wilt thou do in the swelling of the Jordan?€€ƒx¸For even thy brethren, and the house of thy father, even they have fought against thee, and have cried after thee with full voice: believe them not when they speak good things to thee.€€ƒxmI have forsaken my house, I have left my inheritance: I have given my dear soul into the hand of her enemies.€€ƒxnMy inheritance is become to me as a lion in the wood: it hath cried out against me, therefore have I hated it.€€ƒxœIs my inheritance to me as a speckled bird? is it as a bird dyed throughout? come ye, assemble yourselves, all ye beasts of the earth, make haste to devour.€€ƒx•Many pastors have destroyed my vineyard, they have trodden my portion under foot: they have changed my delightful portion into a desolate wilderness.€€ƒx˜They have laid it waste, and it hath mourned for me. With desolation is all the land made desolate; because there is none that considereth in the heart.€€—ƒx·They have sown wheat, and reaped thorns: they have received an inheritance, and it shall not profit them: you shall be ashamed of your fruits, because of the fierce wrath of the Lord.€€ƒxâThus saith the Lord against all wicked neighbours, that touch the inheritance that I have shared out to my people Israel: Behold I will pluck them out of their land, and I will pluck the house of Juda out of the midst of them.€€ƒx£And when I shall have plucked them out, I will return, and have mercy on them: and will bring them back, every man to his inheritance, and every man into his land.€€ƒxåAnd it shall come to pass, if they will be taught, and will learn the ways of my people, to swear by my name: The Lord liveth, as they have taught my people to swear by Baal: that they shall be built up in the midst of my people.€€ƒx\But if they will not hear, I will utterly pluck out and destroy that nation, saith the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxƒThus saith the Lord to me: Go, and get thee a linen girdle, and thou shalt put it about thy loins, and shalt not put it into water.€€ƒxPAnd I got a girdle according to the word of the Lord, and put it about my loins.€€ƒx<And the word of the Lord came to me the second time, saying:€€ƒx‡Take the girdle which thou hast got, which is about thy loins, and arise, go to the Euphrates, and hide it there in a hole of the rock.€€ƒxFAnd I went, and hid it by the Euphrates, as the Lord had commanded me.€€ƒx¡And it came to pass after many days, that the Lord said to me: Arise, go to the Euphrates, and take from thence the girdle, which I commanded thee to hide there.€€ƒx¡And I went to the Euphrates, and digged, and took the girdle out of the place where I had hid it and behold the girdle was rotten, so that it was fit for no use.€€ƒx,And the word of the Lord came to me, saying:€€ƒxnThus saith the Lord: After this manner will I make the pride of Juda, and the great pride of Jerusalem to rot.€€ƒxàThis wicked people, that will not hear my words, and that walk in the perverseness of their heart, and have gone after strange gods to serve them, and to adore them: and they shall be as this girdle ,which is fit for no use.€€ƒyFor as the girdle sticketh close to the loins of a man, so have I brought close to me all the house of Israel, and all the house of Juda, saith the Lord: that they might be my people, and for a name, and for a praise, and for a glory: but they would not hear.€€ƒxÔThou shalt speak therefore to them this word: Thus saith the Lord the God of Israel: Every bottle shall be filled with wine. And they shall say to thee: Do we not know that every bottle shall be filled with wine?€€ƒxıAnd thou shalt say to them: Thus saith the Lord: Behold I will fill all the inhabitants of this land, and the kings of the race of David that sit upon his throne, and the priests, and the prophets, and all the inhabitants of Jerusalem, with drunkenness.€€ƒx½And I will scatter them every man from his brother, and fathers and sons in like manner, saith the Lord: I will not spare, and I will not pardon: nor will I have mercy, but to destroy them.€€ƒx>Hear ye, and give ear: Be not proud, for the Lord hath spoken.€€ƒxÇGive ye glory to the Lord your God, before it be dark, and before your feet stumble upon the dark mountains: you shall look for light, and he will turn it into the shadow of death, and into darkness.€€ƒxÀBut if you will not hear this, my soul shall weep in secret for your pride: weeping it shall weep, and my eyes shall run down with tears, because the flock of the Lord is carried away captive.€€…ƒxThe cities of the south are shut up, and there is none to open them: all Juda is carried away captive with an entire captivity.€€ƒxvLift up your eyes, and see, you that come from the north: where is the flock that is given thee, thy beautiful cattle?€€ƒx¸What wilt thou say when he shall visit thee? for thou hast taught them against thee, and instructed them against thy own head: shall not sorrows lay hold on thee, as a woman in labour?€€ƒx©And if thou shalt say in thy heart: Why are these things come upon me? For the greatness of thy iniquity, thy nakedness is discovered, the soles of thy feet are defiled.€€ƒxqIf the Ethiopian can change his skin, or the leopard his spots: you also may do well, when you have learned evil.€€“ƒx‡This is thy lot, and the portion of thy measure from me, saith the Lord, because thou hast forgotten me, and hast trusted in falsehood.€€ƒxUWherefore I have also bared thy thighs against thy face, and thy shame hath appeared.€€ƒxÏI have seen thy adulteries, and thy neighing, the wickedness of thy fornication: and thy abominations, upon the hills in the field. Woe to thee, Jerusalem, wilt thou not be made clean after me: how long yet?€€ƒ`€€ƒxOThe word of the Lord that came to Jeremiah concerning the words of the drought.€€ƒx€Judea hath mourned, and the gates thereof are fallen, and are become obscure on the ground, and the cry of Jerusalem is gone up.€€ƒx½The great ones sent their inferiors to the water: they came to draw, they found no water, they carried back their vessels empty: they were confounded and afflicted, and covered their heads.€€ƒx…For the destruction of the land, because there came no rain upon the earth, the husbandman were confounded, they covered their heads.€€ƒxWYea, the hind also brought forth in the field, and left it, because there was no grass.€€ƒx~And the wild asses stood upon the rocks, they snuffed up the wind like dragons, their eyes failed, because there was no grass.€€ƒxIf our iniquities have testified against us, O Lord, do thou it for thy name's sake, for our rebellions are many, we have sinned against thee.€€ƒx˜O expectation of Israel, the Saviour thereof in time of trouble: why wilt thou be as a stranger in the land, and as a wayfaring man turning in to lodge?€€ƒx™Why wilt thou be as a wandering man, as a mighty man that cannot save? but thou, O Lord, art among us, and thy name is called upon by us, forsake us not.€€ƒx¸Thus saith the Lord to this people, that have loved to move their feet, and have not rested, and have not pleased the Lord: He will now remember their iniquities, and visit their sins.€€ƒxAAnd the Lord said to me: Pray not for this people for their good.€€ƒx¼When they fast I will not hear their prayers: and if they offer holocausts and victims, I will not receive them: for I will consume them by the sword, and by famine, and by the pestilence.€€ƒx±And I said: Ah, ah, ah, O Lord God, the prophets say to them: You shall not see the sword, and there shall be no famine among you, but he will give you true peace in this place.€€ƒxõAnd the Lord said to me: The prophets prophesy falsely in my name: I sent them not, neither have I commanded them, nor have I spoken to them: they prophesy unto you a lying vision, and divination and deceit, and the seduction of their own heart.€€ƒxĞTherefore thus saith the Lord concerning the prophets that prophesy in my name, whom I did not send, that say: Sword and famine shall not be in this land: By sword and famine shall those prophets be consumed.€€ƒxÚAnd thou shalt speak this word to them: Let my eyes shed down tears night and day, and let them not cease, because the virgin daughter of my people is afflicted with a great affliction, with an exceeding grievous evil.€€ƒxÒIf I go forth into the fields, behold the slain with the sword: and if I enter into the city, behold them that are consumed with famine. The prophet also and the priest are gone into a land which they knew not.€€ƒxäHast thou utterly cast away Juda, or hath thy soul abhorred Sion? why then hast thou struck us, so that there is no healing for us? we have looked for peace, and there is no good: and for the time of healing, and behold trouble.€€ƒxkWe acknowledge, O Lord, our wickedness, the iniquities of our fathers, because we have sinned against thee.€€ƒxGive us not to be a reproach, for thy name's sake, and do not disgrace in us the throne of thy glory: remember, break not thy covenant with us.€€ƒxÇAre there any among the graven things of the Gentiles that can send rain? or can the heavens give showers? art not thou the Lord our God, whom we have looked for? for thou hast made all these things.€€ƒ`€€ƒx›And the Lord said to me: If Moses and Samuel shall stand before me, my soul is not towards this people: cast them out from my sight, and let them go forth.€€ƒy
And if they shall say unto thee: Whither shall we go forth? thou shalt say to them: Thus saith the Lord: Such as are for death, to death: and such as are for the sword, to the sword: and such as are for famine, to famine: and such as are for captivity, to captivity.€€ƒx°And I will visit them with four kinds, saith the Lord: The sword to kill, and the dogs to tear, and the fowls of the air, and the beasts of the earth, to devour and to destroy.€€ƒx¡And I will give them up to the rage of all the kingdoms of the earth: because of Manasses the son of Ezechias the king of Juda, for all that he did in Jerusalem.€€ƒxnFor who shall have pity on thee, O Jerusalem? or who shall bemoan thee? or who shall go to pray for thy peace?€€ƒx£Thou hast forsaken me, saith the Lord, thou art gone backward: and I will stretch out my hand against thee, and I will destroy thee: I am weary of entreating thee.€€ƒx’And I will scatter them with a fan in the gates of the land: I have killed and destroyed my people, and yet they are not returned from their ways.€€ƒxÃTheir widows are multiplied unto me above the sand of the sea: I have brought upon them against the mother of the young man a spoiler at noonday: I have cast a terror on a sudden upon the cities.€€‹ƒx¹Woe is me, my mother: why hast thou borne me a man of strife, a man of contention to all the earth? I have not lent on usury, neither hath any man lent to me on usury: yet all curse me.€€ƒx«The Lord saith to me: Assuredly it shall be well with thy remnant, assuredly I shall help thee in the time of affliction, and in the time of tribulation against the enemy.€€ƒxAShall iron be allied with the iron from the north, and the brass?€€ƒxrThy riches and thy treasures I will give unto spoil for nothing, because of all thy sins, even in all thy borders.€€ƒx}And I will bring thy enemies out of a land, which thou knowest not: for a fire is kindled in my rage, it shall burn upon you.€€ƒx®O Lord, thou knowest, remember me, and visit me, and defend me from them that persecute me, do not defend me in thy patience: know that for thy sake I have suffered reproach.€€ƒx•Thy words were found, and I did eat them, and thy word was to me a joy and gladness of my heart: for thy name is called upon me, O Lord God of hosts.€€ƒxI sat not in the assembly of jesters, nor did I make a boast of the presence of thy hand: I sat alone, because thou hast filled me with threats.€€ƒx§Why is my sorrow become perpetual, and my wound desperate so as to refuse to be healed? it is become to me as the falsehood of deceitful waters that cannot be trusted.€€ƒyTherefore thus saith the Lord: If thou wilt be converted, I will convert thee, and thou shalt stand before my face; and thou wilt separate the precious from the vile, thou shalt be as my mouth: they shall be turned to thee, and thou shalt not be turned to them.€€ƒx¾And I will make thee to this people as a strong wall of brass: and they shall fight against thee, and shall not prevail: for I am with thee to save thee, and to deliver thee, saith the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx,And the word of the Lord came to me, saying:€€ƒxZThou shalt not take thee a wife, neither shalt thou have sons and daughters in this place.€€ƒxÇFor thus saith the Lord concerning the sons and daughters, that are born in this place, and concerning their mothers that bore them: and concerning their fathers, of whom they were born in this land:€€ƒy7They shall die by the death of grievous illnesses: they shall not be lamented, and they shall not be buried, they shall be as dung upon the face of the earth: and they shall be consumed with the sword, and with famine: and their carcasses shall be meat for the fowls of the air, and for the beasts of the earth.€€ƒxÏFor thus saith the Lord: Enter not into the house of feasting, neither go thou to mourn, nor to comfort them: because I have taken away my peace from this people, saith the Lord, my mercy and commiserations.€€ƒx¡Both the great and the little shall die in this land: they shall not be buried nor lamented, and men shall not cut themselves, nor make themselves bald for them.€€ƒx–And they shall not break bread among them to him that mourneth, to comfort him for the dead: neither shall they give them for their father and mother.€€ƒxVAnd do not thou go into the house of feasting, to sit with them, and to eat and drink:€€ƒxæFor thus saith the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: Behold I will take away out of this place in your sight, and in your days the voice of mirth, and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom, and the voice of the bride.€€ƒxíAnd when thou shalt tell this people all these words, and they shall say to thee: Wherefore hath the Lord pronounced against us all this great evil? what is our iniquity? and what is our sin, that we have sinned against the Lord our God?€€ƒx±Thou shalt say to them: Because your fathers forsook me, saith the Lord: and went after strange gods, and served them, and adored them: and they forsook me, and kept not my law.€€ƒx™And you also have done worse than your fathers: for behold every one of you walketh after the perverseness of his evil heart, so as not to hearken to me.€€ƒxµSo I will cast you forth out of this land, into a land which you know not, nor your fathers: and there you shall serve strange gods day and night, which shall not give you any rest.€€ƒx£Therefore behold the days come, saith the Lord, when it shall be said no more: The Lord liveth, that brought forth the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt.€€ƒxÖBut, The Lord liveth, that brought the children of Israel out of the land of the north, and out of all the lands to which I cast them out: and I will bring them again into their land, which I gave to their fathers.€€ƒxvFor my eyes are upon all their ways: they are not hid from my face, and their iniquity hath not been hid from my eyes.€€ƒxÁAnd I will repay first their double iniquities, and their sins: because they have defiled my land with the carcasses of their idols, and they have filled my inheritance with their abominations.€€ƒxåO Lord, my might, and my strength, and my refuge in the day of tribulation: to thee the Gentiles shall come from the ends of the earth, and shall say: Surely our fathers have possessed lies, a vanity which hath not profited them.€€ƒx8Shall a man make gods unto himself and they are no gods?€€ƒxŠTherefore behold I will this once cause them to know, I will shew them my hand and my power: and they shall know that my name is the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx—The sin of Juda is written with a pen of iron, with the point of a diamond, it is graven upon the table of their heart, upon the horns of their altars.€€ƒxqWhen their children shall remember their altars, and their groves, and their green trees upon the high mountains,€€ƒx‡Sacrificing in the field: I will give thy strength, and all thy treasures to the spoil, and thy high places for sin in all thy borders.€€ƒxÕAnd thou shalt be left stripped of thy inheritance, which I gave thee: and I will make thee serve thy enemies in a land which thou knowest not: because thou hast kindled a fire in my wrath, it shall burn for ever.€€ƒxThus saith the Lord: Cursed be the man that trusteth in man, and maketh flesh his arm, and whose heart departeth from the Lord.€€ƒx¥For he shall be like tamaric in the desert, and he shall not see when good shall come: but he shall dwell in dryness in the desert in a salt land, and not inhabited.€€ƒxSBlessed be the man that trusteth in the Lord, and the Lord shall be his confidence.€€ƒy(And he shall be as a tree that is planted by the waters, that spreadeth out its roots towards moisture: and it shall not fear when the heat cometh. And the leaf thereof shall be green, and in the time of drought it shall not be solicitous, neither shall it cease at any time to bring forth fruit.€€‘ƒxI am the Lord who search the heart, and prove the reins: who give to every one according to his way, and according to the fruit of his devices.€€ƒxÇAs the partridge hath hatched eggs which she did not lay: so is he that hath gathered riches, and not by right: in the midst of his days he shall leave them, and in his latter end he shall be a fool.€€ƒxQA high and glorious throne from the beginning is the place of our sanctification.€€ƒxÁO Lord, the hope of Israel: all that forsake thee shall be confounded: they that depart from thee, shall be written in the earth: because they have forsaken the Lord, the vein of living waters.€€ƒx^Heal me, O Lord, and I shall be healed: save me, and I shall be saved: for thou art my praise.€€ƒxBBehold they say to me: Where is the word of the Lord? let it come.€€ƒx§And I am not troubled, following thee for my pastor, and I have not desired the day of man, thou knowest. That which went out of my lips, hath been right in thy sight.€€ƒxHBe not thou a terror unto me, thou art my hope in the day of affliction.€€ƒxÉLet them be confounded that persecute me, and let not me be confounded: let them be afraid, and let not me be afraid: bring upon them the day of affliction, and with a double destruction, destroy them.€€ƒx¦Thus saith the Lord to me: Go, and stand in the gate of the children of the people, by which the kings of Juda come in, and go out, and in all the gates of Jerusalem:€€ƒx™And thou shalt say to them: Hear the word of the Lord, ye kings of Juda, and al Juda, and all the inhabitants of Jerusalem, that enter in by these gates.€€ƒx‡Thus saith the Lord: Take heed to your souls, and carry no burdens on the sabbath day: and bring them not in by the gates of Jerusalem.€€ƒxAnd do not bring burdens out of your houses on the sabbath day, neither do ye any work: sanctify the sabbath day, as I commanded your fathers.€€ƒx†But they did not hear, nor incline their ear: but hardened their neck, that they might not hear me, and might not receive instruction.€€ƒxËAnd it shall come to pass: if you will hearken to me, saith the Lord, to bring in no burdens by the gates of this city on the sabbath day: and if you will sanctify the sabbath day, to do no work therein:€€ƒyThen shall there enter in by the gates of this city kings and princes, sitting upon the throne of David, and riding in chariots and on horses, they and their princes, the men of Juda, and the inhabitants of Jerusalem: and this city shall be inhabited for ever.€€ƒy;And they shall come from the cities of Juda, and from the places round about Jerusalem, and from the land of Benjamin, and from the plains, and from the mountains, and from the south, bringing holocausts, and victims, and sacrifices, and frankincense, and they shall bring in an offering into the house of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx5The word that came to Jeremiah from the Lord, saying:€€ƒxOArise, and go down into the potter's house, and there thou shalt hear my words.€€ƒxUAnd I went down into the potter's house, and behold he was doing a work on the wheel.€€ƒx“And the vessel was broken which he was making of clay with his hands: and turning he made another vessel, as it seemed good in his eyes to make it.€€ƒx-Then the word of the Lord came to me, saying:€€ƒxŸCannot I do with you, as this potter, O house of Israel, saith the Lord? behold as clay is in the hand of the potter, so are you in my hand, O house of Israel.€€ƒxpI will suddenly speak against a nation, and against a kingdom, to root out, and to pull down, and to destroy it.€€ƒx‰If that nation against which I have spoken, shall repent of their evil, I also will repent of the evil that I have thought to do to them.€€ƒxQAnd I will suddenly speak of a nation and of a kingdom, to build up and plant it.€€ƒxwIf it shall do evil in my sight, that it obey not my voice: I will repent of the good that I have spoken to do unto it.€€ƒxÿNow therefore tell the men of Juda, and the inhabitants of Jerusalem, saying: Thus saith the Lord: Behold I frame evil against you, and devise a device against you: let every man of you return from his evil way, and make ye your ways and your doings good.€€ƒx‘And they said; We have no hopes: for we will go after our own thoughts, and we will do every one according to the perverseness of his evil heart.€€ƒx‡Therefore thus saith the Lord: Ask among the nations: Who hath heard such horrible things, as the virgin of Israel hath done to excess?€€ƒx|Shall the snow of Libanus fail from the rock of the field? or can the cold waters that gush out and run down, be taken away?€€Œƒx”That their land might be given up to desolation, and to a perpetual hissing: every one that shall pass by it, shall be astonished, and wag his head.€€ƒx…As a burning wind will I scatter them before the enemy: I will shew them the back, and not the face, in the day of their destruction.€€ƒxÿAnd they said: Come, and let us invent devices against Jeremiah: for the law shall not perish from the priest, nor counsel from the wise, nor the word from the prophet: come, and let us strike him with the tongue, and let us give no heed to all his words.€€ƒx>Give heed to me, O Lord, and hear the voice of my adversaries.€€ƒx¹Shall evil be rendered for good, because they have digged a pit for my soul? Remember that I have stood in thy sight, to speak good for them, and to turn away thy indignation from them.€€ƒxñTherefore deliver up their children to famine, and bring them into the hands of the sword: let their wives be bereaved of children and widows: and let their husbands be slain by death: let their young men be stabbed with the sword in battle.€€ƒx§Let a cry be heard out of their houses: for thou shalt bring the robber upon them suddenly: because they have digged a pit to take me, and have hid snares for my feet.€€ƒxâBut thou, O Lord, knowest all their counsel against me unto death: not their iniquity, and let not their sin be blotted out from thy sight: let them be overthrown before thy eyes, in the time of thy wrath do thou destroy them.€€ƒ`€€ƒxˆThus saith the Lord: Go, and take a potter's earthen bottle, and take of the ancients of the people, and of the ancients of the priests:€€ƒxœAnd go forth into the valley of the son of Ennom, which is by the entry of the earthen gate: and there thou shalt proclaim the words that I shall tell thee.€€ƒxÿAnd thou shalt say: Hear the word of the Lord, O ye kings of Juda, and ye inhabitants of Jerusalem: Thus saith the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: Behold I will bring an affliction upon this place: so that whosoever shall hear it, his ears shall tingle:€€ƒx¸And they have built the high places of Baalim, to burn their children with fire for a holocaust to Baalim: which I did not command, nor speak of, neither did it once come into my mind.€€ƒx¡Therefore behold the days come, saith the Lord, that this place shall no more be called Topheth, nor the valley of the son of Ennom, but the valley of slaughter.€€ƒy!And I will defeat the counsel of Juda and of Jerusalem in this place: and I will destroy them with the sword in the sight of their enemies, and by the hands of them that seek their lives: and I will give their carcasses to be meat for the fowls of the air, and for the beasts of the earth.€€ƒx¢And I will make this city an astonishment, and a hissing: every one that shall pass by it, shall be astonished, and shall hiss because of all the plagues thereof.€€ƒxşAnd I will feed them with the flesh of their sons, and with the flesh of their daughters: and they shall eat every one the flesh of his friend in the siege, and in the distress wherewith their enemies, and they that seek their lives, shall straiten them.€€ƒxPAnd thou shalt break the bottle in the sight of the men that shall go with thee.€€ƒxÿAnd thou shalt say to them: Thus saith the Lord of hosts: Even so will I break this people, and this city, as the potter's vessel is broken, which cannot be made whole again: and they shall be buried in Topheth, because there is no other place to bury in.€€ƒxsThus will I do to this place, saith the Lord, and to the inhabitants thereof: and I will make this city as Topheth.€€ƒxïAnd the houses of Jerusalem, and the houses of the kings of Juda shall be unclean as the place of Topheth: all the houses upon whose roofs they have sacrificed to all the host of heaven, and have poured out drink offerings to strange gods.€€ƒx›Then Jeremiah came from Topheth, whither the Lord had sent him to prophesy, and he stood in the court of the house of the Lord, and said to all the people:€€ƒxïThus saith the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: Behold I will bring in upon this city, and upon all the cities thereof all the evils that I have spoken against it: because they have hardened their necks, that they might not hear my words.€€ƒ`€€ƒxƒNow Phassur the son of Emmer, the priest, who was appointed chief in the house of the Lord, heard Jeremiah prophesying these words.€€ƒx†And Phassur struck Jeremiah the prophet, and put him in the stocks, that were in the upper gate of Benjamin, in the house of the Lord.€€ƒy5For thus saith the Lord: Behold I will deliver thee up to fear, thee and all thy friends: and they shall fall by the sword of their enemies, and thy eyes shall see it, and I will give all Juda into the hand of the king of Babylon: and he shall carry them away to Babylon, and shall strike them with the sword.€€ƒy	And I will give all the substance of this city, and all its labour, and every precious thing thereof, and all the treasures of the kings of Juda will I give into the hands of their enemies: and they shall pillage them, and take them away, and carry them to Babylon.€€ƒxáBut thou Phassur, and all that dwell in thy house, shall go into captivity, and thou shalt go to Babylon, and there thou shalt die, and there thou shalt be buried, thou and all thy friends, to whom thou hast prophesied a lie.€€ƒx¤Thou hast deceived me, O Lord, and I am deceived: thou hast been stronger than I, and thou hast prevailed. I am become a laughingstock all the day, all scoff at me.€€ƒx³For I am speaking now this long time, crying out against iniquity, and I often proclaim devastation: and the word of the Lord is made a reproach to me, and a derision all the day.€€ƒx½Then I said: I will not make mention of him, nor speak any more in his name: and there came in my heart as a burning fire, shut up in my bones, and I was wearied, not being able to bear it.€€ƒyFor I heard the reproaches of many, and terror on every side: Persecute him, and let us persecute him: from all the men that were my familiars, and continued at my side: if by any means he may be deceived, and we may prevail against him, and be revenged on him.€€ƒxçBut the Lord is with me as a strong warrior: therefore they that persecute me shall fall, and shall be weak: they shall be greatly confounded, because they have not understood the everlasting reproach, which never shall be effaced.€€ƒx«And thou, O Lord of hosts, prover of the just, who seest the reins and the heart: let me see, I beseech thee, thy vengeance on them: for to thee I have laid open my cause.€€ƒxsSing ye to the Lord, praise the Lord: because he hath delivered the soul of the poor out of the hand of the wicked.€€ƒx]Cursed be the day wherein I was born: let not the day in which my mother bore me, be blessed.€€ƒx{Cursed be the man that brought the tidings to my father, saying: A man child is born to thee: and made him greatly rejoice.€€ƒxLet that man be as the cities which the Lord hath overthrown, and hath not repented: let him hear a cry in the morning, and howling at noontide:€€ƒxoWho slew me not from the womb, that my mother might have been my grave, and her womb an everlasting conception.€€ƒxdWhy came I out of the womb, to see labour and sorrow, and that my days should be spent in confusion?€€ƒ`€€‰ƒx»Inquire of the Lord for us, for Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon maketh war against us: if so be the Lord will deal with us according to all his wonderful works, that he may depart from us.€€ƒx:And Jeremiah said to them: Thus shall you say to Sedecias:€€ƒyThus saith the Lord, the God of Israel: Behold I will turn back the weapons of war that are in your hands, and with which you fight against the king of Babylon, and the Chaldeans, that besiege you round about the walls: and I will gather them together in the midst of this city.€€ƒxŠAnd I myself will fight against you with an outstretched hand, and with a strong arm, and in fury, and in indignation, and in great wrath.€€ƒx_And I will strike the inhabitants of this city, men and beasts shall die of a great pestilence.€€ƒyÇAnd after this, saith the Lord, I will give Sedecias the king of Juda, and his servants, and his people, and such as are left in this city from the pestilence, and the sword, and the famine, into the hand of Nabuchodonosor the king of Babylon, and into the hand of their enemies, and into the hand of them that seek their life, and he shall strike them with the edge of the sword, and he shall not be moved to pity, nor spare them, nor shew mercy to them.€€ƒxvAnd to this people thou shalt say: Thus saith the Lord: Behold I set before you the way of life, and the way of death.€€ƒxãHe that shall abide in this city, shall die by the sword, and by the famine, and by the pestilence: but he that shall go out and flee over to the Chaldeans, that besiege you, shall live, and his life shall be to him as a spoil.€€ƒx¬For I have set my face against this city for evil, and not for good, saith the Lord: it shall be given into the hand of the king of Babylon, and he shall burn it with fire.€€dJoel¤dnamedJoeliosis_namedJoellabbreviationdJoelhchaptersƒ”ƒx Behold I come to thee that dwellest in a valley upon a rock above a plain, saith the Lord: and you say: Who shall strike us and who shall enter into our houses?€€ƒx¯But I will visit upon you according to the fruit of your doings, saith the Lord: and I will kindle a fire in the forest thereof: and it shall devour all things round about it.€€ƒ`€€ƒxdThus saith the Lord: Go down to the house of the king of Juda, and there thou shalt speak this word,€€ƒx§And thou shalt say: Hear the word of the Lord, king of Juda, that sittest upon the throne of David: thou and thy servants, and thy people, who enter in by these gates.€€ƒxùThus saith the Lord: Execute judgment and justice, and deliver him that is oppressed out of the hand of the oppressor: and afflict not the stranger, the fatherless, and the widow, nor oppress them unjustly: and shed not innocent blood in this place.€€ƒxàFor if you will do this thing indeed, then shall there enter in by the gates of this house, kings of the race of David sitting upon his throne, and riding in chariots and on horses, they and their servants, and their people.€€ƒxyBut if you will not hearken to these words: I swear by myself, saith the Lord, that this house shall become a desolation.€€ƒx¨For thus saith the Lord to the house of the king of Juda: Thou art to me Galaad the head of Libanus: yet surely I will make thee a wilderness, and cities not habitable.€€ƒx•And I will prepare against thee the destroyer and his weapons: and they shall cut down thy chosen cedars, and shall cast them headlong into the fire.€€ƒx†And many nations shall pass by this city: and they shall say every man to his neighbour: Why hath the Lord done so to this great city?€€ƒx„And they shall answer: Because they have forsaken the covenant of the Lord their God, and have adored strange gods, and served them.€€ƒx“Weep not for him that is dead, nor bemoan him with your tears: lament him that goeth away, for he shall return no more, nor see his native country.€€ƒxªFor thus saith the Lord to Sellum the son of Josias the king of Juda, who reigned instead of his father, who went forth out of this place: He shall return hither no more:€€ƒxkBut in the place, to which I have removed him, there shall he die, and he shall not see this land any more.€€ƒx¡Woe to him that buildeth up his house by injustice, and his chambers not in judgment: that will oppress his friend without cause, and will not pay him his wages.€€ƒx™Who saith: I will build me a wide house, and large chambers: who openeth to himself windows, and maketh roofs of cedar, and painteth them with vermilion.€€ƒxœShalt thou reign, because thou comparest thyself to the cedar? did not thy father eat and drink, and do judgment and justice, and it was then well with him?€€ƒxtHe judged the cause of the poor and needy for his own good: was it not therefore because he knew me, saith the Lord?€€ƒxŠBut thy eyes and thy heart are set upon covetousness, and upon shedding innocent blood, and upon oppression, and running after evil works.€€˜ ƒxcHe shall be buried with the burial of an ass, rotten and cast forth without the gates of Jerusalem.€€ƒxzGo up to Libanus, and cry: and lift up thy voice in Basan, and cry to them that pass by, for all thy lovers are destroyed.€€ƒx‘I spoke to thee in thy prosperity: and thou saidst: I will not hear: this hath been thy way from thy youth, because thou hast not heard my voice.€€ƒx‘The wind shall feed all thy pastors, and thy lovers shall go into captivity and then shalt thou be confounded, and ashamed of all thy wickedness.€€ƒx–Thou that sittest in Libanus, and makest thy nest in the cedars, how hast thou mourned when sorrows came upon thee, as the pains of a woman in labour?€€ƒx‚As I live, saith the Lord, if Jechonias the son of Joakim the king of Juda were a ring on my right hand, I would pluck him thence.€€ƒxÊAnd I will give thee into the hand of them that seek thy life, and into the hand of them whose face thou fearest, and into the hand of Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon, and into the hand of the Chaldeans.€€ƒxAnd I will send thee, and thy mother that bore thee, into a strange country, in which you were not born, and there you shall die:€€ƒx]And they shall not return into the land, whereunto they lift up their mind to return thither.€€ƒx²Is this man Jechonias an earthen and a broken vessel? is he a vessel wherein is no pleasure? why are they cast out, he and his seed, and are cast into a land which they know not?€€ƒx1O earth, earth, earth, hear the word of the Lord.€€ƒxÈThus saith the Lord: Write this man barren, a man that shall not prosper in his days: for there shall not be a man of his seed that shall sit upon the throne of David, and have power any more in Juda.€€ƒ`€€ƒxRWoe to the pastors, that destroy and tear the sheep of my pasture, saith the Lord.€€ƒxëTherefore thus saith the Lord the God of Israel to the pastors that feed my people: You have scattered my flock, and driven them away, and have not visited them: behold I will visit upon you for the evil of your doings, saith the Lord.€€ƒxÅAnd I will gather together the remnant of my flock, out of all the lands into which I have cast them out: and I will make them return to their own fields, and they shall increase and be multiplied.€€ƒx²And I will set up pastors over them, and they shall feed them: they shall fear no more, and they shall not be dismayed: and none shall be wanting of their number, saith the Lord.€€ƒx±Behold the days come, saith the Lord, and I will raise up to David a just branch: and a king shall reign, and shall be wise: and shall execute judgment and justice in the earth.€€ƒxŒIn those days shall Juda be saved, and Israel shall dwell confidently: and this is the name that they shall call him: The Lord our just one.€€ƒxœTherefore behold the days come, saith the Lord, and they shall say no more: The Lord liveth, who brought up the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt:€€ƒxÜBut, The Lord liveth, who hath brought out, and brought hither the seed of the house of Israel from the land of the north, and out of all the lands, to which I had cast them forth: and they shall dwell in their own land.€€ƒxÁTo the prophets: My heart is broken within me, all my bones tremble: I am become as a drunken man, and as a man full of wine, at the presence of the Lord, and at the presence of his holy words.€€ƒx¾Because the land is full of adulterers, because the land hath mourned by reason of cursing, the fields of the desert are dried up: and their course is become evil, and their strength unlike.€€ƒxjFor the prophet and the priest are defiled: and in my house I have found their wickedness, saith the Lord.€€ƒxºTherefore their way shall be as a slippery way in the dark: for they shall be driven on, and fall therein: for I will bring evils upon them, the year of their visitation, saith the Lord.€€ƒxhAnd I have seen folly in the prophets of Samaria: they prophesied in Baal and deceived my people Israel.€€ƒyAnd I have seen the likeness of adulterers, and the way of lying in the prophets of Jerusalem: and they strengthened the hands of the wicked, that no man should return from his evil doings, they are all become unto me as Sodom, and the inhabitants thereof as Gomorrha.€€ƒxÏTherefore thus saith the Lord of hosts to the prophets: Behold I will feed them with wormwood, and will give them gall to drink: for from the prophets of Jerusalem corruption is gone forth into all the land.€€ƒx»Thus saith the Lord of hosts: Hearken not to the words of the prophets that prophesy to you, and deceive you: they speak a vision of their own heart, and not out of the mouth of the Lord.€€ƒx¾They say to them that blaspheme me: The Lord hath said: You shall have peace: and to every one that walketh in the perverseness of his own heart, they have said: No evil shall come upon you.€€ƒx{For who hath stood in the counsel of the Lord, and hath seen and heard his word? Who hath considered his word and heard it?€€ƒx„Behold the whirlwind of the Lord's indignation shall come forth, and a tempest shall break out and come upon the head of the wicked.€€•ƒxVI did not send prophets, yet they ran: I have not spoken to them, yet they prophesied.€€ƒx—If they had stood in my counsel, and had made my words known to my people, I should have turned them from their evil way, and from their wicked doings.€€ƒxFAm I, think ye, a God at hand, saith the Lord, and not a God afar off?€€ƒxwShall a man be hid in secret places, and I not see him, saith the Lord? do not I fill heaven and earth, saith the Lord?€€ƒxlI have heard what the prophets said, that prophesy lies in my name, and say: I have dreamed, I have dreamed.€€ƒx{How long shall this be in the heart of the prophets that prophesy lies, and that prophesy the delusions of their own heart?€€ƒx•Who seek to make my people forget my name through their dreams, which they tell every man to his neighbour: as their fathers forgot my name for Baal.€€ƒxªThe prophet that hath a dream, let him tell a dream: and he that hath my word, let him speak my word with truth: what hath the chaff to do with the wheat, saith the Lord?€€ƒx]Are not my words as a fire, saith the Lord: and as a hammer that breaketh the rock in pieces?€€ƒxlTherefore behold I am against the prophets, saith the Lord: who steal my words every one from his neighbour.€€ƒxdBehold I am against the prophets, saith the Lord: who use their tongues, and say: The Lord saith it.€€ƒxüBehold I am against the prophets that have lying dreams, saith the Lord: and tell them, and cause my people to err by their lying, and by their wonders: when I sent them not, nor commanded them, who have not profited this people at all, saith the Lord.€€ƒxÅIf therefore this people, or the prophet, or the priest shall ask thee, saying: What is the burden of the Lord? thou shalt say to them: You are the burden: for I will cast you away, saith the Lord.€€ƒxAnd as for the prophet, and the priest, and the people that shall say: The burden of the Lord: I will visit upon that man, and upon his house.€€ƒx~Thus shall you say every one to his neighbour, and to his brother, What hath the Lord answered? and what hath the Lord spoken?€€ƒx²And the burden of the Lord shall be mentioned no more, for every man's word shall be his burden: for you have perverted the words of the living God, of the Lord of hosts our God.€€ƒxdThus shalt thou say to the prophet: What hath the Lord answered thee? and what hath the Lord spoken?€€ƒxÆBut if you shall say: The burden of the Lord: therefore thus saith the Lord: Because you have said this word: The burden of the Lord: and I have sent to you, saying: Say not, The burden of the Lord:€€ƒx”Therefore behold I will take you away carrying you, and will forsake you, and the city which I gave to you, and to your fathers, out of my presence.€€ƒxhAnd I will bring an everlasting reproach upon you, and a perpetual shame which shall never be forgotten.€€ƒ`€€dAmos¤dnamedAmosiosis_namedAmoslabbreviationdAmoshchapters‰ƒx§And the Lord said to me: What seest thou, Jeremiah? And I said: Figs, the good figs, very good: and the bad figs, very bad, which cannot be eaten because they are bad.€€ƒx,And the word of the Lord came to me, saying:€€ƒx¼Thus saith the Lord the God of Israel: Like these good figs, so will I regard the captives of Juda, whom I have sent forth out of this place into the land of the Chaldeans, for their good.€€ƒx¼And I will set my eyes upon them to be pacified, and I will bring them again into this land: and I will build them up, and not pull them down: and I will plant them, and not pluck them up.€€ƒxªAnd I will give them a heart to know me, that I am the Lord: and they shall be my people, and I will be their God: because they shall return to me with their whole heart.€€ƒxùAnd as the very bad figs, that cannot be eaten, because they are bad: thus saith the Lord: So will I give Sedecias the king of Juda, and his princes, and the residue of Jerusalem, that have remained in this city, and that dwell in the land of Egypt.€€ƒxÈAnd I will deliver them up to vexation, and affliction, to all the kingdoms of the earth: to be a reproach, and a byword, and a proverb, and to be a curse in all places, to which I have cast them out.€€ƒx˜And I will send among them the sword, and the famine, and the pestilence: till they be consumed out of the land which I gave to them, and their fathers.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¾The word that came to Jeremiah concerning all the people of Juda, in the fourth year of Joakim the son of Josias king of Juda, (the same is the first year of Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon,)€€ƒxlWhich Jeremiah the prophet spoke to all the people of Juda, and to all the inhabitants of Jerusalem, saying:€€ƒxóFrom the thirteenth year of Josias the son of Ammon king of Juda until this day: this is the three and twentieth year, the word of the Lord hath come to me, and I have spoken to you, rising before day, and speaking, and you have not hearkened.€€ƒx“And the Lord hath sent to you all his servants the prophets, rising early, and sending, and you have not hearkened, nor inclined your ears to hear.€€ƒx¹When he said: Return ye, every one from his evil way, and from your wicked devices, and you shall dwell in the land which the Lord hath given to you, and your fathers for ever and ever.€€ƒxŒAnd go not after strange gods to serve them, and adore them: nor provoke me to wrath by the works of your hands, and I will not afflict you.€€ƒxLTherefore thus saith the Lord of hosts: Because you have not heard my words:€€ƒybBehold I will send, and take all the kindreds of the north, saith the Lord, and Nabuchodonosor the king of Babylon my servant: and I will bring them against this land, and against the inhabitants thereof, and against all the nations that are round about it: and I will destroy them, and make them an astonishment and a hissing, and perpetual desolations.€€ƒx·And I will take away from them the voice of mirth, and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom, and the voice of the bride, the sound of the mill and the light of the lamp.€€ƒx‚And all this land shall be a desolation, and an astonishment: and all these nations shall serve the king of Babylon seventy years.€€ƒxÍAnd when the seventy years shall be expired, I will punish the king of Babylon, and that nation, saith the Lord, for their iniquity, and the land of the Chaldeans: and I will make it perpetual desolations.€€ƒx¥And I will bring upon that land all my words, that I have spoken against it, all that is written in this book, all that Jeremiah hath prophesied against all nations:€€ƒx¦For they have served them, whereas they were many nations, and great kings: and I will repay them according to their deeds, and according to the works of their hands.€€ƒxµFor thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Take the cup of wine of this fury at my hand: and thou shalt make all the nations to drink thereof, into which I shall send thee.€€ƒxfAnd they shall drink, and be troubled, and be mad because of the sword, which I shall send among them.€€ƒx|And I took the cup at the hand of the Lord, and I presented it to all the nations to drink of it, to which the Lord sent me:€€ƒx¼To wit, Jerusalem, and the cities of Juda, and the kings thereof, and the princes thereof: to make them a desolation, and an astonishment, and a hissing, and a curse, as it is at this day.€€ƒxPPharao the king of Egypt, and his servants, and his princes, and all his people,€€ƒxªAnd all in general: all the kings of the land of Ausitis, and all the kings of the land of the Philistines, and Ascalon, and Gaza, and Accaron, and the remnant of Azotus.€€ƒx.And Edom, and Moab, and the children of Ammon.€€ƒxxAnd all the kings of Tyre, and all the kings of Sidon: and the kings of the land of the islands that are beyond the sea.€€ƒxFAnd Dedan, and Thema, and Buz, and all that have their hair cut round.€€ƒxWAnd all the kings of Zambri, and all the kings of Elam, and all the kings of the Medes:€€ƒx½And all the kings of the north far and near, every one against his brother: and all the kingdoms of the earth, which are upon the face thereof: and the king of Sesac shall drink after them.€€ƒxÀAnd thou shalt say to them: Thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Drink ye, and be drunken, and vomit: and fall, and rise no more, because of the sword, which I shall send among you.€€ƒxˆAnd if they refuse to take the cup at thy hand to drink, thou shalt say to them: Thus saith the Lord of hosts: Drinking you shall drink:€€ƒxóFor behold I begin to bring evil on the city wherein my name is called upon: and shall you be as innocent and escape free? you shall not escape free: for I will call for the sword upon all the inhabitants of the earth, saith the Lord of hosts.€€ƒyFAnd thou shalt prophesy unto them all these words, and thou shalt say to them: I The Lord shall roar from on high, and shall utter his voice from his holy habitation: roaring he shall roar upon the place of his beauty: the shout as it were of them that tread grapes shall be given out against all the inhabitants of the earth.€€ƒxÍThe noise is come even to the ends of the earth: for the Lord entereth into judgment with the nations: he entereth into judgment with all flesh; the wicked I have delivered up to the sword, saith the Lord.€€ƒxThus saith the Lord of hosts: Behold evil shall go forth from nation to nation: and a great whirlwind shall go forth from the ends of the earth.€€ƒxåAnd the slain of the Lord shall be at that day from one end of the earth even to the other end thereof: they shall not be lamented, and they shall not be gathered up, nor buried: they shall lie as dung upon the face of the earth.€€ƒxÈHowl, ye shepherds, and cry: and sprinkle yourselves with ashes, ye leaders of the flock: for the days of your slaughter and your dispersion are accomplished, and you shall fall like precious vessels.€€ƒx]And the shepherds shall have no way to flee, nor the leaders of the flock to save themselves.€€ƒx~A voice of the cry of the shepherds, and a howling of the principal of the flock: because the Lord hath wasted their pastures.€€ƒxQAnd the fields of peace have been silent because of the fierce anger of the Lord.€€ƒx’He hath forsaken his covert as the lion, for the land is laid waste because of the wrath of the dove, and because of the fierce anger of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx÷Thus saith the Lord: stand in the court of the house of the Lord, and speak to all the cities of Juda, out of which they come, to adore in the house of the Lord, all the words which I have commanded thee to speak unto them: leave not out one word.€€ƒxªIf so be they will hearken and be converted every one from his evil way; that I may repent me of the evil that I think to do unto them for the wickedness of their doings.€€ƒxyAnd thou shalt say to them: Thus saith the Lord: If you will not hearken to me to walk in my law, which I have given you:€€ƒx‚To give ear to the words of my servants the prophets, whom I sent to you rising up early: and sending, and you have not hearkened:€€ƒxdI will make this house like Silo: and I will make this city a curse to all the nations of the earth.€€ƒxsAnd the priests, and the prophets, and all the people heard Jeremiah speaking these words in the house of the Lord.€€ƒxÒAnd when Jeremiah had made an end of speaking all that the Lord had commanded him to speak to all the people, the priests, and the prophets, and all the people laid hold on him, saying: Let him be put to death.€€ƒxèWhy hath he prophesied in the name of the Lord, saying: This house shall be like Silo; and this city shall be made desolate, without an inhabitant? And all the people were gathered together against Jeremiah in the house of the Lord.€€ƒx¬And the princes of Juda heard these words: and they went up from the king's house into the house of the Lord, and sat in the entry of the new gate of the house of the Lord.€€ƒxÌAnd the priests and the prophets spoke to the princes, and to all the people, saying: The judgment of death is for this man: because he hath prophesied against this city, as you have heard with your ears.€€ƒx¶Then Jeremiah spoke to all the princes, and to all the people, saying: The Lord sent me to prophesy concerning this house, and concerning this city all the words that you have heard.€€ƒx¨Now therefore amend your ways, and your doings, and hearken to the voice of the Lord your God: and the Lord will repent him of the evil that he hath spoken against you.€€ƒxYBut as for me, behold I am in your hands: do with me what is good and right in your eyes:€€˜ƒx´Then the princes, and all the people said to the priests, and to the prophets: There is no judgment of death for this man: for he hath spoken to us in the name of the Lord our God.€€ƒxgAnd some of the ancients of the land rose up: and they spoke to all the assembly of the people, saying:€€ƒyMicheas of Morasthi was a prophet in the days of Ezechias king of Juda, and he spoke to all the people of Juda, saying: Thus saith the Lord of hosts: Sion shall be ploughed like a field, and Jerusalem shall be a heap of stones: and the mountain of the house the high places of woods.€€ƒxöDid Ezechias king of Juda, and all Juda, condemn him to death? did they not fear the Lord, and beseech the face of the Lord: and the Lord repented of the evil that he had spoken against them? therefore we are doing a great evil against our souls.€€ƒxÉThere was also a man that prophesied in the name of the Lord, Urias the son of Semei of Cariathiarim: and he prophesied against this city, and against this land, according to all the words of Jeremiah.€€ƒx³And Joakim, and all his men in power, and his princes heard these words: and the king sought to put him to death. And Urias heard it, and was afraid, and fled and went into Egypt.€€ƒx^And king Joakim sent men into Egypt, Elnathan the son of Achobor, and men with him into Egypt.€€ƒx¤And they brought Urias out of Egypt: and brought him to king Joakim, and he slew him with the sword: and he cast his dead body into the graves of the common people.€€ƒxSo the hand of Ahicam the son of Saphan was with Jeremiah, that he should not be delivered into the hands of the people, to put him to death.€€ƒ`€€ƒxyIn the beginning of the reign of Joakim the son of Josias king of Juda, this word came to Jeremiah from the Lord, saying:€€ƒx\Thus saith the Lord to me: Make thee bands, and chains: and thou shalt put them on thy neck.€€ƒxúAnd thou shalt send them to the king of Edom, and to the king of Moab, and to the king of the children of Ammon, and to the king of Tyre, and to the king of Sidon: by the hand of the messengers that are come to Jerusalem to Sedecias the king of Juda.€€ƒxŠAnd thou shalt command them to speak to their masters: Thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Thus shall you say to your masters:€€ƒx¸I made the earth, and the men and the beasts that are upon the face of the earth, by my great power, and by my stretched out arm: and I have given it to whom it seemed good in my eyes.€€ƒx¥And now I have given all these lands into the hand of Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon my servant: moreover also the beasts of the field I have given him to serve him.€€ƒxŸAnd all nations shall serve him, and his son, and his son's son: till the time come for his land and himself: and many nations and great kings shall serve him.€€ƒyBut the nation and kingdom that will not serve Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon, and whosoever will not bend his neck under the yoke of the king of Babylon: I will visit upon that nation with the sword, and with famine, and with pestilence, saith the Lord: till I consume them by his hand.€€ƒxTherefore hearken not to your prophets, and diviners, and dreamers, and soothsayers, and sorcerers, that say to you: You shall not serve the king of Babylon.€€ƒxmFor they prophesy lies to you: to remove you far from your country, and cast you out, and to make you perish.€€ƒxÍBut the nation that shall bend down their neck under the yoke of the king of Babylon, and shall serve him: I will let them remain in their own land, saith the Lord: and they shall till it, and dwell in it.€€ƒx½And I spoke to Sedecias the king of Juda according to all these words, saying: Bend down your necks under the yoke of the king of Babylon, and serve him, and his people, and you shall live.€€ƒx­Why will you die, thou and thy people by the sword, and by famine, and by the pestilence, as the Lord hath spoken against the nation that will not serve the king of Babylon?€€ƒx{Hearken not to the words of the prophets that say to you: You shall not serve the king of Babylon: for they tell you a lie.€€ƒx«For I have not sent them, saith the Lord: and they prophesy in my name falsely: to drive you out, and that you may perish, both you, and the prophets that prophesy to you.€€ƒyI spoke also to the priests, and to this people, saying: Thus saith the Lord: Hearken not to the words of your prophets, that prophesy to you, saying: Behold the vessels of the Lord shall now in a short time be brought again from Babylon: for they prophesy a lie unto you.€€ƒx€Therefore hearken not to them, but serve the king of Babylon, that you may live. Why should this city be given up to desolation?€€ƒx‹For thus saith the Lord of hosts to the pillars, and to the sea, and to the bases, and to the rest of the vessels that remain in this city:€€ƒxÁWhich Nabuchodonosor the king of Babylon did not take, when he carried away Jechonias the son of Joakim the king of Juda, from Jerusalem to Babylon, and all the great men of Juda and Jerusalem.€€ƒxFor thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel, to the vessels that are left in the house of the Lord, and in the house of the king of Juda and Jerusalem:€€ƒx³They shall be carried to Babylon, and there they shall be until the day of their visitation, saith the Lord: and I will cause them to be brought, and to be restored in this place.€€ƒ`€€ƒy
And it came to pass in that year, in the beginning of the reign of Sedecias king of Juda, in the fourth year, in the fifth month, that Hananias the son of Azur, a prophet of Gabaon spoke to me, in the house of the Lord before the priests, and all the people, saying:€€ƒx^Thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: I have broken the yoke of the king of Babylon.€€ƒxÔAs yet two years of days, and I will cause all the vessels of the house of the Lord to be brought back into this place, which Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon took away from this place, and carried them to Babylon.€€ƒxÇAnd I will bring back to this place Jechonias the son of Joakim king of Juda, and all the captives of Juda, that are gone to Babylon, saith the Lord: for I will break the yoke of the king of Babylon.€€ƒx And Jeremiah the prophet said to Hananias the prophet in the presence of the priests, and in the presence of all the people that stood in the house of the Lord:€€ƒxìAnd Jeremiah the prophet said: Amen, the Lord do so: the Lord perform thy words, which thou hast prophesied: that the vessels may be brought again into the house of the Lord, and all the captives may return out of Babylon to this place.€€ƒxXNevertheless hear this word that I speak in thy ears, and in the ears of all the people:€€ƒxÂThe prophets that have been before me, and before thee from the beginning, and have prophesied concerning many countries, and concerning great kingdoms, of war, and of affliction, and of famine.€€ƒx}The prophet that prophesied peace: when his word shall come to pass, the prophet shall be known, whom the hath sent in truth.€€‘ƒxÑAnd Hananias spoke in the presence of all the people, saying: Thus saith the Lord: Even so will I break the yoke of Nabuchodonosor the king of Babylon after two full years from off the neck of all the nations.€€ƒx¹And Jeremiah the prophet went his way. And the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah, after that Hananias the prophet had broken the chain from off the neck of Jeremiah the prophet, saying:€€ƒxyGo, and tell Hananias: Thus saith the Lord: Thou hast broken chains of wood, and thou shalt make for them chains of iron.€€ƒxìFor thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: I have put a yoke of iron upon the neck of all these nations, to serve Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon, and they shall serve him: moreover also I have given him the beasts of the earth.€€ƒx™And Jeremiah the prophet said to Hananias the prophet: Hear now, Hananias: the Lord hath not sent thee, and thou hast made this people to trust in a lie.€€ƒxœTherefore thus saith the Lord: Behold I will send thee away from off the face of the earth: this year shalt thou die: for thou hast spoken against the Lord.€€ƒxAAnd Hananias the prophet died in that year, in the seventh month.€€ƒ`€€ƒyNow these are the words of the letter which Jeremiah the prophet sent from Jerusalem to the residue of the ancients that were carried into captivity, and to the priests, and to the prophets, and to all the people, whom Nabuchodonosor had carried away from Jerusalem to Babylon:€€ƒx®After that Jechonias the king, and the queen, and the eunuchs, and the princes of Juda, and of Jerusalem, and the craftsmen, and the engravers were departed out of Jerusalem:€€ƒxBy the hand of Elasa the son of Saphan, and Gamarias the son of Helcias, whom Sedecias king of Juda sent to Babylon to Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon, saying:€€ƒx—Thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel, to all that are carried away captives, whom I have caused to be carried away from Jerusalem to Babylon:€€ƒxRBuild ye houses, and dwell in them: and plant orchards, and eat the fruit of them.€€ƒxËTake ye wives, and beget sons and daughters: and take wives for your sons, and give your daughters to husbands, and let them bear sons and daughters: and be ye multiplied there, and be not few in number.€€ƒx¢And seek the peace of the city, to which I have caused you to be carried away captives; and pray to the Lord for it: for in the peace thereof shall be your peace.€€ƒx·For thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Let not your prophets that are in the midst of you, and your diviners deceive you: and give no heed to your dreams which you dream:€€ƒxVFor they prophesy falsely to you in my name: and I have not sent them, saith the Lord.€€ƒxŒFor I know the thoughts that I think towards you, saith the Lord, thoughts of peace, and not of affliction, to give you an end and patience.€€ƒx\And you shall call upon me, and you shall go. and you shall pray to me, and I will hear you.€€ƒxQYou shall seek me, and shall find me: when you shall seek me with all your heart.€€ƒyAnd I will be found by you, saith the Lord: and I will bring back your captivity, and I will gather you out of all nations, and from all the places to which I have driven you out, saith the Lord: and I will bring you back from the place to which I caused you to be carried away captive.€€ƒxFBecause you have said: The Lord hath raised us up prophets in Babylon:€€ƒx»For thus saith the Lord to the king that sitteth upon the throne of David, and to all the people that dwell in this city, to your brethren that are not gone forth with you into captivity.€€ƒx½Thus saith the Lord of hosts: Behold I will send upon them the sword, and the famine, and the pestilence: and I will make them like bad figs that cannot be eaten, because they are very bad.€€ƒyAnd I will persecute them with the sword, and with famine, and with the pestilence: and I will give them up unto affliction to all the kingdoms of the earth: to be a curse, and an astonishment, and a hissing, and a reproach to all the nations to which I have driven them out:€€ƒx´Because they have not hearkened to my words, saith the Lord: which I sent to them by my servants the prophets, rising by night, and sending: and you have not heard, saith the Lord.€€ƒxpHear ye therefore the word of the Lord, all ye of the captivity, whom I have sent out from Jerusalem to Babylon.€€ƒyThus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel, to Achab the son of Colias, and to Sedecias the son of Maasias, who prophesy unto you in my name falsely: Behold I will deliver them up into the hands of Nabuchodonosor the king of Babylon: and he shall kill them before your eyes.€€ƒx¾And of them shall be taken up a curse by all the captivity of Juda, that are in Babylon, saying: The Lord make thee like Sedecias, and like Achab, whom the king of Babylon fried in the fire:€€ƒxÙBecause they have acted folly in Israel, and have committed adultery with the wives of their friends, and have spoken lying words in my name, which I commanded them not: I am the judge and the witness, saith the Lord.€€ƒx-And to Semeias the Nehelamite thou shalt say:€€ƒxÉThe Lord hath made thee priest instead of Joiada the priest, that thou shouldst be ruler in the house of the Lord, over every man that raveth and prophesieth, to put him in the stocks, and into prison.€€ƒxSAnd now why hast thou not rebuked Jeremiah the Anathothite, who prophesieth to you?€€ƒx•For he hath also sent to us in Babylon, saying: It is a long time: build ye houses, and dwell in them: and plant gardens, and eat the fruits of them.€€ƒxQSo Sophonias the priest read this letter, in the hearing of Jeremiah the prophet.€€ƒx2And the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah, saying:€€ƒx¼Send to all them of the captivity, saying: Thus saith the Lord to Semeias the Nehelamite: Because Semeias hath prophesied to you, and I sent him not: and hath caused you to trust in a lie:€€ƒyTherefore thus saith the Lord: behold I will visit upon Semeias the Nehelamite, and upon his seed: he shall not have a man to sit in the midst of this people, and he shall not see the good that I will do to my people, saith the Lord: because he hath spoken treason against the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx=This is the word that came to Jeremiah from the Lord, saying:€€ƒxoThus saith the Lord, the God of Israel, saying: Write thee all the words that I have spoken to thee, in a book.€€ƒxâFor behold the days come, saith the Lord, and I will bring again the captivity of my people Israel and Juda, saith the Lord: and I will cause them to return to the land which I gave to their fathers, and they shall possess it.€€ƒxHAnd these are the words that the Lord hath spoken to Israel and to Juda:€€ƒxUFor thus saith the Lord: We have heard a voice of terror: there is fear and no peace.€€ƒxœAsk ye, and see if a man bear children? why then have I seen every man with his hands on his loins, like a woman in labour, and all faces are turned yellow?€€ƒxŠAlas, for that day is great, neither is there the like to it; and it is the time of tribulation to Jacob, but he shall be saved out of it.€€dObad¤dnamegObadiahiosis_namedObadlabbreviationdObadhchapters•ƒy1Therefore fear thou not, my servant Jacob, saith the Lord, neither be dismayed, O Israel: for behold, I will save thee from a country afar off, and thy seed from the land of their captivity: and Jacob shall return, and be at rest, and abound with all good things, and there shall be none whom he may fear:€€ƒxùFor I am with thee, saith the Lord, to save thee: for I will utterly consume all the nations, among which I have scattered thee: but I will not utterly consume thee: but I will chastise thee in judgment, that thou mayst not seem to thyself innocent.€€ƒxMFor thus saith the Lord: Thy bruise is incurable, thy wound is very grievous.€€ƒxRThere is none to judge thy judgment to bind it up: thou hast no healing medicines.€€ƒxÓAll thy lovers have forgotten thee, and will not seek after thee: for I have wounded thee with the wound of an enemy, with cruel chastisement: by reason of the multitude of thy iniquities, thy sins are hardened.€€ƒx›Why criest thou for thy affliction? thy sorrow is incurable: for the multitude of thy iniquity, and for thy hardened sins I have done these things to thee.€€ƒxÊTherefore all they that devour thee, shall be devoured: and all thy enemies shall be carried into captivity: and they that waste thee shall be wasted, and all that prey upon thee will I give for a prey.€€ƒx°For I will close up thy scar, and will heal thee of thy wounds, saith the Lord. Because they have called thee, O Sion, an outcast: This is she that hath none to seek after her.€€ƒxçThus saith the Lord: Behold I will bring back the captivity of the pavilions of Jacob, and will have pity on his houses, and the city shall be built in her high place, and the temple shall be founded according to the order thereof.€€ƒx¼And out of them shall come forth praise, and the voice of them that play: and I will multiply them, and they shall not be made few: and I will glorify them, and they shall not be lessened.€€ƒx“And their children shall be as from the beginning, and their assembly shall be permanent before me: and I will visit against all that afflict them.€€ƒxàAnd their leader shall be of themselves: and their prince shall come forth from the midst of them: and I will bring him near, and he shall come to me: for who is this that setteth his heart to approach to me, saith the Lord?€€ƒx3And you shall be my people: and I will be your God.€€ƒxsBehold the whirlwind of the Lord, his fury going forth, a violent storm, it shall rest upon the head of the wicked.€€ƒx­The Lord will not turn away the wrath of his indignation, till he have executed and performed the thought of his heart: in the latter days you shall understand these things.€€ƒ`€€ƒxkAt that time, saith the Lord, I will be the God of all the families of Israel, and they shall be my people.€€ƒx‚Thus saith the Lord: The people that were left and escaped from the sword, found grace in the desert: Israel shall go to his rest.€€ƒx‰The Lord hath appeared from afar to me. Yea I have loved thee with an everlasting love, therefore have I drawn thee, taking pity on thee.€€ƒx²And I will build thee again, and thou shalt be built, O virgin of Israel: thou shalt again be adorned with thy timbrels, and shalt go forth in the dances of them that make merry.€€ƒxŒThou shalt yet plant vineyards in the mountains of Samaria: the planters shall plant, and they shall not gather the vintage before the time.€€eJonah¤dnameeJonahiosis_nameeJonahlabbreviationeJonahhchapters„‘ƒxûBehold I will bring them from the north country, and will gather them from the ends of the earth and among them shall be the blind, and the lame, the woman with child, and she that is bringing forth, together, a great company of them returning hither.€€ƒxãThey shall come with weeping: and I will bring them back in mercy: and I will bring them through the torrents of waters in a right way, and they shall not stumble in it: for I am a father to Israel, and Ephraim is my firstborn.€€ƒxÁHear the word of the Lord, O ye nations, and declare it in the islands that are afar off, and say: He that scattered Israel will gather him: and he will keep him as the shepherd doth his flock.€€ƒxeFor the Lord hath redeemed Jacob, and delivered him out of the hand of one that was mightier than he.€€ƒyAnd they shall come, and shall give praise in mount Sion: and they shall flow together to the good things of the Lord, for the corn, and wine, and oil, and the increase of cattle and herds, and their soul shall be as a watered garden, and they shall be hungry no more.€€ƒx¸Then shall the virgin rejoice in the dance, the young men and old men together: and I will turn their mourning into joy, and will comfort them, and make them joyful after their sorrow.€€ƒxxAnd I will fill the soul of the priests with fatness: and my people shall be filled with my good things, saith the Lord.€€ƒx¸Thus saith the Lord: A voice was heard on high of lamentation, of mourning, and weeping, of Rachel weeping for her children and refusing to be comforted for them, because they are not.€€ƒx²Thus saith the Lord: Let thy voice cease from weeping, and thy eyes tears: for there is a reward for thy work, saith the Lord: and they shall return out of the land of the enemy.€€ƒxgAnd there is hope for thy last end, saith the Lord: and the children shall return to their own borders.€€ƒxÓHearing I heard Ephraim when he went into captivity: thou hast chastised me, and I was instructed, as a young bullock unaccustomed to the yoke. Convert me, and I shall be converted, for thou art the Lord my God.€€ƒx±For after thou didst convert me, I did penance: and after thou didst shew unto me, I struck my thigh: I am confounded and ashamed, because I have borne the reproach of my youth.€€ƒxÏSurely Ephraim is an honourable son to me, surely he is a tender child: for since I spoke of him, I will still remember him. Therefore are my bowels troubled for him: pitying I will pity him, saith the Lord.€€ƒx©Set thee up a watchtower, make to thee bitterness: direct thy heart into the right way, wherein thou hast walked: return, O virgin of Israel, return to these thy cities.€€ƒxšHow long wilt thou be dissolute in deliciousness, O wandering daughter? for the Lord hath created a new thing upon the earth: A WOMAN SHALL COMPASS A MAN.€€ƒxèThus saith the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: As yet shall they say this word in the land of Juda, and in the cities thereof, when I shall bring back their captivity: The Lord bless thee, the beauty of justice, the holy mountain.€€ƒxhAnd Juda and all his cities shall dwell therein together: the husbandman and they that drive the flocks.€€ŠƒxZUpon this I was as it were awaked out of a sleep, and I saw, and my sleep was sweet to me.€€ƒx‘Behold the days come, saith the Lord: and I will sow the house of Israel and the house of Juda with the seed of men, and with the seed of beasts.€€ƒxµAnd as I have watched over them, to pluck up, and to throw down, and to scatter, and destroy, and afflict: so will I watch over them, to build up, and to plant them, saith the Lord.€€ƒxyIn those days they shall say no more: The fathers have eaten a sour grape, and the teeth of the children are set on edge.€€ƒxvBut every one shall die for his own iniquity: every man that shall eat the sour grape, his teeth shall be set on edge.€€ƒx€Behold the days shall come, saith the Lord, and I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel, and with the house of Juda:€€ƒxßNot according to the covenant which I made with their fathers, in the day that I took them by the hand to bring them out of the land of Egypt, the covenant which they made void, and I had dominion over them, saith the Lord.€€ƒxéBut this shall be the covenant that I will make with the house of Israel, after those days, saith the Lord: I will give my law in their bowels, and I will write it in their heart: and I will be their God, and they shall be my people.€€ƒyAnd they shall teach no more every man his neighbour, and every man his brother, saying: Know the Lord: for all shall know me from the least of them even to the greatest, saith the Lord: for I will forgive their iniquity, and I will remember their sin no more.€€ƒxÙThus saith the Lord, who giveth the sun for the light of the day, the order of the moon and of the stars, for the light of the night: who stirreth up the sea, and the waves thereof roar, the Lord of hosts is his name.€€ŠƒxÌThus saith the Lord: If the heavens above can be measured, and the foundations of the earth searched out beneath, I also will cast away all the seed of Israel, for all that they have done, saith the Lord.€€ƒx‰Behold the days come, saith the Lord, that the city shall be built to the Lord from the tower of Hanameel even to the gate of the corner.€€ƒxjAnd the measuring line shall go out farther in his sight upon the hill Gareb: and it shall compass Goatha,€€ƒyAnd the whole valley of dead bodies, and of ashes, and all the country of death, even to the torrent Cedron, and to the corner of the horse gate towards the east, the Holy of the Lord: it shall not be plucked up, and it shall not be destroyed any more for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‹The word that came to Jeremiah from the Lord in the tenth year of Sedecias king of Juda: the same is the eighteenth year of Nabuchodonosor.€€ƒx­At that time the army of the king of Babylon besieged Jerusalem: and Jeremiah the prophet was shut up in the court of the prison, which was in the house of the king of Juda.€€ƒxÀFor Sedecias king of Juda had shut him up, saying: Why dost thou prophesy, saying: Thus saith the Lord: Behold I will give this city into the hand of the king of Babylon, and he shall take it?€€ƒxØAnd Sedecias king of Juda shall not escape out of the hand of the Chaldeans: but he shall be delivered into the hands of the king of Babylon: and he shall speak to him mouth to mouth, and his eyes shall see his eyes.€€ƒx¦And he shall lead Sedecias to Babylon: and he shall be there till I visit him, saith the Lord. But if you will fight against the Chaldeans, you shall have no success.€€‹ƒx¢Behold, Hanameel the son of Sellum thy cousin shall come to thee, saying: Buy thee my field, which is in Anathoth, for it is thy right to buy it, being next akin.€€ƒy6And Hanameel my uncle's son came to me, according to the word of the Lord, to the entry of the prison, and said to me: Buy my field, which is in Anathoth in the land of Benjamin: for the right of inheritance is thine, and thou art next of kin to possess it. And I understood that this was the word of the Lord.€€ƒxAnd I bought the field of Hanameel my uncle's son, that is in Anathoth: and I weighed him the money, seven staters, and ten pieces of silver.€€ƒxhAnd I wrote it in a book and sealed it, and took witnesses: and I weighed him the money in the balances.€€ƒx‰And I took the deed of the purchase that was sealed, and the stipulations, and the ratifications with the seals that were on the outside.€€ƒyAnd I gave the deed of the purchase to Baruch the son of Neri the son of Maasias in the sight of Hanameel my uncle's son, in the presence of the witnesses that subscribed the book of the purchase, and before all the Jews that sat in the court of the prison.€€ƒx)And I charged Baruch before them, saying:€€ƒxÒThus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Take these writings, this deed of the purchase that is sealed up, and this deed that is open: and put them in an earthen vessel, that they may continue many days.€€ƒx|For thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Houses, and fields, and vineyards shall be possessed again in this land.€€ƒxgAnd after I had delivered the deed of purchase to Baruch the son of Neri, I prayed to the Lord, saying:€€ƒx’Alas, alas, alas, O Lord God, behold thou hast made heaven and earth by thy great power, and thy stretched out arm: no word shall be hard to thee:€€cMic¤dnameeMicahiosis_namecMiclabbreviationcMichchapters‡ƒx’Who hast set signs and wonders in the land of Egypt even until this day, and in Israel, and amongst men, and hast made thee a name as at this day.€€ƒx¨And hast brought forth thy people Israel, out of the land of Egypt with signs, and with wonders, and with a strong hand, and a stretched out arm, and with great terror.€€ƒxwAnd hast given them this land which thou didst swear to their fathers, to give them a land flowing with milk and honey.€€ƒxÓAnd they came in, and possessed it: but they obeyed not thy voice, and they walked not in thy law: and they did not any of those things that thou didst command them to do, and all these evils are come upon them.€€ƒxüBehold works are built up against the city to take it: and the city is given into the hands of the Chaldeans, who fight against it, by the sword, and the famine, and the pestilence: and what thou hast spoken, is all come to pass, as thou thyself seest.€€ƒxˆAnd sayest thou to me, O Lord God: Buy a field for money, and take witnesses, whereas the city is given into the hands of the Chaldeans?€€ƒx2And the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah, saying:€€ƒxJBehold I am the Lord the God of all flesh: shall any thing be hard for me?€€ƒx¢Therefore thus saith the Lord: Behold I will deliver this city into the hands of the Chaldeans, and into the hands of the king of Babylon, and they shall take it.€€ƒxäAnd the Chaldeans that fight against this city, shall come and set it on fire, and burn it, with the houses upon whose roofs they offered sacrifice to Baal, and poured out drink offerings to strange gods, to provoke me to wrath.€€ƒxÏFor the children of Israel, and the children of Juda, have continually done evil in my eyes from their youth: the children of Israel who even till now provoke me with the work of their hands, saith the Lord.€€ƒx˜For this city hath been to me a provocation and indignation from the day that they built it, until this day, in which it shall be taken out of my sight.€€ƒxûBecause of all the evil of the children of Israel, and of the children of Juda, which they have done, provoking me to wrath, they and their kings, their princes, and their priests, and their prophets, the men of Juda, and the inhabitants of Jerusalem.€€ƒx­And they have turned their backs to me, and not their faces: when I taught them early in the morning, and instructed them, and they would not hearken to receive instruction.€€ƒxZAnd they have set their idols in the house, in which my name is called upon, to defile it.€€ƒyAnd they have built the high places of Baal, which are in the valley of the son of Ennom, to consecrate their sons and their daughters to Moloch: which I commanded them not, neither entered it into my heart, that they should do this abomination, and cause Juda to sin.€€ƒxäBehold I will gather them together out of all the lands to which I have cast them out in my anger, and in my wrath, and in my great indignation: and I will bring them again into this place, and will cause them to dwell securely.€€ƒx5And they shall be my people, and I will be their God.€€ƒx“And I will give them one heart, and one way, that they may fear me all days: and that it may be well with them, and with their children after them.€€ƒx And I will make an everlasting covenant with them, and will not cease to do them good: and I will give my fear in their heart, that they may not revolt from me.€€ƒxAnd I will rejoice over them, when I shall do them good: and I will plant them in this land in truth, with my whole heart, and with all my soul.€€ƒx‘For thus saith the Lord: As I have brought upon this people all this great evil: so will I bring upon them all the good that I now speak to them.€€ƒx°And fields shall be purchased in this land: whereof you say that it is desolate, because there remaineth neither man nor beast, and it is given into the hands of the Chaldeans.€€ƒy\Fields shall be bought for money, and deeds shall be written, and sealed, and witnesses shall be taken, in the land of Benjamin, and round about Jerusalem, in the cities of Juda, and in the cities on the mountains, and in the cities of the plains, and in the cities that are towards the south: for I will bring back their captivity, saith the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxwAnd the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah the second time, while he was yet shut up in the court of the prison, saying:€€ƒxYThus saith the Lord, who will do, and will form it, and prepare it, the Lord is his name.€€ƒxjCry to me and I will hear thee: and I will shew thee great things, and sure things which thou knowest not.€€ƒx¨For thus saith the Lord the God of Israel to the houses of this city, and to the houses of the king of Juda, which are destroyed, and to the bulwarks, and to the sword.€€Œƒx…Behold I will close their wounds and give them health, and I will cure them: and I will reveal to them the prayer of peace and truth.€€ƒxyAnd I will bring back the captivity of Juda, and the captivity of Jerusalem: and I will build them as from the beginning.€€ƒx´And I will cleanse them from all their iniquity, whereby they have sinned against me: and I will forgive all their iniquities, whereby they have sinned against me, and despised me.€€ƒyAnd it shall be to me a name, and a joy, and a praise, and a gladness before all the nations of the earth, that shall hear of all the good things which I will do to them: and they shall fear and be troubled for all the good things, and for all the peace that I will make for them.€€ƒxúThus saith the Lord: There shall be heard again in this place (which you say is desolate, because there is neither man nor beast: in the cities of Juda, and without Jerusalem, which are desolate without man, and without inhabitant, and without beast)€€ƒyuThe voice of joy and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom and the voice of the bride, the voice of them that shall say: Give ye glory to the Lord of hosts, for the Lord is good, for his mercy endureth for ever: and of them that shall bring their vows into the house of the Lord: for I will bring back the captivity of the land as at the first, saith the Lord.€€ƒxÍThus saith the Lord of hosts: There shall be again in this place that is desolate without man, and without beast, and in all the cities thereof, an habitation of shepherds causing their flocks to lie down.€€ƒyAnd in the cities on the mountains, and in the cities of the plains, and in the cities that are towards the south: and in the land of Benjamin, and round about Jerusalem, and in the cities of Juda shall the flocks pass again under the hand of him that numbereth them, saith the Lord.€€ƒxŒBehold the days come, saith the Lord, that I will perform the good word that I have spoken to the house of Israel, and to the house of Juda.€€ƒxIn those days, and at that time, I will make the bud of justice to spring forth unto David, and he shall do judgment and justice in the earth.€€ƒxŒIn those days shall Juda be saved, and Jerusalem shall dwell securely: and this is the name that they shall call him, The Lord our just one.€€ƒxsFor thus saith the Lord: There shall not be cut off from David a man to sit upon the throne of the house of Israel.€€ƒx2And the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah, saying:€€ƒx›Thus saith the Lord: if my covenant, with the day can be made void, and my covenant with the night, that there should not be day and night in their season:€€ƒxŸAlso my covenant with David my servant may be made void, that he should not have a son to reign upon his throne, and with the Levites and priests my ministers.€€ƒxAs the stars of heaven cannot be numbered, nor the sand of the sea be measured: so will I multiply the seed of David my servant, and the Levites my ministers.€€ƒx2And the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah, saying:€€ƒxÀHast thou not seen what this people hath spoken, saying: The two families which the Lord had chosen, are cast off: and they have despised my people, so that it is no more a nation before them?€€ƒxgThus saith the Lord. If I have not set my covenant between day and night, and laws to heaven and earth:€€ƒxäSurely I will also cast off the seed of Jacob, and of David my servant, so as not to take any of his seed to be rulers of the seed of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob: for I will bring back their captivity, and will have mercy on them.€€ƒ`€€ƒyThe word that came to Jeremiah from the Lord, when Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon, and all his army, and all the kingdoms of the earth, that were under the power of his hand, and all the people fought against Jerusalem and against all the cities thereof, saying:€€ƒxÛThus saith the Lord, the God of Israel: Go, and speak to Sedecias king of Juda, and say to him: Thus saith the Lord: Behold I will deliver this city into the hands of the king of Babylon, and he shall burn it with fire.€€ƒxõAnd thou shalt not escape out of his hand: but thou shalt surely be taken, and thou shalt be delivered into his hand: and thy eyes shall see the eyes of the king of Babylon, and his mouth shall speak with thy mouth, and thou shalt go to Babylon.€€ƒxuYet hear the word of the Lord, O Sedecias king of Juda: Thus saith the Lord to thee: Thou shalt not die by the sword.€€ƒxYAnd Jeremiah the prophet spoke all these words to Sedecias the king of Juda in Jerusalem.€€ƒxÍAnd the army of the king of Babylon fought against Jerusalem, and against all the cities of Juda that were left, against Lachis, and against Azecha: for these remained of the cities of Juda, fenced cities.€€ƒx’The word that came to Jeremiah from the Lord, after that king Sedecias had made a covenant with all the people in Jerusalem making a proclamation:€€ƒxÇThat every man should let his manservant, and every man his maidservant, being Hebrew man or a Hebrew woman, go free: and that they should not lord it over them, to wit, over the Jews their brethren.€€ƒxğAnd all the princes, and all the people who entered into the covenant, heard that every man should let his manservant, and every man his maidservant go free, and should no more have dominion over them: and they obeyed, and let them go free.€€ƒx³But afterwards they turned: and brought back again their servants and their handmaids, whom they had let go free, and brought them into subjection as menservants and maidservants.€€ƒx@And the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah from the Lord, saying:€€ƒx¨Thus saith the Lord the God of Israel: I made a covenant with your fathers in the day that I brought them out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage, saying:€€ƒxúAt the end of seven years, let ye go every man his brother being a Hebrew, who hath been sold to thee, so he shall serve thee six years: and thou shalt let him go free from thee: and your fathers did not hearken to me, nor did they incline their ear.€€ƒxÃAnd you turned to day, and did that which was right in my eyes, in proclaiming liberty every one to his brother: and you made a covenant in my sight, in the house upon which my name is invocated.€€ƒyAnd you are fallen back, and have defiled my name: and you have brought back again every man his manservant, and every man his maidservant, whom you had let go free, and set at liberty: and you have brought them into subjection to be your servants and handmaids.€€ƒy7Therefore thus saith the Lord: You have not hearkened to me, in proclaiming liberty every man to his brother and every man to his friend: behold I proclaim a liberty for you, saith the Lord, to the sword, to the pestilence, and to the famine: and I will cause you to be removed to all the kingdoms of the earth.€€ƒxÕAnd I will give the men that have transgressed my covenant, and have not performed the words of the covenant which they agreed to in my presence, when they cut the calf in two and passed between the parts thereof:€€ƒxšThe princes of Juda, and the princes of Jerusalem, the eunuchs, and the priests, and all the people of the land that passed between the parts of the calf:€€ƒxÇAnd I will give them into the hands of their enemies, and into the hands of them that seek their life: and their dead bodies shall be for meat to the fowls of the air, and to the beasts of the earth.€€ƒxİBehold I will command, saith the Lord, and I will bring them again to this city, and they shall fight against it, and take it, and burn it with fire: and I will make the cities of Juda a desolation, without an inhabitant.€€ƒ`€€ƒxjThe word that came to Jeremiah from the Lord in the days of Joakim the son of Josias king of Juda, saying:€€ƒx³Go to the house of the Rechabites: and speak to them, and bring them into the house of the Lord, into one of the chambers of the treasures, and thou shalt give them wine to drink.€€ƒxˆAnd I took Jezonias the son of Jeremiah the son of Habsanias, and his brethren, and all his sons, and the whole house of the Rechabites.€€ƒxşAnd I brought them into the house of the Lord, to the treasure house of the sons of Hanan, the son of Jegedelias the man of God, which was by the treasure house of the princes, above the treasure of Maasias the son of Sellum, who was keeper of the entry.€€ƒxxAnd I set before the sons of the house of the Rechabites pots full of wine, and cups: and I said to them: Drink ye wine.€€ƒx³And they answered : We will not drink wine: because Jonadab the son of Rechab, our father, commanded us, saying: You shall drink no wine, neither you, nor your children, for ever:€€ƒxÏNeither shall ye build houses, nor sow reed, nor plant vineyards, nor have any: but you shall dwell in tents all your days, that you may live many days upon the face of the earth, in which you are strangers.€€ƒxÑTherefore we have obeyed the voice of Jonadab the son of Rechab, our father, in all things that he commanded us: so as to drink no wine all our days: neither we, nor our wives, nor our sons, nor our daughters:€€ƒxINor to build houses to dwell in, nor to have vineyard, or field, or seed:€€ƒxiBut we have dwelt in tents, and have been obedient according to all that Jonadab our father commanded us.€€ƒxÛBut when Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon came up to our land, we said: Come, let us go into Jerusalem from the face of the army of the Chaldeans, and from the face of the army of Syria: and we have remained in Jerusalem.€€ƒx2And the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah, saying:€€ƒx¸Thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Go, and say to the men of Juda, and to the inhabitants of Jerusalem: Will you not receive instruction, to obey my words, saith the Lord?€€ƒyThe words of Jonadab the son of Rechab, by which he commanded his sons not to drink wine, have prevailed: and they have drunk none to this day, because they have obeyed the commandment of their father: but I have spoken to you, rising early and speaking, and you have not obeyed me.€€”ƒx›So the sons of Jonadab the son of Rechab have constantly kept the commandment of their father, which he commanded them: but this people hath not obeyed me.€€ƒy"Therefore thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Behold I will bring upon Juda, and upon all the inhabitants of Jerusalem all the evil that I have pronounced against them, because I have spoken to them, and they have not heard: I have called to them, and they have not answered me.€€ƒxêAnd Jeremiah said to the house of the Rechabites: Thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Because you have obeyed the commandment of Jonadab your father, and have kept all his precepts, and have done all that he commanded you:€€ƒx¡Therefore thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: There shall not be wanting a man of the race of Jonadab the son of Rechab, standing before me for ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒx…And it came to pass in the fourth year of Joakim the son of Josias king of Juda, that this word came to Jeremiah by the Lord, saying:€€ƒxáTake thee a roll of a book, and thou shalt write in it all the words that I have spoken to thee against Israel and Juda, and against all the nations from the day that I spoke to thee, from the days of Josias even to this day.€€ƒx¿If so be, when the house of Juda shall hear all the evils that I purpose to do unto them, that they may return every man from his wicked way: and I will forgive their iniquity, and their sin.€€ƒx£So Jeremiah called Baruch the son of Nerias: and Baruch wrote from the mouth of Jeremiah all the words of the Lord, which he spoke to him, upon the roll of a book.€€ƒx^And Jeremiah commanded Baruch, saying: I am shut up, and cannot go into the house of the Lord.€€ƒyGo thou in therefore, and read out of the volume, which thou hast written from my mouth, the words of the Lord, in the hearing of all the people in the house of the Lord on the fasting day: and also thou shalt read them in the hearing of all Juda that come out of their cities:€€ƒxÆIf so be they may present their supplication before the Lord, and may return every one from his wicked way: for great is the wrath and indignation which the Lord hath pronounced against this people.€€ƒx©And Baruch the son of Nerias did according to all that Jeremiah the prophet ,had commanded him, reading out of the volume the words of the Lord in the house of the Lord.€€ƒyAnd it came to pass in the fifth year of Joakim the son of Josias king of Juda, in the ninth month, that they proclaimed a fast before the Lord to all the people in Jerusalem, and to all the people that were come together out of the cities of Juda to Jerusalem.€€ƒxøAnd Baruch read out of the volume the words of Jeremiah in the house of the Lord, in the treasury of Gamarias the son of Saphan the scribe, in the upper court, in the entry of the new gate of the house of the Lord, in the hearing of all the people.€€ƒxkAnd when Micheas the son of Gamarias the son of Saphan had heard out of the book all the words of the Lord,€€ƒyHe went down into the king's house to the secretary's chamber: and behold all the princes sat there, Elisama the scribe, and Dalaias the son of Semeias, and Elnathan the son of Achobor, and Gamarias the son of Saphan, and Sedecias the son of Hananias, and all the princes.€€ƒxvAnd Micheas told them all the words that he had heard when Baruch read out of the volume in the hearing of the people.€€ƒyTherefore all the princes sent Judi the son of Nathanias, the son of Selemias, the son of Chusi, to Baruch, saying: Take in thy hand the volume in which thou hast read in the hearing of the people, and come. So Baruch the son of Nerias took the volume in his hand, and came to them.€€ƒxfAnd they said to him: Sit down and read these things in our hearing. And Baruch read in their hearing.€€cNah¤dnameeNahumiosis_namecNahlabbreviationcNahhchaptersƒƒx„And Baruch said to them: With his mouth he pronounced all these words as if he were reading to me: and I wrote in a volume with ink.€€ƒxmAnd the princes said to Baruch: Go, and hide thee, both thou and Jeremiah, and let no man know where you are.€€ƒx¦And they went in to the king into the court: but they laid up the volume in the chamber of Elisama the scribe: and they told all the words in the hearing of the king.€€ƒxÆAnd the king sent Judi that he should take the volume: who bringing it out of the chamber of Elisama the scribe, read it in the hearing of the king, and of all the princes that stood about the king.€€ƒxrNow the king sat in the winter house, in the ninth month: and there was a hearth before him full of burning coals.€€ƒxÇAnd when Judi had read three or four pages, he cut it with the penknife, and he cast it into the fire, that was upon the hearth, till all the volume was consumed with the fire that was on the hearth.€€ƒxoAnd the king and all his servants that heard all these words were not afraid, nor did they rend their garments.€€ƒxkBut yet Elnathan, and Dalaias, and Gamarias spoke to the king, not to burn the book: and he heard them not.€€ƒxÁAnd the king commanded Jeremiel the son of Amelech, and Saraias the son of Ezriel, and Selemias the son of Abdeel, to take up Baruch the scribe, and Jeremiah the prophet: but the Lord hid them.€€ƒxªAnd the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah the prophet, after that the king had burnt the volume, and the words that Baruch had written from the mouth of Jeremiah, saying:€€ƒxŒTake thee again another volume: and write in it all the former words that were in the first volume which Joakim the king of Juda both burnt.€€ƒyAnd thou shalt say to Joakim the king of Juda: Thus saith the Lord: Thou hast burnt that volume, saying: Why hast thou written therein, and said: The king of Babylon shall come speedily, and shall lay waste this land: and shall cause to cease from thence man and beast?€€ƒxÅTherefore thus saith the Lord against Joakim the king of Juda: He shall have none to sit upon the throne of David: and his dead body shall be cast out to the heat by day, and to the frost by night.€€ƒxòAnd I will punish him, and his seed and his servants, for their iniquities, and I will bring upon them, and upon the inhabitants of Jerusalem, and upon the men of Juda all the evil that I have pronounced against them, but they have not heard.€€ƒyAnd Jeremiah took another volume, and gave it to Baruch the son of Nerias the scribe: who wrote in it from the mouth of Jeremiah all the words of the book which Joakim the king of Juda had burnt with fire: and there were added besides many more words than had been before.€€ƒx–Now king Sedecias the son of Josias reigned instead of Jechonias the son of Joakim: whom Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon made king in the land of Juda.€€ƒxBut neither he, nor his servants, nor the people of the land did obey the words of the Lord, that he spoke in the hand of Jeremiah the prophet.€€ƒx And king Sedecias sent Juchal the son of Selemias, and Sophonias the son of Maasias the priest to Jeremiah the prophet, saying: Pray to the Lord our God for us.€€ƒxíNow Jeremiah walked freely in the midst of the people: for they had not as yet cast him into prison. And the army of Pharao was come out of Egypt: and the Chaldeans that besieged Jerusalem, hearing these tidings, departed from Jerusalem.€€ƒx>And the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah the prophet, saying:€€ƒx×Thus saith the Lord the God of Israel: Thus shall you say to the king of Juda, who sent you to inquire of me: Behold the army of Pharao, which is come forth to help you, shall return into their own land, into Egypt.€€ƒxdAnd the Chaldeans shall come again, and fight against this city, and take it, and burn it with fire.€€ƒx‡Thus saith the Lord: Deceive not your souls, saying: The Chaldeans shall surely depart and go away from us: for they shall not go away.€€ƒxĞBut if you should even beat all the army of the Chaldeans that fight against you, and there should be left of them some wounded men: they shall rise up, every man from his heart, and burn this city with fire.€€ƒxZNow when the army of the Chaldeans was gone away from Jerusalem, because of Pharao's army,€€ƒx‡Jeremiah went forth out of Jerusalem to go into the land of Benjamin: and to divide a possession there in the presence of the citizens,€€ƒxöAnd when he was come to the gate of Benjamin, the captain of the gate, who was there in his turn, was one named Jerias, the son of Selemias, the son of Hananias: and he took hold of Jeremiah the prophet, saying: Thou art fleeing to the Chaldeans.€€ƒxœAnd Jeremiah answered: It is not so, I am not fleeing to the Chaldeans. But he hearkened not to him: so Jerias took Jeremiah and brought him to the princes.€€“ƒxkSo Jeremiah went into the house of the prison, and into the dungeon: and Jeremiah remained there many days.€€ƒxóThen Sedecias the king, sending, took him: and asked him secretly in his house, and said: Is there, thinkest thou, any word from the Lord? And Jeremiah said. There is. And he said: Thou shalt be delivered into the hands of the king of Babylon.€€ƒxAnd Jeremiah said to king Sedecias: In what have I offended against thee, or thy servants, or thy people, that thou hast cast me into prison?€€ƒx€Where are your prophets that prophesied to you, and said: The king of Babylon shall not come against you, and against this land?€€ƒx­Now therefore hear, I beseech thee, my lord the king: let my petition be accepted in thy sight: and send me not back into the house of Jonathan the scribe, lest I die there.€€ƒxıThen king Sedecias commanded that Jeremiah should be committed into the entry of the prison: and that they should give him daily a piece of bread, beside broth, till all the bread in the city were spent: and Jeremiah remained in the entry of the prison.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÀNow Saphatias the son of Mathan, and Gedelias the son of Phassur, and Juchal the son of Selemias, and Phassur the son of Melchias heard the words that Jeremiah spoke to all the people, saying:€€ƒxÛThus saith the Lord: Whosoever shall remain in this city, shall die by the sword, and by famine, and by pestilence: but he that shall go forth to the Chaldeans, shall live, and his life shall be safe, and he shall live.€€ƒx€Thus saith the Lord: This city shall surely be delivered into the hand of the army of the king of Babylon, and he shall take it.€€ƒy(And the princes said to the king. We beseech thee that this man may be put to death: for on purpose he weakeneth the hands of the men of war, that remain in this city, and the hands of the people, speaking to them according to these words: for this man seeketh not peace to this people, but evil.€€ƒxlAnd king Sedecias said: Behold he is in your hands: for it is not lawful for the king to deny you any thing.€€ƒxøThen they took Jeremiah and cast him into the dungeon of Melchias the son of Amelech, which was in the entry of the prison: and they let down Jeremiah by ropes into the dungeon, wherein there was no water, but mire. And Jeremiah sunk into the mire.€€ƒx¨Now Abdemelech the Ethiopian, an eunuch that was in the king's house, heard that they had put Jeremiah in the dungeon: but the king was sitting in the gate of Benjamin.€€ƒxKAnd Abdemelech went out of the king's house, and spoke to the king, saying:€€ƒxÂMy lord the king, these men have done evil in all that they have done against Jeremiah the prophet, casting him into the dungeon to die there with hunger, for there is no more bread in the city.€€ƒx£Then the king commanded Abdemelech the Ethiopian, saying: Take from hence thirty men with thee, and draw up Jeremiah the prophet out of the dungeon, before he die.€€ƒxÕSo Abdemelech taking the men with him, went into the king's house that was under the storehouse: and he took from thence old rags, and old rotten things, and he let them down by cords to Jeremiah into the dungeon.€€ƒx›And Abdemelech the Ethiopian said to Jeremiah: Put these old rags and these rent and rotten things under thy arms, and upon the cords: and Jeremiah did so.€€cHab¤dnamehHabakkukiosis_namecHablabbreviationcHabhchaptersƒ‘ƒx–Then Jeremiah said to Sedecias: If I shall declare it to thee, wilt thou not put me to death? and if I give thee counsel, thou wilt not hearken to me.€€ƒxÌThen king Sedecias swore to Jeremiah, in private, saying: As the Lord liveth, that, made us this soul, I will not put thee to death, nor will I deliver thee into the hands of these men that seek thy life.€€ƒyAnd Jeremiah said to Sedecias: Thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: If thou wilt take a resolution and go out to the princes of the king of Babylon, thy soul shall live, and this city shall not be burnt with fire: and thou shalt be safe, and thy house.€€ƒxÏBut if thou wilt not go out to the princes of the king of Babylon, this city shall be delivered into the hands of the Chaldeans, and they shall burn it with fire: and thou shalt not escape out of their hand.€€ƒx¯And king Sedecias said to Jeremiah: I am afraid because of the Jews that are fled over to the Chaldeans: lest I should be delivered into their hands, and they should abuse me.€€ƒx´But Jeremiah answered: They shall not deliver thee: hearken, I beseech thee, to the word of the Lord, which I speak to the, and it shall be well with thee, and thy soul shall live.€€ƒxMBut if thou wilt not go forth, this is the word which the Lord hath shewn me:€€ƒy<Behold all the women that are left in the house of the king of Juda, shall be brought out to the princes of the king of Babylon: and they shall say: Thy men of peace have deceived thee, and have prevailed against thee, they have plunged thy feet in the mire, and in a slippery place and they have departed from thee.€€ƒxÔAnd all thy wives, and thy children shall be brought out to the Chaldeans, and thou shalt not escape their hands, but thou shalt be taken by the hand of the king of Babylon: and he shall burn this city with fire.€€ƒxTThen Sedecias said to Jeremiah: Let no man know these words, and thou shalt not die.€€ƒxâBut if the princes shall hear that I have spoken with thee, and shall come to thee, and say to thee: Tell us what thou hast said to the king, hide it not from us, and we will not kill thee: and also what the king said to thee:€€ƒxThou shalt say to them: I presented my supplication before the king, that he would not command me to be carried back into the house of Jonathan, to die there.€€ƒx³So all the princes came to Jeremiah, and asked him: and he spoke to them according to all the words that the king had commanded him: and they left him: for nothing had been heard.€€ƒx‡But Jeremiah remained in the entry of the prison, until the day that Jerusalem was taken: and it came to pass that Jerusalem was taken.€€ƒ`€€ƒx™In the ninth year of Sedecias king of Juda, in the tenth month, came Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon, and all his army to Jerusalem, and they besieged it.€€ƒxkAnd in the eleventh year of Sedecias, in the fourth month, the fifth day of the month, the city was opened.€€”ƒxüAnd when Sedecias the king of Juda and all the men of war saw them, they fled: and they went forth in the night out of the city by the way of the king's garden, and by the gate that was between the two walls, and they went out to the way of the desert.€€ƒy
But the army of the Chaldeans pursued after them: and they took Sedecias in the plain of the desert of Jericho, and when they had taken him, they brought him to Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon to Reblatha, which is in the land of Emath: and he gave judgment upon him.€€ƒx…And the king of Babylon slew the sons of Sedecias, in Reblatha, before his eyes: and the king of Babylon slew all the nobles of Juda.€€ƒx[He also put out the eyes of Sedecias: and bound him with fetters, to be carried to Babylon.€€ƒx|And the Chaldeans burnt the king's house, and the houses of the people with fire, and they threw down the wall of Jerusalem.€€ƒxÒAnd Nabuzardan the general of the army carried away captive to Babylon the remnant of the people that remained in the city, and the fugitives that had gone over to him, and the rest of the people that remained.€€ƒxœBut Nabuzardan the general left some of the poor people that had nothing at all, in the land of Juda, and he gave them vineyards, and cisterns at that time.€€ƒxjNow Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon had given charge to Nabuzardan the general concerning Jeremiah, saying:€€ƒx_Take him, and set thy eyes upon him, and do him no harm: but as he hath a mind, so do with him.€€ƒx•Therefore Nabuzardan the general sent, and Nabuzardan, and Rabsares, and Neregel, and Sereser, and Rebmag, and all the nobles of the king of Babylon,€€ƒx¬Sent and took Jeremiah out of the court of the prison, and committed him to Godolias the son of Ahicam the son of Saphan, that he might go home, and dwell among the people.€€ƒx•But the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah, when he was yet shut up in the court of the prison, saying: Go, and tell Abdemelech the Ethiopian, saying:€€ƒx²Thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Behold I will bring my words upon this city unto evil, and not unto good: and they shall be accomplished in thy sight in that day.€€ƒx}And I will deliver thee in that day, saith the Lord: and thou shalt not be given into the hands of the men whom thou fearest:€€ƒx§But delivering, I will deliver thee, and thou shalt not fall by the sword: but thy life shall be saved for thee, because thou hast put thy trust in me, saith the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxõThe word that came to Jeremiah from the Lord, after that Nabuzardan the general had let him go from Rama, when he had taken him, being bound with chains, among all them that were carried away from Jerusalem and Juda, and were carried to Babylon.€€ƒxuAnd the general of the army taking Jeremiah, said to him: The Lord thy God hath pronounced this evil upon this place,€€ƒx®And he hath brought it: and the Lord hath done as he hath said: because you have sinned against the Lord, and have not hearkened to his voice, and this word is come upon you.€€ƒy^Now then behold I have loosed thee this day from the chains which were upon thy hands: if it please thee to come with me to Babylon, come: and I will set my eyes upon thee: but if it do not please thee to come with me to Babylon, stay here: behold all the land is before thee, as thou shalt choose, and whither it shall please thee to go, thither go.€€“ƒx†And Jeremiah went to Godolias the son of Ahicam to Masphath: and dwelt with him in the midst of the people that were left in the land.€€ƒy`And when all the captains of the army that were scattered through the countries, they and their companions, had heard that the king of Babylon had made Godolias the son of Ahicam governor of the country, and that he had committed unto him men and women, and children, and of the poor of the land, them that had not been carried away captive to Babylon:€€ƒxşThey came to Godolias to Masphath: and Ismahel the son of Nathanias, and Johanan, and Jonathan, the sons of Caree, and Sareas the son of Thanehumeth, and the children of Ophi, that were of Netophathi, and Jezonias the son of Maachati, they and their men.€€ƒxÑAnd Godolias the son of Ahicam the son of Saphan swore to them and to their companions, saying: Fear not to serve the Chaldeans: dwell in the land, and serve the king of Babylon, and it shall be well with you.€€ƒxğBehold I dwell in Masphath, that I may answer the commandment of the Chaldeans that are sent to us: but as for you, gather ye the vintage, and the harvest, and the oil, and lay it up in your vessels, and abide in your cities which you hold.€€ƒy	Moreover all the Jews that were in Moab, and among the children of Ammon, and in Edom, and in all the countries, when they heard that the king of Babylon had left a remnant in Judea, and that he had made Godolias the son of Ahicam the son of Saphan ruler over them:€€ƒxºAll the Jews, I say, returned out of all the places to which they had fled, and they came into the land of Juda to Godolias to Masphath: and they gathered wine, and a very great harvest.€€ƒxThen Johanan the son of Caree, and all the captains of the army, that had been scattered about in the countries, came to Godolias to Masphath.€€ƒx¯And they said to him: Know that Baalis the king of the children of Ammon hath sent Ismahel the son of Nathanias to kill thee. And Godolias the son of Ahicam believed them not.€€ƒyBut Johanan the son of Caree, spoke to Godolias privately in Masphath, saying: I will go, and I will kill Ismahel the son of Nathanias, and no man shall know it, lest he kill thee, and all the Jews be scattered, that are gathered unto thee, and the remnant of Juda perish.€€ƒx|And Godolias the son of Ahicam said to Johanan the son of Caree: Do not this thing: for what thou sayst of Ismahel is false.€€ƒ`€€ƒyAnd it came to pass in the seventh month, that Ismahel the son of Nathanias, the son of Elisama of the royal blood, and the nobles of the king, and ten men with him, came to Godolias the son of Ahicam into Masphath: and they ate bread there together in Masphath.€€ƒxàAnd Ismahel the son of Nathanias arose, and the ten men that were with him, and they struck Godolias the son of Ahicam, the son of Saphan with the sword, and slew him whom the king of Babylon had made governor over the land.€€ƒx~Ismahel slew also all the Jews that were with Godolias in Masphath, and the Chaldeans that were found there, and the soldiers.€€ƒxJAnd on the second day after he had killed Godolias, no man yet knowing it,€€ƒxáThere came some from Sichem, and from Silo, and from Samaria, fourscore men, with their beards shaven, and their clothes rent, and mourning: and they had offerings and incense in their hand, to offer in the house of the Lord.€€ƒxµAnd Ismahel the son of Nathanias went forth from Masphath to meet them, weeping all along as he went: and when he had met them, he said to them: Come to Godolias, the son of Ahicam.€€ƒx¦And when they were come to the midst of the city, Ismahel the son of Nathanias, slew them, and cast them into the midst of the pit, he and the men that were with him.€€dZeph¤dnameiZephaniahiosis_namedZephlabbreviationdZephhchaptersƒ’ƒyQThen Ismahel carried away captive all the remnant of the people that were in Masphath: the king's daughters, and all the people that remained in Masphath: whom Nabuzardan the general of the army had committed to Godolias the son of Ahicam. And Ismahel the son of Nathanias took them, and he departed, to go over to the children of Ammon.€€ƒx˜But Johanan the son of Caree, and all the captains of the fighting men that were with him, heard of the evil that Ismahel the son of Nathanias had done.€€ƒxAnd taking all the men, they went out to fight against Ismahel the son of Nathanias, and they found him by the great waters that are in Gabaon.€€ƒxAnd when all the people that were with Ismahel, had seen Johanan the son of Caree, and all the captains of the fighting men that were with him, they rejoiced.€€ƒxyAnd all the people whom Ismahel had taken, went back to Masphath: and they returned and went to Johanan the son of Caree.€€ƒxrBut Ismahel the son of Nathanias fled with eight men, from the face of Johanan, and went to the children of Ammon.€€ƒydThen Johanan the son of Caree, and all the captains of the soldiers that were with him, took all the remnant of the people whom they had recovered from Ismahel the son of Nathanias, from Masphath, after that he had slain Godolias the son of Ahicam: valiant men for war, and the women, and the children, and the eunuchs whom he had brought back from Gabaon.€€ƒx{And they departed, and sat as sojourners in Chamaam, which is near Bethlehem: in order to go forward, and enter into Egypt,€€ƒxÈFrom the face of the Chaldeans: for they were afraid of them, because Ismahel the son of Nathanias had slain Godolias the son of Ahicam, whom the king of Babylon had made governor in the land of Juda.€€ƒ`€€ƒxªThen all the captains of the warriors, and Johanan the son of Caree, and Jezonias, the son of Osaias, and the rest of the people from the least to the greatest came near:€€ƒxÉAnd they said to Jeremiah the prophet: Let our supplication fall before thee: and pray thou for us to the Lord thy God for all this remnant, for we are left but a few of many, as thy eyes do behold us.€€ƒx]And let the Lord thy God shew us the way by which we may walk, and the thing that we must do.€€ƒxŞAnd Jeremiah the prophet said to them: I have heard you: behold I will pray to the Lord your God according to your words: and whatsoever thing he shall answer me, I will declare it to you: and I will hide nothing from you.€€ƒx¬And they said to Jeremiah: The Lord be witness between us of truth and faithfulness, if we do not according to every thing for which the Lord thy God shall send thee to us.€€ƒx±Whether it be good or evil, we will obey the voice of the Lord our God, to whom we send thee: that it may be well with us when we shall hearken to the voice of the Lord our God.€€ƒx:Now after ten days, the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah.€€ƒx—And he called Johanan the son of Caree, and all the captains of the fighting men that were with him, and all the people from the least to the greatest.€€ƒx½If you will be quiet and remain in this land, I will build you up, and not pull you down: I will plant you, and not pluck you up: for now I am appeased for the evil that I have done to you.€€ƒx¨Fear not because of the king of Babylon, of whom you are greatly afraid: fear him not, saith the Lord: for I am with you, to save you, and to deliver you from his hand.€€ƒxhAnd I will shew mercies to you, and will take pity on you, and will cause you to dwell in your own land.€€ƒxiBut if you say: We will not dwell in this land, neither will we hearken to the voice of the Lord our God,€€ƒxœSaying: No, but we will go into the land of Egypt: where we shall see no war, nor hear the sound of the trumpet, nor suffer hunger: and there we will dwell.€€ƒx±For this now hear the word of the Lord, ye remnant of Juda: Thus saith the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: If you set your faces to go into Egypt, and enter in to dwell there:€€ƒx§The sword which you fear, shall overtake you there in the land of Egypt: and the famine, whereof you are afraid, shall cleave to you in Egypt, and there you shall die.€€ƒxİAnd all the men that set their faces to go into Egypt, to dwell there, shall die by the sword, and by famine, and by pestilence: none of them shall remain, nor escape from the face of the evil that I will bring upon them.€€ƒyTFor thus saith the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: As my anger and my indignation hath been kindled against the inhabitants of Jerusalem: so shall my indignation be kindled against you, when you shall enter into Egypt, and you shall be an execration, and an astonishment, and a curse, and a reproach: and you shall see this place no more.€€ƒx‰This is the word of the Lord concerning you, O ye remnant of Juda: Go ye not into Egypt: know certainly that I have adjured you this day.€€ƒxØFor you have deceived your own souls: for you sent me to the Lord our God, saying: Pray for us to the Lord our God, and according to all that the Lord our God shall say to thee, so declare unto us, and we will do it.€€ƒx£And now I have declared it to you this day, and you have not obeyed the voice of the Lord your God, with regard to all the things for which he hath sent me to you.€€ƒx•Now therefore know certainly that you shall die by the sword, and by famine, and by pestilence in the place to which you desire to go to dwell there.€€ƒ`€€ƒxºAnd it came to pass, that when Jeremiah had made an end of speaking to the people all the words of the Lord their God, for which the Lord their God had sent him to them, all these words:€€”ƒx«But Baruch the son of Nerias setteth thee on against us, to deliver us into the hands of the Chaldeans, to kill us, and to cause us to be carried away captives to Babylon.€€ƒx—So Johanan the son of Caree, and all the captains of the soldiers, and all the people, obeyed not the voice of the Lord, to remain in the land of Juda.€€ƒxÎBut Johanan the son of Caree, and all the captains of the soldiers took all the remnant of Juda, that were returned out of all nations, to which they had before been scattered, to dwell in the land of Juda:€€ƒxØMen, and women, and children, and the king's daughters, and every soul, which Nabuzardan the general had left with Godolias the son of Ahicam the son of Saphan, and Jeremiah the prophet, and Baruch the son of Nerias.€€ƒxqAnd they went into the land of Egypt, for they obeyed not the voice of the Lord: and they came as far as Taphnis.€€ƒx=And the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah in Taphnis, saying:€€ƒx¬Take great stones in thy hand, and thou shalt hide them in the vault that is under the brick wall at the gate of Pharao's house in Taphnis: in the sight of the men of Juda.€€ƒxüAnd thou shalt say to them: Thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Behold I will send, and take Nabuchodonosor the king of Babylon my servant: and I will set his throne over these stones which I have hid, and he shall set his throne over them.€€ƒxªAnd he shall come and strike the land of Egypt: such as are for death, to death: and such as are for captivity, to captivity: and such as are for the sword, to the sword.€€ƒyAnd he shall kindle a fire in the temples of the gods of Egypt, and he shall burn them, and he shall carry them away captives: and he shall array himself with the land of Egypt, as a shepherd putteth on his garment: and he shall go forth from thence in peace.€€ƒx”And he shall break the statues of the house of the sun, that are in the land of Egypt; and the temples of the gods of Egypt he shall burn with fire.€€ƒ`€€ƒx±The word that came to Jeremiah, concerning all the Jews that dwelt in the land of Egypt, dwelling in Magdal, and in Taphnis, and in Memphis, and in the land of Phatures, saying:€€ƒxßThus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: You have seen all this evil that I have brought upon Jerusalem, and upon all the cities of Juda: and behold they are desolate this day, and there is not an inhabitant in them:€€ƒx·Because of the wickedness which they have committed, to provoke me to wrath, and to go and offer sacrifice, and worship other gods, which neither they, nor you, nor your fathers knew.€€ƒx‰And I sent to you all my servants the prophets, rising early, and sending, and saying: Do not commit this abominable thing, which I hate.€€ƒxnBut they heard not, nor inclined their ear to turn from their evil ways, and not to sacrifice to strange gods.€€ƒx»Wherefore my indignation and my fury was poured forth, and was kindled in the cities of Juda, and in the streets of Jerusalem: and they are turned to desolation and waste, as at this day.€€ƒxíAnd now thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Why do you commit this great evil against your own souls, that there should die of you man and woman, child and suckling out of the midst of Juda, and no remnant should be left you:€€ƒxôIn that you provoke me to wrath with the works of your hands, by sacrificing to other gods in the land of Egypt, into which you are come to dwell there: and that you should perish, and be a curse, and a reproach to all the nations of the earth?€€cHag¤dnamefHaggaiiosis_namecHaglabbreviationcHaghchapters‚ƒxƒTherefore thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Behold I will set my face upon you for evil: and I will destroy all Juda.€€ƒy¡And I will take the remnant of Juda that have set their faces to go into the land of Egypt, and to dwell there; and they shall be all consumed in the land of Egypt: they shall fall by the sword, and by the famine: and they shall be consumed from the least even to the greatest, by the sword, and by the famine shall they die: and they shall be for an execration, and for a wonder, and for a curse, and for a reproach.€€ƒxAnd I will visit them that dwell in the land of Egypt, as I have visited Jerusalem by the sword, and by famine and by pestilence.€€ƒyAnd there shall be none that shall escape, and remain of the remnant of the Jews that are gone to sojourn in the land of Egypt: and that shall return into the land of Juda, to which they have a desire to return to dwell there: there shall none return but they that shall flee.€€ƒxäThen all the men that knew that their wives sacrificed to other gods: and all the women of whom there stood by a great multitude, and all the people of them that dwelt in the land of Egypt in Phatures, answered Jeremiah, saying:€€ƒxbAs for the word which thou hast spoken to us in the name of the Lord, we will not hearken to thee:€€ƒyYBut we will certainly do every word that shall proceed out of our own mouth, to sacrifice to the queen of heaven, and to pour out drink offerings to her, as we and our fathers have done, our kings, and our princes in the cities of Juda, and in the streets of Jerusalem: and we were filled with bread, and it was well with us, and we saw no evil.€€ƒx·But since we left off to offer sacrifice to the queen of heaven, and to pour out frank offerings to her, we have wanted all things, and have been consumed by the sword, and by famine.€€ƒx²And if we offer sacrifice to the queen of heaven, and pour out drink offerings to her: did we make cakes to worship her, to pour out drink offerings to her, without our husbands?€€ƒx‚And Jeremiah spoke to all the people, to the men, and to the women, and to all the people which had given him that answer, saying:€€ƒxùWas it not the sacrifice that you offered in the cities of Juda, and in the streets of Jerusalem, you and your fathers, your kings, and your princes, and the people of the land, which the Lord hath remembered, and hath it not entered into his heart?€€ƒxùSo that the Lord could no longer bear, because of the evil of your doings, and because of the abominations which you have committed: therefore your land is become a desolation, and an astonishment, and a curse, without an inhabitant, as at this day.€€ƒxüBecause you have sacrificed to idols, and have sinned against the Lord: and have not obeyed the voice of the Lord, and have not walked in his law, and in his commandments, and in his testimonies: therefore are these evils come upon you, as at this day.€€ƒx†And Jeremiah said to all the people and to all the women: Hear ye the word of the Lord, all Juda, you that dwell in the land of Egypt:€€ƒyKThus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel, saying: You and your wives have spoken with your mouth, and fulfilled with your hands, saying: Let us perform our vows which we have made, to offer sacrifice to the queen of heaven, and to pour out drink offerings to her: you have fulfilled your vows, and have performed them indeed.€€—ƒx¾Behold I will watch over them for evil, and not for good: and all the men of Juda that are in the land of Egypt, shall be consumed, by the sword, and by famine, till there be an end of them.€€ƒxïAnd a few men that shall flee from the sword, shall return out of the land of Egypt into the land of Juda: and all the remnant of Juda that are gone into the land of Egypt to dwell there, shall know whose word shall stand, mine, or theirs.€€ƒx©And this shall be a sign to you, saith the Lord, that I will punish you in this place: that you may know that my words shall be accomplished indeed against you for evil.€€ƒyThus saith the Lord: Behold I will deliver Pharao Nechao king of Egypt into the hand of his enemies, and into the hand of them that seek his life: as I delivered Sedecias king of Juda into the land of Nabuchodonosor the king of Babylon his enemy, and that sought his life.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÓThe word that Jeremiah the prophet spoke to Baruch the son of Nerias, when he had written these words in a book, out of the mouth of Jeremiah, in the fourth year of Joakim the son of Josias king of Juda, saying:€€ƒx6Thus saith the Lord the God of Israel to thee, Baruch:€€ƒxŠThou hast said: Woe is me, wretch that I am, for the Lord hath added sorrow to my sorrow: I am wearied with my groans, and I find no rest.€€ƒxThus saith the Lord: Thus shalt thou say to him: Behold, them whom I have built, I do destroy: and them whom I have planted, I do pluck up, and all this land.€€ƒxËAnd dost thou seek great things for thyself? Seek not: for behold I will bring evil upon all flesh, saith the Lord! but I will give thee thy life, and save thee in all places whithersoever thou shalt go.€€ƒ`€€ƒxLThe word of the Lord that came to Jeremiah the prophet against the Gentiles,€€ƒxİAgainst Egypt, against the army of Pharao Nechao king of Egypt, which was by the river Euphrates in Charcamis, whom Nabuchodonosor the king of Babylon defeated, in the fourth year of Joakim the son of Josias king of Juda.€€ƒx:Prepare ye the shield and buckler, and go forth to battle.€€ƒxpHarness the horses, and get up, ye horsemen: stand forth with helmets, furbish the spears, put on coats of mail.€€ƒx«What then? I have seen them dismayed, and turning their backs, their valiant ones slain: they fled apace, and they looked not back: terror was round about, saith the Lord.€€ƒxŒLet not the swift flee away, nor the strong think to escape: they are overthrown, and fallen down, towards the north by the river Euphrates.€€ƒxRWho is this that cometh up as a flood: and his streams swell like those of rivers?€€ƒx´Egypt riseth up like a flood, and the waves thereof shall be moved as rivers, and he shall say: I will go up and will cover the earth: I will destroy the city, and its inhabitants.€€ƒx²Get ye up on horses, and glory in chariots, and let the valiant men come forth, the Ethiopians, and the Libyans that hold the shield, and the Lydians that take, and shoot arrows.€€ƒy#For this is the day of the Lord the God of hosts, a day of vengeance, that he may revenge himself of his enemies: the sword shall devour, and shall be filled, and shall be drunk with their blood: for there is a sacrifice of the Lord God of hosts in the north country, by the river Euphrates.€€ƒx„Go up into Galaad, and take balm, O virgin daughter of Egypt: in vain dost thou multiply medicines, there shall be no cure for thee.€€ƒxœThe nations have heard of thy disgrace, and thy howling hath filled the land: for the strong hath stumbled against the strong, and both are fallen together.€€dZech¤dnameiZechariahiosis_namedZechlabbreviationdZechhchapters•ƒx_Why are thy valiant men come to nothing? they stood not: because the Lord hath overthrown them.€€ƒxÁHe hath multiplied them that fall, and one hath fallen upon another, and they shall say: Arise, and let us return to our own people, and to the land of our nativity, from the sword of the dove.€€ƒxECall ye the name of Pharao king of Egypt, a tumult time hath brought.€€ƒx‹As I live, saith the King, (whose name is the Lord of hosts,) as Thabor is among the mountains, and as Carmel by the sea, so shall he come.€€ƒx“Furnish thyself to go into captivity, thou daughter inhabitant of Egypt: for Memphis shall be made desolate, and shall be forsaken and uninhabited.€€ƒxcEgypt is like a fair and beautiful heifer: there shall come from the north one that shall goad her.€€ƒxÜHer hirelings also that lived in the midst of her, like fatted calves are turned back, and are fled away together, and they could not stand, for the day of their slaughter is come upon them, the time of their visitation.€€ƒxƒHer voice shall sound like brass, for they shall hasten with an army, and with axes they shall come against her, as hewers of wood.€€ƒx‚They have cut down her forest, saith the Lord, which cannot be counted: they are multiplied above locusts, and are without number.€€ƒx\The daughter of Egypt is confounded, and delivered into the hand of the people of the north.€€ƒxÛThe Lord of hosts the God of Israel hath said: Behold I will visit upon the tumult of Alexandria, and upon Pharao, and upon Egypt, and upon her gods, and upon her kings, and upon Pharao, and upon them that trust in him.€€ƒxéAnd I will deliver them into the hand of them that seek their lives, and into the hand of Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon, and into the hand of his servants: and afterwards it shall be inhabited, as in the days of old, saith the Lord.€€ƒxıAnd thou my servant Jacob, fear not and be not thou dismayed, O Israel: for behold I will save thee from afar off, and thy seed out of the land of thy captivity: and Jacob shall return and be at rest, and prosper: and there shall be none to terrify him.€€ƒyAnd thou, my servant Jacob, fear not, saith the Lord: because I am with thee, for I will consume all the nations to which I have cast thee out: but thee I will not consume, but I will correct thee in judgment, neither will I spare thee as if thou wert innocent.€€ƒ`€€ƒxpThe word of the Lord that came to Jeremiah the prophet against the people of Palestine, before Pharao took Gaza.€€ƒyThus saith the Lord: Behold there come up waters out of the north, and they shall be as an overflowing torrent, and they shall cover the land, and all that is therein, the city and the inhabitants thereof: then the men shall cry, and all the inhabitants of the land shall howl,€€ƒxÆAt the noise of the marching of arms, and of his soldiers, at the rushing of his chariots, and the multitude of his wheels. The fathers have not looked back to the children, for feebleness of hands,€€ƒxïBecause of the coming of the day, in which all the Philistines shall be laid waste, and Tyre and Sidon shall be destroyed, with all the rest of their helpers. For the Lord hath wasted the Philistines, the remnant of the isle of Cappadocia.€€ƒxzBaldness is come upon Gaza: Ascalon hath held her peace with the remnant of their valley: how long shalt thou cut thyself?€€ƒxdO thou sword of the Lord, how long wilt thou not be quiet? Go into thy scabbard, rest, and be still.€€ƒ`€€ƒxµAgainst Moab thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: Woe to Nabo, for it is laid waste, and confounded: Cariathaim is taken: the strong city is confounded and hath trembled.€€ƒxÊThere is no more rejoicing in Moab over Hesebon: they have devised evil. Come, and let us cut it off from being a nation. Therefore shalt thou in silence hold thy peace, and the sword shall follow thee.€€ƒx=A voice of crying from Oronaim: waste, and great destruction.€€ƒx6Moab is destroyed: proclaim a cry for her little ones.€€ƒxFor by the ascent of Luith shall the mourner go up with weeping: for in the descent of Oronaim the enemies have heard a howling of destruction.€€ƒx9Flee, save your lives: and be as heath in the wilderness.€€ƒx©For because thou hast trusted in thy bulwarks, and in thy treasures, thou also shalt be taken: and Chamos shall go into captivity, his priests, and his princes together.€€ƒx And the spoiler shall come upon every city, and no city shall escape: and the valleys shall perish, and the plains shall be destroyed, for the Lord hath spoken:€€ƒxtGive a flower to Moab, for in its flower it shall go out: and the cities thereof shall be desolate, and uninhabited.€€ƒxpCursed be he that doth the work of the Lord deceitfully: and cursed be he that withholdeth his sword from blood.€€ƒxàMoab hath been fruitful from his youth, and hath rested upon his lees: and hath not been poured out from vessel to vessel, nor hath gone into captivity: therefore his taste hath remained in him, and his scent is not changed.€€ƒxÚTherefore behold the days come, saith the Lord, and I will send him men that shall order and overturn his bottles, and they shall cast him down, and shall empty his vessels, and break their bottles one against another.€€Šƒx7How do you say: We are valiant and stout men in battle?€€ƒx¡Moab is laid waste, and they have cast down her cities: and her choice young men are gone down to the slaughter: saith the king, whose name is the Lord of hosts.€€ƒx^The destruction of Moab is near to come: the calamity thereof shall come on exceeding swiftly.€€ƒx†Comfort him, all you that are round about him, and all you that know his name, say: How is the strong staff broken, the beautiful rod?€€ƒx¡Come down from thy glory, and sit in thirst, O dwelling of the daughter of Dibon: because the spoiler of Moab is come up to thee, he hath destroyed thy bulwarks.€€ƒx‚Stand in the way, and look out, O habitation of Aroer: inquire of him that fleeth: and say to him that hath escaped: What is done?€€ƒxiMoab is confounded, because he is overthrown: howl ye, and cry, tell ye it in Arnon, that Moab is wasted.€€ƒxZAnd judgment is come upon the plain country: upon Helon, and upon Jasa, and upon Mephaath.€€ƒx@And upon Dibon, and upon Nabo, and upon the house of Deblathaim,€€ƒx;And upon Cariathaim, and upon Bethgamul, and upon Bethmaon,€€ƒxCThe horn of Moab is cut off, and his arm is broken, saith the Lord.€€ƒxMake him drunk, because he lifted up himself against the Lord: and Moab shall dash his hand in his own vomit, and he also shall be in derision.€€ƒx·For Israel hath been a derision unto them: as though thou hadst found him amongst thieves: for thy words therefore, which thou hast spoken against him, thou shalt be led away captive.€€ƒxœLeave the cities, and dwell in the rock, you that dwell in Moab: and be ye like the dove that maketh her nest in the mouth of the hole in the highest place.€€ƒxŠWe have heard the pride of Moab, he is exceeding proud: his haughtiness, and his arrogancy, and his pride, and the loftiness of his heart.€€ƒx—I know, saith the Lord, his boasting, and that the strength thereof is not according to it, neither hath it endeavoured to do according as it was able.€€ƒxkTherefore will I lament for Moab, and I will cry out to all Moab, for the men of the brick wall that mourn.€€ƒxÏO vineyard of Sabama, I will weep for thee, with the mourning of Jazer: thy branches are gone over the sea, they are come even to the sea of Jazer: the robber hath rushed in upon thy harvest and thy vintage.€€ƒxÀJoy and gladness is taken away from Carmel, and from the land of Moab, and I have taken away the wine out of the presses: the treader of the grapes shall not sing the accustomed cheerful tune.€€ƒx·From the cry of Hesebon even to Eleale, and to Jasa, they have uttered their voice: from Segor to Oronaim, as a heifer of three years old: the waters also of Nemrim shall be very bad.€€ƒxwAnd I will take away from Moab, saith the Lord, him that offereth in the high places, and that sacrificeth to his gods.€€ƒx¼Therefore my heart shall sound for Moab like pipes and my heart shall sound like pipes for the men of the brick wall: because he hath done more than he could, therefore they have perished.€€ƒxFor every head shall be bald, and every beard shall be shaven: all hands shall be tied together, and upon every back there shall be haircloth.€€ƒxUpon all the housetops of Moab, and in the streets thereof general mourning: because I have broken Moab as an useless vessel, saith the Lord.€€‹ƒx`Thus saith the Lord: Behold he shall fly as an eagle, and shall stretch forth his wings to Moab.€€ƒxCarioth is taken, and the strongholds are won: and the heart of the valiant men of Moab in that day shall be as the heart of a woman in labour.€€ƒxNAnd Moab shall cease to be a people: because he hath gloried against the Lord.€€ƒxVFear, and the pit, and the snare come upon thee, O inhabitant of Moab, saith the Lord.€€ƒxÉHe that shall flee from the fear, shall fall into the pit: and he that shall get up out of the pit, shall be taken in the snare: for I will bring upon Moab the year of their visitation, saith the Lord.€€ƒxàThey that fled from the snare stood in the shadow of Hesebon: but there came a fire out of Hesebon, and a flame out of the midst of Seon, and it shall devour part of Moab, and the crown of the head of the children of tumult.€€ƒxoWoe to thee, Moab, thou hast persisted, O people of Chamos: for thy sons, and thy daughters are taken captives.€€ƒxmAnd I will bring back the captivity of Moab in the last days, saith the Lord. Hitherto the judgments of Moab.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¥Against the children of Ammon. Thus saith the Lord: Hath Israel no sons? or hath he no heir? Why then hath Melchom inherited Gad: and his people dwelt in his cities?€€ƒyTherefore behold the days come, saith the Lord, and I will cause the noise of war to be heard in Rabbath of the children of Ammon, and it shall be destroyed into a heap, and her daughters shall be burnt with fire, and Israel shall possess them that have possessed him, saith the Lord.€€ƒxœWhy gloriest thou in the valleys? thy valley hath flowed away, O delicate daughter, that hast trusted in thy treasures, and hast said: Who shall come to me?€€ƒxåBehold I will bring a fear upon thee, saith the Lord God of hosts, from all that are round about thee: and you shall be scattered every one out of one another's sight, neither shall there be any to gather together them that flee.€€ƒx\And afterwards I will cause the captives of the children of Ammon to return, saith the Lord.€€ƒx”Against Edom. Thus saith the Lord of hosts: Is wisdom no more in Theman? counsel is perished from her children: their wisdom is become unprofitable.€€ƒxŸFlee and turn your backs, go down into the deep hole, ye inhabitants of Dedan: for I have brought the destruction of Esau upon him, the time of his visitation.€€ƒxIf grapegatherers had come to thee, would they not have left a bunch? if thieves in the night, they would have taken what was enough for them.€€ƒx But I have made Esau bare, I have revealed his secrets, and he cannot be hid: his seed is laid waste, and his brethren, and his neighbours, and he shall not be.€€ƒxVLeave thy fatherless children: I will make them live: and thy widows shall hope in me.€€ƒxĞFor thus saith the Lord: Behold they whose judgment was not to drink of the cup, shall certainly drink: and shalt thou come off as innocent? thou shalt not come off as innocent, but drinking thou shalt drink.€€ƒx¬For I have sworn by myself, saith the Lord, that Bosra shall become a desolation, and a reproach, and a desert, and a curse: and all her cities shall be everlasting wastes.€€ƒxI have heard a rumour from the Lord, and an ambassador is sent to the nations: Gather yourselves together, and come against her, and let us rise up to battle.€€ƒxQFor behold I have made thee a little one among the nations, despicable among men.€€ƒyThy arrogancy hath deceived thee, and the pride of thy heart: O thou that dwellest in the clefts of the rock, and endeavourest to lay hold on the height of the hill: but though thou shouldst make thy nest as high as an eagle, I will bring thee down from thence, saith the Lord.€€ƒxtAnd Edom shall be desolate: every one that shall pass by it, shall be astonished, and shall hiss at all its plagues.€€ƒxAs Sodom was overthrown and Gomorrha, and the neighbours thereof, saith the Lord: there shall not a man dwell there, and there shall no son of man inhabit it.€€ƒyTherefore hear ye the counsel of the Lord, which he hath taken concerning Edom: and his thoughts which he hath thought concerning the inhabitants of Theman: surely the little ones of the flock shall cast them down, of a truth they shall destroy them with their habitation.€€ƒx^The earth is moved at the noise of their fall: the cry of their voice is heard in the Red Sea.€€ƒx¹Behold he shall come up as an eagle, and fly: and he shall spread his wings over Bosra: and in that day the heart of the valiant ones of Edom shall be as the heart of a woman in labour.€€ƒxšAgainst Damascus. Emath is confounded and Arphad: for they have heard very bad tidings, they are troubled as in the sea: through care they could not rest.€€ƒx€Damascus is undone, she is put to flight, trembling hath seized on her: anguish and sorrows have taken her as a woman in labour.€€ƒx;How have they forsaken the city of renown, the city of joy!€€ƒxTherefore her young men shall fall in her streets: and all the men of war shall be silent in that day, saith the Lord of hosts.€€ƒxbAnd I will kindle a fire in the wall of Damascus, and it shall devour the strong holds of Benadad.€€ƒx¶Against Cedar and against the kingdoms of Asor, which Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon destroyed. Thus saith the Lord: Arise, and go ye up to Cedar, and waste the children of the east.€€ƒxºThey shall take their tents, and their flocks: and shall carry off for themselves their curtains, and all their vessels, and their camels: and they shall call fear upon them round about.€€ƒxÀFlee ye, get away speedily, sit in deep holes, you that inhabit Asor, saith the Lord: for Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon hath taken counsel against you, and hath conceived designs against you.€€ƒxArise, and go up to a nation that is at ease, and that dwelleth securely, saith the Lord: they have neither gates, nor bars: they dwell alone.€€ƒxìAnd their camels shall be for a spoil and the multitude of their cattle for a booty, and I will scatter into every wind them that have their hair cut round, and I will bring destruction upon them from all their confines, saith the Lord.€€ƒxsAnd Asor shall be a habitation for dragons, desolate for ever: no man shall abide there, nor son of man inhabit it.€€—ƒx\Thus saith the Lord of hosts: Behold I will break the bow of Elam, and their chief strength.€€ƒxÆAnd I will bring upon Elam the four winds from the four quarters of heaven: and I will scatter them into all these winds: and there shall be no nation, to which the fugitives of Elam shall not come.€€ƒxäAnd I will cause Elam to be afraid before their enemies, and in the sight of them that seek their life: and I will bring evil upon them, my fierce wrath, saith the Lord: and I will send the sword after them, till I consume them.€€ƒx\And I will set my throne in Elam, and destroy kings and princes from thence, saith the Lord.€€ƒxTBut in the latter days I will cause the captives of Elam, to return, saith the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx~The word that the Lord hath spoken against Babylon, and against the land of the Chaldeans in the hand of Jeremiah the prophet.€€ƒxáDeclare ye among the nations, and publish it, lift up a standard: proclaim, and conceal it not: say: Babylon is taken, Bel is confounded, Merodach is overthrown, their graven things are confounded, their idols are overthrown.€€ƒxÀFor a nation is come up against her out of the north, which shall make her land desolate: and there shall be none to dwell therein, from man even to beast: yea they are removed, and gone away.€€ƒxÇIn those days, and at that time, saith the Lord, the children of Israel shall come, they and the children of Juda together: going and weeping they shall make haste, and shall seek the Lord their God.€€ƒx¨They shall ask the way to Sion, their faces are hitherward. They shall come, and shall be joined to the Lord by an everlasting covenant, which shall never be forgotten.€€ƒxËMy people have been a lost flock, their shepherds have caused them to go astray, and have made them wander in the mountains: they have gone from mountain to hill, they have forgotten their resting place.€€ƒxÑAll that found them, have devoured them: and their enemies said: We have not sinned in so doing: because they have sinned against the Lord the beauty of justice, and against the Lord the hope of their fathers.€€ƒx~Remove out of the midst of Babylon, and go forth out of the land of the Chaldeans: and be ye as kids at the head of the flock.€€ƒyFor behold I raise up, and will bring against Babylon an assembly of great nations from the land of the north: and they shall be prepared against her, and from thence she shall be taken: their arrows, like those of a mighty man, a destroyer, shall not return in vain.€€ƒxUAnd Chaldea shall be made a prey: all that waste her shall be filled, saith the Lord.€€ƒxšBecause you rejoice, and speak great things, pillaging my inheritance: because you are spread abroad as calves upon the grass, and have bellowed as bulls.€€ƒxªYour mother is confounded exceedingly, and she that bore you is made even with the dust: behold she shall be the last among the nations, a wilderness unpassable, and dry.€€ƒx·Because of the wrath of the Lord it shall not be inhabited, but shall be wholly desolate: every one that shall pass by Babylon, shall be astonished, and shall hiss at all her plagues.€€ƒx™Prepare yourselves against Babylon round about, all you that bend the bow: fight against her, spare not arrows: because she hath sinned against the Lord.€€ƒxÌShout against her, she hath every where given her hand, her foundations are fallen, her walls are thrown down, for it is the vengeance of the Lord. Take vengeance upon her: as she hath done, so do to her.€€ƒxËDestroy the sower out of Babylon, and him that holdeth the sickle in the time of harvest: for fear of the sword of the dove every man shall return to his people, and every one shall flee to his own land.€€ƒx¨Israel is a scattered flock, the lions have driven him away: first the king of Assyria devoured him: and last this Nabuchodonosor king of Babylon hath broken his bones.€€‘ƒx–And I will bring Israel again to his habitation: and he shall feed on Carmel, and Bason, and his soul shall be satisfied in mount Ephraim, and Galaad.€€ƒxİIn those days, and at that time, saith the Lord, the iniquity of Israel shall be sought for, and there shall be none: and the sin of Juda, and there shall none be found: for I will be merciful to them, whom I shall leave.€€ƒx±Go up against the land of the rulers, and punish the inhabitants thereof, waste, and destroy all behind them, saith the Lord: and do according to all that I have commanded thee.€€ƒx4A noise of war in the land, and a great destruction.€€ƒxrHow is the hammer of the whole earth broken, and destroyed! how is Babylon turned into a desert among the nations!€€ƒx¦I have caused thee to fall into a snare, and thou art taken, O Babylon, and thou wast not aware of it: thou art found and caught, because thou hast provoked the Lord.€€ƒx¥The Lord hath opened his armoury, and hath brought forth the weapons of his wrath: for the Lord the God of hosts hath a work to be done in the land of the Chaldeans.€€ƒxÇCome ye against her from the uttermost borders: open that they may go forth that shall tread her down: take the stones out of the way, and make heaps, and destroy her: and let nothing of her be left.€€ƒxDestroy all her valiant men, let them go down to the slaughter: woe to them, for their day is come, the time of their visitation.€€ƒx¥The voice of them that flee, and of them that have escaped out of the land of Babylon: to declare in Sion the revenge of the Lord our God, the revenge of his temple.€€ƒyDeclare to many against Babylon, to all that bend the bow: stand together against her round about, and let none escape; pay her according to her work: according to all that she hath done, do ye to her: for she hath lifted up herself against the Lord, against the Holy One of Israel.€€ƒx}Therefore shall her young men fall in her streets: and all her men of war shall hold their peace in that day, saith the Lord.€€ƒxzBehold I come against thee, O proud one, saith the Lord the God of hosts: for thy day is come, the time of thy visitation.€€ƒxªAnd the proud one shall fall, he shall fall down, and there shall be none to lift him up: and I will kindle a fire in his cities, and it shall devour all round about him.€€ƒx´Thus saith the Lord of hosts: The children of Israel, and the children of Juda are oppressed together: all that have taken them captives, hold them fast, they will not let them go.€€ƒx¡Their redeemer is strong, the Lord of hosts is his name: he will defend their cause in judgment, to terrify the land, and to disquiet the inhabitants of Babylon.€€ƒx€A sword is upon the Chaldeans, saith the Lord, and upon the inhabitants of Babylon, and upon her princes, and upon her wise men.€€ŒƒxÅA sword upon their horses, and upon their chariots, and upon all the people that are in the midst of her: and they shall become as women: a sword upon her treasures, and they shall be made a spoil.€€ƒxyA drought upon her waters, and they shall be dried up: because it is a land of idols, and they glory in monstrous things.€€ƒxÂTherefore shall dragons dwell there with the fig fauns: and ostriches shall dwell therein, and it shall be no more inhabited for ever, neither shall it be built up from generation to generation.€€ƒx˜As the Lord overthrew Sodom and Gomorrha, and their neighbour cities, saith the Lord: no man shall dwell there, neither shall the son of man inhabit it.€€ƒxpBehold a people cometh from the north, and a great nation, and many kings shall rise from the ends of the earth.€€ƒxĞThey shall take the bow and the shield: they are cruel and unmerciful: their voice shall roar like the sea, and they shall ride upon horses: like a man prepared for battle against thee, O daughter of Babylon.€€ƒxThe king of Babylon hath heard the report of them, and his hands are grown feeble: anguish hath taken hold of him, pangs as a woman in labour.€€ƒy=Behold he shall come up like a lion from the swelling of the Jordan to the strong and beautiful: for I will make him run suddenly upon her: and who shall be the chosen one whom I may appoint over her? for who is like to me? and who shall bear up against me? and who is that shepherd that can withstand my countenance?€€ƒyTherefore hear ye the counsel of the Lord, which he hath taken against Babylon: and his thoughts which he hath thought against the land of the Chaldeans: surely the little ones of the flocks shall pull them down, of a truth their habitation shall be destroyed with them.€€ƒxcAt the noise of the taking of Babylon the earth is moved, and the cry is heard amongst the nations.€€ƒ`€€ƒxªThus saith the Lord: Behold I will raise up as it were a pestilential wind against Babylon and against the inhabitants thereof, who have lifted up their heart against me.€€‘ƒxLet not him that bendeth, bend his bow, and let not him go up that is armed with a coat of mail: spare not her young men, destroy all her army.€€ƒx^And the slain shall fall in the land of the Chaldeans, and the wounded in the regions thereof.€€ƒx“For Israel and Juda have not been forsaken by their God the Lord of hosts: but their land hath been filled with sin against the Holy One of Israel.€€ƒxÅFlee ye from the midst of Babylon, and let every one save his own life: be not silent upon her iniquity: for it is the time of revenge from the Lord, he will render unto her what she hath deserved.€€ƒxBabylon hath been a golden cup in the hand of the Lord, that made all the earth drunk: the nations have drunk of her wine, and therefore they have staggered.€€ƒxiBabylon is suddenly fallen, and destroyed: howl for her, take balm for her pain, if so she may be healed.€€ƒxÇWe would have cured Babylon, but she is not healed: let us forsake her, and let us go every man to his own land: because her judgment hath reached even to the heavens, and is lifted up to the clouds.€€ƒxhThe Lord hath brought forth our justices: Come, and let us declare in Sion the work of the Lord our God.€€ƒxØSharpen the arrows, fill the quivers, the Lord hath raised up the spirit of the kings of the Medes: and his mind is against Babylon to destroy it, because it is the vengeance of the Lord, the vengeance of his temple.€€ƒxÏUpon the walls of Babylon set up the standard, strengthen the watch: set up the watchmen, prepare the ambushes: for the Lord hath both purposed, and done all that he spoke against the inhabitants of Babylon.€€ƒxeO thou that dwellest upon many waters, rich in treasures, thy end is come for thy entire destruction.€€ƒxThe Lord of hosts hath sworn by himself, saying: I will fill thee with men as with locusts, and they shall lift up a joyful shout against thee.€€ƒx„He that made the earth by his power, that hath prepared the world by his wisdom, and stretched out the heavens by his understanding.€€ƒxÎWhen he uttereth his voice the waters are multiplied in heaven: he lifteth up the clouds from the ends of the earth, he hath turned lightning into rain: and hath brought forth the wind out of his treasures.€€ƒx–Every man is become foolish by his knowledge: every founder is confounded by his idol, for what he hath cast is a lie, and there is no breath in them.€€ƒxdThey are vain works, and worthy to be laughed at, in the time of their visitation they shall perish.€€ƒx™The portion of Jacob is not like them: for he that made all things he it is, and Israel is the sceptre of his inheritance: the Lord of hosts is his name.€€ƒx‘And with thee I will break in pieces the horse, and his rider, and with thee I will break in pieces the chariot, and him that getteth up into it:€€ƒx¶And with thee I will break in pieces man and woman, and with thee I will break in pieces the old man and the child, and with thee I will break in pieces the young man and the virgin:€€ƒxÄAnd with thee I will break in pieces the shepherd and his flock, and with thee I will break in pieces the husbandman and his yoke of oxen, and with thee I will break in pieces captains and rulers.€€ƒx’And I will render to Babylon, and to all the inhabitants of Chaldea all their evil, that they have done in Sion, before your eyes, saith the Lord.€€ƒxŞBehold I come against thee, thou destroying mountain, saith the Lord, which corruptest the whole earth: and I will stretch out my hand upon thee, and will roll thee down from the rocks, and will make thee a burnt mountain.€€ƒxŠAnd they shall not take of thee a stone for the corner, nor a stone for foundations, but thou shalt be destroyed for ever, saith the Lord.€€ƒxóSet ye up a standard in the land: sound with the trumpet among the nations: prepare the nations against her: call together against her the kings of Ararat, Menni, and Ascenez: number Taphsar against her, bring the horse as the stinging locust.€€ƒx~Prepare the nations against her, the kings of Media, their captains, and all their rulers, and all the land of their dominion.€€ƒxªAnd the land shall be in a commotion, and shall be troubled: for the design of the Lord against Babylon shall awake, to make the land of Babylon desert and uninhabitable.€€ƒxºThe valiant men of Babylon have forborne to fight, they have dwelt in holds: their strength hath failed, and they are become as women: her dwelling places are burnt, her bars are broken.€€ƒx–One running post shall meet another, and messenger shall meet messenger: to tell the king of Babylon that his city is taken from one end to the other:€€ƒxeAnd that the fords are taken, and the marshes are burnt with fire, and the men of war are affrighted.€€ƒxÄFor thus saith the Lord of hosts the God of Israel: The daughter of Babylon is like a thrashingfloor, this is the time of her thrashing: yet a little while, and the time of her harvest shall come.€€ƒxÚNabuchodonosor king of Babylon hath eaten me up, he hath devoured me: he hath made me as an empty vessel: he hath swallowed me up like a dragon, he hath filled his belly with my delicate meats, and he hath cast me out.€€‰ƒxŸTherefore thus saith the Lord: Behold I will judge thy cause, and will take vengeance for thee, and I will make her sea desolate, I and will dry up her spring.€€ƒxƒAnd Babylon shall be reduced to heaps, a dwelling place for dragons, an astonishment and a hissing, because there is no inhabitant.€€ƒxSThey shall roar together like lions, they shall shake their manes like young lions.€€ƒxšIn their heat I will set them drink: and I will make them drunk, that they may slumber, and sleep an everlasting sleep, and awake no more, saith the Lord.€€ƒxLI will bring them down like lambs to the slaughter, and like rams with kids.€€ƒx~How is Sesach taken, and the renowned one of all the earth surprised? How is Babylon become an astonishment among the nations?€€ƒxYThe sea is come up over Babylon : she is covered with the multitude of the waves thereof.€€ƒx†Her cities are become an astonishment, a land uninhabited and desolate, a land wherein none can dwell, nor son of man pass through it.€€ƒxÏAnd I will visit against Bel in Babylon, and I will bring forth out of his mouth that which he had swallowed down: and the nations shall no more flow together to him, for the wall also of Babylon shall fall.€€•ƒxĞAnd lest your hearts faint, and ye fear for the rumour that shall be heard in the land: and a rumour shall come in one year, and after this year another rumour: and iniquity in the land, and ruler upon ruler.€€ƒx Therefore behold the days come, and I will visit the idols of Babylon: and her whole land shall be confounded, and all her slain shall fall in the midst of her.€€ƒxœAnd the heavens and the earth, and all things that are in them shall give praise for Babylon: for spoilers shall come to her from the north, saith the Lord.€€ƒxtAnd as Babylon caused that there should fall slain in Israel: so of Babylon there shall fall slain in all the earth.€€ƒxYou that have escaped the sword, come away, stand not still: remember the Lord afar off, and let Jerusalem come into your mind.€€ƒxšWe are confounded, because we have heard reproach: shame hath covered our faces: because strangers are come upon the sanctuaries of the house of the Lord.€€ƒx€Therefore behold the days come, saith the Lord, and I will visit her graven things, and in all her land the wounded shall groan:€€ƒx†If Babylon should mount up to heaven, and establish her strength on high: from me there should come spoilers upon her, saith the Lord.€€ƒxVThe noise of a cry from Babylon, and great destruction from the land of the Chaldeans:€€ƒxBecause the Lord hath laid Babylon waste, and destroyed out of her the great voice: and their wave shall roar like many waters: their voice hath made a noise:€€ƒxµBecause the spoiler is come upon her, that is, upon Babylon, and her valiant men are taken, and their bow is weakened, because the Lord, who is a strong revenger, will surely repay.€€ƒxÛAnd I will make her princes drunk, and her wise men, and her captains, and her rulers, and her valiant men: and they shall sleep an everlasting sleep, and shall awake no more, saith the king whose name is Lord of hosts.€€ƒxóThus saith the Lord of hosts: That broad wall of Babylon shall be utterly broken down, and her high gates shall be burnt with fire, and the labours of the people shall come to nothing, and of the nations shall go to the fire, and shall perish.€€ƒxÑThe word that Jeremiah the prophet commanded Saraias the son of Nerias, the son of Maasias, when he went with king Sedecias to Babylon, in the fourth year of his reign: now Saraias was chief over the prophecy.€€ƒx|And Jeremiah wrote in one book all the evil that was to come upon Babylon: all these words that are written against Babylon.€€ƒxoAnd Jeremiah said to Saraias: When thou shalt come into Babylon, and shalt see, and shalt read all these words,€€ƒx³Thou shalt say: O Lord, thou hast spoken against this place to destroy it: so that there should be neither man nor beast to dwell therein, and that it should be desolate for ever.€€ƒxŒAnd when thou shalt have made an end of reading this book, thou shalt tie a stone to it, and shalt throw it into the midst of the Euphrates:€€ƒx¾And thou shalt say: Thus shall Babylon sink, and she shall not rise up from the affliction that I will bring upon her, and she shall be utterly destroyed. Thus far are the words of Jeremiah.€€ƒ`€€ƒx±Sedecias was one and twenty years old when he began to reign: and he reigned eleven years in Jerusalem: and the name of his mother was Amital, the daughter of Jeremiah of Lobna.€€cMal¤dnamegMalachiiosis_namecMallabbreviationcMalhchapters„ƒx÷And it came to pass in the ninth year of his reign, in the tenth month, the tenth day of the month, that Nabuchodonosor the king of Babylon came, he and all his army, against Jerusalem, and they besieged it, and built forts against it round about.€€ƒxCAnd the city was besieged until the eleventh year of king Sedecias.€€ƒx…And in the fourth month, the ninth day of the month, a famine overpowered the city: and there was no food for the people of the land.€€ƒyAnd the city was broken up, and the men of war fled, and went out of the city in the night by the way of the gate that is between the two walls, and leadeth to the king's garden, (the Chaldeans besieging the city round about,) and they went by the way that leadeth to the wilderness.€€ƒx¥But the army of the Chaldeans pursued after the king: and they overtook Sedecias in the desert which is near Jericho: and all his companions were scattered from him.€€ƒx”And when they had taken the king, they carried him to the king of Babylon to Reblatha, which is in the land of Emath: and he gave judgment upon him.€€ƒxsAnd the king of Babylon slew the sons of Sedecias before his eyes: and he slew all the princes of Juda in Reblatha.€€ƒx¦And he put out the eyes of Sedecias, and bound him with fetters, and the king of Babylon brought him into Babylon, and he put him in prison till the day of his death.€€ƒxÓAnd in the fifth month, the tenth day of the month, the same is the nineteenth year of Nabuchodonosor, king of Babylon, came Nabuzardan the general of the army, who stood before the king of Babylon in Jerusalem.€€ƒx„And he burnt the house of the Lord, and the king's house, and all the houses of Jerusalem, and every great house he burnt with fire.€€ƒxnAnd all the army of the Chaldeans that were with the general broke down all the wall of Jerusalem round about.€€ƒxæBut Nabuzardan the general carried away captives some of the poor people, and of the rest of the common sort who remained in the city, and of the fugitives that were fled over to the king of Babylon, and the rest of the multitude.€€ƒxcBut of the poor of the land, Nabuzardan the general left some for vinedressers, and for husbandmen.€€ƒxÓThe Chaldeans also broke in pieces the brazen pillars that were in the house of the Lord, and the bases, and the sea of brass that was in the house of the Lord: and they carried all the brass of them to Babylon.€€‘ƒxÓThe general took away the pitchers, and the censers, and the pots, and the basins, and the candlesticks, and the mortars, and the cups: as many as were of gold, in gold: and as many as were of silver, in silver:€€ƒxÂAnd the two pillars, and one sea, and twelve oxen of brass that were under the bases, which king Solomon had made in the house of the Lord: there was no weight of the brass of all these vessels.€€ƒx¶And concerning the pillars, one pillar was eighteen cubits high: and a cord of twelve cubits compassed it about: but the thickness thereof was four fingers, and it was hollow within.€€ƒxÜAnd chapiters of brass were upon both: and the height of one chapiter was five cubits: and network, and pomegranates were upon the chapiters round about, all of brass. The same of the second pillar, and the pomegranates.€€ƒx~And there were ninety-six pomegranates hanging down: and the pomegranates being a hundred in all, were compassed with network.€€ƒxsAnd the general took Saraias the chief priest, and Sophonias the second priest, and the three keepers of the entry.€€ƒy?He also took out of the city one eunuch that was chief over the men of war: and seven men of them that were near the king's person, that were found in the city: and a scribe, an officer of the army who exercised the young soldiers: and threescore men of the people of the land, that were found in the midst of the city.€€ƒx[And Nabuzardan the general took them, and brought them to the king of Babylon, to Reblatha.€€ƒxAnd the king of Babylon struck them, and put them to death in Reblatha, in the land of Emath: and Juda was carried away captive out of his land.€€ƒxwThis is the people whom Nabuchodonosor carried away captive: in the seventh year, three thousand and twenty-three Jews.€€ƒx\In the eighteenth year of Nabuchodonosor, eight hundred and thirty-two souls from Jerusalem.€€ƒx·In the three and twentieth year of Nabuchodonosor, Nabuzardan the general carried away of the Jews seven hundred and forty-five souls. So all the souls were four thousand six hundred.€€ƒy0And it came to pass in the seven and thirtieth year of the captivity of Joachin king of Juda, in the twelfth month, the five and twentieth day of the month, that Evilmerodach king of Babylon, in the first year of his reign, lifted up the head of Joachin king of Juda, and brought him forth out of prison.€€ƒxoAnd he spoke kindly to him, and he set his throne above the thrones of the kings that were with him in Babylon.€€ƒx`And he changed his prison garments, and he ate bread before him always all the days of his life.€€ƒx™And for his diet a continual provision was allowed him by the king of Babylon, every day a portion, until the day of his death, all the days of his life.€€ƒxThe Lamentations of Jeremiah€€’ƒxAleph. How doth the city sit solitary that was full of people! how is the mistress of the Gentiles become as a widow: the princes of provinces made tributary!€€ƒxÍBeth. Weeping, she hath wept in the night, and her tears are on her cheeks: there is none to comfort her among all them that were dear to her: all her friends have despised her, and are become her enemies.€€ƒxäGhimel. Juda hath removed her dwelling place, because of her affliction, and the greatness of her bondage; she hath dwelt among the nations, and she hath found no rest; all her persecutors have taken her in the midst of straits.€€ƒxËDaleth. The ways of Sion mourn, because there are none that come to the solemn feast: all her gates are broken down; her priests sigh; her virgins are in affliction; and she is oppressed with bitterness.€€ƒxØHe. Her adversaries are become her lords; her enemies are enriched; because the Lord hath spoken against her for the multitude of her iniquities; her children are led into captivity, before the face of the oppressor.€€ƒx¿Vau. And from the daughter of Sion, all her beauty is departed; her princes are become like rams that find no pastures; and they are gone away without strength before the face of the pursuer.€€ƒyZain. Jerusalem hath remembered the days of her affliction, and prevarication of all her desirable things which she had from the days of old, when her people fell in the enemy's hand, and there was no helper; the enemies have seen her, and have mocked at her sabbaths.€€ƒxºHeth. Jerusalem hath grievously sinned, therefore is she become unstable; all that honoured her, have despised her, because they have seen her shame; but she sighed, and turned backward.€€ƒx¾Teth. Her filthiness is on her feet, and she hath not remembered her end; she is wonderfully cast down, not having a comforter: behold, O Lord, my affliction, because the enemy is lifted up.€€ƒxÆJod. The enemy hath put out his hand to all her desirable things: for she hath seen the Gentiles enter into her sanctuary, of whom thou gavest commandment that they should not enter into thy church.€€ƒx¤Caph. All her people sigh, they seek bread: they have given all their precious things for food to relieve the soul: see, O Lord, and consider, for I am become vile.€€ƒx³Lamed. O all ye that pass by the way, attend, and see if there be any sorrow like to my sorrow: for he hath made a vintage of me, as the Lord spoke in the day of his fierce anger.€€ƒxÀMem. From above he hath sent fire into my bones, and hath chastised me: he hath spread a net for my feet, he hath turned me back: he hath made me desolate, wasted with sorrow all the day long.€€ƒxÍNun. The yoke of my iniquities hath watched: they are folded together in his hand, and put upon my neck: my strength is weakened: the Lord hath delivered me into a hand, out of which I am not able to rise.€€ƒxÍSamech. The Lord hath taken away all my mighty men out of the midst of me: he hath called against me the time, to destroy my chosen men: the Lord hath trodden the winepress for the virgin daughter of Juda.€€ƒx³Ain. Therefore do I weep, and my eyes run down with water: because the comforter, the relief of my soul, is far from me: my children are desolate because the enemy hath prevailed.€€ƒx»Phe. Sion hath spread forth her hands, there is none to comfort her: the Lord hath commanded against Jacob, his enemies are round about him: Jerusalem is as a menstruous woman among them.€€ƒx©Sade. The Lord is just, for I have provoked his mouth to wrath: hear, I pray you, all ye people, and see my sorrow: my virgins, and my young men are gone into captivity.€€†ƒx»Res. Behold, O Lord, for I am in distress, my bowels are troubled: my heart is turned within me, for I am full of bitterness: abroad the sword destroyeth and at home there is death alike.€€ƒxÛSin. They have heard that I sigh, and there is none to comfort me: all my enemies have heard of my evil, they have rejoiced that thou hast done it: thou hast brought a day of consolation, and they shall be like unto me.€€ƒx³Thau. Let all their evil be present before thee: and make vintage of them, as thou hast made vintage of me for all my iniquities: for my sighs are many, and my heart is sorrowful.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÜAleph. How hath the Lord covered with obscurity the daughter of Sion in his wrath! how hath he cast down from heaven to the earth the glorious one of Israel, and hath not remembered his footstool in the day of his anger.€€ƒyBeth. The Lord hath cast down headlong, and hath not spared, all that was beautiful in Jacob: he hath destroyed in his wrath the strong holds of the virgin of Juda, and brought them down to the ground: he hath made the kingdom unclean, and the princes thereof.€€dMatt¤dnamegMatthewiosis_namedMattlabbreviationdMatthchapters˜˜ƒxQThe book of the generation of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham:€€ƒxSAbraham begot Isaac. And Isaac begot Jacob. And Jacob begot Judas and his brethren.€€ƒxXAnd Judas begot Phares and Zara of Thamar. And Phares begot Esron. And Esron begot Aram.€€ƒxNAnd Aram begot Aminadab. And Aminadab begot Naasson. And Naasson begot Salmon.€€ƒxRAnd Salmon begot Booz of Rahab. And Booz begot Obed of Ruth. And Obed begot Jesse.€€ƒxiAnd Jesse begot David the king. And David the king begot Solomon, of her that had been the wife of Urias.€€ƒxDAnd Solomon begot Roboam. And Roboam begot Abia. And Abia begot Asa.€€ƒxHAnd Asa begot Josaphat. And Josaphat begot Joram. And Joram begot Ozias.€€ƒxKAnd Ozias begot Joatham. And Joatham begot Achaz. And Achaz begot Ezechias.€€ƒxLAnd Ezechias begot Manasses. And Manasses begot Amon. And Amon begot Josias.€€ƒxMAnd Josias begot Jechonias and his brethren in the transmigration of Babylon.€€ƒxbAnd after the transmigration of Babylon, Jechonias begot Salathiel. And Salathiel begot Zorobabel.€€ƒxKAnd Zorobabel begot Abiud. And Abiud begot Eliacim. And Eliacim begot Azor.€€ƒxCAnd Azor begot Sadoc. And Sadoc begot Achim. And Achim begot Eliud.€€ƒxJAnd Eliud begot Eleazar. And Eleazar begot Mathan. And Mathan begot Jacob.€€ƒxYAnd Jacob begot Joseph the husband of Mary, of whom was born Jesus, who is called Christ.€€ƒxİSo all the generations from Abraham to David, are fourteen generations. And from David to the transmigration of Babylon, are fourteen generations: and from the transmigration of Babylon to Christ are fourteen generations.€€ƒx¦Now the generation of Christ was in this wise. When as his mother Mary was espoused to Joseph, before they came together, she was found with child, of the Holy Ghost.€€ƒx}Whereupon Joseph her husband, being a just man, and not willing publicly to expose her, was minded to put her away privately.€€ƒxâBut while he thought on these things, behold the Angel of the Lord appeared to him in his sleep, saying: Joseph, son of David, fear not to take unto thee Mary thy wife, for that which is conceived in her, is of the Holy Ghost.€€ƒxrAnd she shall bring forth a son: and thou shalt call his name Jesus. For he shall save his people from their sins.€€ƒx]Now all this was done that it might be fulfilled which the Lord spoke by the prophet, saying:€€ƒx‹Behold a virgin shall be with child, and bring forth a son, and they shall call his name Emmanuel, which being interpreted is, God with us.€€ƒxlAnd Joseph rising up from sleep, did as the angel of the Lord had commanded him, and took unto him his wife.€€ƒx]And he knew her not, till she brought forth her first born son: and he called his name Jesus.€€—ƒx†When Jesus therefore was born in Bethlehem of Juda, in the days of king Herod, behold, there came wise men from the East to Jerusalem,€€ƒxtSaying: Where is he that is born king of the Jews? For we have seen his star in the East, and are come to adore him.€€ƒxFAnd king Herod hearing this, was troubled, and all Jerusalem with him.€€ƒx}And assembling together all the chief priests and the scribes of the people, he inquired of them where Christ should be born.€€ƒxPBut they said to him: In Bethlehem of Juda. For so it is written by the prophet:€€ƒxŸAnd thou Bethlehem the land of Juda art not the least among the princes of Juda: for out of thee shall come forth the captain that shall rule my people Israel.€€ƒxrThen Herod, privately calling the wise men learned diligently of them the time of the star which appeared to them;€€ƒx§And sending them into Bethlehem, said: Go and diligently inquire after the child, and when you have found him, bring me word again, that I also may come and adore him.€€ƒx£Who having heard the king, went their way; and behold the star which they had seen in the East, went before them, until it came and stood over where the child was.€€ƒx;And seeing the star they rejoiced with exceeding great joy.€€ƒx½And entering into the house, they found the child with Mary his mother, and falling down they adored him: and opening their treasures, they offered him gifts; gold, frankincense, and myrrh.€€ƒx{And having received an answer in sleep that they should not return to Herod, they went back another way into their country.€€ƒy
And after they were departed, behold an angel of the Lord appeared in sleep to Joseph, saying: Arise, and take the child and his mother, and fly into Egypt: and be there until I shall tell thee. For it will come to pass that Herod will seek the child to destroy him.€€ƒxyWho arose, and took the child and his mother by night, and retired into Egypt: and he was there until the death of Herod:€€ƒxjThat it might be fulfilled which the Lord spoke by the prophet, saying: Out of Egypt have I called my son.€€ƒyThen Herod perceiving that he was deluded by the wise men, was exceeding angry: and sending killed all the menchildren that were in Bethlehem, and in all the borders thereof, from two years old and under, according to the time which he had diligently inquired of the wise men.€€ƒxIThen was fulfilled that which was spoken by Jeremias the prophet, saying:€€ƒx‹A voice in Rama was heard, lamentation and great mourning; Rachel bewailing her children, and would not be comforted, because they are not.€€ƒxZBut when Herod was dead, behold an angel of the Lord appeared in sleep to Joseph in Egypt,€€ƒx†Saying: Arise, and take the child and his mother, and go into the land of Israel. For they are dead that sought the life of the child.€€ƒxOWho arose, and took the child and his mother, and came into the land of Israel.€€ƒx©But hearing that Archclaus reigned in Judea in the room of Herod his father, he was afraid to go thither: and being warned in sleep retired into the quarters of Galilee.€€ƒxAnd coming he dwelt in a city called Nazareth: that it might be fulfilled which was said by the prophets: That he shall be called a Nazarene.€€‘ƒxKAnd in those days cometh John the Baptist preaching in the desert of Judea.€€ƒx=And saying: Do penance: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand.€€ƒxFor this is he that was spoken of by Isaias the prophet, saying: A voice of one crying in the desert, Prepare ye the way of the Lord, make straight his paths.€€ƒx‚And the same John had his garment of camel's hair, and a leathern girdle about his loins: and his meat was locusts and wild honey.€€ƒxOThen went out to him Jerusalem and all Judea, and all the country about Jordan:€€ƒx>And were baptized by him in the Jordan, confessing their sins.€€ƒxAnd seeing many of the Pharisees and Sadducees coming to his baptism, he said to them: Ye brood of vipers, who hath shewed you to flee from the wrath to come?€€ƒx.Bring forth therefore fruit worthy of penance.€€ƒx˜And think not to say within yourselves, We have Abraham for our father. For I tell you that God is able of these stones to raise up children to Abraham.€€ƒx‘For now the axe is laid to the root of the trees. Every tree therefore that doth not yield good fruit, shall be cut down, and cast into the fire.€€ƒx¶I indeed baptize you in water unto penance, but he that shall come after me, is mightier than I, whose shoes I am not worthy to bear: he shall baptize you in the Holy Ghost and fire.€€ƒx™Whose fan is in his hand, and he will thoroughly cleanse his floor and gather his wheat into the barn; but the chaff he will burn with unquenchable fire.€€ƒxOThen cometh Jesus from Galilee to the Jordan, unto John, to be baptized by him.€€ƒxSBut John stayed him, saying: I ought to be baptized by thee, and comest thou to me?€€ƒx|And Jesus answering, said to him: Suffer it to be so now. For so it becometh us to fulfil all justice. Then he suffered him.€€ƒxªAnd Jesus being baptized, forthwith came out of the water: and lo, the heavens were opened to him: and he saw the Spirit of God descending as a dove, and coming upon him.€€ƒxYAnd behold a voice from heaven saying: This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.€€˜ƒxMThen Jesus was led by the spirit into the desert, to be tempted by the devil.€€ƒxMAnd when he had fasted forty days and forty nights, afterwards he was hungry.€€ƒxgAnd the tempter coming said to him: If thou be the Son of God, command that these stones be made bread.€€ƒx€Who answered and said: It is written, Not in bread alone doth man live, but in every word that proceedeth from the mouth of God.€€ƒx[Then the devil took him up into the holy city, and set him upon the pinnacle of the temple,€€ƒxŞAnd said to him: If thou be the Son of God, cast thyself down, for it is written: That he hath given his angels charge over thee, and in their hands shall they bear thee up, lest perhaps thou dash thy foot against a stone.€€ƒxNJesus said to him: It is written again: Thou shalt not tempt the Lord thy God.€€ƒx{Again the devil took him up into a very high mountain, and shewed him all the kingdoms of the world, and the glory of them,€€ƒxPAnd said to him: All these will I give thee, if falling down thou wilt adore me.€€ƒx|Then Jesus saith to him: Begone, Satan: for it is written: The Lord thy God shalt thou adore, and him only shalt thou serve.€€ƒxFThen the devil left him; and behold angels came and ministered to him.€€ƒxMAnd when Jesus had heard that John was delivered up, he retired into Galilee:€€ƒx}And leaving the city Nazareth, he came and dwelt in Capharnaum on the sea coast, in the borders of Zabulon and of Nephthalim;€€ƒx@That it might be fulfilled which was said by Isaias the prophet:€€ƒxfLand of Zabulon and land of Nephthalim, the way of the sea beyond the Jordan, Galilee of the Gentiles:€€ƒx†The people that sat in darkness, hath seen great light: and to them that sat in the region of the shadow of death, light is sprung up.€€ƒxcFrom that time Jesus began to preach, and to say: Do penance, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand.€€ƒx¡And Jesus walking by the sea of Galilee, saw two brethren, Simon who is called Peter, and Andrew his brother, casting a net into the sea (for they were fishers).€€ƒxQAnd he saith to them: Come ye after me, and I will make you to be fishers of men.€€ƒx6And they immediately leaving their nets, followed him.€€ƒx±And going on from thence, he saw other two brethren, James the son of Zebedee, and John his brother, in a ship with Zebedee their father, mending their nets: and he called them.€€ƒx@And they forthwith left their nets and father, and followed him.€€ƒx²And Jesus went about all Galilee, teaching in their synagogues, and preaching the gospel of the kingdom: and healing all manner of sickness and every infirmity, among the people.€€ƒxéAnd his fame went throughout all Syria, and they presented to him all sick people that were taken with divers diseases and torments, and such as were possessed by devils, and lunatics, and those that had the palsy, and he cured them:€€ƒx~And much people followed him from Galilee, and from Decapolis, and from Jerusalem, and from Judea, and from beyond the Jordan.€€˜0ƒxmAnd seeing the multitudes, he went up into a mountain, and when he was set down, his disciples came unto him.€€ƒx-And opening his mouth he taught them, saying:€€ƒxDBlessed are the poor in spirit: for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.€€ƒx6Blessed are the meek: for they shall possess the land.€€ƒx9Blessed are they that mourn: for they shall be comforted.€€ƒxVBlessed are they that hunger and thirst after justice: for they shall have their fill.€€ƒx6Blessed are the merciful: for they shall obtain mercy.€€ƒx3Blessed are the clean of heart: they shall see God.€€ƒxJBlessed are the peacemakers: for they shall be called the children of God.€€ƒx`Blessed are they that suffer persecution for justice' sake: for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.€€ƒx{Blessed are ye when they shall revile you, and persecute you, and speak all that is evil against you, untruly, for my sake:€€ƒxvBe glad and rejoice for your reward is very great in heaven. For so they persecuted the prophets that were before you.€€ƒx­You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt lose its savour, wherewith shall it be salted? It is good for nothing anymore but to be cast out, and to be trodden on by men.€€ƒxJYou are the light of the world. A city seated on a mountain cannot be hid.€€ƒx€Neither do men light a candle and put it under a bushel, but upon a candlestick, that it may shine to all that are in the house.€€ƒxpSo let your light shine before men, that they may see your good works, and glorify your Father who is in heaven.€€ƒxiDo not think that I am come to destroy the law, or the prophets. I am not come to destroy, but to fulfil.€€ƒx}For amen I say unto you, till heaven and earth pass, one jot, or one tittle shall not pass of the law, till all be fulfilled.€€ƒxÜHe therefore that shall break one of these least commandments, and shall so teach men shall be called the least in the kingdom of heaven. But he that shall do and teach, he shall be called great in the kingdom of heaven.€€ƒxŒFor I tell you, that unless your justice abound more than that of the scribes and Pharisees, you shall not enter into the kingdom of heaven.€€ƒx‚You have heard that it was said to them of old: Thou shalt not kill. And whosoever shall kill, shall be in danger of the judgment.€€ƒxõBut I say to you, that whosoever is angry with his brother, shall be in danger of the judgment. And whosoever shall say to his brother, Raca, shall be in danger of the council. And whosoever shall say, Thou fool, shall be in danger of hell fire.€€ƒxsIf therefore thou offer thy gift at the altar, and there thou remember that thy brother hath anything against thee;€€ƒxƒLeave there thy offering before the altar, and go first to be reconciled to thy brother, and then coming thou shalt offer thy gift.€€ƒxÏBe at agreement with thy adversary betimes, whilst thou art in the way with him: lest perhaps the adversary deliver thee to the judge, and the judge deliver thee to the officer, and thou be cast into prison.€€ƒxXAmen I say to thee, thou shalt not go out from thence till thou repay the last farthing.€€ƒxOYou have heard that it was said to them of old: Thou shalt not commit adultery.€€ƒx€But I say to you, that whosoever shall look on a woman to lust after her, hath already committed adultery with her in his heart.€€ƒx»And if thy right eye scandalize thee, pluck it out and cast it from thee. For it is expedient for thee that one of thy members should perish, rather than thy whole body be cast into hell.€€ƒx»And if thy right hand scandalize thee, cut it off, and cast it from thee: for it is expedient for thee that one of thy members should perish, rather than that thy whole body go into hell.€€ƒx]And it hath been said, Whosoever shall put away his wife, let him give her a bill of divorce.€€ƒx¿But I say to you, that whosoever shall put away his wife, excepting the cause of fornication, maketh her to commit adultery: and he that shall marry her that is put away, committeth adultery.€€ƒx„Again you have heard that it was said to them of old, thou shalt not forswear thyself: but thou shalt perform thy oaths to the Lord.€€ƒxTBut I say to you not to swear at all, neither by heaven for it is the throne of God:€€ƒxbNor by the earth, for it is his footstool: nor by Jerusalem, for it is the city of the great king:€€ƒxZNeither shalt thou swear by thy head, because thou canst not make one hair white or black.€€ƒx\But let your speech be yea, yea: no, no: and that which is over and above these, is of evil.€€ƒxRYou have heard that it hath been said: An eye for an eye, and a tooth for a tooth.€€ƒxkBut I say to you not to resist evil: but if one strike thee on thy right cheek, turn to him also the other:€€ƒxhAnd if a man will contend with thee in judgment, and take away thy coat, let go thy cloak also unto him.€€ƒx>And whosoever will force thee one mile, go with him other two.€€ƒxVGive to him that asketh of thee, and from him that would borrow of thee turn not away.€€ƒxYYou have heard that it hath been said, Thou shalt love thy neighbour, and hate thy enemy.€€ƒxxBut I say to you, Love your enemies: do good to them that hate you: and pray for them that persecute and calumniate you:€€ƒxšThat you may be the children of your Father who is in heaven, who maketh his sun to rise upon the good, and bad, and raineth upon the just and the unjust.€€ƒx_For if you love them that love you, what reward shall you have? do not even the publicans this?€€ƒxVAnd if you salute your brethren only, what do you more? do not also the heathens this?€€ƒxBBe you therefore perfect, as also your heavenly Father is perfect.€€˜"ƒxTake heed that you do not your justice before men, to be seen by them: otherwise you shall not have a reward of your Father who is in heaven.€€ƒxØTherefore when thou dost an alms-deed, sound not a trumpet before thee, as the hypocrites do in the synagogues and in the streets, that they may be honoured by men. Amen I say to you, they have received their reward.€€ƒxMBut when thou dost alms, let not thy left hand know what thy right hand doth.€€ƒxSThat thy alms may be in secret, and thy Father who seeth in secret will repay thee.€€ƒxÑAnd when ye pray, you shall not be as the hypocrites, that love to stand and pray in the synagogues and corners of the streets, that they may be seen by men: Amen I say to you, they have received their reward.€€ƒx¢But thou when thou shalt pray, enter into thy chamber, and having shut the door, pray to thy Father in secret, and thy father who seeth in secret will repay thee.€€ƒxxAnd when you are praying, speak not much, as the heathens. For they think that in their much speaking they may be heard.€€ƒxfBe not you therefore like to them for your Father knoweth what is needful for you, before you ask him.€€ƒxRThus therefore shall you pray: Our Father who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name.€€ƒx?Thy kingdom come. Thy will be done on earth as it is in heaven.€€ƒx,Give us this day our supersubstantial bread.€€ƒx9And forgive us our debts, as we also forgive our debtors.€€ƒx@And lead us not into temptation. But deliver us from evil. Amen.€€ƒxeFor if you will forgive men their offences, your heavenly Father will forgive you also your offences.€€ƒxTBut if you will not forgive men, neither will your Father forgive you your offences.€€ƒx¬And when you fast, be not as the hypocrites, sad. For they disfigure their faces, that they may appear unto men to fast. Amen I say to you, they have received their reward.€€ƒx?But thou, when thou fastest anoint thy head, and wash thy face;€€ƒx}That thou appear not to men to fast, but to thy Father who is in secret: and thy Father who seeth in secret, will repay thee.€€ƒxzLay not up to yourselves treasures on earth: where the rust, and moth consume, and where thieves break through, and steal.€€ƒxBut lay up to yourselves treasures in heaven: where neither the rust nor moth doth consume, and where thieves do not break through, nor steal.€€ƒx3For where thy treasure is, there is thy heart also.€€ƒxZThe light of thy body is thy eye. If thy eye be single, thy whole body shall be lightsome.€€ƒx“But if thy eye be evil thy whole body shall be darksome. If then the light that is in thee, be darkness: the darkness itself how great shall it be!€€ƒx¦No man can serve two masters. For either he will hate the one, and love the other: or he will sustain the one, and despise the other. You cannot serve God and mammon.€€ƒx¾Therefore I say to you, be not solicitous for your life, what you shall eat, nor for your body, what you shall put on. Is not the life more than the meat: and the body more than the raiment?€€ƒx¬Behold the birds of the air, for they neither sow, nor do they reap, nor gather into barns: and your heavenly Father feedeth them. Are not you of much more value than they?€€ƒxEAnd which of you by taking thought, can add to his stature one cubit?€€ƒxAnd for raiment why are you solicitous? Consider the lilies of the field, how they grow: they labour not, neither do they spin.€€ƒxUBut I say to you, that not even Solomon in all his glory was arrayed as one of these.€€ƒx‘And if the grass of the field, which is to day, and to morrow is cast into the oven, God doth so clothe: how much more you, O ye of little faith?€€ƒxqBe not solicitous therefore, saying: What shall we eat: or what shall we drink, or wherewith shall we be clothed?€€ƒxpFor after all these things do the heathens seek. For your Father knoweth that you have need of all these things.€€ƒxjSeek ye therefore first the kingdom of God, and his justice, and all these things shall be added unto you.€€ƒx„Be not therefore solicitous for to morrow; for the morrow will be solicitous for itself. Sufficient for the day is the evil thereof.€€˜ƒx&Judge not, that you may not be judged.€€ƒxyFor with what judgment you judge, you shall be judged: and with what measure you mete, it shall be measured to you again.€€ƒxhAnd why seest thou the mote that is in thy brother's eye; and seest not the beam that is in thy own eye?€€ƒxlOr how sayest thou to thy brother: Let me cast the mote out of thy eye; and behold a beam is in thy own eye?€€ƒx‚Thou hypocrite, cast out first the beam out of thy own eye, and then shalt thou see to cast out the mote out of thy brother's eye.€€ƒx¤Give not that which is holy to dogs; neither cast ye your pearls before swine, lest perhaps they trample them under their feet, and turning upon you, they tear you.€€ƒx_Ask, and it shall be given you: seek, and you shall find: knock, and it shall be opened to you.€€ƒxqFor every one that asketh, receiveth: and he that seeketh, findeth: and to him that knocketh, it shall be opened.€€ƒx^Or what man is there among you, of whom if his son shall ask bread, will he reach him a stone?€€ƒx;Or if he shall ask him a fish, will he reach him a serpent?€€ƒxIf you then being evil, know how to give good gifts to your children: how much more will your Father who is in heaven, give good things to them that ask him?€€ƒxAll things therefore whatsoever you would that men should do to you, do you also to them. For this is the law and the prophets.€€ƒxEnter ye in at the narrow gate: for wide is the gate, and broad is the way that leadeth to destruction, and many there are who go in thereat.€€ƒxcHow narrow is the gate, and strait is the way that leadeth to life: and few there are that find it!€€ƒxjBeware of false prophets, who come to you in the clothing of sheep, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.€€ƒxYBy their fruits you shall know them. Do men gather grapes of thorns, or figs of thistles?€€ƒx_Even so every good tree bringeth forth good fruit, and the evil tree bringeth forth evil fruit.€€ƒx[A good tree cannot bring forth evil fruit, neither can an evil tree bring forth good fruit.€€ƒxbEvery tree that bringeth not forth good fruit, shall be cut down, and shall be cast into the fire.€€ƒx.Wherefore by their fruits you shall know them.€€ƒx·Not every one that saith to me, Lord, Lord, shall enter into the kingdom of heaven: but he that doth the will of my Father who is in heaven, he shall enter into the kingdom of heaven.€€ƒx•Many will say to me in that day: Lord, Lord, have not we prophesied in thy name, and cast out devils in thy name, and done many miracles in thy name?€€ƒx\And then will I profess unto them, I never knew you: depart from me, you that work iniquity.€€ƒx€Every one therefore that heareth these my words, and doth them, shall be likened to a wise man that built his house upon a rock,€€ƒx‰And the rain fell, and the floods came, and the winds blew, and they beat upon that house, and it fell not, for it was founded on a rock.€€ƒx|And every one that heareth these my words and doth them not, shall be like a foolish man that built his house upon the sand,€€ƒx‡And the rain fell, and the floods came, and the winds blew, and they beat upon that house, and it fell, and great was the fall thereof.€€ƒxjAnd it came to pass when Jesus had fully ended these words, the people were in admiration at his doctrine.€€ƒxSFor he was teaching them as one having power, and not as the scribes and Pharisees.€€˜"ƒxKAnd when he was come down from the mountain, great multitudes followed him:€€ƒx]And behold a leper came and adored him, saying: Lord, if thou wilt, thou canst make me clean.€€ƒx}And Jesus stretching forth his hand, touched him, saying: I will, be thou made clean. And forthwith his leprosy was cleansed.€€ƒx–And Jesus saith to him: See thou tell no man: but go, shew thyself to the priest, and offer the gift which Moses commanded, for a testimony unto them.€€ƒxWAnd when he had entered into Capharnaum, there came to him a centurion, beseeching him,€€ƒxZAnd saying, Lord, my servant lieth at home sick of the palsy, and is grievously tormented.€€ƒx1And Jesus saith to him: I will come and heal him.€€ƒxœAnd the centurion, making answer, said: Lord, I am not worthy that thou shouldst enter under my roof; but only say the word, and my servant shall be healed.€€ƒx¹For I also am a man subject to authority, having under me soldiers; and I say to this, Go, and he goeth, and to another Come, and he cometh, and to my servant, Do this, and he doeth it.€€ƒx„And Jesus hearing this, marvelled; and said to them that followed him. Amen I say to you, I have not found so great faith in Israel.€€ƒxAnd I say to you that many shall come from the east and the west, and shall sit down with Abraham, and Isaac and Jacob in the kingdom of heaven:€€ƒx{But the children of the kingdom shall be cast out into the exterior darkness: there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.€€ƒxƒAnd Jesus said to the centurion: Go, and as thou hast believed, so be it done to thee. And the servant was healed at the same hour.€€ƒx`And when Jesus was come into Peter's house, he saw his wife's mother lying, and sick of a fever;€€ƒxVAnd he touched her hand, and the fever left her, and she arose and ministered to them.€€ƒx¡And when evening was come, they brought to him many that were possessed with devils: and he cast out the spirits with his word: and all that were sick he healed:€€ƒx{That it might be fulfilled, which was spoken by the prophet Isaias, saying: He took our infirmities, and bore our diseases.€€ƒxPAnd Jesus seeing great multitudes about him, gave orders to pass over the water.€€ƒxbAnd a certain scribe came and said to him: Master, I will follow thee whithersoever thou shalt go.€€ƒx€And Jesus saith to him: The foxes have holes, and the birds of the air nests; but the Son of man hath not where to lay his head.€€ƒxYAnd another of his disciples said to him: Lord, suffer me first to go and bury my father.€€ƒxCBut Jesus said to him: Follow me, and let the dead bury their dead.€€ƒx>And when he entered into the boat, his disciples followed him:€€ƒxhAnd behold a great tempest arose in the sea, so that the boat was covered with waves, but he was asleep.€€ƒxGAnd they came to him, and awaked him, saying: Lord, save us, we perish.€€ƒx•And Jesus saith to them: Why are you fearful, O ye of little faith? Then rising up, he commanded the winds, and the sea, and there came a great calm.€€ƒx]But the men wondered, saying: What manner of man is this, for the winds and the sea obey him?€€ƒxŞAnd when he was come on the other side of the water, into the country of the Gerasens, there met him two that were possessed with devils, coming out of the sepulchres, exceeding fierce, so that none could pass by that way.€€ƒx†And behold they cried out, saying: What have we to do with thee, Jesus Son of God? art thou come hither to torment us before the time?€€ƒx?And there was, not far from them, a herd of many swine feeding.€€ƒx_And the devils besought him, saying: If thou cast us out hence, send us into the herd of swine.€€ƒxªAnd he said to them: Go. But they going out went into the swine, and behold the whole herd ran violently down a steep place into the sea: and they perished in the waters.€€ƒx„And they that kept them fled: and coming into the city, told every thing, and concerning them that had been possessed by the devils.€€ƒxAnd behold the whole city went out to meet Jesus, and when they saw him, they besought him that he would depart from their coast.€€˜&ƒxNAnd entering into a boat, he passed over the water and came into his own city.€€ƒx¹And behold they brought to him one sick of the palsy lying in a bed. And Jesus, seeing their faith, said to the man sick of the palsy: Be of good heart, son, thy sins are forgiven thee.€€ƒxFAnd behold some of the scribes said within themselves: He blasphemeth.€€ƒxLAnd Jesus seeing their thoughts, said: Why do you think evil in your hearts?€€ƒxRWhether is easier, to say, Thy sins are forgiven thee: or to say, Arise, and walk?€€ƒxªBut that you may know that the Son of man hath power on earth to forgive sins, (then said he to the man sick of the palsy,) Arise, take up thy bed, and go into thy house.€€ƒx&And he arose, and went into his house.€€ƒxSAnd the multitude seeing it, feared, and glorified God that gave such power to men.€€ƒx And when Jesus passed on from thence, he saw a man sitting in the custom house, named Matthew; and he saith to him: Follow me. And he arose up and followed him.€€ƒxAnd it came to pass as he was sitting at meat in the house, behold many publicans and sinners came, and sat down with Jesus and his disciples.€€ƒxhAnd the Pharisees seeing it, said to his disciples: Why doth your master eat with publicans and sinners?€€ƒx`But Jesus hearing it, said: They that are in health need not a physician, but they that are ill.€€ƒxzGo then and learn what this meaneth, I will have mercy and not sacrifice. For I am not come to call the just, but sinners.€€ƒxwThen came to him the disciples of John, saying: Why do we and the Pharisees, fast often, but thy disciples do not fast?€€ƒxÎAnd Jesus said to them: Can the children of the bridegroom mourn, as long as the bridegroom is with them? But the days will come, when the bridegroom shall be taken away from them, and then they shall fast.€€ƒx—And nobody putteth a piece of raw cloth unto an old garment. For it taketh away the fulness thereof from the garment, and there is made a greater rent.€€ƒx½Neither do they put new wine into old bottles. Otherwise the bottles break, and the wine runneth out, and the bottles perish. But new wine they put into new bottles: and both are preserved.€€ƒx»As he was speaking these things unto them, behold a certain ruler came up, and adored him, saying: Lord, my daughter is even now dead; but come, lay thy hand upon her, and she shall live.€€ƒx5And Jesus rising up followed him, with his disciples.€€ƒx}And behold a woman who was troubled with an issue of blood twelve years, came behind him, and touched the hem of his garment.€€ƒxRFor she said within herself: If I shall touch only his garment, I shall be healed.€€ƒxBut Jesus turning and seeing her, said: Be of good heart, daughter, thy faith hath made thee whole. And the woman was made whole from that hour.€€ƒxkAnd when Jesus was come into the house of the ruler, and saw the minstrels and the multitude making a rout,€€ƒx[He said: Give place, for the girl is not dead, but sleepeth. And they laughed him to scorn.€€ƒx_And when the multitude was put forth, he went in, and took her by the hand. And the maid arose.€€ƒx6And the fame hereof went abroad into all that country.€€ƒxzAnd as Jesus passed from thence, there followed him two blind men crying out and saying, Have mercy on us, O Son of David.€€ƒxŸAnd when he was come to the house, the blind men came to him. And Jesus saith to them, Do you believe, that I can do this unto you? They say to him, Yea, Lord.€€ƒxQThen he touched their eyes, saying, According to your faith, be it done unto you.€€ƒx_And their eyes were opened, and Jesus strictly charged them, saying, See that no man know this.€€ƒx?But they going out, spread his fame abroad in all that country.€€ƒxXAnd when they were gone out, behold they brought him a dumb man, possessed with a devil.€€ƒx}And after the devil was cast out, the dumb man spoke, and the multitudes wondered, saying, Never was the like seen in Israel.€€ƒxFBut the Pharisees said, By the prince of devils he casteth out devils.€€ƒx¥And Jesus went about all the cities and towns, teaching in their synagogues, and preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing every disease, and every infirmity.€€ƒxAnd seeing the multitudes, he had compassion on them: because they were distressed, and lying like sheep that have no shepherd.€€ƒxWThen he saith to his disciples, The harvest indeed is great, but the labourers are few.€€ƒxYPray ye therefore the Lord of the harvest, that he send forth labourers into his harvest.€€˜*ƒx®And having called his twelve disciples together, he gave them power over unclean spirits, to cast them out, and to heal all manner of diseases, and all manner of infirmities.€€ƒxmAnd the names of the twelve Apostles are these: The first, Simon who is called Peter, and Andrew his brother,€€ƒx”James the son of Zebedee, and John his brother, Philip and Bartholomew, Thomas and Matthew the publican, and James the son of Alpheus, and Thaddeus,€€ƒx>Simon the Cananean, and Judas Iscariot, who also betrayed him.€€ƒx‹These twelve Jesus sent: commanding them, saying: Go ye not into the way of the Gentiles, and into the city of the Samaritans enter ye not.€€ƒx:But go ye rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.€€ƒx<And going, preach, saying: The kingdom of heaven is at hand.€€ƒxjHeal the sick, raise the dead, cleanse the lepers, cast out devils: freely have you received, freely give.€€ƒx:Do not possess gold, nor silver, nor money in your purses:€€ƒxiNor scrip for your journey, nor two coats, nor shoes, nor a staff; for the workman is worthy of his meat.€€ƒxrAnd into whatsoever city or town you shall enter, inquire who in it is worthy, and there abide till you go thence.€€ƒxLAnd when you come into the house, salute it, saying: Peace be to this house.€€ƒxtAnd if that house be worthy, your peace shall come upon it; but if it be not worthy, your peace shall return to you.€€ƒx‚And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear your words: going forth out of that house or city shake off the dust from your feet.€€ƒx|Amen I say to you, it shall be more tolerable for the land of Sodom and Gomorrha in the day of judgment, than for that city.€€ƒxhBehold I send you as sheep in the midst of wolves. Be ye therefore wise as serpents and simple as doves.€€ƒxkBut beware of men. For they will deliver you up in councils, and they will scourge you in their synagogues.€€ƒxuAnd you shall be brought before governors, and before kings for my sake, for a testimony to them and to the Gentiles:€€ƒxBut when they shall deliver you up, take no thought how or what to speak: for it shall be given you in that hour what to speak:€€ƒxQFor it is not you that speak, but the spirit of your Father that speaketh in you.€€ƒx¢The brother also shall deliver up the brother to death, and the father the son; and the children shall rise up against their parents, and shall put them to death.€€ƒxrAnd you shall be hated by all men for my name's sake: but he that shall persevere unto the end, he shall be saved.€€ƒxAnd when they shall persecute you in this city, flee into another. Amen I say to you, you shall not finish all the cities of Israel, till the Son of man come.€€ƒxEThe disciple is not above the master, nor the servant above his lord.€€ƒx²It is enough for the disciple that he be as his master, and the servant as his lord. If they have called the good man of the house Beelzebub, how much more them of his household?€€ƒxmTherefore fear them not. For nothing is covered that shall not be revealed: nor hid, that shall not be known.€€ƒx{That which I tell you in the dark, speak ye in the light: and that which you hear in the ear, preach ye upon the housetops.€€ƒxŒAnd fear ye not them that kill the body, and are not able to kill the soul: but rather fear him that can destroy both soul and body in hell.€€ƒxkAre not two sparrows sold for a farthing? and not one of them shall fall on the ground without your Father.€€ƒx1But the very hairs of your head are all numbered.€€ƒx6Fear not therefore: better are you than many sparrows.€€ƒxpEvery one therefore that shall confess me before men, I will also confess him before my Father who is in heaven.€€ƒx]But he that shall deny me before men, I will also deny him before my Father who is in heaven.€€ƒx[Do not think that I came to send peace upon earth: I came not to send peace, but the sword.€€ƒxFor I came to set a man at variance against his father, and the daughter against her mother, and the daughter in law against her mother in law.€€ƒx7And a man's enemies shall be they of his own household.€€ƒxˆHe that loveth father or mother more than me, is not worthy of me; and he that loveth son or daughter more than me, is not worthy of me.€€ƒxKAnd he that taketh not up his cross, and followeth me, is not worthy of me.€€ƒx_He that findeth his life, shall lose it: and he that shall lose his life for me, shall find it.€€ƒxZHe that receiveth you, receiveth me: and he that receiveth me, receiveth him that sent me.€€ƒxÀHe that receiveth a prophet in the name of a prophet, shall receive the reward of a prophet: and he that receiveth a just man in the name of a just man, shall receive the reward of a just man.€€ƒx¡And whosoever shall give to drink to one of these little ones a cup of cold water only in the name of a disciple, amen I say to you he shall not lose his reward.€€˜ƒx’And it came to pass, when Jesus had made an end of commanding his twelve disciples, he passed from thence, to teach and to preach in their cities.€€ƒxcNow when John had heard in prison the works of Christ: sending two of his disciples he said to him:€€ƒx5Art thou he that art to come, or look we for another?€€ƒxYAnd Jesus making answer said to them: Go and relate to John what you have heard and seen.€€ƒx…The blind see, the lame walk, the lepers are cleansed, the deaf hear, the dead rise again, the poor have the gospel preached to them.€€ƒx6And blessed is he that shall not be scandalized in me.€€ƒxšAnd when they went their way, Jesus began to say to the multitudes concerning John: What went you out into the desert to see? a reed shaken with the wind?€€ƒxˆBut what went you out to see? a man clothed in soft garments? Behold they that are clothed in soft garments, are in the houses of kings.€€ƒxQBut what went you out to see? A prophet? Yea I tell you, and more than a prophet.€€ƒxsFor this is he of whom it is written: Behold I send my angel before my face, who shall prepare thy way before thee.€€ƒx±Amen I say to you, there hath not risen among them that are born of women a greater than John the Baptist: yet he that is the lesser in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.€€ƒxxAnd from the days of John the Baptist until now, the kingdom of heaven suffereth violence, and the violent bear it away.€€ƒx7For all the prophets and the law prophesied until John:€€ƒx8And if you will receive it, he is Elias that is to come.€€ƒx(He that hath ears to hear, let him hear.€€ƒxlBut whereunto shall I esteem this generation to be like? It is like to children sitting in the market place.€€ƒx~Who crying to their companions say: We have piped to you, and you have not danced: we have lamented, and you have not mourned.€€ƒxIFor John came neither eating nor drinking; and they say: He hath a devil.€€ƒxµThe Son of man came eating and drinking, and they say: Behold a man that is a glutton and a wine drinker, a friend of publicans and sinners. And wisdom is justified by her children.€€ƒxsThen began he to upbraid the cities wherein were done the most of his miracles, for that they had not done penance.€€ƒx¸Woe thee, Corozain, woe to thee, Bethsaida: for if in Tyre and Sidon had been wrought the miracles that have been wrought in you, they had long ago done penance in sackcloth and ashes.€€ƒxgBut I say unto you, it shall be more tolerable for Tyre and Sidon in the day of judgment, than for you.€€ƒxĞAnd thou Capharnaum, shalt thou be exalted up to heaven? thou shalt go down even unto hell. For if in Sodom had been wrought the miracles that have been wrought in thee, perhaps it had remained unto this day.€€ƒxoBut I say unto you, that it shall be more tolerable for the land of Sodom in the day of judgment than for thee.€€ƒxÁAt that time Jesus answered and said: I confess to thee, O Father, Lord of Heaven and earth, because thou hast hid these things from the wise and prudent, and hast revealed them to little ones.€€ƒx5Yea, Father: for so hath it seemed good in thy sight.€€ƒxÀAll things are delivered to me by my Father. And no one knoweth the Son but the Father: neither doth any one know the Father, but the Son, and he to whom it shall please the Son to reveal him.€€ƒxGCome to me all you that labor and are burdened, and I will refresh you.€€ƒxyTake up my yoke upon you, and learn of me, because I am meek, and humble of heart: And you shall find rest to your souls.€€ƒx)For my yoke is sweet and my burden light.€€˜2ƒx}At that time Jesus went through the corn on the sabbath: and his disciples being hungry, began to pluck the ears, and to eat.€€ƒxwAnd the Pharisees seeing them, said to him: Behold thy disciples do that which is not lawful to do on the sabbath days.€€ƒxfBut he said to them: Have you not read what David did when he was hungry, and they that were with him:€€ƒx¯How he entered into the house of God, and did eat the loaves of proposition, which it was not lawful for him to eat, nor for them that were with him, but for the priests only?€€ƒx|Or have ye not read in the law, that on the sabbath days the priests in the temple break the sabbath, and are without blame?€€ƒx<But I tell you that there is here a greater than the temple.€€ƒxuAnd if you knew what this meaneth: I will have mercy, and not sacrifice: you would never have condemned the innocent.€€ƒx/For the Son of man is Lord even of the sabbath.€€ƒxBAnd when he had passed from thence, he came into their synagogues.€€ƒx•And behold there was a man who had a withered hand, and they asked him, saying: Is it lawful to heal on the sabbath days? that they might accuse him.€€ƒx¬But he said to them: What man shall there be among you, that hath one sheep: and if the same fall into a pit on the sabbath day, will he not take hold on it and lift it up?€€ƒxdHow much better is a man than a sheep? Therefore it is lawful to do a good deed on the sabbath days.€€ƒx}Then he saith to the man: Stretch forth thy hand; and he stretched it forth, and it was restored to health even as the other.€€ƒxXAnd the Pharisees going out made a consultation against him, how they might destroy him.€€ƒxYBut Jesus knowing it, retired from thence: and many followed him, and he healed them all.€€ƒx8And he charged them that they should not make him known.€€ƒxJThat it might be fulfilled which was spoken by Isaias the prophet, saying:€€ƒx£Behold my servant whom I have chosen, my beloved in whom my soul hath been well pleased. I will put my spirit upon him, and he shall shew judgment to the Gentiles.€€ƒxWHe shall not contend, nor cry out, neither shall any man hear his voice in the streets.€€ƒxxThe bruised reed he shall not break: and smoking flax he shall not extinguish: till he send forth judgment unto victory.€€ƒx(And in his name the Gentiles shall hope.€€ƒxpThen was offered to him one possessed with a devil, blind and dumb: and he healed him, so that he spoke and saw.€€ƒxKAnd all the multitudes were amazed, and said: Is not this the son of David?€€ƒxnBut the Pharisees hearing it, said: This man casteth not out devils but by Beelzebub the prince of the devils.€€ƒx¬And Jesus knowing their thoughts, said to them: Every kingdom divided against itself shall be made desolate: and every city or house divided against itself shall not stand.€€ƒx]And if Satan cast out Satan, he is divided against himself: how then shall his kingdom stand?€€ƒxsAnd if I by Beelzebub cast out devils, by whom do your children cast them out? Therefore they shall be your judges.€€ƒxXBut if I by the Spirit of God cast out devils, then is the kingdom of God come upon you.€€ƒxOr how can any one enter into the house of the strong, and rifle his goods, unless he first bind the strong? and then he will rifle his house.€€ƒxUHe that is not with me, is against me: and he that gathereth not with me, scattereth.€€ƒx}Therefore I say to you: Every sin and blasphemy shall be forgiven men, but the blasphemy of the Spirit shall not be forgiven.€€ƒxĞAnd whosoever shall speak a word against the Son of man, it shall be forgiven him: but he that shall speak against the Holy Ghost, it shall not be forgiven him neither in this world, nor in the world to come.€€ƒx|Either make the tree good and its fruit good: or make the tree evil, and its fruit evil. For by the fruit the tree is known.€€ƒx†O generation of vipers, how can you speak good things, whereas you are evil? for out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaketh.€€ƒxA good man out of a good treasure bringeth forth good things: and an evil man out of an evil treasure bringeth forth evil things.€€ƒxzBut I say unto you, that every idle word that men shall speak, they shall render an account for it in the day of judgment.€€ƒxSFor by thy words thou shalt be justified, and by thy words thou shalt be condemned.€€ƒxcThen some of the scribes and Pharisees answered him, saying: Master, we would see a sign from thee.€€ƒx’Who answering said to them: An evil and adulterous generation seeketh a sign: and a sign shall not be given it, but the sign of Jonas the prophet.€€ƒx”For as Jonas was in the whale's belly three days and three nights: so shall the Son of man be in the heart of the earth three days and three nights.€€ƒx®The men of Ninive shall rise in judgment with this generation, and shall condemn it: because they did penance at the preaching of Jonas. And behold a greater than Jonas here.€€ƒxÌThe queen of the south shall rise in judgment with this generation, and shall condemn it: because she came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon, and behold a greater than Solomon here.€€ƒxmAnd when an unclean spirit is gone out of a man he walketh through dry places seeking rest, and findeth none.€€ƒxxThen he saith: I will return into my house from whence I came out. And coming he findeth it empty, swept, and garnished.€€ƒxßThen he goeth, and taketh with him seven other spirits more wicked than himself, and they enter in and dwell there: and the last state of that man is made worse than the first. So shall it be also to this wicked generation.€€ƒxtAs he was yet speaking to the multitudes, behold his mother and his brethren stood without, seeking to speak to him.€€ƒxVAnd one said unto him: Behold thy mother and thy brethren stand without, seeking thee.€€ƒxTBut he answering him that told him, said: Who is my mother, and who are my brethren?€€ƒx_And stretching forth his hand towards his disciples, he said: Behold my mother and my brethren.€€ƒxjFor whosoever shall do the will of my Father, that is in heaven, he is my brother, and sister, and mother.€€˜:ƒx?The same day Jesus going out of the house, sat by the sea side.€€ƒx‡And great multitudes were gathered together unto him, so that he went up into a boat and sat: and all the multitude stood on the shore.€€ƒxYAnd he spoke to them many things in parables, saying: Behold the sower went forth to sow.€€ƒx^And whilst he soweth some fell by the way side, and the birds of the air came and ate them up.€€ƒxŒAnd other some fell upon stony ground, where they had not much earth: and they sprung up immediately, because they had no deepness of earth.€€ƒx^And when the sun was up they were scorched: and because they had not root, they withered away.€€ƒxEAnd others fell among thorns: and the thorns grew up and choked them.€€ƒx|And others fell upon good ground: and they brought forth fruit, some an hundred fold, some sixty fold, and some thirty fold.€€ƒx(He that hath ears to hear, let him hear.€€ƒxNAnd his disciples came and said to him: Why speakest thou to them in parables?€€ƒx†Who answered and said to them: Because to you it is given to know the mysteries of the kingdom of heaven: but to them it is not given.€€ƒx‰For he that hath, to him shall be given, and he shall abound: but he that hath not, from him shall be taken away that also which he hath.€€ƒx}Therefore do I speak to them in parables: because seeing they see not, and hearing they hear not, neither do they understand.€€ƒx¢And the prophecy of Isaias is fulfilled in them, who saith: By hearing you shall hear, and shall not understand: and seeing you shall see, and shall not perceive.€€ƒyFor the heart of this people is grown gross, and with their ears they have been dull of hearing, and their eyes they have shut: lest at any time they should see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their heart, and be converted, and I should heal them.€€ƒxNBut blessed are your eyes, because they see, and your ears, because they hear.€€ƒx¶For, amen, I say to you, many prophets and just men have desired to see the things that you see, and have not seen them: and to hear the things that you hear and have not heard them.€€ƒx,Hear you therefore the parable of the sower.€€ƒxËWhen any one heareth the word of the kingdom, and understandeth it not, there cometh the wicked one, and catcheth away that which was sown in his heart: this is he that received the seed by the way side.€€ƒxtAnd he that received the seed upon stony ground, is he that heareth the word, and immediately receiveth it with joy.€€ƒxYet hath he not root in himself, but is only for a time: and when there ariseth tribulation and persecution because of the word, he is presently scandalized.€€ƒx³And he that received the seed among thorns, is he that heareth the word, and the care of this world and the deceitfulness of riches choketh up the word, and he becometh fruitless.€€ƒx¾But he that received the seed upon good ground, is he that heareth the word, and understandeth, and beareth fruit, and yieldeth the one an hundredfold, and another sixty, and another thirty.€€ƒxyAnother parable he proposed to them, saying: The kingdom of heaven is likened to a man that sowed good seed in his field.€€ƒx`But while men were asleep, his enemy came and oversowed cockle among the wheat and went his way.€€ƒx]And when the blade was sprung up, and had brought forth fruit, then appeared also the cockle.€€ƒxAnd the servants of the good man of the house coming said to him. Sir, didst thou not sow good seed in thy field? Whence then hath it cockle?€€ƒxrAnd he said to them: An enemy hath done this. And the servants said to him: Wilt thou that we go and gather it up?€€ƒxcAnd he said: No, lest perhaps gathering up the cockle, you root up the wheat also together with it.€€ƒxÄSuffer both to grow until the harvest, and in the time of the harvest I will say to the reapers: Gather up first the cockle, and bind it into bundles to burn, but the wheat gather ye into my barn.€€ƒx‘Another parable he proposed unto them, saying: The kingdom of heaven is like to a grain of mustard seed, which a man took and sowed in his field.€€ƒx¹Which is the least indeed of all seeds; but when it is grown up, it is greater than all herbs, and becometh a tree, so that the birds of the air come, and dwell in the branches thereof.€€ƒxAnother parable he spoke to them: The kingdom of heaven is like to leaven, which a woman took and hid in three measures of meal, until the whole was leavened.€€ƒxjAll these things Jesus spoke in parables to the multitudes: and without parables he did not speak to them.€€ƒx¢That it might be fulfilled which was spoken by the prophet, saying: I will open my mouth in parables, I will utter things hidden from the foundation of the world.€€ƒxšThen having sent away the multitudes, he came into the house, and his disciples came to him, saying: Expound to us the parable of the cockle of the field.€€ƒxQWho made answer and said to them: He that soweth the good seed is the Son of man.€€ƒxAnd the field is the world. And the good seed are the children of the kingdom. And the cockle are the children of the wicked one.€€ƒxuAnd the enemy that sowed them, is the devil. But the harvest is the end of the world. And the reapers are the angels.€€ƒxeEven as cockle therefore is gathered up, and burnt with fire: so shall it be at the end of the world.€€ƒxyThe Son of man shall send his angels, and they shall gather out of his kingdom all scandals, and them that work iniquity.€€ƒx[And shall cast them into the furnace of fire: there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.€€ƒxnThen shall the just shine as the sun, in the kingdom of their Father. He that hath ears to hear, let him hear.€€ƒx²The kingdom of heaven is like unto a treasure hidden in a field. Which a man having found, hid it, and for joy thereof goeth, and selleth all that he hath, and buyeth that field.€€ƒxFAgain the kingdom of heaven is like to a merchant seeking good pearls.€€ƒxfWho when he had found one pearl of great price, went his way, and sold all that he had, and bought it.€€ƒxnAgain the kingdom of heaven is like to a net cast into the sea, and gathering together of all kinds of fishes.€€ƒx†Which, when it was filled, they drew out, and sitting by the shore, they chose out the good into vessels, but the bad they cast forth.€€ƒxsSo shall it be at the end of the world. The angels shall go out, and shall separate the wicked from among the just.€€ƒx[And shall cast them into the furnace of fire: there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.€€ƒx:Have ye understood all these things? They say to him: Yes.€€ƒx±He said unto them: Therefore every scribe instructed in the kingdom of heaven, is like to a man that is a householder, who bringeth forth out of his treasure new things and old.€€ƒxSAnd it came to pass: when Jesus had finished these parables, he passed from thence.€€ƒx“And coming into his own country, he taught them in their synagogues, so that they wondered and said: How came this man by this wisdom and miracles?€€ƒxxIs not this the carpenter's son? Is not his mother called Mary, and his brethren James, and Joseph, and Simon, and Jude:€€ƒxUAnd his sisters, are they not all with us? Whence therefore hath he all these things?€€ƒxAnd they were scandalized in his regard. But Jesus said to them: A prophet is not without honour, save in his own country, and in his own house.€€ƒxBAnd he wrought not many miracles there, because of their unbelief.€€˜$ƒx8At that time Herod the Tetrarch heard the fame of Jesus.€€ƒxŠAnd he said to his servants: This is John the Baptist: he is risen from the dead, and therefore mighty works shew forth themselves in him.€€ƒxoFor Herod had apprehended John and bound him, and put him into prison, because of Herodias, his brother's wife.€€ƒx<For John said to him: It is not lawful for thee to have her.€€ƒxdAnd having a mind to put him to death, he feared the people: because they esteemed him as a prophet.€€ƒxXBut on Herod's birthday, the daughter of Herodias danced before them: and pleased Herod.€€ƒxPWhereupon he promised with an oath, to give her whatsoever she would ask of him.€€ƒxiBut she being instructed before by her mother, said: Give me here in a dish the head of John the Baptist.€€ƒx{And the king was struck sad: yet because of his oath, and for them that sat with him at table, he commanded it to be given.€€ƒx-And he sent, and beheaded John in the prison.€€ƒxeAnd his head was brought in a dish: and it was given to the damsel, and she brought it to her mother.€€ƒxQAnd his disciples came and took the body, and buried it, and came and told Jesus.€€ƒx§Which when Jesus had heard, he retired from thence by a boat, into a desert place apart, and the multitudes having heard of it, followed him on foot out of the cities.€€ƒx]And he coming forth saw a great multitude, and had compassion on them, and healed their sick.€€ƒxÆAnd when it was evening, his disciples came to him, saying: This is a desert place, and the hour is now passed: send away the multitudes, that going into the towns, they may buy themselves victuals.€€ƒxFBut Jesus said to them, They have no need to go: give you them to eat.€€ƒxEThey answered him: We have not here, but five loaves, and two fishes.€€ƒx*Who said to them: Bring them hither to me.€€ƒxëAnd when he had commanded the multitude to sit down upon the grass, he took the five loaves and the two fishes, and looking up to heaven, he blessed, and brake, and gave the loaves to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitudes.€€ƒxhAnd they did all eat, and were filled. And they took up what remained, twelve full baskets of fragments.€€ƒxWAnd the number of them that did eat, was five thousand men, besides women and children.€€ƒx„And forthwith Jesus obliged his disciples to go up into the boat, and to go before him over the water, till he dismissed the people.€€ƒxwAnd having dismissed the multitude, he went into a mountain alone to pray. And when it was evening, he was there alone.€€ƒxZBut the boat in the midst of the sea was tossed with the waves: for the wind was contrary.€€ƒxKAnd in the fourth watch of the night, he came to them walking upon the sea.€€ƒxrAnd they seeing him walking upon the sea, were troubled, saying: It is an apparition. And they cried out for fear.€€ƒxTAnd immediately Jesus spoke to them, saying: Be of good heart: it is I, fear ye not.€€ƒxXAnd Peter making answer, said: Lord, if it be thou, bid me come to thee upon the waters.€€ƒx_And he said: Come. And Peter going down out of the boat walked upon the water to come to Jesus.€€ƒxjBut seeing the wind strong, he was afraid: and when he began to sink, he cried out, saying: Lord, save me.€€ƒx€And immediately Jesus stretching forth his hand took hold of him, and said to him: O thou of little faith, why didst thou doubt?€€ƒx:And when they were come up into the boat, the wind ceased.€€ƒx[And they that were in the boat came and adored him, saying: Indeed thou art the Son of God.€€ƒxCAnd having passed the water, they came into the country of Genesar.€€ƒx€And when the men of that place had knowledge of him, they sent into all that country, and brought to him all that were diseased.€€ƒxpAnd they besought him that they might touch but the hem of his garment. And as many as touched, were made whole.€€˜'ƒx>Then came to him from Jerusalem scribes and Pharisees, saying:€€ƒxqWhy do thy disciples transgress the tradition of the ancients? For they wash not their hands when they eat bread.€€ƒxsBut he answering, said to them: Why do you also transgress the commandment of God for your tradition? For God said:€€ƒx_Honour thy father and mother: And: He that shall curse father or mother, let him die the death.€€ƒxpBut you say: Whosoever shall say to father or mother, The gift whatsoever proceedeth from me, shall profit thee.€€ƒxsAnd he shall not honour his father or his mother: and you have made void the commandment of God for your tradition.€€ƒx7Hypocrites, well hath Isaias prophesied of you, saying:€€ƒxIThis people honoureth me with their lips: but their heart is far from me.€€ƒxKAnd in vain do they worship me, teaching doctrines and commandments of men.€€ƒx\And having called together the multitudes unto him, he said to them: Hear ye and understand.€€ƒxjNot that which goeth into the mouth defileth a man: but what cometh out of the mouth, this defileth a man.€€ƒxyThen came his disciples, and said to him: Dost thou know that the Pharisees, when they heard this word, were scandalized?€€ƒxbBut he answering, said: Every plant which my heavenly Father hath not planted, shall be rooted up.€€ƒxrLet them alone: they are blind, and leaders of the blind. And if the blind lead the blind, both fall into the pit.€€ƒx=And Peter answering, said to him: Expound to us this parable.€€ƒx4But he said: Are you also yet without understanding?€€ƒxuDo you not understand, that whatsoever entereth into the mouth, goeth into the belly, and is cast out into the privy?€€ƒxhBut the things which proceed out of the mouth, come forth from the heart, and those things defile a man.€€ƒxwFor from the heart come forth evil thoughts, murders, adulteries, fornications, thefts, false testimonies, blasphemies.€€ƒx]These are the things that defile a man. But to eat with unwashed hands doth not defile a man.€€ƒxIAnd Jesus went from thence, and retired into the coast of Tyre and Sidon.€€ƒx¯And behold a woman of Canaan who came out of those coasts, crying out, said to him: Have mercy on me, O Lord, thou son of David: my daughter is grievously troubled by a devil.€€ƒxuWho answered her not a word. And his disciples came and besought him, saying: Send her away, for she crieth after us:€€ƒx^And he answering, said: I was not sent but to the sheep, that are lost of the house of Israel.€€ƒx3But she came and adored him, saying: Lord, help me.€€ƒxbWho answering, said: It is not good to take the bread of the children, and to cast it to the dogs.€€ƒxiBut she said: Yea, Lord; for the whelps also eat of the crumbs that fall from the table of their masters.€€ƒx‹Then Jesus answering, said to her: O woman, great is thy faith: be it done to thee as thou wilt: and her daughter was cured from that hour.€€ƒxxAnd when Jesus had passed away from thence, he came nigh the sea of Galilee: and going up into a mountain, he sat there.€€ƒx­And there came to him great multitudes, having with them the dumb, the blind, the lame, the maimed, and many others: and they cast them down at his feet, and he healed them:€€ƒx{So that the multitudes marvelled seeing the dumb speak, the lame walk, the blind see: and they glorified the God of Israel.€€ƒxâAnd Jesus called together his disciples, and said: I have compassion on the multitudes, because they continue with me now three days, and have not what to eat, and I will not send them away fasting, lest they faint in the way.€€ƒxyAnd the disciples say unto him: Whence then should we have so many loaves in the desert, as to fill so great a multitude?€€ƒx`And Jesus said to them: How many loaves have you? But they said: Seven, and a few little fishes.€€ƒx;And he commanded the multitude to sit down upon the ground.€€ƒx‰And taking the seven loaves and the fishes, and giving thanks, he brake, and gave to his disciples, and the disciples gave to the people.€€ƒxqAnd they did all eat, and had their fill. And they took up seven baskets full, of what remained of the fragments.€€ƒxIAnd they that did eat, were four thousand men, beside children and women.€€ƒx`And having dismissed the multitude, he went up into a boat, and came into the coasts of Magedan.€€˜ƒxoAnd there came to him the Pharisees and Sadducees tempting: and they asked him to shew them a sign from heaven.€€ƒxkBut he answered and said to them: When it is evening, you say, It will be fair weather, for the sky is red.€€ƒx±And in the morning: To day there will be a storm, for the sky is red and lowering. You know then how to discern the face of the sky: and can you not know the signs of the times?€€ƒxA wicked and adulterous generation seeketh after a sign: and a sign shall not be given it, but the sign of Jonas the prophet. And he left them, and went away.€€ƒxRAnd when his disciples were come over the water, they had forgotten to take bread.€€ƒxTWho said to them: Take heed and beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.€€ƒxKBut they thought within themselves, saying: Because we have taken no bread.€€ƒxqAnd Jesus knowing it, said: Why do you think within yourselves, O ye of little faith, for that you have no bread?€€ƒx}Do you not yet understand, neither do you remember the five loaves among five thousand men, and how many baskets you took up?€€ƒxPNor the seven loaves, among four thousand men, and how many baskets you took up?€€ƒx~Why do you not understand that it was not concerning bread I said to you: Beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees?€€ƒx‰Then they understood that he said not that they should beware of the leaven of bread, but of the doctrine of the Pharisees and Sadducees.€€ƒxAnd Jesus came into the quarters of Cesarea Philippi: and he asked his disciples, saying: Whom do men say that the Son of man is?€€ƒxhBut they said: Some John the Baptist, and other some Elias, and others Jeremias, or one of the prophets.€€ƒx3Jesus saith to them: But whom do you say that I am?€€ƒxJSimon Peter answered and said: Thou art Christ, the Son of the living God.€€ƒx˜And Jesus answering said to him: Blessed art thou, Simon Bar-Jona: because flesh and blood hath not revealed it to thee, but my Father who is in heaven.€€ƒx†And I say to thee: That thou art Peter; and upon this rock I will build my church, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.€€ƒxÔAnd I will give to thee the keys of the kingdom of heaven. And whatsoever thou shalt bind upon earth, it shall be bound also in heaven: and whatsoever thou shalt loose on earth, it shall be loosed also in heaven.€€ƒx[Then he commanded his disciples, that they should tell no one that he was Jesus the Christ.€€ƒxÍFrom that time Jesus began to shew to his disciples, that he must go to Jerusalem, and suffer many things from the ancients and scribes and chief priests, and be put to death, and the third day rise again.€€ƒxjAnd Peter taking him, began to rebuke him, saying: Lord, be it far from thee, this shall not be unto thee.€€ƒx£Who turning, said to Peter: Go behind me, Satan, thou art a scandal unto me: because thou savourest not the things that are of God, but the things that are of men.€€ƒx|Then Jesus said to his disciples: If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.€€ƒxjFor he that will save his life, shall lose it: and he that shall lose his life for my sake, shall find it.€€ƒxFor what doth it profit a man, if he gain the whole world and suffer the loss of his own soul? Or what exchange shall a man give for his soul?€€ƒx†For the Son of man shall come in the glory of his Father with his angels: and then will he render to every man according to his works.€€ƒxŠAmen I say to you, there are some of them that stand here, that shall not taste death, till they see the Son of man coming in his kingdom.€€˜ƒx€And after six days Jesus taketh unto him Peter and James, and John his brother, and bringeth them up into a high mountain apart:€€ƒxnAnd he was transfigured before them. And his face did shine as the sun: and his garments became white as snow.€€ƒxCAnd behold there appeared to them Moses and Elias talking with him.€€ƒx­And Peter answering, said to Jesus: Lord, it is good for us to be here: if thou wilt, let us make here three tabernacles, one for thee, and one for Moses, and one for Elias.€€ƒx­And as he was yet speaking, behold a bright cloud overshadowed them. And lo a voice out of the cloud, saying: This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased: hear ye him.€€ƒxJAnd the disciples hearing fell upon their face, and were very much afraid.€€ƒxGAnd Jesus came and touched them: and said to them: Arise, and fear not.€€ƒx;And they lifting up their eyes, saw no one, but only Jesus.€€ƒx‹And as they came down from the mountain, Jesus charged them, saying: Tell the vision to no man, till the Son of man be risen from the dead.€€ƒx\And his disciples asked him, saying: Why then do the scribes say that Elias must come first?€€ƒxPBut he answering, said to them: Elias indeed shall come, and restore all things.€€ƒx¦But I say to you, that Elias is already come, and they knew him not, But have done unto him whatsoever they had a mind. So also the Son of man shall suffer from them.€€ƒxNThen the disciples understood, that he had spoken to them of John the Baptist.€€ƒxíAnd when he was come to the multitude, there came to him a man falling down on his knees before him saying: Lord, have pity on my son, for he is a lunatic, and suffereth much: for he falleth often into the fire, and often into the water.€€ƒx@And I brought him to thy disciples, and they could not cure him.€€ƒx—Then Jesus answered and said: O unbelieving and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you? How long shall I suffer you? Bring him hither to me.€€ƒx]And Jesus rebuked him, and the devil went out of him, and the child was cured from that hour.€€ƒxSThen came the disciples to Jesus secretly, and said: Why could not we cast him out?€€ƒxêJesus said to them: Because of your unbelief. For, amen I say to you, if you have faith as a grain of mustard seed, you shall say to this mountain: Remove from hence hither, and it shall remove: and nothing shall be impossible to you.€€ƒx8But this kind is not cast out but by prayer and fasting.€€ƒxtAnd when they abode together in Galilee, Jesus said to them: The Son of man shall be betrayed into the hands of men:€€ƒxcAnd they shall kill him, and the third day he shall rise again. And they were troubled exceedingly.€€ƒx‘And when they were come to Capharnaum, they that received the didrachmas, came to Peter, and said to him: Doth not your master pay the didrachma?€€ƒxÖHe said: Yes. And when he was come into the house, Jesus prevented him, saying: What is thy opinion, Simon? The kings of the earth, of whom do they receive tribute or custom, of their own children, or of strangers?€€ƒxIAnd he said: Of strangers. Jesus said to him: Then the children are free.€€ƒxæBut that we may not scandalize them, go to the sea, and cast in a hook: and that fish which shall first come up, take: and when thou hast opened it's mouth, thou shalt find a stater: take that, and give it to them for me and thee.€€ƒ`€€˜#ƒxIAnd Jesus, calling unto him a little child, set him in the midst of them.€€ƒx„And said: amen I say to you, unless you be converted, and become as little children, you shall not enter into the kingdom of heaven.€€ƒxjWhosoever therefore shall humble himself as this little child, he is the greater in the kingdom of heaven.€€ƒxIAnd he that shall receive one such little child in my name, receiveth me.€€ƒxÉBut he that shall scandalize one of these little ones that believe in me, it were better for him that a millstone should be hanged about his neck, and that he should be drowned in the depth of the sea.€€ƒx‹Woe to the world because of scandals. For it must needs be that scandals come: but nevertheless woe to that man by whom the scandal cometh.€€ƒxÌAnd if thy hand, or thy foot, scandalize thee, cut it off, and cast it from thee. It is better for thee to go into life maimed or lame, than having two hands or two feet, to be cast into everlasting fire.€€ƒx­And if thy eye scandalize thee, pluck it out, and cast it from thee. It is better for thee having one eye to enter into life, than having two eyes to be cast into hell fire.€€ƒx“See that you despise not one of these little ones: for I say to you, that their angels in heaven always see the face of my Father who is in heaven.€€ƒx7For the Son of man is come to save that which was lost.€€ƒx²What think you? If a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them should go astray: doth he not leave the ninety-nine in the mountains, and goeth to seek that which is gone astray?€€ƒx~And if it so be that he find it: Amen I say to you, he rejoiceth more for that, than for the ninety-nine that went not astray.€€ƒxiEven so it is not the will of your Father, who is in heaven, that one of these little ones should perish.€€ƒxBut if thy brother shall offend against thee, go, and rebuke him between thee and him alone. If he shall hear thee, thou shalt gain thy brother.€€ƒxAnd if he will not hear thee, take with thee one or two more: that in the mouth of two or three witnesses every word may stand.€€ƒx‚And if he will not hear them: tell the church. And if he will not hear the church, let him be to thee as the heathen and publican.€€ƒx¢Amen I say to you, whatsoever you shall bind upon earth, shall be bound also in heaven: and whatsoever you shall loose upon earth, shall be loosed also in heaven.€€ƒx§Again I say to you, that if two of you shall consent upon earth, concerning anything whatsoever they shall ask, it shall be done to them by my Father who is in heaven.€€ƒx_For where there are two or three gathered together in my name, there am I in the midst of them.€€ƒx{Then came Peter unto him and said: Lord, how often shall my brother offend against me, and I forgive him? till seven times?€€ƒx\Jesus saith to him: I say not to thee, till seven times; but till seventy times seven times.€€ƒx`Therefore is the kingdom of heaven likened to a king, who would take an account of his servants.€€ƒxeAnd when he had begun to take the account, one as brought to him, that owed him ten thousand talents.€€ƒx™And as he had not wherewith to pay it, his lord commanded that he should be sold, and his wife and children, and all that he had, and payment to be made.€€ƒxdBut that servant falling down, besought him, saying: Have patience with me, and I will pay thee all.€€ƒxXAnd the lord of that servant being moved with pity, let him go and forgave him the debt.€€ƒx®But when that servant was gone out, he found one of his fellow-servants that owed him an hundred pence: and laying hold of him, he throttled him, saying: Pay what thou owest.€€ƒxjAnd his fellow-servant falling down, besought him, saying: Have patience with me, and I will pay thee all.€€ƒxKAnd he would not: but went and cast him into prison, till he paid the debt.€€ƒx{Now his fellow servants seeing what was done, were very much grieved, and they came, and told their lord all that was done.€€ƒxyThen his lord called him: and said to him: Thou wicked servant, I forgave thee all the debt, because thou besoughtest me:€€ƒxhShouldst not thou then have had compassion also on thy fellow servant, even as I had compassion on thee?€€ƒxTAnd his lord being angry, delivered him to the torturers until he paid all the debt.€€ƒxfSo also shall my heavenly Father do to you, if you forgive not every one his brother from your hearts.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒx<And great multitudes followed him: and he healed them there.€€ƒxvAnd there came to him the Pharisees tempting him, saying: Is it lawful for a man to put away his wife for every cause?€€ƒxWho answering, said to them: Have ye not read, that he who made man from the beginning, made them male and female? And he said:€€ƒxuFor this cause shall a man leave father and mother, and shall cleave to his wife, and they two shall be in one flesh.€€ƒxoTherefore now they are not two, but one flesh. What therefore God hath joined together, let no man put asunder.€€ƒxWThey say to him: Why then did Moses command to give a bill of divorce, and to put away?€€ƒx“He saith to them: Because Moses by reason of the hardness of your heart permitted you to put away your wives: but from the beginning it was not so.€€ƒxÈAnd I say to you, that whosoever shall put away his wife, except it be for fornication, and shall marry another, committeth adultery: and he that shall marry her that is put away, committeth adultery.€€ƒxcHis disciples say unto him: If the case of a man with his wife be so, it is not expedient to marry.€€ƒxKWho said to them: All men take not this word, but they to whom it is given.€€ƒxæFor there are eunuchs, who were born so from their mothers womb: and there are eunuchs, who were made so by men: and there are eunuchs, who have made themselves eunuchs for the kingdom of heaven. He that can take, let him take it.€€ƒx{Then were little children presented to him, that he should impose hands upon them and pray. And the disciples rebuked them.€€ƒx}But Jesus said to them: Suffer the little children, and forbid them not to come to me: for the kingdom of heaven is for such.€€ƒxAAnd when he had imposed hands upon them, he departed from thence.€€ƒxhAnd behold one came and said to him: Good master, what good shall I do that I may have life everlasting?€€ƒxWho said to him: Why askest thou me concerning good? One is good, God. But if thou wilt enter into life, keep the commandments.€€ƒx˜He said to him: Which? And Jesus said: Thou shalt do no murder, Thou shalt not commit adultery, Thou shalt not steal, Thou shalt not bear false witness.€€ƒxOHonour thy father and thy mother: and, Thou shalt love thy neighbor as thyself.€€ƒx[The young man saith to him: All these have I kept from my youth, what is yet wanting to me?€€ƒx—Jesus saith to him: If thou wilt be perfect, go sell what thou hast, and give to the poor, and thou shalt have treasure in heaven: and come, follow me.€€ƒx[And when the young man had heard this word, he went away sad: for he had great possessions.€€ƒxtThen Jesus said to his disciples: Amen, I say to you, that a rich man shall hardly enter into the kingdom of heaven.€€ƒxAnd again I say to you: It is easier for a camel to pass through the eye of a needle, than for a rich man to enter into the kingdom of heaven.€€ƒxYAnd when they had heard this, the disciples wondered much, saying: Who then can be saved?€€ƒxeAnd Jesus beholding, said to them: With men this is impossible: but with God all things are possible.€€ƒxxThen Peter answering, said to him: Behold we have left all things, and have followed thee: what therefore shall we have?€€ƒxàAnd Jesus said to them: Amen I say to you, that you who have followed me, in the regeneration, when the Son of man shall sit on the seat of his majesty, you also shall sit on twelve seats judging the twelve tribes of Israel.€€ƒxÇAnd every one that hath left house, or brethren, or sisters, or father, or mother, or wife, or children, or lands for my name's sake, shall receive an hundredfold, and shall possess life everlasting.€€ƒxDAnd many that are first, shall be last: and the last shall be first.€€ƒ`€€˜"ƒxWAnd having agreed with the labourers for a penny a day, he sent them into his vineyard.€€ƒxSAnd going out about the third hour, he saw others standing in the marketplace idle.€€ƒxZAnd he said to them: Go you also into my vineyard, and I will give you what shall be just.€€ƒxjAnd they went their way. And again he went out about the sixth and the ninth hour, and did in like manner.€€ƒx}But about the eleventh hour he went out and found others standing, and he saith to them: Why stand you here all the day idle?€€ƒx]They say to him: Because no man hath hired us. He saith to them: Go ye also into my vineyard.€€ƒx And when evening was come, the lord of the vineyard saith to his steward: Call the labourers and pay them their hire, beginning from the last even to the first.€€ƒxaWhen therefore they were come that came about the eleventh hour, they received every man a penny.€€ƒxsBut when the first also came, they thought that they should receive more: And they also received every man a penny.€€ƒx?And receiving it they murmured against the master of the house,€€ƒx†Saying: These last have worked but one hour. and thou hast made them equal to us, that have borne the burden of the day and the heats.€€ƒxkBut he answering said to one of them: friend, I do thee no wrong: didst thou not agree with me for a penny?€€ƒxRTake what is thine, and go thy way: I will also give to this last even as to thee.€€ƒxROr, is it not lawful for me to do what I will? Is thy eye evil, because I am good?€€ƒxRSo shall the last be first and the first last. For many are called but few chosen.€€ƒxRAnd Jesus going up to Jerusalem, took the twelve disciples apart and said to them:€€ƒxBehold we go up to Jerusalem, and the Son of man shall be betrayed to the chief priests and the scribes: and they shall condemn him to death.€€ƒxuAnd shall deliver him to the Gentiles to be mocked and scourged and crucified: and the third day he shall rise again.€€ƒxfThen came to him the mother of the sons of Zebedee with her sons, adoring and asking something of him.€€ƒxœWho said to her: What wilt thou? She saith to him: say that these my two sons may sit, the one on thy right hand, and the other on thy left, in thy kingdom.€€ƒx|And Jesus answering, said: You know not what you ask. Can you drink the chalice that I shall drink? They say to him: We can.€€ƒx¦He saith to them: My chalice indeed you shall drink; but to sit on my right or left hand is not mine to give to you, but to them for whom it is prepared by my Father.€€ƒxNAnd the ten, hearing it, were moved with indignation against the two brethren.€€ƒx¡But Jesus called them to him and said: You know that the princes of the Gentiles lord it over them; and that they that are the greater, exercise power upon them.€€ƒx_It shall not be so among you: but whosoever is the greater among you, let him be your minister.€€ƒx:And he that will be first among you shall be your servant.€€ƒxuEven as the Son of man is not come to be ministered unto, but to minister and to give his life a redemption for many.€€ƒxDAnd when they went out from Jericho, a great multitude followed him.€€ƒx•And behold two blind men sitting by the way side heard that Jesus passed by. And they cried out, saying: O Lord, thou son of David, have mercy on us.€€ƒx“And the multitude rebuked them that they should hold their peace. But they cried out the more, saying: O Lord, thou son of David, have mercy on us.€€ƒxHAnd Jesus stood and called them and said: What will ye that I do to you?€€ƒx/They say to him: Lord, that our eyes be opened.€€ƒxcAnd Jesus having compassion on them, touched their eyes. And immediately they saw and followed him.€€ƒ`€€˜.ƒx¡Saying to them: Go ye into the village that is over against you: and immediately you shall find an ass tied and a colt with her. Loose them and bring them to me.€€ƒxtAnd if any man shall say anything to you, say ye that the Lord hath need of them. And forthwith he will let them go.€€ƒxYNow all this was done that it might be fulfilled which was spoken by the prophet, saying:€€ƒxTell ye the daughter of Sion: Behold thy king cometh to thee, meek and sitting upon an ass and a colt, the foal of her that is used to the yoke.€€ƒx5And the disciples going, did as Jesus commanded them.€€ƒxaAnd they brought the ass and the colt and laid their garments upon them and made him sit thereon.€€ƒx~And a very great multitude spread their garments in the way: and others cut boughs from the trees and strewed them in the way.€€ƒx¬And the multitudes that went before and that followed cried, saying: Hosanna to the son of David: Blessed is he that cometh in the name of the Lord: Hosanna in the highest.€€ƒxSAnd when he was come into Jerusalem, the whole city was moved, saying: Who is this?€€ƒxIAnd the people said: This is Jesus, the prophet from Nazareth of Galilee.€€ƒxµAnd Jesus went into the temple of God and cast out all them that sold and bought in the temple and overthrew the tables of the money changers and the chairs of them that sold doves.€€ƒxyAnd he saith to them: It is written, My house shall be called the house of prayer; but you have made it a den of thieves.€€ƒxOAnd there came to him the blind and the lame in the temple: and he healed them.€€ƒx¶And the chief priests and scribes, seeing the wonderful things that he did and the children crying in the temple and saying: Hosanna to the son of David, were moved with indignation,€€ƒx¨And said to him: Hearest thou what these say? And Jesus said to them: Yea, have you never read: Out of the mouth of infants and of sucklings thou hast perfected praise?€€ƒxJAnd leaving them, he went out of the city into Bethania and remained here.€€ƒx;And in the morning, returning into the city, he was hungry.€€ƒxÖAnd seeing a certain fig tree by the way side, he came to it and found nothing on it but leaves only. And he saith to it: May no fruit grow on thee henceforward for ever. And immediately the fig tree withered away.€€ƒxPAnd the disciples seeing it wondered, saying: How is it presently withered away?€€ƒxğAnd Jesus answering, said to them: Amen, I say to you, if you shall have faith and stagger not, not only this of the fig tree shall you do, but also if you shall say to this mountain, Take up and cast thyself into the sea, it shall be done.€€ƒxOAnd all things whatsoever you shall ask in prayer believing, you shall receive.€€ƒxÔAnd when he was come into the temple, there came to him, as he was teaching, the chief priests and ancients of the people, saying: By what authority dost thou these things? And who hath given thee this authority?€€ƒx’Jesus answering, said to them: I also will ask you one word, which if you shall tell me, I will also tell you by what authority I do these things.€€ƒxhThe baptism of John, whence was it? From heaven or from men? But they thought within themselves, saying:€€ƒx±If we shall say, from heaven, he will say to us: Why then did you not believe him? But if we shall say, from men, we are afraid of the multitude: for all held John as a prophet.€€ƒx}And answering Jesus, they said: We know not. He also said to them: Neither do I tell you by what authority I do these things.€€ƒxuBut what think you? A certain man had two sons: and coming to the first, he said: Son, go work to day in my vineyard.€€ƒxYAnd he answering, said: I will not. But afterwards, being moved with repentance, he went.€€ƒxcAnd coming to the other, he said in like manner. And he answering said: I go, Sir. And he went not.€€ƒxºWhich of the two did the father's will? They say to him: The first. Jesus saith to them: Amen I say to you that the publicans and the harlots shall go into the kingdom of God before you.€€ƒxÈFor John came to you in the way of justice: and you did not believe him. But the publicans and the harlots believed him: but you, seeing it, did not even afterwards repent, that you might believe him.€€ƒxÖHear ye another parable. There was a man, an householder, who planted a vineyard and made a hedge round about it and dug in it a press and built a tower and let it out to husbandmen and went into a strange country.€€ƒx}And when the time of the fruits drew nigh, he sent his servants to the husbandmen that they might receive the fruits thereof.€€ƒx`And the husbandmen laying hands on his servants, beat one and killed another and stoned another.€€ƒxXAgain he sent other servants, more than the former; and they did to them in like manner.€€ƒxLAnd last of all he sent to them his son, saying: They will reverence my son.€€ƒx…But the husbandmen seeing the son, said among themselves: This is the heir: come, let us kill him, and we shall have his inheritance.€€ƒxGAnd taking him, they cast him forth out of the vineyard and killed him.€€ƒxXWhen therefore the lord of the vineyard shall come, what will he do to those husbandmen?€€ƒx˜They say to him: He will bring those evil men to an evil end and let out his vineyard to other husbandmen that shall render him the fruit in due season.€€ƒxÎJesus saith to them: Have you never read in the Scriptures: The stone which the builders rejected, the same is become the head of the corner? By the Lord this has been done; and it is wonderful in our eyes.€€ƒx‚Therefore I say to you that the kingdom of God shall be taken from you and shall be given to a nation yielding the fruits thereof.€€ƒxvAnd whosoever shall fall on this stone shall be broken: but on whomsoever it shall fall, it shall grind him to powder.€€ƒxaAnd when the chief priests and Pharisees had heard his parables, they knew that he spoke of them.€€ƒx`And seeking to lay hands on him, they feared the multitudes, because they held him as a prophet.€€ƒ`€€˜.ƒxKThe kingdom of heaven is likened to a king who made a marriage for his son.€€ƒxaAnd he sent his servants to call them that were invited to the marriage: and they would not come.€€ƒx»Again he sent other servants, saying: Tell them that were invited, Behold, I have prepared my dinner: my beeves and fatlings are killed, and all things are ready. Come ye to the marriage.€€ƒxWBut they neglected and went their ways, one to his farm and another to his merchandise.€€ƒxcAnd the rest laid hands on his servants and, having treated them contumeliously, put them to death.€€ƒx{But when the king had heard of it, he was angry: and sending his armies, he destroyed those murderers and burnt their city.€€ƒxhThen he saith to his servants: The marriage indeed is ready; but they that were invited were not worthy.€€ƒxWGo ye therefore into the highways; and as many as you shall find, call to the marriage.€€ƒxAnd his servants going forth into the ways, gathered together all that they found, both bad and good: and the marriage was filled with guests.€€ƒx`And the king went in to see the guests: and he saw there a man who had not on a wedding garment.€€ƒxjAnd he saith to him: Friend, how camest thou in hither not having on a wedding garment? But he was silent.€€ƒx’Then the king said to the waiters: Bind his hands and feet, and cast him into the exterior darkness. There shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.€€ƒx(For many are called, but few are chosen.€€ƒxVThen the Pharisees going, consulted among themselves how to insnare him in his speech.€€ƒxàAnd they sent to him their disciples with the Herodians, saying: Master, we know that thou art a true speaker and teachest the way of God in truth. Neither carest thou for any man: for thou dost not regard the person of men.€€ƒxWTell us therefore what dost thou think? Is it lawful to give tribute to Caesar, or not?€€ƒxMBut Jesus knowing their wickedness, said: Why do you tempt me, ye hypocrites?€€ƒx>Shew me the coin of the tribute. And they offered him a penny.€€ƒx=And Jesus saith to them: Whose image and inscription is this?€€ƒx‘They say to him: Caesar's. Then he saith to them: Render therefore to Caesar the things that are Caesar's; and to God, the things that are God's.€€ƒxBAnd hearing this, they wondered and, leaving him, went their ways.€€ƒxZThat day there came to him the Sadducees, who say there is no resurrection; and asked him,€€ƒx{Saying: Master, Moses said: If a man die having no son, his brother shall marry his wife and raise up issue to his brother.€€ƒx…Now there were with us seven brethren: and the first having married a wife, died; and not having issue, left his wife to his brother.€€ƒxBIn like manner the second and the third and so on, to the seventh.€€ƒx$And last of all the woman died also.€€ƒxZAt the resurrection therefore, whose wife of the seven shall she be? For they all had her.€€ƒx\And Jesus answering, said to them: You err, not knowing the Scriptures nor the power of God.€€ƒxmFor in the resurrection they shall neither marry nor be married, but shall be as the angels of God in heaven.€€ƒxkAnd concerning the resurrection of the dead, have you not read that which was spoken by God, saying to you:€€ƒxsI am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob? He is not the God of the dead but of the living.€€ƒxAAnd the multitudes hearing it were in admiration at his doctrine.€€ƒxMBut the Pharisees, hearing that he had silenced the Sadducees, came together.€€ƒx>And one of them, a doctor of the law, asked him, tempting him:€€ƒx2Master, which is the great commandment in the law?€€ƒxyJesus said to him: Thou shalt love the Lord thy God with thy whole heart and with thy whole soul and with thy whole mind.€€ƒx/This is the greatest and the first commandment.€€ƒxIAnd the second is like to this: Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself.€€ƒxCOn these two commandments dependeth the whole law and the prophets.€€ƒx<And the Pharisees being gathered together, Jesus asked them,€€ƒxLSaying: What think you of Christ? Whose son is he? They say to him: David's.€€ƒxFHe saith to them: How then doth David in spirit call him Lord, saying:€€ƒxWThe Lord said to my Lord: Sit on my right hand, until I make thy enemies thy footstool?€€ƒx/If David then call him Lord, how is he his son?€€ƒxoAnd no man was able to answer him a word: neither durst any man from that day forth ask him any more questions.€€ƒ`€€˜'ƒxHSaying: The scribes and the Pharisees have sitten on the chair of Moses.€€ƒxˆAll things therefore whatsoever they shall say to you, observe and do: but according to their works do ye not. For they say, and do not.€€ƒx†For they bind heavy and insupportable burdens and lay them on men's shoulders: but with a finger of their own they will not move them.€€ƒxtAnd all their works they do for to be seen of men. For they make their phylacteries broad and enlarge their fringes.€€ƒxPAnd they love the first places at feasts and the first chairs in the synagogues,€€ƒxDAnd salutations in the market place, and to be called by men, Rabbi.€€ƒxNBut be not you called Rabbi. For one is your master: and all you are brethren.€€ƒxOAnd call none your father upon earth; for one is your father, who is in heaven.€€ƒx=Neither be ye called masters: for one is your master, Christ.€€ƒx8He that is the greatest among you shall be your servant.€€ƒxfAnd whosoever shall exalt himself shall be humbled: and he that shall humble himself shall be exalted.€€ƒx¿But woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you shut the kingdom of heaven against men: for you yourselves do not enter in and those that are going in, you suffer not to enter.€€ƒxWoe to you scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you devour the houses of widows, praying long prayers. For this you shall receive the greater judgment.€€ƒxÇWoe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, because you go round about the sea and the land to make one proselyte. And when he is made, you make him the child of hell twofold more than yourselves.€€ƒx–Woe to you, blind guides, that say, Whosoever shall swear by the temple, it is nothing; but he that shall swear by the gold of the temple is a debtor.€€ƒx_Ye foolish and blind: for whether is greater, the gold or the temple that sanctifieth the gold?€€ƒxyAnd whosoever shall swear by the altar, it is nothing; but whosoever shall swear by the gift that is upon it is a debtor.€€ƒx^Ye foolish and blind: for whether is greater, the gift or the altar that sanctifieth the gift?€€ƒxZHe therefore that sweareth by the altar sweareth by it and by all things that are upon it.€€ƒxVAnd whosoever shall swear by the temple sweareth by it and by him that dwelleth in it.€€ƒx]And he that sweareth by heaven sweareth by the throne of God and by him that sitteth thereon.€€ƒxêWoe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites; because you tithe mint and anise and cummin and have left the weightier things of the law: judgment and mercy and faith. These things you ought to have done and not to leave those undone.€€ƒx8Blind guides, who strain out a gnat and swallow a camel.€€ƒx Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites; because you make clean the outside of the cup and of the dish, but within you are full of rapine and uncleanness.€€ƒxoThou blind Pharisee, first make clean the inside of the cup and of the dish, that the outside may become clean.€€ƒxÀWoe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites; because you are like to whited sepulchres, which outwardly appear to men beautiful but within are full of dead men's bones and of all filthiness.€€ƒxeSo you also outwardly indeed appear to men just: but inwardly you are full of hypocrisy and iniquity.€€ƒx}Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, that build the sepulchres of the prophets and adorn the monuments of the just,€€ƒx|And say: If we had been in the days of our fathers, we would not have been partakers with them in the blood of the prophets.€€ƒxgWherefore you are witnesses against yourselves, that you are the sons of them that killed the prophets.€€ƒx,Fill ye up then the measure of your fathers.€€ƒxPYou serpents, generation of vipers, how will you flee from the judgment of hell?€€ƒxÃTherefore behold I send to you prophets and wise men and scribes: and some of them you will put to death and crucify: and some you will scourge in your synagogues and persecute from city to city.€€ƒx×That upon you may come all the just blood that hath been shed upon the earth, from the blood of Abel the just, even unto the blood of Zacharias the son of Barachias, whom you killed between the temple and the altar.€€ƒxDAmen I say to you, all these things shall come upon this generation.€€ƒxàJerusalem, Jerusalem, thou that killest the prophets and stonest them that are sent unto thee, how often would I have gathered together thy children, as the hen doth gather her chickens under her wings, and thou wouldst not?€€ƒx2Behold, your house shall be left to you, desolate.€€ƒxrFor I say to you, you shall not see me henceforth till you say: Blessed is he that cometh in the name of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€˜3ƒxAnd he answering, said to them: Do you see all these things? Amen I say to you, there shall not be left here a stone upon a stone that shall not be destroyed.€€ƒxÈAnd when he was sitting on mount Olivet, the disciples came to him privately, saying: Tell us when shall these things be? And what shall be the sign of thy coming and of the consummation of the world?€€ƒxDAnd Jesus answering, said to them: Take heed that no man seduce you.€€ƒxMFor many will come in my name saying, I am Christ. And they will seduce many.€€ƒxˆAnd you shall hear of wars and rumours of wars. See that ye be not troubled. For these things must come to pass: but the end is not yet.€€ƒxˆFor nation shall rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom: And there shall be pestilences and famines and earthquakes in places.€€ƒx,Now all these are the beginnings of sorrows.€€ƒx„Then shall they deliver you up to be afflicted and shall put you to death: and you shall be hated by all nations for my name's sake.€€ƒx[And then shall many be scandalized and shall betray one another and shall hate one another.€€ƒx9And many false prophets shall rise and shall seduce many.€€ƒxHAnd because iniquity hath abounded, the charity of many shall grow cold.€€ƒx:But he that shall persevere to the end, he shall be saved.€€ƒxŠAnd this gospel of the kingdom shall be preached in the whole world, for a testimony to all nations: and then shall the consummation come.€€ƒx¦When therefore you shall see the abomination of desolation, which was spoken of by Daniel the prophet, standing in the holy place: he that readeth let him understand.€€ƒx<Then they that are in Judea, let them flee to the mountains:€€ƒxYAnd he that is on the housetop, let him not come down to take any thing out of his house:€€ƒxBAnd he that is in the field, let him not go back to take his coat.€€ƒxEAnd woe to them that are with child and that give suck in those days.€€ƒxABut pray that your flight be not in the winter or on the sabbath.€€ƒx}For there shall be then great tribulation, such as hath not been from the beginning of the world until now, neither shall be.€€ƒx€And unless those days had been shortened, no flesh should be saved: but for the sake of the elect those days shall be shortened.€€ƒxRThen if any man shall say to you, Lo here is Christ, or there: do not believe him.€€ƒx“For there shall arise false Christs and false prophets and shall shew great signs and wonders, insomuch as to deceive (if possible) even the elect.€€ƒx)Behold I have told it to you, beforehand.€€ƒx{If therefore they shall say to you, Behold he is in the desert: go ye not out. Behold he is in the closets: believe it not.€€ƒxyFor as lightning cometh out of the east and appeareth even into the west: so shall also the cowling of the Son of man be.€€ƒxPWheresoever the body shall be, there shall the eagles also be gathered together.€€ƒxÂAnd immediately after the tribulation of those days, the sun shall be darkened and the moon shall not give her light and the stars shall fall from heaven and the powers of heaven shall be moved.€€ƒxÇAnd then shall appear the sign of the Son of man in heaven. And then shall all tribes of the earth mourn: and they shall see the Son of man coming in the clouds of heaven with much power and majesty.€€ƒxÁAnd he shall send his angels with a trumpet and a great voice: and they shall gather together his elect from the four winds, from the farthest parts of the heavens to the utmost bounds of them.€€ƒx…And from the fig tree learn a parable: When the branch thereof is now tender and the leaves come forth, you know that summer is nigh.€€ƒx]So you also, when you shall see all these things, know ye that it is nigh, even at the doors.€€ƒxTAmen I say to you that this generation shall not pass till all these things be done.€€ƒx9Heaven and earth shall pass: but my words shall not pass.€€ƒx\But of that day and hour no one knoweth: no, not the angels of heaven, but the Father alone.€€ƒxIAnd as in the days of Noe, so shall also the coming of the Son of man be.€€ƒxšFor, as in the days before the flood they were eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, even till that day in which Noe entered into the ark:€€ƒxlAnd they knew not till the flood came and took them all away: so also shall the coming of the Son of man be.€€ƒxIThen two shall be in the field. One shall be taken and one shall be left.€€ƒxRTwo women shall be grinding at the mill. One shall be taken and one shall be left.€€ƒxGWatch ye therefore, because you know not what hour your Lord will come.€€ƒx¦But this know ye, that, if the goodman of the house knew at what hour the thief would come, he would certainly watch and would not suffer his house to be broken open.€€ƒxXWherefore be you also ready, because at what hour you know not the Son of man will come.€€ƒx~Who, thinkest thou, is a faithful and wise servant, whom his lord hath appointed over his family, to give them meat in season?€€ƒxNBlessed is that servant, whom when his lord shall come he shall find so doing.€€ƒx9Amen I say to you: he shall place him over all his goods.€€ƒxJBut if that evil servant shall say in his heart: My lord is long a coming:€€ƒxUAnd shall begin to strike his fellow servants and shall eat and drink with drunkards:€€ƒxcThe lord of that servant shall come in a day that he hopeth not and at an hour that he knoweth not:€€ƒxqAnd shall separate him and appoint his portion with the hypocrites. There shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.€€ƒ`€€˜.ƒx,And five of them were foolish and five wise.€€ƒxKBut the five foolish, having taken their lamps, did not take oil with them.€€ƒx6But the wise took oil in their vessels with the lamps.€€ƒx:And the bridegroom tarrying, they all slumbered and slept.€€ƒx\And at midnight there was a cry made: Behold the bridegroom cometh. Go ye forth to meet him.€€ƒx5Then all those virgins arose and trimmed their lamps.€€ƒxRAnd the foolish said to the wise: Give us of your oil, for our lamps are gone out.€€ƒx†The wise answered, saying: Lest perhaps there be not enough for us and for you, go ye rather to them that sell and buy for yourselves.€€ƒx‚Now whilst they went to buy the bridegroom came: and they that were ready went in with him to the marriage. And the door was shut.€€ƒxHBut at last came also the other virgins, saying: Lord, Lord, open to us.€€ƒx9But he answering said: Amen I say to you, I know you not.€€ƒx>Watch ye therefore, because you know not the day nor the hour.€€ƒx_For even as a man going into a far country called his servants and delivered to them his goods;€€ƒx›And to one he gave five talents, and to another two, and to another one, to every one according to his proper ability: and immediately he took his journey.€€ƒxfAnd he that had received the five talents went his way and traded with the same and gained other five.€€ƒxAAnd in like manner he that had received the two gained other two.€€ƒx`But he that had received the one, going his way, digged into the earth and hid his lord's money.€€ƒxMBut after a long time the lord of those servants came and reckoned with them.€€ƒx²And he that had received the five talents coming, brought other five talents, saying: Lord, thou didst deliver to me five talents. Behold I have gained other five over and above.€€ƒx·His lord said to him: Well done, good and faithful servant, because thou hast been faithful over a few things, I will place thee over many things. Enter thou into the joy of thy lord.€€ƒx†And he also that had received the two talents came and said: Lord, thou deliveredst two talents to me. Behold I have gained other two.€€ƒx·His lord said to him: Well done, good and faithful servant: because thou hast been faithful over a few things, I will place thee over many things. Enter thou into the joy of thy lord.€€ƒx¯But he that had received the one talent, came and said: Lord, I know that thou art a hard man; thou reapest where thou hast not sown and gatherest where thou hast not strewed.€€ƒxdAnd being afraid, I went and hid thy talent in the earth. Behold here thou hast that which is thine.€€ƒxAnd his lord answering, said to him: Wicked and slothful servant, thou knewest that I reap where I sow not and gather where I have not strewed.€€ƒx}Thou oughtest therefore to have committed my money to the bankers: and at my coming I should have received my own with usury.€€ƒxQTake ye away therefore the talent from him and give it him that hath ten talents.€€ƒx“For to every one that hath shall be given, and he shall abound: but from him that hath not, that also which he seemeth to have shall be taken away.€€ƒxsAnd the unprofitable servant, cast ye out into the exterior darkness. There shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.€€ƒxAnd when the Son of man shall come in his majesty, and all the angels with him, then shall he sit upon the seat of his majesty.€€ƒx˜And all nations shall be gathered together before him: and he shall separate them one from another, as the shepherd separateth the sheep from the goats:€€ƒxHAnd he shall set the sheep on his right hand, but the goats on his left.€€ƒxªThen shall the king say to them that shall be on his right hand: Come, ye blessed of my Father, possess you the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world.€€ƒxxFor I was hungry, and you gave me to eat: I was thirsty, and you gave me to drink: I was a stranger, and you took me in:€€ƒxYNaked, and you covered me: sick, and you visited me: I was in prison, and you came to me.€€ƒxtThen shall the just answer him, saying: Lord, when did we see thee hungry and fed thee: thirsty and gave thee drink?€€ƒxOOr when did we see thee a stranger and took thee in? Or naked and covered thee?€€ƒx;Or when did we see thee sick or in prison and came to thee?€€ƒx‡And the king answering shall say to them: Amen I say to you, as long as you did it to one of these my least brethren, you did it to me.€€ƒx¢Then he shall say to them also that shall be on his left hand: Depart from me, you cursed, into everlasting fire, which was prepared for the devil and his angels.€€ƒxXFor I was hungry and you gave me not to eat: I was thirsty and you gave me not to drink.€€ƒxsI was a stranger and you took me not in: naked and you covered me not: sick and in prison and you did not visit me.€€ƒxŸThen they also shall answer him, saying: Lord, when did we see thee hungry or thirsty or a stranger or naked or sick or in prison and did not minister to thee?€€ƒx„Then he shall answer them, saying: Amen: I say to you, as long as you did it not to one of these least, neither did you do it to me.€€ƒxTAnd these shall go into everlasting punishment: but the just, into life everlasting.€€ƒ`€€˜KƒxjYou know that after two days shall be the pasch: and the Son of man shall be delivered up to be crucified.€€ƒx„Then were gathered together the chief priests and ancients of the people, into the court of the high priest, who was called Caiphas:€€ƒx]And they consulted together that by subtilty they might apprehend Jesus and put him to death.€€ƒx_But they said: Not on the festival day, lest perhaps there should be a tumult among the people.€€ƒx@And when Jesus was in Bethania, in the house of Simon the leper,€€ƒxtThere came to him a woman having an alabaster box of precious ointment and poured it on his head as he was at table.€€ƒxSAnd the disciples seeing it had indignation, saying: To what purpose is this waste?€€ƒx=For this might have been sold for much and given to the poor.€€ƒxlAnd Jesus knowing it, said to them: Why do you trouble this woman? For she hath wrought a good work upon me.€€ƒxBFor the poor you have always with you: but me you have not always.€€ƒxGFor she in pouring this ointment on my body hath done it for my burial.€€ƒx‘Amen I say to you, wheresoever this gospel shall be preached in the whole world, that also which she hath done shall be told for a memory of her.€€ƒxQThen went one of the twelve, who was called Judas Iscariot, to the chief priests.€€ƒxyAnd said to them: What will you give me, and I will deliver him unto you? But they appointed him thirty pieces of silver.€€ƒx9And from thenceforth he sought opportunity to betray him.€€ƒxƒAnd on the first day of the Azymes, the disciples came to Jesus, saying: Where wilt thou that we prepare for thee to eat the pasch?€€ƒxBut Jesus said: Go ye into the city to a certain man and say to him: The master saith, My time is near at hand. With thee I make the pasch with my disciples.€€ƒxNAnd the disciples did as Jesus appointed to them: and they prepared the pasch.€€ƒx?But when it was evening, he sat down with his twelve disciples.€€ƒx^And whilst they were eating, he said: Amen I say to you that one of you is about to betray me.€€ƒxHAnd they being very much troubled began every one to say: Is it I, Lord?€€ƒxXBut he answering said: He that dippeth his hand with me in the dish, he shall betray me.€€ƒxªThe Son of man indeed goeth, as it is written of him. But woe to that man by whom the Son of man shall be betrayed. It were better for him, if that man had not been born.€€ƒx`And Judas that betrayed him answering, said: Is it I, Rabbi? He saith to him: Thou hast said it.€€ƒxŒAnd whilst they were at supper, Jesus took bread and blessed and broke and gave to his disciples and said: Take ye and eat. This is my body.€€ƒxVAnd taking the chalice, he gave thanks and gave to them, saying: Drink ye all of this.€€ƒx_For this is my blood of the new testament, which shall be shed for many unto remission of sins.€€ƒx›And I say to you, I will not drink from henceforth of this fruit of the vine until that day when I shall drink it with you new in the kingdom of my Father.€€ƒx7And a hymn being said, they went out unto mount Olivet.€€ƒx¦Then Jesus saith to them: All you shall be scandalized in me this night. For it is written: I will strike the shepherd: and the sheep of the flock shall be dispersed.€€ƒxDBut after I shall be risen again, I will go before you into Galilee.€€ƒxiAnd Peter answering, said to him: Although all shall be scandalized in thee, I will never be scandalized.€€ƒxhJesus said to him: Amen I say to thee that in this night before the cock crow, thou wilt deny me thrice.€€ƒxxPeter saith to him: Yea, though I should die with thee, I will not deny thee. And in like manner said all the disciples.€€ƒx‘Then Jesus came with them into a country place which is called Gethsemani. And he said to his disciples: Sit you here, till I go yonder and pray.€€ƒx`And taking with him Peter and the two sons of Zebedee, he began to grow sorrowful and to be sad.€€ƒx]Then he saith to them: My soul is sorrowful even unto death. Stay you here and watch with me.€€ƒx±And going a little further, he fell upon his face, praying and saying: My Father, if it be possible, let this chalice pass from me. Nevertheless, not as I will but as thou wilt.€€ƒxzAnd he cometh to his disciples and findeth them asleep. And he saith to Peter: What? Could you not watch one hour with me?€€ƒxjWatch ye: and pray that ye enter not into temptation. The spirit indeed is willing, but the flesh is weak.€€ƒx‡Again the second time, he went and prayed, saying: My Father, if this chalice may not pass away, but I must drink it, thy will be done.€€ƒxIAnd he cometh again and findeth them sleeping: for their eyes were heavy.€€ƒxXAnd leaving them, he went again: and he prayed the third time, saying the selfsame word.€€ƒx®Then he cometh to his disciples and said to them: Sleep ye now and take your rest. Behold the hour is at hand: and the Son of man shall be betrayed into the hands of sinners.€€ƒx:Rise: let us go. Behold he is at hand that will betray me.€€ƒx©As he yet spoke, behold Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and clubs, sent from the chief priests and the ancients of the people.€€ƒxfAnd he that betrayed him gave them a sign, saying: Whomsoever I shall kiss, that is he. Hold him fast.€€ƒxGAnd forthwith coming to Jesus, he said: Hail, Rabbi. And he kissed him.€€ƒxmAnd Jesus said to him: Friend, whereto art thou come? Then they came up and laid hands on Jesus and held him.€€ƒx™And behold one of them that were with Jesus, stretching forth his hand, drew out his sword: and striking the servant of the high priest, cut off his ear.€€ƒxxThen Jesus saith to him: Put up again thy sword into its place: for all that take the sword shall perish with the sword.€€ƒxlThinkest thou that I cannot ask my Father, and he will give me presently more than twelve legions of angels?€€ƒxDHow then shall the scriptures be fulfilled, that so it must be done?€€ƒxÍIn that same hour, Jesus said to the multitudes: You are come out, as it were to a robber, with swords and clubs to apprehend me. I sat daily with you, teaching in the temple: and you laid not hands on me.€€ƒxxNow all this was done that the scriptures of the prophets might be fulfilled. Then the disciples, all leaving him, fled.€€ƒxmBut they holding Jesus led him to Caiphas the high priest, where the scribes and the ancients were assembled.€€ƒx‰And Peter followed him afar off, even to the court of the high priest, And going in, he sat with the servants, that he might see the end.€€ƒxqAnd the chief priests and the whole council sought false witness against Jesus, that they might put him to death.€€ƒxmAnd they found not, whereas many false witnesses had come in. And last of all there came two false witnesses:€€ƒxhAnd they said: This man said, I am able to destroy the temple of God and after three days to rebuild it.€€ƒxrAnd the high priest rising up, said to him: Answerest thou nothing to the things which these witness against thee?€€ƒx“But Jesus held his peace. And the high priest said to him: I adjure thee by the living God, that thou tell us if thou be the Christ the Son of God.€€ƒxºJesus saith to him: Thou hast said it. Nevertheless I say to you, hereafter you shall see the Son of man sitting on the right hand of the power of God and coming in the clouds of heaven.€€ƒx•Then the high priest rent his garments, saying: He hath blasphemed: What further need have we of witnesses? Behold, now you have heard the blasphemy.€€ƒx@What think you? But they answering, said: He is guilty of death.€€ƒxjThen did they spit in his face and buffeted him. And others struck his face with the palms of their hands,€€ƒx?Saying: Prophesy unto us, O Christ. Who is he that struck thee?€€ƒxyBut Peter sat without in the court. And there came to him a servant maid, saying: Thou also wast with Jesus the Galilean.€€ƒxCBut he denied before them all, saying: I know not what thou sayest.€€ƒx†And as he went out of the gate, another maid saw him; and she saith to them that were there: This man also was with Jesus of Nazareth.€€ƒx5And again he denied with an oath: I know not the man.€€ƒxAnd after a little while, they came that stood by and said to Peter: Surely thou also art one of them. For even thy speech doth discover thee.€€ƒx\Then he began to curse and to swear that he knew not the man. And immediately the cock crew.€€ƒxŒAnd Peter remembered the word of Jesus which he had said: Before the cock crow, thou wilt deny me thrice. And going forth, he wept bitterly.€€ƒ`€€˜BƒxLAnd they brought him bound and delivered him to Pontius Pilate the governor.€€ƒxšThen Judas, who betrayed him, seeing that he was condemned, repenting himself, brought back the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and ancients,€€ƒxfSaying: I have sinned in betraying innocent blood. But they said: What is that to us? Look thou to it.€€ƒxlAnd casting down the pieces of silver in the temple, he departed and went and hanged himself with an halter.€€ƒxBut the chief priests having taken the pieces of silver, said: It is not lawful to put them into the corbona, because it is the price of blood.€€ƒxuAnd after they had consulted together, they bought with them the potter's field, to be a burying place for strangers.€€ƒx^For this cause that field was called Haceldama, that is, the field of blood, even to this day.€€ƒxÂThen was fulfilled that which was spoken by Jeremias the prophet, saying: And they took the thirty pieces of silver, the price of him that was prized, whom they prized of the children of Israel.€€ƒxHAnd they gave them unto the potter's field, as the Lord appointed to me.€€ƒx‹And Jesus stood before the governor, and the governor asked him, saying: Art thou the king of the Jews? Jesus saith to him: Thou sayest it.€€ƒxOAnd when he was accused by the chief priests and ancients, he answered nothing.€€ƒx\Then Pilate saith to him: Dost not thou hear how great testimonies they allege against thee?€€ƒxOAnd he answered him to never a word, so that the governor wondered exceedingly.€€ƒxkNow upon the solemn day the governor was accustomed to release to the people one prisoner, whom they would.€€ƒx>And he had then a notorious prisoner that was called Barabbas.€€ƒxƒThey therefore being gathered together, Pilate said: Whom will you that I release to You: Barabbas, or Jesus that is called Christ?€€ƒx1For he knew that for envy they had delivered him.€€ƒx½And as he was sitting in the place of judgment, his wife sent to him, saying: Have thou nothing to do with that just man; for I have suffered many things this day in a dream because of him.€€ƒxjBut the chief priests and ancients persuaded the people that they should ask Barabbas and make Jesus away.€€ƒxwAnd the governor answering, said to them: Whether will you of the two to be released unto you? But they said: Barabbas.€€ƒxpPilate saith to them: What shall I do then with Jesus that is called Christ? They say all: Let him be crucified.€€ƒxrThe governor said to them: Why, what evil hath he done? But they cried out the more, saying: Let him be crucified.€€ƒxÅAnd Pilate seeing that he prevailed nothing, but that rather a tumult was made, taking water washed his hands before the people, saying: I am innocent of the blood of this just man. Look you to it.€€ƒxQAnd the whole people answering, said: His blood be upon us and upon our children.€€ƒxfThen he released to them Barabbas: and having scourged Jesus, delivered him unto them to be crucified.€€ƒxiThen the soldiers of the governor, taking Jesus into the hall, gathered together unto him the whole band.€€ƒx6And stripping him, they put a scarlet cloak about him.€€ƒx©And platting a crown of thorns, they put it upon his head, and a reed in his right hand. And bowing the knee before him, they mocked him, saying: Hail, King of the Jews.€€ƒx>And spitting upon him, they took the reed and struck his head.€€ƒx€And after they had mocked him, they took off the cloak from him and put on him his own garments and led him away to crucify him.€€ƒx]And going out, they found a man of Cyrene, named Simon: him they forced to take up his cross.€€ƒxRAnd they came to the place that is called Golgotha, which is the place of Calvary.€€ƒx^And they gave him wine to drink mingled with gall. And when he had tasted, he would not drink.€€ƒx×And after they had crucified him, they divided his garments, casting lots; that it might be fulfilled which was spoken by the prophet, saying: They divided my garments among them; and upon my vesture they cast lots.€€ƒxAnd they sat and watched him.€€ƒxQAnd they put over his head his cause written: THIS IS JESUS THE KING OF THE JEWS.€€ƒxTThen were crucified with him two thieves: one on the right hand and one on the left.€€ƒx<And they that passed by blasphemed him, wagging their heads,€€ƒx¢And saying: Vah, thou that destroyest the temple of God and in three days dost rebuild it: save thy own self. If thou be the Son of God, come down from the cross.€€ƒxSIn like manner also the chief priests, with the scribes and ancients, mocking said:€€ƒx„He saved others: himself he cannot save. If he be the king of Israel, let him now come down from the cross: and we will believe him.€€ƒxaHe trusted in God: let him now deliver him if he will have him. For he said: I am the Son of God.€€ƒxYAnd the selfsame thing the thieves also that were crucified with him reproached him with.€€ƒxWNow from the sixth hour, there was darkness over the whole earth, until the ninth hour.€€ƒxAnd about the ninth hour, Jesus cried with a loud voice, saying: Eli, Eli, lamma sabacthani? That is, My God, My God, why hast thou forsaken me?€€ƒxAAnd some that stood there and heard said: This man calleth Elias.€€ƒxxAnd immediately one of them running took a sponge and filled it with vinegar and put it on a reed and gave him to drink.€€ƒxOAnd the others said: Let be. Let us see whether Elias will come to deliver him.€€ƒx?And Jesus again crying with a loud voice, yielded up the ghost.€€ƒx€And behold the veil of the temple was rent in two from the top even to the bottom: and the earth quaked and the rocks were rent.€€ƒxOAnd the graves were opened: and many bodies of the saints that had slept arose,€€ƒxaAnd coming out of the tombs after his resurrection, came into the holy city and appeared to many.€€ƒx±Now the centurion and they that were with him watching Jesus, having seen the earthquake and the things that were done, were sore afraid, saying: Indeed this was the Son of God.€€ƒxdAnd there were there many women afar off, who had followed Jesus from Galilee, ministering unto him:€€ƒxkAmong whom was Mary Magdalen and Mary the mother of James and Joseph and the mother of the sons of Zebedee.€€ƒx|And when it was evening, there came a certain rich man of Arimathea, named Joseph, who also himself was a disciple of Jesus.€€ƒxgHe went to Pilate and asked the body of Jesus. Then Pilate commanded that the body should be delivered.€€ƒx@And Joseph taking the body wrapped it up in a clean linen cloth:€€ƒxAnd laid it in his own new monument, which he had hewed out in a rock. And he rolled a great stone to the door of the monument and went his way.€€ƒxYAnd there was there Mary Magdalen and the other Mary, sitting over against the sepulchre.€€ƒxuAnd the next day, which followed the day of preparation, the chief priests and the Pharisees came together to Pilate,€€ƒxtSaying: Sir, we have remembered, that that seducer said, while he was yet alive: After three days I will rise again.€€ƒxÙCommand therefore the sepulchre to be guarded until the third day: lest perhaps his disciples come and steal him away and say to the people: He is risen from the dead. And the last error shall be worse than the first.€€ƒxAPilate saith to them: You have a guard. Go, guard it as you know.€€ƒxRAnd they departing, made the sepulchre sure, sealing the stone and setting guards.€€ƒ`€€”ƒx‰And behold there was a great earthquake. For an angel of the Lord descended from heaven and coming rolled back the stone and sat upon it.€€ƒx=And his countenance was as lightning and his raiment as snow.€€ƒxOAnd for fear of him, the guards were struck with terror and became as dead men.€€ƒxkAnd the angel answering, said to the women: Fear not you: for I know that you seek Jesus who was crucified.€€ƒx]He is not here. For he is risen, as he said. Come, and see the place where the Lord was laid.€€ƒx¡And going quickly, tell ye his disciples that he is risen. And behold he will go before you into Galilee. There you shall see him. Lo, I have foretold it to you.€€ƒxdAnd they went out quickly from the sepulchre with fear and great joy, running to tell his disciples.€€ƒxhAnd behold, Jesus met them, saying: All hail. But they came up and took hold of his feet and adored him.€€ƒxkThen Jesus said to them: Fear not. Go, tell my brethren that they go into Galilee. There they shall see me.€€ƒx„Who when they were departed, behold, some of the guards came into the city and told the chief priests all things that had been done.€€ƒxoAnd they being assembled together with the ancients, taking counsel, gave a great sum of money to the soldiers,€€ƒxTSaying: Say you, His disciples came by night and stole him away when we were asleep.€€ƒxLAnd if the governor shall hear of this, we will persuade him and secure you.€€ƒxuSo they taking the money, did as they were taught: and this word was spread abroad among the Jews even unto this day.€€ƒx]And the eleven disciples went into Galilee, unto the mountain where Jesus had appointed them.€€ƒx-And seeing him they adored: but some doubted.€€ƒxYAnd Jesus coming, spoke to them, saying: All power is given to me in heaven and in earth.€€ƒxuGoing therefore, teach ye all nations: baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Ghost.€€ƒxTeaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you. And behold I am with you all days, even to the consummation of the world.€€ƒrThe Gospel of Mark€€dMark¤dnamedMarkiosis_namedMarklabbreviationdMarkhchapters˜-ƒxvAs it is written in Isaias the prophet: Behold I send my angel before thy face, who shall prepare the way before thee.€€ƒx]A voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare ye the way of the Lord; make straight his paths.€€ƒx_John was in the desert, baptizing and preaching the baptism of penance, unto remission of sins.€€ƒx”And there went out to him all the country of Judea and all they of Jerusalem and were baptized by him in the river of Jordan, confessing their sins.€€ƒxlAnd John was clothed camel's hair, and a leathern girdle about his loins: and he ate locusts and wild honey.€€ƒx‡And he preached, saying: There cometh after me one mightier than I, the latchet of whose shoes I am not worthy to stoop down and loose.€€ƒxMI have baptized you with water: but he shall baptize you with the Holy Ghost.€€ƒxkAnd it came to pass, in those days, Jesus came from Nazareth of Galilee and was baptized by John in Jordan.€€ƒx{And forthwith coming up out of the water, he saw the heavens open and the Spirit as a dove descending and remaining on him.€€ƒxWAnd there came a voice from heaven: Thou art my beloved Son; in thee I am well pleased.€€ƒx9And immediately the Spirit drove him out into the desert.€€ƒx‰And he was in the desert forty days and forty nights, and was tempted by Satan. And he was with beasts: and the angels ministered to him.€€ƒxhAnd after that John was delivered up, Jesus came in Galilee, preaching the gospel of the kingdom of God,€€ƒxfAnd saying: The time is accomplished and the kingdom of God is at hand. Repent and believe the gospel:€€ƒxzAnd passing by the sea of Galilee, he saw Simon and Andrew his brother, casting nets into the sea for they were fishermen.€€ƒxTAnd Jesus said to them: Come after me; and I will make you to become fishers of men.€€ƒx6And immediately leaving their nets, they followed him.€€ƒxAnd going on from thence a little farther, he saw James the son of Zebedee and John his brother, who also were mending their nets in the ship:€€ƒxqAnd forthwith he called them. And leaving their father Zebedee in the ship with his hired men, they followed him.€€ƒxoAnd they entered into Capharnaum: and forthwith upon the sabbath days going into the synagogue, he taught them.€€ƒxoAnd they were astonished at his doctrine. For he was teaching them as one having power, and not as the scribes.€€ƒxPAnd there was in their synagogue a man with an unclean spirit; and he cried out,€€ƒxSaying: What have we to do with thee, Jesus of Nazareth? Art thou come to destroy us? I know who thou art, the Holy One of God.€€ƒxGAnd Jesus threatened him, saying: Speak no more, and go out of the man.€€ƒxVAnd the unclean spirit, tearing him and crying out with a loud voice, went out of him.€€ƒxÉAnd they were all amazed insomuch that they questioned among themselves, saying: What thing is this? What is this new doctrine? For with power he commandeth even the unclean spirits: and they obey him.€€ƒxIAnd the fame of him was spread forthwith into all the country of Galilee.€€ƒxmAnd immediately going out of the synagogue they came into the house of Simon and Andrew, with James and John.€€ƒxVAnd Simon's wife's mother lay in a fit of a fever: and forthwith they tell him of her.€€ƒx~And coming to her, he lifted her up, taking her by the hand; and immediately the fever left her, and she ministered unto them.€€ƒxqAnd when it was evening, after sunset, they brought to him all that were ill and that were possessed with devils.€€ƒx3And all the city was gathered together at the door.€€ƒx’And he healed many that were troubled with divers diseases. And he cast out many devils: and he suffered them not to speak, because they knew him.€€ƒxSAnd rising very early, going out, he went into a desert place: and there he prayed.€€ƒx9And Simon and they that were with him followed after him.€€ƒxAAnd when they had found him, they said to him: All seek for thee.€€ƒx„And he saith to them: Let us go into the neighbouring towns and cities, that I may preach there also; for to this purpose am I come.€€ƒxSAnd he was preaching in their synagogues and in all Galilee and casting out devils.€€ƒxtAnd there came a leper to him, beseeching him and kneeling down, said to him: If thou wilt thou canst make me clean.€€ƒxxAnd Jesus, having compassion on him, stretched forth his hand and touching him saith to him: I will. Be thou made clean.€€ƒxYAnd when he had spoken, immediately the leprosy departed from him: and he was made clean.€€ƒx8And he strictly charged him and forthwith sent him away.€€ƒx¨And he saith to him: See thou tell no one; but go, shew thyself to the high priest and offer for thy cleansing the things that Moses commanded, for a testimony to them.€€ƒx½But he being gone out, began to publish and to blaze abroad the word: so that he could not openly go into the city. but was without in desert places. And they flocked to him from all sides.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒx–And it was heard that he was in the house. And many came together, so that there was no room: no, not even at the door. And he spoke to them the word.€€ƒxNAnd they came to him, bringing one sick of the palsy, who was carried by four.€€ƒx°And when they could not offer him unto him for the multitude, they uncovered the roof where he was: and opening it, they let down the bed wherein the man sick of the palsy lay.€€ƒxhAnd when Jesus had seen their faith, he saith to the sick of the palsy: Son, thy sins are forgiven thee.€€ƒxNAnd there were some of the scribes sitting there and thinking in their hearts:€€ƒxQWhy doth this man speak thus? He blasphemeth. Who can forgive sins, but God only?€€ƒxWhich Jesus presently knowing in his spirit that they so thought within themselves, saith to them: Why think you these things in your hearts?€€ƒxyWhich is easier, to say to the sick of the palsy: Thy sins are forgiven thee; or to say: Arise, take up thy bed and walk?€€ƒxrBut that you may know that the Son of man hath power on earth to forgive sins (he saith to the sick of the palsy):€€ƒx<I say to thee: Arise. Take up thy bed and go into thy house.€€ƒx™And immediately he arose and, taking up his bed, went his way in the sight of all: so that all wondered and glorified God, saying: We never saw the like.€€ƒx_And he went forth again to the sea side: and all the multitude came to him. And he taught them.€€ƒxAnd when he was passing by, he saw Levi, the son of Alpheus, sitting at the receipt of custom; and he saith to him: Follow me. And rising up, he followed him.€€ƒx©And it came to pass as he sat at meat in his house, many Publicans and sinners sat down together with Jesus and his disciples. For they were many, who also followed him.€€ƒx§And the scribes and the Pharisees, seeing that he ate with publicans and sinners, said to his disciples: Why doth your master eat and drink with publicans and sinners?€€ƒx˜Jesus hearing this, saith to them: They that are well have no need of a physician, but they that are sick. For I came not to call the just, but sinners.€€ƒx®And the disciples of John and the Pharisees used to fast. And they come and say to him: Why do the disciples of John and of the Pharisees fast; but thy disciples do not fast?€€ƒx¨And Jesus saith to them: Can the children of the marriage fast, as long as the bridegroom is with them? As long as they have the bridegroom with them, they cannot fast.€€ƒxqBut the days will come when the bridegroom shall be taken away from them: and then they shall fast in those days.€€ƒx‹No man seweth a piece of raw cloth to an old garment: otherwise the new piecing taketh away from the old, and there is made a greater rent.€€ƒxÃAnd no man putteth new wine into old bottles: otherwise the wine will burst the bottles, and both the wine will be spilled and the bottles will be lost. But new wine must be put into new bottles.€€ƒx›And it came to pass again, as the Lord walked through the corn fields on the sabbath, that his disciples began to go forward and to pluck the ears of corn.€€ƒx_And the Pharisees said to him: Behold, why do they on the sabbath day that which is not lawful?€€ƒx}And he said to them: Have you never read what David did when he had need and was hungry, himself and they that were with him?€€ƒx¾How he went into the house of God, under Abiathar the high priest, and did eat the loaves of proposition, which was not lawful to eat but for the priests, and gave to them who were with him?€€ƒxOAnd he said to them: The sabbath was made for man, and not man for the sabbath.€€ƒx5Therefore the Son of man is Lord of the sabbath also.€€ƒ`€€˜#ƒx[And they watched him whether he would heal on the sabbath days, that they might accuse him.€€ƒxHAnd he said to the man who had the withered hand: Stand up in the midst.€€ƒx‰And he saith to them: Is it lawful to do good on the sabbath days, or to do evil? To save life, or to destroy? But they held their peace.€€ƒxÈAnd looking round about on them with anger, being grieved for the blindness of their hearts, he saith to the man: Stretch forth thy hand. And he stretched it forth: and his hand was restored unto him.€€ƒxxAnd the Pharisees going out, immediately made a consultation with the Herodians against him, how they might destroy him.€€ƒxkBut Jesus retired with his disciples to the sea; and a great multitude followed him from Galilee and Judea,€€ƒxŸAnd from Jerusalem, and from Idumea and from beyond the Jordan. And they about Tyre and Sidon, a great multitude, hearing the things which he did, came to him.€€ƒxzAnd he spoke to his disciples that a small ship should wait on him, because of the multitude, lest they should throng him.€€ƒxYFor he healed many, so that they pressed upon him for to touch him, as many as had evils.€€ƒxYAnd the unclean spirits, when they saw him, fell down before him: and they cried, saying:€€ƒxZThou art the Son of God. And he strictly charged them that they should not make him known.€€ƒx]And going up into a mountain, he called unto him whom he would himself: and they came to him.€€ƒxRAnd he made that twelve should be with him, and that he might send them to preach.€€ƒxBAnd he gave them power to heal sicknesses, and to cast out devils.€€ƒx$And to Simon he gave the name Peter:€€ƒxxAnd James the son of Zebedee, and John the brother of James; and he named them Boanerges, which is, The sons of thunder.€€ƒxyAnd Andrew and Philip, and Bartholomew and Matthew, and Thomas and James of Alpheus, and Thaddeus and Simon the Cananean:€€ƒx*And Judas Iscariot, who also betrayed him.€€ƒxoAnd they come to a house, and the multitude cometh together again, so that they could not so much as eat bread.€€ƒxhAnd when his friends had heard of it, they went out to lay hold on him. For they said: He is become mad.€€ƒx~And the scribes who were come down from Jerusalem, said: He hath Beelzebub, and by the prince of devils he casteth out devils.€€ƒxaAnd after he had called them together, he said to them in parables: How can Satan cast out Satan?€€ƒxFAnd if a kingdom be divided against itself, that kingdom cannot stand.€€ƒxBAnd if a house be divided against itself, that house cannot stand.€€ƒx[And if Satan be risen up against himself, he is divided, and cannot stand, but hath an end.€€ƒx“No man can enter into the house of a strong man and rob him of his goods, unless he first bind the strong man, and then shall he plunder his house.€€ƒx{Amen I say to you that all sins shall be forgiven unto the sons of men, and the blasphemies wherewith they shall blaspheme:€€ƒx|But he that shall blaspheme against the Holy Ghost, shall never have forgiveness, but shall be guilty of an everlasting sin.€€ƒx-Because they said: He hath an unclean spirit.€€ƒxWAnd his mother and his brethren came; and standing without, sent unto him, calling him.€€ƒxoAnd the multitude sat about him; and they say to him: Behold thy mother and thy brethren without seek for thee.€€ƒx>And answering them, he said: Who is my mother and my brethren?€€ƒx^And looking round about on them who sat about him, he saith: Behold my mother and my brethren.€€ƒxTFor whosoever shall do the will of God, he is my brother, and my sister, and mother.€€ƒ`€€˜)ƒxOAnd he taught them many things in parables, and said unto them in his doctrine:€€ƒx+Hear ye: Behold, the sower went out to sow.€€ƒx\And whilst he sowed, some fell by the way side, and the birds of the air came and ate it up.€€ƒxAnd other some fell upon stony ground, where it had not much earth; and it shot up immediately, because it had no depth of earth.€€ƒxZAnd when the sun was risen, it was scorched; and because it had no root, it withered away.€€ƒx[And some fell among thorns; and the thorns grew up, and choked it, and it yielded no fruit.€€ƒx’And some fell upon good ground; and brought forth fruit that grew up, and increased and yielded, one thirty, another sixty, and another a hundred.€€ƒx5And he said: He that hath ears to hear, let him hear.€€ƒxKAnd when he was alone, the twelve that were with him asked him the parable.€€ƒx‘And he said to them: To you it is given to know the mystery of the kingdom of God: but to them that are without, all things are done in parables:€€ƒx­That seeing they may see, and not perceive; and hearing they may hear, and not understand; lest at any time they should be converted, and their sins should be forgiven them.€€ƒx]And he saith to them: Are you ignorant of this, parable? and how shall you know all parables?€€ƒx He that soweth, soweth the word.€€ƒx¬And these are they by the way side, where the word is sown, and as soon as they have heard, immediately Satan cometh and taketh away the word that was sown in their hearts.€€ƒx‚And these likewise are they that are sown on the stony ground: who when they have heard the word, immediately receive it with joy.€€ƒxœAnd they have no root in themselves, but are only for a time: and then when tribulation and persecution ariseth for the word they are presently scandalized.€€ƒxRAnd others there are who are sown among thorns: these are they that hear the word,€€ƒx“And the cares of the world, and the deceitfulness of riches, and the lusts after other things entering in choke the word, and it is made fruitless.€€ƒxŸAnd these are they who are sown upon the good ground, who hear the word, and receive it, and yield fruit, the one thirty, another sixty, and another a hundred.€€ƒxxAnd he said to them: Doth a candle come in to be put under a bushel, or under a bed? and not to be set on a candlestick?€€ƒxtFor there is nothing hid, which shall not be made manifest: neither was it made secret, but that it may come abroad.€€ƒx+If any man have ears to hear, let him hear.€€ƒxAnd he said to them: Take heed what you hear. In what measure you shall mete, it shall be measured to you again, and more shall be given to you.€€ƒxtFor he that hath, to him shall be given: and he that hath not, that also which he hath shall be taken away from him.€€ƒxSAnd he said: So is the kingdom of God, as if a man should cast seed into the earth,€€ƒxiAnd should sleep, and rise, night and day, and the seed should spring, and grow up whilst he knoweth not.€€ƒxqFor the earth of itself bringeth forth fruit, first the blade, then the ear, afterwards the full corn in the ear.€€ƒxgAnd when the fruit is brought forth, immediately he putteth in the sickle, because the harvest is come.€€ƒx_And he said: To what shall we liken the kingdom of God? or to what parable shall we compare it?€€ƒxwIt is as a grain of mustard seed: which when it is sown in the earth, is less than all the seeds that are in the earth:€€ƒxªAnd when it is sown, it groweth up, and becometh greater than all herbs, and shooteth out great branches, so that the birds of the air may dwell under the shadow thereof.€€ƒx\And with many such parables, he spoke to them the word, according as they were able to hear.€€ƒxdAnd without parable he did not speak unto them; but apart, he explained all things to his disciples.€€ƒxYAnd he saith to them that day, when evening was come: Let us pass over to the other side.€€ƒxnAnd sending away the multitude, they take him even as he was in the ship: and there were other ships with him.€€ƒxeAnd there arose a great storm of wind, and the waves beat into the ship, so that the ship was filled.€€ƒx˜And he was in the hinder part of the ship, sleeping upon a pillow; and they awake him, and say to him: Master, doth, it not concern thee that we perish?€€ƒxAnd rising up, he rebuked the wind, and said to the sea: Peace, be still. And the wind ceased: and there was made a great calm.€€ƒx¹And he said to them: Why are you fearful? have you not faith yet? And they feared exceedingly: and they said one to another: Who is this (thinkest thou) that both wind and sea obey him?€€ƒ`€€ƒxKAnd they came over the strait of the sea, into the country of the Gerasens.€€˜+ƒxWWho had his dwelling in the tombs, and no man now could bind him, not even with chains.€€ƒxŠFor having been often bound with fetters and chains, he had burst the chains, and broken the fetters in pieces, and no one could tame him.€€ƒxnAnd he was always day and night in the monuments and in the mountains, crying and cutting himself with stones.€€ƒx1And seeing Jesus afar off, he ran and adored him.€€ƒx–And crying with a loud voice, he said: What have I to do with thee, Jesus the Son of the most high God? I adjure thee by God that thou torment me not.€€ƒx=For he said unto him: Go out of the man, thou unclean spirit.€€ƒx\And he asked him: What is thy name? And he saith to him: My name is Legion, for we are many.€€ƒxNAnd he besought him much, that he would not drive him away out of the country.€€ƒxEAnd there was there near the mountain a great herd of swine, feeding.€€ƒxZAnd the spirits besought him, saying: Send us into the swine, that we may enter into them.€€ƒxØAnd Jesus immediately gave them leave. And the unclean spirits going out, entered into the swine: and the herd with great violence was carried headlong into the sea, being about two thousand, were stifled in the sea.€€ƒxoAnd they that fed them fled, and told it in the city and in the fields. And they went out to see what was done:€€ƒxˆAnd they came to Jesus, and they see him that was troubled with the devil, sitting, clothed, and well in his wits, and they were afraid.€€ƒxxAnd they that had seen it, told them, in what manner he had been dealt with who had the devil; and concerning the swine.€€ƒxBAnd they began to pray him that he would depart from their coasts.€€ƒx|And when he went up into the ship, he that had been troubled with the devil, began to beseech him that he might be with him.€€ƒxAnd he admitted him not, but saith him: Go into thy house to thy friends, and tell them how great things the Lord hath done for thee, and hath had mercy thee.€€ƒxuAnd he went his way, and began to publish in Decapolis how great things Jesus had done for him: and all men wondered.€€ƒx‰And when Jesus had passed again in the ship over the strait, a great multitude assembled together unto him, and he was nigh unto the sea.€€ƒxkAnd there cometh one of the rulers of the synagogue named Jairus: and seeing him, falleth down at his feet.€€ƒxˆAnd he besought him much, saying: My daughter is at the point of death, come, lay thy hand upon her, that she may be safe, and may live.€€ƒxPAnd he went with him, and a great multitude followed him, and they thronged him.€€ƒx9And a woman who was under an issue of blood twelve years,€€ƒx€And had suffered many things from many physicians; and had spent all that she had, and was nothing the better, but rather worse,€€ƒxSWhen she had heard of Jesus, came in the crowd behind him, and touched his garment.€€ƒxAFor she said: If I shall touch but his garment, I shall be whole.€€ƒxoAnd forthwith the fountain of her blood was dried up, and she felt in her body that she was healed of the evil.€€ƒxAnd immediately Jesus knowing in himself the virtue that had proceeded from him, turning to the multitude, said: Who hath touched my garments?€€ƒxlAnd his disciples said to him: Thou seest the multitude thronging thee, and sayest thou who hath touched me?€€ƒx1And he looked about to see her who had done this.€€ƒx}But the woman fearing and trembling, knowing what was done in her, came and fell down before him, and told him all the truth.€€ƒxlAnd he said to her: Daughter, thy faith hath made thee whole: go in peace, and be thou whole of thy disease.€€ƒx™While he was yet speaking, some come from the ruler of the synagogue's house, saying: Thy daughter is dead: why dost thou trouble the master any further?€€ƒxmBut Jesus having heard the word that was spoken, saith to the ruler of the synagogue: Fear not, only believe.€€ƒx_And he admitted not any man to follow him, but Peter, and James, and John the brother of James.€€ƒxuAnd they cone to the house of the ruler of the synagogue; and he seeth a tumult, and people weeping and wailing much.€€ƒxeAnd going in, he saith to them Why make you this ado, and weep? the damsel is not dead, but sleepeth.€€ƒx·And they laughed him to scorn. But he having put them all out, taketh the father and the mother of the damsel, and them that were with him, and entereth in where the damsel was lying.€€ƒx|And taking the damsel by the hand, he saith to her: Talitha cumi, which is, being interpreted: Damsel (I say to thee) arise.€€ƒxAnd immediately the damsel rose up, and walked: and she was twelve years old: and they were astonished with a great astonishment.€€ƒxqAnd he charged them strictly that no man should know it: and commanded that something should be given her to eat.€€ƒ`€€ƒxXAnd going out from thence, he went into his own country; and his disciples followed him.€€˜8ƒxµIs not this the carpenter, the son of Mary, the brother of James, and Joseph, and Jude, and Simon? are not also his sisters here with us? And they were scandalized in regard of him.€€ƒxAnd Jesus said to them: A prophet is not without honour, but in his own country, and in his own house, and among his own kindred.€€ƒxlAnd he could not do any miracles there, only that he cured a few that were sick, laying his hands upon them.€€ƒxaAnd he wondered because of their unbelief, and he went through the villages round about teaching.€€ƒxgAnd he called the twelve; and began to send them two and two, and gave them power over unclean spirits.€€ƒx€And he commanded them that they should take nothing for the way, but a staff only; no scrip, no bread, nor money in their purse,€€ƒxGBut to be shod with sandals, and that they should not put on two coats.€€ƒxlAnd he said to them: Wheresoever you shall enter into an house, there abide till you depart from that place.€€ƒx†And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear you; going forth from thence, shake off the dust from your feet for a testimony to them.€€ƒx4And going forth they preached men should do penance:€€ƒxZAnd they cast out many devils, and anointed with oil many that were sick, and healed them.€€ƒx¨And king Herod heard, (for his name was made manifest,) and he said: John the Baptist is risen again from dead, and therefore mighty works shew forth themselves in him.€€ƒxWAnd others said: It is Elias. But others said: It is a prophet, as one of the prophets.€€ƒxQWhich Herod hearing, said: John whom I beheaded, he is risen again from the dead.€€ƒxšFor Herod himself had sent and apprehended John, and bound him prison for the sake of Herodias the wife of Philip his brother, because he had married her.€€ƒxMFor John said to Herod: It is not lawful for thee to have thy brother's wife.€€ƒxUNow Herodias laid snares for him: and was desirous to put him to death and could not.€€ƒxFor Herod feared John, knowing him to be a just and holy man: and kept him, and when he heard him, did many things: and he heard him willingly.€€ƒx‚And when a convenient day was come, Herod made a supper for his birthday, for the princes, and tribunes, and chief men of Galilee.€€ƒxĞAnd when the daughter of the same Herodias had come in, and had danced, and pleased Herod, and them that were at table with him, the king said to the damsel: Ask of me what thou wilt, and I will give it thee.€€ƒxeAnd he swore to her: Whatsoever thou shalt ask I will give thee, though it be the half of my kingdom.€€ƒxsWho when she was gone out, said to her mother, What shall I ask? But her mother said: The head of John the Baptist.€€ƒx›And when she was come in immediately with haste to the king, she asked, saying: I will that forthwith thou give me in a dish, the head of John the Baptist.€€ƒx‚And the king was struck sad. Yet because of his oath, and because of them that were with him at table, he would not displease her:€€ƒxSBut sending an executioner, he commanded that his head should be brought in a dish.€€ƒx}And he beheaded him in the prison, and brought his head in a dish: and gave to the damsel, and the damsel gave it her mother.€€ƒxKWhich his disciples hearing came, and took his body, and laid it in a tomb.€€ƒxeAnd the apostles coming together unto Jesus, related to him all things that they had done and taught.€€ƒx–And he said to them: Come apart into a desert place, and rest a little. For there were many coming and going: and they had not so much as time to eat.€€ƒx>And going up into a ship, they went into a desert place apart.€€ƒx€And they saw them going away, and many knew: and they ran flocking thither foot from all the cities, and were there before them.€€ƒx£And Jesus going out saw a great multitude: and he had compassion on them, because they were as sheep not having a shepherd, and he began to teach them many things.€€ƒxxAnd when the day was now far spent, his disciples came to him, saying: This is a desert place, and the hour is now past:€€ƒxaSend them away, that going into the next villages and towns, they may buy themselves meat to eat.€€ƒx—And he answering said to them: Give you them to eat. And they said to him: Let us go and buy bread for two hundred pence, and we will give them to eat.€€ƒxoAnd he saith to them: How many loaves have you? go and see. And when they knew, they say: Five, and two fishes.€€ƒx`And he commanded them that they should make them all sit down by companies upon the green grass.€€ƒx7And they sat down in ranks, by hundreds and by fifties.€€ƒxÎAnd when he had taken the five loaves, and the two fishes: looking up to heaven, he blessed, and broke the loaves, and gave to his disciples to set before them: and the two fishes he divided among them all.€€ƒx)And they all did eat, and had their fill.€€ƒxSAnd they took up the leavings, twelve full baskets of fragments, and of the fishes.€€ƒx.And they that did eat, were five thousand men.€€ƒx›And immediately he obliged his disciples to go up into the ship, that they might go before him over the water to Bethsaida, whilst he dismissed the people.€€ƒxCAnd when he had dismissed them, he went up to the mountain to pray,€€ƒxZAnd when it was late, the ship was in the midst of the sea, and himself alone on the land.€€ƒx¸And seeing them labouring in rowing, (for the wind was against them,) and about the fourth watch of the night, he cometh to them walking upon the sea, and he would have passed by them.€€ƒx[But they seeing him walking upon the sea, thought it was an apparition, and they cried out.€€ƒxŒFor they all saw him, and were troubled bled. And immediately he spoke with them, and said to them: Have a good heart, it is I, fear ye not.€€ƒxoAnd he went up to them into the ship, and the wind ceased: and they were far more astonished within themselves:€€ƒxKFor they understood not concerning the loaves; for their heart was blinded.€€ƒx[And when they had passed over, they came into the land of Genezareth, and set to the shore.€€ƒxCAnd when they were gone out of the ship, immediately they knew him:€€ƒxxAnd running through that whole country, they began to carry about in beds those that were sick, where they heard he was.€€ƒxÖAnd whithersoever he entered, into towns or into villages or cities, they laid the sick in the streets, and besought him that they might touch but the hem of his garment: and as many as touched him were made whole.€€ƒ`€€ƒxcAnd there assembled together unto him the Pharisees and some of the scribes, coming from Jerusalem.€€˜%ƒxtFor the Pharisees and all the Jews eat not without often washing their hands, holding the tradition of the ancients.€€ƒxÙAnd when they come from the market, unless they be washed, they eat not: and many other things there are that have been delivered to them to observe, the washings of cups and of pots and of brazen vessels and of beds.€€ƒx˜And the Pharisees and scribes asked him: Why do not thy disciples walk according to the tradition of the ancients, but they eat bread with common hands?€€ƒx§But he answering, said to them: Well did Isaias prophesy of you hypocrites, as it is written: This people honoureth me with their lips, but their heart is far from me.€€ƒxGAnd in vain do they worship me, teaching doctrines and precepts of men.€€ƒxFor leaving the commandment of God, you hold the tradition of men, the washing of pots and of cups: and many other things you do like to these.€€ƒxhAnd he said to them: Well do you make void the commandment of God, that you may keep your own tradition.€€ƒxnFor Moses said: Honour thy father and thy mother. And He that shall curse father or mother, dying let him die.€€ƒxzBut you say: If a man shall say to his father or mother, Corban (which is a gift) whatsoever is from me shall profit thee.€€ƒxHAnd further you suffer him not to do any thing for his father or mother,€€ƒxvMaking void the word of God by your own tradition, which you have given forth. And many other such like things you do.€€ƒxYAnd calling again the multitude unto him, he said to them: Hear ye me all and understand.€€ƒx’There is nothing from without a man that entering into him can defile him. But the things which come from a man, those are they that defile a man.€€ƒx+If any man have ears to hear, let him hear.€€ƒx\And when he was come into the house from the multitude, his disciples asked him the parable.€€ƒxAnd he saith to them: So are you also without knowledge? Understand you not that every thing from without entering into a man cannot defile him:€€ƒxpBecause it entereth not into his heart but goeth into his belly and goeth out into the privy, purging all meats?€€ƒxIBut he said that the things which come out from a man, they defile a man.€€ƒxcFor from within, out of the heart of men, proceed evil thoughts, adulteries, fornications, murders,€€ƒxeThefts, covetousness, wickedness, deceit, lasciviousness, an evil eye, blasphemy, pride, foolishness.€€ƒx8All these evil things come from within and defile a man.€€ƒxšAnd rising from thence he went into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon: and entering into a house, he would that no man should know it. And he could not be hid.€€ƒxqFor a woman as soon as she heard of him, whose daughter had an unclean spirit, came in and fell down at his feet.€€ƒxFor the woman was a Gentile, a Syrophenician born. And she besought him that he would cast forth the devil out of her daughter.€€ƒx†Who said to her: suffer first the children to be filled: for it is not good to take the bread of the children and cast it to the dogs.€€ƒxsBut she answered and said to him: Yea, Lord; for the whelps also eat under the table of the crumbs of the children.€€ƒxWAnd he said to her: For this saying, go thy way. The devil is gone out of thy daughter.€€ƒxlAnd when she was come into her house, she found the girl lying upon the bed and that the devil was gone out.€€ƒx„And again going out of the coasts of Tyre, he came by Sidon to the sea of Galilee, through the midst the of the coasts of Decapolis.€€ƒxcAnd they bring to him one deaf and dumb: and they besought him that he would lay his hand upon him.€€ƒxoAnd taking him from the multitude apart, he put his fingers into his ears: and spitting, he touched his tongue.€€ƒxYAnd looking up to heaven, he groaned and said to him: Ephpheta, which is, Be thou opened.€€ƒx`And immediately his ears were opened and the string of his tongue was loosed and he spoke right.€€ƒx‚And he charged them that they should tell no man. But the more he charged them, so much the more a great deal did they publish it.€€ƒx…And so much the more did they wonder, saying: He hath done all things well. He hath made both the deaf to hear and the dumb to speak.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‚In those days again, when there was great multitude and they had nothing to eat; calling his disciples together, he saith to them:€€˜&ƒxuAnd if I shall send them away fasting to their home, they will faint in the way: for some of them came from afar off.€€ƒxdAnd his disciples answered him: From whence can any one fill them here with bread in the wilderness?€€ƒx<And he asked them: How many loaves have ye? Who said: Seven.€€ƒxÂAnd he commanded the people to sit down on the ground. And taking the seven loaves, giving thanks, he broke and gave to his disciples for to set before them. And they set them before the people.€€ƒx_And they had a few little fishes: and he blessed them and commanded them to be set before them.€€ƒxgAnd they did eat and were filled: and they took up that which was left of the fragments, seven baskets.€€ƒxHAnd they that had eaten were about four thousand. And he sent them away.€€ƒx^And immediately going up into a ship with his disciples, he came into the parts of Dalmanutha.€€ƒxiAnd the Pharisees came forth and began to question with him, asking him a sign from heaven, tempting him.€€ƒxAnd sighing deeply in spirit, he saith: Why doth this generation seek a sign? Amen, I say to you, a sign shall not be given to this generation.€€ƒx[And leaving them, he went up again into the ship and passed to the other side of the water.€€ƒxOAnd they forgot to take bread: and they had but one loaf with them in the ship.€€ƒxkAnd he charged them saying: Take heed and beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and of the leaven of Herod.€€ƒxEAnd they reasoned among themselves, saying: Because we have no bread.€€ƒx˜Which Jesus knowing, saith to them: Why do you reason, because you have no bread? Do you not yet know nor understand? Have you still your heart blinded?€€ƒxQHaving eyes, see you not? And having ears, hear you not? Neither do you remember?€€ƒxyWhen I broke the five loves among five thousand, how many baskets full of fragments took you up? They say to him: Twelve.€€ƒxvWhen also the seven loaves among four thousand, how many baskets of fragments took you up? And they say to him: Seven.€€ƒx3And he said to them: How do you not yet understand?€€ƒxmAnd they came to Bethsaida: and they bring to him a blind man. And they besought him that he would touch him.€€ƒx˜And taking the blind man by the hand, he led him out of the town. And spitting upon his eyes, laying his hands on him, he asked him if he saw any thing.€€ƒx>And looking up, he said: I see men, as it were trees, walking.€€ƒxzAfter that again he laid his hands upon his eyes: and he began to see and was restored, so that he saw all things clearly.€€ƒxhAnd he sent him into his house, saying: Go into thy house, and if thou enter into the town, tell nobody.€€ƒxAnd Jesus went out, and his disciples into the towns of Caesarea Philippi. And in the way, he asked his disciples, saying to them: Whom do men say that I am?€€ƒx^Who answered him, saying: John the Baptist; but some Elias, and others as one of the prophets.€€ƒxgThen he saith to them: But whom do you say that I am? Peter answering said to him: Thou art the Christ.€€ƒxFAnd he strictly charged them that they should not tell any man of him.€€ƒx¾And he began to teach them that the Son of man must suffer many things and be rejected by the ancients and by the high priests and the scribes: and be killed and after three days rise again.€€ƒxGAnd he spoke the word openly. And Peter taking him began to rebuke him.€€ƒx¥Who turning about and seeing his disciples, threatened Peter, saying: Go behind me, Satan, because thou savourest not the things that are of God but that are of men.€€ƒxœAnd calling the multitude together with his disciples, he said to them: If any man will follow me, let him deny himself and take up his cross and follow me.€€ƒx{For whosoever will save his life shall lose it: and whosoever shall lose his life for my sake and the gospel shall save it.€€ƒx[For what shall it profit a man, if he gain the whole world and suffer the loss of his soul?€€ƒx2Or what shall a man give in exchange for his soul:€€ƒxÍFor he that shall be ashamed of me and of my words, in this adulterous and sinful generation: the Son of man also will be ashamed of him, when he shall come in the glory of his Father with the holy angels.€€ƒxAnd he said to them: Amen f say to you that there are some of them that stand here who shall not taste death till they see the kingdom of God coming in power.€€ƒ`€€˜2ƒxgAnd his garments became shining and exceeding white as snow, so as no fuller upon earth can make white.€€ƒxNAnd there appeared to them Elias with Moses: and they were talking with Jesus.€€ƒxŸAnd Peter answering, said to Jesus: Rabbi, it is good for us to be here. And let us make three tabernacles, one for thee, and one for Moses, and one for Elias.€€ƒx=For he knew not what he said: for they were struck with fear.€€ƒx~And there was a cloud overshadowing them. And a voice came out of the cloud, saying: This is my most beloved Son. Hear ye him.€€ƒxRAnd immediately looking about, they saw no man any more, but Jesus only with them.€€ƒxŸAnd as they came down from the mountain, he charged them not to tell any man what things they had seen, till the Son of man shall be risen again from the dead.€€ƒxwAnd they kept the word to themselves; questioning together what that should mean, when he shall be risen from the dead.€€ƒxaAnd they asked him, saying: Why then do the Pharisees and scribes say that Elias must come first?€€ƒx¯Who answering, said to then: Elias, when he shall come first, shall restore all things; and as it is written of the Son of man that he must suffer many things and be despised.€€ƒxtBut I say to you that Elias also is come (and they have done to him whatsoever they would), as it is written of him.€€ƒxdAnd coming to his disciples he saw a great multitude about them and the scribes disputing with them.€€ƒxwAnd presently all the people, seeing Jesus, were astonished and struck with fear: and running to him, they saluted him.€€ƒx8And he asked them: What do you question about among you?€€ƒxgAnd one of the multitude, answering, said: Master, I have brought my son to thee, having a dumb spirit.€€ƒxªWho, wheresoever he taketh him, dasheth him: and he foameth and gnasheth with the teeth and pineth away. And I spoke to thy disciples to cast him out: and they could not.€€ƒxWho answering them, said: O incredulous generation, how long shall I be with you? How long shall I suffer you? Bring him unto me.€€ƒx“And they brought him. And when he had seen him, immediately the spirit troubled him and being thrown down upon the ground, he rolled about foaming.€€ƒxmAnd he asked his father: How long time is it since this hath happened unto him? But he sad: From his infancy.€€ƒx“And oftentimes hath he cast him into the fire and into the waters to destroy him. But if thou canst do any thing, help us, having compassion on us.€€ƒx]And Jesus saith to him: If thou canst believe, all things are possible to him that believeth.€€ƒxhAnd immediately the father of the boy crying out, with tears said: I do believe, Lord. Help my unbelief.€€ƒx¸And when Jesus saw the multitude running together, he threatened the unclean spirit, saying to him: Deaf and dumb spirit, I command thee, go out of him and enter not any more into him.€€ƒxqAnd crying out and greatly tearing him, he went our of him. And he became as dead, so that many said: He is dead.€€ƒx>But Jesus taking him by the hand, lifted him up. And he arose.€€ƒxeAnd when he was come into the house, his disciples secretly asked him: Why could not we cast him out?€€ƒxPAnd he said to them: This kind can go out by nothing, but by prayer and fasting.€€ƒxeAnd departing from thence, they passed through Galilee: and he would not that any man should know it.€€ƒx¾And he taught his disciples and said to them: The Son of man shall be betrayed into the hands of men, and they shall kill him; and after that he is killed, he shall rise again the third day.€€ƒxBBut they understood not the word: and they were afraid to ask him.€€ƒxnAnd they came to Capharnaum. And when they were in the house, he asked them: What did you treat of in the way?€€ƒxsBut they held their peace, for in the way they had disputed among themselves, which of them should be the greatest.€€ƒxŒAnd sitting down, he called the twelve and saith to them: If any man desire to be first, he shall be the last of all and be minister of all.€€ƒxaAnd taking a child, he set him in the midst of them. Whom when he had embraced, he saith to them:€€ƒxWhosoever shall receive one such child as this in my name receiveth me. And whosoever shall receive me receiveth not me but him that sent me.€€ƒxwJohn answered him, saying: Master, we saw one casting out devils in thy name, who followeth not us: and we forbade him.€€ƒxsBut Jesus said: Do not forbid him. For there is no man that doth a miracle in my name and can soon speak ill of me.€€ƒx*For he that is not against you is for you.€€ƒxFor whosoever shall give you to drink a cup of water in my name, because you belong to Christ: amen I say to you, he shall not lose his reward.€€ƒx­And whosoever shall scandalize one of these little ones that believe in me: it were better for him that a millstone were hanged about his neck and he were cast into the sea.€€ƒxAnd if thy hand scandalize thee, cut it off: it is better for thee to enter into life, maimed, than having two hands to go into hell, into unquenchable fire:€€ƒx=Where their worm dieth not, and the fire is not extinguished.€€ƒxªAnd if thy foot scandalize thee, cut it off: it is better for thee to enter lame into life everlasting than having two feet to be cast into the hell of unquenchable fire:€€ƒx=Where their worm dieth not, and the fire is not extinguished.€€ƒx¨And if thy eye scandalize thee, pluck it out: it is better for thee with one eye to enter into the kingdom of God than having two eyes to be cast into the hell of fire:€€ƒx=Where their worm dieth not, and the fire is not extinguished.€€ƒxTFor every one shall be salted with fire: and every victim shall be salted with salt.€€ƒxySalt is good. But if the salt become unsavoury, wherewith will you season it? Have salt in you: and have peace among you.€€ƒ`€€ƒx§And rising up from thence, he cometh into the coast of Judea beyond the Jordan: and the multitude flocked to him again. And as he was accustomed, he taught them again.€€˜4ƒx<But he answering, saith to them: What did Moses command you?€€ƒxIWho said: Moses permitted to write a bill of divorce and to put her away.€€ƒx`To whom Jesus answering, said: Because of the hardness of your heart, he wrote you that precept.€€ƒxFBut from the beginning of the creation, God made them male and female.€€ƒxUFor this cause, a man shall leave his father and mother and shall cleave to his wife.€€ƒxRAnd they two shall be in one flesh. Therefore now they are not two, but one flesh.€€ƒx@What therefore God hath joined together, let no man put asunder.€€ƒxIAnd in the house again his disciples asked him concerning the same thing.€€ƒxjAnd he saith to them: Whosoever shall put away his wife and marry another committeth adultery against her.€€ƒx^And if the wife shall put away her husband and be married to another, she committeth adultery.€€ƒxsAnd they brought to him young children, that he might touch them. And the disciples rebuked them that brought them.€€ƒx¡Whom when Jesus saw, he was much displeased and saith to them: Suffer the little children to come unto me and forbid them not: for of such is the kingdom of God.€€ƒxlAmen I say to you, whosoever shall not receive the kingdom of God as a little child shall not enter into it.€€ƒxCAnd embracing them and laying his hands upon them, he blessed them.€€ƒx¨And when he was gone forth into the way, a certain man, running up and kneeling before him, asked him: Good Master, what shall I do that I may receive life everlasting?€€ƒxSAnd Jesus said to him: Why callest thou me good? None is good but one, that is God.€€ƒx”Thou knowest the commandments: Do not commit adultery, do not kill, do not steal, bear not false witness, do no fraud, honour thy father and mother.€€ƒxVBut he answering, said to him: Master, all these things I have observed from my youth.€€ƒxÆAnd Jesus, looking on him, loved him and said to him: One thing is wanting unto thee. Go, sell whatsoever thou hast and give to the poor: and thou shalt have treasure in heaven. And come, follow me.€€ƒxWWho being struck sad at that saying, went away sorrowful: for he had great possessions.€€ƒx|And Jesus looking round about, saith to his disciples: How hardly shall they that have riches enter into the kingdom of God!€€ƒx²And the disciples were astonished at his words. But Jesus again answering, saith to them: Children, how hard is it for them that trust in riches to enter into the kingdom of God?€€ƒxrIt is easier for a camel to pass through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter into the kingdom of God.€€ƒxFWho wondered the more, saying among themselves: Who then can be saved?€€ƒxsAnd Jesus looking on them, saith with men it is impossible; but not with God. For all things are possible with God.€€ƒxXAnd Peter began to say unto him: Behold, we have left all things and have followed thee.€€ƒx­Jesus answering said: Amen I say to you, there is no man who hath left house or brethren or sisters or father or mother or children or lands, for my sake and for the gospel,€€ƒxÃWho shall not receive an hundred times as much, now in this time: houses and brethren and sisters and mothers and children and lands, with persecutions: and in the world to come life everlasting.€€ƒx;But many that are first shall be last: and the last, first.€€ƒxÕAnd they were in the way going up to Jerusalem: and Jesus went before them. And they were astonished and following were afraid. And taking again the twelve, he began to tell them the things that should befall him.€€ƒxËSaying: Behold we go up to Jerusalem, and the Son of man shall be betrayed to the chief priests and to the scribes and ancients. And they shall condemn him to death and shall deliver him to the Gentiles.€€ƒxlAnd they shall mock him and spit on him and scourge him and kill him: and the third day he shall rise again.€€ƒxˆAnd James and John, the sons of Zebedee, come to him, saying: Master, we desire that whatsoever we shall ask, thou wouldst do it for us.€€ƒx=But he said to them: What would you that I should do for you?€€ƒxoAnd they said: Grant to us that we may sit, one on thy right hand and the other on thy left hand, in thy glory.€€ƒx˜And Jesus said to them: You know not what you ask. Can you drink of the chalice that I drink of or be baptized with the baptism wherewith I am baptized?€€ƒx±But they said to him: We can. And Jesus saith to them: You shall indeed drink of the chalice that I drink of; and with the baptism wherewith I am baptized you shall be baptized.€€ƒxjBut to sit on my right hand or on my left is not mine to give to you, but to them for whom it is prepared.€€ƒxGAnd the ten, hearing it, began to be much displeased at James and John.€€ƒx—But Jesus calling them, saith to them: You know that they who seem to rule over the Gentiles lord it over them: and their princes have power over them.€€ƒxQBut it is not so among you: but whosoever will be greater shall be your minister.€€ƒxBAnd whosoever will be first among you shall be the servant of all.€€ƒxvFor the Son of man also is not come to be ministered unto: but to minister and to give his life a redemption for many.€€ƒx¯And they came to Jericho. And as he went out of Jericho with his disciples and a very great multitude, Bartimeus the blind man, the son of Timeus, sat by the way side begging.€€ƒxxWho when he had heard that it was Jesus of Nazareth, began to cry out and to say: Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me.€€ƒxwAnd many rebuked him, that he might hold his peace; but he cried a great deal the more: Son of David, have mercy on me.€€ƒxAnd Jesus, standing still, commanded him to be called. And they call the blind man, saying to him: Be of better comfort. Arise, he calleth thee.€€ƒx6Who casting off his garment leaped up and came to him.€€ƒx‚And Jesus answering, said to him: What wilt thou that I should do to thee? And the blind man said to him: Rabboni. That I may see.€€ƒxwAnd Jesus saith to him: Go thy way. Thy faith hath made thee whole. And immediately he saw and followed him in the way.€€ƒ`€€ƒxvAnd when they were drawing near to Jerusalem and to Bethania, at the mount of Olives, he sendeth two of his disciples,€€˜!ƒxˆAnd if any man shall say to you: What are you doing? Say ye that the Lord hath need of him. And immediately he will let him come hither.€€ƒxvAnd going their way, they found the colt tied before the gate without, in the meeting of two ways. And they loose him.€€ƒxMAnd some of them that stood there said to them: What do you loosing the colt?€€ƒxLWho said to them as Jesus had commanded them. And they let him go with them.€€ƒx\And they brought the colt to Jesus. And they lay their garments on him: and he sat upon him.€€ƒxqAnd many spread their garments in the way: and others cut down boughs from the trees and strewed them in the way.€€ƒx{And they that went before and they that followed cried, saying: Hosanna: Blessed is he that cometh in the name of the Lord.€€ƒxOBlessed be the kingdom of our father David that cometh: Hosanna in the highest.€€ƒx¢And he entered into Jerusalem, into the temple: and having viewed all things round about, when now the eventide was come, he went out to Bethania with the twelve.€€ƒxAAnd the next day when they came out from Bethania, he was hungry.€€ƒxÅAnd when he had seen afar off a fig tree having leaves, he came, if perhaps he might find any thing on it. And when he was come to it, he found nothing but leaves. For it was not the time for figs.€€ƒxrAnd answering he said to it: May no man hereafter eat fruit of thee any more for ever! And his disciples heard it.€€ƒxÙAnd they came to Jerusalem. And when he was entered into the temple, he began to cast out them that sold and bought in the temple: and over threw the tables of the moneychangers and the chairs of them that sold doves.€€ƒxJAnd he suffered not that any man should carry a vessel through the temple.€€ƒx•And he taught, saying to them: Is it not written: My house shall be called the house of prayer to all nations, but you have made it a den of thieves.€€ƒx³Which when the chief priests and the scribes had heard, they sought how they might destroy him. For they feared him, because the whole multitude was in admiration at his doctrine.€€ƒx9And when evening was come, he went forth out of the city.€€ƒxUAnd when they passed by in the morning they saw the fig tree dried up from the roots.€€ƒxgAnd Peter remembering, said to him: Rabbi, behold the fig tree which thou didst curse is withered away.€€ƒx:And Jesus answering, saith to them: Have the faith of God.€€ƒxÜAmen I say to you that whosoever shall say to this mountain, Be thou removed and be cast into the sea, and shall not stagger in his heart, but be believe that whatsoever he saith shall be done; it shall be done unto him.€€ƒx„Therefore I say unto you, all things, whatsoever you ask when ye pray, believe that you shall receive: and they shall come unto you.€€ƒx‘And when you shall stand to pray, forgive, if you have aught against any man: that your Father also, who is in heaven, may forgive you your sins.€€ƒx^But if you will not forgive, neither will your father that is in heaven forgive you your sins.€€ƒxAnd they come again to Jerusalem. And when he was walking in the temple, there come to him the chief priests and the scribes and the ancients.€€ƒx‰And they say to him: By what authority dost thou these things? And who hath given thee this authority that thou shouldst do these things?€€ƒxŒAnd Jesus answering, said to them: I will also ask you one word. And answer you me: and I will tell you by what authority I do these things.€€ƒx?The baptism of John, was it from heaven or from men? Answer me.€€ƒxpBut they thought with themselves, saying: If we say, From heaven; he will say, Why then did you not believe him?€€ƒx_If we say, From men, we fear the people. For all men counted John that he was a prophet indeed.€€ƒxAnd they answering, say to Jesus: We know not. And Jesus answering, saith to them: Neither do I tell you by what authority I do these things.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÎAnd he began to speak to them in parables: A certain man planted a vineyard and made a hedge about it and dug a place for the winefat and built a tower and let it to husbandmen: and went into a far country.€€˜,ƒx<Who, having laid hands on him, beat and sent him away empty.€€ƒxgAnd again he sent to them another servant: and him they wounded in the head and used him reproachfully.€€ƒxpAnd again he sent another, and him they killed: and many others, of whom some they beat, and others they killed.€€ƒx|Therefore, having yet one son, most dear to him, he also sent him unto them last of all, saying: They will reverence my son.€€ƒxqBut the husbandmen said one to another: This is the heir. Come let us kill him and the inheritance shall be ours.€€ƒxIAnd laying hold on him, they killed him and cast him out of the vineyard.€€ƒx€What therefore will the lord of the vineyard do? He will come and destroy those husbandmen and will give the vineyard to others.€€ƒxuAnd have you not read this scripture, The stone which the builders rejected, the same is made the head of the corner:€€ƒx@By the Lord has this been done, and it is wonderful in our eyes.€€ƒx˜And they sought to lay hands on him: but they feared the people. For they knew that he spoke this parable to them. And leaving him, they went their way.€€ƒxiAnd they sent to him some of the Pharisees and of the Herodians: that they should catch him in his words.€€ƒxñWho coming, say to him: Master, we know that thou art a true speaker and carest not for any man; for thou regardest not the person of men, but teachest the way of God in truth. Is it lawful to give tribute to Caesar? Or shall we not give it?€€ƒx`Who knowing their wiliness, saith to them: Why tempt you me? Bring me a penny that I may see it.€€ƒxnAnd they brought it him. And he saith to them: Whose is this image and inscription? They say to him, Caesar's.€€ƒx›And Jesus answering, said to them: Render therefore to Caesar the things that are Caesar's and to God the things that are God's. And they marvelled at him.€€ƒxbAnd there came to him the Sadducees, who say there is no resurrection. And they asked him, saying:€€ƒx±Master, Moses wrote unto us that if any man's brother die and leave his wife behind him and leave no children, his brother should take his wife and raise up seed to his brother.€€ƒxSNow there were seven brethren: and the first took a wife and died leaving no issue.€€ƒxcAnd the second took her and died: and neither did he leave any issue. And the third in like manner.€€ƒxcAnd the seven all took her in like manner and did not leave issue. Last of all the woman also died.€€ƒxzIn the resurrection therefore, when they shall rise again, whose wife shall she be of them? For the seven had her to wife.€€ƒxvAnd Jesus answering, saith to them: Do ye not therefore err, because you know not the scriptures nor the power of God?€€ƒxxFor when they shall rise again from the dead, they shall neither marry, nor be married, but are as the angels in heaven.€€ƒxÄAnd as concerning the dead that they rise again have you not read in the book of Moses, how in the bush God spoke to him, saying: I am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob?€€ƒxOHe is not the God of the dead, but of the living. You therefore do greatly err.€€ƒx¦And there came one of the scribes that had heard them reasoning together, and seeing that he had answered them well, asked him which was the first commandment of all.€€ƒxeAnd Jesus answered him: The first commandment of all is, Hear, O Israel: the Lord thy God is one God.€€ƒx¥And thou shalt love the Lord thy God with thy whole heart and with thy whole soul and with thy whole mind and with thy whole strength. This is the first commandment.€€ƒxyAnd the second is like to it: Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself. There is no other commandment greater than these.€€ƒxzAnd the scribe said to him: Well, Master, thou hast said in truth that there is one God and there is no other besides him.€€ƒxçAnd that he should be loved with the whole heart and with the whole understanding and with the whole soul and with the whole strength. And to love one's neighbour as one's self is a greater thing than all holocausts and sacrifices.€€ƒx–And Jesus seeing that he had answered wisely, said to him: Thou art not far from the kingdom of God. And no man after that durst ask him any question.€€ƒxjAnd Jesus answering, said, teaching in the temple: How do the scribes say that Christ is the son of David?€€ƒx‚For David himself saith by the Holy Ghost: The Lord said to my Lord: Sit on my right hand, until I make thy enemies thy footstool.€€ƒxpDavid therefore himself calleth him Lord. And whence is he then his son? And a great multitude heard him gladly.€€ƒx€And he said to them in his doctrine: Beware of the scribes, who love to walk in long robes and to be saluted in the marketplace,€€ƒx[And to sit in the first chairs in the synagogues and to have the highest places at suppers:€€ƒxhWho devour the houses of widows under the pretence of long prayer. These shall receive greater judgment.€€ƒx†And Jesus sitting over against the treasury, beheld how the people cast money into the treasury. And many that were rich cast in much.€€ƒxVAnd there came a certain poor widow: and she cast in two mites, which make a farthing.€€ƒx™And calling his disciples together, he saith to them: Amen I say to you, this poor widow hath cast in more than all they who have cast into the treasury.€€ƒxlFor all they did cast in of their abundance; but she of her want cast in all she had, even her whole living.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŠAnd as he was going out of the temple, one of his disciples said to him: Master, behold what manner of stones and what buildings are here.€€˜%ƒxrAnd as he sat on the mount of Olivet over against the temple, Peter and James and John and Andrew asked him apart:€€ƒxqTell us, when shall these things be and what shall be the sign when all these things shall begin to be fulfilled?€€ƒxNAnd Jesus answering, began to say to them: Take heed lest any man deceive you.€€ƒxLFor many shall come in my name saying, I am he: and they shall deceive many.€€ƒxxAnd when you shall hear of wars and rumours of wars, fear ye not. For such things must needs be: but the end is not yet.€€ƒx©For nation shall rise against nation and kingdom against kingdom: and there shall be earthquakes in divers places and famines. These things are the beginning of sorrows.€€ƒxÄBut look to yourselves. For they shall deliver you Up to councils: and in the synagogues you shall be beaten: and you shall stand before governors and kings for my sake, for a testimony unto them.€€ƒx7And unto all nations the gospel must first be preached.€€ƒxÔAnd when they shall lead you and deliver you up, be not thoughtful beforehand what you shall speak: but whatsoever shall be given you in that hour, that speak ye. For it is not you that speak, but the Holy Ghost.€€ƒx™And the brother shall betray his brother unto death, and the father his son; and children shall rise up against their parents and shall work their death.€€ƒxoAnd you shall be hated by all men for my name's sake. But he that shall endure unto the end, he shall be saved.€€ƒx°And when you shall see the abomination of desolation, standing where it ought not (he that readeth let him understand): then let them that are in Judea flee unto the mountains.€€ƒxtAnd let him that is on the housetop not go down into the house nor enter therein to take any thing out of the house.€€ƒxLAnd let him that shall be in the field not turn back to take up his garment.€€ƒxEAnd woe to them that are with child and that give suck in those days.€€ƒx3But pray ye that these things happen not in winter.€€ƒxŠFor in those days shall be such tribulations as were not from the beginning of the creation which God created until now: neither shall be.€€ƒx–And unless the Lord had shortened the days, no flesh should be saved: but, for the sake of the elect which he hath chosen, he hath shortened the days.€€ƒxYAnd then if any man shall say to you: Lo, here is Christ. Lo, he is here: do not believe.€€ƒxFor there will rise up false Christs and false prophets: and they shall shew signs and wonders, to seduce (if it were possible) even the elect.€€ƒx@Take you heed therefore: behold, I have foretold you all things.€€ƒxkBut in those days, after that tribulation, the sun shall be darkened and the moon shall not give her light.€€ƒx_And the stars of heaven shall be falling down and the powers that are in heaven shall be moved.€€ƒxXAnd then shall they see the Son of man coming in the clouds, with great power and glory.€€ƒx And then shall he send his angels and shall gather together his elect from the four winds, from the uttermost part of the earth to the uttermost part of heaven.€€ƒxNow of the fig tree learn ye a parable. When the branch thereof is now tender and the leaves are come forth, you know that summer is very near.€€ƒxjSo you also when you shall see these things come to pass, know ye that it is very nigh, even at the doors.€€ƒxVAmen, I say to you that this generation shall not pass until all these things be done.€€ƒxBHeaven and earth shall pass away: but my word shall not pass away.€€ƒxbBut of that day or hour no man knoweth, neither the angels in heaven, nor the Son, but the Father.€€ƒx?Take ye heed, watch and pray. For ye know not when the time is.€€ƒx‘Even as a man who, going into a far country, left his house and gave authority to his servants over every work and commanded the porter to watch.€€ƒxŒWatch ye therefore (for you know not when the lord of the house cometh, at even, or at midnight, or at the cock crowing, or in the morning):€€ƒx.Lest coming on a sudden, he find you sleeping.€€ƒx+And what I say to you, I say to all: Watch.€€ƒ`€€ƒx§Now the feast of the pasch and of the Azymes was after two days: and the chief priests and the scribes sought how they might by some wile lay hold on him and kill him.€€˜HƒxİAnd when he was in Bethania, in the house of Simon the leper, and was at meat, there came a woman having an alabaster box of ointment of precious spikenard. And breaking the alabaster box, she poured it out upon his head.€€ƒxmNow there were some that had indignation within themselves and said: Why was this waste of the ointment made?€€ƒx~For this ointment might have been sold for more than three hundred pence and given to the poor. And they murmured against her.€€ƒx[But Jesus said: Let her alone. Why do You molest her? She hath wrought a good work upon me.€€ƒxqFor the poor you have always with you: and whensoever you will, you may do them good: but me you have not always.€€ƒxVShe hath done what she could: she is come beforehand to anoint my body for the burial.€€ƒx”Amen, I say to you, wheresoever this gospel shall be preached in the whole world, that also which she hath done shall be told for a memorial of her.€€ƒxXAnd Judas Iscariot, one of the twelve, went to the chief priests, to betray him to them.€€ƒx~Who hearing it were glad: and they promised him they would give him money. And he sought how he might conveniently betray him.€€ƒx«Now on the first day of the unleavened bread, when they sacrificed the pasch, the disciples say to him: Whither wilt thou that we go and prepare for thee to eat the pasch?€€ƒx“And he sendeth two of his disciples and saith to them: Go ye into the city; and there shall meet you a man carrying a pitcher of water. Follow him.€€ƒx—And whithersoever he shall go in, say to the master of the house, The master saith, Where is my refectory, where I may eat the pasch with my disciples?€€ƒxPAnd he will shew you a large dining room furnished. And there prepare ye for us.€€ƒxyAnd his disciples went their way and came into the city. And they found as he had told them: and they prepared the pasch.€€ƒx5And when evening was come, he cometh with the twelve.€€ƒxwAnd when they were at table and eating, Jesus saith: Amen I say to you, one of you that eateth with me shall betray me.€€ƒxFBut they began to be sorrowful and to say to him, one by one: Is it I?€€ƒxOWho saith to them: One of the twelve, who dippeth with me his hand in the dish.€€ƒx®And the Son of man indeed goeth, as it is written of him: but woe to that man by whom the Son of man shall be betrayed. It were better for him, if that man had not been born.€€ƒxwAnd whilst they were eating, Jesus took bread; and blessing, broke and gave to them and said: Take ye. This is my body.€€ƒxZAnd having taken the chalice, giving thanks, he gave it to them. And they all drank of it.€€ƒxYAnd he said to them: This is my blood of the new testament, which shall be shed for many.€€ƒx„Amen I say to you that I will drink no more of the fruit of the vine until that day when I shall drink it new in the kingdom of God.€€ƒxGAnd when they had sung an hymn, they went forth to the mount of Olives.€€ƒxAnd Jesus saith to them: You will all be scandalized in my regard this night. For it is written: I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep shall be dispersed.€€ƒxDBut after I shall be risen again, I will go before you into Galilee.€€ƒxMBut Peter saith to him: Although all shall be scandalized in thee, yet not I.€€ƒx~And Jesus saith to him: Amen I say to thee, to-day, even in this night, before the cock crow twice, thou shalt deny me thrice.€€ƒxˆBut he spoke the more vehemently: Although I should die together with thee, I will not deny thee. And in like manner also said they all.€€ƒxeAnd they came to a farm called Gethsemani. And he saith to his disciples: Sit you here, while I pray.€€ƒxVAnd he taketh Peter and James and John with him: and he began to fear and to be heavy.€€ƒxTAnd he saith to them: My soul is sorrowful even unto death. Stay you here and watch.€€ƒx„And when he was gone forward a little, he fell flat on the ground: and he prayed that, if it might be, the hour might pass from him.€€ƒx‚And he saith: Abba, Father, all things are possible to thee: remove this chalice from me; but not what I will, but what thou wilt.€€ƒxvAnd he cometh and findeth them sleeping. And he saith to Peter: Simon, sleepest thou? Couldst thou not watch one hour?€€ƒxkWatch ye: and pray that you enter not into temptation. The spirit indeed is willing, but the flesh is weak.€€ƒx7And going away again, he prayed, saying the same words.€€ƒxsAnd when he returned, he found them again asleep (for their eyes were heavy): and they knew not what to answer him.€€ƒx³And he cometh the third time and saith to them: Sleep ye now and take your rest. It is enough. The hour is come: behold the Son of man shall be betrayed into the hands of sinners.€€ƒx>Rise up: let us go. Behold, he that will betray me is at hand.€€ƒx¸And while he was yet speaking, cometh Judas Iscariot, one of the twelve: and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and the scribes and the ancients.€€ƒxŠAnd he that betrayed him had given them a sign, saying: Whomsoever I shall kiss, that is he. Lay hold on him: and lead him away carefully.€€ƒx[And when he was come, immediately going up to him he saith: Hail, Rabbi! And he kissed him.€€ƒx(But they laid hands on him and held him.€€ƒxiAnd one of them that stood by, drawing a sword, struck a servant of the chief priest and cut off his ear.€€ƒxkAnd Jesus answering, said to them: Are you come out as to a robber, with swords and staves to apprehend me?€€ƒxwI was daily with you in the temple teaching: and you did not lay hands on me. But that the scriptures may be fulfilled.€€ƒx/Then his disciples, leaving him, all fled away.€€ƒxpAnd a certain young man followed him, having a linen cloth cast about his naked body. And they laid hold on him.€€ƒx:But he, casting off the linen cloth, fled from them naked.€€ƒxsAnd they brought Jesus to the high priest. And all the priests and the scribes and the ancients assembled together.€€ƒx…And Peter followed him afar off, even into the court of the high priest. And he sat with the servants at the fire and warmed himself.€€ƒx~And the chief priests and all the council sought for evidence against Jesus, that they might put him to death: and found none.€€ƒxOFor many bore false witness against him: and their evidences were not agreeing.€€ƒx;And some rising up, bore false witness against him, saying:€€ƒx|We heard him say, I Will destroy this temple made with hands and within three days I will build another not made with hands.€€ƒx And their witness did not agree.€€ƒxAnd the high priest rising up in the midst, asked Jesus, saying: Answerest thou nothing to the things that are laid to thy charge by these men?€€ƒxBut he held his peace and answered nothing. Again the high priest asked him and said to him: Art thou the Christ, the Son of the Blessed God?€€ƒx‘And Jesus said to him: I am. And you shall see the Son of man sitting on the right hand of the power of God and coming with the clouds of heaven.€€ƒxUThen the high priest rending his garments, saith: What need we any further witnesses?€€ƒxZYou have heard the blasphemy. What think you? Who all condemned him to be guilty of death.€€ƒxœAnd some began to spit on him and to cover his face and to buffet him and to say unto him: Prophesy. And the servants struck him with the palms their hands.€€ƒx_Now when Peter was in the court below, there cometh one of the maidservants of the high priest.€€ƒxmAnd when she had seen Peter warming himself looking on him, she saith: Thou also wast with Jesus of Nazareth.€€ƒx}But he denied, saying: I neither know nor understand what thou sayest. And he went forth before the court; and the cock crew.€€ƒxYAnd again a maidservant seeing him, began to say to the standers by: This is one of them.€€ƒxŠBut he denied again. And after a, while they that stood by said again to Peter: Surely thou art one of them; for thou art also a Galilean.€€ƒxQBut he began o curse and to swear, saying: I know not this man of whom you speak.€€ƒx­And immediately the cock crew again. And Peter remembered the word that Jesus had said unto him: Before the cock crow twice, thou shalt thrice deny me. And he began to weep,€€ƒ`€€ƒxºAnd straightway in the morning, the chief priests holding a consultation with the ancients and the scribes and the whole council, binding Jesus, led him away and delivered him to Pilate.€€˜/ƒx1And the chief priests accused him in many things.€€ƒxgAnd Pilate again asked him, saying: Answerest thou nothing? Behold in how many things they accuse thee.€€ƒx:But Jesus still answered nothing: so that Pilate wondered.€€ƒxhNow on the festival day he was wont to release unto them one of the prisoners, whomsoever they demanded.€€ƒx{And there was one called Barabbas, who was put in prison with some seditious men, who in the sedition had committed murder.€€ƒxhAnd when the multitude was come up, they began to desire that he would do as he had ever done unto them.€€ƒxWAnd Pilate answered them and said: Will you that I release to you the king of the Jews?€€ƒxDFor he knew that the chief priests had delivered him up out of envy.€€ƒxWBut the chief priests moved the people, that he should rather release Barabbas to them.€€ƒx`And Pilate again answering, saith to them: What will you then that I do to the king of the Jews?€€ƒx&But they again cried out: Crucify him.€€ƒx`And Pilate saith to them: Why, what evil hath he done? But they cried out the more: Crucify him.€€ƒxAnd so Pilate being willing to satisfy the people, released to them Barabbas: and delivered up Jesus, when he had scourged him, to be crucified.€€ƒxdAnd the soldiers led him away into the court of the palace: and they called together the whole band.€€ƒxXAnd they clothed him with purple: and, platting a crown of thorns, they put it upon him.€€ƒx5And they began to salute him: Hail, king of the Jews.€€ƒxhAnd they struck his head with a reed: and they did spit on him. And bowing their knees, they adored him.€€ƒx†And after they had mocked him, they took off the purple from him and put his own garments on him: and they led him out to crucify him.€€ƒxŠAnd they forced one Simon a Cyrenian, who passed by coming out of the country, the father of Alexander and of Rufus, to take up his cross.€€ƒxdAnd they bring him into the place called Golgotha, which being interpreted is, The place of Calvary.€€ƒxGAnd they gave him to drink wine mingled with myrrh. But he took it not.€€ƒxbAnd crucifying him, they divided his garments, casting lots upon them, what every man should take.€€ƒx2And it was the third hour: and they crucified him.€€ƒxHAnd the inscription of his cause was written over: THE KING OF THE JEWS.€€ƒx\And with him they crucify two thieves: the one on his right hand, and the other on his left.€€ƒxQAnd the scripture was fulfilled, which saith: And with the wicked he was reputed.€€ƒx›And they that passed by blasphemed him, wagging their heads and saying: Vah, thou that destroyest the temple of God and in three days buildest it up again:€€ƒx)Save thyself, coming down from the cross.€€ƒx~In like manner also the chief priests, mocking, said with the scribes one to another: He saved others; himself he cannot save.€€ƒxŒLet Christ the king of Israel come down now from the cross, that we may see and believe. And they that were crucified with him, reviled him.€€ƒx_And when the sixth hour was come, there was darkness over the whole earth until the ninth hour.€€ƒx§And at the ninth hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying: Eloi, Eloi, lamma sabacthani? Which is, being interpreted: My God, My God, Why hast thou forsaken me?€€ƒxCAnd some of the standers by hearing, said: Behold he calleth Elias.€€ƒx™And one running and filling a sponge with vinegar and putting it upon a reed, gave him to drink, saying: Stay, let us see if Elias come to take him down.€€ƒxAAnd Jesus, having cried out with a loud voice, gave up the ghost.€€ƒxGAnd the veil of the temple was rent in two, from the top to the bottom.€€ƒx˜And the centurion who stood over against him, seeing that crying out in this manner he had given up the ghost. said: Indeed this man was the son of God.€€ƒx‹And there were also women looking on afar off: among whom was Mary Magdalen and Mary the mother of James the Less and of Joseph and Salome,€€ƒx|Who also when he was in Galilee followed him and ministered to him, and many other women that came up with him to Jerusalem.€€ƒxbAnd when evening was now come (because it was the Parasceve, that is, the day before the sabbath),€€ƒxJoseph of Arimathea, a noble counsellor, who was also himself looking for the kingdom of God, came and went in boldly to Pilate and begged the body of Jesus.€€ƒxxBut Pilate wondered that he should be already dead. And sending for the centurion, he asked him if he were already dead.€€ƒxKAnd when he had understood it by the centurion, he gave the body to Joseph.€€ƒxÂAnd Joseph, buying fine linen and taking him down, wrapped him up in the fine linen and laid him in a sepulchre which was hewed out of a rock. And he rolled a stone to the door of the sepulchre.€€ƒxJAnd Mary Magdalen and Mary the mother of Joseph, beheld where he was laid.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAnd when the sabbath was past, Mary Magdalen and Mary the mother of James and Salome bought sweet spices, that coming, they might anoint Jesus.€€”ƒx^And they said one to another: Who shall roll us back the stone from the door of the sepulchre?€€ƒxCAnd looking, they saw the stone rolled back. For it was very great.€€ƒx…And entering into the sepulchre, they saw a young man sitting on the right side, clothed with a white robe: and they were astonished.€€ƒx—Who saith to them: Be not affrighted. you seek Jesus of Nazareth, who was crucified. He is risen: he is not here. Behold the place where they laid him.€€ƒxtBut go, tell his disciples and Peter that he goeth before you into Galilee. There you shall see him, as he told you.€€ƒxBut they going out, fled from the sepulchre: for a trembling and fear had seized them. And they said nothing to any man: for they were afraid.€€ƒxuBut he rising early the first day of the week, appeared first to Mary Magdalen; out of whom he had cast seven devils.€€ƒxMShe went and told them that had been with him, who were mourning and weeping.€€ƒxMAnd they hearing that he was alive and had been seen by her, did not believe.€€ƒxhAnd after that he appeared in another shape to two of them walking, as they were going into the country.€€ƒxBAnd they going told it to the rest: neither did they believe them.€€ƒxËAt length he appeared to the eleven as they were at table: and he upbraided them with their incredulity and hardness of heart, because they did not believe them who had seen him after he was risen again.€€ƒxXAnd he said to them: Go ye into the whole world and preach the gospel to every creature.€€ƒx_He that believeth and is baptized shall be saved: but he that believeth not shall he condemned.€€ƒxyAnd these signs shall follow them that believe: In my name they shall cast out devils. They shall speak with new tongues.€€ƒxŸThey shall take up serpents: and if they shall drink any deadly thing, it shall not hurt them. They shall lay their hand upon the sick: and they shall recover.€€ƒxoAnd the Lord Jesus, after he had spoken to them, was taken up into heaven and sitteth on the right hand of God.€€ƒxuBut they going forth preached every where: the Lord working withal, and confirming the word with signs that followed.€€ƒrThe Gospel of Luke€€ƒ`€€dLuke¤dnamedLukeiosis_namedLukelabbreviationdLukehchapters˜˜PƒxIt seemed good to me also, having diligently attained to all things from the beginning, to write to thee in order, most excellent Theophilus,€€ƒxSThat thou mayest know the verity of those words in which thou hast been instructed.€€ƒx­There was in the days of Herod, the king of Judea, a certain priest named Zachary, of the course of Abia: and his wife was of the daughters of Aaron, and her name Elizabeth.€€ƒxqAnd they were both just before God, walking in all the commandments and justifications of the Lord without blame.€€ƒx^And they had no son, for that Elizabeth was barren: and they both were well advanced in years.€€ƒxbAnd it came to pass, when he executed the priestly function in the order of his course before God,€€ƒxsAccording to the custom of the priestly office, it was his lot to offer incense, going into the temple of the Lord.€€ƒxPAnd all the multitude of the people was praying without, at the hour of incense.€€ƒxcAnd there appeared to him an angel of the Lord, standing on the right side of the altar of incense.€€ƒx=And Zachary seeing him, was troubled: and fear fell upon him.€€ƒx—But the angel said to him: Fear not, Zachary, for thy prayer is heard: and thy wife Elizabeth shall bear thee a son. And thou shalt call his name John.€€ƒxMAnd thou shalt have joy and gladness: and many shall rejoice in his nativity.€€ƒx˜For he shall be great before the Lord and shall drink no wine nor strong drink: and he shall be filled with the Holy Ghost, even from his mother's womb.€€ƒxJAnd he shall convert many of the children of Israel to the Lord their God.€€ƒxÓAnd he shall go before him in the spirit and power of Elias: that he may turn the hearts of the fathers unto the children and the incredulous to the wisdom of the just, to prepare unto the Lord a perfect people.€€ƒxpAnd Zachary said to the angel: Whereby shall I know this? For I am an old man, and my wife is advanced in years.€€ƒx‹And the angel answering, said to him: I am Gabriel, who stand before God and am sent to speak to thee and to bring thee these good tidings.€€ƒxÅAnd behold, thou shalt be dumb and shalt not be able to speak until the day wherein these things shall come to pass: because thou hast not believed my words, which shall be fulfilled in their time.€€ƒxaAnd the people were waiting for Zachary: and they wondered that he tarried so long in the temple.€€ƒx›And when he came out, he could not speak to them: and they understood that he had seen a vision in the temple. And he made signs to them and remained dumb.€€ƒxbAnd it came to pass, after the days of his office were accomplished, he departed to his own house.€€ƒxWAnd after those days, Elizabeth his wife conceived and hid herself five months, saying:€€ƒxkThus hath the Lord dealt with me in the days wherein he hath had regard to take away my reproach among men.€€ƒxdAnd in the sixth month, the angel Gabriel was sent from God into a city of Galilee, called Nazareth,€€ƒxkTo a virgin espoused to a man whose name was Joseph, of the house of David: and the virgin's name was Mary.€€ƒxuAnd the angel being come in, said unto her: Hail, full of grace, the Lord is with thee: blessed art thou among women.€€ƒxoWho having heard, was troubled at his saying and thought with herself what manner of salutation this should be.€€ƒxNAnd the angel said to her: Fear not, Mary, for thou hast found grace with God.€€ƒxgBehold thou shalt conceive in thy womb and shalt bring forth a son: and thou shalt call his name Jesus.€€ƒx·He shall be great and shall be called the Son of the Most High. And the Lord God shall give unto him the throne of David his father: and he shall reign in the house of Jacob for ever.€€ƒx)And of his kingdom there shall be no end.€€ƒxKAnd Mary said to the angel: How shall this be done, because I know not man?€€ƒx×And the angel answering, said to her: The Holy Ghost shall come upon thee and the power of the Most High shall overshadow thee. And therefore also the Holy which shall be born of thee shall be called the Son of God.€€ƒxŠAnd behold thy cousin Elizabeth, she also hath conceived a son in her old age: and this is the sixth month with her that is called barren.€€ƒx-Because no word shall be impossible with God.€€ƒxxAnd Mary said: Behold the handmaid of the Lord: be it done to me according to thy word. And the angel departed from her.€€ƒx\And Mary rising up in those days, went into the hill country with haste into a city of Juda.€€ƒx@And she entered into the house of Zachary and saluted Elizabeth.€€ƒx’And it came to pass that when Elizabeth heard the salutation of Mary, the infant leaped in her womb. And Elizabeth was filled with the Holy Ghost.€€ƒxpAnd she cried out with a loud voice and said: Blessed art thou among women and blessed is the fruit of thy womb.€€ƒxFAnd whence is this to me that the mother of my Lord should come to me?€€ƒxkFor behold as soon as the voice of thy salutation sounded in my ears, the infant in my womb leaped for joy.€€ƒxyAnd blessed art thou that hast believed, because those things shall be accomplished that were spoken to thee by the Lord.€€ƒx-And Mary said: My soul doth magnify the Lord.€€ƒx.And my spirit hath rejoiced in God my Saviour.€€ƒxxBecause he hath regarded the humility of his handmaid: for behold from henceforth all generations shall call me blessed.€€ƒxMBecause he that is mighty hath done great things to me: and holy is his name.€€ƒxIAnd his mercy is from generation unto generations, to them that fear him.€€ƒx[He hath shewed might in his arm: he hath scattered the proud in the conceit of their heart.€€ƒxHHe hath put down the mighty from their seat and hath exalted the humble.€€ƒxQHe hath filled the hungry with good things: and the rich he hath sent empty away.€€ƒx@He hath received Israel his servant, being mindful of his mercy.€€ƒx@As he spoke to our fathers: to Abraham and to his seed for ever.€€ƒxNAnd Mary abode with her about three months. And she returned to her own house.€€ƒxSNow Elizabeth's full time of being delivered was come: and she brought forth a son.€€ƒx|And her neighbors and kinsfolks heard that the Lord had shewed his great mercy towards her: and they congratulated with her.€€ƒxAnd it came to pass that on the eighth day they came to circumcise the child: and they called him by his father's name Zachary.€€ƒxDAnd his mother answering, said: Not so. But he shall be called John.€€ƒxOAnd they said to her: There is none of thy kindred that is called by this name.€€ƒx@And they made signs to his father, how he would have him called.€€ƒxYAnd demanding a writing table, he wrote, saying: John is his name. And they all wondered.€€ƒxWAnd immediately his mouth was opened and his tongue loosed: and he spoke, blessing God.€€ƒxtAnd fear came upon all their neighbours: and all these things were noised abroad over all the hill country of Judea.€€ƒx˜And all they that had heard them laid them up in their heart, saying: What an one, think ye, shall this child be? For the hand of the Lord was with him.€€ƒxQAnd Zachary his father was filled with the Holy Ghost. And he prophesied, saying:€€ƒxdBlessed be the Lord God of Israel: because he hath visited and wrought the redemption of his people.€€ƒxQAnd hath raised up an horn of salvation to us, in the house of David his servant.€€ƒxJAs he spoke by the mouth of his holy prophets, who are from the beginning.€€ƒxASalvation from our enemies and from the hand of all that hate us.€€ƒxCTo perform mercy to our fathers and to remember his holy testament.€€ƒxJThe oath, which he swore to Abraham our father, that he would grant to us.€€ƒxQThat being delivered from the hand of our enemies, we may serve him without fear:€€ƒx1In holiness and justice before him, all our days.€€ƒx€And thou, child, shalt be called the prophet of the Highest: for thou shalt, go before the face of the Lord to prepare his ways:€€ƒxOTo give knowledge of salvation to his people, unto the remission of their sins.€€ƒx]Through the bowels of the mercy of our God, in which the Orient from on high hath visited us:€€ƒxlTo enlighten them that sit in darkness and in the shadow of death: to direct our feet into the way of peace.€€ƒxwAnd the child grew and was strengthened in spirit: and was in the deserts until the day of his manifestation to Israel.€€ƒ`€€ƒx|And it came to pass that in those days there went out a decree from Caesar Augustus that the whole world should be enrolled.€€˜4ƒx9And all went to be enrolled, every one into his own city.€€ƒx°And Joseph also went up from Galilee, out of the city of Nazareth, into Judea, to the city of David, which is called Bethlehem: because he was of the house and family of David.€€ƒx?To be enrolled with Mary his espoused wife, who was with child.€€ƒxgAnd it came to pass that when they were there, her days were accomplished that she should be delivered.€€ƒx™And she brought forth her first born son and wrapped him up in swaddling clothes and laid him in a manger: because there was no room for them in the inn.€€ƒxeAnd there were in the same country shepherds watching and keeping the night watches over their flock.€€ƒx‚And behold an angel of the Lord stood by them and the brightness of God shone round about them: and they feared with a great fear.€€ƒxyAnd the angel said to them: Fear not; for, behold, I bring you good tidings of great joy that shall be to all the people:€€ƒxUFor, this day is born to you a Saviour, who is Christ the Lord, in the city of David.€€ƒxoAnd this shall be a sign unto you. You shall find the infant wrapped in swaddling clothes and laid in a manger.€€ƒx`And suddenly there was with the angel a multitude of the heavenly army, praising God and saying:€€ƒxDGlory to God in the highest: and on earth peace to men of good will.€€ƒxÕAnd it came to pass, after the angels departed from them into heaven, the shepherds said one to another: Let us go over to Bethlehem and let us see this word that is come to pass, which the Lord hath shewed to us.€€ƒx]And they came with haste: and they found Mary and Joseph, and the infant lying in the manger.€€ƒx[And seeing, they understood of the word that had been spoken to them concerning this child.€€ƒxVAnd all that heard wondered: and at those things that were told them by the shepherds.€€ƒx;But Mary kept all these words, pondering them in her heart.€€ƒx}And the shepherds returned, glorifying and praising God for all the things they had heard and seen, as it was told unto them.€€ƒx«And after eight days were accomplished, that the child should be circumcised, his name was called JESUS, which was called by the angel before he was conceived in the womb.€€ƒx”And after the days of her purification, according to the law of Moses, were accomplished, they carried him to Jerusalem, to present him to the Lord:€€ƒxfAs it is written in the law of the Lord: Every male opening the womb shall be called holy to the Lord:€€ƒxxAnd to offer a sacrifice, according as it is written in the law of the Lord, a pair of turtledoves or two young pigeons:€€ƒxAnd behold there was a man in Jerusalem named Simeon: and this man was just and devout, waiting for the consolation of Israel. And the Holy Ghost was in him.€€ƒxzAnd he had received an answer from the Holy Ghost, that he should not see death before he had seen the Christ of the Lord.€€ƒxAnd he came by the Spirit into the temple. And when his parents brought in the child Jesus, to do for him according to the custom of the law,€€ƒx7He also took him into his arms and blessed God and said€€ƒxJNow thou dost dismiss thy servant, O Lord, according to thy word in peace:€€ƒx(Because my eyes have seen thy salvation,€€ƒx8Which thou hast prepared before the face of all peoples:€€ƒxMA light to the revelation of the Gentiles and the glory of thy people Israel.€€ƒxZAnd his father and mother were wondering at those things which were spoken concerning him.€€ƒx±And Simeon blessed them and said to Mary his mother: Behold this child is set for the fall and for the resurrection of many in Israel and for a sign which shall be contradicted.€€ƒxYAnd thy own soul a sword shall pierce, that, out of many hearts thoughts may be revealed.€€ƒx±And there was one Anna, a prophetess, the daughter of Phanuel, of the tribe of Aser. She was far advanced in years and had lived with her husband seven years from her virginity.€€ƒx„And she was a widow until fourscore and four years: who departed not from the temple, by fastings and prayers serving night and day.€€ƒx~Now she, at the same hour, coming in, confessed to the Lord: and spoke of him to all that looked for the redemption of Israel.€€ƒx}And after they had performed all things according to the law of the Lord, they returned into Galilee, to their city Nazareth.€€ƒxUAnd the child grew and waxed strong, full of wisdom: and the grace of God was in him.€€ƒxMAnd his parents went every year to Jerusalem, at the solemn day of the pasch.€€ƒxeAnd when he was twelve years old, they going up into Jerusalem, according to the custom of the feast,€€ƒxvAnd having fulfilled the days, when they returned, the child Jesus remained in Jerusalem. And his parents knew it not.€€ƒxyAnd thinking that he was in the company, they came a day's journey and sought him among their kinsfolks and acquaintance.€€ƒx?And not finding him, they returned into Jerusalem, seeking him.€€ƒx—And it came to pass, that, after three days, they found him in the temple, sitting in the midst of the doctors, hearing them and asking them questions.€€ƒxEAnd all that heard him were astonished at his wisdom and his answers.€€ƒxAnd seeing him, they wondered. And his mother said to him: Son, why hast thou done so to us? Behold thy father and I have sought thee sorrowing.€€ƒxnAnd he said to them: How is it that you sought me? Did you not know that I must be about my father's business?€€ƒx9And they understood not the word that he spoke unto them.€€ƒxzAnd he went down with them and came to Nazareth and was subject to them. And his mother kept all these words in her heart.€€ƒx@And Jesus advanced in wisdom and age and grace with God and men.€€ƒ`€€ƒxóNow in the fifteenth year of the reign of Tiberius Caesar, Pontius Pilate being governor of Judea, and Herod being tetrarch of Galilee, and Philip his brother tetrarch of Iturea and the country of Trachonitis, and Lysanias tetrarch of Abilina:€€˜&ƒxnAnd he came into all the country about the Jordan, preaching the baptism of penance for the remission of sins.€€ƒx¥As it was written in the book of the sayings of Isaias the prophet: A voice of one crying in the wilderness: Prepare ye the way of the Lord, make straight his paths.€€ƒxEvery valley shall be filled and every mountain and hill shall be brought low: and the crooked shall be made straight, and the rough ways plain.€€ƒx-And all flesh shall see the salvation of God.€€ƒx–He said therefore to the multitudes that went forth to be baptized by him: Ye offspring of vipers, who hath shewed you to flee from the wrath to come?€€ƒx¾Bring forth therefore fruits worthy of penance: and do not begin to say, We have Abraham for our father. For I say unto you that God is able of these stones, to raise up children to Abraham.€€ƒx“For now the axe is laid to the root of the trees. Every tree therefore that bringeth not forth good fruit shall be cut down and cast into the fire.€€ƒx8And the people asked him, saying: What then shall we do?€€ƒxAnd he answering, said to them: He that hath two coats, let him give to him that hath none; and he that hath meat, let him do in like manner.€€ƒxUAnd the publicans also came to be baptized and said to him: Master, what shall we do?€€ƒxFBut he said to them: Do nothing more than that which is appointed you.€€ƒx¤And the soldiers also asked him, saying: And what shall we do? And he said to them: Do violence to no man, neither calumniate any man; and be content with your pay.€€ƒxvAnd as the people were of opinion, and all were thinking in their hearts of John, that perhaps he might be the Christ:€€ƒx×John answered, saying unto all: I indeed baptize you with water: but there shall come one mightier than I, the latchet of whose shoes I am not worthy to loose. He shall baptize you with the Holy Ghost and with fire;€€ƒx‘Whose fan is in his hand: and he will purge his floor and will gather the wheat into his barn: but the chaff he will burn with unquenchable fire.€€ƒx<And many other things exhorting did he preach to the people.€€ƒxBut Herod the tetrarch, when he was reproved by him for Herodias, his brother's wife, and for all the evils which Herod had done:€€ƒx8He added this also above all and shut up John in prison.€€ƒxvNow it came to pass, when all the people were baptized, that Jesus also being baptized and praying, heaven was opened.€€ƒx–And the Holy Ghost descended in a bodily shape, as a dove, upon him. And a voice came from heaven: Thou art my beloved Son. In thee I am well pleased.€€ƒxAnd Jesus himself was beginning about the age of thirty years: being (as it was supposed) the son of Joseph, who was of Heli, who was of Mathat,€€ƒxHWho was of Levi, who was of Melchi. who was of Janne, who was of Joseph,€€ƒx]Who was of Mathathias, who was of Amos, who was of Nahum, who was of Hesli, who was of Nagge,€€ƒx_Who was of Mahath, who was of Mathathias, who was of Semei, who was of Joseph, who was of Juda,€€ƒx`Who was of Joanna, who was of Reza, who was of Zorobabel, who was of Salathiel, who was of Neri,€€ƒxZWho was of Melchi, who was of Addi, who was of Cosan, who was of Helmadan, who was of Her,€€ƒx[Who was of Jesus, who was of Eliezer, who was of Jorim, who was of Mathat, who was of Levi,€€ƒx\Who was of Simeon, who was of Judas, who was of Joseph, who was of Jona, who was of Eliakim,€€ƒx]Who was of Melea, who was of Menna, who was of Mathatha, who was of Nathan, who was of David,€€ƒxZWho was of Jesse, who was of Obed, who was of Booz, who was of Salmon, who was of Naasson,€€ƒx\Who was of Aminadab, who was of Aram, who was of Esron, who was of Phares, who was of Judas,€€ƒx\Who was of Jacob, who was of Isaac, who was of Abraham, who was of Thare, who was of Nachor,€€ƒxYWho was of Sarug, who was of Ragau, who was of Phaleg, who was of Heber, who was of Sale,€€ƒxZWho was of Cainan, who was of Arphaxad, who was of Sem, who was Of Noe, who was of Lamech,€€ƒxbWho was of Mathusale, who was of Henoch, who was of Jared, who was of Malaleel, who was of Cainan,€€ƒxCWho was of Henos, who was of Seth, who was of Adam, who was of God.€€ƒ`€€ƒxoAnd Jesus being full of the Holy Ghost, returned from the Jordan and was led the by the spirit into the desert,€€˜,ƒx^And the devil said to him: If thou be the Son of God, say to this stone that it be made bread.€€ƒx`And Jesus answered him: is written that Man liveth not by bread alone, but by every word of God.€€ƒxlAnd the devil led him into a high mountain and shewed him all the kingdoms of the world in a moment of time.€€ƒxŒAnd he said to him: To thee will I give all this power and the glory of them. For to me they are delivered: and to whom I will, I give them.€€ƒx;If thou therefore wilt adore before me, all shall be thine.€€ƒxqAnd Jesus answering said to him. It is written: Thou shalt adore the Lord thy God, and him only shalt thou serve.€€ƒxŒAnd he brought him to Jerusalem and set him on a pinnacle of the temple and said to him: If thou be the Son of God, cast thyself from hence.€€ƒxUFor it is written that He hath given his angels charge over thee that they keep thee.€€ƒxaAnd that in their hands they shall bear thee up, lest perhaps thou dash thy foot against a stone.€€ƒxTAnd Jesus answering, said to him: It is said: Thou shalt not tempt the Lord thy God.€€ƒxKAnd all the temptation being ended, the devil departed from him for a time.€€ƒxtAnd Jesus returned in the power of the spirit, into Galilee: and the fame of him went out through the whole country.€€ƒx;And he taught in their synagogues and was magnified by all.€€ƒx–And he came to Nazareth, where he was brought up: and he went into the synagogue, according to his custom, on the sabbath day: and he rose up to read.€€ƒx€And the book of Isaias the prophet was delivered unto him. And as he unfolded the book, he found the place where it was written:€€ƒx‘The spirit of the Lord is upon me. Wherefore he hath anointed me to preach the gospel to the poor, he hath sent me to heal the contrite of heart,€€ƒx§To preach deliverance to the captives and sight to the blind, to set at liberty them that are bruised, to preach the acceptable year of the Lord and the day of reward.€€ƒx…And when he had folded the book, he restored it to the minister and sat down. And the eyes of all in the synagogue were fixed on him.€€ƒxOAnd he began to say to them: This day is fulfilled this scripture in your ears.€€ƒx“And all gave testimony to him. And they wondered at the words of grace that proceeded from his mouth. And they said: Is not this the son of Joseph?€€ƒx±And he said to them: Doubtless you will say to me this similitude: Physician, heal thyself. As great things as we have heard done in Capharnaum, do also here in thy own country.€€ƒxNAnd he said: Amen I say to you that no prophet is accepted in his own country.€€ƒx¹In truth I say to You, there were many widows in the days of Elias in Israel, when heaven was shut up three years and six months, when there was a great famine throughout all the earth.€€ƒxNAnd to none of them was Elias sent, but to Sarepta of Sidon, to a widow woman.€€ƒx}And there were many lepers in Israel in the time of Eliseus the prophet: and none of them was cleansed but Naaman the Syrian.€€ƒxLAnd all they in the synagogue, hearing these things, were filled with anger.€€ƒx£And they rose up and thrust him out of the city: and they brought him to the brow of the hill whereon their city was built, that they might cast him down headlong.€€ƒx7But he passing through the midst of them, went his way.€€ƒxbAnd he went down into Capharnaum, a city of Galilee: and there he taught them on the sabbath days.€€ƒxHAnd they were astonished at his doctrine: for his speech was with power.€€ƒxbAnd in the synagogue there was a man who had an unclean devil: and he cried out with a loud voice,€€ƒx’Saying: Let us alone. What have we to do with thee, Jesus of Nazareth? Art thou come to destroy us? I know thee who thou art, the holy one of God.€€ƒxAnd Jesus rebuked him, saying: Hold thy peace and go out of him. And when the devil had thrown him into the midst, he went out of him and hurt him not at all.€€ƒx«And there came fear upon all; and they talked among themselves, saying: What word is this, for with authority and power he commandeth the unclean spirits, and they go out?€€ƒxBAnd the fame of him was published into every place of the country.€€ƒx™And Jesus rising up out of the synagogue, went into Simon's house. And Simon's wife's mother was taken with a great fever: and they besought him for her.€€ƒxoAnd standing over her, he commanded the fever: and it left her. And immediately rising, she ministered to them.€€ƒx›And when the sun was down, all they that had any sick with divers diseases brought them to him. But he, laying his hands on every one of them, healed them.€€ƒx¡And devils went out from many, crying out and saying: Thou art the son of God. And rebuking them he suffered them not to speak; for they knew that he was Christ.€€ƒx£And when it was day, going out he went into a desert place: and the multitudes sought him, and came unto him. And they stayed him that should not depart from them.€€ƒx`To whom he said: To other cities also I must preach the kingdom of God: for therefore am I sent.€€ƒx2And he was preaching in the synagogues of Galilee.€€ƒ`€€ƒx{And it came to pass, that when the multitudes pressed upon him to hear the word of God, he stood by the lake of Genesareth,€€˜'ƒxœAnd going into one of the ships that was Simon's, he desired him to draw back a little from the land. And sitting, he taught the multitudes out of the ship.€€ƒxqNow when he had ceased to speak, he said to Simon: Launch out into the deep and let down your nets for a draught.€€ƒxˆAnd Simon answering said to him: Master, we have laboured all the night and have taken nothing: but at thy word I will let down the net.€€ƒxaAnd when they had done this, they enclosed a very great multitude of fishes: and their net broke.€€ƒx°And they beckoned to their partners that were in the other ship, that they should come and help them. And they came and filled both the ships, so that they were almost sinking.€€ƒxpWhich when Simon Peter saw, he fell down at Jesus' knees, saying: Depart from me, for I am a sinful man, O Lord.€€ƒxlFor he was wholly astonished, and all that were with him, at the draught of the fishes which they had taken.€€ƒxšAnd so were also James and John, the sons of Zebedee, who were Simon's partners. And Jesus saith to Simon: Fear not: from henceforth thou shalt catch men.€€ƒxNAnd having brought their ships to land, leaving all things, they followed him.€€ƒx¾And it came to pass, when he was in a certain city, behold a man full of leprosy who, seeing Jesus and falling on his face, besought him saying: Lord, if thou wilt, thou canst make me clean.€€ƒxAnd stretching forth his hand, he touched him, saying: I will. Be thou cleansed. And immediately the leprosy departed from him.€€ƒx¥And he charged him that he should tell no man, but: Go, shew thyself to the priest and offer for thy cleansing according as Moses commanded, for a testimony to them.€€ƒx‚But the fame of him went abroad the more: and great multitudes came together to hear and to be healed by him of their infirmities.€€ƒx+And he retired into the desert; and prayed.€€ƒxìAnd it came to pass on a certain day, as he sat teaching, that there were also Pharisees and doctors of the law sitting by, that were come out of every town of Galilee and Judea and Jerusalem: and the power of the Lord was to heal them.€€ƒxzAnd behold, men brought in a bed a man who had the palsy: and they sought means to bring him in and to lay him before him.€€ƒxÃAnd when they could not find by what way they might bring him in, because of the multitude, they went up upon the roof and let him down through the tiles with his bed into the midst before Jesus.€€ƒxBWhose faith when he saw, he said: Man, thy sins are forgiven thee.€€ƒx€And the scribes and Pharisees began to think, saying: Who is this who speaketh blasphemies? Who can forgive sins, but God alone?€€ƒxcAnd when Jesus knew their thoughts, answering he said to them: What is it you think in your hearts?€€ƒxNWhich is easier to say: Thy sins are forgiven thee; or to say: Arise and walk?€€ƒx¶But that you may know that the Son of man hath the power on earth to forgive sins (he saith to the sick of the palsy), I say to thee to: Arise, take up thy bed and go into thy house.€€ƒx}And immediately rising up before them, he took up the bed on which he lay: and he went away to his own house, glorifying God.€€ƒx~And all were astonished: and they glorified God. And they were filled with fear, saying: We have seen wonderful things to-day.€€ƒx…And after these things, he went forth and saw a publican named Levi, sitting at the receipt of custom: and he said to him: Follow me.€€ƒx4And leaving all things, he rose up and followed him.€€ƒxˆAnd Levi made him a great feast in his own house: And there was a great company of publicans and of others that were at table with them.€€ƒxuBut the Pharisees and scribes murmured, saying to his disciples: Why do you eat and drink with publicans and sinners?€€ƒxfAnd Jesus answering, said to them: They that are whole need not the physician: but they that are sick.€€ƒx4I came not to call the just, but sinners to penance.€€ƒx›And they said to him: Why do the disciples of John fast often and make prayers, and the disciples of the Pharisees in like manner; but thine eat and drink?€€ƒxeTo whom he said: Can you make the children of the bridegroom fast whilst the bridegroom is with them?€€ƒxmBut the days will come when the bridegroom shall be taken away from them: then shall they fast in those days.€€ƒxÉAnd he spoke also a similitude to them: That no man putteth a piece from a new garment upon an old garment: otherwise he both rendeth the new, and the piece taken from the new agreeth not with the old.€€ƒx‘And no man putteth new wine into old bottles: otherwise the new wine will break the bottles; and it will be spilled and the bottles will be lost.€€ƒxBBut new wine must be put into new bottles: and both are preserved.€€ƒxVAnd no man drinking old hath presently a mind to new: for he saith: The old is better.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¢And it came to pass on the second first sabbath that, as he went through the corn fields, his disciples plucked the ears and did eat, rubbing them in their hands.€€˜1ƒx‡And Jesus answering them, said: Have you not read so much as this, what David did, when himself was hungry and they that were with him:€€ƒx¥How he went into the house of God and took and ate the bread of proposition and gave to them that were with him, which is not lawful to eat but only for the priests?€€ƒx@And he said to them: The Son of man is Lord also of the sabbath.€€ƒxAnd it came to pass also, on another sabbath, that he entered into the synagogue and taught. And there was a man whose right hand was withered.€€ƒxvAnd the scribes and Pharisees watched if he would heal on the sabbath: that they might find an accusation against him.€€ƒxˆBut he knew their thoughts and said to the man who had the withered hand: Arise and stand forth in the midst. And rising he stood forth.€€ƒx}Then Jesus said to them: I ask you, if it be lawful on the sabbath days to do good or to do evil? To save life or to destroy?€€ƒx†And looking round about on them all, he said to the man: Stretch forth thy hand. And he stretched it forth. And his hand was restored.€€ƒxaAnd they were filled with madness: and they talked one with another, what they might do to Jesus.€€ƒx€And it came to pass in those days, that he went out into a mountain to pray: and he passed the whole night in the prayer of God.€€ƒxsAnd when day was come, he called unto him his disciples: and he chose twelve of them (whom also he named apostles):€€ƒx^Simon, whom he surnamed Peter, and Andrew his brother, James and John, Philip and Bartholomew,€€ƒxNMatthew and Thomas, James the son of Alpheus, and Simon who is called Zelotes,€€ƒxGAnd Jude the brother of James, and Judas Iscariot, who was the traitor.€€ƒxÃAnd coming down with them, he stood in a plain place: and the company of his disciples and a very great multitude of people from all Judea and Jerusalem and the sea coast, both of Tyre and Sidon,€€ƒxzWho were come to hear him and to be healed of their diseases. And they that were troubled with unclean spirits were cured.€€ƒxWAnd all the multitude sought to touch him: for virtue went out from him and healed all.€€ƒxiAnd he, lifting up his eyes on his disciples, said: Blessed are ye poor: for yours is the kingdom of God.€€ƒxkBlessed are ye that hunger now: for you shall be filled. Blessed are ye that weep now: for you shall laugh.€€ƒx Blessed shall you be when men shall hate you, and when they shall separate you and shall reproach you and cast out your name as evil, for the Son of man's sake.€€ƒxBe glad in that day and rejoice: for behold, your reward is great in heaven, For according to these things did their fathers to the prophets.€€ƒx<But woe to you that are rich: for you have your consolation.€€ƒxjWoe to you that are filled: for you shall hunger. Woe to you that now laugh: for you shall mourn and weep.€€ƒxkWoe to you when men shall bless you: for according to these things did their fathers to the false prophets.€€ƒxMBut I say to you that hear: Love your enemies. Do good to them that hate you.€€ƒx@Bless them that curse you and pray for them that calumniate you.€€ƒx•And to him that striketh thee on the one cheek, offer also the other. And him that taketh away from thee thy cloak, forbid not to take thy coat also.€€ƒx^Give to every one that asketh thee: and of him that taketh away thy goods, ask them not again.€€ƒxOAnd as you would that men should do to you, do you also to them in like manner.€€ƒxgAnd if you love them that love you, what thanks are to you? For sinners also love those that love them.€€ƒx`And if you do good to them who do good to you, what thanks are to you? For sinners also do this.€€ƒx†And if you lend to them of whom you hope to receive, what thanks are to you? For sinners also lend to sinners, for to receive as much.€€ƒxÄBut love ye your enemies: do good, and lend, hoping for nothing thereby: and your reward shall be great, and you shall be the sons of the Highest. For he is kind to the unthankful and to the evil.€€ƒx:Be ye therefore merciful, as your Father also is merciful.€€ƒxxJudge not: and you shall not be judged. Condemn not: and you shall not be condemned. Forgive: and you shall be forgiven.€€ƒxàGive: and it shall be given to you: good measure and pressed down and shaken together and running over shall they give into your bosom. For with the same measure that you shall mete withal, it shall be measured to you again.€€ƒxkAnd he spoke also to them a similitude: Can the blind lead the blind? Do they not both fall into the ditch?€€ƒx]The disciple is not above his master: but every one shall be perfect, if he be as his master.€€ƒxkAnd why seest thou the mote in thy brother's eye: but the beam that is in thy own eye thou considerest not?€€ƒyOr how canst thou say to thy brother: Brother, let me pull the mote out of thy eye, when thou thyself seest not the beam in thy own eye? Hypocrite, cast first the beam out of thy own eye: and then shalt thou see clearly to take out the mote from thy brother's eye.€€ƒxjFor there is no good tree that bringeth forth evil fruit: nor an evil tree that bringeth forth good fruit.€€ƒxFor every tree is known by its fruit. For men do not gather figs from thorns: nor from a bramble bush do they gather the grape.€€ƒxÙA good man out of the good treasure of his heart bringeth forth that which is good: and an evil man out of the evil treasure bringeth forth that which is evil. For out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaketh.€€ƒxCAnd why call you me, Lord, Lord; and do not the things which I say?€€ƒxcEvery one that cometh to me and heareth my words and doth them, I will shew you to whom he is like.€€ƒxÖHe is like to a man building a house, who digged deep and laid the foundation upon a rock. And when a flood came, the stream beat vehemently upon that house: and it could not shake it: for it was founded on a rock.€€ƒxÒBut he that heareth and doth not is like to a man building his house upon the earth without a foundation: against which the stream beat vehemently. And immediately it fell: and the ruin of that house was great.€€ƒ`€€ƒx`And when he had finished all his words in the hearing of the people, he entered into Capharnaum.€€˜2ƒxuAnd when he had heard of Jesus, he sent unto him the ancients of the Jews, desiring him to come and heal his servant.€€ƒxzAnd when they came to Jesus, they besought him earnestly, saying to him: He is worthy that thou shouldest do this for him.€€ƒx;For he loveth our nation: and he hath built us a synagogue.€€ƒxËAnd Jesus went with them. And when he was now not far from the house, the centurion sent his friends to him, saying: Lord, trouble not thyself; for I am not worthy that thou shouldst enter under my roof.€€ƒxtFor which cause neither did I think myself worthy to come to thee: but say the word, and my servant shall be healed.€€ƒx¸For I also am a man subject to authority, having under me soldiers: and I say to one, Go, and he goeth: and to another, Come, and he cometh; and to my servant, Do this, and he doth it.€€ƒx¦Which Jesus hearing, marvelled: and turning about to the multitude that followed him, he said: Amen I say to you, I have not found so great faith, not even in Israel.€€ƒx_And they who were sent, being returned to the house, found the servant whole who had been sick.€€ƒx‰And it came to pass afterwards that he went into a city that is called Naim: and there went with him his disciples and a great multitude.€€ƒx²And when he came nigh to the gate of the city, behold a dead man was carried out, the only son of his mother: and she was a widow. And a great multitude of the city was with her.€€ƒxZWhom when the Lord had seen, being moved with mercy towards her, he said to her: Weep not.€€ƒxzAnd he came near and touched the bier. And they that carried it stood still. And he said: Young man, I say to thee, arise.€€ƒxNAnd he that was dead sat up and begun to speak. And he gave him to his mother.€€ƒx‹And there came a fear upon them all: and they glorified God saying: A great prophet is risen up among us: and, God hath visited his people.€€ƒxbAnd this rumour of him went forth throughout all Judea and throughout all the country round about.€€ƒx2And John's disciples told him of all these things.€€ƒxAnd John called to him two of his disciples and sent them to Jesus, saying: Art thou he that art to come? Or look we for another?€€ƒx”And when the men were come unto him, they said: John the Baptist hath sent us to thee, saying: Art thou he that art to come? Or look we for another?€€ƒx(And in that same hour, he cured many of their diseases and hurts and evil spirits: and to many that were blind he gave sight.)€€ƒxÔAnd answering, he said to them: Go and relate to John what you have heard and seen: the blind see, the lame walk, the lepers are made clean, the deaf hear, the dead rise again, to the poor the gospel is preached.€€ƒx;And blessed is he whosoever shall not be scandalized in me.€€ƒx©And when the messengers of John were departed, he began to speak to the multitudes concerning John. What went ye out into the desert to see? A reed shaken with the wind?€€ƒx”But what went you out to see? A man clothed in soft garments? Behold they that are in costly apparel and live delicately are in the houses of kings.€€ƒxTBut what went you out to see? A prophet? Yea, I say to you, and more than a prophet.€€ƒxpThis is he of whom it is written: Behold I send my angel before thy face, who shall prepare thy way before thee.€€ƒx¯For I say to you: Amongst those that are born of men, there is not a greater prophet than John the Baptist. But he that is the lesser in the kingdom of God is greater than he.€€ƒxaAnd all the people hearing, and the publicans, justified God, being baptized with John's baptism.€€ƒxlBut the Pharisees and the lawyers despised the counsel of God against themselves, being not baptized by him.€€ƒxfAnd the Lord said: Whereunto then shall I liken the men of this generation? And to what are they like?€€ƒx³They are like to children sitting in the marketplace and speaking one to another and saying: We have piped to you, and you have not danced: we have mourned, and you have not wept.€€ƒx_For John the Baptist came neither eating bread nor drinking wine. And you say: He hath a devil.€€ƒx‘The Son of man is come eating and drinking. And you say: Behold a man that is a glutton and a drinker of wine, a friend of publicans and sinners.€€ƒx,And wisdom is justified by all her children.€€ƒxvAnd one of the Pharisees desired him to eat with him. And he went into the house of the Pharisee and sat down to meat.€€ƒx“And behold a woman that was in the city, a sinner, when she knew that he sat at meat in the Pharisee's house, brought an alabaster box of ointment.€€ƒx©And standing behind at his feet. she began to wash his feet with tears and wiped them with the hairs of her head and kissed his feet and anointed them with the ointment.€€ƒxÒAnd the Pharisee, who had invited him, seeing it, spoke within himself, saying: This man, if he were if a prophet, would know surely who and what manner of woman this is that toucheth him, that she is a sinner.€€ƒxeAnd Jesus answering, said to him: Simon, I have somewhat to say to thee. But he said: Master, say it.€€ƒxXA certain creditor had two debtors: the one owed five hundred pence and the other fifty.€€ƒxlAnd whereas they had not wherewith to pay, he forgave them both. Which therefore of the two loveth him most?€€ƒxoSimon answering, said: I suppose that he to whom he forgave most. And he said to him: Thou hast judged rightly.€€ƒxÒAnd turning to the woman, he said unto Simon: Dost thou see this woman? I entered into thy house: thou gavest me no water for my feet. But she with tears hath washed my feet; and with her hairs hath wiped them.€€ƒxTThou gavest me no kiss. But she, since she cane in, hath not ceased to kiss my feet.€€ƒxTMy head with oil thou didst not anoint. But she with ointment hath anointed my feet.€€ƒx€Wherefore, I say to thee: Many sins are forgiven her, because she hath loved much. But to whom less is forgiven, he loveth less.€€ƒx/And he said to her: Thy sins are forgiven thee.€€ƒxhAnd they that sat at meat with him began to say within themselves: Who is this that forgiveth sins also?€€ƒxEAnd he said to the woman: Thy faith hath made thee safe. Go in peace.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‘And it came to pass afterwards he travelled through the cities and towns, preaching and evangelizing the kingdom of God: and the twelve with him:€€˜8ƒxvAnd Joanna the wife of Chusa, Herod's steward, and Susanna and many others who ministered unto him of their substance.€€ƒxyAnd when a very great multitude was gathered together and hastened out of the cities, unto him, he spoke by a similitude.€€ƒxThe sower went out to sow his seed. And as he sowed, some fell by the way side. And it was trodden down: and the fowls of the air devoured it.€€ƒxoAnd other some fell upon a rock. And as soon as it was sprung up, it withered away, because it had no moisture.€€ƒxOAnd other some fell among thorns. And the thorns growing up with it, choked it.€€ƒx£And other some fell upon good ground and, being sprung up, yielded fruit a hundredfold. Saying these things, he cried out: He that hath ears to hear, let him hear.€€ƒx7And his disciples asked him what this parable might be.€€ƒx¨To whom he said: To you it is given to know the mystery of the kingdom of God; but to the rest in parables, that seeing they may not see and hearing may not understand.€€ƒx5Now the parable is this: The seed is the word of God.€€ƒxAnd they by the way side are they that hear: then the devil cometh and taketh the word out of their heart, lest believing they should be saved.€€ƒx­Now they upon the rock are they who when they hear receive the word with joy: and these have no roots: for they believe for a while and in time of temptation they fall away.€€ƒx¢And that which fell among thorns are they who have heard and, going their way, are choked with the cares and riches and pleasures of this life and yield no fruit.€€ƒx‚But that on the good ground are they who in a good and perfect heart, hearing the word, keep it and bring forth fruit in patience.€€ƒxNow no man lighting a candle covereth it with a vessel or putteth it under a bed: but setteth it upon a candlestick, that they who come in may see the light.€€ƒxvFor there is not any thing secret that shall not be made manifest, nor hidden that shall not be known and come abroad.€€ƒx¦Take heed therefore how you hear. For whosoever hath, to him shall be given: and whosoever hath not, that also which he thinketh he hath shall be taken away from him.€€ƒxXAnd his mother and brethren came unto him: and they could not come at him for the crowd.€€ƒxUAnd it was told him: Thy mother and thy brethren stand without, desiring to see thee.€€ƒxcWho answering, said to them: My mother and my brethren are they who hear the word of God and do it.€€ƒx´And it came to pass on a certain day that he went into a little ship with his disciples. And he said to them: Let us go over to the other side of the lake. And they launched forth.€€ƒxAnd when they were sailing, he slept. And there came down a storm of wind upon the lake: and they were filled and were in danger.€€ƒx™And they came and awaked him, saying: Master, we perish. But he arising, rebuked the wind and the rage of the water. And it ceased: and there was a calm.€€ƒx·And he said to them: Where is your faith? Who being afraid, wondered, saying one to another: Who is this (think you), that he commandeth both the winds and the sea: and they obey him?€€ƒxNAnd they sailed to the country of the Gerasens, which is over against Galilee.€€ƒx¹And when he was come forth to the land, there met him a certain man who had a devil now a very long time. And he wore no clothes: neither did he abide in a house, but in the sepulchres.€€ƒx»And when he saw Jesus, he fell down before him. And crying out with a loud voice, he said: What have I to do with thee, Jesus, Son of the most high God? I beseech thee, do not torment me.€€ƒxÎFor he commanded the unclean spirit to go out of the man. For many times it seized him: and he was bound with chains and kept in fetters: and breaking the bonds, he was driven by the devil into the deserts.€€ƒxnAnd Jesus asked him, saying: What is thy name? But he said: Legion. Because many devils were entered into him.€€ƒxJAnd they besought him that he would not command them to go into the abyss.€€ƒx›And there was there a herd of many swine feeding on the mountain: and they besought him that he would suffer them to enter into them. And he suffered them.€€ƒx’The devils therefore went out of the man and entered into the swine. And the herd ran violently down a steep place into the lake and were stifled.€€ƒxcWhich when they that fed them saw done, they fled away and told it in the city and in the villages.€€ƒxÀAnd they went out to see what was done. And they came to Jesus and found the man out of whom the devils were departed, sitting at his feet, clothed and in his right mind. And they were afraid.€€ƒxMAnd they also that had seen told them how he had been healed from the legion.€€ƒx°And all the multitude of the country of the Gerasens besought him to depart from them: for they were taken with great fear. And he, going up into the ship, returned back again.€€ƒxyNow the man out of whom the devils were departed besought him that he might be with him. But Jesus sent him away, saying:€€ƒx›Return to thy house and tell how great things God hath done to thee. And he went through the whole city, publishing how great things Jesus had done to him.€€ƒxpAnd it came to pass that when Jesus was returned, the multitude received him: for they were all waiting for him.€€ƒx°And behold there came a man whose name was Jairus: and he was a ruler of the synagogue. And he fell down at the feet of Jesus, beseeching him that he would come into his house:€€ƒx‹For he had an only daughter, almost twelve years old, and she was dying. And it happened as he went that he was thronged by the multitudes.€€ƒx•And there was a certain woman having an issue of blood twelve years, who had bestowed all her substance on physicians and could not be healed by any.€€ƒxgShe came behind him and touched the hem of his garment: and immediately the issue of her blood stopped.€€ƒx´And Jesus said: Who is it that touched me? And all denying, Peter and they that were with him said: Master, the multitudes throng and press thee; and dost thou say, who touched me?€€ƒxUAnd Jesus said: Somebody hath touched me; for I know that virtue is gone out from me.€€ƒxÂAnd the woman seeing that she was not hid, came trembling and fell down before his feet and declared before all the people for what cause she had touched him, and how she was immediately healed.€€ƒxRBut he said to her: Daughter, thy faith hath made thee whole. Go thy way in peace.€€ƒx}As he was yet speaking, there cometh one to the ruler of the synagogue, saying to him: Thy daughter is dead: trouble him not.€€ƒxlAnd Jesus hearing this word, answered the father of the maid: Fear not. Believe only: and she shall be safe.€€ƒx”And when he was come to the house, he suffered not any man to go in with him, but Peter and James and John, and the father and mother of the maiden.€€ƒx\And all wept and mourned for her. But he said: Weep not. The maid is not dead, but sleepeth.€€ƒx9And they laughed him to scorn, knowing that she was dead.€€ƒx>But he taking her by the hand, cried out, saying: Maid, arise.€€ƒxTAnd her spirit returned: and she arose immediately. And he bid them give her to eat.€€ƒxNAnd her parents were astonished, whom he charged to tell no man what was done.€€ƒ`€€ƒxqThen calling together the twelve apostles, he gave them power and authority over all devils and to cure diseases.€€˜>ƒx{And he said to them: Take nothing for your journey, neither staff, nor scrip, nor bread, nor money; neither have two coats.€€ƒxRAnd whatsoever house you shall enter into, abide there and depart not from thence.€€ƒx„And whosoever will not receive you, when ye go out of that city, shake off even the dust of your feet, for a testimony against them.€€ƒx_And going out, they went about through the towns, preaching the gospel and healing every where.€€ƒxnNow Herod, the tetrarch, heard of all things that were done by him. And he was in a doubt, because it was said€€ƒx“By some that John was risen from the dead: but by other some, that Elias had appeared: and by others, that one of the old prophets was risen again.€€ƒxkAnd Herod said: John I have beheaded. But who is this of whom I hear such things? And he sought to see him.€€ƒxŸAnd the apostles, when they were returned, told him all they had done. And taking them, he went aside into a desert place, apart, which belongeth to Bethsaida.€€ƒx”Which when the people knew, they followed him: and he received them and spoke to them of the kingdom of God and healed them who had need of healing.€€ƒxÔNow the day began to decline. And the twelve came and said to him: Send away the multitude, that, going into the towns and villages round about, they may lodge and get victuals; for we are here in a desert place.€€ƒx¬But he said to them: Give you them to eat. And they said: We have no more than five loaves and two fishes; unless perhaps, we should go and buy food for all this multitude.€€ƒxqNow there were about five thousand men. And he said to his disciples: Make them sit down by fifties in a company.€€ƒx+And they did so and made them all sit down.€€ƒx£And taking the five loaves and the two fishes, he looked up to heaven and blessed them: and he broke and distributed to his disciples, to set before the multitude.€€ƒxqAnd they did all eat and were filled. And there were taken up of fragments that remained to them, twelve baskets.€€ƒxŒAnd it came to pass, as he was alone praying, his disciples also were with him: and he asked them, saying: Whom do the people say that I am?€€ƒx€But they answered and said: John the Baptist; but some say Elias: and others say that one of the former prophets is risen again.€€ƒxcAnd he said to them: But whom do you say that I am? Simon Peter answering, said: The Christ of God.€€ƒxIBut he strictly charging them, commanded they should tell this to no man.€€ƒx˜Saying: The Son of man must suffer many things and be rejected by the ancients and chief priests and scribes and be killed and the third day rise again.€€ƒxrAnd he said to all: If any man will come after me, let him deny himself and take up his cross daily and follow me.€€ƒxjFor whosoever will save his life shall lose it: for he that shall lose his life for my sake shall save it.€€ƒx`For what is a man advantaged, if he gain the whole world and lose himself and cast away himself?€€ƒx¬For he that shall be ashamed of me and of my words, of him the Son of man shall be ashamed, when he shall come in his majesty and that of his Father and of the holy angels.€€ƒxtBut I tell you of a truth: There are some standing here that shall not taste death till they see the kingdom of God.€€ƒxƒAnd it came to pass, about eight days after these words, that he took Peter and James and John and went up into a mountain to pray.€€ƒxkAnd whilst he prayed, the shape of his countenance was altered and his raiment became white and glittering.€€ƒxHAnd behold two men were talking with him. And they were Moses and Elias,€€ƒx[Appearing in majesty. And they spoke of his decease that he should accomplish in Jerusalem.€€ƒx€But Peter and they that were with him were heavy with sleep. And waking, they saw his glory and the two men that stood with him.€€ƒxçAnd it came to pass that, as they were departing from him, Peter saith to Jesus: Master, it is good for us to be here: and let us make three tabernacles, one for thee, and one for Moses; and one for Elias: not knowing what he said.€€ƒx~And as he spoke these things, there came a cloud and overshadowed them. And they were afraid when they entered into the cloud.€€ƒxLAnd a voice came out of the cloud; saying: This is my beloved son. Hear him.€€ƒx˜And whilst the voice was uttered Jesus was found alone. And they held their peace and told no man in those days any of these things which they had seen.€€ƒxnAnd it came to pass the day following, when they came down from the mountain, there met him a great multitude.€€ƒxxAnd behold a man among the crowd cried out, saying: Master, I beseech thee, look upon my son, because he is my only one.€€ƒx§And lo, a spirit seizeth him, and he suddenly crieth out, and he throweth him down and teareth him, so that he foameth; and bruising him, he hardly departeth from him.€€ƒx@And I desired thy disciples to cast him out: and they could not.€€ƒx„And Jesus, answering:, said: O faithless and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you and suffer you? Bring hither thy son.€€ƒxCAnd as he was coming to him, the devil threw him down and tore him.€€ƒxVAnd Jesus rebuked the unclean spirit and cured the boy and restored him to his father.€€ƒxıAnd all were astonished at the mighty power of God. But while all wondered at all the things he did, he said to his disciples: Lay you up in your hearts these words, for it shall come to pass that the Son of man shall be delivered into the hands of men.€€ƒx‘But they understood not this word: and it was hid from them, so that they perceived it not. And they were afraid to ask him concerning this word.€€ƒxGAnd there entered a thought into them, which of them should be greater.€€ƒxOBut Jesus seeing the thoughts of their hearts, took a child and set him by him,€€ƒxÄAnd said to them: Whosoever shall receive this child in my name receiveth me; and whosoever shall receive me receiveth him that sent me. For he that is the lesser among you all, he is the greater.€€ƒxAnd John, answering, said: Master, we saw a certain man casting out devils in thy name: and we forbade him, because he followeth not with us.€€ƒxQAnd Jesus said to him: Forbid him not: for he that is not against you is for you.€€ƒx}And it came to pass, when the days of his assumption were accomplishing, that he steadfastly set his face to go to Jerusalem.€€ƒxrAnd he sent messengers before his face: and going, they entered into a city of the Samaritans, to prepare for him.€€ƒxJAnd they received him not, because his face was of one going to Jerusalem.€€ƒx‘And when his disciples, James and John, had seen this, they said: Lord, wilt thou that we command fire to come down from heaven and consume them?€€ƒxJAnd turning, he rebuked them, saying: you know not of what spirit you are.€€ƒxWThe Son of man came not to destroy souls, but to save. And they went into another town.€€ƒx|And it came to pass, as they walked in the way, that a certain man said to him: I will follow thee whithersoever thou goest.€€ƒx{Jesus said to him: The foxes have holes, and the birds of the air nests: but the Son of man hath not where to lay his head.€€ƒxbBut he said to another: Follow me. And he said: Lord, suffer me first to go and to bury my father.€€ƒx_And Jesus said to him: Let the dead bury their dead: but go thou and preach the kingdom of God.€€ƒxhAnd another said: I will follow thee, Lord; but let me first take my leave of them that are at my house.€€ƒxhJesus said to him: No man putting his hand to the plough and looking back is fit for the kingdom of God.€€ƒ`€€ƒx©And after these things, the Lord appointed also other seventy-two. And he sent them two and two before his face into every city and place whither he himself was to come.€€˜*ƒx,Go: Behold I send you as lambs among wolves.€€ƒxHCarry neither purse, nor scrip, nor shoes: and salute no man by the way.€€ƒxAInto whatever house you enter, first say: Peace be to this house.€€ƒxeAnd if the son of peace be there, your peace shall rest upon him: but if not, it shall return to you.€€ƒx”And in the same house, remain, eating and drinking such things as they have: for the labourer is worthy of his hire. Remove not from house to house.€€ƒxaAnd into what city soever you enter, and they receive you, eat such things as are set before you.€€ƒx]And heal the sick that are therein and say to them: The kingdom of God is come nigh unto you.€€ƒxhBut into whatsoever city you enter, and they receive you not, going forth into the streets thereof, say:€€ƒx€Even the very dust of your city that cleaveth to us, we wipe off against you. Yet know this, that the kingdom of God is at hand.€€ƒxRI say to you, it shall be more tolerable at that day for Sodom than for that city.€€ƒxÏWoe to thee, Corozain! Woe to thee, Bethsaida! For if in Tyre and Sidon had been wrought the mighty works that have been wrought in you, they would have done penance long ago, sitting in sackcloth and ashes.€€ƒxOBut it shall be more tolerable for Tyre and Sidon at the judgment than for you.€€ƒxWAnd thou, Capharnaum, which art exalted unto heaven, thou shalt be thrust down to hell.€€ƒx|He that heareth you heareth me: and he that despiseth you despiseth me: and he that despiseth me despiseth him that sent me.€€ƒxcAnd the seventy-two returned with joy, saying: Lord, the devils also are subject to us in thy name.€€ƒxDAnd he said to them: I saw Satan like lightning falling from heaven.€€ƒx„Behold, I have given you power to tread upon serpents and scorpions and upon all the power of the enemy: and nothing shall hurt you.€€ƒx{But yet rejoice not in this, that spirits are subject unto you: but rejoice in this, that your names are written in heaven.€€ƒyIn that same hour, he rejoiced in the Holy Ghost and said: I confess to thee, O Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because thou hast hidden these things from the wise and prudent and hast revealed them to little ones. Yea, Father, for so it hath seemed good in thy sight.€€ƒx§All things are delivered to me by my Father. And no one knoweth who the Son is, but the Father: and who the Father is, but the Son and to whom the Son will reveal him.€€ƒx^And turning to his disciples, he said: Blessed are the eyes that see the things which you see.€€ƒx¯For I say to you that many prophets and kings have desired to see the things that you see and have not seen them; and to hear the things that you hear and have not heard them.€€ƒxnAnd behold a certain lawyer stood up, tempting him and saying, Master, what must I do to possess eternal life?€€ƒxABut he said to him: What is written in the law? How readest thou?€€ƒx°He answering, said: Thou shalt love the Lord thy God with thy whole heart and with thy whole soul and with all thy strength and with all thy mind: and thy neighbour as thyself.€€ƒxKAnd he said to him: Thou hast answered right. This do: and thou shalt live.€€ƒxJBut he willing to justify himself, said to Jesus: And who is my neighbour?€€ƒx³And Jesus answering, said: A certain man went down from Jerusalem to Jericho and fell among robbers, who also stripped him and having wounded him went away, leaving him half dead.€€ƒxXAnd it chanced, that a certain priest went down the same way: and seeing him, passed by.€€ƒxPIn like manner also a Levite, when he was near the place and saw him, passed by.€€ƒxhBut a certain Samaritan, being on his journey, came near him: and seeing him, was moved with compassion:€€ƒx’And going up to him, bound up his wounds, pouring in oil and wine: and setting him upon his own beast, brought him to an inn and took care of him.€€ƒx©And the next day he took out two pence and gave to the host and said: Take care of him; and whatsoever thou shalt spend over and above, I, at my return, will repay thee.€€ƒxWWhich of these three, in thy opinion, was neighbour to him that fell among the robbers?€€ƒx`But he said: He that shewed mercy to him. And Jesus said to him: Go, and do thou in like manner.€€ƒx…Now it came to pass, as they went, that he entered into a certain town: and a certain woman named Martha received him into her house.€€ƒxWAnd she had a sister called Mary. who, sitting also at the Lord's feet, heard his word.€€ƒx©But Martha was busy about much serving. Who stood and said: Lord, hast thou no care that my sister hath left me alone to serve? Speak to her therefore, that she help me.€€ƒxiAnd the Lord answering, said to her: Martha, Martha, thou art careful and art troubled about many things:€€ƒxcBut one thing is necessary. Mary hath chosen the best part, which shall not be taken away from her.€€ƒ`€€ƒx«And it came to pass that as he was in a certain place praying, when he ceased, one of his disciples said to him: Lord, teach us to pray, as John also taught his disciples.€€˜6ƒx!Give us this day our daily bread.€€ƒxoAnd forgive us our sins, for we also forgive every one that is indebted to us. And lead us not into temptation.€€ƒx‰And he said to them: Which of you shall have a friend and shall go to him at midnight and shall say to him: Friend, lend me three loaves,€€ƒx]Because a friend of mine is come off his journey to me and I have not what to set before him.€€ƒxAnd he from within should answer and say: Trouble me not; the door is now shut, and my children are with me in bed. I cannot rise and give thee.€€ƒxÃYet if he shall continue knocking, I say to you, although he will not rise and give him because he is his friend; yet, because of his importunity, he will rise and give him as many as he needeth.€€ƒxqAnd I say to you: Ask, and it shall be given you: seek, and you shall find: knock, and it shall be opened to you.€€ƒxnFor every one that asketh receiveth: and he that seeketh findeth: and to him that knocketh it shall be opened:€€ƒxyAnd which of you, if he ask his father bread, will he give him a stone? Or a fish, will he for a fish give him a serpent?€€ƒx8Or if he shall ask an egg, will he reach him a scorpion?€€ƒxœIf you then, being evil, know how to give good gifts to your children, how much more will your Father from heaven give the good Spirit to them that ask him?€€ƒx˜And he was casting out a devil: and the same was dumb. And when he had cast out the devil, the dumb spoke: and the multitudes, were in admiration at it.€€ƒxPBut some of them said: He casteth out devils by Beelzebub, the prince of devils.€€ƒx5And others tempting, asked of him a sign from heaven.€€ƒx‘But he seeing their thoughts, said to them: Every kingdom divided against itself shall be brought to desolation; and house upon house shall fall.€€ƒx„And if Satan also be divided against himself, how shall his kingdom stand? Because you say that through Beelzebub I cast out devils.€€ƒxtNow if I cast out devils by Beelzebub, by whom do your children cast them out? Therefore, they shall be your judges.€€ƒx]But if I by the finger of God cast out devils, doubtless the kingdom of God is come upon you.€€ƒxYWhen a strong man armed keepeth his court, those things are in peace which he possesseth.€€ƒxBut if a stronger than he come upon him and overcome him, he will take away all his armour wherein he trusted and will distribute his spoils.€€ƒxSHe that is not with me is against me; and he that gathereth not with me scattereth.€€ƒx®When the unclean spirit is gone out of a man, he walketh through places without water, seeking rest: and not finding, he saith: I will return into my house whence I came out.€€ƒx7And when he is come, he findeth it swept and garnished.€€ƒx®Then he goeth and taketh with him seven other spirits more wicked than himself: and entering in they dwell there. And the last state of that man becomes worse than the first.€€ƒx¶And it came to pass, as he spoke these things, a certain woman from the crowd, lifting up her voice, said to him: Blessed is the womb that bore thee and the paps that gave thee suck.€€ƒxOBut he said: Yea rather, blessed are they who hear the word of God and keep it.€€ƒx´And the multitudes running together, he began to say: This generation is a wicked generation. It asketh a sign: and a sign shall not be given it, but the sign of Jonas the prophet.€€ƒx]For as Jonas was a sign to the Ninivites; so shall the Son of man also be to this generation.€€ƒx×The queen of the south shall rise in the judgment with the men of this generation and shall condemn them: because she came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon. And behold more than Solomon here.€€ƒx¬The men of Ninive shall rise in the judgment with this generation and shall condemn it; Because they did penance at the preaching of Jonas. And behold more than Jonas here.€€ƒxNo man lighteth a candle and putteth it in a hidden place, nor under a bushel: but upon a candlestick, that they that come in may see the light.€€ƒxŒThe light of thy body is thy eye. If thy eye be single, thy whole body will be lightsome: but if it be evil, thy body also will be darksome.€€ƒxDTake heed therefore that the light which is in thee be not darkness.€€ƒxŠIf then thy whole body be lightsome, having no part of darkness: the whole shall be lightsome and, as a bright lamp, shall enlighten thee.€€ƒxtAnd as he was speaking, a certain Pharisee prayed him that he would dine with him. And he going in, sat down to eat.€€ƒx\And the Pharisee began to say, thinking within himself, why he was not washed before dinner.€€ƒx“And the Lord said to him: Now you, Pharisees, make clean the outside of the cup and of the platter: but your inside is full of rapine and iniquity.€€ƒxTYe fools, did not he that made that which is without make also that which is within?€€ƒxSBut yet that which remaineth, give alms: and behold, all things are clean unto you.€€ƒxÃBut woe to you, Pharisees, because you tithe mint and rue and every herb and pass over judgment and the charity of God. Now these things you ought to have done, and not to leave the other undone.€€ƒxqWoe to you, Pharisees, because you love the uppermost seats in the synagogues and salutations in the marketplace.€€ƒx`Woe to you, because you are as sepulchres that appear not: and men that walk over are not aware.€€ƒxiAnd one of the lawyers answering, saith to him: Master, in saying these things, thou reproachest us also.€€ƒxŸBut he said: Woe to you lawyers also, because you load men with burdens which they cannot bear and you yourselves touch not the packs with one of your fingers.€€ƒxQWoe to you who build the monuments of the prophets: and your fathers killed them.€€ƒxƒTruly you bear witness that you consent to the doings of your fathers. For they indeed killed them: and you build their sepulchres.€€ƒx…For this cause also the wisdom of God said: I will send to them prophets and apostles: and some of them they will kill and persecute.€€ƒxvThat the blood of all the prophets which was shed from the foundation of the world may be required of this generation,€€ƒxFrom the blood of Abel unto the blood of Zacharias, who was slain between the altar and the temple. Yea I say to you: It shall be required of this generation.€€ƒx™Woe to you lawyers, for you have taken away the key of knowledge. You yourselves have not entered in: and those that were entering in, you have hindered.€€ƒxAnd as he was saying these things to them, the Pharisees and the lawyers began violently to urge him and to oppress his mouth about many things,€€ƒx`Lying in wait for him and seeking to catch something from his mouth, that they might accuse him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx®And when great multitudes stood about him, so that they trod one upon another, he began to say to his disciples: Beware ye of the leaven of the Pharisees, which is hypocrisy.€€˜;ƒx°For whatsoever things you have spoken in darkness shall be published in the light: and that which you have spoken in the ear in the chambers shall be preached on the housetops.€€ƒxsAnd I say to you, my friends: Be not afraid of them who kill the body and after that have no more that they can do.€€ƒxŠBut I will shew you whom you shall fear: Fear ye him who, after he hath killed, hath power to cast into hell. Yea, I say to you: Fear him.€€ƒxZAre not five sparrows sold for two farthings, and not one of them is forgotten before God?€€ƒxpYea, the very hairs of your head are all numbered. Fear not therefore: you are of more value than many sparrows.€€ƒxxAnd I say to you: Whosoever shall confess me before men, him shall the Son of man also confess before the angels of God.€€ƒxNBut he that shall deny me before men shall be denied before the angels of God.€€ƒx¡And whosoever speaketh a word against the Son of man, it shall be forgiven him: but to him that shall blaspheme against the Holy Ghost, it shall not be forgiven.€€ƒx—And when they shall bring you into the synagogues and to magistrates and powers, be not solicitous how or what you shall answer, or what you shall say.€€ƒxFFor the Holy Ghost shall teach you in the same hour what you must say.€€ƒxiAnd one of the multitude said to him: Master, speak to my brother that he divide the inheritance with me.€€ƒxIBut he said to him: Man, who hath appointed me judge or divider over you?€€ƒxAnd he said to them: Take heed and beware of all covetousness: for a man's life doth not consist in the abundance of things which he possesseth.€€ƒxiAnd he spoke a similitude to them, saying: The land of a certain rich man brought forth plenty of fruits.€€ƒxiAnd he thought within himself, saying: What shall I do, because I have no room where to bestow my fruits?€€ƒx˜And he said: This will I do: I will pull down my barns and will build greater: and into them will I gather all things that are grown to me and my goods.€€ƒxyAnd I will say to my soul: Soul, thou hast much goods laid up for many years. Take thy rest: eat, drink, make good cheer.€€ƒx†But God said to him: Thou fool, this night do they require thy soul of thee. And whose shall those things be which thou hast provided?€€ƒxISo is he that layeth up treasure for himself and is not rich towards God.€€ƒx”And he said to his disciples: Therefore I say to you: Be not solicitous for your life, what you shall eat, nor for your body, what you shall put on.€€ƒxFThe life is more than the meat: and the body is more than the raiment.€€ƒx£Consider the ravens, for they sow not, neither do they reap, neither have they storehouse nor barn, and God feedeth them. How much are you more valuable than they?€€ƒxDAnd which of you by taking thought can add to his stature one cubit?€€ƒx]If then ye be not able to do so much as the least thing, why are you solicitous for the rest?€€ƒxConsider the lilies, how they grow: they labour not, neither do they spin. But I say to you, not even Solomon in all his glory was clothed like one of these.€€ƒx•Now, if God clothe in this manner the grass that is to-day in the field and to-morrow is cast into the oven: how much more you, O ye of little faith?€€ƒxVAnd seek not what you shall eat or what you shall drink: and be not lifted up on high.€€ƒxrFor all these things do the nations of the world seek. But your Father knoweth that you have need of these things.€€ƒxcBut seek ye first the kingdom of God and his justice: and all these things shall be added unto you.€€ƒxNFear not, little flock, for it hath pleased your Father to give you a kingdom.€€ƒx©Sell what you possess and give alms. Make to yourselves bags which grow not old, a treasure in heaven which faileth not: where no thief approacheth, nor moth corrupteth.€€ƒx:For where your treasure is, there will your heart be also.€€ƒx7Let your loins be girt and lamps burning in your hands.€€ƒx¢And you yourselves like to men who wait for their lord, when he shall return from the wedding; that when he cometh and knocketh, they may open to him immediately.€€ƒx¾Blessed are those servants whom the Lord, when he cometh, shall find watching. Amen I say to you that he will gird himself and make them sit down to meat and passing will minister unto them.€€ƒxqAnd if he shall come in the second watch or come in the third watch and find them so, blessed are those servants.€€ƒxBut this know ye, that if the householder did know at what hour the thief would come, he would surely watch and would not suffer his house to be broken open.€€ƒxPBe you then also ready: for at what hour you think not the Son of man will come.€€ƒxTAnd Peter said to him: Lord, dost thou speak this parable to us, or likewise to all?€€ƒx And the Lord said: Who thinkest thou is the faithful and wise steward, whom his lord setteth over his family, to give them their measure of wheat in due season?€€ƒxOBlessed is that servant whom, when his lord shall come, he shall find so doing.€€ƒxAVerily I say to you, he will set him over all that he possesseth.€€ƒx©But if that servant shall say in his heart: My Lord is long a coming; and shall begin to strike the men-servants and maid-servants, and to eat and to drink and be drunk:€€ƒx±The lord of that servant will come in the day that he hopeth not, and at the hour that he knoweth not: and shall separate him and shall appoint him his portion with unbelievers.€€ƒxAnd that servant, who knew the will of his lord and prepared not himself and did not according to his will, shall be beaten with many stripes.€€ƒxáBut he that knew not and did things worthy of stripes shall be beaten with few stripes. And unto whomsoever much is given, of him much shall be required: and to whom they have committed much, of him they will demand the more.€€ƒxMI am come to cast fire on the earth. And what will I, but that it be kindled?€€ƒxeAnd I have a baptism wherewith I am to be baptized. And how am I straitened until it be accomplished?€€ƒxPThink ye, that I am come to give peace on earth? I tell you, no; but separation.€€ƒxgFor there shall be from henceforth five in one house divided: three against two, and two against three.€€ƒxñThe father shall be divided against the son and the son against his father: the mother against the daughter and the daughter against her mother: the mother-in-law against the daughter-in-law and the daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law.€€ƒxŠAnd he said also to the multitudes: When you see a cloud rising from the west, presently you say: A shower is coming. And so it happeneth.€€ƒxUAnd when ye see the south wind blow, you say: There will heat. And it cometh to pass.€€ƒxYou hypocrites, you know how to discern the face of the heaven and of the earth: but how is it that you do not discern this time?€€ƒxAAnd why, even of yourselves, do you not judge that which is just?€€ƒxîAnd when thou goest with thy adversary to the prince, whilst thou art in the way, endeavour to be delivered from him: lest perhaps he draw thee to he judge, and the judge deliver thee to the exacter, and the exacter cast thee into prison.€€ƒxNI say to thee, thou shalt not go out thence until thou pay the very last mite.€€ƒ`€€ƒx…And there were present, at that very time, some that told him of the Galileans, whose blood Pilate had mingled with their sacrifices.€€˜#ƒxQNo, I say to you: but unless you shall do penance, you shall all likewise perish.€€ƒx“Or those eighteen upon whom the tower fell in Siloe and slew them: think you that they also were debtors above all the men that dwelt in Jerusalem?€€ƒxKNo, I say to you: but except you do penance, you shall all likewise perish.€€ƒxHe spoke also this parable: A certain man had a fig tree planted in his vineyard: and he came seeking fruit on it and found none.€€ƒx´And he said to the dresser of the vineyard: Behold, for these three years I come seeking fruit on this fig tree and I find none. Cut it down therefore. Why cumbereth it the ground?€€ƒxcBut he answering, said to him: Lord, let it alone this year also, until I dig about it and dung it.€€ƒxQAnd if happily it bear fruit: but if not, then after that thou shalt cut it down.€€ƒx8And he was teaching in their synagogue on their sabbath.€€ƒxAnd behold there was a woman who had a spirit of infirmity eighteen years. And she was bowed together: neither could she look upwards at all.€€ƒxjWhom when Jesus saw, he called her unto him and said to her: Woman, thou art delivered from thy infirmity.€€ƒxXAnd he laid his hands upon her: and immediately she was made straight and glorified God.€€ƒxáAnd the ruler of the synagogue being angry that Jesus had healed on the sabbath answering, said to the multitude: Six days there are wherein you ought to work. In them therefore come and be healed: and not on the sabbath day.€€ƒxŸAnd the Lord answering him, said: Ye hypocrites, doth not every one of you, on the sabbath day, loose his ox or his ass from the manger and lead them to water?€€ƒx…And ought not this daughter of Abraham, whom Satan hath bound, lo, these eighteen years, be loosed from this bond on the sabbath day?€€ƒx‘And when he said these things, all his adversaries were ashamed: and all the people rejoiced for all the things that were gloriously done by him.€€ƒxYHe said therefore: To what is the kingdom of God like, and whereunto shall I resemble it?€€ƒx¯It is like to a grain of mustard seed, which a man took and cast into his garden: and it grew and became a great tree, and the birds of the air lodged in the branches thereof.€€ƒxJAnd again he said: Whereunto shall I esteem the kingdom of God to be like?€€ƒxhIt is like to leaven, which a woman took and hid in three measures of meal, till the whole was leavened.€€ƒxVAnd he went through the cities and towns teaching and making his journey to Jerusalem.€€ƒxVAnd a certain man said to him: Lord, are they few that are saved? But he said to them:€€ƒxfStrive to enter by the narrow gate: for many, I say to you, shall seek to enter and shall not be able.€€ƒxáBut when the master of the house shall be gone in and shall shut the door, you shall begin to stand without; and knock at the door, saying: Lord, open to us. And he answering, shall say to you: I know you not, whence you are.€€ƒxjThen you shall begin to say: We have eaten and drunk in thy presence: and thou hast taught in our streets.€€ƒxdAnd he shall say to you: I know you not, whence you are. Depart from me, all ye workers of iniquity.€€ƒx¨There shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth; when you shall see Abraham and Isaac and Jacob and all the prophets, in the kingdom of God: and you yourselves thrust out.€€ƒxvAnd there shall come from the east and the west and the north and the south: and shall sit down in the kingdom of God.€€ƒxUAnd behold, they are last that shall be first: and they are first that shall be last.€€ƒx~The same day, there came some of the Pharisees, saying to him: Depart, and get thee hence, for Herod hath a mind to kill thee.€€ƒxŒAnd he said to them: Go and tell that fox: Behold, I cast out devils and do cures, to-day and to-morrow, and the third day I am consummated.€€ƒxƒNevertheless, I must walk to-day and to-morrow and the day following, because it cannot be that a prophet perish, out of Jerusalem.€€ƒxÈJerusalem, Jerusalem, that killest the prophets; and stonest them that are sent to thee, how often would I have gathered thy children as the bird doth her brood under her wings, and thou wouldest not?€€ƒxµBehold your house shall be left to you desolate. And I say to you that you shall not see me till the time come when you shall say: Blessed is he that cometh in the name of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxwAnd it came to pass, when Jesus went into the house of one of the Pharisees, on the sabbath day, that they watched him.€€˜#ƒxiAnd Jesus answering, spoke to the lawyers and Pharisees, saying: Is it lawful to heal on the sabbath day?€€ƒxKBut they held their peace. But he taking him, healed him and sent him away.€€ƒxAnd answering them, he said: Which of you shall have an ass or an ox fall into a pit and will not immediately draw him out, on the sabbath day?€€ƒx.And they could not answer him to these things.€€ƒx{And he spoke a parable also to them that were invited, marking how they chose the first seats at the table, saying to them:€€ƒx‚When thou art invited to a wedding, sit not down in the first place, lest perhaps one more honourable than thou be invited by him:€€ƒx…And he that invited thee and him, come and say to thee: Give this man place. And then thou begin with shame to take the lowest place.€€ƒxĞBut when thou art invited, go, sit down in the lowest place; that when he who invited thee cometh, he may say to thee: Friend, go up higher. Then shalt thou have glory before them that sit at table with thee.€€ƒxhBecause every one that exalteth himself shall be humbled: and he that humbleth himself shall be exalted.€€ƒx÷And he said to him also that had invited him: When thou makest a dinner or a supper, call not thy friends nor thy brethren nor thy kinsmen nor thy neighbours who are rich; lest perhaps they also invite thee again, and a recompense be made to thee.€€ƒxPBut when thou makest a feast, call the poor, the maimed, the lame and the blind.€€ƒx–And thou shalt be blessed, because they have not wherewith to make thee recompense: for recompense shall be made thee at the resurrection of the just.€€ƒxWhen one of them that sat at table with him had heard these things, he said to him: Blessed is he that shall eat bread in the kingdom of God.€€ƒxGBut he said to him: A certain man made a great supper and invited many.€€ƒx„And he sent his servant at the hour of supper to say to them that were invited, that they should come: for now all things are ready.€€ƒx˜And they began all at once to make excuse. The first said to him: I have bought a farm and I must needs go out and see it. I pray thee, hold me excused.€€ƒxeAnd another said: I have bought five yoke of oxen and I go to try them. I pray thee, hold me excused.€€ƒxEAnd another said: I have married a wife; and therefore I cannot come.€€ƒxøAnd the servant returning, told these things to his lord. Then the master of the house, being angry, said to his servant: Go out quickly into the streets and lanes of the city; and bring in hither the poor and the feeble and the blind and the lame.€€ƒxUAnd the servant said: Lord, it is done as thou hast commanded; and yet there is room.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to the servant: Go out into the highways and hedges, and compel them to come in, that my house may be filled.€€ƒxUBut I say unto you that none of those men that were invited shall taste of my supper.€€ƒxGAnd there went great multitudes with him. And turning, he said to them:€€ƒxIf any man come to me, and hate not his father and mother and wife and children and brethren and sisters, yea and his own life also, he cannot be my disciple.€€ƒxOAnd whosoever doth not carry his cross and come after me cannot be my disciple.€€ƒxFor which of you, having a mind to build a tower, doth not first sit down and reckon the charges that are necessary, whether he have wherewithal to finish it:€€ƒxhLest, after he hath laid the foundation and is not able to finish it, all that see it begin to mock him,€€ƒx;Saying: This man began to build and was not able to finish.€€ƒxÁOr, what king, about to go to make war against another king, doth not first sit down and think whether he be able, with ten thousand, to meet him that, with twenty thousand, cometh against him?€€ƒx^Or else, while the other is yet afar off, sending an embassy, he desireth conditions of peace.€€ƒxaSo likewise every one of you that doth not renounce all that he possesseth cannot be my disciple.€€ƒxTSalt is good. But if the salt shall lose its savour, wherewith shall it be seasoned?€€ƒx{It is neither profitable for the land nor for the dunghill: but shall be cast out. He that hath ears to hear, let him hear.€€ƒ`€€ƒx=Now the publicans and sinners drew near unto him to hear him.€€˜ ƒx*And he spoke to them this parable, saying:€€ƒx±What man of you that hath an hundred sheep, and if he shall lose one of them, doth he not leave the ninety-nine in the desert and go after that which was lost, until he find it?€€ƒx@And when he hath found it, lay it upon his shoulders, rejoicing?€€ƒxˆAnd coming home, call together his friends and neighbours, saying to them: Rejoice with me, because I have found my sheep that was lost?€€ƒxI say to you that even so there shall be joy in heaven upon one sinner that doth penance, more than upon ninety-nine just who need not penance.€€ƒxŠOr what woman having ten groats, if she lose one groat, doth not light a candle and sweep the house and seek diligently until she find it?€€ƒxAnd when she hath found it, call together her friends and neighbours, saying: Rejoice with me, because I have found the groat which I had lost.€€ƒx[So I say to you, there shall be joy before the angels of God upon one sinner doing penance.€€ƒx(And he said: A certain man had two sons.€€ƒxAnd the younger of them said to his father: Father, give me the portion of substance that falleth to me. And he divided unto them his substance.€€ƒx“And not many days after, the younger son, gathering all together, went abroad into a far country: and there wasted his substance, living riotously.€€ƒxcAnd after he had spent all, there came a mighty famine in that country: and he began to be in want.€€ƒxlAnd he went and cleaved to one of the citizens of that country. And he sent him into his farm to feed swine.€€ƒxcAnd he would fain have filled his belly with the husks the swine did eat: and no man gave unto him.€€ƒxAnd returning to himself, he said: How many hired servants in my father's house abound with bread, and I here perish with hunger!€€ƒxkI will arise and will go to my father and say to him: Father, I have sinned against heaven and before thee.€€ƒxKI am not worthy to be called thy son: make me as one of thy hired servants.€€ƒx±And rising up, he came to his father. And when he was yet a great way off, his father saw him and was moved with compassion and running to him fell upon his neck and kissed him.€€ƒxwAnd the son said to him: Father: I have sinned against heaven and before thee I am not now worthy to be called thy son.€€ƒxŒAnd the father said to his servants: Bring forth quickly the first robe and put it on him: and put a ring on his hand and shoes on his feet.€€ƒxMAnd bring hither the fatted calf, and kill it: and let us eat and make merry:€€ƒxjBecause this my son was dead and is come to life again, was lost and is found. And they began to be merry.€€ƒxkNow his elder son was in the field and when he came and drew nigh to the house, he heard music and dancing.€€ƒxEAnd he called one of the servants, and asked what these things meant.€€ƒxvAnd he said to him: Thy brother is come and thy father hath killed the fatted calf, because he hath received him safe.€€ƒx[And he was angry and would not go in. His father therefore coming out began to entreat him.€€ƒxÄAnd he answering, said to his father: Behold, for so many years do I serve thee and I have never transgressed thy commandment: and yet thou hast never given me a kid to make merry with my friends.€€ƒx|But as soon as this thy son is come, who hath devoured his substance with harlots, thou hast killed for him the fatted calf.€€ƒxJBut he said to him: Son, thou art always with me; and all I have is thine.€€ƒx‰But it was fit that we should make merry and be glad: for this thy brother was dead and is come to life again; he was lost, and is found.€€ƒ`€€ƒx“And he said also to his disciples: There was a certain rich man who had a steward: and the same was accused unto him, that he had wasted his goods.€€˜ƒx•And the steward said within himself: What shall I do, because my lord taketh away from me the stewardship? To dig I am not able; to beg I am ashamed.€€ƒxpI know what I will do, that when I shall be removed from the stewardship, they may receive me into their houses.€€ƒxrTherefore, calling together every one of his lord's debtors, he said to the first: How much dost thou owe my lord?€€ƒxoBut he said: An hundred barrels of oil. And he said to him: Take thy bill and sit down quickly and write fifty.€€ƒxŒThen he said to another: And how much dost thou owe? Who said: An hundred quarters of wheat. He said to him: Take thy bill and write eighty.€€ƒx¤And the lord commended the unjust steward, forasmuch as he had done wisely: for the children of this world are wiser in their generation than the children of light.€€ƒxAnd I say to you: Make unto you friends of the mammon of iniquity: that when you shall fail, they may receive you into everlasting dwellings.€€ƒx¬He that is faithful in that which is least is faithful also in that which is greater: and he that is unjust in that which is little is unjust also in that which is greater.€€ƒxhIf then you have not been faithful in the unjust mammon, who will trust you with that which is the true?€€ƒxgAnd if you have not been faithful in that which is another's, who will give you that which is your own?€€ƒx¨No servant can serve two masters: for either he will hate the one and love the other: or he will hold to the one and despise the other. You cannot serve God and mammon.€€ƒxSNow the Pharisees, who were covetous, heard all these things: and they derided him.€€ƒxAnd he said to them: you are they who justify yourselves before men, but God knoweth your hearts. For that which is high to men is an abomination before God.€€ƒxThe law and the prophets were until John. From that time the kingdom of God is preached: and every one useth violence towards it.€€ƒxQAnd it is easier for heaven and earth to pass than one tittle of the law to fall.€€ƒxEvery one that putteth away his wife and marrieth another committeth adultery: and he that marrieth her that is put away from her husband committeth adultery.€€ƒxhThere was a certain rich man who was clothed in purple and fine linen and feasted sumptuously every day.€€ƒxRAnd there was a certain beggar, named Lazarus, who lay at his gate, full of sores,€€ƒxDesiring to be filled with the crumbs that fell from the rich man's table. And no one did give him: moreover the dogs came and licked his sores.€€ƒx“And it came to pass that the beggar died and was carried by the angels into Abraham's bosom. And the rich man also died: and he was buried in hell.€€ƒxbAnd lifting up his eyes when he was in torments, he saw Abraham afar off and Lazarus in his bosom:€€ƒx­And he cried and said: Father Abraham, have mercy on me and send Lazarus, that he may dip the tip of his finger in water to cool my tongue: for I am tormented in this flame.€€ƒx­And Abraham said to him: Son, remember that thou didst receive good things in thy lifetime, and likewise Lazarus evil things: but now he is comforted and thou art tormented.€€ƒxšAnd besides all this, between us and you, there is fixed a great chaos: so that they who would pass from hence to you cannot, nor from thence come hither.€€ƒxtAnd he said: Then, father, I beseech thee that thou wouldst send him to my father's house, for I have five brethren,€€ƒxOThat he may testify unto them, lest they also come into this place of torments.€€ƒxNAnd Abraham said to him: They have Moses and the prophets. Let them hear them.€€ƒx]But he said: No, father Abraham: but if one went to them from the dead, they will do penance.€€ƒxxAnd he said to him: If they hear not Moses and the prophets, neither will they believe, if one rise again from the dead.€€ƒ`€€ƒxtAnd he said to his disciples: It is impossible that scandals should not come. But woe to him through whom they come!€€˜%ƒxiTake heed to yourselves. If thy brother sin against thee, reprove him: and if he do penance, forgive him.€€ƒx€And if he sin against thee seven times in a day, and seven times in a day be converted unto thee, saying: I repent: forgive him.€€ƒx6And the apostles said to the Lord: Increase our faith.€€ƒx¹And the Lord said: If you had faith like to a grain of mustard seed, you might say to this mulberry tree: Be thou rooted up and be thou transplanted into the sea. And it would obey you.€€ƒx’But which of you, having a servant ploughing or feeding cattle, will say to him, when he is come from the field: Immediately go. Sit down to meat.€€ƒx”And will not rather say to him: Make ready my supper and gird thyself and serve me, whilst I eat and drink; and afterwards thou shalt eat and drink?€€ƒxGDoth he thank that servant for doing the things which he commanded him?€€ƒx¦I think not. So you also, when you shall have done all these things that are commanded you, say: We are unprofitable servants; we have done that which we ought to do.€€ƒxfAnd it came to pass, as he was going to Jerusalem, he passed through the midst of Samaria and Galilee.€€ƒxbAnd as he entered into a certain town, there met him ten men that were lepers, who stood afar off.€€ƒxCAnd lifted up their voice, saying: Jesus, Master, have mercy on us.€€ƒxwWhom when he saw, he said: Go, shew yourselves to the priests. And it came to pass, as they went, they were made clean.€€ƒxaAnd one of them, when he saw that he was made clean, went back, with a loud voice glorifying God.€€ƒxQAnd he fell on his face before his feet, giving thanks. And this was a Samaritan.€€ƒxKAnd Jesus answering, said: Were not ten made clean? And where are the nine?€€ƒxIThere is no one found to return and give glory to God, but this stranger.€€ƒxJAnd he said to him: Arise, go thy way; for thy faith hath made thee whole.€€ƒx‘And being asked by the Pharisees when the kingdom of God should come, he answering them and said: The kingdom of God cometh not with observation.€€ƒx_Neither shall they say: Behold here, or behold there. For lo, the kingdom of God is within you.€€ƒx‚And he said to his disciples: The days will come when you shall desire to see one day of the Son of man. And you shall not see it.€€ƒxTAnd they will say to you: See here, and see there. Go ye not after, nor follow them.€€ƒx‹For as the lightning that lighteneth from under heaven shineth unto the parts that are under heaven, so shall the Son of man be in his day.€€ƒxHBut first he must suffer many things and be rejected by this generation.€€ƒx]And as it came to pass in the days of Noe, so shall it be also in the days of the Son of man.€€ƒxThey did eat and drink, they married wives and were given in marriage, until the day that Noe entered into the ark and the flood came and destroyed them all.€€ƒxuLikewise as it came to pass in the days of Lot. They did eat and drink, they bought and sold, they planted and built.€€ƒxkAnd in the day that Lot went out of Sodom, it rained fire and brimstone from heaven and destroyed them all.€€ƒxGEven thus shall it be in the day when the Son of man shall be revealed.€€ƒx¾In that hour, he that shall be on the housetop, and his goods in the house, let him not go down to take them away: and he that shall be in the field, in like manner, let him not return back.€€ƒtRemember Lot's wife.€€ƒxcWhosoever shall seek to save his life shall lose it: and whosoever shall lose it shall preserve it.€€ƒxrI say to you: In that night there shall be two men in one bed. The one shall be taken and the other shall be left.€€ƒx¬Two women shall be grinding together. The one shall be taken and the other shall be left. Two men shall be in the field. The one shall be taken and the other shall be left.€€ƒx(They answering, say to him: Where, Lord?€€ƒxcWho said to them: Wheresoever the body shall be, thither will the eagles also be gathered together.€€ƒ`€€ƒxSAnd he spoke also a parable to them, that we ought always to pray and not to faint,€€˜+ƒxcAnd there was a certain widow in that city; and she came to him, saying: Avenge me of my adversary.€€ƒxpAnd he would not for a long time. But afterwards he said within himself: Although I fear not God nor regard man,€€ƒxeYet because this widow is troublesome to me, I will avenge her, lest continually coming she weary me.€€ƒx4And the Lord said: Hear what the unjust judge saith.€€ƒxkAnd will not God revenge his elect who cry to him day and night? And will he have patience in their regard?€€ƒxI say to you that he will quickly revenge them. But yet the Son of man, when he cometh, shall he find, think you, faith on earth?€€ƒx^And to some who trusted in themselves as just and despised others, he spoke also this parable:€€ƒxUTwo men went up into the temple to pray: the one a Pharisee and the other a publican.€€ƒx¨The Pharisee standing, prayed thus with himself: O God, I give thee thanks that I am not as the rest of men, extortioners, unjust, adulterers, as also is this publican.€€ƒx<I fast twice in a week: I give tithes of all that I possess.€€ƒxœAnd the publican, standing afar off, would not so much as lift up his eyes towards heaven; but struck his breast, saying: O God, be merciful to me a sinner.€€ƒx¹I say to you, this man went down into his house justified rather than the other: because every one that exalteth himself shall be humbled: and he that humbleth himself shall be exalted.€€ƒxrAnd they brought unto him also infants, that he might touch them. Which when the disciples saw, they rebuked them.€€ƒx}But Jesus, calling them together, said: Suffer children to come to me and forbid them not: for of such is the kingdom of God.€€ƒxfAmen, I say to you: Whosoever shall not receive the kingdom of God as a child shall not enter into it.€€ƒx`And a certain ruler asked him, saying: Good master, what shall I do to possess everlasting life?€€ƒxNAnd Jesus said to him: Why dost thou call me good? None is good but God alone.€€ƒxªThou knowest the commandments: Thou shalt not kill. Thou shalt not commit adultery: Thou shalt not steal: Thou shalt not bear false witness: Honour thy father and mother.€€ƒx5Who said: All these things have I kept from my youth.€€ƒx¼Which when Jesus had heard, he said to him: Yet one thing is wanting to thee. Sell all whatever thou hast and give to the poor: and thou shalt have treasure in heaven. And come, follow me.€€ƒxEHe having heard these things, became sorrowful: for he was very rich.€€ƒxqAnd Jesus seeing him become sorrowful, said: How hardly shall they that have riches enter into the kingdom of God€€ƒxvFor it is easier for a camel to pass through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter into the kingdom of God.€€ƒx3And they that heard it said: Who then can be saved?€€ƒxOHe said to them: The things that are impossible with men are possible with God.€€ƒxHThen Peter said: Behold, we have left all things and have followed thee.€€ƒx”Who said to them: Amen, I say to you, there is no man that hath left home or parents or brethren or wife or children, for the kingdom of God's sake,€€ƒx`Who shall not receive much more in this present time, and in the world to come life everlasting.€€ƒx·Then Jesus took unto him the twelve and said to them: Behold, we go up to Jerusalem; and all things shall be accomplished which were written by the prophets concerning the Son of man.€€ƒxYFor he shall be delivered to the Gentiles and shall be mocked and scourged and spit upon.€€ƒxdAnd after they have scourged him, they will put him to death. And the third day he shall rise again.€€ƒx}And they understood none of these things, and this word was hid from them: and they understood not the things that were said.€€ƒxiNow it came to pass, when he drew nigh to Jericho, that a certain blind man sat by the way side, begging.€€ƒxEAnd when he heard the multitude passing by, he asked what this meant.€€ƒx8And they told him that Jesus of Nazareth was passing by.€€ƒx@And he cried out, saying: Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me.€€ƒxAnd they that went before rebuked him, that he should hold his peace: but he cried out much more: Son of David, have mercy on me.€€ƒxbAnd Jesus standing, commanded him to be brought unto him. And when he was come near, he asked him,€€ƒxLSaying; What wilt thou that I do to thee? But he said: Lord, that I may see.€€ƒxIAnd Jesus said to him: Receive thy sight: thy faith hath made thee whole.€€ƒxrAnd immediately he saw and followed him, glorifying God. And all the people, when they saw it, gave praise to God.€€ƒ`€€ƒx'And entering he walked through Jericho.€€˜0ƒxeAnd he sought to see Jesus who he was: and he could not for the crowd, because he was low of stature.€€ƒxkAnd running before, he climbed up into a sycamore tree, that he might see him: for he was to pass that way.€€ƒx˜And when Jesus was come to the place, looking up, he saw him and said to him: Zacheus, make haste and come down: for this day I must abide in thy house.€€ƒx:And he made haste and came down and received him with joy.€€ƒxhAnd when all saw it, they murmured, saying, that he was gone to be a guest with a man that was a sinner.€€ƒx¢But Zacheus standing, said to the Lord: Behold, Lord, the half of my goods I give to the poor; and if I have wronged any man of any thing, I restore him fourfold.€€ƒxaJesus said to him: This day is salvation come to this house, because he also is a son of Abraham.€€ƒxCFor the Son of man is come to seek and to save that which was lost.€€ƒx´As they were hearing these things, he added and spoke a parable, because he was nigh to Jerusalem and because they thought that the kingdom of God should immediately be manifested.€€ƒxnHe said therefore: a certain nobleman went into a far country, to receive for himself a kingdom and to return.€€ƒxZAnd calling his ten servants, he gave them ten pounds and said to them: Trade till I come.€€ƒxtBut his citizens hated him and they sent an embassage after him, saying: We will not have this man to reign over us.€€ƒxËAnd it came to pass that he returned, having received the kingdom: and he commanded his servants to be called, to whom he had given the money, that he might know how much every man had gained by trading,€€ƒxBAnd the first came saying: Lord, thy pound hath gained ten pounds.€€ƒx…And he said to him: Well done, thou good servant, because thou hast been faithful in a little, thou shalt have power over ten cities.€€ƒxEAnd the second came, saying: Lord, thy pound hath gained five pounds.€€ƒx2And he said to him: Be thou also over five cities.€€ƒx`And another came, saying: Lord, behold here is thy pound, which I have kept laid up in a napkin.€€ƒxFor I feared thee, because thou art an austere man: thou takest up what thou didst not lay down: and thou reapest that which thou didst not sow.€€ƒxµHe saith to him: Out of thy own mouth I judge thee, thou wicked servant. Thou knewest that I was an austere man, taking up what I laid not down and reaping that which I did not sow.€€ƒxnAnd why then didst thou not give my money into the bank, that at my coming I might have exacted it with usury?€€ƒxhAnd he said to them that stood by: Take the pound away from him and give it to him that hath ten pounds.€€ƒx/And they said to him: Lord, he hath ten pounds.€€ƒxBut I say to you that to every one that hath shall be given, and he shall abound: and from him that hath not, even that which he hath shall be taken from him.€€ƒxnBut as for those my enemies, who would not have me reign over them, bring them hither and kill them before me.€€ƒxDAnd having said these things, he went before, going up to Jerusalem.€€ƒxAnd it came to pass, when he was come nigh to Bethphage and Bethania, unto the mount called Olivet, he sent two of his disciples,€€ƒx¹Saying: Go into the town which is over against you, at your entering into which you shall find the colt of an ass tied, on which no man ever hath sitten: loose him and bring him hither.€€ƒx{And if any man shall ask you: Why do you loose him? You shall say thus unto him: Because the Lord hath need of his service.€€ƒxYAnd they that were sent went their way and found the colt standing, as he said unto them.€€ƒx[And as they were loosing the colt, the owners thereof said to them: Why loose you the colt?€€ƒx1But they said: Because the Lord hath need of him.€€ƒx^And they brought him to Jesus. And casting their garments on the colt, they set Jesus thereon.€€ƒx@And as he went, they spread their clothes underneath in the way.€€ƒx¹And when he was now coming near the descent of Mount Olivet, the whole multitude of his disciples began with joy to praise God with a loud voice, for all the mighty works they had seen,€€ƒxbSaying: Blessed be the king who cometh in the name of the Lord! Peace in heaven and glory on high!€€ƒxaAnd some of the Pharisees, from amongst the multitude, said to him: Master, rebuke thy disciples.€€ƒx\To whom he said: I say to you that if these shall hold their peace, the stones will cry out.€€ƒx@And when he drew near, seeing the city, he wept over it, saying:€€ƒx|If thou also hadst known, and that in this thy day, the things that are to thy peace: but now they are hidden from thy eyes.€€ƒx‰For the days shall come upon thee: and thy enemies shall cast a trench about thee and compass thee round and straiten thee on every side,€€ƒx²And beat thee flat to the ground, and thy children who are in thee. And they shall not leave in thee a stone upon a stone: because thou hast not known the time of thy visitation.€€ƒx_And entering into the temple, he began to cast out them that sold therein and them that bought.€€ƒxfSaying to them: It is written: My house is the house of prayer. But you have made it a den of thieves.€€ƒx‚And he was teaching daily in the temple. And the chief priests and the scribes and the rulers of the people sought to destroy him.€€ƒxYAnd they found not what to do to him: for all the people were very attentive to hear him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¶And it came to pass that on one of the days, as he was teaching the people in the temple and preaching the gospel, the chief priests and the scribes, with the ancients, met together,€€˜/ƒxLAnd Jesus answering, said to them: I will also ask you one thing. Answer me:€€ƒx3The baptism of John, was it from heaven, or of men?€€ƒx{But they thought within themselves, saying: If we shall say, From heaven: he will say: Why then did you not believe in him?€€ƒxfBut if we say, of men: the whole people will stone us. For they are persuaded that John was a prophet.€€ƒx3And they answered that they knew not whence it was.€€ƒxRAnd Jesus said to them: Neither do I tell you by what authority I do these things.€€ƒx“And he began to speak to the people this parable: A certain man planted a vineyard and let it out to husbandmen: and he was abroad for a long time.€€ƒx•And at the season he sent a servant to the husbandmen, that they should give him of the fruit of the vineyard. Who, beating him, sent him away empty.€€ƒxoAnd again he sent another servant. But they beat him also and, treating him reproachfully, sent him away empty.€€ƒxHAnd again he sent the third: and they wounded him also and cast him out.€€ƒx‡Then the lord of the vineyard said: What shall I do? I will send my beloved son. It may be, when they see him, they will reverence him.€€ƒx‹Whom, when the husbandmen saw, they thought within themselves, saying: This is the heir. Let us kill him, that the inheritance may be ours.€€ƒxmSo casting him out of the vineyard, they killed him. What therefore will the lord of the vineyard do to them?€€ƒxHe will come and will destroy these husbandmen and will give the vineyard to others. Which they hearing, said to him: God forbid.€€ƒx“But he looking on them, said: What is this then that is written, The stone, which the builders rejected, the same is become the head of the corner?€€ƒxvWhosoever shall fall upon that stone shall be bruised: and upon whomsoever it shall fall, it will grind him to powder.€€ƒxAnd the chief priests and the scribes sought to lay hands on him the same hour: but they feared the people, for they knew that he spoke this parable to them.€€ƒxÆAnd being upon the watch, they sent spies, who should feign themselves just, that they might take hold of him in his words, that they might deliver him up to the authority and power of the governor.€€ƒx And they asked him, saying: Master, we know that thou speakest and teachest rightly: and thou dost not respect any person, but teachest the way of God in truth.€€ƒx5Is it lawful for us to give tribute to Caesar, or no?€€ƒx@But he, considering their guile, said to them: Why tempt you me?€€ƒx\Shew me a penny. Whose image and inscription hath it? They answering, said to him: Caesar's.€€ƒxtAnd he said to them: Render therefore to Caesar the things, that are Caesar's: and to God the things that are God's.€€ƒxlAnd they could not reprehend his word before the people: and wondering at his answer, they held their peace.€€ƒxiAnd there came to him some of the Sadducees, who deny that there is any resurrection: and they asked him,€€ƒx´Saying: Master, Moses wrote unto us: If any man's brother die, having a wife, and he leave no children, that his brother should take her to wife and raise up seed unto his brother.€€ƒxYThere were therefore seven brethren: and the first took a wife and died without children.€€ƒx:And the next took her to wife: and he also died childless.€€ƒx^And the third took her. And in like manner, all the seven: and they left no children and died.€€ƒx Last of all the woman died also.€€ƒxbIn the resurrection therefore, whose wife of them shall she be? For all the seven had her to wife.€€ƒxSAnd Jesus said to them: The children of this world marry and are given in marriage:€€ƒx„But they that shall be accounted worthy of that world and of the resurrection from the dead shall neither be married nor take wives.€€ƒxƒNeither can they die any more for they are equal to the angels and are the children of God, being the children of the resurrection.€€ƒx“Now that the dead rise again, Moses also shewed at the bush, when he called the Lord: The God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob.€€ƒxJFor he is not the God of the dead, but of the living: for all live to him.€€ƒxLAnd some of the scribes answering, said to him: Master, thou hast said well.€€ƒx9And after that they durst not ask him any more questions.€€ƒxBBut he said to them: How say they that Christ is the son of David?€€ƒxcAnd David himself saith in the book of Psalms: The Lord said to my Lord, sit thou on my right hand,€€ƒx&Till I make thy enemies thy footstool.€€ƒx3David then calleth him Lord. And how is he his son?€€ƒx?And in the hearing of all the people, he said to his disciples:€€ƒx¦Beware of the scribes, who desire to walk in long robes and love salutations in the market place and the first chairs in the synagogues and the chief rooms at feasts:€€ƒx]Who devour the houses of widows, feigning long prayer. These shall receive greater damnation.€€ƒ`€€ƒxGAnd looking on, he saw the rich men cast their gifts into the treasury.€€˜&ƒxWAnd he said: Verily, I say to you that this poor widow hath cast in more than they all.€€ƒxƒFor all these have of their abundance cast into the offerings of God: but she of her want hath cast in all the living that she had.€€ƒxXAnd some saying of the temple that it was adorned with goodly stones and gifts, he said:€€ƒxƒThese things which you see, the days will come in which there shall not be left a stone upon a stone that shall not be thrown down.€€ƒxAnd they asked him, saying: Master, when shall these things be? And what shall be the sign when they shall begin to come to pass?€€ƒxWho said: Take heed you be not seduced: for many will come in my name, saying: I am he and the time is at hand. Go ye not therefore after them.€€ƒxˆAnd when you shall hear of wars and seditions, be not terrified. These things must first come to pass: but the end is not yet presently.€€ƒxTThen he said to them: Nation shall rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom.€€ƒxŠAnd there shall be great earthquakes in divers places and pestilences and famines and terrors from heaven: and there shall be great signs.€€ƒxÃBut before all these things, they will lay their hands on you and persecute you, delivering you up to the synagogues and into prisons, dragging you before kings and governors, for my name's sake.€€ƒx-And it shall happen unto you for a testimony.€€ƒxPLay it up therefore in your hearts, not to meditate before how you shall answer:€€ƒxkFor I will give you a mouth and wisdom, which all your adversaries shall not be able to resist and gainsay.€€ƒxwAnd you shall be betrayed by your parents and brethren and kinsmen and friends: and some of you they will put to death.€€ƒx5And you shall be hated by all men for my name's sake.€€ƒx)But a hair of your head shall not perish.€€ƒx.In your patience you shall possess your souls.€€ƒxpAnd when you shall see Jerusalem compassed about with an army, then know that the desolation thereof is at hand.€€ƒxŸThen let those who are in Judea flee to the mountains: and those who are in the midst thereof depart out: and those who are in the countries not enter into it.€€ƒxXFor these are the days of vengeance, that all things may be fulfilled, that are written.€€ƒxŠBut woe to them that are with child and give suck in those days: for there shall be great distress in the land and wrath upon this people.€€ƒx½And they shall fall by the edge of the sword and shall be led away captives into all nations: and Jerusalem shall be trodden down by the Gentiles till the times of the nations be fulfilled.€€ƒxµAnd there shall be signs in the sun and in the moon and in the stars; and upon the earth distress of nations, by reason of the confusion of the roaring of the sea, and of the waves:€€ƒx}Men withering away for fear and expectation of what shall come upon the whole world. For the powers of heaven shall be moved.€€ƒxWAnd then they shall see the Son of man coming in a cloud, with great power and majesty.€€ƒxpBut when these things begin to come to pass, look up and lift up your heads, because your redemption is at hand.€€ƒxFAnd he spoke to them a similitude. See the fig tree and all the trees:€€ƒxDWhen they now shoot forth their fruit, you know that summer is nigh;€€ƒxcSo you also, when you shall see these things come to pass, know that the kingdom of God is at hand.€€ƒxUAmen, I say to you, this generation shall not pass away till all things be fulfilled.€€ƒxCHeaven and earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away.€€ƒx¥And take heed to yourselves, lest perhaps your hearts be overcharged with surfeiting and drunkenness and the cares of this life: and that day come upon you suddenly.€€ƒxPFor as a snare shall it come upon all that sit upon the face of the whole earth.€€ƒx›Watch ye, therefore, praying at all times, that you may be accounted worthy to escape all these things that are to come and to stand before the Son of man.€€ƒxwAnd in the daytime, he was teaching in the temple: but at night going out, he abode in the mount that is called Olivet.€€ƒxOAnd all the people came early in the morning to him in the temple, to hear him.€€ƒ`€€ƒxJNow the feast of unleavened bread, which is called the pasch, was at hand.€€˜GƒxKAnd Satan entered into Judas, who was surnamed Iscariot, one of the twelve.€€ƒxgAnd he went and discoursed with the chief priests and the magistrates, how he might betray him to them.€€ƒx4And they were glad and covenanted to give him money.€€ƒxYAnd he promised. And he sought opportunity to betray him in the absence of the multitude.€€ƒxdAnd the day of the unleavened bread came, on which it was necessary that the pasch should be killed.€€ƒxVAnd he sent Peter and John, saying: Go, and prepare for us the pasch, that we may eat.€€ƒx/But they said: Where wilt thou that we prepare?€€ƒxAnd he said to them: Behold, as you go into the city, there shall meet you a man carrying a pitcher of water: follow him into the house where he entereth in.€€ƒx‘And you shall say to the goodman of the house: The master saith to thee: Where is the guest chamber, where I may eat the pasch with my disciples?€€ƒxGAnd he will shew you a large dining room, furnished. And there prepare.€€ƒxFAnd they going, found as he had said to them and made ready the pasch.€€ƒxJAnd when the hour was come, he sat down: and the twelve apostles with him.€€ƒx\And he said to them: With desire I have desired to eat this pasch with you, before I suffer.€€ƒxcFor I say to you that from this time I will not eat it, till it be fulfilled in the kingdom of God.€€ƒxTAnd having taken the chalice, he gave thanks and said: Take and divide it among you.€€ƒx^For I say to you that I will not drink of the fruit of the vine, till the kingdom of God come.€€ƒxAnd taking bread, he gave thanks and brake and gave to them, saying: This is my body, which is given for you. Do this for a commemoration of me.€€ƒxIn like manner, the chalice also, after he had supped, saying: This is the chalice, the new testament in my blood, which shall be shed for you.€€ƒxJBut yet behold: the hand of him that betrayeth me is with me on the table.€€ƒx~And the Son of man indeed goeth, according to that which is determined: but yet, woe to that man by whom he shall be betrayed.€€ƒx[And they began to inquire among themselves, which of them it was that should do this thing.€€ƒxVAnd there was also a strife amongst them, which of them should seem to be the greater.€€ƒx{And he said to them: The kings of the Gentiles lord it over them; and they that have power over them are called beneficent.€€ƒxƒBut you not so: but he that is the greater among you, let him become as the younger: and he that is the leader, as he that serveth.€€ƒx•For which is greater, he that sitteth at table or he that serveth? Is not he that sitteth at table? But I am in the midst of you, as he that serveth.€€ƒx>And you are they who have continued with me in my temptations:€€ƒxBAnd I dispose to you, as my Father hath disposed to me, a kingdom;€€ƒxuThat you may eat and drink at my table, in my kingdom: and may sit upon thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel.€€ƒxfAnd the Lord said: Simon, Simon, behold Satan hath desired to have you, that he may sift you as wheat.€€ƒxjBut I have prayed for thee, that thy faith fail not: and thou, being once converted, confirm thy brethren.€€ƒxQWho said to him: Lord, I am ready to go with thee, both into prison and to death.€€ƒxˆAnd he said: I say to thee, Peter, the cock shall not crow this day, till thou thrice deniest that thou knowest me. And he said to them:€€ƒxIWhen I sent you without purse and scrip and shoes, did you want anything?€€ƒx±But they said: Nothing. Then said he unto them: But now he that hath a purse, let him take it, and likewise a scrip: and he that hath not, let him sell his coat and buy a sword.€€ƒx–For I say to you that this that is written must yet be fulfilled in me. And with the wicked was he reckoned. For the things concerning me have an end.€€ƒxTBut they said: Lord, behold, here are two swords. And he said to them: It is enough.€€ƒxmAnd going out, he went, according to his custom, to the Mount of Olives. And his disciples also followed him.€€ƒxXAnd when he was come to the place, he said to them: Pray, lest ye enter into temptation.€€ƒxQAnd he was withdrawn away from them a stone's cast. And kneeling down, he prayed.€€ƒxbSaying: Father, if thou wilt, remove this chalice from me: but yet not my will, but thine be done.€€ƒxoAnd there appeared to him an angel from heaven, strengthening him. And being in an agony, he prayed the longer.€€ƒxGAnd his sweat became as drops of blood, trickling down upon the ground.€€ƒxaAnd when he rose up from prayer and was come to the disciples, he found them sleeping for sorrow.€€ƒxPAnd he said to them: Why sleep you? Arise: pray: lest you enter into temptation.€€ƒx–As he was yet speaking, behold a multitude; and he that was called Judas, one of the twelve, went before them and drew near to Jesus, for to kiss him.€€ƒxJAnd Jesus said to him: Judas, dost thou betray the Son of man with a kiss?€€ƒxjAnd they that were about him, seeing what would follow, said to him: Lord, shall we strike with the sword?€€ƒxPAnd one of them struck the servant of the high priest and cut off his right ear.€€ƒx^But Jesus answering, said: Suffer ye thus far. And when he had touched his ear, he healed him.€€ƒx°And Jesus said to the chief priests and magistrates of the temple and the ancients, that were come unto him: Are ye come out, as it were against a thief, with swords and clubs?€€ƒxŠWhen I was daily with you in the temple, you did not stretch forth your hands against me: but this is your hour and the power of darkness.€€ƒx[And apprehending him, they led him to the high priest's house. But Peter followed afar off.€€ƒxtAnd when they had kindled a fire in the midst of the hall and were sitting about it, Peter was in the midst of them.€€ƒx‚Whom when a certain servant maid had seen sitting at the light and had earnestly beheld him, she said: This man also was with him.€€ƒx1But he denied him, saying: Woman, I know him not.€€ƒxoAnd after a little while, another seeing him, said: Thou also art one of them. But Peter said: O man, I am not.€€ƒx•And after the space, as it were of one hour, another certain man affirmed, saying: Of a truth, this man was also with him: for he is also a Galilean.€€ƒxiAnd Peter said: Man, I know not what thou sayest. And immediately, as he was yet speaking, the cock crew.€€ƒxAnd the Lord turning looked on Peter. And Peter remembered the word of the Lord, as he had said: Before the cock crow, thou shalt deny thrice.€€ƒx#And Peter going out, wept bitterly.€€ƒx4And the men that held him mocked him and struck him.€€ƒxmAnd they blindfolded him and smote his face. And they asked him saying: Prophesy: Who is it that struck thee?€€ƒx9And blaspheming, many other things they said against him.€€ƒx¶And as soon as it was day, the ancients of the people and the chief priests and scribes came together. And they brought him into their council saying: If thou be the Christ, tell us.€€ƒxCAnd he saith to them: If I shall tell you, you will not believe me.€€ƒxCAnd if I shall also ask you, you will not answer me, nor let me go.€€ƒxTBut hereafter the Son of man shall be sitting on the right hand of the power of God.€€ƒxNThen said they all: Art thou then the Son of God? Who said: You say that I am.€€ƒxeAnd they said: What need we any further testimony? For we ourselves have heard it from his own mouth.€€ƒ`€€ƒx>And the whole multitude of them, rising up, led him to Pilate.€€˜8ƒxdAnd Pilate asked him, saying: Art thou the king of the Jews? But he answering, said: Thou sayest it.€€ƒxXAnd Pilate said to the chief priests and to the multitudes: I find no cause in this man.€€ƒxƒBut they were more earnest, saying: He stirreth up the people, teaching throughout all Judea, beginning from Galilee to this place.€€ƒx=But Pilate hearing Galilee, asked if the man were of Galilee?€€ƒx‡And when he understood that he was of Herod's jurisdiction, he sent him away to Herod, who was also himself at Jerusalem in those days.€€ƒx¬And Herod seeing Jesus, was very glad: for he was desirous of a long time to see him, because he had heard many things of him; and he hoped to see some sign wrought by him.€€ƒxAAnd he questioned him in many words. But he answered him nothing.€€ƒxGAnd the chief priests and the scribes stood by, earnestly accusing him.€€ƒxvAnd Herod with his army set him at nought and mocked him, putting on him a white garment: and sent him back to Pilate.€€ƒxcAnd Herod and Pilate were made friends, that same day: for before they were enemies one to another.€€ƒxRAnd Pilate, calling together the chief priests and the magistrates and the people,€€ƒxÅSaid to them: You have presented unto me this man as one that perverteth the people. And behold I, having examined him before you, find no cause in this man, in those things wherein you accuse him.€€ƒxbNo, nor Herod neither. For, I sent you to him: and behold, nothing worthy of death is done to him.€€ƒx.I will chastise him therefore and release him.€€ƒxDNow of necessity he was to release unto them one upon the feast day.€€ƒxeBut the whole multitude together cried out, saying: Away with this man, and release unto us Barabbas:€€ƒxTWho, for a certain sedition made in the city and for a murder, was cast into prison.€€ƒx:And Pilate again spoke to them, desiring to release Jesus.€€ƒx7But they cried again, saying: Crucify him, Crucify him.€€ƒx•And he said to them the third time: Why, what evil hath this man done? I find no cause of death in him. I will chastise him therefore and let him go.€€ƒxiBut they were instant with loud voices, requiring that he might be crucified. And their voices prevailed.€€ƒx<And Pilate gave sentence that it should be as they required.€€ƒx”And he released unto them him who for murder and sedition had been cast into prison, whom they had desired. But Jesus he delivered up to their will.€€ƒxAnd as they led him away, they laid hold of one Simon of Cyrene, coming from the country; and they laid the cross on him to carry after Jesus.€€ƒx_And there followed him a great multitude of people and of women, who bewailed and lamented him.€€ƒxyBut Jesus turning to them, said: Daughters of Jerusalem, weep not over me; but weep for yourselves and for your children.€€ƒx—For behold, the days shall come, wherein they will say: Blessed are the barren and the wombs that have not borne and the paps that have not given suck.€€ƒxXThen shall they begin to say to the mountains: Fall upon us. And to the hills: Cover us.€€ƒxMFor if in the green wood they do these things, what shall be done in the dry?€€ƒxJAnd there were also two other malefactors led with him to be put to death.€€ƒxšAnd when they were come to the place which is called Calvary, they crucified him there: and the robbers, one on the right hand, and the other on the left.€€ƒxqAnd Jesus said: Father, forgive them, for they know not what they do. But they, dividing his garments, cast lots.€€ƒx—And the people stood beholding. And the rulers with them derided him, saying: He saved others: let him save himself, if he be Christ, the elect of God.€€ƒxIAnd the soldiers also mocked him, coming to him and offering him vinegar,€€ƒx:And saying: If thou be the king of the Jews, save thyself.€€ƒx{And there was also a superscription written over him in letters of Greek and Latin and Hebrew THIS IS THE KING OF THE JEWS.€€ƒxhAnd one of those robbers who were hanged blasphemed him, saying: If thou be Christ, save thyself and us.€€ƒxwBut the other answering, rebuked him, saying: Neither dost thou fear God, seeing; thou art under the same condemnation?€€ƒxaAnd we indeed justly: for we receive the due reward of our deeds. But this man hath done no evil.€€ƒxNAnd he said to Jesus: Lord, remember me when thou shalt come into thy kingdom.€€ƒxVAnd Jesus said to him: Amen I say to thee: This day thou shalt be with me in paradise.€€ƒxaAnd it was almost the sixth hour: and there was darkness over all the earth until the ninth hour.€€ƒxKAnd the sun was darkened, and the veil of the temple was rent in the midst.€€ƒx|And Jesus crying with a loud voice, said: Father, into thy hands I commend my spirit. And saying this, he gave up the ghost.€€ƒx\Now, the centurion, seeing what was done, glorified God, saying: Indeed this was a just man.€€ƒx‡And all the multitude of them that were come together to that sight and saw the things that were done returned, striking their breasts.€€ƒxqAnd all his acquaintance and the women that had followed him from Galilee stood afar off, beholding these things.€€ƒxTAnd behold there was a man named Joseph who was a counsellor, a good and a just man,€€ƒx‡(The same had not consented to their counsel and doings) of Arimathea, a city of Judea: who also himself looked for the kingdom of God.€€ƒx5This man went to Pilate and begged the body of Jesus.€€ƒxAnd taking him down, he wrapped him in fine linen and laid him in a sepulchre that was hewed in stone, wherein never yet any man had been laid.€€ƒx=And it was the day of the Parasceve: and the sabbath drew on.€€ƒxqAnd the women that were come with him from Galilee, following after, saw the sepulchre and how his body was laid.€€ƒxtAnd returning, they prepared spices and ointments: and on the sabbath day they rested, according to the commandment.€€ƒ`€€ƒx…And on the first day of the week, very early in the morning, they came to the sepulchre, bringing the spices which they had prepared.€€˜5ƒx8And going in, they found not the body of the Lord Jesus.€€ƒxvAnd it came to pass, as they were astonished in their mind at this, behold, two men stood by them, in shining apparel.€€ƒxˆAnd as they were afraid and bowed down their countenance towards the ground, they said unto them: Why seek you the living with the dead?€€ƒxYHe is not here, but is risen. Remember how he spoke unto you, when he was yet in Galilee,€€ƒxtSaying: The Son of man must be delivered into the hands of sinful men and be crucified and the third day rise again.€€ƒxAnd they remembered his words.€€ƒx`And going back from the sepulchre, they told all these things to the eleven and to all the rest.€€ƒx…And it was Mary Magdalen and Joanna and Mary of James and the other women that were with them, who told these things to the apostles.€€ƒxLAnd these words seemed to them as idle tales: and they did not believe them.€€ƒx¬But Peter rising up, ran to the sepulchre and, stooping down, he saw the linen cloths laid by themselves: and went away wondering in himself at that which was come to pass.€€ƒxlAnd behold, two of them went, the same day, to a town which was sixty furlongs from Jerusalem, named Emmaus.€€ƒx@And they talked together of all these things which had happened.€€ƒxzAnd it came to pass that while they talked and reasoned with themselves, Jesus himself also, drawing near, went with them.€€ƒx8But their eyes were held, that they should not know him.€€ƒxfAnd he said to them: What are these discourses that you hold one with another as you walk and are sad?€€ƒx³And the one of them, whose name was Cleophas, answering, said to him: Art thou only a stranger in Jerusalem, and hast not known the things that have been done there in these days?€€ƒx”To whom he said: What things? And they said: Concerning Jesus of Nazareth, who was a prophet, mighty in work and word before God and all the people.€€ƒx_And how our chief priests and princes delivered him to be condemned to death and crucified him.€€ƒxBut we hoped that it was he that should have redeemed Israel. And now besides all this, to-day is the third day since these things were done.€€ƒxhYea and certain women also of our company affrighted us who, before it was light, were at the sepulchre,€€ƒxkAnd not finding his body, came, saying that they had all seen a vision of angels, who say that he is alive.€€ƒxkAnd some of our people went to the sepulchre and found it so as the women had said: but him they found not.€€ƒxkThen he said to them: O foolish and slow of heart to believe in all things, Which the prophets have spoken.€€ƒxOOught not Christ to have suffered these things and so, to enter into his glory?€€ƒx|And beginning at Moses and all the prophets, he expounded to them in all the scriptures the things that were concerning him.€€ƒxbAnd they drew nigh to the town whither they were going: and he made as though he would go farther.€€ƒx…But they constrained him, saying: Stay with us, because it is towards evening and the day is now far spent. And he went in with them.€€ƒxlAnd it came to pass, whilst he was at table with them, he took bread and blessed and brake and gave to them.€€ƒxRAnd their eyes were opened: and they knew him. And he vanished out of their sight.€€ƒx€And they said one to the other: Was not our heart burning within us, whilst he spoke in the way and opened to us the scriptures?€€ƒx†And rising up, the same hour, they went back to Jerusalem: and they found the eleven gathered together, and those that were with them,€€ƒx<Saying: The Lord is risen indeed and hath appeared to Simon.€€ƒx_And they told what things were done in the way: and how they knew him in the breaking of bread.€€ƒx„Now, whilst they were speaking these things, Jesus stood in the midst of them and saith to them: Peace be to you. It is I: Fear not.€€ƒxHBut they being troubled and frightened, supposed that they saw a spirit.€€ƒxTAnd he said to them: Why are you troubled, and why do thoughts arise in your hearts?€€ƒxzSee my hands and feet, that it is I myself. Handle, and see: for a spirit hath not flesh and bones, as you see me to have.€€ƒx=And when he had said this, he shewed them his hands and feet.€€ƒx^But while they yet believed not and wondered for joy, he said: Have you here any thing to eat?€€ƒx?And they offered him a piece of a broiled fish and a honeycomb.€€ƒxGAnd when he had eaten before them, taking the remains, he gave to them.€€ƒxÛAnd he said to them: These are the words which I spoke to you while I was yet with you, that all things must needs be fulfilled which are written in the law of Moses and in the prophets and in the psalms, concerning me.€€ƒxNThen he opened their understanding, that they might understand the scriptures.€€ƒx}And he said to them: Thus it is written, and thus it behoved Christ to suffer and to rise again from the dead, the third day:€€ƒxpAnd that penance and remission of sins should be preached in his name, unto all nations, beginning at Jerusalem.€€ƒx&And you are witnesses of these things.€€ƒxrAnd I send the promise of my Father upon you: but stay you in the city till you be endued with power from on high.€€ƒxRAnd he led them out as far as Bethania: and lifting up his hands, he blessed them.€€ƒx`And it came to pass, whilst he blessed them, he departed from them and was carried up to heaven.€€ƒx9And they adoring went back into Jerusalem with great joy.€€ƒxDAnd they were always in the temple, praising and blessing God. Amen.€€ƒrThe Gospel of John€€ƒ`€€dJohn¤dnamedJohniosis_namedJohnlabbreviationdJohnhchapters•˜3ƒxLAll things were made by him: and without him was made nothing that was made.€€ƒx3In him was life: and the life was the light of men.€€ƒxJAnd the light shineth in darkness: and the darkness did not comprehend it.€€ƒx3There was a man sent from God, whose name was John.€€ƒxdThis man came for a witness, to give testimony of the light, that all men might believe through him.€€ƒx=He was not the light, but was to give testimony of the light.€€ƒxRThat was the true light, which enlighteneth every man that cometh into this world.€€ƒxOHe was in the world: and the world was made by him: and the world knew him not.€€ƒx3He came unto his own: and his own received him not.€€ƒxmBut as many as received him, he gave them power to be made the sons of God, to them that believe in his name.€€ƒx]Who are born, not of blood, nor of the will of the flesh, nor of the will of man, but of God.€€ƒx˜And the Word was made flesh and dwelt among us (and we saw his glory, the glory as it were of the only begotten of the Father), full of grace and truth.€€ƒxJohn beareth witness of him and crieth out, saying: This was he of whom I spoke: He that shall come after me is preferred before me: because he was before me.€€ƒx=And of his fulness we all have received: and grace for grace.€€ƒxEFor the law was given by Moses: grace and truth came by Jesus Christ.€€ƒxpNo man hath seen God at any time: the only begotten Son who is in the Bosom of the Father, he hath declared him.€€ƒxzAnd this is the testimony of John, when the Jews sent from Jerusalem priests and Levites to him, to ask him: Who art thou?€€ƒxIAnd he confessed and did not deny: and he confessed: I am not the Christ.€€ƒxpAnd they asked him: What then? Art thou Elias? And he said: I am not. Art thou the prophet? And he answered: No.€€ƒxyThey said therefore unto him: Who art thou, that we may give an answer to them that sent us? What sayest thou of thyself?€€ƒxwHe said: I am the voice of one crying in the wilderness, make straight the way of the Lord, as said the prophet Isaias.€€ƒx.And they that were sent were of the Pharisees.€€ƒxrAnd they asked him and said to him: Why then dost thou baptize, if thou be not Christ, nor Elias, nor the prophet?€€ƒxrJohn answered them, saying: I baptize with water: but there hath stood one in the midst of you, whom you know not.€€ƒxxThe same is he that shall come after me, who is preferred before me: the latchet of whose shoe I am not worthy to loose.€€ƒxPThese things were done in Bethania, beyond the Jordan, where John was baptizing.€€ƒx‚The next day, John saw Jesus coming to him; and he saith: Behold the Lamb of God. Behold him who taketh away the sin of the world.€€ƒxmThis is he of whom I said: After me there cometh a man, who is preferred before me: because he was before me.€€ƒxiAnd I knew him not: but that he may be made manifest in Israel, therefore am I come baptizing with water.€€ƒxoAnd John gave testimony, saying: I saw the Spirit coming down, as a dove from heaven; and he remained upon him.€€ƒx¿And I knew him not: but he who sent me to baptize with water said to me: He upon whom thou shalt see the Spirit descending and remaining upon him, he it is that baptizeth with the Holy Ghost.€€ƒx<And I saw: and I gave testimony that this is the Son of God.€€ƒx7The next day again John stood and two of his disciples.€€ƒx>And beholding Jesus walking, he saith: Behold the Lamb of God.€€ƒx?And the two disciples heard him speak: and they followed Jesus.€€ƒx¨And Jesus turning and seeing them following him, saith to them: What seek you? Who said to him: Rabbi (which is to say, being interpreted, Master), where dwellest thou?€€ƒx…He saith to them: Come and see. They came and saw where he abode: and they stayed with him that day. Now it was about the tenth hour.€€ƒxbAnd Andrew, the brother of Simon Peter, was one of the two who had heard of John and followed him.€€ƒxxHe findeth first his brother Simon and saith to him: We have found the Messias, which is, being interpreted, the Christ.€€ƒx—And he brought him to Jesus. And Jesus looking upon him, said: Thou art Simon the son of Jona. Thou shalt be called Cephas, which is interpreted Peter.€€ƒxoOn the following day, he would go forth into Galilee: and he findeth Philip, And Jesus saith to him: follow me.€€ƒx:Now Philip was of Bethsaida, the city of Andrew and Peter.€€ƒx—Philip findeth Nathanael and saith to him: We have found him of whom Moses, in the law and the prophets did write, Jesus the son of Joseph of Nazareth.€€ƒxgAnd Nathanael said to him: Can any thing of good come from Nazareth? Philip saith to him: Come and see.€€ƒxmJesus saw Nathanael coming to him and he saith of him: Behold an Israelite indeed, in whom there is no guile.€€ƒxNathanael saith to him: Whence knowest thou me? Jesus answered and said to him: Before that Philip called thee, when thou wast under the fig tree, I saw thee.€€ƒx]Nathanael answered him and said: Rabbi: Thou art the Son of God. Thou art the King of Israel.€€ƒx’Jesus answered and said to him: Because I said unto thee, I saw thee under the fig tree, thou believest: greater things than these shalt thou see.€€ƒx’And he saith to him: Amen, amen, I say to you, you shall see the heaven opened and the angels of God ascending and descending upon the Son of man.€€ƒ`€€ƒx^And the third day, there was a marriage in Cana of Galilee: and the mother of Jesus was there.€€˜ƒxJAnd the wine failing, the mother of Jesus saith to him: They have no wine.€€ƒxWAnd Jesus saith to her: Woman, what is that to me and to thee? My hour is not yet come.€€ƒxGHis mother saith to the waiters: Whatsoever he shall say to you, do ye.€€ƒxNow there were set there six waterpots of stone, according to the manner of the purifying of the Jews, containing two or three measures apiece.€€ƒxXJesus saith to them: Fill the waterpots with water. And they filled them up to the brim.€€ƒxgAnd Jesus saith to them: Draw out now and carry to the chief steward of the feast. And they carried it.€€ƒx­And when the chief steward had tasted the water made wine and knew not whence it was, but the waiters knew who had drawn the water: the chief steward calleth the bridegroom,€€ƒx¡And saith to him: Every man at first setteth forth good wine, and when men have well drunk, then that which is worse. But thou hast kept the good wine until now.€€ƒxtThis beginning of miracles did Jesus in Cana of Galilee and manifested his glory. And his disciples believed in him.€€ƒx„After this, he went down to Capharnaum, he and his mother and his brethren and his disciples: and they remained there not many days.€€ƒxFAnd the pasch of the Jews was at hand: and Jesus went up to Jerusalem.€€ƒxfAnd he found in the temple them that sold oxen and sheep and doves, and the changers of money sitting.€€ƒxÈAnd when he had made, as it were, a scourge of little cords, he drove them all out of the temple, the sheep also and the oxen: and the money of the changers he poured out, and the tables he overthrew.€€ƒxuAnd to them that sold doves he said: Take these things hence, and make not the house of my Father a house of traffic.€€ƒxZAnd his disciples remembered, that it was written: The zeal of thy house hath eaten me up.€€ƒxpThe Jews, therefore, answered, and said to him: What sign dost thou shew unto us, seeing thou dost these things?€€ƒx[Jesus answered and said to them: Destroy this temple; and in three days I will raise it up.€€ƒxmThe Jews then said: Six and forty years was this temple in building; and wilt thou raise it up in three days?€€ƒx'But he spoke of the temple of his body.€€ƒx¢When therefore he was risen again from the dead, his disciples remembered that he had said this: and they believed the scripture and the word that Jesus had said.€€ƒx|Now when he was at Jerusalem, at the pasch, upon the festival day, many believed in his name, seeing his signs which he did.€€ƒxDBut Jesus did not trust himself unto them: for that he knew all men,€€ƒx]And because he needed not that any should give testimony of man: for he knew what was in man.€€ƒ`€€ƒxKAnd there was a man of the Pharisees, named Nicodemus, a ruler of the Jews.€€˜$ƒxxJesus answered and said to him: Amen, amen, I say to thee, unless a man be born again, he cannot see the kingdom of God.€€ƒx‚Nicodemus saith to him: How can a man be born when he is old? Can he enter a second time into his mother's womb and be born again?€€ƒx‹Jesus answered: Amen, amen, I say to thee, unless a man be born again of water and the Holy Ghost, he cannot enter into the kingdom of God.€€ƒxYThat which is born of the flesh is flesh: and that which is born of the Spirit is spirit.€€ƒx7Wonder not that I said to thee: You must be born again.€€ƒx¦The Spirit breatheth where he will and thou hearest his voice: but thou knowest not whence he cometh and whither he goeth. So is every one that is born of the Spirit.€€ƒxANicodemus answered and said to him: How can these things be done?€€ƒxZJesus answered and said to him: Art thou a master in Israel, and knowest not these things?€€ƒxyAmen, amen, I say to thee that we speak what we know and we testify what we have seen: and you receive not our testimony.€€ƒx{If I have spoken to you earthly things, and you believe not: how will you believe, if I shall speak to you heavenly things?€€ƒxiAnd no man hath ascended into heaven, but he that descended from heaven, the Son of man who is in heaven.€€ƒxVAnd as Moses lifted up the serpent in the desert, so must the Son of man be lifted up:€€ƒxNThat whosoever believeth in him may not perish, but may have life everlasting.€€ƒxŒFor God so loved the world, as to give his only begotten Son: that whosoever believeth in him may not perish, but may have life everlasting.€€ƒxdFor God sent not his Son into the world, to judge the world: but that the world may be saved by him.€€ƒxHe that believeth in him is not judged. But he that doth not believe is already judged: because he believeth not in the name of the only begotten Son of God.€€ƒx‹And this is the judgment: Because the light is come into the world and men loved darkness rather than the light: for their works were evil.€€ƒxnFor every one that doth evil hateth the light and cometh not to the light, that his works may not be reproved.€€ƒxnBut he that doth truth cometh to the light, that his works may be made manifest: because they are done in God.€€ƒxsAfter these things, Jesus and his disciples came into the land of Judea: and there he abode with them and baptized.€€ƒxuAnd John also was baptizing in Ennon near Salim: because there was much water there. And they came and were baptized.€€ƒx&For John was not yet cast into prison.€€ƒxbAnd there arose a question between some of John's disciples and the Jews, concerning purification.€€ƒx£And they came to John and said to him: Rabbi, he that was with thee beyond the Jordan, to whom thou gavest testimony: behold, he baptizeth and all men come to him.€€ƒx[John answered and said: A man cannot receive any thing, unless it be given him from heaven.€€ƒxbYou yourselves do bear me witness that I said that I am not Christ, but that I am sent before him.€€ƒxÃHe that hath the bride is the bridegroom: but the friend of the bridegroom, who standeth and heareth Him, rejoiceth with joy because of the bridegroom's voice. This my joy therefore is fulfilled.€€ƒx&He must increase: but I must decrease.€€ƒx›He that cometh from above is above all. He that is of the earth, of the earth he is, and of the earth he speaketh. He that cometh from heaven is above all.€€ƒxXAnd what he hath seen and heard, that he testifieth: and no man receiveth his testimony.€€ƒxJHe that hath received his testimony hath set to his seal that God is true.€€ƒxaFor he whom God hath sent speaketh the words of God: for God doth not give the Spirit by measure.€€ƒxFThe Father loveth the Son: and he hath given all things into his hand.€€ƒxHe that believeth in the Son hath life everlasting: but he that believeth not the Son shall not see life: but the wrath of God abideth on him.€€ƒ`€€ƒxuWhen Jesus therefore understood the Pharisees had heard that Jesus maketh more disciples and baptizeth more than John€€˜6ƒx*He left Judea and went again into Galilee.€€ƒx0And he was of necessity to pass through Samaria.€€ƒxsHe cometh therefore to a city of Samaria, which is called Sichar, near the land which Jacob gave to his son Joseph.€€ƒxNow Jacob's well was there. Jesus therefore, being wearied with his journey, sat thus on the well. It was about the sixth hour.€€ƒxUThere cometh a woman of Samaria, to draw water. Jesus saith to her: Give me to drink.€€ƒx7For his disciples were gone into the city to buy meats.€€ƒx¦Then that Samaritan woman saith to him: How dost thou, being a Jew; ask of me to drink, who am a Samaritan woman? For the Jews do not communicate with the Samaritans.€€ƒxÉJesus answered and said to her: If thou didst know the gift of God and who he is that saith to thee: Give me to drink; thou perhaps wouldst have asked of him, and he would have given thee living water.€€ƒx~The woman saith to him: Sir, thou hast nothing wherein to draw, and the well is deep. From whence then hast thou living water?€€ƒxxArt thou greater than our father Jacob, who gave us the well and drank thereof, himself and his children and his cattle?€€ƒx©Jesus answered and said to her: Whosoever drinketh of this water shall thirst again: but he that shall drink of the water that I will give him shall not thirst for ever.€€ƒxoBut the water that I will give him shall become in him a fountain of water, springing up into life everlasting.€€ƒx_The woman said to him: Sir, give me this water, that I may not thirst, nor come hither to draw.€€ƒx:Jesus saith to her: Go, call thy husband, and come hither.€€ƒxjThe woman answered and said: I have no husband. Jesus said to her: Thou hast said well: I have no husband.€€ƒxjFor thou hast had five husbands: and he whom thou now hast is not thy husband. This, thou hast said truly.€€ƒx@The woman saith to him: Sir, I perceive that thou art a prophet.€€ƒxeOur fathers adored on this mountain: and you say that at Jerusalem is the place where men must adore.€€ƒxˆJesus saith to her: Woman, believe me that the hour cometh, when you shall neither on this mountain, nor in Jerusalem, adore the Father.€€ƒx]You adore that which you know not: we adore that which we know. For salvation is of the Jews.€€ƒx“But the hour cometh and now is, when the true adorers shall adore the Father in spirit and in truth. For the Father also seeketh such to adore him.€€ƒxOGod is a spirit: and they that adore him must adore him in spirit and in truth.€€ƒx†The woman saith to him: I know that the Messias cometh (who is called Christ): therefore, when he is come, he will tell us all things.€€ƒx7Jesus saith to her: I am he, who am speaking with thee.€€ƒx—And immediately his disciples came. And they wondered that he talked with the woman. Yet no man said: What seekest thou? Or: Why talkest thou with her?€€ƒx`The woman therefore left her waterpot and went her way into the city and saith to the men there:€€ƒx\Come, and see a man who has told me all things whatsoever I have done. Is not he the Christ?€€ƒx6They went therefore out of the city and came unto him.€€ƒx?In the mean time, the disciples prayed him, saying: Rabbi, eat.€€ƒx;But he said to them: I have meat to eat which you know not.€€ƒxMThe disciples therefore said one to another: Hath any man brought him to eat?€€ƒx`Jesus saith to them: My meat is to do the will of him that sent me, that I may perfect his work.€€ƒx®Do not you say: There are yet four months, and then the harvest cometh? Behold, I say to you, lift up your eyes, and see the countries. For they are white already to harvest.€€ƒx‘And he that reapeth receiveth wages and gathereth fruit unto life everlasting: that both he that soweth and he that reapeth may rejoice together.€€ƒx_For in this is the saying true: That it is one man that soweth, and it is another that reapeth.€€ƒxxI have sent you to reap that in which you did not labour. Others have laboured: and you have entered into their labours.€€ƒx’Now of that city many of the Samaritans believed in him, for the word of the woman giving testimony: He told me all things whatsoever I have done.€€ƒxmSo when the Samaritans were come to him, they desired that he would tarry there. And he abode there two days.€€ƒx7And many more believed in him, because of his own word.€€ƒx–And they said to the woman: We now believe, not for thy saying: for we ourselves have heard him and know that this is indeed the Saviour of the world.€€ƒx=Now after two days, he departed thence and went into Galilee.€€ƒxRFor Jesus himself gave testimony that a prophet hath no honour in his own country.€€ƒx¯And when he was come into Galilee, the Galileans received him, having seen all the things he had done at Jerusalem on the festival day: for they also went to the festival day.€€ƒxŒHe came again therefore into Cana of Galilee, where he made the water wine. And there was a certain ruler, whose son was sick at Capharnaum.€€ƒx˜He having heard that Jesus was come from Judea into Galilee, sent to him and prayed him to come down and heal his son: for he was at the point of death.€€ƒxOJesus therefore said to him: Unless you see signs and wonders, you believe not.€€ƒx?The ruler saith to him: Lord, come down before that my son die.€€ƒxsJesus saith to him: Go thy way. Thy son liveth. The man believed the word which Jesus said to him and went his way.€€ƒxbAnd as he was going down, his servants met him: and they brought word, saying, that his son lived.€€ƒx„He asked therefore of them the hour wherein he grew better. And they said to him: Yesterday at the seventh hour, the fever left him.€€ƒx‰The father therefore knew that it was at the same hour that Jesus said to him: Thy son liveth. And himself believed, and his whole house.€€ƒx]This is again the second miracle that Jesus did, when he was come out of Judea. into Galilee.€€ƒ`€€ƒxRAfter these things was a festival day of the Jews: and Jesus went up to Jerusalem.€€˜/ƒxlIn these lay a great multitude of sick, of blind, of lame, of withered: waiting for the moving of the water.€€ƒx×And an angel of the Lord descended at certain times into the pond and the water was moved. And he that went down first into the pond after the motion of the water was made whole of whatsoever infirmity he lay under.€€ƒx[And there was a certain man there that had been eight and thirty years under his infirmity.€€ƒxsHim when Jesus had seen lying, and knew that he had been now a long time, he saith to him: Wilt thou be made whole?€€ƒx›The infirm man answered him: Sir, I have no man, when the water is troubled, to put me into the pond. For whilst I am coming, another goeth down before me.€€ƒx4Jesus saith to him: Arise, take up thy bed and walk.€€ƒxkAnd immediately the man was made whole: and he took up his bed and walked. And it was the sabbath that day.€€ƒxpThe Jews therefore said to him that was healed: It is the sabbath. It is not lawful for thee to take up thy bed.€€ƒxQHe answered them: He that made me whole, he said to me: Take up thy bed and walk.€€ƒxUThey asked him therefore: Who is that man who said to thee: Take up thy bed and walk?€€ƒxiBut he who was healed knew not who it was: for Jesus went aside from the multitude standing in the place.€€ƒxŒAfterwards, Jesus findeth him in the temple and saith to him: Behold thou art made whole: sin no more, lest some worse thing happen to thee.€€ƒxPThe man went his way and told the Jews that it was Jesus who had made him whole.€€ƒxSTherefore did the Jews persecute Jesus, because he did these things on the sabbath.€€ƒxABut Jesus answered them: My Father worketh until now; and I work.€€ƒx¡Hereupon therefore the Jews sought the more to kill him, because he did not only break the sabbath but also said God was his Father, making himself equal to God.€€ƒxÕThen Jesus answered and said to them: Amen, amen, I say unto you, the Son cannot do any thing of himself, but what he seeth the Father doing: for what things soever he doth, these the Son also doth in like manner.€€ƒxFor the Father loveth the Son and sheweth him all things which himself doth: and greater works than these will he shew him, that you may wonder.€€ƒxcFor as the Father raiseth up the dead and giveth life: so the Son also giveth life to whom he will.€€ƒxRFor neither does the Father judge any man: but hath given all judgment to the Son.€€ƒx„That all men may honour the Son, as they honour the Father. He who honoureth not the Son honoureth not the Father who hath sent him.€€ƒx¬Amen, amen, I say unto you that he who heareth my word and believeth him that sent me hath life everlasting: and cometh not into judgment, but is passed from death to life.€€ƒx’Amen, amen, I say unto you, that the hour cometh, and now is, when the dead shall hear the voice of the Son of God: and they that hear shall live.€€ƒxaFor as the Father hath life in himself, so he hath given to the Son also to have life in himself.€€ƒxIAnd he hath given him power to do judgment, because he is the Son of man.€€ƒxrWonder not at this: for the hour cometh wherein all that are in the graves shall hear the voice of the Son of God.€€ƒx”And they that have done good things shall come forth unto the resurrection of life: but they that have done evil, unto the resurrection of judgment.€€ƒx’I cannot of myself do any thing. As I hear, so I judge. And my judgment is just: because I seek not my own will. but the will of him that sent me.€€ƒx4If I bear witness of myself, my witness is not true.€€ƒxkThere is another that beareth witness of me: and I know that the witness which he witnesseth of me is true.€€ƒx5You sent to John: and he gave testimony to the truth.€€ƒxTBut I receive not testimony from man: but I say these things, that you may be saved.€€ƒx^He was a burning and a shining light: and you were willing for a time to rejoice in his light.€€ƒx¿But I have a greater testimony than that of John: for the works which the Father hath given me to perfect, the works themselves which I do, give testimony of me, that the Father hath sent me.€€ƒx…And the Father himself who hath sent me hath given testimony of me: neither have you heard his voice at any time, nor seen his shape.€€ƒxUAnd you have not his word abiding in you: for whom he hath sent, him you believe not.€€ƒxwSearch the scriptures: for you think in them to have life everlasting. And the same are they that give testimony of me.€€ƒx3And you will not come to me that you may have life.€€ƒxI receive not glory from men.€€ƒx9But I know you, that you have not the love of God in you.€€ƒxxI am come in the name of my Father, and you receive me not: if another shall come in his own name, him you will receive.€€ƒxpHow can you believe, who receive glory one from another: and the glory which is from God alone, you do not seek?€€ƒxiThink not that I will accuse you to the Father. There is one that accuseth you, Moses, in whom you trust.€€ƒxTFor if you did believe Moses, you would perhaps believe me also: for he wrote of me.€€ƒxFBut if you do not believe his writings, how will you believe my words?€€ƒ`€€ƒxQAfter these things Jesus went over the sea of Galilee, which is that of Tiberias.€€˜GƒxMJesus therefore went up into a mountain: and there he sat with his disciples.€€ƒx>Now the pasch, the festival day of the Jews, was near at hand.€€ƒx¡When Jesus therefore had lifted up his eyes and seen that a very great multitude cometh to him, he said to Philip: Whence shall we buy bread, that these may eat?€€ƒxBAnd this he said to try him: for he himself knew what he would do.€€ƒxqPhilip answered him: Two hundred pennyworth of bread is not sufficient for them that every one may take a little.€€ƒxGOne of his disciples, Andrew, the brother of Simon Peter, saith to him:€€ƒxbThere is a boy here that hath five barley loaves and two fishes. But what are these among so many?€€ƒxŠThen Jesus said: Make the men sit down. Now, there was much grass in the place. The men therefore sat down, in number about five thousand.€€ƒxAnd Jesus took the loaves: and when he had given thanks, he distributed to them that were set down. In like manner also of the fishes, as much as they would.€€ƒxlAnd when they were filled, he said to his disciples: gather up the fragments that remain, lest they be lost.€€ƒx—They gathered up therefore and filled twelve baskets with the fragments of the five barley loaves which remained over and above to them that had eaten.€€ƒx…Now those men, when they had seen what a miracle Jesus had done, said: This is of a truth the prophet that is to come into the world.€€ƒxˆJesus therefore, when he knew that they would come to take him by force and make him king, fled again into the mountains, himself alone.€€ƒx>And when evening was come, his disciples went down to the sea.€€ƒxƒAnd when they had gone up into a ship, they went over the sea to Capharnaum. And it was now dark: and Jesus was not come unto them.€€ƒx7And the sea arose, by reason of a great wind that blew.€€ƒxŸWhen they had rowed therefore about five and twenty or thirty furlongs, they see Jesus walking upon the sea and drawing nigh to the ship. And they were afraid.€€ƒx-But he saith to them: It is I. Be not afraid.€€ƒxwThey were willing therefore to take him into the ship. And presently the ship was at the land to which they were going.€€ƒxáThe next day, the multitude that stood on the other side of the sea saw that there was no other ship there but one: and that Jesus had not entered into the ship with his disciples, but that his disciples were gone away alone.€€ƒxrBut other ships came in from Tiberias, nigh unto the place where they had eaten the bread, the Lord giving thanks.€€ƒx‹When therefore the multitude saw that Jesus was not there, nor his disciples, they took shipping and came to Capharnaum, seeking for Jesus.€€ƒxlAnd when they had found him on that other side of the sea, they said to him: Rabbi, when camest thou hither?€€ƒxŸJesus answered them and said: Amen, amen, I say to you, you seek me, not because you have seen miracles, but because you did eat of the loaves and were filled.€€ƒx¥Labour not for the meat which perisheth, but for that which endureth unto life everlasting, which the Son of man will give you. For him hath God, the Father, sealed.€€ƒxRThey said therefore unto him: What shall we do, that we may work the works of God?€€ƒxdJesus answered and said to them: This is the work of God, that you believe in him whom he hath sent.€€ƒxyThey said therefore to him: What sign therefore dost thou shew that we may see and may believe thee? What dost thou work?€€ƒxaOur fathers did eat manna in the desert, as it is written: He gave them bread from heaven to eat.€€ƒxThen Jesus said to them: Amen, amen, I say to you; Moses gave you not bread from heaven, but my Father giveth you the true bread from heaven.€€ƒxXFor the bread of God is that which cometh down from heaven and giveth life to the world.€€ƒx>They said therefore unto him: Lord, give us always this bread.€€ƒx†And Jesus said to them: I am the bread of life. He that cometh to me shall not hunger: and he that believeth in me shall never thirst.€€ƒxDBut I said unto you that you also have seen me, and you believe not.€€ƒxbAll that the Father giveth to me shall come to me: and him that cometh to me, I will not cast out.€€ƒxXBecause I came down from heaven, not to do my own will but the will of him that sent me.€€ƒx—Now this is the will of the Father who sent me: that of all that he hath given me, I should lose nothing; but should raise it up again in the last day.€€ƒx©And this is the will of my Father that sent me: that every one who seeth the Son and believeth in him may have life everlasting. And I will raise him up in the last day.€€ƒxkThe Jews therefore murmured at him, because he had said: I am the living bread which came down from heaven.€€ƒxAnd they said: Is not this Jesus, the son of Joseph, whose father and mother we know? How then saith he: I came down from heaven?€€ƒxGJesus therefore answered and said to them: Murmur not among yourselves.€€ƒxnNo man can come to me, except the Father, who hath sent me, draw him. And I will raise him up in the last day.€€ƒxIt is written in the prophets: And they shall all be taught of God. Every one that hath heard of the Father and hath learned cometh forth me.€€ƒxUNot that any man hath seen the Father: but he who is of God, he hath seen the Father.€€ƒxJAmen, amen, I say unto you: He that believeth in me hath everlasting life.€€ƒwI am the bread of life.€€ƒx7Your fathers did eat manna in the desert: and are dead.€€ƒx[This is the bread which cometh down from heaven: that if any man eat of it, he may not die.€€ƒx2I am the living bread which came down from heaven.€€ƒx|If any man eat of this bread, he shall live for ever: and the bread that I will give is my flesh, for the life of the world.€€ƒx^The Jews therefore strove among themselves, saying: How can this man give us his flesh to eat?€€ƒx”Then Jesus said to them: Amen, amen, I say unto you: except you eat the flesh of the Son of man and drink his blood, you shall not have life in you.€€ƒxmHe that eateth my flesh and drinketh my blood hath everlasting life: and I will raise him up in the last day.€€ƒx:For my flesh is meat indeed: and my blood is drink indeed.€€ƒxJHe that eateth my flesh and drinketh my blood abideth in me: and I in him.€€ƒxqAs the living Father hath sent me and I live by the Father: so he that eateth me, the same also shall live by me.€€ƒxŒThis is the bread that came down from heaven. Not as your fathers did eat manna and are dead. He that eateth this bread shall live for ever.€€ƒx?These things he said, teaching in the synagogue, in Capharnaum.€€ƒx\Many therefore of his disciples, hearing it, said: This saying is hard; and who can hear it?€€ƒxjBut Jesus, knowing in himself that his disciples murmured at this, said to them: Doth this scandalize you?€€ƒxCIf then you shall see the Son of man ascend up where he was before?€€ƒxwIt is the spirit that quickeneth: the flesh profiteth nothing. The words that I have spoken to you are spirit and life.€€ƒx–But there are some of you that believe not. For Jesus knew from the beginning who they were that did not believe and who he was that would betray him.€€ƒxhAnd he said: Therefore did I say to you that no man can come to me, unless it be given him by my Father.€€ƒxHAfter this, many of his disciples went back and walked no more with him.€€ƒx5Then Jesus said to the twelve: Will you also go away?€€ƒx]And Simon Peter answered him: Lord, to whom shall we go? Thou hast the words of eternal life.€€ƒxMAnd we have believed and have known that thou art the Christ, the Son of God.€€ƒxMJesus answered them: Have not I chosen you twelve? And one of you is a devil.€€ƒxwNow he meant Judas Iscariot, the son of Simon: for this same was about to betray him, whereas he was one of the twelve.€€ƒ`€€˜5ƒx.Now the Jews feast of tabernacles was at hand.€€ƒx|And his brethren said to, him: Pass from hence and go into Judea, that thy disciples also may see thy works which thou dost.€€ƒx•For there is no man that doth any thing in secret, and he himself seeketh to be known openly. If thou do these things, manifest thyself to the world.€€ƒx,For neither did his brethren believe in him.€€ƒxPThen Jesus said to them: My time is not yet come; but your time is always ready.€€ƒxmThe world cannot hate you: but me it hateth, because I give testimony of it, that the works thereof are evil,€€ƒxjGo you up to this festival day: but I go not up to this festival day, because my time is not accomplished.€€ƒx<When he had said these things, he himself stayed in Galilee.€€ƒxoBut after his brethren were gone up, then he also went up to the feast, not openly, but, as it were, in secret.€€ƒxHThe Jews therefore sought him on the festival day and said: Where is he?€€ƒx’And there was much murmuring among the multitude concerning him. For some said: He is a good man. And others said: No, but he seduceth the people.€€ƒx5Yet no man spoke openly of him, for fear of the Jews.€€ƒxLNow, about the midst of the feast, Jesus went up into the temple and taught.€€ƒxTAnd the Jews wondered, saying: How doth this man know letters, having never learned?€€ƒxLJesus answered them and said: My doctrine is not mine, but his that sent me.€€ƒxvIf any man will do the will of him, he shall know of the doctrine, whether it be of God, or whether I speak of myself.€€ƒx“He that speaketh of himself seeketh his own glory: but he that seeketh the glory of him that sent him, he is true and there is no injustice in him.€€ƒxDDid not Moses give you the law, and yet none of you keepeth the law?€€ƒxfWhy seek you to kill me? The multitude answered and said: Thou hast a devil. Who seeketh to kill thee?€€ƒxJJesus answered and said to them: One work I have done: and you all wonder.€€ƒx…Therefore, Moses gave you circumcision (not because it is of Moses, but of the fathers): and on the sabbath day you circumcise a man.€€ƒx§If a man receive circumcision on the sabbath day, that the law of Moses may not be broken: are you angry at me, because I have healed the whole man on the sabbath day?€€ƒx?Judge not according to the appearance: but judge just judgment.€€ƒxHSome therefore of Jerusalem said: Is not this he whom they seek to kill?€€ƒxwAnd behold, he speaketh openly: and they say nothing to him. Have the rulers known for a truth that this is the Christ?€€ƒx]But we know this man, whence he is: but when the Christ cometh, no man knoweth, whence he is.€€ƒx¶Jesus therefore cried out in the temple, teaching and saying: You both know me, and you know whence I am. And I am not come of myself: but he that sent me is true, whom you know not.€€ƒx7I know him, because I am from him: and he hath sent me.€€ƒxhThey sought therefore to apprehend him: and no man laid hands on him, because his hour was not yet come.€€ƒxvBut of the people many believed in him and said: When the Christ cometh, shall he do more miracles than this man doth?€€ƒxƒThe Pharisees heard the people murmuring these things concerning him: and the rulers and Pharisees sent ministers to apprehend him.€€ƒxbJesus therefore said to them: Yet a little while I am with you: and then I go to him that sent me.€€ƒxQYou shall seek me and shall not find me: and where I am, thither you cannot come.€€ƒx¢The Jews therefore said among themselves: Whither will he go, that we shall not find him? Will he go unto the dispersed among the Gentiles and teach the Gentiles?€€ƒxqWhat is this saying that he hath said: You shall seek me and shall not find me? And: Where I am, you cannot come?€€ƒx€And on the last, and great day of the festivity, Jesus stood and cried, saying: If any man thirst, let him come to me and drink.€€ƒxdHe that believeth in me, as the scripture saith: Out of his belly shall flow rivers of living water.€€ƒx—Now this he said of the Spirit which they should receive who believed in him: for as yet the Spirit was not given, because Jesus was not yet glorified.€€ƒxkOf that multitude therefore, when they had heard these words of his, some said: This is the prophet indeed.€€ƒxTOthers said: This is the Christ. But some said: Doth the Christ come out of Galilee?€€ƒxpDoth not the scripture say: That Christ cometh of the seed of David and from Bethlehem the town where David was?€€ƒx<So there arose a dissension among the people because of him.€€ƒxLAnd some of them would have apprehended him: but no man laid hands upon him.€€ƒxyThe ministers therefore came to the chief priests and the Pharisees. And they said to them: Why have you not brought him?€€ƒx:The ministers answered: Never did man speak like this man.€€ƒx<The Pharisees therefore answered them: Are you also seduced?€€ƒx@Hath any one of the rulers believed in him, or of the Pharisees?€€ƒx;But this multitude, that knoweth not the law, are accursed.€€ƒxKNicodemus said to them (he that came to him by night, who was one of them):€€ƒxKDoth our law judge any man, unless it first hear him and know what he doth?€€ƒxThey answered and said to him: Art thou also a Galilean? Search the scriptures, and see that out of Galilee a prophet riseth not.€€ƒx(And every man returned to his own house.€€ƒ`€€˜;ƒxxAnd early in the morning he came again into the temple: and all the people came to him. And sitting down he taught them.€€ƒxfAnd the scribes and Pharisees bring unto him a woman taken in adultery: and they set her in the midst,€€ƒxCAnd said to him: Master, this woman was even now taken in adultery.€€ƒxLNow Moses in the law commanded us to stone such a one. But what sayest thou?€€ƒx€And this they said tempting him, that they might accuse him. But Jesus bowing himself down, wrote with his finger on the ground.€€ƒx•When therefore they continued asking him, he lifted up himself and said to them: He that is without sin among you, let him first cast a stone at her.€€ƒx0And again stooping down, he wrote on the ground.€€ƒxƒBut they hearing this, went out one by one, beginning at the eldest. And Jesus alone remained, and the woman standing in the midst.€€ƒxpThen Jesus lifting up himself, said to her: Woman, where are they that accused thee? Hath no man condemned thee?€€ƒx]Who said: No man, Lord. And Jesus said: Neither will I condemn thee. Go, and now sin no more.€€ƒx›Again therefore, Jesus spoke to: them, saying: I am the light of the world. He that followeth me walketh not in darkness, but shall have the light of life.€€ƒxaThe Pharisees therefore said to him: Thou givest testimony of thyself. Thy testimony is not true.€€ƒx†Jesus answered and said to them: Although I give testimony of myself, my testimony is true: for I know whence I came and whither I go.€€ƒx6You judge according to the flesh: I judge not any man.€€ƒxbAnd if I do judge, my judgment is true: because I am not alone, but I and the Father that sent me.€€ƒxDAnd in your law it is written that the testimony of two men is true.€€ƒx[I am one that give testimony of myself: and the Father that sent me giveth testimony of me.€€ƒx¢They said therefore to him: Where is thy Father? Jesus answered: Neither me do you know, nor my Father. If you did know me, perhaps you would know my Father also.€€ƒxThese words Jesus spoke in the treasury, teaching in the temple: and no man laid hands on him, because his hour was not yet come.€€ƒx~Again therefore Jesus said to them: I go: and you shall seek me. And you shall die in your sin. Whither I go, you cannot come.€€ƒx]The Jews therefore said: Will he kill himself, because he said: Whither I go you cannot come?€€ƒxjAnd he said to them: You are from beneath: I am from above. You are of this world: I am not of this world.€€ƒxxTherefore I said to you that you shall die in your sins. For if you believe not that I am he, you shall die in your sin.€€ƒxeThey said therefore to him: Who art thou? Jesus said to them: The beginning, who also speak unto you.€€ƒx“Many things I have to speak and to judge of you. But he that sent me, is true: and the things I have heard of him, these same I speak in the world.€€ƒx6And they understood not that he called God his Father.€€ƒxÆJesus therefore said to them: When you shall have lifted up, the Son of man, then shall you know that I am he and that I do nothing of myself. But as the Father hath taught me, these things I speak.€€ƒxjAnd he that sent me is with me: and he hath not left me alone. For I do always the things that please him.€€ƒx1When he spoke these things, many believed in him.€€ƒxmThen Jesus said to those Jews who believed him: If you continue in my word, you shall be my disciples indeed.€€ƒx@And you shall know the truth: and the truth shall make you free.€€ƒx|They answered him: We are the seed of Abraham: and we have never been slaves to any man. How sayest thou: You shall be free?€€ƒxdJesus answered them: Amen, amen, I say unto you that whosoever committeth sin is the servant of sin.€€ƒxPNow the servant abideth not in the house for ever: but the son abideth for ever.€€ƒxCIf therefore the son shall make you free, you shall be free indeed.€€ƒxkI know that you are the children of Abraham: but you seek to kill me, because my word hath no place in you.€€ƒxiI speak that which I have seen with my Father: and you do the things that you have seen with your father.€€ƒxƒThey answered and said to him: Abraham is our father. Jesus saith them: If you be the children of Abraham, do the works of Abraham.€€ƒxuBut now you seek to kill me, a man who have spoken the truth to you, which I have heard of God. This Abraham did not.€€ƒxzYou do the works of your father. They said therefore to him: We are not born of fornication: we have one Father, even God.€€ƒxJesus therefore said to them: If God were your Father, you would indeed love me. For from God I proceeded and came. For I came not of myself: but he sent me.€€ƒx?Why do you not know my speech? Because you cannot hear my word.€€ƒy	You are of your father the devil: and the desires of your father you will do. He was a murderer from the beginning: and he stood not in the truth, because truth is not in him. When he speaketh a lie, he speaketh of his own: for he is a liar, and the father thereof.€€ƒx+But if I say the truth, you believe me not.€€ƒx\Which of you shall convince me of sin? If I say the truth to you, why do you not believe me:€€ƒxdHe that is of God heareth the words of God. Therefore you hear them not, because you are not of God.€€ƒxkThe Jews therefore answered and said to him: Do not we say well that thou art a Samaritan and hast a devil?€€ƒxXJesus answered: I have not a devil: but I honour my Father. And you have dishonoured me.€€ƒxCBut I seek not my own glory: there is one that seeketh and judgeth.€€ƒxSAmen, amen, I say to you: If any man keep my word, he shall not see death for ever.€€ƒx¬The Jews therefore said: Now we know that thou hast a devil. Abraham is dead, and the prophets: and thou sayest: If any man keep my word, he shall not taste death for ever.€€ƒxmArt thou greater than our father Abraham who is dead? And the prophets are dead. Whom dost thou make thyself?€€ƒx‚Jesus answered: If I glorify myself, my glory is nothing. It is my Father that glorifieth me, of whom you say that he is your God.€€ƒx›And you have not known him: but I know him. And if I shall say that I know him not, I shall be like to you, a liar. But I do know him and do keep his word.€€ƒxNAbraham your father rejoiced that he might see my day: he saw it and was glad.€€ƒx]The Jews therefore said to him: Thou art not yet fifty years old. And hast thou seen Abraham?€€ƒxLJesus said to them: Amen, amen, I say to you, before Abraham was made, I AM.€€ƒx_They took up stones therefore to cast at him. But Jesus hid himself and went out of the temple.€€ƒ`€€˜)ƒxkAnd his disciples asked him: Rabbi, who hath sinned, this man or his parents, that he should be born blind?€€ƒxxJesus answered: Neither hath this man sinned, nor his parents; but that the works of God should be made manifest in him.€€ƒxdI must work the works of him that sent me, whilst it is day: the night cometh, when no man can work.€€ƒx:As long as I am in the world, I am the light of the world.€€ƒxtWhen he had said these things, he spat on the ground and made clay of the spittle and spread the clay upon his eyes,€€ƒx}And said to him: Go, wash in the pool of Siloe, which is interpreted, Sent. He went therefore and washed: and he came seeing.€€ƒx’The neighbours, therefore, and they who had seen him before that he was a beggar, said: Is not this he that sat and begged? Some said: This is he.€€ƒx>But others said: No, but he is like him. But he said: I am he.€€ƒx5They said therefore to him: How were thy eyes opened?€€ƒxœHe answered: That man that is called Jesus made clay and anointed my eyes and said to me: Go to the pool of Siloe and wash. And I went: I washed: and I see.€€ƒx8And they said to him: Where is he? He saith: I know not.€€ƒx4They bring him that had been blind to the Pharisees.€€ƒxENow it was the sabbath, when Jesus made the clay and opened his eyes.€€ƒxAgain therefore the Pharisees asked him how he had received his sight. But he said to them: He put clay upon my eyes: and I washed: and I see.€€ƒxÁSome therefore of the Pharisees said: This man is not of God, who keepeth not the sabbath. But others said: How can a man that is a sinner do such miracles? And there was a division among them.€€ƒx{They say therefore to the blind man again: What sayest thou of him that hath opened thy eyes? And he said: He is a prophet.€€ƒx¢The Jews then did not believe concerning him, that he had been blind and had received his sight, until they called the parents of him that had received his sight,€€ƒx_And asked them, saying: Is this your son, who you say was born blind? How then doth he now see?€€ƒx\His parents answered them and said: We know that this is our son and that he was born blind:€€ƒx‚But how he now seeth, we know not: or who hath opened his eyes, we know not. Ask himself: he is of age: Let him speak for himself.€€ƒxÅThese things his parents said, because they feared the Jews: for the Jews had already agreed among themselves that if any man should confess him to be Christ, he should be put out of the synagogue.€€ƒx9Therefore did his parents say: He is of age. Ask himself.€€ƒx~They therefore called the man again that had been blind and said to him: Give glory to God. We know that this man is a sinner.€€ƒxpHe said therefore to them: If he be a sinner, I know not. One thing I know, that whereas I was blind. now I see.€€ƒxEThey said then to him: What did he to thee? How did he open thy eyes?€€ƒxHe answered them: I have told you already, and you have heard. Why would you hear it again? Will you also become his disciples?€€ƒx]They reviled him therefore and said: Be thou his disciple; but we are the disciples of Moses.€€ƒxSWe know that God spoke to Moses: but as to this man, we know not from whence he is.€€ƒx…The man answered and said to them: why, herein is a wonderful thing, that you know not from whence he is, and he hath opened my eyes.€€ƒxoNow we know that God doth not hear sinners: but if a man be a server of God and doth his, will, him he heareth.€€ƒxlFrom the beginning of the world it hath not been heard, that any man hath opened the eyes of one born blind.€€ƒx6Unless this man were of God, he could not do anything.€€ƒxlThey answered and said to him: Thou wast wholly born in sins; and dost thou teach us? And they cast him out.€€ƒxwJesus heard that they had cast him out. And when he had found him, he said to him: Dost thou believe in the Son of God?€€ƒxBHe answered, and said: Who is he, Lord, that I may believe in him?€€ƒxTAnd Jesus said to him: Thou hast both seen him; and it is he that talketh with thee.€€ƒx>And he said: I believe, Lord. And falling down, he adored him.€€ƒxyAnd Jesus said: For judgment I am come into this world: that they who see not may see; and they who see may become blind.€€ƒx_And some of the Pharisees, who were with him, heard: and they said unto him: Are we also blind?€€ƒxlJesus said to them: If you were blind, you should not have sin: but now you say: We see. Your sin remaineth.€€ƒ`€€˜*ƒxABut he that entereth in by the door is the shepherd of the sheep.€€ƒxsTo him the porter openeth: and the sheep hear his voice. And he calleth his own sheep by name and leadeth them out.€€ƒxtAnd when he hath let out his own sheep, he goeth before them: and the sheep follow him, because they know his voice.€€ƒx_But a stranger they follow not, but fly from him, because they know not the voice of strangers.€€ƒxHThis proverb Jesus spoke to them. But they understood not what he spoke.€€ƒxYJesus therefore said to them again: Amen, amen, I say to you, I am the door of the sheep.€€ƒxXAll others, as many as have come, are thieves and robbers: and the sheep heard them not.€€ƒxuI am the door. By me, if any man enter in, he shall be saved: and he shall go in and go out, and shall find pastures.€€ƒx…The thief cometh not, but for to steal and to kill and to destroy. I am come that they may have life and may have it more abundantly.€€ƒxHI am the good shepherd. The good shepherd giveth his life for his sheep.€€ƒxµBut the hireling and he that is not the shepherd, whose own the sheep are not, seeth the wolf coming and leaveth the sheep and flieth: and the wolf casteth and scattereth the sheep,€€ƒxUAnd the hireling flieth, because he is a hireling: and he hath no care for the sheep.€€ƒx:I am the good shepherd: and I know mine, and mine know me.€€ƒxUAs the Father knoweth me, and I know the Father: and I lay down my life for my sheep.€€ƒx•And other sheep I have that are not of this fold: them also I must bring. And they shall hear my voice: And there shall be one fold and one shepherd.€€ƒxXTherefore doth the Father love me: because I lay down my life, that I may take it again.€€ƒx±No man taketh it away from me: but I lay it down of myself. And I have power to lay it down: and I have power to take it up again. This commandment have I received of my Father.€€ƒx7A dissension rose again among the Jews for these words.€€ƒxDAnd many of them said: He hath a devil and is mad. Why hear you him?€€ƒxfOthers said: These are not the words of one that hath a devil. Can a devil open the eyes of the blind?€€ƒxGAnd it was the feast of the dedication at Jerusalem: and it was winter.€€ƒx3And Jesus walked in the temple, in Solomon's porch.€€ƒxThe Jews therefore came round about him and said to him: How long dost thou hold our souls in suspense? If thou be the Christ, tell us plainly.€€ƒx‚Jesus answered them: I speak to you, and you believe not: the works that I do in the name of my Father, they give testimony of me.€€ƒx8But you do not believe, because you are not of my sheep.€€ƒx<My sheep hear my voice. And I know them: and they follow me.€€ƒxqAnd I give them life everlasting: and they shall not perish for ever. And no man shall pluck them out of my hand.€€ƒxpThat which my Father hath given me is greater than all: and no one can snatch them out of the hand of my Father.€€ƒxI and the Father are one.€€ƒx*The Jews then took up stones to stone him.€€ƒxpJesus answered them: Many good works I have shewed you from my Father. For which of those works do you stone me?€€ƒx…The Jews answered him: For a good work we stone thee not, but for blasphemy: and because that thou. being a, man, makest thyself God.€€ƒxIJesus answered them: Is it not written in your law: I said, you are gods?€€ƒx^If he called them gods to whom the word of God was spoken; and the scripture cannot be broken:€€ƒxDo you say of him whom the Father hath sanctified and sent into the world: Thou blasphemest; because I said: I am the Son of God?€€ƒx3If I do not the works of my Father, believe me not.€€ƒx‹But if I do, though you will not believe me, believe the works: that you may know and believe that the Father is in me and I in the Father.€€ƒxEThey sought therefore to take him: and he escaped out of their hands.€€ƒxhAnd he went again beyond the Jordan, into that place where John was baptizing first. And there he abode.€€ƒxAAnd many resorted to him: and they said: John indeed did no sign.€€ƒxSBut all things whatsoever John said of this man were true. And many believed n him.€€ƒ`€€˜9ƒxy(And Mary was she that anointed the Lord with ointment and wiped his feet with her hair: whose brother Lazarus was sick.)€€ƒxUHis sisters therefore sent to him, saying: Lord, behold, he whom thou lovest is sick.€€ƒxŠAnd Jesus hearing it, said to them: This sickness is not unto death, but for the glory of God: that the Son of God may be glorified by it.€€ƒx7Now Jesus loved Martha and her sister Mary and Lazarus.€€ƒx[When he had heard therefore that he was sick, he still remained in the same place two days.€€ƒxFThen after that, he said to his disciples: Let us go into Judea again.€€ƒxeThe disciples say to him: Rabbi, the Jews but now sought to stone thee. And goest thou thither again?€€ƒx‹Jesus answered: Are there not twelve hours of the day? If a man walk in the day he stumbleth not, because he seeth the light of this world:€€ƒxKBut if he walk in the night, he stumbleth, because the light is not in him.€€ƒx~These things he said; and after that he said to them: Lazarus our friend sleepeth: but I go that I may awake him out of sleep.€€ƒxBHis disciples therefore said: Lord, if he sleep, he shall do well.€€ƒxTBut Jesus spoke of his death: and they thought that he spoke of the repose of sleep.€€ƒx;Then therefore Jesus said to them plainly: Lazarus is dead.€€ƒxaAnd I am glad, for your sakes; that I was not there, that you may believe. But, let us go to him.€€ƒxpThomas therefore, who is called Didymus, said to his fellow disciples: Let us also go, that we may die with him.€€ƒxPJesus therefore came: and found that he had been four days already in the grave.€€ƒx>(Now Bethania was near Jerusalem, about fifteen furlongs off.)€€ƒx\And many of the Jews were come to Martha and Mary, to comfort them concerning their brother.€€ƒxcMartha therefore, as soon as she heard that Jesus was come, went to meet him: but Mary sat at home.€€ƒxWMartha therefore said to Jesus: Lord, if thou hadst been here, my brother had not died.€€ƒxPBut now also I know that whatsoever thou wilt ask of God, God will give it thee.€€ƒx1Jesus saith to her: Thy brother shall rise again.€€ƒxZMartha saith to him: I know that he shall rise again, in the resurrection at the last day.€€ƒxpJesus said to her: I am the resurrection and the life: he that believeth in me, although he be dead, shall live:€€ƒxZAnd every one that liveth and believeth in me shall not die for ever. Believest thou this?€€ƒx{She saith to him: Yea, Lord, I have believed that thou art Christ, the Son of the living God, who art come into this world.€€ƒx‚And when she had said these things, she went and called her sister Mary secretly, saying: The master is come and calleth for thee.€€ƒxAShe, as soon as she heard this, riseth quickly and cometh to him.€€ƒxbFor Jesus was not yet come into the town: but he was still in that place where Martha had met him.€€ƒx½The Jews therefore, who were with her in the house and comforted her, when they saw Mary, that she rose up speedily and went out, followed her, saying: She goeth to the grave to weep there.€€ƒxWhen Mary therefore was come where Jesus was, seeing him, she fell down at his feet and saith to him. Lord, if thou hadst been here, my brother had not died.€€ƒx„Jesus, therefore, when he saw her weeping, and the Jews that were come with her weeping, groaned in the spirit and troubled himself,€€ƒxGAnd said: Where have you laid him? They say to him: Lord, come and see.€€ƒoAnd Jesus wept.€€ƒx1The Jews therefore said: Behold how he loved him.€€ƒxxBut some of them said: Could not he that opened the eyes of the man born blind have caused that this man should not die?€€ƒxtJesus therefore again groaning in himself, cometh to the sepulchre. Now it was a cave; and a stone was laid over it.€€ƒx”Jesus saith: Take away the stone. Martha, the sister of him that was dead, saith to him: Lord, by this time he stinketh, for he is now of four days.€€ƒx`Jesus saith to her: Did not I say to thee that if thou believe, thou shalt see the glory of God?€€ƒx|They took therefore the stone away. And Jesus lifting up his eyes, said: Father, I give thee thanks that thou hast heard me.€€ƒxAnd I knew that thou hearest me always: but because of the people who stand about have I said it, that they may believe that thou hast sent me.€€ƒxOWhen he had said these things, he cried with a loud voice: Lazarus, come forth.€€ƒx²And presently he that had been dead came forth, bound feet and hands with winding bands. And his face was bound about with a napkin. Jesus said to them: Loose him and let him go.€€ƒxuMany therefore of the Jews, who were come to Mary and Martha and had seen the things that Jesus did, believed in him.€€ƒxTBut some of them went to the Pharisees and told them the things that Jesus had done.€€ƒxyThe chief priests, therefore, and the Pharisees gathered a council and said: What do we, for this man doth many miracles?€€ƒxnIf we let him alone so, all will believe in him; and the Romans will come, and take away our place and nation.€€ƒx`But one of them, named Caiphas, being the high priest that year, said to them: You know nothing.€€ƒxNeither do you consider that it is expedient for you that one man should die for the people and that the whole nation perish not.€€ƒx}And this he spoke not of himself: but being the high priest of that year, he prophesied that Jesus should die for the nation.€€ƒxcAnd not only for the nation, but to gather together in one the children of God that were dispersed.€€ƒx9From that day therefore they devised to put him to death.€€ƒx«Wherefore Jesus walked no more openly among the Jews: but he went into a country near the desert, unto a city that is called Ephrem. And there he abode with his disciples.€€ƒx~And the pasch of the Jews was at hand: and many from the country went up to Jerusalem, before the pasch, to purify themselves.€€ƒy%They sought therefore for Jesus; and they discoursed one with another, standing in the temple: What think you that he is not come to the festival day? And the chief priests and Pharisees had given a commandment that, if any man knew where he was, he should tell, that they might apprehend him.€€ƒ`€€ƒ`€€˜2ƒxmAnd they made him a supper there: and Martha served. But Lazarus was one of them that were at table with him.€€ƒxÅMary therefore took a pound of ointment of right spikenard, of great price, and anointed the feet of Jesus and wiped his feet with her hair. And the house was filled with the odour of the ointment.€€ƒxQThen one of his disciples, Judas Iscariot, he that was about to betray him, said:€€ƒxMWhy was not this ointment sold for three hundred pence and given to the poor?€€ƒxNow he said this not because he cared for the poor; but because he was a thief and, having the purse, carried the things that were put therein.€€ƒxWJesus therefore said: Let her alone, that she may keep it against the day of my burial.€€ƒxBFor the poor you have always with you: but me you have not always.€€ƒx«A great multitude therefore of the Jews knew that he was there; and they came, not for Jesus' sake only, but that they might see Lazarus, whom he had raised from the dead.€€ƒx3But the chief priests thought to kill Lazarus also:€€ƒxLBecause many of the Jews, by reason of him, went away and believed in Jesus.€€ƒxAnd on the next day, a great multitude that was come to the festival day, when they had heard that Jesus was coming to Jerusalem,€€ƒxTook branches of palm trees and went forth to meet him and cried Hosanna. Blessed is he that cometh in the name of the Lord, the king of Israel.€€ƒx>And Jesus found a young ass and sat upon it, as it is written:€€ƒxMFear not, daughter of Sion: behold thy king cometh, sitting on an ass's colt.€€ƒxºThese things his disciples did not know at the first: but when Jesus was glorified, then they remembered that these things were written of him and that they had done these things to him.€€ƒxThe multitude therefore gave testimony, which was with him, when he called Lazarus out of the grave and raised him from the dead.€€ƒxdFor which reason also the people came to meet him, because they heard that he had done this miracle.€€ƒx}The Pharisees therefore said among themselves: Do you see that we prevail nothing? Behold, the whole world is gone after him.€€ƒxUNow there were certain Gentiles among them, who came up to adore on the festival day.€€ƒxrThese therefore came to Philip, who was of Bethsaida of Galilee, and desired him, saying: Sir, we would see Jesus.€€ƒxEPhilip cometh and telleth Andrew. Again Andrew and Philip told Jesus.€€ƒxZBut Jesus answered them, saying: The hour is come that the Son of man should be glorified.€€ƒxPAmen, amen, I say to you, unless the grain of wheat falling into the ground die,€€ƒx±Itself remaineth alone. But if it die it bringeth forth much fruit. He that loveth his life shall lose it and he that hateth his life in this world keepeth it unto life eternal.€€ƒx”If any man minister to me, let him follow me: and where I am, there also shall my minister be. If any man minister to me, him will my Father honour.€€ƒxxNow is my soul troubled. And what shall I say? Father, save me from this hour. But for this cause I came unto this hour.€€ƒxqFather, glorify thy name. A voice therefore came from heaven: I have both glorified it and will glorify it again.€€ƒxhThe multitude therefore that stood and heard said that it thundered. Others said: An angel spoke to him.€€ƒxOJesus answered and said: This voice came not because of me, but for your sakes.€€ƒxQNow is the judgment of the world: now shall the prince of this world be cast out.€€ƒxHAnd I, if I be lifted up from the earth, will draw all things to myself.€€ƒx8(Now this he said, signifying what death he should die.)€€ƒx¥The multitude answered him: We have heard out of the law that Christ abideth for ever. And how sayest thou: The Son of man must be lifted up? Who is this Son of man?€€ƒxÊJesus therefore said to them: Yet a little while, the light is among you. Walk whilst you have the light, and the darkness overtake you not. And he that walketh in darkness knoweth not whither be goeth.€€ƒxWhilst you have the light, believe in the light, that you may be the children of light. These things Jesus spoke: and he went away and hid himself from them.€€ƒxOAnd whereas he had done so many miracles before them, they believed not in him:€€ƒx¡That the saying of Isaias the prophet might be fulfilled, which he said: Lord, who hath believed our hearing? And to whom hath the arm of the Lord been revealed?€€ƒx<Therefore they could not believe, because Isaias said again:€€ƒx¨He hath blinded their eyes and hardened their heart, that they should not see with their eyes, nor understand with their heart and be converted: and I should heal them.€€ƒxBThese things said Isaias, when he saw his glory, and spoke of him.€€ƒxHowever, many of the chief men also believed in him: but because of the Pharisees they did not confess him, that they might not be cast out of the synagogue.€€ƒx;For they loved the glory of men more than the glory of God.€€ƒxbBut Jesus cried and said: He that believeth in me doth not believe in me, but in him that sent me.€€ƒx-And he that seeth me, seeth him that sent me.€€ƒx]I am come, a light into the world, that whosoever believeth in me may not remain in darkness.€€ƒx|And if any man hear my words and keep them not, I do not judge him for I came not to judge the world, but to save the world.€€ƒx‘He that despiseth me and receiveth not my words hath one that judgeth him. The word that I have spoken, the same shall judge him in the last day.€€ƒx~For I have not spoken of myself: but the Father who sent me, he gave me commandment what I should say and what I should speak.€€ƒx‡And I know that his commandment is life everlasting. The things therefore that I speak, even as the Father said unto me, so do I speak.€€ƒ`€€˜&ƒxvAnd when supper was done (the devil having now put into the heart of Judas Iscariot, the son of Simon, to betray him),€€ƒxkKnowing that the Father had given him all things into his hands and that he came from God and goeth to God,€€ƒx^He riseth from supper and layeth aside his garments and, having taken a towel, girded himself.€€ƒxAfter that, he putteth water into a basin and began to wash the feet of the disciples and to wipe them with the towel wherewith he was girded.€€ƒxYHe cometh therefore to Simon Peter. And Peter saith to him: Lord, dost thou wash my feet?€€ƒx_Jesus answered and said to him: What I do, thou knowest not now; but thou shalt know hereafter.€€ƒx{Peter saith to him: Thou shalt never wash my feet, Jesus answered him: If I wash thee not, thou shalt have no part with me.€€ƒxPSimon Peter saith to him: Lord, not only my feet, but also my hands and my head.€€ƒx|Jesus saith to him: He that is washed needeth not but to wash his feet, but is clean wholly. And you are clean, but not all.€€ƒxWFor he knew who he was that would betray him; therefore he said: You are not all clean.€€ƒx„Then after he had washed their feet and taken his garments, being set down again, he said to them: Know you what I have done to you?€€ƒx;You call me Master and Lord. And you say well: for so I am.€€ƒxgIf then I being your Lord and Master, have washed your feet; you also ought to wash one another's feet.€€ƒxLFor I have given you an example, that as I have done to you, so you do also.€€ƒxyAmen, amen, I say to you: The servant is not greater than his lord: neither is the apostle greater than he that sent him.€€ƒx>If you know these things, you shall be blessed if you do them.€€ƒx›I speak not of you all: I know whom I have chosen. But that the scripture may be fulfilled: He that eateth bread with me shall lift up his heel against me,€€ƒxmAt present I tell you, before it come to pass: that when it shall come to pass, you may believe that I am he.€€ƒx€Amen, amen, I say to you, he that receiveth whomsoever I send receiveth me: and he that receiveth me receiveth him that sent me.€€ƒxWhen Jesus had said these things, he was troubled in spirit; and he testified, and said: Amen, amen, I say to you, one of you shall betray me.€€ƒxKThe disciples therefore looked one upon another, doubting of whom he spoke.€€ƒxMNow there was leaning on Jesus' bosom one of his disciples, whom Jesus loved.€€ƒxUSimon Peter therefore beckoned to him and said to him: Who is it of whom he speaketh?€€ƒxLHe therefore, leaning on the breast of Jesus, saith to him: Lord, who is it?€€ƒxJesus answered: He it is to whom I shall reach bread dipped. And when he had dipped the bread, he gave it to Judas Iscariot, the son of Simon.€€ƒxfAnd after the morsel, Satan entered into him. And Jesus said to him: That which thou dost, do quickly.€€ƒxCNow no man at the table knew to what purpose he said this unto him.€€ƒxµFor some thought, because Judas had the purse, that Jesus had said to him: Buy those things which we have need of for the festival day: or that he should give something to the poor.€€ƒxQHe therefore, having received the morsel, went out immediately. And it was night.€€ƒxiWhen he therefore was gone out, Jesus said: Now is the Son of man glorified; and God is glorified in him.€€ƒxfIf God be glorified in him, God also will glorify him in himself: and immediately will he glorify him.€€ƒx“Little children, yet a little while I am with you. You shall seek me. And as I said to the Jews: Whither I go you cannot come; so I say to you now.€€ƒxrA new commandment I give unto you: That you love one another, as I have loved you, that you also love one another.€€ƒxWBy this shall all men know that you are my disciples, if you have love one for another.€€ƒxSimon Peter saith to him: Lord, whither goest thou? Jesus answered: Whither I go, thou canst not follow me now: but thou shalt follow hereafter.€€ƒxSPeter saith to him: Why cannot I follow thee now? I will lay down my life for thee.€€ƒx…Jesus answered him: Wilt thou lay down thy life for me? Amen, amen, I say to thee, the cock shall not crow, till thou deny me thrice.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒxuIn my Father's house there are many mansions. If not, I would have told you: because I go to prepare a place for you.€€ƒxAnd if I shall go and prepare a place for you, I will come again and will take you to myself: that where I am, you also may be.€€ƒx0And whither I go you know: and the way you know.€€ƒxWThomas saith to him: Lord, we know not whither thou goest. And how can we know the way?€€ƒxfJesus saith to him: I am the way, and the truth, and the life. No man cometh to the Father, but by me.€€ƒx†If you had known me, you would without doubt have known my Father also: and from henceforth you shall know him. And you have seen him.€€ƒxGPhilip saith to him: Lord, shew us the Father; and it is enough for us.€€ƒx§Jesus saith to him: Have I been so long a time with you and have you not known me? Philip, he that seeth me seeth the Father also. How sayest thou: Shew us the Father?€€ƒx«Do you not believe that I am in the Father and the Father in me? The words that I speak to you, I speak not of myself. But the Father who abideth in me, he doth the works.€€ƒx=Believe you not that I am in the Father and the Father in me?€€ƒx©Otherwise believe for the very works' sake. Amen, amen, I say to you, he that believeth in me, the works that I do, he also shall do: and greater than these shall he do.€€ƒxŒBecause I go to the Father: and whatsoever you shall ask the Father in my name, that will I do: that the Father may be glorified in the Son.€€ƒx9If you shall ask me any thing in my name, that I will do.€€ƒx%If you love me, keep my commandments.€€ƒxhAnd I will ask the Father: and he shall give you another Paraclete, that he may abide with you for ever:€€ƒx«The spirit of truth, whom the world cannot receive, because it seeth him not, nor knoweth him. But you shall know him; because he shall abide with you and shall be in you.€€ƒx1I will not leave you orphans: I will come to you.€€ƒxfYet a little while and the world seeth me no more. But you see me: because I live, and you shall live.€€ƒxOIn that day you shall know that I am in my Father: and you in me, and I in you.€€ƒx¯He that hath my commandments and keepeth them; he it is that loveth me. And he that loveth me shall be loved of my Father: and I will love him and will manifest myself to him.€€ƒxrJudas saith to him, not the Iscariot: Lord, how is it that thou wilt manifest thyself to us, and not to the world?€€ƒxŸJesus answered and said to him: If any one love me, he will keep my word. And my Father will love him and we will come to him and will make our abode with him.€€ƒxxHe that loveth me not keepeth not my words. And the word which you have heard is not mine; but the Father's who sent me.€€ƒx4These things have I spoken to you, abiding with you.€€ƒx­But the Paraclete, the Holy Ghost, whom the Father will send in my name, he will teach you all things and bring all things to your mind, whatsoever I shall have said to you.€€ƒx”Peace I leave with you: my peace I give unto you: not as the world giveth, do I give unto you. Let not your heart be troubled: nor let it be afraid.€€ƒxªYou have heard that I said to you: I go away, and I come unto you. If you loved me you would indeed be glad, because I go to the Father: for the Father is greater than I.€€ƒxaAnd now I have told you before it come to pass: that when it shall come to pass, you may believe.€€ƒxqI will not now speak many things with you. For the prince of this world: cometh: and in me he hath not any thing.€€ƒx‚But that the world may know that I love the Father: and as the Father hath given me commandments, so do I. Arise, let us go hence.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒx•Every branch in me that beareth not fruit, he will take away: and every one that beareth fruit, he will purge it, that it may bring forth more fruit.€€ƒxDNow you are clean, by reason of the word which I have spoken to you.€€ƒxAbide in me: and I in you. As the branch cannot bear fruit of itself, unless it abide in the vine, so neither can you, unless you abide in me.€€ƒx…I am the vine: you the branches. He that abideth in me, and I in him, the same beareth much fruit: for without me you can do nothing.€€ƒx™If any one abide not in me, he shall be cast forth as a branch and shall wither: and they shall gather him up and cast him into the fire: and he burneth.€€ƒxmIf you abide in me and my words abide in you, you shall ask whatever you will: and it shall be done unto you.€€ƒx]In this is my Father glorified: that you bring forth very much fruit and become my disciples.€€ƒxEAs the Father hath loved me, I also have loved you. Abide in my love.€€ƒxIf you keep my commandments, you shall abide in my love: as I also have kept my Father's commandments and do abide in his love.€€ƒxYThese things I have spoken to you, that my joy may be in you, and your joy may be filled.€€ƒxGThis is my commandment, that you love one another, as I have loved you.€€ƒxQGreater love than this no man hath, that a man lay down his life for his friends.€€ƒx<You are my friends, if you do the things that I command you.€€ƒxÄI will not now call you servants: for the servant knoweth not what his lord doth. But I have called you friends. because all things, whatsoever I have heard of my Father, I have made known to you.€€ƒxáYou have not chosen me: but I have chosen you; and have appointed you, that you should go and should bring forth fruit; and your fruit should remain: that whatsoever you shall ask of the Father in my name, he may give it you.€€ƒx6These things I command you, that you love one another.€€ƒx@If the world hate you, know ye that it hath hated me before you.€€ƒx©If you had been of the world, the world would love its own: but because you are not of the world, but I have chosen you out of the world, therefore the world hateth you.€€ƒxÀRemember my word that I said to you: The servant is not greater than his master. If they have persecuted me, they will also persecute you. If they have kept my word, they will keep yours also.€€ƒxdBut all these things they will do to you for my name's sake: because they know not him that sent me.€€ƒxiIf I had not come and spoken to them, they would not have sin: but now they have no excuse for their sin.€€ƒx(He that hateth me hateth my Father also.€€ƒx™If I had not done among them the works that no other man hath done, they would not have sin: but now they have both seen and hated both me and my Father.€€ƒx^But that the word may be fulfilled which is written in their law: they hated me without cause.€€ƒx˜But when the Paraclete cometh, whom I will send you from the Father, the Spirit of truth, who proceedeth from the Father, he shall give testimony of me.€€ƒxIAnd you shall give testimony, because you are with me from the beginning.€€ƒ`€€˜!ƒxƒThey will put you out of the synagogues: yea, the hour cometh, that whosoever killeth you will think that he doth a service to God.€€ƒxTAnd these things will they do to you; because they have not known the Father nor me.€€ƒxjBut these things I have told you, that when the hour shall come, you may remember that I told you of them.€€ƒxœBut I told you not these things from the beginning, because I was with you. And now I go to him that sent me, and none of you asketh me: Whither goest thou?€€ƒxMBut because I have spoken these things to you, sorrow hath filled your heart.€€ƒx•But I tell you the truth: it is expedient to you that I go. For if I go not, the Paraclete will not come to you: but if I go, I will send him to you.€€ƒxVAnd when he is come, he will convince the world of sin and of justice and of judgment.€€ƒx(Of sin: because they believed not in me.€€ƒxKAnd of justice: because I go to the Father: and you shall see me no longer.€€ƒxDAnd of judgment: because the prince of this world is already judged.€€ƒxCI have yet many things to say to you: but you cannot bear them now.€€ƒxÔBut when he, the Spirit of truth, is come, he will teach you all truth. For he shall not speak of himself: but what things soever he shall hear, he shall speak. And the things that are to come, he shall shew you.€€ƒxOHe shall glorify me: because he shall receive of mine and shall shew it to you.€€ƒxpAll things whatsoever the Father hath are mine. Therefore I said that he shall receive of me and shew it to you.€€ƒxyA little while, and now you shall not see me: and again a little while, and you shall see me: because I go to the Father.€€ƒxÌThen some of his disciples said one to another: What is this that he saith to us: A little while, and you shall not see me: and again a little while, and you shall see me, and, Because I go to the Father?€€ƒx^They said therefore: What is this that he saith, A little while? We know not what he speaketh.€€ƒx×And Jesus knew that they had a mind to ask him. And he said to them: Of this do you inquire among yourselves, because I said: A little while, and you shall not see me; and again a little while, and you shall see me?€€ƒx¡Amen, amen, I say to you, that you shall lament and weep, but the world shall rejoice: and you shall be made sorrowful, but your sorrow shall be turned into joy.€€ƒxÁA woman, when she is in labour, hath sorrow, because her hour is come; but when she hath brought forth the child, she remembereth no more the anguish, for joy that a man is born into the world.€€ƒxƒSo also you now indeed have sorrow: but I will see you again and your heart shall rejoice. And your joy no man shall take from you.€€ƒxŠAnd in that day you shall not ask me any thing. Amen, amen, I say to you: if you ask the Father any thing in my name, he will give it you.€€ƒxiHitherto, you have not asked any thing in my name. Ask, and you shall receive; that your joy may be full.€€ƒx—These things I have spoken to you in proverbs. The hour cometh when I will no longer speak to you in proverbs, but will shew you plainly of the Father.€€ƒx_In that day, you shall ask in my name: and I say not to you that I will ask the Father for you.€€ƒxhFor the Father himself loveth you, because you have loved me and have believed that I came out from God.€€ƒxhI came forth from the Father and am come into the world: again I leave the world and I go to the Father.€€ƒxTHis disciples say to him: Behold, now thou speakest plainly and speakest no proverb.€€ƒxNow we know that thou knowest all things and thou needest not that any man should ask thee. By this we believe that thou camest forth from God.€€ƒx(Jesus answered them: Do you now believe?€€ƒx®Behold, the hour cometh, and it is now come, that you shall be scattered every man to his own and shall leave me alone. And yet I am not alone, because the Father is with me.€€ƒx—These things I have spoken to you, that in me you may have peace. In the world you shall have distress. But have confidence. I have overcome the world.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒxkAs thou hast given him power over all flesh, that he may give eternal life to all whom thou hast given him.€€ƒxlNow this is eternal life: That they may know thee, the only true God, and Jesus Christ, whom thou hast sent.€€ƒxXI have glorified thee on the earth; I have finished the work which thou gavest me to do.€€ƒxmAnd now glorify thou me, O Father, with thyself, with the glory which I had, before the world was, with thee.€€ƒx™I have manifested thy name to the men whom thou hast given me out of the world. Thine they were: and to me thou gavest them. And they have kept thy word.€€ƒxKNow they have known that all things which thou hast given me are from thee:€€ƒxÀBecause the words which thou gavest me, I have given to them. And they have received them and have known in very deed that I came out from thee: and they have believed that thou didst send me.€€ƒxhI pray for them. I pray not for the world, but for them whom thou hast given me: because they are thine.€€ƒxLAnd all my things are thine, and thine are mine: and I am glorified in them.€€ƒx°And now I am not in the world, and these are in the world, and I come to thee. Holy Father, keep them in thy name whom thou hast given me: that they may be one, as we also are.€€ƒx¯While I was with them, I kept them in thy name. Those whom thou gavest me have I kept: and none of them is lost, but the son of perdition: that the scripture may be fulfilled.€€ƒxnAnd now I come to thee: and these things I speak in the world, that they may have my joy filled in themselves.€€ƒx|I have given them thy word, and the world hath hated them: because they are not of the world, as I also am not of the world.€€ƒxeI pray not that thou shouldst take them out of the world, but that thou shouldst keep them from evil.€€ƒx9They are not of the world, as I also am not of the world.€€ƒx*Sanctify them in truth. Thy word is truth.€€ƒxJAs thou hast sent me into the world, I also have sent them into the world.€€ƒxMAnd for them do I sanctify myself, that they also may be sanctified in truth.€€ƒx^And not for them only do I pray, but for them also who through their word shall believe in me.€€ƒx”That they all may be one, as thou, Father, in me, and I in thee; that they also may be one in us: that the world may believe that thou hast sent me.€€ƒxhAnd the glory which thou hast given me, I have given to them: that, they may be one, as we also are one.€€ƒxŸI in them, and thou in me: that they may be made perfect in one: and the world may know that thou hast sent me and hast loved them, as thou hast also loved me.€€ƒxÃFather, I will that where I am, they also whom thou hast given me may be with me: that they may see my glory which thou hast given me, because thou hast loved me before the creation of the world.€€ƒxoJust Father, the world hath not known thee: but I have known thee. And these have known that thou hast sent me.€€ƒxˆAnd I have made known thy name to them and will make it known: that the love wherewith thou hast loved me may be in them, and I in them.€€ƒ`€€˜(ƒxwAnd Judas also, who betrayed him, knew the place: because Jesus had often resorted thither together with his disciples.€€ƒxŸJudas therefore having received a band of soldiers and servants from the chief priests and the Pharisees, cometh thither with lanterns and torches and weapons.€€ƒxiJesus therefore, knowing all things that should come upon him, went forth and said to them: Whom seek ye?€€ƒxvThey answered him: Jesus of Nazareth. Jesus saith to them: I am he. And Judas also, who betrayed him, stood with them.€€ƒx]As soon therefore as he had said to them: I am he; they went backward and fell to the ground.€€ƒxNAgain therefore he asked them: Whom seek ye? And they said: Jesus of Nazareth.€€ƒx_Jesus answered: I have told you that I am he. If therefore you seek me, let these go their way,€€ƒxiThat the word might be fulfilled which he said: Of them whom thou hast given me, I have not lost any one.€€ƒx˜Then Simon Peter, having a sword, drew it and struck the servant of the high priest and cut off his right ear. And the name of thee servant was Malchus.€€ƒxƒJesus therefore said to Peter: Put up thy sword into the scabbard. The chalice which my father hath given me, shall I not drink it?€€ƒxTThen the band and the tribune and the servants of the Jews took Jesus and bound him.€€ƒxpAnd they led him away to Annas first, for he was father-in-law to Caiphas, who was the high priest of that year.€€ƒxwNow Caiphas was he who had given the counsel to the Jews: That it was expedient that one man should die for the people.€€ƒx¥And Simon Peter followed Jesus: and so did another disciple. And that disciple was known to the high priest and went in with Jesus into the court of the high priest.€€ƒxBut Peter stood at the door without. The other disciple therefore, who was known to the high priest, went out and spoke to the portress and brought in Peter.€€ƒxuThe maid therefore that was portress saith to Peter: Art not thou also one of this man's disciple? He saith I am not.€€ƒx Now the servants and ministers stood at a fire of coals, because it was cold, and warmed themselves. And with them was Peter also, standing and warming himself.€€ƒxKThe high priest therefore asked Jesus of his disciples and of his doctrine.€€ƒx±Jesus answered him: I have spoken openly to the world. I have always taught in the synagogue and in the temple, whither all the Jews resort: and in secret I have spoken nothing.€€ƒxsWhy askest thou me? Ask them who have heard what I have spoken unto them. Behold they know what things I have said.€€ƒx€And when he had said these things, one of the servants standing by gave Jesus a blow, saying: Answerest thou the high priest so?€€ƒxiJesus answered him: If I have spoken evil, give testimony of the evil; but if well, why strikest thou me?€€ƒx4And Annas sent him bound to Caiphas the high priest.€€ƒx–And Simon Peter was standing and warming himself. They said therefore to him: Art not thou also one of his disciples? He denied it and said: I am not.€€ƒxŠOne of the servants of the high priest (a kinsman to him whose ear Peter cut off) saith to him: Did not I see thee in the garden with him?€€ƒx<Again therefore Peter denied: and immediately the cock crew.€€ƒx°Then they led Jesus from Caiphas to the governor's hall. And it was morning: and they went not into the hall, that they might not be defiled, but that they might eat the pasch.€€ƒxXPilate therefore went out to them, and said: What accusation bring you against this man?€€ƒxgThey answered and said to him: If he were not a malefactor, we would not have delivered him up to thee.€€ƒx¢Pilate therefore said to them: Take him you, and judge him according to your law. The Jews therefore said to him: It is not lawful for us to put any man to death.€€ƒx^That the word of Jesus might be fulfilled, which he said, signifying what death he should die.€€ƒxjPilate therefore went into the hall again and called Jesus and said to him: Art thou the king of the Jews?€€ƒxUJesus answered: Sayest thou this thing of thyself, or have others told it thee of me?€€ƒxtPilate answered: Am I a Jew? Thy own nation and the chief priests have delivered thee up to me. What hast thou done?€€ƒxÇJesus answered: My kingdom is not of this world. If my kingdom were of this world, my servants would certainly strive that I should not be delivered to the Jews: but now my kingdom is not from hence.€€ƒxöPilate therefore said to him: Art thou a king then? Jesus answered: Thou sayest that I am a king. For this was I born, and for this came I into the world; that I should give testimony to the truth. Every one that is of the truth heareth my voice.€€ƒxƒPilate saith to him: What is truth? And when he said this, he went out again to the Jews and saith to them: I find no cause in him.€€ƒx‰But you have a custom that I should release one unto you at the Pasch. Will you, therefore, that I release unto you the king of the Jews?€€ƒxYThen cried they all again, saying: Not this man, but Barabbas. Now Barabbas was a robber.€€ƒ`€€˜*ƒxhAnd the soldiers platting a crown of thorns, put it upon his head: and they put on him a purple garment.€€ƒxOAnd they came to him and said: Hail, king of the Jews. And they gave him blows.€€ƒx‡Pilate therefore went forth again and saith to them: Behold, I bring him forth unto you, that you may know that I find no cause in him.€€ƒxw(Jesus therefore came forth, bearing the crown of thorns and the purple garment.) And he saith to them: Behold the Man.€€ƒxÄWhen the chief priests, therefore, and the servants had seen him, they cried out, saying: Crucify him, Crucify him. Pilate saith to them: Take him you, and crucify him: for I find no cause in him.€€ƒxwThe Jews answered him: We have a law; and according to the law he ought to die, because he made himself the Son of God.€€ƒx@When Pilate therefore had heard this saying, he feared the more.€€ƒxhAnd he entered into the hall again; and he said to Jesus: Whence art thou? But Jesus gave him no answer.€€ƒxPilate therefore saith to him: Speakest thou not to me? Knowest thou not that I have power to crucify thee, and I have power to release thee?€€ƒx¥Jesus answered: Thou shouldst not have any power against me, unless it were given thee from above. Therefore, he that hath delivered me to thee hath the greater sin.€€ƒxÆAnd from henceforth Pilate sought to release him. But the Jews cried out, saying: If thou release this man, thou art not Caesar's friend. For whosoever maketh himself a king speaketh against Caesar.€€ƒx¢Now when Pilate had heard these words, he brought Jesus forth and sat down in the judgment seat, in the place that is called Lithostrotos, and in Hebrew Gabbatha.€€ƒxhAnd it was the parasceve of the pasch, about the sixth hour: and he saith to the Jews: Behold your king.€€ƒx§But they cried out: Away with him: Away with him: Crucify him. Pilate saith to them: shall I crucify your king? The chief priests answered: We have no king but Caesar.€€ƒx_Then therefore he delivered him to them to be crucified. And they took Jesus and led him forth.€€ƒxfAnd bearing his own cross, he went forth to the place which is called Calvary, but in Hebrew Golgotha.€€ƒx\Where they crucified him, and with him two others, one on each side, and Jesus in the midst.€€ƒxzAnd Pilate wrote a title also: and he put it upon the cross. And the writing was: JESUS OF NAZARETH, THE KING OF THE JEWS.€€ƒx§This title therefore many of the Jews did read: because the place where Jesus was crucified was nigh to the city. And it was written in Hebrew, in Greek, and in Latin.€€ƒx€Then the chief priests of the Jews said to Pilate: Write not: The King of the Jews. But that he said: I am the King of the Jews.€€ƒx5Pilate answered: What I have written, I have written.€€ƒxÌThe soldiers therefore, when they had crucified him, took his garments, (and they made four parts, to every soldier a part) and also his coat. Now the coat was without seam, woven from the top throughout.€€ƒyThey said then one to another: Let us not cut it but let us cast lots for it, whose it shall be; that the scripture might be fulfilled, saying: They have parted my garments among them, and upon my vesture they have cast lots. And the soldiers indeed did these things.€€ƒxoNow there stood by the cross of Jesus, his mother and his mother's sister, Mary of Cleophas, and Mary Magdalen.€€ƒx€When Jesus therefore had seen his mother and the disciple standing whom he loved, he saith to his mother: Woman, behold thy son.€€ƒxnAfter that, he saith to the disciple: Behold thy mother. And from that hour, the disciple took her to his own.€€ƒxwAfterwards, Jesus knowing that all things were now accomplished, that the scripture might be fulfilled, said: I thirst.€€ƒx€Now there was a vessel set there, full of vinegar. And they, putting a sponge full of vinegar about hyssop, put it to his mouth.€€ƒxsJesus therefore, when he had taken the vinegar, said: It is consummated. And bowing his head, he gave up the ghost.€€ƒxèThen the Jews (because it was the parasceve), that the bodies might not remain upon the cross on the sabbath day (for that was a great sabbath day), besought Pilate that their legs might be broken: and that they might be taken away.€€ƒxpThe soldiers therefore came: and they broke the legs of the first, and of the other that was crucified with him.€€ƒxgBut after they were come to Jesus, when they saw that he was already dead, they did not break his legs.€€ƒxeBut one of the soldiers with a spear opened his side: and immediately there came out blood and water.€€ƒxAnd he that saw it hath given testimony: and his testimony is true. And he knoweth that he saith true: that you also may believe.€€ƒxdFor these things were done that the scripture might be fulfilled: You shall not break a bone of him.€€ƒxLAnd again another scripture saith: They shall look on him whom they pierced.€€ƒxùAnd after these things, Joseph of Arimathea (because he was a disciple of Jesus, but secretly for fear of the Jews), besought Pilate that he might take away the body of Jesus. And Pilate gave leave. He came therefore and took away the body of Jesus.€€ƒx‹And Nicodemus also came (he who at the first came to Jesus by night), bringing a mixture of myrrh and aloes, about an hundred pound weight.€€ƒxzThey took therefore the body of Jesus and bound it in linen cloths, with the spices, as the manner of the Jews is to bury.€€ƒx€Now there was in the place where he was crucified a garden: and in the garden a new sepulchre, wherein no man yet had been laid.€€ƒxpThere, therefore, because of the parasceve of the Jews, they laid Jesus: because the sepulchre was nigh at hand.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒxÇShe ran therefore and cometh to Simon Peter and to the other disciple whom Jesus loved and saith to them: They have taken away the Lord out of the sepulchre: and we know not where they have laid him.€€ƒxQPeter therefore went out, and the other disciple: and they came to the sepulchre.€€ƒxeAnd they both ran together: and that other disciple did outrun Peter and came first to the sepulchre.€€ƒxPAnd when he stooped down, he saw the linen cloths lying: but yet he went not in.€€ƒxdThen cometh Simon Peter, following him, and went into the sepulchre: and saw the linen cloths lying,€€ƒxsAnd the napkin that had been about his head, not lying with the linen cloths, but apart, wrapped up into one place.€€ƒx`Then that other disciple also went in, who came first to the sepulchre: and he saw and believed.€€ƒxNFor as yet they knew not the scripture, that he must rise again from the dead.€€ƒx5The disciples therefore departed again to their home.€€ƒxyBut Mary stood at the sepulchre without, weeping. Now as she was weeping, she stooped down and looked into the sepulchre,€€ƒxvAnd she saw two angels in white, sitting, one at the head, and one at the feet, where the body of Jesus had been laid.€€ƒx‹They say to her: Woman, why weepest thou? She saith to them: Because they have taken away my Lord: and I know not where they have laid him.€€ƒxkWhen she had thus said, she turned herself back and saw Jesus standing: and she knew not that it was Jesus.€€ƒxÔJesus saith to her: Woman, why weepest thou? Whom seekest thou? She, thinking that it was the gardener, saith to him: Sir, if thou hast taken him hence, tell me where thou hast laid him: and I will take him away.€€ƒxWJesus saith to her: Mary. She turning, saith to him: Rabboni (which is to say, Master).€€ƒx¸Jesus saith to her: Do not touch me: for I am not yet ascended to my Father. But go to my brethren and say to them: I ascend to my Father and to your Father, to my God and to your God.€€ƒxeMary Magdalen cometh and telleth the disciples: I have seen the Lord; and these things he said to me.€€ƒxÙNow when it was late the same day, the first of the week, and the doors were shut, where the disciples were gathered together, for fear of the Jews, Jesus came and stood in the midst and said to them: Peace be to you.€€ƒx|And when he had said this, he shewed them his hands and his side. The disciples therefore were glad, when they saw the Lord.€€ƒx^He said therefore to them again: Peace be to you. As the Father hath sent me, I also send you.€€ƒx[When he had said this, he breathed on them; and he said to them: Receive ye the Holy Ghost.€€ƒxiWhose sins you shall forgive, they are forgiven them: and whose sins you shall retain, they are retained.€€ƒxXNow Thomas, one of the twelve, who is called Didymus, was not with them when Jesus came.€€ƒxîThe other disciples therefore said to him: We have seen the Lord. But he said to them: Except I shall see in his hands the print of the nails and put my finger into the place of the nails and put my hand into his side, I will not believe.€€ƒx¢And after eight days, again his disciples were within, and Thomas with them. Jesus cometh, the doors being shut, and stood in the midst and said: Peace be to you.€€ƒxšThen he said to Thomas: Put in thy finger hither and see my hands. And bring hither the hand and put it into my side. And be not faithless, but believing.€€ƒx4Thomas answered and said to him: My Lord and my God.€€ƒxJesus saith to him: Because thou hast seen me, Thomas, thou hast believed: blessed are they that have not seen and have believed.€€ƒxbMany other signs also did Jesus in the sight of his disciples, which are not written in this book.€€ƒxˆBut these are written, that you may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God: and that believing, you may have life in his name.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒx§There were together: Simon Peter and Thomas, who is called Didymus, and Nathanael, who was of Cana of Galilee, and the sons of Zebedee and two others of his disciples.€€ƒx¦Simon Peter saith to them: I go a fishing. They say to him: We also come with thee. And they went forth and entered into the ship: and that night they caught nothing.€€ƒxfBut when the morning was come, Jesus stood on the shore: yet the disciples knew not that it was Jesus.€€ƒxQJesus therefore said to them: Children, have you any meat? They answered him: No.€€ƒxªHe saith to them: Cast the net on the right side of the ship; and you shall find. They cast therefore: and now they were not able to draw it, for the multitude of fishes.€€ƒxÂThat disciple therefore whom Jesus loved said to Peter: It is the Lord. Simon Peter, when he heard that it was the Lord, girt his coat about him (for he was naked) and cast himself into the sea.€€ƒxBut the other disciples came in the ship (for they were not far from the land, but as it were two hundred cubits) dragging the net with fishes.€€ƒx_As soon then as they came to land they saw hot coals lying, and a fish laid thereon, and bread.€€ƒxJJesus saith to them: Bring hither of the fishes which you have now caught.€€ƒx™Simon Peter went up and drew the net to land, full of great fishes, one hundred and fifty-three. And although there were so many, the net was not broken.€€ƒxJesus saith to them: Come and dine. And none of them who were at meat, durst ask him: Who art thou? Knowing that it was the Lord.€€ƒxKAnd Jesus cometh and taketh bread and giveth them: and fish in like manner.€€ƒxhThis is now the third time that Jesus was manifested to his disciples, after he was risen from the dead.€€ƒxÉWhen therefore they had dined, Jesus saith to Simon Peter: Simon, son of John, lovest thou me more than these? He saith to him: Yea, Lord, thou knowest that I love thee. He saith to him: Feed my lambs.€€ƒx•He saith to him again: Simon, son of John, lovest thou me? He saith to him: yea, Lord, thou knowest that I love thee. He saith to him: Feed my lambs.€€ƒy␀He said to him the third time: Simon, son of John, lovest thou me? Peter was grieved because he had said to him the third time: Lovest thou me? And he said to him: Lord, thou knowest all things: thou knowest that I love thee. He said to him: Feed my sheep.€€ƒxñAmen, amen, I say to thee, When thou wast younger, thou didst gird thyself and didst walk where thou wouldst. But when thou shalt be old, thou shalt stretch forth thy hands, and another shall gird thee and lead thee whither thou wouldst not.€€ƒxxAnd this he said, signifying by what death he should glorify God. And when he had said this, he saith to him: Follow me.€€ƒxœPeter turning about, saw that disciple whom Jesus loved following, who also leaned on his breast at supper and said: Lord, who is he that shall betray thee?€€ƒxWHim therefore when Peter had seen, he saith to Jesus: Lord, and what shall this man do?€€ƒxaJesus saith to him: So I will have him to remain till I come, what is it to thee? Follow thou me.€€ƒxÌThis saying therefore went abroad among the brethren, that that disciple should not die. And Jesus did not say to him: He should not die; but: So I will have him to remain till I come, what is it to thee?€€ƒxThis is that disciple who giveth testimony of these things and hath written these things: and we know that his testimony is true.€€ƒxµBut there are also many other things which Jesus did which, if they were written every one, the world itself. I think, would not be able to contain the books that should be written.€€ƒxThe Acts of the Apostles€€dActs¤dnamedActsiosis_namedActslabbreviationdActshchapters˜˜ƒxrUntil the day on which, giving commandments by the Holy Ghost to the apostles whom he had chosen, he was taken up.€€ƒxTo whom also he shewed himself alive after his passion, by many proofs, for forty days appearing to them, and speaking of the kingdom of God.€€ƒx¹And eating together with them, he commanded them, that they should not depart from Jerusalem, but should wait for the promise of the Father, which you have heard (saith he) by my mouth.€€ƒxhFor John indeed baptized with water: but you shall be baptized with the Holy Ghost, not many days hence.€€ƒx{They therefore who were come together, asked him, saying: Lord, wilt thou at this time restore again the kingdom of Israel?€€ƒxoBut he said to them: It is not for you to know the time or moments, which the Father hath put in his own power:€€ƒx¿But you shall receive the power of the Holy Ghost coming upon you, and you shall be witnesses unto me in Jerusalem, and in all Judea, and Samaria, and even to the uttermost part of the earth.€€ƒxwAnd when he had said these things, while they looked on, he was raised up: and a cloud received him out of their sight.€€ƒxeAnd while they were beholding him going up to heaven, behold two men stood by them in white garments.€€ƒx¬Who also said: Ye men of Galilee, why stand you looking up to heaven? This Jesus who is taken up from you into heaven, shall so come as you have seen him going into heaven.€€ƒx~Then they returned to Jerusalem from the mount that is called Olivet, which is nigh Jerusalem, within a sabbath day's journey.€€ƒxØAnd when they were come in, they went up into an upper room, where abode Peter and John, James and Andrew, Philip and Thomas, Bartholomew and Matthew, James of Alpheus and Simon Zelotes and Jude the brother of James.€€ƒxwAll these were persevering with one mind in prayer with the women, and Mary the mother of Jesus, and with his brethren.€€ƒx†In those days Peter rising up in the midst of the brethren, said (now the number of persons together was about an hundred and twenty):€€ƒx²Men, brethren, the scripture must needs be fulfilled, which the Holy Ghost spoke before by the mouth of David concerning Judas, who was the leader of them that apprehended Jesus:€€ƒxAWho was numbered with us, and had obtained part of this ministry.€€ƒxŒAnd he indeed hath possessed a field of the reward of iniquity, and being hanged, burst asunder in the midst: and all his bowels gushed out.€€ƒxšAnd it became known to all the inhabitants of Jerusalem: so that the same field was called in their tongue, Haceldama, that is to say, The field of blood.€€ƒxšFor it is written in the book of Psalms: Let their habitation become desolate, and let there be none to dwell therein. And his bishopric let another take.€€ƒxrWherefore of these men who have companied with us, all the time that the Lord Jesus came in and went out among us,€€ƒx“Beginning from the baptism of John, until the day wherein he was taken up from us, one of these must be made a witness with us of his resurrection.€€ƒxWAnd they appointed two, Joseph, called Barsabas, who was surnamed Justus, and Matthias.€€ƒxqAnd praying, they said: Thou, Lord, who knowest the heart of all men, shew whether of these two thou hast chosen,€€ƒx…To take the place of this ministry and apostleship, from which Judas hath by transgression fallen, that he might go to his own place.€€ƒxeAnd they gave them lot, and the lot fell upon Matthias, and he was numbered with the eleven apostles.€€ƒ`€€˜/ƒxAnd suddenly there came a sound from heaven, as of a mighty wind coming: and it filled the whole house where they were sitting.€€ƒxaAnd there appeared to them parted tongues, as it were of fire: and it sat upon every one of them.€€ƒxŠAnd they were all filled with the Holy Ghost: and they began to speak with divers tongues, according as the Holy Ghost gave them to speak.€€ƒxYNow there were dwelling at Jerusalem, Jews, devout men, out of every nation under heaven.€€ƒx•And when this was noised abroad, the multitude came together, and were confounded in mind, because that every man heard them speak in his own tongue.€€ƒx^And they were all amazed, and wondered, saying: Behold, are not all these that speak Galilean?€€ƒxEAnd how have we heard, every man our own tongue wherein we were born?€€ƒxhParthians and Medes and Elamites and inhabitants of Mesopotamia, Judea, and Cappadocia, Pontus and Asia,€€ƒxXPhrygia and Pamphylia, Egypt and the parts of Libya about Cyrene, and strangers of Rome,€€ƒxxJews also, and proselytes, Cretes, and Arabians: we have heard them speak in our own tongues the wonderful works of God.€€ƒxUAnd they were all astonished, and wondered, saying one to another: What meaneth this?€€ƒx9But others mocking, said: These men are full of new wine.€€ƒx¾But Peter standing up with the eleven, lifted up his voice, and spoke to them: Ye men of Judea, and all you that dwell in Jerusalem, be this known to you and with your ears receive my words.€€ƒxTFor these are not drunk, as you suppose, seeing it is but the third hour of the day:€€ƒx9But this is that which was spoken of by the prophet Joel:€€ƒxçAnd it shall come to pass, in the last days, (saith the Lord), I will pour out of my Spirit upon all flesh: and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy: and your young men shall see visions, and your old men shall dream dreams.€€ƒxvAnd upon my servants indeed and upon my handmaids will I pour out in those days of my spirit: and they shall prophesy.€€ƒxqAnd I will shew wonders in the heaven above, and signs on the earth beneath: blood and fire, and vapour of smoke.€€ƒxuThe sun shall be turned into darkness and the moon into blood, before the great and manifest day of the Lord to come.€€ƒx^And it shalt come to pass, that whosoever shall call upon the name of the Lord shall be saved.€€ƒx¶Ye men of Israel, hear these words: Jesus of Nazareth, a man approved of God among you by miracles and wonders and signs, which God did by him, in the midst of you, as you also know:€€ƒx‹This same being delivered up, by the determinate counsel and foreknowledge of God, you by the hands of wicked men have crucified and slain.€€ƒxpWhom God hath raised up, having loosed the sorrows of hell, as it was impossible that he should be holden by it.€€ƒx{For David saith concerning him: I foresaw the Lord before my face: because he is at my right hand, that I may not be moved.€€ƒxiFor this my heart hath been glad, and my tongue hath rejoiced: moreover my flesh also shall rest in hope.€€ƒxWBecause thou wilt not leave my soul in hell: nor suffer thy Holy One to see corruption.€€ƒxaThou hast made known to me the ways of life: thou shalt make me full of joy with thy countenance.€€ƒx“Ye men, brethren, let me freely speak to you of the patriarch David: that he died and was buried; and his sepulchre is with us to this present say.€€ƒx“Whereas therefore he was a prophet and knew that God hath sworn to him with an oath, that of the fruit of his loins one should sit upon his throne.€€ƒxForeseeing this, he spoke of the resurrection of Christ. For neither was he left in hell: neither did his flesh see corruption.€€ƒx?This Jesus hath God raised again, whereof all we are witnesses.€€ƒx£Being exalted therefore by the right hand of God and having received of the Father the promise of the Holy Ghost, he hath poured forth this which you see and hear.€€ƒxmFor David ascended not into heaven; but he himself said: The Lord said to my Lord: Sit thou on my right hand,€€ƒx'Until I make thy enemies thy footstool.€€ƒxŒTherefore let all the house of Israel know most certainly that God hath made both Lord and Christ, this same Jesus, whom you have crucified.€€ƒx Now when they had heard these things, they had compunction in their heart and said to Peter and to the rest of the apostles: What shall we do, men and brethren?€€ƒx³But Peter said to them: Do penance: and be baptized every one of you in the name of Jesus Christ, for the remission of your sins. And you shall receive the gift of the Holy Ghost.€€ƒxsFor the promise is to you and to your children and to all that are far off, whomsoever the Lord our God shall call.€€ƒxuAnd with very many other words did he testify and exhort them, saying: Save yourselves from this perverse generation.€€ƒxqThey therefore that received his word were baptized: and there were added in that day about three thousand souls.€€ƒx{And they were persevering in the doctrine of the apostles and in the communication of the breaking of bread and in prayers.€€ƒx…And fear came upon every soul. Many wonders also and signs were done by the apostles in Jerusalem: and there was great fervor in all.€€ƒxCAnd all they that believed were together and had all things common.€€ƒx_Their possessions and goods they sold and divided them to all, according as every one had need.€€ƒx–And continuing daily with one accord in the temple and breaking bread from house to house, they took their meat with gladness and simplicity of heart:€€ƒxrPraising God and having favour with all the people. And the Lord increased daily together such as should be saved.€€ƒ`€€˜ƒxËAnd a certain man who was lame from his mother's womb was carried: whom they laid every day at the gate of the temple, which is called Beautiful, that he might ask alms of them that went into the temple.€€ƒx[He, when he had seen Peter and John, about to go into the temple, asked to receive an alms.€€ƒxEBut Peter with John, fastening his eyes upon him, said: Look upon us.€€ƒxSBut he looked earnestly upon them, hoping that he should receive something of them.€€ƒxƒBut Peter said: Silver and gold I have none; but what I have, I give thee. In the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, arise and walk.€€ƒxgAnd taking him by the right hand, he lifted him up: and forthwith his feet and soles received strength.€€ƒxpAnd he leaping up, stood and walked and went in with them into the temple, walking and leaping and praising God.€€ƒx4And all the people saw him walking and praising God.€€ƒx­And they knew him, that it was he who sat begging alms at the Beautiful gate of the temple: and they were filled with wonder and amazement at that which had happened to him.€€ƒxuAnd as he held Peter and John, all the people ran to them, to the porch which is called Solomon's, greatly wondering.€€ƒx¬But Peter seeing, made answer to the people: Ye men of Israel, why wonder you at this? Or why look you upon us, as if by our strength or power we had made this man to walk?€€ƒxÜThe God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob, the God of our fathers, hath glorified his Son Jesus, whom you indeed delivered up and denied before the face of Pilate, when he judged he should be released.€€ƒxXBut you denied the Holy One and the Just: and desired a murderer to be granted unto you.€€ƒxaBut the author of life you killed, whom God hath raised from the dead: of which we are witnesses.€€ƒxºAnd in the faith of his name, this man, whom you have seen and known, hath his name strengthened. And the faith which is by him hath given this perfect soundness in the sight of you all.€€ƒxUAnd now, brethren, I know that you did it through ignorance: as did also your rulers.€€ƒxƒBut those things which God before had shewed by the mouth of all the prophets, that his Christ should suffer, he hath so fulfilled.€€ƒxLBe penitent, therefore, and be converted, that your sins may be blotted out.€€ƒx‘That when the times of refreshment shall come from the presence of the Lord, and he shall send him who hath been preached unto you, Jesus Christ.€€ƒx¬Whom heaven indeed must receive, until the times of the restitution of all things, which God hath spoken by the mouth of his holy prophets, from the beginning of the world.€€ƒx°For Moses said: A prophet shall the Lord your God raise up unto you of your brethren, like unto me: him you shall hear according to all things whatsoever he shall speak to you.€€ƒxkAnd it shall be, that every soul which will not hear that prophet shall be destroyed from among the people.€€ƒx[And all the prophets, from Samuel and afterwards, who have spoken, have told of these days.€€ƒx«You are the children of the prophets and of the testament which God made to our fathers, saying to Abraham: And in thy seed shall all the kindreds of the earth be blessed.€€ƒxzTo you first, God, raising up his Son, hath sent him to bless you: that every one may convert himself from his wickedness.€€ƒ`€€˜%ƒx_Being grieved that they taught the people and preached in Jesus the resurrection from the dead:€€ƒx]And they laid hands upon them and put them in hold till the next day: for it was now evening.€€ƒxcBut many of them who had heard the word believed: and the number of the men was made five thousand.€€ƒxsAnd it came to pass on the morrow, that their princes and ancients and scribes were gathered together in Jerusalem.€€ƒxtAnd Annas the high priest and Caiphas and John and Alexander: and as many as were of the kindred of the high priest.€€ƒx]And setting them in the midst, they asked: By what power or by what name, have you done this?€€ƒxbThen Peter, filled with the Holy Ghost, said to them: Ye princes of the people and ancients, hear.€€ƒxsIf we this day are examined concerning the good deed done to the infirm man, by what means he hath been made whole:€€ƒxİBe it known to you all and to all the people of Israel, that by the name of our Lord Jesus Christ of Nazareth, whom you crucified, whom God hath raised from the dead, even by him, this man standeth here before you, whole.€€ƒxaThis is the stone which was rejected by you the builders, which is become the head of the corner.€€ƒxxNeither is there salvation in any other. For there is no other name under heaven given to men, whereby we must be saved.€€ƒx©Now seeing the constancy of Peter and of John, understanding that they were illiterate and ignorant men, they wondered: and they knew them that they had been with Jesus.€€ƒx_Seeing the man also who had been healed, standing with them, they could say nothing against it.€€ƒx\But they commanded them to go aside out of the council: and they conferred among themselves,€€ƒx¥Saying: What shall we do to these men? For indeed a miracle hath been done by them, known to all the inhabitants of Jerusalem. It is manifest: and we cannot deny it.€€ƒx|But that it may be no farther spread among the people, let us threaten them that they speak no more in this name to any man.€€ƒxXAnd calling them, they charged them not to speak at all, nor teach in the name of Jesus.€€ƒxvBut Peter and John answering, said to them: If it be just, in the sight of God, to hear you rather than God, judge ye.€€ƒx@For we cannot but speak the things which we have seen and heard.€€ƒx¯But they, threatening, sent them away, not finding how they might punish them, because of the people: for all men glorified what had been done, in that which had come to pass.€€ƒxUFor the man was above forty years old, in whom that miraculous cure had been wrought.€€ƒxvAnd being let go, they came to their own company and related all that the chief priests and ancients had said to them.€€ƒx¨Who having heard it, with one accord lifted up their voice to God and said: Lord, thou art he that didst make heaven and earth, the sea and all things that are in them.€€ƒx‘Who, by the Holy Ghost, by the mouth of our father David, thy servant, hast said: Why did the Gentiles rage: and the people meditate vain things?€€ƒxdThe kings of the earth stood up: and the princes assembled together against the Lord and his Christ.€€ƒx²For of a truth there assembled together in this city against thy holy child Jesus, whom thou hast anointed, Herod, and Pontius Pilate, with the Gentiles and the people of Israel,€€ƒx7To do what thy hand and thy counsel decreed to be done.€€ƒxwAnd now, Lord, behold their threatenings: and grant unto thy servants that with all confidence they may speak thy word,€€ƒxkBy stretching forth thy hand to cures and signs and wonders, to be done by the name of thy holy Son, Jesus.€€ƒx¨And when they had prayed, the place was moved wherein they were assembled: and they were all filled with the Holy Ghost: and they spoke the word of God with confidence.€€ƒxµAnd the multitude of believers had but one heart and one soul. Neither did any one say that aught of the things which he possessed was his own: but all things were common unto them.€€ƒxƒAnd with great power did the Apostles give testimony of the resurrection of Jesus Christ our Lord: and great grace was in them all.€€ƒx–For neither was there any one needy among them. For as many as were owners of lands or houses sold them and brought the price of the things they sold,€€ƒxsAnd laid it down before the feet of the apostles. And distribution was made to every one, according as he had need.€€ƒxˆAnd Joseph, who, by the apostles, was surnamed Barnabas (which is, by interpretation, The son of consolation), a Levite, a Cyprian born,€€ƒxSHaving land, sold it and brought the price and laid it at the feet of the Apostles.€€ƒ`€€˜*ƒxAnd by fraud kept back part of the price of the land, his wife being privy thereunto: and bringing a certain part of it, laid it at the feet of the apostles.€€ƒx”But Peter said: Ananias, why hath Satan tempted thy heart, that thou shouldst lie to the Holy Ghost and by fraud keep part of the price of the land?€€ƒx¶Whilst it remained, did it not remain to thee? And after it was sold, was it not in thy power? Why hast thou conceived this thing in thy heart? Thou hast not lied to men, but to God.€€ƒxtAnd Ananias, hearing these words, fell down and gave up the ghost. And there came great fear upon all that heard it.€€ƒxKAnd the young men rising up, removed him, and carrying him out, buried him.€€ƒxgAnd it was about the space of three hours after, when his wife, not knowing what had happened, came in.€€ƒxmAnd Peter said to her: Tell me, woman, whether you sold the land for so much? And she said: Yea, for so much.€€ƒxºAnd Peter said unto her: Why have you agreed together to tempt the spirit of the Lord? Behold the feet of them who have buried thy husband are at the door: and they shall carry thee out,€€ƒx Immediately, she fell down before his feet and gave up the ghost. And the young men coming in found her dead: and carried her out and buried her by her husband.€€ƒxUAnd there came great fear upon the whole church and upon all that heard these things.€€ƒxŒAnd by the hands of the apostles were many signs and wonders wrought among the people. And they were all with one accord in Solomon's porch.€€ƒxSBut of the rest no man durst join himself unto them: but the people magnified them.€€ƒxOAnd the multitude of men and women who believed in the Lord was more increased:€€ƒxİInsomuch that they brought forth the sick into the streets and laid them on beds and couches, that, when Peter came, his shadow at the least might overshadow any of them and they might be delivered from their infirmities.€€ƒx°And there came also together to Jerusalem a multitude out of the neighbouring cities, bringing sick persons and such as were troubled with unclean spirits: who were all healed.€€ƒx}Then the high priest rising up, and all they that were with him (which is the heresy of the Sadducees) were filled with envy.€€ƒxFAnd they laid hands on the apostles and put them in the common prison.€€ƒx^But an angel of the Lord by night, opening the doors of the prison and leading them out, said:€€ƒxNGo, and standing speak in the temple to the people all the words of this life.€€ƒyWho having heard this, early in the morning, entered into the temple and taught. And the high priest coming, and they that were with him, called together the council and all the ancients of the children of Israel: and they sent to the prison to have them brought.€€ƒx`But when the ministers came and opening the prison found them not there, they returned and told,€€ƒxSaying: The prison indeed we found shut with all diligence, and the keepers standing before the door: but opening it, we found no man within.€€ƒxˆNow when the officer of the temple and the chief priests heard these words, they were in doubt concerning them, what would come to pass.€€ƒxwBut one came and told them: Behold, the men whom you put in prison are in the temple, standing and teaching the people.€€ƒxƒThen went the officer with the ministers and brought them without violence: for they feared the people, lest they should be stoned.€€ƒxaAnd when they had brought them, they set them before the council. And the high priest asked them,€€ƒxÂSaying: Commanding, we commanded you that you should not teach in this name. And behold, you have filled Jerusalem with your doctrine: and you have a mind to bring the blood of this man upon us.€€ƒxQBut Peter and the apostles answering, said: We ought to obey God rather than men.€€ƒx\The God of our fathers hath raised up Jesus, whom you put to death, hanging him upon a tree.€€ƒxwHim hath God exalted with his right hand, to be Prince and Saviour. to give repentance to Israel and remission of sins.€€ƒxcAnd we are witnesses of these things: and the Holy Ghost, whom God hath given to all that obey him.€€ƒxdWhen they had heard these things, they were cut to the heart: and they thought to put them to death.€€ƒx¡But one in the council rising up, a Pharisee, named Gamaliel, a doctor of the law, respected by all the people, commanded the men to be put forth a little while.€€ƒxlAnd he said to them: Ye men of Israel, take heed to yourselves what you intend to do, as touching these men.€€ƒxØFor before these days rose up Theodas, affirming himself to be somebody, to whom a number of men, about four hundred, joined themselves. Who was slain: and all that believed him were scattered and brought to nothing.€€ƒx¸After this man, rose up Judas of Galilee, in the days of the enrolling, and drew away the people after him. He also perished: and all, even as many as consented to him, were dispersed.€€ƒxAnd now, therefore, I say to you: Refrain from these men and let them alone. For if this council or this work be of men, it will come to nought:€€ƒx}But if it be of God, you cannot overthrow it, lest perhaps you be found even to fight against God. And they consented to him.€€ƒxAnd calling in the apostles, after they had scourged them, they charged them that they should not speak at all in the name of Jesus. And they dismissed them.€€ƒxŠAnd they indeed went from the presence of the council, rejoicing that they were accounted worthy to suffer reproach for the name of Jesus.€€ƒxgAnd every day they ceased not, in the temple and from house to house, to teach and preach Christ Jesus.€€ƒ`€€ƒxThen the twelve, calling together the multitude of the disciples, said: It is not reason that we should leave the word of God and serve tables.€€ƒx“Wherefore, brethren, look ye out among you seven men of good reputation, full of the Holy Ghost and wisdom, whom we may appoint over this business.€€ƒxQBut we will give ourselves continually to prayer and to the ministry of the word.€€ƒxÓAnd the saying was liked by all the multitude. And they chose Stephen, a man full of faith and of the Holy Ghost, and Philip and Prochorus and Nicanor, and Timon and Parmenas and Nicolas, a proselyte of Antioch.€€ƒxNThese they set before the apostles: and they praying, imposed hands upon them.€€ƒx¤And the word of the Lord increased: and the number of the disciples was multiplied in Jerusalem exceedingly. A great multitude also of the priests obeyed the faith.€€ƒxWAnd Stephen, full of grace and fortitude, did great wonders and signs among the people.€€ƒx½Now there arose some, of that which is called the synagogue of the Libertines and of the Cyrenians and of the Alexandrians and of them that were of Cilicia and Asia, disputing with Stephen.€€ƒxFAnd they were not able to resist the wisdom and the spirit that spoke.€€ƒxhThen they suborned men to say they had heard him speak words of blasphemy against Moses and against God.€€ƒx„And they stirred up the people and the ancients and the scribes. And running together, they took him and brought him to the council.€€ƒxrAnd they set up false witnesses, who said: This man ceaseth not to speak words against the holy place and the law.€€ƒxFor we have heard him say that this Jesus of Nazareth shall destroy this place and shall change the traditions which Moses delivered unto us.€€ƒxeAnd all that sat in the council, looking on him, saw his face as if it had been the face of an angel.€€ƒ`€€˜<ƒx•Who said: Ye men, brethren and fathers, hear. The God of glory appeared to our father Abraham, when he was in Mesopotamia, before he dwelt in Charan.€€ƒxrAnd said to him: Go forth out of thy country and from thy kindred: and come into the land which I shall shew thee.€€ƒx¤Then he went out of the land of the Chaldeans and dwelt in Charan. And from thence, after his father was dead, he removed him into this land, wherein you now dwell.€€ƒx§And he gave him no inheritance in it: no, not the pace of a foot. But he promised to give it him in possession, and to his seed after him, when as yet he had no child.€€ƒxAnd God said to him: That his seed should sojourn in a strange country, and that they should bring them under bondage and treat them evil four hundred years.€€ƒxAnd the nation which they shall serve will I judge (said the Lord): and after these things they shall go out and shall serve me in this place.€€ƒx And he gave him the covenant of circumcision. And so he begot Isaac and circumcised him the eighth day: and Isaac begot Jacob: and Jacob, the twelve patriarchs.€€ƒxOAnd the patriarchs, through envy, sold Joseph into Egypt. And God was with him,€€ƒx¼And delivered him out of all his tribulations: and he gave him favour and wisdom in the sight of Pharao, the king of Egypt. And he appointed him governor over Egypt and over all his house.€€ƒxiNow there came a famine upon all Egypt and Chanaan, and great tribulation: and our fathers found no food.€€ƒxQBut when Jacob had heard that there was corn in Egypt, he sent our fathers first.€€ƒxcAnd at the second time, Joseph was known by his brethren: and his kindred was made known to Pharao.€€ƒx^And Joseph sending, called thither Jacob, his father, and all his kindred, seventy-five souls.€€ƒx<So Jacob went down into Egypt. And he died, and our fathers.€€ƒx“And they were translated into Sichem and were laid in the sepulchre that Abraham bought for a sum of money of the sons of Hemor, the son of Sichem.€€ƒxAnd when the time of the promise drew near, which God had promised to Abraham, the people increased and were multiplied in Egypt.€€ƒx6Till another king arose in Egypt, who knew not Joseph.€€ƒx’This same, dealing craftily with our race, afflicted our fathers, that they should expose their children, to the end they might not be kept alive.€€ƒxtAt the same time was Moses born: and he was acceptable to God. Who was nourished three months in his father's house.€€ƒxYAnd when he was exposed, Pharao's daughter took him up and nourished him for her own son.€€ƒxmAnd Moses was instructed in all the wisdom of the Egyptians: and he was mighty in his words and in his deeds.€€ƒxkAnd when he was full forty years old, it came into his heart to visit his brethren, the children of Israel.€€ƒx‚And when he had seen one of them suffer wrong, he defended him: and striking the Egyptian, he avenged him who suffered the injury.€€ƒxmAnd he thought that his brethren understood that God by his hand would save them. But they understood it not.€€ƒxªAnd the day following, he shewed himself to them when they were at strife and would have reconciled them in peace, saying: Men, ye are brethren. Why hurt you one another?€€ƒxvBut he that did the injury to his neighbour thrust him away, saying: Who hath appointed thee prince and judge over us:€€ƒxCWhat! Wilt thou kill me, as thou didst yesterday kill the Egyptian?€€ƒxaAnd Moses fled upon this word: and was a stranger in the land of Madian, where he begot two sons.€€ƒx}And when forty years were expired, there appeared to him, in the desert of mount Sina, an angel in a flame of fire in a bush.€€ƒxwAnd Moses seeing it wondered at the sight. And as he drew near to view it, the voice of the Lord came unto him, saying:€€ƒxƒI am the God of thy fathers: the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob. And Moses being terrified durst not behold.€€ƒxlAnd the Lord said to him: Loose the shoes from thy feet: for the place wherein thou standest is holy ground.€€ƒx³Seeing, I have seen the affliction of my people which is in Egypt: and I have heard their groaning and am come down to deliver them. And now come: and I will send thee into Egypt.€€ƒxºThis Moses, whom they refused, saying: Who hath appointed thee prince and judge? Him God sent to be prince and redeemer, by the hand of the angel who appeared to him in the burning bush.€€ƒxtHe brought them out, doing wonders and signs in the land of Egypt and in the Red Sea and in the desert, forty years.€€ƒxThis is that Moses who said to the children of Israel: A prophet shall God raise up to you of your own brethren, as myself. Him shall you hear.€€ƒx¨This is he that was in the church in the wilderness, with the angel who spoke to him on Mount Sina and with our fathers. Who received the words of life to give unto us.€€ƒx`Whom our fathers would not obey: but thrust him away and in their hearts turned back into Egypt,€€ƒx‘Saying to Aaron: Make us gods to go before us. For as for this Moses, who brought us out of the land of Egypt, we know not what is become of him.€€ƒxsAnd they made a calf in those days and offered sacrifices to the idol and rejoiced in the works of their own hands.€€ƒxÉAnd God turned and gave them up to serve the host of heaven, as it is written in the books of the prophets: Did you offer victims and sacrifices to me for forty years, in the desert, O house of Israel?€€ƒx And you took unto you the tabernacle of Moloch and the star of your god Rempham, figures which you made to adore them. And I will carry you away beyond Babylon.€€ƒx°The tabernacle of the testimony was with our fathers in the desert, as God ordained for them, speaking to Moses, that he should make it according to the form which he had seen.€€ƒx¨Which also our fathers receiving, brought in with Jesus, into the possession of the Gentiles: whom God drove out before the face of our fathers, unto the days of David,€€ƒxQWho found grace before God and desired to find a tabernacle for the God of Jacob.€€ƒxBut Solomon built him a house,€€ƒxMYet the most High dwelleth not in houses made by hands, as the prophet saith:€€ƒx‚Heaven is my throne and the earth my footstool. What house will you build me (saith the Lord)? Or what is the place of my resting?€€ƒx'Hath not my hand made all these things?€€ƒx{You stiffnecked and uncircumcised in heart and ears, you always resist the Holy Ghost. As your fathers did, so do you also.€€ƒx³Which of the prophets have not your fathers persecuted? And they have slain them who foretold of the coming of the Just One: of whom you have been now the betrayers and murderers.€€ƒxLWho have received the law by the disposition of angels and have not kept it.€€ƒx_Now hearing these things, they were cut to the heart: and they gnashed with their teeth at him.€€ƒxíBut he, being full of the Holy Ghost, looking up steadfastly to heaven, saw the glory of God and Jesus standing on the right hand of God. And he said: Behold, I see the heavens opened and the Son of man standing on the right hand of God.€€ƒxfAnd they, crying out with a loud voice, stopped their ears and with one accord ran violently upon him.€€ƒx”And casting him forth without the city. they stoned him. And the witnesses laid down their garments at the feet of a young man, whose name was Saul.€€ƒxLAnd they stoned Stephen, invoking and saying: Lord Jesus, receive my spirit.€€ƒxÁAnd falling on his knees, he cried with a loud voice, saying: Lord, lay not his sin to their charge: And when he had said this, he fell asleep in the Lord. And Saul was consenting to his death.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¿And at that time, there was raised a great persecution against the church which was at Jerusalem. And they were all dispersed through the countries of Judea, and Samaria, except the apostles.€€˜(ƒxBut Saul made havock of the church, entering in from house to house: and dragging away men and women, committed them to prison.€€ƒxHThey therefore that were dispersed went about preaching the word of God.€€ƒxIAnd Philip, going down to the city of Samaria, preached Christ unto them.€€ƒx‡And the people with one accord were attentive to those things which were said by Philip, hearing, and seeing the miracles which he did.€€ƒxMFor many of them who had unclean spirits, crying with a loud voice, went out.€€ƒx@And many, taken with the palsy, and that were lame, were healed.€€ƒxÇThere was therefore great joy in that city. Now there was a certain man named Simon who before had been a magician in that city, seducing the people of Samaria, giving out that he was some great one:€€ƒxwTo whom they all gave ear, from the least to the greatest, saying: This man is the power of God, which is called great.€€ƒxkAnd they were attentive to him, because, for a long time, he had bewitched them with his magical practices.€€ƒx‡But when they had believed Philip preaching of the kingdom of God, in the name of Jesus Christ, they were baptized, both men and women.€€ƒx©Then Simon himself believed also and, being baptized, he adhered to Philip. And being astonished, wondered to see the signs and exceeding great miracles which were done.€€ƒx‡Now, when the apostles, who were in Jerusalem, had heard that Samaria had received the word of God, they sent unto them Peter and John.€€ƒxQWho, when they were come, prayed for them that they might receive the Holy Ghost.€€ƒxgFor he was not as yet come upon any of them: but they were only baptized in the name of the Lord Jesus.€€ƒxGThen they laid their hands upon them: and they received the Holy Ghost.€€ƒxyAnd when Simon saw that, by the imposition of the hands of the apostles, the Holy Ghost was given, he offered them money,€€ƒxSaying: Give me also this power, that on whomsoever I shall lay my hands, he may receive the Holy Ghost. But Peter said to him:€€ƒx{Keep thy money to thyself, to perish with thee: because thou hast thought that the gift of God may be purchased with money.€€ƒxYThou hast no part nor lot in this matter. For thy heart is not right in the sight of God.€€ƒx{Do penance therefore for this thy wickedness: and pray to God, that perhaps this thought of thy heart may be forgiven thee.€€ƒxJFor I see thou art in the gall of bitterness and in the bonds of iniquity.€€ƒxyThen Simon answering, said: Pray you for me to the Lord that none of these things which you have spoken may come upon me.€€ƒx˜And they indeed, having testified and preached the word of the Lord, returned to Jerusalem: and preached the gospel to many countries of the Samaritans.€€ƒx“Now an angel of the Lord spoke to Philip, saying: Arise, go towards the south, to the way that goeth down from Jerusalem into Gaza: this is desert.€€ƒxÆAnd rising up, he went. And behold, a man of Ethiopia, an eunuch, of great authority under Candace the queen of the Ethiopians, who had charge over all her treasures, had come to Jerusalem to adore.€€ƒxLAnd he was returning, sitting in his chariot and reading Isaias the prophet.€€ƒxHAnd the Spirit said to Philip: Go near and join thyself to this chariot.€€ƒx‡And Philip running thither, heard him reading the prophet Isaias. And he said: Thinkest thou that thou understandest what thou readest?€€ƒxoWho said: And how can I, unless some man shew me? And he desired Philip that he would come up and sit with him.€€ƒx´And the place of the scripture which he was reading was this: He was led as a sheep to the slaughter: and like a lamb without voice before his shearer, so openeth he not his mouth.€€ƒxvIn humility his judgment was taken away. His generation who shall declare, for his life shall be taken from the earth?€€ƒx}And the eunuch answering Philip, said: I beseech thee, of whom doth the prophet speak this? Of himself, or of some other man?€€ƒxXThen Philip, opening his mouth and beginning at this scripture, preached unto him Jesus.€€ƒxAnd as they went on their way, they came to a certain water. And the eunuch said: See, here is water: What doth hinder me from being baptized?€€ƒxŠAnd Philip said: If thou believest with all thy heart, thou mayest. And he answering, said: I believe that Jesus Christ is the Son of God.€€ƒx€And he commanded the chariot to stand still. And they went down into the water, both Philip and the eunuch. And he baptized him.€€ƒx—And when they were come up out of the water, the Spirit of the Lord took away Philip: and the eunuch saw him no more. And he went on his way rejoicing.€€ƒxxBut Philip was found in Azotus: and passing through, he preached the gospel to all the cities, till he came to Caesarea.€€ƒ`€€ƒxtAnd Saul, as yet breathing out threatenings and slaughter against the disciples of the Lord, went to the high priest€€˜+ƒx†And as he went on his journey, it came to pass that he drew nigh to Damascus. And suddenly a light from heaven shined round about him.€€ƒx_And falling on the ground, he heard a voice saying to him: Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me?€€ƒxuWho said: Who art thou, Lord? And he: I am Jesus whom thou persecutest. It is hard for thee to kick against the goad.€€ƒxKAnd he, trembling and astonished, said: Lord, what wilt thou have me to do?€€ƒxÉAnd the Lord said to him: Arise and go into the city; and there it shall be told thee what thou must do. Now the men who went in company with him stood amazed, hearing indeed a voice but seeing no man.€€ƒx‹And Saul arose from the ground: and when his eyes were opened, he saw nothing. But they, leading him by the hands, brought him to Damascus.€€ƒxLAnd he was there three days without sight: and he did neither eat nor drink.€€ƒxNow there was a certain disciple at Damascus, named Ananias. And the Lord said to him in a vision: Ananias, And he said: Behold I am here, Lord.€€ƒxAnd the Lord said to him: Arise and go into the street that is called Strait and seek in the house of Judas, one named Saul of Tarsus. For behold he prayeth.€€ƒxk(And he saw a man named Ananias coming in and putting his hands upon him, that he might receive his sight.)€€ƒxtBut Ananias answered: Lord, I have heard by many of this man, how much evil he hath done to thy saints in Jerusalem.€€ƒxSAnd here he hath authority from the chief priests to bind all that invoke thy name.€€ƒxœAnd the Lord said to him: Go thy way: for this man is to me a vessel of election, to carry my name before the Gentiles and kings and the children of Israel.€€ƒxGFor I will shew him how great things he must suffer for my name's sake.€€ƒyAnd Ananias went his way and entered into the house. And laying his hands upon him, he said: Brother Saul, the Lord Jesus hath sent me, he that appeared to thee in the way as thou camest, that thou mayest receive thy sight and be filled with the Holy Ghost.€€ƒxvAnd immediately there fell from his eyes as it were scales: and he received his sight. And rising up, he was baptized.€€ƒxtAnd when he had taken meat, he was strengthened. And he was with the disciples that were at Damascus, for some days.€€ƒxOAnd immediately he preached Jesus in the synagogues, that he is the son of God.€€ƒxÒAnd all that heard him were astonished and said: Is not this he who persecuted in Jerusalem those that called upon this name and came hither for that intent, that he might carry them bound to the chief priests?€€ƒxzBut Saul increased much more in strength and confounded the Jews who dwelt at Damascus, affirming that this is the Christ.€€ƒxHAnd when many days were passed, the Jews consulted together to kill him.€€ƒxxBut their lying in wait was made known to Saul. And they watched the gates also day and night, that they might kill him.€€ƒxhBut the disciples, taking him in the night, conveyed him away by the wall, letting him down in a basket.€€ƒx˜And when he was come into Jerusalem, he essayed to join himself to the disciples: and they all were afraid of him, not believing that he was a disciple.€€ƒx¿But Barnabas took him and brought him to the apostles and told them how he had seen the Lord, and that he had spoken to him: and how in Damascus he had dealt confidently in the name of Jesus.€€ƒxkAnd he was with them, coming in and going out in Jerusalem and dealing confidently in the name of the Lord.€€ƒxXHe spoke also to the Gentiles and disputed with the Greeks. But they sought to kill him.€€ƒxaWhich when the brethren had known, they brought him down to Caesarea and sent him away to Tarsus.€€ƒx°Now, the church had peace throughout all Judea and Galilee and Samaria: and was edified, walking in the fear of the Lord: and was filled with the consolation of the Holy Ghost.€€ƒxjAnd it came to pass that Peter, as he passed through, visiting all, came to the saints who dwelt at Lydda.€€ƒxmAnd he found there a certain man named Eneas, who had kept his bed for eight years, who was ill of the palsy.€€ƒxsAnd Peter said to him: Eneas, the Lord Jesus Christ healeth thee. Arise and make thy bed. And immediately he arose.€€ƒxNAnd all that dwelt at Lydda and Saron saw him: who were converted to the Lord.€€ƒx¡And in Joppe there was a certain disciple named Tabitha, which by interpretation is called Dorcas. This woman was full of good works and almsdeeds which she did.€€ƒx{And it came to pass in those days that she was sick and died. Whom when they had washed, they laid her in an upper chamber.€€ƒx¨And forasmuch as Lydda was nigh to Joppe, the disciples, hearing that Peter was there, sent unto him two men, desiring him that he would not be slack to come unto them.€€ƒxÍAnd Peter rising up went with them. And when he was come, they brought him into the upper chamber. And all the widows stood about him, weeping and shewing him the coats and garments which Dorcas made them.€€ƒx And they all being put forth, Peter, kneeling down, prayed. And turning to the body, he said: Tabitha, arise. And she opened her eyes and, seeing Peter, sat up.€€ƒxtAnd giving her his hand, he lifted her up. And when he had called the saints and the widows, he presented her alive.€€ƒxJAnd it was made known throughout all Joppe. And many believed in the Lord.€€ƒxNAnd it cane to pass that he abode many days in Joppe, with one Simon a tanner.€€ƒ`€€ƒxoAnd there was a certain man in Caesarea, named Cornelius, a centurion of that which is called the Italian band:€€˜0ƒx†This man saw in a vision manifestly, about the ninth hour of the day, an angel of God coming in unto him and saying to him: Cornelius.€€ƒx¤And he, beholding him. being seized with fear, said: What is it, Lord? And he said to him: Thy prayers and thy alms are ascended for a memorial in the sight of God.€€ƒxLAnd now send men to Joppe: and call hither one Simon, who is surnamed Peter.€€ƒxhHe lodgeth with one Simon a tanner, whose house is by the sea side. He will tell thee what thou must do.€€ƒx™And when the angel who spoke to him was departed, he called two of his household servants and a soldier who feared the Lord, of them that were under him.€€ƒx7To whom when he had related all, he sent them to Joppe.€€ƒx¨And on the next day, whilst they were going on their journey and drawing nigh to the city, Peter went up to the higher parts of the house to pray, about the sixth hour.€€ƒxxAnd being hungry, he was desirous to taste somewhat. And as they were preparing, there came upon him an ecstasy of mind.€€ƒx“And he saw the heaven opened and a certain vessel descending, as it were a great linen sheet let down by the four corners from heaven to the earth:€€ƒxdWherein were all manner of four-footed beasts and creeping things of the earth and fowls of the air.€€ƒx:And there came a voice to him: Arise, Peter. Kill and eat.€€ƒx\But Peter said: Far be it from me. For I never did eat any thing that is common and unclean.€€ƒxhAnd the voice spoke to him again the second time: That which God hath cleansed, do not thou call common.€€ƒxLAnd this was done thrice. And presently the vessel was taken up into heaven.€€ƒx¸Now, whilst Peter was doubting within himself what the vision that he had seen should mean, behold the men who were sent from Cornelius, inquiring for Simon's house, stood at the gate.€€ƒxXAnd when they had called, they asked if Simon, who is surnamed Peter, were lodged there.€€ƒx\And as Peter was thinking of the vision, the Spirit said to him: Behold three men seek thee.€€ƒxYArise, therefore: get thee down and go with them, doubting nothing: for I have sent them.€€ƒxqThen Peter, going down to the men, said: Behold, I am he whom you seek. What is the cause for which you are come?€€ƒxáWho said: Cornelius, a centurion, a just man and one that feareth God, and having good testimony from all the nation of the Jews, received an answer of an holy angel, to send for thee into his house And to hear words of thee.€€ƒxThen bringing them in, he lodged them. And the day following, he arose and went with them: and some of the brethren from Joppe accompanied him.€€ƒx†And the morrow after, he entered into Caesarea. And Cornelius waited for them, having called together his kinsmen and special friends.€€ƒxkAnd it came to pass that when Peter was come in, Cornelius came to meet him and falling at his feet adored.€€ƒx?But Peter lifted him up, saying: Arise: I myself also am a man.€€ƒxHAnd talking with him, he went in and found many that were come together.€€ƒx¾And he said to them: you know how abominable it is for a man that is a Jew to keep company or to come unto one of another nation: but God hath shewed to me, to call no man common or unclean.€€ƒxtFor which cause, making no doubt, I came when I was sent for. I ask, therefore, for what cause you have sent for me?€€ƒx›And Cornelius said: Four days ago, unto this hour, I was praying in my house, at the ninth hour and behold a man stood before me in white apparel and said:€€ƒxWCornelius, thy prayer is heard and thy alms are had in remembrance in the sight of God.€€ƒx‚Send therefore to Joppe: and call hither Simon, who is surnamed Peter. He lodgeth in the house of Simon a tanner, by the sea side.€€ƒx¶Immediately therefore I sent to thee: and thou hast done well in coming. Now, therefore, all we are present in thy sight to hear all things whatsoever are commanded thee by the Lord.€€ƒxbAnd Peter opening his mouth, said: in very deed I perceive that God is not a respecter of persons.€€ƒxRBut in every nation, he that feareth him and worketh justice is acceptable to him.€€ƒxaGod sent the word to the children of Israel, preaching peace by Jesus Christ (He is Lord of all).€€ƒx€You know the word which hath been published through all Judea: for it began from Galilee, after the baptism which John preached.€€ƒx­Jesus of Nazareth: how God anointed him with the Holy Ghost and with power, who went about doing good and healing all that were oppressed by the devil, for God was with him.€€ƒxƒAnd we are witnesses of all things that he did in the land of the Jews and in Jerusalem: whom they killed, hanging him upon a tree.€€ƒxAHim God raised up the third day and gave him to be made manifest,€€ƒx‹Not to all the people, but to witnesses preordained by God, even to us, who did eat and drink with him, after he arose again from the dead.€€ƒxŒAnd he commanded us to preach to the people and to testify that it is he who was appointed by God to be judge of the living and of the dead.€€ƒxkTo him all the prophets give testimony, that by his name all receive remission of sins, who believe in him.€€ƒx^While Peter was yet speaking these words, the Holy Ghost fell on all them that heard the word.€€ƒx–And the faithful of the circumcision, who came with Peter, were astonished for that the grace of the Holy Ghost was poured out upon the Gentiles also.€€ƒx=For they heard them speaking with tongues and magnifying God.€€ƒx‚Then Peter answered: Can any man forbid water, that these should not be baptized, who have received the Holy Ghost, as well as we?€€ƒx~And he commanded them to be baptized in the name of the Lord Jesus Christ. Then they desired him to tarry with them some days.€€ƒ`€€ƒxlAnd the apostles and brethren, who were in Judea, heard that the Gentiles also had received the word of God.€€˜ƒxJSaying: Why didst thou go in to men uncircumcised and didst eat with them?€€ƒxABut Peter began and declared to them the matter in order, saying:€€ƒxÃI was in the city of Joppe praying: and I saw in an ecstasy of mind a vision, a certain vessel descending, as it were a great sheet let down from heaven by four corners. And it came even unto me.€€ƒxInto which looking, I considered and saw fourfooted creatures of the earth and beasts and creeping things and fowls of the air.€€ƒxBAnd I heard also a voice saying to me: Arise, Peter. Kill and eat.€€ƒxXAnd I said: Not so, Lord: for nothing common or unclean hath ever entered into my mouth.€€ƒx\And the voice answered again from heaven: What God hath made clean, do not thou call common.€€ƒxGAnd this was done three times. And all were taken up again into heaven.€€ƒxgAnd behold, immediately there were three men come to the house wherein I was, sent to me from Caesarea.€€ƒx–And the Spirit said to me that I should go with them, nothing doubting. And these six brethren went with me also: and we entered into the man's house.€€ƒxAnd he told us how he had seen an angel in his house, standing and saying to him: Send to Joppe and call hither Simon, who is surnamed Peter,€€ƒxMWho shall speak to thee words whereby thou shalt be saved, and all thy house.€€ƒx_And when I had begun to speak, the Holy Ghost fell upon them, as upon us also in the beginning.€€ƒx‡And I remembered the word of the Lord, how that he said: John indeed baptized with water but you shall be baptized with the Holy Ghost.€€ƒx~If then God gave them the same grace as to us also who believed in the Lord Jesus Christ: who was I, that could withstand God?€€ƒx‹Having heard these things, they held their peace and glorified God, saying: God then hath also to the Gentiles given repentance, unto life.€€ƒxºNow they who had been dispersed by the persecution that arose on occasion of Stephen went about as far as Phenice and Cyprus and Antioch, speaking the word to none, but to the Jews only.€€ƒxBut some of them were men of Cyprus and Cyrene, who, when they were entered into Antioch, spoke also to the Greeks, preaching the Lord Jesus.€€ƒx`And the hand of he Lord was with them: and a great number believing, were converted to the Lord.€€ƒx†And the tidings came to the ears of the church that was at Jerusalem, touching these things: and they sent Barnabas as far as Antioch.€€ƒx†Who, when he was come and had seen the grace of God, rejoiced. And he exhorted them all with purpose of heart to continue in the Lord.€€ƒxkFor he was a good man and full of the Holy Ghost and of faith. And a great multitude was added to the Lord.€€ƒxYAnd Barnabas went to Tarsus to seek Saul: whom, when he had found, he brought to Antioch.€€ƒx•And they conversed there in the church a whole year: and they taught a great multitude, so that at Antioch the disciples were first named Christians.€€ƒx@And in these days there came prophets from Jerusalem to Antioch.€€ƒxAnd one of them named Agabus, rising up, signified by the Spirit that there should be a great famine over the whole world, which came to pass under Claudius.€€ƒxrAnd the disciples, every man according to his ability, purposed to send relief to the brethren who dwelt in Judea.€€ƒxSWhich also they did, sending it to the ancients, by the hands of Barnabas and Saul.€€ƒ`€€ƒx^And at the same time, Herod the king stretched forth his hands, to afflict some of the church.€€˜ƒxnAnd seeing that it pleased the Jews, he proceeded to take up Peter also. Now it was in the days of the Azymes.€€ƒx­And when he had apprehended him, he cast him into prison, delivering him to four files of soldiers, to be kept, intending, after the pasch, to bring him forth to the people.€€ƒxgPeter therefore was kept in prison. But prayer was made without ceasing by the church unto God for him.€€ƒx­And when Herod would have brought him forth, the same night, Peter was sleeping between two soldiers, bound with two chains: and the keepers before the door kept the prison.€€ƒx¾And behold an angel of the Lord stood by him and a light shined in the room. And he, striking Peter on the side, raised him up, saying: Arise quickly. And the chains fell off from his hands.€€ƒxAnd the angel said to him: Gird thyself and put on thy sandals. And he did so. And he said to him: Cast thy garment about thee and follow me,€€ƒxzAnd going out, he followed him. And he knew not that it was true which was done by the angel: but thought he saw a vision.€€ƒxåAnd passing through the first and the second ward, they came to the iron gate that leadeth to the city which of itself opened to them. And going out, they passed on through one street. And immediately the angel departed from him.€€ƒxÃAnd Peter coming to himself, said: Now I know in very deed that the Lord hath sent his angel and hath delivered me out of the hand of Herod and from all the expectation of the people of the Jews.€€ƒx‡And considering, he came to the house of Mary the mother of John, who was surnamed Mark, where many were gathered together and praying.€€ƒx\And when he knocked at the door of the gate, a damsel came to hearken. whose name was Rhode.€€ƒxAnd as soon as she knew Peter's voice, she opened not the gate for joy: but running in she told that Peter stood before the gate.€€ƒxeBut they said to her: Thou art mad. But she affirmed that it was so. Then said they: It is his angel.€€ƒxYBut Peter continued knocking. And when they had opened, they saw him and were astonished.€€ƒxÕBut he, beckoning to them with his hand to hold their peace, told how the Lord had brought him out of prison. And he said: Tell these things to James and to the brethren. And going out, he went into another place.€€ƒx\Now when day was come, there was no small stir among the soldiers, what was become of Peter.€€ƒx²And when Herod had sought for him and found him not, having examined the keepers, he commanded they should be put to death. And going down from Judea to Caesarea, he abode there.€€ƒxØAnd he was angry with the Tyrians and the Sidonians. But they with one accord came to him: and, having gained Blastus who was the king's chamberlain, they desired peace, because their countries were nourished by him.€€ƒxvAnd upon a day appointed, Herod being arrayed in kingly apparel, sat in the judgment seat and made an oration to them.€€ƒxTAnd the people made acclamation, saying: It is the voice of a god, and not of a man.€€ƒxAnd forthwith an angel of the Lord struck him, because he had not given the honour to God: and, being eaten up by worms, he gave up the ghost.€€ƒx2But the word of the Lord increased and multiplied.€€ƒx}And Barnabas and Saul, returned from Jerusalem, having fulfilled their ministry, taking with them John who was surnamed Mark.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÜNow there were in the church which was at Antioch prophets and doctors, among whom was Barnabas and Simon who was called Niger, and Lucius of Cyrene and Manahen who was the foster brother of Herod the tetrarch, and Saul.€€˜4ƒxQThen they fasting and praying and imposing their hands upon them, sent them away.€€ƒx_So they, being sent by the Holy Ghost, went to Seleucia: and from thence they sailed to Cyprus.€€ƒx‰And when they were come to Salamina, they preached the word of God in the synagogues of the Jews. And they had John also in the ministry.€€ƒx™And when they had gone through the whole island, as far as Paphos, they found a certain man, a magician, a false prophet, a Jew, whose name was Bar-Jesu:€€ƒx}Who was with the proconsul Sergius Paulus, a prudent man. He, sending for Barnabas and Saul, desired to hear the word of God.€€ƒx{But Elymas the magician (for so his name is interpreted) withstood them, seeking to turn away the proconsul from the faith.€€ƒxHThen Saul, otherwise Paul, filled with the Holy Ghost, looking upon him,€€ƒxSaid: O full of all guile and of all deceit, child of the devil, enemy of all justice, thou ceases not to pervert the right ways of the Lord.€€ƒxàAnd now behold, the hand of the Lord is upon thee: and thou shalt be blind, not seeing the sun for a time. And immediately there fell a mist and darkness upon him: and going about, he sought some one to lead him by the hand.€€ƒxcThen the proconsul, when he had seen what was done, believed, admiring at the doctrine of the Lord.€€ƒx—Now when Paul and they that were with him had sailed from Paphos, they came to Perge in Pamphylia. And John departing from them, returned to Jerusalem.€€ƒx€But they, passing through Perge, came to Antioch in Pisidia: and, entering into the Synagogue on the sabbath day, they sat down.€€ƒx¸And after the reading of the law and the prophets, the rulers of the synagogue sent to them, saying: Ye men, brethren, if you have any word of exhortation to make to the people, speak.€€ƒxqThen Paul rising up and with his hand bespeaking silence, said: Ye men of Israel and you that fear God, give ear.€€ƒx«The God of the people of Israel chose our fathers and exalted the people when they were sojourners in the land of Egypt: And with an high arm brought them out from thence:€€ƒxEAnd for the space of forty years endured their manners in the desert:€€ƒx[And, destroying seven nations in the land of Chaanan, divided their land among them by lot.€€ƒx{As it were, after four hundred and fifty years. And after these things, he gave unto them judges, until Samuel the prophet.€€ƒxwAnd after that they desired a king: and God gave them Saul the son of Cis, a man of the tribe of Benjamin, forty years.€€ƒxÅAnd when he had removed him, he raised them up David to be king: to whom giving testimony, he said: I have found David, the son of Jesse, a man according to my own heart, who shall do all my wills.€€ƒx\Of this man's seed, God, according to his promise, hath raised up to Israel a Saviour Jesus:€€ƒx\John first preaching, before his coming, the baptism of penance to all the people of Israel.€€ƒx¯And when John was fulfilling his course, he said: I am not he whom you think me to be. But behold, there cometh one after me, whose shoes of his feet I am not worthy to loose.€€ƒx}Men, brethren, children of the stock of Abraham, and whosoever among you fear God: to you the word of this salvation is sent.€€ƒx©For they that inhabited Jerusalem and the rulers thereof, not knowing him, nor the voices of the prophets which are read every sabbath, judging him, have fulfilled them.€€ƒxVAnd finding no cause of death in him, they desired of Pilate that they might kill him.€€ƒx}And when they had fulfilled all things that were written of him, taking him down from the tree, they laid him in a sepulchre.€€ƒx2But God raised him up from the dead the third day.€€ƒx‡Who was seen for many days by them who came up with him from Galilee to Jerusalem, who to this present are his witnesses to the people.€€ƒxGAnd we declare unto you that the promise which was made to our fathers,€€ƒx—This same God hath fulfilled to our children, raising up Jesus, as in the second psalm also is written: Thou art my Son: this day have I begotten thee.€€ƒxœAnd to shew that he raised him up from the dead, not to return now any more to corruption, he said thus: I will give you the holy things of David, faithful.€€ƒxeAnd therefore, in another place also, he saith: Thou shalt not suffer thy holy one to see corruption.€€ƒx‡For David, when he had served in his generation, according to the will of God, slept: and was laid unto his fathers and saw corruption.€€ƒx<But he whom God hath raised from the dead saw no corruption.€€ƒx¸Be it known therefore to you, men, brethren, that through him forgiveness of sins is preached to you: and from all the things from which you could not be justified by the law of Moses.€€ƒx-In him every one that believeth is justified.€€ƒxKBeware, therefore, lest that come upon you which is spoken in the prophets:€€ƒxBehold, ye despisers, and wonder and perish: for I work a work in your days, a work which you will not believe, if any man shall tell it you.€€ƒxhAnd as they went out, they desired them that on the next sabbath they would speak unto them these words.€€ƒx½And when the synagogue was broken up, many of the Jews and of the strangers who served God followed Paul and Barnabas: who, speaking to them, persuaded them to continue in the grace of God.€€ƒxWBut the next sabbath day, the whole city almost came together, to hear the word of God.€€ƒx~And the Jews, seeing the multitudes, were filled with envy and contradicted those things which were said by Paul, blaspheming.€€ƒxÁThen Paul and Barnabas said boldly: To you it behoved us first to speak the word of God: but because you reject it and judge yourselves unworthy of eternal life, behold we turn to the Gentiles.€€ƒx˜For so the Lord hath commanded us: I have set thee to be the light of the Gentiles: that thou mayest be for salvation unto the utmost part of the earth.€€ƒx„And the Gentiles hearing it were glad and glorified the word of the Lord: and as many as were ordained to life everlasting believed.€€ƒxDAnd the word of the Lord was published throughout the whole country.€€ƒxªBut the Jews stirred up religious and honourable women and the chief men of the city: and raised persecution against Paul and Barnabas: and cast them out of their coasts.€€ƒxKBut they, shaking off the dust of their feet against them, came to Iconium.€€ƒx?And the disciples were filled with joy and with the Holy Ghost.€€ƒ`€€ƒx±And it came to pass in Iconium that they entered together into the synagogue of the Jews and so spoke that a very great multitude both of the Jews and of the Greeks did believe.€€˜ƒx«A long time therefore they abode there, dealing confidently in the Lord, who gave testimony to the word of his grace, granting signs and wonders to be done by their hands.€€ƒxrAnd the multitude of the city was divided. And some of them indeed held with the Jews, but some with the apostles.€€ƒx€And when there was an assault made by the Gentiles and the Jews with their rulers, to use them contumeliously and to stone them:€€ƒxThey, understanding it, fled to Lystra and Derbe, cities of Lycaonia, and to the whole country round about: and were there preaching the gospel.€€ƒxtAnd there sat a certain man at Lystra, impotent in his feet, a cripple from his mother's womb, who never had walked.€€ƒx^This same heard Paul speaking. Who looking upon him and seeing that he had faith to be healed,€€ƒxOSaid with a loud voice: Stand upright on thy feet. And he leaped up and walked.€€ƒx¥And when the multitudes had seen what Paul had done, they lifted up their voice in the Lycaonian tongue, saying: The gods are come down to us in the likeness of men.€€ƒxSAnd they called Barnabas, Jupiter: but Paul, Mercury: because he was chief speaker.€€ƒxThe priest also of Jupiter that was before the city, bringing oxen and garlands before the gate, would have offered sacrifice with the people.€€ƒxvWhich, when the apostles Barnabas and Paul had heard, rending their clothes, they leaped out among the people, crying,€€ƒxíAnd saying: Ye men, why do ye these things? We also are mortals, men like unto you, preaching to you to be converted from these vain things to the living God, who made the heaven and the earth and the sea and all things that are in them:€€ƒxBWho in times past, suffered all nations to walk in their own ways.€€ƒx™Nevertheless he left not himself without testimony, doing good from heaven, giving rains and fruitful Seasons, filling our hearts with food and gladness.€€ƒxVAnd speaking these things, they scarce restrained the people from sacrificing to them.€€ƒx Now there came thither certain Jews from Antioch and Iconium: and, persuading the multitude and stoning Paul, drew him out of the city, thinking him to be dead.€€ƒx†But as the disciples stood round about him, he rose up and entered into the city: and the next day he departed with Barnabas to Derbe.€€ƒx„And when they had preached the gospel to that city and had taught many, they returned again to Lystra and to Iconium and to Antioch:€€ƒxœConfirming the souls of the disciples and exhorting them to continue in the faith: and that through many tribulations we must enter into the kingdom of God.€€ƒxAnd when they had ordained to them priests in every church and had prayed with fasting, they commended them to the Lord, in whom they believed.€€ƒx6And passing through Pisidia, they came into Pamphylia.€€ƒxMAnd having spoken the word of the Lord in Perge, they went down into Attalia.€€ƒx‚And thence they sailed to Antioch, from whence they had been delivered to the grace of God, unto the work which they accomplished.€€ƒx¤And when they were come and had assembled the church, they related what great things God had done with them and how he had opened the door of faith to the Gentiles.€€ƒx0And they abode no small time with the disciples.€€ƒ`€€ƒx†And some, coming down from Judea, taught the brethren: That, except you be circumcised after the manner of Moses, you cannot be saved.€€ƒxÒAnd when Paul and Barnabas had no small contest with them, they determined that Paul and Barnabas and certain others of the other side should go up to the apostles and priests to Jerusalem, about this question.€€˜)ƒx›And when they were come to Jerusalem, they were received by the church and by the apostles and ancients, declaring how great things God had done with them.€€ƒxBut there arose of the sect of the Pharisees some that believed, saying: They must be circumcised and be commanded to observe the law of Moses.€€ƒxCAnd the apostles and ancients assembled to consider of this matter.€€ƒxÛAnd when there had been much disputing, Peter, rising up, said to them: Men, brethren, you know that in former days God made choice among us, that by my mouth the Gentiles should hear the word of the gospel and believe.€€ƒxcAnd God, who knoweth the hearts, gave testimony, giving unto them the Holy Ghost, as well as to us:€€ƒxKAnd put no difference between us and them, purifying their hearts by faith.€€ƒx‡Now therefore, why tempt you God to put a yoke upon the necks of the disciples which neither our fathers nor we have been able to bear?€€ƒx_But by the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ, we believe to be saved, in like manner as they also.€€ƒx™And all the multitude held their peace: and they heard Barnabas and Paul telling what great signs and wonders God had wrought among the Gentiles by them.€€ƒxTAnd after they had held their peace, James answered, saying: Men, brethren, hear me.€€ƒxWSimon hath related how God first visited to take to the Gentiles, a people to his name.€€ƒx>And to this agree the words of the prophets, as it is written:€€ƒxœAfter these things I will return and will rebuild the tabernacle of David, which is fallen down: and the ruins thereof I will rebuild. And I will set it up:€€ƒx…That the residue of men may seek after the Lord, and all nations upon whom my name is invoked, saith the Lord, who doth these things.€€ƒxCTo the Lord was his own work known from the beginning of the world.€€ƒxkFor which cause, judge that they who from among the Gentiles are converted to God are not to be disquieted:€€ƒx•But that we write unto them, that they refrain themselves from the pollutions of idols and from fornication and from things strangled and from blood.€€ƒxpFor Moses of old time hath in every city them that preach him in the synagogues, where he is read every sabbath.€€ƒxæThen it pleased the apostles and ancients, with the whole church, to choose men of their own company and to send to Antioch with Paul and Barnabas, namely, Judas, who was surnamed Barsabas, and Silas, chief men among the brethren.€€ƒx”Writing by their hands: The apostles and ancients, brethren, to the brethren of the Gentiles that are at Antioch and in Syria and Cilicia, greeting.€€ƒx‹Forasmuch as we have heard that some going out from us have troubled you with words, subverting your souls, to whom we gave no commandment:€€ƒxŠIt hath seemed good to us, being assembled together, to choose out men and to send them unto you, with our well beloved Barnabas and Paul:€€ƒxFMen that have given their lives for the name of our Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒxmWe have sent therefore Judas and Silas, who themselves also will, by word of mouth, tell you the same things.€€ƒxrFor it hath seemed good to the Holy Ghost and to us to lay no further burden upon you than these necessary things:€€ƒx¶That you abstain from things sacrificed to idols and from blood and from things strangled and from fornication: from which things keeping yourselves, you shall do well. Fare ye well.€€ƒxsThey therefore, being dismissed, went down to Antioch and, gathering together the multitude, delivered the epistle.€€ƒx<Which when they had read, they rejoiced for the consolation.€€ƒxoBut Judas and Silas, being prophets also themselves, with many words comforted the brethren and confirmed them.€€ƒxsAnd after they had spent some time there, they were let go with peace by the brethren unto them that had sent them.€€ƒxUBut it seemed good unto Silas to remain there: and Judas alone departed to Jerusalem.€€ƒxkAnd Paul and Barnabas continued at Antioch, teaching and preaching, with many others, the word of the Lord.€€ƒx¥And after some days, Paul said to Barnabas: Let us return and visit our brethren in all the cities wherein we have preached the word of the Lord, to see how they do.€€ƒxJAnd Barnabas would have taken with them John also, that was surnamed Mark.€€ƒx‚But Paul desired that he (as having departed from them out of Pamphylia and not gone with them to the work) might not be received.€€ƒxxAnd there arose a dissension so that they departed one from another. And Barnabas indeed, taking Mark, sailed to Cyprus.€€ƒxXBut Paul, choosing Silas, departed, being delivered by the brethren to the grace of God.€€ƒx†And he went through Syria and Cilicia, confirming the churches, commanding them to keep the precepts of the apostles and the ancients.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¥And he came to Derbe and Lystra. And behold, there was a certain disciple there named Timothy, the son of a Jewish woman that believed: but his father was a Gentile.€€ƒxOTo this man the brethren that were in Lystra and Iconium gave a good testimony.€€˜(ƒxAnd as they passed through the cities, they delivered unto them the decrees for to keep, that were decreed by the apostles and ancients who were at Jerusalem.€€ƒxGAnd the churches were confirmed in faith and increased in number daily.€€ƒx†And when they had passed through Phrygia and the country of Galatia, they were forbidden by the Holy Ghost to preach the word in Asia.€€ƒxrAnd when they were come into Mysia, they attempted to go into Bithynia: and the Spirit of Jesus suffered them not.€€ƒx@And when they had passed through Mysia, they went down to Troas.€€ƒx˜And a vision was shewed to Paul in the night, which was a man of Macedonia standing and beseeching him and saying: Pass over into Macedonia and help us.€€ƒx•And as soon as he had seen the vision, immediately we sought to go into Macedonia: being assured that God had called us to preach the gospel to them.€€ƒxiAnd sailing from Troas, we came with a straight course to Samothracia, and the day following to Neapolis.€€ƒxŒAnd from thence to Philippi, which is the chief city of part of Macedonia, a colony. And we were in this city some days conferring together.€€ƒx­And upon the Sabbath day, we went forth without the gate by a river side, where it seemed that there was prayer: and sitting down, we spoke to the women that were assembled.€€ƒx¿And a certain woman named Lydia, a seller of purple, of the city of Thyatira, one that worshipped God, did hear: whose heart the Lord opened to attend to those things which were said by Paul.€€ƒx´And when she was baptized, and her household, she besought us, saying: If you have judged me to be faithful to the Lord, come into my house and abide there. And she constrained us.€€ƒxAnd it came to pass, as we went to prayer, a certain girl having a pythonical spirit met us, who brought to her masters much gain by divining.€€ƒxThis same following Paul and us, cried out, saying: These men are the servants of the Most High God, who preach unto you the way of salvation.€€ƒx®And this she did many days. But Paul being grieved, turned and said to the spirit: I command thee, in the name of Jesus Christ, to go from her. And he went out the same hour.€€ƒxŒBut her masters, seeing that the hope of their gain was gone, apprehending Paul and Silas, brought them into the market place to the rulers.€€ƒxZAnd presenting them to the magistrates, they said: These men disturb our city, being Jews:€€ƒxXAnd preach a fashion which it is not lawful for us to receive nor observe, being Romans.€€ƒx€And the people ran together against them: and the magistrates, rending off their clothes, commanded them to be beaten with rods.€€ƒxwAnd when they had laid many stripes upon them, they cast them into prison, charging the gaoler to keep them diligently.€€ƒxlWho having received such a charge, thrust them into the inner prison and made their feet fast in the stocks.€€ƒx_And at midnight, Paul and Silas, praying, praised God. And they that were in prison heard them.€€ƒx©And suddenly there was a great earthquake, so that the foundations of the prison were shaken. And immediately all the doors were opened and the bands of all were loosed.€€ƒx»And the keeper of the prison, awakening out of his sleep and seeing the doors of the prison open, drawing his sword, would have killed himself, supposing that the prisoners had been fled.€€ƒxRBut Paul cried with a loud voice, saying: Do thyself no harm, for we all are here.€€ƒx]Then calling for a light, he went in: and trembling, fell down at the feet of Paul and Silas.€€ƒxMAnd bringing them out, he said: Masters, what must I do, that I may be saved?€€ƒxQBut they said: believe in the Lord Jesus: and thou shalt be saved, and thy house.€€ƒxPAnd they preached the word of the Lord to him and to all that were in his house.€€ƒx~And he, taking them the same hour of the night, washed their stripes: and himself was baptized, and all his house immediately.€€ƒx|And when he had brought them into his own house, he laid the table for them: and rejoiced with all his house, believing God.€€ƒxXAnd when the day was come, the magistrates sent the serjeants, saying: Let those men go.€€ƒx†And the keeper of the prison told these words to Paul: The magistrates have sent to let you go. Now therefore depart. And go in peace.€€ƒx¸But Paul said to them: They have beaten us publicly, uncondemned, men that are Romans, and have cast us into prison. And now do they thrust us out privately? Not so: but let them come.€€ƒx†And let us out themselves. And the serjeants told these words to the magistrates. And they were afraid: hearing that they were Romans.€€ƒxcAnd coming, they besought them: and bringing them out, they desired them to depart out of the city.€€ƒx„And they went out of the prison and entered into the house of Lydia: and having seen the brethren, they comforted them and departed.€€ƒ`€€ƒx~And when they had passed through Amphipolis and Apollonia, they came to Thessalonica, where there was a synagogue of the Jews.€€ƒx}And Paul, according to his custom, went in unto them. And for three sabbath days he reasoned with them out of the scriptures:€€˜"ƒx¢And some of them believed and were associated to Paul and Silas: and of those that served God and of the Gentiles a great multitude: and of noble women not a few.€€ƒxÌBut the Jews, moved with envy and taking unto them some wicked men of the vulgar sort and making a tumult, set the city in an uproar: and besetting Jason's house, sought to bring them out unto the people.€€ƒx—And not finding them, they drew Jason and certain brethren to the rulers of the city, crying: They that set the city in an uproar are come hither also:€€ƒxwWhom Jason hath received. And these all do contrary to the decrees of Caesar, saying that there is another king, Jesus.€€ƒxQAnd they stirred up the people: and the rulers of the city, hearing these things,€€ƒxIAnd having taken satisfaction of Jason and of the rest, they let them go.€€ƒx‘But the brethren immediately sent away Paul and Silas by night unto Berea. Who, when they were come thither, went into the synagogue of the Jews.€€ƒxNow these were more noble than those in Thessalonica, who received the word with all eagerness, daily searching the scriptures, whether these things were so.€€ƒxcAnd many indeed of them believed: and of honourable women that were Gentiles and of men, not a few.€€ƒx©And when the Jews of Thessalonica had knowledge that the word of God was also preached by Paul at Berea, they came thither also, stirring up and troubling the multitude.€€ƒxkAnd then immediately the brethren sent away Paul, to go unto the sea: but Silas and Timothy remained there.€€ƒx±And they that conducted Paul brought him as far as Athens: and receiving a commandment from him to Silas and Timothy, that they should come to him with all speed, they departed.€€ƒxwNow whilst Paul waited for them at Athens, his spirit was stirred within him, seeing the city wholly given to idolatry.€€ƒx”He disputed, therefore, in the synagogue with the Jews and with them that served God: and in the market place, every day, with them that were there.€€ƒxúAnd certain philosophers of the Epicureans and of the Stoics disputed with him. And some said: What is it that this word sower would say? But others: He seemeth to be a setter forth of new gods. Because he preached to them Jesus and the resurrection.€€ƒxyAnd taking him, they brought him to the Areopagus, saying: May we know what this new doctrine is, which thou speakest of?€€ƒxdFor thou bringest in certain new things to our ears. We would know therefore what these things mean.€€ƒx(Now all the Athenians and strangers that were there employed themselves in nothing else, but either in telling or in hearing some new thing.)€€ƒx‚But Paul, standing in the midst of the Areopagus, said: Ye men of Athens, I perceive that in all things you are too superstitious.€€ƒx«For passing by and seeing your idols, I found an altar also, on which was written: To the Unknown God. What therefore you worship without knowing it, that I preach to you:€€ƒx{God, who made the world and all things therein, he being Lord of heaven and earth, dwelleth not in temples made with hands.€€ƒx‡Neither is he served with men's hands, as though he needed any thing: seeing it is he who giveth to all life and breath and all things:€€ƒxAnd hath made of one, all mankind, to dwell upon the whole face of the earth, determining appointed times and the limits of their habitation.€€ƒxuThat they should seek God, if haply they may feel after him or find him, although he be not far from every one of us.€€ƒxhFor in him we live and move and are: as some also of your own poets said: For we are also his offspring.€€ƒx•Being therefore the offspring of God, we must not suppose the divinity to be like unto gold or silver or stone, the graving of art and device of man.€€ƒx{And God indeed having winked at the times of this ignorance, now declareth unto men that all should every where do penance.€€ƒx£Because he hath appointed a day wherein he will judge the world in equity, by the man whom he hath appointed: giving faith to all, by raising him up from the dead.€€ƒxAnd when they had heard of the resurrection of the dead, some indeed mocked. But others said: We will hear thee again concerning this matter.€€ƒx!So Paul went out from among them.€€ƒx‹But certain men, adhering to him, did believe: among whom was also Dionysius the Areopagite and a woman named Damaris and others with them.€€ƒ`€€ƒx>After these things, departing from Athens, he came to Corinth.€€ƒx½And finding a certain Jew, named Aquila, born in Pontus, lately come from Italy, with Priscilla his wife (because that Claudius had commanded all Jews to depart from Rome), he came to them.€€˜ƒxAnd he reasoned in the synagogue every sabbath, bringing in the name of the Lord Jesus. And he persuaded the Jews and the Greeks.€€ƒx„And when Silas and Timothy were come from Macedonia, Paul was earnest in preaching, testifying to the Jews that Jesus is the Christ.€€ƒx¨But they gainsaying and blaspheming, he shook his garments and said to them: Your blood be upon your own heads: I am clean. From henceforth I will go unto the Gentiles.€€ƒxšAnd departing thence, he entered into the house of a certain man, named Titus Justus, one that worshipped God, whose house was adjoining to the synagogue.€€ƒx“And Crispus, the ruler of the synagogue, believed in the Lord, with all his house. And many of the Corinthians hearing, believed and were baptized.€€ƒxdAnd the Lord said to Paul in the night, by a vision: Do not fear, but speak. And hold not thy peace,€€ƒxiBecause I am with thee and no man shall set upon thee, to hurt thee. For I have much people in this city.€€ƒxOAnd he stayed there a year and six months, teaching among them the word of God.€€ƒx|But when Gallio was proconsul of Achaia, the Jews with one accord rose up against Paul and brought him to the judgment seat,€€ƒxCSaying: This man persuadeth men to worship God contrary to the law.€€ƒx«And when Paul was beginning to open his mouth, Gallio said to the Jews: If it were some matter of injustice or an heinous deed, O Jews, I should with reason bear with you.€€ƒxoBut if they be questions of word and names and of your law, look you to it. I will not be judge of such things.€€ƒx)And he drove them from the judgment seat.€€ƒx‹And all laying hold on Sosthenes, the ruler of the synagogue, beat him before the judgment seat. And Gallio cared for none of those things.€€ƒxÀBut Paul, when he had stayed yet many days, taking his leave of the brethren, sailed thence into Syria (and with him Priscilla and Aquila), having shorn his head in Cenchrae. For he had a vow.€€ƒxpAnd he came to Ephesus and left them there. But he himself, entering into the synagogue, disputed with the Jews.€€ƒxNAnd when they desired him that he would tarry a longer time, he consented not:€€ƒxcBut taking his leave and saying: I will return to you again, God willing, he departed from Ephesus.€€ƒxhAnd going down to Caesarea, he went up to Jerusalem and saluted the church: and so came down to Antioch.€€ƒxAnd after he had spent some time there, he departed and went through the country of Galatia and Phrygia, in order, confirming all the disciples.€€ƒxtNow a certain Jew, named Apollo, born at Alexandria, an eloquent man, came to Ephesus, one mighty in the scriptures.€€ƒx¨This man was instructed in the way of the Lord: and being fervent in spirit, spoke and taught diligently the things that are of Jesus, knowing only the baptism of John.€€ƒx´This man therefore began to speak boldly in the synagogue. Whom when Priscilla and Aquila had heard, they took him to them and expounded to him the way of the Lord more diligently.€€ƒx¤And whereas he was desirous to go to Achaia, the brethren exhorting wrote to the disciples to receive him. Who, when he was come, helped them much who had believed.€€ƒxfFor with much vigour he convinced the Jews openly, shewing by the scriptures that Jesus is the Christ.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‘And it came to pass, while Apollo was at Corinth, that Paul, having passed through the upper coasts, came to Ephesus and found certain disciples.€€ƒxšAnd he said to them: Have you received the Holy Ghost since ye believed? But they said to him: We have not so much as heard whether there be a Holy Ghost.€€˜)ƒx¢Then Paul said: John baptized the people with the baptism of penance saying: That they should believe in him, who was to come after him, that is to say, in Jesus.€€ƒxLHaving heard these things, they were baptized in the name of the Lord Jesus.€€ƒxwAnd when Paul had imposed his hands on them, the Holy Ghost came upon them: and they spoke with tongues and prophesied.€€ƒx"And all the men were about twelve.€€ƒx†And entering into the synagogue, he spoke boldly for the space of three months, disputing and exhorting concerning the kingdom of God.€€ƒxÈBut when some were hardened and believed not, speaking evil of the way of the Lord before the multitude, departing from them, he separated the disciples, disputing daily in the school of one Tyrannus.€€ƒx€And this continued for the space of two years, so that all who dwelt in Asia heard the word of the Lord, both Jews and Gentiles.€€ƒx>And God wrought by the hand of Paul more than common miracles.€€ƒx¢So that even there were brought from his body to the sick, handkerchiefs and aprons: and the diseases departed from them: and the wicked spirits went out of them.€€ƒx»Now some also of the Jewish exorcists, who went about, attempted to invoke over them that had evil spirits the name of the Lord Jesus, saying: I conjure you by Jesus, whom Paul preacheth.€€ƒxVAnd there were certain men, seven sons of Sceva, a Jew, a chief priest, that did this.€€ƒx_But the wicked spirit, answering, said to them: Jesus I know: and Paul I know. But who are you?€€ƒx¤And the man in whom the wicked spirit was, leaping upon them and mastering them both, prevailed against them, so that they fled out of that house naked and wounded.€€ƒx•And this became known to all the Jews and the Gentiles that dwelt a Ephesus. And fear fell on them all: and the name of the Lord Jesus was magnified.€€ƒxIAnd many of them that believed came, confessing and declaring their deeds€€ƒxÃAnd many of them who had followed curious arts brought together their books and burnt them before all. And, counting the price of them, they found the money to be fifty thousand pieces of silver.€€ƒx3So mightily grew the word of God and was confirmed.€€ƒxºAnd when these things were ended, Paul purposed in the spirit, when he had passed through Macedonia and Achaia, to go to Jerusalem, saying: After I have been there, I must see Rome also.€€ƒx{And sending into Macedonia two of them that ministered to him, Timothy and Erastus, he himself remained for a time in Asia.€€ƒxLNow at that time there arose no small disturbance about the way of the Lord.€€ƒx|For a certain man named Demetrius, a silversmith, who made silver temples for Diana, brought no small gain to the craftsmen.€€ƒxrWhom he calling together with the workmen of like occupation, said: Sirs, you know that our gain is by this trade.€€ƒxµAnd you see and hear that this Paul, by persuasion hath drawn away a great multitude, not only of Ephesus, but almost of all Asia, saying: they are not gods which are made by hands.€€ƒxİSo that not only this our craft is in danger to be set at nought, but also the temple of great Diana shall be reputed for nothing! Yea, and her majesty shall begin to be destroyed, whom all Asia and the world worshippeth.€€ƒxjHaving heard these things, they were full of anger and cried out, saying: Great is Diana of the Ephesians!€€ƒx©And the whole city was filled with confusion. And having caught Gaius and Aristarchus, men of Macedonia, Paul's companions, they rushed with one accord into the theatre.€€ƒxTAnd when Paul would have entered in unto the people, the disciples suffered him not.€€ƒx†And some also of the rulers of Asia, who were his friends, sent unto him, desiring that he would not venture himself into the theatre.€€ƒxŒNow some cried one thing, some another. For the assembly was confused: and the greater part knew not for what cause they were come together.€€ƒx±And they drew forth Alexander out of the multitude, the Jews thrusting him forward. And Alexander, beckoning with his hand for silence, would have given the people satisfaction,€€ƒx‘But as soon as they perceived him to be a Jew, all with one voice, for the space of about two, hours, cried out: Great is Diana of the Ephesians!€€ƒxÑAnd when the town clerk had appeased the multitudes, he said: Ye men of Ephesus, what man is there that knoweth not that the city of the Ephesians is a worshipper of the great Diana and of Jupiter's offspring?€€ƒxmFor as much therefore as these things cannot be contradicted, you ought to be quiet and to do nothing rashly.€€ƒxqFor you have brought hither these men, who are neither guilty of sacrilege nor of blasphemy against your goddess.€€ƒxªBut if Demetrius and the craftsmen that are with him have a matter against any man, the courts of justice are open: and there are proconsuls. Let them accuse one another.€€ƒxRAnd if you inquire after any other matter, it may be decided in a lawful assembly.€€ƒxÒFor we are even in danger to be called in question for this day's uproar, there being no man guilty (of whom we may give account) of this concourse. And when he had said these things, he dismissed the assembly.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‹And after the tumult was ceased, Paul calling to him the disciples and exhorting them, took his leave and set forward to go into Macedonia.€€ƒxaAnd when he had gone over those parts and had exhorted them with many words, he came into Greece:€€ƒx›Where, when he had spent three months, the Jews laid wait for him, as he was about to sail into Syria. So he took a resolution to return through Macedonia.€€˜&ƒx,These, going before, stayed for us at Troas.€€ƒx{But we sailed from Philippi after the days of the azymes and came to them to Troas in five days, where we abode seven days.€€ƒx®And on the first day of the week, when we were assembled to break bread, Paul discoursed with them, being to depart on the morrow. And he continued his speech until midnight.€€ƒxTAnd there were a great number of lamps in the upper chamber where we were assembled.€€ƒxÌAnd a certain young man named Eutychus, sitting on the window, being oppressed with a deep sleep (as Paul was long preaching), by occasion of his sleep fell from the third loft down and was taken up dead.€€ƒx}To whom, when Paul had gone down, he laid himself upon him and, embracing him, said: Be not troubled, for his soul is in him.€€ƒxsThen going up and breaking bread and tasting and having talked a long time to them, until daylight, so he departed.€€ƒxAAnd they brought the youth alive and were not a little comforted.€€ƒx‰But we going aboard the ship, sailed to Assos, being there to take in Paul. For so he had appointed, himself purposing to travel by land.€€ƒxJAnd when he had met with us at Assos, we took him in and came to Mitylene.€€ƒx‘And sailing thence, the day following we came over against Chios: and the next day we arrived at Samos: and the day following we came to Miletus.€€ƒx­For Paul had determined to sail by Ephesus, lest he should be stayed any time in Asia. For he hasted. if it were possible for him, to keep the day of Pentecost at Jerusalem.€€ƒxJAnd sending from Miletus to Ephesus, he called the ancients of the church.€€ƒx¬And when they were come to him and were together, he said to them: You know from the first day that I came into Asia, in what manner I have been with you, for all the time.€€ƒxrServing the Lord with all humility and with tears and temptations which befell me by the conspiracies of the Jews:€€ƒx‡How I have kept back nothing that was profitable to you, but have preached it to you, and taught you publicly, and from house to house,€€ƒx\Testifying both to Jews and Gentiles penance towards God and faith in our Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒxrAnd now, behold, being bound in the spirit, I go to Jerusalem: not knowing the things which shall befall me there:€€ƒxuSave that the Holy Ghost in every city witnesseth to me, saying: That bands and afflictions wait for me at Jerusalem.€€ƒxçBut I fear none of these things, neither do I count my life more precious than myself, so that I may consummate my course and the ministry of the word which I received from the Lord Jesus, to testify the gospel of the grace of God.€€ƒxtAnd now behold, I know that all you, among whom I have gone preaching the kingdom of God, shall see my face no more.€€ƒxSWherefore I take you to witness this day that I am clear from the blood of all men.€€ƒxBFor I have not spread to declare unto you all, the counsel of God.€€ƒx¥Take heed to yourselves and to the whole flock, wherein the Holy Ghost hath placed you bishops, to rule the Church of God which he hath purchased with his own blood.€€ƒx^I know that after my departure ravening wolves will enter in among you, not sparing the flock.€€ƒxcAnd of your own selves shall arise men speaking perverse things, to draw away disciples after them.€€ƒx|Therefore watch, keeping in memory that for three years I ceased not with tears to admonish every one of you, night and day.€€ƒx‡And now I commend you to God and to the word of his grace, who is able to build up and to give an inheritance among all the sanctified.€€ƒx8I have not coveted any man's silver, gold or apparel, as€€ƒxrYou yourselves know. For such things as were needful for me and them that are with me, these hands have furnished.€€ƒxÆI have shewed you all things, how that so labouring you ought to support the weak and to remember the word of the Lord Jesus, how he said: It is a more blessed thing to give, rather than to receive.€€ƒxJAnd when he had said these things, kneeling down, he prayed with them all.€€ƒx\And there was much weeping among them all. And falling on the neck of Paul, they kissed him,€€ƒxBeing grieved most of all for the word which he had said, that they should see his face no more. And they brought him on his way to the ship.€€ƒ`€€ƒx§And when it came to pass that, being parted from them, we set sail, we came with a straight course to Coos, and the day following to Rhodes: and from thence to Patara.€€ƒxSAnd when we had found a ship sailing over to Phenice, we went aboard and set forth.€€ƒx”And when we had discovered Cyprus, leaving it on the left hand, we sailed into Syria, and came to Tyre: for there the ship was to unlade her burden.€€˜(ƒxÅAnd the days being expired, departing we went forward, they all bringing us on our way, with their wives and children, till we were out of the city. And we kneeled down on the shore: and we prayed.€€ƒxOAnd when we had bid one another farewell, we took ship. And they returned home.€€ƒxƒBut we, having finished the voyage by sea, from Tyre came down to Ptolemais: and saluting the brethren, we abode one day with them.€€ƒx“And the next day departing, we came to Caesarea. And entering into the house of Philip the evangelist, who was one of the seven, we abode with him.€€ƒx5And he had four daughters, virgins, who did prophesy.€€ƒx]And as we tarried there for some days, there came from Judea a certain prophet, named Agabus.€€ƒxüWho, when he was come to us, took Paul's girdle: and binding his own feet and hands, he said: Thus saith the Holy Ghost: The man whose girdle this is, the Jews shall bind in this manner in Jerusalem and shall deliver him into the hands of the Gentiles.€€ƒxsWhich when we had heard, both we and they that were of that place desired him that he would not go up to Jerusalem.€€ƒx²Then Paul answered and said: What do you mean, weeping and afflicting my heart? For I am ready not only to be bound, but to die also in Jerusalem, for the name of the Lord Jesus.€€ƒxTAnd when we could not persuade him, we ceased, saying: The will of the Lord be done.€€ƒx>And after those days, being prepared, we went up to Jerusalem.€€ƒx•And there went also with us some of the disciples from Caesarea, bringing with them one Mnason a Cyprian, an old disciple, with whom we should lodge.€€ƒxDAnd when we were come to Jerusalem, the brethren received us gladly.€€ƒx\And the day following, Paul went in with us unto James: and all the ancients were assembled.€€ƒxqWhom when he had saluted, he related particularly what things God had wrought among the Gentiles by his ministry.€€ƒx®But they hearing it, glorified God and said to him: Thou seest, brother, how many thousands there are among the Jews that have believed: and they are all zealous for the law.€€ƒxÆNow they have heard of thee that thou teachest those Jews, who are among the Gentiles to depart from Moses: saying that they ought not to circumcise their children, nor walk according to the custom.€€ƒxdWhat is it therefore? The multitude must needs come together: for they will hear that thou art come.€€ƒxPDo therefore this that we say to thee. We have four men, who have a vow on them.€€ƒxßTake these and sanctify thyself with them: and bestow on them, that they may shave their heads. And all will know that the things which they have heard of these are false: but that thou thyself also walkest keeping the law.€€ƒxØBut, as touching the Gentiles that believe, we have written, decreeing that they should only refrain themselves from that which has been offered to idols and from blood and from things strangled and from fornication.€€ƒx×Then Paul took the men and, the next day being purified with them, entered into the temple, giving notice of the accomplishment of the days of purification, until an oblation should be offered for every one of them.€€ƒx­But when the seven days were drawing to an end, those Jews that were of Asia, when they saw him in the temple, stirred up all the people and laid hands upon him, crying out:€€ƒxÎMen of Israel, help: This is the man that teacheth all men every where against the people and the law and this place; and moreover hath brought in Gentiles into the temple and hath violated this holy place.€€ƒxz(For they had seen Trophimus the Ephesian in the city with him. whom they supposed that Paul had brought into the temple.)€€ƒx—And he whole city was in an uproar: and the people ran together. And taking Paul, they drew him out of the temple: and immediately the doors were shut.€€ƒxlAnd as they went about to kill him, it was told the tribune of the band that all Jerusalem was in confusion.€€ƒx•Who, forthwith taking with him soldiers and centurions, ran down to them. And when they saw the tribune and the soldiers, they left off beating Paul.€€ƒx„Then the tribune, coming near, took him and commanded him to be bound with two chains: and demanded who he was and what he had done.€€ƒx¥And some cried one thing, some another, among the multitude. And when he could not know the certainty for the tumult, he commanded him to be carried into the castle.€€ƒx{And when he was come to the stairs, it fell out that he was carried by the soldiers, because of the violence of the people.€€ƒxFFor the multitude of the people followed after, crying: Away with him!€€ƒxAnd as Paul was about to be brought into the castle, he saith to the tribune: May I speak something to thee? Who said: Canst thou speak Greek?€€ƒx‘Art not thou that Egyptian who before these days didst raise a tumult and didst lead forth into the desert four thousand men that were murderers?€€ƒx‡But Paul said to him: I am a Jew of Tarsus in Cilicia, a citizen of no mean city. And I beseech thee, suffer me to speak to the people.€€ƒx´And when he had given him leave, Paul standing on the stairs, beckoned with his hand to the people. And a great silence being made, he spoke unto them in the Hebrew tongue, saying:€€ƒ`€€ƒxIMen, brethren and fathers, hear ye the account which I now give unto you.€€ƒx](And when they heard that he spoke to them in the Hebrew tongue, they kept the more silence.)€€ƒxÖAnd he saith: I am a Jew, born at Tarsus in Cilicia, but brought up in this city, at the feet of Gamaliel, taught according to the truth of the law of the fathers, zealous for the law, as also all you are this day:€€˜ƒxÊAs the high priest doth bear me witness and all the ancients. From whom also receiving letters to the brethren, I went to Damascus, that I might bring them bound from thence to Jerusalem to be punished.€€ƒx‘And it came to pass, as I was going and drawing nigh to Damascus, at mid-day, that suddenly from heaven there shone round about me a great light:€€ƒx]And falling on the ground, I heard a voice saying to me: Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me?€€ƒxeAnd I answered: Who art thou, Lord? And he said to me: I am Jesus of Nazareth, whom thou persecutest.€€ƒxhAnd they that were with me saw indeed the light: but they heard not the voice of him that spoke with me.€€ƒx–And I said: What shall I do, Lord? And the Lord said to me: Arise and go to Damascus; and there it shall be told thee of all things that thou must do.€€ƒxwAnd whereas I did not see for the brightness of that light, being led by the hand by my companions, I came to Damascus,€€ƒx^And one Ananias, a man according to the law, having testimony of all the Jews who dwelt there,€€ƒxjComing to me and standing by me, said to me: Brother Saul, look up. And I, the same hour, looked upon him.€€ƒx›But he said: The God of our fathers hath preordained thee that thou shouldst know his will and see the Just One and shouldst hear the voice from his mouth.€€ƒxXFor thou shalt be his witness to all men of those things which thou hast seen and heard.€€ƒx]And now why tarriest thou? Rise up and be baptized and wash away thy sins, invoking his name.€€ƒxnAnd it came to pass, when I was come again to Jerusalem and was praying in the temple, that I was in a trance,€€ƒxˆAnd saw him saying unto me: Make haste and get thee quickly out of Jerusalem: because they will not receive thy testimony concerning me.€€ƒxkAnd I said: Lord, they know that I cast into prison and beat in every synagogue them that believed in thee.€€ƒx|And when the blood of Stephen thy witness was shed, I stood by and consented: and kept the garments of them that killed him.€€ƒxGAnd he said to me: Go, for unto the Gentiles afar off will I send thee.€€ƒx—And they heard him until this word and then lifted up their voice, saying: Away with such an one from the earth. For it is not fit that he should live.€€ƒxNAnd as they cried out and threw off their garments and cast dust into the air,€€ƒxThe tribune commanded him to be brought into the castle, and that he should be scourged and tortured: to know for what cause they did so cry out against him.€€ƒxAnd when they had bound him with thongs, Paul saith to the centurion that stood by him: Is it lawful for you to scourge a man that is a Roman and uncondemned?€€ƒx‚Which the centurion hearing, went to the tribune and told him, saying: What art thou about to do? For this man is a Roman citizen.€€ƒxQAnd the tribune coming, said to him: Tell me. Art thou a Roman? But he said: Yea.€€ƒxtAnd the tribune answered: I obtained the being free of this city with a great sum. And Paul said: But I was born so.€€ƒx¶Immediately therefore they departed from him that were about to torture him. The tribune also was afraid after he understood that he was a Roman citizen and because he had bound him.€€ƒxßBut on the next day, meaning to know more diligently for what cause he was accused by the Jews, he loosed him and commanded the priests to come together and all the council: and, bringing forth Paul, he set him before them.€€ƒ`€€ƒx…And Paul, looking upon the council, said: Men, brethren, I have conversed with all good conscience before God until this present day.€€ƒxZAnd the high priest, Ananias, commanded them that stood by him to strike him on the mouth.€€ƒx¨Then Paul said to him: God shall strike thee, thou whited wall. For, sittest thou to judge me according to the law and, contrary to the law, commandest me to be struck?€€˜#ƒxŠAnd Paul said: I knew not, brethren, that he is the high priest. For it is written: Thou shalt not speak evil of the prince of thy people.€€ƒxåAnd Paul, knowing that the one part were Sadducees and the other Pharisees, cried out in the council: Men, brethren, I am a Pharisee, the son of Pharisees: concerning the hope and resurrection of the dead I am called in question.€€ƒxyAnd when he had so said, there arose a dissension between the Pharisees and the Sadducees. And the multitude was divided.€€ƒxoFor the Sadducees say that there is no resurrection, neither angel, nor spirit: but the Pharisees confess both.€€ƒx And there arose a great cry. And some of the Pharisees rising up, strove, saying: We find no evil in this man. What if a spirit hath spoken to him, or an angel?€€ƒxØAnd when there arose a great dissension, the tribune, fearing lest Paul should be pulled in pieces by them, commanded the soldiers to go down and to take him by force from among them and to bring him into the castle.€€ƒxœAnd the night following, the Lord standing by him, said: Be constant: for as thou hast testified of me in Jerusalem, so must thou bear witness also at Rome.€€ƒx¡And when day was come, some of the Jews gathered together and bound themselves under a curse, saying that they would neither eat nor drink till they killed Paul.€€ƒx@And they were more than forty men that had made this conspiracy.€€ƒx–Who came to the chief priests and the ancients and said: We have bound ourselves under a great curse that we will eat nothing till we have slain Paul.€€ƒxÎNow therefore do you with the council signify to the tribune, that he bring him forth to you, as if you meant to know something more certain touching him. And we, before he come near, are ready to kill him.€€ƒxtWhich when Paul's sister's son had heard, of their lying in wait, he came and entered into the castle and told Paul.€€ƒx~And Paul, calling to him one of the centurions, said: Bring this young man to the tribune: for he hath some thing to tell him.€€ƒxAnd he, taking him, brought him to the tribune and said: Paul, the prisoner, desired me to bring this young man unto thee, who hath some thing to say to thee.€€ƒx{And the tribune, taking him by the hand, went aside with him privately and asked him: What is it that thou hast to tell me?€€ƒx±And he said: The Jews have agreed to desire thee that thou wouldst bring forth Paul to-morrow into the council, as if they meant to inquire some thing more certain touching him.€€ƒxòBut do not thou give credit to them: for there lie in wait for him more than forty men of them, who have bound themselves by oath neither to eat nor to drink, till they have killed him. And they are now ready, looking for a promise from thee.€€ƒx„The tribune therefore dismissed the young man, charging him that he should tell no man that he had made known these things unto him.€€ƒx½Then having called two centurions, he said to them: Make ready two hundred soldiers to go as far as Caesarea: and seventy horsemen and two hundred spearmen, for the third hour of the night.€€ƒxWAnd provide beasts, that they may set Paul on and bring him safe to Felix the governor.€€ƒx¼(For he feared lest perhaps the Jews might take him away by force and kill him: and he should afterwards be slandered, as if he was to take money.) And he wrote a letter after this manner:€€ƒx@Claudius Lysias to the most excellent governor, Felix, greeting:€€ƒx†This man, being taken by the Jews and ready to be killed by them, I rescued, coming in with an army, understanding that he is a Roman.€€ƒxcAnd meaning to know the cause which they objected unto him, I brought him forth into their council.€€ƒx€Whom I found to be accused concerning questions of their law; but having nothing laid to his charge worthy of death or of bands.€€ƒx“And when I was told of ambushes that they had prepared for him, I sent him to thee, signifying also to his accusers to plead before thee. Farewell.€€ƒxgThen the soldiers, according as it was commanded them, taking Paul, brought him by night to Antipatris.€€ƒxSAnd the next day, leaving the horsemen to go with him, they returned to the castle.€€ƒxtWho, when they were come to Caesarea and had delivered the letter to the governor, did also present Paul before him.€€ƒxdAnd when he had read it and had asked of what province he was and understood that he was of Cilicia:€€ƒxlI will hear thee, said he, when thy accusers come. And he commanded him to be kept in Herod's judgment hall.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAnd after five days, the high priest, Ananias, came down with some ancients and one Tertullus, an orator, who went to the governor against Paul.€€ƒxAnd Paul being called for, Tertullus began to accuse him, saying: Whereas, through thee we live in much peace and many things are rectified by thy providence,€€ƒxSWe accept it always and in all places, most excellent Felix, with all thanksgiving.€€˜ƒx We have found this to be a pestilent man and raising seditions among all the Jews throughout the world: and author of the sedition of the sect of the Nazarenes.€€ƒxyWho also hath gone about to profane the temple: whom, we having apprehended, would also have judged according to our law.€€ƒx[But Lysias the tribune, coming upon us with great violence, took him away out of our hands;€€ƒxCommanding his accusers to come to thee. Of whom thou mayest thyself, by examination, have knowledge of all these things whereof we accuse him.€€ƒx;And the Jews also added and said that these things were so.€€ƒx¯Then Paul answered (the governor making a sign to him to speak): Knowing that for many years thou hast been judge over this nation, I will with good courage answer for myself.€€ƒxdFor thou mayest understand that there are yet but twelve days since I went up to adore in Jerusalem:€€ƒx•And neither in the temple did they find me disputing with any man or causing any concourse of the people: neither in the synagogues, nor in the city.€€ƒxGNeither can they prove unto thee the things whereof they now accuse me.€€ƒx·But this I confess to thee that according to the way which they call a heresy, so do I serve the Father and my God, believing all things which are written in the law and the prophets:€€ƒxtHaving hope in God, which these also themselves look for, that there shall be a resurrection of the just and unjust.€€ƒxcAnd herein do I endeavour to have always a conscience without offence, towards God and towards men.€€ƒxONow after many years, I came to bring alms to my nation and offerings and vows.€€ƒxTIn which I was found purified in the temple: neither with multitude nor with tumult.€€ƒxmBut certain Jews of Asia, who ought to be present before thee and to accuse, if they had anything against me:€€ƒxcOr let these men themselves say if they found in me any iniquity, when standing before the council,€€ƒxŒExcept it be for this one voice only that I cried, standing among them: Concerning the resurrection of the dead am I judged this day by you.€€ƒx„And Felix put them off, having most certain knowledge of this way, saying: When Lysias the tribune shall come down, I will hear you.€€ƒxAnd he commanded a centurion to keep him: and that he should be easy and that he should not prohibit any of his friends to minister unto him.€€ƒx‹And after some days, Felix, coming with Drusilla his wife, who was a Jew, sent for Paul and heard of him the faith that is in Christ Jesus.€€ƒx¼And as he treated of justice and chastity and of the judgment to come, Felix, being terrified, answered: For this time, go thy way: but when I have a convenient time, I will send for thee.€€ƒx~Hoping also withal that money should be given him by Paul: for which cause also oftentimes sending for him, he spoke with him.€€ƒxŒBut when two years were ended, Felix had for successor Portius Festus. And Felix being willing to shew the Jews a pleasure, left Paul bound.€€ƒ`€€ƒxcNow when Festus was come into the province, after three days, he went up to Jerusalem from Cesarea.€€ƒxfAnd the chief priests and principal men of the Jews went unto him against Paul: and they besought him,€€ƒxxRequesting favour against him, that he would command him to be brought to Jerusalem, laying wait to kill him in the way.€€˜ƒxyLet them, therefore, saith he, among you that are able, go down with me and accuse him, if there be any crime in the man.€€ƒx§And having tarried among them no more than eight or ten days, he went down to Caesarea. And the next day, he sat in the judgment seat and commanded Paul to be brought.€€ƒxWho being brought, the Jews stood about him, who were come down from Jerusalem, objecting many and grievious causes, which they could not prove:€€ƒxPaul making answer for himself: Neither against the law of the Jews, nor against the temple, nor against Caesar, have I offended in any thing.€€ƒx’But Festus, willing to shew the Jews a pleasure, answering Paul, said: Wilt thou go up to Jerusalem and there be judged of these things before me?€€ƒxŒThen Paul said: I stand at Caesar's judgment seat, where I ought to be judged. To the Jews I have done no injury, as thou very well knowest.€€ƒxÌFor if I have injured them or have committed any thing worthy of death, I refuse not to die. But if there be none of these things whereof they accuse me, no man may deliver me to them. I appeal to Caesar.€€ƒxpThen Festus, having conferred with the council, answered: Hast thou appealed to Caesar? To Caesar shalt thou go.€€ƒxVAnd after some days, king Agrippa and Bernice came down to Caesarea, to salute Festus.€€ƒxtAnd as they tarried there many days, Festus told the king of Paul, saying: A certain man was left prisoner by Felix.€€ƒx„About whom, when I was at Jerusalem, the chief priests and the ancients of the Jews came unto me, desiring condemnation against him.€€ƒxÚTo whom I answered: it is not the custom of the Romans to condemn any man, before that he who is accused have his accusers present and have liberty to make his answer, to clear himself of the things laid to his charge.€€ƒxWhen therefore they were come hither, without any delay, on the day following, sitting in the judgment seat, I commanded the man to be brought.€€ƒxdAgainst whom, when the accusers stood up, they brought no accusation of this which I thought ill of:€€ƒx{But had certain questions of their own superstition against him, and of one Jesus deceased, whom Paul affirmed to be alive.€€ƒxˆI therefore being in a doubt of this manner of question, asked him whether he would go to Jerusalem and there be judged of these things.€€ƒx}But Paul, appealing to be reserved unto the hearing of Augustus, I commanded him to be kept, till I might send him to Caesar.€€ƒxgAnd Agrippa said to Festus: I would also hear the man, myself. To-morrow, said he, thou shalt hear him.€€ƒxÒAnd on the next day, when Agrippa and Bernice were come with great pomp and had entered into the hall of audience with the tribunes and principal men of the city, at Festus' commandment, Paul was brought forth.€€ƒxâAnd Festus saith: King Agrippa and all ye men who are here present with us, you see this man, about whom all the multitude of the Jews dealt with me at Jerusalem, requesting and crying out that he ought not to live any longer.€€ƒx–Yet have I found nothing that he hath committed worthy of death. But forasmuch as he himself hath appealed to Augustus, I have determined to send him.€€ƒxÍOf whom I have nothing certain to write to my lord. For which cause, I have brought him forth before you, and especially before thee, O king Agrippa, that, examination being made, I may have what to write.€€ƒxfFor it seemeth to me unreasonable to send a prisoner and not to signify the things laid to his charge.€€ƒ`€€ƒxƒThen Agrippa said to Paul: Thou art permitted to speak for thyself. Then Paul, stretching forth his hand, began to make his answer.€€ƒx”I think myself happy, O king Agrippa, that I am to answer for myself this day before thee, touching all the things whereof I am accused by the Jews.€€ƒxƒEspecially as thou knowest all, both customs and questions, that are among the Jews. Wherefore I beseech thee to hear me patiently.€€˜ ƒxŠHaving known me from the beginning (if they will give testimony) that according to the most sure sect of our religion I lived, a Pharisee.€€ƒxhAnd now for the hope of the promise that was made by God to the fathers, do I stand subject to judgment:€€ƒxuUnto which, our twelve tribes, serving night and day, hope to come. For which hope, O king, I am accused by the Jews.€€ƒxKWhy should it be thought a thing incredible that God should raise the dead?€€ƒxiAnd I indeed did formerly think that I ought to do many things contrary to the name of Jesus of Nazareth.€€ƒx·Which also I did at Jerusalem: and many of the saints did I shut up in prison, having received authority of the chief priests. And when they were put to death, I brought the sentence.€€ƒx¢And oftentimes punishing them, in every synagogue, I compelled them to blaspheme: and being yet more mad against them, I persecuted them even unto foreign cities.€€ƒxZWhereupon, when I was going to Damascus with authority and permission of the chief priest,€€ƒx—At midday, O king, I saw in the way a light from heaven, above the brightness of the sun, shining round about me and them that were in company with me.€€ƒx·And when we were all fallen down on the ground, I heard a voice speaking to me in the Hebrew tongue: Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me? It is hard for thee to kick against the good.€€ƒxXAnd I said: Who art thou, Lord? And the Lord answered: I am Jesus whom thou persecutest.€€ƒxÓBut rise up and stand upon thy feet: for to this end have I appeared to thee, that I may make thee a minister and a witness of those things which thou hast seen and of those things wherein I will appear to thee,€€ƒxPDelivering thee from the people and from the nations unto which now I send thee:€€ƒxËTo open their eyes, that they may be converted from darkness to light and from the power of Satan to God, that they may receive forgiveness of sins and a lot among the saints, by the faith that is in me.€€ƒxHWhereupon, O king Agrippa, I was not incredulous to the heavenly vision.€€ƒxÉBut to them first that are at Damascus and at Jerusalem, and unto all the country of Judea, and to the Gentiles did I preach, that they should do penance and turn to God, doing works worthy of penance.€€ƒxaFor this cause, the Jews, when I was in the temple, having apprehended me, went about to kill me.€€ƒx¹But being aided by the help of God, I stand unto this day, witnessing both to small and great, saying no other thing than those which the prophets and Moses did say should come to pass:€€ƒx‘That Christ should suffer and that he should be the first that should rise from the dead and should shew light to the people and to the Gentiles.€€ƒxAs he spoke these things and made his answer, Festus said with a loud voice: Paul, thou art beside thyself: much learning doth make thee mad.€€ƒx]And Paul said: I am not mad, most excellent Festus, but I speak words of truth and soberness.€€ƒxÃFor the king knoweth of these things, to whom also I speak with confidence. For I am persuaded that none of these things are hidden from him. For neither was any of these things done in a corner.€€ƒxHBelievest thou the prophets, O king Agrippa? I know that thou believest.€€ƒxOAnd Agrippa said to Paul: In a little thou persuadest me to become a Christian.€€ƒx¨And Paul said: I would to God that both in a little and in much, not only thou, but also all that hear me this day, should become such as I also am, except these bands.€€ƒxOAnd the king rose up, and the governor and Bernice and they that sat with them.€€ƒx{And when they were gone aside, they spoke among themselves, saying: This man hath done nothing worthy of death or of bands.€€ƒxfAnd Agrippa said to Festus: This man might have been set at liberty, if he had not appealed to Caesar.€€ƒ`€€ƒx©And when it was determined that he should sail into Italy and that Paul, with the other prisoners, should be delivered to a centurion, named Julius, of the band Augusta,€€ƒx˜Going on board a ship of Adrumetum, we launched, meaning to sail by the coasts of Asia, Aristarchus, the Macedonian of Thessalonica, continuing with us.€€ƒxAnd the day following, we came to Sidon. And Julius, treating Paul courteously, permitted him to go to his friends and to take care of himself.€€˜,ƒxXAnd sailing over the sea of Cilicia and Pamphylia, we came to Lystra, which is in Lycia.€€ƒx^And there, the centurion, finding a ship of Alexandria sailing into Italy, removed us into it.€€ƒx‘And when for many days we had sailed slowly and were scarce come over against Gnidus, the wind not suffering us, we sailed near Crete by Salmone.€€ƒxƒAnd with much ado sailing by it, we came into a certain place, which is called Good-havens, nigh to which was the city of Thalassa.€€ƒxtAnd when much time was spent and when sailing now was dangerous, because the fast was now past, Paul comforted them,€€ƒx’Saying to them: Ye men, I see that the voyage beginneth to be with injury and much damage, not only of the lading and ship, but also of our lives.€€ƒxpBut the centurion believed the pilot and the master of the ship, more than those things which were said by Paul.€€ƒxéAnd whereas it was not a commodious haven to winter in, the greatest part gave counsel to sail thence, if by any means they might reach Phenice, to winter there, which is a haven of Crete, looking towards the southwest and northwest.€€ƒxAnd the south wind gently blowing, thinking that they had obtained their purpose, when they had loosed from Asson, they sailed close by Crete.€€ƒxQBut not long after, there arose against it a tempestuous wind, called Euroaquilo.€€ƒxuAnd when the ship was caught and could not bear up against the wind, giving up the ship to the winds, we were driven.€€ƒx^And running under a certain island that is called Cauda, we had much work to come by the boat.€€ƒx¤Which being taken up, they used helps, undergirding the ship: and fearing lest they should fall into the quicksands, they let down the sail yard and so were driven.€€ƒxUAnd we, being mightily tossed with the tempest, the next day they lightened the ship.€€ƒxMAnd the third day they cast out with their own hands the tacking of the ship.€€ƒx„And when neither sun nor stars appeared for many days and no small storms lay on us, all hope of our being saved was now taken away.€€ƒxÍAnd after they had fasted a long time, Paul standing forth in the midst of them, said: You should indeed, O ye men, have hearkened unto me and not have loosed from Crete and have gained this harm and loss.€€ƒxwAnd now I exhort you to be of good cheer. For there shall be no loss of any man's life among you, but only of the ship.€€ƒxIFor an angel of God, whose I am and whom I serve, stood by me this night,€€ƒxySaying: Fear not, Paul, thou must be brought before Caesar; and behold, God hath given thee all them that sail with thee.€€ƒxcWherefore, sirs, be of good cheer: for I believe God, that it shall so be, as it hath been told me.€€ƒx'And we must come unto a certain island.€€ƒx‹But after the fourteenth night was come, as we were sailing in Adria, about midnight, the shipmen deemed that they discovered some country.€€ƒxcWho also sounding, found twenty fathoms: and going on a little further, they found fifteen fathoms.€€ƒxtThen fearing lest we should fall upon rough places, they cast four anchors out of the stern: and wished for the day.€€ƒx®But as the shipmen sought to fly out of the ship, having let down the boat into the sea, under colour, as though they would have cast anchors out of the forepart of the ship,€€ƒxcPaul said to the centurion and to the soldiers: Except these stay in the ship, you cannot be saved.€€ƒxEThen the soldiers cut off the ropes of the boat and let her fall off.€€ƒx¦And when it began to be light, Paul besought them all to take meat, saying: This day is the fourteenth day that you have waited and continued fasting, taking nothing.€€ƒx}Wherefore, I pray you to take some meat for your health's sake: for there shall not an hair of the head of any of you perish.€€ƒxŒAnd when he had said these things, taking bread, he gave thanks to God in the sight of them all. And when he had broken it, he began to eat.€€ƒxAThen were they all of better cheer: and they also took some meat.€€ƒxHAnd we were in all in the ship two hundred threescore and sixteen souls.€€ƒxXAnd when they had eaten enough, they lightened the ship, casting the wheat into the sea.€€ƒx And when it was day, they knew not the land. But they discovered a certain creek that had a shore, into which they minded, if they could, to thrust in the ship.€€ƒx±And when they had taken up the anchors, they committed themselves to the sea, loosing withal the rudder bands. And hoisting up the mainsail to the wind, they made towards shore.€€ƒxÍAnd when we were fallen into a place where two seas met, they run the ship aground. And the forepart indeed, sticking fast, remained unmoveable: but the hinder part was broken with the violence of the sea.€€ƒxpAnd the soldiers' counsel was that they should kill the prisoners, lest any of them, swimming out should escape.€€ƒx¸But the centurion, willing to save Paul, forbade it to be done. And he commanded that they who could swim should cast themselves first into the sea and save themselves and get to land.€€ƒx–And the rest, some they carried on boards and some on those things that belonged to the ship. And so it came to pass that every soul got safe to land.€€ƒ`€€ƒxxAnd when we had escaped, then we knew that the island was called Melita. But the barbarians shewed us no small courtesy.€€ƒxXFor kindling a fire, they refreshed us all, because of the present rain and of the cold.€€ƒxŒAnd when Paul had gathered together a bundle of sticks and had laid them on the fire, a viper, coming out of the heat, fastened on his hand.€€˜ƒxEAnd he indeed, shaking off the beast into the fire, suffered no harm.€€ƒxĞBut they supposed that he would begin to swell up and that he would suddenly fall down and die. But expecting long and seeing that there came no harm to him, changing their minds, they said that he was a god.€€ƒx‘Now in these places were possessions of the chief man of the island, named Publius: who, receiving us for three days, entertained us courteously.€€ƒx®And it happened that the father of Publius lay sick of a fever and of a bloody flux. To whom Paul entered in. And when he had prayed and laid his hands on him, he healed him.€€ƒxKWhich being done, all that had diseases in the island came and were healed.€€ƒxwWho also honoured us with many honours: and when we were to set sail, they laded us with such things as were necessary.€€ƒxwAnd after three months, we sailed in a ship of Alexandria, that had wintered in the island, whose sign was the Castors.€€ƒx?And when we were come to Syracusa, we tarried there three days.€€ƒx‡From thence, compassing by the shore, we came to Rhegium: and after one day, the south wind blowing, we came the second day to Puteoli:€€ƒx_Where, finding brethren, we were desired to tarry with them seven days. And so we went to Rome.€€ƒx±And from thence, when the brethren had heard of us, they came to meet us as far as Appii Forum and the Three Taverns. Whom when Paul saw, he gave thanks to God and took courage.€€ƒxcAnd when we were come to Rome, Paul was suffered to dwell by himself, with a soldier that kept him.€€ƒyAnd after the third day, he called together the chief of the Jews. And when they were assembled, he said to them: Men, brethren, I, having done nothing against the people or the custom of our fathers, was delivered prisoner from Jerusalem into the hands of the Romans.€€ƒxcWho, when they had examined me, would have released me, for that there was no cause of death in me.€€ƒxwBut the Jews contradicting it, I was constrained to appeal unto Caesar: not that I had anything to accuse my nation of.€€ƒxƒFor this cause therefore I desired to see you and to speak to you. Because that for the hope of Israel, I am bound with this chain.€€ƒx But they said to him: We neither received letters concerning thee from Judea: neither did any of the brethren that came hither relate or speak any evil of thee.€€ƒx{But we desire to hear of thee what thou thinkest: for as concerning this sect, we know that it is every where contradicted.€€ƒxõAnd when they had appointed him a day, there came very many to him unto his lodgings. To whom he expounded, testifying the kingdom of God and persuading them concerning Jesus, out of the law of Moses and the prophets, from morning until evening.€€ƒxCAnd some believed the things that were said: but some believed not.€€ƒxšAnd when they agreed not among themselves, they departed, Paul speaking this one word: Well did the Holy Ghost speak to our fathers by Isaias the prophet,€€ƒx‘Saying: Go to this people and say to them: With the ear you shall hear and shall not understand: and seeing you shall see and shall not perceive.€€ƒyFor the heart of this people is grown gross, and with their ears have they heard heavily and their eyes they have shut, lest perhaps they should see with their eyes and hear with their ears and understand with their heart and should be converted: and I should heal them.€€ƒxgBe it known therefore to you that this salvation of God is sent to the Gentiles: and they will hear it.€€ƒxfAnd when he had said these things, the Jews went out from him, having much reasoning among themselves.€€ƒxbAnd he remained two whole years in his own hired lodging: and he received all that came in to him,€€ƒxƒPreaching the kingdom of God and teaching the things which concern the Lord Jesus Christ, with all confidence, without prohibition.€€ƒxThe Letter to the Romans€€ƒ`€€ƒx[Paul, a servant of Jesus Christ, called to be an apostle, separated unto the gospel of God.€€ƒxFWhich he had promised before, by his prophets, in the holy scriptures,€€cRom¤dnamefRomansiosis_namecRomlabbreviationcRomhchapters˜ ƒxhBy whom we have received grace and apostleship for obedience to the faith, in all nations, for his name:€€ƒx3Among whom are you also the called of Jesus Christ:€€ƒxTo all that are at Rome, the beloved of God, called to be saints. Grace to you and peace, from God our Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒxvFirst, I give thanks to my God, through Jesus Christ, for you all: because your faith is spoken of in the whole world.€€ƒx~For God is my witness, whom I serve in my spirit in the gospel of his Son, that without ceasing I make a commemoration of you:€€ƒx‰Always in my prayers making request, if by any means now at length I may have a prosperous journey, by the will of God, to come unto you.€€ƒxYFor I long to see you that I may impart unto you some spiritual grace, to strengthen you:€€ƒxpThat is to say, that I may be comforted together in you by that which is common to us both, your faith and mine.€€ƒxÅAnd I would not have you ignorant, brethren, that I have often purposed to come unto you (and have been hindered hitherto) that I might have some fruit among you also, even as among other Gentiles.€€ƒxRTo the Greeks and to the barbarians, to the wise and to the unwise, I am a debtor.€€ƒxVSo (as much as is in me) I am ready to preach the gospel to you also that are at Rome.€€ƒxFor I am not ashamed of the gospel. For it is the power of God unto salvation to every one that believeth: to the Jew first and to the Greek.€€ƒxrFor the justice of God is revealed therein, from faith unto faith, as it is written: The just man liveth by faith.€€ƒxŠFor the wrath of God is revealed from heaven against all ungodliness and injustice of those men that detain the truth of God in injustice:€€ƒx]Because that which is known of God is manifest in them. For God hath manifested it unto them.€€ƒxÁFor the invisible things of him from the creation of the world are clearly seen, being understood by the things that are made. His eternal power also and divinity: so that they are inexcusable.€€ƒxBecause that, when they knew God, they have not glorified him as God or given thanks: but became vain in their thoughts. And their foolish heart was darkened.€€ƒx9For, professing themselves to be wise, they became fools.€€ƒx¦And they changed the glory of the incorruptible God into the likeness of the image of a corruptible man and of birds, and of fourfooted beasts and of creeping things.€€ƒx|Wherefore, God gave them up to the desires of their heart, unto uncleanness: to dishonour their own bodies among themselves.€€ƒx†Who changed the truth of God into a lie and worshipped and served the creature rather than the Creator, who is blessed for ever. Amen.€€ƒx‘For this cause, God delivered them up to shameful affections. For their women have changed the natural use into that use which is against nature.€€ƒxíAnd, in like manner, the men also, leaving the natural use of the women, have burned in their lusts, one towards another: men with men, working that which is filthy and receiving in themselves the recompense which was due to their error.€€ƒxAnd as they liked not to have God in their knowledge, God delivered them up to a reprobate sense, to do those things which are not convenient.€€ƒxŠBeing filled with all iniquity, malice, fornication, avarice, wickedness: full of envy, murder, contention, deceit, malignity: whisperers,€€ƒxkDetractors, hateful to God, contumelious, proud, haughty, inventors of evil things, disobedient to parents,€€ƒxGFoolish, dissolute: without affection, without fidelity, without mercy.€€ƒx¼Who, having known the justice of God, did not understand that they who do such things, are worthy of death: and not only they that do them, but they also that consent to them that do them.€€ƒ`€€ƒx´Wherefore thou art inexcusable, O man, whosoever thou art that judgest. For wherein thou judgest another, thou condemnest thyself. For thou dost the same things which thou judgest.€€ƒx^For we know that the judgment of God is, according to truth, against them that do such things.€€ƒx‚And thinkest thou this, O man, that judgest them who do such things and dost the same, that thou shalt escape the judgment of God?€€˜ƒxBut according to thy hardness and impenitent heart, thou treasurest up to thyself wrath, against the day of wrath and revelation of the just judgment of God:€€ƒx4Who will render to every man according to his works.€€ƒxmTo them indeed who, according to patience in good work, seek glory and honour and incorruption, eternal life:€€ƒxoBut to them that are contentious and who obey not the truth but give credit to iniquity, wrath and indignation.€€ƒxjTribulation and anguish upon every soul of man that worketh evil: of the Jew first, and also of the Greek.€€ƒxgBut glory and honour and peace to every one that worketh good: to the Jew first, and also to the Greek.€€ƒx,For there is no respect of persons with God.€€ƒxˆFor whosoever have sinned without the law shall perish without the law: and whosoever have sinned in the law shall be judged by the law.€€ƒx`For not the hearers of the law are just before God: but the doers of the law shall be justified.€€ƒxFor when the Gentiles, who have not the law, do by nature those things that are of the law; these, having not the law, are a law to themselves.€€ƒx­Who shew the work of the law written in their hearts, their conscience bearing witness to them: and their thoughts between themselves accusing or also defending one another,€€ƒx[In the day when God shall judge the secrets of men by Jesus Christ, according to my gospel.€€ƒxPBut if thou art called a Jew and restest in the law and makest thy boast of God,€€ƒx[And knowest his will and approvest the more profitable things, being instructed by the law:€€ƒx_Art confident that thou thyself art a guide of the blind, a light of them that are in darkness,€€ƒxiAn instructor of the foolish, a teacher of infants, having the form of knowledge and of truth in the law.€€ƒxvThou therefore, that teachest another, teachest not thyself: thou, that preachest that men should not steal, stealest.€€ƒxxThou, that sayest men should not commit adultery, committest adultery: thou, that abhorrest idols, committest sacrilege:€€ƒxUThou, that makest thy boast of the law, by transgression of the law dishonourest God.€€ƒxU(For the name of God through you is blasphemed among the Gentiles, as it is written.)€€ƒx‡Circumcision profiteth indeed, if thou keep the law: but if thou be a transgressor of the law, thy circumcision is made uncircumcision.€€ƒxsIf then, the uncircumcised keep the justices of the law, shall not this uncircumcision be counted for circumcision?€€ƒx™And shall not that which by nature is uncircumcision, if it fulfil the law, judge thee, who by the letter and circumcision art a transgressor of the law?€€ƒxiFor it is not he is a Jew, who is so outwardly: nor is that circumcision which is outwardly in the flesh.€€ƒx˜But he is a Jew that is one inwardly and the circumcision is that of the heart, in the spirit not in the letter: whose praise is not of men, but of God.€€ƒ`€€ƒxHWhat advantage then hath the Jew: or what is the profit of circumcision?€€ƒxNMuch every way. First indeed, because the words of God were committed to them.€€ƒxrFor what if some of them have not believed? Shall their unbelief make the faith of God without effect? God forbid!€€˜ƒxgBut if our injustice commend the justice of God, what shall we say? Is God unjust, who executeth wrath?€€ƒxQ(I speak according to man.) God forbid! Otherwise how shall God judge this world?€€ƒxpFor if the truth of God hath more abounded through my lie, unto his glory, why am I also yet judged as a sinner?€€ƒx…And not rather (as we are slandered and as some affirm that we say) let us do evil that there may come good? Whose damnation is just.€€ƒxoWhat then? Do we excel them? No, not so. For we have charged both Jews and Greeks, that they are all under sin.€€ƒx,As it is written: There is not any man just.€€ƒxGThere is none that understandeth: there is none that seeketh after God.€€ƒxAll have turned out of the way: they are become unprofitable together: there is none that doth good, there is not so much as one.€€ƒxyTheir throat is an open sepulchre: with their tongues they have dealt deceitfully. The venom of asps is under their lips.€€ƒx.Whose mouth is full of cursing and bitterness:€€ƒxTheir feet swift to shed blood:€€ƒx%Destruction and misery in their ways:€€ƒx)And the way of peace they have not known.€€ƒx*There is no fear of God before their eyes.€€ƒx¬Now we know that what things soever the law speaketh, it speaketh to them that are in the law: that every mouth may be stopped and all the world may be made subject to God.€€ƒxoBecause by the works of the law no flesh shall be justified before him. For by the law is the knowledge of sin.€€ƒxkBut now, without the law, the justice of God is made manifest, being witnessed by the law and the prophets.€€ƒx€Even the justice of God, by faith of Jesus Christ, unto all, and upon all them that believe in him: for there is no distinction.€€ƒx1For all have sinned and do need the glory of God.€€ƒxTBeing justified freely by his grace, through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus,€€ƒx‰Whom God hath proposed to be a propitiation, through faith in his blood, to the shewing of his justice, for the remission of former sins,€€ƒx¥Through the forbearance of God, for the shewing of his justice in this time: that he himself may be just and the justifier of him who is of the faith of Jesus Christ€€ƒx_Where is then thy boasting? It is excluded. By what law? Of works? No, but by the law of faith.€€ƒxLFor we account a man to be justified by faith, without the works of the law.€€ƒxZIs he the God of the Jews only? Is he not also of the Gentiles? yes, of the Gentiles also.€€ƒxYFor it is one God that justifieth circumcision by faith and uncircumcision through faith.€€ƒxPDo we then, destroy the law through faith? God forbid! But we establish the law.€€ƒ`€€ƒxYWhat shall we say then that Abraham hath found, who is our father according to the flesh?€€ƒxUFor if Abraham were justified by works, he hath whereof to glory, but not before God.€€ƒx[For what saith the scripture? Abraham believed God: and it was reputed to him unto justice.€€˜ƒxBut to him that worketh not, yet believeth in him that justifieth the ungodly, his faith is reputed to justice, according to the purpose of the grace of God.€€ƒxZAs David also termeth the blessedness of a man to whom God reputeth justice without works:€€ƒxKBlessed are they whose iniquities are forgiven: and whose sins are covered.€€ƒx9Blessed is the man to whom the Lord hath not imputed sin.€€ƒx˜This blessedness then, doth it remain in the circumcision only or in the uncircumcision also? For we say that unto Abraham faith was reputed to justice.€€ƒxvHow then was it reputed? When he was in circumcision or in uncircumcision? Not in circumcision, but in uncircumcision.€€ƒxìAnd he received the sign of circumcision, a seal of the justice of the faith which he had, being uncircumcised: that he might be the father of all them that believe, being uncircumcised: that unto them also it may be reputed to justice:€€ƒxÃAnd he might be the father of circumcision; not to them only that are of the circumcision, but to them also that follow the steps of the faith that is in the uncircumcision of our father Abraham.€€ƒx‰For not through the law was the promise to Abraham or to his seed, that he should be heir of the world: but through the justice of faith.€€ƒx^For if they who are of the law be heirs, faith is made void: the promise is made of no effect.€€ƒxUFor the law worketh wrath. For where there is no law, neither is there transgression.€€ƒxÒTherefore is it of faith, that according to grace the promise might be firm to all the seed: not to that only which is of the law, but to that also which is of the faith of Abraham, who is the father of us all,€€ƒx®(As it is written: I have made thee a father of many nations), before God, whom he believed: who quickeneth the dead and calleth those things that are not, as those that are.€€ƒx“Who against hope believed in hope; that he might be made the father of many nations, according to that which was said to him: So shall thy seed be.€€ƒx•And he was not weak in faith. Neither did he consider his own body, now dead (whereas he was almost an hundred years old), nor the dead womb of Sara.€€ƒxlIn the promise also of God he staggered not by distrust: but was strengthened in faith, giving glory to God:€€ƒxOMost fully knowing that whatsoever he has promised, he is able also to perform.€€ƒx1And therefore it was reputed to him unto justice.€€ƒxLNow it is not written only for him. that it was reputed to him unto justice,€€ƒxxBut also for us, to whom it shall be reputed, if we believe in him that raised up Jesus Christ, our Lord, from the dead,€€ƒxGWho was delivered up for our sins and rose again for our justification.€€ƒ`€€ƒx^Being justified therefore by faith, let us have peace with God, through our Lord Jesus Christ:€€ƒx‚By whom also we have access through faith into this grace wherein we stand: and glory in the hope of the glory of the sons of God.€€ƒx]And not only so: but we glory also in tribulation, knowing that tribulation worketh patience;€€•ƒxyAnd hope confoundeth not: because the charity of God is poured forth in our hearts, by the Holy Ghost who is given to us.€€ƒxYFor why did Christ, when as yet we were weak, according to the time, die for the ungodly?€€ƒx^For scarce for a just man will one die: yet perhaps for a good man some one would dare to die.€€ƒxeBut God commendeth his charity towards us: because when as yet we were sinners according to the time.€€ƒxtChrist died for us. Much more therefore, being now justified by his blood, shall we be saved from wrath through him.€€ƒxŒFor if, when we were enemies, we were reconciled to God by the death of his Son: much more, being reconciled, shall we be saved by his life.€€ƒxvAnd not only so: but also we glory in God, through our Lord Jesus Christ, by whom we have now received reconciliation.€€ƒx€Wherefore as by one man sin entered into this world and by sin death: and so death passed upon all men, in whom all have sinned.€€ƒxVFor until the law sin was in the world: but sin was not imputed, when the law was not.€€ƒx«But death reigned from Adam unto Moses, even over them also who have not sinned, after the similitude of the transgression of Adam, who is a figure of him who was to come.€€ƒx»But not as the offence, so also the gift. For if by the offence of one, many died: much more the grace of God and the gift, by the grace of one man, Jesus Christ, hath abounded unto many.€€ƒx–And not as it was by one sin, so also is the gift. For judgment indeed was by one unto condemnation: but grace is of many offences unto justification.€€ƒx²For if by one man's offence death reigned through one; much more they who receive abundance of grace and of the gift and of justice shall reign in life through one, Jesus Christ.€€ƒxˆTherefore, as by the offence of one, unto all men to condemnation: so also by the justice of one, unto all men to justification of life.€€ƒxxFor as by the disobedience of one man, many were made sinners: so also by the obedience of one, many shall be made just.€€ƒx\Now the law entered in that sin might abound. And where sin abounded, grace did more abound.€€ƒx}That as sin hath reigned to death: so also grace might reign by justice unto life everlasting, through Jesus Christ our Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxIWhat shall we say, then? Shall we continue in sin, that grace may abound?€€ƒxNGod forbid! For we that are dead to sin, how shall we live any longer therein?€€ƒxTKnow you not that all we who are baptized in Christ Jesus are baptized in his death?€€—ƒxxFor if we have been planted together in the likeness of his death, we shall be also in the likeness of his resurrection.€€ƒxˆKnowing this, that our old man is crucified with him, that the body of sin may be destroyed, to the end that we may serve sin no longer.€€ƒx*For he that is dead is justified from sin.€€ƒxXNow, if we be dead with Christ, we believe that we shall live also together with Christ.€€ƒxoKnowing that Christ, rising again from the dead, dieth now no more. Death shall no more have dominion over him.€€ƒxTFor in that he died to sin, he died once: but in that he liveth, he liveth unto God.€€ƒx]So do you also reckon that you are dead to sin, but alive unto God, in Christ Jesus our Lord.€€ƒxQLet not sin therefore reign in your mortal body, so as to obey the lusts thereof.€€ƒx½Neither yield ye your members as instruments of iniquity unto sin: but present yourselves to God, as those that are alive from the dead; and your members as instruments of justice unto God.€€ƒxYFor sin shall not have dominion over you: for you are not under the law, but under grace.€€ƒxWWhat then? Shall we sin, because we are not under the law, but under grace? God forbid!€€ƒx¢Know you not that to whom you yield yourselves servants to obey, his servants you are whom you obey, whether it be of sin unto death or of obedience unto justice.€€ƒx•But thanks be to God, that you were the servants of sin but have obeyed from the heart unto that form of doctrine into which you have been delivered.€€ƒxABeing then freed from sin, we have been made servants of justice.€€ƒx×I speak an human thing, because of the infirmity of your flesh. For as you have yielded your members to serve uncleanness and iniquity, unto iniquity: so now yield your members to serve justice, unto sanctification.€€ƒxDFor when you were the servants of sin, you were free men to justice.€€ƒxmWhat fruit therefore had you then in those things of which you are now ashamed? For the end of them is death.€€ƒxƒBut now being made free from sin and become servants to God, you have your fruit unto sanctification, and the end life everlasting.€€ƒx_For the wages of sin is death. But the grace of God, life everlasting in Christ Jesus our Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒxzKnow you not, brethren (for I speak to them that know the law) that the law hath dominion over a man as long as it liveth?€€ƒx™For the woman that hath an husband, whilst her husband liveth is bound to the law. But if her husband be dead, she is loosed from the law of her husband.€€ƒxìTherefore, whilst her husband liveth, she shall be called an adulteress, if she be with another man: but if her husband be dead, she is delivered from the law of her husband: so that she is not an adulteress, if she be with another man.€€˜ƒx…For when we were in the flesh, the passions of sins, which were by the law, did work in our members, to bring forth fruit unto death.€€ƒx™But now we are loosed from the law of death wherein we were detained; so that we should serve in newness of spirit, and not in the oldness of the letter.€€ƒx¬What shall we say, then? Is the law sin? God forbid! But I do not know sin, but by the law. For I had not known concupiscence, if the law did not say: Thou shalt not covet.€€ƒxyBut sin, taking occasion by the commandment, wrought in me all manner of concupiscence. For without the law sin was dead.€€ƒxRAnd I lived some time without the law. But when the commandment came, sin revived,€€ƒxeAnd I died. And the commandment that was ordained to life, the same was found to be unto death to me.€€ƒxMFor sin, taking occasion by the commandment, seduced me: and by it killed me.€€ƒxMWherefore the law indeed is holy: and the commandment holy and just and good.€€ƒxÉWas that then which is good made death unto me? God forbid! But sin, that it may appear sin, by that which is good, wrought death in me: that sin, by the commandment, might become sinful above measure.€€ƒxGFor we know that the law is spiritual. But I am carnal, sold under sin.€€ƒxsFor that which I work, I understand not. For I do not that good which I will: but the evil which I hate, that I do.€€ƒxJIf then I do that which I will not, I consent to the law, that it is good.€€ƒxANow then it is no more I that do it: but sin that dwelleth in me.€€ƒx¬For I know that there dwelleth not in me, that is to say, in my flesh, that which is good. For to will is present with me: but to accomplish that which is good, I find not.€€ƒxNFor the good which I will, I do not: but the evil which I will not, that I do.€€ƒx[Now if I do that which I will not, it is no more I that do it: but sin that dwelleth in me.€€ƒxOI find then a law, that when I have a will to do good, evil is present with me.€€ƒxDFor I am delighted with the law of God, according to the inward man:€€ƒx„But I see another law in my members, fighting against the law of my mind and captivating me in the law of sin that is in my members.€€ƒxHUnhappy man that I am, who shall deliver me from the body of this death?€€ƒxˆThe grace of God, by Jesus Christ our Lord. Therefore, I myself, with the mind serve the law of God: but with the flesh, the law of sin.€€ƒ`€€ƒxmThere is now therefore no condemnation to them that are in Christ Jesus, who walk not according to the flesh.€€ƒxgFor the law of the spirit of life, in Christ Jesus, hath delivered me from the law of sin and of death.€€ƒx«For what the law could not do, in that it was weak through the flesh, God, sending his own Son in the likeness of sinful flesh and of sin, hath condemned sin in the flesh.€€˜'ƒx¡For they that are according to the flesh mind the things that are of the flesh: but they that are according to the spirit mind the things that are of the spirit.€€ƒxUFor the wisdom of the flesh is death: but the wisdom of the spirit is life and peace.€€ƒxoBecause the wisdom of the flesh is an enemy to God. For it is not subject to the law of God: neither can it be.€€ƒx0And they who are in the flesh cannot please God.€€ƒxœBut you are not in the flesh, but the spirit, if so be that the Spirit of God dwell in you. Now if any man have not the Spirit of Christ, he is none of his.€€ƒxrAnd if Christ be in you, the body indeed is dead, because of sin: but the spirit liveth, because of justification.€€ƒxÊAnd if the Spirit of him that raised up Jesus from the dead dwell in you; he that raised up Jesus Christ, from the dead shall quicken also your mortal bodies, because of his Spirit that dwelleth in you.€€ƒxUTherefore, brethren, we are debtors, not to the flesh to live according to the flesh.€€ƒxFor if you live according to the flesh, you shall die: but if by the Spirit you mortify the deeds of the flesh, you shall live.€€ƒxEFor whosoever are led by the Spirit of God, they are the sons of God.€€ƒx“For you have not received the spirit of bondage again in fear: but you have received the spirit of adoption of sons, whereby we cry: Abba (Father).€€ƒxRFor the Spirit himself giveth testimony to our spirit that we are the sons of God.€€ƒxAnd if sons, heirs also; heirs indeed of God and joint heirs with Christ: yet so, if we suffer with him, that we may be also glorified with him.€€ƒx€For I reckon that the sufferings of this time are not worthy to be compared with the glory to come that shall be revealed in us.€€ƒxRFor the expectation of the creature waiteth for the revelation of the sons of God.€€ƒxoFor the creature was made subject to vanity: not willingly, but by reason of him that made it subject, in hope.€€ƒx‹Because the creature also itself shall be delivered from the servitude of corruption, into the liberty of the glory of the children of God.€€ƒxOFor we know that every creature groaneth and travaileth in pain, even till now.€€ƒx¿And not only it, but ourselves also, who have the firstfruits of the Spirit: even we ourselves groan within ourselves, waiting for the adoption of the sons of God, the redemption of our body.€€ƒxhFor we are saved by hope. But hope that is seen is not hope. For what a man seeth, why doth he hope for?€€ƒxGBut if we hope for that which we see not, we wait for it with patience.€€ƒx§Likewise, the Spirit also helpeth our infirmity. For, we know not what we should pray for as we ought: but the Spirit himself asketh for us with unspeakable groanings,€€ƒxuAnd he that searcheth the hearts knoweth what the Spirit desireth: because he asketh for the saints according to God.€€ƒx‰And we know that to them that love God all things work together unto good: to such as, according to his purpose, are called to be saints.€€ƒx‘For whom he foreknew, he also predestinated to be made conformable to the image of his Son: that he might be the Firstborn amongst many brethren.€€ƒxŠAnd whom he predestinated, them he also called. And whom he called, them he also justified. And whom he justified, them he also glorified.€€ƒxLWhat shall we then say to these things? If God be for us, who is against us?€€ƒxzHe that spared not even his own Son, but delivered him up for us all, how hath he not also, with him, given us all things?€€ƒxEWho shall accuse against the elect of God? God is he that justifieth:€€ƒx™Who is he that shall condemn? Christ Jesus that died: yea that is risen also again, who is at the right hand of God, who also maketh intercession for us.€€ƒx•Who then shall separate us from the love of Christ? Shall tribulation? Or distress? Or famine? Or nakedness? Or danger? Or persecution? Or the sword?€€ƒxt(As it is written: For thy sake, we are put to death all the day long. We are accounted as sheep for the slaughter.)€€ƒxGBut in all these things we overcome, because of him that hath loved us.€€ƒxŠFor I am sure that neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor powers, nor things present, nor things to come, nor might,€€ƒxƒNor height, nor depth, nor any other creature, shall be able to separate us from the love of God which is in Christ Jesus our Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx[I speak the truth in Christ: I lie not, my conscience bearing me witness in the Holy Ghost:€€ƒx;That I have great sadness and continual sorrow in my heart.€€ƒxnFor I wished myself to be an anathema from Christ, for my brethren: who are my kinsmen according to the flesh:€€˜!ƒxxWhose are the fathers and of whom is Christ, according to the flesh, who is over all things, God blessed for ever. Amen.€€ƒx]Not as though the word of God hath miscarried. For all are not Israelites that are of Israel.€€ƒxcNeither are all they that are the seed of Abraham, children: but in Isaac shall thy seed be called.€€ƒxThat is to say, not they that are the children of the flesh are the children of God: but they that are the children of the promise are accounted for the seed.€€ƒx_For this is the word of promise: According to this time will I come. And Sara shall have a son.€€ƒxRAnd not only she. But when Rebecca also had conceived at once of Isaac our father.€€ƒxƒFor when the children were not yet born, nor had done any good or evil (that the purpose of God according to election might stand):€€ƒx]Not of works, but of him that calleth, it was said to her: The elder shall serve the younger.€€ƒx<As it is written: Jacob I have loved: but Esau I have hated.€€ƒx@What shall we say then? Is there injustice with God? God forbid!€€ƒxtFor he saith to Moses: I will have mercy on whom I will have mercy. And I will shew mercy to whom I will shew mercy.€€ƒx^So then it is not of him that willeth, nor of him that runneth, but of God that sheweth mercy.€€ƒx¢For the scripture saith to Pharao: To this purpose have I raised thee, that I may shew my power in thee and that my name may be declared throughout all the earth.€€ƒxHTherefore he hath mercy on whom he will. And whom he will, he hardeneth.€€ƒxWThou wilt say therefore to me: Why doth he then find fault? For who resisteth his will?€€ƒx|O man, who art thou that repliest against God? Shall the thing formed say to him that formed it: Why hast thou made me thus?€€ƒxxOr hath not the potter power over the clay, of the same lump, to make one vessel unto honour and another unto dishonour?€€ƒxˆWhat if God, willing to shew his wrath and to make his power known, endured with much patience vessels of wrath, fitted for destruction,€€ƒxeThat he might shew the riches of his glory on the vessels of mercy which he hath prepared unto glory?€€ƒxQEven us, whom also he hath called, not only of the Jews but also of the Gentiles.€€ƒx³As in Osee he saith: I will call that which was not my people, my people; and her that was not beloved, beloved; and her that had not obtained mercy; one that hath obtained mercy.€€ƒx‡And it shalt be in the place where it was said unto them: you are not my people; there they shall be called the sons of the living God.€€ƒx„And Isaias cried out concerning Israel: If the number of the children of Israel be as the sand of the sea, a remnant shall be saved.€€ƒxrFor he shall finish his word and cut it short in justice: because a short word shall the Lord make upon the earth.€€ƒxAnd Isaias foretold: Unless the Lord of Sabbath had left us a seed, we had been made as Sodom and we had been like unto Gomorrha.€€ƒx…What then shall we say? That the Gentiles who followed not after justice have attained to justice, even the justice that is of faith.€€ƒxWBut Israel, by following after the law of justice, is not come unto the law of justice.€€ƒxnWhy so? Because they sought it not by faith, but as it were of works. For they stumbled at the stumblingstone.€€ƒx‡As it is written: Behold I lay in Sion a stumbling-stone and a rock of scandal. And whosoever believeth in him shall not be confounded.€€ƒ`€€ƒxWBrethren, the will of my heart, indeed and my prayer to God is for them unto salvation.€€ƒxUFor I bear them witness that they have a zeal of God, but not according to knowledge.€€ƒxFor they, not knowing the justice of God and seeking to establish their own, have not submitted themselves to the justice of God.€€•ƒx`For Moses wrote that the justice which is of the law: The man that shall do it shall live by it.€€ƒxƒBut the justice which is of faith, speaketh thus: Say not in thy heart: Who shall ascend into heaven? That is to bring Christ down;€€ƒxTOr who shall descend into the deep? That is, to bring up Christ again from the dead.€€ƒx„But what saith the scripture? The word is nigh thee; even in thy mouth and in thy heart. This is the word of faith, which we preach.€€ƒxŠFor if thou confess with thy mouth the Lord Jesus and believe in thy heart that God hath raised him up from the dead, thou shalt be saved.€€ƒxeFor, with the heart, we believe unto justice: but, with the mouth, confession is made unto salvation.€€ƒxLFor the scripture saith: Whosoever believeth in him shall not be confounded.€€ƒxvFor there is no distinction of the Jew and the Greek: for the same is Lord over all, rich unto all that call upon him.€€ƒxBFor whosoever shall call upon the name of the Lord shall be saved.€€ƒx¦How then shall they call on him in whom they have not believed? Or how shall they believe him of whom they have not heard? And how shall they hear without a preacher?€€ƒx´And how shall they preach unless they be sent, as it is written: How beautiful are the feet of them that preach the gospel of peace, of them that bring glad tidings of good things?€€ƒxUBut all do not obey the gospel. For Isaias saith: Lord, who hath believed our report?€€ƒx@Faith then cometh by hearing; and hearing by the word of Christ.€€ƒxBut I say: Have they not heard? Yes, verily: Their sound hath gone forth into all the earth: and their words unto the ends of the whole world.€€ƒx™But I say: Hath not Israel known? First, Moses saith: I will provoke you to jealousy by that which is not a nation: by a foolish nation I will anger you.€€ƒx{But Isaias is bold, and saith: I was found by them that did not seek me. I appeared openly to them that asked not after me.€€ƒxtBut to Israel he saith: All the day long have I spread my hands to a people that believeth not and contradicteth me.€€ƒ`€€ƒxƒI say then: Hath God cast away his people? God forbid! For I also am an Israelite of the seed of Abraham, of the tribe of Benjamin.€€ƒxŠGod hath not cast away his people which he foreknew. Know you not what the scripture saith of Elias, how he calleth on God against Israel?€€ƒxnLord, they have slain thy prophets, they have dug down thy altars. And I am left alone: and they seek my life.€€˜$ƒxeEven so then, at this present time also, there is a remnant saved according to the election of grace.€€ƒxJAnd if by grace, it is not now by works: otherwise grace is no more grace.€€ƒx}What then? That which Israel sought, he hath not obtained: but the election hath obtained it. And the rest have been blinded.€€ƒxœAs it is written: God hath given them the spirit of insensibility; eyes that they should not see and ears that they should not hear, until this present day.€€ƒxmAnd David saith: Let their table be made a snare and a trap and a stumbling block and a recompense unto them.€€ƒxRLet their eyes be darkened, that they may not see: and bow down their back always.€€ƒxŸI say then: Have they so stumbled, that they should fall? God forbid! But by their offence salvation is come to the Gentiles, that they may be emulous of them.€€ƒxNow if the offence of them be the riches of the world and the diminution of them the riches of the Gentiles: how much more the fulness of them?€€ƒxjFor I say to you, Gentiles: As long indeed as I am the apostle of the Gentiles, I will honour my ministry,€€ƒx]If, by any means, I may provoke to emulation them who are my flesh and may save some of them.€€ƒxxFor if the loss of them be the reconciliation of the world, what shall the receiving of them be, but life from the dead?€€ƒxaFor if the firstfruit be holy, so is the lump also: and if the root be holy, so are the branches.€€ƒx¡And if some of the branches be broken and thou, being a wild olive, art ingrafted in them and art made partaker of the root and of the fatness of the olive tree:€€ƒx`Boast not against the branches. But if thou boast, thou bearest not the root: but the root thee.€€ƒxLThou wilt say then: The branches were broken off that I might be grafted in.€€ƒxhWell: because of unbelief they were broken off. But thou standest by faith. Be not highminded, but fear.€€ƒxZFor if God hath not spared the natural branches, fear lest perhaps also he spare not thee.€€ƒxÉSee then the goodness and the severity of God: towards them indeed that are fallen, the severity; but towards thee, the goodness of God, if thou abide in goodness. Otherwise thou also shalt be cut off.€€ƒxpAnd they also, if they abide not still in unbelief, shall be grafted in: for God is able to graft them in again.€€ƒxçFor if thou were cut out of the wild olive tree, which is natural to thee; and, contrary to nature, wert grafted into the good olive tree: how much more shall they that are the natural branches be grafted into their own olive tree?€€ƒxÌFor I would not have you ignorant, brethren, of this mystery (lest you should be wise in your own conceits) that blindness in part has happened in Israel, until the fulness of the Gentiles should come in.€€ƒx”And so all Israel should be saved, as it is written: There shall come out of Sion, he that shall deliver and shall turn away ungodliness from Jacob.€€ƒxCAnd this is to them my covenant: when I shall take away their sins.€€ƒxAs concerning the gospel, indeed, they are enemies for your sake: but as touching the election, they are most dear for the sake of the fathers.€€ƒx<For the gifts and the calling of God are without repentance.€€ƒxgFor as you also in times past did not believe God, but now have obtained mercy, through their unbelief:€€ƒxUSo these also now have not believed, for your mercy, that they also may obtain mercy.€€ƒxFFor God hath concluded all in unbelief, that he may have mercy on all.€€ƒx‹O the depth of the riches of the wisdom and of the knowledge of God! How incomprehensible are his judgments, and how unsearchable his ways!€€ƒxIFor who hath known the mind of the Lord? Or who hath been his counsellor?€€ƒxAOr who hath first given to him, and recompense shall be made him?€€ƒxSFor of him, and by him, and in him, are all things: to him be glory for ever. Amen.€€ƒ`€€ƒxšI beseech you therefore, brethren, by the mercy of God, that you present your bodies a living sacrifice, holy, pleasing unto God, your reasonable service.€€ƒx¤And be not conformed to this world: but be reformed in the newness of your mind, that you may prove what is the good and the acceptable and the perfect will of God.€€ƒx×For I say, by the grace that is given me, to all that are among you, not to be more wise than it behoveth to be wise, but to be wise unto sobriety and according as God hath divided to every one the measure of faith.€€•ƒxPSo we, being many, are one body in Christ; and every one members one of another:€€ƒx€And having different gifts, according to the grace that is given us, either prophecy, to be used according to the rule of faith;€€ƒx>Or ministry, in ministering; or he that teacheth, in doctrine;€€ƒxHe that exhorteth, in exhorting; he that giveth, with simplicity; he that ruleth, with carefulness; he that sheweth mercy, with cheerfulness.€€ƒx]Let love be without dissimulation. Hating that which is evil, cleaving to that which is good,€€ƒxWLoving one another with the charity of brotherhood: with honour preventing one another.€€ƒxAIn carefulness not slothful. In spirit fervent. Serving the Lord.€€ƒx=Rejoicing in hope. Patient in tribulation. Instant in prayer.€€ƒxECommunicating to the necessities of the saints. Pursuing hospitality.€€ƒx4Bless them that persecute you: bless, and curse not.€€ƒx9Rejoice with them that rejoice: weep with them that weep.€€ƒxBeing of one mind one towards another. Not minding high things, but consenting to the humble. Be not wise in your own conceits.€€ƒxxTo no man rendering evil for evil. Providing good things, not only in the sight of God but also in the sight of all men.€€ƒxAIf it be possible, as much as is in you, have peace with all men.€€ƒx‡Revenge not yourselves, my dearly beloved; but give place unto wrath, for it is written: Revenge is mine, I will repay, saith the Lord.€€ƒx‹But if the enemy be hungry, give him to eat; if he thirst, give him to drink. For, doing this, thou shalt heap coals of fire upon his head.€€ƒx3Be not overcome by evil: but overcome evil by good.€€ƒ`€€ƒxsLet every soul be subject to higher powers. For there is no power but from God: and those that are ordained of God.€€ƒx}Therefore, he that resisteth the power resisteth the ordinance of God. And they that resist purchase to themselves damnation.€€ƒx«For princes are not a terror to the good work, but to the evil. Wilt thou then not be afraid of the power? Do that which is good: and thou shalt have praise from the same.€€ƒxUWherefore be subject of necessity: not only for wrath, but also for conscience' sake.€€ƒxaFor therefore also you pay tribute. For they are the ministers of God, serving unto this purpose.€€ƒxŒRender therefore to all men their dues. Tribute, to whom tribute is due: custom, to whom custom: fear, to whom fear: honour, to whom honour.€€ƒxgOwe no man any thing, but to love one another. For he that loveth his neighbour hath fulfilled the law.€€ƒx÷For: Thou shalt not commit adultery: Thou shalt not kill: Thou shalt not steal: Thou shalt not bear false witness: Thou shalt not covet. And if there be any other commandment, it is comprised in this word: Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself.€€ƒxWThe love of our neighbour worketh no evil. Love therefore is the fulfilling of the law.€€ƒx‡And that, knowing the season, that it is now the hour for us to rise from sleep. For now our salvation is nearer than when we believed.€€ƒx|The night is passed And the day is at hand. Let us, therefore cast off the works of darkness and put on the armour of light.€€ƒx‚Let us walk honestly, as in the day: not in rioting and drunkenness, not in chambering and impurities, not in contention and envy.€€ƒx`But put ye on the Lord Jesus Christ: and make not provision for the flesh in its concupiscences.€€ƒ`€€ƒxMNow him that is weak in faith, take unto you: not in disputes about thoughts.€€ƒxUFor one believeth that he may eat all things: but he that is weak, let him eat herbs.€€ƒxLet not him that eateth despise him that eateth not: and he that eateth not, let him not judge him that eateth. For God hath taken him to him.€€—ƒxjFor one judgeth between day and day: and another judgeth every day. Let every man abound in his own sense.€€ƒxÅHe that regardeth the day regardeth it unto the Lord. And he that eateth eateth to the Lord: for he giveth thanks to God. And he that eateth not, to the Lord he eateth not and giveth thanks to God.€€ƒx>For none of us liveth to himself: and no man dieth to himself.€€ƒx•For whether we live, we live unto the Lord: or whether we die, we die unto the Lord. Therefore, whether we live or whether we die, we are the Lord's.€€ƒxeFor to this end Christ died and rose again: that he might be Lord both of the dead and of the living.€€ƒxBut thou, why judgest thou thy brother? Or thou, why dost thou despise thy brother? For we shall all stand before the judgment seat of Christ.€€ƒxoFor it is written: As I live, saith the Lord, every knee shall bow to me and every tongue shall confess to God.€€ƒxBTherefore every one of us shall render account to God for himself.€€ƒxLet us not therefore judge one another any more. But judge this rather, that you put not a stumblingblock or a scandal in your brother's way.€€ƒx—I know, and am confident in the Lord Jesus, that nothing is unclean of itself: but to him that esteemeth any thing to be unclean, to him it is unclean.€€ƒx”For if, because of thy meat, thy brother be grieved, thou walkest not now according to charity. Destroy not him with thy meat, for whom Christ died.€€ƒx(Let not then our good be evil spoken of.€€ƒx^For the kingdom of God is not meat and drink: but justice and peace and joy in the Holy Ghost.€€ƒxGFor he that in this serveth Christ pleaseth God and is approved of men.€€ƒx}Therefore, let us follow after the things that are of peace and keep the things that are of edification, one towards another.€€ƒxwDestroy not the work of God for meat. All things indeed are clean: but it is evil for that man who eateth with offence.€€ƒx}It is good not to eat flesh and not to drink wine: nor any thing whereby thy brother is offended or scandalized or made weak.€€ƒxtHast thou faith? Have it to thyself before God. Blessed is he that condemneth not himself in that which he alloweth.€€ƒxkBut he that discerneth, if he eat, is condemned; because not of faith. For all that is not of faith is sin.€€ƒ`€€ƒx`Now, we that are stronger ought to bear the infirmities of the weak and not to please ourselves.€€ƒxDLet every one of you Please his neighbour unto good, to edification.€€ƒxsFor Christ did not please himself: but, as it is written: The reproaches of them that reproached thee fell upon me.€€˜!ƒxsNow the God of patience and of comfort grant you to be of one mind, one towards another, according to Jesus Christ:€€ƒxbThat with one mind and with one mouth you may glorify God and the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒxYWherefore, receive one another, as Christ also hath received you, unto the honour of God.€€ƒxFor I say that Christ Jesus was minister of the circumcision for the truth of God, to confirm the promises made unto the fathers:€€ƒx¡But that the Gentiles are to glorify God for his mercy, as it is written: Therefore will I confess to thee, O Lord, among the Gentiles and will sing to thy name.€€ƒx9And again he saith: rejoice ye Gentiles, with his people.€€ƒxLAnd again: praise the Lord, all ye Gentiles: and magnify him, all ye people.€€ƒxˆAnd again, Isaias saith: There shall be a root of Jesse; and he that shall rise up to rule the Gentiles, in him the Gentiles shall hope.€€ƒxNow the God of hope fill you with all joy and peace in believing: that you may abound in hope and in the power of the Holy Ghost.€€ƒxŸAnd I myself also, my brethren, am assured of you that you also are full of love, replenished with all knowledge, so that you are able to admonish one another.€€ƒxBut I have written to you, brethren, more boldly in some sort, as it were putting you in mind, because of the grace which is given me from God,€€ƒx»That I should be the minister of Christ Jesus among the Gentiles: sanctifying the gospel of God, that the oblation of the Gentiles may be made acceptable and sanctified in the Holy Ghost.€€ƒx3I have therefore glory in Christ Jesus towards God.€€ƒxƒFor I dare not to speak of any of those things which Christ worketh not by me, for the obedience of the Gentiles, by word and deed,€€ƒxªBy the virtue of signs and wonders, in the power of the Holy Ghost, so that from Jerusalem round about, as far as unto Illyricum, I have replenished the gospel of Christ.€€ƒxrAnd I have so preached this gospel, not where Christ was named, lest I should build upon another man a foundation.€€ƒxqBut as it is written: They to whom he was not spoken of shall see: and they that have not heard shall understand.€€ƒxcFor which cause also, I was hindered very much from coming to you and have been kept away till now.€€ƒxrBut now, having no more place in these countries and having a great desire these many years past to come unto you,€€ƒx¯When I shall begin to take my journey into Spain, I hope that, as I pass, I shall see you and be brought on my way thither by you: if first, in part, I shall have enjoyed you.€€ƒx=But now I shall go to Jerusalem, to minister unto the saints.€€ƒxyFor it hath pleased them of Macedonia and Achaia to make a contribution for the poor of the saints that are in Jerusalem.€€ƒx´For it hath pleased them: and they are their debtors. For, if the Gentiles have been made partakers of their spiritual things, they ought also in carnal things to minister to them.€€ƒxnWhen therefore I shall have accomplished this and consigned to them this fruit, I will come by you into Spain.€€ƒxiAnd I know that when I come to you I shall come in the abundance of the blessing of the gospel of Christ.€€ƒx–I beseech you therefore, brethren, through our Lord Jesus Christ and by the charity of the Holy Ghost, that you help me in your prayers for me to God,€€ƒxThat I may be delivered from the unbelievers that are in Judea and that the oblation of my service may be acceptable in Jerusalem to the saints.€€ƒxSThat I may come to you with joy, by the will of God, and may be refreshed with you.€€ƒx,Now the God of peace be with, you all. Amen.€€ƒ`€€ƒxbAnd I commend to you Phebe, our sister, who is in the ministry of the church, that is in Cenchrae:€€ƒx°That you receive her in the Lord as becometh saints and that you assist her in whatsoever business she shall have need of you. For she also hath assisted many, and myself also.€€ƒx5Salute Prisca and Aquila, my helpers, in Christ Jesus€€˜ƒxnAnd the church which is in their house. Salute Epenetus, my beloved: who is the firstfruits of Asia in Christ.€€ƒx.Salute Mary, who hath laboured much among you.€€ƒx…Salute Andronicus and Junias, my kinsmen and fellow prisoners: who are of note among the apostles, who also were in Christ before me.€€ƒx1Salute Ampliatus, most beloved to me in the Lord.€€ƒxCSalute Urbanus, our helper in Christ Jesus and Stachys, my beloved.€€ƒx#Salute Apellas, approved in Christ.€€ƒxSalute them that are of Aristobulus' household. Salute Herodian, my kinsman. Salute them that are of Narcissus' household, who are in the Lord.€€ƒx}Salute Tryphaena and Tryphosa, who labour in the Lord. Salute Persis, the dearly beloved, who hath much laboured in the Lord.€€ƒx9Salute Rufus, elect in the Lord, and his mother and mine.€€ƒxZSalute Asyncritus, Phlegon, Hermas, Patrobas, Hermes: and the brethren that are with them.€€ƒxhSalute Philologus and Julia, Nereus and his sister, and Olympias: and all the saints that are with them.€€ƒxLSalute one another with an holy kiss. All the churches of Christ salute you.€€ƒx‹Now I beseech you, brethren, to mark them who make dissensions and offences contrary to the doctrine which you have learned and avoid them.€€ƒxFor they that are such serve not Christ our Lord but their own belly: and by pleasing speeches and good words seduce the hearts of the innocent.€€ƒx‡For your obedience is published in every place. I rejoice therefore in you. But I would have you to be wise in good and simple in evil.€€ƒxjAnd the God of peace crush Satan under your feet speedily. The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you.€€ƒxZTimothy, my fellow labourer, saluteth you: and Lucius and Jason and Sosipater, my kinsmen.€€ƒx;I, Tertius, who wrote this epistle, salute you in the Lord.€€ƒx|Caius, my host, and the whole church saluteth you. Erastus, the treasurer of the city, saluteth you: and Quartus, a brother.€€ƒx9The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you all. Amen.€€ƒx³Now to him that is able to establish you, according to my gospel and the preaching of Jesus Christ, according to the revelation of the mystery which was kept secret from eternity;€€ƒx (Which now is made manifest by the scriptures of the prophets, according to the precept of the eternal God, for the obedience of faith) known among all nations:€€ƒxaTo God, the only wise, through Jesus Christ, to whom be honour and glory for ever and ever. Amen.€€ƒx#The First Letter to the Corinthians€€ƒ`€€ƒxZPaul, called to be an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God, and Sosthenes a brother,€€ƒxÄTo the church of God that is at Corinth, to them that are sanctified in Christ Jesus, called to be saints, with all that invoke the name of our Lord Jesus Christ in every place of theirs and ours.€€d1Cor¤dnamemI Corinthiansiosis_named1Corlabbreviationd1Corhchapters˜ƒxSThat in all things you are made rich in him, in all utterance and in all knowledge;€€ƒx0As the testimony of Christ was confirmed in you,€€ƒxgSo that nothing is wanting to you in any grace, waiting for the manifestation of our Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒxiWho also will confirm you unto the end without crime, in the days of the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒx^God is faithful: by whom you are called unto the fellowship of his Son, Jesus Christ our Lord.€€ƒxÏNow I beseech you, brethren, by the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, that you all speak the same thing and that there be no schisms among you: but that you be perfect in the same mind and in the same judgment.€€ƒx†For it hath been signified unto me, my brethren, of you, by them that are of the house of Chloe, that there are contentions among you.€€ƒxwNow this I say, that every one of you saith: I indeed am of Paul; and I am of Apollo; and I of Cephas; and I of Christ.€€ƒx]Is Christ divided? Was Paul then crucified for you? Or were you baptized in the name of Paul?€€ƒxEI give God thanks, that I baptized none of you but Crispus and Caius:€€ƒx6Lest any should say that you were baptized in my name.€€ƒxaAnd I baptized also the household of Stephanus. Besides, I know not whether I baptized any other.€€ƒxƒFor Christ sent me not to baptize, but to preach the gospel: not in wisdom of speech, lest the cross of Christ should be made void.€€ƒxŠFor the word of the cross, to them indeed that perish, is foolishness: but to them that are saved, that is, to us, it is the power of God.€€ƒxhFor it is written: I will destroy the wisdom of the wise: and the prudence of the prudent I will reject.€€ƒx€Where is the wise? Where is the scribe? Where is the disputer of this world? Hath not God made foolish the wisdom of this world?€€ƒxšFor, seeing that in the wisdom of God, the world, by wisdom, knew not God, it pleased God, by the foolishness of our preaching, to save them that believe.€€ƒxBFor both the Jews require signs: and the Greeks seek after wisdom.€€ƒxiBut we preach Christ crucified: unto the Jews indeed a stumblingblock, and unto the Gentiles foolishness:€€ƒxdBut unto them that are called, both Jews and Greeks, Christ, the power of God and the wisdom of God.€€ƒx[For the foolishness of God is wiser than men: and the weakness of God is stronger than men.€€ƒxvFor see your vocation, brethren, that there are not many wise according to the flesh, not many mighty, not many noble.€€ƒx¦But the foolish things of the world hath God chosen, that he may confound the wise: and the weak things of the world hath God chosen, that he may confound the strong.€€ƒxŸAnd the base things of the world and the things that are contemptible, hath God chosen: and things that are not, that he might bring to nought things that are:€€ƒx(That no flesh should glory in his sight.€€ƒxtBut of him are you in Christ Jesus, who of God is made unto us wisdom and justice and sanctification and redemption:€€ƒx?That, as it is written: He that glorieth may glory in the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx~And I, brethren, when I came to you, came not in loftiness of speech or of wisdom, declaring unto you the testimony of Christ.€€ƒxXFor I judged not myself to know anything among you, but Jesus Christ: and him crucified.€€ƒxAAnd I was with you in weakness and in fear and in much trembling.€€ƒxNThat your faith might not stand on the wisdom of men, but on the power of God.€€ƒx†Howbeit we speak wisdom among the perfect: yet not the wisdom of this world, neither of the princes of this world that come to nought.€€ƒx{But we speak the wisdom of God in a mystery, a wisdom which is hidden, which God ordained before the world, unto our glory:€€ƒxzWhich none of the princes of this world knew. For if they had known it, they would never have crucified the Lord of glory.€€ƒx¢But, as it is written: That eye hath not seen, nor ear heard: neither hath it entered into the heart of man, what things God hath prepared for them that love him.€€ƒxqBut to us God hath revealed them by his Spirit. For the Spirit searcheth all things, yea, the deep things of God.€€ƒxœFor what man knoweth the things of a man, but the spirit of a man that is in him? So the things also that are of God, no man knoweth, but the Spirit of God.€€ƒxŠNow, we have received not the spirit of this world, but the Spirit that is of God: that we may know the things that are given us from God.€€ƒx“Which things also we speak: not in the learned words of human wisdom, but in the doctrine of the Spirit, comparing spiritual things with spiritual.€€ƒxªBut the sensual man perceiveth not these things that are of the Spirit of God. For it is foolishness to him: and he cannot understand, because it is spiritually examined.€€ƒxMBut the spiritual man judgeth all things: and he himself is judged of no man.€€ƒxbFor who hath known the mind of the Lord, that he may instruct him? But we have the mind of Christ.€€ƒ`€€ƒxmAnd I, brethren, could not speak to you as unto spiritual, but as unto carnal. As unto little ones in Christ.€€ƒx~I gave you milk to drink, not meat: for you were not able as yet. But neither indeed are you now able: for you are yet carnal.€€ƒxmFor, whereas there is among you envying and contention, are you not carnal and walk you not according to man?€€—ƒxUThe ministers of him whom you have believed: and to every one as the Lord hath given.€€ƒx:I have planted, Apollo watered: but God gave the increase.€€ƒxiTherefore, neither he that planteth is any thing, nor he that watereth: but God that giveth the increase.€€ƒx|Now he that planteth and he that watereth, are one. And every man shall receive his own reward, according to his own labour.€€ƒxMFor we are God's coadjutors. You are God's husbandry: you are God's building.€€ƒx¸According to the grace of God that is given to me, as a wise architect, I have laid the foundation: and another buildeth thereon. But let every man take heed how he buildeth thereupon.€€ƒxSFor other foundation no man can lay, but that which is laid: which is Christ Jesus.€€ƒx^Now, if any man build upon this foundation, gold, silver, precious stones, wood, hay, stubble:€€ƒx°Every man's work shall be manifest. For the day of the Lord shall declare it, because it shall be revealed in fire. And the fire shall try every man's work, of what sort it is.€€ƒxRIf any man's work abide, which he hath built thereupon, he shall receive a reward.€€ƒx^If any mans work burn, he shall suffer loss: but he himself shall be saved, yet so as by fire.€€ƒxWKnow you not that you are the temple of God and that the Spirit of God dwelleth in you?€€ƒxnBut if any man violate the temple of God, him shall God destroy. For the temple of God is holy, which you are.€€ƒx{Let no man deceive himself. If any man among you seem to be wise in this world, let him become a fool, that he may be wise.€€ƒxwFor the wisdom of this world is foolishness with God. For it is written: I will catch the wise in their own craftiness.€€ƒxIAnd again: The Lord knoweth the thoughts of the wise, that they are vain.€€ƒx"Let no man therefore glory in men.€€ƒx›For all things are yours, whether it be Paul or Apollo or Cephas, or the world, or life, or death, or things present, or things to come. For all are yours.€€ƒx*And you are Christ's. And Christ is God's.€€ƒ`€€ƒxdLet a man so account of us as of the ministers of Christ and the dispensers of the mysteries of God.€€ƒxJHere now it is required among the dispensers that a man be found faithful.€€ƒxkBut to me it is a very small thing to be judged by you or by man's day. But neither do I judge my own self.€€•ƒx×Therefore, judge not before the time: until the Lord come, who both will bring to light the hidden things of darkness and will make manifest the counsels of the hearts. And then shall every man have praise from God.€€ƒxÒBut these things, brethren, I have in a figure transferred to myself and to Apollo, for your sakes: that in us you may learn that one be not puffed up against the other for another, above that which is written.€€ƒxFor who distinguisheth thee? Or what hast thou that thou hast not received, and if thou hast received, why dost thou glory, as if thou hadst not received it?€€ƒx…You are now full: you are now become rich: you reign without us; and I would to God you did reign, that we also might reign with you.€€ƒxœFor I think that God hath set forth us apostles, the last, as it were men appointed to death. We are made a spectacle to the world and to angels and to men.€€ƒx†We are fools for Christs sake, but you are wise in Christ: we are weak, but you are strong: you are honourable, but we without honour.€€ƒxeEven unto this hour we both hunger and thirst and are naked and are buffeted and have no fixed abode.€€ƒxmAnd we labour, working with our own hands. We are reviled: and we bless. We are persecuted: and we suffer it.€€ƒxsWe are blasphemed: and we entreat. We are made as the refuse of this world, the offscouring of all, even until now.€€ƒxTI write not these things to confound you: but I admonish you as my dearest children.€€ƒx‚For if you have ten thousand instructors in Christ, yet not many fathers. For in Christ Jesus, by the gospel, I have begotten you.€€ƒxGWherefore, I beseech you, be ye followers of me as I also am of Christ.€€ƒxÂFor this cause have I sent to you Timothy, who is my dearest son and faithful in the Lord. Who will put you in mind of my ways, which are in Christ Jesus: as I teach every where in every church.€€ƒx5As if I would not come to you, so some are puffed up.€€ƒxzBut I will come to you shortly, if the Lord will: and will know, not the speech of them that are puffed up, but the power.€€ƒx6For the kingdom of God is not in speech, but in power.€€ƒx[What will you? Shall I come to you with a rod? Or in charity and in the spirit of meekness?€€ƒ`€€ƒxIt is absolutely heard that there is fornication among you and such fornication as the like is not among the heathens: that one should have his father's wife.€€ƒxxAnd you are puffed up and have not rather mourned: that he might be taken away from among you that hath done this thing.€€ƒxuI indeed, absent in body but present in spirit, have already judged, as though I were present, him that hath so done,€€ƒx‚To deliver such a one to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved in the day of our Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒxWYour glorying is not good. Know you not that a little leaven corrupteth the whole lump?€€ƒxqPurge out the old leaven, that you may be a new paste, as you are unleavened. For Christ our pasch is sacrificed.€€ƒx•Therefore, let us feast, not with the old leaven, nor with the leaven of malice and wickedness: but with the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth.€€ƒxCI wrote to you in an epistle, not to keep company with fornicators.€€ƒxI mean not with the fornicators of this world or with the covetous or the extortioners or the servers of idols: otherwise you must needs go out of this world.€€ƒxÜBut now I have written to you, not to keep company, if any man that is named a brother be a fornicator or covetous or a server of idols or a railer or a drunkard or an extortioner: with such a one, not so much as to eat.€€ƒx\For what have I to do to judge them that are without? Do not you judge them that are within?€€ƒxWFor them that are without, God will judge. Put away the evil one from among yourselves.€€ƒ`€€ƒxoDare any of you, having a matter against another, go to be judged before the unjust: and not before the saints?€€ƒxKnow you not that the saints shall judge this world? And if the world shall be judged by you, are you unworthy to judge the smallest matters?€€ƒxLKnow you not that we shall judge angels? How much more things of this world?€€”ƒxxI speak to your shame. Is it so that there is not among you any one wise man that is able to judge between his brethren?€€ƒxCBut brother goeth to law with brother: and that before unbelievers.€€ƒx·Already indeed there is plainly a fault among you, that you have law suits one with another. Why do you not rather take wrong? Why do you not rather suffer yourselves to be defrauded?€€ƒx8But you do wrong and defraud: and that to your brethren.€€ƒx€Know you not that the unjust shall not possess the kingdom of God? Do not err: Neither fornicators nor idolaters nor adulterers:€€ƒxNor the effeminate nor liers with mankind nor thieves nor covetous nor drunkards nor railers nor extortioners shall possess the kingdom of God.€€ƒxAnd such some of you were. But you are washed: but you are sanctified: but you are justified: in the name of our Lord Jesus Christ and the Spirit of our God.€€ƒxAll things are lawful to me: but all things are not expedient. All things are lawful to me: but I will not be brought under the power of any.€€ƒx©Meat for the belly and the belly for the meats: but God shall destroy both it and them. But the body is not for fornication, but for the Lord: and the Lord for the body.€€ƒxGNow God hath raised up the Lord and will raise us up also by his power.€€ƒx”Know you not that your bodies are the members of Christ? Shall I then take the members of Christ and make them the members of an harlot? God forbid!€€ƒxrOr know you not that he who is joined to a harlot is made one body? For they shall be, saith he, two in one flesh.€€ƒx/But he who is joined to the Lord is one spirit.€€ƒx€Fly fornication. Every sin that a man doth is without the body: but he that committeth fornication sinneth against his own body.€€ƒx„Or know you not that your members are the temple of the Holy Ghost, who is in you, whom you have from God: and you are not your own?€€ƒxIFor you are bought with a great price. Glorify and bear God in your body.€€ƒ`€€ƒx]Now concerning the things whereof you wrote to me: It is good for a man not to touch a woman.€€ƒxgBut for fear of fornication, let every man have his own wife: and let every woman have her own husband.€€ƒx]Let the husband render the debt to his wife: and the wife also in like manner to the husband.€€˜(ƒx°Defraud not one another, except, perhaps, by consent, for a time, that you may give yourselves to prayer: and return together again, lest Satan tempt you for your incontinency.€€ƒx3But I speak this by indulgence, not by commandment.€€ƒx‰For I would that all men were even as myself. But every one hath his proper gift from God: one after this manner, and another after that.€€ƒxaBut I say to the unmarried and to the widows: It is good for them if they so continue, even as I.€€ƒxbBut if they do not contain themselves, let them marry. For it is better to marry than to be burnt.€€ƒxhBut to them that are married, not I, but the Lord, commandeth that the wife depart not from her husband.€€ƒxxAnd if she depart, that she remain unmarried or be reconciled to her husband. And let not the husband put away his wife.€€ƒx‘For to the rest I speak, not the Lord. If any brother hath a wife that believeth not and she consent to dwell with him: let him not put her away.€€ƒxvAnd if any woman hath a husband that believeth not and he consent to dwell with her: let her not put away her husband.€€ƒxÃFor the unbelieving husband is sanctified by the believing wife: and the unbelieving wife is sanctified by the believing husband. Otherwise your children should be unclean: but now they are holy.€€ƒxŒBut if the unbeliever depart, let him depart. For a brother or sister is not under servitude in such cases. But God hath called us in peace.€€ƒx€For how knowest thou, O wife, whether thou shalt save thy husband? Or how knowest thou, O man, whether thou shalt save thy wife?€€ƒx}But as the Lord hath distributed to every one, as God hath called every one: so let him walk. And so in all churches I teach.€€ƒxŠIs any man called, being circumcised? Let him not procure uncircumcision. Is any man called in uncircumcision? Let him not be circumcised.€€ƒxeCircumcision is nothing and uncircumcision is nothing: but the observance of the commandments of God.€€ƒx?Let every man abide in the same calling in which he was called.€€ƒxcWast thou called, being a bondman? Care not for it: but if thou mayest be made free, use it rather.€€ƒx‘For he that is called in the Lord, being a bondman, is the freeman of the Lord. Likewise he that is called, being free, is the bondman of Christ.€€ƒx?You are bought with a price: be not made the bondslaves of men.€€ƒxGBrethren, let every man, wherein he was called, therein abide with God.€€ƒx…Now, concerning virgins, I have no commandment of the Lord: but I give counsel, as having obtained mercy of the Lord, to be faithful.€€ƒxbI think therefore that this is good for the present necessity: that it is good for a man so to be.€€ƒx^Art thou bound to a wife? Seek not to be loosed. Art thou loosed from a wife? Seek not a wife.€€ƒx£But if thou take a wife, thou hast not sinned. And if a virgin marry, she hath not sinned: nevertheless, such shall have tribulation of the flesh. But I spare you.€€ƒxvThis therefore I say, brethren: The time is short. It remaineth, that they also who have wives be as if they had none:€€ƒxˆAnd they that weep, as though they wept not: and they that rejoice, as if they rejoiced not: and they that buy as if they possessed not:€€ƒxaAnd they that use this world, as if they used it not. For the fashion of this world passeth away.€€ƒx•But I would have you to be without solicitude. He that is without a wife is solicitous for the things that belong to the Lord: how he may please God.€€ƒxtBut he that is with a wife is solicitous for the things of the world: how he may please his wife. And he is divided.€€ƒxØAnd the unmarried woman and the virgin thinketh on the things of the Lord: that she may be holy both in body and in spirit. But she that is married thinketh on the things of the world: how she may please her husband.€€ƒx¦And this I speak for your profit, not to cast a snare upon you, but for that which is decent and which may give you power to attend upon the Lord, without impediment.€€ƒx³But if any man think that he seemeth dishonoured with regard to his virgin, for that she is above the age, and it must so be: let him do what he will. He sinneth not if she marry.€€ƒx³For he that hath determined, being steadfast in his heart, having no necessity, but having power of his own will: and hath judged this in his heart, to keep his virgin, doth well.€€ƒxgTherefore both he that giveth his virgin in marriage doth well: and he that giveth her not doth better.€€ƒx—A woman is bound by the law as long as her husband liveth: but if her husband die, she is at liberty. Let her marry to whom she will: only in the Lord.€€ƒxyBut more blessed shall she be, if she so remain, according to my counsel. And I think that I also have the spirit of God.€€ƒ`€€ƒx„Now concerning those things that are sacrificed to idols: we know we all have knowledge. Knowledge puffeth up: but charity edifieth.€€ƒxZAnd if any man think that he knoweth any thing, he hath not yet known as he ought to know.€€ƒx2But if any man love God, the same is known by him.€€ƒxqFor although there be that are called gods, either in heaven or on earth (for there be gods many and lords many):€€ƒx–Yet to us there is but one God, the Father, of whom are all things, and we unto him: and one Lord Jesus Christ, by whom are all things, and we by him.€€ƒx¶But there is not knowledge in every one. For some until this present, with conscience of the idol, eat as a thing sacrificed to an idol: and their conscience, being weak, is defiled.€€ƒx€But meat doth not commend us to God. For neither, if we eat, shall we have the more: nor, if we eat not, shall we have the less.€€ƒxQBut take heed lest perhaps this your liberty become a stumblingblock to the weak.€€ƒx±For if a man see him that hath knowledge sit at meat in the idol's temple, shall not his conscience, being weak, be emboldened to eat those things which are sacrificed to idols?€€ƒxSAnd through thy knowledge shall the weak brother perish, for whom Christ hath died?€€ƒxcNow when you sin thus against the brethren and wound their weak conscience, you sin against Christ.€€ƒxfWherefore, if meat scandalize my brother, I will never eat flesh, lest I should scandalize my brother.€€ƒ`€€ƒxmAm I not I free? Am not I an apostle? Have not I seen Christ Jesus our Lord? Are not you my work in the Lord?€€ƒxpAnd if unto others I be not an apostle, but yet to you I am. For you are the seal of my apostleship in the Lord.€€ƒx0My defence with them that do examine me is this.€€˜ƒxHave we not power to carry about a woman, a sister as well as the rest of the apostles and the brethren of the Lord and Cephas?€€ƒx5Or I only and Barnabas, have not we power to do this?€€ƒx¶Who serveth as a soldier, at any time, at his own charges? Who planteth a vineyard and eateth not of the fruit thereof? Who feedeth the flock and eateth not of the milk of the flock?€€ƒxRSpeak I these things according to man? Or doth not the law also say; these things?€€ƒx‰For it is written in the law of Moses: Thou shalt not muzzle the mouth of the ox that treadeth out the corn. Doth God take care for oxen?€€ƒx´Or doth he say this indeed for our sakes? For these things are written for our sakes: that he that plougheth, should plough in hope and he that thrasheth, in hope to receive fruit.€€ƒx^If we have sown unto you spiritual things, is it a great matter if we reap your carnal things?€€ƒx¿If others be partakers of this power over you, why not we rather? Nevertheless, we have not used this power: but we bear all things, lest we should give any hindrance to the gospel of Christ.€€ƒx’Know you not that they who work in the holy place eat the things that are of the holy place; and they that serve the altar partake with the altar?€€ƒxTSo also the Lord ordained that they who preach the gospel should live by the gospel.€€ƒxÀBut I have used none of these things. Neither have I written these things, that they should be so done unto me: for it is good for me to die rather than that any man should make my glory void.€€ƒxFor if I preach the gospel, it is no glory to me: for a necessity lieth upon me. For woe is unto me if I preach not the gospel.€€ƒxmFor if I do this thing willingly, I have a reward: but if against my will, a dispensation is committed to me.€€ƒx„What is my reward then? That preaching the gospel, I may deliver the gospel without charge, that I abuse not my power in the gospel.€€ƒx_For whereas I was free as to all, I made myself the servant of all, that I might gain the more.€€ƒx;And I became to the Jews a Jew, that I might gain the Jews:€€ƒyPTo them that are under the law, as if I were under the law, (whereas myself was not under the law,) that I might gain them that were under the law. To them that were without the law, as if I were without the law, (whereas I was not without the law of God, but was in the law of Christ,) that I might gain them that were without the law.€€ƒxmTo the weak I became weak, that I might gain the weak. I became all things to all men, that I might save all.€€ƒxOAnd I do all things for the gospel's sake, that I may be made partaker thereof.€€ƒxuKnow you not that they that run in the race, all run indeed, but one receiveth the prize. So run that you may obtain.€€ƒx§And every one that striveth for the mastery refraineth himself from all things. And they indeed that they may receive a corruptible crown: but we an incorruptible one.€€ƒxUI therefore so run, not as at an uncertainty: I so fight, not as one beating the air.€€ƒx…But I chastise my body and bring it into subjection: lest perhaps, when I have preached to others, I myself should become a castaway.€€ƒ`€€ƒxwFor I would not have you ignorant, brethren, that our fathers were all under the cloud: and all passed through the sea.€€ƒx<And all in Moses were baptized, in the cloud and in the sea:€€ƒx(And did all eat the same spiritual food:€€˜!ƒxWBut with most of them God was not well pleased: for they were overthrown in the desert.€€ƒxiNow these things were done in a figure of us, that we should not covet evil things, as they also coveted.€€ƒxyNeither become ye idolaters, as some of them, as it is written: The people sat down to eat and drink and rose up to play.€€ƒxƒNeither let us commit fornication, as some of them that committed fornication: and there fell in one day three and twenty thousand.€€ƒxQNeither let us tempt Christ, as some of them tempted and perished by the serpent.€€ƒxTNeither do you murmur, as some of them murmured and were destroyed by the destroyer.€€ƒxƒNow all these things happened to them in figure: and they are written for our correction, upon whom the ends of the world are come.€€ƒxMWherefore, he that thinketh himself to stand, let him take heed lest he fall.€€ƒxŞLet no temptation take hold on you, but such as is human. And God is faithful, who will not suffer you to be tempted above that which you are able: but will make also with temptation issue, that you may be able to bear it.€€ƒx<Wherefore, my dearly beloved, fly from the service of idols.€€ƒx7I speak as to wise men: judge ye yourselves what I say.€€ƒx©The chalice of benediction which we bless, is it not the communion of the blood of Christ? And the bread which we break, is it not the partaking of the body of the Lord?€€ƒxKFor we, being many, are one bread, one body: all that partake of one bread.€€ƒxeBehold Israel according to the flesh. Are not they that eat of the sacrifices partakers of the altar?€€ƒxkWhat then? Do I say that what is offered in sacrifice to idols is any thing? Or that the idol is any thing?€€ƒx”But the things which the heathens sacrifice, they sacrifice to devils and not to God. And I would not that you should be made partakers with devils.€€ƒxYou cannot drink the chalice of the Lord and the chalice of devils: you cannot be partakers of the table of the Lord and of the table of devils.€€ƒx|Do we provoke the Lord to jealousy? Are we stronger than he? All things are lawful for me: but all things are not expedient.€€ƒx:All things are lawful for me: but all things do not edify.€€ƒx5Let no man seek his own, but that which is another's.€€ƒxQWhatsoever is sold in the shambles, eat: asking no question for conscience' sake.€€ƒx0The earth is the Lord's and the fulness thereof.€€ƒx˜If any of them that believe not, invite you, and you be willing to go: eat of any thing that is set before you, asking no question for conscience' sake.€€ƒx|But if any man say: This has been sacrificed to idols: do not eat of it, for his sake that told it and for conscience' sake.€€ƒxiConscience I say, not thy own, but the other's. For why is my liberty judged by another man's conscience?€€ƒxYIf I partake with thanksgiving, why am I evil spoken of for that for which I give thanks?€€ƒx[Therefore, whether you eat or drink, or whatsoever else you do, do all to the glory of God.€€ƒxLBe without offence to the Jew, and to the Gentiles and to the church of God:€€ƒx{As I also in all things please all men, not seeking that which is profitable to myself but to many: that they may be saved.€€ƒ`€€ƒx.Be ye followers of me, as I also am of Christ.€€ƒx|Now I praise you, brethren, that in all things you are mindful of me and keep my ordinances as I have delivered them to you.€€ƒx„But I would have you know that the head of every man is Christ: and the head of the woman is the man: and the head of Christ is God.€€˜"ƒx~But every woman praying or prophesying with her head not covered disgraceth her head: for it is all one as if she were shaven.€€ƒx‚For if a woman be not covered, let her be shorn. But if it be a shame to a woman to be shorn or made bald, let her cover her head.€€ƒx|The man indeed ought not to cover his head: because he is the image and glory of God. But the woman is the glory of the man.€€ƒx:For the man is not of the woman: but the woman of the man.€€ƒxEFor the man was not created for the woman: but the woman for the man.€€ƒxOTherefore ought the woman to have a power over her head, because of the angels.€€ƒxYBut yet neither is the man without the woman, nor the woman without the man, in the Lord.€€ƒxWFor as the woman is of the man, so also is the man by the woman: but all things of God.€€ƒxHYou yourselves judge. Doth it become a woman to pray unto God uncovered?€€ƒxhDoth not even nature itself teach you that a man indeed, if he nourish his hair, it is a shame unto him?€€ƒxcBut if a woman nourish her hair, it is a glory to her; for her hair is given to her for a covering.€€ƒxUBut if any man seem to be contentious, we have no such custom, nor the Church of God.€€ƒxcNow this I ordain: not praising you, that you come together, not for the better, but for the worse.€€ƒxyFor first of all I hear that when you come together in the church, there are schisms among you. And in part I believe it.€€ƒxaFor there must be also heresies: that they also, who are approved may be made manifest among you.€€ƒxXWhen you come therefore together into one place, it is not now to eat the Lord's supper.€€ƒxaFor every one taketh before his own supper to eat. And one indeed is hungry and another is drunk.€€ƒx¹What, have you no houses to eat and to drink in? Or despise ye the church of God and put them to shame that have not? What shall I say to you? Do I praise you? In this I praise you not.€€ƒxFor I have received of the Lord that which also I delivered unto you, that the Lord Jesus, the same night in which he was betrayed, took bread,€€ƒx‹And giving thanks, broke and said: Take ye and eat: This is my body, which shall be delivered for you. This do for the commemoration of me.€€ƒx²In like manner also the chalice, after he had supped, saying: This chalice is the new testament in my blood. This do ye, as often as you shall drink, for the commemoration of me.€€ƒxtFor as often as you shall eat this bread and drink the chalice, you shall shew the death of the Lord, until he come.€€ƒx‘Therefore, whosoever shall eat this bread, or drink the chalice of the Lord unworthily, shall be guilty of the body and of the blood of the Lord.€€ƒxWBut let a man prove himself: and so let him eat of that bread and drink of the chalice.€€ƒxxFor he that eateth and drinketh unworthily eateth and drinketh judgment to himself, not discerning the body of the Lord.€€ƒxCTherefore are there many infirm and weak among you: and many sleep.€€ƒx9But if we would judge ourselves, we should not be judged.€€ƒxaBut whilst we are judged, we are chastised by the Lord, that we be not condemned with this world.€€ƒxLWherefore, my brethren, when you come together to eat, wait for one another.€€ƒxƒIf any man be hungry, let him eat at home; that you come not together unto judgment. And the rest I will set in order, when I come.€€ƒ`€€ƒxLNow concerning spiritual things, my brethren, I would not have you ignorant.€€ƒxXYou know that when you were heathens, you went to dumb idols, according as you were led.€€ƒx¢Wherefore, I give you to understand that no man, speaking by the Spirit of God, saith Anathema to Jesus. And no man can say The Lord Jesus, but by the Holy Ghost.€€˜ƒx;And there are diversities of ministries. but the same Lord.€€ƒxRAnd there are diversities of operations, but the same God, who worketh all in all.€€ƒxFAnd the manifestation of the Spirit is given to every man unto profit.€€ƒxTo one indeed, by the Spirit, is given the word of wisdom: and to another, the word of knowledge, according to the same Spirit:€€ƒxUTo another, faith in the same spirit: to another, the grace of healing in one Spirit:€€ƒx®To another the working of miracles: to another, prophecy: to another, the discerning of spirits: to another, diverse kinds of tongues: to another, interpretation of speeches.€€ƒxbBut all these things, one and the same Spirit worketh, dividing to every one according as he will.€€ƒxŠFor as the body is one and hath many members; and all the members of the body, whereas they are many, yet are one body: So also is Christ.€€ƒx—For in one Spirit were we all baptized into one body, whether Jews or Gentiles, whether bond or free: and in one Spirit we have all been made to drink.€€ƒx.For the body also is not one member, but many.€€ƒxiIf the foot should say: Because I am not the hand, I am not of the body: Is it therefore not of the Body?€€ƒxkAnd if the ear should say: Because I am not the eye, I am not of the body: Is it therefore not of the body?€€ƒxsIf the whole body were the eye, where would be the hearing? If the whole were hearing, where would be the smelling?€€ƒxXBut now God hath set the members, every one of them, in the body as it hath pleased him.€€ƒx9And if they all were one member, where would be the body?€€ƒx4But now there are many members indeed, yet one body.€€ƒxoAnd the eye cannot say to the hand: I need not thy help. Nor again the head to the feet: I have no need of you.€€ƒx\Yea, much, more those that seem to be the more feeble members of the body are more necessary€€ƒx±And such as we think to be the less honourable members of the body, about these we put more abundant honour: and those that are our uncomely parts have more abundant comeliness.€€ƒxBut our comely parts have no need: but God hath tempered the body together, giving to that which wanted the more abundant honour.€€ƒxeThat there might be no schism in the body: but the members might be mutually careful one for another.€€ƒx|And if one member suffer any thing, all the members suffer with it: or if one member glory, all the members rejoice with it.€€ƒx5Now you are the body of Christ and members of member.€€ƒxÔAnd God indeed hath set some in the church; first apostles, secondly prophets, thirdly doctors: after that miracles: then the graces of healings, helps, governments, kinds of tongues, interpretations of speeches.€€ƒx4Are all apostles? Are all prophets? Are all doctors?€€ƒxhAre all workers of miracles? Have all the grace of healing? Do all speak with tongues? Do all interpret?€€ƒxRBut be zealous for the better gifts. And I shew unto you yet a more excellent way.€€ƒ`€€ƒx|If I speak with the tongues of men and of angels, and have not charity, I am become as sounding brass, or a tinkling cymbal.€€ƒx´And if I should have prophecy and should know all mysteries and all knowledge, and if I should have all faith, so that I could remove mountains, and have not charity, I am nothing.€€ƒx–And if I should distribute all my goods to feed the poor, and if I should deliver my body to be burned, and have not charity, it profiteth me nothing.€€ƒxRIs not ambitious, seeketh not her own, is not provoked to anger, thinketh no evil:€€ƒx8Rejoiceth not in iniquity, but rejoiceth with the truth:€€ƒxQBeareth all things, believeth all things, hopeth all things, endureth all things.€€ƒxyCharity never falleth away: whether prophecies shall be made void or tongues shall cease or knowledge shall be destroyed.€€ƒx-For we know in part: and we prophesy in part.€€ƒxQBut when that which is perfect is come, that which is in part shall be done away.€€ƒx’When I was a child, I spoke as a child, I understood as a child, I thought as a child. But, when I became a man, I put away the things of a child.€€ƒxWe see now through a glass in a dark manner: but then face to face. Now I know in part: but then I shall know even as I am known.€€ƒxaAnd now there remain faith, hope, and charity, these three: but the greatest of these is charity.€€ƒ`€€ƒxWFollow after charity, be zealous for spiritual gifts; but rather that you may prophesy.€€ƒx‚For he that speaketh in a tongue speaketh not unto men, but unto God: for no man heareth. Yet by the Spirit he speaketh mysteries.€€ƒxUBut he that prophesieth speaketh to men unto edification and exhortation and comfort.€€˜(ƒxÔAnd I would have you all to speak with tongues, but rather to prophesy. For greater is he that prophesieth than he that speaketh with tongues: unless perhaps he interpret, that the church may receive edification.€€ƒx­But now, brethren, if I come to you speaking with tongues, what shall I profit you, unless I speak to you either in revelation or in knowledge or in prophecy or in doctrine?€€ƒx˜Even things without life that give sound, whether pipe or harp, except they give a distinction of sounds, how shall it be known what is piped or harped?€€ƒxTFor if the trumpet give an uncertain sound, who shall prepare himself to the battle?€€ƒx‰So likewise you, except you utter by the tongue plain speech, how shall it be known what is said? For you shall be speaking into the air.€€ƒxZThere are, for example, so many kinds of tongues in this world: and none is without voice.€€ƒxIf then I know not the power of the voice, I shall be to him to whom I speak a barbarian: and he that speaketh a barbarian to me.€€ƒxeSo you also, forasmuch as you are zealous of spirits, seek to abound unto the edifying of the church.€€ƒxOAnd therefore he that speaketh by a tongue, let him pray that he may interpret.€€ƒxTFor if I pray in a tongue, my spirit prayeth: but my understanding is without fruit.€€ƒxœWhat is it then? I will pray with the spirit, I will pray also with the understanding, I will sing with the spirit, I will sing also with the understanding.€€ƒx¤Else, if thou shalt bless with the spirit, how shall he that holdeth the place of the unlearned say, Amen, to thy blessing? Because he knoweth not what thou sayest.€€ƒxAFor thou indeed givest thanks well: but the other is not edified.€€ƒx-I thank my God I speak with all your tongues.€€ƒxŒBut in the church I had rather speak five words with my understanding, that I may instruct others also: than ten thousand words in a tongue.€€ƒx^Brethren, do not become children in sense. But in malice be children: and in sense be perfect.€€ƒxˆIn the law it is written: In other tongues and other lips I will speak to this people: and neither so will they hear me, saith the Lord.€€ƒx{Wherefore tongues are for a sign, not to believers but to unbelievers: but prophecies, not to unbelievers but to believers.€€ƒx¬If therefore the whole church come together into one place, and all speak with tongues, and there come in unlearned persons or infidels, will they not say that you are mad?€€ƒx‚But if all prophesy, and there come in one that believeth not or an unlearned person, he is convinced of all: he is judged of all.€€ƒxˆThe secrets of his heart are made manifest. And so, falling down on his face, he will adore God, affirming that God is among you indeed.€€ƒxÂHow is it then, brethren? When you come together, every one of you hath a psalm, hath a doctrine, hath a revelation, hath a tongue, hath an interpretation: let all things be done to edification.€€ƒxlIf any speak with a tongue, let it be by two, or at the most by three, and in course: and let one interpret.€€ƒxeBut if there be no interpreter, let him hold his peace in the church and speak to himself and to God.€€ƒxAAnd let the prophets speak, two or three: and let the rest judge.€€ƒxNBut if any thing be revealed to another sitting, let the first hold his peace.€€ƒxQFor you may all prophesy, one by one, that all may learn and all may be exhorted.€€ƒx<And the spirits of the prophets are subject to the prophets.€€ƒxfFor God is not the God of dissension, but of peace: as also I teach in all the churches of the saints.€€ƒxwLet women keep silence in the churches: for it is not permitted them to speak but to be subject, as also the law saith.€€ƒx|But if they would learn anything, let them ask their husbands at home. For it is a shame for a woman to speak in the church.€€ƒxCOr did the word of God come out from you? Or came it only unto you?€€ƒx‚If any seem to be a prophet or spiritual, let him know the things that I write to you, that they are the commandments of the Lord.€€ƒx/But if any man know not, he shall not be known.€€ƒxRWherefore, brethren, be zealous to prophesy: and forbid not to speak with tongues.€€ƒx;But let all things be done decently and according to order.€€ƒ`€€ƒx|Now I make known unto you, brethren, the gospel which I preached to you, which also you have received and wherein you stand.€€ƒxvBy which also you are saved, if you hold fast after what manner I preached unto you, unless you have believed in vain.€€ƒx}For I delivered unto you first of all, which I also received: how that Christ died for our sins, according to the scriptures:€€˜:ƒx=And that he was seen by Cephas, and after that by the eleven.€€ƒx€Then was he seen by more than five hundred brethren at once: of whom many remain until this present, and some are fallen asleep.€€ƒx;After that, he was seen by James: then by all the apostles.€€ƒxHAnd last of all, he was seen also by me, as by one born out of due tine.€€ƒxvFor I am the least of the apostles, who am not worthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the church of God.€€ƒx¬But by the grace of God, I am what I am. And his grace in me hath not been void: but I have laboured more abundantly than all they. Yet not I, but the grace of God with me:€€ƒx>For whether I or they, so we preach: and so you have believed.€€ƒx‚Now if Christ be preached, that he arose again from the dead, how do some among you say that there is no resurrection of the dead?€€ƒxLBut if there be no resurrection of the dead, then Christ is not risen again.€€ƒxZAnd if Christ be not risen again, then is our preaching vain: and your faith is also vain.€€ƒx±Yea, and we are found false witnesses of God: because we have given testimony against God, that he hath raised up Christ, whom he hath not raised up, if the dead rise not again.€€ƒx>For if the dead rise not again, neither is Christ risen again.€€ƒxSAnd if Christ be not risen again, your faith is vain: for you are yet in your sins.€€ƒx=Then they also that are fallen asleep in Christ are perished.€€ƒxNIf in this life only we have hope in Christ, we are of all men most miserable.€€ƒxJBut now Christ is risen from the dead, the firstfruits of them that sleep:€€ƒxCFor by a man came death: and by a man the resurrection of the dead.€€ƒxBAnd as in Adam all die, so also in Christ all shall be made alive.€€ƒxwBut every one in his own order: the firstfruits, Christ: then they that are of Christ, who have believed in his coming.€€ƒx¢Afterwards the end: when he shall have delivered up the kingdom to God and the Father: when he shall have brought to nought all principality and power and virtue.€€ƒxDFor he must reign, until he hath put all his enemies under his feet.€€ƒxoAnd the enemy, death, shall be destroyed last: For he hath put all things under his feet. And whereas he saith:€€ƒxXAll things are put under him; undoubtedly, he is excepted, who put all things under him.€€ƒxAnd when all things shall be subdued unto him, then the Son also himself shall be subject unto him that put all things under him, that God may be all in all.€€ƒx…Otherwise, what shall they do that are baptized for the dead, if the dead rise not again at all? Why are they then baptized for them?€€ƒx%Why also are we in danger every hour?€€ƒxVI die daily, I protest by your glory, brethren, which I have in Christ Jesus our Lord.€€ƒxœIf (according to man) I fought with beasts at Ephesus, what doth it profit me, if the dead rise not again? Let us eat and drink, for to morrow we shall die.€€ƒx9Be not seduced: Evil communications corrupt good manners.€€ƒx^Awake, ye just, and sin not. For some have not the knowledge of God. I speak it to your shame.€€ƒx_But some man will say: How do the dead rise again? Or with what manner of body shall they come?€€ƒxLSenseless man, that which thou sowest is not quickened, except it die first.€€ƒxxAnd that which thou sowest, thou sowest not the body that shall be: but bare grain, as of wheat, or of some of the rest.€€ƒxGBut God giveth it a body as he will: and to every seed its proper body.€€ƒxsAll flesh is not the same flesh: but one is the flesh of men, another of beasts, other of birds, another of fishes.€€ƒx~And there are bodies celestial and bodies terrestrial: but, one is the glory of the celestial, and another of the terrestrial.€€ƒx†One is the glory of the sun, another the glory of the moon, and another the glory of the stars. For star differeth from star in glory.€€ƒxaSo also is the resurrection of the dead. It is sown in corruption: it shall rise in incorruption.€€ƒx`It is sown in dishonour: it shall rise in glory. It is sown in weakness: it shall rise in power.€€ƒxˆIt is sown a natural body: it shall rise a spiritual body. If there be a natural body, there is also a spiritual body, as it is written:€€ƒxWThe first man Adam was made into a living soul; the last Adam into a quickening spirit.€€ƒxiYet that was not first which is spiritual, but that which is natural: afterwards that which is spiritual.€€ƒxOThe first man was of the earth, earthly: the second man, from heaven, heavenly.€€ƒxuSuch as is the earthly, such also are the earthly: and such as is the heavenly, such also are they that are heavenly.€€ƒxaTherefore, as we have borne the image of the earthly, let us bear also the image of the heavenly.€€ƒx€Now this I say, brethren, that flesh and blood cannot possess the kingdom of God: neither shall corruption possess incorruption.€€ƒx^Behold, I tell you a mystery. We shall all indeed rise again: but we shall not all be changed.€€ƒxŸIn a moment, in the twinkling of an eye, at the last trumpet: for the trumpet shall sound and the dead shall rise again incorruptible. And we shall be changed.€€ƒxWFor this corruptible must put on incorruption: and this mortal must put on immortality.€€ƒxƒAnd when this mortal hath put on immortality, then shall come to pass the saying that is written: Death is swallowed up in victory.€€ƒx;O death, where is thy victory? O death, where is thy sting?€€ƒx?Now the sting of death is sin: and the power of sin is the law.€€ƒxRBut thanks be to God, who hath given us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒxTherefore, my beloved brethren, be ye steadfast and unmoveable: always abounding in the work of the Lord, knowing that your labour is not in vain in the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx}Now concerning the collections that are made for the saints: as I have given order to the churches of Galatia, so do ye also.€€ƒx­On the first day of the week, let every one of you put apart with himself, laying up what it shall well please him: that when I come, the collections be not then to be made.€€ƒxyAnd when I shall be with you, whomsoever you shall approve by letters, them will I send to carry your grace to Jerusalem.€€˜ƒxgNow I will come to you, when I shall have passed through Macedonia. For I shall pass through Macedonia.€€ƒxwAnd with you perhaps I shall abide, or even spend the winter: that you may bring me on my way whithersoever I shall go.€€ƒxmFor I will not see you now by the way: for I trust that I shall abide with you some time, if the Lord permit.€€ƒx-But I will tarry at Ephesus, until Pentecost.€€ƒxEFor a great door and evident is opened unto me: and many adversaries.€€ƒxmNow if Timothy come, see that he be with you without fear: for he worketh the work of the Lord, as I also do.€€ƒx‡Let no man therefore despise him: but conduct ye him on his way in peace, that he may come to me. For I look for him with the brethren.€€ƒxàAnd touching our brother Apollo, I give you to understand that I much entreated him to come unto you with the brethren: and indeed it was not his will at all to come at this time. But he will come when he shall have leisure.€€ƒxCWatch ye: stand fast in the faith: do manfully and be strengthened.€€ƒx'Let all your things be done in charity.€€ƒxÇAnd I beseech you, brethren, you know the house of Stephanus, and of Fortunatus, and of Achaicus, that they are the firstfruits of Achaia, and have dedicated themselves to the ministry of the saints:€€ƒxUThat you also be subject to such and to every one that worketh with us and laboureth.€€ƒxˆAnd I rejoice in the presence of Stephanus and Fortunatus and Achaicus: because that which was wanting on your part, they have supplied.€€ƒxVFor they have refreshed both my spirit and yours. Know them, therefore, that are such.€€ƒx’The churches of Asia salute you. Aquila and Priscilla salute you much in the Lord, with the church that is in their house, with whom I also lodge.€€ƒxAAll the brethren salute you. Salute one another with a holy kiss.€€ƒx,The salutation of me Paul, with my own hand.€€ƒxJIf any man love not our Lord Jesus Christ, let him be anathema, maranatha.€€ƒx/The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you.€€ƒx1My charity be with you all in Christ Jesus. Amen.€€ƒx$The Second Letter to the Corinthians€€ƒ`€€ƒx¢Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God, and Timothy our brother: to the church of God that is at Corinth, with all the saints that are in all Achaia:€€ƒxLGrace unto you and peace from God our Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ.€€d2Cor¤dnamenII Corinthiansiosis_named2Corlabbreviationd2Corhchapters˜ƒxXFor as the sufferings of Christ abound in us: so also by Christ doth our comfort abound.€€ƒyNow whether we be in tribulation, it is for your exhortation and salvation: or whether we be comforted, it is for your consolation: or whether we be exhorted, it is for your exhortation and salvation, which worketh the enduring of the same sufferings which we also suffer.€€ƒx…That our hope for you may be steadfast: knowing that as you are partakers of the sufferings, so shall you be also of the consolation.€€ƒx¶For we would not have you ignorant, brethren, of our tribulation which came to us in Asia: that we were pressed out of measure above our strength, so that we were weary even of life.€€ƒxtBut we had in ourselves the answer of death, that we should not trust in ourselves, but in God who raiseth the dead.€€ƒxrWho hath delivered and doth deliver us out of so great dangers: in whom we trust that he will yet also deliver us,€€ƒx‘You helping withal in prayer for us. That for this gift obtained for us, by the means of many persons, thanks may be given by many in our behalf.€€ƒxŞFor our glory is this: the testimony of our conscience, that in simplicity of heart and sincerity of God, and not in carnal wisdom, but in the grace of God, we have conversed in this world: and more abundantly towards you.€€ƒxsFor we write no other things to you than what you have read and known. And I hope that you shall know unto the end.€€ƒxuAs also you have known us in part, that we are your glory: as you also are ours, in the day of our Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒx^And in this confidence I had a mind to come to you before, that you might have a second grace:€€ƒx}And to pass by you into Macedonia: and again from Macedonia to come to you, and by you to be brought on my way towards Judea.€€ƒx¬Whereas then I was thus minded, did I use lightness? Or, the things that I purpose, do I purpose according to the flesh, that there should be with me, It is, and It is not?€€ƒxWBut God is faithful: for our preaching which was to you, was not, It is, and It is not.€€ƒx™For the Son of God, Jesus Christ, who was preached among you by us, by me and Sylvanus and Timothy, was not: It is and It is not. But, It is, was in him.€€ƒxbFor all the promises of God are in him, It is. Therefore also by him, amen to God, unto our glory.€€ƒxONow he that confirmeth us with you in Christ and that hath anointed us, is God:€€ƒxIWho also hath sealed us and given the pledge of the Spirit in our hearts.€€ƒxÃBut I call God to witness upon my soul that to spare you, I came not any more to Corinth: not because we exercise dominion over your faith: but we are helpers of your joy. For in faith you stand.€€ƒ`€€ƒ`€€ƒxBBut I determined this with myself, to come to you again in sorrow.€€ƒxlFor if I make you sorrowful, who is he then that can make me glad, but the same who is made sorrowful by me?€€ƒx¹And I wrote this same to you: that I may not, when I come, have sorrow upon sorrow from them of whom I ought to rejoice: having confidence in you all, that my joy is the joy of you all.€€‘ƒxeAnd if any one have caused grief, he hath not grieved me: but in part, that I may not burden you all.€€ƒxLTo him who is such a one, this rebuke is sufficient, which is given by many.€€ƒx…So that on the contrary, you should rather forgive him and comfort him, lest perhaps such a one be swallowed up with overmuch sorrow.€€ƒxIWherefore, I beseech you that you would confirm your charity towards him.€€ƒxoFor to this end also did I write, that I may know the experiment of you, whether you be obedient in all things.€€ƒx And to whom you have pardoned any thing, I also. For, what I have pardoned, if I have pardoned any thing, for your sakes have I done it in the person of Christ:€€ƒxLThat we be not overreached by Satan. For we are not ignorant of his devices.€€ƒx`And when I was come to Troas for the gospel of Christ and a door was opened unto me in the Lord,€€ƒxsI had no rest in my spirit, because I found not Titus my brother: but bidding them farewell, I went into Macedonia.€€ƒx†Now thanks be to God, who always maketh us to triumph in Christ Jesus and manifesteth the odour of his knowledge by us in every place.€€ƒx]For we are the good odour of Christ unto God, in them that are saved and in them that perish.€€ƒxŠTo the one indeed the odour of death unto death: but to the others the odour of life unto life. And for these things who is so sufficient?€€ƒxzFor we are not as many, adulterating the word of God: but with sincerity: but as from God, before God, in Christ we speak.€€ƒ`€€ƒxpDo we begin again to commend ourselves? Or do we need (as some do) epistles of commendation to you, or from you?€€ƒxOYou are our epistle, written in our hearts, which is known and read by all men:€€ƒxÇBeing manifested, that you are the epistle of Christ, ministered by us, and written: not with ink but with the Spirit of the living God: not in tables of stone but in the fleshly tables of the heart.€€’ƒxmNot that we are sufficient to think any thing of ourselves, as of ourselves: but our sufficiency is from God.€€ƒx‘Who also hath made us fit ministers of the new testament, not in the letter but in the spirit. For the letter killeth: but the spirit quickeneth.€€ƒxØNow if the ministration of death, engraven with letters upon stones, was glorious (so that the children of Israel could not steadfastly behold the face of Moses, for the glory of his countenance), which is made void:€€ƒx@How shall not the ministration of the spirit be rather in glory?€€ƒxkFor if the ministration of condemnation be glory, much more the ministration of justice aboundeth in glory.€€ƒxfFor even that which was glorious in this part was not glorified by reason of the glory that excelleth.€€ƒxXFor if that which is done away was glorious, much more that which remaineth is in glory.€€ƒx3Having therefore such hope, we use much confidence.€€ƒx‰And not as Moses put a veil upon his face, that the children of Israel might not steadfastly look on the face of that which is made void.€€ƒx³But their senses were made dull. For, until this present day, the selfsame veil, in the reading of the old testament, remaineth not taken away (because in Christ it is made void).€€ƒxJBut even until this day, when Moses is read, the veil is upon their heart.€€ƒxKBut when they shall be converted to the Lord, the veil shall be taken away.€€ƒxPNow the Lord is a Spirit. And where the Spirit of the Lord is, there is liberty.€€ƒx’But we all, beholding the glory of the Lord with open face, are transformed into the same image from glory to glory, as by the Spirit of the Lord.€€ƒ`€€ƒx^Therefore seeing we have this ministration, according as we have obtained mercy, we faint not.€€ƒxÓBut we renounce the hidden things of dishonesty, not walking in craftiness nor adulterating the word of God: but by manifestation of the truth commending ourselves to every man's conscience, in the sight of God.€€ƒx?And if our gospel be also hid, it is hid to them that are lost,€€’ƒxbFor we preach not ourselves, but Jesus Christ our Lord: and ourselves your servants through Jesus.€€ƒxªFor God, who commanded the light to shine out of darkness, hath shined in our hearts, to give the light of the knowledge of the glory of God, in the face of Christ Jesus.€€ƒxkBut we have this treasure in earthen vessels, that the excellency may be of the power of God and not of us.€€ƒxfIn all things we suffer tribulation: but are not distressed. We are straitened: but are not destitute.€€ƒxQWe suffer persecution: but are not forsaken. We are cast down: but we perish not.€€ƒx|Always bearing about in our body the mortification of Jesus, that the life also of Jesus may be made manifest in our bodies.€€ƒx†For we who live are always delivered unto death for Jesus' sake: that the life also of Jesus may be made manifest in our mortal flesh.€€ƒx-So then death worketh in us: but life in you.€€ƒx‘But having the same spirit of faith, as it is written: I believed, for which cause I have spoken; we also believe. For which cause we speak also:€€ƒx[Knowing that he who raised up Jesus will raise us up also with Jesus and place us with you.€€ƒx|For all things are for your sakes: that the grace, abounding through many, may abound in thanksgiving unto the glory of God.€€ƒxpFor which cause we faint not: but though our outward man is corrupted, yet the inward man is renewed day by day.€€ƒx‰For that which is at present momentary and light of our tribulation worketh for us above measure, exceedingly an eternal weight of glory.€€ƒx±While we look not at the things which are seen, but at the things which are not seen. For the things which are seen are temporal: but the things which are not seen, are eternal.€€ƒ`€€ƒx’For we know, if our earthly house of this habitation be dissolved, that we have a building of God, a house not made with hands, eternal in heaven.€€ƒx_For in this also we groan, desiring to be clothed upon with our habitation that is from heaven.€€ƒx+Yet so that we be found clothed, not naked.€€•ƒx]Now he that maketh us for this very thing is God, who hath given us the pledge of the Spirit,€€ƒxfTherefore having always confidence, knowing that while we are in the body we are absent from the Lord.€€ƒx((For we walk by faith and not by sight.)€€ƒxlBut we are confident and have a good will to be absent rather from the body and to be present with the Lord.€€ƒxBAnd therefore we labour, whether absent or present, to please him.€€ƒx²For we must all be manifested before the judgment seat of Christ, that every one may receive the proper things of the body, according as he hath done, whether it be good or evil.€€ƒx˜Knowing therefore the fear of the Lord, we use persuasion to men: but to God we are manifest. And I trust also that in your consciences we are manifest.€€ƒx£We commend not ourselves again to you, but give you occasion to glory in our behalf: that you may have somewhat to answer them who glory in face, and not in heart.€€ƒx[For whether we be transported in mind, it is to God: or whether we be sober, it is for you.€€ƒxbFor the charity of Christ presseth us: judging this, that if one died for all, then all were dead.€€ƒxAnd Christ died for all: that they also who live may not now live to themselves, but unto him who died for them and rose again.€€ƒx’Wherefore henceforth, we know no man according to the flesh. And if we have known Christ according to the flesh: but now we know him so no longer.€€ƒxhIf then any be in Christ a new creature, the old things are passed away. Behold all things are made new.€€ƒx{But all things are of God, who hath reconciled us to himself by Christ and hath given to us the ministry of reconciliation.€€ƒx•For God indeed was in Christ, reconciling the world to himself, not imputing to them their sins. And he hath placed in us the word of reconciliation.€€ƒxzFor Christ therefore we are ambassadors, God as it were exhorting by us, for Christ, we beseech you, be reconciled to God.€€ƒx_Him, who knew no sin, he hath made sin for us: that we might be made the justice of God in him.€€ƒ`€€ƒxKAnd we helping do exhort you that you receive not the grace of God in vain.€€ƒx¬For he saith: In an accepted time have I heard thee and in the day of salvation have I helped thee. Behold, now is the acceptable time: behold, now is the day of salvation.€€ƒx>Giving no offence to any man, that our ministry be not blamed.€€’ƒxLIn stripes, in prisons, in seditions, in labours, in watchings, in fastings,€€ƒxcIn chastity, in knowledge, in longsuffering, in sweetness, in the Holy Ghost, in charity unfeigned,€€ƒxfIn the word of truth, in the power of God: by the armour of justice on the right hand and on the left:€€ƒxmBy honour and dishonour: by evil report and good report: as deceivers and yet true: as unknown and yet known:€€ƒx9As dying and behold we live: as chastised and not killed:€€ƒxnAs sorrowful, yet always rejoicing: as needy, yet enriching many: as having nothing and possessing all things.€€ƒxBOur mouth is open to you, O ye Corinthians: our heart is enlarged.€€ƒxHYou are not straitened in us: but in your own bowels you are straitened.€€ƒxQBut having the same recompense (I speak as to my children): be you also enlarged.€€ƒx„Bear not the yoke with unbelievers. For what participation hath justice with injustice? Or what fellowship hath light with darkness?€€ƒx]And what concord hath Christ with Belial? Or what part hath the faithful with the unbeliever?€€ƒxÍAnd what agreement hath the temple of God with idols? For you are the temple of the living God: as God saith: I will dwell in them and walk among them. And I will be their God: and they shall be my people.€€ƒxfWherefore: Go out from among them and be ye separate, saith the Lord, and touch not the unclean thing:€€ƒxuAnd I will receive you. And will be a Father to you: and you shall be my sons and daughters, saith the Lord Almighty.€€ƒ`€€ƒx«Having therefore these promises, dearly beloved, let us cleanse ourselves from all defilement of the flesh and of the spirit, perfecting sanctification in the fear of God.€€ƒxYReceive us. We have injured no man: we have corrupted no man: we have overreached no man.€€ƒx€I speak not this to your condemnation. For we have said before that you are in our hearts: to die together and to live together.€€ƒx‚For also, when we were come into Macedonia, our flesh had no rest: but we suffered all tribulation. Combats without: fears within.€€ƒxHBut God, who comforteth the humble, comforted us by the coming of Titus.€€ƒx´And not by his coming only, but also by the consolation wherewith he was comforted in you, relating to us your desire, your mourning, your zeal for me: so that I rejoiced the more.€€ƒx¥For although I made you sorrowful by my epistle, I do not repent. And if I did repent, seeing that the same epistle (although but for a time) did make you sorrowful,€€ƒxÂNow I am glad: not because you were made sorrowful, but because you were made sorrowful unto penance. For you were made sorrowful according to God, that you might suffer damage by us in nothing.€€ƒx}For the sorrow that is according to God worketh penance, steadfast unto salvation: but the sorrow of the world worketh death.€€ƒy
For behold this selfsame thing, that you were made sorrowful according to God, how great carefulness it worketh in you: yea defence, yea indignation, yea fear, yea desire, yea zeal, yea revenge. In all things you have shewed yourselves to be undefiled in the matter.€€ƒx¡Wherefore although I wrote to you, it was not for his sake that did the wrong, nor for him that suffered it: but to manifest our carefulness that we have for you€€ƒx¥Before God: therefore we were comforted. But in our consolation we did the more abundantly rejoice for the joy of Titus, because his spirit was refreshed by you all.€€ƒx¼And if I have boasted any thing to him of you, I have not been put to shame: but as we have spoken all things to you in truth, so also our boasting that was made to Titus is found a truth.€€ƒxƒAnd his bowels are more abundantly towards you: remembering the obedience of you all, how with fear and trembling you received him.€€ƒx6I rejoice that in all things I have confidence in you.€€ƒ`€€ƒxiNow we make known unto you, brethren, the grace of God that hath been given in the churches of Macedonia.€€ƒx•That in much experience of tribulation, they have had abundance of joy and their very deep poverty hath abounded unto the riches of their simplicity.€€ƒx]For according to their power (I bear them witness) and beyond their power, they were willing:€€˜ƒxfAnd not as we hoped: but they gave their own selves, first to the Lord, then to us by the will of God;€€ƒxjInsomuch, that we desired Titus, that, as he had begun, so also he would finish among you this same grace.€€ƒx¥That as in all things you abound in faith and word and knowledge and all carefulness, moreover also in your charity towards us: so in this grace also you may abound.€€ƒxqI speak not as commanding: but by the carefulness of others, approving also the good disposition of your charity.€€ƒx‹For you know the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, that being rich he became poor for your sakes: that through his poverty you might be rich.€€ƒx}And herein I give my advice: for this is profitable for you who have begun not only to do but also to be willing, a year ago.€€ƒxNow therefore perform ye it also in deed: that as your mind is forward to be willing, so it may be also to perform, out of that which you have.€€ƒxwFor if the will be forward, it is accepted according to that which a man hath: not according to that which he hath not.€€ƒxPFor I mean not that others should be eased and you burdened, but by an equality.€€ƒx‰In this present time let your abundance supply their want, that their abundance also may supply your want: that there may be an equality,€€ƒxXAs it is written: He that had much had nothing over; and he that had little had no want.€€ƒxXAnd thanks be to God, who hath given the same carefulness for you in the heart of Titus.€€ƒxbFor indeed he accepted the exhortation: but, being more careful, of his own will he went unto you.€€ƒx^We have sent also with him the brother whose praise is in the gospel through all the churches.€€ƒx´And not that only: but he was also ordained by the churches companion of our travels, for this grace, which is administered by us, to the glory of the Lord and our determined will:€€ƒxZAvoiding this, lest any man should blame us in this abundance which is administered by us.€€ƒxJFor we forecast what may be good, not only before God but also before men.€€ƒx˜And we have sent with them our brother also, whom we have often proved diligent in many things, but now much more diligent: with much confidence in you,€€ƒxŠEither for Titus, who is my companion and fellow labourer towards you, or our brethren, the apostles of the churches, the glory of Christ.€€ƒxyWherefore shew ye to them, in the sight of the churches, the evidence of your charity and of our boasting on your behalf.€€ƒ`€€ƒxhFor concerning the ministry that is done towards the saints, it is superfluous for me to write unto you.€€ƒx¤For I know your forward mind: for which I boast of you to the Macedonians, that Achaia also is ready from the year past. And your emulation hath provoked very many.€€ƒx–Now I have sent the brethren, that the thing which we boast of concerning you be not made void in this behalf, that (as I have said) you may be ready:€€ƒx·Therefore I thought it necessary to desire the brethren that they would go to you before and prepare this blessing before promised, to be ready, so as a blessing, not as covetousness.€€ƒx|Now this I say: He who soweth sparingly shall also reap sparingly: and he who soweth in blessings shall also reap blessings.€€ƒxpEvery one as he hath determined in his heart, not with sadness or of necessity: for God loveth a cheerful giver.€€ƒx†And God is able to make all grace abound in you: that ye always, having all sufficiently in all things, may abound to every good work,€€ƒxfAs it is written: He hath dispersed abroad, he hath given to the poor: his justice remaineth for ever.€€ƒxœAnd he that ministereth seed to the sower will both give you bread to eat and will multiply your seed and increase the growth of the fruits of your justice:€€ƒxsThat being enriched in all things, you may abound unto all simplicity which worketh through us thanksgiving to God.€€ƒxŒBecause the administration of this office doth not only supply the want of the saints, but aboundeth also by many thanksgivings in the Lord.€€ƒx±By the proof of this ministry, glorifying God for the obedience of your confession unto the gospel of Christ and for the simplicity of your communicating unto them and unto all.€€ƒxbAnd in their praying for you, being desirous of you, because of the excellent grace of God in you.€€ƒx*Thanks be to God for his unspeakable gift.€€ƒ`€€ƒx–Now I Paul, myself beseech you, by the mildness and modesty of Christ: who in presence indeed am lowly among you, but being absent am bold toward you.€€ƒx¶But I beseech you, that I may not be bold when I am present with that confidence wherewith I am thought to be bold, against some who reckon us as if we walked according to the flesh.€€ƒxFFor though we walk in the flesh, we do not war according to the flesh.€€’ƒx‘And every height that exalteth itself against the knowledge of God: and bringing into captivity every understanding unto the obedience of Christ:€€ƒx\And having in readiness to revenge all disobedience, when your obedience shall be fulfilled.€€ƒx¸See the things that are according to outward appearance. If any man trust to himself, that he is Christ's let him think this again with himself, that as he is Christ's, so are we also.€€ƒx›For if also I should boast somewhat more of our power, which the Lord hath given us unto edification and not for your destruction, I should not be ashamed.€€ƒxDBut that I may not be thought as it were to terrify you by epistles,€€ƒxy(For his epistles indeed, say they, are weighty and strong; but his bodily presence is weak and his speech contemptible):€€ƒxyLet such a one think this, that such as we are in word by epistles when absent, such also we will be indeed when present.€€ƒx™For we dare not match or compare ourselves with some that commend themselves: but we measure ourselves by ourselves and compare ourselves with ourselves.€€ƒx“But we will not glory beyond our measure: but according to the measure of the rule which God hath measured to us, a measure to reach even unto you.€€ƒx‰For we stretch not ourselves beyond our measure, as if we reached not unto you. For we are come as far as to you in the Gospel of Christ.€€ƒx–Not glorying beyond measure in other men's labours: but having hope of your increasing faith, to be magnified in you according to our rule abundantly.€€ƒx•Yea, unto those places that are beyond you to preach the gospel: not to glory in another man's rule, in those things that are made ready to our hand.€€ƒx0But he that glorieth, let him glory in the Lord.€€ƒxKFor not he who commendeth himself is approved: but he, whom God commendeth.€€ƒ`€€ƒxNWould to God you could bear with some little of my folly! But do bear with me.€€ƒxFor I am jealous of you with the jealousy of God. For I have espoused you to one husband, that I may present you as a chaste virgin to Christ.€€ƒxBut I fear lest, as the serpent seduced Eve by his subtilty, so your minds should be corrupted and fall from the simplicity that is in Christ.€€˜!ƒxDFor I suppose that I have done nothing less than the great apostles.€€ƒxlFor although I be rude in speech, yet not in knowledge: but in all things we have been made manifest to you.€€ƒxyOr did I commit a fault, humbling myself that you might be exalted, because I preached unto you the Gospel of God freely?€€ƒxLI have taken from other churches, receiving wages of them for your ministry.€€ƒxøAnd, when I was present with you and wanted, I was chargeable to no man: for that which was wanting to me, the brethren supplied who came from Macedonia. And in all things I have kept myself from being burthensome to you: and so I will keep myself.€€ƒxhThe truth of Christ is in me, that this glorying shall not be broken off in me in the regions of Achaia.€€ƒx2Wherefore? Because I love you not? God knoweth it.€€ƒx•But what I do, that I will do: that I may cut off the occasion from them that desire occasion: that wherein they glory, they may be found even as we.€€ƒxcFor such false apostles are deceitful workmen, transforming themselves into the apostles of Christ.€€ƒxMAnd no wonder: for Satan himself transformeth himself into an angel of light.€€ƒxˆTherefore it is no great thing if his ministers be transformed as the ministers of justice, whose end shall be according to their works.€€ƒxrI say again (Let no man think me to be foolish: otherwise take me as one foolish, that I also may glory a little):€€ƒxlThat which I speak, I speak not according to God: but as it were in foolishness, in this matter of glorying.€€ƒxASeeing that many glory according to the flesh, I will glory also.€€ƒx?For you gladly suffer the foolish: whereas yourselves are wise.€€ƒx”For you suffer if a man bring you into bondage, if a man devour you, if a man take from you, if a man be lifted up, if a man strike you on the face.€€ƒx}I seek according to dishonour, as if we had been weak in this part. Wherein if any man dare (I speak foolishly), I dare also.€€ƒx_They are Hebrews: so am I. They are Israelites: so am I. They are the seed of Abraham: so am I.€€ƒx¤They are the ministers of Christ (I speak as one less wise): I am more; in many more labours, in prisons more frequently, in stripes above measure, in deaths often.€€ƒx<Of the Jews five times did I receive forty stripes save one.€€ƒxThrice was I beaten with rods: once I was stoned: thrice I suffered shipwreck: a night and a day I was in the depth of the sea.€€ƒxãIn journeying often, in perils of waters, in perils of robbers, in perils from my own nation, in perils from the Gentiles, in perils in the city, in perils in the wilderness, in perils in the sea, in perils from false brethren:€€ƒxmIn labour and painfulness, in much watchings, in hunger and thirst, in fastings often, in cold and nakedness:€€ƒx_Besides those things which are without: my daily instance, the solicitude for all the churches.€€ƒxIWho is weak, and I am not weak? Who is scandalized, and I am not on fire?€€ƒxLIf I must needs glory, I will glory of the things that concern my infirmity.€€ƒx]The God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who is blessed for ever, knoweth that I lie not.€€ƒxsAt Damascus, the governor of the nation under Aretas the king, guarded the city of the Damascenes, to apprehend me.€€ƒxVAnd through a window in a basket was I let down by the wall: and so escaped his hands.€€ƒ`€€ƒxdIf I must glory (it is not expedient indeed) but I will come to visions and revelations of the Lord.€€ƒxªI know a man in Christ: above fourteen years ago (whether in the body, I know not, or out of the body, I know not: God knoweth), such a one caught up to the third heaven.€€ƒxYAnd I know such a man (whether in the body, or out of the body, I know not: God knoweth):€€•ƒxXFor such an one I will glory: but for myself I will glory nothing but in my infirmities.€€ƒxÌFor though I should have a mind to glory, I shall not be foolish: for I will say the truth. But I forbear, lest any man should think of me above that which he seeth in me, or any thing he heareth from me.€€ƒxƒAnd lest the greatness of the revelations should exalt me, there was given me a sting of my flesh, an angel of Satan, to buffet me.€€ƒxIFor which thing, thrice I besought the Lord that it might depart from me.€€ƒx¶And he said to me: My grace is sufficient for thee: for power is made perfect in infirmity. Gladly therefore will I glory in my infirmities, that the power of Christ may dwell in me.€€ƒx¥For which cause I please myself in my infirmities, in reproaches, in necessities, in persecutions, in distresses, for Christ. For when I am weak, then am I powerful.€€ƒx³I am become foolish. You have compelled me: for I ought to have been commended by you. For I have no way come short of them that are above measure apostles, although I be nothing.€€ƒxqYet the signs of my apostleship have been wrought on you, in all patience, in signs and wonders and mighty deeds.€€ƒx…For what is there that you have had less than the other churches but that I myself was not burthensome to you? Pardon me this injury.€€ƒxêBehold now the third time I am ready to come to you and I will not be burthensome unto you. For I seek not the things that are yours, but you. For neither ought the children to lay up for the parents, but the parents for the children.€€ƒxkBut I most gladly will spend and be spent myself for your souls: although loving you more, I be loved less.€€ƒxMBut be it so: I did not burthen you: but being crafty, I caught you by guile.€€ƒx6Did I overreach you by any of them whom I sent to you?€€ƒxŒI desired Titus: and I sent with him a brother. Did Titus overreach you? Did we not walk with the same spirit? Did we not in the same steps?€€ƒxŠOf old, think you that we excuse ourselves to you? We speak before God in Christ: but all things, my dearly beloved, for your edification.€€ƒxÿFor I fear lest perhaps, when I come, I shall not find you such as I would, and that I shall be found by you such as you would not. Lest perhaps contentions, envyings, animosities, dissensions, detractions, whisperings, swellings, seditions, be among you.€€ƒxÈLest again, when I come, God humble me among you: and I mourn many of them that sinned before and have not done penance for the uncleanness and fornication and lasciviousness that they have committed.€€ƒ`€€ƒxqBehold, this is the third time I am coming to you: In the mouth of two or three witnesses shall every word stand.€€ƒx“I have told before and foretell, as present and now absent, to them that sinned before and to all the rest, that if I come again, I will not spare.€€ƒxeDo you seek a proof of Christ that speaketh in me, who towards you is not weak, but is mighty in you?€€ƒxTry your own selves if you be in the faith: prove ye yourselves. Know you not your own selves, that Christ Jesus is in you, unless perhaps you be reprobates?€€ƒx;But I trust that you shall know that we are not reprobates.€€ƒx’Now we pray God that you may do no evil, not that we may appear approved, but that you may do that which is good and that we may be as reprobates.€€ƒx;For we can do nothing against the truth: but for the truth.€€ƒx[For we rejoice that we are weak and you are strong. This also we pray for, your perfection.€€ƒx¿Therefore I write these things, being absent, that, being present, I may not deal more severely, according to the power which the Lord hath given me unto edification and not unto destruction.€€ƒxFor the rest, brethren, rejoice, be perfect, take exhortation, be of one mind, have peace. And the God of grace and of love shall be with you.€€ƒx?Salute one another with a holy kiss. All the saints salute you.€€ƒxxThe grace of our Lord Jesus Christ and the charity of God and the communication of the Holy Ghost be with you all. Amen.€€ƒxThe Letter to the Galatians€€ƒ`€€ƒxsPaul, an apostle, not of men, neither by man, but by Jesus Christ and God the Father, who raised him from the dead:€€ƒxAAnd all the brethren who are with me: to the churches of Galatia.€€ƒxNGrace be to you, and peace from God the Father and from our Lord Jesus Christ,€€cGal¤dnameiGalatiansiosis_namecGallabbreviationcGalhchapters†˜ƒxmI wonder that you are so soon removed from him that called you into the grace of Christ, unto another gospel.€€ƒxbWhich is not another: only there are some that trouble you and would pervert the gospel of Christ.€€ƒxBut though we, or an angel from heaven, preach a gospel to you besides that which we have preached to you, let him be anathema.€€ƒx„As we said before, so now I say again: If any one preach to you a gospel, besides that which you have received, let him be anathema.€€ƒx{For do I now persuade men, or God? Or do I seek to please men? If I yet pleased men, I should not be the servant of Christ.€€ƒxiFor I give you to understand, brethren, that the gospel which was preached by me is not according to man.€€ƒx^For neither did I receive it of man: nor did I learn it but by the revelation of Jesus Christ.€€ƒx‘For you have heard of my conversation in time past in the Jews' religion: how that, beyond measure, I persecuted the church of God and wasted it.€€ƒx“And I made progress in the Jew's religion above many of my equals in my own nation, being more abundantly zealous for the traditions of my fathers.€€ƒxZBut when it pleased him who separated me from my mother's womb and called me by his grace,€€ƒxwTo reveal his Son in me, that I might preach him among the Gentiles: immediately I condescended not to flesh and blood.€€ƒxzNeither went I to Jerusalem, to the apostles who were before me: but I went into Arabia, and again I returned to Damascus.€€ƒx_Then, after three years, I went to Jerusalem to see Peter: and I tarried with him fifteen days.€€ƒxKBut other of the apostles I saw none, saving James the brother of the Lord.€€ƒxCNow the things which I write to you, behold, before God, I lie not.€€ƒx9Afterwards, I came into the regions of Syria and Cilicia.€€ƒxIAnd I was unknown by face to the churches of Judea, which were in Christ:€€ƒxnBut they had heard only: He, who persecuted us in times past doth now preach the faith which once he impugned.€€ƒxAnd they glorified God in me.€€ƒ`€€ƒxbThen, after fourteen years, I went up again to Jerusalem with Barnabas, taking Titus also with me.€€ƒxÉAnd I went up according to revelation and communicated to them the gospel which I preach among the Gentiles: but apart to them who seemed to be some thing: lest perhaps I should run or had run in vain.€€ƒxUBut neither Titus, who was with me, being a Gentile, was compelled to be circumcised.€€ƒx£But because of false brethren unawares brought in, who came in privately to spy our liberty which we have in Christ Jesus, that they might bring us into servitude.€€•ƒx¸But of them who seemed to be some thing, (what they were some time it is nothing to me, God accepteth not the person of man): for to me they that seemed to be some thing added nothing.€€ƒx‰But contrariwise, when they had seen that to me was committed the gospel of the uncircumcision, as to Peter was that of the circumcision.€€ƒxk(For he who wrought in Peter to the apostleship of the circumcision wrought in me also among the Gentiles.)€€ƒxèAnd when they had known the grace that was given to me, James and Cephas and John, who seemed to be pillars, gave to me and Barnabas the right hands of fellowship: that we should go unto the Gentiles, and they unto the circumcision:€€ƒxVOnly that we should be mindful of the poor: which same thing also I was careful to do.€€ƒx^But when Cephas was come to Antioch, I withstood him to the face, because he was to be blamed.€€ƒxªFor before that some came from James, he did eat with the Gentiles: but when they were come, he withdrew and separated himself, fearing them who were of the circumcision.€€ƒxwAnd to his dissimulation the rest of the Jews consented: so that Barnabas also was led by them into that dissimulation.€€ƒxıBut when I saw that they walked not uprightly unto the truth of the gospel, I said to Cephas before them all: If thou, being a Jew, livest after the manner of the Gentiles and not as the Jews do, how dost thou compel the Gentiles to live as do the Jews?€€ƒx8We by nature are Jews: and not of the Gentiles, sinners.€€ƒyBut knowing that man is not justified by the works of the law, but by the faith of Jesus Christ, we also believe in Christ Jesus, that we may be justified by the faith of Christ and not by the works of the law: because by the works of the law no flesh shall be justified.€€ƒx…But if, while we seek to be justified in Christ, we ourselves also are found sinners, is Christ then the minister of sin? God forbid!€€ƒxXFor if I build up again the things which I have destroyed, I make myself a prevaricator.€€ƒxiFor I, through the law, am dead to the law, that I may live to God; with Christ I am nailed to the cross.€€ƒx£And I live, now not I: but Christ liveth in me. And that I live now in the flesh: I live in the faith of the Son of God, who loved me and delivered himself for me.€€ƒxYI cast not away the grace of God. For if justice be by the law, then Christ died in vain.€€ƒ`€€ƒxšO senseless Galatians, who hath bewitched you that you should not obey the truth: before whose eyes Jesus Christ hath been set forth, crucified among you?€€ƒxnThis only would I learn of you: Did you receive the Spirit by the works of the law or by the hearing of faith?€€ƒxeAre you so foolish that, whereas you began in the Spirit, you would now be made perfect by the flesh?€€ƒx@Have you suffered so great things in vain? If it be yet in vain.€€˜ƒxOAs it is written: Abraham believed God: and it was reputed to him unto justice.€€ƒxUKnow ye, therefore, that they who are of faith, the same are the children of Abraham.€€ƒxˆAnd the scripture, foreseeing that God justifieth the Gentiles by faith, told unto Abraham before: In thee shall all nations be blessed.€€ƒxITherefore, they that are of faith shall be blessed with faithful Abraham.€€ƒx¹For as many as are of the works of the law are under a curse. For it is written: Cursed is every one that abideth, not in all things which are written in the book of the law to do them.€€ƒxgBut that in the law no man is justified with God, it is manifest: because the just man liveth by faith.€€ƒxNBut the law is not of faith: but he that doth those things shall live in them.€€ƒxChrist hath redeemed us from the curse of the law, being made a curse for us (for it is written: Cursed is every one that hangeth on a tree).€€ƒx…That the blessing of Abraham might come on the Gentiles through Christ Jesus: that we may receive the promise of the Spirit by faith.€€ƒxyBrethren (I speak after the manner of man), yet a man's testament, if it be confirmed, no man despiseth nor addeth to it.€€ƒxTo Abraham were the promises made and to his seed. He saith not: And to his seeds as of many. But as of one: And to thy seed, which is Christ.€€ƒx®Now this I say: that the testament which was confirmed by God, the law which was made after four hundred and thirty years doth not disannul, to make the promise of no effect.€€ƒxfFor if the inheritance be of the law, it is no more of promise. But God gave it to Abraham by promise.€€ƒx§Why then was the law? It was set because of transgressions, until the seed should come to whom he made the promise, being ordained by angels in the hand of a mediator.€€ƒx-Now a mediator is not of one: but God is one.€€ƒxWas the law then against the promises of God: God forbid! For if there had been a law given which could give life, verily justice should have been by the law.€€ƒx„But the scripture hath concluded all under sin, that the promise, by the faith of Jesus Christ, might be given to them that believe.€€ƒxhBut before the faith came, we were kept under the law shut up, unto that faith which was to be revealed.€€ƒxSWherefore the law was our pedagogue in Christ: that we might be justified by faith.€€ƒx@But after the faith is come, we are no longer under a pedagogue.€€ƒx>For you are all the children of God, by faith in Christ Jesus.€€ƒxFFor as many of you as have been baptized in Christ have put on Christ.€€ƒx†There is neither Jew nor Greek: there is neither bond nor free: there is neither male nor female. For you are all one in Christ Jesus.€€ƒxYAnd if you be Christ's, then are you the seed of Abraham, heirs according to the promise.€€ƒ`€€ƒx^As long as the heir is a child, he differeth nothing from a servant, though he be lord of all,€€ƒxIBut is under tutors and governors until the time appointed by the father.€€ƒxPSo we also, when we were children, were serving under the elements of the world.€€ƒxaBut when the fulness of the time was come, God sent his Son, made of a woman, made under the law:€€˜ƒxeAnd because you are sons, God hath sent the Spirit of his Son into your hearts, crying: Abba, Father.€€ƒxVTherefore, now he is not a servant, but a son. And if a son, an heir also through God.€€ƒxOBut then indeed, not knowing God, you served them who, by nature, are not gods.€€ƒx–But now, after that you have known God, or rather are known by God: how turn you again to the weak and needy elements which you desire to serve again?€€ƒx1You observe days and months and times, and years.€€ƒxCI am afraid of you, lest perhaps I have laboured in vain among you.€€ƒx]Be ye as I, because I also am as you brethren, I beseech you. You have not injured me at all.€€ƒxzAnd you know how, through infirmity of the flesh, I preached the gospel to you heretofore: and your temptation in my flesh€€ƒxYYou despised not, nor rejected: but received me as an angel of God, even as Christ Jesus.€€ƒx›Where is then your blessedness? For I bear you witness that, if it could be done, you would have plucked out your own eyes and would have given them to me.€€ƒx:Am I then become your enemy, because I tell you the truth?€€ƒxiThey are zealous in your regard not well: but they would exclude you, that you might be zealous for them.€€ƒxfBut be zealous for that which is good in a good thing always: and not only when I am present with you.€€ƒxPMy little children, of whom I am in labour again, until Christ be formed in you.€€ƒx`And I would willingly be present with you now and change my voice: because I am ashamed for you.€€ƒxHTell me, you that desire to be under the law, have you not read the law?€€ƒxbFor it is written that Abraham had two sons: the one by a bondwoman and the other by a free woman.€€ƒxiBut he who was of the bondwoman was born according to the flesh: but he of the free woman was by promise.€€ƒx‰Which things are said by an allegory. For these are the two testaments. The one from Mount Sina, engendering unto bondage, which is Agar.€€ƒxzFor Sina is a mountain in Arabia, which hath affinity to that Jerusalem which now is: and is in bondage with her children.€€ƒx?But that Jerusalem which is above is free: which is our mother.€€ƒx¹For it is written: Rejoice, thou barren, that bearest not: break forth and cry thou that travailest not: for many are the children of the desolate, more than of her that hath a husband.€€ƒx<Now we, brethren, as Isaac was, are the children of promise.€€ƒxpBut as then he that was born according to the flesh persecuted him that was after the spirit: so also it is now.€€ƒxBut what saith the scripture? Cast out the bondwoman and her son: for the son of the bondwoman shall not be heir with the son of the free woman.€€ƒx~So then, brethren, we are not the children of the bondwoman but of the free: by the freedom wherewith Christ has made us free.€€ƒ`€€ƒx;Stand fast and be not held again under the yoke of bondage.€€ƒxUBehold, I Paul tell you, that if you be circumcised, Christ shall profit you nothing.€€ƒx^And I testify again to every man circumcising himself that he is a debtor to do the whole law.€€ƒxYYou are made void of Christ, you who are justified in the law: you are fallen from grace.€€˜ƒxrFor in Christ Jesus neither circumcision availeth any thing nor uncircumcision: but faith that worketh by Charity.€€ƒxMYou did run well. What hath hindered you, that you should not obey the truth?€€ƒx1This persuasion is not from him that calleth you.€€ƒx*A little leaven corrupteth the whole lump.€€ƒxI have confidence in you in the Lord that you will not be of another mind: but he that troubleth you shall bear the judgment, whosoever he be.€€ƒx{And I, brethren, if I yet preach circumcision, why do I yet suffer persecution? Then is the scandal of the cross made void.€€ƒx0I would they were even cut off, who trouble you.€€ƒx‘For you, brethren, have been called unto liberty. Only make not liberty an occasion to the flesh: but by charity of the spirit serve one another.€€ƒxSFor all the law is fulfilled in one word: Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself.€€ƒxUBut if you bite and devour one another: take heed you be not consumed one of another.€€ƒxQI say then: Walk in the spirit: and you shall not fulfill the lusts of the flesh.€€ƒx For the flesh lusteth against the spirit: and the spirit against the flesh: For these are contrary one to another: so that you do not the things that you would.€€ƒx<But if you are led by the spirit, you are not under the law.€€ƒx_Now the works of the flesh are manifest: which are fornication, uncleanness, immodesty, luxury,€€ƒx_Idolatry, witchcrafts, enmities, contentions, emulations, wraths, quarrels, dissensions, sects,€€ƒx²Envies, murders, drunkenness, revellings, and such like. Of the which I foretell you, as I have foretold to you, that they who do such things shall not obtain the kingdom of God.€€ƒx`But the fruit of the Spirit is, charity, joy, peace, patience, benignity, goodness, longanimity,€€ƒxMMildness, faith, modesty, continency, chastity. Against such there is no law.€€ƒxYAnd they that are Christ's have crucified their flesh, with the vices and concupiscences.€€ƒx9If we live in the Spirit, let us also walk in the Spirit.€€ƒxVLet us not be made desirous of vain glory, provoking one another, envying one another.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¨Brethren, and if a man be overtaken in any fault, you, who are spiritual, instruct such a one in the spirit of meekness, considering thyself, lest thou also be tempted.€€ƒxIBear ye one another's burdens: and so you shall fulfil the law of Christ.€€ƒx[For if any man think himself to be some thing, whereas he is nothing, he deceiveth himself.€€ƒxdBut let every one prove his own work: and so he shall have glory in himself only and not in another.€€’ƒxgAnd let him that is instructed in the word communicate to him that instructeth him, in all good things.€€ƒx#Be not deceived: God is not mocked.€€ƒxÏFor what things a man shall sow, those also shall he reap. For he that soweth in his flesh of the flesh also shall reap corruption. But he that soweth in the spirit of the spirit shall reap life everlasting.€€ƒxOAnd in doing good, let us not fail. For in due time we shall reap, not failing.€€ƒx{Therefore, whilst we have time, let us work good to all men, but especially to those who are of the household of the faith.€€ƒx9See what a letter I have written to you with my own hand.€€ƒx™For as many as desire to please in the flesh, they constrain you to be circumcised, only that they may not suffer the persecution of the cross of Christ.€€ƒxŠFor neither they themselves who are circumcised keep the law: but they will have you to be circumcised, that they may glory in your flesh.€€ƒx‰But God forbid that I should glory, save in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ: by whom the world is crucified to me, and I to the world.€€ƒxdFor in Christ Jesus neither circumcision availeth any thing, nor uncircumcision: but a new creature.€€ƒxZAnd whosoever shall follow this rule, peace on them and mercy: and upon the Israel of God.€€ƒxcFrom henceforth let no man be troublesome to me: for I bear the marks of the Lord Jesus in my body.€€ƒxGThe grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit, brethren. Amen.€€ƒxThe Letter to the Ephesians€€ƒ`€€ƒxPaul, an apostle of Jesus Christ, by the will of God, to all the saints who are at Ephesus and to the faithful in Christ Jesus.€€ƒxNGrace be to you and peace, from God the Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒxƒBlessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who hath blessed us with spiritual blessings in heavenly places, in Christ:€€cEph¤dnameiEphesiansiosis_namecEphlabbreviationcEphhchapters†—ƒxZUnto the praise of the glory of his grace, in which he hath graced us, in his beloved son.€€ƒxkIn whom we have redemption through his blood, the remission of sins, according to the riches of his, grace,€€ƒx;Which hath superabounded in us, in all wisdom and prudence,€€ƒxxThat he might make known unto us the mystery of his will, according to his good pleasure, which he hath purposed in him,€€ƒx{In the dispensation of the fulness of times, to re-establish all things in Christ, that are in heaven and on earth, in him.€€ƒx“In whom we also are called by lot, being predestinated according to the purpose of him who worketh all things according to the counsel of his will.€€ƒxKThat we may be unto the praise of his glory: we who before hoped in Christ:€€ƒx In whom you also, after you had heard the word of truth (the gospel of your salvation), in whom also believing, you were signed with the holy Spirit of promise.€€ƒxgWho is the pledge of our inheritance, unto the redemption of acquisition, unto the praise of his glory.€€ƒxkWherefore, I also, hearing of your faith that is in the Lord Jesus and of your love towards all the saints,€€ƒxLCease not to give thanks for you, making commemoration of you in my prayers,€€ƒxThat the God of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of glory, may give unto you the spirit of wisdom and of revelation, in the knowledge of him:€€ƒx›The eyes of your heart enlightened that you may know what the hope is of his calling and what are the riches of the glory of his inheritance in the saints.€€ƒx~And what is the exceeding greatness of his power towards us, who believe according to the operation of the might of his power,€€ƒxrWhich he wrought in Christ, raising him up from the dead and setting him on his right hand in the heavenly places.€€ƒx‘Above all principality and power and virtue and dominion and every name that is named, not only in this world, but also in that which is to come.€€ƒx[And he hath subjected all things under his feet and hath made him head over all the church,€€ƒxBWhich is his body and the fulness of him who is filled all in all.€€ƒ`€€ƒx6And you, when you were dead in your offences and sins,€€ƒx´Wherein in time past you walked according to the course of this world, according to the prince of the power of this air, of the spirit that now worketh on the children of unbelief:€€ƒx·In which also we all conversed in time past, in the desires of our flesh, fulfilling the will of the flesh and of our thoughts, and were by nature children of wrath, even as the rest:€€ƒxNBut God (who is rich in mercy) for his exceeding charity wherewith he loved us€€–ƒxjAnd hath raised us up together and hath made us sit together in the heavenly places, through Christ Jesus.€€ƒxrThat he might shew in the ages to come the abundant riches of his grace, in his bounty towards us in Christ Jesus.€€ƒx`For by grace you are saved through faith: and that not of yourselves, for it is the gift of God.€€ƒx$Not of works, that no man may glory.€€ƒxwFor we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus in good works, which God hath prepared that we should walk in them.€€ƒx¬For which cause be mindful that you, being heretofore gentiles is the flesh, who are called uncircumcision by that which is called circumcision in the flesh, made by hands:€€ƒx´That you were at that time without Christ, being aliens from the conversation of Israel and strangers to the testament, having no hope of the promise and without God in this world.€€ƒx`But now in Christ Jesus, you, who some time were afar off, are made nigh by the blood of Christ.€€ƒxwFor he is our peace, who hath made both one, and breaking down the middle wall of partition, the enmities in his flesh:€€ƒx~Making void the law of commandments contained in decrees: that he might make the two in himself into one new man, making peace€€ƒxZAnd might reconcile both to God in one body by the cross, killing the enmities in himself.€€ƒxZAnd coming, he preached peace to you that were afar off: and peace to them that were nigh.€€ƒx;For by him we have access both in one Spirit to the Father.€€ƒx}Now therefore you are no more strangers and foreigners: but you are fellow citizens with the saints and the domestics of God,€€ƒxjBuilt upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ himself being the chief corner stone:€€ƒx\In whom all the building, being framed together, groweth up into an holy temple in the Lord.€€ƒxLIn whom you also are built together into an habitation of God in the Spirit.€€ƒ`€€ƒxGFor this cause, I Paul, the prisoner of Jesus Christ, for you Gentiles:€€ƒx\If yet you have heard of the dispensation of the grace of God which is given me towards you:€€ƒxqHow that, according to revelation, the mystery has been made known to me, as I have written above in a few words:€€ƒxEAs you reading, may understand my knowledge in the mystery of Christ,€€•ƒx{That the Gentiles should be fellow heirs and of the same body: and copartners of his promise in Christ Jesus, by the gospel€€ƒx‡Of which I am made a minister, according to the gift of the grace of God, which is given to me according to the operation of his power.€€ƒxxTo me, the least of all the saints, is given this grace, to preach among the Gentiles the unsearchable riches of Christ:€€ƒx—And to enlighten all men, that they may see what is the dispensation of the mystery which hath been hidden from eternity in God who created all things:€€ƒxyThat the manifold wisdom of God may be made known to the principalities and powers in heavenly places through the church,€€ƒxHAccording to the eternal purpose which he made in Christ Jesus our Lord:€€ƒxHIn whom we have boldness and access with confidence by the faith of him.€€ƒxRWherefore I pray you not to faint at my tribulations for you, which is your glory.€€ƒxEFor this cause I bow my knees to the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ,€€ƒx3Of whom all paternity in heaven and earth is named:€€ƒxThat he would grant you, according to the riches of his glory, to be strengthened by his Spirit with might unto the inward man:€€ƒxYThat Christ may dwell by faith in your hearts: that, being rooted and founded in charity,€€ƒxhYou may be able to comprehend, with all the saints, what is the breadth and length and height and depth,€€ƒxwTo know also the charity of Christ, which surpasseth all knowledge: that you may be filled unto all the fulness of God.€€ƒx€Now to him who is able to do all things more abundantly than we desire or understand, according to the power that worketh in us:€€ƒxaTo him be glory in the church and in Christ Jesus, unto all generations, world without end. Amen.€€ƒ`€€ƒxnI therefore, a prisoner in the Lord, beseech you that you walk worthy of the vocation in which you are called:€€ƒxQWith all humility and mildness, with patience, supporting one another in charity.€€ƒx=Careful to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace.€€ƒxGOne body and one Spirit: as you are called in one hope of your calling.€€˜ ƒxLOne God and Father of all, who is above all, and through all, and in us all.€€ƒxXBut to every one of us is given grace, according to the measure of the giving of Christ.€€ƒxVWherefore he saith: Ascending on high, he led captivity captive: he gave gifts to men.€€ƒxhNow that he ascended, what is it, but because he also descended first into the lower parts of the earth?€€ƒxfHe that descended is the same also that ascended above all the heavens: that he might fill all things.€€ƒxmAnd he gave some apostles, and some prophets, and other some evangelists, and other some pastors and doctors:€€ƒxgFor the perfecting of the saints, for the word of the ministry, for the edifying of the body of Christ:€€ƒx›Until we all meet into the unity of faith and of the knowledge of the Son of God, unto a perfect man, unto the measure of the age of the fulness of Christ:€€ƒx½That henceforth we be no more children tossed to and fro and carried about with every wind of doctrine, by the wickedness of men, by cunning craftiness by which they lie in wait to deceive.€€ƒxaBut doing the truth in charity, we may in all things grow up in him who is the head, even Christ:€€ƒxáFrom whom the whole body, being compacted and fitly joined together, by what every joint supplieth, according to the operation in the measure of every part, maketh increase of the body, unto the edifying of itself in charity.€€ƒx~This then I say and testify in the Lord: That henceforward you walk not as also the Gentiles walk in the vanity of their mind:€€ƒxšHaving their understanding darkened: being alienated from the life of God through the ignorance that is in them, because of the blindness of their hearts.€€ƒxrWho despairing have given themselves up to lasciviousness, unto the working of all uncleanness, unto covetousness.€€ƒx#But you have not so learned Christ:€€ƒxWIf so be that you have heard him and have been taught in him, as the truth is in Jesus:€€ƒxmTo put off, according to former conversation, the old man, who is corrupted according to the desire of error.€€ƒx&And be renewed in spirit of your mind:€€ƒxYAnd put on the new man, who according to God is created in justice and holiness of truth.€€ƒxsWherefore, putting away lying, speak ye the truth, every man with his neighbour. For we are members one of another.€€ƒx?Be angry: and sin not. Let not the sun go down upon your anger.€€ƒxGive not place to the devil.€€ƒx³He that stole, let him now steal no more: but rather let him labour, working with his hands the thing which is good, that he may have something to give to him that suffereth need.€€ƒxLet no evil speech proceed from your mouth: but that which is good, to the edification of faith: that it may administer grace to the hearers.€€ƒxYAnd grieve not the holy Spirit of God: whereby you are sealed unto the day of redemption.€€ƒxmLet all bitterness and anger and indignation and clamour and blasphemy be put away from you, with all malice.€€ƒxhAnd be ye kind one to another: merciful, forgiving one another, even as God hath forgiven you in Christ.€€ƒ`€€ƒx8Be ye therefore followers of God, as most dear children:€€ƒxAnd walk in love, as Christ also hath loved us and hath delivered himself for us, an oblation and a sacrifice to God for an odour of sweetness.€€ƒxrBut fornication and all uncleanness or covetousness, let it not so much as be named among you, as becometh saints:€€ƒxcOr obscenity or foolish talking or scurrility, which is to no purpose: but rather giving of thanks.€€˜!ƒxzLet no man deceive you with vain words. For because of these things cometh the anger of God upon the children of unbelief.€€ƒx(Be ye not therefore partakers with them.€€ƒx`For you were heretofore darkness, but now light in the Lord. Walk then as children of the light.€€ƒxDFor the fruit of the light is in all goodness and justice and truth:€€ƒx%Proving what is well pleasing to God.€€ƒxVAnd have no fellowship with the unfruitful works of darkness: but rather reprove them.€€ƒxOFor the things that are done by them in secret, it is a shame even to speak of.€€ƒxhBut all things that are reproved are made manifest by the light: for all that is made manifest is light.€€ƒxgWherefore he saith: Rise, thou that sleepest, and arise from the dead: and Christ shall enlighten thee.€€ƒxCSee therefore, brethren, how you walk circumspectly: not as unwise,€€ƒx;But as wise: redeeming the time, because the days are evil.€€ƒxHWherefore, become not unwise: but understanding what is the will of God.€€ƒxUAnd be not drunk with wine, wherein is luxury: but be ye filled with the Holy Spirit,€€ƒxySpeaking to yourselves in psalms and hymns and spiritual canticles, singing and making melody in your hearts to the Lord:€€ƒxaGiving thanks always for all things, in the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, to God and the Father:€€ƒx4Being subject one to another, in the fear of Christ.€€ƒx7Let women be subject to their husbands, as to the Lord:€€ƒxpBecause the husband is the head of the wife, as Christ is the head of the church. He is the saviour of his body.€€ƒxgTherefore as the church is subject to Christ: so also let the wives be to their husbands in all things.€€ƒx[Husbands, love your wives, as Christ also loved the church and delivered himself up for it:€€ƒxRThat he might sanctify it, cleansing it by the laver of water in the word of life:€€ƒx•That he might present it to himself, a glorious church, not having spot or wrinkle or any such thing; but that it should be holy and without blemish.€€ƒxbSo also ought men to love their wives as their own bodies. He that loveth his wife loveth himself.€€ƒxfFor no man ever hated his own flesh, but nourisheth and cherisheth it, as also Christ doth the church:€€ƒxCBecause we are members of him, body, of his flesh and of his bones.€€ƒxyFor this cause shall a man leave his father and mother: and shall cleave to his wife. And they shall be two in one flesh.€€ƒxCThis is a great sacrament: but I speak in Christ and in the church.€€ƒxqNevertheless, let every one of you in particular love for his wife as himself: And let the wife fear her husband.€€ƒ`€€ƒx:Children, obey your parents in the Lord: for this is just.€€ƒxPHonour thy father and thy mother, which is the first commandment with a promise:€€ƒxHThat it may be well with thee, and thou mayest be long lived upon earth.€€ƒxuAnd you, fathers, provoke not your children to anger: but bring them up in the discipline and correction of the Lord.€€˜ƒxvNot serving to the eye, as it were pleasing men: but, as the servants of Christ, doing the will of God from the heart.€€ƒx9With a good will serving, as to the Lord, and not to men.€€ƒxyKnowing that whatsoever good thing any man shall do, the same shall he receive from the Lord, whether he be bond or free.€€ƒx¬And you, masters, do the same things to them, forbearing threatenings: knowing that the Lord both of them and you is in heaven. And there is no respect of persons with him.€€ƒxMFinally, brethren, be strengthened in the Lord and in the might of his power.€€ƒx]Put you on the armour of God, that you may be able to stand against the deceits of the devil.€€ƒxÀFor our wrestling is not against flesh and blood; but against principalities and powers, against the rulers of the world of this darkness, against the spirits of wickedness in the high places.€€ƒx~Therefore, take unto you the armour of God, that you may be able to resist in the evil day and to stand in all things perfect.€€ƒxbStand therefore, having your loins girt about with truth and having on the breastplate of justice:€€ƒx?And your feet shod with the preparation of the gospel of peace.€€ƒx}In all things taking the shield of faith, wherewith you may be able to extinguish all the fiery darts of the most wicked one.€€ƒxaAnd take unto you the helmet of salvation and the sword of the Spirit (which is the word of God).€€ƒx’By all prayer and supplication praying at all times in the spirit: and in the same watching with all instance and supplication for all the saints:€€ƒx{And for me, that speech may be given me, that I may open my mouth with confidence, to make known the mystery of the gospel,€€ƒxeFor which I am an ambassador in a chain: so that therein I may be bold to speak according as I ought.€€ƒx­But that you also may know the things that concern me and what I am doing, Tychicus, my dearest brother and faithful minister in the Lord, will make known to you all things:€€ƒxWhom I have sent to you for this same purpose: that you may know the things concerning us, and that he may comfort your hearts.€€ƒx_Peace be to the brethren and charity with faith, from God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒxMGrace be with all them that love our Lord Jesus Christ in incorruption. Amen.€€ƒxThe Letter to the Philippians€€ƒ`€€ƒx„Paul and Timothy, the servants of Jesus Christ: to all the saints in Christ Jesus who are at Philippi, with the bishops and deacons.€€ƒxPGrace be unto you and peace, from God our Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒx4I give thanks to my God in every remembrance of you:€€dPhil¤dnamekPhilippiansiosis_namedPhillabbreviationdPhilhchapters„˜ƒx{Being confident of this very thing: that he who hath begun a good work in you will perfect it unto the day of Christ Jesus.€€ƒxºAs it is meet for me to think this for you all, for that I have you in my heart; and that, in my bands and in the defence and confirmation of the gospel, you all are partakers of my joy.€€ƒxMFor God is my witness how I long after you all in the bowels of Jesus Christ.€€ƒxbAnd this I pray: That your charity may more and more abound in knowledge and in all understanding:€€ƒxkThat you may approve the better things: that you may be sincere and without offence unto the day of Christ:€€ƒxYFilled with the fruit of justice, through Jesus Christ, unto the glory and praise of God.€€ƒxŠNow, brethren, I desire you should know that the things which have happened to me have fallen out rather to the furtherance of the gospel:€€ƒxWSo that my bands are made manifest in Christ, in all the court and in all other places.€€ƒx~And many of the brethren in the Lord, growing confident by my bands, are much more bold to speak the word of God without fear.€€ƒxXSome indeed, even out of envy and contention: but some also for good will preach Christ.€€ƒxISome out of charity, knowing that I am set for the defence of the gospel.€€ƒxiAnd some out of contention preach Christ not sincerely: supposing that they raise affliction to my bands.€€ƒxˆBut what then? So that by all means, whether by occasion or by truth, Christ be preached: in this also I rejoice, yea, and will rejoice.€€ƒx{For I know that this shall fall out to me unto salvation, through your prayer and the supply of the Spirit of Jesus Christ,€€ƒxÆAccording to my expectation and hope; that in nothing I shall be confounded: but with all confidence, as always, so now also, shall Christ be magnified in my body, whether it be by life or by death.€€ƒx1For to me, to live is Christ: and to die is gain.€€ƒxcAnd if to live in the flesh: this is to me the fruit of labour. And what I shall choose I know not.€€ƒxrBut I am straitened between two: having a desire to be dissolved and to be with Christ, a thing by far the better.€€ƒx3But to abide still in the flesh is needful for you.€€ƒxwAnd having this confidence, I know that I shall abide and continue with you all, for your furtherance and joy of faith:€€ƒxQThat your rejoicing may abound in Christ Jesus for me, by my coming to you again.€€ƒxãOnly let your conversation be worthy of the gospel of Christ: that, whether I come and see you, or, being absent, may hear of you, that you stand fast in one spirit, with one mind labouring together for the faith of the gospel.€€ƒx…And in nothing be ye terrified by the adversaries: which to them is a cause of perdition, but to you of salvation, and this from God.€€ƒx\For unto you it is given for Christ, not only to believe in him, but also to suffer for him:€€ƒxTHaving the same conflict as that which you have seen in me and now have heard of me.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‰If there be therefore any consolation in Christ, if any comfort of charity, if any society of the spirit, if any bowels of commiseration:€€ƒxoFulfil ye my joy, that you be of one mind, having the same charity, being of one accord, agreeing in sentiment.€€ƒx~Let nothing be done through contention: neither by vain glory. But in humility, let each esteem others better than themselves:€€ƒxUEach one not considering the things that are his own, but those that are other men's.€€˜ƒxJWho being in the form of God, thought it not robbery to be equal with God:€€ƒxrBut emptied himself, taking the form of a servant, being made in the likeness of men, and in habit found as a man.€€ƒxQHe humbled himself, becoming obedient unto death, even to the death of the cross.€€ƒx^For which cause, God also hath exalted him and hath given him a name which is above all names:€€ƒxlThat in the name of Jesus every knee should bow, of those that are in heaven, on earth, and under the earth:€€ƒxbAnd that every tongue should confess that the Lord Jesus Christ is in the glory of God the Father.€€ƒx¦Wherefore, my dearly beloved, (as you have always obeyed, not as in my presence only but much more now in my absence) with fear and trembling work out your salvation.€€ƒx]For it is God who worketh in you, both to will and to accomplish, according to his good will.€€ƒx8And do ye all things without murmurings and hesitations:€€ƒx§That you may be blameless and sincere children of God, without reproof, in the midst of a crooked and perverse generation: among whom you shine as lights in the world.€€ƒxvHolding forth the word of life to my glory in the day of Christ: because I have not run in vain, nor laboured in vain.€€ƒxuYea, and if I be made a victim upon the sacrifice and service of your faith, I rejoice and congratulate with you all.€€ƒxIAnd for the selfsame thing, do you also rejoice and congratulate with me.€€ƒx‰And I hope in the Lord Jesus to send Timothy unto you shortly, that I also may be of good comfort, when I know the things concerning you.€€ƒxXFor I have no man so of the same mind, who with sincere affection is solicitous for you.€€ƒxRFor all seek the things that are their own not the things that are Jesus Christ's.€€ƒxeNow know ye the proof of him: that as a son with the father, so hath he served with me in the gospel.€€ƒxaHim therefore I hope to send unto you immediately: so soon as I shall see how it will go with me.€€ƒxEAnd I trust in the Lord that I myself also shall come to you shortly.€€ƒxªBut I have thought it necessary to send to you Epaphroditus, my brother and fellow labourer and fellow soldier, but your apostle: and he that hath ministered to my wants.€€ƒxYFor indeed he longed after you all: and was sad, for that you had heard that he was sick.€€ƒxFor indeed he was sick, nigh unto death: but God had mercy on him. And not only on him, but on me also, lest I should have sorrow upon sorrow.€€ƒxmTherefore, I sent him the more speedily: that seeing him again, you may rejoice, and I may be without sorrow.€€ƒxTReceive him therefore with all joy in the Lord: and treat with honour such as he is.€€ƒxŸBecause for the work of Christ he came to the point of death: delivering his life, that he might fulfil that which on your part was wanting towards my service.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŠAs to the rest, my brethren, rejoice in the Lord. To write the same things to you, to me indeed is not wearisome, but to you is necessary.€€ƒx@Beware of dogs: beware of evil workers: beware of the concision.€€ƒxsFor we are the circumcision, who in spirit serve God and glory in Christ Jesus, not having confidence in the flesh.€€ƒxtThough I might also have confidence in the flesh. If any other thinketh he may have confidence in the flesh, I more:€€•ƒxxAccording to zeal, persecuting the church of God: According to the justice that is in the law, conversing without blame.€€ƒxMBut the things that were gain to me, the same I have counted loss for Christ.€€ƒxÈFurthermore, I count all things to be but loss for the excellent knowledge of Jesus Christ, my Lord: for whom I have suffered the loss of all things and count them but as dung, that I may gain Christ.€€ƒx—And may be found in him, not having my justice, which is of the law, but that which is of the faith of Christ Jesus, which is of God: justice in faith.€€ƒx€That I may know him and the power of his resurrection and the fellowship of his sufferings: being made conformable to his death,€€ƒxHIf by any means I may attain to the resurrection which is from the dead.€€ƒx¢Not as though I had already attained, or were already perfect: but I follow after, if I may by any means apprehend, wherein I am also apprehended by Christ Jesus.€€ƒx¤Brethren, I do not count myself to have apprehended. But one thing I do: Forgetting the things that are behind and stretching forth myself to those that are before,€€ƒxWI press towards the mark, to the prize of the supernal vocation of God in Christ Jesus.€€ƒxˆLet us therefore, as many as are perfect, be thus minded: and if in any thing you be otherwise minded, this also God will reveal to you,€€ƒxhNevertheless, whereunto we are come, that we be of the same mind, let us also continue in the same rule.€€ƒxTBe ye followers of me, brethren: and observe them who walk so as you have our model.€€ƒxuFor many walk, of whom I have told you often (and now tell you weeping) that they are enemies of the cross of Christ:€€ƒxoWhose end is destruction: whose God is their belly: and whose glory is in their shame: who mind earthly things.€€ƒxcBut our conversation is in heaven: from whence also we look for the Saviour, our Lord Jesus Christ,€€ƒx¢Who will reform the body of our lowness, made like to the body of his glory, according to the operation whereby also he is able to subdue all things unto himself.€€ƒ`€€ƒxyTherefore my dearly beloved brethren and most desired, my joy and my crown: so stand fast in the Lord, my dearly beloved.€€ƒxEI beg of Evodia and I beseech Syntyche to be of one mind in the Lord.€€ƒx¿And I entreat thee also, my sincere companion, help those women who have laboured with me in the gospel, with Clement and the rest of my fellow labourers, whose names are in the book of life.€€ƒx2Rejoice in the Lord always: again, I say, rejoice.€€—ƒx‚Be nothing solicitous: but in every thing, by prayer and supplication, with thanksgiving, let your petitions be made known to God.€€ƒxeAnd the peace of God, which surpasseth all understanding, keep your hearts and minds in Christ Jesus.€€ƒxàFor the rest, brethren, whatsoever things are true, whatsoever modest, whatsoever just, whatsoever holy, whatsoever lovely, whatsoever of good fame, if there be any virtue, if any praise of discipline: think on these things.€€ƒx‚The things which you have both learned and received and heard and seen in me, these do ye: and the God of peace shall be with you.€€ƒxNow I rejoice in the Lord exceedingly that now at length your thought for me hath flourished again, as you did also think; but you were busied.€€ƒxgI speak not as it were for want. For I have learned, in whatsoever state I am, to be content therewith.€€ƒx±I know both how to be brought low, and I know how to abound (every where and in all things I am instructed): both to be full and to be hungry: both to abound and to suffer need.€€ƒx0I can do all things in him who strengtheneth me.€€ƒxDNevertheless, you have done well in communicating to my tribulation.€€ƒx·And you also know, O Philippians, that in the beginning of the gospel, when I departed from Macedonia, no church communicated with me as concerning giving and receiving, but you only.€€ƒx>For unto Thessalonica also you sent once and again for my use.€€ƒxONot that I seek the gift: but I seek the fruit that may abound to your account.€€ƒxŸBut I have all and abound: I am filled, having received from Epaphroditus the things you sent, an odour of sweetness, an acceptable sacrifice, pleasing to God.€€ƒxVAnd may my God supply all your want, according to his riches in glory in Christ Jesus.€€ƒx<Now to God and our Father be glory, world without end. Amen.€€ƒx&Salute ye every saint in Christ Jesus.€€ƒxsThe brethren who are with me salute you. All the saints salute you: especially they that are of Caesar's household.€€ƒx=The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit. Amen.€€ƒxThe Letter to the Colossians€€ƒ`€€ƒxMPaul, an apostle of Jesus Christ, by the will of God, and Timothy, a brother:€€ƒxGTo the saints and faithful brethren in Christ Jesus who are at Colossa.€€ƒx¥Grace be to you and peace, from God our Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ. We give thanks to God and the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, praying always for you.€€cCol¤dnamejColossiansiosis_namecCollabbreviationcColhchapters„˜ƒx²Which is come unto you, as also it is in the whole world and bringeth forth fruit and groweth, even as it doth in you, since the day you heard and knew the grace of God in truth.€€ƒxpAs you learned of Epaphras, our most beloved fellow servant, who is for you a faithful minister of Christ Jesus;€€ƒx1Who also hath manifested your love in the spirit.€€ƒx¸Therefore we also, from the day that we heard it, cease not to pray for you and to beg that you may be filled with the knowledge of his will, in all wisdom and spiritual understanding:€€ƒx‚That you may walk worthy of God, in all things pleasing; being fruitful in every good work and increasing in the knowledge of God:€€ƒxlStrengthened with all might according to the power of his glory, in all patience and longsuffering with joy,€€ƒxkGiving thanks to God the Father, who hath made us worthy to be partakers of the lot of the saints in light:€€ƒxpWho hath delivered us from the power of darkness and hath translated us into the kingdom of the Son of his love,€€ƒxDIn whom we have redemption through his blood, the remission of sins:€€ƒxGWho is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of every creature:€€ƒx»For in him were all things created in heaven and on earth, visible and invisible, whether thrones, or dominations, or principalities, or powers. All things were created by him and in him.€€ƒx4And he is before all: and by him all things consist.€€ƒxŠAnd he is the head of the body, the church: who is the beginning, the firstborn from the dead, that in all things he may hold the primacy:€€ƒxNBecause in him, it hath well pleased the Father that all fulness should dwell:€€ƒx­And through him to reconcile all things unto himself, making peace through the blood of his cross, both as to the things that are on earth and the things that are in heaven.€€ƒxPAnd you, whereas you were some time alienated and enemies in mind in evil works:€€ƒx~Yet now he hath reconciled in the body of his flesh through death, to present you holy and unspotted and blameless before him:€€ƒxÕIf so ye continue in the faith, grounded and settled, and immoveable from the hope of the gospel which you have heard, which is preached in all the creation that is under heaven: whereof I Paul am made a minister.€€ƒxŸWho now rejoice in my sufferings for you and fill up those things that are wanting of the sufferings of Christ, in my flesh, for his body, which is the church:€€ƒx„Whereof I am made a minister according to the dispensation of God, which is given me towards you, that I may fulfil the word of God:€€ƒxbThe mystery which hath been hidden from ages and generations, but now is manifested to his saints,€€ƒxƒTo whom God would make known the riches of the glory of this mystery among the Gentiles, which is Christ, in you the hope of glory.€€ƒx‚Whom we preach, admonishing every man and teaching every man in all wisdom, that we may present every man perfect in Christ Jesus.€€ƒxYWherein also I labour, striving according to his working which he worketh in me in power.€€ƒ`€€ƒxFor I would have you know what manner of care I have for you and for them that are at Laodicea and whosoever have not seen my face in the flesh:€€ƒx¹That their hearts may be comforted, being instructed in charity and unto all riches of fulness of understanding, unto the knowledge of the mystery of God the Father and of Christ Jesus:€€ƒx:In whom are hid all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge.€€ƒxBNow this I say, that no man may deceive you by loftiness of words.€€—ƒxEAs therefore you have received Jesus Christ the Lord, walk ye in him:€€ƒxrRooted and built up in him and confirmed in the faith, as also you have learned: abounding in him in thanksgiving.€€ƒx¢Beware lest any man cheat you by philosophy and vain deceit: according to the tradition of men according to the elements of the world and not according to Christ.€€ƒx?For in him dwelleth all the fulness of the Godhead corporeally.€€ƒxIAnd you are filled in him, who is the head of all principality and power.€€ƒxIn whom also you are circumcised with circumcision not made by hand in despoiling of the body of the flesh: but in the circumcision of Christ.€€ƒx‡Buried with him in baptism: in whom also you are risen again by the faith of the operation of God who hath raised him up from the dead.€€ƒxAnd you, when you were dead in your sins and the uncircumcision of your flesh, he hath quickened together with him, forgiving you all offences:€€ƒxŸBlotting out the handwriting of the decree that was against us, which was contrary to us. And he hath taken the same out of the way, fastening it to the cross.€€ƒx}And despoiling the principalities and powers, he hath exposed them confidently in open shew, triumphing over them in himself.€€ƒxyLet no man therefore judge you in meat or in drink or in respect of a festival day or of the new moon or of the sabbaths,€€ƒx@Which are a shadow of things to come: but the body is of Christ.€€ƒxLet no man seduce you, willing in humility and religion of angels, walking in the things which he hath not seen, in vain puffed up by the sense of his flesh:€€ƒxšAnd not holding the head, from which the whole body, by joints and bands, being supplied with nourishment and compacted, groweth into the increase of God.€€ƒxuIf then you be dead with Christ from the elements of this world, why do you yet decree as though living in the world?€€ƒx!Touch not: taste not: handle not.€€ƒx_Which all are unto destruction by the very use, according to the precepts and doctrines of men.€€ƒxWhich things have indeed a shew of wisdom in superstition and humility, and not sparing the body; not in any honour to the filling of the flesh.€€ƒ`€€ƒxxTherefore if you be risen with Christ, seek the things that are above, where Christ is sitting at the right hand of God.€€ƒxGMind the things that are above, not the things that are upon the earth.€€ƒx:For you are dead: and your life is hid with Christ in God.€€ƒxYWhen Christ shall appear, who is your life, then you also shall appear with him in glory.€€˜ƒxGFor which things the wrath of God cometh upon the children of unbelief.€€ƒx;In which you also walked some time, when you lived in them.€€ƒxfBut now put you also all away: anger, indignation, malice, blasphemy, filthy speech out of your mouth.€€ƒxKLie not one to another: stripping yourselves of the old man with his deeds,€€ƒxjAnd putting on the new, him who is renewed unto knowledge, according to the image of him that created him.€€ƒxWhere there is neither Gentile nor Jew, circumcision nor uncircumcision, Barbarian nor Scythian, bond nor free. But Christ is all and in all.€€ƒxxPut ye on therefore, as the elect of God, holy and beloved, the bowels of mercy, benignity, humility, modesty, patience:€€ƒxBearing with one another and forgiving one another, if any have a complaint against another. Even as the Lord hath forgiven you, so do you also.€€ƒxIBut above all these things have charity, which is the bond of perfection.€€ƒxpAnd let the peace of Christ rejoice in your hearts, wherein also you are called in one body: and be ye thankful.€€ƒxµLet the word of Christ dwell in you abundantly: in all wisdom, teaching and admonishing one another in psalms, hymns and spiritual canticles, singing in grace in your hearts to God.€€ƒx‚All whatsoever you do in word or in work, do all in the name of the Lord Jesus Christ, giving thanks to God and the Father by him.€€ƒx?Wives, be subject to your husbands, as it behoveth in the Lord.€€ƒx9Husbands, love your wives and be not bitter towards them.€€ƒxQChildren, obey your parents in all things: for this is well pleasing to the Lord.€€ƒxLFathers, provoke not your children to indignation, lest they be discouraged.€€ƒx“Servants, obey in all things your masters according to the flesh: not serving to the eye, as pleasing men: but in simplicity of heart, fearing God.€€ƒxHWhatsoever you do, do it from the heart, as to the Lord, and not to men:€€ƒx_Knowing that you shall receive of the Lord the reward of inheritance. Serve ye the Lord Christ.€€ƒxyFor he that doth wrong shall receive for that which he hath done wrongfully. And there is no respect of persons with God.€€ƒ`€€ƒxiMasters, do to your servants that which is just and equal: knowing that you also have a master in heaven.€€ƒx7Be instant in prayer: watching in it with thanksgiving.€€ƒx‚Praying withal for us also, that God may open unto us a door of speech to speak the mystery of Christ (for which also I am bound):€€ƒx0That I may make it manifest as I ought to speak.€€’ƒxkLet your speech be always in grace seasoned with salt: that you may know how you ought to answer every man.€€ƒx‹All the things that concern me, Tychicus, our dearest brother and faithful minister and fellow servant in the Lord, will make known to you.€€ƒxtWhat I have sent to you for this same purpose, that he may know the things that concern you and comfort your hearts:€€ƒxƒWith Onesimus, a most beloved and faithful brother, who is one of you. All things that are done here, they shall make known to you.€€ƒx§Aristarchus, my fellow prisoner, saluteth you: and Mark, the cousin german of Barnabas, touching whom you have received commandments. If he come unto you, receive him.€€ƒxAnd Jesus that is called Justus: who are of the circumcision. These only are my helpers, in the kingdom of God: who have been a comfort to me.€€ƒx­Epaphras saluteth you, who is one of you, a servant of Christ Jesus, who is always solicitous for you in prayers, that you may stand perfect and full in all the will of God.€€ƒxsFor I bear him testimony that he hath much labour for you and for them that are at Laodicea and them at Hierapolis.€€ƒx7Luke, the most dear physician, saluteth you: and Demas.€€ƒxYSalute the brethren who are at Laodicea: and Nymphas and the church that is in his house.€€ƒx£And when this epistle shall have been read with you, cause that it be read also in the church of the Laodiceans: and that you read that which is of the Laodiceans.€€ƒxjAnd say to Archippus: Take heed to the ministry which thou hast received in the Lord, that thou fulfil it.€€ƒxYThe salutation of Paul with my own hand. Be mindful of my bands. Grace be with you. Amen.€€ƒx%The First Letter to the Thessalonians€€ƒ`€€ƒxqPaul and Sylvanus and Timothy to the church of the Thessalonians: in God the Father and in the Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒx€Grace be to you and peace. We give thanks to God always for you all: making a remembrance of you in our prayers without ceasing,€€ƒx“Being mindful of the work of your faith and labour and charity: and of the enduring of the hope of our Lord Jesus Christ before God and our Father.€€f1Thess¤dnameoI Thessaloniansiosis_namef1Thesslabbreviationf1Thesshchapters…ŠƒxsAnd you became followers of us and of the Lord: receiving the word in much tribulation, with joy of the Holy Ghost:€€ƒxOSo that you were made a pattern to all that believe in Macedonia and in Achaia.€€ƒxÈFor from you was spread abroad the word of the Lord not only in Macedonia and in Achaia but also in every place: your faith which is towards God, is gone forth, so that we need not to speak any thing.€€ƒx”For they themselves relate of us, what manner of entering in we had unto you: and how you turned to God from idols to serve the living and true God.€€ƒx{And to wait for his Son from heaven (whom he raised up from the dead), Jesus, who hath delivered us from the wrath to come.€€ƒ`€€ƒxQFor yourselves know, brethren, our entrance in unto you, that it was not in vain:€€ƒxµBut having suffered many things before and been shamefully treated, (as you know) at Philippi, we had confidence in our God, to speak unto you the gospel of God in much carefulness.€€ƒxHFor our exhortation was not of error, nor of uncleanness, nor in deceit.€€ƒxBut as we were approved by God that the gospel should be committed to us: even so we speak, not as pleasing men but God, who proveth our hearts.€€”ƒx:Nor sought we glory of men, neither of you, nor of others.€€ƒx¢Whereas we might have been burdensome to you, as the apostles of Christ: but we became little ones in the midst of you, as if a nurse should cherish her children:€€ƒx‘So desirous of you, we would gladly impart unto you not only the gospel of God but also our own souls: because you were become most dear unto us.€€ƒxœFor you remember, brethren, our labour and toil: working night and day, lest we should be chargeable to any of you, we preached among you the gospel of God.€€ƒxpYou are witnesses, and God also, how holily and justly and without blame we have been to you that have believed:€€ƒxZAs you know in what manner, entreating and comforting you (as a father doth his children),€€ƒxsWe testified to every one of you that you would walk worthy of God, who hath called you unto his kingdom and glory.€€ƒxöTherefore, we also give thanks to God without ceasing: because, that when you had received of us the word of the hearing of God, you received it not as the word of men, but (as it is indeed) the word of God, who worketh in you that have believed.€€ƒxÉFor you, brethren, are become followers of the churches of God which are in Judea, in Christ Jesus: for you also have suffered the same things from your own countrymen, even as they have from the Jews:€€ƒx}Who both killed the Lord Jesus, and the prophets, and have persecuted us, and please not God, and are adversaries to all men;€€ƒx‘Prohibiting us to speak to the Gentiles, that they may be saved, to fill up their sins always: for the wrath of God is come upon them to the end.€€ƒx›But we, brethren, being taken away from you for a short time, in sight, not in heart, have hastened the more abundantly to see your face with great desire.€€ƒx[For we would have come unto you, I Paul indeed, once and again: but Satan hath hindered us.€€ƒxsFor what is our hope or joy or crown of glory? Are not you, in the presence of our Lord Jesus Christ at his coming?€€ƒxFor you are our glory and joy.€€ƒ`€€ƒxTFor which cause, forbearing no longer, we thought it good to remain at Athens alone.€€ƒx†And we sent Timothy, our brother and the minister of God in the gospel of Christ, to confirm you and exhort you concerning your faith:€€ƒxgThat no man should be moved in these tribulations: for yourselves know that we are appointed thereunto.€€ƒx}For even when we were with you, we foretold you that we should suffer tribulations: as also it is come to pass, and you know.€€ƒx¯But now when Timothy came to us from you and related to us your faith and charity, and that you have a good remembrance of us always, desiring to see us as we also to see you:€€ƒxcTherefore we were comforted, brethren, in you, in all our necessity and tribulation, by your faith.€€ƒx.Because now we live, if you stand in the Lord.€€ƒxiFor what thanks can we return to God for you, in all the joy wherewith we rejoice for you before our God,€€ƒxNight and day more abundantly praying that we may see your face and may accomplish those things that are wanting to your faith?€€ƒxRNow God himself and our Father and our Lord Jesus Christ, direct our way unto you.€€ƒx€And may the Lord multiply you and make you abound in charity towards one another and towards all men: as we do also towards you,€€ƒxTo confirm your hearts without blame, in holiness, before God and our Father, at the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, with all his saints. Amen.€€ƒ`€€ƒxËFor the rest therefore, brethren, pray and beseech you in the Lord Jesus that, as you have received from us, how you ought to walk and to please God, so also you would walk, that you may abound the more.€€ƒxAFor you know what precepts I have given to you by the Lord Jesus.€€ƒx[For this is the will of God, your sanctification: That you should abstain from fornication:€€ƒxYThat every one of you should know how to possess his vessel in sanctification and honour,€€’ƒx¥And that no man overreach nor circumvent his brother in business: because the Lord is the avenger of all these things, as we have told you before and have testified.€€ƒxEFor God hath not called us unto uncleanness, but unto sanctification.€€ƒxqTherefore, he that despiseth these things, despiseth not man, but God, who also hath given his holy Spirit in us.€€ƒx„But as touching the charity of brotherhood, we have no need to write to you: for yourselves have learned of God to love one another.€€ƒxsFor indeed you do it towards all the brethren in all Macedonia. But we entreat you, brethren, that you abound more:€€ƒxãAnd that you use your endeavour to be quiet: and that you do your own business and work with your own hands, as we commanded you: and that you walk honestly towards them that are without: and that you want nothing of any man's.€€ƒxˆAnd we will not have you ignorant brethren, concerning them that are asleep, that you be not sorrowful, even as others who have no hope.€€ƒxuFor if we believe that Jesus died and rose again: even so them who have slept through Jesus, will God bring with him.€€ƒx—For this we say unto you in the word of the Lord, that we who are alive, who remain unto the coming of the Lord, shall not prevent them who have slept.€€ƒx²For the Lord himself shall come down from heaven with commandment and with the voice of an archangel and with the trumpet of God: and the dead who are in Christ shall rise first.€€ƒxThen we who are alive, who are left, shall be taken up together with them in the clouds to meet Christ, into the air: and so shall we be always with the Lord.€€ƒx3Wherefore, comfort ye one another with these words.€€ƒ`€€ƒ`€€ƒxRBut of the times and moments, brethren, you need not, that we should write to you:€€ƒx]For yourselves know perfectly that the day of the Lord shall so come as a thief in the night.€€ƒxŸFor when they shall say: Peace and security; then shall sudden destruction come upon them, as the pains upon her that is with child, and they shall not escape.€€ƒxTBut you, brethren, are not in darkness, that the day should overtake you as a thief.€€˜ƒxJTherefore, let us not sleep, as others do: but let us watch, and be sober.€€ƒxYFor they that sleep, sleep in the night; and they that are drunk, are drunk in the night.€€ƒxƒBut let us, who are of the day, be sober, having on the breast plate of faith and charity and, for a helmet, the hope of salvation.€€ƒxhFor God hath not appointed us unto wrath: but unto the purchasing of salvation by our Lord Jesus Christ,€€ƒxPWho died for us: that, whether we watch or sleep, we may live together with him.€€ƒxJFor which cause comfort one another and edify one another, as you also do.€€ƒxnAnd we beseech you, brethren, to know them who labour among you and are over you in the Lord and admonish you;€€ƒx]That you esteem them more abundantly in charity, for their work's sake. Have peace with them.€€ƒxzAnd we beseech you, brethren, rebuke the unquiet: comfort the feeble minded: support the weak: be patient towards all men.€€ƒxySee that none render evil for evil to any man: but ever follow that which is good towards each other and towards all men.€€ƒoAlways rejoice.€€ƒuPray without ceasing.€€ƒxYIn all things give thanks for this is the will of God in Christ Jesus concerning you all.€€ƒxExtinguish not the spirit.€€ƒwDespise not prophecies.€€ƒx3But prove all things: hold fast that which is good.€€ƒx/From all appearance of evil refrain yourselves.€€ƒx¨And may the God of peace himself sanctify you in all things: that your whole spirit and soul and body may be preserved blameless in the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒx8He is faithful who hath called you, who also will do it.€€ƒvBrethren, pray for us.€€ƒx)Salute all the brethren with a holy kiss.€€ƒxLI charge you by the Lord that this epistle be read to all the holy brethren.€€ƒx5The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you. Amen.€€ƒx&The Second Letter to the Thessalonians€€ƒ`€€ƒxoPaul and Sylvanus and Timothy, to the church of the Thessalonians. In God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ,€€ƒxMGrace unto you: and peace from God our Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒx·We are bound to give thanks always to God for you, brethren, as it is fitting, because your faith groweth exceedingly and the charity of every one of you towards each other aboundeth.€€f2Thess¤dnamepII Thessaloniansiosis_namef2Thesslabbreviationf2ThesshchaptersƒŒƒxQSeeing it is a just thing with God to repay tribulation to them that trouble you:€€ƒxzAnd to you who are troubled, rest with us, when the Lord Jesus shall be revealed from heaven with the angels of his power:€€ƒxsIn a flame of fire, giving vengeance to them who know not God and who obey not the gospel of our Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒxnWho shall suffer eternal punishment in destruction, from the face of the Lord and from the glory of his power:€€ƒx¡When he shall come to be glorified in his saints and to be made wonderful in all them who have believed; because our testimony was believed upon you in that day.€€ƒxªWherefore also we pray always for you: That our God would make you worthy of his vocation and fulfil all the good pleasure of his goodness and the work of faith in power:€€ƒxˆThat the name of our Lord Jesus may be glorified in you, and you in him, according to the grace of our God and of the Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒ`€€ƒxlAnd we beseech you, brethren, by the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ and of our gathering together unto him:€€ƒx¥That you be not easily moved from your sense nor be terrified, neither by spirit nor by word nor by epistle. as sent from us, as if the day of the Lord were at hand.€€ƒxLet no man deceive you by any means: for unless there come a revolt first, and the man of sin be revealed, the son of perdition€€ƒxWho opposeth and is lifted up above all that is called God or that is worshipped, so that he sitteth in the temple of God, shewing himself as if he were God.€€‘ƒxGAnd now you know what withholdeth, that he may be revealed in his time.€€ƒxtFor the mystery of iniquity already worketh: only that he who now holdeth do hold, until he be taken out of the way.€€ƒx And then that wicked one shall be revealed: whom the Lord Jesus shall kill with the spirit of his mouth and shall destroy with the brightness of his coming: him€€ƒx\Whose coming is according to the working of Satan, in all power and signs and lying wonders:€€ƒxÇAnd in all seduction of iniquity to them that perish: because they receive not the love of the truth, that they might be saved. Therefore God shall send them the operation of error, to believe lying:€€ƒxVThat all may be judged who have not believed the truth but have consented to iniquity.€€ƒx½But we ought to give thanks to God always for you, brethren, beloved of God, for that God hath chosen you firstfruits unto salvation, in sanctification of the spirit and faith of the truth:€€ƒxkWhereunto also he hath called you by our gospel, unto the purchasing of the glory of our Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒxtTherefore, brethren, stand fast: and hold the traditions, which you have learned, whether by word or by our epistle.€€ƒxNow our Lord Jesus Christ himself, and God and our Father, who hath loved us and hath given us everlasting consolation and good hope in grace,€€ƒx?Exhort your hearts and confirm you in every good work and word.€€ƒ`€€ƒ`€€ƒxiFor the rest, brethren, pray for us that the word of God may run and may be glorified, even as among you:€€ƒxWAnd that we may be delivered from importunate and evil men: for all men have not faith.€€ƒx@But God is faithful, who will strengthen and keep you from evil.€€ƒxlAnd we have confidence concerning you in the Lord that the things which we command, you both do and will do.€€’ƒxÈAnd we charge you, brethren, in the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, that you withdraw yourselves from every brother walking disorderly and not according to the tradition which they have received of us.€€ƒxVFor yourselves know how you ought to imitate us. For we were not disorderly among you.€€ƒxNeither did we eat any man's bread for nothing: but in labour and in toil we worked night and day, lest we should be chargeable to any of you.€€ƒx_Not as if we had not power: but that we might give ourselves a pattern unto you, to imitate us.€€ƒxnFor also, when we were with you, this we declared to you: that, if any man will not work, neither let him eat.€€ƒxkFor we have heard there are some among you who walk disorderly: working not at all, but curiously meddling.€€ƒx†Now we charge them that are such and beseech them by the Lord Jesus Christ that, working with silence, they would eat their own bread.€€ƒx.But you, brethren, be not weary in well doing.€€ƒxyAnd if any man obey not our word by this epistle, note that man and do not keep company with him, that he may be ashamed.€€ƒx@Yet do not esteem him as an enemy but admonish him as a brother.€€ƒxbNow the Lord of peace himself give you everlasting peace in every place. The Lord be with you all.€€ƒxXThe salutation of Paul with my own hand: which is the sign in every epistle. So I write.€€ƒx9The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you all. Amen.€€ƒxThe First Letter to Timothy€€ƒ`€€ƒxlPaul, an apostle of Jesus Christ, according to the commandment of God our Saviour and Christ Jesus our hope:€€ƒxqTo Timothy, his beloved son in faith. Grace, mercy and peace, from God the Father and from Christ Jesus our Lord.€€ƒxyAs I desired thee to remain at Ephesus when I went into Macedonia, that thou mightest charge some not to teach otherwise:€€d1Tim¤dnameiI Timothyiosis_named1Timlabbreviationd1Timhchapters†”ƒxJFrom which things some, going astray, are turned aside unto vain babbling:€€ƒxgDesiring to be teachers of the law: understanding neither the things they say, nor whereof they affirm.€€ƒx;But we know that the law is good, if a man use it lawfully.€€ƒxÛKnowing this: That the law is not made for the just man but for the unjust and disobedient, for the ungodly and for sinners, for the wicked and defiled, for murderers of fathers and murderers of mothers, for manslayers,€€ƒx§For fornicators, for them who defile themselves with mankind, for menstealers, for liars, for perjured persons, and whatever other thing is contrary to sound doctrine:€€ƒxgWhich is according to the gospel of the glory of the blessed God which hath been committed to my trust.€€ƒxŒI give him thanks who hath strengthened me, even to Christ Jesus our Lord, for that he hath counted me faithful, putting me in the ministry:€€ƒxˆWho before was a blasphemer and a persecutor and contumelious. But I obtained the mercy of God, because I did it ignorantly in unbelief.€€ƒxbNow the grace of our Lord hath abounded exceedingly with faith and love, which is in Christ Jesus.€€ƒx€A faithful saying, and worthy of all acceptation, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners, of whom I am the chief.€€ƒx³But for this cause have I obtained mercy: that in me first Christ Jesus might shew forth all patience, for the information of them that shall believe in him unto life everlasting.€€ƒxhNow to the king of ages, immortal, invisible, the only God, be honour and glory for ever and ever. Amen.€€ƒx‡This precept, I commend to thee, O son Timothy: according to the prophecies going before on thee, that thou war in them a good warfare,€€ƒxbHaving faith and a good conscience, which some rejecting have made shipwreck concerning the faith.€€ƒxkOf whom is Hymeneus and Alexander, whom I have delivered up to Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme.€€ƒ`€€ƒxsI desire therefore, first of all, that supplications, prayers, intercessions and thanksgivings be made for all men:€€ƒxxFor kings and for all that are in high station: that we may lead a quiet and a peaceable life in all piety and chastity.€€ƒx@For this is good and acceptable in the sight of God our Saviour,€€ƒxLWho will have all men to be saved and to come to the knowledge of the truth.€€ƒx@Who gave himself a redemption for all, a testimony in due times.€€ƒx}Whereunto I am appointed a preacher and an apostle (I say the truth, I lie not), a doctor of the Gentiles in faith and truth.€€ƒxcI will therefore that men pray in every place, lifting up pure hands, without anger and contention.€€ƒx™In like manner, women also in decent apparel: adorning themselves with modesty and sobriety, not with plaited hair, or gold, or pearls, or costly attire:€€ƒx@But, as it becometh women professing godliness, with good works.€€ƒx3Let the woman learn in silence with all subjection.€€ƒx[But I suffer not a woman to teach, nor to use authority over the man: but to be in silence.€€ƒx$For Adam was first formed; then Eve.€€ƒxQAnd Adam was not seduced; but the woman, being seduced, was in the transgression.€€ƒxqYet she shall be saved through child bearing; if she continue in faith and love and sanctification with sobriety.€€ƒ`€€ƒxQA faithful saying: If a man desire the office of a bishop, he desireth good work.€€ƒx”It behoveth therefore a bishop to be blameless, the husband of one wife, sober, prudent, of good behaviour, chaste, given to hospitality, a teacher,€€ƒxMNot given to wine, no striker, but modest, not quarrelsome, not covetous, but€€ƒxXOne that ruleth well his own house, having his children in subjection with all chastity.€€ƒxYNot a neophyte: lest, being puffed up with pride, he fall into the judgment of the devil.€€ƒxwMoreover, he must have a good testimony of them who are without: lest he fall into reproach and the snare of the devil.€€ƒxgDeacons in like manner: chaste, not double tongued, not given to much wine, not greedy of filthy lucre:€€ƒx2Holding the mystery of faith in a pure conscience.€€ƒxNAnd let these also first be proved: and so let them minister, having no crime.€€ƒxTThe women in like manner: chaste, not slanderers, but sober, faithful in all things.€€ƒx[Let deacons be the husbands of one wife: who rule well their children and their own houses.€€ƒxˆFor they that have ministered well shall purchase to themselves a good degree and much confidence in the faith which is in Christ Jesus.€€ƒxGThese things I write to thee, hoping that I shall come to thee shortly.€€ƒx®But if I tarry long, that thou mayest know how thou oughtest to behave thyself in the house of God, which is the church of the living God, the pillar and ground of the truth.€€ƒxÜAnd evidently great is the mystery of godliness, which was manifested in the flesh, was justified in the spirit, appeared unto angels, hath been preached unto the Gentiles, is believed in the world, is taken up in glory.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‘Now the Spirit manifestly saith that in the last times some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to spirits of error and doctrines of devils,€€ƒx>Speaking lies in hypocrisy and having their conscience seared,€€ƒxšForbidding to marry, to abstain from meats, which God hath created to be received with thanksgiving by the faithful and by them that have known the truth.€€ƒxaFor every creature of God is good, and nothing to be rejected that is received with thanksgiving:€€ƒx±These things proposing to the brethren, thou shalt be a good minister of Christ Jesus, nourished up in the words of faith and of the good doctrine which thou hast attained unto.€€ƒxLBut avoid foolish and old wives fables: and exercise thyself unto godliness.€€ƒxœFor bodily exercise is profitable to little: but godliness is profitable to all things, having promise of the life that now is and of that which is to come.€€ƒx0A faithful saying and worthy of all acceptation.€€ƒx†For therefore we labour and are reviled, because we hope in the living God, who is the Saviour of all men, especially of the faithful.€€ƒxThese things command and teach:€€ƒx‚Let no man despise thy youth: but be thou an example of the faithful, in word, in conversation, in charity, in faith, in chastity.€€ƒxATill I come, attend unto reading, to exhortation and to doctrine.€€ƒxxNeglect not the grace that is in thee, which was given thee by prophecy, with imposition of the hands of the priesthood.€€ƒxaMeditate upon these things, be wholly in these things: that thy profiting may be manifest to all.€€ƒxTake heed to thyself and to doctrine: be earnest in them. For in doing this thou shalt both save thyself and them that hear thee.€€ƒ`€€ƒxOAn ancient man rebuke not, but entreat him as a father: young men, as brethren:€€ƒx@Old women, as mothers: young women, as sisters, in all chastity.€€ƒx%Honour widows that are widows indeed.€€ƒx¬But if any widow have children or grandchildren, let her learn first to govern her own house and to make a return of duty to her parents; for this is acceptable before God.€€˜ƒx=For she that liveth in pleasures is dead while she is living.€€ƒx4And this give in charge, that they may be blameless.€€ƒx„But if any man have not care of his own and especially of those of his house, he hath denied the faith and is worse than an infidel.€€ƒxeLet a widow be chosen of no less than threescore years of age, who hath been the wife of one husband.€€ƒxôHaving testimony for her good works, if she have brought up children, if she have received to harbour, if she have washed the saints' feet, if she have ministered to them that suffer tribulation, if she have diligently followed every good work.€€ƒxYBut the younger widows avoid. For when they have grown wanton in Christ, they will marry:€€ƒx@Having damnation, because they have made void their first faith.€€ƒx¡And withal being idle they learn to go about from house to house: and are not only idle, but tattlers also and busy bodies, speaking things which they ought not.€€ƒxŒI will, therefore, that the younger should marry, bear children, be mistresses of families, give no occasion to the adversary to speak evil.€€ƒx.For some are already turned aside after Satan.€€ƒxIf any of the faithful have widows, let him minister to them, and let not the church be charged: that there may be sufficient for them that are widows indeed.€€ƒxxLet the priests that rule well be esteemed worthy of double honour: especially they who labour in the word and doctrine.€€ƒx|For the scripture saith: Thou shalt not muzzle the ox that treadeth out the corn: and, The labourer is worthy of his reward.€€ƒxMAgainst a priest receive not an accusation, but under two or three witnesses.€€ƒxBThem that sin reprove before all that the rest also may have fear.€€ƒxI charge thee, before God and Christ Jesus and the elect angels, that thou observe these things without prejudice, doing nothing by declining to either side.€€ƒxdImpose not hands lightly upon any man, neither be partaker of other men's sins. Keep thyself chaste.€€ƒxdDo not still drink water, but use a little wine for thy stomach's sake and thy frequent infirmities.€€ƒxWSome men's sins are manifest, going before to judgment: and some men they follow after.€€ƒxWIn like manner also good deeds are manifest: and they that are otherwise cannot be hid.€€ƒ`€€ƒx“Whosoever are servants under the yoke, let them count their masters worthy of all honour; lest the name of the Lord and his doctrine be blasphemed.€€ƒxŞBut they that have believing masters, let them not despise them, because they are brethren; but serve them the rather, because they are faithful and beloved, who are partakers of the benefit. These things teach and exhort.€€ƒxŒIf any man teach otherwise and consent not to the sound words of our Lord Jesus Christ and to that doctrine which is according to godliness,€€ƒxHe is proud, knowing nothing, but sick about questions and strifes of words; from which arise envies, contentions, blasphemies, evil suspicions,€€•ƒx-But godliness with contentment is great gain.€€ƒxOFor we brought nothing into this world: and certainly we can carry nothing out.€€ƒxGBut having food and wherewith to be covered, with these we are content.€€ƒx³For they that will become rich fall into temptation and into the snare of the devil and into many unprofitable and hurtful desires, which drown men into destruction and perdition.€€ƒxFor the desire of money is the root of all evils; which some coveting have erred from the faith and have entangled themselves in many sorrows.€€ƒxlBut thou, O man of God, fly these things: and pursue justice, godliness, faith, charity, patience, mildness.€€ƒxFight the good fight of faith. Lay hold on eternal life, whereunto thou art called and be it confessed a good confession before many witnesses.€€ƒx‡I charge thee before God who quickeneth all things, and before Christ Jesus who gave testimony under Pontius Pilate, a good confession:€€ƒxaThat thou keep the commandment without spot, blameless, unto the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ,€€ƒxjWhich in his times he shall shew, who is the Blessed and only Mighty, the King of kings and Lord of lords:€€ƒxWho only hath immortality and inhabiteth light inaccessible: whom no man hath seen, nor can see: to whom be honour and empire everlasting. Amen.€€ƒx¢Charge the rich of this world not to be highminded nor to trust in the uncertainty of riches, but in the living God (who giveth us abundantly all things to enjoy)€€ƒxNTo do good, to be rich in good work, to give easily, to communicate to others,€€ƒxvTo lay up in store for themselves a good foundation against the time to come, that they may lay hold on the true life.€€ƒxO Timothy, keep that which is committed to thy trust, avoiding the profane novelties of words and oppositions of knowledge falsely so called.€€ƒxPWhich some promising, have erred concerning the faith. Grace be with thee. Amen.€€ƒxThe Second Letter to Timothy€€ƒ`€€ƒxpPaul, an apostle of Jesus Christ, by the will of God, according to the promise of life which is in Christ Jesus:€€ƒxnTo Timothy, my dearly beloved son. Grace, mercy and peace, from God the Father and from Christ Jesus our Lord.€€ƒxŸI give thanks to God, whom I serve from my forefathers, with a pure conscience, that without ceasing I have a remembrance of thee in my prayers, night and day.€€d2Tim¤dnamejII Timothyiosis_named2Timlabbreviationd2Timhchapters„’ƒxrFor which cause I admonish thee that thou stir up the grace of God which is in thee by the imposition of my hands.€€ƒxWFor God hath not given us the spirit of fear: but of power and of love and of sobriety.€€ƒxBe not thou therefore ashamed of the testimony of our Lord, nor of me his prisoner: but labour with the gospel, according to the power of God.€€ƒxÆWho hath delivered us and called us by his holy calling, not according to our own works, but according to his own purpose and grace, which was given us in Christ Jesus before the times of the world:€€ƒx¡But is now made manifest by the illumination of our Saviour Jesus Christ, who hath destroyed death and hath brought to light life and incorruption by the gospel.€€ƒxMWherein I am appointed a preacher and an apostle and teacher of the Gentiles.€€ƒxÃFor which cause, I also suffer these things: but I am not ashamed. For I know whom I have believed and I am certain that he is able to keep that which I have committed unto him, against that day.€€ƒxlHold the form of sound words which thou hast heard of me: in faith and in the love which is in Christ Jesus.€€ƒxPKeep the good thing committed to thy trust by the Holy Ghost who dwelleth in us.€€ƒxoThou knowest this, that all they who are in Asia are turned away from me: of whom are Phigellus and Hermogenes.€€ƒxzThe Lord give mercy to the house of Onesiphorus: because he hath often refreshed me and hath not been ashamed of my chain:€€ƒxBBut when he was come to Rome, he carefully sought me and found me.€€ƒxThe Lord grant unto him to find mercy of the Lord in that day. And in how many things he ministered unto me at Ephesus, thou very well knowest.€€ƒ`€€ƒx2Thou therefore, my son, be strong in Christ Jesus:€€ƒx…And the things which thou hast heard of me by many witnesses, the same commend to faithful men who shall be fit to teach others also.€€ƒx)Labour as a good soldier of Christ Jesus.€€ƒxƒNo man, being a soldier to God, entangleth himself with secular businesses: that he may please him to whom he hath engaged himself.€€˜ƒx?The husbandman that laboureth must first partake of the fruits.€€ƒxOUnderstand what I say: for the Lord will give thee in all things understanding.€€ƒxqBe mindful that the Lord Jesus Christ is risen again from the dead, of the seed of David, according to my gospel:€€ƒxSWherein I labour even unto bands, as an evildoer. But the word of God is not bound.€€ƒxTherefore I endure all things for the sake of the elect, that they also may obtain the salvation which is in Christ Jesus, with heavenly glory.€€ƒxKA faithful saying: for if we be dead with him, we shall live also with him.€€ƒxQIf we suffer, we shall also reign with him. If we deny him, he will also deny us.€€ƒxBIf we believe not, he continueth faithful, he cannot deny himself.€€ƒx”Of these things put them in mind, charging them before the Lord. Contend not in words: for it is to no profit, but to the subverting of the hearers.€€ƒxƒCarefully study to present thyself approved unto God, a workman that needeth not to be ashamed, rightly handling the word of truth.€€ƒxLBut shun profane and vain babblings: for they grow much towards ungodliness.€€ƒxLAnd their speech spreadeth like a canker: of whom are Hymeneus and Philetus:€€ƒxrWho have erred from the truth, saying that the resurrection is past already, and have subverted the faith of some.€€ƒx¥But the sure foundation of God standeth firm, having this seal: the Lord knoweth who are his; and let every one depart from iniquity who nameth the name of the Lord.€€ƒx›But in a great house there are not only vessels of gold and of silver, but also of wood and of earth: and some indeed unto honour, but some unto dishonour.€€ƒxIf any man therefore shall cleanse himself from these, he shall be a vessel unto honour, sanctified and profitable to the Lord, prepared unto every good work.€€ƒxBut flee thou youthful desires, and pursue justice, faith, charity and peace with them that call on the Lord out of a pure heart.€€ƒxKAnd avoid foolish and unlearned questions, knowing that they beget strifes.€€ƒx`But the servant of the Lord must not wrangle: but be mild toward all men, apt to teach, patient,€€ƒxtWith modesty admonishing them that resist the truth: if peradventure God may give them repentance to know the truth;€€ƒxgAnd they may recover themselves from the snares of the devil by whom they are held captive at his will.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAKnow also this, that in the last days shall come dangerous times.€€ƒxuMen shall be lovers of themselves, covetous, haughty, proud, blasphemers, disobedient to parents, ungrateful, wicked,€€ƒxXWithout affection, without peace, slanderers, incontinent, unmerciful, without kindness,€€ƒxGTraitors, stubborn, puffed up, and lovers of pleasure more than of God:€€‘ƒx„For of these sort are they who creep into houses and lead captive silly women laden with sins, who are led away with divers desires:€€ƒxAEver learning, and never attaining to the knowledge of the truth.€€ƒx€Now as Jannes and Mambres resisted Moses, so these also resist the truth, men corrupted in mind, reprobate concerning the faith.€€ƒxdBut they shall proceed no farther: for their folly shall be manifest to all men, as theirs also was.€€ƒxeBut thou hast fully known my doctrine, manner of life, purpose, faith, longsuffering, love, patience,€€ƒxPersecutions, afflictions: such as came upon me at Antioch, at Iconium and at Lystra: what persecutions I endured, and out of them all the Lord delivered me.€€ƒxFAnd all that will live godly in Christ Jesus shall suffer persecution.€€ƒxUBut evil men and seducers shall grow worse and worse: erring, and driving into error,€€ƒxˆBut continue thou in those things which thou hast learned and which have been committed to thee. Knowing of whom thou hast learned them:€€ƒxŒAnd because from thy infancy thou hast known the holy scriptures which can instruct thee to salvation by the faith which is in Christ Jesus.€€ƒxgAll scripture, inspired of God, is profitable to teach, to reprove, to correct, to instruct in justice:€€ƒxAThat the man of God may be perfect, furnished to every good work.€€ƒ`€€ƒxsI charge thee, before God and Jesus Christ, who shall judge the living and the dead, by his coming and his kingdom:€€ƒxlPreach the word: be instant in season, out of season: reprove, entreat, rebuke in all patience and doctrine.€€ƒx¢For there shall be a time when they will not endure sound doctrine but, according to their own desires, they will heap to themselves teachers having itching ears:€€ƒxWAnd will indeed turn away their hearing from the truth, but will be turned unto fables.€€–ƒxTFor I am even now ready to be sacrificed: and the time of my dissolution is at hand.€€ƒxMI have fought a good fight: I have finished my course: I have kept the faith.€€ƒxÔAs to the rest, there is laid up for me a crown of justice which the Lord the just judge will render to me in that day: and not only to me, but to them also that love his coming. Make haste to come to me quickly.€€ƒxGFor Demas hath left me, loving this world, and is gone to Thessalonica:€€ƒx+Crescens into Galatia, Titus into Dalmatia.€€ƒxeOnly Luke is with me. Take Mark and bring him with thee: for he is profitable to me for the ministry.€€ƒx$But Tychicus I have sent to Ephesus.€€ƒxyThe cloak that I left at Troas, with Carpus, when thou comest, bring with thee: and the books, especially the parchments.€€ƒxbAlexander the coppersmith hath done me much evil: the Lord will reward him according to his works:€€ƒxAWhom do thou also avoid: for he hath greatly withstood our words.€€ƒxaAt my first answer, no man stood with me: but all forsook me. May it not be laid to their charge!€€ƒx°But the Lord stood by me and strengthened me, that by me the preaching may be accomplished and that all the Gentiles may hear. And I was delivered out of the mouth of the lion.€€ƒx‰The Lord hath delivered me from every evil work and will preserve me unto his heavenly kingdom. To whom be glory for ever and ever. Amen.€€ƒx;Salute Prisca, and Aquila and the household of Onesiphorus.€€ƒxBErastus remained at Corinth. And Trophimus I left sick at Miletus.€€ƒxmMake haste to come before winter. Eubulus and Pudens and Linus and Claudia and all the brethren, salute thee.€€ƒxBThe Lord Jesus Christ be with thy spirit. Grace be with you. Amen.€€ƒsThe Letter to Titus€€ƒ`€€ƒx¦Paul, a servant of God and an apostle of Jesus Christ, according to the faith of the elect of God and the acknowledging of the truth, which is according to godliness:€€ƒxiUnto the hope of life everlasting, which God, who lieth not, hath promised before the times of the world:€€ƒxBut hath in due times manifested his word in preaching, which is committed to me according to the commandment of God our Saviour:€€eTitus¤dnameeTitusiosis_nameeTituslabbreviationeTitushchaptersƒƒxjIf any be without crime, the husband of one wife. having faithful children, not accused of riot or unruly.€€ƒx–For a bishop must be without crime, as the steward of God: not proud, not subject to anger, nor given to wine, no striker, not greedy of filthy lucre:€€ƒx?But given to hospitality, gentle, sober, just, holy, continent:€€ƒxŒEmbracing that faithful word which is according to doctrine, that he may be able to exhort in sound doctrine and to convince the gainsayers.€€ƒxlFor there are also many disobedient, vain talkers and seducers: especially they who are of the circumcision.€€ƒxnWho must be reproved, who subvert whole houses, teaching things which they ought not, for filthy lucre's sake.€€ƒxfOne of them a prophet of their own, said: The Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, slothful bellies.€€ƒx\This testimony is true. Wherefore, rebuke them sharply, that they may be sound in the faith:€€ƒxaNot giving heed to Jewish fables and commandments of men who turn themselves away from the truth.€€ƒx›All things are clean to the clean: but to them that are defiled and to unbelievers, nothing is clean: but both their mind and their conscience are defiled.€€ƒx…They profess that they know God: but in their works they deny him: being abominable and incredulous and to every good work reprobate.€€ƒ`€€ƒx5But speak thou the things that become sound doctrine:€€ƒxRThat the aged men be sober, chaste, prudent, sound in faith, in love, in patience.€€ƒxjThe aged women, in like manner, in holy attire, not false accusers, not given to much wine, teaching well:€€ƒx_That they may teach the young women to be wise, to love their husbands, to love their children.€€ƒx5Young men, in like manner, exhort that they be sober.€€ƒx[In all things shew thyself an example of good works, in doctrine, in integrity, in gravity,€€ƒxvThe sound word that can not be blamed: that he who is on the contrary part may be afraid, having no evil to say of us.€€ƒxXExhort servants to be obedient to their masters: in all things pleasing, not gainsaying:€€ƒx{Not defrauding, but in all things shewing good fidelity, that they may adorn the doctrine of God our Saviour in all things.€€ƒx:For the grace of God our Saviour hath appeared to all men:€€ƒxyInstructing us, that, denying ungodliness and worldly desires, we should live soberly and justly and godly in this world,€€ƒxcLooking for the blessed hope and coming of the glory of the great God and our Saviour Jesus Christ.€€ƒxWho gave himself for us, that he might redeem us from all iniquity and might cleanse to himself a people acceptable, a pursuer of good works.€€ƒxUThese things speak and exhort and rebuke with all authority. Let no man despise thee.€€ƒ`€€ƒxeAdmonish them to be subject to princes and powers, to obey at a word, to be ready to every good work.€€ƒx^To speak evil of no man, not to be litigious but gentle: shewing all mildness towards all men.€€ƒx¤For we ourselves also were some time unwise, incredulous, erring, slaves to divers desires and pleasures, living in malice and envy, hateful and hating one another.€€ƒx?But when the goodness and kindness of God our Saviour appeared:€€ƒxOWhom he hath poured forth upon us abundantly, through Jesus Christ our Saviour:€€ƒxZThat, being justified by his grace, we may be heirs according to hope of life everlasting.€€ƒxÀIt is a faithful saying. And these things I will have thee affirm constantly, that they who believe in God may be careful to excel in good works. These things are good and profitable unto men.€€ƒx|But avoid foolish questions and genealogies and contentions and strivings about the law. For they are unprofitable and vain.€€ƒxFA man that is a heretic, after the first and second admonition, avoid:€€ƒxbKnowing that he that is such an one is subverted and sinneth, being condemned by his own judgment.€€ƒx~When I shall send to thee Artemas or Tychicus, make haste to come unto me to Nicopolis. For there I have determined to winter.€€ƒxTSend forward Zenas the lawyer and Apollo, with care that nothing be wanting to them.€€ƒxbAnd let our men also learn to excel in good works for necessary uses: that they be not unfruitful.€€ƒxpAll that are with me salute thee. Salute them that love us in the faith. The grace of God be with you all. Amen.€€ƒvThe Letter to Philemon€€ƒ`€€ƒxgPaul, a prisoner of Christ Jesus, and Timothy, a brother: to Philemon, our beloved and fellow labourer,€€ƒxpAnd to Appia, our dearest sister, and to Archippus, our fellow soldier, and to the church which is in thy house.€€ƒxKGrace to you and peace, from God our Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ.€€dPhlm¤dnamehPhilemoniosis_namedPhlmlabbreviationdPhlmhchapters˜ƒx€That the communication of thy faith may be made evident in the acknowledgment of every good work that is in you in Christ Jesus.€€ƒxFor I have had great joy and consolation in thy charity, because the bowels of the saints have been refreshed by thee, brother.€€ƒxfWherefore, though I have much confidence in Christ Jesus to command thee that which is to the purpose:€€ƒx|For charity sake I rather beseech, whereas thou art such a one, as Paul, an old man and now a prisoner also of Jesus Christ.€€ƒxFI beseech thee for my son, whom I have begotten in my bands, Onesimus,€€ƒxXWho hath been heretofore unprofitable to thee but now is profitable both to me and thee:€€ƒxHWhom I have sent back to thee. And do thou receive him as my own bowels.€€ƒxpWhom I would have retained with me, that in thy stead he might have ministered to me in the bands of the gospel.€€ƒxsBut without thy counsel I would do nothing: that thy good deed might not be as it were of necessity, but voluntary.€€ƒxgFor perhaps he therefore departed for a season from thee that thou mightest receive him again for ever:€€ƒx”Not now as a servant, but instead of a servant, a most dear brother, especially to me. But how much more to thee, both in the flesh and in the Lord?€€ƒx<If therefore thou count me a partner, receive him as myself.€€ƒxSAnd if he hath wronged thee in any thing or is in thy debt, put that to my account.€€ƒxrI Paul have written it with my own hand: I will repay it: not to say to thee that thou owest me thy own self also.€€ƒxJYea, brother. May I enjoy thee in the Lord! Refresh my bowels in the Lord.€€ƒxbTrusting in thy obedience, I have written to thee: knowing that thou wilt also do more than I say.€€ƒxeBut withal prepare me also a lodging. For I hope that through your prayers I shall be given unto you.€€ƒx?There salute thee Epaphras, my fellow prisoner in Christ Jesus:€€ƒx7Mark, Aristarchus, Demas and Luke, my fellow labourers.€€ƒx=The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit. Amen.€€ƒxThe Letter to the Hebrews€€ƒ`€€ƒxqGod, who, at sundry times and in divers manners, spoke in times past to the fathers by the prophets, last of all,€€ƒxwIn these days, hath spoken to us by his Son, whom he hath appointed heir of all things, by whom also he made the world.€€ƒxÄWho being the brightness of his glory and the figure of his substance and upholding all things by the word of his power, making purgation of sins, sitteth on the right hand of the majesty on high:€€cHeb¤dnamegHebrewsiosis_namecHeblabbreviationcHebhchaptersƒxtAnd again, when he bringeth in the first begotten into the world, he saith: And let all the angels of God adore him.€€ƒxgAnd to the angels indeed he saith: He that maketh his angels spirits and his ministers a flame of fire.€€ƒxlBut to the Son: Thy throne, O God, is for ever and ever: a sceptre of justice is the sceptre of thy kingdom.€€ƒx‚Thou hast loved justice and hated iniquity: therefore God, thy God, hath anointed thee with the oil of gladness above thy fellows.€€ƒxfAnd: Thou in the beginning, O Lord, didst found the earth: and the works of thy hands are the heavens.€€ƒxUThey shall perish: but thou shalt continue: and they shall all grow old as a garment.€€ƒx|And as a vesture shalt thou change them, and they shalt be changed. But thou art the selfsame: and thy years shall not fail.€€ƒxmBut to which of the angels said he at any time: Sit on my right hand, until I make thy enemies thy footstool?€€ƒxoAre they not all ministering spirits, sent to minister for them who shall receive the inheritance of salvation?€€ƒ`€€ƒxrTherefore ought we more diligently to observe the things which we have heard lest perhaps we should let them slip.€€ƒx€For if the word spoken by angels became steadfast and every transgression and disobedience received a just recompense of reward:€€ƒx“How shall we escape if we neglect so great salvation? Which, having begun to be declared by the Lord, was confirmed unto us by them that heard him.€€ƒx†God also bearing them witness by signs and wonders and divers miracles and distributions of the Holy Ghost, according to his own will.€€’ƒxˆBut one in a certain place hath testified, saying: What is man, that thou art mindful of him? Or the son of man, that thou visitest him?€€ƒxŒThou hast made him a little lower than the angels: thou hast crowned him with glory and honour and hast set him over the works of thy hands.€€ƒx·Thou hast subjected all things under his feet. For in that he hath subjected all things to him he left nothing not subject to him. But now we see not as yet all things subject to him.€€ƒx¶But we see Jesus, who was made a little lower than the angels, for the suffering of death, crowned with glory and honour: that, through the grace of God he might taste death for all.€€ƒx©For it became him for whom are all things and by whom are all things, who had brought many children into glory, to perfect the author of their salvation, by his passion.€€ƒx‰For both he that sanctifieth and they who are sanctified are all of one. For which cause he is not ashamed to call them brethren, saying:€€ƒxVI will declare thy name to my brethren: in the midst of the church will I praise thee.€€ƒxcAnd again: I will put my trust in him. And again: Behold I and my children, whom God hath given me.€€ƒxáTherefore because the children are partakers of flesh and blood, he also himself in like manner hath been partaker of the same: that, through death, he might destroy him who had the empire of death, that is to say, the devil:€€ƒxcAnd might deliver them, who through the fear of death were all their lifetime subject to servitude.€€ƒxWFor nowhere doth he take hold of the angels: but of the seed of Abraham he taketh hold.€€ƒxËWherefore, it behoved him in all things to be made like unto his brethren, that he might become a merciful and faithful high priest before God, that he might be a propitiation for the sins of the people.€€ƒxoFor in that wherein he himself hath suffered and been tempted he is able to succour them also that are tempted.€€ƒ`€€ƒx{Wherefore, holy brethren, partakers of the heavenly vocation consider the apostle and high priest of our confession, Jesus:€€ƒxIWho is faithful to him that made him, as was also Moses in all his house.€€ƒx‹For this man was counted worthy of greater glory than Moses, by so much as he that hath built the house hath greater honour than the house.€€ƒxLFor every house is built by some man: but he that created all things is God.€€“ƒx{But Christ, as the Son in his own house: which house are we, if we hold fast the confidence and glory of hope unto the end.€€ƒxGWherefore, as the Holy Ghost saith: To-day if you shall hear his voice,€€ƒxVHarden not your hearts, as in the provocation, in the day of temptation in the desert,€€ƒx7Where your fathers tempted me, proved and saw my works,€€ƒxˆForty years: for which cause I was offended with this generation, and I said: They always err in heart. And they have not known my ways.€€ƒx>As I have sworn in my wrath: If they shall enter into my rest.€€ƒxrTake heed, brethren, lest perhaps there be in any of you an evil heart of unbelief, to depart from the living God.€€ƒx|But exhort one another every day, whilst it is called to day, that none of you be hardened through the deceitfulness of sin.€€ƒxiFor we are made partakers of Christ: yet so, if we hold the beginning of his substance firm unto the end.€€ƒxfWhile it is said: To day, if you shall hear his voice, harden not your hearts, as in that provocation.€€ƒxLFor some who heard did provoke: but not all that came out of Egypt by Moses.€€ƒx{And with whom was he offended forty years? Was it not with them that sinned, whose carcasses were overthrown in the desert?€€ƒxfAnd to whom did he swear, that they should not enter into his rest: but to them that were incredulous?€€ƒx=And we see that they could not enter in, because of unbelief.€€ƒ`€€ƒxyLet us fear therefore lest, the promise being left of entering into his rest, any of you should be thought to be wanting.€€ƒx§For unto us also it hath been declared in like manner as unto them. But the word of hearing did not profit them, not being mixed with faith of those things they heard.€€ƒxËFor we, who have believed, shall enter into rest; as he said: As I have sworn in my wrath: If they shall enter into my rest; and this indeed when the works from the foundation of the world were finished.€€ƒxkFor in a certain place he spoke of the seventh day thus: And God rested the seventh day from all his works.€€ƒx‚Seeing then it remaineth that some are to enter into it, and they to whom it was first preached did not enter because of unbelief:€€ƒxŸAgain he limiteth a certain day, saying in David; To day, after so long a time as it is above said: To day if you shall hear his voice, harden not your hearts.€€ƒxVFor if Jesus had given them rest he would never have afterwards spoken of another day.€€ƒx>There remaineth therefore a day of rest for the people of God.€€ƒxdFor he that is entered into his rest, the same also hath rested from his works, as God did from his.€€ƒxeLet us hasten therefore to enter into that rest: lest any man fall into the same example of unbelief.€€ƒxòFor the word of God is living and effectual and more piercing than any two edged sword; and reaching unto the division of the soul and the spirit, of the joints also and the marrow: and is a discerner of the thoughts and intents of the heart.€€ƒx{Neither is there any creature invisible in his sight: but all things are naked and open to his eyes, to whom our speech is.€€ƒx~Having therefore a great high priest that hath passed into the heavens, Jesus the Son of God: let us hold fast our confession.€€ƒx‡For we have not a high priest who cannot have compassion on our infirmities: but one tempted in all things like as we are, without sin.€€ƒxvLet us go therefore with confidence to the throne of grace: that we may obtain mercy and find grace in seasonable aid.€€ƒ`€€ƒx—For every high priest taken from among men is ordained for men in the things that appertain to God, that he may offer up gifts and sacrifices for sins:€€ƒxtWho can have compassion on them that are ignorant and that err: because he himself also is compassed with infirmity.€€ƒxRAnd therefore he ought, as for the people, so also for himself, to offer for sins.€€ƒx\Neither doth any man take the honour to himself, but he that is called by God, as Aaron was.€€ƒxfAs he saith also in another place: Thou art a priest for ever, according to the order of Melchisedech.€€ƒxªWho in the days of his flesh, with a strong cry and tears, offering up prayers and supplications to him that was able to save him from death, was heard for his reverence.€€ƒx_And whereas indeed he was the Son of God, he learned obedience by the things which he suffered.€€ƒxWAnd being consummated, he became, to all that obey him, the cause of eternal salvation:€€ƒxDCalled by God a high priest, according to the order of Melchisedech.€€ƒxeOf whom we have much to say and hard to be intelligibly uttered: because you are become weak to hear.€€ƒxÈFor whereas for the time you ought to be masters, you have need to be taught again what are the first elements of the words of God: and you are become such as have need of milk and not of strong meat.€€ƒxgFor every one that is a partaker of milk is unskilful in the word of justice: for he is a little child.€€ƒxzBut strong meat is for the perfect: for them who by custom have their senses exercised to the discerning of good and evil.€€ƒ`€€ƒx±Wherefore, leaving the word of the beginning of Christ, let us go on to things more perfect: not laying again the foundation of penance from dead works and of faith towards God,€€ƒxrOf the doctrine of baptisms and imposition of hands, and of the resurrection of the dead, and of eternal judgment.€€ƒx#And this will we do, if God permit.€€ƒx‡For it is impossible for those who were once illuminated, have tasted also the heavenly gift and were made partakers of the Holy Ghost,€€”ƒx|And are fallen away: to be renewed again to penance, crucifying again to themselves the Son of God and making him a mockery.€€ƒxFor the earth, that drinketh in the rain which cometh often upon it and bringeth forth herbs meet for them by whom it is tilled, receiveth blessing from God.€€ƒxrBut that which bringeth forth thorns and briers is reprobate and very near unto a curse: whose end is to be burnt.€€ƒxeBut, my dearly beloved, we trust better things of you, and nearer to salvation; though we speak thus.€€ƒxœFor God is not unjust, that he should forget your work and the love which you have shewn in his name, you who have ministered and do minister to the saints.€€ƒxnAnd we desire that every one of you shew forth the same carefulness to the accomplishing of hope unto the end:€€ƒxnThat you become not slothful, but followers of them who through faith and patience shall inherit the promises.€€ƒxkFor God making promises to Abraham, because he had no one greater by whom he might swear, swore by himself,€€ƒxQSaying: Unless blessing I shall bless thee and multiplying I shall multiply thee.€€ƒx2And so patiently enduring he obtained the promise.€€ƒxoFor men swear by one greater than themselves: and an oath for confirmation is the end of all their controversy.€€ƒx}Wherein God, meaning more abundantly to shew to the heirs of the promise the immutability of his counsel, interposed an oath:€€ƒxªThat by two immutable things in which it is impossible for God to lie, we may have the strongest comfort, we who have fled for refuge to hold fast the hope set before us.€€ƒxbWhich we have as an anchor of the soul, sure and firm, and which entereth in even within the veil:€€ƒxqWhere the forerunner Jesus is entered for us, made a high priest for ever according to the order of Melchisedech.€€ƒ`€€ƒxFor this Melchisedech was king of Salem, priest of the most high God, who met Abraham returning from the slaughter of the kings and blessed him:€€ƒx›To whom also Abraham divided the tithes of all: who first indeed by interpretation is king of justice: and then also king of Salem, that is, king of peace:€€ƒx£Without father, without mother, without genealogy, having neither beginning of days nor end of life, but likened unto the Son of God, continueth a priest for ever.€€ƒxoNow consider how great this man is, to whom also Abraham the patriarch gave tithes out of the principal things.€€˜ƒxtBut he, whose pedigree is not numbered among them, received tithes of Abraham and blessed him that had the promises.€€ƒxKAnd without all contradiction, that which is less is blessed by the better.€€ƒxXAnd here indeed, men that die receive tithes: but there, he hath witness that he liveth.€€ƒxMAnd (as it may be said) even Levi who received tithes paid tithes in Abraham:€€ƒxDFor he was yet in the loins of his father when Melchisedech met him.€€ƒxõIf then perfection was by the Levitical priesthood (for under it the people received the law), what further need was there that another priest should rise according to the order of Melchisedech: and not be called according to the order of Aaron?€€ƒx`For the priesthood being translated, it is necessary that a translation also be made of the law,€€ƒxbFor he of whom these things are spoken is of another tribe, of which no one attended on the altar.€€ƒxjFor it is evident that our Lord sprung out of Juda: in which tribe Moses spoke nothing concerning priests.€€ƒxlAnd it is yet far more evident: if according to the similitude of Melchisedech there ariseth another priest,€€ƒx{Who is made, not according to the law of a law of a carnal commandment, but according to the power of an indissoluble life.€€ƒxUFor he testifieth: Thou art a priest for ever according to the order of Melchisedech.€€ƒxpThere is indeed a setting aside of the former commandment, because of the weakness and unprofitableness thereof:€€ƒxlFor the law brought nothing to perfection: but a bringing in of a better hope, by which we draw nigh to God.€€ƒxcAnd inasmuch as it is not without an oath (for the others indeed were made priests without an oath:€€ƒxzBut this with an oath, by him that said unto him: The Lord hath sworn and he will not repent: Thou art a priest for ever).€€ƒx8By so much is Jesus made a surety of a better testament.€€ƒxlAnd the others indeed were made many priests, because by reason of death they were not suffered to continue:€€ƒxJBut this, for that he continueth for ever, hath an everlasting priesthood:€€ƒxqWhereby he is able also to save for ever them that come to God by him; always living to make intercession for us.€€ƒxFor it was fitting that we should have such a high priest, holy, innocent, undefiled, separated from sinners, and made higher than the heavens:€€ƒxŸWho needeth not daily (as the other priests) to offer sacrifices, first for his own sins, and then for the people's: for this he did once, in offering himself.€€ƒxFor the law maketh men priests, who have infirmity: but the word of the oath (which was since the law) the Son who is perfected for evermore.€€ƒ`€€ƒxšNow of the things which we have spoken, this is the sum: We have such an high priest who is set on the right hand of the throne of majesty in the heavens,€€ƒx^A minister of the holies and of the true tabernacle, which the Lord hath pitched, and not man.€€ƒx‰For every high priest is appointed to offer gifts and sacrifices: wherefore it is necessary that he also should have some thing to offer.€€ƒxzIf then he were on earth, he would not be a priest: seeing that there would be others to offer gifts according to the law.€€ƒxŒBut now he hath obtained a better ministry, by how much also he is a mediator of a better testament which is established on better promises.€€ƒxeFor if that former had been faultless, there should not indeed a place have been sought for a second.€€ƒx­For, finding fault with them, he saith: Behold the days shall come, saith the Lord: and I will perfect, unto the house of Israel and unto the house of Juda, a new testament:€€ƒxáNot according to the testament which I made to their fathers, on the day when I took them by the hand to lead them out of the land of Egypt: because they continued not in my testament: and I regarded them not, saith the Lord.€€ƒxåFor this is the testament which I will make to the house of Israel after those days, saith the Lord: I will give my laws into their mind: and in their heart will I write them. And I will be their God: and they shall be my people.€€ƒx¡And they shall not teach every man his neighbour and every man his brother, saying: Know the Lord. For all shall know me, from the least to the greatest of them.€€ƒxWBecause I will be merciful to their iniquities: and their sins I will remember no more.€€ƒxjNow in saying a new, he hath made the former old. And that which decayeth and groweth old is near its end.€€ƒ`€€ƒxLThe former indeed had also justifications of divine service and a sanctuary.€€ƒx‘For there was a tabernacle made the first, wherein were the candlesticks and the table and the setting forth of loaves, which is called the Holy.€€ƒxMAnd after the second veil, the tabernacle which is called the Holy of Holies:€€ƒxÌHaving a golden censer and the ark of the testament covered about on every part with gold, in which was a golden pot that had manna and the rod of Aaron that had blossomed and the tables of the testament.€€˜ƒx‹Now these things being thus ordered, into the first tabernacle, the priests indeed always entered, accomplishing the offices of sacrifices.€€ƒx…But into the second, the high priest alone, once a year: not without blood, which he offereth for his own and the people's ignorance:€€ƒx†The Holy Ghost signifying this: That the way into the Holies was not yet made manifest, whilst the former tabernacle was yet standing.€€ƒx¼Which is a parable of the time present: according to which gifts and sacrifices are offered, which cannot, as to the conscience, make him perfect that serveth, only in meats and in drinks,€€ƒxXAnd divers washings and justices of the flesh laid on them until the time of correction.€€ƒxBut Christ, being come an high Priest of the good things to come, by a greater and more perfect tabernacle, not made with hand, that is, not of this creation:€€ƒxƒNeither by the blood of goats or of calves, but by his own blood, entered once into the Holies, having obtained eternal redemption.€€ƒxFor if the blood of goats and of oxen, and the ashes of an heifer, being sprinkled, sanctify such as are defiled, to the cleansing of the flesh:€€ƒx£How much more shall the blood of Christ, who by the Holy Ghost offered himself unspotted unto God, cleanse our conscience from dead works, to serve the living God?€€ƒxèAnd therefore he is the mediator of the new testament: that by means of his death for the redemption of those transgressions which were under the former testament, they that are called may receive the promise of eternal inheritance.€€ƒxSFor where there is a testament the death of the testator must of necessity come in.€€ƒxrFor a testament is of force after men are dead: otherwise it is as yet of no strength, whilst the testator liveth.€€ƒx?Whereupon neither was the first indeed dedicated without blood.€€ƒxÖFor when every commandment of the law had been read by Moses to all the people, he took the blood of calves and goats, with water, and scarlet wool and hyssop, and sprinkled both the book itself and all the people.€€ƒxLSaying: This is the blood of the testament which God hath enjoined unto you.€€ƒxaThe tabernacle also and all the vessels of the ministry, in like manner, he sprinkled with blood.€€ƒxzAnd almost all things, according to the law, are cleansed with blood: and without shedding of blood there is no remission.€€ƒx£It is necessary therefore that the patterns of heavenly things should be cleansed with these: but the heavenly things themselves with better sacrifices than these.€€ƒx¡For Jesus is not entered into the Holies made with hands, the patterns of the true: but into Heaven itself, that he may appear now in the presence of God for us.€€ƒx|Nor yet that he should offer himself often, as the high priest entereth into the Holies every year with the blood of others:€€ƒx´For then he ought to have suffered often from the beginning of the world. But now once, at the end of ages, he hath appeared for the destruction of sin by the sacrifice of himself.€€ƒxIAnd as it is appointed unto men once to die, and after this the judgment:€€ƒxSo also Christ was offered once to exhaust the sins of many. The second time he shall appear without sin to them that expect him unto salvation.€€ƒ`€€ƒxËFor the law, having a shadow of the good things to come, not the very image of the things, by the selfsame sacrifices which they offer continually every year, can never make the comers thereunto perfect.€€ƒxFor then they would have ceased to be offered: because the worshippers once cleansed should have no conscience of sin any longer.€€ƒx=But in them there is made a commemoration of sins every year:€€ƒxTFor it is impossible that with the blood of oxen and goats sin should be taken away.€€˜'ƒx'Holocausts for sin did not please thee.€€ƒxjThen said I: Behold I come: in the head of the book it is written of me: that I should do thy will, O God.€€ƒx¡In saying before, Sacrifices, and oblations, and holocausts for sin thou wouldest not, neither are they pleasing to thee, which are offered according to the law.€€ƒxxThen said I: Behold, I come to do thy will, O God: He taketh away the first, that he may establish that which followeth.€€ƒxVIn the which will, we are sanctified by the oblation of the body of Jesus Christ once.€€ƒxyAnd every priest indeed standeth daily ministering and often offering the same sacrifices which can never take away sins.€€ƒxYBut this man, offering one sacrifice for sins, for ever sitteth on the right hand of God,€€ƒxBFrom henceforth expecting until his enemies be made his footstool.€€ƒxHFor by one oblation he hath perfected for ever them that are sanctified.€€ƒxHAnd the Holy Ghost also doth testify this to us. For after that he said:€€ƒx¡And this is the testament which I will make unto them after those days, saith the Lord. I will give my laws in their hearts and on their minds will I write them:€€ƒx6And their sins and iniquities I will remember no more.€€ƒxONow, where there is a remission of these, there is no more an oblation for sin.€€ƒx`Having therefore, brethren, a confidence in the entering into the holies by the blood of Christ:€€ƒx`A new and living way which he hath dedicated for us through the veil, that is to say, his flesh:€€ƒx(And a high priest over the house of God:€€ƒx”Let us draw near with a true heart, in fulness of faith, having our hearts sprinkled from an evil conscience and our bodies washed with clean water.€€ƒxeLet us hold fast the confession of our hope without wavering (for he is faithful that hath promised):€€ƒxKAnd let us consider one another, to provoke unto charity and to good works:€€ƒxƒNot forsaking our assembly, as some are accustomed: but comforting one anther, and so much the more as you see the day approaching.€€ƒxhFor if we sin wilfully after having the knowledge of the truth, there is now left no sacrifice for sins:€€ƒxkBut a certain dreadful expectation of judgment, and the rage of a fire which shall consume the adversaries.€€ƒxXA man making void the law of Moses dieth without any mercy under two or three witnesses:€€ƒxìHow much more, do you think he deserveth worse punishments, who hath trodden under foot the Son of God and hath esteemed the blood of the testament unclean, by which he was sanctified, and hath offered an affront to the Spirit of grace?€€ƒxxFor we know him that hath said: Vengeance belongeth to me, and I will repay. And again: The Lord shall judge his people.€€ƒx?It is a fearful thing to fall into the hands of the living God.€€ƒxgBut call to mind the former days, wherein, being illuminated, you endured a great fight of afflictions.€€ƒxAnd on the one hand indeed, by reproaches and tribulations, were made a gazingstock; and on the other, became companions of them that were used in such sort.€€ƒx¦For you both had compassion on them that were in bands and took with joy the being stripped of your own goods, knowing that you have a better and a lasting substance.€€ƒx@Do not therefore lose your confidence which hath a great reward.€€ƒx\For patience is necessary for you: that, doing the will of God, you may receive the promise.€€ƒx^For yet a little and a very little while, and he that is to come will come and will not delay.€€ƒxYBut my just man liveth by faith: but if he withdraw himself, he shall not please my soul.€€ƒxbBut we are not the children of withdrawing unto perdition, but of faith to the saving of the soul.€€ƒ`€€ƒx^Now, faith is the substance of things to be hoped for, the evidence of things that appear not.€€ƒx.For by this the ancients obtained a testimony.€€ƒx}By faith we understand that the world was framed by the word of God: that from invisible things visible things might be made.€€ƒx¼By faith Abel offered to God a sacrifice exceeding that of Cain, by which he obtained a testimony that he was just, God giving testimony to his gifts. And by it he being dead yet speaketh.€€˜(ƒxBut without faith it is impossible to please God. For he that cometh to God must believe that he is: and is a rewarder to them that seek him.€€ƒxöBy faith Noe, having received an answer concerning those things which as yet were not seen, moved with fear, framed the ark for the saving of his house: by the which he condemned the world and was instituted heir of the justice which is by faith.€€ƒxšBy faith he that is called Abraham obeyed to go out into a place which he was to receive for an inheritance. And he went out, not knowing whither he went.€€ƒxBy faith he abode in the land of promise, as in a strange country, dwelling in cottages, with Isaac and Jacob, the co-heirs of the same promise.€€ƒxOFor he looked for a city that hath foundations: whose builder and maker is God.€€ƒx¤By faith also Sara herself, being barren, received strength to conceive seed, even past the time of age: because she believed that he was faithful who had promised,€€ƒxŸFor which cause there sprung even from one (and him as good as dead) as the stars of heaven in multitude and as the sand which is by the sea shore innumerable.€€ƒx³All these died according to faith, not having received the promises but beholding them afar off and saluting them and confessing that they are pilgrims and strangers on the earth.€€ƒxCFor they that say these things do signify that they seek a country.€€ƒxjAnd truly, if they had been mindful of that from whence they came out, they had doubtless, time to return.€€ƒxBut now they desire a better, that is to say, a heavenly country. Therefore, God is not ashamed to be called their God: for he hath prepared for them a city.€€ƒx{By faith Abraham, when he was tried, offered Isaac: and he that had received the promises offered up his only begotten son,€€ƒx9(To whom it was said: In Isaac shalt thy seed be called:)€€ƒxiAccounting that God is able to raise up even from the dead. Whereupon also he received him for a parable.€€ƒx=By faith also of things to come Isaac blessed Jacob and Esau.€€ƒxXBy faith Jacob, dying, blessed each of the sons of Joseph and adored the top of his rod.€€ƒx†By faith Joseph, when he was dying, made mention of the going out of the children of Israel and gave commandment concerning his bones.€€ƒx“By faith Moses, when he was born, was hid three months by his parents: because they saw he was a comely babe, and they feared not the king's edict.€€ƒxXBy faith Moses, when he was grown up, denied himself to be the son of Pharao's daughter:€€ƒxcRather choosing to be afflicted with the people of God than to have the pleasure of sin for a time:€€ƒxrEsteeming the reproach of Christ greater riches than the treasure of the Egyptians. For he looked unto the reward.€€ƒxpBy faith he left Egypt, not fearing the fierceness of the king: for he endured, as seeing him that is invisible.€€ƒxyBy faith he celebrated the pasch and the shedding of the blood: that he who destroyed the firstborn might not touch them.€€ƒxlBy faith they passed through the Red Sea, as by dry land: which the Egyptians attempting, were swallowed up.€€ƒxLBy faith the walls of Jericho fell down, by the going round them seven days.€€ƒx\By faith Rahab the harlot perished not with the unbelievers, receiving the spies with peace.€€ƒx€And what shall I yet say? For the time would fail me to tell of Gedeon, Barac, Samson, Jephthe, David, Samuel, and the prophets:€€ƒxaWho by faith conquered kingdoms, wrought justice, obtained promises, stopped the mouths of lions,€€ƒx¡Quenched the violence of fire, escaped the edge of the sword, recovered strength from weakness, became valiant in battle, put to flight the armies of foreigners.€€ƒxWomen received their dead raised to life again. But others were racked, not accepting deliverance, that they might find a better resurrection.€€ƒxRAnd others had trial of mockeries and stripes: moreover also of bands and prisons.€€ƒx·They were stoned, they were cut asunder, they were tempted, they were put to death by the sword, they wandered about in sheepskins, in goatskins, being in want, distressed, afflicted:€€ƒxkOf whom the world was not worthy: wandering in deserts, in mountains and in dens and in caves of the earth.€€ƒxRAnd all these, being approved by the testimony of faith, received not the promise:€€ƒxUGod providing some better thing for us, that they should not be perfected without us.€€ƒ`€€ƒx³And therefore we also having so great a cloud of witnesses over our head, laying aside every weight and sin which surrounds us, let us run by patience to the fight proposed to us:€€ƒx³Looking on Jesus, the author and finisher of faith, who, having joy set before him, endured the cross, despising the shame, and now sitteth on the right hand of the throne of God.€€ƒxˆFor think diligently upon him that endured such opposition from sinners against himself that you be not wearied, fainting in your minds.€€ƒx?For you have not yet resisted unto blood, striving against sin.€€˜ƒxUFor whom the Lord loveth he chastiseth: and he scourgeth every son whom he receiveth.€€ƒxzPersevere under discipline. God dealeth with you as with his sons. For what son is there whom the father doth not correct?€€ƒxgBut if you be without chastisement, whereof all are made partakers, then are you bastards and not sons.€€ƒxMoreover, we have had fathers of our flesh for instructors, and we reverenced them. Shall we not much more obey the Father of spirits and live?€€ƒx‘And they indeed for a few days, according to their own pleasure, instructed us: but he, for our profit, that we might receive his sanctification.€€ƒx¼Now all chastisement for the present indeed seemeth not to bring with it joy, but sorrow: but afterwards it will yield to them that are exercised by it the most peaceable fruit of justice.€€ƒxBWherefore, lift up the hands which hang down and the feeble knees:€€ƒxjAnd make straight steps with your feet: that no one, halting, may go out of the way; but rather be healed.€€ƒxKFollow peace with all men and holiness: without which no man shall see God.€€ƒxLooking diligently, lest any man be wanting to the grace of God: lest any root of bitterness springing up do hinder and by it many be defiled:€€ƒxcLest there be any fornicator or profane person, as Esau who for one mess sold his first birthright.€€ƒx¤For know ye that afterwards, when he desired to inherit the benediction, he was rejected. For he found no place of repentance, although with tears he had sought it.€€ƒxsFor you are not come to a mountain that might be touched and a burning fire and a whirlwind and darkness and storm,€€ƒx…And the sound of a trumpet and the voice of words, which they that had excused themselves, that the word might not be spoken to them.€€ƒxqFor they did not endure that which was said: and if so much as a beast shall touch the mount, it shall be stoned.€€ƒxPAnd so terrible was that which was seen, Moses said: I am frighted, and tremble.€€ƒx‰But you are come to mount Sion and to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, and to the company of many thousands of angels,€€ƒxŒAnd to the church of the firstborn who are written in the heavens, and to God the judge of all, and to the spirits of the just made perfect,€€ƒxwAnd to Jesus the mediator of the new testament, and to the sprinkling of blood which speaketh better than that of Abel.€€ƒx¹See that you refuse him not that speaketh. For if they escaped not who refused him that spoke upon earth, much more shall not we that turn away from him that speaketh to us from heaven.€€ƒx„Whose voice then moved the earth; but now he promiseth, saying: Yet once more: and I will move, not only the earth, but heaven also.€€ƒx•And in that he saith: Yet once more, he signifieth the translation of the moveable things as made, that those things may remain which are immoveable.€€ƒxwTherefore, receiving an immoveable kingdom, we have grace: whereby let us serve, pleasing God, with fear and reverence.€€ƒx For our God is a consuming fire.€€ƒ`€€ƒx0Let the charity of the brotherhood abide in you.€€ƒx`And hospitality do not forget: for by this some, being not aware of it, have entertained angels.€€ƒx|Remember them that are in bands, as if you were bound with them: and them that labour, as being yourselves also in the body.€€ƒxaMarriage honourable in all, and the bed undefiled. For fornicators and adulterers God will judge.€€˜ƒx_So that we may confidently say: The Lord is my helper: I will not fear what man shall do to me.€€ƒx}Remember your prelates who have spoken the word of God to you: whose faith follow, considering the end of their conversation,€€ƒx:Jesus Christ, yesterday, and today: and the same for ever.€€ƒx­Be not led away with various and strange doctrines. For it is best that the heart be established with grace, not with meats: which have not profited those that walk in them.€€ƒxLWe have an altar whereof they have no power to eat who serve the tabernacle.€€ƒx}For the bodies of those beasts whose blood is brought into the holies by the high priest for sin are burned without the camp.€€ƒxdWherefore Jesus also, that he might sanctify the people by his own blood, suffered without the gate.€€ƒxHLet us go forth therefore to him without the camp, bearing his reproach.€€ƒxFFor, we have not here a lasting city: but we seek one that is to come.€€ƒx~By him therefore let us offer the sacrifice of praise always to God, that is to say, the fruit of lips confessing to his name.€€ƒx\And do not forget to do good and to impart: for by such sacrifices God's favour is obtained.€€ƒx¼Obey your prelates and be subject to them. For they watch as being to render an account of your souls: that they may do this with joy and not with grief. For this is not expedient for you.€€ƒxjPray for us. For we trust we have a good conscience, being willing to behave ourselves well in all things.€€ƒxPAnd I beseech you the more to do this, that I may be restored to you the sooner.€€ƒxšAnd may the God of peace, who brought again from the dead the great pastor of the sheep, our Lord Jesus Christ, in the blood of the everlasting testament,€€ƒx©Fit you in all goodness, that you may do his will; doing in you that which is well pleasing in his sight, through Jesus Christ, to whom is glory for ever and ever. Amen.€€ƒxpAnd I beseech you, brethren, that you suffer this word of consolation. For I have written to you in a few words.€€ƒxbKnow ye that our brother Timothy is set at liberty: with whom (if he come shortly) I will see you.€€ƒxPSalute all your prelates and all the saints. The brethren from Italy salute you.€€ƒxGrace be with you all. Amen.€€ƒsThe Letter of James€€ƒ`€€ƒxrJames, the servant of God and of our Lord Jesus Christ, to the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad, greeting.€€ƒxKMy brethren, count it all joy, when you shall fall into divers temptations:€€ƒx6Knowing that the trying of your faith worketh patience€€cJas¤dnameeJamesiosis_namecJaslabbreviationcJashchapters…˜ƒx‰But let him ask in faith, nothing wavering. For he that wavereth is like a wave of the sea, which is moved and carried about by the wind.€€ƒxMTherefore let not that man think that he shall receive any thing of the Lord.€€ƒx2A double minded man is inconstant in all his ways.€€ƒx=But let the brother of low condition glory in his exaltation:€€ƒxVAnd the rich, in his being low: because as the flower of the grass shall he pass away.€€ƒx¼For the sun rose with a burning heat and parched the grass: and the flower thereof fell off, and the beauty of the shape thereof perished. So also shall the rich man fade away in his ways.€€ƒxBlessed is the man that endureth temptation: for, when he hath been proved, he shall receive the crown of life which God hath promised to them that love him.€€ƒxyLet no man, when he is tempted, say that he is tempted by God. For God is not a tempter of evils: and he tempteth no man.€€ƒxPBut every man is tempted by his own concupiscence, being drawn away and allured.€€ƒxoThen, when concupiscence hath conceived, it bringeth forth sin. But sin, when it is completed, begetteth death.€€ƒx+Do not err, therefore, my dearest brethren.€€ƒx“Every best gift and every perfect gift is from above, coming down from the Father of lights, with whom there is no change nor shadow of alteration.€€ƒxnFor of his own will hath he begotten us by the word of truth, that we might be some beginning of his creature.€€ƒxgYou know, my dearest brethren. And let every man be swift to hear, but slow to speak and slow to anger.€€ƒx4For the anger of man worketh not the justice of God.€€ƒx‘Wherefore, casting away all uncleanness and abundance of naughtiness, with meekness receive the ingrafted word, which is able to save your souls.€€ƒxLBut be ye doers of the word and not hearers only, deceiving your own selves.€€ƒx|For if a man be a hearer of the word and not a doer, he shall be compared to a man beholding his own countenance in a glass.€€ƒxVFor he beheld himself and went his way and presently forgot what manner of man he was.€€ƒx²But he that hath looked into the perfect law of liberty and hath continued therein, not becoming a forgetful hearer but a doer of the work: this man shall be blessed in his deed.€€ƒxAnd if any man think himself to be religious, not bridling his tongue but deceiving his own heart, this man's religion is vain.€€ƒx©Religion clean and undefiled before God and the Father is this: to visit the fatherless and widows in their tribulation and to keep one's self unspotted from this world.€€ƒ`€€ƒx[My brethren, have not the faith of our Lord Jesus Christ of glory, with respect of persons.€€ƒxFor if there shall come into your assembly a man having a golden ring, in fine apparel; and there shall come in also a poor man in mean attire:€€ƒx²And you have respect to him that is clothed with the fine apparel and shall say to him: Sit thou here well: but say to the poor man: Stand thou there, or: Sit under my footstool:€€ƒxMDo you not judge within yourselves, and are become judges of unjust thoughts?€€˜ƒx€But you have dishonoured the poor man. Do not the rich oppress you by might? And do not they draw you before the judgment seats?€€ƒx=Do not they blaspheme the good name that is invoked upon you?€€ƒxuIf then you fulfil the royal law, according to the scriptures: Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself; you do well.€€ƒx_But if you have respect to persons, you commit sin, being reproved by the law as transgressors.€€ƒxYAnd whosoever shall keep the whole law, but offend in one point, is become guilty of all.€€ƒx°For he that said: Thou shalt not commit adultery, said also: Thou shalt not kill. Now if thou do not commit adultery, but shalt kill, thou art become a transgressor of the law.€€ƒxCSo speak ye and so do, as being to be judged by the law of liberty.€€ƒxeFor judgment without mercy to him that hath not done mercy. And mercy exalteth itself above judgment.€€ƒxsWhat shall it profit, my brethren, if a man say he hath faith, but hath not works? Shall faith be able to save him?€€ƒx8And if a brother or sister be naked and want daily food:€€ƒx—And one of you say to them: Go in peace, be ye warmed and filled; yet give them not those things that are necessary for the body, what shall it profit?€€ƒx7So faith also, if it have not works, is dead in itself.€€ƒx„But some man will say: Thou hast faith, and I have works. Shew me thy faith without works; and I will shew thee, by works, my faith.€€ƒxZThou believest that there is one God. Thou dost well: the devils also believe and tremble.€€ƒxABut wilt thou know, O vain man, that faith without works is dead?€€ƒxXWas not Abraham our father justified by works, offering up Isaac his son upon the altar?€€ƒxWSeest thou that faith did cooperate with his works and by works faith was made perfect?€€ƒx‰And the scripture was fulfilled, saying: Abraham believed God, and it was reputed to him to justice, and he was called the friend of God.€€ƒxCDo you see that by works a man is justified, and not by faith only?€€ƒx„And in like manner also Rahab the harlot, was not she justified by works, receiving the messengers and sending them out another way?€€ƒxUFor even as the body without the spirit is dead: so also faith without works is dead.€€ƒ`€€ƒxSBe ye not many masters, my brethren, knowing that you receive the greater judgment.€€ƒx—For in many things we all offend. If any man offend not in word, the same is a perfect man. He is able also with a bridle to lead about the whole body.€€ƒxhFor if we put bits into the mouths of horses, that they may obey us: and we turn about their whole body.€€ƒx§Behold also ships, whereas they are great and are driven by strong winds, yet are they turned about with a small helm, whithersoever the force of the governor willeth.€€’ƒx¸And the tongue is a fire, a world of iniquity. The tongue is placed among our members, which defileth the whole body and inflameth the wheel of our nativity, being set on fire by hell.€€ƒx{For every nature of beasts and of birds and of serpents and of the rest is tamed and hath been tamed, by the nature of man.€€ƒxGBut the tongue no man can tame, an unquiet evil, full of deadly poison.€€ƒxaBy it we bless God and the Father: and by it we curse men who are made after the likeness of God.€€ƒxdOut of the same mouth proceedeth blessing and cursing. My brethren, these things ought not so to be.€€ƒxIDoth a fountain send forth, out of the same hole, sweet and bitter water?€€ƒxiCan the fig tree, my brethren, bear grapes? Or the vine, figs? So neither can the salt water yield sweet.€€ƒxWho is a wise man and endued with knowledge, among you? Let him shew, by a good contestation, his work in the meekness of wisdom.€€ƒxrBut if you have bitter zeal, and there be contention in your hearts: glory not and be not liars against the truth.€€ƒxNFor this is not wisdom, descending from above: but earthly, sensual, devilish.€€ƒxNFor where envying and contention is: there is inconstancy and every evil work.€€ƒxÇBut the wisdom that is from above, first indeed is chaste, then peaceable, modest, easy to be persuaded, consenting to the good, full of mercy and good fruits, without judging, without dissimulation.€€ƒxCAnd the fruit of justice is sown in peace, to them that make peace.€€ƒ`€€ƒxxFrom whence are wars and contentions among you? Are they not hence, from your concupiscences, which war in your members?€€ƒxyYou covet, and have not: you kill and envy and cannot obtain. You contend and war, and you have not: because you ask not.€€ƒx_You ask and receive not: because you ask amiss, that you may consume it on your concupiscences.€€ƒxœAdulterers, know you not that the friendship of this world is the enemy of God? Whosoever therefore will be a friend of this world becometh an enemy of God.€€‘ƒxhBut he giveth greater grace. Wherefore he saith: God resisteth the proud and giveth grace to the humble.€€ƒxLBe subject therefore to God. But resist the devil: and he will fly from you.€€ƒxyDraw nigh to God: and he will draw nigh to you. Cleanse your hands, ye sinners, and purify your hearts, ye double minded.€€ƒxdBe afflicted and mourn and weep: let your laughter be turned into mourning and your joy into sorrow.€€ƒx;Be humbled in the sight of the Lord: and he will exalt you.€€ƒxÕDetract not one another, my brethren. He that detracteth his brother, or he that judgeth his brother, detracteth the law and judgeth the law. But if thou judge the law, thou art not a doer of the law, but a judge.€€ƒxHThere is one lawgiver and judge, that is able to destroy and to deliver.€€ƒxºBut who art thou that judgest thy neighbour? Behold, now you that say: To-day or to-morrow we will go into such a city, and there we will spend a year and will traffic and make our gain.€€ƒx1Whereas you know not what shall be on the morrow.€€ƒxÅFor what is your life? It is a vapour which appeareth for a little while and afterwards shall vanish away. For that you should say: If the Lord will, and, If we shall live, we will do this or that.€€ƒxFBut now you rejoice in your arrogancies. All such rejoicing is wicked.€€ƒxJTo him therefore who knoweth to do good and doth it not, to him it is sin.€€ƒ`€€ƒxRGo to now, ye rich men: weep and howl in your miseries, which shall come upon you.€€ƒx;Your riches are corrupted: and your garments are motheaten.€€ƒx½Your gold and silver is cankered: and the rust of them shall be for a testimony against you and shall eat your flesh like fire. You have stored up to yourselves wrath against the last days.€€ƒx»Behold the hire of the labourers who have reaped down your fields, which by fraud has been kept back by you, crieth: and the cry of them hath entered into the ears of the Lord of Sabaoth.€€”ƒxJYou have condemned and put to death the Just One: and he resisted you not.€€ƒx¾Be patient therefore, brethren, until the coming of the Lord. Behold, the husbandman waiteth for the precious fruit of the earth: patiently bearing till he receive the early and latter rain.€€ƒx`Be you therefore also patient and strengthen your hearts: for the coming of the Lord is at hand.€€ƒxqGrudge not, brethren, one against another, that you may not be judged. Behold the judge standeth before the door.€€ƒxyTake, my brethren, for example of suffering evil, of labour and patience, the prophets who spoke in the name of the Lord.€€ƒx«Behold, we account them blessed who have endured. You have heard of the patience of Job and you have seen the end of the Lord, that the Lord is merciful and compassionate.€€ƒxµBut above all things, my brethren, swear not, neither by heaven, nor by the earth, nor by any other oath. But let your speech be: Yea, Yea: No, No: that you fall not under judgment.€€ƒxFIs any of you sad? Let him pray: Is he cheerful in mind? Let him sing.€€ƒx‘Is any man sick among you? Let him bring in the priests of the church and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord.€€ƒxƒAnd the prayer of faith shall save the sick man. And the Lord shall raise him up: and if he be in sins, they shall be forgiven him.€€ƒx’Confess therefore your sins one to another: and pray one for another, that you may be saved. For the continual prayer of a just man availeth much.€€ƒx™Elias was a man passible like unto us: and with prayer he prayed that it might not rain upon the earth. And it rained not for three years and six months.€€ƒxUAnd he prayed again. And the heaven gave rain: and the earth brought forth her fruit.€€ƒxBMy brethren, if any of you err from the truth and one convert him:€€ƒx—He must know that he who causeth a sinner to be converted from the error of his way shall save his soul from death and shall cover a multitude of sins.€€ƒxThe First Letter of Peter€€ƒ`€€ƒxsPeter, an apostle of Jesus Christ, to the strangers dispersed through Pontus, Cappadocia, Asia and Bithynia, elect,€€ƒx¼According to the foreknowledge of God the Father, unto the sanctification of the Spirit, unto obedience and sprinkling of the blood of Jesus Christ. Grace unto you and peace be multiplied.€€ƒx³Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who according to his great mercy hath regenerated us unto a lively hope, by the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead:€€d1Pet¤dnamegI Peteriosis_named1Petlabbreviationd1Pethchapters…˜ƒxmWherein you shalt greatly rejoice, if now you must be for a little time made sorrowful in divers temptations:€€ƒx¦That the trial of your faith (much more precious than gold which is tried by the fire) may be found unto praise and glory and honour at the appearing of Jesus Christ.€€ƒx–Whom having not seen, you love: in whom also now though you see him not, you believe and, believing, shall rejoice with joy unspeakable and glorified;€€ƒxBReceiving the end of your faith, even the salvation of your souls.€€ƒxrOf which salvation the prophets have inquired and diligently searched, who prophesied of the grace to come in you.€€ƒx¨Searching what or what manner of time the Spirit of Christ in them did signify, when it foretold those sufferings that are in Christ and the glories that should follow.€€ƒxóTo whom it was revealed that, not to themselves but to you, they ministered those things which are now declared to you by them that have preached the gospel to you: the Holy Ghost being sent down from heaven, on whom the angels desire to look.€€ƒx“Wherefore, having the loins of your mind girt up, being sober, trust perfectly in the grace which is offered you in the revelation of Jesus Christ.€€ƒxZAs children of obedience, not fashioned according to the former desires of your ignorance,€€ƒxgBut according to him that hath called you, who is holy, be you also in all manner of conversation holy:€€ƒx8Because it is written: You shall be holy, for I am holy.€€ƒx¡And if you invoke as Father him who, without respect of persons, judgeth according to every one's work: converse in fear during the time of your sojourning here.€€ƒxŒKnowing that you were not redeemed with corruptible things, as gold or silver, from your vain conversation of the tradition of your fathers:€€ƒxKBut with the precious blood of Christ, as of a lamb unspotted and undefiled€€ƒx^Foreknown indeed before the foundation of the world, but manifested in the last times for you:€€ƒx‡Who through him are faithful in God who raised him up from the dead and hath given him glory, that your faith and hope might be in God.€€ƒxyPurifying your souls in the obedience of charity, with a brotherly love, from a sincere heart love one another earnestly:€€ƒxsBeing born again, not of corruptible seed, but incorruptible, by the word of God who liveth and remaineth for ever.€€ƒxˆFor all flesh is as grass and all the glory thereof as the flower of grass. The grass is withered and the flower thereof is fallen away.€€ƒxqBut the word of the Lord endureth for ever. And this is the word which by the gospel hath been preached unto you.€€ƒ`€€ƒxaWherefore laying away all malice and all guile and dissimulations and envies and all detractions,€€ƒxcAs newborn babes, desire the rational milk without guile, that thereby you may grow unto salvation:€€ƒx0If so be you have tasted that the Lord is sweet.€€ƒxeUnto whom coming, as to a living stone, rejected indeed by men but chosen and made honourable by God:€€˜ƒxWherefore it is said in the scripture: Behold, I lay in Sion a chief corner stone, elect, precious. And he that shall believe in him shall not be confounded.€€ƒxšTo you therefore that believe, he is honour: but to them that believe not, the stone which the builders rejected, the same is made the head of the corner:€€ƒxAnd a stone of stumbling and a rock of scandal, to them who stumble at the word, neither do believe, whereunto also they are set.€€ƒx¸But you are a chosen generation, a kingly priesthood, a holy nation, a purchased people: that you may declare his virtues, who hath called you out of darkness into his marvelous light:€€ƒx|Who in times past were not a people: but are now the people of God. Who had not obtained mercy: but now have obtained mercy.€€ƒxDearly beloved, I beseech you, as strangers and pilgrims, to refrain yourselves from carnal desires which war against the soul,€€ƒxÅHaving your conversation good among the Gentiles: that whereas they speak against you as evildoers, they may, by the good works which they shall behold in you, glorify God in the day of visitation.€€ƒxgBe ye subject therefore to every human creature for God's sake: whether it be to the king as excelling,€€ƒx^Or to governors as sent by him for the punishment of evildoers and for the praise of the good.€€ƒxbFor so is the will of God, that by doing well you may put to silence the ignorance of foolish men:€€ƒxQAs free and not as making liberty a cloak for malice, but as the servants of God.€€ƒx@Honour all men. Love the brotherhood. Fear God. Honour the king.€€ƒxlServants, be subject to your masters with all fear, not only to the good and gentle but also to the froward.€€ƒxdFor this is thankworthy: if, for conscience towards God, a man endure sorrows, suffering wrongfully.€€ƒx—For what glory is it, if, committing sin and being buffeted for it, you endure? But if doing well you suffer patiently: this is thankworthy before God.€€ƒx{For unto this are you called: because Christ also suffered for us, leaving you an example that you should follow his steps.€€ƒx5Who did no sin, neither was guile found in his mouth.€€ƒx…Who, when he was reviled, did not revile: when he suffered, he threatened not, but delivered himself to him that judged him unjustly.€€ƒxWho his own self bore our sins in his body upon the tree: that we, being dead to sins, should live to justice: by whose stripes you were healed.€€ƒxgFor you were as sheep going astray: but you are now converted to the shepherd and bishop of your souls.€€ƒ`€€ƒx£In like manner also, let wives be subject to their husbands: that, if any believe not the word, they may be won without the word, by the conversation of the wives,€€ƒx/Considering your chaste conversation with fear.€€ƒxuWhose adorning, let it not be the outward plaiting of the hair, or the wearing of gold, or the putting on of apparel:€€ƒxxBut the hidden man of the heart, in the incorruptibility of a quiet and a meek spirit which is rich in the sight of God.€€–ƒxnAs Sara obeyed Abraham, calling him lord: whose daughters you are, doing well and not fearing any disturbance.€€ƒxÄYe husbands, likewise dwelling with them according to knowledge, giving honour to the female as to the weaker vessel and as to the co-heirs of the grace of life: that your prayers be not hindered.€€ƒx€And in fine, be ye all of one mind, having compassion one of another, being lovers of the brotherhood, merciful, modest, humble:€€ƒxNot rendering evil for evil, nor railing for railing, but contrariwise, blessing: for unto this are you called, that you may inherit a blessing.€€ƒxzFor he that will love life and see good days, let him refrain his tongue from evil, and his lips that they speak no guile.€€ƒxNLet him decline from evil and do good: Let him seek after peace and pursue it:€€ƒxBecause the eyes of the Lord are upon the just, and his ears unto their prayers but the countenance of the Lord upon them that do evil things.€€ƒx;And who is he that can hurt you, if you be zealous of good?€€ƒxyBut if also you suffer any thing for justice' sake, blessed are ye. And be not afraid of their fear: and be not troubled.€€ƒx‹But sanctify the Lord Christ in your hearts, being ready always to satisfy every one that asketh you a reason of that hope which is in you.€€ƒx¢But with modesty and fear, having a good conscience: that whereas they speak evil of you, they may be ashamed who falsely accuse your good conversation in Christ.€€ƒxRFor it is better doing well (if such be the will of God) to suffer than doing ill.€€ƒx¨Because Christ also died once for our sins, the just for the unjust: that he might offer us to God, being put to death indeed in the flesh, but enlivened in the spirit,€€ƒxFIn which also coming he preached to those spirits that were in prison:€€ƒx¹Which had been some time incredulous, when they waited for the patience of God in the days of Noe, when the ark was a building: wherein a few, that is, eight souls, were saved by water.€€ƒxÊWhereunto baptism, being of the like form, now saveth you also: not the putting away of the filth of the flesh, but, the examination of a good conscience towards God by the resurrection of Jesus Christ.€€ƒx¼Who is on the right hand of God, swallowing down death that we might be made heirs of life everlasting: being gone into heaven, the angels and powers and virtues being made subject to him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx•Christ therefore having suffered in the flesh, be you also armed with the same thought: for he that hath suffered in the flesh hath ceased from sins:€€ƒxvThat now he may live the rest of his time in the flesh, not after the desires of men but according to the will of God.€€ƒxÆFor the time past is sufficient to have fulfilled the will of the Gentiles, for them who have walked in riotousness, lusts, excess of wine, revellings, banquetings and unlawful worshipping of idols.€€ƒxvWherein they think it strange that you run not with them into the same confusion of riotousness: speaking evil of you.€€“ƒx¬For, for this cause was the gospel preached also to the dead: That they might be judged indeed according to men, in the flesh: but may live according to God, in the Spirit.€€ƒxIBut the end of all is at hand. Be prudent therefore and watch in prayers.€€ƒxpBut before all things have a constant mutual charity among yourselves: for charity covereth a multitude of sins.€€ƒx9Using hospitality one towards another, without murmuring,€€ƒxuAs every man hath received grace, ministering the same one to another: as good stewards of the manifold grace of God.€€ƒxğIf any man speak, let him speak, as the words of God. If any minister, let him do it, as of the power which God administereth: that in all things God may be honoured through Jesus Christ: to whom is glory and empire for ever and ever. Amen.€€ƒxmDearly beloved, think not strange the burning heat which is to try you: as if some new thing happened to you.€€ƒxˆBut if you partake of the sufferings of Christ, rejoice that, when his glory shall be revealed, you may also be glad with exceeding joy.€€ƒxªIf you be reproached for the name of Christ, you shall be blessed: for that which is of the honour, glory and power of God, and that which is his Spirit resteth upon you.€€ƒxaBut let none of you suffer as a murderer or a thief or a railer or coveter of other men's things.€€ƒxUBut, if as a Christian, let him not be ashamed: but let him glorify God in that name.€€ƒx™For the time is, that judgment should begin at the house of God. And if at first at us, what shall be the end of them that believe not the gospel of God?€€ƒx[And if the just man shall scarcely be saved, where shall the ungodly and the sinner appear?€€ƒx{Wherefore let them also that suffer according to the will of God commend their souls in good deeds to the faithful Creator.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÇThe ancients therefore that are among you, I beseech who am myself also an ancient and a witness of the sufferings of Christ, as also a partaker of that glory which is to be revealed in time to come:€€ƒxœFeed the flock of God which is among you, taking care of it, not by constraint but willingly, according to God: not for filthy lucre's sake but voluntarily:€€ƒx[Neither as lording it over the clergy but being made a pattern of the flock from the heart.€€ƒx]And when the prince of pastors shall appear, you shall receive a never fading crown of glory.€€ƒxgBe you humbled therefore under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in the time of visitation:€€ƒx8Casting all your care upon him, for he hath care of you.€€ƒxpBe sober and watch: because your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, goeth about seeking whom he may devour.€€ƒxnWhom resist ye, strong in faith: knowing that the same affliction befalls, your brethren who are in the world.€€ƒx²But the God of all grace, who hath called us unto his eternal glory in Christ Jesus, after you have suffered a little, will himself perfect you and confirm you and establish you.€€ƒx4To him be glory and empire, for ever and ever. Amen.€€ƒxBy Sylvanus, a faithful brother unto you, as I think, I have written briefly: beseeching and testifying that this is the true grace of God, wherein you stand.€€ƒxaThe church that is in Babylon, elected together with you, saluteth you. And so doth my son, Mark.€€ƒxWSalute one another with a holy kiss. Grace be to all you who are in Christ Jesus. Amen.€€ƒxThe Second Letter of Peter€€ƒ`€€ƒx”Simon Peter, servant and apostle of Jesus Christ: to them that have obtained equal faith with us in the justice of our God and Saviour Jesus Christ.€€ƒx\Grace to you and peace be accomplished in the knowledge of God and of Christ Jesus our Lord.€€ƒx¨As all things of his divine power which appertain to life and godliness are given us through the knowledge of him who hath called us by his own proper glory and virtue.€€d2Pet¤dnamehII Peteriosis_named2Petlabbreviationd2Pethchaptersƒ•ƒxVAnd in knowledge, abstinence: and in abstinence, patience: and in patience, godliness:€€ƒxKAnd in godliness, love of brotherhood: and in love of brotherhood, charity.€€ƒxŒFor if these things be with you and abound, they will make you to be neither empty nor unfruitful in the knowledge of our Lord Jesus Christ.€€ƒxwFor he that hath not these things with him is blind and groping, having forgotten that he was purged from his old sins.€€ƒxœWherefore, brethren, labour the more, that by good works you may make sure your calling and election. For doing these things, you shall not sin at any time.€€ƒx|For so an entrance shall be ministered to you abundantly into the ever-lasting kingdom of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ.€€ƒx“For which cause, I will begin to put you always in remembrance of these things: though indeed you know them and are confirmed in the present truth.€€ƒxfBut I think it meet, as long as I am in this tabernacle, to stir you up by putting you in remembrance.€€ƒx‚Being assured that the laying away of this my tabernacle is at hand, according as our Lord Jesus Christ also hath signified to me.€€ƒxmAnd I will endeavour that you frequently have after my decease whereby you may keep a memory of these things.€€ƒxœFor we have not by following artificial fables made known to you the power and presence of our Lord Jesus Christ: but we were eyewitnesses of his greatness.€€ƒx­For he received from God the Father honour and glory, this voice coming down to him from the excellent glory: This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. Hear ye him.€€ƒxVAnd this voice, we heard brought from heaven, when we were with him in the holy mount.€€ƒx²And we have the more firm prophetical word: whereunto you do well to attend, as to a light that shineth in a dark place, until the day dawn and the day star arise in your hearts.€€ƒxZUnderstanding this first: That no prophecy of scripture is made by private interpretation.€€ƒxpFor prophecy came not by the will of man at any time: but the holy men of God spoke, inspired by the Holy Ghost.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÙBut there were also false prophets among the people, even as there shall be among you lying teachers who shall bring in sects of perdition and deny the Lord who bought them: bringing upon themselves swift destruction.€€ƒx_And many shall follow their riotousness, through whom the way of truth shall be evil spoken of.€€ƒx¤And through covetousness shall they with feigned words make merchandise of you. Whose judgment now of a long time lingereth not: and their perdition slumbereth not.€€ƒxFor if God spared not the angels that sinned, but delivered them, drawn down by infernal ropes to the lower hell, unto torments, to be reserved unto judgment:€€–ƒx¬And reducing the cities of the Sodomites and of the Gomorrhites into ashes, condemned them to be overthrown, making them an example to those that should after act wickedly,€€ƒxWAnd delivered just Lot, oppressed by the injustice and lewd conversation of the wicked:€€ƒxtFor in sight and hearing he was just, dwelling among them who from day to day vexed the just soul with unjust works.€€ƒx~The Lord knoweth how to deliver the godly from temptation, but to reserve the unjust unto the day of judgment to be tormented:€€ƒx¥And especially them who walk after the flesh in the lust of uncleanness and despise government: audacious, self willed, they fear not to bring in sects, blaspheming.€€ƒxgWhereas angels, who are greater in strength and power, bring not against themselves a railing judgment.€€ƒx§But these men, as irrational beasts, naturally tending to the snare and to destruction, blaspheming those things which they know not, shall perish in their corruption:€€ƒxªReceiving the reward of their injustice, counting for a pleasure the delights of a day: stains and spots, sporting themselves to excess, rioting in their feasts with you:€€ƒx›Having eyes full of adultery and of sin that ceaseth not: alluring unstable souls: having their heart exercised with covetousness: children of malediction.€€ƒxyLeaving the right way, they have gone astray, having followed the way of Balaam of Bosor who loved the wages of iniquity,€€ƒx„But had a check of his madness, the dumb beast used to the yoke, which, speaking with man's voice, forbade the folly of the prophet.€€ƒxnThese are fountains without water and clouds tossed with whirlwinds, to whom the mist of darkness is reserved.€€ƒx–For, speaking proud words of vanity, they allure by the desires of fleshly riotousness those who for a little while escape, such as converse in error:€€ƒxPromising them liberty, whereas they themselves are the slaves of corruption. For by whom a man is overcome, of the same also he is the slave.€€ƒxØFor if, flying from the pollutions of the world, through the knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ, they be again entangled in them and overcome: their latter state is become unto them worse than the former.€€ƒx©For it had been better for them not to have known the way of justice than, after they have known it, to turn back from that holy commandment which was delivered to them.€€ƒxFor, that of the true proverb has happened to them: The dog is returned to his vomit; and: The sow that was washed to her wallowing in the mire.€€ƒ`€€ƒxyBehold this second epistle I write to you, my dearly beloved, in which, I stir up by way of admonition your sincere mind:€€ƒx“That you may be mindful of those words which I told you before from the holy prophet and of your apostles, of the precepts of the Lord and Saviour.€€ƒxmKnowing this first: That in the last days there shall come deceitful scoffers, walking after their own lusts,€€ƒx›Saying: Where is his promise or his coming? For since the time that the fathers slept, all things continue as they were from the beginning of the creation.€€’ƒxGWhereby the world that then was, being overflowed with water, perished.€€ƒx¡But the heavens and the earth which are now, by the same word are kept in store, reserved unto fire against the day of judgment and perdition of the ungodly men.€€ƒx†But of this one thing be not ignorant, my beloved, that one day with the Lord is as a thousand years, and a thousand years as one day.€€ƒx£The Lord delayeth not his promise, as some imagine, but dealeth patiently for your sake, not willing that any should perish, but that all should return to penance,€€ƒxÑBut the day of the Lord shall come as a thief, in which the heavens shall pass away with great violence and the elements shall be melted with heat and the earth and the works which are in it shall be burnt up.€€ƒx€Seeing then that all these things are to be dissolved, what manner of people ought you to be in holy conversation and godliness?€€ƒx©Looking for and hasting unto the coming of the day of the Lord, by which the heavens being on fire shall be dissolved, and the elements shall melt with the burning heat?€€ƒxaBut we look for new heavens and a new earth according to his promises, in which justice dwelleth.€€ƒxƒWherefore, dearly beloved, waiting for these things, be diligent that you may be found before him unspotted and blameless in peace.€€ƒx‘And account the longsuffering of our Lord, salvation: as also our most dear brother Paul, according to the wisdom given him, hath written to you:€€ƒxÙAs also in all his epistles, speaking in them of these things; in which are certain things hard to be understood, which the unlearned and unstable wrest, as they do also the other scriptures, to their own destruction.€€ƒx—You therefore, brethren, knowing these things before, take heed, lest being led aside by the error of the unwise, you fall from your own steadfastness.€€ƒx‰But grow in grace and in the knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ. To him be glory both now and unto the day of eternity, Amen.€€ƒxThe First Letter of John€€ƒ`€€ƒx¤That which was from the beginning, which we have heard, which we have seen with our eyes, which we have looked upon and our hands have handled, of the word of life.€€ƒx›For the life was manifested: and we have seen and do bear witness and declare unto you the life eternal, which was with the Father and hath appeared to us.€€ƒx¯That which we have seen and have heard, we declare unto you: that you also may have fellowship with us and our fellowship may be with the Father and with his Son Jesus Christ.€€e1John¤dnamefI Johniosis_namee1Johnlabbreviatione1Johnhchapters…Šƒx]If we say that we have fellowship with him and walk in darkness, we lie and do not the truth.€€ƒxBut if we walk in the light, as he also is in the light, we have fellowship one with another: And the blood of Jesus Christ his Son cleanseth us from all sin.€€ƒxOIf we say that we have no sin, we deceive ourselves and the truth is not in us.€€ƒxlIf we confess our sins, he is faithful and just, to forgive us our sins and to cleanse us from all iniquity.€€ƒxQIf we say that we have not sinned, we make him a liar: and his word is not in us.€€ƒ`€€ƒx–My little children, these things I write to you, that you may not sin. But if any man sin, we have an advocate with the Father, Jesus Christ the just.€€ƒxfAnd he is the propitiation for our sins: and not for ours only, but also for those of the whole world.€€ƒxHAnd by this we know that we have known him, if we keep his commandments.€€ƒxiHe who saith that he knoweth him and keepeth not his commandments is a liar: and the truth is not in him.€€˜ƒxMHe that saith he abideth in him ought himself also to walk even as he walked.€€ƒx¤Dearly beloved, I write not a new commandment to you, but an old commandment which you had from the beginning. The old commandment is the word which you have heard.€€ƒx”Again a new commandment I write unto you: which thing is true both in him and in you, because the darkness is passed and the true light now shineth.€€ƒxVHe that saith he is in the light and hateth his brother is in darkness even until now.€€ƒxPHe that loveth his brother abideth in the light: and there is no scandal in him.€€ƒx“But he that hateth his brother is in darkness and walketh in darkness and knoweth not whither he goeth: because the darkness hath blinded his eyes.€€ƒxZI write unto you, little children, because your sins are forgiven you for his name's sake.€€ƒx—I write unto you, fathers, because you have known him who is from the beginning. I write unto you, young men, because you have overcome the wicked one.€€ƒxºI write unto you, babes, because you have known the Father. I write unto you, young men, because you are strong, and the word of God abideth in you, and you have overcome the wicked one.€€ƒx~Love not the world, nor the things which are in the world. If any man love the world, the charity of the Father is not in him.€€ƒx§For all that is in the world is the concupiscence of the flesh and the concupiscence of the eyes and the pride of life, which is not of the Father but is of the world.€€ƒxlAnd the world passeth away and the concupiscence thereof: but he that doth the will of God abideth for ever.€€ƒx©Little children, it is the last hour: and as you have heard that Antichrist cometh, even now there are become many Antichrists: whereby we know that it is the last hour.€€ƒx±They went out from us but they were not of us. For if they had been of us, they would no doubt have remained with us: but that they may be manifest, that they are not all of us.€€ƒx?But you have the unction from the Holy One and know all things.€€ƒx{I have not written to you as to them that know not the truth, but as to them that know it: and that no lie is of the truth.€€ƒxsWho is a liar, but he who denieth that Jesus is the Christ? This is Antichrist, who denieth the Father and the Son.€€ƒxiWhosoever denieth the Son, the same hath not the Father. He that confesseth the Son hath the Father also.€€ƒx¼As for you, let that which you have heard from the beginning abide in you. If that abide in you, which you have heard from the beginning, you also shall abide in the Son and in the Father.€€ƒxDAnd this is the promise which he hath promised us, life everlasting.€€ƒxCThese things have I written to you concerning them that seduce you.€€ƒxïAnd as for you, let the unction, which you have received from him abide in you. And you have no need that any man teach you: but as his unction teacheth you of all things and is truth and is no lie. And as it hath taught you, abide in him.€€ƒx„And now, little children, abide in him, that when he shall appear we may have confidence and not be confounded by him at his coming.€€ƒxZIf you know that he is just, know ye, that every one also who doth justice is born of him.€€ƒ`€€ƒx´Behold what manner of charity the Father hath bestowed upon us, that we should be called and should be the sons of God. Therefore the world knoweth not us, because it knew not him.€€ƒx¸Dearly beloved, we are now the sons of God: and it hath not yet appeared what we shall be. We know that when he shall appear we shall be like to him: because we shall see him as he is.€€ƒxQAnd every one that hath this hope in him sanctifieth himself, as he also is holy.€€ƒxGWhosoever committeth sin committeth also iniquity. And sin is iniquity.€€˜ƒx\Whosoever abideth in him sinneth not: and whosoever sinneth hath not seen him nor known him.€€ƒxZLittle children, let no man deceive you. He that doth justice is just, even as he is just.€€ƒx©He that committeth sin is of the devil: for the devil sinneth from the beginning. For this purpose the Son of God appeared, that he might destroy the works of the devil.€€ƒxwWhosoever is born of God committeth not sin: for his seed abideth in him. And he cannot sin, because he is born of God.€€ƒxIn this the children of God are manifest, and the children of the devil. Whosoever is not just is not of God, or he that loveth not his brother.€€ƒxfFor this is the declaration which you have heard from the beginning, that you should love one another.€€ƒx˜Not as Cain, who was of the wicked one and killed his brother. And wherefore did he kill him? Because his own works were wicked: and his brother's just.€€ƒx,Wonder not, brethren, if the world hate you.€€ƒxrWe know that we have passed from death to life, because we love the brethren. He that loveth not abideth in death.€€ƒxoWhosoever hateth his brother is a murderer. And you know that no murderer hath eternal life abiding in himself.€€ƒx‰In this we have known the charity of God, because he hath laid down his life for us: and we ought to lay down our lives for the brethren.€€ƒx›He that hath the substance of this world and shall see his brother in need and shall shut up his bowels from him: how doth the charity of God abide in him?€€ƒxTMy little children, let us not love in word nor in tongue, but in deed and in truth.€€ƒxTIn this we know that we are of the truth and in his sight shall persuade our hearts.€€ƒxTFor if our heart reprehend us, God is greater than our heart and knoweth all things.€€ƒxQDearly beloved, if our heart do not reprehend us, we have confidence towards God.€€ƒx‹And whatsoever we shall ask, we shall receive of him: because we keep his commandments and do those things which are pleasing in his sight.€€ƒx“And this is his commandment: That we should believe in the name of his Son Jesus Christ and love one another, as he hath given commandment unto us.€€ƒx“And he that keepeth his commandments abideth in him, and he in him. And in this we know that he abideth in us by the Spirit which he hath given us.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‰Dearly beloved, believe not every spirit, but try the spirits if they be of God: because many false prophets are gone out into the world.€€ƒxsBy this is the spirit of God known. Every spirit which confesseth that Jesus Christ is come in the flesh is of God:€€ƒx˜And every spirit that dissolveth Jesus is not of God. And this is Antichrist, of whom you have heard that he cometh: and he is now already in the world.€€ƒx{You are of God, little children, and have overcome him. Because greater is he that is in you, than he that is in the world.€€•ƒx‘We are of God. He that knoweth God heareth us. He that is not of God heareth us not. By this we know the spirit of truth and the spirit of error.€€ƒxyDearly beloved, let us love one another: for charity is of God. And every one that loveth is born of God and knoweth God.€€ƒx7He that loveth not knoweth not God: for God is charity.€€ƒx‰By this hath the charity of God appeared towards us, because God hath sent his only begotten Son into the world, that we may live by him.€€ƒx‹In this is charity: not as though we had loved God, but because he hath first loved us, and sent his Son to be a propitiation for our sins.€€ƒxGMy dearest, if God hath so loved us, we also ought to love one another.€€ƒxpNo man hath seen God at any time. If we love one another, God abideth in us: and his charity is perfected in us.€€ƒx[In this we know that we abide in him, and he in us: because he hath given us of his spirit.€€ƒxaAnd we have seen and do testify that the Father hath sent his Son to be the Saviour of the world.€€ƒxXWhosoever shall confess that Jesus is the Son of God, God abideth in him, and he in God.€€ƒx”And we have known and have believed the charity which God hath to us. God is charity: and he that abideth in charity abideth in God, and God in him.€€ƒx‘In this is the charity of God perfected with us, that we may have confidence in the day of judgment: because as he is, we also are in this world.€€ƒx…Fear is not in charity: but perfect charity casteth out fear, because fear hath sin. And he that feareth is not perfected in charity.€€ƒx;Let us therefore love God: because God first hath loved us.€€ƒxšIf any man say: I love God, and hateth his brother; he is a liar. For he that loveth not his brother whom he seeth, how can he love God whom he seeth not?€€ƒxTAnd this commandment we have from God, that he who loveth God love also his brother.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŠWhosoever believeth that Jesus is the Christ, is born of God. And every one that loveth him who begot, loveth him also who is born of him.€€ƒx]In this we know that we love the children of God: when we love God and keep his commandments.€€ƒxbFor this is the charity of God: That we keep his commandments. And his commandments are not heavy.€€ƒxrFor whatsoever is born of God overcometh the world. And this is the victory which overcameth the world: Our faith.€€•ƒx This is he that came by water and blood, Jesus Christ: not by water only but by water and blood. And it is the Spirit which testifieth that Christ is the truth.€€ƒxtAnd there are Three who give testimony in heaven, the Father, the Word, and the Holy Ghost. And these three are one.€€ƒxrAnd there are three that give testimony on earth: the spirit and the water and the blood. And these three are one.€€ƒxIf we receive the testimony of men, the testimony of God is greater. For this is the testimony of God, which is greater, because he hath testified of his Son.€€ƒxÉHe that believeth in the Son of God hath the testimony of God in himself. He that believeth not the Son maketh him a liar: because he believeth not in the testimony which God hath testified of his Son.€€ƒx^And this is the testimony that God hath given to us eternal life. And this life is in his Son.€€ƒxGHe that hath the Son hath life. He that hath not the Son hath not life.€€ƒxxThese things I write to you that you may know that you have eternal life: you who believe in the name of the Son of God.€€ƒxyAnd this is the confidence which we have towards him: That, whatsoever we shall ask according to his will, he heareth us.€€ƒxmAnd we know that he heareth us whatsoever we ask: we know that we have the petitions which we request of him.€€ƒxÅHe that knoweth his brother to sin a sin which is not to death, let him ask: and life shall be given to him who sinneth not to death. There is a sin unto death. For that I say not that any man ask.€€ƒx3All iniquity is sin. And there is a sin unto death.€€ƒx€We know that whosoever is born of God sinneth not: but the generation of God preserveth him and the wicked one toucheth him not.€€ƒxGWe know that we are of God and the whole world is seated in wickedness.€€ƒx¬And we know that the Son of God is come. And he hath given us understanding that we may know the true God and may be in his true Son. This is the true God and life eternal.€€ƒx2Little children, keep yourselves from idols. Amen.€€ƒxThe Second Letter of John€€ƒ`€€ƒx†The Ancient to the lady Elect and her children, whom I love in the truth: and not I only, but also all they that have known the truth,€€ƒxMFor the sake of the truth which dwelleth in us and shall be with us for ever.€€ƒxyGrace be with you, mercy and peace from God the Father and from Christ Jesus the Son of the Father: in truth and charity.€€e2John¤dnamegII Johniosis_namee2Johnlabbreviatione2Johnhchaptersƒx¥And this is charity: That we walk according to his commandments. For this is the commandment that, as you have heard from the beginning, you should walk in the same:€€ƒxŠFor many seducers are gone out into the world who confess not that Jesus Christ is come in the flesh. This is a seducer and an antichrist.€€ƒxpLook to yourselves, that you lose not the things which you have wrought: but that you may receive a full reward.€€ƒxWhosoever revolteth and continueth not in the doctrine of Christ hath not God. He that continueth in the doctrine, the same hath both the Father and the Son.€€ƒxqIf any man come to you and bring not this doctrine, receive him not into the house nor say to him: God speed you.€€ƒxOFor he that saith unto him: God speed you, communicateth with his wicked works.€€ƒxšHaving more things to write unto you, I would not by paper and ink: for I hope that I shall be with you and speak face to face, that your joy may be full.€€ƒx-The children of thy sister Elect salute thee.€€ƒxThe Third Letter of John€€ƒ`€€ƒx?The Ancient, to the dearly beloved Gaius, whom I love in truth.€€ƒxDearly beloved, concerning all things I make it my prayer that thou mayest proceed prosperously and fare well, as thy soul doth prosperously.€€ƒxyI was exceedingly glad when the brethren came and gave testimony to the truth in thee, even as thou walkest in the truth.€€e3John¤dnamehIII Johniosis_namee3Johnlabbreviatione3Johnhchaptersƒx”Who have given testimony to thy charity in the sight of the church. Whom thou shalt do well to bring forward on their way in a manner worthy of God:€€ƒxDBecause, for his name they went out, taking nothing of the Gentiles.€€ƒxOWe therefore ought to receive such: that we may be fellow helpers of the truth.€€ƒxxI had written perhaps to the church: but Diotrephes, who loveth to have the preeminence among them, doth not receive us.€€ƒyFor this cause, if I come, I will advertise his works which he doth; with malicious words prating against us. And as if these things were not enough for him, neither doth he himself receive the brethren, and them that do receive them he forbiddeth, and casteth out of the church.€€ƒxˆDearly beloved, follow not that which is evil: but that which is good. He that doth good is of God: he that doth evil hath not seen God.€€ƒxTo Demetrius, testimony is given by all, and by the truth itself: yea and we also give testimony. And thou knowest that our testimony is true.€€ƒxSI had many things to write unto thee: but I would not by ink and pen write to thee.€€ƒx‰But I hope speedily to see thee: and we will speak mouth to mouth. Peace be to thee. Our friends salute thee. Salute the friends by name.€€ƒ`€€ƒrThe Letter of Jude€€ƒ`€€ƒxŒJude, the servant of Jesus Christ and brother of James: to them that are beloved in God the Father and preserved in Jesus Christ and called.€€ƒx3Mercy unto you and peace: and charity be fulfilled.€€dJude¤dnamedJudeiosis_namedJudelabbreviationdJudehchapters˜ƒx«I will therefore admonish you, though ye once knew all things, that Jesus, having saved the people out of the land of Egypt, did afterwards destroy them that believed not.€€ƒx«And the angels who kept not their principality but forsook their own habitation, he hath reserved under darkness in everlasting chains, unto the judgment of the great day.€€ƒxÆAs Sodom and Gomorrha and the neighbouring cities, in like manner, having given themselves to fornication and going after other flesh, were made an example, suffering the punishment of eternal fire.€€ƒx[In like manner, these men also defile the flesh and despise dominion and blaspheme majesty.€€ƒx¸When Michael the archangel, disputing with the devil, contended about the body of Moses, he durst not bring against him the judgment of railing speech, but said: The Lord command thee.€€ƒx‘But these men blaspheme whatever things they know not: and what things soever they naturally know, like dumb beasts, in these they are corrupted.€€ƒx®Woe unto them! For they have gone in the way of Cain: and after the error of Balaam they have for reward poured out themselves and have perished in the contradiction of Core.€€ƒxÔThese are spots in their banquets, feasting together without fear, feeding themselves: clouds without water, which are carried about by winds: trees of the autumn, unfruitful, twice dead, plucked up by the roots:€€ƒx~Raging waves of the sea, foaming out their own confusion: wandering stars, to whom the storm of darkness is reserved for ever.€€ƒxyNow of these Enoch also, the seventh from Adam, prophesied, saying: Behold, the Lord cometh with thousands of his saints:€€ƒxÎTo execute judgment upon all and to reprove all the ungodly for all the works of their ungodliness, whereby they have done ungodly: and for all the hard things which ungodly sinners have spoken against God.€€ƒxšThese are murmurers, full of complaints, walking according to their own desires: and their mouth speaketh proud things, admiring persons, for gain's sake.€€ƒx{But you, my dearly beloved, be mindful of the words which have been spoken before by the apostles of our Lord Jesus Christ:€€ƒxvWho told you that in the last time there should come mockers, walking according to their own desires in ungodlinesses.€€ƒxKThese are they who separate themselves, sensual men, having not the Spirit.€€ƒx^But you, my beloved, building yourselves upon your most holy faith, praying in the Holy Ghost,€€ƒxjKeep yourselves in the love of God, waiting for the mercy of our Lord Jesus Christ, unto life everlasting.€€ƒx&And some indeed reprove, being judged:€€ƒx‚But others save, pulling them out of the fire. And on others have mercy, in fear, hating also the spotted garment which is carnal.€€ƒx«Now to him who is able to preserve you without sin and to present you spotless before the presence of his glory with exceeding joy, in the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ:€€ƒx¡To the only God our Saviour through Jesus Christ our Lord, be glory and magnificence, empire and power, before all ages, and now, and for all ages of ages. Amen.€€ƒxThe Revelation of Jesus Christ€€ƒ`€€ƒx»The Revelation of Jesus Christ, which God gave unto him, to make known to his servants the things which must shortly come to pass: and signified, sending by his angel to his servant John,€€ƒxoWho hath given testimony to the word of God and the testimony of Jesus Christ, what things soever he hath seen.€€cRev¤dnamerRevelation of Johniosis_namecRevlabbreviationcRevhchapters–”ƒx»And from Jesus Christ, who is the faithful witness, the first begotten of the dead and the prince of the kings of the earth, who hath loved us and washed us from our sins in his own blood€€ƒxrAnd hath made us a kingdom, and priests to God and his Father. To him be glory and empire for ever and ever. Amen.€€ƒx¶Behold, he cometh with the clouds, and every eye shall see him: and they also that pierced him. And all the tribes of the earth shall bewail themselves because of him. Even so. Amen.€€ƒxyI am Alpha and Omega, the beginning and the end, saith the Lord God, who is and who was and who is to come, the Almighty.€€ƒxÄI, John, your brother and your partner in tribulation and in the kingdom and patience in Christ Jesus, was in the island which is called Patmos, for the word of God and for the testimony of Jesus.€€ƒxYI was in the spirit on the Lord's day and heard behind me a great voice, as of a trumpet,€€ƒxÆSaying: What thou seest, write in a book and send to the seven churches which are in Asia: to Ephesus and to Smyrna and to Pergamus and to Thyatira and to Sardis and to Philadelphia and to Laodicea.€€ƒxdAnd I turned to see the voice that spoke with me. And being turned, I saw seven golden candlesticks:€€ƒx¥And in the midst of the seven golden candlesticks, one like to the Son of man, clothed with a garment down to the feet, and girt about the paps with a golden girdle.€€ƒxfAnd his head and his hairs were white as white wool and as snow. And his eyes were as a flame of fire:€€ƒxfAnd his feet like unto fine brass, as in a burning furnace. And his voice as the sound of many waters.€€ƒxAnd he had in his right hand seven stars. And from his mouth came out a sharp two-edged sword. And his face was as the sun shineth in his power.€€ƒx‡And when I had seen him, I fell at his feet as dead. And he laid his right hand upon me, saying: Fear not. I am the First and the Last,€€ƒxiAnd alive, and was dead. And behold I am living for ever and ever and have the keys of death and of hell.€€ƒxaWrite therefore the things which thou hast seen: and which are: and which must be done hereafter.€€ƒxÎThe mystery of the seven stars, which thou sawest in my right hand and the seven golden candlesticks. The seven stars are the angels of the seven churches. And the seven candlesticks are the seven churches.€€ƒ`€€ƒx®Unto the angel of the church of Ephesus write: These things saith he who holdeth the seven stars in his right hand, who walketh in the midst of the seven golden candlesticks:€€ƒx»I know thy works and thy labour and thy patience and how thou canst not bear them that are evil. And thou hast tried them who say they are apostles and are not: and hast found them liars:€€ƒxIAnd thou hast patience and hast endured for my name and hast not fainted.€€˜ƒx·Be mindful therefore from whence thou art fallen: and do penance and do the first works. Or else I come to thee and will move thy candlestick out of its place, except thou do penance.€€ƒxTBut this thou hast, that thou hatest the deeds of the Nicolaites, which I also hate.€€ƒx©He that hath an ear let him hear what the Spirit saith to the churches: To him that overcometh I will give to eat of the tree of life which is in the paradise of my God.€€ƒxuAnd to the angel of the church of Smyrna write: These things saith the First and the Last, who was dead and is alive:€€ƒxI know thy tribulation and thy poverty: but thou art rich. And thou art blasphemed by them that say they are Jews and are not, but are the synagogue of Satan.€€ƒxìFear none of those things which thou shalt suffer. Behold, the devil will cast some of you into prison, that you may be tried: and you shall have tribulation ten days. Be thou faithful unto death: and I will give thee the crown of life.€€ƒx†He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the churches: He that shall overcome shall not be hurt by the second death.€€ƒxlAnd to the angel of the church of Pergamus write: These things saith he that hath the sharp two-edged sword:€€ƒxÛI know where thou dwellest, where the seat of Satan is. And thou holdest fast my name and hast not denied my faith. Even in those days when Antipas was my faithful witness, who was slain among you, where Satan dwelleth.€€ƒxÎBut I have against thee a few things: because thou hast there them that hold the doctrine of Balaam who taught Balac to cast a stumblingblock before the children of Israel, to eat and to commit fornication.€€ƒx@So hast thou also them that hold the doctrine of the Nicolaites.€€ƒxvIn like manner do penance. If not, I will come to thee quickly and will fight against them with the sword of my mouth.€€ƒxöHe that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the churches: To him that overcometh I will give the hidden manna and will give him a white counter: and in the counter, a new name written, which no man knoweth but he that receiveth it.€€ƒxŸAnd to the angel of the church of Thyatira write: These things saith the Son of God, who hath his eyes like to a flame of fire and his feet like to fine brass.€€ƒx„I know thy works and thy faith and thy charity and thy ministry and thy patience and thy last works, which are more than the former.€€ƒxÕBut I have against thee a few things: because thou sufferest the woman Jezabel, who calleth herself a prophetess, to teach and to seduce my servants, to commit fornication and to eat of things sacrificed to idols.€€ƒx\And I gave her a time that she might do penance: and she will not repent of her fornication.€€ƒx—Behold, I will cast her into a bed: and they that commit adultery with her shall be in very great tribulation, except they do penance from their deeds,€€ƒxÈAnd I will kill her children with death: and all the churches shall know that I am he that searcheth the reins and hearts. And I will give to every one of you according to your works. But to you I say€€ƒx©And to the rest who are at Thyatira: Whosoever have not this doctrine and who have not known the depths of Satan, as they say: I will not put upon you any other burthen.€€ƒx/Yet that which you have, hold fast till I come.€€ƒxbAnd he that shall overcome and keep my works unto the end, I will give him power over the nations.€€ƒx^And he shall rule them with a rod of iron: and as the vessel of a potter they shall be broken:€€ƒxKAs I also have received of my Father. And I will give him the morning star.€€ƒxHHe that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the churches.€€ƒ`€€ƒxÎAnd to the angel of the church of Sardis write: These things saith he that hath the seven spirits of God and the seven stars: I know thy works, and that thou hast the name of being alive. And thou art dead.€€ƒxwBe watchful and strengthen the things that remain, which are ready to die. For I find not thy works full before my God.€€ƒx×Have in mind therefore in what manner thou hast received and heard: and observe and do penance: If then thou shalt not watch, I will come to thee as a thief: and thou shalt not know at what hour I will come to thee.€€–ƒx½He that shall overcome shall thus be clothed in white garments: and I will not blot out his name out of the book of life. And I will confess his name before my Father and before his angels.€€ƒxHHe that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the churches.€€ƒxÈAnd to the angel of the church of Philadelphia write: These things saith the Holy One and the true one, he that hath the key of David, he that openeth and no man shutteth, shutteth and no man openeth:€€ƒx¯I know thy works. Behold, I have given before thee a door opened, which no man can shut: because thou hast a little strength and hast kept my word and hast not denied my name.€€ƒxÆBehold, I will bring of the synagogue of Satan, who say they are Jews and are not, but do lie. Behold, I will make them to come and adore before thy feet. And they shall know that I have loved thee.€€ƒx¯Because thou hast kept the word of my patience, I will also keep thee from the hour of temptation, which shall come upon the whole world to try them that dwell upon the earth.€€ƒxSBehold, I come quickly: hold fast that which thou hast, that no man take thy crown.€€ƒyHe that shall overcome, I will make him a pillar in the temple of my God: and he shall go out no more. And I will write upon him the name of my God and the name of the city of my God, the new Jerusalem, which cometh down out of heaven from my God, and my new name.€€ƒxHHe that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the churches.€€ƒxšAnd to the angel of the church of Laodicea write: These things saith the Amen, the faithful and true witness, who is the beginning of the creation of God:€€ƒxTI know thy works, that thou art neither cold nor hot. I would thou wert cold or hot.€€ƒxcBut because thou art lukewarm and neither cold nor hot, I will begin to vomit thee out of my mouth.€€ƒxœBecause thou sayest: I am rich and made wealthy and have need of nothing: and knowest not that thou art wretched and miserable and poor and blind and naked.€€ƒxáI counsel thee to buy of me gold, fire tried, that thou mayest be made rich and mayest be clothed in white garments: and that the shame of thy nakedness may not appear. And anoint thy eyes with eyesalve, that thou mayest see.€€ƒxKSuch as I love, I rebuke and chastise. Be zealous therefore and do penance.€€ƒx›Behold, I stand at the gate and knock. If any man shall hear my voice and open to me the door, I will come in to him and will sup with him: and he with me.€€ƒxŠTo him that shall overcome, I will give to sit with me in my throne: as I also have overcome and am set down with my Father in his throne.€€ƒxHHe that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the churches.€€ƒ`€€ƒxæAfter these things I looked, and behold a door was opened in heaven, and the first voice which I heard, as it were, of a trumpet speaking with me, said: Come up hither, and I will shew thee the things which must be done hereafter.€€ƒxsAnd immediately I was in the spirit. And behold, there was a throne set in heaven, and upon the throne one sitting.€€ƒx–And he that sat was to the sight like the jasper and the sardine stone. And there was a rainbow round about the throne, in sight like unto an emerald.€€‹ƒx›And from the throne proceeded lightnings and voices and thunders. And there were seven lamps burning before the throne, which are the seven Spirits of God.€€ƒxÇAnd in the sight of the throne was, as it were, a sea of glass like to crystal: and in the midst of the throne, and round about the throne, were four living creatures, full of eyes before and behind.€€ƒxÙAnd the first living creature was like a lion: and the second living creature like a calf: and the third living creature, having the face, as it were, of a man: and the fourth living creature was like an eagle flying.€€ƒxâAnd the four living creatures had each of them six wings: and round about and within they are full of eyes. And they rested not day and night, saying: Holy, Holy, Holy, Lord God Almighty, who was and who is and who is to come.€€ƒx†And when those living creatures gave glory and honour and benediction to him that sitteth on the throne, who liveth for ever and ever:€€ƒxªThe four and twenty ancients fell down before him that sitteth on the throne and adored him that liveth for ever and ever and cast their crowns before the throne, saying:€€ƒxŸThou art worthy, O Lord our God, to receive glory and honour and power. Because thou hast created all things: and for thy will they were and have been created.€€ƒ`€€ƒxxAnd I saw, in the right hand of him that sat on the throne, a book, written within and without, sealed with seven seals.€€ƒxwAnd I saw a strong angel, proclaiming with a loud voice: Who is worthy to open the book and to loose the seals thereof?€€ƒxmAnd no man was able, neither in heaven nor on earth nor under the earth, to open the book, nor to look on it.€€ƒx¬And one of the ancients said to me: Weep not: behold the lion of the tribe of Juda, the root of David, hath prevailed to open the book and to loose the seven seals thereof.€€ƒxüAnd I saw: and behold in the midst of the throne and of the four living creatures and in the midst of the ancients, a Lamb standing, as it were slain, having seven horns and seven eyes: which are the seven Spirits of God, sent forth into all the earth.€€ƒxRAnd he came and took the book out of the right hand of him that sat on the throne.€€ƒx×And when he had opened the book, the four living creatures and the four and twenty ancients fell down before the Lamb, having every one of them harps and golden vials full of odours, which are the prayers of saints.€€ƒxæAnd they sung a new canticle, saying: Thou art worthy, O Lord, to take the book and to open the seals thereof: because thou wast slain and hast redeemed us to God, in thy blood, out of every tribe and tongue and people and nation:€€ƒxSAnd hast made us to our God a kingdom and priests, and we shall reign on the earth.€€ƒx¨And I beheld, and I heard the voice of many angels round about the throne and the living creatures and the ancients (and the number of them was thousands of thousands),€€ƒx—Saying with a loud voice: The Lamb that was slain is worthy to receive power and divinity and wisdom and strength and honour and glory and benediction.€€ƒyAnd every creature which is in heaven and on the earth and under the earth, and such as are in the sea, and all that are in them, I heard all saying: To him that sitteth on the throne and to the Lamb, benediction and honour and glory and power, for ever and ever.€€ƒx‘And the four living creatures said: Amen. And the four and twenty ancients fell down on their faces and adored him that liveth for ever and ever.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŸAnd I saw that the Lamb had opened one of the seven seals: and I heard one of the four living creatures, as it were the voice of thunder, saying: Come and see.€€ƒx›And I saw: and behold a white horse, and he that sat on him had a bow, and there was a crown given him, and he went forth conquering that he might conquer.€€ƒx`And when he had opened the second seal, I heard the second living creature saying: Come and see.€€‘ƒx±And when he had opened the third seal, I heard the third living creature saying: Come and see. And behold a black horse. And he that sat on him had a pair of scales in his hand.€€ƒxÌAnd I heard, as it were a voice in the midst of the four living creatures, saying: Two pounds of wheat for a penny, and thrice two pounds of barley for a penny: and see thou hurt not the wine and the oil.€€ƒxmAnd when he had opened the fourth seal, I heard the voice of the fourth living creature saying: Come and see.€€ƒxëAnd behold a pale horse: and he that sat upon him, his name was Death. And hell followed him. And power was given to him over the four parts of the earth, to kill with sword, with famine and with death and with the beasts of the earth.€€ƒx™And when he had opened the fifth seal, I saw under the altar the souls of them that were slain for the word of God and for the testimony which they held.€€ƒx–And they cried with a loud voice, saying: How long, O Lord (Holy and True), dost thou not judge and revenge our blood on them that dwell on the earth?€€ƒxÚAnd white robes were given to every one of them one; And it was said to them that they should rest for a little time till their fellow servants and their brethren, who are to be slain even as they, should be filled up.€€ƒx©And I saw, when he had opened the sixth seal: and behold there was a great earthquake. And the sun became black as sackcloth of hair: and the whole moon became as blood.€€ƒxxAnd the stars from heaven fell upon the earth, as the fig tree casteth its green figs when it is shaken by a great wind.€€ƒxqAnd the heaven departed as a book folded up. And every mountain, and the islands, were moved out of their places.€€ƒx±And the kings of the earth and the princes and tribunes and the rich and the strong and every bondman and every freeman hid themselves in the dens and in the rocks of mountains:€€ƒx—And they say to the mountains and the rocks: Fall upon us and hide us from the face of him that sitteth upon the throne and from the wrath of the Lamb.€€ƒxIFor the great day of their wrath is come. And who shall be able to stand?€€ƒ`€€ƒxÀAfter these things, I saw four angels standing on the four corners of the earth, holding the four winds of the earth, that they should not blow upon the earth nor upon the sea nor on any tree.€€ƒxÆAnd I saw another angel ascending from the rising of the sun, having the sign of the living God. And he cried with a loud voice to the four angels to whom it was given to hurt the earth and the sea,€€ƒxnSaying: Hurt not the earth nor the sea nor the trees, till we sign the servants of our God in their foreheads.€€‘ƒx‰Of the tribe of Juda, twelve thousand signed: Of the tribe of Ruben, twelve thousand signed: Of the tribe of Gad, twelve thousand signed:€€ƒx’Of the tribe of Aser, twelve thousand signed: Of the tribe of Nephthali, twelve thousand signed: Of the tribe of Manasses, twelve thousand signed:€€ƒxOf the tribe of Simeon, twelve thousand signed: Of the tribe of Levi, twelve thousand signed: Of the tribe of Issachar, twelve thousand signed:€€ƒx’Of the tribe of Zabulon, twelve thousand signed: Of the tribe of Joseph, twelve thousand signed: Of the tribe of Benjamin, twelve thousand signed.€€ƒxÛAfter this, I saw a great multitude, which no man could number, of all nations and tribes and peoples and tongues, standing before the throne and in sight of the Lamb, clothed with white robes, and palms in their hands.€€ƒxlAnd they cried with a loud voice, saying: Salvation to our God, who sitteth upon the throne and to the Lamb.€€ƒx¥And all the angels stood round about the throne and the ancients and the four living creatures. And they fell down before the throne upon their faces and adored God,€€ƒx„Saying: Amen. Benediction and glory and wisdom and thanksgiving, honour and power and strength, to our God, for ever and ever. Amen.€€ƒx{And one of the ancients answered and said to me: These that are clothed in white robes, who are they? And whence came they?€€ƒxÀAnd I said to him: My Lord, thou knowest. And he said to me: These are they who are come out of great tribulation and have washed their robes and have made them white in the blood of the Lamb.€€ƒx–Therefore, they are before the throne of God: and they serve him day and night in his temple. And he that sitteth on the throne shall dwell over them.€€ƒxWThey shall no more hunger nor thirst: neither shall the sun fall on them, nor any heat.€€ƒx±For the Lamb, which is in the midst of the throne, shall rule them and shall lead them to the fountains of the waters of life: and God shall wipe away all tears from their eyes.€€ƒ`€€ƒxbAnd when he had opened the seventh seal, there was silence in heaven, as it were for half an hour.€€ƒxdAnd I saw seven angels standing in the presence of God: and there were given to them seven trumpets.€€ƒxßAnd another angel came and stood before the altar, having a golden censer: and there was given to him much incense, that he should offer of the prayers of all saints, upon the golden altar which is before the throne of God.€€ƒxªAnd the angel took the censer and filled it with the fire of the altar and cast it on the earth: and there were thunders and voices and lightnings and a great earthquake.€€ƒxYAnd the seven angels who had the seven trumpets prepared themselves to sound the trumpet.€€ƒxúAnd the first angel sounded the trumpet: and there followed hail and fire, mingled with blood: and it was cast on the earth. And the third part of the earth was burnt up: and the third part of the trees was burnt up: and all green grass was burnt up.€€ƒx¢And the second angel sounded the trumpet: and, as it were, a great mountain, burning with fire, was cast into the sea. And the third part of the sea became blood.€€ƒxtAnd the third part of those creatures died which had life in the sea: and the third part of the ships was destroyed.€€ƒxµAnd the third angel sounded the trumpet: and a great star fell from heaven, burning as it were a torch. And it fell on the third part of the rivers and upon the fountains of waters:€€ƒxAnd the name of the star is called Wormwood. And the third part of the waters became wormwood. And many men died of the waters, because they were made bitter.€€ƒyAnd the fourth angel sounded the trumpet: and the third part of the sun was smitten, and the third part of the moon, and the third part of the stars, so that the third part of them was darkened. And the day did not shine for a third part of it: and the night in like manner.€€ƒxöAnd I beheld: and heard the voice of one eagle flying through the midst of heaven, saying with a loud voice: Woe, Woe, Woe to the inhabitants of the earth, by reason of the rest of the voices of the three angels, who are yet to sound the trumpet!€€ƒ`€€ƒx”And the fifth angel sounded the trumpet: and I saw a star fall from heaven upon the earth. And there was given to him the key of the bottomless pit.€€ƒx£And he opened the bottomless pit: and the smoke of the pit arose, as the smoke of a great furnace. And the sun and the air were darkened with the smoke of the pit.€€ƒx‹And from the smoke of the pit there came out locusts upon the earth. And power was given to them, as the scorpions of the earth have power.€€•ƒx´And it was given unto them that they should not kill them: but that they should torment them five months. And their torment was as the torment of a scorpion when he striketh a man.€€ƒx{And in those days, men shall seek death and shall not find it. And they shall desire to die: and death shall fly from them.€€ƒxªAnd the shapes of the locusts were like unto horses prepared unto battle. And on their heads were, as it were, crowns like gold: and their faces were as the faces of men.€€ƒxFAnd they had hair as the hair of women: and their teeth were as lions.€€ƒxAnd they had breastplates as breastplates of iron: and the noise of their wings was as the noise of chariots and many horses running to battle.€€ƒxAnd they had tails like to scorpions: and there were stings in their tails. And their power was to hurt men, five months. And they had over them€€ƒxvA king, the angel of the bottomless pit (whose name in Hebrew is Abaddon and in Greek Apollyon, in Latin Exterminans).€€ƒxCOne woe is past: and behold there come yet two woes more hereafter.€€ƒx…And the sixth angel sounded the trumpet: and I heard a voice from the four horns of the golden altar which is before the eyes of God,€€ƒxpSaying to the sixth angel who had the trumpet: Loose the four angels who are bound in the great river Euphrates.€€ƒx†And the four angels were loosed, who were prepared for an hour, and a day, and a month, and a year: for to kill the third part of men.€€ƒxnAnd the number of the army of horsemen was twenty thousand times ten thousand. And I heard the number of them.€€ƒxóAnd thus I saw the horses in the vision. And they that sat on them had breastplates of fire and of hyacinth and of brimstone. And the heads of the horses were as the heads of lions: and from their mouths proceeded fire and smoke and brimstone.€€ƒxAnd by these three plagues was slain the third part of men, by the fire and by the smoke and by the brimstone which issued out of their mouths.€€ƒx‘For the power of the horses is in their mouths and in their tails. For, their tails are like to serpents and have heads: and with them they hurt.€€ƒxõAnd the rest of the men, who were not slain by these plagues, did not do penance from the works of their hands, that they should not adore devils and idols of gold and silver and brass and stone and wood, which neither can see nor hear nor walk:€€ƒxvNeither did they penance from their murders nor from their sorceries nor from their fornication nor from their thefts.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¥And I saw another mighty angel come down from heaven, clothed with a cloud. And a rainbow was on his head: and his face, as the sun, and his feet as pillars of fire.€€ƒxuAnd he had in his hand a little book, open. And he set his right foot upon the sea, and his left foot upon the earth.€€ƒxrAnd he cried with a loud voice as when a lion roareth. And when he had cried, seven thunders uttered their voices.€€‹ƒx_And the angel whom I saw standing upon the sea and upon the earth lifted up his hand to heaven.€€ƒxáAnd he swore by him that liveth for ever and ever, who created heaven and the things which are therein, and the earth and the things which are in it, and the sea and the things which are therein: That time shall be no longer.€€ƒx´But in the days of the voice of the seventh angel, when he shall begin to sound the trumpet, the mystery of God shall be finished, as he hath declared by his servants the prophets.€€ƒx­And I heard a voice from heaven, again speaking to me and saying: Go and take the book that is open, from the hand of the angel who standeth upon the sea and upon the earth.€€ƒxÊAnd I went to the angel, saying unto him that he should give me the book. And he said to me: Take the book and eat it up. And it shall make thy belly bitter: but in thy mouth it shall be sweet as honey.€€ƒx“And I took the book from the hand of the angel and ate it up: and it was in my mouth, sweet as honey. And when I had eaten it, my belly was bitter.€€ƒx^And he said to me: Thou must prophesy again to many nations and peoples and tongues and kings.€€ƒ`€€ƒx–And there was given me a reed, like unto a rod. And it was said to me: Arise, and measure the temple of God and the altar and them that adore therein.€€ƒx³But the court which is without the temple, cast out and measure it not: because it is given unto the Gentiles. And the holy city they shall tread under foot, two and forty months:€€ƒxxAnd I will give unto my two witnesses: and they shall prophesy, a thousand two hundred sixty days, clothed in sackcloth.€€“ƒx¢And if any man will hurt them, fire shall come out of their mouths and shall devour their enemies. And if any man will hurt them, in this manner must he be slain.€€ƒxÊThese have power to shut heaven, that it rain not in the days of their prophecy: And they have power over waters, to turn them into blood and to strike the earth with all plagues, as often as they will.€€ƒxŸAnd when they shall have finished their testimony, the beast that ascendeth out of the abyss shall make war against them and shall overcome them and kill them.€€ƒxAnd their bodies shall lie in the streets of the great city which is called spiritually, Sodom and Egypt: where their Lord also was crucified.€€ƒx­And they of the tribes and peoples and tongues and nations shall see their bodies for three days and a half: and they shall not suffer their bodies to be laid in sepulchres.€€ƒx´And they that dwell upon the earth shall rejoice over them and make merry: and shall send gifts one to another, because these two prophets tormented them that dwelt upon the earth.€€ƒxœAnd after three days and a half, the spirit of life from God entered into them. And they stood upon their feet: and great fear fell upon them that saw them.€€ƒxŒAnd they heard a great voice from heaven, saying to them: Come up hither. And they went up to heaven in a cloud: and their enemies saw them.€€ƒxæAnd at that hour there was made a great earthquake: and the tenth part of the city fell. And there were slain in the earthquake, names of men, seven thousand: and the rest were cast into a fear and gave glory to the God of heaven.€€ƒxCThe second woe is past: and behold the third woe will come quickly.€€ƒxÆAnd the seventh angel sounded the trumpet: and there were great voices in heaven, saying: The kingdom of this world is become our Lord's and his Christ's, and he shall reign for ever and ever. Amen.€€ƒxxAnd the four and twenty ancients who sit on their seats in the sight of God, fell on their faces and adored God, saying:€€ƒx›We give thee thanks, O Lord God Almighty, who art and who wast and who art to come: because thou hast taken to thee thy great power, and thou hast reigned.€€ƒy%And the nations were angry: and thy wrath is come. And the time of the dead, that they should be judged and that thou shouldest render reward to thy servants the prophets and the saints, and to them that fear thy name, little and great: and shouldest destroy them who have corrupted the earth.€€ƒx§And the temple of God was opened in heaven: and the ark of his testament was seen in his temple. And there were lightnings and voices and an earthquake and great hail.€€ƒ`€€ƒxˆAnd a great sign appeared in heaven: A woman clothed with the sun, and the moon under her feet, and on her head a crown of twelve stars.€€ƒxUAnd being with child, she cried travailing in birth: and was in pain to be delivered.€€ƒxŠAnd there was seen another sign in heaven. And behold a great red dragon, having seven heads and ten horns and on his heads seven diadems.€€‘ƒxƒAnd she brought forth a man child, who was to rule all nations with an iron rod. And her son was taken up to God and to his throne.€€ƒx’And the woman fled into the wilderness, where she had a place prepared by God, that there they should feed her, a thousand two hundred sixty days.€€ƒx}And there was a great battle in heaven: Michael and his angels fought with the dragon, and the dragon fought, and his angels.€€ƒxIAnd they prevailed not: neither was their place found any more in heaven.€€ƒxÀAnd that great dragon was cast out, that old serpent, who is called the devil and Satan, who seduceth the whole world. And he was cast unto the earth: and his angels were thrown down with him.€€ƒxèAnd I heard a loud voice in heaven, saying: Now is come salvation and strength and the kingdom of our God and the power of his Christ: because the accuser of our brethren is cast forth, who accused them before our God day and night.€€ƒx{And they overcame him by the blood of the Lamb and by the word of the testimony: and they loved not their lives unto death.€€ƒx¿Therefore, rejoice, O heavens, and you that dwell therein. Woe to the earth and to the sea, because the devil is come down unto you, having great wrath, knowing that he hath but a short time.€€ƒxqAnd when the dragon saw that he was cast unto the earth, he persecuted the woman who brought forth the man child.€€ƒxÍAnd there were given to the woman two wings of a great eagle, that she might fly into the desert, unto her place, where she is nourished for a time and times, and half a time, from the face of the serpent.€€ƒx‹And the serpent cast out of his mouth, after the woman, water, as it were a river: that he might cause her to be carried away by the river.€€ƒxAnd the earth helped the woman: and the earth opened her mouth and swallowed up the river which the dragon cast out of his mouth.€€ƒx¤And the dragon was angry against the woman: and went to make war with the rest of her seed, who keep the commandments of God and have the testimony of Jesus Christ.€€ƒx&And he stood upon the sand of the sea.€€ƒ`€€ƒx“And I saw a beast coming up out the sea, having seven heads and ten horns: and upon his horns, ten diadems: and upon his heads, names of blasphemy.€€ƒx¹And the beast which I saw was like to a leopard: and his feet were as the feet of a bear, and his mouth as the mouth of a lion. And the dragon gave him his own strength and great power.€€’ƒxŸAnd they adored the dragon which gave power to the beast. And they adored the beast, saying: Who is like to the beast? And who shall be able to fight with him?€€ƒxAnd there was given to him a mouth speaking great things and blasphemies: and power was given to him to do, two and forty months.€€ƒx}And he opened his mouth unto blasphemies against God, to blaspheme his name and his tabernacle and them that dwell in heaven.€€ƒx–And it was given unto him to make war with the saints and to overcome them. And power was given him over every tribe and people and tongue and nation.€€ƒxšAnd all that dwell upon the earth adored him, whose names are not written in the book of life of the Lamb which was slain from the beginning of the world.€€ƒx%If any man have an ear, let him hear.€€ƒx©He that shall lead into captivity shall go into captivity: he that shall kill by the sword must be killed by the sword. Here is the patience and the faith of the saints.€€ƒxpAnd I saw another beast coming up out of the earth: and he had two horns, like a lamb: and he spoke as a dragon.€€ƒx®And he executed all the power of the former beast in his sight. And he caused the earth and them that dwell therein to adore the first beast, whose wound to death was healed.€€ƒxoAnd he did great signs, so that he made also fire to come down from heaven unto the earth, in the sight of men.€€ƒxğAnd he seduced them that dwell on the earth, for the signs which were given him to do in the sight of the beast: saying to them that dwell on the earth that they should make the image of the beast which had the wound by the sword and lived.€€ƒxÁAnd it was given him to give life to the image of the beast: and that the image of the beast should speak: and should cause that whosoever will not adore the image of the beast should be slain.€€ƒxAnd he shall make all, both little and great, rich and poor, freemen and bondmen, to have a character in their right hand or on their foreheads:€€ƒxwAnd that no man might buy or sell, but he that hath the character, or the name of the beast, or the number of his name.€€ƒx¡Here is wisdom. He that hath understanding, let him count the number of the beast. For it is the number of a man: and the number of him is six hundred sixty-six.€€ƒ`€€ƒx¦And I beheld: and lo a Lamb stood upon mount Sion, and with him an hundred forty-four thousand, having his name and the name of his Father written on their foreheads.€€ƒx°And I heard a voice from heaven, as the noise of many waters and as the voice of great thunder. And the voice which I heard was as the voice of harpers, harping on their harps.€€”ƒxÀThese are they who were not defiled with women: for they are virgins. These follow the Lamb whithersoever he goeth. These were purchased from among men, the firstfruits to God and to the Lamb.€€ƒx^And in their mouth there was found no lie: for they are without spot before the throne of God.€€ƒx¹And I saw another angel flying through the midst of heaven, having the eternal gospel, to preach unto them that sit upon the earth and over every nation and tribe and tongue and people:€€ƒx¸Saying with a loud voice: Fear the Lord and give him honour, because the hour of his judgment is come. And adore ye him that made heaven and earth, the sea and the fountains of waters.€€ƒx™And another angel followed, saying: That great Babylon is fallen, is fallen; which made all nations to drink of the wine of the wrath of her fornication.€€ƒx¥And the third angel followed them, saying with a loud voice: If any man shall adore the beast and his image and receive his character in his forehead or in his hand,€€ƒxÚHe also shall drink of the wine of the wrath of God, which is mingled with pure wine in the cup of his wrath: and shall be tormented with fire and brimstone in the sight of the holy angels and in the sight of the Lamb.€€ƒxÂAnd the smoke of their torments, shall ascend up for ever and ever: neither have they rest day nor night, who have adored the beast and his image and whoever receiveth the character of his name.€€ƒx\Here is the patience of the saints, who keep the commandments of God and the faith of Jesus.€€ƒxÊAnd I heard a voice from heaven, saying to me: Write: Blessed are the dead who die in the Lord. From henceforth now, saith the Spirit, that they may rest from their labours. For their works follow them.€€ƒxAnd I saw: and behold a white cloud and upon the cloud one sitting like to the Son of man, having on his head a crown of gold and in his hand a sharp sickle.€€ƒxËAnd another angel came out from the temple, crying with a loud voice to him that sat upon the cloud: Thrust in thy sickle and reap, because the hour is come to reap. For the harvest of the earth is ripe.€€ƒxXAnd he that sat on the cloud thrust his sickle into the earth: and the earth was reaped.€€ƒx[And another angel came out of the temple which is in heaven, he also having a sharp sickle.€€ƒyAnd another angel came out from the altar, who had power over fire. And he cried with a loud voice to him that had the sharp sickle, saying: Thrust in thy sharp sickle and gather the clusters of the vineyard of the earth, because the grapes thereof are ripe.€€ƒx”And the angel thrust in his sharp sickle into the earth and gathered the vineyard of the earth and cast it into the great press of the wrath of God:€€ƒxAnd the press was trodden without the city, and blood came out of the press, up to the horses' bridles, for a thousand and six hundred furlongs.€€ƒ`€€ƒxAnd I saw another sign in heaven, great and wonderful: seven angels having the seven last plagues. For in them is filled up the wrath of God.€€ƒx¼And I saw as it were a sea of glass mingled with fire: and them that had overcome the beast and his image and the number of his name, standing on the sea of glass, having the harps of God:€€ˆƒx«Who shall not fear thee, O Lord, and magnify thy name? For thou only art holy. For all nations shall come and shall adore in thy sight, because thy judgments are manifest.€€ƒxqAnd after these things, I looked: and behold, the temple of the tabernacle of the testimony in heaven was opened.€€ƒx™And the seven angels came out of the temple, having the seven plagues, clothed with clean and white linen and girt about the breasts with golden girdles.€€ƒx‰And one of the four living creatures gave to the seven angels seven golden vials, full of the wrath of God, who liveth for ever and ever.€€ƒx¹And the temple was filled with smoke from the majesty of God and from his power. And no man was able to enter into the temple, till the seven plagues of the seven angels were fulfilled.€€ƒ`€€ƒxŒAnd I heard a great voice out of the temple, saying to the seven angels: Go and pour out the seven vials of the wrath of God upon the earth.€€ƒx½And the first went and poured out his vial upon the earth. And there fell a sore and grievous wound upon men who had the character of the beast: and upon them that adored the image thereof.€€•ƒxhAnd the third poured out his vial upon the rivers and the fountains of waters. And there was made blood.€€ƒxAnd I heard the angel of the waters saying: Thou art just, O Lord, who art and who wast, the Holy One, because thou hast judged these things.€€ƒxrFor they have shed the blood of saints and prophets: and thou hast given them blood to drink. For they are worthy.€€ƒxgAnd I heard another, from the altar, saying: Yea, O Lord God Almighty, true and just are thy judgments.€€ƒxsAnd the fourth angel poured out his vial upon the sun. And it was given unto him to afflict men with heat and fire.€€ƒxšAnd men were scorched with great heat: and they blasphemed the name of God, who hath power over these plagues. Neither did they penance to give him glory.€€ƒxˆAnd the fifth angel poured out his vial upon the seat of the beast. And his kingdom became dark: and they gnawed their tongues for pain.€€ƒxnAnd they blasphemed the God of heaven, because of their pains and wounds: and did not penance for their works.€€ƒx®And the sixth angel poured out his vial upon that great river Euphrates and dried up the water thereof, that a way might be prepared for the kings from the rising of the sun.€€ƒx‘And I saw from the mouth of the dragon and from the mouth of the beast and from the mouth of the false prophet, three unclean spirits like frogs.€€ƒx«For they are the spirits of devils, working signs: and they go forth unto the kings of the whole earth, to gather them to battle against the great day of the Almighty God.€€ƒx|Behold, I come as a thief. Blessed is he that watcheth and keepeth his garments, lest he walk naked, and they see his shame.€€ƒxSAnd he shall gather them together into a place which in Hebrew is called Armagedon.€€ƒx‹And the seventh angel poured out his vial upon the air. And there came a great voice out of the temple from the throne, saying: It is done.€€ƒx¯And there were lightnings and voices and thunders: and there was a great earthquake, such an one as never had been since men were upon the earth, such an earthquake, so great.€€ƒxÈAnd the great city was divided into three parts: and the cities of the Gentiles fell. And great Babylon came in remembrance before God, to give her the cup of the wine of the indignation of his wrath.€€ƒx=And every island fled away: and the mountains were not found.€€ƒx’And great hail, like a talent, came down from heaven upon men: and men blasphemed God, for the plague of the hail: because it was exceeding great.€€ƒ`€€ƒx´And there came one of the seven angels who had the seven vials and spoke with me, saying: Come, I will shew thee the condemnation of the great harlot, who sitteth upon many waters:€€ƒxWith whom the kings of the earth have committed fornication. And they who inhabit the earth have been made drunk with the wine of her whoredom.€€’ƒxÍAnd the woman was clothed round about with purple and scarlet, and gilt with gold and precious stones and pearls, having a golden cup in her hand, full of the abomination and filthiness of her fornication.€€ƒx‡And on her forehead a name was written: A mystery: Babylon the great, the mother of the fornications and the abominations of the earth.€€ƒxAnd I saw the woman drunk with the blood of the saints and with the blood of the martyrs of Jesus. And I wondered, when I had seen her, with great admiration.€€ƒx¨And the angel said to me: Why dost thou wonder? I will tell thee the mystery of the woman and of the beast which carrieth her, which hath the seven heads and ten horns.€€ƒyThe beast which thou sawest, was, and is not, and shall come up out of the bottomless pit and go into destruction. And the inhabitants on the earth (whose names are not written in the book of life from the foundation of the world) shall wonder, seeing the beast that was and is not.€€ƒxŒAnd here is the understanding that hath wisdom. The seven heads are seven mountains, upon which the woman sitteth: and they are seven kings.€€ƒxiFive are fallen, one is, and the other is not yet come: and when he is come, he must remain a short time.€€ƒxqAnd the beast which was and is not: the same also is the eighth, and is of the seven, and goeth into destruction.€€ƒx“And the ten horns which thou sawest are ten kings, who have not yet received a kingdom: but shall receive power as kings, one hour after the beast.€€ƒxTThese have one design: and their strength and power they shall deliver to the beast.€€ƒx®These shall fight with the Lamb. And the Lamb shall overcome them because he is Lord of lords and King of kings: and they that are with him are called and elect and faithful.€€ƒxoAnd he said to me: The waters which thou sawest, where the harlot sitteth, are peoples and nations and tongues.€€ƒx©And the ten horns which thou sawest in the beast: These shall hate the harlot and shall make her desolate and naked and shall eat her flesh and shall burn her with fire.€€ƒx’For God hath given into their hearts to do that which pleaseth him: that they give their kingdom to the beast, till the words of God be fulfilled.€€ƒxaAnd the woman which thou sawest is the great city which hath kingdom over the kings of the earth.€€ƒ`€€ƒx„And after these things, I saw another angel come down from heaven, having great power: and the earth was enlightened with his glory.€€ƒxÑAnd he cried out with a strong voice, saying: Babylon the great is fallen, is fallen: and is become the habitation of devils and the hold of every unclean spirit and the hold of every unclean and hateful bird:€€˜ƒx™And I heard another voice from heaven, saying: Go out from her, my people; that you be not partakers of her sins and that you receive not of her plagues.€€ƒxSFor her sins have reached unto heaven: and the Lord hath remembered her iniquities.€€ƒx£Render to her as she also hath rendered to you: and double unto her double, according to her works. In the cup wherein she hath mingled, mingle ye double unto her.€€ƒxÄAs much as she hath glorified herself and lived in delicacies, so much torment and sorrow give ye to her. Because she saith in her heart: I sit a queen and am no widow: and sorrow I shall not see.€€ƒxTherefore, shall her plagues come in one day, death and mourning and famine. And she shall be burnt with the fire: because God is strong, who shall judge her.€€ƒxµAnd the kings of the earth, who have committed fornication and lived in delicacies with her, shall weep and bewail themselves over her, when they shall see the smoke of her burning:€€ƒx‘Standing afar off for fear of her torments, saying: Alas! alas! that great city, Babylon, that mighty city: for in one hour is thy judgment come.€€ƒxnAnd the merchants of the earth shall weep and mourn over her: for no man shall buy their merchandise any more.€€ƒxşMerchandise of gold and silver and precious stones: and of pearls and fine linen and purple and silk and scarlet: and all thyine wood: and all manner of vessels of ivory: and all manner of vessels of precious stone and of brass and of iron and of marble:€€ƒxªAnd cinnamon and odours and ointment and frankincense and wine and oil and fine flour and wheat and beasts and sheep and horses and chariots: and slaves and souls of men.€€ƒxŸAnd the fruits of the desire of thy soul are departed from thee: and all fat and goodly things are perished from thee. And they shall find them no more at all.€€ƒxThe merchants of these things, who were made rich, shall stand afar off from her, for fear of her torments, weeping and mourning,€€ƒx˜And saying: Alas! alas! that great city, which was clothed with fine linen and purple and scarlet and was gilt with gold and precious stones and pearls.€€ƒx§For in one hour are so great riches come to nought. And every shipmaster and all that sail into the lake, and mariners, and as many as work in the sea, stood afar off,€€ƒxYAnd cried, seeing the place of her burning, saying: What city is like to this great city?€€ƒxÜAnd they cast dust upon their heads and cried, weeping and mourning, saying: Alas! alas! that great city, wherein all were made rich, that had ships at sea, by reason of her prices. For, in one hour she is made desolate.€€ƒxjRejoice over her, thou heaven and ye holy apostles and prophets. For God hath judged your judgment on her.€€ƒxÑAnd a mighty angel took up a stone, as it were a great millstone, and cast it into the sea, saying: With such violence as this, shall Babylon, that great city, be thrown down and shall be found no more at all.€€ƒyAnd the voice of harpers and of musicians and of them that play on the pipe and on the trumpet shall no more be heard at all in thee: and no craftsman of any art whatsoever shall be found any more at all in thee: and the sound of the mill shall be heard no more at all in thee:€€ƒxıAnd the light of the lamp shall shine no more at all in thee: and the voice of the bridegroom and the bride shall be heard no more at all in thee. For thy merchants were the great men of the earth: for all nations have been deceived by thy enchantments.€€ƒxcAnd in her was found the blood of prophets and of saints and of all that were slain upon the earth.€€ƒ`€€ƒx‰After these things, I heard as it were the voice of much people in heaven, saying: Alleluia. Salvation and glory and power is to our God.€€ƒx¯For true and just are his judgments, who hath judged the great harlot which corrupted the earth with her fornication and hath revenged the blood of his servants, at her hands.€€•ƒxAnd the four and twenty ancients and the four living creatures fell down and adored God that sitteth upon the throne, saying: Amen. Alleluia.€€ƒxƒAnd a voice came out from the throne, saying: Give praise to our God, all ye his servants: and you that fear him, little and great.€€ƒx¿And I heard as it were the voice of a great multitude, and as the voice of many waters, and as the voice of great thunders, saying: Alleluia: for the Lord our God, the Almighty, hath reigned.€€ƒx{Let us be glad and rejoice and give glory to him. For the marriage of the Lamb is come: and his wife hath prepared herself.€€ƒx“And it is granted to her that she should clothe herself with fine linen, glittering and white. For the fine linen are the justifications of saints.€€ƒxAnd he said to me: Write: Blessed are they that are called to the marriage supper of the Lamb. And he saith to me: These words of God are true.€€ƒxäAnd I fell down before his feet, to adore him. And he saith to me: See thou do it not. I am thy fellow servant and of thy brethren who have the testimony of Jesus. Adore God. For the testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy.€€ƒx“And I saw heaven opened: and behold a white horse. And he that sat upon him was called faithful and true: and with justice doth he judge and fight.€€ƒx…And his eyes were as a flame of fire: and on his head were many diadems. And he had a name written, which no man knoweth but himself.€€ƒx`And he was clothed with a garment sprinkled with blood. And his name is called: THE WORD OF GOD.€€ƒxgAnd the armies that are in heaven followed him on white horses, clothed in fine linen, white and clean.€€ƒxİAnd out of his mouth proceedeth a sharp two-edged sword, that with it he may strike the nations. And he shall rule them with a rod of iron: and he treadeth the winepress of the fierceness of the wrath of God the Almighty.€€ƒxUAnd he hath on his garment and on his thigh written: KING OF KINGS AND LORD OF LORDS.€€ƒxÆAnd I saw an angel standing in the sun: and he cried with a loud voice, saying to all the birds that did fly through the midst of heaven: Come, gather yourselves together to the great supper of God:€€ƒxÔThat you may eat the flesh of kings and the flesh of tribunes and the flesh of mighty men and the flesh of horses and of them that sit on them: and the flesh of all freemen and bondmen and of little and of great.€€ƒx’And I saw the beast and the kings of the earth and their armies, gathered together to make war with him that sat upon the horse and with his army.€€ƒxùAnd the beast was taken, and with him the false prophet who wrought signs before him, wherewith he seduced them who received the character of the beast and who adored his image. These two were cast alive into the pool of fire burning with brimstone.€€ƒx›And the rest were slain by the sword of him that sitteth upon the horse, which proceedeth out of his mouth: and all the birds were filled with their flesh.€€ƒ`€€ƒxoAnd I saw an angel coming down from heaven, having the key of the bottomless pit and a great chain in his hand.€€ƒxrAnd he laid hold on the dragon, the old serpent, which is the devil and Satan, and bound him for a thousand years.€€ƒyOAnd I saw seats. And they sat upon them: and judgment was given unto them. And the souls of them that were beheaded for the testimony of Jesus and for the word of God and who had not adored the beast nor his image nor received his character on their foreheads or in their hands. And they lived and reigned with Christ a thousand years.€€ƒxfThe rest of the dead lived not, till the thousand years were finished. This is the first resurrection.€€ƒxÄBlessed and holy is he that hath part in the first resurrection. In these the second death hath no power. But they shall be priests of God and of Christ: and shall reign with him a thousand years.€€ƒyAnd when the thousand years shall be finished, Satan shall be loosed out of his prison and shall go forth and seduce the nations which are over the four quarters of the earth, Gog and Magog: and shall gather them together to battle, the number of whom is as the sand of the sea.€€ƒxhAnd they came upon the breadth of the earth and encompassed the camp of the saints and the beloved city.€€ƒx¦And there came down fire from God out of heaven and devoured them: and the devil, who seduced them, was cast into the pool of fire and brimstone, where both the beast€€ƒxIAnd the false prophet shall be tormented day and night for ever and ever.€€ƒxAnd I saw a great white throne and one sitting upon it, from whose face the earth and heaven fled away: and there was no place found for them€€ƒyAnd I saw the dead, great and small, standing in the presence of the throne. And the books were opened: and another book was opened, which was the book of life. And the dead were judged by those things which were written in the books, according to their works.€€ƒx And the sea gave up the dead that were in it: and death and hell gave up their dead that were in them. And they were judged, every one according to their works.€€ƒxMAnd hell and death were cast into the pool of fire. This is the second death.€€ƒxWAnd whosoever was not found written in the book of life was cast into the pool of fire.€€ƒ`€€ƒxrI saw a new heaven and a new earth. For the first heaven and the first earth was gone: and the sea is now no more.€€ƒxƒAnd I, John, saw the holy city, the new Jerusalem, coming down out of heaven from God, prepared as a bride adorned for her husband.€€˜ƒx­And God shall wipe away all tears from their eyes: and death shall be no more. Nor mourning, nor crying, nor sorrow shall be any more, for the former things are passed away.€€ƒx‰And he that sat on the throne, said: Behold, I make all things new. And he said to me: Write. For these words are most faithful and true.€€ƒx And he said to me: It is done. I am Alpha and Omega: the Beginning and the End. To him that thirsteth, I will give of the fountain of the water of life, freely.€€ƒxaHe that shall overcome shall possess these things. And I will be his God: and he shall be my son.€€ƒxâBut the fearful and unbelieving and the abominable and murderers and whoremongers and sorcerers and idolaters and all liars, they shall have their portion in the pool burning with fire and brimstone, which is the second death.€€ƒx¯And there came one of the seven angels, who had the vials full of the seven last plagues, and spoke with me, saying: Come and I will shew thee the bride, the wife of the Lamb.€€ƒx‡And he took me up in spirit to a great and high mountain: and he shewed me the holy city Jerusalem, coming down out of heaven from God,€€ƒxtHaving the glory of God, and the light thereof was like to a precious stone, as to the jasper stone even as crystal.€€ƒxµAnd it had a wall great and high, having twelve gates, and in the gates twelve angels, and names written thereon, which are the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Israel.€€ƒxuOn the east, three gates: and on the north, three gates: and on the south, three gates: and on the west, three gates.€€ƒxrAnd the wall of the city had twelve foundations: And in them, the twelve names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb,€€ƒxrAnd he that spoke with me had a measure of a reed of gold, to measure the city and the gates thereof and the wall.€€ƒxäAnd the city lieth in a four-square: and the length thereof is as great as the breadth. And he measured the city with the golden reed for twelve thousand furlongs: and the length and the height and the breadth thereof are equal.€€ƒxjAnd he measured the wall thereof an hundred forty-four cubits, the measure of a man, which is of an angel.€€ƒxlAnd the building of the wall thereof was of jasper stone: but the city itself pure gold like to clear glass.€€ƒxÄAnd the foundations of the wall of the city were adorned with all manner of precious stones. The first foundation was jasper: the second, sapphire: the third; a chalcedony: the fourth, an emerald:€€ƒx¶The fifth, sardonyx: the sixth, sardius: the seventh, chrysolite: the eighth, beryl: the ninth, a topaz: the tenth, a chrysoprasus: the eleventh, a jacinth: the twelfth, an amethyst.€€ƒx®And the twelve gates are twelve pearls, one to each: and every several gate was of one several pearl. And the street of the city was pure gold, as it were, transparent glass.€€ƒx[And I saw no temple therein. For the Lord God Almighty is the temple thereof, and the Lamb.€€ƒx’And the city hath no need of the sun, nor of the moon, to shine in it. For the glory of God hath enlightened it: and the Lamb is the lamp thereof.€€ƒxuAnd the nations shall walk in the light of it: and the kings of the earth shall bring their glory and honour into it.€€ƒxRAnd the gates thereof shall not be shut by day: for there shall be no night there.€€ƒxAAnd they shall bring the glory and honour of the nations into it.€€ƒx—There shall not enter into it any thing defiled or that worketh abomination or maketh a lie: but they that are written in the book of life of the Lamb.€€ƒ`€€ƒxoAnd he shewed me a river of water of life, clear as crystal, proceeding from the throne of God and of the Lamb.€€ƒxÈIn the midst of the street thereof, and on both sides of the river, was the tree of life, bearing twelve fruits, yielding its fruits every month: the leaves of the tree for the healing of the nations.€€•ƒxFAnd they shall see his face: and his name shall be on their foreheads.€€ƒx·And night shall be no more. And they shall not need the light of the lamp, nor the light of the sun, because the Lord God shall enlighten then. And they shall reign for ever and ever.€€ƒx´And he said to me: These words are most faithful and true. And the Lord God of the spirits of the prophets sent his angel to shew his servant the things which must be done shortly.€€ƒx^And: Behold I come quickly. Blessed is he that keepeth the words of the prophecy of this book.€€ƒxŸAnd I, John, who have heard and seen these things. And, after I had heard and seen, I fell down to adore before the feet of the angel who shewed me the things.€€ƒx­And he said to me: See thou do it not. For I am thy fellow servant, and of thy brethren the prophets and of them that keep the words of the prophecy of this book. Adore God.€€ƒx]And he saith to me: Seal not the words of the prophecy of this book. For the time is at hand.€€ƒx·He that hurteth, let him hurt still: and he that is filthy, let him be filthy still: and he that is just, let him be justified still: and he that is holy, let him be sanctified still.€€ƒxaBehold, I come quickly: and my reward is with me, to render to every, man according to his works.€€ƒxHI am Alpha and Omega, the First and the Last, the Beginning and the End.€€ƒxœBlessed are they that wash their robes in the blood of the Lamb: that they may have a right to the tree of life and may enter in by the gates into the city.€€ƒxzWithout are dogs and sorcerers and unchaste and murderers and servers of idols and every one that loveth and maketh a lie.€€ƒxŒI, Jesus, have sent my angel, to testify to you these things in the churches. I am the root and stock of David, the bright and morning star.€€ƒx®And the spirit and the bride say: Come. And he that heareth, let him say: Come. And he that thirsteth, let him come. And he that will, let him take the water of life, freely.€€ƒx®For I testify to every one that heareth the words of the prophecy of this book: If any man shall add to these things, God shall add unto him the plagues written in this book.€€ƒxĞAnd if any man shall take away from the words of the book of this prophecy, God shall take away his part out of the book of life, and out of the holy city, and from these things that are written in this book.€€ƒx`He that giveth testimony of these things, saith: Surely, I come quickly: Amen. Come, Lord Jesus.€€ƒx9The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you all. Amen.€€ƒ`€€ƒ`€€ƒ`€€hlanguagebenkdescriptionxAn English translation of the Latin Vulgate produced by English Catholic scholars, most commonly used in its 18th-century Challoner revision.